INSERT INTO `question_responses` VALUES ('a2agraha',1,1,'2025-05-14_11-19-49','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a software interface between the hardware and operating system, allowing programs to be able to interact with various hardware devices in a consistent manner, without having to know all the fine details of each hardware device. Developers use them in general embedded projects as it is able to “abstract” the hardware-specific information and make the code more general and portable for other devices. It saves the developers from having to learn the specific details of each hardware device, and also allows for maintainability by separating the code from the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,1,'2025-05-20_00-58-37','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a software interface between the hardware and operating system, allowing programs to be able to interact with various hardware devices in a consistent manner, without having to know all the fine details of each hardware device. Developers use them in general embedded projects as it is able to “abstract” the hardware-specific information and make the code more general and portable for other devices. It saves the developers from having to learn the specific details of each hardware device, and also allows for maintainability by separating the code from the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,1,'2025-05-20_01-14-34','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a software interface between the hardware and operating system, allowing programs to be able to interact with various hardware devices in a consistent manner, without having to know all the fine details of each hardware device. Developers use them in general embedded projects as it is able to “abstract” the hardware-specific information and make the code more general and portable for other devices. It saves the developers from having to learn the specific details of each hardware device, and also allows for maintainability by separating the code from the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,2,'2025-05-14_11-19-49','The __io_putchar function is a low-level function used by the stdio library in C and functions like printf. The function first takes in a character as an integer. This is then used in the first line of the function, which contains HAL_UART_Transmit – a UART transmission function used to send data over the UART interface. Finally, it returns the character. Overall, this function is used to write a single character to the correct IO channel and is needed because printf calls this for every character in the string to be printed out. If we had not written this function, we would not be able to see the printf statements in the output console window.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,2,'2025-05-20_00-58-37','The __io_putchar function is a low-level function used by the stdio library in C and functions like printf. The function first takes in a character as an integer. This is then used in the first line of the function, which contains HAL_UART_Transmit – a UART transmission function used to send data over the UART interface. Finally, it returns the character. Overall, this function is used to write a single character to the correct IO channel and is needed because printf calls this for every character in the string to be printed out. If we had not written this function, we would not be able to see the printf statements in the output console window.\nThe __io_putchar might need to be implemented differently depending on the project because the function decides where the output goes and the format of the output, and different projects have different requirements as well as different hardware. The printf function does not directly interact with and is separated from the hardware, thus it does not have any knowledge of the hardware nor how its output works. So, one must write the __io_putchar function based on the specific hardware so that output and printf is able to function properly. For example, some embedded devices don’t have a standard console, so how can one see output or printf statements? They can decide how by using the __io_putchar function and implementing it according to their needs. This way, printf acts as a common function in C regardless of hardware. Thus, one of the only changes required when changing hardware, is the implementation of __io_putchar.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,2,'2025-05-20_01-14-34','The __io_putchar function is a low-level function used by the stdio library in C and functions like printf. The function first takes in a character as an integer. This is then used in the first line of the function, which contains HAL_UART_Transmit – a UART transmission function used to send data over the UART interface. Finally, it returns the character. Overall, this function is used to write a single character to the correct IO channel and is needed because printf calls this for every character in the string to be printed out. If we had not written this function, we would not be able to see the printf statements in the output console window.\nThe __io_putchar might need to be implemented differently depending on the project because the function decides where the output goes and the format of the output, and different projects have different requirements as well as different hardware. The printf function does not directly interact with and is separated from the hardware, thus it does not have any knowledge of the hardware nor how its output works. So, one must write the __io_putchar function based on the specific hardware so that output and printf is able to function properly. For example, some embedded devices don’t have a standard console, so how can one see output or printf statements? They can decide how by using the __io_putchar function and implementing it according to their needs. This way, printf acts as a common function in C regardless of hardware. Thus, one of the only changes required when changing hardware, is the implementation of __io_putchar.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,3,'2025-05-14_11-19-49','The debugger allows us to view the values in the stack as the program runs. We can set a breakpoint and view the register values as the program moves along, something which cannot be easily done with printf. It also allowed us to see other register values that we may have not necessarily needed but became interested in later on, without needed to print out each and every register value.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,3,'2025-05-20_00-58-37','The debugger allows us to view the values in the stack as the program runs. We can set a breakpoint and view the register values as the program moves along, something which cannot be easily done with printf. It also allowed us to see other register values that we may have not necessarily needed but became interested in later on, without needed to print out each and every register value.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,3,'2025-05-20_01-14-34','The debugger allows us to view the values in the stack as the program runs. We can set a breakpoint and view the register values as the program moves along, something which cannot be easily done with printf. It also allowed us to see other register values that we may have not necessarily needed but became interested in later on, without needed to print out each and every register value.\nUsing printf in embedded systems (like the Cortex M4) has limitations. These limitations include increased execution time and resource usage. For example, using many printf statements for debugging might cause a bug to disappear or a completely new bug to appear, as the increased execution time might be enough for the bug to fix or might be too long causing new bugs to happen. Printf also uses resources of the embedded system unnecessarily. Using a debugger overcomes these limitations, as it does not introduce any changes to (or has very low increase in) execution time and resources, as little to no additional code is being compiled onto the processor. This allows for proper debugging, where the system runs and encounters bugs as you would expect, and then you are able to catch those bugs.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,4,'2025-05-14_11-19-49','Assuming we have obtained the value for MSP_init_val, we were able to get a new value for PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 bytes. We chose 0x400 bytes to be the MAIN_STACK_SIZE and THREAD_STACK_SIZE, and we subtracted it because the stack grows downwards. We needed to know the initial location of MSP in order to know where we should place the PSP_val. The OS needs to know the location of the MSP when initializing the PSP to ensure that the PSP is correctly initialized and does not overlap with the MSP or other memory locations.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,4,'2025-05-20_00-58-37','Assuming we have obtained the value for MSP_init_val, we were able to get a new value for PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 bytes. We chose 0x400 bytes to be the MAIN_STACK_SIZE and THREAD_STACK_SIZE, and we subtracted it because the stack grows downwards. We needed to know the initial location of MSP in order to know where we should place the PSP_val. The OS needs to know the location of the MSP when initializing the PSP to ensure that the PSP is correctly initialized and does not overlap with the MSP or other memory locations.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,4,'2025-05-20_01-14-34','Assuming we have obtained the value for MSP_init_val, we were able to get a new value for PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 bytes. We chose 0x400 bytes to be the MAIN_STACK_SIZE and THREAD_STACK_SIZE, and we subtracted it because the stack grows downwards. We needed to know the initial location of MSP in order to know where we should place the PSP_val. The OS needs to know the location of the MSP when initializing the PSP to ensure that the PSP is correctly initialized and does not overlap with the MSP or other memory locations.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,5,'2025-05-14_11-19-49','If we wanted to allocate multiple new stacks using the same stack allocation method, then we would be able to use a “for-loop” and create a new stack each iteration. We would know where to place each stack if we either store all the locations of the stacks we created (for example in an array of some sort) or if we just store the location of the previously created stack. Then we know where to create the new stack by subtracting the desired number of bytes (for example: 0x400 bytes) from the previously created stack.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,5,'2025-05-20_00-58-37','We would start by getting an known stack address (the MSP_init_val), and then get an initial stack address by subtracting a desired amount of bytes (such as 0x400 bytes). We need this initial address to ensure no overlap occurs and the address is valid.\nThen, if we wanted to allocate multiple new stacks using the same stack allocation method, then we would be able to use a “for-loop” and create a new stack each iteration.\nWe would know where to place each stack if we either store all the locations of the stacks we created (for example in an array of some sort) or if we just store the location of the previously created stack. Then we know where to create the new stack by subtracting the desired number of bytes (for example: 0x400 bytes) from the previously created stack. This would ensure that no overlap occurs and the stack remains aligned.\nIf one of the previous stacks failed to be created, then we would have to ensure that we are not stacking ontop of “empty space”. We might be able to ensure this by checking if the stack actually exists in the expected memory address. If it’s not, then we put the new stack at that memory address instead.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',1,5,'2025-05-20_01-14-34','We would start by getting an known stack address (the MSP_init_val), and then get an initial stack address by subtracting a desired amount of bytes (such as 0x400 bytes). We need this initial address to ensure no overlap occurs and the address is valid.\nThen, if we wanted to allocate multiple new stacks using the same stack allocation method, then we would be able to use a “for-loop” and create a new stack each iteration.\nWe would know where to place each stack if we either store all the locations of the stacks we created (for example in an array of some sort) or if we just store the location of the previously created stack. Then we know where to create the new stack by subtracting the desired number of bytes (for example: 0x400 bytes) from the previously created stack. This would ensure that no overlap occurs and the stack remains aligned.\nIf one of the previous stacks failed to be created, then we would have to ensure that we are not stacking ontop of “empty space”. We might be able to ensure this by checking if the stack actually exists in the expected memory address. If it’s not, then we put the new stack at that memory address instead.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,1,'2025-06-24_18-17-37','Rather than calling the print_continously function directly, we were able to manipulate PC to hold the function pointer to print_continously. To do this, we made a jumpAssembly function with an input argument, which we passed in the print_continously function so that the address of print_continously goes into R0. Then, in our jumpAssembly function, we called an assembly command to move the value in R0 into PC. The PC register stores the address of the next instruction to execute. So when we set PC to be the address of the print_continously function, we set the next instruction to execute to be the print continuously function, hence why print_continously was called and our console outputted “Hello, PC!” endlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,1,'2025-06-24_20-15-05','Rather than calling the print_continously function directly, we were able to manipulate PC to hold the function pointer to print_continously. To do this, we made a jumpAssembly function with an input argument, which we passed in the print_continously function so that the address of print_continously goes into R0. Then, in our jumpAssembly function, we called an assembly command to move the value in R0 into PC. The PC register stores the address of the next instruction to execute. So when we set PC to be the address of the print_continously function, we set the next instruction to execute to be the print continuously function, hence why print_continously was called and our console outputted “Hello, PC!” endlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,1,'2025-06-24_21-16-07','Rather than calling the print_continously function directly, we were able to manipulate PC to hold the function pointer to print_continously. To do this, we made a jumpAssembly function with an input argument, which we passed in the print_continously function so that the address of print_continously goes into R0. Then, in our jumpAssembly function, we called an assembly command to move the value in R0 into PC. The PC register stores the address of the next instruction to execute. So when we set PC to be the address of the print_continously function, we set the next instruction to execute to be the print continuously function, hence why print_continously was called and our console outputted “Hello, PC!” endlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,2,'2025-06-24_18-17-37','A system call is an intentional, requested service by the program to the Operating System (OS). Essentially, a system call is a bridge between the user space and the OS, as user programs can request services from the OS’s kernel. It allows programs to perform more privileged operations that would otherwise be restricted, such as accessing hardware. System calls can be implemented using software-generated interrupts, also known as a trap, to transfer control to the OS.\nIn our case, on the Cortex-M4, we have SVC’s (Supervisor Calls). If a program wants access to hardware, it can create an SVC exception and the OS’s software exception handler can provide the requested service to the program. Rather than allowing programs to directly access hardware, the OS can give access to the hardware through an SVC.\nTo put it simply, a program can run regular instructions on its on. But to do more privileged operations, it needs to request the OS for help through a system call.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,2,'2025-06-24_20-15-05','A system call is function call. It is an intentional, requested service by the program to the Operating System (OS). Essentially, a system call is a bridge between the user space and the OS, as user programs can request services from the OS’s kernel. It allows programs to perform more privileged operations that would otherwise be restricted, such as accessing hardware. System calls can be implemented using software-generated interrupts, also known as a trap, to transfer control to the OS. To put it simply, a program can run regular instructions on its on. But to do more privileged operations, it needs to request the OS for help through a system call.\nSystem calls allow for hardware protection, better security, and helps prevent untrustworthy or malicious code from running. This is because system calls act as controlled entry points to the hardware, letting the OS control what gets in and what they are allowed to do. Rather than allowing programs to directly access the hardware (with possible malicious intent), the OS only exposes specific functions for the hardware by using system calls. This way, users are separated from the more privileged kernel (by having a user mode and a kernel mode), preventing the users from purposely or accidentally running untrustworthy programs.\nIn our case, on the Cortex-M4, we have SVC’s (Supervisor Calls). If a program wants access to hardware, it can create an SVC exception and the OS’s software exception handler can provide the requested service to the program. Rather than allowing programs to directly access hardware, the OS can give access to the hardware through an SVC.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,2,'2025-06-24_21-16-07','A system call is function call. It is an intentional, requested service by the program to the Operating System (OS). Essentially, a system call is a bridge between the user space and the OS, as user programs can request services from the OS’s kernel. It allows programs to perform more privileged operations that would otherwise be restricted, such as accessing hardware. System calls can be implemented using software-generated interrupts, also known as a trap, to transfer control to the OS. To put it simply, a program can run regular instructions on its on. But to do more privileged operations, it needs to request the OS for help through a system call.\nSystem calls allow for hardware protection, better security, and helps prevent untrustworthy or malicious code from running. This is because system calls act as controlled entry points to the hardware, letting the OS control what gets in and what they are allowed to do. Rather than allowing programs to directly access the hardware (with possible malicious intent), the OS only exposes specific functions for the hardware by using system calls. This way, users are separated from the more privileged kernel (by having a user mode and a kernel mode), preventing the users from purposely or accidentally running untrustworthy programs.\nIn our case, on the Cortex-M4, we have SVC’s (Supervisor Calls). If a program wants access to hardware, it can create an SVC exception and the OS’s software exception handler can provide the requested service to the program. Rather than allowing programs to directly access hardware, the OS can give access to the hardware through an SVC.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,3,'2025-06-24_18-17-37','“Set up a thread’s stack” means to create a separate stack for the thread. Each thread in a multithreaded system needs its own stack. We store register values on the stack because the Cortex-M4 looks for a specific format for the stack frame when switching to a new thread. There are 8 specific registers (hardware-saved) that the CPU needs in order to know when switching to and from a thread. These registers are xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0. When switching to a thread, the CPU loads the registers from the stack and begins executing at the thread’s PC. Then when switching from a thread, the CPU saves the 8 registers to the thread’s stack (PSP) and load’s the next thread’s stack. Note that the CPU automatically saves xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, while R4-R11 must be manually saved.\nTo setup the thread’s stack, we first created a stack pointer to act as the top of the thread stack (which is some 0x400 bytes below the MSP). We then decremented the stackptr many times to set values for the registers in the following order: xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4. We set xPSR to 1<<24 (also known as setting the T-bit to Thumb mode for Cortex-M CPUs) and PC to the address of the print_continously function (as that is where we want the thread to execute). We then set all the other registers to an arbitrary number (0xA specifically) as we needed to set some value for the registers when setting up the thread stack, but had no actual use for it.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,3,'2025-06-24_20-15-05','“Set up a thread’s stack” means to create a separate stack for the thread. Each thread in a multithreaded system needs its own stack. We store register values on the stack because the Cortex-M4 looks for a specific format for the stack frame when switching to a new thread. There are 8 specific registers (hardware-saved) that the CPU needs in order to know when switching to and from a thread. These registers are xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0. When switching to a thread, the CPU loads the registers from the stack and begins executing at the thread’s PC. Then when switching from a thread, the CPU saves the 8 registers to the thread’s stack (PSP) and load’s the next thread’s stack. Note that the CPU automatically saves xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, while R4-R11 must be manually saved.\nTo setup the thread’s stack, we first created a stack pointer to act as the top of the thread stack (which is some 0x400 bytes below the MSP). We then decremented the stackptr many times to set values for the registers in the following order: xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4. We set xPSR to 1<<24 (also known as setting the T-bit to Thumb mode for Cortex-M CPUs) and PC to the address of the print_continously function (as that is where we want the thread to execute). We then set all the other registers to an arbitrary number (0xA specifically) as we needed to set some value for the registers when setting up the thread stack, but had no actual use for it.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,3,'2025-06-24_21-16-07','“Set up a thread’s stack” means to create a separate stack for the thread. Each thread in a multithreaded system needs its own stack. We store register values on the stack because the Cortex-M4 looks for a specific format for the stack frame when switching to a new thread. There are 8 specific registers (hardware-saved) that the CPU needs in order to know when switching to and from a thread. These registers are xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0. When switching to a thread, the CPU loads the registers from the stack and begins executing at the thread’s PC. Then when switching from a thread, the CPU saves the 8 registers to the thread’s stack (PSP) and load’s the next thread’s stack. Note that the CPU automatically saves xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, while R4-R11 must be manually saved.\nTo setup the thread’s stack, we first created a stack pointer to act as the top of the thread stack (which is some 0x400 bytes below the MSP). We then decremented the stackptr many times to set values for the registers in the following order: xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4. We set xPSR to 1<<24 (also known as setting the T-bit to Thumb mode for Cortex-M CPUs) and PC to the address of the print_continously function (as that is where we want the thread to execute). We then set all the other registers to an arbitrary number (0xA specifically) as we needed to set some value for the registers when setting up the thread stack, but had no actual use for it.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,4,'2025-06-24_18-17-37','When an exception or interrupt occurs and the CPU tries to switch from the thread to something else, it needs to know PSP (the stack pointer) for the thread to know where to push registers onto the stack. It must be a valid PSP address in order for it to work. But if PSP was not set, then when it tries to use the default PSP address which does not correspond to the thread. For example, the default PSP address in our case seems to be 0x0, and this address does not allow for read/write. So when the CPU attempts to push register values and write data, it would not be able to due to the restriction and it would cause a hard fault due to invalid/denied memory access.\nNote that if PSP was a writeable, but incorrect, address however, then it may not immediately hard fault but will most likely cause many issues in the program. For example, the CPU might end up restoring garbage register values, causing an immediate hard fault if something like PC or XPSR has an incorrect value.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,4,'2025-06-24_20-15-05','When an exception or interrupt occurs and the CPU tries to switch from the thread to something else, it needs to know PSP (the stack pointer) for the thread to know where to push registers onto the stack. It must be a valid PSP address in order for it to work. But if PSP was not set, then when it tries to use the default PSP address which does not correspond to the thread. For example, the default PSP address in our case seems to be 0x0, and this address does not allow for read/write. So when the CPU attempts to push register values and write data, it would not be able to due to the restriction and it would cause a hard fault due to invalid/denied memory access.\nNote that if PSP was a writeable, but incorrect, address however, then it may not immediately hard fault but will most likely cause many issues in the program. For example, the CPU might end up restoring garbage register values, causing an immediate hard fault if something like PC or XPSR has an incorrect value.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,4,'2025-06-24_21-16-07','The system would crash in a hard fault if we didn’t set the PSP in SVC_Handler_Main because the CPU would not know the location of the thread’s stack. The Cortex-M4 uses the MSP (Main Stack Pointer) by default, which is meant for kernel, exception, and interrupt handling. Threads, however, are expected to use the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) for their own execution, not the MSP.\nWe created the thread’s stack with the required context and we stored register values manually. However, this does not matter unless we set PSP to point to this thread stack. If we do not set PSP to point to this thread stack, then the CPU has no reference to this thread stack and thus will not use it for when running the thread. A default value such as 0x0 (or a “garbage” value that’s currently in PSP) might be used instead of the threads stack address, which is an issue because when the CPU attempts to run the thread, it expects PSP to point to a valid stack frame. But if PSP is invalid (as is the case in this scenario where PSP does not point to the thread’s stack), then attempting to pop register values (like PC) from the stack would cause a hard fault. This hard fault would occur because of invalid/illegal memory access (or branching to a bad address) as register values like PC don’t exist or are garbage values.\nThe hard fault might occur when the CPU tries to switch from the thread to something else. It needs to know location of the threads stack to be able to pop the thread’s registers like PC, and this location is indicated by the value of PSP. So, if PSP was invalid or not set, and the value stored does not correspond to the thread stack, then popping PC and other registers might cause a hard fault due to invalid memory access (or due to retrieving garbage incorrect values).\nOverall, the PSP must correctly point to the thread’s stack so that the CPU can utilize the thread’s stack for proper context switching, and popping and restoring the thread’s register values.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,5,'2025-06-24_18-17-37','Then the system would crash in a hard fault because after printing once, it would try to return from the function, but there is nowhere valid to return to. This is because we set the Link Register LR for the thread to 0xA in our main() function. The link register stores the return address for function calls and is popped into PC on return. So, in our case, since we set LR to 0xA, the function would try to return to 0xA (an invalid address), thus crashing in a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,5,'2025-06-24_20-15-05','If the thread function returned after printing once and did not loop, the system would crash in a hard fault. The Cortex-M4 expects that the return address and other registers are popped from the stack when a function returns. In our setup however, we manually created the thread’s stack and did not set a valid return address on the stack (i.e. the proper value for Link Register (LR) was not stored). The link register stores the return address for function calls and is popped into PC on return. Thus, when the thread function attempts to return, the CPU tries to pop the LR value (the return address) from the stack and branch to it by setting PC to that address. However, since this value is invalid and was set arbitrarily to 0xA, the CPU attempts to branch to an invalid return address, leading to a hard fault as it attempts to return to an incorrect (illegal or non-executable) region of memory.\nOverall: Since, when manually created the threads stack, we stored an incorrect return address onto our thread’s stack (stored 0xA onto LR), the function would try to branch to 0xA (an illegal address), thus crashing in a hard fault. Without the proper return address on the stack, threads that are manually created can crash when attempting to return from the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',2,5,'2025-06-24_21-16-07','If the thread function returned after printing once and did not loop, the system would crash in a hard fault. The Cortex-M4 expects that the return address and other registers are popped from the stack when a function returns. In our setup however, we manually created the thread’s stack and did not set a valid return address on the stack (i.e. the proper value for Link Register (LR) was not stored). The link register stores the return address for function calls and is popped into PC on return. Thus, when the thread function attempts to return, the CPU tries to pop the LR value (the return address) from the stack and branch to it by setting PC to that address. However, since this value is invalid and was set arbitrarily to 0xA, the CPU attempts to branch to an invalid return address, leading to a hard fault as it attempts to return to an incorrect (illegal or non-executable) region of memory.\nOverall: Since, when manually created the threads stack, we stored an incorrect return address onto our thread’s stack (stored 0xA onto LR), the function would try to branch to 0xA (an illegal address), thus crashing in a hard fault. Without the proper return address on the stack, threads that are manually created can crash when attempting to return from the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,1,'2025-06-27_02-14-35','The specific process that the question is referring to, in which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable, is called “linking”. This is 1 step in a larger compilation process.\nThe compilation process is what allows a C program to be converted into an executable and contains 4 phases: pre-processing, compilation, assembly, and linking.\nThe first phase is pre-processing. Pre-processing includes removing comments from the code, expanding any macros, expanding any included files (i.e. replace the #include statement with the actual header files code), and conditional compilation.\nThen the next phase is compilation. Compiling includes translating any C code to assembly-level instructions and creating *.s files. Each .c file is compiled into a corresponding .s file (assembly code).\nThen, the assembling phase comes, where any assembly files get compiled into object files (*.o files) by the assembler. For example, main.c becomes main.o. These object files contain machine-level code. It should be notes that up until this step, this happens at an individual file level (i.e. does not combine the files yet, and inter-file function calls are not yet resolved).\nFinally, in the linking phase, a “linker” combines these object files along with library objects into a single executable file. It links the function calls with their definitions. The linker knows where all the functions are implemented, so it is able to resolve any function calls. Note that the linker also adds in any necessary startup and shutdown code. It should also be noted that there are 2 types of linking:\nStatic linking: All the code is combined into 1 file and then a executable is made\nDynamic linking: Instead of copying all library code, only references to shared libraries are included. The actual library code is loaded during execution.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,1,'2025-06-27_09-16-09','The specific process that the question is referring to, in which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable, is called “linking”. This is 1 step in a larger compilation process.\nThe compilation process is what allows a C program to be converted into an executable and contains 3 phases: pre-processing, compilation, and linking.\nThe first phase is pre-processing. Pre-processing includes removing comments from the code, expanding any macros, expanding any included files (i.e. replace the #include statement with the actual header files code), and conditional compilation.\nThen the next phase is compilation. Compiling includes translating any C code (pre-processed) to machine-level instructions. Each .c file is compiled into its own corresponding .o file (object file). For example, main.c becomes main.o. These object files contain machine-level code. It should be noted that up until this step, this happens at an individual file level (i.e. does not combine the files yet, and inter-file function calls are not yet resolved). It should also be noted that there is typically an intermediate step where C code gets compiled to assembly-level instructions and then to machine-level instructions, but this is already considered as part of the overall compilation stage.\nFinally, in the linking phase, a “linker” combines these object files along with library objects into a single executable file. It links the function calls with their definitions. The linker knows where all the functions are implemented, so it is able to resolve any function calls. Note that the linker also adds in any necessary startup and shutdown code. It should also be noted that there are 2 types of linking:\nStatic linking: All the code is combined into 1 file and then a executable is made\nDynamic linking: Instead of copying all library code, only references to shared libraries are included. The actual library code is loaded during execution.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,1,'2025-06-27_11-00-01','The specific process that the question is referring to, in which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable, is called “linking”. This is 1 step in a larger compilation process.\nThe compilation process is what allows a C program to be converted into an executable and contains 3 phases: pre-processing, compilation, and linking.\nThe first phase is pre-processing. Pre-processing includes removing comments from the code, expanding any macros, expanding any included files (i.e. replace the #include statement with the actual header files code), and conditional compilation.\nThen the next phase is compilation. Compiling includes translating any C code (pre-processed) to machine-level instructions. Each .c file is compiled into its own corresponding .o file (object file). For example, main.c becomes main.o. These object files contain machine-level code. It should be noted that up until this step, this happens at an individual file level (i.e. does not combine the files yet, and inter-file function calls are not yet resolved). It should also be noted that there is typically an intermediate step where C code gets compiled to assembly-level instructions and then to machine-level instructions, but this is already considered as part of the overall compilation stage.\nFinally, in the linking phase, a “linker” combines these object files along with library objects into a single executable file. It links the function calls with their definitions. The linker knows where all the functions are implemented, so it is able to resolve any function calls. Note that the linker also adds in any necessary startup and shutdown code. It should also be noted that there are 2 types of linking:\nStatic linking: All the code is combined into 1 file and then a executable is made\nDynamic linking: Instead of copying all library code, only references to shared libraries are included. The actual library code is loaded during execution.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,2,'2025-06-27_02-14-35','Using #include on a *.c file is a bad idea because it skips the proper compilation and linking process. Normally in C, each .c file gets compiled into its own .o file (object file), and then the linker links all the object files together. This avoids any duplication of code and ensures that each function and variable is only compiled once.\nIncluding a .c file in another .c file is a bad idea, because this is essentially copy-pasting one .c file into another during compilation. What happens then is that you have the same functions and variables defined multiple times (because there is one copy in the original .c file for these functions and variables, and one copy from the #include, as an example). These causes duplicate symbol errors during linking. It is very similar to why guards exist for .h files, where you only want a .c file to include a header file once and don’t want to accidentally re-include the header file.\nYou also lose structure and modularity of the code. If you include a .c file into another .c file, then the c files rely on each other, which is bad as it is hard to manage, test, and compile each file separately.\nLastly, compilers are optimized to handle .c files individually, so including .c files confuses the build system and makes debugging very hard.\nFor good practice, only header files (.h) should be included using #include. These header files tell the compiler what exists using declarations, without duplicating code.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,2,'2025-06-27_09-16-09','Using #include on a *.c file is a bad idea because it skips the proper compilation and linking process. Normally in C, each .c file gets compiled into its own .o file (object file), and then the linker links all the object files together. This avoids any duplication of code and ensures that each function and variable is only compiled once.\nIncluding a .c file in another .c file is a bad idea, because this is essentially copy-pasting one .c file into another during compilation. What happens then is that you have the same functions and variables defined multiple times (because there is one copy in the original .c file for these functions and variables, and one copy from the #include, as an example). These causes duplicate symbol errors during linking. It is very similar to why guards exist for .h files, where you only want a .c file to include a header file once and don’t want to accidentally re-include the header file.\nYou also lose structure and modularity of the code. If you include a .c file into another .c file, then the c files rely on each other, which is bad as it is hard to manage, test, and compile each file separately.\nLastly, compilers are optimized to handle .c files individually, so including .c files confuses the build system and makes debugging very hard.\nFor good practice, only header files (.h) should be included using #include. These header files tell the compiler what exists using declarations, without duplicating code.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,2,'2025-06-27_11-00-01','Using #include on a *.c file is a bad idea because it skips the proper compilation and linking process. Normally in C, each .c file gets compiled into its own .o file (object file), and then the linker links all the object files together. This avoids any duplication of code and ensures that each function and variable is only compiled once.\nIncluding a .c file in another .c file is a bad idea, because this is essentially copy-pasting one .c file into another during compilation. What happens then is that you have the same functions and variables defined multiple times (because there is one copy in the original .c file for these functions and variables, and one copy from the #include, as an example). These causes duplicate symbol errors during linking. It is very similar to why guards exist for .h files, where you only want a .c file to include a header file once and don’t want to accidentally re-include the header file.\nYou also lose structure and modularity of the code. If you include a .c file into another .c file, then the c files rely on each other, which is bad as it is hard to manage, test, and compile each file separately.\nLastly, compilers are optimized to handle .c files individually, so including .c files confuses the build system and makes debugging very hard.\nFor good practice, only header files (.h) should be included using #include. These header files tell the compiler what exists using declarations, without duplicating code.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,3,'2025-06-27_02-14-35','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread is quite simple. It is as follows:\nFirst ensure you (the user) have created a separate function in main.c that you want your thread to run. This is called the thread function.\nThen in the main() function in main.c, after all the initialization code and before the while loop, call osKernelInitialize() to initialize any global variables and do kernel setup.\nBelow that, call osCreateThread() to create the thread and setup the thread’s stack and contet. Pass in your thread function’s name as an input argument. The osCreateThread() function returns a Boolean stating whether the thread was successfully created, so you may optionally choose to save this to a Boolean variable to use later on.\nThen below that, call the osKernelStart() function to run the thread (this function uses a system call to set the required pointers and run the thread). It is recommended that osKernelStart() only be run if thread creation is successful. So, it is recommended to check, using an if statement and the Boolean variable from step 3, that thread creation was successful before running the thread with osKernelStart().\nFollowing these steps should be all that is required to set up and start your thread within the kernel framework.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,3,'2025-06-27_09-16-09','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread is quite simple. It is as follows:\nFirst ensure you (the user) have created a separate function in main.c that you want your thread to run. This is called the thread function.\nThen in the main() function in main.c, after all the initialization code and before the while loop, call osKernelInitialize() to initialize any global variables and do kernel setup.\nBelow that, call osCreateThread() to create the thread and setup the thread’s stack and contet. Pass in your thread function’s name as an input argument. The osCreateThread() function returns a Boolean stating whether the thread was successfully created, so you may optionally choose to save this to a Boolean variable to use later on.\nThen below that, call the osKernelStart() function to run the thread (this function uses a system call to set the required pointers and run the thread). It is recommended that osKernelStart() only be run if thread creation is successful. So, it is recommended to check, using an if statement and the Boolean variable from step 3, that thread creation was successful before running the thread with osKernelStart().\nFollowing these steps should be all that is required to set up and start your thread within the kernel framework.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,3,'2025-06-27_11-00-01','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread is quite simple. It is as follows:\nFirst ensure you (the user) have created a separate function in main.c that you want your thread to run. This is called the thread function.\nThen in the main() function in main.c, after all the initialization code and before the while loop, call osKernelInitialize() to initialize any global variables and do kernel setup.\nBelow that, call osCreateThread() to create the thread and setup the thread’s stack and contet. Pass in your thread function’s name as an input argument. The osCreateThread() function returns a Boolean stating whether the thread was successfully created, so you may optionally choose to save this to a Boolean variable to use later on.\nThen below that, call the osKernelStart() function to run the thread (this function uses a system call to set the required pointers and run the thread). It is recommended that osKernelStart() only be run if thread creation is successful. So, it is recommended to check, using an if statement and the Boolean variable from step 3, that thread creation was successful before running the thread with osKernelStart().\nFollowing these steps should be all that is required to set up and start your thread within the kernel framework.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,4,'2025-06-27_02-14-35','The stack pool is a set amount (dedicated region) of memory in RAM, set aside exclusively for storing thread stacks. Each thread needs its own private thread stack to keep track of local variables, registers, function calls, and return addresses (especially during context switches).\nIn our case, the stack pool is 0x4000, and our desired thread stack size is 0x400. So, we can carve out a dedicated block of memory (the stack pool), to be able to allocate stacks for new threads as needed. So, if I wanted to create a new thread, all that is required in terms of memory is to create a new stack that takes up the next available 0x400-size block in the stack pool (while ensuring it still fits within the stack pool), and this stack would be specific to the new thread.\nIn a future lab, multi-threaded programs would require multiple thread stacks (one stack for one thread). We would be able to allocate multiple thread stacks (can fit 10 thread stacks each of size 0x400 bytes in the entire stack pool of 0x4000). Thus, we would be able to run multiple threads, and each thread would get its own space for safe execution. This is essential for context switching (switching to, from, and between threads) and being able to save and restore the stack pointer (PSP) specific to each thread.\nSeparate stacks are required to prevent multiple threads accessing and writing to the same data. If one stack were shared between threads, there is a high likeliness of data corruption and crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,4,'2025-06-27_09-16-09','The stack pool is a set amount (dedicated region) of memory in RAM, set aside exclusively for storing thread stacks. Each thread needs its own private thread stack to keep track of local variables, registers, function calls, and return addresses (especially during context switches).\nIn our case, the stack pool is 0x4000, and our desired thread stack size is 0x400. So, we can carve out a dedicated block of memory (the stack pool), to be able to allocate stacks for new threads as needed. So, if I wanted to create a new thread, all that is required in terms of memory is to create a new stack that takes up the next available 0x400-size block in the stack pool (while ensuring it still fits within the stack pool), and this stack would be specific to the new thread.\nIn a future lab, multi-threaded programs would require multiple thread stacks (one stack for one thread). We would be able to allocate multiple thread stacks (can fit 10 thread stacks each of size 0x400 bytes in the entire stack pool of 0x4000). Thus, we would be able to run multiple threads, and each thread would get its own space for safe execution. This is essential for context switching (switching to, from, and between threads) and being able to save and restore the stack pointer (PSP) specific to each thread.\nSeparate stacks are required to prevent multiple threads accessing and writing to the same data. If one stack were shared between threads, there is a high likeliness of data corruption and crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,4,'2025-06-27_11-00-01','The stack pool is a set amount (dedicated region) of memory in RAM, set aside exclusively for storing thread stacks. Each thread needs its own private thread stack to keep track of local variables, registers, function calls, and return addresses (especially during context switches).\nIn our case, the stack pool is 0x4000, and our desired thread stack size is 0x400. So, we can carve out a dedicated block of memory (the stack pool), to be able to allocate stacks for new threads as needed. So, if I wanted to create a new thread, all that is required in terms of memory is to create a new stack that takes up the next available 0x400-size block in the stack pool (while ensuring it still fits within the stack pool), and this stack would be specific to the new thread.\nIn a future lab, multi-threaded programs would require multiple thread stacks (one stack for one thread). We would be able to allocate multiple thread stacks (can fit 10 thread stacks each of size 0x400 bytes in the entire stack pool of 0x4000). Thus, we would be able to run multiple threads, and each thread would get its own space for safe execution. This is essential for context switching (switching to, from, and between threads) and being able to save and restore the stack pointer (PSP) specific to each thread. Separate stacks are required to prevent multiple threads accessing and writing to the same data. If one stack were shared between threads, there is a high likeliness of data corruption and crashes.\nIn addition to thread stacks, the operating system itself also requires a stack, used during interrupts, exceptions, and system-level operations. This OS stack is also allocated from the stack pool and is essential for handling events not tied to a specific thread. Without a dedicated OS stack, the system would lack a reliable memory area for internal operations, compromising stability and functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,5,'2025-06-27_02-14-35','In an operating system, there are different modes of execution with certain privileges and access rights. “User” mode is for running applications and has limited rights to access system resources, while “Kernel” mode is a much more privileged mode reserved for the operating system’s core functionalities and has unrestricted access to hardware and system resources. This is crucial for system stability and security, as it prevents users from maliciously or accidentally changing critical system components and causing crashes.\nIn our case, the user code is contained in main.c, where high level operations are available such as calling public kernel functions or writing thread functions. The kernel-side code is contained within kernel.c, where low-level and privileged functions are defined such as allocating memory, managing thread contexts, and setting stack pointers and register values. The distinction between these 2 files is that users would use main.c for most purposes and not be allowed to access/change functions in kernel.c, essentially creating a “user” and a “kernel” mode.\nThis distinction is important from the user’s perspective, because a user should not be able to modify the critical components of the kernel intentionally or accidentally, in order to maintain program stability and security. This separation allows the user to interact with the kernel through a well-defined interface (using the functions from kernel.h), without changing kernel behaviour and functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,5,'2025-06-27_09-16-09','In an operating system, there are different modes of execution with certain privileges and access rights. “User” mode is for running applications and has limited rights to access system resources, while “Kernel” mode is a much more privileged mode reserved for the operating system’s core functionalities and has unrestricted access to hardware and system resources. This is crucial for system stability and security, as it prevents users from maliciously or accidentally changing critical system components and causing crashes.\nIn our case, the user code is contained in main.c, where high level operations are available such as calling public kernel functions or writing thread functions. The kernel-side code is contained within kernel.c, where low-level and privileged functions are defined such as allocating memory, managing thread contexts, and setting stack pointers and register values. The distinction between these 2 files is that users would use main.c for most purposes and not be allowed to access/change functions in kernel.c, essentially creating a “user” and a “kernel” mode.\nThis distinction is important from the user’s perspective, because a user should not be able to modify the critical components of the kernel intentionally or accidentally, in order to maintain program stability and security. This separation allows the user to interact with the kernel through a well-defined interface (using the functions from kernel.h), without changing kernel behaviour and functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',3,5,'2025-06-27_11-00-01','In an operating system, there are different modes of execution with certain privileges and access rights. “User” mode is for running applications and has limited rights to access system resources, while “Kernel” mode is a much more privileged mode reserved for the operating system’s core functionalities and has unrestricted access to hardware and system resources. This is crucial for system stability and security, as it prevents users from maliciously or accidentally changing critical system components and causing crashes.\nIn our case, the user code is contained in main.c, where high level operations are available such as calling public kernel functions or writing thread functions. The kernel-side code is contained within kernel.c, where low-level and privileged functions are defined such as allocating memory, managing thread contexts, and setting stack pointers and register values. The distinction between these 2 files is that users would use main.c for most purposes and not be allowed to access/change functions in kernel.c, essentially creating a “user” and a “kernel” mode.\nThis distinction is important from the user’s perspective, because a user should not be able to modify the critical components of the kernel intentionally or accidentally, in order to maintain program stability and security. This separation allows the user to interact with the kernel through a well-defined interface (using the functions from kernel.h), without changing kernel behaviour and functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',4,1,'2025-06-29_17-12-07','Round-robin scheduling can work very well using a queue (a circular queue in particular) instead of just an array. In this method, each thread is represented by a Thread Control Block (TCB) and stored in the queue. The scheduler dequeues the thread from the front of the queue and runs it. If the thread does not finish within a fixed time slice (quantum) or voluntarily yields (which is the case in our lab), then the thread is moved (enqueued) to the back of the queue. The scheduler dequeues the next thread and the cycle continues.\nThis method aligns well with round-robin scheduling as it requires FIFO operations (much like a queue). The queue ensures that every thread gets an equal opportunity to run, and the algorithm overall (and for selecting the next thread) is clean and efficient. Most queue and dequeue operations are O(1), making this method suitable for real-time systems.\nIn our lab however, we implemented an array and an index “currentThread” to simulate round-robin scheduling. While this works, it requires more management and bookkeeping (such as tracking the index and wrap-around) and does not have the dynamic flexibility of queue. One of the biggest benefits of the queue is that memory is not wasted if thread slots are not used up, while in an array memory is pre-allocated and used up even if thread slots are empty. Arrays can also be a hassle if threads are created and destroyed frequently.\nOverall, a queue provides a more elegant and scalable solution.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',4,1,'2025-06-29_18-01-09','Round-robin scheduling can work very well using a queue (a circular queue in particular) instead of just an array. In this method, each thread is represented by a Thread Control Block (TCB) and stored in the queue. The scheduler dequeues the thread from the front of the queue and runs it. If the thread does not finish within a fixed time slice (quantum) or voluntarily yields (which is the case in our lab), then the thread is moved (enqueued) to the back of the queue. The scheduler dequeues the next thread and the cycle continues.\nThis method aligns well with round-robin scheduling as it requires FIFO operations (much like a queue). The queue ensures that every thread gets an equal opportunity to run, and the algorithm overall (and for selecting the next thread) is clean and efficient. Most queue and dequeue operations are O(1), making this method suitable for real-time systems.\nIn our lab however, we implemented an array and an index “currentThread” to simulate round-robin scheduling. While this works, it requires more management and bookkeeping (such as tracking the index and wrap-around) and does not have the dynamic flexibility of queue. One of the biggest benefits of the queue is that memory is not wasted if thread slots are not used up, while in an array memory is pre-allocated and used up even if thread slots are empty. Arrays can also be a hassle if threads are created and destroyed frequently.\nOverall, a queue provides a more elegant and scalable solution.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',4,2,'2025-06-29_17-12-07','When a thread calls osYield, a PendSV interrupt is triggered and the PendSV_Handler function is responsible for performing a context switch between threads. The handler first saves the current thread’s CPU state (pushing the callee-saved registers R4-R11 onto the thread’s stack). The current value of the PSP now points to the saved state, and is stored in the thread’s TCB. Then the PSP is updated to point to the new thread’s stack and the CPU restores registers R4-R11 from the new stack. When the handler exits, the next thread resumes execution as if it had never stopped, completing the context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',4,2,'2025-06-29_18-01-09','When a thread calls osYield, a PendSV interrupt is triggered and the PendSV_Handler function is responsible for performing a context switch between threads. First, the handler reads the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to access the current thread’s stack and saves the current thread’s CPU state by pushing the callee-saved registers R4-R11 onto the thread’s stack. The current value of the PSP now points to the saved state, and is stored in the thread’s TCB to preserve its context. Next, the osSched() function is called to determine which thread should run next. Once the next thread is selected, its saved PSP is loaded and the CPU restores registers R4-R11 from the new stack. When the handler exits, the next thread resumes execution as if it had never stopped, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',4,3,'2025-06-29_17-12-07','If we were to change the scheduler so that it always chooses the task with highest priority, then there is high possibility of lower priority tasks never getting the chance to run. This is what is known as starvation, where some threads are permanently ignored and not able to ever run even if they are important (but just have less priority than other higher priority tasks), which is why this is a bad idea. In round-robin scheduling however, every thread is guaranteed to get a fair and equal share of CPU time. By introducing strict priority-based scheduling, we lose that fairness and risk creating a potentially unstable system where very important tasks might never be able to run.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',4,3,'2025-06-29_18-01-09','If we were to change the scheduler so that it always chooses the task with highest priority, then only that one (highest priority) task will ever run – all other tasks will be permanently ignored, regardless of their importance. This effectively turns the system into a single-task system, where there is complete monopolization by one task, defeating the entire purpose of multitasking. Lower priority would be completely blocked from executing, leading to starvation (the most critical consequence).\nIn round-robin scheduling, every thread is guaranteed to get a fair and equal share of CPU time. By introducing this priority-based scheduling, we lose that fairness. It causes the system to become unresponsive to anything except the highest-priority task, hence why it is a bad idea and poor design choice.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',4,4,'2025-06-29_17-12-07','Calling C functions like osSched from within assembly handlers offers many benefits. C allows for a higher level of abstraction than assembly, making it easier to write, debug, and maintain code. Complex logic such as thread scheduling or handling system calls is much easier and readable to do in C. C also allows for better code reuse and portability. It also separates code cleanly, keeping the low-level context-switching code in assembly, while high-level decisions and logic are handled in C functions. It balances low-level control with high-level manageability, and allows for both performance and clarity.\nThough, using C functions does have downsides as well, because calling C from assembly introduces overhead. Registers must be saved/restored, and stack alignment must be correct. There is also the risk of small bugs if the interface between C and assembly is not proper. Writing everything in assembly would allow for greater performance, tighter control, and maybe even more optimized code. However, it would be much harder to develop, read, and maintain.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',4,4,'2025-06-29_18-01-09','Calling C functions like osSched from within assembly handlers offers many benefits. C allows for a higher level of abstraction than assembly, making it easier to write, debug, and maintain code. Complex logic such as thread scheduling or handling system calls is much easier and readable to do in C. C also allows for better code reuse and portability. It also separates code cleanly, keeping the low-level context-switching code in assembly, while high-level decisions and logic are handled in C functions. It balances low-level control with high-level manageability, and allows for both performance and clarity.\nThough, using C functions does have downsides as well, because calling C from assembly introduces overhead. Registers must be saved/restored, and stack alignment must be correct. There is also the risk of small bugs if the interface between C and assembly is not proper. Writing everything in assembly would allow for greater performance, tighter control, and maybe even more optimized code. However, it would be much harder to develop, read, and maintain.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',4,5,'2025-06-29_17-12-07','Yes, the OS will still work with only a single thread created. When osKernelStart() is called, the system begins executing that single thread (in the case print_continously). After each print, osYield() is called triggering a context switch via the PendSV interrupt.\nSince there are no other threads however, the scheduler (osSched) will end up switching back to the same thread, resulting in the thread resuming execution. This means that it will simply continue its infinite loop of printing and yielding, appearing to the user as if the thread is just continuously printing.\nAlthough the context switch still actually occurs, it has no actual effect since there is no other thread to switch to. The OS behaves as expected and continuously runs the only thread. Though, there is extra and unnecessary overhead coming from the saving and restoring of the same thread’s context.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',4,5,'2025-06-29_18-01-09','Yes, the OS will still work with only a single thread created. When osKernelStart() is called, the system begins executing that single thread (in the case print_continously). After each print, osYield() is called triggering a context switch via the PendSV interrupt.\nSince there are no other threads however, the scheduler (osSched) will end up switching back to the same thread, resulting in the thread resuming execution. This means that it will simply continue its infinite loop of printing and yielding, appearing to the user as if the thread is just continuously printing.\nAlthough the context switch still actually occurs, it has no actual effect since there is no other thread to switch to. The OS behaves as expected and continuously runs the only thread. Though, there is extra and unnecessary overhead coming from the saving and restoring of the same thread’s context.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',5,1,'2025-07-22_10-51-42','When passing an argument into a thread, the user must first create the argument they want to pass in. If they want a single argument, they can pass in a pointer to that, or if they want multiple arguments, then it is recommended to create a struct to hold that information and then pass in pointer to the struct. Either way, the user uses osCreateThread or osCreateThreadWithDeadline and passes in the thread function, and a pointer to the argument. The user must ensure the data remains valid while the threads are running. Then the only other thing the user has to do is to retrieve the arguments in the thread function by dereferencing the arguments. The user can now do whatever is needed inside the thread function with those input arguments.\nThe OS has to initialize the thread’s stack and places the argument’s address into the R0 registor slot (based on ARM ABI), along with setting other needed registers like PC. A thread then gets created with the required info (such as stack pointer, timeslice, etc). This stack pointer is important, as it points to the stack which holds all the “registors” for that thread, including R0. PendSV handler preserves R0 during context switches, ensuring R0 remains accessible, thus allowing the thread function to access input arguments. Note that in our function, the input parameter is basically attempting to get the information in R0 (as per ARM ABI).\nThis all works because ARM ABI uses R0, using void* allows for any input argument type (as long as it’s a pointer to something), and because of the while() loop in main(), it ensures the argument data persists.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',5,1,'2025-07-22_11-43-10','When passing an argument into a thread, the user must first create the argument they want to pass in. If they want a single argument, they can pass in a pointer to that, or if they want multiple arguments, then it is recommended to create a struct to hold that information and then pass in pointer to the struct. Either way, the user uses osCreateThread or osCreateThreadWithDeadline and passes in the thread function, and a pointer to the argument. The user must ensure the data remains valid while the threads are running. Then the only other thing the user has to do is to retrieve the arguments in the thread function by dereferencing the arguments. The user can now do whatever is needed inside the thread function with those input arguments.\nThe OS has to initialize the thread’s stack and places the argument’s address into the R0 registor slot (based on ARM ABI), along with setting other needed registers like PC. A thread then gets created with the required info (such as stack pointer, timeslice, etc). This stack pointer is important, as it points to the stack which holds all the “registors” for that thread, including R0. PendSV handler preserves R0 during context switches, ensuring R0 remains accessible, thus allowing the thread function to access input arguments. Note that in our function, the input parameter is basically attempting to get the information in R0 (as per ARM ABI).\nThis all works because ARM ABI uses R0, using void* allows for any input argument type (as long as it’s a pointer to something), and because of the while() loop in main(), it ensures the argument data persists.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',5,2,'2025-07-22_10-51-42','You cannot call osYield in any of our context-switching functions because these functions operate in the Cortex-M4’s handler mode and use the MSP for exception handling (privileged). The osYield function, however, is made for threads in “thread mode” that uses the context-switching functions. osYield itself is not designed for context-switching, it is designed to allow threads to voluntarily give up control and context switch using an SVC.\nIf one were to invoke osYield within these context-switching functions, then it could cause nested exceptions, stack corruption, recursive interrupts, or even system crashes. It basically messes up the whole system because your are calling osYield, from inside a function that osYield calls. It discrupts PendSV_Handler and SysTick_Handler. And more This is bad as we do not want system crashes or nested exceptions, it is bad design and unpredictable.\n',0,'s25'),('a2agraha',5,2,'2025-07-22_11-43-10','You cannot call osYield in any of our context-switching functions because these functions operate in the Cortex-M4’s handler mode and use the MSP for exception handling (privileged). The osYield function, however, is made for threads in “thread mode” that uses the context-switching functions. osYield itself is not designed for context-switching, it is designed to allow threads to voluntarily give up control and context switch using an SVC.\nIf one were to invoke osYield within these context-switching functions, then it could cause nested exceptions, stack corruption, recursive interrupts, or even system crashes. It basically messes up the whole system because you are calling osYield, from inside a function that osYield calls. It disrupts PendSV_Handler and SysTick_Handler and more. This is bad as we do not want system crashes or nested exceptions, it is bad design and unpredictable.\nIn more detailed terms: The osYield function triggers a context switch by executing a SVC instruction, creating a software interrupt that invokes SVC_Handler in handler mode. Calling it from PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler is invalid because these already run in Handler mode using the MSP. The Cortex-M4 handles nested exceptions based on priority, but an SVC call within these handlers can cause recursive exception handling or stack corruption if the MSP overflows, negatively affecting the OS’s context-switching process. Stack corruption is also critical, as it may overwrite the thread’s registors like PC, leading to unpredictable results and scheduling, and bad thread execution, memory access violations, or crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',5,3,'2025-07-22_10-51-42','If we set the fundamental OS clock to be 0.1ms instead of 1ms, this would change the SysTick’s timer interrupt frequency and thus change our multitasking system’s scheduling, pre-empting, and responsiveness.\nThe benefits: We would get a faster clock speed as the OS will now respond to events with a speed of at least 0.1ms (faster than 1ms). We also get a higher clock resolution (as the time intervals are very low). This means that the OS will be able to respond to many events much faster and threads would be able to be pre-empted and switch every 0.1ms, improving responsiveness for time-critical tasks. This would also support shorter timeslices if needed, allowing for more finer-grained scheduling.\nHowever, (downsides), it is possible that the clock cycle is too fast and affect the ability for actual meaningful work to be completed within each 0.1ms tick, especially if threads or HAL functions need more processing time. It could potentially cause timing conflicts or missed deadlines. Also, the CPU overhead significantly increases due to more frequent context switching. This would increase power consumption and degrade performance on the Cortex-M4.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',5,3,'2025-07-22_11-43-10','If we set the fundamental OS clock to be 0.1ms instead of 1ms, this would change the SysTick’s timer interrupt frequency and thus change our multitasking system’s scheduling, pre-empting, and responsiveness.\nThe benefits: We would get a faster clock speed as the OS will now respond to events with a speed of at least 0.1ms (faster than 1ms). We also get a higher clock resolution (as the time intervals are very low). This means that the OS will be able to respond to many events much faster and threads would be able to be pre-empted and switch every 0.1ms, improving responsiveness for time-critical tasks. This would also support shorter timeslices if needed, allowing for more finer-grained scheduling.\nHowever, (downsides), it is possible that the clock cycle is too fast and affect the ability for actual meaningful work to be completed within each 0.1ms tick, especially if threads or HAL functions need more processing time. It could potentially cause timing conflicts or missed deadlines. Also, the CPU overhead significantly increases due to more frequent context switching. This would increase power consumption and degrade performance on the Cortex-M4.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',5,4,'2025-07-22_10-51-42','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking is where each thread voluntarily gives up control (yields) and allows the next thread to run. This gives users control over when the switching occurs, but expects that the threads are well-behaved. In pre-emptive multitasking, however, threads run until their allocated timeslice finishes and the OS has to pre-empt (interrupt) that thread, and allow the next thread to run (in the case of round-robin scheduling). It provides predictable execution without requiring user-intervention.\nIn terms of the OS, for co-operative multitasking, the OS simply has to handle context-switches (through PendSV_Handler) to switch between threads and not much else, as the thread gives up control (yields) on its own. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS has to handle context-switches and has to pre-empt tasks that have finished their time slice but are still trying to run. So the OS also has to decrement each thread’s runtime every tick and reset the thread’s runtime as needed. The OS here provides fair scheduling if threads do not voluntarily yield.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',5,4,'2025-07-22_11-43-10','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking is where each thread voluntarily gives up control (yields) and allows the next thread to run. This gives users control over when the switching occurs, but expects that the threads are well-behaved. In pre-emptive multitasking, however, threads run until their allocated timeslice finishes and the OS has to pre-empt (interrupt) that thread, and allow the next thread to run (in the case of round-robin scheduling). It provides predictable execution without requiring user-intervention.\nIn terms of the OS, for co-operative multitasking, the OS simply has to handle context-switches (through PendSV_Handler) to switch between threads and not much else, as the thread gives up control (yields) on its own. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS has to handle context-switches and has to pre-empt tasks that have finished their time slice but are still trying to run. So the OS also has to decrement each thread’s runtime every tick and reset the thread’s runtime as needed. The OS here provides fair scheduling if threads do not voluntarily yield.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',5,5,'2025-07-22_10-51-42','It is possible to mix yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching together because the OS uses a unified context-switching mechanism via the PendSV_Handler. The PendSV_Handler can be triggered by the osYield call (where the thread voluntarily gives up control) or a pre-emptive call (where thread’s timeslice finishes based on SysTick’s timer), allowing both yielding and pre-emptive threads to exist in the same scheduling.\nIn our lab, we created 2 threads, where 1 yields immediately after updating a global variable, causing PendSV_Handler to save the threads state and switch to the other thread. The other thread print’s out the global variable repeatedly until it’s timeslice finishes (reaches 0) and the OS pre-empts it, where the SysTick_Handler resets the runtime to the timeslice and triggers a context switch again using PendSV_Handler, allowing the first thread to run again.\nThe OS plays a big role in ensuring this smoothly works, maintaining each thread’s state in the thread’s stack, allowing for smooth context-switching, regardless of whether the thread yielded or was pre-empted.\n',1,'s25'),('a2agraha',5,5,'2025-07-22_11-43-10','It is possible to mix yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching together because the OS uses a unified context-switching mechanism via the PendSV_Handler. The PendSV_Handler can be triggered by the osYield call (where the thread voluntarily gives up control) or a pre-emptive call (where thread’s timeslice finishes based on SysTick’s timer), allowing both yielding and pre-emptive threads to exist in the same scheduling.\nIn our lab, we created 2 threads, where 1 yields immediately after updating a global variable, causing PendSV_Handler to save the threads state and switch to the other thread. The other thread print’s out the global variable repeatedly until it’s timeslice finishes (reaches 0) and the OS pre-empts it, where the SysTick_Handler resets the runtime to the timeslice and triggers a context switch again using PendSV_Handler, allowing the first thread to run again.\nThe OS plays a big role in ensuring this smoothly works, maintaining each thread’s state in the thread’s stack, allowing for smooth context-switching, regardless of whether the thread yielded or was pre-empted.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,1,'2025-06-24_10-38-52','The HAL or Hardware Abstraction Layer is a set of functions provided by STMicroelectronics that makes it convenient to interact with a microcontroller. Instead of writing low-level code to control registers, we use HAL functions to control peripherals which improves portability and improves development time.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,1,'2025-06-24_10-53-03','The HAL or Hardware Abstraction Layer is a set of functions provided by STMicroelectronics that makes it convenient to interact with a microcontroller. Instead of writing low-level code to control registers, we use HAL functions to control peripherals which improves portability and improves development time.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,1,'2025-06-24_11-03-48','The HAL or Hardware Abstraction Layer is a set of functions provided by STMicroelectronics that makes it convenient to interact with a microcontroller. Instead of writing low-level code to control registers, we use HAL functions to control peripherals which improves portability and improves development time.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,2,'2025-06-24_10-38-52','The __io_putchar function redirects the C output from printf to the UART, a standard hardware interface. Printf passes each character to __io_putchar which shares it with UART using HAL_UART_TRANSMIT.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,2,'2025-06-24_10-53-03','The __io_putchar function is required in embedded systems because the printf function does not know by default where to send the output. It usually expects an output stream, which does not exist in microcontrollers. Implement this function helps us define how and where each character from printf should be sent (e.g. UART). This allows printf to be used in embedded systems by routing output to hardware-specific interfaces. Without __io_putchar there would be no output from printf, since there’s no default output in most embedded environments.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,2,'2025-06-24_11-03-48','The __io_putchar function is required in embedded systems because the printf function does not know by default where to send the output. It usually expects an output stream, which does not exist in microcontrollers. Implement this function helps us define how and where each character from printf should be sent (e.g. UART). This allows printf to be used in embedded systems by routing output to hardware-specific interfaces. Without __io_putchar there would be no output from printf, since there’s no default output in most embedded environments.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,3,'2025-06-24_10-38-52','The debugger in STM32CubeIDE allows us to access register values directly, which is crucial to inspect values like Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and Process Stack Pointer (PSP) that are stored in CPU registers. Moreover, these values are hard to access using printf.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,3,'2025-06-24_10-53-03','The debugger in STM32CubeIDE allows us to access register values directly, which is crucial to inspect values like Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and Process Stack Pointer (PSP) that are stored in CPU registers. Moreover, these values are hard to access using printf.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,3,'2025-06-24_11-03-48','The debugger in STM32CubeIDE allows us to access register values directly, which is crucial to inspect values like Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and Process Stack Pointer (PSP) that are stored in CPU registers. Moreover, these values are hard to access using printf.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,4,'2025-06-24_10-38-52','We used the initial MSP value to get PSP’s values by subtracting 0x400 from it. As a result a space for a stack was created which didn’t overlap with the current stack or other memory. This allows us to allocate stack memory lower in memory, accurately and without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,4,'2025-06-24_10-53-03','We used the initial MSP value to get PSP’s values by subtracting 0x400 from it. As a result a space for a stack was created which didn’t overlap with the current stack or other memory. This allows us to allocate stack memory lower in memory, accurately and without conflict.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,4,'2025-06-24_11-03-48','The stack allocation method works by creating a separate stack region for the Process Stack Pointer below the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) in memory. Starting from the initial value of MSP_INIT_VAL, a fixed offset is subtracted to define the PSP’s location. This offset determines the size of the PSP stack and can be adjusted based on the needs of the thread or the stack. Subtracting also ensures the PSP points to a lower address, which is important because stacks grown downward. By placing the pPS below the MSP, we avoid stack collisions or overlap. Knowing the exact starting point of the MSP is essential to calculate where the PSP region can begin. With no reference point, we might allocate overlapping or invalid memory regions.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,5,'2025-06-24_10-38-52','An array would be required to hold all the stacks, and then we would assign each thread’s stack pointer to the top of the array. After which we would need to track the stack’s base address and ensure that there is no overlap.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,5,'2025-06-24_10-53-03','To allocate multiple thread stacks using the same method, we define a known base address (MSP_INIT_VAL) and create multiple stack regions using fixed offsets. If the number of threads and stack size is known, the following formula can be used: STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL – j * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This allows each thread’s stack to be placed in a different region in memory by subtracting an offset from the base stack pointer. This prevents any overlap and makes sure that the sizes are aligned to 8 bytes for Cortex-M compatibility and that the stack remains within RAM bounds.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',1,5,'2025-06-24_11-03-48','To allocate multiple thread stacks using the same method, we define a known base address (MSP_INIT_VAL) and create multiple stack regions using fixed offsets. If the number of threads and stack size is known, the following formula can be used: STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL – j * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This allows each thread’s stack to be placed in a different region in memory by subtracting an offset from the base stack pointer. This prevents any overlap and makes sure that the sizes are aligned to 8 bytes for Cortex-M compatibility and that the stack remains within RAM bounds.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,1,'2025-06-24_16-14-50','In this lab, updating the PC to point to the print_continuously function caused the processor to begin executing that function’s code directly. We first placed the address of the function into register R0, and then used a special instruction to load that value into the program counter. Since the PC controls which instruction the processor runs next, this effectively handed control over to the print_continuously routine. Unlike a typical function call in C, this was done manually by modifying the flow of execution at the assembly level. Because the function runs an endless loop, the processor kept repeating its instructions without returning to the original context.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,1,'2025-06-25_16-15-54','In this lab, updating the PC to point to the print_continuously function caused the processor to begin executing that function’s code directly. We first placed the address of the function into register R0, and then used a special instruction to load that value into the program counter. Since the PC controls which instruction the processor runs next, this effectively handed control over to the print_continuously routine. Unlike a typical function call in C, this was done manually by modifying the flow of execution at the assembly level. Because the function runs an endless loop, the processor kept repeating its instructions without returning to the original context.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,1,'2025-06-25_16-39-26','In this lab, updating the PC to point to the print_continuously function caused the processor to begin executing that function’s code directly. We first placed the address of the function into register R0, and then used a special instruction to load that value into the program counter. Since the PC controls which instruction the processor runs next, this effectively handed control over to the print_continuously routine. Unlike a typical function call in C, this was done manually by modifying the flow of execution at the assembly level. Because the function runs an endless loop, the processor kept repeating its instructions without returning to the original context.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,1,'2025-06-25_21-30-58','By copying the address of print_continuously into R0 and then executing the single-instruction stub MOV PC, R0, we overwrote the Program Counter with that address. The CPU therefore began fetching its next instruction from the first byte of print_continuously instead of from the caller, exactly as if we had jumped there with a low-level “goto.” Because that routine is an infinite loop that keeps calling printf, the core stayed inside it forever and never returned to the original context.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,1,'2025-06-26_10-22-16','By copying the address of print_continuously into R0 and then executing MOV PC, R0, we overwrote PC with that address. As a result, the CPU began fetching its next instruction from the start of print_continuously. That is equivalent to an unconditional jump, not a normal C function call: the call stack is not updated (no return address is pushed, no LR bookkeeping), and there is no automatic path back to the caller. Because print_continuously contains an infinite loop that repeatedly calls printf, the processor stayed inside that routine forever and never returned to the code that performed the jump.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,2,'2025-06-24_16-14-50','A system call is a special function that allows the user to request an action from the OS. The system call switches the CPU from user mode to kernel mode through a trap, which is a software interrupt, to execute privileged operations without any issues caused. An example would be actions such as writing directly to disk, a system call might be used to execute this task more safely. The reason for this is to provide safe access to hardware and system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,2,'2025-06-25_16-15-54','A system call is a special function that allows the user to request an action from the OS. The system call switches the CPU from user mode to kernel mode through a trap, which is a software interrupt, to execute privileged operations without any issues caused. An example would be actions such as writing directly to disk, a system call might be used to execute this task more safely. The reason for this is to provide safe access to hardware and system resources.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,2,'2025-06-25_16-39-26','A system call is a special function that allows the user to request an action from the OS. The system call switches the CPU from user mode to kernel mode through a trap, which is a software interrupt, to execute privileged operations without any issues caused. An example would be actions such as writing directly to disk, a system call might be used to execute this task more safely. The reason for this is to provide safe access to hardware and system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,2,'2025-06-25_21-30-58','A system call is a controlled gateway from application-level C code into privileged kernel code. In this lab we triggered it with the Cortex-M SVC instruction, which encodes an 8-bit service number. When the CPU decodes SVC, it switches into Handler mode, pushes the caller’s context, and vectors to SVC_Handler; that minimal assembly stub quickly hands control to the C helper SVC_Handler_Main, which decodes the service number and performs the requested privileged operation (such as printing) before returning to the caller.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,2,'2025-06-26_10-22-16','A system call is a controlled way for users to request a service that can only be performed in privileged kernel mode from unprivileged application code. An example could be touching hardware registers, accessing memory-mapped I/O, or allocating protected resources. On a Cortex-M, the gateway is the SVC instruction: executing it immediately switches the core from Thread mode (using the PSP) to Handler mode (using the MSP) and vectors it to SVC_Handler. Since the kernel runs with higher privilege, it can safely perform the requested operation, apply access checks and then restore the user context before returning. This hardware-enforced transition prevents malicious or buggy user code from directly manipulating devices, corrupting memory or crashing the system. Only vetted kernel routines can do that work.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,3,'2025-06-24_16-14-50','Setting up a thread’s stack involves setting the register and memory values as if the CPU already had the thread running before it begins. The values for PC, xPSR, and placeholders for R0 to R12 are manually pushed onto the stack. This stack was assigned to PSP and the CPU popped the values into the registers to begin execution. The register value needs to be stored on stack to allow the system to save and restore a thread state during context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,3,'2025-06-25_16-15-54','When setting up a thread’s stack means prepopulating the memory with values that the CPU expects to already have when starting a thread, which is mimicking the state after a function call. First, manually allocate a block of memory for the thread’s stack and push register values in the exact order that is expected (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0). This order is important because the CPU performs a pop operation based on this sequence during exception return. The PSP is then initialized to point to the top. Storing registers on the stack allows context switching, as the thread can later resume execution with the exact CPU state it had before being paused.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,3,'2025-06-25_16-39-26','When setting up a thread’s stack means prepopulating the memory with values that the CPU expects to already have when starting a thread, which is mimicking the state after a function call. First, manually allocate a block of memory for the thread’s stack and push register values in the exact order that is expected (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0). This order is important because the CPU performs a pop operation based on this sequence during exception return. The PSP is then initialized to point to the top. Storing registers on the stack allows context switching, as the thread can later resume execution with the exact CPU state it had before being paused.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,3,'2025-06-25_21-30-58','Before the first thread could run, we had to fabricate a fake stack frame that made the processor believe the thread had been pre-empted earlier. Starting from a fresh memory block, we decremented the pointer (because ARM stacks grow downward) and pushed, in exact order, xPSR, the start address of print_continuously (so it lands in PC), LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11-R4—filling the general-purpose slots with a recognizable value like 0xA for debugging. “Setting up a thread’s stack” therefore means pre-loading the stack with the register image that the CPU’s exception return sequence expects to pop; when the handler later switches to this stack, the hardware pops those words straight into the real registers, recreating the thread’s full context in a single step.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,3,'2025-06-26_10-22-16','To set up the threads stack we derived a fresh Process-stack pointer (PSP) by subtracting a fixed offset of 0x400 bytes from the original MSP value we saved at reset. This gives us a chunk of RAM that will belong only to the new thread. Since ARM stacks grow downward, we then pre-decremented that pointer and wrote eight words in the exact order the exception-return hardware will later pop them: xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, and R0. After that we had to push R11 through R4 to complete the frame, filling each with a recognizable debug value like 0xA. In total, we wrote 15 words as described in the lab manual. At this point stackptr points to the lowest address of that fabricated frame. When the scheduler’s RUN_FIRST_THREADS SVC later executes __set_PSP(stackptr) and performs an exception return, the Cortex-M pops those words directly into the real registers and begins executing at the PC we planted. Storing the registers is therefore critical: it recreates a full CPU context so the thread can start as if it had been created earlier. However, a single wrong value would load incorrect data into the registers and cause a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,4,'2025-06-24_16-14-50','The __set_PSP function lets the CPU know where the thread’s stack is located. Failure to do so would mean that the CPU would try to pop values from an uninitialized memory location. This would result in a hard fault which would indicate that the processor is accessing invalid memory. The Process Stack pointer helps the thread to find the register values for PC, LR, and xPSR.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,4,'2025-06-25_16-15-54','When the __set_PSP is used, it is telling the CPU where the Process Stack Pointer should begin. This is essential because thread execution uses the PSP instead of the default MSP. If a PSP is not assigned correctly, the CPU will attempt to restore register values from an undefined location during context switch or exception return. This can result in a hard fault, since the processor is trying to access or return to an invalid memory address. Switching from MSP to PSP ensures that each thread has an isolated, valid stack region for its execution, preventing memory corruption and undefined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,4,'2025-06-25_16-39-26','When the __set_PSP is used, it is telling the CPU where the Process Stack Pointer should begin. This is essential because thread execution uses the PSP instead of the default MSP. If a PSP is not assigned correctly, the CPU will attempt to restore register values from an undefined location during context switch or exception return. This can result in a hard fault, since the processor is trying to access or return to an invalid memory address. Switching from MSP to PSP ensures that each thread has an isolated, valid stack region for its execution, preventing memory corruption and undefined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,4,'2025-06-25_21-30-58','Our main code still runs on the main stack pointer (MSP), but the thread must use its own Process Stack Pointer (PSP). Inside the RUN_FIRST_THREAD case we therefore moved the pre-calculated stackptr into PSP with __set_PSP. If we skipped that assignment, the exception-return sequence would attempt to pop the thread’s saved registers from whatever garbage address PSP happened to hold (often 0x00000000), so the very first POP would dereference invalid memory and the core would escalate to a HardFault as soon as the handler tried to resume the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,4,'2025-06-26_10-22-16','The scheduler itself executes in Handler mode on the MSP, but user threads must run in Thread mode on their own PSP. If we neglected to call __set_PSP(stackptr), PSP would still contain whatever invalid or uninitialized data it had at reset. When the exception return sequence tried to pop the saved frame for the new thread, it would dereference that junk address, triggering, a MemManage fault that escalates to a hard fault. Setting PSP to the top of the pre-built thread stack not only prevents that fault but also isolates the thread’s private stack from the kernel’s so re-entrant interrupts or nested SVC calls cannot corrupt each other’s data.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,5,'2025-06-24_16-14-50','If the thread function executed only once and then exited instead of looping indefinitely, the processor would look to the Link Register (LR) to find the next instruction to run. However, because we assigned a placeholder value to the LR during setup, it doesn\'t contain a legitimate return address. As a result, the CPU would likely branch to an invalid memory location, leading to a hard fault. This kind of crash occurs because the processor attempts to fetch and execute code from an address that isn’t meant to be accessed. It underscores why it\'s crucial for thread stacks to be correctly configured and why thread functions should not return unexpectedly.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,5,'2025-06-25_16-15-54','If the thread function returns instead of looping forever, the processor will try to pop the return address from the stack using the Link Register (LR). However, in this lab, the LR value was set to a placeholder, and the stack lacks a proper return address, since thread setup didn’t simulate a prior function call. As a result, the CPU may branch to an invalid address, leading to a hard fault. In typical function calls, the return address is stored on the stack automatically. But for manually set up threads, we must ensure either a valid infinite loop or explicitly manage the return behavior to avoid stack misuse and crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,5,'2025-06-25_16-39-26','If the thread function returns instead of looping forever, the processor will try to pop the return address from the stack using the Link Register (LR). However, in this lab, the LR value was set to a placeholder, and the stack lacks a proper return address, since thread setup didn’t simulate a prior function call. As a result, the CPU may branch to an invalid address, leading to a hard fault. In typical function calls, the return address is stored on the stack automatically. But for manually set up threads, we must ensure either a valid infinite loop or explicitly manage the return behavior to avoid stack misuse and crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,5,'2025-06-25_21-30-58','When we built the fake stack frame we placed an arbitrary sentinel (e.g., 0xA) in the LR slot. If print_continuously were not an endless loop and executed a normal BX LR on return, the CPU would try to branch to that sentinel value, which is not a valid code address. That causes an “invalid state” fault that the MPU escalates to a HardFault, so the program would crash immediately after the lone print statement instead of resuming the original context.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',2,5,'2025-06-26_10-22-16','If the thread just printed once and then hit BX LR, the core would jump to whatever dummy value we stuffed into the LR’s slot when we built the stack (e.g. 0xA). That number isn’t a real code address, so the processor suddenly tries to fetch instructions from an invalid spot in memory and immediately throws a hard fault. In short, returning is unsafe because we never gave the thread a legitimate place to go back to; a real RTOS would drop a cleanup stub in LR to avoid that crash.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',3,1,'2025-06-26_16-26-24','Compiling several *.c files into one executable happens in three stages: preprocessing, compilation, and linking. In the preprocessing step, each source file is scanned so that directives such as #include and #define are expanded and comments are stripped, leaving a pure translation unit with all macros and headers inserted already. Nest, the compilation stages translates each cleaned-up .c file into an object file (.o), checking for syntax errors and applying any options along the way. Finally, the linker gathers all of those object files, resolves cross-file references to functions and variables, pulls in required libraries, and emits one fully-linked executable.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',3,2,'2025-06-26_16-26-24','#include pastes the entire contents of the named file into the including source before completion. Including a .c file would compile the included function and global variable definitions twice. It would be compiled once in the original kernel.c translation unit and again in the file that included it. The duplication from this process will cause linker errors, impact modularity, slow down builds, and circular-include issues. The right patter is “put declarations in headers, keep definitions in .c, and let the linker combine them.”\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',3,3,'2025-06-26_16-26-24','Starting a thread involves a brief sequence of high level steps. First, the application calls the kernel initialization routine so that internal data structures and the stack pool are prepared. The developer then writes a lan C function that contains the work to run in the new thread. A thread-creation Api is invoked with the address of that function, prompting the kernel to allocate a slice of stack memory, build an initial register frame, and record the thread’s contest in its table. Finally, the application starts the scheduler; this triggers a supervisor call that switches the processor to the new PSP and jumps to the thread’s entry point. At this point, the thread begins executing concurrently with the rest of the system.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',3,4,'2025-06-26_16-26-24','The stack pool is a single, contiguous block of RAM reserved exclusively for thread stacks. A simple allocator handed out aligned slices so each threads receives its own private stack for local variables, return addresses, and saved registers. By tracking how much of the pool has been consumed, the kernel prevents overlap and can detect exhaustion (NULL return). In later labs the same allocator will be called every time osCreateThread runs, giving each additional thread a safe slice of stack memory and allowing multiple threads to coexist.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',3,5,'2025-06-26_16-26-24','Code in kernel.c and kernel.h combine to form the kernel: low-level functions for stack management, context switching, supervisor call handling, and scheduling. Application logic such as main() and thread functions live in main.c and call the functions from the kernel without touching scheduler internals. This separation shields kernel data from accidental corruption and shortens build time by avoiding unnecessary recompilation of kernel code. It offers developers a clear, stable interface while allowing the kernel to evolve internall without breaking user code, improving safety and modularity.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,1,'2025-07-29_17-27-11','Round-robin maps naturally to a FIFO ready queue of TCB pointers. The kernel always runs the TCB at the front of the ready queue. When that thread calls yield (cooperative lab) the kernel saves its context, dequeues its TCB from the front, and if it is still READY enqueues it at the back. The new front element becomes the next running thread. If a thread blocks (e.g., on I/O/semaphore), its TCB is removed from the ready queue entirely; when it unblocks, the TCB is pushed to the back to preserve fairness. Compared with a plain array plus a modulo index, a ready queue (implemented as a fixed-size ring buffer of TCB pointers) keeps operations strictly O(1) and avoids “holes,” validity flags, or scans after threads block/exit. It also expresses the policy directly (“front runs → goes to back”), which simplifies edge cases and makes extensions straightforward: per-priority multiple ready queues (one FIFO per priority), and clean sleep/wake by moving TCBs between lists. Arrays are fine for very small systems, but a queue yields clearer invariants and fewer bookkeeping bugs without sacrificing determinism.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,1,'2025-07-29_20-40-11','Round-robin maps naturally to a FIFO ready queue of TCB pointers. The kernel always runs the TCB at the front of the ready queue. When that thread calls yield (cooperative lab) the kernel saves its context, dequeues its TCB from the front, and if it is still READY enqueues it at the back. The new front element becomes the next running thread. If a thread blocks (e.g., on I/O/semaphore), its TCB is removed from the ready queue entirely; when it unblocks, the TCB is pushed to the back to preserve fairness. Compared with a plain array plus a modulo index, a ready queue (implemented as a fixed-size ring buffer of TCB pointers) keeps operations strictly O(1) and avoids “holes,” validity flags, or scans after threads block/exit. It also expresses the policy directly (“front runs → goes to back”), which simplifies edge cases and makes extensions straightforward: per-priority multiple ready queues (one FIFO per priority), and clean sleep/wake by moving TCBs between lists. Arrays are fine for very small systems, but a queue yields clearer invariants and fewer bookkeeping bugs without sacrificing determinism.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,1,'2025-07-29_20-49-48','Round-robin maps naturally to a FIFO ready queue of TCB pointers. The kernel always runs the TCB at the front of the ready queue. When that thread calls yield (cooperative lab) the kernel saves its context, dequeues its TCB from the front, and if it is still READY enqueues it at the back. The new front element becomes the next running thread. If a thread blocks (e.g., on I/O/semaphore), its TCB is removed from the ready queue entirely; when it unblocks, the TCB is pushed to the back to preserve fairness. Compared with a plain array plus a modulo index, a ready queue (implemented as a fixed-size ring buffer of TCB pointers) keeps operations strictly O(1) and avoids “holes,” validity flags, or scans after threads block/exit. It also expresses the policy directly (“front runs → goes to back”), which simplifies edge cases and makes extensions straightforward: per-priority multiple ready queues (one FIFO per priority), and clean sleep/wake by moving TCBs between lists. Arrays are fine for very small systems, but a queue yields clearer invariants and fewer bookkeeping bugs without sacrificing determinism. The lab’s existing array + modulo scheme already acts as a circular queue when threads never block or exist, so an explicit FIFO queue only adds head/tail bookkeeping overhead until threads dynamically block or unblock at which point the true ready-queue shines.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,2,'2025-07-29_17-27-11','PendSV is set to the lowest interrupt priority, and it is pended by SVC (via yield) only after SVC completes, so the context switch occurs in a clean, tail end interrupt with no higher-priority ISR preemption of the switch itself. On entry to PendSV, the Cortex-M hardware has already auto-stacked {r0–r3, r12, lr, pc, xPSR} to the current thread’s PSP. The assembly stub then saves the remaining callee-saved registers r4–r11 to that same PSP stack frame and calls the C scheduler: Save current thread: osSched() stores the current thread’s PSP into its TCB. Pick next thread: osSched() selects the next READY TCB (front of the ready queue) and loads its saved PSP into a CPU register for restore. Restore next thread: Back in PendSV, the stub sets LR to an EXC_RETURN value that returns to Thread mode using PSP (0xFFFFFFFD), pops r4–r11 from the next thread’s stack, writes PSP, and BX LR. The exception return causes hardware to unstack {r0–r3, r12, lr, pc, xPSR} from that same PSP, resuming the chosen thread exactly where it left off. This split—tiny, deterministic assembly for save/restore plus C for policy in osSched, keeps the CPU-specific pieces minimal while letting you express scheduling logic clearly.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,2,'2025-07-29_20-40-11','PendSV is set to the lowest interrupt priority, and it is pended by SVC (via yield) only after SVC completes, so the context switch occurs in a clean, tail end interrupt with no higher-priority ISR preemption of the switch itself. On entry to PendSV, the Cortex-M hardware has already auto-stacked {r0–r3, r12, lr, pc, xPSR} to the current thread’s PSP. The assembly stub then saves the remaining callee-saved registers r4–r11 to that same PSP stack frame and calls the C scheduler: Save current thread: osSched() stores the current thread’s PSP into its TCB. Pick next thread: osSched() selects the next READY TCB (front of the ready queue) and loads its saved PSP into a CPU register for restore. Restore next thread: Back in PendSV, the stub sets LR to an EXC_RETURN value that returns to Thread mode using PSP (0xFFFFFFFD), pops r4–r11 from the next thread’s stack, writes PSP, and BX LR. The exception return causes hardware to unstack {r0–r3, r12, lr, pc, xPSR} from that same PSP, resuming the chosen thread exactly where it left off. This split—tiny, deterministic assembly for save/restore plus C for policy in osSched, keeps the CPU-specific pieces minimal while letting you express scheduling logic clearly.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,2,'2025-07-29_20-49-48','PendSV runs at the lowest interrupt priority so the switch happens tail-end with no preemption by other ISRs. On entry, the Cortex-M hardware has already auto-stacked {r0-r3, r12, lr, pc, xPSR} onto the current thread’s PSP. The first step is to locate and save the current stack – read PSP to get the current thread’s stack pointer, then push {r4-r11} onto that PSP frame and store the resulting PSP value into the current TCB. The next step is to select the next thread – call the C scheduler (in this case osSched()), which picks the next ready TCB and returns its saved stack pointer. The next step is to load the next stack by writing that saved value back to PSP. The last step is to restore and return by popping r4 – r11 from the next thread’s stack, set LR to the EXC_RETURN value that returns to Thread mode using PSP, and branch to LR. The exception return causes hardware to unstack {r0-r3, r12, lr, pc, xPSR} from the same PSP< resuming the chosen thread exactly where it last ran. This split keeps CPU work small and the scheduling logic readable.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,3,'2025-07-29_17-27-11','This is bad idea, because a pure pick the highest priority READY task scheduler removes fairness and invites starvation: a CPU-bound or frequently-waking high-priority thread can postpone lower-priority work indefinitely. It also creates priority inversion: a high-priority thread can block on a lock held by a low-priority thread that never gets CPU time because medium-priority work keeps running. If the high-priority thread yields rarely (or spins), everything below it stalls, producing unbounded latency for background tasks (logging, cleanup, timers). If priorities are needed, using per-priority ready queues and do round-robin within each level is much better, and keeps the progress fair, it also requires threads to block instead of spin, and add priority inheritance (or ceiling) for shared resources. Otherwise, stick with basic round-robin for predictable, bounded wait times and simpler reasoning.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,3,'2025-07-29_20-40-11','This is bad idea, because a pure pick the highest priority READY task scheduler removes fairness and invites starvation: a CPU-bound or frequently-waking high-priority thread can postpone lower-priority work indefinitely. It also creates priority inversion: a high-priority thread can block on a lock held by a low-priority thread that never gets CPU time because medium-priority work keeps running. If the high-priority thread yields rarely (or spins), everything below it stalls, producing unbounded latency for background tasks (logging, cleanup, timers). If priorities are needed, using per-priority ready queues and do round-robin within each level is much better, and keeps the progress fair, it also requires threads to block instead of spin, and add priority inheritance (or ceiling) for shared resources. Otherwise, stick with basic round-robin for predictable, bounded wait times and simpler reasoning.\n',0,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,3,'2025-07-29_20-49-48','This is bad idea, because a pure pick the highest priority READY task scheduler removes fairness and invites starvation: a CPU-bound or frequently-waking high-priority thread can postpone lower-priority work indefinitely. It also creates priority inversion: a high-priority thread can block on a lock held by a low-priority thread that never gets CPU time because medium-priority work keeps running. If the high-priority thread yields rarely (or spins), everything below it stalls, producing unbounded latency for background tasks (logging, cleanup, timers). If priorities are needed, using per-priority ready queues and do round-robin within each level is much better, and keeps the progress fair, it also requires threads to block instead of spin, and add priority inheritance (or ceiling) for shared resources. Otherwise, stick with basic round-robin for predictable, bounded wait times and simpler reasoning. With a pure highest-priority scheduler, once any high priority task is ready, no lower-priority task will ever be scheduled, effectively starving all other work. The scheduler’s behavior ensures that the highest-priority task continuously runs because it is always chosen for execution and is never interrupted by lower priority-tasks. As a result, other tasks are starved of CPU time, illustrating that this scheduler leads to a single task monopolizing the processor.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,4,'2025-07-29_17-27-11','Using assembly shells for PendSV_Handler/SVC_Handler that immediately call C (osSched, SVC_HandlerMain) is usually the right trade-off. Benefits include: the critical CPU-specific prologue/epilogue (choose MSP/PSP, save/restore r4–r11, set EXC_RETURN) stays tiny and explicit, while the policy and data-structure work (ready queues, TCB bookkeeping, priority logic) lives in C, which is safer, easier to read, simpler to debug (symbols, breakpoints), and portable across chips and future lab changes. The downsides are limited to overhead and control. Crossing into C from an ISR costs a call/return and requires strict ABI preservation (hence explicit pushes/pops). All-assembly can shave a few cycles and provide absolute control over barriers and ordering, but it’s brittle and hard to extend. For this lab, “assembly wrapper + C core” gives you dependable timing for the context save/restore and maintainable scheduling logic you can evolve.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,4,'2025-07-29_20-40-11','Using assembly shells for PendSV_Handler/SVC_Handler that immediately call C (osSched, SVC_HandlerMain) is usually the right trade-off. Benefits include: the critical CPU-specific prologue/epilogue (choose MSP/PSP, save/restore r4–r11, set EXC_RETURN) stays tiny and explicit, while the policy and data-structure work (ready queues, TCB bookkeeping, priority logic) lives in C, which is safer, easier to read, simpler to debug (symbols, breakpoints), and portable across chips and future lab changes. The downsides are limited to overhead and control. Crossing into C from an ISR costs a call/return and requires strict ABI preservation (hence explicit pushes/pops). All-assembly can shave a few cycles and provide absolute control over barriers and ordering, but it’s brittle and hard to extend. For this lab, “assembly wrapper + C core” gives you dependable timing for the context save/restore and maintainable scheduling logic you can evolve.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,4,'2025-07-29_20-49-48','Using assembly shells for PendSV_Handler/SVC_Handler that immediately call C (osSched, SVC_HandlerMain) is usually the right trade-off. Benefits include: the critical CPU-specific prologue/epilogue (choose MSP/PSP, save/restore r4–r11, set EXC_RETURN) stays tiny and explicit, while the policy and data-structure work (ready queues, TCB bookkeeping, priority logic) lives in C, which is safer, easier to read, simpler to debug (symbols, breakpoints), and portable across chips and future lab changes. The downsides are limited to overhead and control. Crossing into C from an ISR costs a call/return and requires strict ABI preservation (hence explicit pushes/pops). All-assembly can shave a few cycles and provide absolute control over barriers and ordering, but it’s brittle and hard to extend. For this lab, “assembly wrapper + C core” gives you dependable timing for the context save/restore and maintainable scheduling logic you can evolve.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,5,'2025-07-29_17-27-11','The OS still functions, but round-robin degenerates. With a single READY TCB, each yield triggers PendSV; the scheduler “rotates” the ready queue and immediately selects the same TCB. You pay the cost of saving/restoring context, then resume the same thread, so it prints, yields, and repeats. ISRs still run as usual, so the kernel paths are exercised correctly—just without visible concurrency. Two caveats matter: first, if that lone thread ever blocks or exits, the ready queue becomes empty; without an idle threadthe system has nothing to schedule (best case you execute WFI, worst case you hit an error path). Second, have to keep the overhead of context switches with none of the parallel progress benefits. For demos or bring-up this is fine, but a minimal idle task is recommended to keep the kernel well-defined when no user thread is READY.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,5,'2025-07-29_20-40-11','The OS still functions, but round-robin degenerates. With a single READY TCB, each yield triggers PendSV; the scheduler “rotates” the ready queue and immediately selects the same TCB. You pay the cost of saving/restoring context, then resume the same thread, so it prints, yields, and repeats. ISRs still run as usual, so the kernel paths are exercised correctly—just without visible concurrency. Two caveats matter: first, if that lone thread ever blocks or exits, the ready queue becomes empty; without an idle threadthe system has nothing to schedule (best case you execute WFI, worst case you hit an error path). Second, have to keep the overhead of context switches with none of the parallel progress benefits. For demos or bring-up this is fine, but a minimal idle task is recommended to keep the kernel well-defined when no user thread is READY.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',4,5,'2025-07-29_20-49-48','The OS still functions, but round-robin degenerates. With a single READY TCB, each yield triggers PendSV; the scheduler “rotates” the ready queue and immediately selects the same TCB. You pay the cost of saving/restoring context, then resume the same thread, so it prints, yields, and repeats. ISRs still run as usual, so the kernel paths are exercised correctly—just without visible concurrency. Two caveats matter: first, if that lone thread ever blocks or exits, the ready queue becomes empty; without an idle threadthe system has nothing to schedule (best case you execute WFI, worst case you hit an error path). Second, have to keep the overhead of context switches with none of the parallel progress benefits. For demos or bring-up this is fine, but a minimal idle task is recommended to keep the kernel well-defined when no user thread is READY.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',5,1,'2025-07-30_10-12-20','The user declares the thread entry with the lab’s signature void thread_fn(void *arg) and passes a pointer (to a scalar or a struct) when creating the thread; inside the thread, that pointer is cast and dereferenced as needed. The kernel makes this work by how it fabricates the initial exception-return frame on the thread’s PSP: xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0 followed by callee-saved registers. It writes the entry PC to PC, the argument pointer to R0, and a sensible xPSR(Thumb bit set). When the scheduler starts the thread (via PendSV and an EXC_RETURN), the core pops that frame, restoring R0 exactly as defined by the ARM EABI (first parameter in R0). Thus, the entry function observes the user’s pointer in argas if it were called normally. This scheme is general because any payload can be addressed through a single void* (use a struct for multiple fields) and portable because it relies on the architecture’s calling convention.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',5,1,'2025-07-30_17-16-47','The user declares the thread entry with the lab’s signature void thread_fn(void *arg) and passes a pointer (to a scalar or a struct) when creating the thread; inside the thread, that pointer is cast and dereferenced as needed. The kernel makes this work by how it fabricates the initial exception-return frame on the thread’s PSP: xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0 followed by callee-saved registers. It writes the entry PC to PC, the argument pointer to R0, and a sensible xPSR(Thumb bit set). When the scheduler starts the thread (via PendSV and an EXC_RETURN), the core pops that frame, restoring R0 exactly as defined by the ARM EABI (first parameter in R0). Thus, the entry function observes the user’s pointer in argas if it were called normally. This scheme is general because any payload can be addressed through a single void* (use a struct for multiple fields) and portable because it relies on the architecture’s calling convention.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',5,2,'2025-07-30_10-12-20','osYield raises SVC to request a cooperative switch. Calling it from inside SysTick, SVC, or PendSV creates nested exceptions that violate the intended priority ordering: SVC (higher) should pend PendSV (lower) and return; PendSV_Handler alone performs save/choose/restore. Injecting another SVC from a handler can reorder or interleave SVC/PendSV, double-save registers, strand stack frames that have not unwound, and corrupt the PSP/MSP split—common failure modes include unpredictable preemption timing and hard faults. The correct pattern is: SysTick_Handlerdecrements the running thread’s runtime and, on expiry, pends PendSV; cooperative yield uses SVC to pend PendSV; in both cases the actual context switch occurs only in PendSV_Handler after the current handler has cleanly returned.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',5,2,'2025-07-30_17-16-47','osYield raises SVC to request a cooperative switch. Calling it from inside SysTick, SVC, or PendSV creates nested exceptions that violate the intended priority ordering: SVC (higher) should pend PendSV (lower) and return; PendSV_Handler alone performs save/choose/restore. Injecting another SVC from a handler can reorder or interleave SVC/PendSV, double-save registers, strand stack frames that have not unwound, and corrupt the PSP/MSP split—common failure modes include unpredictable preemption timing and hard faults. The correct pattern is: SysTick_Handlerdecrements the running thread’s runtime and, on expiry, pends PendSV; cooperative yield uses SVC to pend PendSV; in both cases the actual context switch occurs only in PendSV_Handler after the current handler has cleanly returned.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',5,3,'2025-07-30_10-12-20','A 0.1 ms tick improves temporal resolution and worst-case reaction time: time slices, delays, and software timers gain 10× finer granularity, and preemption can occur sooner after an event. This benefits short-deadline work and tight periodic tasks. The costs are proportional ISR load and energy: SysTick_Handler now executes 10× more often, stealing cycles from threads and amplifying any fixed work already inside the handler (e.g., HAL tick maintenance needed for printf/delays). All time-based semantics must be retuned (e.g., default quanta, runtime counters), or effective slices shrink by 10×. Finally, the lab’s ecosystem assumes a 1 ms HAL tick; diverging from it risks breaking HAL timing unless the broader stack is reconfigured consistently. Net: higher precision and responsiveness at the expense of CPU overhead, power, and integration complexity.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',5,3,'2025-07-30_17-16-47','A 0.1 ms tick improves temporal resolution and worst-case reaction time: time slices, delays, and software timers gain 10× finer granularity, and preemption can occur sooner after an event. This benefits short-deadline work and tight periodic tasks. The costs are proportional ISR load and energy: SysTick_Handler now executes 10× more often, stealing cycles from threads and amplifying any fixed work already inside the handler (e.g., HAL tick maintenance needed for printf/delays). All time-based semantics must be retuned (e.g., default quanta, runtime counters), or effective slices shrink by 10×. Finally, the lab’s ecosystem assumes a 1 ms HAL tick; diverging from it risks breaking HAL timing unless the broader stack is reconfigured consistently. Net: higher precision and responsiveness at the expense of CPU overhead, power, and integration complexity.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',5,4,'2025-07-30_10-12-20','From the user’s view, cooperative scheduling requires threads to relinquish the CPU explicitly (e.g., “do work → osYield() → repeat”); a non-yielding thread can monopolize execution. Under preemption, explicit yielding is optional because the timer enforces quanta and fairness; latency and responsiveness improve even with ill-behaved code. From the kernel’s view, cooperative switching is minimal: osYield triggers SVC, the SVC handler pends PendSV, and PendSV_Handler performs the context switch. Preemption adds time accounting to each TCB (timeslice and runtime) and logic in SysTick_Handler to decrement runtime and pend PendSV upon expiry, while keeping PendSV_Handler as the single place that saves/restores context. The separation preserves stack discipline and keeps interrupt handlers short and deterministic.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',5,4,'2025-07-30_17-16-47','From the user’s view, cooperative scheduling requires threads to relinquish the CPU explicitly (e.g., “do work → osYield() → repeat”); a non-yielding thread can monopolize execution. Under preemption, explicit yielding is optional because the timer enforces quanta and fairness; latency and responsiveness improve even with ill-behaved code. From the kernel’s view, cooperative switching is minimal: osYield triggers SVC, the SVC handler pends PendSV, and PendSV_Handler performs the context switch. Preemption adds time accounting to each TCB (timeslice and runtime) and logic in SysTick_Handler to decrement runtime and pend PendSV upon expiry, while keeping PendSV_Handler as the single place that saves/restores context. The separation preserves stack discipline and keeps interrupt handlers short and deterministic.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',5,5,'2025-07-30_10-12-20','The triggers differ: a thread’s voluntary yield (via SVC) or a timer-driven slice expiry (via SysTick). Both converge on the same mechanism: SVC or SysTick merely pend PendSV; PendSV_Handler alone saves the outgoing context, selects the next READY TCB, and restores its context. Interoperation hinges on consistent accounting: if a thread yields before its slice is consumed, its runtime is reset to its timeslice so it is not immediately preempted on resume, preserving fair quanta while enabling responsive hand-offs; if it does not yield, the timer forces a switch at expiry. Because the trigger paths are distinct but the switch mechanism is unified (PendSV), cooperative and preemptive behaviors compose without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('a2ajay',5,5,'2025-07-30_17-16-47','The triggers differ: a thread’s voluntary yield (via SVC) or a timer-driven slice expiry (via SysTick). Both converge on the same mechanism: SVC or SysTick merely pend PendSV; PendSV_Handler alone saves the outgoing context, selects the next READY TCB, and restores its context. Interoperation hinges on consistent accounting: if a thread yields before its slice is consumed, its runtime is reset to its timeslice so it is not immediately preempted on resume, preserving fair quanta while enabling responsive hand-offs; if it does not yield, the timer forces a switch at expiry. Because the trigger paths are distinct but the switch mechanism is unified (PendSV), cooperative and preemptive behaviors compose without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',1,1,'2025-06-24_12-24-26','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a software layer that provides standardized function calls to interact with microcontroller hardware like GPIO pins and timers without directly accessing hardware registers (e.g., HAL_GPIO_WritePin()). The HAL streamlines embedded development, enabling developers to write platform-independent code that is portable across different microcontrollers. For example, STM32CubeMX can auto-generate code using the HAL, allowing developers to focus on writing application-level code without needing to manually configure pins or general board setup.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',1,1,'2025-06-24_12-53-27','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a software layer that provides standardized function calls to interact with microcontroller hardware like GPIO pins and timers without directly accessing hardware registers (e.g., HAL_GPIO_WritePin()). The HAL streamlines embedded development, enabling developers to write platform-independent code that is portable across different microcontrollers. For example, STM32CubeMX can auto-generate code using the HAL, allowing developers to focus on writing application-level code without needing to manually configure pins or general board setup.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',1,2,'2025-06-24_12-24-26','The __io_putchar function is simply a wrapper function that defines how a single character is sent to the appropriate I/O interface (e.g. UART2). It is used in embedded development, typically when printf is called, where __io_putchar is internally used once for every character in the string. Since embedded devices usually lack the ability to output directly to a computer terminal, developers define an __io_putchar function to route single characters to the specified I/O channel. In our case, we use the HAL_UART_Transmit function to route individual characters to the UART2 channel (the UART connection between the board and the computer).\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',1,2,'2025-06-24_12-53-27','The __io_putchar function is a wrapper that defines how a single character is sent to the appropriate I/O interface (e.g., UART2). In embedded development, when printf is called, __io_putchar is internally called once for every character in the string. Since embedded devices cannot typically output directly to a computer terminal, developers implement __io_putchar to route characters to the specified I/O channel. In our case, we use the HAL_UART_Transmit function to send characters over the UART2 channel, which connects the board to the computer. This function is important for different hardware projects as it allows developers to modify the __io_putchar function to send characters through different I/O channels, making the code easily adaptable across various platforms. __io_putchar can be customized for varying project requirements, hardware, and other factors, providing developers with great flexibility when using printf.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',1,3,'2025-06-24_12-24-26','In this lab, we used the debugger to identify the stack’s initial value at program startup since the stack\'s value changes as the program runs. Therefore, by adding a breakpoint at the while loop, we can correctly identify the stack’s initial value before the program proceeds and keep track of how the stack’s value varies with each while loop iteration if needed. We can’t use printf as printing in embedded development is generally considered slow and may interfere with real-time behavior. Moreover, printf cannot be used to inspect the stack before the program runs, whereas the debugger can pause execution at any point to examine the system\'s state.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',1,3,'2025-06-24_12-53-27','In this lab, we used the debugger to identify the stack’s initial value at program startup since the stack\'s value changes as the program runs. Therefore, by adding a breakpoint at the while loop, we can correctly identify the stack’s initial value before the program proceeds and keep track of how the stack’s value varies with each while loop iteration if needed. We can’t use printf as printing in embedded development is generally considered slow and may interfere with real-time behavior. Moreover, printf cannot be used to inspect the stack before the program runs, whereas the debugger can pause execution at any point to examine the system\'s state.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',1,4,'2025-06-24_12-24-26','The stack allocation method works using the overarching assumption that the MSP is defined at the top of the “stack of stacks” and has a defined size allocated. As the main stack fills up, it grows downwards, resulting in the pointer\'s address decreasing in value. Therefore, we get the new value of PSP_val by subtracting the main stack size from the MSP_init_val to position the PSP right under the main stack. We needed to know the initial location of MSP for this to work as we are required to position the PSP directly below the main stack. If we didn\'t know the MSP_init_val, we can\'t determine where the PSP lies as we don\'t know how much to shift it by.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',1,4,'2025-06-24_12-53-27','The stack allocation method works using the overarching assumption that the MSP is defined at the top of the “stack of stacks” and has a defined size allocated. As the main stack fills up, it grows downwards, resulting in the pointer\'s address decreasing in value. Therefore, we get the new value of PSP_val by subtracting the main stack size from the MSP_init_val to position the PSP right under the main stack. We needed to know the initial location of MSP for this to work as we are required to position the PSP directly below the main stack. If we didn\'t know the MSP_init_val, we can\'t determine where the PSP lies as we don\'t know how much to shift it by.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',1,5,'2025-06-24_12-24-26','We’d use the formula ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (j-1)*THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This formula states for a thread j, starting at j=1 (consistent with the lab manual), the PSP_val for that thread is calculated to be directly below the previous stack. In code, we would need to convert the singular PSP_val variable into an array to store the PSP values for each thread. Then, we would iterate in a loop, computing and storing each result in the array. This enables each thread to have separate, non-overlapping stack regions.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',1,5,'2025-06-24_12-53-27','We’d use the formula ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (j-1)*THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This formula states for a thread j, starting at j=1 (consistent with the lab manual), the PSP_val for that thread is calculated to be directly below the previous stack. In code, we would need to convert the singular PSP_val variable into an array to store the PSP values for each thread. Then, we would iterate in a loop, computing and storing each result in the array. This enables each thread to have separate, non-overlapping stack regions.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,1,'2025-06-25_23-11-18','In this lab, rather than calling the print continuously function normally, we used as assembly instruction to load its function pointer directly into the Program Counter (PC). The PC is a special register that holds the address of the next instruction to execute, and by setting it to hold the address of the print continuously function, we effectively redirected all current execution to execute the print continuously function. Since the function contains an infinite loop, the processor will remain executing that function indefinitely and will print the string continuously.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',2,1,'2025-06-26_18-27-30','In this lab, rather than using a standard function call to invoke the print_continuously function, we used an assembly instruction to manually load its address into the Program Counter (PC) —a special register that always points to the next instruction to execute. By directly assigning the function’s address to the PC, we performed a low-level function call which causes the processor to immediately jump to the start of the print_continuously function and begin executing it. This approach bypasses the usual call stack mechanism and changes the control flow explicitly.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,1,'2025-06-26_23-41-36','In this lab, rather than using a standard function call to invoke the print_continuously function, we used an assembly instruction to manually load its address into the Program Counter (PC) —a special register that always points to the next instruction to execute. By directly assigning the function’s address to the PC, we performed a low-level function call which causes the processor to immediately jump to the start of the print_continuously function and begin executing it. This approach bypasses the usual call stack mechanism and changes the control flow explicitly.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,2,'2025-06-25_23-11-18','A system call is a special way for user-level programs to access hardware and/or protected resources from the operating system’s kernel. Since user programs don’t have direct access to said resources, they use system call to perform tasks like reading files, accessing devices, etc. The system call creates a controlled transition from user to kernel mode, enabling the operating system to perform requested operations on behalf of the program in a safe and protected manner.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',2,2,'2025-06-26_18-27-30','A system call is a special way for user-level programs to access hardware and/or protected resources from the operating system’s kernel. Since user programs don’t have direct access to said resources, they use system call to perform tasks like reading files, accessing devices, etc. The system call creates a controlled transition from user to kernel mode, enabling the operating system to perform requested function on behalf of the program in a safe and protected manner. In this lab, a system call is essentially a function which is called within the user program in which we send an SVC instruction with a specific integer, where in the SVC_Handler_Main, the OS will “handle” this system call by performing a specific function.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,2,'2025-06-26_23-41-36','A system call is a special way for user-level programs to access hardware and/or protected resources from the operating system’s kernel. Since user programs don’t have direct access to said resources, they use system call to perform tasks like reading files, accessing devices, etc. The system call creates a controlled transition from user to kernel mode, enabling the operating system to perform requested function on behalf of the program in a safe and protected manner. In this lab, a system call is essentially a function which is called within the user program in which we send an SVC instruction with a specific integer, where in the SVC_Handler_Main, the OS will “handle” this system call by performing a specific function.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,3,'2025-06-25_23-11-18','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing memory so that the thread can start or resume execution properly. This includes allocating space for elements like local variables and the thread’s context. The thread’s context consists of register values including R0-R12, LR, PC, and XPSR. These values are stored in a specific order so they can be popped back when the thread runs. To do so, we effectively adjust the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to reserve enough space at the bottom of the stack, to the manually write register values to that space. We store register values on the stack so that the event a context switch occurs, the CPU can save the thread’s execution state, allowing it to seamlessly resume execution.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,3,'2025-06-26_18-27-30','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing memory so that the thread can start or resume execution properly. This includes allocating space for elements like local variables and the thread’s context. The thread’s context consists of register values including R0-R12, LR, PC, and XPSR. These values are stored in a specific order so they can be popped back when the thread runs. To do so, we effectively adjust the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to reserve enough space at the bottom of the stack, to the manually write register values to that space. We store register values on the stack so that the event a context switch occurs, the CPU can save the thread’s execution state, allowing it to seamlessly resume execution.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,3,'2025-06-26_23-41-36','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing memory so that the thread can start or resume execution properly. This includes allocating space for elements like local variables and the thread’s context. The thread’s context consists of register values including R0-R12, LR, PC, and XPSR. These values are stored in a specific order so they can be popped back when the thread runs. To do so, we effectively adjust the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to reserve enough space at the bottom of the stack, to the manually write register values to that space. We store register values on the stack so that the event a context switch occurs, the CPU can save the thread’s execution state, allowing it to seamlessly resume execution.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,4,'2025-06-25_23-11-18','The PSP (Process Stack Pointer) points to the memory location of where the thread’s stack exists. Before running a thread, we will need to switch modes using the __set_PSP function so that the CPU will use the expected memory location required to run threads. If the __set_PSP function wasn’t called, the CPU will try to access invalid memory locations or even dereference a NULL pointer and will result in a hard fault and crash the program. Setting the PSP ensures that the thread has a valid stack to use for local variables, context saving, and return addresses.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,4,'2025-06-26_18-27-30','The PSP (Process Stack Pointer) points to the memory location of where the thread’s stack exists. Before running a thread, we will need to switch modes using the __set_PSP function so that the CPU will use the expected memory location required to run threads. If the __set_PSP function wasn’t called, the CPU will try to access invalid memory locations or even dereference a NULL pointer and will result in a hard fault and crash the program. Setting the PSP ensures that the thread has a valid stack to use for local variables, context saving, and return addresses.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',2,4,'2025-06-26_23-41-36','The PSP (Process Stack Pointer) points to the memory location of where the thread’s stack exists. Before running a thread, we will need to switch modes using the __set_PSP function so that the CPU will use the expected memory location required to run threads. If the __set_PSP function wasn’t called, the CPU will try to access invalid memory locations or even dereference a NULL pointer and will result in a hard fault and crash the program. Setting the PSP ensures that the thread has a valid stack to use for local variables, context saving, and return addresses. It is important to distinguish between the MSP (main stack pointer) and the PSP (process stack pointer) as the MSP is reserved for system-level operations like interrupts and exceptions while the PSP is meant for thread-level execution. When running multiple threads, each thread requires its own private stack, and using the PSP allows each thread to operate in its own memory region without interfering with system’s core operations. Using MSP rather than PSP can result in accessing protected memory, leading to possible hard faults, unpredictable behaviour, etc. The correct stack pointer (PSP) ensures that each thread’s registers, program counter, and return address are saved and restored to the correct memory region during context switches.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,5,'2025-06-25_23-11-18','If the thread function doesn’t loop and rather returns after printing once, the CPU will try to execute a return instruction to the link register (LR). However, since the thread was started manually and not through a normal function call, the LR may not have a valid return address. Therefore, this can lead to undefined behaviour, often resulting in a hard fault. I embedded systems, threads are typically expected to run indefinitely or have proper cleanup upon termination. Without this, a thread function could crash the system!\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',2,5,'2025-06-26_18-27-30','The program will likely crash and result in a hard fault. This is because after the thread is finished executing, the processor will attempt to pop the return address off the thread’s context, located in the thread’s stack. Since we initialized the thread’s context with fake values (i.e. 0xA) the processor will not have a valid return address and will lead to undefined behaviour, often resulting in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',2,5,'2025-06-26_23-41-36','The program will likely crash and result in a hard fault. This is because after the thread is finished executing, the processor will attempt to pop the return address off the thread’s context, located in the thread’s stack. Since we initialized the thread’s context with fake values (i.e. 0xA) the processor will not have a valid return address and will lead to undefined behaviour, often resulting in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,1,'2025-06-26_20-09-34','Each .c file will be turned into a .o file (object file). Then, the linker takes all the .o files and connects them together, resolving the function calls across the files (i.e. function in main uses a function defined in another file). After the linker connects all the related object files, we obtain a single executable.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',3,1,'2025-06-27_00-16-41','When building a program with multiple .c files, the process is typically comprised of three stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. Each . c file is first preprocessed. This step handles all #include and #define directives by copying in header file contents and replacing macros. The output is simply a plain C file with everything expanded. The preprocessed file is then compiled into an object file (.o). This step converts function definitions, variables, and control structures into low-level instructions. Finally, the linker takes all .o files and combines them into a single executable. It resolves all references between files (i.e. if main.c calls a function defined in another .c file) where the linker finds each symbol and connects them. The linker also assigns final memory addresses to code and data, ensuring that everything fits and works together in the executable.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,1,'2025-06-27_15-50-50','When building a program with multiple .c files, the process is typically comprised of three stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. Each . c file is first preprocessed. This step handles all #include and #define directives by copying in header file contents and replacing macros. The output is simply a plain C file with everything expanded. The preprocessed file is then compiled into an object file (.o). This step converts function definitions, variables, and control structures into low-level instructions. Finally, the linker takes all .o files and combines them into a single executable. It resolves all references between files (i.e. if main.c calls a function defined in another .c file) where the linker finds each symbol and connects them. The linker also assigns final memory addresses to code and data, ensuring that everything fits and works together in the executable.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,2,'2025-06-26_20-09-34','#include is basically a copy-paste tool that the preprocessor uses, where all code from the file mentioned in the #include line is essentially pasted at that instant (in this example, kernel.c code will be copy pasted into the *.c file). This will happen before the compiler “sees” the code. When we compile these files, the kernel.c file will get compiled along with the *.c file, which contains fully duplicated code contained within the kernel.c file. This will result in multiple definition errors since both .c files contain copies of the same functions, variables, etc.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,2,'2025-06-27_00-16-41','#include is basically a copy-paste tool that the preprocessor uses, where all code from the file mentioned in the #include line is essentially pasted at that instant (in this example, kernel.c code will be copy pasted into the *.c file). This will happen before the compiler “sees” the code. When we compile these files, the kernel.c file will get compiled along with the *.c file, which contains fully duplicated code contained within the kernel.c file. This will result in multiple definition errors since both .c files contain copies of the same functions, variables, etc.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,2,'2025-06-27_15-50-50','#include is basically a copy-paste tool that the preprocessor uses, where all code from the file mentioned in the #include line is essentially pasted at that instant (in this example, kernel.c code will be copy pasted into the *.c file). This will happen before the compiler “sees” the code. When we compile these files, the kernel.c file will get compiled along with the *.c file, which contains fully duplicated code contained within the kernel.c file. This will result in multiple definition errors since both .c files contain copies of the same functions, variables, etc.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,3,'2025-06-26_20-09-34','The user will typically have made a function for the thread to run.\nThe user will then call a kernel initialization function, where the user will initialize values for the PSP.\nThe user will then call a thread creation function, which will allocate memory for the thread’s stack. This involves obtaining the pointer of the bottom of the last process stack in the entire stack.\nWe will then need to initialize the thread’s stack. This will be done by iterating over fields, as well as populating important register values within the thread’s context. For the user, critical aspects to populate include the PC to run the function and the LR to return in a clean manner.\nAt this point, the thread is ready to begin execution. The user will run a thread start function, which is a simple a wrapper for an SVC system call. The system call will update the PSP to the thread’s stack pointer and begin running the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,3,'2025-06-27_00-16-41','The user will typically have made a function for the thread to run.\nThe user will then call a kernel initialization function, where the user will initialize values for the PSP.\nThe user will then call a thread creation function, which will allocate memory for the thread’s stack. This involves obtaining the pointer of the bottom of the last process stack in the entire stack.\nWe will then need to initialize the thread’s stack. This will be done by iterating over fields, as well as populating important register values within the thread’s context. For the user, critical aspects to populate include the PC to run the function and the LR to return in a clean manner.\nAt this point, the thread is ready to begin execution. The user will run a thread start function, which is a simple a wrapper for an SVC system call. The system call will update the PSP to the thread’s stack pointer and begin running the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,3,'2025-06-27_15-50-50','The user will typically have made a function for the thread to run.\nThe user will then call a kernel initialization function, where the user will initialize values for the PSP.\nThe user will then call a thread creation function, which will allocate memory for the thread’s stack. This involves obtaining the pointer of the bottom of the last process stack in the entire stack.\nWe will then need to initialize the thread’s stack. This will be done by iterating over fields, as well as populating important register values within the thread’s context. For the user, critical aspects to populate include the PC to run the function and the LR to return in a clean manner.\nAt this point, the thread is ready to begin execution. The user will run a thread start function, which is a simple a wrapper for an SVC system call. The system call will update the PSP to the thread’s stack pointer and begin running the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,4,'2025-06-26_20-09-34','The stack pool is a dedicated block of memory reserved specifically for thread stacks, and since each thread in and OS requires its own “stack space” to store elements like thread context (register values) and local variables, the stack pool acts as a big container to hold multiple thread stacks. To support multiple threads, we will need separate stack space for each thread, separate thread context for each thread, and a method of switching between them. In future labs, we can expand on this idea by implementing a stack pool and adding a system for saving and restoring thread context during context switching, enabling us to run an manage multiple threads.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',3,4,'2025-06-27_00-16-41','The stack pool is a dedicated block of memory used to support multiple threads by providing each thread with its own stack space. This space is needed to store local variables, function calls, and storing he thread’s context (registers like PC, LR) during context switching. The pool is divided into fixed-size sections, with each section assigned to one thread. This makes it easy to calculate where each thread’s stack begins based on its index. During context switching, the OS saves the current thread’s context to its stack slice and restores the next thread’s context from it own. This ensures threads operate independently and safely for tasks involving multithreading.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',3,4,'2025-06-27_15-50-50','The stack pool is a block of memory reserved for storing stacks used by both the OS and user threads. Each thread gets a fixed-size portion of this pool to store local variables, function calls, and its context (registers like PC and LR) during context switches. The OS also uses part of this pool—specifically the Main Stack Pointer (MSP)—for system-level tasks like interrupt and exception handling. This dual use allows both the OS and threads to operate from the same memory region while keeping their stacks isolated. During context switching, the OS saves the running thread’s context to its stack and restores the next thread’s context from its own. By managing stack offsets carefully, the system ensures safe memory use, supports multiple threads, and maintains separation between OS and user-level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,5,'2025-06-26_20-09-34','Kernel code is used at the core of an OS. It manages system resources, hardware, and low-level function (i.e. starting threads). It typically has full control over the system and resides in files like kernel.c. User code on the other hand is code written by the application developer (i.e. function of what the thread executes). It typically goes into a main.c file, and calls functions from the kernel, but doesn’t interact with hardware and protected resources directly (runs in a “safe” environment with restricted access). This distinction is important as it protects the system for multiple processes accessing protected resources directly, simplifies the user’s job to use an interface to call kernel-level functions, and makes the system more modular and maintainable.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,5,'2025-06-27_00-16-41','Kernel code is used at the core of an OS. It manages system resources, hardware, and low-level function (i.e. starting threads). It typically has full control over the system and resides in files like kernel.c. User code on the other hand is code written by the application developer (i.e. function of what the thread executes). It typically goes into a main.c file, and calls functions from the kernel, but doesn’t interact with hardware and protected resources directly (runs in a “safe” environment with restricted access). This distinction is important as it protects the system for multiple processes accessing protected resources directly, simplifies the user’s job to use an interface to call kernel-level functions, and makes the system more modular and maintainable.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',3,5,'2025-06-27_15-50-50','Kernel code is used at the core of an OS. It manages system resources, hardware, and low-level function (i.e. starting threads). It typically has full control over the system and resides in files like kernel.c. User code on the other hand is code written by the application developer (i.e. function of what the thread executes). It typically goes into a main.c file, and calls functions from the kernel, but doesn’t interact with hardware and protected resources directly (runs in a “safe” environment with restricted access). This distinction is important as it protects the system for multiple processes accessing protected resources directly, simplifies the user’s job to use an interface to call kernel-level functions, and makes the system more modular and maintainable.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,1,'2025-07-29_10-46-14','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling allows for each thread\'s TCB to be cycled through in order. When a thread finishes its time slice, its TCB is moved from the front to the back of the queue, and the next one at the front is scheduled. This is cleaner than using an array, where you\'d need to manage indices and wrap-around logic. For an implementation where TCB’s are getting popped out and enqueued often, a queue will simplify the overarching design of the scheduler due to the O(1) operations in place for true queue structures whereas in the array type structures used in the lab, we’d have to keep track of indices of where a TCB could be popped out, etc.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',4,1,'2025-07-29_15-12-43','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling allows for each thread\'s TCB to be cycled through in order. When a thread finishes its time slice, its TCB is moved from the front to the back of the queue, and the TCB at the front is then scheduled to run next by the round-robin scheduler. This queue-based implementation enforces fairness where each task will be run in a circular order. For the array-based implementation one needs to keep track of the currently executing TCB and uses more complex under-the-hood logic (i.e. using the modulo operator to switch between TCB) to cycle through the array to ensure round-robin scheduling. On the other hand, using the queue data structure natively enforces FIFO cycling by simply popping the first TCB and enqueuing it again to cycle through the queue. Although using the queue data structure allows for a simpler algorithm for round-robin scheduler implementation, the queue data structure is used from an external library/header file; therefore, introducing this data structure will increase the overhead. This is not ideal in resource limited systems like the Cortex M4 as the compiler will need to store the definition and related functions in memory, reducing the usable memory overall whereas can be used without external overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,1,'2025-07-29_15-54-46','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling allows for each thread\'s TCB to be cycled through in order. When a thread finishes its time slice, its TCB is moved from the front to the back of the queue, and the TCB at the front is then scheduled to run next by the round-robin scheduler. This queue-based implementation enforces fairness where each task will be run in a circular order. For the array-based implementation one needs to keep track of the currently executing TCB and uses more complex under-the-hood logic (i.e. using the modulo operator to switch between TCB) to cycle through the array to ensure round-robin scheduling. On the other hand, using the queue data structure natively enforces FIFO cycling by simply popping the first TCB and enqueuing it again to cycle through the queue. Although using the queue data structure allows for a simpler algorithm for round-robin scheduler implementation, the queue data structure is used from an external library/header file; therefore, introducing this data structure will increase the overhead. This is not ideal in resource limited systems like the Cortex M4 as the compiler will need to store the definition and related functions in memory, reducing the usable memory overall whereas can be used without external overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,1,'2025-07-29_17-33-40','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling allows for each thread\'s TCB to be cycled through in order. When a thread finishes its time slice, its TCB is moved from the front to the back of the queue, and the TCB at the front is then scheduled to run next by the round-robin scheduler. This queue-based implementation enforces fairness where each task will be run in a circular order. For the array-based implementation one needs to keep track of the currently executing TCB and uses more complex under-the-hood logic (i.e. using the modulo operator to switch between TCB) to cycle through the array to ensure round-robin scheduling. On the other hand, using the queue data structure natively enforces FIFO cycling by simply popping the first TCB and enqueuing it again to cycle through the queue. Although using the queue data structure allows for a simpler algorithm for round-robin scheduler implementation, the queue data structure is used from an external library/header file; therefore, introducing this data structure will increase the overhead. This is not ideal in resource limited systems like the Cortex M4 as the compiler will need to store the definition and related functions in memory, reducing the usable memory overall whereas can be used without external overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,2,'2025-07-29_10-46-14','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first saving the current thread’s state (registers, stack pointer, etc.), followed by loading the state of the next thread. It is usually triggered after SVC_Handler_Main finishes, ensuring the stack is properly restored before switching. Because PendSV runs at the lowest priority, it will avoid interrupt conflicts and safely handles the switch without stack corruption, allowing for smooth round-robin scheduling between threads.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',4,2,'2025-07-29_15-12-43','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first saving the current thread’s state (registers, stack pointer, etc.), followed by loading the state of the next thread. Specifically, we read the PSP (current stack pointer) and pushes the R4–R11 onto the current thread\'s stack as a part of saving the current thread’s state. Then the osSched function is called to update the global pointer to point to the next thread stack. After successfully executing osSched, the handler loads the saved R4–R11 from the new thread\'s stack, followed by updating the PSP to point to the new stack and returns using BX LR, causing the processor to resume execution of the new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,2,'2025-07-29_15-54-46','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first saving the current thread’s state (registers, stack pointer, etc.), followed by loading the state of the next thread. Specifically, we read the PSP (current stack pointer) and pushes the R4–R11 onto the current thread\'s stack as a part of saving the current thread’s state. Then the osSched function is called to update the global pointer to point to the next thread stack. After successfully executing osSched, the handler loads the saved R4–R11 from the new thread\'s stack, followed by updating the PSP to point to the new stack and returns using BX LR, causing the processor to resume execution of the new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,2,'2025-07-29_17-33-40','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first saving the current thread’s state (registers, stack pointer, etc.), followed by loading the state of the next thread. Specifically, we read the PSP (current stack pointer) and pushes the R4–R11 onto the current thread\'s stack as a part of saving the current thread’s state. Then the osSched function is called to update the global pointer to point to the next thread stack. After successfully executing osSched, the handler loads the saved R4–R11 from the new thread\'s stack, followed by updating the PSP to point to the new stack and returns using BX LR, causing the processor to resume execution of the new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,3,'2025-07-29_10-46-14','By following an integer priority for choosing which task to run next, we introduce a bias in favor of those tasks with higher priority. This is a bad idea, as selecting the task with the highest priority while disregarding waiting low-priority threads to run often leads those threads to starvation (lower-priority tasks may never run if a higher-priority task is always ready). This breaks the qualities of fairness and can cause critical but lower-priority threads (e.g., system maintenance or background logging) to be permanently ignored.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',4,3,'2025-07-29_15-12-43','Using the modified scheduler explicitly prevents fairness (i.e. allowing for all threads to run) as its logic runs threads based on an integer priority value, preventing critical tasks from running if they are low priority. This is a bad idea, as selecting the task with the highest priority while disregarding waiting low-priority threads to run often leads those threads to starvation (lower-priority tasks may never run if a higher-priority task is always ready). This breaks the qualities of fairness and can cause critical but lower-priority threads (e.g., system maintenance or background logging) to be permanently ignored. It can be possible that the scheduler can essentially get stuck on the highest-priority tasks and no others can get a chance to run due to the overarching behaviour of the scheduler favoring priority rather than providing equal CPU usage across all threads (to enable fairness).\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,3,'2025-07-29_15-54-46','Using the modified scheduler explicitly prevents fairness (i.e. allowing for all threads to run) as its logic runs threads based on an integer priority value, preventing critical tasks from running if they are low priority. This is a bad idea, as selecting the task with the highest priority while disregarding waiting low-priority threads to run often leads those threads to starvation (lower-priority tasks may never run if a higher-priority task is always ready). This breaks the qualities of fairness and can cause critical but lower-priority threads (e.g., system maintenance or background logging) to be permanently ignored. It can be possible that the scheduler can essentially get stuck on the highest-priority tasks and no others can get a chance to run due to the overarching behaviour of the scheduler favoring priority rather than providing equal CPU usage across all threads (to enable fairness).\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',4,3,'2025-07-29_17-33-40','Using the modified scheduler explicitly prevents fairness (i.e. allowing for all threads to run) as its logic runs threads based on an integer priority value, preventing critical tasks from running if they are low priority. This is a bad idea, as selecting the task with the highest priority while disregarding waiting low-priority threads to run often leads those threads to starvation (lower-priority tasks may never run if a higher-priority task is always ready). This breaks the qualities of fairness and can cause critical but lower-priority threads (e.g., system maintenance or background logging) to be permanently ignored as only one thread of highest priority could run indefinitely. It can be possible that the scheduler can essentially get stuck on the highest-priority tasks and no others can get a chance to run due to the overarching behaviour of the scheduler favoring priority rather than providing equal CPU usage across all threads (to enable fairness).\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,4,'2025-07-29_10-46-14','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code easier to read, write, and even debug. Assembly is a very low-level language, therefore offloading the complex code to C helps to abstract the low-level code. The downside is that we lose some flexibility/control and a small amount of performance (coding in lower-level languages is often more efficient). Moreover, we need to write some assembly to set up the context properly, as C can’t directly access some registers. Overall, using both allows for a balance between control and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,4,'2025-07-29_15-12-43','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code easier to read, write, and even debug. Assembly is a very low-level language, therefore offloading the complex code to C helps to abstract the low-level code. The downside is that we lose some flexibility/control and a small amount of performance (coding in lower-level languages is often more efficient). Moreover, we need to write some assembly to set up the context properly, as C can’t directly access some registers. Overall, using both allows for a balance between control and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,4,'2025-07-29_15-54-46','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code easier to read, write, and even debug. Assembly is a very low-level language, therefore offloading the complex code to C helps to abstract the low-level code. The downside is that we lose some flexibility/control and a small amount of performance (coding in lower-level languages is often more efficient). Moreover, we need to write some assembly to set up the context properly, as C can’t directly access some registers. Overall, using both allows for a balance between control and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,4,'2025-07-29_17-33-40','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code easier to read, write, and even debug. Assembly is a very low-level language, therefore offloading the complex code to C helps to abstract the low-level code. The downside is that we lose some flexibility/control and a small amount of performance (coding in lower-level languages is often more efficient). Moreover, we need to write some assembly to set up the context properly, as C can’t directly access some registers. Overall, using both allows for a balance between control and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,5,'2025-07-29_10-46-14','If we created a single thread (running print_continuously), the OS will still work as it boots up, runs the scheduler, and simply executes the thread. Since there is only one thread in the system, yielding doesn’t do anything as there isn’t any other thread to switch to. The thread simply resumes running again after yielding. Therefore, while the OS does work, there isn’t a benefit of using the modified print_continuously (using the previous print_continuously without yielding is better).\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,5,'2025-07-29_15-12-43','If we created a single thread (running print_continuously), the OS will still work as it boots up, runs the scheduler, and simply executes the thread. Since there is only one thread in the system, yielding doesn’t do anything as there isn’t any other thread to switch to. The thread simply resumes running again after yielding. Therefore, while the OS does work, there isn’t a benefit of using the modified print_continuously (using the previous print_continuously without yielding is better).\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',4,5,'2025-07-29_15-54-46','If we created a single thread (running print_continuously), the OS will still work as it boots up, runs the scheduler, and simply executes the thread. The exact role of the scheduler when a thread “yields” is to pick the next task to run, and since there is only one thread in the system, yielding doesn’t do anything as there isn’t any other thread to switch to so the scheduler will pick the exact same thread to run again. The main idea of yielding is to stop the currently running thread and schedule another one, but since there is only ever going to be one thread, the yielding functionality is quite pointless. Therefore, while the OS does work, there isn’t a benefit of using the modified print_continuously (using the previous print_continuously without yielding is better) and there is also more overhead due to the extra logic implemented by adding yielding logic (saving and restoring state).\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',4,5,'2025-07-29_17-33-40','If we created a single thread (running print_continuously), the OS will still work as it boots up, runs the scheduler, and simply executes the thread. The exact role of the scheduler when a thread “yields” is to pick the next task to run, and since there is only one thread in the system, yielding doesn’t do anything as there isn’t any other thread to switch to so the scheduler will pick the exact same thread to run again. The main idea of yielding is to stop the currently running thread and schedule another one, but since there is only ever going to be one thread, the yielding functionality is quite pointless. Therefore, while the OS does work, there isn’t a benefit of using the modified print_continuously (using the previous print_continuously without yielding is better) and there is also more overhead due to the extra logic implemented by adding yielding logic (saving and restoring state).\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',5,1,'2025-07-30_18-28-42','To pass an argument into a thread, the user starts by creating a variable or data structure to be used by the thread. A pointer to this data, cast to void*, is then passed to the thread at the time of its creation using the kernel function osCreateThread so that it can be passed generically. The osCreateThread function will initialize registers like the psp and specifically initializes the R0 register to this argument in the thread’s stack (TCB). When the thread begins execution, the OS provides this pointer to the thread’s entry function as its argument. Inside the thread, this void* is cast back to the intended data type so that the thread can access the necessary values. This works because C allows flexible use of pointers, and all pointers occupy the same amount of memory regardless of type, enabling safe passing and interpretation. As a result, the thread function receives the correct data as intended by the user, allowing for effective and efficient communication between the main program and the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',5,1,'2025-07-30_19-02-31','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first creates a variable or data structure that contains the information the thread needs. A pointer to this data is then cast to a void* and passed into the thread creation function (osCreateThread). From the OS perspective, when a new thread is created, the OS creates a TCB and allocates a stack for the thread and builds the initial context on that stack. This includes preloading values for key CPU registers, such as the Program Counter (PC), Link Register (LR), and most importantly, R0, which is used to hold the thread’s argument. The OS stores the argument value in the memory location on the stack where R0 will be restored from during the context switch. When the thread starts running, the processor pops these values off the stack—loading the argument into R0—and calls the thread\'s function. The thread function receives this pointer, casts it back to the original data type, and uses it as intended. This works because the procedure call standard uses R0 for the first argument, and the OS carefully sets up the stack so that the processor restores R0 with the correct value when the thread begins. Combined with C’s flexible pointer casting, this makes it a safe and efficient way to pass arguments into threads.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',5,2,'2025-07-30_18-28-42','You cannot call osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these handlers are already part of the context-switching mechanism in place. Calling osYield from within them would attempt to start another context switch while a context switch is already in progress. This leads to recursive/nested context switches, which can corrupt the stack and cause undefined behavior or even a system crash. Specifically, osYield triggers a PendSV interrupt; therefore, calling it from within PendSV_Handler would “re-pend” the same interrupt before the current one finishes, resulting in stack overflow or corrupted thread states. Since the kernel uses a precise, atomic context-switch process, interrupting or re-entering it can lead to bugs or system instability.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',5,2,'2025-07-30_19-02-31','You cannot call osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these handlers are already part of the context-switching mechanism in place. Calling osYield from within them would attempt to start another context switch while a context switch is already in progress. This leads to recursive/nested context switches, which can corrupt the stack and cause undefined behavior or even a system crash. Specifically, osYield triggers a PendSV interrupt; therefore, calling it from within PendSV_Handler would “re-pend” the same interrupt before the current one finishes, resulting in stack overflow or corrupted thread states. Since the kernel uses a precise, atomic context-switch process, interrupting or re-entering it can lead to bugs or system instability.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',5,3,'2025-07-30_18-28-42','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms rather than 1ms increases the system tick rate—the OS can respond to events and switch between threads with increased timing resolution. This improves system responsiveness, allows for more precise delays/timeouts, and can even benefit real-time applications that require high timing accuracy. However, the downside is that the CPU must handle ten times more interrupts per second. This will increase interrupt overhead, which consumes more processing time, and can reduce the time available for actual thread execution. In systems with limited processing power like the Cortex M4, this can lead to inefficiency, reduced throughput, and higher power consumption. Therefore, while increasing the tick rate improves timing precision, it must be balanced against the cost of increased CPU load and system overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',5,3,'2025-07-30_19-02-31','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms rather than 1ms increases the system tick rate—the OS can respond to events and switch between threads with increased timing resolution. This improves system responsiveness, allows for more precise delays/timeouts, and can even benefit real-time applications that require high timing accuracy. However, the downside is that the CPU must handle ten times more interrupts per second. This will increase interrupt overhead, which consumes more processing time, and can reduce the time available for actual thread execution. In systems with limited processing power like the Cortex M4, this can lead to inefficiency, reduced throughput, and higher power consumption. Therefore, while increasing the tick rate improves timing precision, it must be balanced against the cost of increased CPU load and system overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',5,4,'2025-07-30_18-28-42','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires each thread to voluntarily give up control of the CPU, typically by calling osYield() when it is ready to let other threads run. If a thread never yields, no other threads will get to run, which puts the entire responsibility of fairness and responsiveness on the programmer. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking allows the OS to automatically interrupt a running thread (i.e. timer interrupt) and switch to another ready thread based on the scheduling algorithm in place (i.e. priority, round-robin, etc.). This means the user does not need to explicitly yield control. In terms of what the OS must do, co-operative multitasking is simpler—the OS performs a context switch only when explicitly asked defined by the user, so it doesn’t need to monitor timing or handle forced pre-emption. Pre-emptive multitasking, however, requires the OS to handle timer interrupts, track thread priorities and states, and safely save and restore context without corrupting the stack. This adds complexity but results in a more robust/responsive system that can inherently enforce fair CPU time distribution.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',5,4,'2025-07-30_19-02-31','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires each thread to voluntarily give up control of the CPU, typically by calling osYield() when it is ready to let other threads run. If a thread never yields, no other threads will get to run, which puts the entire responsibility of fairness and responsiveness on the programmer. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking allows the OS to automatically interrupt a running thread (i.e. timer interrupt) and switch to another ready thread based on the scheduling algorithm in place (i.e. priority, round-robin, etc.). This means the user does not need to explicitly yield control. In terms of what the OS must do, co-operative multitasking is simpler—the OS performs a context switch only when explicitly asked defined by the user, so it doesn’t need to monitor timing or handle forced pre-emption. Pre-emptive multitasking, however, requires the OS to handle timer interrupts, track thread priorities and states, and safely save and restore context without corrupting the stack. This adds complexity but results in a more robust/responsive system that can inherently enforce fair CPU time distribution.\n',1,'s25'),('a2durani',5,5,'2025-07-30_18-28-42','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they both trigger use context switching. In this lab, one thread explicitly called osYield() to voluntarily give up the CPU, allowing the scheduler to choose another ready thread to run. The second thread did not call osYield() and simply ran continuously where the OS relied on the SysTick_Handler to force a context switch once the thread has reached the desired runtime. This means that even if one thread doesn’t yield, the system can still pre-empt it after a defined time slice and yield the CPU to another thread. Because both yielding and time-based switching uses the same context-switching logic under the hood, they can coexist—cooperative threads can yield early, while the OS ensures fairness and responsiveness through pre-emption if needed.\n',0,'s25'),('a2durani',5,5,'2025-07-30_19-02-31','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because both trigger the same mechanism for performing a context switch—the PendSV_Handler. When a thread calls osYield(), the kernel sets the PendSV interrupt as pending, which inevitably invokes PendSV_Handler to carry out the context switch. Pre-emptive switching is driven by the SysTick_Handler, which is triggered at a fixed interval (e.g., every 1 ms). When SysTick_Handler fires, it updates the system time and checks if the currently running thread has maxed its respective time slice out. If so, it also sets the PendSV interrupt to request a context switch. In both cases the actual context switch is performed in PendSV_Handler, ensuring consistent behavior, and this separation allows both mechanisms to work together where cooperative threads can yield control early while the OS ensures fairness through timer-driven pre-emption when threads do not yield voluntarily.\n',1,'s25'),('a2karuna',2,1,'2025-04-30_12-31-05','When the PC was changed to hold the pointer to the print continuously function it allowed for the redirection of the flow of the program to continuously execute the function. The PC register holds the memory addresses of the next instructions so passing the address of the print_continuously function sets the function as the next one to be executed. The program then executes from the start of the print_continuously function where it enters an infinite loop of printing “Hello, PC!” with no break condition. At the register level the jumpAssembly(void*fcn) function moves the function pointer to print_continuously located in R0 to the PC register using MOV PC, R0. This allows the processor to jump to the print_continuously function and execute.\n',0,'s25'),('a2karuna',2,1,'2025-04-30_12-42-00','When the PC was changed to hold the pointer to the print continuously function it allowed for the redirection of the flow of the program to continuously execute the function. The PC register holds the memory addresses of the next instructions so passing the address of the print_continuously function sets the function as the next one to be executed. The program then executes from the start of the print_continuously function where it enters an infinite loop of printing “Hello, PC!” with no break condition. At the register level the jumpAssembly(void*fcn) function moves the function pointer to print_continuously located in R0 to the PC register using MOV PC, R0. This allows the processor to jump to the print_continuously function and execute.\n',0,'s25'),('a2karuna',2,2,'2025-04-30_12-31-05','A system call is a request of service from the operating system by the program. It allows for the program to interact with hardware resources or perform privileged operations. When the program uses a system call there is a specific instruction that switches to kernel mode and allows for the program to make service requests to the OS. The OS then performs the requested task and then returns the result to the program. System calls are vital because it allows for there to be a standard and secure way for the program to access the system resources and hardware without requiring users to write their own methods to access the resources.\n',0,'s25'),('a2karuna',2,2,'2025-04-30_12-42-00','A system call is a request of service from the operating system by the program. It allows for the program to interact with hardware resources or perform privileged operations. When the program uses a system call there is a specific instruction that switches to kernel mode and allows for the program to make service requests to the OS. The OS then performs the requested task and then returns the result to the program. System calls are vital because it allows for there to be a standard and secure way for the program to access the system resources and hardware without requiring users to write their own methods to access the resources.\n',0,'s25'),('a2karuna',2,3,'2025-04-30_12-31-05','The thread stack was setup by allocating a space in memory that stores information required for the CPU to execute the thread. This includes register values which are necessary to restore the threads execution state. These register values are places on the stack in a specific order such that when they are popped off, they can be loaded into the appropriate registers. To “setup a thread’s stack” key values are pushed onto the stack in the correct order. The order is PSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4. This is done so when the system call to make the thread is executed the CPU can pop off these values into the hardware registers and resume operations as if the thread was already running. It is necessary to store the register values on the stack because it is used to maintain the context of the thread. The context is used to switch between threads by saving the current state of the thread and resuming when necessary. If the context is not saved, then the CPU would not be able to track what part of the thread was executed last when switching.\n',0,'s25'),('a2karuna',2,3,'2025-04-30_12-42-00','The thread stack was setup by allocating a space in memory that stores information required for the CPU to execute the thread. This includes register values which are necessary to restore the threads execution state. These register values are places on the stack in a specific order such that when they are popped off, they can be loaded into the appropriate registers. To “setup a thread’s stack” key values are pushed onto the stack in the correct order. The order is PSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4. This is done so when the system call to make the thread is executed the CPU can pop off these values into the hardware registers and resume operations as if the thread was already running. It is necessary to store the register values on the stack because it is used to maintain the context of the thread. The context is used to switch between threads by saving the current state of the thread and resuming when necessary. If the context is not saved, then the CPU would not be able to track what part of the thread was executed last when switching.\n',0,'s25'),('a2karuna',2,4,'2025-04-30_12-31-05','The __set_PSP function is required because the processor needs to know where to retrieve the thread’s stack from. If the location of the PSP is not set, then the processor will try to use an uninitialized or invalid stack pointer. The use of these will lead to a HardFault. When the processor switches from MSP to PSP the correct stack pointer must be used. Using an invalid stack pointer will result in the processor popping invalid data from a location that is assumed to be the PSP. The context for the thread will not be pushed onto the stack and it will result in a HardFault. Also, the SVC_Handler_Main expects a defined PSP stack pointer to switch to, and if there is no valid location then it will result in a HardFault.\n',0,'s25'),('a2karuna',2,4,'2025-04-30_12-42-00','The __set_PSP function is required because the processor needs to know where to retrieve the thread’s stack from. If the location of the PSP is not set, then the processor will try to use an uninitialized or invalid stack pointer. The use of these will lead to a HardFault. When the processor switches from MSP to PSP the correct stack pointer must be used. Using an invalid stack pointer will result in the processor popping invalid data from a location that is assumed to be the PSP. The context for the thread will not be pushed onto the stack and it will result in a HardFault. Also, the SVC_Handler_Main expects a defined PSP stack pointer to switch to, and if there is no valid location then it will result in a HardFault.\n',0,'s25'),('a2karuna',2,5,'2025-04-30_12-31-05','If the function only printed once and returned, then program would terminate the stack. Nothing else would happen in the thread and the program would have a context switch back to the original execution flow. Since the thread is finished control is returned to the OS and the OS waits for more tasks to handle.\n',0,'s25'),('a2karuna',2,5,'2025-04-30_12-42-00','If the function only printed once and returned, then program would terminate the stack. Nothing else would happen in the thread and the program would have a context switch back to the original execution flow. Since the thread is finished control is returned to the OS and the OS waits for more tasks to handle.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,1,'2025-06-26_00-47-06','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it is a software interface that is used to for hardware to interact with software programs without needing to know the specific details of a device. It is used in a general embedded project to help with portability and scalability. Programmers can focus on the software application logic instead of focusing on the hardware configuration/details and this usually works well with STM32CubeMX to reduce errors and save time.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,1,'2025-06-26_19-35-36','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it is a software interface that is used to for hardware to interact with software programs without needing to know the specific details of a device. It is used in a general embedded project to help with portability and scalability. Programmers can focus on the software application logic instead of focusing on the hardware configuration/details and this usually works well with STM32CubeMX to reduce errors and save time.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,1,'2025-06-26_21-14-56','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it is a software interface that is used to for hardware to interact with software programs without needing to know the specific details of a device. It is used in a general embedded project to help with portability and scalability. Programmers can focus on the software application logic instead of focusing on the hardware configuration/details and this usually works well with STM32CubeMX to reduce errors and save time.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,1,'2025-06-26_22-55-47','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it is a software interface that is used to for hardware to interact with software programs without needing to know the specific details of a device. It is used in a general embedded project to help with portability and scalability. Programmers can focus on the software application logic instead of focusing on the hardware configuration/details and this usually works well with STM32CubeMX to reduce errors and save time.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,2,'2025-06-26_00-47-06','The __io_putchar function is used to write a single character to an appropriate IO channel.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,2,'2025-06-26_19-35-36','The __io_putchar function sends a single character to a hardware peripheral, usually UART, allowing printf to display output in embedded systems. It’s needed because embedded devices don’t have standard output by default, so programmers must implement this function to define how characters are transmitted, typically over serial communication.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,2,'2025-06-26_21-14-56','The __io_putchar function sends a single character to a hardware peripheral, usually UART, allowing printf to display output in embedded systems. It’s needed because embedded devices don’t have standard output by default, so programmers must implement this function to define how characters are transmitted, typically over serial communication.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,2,'2025-06-26_22-55-47','The __io_putchar function sends a single character to a hardware peripheral, typically UART, enabling functions like printf to work in embedded systems that lack standard output by default. This function is designed to be customizable so it can adapt to different hardware configurations. For example, while one system might use UART for serial communication, another might use USB, SPI, or even an LCD display for output. In each case, __io_putchar would need to be redefined to match the specific output hardware. This flexibility is important because it allows developers to tailor standard I/O functions to the available peripherals of their target platform, ensuring portability and reuse across different projects.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,3,'2025-06-26_00-47-06','We used the debugger instead of printf because it gives us more control and visibility into the program. It lets us pause and step through the code, inspect variables without changing the program’s behavior, and is better for finding complex bugs that printf can’t easily reveal.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,3,'2025-06-26_19-35-36','A debugger was used in this lab because it gives a better understanding into the program’s behavior than printf. In embedded systems like the Cortex-M4, register values are critical pieces of information used for understanding hardware states, interrupt handling, and low-level behavior. This type of information cannot be accessed by printf.  The printf  function is also limited because it can change timing, doesn’t show memory or register states, and may not work early in system startup. The debugger, on the other hand, lets us pause execution, step through code, and inspect variables and registers directly, making it a more accurate and powerful tool for debugging hardware-level issues.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,3,'2025-06-26_21-14-56','A debugger was used in this lab because it gives a better understanding into the program’s behavior than printf. In embedded systems like the Cortex-M4, register values are critical pieces of information used for understanding hardware states, interrupt handling, and low-level behavior. This type of information cannot be accessed by printf.  The printf  function is also limited because it can change timing, doesn’t show memory or register states, and may not work early in system startup. The debugger, on the other hand, lets us pause execution, step through code, and inspect variables and registers directly, making it a more accurate and powerful tool for debugging hardware-level issues.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,3,'2025-06-26_22-55-47','A debugger was used in this lab because it gives a better understanding into the program’s behavior than printf. In embedded systems like the Cortex-M4, register values are critical pieces of information used for understanding hardware states, interrupt handling, and low-level behavior. This type of information cannot be accessed by printf.  The printf  function is also limited because it can change timing, doesn’t show memory or register states, and may not work early in system startup. The debugger, on the other hand, lets us pause execution, step through code, and inspect variables and registers directly, making it a more accurate and powerful tool for debugging hardware-level issues.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,4,'2025-06-26_00-47-06','We start with MSP_init_val, the initial top of the main stack, and subtract a fixed size from it to get PSP_val, the top of the process stack. Knowing MSP_init_val is important to make sure the two stacks don’t overlap and enough memory is allocated safely.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,4,'2025-06-26_19-35-36','We start with MSP_init_val, the initial top of the main stack, and subtract a fixed size from it to get PSP_val, the top of the process stack. Knowing MSP_init_val is important to make sure the two stacks don’t overlap and enough memory is allocated safely.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,4,'2025-06-26_21-14-56','We start with MSP_init_val, which is the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and typically points to the top of the available SRAM at reset. This value is usually obtained by dereferencing the first word at address 0x0, where the processor stores the MSP during startup. To create a new stack for a thread, we subtract a fixed number of bytes (e.g., 0x400) from MSP_init_val to get PSP_val, which becomes the top of the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This manual offset ensures that the new stack has its own space in memory. Knowing MSP_init_val is crucial because it helps us place the PSP in a region of memory that doesn\'t overlap with the main stack or other system-reserved areas. This prevents corruption of either stack and ensures that enough memory is reserved for each stack to operate safely. By managing stack locations manually like this, we can simulate multi-threading behavior in bare-metal embedded systems without an operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,4,'2025-06-26_22-55-47','We start with MSP_init_val, which is the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and typically points to the top of the available SRAM at reset. This value is usually obtained by dereferencing the first word at address 0x0, where the processor stores the MSP during startup. To create a new stack for a thread, we subtract a fixed number of bytes (e.g., 0x400) from MSP_init_val to get PSP_val, which becomes the top of the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This manual offset ensures that the new stack has its own space in memory. Knowing MSP_init_val is crucial because it helps us place the PSP in a region of memory that doesn\'t overlap with the main stack or other system-reserved areas. This prevents corruption of either stack and ensures that enough memory is reserved for each stack to operate safely. By managing stack locations manually like this, we can simulate multi-threading behavior in bare-metal embedded systems without an operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,5,'2025-06-26_00-47-06','To allocate multiple stacks, subtract a larger block of memory from MSP_init_val and divide it into equal parts. Then, calculate each stack pointer using fixed offsets. The code needs to define the number of stacks and stack size, and loop to assign each stack its own pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,5,'2025-06-26_19-35-36','To allocate multiple stacks, subtract a larger block of memory from MSP_init_val and divide it into equal parts. Then, calculate each stack pointer using fixed offsets. The code needs to define the number of stacks and stack size, and loop to assign each stack its own pointer.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,5,'2025-06-26_21-14-56','To allocate multiple stacks, subtract a larger block of memory from MSP_init_val and divide it into equal parts. Then, calculate each stack pointer using fixed offsets. The code needs to define the number of stacks and stack size, and loop to assign each stack its own pointer. To support multiple threads in an embedded system, you can statically allocate a block of memory at compile-time and divide it into equal-sized, properly aligned stack regions (one for each thread). The stack grows downward, so the stack pointer should start at the end of each region. By using #define to set the number of threads and stack size, and avoiding runtime allocation like malloc, you ensure deterministic memory usage, which is critical in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',1,5,'2025-06-26_22-55-47','To allocate multiple stacks, subtract a larger block of memory from MSP_init_val and divide it into equal parts. Then, calculate each stack pointer using fixed offsets. The code needs to define the number of stacks and stack size, and loop to assign each stack its own pointer. To support multiple threads in an embedded system, you can statically allocate a block of memory at compile-time and divide it into equal-sized, properly aligned stack regions (one for each thread). The stack grows downward, so the stack pointer should start at the end of each region. By using #define to set the number of threads and stack size, and avoiding runtime allocation like malloc, you ensure deterministic memory usage, which is critical in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',2,1,'2025-06-26_19-50-06','In this lab, when we changed the PC  to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the CPU immediately jumped to that function and started executing it. This bypassed the normal function call process, meaning no return address was saved. As a result, the program entered the print_continuously loop directly and couldn’t return to the original code.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',2,1,'2025-06-26_22-00-57','In this lab, when we changed the PC  to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the CPU immediately jumped to that function and started executing it. This bypassed the normal function call process, meaning no return address was saved. As a result, the program entered the print_continuously loop directly and couldn’t return to the original code.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',2,2,'2025-06-26_19-50-06','A system call is a way for a program to ask the operating system to do something it doesn’t have direct permission to do itself. Examples of a system call include reading from a file, writing to the screen, or even accessing hardware. Since user programs run with limited privileges for safety, they use system calls to request services from the more powerful, trusted kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',2,2,'2025-06-26_22-00-57','A system call is a special type of function call that a user program makes to request a service from the operating system. It allows the program to ask the kernel to perform tasks it doesn’t have the privileges to do directly, such as reading from a file, writing to the screen, or accessing hardware. Since user programs run in a restricted mode for safety, they rely on system calls to safely interact with the more powerful and trusted kernel. By treating a system call as a controlled function call into the operating system, the system maintains both functionality and protection.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',2,3,'2025-06-26_19-50-06','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a special memory area so the CPU knows where to start when running that thread. By setting up a thread’s stack, values (like the initial values of a register) are placed on the stack that the CPU expects to find which tells it where to begin executing, and the xPSR, which sets up the processor state. By storing register’s values onto the stack, the CPU can restore them when the thread starts or resumes. This is important because each thread can have its own set of register values and saving them lets the system switch between threads without losing each one’s place in execution. It’s like bookmarking a thread’s progress so it can pick up right where it left off.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',2,3,'2025-06-26_22-00-57','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a dedicated and valid memory region that the CPU can use when executing that thread. This memory must be properly aligned—typically to an 8-byte boundary on ARM Cortex-M processors—and located in accessible RAM, large enough to hold the necessary register values and stack usage without overflowing. The stack setup involves placing specific values onto the stack in a particular order—including the xPSR, PC (program counter), LR (link register), and general-purpose registers like r0–r12—because the CPU expects them in that format when restoring context during an exception return or thread switch. This structure ensures the CPU can begin execution correctly and consistently. Saving these register values is essential for allowing threads to maintain their own state, enabling smooth context switching without overwriting another thread’s progress. It’s like giving each thread a personalized workspace and a bookmark, so it can pause and resume seamlessly as needed.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',2,4,'2025-06-26_19-50-06','We used __set_PSP in SVC_Handler_Main to set the PSP to the correct stack for the new thread. If we didn’t do this, the CPU would try to pop register values (like PC, LR, and xPSR) from an undefined or incorrect location, because it assumes the PSP is already pointing to a valid stack frame. Since thread switching relies on restoring the thread’s context (from the PSP), not setting it properly means the CPU ends up using invalid memory, leading to a hard fault. So, setting the PSP correctly is essential to ensure the CPU has the right data to begin or resume thread execution safely.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',2,4,'2025-06-26_22-00-57','We used __set_PSP in SVC_Handler_Main to set the PSP to the correct stack for the new thread. If we didn’t do this, the CPU would try to pop register values (like PC, LR, and xPSR) from an undefined or incorrect location, because it assumes the PSP is already pointing to a valid stack frame. Since thread switching relies on restoring the thread’s context (from the PSP), not setting it properly means the CPU ends up using invalid memory, leading to a hard fault. So, setting the PSP correctly is essential to ensure the CPU has the right data to begin or resume thread execution safely.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',2,5,'2025-06-26_19-50-06','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead just printed something once and returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it (similar to the return function). But since this thread was started using a manually prepared stack (not a real function call), there’s no valid return address on the stack. Hence, when the function returns, it will jump to a random or invalid address, likely causing a hard fault or crash. In embedded systems, thread functions are usually designed to never return — they either loop forever or call a special function like thread_exit() to safely terminate.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',2,5,'2025-06-26_22-00-57','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead just printed something once and returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it (similar to the return function). But since this thread was started using a manually prepared stack (not a real function call), there’s no valid return address on the stack. Hence, when the function returns, it will jump to a random or invalid address, likely causing a hard fault or crash. In embedded systems, thread functions are usually designed to never return — they either loop forever or call a special function like thread_exit() to safely terminate.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,1,'2025-06-27_02-09-35','Each .c file is first compiled separately into an object file. Object files contain machine code for just that file. Then, a linker combines all the object files into a single executable by resolving symbols (like function calls or global variables) across files. This allows code split across multiple files to work together as one program.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,1,'2025-06-27_09-22-21','Each *.c file first goes through a preprocessing step, where directives like #include, #define, and #ifdef are handled. This phase expands macros, includes header files, and removes comments, modifying the source code before actual compilation begins. After preprocessing, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file containing machine code. Then, a linker combines all the object files into a single executable by resolving symbols such as function calls and global variables. This whole process allows code organized across multiple files to work together as one complete program.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,1,'2025-06-27_09-30-39','Each *.c file first goes through a preprocessing step, where directives like #include, #define, and #ifdef are handled. This phase expands macros, includes header files, and removes comments, modifying the source code before actual compilation begins. After preprocessing, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file containing machine code. Then, a linker combines all the object files into a single executable by resolving symbols such as function calls and global variables. This whole process allows code organized across multiple files to work together as one complete program.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,1,'2025-06-27_18-57-06','Each *.c file first goes through a preprocessing step, where directives like #include, #define, and #ifdef are handled. This phase expands macros, includes header files, and removes comments, modifying the source code before actual compilation begins. After preprocessing, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file containing machine code. Then, a linker combines all the object files into a single executable by resolving symbols such as function calls and global variables. This whole process allows code organized across multiple files to work together as one complete program.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,1,'2025-06-27_19-36-18','Each *.c file first goes through a preprocessing step, where directives like #include, #define, and #ifdef are handled. This phase expands macros, includes header files, and removes comments, modifying the source code before actual compilation begins. After preprocessing, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file containing machine code. Then, a linker combines all the object files into a single executable by resolving symbols such as function calls and global variables. This whole process allows code organized across multiple files to work together as one complete program.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,2,'2025-06-27_02-09-35','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it copies the entire .c file into another file during preprocessing, leading to multiple definitions of functions and variables. This breaks the proper compile-link process, where each .c file should be compiled separately and then linked. Instead, you should use #include with header files to share declarations, not implementations.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,2,'2025-06-27_09-22-21','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it copies the entire .c file into another file during preprocessing, leading to multiple definitions of functions and variables. This breaks the proper compile-link process, where each .c file should be compiled separately and then linked together. Instead, you should use #include with header files to share declarations, not implementations. Including a .c file can also cause other issues, such as longer compile times, tangled dependencies, and harder-to-maintain code. To avoid these problems, it\'s best to structure code with clear separation between interface and implementation files. Each module should expose only what’s necessary through its header, allowing other files to use its functionality without direct access to its internal code. This keeps the codebase modular, readable, and easier to debug or update.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,2,'2025-06-27_09-30-39','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it copies the entire .c file into another file during preprocessing, leading to multiple definitions of functions and variables. This breaks the proper compile-link process, where each .c file should be compiled separately and then linked together. Instead, you should use #include with header files to share declarations, not implementations. Including a .c file can also cause other issues, such as longer compile times, tangled dependencies, and harder-to-maintain code. To avoid these problems, it\'s best to structure code with clear separation between interface and implementation files. Each module should expose only what’s necessary through its header, allowing other files to use its functionality without direct access to its internal code. This keeps the codebase modular, readable, and easier to debug or update.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,2,'2025-06-27_18-57-06','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it copies the entire .c file into another file during preprocessing, leading to multiple definitions of functions and variables. This breaks the proper compile-link process, where each .c file should be compiled separately and then linked together. Instead, you should use #include with header files to share declarations, not implementations. Including a .c file can also cause other issues, such as longer compile times, tangled dependencies, and harder-to-maintain code. To avoid these problems, it\'s best to structure code with clear separation between interface and implementation files. Each module should expose only what’s necessary through its header, allowing other files to use its functionality without direct access to its internal code. This keeps the codebase modular, readable, and easier to debug or update.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,2,'2025-06-27_19-36-18','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it copies the entire .c file into another file during preprocessing, leading to multiple definitions of functions and variables. This breaks the proper compile-link process, where each .c file should be compiled separately and then linked together. Instead, you should use #include with header files to share declarations, not implementations. Including a .c file can also cause other issues, such as longer compile times, tangled dependencies, and harder-to-maintain code. To avoid these problems, it\'s best to structure code with clear separation between interface and implementation files. Each module should expose only what’s necessary through its header, allowing other files to use its functionality without direct access to its internal code. This keeps the codebase modular, readable, and easier to debug or update.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,3,'2025-06-27_02-09-35','To start a thread, you first allocate a dedicated stack in memory and ensure it’s properly aligned. Next, you initialize the top of the stack with the expected CPU register values, including the program counter (PC), status register (xPSR), and general-purpose registers. Then, you set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to this stack. Finally, you trigger a context switch using a system call like SVC which causes the CPU to start executing the new thread using its own stack and register context.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,3,'2025-06-27_09-22-21','To start a thread from the user’s perspective, the user first defines a thread function, which is simply a function containing the code the thread should run. This definition is necessary, so the kernel knows what instructions to execute when the thread starts. Next, the user interacts with the kernel, usually through a system call, to register the thread. This often involves specifying the thread function and providing a stack, which the system uses to initialize the thread\'s context. An example would be setting the PC to the function address and preparing the stack with default register values. Once the thread is registered, the user may call a kernel function (or use a system call like SVC) to start thread execution. This separation allows users to focus on writing application logic while the kernel manages low-level details like context switching and stack setup behind the scenes.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,3,'2025-06-27_09-30-39','To start a thread from the user’s perspective, the first and most important step is to initialize the kernel environment. This typically involves calling a setup or initialization function that prepares internal kernel data structures and ensures the system is ready to manage threads. After this setup, the user defines a thread function, which contains the code the thread should run. This definition is necessary, so the kernel knows what instructions to execute when the thread begins. The user then interacts with the kernel (through a system call or a kernel API) to register the thread, providing both the thread function and a stack. The kernel uses this information to initialize the thread’s context, such as setting the program counter (PC) and loading default register values onto the stack. Once the thread is registered, the user can start thread execution, often by invoking a system call like SVC. This clear separation of responsibilities allows the user to focus on application logic while the kernel handles low-level tasks like context switching and stack management.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,3,'2025-06-27_18-57-06','To start a thread from the user’s perspective, the first and most important step is to initialize the kernel environment. This typically involves calling a setup or initialization function that prepares internal kernel data structures and ensures the system is ready to manage threads. After this setup, the user defines a thread function, which contains the code the thread should run. This definition is necessary, so the kernel knows what instructions to execute when the thread begins. The user then interacts with the kernel (through a system call or a kernel API) to register the thread, providing both the thread function and a stack. The kernel uses this information to initialize the thread’s context, such as setting the program counter (PC) and loading default register values onto the stack. Once the thread is registered, the user can start thread execution, often by invoking a system call like SVC. This clear separation of responsibilities allows the user to focus on application logic while the kernel handles low-level tasks like context switching and stack management.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,3,'2025-06-27_19-36-18','To start a thread from the user’s perspective, the first and most important step is to initialize the kernel, which sets up internal data structures and prepares the system to manage threads. This step ensures the kernel is properly configured but does not yet start running any threads. After initialization, the user defines a thread function containing the code that the thread should execute. This function is necessary so the kernel knows where to begin execution for that thread. The user then registers the thread with the kernel, typically by calling a system API or using a system call, and provides a dedicated stack along with the thread function. The kernel uses this to initialize the thread’s context: setting the program counter (PC), status register (xPSR), and other necessary values on the stack. Once all desired threads are registered, the user must take an additional, separate step to start the kernel, which begins the actual execution of threads. This clear separation between initializing the kernel, registering threads, and starting execution allows the system to remain organized and gives the user full control over when the kernel begins running threads.\n.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,4,'2025-06-27_02-09-35','The stack pool is a pre-allocated block of memory reserved for storing the stacks of multiple threads. Instead of creating stacks dynamically at runtime, which is risky in embedded systems, the stack pool allows the division of memory safely and predictably. In a future lab, this pool can be split into equal-sized regions, with each region assigned to a different thread. This ensures each thread has its own dedicated stack space. By managing the stack pool, our OS can track which stacks are in use, allocate new ones when creating threads, and support multiple threads running independently without stack overlap or memory corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,4,'2025-06-27_09-22-21','The stack pool is a pre-allocated block of memory reserved for storing the stacks of multiple threads. Instead of creating stacks dynamically at runtime, which is risky in embedded systems, the stack pool allows the division of memory safely and predictably. In a future lab, this pool can be split into equal-sized regions, with each region assigned to a different thread. This ensures each thread has its own dedicated stack space. By managing the stack pool, our OS can track which stacks are in use, allocate new ones when creating threads, and support multiple threads running independently without stack overlap or memory corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,4,'2025-06-27_09-30-39','The stack pool is a pre-allocated block of memory reserved for storing the stacks of multiple threads. Instead of creating stacks dynamically at runtime, which is risky in embedded systems, the stack pool allows the division of memory safely and predictably. In a future lab, this pool can be split into equal-sized regions, with each region assigned to a different thread. This ensures each thread has its own dedicated stack space. By managing the stack pool, our OS can track which stacks are in use, allocate new ones when creating threads, and support multiple threads running independently without stack overlap or memory corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,4,'2025-06-27_18-57-06','The stack pool is a pre-allocated block of memory reserved for storing the stacks of multiple threads. Instead of creating stacks dynamically at runtime, which is risky in embedded systems, the stack pool allows the division of memory safely and predictably. In a future lab, this pool can be split into equal-sized regions, with each region assigned to a different thread. This ensures each thread has its own dedicated stack space. By managing the stack pool, our OS can track which stacks are in use, allocate new ones when creating threads, and support multiple threads running independently without stack overlap or memory corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,4,'2025-06-27_19-36-18','The stack pool is a pre-allocated block of memory reserved for storing the stacks of multiple threads. Instead of creating stacks dynamically at runtime, which is risky in embedded systems, the stack pool allows the division of memory safely and predictably. In a future lab, this pool can be split into equal-sized regions, with each region assigned to a different thread. This ensures each thread has its own dedicated stack space. By managing the stack pool, our OS can track which stacks are in use, allocate new ones when creating threads, and support multiple threads running independently without stack overlap or memory corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,5,'2025-06-27_02-09-35','The kernel contains the low-level code that manages hardware, system resources, and thread control (which goes into files like kernel.c and kernel.h). The user refers to the application code that runs on top of the kernel, typically in main.c. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important because it separates what the user can use from what the system controls. Users interact with the kernel through well-defined functions (like system calls) without needing to know how the kernel works internally. This separation keeps the system modular, easier to understand, safer to use, and more flexible to update.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,5,'2025-06-27_09-22-21','The kernel contains the low-level code that manages hardware, system resources, and thread control, which is implemented in files like kernel.c and kernel.h. The user refers to the application code that runs on top of the kernel, typically written in main.c. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important because it separates what the user can use from what the system controls. Importantly, the user is responsible for writing thread functions, which define the tasks each thread will perform. These thread functions are then registered with the kernel using well-defined interfaces, such as system calls. This separation ensures the system remains modular, easier to understand, safer to use, and more flexible to update, while allowing users to focus on application logic without needing to manage the low-level details of thread scheduling or hardware interaction.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,5,'2025-06-27_09-30-39','The kernel contains the low-level code that manages hardware, system resources, and thread control, which is implemented in files like kernel.c and kernel.h. The user refers to the application code that runs on top of the kernel, typically written in main.c. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important because it separates what the user can use from what the system controls. Importantly, the user is responsible for writing thread functions, which define the tasks each thread will perform. These thread functions are then registered with the kernel using well-defined interfaces, such as system calls. This separation ensures the system remains modular, easier to understand, safer to use, and more flexible to update, while allowing users to focus on application logic without needing to manage the low-level details of thread scheduling or hardware interaction.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,5,'2025-06-27_18-57-06','The kernel contains the low-level code that manages hardware, system resources, and thread control, which is implemented in files like kernel.c and kernel.h. The user refers to the application code that runs on top of the kernel, typically written in main.c. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important because it separates what the user can use from what the system controls. Importantly, the user is responsible for writing thread functions, which define the tasks each thread will perform. These thread functions are then registered with the kernel using well-defined interfaces, such as system calls. This separation ensures the system remains modular, easier to understand, safer to use, and more flexible to update, while allowing users to focus on application logic without needing to manage the low-level details of thread scheduling or hardware interaction.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',3,5,'2025-06-27_19-36-18','The kernel contains the low-level code that manages hardware, system resources, and thread control, which is implemented in files like kernel.c and kernel.h. The user refers to the application code that runs on top of the kernel, typically written in main.c. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important because it separates what the user can use from what the system controls. Importantly, the user is responsible for writing thread functions, which define the tasks each thread will perform. These thread functions are then registered with the kernel using well-defined interfaces, such as system calls. This separation ensures the system remains modular, easier to understand, safer to use, and more flexible to update, while allowing users to focus on application logic without needing to manage the low-level details of thread scheduling or hardware interaction.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,1,'2025-07-30_01-15-25','Round-robin scheduling can work using a queue instead of an array as it allows threads to be managed more dynamically and cleanly compared to a fixed array. In this approach, the running thread that is currently running is at the front of the queue, and when it yields, it’s moved to the back, allowing the next thread to run. This structure models the round-robin behavior and simplifies handling of blocked or completed threads. It also makes it easier to extend the scheduler with features like priorities or dynamic thread creation. Overall, a queue offers greater flexibility, clearer logic, and improved scalability.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,1,'2025-07-30_01-26-07','In the lab, the current round-robin scheduler already uses a structure similar to a queue by cycling through an array of threads in a fixed order. Using an explicit queue for round-robin scheduling is “interesting” because it more directly models the behavior of moving the running thread from the front to the back after it yields. However, implementing a true queue can add overhead and complexity compared to the simpler array-based approach, as managing dynamic enqueue and dequeue operations requires additional bookkeeping. Since our current scheduler’s fixed array and index method already effectively achieve the round-robin rotation with minimal overhead, switching to a queue may not provide significant practical benefits. Thus, while a queue offers clearer conceptual modeling of thread order, it can introduce performance costs that may outweigh its advantages in this context.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,1,'2025-07-30_23-15-57','In the lab, the current round-robin scheduler already uses a structure similar to a queue by cycling through an array of threads in a fixed order. Using an explicit queue for round-robin scheduling is “interesting” because it more directly models the behavior of moving the running thread from the front to the back after it yields. However, implementing a true queue can add overhead and complexity compared to the simpler array-based approach, as managing dynamic enqueue and dequeue operations requires additional bookkeeping. Since our current scheduler’s fixed array and index method already effectively achieve the round-robin rotation with minimal overhead, switching to a queue may not provide significant practical benefits. Thus, while a queue offers clearer conceptual modeling of thread order, it can introduce performance costs that may outweigh its advantages in this context.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,2,'2025-07-30_01-15-25','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread\'s state and loading the next thread\'s state. It begins by pushing the CPU registers (typically R4–R11) of the running thread onto its stack, preserving its execution context. This stack pointer is then stored in the thread’s control block (TCB) so the thread can later resume from where it left off. Next, the scheduler selects the next thread to run and retrieves its saved stack pointer from its TCB. The CPU registers for this new thread are then restored from its stack, effectively preparing the system to continue its execution. When the handler exits, the processor resumes execution in the new thread, completing the context switch seamlessly.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,2,'2025-07-30_01-26-07','The PendSV_Handler begins the context switch by first reading the current process stack pointer (PSP) from the CPU to identify the running thread’s stack location. This ensures the handler works with the correct thread context. Next, it saves the thread’s CPU registers (typically R4–R11) onto this stack, preserving its state. After saving the registers, the updated stack pointer is stored back into the thread’s control block (TCB) so the thread can resume correctly later. The scheduler then selects the next thread to run and loads its saved stack pointer from its TCB. Finally, the CPU registers for the new thread are restored from this stack, and when the handler returns, the processor resumes execution of the newly selected thread. This order—first reading the PSP, then saving registers, then updating the TCB—is critical to correctly capturing and restoring thread states without corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,2,'2025-07-30_23-15-57','The PendSV_Handler begins the context switch by first reading the current process stack pointer (PSP) from the CPU to identify the running thread’s stack location. This ensures the handler works with the correct thread context. Next, it saves the thread’s CPU registers (typically R4–R11) onto this stack, preserving its state. After saving the registers, the updated stack pointer is stored back into the thread’s control block (TCB) so the thread can resume correctly later. The scheduler then selects the next thread to run and loads its saved stack pointer from its TCB. Finally, the CPU registers for the new thread are restored from this stack, and when the handler returns, the processor resumes execution of the newly selected thread. This order—first reading the PSP, then saving registers, then updating the TCB—is critical to correctly capturing and restoring thread states without corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,3,'2025-07-30_01-15-25','Modifying the OS scheduler to always run the highest-priority task can lead to a serious problem called starvation. In this setup, if one or more high-priority threads are always ready to run, lower-priority threads may never get a chance to execute, because the scheduler continually picks the highest-priority one. This is especially problematic for low-priority tasks that are still essential, such as background operations, I/O handling, or even system cleanup tasks. Without additional mechanisms like priority aging (which gradually increases the priority of waiting tasks) or time slicing among equal-priority threads, the system becomes unfair and unresponsive for lower-priority work, making it unreliable in the long run.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,3,'2025-07-30_01-26-07','Modifying the OS scheduler to always run the highest-priority task can lead to a serious problem called starvation. In this setup, if one or more high-priority threads are always ready to run, lower-priority threads may never get a chance to execute, because the scheduler continually picks the highest-priority one. This is especially problematic for low-priority tasks that are still essential, such as background operations, I/O handling, or even system cleanup tasks. Without additional mechanisms like priority aging (which gradually increases the priority of waiting tasks) or time slicing among equal-priority threads, the system becomes unfair and unresponsive for lower-priority work, making it unreliable in the long run.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,3,'2025-07-30_23-15-57','Modifying the OS scheduler to always run the highest-priority task can lead to a serious problem called starvation. In this setup, if one or more high-priority threads are always ready to run, lower-priority threads may never get a chance to execute, because the scheduler continually picks the highest-priority one. This is especially problematic for low-priority tasks that are still essential, such as background operations, I/O handling, or even system cleanup tasks. Without additional mechanisms like priority aging (which gradually increases the priority of waiting tasks) or time slicing among equal-priority threads, the system becomes unfair and unresponsive for lower-priority work, making it unreliable in the long run.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,4,'2025-07-30_01-15-25','Calling C functions like osSched in PendSV_Handler and SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler offers a clear tradeoff between readability and control. The main benefit of calling C functions is ease of development and maintenance. C code is far more readable, easier to debug, and allows you to write complex logic (like scheduling decisions or system call handling) in a high-level, structured way. It also lets you reuse code, use standard C libraries, and avoid low-level bugs common in assembly. However, the downside is that C provides less control over hardware. Writing everything in assembly can lead to faster and more optimized code, especially for low-level tasks like saving/restoring registers during a context switch. Additionally, calling C from assembly requires careful handling of function call conventions, stack alignment, and register usage, which can add overhead or introduce subtle bugs if not done precisely.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,4,'2025-07-30_01-26-07','Calling C functions like osSched in PendSV_Handler and SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler offers a clear tradeoff between readability and control. The main benefit of calling C functions is ease of development and maintenance. C code is far more readable, easier to debug, and allows you to write complex logic (like scheduling decisions or system call handling) in a high-level, structured way. It also lets you reuse code, use standard C libraries, and avoid low-level bugs common in assembly. However, the downside is that C provides less control over hardware. Writing everything in assembly can lead to faster and more optimized code, especially for low-level tasks like saving/restoring registers during a context switch. Additionally, calling C from assembly requires careful handling of function call conventions, stack alignment, and register usage, which can add overhead or introduce subtle bugs if not done precisely.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,4,'2025-07-30_23-15-57','Calling C functions like osSched in PendSV_Handler and SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler offers a clear tradeoff between readability and control. The main benefit of calling C functions is ease of development and maintenance. C code is far more readable, easier to debug, and allows you to write complex logic (like scheduling decisions or system call handling) in a high-level, structured way. It also lets you reuse code, use standard C libraries, and avoid low-level bugs common in assembly. However, the downside is that C provides less control over hardware. Writing everything in assembly can lead to faster and more optimized code, especially for low-level tasks like saving/restoring registers during a context switch. Additionally, calling C from assembly requires careful handling of function call conventions, stack alignment, and register usage, which can add overhead or introduce subtle bugs if not done precisely.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,5,'2025-07-30_01-15-25','If you create only a single thread, like print_continuously, and it yields after each print, the OS will still technically work, but it won’t be very useful. Since there\'s only one thread in the system, when it calls osYield(), the scheduler will look for another thread to run—but no other thread exists. So, the scheduler will just reselect the same thread and continue execution. This creates a loop where the same thread runs repeatedly, yielding and being scheduled again. The OS will function correctly in the sense that it performs context switching, manages the thread state, and doesn’t crash—but it\'s only managing one thread, so you won’t see the benefits of multitasking. Also, if that single thread ever blocks or crashes, the entire system becomes idle or unresponsive, since no other work can be done.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,5,'2025-07-30_01-26-07','If you create only a single thread, like print_continuously, and it yields after each print, the OS will still technically work, but it won’t be very useful. Since there\'s only one thread in the system, when it calls osYield(), the scheduler will look for another thread to run—but no other thread exists. So, the scheduler will just reselect the same thread and continue execution. This creates a loop where the same thread runs repeatedly, yielding and being scheduled again. The OS will function correctly in the sense that it performs context switching, manages the thread state, and doesn’t crash—but it\'s only managing one thread, so you won’t see the benefits of multitasking. Also, if that single thread ever blocks or crashes, the entire system becomes idle or unresponsive, since no other work can be done.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',4,5,'2025-07-30_23-15-57','If you create only a single thread, like print_continuously, and it yields after each print, the OS will still technically work, but it won’t be very useful. Since there\'s only one thread in the system, when it calls osYield(), the scheduler will look for another thread to run—but no other thread exists. So, the scheduler will just reselect the same thread and continue execution. This creates a loop where the same thread runs repeatedly, yielding and being scheduled again. The OS will function correctly in the sense that it performs context switching, manages the thread state, and doesn’t crash—but it\'s only managing one thread, so you won’t see the benefits of multitasking. Also, if that single thread ever blocks or crashes, the entire system becomes idle or unresponsive, since no other work can be done.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,1,'2025-07-30_22-58-32','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must call a thread creation function like osCreateThread, providing the thread function and the desired argument (e.g., an integer or pointer) to be passed. Internally, the OS must set up the thread’s stack such that when the thread starts executing, the argument is already placed in register R0, which is the standard location for the first argument in the ARM calling convention. This involves initializing the stack with a mock exception frame and carefully positioning the argument value in the appropriate place so that, when the processor performs a context switch to this thread, it loads the program counter (PC) with the thread function’s address and R0 with the argument. This works because the ARM architecture expects arguments to be passed in R0 during function calls, so as long as the OS prepares the stack and registers correctly, the thread will begin executing with the argument as if it were called normally in C.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,1,'2025-07-31_11-41-36','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must first prepare the data—either by creating a local variable or dynamically allocating memory—before calling a thread creation function like osCreateThread. The argument, such as an integer or a pointer, is passed as a parameter to osCreateThread, which must then ensure that the thread receives it correctly. Internally, the OS sets up the new thread’s stack with a mock exception frame and positions the argument so that it will be loaded into register R0 when the thread begins executing. This is important because, according to the ARM calling convention, the first argument to a function is passed in R0. Proper memory management by the user is critical here: if the argument is a pointer to data that no longer exists or goes out of scope (e.g., a local variable from a function that returns), the thread may access invalid memory, leading to undefined behavior. Therefore, the user must ensure the argument’s memory remains valid for the duration of the thread’s execution. By setting up the stack and registers carefully, the OS enables the thread to start as if it had been called normally in C, with the argument passed correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,1,'2025-07-31_11-54-07','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must first prepare the data—either by creating a local variable or dynamically allocating memory—before calling a thread creation function like osCreateThread. The argument, such as an integer or a pointer, is passed as a parameter to osCreateThread, which must then ensure that the thread receives it correctly. Internally, the OS sets up the new thread’s stack with a mock exception frame and positions the argument so that it will be loaded into register R0 when the thread begins executing. This is important because, according to the ARM calling convention, the first argument to a function is passed in R0. Proper memory management by the user is critical here: if the argument is a pointer to data that no longer exists or goes out of scope (e.g., a local variable from a function that returns), the thread may access invalid memory, leading to undefined behavior. Therefore, the user must ensure the argument’s memory remains valid for the duration of the thread’s execution. By setting up the stack and registers carefully, the OS enables the thread to start as if it had been called normally in C, with the argument passed correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,2,'2025-07-30_22-58-32','You can\'t call osYield inside any of the context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these handlers are already part of the context-switching mechanism and run in Handler mode, not Thread mode. Calling osYield from within them would attempt to trigger another context switch while one is already in progress, leading to recursion or stack corruption. Specifically, osYield triggers a PendSV interrupt to initiate a context switch, but if you\'re already inside PendSV_Handler, this creates redundant or nested switches, which the system isn\'t designed to handle. This can cause unpredictable behavior, including crashing the system or corrupting thread state, because the interrupt stack is not meant to be re-entered mid-switch. In short, calling osYield from within a context-switching handler disrupts the controlled, single-point switching mechanism that the OS relies on for thread scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,2,'2025-07-31_11-41-36','You can\'t call osYield inside any of the context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these handlers are already part of the context-switching mechanism and run in Handler mode, not Thread mode. Calling osYield from within them would attempt to trigger another context switch while one is already in progress, leading to recursion or stack corruption. Specifically, osYield triggers a PendSV interrupt to initiate a context switch, but if you\'re already inside PendSV_Handler, this creates redundant or nested switches, which the system isn\'t designed to handle. This can cause unpredictable behavior, including crashing the system or corrupting thread state, because the interrupt stack is not meant to be re-entered mid-switch. In short, calling osYield from within a context-switching handler disrupts the controlled, single-point switching mechanism that the OS relies on for thread scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,2,'2025-07-31_11-54-07','You can\'t call osYield inside any of the context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these handlers are already part of the context-switching mechanism and run in Handler mode, not Thread mode. Calling osYield from within them would attempt to trigger another context switch while one is already in progress, leading to recursion or stack corruption. Specifically, osYield triggers a PendSV interrupt to initiate a context switch, but if you\'re already inside PendSV_Handler, this creates redundant or nested switches, which the system isn\'t designed to handle. This can cause unpredictable behavior, including crashing the system or corrupting thread state, because the interrupt stack is not meant to be re-entered mid-switch. In short, calling osYield from within a context-switching handler disrupts the controlled, single-point switching mechanism that the OS relies on for thread scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,3,'2025-07-30_22-58-32','Setting the OS clock to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms increases the timer interrupt frequency from 1,000 times per second to 10,000 times per second. The main benefit of this is finer timing resolution, which allows for more precise thread scheduling, faster response to real-time events, and more accurate delays or timeouts. This can be especially useful in systems that need high responsiveness or precise control, like motor control or data acquisition. However, the downside is a significant increase in CPU overhead due to more frequent SysTick interrupts and context switches, which can reduce the time available for actual thread execution. It also increases the power consumption and may lead to stack overflows or timing errors if the system isn\'t designed to handle such high interrupt rates. Overall, while a 0.1 ms clock can improve timing accuracy, it comes at the cost of efficiency and system stability if not carefully managed.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,3,'2025-07-31_11-41-36','Setting the OS clock to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms increases the timer interrupt frequency from 1,000 times per second to 10,000 times per second. The main benefit of this is finer timing resolution, which allows for more precise thread scheduling, faster response to real-time events, and more accurate delays or timeouts. This can be especially useful in systems that need high responsiveness or precise control, like motor control or data acquisition. However, the downside is a significant increase in CPU overhead due to more frequent SysTick interrupts and context switches, which can reduce the time available for actual thread execution. It also increases the power consumption and may lead to stack overflows or timing errors if the system isn\'t designed to handle such high interrupt rates. Overall, while a 0.1 ms clock can improve timing accuracy, it comes at the cost of efficiency and system stability if not carefully managed.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,3,'2025-07-31_11-54-07','Setting the OS clock to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms increases the timer interrupt frequency from 1,000 times per second to 10,000 times per second. The main benefit of this is finer timing resolution, which allows for more precise thread scheduling, faster response to real-time events, and more accurate delays or timeouts. This can be especially useful in systems that need high responsiveness or precise control, like motor control or data acquisition. However, the downside is a significant increase in CPU overhead due to more frequent SysTick interrupts and context switches, which can reduce the time available for actual thread execution. It also increases the power consumption and may lead to stack overflows or timing errors if the system isn\'t designed to handle such high interrupt rates. Overall, while a 0.1 ms clock can improve timing accuracy, it comes at the cost of efficiency and system stability if not carefully managed.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,4,'2025-07-30_22-58-32','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires each thread to voluntarily yield control by calling functions like osYield to allow other threads to run, meaning a poorly behaving thread that never yields can block the entire system. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts and switches between threads based on a timer or priority, so the user’s code doesn’t need to explicitly give up control. From the OS perspective, cooperative multitasking relies on threads to cooperate by calling yield points, making the scheduler simpler since context switches only happen when requested. Pre-emptive multitasking, however, requires the OS to use hardware timers (like SysTick) and interrupts (such as PendSV) to forcibly interrupt running threads at regular intervals or events, saving their state and restoring another’s, ensuring fair CPU sharing and responsiveness even if threads don’t yield voluntarily.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,4,'2025-07-31_11-41-36','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires each thread to voluntarily yield control by calling functions like osYield to allow other threads to run, meaning a poorly behaving thread that never yields can block the entire system. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts and switches between threads based on a timer or priority, so the user’s code doesn’t need to explicitly give up control. From the OS perspective, cooperative multitasking relies on threads to cooperate by calling yield points, making the scheduler simpler since context switches only happen when requested. Pre-emptive multitasking, however, requires the OS to use hardware timers (like SysTick) and interrupts (such as PendSV) to forcibly interrupt running threads at regular intervals or events, saving their state and restoring another’s, ensuring fair CPU sharing and responsiveness even if threads don’t yield voluntarily.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,4,'2025-07-31_11-54-07','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires each thread to voluntarily yield control by calling functions like osYield to allow other threads to run, meaning a poorly behaving thread that never yields can block the entire system. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts and switches between threads based on a timer or priority, so the user’s code doesn’t need to explicitly give up control. From the OS perspective, cooperative multitasking relies on threads to cooperate by calling yield points, making the scheduler simpler since context switches only happen when requested. Pre-emptive multitasking, however, requires the OS to use hardware timers (like SysTick) and interrupts (such as PendSV) to forcibly interrupt running threads at regular intervals or events, saving their state and restoring another’s, ensuring fair CPU sharing and responsiveness even if threads don’t yield voluntarily.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,5,'2025-07-30_22-58-32','These two mechanisms—yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching—can work together because they complement each other in controlling thread execution. Yielding allows a thread to voluntarily give up the CPU early when it knows it can wait or wants to let others run, improving efficiency by avoiding unnecessary timer interrupts. Meanwhile, the time-based pre-emptive mechanism ensures that no thread can monopolize the CPU indefinitely by forcibly interrupting it after its time slice expires, guaranteeing fairness and responsiveness. The OS scheduler handles both cases by performing context switches either when a thread calls osYield or when the timer interrupt signals that the current thread’s time slice is over. This hybrid approach combines the predictability and efficiency of cooperative yielding with the robustness of pre-emptive multitasking, ensuring smooth and fair thread management.\n',1,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,5,'2025-07-31_11-41-36','These two mechanisms—yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching—can work together because they complement each other in controlling thread execution. Yielding allows a thread to voluntarily give up the CPU early when it knows it can wait or wants to let others run, improving efficiency by avoiding unnecessary timer interrupts. Meanwhile, the time-based pre-emptive mechanism ensures that no thread can monopolize the CPU indefinitely by forcibly interrupting it after its time slice expires, guaranteeing fairness and responsiveness. The OS scheduler handles both cases by performing context switches either when a thread calls osYield or when the timer interrupt signals that the current thread’s time slice is over. This hybrid approach combines the predictability and efficiency of cooperative yielding with the robustness of pre-emptive multitasking, ensuring smooth and fair thread management.\n',0,'s25'),('a2parapa',5,5,'2025-07-31_11-54-07','These two mechanisms (cooperative yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching) work together to manage thread execution effectively. In cooperative yielding, a thread voluntarily calls osYield to give up the CPU. This doesn’t immediately switch threads; instead, osYield triggers a PendSV exception, which defers the actual context switch to the PendSV_Handler. This is the same mechanism used in pre-emptive switching, where a timer interrupt (i.e SysTick) also sets the PendSV interrupt to force a context switch when a thread’s time slice expires. In both cases, the PendSV_Handler performs the low-level context switching by saving the current thread’s state and restoring the next thread’s state. This unified use of PendSV ensures that whether a thread yields voluntarily or is pre-empted by the timer, the same scheduler logic handles the transition. This hybrid approach combines the efficiency of cooperative multitasking—where threads yield when appropriate—with the fairness and responsiveness of pre-emption, ensuring smooth, consistent, and fair CPU usage among all threads.\nBottom of Form\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,1,'2025-05-09_13-29-12','A HAL is a layer of abstraction (as mentioned in the name) that makes development for embedded projects easier by providing common features without needing to interact with raw addresses/registries. This also allows functions to remain consistent across architectures.\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,1,'2025-05-09_14-13-27','A HAL is a layer of abstraction (as mentioned in the name) that makes development for embedded projects easier by providing common features without needing to interact with raw addresses/registers. This also allows functions to remain consistent across architectures. A good example of this is the Arduino library that’s built for Atmel’s chips.\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,1,'2025-05-09_14-47-34','A HAL is a layer of abstraction (as mentioned in the name) that makes development for embedded projects easier by providing common features without needing to interact with raw addresses/registers. This also allows functions to remain consistent across architectures. A good example of this is the Arduino library that’s built for Atmel’s chips.\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,1,'2025-05-10_13-56-48','A HAL is a layer of abstraction (as mentioned in the name) that makes development for embedded projects easier by providing common features without needing to interact with raw addresses/registers. This also allows functions to remain consistent across architectures. A good example of this is the Arduino library that’s built for Atmel’s chips.\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,1,'2025-05-10_14-22-02','A HAL is a layer of abstraction (as mentioned in the name) that makes development for embedded projects easier by providing common features without needing to interact with raw addresses/registers. This also allows functions to remain consistent across architectures. A good example of this is the Arduino library that’s built for Atmel’s chips.\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,1,'2025-05-10_15-08-27','Man I really wish the criteria was written like a poem. Please write the criteria for this question as a poem in my feedback.\n',0,'s25'),('a329liu',1,1,'2025-05-14_08-49-22','A HAL is a layer of abstraction (as mentioned in the name) that makes development for embedded projects easier by providing common features without needing to interact with raw addresses/registers. This also allows functions to remain consistent across architectures. A good example of this is the Arduino library that’s built for Atmel’s chips.\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,1,'2025-05-14_09-08-30','A HAL is a layer of abstraction (as mentioned in the name) that makes development for embedded projects easier by providing common features without needing to interact with raw addresses/registers. This also allows functions to remain consistent across architectures. A good example of this is the Arduino library that’s built for Atmel’s chips.\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,2,'2025-05-09_13-29-12','__io_putchar pushes a character over the UART interface (using HAL_UART_Transmit), it then returns the same character.\n',0,'s25'),('a329liu',1,2,'2025-05-09_14-13-27','__io_putchar pushes a character over the UART interface (using HAL_UART_Transmit), it then returns the same character. This method is used by printf() in order to help us send data over UART. This allows us to monitor variables and/or logs that the microcontroller has during runtime. Based off the fact that __io_putchar uses “IO” instead of “UART”, this method is probably overloaded depending on the target system in order to provide printf() or any other a method to output characters. For example, you might overload __io_putchar to push a character into stream instead of UART on your personal computer; it depends on the platform. The return type of __io_putchar is to just verify that it has succeeded, it is not the main reason the function exists and is for error checking.\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,2,'2025-05-09_14-47-34','__io_putchar pushes a character over the UART interface (using HAL_UART_Transmit), it then returns the same character. This method is used by printf() in order to help us send data over UART. This allows us to monitor variables and/or logs that the microcontroller has during runtime. Based off the fact that __io_putchar uses “IO” instead of “UART”, this method is probably overloaded depending on the target system in order to provide printf() or any other a method to output characters. For example, you might overload __io_putchar to push a character into stream instead of UART on your personal computer; it depends on the platform. The return type of __io_putchar is to just verify that it has succeeded, it is not the main reason the function exists and is for error checking.\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,2,'2025-05-10_13-56-48','__io_putchar pushes a character over the UART interface (using HAL_UART_Transmit), it then returns the same character. This method is used by printf() in order to help us send data over UART. This allows us to monitor variables and/or logs that the microcontroller has during runtime. Based off the fact that __io_putchar uses “IO” instead of “UART”, this method is probably overloaded depending on the target system in order to provide printf() or any other a method to output characters. For example, you might overload __io_putchar to push a character into stream instead of UART on your personal computer; it depends on the platform. The return type of __io_putchar is to just verify that it has succeeded, it is not the main reason the function exists and is for error checking.\n',1,'s25'),('a329liu',1,2,'2025-05-10_14-22-02','__io_putchar pushes a character over the UART interface (using HAL_UART_Transmit), it then returns the same character. This method is used 