 the linker properly connect everything into a final executable.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,2,'2025-06-25_15-30-38','Using #include <kernel.c> or any other .c file is a bad idea because it goes against how the compiler is designed to work. When you include a .c file, the preprocessor literally pastes the entire contents of that file into the one you\'re compiling, which can lead to duplicate definitions of functions or variables and make the code harder to manage. Instead, each .c file should be compiled separately into its own object file, and only header files (.h) should be included to share declarations between files. This keeps the build process organized, avoids errors, and lets the linker properly connect everything into a final executable.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,2,'2025-06-26_14-38-08','Using #include <kernel.c> or any other .c file is a bad idea because it goes against how the compiler is designed to work. When you include a .c file, the preprocessor literally pastes the entire contents of that file into the one you\'re compiling, which can lead to duplicate definitions of functions or variables and make the code harder to manage. Instead, each .c file should be compiled separately into its own object file, and only header files (.h) should be included to share declarations between files. This keeps the build process organized, avoids errors, and lets the linker properly connect everything into a final executable.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,3,'2025-06-23_17-29-05','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, you begin by initializing the kernel, which prepares any necessary data structures and internal variables the operating system needs. Next, you write a thread function that contains the code you want the thread to run. This function must follow the expected format, typically taking a void* parameter and returning nothing. After that, you create the thread by calling a kernel function that allocates a stack and sets up the thread’s context so it’s ready to run. Finally, you start the kernel, which begins executing the thread by switching to its stack and running the function you defined. If everything is set up correctly, the thread will begin executing, and you can observe its behavior, usually through printed output.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,3,'2025-06-23_17-40-42','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, you begin by initializing the kernel, which prepares any necessary data structures and internal variables the operating system needs. Next, you write a thread function that contains the code you want the thread to run. This function must follow the expected format, typically taking a void* parameter and returning nothing. After that, you create the thread by calling a kernel function that allocates a stack and sets up the thread’s context so it’s ready to run. Finally, you start the kernel, which begins executing the thread by switching to its stack and running the function you defined. If everything is set up correctly, the thread will begin executing, and you can observe its behavior, usually through printed output.\n',0,'s25'),('a382chen',3,3,'2025-06-23_18-02-30','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, you begin by initializing the kernel, which prepares the internal data structures and variables needed by the operating system. Then, you write a thread function that contains the code you want the thread to execute. This function must follow the required format, typically taking a void* argument and returning nothing. When creating the thread, you provide the address of this function to the kernel, which is how the operating system knows what code the thread should run. Supplying the address is different from just writing the function, because the kernel needs a reference it can use to begin execution. After the thread is created and properly set up with its own stack, you start the kernel, which begins executing the thread by switching to its context and calling the function at the provided address. If everything is done correctly, the thread will start running and its behavior can be observed.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,3,'2025-06-25_11-46-06','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, you begin by initializing the kernel, which prepares the internal data structures and variables needed by the operating system. Then, you write a thread function that contains the code you want the thread to execute. This function must follow the required format, typically taking a void* argument and returning nothing. When creating the thread, you provide the address of this function to the kernel, which is how the operating system knows what code the thread should run. Supplying the address is different from just writing the function, because the kernel needs a reference it can use to begin execution. After the thread is created and properly set up with its own stack, you start the kernel, which begins executing the thread by switching to its context and calling the function at the provided address. If everything is done correctly, the thread will start running and its behavior can be observed.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,3,'2025-06-25_13-34-10','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, you begin by initializing the kernel, which prepares the internal data structures and variables needed by the operating system. Then, you write a thread function that contains the code you want the thread to execute. This function must follow the required format, typically taking a void* argument and returning nothing. When creating the thread, you provide the address of this function to the kernel, which is how the operating system knows what code the thread should run. Supplying the address is different from just writing the function, because the kernel needs a reference it can use to begin execution. After the thread is created and properly set up with its own stack, you start the kernel, which begins executing the thread by switching to its context and calling the function at the provided address. If everything is done correctly, the thread will start running and its behavior can be observed.\n',0,'s25'),('a382chen',3,3,'2025-06-25_15-05-18','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, you begin by initializing the kernel, which prepares the internal data structures and variables needed by the operating system. Then, you write a thread function that contains the code you want the thread to execute. This function must follow the required format, typically taking a void* argument and returning nothing. When creating the thread, you provide the address of this function to the kernel, which is how the operating system knows what code the thread should run. Supplying the address is different from just writing the function, because the kernel needs a reference it can use to begin execution. After the thread is created and properly set up with its own stack, you start the kernel, which begins executing the thread by switching to its context and calling the function at the provided address. If everything is done correctly, the thread will start running and its behavior can be observed.\n',0,'s25'),('a382chen',3,3,'2025-06-25_15-30-38','From a user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves several key steps that require the use of specific function calls. First, the user must call a function to initialize the kernel, which prepares the necessary internal data structures and resources for thread management. Next, the user writes a thread function that contains the code to be executed by the thread. This function must follow a specific format, usually taking a void pointer as an argument and returning nothing. To create the thread, the user must call a thread creation function and pass the address of the thread function so the kernel knows where to begin execution. After the thread is created and assigned its own stack, the user calls another function to start the kernel. This function call begins the scheduling process and allows the thread to start running. Throughout this entire setup, function calls must be used to properly interact with the system’s threading features and ensure the thread executes as expected.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,3,'2025-06-26_14-38-08','From a user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves several key steps that require the use of specific function calls. First, the user must call a function to initialize the kernel, which prepares the necessary internal data structures and resources for thread management. Next, the user writes a thread function that contains the code to be executed by the thread. This function must follow a specific format, usually taking a void pointer as an argument and returning nothing. To create the thread, the user must call a thread creation function and pass the address of the thread function so the kernel knows where to begin execution. After the thread is created and assigned its own stack, the user calls another function to start the kernel. This function call begins the scheduling process and allows the thread to start running. Throughout this entire setup, function calls must be used to properly interact with the system’s threading features and ensure the thread executes as expected.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,4,'2025-06-23_17-29-05','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory set aside specifically for storing the stacks of multiple threads. Each thread in the operating system needs its own separate stack space to keep track of local variables, return addresses, and function calls. In this lab, the stack pool ensures that every thread gets a unique section of memory for its stack, preventing interference between threads. In future labs, this stack pool will allow us to support multiple threads by dividing it into fixed-size sections and assigning each new thread its own stack. This organized approach makes it possible for the OS to switch between threads safely, as each one will have its own dedicated memory space to resume from.\n',0,'s25'),('a382chen',3,4,'2025-06-23_17-40-42','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory used to provide stack space for both the operating system and user-created threads. Each thread needs its own separate stack to store local variables, return addresses, and function call data, and the stack pool ensures that each thread gets a unique section of memory to prevent overlap. In this lab, the stack pool is used to allocate memory for a single thread, but in future labs it will be divided into equal-sized sections so multiple threads can be supported. The operating system also uses the stack pool for its own internal operations, such as handling system calls and managing context switching. Including the operating system’s use of the stack pool gives a complete picture of how this memory region supports both the execution of threads and the core functions of the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,4,'2025-06-23_18-02-30','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory used to provide stack space for both the operating system and user-created threads. Each thread needs its own separate stack to store local variables, return addresses, and function call data, and the stack pool ensures that each thread gets a unique section of memory to prevent overlap. In this lab, the stack pool is used to allocate memory for a single thread, but in future labs it will be divided into equal-sized sections so multiple threads can be supported. The operating system also uses the stack pool for its own internal operations, such as handling system calls and managing context switching. Including the operating system’s use of the stack pool gives a complete picture of how this memory region supports both the execution of threads and the core functions of the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,4,'2025-06-25_11-46-06','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory used to provide stack space for both the operating