lap. In this lab, the stack pool is used to allocate memory for a single thread, but in future labs it will be divided into equal-sized sections so multiple threads can be supported. The operating system also uses the stack pool for its own internal operations, such as handling system calls and managing context switching. Including the operating system’s use of the stack pool gives a complete picture of how this memory region supports both the execution of threads and the core functions of the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,4,'2025-06-25_15-30-38','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory used to provide stack space for both the operating system and user-created threads. Each thread needs its own separate stack to store local variables, return addresses, and function call data, and the stack pool ensures that each thread gets a unique section of memory to prevent overlap. In this lab, the stack pool is used to allocate memory for a single thread, but in future labs it will be divided into equal-sized sections so multiple threads can be supported. The operating system also uses the stack pool for its own internal operations, such as handling system calls and managing context switching. Including the operating system’s use of the stack pool gives a complete picture of how this memory region supports both the execution of threads and the core functions of the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,4,'2025-06-26_14-38-08','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory used to provide stack space for both the operating system and user-created threads. Each thread needs its own separate stack to store local variables, return addresses, and function call data, and the stack pool ensures that each thread gets a unique section of memory to prevent overlap. In this lab, the stack pool is used to allocate memory for a single thread, but in future labs it will be divided into equal-sized sections so multiple threads can be supported. The operating system also uses the stack pool for its own internal operations, such as handling system calls and managing context switching. Including the operating system’s use of the stack pool gives a complete picture of how this memory region supports both the execution of threads and the core functions of the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,5,'2025-06-23_17-29-05','The “user” refers to the part of the code that defines what the program should do, such as thread functions and the main function itself. This code goes into the main.c file. The “kernel” contains the low-level operating system functions that manage threads, stacks, and system behavior, and these go into kernel.c and are declared in kernel.h. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows the user to write and manage their program without needing to understand or modify the internal workings of the operating system. It keeps the interface clear and organized, making it easier to write, test, and expand user programs while relying on the kernel to handle system level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,5,'2025-06-23_17-40-42','The “user” refers to the part of the code that defines what the program should do, such as thread functions and the main function itself. This code goes into the main.c file. The “kernel” contains the low-level operating system functions that manage threads, stacks, and system behavior, and these go into kernel.c and are declared in kernel.h. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows the user to write and manage their program without needing to understand or modify the internal workings of the operating system. It keeps the interface clear and organized, making it easier to write, test, and expand user programs while relying on the kernel to handle system level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,5,'2025-06-23_18-02-30','The “user” refers to the part of the code that defines what the program should do, such as thread functions and the main function itself. This code goes into the main.c file. The “kernel” contains the low-level operating system functions that manage threads, stacks, and system behavior, and these go into kernel.c and are declared in kernel.h. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows the user to write and manage their program without needing to understand or modify the internal workings of the operating system. It keeps the interface clear and organized, making it easier to write, test, and expand user programs while relying on the kernel to handle system level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,5,'2025-06-25_11-46-06','The “user” refers to the part of the code that defines what the program should do, such as thread functions and the main function itself. This code goes into the main.c file. The “kernel” contains the low-level operating system functions that manage threads, stacks, and system behavior, and these go into kernel.c and are declared in kernel.h. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows the user to write and manage their program without needing to understand or modify the internal workings of the operating system. It keeps the interface clear and organized, making it easier to write, test, and expand user programs while relying on the kernel to handle system level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,5,'2025-06-25_13-34-10','The “user” refers to the part of the code that defines what the program should do, such as thread functions and the main function itself. This code goes into the main.c file. The “kernel” contains the low-level operating system functions that manage threads, stacks, and system behavior, and these go into kernel.c and are declared in kernel.h. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows the user to write and manage their program without needing to understand or modify the internal workings of the operating system. It keeps the interface clear and organized, making it easier to write, test, and expand user programs while relying on the kernel to handle system level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,5,'2025-06-25_15-05-18','The “user” refers to the part of the code that defines what the program should do, such as thread functions and the main function itself. This code goes into the main.c file. The “kernel” contains the low-level operating system functions that manage threads, stacks, and system behavior, and these go into kernel.c and are declared in kernel.h. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows the user to write and manage their program without needing to understand or modify the internal workings of the operating system. It keeps the interface clear and organized, making it easier to write, test, and expand user programs while relying on the kernel to handle system level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,5,'2025-06-25_15-30-38','The “user” refers to the part of the code that defines what the program should do, such as thread functions and the main function itself. This code goes into the main.c file. The “kernel” contains the low-level operating system functions that manage threads, stacks, and system behavior, and these go into kernel.c and are declared in kernel.h. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows the user to write and manage their program without needing to understand or modify the internal workings of the operating system. It keeps the interface clear and organized, making it easier to write, test, and expand user programs while relying on the kernel to handle system level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',3,5,'2025-06-26_14-38-08','The “user” refers to the part of the code that defines what the program should do, such as thread functions and the main function itself. This code goes into the main.c file. The “kernel” contains the low-level operating system functions that manage threads, stacks, and system behavior, and these go into kernel.c and are declared in kernel.h. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows the user to write and manage their program without needing to understand or modify the internal workings of the operating system. It keeps the interface clear and organized, making it easier to write, test, and expand user programs while relying on the kernel to handle system level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,1,'2025-07-10_21-19-18','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling allows threads to be processed in the order they arrive. After a thread finishes its time slice, it is moved to the back of the queue, and the next thread at the front is scheduled to run. This approach maintains fairness and ensures that all threads get equal CPU time without needing to manage array indices manually. Compared to a static array, a queue offers more flexibility in dynamically adding or removing threads at runtime. It also simplifies the scheduler logic by avoiding index calculations and directly reflecting the circular nature of round-robin scheduling.\n',0,'s25'),('a382chen',4,1,'2025-07-10_21-30-14','Using a queue in round-robin scheduling means placing each ready thread at the back of the line when its time slice ends and pulling the next thread from the front, so you never have to handle manual index wrap-arounds or modulo operations. This also makes it easy to insert or remove threads at any point without shifting array elements. On a Cortex-M4, the trade-off is that pointer updates and potential linked-list logic add code size and may lead to extra cache misses. A practical compromise is to use a fixed-size ring buffer of thread pointers, which preserves constant-time enqueue and dequeue operations and keeps memory usage predictable, all without relying on dynamic allocation.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,1,'2025-07-10_21-35-38','Using a queue in round-robin scheduling means placing each ready thread at the back of the line when its time slice ends and pulling the next thread from the front, so you never have to handle manual index wrap-arounds or modulo operations. This also makes it easy to insert or remove threads at any point without shifting array elements. On a Cortex-M4, the trade-off is that pointer updates and potential linked-list logic add code size and may lead to extra cache misses. A practical compromise is to use a fixed-size ring buffer of thread pointers, which preserves constant-time enqueue and dequeue operations and keeps memory usage predictable, all without relying on dynamic allocation.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,1,'2025-07-10_21-43-10','Using a queue in round-robin scheduling means placing each ready thread at the back of the line when its time slice ends and pulling the next thread from the front, so you never have to handle manual index wrap-arounds or modulo operations. This also makes it easy to insert or remove threads at any point without shifting array elements. On a Cortex-M4, the trade-off is that pointer updates and potential linked-list logic add code size and may lead to extra cache misses. A practical compromise is to use a fixed-size ring buffer of thread pointers, which preserves constant-time enqueue and dequeue operations and keeps memory usage predictable, all without relying on dynamic allocation.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,1,'2025-07-10_21-49-24','Using a queue in round-robin scheduling means placing each ready thread at the back of the line when its time slice ends and pulling the next thread from the front, so you never have to handle manual index wrap-arounds or modulo operations. This also makes it easy to insert or remove threads at any point without shifting array elements. On a Cortex-M4, the trade-off is that pointer updates and potential linked-list logic add code size and may lead to extra cache misses. A practical compromise is to use a fixed-size ring buffer of thread pointers, which preserves constant-time enqueue and dequeue operations and keeps memory usage predictable, all without relying on dynamic allocation.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,2,'2025-07-10_21-19-18','The PendSV_Handler is triggered when a context switch is needed, such as when a thread yields control. It first saves the current thread’s registers (R4 to R11) onto its stack so that it can resume later without losing its state. Then, it updates the thread’s control block with the current stack pointer. After that, it selects the next thread to run, restores its saved stack pointer, and pops its registers back into the CPU. Finally, it returns from the interrupt, allowing the new thread to continue execution right where it left off.\n',0,'s25'),('a382chen',4,2,'2025-07-10_21-30-14','At entry, PendSV_Handler reads the process stack pointer (PSP) to establish the current thread’s stack location, then calls osSched to decide which thread should run next. It pushes the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto the current thread’s stack, stores the updated PSP value into that thread’s control block, and loads the PSP value from the chosen thread’s control block. Finally, it pops R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack and executes BX LR to return from the exception, resuming the new thread exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,2,'2025-07-10_21-35-38','At entry, PendSV_Handler reads the process stack pointer (PSP) to establish the current thread’s stack location, then calls osSched to decide which thread should run next. It pushes the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto the current thread’s stack, stores the updated PSP value into that thread’s control block, and loads the PSP value from the chosen thread’s control block. Finally, it pops R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack and executes BX LR to return from the exception, resuming the new thread exactly where it left off.\n',0,'s25'),('a382chen',4,2,'2025-07-10_21-43-10','In PendSV_Handler, the first step is to push the callee-saved registers R4 through R11 onto the process stack, preserving the current thread’s context before any decisions are made. Next, the handler reads the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) and writes that value into the current thread’s TCB so its exact stack location is recorded. Only after the context is safely stored does it call osSched to select the next thread. Once osSched returns, the handler loads the chosen thread’s saved PSP from its TCB, pops R4 through R11 from that thread’s stack to restore its register state, and then performs the exception return, which restores the remaining registers and transfers control to the new thread. If osSched ran before saving the registers, the outgoing thread’s state could be lost or corrupted, making a later resume unreliable. Maintaining this strict order of save, schedule, restore is critical to ensure each thread’s state remains intact through the context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('a382chen',4,2,'2025-07-10_21-49-24','On entry, PendSV_Handler first reads the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) into a register so it captures the exact start of the current thread’s stack. It then pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 onto that stack, adjusting the PSP accordingly, and writes the updated PSP value back into the thread’s control block. Only once the full context is safely stored does it call osSched to pick the next thread. After osSched returns, the handler loads the new thread’s saved PSP from its TCB, pops R4–R11 to restore its registers, and performs the exception return to reload the remaining registers and resume execution. Reading the PSP before pushing is crucial—if you push first and then read, you’d record a shifted pointer and later restore the wrong stack frame, corrupting the thread’s state and making a correct resume impossible.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,3,'2025-07-10_21-19-18','Always choosing the highest-priority task can lead to starvation, where lower-priority tasks never get a chance to run if a higher-priority task is always ready. This breaks fairness and can cause important background tasks, like I/O handling or memory cleanup, to be ignored indefinitely. It also makes the system less predictable and harder to debug, especially if one task unintentionally holds the CPU for too long. A better approach is to use priority-based scheduling with time slicing or aging, so all tasks get CPU time while still respecting their priority levels.\n',0,'s25'),('a382chen',4,3,'2025-07-10_21-30-14','If the scheduler always picks the ready thread with the highest priority, then as long as that top‐priority task remains ready it will be chosen again and again, and no lower‐priority thread will ever get CPU time. In effect you end up with a single task running indefinitely while all others starve. Critical work like I/O handling or cleanup would be postponed forever, undermining system responsiveness and predictability. To avoid this, priority scheduling must be combined with mechanisms such as time slicing among equal‐priority tasks or priority aging so that lower‐priority threads still receive service.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,3,'2025-07-10_21-35-38','If the scheduler always picks the ready thread with the highest priority, then as long as that top‐priority task remains ready it will be chosen again and again, and no lower‐priority thread will ever get CPU time. In effect you end up with a single task running indefinitely while all others starve. Critical work like I/O handling or cleanup would be postponed forever, undermining system responsiveness and predictability. To avoid this, priority scheduling must be combined with mechanisms such as time slicing among equal‐priority tasks or priority aging so that lower‐priority threads still receive service.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,3,'2025-07-10_21-43-10','If the scheduler always picks the ready thread with the highest priority, then as long as that top‐priority task remains ready it will be chosen again and again, and no lower‐priority thread will ever get CPU time. In effect you end up with a single task running indefinitely while all others starve. Critical work like I/O handling or cleanup would be postponed forever, undermining system responsiveness and predictability. To avoid this, priority scheduling must be combined with mechanisms such as time slicing among equal‐priority tasks or priority aging so that lower‐priority threads still receive service.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,3,'2025-07-10_21-49-24','If the scheduler always picks the ready thread with the highest priority, then as long as that top‐priority task remains ready it will be chosen again and again, and no lower‐priority thread will ever get CPU time. In effect you end up with a single task running indefinitely while all others starve. Critical work like I/O handling or cleanup would be postponed forever, undermining system responsiveness and predictability. To avoid this, priority scheduling must be combined with mechanisms such as time slicing among equal‐priority tasks or priority aging so that lower‐priority threads still receive service.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,4,'2025-07-10_21-19-18','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain from the assembly handlers makes the code much easier to read, write, and maintain. C provides better abstraction and structure, which helps with debugging and reduces the chance of low-level mistakes. It also makes the logic more portable and understandable to others who may not be familiar with ARM assembly. However, switching between assembly and C adds overhead, especially when preserving registers and managing the stack, which can slightly affect performance. Writing everything in assembly would be faster and give more control, but it would make the code harder to manage and more error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,4,'2025-07-10_21-30-14','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain from the assembly handlers makes the code much easier to read, write, and maintain. C provides better abstraction and structure, which helps with debugging and reduces the chance of low-level mistakes. It also makes the logic more portable and understandable to others who may not be familiar with ARM assembly. However, switching between assembly and C adds overhead, especially when preserving registers and managing the stack, which can slightly affect performance. Writing everything in assembly would be faster and give more control, but it would make the code harder to manage and more error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,4,'2025-07-10_21-35-38','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain from the assembly handlers makes the code much easier to read, write, and maintain. C provides better abstraction and structure, which helps with debugging and reduces the chance of low-level mistakes. It also makes the logic more portable and understandable to others who may not be familiar with ARM assembly. However, switching between assembly and C adds overhead, especially when preserving registers and managing the stack, which can slightly affect performance. Writing everything in assembly would be faster and give more control, but it would make the code harder to manage and more error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,4,'2025-07-10_21-43-10','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain from the assembly handlers makes the code much easier to read, write, and maintain. C provides better abstraction and structure, which helps with debugging and reduces the chance of low-level mistakes. It also makes the logic more portable and understandable to others who may not be familiar with ARM assembly. However, switching between assembly and C adds overhead, especially when preserving registers and managing the stack, which can slightly affect performance. Writing everything in assembly would be faster and give more control, but it would make the code harder to manage and more error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,4,'2025-07-10_21-49-24','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain from the assembly handlers makes the code much easier to read, write, and maintain. C provides better abstraction and structure, which helps with debugging and reduces the chance of low-level mistakes. It also makes the logic more portable and understandable to others who may not be familiar with ARM assembly. However, switching between assembly and C adds overhead, especially when preserving registers and managing the stack, which can slightly affect performance. Writing everything in assembly would be faster and give more control, but it would make the code harder to manage and more error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,5,'2025-07-10_21-19-18','Yes, the OS will still work with a single thread, but it will behave differently. Since there is only one thread in the system, yielding will cause a context switch back to the same thread. The PendSV handler will save and restore the same thread’s context, which adds some unnecessary overhead but does not break the system. However, because there are no other threads to run, the CPU will keep switching back to the same thread repeatedly. This setup is useful for testing the context switch mechanism but defeats the purpose of multitasking, as there is no actual sharing of CPU time between multiple tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,5,'2025-07-10_21-30-14','Yes, the OS will still work with a single thread, but it will behave differently. Since there is only one thread in the system, yielding will cause a context switch back to the same thread. The PendSV handler will save and restore the same thread’s context, which adds some unnecessary overhead but does not break the system. However, because there are no other threads to run, the CPU will keep switching back to the same thread repeatedly. This setup is useful for testing the context switch mechanism but defeats the purpose of multitasking, as there is no actual sharing of CPU time between multiple tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,5,'2025-07-10_21-35-38','Yes, the OS will still work with a single thread, but it will behave differently. Since there is only one thread in the system, yielding will cause a context switch back to the same thread. The PendSV handler will save and restore the same thread’s context, which adds some unnecessary overhead but does not break the system. However, because there are no other threads to run, the CPU will keep switching back to the same thread repeatedly. This setup is useful for testing the context switch mechanism but defeats the purpose of multitasking, as there is no actual sharing of CPU time between multiple tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,5,'2025-07-10_21-43-10','Yes, the OS will still work with a single thread, but it will behave differently. Since there is only one thread in the system, yielding will cause a context switch back to the same thread. The PendSV handler will save and restore the same thread’s context, which adds some unnecessary overhead but does not break the system. However, because there are no other threads to run, the CPU will keep switching back to the same thread repeatedly. This setup is useful for testing the context switch mechanism but defeats the purpose of multitasking, as there is no actual sharing of CPU time between multiple tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',4,5,'2025-07-10_21-49-24','Yes, the OS will still work with a single thread, but it will behave differently. Since there is only one thread in the system, yielding will cause a context switch back to the same thread. The PendSV handler will save and restore the same thread’s context, which adds some unnecessary overhead but does not break the system. However, because there are no other threads to run, the CPU will keep switching back to the same thread repeatedly. This setup is useful for testing the context switch mechanism but defeats the purpose of multitasking, as there is no actual sharing of CPU time between multiple tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',5,1,'2025-07-26_19-37-54','When you create a thread you hand the scheduler a single void * that points at whatever data you need. The kernel copies that pointer into the R0 field of the thread’s freshly built stack frame, right next to the saved program counter and status word. R0 is the register the ARM calling convention uses for the first argument, so when the scheduler later pops that frame the pointer is waiting in the correct register and the C entry function receives it exactly as if the thread had been called by ordinary code. Inside the thread you just cast the pointer back to its real type and carry on. The lab notes spell out both the stack layout and the choice of a single pointer argument to avoid guessing how many parameters a user might need.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',5,2,'2025-07-26_19-37-54','PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, and SysTick_Handler are already running in handler mode while the kernel is either saving or restoring a context. If you call osYield inside any of them you would request an SVC interrupt before the first context switch has finished, effectively stacking a second scheduler invocation on top of half‑updated data structures. The lab makes it clear that yielding is something threads do, not something the timer or PendSV code itself should attempt, and it reminds you to let those handlers finish their work before anything else happens. Nesting the calls can corrupt the saved register frame or the ready list and usually ends in a hard fault, which is why it is forbidden.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',5,3,'2025-07-26_19-37-54','The current design fires SysTick every millisecond, which means the OS can never notice or time anything finer than that interval. Shortening the period to 0.1 ms would give ten times better timer resolution and quicker response to deadlines, but it would also make the CPU service ten times as many interrupts, increase context-switch overhead, raise power consumption, and force you to revisit every time-slice constant in the code base. Whether the gain in latency is worth the extra load depends on how hard-real-time your application really is.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',5,4,'2025-07-26_19-37-54','From the programmer’s point of view, co‑operative multitasking means each thread must remember to call osYield or block; if it forgets, nothing else runs. In pre‑emptive multitasking the timer interrupt decrements a runtime counter every tick and, when the counter reaches zero, the kernel raises PendSV to move to another thread, so the application can ignore scheduling concerns. The lab emphasises that, once pre‑emption is enabled, threads no longer need to yield although they still may if they want to give up the CPU early. That autonomy comes at the cost of the extra interrupt and context‑save work the OS must now perform on every tick.\n',1,'s25'),('a382chen',5,5,'2025-07-26_19-37-54','Both voluntary yielding and time‑slice expiry end up in exactly the same place: they set the PendSV bit and reset the thread’s runtime counter. When a thread calls osYield, the kernel reloads the counter and pends PendSV right away so another task can run. Separately, each SysTick interrupt subtracts one from the running task’s counter and, wh==en it hits zero, does the same reload‑and‑pend sequence. Because both paths share the same bookkeeping and the same context‑switch code, a co‑operative thread can give up the CPU early while a compute‑bound one is still pre‑empted automatically, letting the two strategies work together without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',1,1,'2025-06-26_17-30-27','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is an intermediate layer of software that lies between the user application code and the hardware specific components in an embedded system such as a microcontroller. The HAL acts as an intermediary that provides a standardize and simplified interface for the user to interact with hardware peripherals and embedded level software. Protocols and serial interfaces like UART, SPI, I2C, and ADCs are made much easier to interact with using a HAL. This effectively hides the complex intricacies with direct register manipulation and involving memory management associated with embedded programming at a bare-metal level. Instead of writing to specific memory addresses and bit-shifting values into control registers, functions like HAL_GPIO_WritePin() or HAL_UART_Transmit() that handle all the low-level details, adding a layer of abstraction to the software, hardware interaction.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',1,2,'2025-06-26_17-30-27','The __io_putchar function acts as a connection between the C standard library’s printf function and specific hardware output interfaces by sending a single character from the printf function to a specific hardware output. When printf is called in an embedded system, each character in the formatted string is passed individually to the __io_putchar function which then handles the transmission aspect to the specified hardware peripheral. In the implementation used in this assignment, __io_putchar routes characters to the UART interface, enabling text output to appear in a serial console on the user’s connected PC. This function is essential because printf and other standard C library output functions have no inherent knowledge of embedded hardware. Specific hardware expects the underlying system to provide a mechanism for character output. On a desktop computer, this might be handled by the operating system routing to a terminal window. In embedded systems, this pathway needs to be explicitly defined by implementing __io_putchar.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',1,3,'2025-06-26_17-30-27','The debugger was used in this lab because the printf function is not able to provide the full range of capabilities required for the real-time, low-level access needed for this lab. When examining stack pointers like MSP and PSP, exact values at specific instances in time, as well as how they change during execution are crucial pieces of information. printf only gives snapshots at specific points and modifies the system state each time it runs, effectively using stack space and changing registers. This means printf would interfere with the very things trying to be observed. The debugger allows for a pause in execution to examine processor registers, memory, and stack pointers without affecting the system state. It provides non-intrusive, real-time observation that\'s essential for understanding low-level processor behavior. Additionally, if there are stack or communication issues, printf might not work, whereas the debugger operates independently of the program\'s execution state.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',1,4,'2025-06-26_17-30-27','The stack allocation method used in this lab treats stack memory as a fixed-size region by using MSP_INIT_VAL as the starting reference point. Since stacks grow downward in ARM processors, the PSP is determined by subtracting a fixed offset (0x400 bytes) from the MSP\'s initial address. Knowing MSP_INIT_VAL is critical for memory safety. Without this reference point, the PSP could be placed in reserved memory regions, causing corruption or access violations. The initial MSP value ensures that the PSP can be allocated in valid stack memory space.\nThis downward allocation pattern works because ARM stacks grow from high to low addresses. By subtracting 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL, a separate process stack that doesn\'t overlap with the main stack. This separation is essential for multitasking systems where different threads or processes need isolated stack spaces. The fixed-offset approach provides predictable memory layout and prevents stack collisions, which is crucial for reliable threading and task management in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',1,5,'2025-06-26_17-30-27','The way to accomplish based on the approach taken from the lab is to use a stack allocation method that divides memory into fixed-size blocks, with each thread getting its own dedicated stack space. Starting from MSP_INIT_VAL, each thread\'s stack address is calculated using:\nADDRESS_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) × THREAD_STACK_SIZE\nThe formula places the main stack first, then allocates thread stacks sequentially above it in memory. Since j starts at 1, the first thread stack begins right after the main stack, the second thread stack follows that, and so on. Two critical requirements ensure this works safely would be 8-byte alignment and memory bound checking. 8-byte alignment ensures that THREAD_STACK_SIZE and MAIN_STACK_SIZE must be multiples of 8. The Cortex-M4 requires 8-byte stack alignment and violating this triggers a hard fault. Additionally, the total allocated memory (main stack + all thread stacks) must not exceed MAX_STACK_SIZE and must stay within valid RAM regions which is considered as memory bound checking. Some benefits of this approach include the fact that addresses are calculated at compile time, each thread gets isolated stack space with no overlap, and the predictable memory layout simplifies debugging and memory management.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',2,1,'2025-06-27_10-44-05','When the PC was instructed to hold the function pointer to print_continuously, this effectively performed the action of manually redirected CPU execution to that function\'s memory location. The process worked like this: the function address was loaded into R0, then the assembly jump function copied that value directly into the PC register. Since the PC determines the next instruction to execute, this immediately forced the CPU to start running print_continuously. This bypassed normal function calling conventions - no return address was saved, no registers were preserved, and no proper function entry occurred. This action essentially hijacked execution flow by directly manipulating the PC. Once the PC pointed to print_continuously, the CPU executed its infinite loop instructions. Since no return mechanism was established, the program got stuck in that loop permanently. By directly manipulating it, one can force the processor to jump to any valid memory address and execute code there, implementing custom control flow outside standard function call mechanisms.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',2,1,'2025-06-27_23-09-32','When the PC was instructed to hold the function pointer to print_continuously, this effectively performed the action of manually redirected CPU execution to that function\'s memory location. The process worked like this: the function address was loaded into R0, then the assembly jump function copied that value directly into the PC register. Since the PC determines the next instruction to execute, this immediately forced the CPU to start running print_continuously. This bypassed normal function calling conventions - no return address was saved, no registers were preserved, and no proper function entry occurred. This action essentially hijacked execution flow by directly manipulating the PC. Once the PC pointed to print_continuously, the CPU executed its infinite loop instructions. Since no return mechanism was established, the program got stuck in that loop permanently. By directly manipulating it, one can force the processor to jump to any valid memory address and execute code there, implementing custom control flow outside standard function call mechanisms.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',2,2,'2025-06-27_10-44-05','A system call is a controlled interface that allows programs at a user-operated level to request services from the operating system. The system call request acts as a bridge between application code and the OS kernel for operations that require more elevated and controlled privileges. When a program needs to perform tasks like reading files, allocating memory, or network communication duties, users cannot directly make these commands due to security and stability reasons. System calls can be used in this scenario, where a special software interrupt known as a trap switches the COU from user mode to privileged kernel mode. This mode switch is important because it allows the OS to safely execute operations that user programs shouldn’t handle directly. The kernel has access to hardware resources and enforces security policies, resource limitations, and data integrity checks, hence why it requires system calls for users to access such resources. For example, when the read() function is called to access a file, the program doesn\'t directly manipulate disk hardware. Instead, the system call hands control to the OS, which verifies permissions, manages file system structures, handles device drivers, and returns the requested data safely to the program. System calls provide several benefits: they prevent programs from corrupting system resources, enable resource sharing between multiple programs, maintain security boundaries, and provide a consistent interface regardless of underlying hardware differences. Common examples include open(), write(), malloc(), and fork().\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',2,2,'2025-06-27_23-09-32','A system call is a controlled interface that allows programs at a user-operated level to request services from the operating system. The system call request acts as a bridge between application code and the OS kernel for operations that require more elevated and controlled privileges. When a program needs to perform tasks like reading files, allocating memory, or network communication duties, users cannot directly make these commands due to security and stability reasons. System calls can be used in this scenario, where a special software interrupt known as a trap switches the COU from user mode to privileged kernel mode. This mode switch is important because it allows the OS to safely execute operations that user programs shouldn’t handle directly. The kernel has access to hardware resources and enforces security policies, resource limitations, and data integrity checks, hence why it requires system calls for users to access such resources. For example, when the read() function is called to access a file, the program doesn\'t directly manipulate disk hardware. Instead, the system call hands control to the OS, which verifies permissions, manages file system structures, handles device drivers, and returns the requested data safely to the program. System calls provide several benefits: they prevent programs from corrupting system resources, enable resource sharing between multiple programs, maintain security boundaries, and provide a consistent interface regardless of underlying hardware differences. Common examples include open(), write(), malloc(), and fork().\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',2,3,'2025-06-27_10-44-05','Setting up the thread\'s stack in this project involved pre-loading the stack memory with register values that simulated what would be there if the thread had been previously running and then interrupted. This is essentially creating a fake \"saved state\" for a thread that hasn\'t been executed yet. The setup process involves manually pushing specific values onto the new stack in a precise order: the program status register (xPSR), program counter (PC), link register (LR), and general-purpose registers R0-R12. These values are arranged exactly as the Cortex-M4 hardware would arrange them during a real context switch. These register values are stored on the stack because it enables context switching, which allows for the ability to pause one thread and resume another in a cohesive manner. When the OS switches between threads, it saves the current thread\'s register state to its stack and restores the next thread\'s register state from its stack. This allows multiple threads to share the same CPU while maintaining their individual execution contexts. The stack order is critical because the CPU\'s context-switching hardware expects registers in a specific sequence. When resuming a thread, the processor automatically pops values from the stack into registers. If values are stored in the wrong order, registers get loaded with incorrect data, causing unpredictable behavior or crashes. Pre-configuring the stack eliminates this risk, enabling the appearance of a brand-new thread to appear to the CPU as if it\'s simply resuming from where it left off, even though it\'s starting for the first time.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',2,3,'2025-06-27_23-09-32','Setting up the thread\'s stack in this project involved pre-loading the stack memory with register values that simulated what would be there if the thread had been previously running and then interrupted. This is essentially creating a fake \"saved state\" for a thread that hasn\'t been executed yet. The setup process involves manually pushing specific values onto the new stack in a precise order: the program status register (xPSR), program counter (PC), link register (LR), and general-purpose registers R0-R12. These values are arranged exactly as the Cortex-M4 hardware would arrange them during a real context switch. These register values are stored on the stack because it enables context switching, which allows for the ability to pause one thread and resume another in a cohesive manner. When the OS switches between threads, it saves the current thread\'s register state to its stack and restores the next thread\'s register state from its stack. This allows multiple threads to share the same CPU while maintaining their individual execution contexts. The stack order is critical because the CPU\'s context-switching hardware expects registers in a specific sequence. When resuming a thread, the processor automatically pops values from the stack into registers. If values are stored in the wrong order, registers get loaded with incorrect data, causing unpredictable behavior or crashes. Pre-configuring the stack eliminates this risk, enabling the appearance of a brand-new thread to appear to the CPU as if it\'s simply resuming from where it left off, even though it\'s starting for the first time.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',2,4,'2025-06-27_10-44-05','If __set_PSP wasn’t used to set the PSP using, the CPU wouldn\'t know where the thread\'s stack is located, causing it to access invalid memory. When switching to thread mode, the processor expects the PSP to point to a valid stack containing the thread\'s saved register values (PC, LR, xPSR, etc.). Without initializing PSP to the correct stack address, the CPU would attempt to pop register values from whatever random memory location PSP happened to contain. This creates several problems as the CPU might try to access protected or non-existent memory regions, load garbage values into critical registers like the PC (causing execution to jump to invalid addresses), or corrupt other parts of the system by writing to unintended memory locations. The Cortex-M4\'s memory protection unit detects these invalid memory accesses and triggers a hard fault which is the processor\'s way of handling critical errors that threaten system stability. The hard fault essentially stops program execution to prevent further damage. Calling __set_PSP with the correct stack address, ensures the processor can properly access the thread\'s pre-configured stack data, allowing normal context switching and thread execution to proceed safely.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',2,4,'2025-06-27_23-09-32','If __set_PSP wasn’t used to set the PSP using, the CPU wouldn\'t know where the thread\'s stack is located, causing it to access invalid memory. When switching to thread mode, the processor expects the PSP to point to a valid stack containing the thread\'s saved register values (PC, LR, xPSR, etc.). Without initializing PSP to the correct stack address, the CPU would attempt to pop register values from whatever random memory location PSP happened to contain. This creates several problems as the CPU might try to access protected or non-existent memory regions, load garbage values into critical registers like the PC (causing execution to jump to invalid addresses), or corrupt other parts of the system by writing to unintended memory locations. The Cortex-M4\'s memory protection unit detects these invalid memory accesses and triggers a hard fault which is the processor\'s way of handling critical errors that threaten system stability. The hard fault essentially stops program execution to prevent further damage. Calling __set_PSP with the correct stack address, ensures the processor can properly access the thread\'s pre-configured stack data, allowing normal context switching and thread execution to proceed safely.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',2,5,'2025-06-27_10-44-05','If the thread function printed once and returned, the CPU would try to return using the Link Register (LR), which contains the dummy value set during stack setup. Since there\'s no valid return address, the processor would jump to whatever invalid memory location was stored in LR which likely contains undefined memory or garbage data. When the CPU tries to execute instructions from this invalid location, it triggers a hard fault. This is why embedded threads typically use infinite loops rather than returning. Unlike desktop programs that can return to the operating system, embedded threads have no valid \"parent\" to return to. The dummy LR value works fine for continuous loops but causes crashes if the thread tries to return. To handle this properly, threads should either loop indefinitely or have their LR set to a valid cleanup routine for proper thread termination.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',2,5,'2025-06-27_23-09-32','If the thread function printed once and returned, the CPU would try to return using the Link Register (LR), which contains the dummy value set during stack setup. Since there\'s no valid return address, the processor would jump to whatever invalid memory location was stored in LR which likely contains undefined memory or garbage data. When the CPU tries to execute instructions from this invalid location, it triggers a hard fault. This is why embedded threads typically use infinite loops rather than returning. Unlike desktop programs that can return to the operating system, embedded threads have no valid \"parent\" to return to. The dummy LR value works fine for continuous loops but causes crashes if the thread tries to return. To handle this properly, threads should either loop indefinitely or have their LR set to a valid cleanup routine for proper thread termination.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',3,1,'2025-06-27_22-55-22','The compilation process transforms multiple *.c files into a single executable through three main stages: preprocessing, compilation, and linking. During preprocessing, each C file is processed individually. The preprocessor expands directives like #include and #define, removes comments, and inlines all macros and headers, producing clean C source code ready for compilation. The compilation stage converts each preprocessed .c file into separate object files (.o files). The compiler checks syntax, performs optimizations, and translates the C code into machine-level instructions. Each object file contains compiled code but with unresolved references to functions and variables from other files. Finally, the linker combines all object files into a single executable. It resolves cross-file references by connecting function calls and variable references between different object files. The linker also incorporates necessary standard library functions (like printf()) and creates the final executable with all dependencies resolved.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',3,1,'2025-06-28_00-05-45','The compilation process transforms multiple *.c files into a single executable through three main stages: preprocessing, compilation, and linking. During preprocessing, each C file is processed individually. The preprocessor expands directives like #include and #define, removes comments, and inlines all macros and headers, producing clean C source code ready for compilation. The compilation stage converts each preprocessed .c file into separate object files (.o files). The compiler checks syntax, performs optimizations, and translates the C code into machine-level instructions. Each object file contains compiled code but with unresolved references to functions and variables from other files. Finally, the linker combines all object files into a single executable. It resolves cross-file references by connecting function calls and variable references between different object files. The linker also incorporates necessary standard library functions (like printf()) and creates the final executable with all dependencies resolved.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',3,2,'2025-06-27_22-55-22','Using #include on *.c files causes the preprocessor to copy and paste the entire source file into the including file, creating multiple copies of the same code. This leads to duplicate symbol definitions where the same functions and variables get compiled multiple times, causing linker errors when it encounters multiple definitions of identical symbols. It also breaks proper modularity by bypassing the intended compilation process where source files should be compiled independently into separate object files, then linked together. The correct approach is using header files (.h) for #include directives. Headers contain only declarations (function prototypes, constants, type definitions) that tell the compiler what exists elsewhere, while implementations stay in separate .c files that compile independently and link together properly.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',3,2,'2025-06-28_00-05-45','Using #include on *.c files causes the preprocessor to copy and paste the entire source file into the including file, creating multiple copies of the same code. This leads to duplicate symbol definitions where the same functions and variables get compiled multiple times, causing linker errors when it encounters multiple definitions of identical symbols. It also breaks proper modularity by bypassing the intended compilation process where source files should be compiled independently into separate object files, then linked together. The correct approach is using header files (.h) for #include directives. Headers contain only declarations (function prototypes, constants, type definitions) that tell the compiler what exists elsewhere, while implementations stay in separate .c files that compile independently and link together properly.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',3,3,'2025-06-27_22-55-22','',0,'s25'),('a3chai',3,3,'2025-06-28_00-05-45','To set up and start a thread, first initialize the kernel to prepare internal data structures and allocate memory for thread stacks. Next, write a thread function containing the desired code to be executed, which should take a single void pointer parameter with no return value. Then create the thread by calling the thread creation function and passing it the function’s address, allowing the kernel to know what code to execute while setting up the thread\'s stack and initial context. After that, start the kernel by calling the kernel start function, which triggers a system call that switches to the thread\'s stack pointer and begins execution. Once complete, your thread runs independently from the main program, enabling concurrent execution with the kernel handling all low-level context switching and execution management details.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',3,4,'2025-06-27_22-55-22','To set up and start a thread, first initialize the kernel to prepare internal data structures and allocate memory for thread stacks. Next, write a thread function containing the desired code to be executed, which should take a single void pointer parameter with no return value. Then create the thread by calling the thread creation function and passing it the function’s address, allowing the kernel to know what code to execute while setting up the thread\'s stack and initial context. After that, start the kernel by calling the kernel start function, which triggers a system call that switches to the thread\'s stack pointer and begins execution. Once complete, your thread runs independently from the main program, enabling concurrent execution with the kernel handling all low-level context switching and execution management details.\n',0,'s25'),('a3chai',3,4,'2025-06-28_00-05-45','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory that serves as a centralized resource for allocating individual stacks to each thread in the operating system. Its primary purpose is enabling thread isolation, where each thread receives its own dedicated stack space for storing local variables, function return addresses, and saved CPU registers without interfering with other threads. The stack pool provides organized memory management by allowing the OS to systematically allocate fixed-size stack portions to new threads. In future labs, this enables multiple threads to coexist by giving each thread its own memory space within the pool. When creating a new thread, the OS can simply assign the next available stack segment from the pool, ensuring proper separation between threads and preventing stack overflow from one thread affecting another. This approach scales efficiently as more threads are added where instead of dynamically allocating memory for each stack, the OS pre-allocates a large pool and divides it into consistent segments..\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',3,5,'2025-06-27_22-55-22','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory that serves as a centralized resource for allocating individual stacks to each thread in the operating system. Its primary purpose is enabling thread isolation, where each thread receives its own dedicated stack space for storing local variables, function return addresses, and saved CPU registers without interfering with other threads. The stack pool provides organized memory management by allowing the OS to systematically allocate fixed-size stack portions to new threads. In future labs, this enables multiple threads to coexist by giving each thread its own memory space within the pool. When creating a new thread, the OS can simply assign the next available stack segment from the pool, ensuring proper separation between threads and preventing stack overflow from one thread affecting another. This approach scales efficiently as more threads are added where instead of dynamically allocating memory for each stack, the OS pre-allocates a large pool and divides it into consistent segments.\n',0,'s25'),('a3chai',3,5,'2025-06-28_00-05-45','The distinction between \"user\" and \"kernel\" refers to separating application code from operating system code into different files and execution contexts.\nUser files contain application-specific code - the actual programs and functions that accomplish your desired tasks. This includes thread functions, business logic, user interface code, and application data processing. User code runs with limited privileges and cannot directly access hardware or critical system resources.\nKernel files contain the operating system\'s core functionality - thread management, system calls, interrupt handlers, memory management, and hardware abstraction layers. Kernel code runs with elevated privileges and has direct access to hardware registers, memory management units, and other critical system resources.\nThis separation is important from the user\'s perspective for several key reasons:\nSimplified development - You can focus on writing your application logic without worrying about low-level hardware details, stack management, or context switching. The kernel handles these complexities behind well-defined interfaces.\nSystem stability - User code cannot accidentally corrupt critical system functions or crash the entire system. If your application has a bug, it affects only your code, not the underlying OS.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',4,1,'2025-07-29_11-36-59','Round-robin scheduling implemented with a queue works by enqueuing threads in the order they are ready to run. The scheduler always selects the thread at the front of the queue to execute and, once its time slice is over or it yields, the thread is moved to the back of the queue. This creates a simple and fair rotation, ensuring that all threads get a turn to run in a cyclic manner.\nWhile the array-based approach already achieves similar behavior using index tracking and modulo arithmetic, a queue structure provides a more intuitive and flexible design. It allows for cleaner rotation logic and simplifies adding, removing, or blocking threads since queue operations handle these cases naturally without manually shifting array elements or adjusting indices. However, using a queue can introduce additional performance overhead, especially in small embedded systems, due to dynamic memory management, pointer updates, or longer access times compared to a fixed-size array. In resource-constrained environments, the array approach might remain preferable for its simplicity and speed, even though a queue is conceptually more elegant and scalable.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',4,1,'2025-07-29_12-00-02','Round-robin scheduling implemented with a queue works by enqueuing threads in the order they are ready to run. The scheduler always selects the thread at the front of the queue to execute and, once its time slice is over or it yields, the thread is moved to the back of the queue. This creates a simple and fair rotation, ensuring that all threads get a turn to run in a cyclic manner.\nWhile the array-based approach already achieves similar behavior using index tracking and modulo arithmetic, a queue structure provides a more intuitive and flexible design. It allows for cleaner rotation logic and simplifies adding, removing, or blocking threads since queue operations handle these cases naturally without manually shifting array elements or adjusting indices. However, using a queue can introduce additional performance overhead, especially in small embedded systems, due to dynamic memory management, pointer updates, or longer access times compared to a fixed-size array. In resource-constrained environments, the array approach might remain preferable for its simplicity and speed, even though a queue is conceptually more elegant and scalable.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',4,2,'2025-07-29_11-36-59','The PendSV_Handler is invoked whenever the operating system decides that a context switch is required. When triggered, it first reads the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which indicates the current location of the running thread’s stack. It then saves the thread’s working state by pushing registers R4 through R11 onto the stack. This preserves the thread’s CPU context so it can be resumed later without losing data.\nAfter saving the context, the updated PSP is stored in the thread\'s control block (TCB) within threadArray[currentThread]. The handler then calls osSched(), which uses round-robin scheduling to select the next thread to run, updates currentThread to point to this new thread, and loads its saved PSP from the TCB. Finally, PendSV_Handler restores the new thread’s context by popping R4–R11 from the stack and returns execution to that thread. This mechanism allows smooth switching between threads, giving the illusion of concurrent execution.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',4,2,'2025-07-29_12-00-02','The PendSV_Handler is invoked whenever the operating system decides that a context switch is required. When triggered, it first reads the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which indicates the current location of the running thread’s stack. It then saves the thread’s working state by pushing registers R4 through R11 onto the stack. This preserves the thread’s CPU context so it can be resumed later without losing data.\nAfter saving the context, the updated PSP is stored in the thread\'s control block (TCB) within threadArray[currentThread]. The handler then calls osSched(), which uses round-robin scheduling to select the next thread to run, updates currentThread to point to this new thread, and loads its saved PSP from the TCB. Finally, PendSV_Handler restores the new thread’s context by popping R4–R11 from the stack and returns execution to that thread. This mechanism allows smooth switching between threads, giving the illusion of concurrent execution.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',4,3,'2025-07-29_11-36-59','If the scheduler always chooses the highest-priority task whenever it is ready to run, lower-priority tasks risk being completely starved of CPU time. A high-priority task that never yields or is frequently ready to execute will continuously preempt lower-priority tasks, preventing them from running. This undermines fairness in scheduling and can lead to unstable or unpredictable system behavior. Important but lower-priority background operations, such as I/O handling, data logging, or maintenance tasks, might never execute, potentially causing the system to miss deadlines, lose data, or even crash due to unserviced events. A well-designed scheduler must ensure that all tasks, regardless of priority, receive some level of execution time to maintain system stability and fairness.\n',0,'s25'),('a3chai',4,3,'2025-07-29_12-00-02','The fundamental mechanism of this scheduler design means that only the highest-priority task would ever run. Since the scheduler always selects the task with the highest priority whenever it makes a scheduling decision, any task with lower priority will never be selected as long as a higher-priority task exists and is ready to execute.\nThis creates a critical distinction: lower-priority tasks don\'t just face starvation (reduced CPU time) – they face complete exclusion from execution. If the highest-priority task is consistently ready to run, it will monopolize the CPU indefinitely, and all other tasks will never execute at all.\nThe scheduler\'s behavior leads to this conclusion because it lacks any mechanism to ensure fairness or time-sharing. Unlike schedulers that use time slices, aging, or priority decay, this design provides no pathway for lower-priority tasks to ever gain CPU access when higher-priority tasks are available. The difference between \"being starved\" and \"never executing\" is absolute: starvation implies reduced access to resources, while this scheduler design creates complete denial of execution for all but the single highest-priority task.\nThis design flaw has severe system implications. Critical background operations such as I/O handling, data logging, or maintenance tasks assigned lower priorities would never execute, potentially causing the system to miss deadlines, lose data, or crash due to unserviced events. A functional scheduler must incorporate mechanisms like time slicing, priority aging, or guaranteed minimum execution time to ensure all tasks receive some level of CPU access, maintaining both system stability and fairness.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',4,4,'2025-07-29_11-36-59','Some advantages of using C instead of pure assembly include improved readability and maintainability, as C code is generally easier to understand and modify, making it less prone to human error. It also simplifies debugging and extending functionality since high-level constructs are easier to manage than low-level instructions. Additionally, C allows the use of advanced data structures like arrays, queues, and linked lists without requiring manual memory and register management, which greatly reduces development complexity.\nHowever, C also has some drawbacks compared to assembly. It introduces additional overhead, meaning the generated code may not be as compact or optimized as hand-written assembly. C provides less precise control over low-level hardware behaviors, such as direct register manipulation and cycle-by-cycle execution timing. Finally, mixing C with assembly requires strict adherence to calling conventions and context-saving rules to ensure smooth transitions between the two languages, adding some complexity to the development process.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',4,4,'2025-07-29_12-00-02','Some advantages of using C instead of pure assembly include improved readability and maintainability, as C code is generally easier to understand and modify, making it less prone to human error. It also simplifies debugging and extending functionality since high-level constructs are easier to manage than low-level instructions. Additionally, C allows the use of advanced data structures like arrays, queues, and linked lists without requiring manual memory and register management, which greatly reduces development complexity.\nHowever, C also has some drawbacks compared to assembly. It introduces additional overhead, meaning the generated code may not be as compact or optimized as hand-written assembly. C provides less precise control over low-level hardware behaviors, such as direct register manipulation and cycle-by-cycle execution timing. Finally, mixing C with assembly requires strict adherence to calling conventions and context-saving rules to ensure smooth transitions between the two languages, adding some complexity to the development process.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',4,5,'2025-07-29_11-36-59','Yes, the operating system would still function even with only a single thread. When the thread calls osYield(), the scheduler attempts to switch to the next available thread. Since there is only one thread in the system, the scheduler selects the same thread again, effectively resuming its execution immediately. The behavior is essentially the same as running a single cooperative thread without multitasking, as the context switch does not change which thread is running. This results in a functional but redundant context switch where the processor briefly saves and restores the same thread state.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',4,5,'2025-07-29_12-00-02','Yes, the operating system would still function even with only a single thread. When the thread calls osYield(), the scheduler attempts to switch to the next available thread. Since there is only one thread in the system, the scheduler selects the same thread again, effectively resuming its execution immediately. The behavior is essentially the same as running a single cooperative thread without multitasking, as the context switch does not change which thread is running. This results in a functional but redundant context switch where the processor briefly saves and restores the same thread state.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',5,1,'2025-07-29_11-42-22','Internally, when a new thread is created, the OS allocates a dedicated stack for it and manually constructs an initial stack frame that mimics the one expected during an exception return. This frame includes default or dummy values for registers such as R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR, matching what the Cortex-M4 CPU expects when resuming from an interrupt or exception.\nTo pass an argument to the thread function, the OS places the pointer value into the memory slot corresponding to R0 within this synthetic stack frame. When the thread is scheduled for the first time, the CPU performs an exception return, which automatically pops these register values from the stack. As a result, R0 is loaded with the correct argument, and the thread begins executing as if it had been called normally with that parameter.\nThis mechanism works seamlessly because the Cortex-M4 architecture is designed to restore CPU state from the stack during context switches. The OS leverages this hardware feature to simulate a function call into the thread, preloading it with the appropriate register state and entry point.\n',0,'s25'),('a3chai',5,1,'2025-07-29_12-04-59','To pass an argument to a thread, the user must create a variable containing the data, obtain a pointer to it using the address-of operator, and pass this pointer to osCreateThread(). For example: int my_data = 42; osCreateThread(thread_function, &my_data, ...);\nWhen osCreateThread() is called, the OS allocates a dedicated stack for the new thread and constructs a synthetic stack frame that mimics an exception return sequence. This frame contains initial values for registers R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR, with the user\'s argument pointer placed in the R0 slot and the PC set to the thread function address. The thread is then added to the scheduler\'s ready queue.\nThis mechanism works by combining the ARM EABI calling convention with the Cortex-M4\'s exception handling hardware. The ARM EABI requires function arguments to be passed in registers R0-R3, with the first argument in R0. When the thread is first scheduled, the CPU performs an exception return that automatically pops the synthetic stack frame values into the actual registers. This loads the argument pointer into R0 and jumps to the thread function via PC, making the thread appear to execute as a normal function call with the correct parameter already available.\nThe design creates a transparent interface where the thread function receives its argument through standard calling conventions, despite being initiated through the OS threading mechanism rather than a direct function call.This mechanism works seamlessly because the Cortex-M4 architecture is designed to restore CPU state from the stack during context switches. The OS leverages this hardware feature to simulate a function call into the thread, preloading it with the appropriate register state and entry point.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',5,2,'2025-07-29_11-42-22','Calling osYield() inside any context-switching handler (such as PendSV_Handler or SVC_Handler) is unsafe because it triggers a supervisor call (SVC) while the system is already handling an exception. This leads to nested exceptions or recursive context switches, which can corrupt the stack and result in a system crash.\nThese exception handlers are designed solely for low-level context management, such as saving/restoring registers and switching stack pointers. Invoking higher-level OS services like osYield() from within them violates the separation of concerns between the OS kernel and its system call interface, and can lead to unpredictable behavior.\nTo maintain system integrity, higher-level operations should be performed in thread mode, not exception mode.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',5,2,'2025-07-29_12-04-59','Calling osYield() inside any context-switching handler (such as PendSV_Handler or SVC_Handler) is unsafe because it triggers a supervisor call (SVC) while the system is already handling an exception. This leads to nested exceptions or recursive context switches, which can corrupt the stack and result in a system crash.\nThese exception handlers are designed solely for low-level context management, such as saving/restoring registers and switching stack pointers. Invoking higher-level OS services like osYield() from within them violates the separation of concerns between the OS kernel and its system call interface, and can lead to unpredictable behavior.\nTo maintain system integrity, higher-level operations should be performed in thread mode, not exception mode.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',5,3,'2025-07-29_11-42-22','Increasing the OS tick rate to 0.1 milliseconds can improve the responsiveness of the system and enable more precise time-based features such as preemptive context switching, task delays, and timeouts. With a faster tick, the scheduler has finer control over timing, which can be beneficial in applications that require tight timing constraints or high responsiveness.\nHowever, this improvement comes at a cost. A higher tick frequency leads to more frequent interrupts, and each tick invokes the SysTick_Handler, which consumes CPU cycles. This reduces the amount of time available for actual application code, potentially degrading overall system performance. Additionally, power consumption tends to increase with higher interrupt rates, which can be problematic in battery-powered or low-power systems. From a development perspective, frequent interrupts can also make debugging more difficult, as the system context changes rapidly, making it harder to trace and isolate bugs.\nIn summary, while a faster tick improves timing accuracy, it introduces overhead that must be carefully balanced against performance, power, and maintainability concern\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',5,3,'2025-07-29_12-04-59','Increasing the OS tick rate to 0.1 milliseconds can improve the responsiveness of the system and enable more precise time-based features such as preemptive context switching, task delays, and timeouts. With a faster tick, the scheduler has finer control over timing, which can be beneficial in applications that require tight timing constraints or high responsiveness.\nHowever, this improvement comes at a cost. A higher tick frequency leads to more frequent interrupts, and each tick invokes the SysTick_Handler, which consumes CPU cycles. This reduces the amount of time available for actual application code, potentially degrading overall system performance. Additionally, power consumption tends to increase with higher interrupt rates, which can be problematic in battery-powered or low-power systems. From a development perspective, frequent interrupts can also make debugging more difficult, as the system context changes rapidly, making it harder to trace and isolate bugs.\nIn summary, while a faster tick improves timing accuracy, it introduces overhead that must be carefully balanced against performance, power, and maintainability concern\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',5,4,'2025-07-29_11-42-22','From the user\'s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires each thread to explicitly call osYield() in order to give up the CPU. This means that the responsibility for fairness and responsiveness lies with the programmer, as the system depends on well-behaved threads voluntarily yielding control. If a thread forgets to yield or runs for too long, it can block other threads and degrade system responsiveness.\nIn contrast, preemptive multitasking relies on a hardware timer to generate periodic interrupts that allow the operating system to automatically switch context. This happens regardless of the state or behavior of the currently running thread. As a result, the OS can enforce fairness and ensure that all threads get CPU time, improving responsiveness and system robustness without relying on the cooperation of individual threads.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',5,4,'2025-07-29_12-04-59','From the user\'s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires each thread to explicitly call osYield() in order to give up the CPU. This means that the responsibility for fairness and responsiveness lies with the programmer, as the system depends on well-behaved threads voluntarily yielding control. If a thread forgets to yield or runs for too long, it can block other threads and degrade system responsiveness.\nIn contrast, preemptive multitasking relies on a hardware timer to generate periodic interrupts that allow the operating system to automatically switch context. This happens regardless of the state or behavior of the currently running thread. As a result, the OS can enforce fairness and ensure that all threads get CPU time, improving responsiveness and system robustness without relying on the cooperation of individual threads.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',5,5,'2025-07-29_11-42-22','Cooperative and preemptive context switching can coexist in an operating system because they rely on different triggers to initiate the same context switching mechanism. In cooperative multitasking, a thread explicitly calls osYield(), which generates a supervisor call (SVC) to request a context switch. In contrast, preemptive multitasking uses the system timer (SysTick) to generate regular interrupts that set the PendSV exception, triggering a context switch automatically.\nDespite these different entry points, both methods ultimately lead to the PendSV_Handler, which performs the actual context switching by saving the current thread\'s state and restoring the next one. Since the triggers are distinct but the core switching mechanism is shared, cooperative and preemptive switching can function together seamlessly within the same system.\n',1,'s25'),('a3chai',5,5,'2025-07-29_12-04-59','Cooperative and preemptive context switching can coexist in an operating system because they rely on different triggers to initiate the same context switching mechanism. In cooperative multitasking, a thread explicitly calls osYield(), which generates a supervisor call (SVC) to request a context switch. In contrast, preemptive multitasking uses the system timer (SysTick) to generate regular interrupts that set the PendSV exception, triggering a context switch automatically.\nDespite these different entry points, both methods ultimately lead to the PendSV_Handler, which performs the actual context switching by saving the current thread\'s state and restoring the next one. Since the triggers are distinct but the core switching mechanism is shared, cooperative and preemptive switching can function together seamlessly within the same system.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,1,'2025-05-15_09-38-38','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer, and it’s used in general embedded projects because it simplifies the programming process of embedded systems. It takes care of the interaction with the low level hardware while the programmer can focus on coding at a higher level, thereby abstracting the hardware level.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,1,'2025-05-15_09-50-35','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer, and it’s used in general embedded projects because it simplifies the programming process of embedded systems. It takes care of the interaction with the low level hardware while the programmer can focus on coding at a higher level, thereby abstracting the hardware level.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,1,'2025-05-15_10-03-18','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer, and it’s used in general embedded projects because it simplifies the programming process of embedded systems. It takes care of the interaction with the low level hardware while the programmer can focus on coding at a higher level, thereby abstracting the hardware level.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,1,'2025-05-16_09-44-47','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer, and it’s used in general embedded projects because it simplifies the programming process of embedded systems. It takes care of the interaction with the low level hardware while the programmer can focus on coding at a higher level, thereby abstracting the hardware level.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,1,'2025-05-29_08-31-04','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer, and it’s used in general embedded projects because it simplifies the programming process of embedded systems. It takes care of the interaction with the low level hardware while the programmer can focus on coding at a higher level, thereby abstracting the hardware level.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,1,'2025-05-29_12-02-34','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer, and it’s used in general embedded projects because it simplifies the programming process of embedded systems. It takes care of the interaction with the low level hardware while the programmer can focus on coding at a higher level, thereby abstracting the hardware level.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,2,'2025-05-15_09-38-38','The __io_putchar function redirects the output of printf to the serial port for UART communication. Without it, printf would output nothing over UART and we wouldn’t be able to see the output on our computer.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',1,2,'2025-05-15_09-50-35','The __io_putchar function redirects the output of printf to the serial port for UART communication. This happens because printf calls __io_putchar which will take each character in printf and output it to the serial port. Without it, printf would output nothing over UART and we wouldn’t be able to see the output on our computer. Thus, printf is used when you want to receive data from the STM32 board on your computer through serial communication like UART.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',1,2,'2025-05-15_10-03-18','The __io_putchar function redirects the output of printf to the serial port for UART communication. Because printf relies on the stdio library which requires helper functions to interact with the hardware, __io_putchar is repeatedly called by printf so that it can be outputted to the serial port. Without it, printf would output nothing over UART and we wouldn’t be able to see the output on our computer. Thus, __io_putchar is used when you want to receive data from the STM32 board on your computer through serial communication like UART. It also gives the flexibility of using printf with different hardware, since __io_putchar will handle the transmission of the characters from printf for the given piece of hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,2,'2025-05-16_09-44-47','The __io_putchar function redirects the output of printf to the serial port for UART communication. Because printf relies on the stdio library which requires helper functions to interact with the hardware, __io_putchar is repeatedly called by printf so that it can be outputted to the serial port. Without it, printf would output nothing over UART and we wouldn’t be able to see the output on our computer. Thus, __io_putchar is used when you want to receive data from the STM32 board on your computer through serial communication like UART. It also gives the flexibility of using printf with different hardware, since __io_putchar will handle the transmission of the characters from printf for the given piece of hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,2,'2025-05-29_08-31-04','The __io_putchar function redirects the output of printf to the serial port for UART communication. Because printf relies on the stdio library which requires helper functions to interact with the hardware, __io_putchar is repeatedly called by printf so that it can be outputted to the serial port. Without it, printf would output nothing over UART and we wouldn’t be able to see the output on our computer. Thus, __io_putchar is used when you want to receive data from the STM32 board on your computer through serial communication like UART. It also gives the flexibility of using printf with different hardware, since __io_putchar will handle the transmission of the characters from printf for the given piece of hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,2,'2025-05-29_12-02-34','The __io_putchar function redirects the output of printf to the serial port for UART communication. Because printf relies on the stdio library which requires helper functions to interact with the hardware, __io_putchar is repeatedly called by printf so that it can be outputted to the serial port. Without it, printf would output nothing over UART and we wouldn’t be able to see the output on our computer. Thus, __io_putchar is used when you want to receive data from the STM32 board on your computer through serial communication like UART. It also gives the flexibility of using printf with different hardware, since __io_putchar will handle the transmission of the characters from printf for the given piece of hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,3,'2025-05-15_09-38-38','The debugger was used to look at the actual memory locations of our variables and pointers. It also allowed us to see how these values would change throughout the program by using set points, whereas using only printf would only let us see the final values.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',1,3,'2025-05-15_09-50-35','The debugger was used to inspect the registers of our hardware in real time. We could see the values stored in the registers as we stepped through line by line in the program, whereas using only printf doesn’t let us inspect the actual hardware register values and also doesn’t let us step through our program line by line.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,3,'2025-05-15_10-03-18','The debugger was used to inspect the registers of our hardware in real time. We could see the values stored in the registers as we stepped through line by line in the program, whereas using only printf doesn’t let us inspect the actual hardware register values and also doesn’t let us step through our program line by line.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,3,'2025-05-16_09-44-47','The debugger was used to inspect the registers of our hardware in real time. We could see the values stored in the registers as we stepped through line by line in the program, whereas using only printf doesn’t let us inspect the actual hardware register values and also doesn’t let us step through our program line by line.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,3,'2025-05-29_08-31-04','The debugger was used to inspect the registers of our hardware in real time. We could see the values stored in the registers as we stepped through line by line in the program, whereas using only printf doesn’t let us inspect the actual hardware register values and also doesn’t let us step through our program line by line.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,3,'2025-05-29_12-02-34','The debugger was used to inspect the registers of our hardware in real time. We could see the values stored in the registers as we stepped through line by line in the program, whereas using only printf doesn’t let us inspect the actual hardware register values and also doesn’t let us step through our program line by line.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,4,'2025-05-15_09-38-38','Once we have obtained the initial value for the MSP, we subtracted 0x400 bytes to obtain the location of the PSP. We chose 0x400 because that was the size we defined for both the main stack size and thread stack size. The PSP points to the start of the thread stack, so we subtract 0x400 bytes (size of the main stack) to go to the start of the thread stack. Without knowing the initial location of MSP, we wouldn’t know where the main stack started and thus would not be able to figure out where the thread stack starts either.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,4,'2025-05-15_09-50-35','Once we have obtained the initial value for the MSP, we subtracted 0x400 bytes to obtain the location of the PSP. We chose 0x400 because that was the size we defined for both the main stack size and thread stack size. The PSP points to the start of the thread stack, so we subtract 0x400 bytes (size of the main stack) to go to the start of the thread stack. Without knowing the initial location of MSP, we wouldn’t know where the main stack started and thus would not be able to figure out where the thread stack starts either.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,4,'2025-05-15_10-03-18','Once we have obtained the initial value for the MSP, we subtracted 0x400 bytes to obtain the location of the PSP. We chose 0x400 because that was the size we defined for both the main stack size and thread stack size. The PSP points to the start of the thread stack, so we subtract 0x400 bytes (size of the main stack) to go to the start of the thread stack. Without knowing the initial location of MSP, we wouldn’t know where the main stack started and thus would not be able to figure out where the thread stack starts either.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,4,'2025-05-16_09-44-47','Once we have obtained the initial value for the MSP, we subtracted 0x400 bytes to obtain the location of the PSP. We chose 0x400 because that was the size we defined for both the main stack size and thread stack size. The PSP points to the start of the thread stack, so we subtract 0x400 bytes (size of the main stack) to go to the start of the thread stack. Without knowing the initial location of MSP, we wouldn’t know where the main stack started and thus would not be able to figure out where the thread stack starts either.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,4,'2025-05-29_08-31-04','Once we have obtained the initial value for the MSP, we subtracted 0x400 bytes to obtain the location of the PSP. We chose 0x400 because that was the size we defined for both the main stack size and thread stack size. The PSP points to the start of the thread stack, so we subtract 0x400 bytes (size of the main stack) to go to the start of the thread stack. Without knowing the initial location of MSP, we wouldn’t know where the main stack started and thus would not be able to figure out where the thread stack starts either.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,4,'2025-05-29_12-02-34','Once we have obtained the initial value for the MSP, we subtracted 0x400 bytes to obtain the location of the PSP. We chose 0x400 because that was the size we defined for both the main stack size and thread stack size. The PSP points to the start of the thread stack, so we subtract 0x400 bytes (size of the main stack) to go to the start of the thread stack. Without knowing the initial location of MSP, we wouldn’t know where the main stack started and thus would not be able to figure out where the thread stack starts either.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,5,'2025-05-15_09-38-38','Since we defined the thread stack size to be 0x400 bytes, we can subtract 0x400 bytes from the current PSP which is located at the start of the first thread to find the start of the second thread. We would then set PSP to this new value. If we wanted to find the start location of another thread, continue subtracting 0x400 bytes until you reach that thread starting location.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,5,'2025-05-15_09-50-35','Since we defined the thread stack size to be 0x400 bytes, we can subtract 0x400 bytes from the current PSP which is located at the start of the first thread to find the start of the second thread. We would then set PSP to this new value. If we wanted to find the start location of another thread, continue subtracting 0x400 bytes until you reach that thread starting location.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',1,5,'2025-05-15_10-03-18','Since we defined the thread stack size to be 0x400 bytes, we can subtract 0x400 bytes from the current PSP which is located at the start of the first thread to find the start of the second thread. We would then set PSP to this new value. If we wanted to find the start location of another thread, continue subtracting 0x400 bytes until you reach that thread starting location.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',1,5,'2025-05-16_09-44-47','We can allocate a block of memory to hold j number of threads by essentially creating a large array of integers which will be broken up into multiple stacks, including the main stack and the thread stacks. Since we defined the thread stack size to be 0x400 bytes, we can subtract 0x400 bytes from the current PSP which is located at the start of the first thread to find the start of the second thread. We would then set PSP to this new value. If we wanted to find the start location of another thread, continue subtracting 0x400 bytes until you reach that thread starting location. The exact formula would be ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE, which can be used to locate the starting address of the jth thread stack.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',1,5,'2025-05-29_08-31-04','Since we defined the thread stack size to be 0x400 bytes, we can subtract 0x400 bytes from the current PSP which is located at the start of the first thread to find the start of the second thread. We would then set PSP to this new value. If we wanted to find the start location of another thread, continue subtracting 0x400 bytes until you reach that thread starting location.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',1,5,'2025-05-29_12-02-34','Since we defined the thread stack size to be 0x400 bytes, we can subtract 0x400 bytes from the current PSP which is located at the start of the first thread to find the start of the second thread. We would then set PSP to this new value. If we wanted to find the start location of another thread, continue subtracting 0x400 bytes until you reach that thread starting location.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',2,1,'2025-05-29_21-22-19','When we changed PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, it loaded it as the next instruction for the CPU to execute. The CPU then executed this function, making it print the phrase “Hello, PC!” to console repeatedly as defined in the print continuously function.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',2,1,'2025-06-05_13-35-29','When we changed PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, it loaded it as the next instruction for the CPU to execute. The CPU then executed this function, making it print the phrase “Hello, PC!” to console repeatedly as defined in the print continuously function.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',2,1,'2025-06-07_18-14-05','When we changed PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, it loaded it as the next instruction for the CPU to execute. The CPU then executed this function, making it print the phrase “Hello, PC!” to console repeatedly as defined in the print continuously function.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',2,2,'2025-05-29_21-22-19','A system call is a request to the OS from a program in user-mode to execute a privileged command. If the user-mode program wants to do something that requires privilege, such as printing to the console, it must create a software interrupt to get the OS to do this through a system call. System calls are typically done via SVGs on the ARM Cortex-M.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',2,2,'2025-06-05_13-35-29','A system call is a request to the kernel from a program in user-mode to execute a privileged command. The kernel in an operating system handles the majority of tasks including memory management and I/O management. If the user-mode program wants to do something that requires privilege, such as printing to the console, it must create a software interrupt to get the kernel to do this through a system call. System calls are typically done via SVGs on the ARM Cortex-M. The point of requiring a system call to access sensitive privileged commands is for added security in preventing a program from damaging the OS and misusing system memory. These system call commands are typically wrapped in an API function which allows the user program to easily interface with the lower level command.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',2,2,'2025-06-07_18-14-05','A system call is a function call where a user-mode program tells the kernel to perform a privileged task, like console output. The kernel manages important commands relating to memory and I/O. To execute a privileged command, the program triggers a software interrupt using the SVC instruction on the ARM Cortex-M4. This ensures a secure transition to kernel mode. System calls prevent programs from damaging the OS or misusing memory and are wrapped in API functions for easy access.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',2,3,'2025-05-29_21-22-19','First, the thread stack must be defined by allocating a chunk of memory after the main stack. A thread stack pointer initially points to the start of this thread stack. The thread stack pointer decrements through the thread stack and pushes the initial values of the thread stack registers (this is known as the context). These values in the stack are then popped into the actual hardware registers through system calls. The reason why we need to store the register values on the stack is because we need to trick the OS into thinking the thread stack is already running. By storing the register values on the stack, we can push them onto the registers and the OS can “resume” the thread operation even though it hadn’t been started before.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',2,3,'2025-06-05_13-35-29','First, the thread stack must be defined by allocating a chunk of memory after the main stack. A thread stack pointer initially points to the start of this thread stack. The thread stack pointer decrements through the thread stack and pushes the initial values of the thread stack registers (this is known as the context). These values in the stack are then popped into the actual hardware registers through system calls. The reason why we need to store the register values on the stack is because we need to trick the OS into thinking the thread stack is already running. By storing the register values on the stack, we can push them onto the registers and the OS can “resume” the thread operation even though it hadn’t been started before.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',2,3,'2025-06-07_18-14-05','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a chunk of memory right after the main stack and preparing it so the CPU can run the thread like it’s already in action. First, we allocate some memory for the thread stack right after the main stack. The thread stack pointer starts at the top of this chunk and moves down as we push the initial register values onto the stack in a specific order. This order matters because the CPU expects these values in exact sequence to pop them back into the hardware registers correctly. The OS uses system calls to pop these values into the registers, letting the CPU resume the thread’s execution as if it was already running. Storing register values on the stack sets up the CPU to jump right into the thread’s code, while the OS manages the switch, ensuring the thread starts smoothly without messing up the system.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',2,4,'2025-05-29_21-22-19','If __set_PSP wasn’t called in SVC_Handler_Main, then the location of the PSP would still be the default, which is in the main stack and not the thread stack that we defined. When we try to push the values to initialize the thread stack, we’d be pushing onto some restricted area of memory in the main stack, leading to a crash.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',2,4,'2025-06-05_13-35-29','If __set_PSP wasn’t called in SVC_Handler_Main, then the location of the PSP would still be the default, which is in the main stack and not the thread stack that we defined which contains the variables and data needed for our program. The main stack and thread stack are two distinct areas of memory so when we try to push the values to initialize the thread stack, we’d be pushing onto some area of memory in the main stack which stores sensitive data on the actual operating system instead of the program like we want. Modifying this main stack data will most likely lead to a hard fault since it is trying to modify values it shouldn’t be able to access.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',2,4,'2025-06-07_18-14-05','If __set_PSP wasn’t called in SVC_Handler_Main, then the location of the PSP would still be the default, which is in the main stack and not the thread stack that we defined which contains the variables and data needed for our program. The main stack and thread stack are two distinct areas of memory so when we try to push the values to initialize the thread stack, we’d be pushing onto some area of memory in the main stack which stores sensitive data on the actual operating system instead of the program like we want. Modifying this main stack data will most likely lead to a hard fault since it is trying to modify values it shouldn’t be able to access.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',2,5,'2025-05-29_21-22-19','This would result in a hard fault because the link register is pointing to an arbitrary value and is likely an invalid memory address. When the function call ends and terminates due to the lack of a loop, the program will attempt to return to the address specified in the link register and thus cause a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',2,5,'2025-06-05_13-35-29','This would result in a hard fault because the link register is pointing to an arbitrary value and is likely an invalid memory address. When the function call ends and terminates due to the lack of a loop, the program will attempt to return to the address specified in the link register and thus cause a crash.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',2,5,'2025-06-07_18-14-05','If the thread function prints once and returns instead of looping, it causes a hard fault because the link register holds an invalid return address. When setting up a thread’s stack, we push initial register values onto the stack to mimic a running thread. The stack manages the thread’s context, storing values like the program counter and link register, which the CPU uses to resume execution during context switches. Normally, the link register points to a valid return address on the stack, but if the thread function returns, the stack wasn’t set up with a proper return address in this case. When the function tries to return, the CPU tries to jump to the invalid address in the link register, causing a hard fault and a crash\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',3,1,'2025-06-25_17-14-01','First, each file is compiled independently into a .o object file. Next, a linker takes all the object files and any library objects needed and creates a single executable. This way, the final executable can be run with all the links to the different .c files included.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',3,1,'2025-06-26_12-16-26','First, each .c file is preprocessed and all include statements are replaced by inserting the contents of the header files, which prepares the code for compilation by simplifying the source code. After it is preprocessed, each .c file is independently compiled into a .o file by translating the C code into machine code. Then, a linker takes all the object files alongside the required libraries and combines them all into a single executable. The linker resolves references between files and makes it so a single executable file for the program can be run.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',3,2,'2025-06-25_17-14-01','Usually, you should only include the header file and not the whole .c file in main.c. If the .c file was included in main.c, it would mean the entire contents of the .c file is dumped into main before compilation. This may lead to duplicate definitions of code since the .c file will also get compiled in addition to main.c, which already includes the contents of the .c file. Instead, the header file should be included in main.c so that there is no risk of duplicate code and so that code is more organized which makes debugging much more streamlined in larger code bases.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',3,2,'2025-06-26_12-16-26','Usually, you should only include the header file and not the whole .c file in main.c. If the .c file was included in main.c, it would mean the entire contents of the .c file is dumped into main before compilation. This may lead to duplicate definitions of code since the .c file will also get compiled in addition to main.c, which already includes the contents of the .c file. Instead, the header file should be included in main.c so that there is no risk of duplicate code and so that code is more organized which makes debugging much more streamlined in larger code bases.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',3,3,'2025-06-25_17-14-01','First, we need to carve out the allocated memory for the stack pool and allocate a chunk of memory for the new thread by subtracting the size of the thread stack from the location of the previous stack allocation. Set the new thread stack pointer to that location. Next, we need to pre-fill the stack frame of the new thread with arbitrary values for register data. This makes the CPU think it is just resuming the thread and thus the thread will execute and start.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',3,3,'2025-06-26_12-16-26','First, we need to define a thread function which defines the code that a thread will run when started. After the kernel has been initialized correctly, the user can create a thread by calling a high-level function like create_thread() and passing the thread function along with any arguments. This function allocates memory in the stack space and prepares the thread’s initial state. Once all the threads have been created, we can start the system by calling another high-level function like start_kernel() which will begin the thread scheduling process and runs the threads. Going through this procedure to set up and run a thread avoids interacting directly with memory and allows the user to focus on the high-level functionality of the code.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',3,4,'2025-06-25_17-14-01','The stack pool is a block of memory in RAM which holds the stack memory for all the threads that the OS manages. This block of memory is carved out to yield numerous equally-sized thread stack sizes. This is important since each thread requires its own memory so they don’t overwrite each other’s data. In this lab, only a single thread was carved out of the stack pool but in future labs, more threads can be allocated by carving more memory out of the stack pool in the same way we did for this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',3,4,'2025-06-26_12-16-26','The stack pool is a block of memory in RAM which holds the stack memory for all the threads that the OS manages. This block of memory is carved out to yield numerous equally-sized thread stack sizes. This is important since each thread requires its own memory so they don’t overwrite each other’s data. In this lab, only a single thread was carved out of the stack pool but in future labs, more threads can be allocated by carving more memory out of the stack pool in the same way we did for this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',3,5,'2025-06-25_17-14-01','The kernel files manage and define the OS’s resources like threads, stack and memory allocation, and system calls. On the other hand, user files like main.c define what the threads do at a higher level (i.e. printing infinitely in a loop). Separating code into these two different files is important since it adds abstraction for the user and the user doesn’t need to worry about interacting with the lower level hardware, but only the high level functionality of the code. Not only does it simplify the coding process by making code modular, but it also adds security since the user can’t modify the OS internals and potentially break things without directly accessing the kernel files.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',3,5,'2025-06-26_12-16-26','The kernel files manage and define the OS’s resources like threads, stack and memory allocation, and system calls. On the other hand, user files like main.c define what the threads do at a higher level (i.e. printing infinitely in a loop). Separating code into these two different files is important since it adds abstraction for the user and the user doesn’t need to worry about interacting with the lower level hardware, but only the high level functionality of the code. Not only does it simplify the coding process by making code modular, but it also adds security since the user can’t modify the OS internals and potentially break things without directly accessing the kernel files.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,1,'2025-07-03_11-05-22','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a circular queue so that when the last thread needs to be switched, it returns to the thread at the front of the queue. If a thread yields, it is removed from the front of the queue and placed at the back and the next thread in line is scheduled. This results in equal fairness which is necessary for the round-robin strategy and mimics the rotating index of the array we implemented. The benefit of a queue is that it’s more flexible, making it easier to add or remove threads in O(1) time as opposed to O(n) time which an array would require.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,1,'2025-07-17_13-31-15','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a circular queue so that when the last thread needs to be switched, it returns to the thread at the front of the queue. If a thread yields, it is removed from the front of the queue and placed at the back and the next thread in line is scheduled. This results in equal fairness which is necessary for the round-robin strategy and mimics the rotating index of the array we implemented. The benefit of a queue is that it’s more flexible, making it easier to add or remove threads in O(1) time as opposed to O(n) time which an array would require.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,1,'2025-07-17_13-57-11','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a circular queue so that when the last thread needs to be switched, it returns to the thread at the front of the queue. If a thread yields, it is removed from the front of the queue and placed at the back and the next thread in line is scheduled. This results in equal fairness which is necessary for the round-robin strategy and mimics the rotating index of the array we implemented. The benefit of a queue is that it’s more flexible, making it easier to add or remove threads in O(1) time as opposed to O(n) time which an array would require.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,1,'2025-07-26_19-16-28','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a circular queue so that when the last thread needs to be switched, it returns to the thread at the front of the queue. If a thread yields, it is removed from the front of the queue and placed at the back and the next thread in line is scheduled. This results in equal fairness which is necessary for the round-robin strategy and mimics the rotating index of the array we implemented. The benefit of a queue is that it’s more flexible, making it easier to add or remove threads in O(1) time as opposed to O(n) time which an array would require.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,2,'2025-07-03_11-05-22','PendSV is a type of low priority interrupt that will wait for other interrupts to finish executing. When a context switch happens, PendSV will be ready to run inside SVC but will let the SVC interrupt finish executing first. This allows local variables on the stack to be restored and prevent a stack overflow error since SVC finishes running. The PendSV interrupt will then be triggered and a context switch can happen without using the stack.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',4,2,'2025-07-17_13-31-15','PendSV is a low-priority interrupt critical for pre-emptive multitasking, ensuring context switches occur efficiently after other operations. When a switch is needed, the PendSV_Handler executes, which first causes the hardware to save the essential registers onto the current thread’s PSP. Then, software saves the remaining registers onto the same stack and updates PSP to reflect the full saved context. The osSched function is then called to select the next thread and update the stack pointers. Finally, the handler restores the remaining registers from the new thread’s stack and the hardware restores the rest which allows the new thread to resume. A context switch cannot occur without this entire process.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',4,2,'2025-07-17_13-57-11','PendSV is a low-priority interrupt important for multitasking, enabling efficient context switches. When triggered, the PendSV_Handler begins by saving the current thread\'s state. The hardware pushes essential registers (R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) onto the PSP, then software explicitly saves R4-R11 to the same stack. After the osSched function selects the next thread, the handler updates the PSP to the new thread\'s context. Finally, it restores R4-R11, and hardware restores the remaining registers, allowing the new thread to seamlessly resume execution. This process of saving and restoring specific registers is fundamental for successful multitasking.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',4,2,'2025-07-26_19-16-28','PendSV is a low-priority interrupt used for context switching in multitasking systems. When PendSV_Handler runs, the hardware saves registers (R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) to the PSP, and the software saves R4-R11 after reading the PSP to locate the current thread’s stack. The scheduler osSched selects the next thread and the handler loads the new PSP value from that thread’s control block. It then restores R4-R11 and the hardware restores the remaining registers, allowing the new thread to resume execution.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,3,'2025-07-03_11-05-22','Adding priority to the TCB and scheduling tasks purely based on priority is a bad idea because this introduces starvation for certain low priority tasks. As more tasks are placed in the queue, tasks with very low priority will always be left behind since there will likely always be higher priority tasks being added or waiting to be executed. This increases the total wait time, so typically a strategy like round-robin is implemented to prevent starvation and ensure fairness while reducing the total task wait time.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',4,3,'2025-07-17_13-31-15','Adding a priority integer to the TCB and always scheduling the highest-priority task is a bad idea because this would cause only the highest-priority task to ever execute. The scheduling logic dictates that it will never move to lower-priority tasks if a higher-priority task is available. This almost guarantees starvation for any task with a lower priority than the frequently ready high-priority task. This leads to unfairness since the lower-priority tasks will never receive CPU time, whereas strategies like round-robin ensure that all tasks will eventually get a chance to execute.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,3,'2025-07-17_13-57-11','Adding a priority integer to the TCB and always scheduling the highest-priority task is a bad idea because this would cause only the highest-priority task to ever execute. The scheduling logic dictates that it will never move to lower-priority tasks if a higher-priority task is available. This almost guarantees starvation for any task with a lower priority than the frequently ready high-priority task. This leads to unfairness since the lower-priority tasks will never receive CPU time, whereas strategies like round-robin ensure that all tasks will eventually get a chance to execute.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,3,'2025-07-26_19-16-28','Adding a priority integer to the TCB and always scheduling the highest-priority task is a bad idea because this would cause only the highest-priority task to ever execute. The scheduling logic dictates that it will never move to lower-priority tasks if a higher-priority task is available. This almost guarantees starvation for any task with a lower priority than the frequently ready high-priority task. This leads to unfairness since the lower-priority tasks will never receive CPU time, whereas strategies like round-robin ensure that all tasks will eventually get a chance to execute.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,4,'2025-07-03_11-05-22','A benefit of calling the C functions is better readability and debuggability. Compared to assembly, the C language is much easier to understand, providing a higher level of abstraction making it easier to determine the functionality of a program and spot potential issues. C is also easier to write compared to assembly. Another benefit is that C is universal across different operating systems, whereas assembly may differ depending on the specific architecture of the computer. A downside of C is that it won’t run as quickly as assembly due to the need for overhead which assembly does not require. Assembly can also be optimized to be more efficient, leading to better performance.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,4,'2025-07-17_13-31-15','A benefit of calling the C functions is better readability and debuggability. Compared to assembly, the C language is much easier to understand, providing a higher level of abstraction making it easier to determine the functionality of a program and spot potential issues. C is also easier to write compared to assembly. Another benefit is that C is universal across different operating systems, whereas assembly may differ depending on the specific architecture of the computer. A downside of C is that it won’t run as quickly as assembly due to the need for overhead which assembly does not require. Assembly can also be optimized to be more efficient, leading to better performance.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,4,'2025-07-17_13-57-11','A benefit of calling the C functions is better readability and debuggability. Compared to assembly, the C language is much easier to understand, providing a higher level of abstraction making it easier to determine the functionality of a program and spot potential issues. C is also easier to write compared to assembly. Another benefit is that C is universal across different operating systems, whereas assembly may differ depending on the specific architecture of the computer. A downside of C is that it won’t run as quickly as assembly due to the need for overhead which assembly does not require. Assembly can also be optimized to be more efficient, leading to better performance.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,4,'2025-07-26_19-16-28','A benefit of calling the C functions is better readability and debuggability. Compared to assembly, the C language is much easier to understand, providing a higher level of abstraction making it easier to determine the functionality of a program and spot potential issues. C is also easier to write compared to assembly. Another benefit is that C is universal across different operating systems, whereas assembly may differ depending on the specific architecture of the computer. A downside of C is that it won’t run as quickly as assembly due to the need for overhead which assembly does not require. Assembly can also be optimized to be more efficient, leading to better performance.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,5,'2025-07-03_11-05-22','If only a single thread was created, the OS will still work but the context switching will be rendered useless and the performance of the single thread will be limited. After the thread prints, it will yield, then call the PendSV interrupt. This causes the OS scheduler to implement the round-robin scheduling strategy, but since there is only one thread, it will just switch back to the current thread and the current thread will start executing again. The overhead required to switch to the same thread again serves no purpose when there is only one thread and only degrades the performance of that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,5,'2025-07-17_13-31-15','If only a single thread was created, the OS will still work but the context switching will be rendered useless and the performance of the single thread will be limited. After the thread prints, it will yield, then call the PendSV interrupt. This causes the OS scheduler to implement the round-robin scheduling strategy, but since there is only one thread, it will just switch back to the current thread and the current thread will start executing again. The overhead required to switch to the same thread again serves no purpose when there is only one thread and only degrades the performance of that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,5,'2025-07-17_13-57-11','If only a single thread was created, the OS will still work but the context switching will be rendered useless and the performance of the single thread will be limited. After the thread prints, it will yield, then call the PendSV interrupt. This causes the OS scheduler to implement the round-robin scheduling strategy, but since there is only one thread, it will just switch back to the current thread and the current thread will start executing again. The overhead required to switch to the same thread again serves no purpose when there is only one thread and only degrades the performance of that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',4,5,'2025-07-26_19-16-28','If only a single thread was created, the OS will still work but the context switching will be rendered useless and the performance of the single thread will be limited. After the thread prints, it will yield, then call the PendSV interrupt. This causes the OS scheduler to implement the round-robin scheduling strategy, but since there is only one thread, it will just switch back to the current thread and the current thread will start executing again. The overhead required to switch to the same thread again serves no purpose when there is only one thread and only degrades the performance of that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,1,'2025-07-17_13-37-39','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first needs to define their thread function to accept a single void* argument. If multiple arguments are needed, the user can group them into a struct and pass a pointer to the struct instead. The OS then modifies the osCreateThread function to fill the R0 register with a pointer to the user’s data. This works because input arguments to functions are passed via registers R0 through R3, and a void* can be cast to an int and stored in R0. When the thread runs, it can acst the void* argument back to the appropriate type and dereference it to access the data.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,1,'2025-07-26_19-44-43','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first needs to define their thread function to accept a single void* argument. If multiple arguments are needed, the user can group them into a struct and pass a pointer to the struct instead. The OS then modifies the osCreateThread function to fill the R0 register with a pointer to the user’s data. This works because input arguments to functions are passed via registers R0 through R3, and a void* can be cast to an int and stored in R0. When the thread runs, it can acst the void* argument back to the appropriate type and dereference it to access the data.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',5,1,'2025-07-28_14-42-23','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must first define their thread function to accept a single void* argument. If multiple arguments are needed, they can be bundled into a struct and the user passes a pointer to that struct. WHen the user calls osCreateThread, they supply this pointer as a parameter. The OS, during thread creation, sets up the thread’s initial stack frame so that when the thread starts executing, the processor’s context is restored with the pointer argument placed in register R0. This setup aligns with the ABI which specifies that the first function argument is passed in R0. Since void* is a 32-bit pointer on a 32-bit system like Cortex-M4, it naturally fits in R0 without any casting or conversion. When the thread starts running, it retrieves the argument from R0, casts it to the appropriate type, and dereferences it if needed. This mechanism works because the OS prepares the stack and register context according to the ABI, enabling seamless function invocation when the thread is resumed.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,2,'2025-07-17_13-37-39','Calling osYield osYield inside context_switching functions like PendSV_Handler and SysTick_handler is forbidden because osYield triggers a context switch but these handlers are already executing the OS’s context-switching logic. Doing this would cause a nested context switch which can cause unpredictable issues like stack corruption.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',5,2,'2025-07-26_19-44-43','You can’t call osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler or SysTick_Handler because osYield triggers an SVC interrupt, which relies on a clean interrupt context to perform a context switch. Calling it during another context switch creates a nested context switch, which can corrupt the stack. This stack corruption can lead to a hardfault. Avoiding this ensures the OS maintains reliable and predictable behavior during multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,2,'2025-07-28_14-42-23','You can’t call osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler or SysTick_Handler because osYield triggers an SVC interrupt, which relies on a clean interrupt context to perform a context switch. Calling it during another context switch creates a nested context switch, which can corrupt the stack. This stack corruption can lead to a hardfault. Avoiding this ensures the OS maintains reliable and predictable behavior during multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,3,'2025-07-17_13-37-39','Changing the OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms instead would allow for faster response times and higher resolution of the OS. This allows the OS to respond to events with greater agility compared to the current 1ms limit. The downside of doing so however would be the large overhead required to do so. The SysTick_Handler interrupt would trigger 10 times more often and consume more CPU cycles for interrupt processing, leading to higher power consumption and reducing processing time available for user threads.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,3,'2025-07-26_19-44-43','Changing the OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms instead would allow for faster response times and higher resolution of the OS. This allows the OS to respond to events with greater agility compared to the current 1ms limit. The downside of doing so however would be the large overhead required to do so. The SysTick_Handler interrupt would trigger 10 times more often and consume more CPU cycles for interrupt processing, leading to higher power consumption and reducing processing time available for user threads.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,3,'2025-07-28_14-42-23','Changing the OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms instead would allow for faster response times and higher resolution of the OS. This allows the OS to respond to events with greater agility compared to the current 1ms limit. The downside of doing so however would be the large overhead required to do so. The SysTick_Handler interrupt would trigger 10 times more often and consume more CPU cycles for interrupt processing, leading to higher power consumption and reducing processing time available for user threads.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,4,'2025-07-17_13-37-39','Co-operative multitasking from the user’s perspective requires threads to explicitly yield control of the CPU by calling a function like osYield. In this model, the OS performs a context switch only when a thread voluntarily gives up its execution time. In pre-emptive multitasking, threads don’t need to yield and the OS takes responsibility for switching tasks automatically. The OS does this by using SysTick_Handler to decrement the currently running thread’s runtime, which when reaches zero, triggers a context switch using PendSV.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,4,'2025-07-26_19-44-43','Co-operative multitasking from the user’s perspective requires threads to explicitly yield control of the CPU by calling a function like osYield. In this model, the OS performs a context switch only when a thread voluntarily gives up its execution time. In pre-emptive multitasking, threads don’t need to yield and the OS takes responsibility for switching tasks automatically. The OS does this by using SysTick_Handler to decrement the currently running thread’s runtime, which when reaches zero, triggers a context switch using PendSV.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,4,'2025-07-28_14-42-23','Co-operative multitasking from the user’s perspective requires threads to explicitly yield control of the CPU by calling a function like osYield. In this model, the OS performs a context switch only when a thread voluntarily gives up its execution time. In pre-emptive multitasking, threads don’t need to yield and the OS takes responsibility for switching tasks automatically. The OS does this by using SysTick_Handler to decrement the currently running thread’s runtime, which when reaches zero, triggers a context switch using PendSV.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,5,'2025-07-17_13-37-39','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking work together by allowing threads to either voluntarily yield control using osYield, or be involuntarily switched by the OS based on its allocated runtime. If a thread yields, the OS performs a context switch and resets its runtime. If it doesn’t yield, the SysTick_Handler decrements its runtime and PendSV triggers a pre-emptive switch when the time expires. This approach of using both ensures CPU fairness and optional thread control.\n',0,'s25'),('a52cao',5,5,'2025-07-26_19-44-43','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking work together by both ultimately relying on the same context switch mechanism, the PendSV handler. When a thread calls osYield, it voluntarily triggers an SVC interrupt, which eventually leads to PendSV being set. In pre-emptive switching, the SysTick_Handler decrements a thread’s runtime and when it reaches zero, it also sets PendSV. In both cases, the actual context switch is performed by the PendSV handler, meaning the same low-level code path is used. This shared mechanism ensures consistency in how context switches are handled, regardless of how they are initiated, simplifying the OS design and maintaining fairness and control.\n',1,'s25'),('a52cao',5,5,'2025-07-28_14-42-23','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking work together by both ultimately relying on the same context switch mechanism, the PendSV handler. When a thread calls osYield, it voluntarily triggers an SVC interrupt, which eventually leads to PendSV being set. In pre-emptive switching, the SysTick_Handler decrements a thread’s runtime and when it reaches zero, it also sets PendSV. In both cases, the actual context switch is performed by the PendSV handler, meaning the same low-level code path is used. This shared mechanism ensures consistency in how context switches are handled, regardless of how they are initiated, simplifying the OS design and maintaining fairness and control.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,1,'2025-05-21_18-24-03','HAL stands for Hardware Extraction Layer and it represents  the interface between hardware and operating system, essentially it allows software code to be implemented on a physical hardware component.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,1,'2025-05-21_18-41-30','The __io_putchar function is used to send a single character to an output device, such as UART2, and is repeatedly called by printf to print strings character by character. It needs to be implemented or redefined in embedded projects to match the specific hardware configuration, since standard printf doesn\'t know how to handle hardware I/O on its own. Without this function, printf would not know where to send its output, making it unusable for debugging or communication on different devices or UART channels.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,1,'2025-05-21_18-48-43','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it serves as an interface between the software and the underlying hardware in an embedded system. It simplifies development by providing standardized functions to interact with hardware components, allowing developers to write code without needing to manage hardware details directly. This improves portability and code reuse, as the same application code can run on different hardware platforms with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,2,'2025-05-21_18-24-03','The purpose of this function is to write a single character to the appropriate IO channel which in this case is uart2. The printf function repeatedly calls this when we pass in a string to print out.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,2,'2025-05-21_18-48-43','The __io_putchar function is used to send a single character to an output device, such as UART2, and is repeatedly called by printf to print strings character by character. It needs to be implemented or redefined in embedded projects to match the specific hardware configuration, since standard printf doesn\'t know how to handle hardware I/O on its own. Without this function, printf would not know where to send its output, making it unusable for debugging or communication on different devices or UART channels.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,3,'2025-05-21_18-24-03','We used the debugger because it provides real-time visibility into the components internal state such as register values, memory content and stack (including msp, psp). ‘printf’ only gives limited text output while the debugger allows us to set breakpoints and step through the code line by line.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,3,'2025-05-21_18-48-43','We used the debugger because it allows direct inspection of low-level hardware state, such as processor registers, memory, and stack pointers, which `printf` cannot access. `printf` is limited to displaying high-level program output and relies on properly initialized peripherals like UART, which may not be set up or usable early in the program. In contrast, the debugger works independently of the code\'s execution state and provides precise control with features like breakpoints and single-stepping, making it essential for diagnosing hardware-level issues.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,4,'2025-05-21_18-24-03','We had the initial MSP value from address 0x0, which marks the top of the main stack. To create a new stack for PSP, we subtracted the desired stack size (0x400 bytes) from the MSP value. Knowing the initial MSP location is essential to avoid memory overlap and to correctly place new stack regions.??\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,4,'2025-05-21_18-48-43','We obtained the initial MSP value from address 0x0, which gives us the top of the main stack. To create a new stack for the PSP, we subtracted a fixed stack size (e.g., 0x400 bytes) from the MSP to get a new, valid stack region. Knowing the initial MSP is crucial because it serves as a fixed reference point in memory, ensuring that the PSP is placed within a safe and reserved area of RAM. Without this known starting point, we couldn\'t guarantee that the PSP wouldn\'t overlap with other memory regions or fall outside the valid stack space.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,5,'2025-05-21_18-24-03','To support multiple stacks with the same allocation method, you would allocate a contiguous block of memory large enough to hold all stacks and compute each stack\'s starting address using an offset based on its index and the fixed stack size. Since the number of stacks is known at compile-time, this offset can be calculated deterministically using simple arithmetic.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',1,5,'2025-05-21_18-48-43','To support multiple stacks, we allocate a contiguous block of memory large enough to hold all stacks and compute each stack’s starting address by offsetting from a base address using the stack’s index and a fixed, properly aligned stack size. This ensures each stack starts at a valid, aligned address and avoids overlap with neighboring stacks. Ignoring alignment or incorrect offset calculations could lead to invalid memory access or stack corruption, so careful sizing and alignment are critical to keeping each stack isolated and valid.',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',2,1,'2025-05-27_10-06-39','In the lab, when we changed PC to hold the function pointer to our print continuously function, we are essentially telling the CPU to jump to the memory address where the print continuously function lives and start executing there. This manual change allows us to simulate starting a new thread by setting up the context and tricking the CPU into thinking it had already been running that function.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',2,1,'2025-05-27_10-25-50','In the lab, when we changed PC to hold the function pointer to our print continuously function, we are essentially telling the CPU to jump to the memory address where the print continuously function lives and start executing there. This manual change allows us to simulate starting a new thread by setting up the context and tricking the CPU into thinking it had already been running that function.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',2,2,'2025-05-27_10-06-39','A system call is a way for a program to ask the operating system (or kernel) to do something it normally can’t do directly like accessing hardware, switching threads or managing memory. In embedded systems, a system call is usually done using the SVC instruction which triggers an interrupt. This causes the CPU to switch into a special privileged mode and jump to a handler where special operations like starting a thread or switching stack pointers can happen safely.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',2,2,'2025-05-27_10-25-50','A system call is a special kind of function call that allows a user-level program to request a service from the operating system or kernel, such as accessing hardware, switching threads, or managing memory. Unlike normal function calls, system calls use a controlled interface—typically through an instruction like `SVC` (Supervisor Call)—which triggers an interrupt and switches the CPU into privileged mode. This mechanism not only enables low-level operations but also enforces security by preventing untrusted user code from directly accessing or modifying critical system resources. The kernel handles the request in a safe and controlled way, ensuring that only approved operations are performed on hardware and sensitive data.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',2,3,'2025-05-27_10-06-39','Setting up a thread stack means preparing a section of memory that the thread will use to store temporary data while it runs, including register values. We manually placed specific values on the stack starting with xPSR and function’s address (PC) followed by other registers. These register values are stored on a stack so that when the system call runs and the CPU pops them back into the real hardware registers, it can resume execution as if the thread had already been running. This setup tricks the processor into entering a new thread function correctly, with all the context it expects.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',2,3,'2025-05-27_10-25-50','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a section of memory that the thread will use to store temporary data while it runs, including register values. Before placing any values on the stack, we must allocate a valid memory location for the stack—this usually means assigning a block of memory and setting the stack pointer (`PSP`) to point to the top of that block. Once this is done, we manually place specific values onto the stack, starting with the xPSR and the function’s address (PC), followed by other registers. These register values are stored so that when the system call runs and the CPU pops them back into the hardware registers, it can resume execution as if the thread had already been running. This setup tricks the processor into entering a new thread function correctly, with all the context it expects.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',2,4,'2025-05-27_10-06-39','If we didn’t set PSP using __set_PSP function before running the thread, the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread’s stack is located. It would try to use an invalid or uninitialized stack pointer and when it attempts to pop register values from that bad location, it would access memory that doesn’t belong to it or doesn’t exist. This causes a hard fault, which is a type of crash that happens when the processor hits a serious error like accessing invalid memory. Setting PSP correctly ensures the CPU uses the right stack for the thread safely to restore its context and start running.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',2,4,'2025-05-27_10-25-50','If we didn’t set the PSP using the `__set_PSP` function before running the thread, the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread’s stack is located. Unlike the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is used for kernel and interrupt-level code, the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is specifically used for managing user threads. If PSP is not correctly set to the exact address of the thread’s stack, the CPU will try to pop register values from an invalid or uninitialized memory location, which can result in accessing memory that doesn’t belong to the thread or doesn’t exist at all. This triggers a hard fault—a serious crash caused by illegal memory access. Setting PSP properly ensures that the CPU uses the thread’s assigned stack to restore context safely and start execution correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',2,5,'2025-05-27_10-06-39','The looped thread function keeps printing the message we intend over and over forever. However, if it did not loop continuously and just printed once and returned, the CPU would try to continue execution from wherever the function returned to, but in this case, there isn’t a valid return address. Since we manually set up stack and context for the thread, there’s no proper place for it to return to. This usually causes a crash, often a hard fault, because the CPU jumps to an invalid memory location. In short, if the thread function returns without an infinite loop, the system doesn’t know where to go next and will likely crash.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',2,5,'2025-05-27_10-25-50','The looped thread function keeps printing the message continuously as intended. However, if the thread function only printed once and returned, the CPU would attempt to continue execution using a return address popped from the thread’s stack. Because we manually set up the thread’s stack and context but did not include a valid return address on that stack, the CPU would pop an invalid or missing return address. This causes it to jump to an undefined or invalid memory location, leading to a crash—often a hard fault. In short, the absence of a proper return address on the thread’s manually prepared stack means the CPU does not know where to return after the function ends, causing the system to fail.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,1,'2025-06-24_14-14-16','When a program has multiple .c files, each file is compiled individually into an object file. These object files contain machine code as well as a list of unresolved symbols, such as functions or variables that are used but not defined within that file. The linker then combines all the object files, resolving symbols by matching each use with its definition across the project. This linking process produces a single executable file that can be run on the hardware. Compiling files separately helps keep the code modular, easier to maintain, and more efficient to update.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,1,'2025-06-24_14-44-34','Including a .c file using a #include directive is a bad idea because it pastes the entire contents of the file into another before compilation. This causes multiple definitions of the same functions or variables, which leads to compiler or linker errors. Instead, only header files (.h) should be included, as they contain declarations and not actual implementations. This approach keeps code well-organized and ensures each source file is compiled only once. It also avoids unnecessary duplication and makes debugging and project structure more manageable.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,1,'2025-06-24_14-50-26','The compilation process refers to the set of steps that convert human-readable source code into a machine-executable program. This process typically involves three main stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. In the preprocessing stage, the compiler handles special commands in the code, such as include statements and macro definitions. This step inserts the contents of header files and replaces macros, which prepares the source code for the next stage. Next, during the compiling stage, each source file is translated into an object file. These object files contain machine code but may also include references to functions or variables that are not defined in the same file. This modular approach allows programs to be split across multiple files for better organization and easier maintenance. Finally, the linking stage takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable file. The linker resolves any remaining references between the files by matching each function or variable use with its correct definition. Once all symbols are resolved, the result is a complete executable that can run on the target system.\nThis structured process ensures that large programs can be built efficiently while keeping the codebase modular and manageable.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,1,'2025-06-26_19-55-10','The compilation process refers to the set of steps that convert human-readable source code into a machine-executable program. This process typically involves three main stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. In the preprocessing stage, the compiler handles special commands in the code, such as include statements and macro definitions. This step inserts the contents of header files and replaces macros, which prepares the source code for the next stage. Next, during the compiling stage, each source file is translated into an object file. These object files contain machine code but may also include references to functions or variables that are not defined in the same file. This modular approach allows programs to be split across multiple files for better organization and easier maintenance. Finally, the linking stage takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable file. The linker resolves any remaining references between the files by matching each function or variable use with its correct definition. Once all symbols are resolved, the result is a complete executable that can run on the target system.\nThis structured process ensures that large programs can be built efficiently while keeping the codebase modular and manageable.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,2,'2025-06-24_14-14-16','Including a .c file using a #include directive is a bad idea because it pastes the entire contents of the file into another before compilation. This causes multiple definitions of the same functions or variables, which leads to compiler or linker errors. Instead, only header files (.h) should be included, as they contain declarations and not actual implementations. This approach keeps code well-organized and ensures each source file is compiled only once. It also avoids unnecessary duplication and makes debugging and project structure more manageable.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,2,'2025-06-24_14-44-34','To set up and start a thread, the user first calls a kernel initialization function to prepare internal kernel variables and the stack pool. Then, the user writes a thread function in the main file that contains the code they want to run. After that, the user creates a thread by passing this function to the thread creation API, which allocates a stack and prepares the thread\'s execution context. Finally, the user starts the kernel, which triggers the scheduler and begins running the created thread. This entire process ensures that the thread is set up and executed properly using the kernel’s support.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,2,'2025-06-24_14-50-26','Including a .c file using a #include directive is a bad idea because it pastes the entire contents of the file into another before compilation. This causes multiple definitions of the same functions or variables, which leads to compiler or linker errors. Instead, only header files (.h) should be included, as they contain declarations and not actual implementations. This approach keeps code well-organized and ensures each source file is compiled only once. It also avoids unnecessary duplication and makes debugging and project structure more manageable.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,2,'2025-06-26_19-55-10','Including a .c file using a #include directive is a bad idea because it pastes the entire contents of the file into another before compilation. This causes multiple definitions of the same functions or variables, which leads to compiler or linker errors. Instead, only header files (.h) should be included, as they contain declarations and not actual implementations. This approach keeps code well-organized and ensures each source file is compiled only once. It also avoids unnecessary duplication and makes debugging and project structure more manageable.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,3,'2025-06-24_14-14-16','To set up and start a thread, the user first calls a kernel initialization function to prepare internal kernel variables and the stack pool. Then, the user writes a thread function in the main file that contains the code they want to run. After that, the user creates a thread by passing this function to the thread creation API, which allocates a stack and prepares the thread\'s execution context. Finally, the user starts the kernel, which triggers the scheduler and begins running the created thread. This entire process ensures that the thread is set up and executed properly using the kernel’s support.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,3,'2025-06-24_14-44-34','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used to give each thread its own private stack space. It is carved up into equal-sized segments, with each new thread receiving a stack from the top down. This prevents different threads from interfering with each other’s memory during execution. In future labs, this system will make it possible to support multiple threads at once by tracking each thread’s stack location and keeping them isolated. This design also improves system safety and predictability, especially in a real-time embedded environment.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,3,'2025-06-24_14-50-26','To set up and start a thread, the user first calls a kernel initialization function to prepare internal kernel variables and the stack pool. Then, the user writes a thread function in the main file that contains the code they want to run. After that, the user creates a thread by passing this function to the thread creation API, which allocates a stack and prepares the thread\'s execution context. Finally, the user starts the kernel, which triggers the scheduler and begins running the created thread. This entire process ensures that the thread is set up and executed properly using the kernel’s support.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,3,'2025-06-26_19-55-10','To set up and start a thread, the user first calls a kernel initialization function to prepare internal kernel variables and the stack pool. Then, the user writes a thread function in the main file that contains the code they want to run. After that, the user creates a thread by passing this function to the thread creation API, which allocates a stack and prepares the thread\'s execution context. Finally, the user starts the kernel, which triggers the scheduler and begins running the created thread. This entire process ensures that the thread is set up and executed properly using the kernel’s support.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,4,'2025-06-24_14-14-16','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used to give each thread its own private stack space. It is carved up into equal-sized segments, with each new thread receiving a stack from the top down. This prevents different threads from interfering with each other’s memory during execution. In future labs, this system will make it possible to support multiple threads at once by tracking each thread’s stack location and keeping them isolated. This design also improves system safety and predictability, especially in a real-time embedded environment.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,4,'2025-06-24_14-44-34','The kernel handles low-level system operations and lives in the kernel.c and kernel.h files. It manages memory, thread creation, and execution, and exposes a clean interface that the user can call. The user code, written in main.c, focuses only on writing thread functions and calling kernel services. Keeping this separation ensures that the user does not accidentally break or modify the kernel’s internal logic. It also makes the system more modular and easier to understand, test, and expand in future labs.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,4,'2025-06-24_14-50-26','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used to give each thread its own private stack space. It is carved up into equal-sized segments, with each new thread receiving a stack from the top down. This prevents different threads from interfering with each other’s memory during execution. In future labs, this system will make it possible to support multiple threads at once by tracking each thread’s stack location and keeping them isolated. This design also improves system safety and predictability, especially in a real-time embedded environment.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,4,'2025-06-26_19-55-10','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used to give each thread its own private stack space. It is carved up into equal-sized segments, with each new thread receiving a stack from the top down. This prevents different threads from interfering with each other’s memory during execution. In future labs, this system will make it possible to support multiple threads at once by tracking each thread’s stack location and keeping them isolated. This design also improves system safety and predictability, especially in a real-time embedded environment.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,5,'2025-06-24_14-14-16','The kernel handles low-level system operations and lives in the kernel.c and kernel.h files. It manages memory, thread creation, and execution, and exposes a clean interface that the user can call. The user code, written in main.c, focuses only on writing thread functions and calling kernel services. Keeping this separation ensures that the user does not accidentally break or modify the kernel’s internal logic. It also makes the system more modular and easier to understand, test, and expand in future labs.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,5,'2025-06-24_14-50-26','The kernel handles low-level system operations and lives in the kernel.c and kernel.h files. It manages memory, thread creation, and execution, and exposes a clean interface that the user can call. The user code, written in main.c, focuses only on writing thread functions and calling kernel services. Keeping this separation ensures that the user does not accidentally break or modify the kernel’s internal logic. It also makes the system more modular and easier to understand, test, and expand in future labs.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',3,5,'2025-06-26_19-55-10','The kernel handles low-level system operations and lives in the kernel.c and kernel.h files. It manages memory, thread creation, and execution, and exposes a clean interface that the user can call. The user code, written in main.c, focuses only on writing thread functions and calling kernel services. Keeping this separation ensures that the user does not accidentally break or modify the kernel’s internal logic. It also makes the system more modular and easier to understand, test, and expand in future labs.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,1,'2025-07-02_09-46-49','Round-robin scheduling naturally matches how a queue works: you take the task at the front of the line, let it run, and then put it at the back when it yields. This behavior is exactly what queues are designed for. Using an array can work too, but then you have to manually track which index you\'re at and wrap around when needed. With a queue, advancing to the next thread happens automatically through enqueue and dequeue operations. Queues also make it easier to add or remove tasks (for example, if a thread blocks or exits), while arrays may leave unused slots or need compacting. However, arrays use less memory per thread, which is important in embedded systems. In this lab, both approaches are fine, but a queue better models real multitasking behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,1,'2025-07-02_09-53-46','Round-robin scheduling naturally matches how a queue works: you take the task at the front of the line, let it run, and then put it at the back when it yields. This behavior is exactly what queues are designed for. Using an array can work too, but then you have to manually track which index you\'re at and wrap around when needed. With a queue, advancing to the next thread happens automatically through enqueue and dequeue operations. Queues also make it easier to add or remove tasks (for example, if a thread blocks or exits), while arrays may leave unused slots or need compacting. However, arrays use less memory per thread, which is important in embedded systems. In this lab, both approaches are fine, but a queue better models real multitasking behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,1,'2025-07-02_10-03-23','The round-robin scheduler we use in this lab already behaves like a queue, even though it’s implemented with an array and an index. Each time a thread yields, the scheduler saves that thread’s state and moves to the next one by incrementing an index. Once the end of the array is reached, it wraps back to the beginning. This rotation mimics how a queue operates — taking the task at the front, running it, and then placing it at the back — just done through indexing instead of actual enqueue and dequeue operations. Using a real queue instead of an array introduces some trade-offs. While a queue might seem more elegant in modeling task rotation, it adds memory overhead because each thread needs to store a pointer to the next one. It also introduces extra pointer operations, which can slightly increase the time it takes to schedule threads. On a small embedded system where memory and performance are limited, these extra costs are often not justified. Since the current array-based method achieves the same result with less overhead, it is typically the better choice for a simple cooperative multitasking system like the one in this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,2,'2025-07-02_09-46-49','The PendSV_Handler function handles switching from one thread to another. When it runs, it first saves the current thread’s state by pushing its registers (R4 to R11) onto its stack. Then it stores the resulting stack pointer into the thread’s metadata so the thread can be resumed later. After that, it calls the scheduler function, osSched, which chooses the next thread to run and returns that thread’s saved stack pointer. PendSV_Handler then loads this new stack pointer, restores the new thread’s registers by popping them from its stack, and exits the interrupt. At that point, the CPU automatically restores the rest of the context and begins executing the new thread. This lets one thread yield the CPU and another resume from where it left off.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,2,'2025-07-02_09-53-46','When PendSV_Handler begins executing, the first thing it must do is retrieve the stack pointer of the currently running thread. This is done by reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) register, which holds the address of the current thread\'s stack. This is a critical step because all further register saves must be written to this stack. Once the PSP is read, the handler pushes the software-saved registers (R4 to R11) onto the stack, preserving the thread’s context. The updated PSP value, now pointing below the saved registers, is stored into the thread’s metadata (typically a thread control block) so the thread can be resumed later. The handler then calls the C function osSched, which selects the next thread to run and returns its saved PSP. PendSV_Handler sets the PSP to this new value, pops the registers from the new thread\'s stack to restore its context, and performs an exception return. This causes the CPU to automatically restore the rest of the hardware-saved registers and resume execution of the newly selected thread. This entire process allows one thread to yield and another to resume seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,2,'2025-07-02_10-03-23','When PendSV_Handler begins executing, the first thing it must do is retrieve the stack pointer of the currently running thread. This is done by reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) register, which holds the address of the current thread\'s stack. This is a critical step because all further register saves must be written to this stack. Once the PSP is read, the handler pushes the software-saved registers (R4 to R11) onto the stack, preserving the thread’s context. The updated PSP value, now pointing below the saved registers, is stored into the thread’s metadata (typically a thread control block) so the thread can be resumed later. The handler then calls the C function osSched, which selects the next thread to run and returns its saved PSP. PendSV_Handler sets the PSP to this new value, pops the registers from the new thread\'s stack to restore its context, and performs an exception return. This causes the CPU to automatically restore the rest of the hardware-saved registers and resume execution of the newly selected thread. This entire process allows one thread to yield and another to resume seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,3,'2025-07-02_09-46-49','If your scheduler always picks the thread with the highest priority value, it may keep selecting the same thread over and over again, especially if that thread never blocks. This causes lower-priority threads to be starved and never get a chance to run, even if they’re ready. In more advanced operating systems, techniques like aging or priority inheritance are used to prevent starvation. But in our simple cooperative system, picking only the highest-priority thread can be unfair and unsafe. That’s why round-robin is preferred in this lab — it ensures that every thread gets a turn, making the system predictable and fair.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,3,'2025-07-02_09-53-46','If your scheduler always picks the thread with the highest priority value, it may keep selecting the same thread over and over again, especially if that thread never blocks. This causes lower-priority threads to be starved and never get a chance to run, even if they’re ready. In more advanced operating systems, techniques like aging or priority inheritance are used to prevent starvation. But in our simple cooperative system, picking only the highest-priority thread can be unfair and unsafe. That’s why round-robin is preferred in this lab — it ensures that every thread gets a turn, making the system predictable and fair.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,3,'2025-07-02_10-03-23','If your scheduler always picks the thread with the highest priority value, it may keep selecting the same thread over and over again, especially if that thread never blocks. This causes lower-priority threads to be starved and never get a chance to run, even if they’re ready. In more advanced operating systems, techniques like aging or priority inheritance are used to prevent starvation. But in our simple cooperative system, picking only the highest-priority thread can be unfair and unsafe. That’s why round-robin is preferred in this lab — it ensures that every thread gets a turn, making the system predictable and fair.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,4,'2025-07-02_09-46-49','Using C for logic like the scheduler makes the code much easier to write, read, and debug. It lets you use familiar syntax, avoid manual register management, and make changes quickly. On the other hand, some things — like saving and restoring CPU registers or performing an exception return — must be done in assembly because they directly interact with the hardware. By combining both languages, you get the best of both worlds: you use assembly where it’s necessary for low-level control, and C where it’s better for logic and readability. This mixed approach is common in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,4,'2025-07-02_09-53-46','Using C for logic like the scheduler makes the code much easier to write, read, and debug. It lets you use familiar syntax, avoid manual register management, and make changes quickly. On the other hand, some things — like saving and restoring CPU registers or performing an exception return — must be done in assembly because they directly interact with the hardware. By combining both languages, you get the best of both worlds: you use assembly where it’s necessary for low-level control, and C where it’s better for logic and readability. This mixed approach is common in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,4,'2025-07-02_10-03-23','Using C for logic like the scheduler makes the code much easier to write, read, and debug. It lets you use familiar syntax, avoid manual register management, and make changes quickly. On the other hand, some things — like saving and restoring CPU registers or performing an exception return — must be done in assembly because they directly interact with the hardware. By combining both languages, you get the best of both worlds: you use assembly where it’s necessary for low-level control, and C where it’s better for logic and readability. This mixed approach is common in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,5,'2025-07-02_09-46-49','Even with just one thread, the kernel still goes through all the motions of context switching. When the thread calls osYield, the system saves its state, picks the “next” thread (which is itself), and restores its state. So while nothing really changes, the system still functions correctly. The thread keeps running and yielding, but since there are no other threads to switch to, it just resumes itself. This shows that the multitasking infrastructure is in place, even if it’s not fully used yet. That’s why the system continues printing, and it’s a good sign that the core threading logic is working.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,5,'2025-07-02_09-53-46','Even with just one thread, the kernel still goes through all the motions of context switching. When the thread calls osYield, the system saves its state, picks the “next” thread (which is itself), and restores its state. So while nothing really changes, the system still functions correctly. The thread keeps running and yielding, but since there are no other threads to switch to, it just resumes itself. This shows that the multitasking infrastructure is in place, even if it’s not fully used yet. That’s why the system continues printing, and it’s a good sign that the core threading logic is working.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',4,5,'2025-07-02_10-03-23','Even with just one thread, the kernel still goes through all the motions of context switching. When the thread calls osYield, the system saves its state, picks the “next” thread (which is itself), and restores its state. So while nothing really changes, the system still functions correctly. The thread keeps running and yielding, but since there are no other threads to switch to, it just resumes itself. This shows that the multitasking infrastructure is in place, even if it’s not fully used yet. That’s why the system continues printing, and it’s a good sign that the core threading logic is working.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,1,'2025-07-13_15-29-13','To pass an argument into a thread, the user designs their thread function to accept a single void pointer parameter. This pointer can point to any data type or structure the user wants to pass. When creating the thread, the user provides a pointer to the data as the argument. On the operating system side, during thread creation, the OS allocates and sets up the thread’s stack. It specifically places the pointer to the argument in the location on the stack corresponding to the processor register R0, which is used to pass the first argument in the ARM calling convention. This ensures that when the thread starts executing, the argument pointer is already in the correct register for the thread function to access. This works because the OS follows the ARM ABI rules for function calls, so the thread function receives the pointer seamlessly without the OS needing to know the data’s type or structure.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,1,'2025-07-13_16-53-41','To pass an argument into a thread, the user designs their thread function to accept a single void pointer parameter. This pointer can point to any data type or structure the user wants to pass. When creating the thread, the user provides a pointer to the data as the argument. On the operating system side, during thread creation, the OS allocates and sets up the thread’s stack. It specifically places the pointer to the argument in the location on the stack corresponding to the processor register R0, which is used to pass the first argument in the ARM calling convention. This ensures that when the thread starts executing, the argument pointer is already in the correct register for the thread function to access. This works because the OS follows the ARM ABI rules for function calls, so the thread function receives the pointer seamlessly without the OS needing to know the data’s type or structure.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,1,'2025-07-13_17-02-12','To pass an argument into a thread, the user designs their thread function to accept a single void pointer parameter. This pointer can point to any data type or structure the user wants to pass. When creating the thread, the user provides a pointer to the data as the argument. On the operating system side, during thread creation, the OS allocates and sets up the thread’s stack. It specifically places the pointer to the argument in the location on the stack corresponding to the processor register R0, which is used to pass the first argument in the ARM calling convention. This ensures that when the thread starts executing, the argument pointer is already in the correct register for the thread function to access. This works because the OS follows the ARM ABI rules for function calls, so the thread function receives the pointer seamlessly without the OS needing to know the data’s type or structure.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,1,'2025-07-13_17-09-39','Calling osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV Handler, SVC Handler, or SysTick Handler is problematic because osYield itself triggers a software interrupt to request a context switch. If this is done within another interrupt handler, it causes nested or recursive interrupt behavior, which can quickly overwhelm the call stack. In the Cortex-M4 architecture, such critical issues are detected by a dedicated mechanism called the HardFault handler. This handler is specifically designed to respond to serious faults such as stack corruption, invalid memory access, or nested exceptions beyond allowed depth. Unlike general system crashes that may result in erratic behavior, the HardFault mechanism traps the processor in a controlled infinite loop to prevent further damage. Triggering osYield inside an interrupt handler risks corrupting the processor’s stack frame and register state, which leads directly to a HardFault. This not only stops the system but also makes debugging much harder, which is why calling osYield in these contexts must be strictly avoided.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,1,'2025-07-13_17-17-20','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must first create the variable or allocate the required memory that will hold the data they want to pass. This can be a simple variable, like an integer, or a more complex structure, depending on the needs of the thread. The user then provides a pointer to this data as the input argument when creating the thread. On the operating system side, during the thread creation process, the OS sets up the thread’s stack and places the pointer to the argument in the location that corresponds to register R0, which is used for passing the first argument in the ARM calling convention. This ensures that when the thread begins execution, the argument pointer is already available in the correct register, allowing the thread function to receive and use the data as intended. This system works reliably because it follows the ABI standard for function calls, and by placing the pointer in R0 during stack setup, the OS guarantees that the thread function starts with access to its argument, regardless of its type or structure.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,2,'2025-07-13_15-29-13','You cannot call osYield inside context-switching functions such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because osYield itself triggers a software interrupt to request a context switch. Calling it within these interrupt handlers causes nested or recursive interrupts, which can lead to stack overflow, corrupted processor state, and unpredictable behavior. Interrupt handlers are meant to be brief and atomic, and triggering another interrupt within them breaks this assumption. This recursive behavior can overwhelm the system’s resources, causing crashes or the system to become unresponsive, which is why calling osYield in those functions is bad practice.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,2,'2025-07-13_16-53-41','You cannot call osYield inside context-switching functions such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because osYield itself triggers a software interrupt to request a context switch. Calling it within these interrupt handlers causes nested or recursive interrupts, which can lead to stack overflow, corrupted processor state, and unpredictable behavior. Interrupt handlers are meant to be brief and atomic, and triggering another interrupt within them breaks this assumption. This recursive behavior can overwhelm the system’s resources, causing crashes or the system to become unresponsive, which is why calling osYield in those functions is bad practice.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,2,'2025-07-13_17-02-12','Calling osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV Handler, SVC Handler, or SysTick Handler is problematic because osYield itself triggers a software interrupt to request a context switch. If this is done within another interrupt handler, it causes nested or recursive interrupt behavior, which can quickly overwhelm the call stack. In the Cortex-M4 architecture, such critical issues are detected by a dedicated mechanism called the HardFault handler. This handler is specifically designed to respond to serious faults such as stack corruption, invalid memory access, or nested exceptions beyond allowed depth. Unlike general system crashes that may result in erratic behavior, the HardFault mechanism traps the processor in a controlled infinite loop to prevent further damage. Triggering osYield inside an interrupt handler risks corrupting the processor’s stack frame and register state, which leads directly to a HardFault. This not only stops the system but also makes debugging much harder, which is why calling osYield in these contexts must be strictly avoided.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,2,'2025-07-13_17-09-39','Setting the OS clock to 0.1 milliseconds instead of 1 millisecond improves the system’s responsiveness and allows the OS to handle tasks and events with finer timing resolution. This can benefit applications requiring precise timing or fast reaction times. However, the downside is that the processor must handle the system tick interrupt ten times more frequently, increasing overhead and reducing the CPU time available for actual application tasks. This can cause higher power consumption and reduce efficiency, especially if most tasks do not require such fine timing. Additionally, the increased interrupt rate can lead to scheduling jitter and make it harder to meet real-time deadlines in some systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,2,'2025-07-13_17-17-20','Calling osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV Handler, SVC Handler, or SysTick Handler is problematic because osYield itself triggers a software interrupt to request a context switch. If this is done within another interrupt handler, it causes nested or recursive interrupt behavior, which can quickly overwhelm the call stack. In the Cortex-M4 architecture, such critical issues are detected by a dedicated mechanism called the HardFault handler. This handler is specifically designed to respond to serious faults such as stack corruption, invalid memory access, or nested exceptions beyond allowed depth. Unlike general system crashes that may result in erratic behavior, the HardFault mechanism traps the processor in a controlled infinite loop to prevent further damage. Triggering osYield inside an interrupt handler risks corrupting the processor’s stack frame and register state, which leads directly to a HardFault. This not only stops the system but also makes debugging much harder, which is why calling osYield in these contexts must be strictly avoided.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,3,'2025-07-13_15-29-13','Setting the OS clock to 0.1 milliseconds instead of 1 millisecond improves the system’s responsiveness and allows the OS to handle tasks and events with finer timing resolution. This can benefit applications requiring precise timing or fast reaction times. However, the downside is that the processor must handle the system tick interrupt ten times more frequently, increasing overhead and reducing the CPU time available for actual application tasks. This can cause higher power consumption and reduce efficiency, especially if most tasks do not require such fine timing. Additionally, the increased interrupt rate can lead to scheduling jitter and make it harder to meet real-time deadlines in some systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,3,'2025-07-13_16-53-41','Setting the OS clock to 0.1 milliseconds instead of 1 millisecond improves the system’s responsiveness and allows the OS to handle tasks and events with finer timing resolution. This can benefit applications requiring precise timing or fast reaction times. However, the downside is that the processor must handle the system tick interrupt ten times more frequently, increasing overhead and reducing the CPU time available for actual application tasks. This can cause higher power consumption and reduce efficiency, especially if most tasks do not require such fine timing. Additionally, the increased interrupt rate can lead to scheduling jitter and make it harder to meet real-time deadlines in some systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,3,'2025-07-13_17-02-12','Setting the OS clock to 0.1 milliseconds instead of 1 millisecond improves the system’s responsiveness and allows the OS to handle tasks and events with finer timing resolution. This can benefit applications requiring precise timing or fast reaction times. However, the downside is that the processor must handle the system tick interrupt ten times more frequently, increasing overhead and reducing the CPU time available for actual application tasks. This can cause higher power consumption and reduce efficiency, especially if most tasks do not require such fine timing. Additionally, the increased interrupt rate can lead to scheduling jitter and make it harder to meet real-time deadlines in some systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,3,'2025-07-13_17-09-39','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily give up control by calling a function like osYield. This means that the thread controls when it stops running, which can cause problems if a thread does not yield often enough. The OS in cooperative multitasking only switches context when a thread explicitly yields. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts threads based on a timer, forcing them to give up the CPU when their time slice expires, without relying on the thread’s cooperation. From the OS perspective, pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to monitor the timer, decrement runtime counters, and trigger context switches independently of thread actions, ensuring fair CPU sharing and better responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,3,'2025-07-13_17-17-20','Setting the OS clock to 0.1 milliseconds instead of 1 millisecond improves the system’s responsiveness and allows the OS to handle tasks and events with finer timing resolution. This can benefit applications requiring precise timing or fast reaction times. However, the downside is that the processor must handle the system tick interrupt ten times more frequently, increasing overhead and reducing the CPU time available for actual application tasks. This can cause higher power consumption and reduce efficiency, especially if most tasks do not require such fine timing. Additionally, the increased interrupt rate can lead to scheduling jitter and make it harder to meet real-time deadlines in some systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,4,'2025-07-13_15-29-13','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily give up control by calling a function like osYield. This means that the thread controls when it stops running, which can cause problems if a thread does not yield often enough. The OS in cooperative multitasking only switches context when a thread explicitly yields. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts threads based on a timer, forcing them to give up the CPU when their time slice expires, without relying on the thread’s cooperation. From the OS perspective, pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to monitor the timer, decrement runtime counters, and trigger context switches independently of thread actions, ensuring fair CPU sharing and better responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,4,'2025-07-13_16-53-41','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily give up control by calling a function like osYield. This means that the thread controls when it stops running, which can cause problems if a thread does not yield often enough. The OS in cooperative multitasking only switches context when a thread explicitly yields. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts threads based on a timer, forcing them to give up the CPU when their time slice expires, without relying on the thread’s cooperation. From the OS perspective, pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to monitor the timer, decrement runtime counters, and trigger context switches independently of thread actions, ensuring fair CPU sharing and better responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,4,'2025-07-13_17-02-12','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily give up control by calling a function like osYield. This means that the thread controls when it stops running, which can cause problems if a thread does not yield often enough. The OS in cooperative multitasking only switches context when a thread explicitly yields. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts threads based on a timer, forcing them to give up the CPU when their time slice expires, without relying on the thread’s cooperation. From the OS perspective, pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to monitor the timer, decrement runtime counters, and trigger context switches independently of thread actions, ensuring fair CPU sharing and better responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,4,'2025-07-13_17-09-39','Cooperative and pre-emptive context switching are able to work together because they both rely on the same underlying mechanism to perform a context switch. In this system, all context switches are ultimately triggered by the PendSV interrupt, which is responsible for switching from one thread to another. In the cooperative model, when a thread calls the yield function, the operating system responds by setting the PendSV interrupt to pending status. In the pre-emptive model, the SysTick timer generates an interrupt every millisecond, and inside the SysTick handler, the system decrements the current thread’s runtime counter. When the counter reaches zero, the system again sets the PendSV interrupt to pending. Even though one mechanism is triggered by a thread voluntarily giving up control and the other is based on a timer expiring, both rely on the same PendSV handler to do the actual switching. This shared use of PendSV allows both approaches to work seamlessly together in the same system.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,4,'2025-07-13_17-17-20','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily give up control by calling a function like osYield. This means that the thread controls when it stops running, which can cause problems if a thread does not yield often enough. The OS in cooperative multitasking only switches context when a thread explicitly yields. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts threads based on a timer, forcing them to give up the CPU when their time slice expires, without relying on the thread’s cooperation. From the OS perspective, pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to monitor the timer, decrement runtime counters, and trigger context switches independently of thread actions, ensuring fair CPU sharing and better responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,5,'2025-07-13_15-29-13','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching work together because they serve as complementary ways to switch threads. Yielding allows a thread to voluntarily give up the CPU before its time slice expires, signaling the OS to switch context immediately. Pre-emptive context switching ensures that if a thread does not yield, it will still be interrupted and switched out once its allocated time slice runs out. The OS tracks the runtime of each thread and switches context either when a yield occurs or when the timer expires, whichever happens first. This combination allows well-behaved threads to cooperate efficiently while still preventing any thread from monopolizing the CPU, creating a flexible and fair multitasking environment.\n',0,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,5,'2025-07-13_16-53-41','Cooperative and pre-emptive context switching are able to work together because they both rely on the same underlying mechanism to perform a context switch. In this system, all context switches are ultimately triggered by the PendSV interrupt, which is responsible for switching from one thread to another. In the cooperative model, when a thread calls the yield function, the operating system responds by setting the PendSV interrupt to pending status. In the pre-emptive model, the SysTick timer generates an interrupt every millisecond, and inside the SysTick handler, the system decrements the current thread’s runtime counter. When the counter reaches zero, the system again sets the PendSV interrupt to pending. Even though one mechanism is triggered by a thread voluntarily giving up control and the other is based on a timer expiring, both rely on the same PendSV handler to do the actual switching. This shared use of PendSV allows both approaches to work seamlessly together in the same system.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,5,'2025-07-13_17-02-12','Cooperative and pre-emptive context switching are able to work together because they both rely on the same underlying mechanism to perform a context switch. In this system, all context switches are ultimately triggered by the PendSV interrupt, which is responsible for switching from one thread to another. In the cooperative model, when a thread calls the yield function, the operating system responds by setting the PendSV interrupt to pending status. In the pre-emptive model, the SysTick timer generates an interrupt every millisecond, and inside the SysTick handler, the system decrements the current thread’s runtime counter. When the counter reaches zero, the system again sets the PendSV interrupt to pending. Even though one mechanism is triggered by a thread voluntarily giving up control and the other is based on a timer expiring, both rely on the same PendSV handler to do the actual switching. This shared use of PendSV allows both approaches to work seamlessly together in the same system.\n',1,'s25'),('a52sinha',5,5,'2025-07-13_17-17-20','Cooperative and pre-emptive context switching are able to work together because they both rely on the same underlying mechanism to perform a context switch. In this system, all context switches are ultimately triggered by the PendSV interrupt, which is responsible for switching from one thread to another. In the cooperative model, when a thread calls the yield function, the operating system responds by setting the PendSV interrupt to pending status. In the pre-emptive model, the SysTick timer generates an interrupt every millisecond, and inside the SysTick handler, the system decrements the current thread’s runtime counter. When the counter reaches zero, the system again sets the PendSV interrupt to pending. Even though one mechanism is triggered by a thread voluntarily giving up control and the other is based on a timer expiring, both rely on the same PendSV handler to do the actual switching. This shared use of PendSV allows both approaches to work seamlessly together in the same system.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,1,'2025-06-24_23-27-00','HAL is a set of prewritten functions that helps us control the microcontroller using high-level C functions and eliminates the need for low-level register manipulation. This abstraction makes the code more user-friendly and easier to understand, especially for beginners. It also allows the same code to work on different microcontrollers within the STM32 family. HAL takes care of setting up things like GPIOs, timers, and UART correctly, which helps avoid hardware bugs and makes development faster and safer.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,1,'2025-06-24_23-42-35','__io_putchar is a function that the programmer needs to write to connect printf to an actual hardware output—usually a serial port. Since microcontrollers don’t have built-in displays, printf needs to know where to send the text. __io_putchar acts like a bridge between printf and something like UART or USB. This function isn’t built-in because every board might use different hardware, so it’s the programmer’s job to implement it based on how they want to route output. Without defining this function, printf won’t work because it has no way to transmit characters.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,1,'2025-06-25_00-03-16','__io_putchar is a function that the programmer needs to write to connect printf to an actual hardware output—usually a serial port. Since microcontrollers don’t have built-in displays, printf needs to know where to send the text. __io_putchar acts like a bridge between printf and something like UART or USB. This function isn’t built-in because every board might use different hardware, so it’s the programmer’s job to implement it based on how they want to route output. Without defining this function, printf won’t work because it has no way to transmit characters.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,1,'2025-06-25_23-47-05','__io_putchar is a function that the programmer needs to write to connect printf to an actual hardware output—usually a serial port. Since microcontrollers don’t have built-in displays, printf needs to know where to send the text. __io_putchar acts like a bridge between printf and something like UART or USB. This function isn’t built-in because every board might use different hardware, so it’s the programmer’s job to implement it based on how they want to route output. Without defining this function, printf won’t work because it has no way to transmit characters.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,1,'2025-06-26_00-01-33','The Hal is a set of highlevel C functions that makes it easier to control the STM32 microcontroller hardware without needing to directly write to low-level registers. Its main purpose is to simplify embedded development by hiding complex hardware details and letting us work with more readable and portable code. Instead of manually setting bits in control registers, we can just call HAL functions which hand the configuration and interactions with hardware in the background. This reduces bugs and saves time, especially for beginners or when working on tight deadlines. HAL also improves code portability so it can be used across different STM 32 microcontrollers without major changes. HAL overall makes development faster, safer, and easier to maintain across different setups.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,2,'2025-06-24_23-27-00','__io_putchar is a function that tells the microcontroller how to send characters when the printf function is used. Since microcontrollers don’t have built-in screens, we need a way to send the output somewhere we can see it—like a computer. __io_putchar connects printf to a USB or UART port so the output shows up on a serial monitor. It basically acts like a translator between printf and the outside world. Without it, printf doesn’t know where to send the text, so nothing shows up.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,2,'2025-06-24_23-42-35','The debugger was used to see what was happening when the microcontroller was running. It lets us inspect the processor state at a very low level, like the values of the MSP (Main Stack Pointer) and PSP (Process Stack Pointer), and how they change during runtime. We can pause the program, step through instructions, and check memory or register values directly. We can’t just use printf for this because it only gives us text output not actual hardware details. Plus, using printf can sometimes interfere with the stack itself, which would defeat the purpose of checking if our stack setup is working properly.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,2,'2025-06-25_00-03-16','The debugger was used to see what was happening when the microcontroller was running. It lets us inspect the processor state at a very low level, like the values of the MSP (Main Stack Pointer) and PSP (Process Stack Pointer), and how they change during runtime. We can pause the program, step through instructions, and check memory or register values directly. We can’t just use printf for this because it only gives us text output not actual hardware details. Plus, using printf can sometimes interfere with the stack itself, which would defeat the purpose of checking if our stack setup is working properly.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,2,'2025-06-25_23-47-05','The debugger was used to see what was happening when the microcontroller was running. It lets us inspect the processor state at a very low level, like the values of the MSP (Main Stack Pointer) and PSP (Process Stack Pointer), and how they change during runtime. We can pause the program, step through instructions, and check memory or register values directly. We can’t just use printf for this because it only gives us text output not actual hardware details. Plus, using printf can sometimes interfere with the stack itself, which would defeat the purpose of checking if our stack setup is working properly.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,2,'2025-06-26_00-01-33','__io_putchar is a function that the programmer needs to write to connect printf to an actual hardware output—usually a serial port. Since microcontrollers don’t have built-in displays, printf needs to know where to send the text. __io_putchar acts like a bridge between printf and something like UART or USB. This function isn’t built-in because every board might use different hardware, so it’s the programmer’s job to implement it based on how they want to route output. Without defining this function, printf won’t work because it has no way to transmit characters.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,3,'2025-06-24_23-27-00','The debugger was used to see what was happening when the microcontroller was running. It lets us inspect the processor state at a very low level, like the values of the MSP (Main Stack Pointer) and PSP (Process Stack Pointer), and how they change during runtime. We can pause the program, step through instructions, and check memory or register values directly. We can’t just use printf for this because it only gives us text output not actual hardware details. Plus, using printf can sometimes interfere with the stack itself, which would defeat the purpose of checking if our stack setup is working properly.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,3,'2025-06-24_23-42-35','To allocate a PSP stack, we start by getting the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which tells us where the current stack begins in memory. From there, we subtract a fixed offset to carve out space for the PSP, making sure it doesn’t overlap with the MSP stack or other memory regions. The initial MSP value is critical because it ensures that the new PSP address is valid—it must be aligned properly, located in usable RAM, and not interfere with anything else. Without knowing where the MSP starts, we can’t safely calculate a PSP location that the processor would accept as a stack.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,3,'2025-06-25_00-03-16','To allocate a PSP stack, we start by getting the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which tells us where the current stack begins in memory. From there, we subtract a fixed offset to carve out space for the PSP, making sure it doesn’t overlap with the MSP stack or other memory regions. The initial MSP value is critical because it ensures that the new PSP address is valid—it must be aligned properly, located in usable RAM, and not interfere with anything else. Without knowing where the MSP starts, we can’t safely calculate a PSP location that the processor would accept as a stack.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,3,'2025-06-25_23-47-05','To allocate a PSP stack, we start by getting the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which tells us where the current stack begins in memory. From there, we subtract a fixed offset to carve out space for the PSP, making sure it doesn’t overlap with the MSP stack or other memory regions. The initial MSP value is critical because it ensures that the new PSP address is valid—it must be aligned properly, located in usable RAM, and not interfere with anything else. Without knowing where the MSP starts, we can’t safely calculate a PSP location that the processor would accept as a stack.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,3,'2025-06-26_00-01-33','The debugger was used to see what was happening when the microcontroller was running. It lets us inspect the processor state at a very low level, like the values of the MSP (Main Stack Pointer) and PSP (Process Stack Pointer), and how they change during runtime. We can pause the program, step through instructions, and check memory or register values directly. We can’t just use printf for this because it only gives us text output not actual hardware details. Plus, using printf can sometimes interfere with the stack itself, which would defeat the purpose of checking if our stack setup is working properly.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,4,'2025-06-24_23-27-00','The stack allocation method worked by using the MSP and using that value to try to figure out where to place the PSP. First, an initial value of the MSP is obtained, then a fixed value is subtracted from that value to create space for the PSP. The initial MSP value tells us how much memory we have to work with, so other regions of the memory isn’t interfered with. By calculating the PSP location based on this, we can avoid overwriting important memory regions like global variables, the heap, or the default stack. This method gives us control over where each stack is placed, which is important when working with multiple execution contexts or threads.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,4,'2025-06-24_23-42-35','To set up multiple stacks using the same method from the lab, we just start with the initial MSP value and divide up the memory into equal-sized blocks for each thread. Each thread gets a fixed stack size, and we also leave space for the main stack. The starting address for thread j is found by adding the main stack size to the MSP value, then adding (j - 1) times the thread stack size. This way, each thread’s stack gets placed one after the other in memory, right after the main stack. We also need to make sure the stack sizes are multiples of 8 to stay aligned—otherwise the processor could crash. On top of that, we have to check that all the stack memory fits in RAM and doesn’t overlap with anything else. As long as we do all that, we can figure out safe and separate stack locations for every thread ahead of time.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,4,'2025-06-25_00-03-16','To set up multiple stacks using the same method from the lab, we just start with the initial MSP value and divide up the memory into equal-sized blocks for each thread. Each thread gets a fixed stack size, and we also leave space for the main stack. The starting address for thread j is found by adding the main stack size to the MSP value, then adding (j - 1) times the thread stack size. This way, each thread’s stack gets placed one after the other in memory, right after the main stack. We also need to make sure the stack sizes are multiples of 8 to stay aligned—otherwise the processor could crash. On top of that, we have to check that all the stack memory fits in RAM and doesn’t overlap with anything else. As long as we do all that, we can figure out safe and separate stack locations for every thread ahead of time.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,4,'2025-06-25_23-47-05','To set up multiple stacks using the same method from the lab, we just start with the initial MSP value and divide up the memory into equal-sized blocks for each thread. Each thread gets a fixed stack size, and we also leave space for the main stack. The starting address for thread j is found by adding the main stack size to the MSP value, then adding (j - 1) times the thread stack size. This way, each thread’s stack gets placed one after the other in memory, right after the main stack. We also need to make sure the stack sizes are multiples of 8 to stay aligned—otherwise the processor could crash. On top of that, we have to check that all the stack memory fits in RAM and doesn’t overlap with anything else. As long as we do all that, we can figure out safe and separate stack locations for every thread ahead of time.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,4,'2025-06-26_00-01-33','To allocate a PSP stack, we start by getting the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which tells us where the current stack begins in memory. From there, we subtract a fixed offset to carve out space for the PSP, making sure it doesn’t overlap with the MSP stack or other memory regions. The initial MSP value is critical because it ensures that the new PSP address is valid—it must be aligned properly, located in usable RAM, and not interfere with anything else. Without knowing where the MSP starts, we can’t safely calculate a PSP location that the processor would accept as a stack.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,5,'2025-06-24_23-27-00','To set up multiple stacks using the same method from the lab, we just start with the initial MSP value and divide up the memory into equal-sized blocks for each thread. Each thread gets a fixed stack size, and we also leave space for the main stack. The starting address for thread j is found by adding the main stack size to the MSP value, then adding (j - 1) times the thread stack size. This way, each thread’s stack gets placed one after the other in memory, right after the main stack. We also need to make sure the stack sizes are multiples of 8 to stay aligned—otherwise the processor could crash. On top of that, we have to check that all the stack memory fits in RAM and doesn’t overlap with anything else. As long as we do all that, we can figure out safe and separate stack locations for every thread ahead of time.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',1,5,'2025-06-26_00-01-33','To set up multiple stacks using the same method from the lab, we just start with the initial MSP value and divide up the memory into equal-sized blocks for each thread. Each thread gets a fixed stack size, and we also leave space for the main stack. The starting address for thread j is found by adding the main stack size to the MSP value, then adding (j - 1) times the thread stack size. This way, each thread’s stack gets placed one after the other in memory, right after the main stack. We also need to make sure the stack sizes are multiples of 8 to stay aligned—otherwise the processor could crash. On top of that, we have to check that all the stack memory fits in RAM and doesn’t overlap with anything else. As long as we do all that, we can figure out safe and separate stack locations for every thread ahead of time.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',2,1,'2025-06-26_10-42-41','When I set the PC to the address of print_continuously, I basically told the processor to jump straight to that function and start running it. First, the function’s address goes into R0, and then we use an assembly instruction to move that into the PC. Since the PC controls what the CPU runs next, it instantly begins executing whatever’s at that address in this case, the print_continuously function. It’s kind of like calling a function in regular code, but instead of using a function call, we’re manually changing where the CPU goes next by updating the PC.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',2,2,'2025-06-26_10-42-41','A system call is how a program gets help from the operating system to do things it normally isn’t allowed to do on its own, like handling files, using hardware, or allocating memory. When a system call happens, the CPU switches from user mode to kernel mode using something called a trap, which basically hands over control to the OS. This switch makes sure only the OS can safely carry out sensitive tasks. So instead of a program writing directly to the hard drive, it uses a system call like write() to let the OS handle the job safely and correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',2,3,'2025-06-26_10-42-41','Setting up a thread’s stack basically means getting all the memory and register values in place so the CPU can jump into the thread as if it had already been running. We manually loaded values onto a fresh stack things like xPSR, the function address for PC, and dummy values for R0 to R12 and then pointed the PSP to that stack. When the thread starts, the CPU pops those values back into the actual registers and begins execution. This setup is important because during context switching, the system needs to save and restore everything the thread was doing. If the values aren’t in the right order, the CPU could restore the wrong data into the wrong register, which would lead to weird behavior or a crash. So the stack layout has to be spot-on for everything to work right.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',2,4,'2025-06-26_10-42-41','If we don’t set the PSP with __set_PSP, the CPU has no idea where the thread’s stack is located. Since threads use the Process Stack Pointer to pull values like the program counter, link register, and xPSR when starting up, not setting it means the processor ends up looking at junk memory. It’ll try to load registers from an invalid address, which causes a hard fault — basically the CPU’s way of saying something went seriously wrong, like accessing memory that doesn’t exist or isn’t allowed.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',2,5,'2025-06-26_10-42-41','If the thread didn’t loop and just ran once before returning, the CPU would try to continue execution using whatever’s in the Link Register (LR). But since we set LR to a dummy value when we built the thread’s stack, it doesn’t point to a real instruction. So when the function finishes and tries to return, the processor jumps to some invalid spot in memory. That ends up causing a hard fault because the CPU can’t safely run from a bogus address. This just shows how important it is to either keep the thread running or properly set up the stack with a valid return path.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',3,1,'2025-06-27_14-08-18','When you have multiple .c files in a project, each one is handled separately at first, going through preprocessing to generate a clean version of the code. Then each file is compiled into its own .o (object) file, which contains machine code but still has unresolved references to functions or variables in other files. The final step is linking, where all those object files are combined into a single executable. The linker resolves cross-file references, connects calls to external libraries like printf(), and ensures everything fits together properly. Sometimes linker scripts or startup code are also used, especially in embedded projects, to define memory layout or entry points.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',3,2,'2025-06-27_14-08-18','Including a .c file using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it basically dumps the entire contents of that source file into another one before compiling. That might sound harmless, but it messes things up—now the same code gets compiled multiple times, which can lead to errors like duplicate function definitions when the linker tries to combine everything. It also defeats the whole point of keeping your code modular and organized. Instead, the right way is to include the .h file (like #include \"kernel.h\") for the function declarations, and let each .c file compile on its own.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',3,3,'2025-06-27_14-08-18','First, you call a function to set up the kernel, which gets everything ready behind the scenes — like stack space and any internal tracking.\nThen you write the function that your thread will run. It just needs to take a void* argument and shouldn’t return anything.\nOnce that’s done, you create the thread by passing that function to a thread creation call. This tells the kernel what code to run and sets up the stack for it.\nAfter the thread is created, you start the kernel, which makes a system call to load the thread and begin running it.\nFrom there, the thread takes over and runs separately from main(), doing its own thing.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',3,4,'2025-06-27_14-08-18','The stack pool is just a reserved chunk of memory that the OS uses to give each thread its own space to work with. Every thread needs its own stack to keep track of stuff like function calls, local variables, and CPU registers. Without separate stacks, threads would end up messing with each other’s data. The stack pool makes sure that doesn’t happen by handing out a clean section of memory to each new thread. Later on, when we start running multiple threads, the OS will use this pool to set up each one with its own stack, so they can run safely at the same time.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',3,5,'2025-06-27_14-08-18','The kernel and user code are split up because they do totally different jobs. The kernel files (like kernel.c and kernel.h) handle all the low-level stuff that makes the OS run things like creating threads, setting up stacks, and handling system calls. You can think of it as the part that builds the foundation. The user code, which you write in main.c, is where you actually decide what your program does. That’s where you define your thread functions and call the kernel’s functions to get things going. This split is important because it keeps things clean — you don’t have to mess with the complicated inner workings of the OS. You just use the tools the kernel gives you, which makes your life a lot easier and your code way more organized.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,1,'2025-07-30_17-23-02','Using a queue to store your TCBs in round-robin scheduling makes the whole system easier to manage and much more reliable. Every time a thread finishes its time slice, you just move its TCB from the front of the queue to the back, so you’re always working with the next thread in line. This gets rid of the need to track positions with array indexes or deal with empty slots, which can easily happen if you use other data structures. It also makes adding or removing threads straightforward, since you don’t have to shift anything around manually the queue handles all of that for you. Overall, queues keep the scheduling process clean and efficient, especially as your OS grows and you start working with more threads.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,1,'2025-07-30_17-38-34','Using a queue to store your TCBs in round-robin scheduling makes the whole system easier to manage and much more reliable. Every time a thread finishes its time slice, you just move its TCB from the front of the queue to the back, so you’re always working with the next thread in line. This gets rid of the need to track positions with array indexes or deal with empty slots, which can easily happen if you use other data structures. It also makes adding or removing threads straightforward, since you don’t have to shift anything around manually the queue handles all of that for you. Overall, queues keep the scheduling process clean and efficient, especially as your OS grows and you start working with more threads.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,1,'2025-07-30_18-00-06','Using a queue to store your TCBs in round-robin scheduling makes the whole system easier to manage and much more reliable. Every time a thread finishes its time slice, you just move its TCB from the front of the queue to the back, so you’re always working with the next thread in line. This gets rid of the need to track positions with array indexes or deal with empty slots, which can easily happen if you use other data structures. It also makes adding or removing threads straightforward, since you don’t have to shift anything around manually the queue handles all of that for you. Overall, queues keep the scheduling process clean and efficient, especially as your OS grows and you start working with more threads.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,1,'2025-07-30_18-23-50','Using a queue to store your TCBs in round-robin scheduling makes the whole system easier to manage and much more reliable. Every time a thread finishes its time slice, you just move its TCB from the front of the queue to the back, so you’re always working with the next thread in line. This gets rid of the need to track positions with array indexes or deal with empty slots, which can easily happen if you use other data structures. It also makes adding or removing threads straightforward, since you don’t have to shift anything around manually the queue handles all of that for you. Overall, queues keep the scheduling process clean and efficient, especially as your OS grows and you start working with more threads.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,1,'2025-07-30_18-41-00','Using a queue to store your TCBs in round-robin scheduling makes the whole system easier to manage and much more reliable. Every time a thread finishes its time slice, you just move its TCB from the front of the queue to the back, so you’re always working with the next thread in line. This gets rid of the need to track positions with array indexes or deal with empty slots, which can easily happen if you use other data structures. It also makes adding or removing threads straightforward, since you don’t have to shift anything around manually the queue handles all of that for you. Overall, queues keep the scheduling process clean and efficient, especially as your OS grows and you start working with more threads.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,1,'2025-07-31_11-10-21','Using a queue to store your TCBs in round-robin scheduling makes the whole system easier to manage and much more reliable. Every time a thread finishes its time slice, you just move its TCB from the front of the queue to the back, so you’re always working with the next thread in line. This gets rid of the need to track positions with array indexes or deal with empty slots, which can easily happen if you use other data structures. It also makes adding or removing threads straightforward, since you don’t have to shift anything around manually the queue handles all of that for you. Overall, queues keep the scheduling process clean and efficient, especially as your OS grows and you start working with more threads.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,1,'2025-07-31_11-20-07','Using a queue to store your TCBs in round-robin scheduling makes the whole system easier to manage and much more reliable. Every time a thread finishes its time slice, you just move its TCB from the front of the queue to the back, so you’re always working with the next thread in line. This gets rid of the need to track positions with array indexes or deal with empty slots, which can easily happen if you use other data structures. It also makes adding or removing threads straightforward, since you don’t have to shift anything around manually the queue handles all of that for you. Overall, queues keep the scheduling process clean and efficient, especially as your OS grows and you start working with more threads.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,2,'2025-07-30_17-23-02','The PendSV_Handler function handles switching between threads in your OS. When it gets called, it first saves the state (like register values) of the thread that’s currently running, so nothing is lost. Then, it grabs the next thread’s info from the queue and updates the stack pointer to point to that thread’s saved state. This way, when the CPU start processing again, it picks up exactly where that thread left off. Thanks to this setup, thread switches happen smoothly in the background, and the CPU doesn’t get held up with delays PendSV_Handler does all the work in the background.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,2,'2025-07-30_17-38-34','The PendSV_Handler function handles switching between threads in your OS. When it gets called, it first saves the state (like register values) of the thread that’s currently running, so nothing is lost. Then, it grabs the next thread’s info from the queue and updates the stack pointer to point to that thread’s saved state. This way, when the CPU start processing again, it picks up exactly where that thread left off. Thanks to this setup, thread switches happen smoothly in the background, and the CPU doesn’t get held up with delays PendSV_Handler does all the work in the background.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,2,'2025-07-30_18-00-06','The PendSV_Handler function handles switching between threads in your OS. When it gets called, it first saves the state (like register values) of the thread that’s currently running, so nothing is lost. Then, it grabs the next thread’s info from the queue and updates the stack pointer to point to that thread’s saved state. This way, when the CPU start processing again, it picks up exactly where that thread left off. Thanks to this setup, thread switches happen smoothly in the background, and the CPU doesn’t get held up with delays PendSV_Handler does all the work in the background.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,2,'2025-07-30_18-23-50','The PendSV_Handler function handles switching between threads in your OS. When it gets called, it first saves the state (like register values) of the thread that’s currently running, so nothing is lost. Then, it grabs the next thread’s info from the queue and updates the stack pointer to point to that thread’s saved state. This way, when the CPU start processing again, it picks up exactly where that thread left off. Thanks to this setup, thread switches happen smoothly in the background, and the CPU doesn’t get held up with delays PendSV_Handler does all the work in the background.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,2,'2025-07-30_18-41-00','The PendSV_Handler function handles switching between threads in your OS. When it gets called, it first saves the state (like register values) of the thread that’s currently running, so nothing is lost. Then, it grabs the next thread’s info from the queue and updates the stack pointer to point to that thread’s saved state. This way, when the CPU start processing again, it picks up exactly where that thread left off. Thanks to this setup, thread switches happen smoothly in the background, and the CPU doesn’t get held up with delays PendSV_Handler does all the work in the background.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,2,'2025-07-31_11-10-21','The PendSV_Handler function handles switching between threads in your OS. When it gets called, it first saves the state (like register values) of the thread that’s currently running, so nothing is lost. Then, it grabs the next thread’s info from the queue and updates the stack pointer to point to that thread’s saved state. This way, when the CPU start processing again, it picks up exactly where that thread left off. Thanks to this setup, thread switches happen smoothly in the background, and the CPU doesn’t get held up with delays PendSV_Handler does all the work in the background.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,2,'2025-07-31_11-20-07','The PendSV_Handler function handles switching between threads in your OS. When it gets called, it first saves the state (like register values) of the thread that’s currently running, so nothing is lost. Then, it grabs the next thread’s info from the queue and updates the stack pointer to point to that thread’s saved state. This way, when the CPU start processing again, it picks up exactly where that thread left off. Thanks to this setup, thread switches happen smoothly in the background, and the CPU doesn’t get held up with delays PendSV_Handler does all the work in the background.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,3,'2025-07-30_17-23-02','If you set up the scheduler so that it always runs the highest priority thread, you run into the risk of possibly never having the lower priority threads ever run. This is because a repetitive cycle is risked being made that ensures that there is always another “highest thread” waiting and certain threads may never get a chance to run. Having low priority threads not run risks, certain tasks, while they are low priority they are still important, not being run, making the system more susceptible to run into an error.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,3,'2025-07-30_17-38-34','If you set up the scheduler so that it always runs the highest priority thread, you run into the risk of possibly never having the lower priority threads ever run. This is because a repetitive cycle is risked being made that ensures that there is always another “highest thread” waiting and certain threads may never get a chance to run. Having low priority threads not run risks, certain tasks, while they are low priority they are still important, not being run, making the system more susceptible to run into an error.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,3,'2025-07-30_18-00-06','If you set up the scheduler so that it always runs the highest priority thread, you run into the risk of possibly never having the lower priority threads ever run. This is because a repetitive cycle is risked being made that ensures that there is always another “highest thread” waiting and certain threads may never get a chance to run. Having low priority threads not run risks, certain tasks, while they are low priority they are still important, not being run, making the system more susceptible to run into an error.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,3,'2025-07-30_18-23-50','If you set up the scheduler so that it always runs the highest priority thread, you run into the risk of possibly never having the lower priority threads ever run. This is because a repetitive cycle is risked being made that ensures that there is always another “highest thread” waiting and certain threads may never get a chance to run. Having low priority threads not run risks, certain tasks, while they are low priority they are still important, not being run, making the system more susceptible to run into an error.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,3,'2025-07-30_18-41-00','If you set up the scheduler so that it always runs the highest priority thread, you run into the risk of possibly never having the lower priority threads ever run. This is because a repetitive cycle is risked being made that ensures that there is always another “highest thread” waiting and certain threads may never get a chance to run. Having low priority threads not run risks, certain tasks, while they are low priority they are still important, not being run, making the system more susceptible to run into an error.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,3,'2025-07-31_11-10-21','If you set up the scheduler so that it always runs the highest priority thread, you run into the risk of possibly never having the lower priority threads ever run. This is because a repetitive cycle is risked being made that ensures that there is always another “highest thread” waiting and certain threads may never get a chance to run. Having low priority threads not run risks, certain tasks, while they are low priority they are still important, not being run, making the system more susceptible to run into an error.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,3,'2025-07-31_11-20-07','If you set up the scheduler so that it always runs the highest priority thread, you run into the risk of possibly never having the lower priority threads ever run. This is because a repetitive cycle is risked being made that ensures that there is always another “highest thread” waiting and certain threads may never get a chance to run. Having low priority threads not run risks, certain tasks, while they are low priority they are still important, not being run, making the system more susceptible to run into an error.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,4,'2025-07-30_17-23-02','Calling C functions from something like SVC_HandlerMain makes your code a lot easier to read, maintain, and debug compared to writing everything directly in assembly. When you handle most of the logic in C, it’s much simpler to update, add, or remove features—you always know where to look and aren’t digging through low-level assembly code. The tradeoff is that calling C functions adds a bit of overhead and isn’t as efficient as pure assembly, especially for time-critical or memory-heavy tasks. Still, for most projects, using C keeps your code much more organized and maintainable, so the benefits usually outweigh the small performance cost.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,4,'2025-07-30_17-38-34','Calling C functions from something like SVC_HandlerMain makes your code a lot easier to read, maintain, and debug compared to writing everything directly in assembly. When you handle most of the logic in C, it’s much simpler to update, add, or remove features—you always know where to look and aren’t digging through low-level assembly code. The tradeoff is that calling C functions adds a bit of overhead and isn’t as efficient as pure assembly, especially for time-critical or memory-heavy tasks. Still, for most projects, using C keeps your code much more organized and maintainable, so the benefits usually outweigh the small performance cost.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,4,'2025-07-30_18-00-06','Calling C functions from something like SVC_HandlerMain makes your code a lot easier to read, maintain, and debug compared to writing everything directly in assembly. When you handle most of the logic in C, it’s much simpler to update, add, or remove features—you always know where to look and aren’t digging through low-level assembly code. The tradeoff is that calling C functions adds a bit of overhead and isn’t as efficient as pure assembly, especially for time-critical or memory-heavy tasks. Still, for most projects, using C keeps your code much more organized and maintainable, so the benefits usually outweigh the small performance cost.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,4,'2025-07-30_18-23-50','Calling C functions from something like SVC_HandlerMain makes your code a lot easier to read, maintain, and debug compared to writing everything directly in assembly. When you handle most of the logic in C, it’s much simpler to update, add, or remove features—you always know where to look and aren’t digging through low-level assembly code. The tradeoff is that calling C functions adds a bit of overhead and isn’t as efficient as pure assembly, especially for time-critical or memory-heavy tasks. Still, for most projects, using C keeps your code much more organized and maintainable, so the benefits usually outweigh the small performance cost.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,4,'2025-07-30_18-41-00','Calling C functions from something like SVC_HandlerMain makes your code a lot easier to read, maintain, and debug compared to writing everything directly in assembly. When you handle most of the logic in C, it’s much simpler to update, add, or remove features—you always know where to look and aren’t digging through low-level assembly code. The tradeoff is that calling C functions adds a bit of overhead and isn’t as efficient as pure assembly, especially for time-critical or memory-heavy tasks. Still, for most projects, using C keeps your code much more organized and maintainable, so the benefits usually outweigh the small performance cost.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,4,'2025-07-31_11-10-21','Calling C functions from something like SVC_HandlerMain makes your code a lot easier to read, maintain, and debug compared to writing everything directly in assembly. When you handle most of the logic in C, it’s much simpler to update, add, or remove features—you always know where to look and aren’t digging through low-level assembly code. The tradeoff is that calling C functions adds a bit of overhead and isn’t as efficient as pure assembly, especially for time-critical or memory-heavy tasks. Still, for most projects, using C keeps your code much more organized and maintainable, so the benefits usually outweigh the small performance cost.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,4,'2025-07-31_11-20-07','Calling C functions from something like SVC_HandlerMain makes your code a lot easier to read, maintain, and debug compared to writing everything directly in assembly. When you handle most of the logic in C, it’s much simpler to update, add, or remove features—you always know where to look and aren’t digging through low-level assembly code. The tradeoff is that calling C functions adds a bit of overhead and isn’t as efficient as pure assembly, especially for time-critical or memory-heavy tasks. Still, for most projects, using C keeps your code much more organized and maintainable, so the benefits usually outweigh the small performance cost.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,5,'2025-07-30_17-23-02','If you only set up one thread, like print_continuously, the operating system would still work as intended. However, as there is only a singular thread, every time the thread is done, the operating system will just transition back to the same thread. The thread will keep repeadetly printing because it is being selected and being run on repeat. The operating system is working as intended, the user is not taking advantage of the system’s ability to multitask and run multiple threads seamlessly.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,5,'2025-07-30_17-38-34','If you only set up one thread, like print_continuously, the operating system would still work as intended. However, as there is only a singular thread, every time the thread is done, the operating system will just transition back to the same thread. The thread will keep repeadetly printing because it is being selected and being run on repeat. The operating system is working as intended, the user is not taking advantage of the system’s ability to multitask and run multiple threads seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,5,'2025-07-30_18-00-06','If you only set up one thread, like print_continuously, the operating system would still work as intended. However, as there is only a singular thread, every time the thread is done, the operating system will just transition back to the same thread. The thread will keep repeadetly printing because it is being selected and being run on repeat. The operating system is working as intended, the user is not taking advantage of the system’s ability to multitask and run multiple threads seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,5,'2025-07-30_18-23-50','If you only set up one thread, like print_continuously, the operating system would still work as intended. However, as there is only a singular thread, every time the thread is done, the operating system will just transition back to the same thread. The thread will keep repeadetly printing because it is being selected and being run on repeat. The operating system is working as intended, the user is not taking advantage of the system’s ability to multitask and run multiple threads seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,5,'2025-07-30_18-41-00','If you only set up one thread, like print_continuously, the operating system would still work as intended. However, as there is only a singular thread, every time the thread is done, the operating system will just transition back to the same thread. The thread will keep repeadetly printing because it is being selected and being run on repeat. The operating system is working as intended, the user is not taking advantage of the system’s ability to multitask and run multiple threads seamlessly.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,5,'2025-07-31_11-10-21','If you only set up one thread, like print_continuously, the operating system would still work as intended. However, as there is only a singular thread, every time the thread is done, the operating system will just transition back to the same thread. The thread will keep repeadetly printing because it is being selected and being run on repeat. The operating system is working as intended, the user is not taking advantage of the system’s ability to multitask and run multiple threads seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',4,5,'2025-07-31_11-20-07','If you only set up one thread, like print_continuously, the operating system would still work as intended. However, as there is only a singular thread, every time the thread is done, the operating system will just transition back to the same thread. The thread will keep repeadetly printing because it is being selected and being run on repeat. The operating system is working as intended, the user is not taking advantage of the system’s ability to multitask and run multiple threads seamlessly.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',5,1,'2025-07-31_12-09-55','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first writes the thread function to take a void* argument and passes in their desired data (like a pointer to a struct or integer) when creating the thread. On the OS side, when the thread is being initialized, it sets up the stack for the new thread and manually places that argument in register R0, which is how the ARM Cortex-M architecture expects the first argument to be passed into a function. The OS also sets the PC to point to the thread function and sets the PSP to the top of the new thread\'s stack. This setup tricks the processor into thinking it\'s just calling a regular function with an argument, so when the thread starts running, it grabs the value from R0 and uses it as intended. This works because the whole process mimics the normal calling convention at the hardware level, so the thread behaves like a standard function call with a passed-in argument.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',5,1,'2025-07-31_12-28-01','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first writes the thread function to take a void* argument and passes in their desired data (like a pointer to a struct or integer) when creating the thread. On the OS side, when the thread is being initialized, it sets up the stack for the new thread and manually places that argument in register R0, which is how the ARM Cortex-M architecture expects the first argument to be passed into a function. The OS also sets the PC to point to the thread function and sets the PSP to the top of the new thread\'s stack. This setup tricks the processor into thinking it\'s just calling a regular function with an argument, so when the thread starts running, it grabs the value from R0 and uses it as intended. This works because the whole process mimics the normal calling convention at the hardware level, so the thread behaves like a standard function call with a passed-in argument.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',5,2,'2025-07-31_12-09-55','You can’t call osYield() inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because those functions are already part of the context switch process. They’re running in exception mode, not thread mode, and osYield() is meant to be called from thread mode to trigger a context switch, not perform one. If you try to call it from inside one of these handlers, you’ll just end up triggering another context switch while already handling one, which can cause recursive exceptions or corrupt the stack. It basically breaks the whole flow of the OS and can make the system crash or behave unpredictably.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',5,2,'2025-07-31_12-28-01','You can’t call osYield() inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because those functions are already part of the context switch process. They’re running in exception mode, not thread mode, and osYield() is meant to be called from thread mode to trigger a context switch, not perform one. If you try to call it from inside one of these handlers, you’ll just end up triggering another context switch while already handling one, which can cause recursive exceptions or corrupt the stack. It basically breaks the whole flow of the OS and can make the system crash or behave unpredictably.\n',0,'s25'),('a5sahoo',5,3,'2025-07-31_12-09-55','If we made the OS clock tick every 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms, the system would be able to react to things a lot faster and switch between threads more frequently, which is great for really time-sensitive tasks. The tradeoff, though, is that the CPU now has to deal with ten times more SysTick interrupts every second. That adds a lot of overhead because each interrupt means saving registers, running the scheduler, and maybe switching threads. So now the CPU spends more time doing OS work and less time running the actual threads. It could also waste more power and even cause issues if things start falling behind.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',5,3,'2025-07-31_12-28-01','If we made the OS clock tick every 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms, the system would be able to react to things a lot faster and switch between threads more frequently, which is great for really time-sensitive tasks. The tradeoff, though, is that the CPU now has to deal with ten times more SysTick interrupts every second. That adds a lot of overhead because each interrupt means saving registers, running the scheduler, and maybe switching threads. So now the CPU spends more time doing OS work and less time running the actual threads. It could also waste more power and even cause issues if things start falling behind.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',5,4,'2025-07-31_12-09-55','With cooperative multitasking, it’s up to the thread to give up control—so the user has to remember to call osYield() at the right times. If the thread never yields, nothing else gets to run. Preemptive multitasking is different because the OS handles it automatically using something like the SysTick timer. It interrupts threads on its own after a set amount of time and switches to the next one without the user having to do anything. On the OS side, cooperative is easier since it just waits for a thread to yield before switching. Preemptive takes more work—the OS has to set up timer interrupts and be able to safely switch context even if the thread didn’t expect to be interrupted.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',5,4,'2025-07-31_12-28-01','With cooperative multitasking, it’s up to the thread to give up control—so the user has to remember to call osYield() at the right times. If the thread never yields, nothing else gets to run. Preemptive multitasking is different because the OS handles it automatically using something like the SysTick timer. It interrupts threads on its own after a set amount of time and switches to the next one without the user having to do anything. On the OS side, cooperative is easier since it just waits for a thread to yield before switching. Preemptive takes more work—the OS has to set up timer interrupts and be able to safely switch context even if the thread didn’t expect to be interrupted.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',5,5,'2025-07-31_12-09-55','Yielding and preemptive context switching can work together because, in the end, they both use the same mechanism to switch threads—the PendSV handler. When a thread calls osYield(), it’s basically saying, “I’m done for now,” and the OS sets the PendSV interrupt to handle the switch. With preemptive multitasking, the SysTick timer goes off automatically after a certain time, and the OS also sets PendSV to do the context switch. So even though one is manual and the other is automatic, they both end up triggering the same process, which is why the two methods can be used side-by-side without issues.\n',1,'s25'),('a5sahoo',5,5,'2025-07-31_12-28-01','Yielding and preemptive context switching can work together because, in the end, they both use the same mechanism to switch threads—the PendSV handler. When a thread calls osYield(), it’s basically saying, “I’m done for now,” and the OS sets the PendSV interrupt to handle the switch. With preemptive multitasking, the SysTick timer goes off automatically after a certain time, and the OS also sets PendSV to do the context switch. So even though one is manual and the other is automatic, they both end up triggering the same process, which is why the two methods can be used side-by-side without issues.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,1,'2025-06-26_14-24-03','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a software layer that simplifies interaction with a microcontroller’s hardware by providing standardized, high-level functions. It allows developers to write code without dealing directly with hardware registers, making projects easier to develop, read, and maintain. HAL is used to improve portability across devices and reduce development time in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,1,'2025-06-26_17-25-54','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a software layer that simplifies interaction with a microcontroller’s hardware by providing standardized, high-level functions. It allows developers to write code without dealing directly with hardware registers, making projects easier to develop, read, and maintain. HAL is used to improve portability across devices and reduce development time in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,1,'2025-06-26_20-24-58','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a software layer that simplifies interaction with a microcontroller’s hardware by providing standardized, high-level functions. It allows developers to write code without dealing directly with hardware registers, making projects easier to develop, read, and maintain. HAL is used to improve portability across devices and reduce development time in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,2,'2025-06-26_14-24-03','The __io_putchar function is used to redirect character output (usually from functions like printf) to a specific output, such as a UART serial port. It sends each character to the hardware so the message can be transmitted, often for debugging purposes. This function is needed because embedded systems don\'t have a standard output like a computer screen, so we must define how and where the characters should be sent.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,2,'2025-06-26_17-25-54','The __io_putchar function is used to redirect character output (usually from functions like printf) to a specific output, such as a UART serial port. It sends each character to the hardware so the message can be transmitted, often for debugging purposes. This function is needed because embedded systems don\'t have a standard output like a computer screen, so we must define how and where the characters should be sent.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,2,'2025-06-26_20-24-58','The __io_putchar function is used to redirect character output (usually from functions like printf) to a specific output, such as a UART serial port. It sends each character to the hardware so the message can be transmitted, often for debugging purposes. This function is needed because embedded systems don\'t have a standard output like a computer screen, so we must define how and where the characters should be sent.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,3,'2025-06-26_14-24-03','In this lab, the debugger was used because it allows you to step through the code, monitor variable values in real time, and identify the exact location of issues in the program\'s execution. Using printf for instrumentation could be less effective in embedded systems, as it can introduce delays, require additional setup for output redirection (such as UART), and might not work effectively in real-time systems. The debugger provides a more efficient and accurate way to track down problems without altering the timing or behavior of the system.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,3,'2025-06-26_17-25-54','In this lab, we used the debugger because it allows us to inspect not only variable values and program flow, but also register values, which are critical in embedded systems. Understanding the contents of registers like PC, SP, and LR is essential for verifying context switching and stack manipulation—something that printf cannot show, since it only accesses higher-level variables and requires extra setup like UART. The debugger gives direct, real-time access to the hardware state, which is crucial for low-level tasks such as setting up thread contexts and validating that registers hold expected values during execution.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,3,'2025-06-26_20-24-58','In this lab, we used the debugger because it allows us to inspect not only variable values and program flow, but also register values, which are critical in embedded systems. Understanding the contents of registers like PC, SP, and LR is essential for verifying context switching and stack manipulation—something that printf cannot show, since it only accesses higher-level variables and requires extra setup like UART. The debugger gives direct, real-time access to the hardware state, which is crucial for low-level tasks such as setting up thread contexts and validating that registers hold expected values during execution.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,4,'2025-06-26_14-24-03','The stack allocation method used in this lab involves manually setting the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) based on the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). Once we obtained the initial value of MSP_init_val, we subtracted a specific amount of memory from it to calculate a safe and separate space for the PSP — this new address became PSP_val. We needed to know the original MSP location so that we could avoid overlapping memory regions and ensure the PSP was placed in a valid, unused section of RAM, allowing both stacks to function independently without corrupting each other\'s data.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,4,'2025-06-26_17-25-54','The stack allocation method used in this lab involves manually setting the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) based on the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). Once we obtained the initial value of MSP_init_val, we subtracted a specific amount of memory from it to calculate a safe and separate space for the PSP — this new address became PSP_val. We needed to know the original MSP location so that we could avoid overlapping memory regions and ensure the PSP was placed in a valid, unused section of RAM, allowing both stacks to function independently without corrupting each other\'s data.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,4,'2025-06-26_20-24-58','The stack allocation method used in this lab involves manually setting the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) based on the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). Once we obtained the initial value of MSP_init_val, we subtracted a specific amount of memory from it to calculate a safe and separate space for the PSP — this new address became PSP_val. We needed to know the original MSP location so that we could avoid overlapping memory regions and ensure the PSP was placed in a valid, unused section of RAM, allowing both stacks to function independently without corrupting each other\'s data.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,5,'2025-06-26_14-24-03','To allocate multiple stacks using the same method, we would need to define a separate memory region for each thread’s stack. Starting from MSP_init_val, we would subtract fixed-size chunks of memory — one per thread — to determine each new stack’s base address. In the code, we could create an array to store the stack pointer values, where each entry is calculated as:\nPSP_base[i] = MSP_init_val - (i + 1) * STACK_SIZE;\nThis ensures each stack starts at a unique, non-overlapping memory location determined at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,5,'2025-06-26_17-25-54','To allocate multiple stacks using the same method, we would need to define a separate memory region for each thread’s stack. Starting from MSP_init_val, we would subtract fixed-size chunks of memory — one per thread — to determine each new stack’s base address. In the code, we could create an array to store the stack pointer values, where each entry is calculated as:\nPSP_base[i] = MSP_init_val - (i + 1) * STACK_SIZE;\nThis ensures each stack starts at a unique, non-overlapping memory location determined at compile time.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',1,5,'2025-06-26_20-24-58','To allocate multiple stacks at compile time, we need to define separate, non-overlapping memory regions for each thread’s stack, ensuring they meet the 8-byte alignment requirement of the Cortex-M4. Instead of dynamically calculating offsets from MSP_init_val, which risks overwriting the main stack, it\'s better to reserve stack memory explicitly in the linker script or allocate a large static array in C (e.g., uint32_t thread_stacks[NUM_THREADS][STACK_SIZE]) where each sub-array is properly aligned. Then, we assign each thread’s stack pointer to the top of its designated region like PSP_base[i] = &thread_stacks[i][STACK_SIZE], ensuring clean separation and compliance with the architecture’s requirements.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',2,1,'2025-06-26_15-08-29','When I changed the PC (Program Counter) to hold the function pointer for print_continuously, the processor immediately started executing that function instead of continuing the normal program flow. Since print_continuously is an infinite loop that prints “Hello, PC!”, it kept printing that message repeatedly, showing that the PC was now pointing to the start of that function. This direct manipulation of the PC demonstrated how the OS can switch context and start running a new thread by changing what instruction the processor executes next.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',2,1,'2025-06-27_16-30-41','When I changed the PC (Program Counter) to hold the function pointer for print_continuously, the processor immediately started executing that function instead of continuing the normal program flow. Since print_continuously is an infinite loop that prints “Hello, PC!”, it kept printing that message repeatedly, showing that the PC was now pointing to the start of that function. This direct manipulation of the PC demonstrated how the OS can switch context and start running a new thread by changing what instruction the processor executes next.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',2,2,'2025-06-26_15-08-29','A system call is a way for a program to request a service or function from the operating system, such as printing to the screen or accessing hardware. It acts like a controlled entry point where user code can safely interact with privileged system functions. In embedded systems, system calls help manage low-level tasks like switching threads or handling I/O while keeping the main program code simpler and more secure.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',2,2,'2025-06-27_16-30-41','A system call is a specific type of function call that allows a user-level program to request services directly from the kernel, which is the core part of the operating system responsible for managing hardware and system resources. Unlike regular function calls that stay within the boundaries of an application, a system call provides a secure and controlled mechanism for transitioning from user mode to kernel mode, where the operating system can safely carry out privileged operations.\nFor example, when a program wants to read input from a keyboard, write data to a file, allocate memory, or create a new process, it cannot perform these actions directly because they involve interacting with hardware or critical system resources. Instead, it uses system calls, which act as intermediaries that validate and execute these requests on behalf of the application. This ensures that user programs cannot access or interfere with sensitive operations, maintaining system security and stability.\nIn embedded systems, where efficiency and direct hardware interaction are crucial, system calls play an important role in simplifying application code while still enabling low-level functionality. For instance, a system call might be used to toggle a GPIO pin, manage timers, or handle thread scheduling—all tasks that require coordination with the hardware and the real-time operating system kernel. By using system calls, developers can write modular, portable code without needing to directly manipulate the hardware or violate system safety constraints.\nOverall, system calls are an essential bridge between user applications and the kernel. They are implemented as function calls that follow a strict interface, enabling applications to perform powerful operations safely and efficiently under the supervision of the operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',2,3,'2025-06-26_15-08-29','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a section of memory with all the values the processor expects to see when it starts running that thread. This includes key register values like xPSR, PC, LR, and general-purpose registers (R0–R12). We store these on the stack because, during a context switch or interrupt, the processor restores its state by popping values off the stack — so by placing the right values there ahead of time, we trick the processor into starting the thread as if it had been running all along. This ensures smooth execution from the moment the thread begins.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',2,3,'2025-06-27_16-30-41','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a dedicated memory space with all the necessary values that the processor expects to find when switching to that thread. This process allows the thread to begin execution as if it had already been running, by mimicking the processor’s state at the time of an interrupt or context switch.\nTo do this correctly, we must store specific register values—including the program counter (PC), link register (LR), program status register (xPSR), and general-purpose registers (R0–R12)—onto the stack. But it’s not just about placing the right values; it’s crucial that these values are stored in a specific order that matches the processor’s exception return mechanism.\nProcessors like ARM Cortex-M, for instance, follow a strict convention for the stack frame layout during context switches. If the register values are not stored in this exact sequence, the processor may misinterpret which value belongs to which register when it pops them off the stack to restore the thread\'s state. This could lead to erratic behavior, corrupted execution, or even crashes.\nTherefore, setting up a thread’s stack involves not only saving the correct data but also doing so in the exact order the processor expects. This precise setup ensures the thread will resume execution smoothly and safely, starting from the correct instruction with the appropriate context and register values fully restored.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',2,4,'2025-06-26_15-08-29','If we didn’t set the PSP using __set_PSP before running the thread, the processor wouldn’t know where to find the thread’s stack. When the thread starts running and tries to pop register values from the stack, it would access an invalid or uninitialized memory location, leading to a hard fault. Setting the PSP ensures that the processor has a correct and valid stack pointer, so it can safely restore the thread’s context and begin execution without crashing.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',2,4,'2025-06-27_16-30-41','In ARM Cortex-M processors, there are two stack pointers: the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). By default, the processor uses the MSP, which is typically reserved for exception handling and privileged-level operations such as boot code or interrupt service routines. In contrast, threads that run in unprivileged mode are expected to use the PSP.\nWhen we create a new thread, we allocate a dedicated stack for it in memory. However, the processor won’t automatically know to use this thread-specific stack. If we fail to explicitly set the PSP using the __set_PSP() function before starting the thread, the processor will continue using the MSP. Since the thread’s context (register values like PC, LR, xPSR, etc.) was pushed onto the thread’s stack—not the MSP—this mismatch leads the processor to pop incorrect or invalid values during context restoration. This often results in a hard fault due to accessing uninitialized memory or executing garbage instructions.\nBy calling __set_PSP() with the address of the thread’s stack, we instruct the processor to switch to using the PSP for stack operations. This ensures the processor pops the correct register values from the correct location, restoring the thread\'s context properly and allowing it to begin execution safely. Therefore, distinguishing between MSP and PSP is essential for correct thread management and preventing runtime faults during context switches.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',2,5,'2025-06-26_15-08-29','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned, the processor would try to pop the return address from the stack and continue execution from there. But since we manually set up the stack and likely didn’t provide a valid return address in the LR (Link Register), it would jump to an invalid memory location. This would almost certainly result in a hard fault or crash, because the CPU would try to execute garbage or protected memory.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',2,5,'2025-06-27_16-30-41','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned, the processor would try to pop the return address from the stack and continue execution from there. But since we manually set up the stack and likely didn’t provide a valid return address in the LR (Link Register), it would jump to an invalid memory location. This would almost certainly result in a hard fault or crash, because the CPU would try to execute garbage or protected memory.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,1,'2025-06-27_17-07-33','When a program consists of multiple .c files, the compiler doesn’t combine them all at once. Instead, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o) through a process called compilation. During this step, the compiler translates each source file’s C code into machine code while keeping track of symbols (like function names or global variables) that may be defined or referenced across files.\nOnce all .c files are compiled into their respective object files, the linker takes over. The linker’s job is to combine all the object files into a single executable. It does this by resolving symbols — matching function calls and variable references from one file to their definitions in others. The linker also connects the program with any required libraries (like the C standard library), arranges memory layout, and produces a final binary that can be run on the target system.\nThis two-step process — compilation followed by linking — allows developers to organize code across multiple files for clarity and reuse, while still producing a single executable file.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,1,'2025-06-27_17-27-29','When a program contains multiple .c files, building it into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compilation, and linking.\nPreprocessing:\nBefore the compiler runs, the preprocessor scans each .c file and handles directives like #include, #define, and #if. This step effectively copies the contents of header files into the source file, expands macros, and includes conditional code. If this step were skipped, the compiler wouldn\'t know about function declarations, constants, or macros, resulting in errors.\nCompilation:\nAfter preprocessing, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o). The compiler translates C code into machine code, keeping track of symbols (like function names or global variables) that may be defined in other files. These symbols remain unresolved at this stage.\nLinking:\nOnce all object files are created, the linker combines them into a single executable. It matches function calls and variable references across files and links in external libraries (such as the C standard library). It also assigns memory addresses and finalizes the program structure.\nThis complete process — preprocessing → compilation → linking — allows large programs to be modular, maintainable, and efficient, while still resulting in a single, functional executable.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,1,'2025-06-27_17-34-45','When a program contains multiple .c files, building it into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compilation, and linking.\nPreprocessing:\nBefore the compiler runs, the preprocessor scans each .c file and handles directives like #include, #define, and #if. This step effectively copies the contents of header files into the source file, expands macros, and includes conditional code. If this step were skipped, the compiler wouldn\'t know about function declarations, constants, or macros, resulting in errors.\nCompilation:\nAfter preprocessing, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o). The compiler translates C code into machine code, keeping track of symbols (like function names or global variables) that may be defined in other files. These symbols remain unresolved at this stage.\nLinking:\nOnce all object files are created, the linker combines them into a single executable. It matches function calls and variable references across files and links in external libraries (such as the C standard library). It also assigns memory addresses and finalizes the program structure.\nThis complete process — preprocessing → compilation → linking — allows large programs to be modular, maintainable, and efficient, while still resulting in a single, functional executable.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,1,'2025-06-27_17-48-32','When a program contains multiple .c files, building it into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compilation, and linking.\nPreprocessing:\nBefore the compiler runs, the preprocessor scans each .c file and handles directives like #include, #define, and #if. This step effectively copies the contents of header files into the source file, expands macros, and includes conditional code. If this step were skipped, the compiler wouldn\'t know about function declarations, constants, or macros, resulting in errors.\nCompilation:\nAfter preprocessing, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o). The compiler translates C code into machine code, keeping track of symbols (like function names or global variables) that may be defined in other files. These symbols remain unresolved at this stage.\nLinking:\nOnce all object files are created, the linker combines them into a single executable. It matches function calls and variable references across files and links in external libraries (such as the C standard library). It also assigns memory addresses and finalizes the program structure.\nThis complete process — preprocessing → compilation → linking — allows large programs to be modular, maintainable, and efficient, while still resulting in a single, functional executable.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,2,'2025-06-27_17-07-33','Using #include <kernel.c> or any other .c file in an #include directive is considered bad practice because it breaks the proper compilation and linking process in C programming.\n.c files are meant to be compiled separately into object files, and then linked together by the linker. When you #include a .c file, you are instead copy-pasting the entire contents of that file into another, which leads to several issues:\nCode duplication: If multiple source files include the same .c file, it can result in duplicate definitions of functions or variables, leading to linker errors like “multiple definition” or unexpected behavior.\nLoss of modularity: Each .c file should be a self-contained compilation unit. Including .c files breaks this model and makes code harder to maintain, test, or reuse.\nDependency confusion: The build system (like make) relies on tracking which files need to be recompiled. Including .c files messes with this dependency tracking and can cause unnecessary recompilation or missed changes.\nThe correct approach is to put declarations (like function prototypes and extern variable declarations) in a header file (.h), and include that with #include <kernel.h>. Then, compile the .c files normally and link them.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,2,'2025-06-27_17-27-29','Using #include <kernel.c> or any other .c file in an #include directive is considered bad practice because it breaks the proper compilation and linking process in C programming.\n.c files are meant to be compiled separately into object files, and then linked together by the linker. When you #include a .c file, you are instead copy-pasting the entire contents of that file into another, which leads to several issues:\nCode duplication: If multiple source files include the same .c file, it can result in duplicate definitions of functions or variables, leading to linker errors like “multiple definition” or unexpected behavior.\nLoss of modularity: Each .c file should be a self-contained compilation unit. Including .c files breaks this model and makes code harder to maintain, test, or reuse.\nDependency confusion: The build system (like make) relies on tracking which files need to be recompiled. Including .c files messes with this dependency tracking and can cause unnecessary recompilation or missed changes.\nThe correct approach is to put declarations (like function prototypes and extern variable declarations) in a header file (.h), and include that with #include <kernel.h>. Then, compile the .c files normally and link them.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,2,'2025-06-27_17-34-45','Using #include <kernel.c> or any other .c file in an #include directive is considered bad practice because it breaks the proper compilation and linking process in C programming.\n.c files are meant to be compiled separately into object files, and then linked together by the linker. When you #include a .c file, you are instead copy-pasting the entire contents of that file into another, which leads to several issues:\nCode duplication: If multiple source files include the same .c file, it can result in duplicate definitions of functions or variables, leading to linker errors like “multiple definition” or unexpected behavior.\nLoss of modularity: Each .c file should be a self-contained compilation unit. Including .c files breaks this model and makes code harder to maintain, test, or reuse.\nDependency confusion: The build system (like make) relies on tracking which files need to be recompiled. Including .c files messes with this dependency tracking and can cause unnecessary recompilation or missed changes.\nThe correct approach is to put declarations (like function prototypes and extern variable declarations) in a header file (.h), and include that with #include <kernel.h>. Then, compile the .c files normally and link them.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,2,'2025-06-27_17-48-32','Using #include <kernel.c> or any other .c file in an #include directive is considered bad practice because it breaks the proper compilation and linking process in C programming.\n.c files are meant to be compiled separately into object files, and then linked together by the linker. When you #include a .c file, you are instead copy-pasting the entire contents of that file into another, which leads to several issues:\nCode duplication: If multiple source files include the same .c file, it can result in duplicate definitions of functions or variables, leading to linker errors like “multiple definition” or unexpected behavior.\nLoss of modularity: Each .c file should be a self-contained compilation unit. Including .c files breaks this model and makes code harder to maintain, test, or reuse.\nDependency confusion: The build system (like make) relies on tracking which files need to be recompiled. Including .c files messes with this dependency tracking and can cause unnecessary recompilation or missed changes.\nThe correct approach is to put declarations (like function prototypes and extern variable declarations) in a header file (.h), and include that with #include <kernel.h>. Then, compile the .c files normally and link them.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,3,'2025-06-27_17-07-33','How to Set Up and Start a Thread (User Perspective):\nAllocate Stack Memory:\nReserve a block of memory that the thread will use as its stack. This stack will hold the thread’s context (register values) during execution.\nInitialize the Stack Frame:\nPrepare the thread’s stack by placing the necessary register values on it (such as xPSR, PC, LR, and general-purpose registers). This simulates what the processor expects to see when the thread starts running.\nSet the PSP (Process Stack Pointer):\nPoint the PSP to the top of the thread’s stack so the processor knows where to load register values from when starting the thread.\nCreate a Thread Control Block (TCB):\nSet up a data structure that holds information about the thread, including its stack pointer, state, and priority.\nRegister the Thread:\nAdd the thread to the scheduler’s list so it knows about the new thread and can switch to it when appropriate.\nStart the Scheduler:\nBegin the scheduling process, which will eventually trigger a context switch and allow your thread to run.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,3,'2025-06-27_17-27-29','How to Set Up and Start a Thread (User Perspective):\nDefine the Thread Function:\nFirst, write the function that contains the code you want the thread to execute. This function is the entry point for the thread and must match the expected format (e.g., it should take no arguments and not return a value).\nInitialize the Kernel:\nBefore creating any threads, the kernel must be initialized using a function like kernel_init(). This sets up the scheduler and other internal data structures required for thread management.\nCreate the Thread:\nUse a function like thread_create() to register your thread with the kernel. This function typically requires you to provide the thread function, a priority level, and possibly a stack size. Internally, this step allocates memory from the stack pool, sets up the initial stack frame with the correct register values (xPSR, PC, LR, etc.), and creates a Thread Control Block (TCB) for the thread.\nRegister the Thread with the Kernel Scheduler:\nDuring thread_create(), the new thread is added to the kernel’s list of runnable threads. This allows the scheduler to consider it during context switches.\nStart the Scheduler:\nOnce all desired threads are created, call kernel_start() (or a similar function) to start the scheduler. The kernel will begin selecting threads to run based on priority and state, and will perform context switching using each thread’s stack.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,3,'2025-06-27_17-34-45','How to Set Up and Start a Thread (User Perspective):\nDefine the Thread Function: First, write the function that contains the code you want the thread to execute. This function is the entry point for the thread and must match the expected format (e.g., it should take no arguments and not return a value). Initialize the Kernel: Before creating any threads, the kernel must be initialized using a function like kernel_init(). This sets up the scheduler and other internal data structures required for thread management. Create the Thread: Use a function like thread_create() to register your thread with the kernel. This function typically requires you to provide the thread function, a priority level, and possibly a stack size. Internally, this step allocates memory from the stack pool, sets up the initial stack frame with the correct register values (xPSR, PC, LR, etc.), and creates a Thread Control Block (TCB) for the thread. Register the Thread with the Kernel Scheduler: During thread_create(), the new thread is added to the kernel’s list of runnable threads. This allows the scheduler to consider it during context switches. Start the Scheduler: Once all desired threads are created, call kernel_start() (or a similar function) to start the scheduler. The kernel will begin selecting threads to run based on priority and state, and will perform context switching using each thread’s stack.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,3,'2025-06-27_17-48-32','How to Set Up and Start a Thread (User Perspective):\nDefine the Thread Function: First, write the function that contains the code you want the thread to execute. This function is the entry point for the thread and must match the expected format (e.g., it should take no arguments and not return a value). Initialize the Kernel: Before creating any threads, the kernel must be initialized using a function like kernel_init(). This sets up the scheduler and other internal data structures required for thread management. Create the Thread: Use a function like thread_create() to register your thread with the kernel. This function typically requires you to provide the thread function, a priority level, and possibly a stack size. Internally, this step allocates memory from the stack pool, sets up the initial stack frame with the correct register values (xPSR, PC, LR, etc.), and creates a Thread Control Block (TCB) for the thread. Register the Thread with the Kernel Scheduler: During thread_create(), the new thread is added to the kernel’s list of runnable threads. This allows the scheduler to consider it during context switches. Start the Scheduler: Once all desired threads are created, call kernel_start() (or a similar function) to start the scheduler. The kernel will begin selecting threads to run based on priority and state, and will perform context switching using each thread’s stack.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,4,'2025-06-27_17-07-33','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory in the operating system specifically set aside for thread stacks. Each thread in an OS needs its own stack so that it can store local variables, function call information, and CPU register values during context switches. The stack pool acts as a centralized source from which we can allocate individual stacks for each thread.\nIn a future lab, the stack pool will be used to support multiple threads by dividing it into separate stack regions—one per thread. When a new thread is created, the OS will assign it a portion of the stack pool. This allows each thread to have its own isolated space for execution, preventing data from overlapping or corrupting other threads.\nBy using the stack pool:\nThe OS can manage memory more efficiently.\nThread stacks are kept organized and safe from interference.\nThe scheduler can switch between threads by saving and restoring their contexts from their assigned stack areas.\nIn short, the stack pool is essential for enabling multitasking, as it provides the foundation for supporting multiple threads in a clean and scalable way.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,4,'2025-06-27_17-27-29','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory in the operating system specifically set aside for thread stacks. Each thread in an OS needs its own stack so that it can store local variables, function call information, and CPU register values during context switches. The stack pool acts as a centralized source from which we can allocate individual stacks for each thread.\nIn a future lab, the stack pool will be used to support multiple threads by dividing it into separate stack regions—one per thread. When a new thread is created, the OS will assign it a portion of the stack pool. This allows each thread to have its own isolated space for execution, preventing data from overlapping or corrupting other threads.\nBy using the stack pool:\nThe OS can manage memory more efficiently.\nThread stacks are kept organized and safe from interference.\nThe scheduler can switch between threads by saving and restoring their contexts from their assigned stack areas.\nIn short, the stack pool is essential for enabling multitasking, as it provides the foundation for supporting multiple threads in a clean and scalable way.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,4,'2025-06-27_17-34-45','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory in the operating system specifically set aside for thread stacks. Each thread in an OS needs its own stack so that it can store local variables, function call information, and CPU register values during context switches. The stack pool acts as a centralized source from which we can allocate individual stacks for each thread.\nIn a future lab, the stack pool will be used to support multiple threads by dividing it into separate stack regions—one per thread. When a new thread is created, the OS will assign it a portion of the stack pool. This allows each thread to have its own isolated space for execution, preventing data from overlapping or corrupting other threads.\nBy using the stack pool:\nThe OS can manage memory more efficiently.\nThread stacks are kept organized and safe from interference.\nThe scheduler can switch between threads by saving and restoring their contexts from their assigned stack areas.\nIn short, the stack pool is essential for enabling multitasking, as it provides the foundation for supporting multiple threads in a clean and scalable way.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,4,'2025-06-27_17-48-32','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory in the operating system specifically set aside for thread stacks. Each thread in an OS needs its own stack so that it can store local variables, function call information, and CPU register values during context switches. The stack pool acts as a centralized source from which we can allocate individual stacks for each thread.\nIn a future lab, the stack pool will be used to support multiple threads by dividing it into separate stack regions—one per thread. When a new thread is created, the OS will assign it a portion of the stack pool. This allows each thread to have its own isolated space for execution, preventing data from overlapping or corrupting other threads.\nBy using the stack pool:\nThe OS can manage memory more efficiently.\nThread stacks are kept organized and safe from interference.\nThe scheduler can switch between threads by saving and restoring their contexts from their assigned stack areas.\nIn short, the stack pool is essential for enabling multitasking, as it provides the foundation for supporting multiple threads in a clean and scalable way.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,5,'2025-06-27_17-07-33','In an operating system, there is a critical separation between user code and kernel code, and this distinction determines which files different parts of the code belong to.\nKernel code is the core of the operating system. It controls hardware, manages memory, handles scheduling, and performs context switching between threads. This code goes into files like kernel.c and is written to run in privileged mode, meaning it has full access to the processor and hardware.\nUser code includes the functions and logic written by application developers to perform tasks using the OS’s services. This code goes into files like main.c or other application-specific files and runs in unprivileged mode, meaning it cannot directly access hardware or critical system functions.\nFrom the user\'s perspective, this separation is important for several reasons:\nSafety and Security: Keeping user code separate from kernel code prevents bugs or malicious behavior in applications from crashing or corrupting the entire system.\nModularity: The user only needs to know how to interact with the kernel through defined interfaces (e.g., system calls). This simplifies development and makes it easier to use or replace components.\nClear Boundaries: By knowing what goes where, users can focus on writing application logic without worrying about low-level hardware details, while kernel developers can ensure consistent behavior and resource management.\nOverall, the distinction ensures a more stable, maintainable, and secure operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,5,'2025-06-27_17-27-29','In an operating system, there is a critical separation between user code and kernel code, and this distinction determines which files different parts of the code belong to.\nKernel code is the core of the operating system. It controls hardware, manages memory, handles scheduling, and performs context switching between threads. This code goes into files like kernel.c and is written to run in privileged mode, meaning it has full access to the processor and hardware.\nUser code includes the functions and logic written by application developers to perform tasks using the OS’s services. This code goes into files like main.c or other application-specific files and runs in unprivileged mode, meaning it cannot directly access hardware or critical system functions.\nFrom the user\'s perspective, this separation is important for several reasons:\nSafety and Security: Keeping user code separate from kernel code prevents bugs or malicious behavior in applications from crashing or corrupting the entire system.\nModularity: The user only needs to know how to interact with the kernel through defined interfaces (e.g., system calls). This simplifies development and makes it easier to use or replace components.\nClear Boundaries: By knowing what goes where, users can focus on writing application logic without worrying about low-level hardware details, while kernel developers can ensure consistent behavior and resource management.\nOverall, the distinction ensures a more stable, maintainable, and secure operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,5,'2025-06-27_17-34-45','In an operating system, there is a critical separation between user code and kernel code, and this distinction determines which files different parts of the code belong to.\nKernel code is the core of the operating system. It controls hardware, manages memory, handles scheduling, and performs context switching between threads. This code goes into files like kernel.c and is written to run in privileged mode, meaning it has full access to the processor and hardware.\nUser code includes the functions and logic written by application developers to perform tasks using the OS’s services. This code goes into files like main.c or other application-specific files and runs in unprivileged mode, meaning it cannot directly access hardware or critical system functions.\nFrom the user\'s perspective, this separation is important for several reasons:\nSafety and Security: Keeping user code separate from kernel code prevents bugs or malicious behavior in applications from crashing or corrupting the entire system.\nModularity: The user only needs to know how to interact with the kernel through defined interfaces (e.g., system calls). This simplifies development and makes it easier to use or replace components.\nClear Boundaries: By knowing what goes where, users can focus on writing application logic without worrying about low-level hardware details, while kernel developers can ensure consistent behavior and resource management.\nOverall, the distinction ensures a more stable, maintainable, and secure operating system.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',3,5,'2025-06-27_17-48-32','In an operating system, there is a critical separation between user code and kernel code, and this distinction helps determine what goes into which file.\nKernel code includes the core parts of the operating system responsible for managing hardware, memory, scheduling, and context switching. This code typically goes into files like kernel.c and runs in privileged mode, meaning it has full access to the processor and hardware resources.\nUser code, on the other hand, includes the application logic written by the programmer to define what the system actually does. This is written in files like main.c or other user-defined files. It runs in unprivileged mode and cannot directly access hardware. Instead, it relies on services provided by the kernel through well-defined interfaces.\nImportantly, the user is responsible for writing the thread functions—the individual units of work that the OS will run. Each thread function must be explicitly written by the user and registered with the kernel so the scheduler can manage it. This is a key part of how the user interacts with the OS.\nFrom the user\'s perspective, this separation is important because:\nSafety and Security: Separating user and kernel code ensures that faults or bugs in user code cannot crash or corrupt the system.\nModularity and Clarity: The user can focus on writing thread functions and application logic without needing to understand the low-level kernel details.\nDefined Interfaces: The user only interacts with the kernel through function calls like thread_create() or kernel_start(), keeping responsibilities clear and manageable.\nIn short, the kernel provides the infrastructure, while the user is responsible for writing the threads that the OS will run.\nOverall, the distinction ensures a more stable, maintainable, and secure operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',4,1,'2025-07-28_14-26-01','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a queue by placing each thread’s Task Control Block (TCB) into the queue in the order they should run. The scheduler removes the thread at the front of the queue, allows it to execute for a time slice, and then pushes it to the back of the queue once its time is up. This process repeats, giving each thread equal and fair access to the CPU. Using a queue instead of an array makes the scheduling logic cleaner, as it naturally supports the first-in, first-out behavior of round-robin scheduling without the need to manually manage and wrap an index. Additionally, a queue allows for greater flexibility, making it easier to add or remove threads dynamically, which can be more cumbersome with a fixed-size array. Overall, using a queue can simplify implementation and improve the scalability of the scheduler.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',4,1,'2025-07-29_18-12-43','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a queue by storing each thread’s Task Control Block (TCB) in a first-in, first-out (FIFO) structure. The scheduler dequeues the TCB at the front, runs that thread for its time slice, and then enqueues it at the back of the queue. This rotation continues, ensuring fairness among all threads.\nCompared to the current array-based implementation described in the lab manual, which uses a circular index to track the next thread, a queue-based approach can simplify the logic by abstracting away manual index management. However, this comes at a potential cost: queue operations like enqueue and dequeue may introduce overhead, especially if the queue is implemented with dynamic memory or involves shifting elements. In contrast, using a simple array with an index pointer has minimal overhead since it relies on direct indexing without additional data structure operations.\nUltimately, using a queue may offer better scalability and readability, especially when dynamically adding or removing threads, but it may introduce slight computational overhead compared to the more lightweight array-based method.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',4,2,'2025-07-28_14-26-01','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the state of the currently running thread and loading the state of the next thread to run. When a thread calls osYield, a system call triggers the PendSV interrupt, which is specially designed to run only when no higher-priority interrupt is active. Inside PendSV_Handler, the processor first saves the contents of registers R4–R11 (which are not automatically saved during interrupts) to the current thread\'s stack. Then, the scheduler function osSched is called, which updates the current thread index and sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to the new thread’s saved stack pointer. After that, the handler loads the R4–R11 registers for the new thread from its stack, effectively restoring its context. Once PendSV_Handler finishes, the processor resumes execution in the newly loaded thread, making it appear as if the new thread had been running all along. This mechanism enables seamless switching between threads without losing their state.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',4,2,'2025-07-29_18-12-43','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the context of the currently running thread and restoring the context of the next thread selected by the scheduler. When osYield() is called, it triggers the PendSV interrupt. The first step the processor takes is to load the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) so it can access the stack of the current thread. Using the PSP, the handler manually stores the non-automatically saved registers (R4–R11) onto the current thread’s stack. This ensures the current thread’s state is preserved.\nNext, the scheduler (osSched) is invoked. This function determines which thread should run next by updating the currentThread index and loading the stack pointer (PSP) of the new thread. With this updated PSP, the processor now points to the saved state of the new thread. The handler then restores the new thread’s context by popping the saved R4–R11 registers from its stack. After the context is fully restored, control is returned to the new thread, allowing it to resume execution from where it left off.\nThis process ensures that threads can yield and resume without losing their execution state, enabling reliable cooperative multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',4,3,'2025-07-28_14-26-01','Modifying the OS to always select the highest-priority task may seem logical, but it leads to a major issue known as starvation. If a high-priority thread never yields or completes, lower-priority threads will never get a chance to run, even if they are ready and waiting. This breaks fairness and can prevent important background or maintenance tasks from ever executing. In real-time systems, this can lead to missed deadlines, unresponsive behavior, or even system failure if those low-priority tasks are essential for long-term operation.\n',0,'s25'),('a6lakhan',4,3,'2025-07-29_18-12-43','Modifying the OS scheduler to always select the task with the highest priority is a bad idea because it causes only the highest-priority task to run, regardless of whether other tasks are ready. This is not dependent on the behavior of the high-priority task—it’s a direct result of how the scheduler operates. Even if lower-priority tasks are waiting, the scheduler will repeatedly choose the highest-priority one as long as it is ready, which leads to starvation of all lower-priority tasks. This lack of fairness can be especially dangerous in real-time systems where lower-priority tasks may be responsible for essential background operations like logging, health monitoring, or resource cleanup. Without mechanisms like priority aging or time slicing among priorities, this strict priority-based scheduling undermines system reliability and responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',4,4,'2025-07-28_14-26-01','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain instead of writing everything in assembly offers several benefits. C is significantly easier to read, write, and debug than assembly, making the code more maintainable and accessible to a wider range of developers. It also allows for faster development and reuse of existing high-level logic and data structures. However, this approach comes with some downsides. Calling C functions from assembly introduces overhead due to the need for context preservation, and it relies on compiler-generated conventions, which can add complexity or inefficiency in low-level systems. Additionally, some operations—like precise control over registers or stack manipulation—are more naturally and efficiently done in pure assembly. Overall, using C provides clarity and maintainability, while assembly offers control and performance, so the right choice depends on the specific needs of the operation.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',4,4,'2025-07-29_18-12-43','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain instead of writing everything in assembly offers several benefits. C is significantly easier to read, write, and debug than assembly, making the code more maintainable and accessible to a wider range of developers. It also allows for faster development and reuse of existing high-level logic and data structures. However, this approach comes with some downsides. Calling C functions from assembly introduces overhead due to the need for context preservation, and it relies on compiler-generated conventions, which can add complexity or inefficiency in low-level systems. Additionally, some operations—like precise control over registers or stack manipulation—are more naturally and efficiently done in pure assembly. Overall, using C provides clarity and maintainability, while assembly offers control and performance, so the right choice depends on the specific needs of the operation.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',4,5,'2025-07-28_14-26-01','If only a single thread is created—such as a modified print_continuously thread that yields after printing—the OS will still run, but it won’t behave as expected. Each time the thread yields, the scheduler will try to switch to the “next” thread. However, since only one thread exists, the scheduler will simply switch back to the same thread. This means the thread will keep yielding to itself in a loop. While this won\'t cause a crash, it’s inefficient and highlights that context switching only makes sense when there are multiple threads to alternate between. The system will function, but without any meaningful multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',4,5,'2025-07-29_18-12-43','If only a single thread is created—such as a modified print_continuously thread that yields after printing—the OS will still run, but it won’t behave as expected. Each time the thread yields, the scheduler will try to switch to the “next” thread. However, since only one thread exists, the scheduler will simply switch back to the same thread. This means the thread will keep yielding to itself in a loop. While this won\'t cause a crash, it’s inefficient and highlights that context switching only makes sense when there are multiple threads to alternate between. The system will function, but without any meaningful multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',5,1,'2025-07-31_15-23-07','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first defines the thread function to take a single void* parameter, allowing flexibility in the type of data passed. When calling osCreateThread, the user passes the address of the data (e.g., an int or a pointer to a struct) as the second argument. Inside the thread function, this pointer is then cast to the appropriate type and dereferenced. This design ensures that thread functions have a consistent interface while still allowing the user to pass in any kind of input data they need.\nThe OS, in turn, is responsible for preparing the thread\'s stack so that the CPU will interpret it correctly when the thread is launched. Specifically, osCreateThread preloads the stack with the standard exception return frame expected by the ARM Cortex-M processor: xPSR, PC (the thread function), LR, and R12 through R0. By placing the pointer to the user data into the stack position for R0, the CPU will automatically load this value into the R0 register when the thread starts running. Since R0 is where arguments are passed in the ARM procedure call standard, the thread receives the correct input value seamlessly as if it had been called like a regular function.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',5,2,'2025-07-31_15-23-07','You cannot call osYield() from within PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because these handlers already run in an exception context, and osYield() triggers another exception (via SVC #4). Triggering an exception while already inside one can lead to nested exceptions, which can corrupt the stack or push the system into a fault state. For example, if osYield() is called in PendSV_Handler, it would request another context switch while one is already in progress—this recursion leads to stack overflow or unexpected behavior.\nThis is bad because it breaks the atomicity and determinism of your context switching mechanism. The context switch process depends on saving and restoring a very specific register state; nesting another exception in the middle of that process risks corrupting the saved context or losing track of which thread was supposed to resume. Instead, the handlers should only perform the mechanics of switching and not make any further system calls or scheduling requests. Keeping these layers cleanly separated preserves system stability and ensures predictable multithreading behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',5,3,'2025-07-31_15-23-07','Reducing the OS clock tick interval from 1 ms to 0.1 ms increases the timer resolution, allowing for finer-grained control over task scheduling and timing functions. This can improve responsiveness for real-time tasks that require more precise timing, such as high-frequency data sampling or motor control. A shorter tick period enables the scheduler to react faster to osYield or time-based delays, making time slices feel smoother and improving the system’s ability to handle fast-changing workloads.\nHowever, this change comes with downsides. A 0.1 ms tick means the system will generate 10× more interrupts than before, significantly increasing the CPU overhead spent handling the SysTick timer. This can reduce the time available for user threads and increase power consumption, especially in battery-powered or energy-constrained devices. Additionally, the increased interrupt rate could introduce more context switching, which degrades overall system performance and can make debugging more difficult due to higher complexity and less predictable behavior. For most embedded applications, a balance must be struck between responsiveness and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',5,4,'2025-07-31_15-23-07','From the user\'s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires that each thread voluntarily yield control of the CPU using a call like osYield. This means that well-behaved programs must be written to periodically give up control, otherwise other threads won\'t get a chance to run. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking allows the OS to forcibly interrupt running threads, typically using a timer interrupt like SysTick. This makes scheduling automatic and responsive, as threads are switched based on timing or priority rather than programmer discipline.\nFrom the OS\'s perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to implement: the scheduler only needs to switch context when the currently running thread explicitly requests it. This reduces the need for complex interrupt handling or saving/restoring CPU state at arbitrary times. Pre-emptive multitasking, however, requires the OS to handle context switching asynchronously—saving the current thread\'s state at interrupt time, deciding what thread should run next, and restoring its context—all without corrupting data or introducing race conditions. While more powerful, pre-emption introduces complexity in timing, synchronization, and critical section protection.\n',1,'s25'),('a6lakhan',5,5,'2025-07-31_15-23-07','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately invoke the same core mechanism in the OS: a context switch. Whether a thread voluntarily calls osYield() (cooperative) or is forcibly interrupted by a timer like SysTick_Handler (pre-emptive), the result is the same—control is transferred to the scheduler (osSched()), which saves the current thread\'s state and restores another’s. From the scheduler\'s perspective, it doesn\'t matter why a context switch was triggered; it just manages the transition between threads.\nThis cooperation enables more flexible multitasking. Time-based pre-emption ensures that even if a thread forgets to yield, the system stays responsive and fair. Meanwhile, allowing threads to yield voluntarily can reduce the number of interrupts and improve efficiency in well-structured applications. Together, they create a hybrid system that combines the predictability of cooperative multitasking with the robustness of pre-emptive multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',1,1,'2025-05-13_10-11-29','The HAL, short for Hardware Abstraction Layer, allows the user to ‘black-box’ the specific implementation of communication buses such as UART, which we saw in the lab. As a user, I can ignore the low-level hardware and use the driver functions that already exist to use these peripherals.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',1,1,'2025-05-13_10-49-30','The HAL, short for Hardware Abstraction Layer, allows the user to ‘black-box’ the specific implementation of communication buses such as UART, which we saw in the lab. As a user, I can ignore the low-level hardware and use the driver functions that already exist to use these peripherals.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',1,2,'2025-05-13_10-11-29','__io_putchar is a function that takes in an integer (representing a character), transmits the character over UART, and returns the same integer. The reason we need this function is because printf relies on the __io_putchar auxiliary function to work.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',1,2,'2025-05-13_10-49-30','__io_putchar is a function that takes in an integer (representing a character), transmits the character over UART, and returns the same integer. The reason we need this function is because printf relies on the __io_putchar auxiliary function to work. The desired output of printf may be different between projects – for example this project uses printf to print to console, but another project may want to use printf to interface with an LCD screen, that communicates via a non-UART protocol. Using this auxillary function allows the main function to remain the same, while leaving the lower-level implementation able to change on different platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',1,3,'2025-05-13_10-11-29','Because the MSP changes every time an instruction is executed, using printf() to print the MSP to the debug console changes the value of the MSP. Using the debugger allows us to see the MSP without executing an instruction.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',1,3,'2025-05-13_10-49-30','Because the MSP changes every time an instruction is executed, using printf() to print the MSP to the debug console changes the value of the MSP. Hence, using printf() would not let us accurately see the MSP, but using the debugger allows us to see the MSP without executing an instruction.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',1,4,'2025-05-13_10-11-29','The new value for PSP_val is one stack size greater than MSP_init_val, so to obtain it we add the min stack size to MSP_init_val. The reason we need to know the initial location of the MSP is so that we can use it to move ‘forward’ by one stack size, ensuring that we have allocated enough space for the main stack before our first process stack starts.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',1,4,'2025-05-13_10-49-30','The new value for PSP_val is one stack size greater than MSP_init_val, so to obtain it we add _Min_Stack_Size to MSP_init_val. The reason we need to know the initial location of the MSP is so that we can use it to increment the PSP_val by _Min_Stack_Size, ensuring that we have allocated enough space for the main stack before our first process stack starts.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',1,5,'2025-05-13_10-11-29','To make n stacks for multiple threads, we could make n variables, such as PSP0_val, PSP1_val, and so forth, such that PSPn+1_val = PSPn_val + 1 stack size (in our case, 0x4000). Hence, if MSP is at 0x0000, PSP1_val would be at 0x4000, PSP2_val would be at 0x8000, and PSP3_val would be at 0xc000.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',1,5,'2025-05-13_10-49-30','To make n stacks for multiple threads, we could make n variables, such as PSP0_val, PSP1_val, and so forth, such that PSPn+1_val = PSPn_val + _Min_Stack_Size (in our case, 0x4000). Hence, if MSP is at 0x0000, PSP1_val would be at 0x4000, PSP2_val would be at 0x8000, and PSP3_val would be at 0xc000. These values should be compared to the valid RAM address space  (in this case, starting at 0x20000000 with a size of 96kb), to make sure none of these addresses are outside the bounds of RAM.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',2,1,'2025-06-27_00-42-15','When the PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, this told the CPU that the next instruction to run would be the first instruction of the print continuously function, and to proceed from there. This is similar to what happens when a function is called the normal way, but without the initialization of stack variables.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',2,1,'2025-06-27_09-43-37','When the PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, this told the CPU that the next instruction to run would be the first instruction of the print continuously function, and to proceed from there. This is similar to what happens when a function is called the normal way, but without the initialization of stack variables.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',2,1,'2025-06-27_15-02-53','When the PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, this told the CPU that the next instruction to run would be the first instruction of the print continuously function, and to proceed from there. This is exactly what happens when a function is called – the next instruction to run will be the first instruction of the function, and hence PC is set to a pointer to the function.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',2,2,'2025-06-27_00-42-15','A system call is a request from a user application to the operating system to access hardware or to perform privileged tasks, like creating a new process. This allows a balance between allowing programs to utilize these resources and perform these tasks, while maintaining system control and preventing situations like one process ending other processes to free up CPU time.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',2,2,'2025-06-27_09-43-37','A system call is a function called by a user application that makes a request to the operating system to access hardware or to perform privileged tasks, like creating a new process. This allows a balance between allowing programs to utilize these resources and perform these tasks, while maintaining system control and preventing situations like one process ending other processes to free up CPU time. By requiring the process to go through the operating system, rather than granting direct access, the OS can at any time stop providing access to one program to grant it to another, or to ensure that the program is not attempting to undertake malicious actions.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',2,2,'2025-06-27_15-02-53','A system call is a function called by a user application that makes a request to the operating system to access hardware or to perform privileged tasks, like creating a new process. This allows a balance between allowing programs to utilize these resources and perform these tasks, while maintaining system control and preventing situations like one process ending other processes to free up CPU time. By requiring the process to go through the operating system, rather than granting direct access, the OS can at any time stop providing access to one program to grant it to another, or to ensure that the program is not attempting to undertake malicious actions.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',2,3,'2025-06-27_00-42-15','The thread’s stack was setup by pushing the register values onto the stack in a specific order, so that when the stack is ‘popped’ to restore the thread’s context, the right register values end up in the correct register. The process of setting up a thread’s stack involves setting up the correct program counter, link register, program status registers, and at minimum, R0 through R3. These values need to be stored such that the thread runs properly.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',2,3,'2025-06-27_09-43-37','The thread’s stack was setup by pushing the register values onto the stack in a specific order, so that when the stack is ‘popped’ to restore the thread’s context (the state of the CPU registers when the thread was switched away from), the right register values end up in the correct register. Note that this happens before the thread is run – this is to ‘trick’ the processor into resuming from ‘where it left off’ – the start of the thread. The process of setting up a thread’s stack involves setting up the correct program counter, link register, program status registers, and at minimum, R0 through R3. These values need to be stored such that the thread runs properly. For example, an incorrect restoration of the PC will result in arbitrary code running, or an incorrect restoration of R1 could result in a function being called with an incorrect or invalid argument.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',2,3,'2025-06-27_15-02-53','The thread’s stack was setup by allocating a valid block of memory, with a pointer to the ‘top’ of the stack (since on the Cortex M4 the stack grows downward, to lesser memory addresses), and pushing the register values onto the stack in a specific order. The allotment of valid memory is necessary to prevent an attempt at reading invalid memory, causing a hard fault, or an attempt at overwriting the main stack. Once the stack is ‘popped’ to restore the thread’s context (the state of the CPU registers when the thread was switched away from), the right register values end up in the correct register. Note that this happens before the thread is run – this is to ‘trick’ the processor into resuming from ‘where it left off’ – the start of the thread. The process of setting up a thread’s stack involves setting up the correct program counter, link register, program status registers, and at minimum, R0 through R3. These values need to be stored such that the thread runs properly. For example, an incorrect restoration of the PC will result in arbitrary code running, or an incorrect restoration of R1 could result in a function being called with an incorrect or invalid argument.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',2,4,'2025-06-27_00-42-15','In the SVC_handler function, we are using MSP to track our progression through this handler. If we were to not setup PSP, it is zero by default, and so switching to PSP would result in a hard fault, because 0x0 is an invalid memory address. If you don’t switch to PSP, the Cortex M4 would use MSP by default, and so the thread and ISR would share the same stack, resulting in a likely stack corruption.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',2,4,'2025-06-27_09-43-37','In the SVC_handler function, we are using MSP to track our progression through this handler. If we were to not setup PSP, it is zero by default, and so switching to PSP would result in a hard fault, because 0x0 is an invalid memory address. If you don’t switch to PSP, the Cortex M4 would use MSP by default, and so the thread and ISR would share the same stack, resulting in a likely stack corruption. The thread having its own valid memory address, separate from the default stack pointer, or any other threads, prevent other threads from overwriting local variables, or even worse, register values saved during a context switch. If PSP is not set to the thread’s stack address, the thread may use another, invalid stack, or overwrite another thread’s stack.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',2,4,'2025-06-27_15-02-53','In the SVC_handler function, we are using MSP to track our progression through this handler. If we were to not setup PSP, it is zero by default, and so switching to PSP would result in a hard fault, because 0x0 is an invalid memory address. If you don’t switch to PSP, the Cortex M4 would use MSP by default, and so the thread and ISR would share the same stack, resulting in a likely stack corruption. The thread having its own valid memory address, separate from the default stack pointer, or any other threads, prevent other threads from overwriting local variables, or even worse, register values saved during a context switch. If PSP is not set to the thread’s stack address, the thread may use another, invalid stack, or overwrite another thread’s stack.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',2,5,'2025-06-27_00-42-15','If the thread terminated, it would return to the link register, which was defined as 0xA in this case. This is an invalid address, and hence the processor would hard fault. This could be prevented by setting the link register to a pointer to some function to terminate a thread nicely, rather than returning to an invalid address or jumping to memory with unknown contents.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',2,5,'2025-06-27_09-43-37','As the thread terminates, the Cortex M4 will pop the return address from the stack and store it in LR. In  this case, 0xA was stored, which is an invalid memory address, and hence the processor will hardfault. This is because as the Cortex M4 context-switches between threads, it relies on the stack being a valid processor state, but LR = 0xA is not a possible state of the system. To prevent this, a pointer to a thread termination function could be pushed onto the stack at the correct point such that this address is loaded into LR upon the termination of the thread, so that the remaining artifacts from the thread are tidied up properly.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',2,5,'2025-06-27_15-02-53','As the thread terminates, the Cortex M4 will pop the return address from the stack and store it in LR. In  this case, 0xA was stored, which is an invalid memory address, and hence the processor will hardfault. This is because as the Cortex M4 context-switches between threads, it relies on the stack being a valid processor state, but LR = 0xA is not a possible state of the system. To prevent this, a pointer to a thread termination function could be pushed onto the stack at the correct point such that this address is loaded into LR upon the termination of the thread, so that the remaining artifacts from the thread are tidied up properly.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',3,1,'2025-06-27_19-56-50','First, each .c file is compiled by itself into its own .obj file by the compiler. Then, the linker combines all of these separate object files – the code sections (.text), data (.data), uninitialized data (.bss), and symbols, and resolves cross-references (such as function calls in main of functions described elsewhere). In addition, the linker script specifies the format and output of the final executable, as well as the memory layout.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',3,1,'2025-06-27_21-47-19','First, each file is preprocessed by the compiler. This involves steps such as parsing preprocessor directives (marked with a # in front of each, such as #includes, #define, and etcetra). In addition, the files are optimized to remove unnecessary instructions or branches, such as if a conditional statement’s condition is never true. Then, each .c file is compiled by itself into its own .obj file by the compiler. Then, the linker combines all of these separate object files – the code sections (.text), data (.data), uninitialized data (.bss), and symbols, and resolves cross-references (such as function calls in main of functions described elsewhere). In addition, the linker script specifies the format and output of the final executable, as well as the memory layout.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',3,1,'2025-06-28_00-00-58','First, each file is preprocessed by the compiler. This involves steps such as parsing preprocessor directives (marked with a # in front of each, such as #includes, #define, and etcetra). In addition, the files are optimized to remove unnecessary instructions or branches, such as if a conditional statement’s condition is never true. Then, each .c file is compiled by itself into its own .obj file by the compiler. Then, the linker combines all of these separate object files – the code sections (.text), data (.data), uninitialized data (.bss), and symbols, and resolves cross-references (such as function calls in main of functions described elsewhere). In addition, the linker script specifies the format and output of the final executable, as well as the memory layout.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',3,2,'2025-06-27_19-56-50','Including .c files cause the same file to get compiled multiple times (ex. If you include kernel.c in main, then it will get compiled separated as kernel.c, and also as a part of main.c). If, for example, you compile the STM32 project and then update kernel.c, you need to recompile both kernel.c and main.c. Furthermore, incorrect includes statements can lead to a linker error, as the linker will see any symbol in kernel.c twice.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',3,2,'2025-06-27_21-47-19','Including .c files cause the same file to get compiled multiple times (ex. If you include kernel.c in main, then it will get compiled separated as kernel.c, and also as a part of main.c). If, for example, you compile the STM32 project and then update kernel.c, you need to recompile both kernel.c and main.c. Furthermore, incorrect includes statements can lead to a linker error, as the linker will see any symbol in kernel.c twice.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',3,2,'2025-06-28_00-00-58','Including .c files cause the same file to get compiled multiple times (ex. If you include kernel.c in main, then it will get compiled separated as kernel.c, and also as a part of main.c). If, for example, you compile the STM32 project and then update kernel.c, you need to recompile both kernel.c and main.c. Furthermore, incorrect includes statements can lead to a linker error, as the linker will see any symbol in kernel.c twice.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',3,3,'2025-06-27_19-56-50','Write function to run as a thread\nInitialize kernel\nCreate thread by passing function to run as an argument\nStart kernel\nSuccess!\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',3,3,'2025-06-27_21-47-19','Write function to run as a thread\nInitialize kernel\nCreate thread by passing function to run as an argument\nStart kernel\nSuccess!\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',3,3,'2025-06-28_00-00-58','Write function to run as a thread\nInitialize kernel\nCreate thread by passing function to run as an argument\nStart kernel\nSuccess!\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',3,4,'2025-06-27_19-56-50','The stack pool is a region of reserved memory for the sole purpose of creating thread stacks from. Making this large enough allows the kernel to run multiple threads at once, as each thread can have its own stack within this ‘pool’ of memory. In a future lab, we can create multiple threads by calling osCreateThread multiple times, each with its own function passed as an argument, and then call osKernelStart to start running all threads at the same time.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',3,4,'2025-06-27_21-47-19','The stack pool is a region of reserved memory for the sole purpose of creating thread stacks from. Making this large enough allows the kernel to run multiple threads at once, as each thread can have its own stack within this ‘pool’ of memory. In a future lab, we can create multiple threads by calling osCreateThread multiple times, each with its own function passed as an argument, and then call osKernelStart to start running all threads at the same time. The purpose of subdividing this pool into smaller blocks is to prevent threads from overwriting each other’s stacks – for example, a thread overwriting another thread’s PC could lead to arbitrary code execution, a huge concern security-wise and also leading to unknown thread behavior. The mechanism for tracking which subdivisions are used could be a bitmap, for example – if you have 16 blocks of 4kB memory, you can use 16 bits that are 0 if the block is unused and 1 if the block is being used by a thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',3,4,'2025-06-28_00-00-58','The stack pool is a region of reserved memory for the sole purpose of creating thread stacks from. Making this large enough allows the kernel to run multiple threads at once, as each thread can have its own stack within this ‘pool’ of memory. In a future lab, we can create multiple threads by calling osCreateThread multiple times, each with its own function passed as an argument, and then call osKernelStart to start running all threads at the same time. The purpose of subdividing this pool into smaller blocks is to prevent threads from overwriting each other’s stacks – for example, a thread overwriting another thread’s PC could lead to arbitrary code execution, a huge concern security-wise and also leading to unknown thread behavior. The mechanism for tracking which subdivisions are used could be a bitmap, for example – if you have 16 blocks of 4kB memory, you can use 16 bits that are 0 if the block is unused and 1 if the block is being used by a thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',3,5,'2025-06-27_19-56-50','The kernel file contains all the code and management necessary to create and run threads, while the user file is much simpler, and contains only what each thread should do. From the user’s perspective, the difficulty of creating and running threads is abstracted away, like how opening an application on a desktop computer only involves double clicking an icon, while the spawning of processes and creation of virtual memory for the program goes unnoticed by the user.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',3,5,'2025-06-27_21-47-19','The kernel file contains all the code and management necessary to create and run threads, while the user file is much simpler, and contains only what each thread should do. From the user’s perspective, the difficulty of creating and running threads is abstracted away, like how opening an application on a desktop computer only involves double clicking an icon, while the spawning of processes and creation of virtual memory for the program goes unnoticed by the user. In addition, the kernel file contains all of the privileged instructions, while the user file contains only a limited number of ways to interact with privileged instructions – these are system calls, where the user requests the system to spawn a new thread, for example, but maintains the principle of least privilege.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',3,5,'2025-06-28_00-00-58','The kernel file contains all the code and management necessary to create and run threads, while the user file is much simpler, and contains only what each thread should do. From the user’s perspective, the difficulty of creating and running threads is abstracted away, like how opening an application on a desktop computer only involves double clicking an icon, while the spawning of processes and creation of virtual memory for the program goes unnoticed by the user. In addition, the kernel file contains all of the privileged instructions, while the user file contains only a limited number of ways to interact with privileged instructions – these are system calls, where the user requests the system to spawn a new thread, for example, but maintains the principle of least privilege.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',4,1,'2025-07-28_13-01-05','Using a queue to store TCBs is much more efficient than using an array. Firstly, if threads are added, they are added to the back of the queue by definition (queues are FIFO inherently). Also, adding/removing threads (ie if they are finished running and no longer need CPU time) is more time-efficient in a queue – in an array, these are O(n) but a queue is O(1). Furthermore, the implementation is easier – when you’re done running a thread, pop it and append it to the back if you need to come back to it. For round-robin scheduling, instead of tracking the current thread’s index and context switching to the thread in index + 1, just dequeue (and enqueue if necessary) the current thread.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',4,1,'2025-07-30_10-49-12','Using a queue to store TCBs is much more efficient than using an array. Firstly, if threads are added, they are added to the back of the queue (as queues are FIFO inherently). Also, adding/removing threads (ie if they are finished running and no longer need CPU time) is more time-efficient in a queue – in an array, these are O(n) but a queue is O(1). Furthermore, the implementation is easier – when you’re done running a thread, pop it and append it to the back if you need to come back to it. For round-robin scheduling, instead of tracking the current thread’s index and context switching to the thread in index + 1, just dequeue (and enqueue if necessary) the current thread. Because of what the scheduler is doing (finding the next thread to run, switching to it), it is already inherently using a system very similar to what a queue would look like, but using an array to store all threads. Swapping to a queue from an array would speed up the scheduling process very slightly, because in this case, dequeueing and enqueueing are O(1), whereas accessing the next thread in an array is O(n).\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',4,2,'2025-07-28_13-01-05','First, the current PSP is stored in R0. Then, R4-R11 are stored on the current thread’s stack and then updates R0 to point to the top of this stack (now R4). After this, the osSched() function is called, which sets PSP to the stack pointer of the next thread to run. Then, the link register is set to 0xFFFFFFFD, which will tell the processor to return to thread mode using PSP via an exception return, which will pop PC and xPSR automatically. Finally, R4-R11 are loaded from the new thread’s stack, and the new stack pointer is written to R0, restoring the context of the next thread to run. Once BX LR is called, the processor returns to thread mode (as detailed above), and proceeds with running the next thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',4,2,'2025-07-30_10-49-12','First, the current PSP is stored in R0. Then, R4-R11 are stored on the current thread’s stack and then updates R0 to point to the top of this stack (now R4). After this, the osSched() function is called, which sets PSP to the stack pointer of the next thread to run. Then, the link register is set to 0xFFFFFFFD, which will tell the processor to return to thread mode using PSP via an exception return, which will pop PC and xPSR automatically. Finally, R4-R11 are loaded from the new thread’s stack, and the new stack pointer is written to R0, restoring the context of the next thread to run. Once BX LR is called, the processor returns to thread mode (as detailed above), and proceeds with running the next thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',4,3,'2025-07-28_13-01-05','In theory, if higher priority tasks keep getting added, low priority tasks may never run. In some systems, this may be fine, but in real time systems, where all tasks must guarantee completion in some amount of time, this completely violates this rule. Running tasks solely on priority can lead to starvation of some threads, and if programs can assign their own priority (god forbid!), malicious programs can create many threads all with very high priorities, such that that program is the only program who’s threads can run.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',4,3,'2025-07-30_10-49-12','In theory, if higher priority tasks keep getting added, low priority tasks will never run, and in fact only the highest priority task will ever run. In some systems, this may be fine, but in real time systems, where all tasks must guarantee completion in some amount of time, this completely violates this rule. Running tasks solely on priority will lead to starvation of all threads except for the thread with highest priority (specifically, starvation of cpu time), and if programs can assign their own priority (god forbid!), malicious programs can create many threads all with very high priorities, such that that program is the only program whose threads can run. In fact, “switching tasks” based only on priority will lead to pointless overhead, as no other thread will be switched to, so having a thread “yield” will just waste time.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',4,4,'2025-07-28_13-01-05','Writing and calling C functions offers several advantages. Firstly, C code is more readable and easier to debug with less experience, and coming back to it (for example, in a future lab) for further development is easier compared to assembly. In addition, writing C code tends to be faster for development, and is easier to integrate into the rest of the OS kernel (written in C). However, writing these functions in C allows for less control over the result of the compiled code (compared to assembly which doesn’t need to be compiled because it is just assembly). Additionally, function calls in C introduce more overhead (setting up the registers for the call stack), which is unnecessary work, which could instead be spent running the next thread.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',4,4,'2025-07-30_10-49-12','Writing and calling C functions offers several advantages. Firstly, C code is more readable and easier to debug with less experience, and coming back to it (for example, in a future lab) for further development is easier compared to assembly. In addition, writing C code tends to be faster for development, and is easier to integrate into the rest of the OS kernel (written in C). However, writing these functions in C allows for less control over the result of the compiled code (compared to assembly which doesn’t need to be compiled because it is just assembly). Additionally, function calls in C introduce more overhead (setting up the registers for the call stack), which is unnecessary work, which could instead be spent running the next thread.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',4,5,'2025-07-28_13-01-05','Yes, the OS will still work. There is only a single thread, so the number of elements in the thread array is one, and hence the next scheduled thread will be the thread that just yielded. The output will now be the repeated output of print_continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',4,5,'2025-07-30_10-49-12','Yes, the OS will still work. There is only a single thread, so the number of elements in the thread array is one, and hence the next scheduled thread will be the thread that just yielded. The output will now be the repeated output of print_continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',5,1,'2025-07-30_13-56-56','How an argument gets passed into a thread:\nUser: Defines a thread function that takes a void* argument and calls the OS’s thread creation function, passing the function pointer and a pointer to the argument data.\nOS: Allocates a stack for the new thread and sets up an initial CPU stack frame that includes the thread function’s address (PC) and the argument pointer in the register (R0 on ARM). When the scheduler runs this thread, the CPU restores these registers, making it appear as if the function was called normally with the argument.\nWhy it works: Because the CPU calling conventions and exception return restore the function’s argument register (R0) from the prepared stack, the thread starts executing with the correct argument without extra code.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',5,2,'2025-07-30_13-56-56','You can’t call osYield() in PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because…\nThese handlers are themselves part of the context-switching mechanism or critical OS infrastructure.\nCalling osYield() inside them would attempt to start another context switch while one is already in progress.\nThis causes re-entrant or nested context switches, which your kernel is usually not designed to handle.\nStack corruption, inconsistent CPU state, or lost registers may occur.\nIt can cause deadlocks or hard faults, crashing the system.\nIf you call osYield() inside of an exception:\nThe kernel tries to trigger a new PendSV exception or context switch while already switching.\nSince the CPU is in an interrupt or exception context, it may mask or defer the new interrupt, causing undefined or delayed behavior.\nYour saved CPU state might get corrupted if multiple context switches overlap.\nThe scheduler’s state variables (curr_thread, stack pointers) may become inconsistent.\nThis usually leads to system crashes or unpredictable behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('a7lang',5,3,'2025-07-30_13-56-56','Benefits of a 0.1 ms Tick\nFiner Timing Resolution:\nYou get more precise control over task delays, timeouts, and scheduling granularity. This helps with real-time tasks that require sub-millisecond accuracy.\nMore Responsive Scheduling:\nShorter ticks mean the OS can respond to events and switch tasks more frequently, improving responsiveness, especially in systems with many short tasks or interrupts.\nBetter Support for Short Timeouts:\nTasks that need to wait for very short durations can do so more accurately.\nDownsides of a 0.1 ms Tick\nIncreased CPU Overhead:\nThe timer interrupt (SysTick) fires 10 times more often, which means more frequent context switching checks and interrupt handling. This consumes more CPU cycles, reducing available time for application code.\nPotential Scheduling Jitter:\nIf the system can\'t keep up with the high interrupt rate, timing may become irregular or cause missed deadlines.\nMore Complexity in Timekeeping:\nSome OS time-related calculations and counters might need to be adjusted for the finer tick, possibly increasing code complexity.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',5,4,'2025-07-30_13-56-56','From the User’s View\nCooperative multitasking:\nTasks run until they choose to give up control (by calling something like yield). If a task doesn’t yield, others can’t run.\nPre-emptive multitasking:\nThe OS can interrupt tasks at any time to switch to another task, even if the task doesn’t want to give up control.\nFrom the OS’s View\nCooperative:\nThe OS switches tasks only when a task tells it to (when it yields). The OS just waits.\nPre-emptive:\nThe OS uses timer interrupts to force task switching regularly, no matter what the tasks are doing.\n',1,'s25'),('a7lang',5,5,'2025-07-30_13-56-56','How they coexist:\nYielding is when a running thread voluntarily gives up the CPU by calling osYield(). This allows the scheduler to run and switch to another thread right away.\nTime-based preemption uses a hardware timer (like SysTick) to interrupt the running thread at regular intervals (e.g., every 1 ms). When the timer fires, it triggers a context switch automatically—even if the thread doesn’t call osYield().\nWhy mixing both makes sense:\nYielding gives threads a way to switch early if they finish work or want to wait, improving efficiency by not waiting for the timer.\nPreemption ensures fairness and responsiveness, so if a thread never yields, it still gets interrupted and others get CPU time.\nTogether, the OS scheduler:\nResponds immediately to explicit yields (fast voluntary switch).\nAlso guarantees that no thread can hog the CPU indefinitely thanks to periodic preemption.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,1,'2025-06-25_21-20-21','HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer and it is a software tool used to help interface between the operating system and the hardware of the device by providing functions that abstract low-level details of a device. It is used in general embedded projects because it helps simplify the programming process so that programmers do not have to know all the low level details of their hardware and can instead use the interface provided by the HAL.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,1,'2025-06-25_21-34-15','HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer and it is a software tool used to help interface between the operating system and the hardware of the device by providing functions that abstract low-level details of a device. It is used in general embedded projects because it helps simplify the programming process so that programmers do not have to know all the low level details of their hardware and can instead use the interface provided by the HAL.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,1,'2025-06-25_22-09-17','HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer and it is a software tool used to help interface between the operating system and the hardware of the device by providing functions that abstract low-level details of a device. It is used in general embedded projects because it helps simplify the programming process so that programmers do not have to know all the low level details of their hardware and can instead use the interface provided by the HAL.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,1,'2025-06-25_22-47-08','HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer and it is a software tool used to help interface between the operating system and the hardware of the device by providing functions that abstract low-level details of a device. It is used in general embedded projects because it helps simplify the programming process so that programmers do not have to know all the low level details of their hardware and can instead use the interface provided by the HAL.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,2,'2025-06-25_21-20-21','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that writes a single character to the correct input/output channels and is called repeatedly by the printf function to print out a string. It is needed because the printf function is found in the stdio.h library which relies on helper functions to help it interact with the hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,2,'2025-06-25_21-34-15','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that writes a single character to the correct input/output channels and is called repeatedly by the printf function to print out a string. It is needed because the printf function is found in the stdio.h library which relies on helper functions to help it interact with the hardware. Different pieces of hardware would have different ways of outputting a character and so they will have different helper functions to carry out the task.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,2,'2025-06-25_22-09-17','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that writes a single character to the correct input/output channels and is called repeatedly by the printf function to print out a string. It is needed because the printf function is found in the stdio.h library which relies on helper functions to help it interact with the hardware. Different pieces of hardware would have different ways of outputting a character and so they will have different helper functions written by the user to carry out the task.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,2,'2025-06-25_22-47-08','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that writes a single character to the input/output channel, which in this case is the UART2 and is called repeatedly by the printf function to print out a string. It is needed because the printf function is found in the stdio.h library which relies on helper functions to help it interact with the hardware. Different pieces of hardware would have different ways of outputting a character and so they will have different helper functions written by the user to carry out the task.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,3,'2025-06-25_21-20-21','The debugger is used in this lab so that we can see the values of the stack and registers at any point within the program by creating breakpoints. Printf cannot be used because running the function to print the code would alter the values of the registers that we are trying to check.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,3,'2025-06-25_21-34-15','The debugger is used in this lab so that we can see the values of the stack and registers at any point within the program by creating breakpoints. Printf cannot be used because running the function to print the code would alter the values of the registers that we are trying to check.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,3,'2025-06-25_22-09-17','The debugger is used in this lab so that we can see the values of the stack and registers at any point within the program by creating breakpoints. Breakpoints allow the user to see the state of the stack and the registers without having any side effects. Printf cannot be used because running the function to print the code would alter the values of the registers that we are trying to check.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,3,'2025-06-25_22-47-08','The debugger is used in this lab so that we can see the values of the stack and registers at any point within the program by creating breakpoints. Breakpoints allow the user to see the state of the stack and the registers without having any side effects. Printf cannot be used because running the function to print the code would alter the values of the registers that we are trying to check.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,4,'2025-06-25_21-20-21','The new value for PSP_val was obtained by subtracting the size of the main stack from MSP_init_val because subtracting the size of the main stack from its initial value will give us the end of the main stack which would be where the process stack starts. The subtraction operation was used because the stack grows downwards. The method will not work if we do not know the initial location of the MSP because we will not have a set point to start measuring the size of MSP from and therefore will not find the start of the process stack to set PSP to.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,4,'2025-06-25_21-34-15','The new value for PSP_val was obtained by subtracting the size of the main stack from MSP_init_val because subtracting the size of the main stack from its initial value will give us the end of the main stack which would be where the process stack starts. The subtraction operation was used because the stack grows downwards. The method will not work if we do not know the initial location of the MSP because we will not have a set point to start measuring the size of MSP from and therefore will not find the start of the process stack to set PSP to.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,4,'2025-06-25_22-09-17','The new value for PSP_val was obtained by subtracting the size of the main stack from MSP_init_val because subtracting the size of the main stack from its initial value will give us the end of the main stack which would be where the process stack starts. The subtraction operation was used because the stack grows downwards. The method will not work if we do not know the initial location of the MSP because we will not have a set point to start measuring the size of MSP from and therefore will not find the start of the process stack to set PSP to.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,4,'2025-06-25_22-47-08','The new value for PSP_val was obtained by subtracting the size of the main stack from MSP_init_val because subtracting the size of the main stack from its initial value will give us the end of the main stack which would be where the process stack starts. The subtraction operation was used because the stack grows downwards. The method will not work if we do not know the initial location of the MSP because we will not have a set point to start measuring the size of MSP from and therefore will not find the start of the process stack to set PSP to.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,5,'2025-06-25_21-20-21','To find where each stack starts in the memory, we would need to obtain the size of memory occupied by previous stacks by multiplying the stack size by the number of stacks that came before the current stack. We could then subtract that value from the initial location of MSP to find the start of the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,5,'2025-06-25_21-34-15','To find where each stack starts in the memory, we would need to obtain the size of memory occupied by previous stacks by multiplying the stack size by the number of stacks that came before the current stack. We could then subtract that value from the initial location of MSP to find the start of the stack.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,5,'2025-06-25_22-09-17','To find where each stack starts in the memory, we would need to obtain the size of memory occupied by previous stacks by multiplying the stack size by the number of stacks that came before the current stack. We could then subtract that value from the initial location of MSP to find the start of the stack (the subtraction operation is chosen because the stack grows downwards). There must also be a check implemented to ensure that the memory boundaries are not exceeded and that alignment of the stacks is maintained.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',1,5,'2025-06-25_22-47-08','To find where each stack starts in the memory, we would need to obtain the size of memory occupied by previous stacks by multiplying the stack size by the number of stacks that came before the current stack. We could then subtract that value from the initial location of MSP to find the start of the stack (the subtraction operation is chosen because the stack grows downwards). There must also be a check implemented to ensure that the memory boundaries are not exceeded and that alignment of the stacks is maintained.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,1,'2025-06-25_23-32-16','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the program would immediately jump to executing the function which included an infinite while loop where a message was printed repeatedly.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,1,'2025-06-26_17-53-07','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the program would immediately jump to executing the function which included an infinite while loop where a message was printed repeatedly.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,1,'2025-06-27_01-55-10','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the program would immediately jump to executing the function which included an infinite while loop where a message was printed repeatedly.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,2,'2025-06-25_23-32-16','A system call is an intentionally generated software trap that a program initiates when it needs the attention of the OS system to interface with hardware or perform tasks that require privileged access. The OS must pause its current operation to enter into kernel mode and use a handler to execute the requested task. Then the control is returned to the user program and may include a return value.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,2,'2025-06-26_17-53-07','A system call is an intentionally generated software trap (interrupt) that a program initiates when it needs the attention of the OS system to interface with hardware or perform tasks that require privileged access. It acts like a special kind of function call that transitions the processor from user mode to kernel mode, allowing the OS to safely perform operations such as accessing hardware, managing files, or creating processes. This prevents untrusted user code from directly interacting with hardware or critical resources, ensuring system security and stability. The OS must pause its current operation to enter into kernel mode and use a handler to execute the requested task. Then the control is returned to the user program and may include a return value.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,2,'2025-06-27_01-55-10','A system call is an intentionally generated software trap (interrupt) that a program initiates when it needs the attention of the OS system to interface with hardware or perform tasks that require privileged access. It acts like a special kind of function call that transitions the processor from user mode to kernel mode, allowing the OS to safely perform operations such as accessing hardware, managing files, or creating processes. This prevents untrusted user code from directly interacting with hardware or critical resources, ensuring system security and stability. The OS must pause its current operation to enter into kernel mode and use a handler to execute the requested task. Then the control is returned to the user program and may include a return value.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,3,'2025-06-25_23-32-16','Setting up a thread’s stack is to push register values that the thread would need to run onto a stack so that when the thread starts to run the register values will be popped off the stack and stored in the physical registers of the chip. The thread’s stack was set up by pushing the register values we would want to have if the thread were already running onto the thread stack, ensuring that PC contains the address of the thread’s function so that it will be the next instruction executed.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,3,'2025-06-26_17-53-07','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing an area in memory that will serve as the thread’s stack space, ensuring the thread has its own storage for function calls, local variables and context switching. Setting up a thread’s stack involves pushing specific register values representing the thread’s initial context onto the allocated section of the stack, ensuring that the PC register will contain the address of the thread’s function so that it will be the next instruction executed. When the thread starts to run, the CPU will pop the register values from the stack according to the order they were pushed in and stores them on the physical registers. Then the thread resumes execution starting from the PC value. The order in which the register values are stored on the stack is important because the CPU expects them in a specific sequence during context switching, so if their order is incorrect the CPU would restore wrong values to the registers which could cause a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,3,'2025-06-27_01-55-10','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing an area in memory that will serve as the thread’s stack space, ensuring the thread has its own storage for function calls, local variables and context switching. Setting up a thread’s stack involves pushing specific register values representing the thread’s initial context onto the allocated section of the stack, ensuring that the PC register will contain the address of the thread’s function so that it will be the next instruction executed. When the thread starts to run, the CPU will pop the register values from the stack according to the order they were pushed in and stores them on the physical registers. Then the thread resumes execution starting from the PC value. The order in which the register values are stored on the stack is important because the CPU expects them in a specific sequence during context switching, so if their order is incorrect the CPU would restore wrong values to the registers which could cause a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,4,'2025-06-25_23-32-16','If the PSP is not set before running the thread, the incorrect stack pointer will be used when the register values are pushed onto the stack. When attempting to restore the thread\'s context the processor will load register values from the wrong memory location on the stack. As a result, the register values popped off the stack and onto the physical registers will be invalid or corrupted, causing the program to behave unpredictably and trigger a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,4,'2025-06-26_17-53-07','The processor uses the MSP by default, so it is essential that the user switches from MSP to PSP when setting up a thread. If the PSP is not set before running the thread, the incorrect stack pointer, either MSP or an uninitialized PSP, will be used when the register values are pushed onto the stack. When attempting to restore the thread\'s context the processor will load register values from the wrong memory location on the stack. As a result, the register values popped off the stack and onto the physical registers will be invalid or corrupted, causing the program to behave unpredictably and trigger a hard fault. Setting the PSP correctly ensures that the CPU will use the thread’s correct stack space during execution. This stack needs to be properly aligned and contain the expected register values in the right order to reliably manage running the thread functions.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,4,'2025-06-27_01-55-10','The processor uses the MSP by default, so it is essential that the user switches from MSP to PSP when setting up a thread. If the PSP is not set before running the thread, the incorrect stack pointer, either MSP or an uninitialized PSP, will be used when the register values are pushed onto the stack. When attempting to restore the thread\'s context the processor will load register values from the wrong memory location on the stack. As a result, the register values popped off the stack and onto the physical registers will be invalid or corrupted, causing the program to behave unpredictably and trigger a hard fault. Setting the PSP correctly ensures that the CPU will use the thread’s correct stack space during execution. This stack needs to be properly aligned and contain the expected register values in the right order to reliably manage running the thread functions.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,5,'2025-06-25_23-32-16','The PC would increment, and the processor would move on to execute the next thing on the stack as if it were an instruction since there is no return address in the thread function\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,5,'2025-06-26_17-53-07','If the thread function were to print something once and then return, the processor will try to pop a return address from the stack despite not having a valid return address stored there since thread functions do not have a saved return address. The CPU will try to execute whatever value was stored next on the stack as an instruction which would lead to undefined behaviour or a hard fault since the instruction would be invalid. The processor includes hardware mechanisms to catch this kind of fault, but the end result is that the system crashes unless the behavior is explicitly handled. To prevent this, thread functions should either loop indefinitely or call a thread exit handler provided by the operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',2,5,'2025-06-27_01-55-10','If the thread function were to print something once and then return, the processor will try to pop a return address from the stack despite not having a valid return address stored there since thread functions do not have a saved return address. The CPU will try to execute whatever value was stored next on the stack as an instruction which would lead to undefined behaviour or a hard fault since the instruction would be invalid. The processor includes hardware mechanisms to catch this kind of fault, but the end result is that the system crashes unless the behavior is explicitly handled. To prevent this, thread functions should either loop indefinitely or call a thread exit handler provided by the operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',3,1,'2025-06-27_20-07-20','The process by which multiple .c files get compiled into a single executable can be broken down into three steps: preprocessing, compilation, and linking. The preprocessing step removes all comments a and expands all code lines starting with a #, such as #include, #define, and other macros. It replaces them with the corresponding information in their header files, then the result is converted into a .c file that is ready for compilation. Every preprocessed .c file is compiled into its own object file containing machine code that is still not ready to be executed yet during the compilation process. Finally, the linking step links all the object files into one executable and matches up function calls with their definitions, ensuring that there exists only one definition for every function called.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',3,1,'2025-06-27_21-17-16','The process by which multiple .c files get compiled into a single executable can be broken down into three steps: preprocessing, compilation, and linking. The preprocessing step removes all comments a and expands all code lines starting with a #, such as #include, #define, and other macros. It replaces them with the corresponding information in their header files, then the result is converted into a .c file that is ready for compilation. Every preprocessed .c file is compiled into its own object file containing machine code that is still not ready to be executed yet during the compilation process. Finally, the linking step links all the object files into one executable and matches up function calls with their definitions, ensuring that there exists only one definition for every function called.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',3,2,'2025-06-27_20-07-20','Using #include on a source file can be a bad idea because a variable or function may have duplicate definitions in each instance the .c file was included in a source file which will cause issues during the linking process, prohibiting the code from being compiled into an executable. This is why functions and variables used across different .c files should be declared in a header file which can then be included in the source files. Additionally, including .c files using the #include directive causes the whole file’s content to be copied into the other source file during preprocessing, which can cause problems during compilation since the compiler will attempt to compile a very large file at once.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',3,2,'2025-06-27_21-17-16','Using #include on a source file can be a bad idea because a variable or function may have duplicate definitions in each instance the .c file was included in a source file which will cause issues during the linking process, prohibiting the code from being compiled into an executable. This is why functions and variables used across different .c files should be declared in a header file which can then be included in the source files. Additionally, including .c files using the #include directive causes the whole file’s content to be copied into the other source file during preprocessing, which can cause problems during compilation since the compiler will attempt to compile a very large file at once.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',3,3,'2025-06-27_20-07-20','The kernel needs to be initialized, by finding the MSP’s initial value, ensuring that there is enough memory space to allocate a thread stack, and performing the required calculations with the initial MSP value to set the address of the thread’s stack pointer.\nThe user needs to write the thread function to describe what task they want the thread to perform when it is started.\nThe user calls a function to create the thread using the information obtained during kernel initialization. The thread’s context (registers information) is pushed onto the thread stack, ensuring that the thread function is set to be popped into the PC register.\nCall a function to start the kernel. The function will trigger a system call with a case in SVC Main handler that starts the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',3,3,'2025-06-27_21-17-16','The kernel needs to be initialized, by finding the MSP’s initial value, ensuring that there is enough memory space to allocate a thread stack, and performing the required calculations with the initial MSP value to set the address of the thread’s stack pointer.\nThe user needs to write the thread function to describe what task they want the thread to perform when it is started.\nThe user calls a function to create the thread using the information obtained during kernel initialization. The thread’s context (registers information) is pushed onto the thread stack, ensuring that the thread function is set to be popped into the PC register.\nCall a function to start the kernel. The function will trigger a system call with a case in SVC Main handler that starts the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',3,4,'2025-06-27_20-07-20','The stack pool is an area in the memory allocated for the different stacks used by the system, including the operating system’s kernel stack as well as all the thread stacks created by the user. When a thread is created it will be assigned its own fixed-size block of memory from the stack pool to store its variables, functions, and register values for context switching. It will also have a thread stack pointer pointing the beginning address of its stack. This can be useful for future labs with multiple threads because each thread will have its own stack of fixed size that contains all the data it needs, so the threads will not interfere with each other and each thread’s context is stored individually, making switching between threads safer.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',3,4,'2025-06-27_21-17-16','The stack pool is an area in the memory allocated for the different stacks used by the system, including the operating system’s kernel stack as well as all the thread stacks created by the user. When a thread is created it will be assigned its own fixed-size block of memory from the stack pool to store its variables, functions, and register values for context switching. It will also have a thread stack pointer pointing the beginning address of its stack. This can be useful for future labs with multiple threads because each thread will have its own stack of fixed size that contains all the data it needs, so the threads will not interfere with each other and each thread’s context is stored individually, making switching between threads safer.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',3,5,'2025-06-27_20-07-20','The user can only access files related to the application and the kernel can access privileged files that the user cannot interfere with. The privileged files typically include operating system information such as memory allocation details, hardware configuration, thread creation, and system call and interrupt protocols. If the user requires any operation that can only be accessed by the kernel it will use a system call to have the operating system access the kernel and perform the operation. This distinction between user and kernel is important because it allows the user to only deal with application logic without having to worry about any low-level operations since the kernel will handle them in the background. This divide also keeps the system safe and modular since the user cannot interfere with the operating system.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',3,5,'2025-06-27_21-17-16','The user can only access files related to the application while the kernel can access privileged files that control low-level system functions, such as hardware management, memory allocation, interrupt handling, and system call processing. Although users are responsible for writing application logic and writing user threads, they must rely on the kernel to perform privileged operations. This happens through system calls which are specific functions or APIs provided by the operating system that serve as a controlled entrance into the kernel.\nThis distinction between user and kernel is important because it increases system security and stability by preventing direct access to the kernel, which ensures that users cannot directly manipulate harder or interrupt important operations. Instead, the kernel will handle these processes in the background in a safer manner by responding to system calls and communicating with peripheral devices.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',4,1,'2025-07-28_20-03-09','In round-robin, each task gets in a cyclic order, which is something more intuitive to achieve using a queue due to its FIFO operation. Arrays require manual index tracking and shifting elements after each operation, while queues allow easy enqueue and dequeue operations, making scheduling more efficient and the code simpler. Queues also handle dynamic process management more effectively, allowing easy insertion and removal of tasks. Overall, using a queue aligns more naturally with the round-robin approach and improves scalability and performance.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',4,1,'2025-07-28_20-34-25','Round-robin schedulers already mimic queue-like behavior using a circular array with a pointer that moves through each thread’s TCB in turn. Therefore, it is more intuitive to implement round-robin scheduling using a queue. Arrays require manual index tracking and shifting elements after each operation, while queues allow easy enqueue and dequeue operations, making scheduling more efficient and the code simpler. Queues also handle dynamic process management more effectively, allowing easy insertion and removal of tasks, though dynamic queues come with additional overhead in terms of memory allocation, pointer dereferencing, and potential fragmentation. Overall, using a queue aligns more naturally with the round-robin approach and improves scalability and performance.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',4,2,'2025-07-28_20-03-09','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the current thread\'s CPU state onto its stack and storing the current PSP in its TCB. It then calls osSched to select the next thread to run. The PSP and saved registers of the selected thread are restored from its TCB and stack, allowing it to resume execution from where it left off.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',4,2,'2025-07-28_20-34-25','The PendSV_Handler begins a context switch by reading the PSP, which points to the current thread’s stack. It then pushes registers R4–R11 onto that stack to save the thread’s CPU context, as these are not automatically saved by the hardware during exceptions. After saving the context, the handler stores the updated PSP value into the current thread’s TCB. It then calls osSched to select the next thread to run. The handler retrieves the PSP from the new thread’s TCB and restores registers R4–R11 from that thread’s stack. Finally, it updates the PSP register and exits the handler, allowing the new thread to resume execution where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',4,3,'2025-07-28_20-03-09','Modifying the OS scheduler to always choose the task with the highest priority can lead to starvation since lower-priority tasks may never get CPU time if higher-priority tasks are always available. This breaks fairness and can cause critical background tasks to stall degrading overall system performance and responsiveness.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',4,3,'2025-07-28_20-34-25','Modifying the scheduler to always run the highest-priority task means that if one task has a higher priority than all others, it will always be selected and may run indefinitely. This leads to starvation of lower-priority tasks because the scheduler is designed to always favor the top priority without giving others a chance This breaks fairness and can cause critical background tasks to stall, degrading overall system performance and responsiveness. To avoid this, a better approach would be to implement priority-based scheduling with aging, where waiting tasks gradually increase in priority, ensuring that all tasks eventually get CPU time. The trade-off is added complexity and slightly less predictability for high-priority task execution.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',4,4,'2025-07-28_20-03-09','Calling C functions provides significant benefits in terms of readability, maintainability, and development speed. C code is easier to write, debug, and understand compared to assembly, especially for complex logic such as scheduling or system call handling. The downsides of calling C from assembly introduces function call overhead, such as restoring registers and stack management, which can slightly reduce performance. Additionally, writing everything in assembly allows precise control over performance and hardware, which can be critical in real-time or resource-constrained systems.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',4,4,'2025-07-28_20-34-25','Calling C functions provides significant benefits in terms of readability, maintainability, and development speed. C code is easier to write, debug, and understand compared to assembly, especially for complex logic such as scheduling or system call handling. The downsides of calling C from assembly introduces function call overhead, such as restoring registers and stack management, which can slightly reduce performance. Additionally, writing everything in assembly allows precise control over performance and hardware, which can be critical in real-time or resource-constrained systems.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',4,5,'2025-07-28_20-03-09','If only a single thread is created, the OS will still function. The OS\'s core mechanisms like context switching, the scheduler, and interrupt handling will still operate correctly, but with only one thread in the system, there’s no other thread to switch to when the current thread yields. As a result, when print_continuously yields, the scheduler will be invoked, but it will simply return control to the same thread, since it’s the only task. This creates the appearance of continuous execution, but without actual thread switching between different tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',4,5,'2025-07-28_20-34-25','If only a single thread is created, the OS will still function. The OS\'s core mechanisms like context switching, the scheduler, and interrupt handling will still operate correctly, but with only one thread in the system, there’s no other thread to switch to when the current thread yields. As a result, when print_continuously yields, the scheduler will be invoked, but it will simply return control to the same thread, since it’s the only task. This creates the appearance of continuous execution, but without actual thread switching between different tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,1,'2025-07-28_13-49-36','The thread function is altered so that its input argument is a void pointer and the void pointer is dereferenced within the function to be used. The user must enter the value needed as a variable and then a pointer to that variable is passed into the thread creation function as an argument. Within the thread creation function, register R0 is assigned the pointer to the variable. When the thread is run the thread function will have the contents of R0 as its argument, which would be the value the user set at the start.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,1,'2025-07-28_15-16-46','The thread function is altered so that its input argument is a void pointer and the void pointer is dereferenced within the function to be used. The user must enter the value needed as a variable and then a pointer to that variable is passed into the thread creation function as an argument. Within the thread creation function, register R0 is assigned the pointer to the variable. When the thread is run the thread function will have the contents of R0 as its argument, which would be the value the user set at the start.\nTo pass an argument into a thread, the user first prepares the data and passes a pointer to the thread as a void* argument when creating the thread. The operating system then allocates a new stack for the thread and initializes its CPU context according to the system’s ABI. This includes setting the appropriate register (R0) with the pointer value so that when the thread starts, the thread function receives the argument in the expected register. The stack setup ensures the thread has its own private execution space and a correctly configured stack frame, enabling the function to safely access the argument. This cooperation between the user, OS, and ABI guarantees that the argument is correctly passed and accessible when the thread begins execution.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,1,'2025-07-28_15-33-27','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first prepares the data and passes a pointer to the thread as a void* argument when creating the thread. The operating system then allocates a new stack for the thread and initializes its CPU context according to the system’s ABI. This includes setting the appropriate register (R0) with the pointer value so that when the thread starts, the thread function receives the argument in the expected register. The stack setup ensures the thread has its own private execution space and a correctly configured stack frame, enabling the function to safely access the argument. This cooperation between the user, OS, and ABI guarantees that the argument is correctly passed and accessible when the thread begins execution.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,2,'2025-07-28_13-49-36','osYield cannot be called in the context switching functions because osYield triggers a system call that would go to the SVC handler. Having osYield in SVC Handler would just trigger an infinite loop and cause the program to hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,2,'2025-07-28_15-16-46','osYield cannot be called within context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because osYield triggers an SVC system call, which would attempt to re-enter the SVC handler while it’s already active. This causes nested interrupts that lead to an infinite loop and eventually a hardfault. More importantly, nested interrupts without proper handling can corrupt the system stack because each interrupt saves its context on the stack. If the SVC handler is re-entered before the previous context is fully restored, the stack can become inconsistent or overflow. This stack corruption disrupts the CPU’s ability to return correctly from interrupts, leading to undefined behavior and hardfaults. Therefore, calling osYield inside these handlers is unsafe as it breaks the proper interrupt and context management required for reliable thread switching.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,2,'2025-07-28_15-33-27','osYield cannot be called within context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because osYield triggers an SVC system call, which would attempt to re-enter the SVC handler while it’s already active. This causes nested interrupts that lead to an infinite loop and eventually a hardfault. More importantly, nested interrupts without proper handling can corrupt the system stack because each interrupt saves its context on the stack. If the SVC handler is re-entered before the previous context is fully restored, the stack can become inconsistent or overflow. This stack corruption disrupts the CPU’s ability to return correctly from interrupts, leading to undefined behavior and hardfaults. Therefore, calling osYield inside these handlers is unsafe as it breaks the proper interrupt and context management required for reliable thread switching.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,3,'2025-07-28_13-49-36','The hardware used to run the timer for the OS clock might not be able to keep up with the speed it is set to so it will not be able to respond at the required speed. Additionally, it might miss some of the code due to the resolution.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,3,'2025-07-28_15-16-46','Reducing the OS clock increases the timer resolution, which allows the system to perform more frequent context switches and finer-grained scheduling. This can improve system responsiveness and timing precision for real-time tasks that require quick reaction times. However, the downside is that the hardware timer and CPU must handle interrupts ten times more frequently, which increases CPU overhead and can reduce overall system efficiency. The higher interrupt rate may also cause the timer hardware or interrupt controller to miss interrupts if it cannot keep up, leading to missed ticks. Additionally, more frequent interrupts increase the risk of interrupt latency and stack usage, potentially causing timing anomalies or even system instability if not managed carefully.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,3,'2025-07-28_15-33-27','Reducing the OS clock increases the timer resolution, which allows the system to perform more frequent context switches and finer-grained scheduling. This can improve system responsiveness and timing precision for real-time tasks that require quick reaction times. However, the downside is that the hardware timer and CPU must handle interrupts ten times more frequently, which increases CPU overhead and can reduce overall system efficiency. The higher interrupt rate may also cause the timer hardware or interrupt controller to miss interrupts if it cannot keep up, leading to missed ticks. Additionally, more frequent interrupts increase the risk of interrupt latency and stack usage, potentially causing timing anomalies or even system instability if not managed carefully.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,4,'2025-07-28_13-49-36','Sure!\nFrom the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking can feel unresponsive or freeze if a program doesn’t voluntarily give up control, while pre-emptive multitasking feels smooth because the OS forcibly interrupts tasks to share the CPU fairly. For the OS, co-operative multitasking means it relies on programs to yield control themselves, making it simpler but less reliable, whereas pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to use timer interrupts to take control back and switch tasks, ensuring better responsiveness and stability.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,4,'2025-07-28_15-16-46','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking can feel unresponsive or freeze if a program doesn’t voluntarily give up control, while pre-emptive multitasking feels smooth because the OS forcibly interrupts tasks to share the CPU fairly. Co-operative multitasking means it relies on programs to yield control themselves, making it simpler but less reliable, whereas pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to use timer interrupts to take control back and switch tasks, ensuring better responsiveness and stability.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,4,'2025-07-28_15-33-27','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking can feel unresponsive or freeze if a program doesn’t voluntarily give up control, while pre-emptive multitasking feels smooth because the OS forcibly interrupts tasks to share the CPU fairly. Co-operative multitasking means it relies on programs to yield control themselves, making it simpler but less reliable, whereas pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to use timer interrupts to take control back and switch tasks, ensuring better responsiveness and stability.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,5,'2025-07-28_13-49-36','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching threads can work together since both methods will call pendSV to trigger a context switch, the only difference is the criteria required to be met to trigger it. Yielding threads will only call pendSV for context switching when they have completed their task whereas pre-emptive threads will call pendSV when their time has ran out regardless of if the task was completed.\n',0,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,5,'2025-07-28_15-16-46','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching can work together since both methods use the same PendSV handler to perform the context switch. The difference is that what triggers the context switch is different in either mechanism. Yielding occurs when a thread voluntarily signals it is ready to give up the CPU, while pre-emptive switching happens when a timer interrupt signals that the thread’s time slice has run out. The PendSV handler contains unified code that saves and restores thread contexts in both cases. This reuse of the PendSV handler’s logic ensures consistent and efficient context switching regardless of whether the switch was voluntary or forced by the scheduler, allowing the OS to seamlessly combine cooperative and pre-emptive multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('aelwasil',5,5,'2025-07-28_15-33-27','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching can work together since both methods use the same PendSV handler to perform the context switch. The difference is that what triggers the context switch is different in either mechanism. Yielding occurs when a thread voluntarily signals it is ready to give up the CPU, while pre-emptive switching happens when a timer interrupt signals that the thread’s time slice has run out. The PendSV handler contains unified code that saves and restores thread contexts in both cases. This reuse of the PendSV handler’s logic ensures consistent and efficient context switching regardless of whether the switch was voluntary or forced by the scheduler, allowing the OS to seamlessly combine cooperative and pre-emptive multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,1,'2025-06-25_15-10-35','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a software library that allows for communication between written code and the STM32’s hardware. This library written in C allows developers to easily call functions for specific embedded hardware like timers, GPIO, etc. without having to write extensive scripts to access them. As such, its easy accessibility is why it is used so often for general embedded projects.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,1,'2025-06-25_15-56-30','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a software library that allows for communication between written code and the STM32’s hardware. This library written in C allows developers to easily call functions for specific embedded hardware like timers, GPIO, etc. without having to write extensive scripts to access them. As such, its easy accessibility is why it is used so often for general embedded projects.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,1,'2025-06-25_16-48-12','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a software library that allows for communication between written code and the STM32’s hardware. This library written in C allows developers to easily call functions for specific embedded hardware like timers, GPIO, etc. without having to write extensive scripts to access them. As such, its easy accessibility is why it is used so often for general embedded projects.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,1,'2025-06-25_19-04-54','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a software library that allows for communication between written code and the STM32’s hardware. This library written in C allows developers to easily call functions for specific embedded hardware like timers, GPIO, etc. without having to write extensive scripts to access them. As such, its easy accessibility is why it is used so often for general embedded projects.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,2,'2025-06-25_15-10-35','In typical C programming, printing functions is done with the “printf” function. However, accomplishing this in an embedded system like the STM32 requires a helper function to complete this. By using __io_putchar, every time printf is used, this helper function will be called and repeatedly print out singular characters until it has printed the required statement.\nDepending on the peripherals that are available on the board, the output may vary from outputting on a COM port to a debugger, etc. This is important to note because changes to the __io_putchar function will be required in order to ensure that the output is using the right hardware and appearing at the right destination.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,2,'2025-06-25_15-56-30','In typical C programming, printing functions is done with the “printf” function. However, accomplishing this in an embedded system like the STM32 requires a helper function to complete this. By using __io_putchar, every time printf is used, this helper function will be called and repeatedly print out singular characters until it has printed the required statement.\nDepending on the peripherals that are available on the board, the output may vary from outputting on a COM port to a debugger, etc. This is important to note because changes to the __io_putchar function will be required in order to ensure that the output is using the right hardware and appearing at the right destination.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,2,'2025-06-25_16-48-12','In typical C programming, printing functions is done with the “printf” function. However, accomplishing this in an embedded system like the STM32 requires a helper function to complete this. By using __io_putchar, every time printf is used, this helper function will be called and repeatedly print out singular characters until it has printed the required statement.\nDepending on the peripherals that are available on the board, the output may vary from outputting on a COM port to a debugger, etc. This is important to note because changes to the __io_putchar function will be required in order to ensure that the output is using the right hardware and appearing at the right destination.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,2,'2025-06-25_19-04-54','In typical C programming, printing functions is done with the “printf” function. However, accomplishing this in an embedded system like the STM32 requires a helper function to complete this. By using __io_putchar, every time printf is used, this helper function will be called and repeatedly print out singular characters until it has printed the required statement.\nDepending on the peripherals that are available on the board, the output may vary from outputting on a COM port to a debugger, etc. This is important to note because changes to the __io_putchar function will be required in order to ensure that the output is using the right hardware and appearing at the right destination.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,3,'2025-06-25_15-10-35','In order to complete this lab, we had to use the debugger in order to inspect the register values of the Cortex M4 to ensure that the proper values including MSP, PSP, etc. were being correctly set. Examining these register values provides insight at every step of the code, allowing developers to step through the code and monitor what is happening in the background at any desired point. Moreover, no additional code is required in order to inspect these states, only requiring breakpoints at your desired states for monitoring.\nThis is also why ‘printf’ is so limited in the context of embedded systems, because the function cannot necessarily print what it cannot access - such as register values and other peripherals. In this case, it is much more convenient to use debugging.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,3,'2025-06-25_15-56-30','In order to complete this lab, we had to use the debugger in order to inspect the register values of the Cortex M4 to ensure that the proper values including MSP, PSP, etc. were being correctly set. Examining these register values provides insight at every step of the code, allowing developers to step through the code and monitor what is happening in the background at any desired point. Moreover, no additional code is required in order to inspect these states, only requiring breakpoints at your desired states for monitoring.\nThis is also why ‘printf’ is so limited in the context of embedded systems, because the function cannot necessarily print what it cannot access - such as register values and other peripherals. In this case, it is much more convenient to use debugging.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,3,'2025-06-25_16-48-12','In order to complete this lab, we had to use the debugger in order to inspect the register values of the Cortex M4 to ensure that the proper values including MSP, PSP, etc. were being correctly set. Examining these register values provides insight at every step of the code, allowing developers to step through the code and monitor what is happening in the background at any desired point. Moreover, no additional code is required in order to inspect these states, only requiring breakpoints at your desired states for monitoring.\nThis is also why ‘printf’ is so limited in the context of embedded systems, because the function cannot necessarily print what it cannot access - such as register values and other peripherals. In this case, it is much more convenient to use debugging.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,3,'2025-06-25_19-04-54','In order to complete this lab, we had to use the debugger in order to inspect the register values of the Cortex M4 to ensure that the proper values including MSP, PSP, etc. were being correctly set. Examining these register values provides insight at every step of the code, allowing developers to step through the code and monitor what is happening in the background at any desired point. Moreover, no additional code is required in order to inspect these states, only requiring breakpoints at your desired states for monitoring.\nThis is also why ‘printf’ is so limited in the context of embedded systems, because the function cannot necessarily print what it cannot access - such as register values and other peripherals. In this case, it is much more convenient to use debugging.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,4,'2025-06-25_15-10-35','In the Cortex-M4, stack allocation must be done manually by the user. By design, stacks grow downwards in code, and will start at the already obtained MSP_init_val. This value is the original value of the Main Stack Pointer once the system starts up and tells you where the main stack starts. All following thread stacks will use this MSP_init_val as a reference point for later use.\nIn order to find the PSP_val of the stack, it is important to keep in mind that the stack grows downwards, therefore the starting address of the Process Stack Pointer will be less than that of the MSP. To obtain this value, we thus need to subtract the required size of the main stack (in this case 0x400) from the initial value of our MSP. The equation:\nPSP_val = MSP_init_val – offset\nmust be used in order to determine our PSP_val. Without any initial location for MSP, the PSP_val does not have a reference point and thus, will not provide an accurate address for the processor.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,4,'2025-06-25_15-56-30','In the Cortex-M4, stack allocation must be done manually by the user. By design, stacks grow downwards in code, and will start at the already obtained MSP_init_val. This value is the original value of the Main Stack Pointer once the system starts up and tells you where the main stack starts. All following thread stacks will use this MSP_init_val as a reference point for later use.\nIn order to find the PSP_val of the stack, it is important to keep in mind that the stack grows downwards, therefore the starting address of the Process Stack Pointer will be less than that of the MSP. To obtain this value, we thus need to subtract the required size of the main stack (in this case 0x400) from the initial value of our MSP. The equation:\nPSP_val = MSP_init_val – offset\nmust be used in order to determine our PSP_val. Without any initial location for MSP, the PSP_val does not have a reference point and thus, will not provide an accurate address for the processor.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,4,'2025-06-25_16-48-12','In the Cortex-M4, stack allocation must be done manually by the user. By design, stacks grow downwards in code, and will start at the already obtained MSP_init_val. This value is the original value of the Main Stack Pointer once the system starts up and tells you where the main stack starts. All following thread stacks will use this MSP_init_val as a reference point for later use.\nIn order to find the PSP_val of the stack, it is important to keep in mind that the stack grows downwards, therefore the starting address of the Process Stack Pointer will be less than that of the MSP. To obtain this value, we thus need to subtract the required size of the main stack (in this case 0x400) from the initial value of our MSP. The equation:\nPSP_val = MSP_init_val – offset\nmust be used in order to determine our PSP_val. Without any initial location for MSP, the PSP_val does not have a reference point and thus, will not provide an accurate address for the processor.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',1,4,'2025-06-25_19-04-54','In the Cortex-M4, stack allocation must be done manually by the user. By design, stacks grow downwards in code, and will start at the already obtained MSP_init_val. At system startup, the processor loads the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) from the vector table — this value (MSP_init_val) tells us where the main stack begins in memory. All following thread stacks will use this MSP_init_val as a reference point for later use.\nIn order to find the PSP_val of the stack, it is important to keep in mind that the stack grows downwards, therefore the starting address of the Process Stack Pointer will be less than that of the MSP. To obtain this value, we thus need to subtract the required size of the main stack (in this case 0x400) from the initial value of our MSP. The equation:\nPSP_val = MSP_init_val – offset\nmust be used in order to determine our PSP_val. By using this, it ensures that the PSP is placed in a valid RAM region and there is no collision between the main stack and this thread stack.\nWithout any initial location for MSP, the PSP_val does not have a reference point and thus, will not provide an accurate address for the processor. Moreover, if there is no certainty in the initial MSP value, then there is the possibility of invalid memory access and later errors down the road when calculating future PSP values.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,5,'2025-06-25_15-10-35','If we wanted to allocate multiple new stacks, we would have to define a static size of memory that dictates the size of each new stack. This could be stored in an array for example, and implemented in an equation similar to the PSP calculation in order to determine the initial value for each new stack. Mathematically, this would include subtracting each stack size from the most previously calculated initial value until an init_val for each stack has been obtained. In order to ensure validity of each address, ensure that the stack’s address exists between the capacity of the stack’s total size and the MSP_init_val.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',1,5,'2025-06-25_15-56-30','If we wanted to allocate multiple new stacks, we would have to define a static size of memory that dictates the size (block of memory) of each new stack. This could be stored in an array for example and implemented in an equation similar to the PSP calculation in order to determine the initial value for each new stack. It would follow the equation:\nPSP(n) = base_address + (n+1) x size_of_stack\nWhere the base_address is the starting address of the thread stacks, size_of_stack is the fixed size of each thread, and n is the thread index.\nBy using this approach where we are always using the same reference point as defined at compile-time, it ensures that the stack is placed without overlap and does not contain any holes in between one another. In the Cortex M-4, you can further ensure each stack address is valid by ensuring each PSP value is 8-byte aligned (ensure the stack size is a multiple of 8 or use aligned(8)) and that the total region occupied by all stacks is less than the maximum total stack size, therefore fitting within available RAM. It is also important to ensure that the allocated thread region of memory does not overlap with the main stack.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',1,5,'2025-06-25_16-48-12','If we wanted to allocate multiple new stacks, we would have to define a static size of memory that dictates the size (block of memory) of each new stack. This could be stored in an array for example and implemented in an equation similar to the PSP calculation to determine the initial value for each new stack. Since the Cortex-M4 stack grows downward (from higher to lower memory addresses), the calculation of each thread’s initial stack pointer needs to account for this.\nPSP(n) = base_address - ((n+1) x size_of_stack)\nWhere the base_address is the highest address (starting address) of the thread stacks, size_of_stack is the fixed size of each thread, and n is the thread index. This downward calculation prevents placing stacks at higher addresses which, in a system with limited memory, could cause overlap or memory corruption as stacks grow downward. Starting from a high address and moving downward for each stack ensures that stacks do not overlap and respects the downward growth behavior of the Cortex-M4 stack.\nBy using this approach where we are also always using the same reference point as defined at compile-time, it ensures that the stack is contiguous. In the Cortex M-4, you can further ensure each stack address is valid by ensuring each PSP value is 8-byte aligned (ensure the stack size is a multiple of 8 or use __attribute__(aligned(8))) and that the total region occupied by all stacks fits within available RAM (where base_address – (num_threads x size_of_stack)). It is also important to ensure that the allocated thread region of memory does not overlap with the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',1,5,'2025-06-25_19-04-54','If we wanted to allocate multiple new stacks, we would have to define a static size of memory that dictates the size (block of memory) of each new stack. This could be stored in an array for example and implemented in an equation similar to the PSP calculation to determine the initial value for each new stack. Since the Cortex-M4 stack grows downward (from higher to lower memory addresses), the calculation of each thread’s initial stack pointer needs to account for this.\nPSP(n) = base_address - ((n+1) x size_of_stack)\nWhere the base_address is the highest address (starting address) of the thread stacks, size_of_stack is the fixed size of each thread, and n is the thread index. This downward calculation prevents placing stacks at higher addresses which, in a system with limited memory, could cause overlap or memory corruption as stacks grow downward. Starting from a high address and moving downward for each stack ensures that stacks do not overlap and respects the downward growth behavior of the Cortex-M4 stack.\nBy using this approach where we are also always using the same reference point as defined at compile-time, it ensures that the stack is contiguous. In the Cortex M-4, you can further ensure each stack address is valid by ensuring each PSP value is 8-byte aligned (ensure the stack size is a multiple of 8 or use __attribute__(aligned(8))) and that the total region occupied by all stacks fits within available RAM (where base_address – (num_threads x size_of_stack)). It is also important to ensure that the allocated thread region of memory does not overlap with the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',2,1,'2025-06-25_19-20-10','When I changed the PC to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor directly jumped to that function’s address. This is because of the assembly, which takes an argument, stores it in r0, and moves it to the PC register. This then causes the immediate execution of the print_continuously function, outputting messages in the Cortex-M4. By doing this, we have bypassed many steps just by manipulating the PC.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',2,1,'2025-06-26_16-51-16','When I changed the PC to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor directly jumped to that function’s address. This is because of the assembly, which takes an argument, stores it in r0, and moves it to the PC register. This then causes the immediate execution of the print_continuously function, outputting messages in the Cortex-M4. By doing this, we have bypassed many steps just by manipulating the PC.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',2,1,'2025-06-26_20-04-19','When I changed the PC to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to that location and began executing it. On the ARM Cortex-M4, this is typically done using assembly instructions like LDR r0 = print_continuously to load the function’s address into r0, followed by BX r0 to branch to it. Changing the PC in this way is effectively the same as making a function call because it transfers control directly to the function’s code. However, unlike the normal function call instruction Branch with Link, this method bypasses storing a return address, which means it doesn’t expect the function to return. By doing this, we gain low-level control over execution flow, which is useful for starting threads that are designed to run continuously without returning, as demonstrated in this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',2,2,'2025-06-25_19-20-10','A system call is a way for a computer program to make a request to the OS. It serves as the bridge between user and kernel mode, as approval of a system call typically involves handing over privileged mode to the required program. Examples of system calls include fork(), exit(), etc.\nIn the Cortex-M4, the SVC instruction is what triggers system calls. Dictated by an SVC_handler, exceptions are typically passed, checked for whether or not it matches a case in the handler, and performs its respective action. By executing system calls like this, it ensures a level of security while also ensuring there is a reaction for every exception.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',2,2,'2025-06-26_16-51-16','A system call is a function call made by a user program to request a service from the operating system kernel. It acts as a controlled interface through which user code asks the kernel to perform privileged operations on its behalf, such as managing processes, accessing hardware, or handling I/O.\nIn the Cortex-M4 microcontroller, system calls are triggered by executing the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction. When the user code executes this instruction, it causes an exception that transfers control to the SVC handler within the kernel. The handler then examines the requested service, performs the necessary privileged actions, and returns control back to the user program. This mechanism ensures that user applications can safely request kernel services while maintaining system security and stability.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',2,2,'2025-06-26_20-04-19','A system call is a function call made by a user program to request a service from the operating system kernel. It acts as a controlled interface through which user code asks the kernel to perform privileged operations on its behalf, such as managing processes, accessing hardware, or handling I/O.\nIn the Cortex-M4 microcontroller, system calls are triggered by executing the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction. When the user code executes this instruction, it causes an exception that transfers control to the SVC handler within the kernel. The handler then examines the requested service, performs the necessary privileged actions, and returns control back to the user program. This mechanism ensures that user applications can safely request kernel services while maintaining system security and stability.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',2,3,'2025-06-25_19-20-10','“Setting up a thread’s stack” simply means to prepare a stack identical to what the CPU is expecting, giving it the illusion that the thread was always running, even if this is not necessarily true. To do this, we must manually push register values onto our thread’s stack in a very specific order. This includes registers like the xPSR, program counter, and general-purpose registers r0-r12. Not only are these registers required in our thread’s stack so that the CPU can run as expected, but it also must simply continue storing temporary data like variables, addresses, etc. for our programs to run.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',2,3,'2025-06-26_16-51-16','“Setting up a thread’s stack” simply means to prepare a stack identical to what the CPU is expecting, giving it the illusion that the thread was always running, even if this is not necessarily true. To do this, we must manually push register values onto our thread’s stack in a very specific order. This includes registers like the xPSR, program counter, and general-purpose registers r0-r12. Not only are these registers required in our thread’s stack so that the CPU can run as expected, but it also must simply continue storing temporary data like variables, addresses, etc. for our programs to run.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',2,3,'2025-06-26_20-04-19','“Setting up a thread’s stack” simply means preparing a dedicated region of memory to hold the thread’s saved CPU state so that it can start or resume execution correctly. Specifically, the stack is initialized to mimic the exact register values the CPU expects during an exception return, including registers like the xPSR, program counter (PC), link register (LR), and general-purpose registers (r0–r12). These register values are pushed onto the stack in the proper order to create a context frame.\nStoring these register values on the stack is essential because, during a context switch, the CPU uses them to restore the thread’s execution context exactly as it was when the thread was last paused. Without this, the thread would not be able to resume correctly and would likely cause faults or unpredictable behavior.\nIt is also critical that this stack is allocated in a valid, reserved memory area. If the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) points to an invalid or unallocated memory region, attempts to access the stack will cause a hard fault.\nWhile the stack also holds temporary data such as local variables and return addresses during normal function execution, the primary role during thread setup is to store these saved register values to maintain the thread’s state. Keeping the setup of the saved context distinct from general stack use ensures system stability and proper thread management.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',2,4,'2025-06-25_19-20-10','When we set the PSP using __set_PSP, we are telling the CPU where the thread’s stack begins. Thus, if we were to not set the PSP, the value may point to some invalid/unused address. This presents several issues because the PSP_init_val is the reference point used to determine the addresses of the rest of the registers. Without a valid base address, the CPU may try to access illegal addresses and memory, thus triggering a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',2,4,'2025-06-26_16-51-16','The Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is the CPU’s default stack pointer used during system startup and for handling exceptions, while the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is specifically used for thread execution to manage each thread’s individual stack.\nWhen we set the PSP using the __set_PSP function, we inform the CPU of the correct starting address of the current thread’s stack. If we fail to set the PSP before running the thread, the CPU will attempt to use an invalid or uninitialized stack pointer. This causes the thread’s stack operations—like pushing and popping registers—to access illegal memory locations. As a result, the CPU triggers a hard fault due to the invalid memory access, causing the program to crash.\nTherefore, explicitly setting the PSP is essential to ensure each thread has a valid stack context to operate safely and correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',2,4,'2025-06-26_20-04-19','The Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is the CPU’s default stack pointer used during system startup and for handling exceptions, while the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is specifically used for thread execution to manage each thread’s individual stack.\nWhen we set the PSP using the __set_PSP function, we inform the CPU of the correct starting address of the current thread’s stack. If we fail to set the PSP before running the thread, the CPU will attempt to use an invalid or uninitialized stack pointer. This causes the thread’s stack operations—like pushing and popping registers—to access illegal memory locations. As a result, the CPU triggers a hard fault due to the invalid memory access, causing the program to crash.\nTherefore, explicitly setting the PSP is essential to ensure each thread has a valid stack context to operate safely and correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',2,5,'2025-06-25_19-20-10','If our thread function did not loop continuously (and for example, just printed our statement once and exited the function), we would eventually use our link register value, which is responsible for storing all return addresses. However for this lab, all of our register values were set to an arbitrary dummy value. Therefore, when the CPU attempts to access this invalid address, it will most likely result in a hard fault. Overall this would also result in a crash of the thread, assuming there is no other precautions taken to ensure its expected termination.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',2,5,'2025-06-26_16-51-16','In the Cortex-M4, when a thread function executes, its return address is not stored on the thread’s stack (managed by the Process Stack Pointer, PSP). Instead, return addresses for exception handlers are saved on the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), used in handler mode.\nIf the thread function simply returned after printing once, the CPU would try to use the link register (LR) to determine where to return. However, since this thread’s registers—including the LR—were initialized with arbitrary dummy values (because no valid return address was set), the CPU would attempt to jump to an invalid memory location.\nThis mismatch between the expected return address and the actual dummy LR value causes the CPU to generate a hard fault, crashing the thread. This happens because threads do not have a valid return address saved on their stack to safely return to, unlike handler mode, which uses MSP for storing return addresses during exceptions.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',2,5,'2025-06-26_20-04-19','In the Cortex-M4, when a thread function executes, its return address is not stored on the thread’s stack (managed by the Process Stack Pointer, PSP). Instead, return addresses for exception handlers are saved on the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), used in handler mode.\nIf the thread function simply returned after printing once, the CPU would try to use the link register (LR) to determine where to return. However, since this thread’s registers—including the LR—were initialized with arbitrary dummy values (because no valid return address was set), the CPU would attempt to jump to an invalid memory location.\nThis mismatch between the expected return address and the actual dummy LR value causes the CPU to generate a hard fault, crashing the thread. This happens because threads do not have a valid return address saved on their stack to safely return to, unlike handler mode, which uses MSP for storing return addresses during exceptions.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',3,1,'2025-06-26_16-11-27','In order to compile multiple .c files into a single executable, there are 4 steps in the process; preprocessing, compilation, linking, and output. In preprocessing, each of the .c files get processed individually. This step of the stage handles directives like #include, #define, etc.\nAfter this, compilation takes place, where each.c file is compiled into their respective object files (eg. main.c becomes main.o). The next step of linking is what combines all of these object files into a single executable. The linker assesses all references between files, links all necessary requirements for each file, and sets up the final memory layout for the code.\nFrom here, an output is provided in the form of a single executable file that can be loaded and run by the system.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',3,1,'2025-06-26_18-12-44','To compile multiple .c files into a single executable, the process involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking.\nIn the preprocessing stage, each .c file is processed individually to handle directives such as #include, #define, and conditional compilation. This step expands macros and inserts the contents of included header files, preparing the code for compilation.\nDuring the compilation stage, each preprocessed .c file is translated into a corresponding object file (e.g., main.c becomes main.o). These object files contain machine code but are still separate and not yet combined into a working program.\nIn the linking stage, all the generated object files are combined into a single executable. The linker resolves references between files—such as function calls and shared global variables—and arranges the code and data into the final memory layout. The result of this stage is a single executable file that can be loaded and run by the system.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',3,2,'2025-06-26_16-11-27','It is not a good idea to include #include directives for .c files they are meant to be compiled separately. As mentioned previously, each .c file is loaded individually before being linked later in the proves. By including a .c file, you are essentially bypassing this proper build process and not pre-processing anything in the file. Moreover, this may also cause code duplication in the early stages of compilation.\nInstead, the proper way to share declarations is to place them in a header file, include the required function prototypes, structs, etc. in the header, and #include this in all other required .c files.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',3,2,'2025-06-26_18-12-44','Including a .c file using #include (e.g. #include <kernel.c>) is a bad idea because .c files are meant to be compiled separately into object files. Each object file should represent a distinct compiled unit that the linker later combines into a single executable. When you include a .c file directly into another, its code is textually inserted into the including file before compilation. If this is done in multiple places, the same function or variable definitions are compiled multiple times, resulting in duplicate symbols across multiple object files.\nDuring the linking phase, the linker attempts to combine all object files and expects each symbol (function or global variable) to be defined only once. If the same definition appears in more than one object file—due to repeated inclusion of the same .c file—the linker cannot resolve the ambiguity and throws a multiple definition or symbol redefinition error.\nThe correct approach is to use header (.h) files to share declarations like function prototypes, structs, or constants. These headers can safely be included across multiple .c files without duplicating actual definitions, allowing the linker to assemble the final executable without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',3,3,'2025-06-26_16-11-27','From a user’s perspective, these are the following steps to set up and start a thread.\nCreate a thread function in your main.c that you want to run in your thread. This function will act as the thread’s main behaviour.\nInitialize the kernel to set up any required internal structures, set up anything else that might be needed by the thread before running (like MSP initial value), etc.\nUsing a stack allocation function, set aside memory for the thread’s stack. This includes specifying the size required for the stack, which will store local variables, return addresses, and register values.\nThen call the thread creation function. This will typically require setting up a thread struct, creating an instance of this thread, and setting up the stack for this thread with the required order or registers that the CPU expects.\nAfter all threads have been prepared, start the kernel to begin executing. This involves giving the program administrative access and allowing the scheduler to take over and run the threads based on all of the previous configurations.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',3,3,'2025-06-26_18-12-44','From a user’s perspective, these are the following steps to set up and start a thread.\nCreate a thread function in your main.c that you want to run in your thread. This function will act as the thread’s main behaviour.\nInitialize the kernel to set up any required internal structures, set up anything else that might be needed by the thread before running (like MSP initial value), etc.\nUsing a stack allocation function, set aside memory for the thread’s stack. This includes specifying the size required for the stack, which will store local variables, return addresses, and register values.\nThen call the thread creation function. This will typically require setting up a thread struct, creating an instance of this thread, and setting up the stack for this thread with the required order or registers that the CPU expects.\nAfter all threads have been prepared, start the kernel to begin executing. This involves giving the program administrative access and allowing the scheduler to take over and run the threads based on all of the previous configurations.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',3,4,'2025-06-26_16-11-27','The stack pool is essentially a pre-allocated block of memory that is set aside specifically for allocating individual stacks for each thread. It follows a last-in, first-out allocation strategy, and is best for scenarios where temporary data is frequently pushed/popped from the stack. It also manages the stack space per thread, keeping track of its own function calls, local variables, and saved register values.\nIn future labs, the stack pool will be essential for supporting multiple threads by giving each thread its own dedicated stack. When creating threads, each one will be assigned a stack from the pool, ensuring that stacks do not overlap in memory. This separation supports isolation and safety by preventing threads from interfering with one another\'s stack space, reducing the risk of memory corruption or crashes. Additionally, the stack pool allows the OS to scale with the number of threads, dynamically managing them as long as there is available stack space. It also simplifies context switching, since the OS can switch the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the correct stack in the pool whenever a thread switch occurs.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',3,4,'2025-06-26_18-12-44','The stack pool is essentially a pre-allocated block of memory that is set aside specifically for allocating individual stacks for each thread. It follows a last-in, first-out allocation strategy, and is best for scenarios where temporary data is frequently pushed/popped from the stack. It also manages the stack space per thread, keeping track of its own function calls, local variables, and saved register values.\nIn future labs, the stack pool will be essential for supporting multiple threads by giving each thread its own dedicated stack. When creating threads, each one will be assigned a stack from the pool, ensuring that stacks do not overlap in memory. This separation supports isolation and safety by preventing threads from interfering with one another\'s stack space, reducing the risk of memory corruption or crashes. Additionally, the stack pool allows the OS to scale with the number of threads, dynamically managing them as long as there is available stack space. It also simplifies context switching, since the OS can switch the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the correct stack in the pool whenever a thread switch occurs.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',3,5,'2025-06-26_16-11-27','When differentiating between the user and kernel in terms of what goes into which file, it is a matter of whether or not the code is required to run with privileged or unprivileged access. For example, user code is typically placed in files like main.c. These types of code contain very high-level application logic like thread functions, print statements, and system calls. All of this runs in unprivileged mode and has limited access to hardware in the Cortex-M4.\nOn the other hand, kernel code will be placed in files like kernel.c. This contains very low-level OS logic, which in this lab included doing things like thread creation, scheduling, system call handlers, and more. All of this runs in privileged mode because of how imperative the proper execution of these is, and there is almost full access to hardware and protected memory.\nThe distinction between user and kernel code is important because it creates a clear separation of responsibilities. User code remains focused on application-level tasks, such as what each thread is doing, without needing to manage or understand the underlying OS mechanisms. This separation also promotes safety and stability—since user code runs in unprivileged mode, it cannot accidentally corrupt kernel data or critical system resources, helping to maintain overall system reliability. Additionally, it simplifies debugging by making it easier to isolate errors in user code without digging into complex kernel logic. Finally, this modular design allows the kernel code to remain reusable and isolated, while user code can be modified freely without the risk of breaking core system functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',3,5,'2025-06-26_18-12-44','When differentiating between the user and kernel in terms of what goes into which file, it is a matter of whether or not the code is required to run with privileged or unprivileged access. For example, user code is typically placed in files like main.c. These types of code contain very high-level application logic like thread functions, print statements, and system calls. All of this runs in unprivileged mode and has limited access to hardware in the Cortex-M4.\nOn the other hand, kernel code will be placed in files like kernel.c. This contains very low-level OS logic, which in this lab included doing things like thread creation, scheduling, system call handlers, and more. All of this runs in privileged mode because of how imperative the proper execution of these is, and there is almost full access to hardware and protected memory.\nThe distinction between user and kernel code is important because it creates a clear separation of responsibilities. User code remains focused on application-level tasks, such as what each thread is doing, without needing to manage or understand the underlying OS mechanisms. This separation also promotes safety and stability—since user code runs in unprivileged mode, it cannot accidentally corrupt kernel data or critical system resources, helping to maintain overall system reliability. Additionally, it simplifies debugging by making it easier to isolate errors in user code without digging into complex kernel logic. Finally, this modular design allows the kernel code to remain reusable and isolated, while user code can be modified freely without the risk of breaking core system functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,1,'2025-07-28_16-30-28','When using round-robin scheduling with an array, each thread is stored at a specific index within. Using a global variable, the scheduler moves to the indicated index and runs the thread until it yields (or the the thread terminates) and will then move to the next index in the array, wrapping around to the beginning once it reaches the end.\nHowever, storing your TCBs in a queue is slightly different. While it still operates in a circular motion, instead of using fixed positions for each thread and iterating through, threads are instead queued and once executed, moves to the back of the queue. In this case, the scheduler will always pick the front thread to run.\nUsing a queue instead is beneficial for a few reasons. Manual indexing (which is what arrays use) can be prone to several errors and may at times include complex logic, which is eliminated when always simply running the first thread. Moreover, it is easier to dynamically manage threads — adding, removing, or reordering them — since queue operations like enqueue and dequeue naturally reflect the flow of round-robin scheduling without requiring index recalculation or slot tracking. Finally, a queue structure makes it easier to extend the scheduler in the future (e.g., to support priorities or time slicing), offering greater flexibility than a fixed array.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',4,1,'2025-07-28_16-57-58','In round-robin scheduling with an array, each thread is stored at a fixed index, and a global index is incremented to select the next thread in a circular fashion. This behaves similarly to a queue, as each thread gets a turn in order before wrapping around. So while the underlying structure is different, the array-based scheduler already mimics queue-like behavior. Using an actual queue can make this logic more explicit by always running the thread at the front and then moving it to the back after it yields. This can reduce the complexity of manual indexing and make it easier to manage threads dynamically—such as adding, removing, or reordering them. A queue also offers flexibility for future extensions, like priority-based scheduling.\nHowever, this flexibility comes with trade-offs. Queue operations involve memory management or pointer updates, which may introduce overhead. In contrast, the array-based method is lightweight and fast, especially for a small, fixed number of threads. Ultimately, while a queue better reflects the conceptual model of round-robin scheduling and supports extensibility, it may not offer significant functional benefits over arrays in simple systems like the one used in this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,1,'2025-07-28_17-12-06','In round-robin scheduling with an array, each thread is stored at a fixed index, and a global index is incremented to select the next thread in a circular fashion. This behaves similarly to a queue, as each thread gets a turn in order before wrapping around. So while the underlying structure is different, the array-based scheduler already mimics queue-like behavior. Using an actual queue can make this logic more explicit by always running the thread at the front and then moving it to the back after it yields. This can reduce the complexity of manual indexing and make it easier to manage threads dynamically—such as adding, removing, or reordering them. A queue also offers flexibility for future extensions, like priority-based scheduling.\nHowever, this flexibility comes with trade-offs. Queue operations involve memory management or pointer updates, which may introduce overhead. In contrast, the array-based method is lightweight and fast, especially for a small, fixed number of threads. Ultimately, while a queue better reflects the conceptual model of round-robin scheduling and supports extensibility, it may not offer significant functional benefits over arrays in simple systems like the one used in this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,2,'2025-07-28_16-30-28','PendSV is an interrupt that only executes when no other higher priority interrupts (such as SVC) are running. Therefore, the PendSV_Handler function will set PendSV to pending once a thread yields or the scheduler proceeds, but does not run immediately. Instead, it will wait until all other higher priority interrupts have executed. Once this list of threads has been executed, the function saves the current thread’s registers onto its own local stack. It will then run the scheduler to select the next thread, restore that thread’s saved context from its stack, update the stack pointer accordingly, and finally switch execution to the new thread by returning from the interrupt.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',4,2,'2025-07-28_16-57-58','PendSV is a low-priority interrupt that only executes when no higher-priority interrupts, such as SVC, are active. When a thread yields or the scheduler decides it\'s time to switch threads, PendSV is set to pending but does not run immediately. Once it begins executing, PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s state.\nSpecifically, it pushes registers R4–R11—the callee-saved registers—onto the stack pointed to by the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This ensures that all necessary context for the current thread is preserved. Then, it calls the scheduler (osSched), which updates the global thread index and sets the PSP to the saved stack pointer of the next thread.\nAfter that, PendSV_Handler restores the next thread’s context by popping R4–R11 from its stack (again using the PSP), effectively preparing the CPU to resume execution of that thread. The handler then exits, and control transfers to the newly scheduled thread using the updated PSP.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',4,2,'2025-07-28_17-12-06','PendSV is a low-priority interrupt that only executes when no higher-priority interrupts (such as SVC) are active. When a thread yields or the scheduler initiates a switch, PendSV is set to pending but does not run immediately. Once it begins executing, PendSV_Handler performs a context switch in four clear steps:\nThe PSP (Process Stack Pointer) is read to determine the location of the current thread’s stack.\nRegisters R4–R11—the callee-saved registers—are pushed onto this stack, preserving the current thread’s context.\nThe scheduler (osSched) is called to update the global thread index and set the PSP to point to the next thread’s saved stack location.\nRegisters R4–R11 are popped from the new thread’s stack, restoring its saved context so that execution can resume from where it last yielded.\nAfter these steps, the interrupt handler exits, and the processor continues executing the newly scheduled thread using the updated PSP. This structured process ensures seamless cooperative multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,3,'2025-07-28_16-30-28','There are multiple reasons why changing the OS in this way would cause several issues later down the road. By only prioritizing higher integers for higher priority, tasks with lower priority may become outdated or worse, may never be addressed as higher priority tasks continue to be addressed. This also means that any higher priority tasks that are dependent on simple fixes from lower priority tasks filed long ago will cause failures.\nMoreover, If the scheduler has to constantly search for the highest priority task, there will be an increased overhead time contributed to this search. In comparison, using round-robin with a queue for example is much quicker because the scheduler always knows to simply run the first in queue.\nFinally, relying solely on static priority values reduces system flexibility and adaptability, making it difficult to handle dynamic workloads or to implement features like priority aging, which helps prevent starvation by gradually increasing the priority of waiting tasks over time.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,3,'2025-07-28_16-57-58','There are multiple reasons why changing the OS in this way would cause several issues later down the road. By only prioritizing higher integers for higher priority, tasks with lower priority may become outdated or worse, may never be addressed as higher priority tasks continue to be addressed. This also means that any higher priority tasks that are dependent on simple fixes from lower priority tasks filed long ago will cause failures.\nMoreover, If the scheduler has to constantly search for the highest priority task, there will be an increased overhead time contributed to this search. In comparison, using round-robin with a queue for example is much quicker because the scheduler always knows to simply run the first in queue.\nFinally, relying solely on static priority values reduces system flexibility and adaptability, making it difficult to handle dynamic workloads or to implement features like priority aging, which helps prevent starvation by gradually increasing the priority of waiting tasks over time.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',4,3,'2025-07-28_17-12-06','Modifying the OS to always select the task with the highest integer priority introduces a critical flaw: only the highest-priority task will ever run. If that task never yields or terminates, all lower-priority tasks will be permanently starved and never get scheduled. This makes the system unfair and potentially unsafe, especially if essential background or maintenance tasks have lower priorities.\nAdditionally, tasks that depend on lower-priority operations may fail to complete, causing deadlocks or system instability. Searching through all tasks to find the highest priority also increases scheduling overhead compared to simpler strategies like round-robin, which just picks the next task in order.\nFinally, using a static priority system limits flexibility. Features like priority aging, which can gradually increase a waiting task’s priority to prevent starvation, are difficult to implement in such a rigid scheme.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,4,'2025-07-28_16-30-28','On the bright side of using C functions, readability and ability to maintain the code becomes much easier. C code is typically more readable, less prone to errors, and has more tools to debug the code when errors arise. Moreover, because C is considered a high-level language, compilation and modification of the code becomes much easier. Finally, if we were to use a different microcontroller (eg. not an STM board), the adaptability of C code makes it much easier to switch contexts.\nOn the other hand, calling C functions like what was done in our code provides less control over the STM board’s registers, hardware-specific instructions, and timing. In addition, although compilation in C may be faster, there is also an added time overhead (although rather small) from stack manipulation. C code behaviour also depends on each compiler’s optimizations and settings, which may change outputs when compiling and developing in different environments.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,4,'2025-07-28_16-57-58','On the bright side of using C functions, readability and ability to maintain the code becomes much easier. C code is typically more readable, less prone to errors, and has more tools to debug the code when errors arise. Moreover, because C is considered a high-level language, compilation and modification of the code becomes much easier. Finally, if we were to use a different microcontroller (eg. not an STM board), the adaptability of C code makes it much easier to switch contexts.\nOn the other hand, calling C functions like what was done in our code provides less control over the STM board’s registers, hardware-specific instructions, and timing. In addition, although compilation in C may be faster, there is also an added time overhead (although rather small) from stack manipulation. C code behaviour also depends on each compiler’s optimizations and settings, which may change outputs when compiling and developing in different environments.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,4,'2025-07-28_17-12-06','On the bright side, calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain instead of writing everything in assembly offers several benefits, especially in terms of readability, maintainability, and portability. C code is easier to understand and modify, reducing the likelihood of subtle bugs that are common in low-level assembly. It also allows the use of standard debugging tools, and makes it easier to adapt the code to different microcontrollers, since C abstracts away many hardware-specific details.\nHowever, using C comes with trade-offs. It provides less direct control over low-level operations, such as register manipulation and timing, which can be crucial in embedded systems. Additionally, calling C functions from an interrupt handler like PendSV_Handler introduces a small amount of overhead—due to function call conventions and stack frame setup—which could impact performance in timing-critical applications. There’s also some dependency on how the compiler translates the C code, which may introduce variability across platforms or optimization levels.\nOverall, the decision to use C enhances clarity and development speed but sacrifices some precision and efficiency. For educational or prototyping purposes, this is often a worthwhile trade-off—but in production or performance-critical systems, more of the logic may need to be done in carefully written assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,5,'2025-07-28_16-30-28','If instead of creating multiple threads, only one single thread was created, the OS should still work as expected. For example in the current code implementation, the scheduler iterates through an array of TCBs and once it reaches the end, will wrap around back to the beginning and run each thread once more. If the array is only one thread long, then once the thread yields, the scheduler will simply loop back to the start (index 0) and execute as expected. Therefore, the OS will still work.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,5,'2025-07-28_16-57-58','If only a single thread is created, such as a modified print_continuously thread that yields after each print, the OS will still function correctly. In this case, the scheduler has only one thread in the thread table to manage, so when the thread yields, the scheduler will simply select the same thread again. The osYield() call will still trigger a context switch, but the switch is trivial—it saves and restores the same thread’s context. While this maintains the illusion of concurrency and proves the multitasking infrastructure works, it doesn’t provide actual multitasking. A limitation in this scenario is that yielding becomes redundant, and if future features (like sleep or blocking I/O) are added, having only one runnable thread could result in system idling or deadlock. Nonetheless, under the current cooperative scheduling model, the OS behaves as expected.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',4,5,'2025-07-28_17-12-06','If only a single thread is created, such as a modified print_continuously thread that yields after each print, the OS will still function correctly. In this case, the scheduler has only one thread in the thread table to manage, so when the thread yields, the scheduler will simply select the same thread again. The osYield() call will still trigger a context switch, but the switch is trivial—it saves and restores the same thread’s context. While this maintains the illusion of concurrency and proves the multitasking infrastructure works, it doesn’t provide actual multitasking. A limitation in this scenario is that yielding becomes redundant, and if future features (like sleep or blocking I/O) are added, having only one runnable thread could result in system idling or deadlock. Nonetheless, under the current cooperative scheduling model, the OS behaves as expected.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,1,'2025-07-29_09-13-57','In this case, the user’s role consists mostly of how to utilize the thread. A thread function would have to be written to accept a parameter that takes in arguments indicated by the user. This function will then be passed into the osCreateThread with the indicated argument. From there, the function will be executed once the scheduler has allowed the thread to start.\nOn the other hand, the OS is responsible for placing the argument pointer into the thread\'s initial stack frame—specifically in the R0 register—so that when the thread starts executing, it receives the argument as expected. The OS must also manage context switching and ensure the stack is correctly set up to mimic a normal function call, allowing the user-defined thread function to access the data seamlessly.\nThis approach works because it takes advantage of the ARM Cortex-M processor’s calling convention, where function arguments are passed through registers R0 to R3, with R0 holding the first argument. When the OS creates a new thread, it manually constructs the initial stack frame for that thread to simulate what the processor expects at the start of a function call. Specifically, the OS places the pointer to the user-provided argument in the R0 register location within this stack frame. As a result, when the processor begins executing the thread, it behaves exactly like a standard function call, and the thread function receives the argument in R0 just as intended. This seamless integration is what allows user-defined data—whether a simple integer or a complex struct—to be reliably passed into threads at startup.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,1,'2025-07-29_09-35-33','In this case, the user’s role consists mostly of how to utilize the thread. A thread function would have to be written to accept a parameter that takes in arguments indicated by the user. This function will then be passed into the osCreateThread with the indicated argument. From there, the function will be executed once the scheduler has allowed the thread to start.\nOn the other hand, the OS is responsible for placing the argument pointer into the thread\'s initial stack frame—specifically in the R0 register—so that when the thread starts executing, it receives the argument as expected. The OS must also manage context switching and ensure the stack is correctly set up to mimic a normal function call, allowing the user-defined thread function to access the data seamlessly.\nThis approach works because it takes advantage of the ARM Cortex-M processor’s calling convention, where function arguments are passed through registers R0 to R3, with R0 holding the first argument. When the OS creates a new thread, it manually constructs the initial stack frame for that thread to simulate what the processor expects at the start of a function call. Specifically, the OS places the pointer to the user-provided argument in the R0 register location within this stack frame. As a result, when the processor begins executing the thread, it behaves exactly like a standard function call, and the thread function receives the argument in R0 just as intended. This seamless integration is what allows user-defined data—whether a simple integer or a complex struct—to be reliably passed into threads at startup.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',5,1,'2025-07-29_09-53-12','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must first define a thread function that accepts a single void* parameter. Before calling osCreateThread, the user is responsible for creating or allocating memory for the data they want to pass—whether it\'s a simple variable (like an int) or a more complex structure. For example, the user might define a local or dynamically allocated struct, fill it with the necessary values, and then pass a pointer to that struct as the argument to osCreateThread. This ensures that the thread will have access to valid data when it begins execution.\nOn the OS side, its role is to construct the thread\'s initial stack frame in memory. As part of this setup, the OS places the user’s argument pointer into the R0 register slot in the stack frame, because the ARM Cortex-M calling convention specifies that the first argument to a function is passed in R0. The OS also sets up other registers (like PC, xPSR, etc.) so that when the thread is scheduled for the first time, the processor behaves as if the function is being called normally. The OS must also manage context switching and stack alignment to maintain consistent thread behavior.\nThis system works because it mimics the standard function call mechanism defined by the ARM ABI. By preloading R0 with the pointer to the user’s data and setting up the stack accordingly, the thread function receives the argument exactly as if it had been called directly by another function. This approach provides flexibility for the user to pass any type of data to a thread and ensures that the OS remains in control of thread scheduling and execution setup.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,2,'2025-07-29_09-13-57','You can’t call osYield in context-switching functions because the purpose of osYield is for threads, not for these handlers (which are intended for interrupts). If osYield is called from within an interrupt handler, the system would attempt to initiate a context switch while it is already in the middle of one. This can corrupt the stack, cause nested or overlapping context switches, and lead to undefined behavior such as register corruption or crashes. Additionally, interrupt handlers don’t run in thread mode, so using osYield—which is designed for voluntary thread preemption—breaks the thread scheduling model and violates the assumptions the OS makes about control flow.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,2,'2025-07-29_09-35-33','You can\'t call osYield in these handlers because they run in interrupt (handler) mode, whereas osYield is designed for thread mode. osYield works by issuing an SVC (Supervisor Call), which triggers a context switch via the PendSV interrupt. If it\'s called from within a handler, you\'re attempting to request a context switch during an existing one, leading to nested context switches, stack corruption, or register misalignment.\nThe OS expects a clean thread-mode stack when switching contexts. Violating this by using osYield in a handler can destabilize the system and potentially cause a hardfault, which is more severe than a regular thread crash because it halts or resets the entire system. The separation between thread and interrupt mode is essential for safe and predictable OS behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,2,'2025-07-29_09-53-12','You can\'t call osYield in these handlers because they run in interrupt (handler) mode, whereas osYield is designed for thread mode. osYield works by issuing an SVC (Supervisor Call), which triggers a context switch via the PendSV interrupt. If it\'s called from within a handler, you\'re attempting to request a context switch during an existing one, leading to nested context switches, stack corruption, or register misalignment.\nThe OS expects a clean thread-mode stack when switching contexts. Violating this by using osYield in a handler can destabilize the system and potentially cause a hardfault, which is more severe than a regular thread crash because it halts or resets the entire system. The separation between thread and interrupt mode is essential for safe and predictable OS behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,3,'2025-07-29_09-13-57','The main benefit of setting the fundamental OS to 0.1 ms is the increase in time resolution, allowing for quicker response of the OS should several high-priority tasks present themselves at a time. This type of increase in resolution will really benefit high-speed RTOS systems.\nHowever, there are several downsides to doing this. By decreasing the fundamental OS clock, the processor will have to spend more time handling interrupts when they arise as the ticks are triggered more frequently. Moreover, because more time is spent on these background tasks, CPU is reduced and it has less time available to execute actual user threads, potentially slowing down overall system performance. This increased interrupt load also leads to higher power consumption, which is especially problematic in battery-powered or energy-efficient systems. Additionally, the system becomes more complex to debug and maintain, and in many practical applications, the benefit of sub-millisecond timing does not justify the cost. Unless the application specifically demands extremely high timing precision, setting the OS clock to 0.1 ms can introduce more drawbacks than advantages.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,3,'2025-07-29_09-35-33','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1 ms increases the system’s timing resolution, which allows the OS to respond more quickly to events. This can be beneficial in real-time applications such as motor control, audio processing, or high-frequency sensor sampling, where fast and precise timing is critical to system correctness. It can also help in systems with many short tasks that require rapid context switching for responsiveness.\nHowever, there are significant downsides. A shorter clock period results in more frequent SysTick interrupts, which increases CPU time spent in interrupt handling rather than running actual user threads. This can reduce system throughput, increase power consumption, and add complexity to debugging due to the higher interrupt frequency. These effects are especially problematic in battery-powered or low-power systems. Additionally, unless the application has strict real-time requirements, the extra resolution may provide little practical benefit. For general-purpose or batch-processing systems, a 1 ms resolution is typically sufficient.\nTo balance precision and performance, designers might consider adaptive tick rates (tickless idle), grouping time-critical tasks under higher-frequency timers while leaving others under a standard 1 ms system tick. This approach helps maintain power efficiency without sacrificing timing precision where it matters most.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,3,'2025-07-29_09-53-12','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1 ms increases the system’s timing resolution, which allows the OS to respond more quickly to events. This can be beneficial in real-time applications such as motor control, audio processing, or high-frequency sensor sampling, where fast and precise timing is critical to system correctness. It can also help in systems with many short tasks that require rapid context switching for responsiveness.\nHowever, there are significant downsides. A shorter clock period results in more frequent SysTick interrupts, which increases CPU time spent in interrupt handling rather than running actual user threads. This can reduce system throughput, increase power consumption, and add complexity to debugging due to the higher interrupt frequency. These effects are especially problematic in battery-powered or low-power systems. Additionally, unless the application has strict real-time requirements, the extra resolution may provide little practical benefit. For general-purpose or batch-processing systems, a 1 ms resolution is typically sufficient.\nTo balance precision and performance, designers might consider adaptive tick rates (tickless idle), grouping time-critical tasks under higher-frequency timers while leaving others under a standard 1 ms system tick. This approach helps maintain power efficiency without sacrificing timing precision where it matters most.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,4,'2025-07-29_09-13-57','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking requires the user to manually call osYield() and yield control of the CPU in their thread. This means that the responsibility falls upon the user to ensure fair scheduling and making sure one thread does not forget to yield, therefore keeping the processor from other threads. The OS does little to no work in co-operative multitasking.\nOn the other hand, pre-emptive multitasking now shifts that responsibility to the OS. The user no longer has to manually yield and simply writes the thread function. The OS will then automatically yield after a certain runtime has been exceeded (eg. SysTick). Once this time passes, the OS triggers an interrupt —typically via the SysTick_Handler—which then sets the PendSV interrupt to initiate a context switch. This allows the operating system to forcibly take control from the running thread and switch to another, ensuring that all threads get a fair share of CPU time regardless of whether they yield voluntarily. This makes pre-emptive multitasking more robust and reliable, especially in systems where threads may block or run indefinitely without yielding.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,4,'2025-07-29_09-35-33','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking requires the user to manually call osYield() and yield control of the CPU in their thread. This means that the responsibility falls upon the user to ensure fair scheduling and making sure one thread does not forget to yield, therefore keeping the processor from other threads. The OS does little to no work in co-operative multitasking.\nOn the other hand, pre-emptive multitasking now shifts that responsibility to the OS. The user no longer has to manually yield and simply writes the thread function. The OS will then automatically yield after a certain runtime has been exceeded (eg. SysTick). Once this time passes, the OS triggers an interrupt —typically via the SysTick_Handler—which then sets the PendSV interrupt to initiate a context switch. This allows the operating system to forcibly take control from the running thread and switch to another, ensuring that all threads get a fair share of CPU time regardless of whether they yield voluntarily. This makes pre-emptive multitasking more robust and reliable, especially in systems where threads may block or run indefinitely without yielding.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,4,'2025-07-29_09-53-12','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking requires the user to manually call osYield() to voluntarily give up control of the CPU. This means the user is responsible for ensuring fair scheduling by making sure no thread hogs the processor by failing to yield. In this model, the OS plays a minimal role in managing thread switching and relies heavily on the user’s cooperation.\nPre-emptive multitasking, by contrast, shifts this responsibility to the OS. Users simply write their thread functions without needing to explicitly yield. The OS enforces fairness by using a hardware timer (such as SysTick) to measure how long a thread runs. When the thread exceeds its assigned timeslice, the OS triggers an interrupt (via the SysTick_Handler), which sets the PendSV interrupt to initiate a context switch. This mechanism forcibly pre-empts the running thread, allowing the OS to allocate CPU time fairly among all threads, regardless of whether they voluntarily yield.\nIn some cases, the boundary between user and OS responsibility can blur—for example, threads may still call osYield() to improve responsiveness or to wait on specific events, even under a pre-emptive system. The OS must handle these voluntary yields gracefully alongside pre-emptive switches. Moreover, the OS often implements scheduling policies beyond simple time slicing, such as priority-based scheduling or aging, to ensure responsiveness and prevent starvation, especially in complex systems with real-time requirements.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,5,'2025-07-29_09-13-57','These two mechanisms are able to work together because the conditions they depend upon do not clash with one another. When a thread calls osYield(), it is voluntarily giving itself up to the CPU to allow the next thread to run, regardless of if this happens before the timeslice has been reached. On the other hand, using a hardware timer and tracking the runtime will only pre-emptively cause a context switch if osYield() has not been called in time. Thus, both are able to coexist with one another.\n',0,'s25'),('almedina',5,5,'2025-07-29_09-35-33','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because both mechanisms ultimately trigger the same outcome—a context switch—using the same underlying mechanism: the PendSV interrupt. In cooperative multitasking, when a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily requests a context switch by setting the PendSV bit (_ICSR |= 1 << 28) and executing an ISB instruction to synchronize. This triggers the PendSV_Handler, which performs the context switch.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS uses the SysTick timer, which fires at regular intervals (e.g., every 1 ms). Each time the SysTick_Handler is invoked, it decrements the running thread’s remaining timeslice. If the timeslice reaches zero, the handler resets the thread’s runtime and also triggers the PendSV interrupt to switch threads.\nBecause both osYield() and SysTick-based pre-emption rely on PendSV to do the actual switching, they can seamlessly coexist. A yielding thread can give up the CPU before its timeslice expires, or the OS can forcibly pre-empt the thread if it runs too long. Since both mechanisms funnel into the same context-switching code, the OS remains consistent and stable regardless of how the switch is initiated.\n',1,'s25'),('almedina',5,5,'2025-07-29_09-53-12','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because both mechanisms ultimately trigger the same outcome—a context switch—using the same underlying mechanism: the PendSV interrupt. In cooperative multitasking, when a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily requests a context switch by setting the PendSV bit (_ICSR |= 1 << 28) and executing an ISB instruction to synchronize. This triggers the PendSV_Handler, which performs the context switch.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS uses the SysTick timer, which fires at regular intervals (e.g., every 1 ms). Each time the SysTick_Handler is invoked, it decrements the running thread’s remaining timeslice. If the timeslice reaches zero, the handler resets the thread’s runtime and also triggers the PendSV interrupt to switch threads.\nBecause both osYield() and SysTick-based pre-emption rely on PendSV to do the actual switching, they can seamlessly coexist. A yielding thread can give up the CPU before its timeslice expires, or the OS can forcibly pre-empt the thread if it runs too long. Since both mechanisms funnel into the same context-switching code, the OS remains consistent and stable regardless of how the switch is initiated.\n',1,'s25'),('apelteki',1,1,'2025-06-27_22-21-36','HAL is the hardware abstraction layer, a set of pre-written libraries that simplify the access to the microcontroller’s hardware. It could be used to avoid low-level register manipulation making embedded programming less error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('apelteki',1,2,'2025-06-27_22-21-36','__io_putchar is a function used by “printf” to send a single character through UART. Once printf is connected to the UART hardware, the output is visible in the serial console.\n',0,'s25'),('apelteki',1,3,'2025-06-27_22-21-36','The debugger is used to be able to inspect low level system states (i.e. register values and memory), it helps verify things like the values of MSP and PSP during runtime. Printf cannot access these low-level system states.\n',1,'s25'),('apelteki',1,4,'2025-06-27_22-21-36','We get the initial value of MSP from 0x0 address from vector table. Then, we subtract 0x400 (or any fixed stack size) to get a new value for PSP. To calculate where new thread stacks can start safely, knowing the original MSP value is essential to not overlap the new PSP with the existing one.\n',1,'s25'),('apelteki',1,5,'2025-06-27_22-21-36','If we knew the number of stacks at compile-time, we could calculate the starting address of each one using the same logic:  start from MSP, add the main stack size, then add (j-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE for each new stack. This helps us know exactly where each thread’s stack begins in memory.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',1,1,'2025-05-31_14-49-57','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. It’s useful because developing for a microcontroller requires a lot of set up code and that is difficult to write. The HAL abstracts complexities of using hardware and means that the software can control and interact with hardware without needing to know a lot of specific things about the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,1,'2025-05-31_15-48-48','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. It’s useful because developing for a microcontroller requires a lot of set up code and that is difficult to write. The HAL abstracts complexities of using hardware and means that the software can control and interact with hardware without needing to know a lot of specific things about the hardware. Abstracting away specifics of hardware also means the same software could be used with different hardware, as long as the HAL handles the differences in hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,1,'2025-05-31_18-14-23','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. It’s useful because developing for a microcontroller requires a lot of set up code and that is difficult to write. The HAL abstracts complexities of using hardware and means that the software can control and interact with hardware without needing to know a lot of specific things about the hardware. Abstracting away specifics of hardware also means the same software could be used with different hardware, as long as the HAL handles the differences in hardware. A HAL could shorten the development time of a software development project since it reduces set up code and abstracts away complexities of hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,2,'2025-05-31_14-49-57','The __io_putchar function is used by the printf function, which is from the <std.io> library in C. The printf function is used to easily print strings to the right channel, and it does this by calling __io_putchar over and over again to output characters. We need to write the __io_putchar function so that it will output the character to the right place, in this case the UART IO channel.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',1,2,'2025-05-31_15-48-48','The __io_putchar function is used by the printf function, which is from the <std.io> library in C. The printf function is used to easily print strings to the right channel, and it does this by calling __io_putchar over and over again to output characters. We need to write the __io_putchar function so that it will output the character to the right place, in this case the UART IO channel. The printf function doesn’t specify which IO channel to output to. By implementing a function like __io_putchar, the same printf function can be reused for different projects that might need to output to different channels. If __io_putchar was not provided for a project that used a different output channel than UART, then the printf may not output to the correct channel, or could crash if no output channel was specified.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,2,'2025-05-31_18-14-23','The __io_putchar function is used by the printf function, which is from the <std.io> library in C. The printf function is used to easily print strings to the right channel, and it does this by calling __io_putchar over and over again to output characters. We need to write the __io_putchar function so that it will output the character to the right place, in this case the UART IO channel. The printf function doesn’t specify which IO channel to output to. By implementing a function like __io_putchar, the same printf function can be reused for different projects that might need to output to different channels. If __io_putchar was not provided for a project that used a different output channel than UART, then the printf may not output to the correct channel, or could crash if no output channel was specified. Since printf depends on __io_putchar, an incorrect implementation of __io_putchar could cause the printf function to crash.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,3,'2025-05-31_14-49-57','The debugger can show us what the current values are on the registers. If we use printf, the arguments to printf will be loaded onto the registers, which will change the values on the registers. We are using the debugger because we want to know the current values on the registers without changing them with a function call.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,3,'2025-05-31_15-48-48','The debugger can show us what the current values are on the registers. If we use printf, the arguments to printf will be loaded onto the registers, which will change the values on the registers. We are using the debugger because we want to know the current values on the registers without changing them with a function call. Also, it is much easier to view the current values of all variables by using the debugger, since that could require many printf statements.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,3,'2025-05-31_18-14-23','The debugger can show us what the current values are on the registers. If we use printf, the arguments to printf will be loaded onto the registers, which will change the values on the registers. We are using the debugger because we want to know the current values on the registers without changing them with a function call. If there are parts of the code that depend on specific register values, then adding a printf statement could alter the behaviour of the code. Also, it is much easier to view the current values of all variables by using the debugger, since that could require many printf statements.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,4,'2025-05-31_14-49-57','We got a new value for PSP_val by taking the location for MSP_init_val and decreasing by the minimum stack size. We need to know the initial location of MSP for this because we want to choose a new location for the new stack that is a specific distance from MSP. The stack allocation method works by designating a location for a new stack, and then moves the PSP to that location to run another thread concurrently to the main thread, which is located at the location stored in the MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,4,'2025-05-31_15-48-48','We got a new value for PSP_val by taking the location for MSP_init_val and decreasing by the minimum stack size. We need to know the initial location of MSP for this because we want to choose a new location for the new stack that is a specific number of bytes from MSP. The stack allocation method works by designating a location for a new stack, and then moves the PSP to that location to run another thread concurrently to the main thread, which is located at the location stored in the MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,4,'2025-05-31_18-14-23','We got a new value for PSP_val by taking the location for MSP_init_val and decreasing by the minimum stack size. The minimum stack size is constant and can be manually set by the user. We need to know the initial location of MSP for this because we want to choose a new location for the new stack that is a specific number of bytes from MSP. The stack allocation method works by designating a location for a new stack, and then moves the PSP to that location to run another thread concurrently to the main thread, which is located at the location stored in the MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,5,'2025-05-31_14-49-57','We want each stack to be the same size, specifically the minimum stack size. For multiple stacks, we would have to know the location of each stack. The first stack PSP value is found by taking location of MSP and moving by the minimum stack size. Subsequent stacks will be located at multiples of the minimum stack from the MSP. Those locations could be stored as variables. Then, to move to each new stack, PSP would be set to any of those locations.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',1,5,'2025-05-31_15-48-48','We want each stack to be the same size, specifically the minimum stack size. The minimum stack size can be manually set while programming the OS; however, if it is too large for the number of stacks to be allocated, it may result in invalid addresses for some stacks. For multiple stacks, we would have to know the location of each stack. The first stack PSP value is found by taking location of MSP and moving by the minimum stack size. Subsequent stacks will be located at multiples of the minimum stack from the MSP. Those locations could be stored as variables. Then, to move to each new stack, PSP would be set to any of those locations.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',1,5,'2025-05-31_18-14-23','We want each stack to be the same size, specifically the minimum stack size. For multiple stacks, we would have to know the location of each stack. The first stack PSP value is found by taking location of MSP and moving by the minimum stack size. Subsequent stacks will be located at multiples of the minimum stack from the MSP. Those locations could be stored as variables. Then, to move to each new stack, PSP would be set to any of those locations.\nThe minimum stack size can be manually set while programming the OS; however, if it is too large for the number of stacks to be allocated, it may result in invalid addresses for some stacks. This could be prevented by either checking that the addresses generated for each stack don’t exceed the size of the memory, and discarding any invalid addresses. Alternatively, the minimum stack size could be chosen by evenly dividing the available memory space by the number of stacks needed. This would ensure no invalid addresses are created.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,1,'2025-05-29_10-35-19','PC is the program counter, so it holds the next instruction and the CPU will automatically run what is stored in PC. In this case, since the PC is holding the function pointer to the print continuously function, then the print continuously function will run next.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,1,'2025-05-29_11-07-41','PC is the program counter, so it holds the next instruction and the CPU will automatically run what is stored in PC. In this case, since the PC is holding the function pointer to the print continuously function, then the print continuously function will run next. The difference between changing PC to a function pointer and calling a function is that when calling a function, the arguments for the function will be saved to the registers. In the example of print continuously, there are no arguments, but it were a different function that required arguments, then just changing PC might cause the code to crash since correct arguments aren’t passed.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,1,'2025-05-30_17-18-30','PC is the program counter, so it holds the next instruction and the CPU will automatically run what is stored in PC. In this case, since the PC is holding the function pointer to the print continuously function, then the print continuously function will run next. The difference between changing PC to a function pointer and calling a function is that when calling a function, the arguments for the function will be saved to the registers. In the example of print continuously, there are no arguments, but if it were a different function that required arguments, then just changing PC might cause the code to crash since correct arguments aren’t passed. Saving the arguments to the registers is part of a function call, that does not happen when the PC is manually changed.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,2,'2025-05-29_10-35-19','A system call is a type of trap where a program calls the OS to perform a service that requires kernel mode privileges.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',2,2,'2025-05-29_11-07-41','A system call is a type of trap where a program calls the OS to perform a service that requires kernel mode privileges. This is important because it increases security. The user cannot directly perform actions that require kernel privileges; instead the OS handles it for the user. This protects the system from malicious or erroneous user actions or instructions. When a function that requires a system call gets called, first the function prepares for the system call by pushing arguments onto the stack. Then, when the system call happens, the OS takes over (switches to kernel mode) and the trap handler performs a service. Afterwards, the mode is changed back to user from kernel and the function finishes.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',2,2,'2025-05-30_17-18-30','A system call is a type of trap where a program calls the OS to perform a service that requires kernel mode privileges. It is a type of function call that the OS responds to. It is important because it increases security. The user cannot directly perform actions that require kernel privileges; instead the OS handles it for the user. This protects the system from malicious or erroneous user actions or instructions. When a function that requires a system call gets called, first the function prepares for the system call by pushing arguments onto the stack. Then, when the system call happens, the OS takes over (switches to kernel mode) and the trap handler performs a service. Afterwards, the mode is changed back to user from kernel and the function finishes.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,3,'2025-05-29_10-35-19','Before jumping to a new thread, the values we want on the registers in the new thread are pushed onto a stack in the right order. Then, after moving to the new thread, assembly code is used to pop those values off the stack and put them in the registers. Setting up a thread’s stack means pushing the correct values onto the stack before jumping to it so the registers will have the right values after moving to the right stack. We need to store register values on the stack because there are only 16 registers and we need to have the original values on the register before switching, but have the new thread values ready.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,3,'2025-05-29_11-07-41','Before jumping to a new thread, the values we want on the registers in the new thread are pushed onto a stack in the right order. We want to reserve a space in memory for when starting a new thread. Then, after moving to the new thread, assembly code is used to pop those values off the stack and put them in the registers. Setting up a thread’s stack means pushing the correct values onto the stack before jumping to it so the registers will have the right values after moving to the right stack. We need to store register values on the stack because there are only 16 registers, and we want to save both the original values on the stack for when we return from the new thread, but also be prepared with the new register values we want for the new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,3,'2025-05-30_17-18-30','Before jumping to a new thread, the values we want on the registers in the new thread are pushed onto a stack in the right order. We want to reserve a space in memory for when starting a new thread. Then, after moving to the new thread, assembly code is used to pop those values off the stack and put them in the registers. Setting up a thread’s stack means pushing the correct values onto the stack before jumping to it so the registers will have the right values after moving to the right stack. We need to store register values on the stack because there are only 16 registers, and we want to save both the original values on the stack for when we return from the new thread, but also be prepared with the new register values we want for the new thread. The register values saved for the new thread must be saved in a specific order so that the right values are populated later. For example, the xPSR register is the first value to be popped off the stack and requires a specific value that shouldn’t be mixed up with a value for another register.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,4,'2025-05-29_10-35-19','This is because we stored the register values for the new thread in a stack, then set PSP to be that stack. If we didn’t set PSP, then the wrong values would get taken off the stack to put in the registers and there would be no point in setting up the stack. The code would crash if the stack wasn’t set up because a random value would be put in PC and then random code would start running, which would crash right away.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,4,'2025-05-29_11-07-41','This is because we stored the register values for the new thread in a stack, then set PSP to be that stack. If we didn’t set PSP, then the wrong values would get taken off the stack to put in the registers and there would be no point in setting up the stack. Also, if we don’t set PSP, we don’t switch to a new stack and instead stay at the main stack or location of MSP. This is bad because we want to preserve the values in the main stack for when we exit from the new thread. The code would crash if the stack wasn’t set up because a random value would be put in PC and then random code would start running, which would crash right away.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,4,'2025-05-30_17-18-30','This is because we stored the register values for the new thread in a stack, then set PSP to be that stack. If we didn’t set PSP, then the wrong values would get taken off the stack to put in the registers and there would be no point in setting up the stack. Also, if we don’t set PSP, we don’t switch to a new stack and instead stay at the main stack or location of MSP, assuming that we are starting from the main stack. This is bad because we want to preserve the values in the main stack for when we exit from the new thread. The code would crash if the stack wasn’t set up because a random value would be put in PC and then random code would start running, which would crash right away.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',2,5,'2025-05-29_10-35-19','The PC is holding the function pointer to the prints continuously function. The value held in the PC won’t change unless the OS changes it, so the function would run over and over again.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',2,5,'2025-05-29_11-07-41','The original print continuously function did not return anything. R14, the link register, holds the address that the program should return to after a function call. We set R14 to be a random value in memory when creating the new thread. This means if the new function in the thread tries to return, it will cause a crash in the code because after the function the program will return to this random point in memory.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',2,5,'2025-05-30_17-18-30','The original print continuously function did not return anything. R14, the link register, holds the address that the program should return to after a function call. We set up the stack to populate a random value into R14 once the new thread starts, which will be an invalid address. So, the return address was not stored on the stack when we started the new thread. This means there will be a hard fault when the CPU tries to return after the function, because there is an invalid address at R14.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,1,'2025-06-23_11-38-55','The first step is preprocessing. This part handles #include statements and if statements. It produces preprocessed source files. Next is compilation, where each preprocessed file is compiled individually by the compiler into object (*.o) files. This is not an executable yet. Finally, the linker combines all the object files into one executable file. This resolves symbol references between files, such as functions referenced in one file and defined in another. It also links necessary libraries. The output is a single executable.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,1,'2025-06-23_14-52-04','The first step is preprocessing. This part handles #include statements and conditional compilation directives #if statements. It produces preprocessed source files. Next is compilation, where each preprocessed file is compiled individually by the compiler into object (*.o) files. This is not an executable yet. Finally, the linker combines all the object files into one executable file. This resolves symbol references between files, such as functions referenced in one file and defined in another. It also links necessary libraries. The output is a single executable.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,1,'2025-06-23_16-00-34','The first step is preprocessing. This part handles #include statements and conditional compilation directives #if statements. It produces preprocessed source files. If this stage was skipped or not done properly, then #includes not being handled would lead to many functions not being defined. Next is compilation, where each preprocessed file is compiled individually by the compiler into object (*.o) files. This is not an executable yet. Finally, the linker combines all the object files into one executable file. This resolves symbol references between files, such as functions referenced in one file and defined in another. It also links necessary libraries. The output is a single executable.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,1,'2025-06-24_13-48-34','The first step is preprocessing. This part handles #include statements and conditional compilation directives #if statements. It produces preprocessed source files. If this stage was skipped or not done properly, then #includes not being handled would lead to many functions not being defined. Some common #includes include C libraries like stdio, which defines functions like printf(). Next is compilation, where each preprocessed file is compiled individually by the compiler into object (*.o) files. This is not an executable yet. Finally, the linker combines all the object files into one executable file. This resolves symbol references between files, such as functions referenced in one file and defined in another. It also links necessary libraries. The output is a single executable.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,1,'2025-06-24_17-18-11','The first step is preprocessing. This part handles #include statements and conditional compilation directives #if statements. It produces preprocessed source files. If this stage was skipped or not done properly, then #includes not being handled would lead to many functions not being defined, such as functions from libraries. Some common #includes include C libraries like stdio, which defines functions like printf(). Next is compilation, where each preprocessed file is compiled individually by the compiler into object (*.o) files. This is not an executable yet. Finally, the linker combines all the object files into one executable file. This resolves symbol references between files, such as functions referenced in one file and defined in another. It also links necessary libraries. The output is a single executable.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,2,'2025-06-23_11-38-55','The #include directive is meant to be used for header files, such as #include <kernel.h>. This is because the header files contain just the function declarations, while the *.c file contains function definitions. Including the *.c file means the entirety of the *.c file is pasted in. If multiple files are compiled, like the original *.c file and a *.c file that contains the include, this may lead to multiple definitions of the same function.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,2,'2025-06-23_14-52-04','The #include directive is meant to be used for header files, such as #include <kernel.h>. This is because the header files contain just the function declarations, while the *.c file contains function definitions. Including the *.c file means the entirety of the *.c file is pasted in. If multiple files are compiled, like the original *.c file and a *.c file that contains the include, this may lead to multiple definitions of the same function. Including a header file instead of a *.c means functions are only declared once. The header file can be included in the *.c function definition file.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,2,'2025-06-23_16-00-34','The #include directive is meant to be used for header files, such as #include <kernel.h>. This is because the header files contain just the function declarations, while the *.c file contains function definitions. Including the *.c file means the entirety of the *.c file is pasted in. If multiple files are compiled, like the original *.c file and a *.c file that contains the include, this may lead to multiple definitions of the same function. Specifically, this would cause a “multiple definition” linker error during the linking process. Including a header file instead of a *.c means functions are only declared once. The header file can be included in the *.c function definition file.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,2,'2025-06-24_13-48-34','The #include directive is meant to be used for header files, such as #include <kernel.h>. This is because the header files contain just the function declarations, while the *.c file contains function definitions. Including the *.c file means the entirety of the *.c file is pasted in. If multiple files are compiled, like the original *.c file and a *.c file that contains the include, this may lead to multiple definitions of the same function. Specifically, this would cause a “multiple definition” linker error during the linking process. Including a header file instead of a *.c means functions are only declared once. The header file can be included in the *.c function definition file. Using a #include directive on a *.c file is almost always discouraged, but one case where you might do that could be in very small project like an embedded system, which could help optimize for speed.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,2,'2025-06-24_17-18-11','The #include directive is meant to be used for header files, such as #include <kernel.h>. This is because the header files contain just the function declarations, while the *.c file contains function definitions. Including the *.c file means the entirety of the *.c file is pasted in. If multiple files are compiled, like the original *.c file and a *.c file that contains the include, this may lead to multiple definitions of the same function. Specifically, this would cause a “multiple definition” linker error during the linking process. Including a header file instead of a *.c means functions are only declared once. The header file can be included in the *.c function definition file. Using a #include directive on a *.c file is almost always discouraged, but one case where you might do that could be in very small project like an embedded system, which could help optimize for speed. However, this must be done very carefully to avoid multiple definition error.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,3,'2025-06-23_11-38-55','From the user’s perspective:\nFirst, initialize the kernel\nSecond, create a thread and pass a function pointer to it\nFinally, start the kernel to switch to the new thread\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',3,3,'2025-06-23_14-52-04','From the user’s perspective:\nFirst, initialize the kernel by calling the kernel initialization function\nNext, write a function\nCreate a thread by calling the “create thread” function and pass the function pointer of the user defined function\nFinally, start the kernel to switch to the new thread by calling the “start kernel” function\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,3,'2025-06-23_16-00-34','From the user’s perspective:\nFirst, initialize the kernel by calling the kernel initialization function\nNext, write the thread function\nCreate a thread by calling the “create thread” function and pass a pointer to the thread function\nFinally, start the kernel to switch to the new thread by calling the “start kernel” function\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,3,'2025-06-24_13-48-34','From the user’s perspective:\nFirst, initialize the kernel by calling the kernel initialization function\nNext, the user must write the thread function\nCreate a thread by calling the “create thread” function and pass a pointer to the thread function (the function pointer)\nFinally, start the kernel to switch to the new thread by calling the “start kernel” function\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,3,'2025-06-24_17-18-11','From the user’s perspective:\nFirst, initialize the kernel by calling the kernel initialization function\nNext, the user is responsible for writing the thread function\nCreate a thread by calling the “create thread” function and pass a pointer to the thread function (the function pointer)\nFinally, start the kernel to switch to the new thread by calling the “start kernel” function\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,4,'2025-06-23_11-38-55','The stack pool is a segment of memory allocated to creating threads. We allocated 0x0 to 0x4000 to creating threads. Multiple threads that are each 0x400 big can be created in this space.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',3,4,'2025-06-23_14-52-04','The stack pool is a segment of memory allocated to creating threads. We set the size of this stack to be 0x4000. Each thread will be sized 0x400 and will be contained within the stack. The stack will also hold register values that are set up before jumping to a new thread. Allocated stacks to multiple threads can be done by saving the location of the previous thread stack in a kernel global variable, and the next thread will be 0x400 below the previous thread stack.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',3,4,'2025-06-23_16-00-34','The stack pool is a segment of memory for creating stacks. Specifically, it contains the OS stack and thread stacks. We set the size of this stack to be 0x4000. Each thread will be sized 0x400 and will be contained within the stack. The thread stacks will also hold register values that are set up before jumping to a new thread. In this lab, thread stack allocation begins at MSP. Then, a stack is allocated to a new thread by find a location 0x400 below the previous stack and saving a pointer to that location.\nThe kernel tracks which parts of the stack are in use by saving a pointer to the location of the last stack. All stack space below that pointer is not yet allocated.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,4,'2025-06-24_13-48-34','The stack pool is a segment of memory for creating stacks. Specifically, it contains the OS stack and thread stacks. We set the size of this stack to be 0x4000. Each thread will be sized 0x400 and will be contained within the stack. The thread stacks will also hold register values that are set up before jumping to a new thread. In this lab, thread stack allocation begins at MSP. Then, a stack is allocated to a new thread by find a location 0x400 below the previous stack and saving a pointer to that location.\nThe kernel tracks which parts of the stack are in use by saving a pointer to the location of the last stack. All stack space below that pointer is not yet allocated.\nThis could be extended to support multiple threads in the same stack pool, since this method of allocation ensures no thread stacks will overlap. Each stack pointer to a thread stack will have to be saved individually, in addition to a global kernel pointer to the location of the last stack.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,4,'2025-06-24_17-18-11','The stack pool is a segment of memory for creating stacks. Specifically, it contains the OS stack and thread stacks. We set the size of this stack to be 0x4000. Each thread will be sized 0x400 and will be contained within the stack. The thread stacks will also hold register values that are set up before jumping to a new thread. In this lab, thread stack allocation begins at MSP. Then, a stack is allocated to a new thread by find a location 0x400 below the previous stack and saving a pointer to that location.\nThe kernel tracks which parts of the stack are in use by saving a pointer to the location of the last stack. All stack space below that pointer is not yet allocated.\nOne disadvantage of this method of allocating thread stacks is that each thread is allocated a fixed size chunk of memory. This could be problematic if a thread requires more space.\nThis could be extended to support multiple threads in the same stack pool, since this method of allocation ensures no thread stacks will overlap. Each stack pointer to a thread stack will have to be saved individually, in addition to a global kernel pointer to the location of the last stack.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,5,'2025-06-23_11-38-55','This is to split user privileges and kernel privileges. The user should not have permission to access or ability to see actions that the kernel performs. Things only the kernel should be able to do or see include manipulating the register values or move the stack pointer. This matters to the user because it means the user doesn’t need to know the specifics of how these OS services are performed, which abstracts away the complexities of these actions.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',3,5,'2025-06-23_14-52-04','This is to split user privileges and kernel privileges. The user should not have permission to access or ability to see actions that the kernel performs. Things only the kernel should be able to do or see include managing system resources, handling system calls, managing processes, etc. This matters to the user because it means the user doesn’t need to know the specifics of how these OS services are performed, which abstracts away the complexities of these actions. The user’s responsibility is to call kernel functions that will create new threads.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',3,5,'2025-06-23_16-00-34','This is to split user privileges and kernel privileges. The user should not have permission to access or ability to see actions that the kernel performs. Things only the kernel should be able to do or see include managing system resources, handling system calls, managing processes, controlling access to hardware, etc. This matters to the user because it means the user doesn’t need to know the specifics of how these OS services are performed, which abstracts away the complexities of these actions. The user’s responsibility is to call kernel functions that will create new threads.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',3,5,'2025-06-24_13-48-34','This is to split user privileges and kernel privileges. The user should not have permission to access or ability to see actions that the kernel performs. Things only the kernel should be able to do or see include managing system resources like memory, handling system calls, managing processes, controlling access to hardware, etc. This matters to the user because it means the user doesn’t need to know the specifics of how these OS services are performed, which abstracts away the complexities of these actions. The user’s responsibility is to call kernel functions that will create new threads. The user does not write the thread code themselves.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',3,5,'2025-06-24_17-18-11','This is to split user privileges and kernel privileges. The user should not have permission to access or ability to see actions that the kernel performs. Things only the kernel should be able to do or see include managing system resources like memory, handling system calls, managing processes, controlling access to hardware, etc. This matters to the user because it means the user doesn’t need to know the specifics of how these OS services are performed, which abstracts away the complexities of these actions. The user’s responsibility is to call kernel functions that will create new threads. The user writes the thread function code, but the kernel handles the execution of the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',4,1,'2025-07-03_10-34-28','With a queue, round-robin scheduling can work by moving to the next task in the queue for each context switch. At the end of the queue, the first thread in the queue goes next. When using an array for round-robin scheduling, the kernel uses the index of a thread in the array to select the next thread. Implementation with a queue is beneficial because it’s more efficient to traverse the queue than to calculate the next array index. Additionally, queues are a more memory-efficient data structure.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',4,1,'2025-07-03_13-44-33','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a circular queue. The next thread to jump to during a context switch can be found by dequeuing. When using an array for round-robin scheduling, the kernel uses the index of a thread in the array to select the next thread. Implementation with a queue is beneficial because it’s more efficient to traverse the queue than to calculate the next array index. Enqueuing and dequeuing are O(1). Additionally, queues are a more memory-efficient data structure, since they aren’t a fixed size. Arrays are a fixed size, and if that size is exceeded, resizing is resource expensive. Queues add additional overhead, since a node structure is required to implement the queue.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',4,2,'2025-07-03_10-34-28','The PendSV_Handler first puts the current register values onto the original thread stack. Then, it calls osSched() to set PSP to the thread to switch to. It then takes the values off of the new thread stack, which were stored there previously either when the thread was created or during a context switch, and puts them onto the registers. Finally, it moves the thread function pointer to the link register and the thread function starts running.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',4,2,'2025-07-03_13-44-33','The PendSV_Handler first saves the PSP value on register 0. Next, the values of registers 4-11 are saved on to the current thread stack. Then, osSched() is called to select the next thread. Specifically, it sets PSP to the stack pointer of the next thread. The new thread stack pointer is saved to register 0. Registers 4-11 are loaded from the new stack thread. PSP is restored from register 0. Finally, the new thread is resumed.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',4,3,'2025-07-03_10-34-28','Since the scheduler always chooses the task with the highest priority, tasks with higher priority will always get scheduled first. This means tasks with lower priority will rarely get scheduled and may never run. In this lab, there are two tasks that each run endlessly. If the scheduler only ran one of them that had a higher priority, then the other task would never run.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',4,3,'2025-07-03_13-44-33','Since the scheduler always chooses the task with the highest priority, tasks with higher priority will always get scheduled first. This means tasks with lower priority will rarely get scheduled and may never run. In this lab, there are two tasks that each run endlessly. If the scheduler only ran one of them that had a higher priority, then the other task would never run.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',4,4,'2025-07-03_10-34-28','Calling the C functions is beneficial because C code is much easier to write, debug, and understand due to its greater level of abstraction. Downsides to calling the C functions instead of writing in assembly are that assembly is more efficient for speed and memory usage. Assembly code is also more precise and has full control over registers, which C functions can’t do. That’s why some amount of assembly code is still required, to perform actions that can’t be done in C.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',4,4,'2025-07-03_13-44-33','Calling the C functions is beneficial because C code is much easier to write, debug, and understand due to its greater level of abstraction. Downsides to calling the C functions instead of writing in assembly are that assembly is more efficient for speed and memory usage. Assembly code is also more precise and has full control over registers, which C functions can’t do. That’s why some amount of assembly code is still required, to perform actions that can’t be done in C.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',4,5,'2025-07-03_10-34-28','Yes, the OS would still work. The function osYield() would still be called by the thread function, but then, osSched() would determine that the next thread to switch to is the same as the original thread, ie context switch results in the same thread as before. Because of this, the print_continuously function would continue printing. It would be less efficient than a single thread without a yield, since resources are needed to perform the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',4,5,'2025-07-03_13-44-33','Yes, the OS would still work. The function osYield() would still be called by the thread function, but then, osSched() would determine that the next thread to switch to is the same as the original thread, ie context switch results in the same thread as before. Because of this, the print_continuously function would continue printing. It would be less efficient than a single thread without a yield, since resources are needed to perform the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,1,'2025-07-17_16-59-54','In order to pass an argument to a thread, the user must define a variable containing the data to be passed. Then, the user passes a pointer to that variable to the os in the osCreateThread() function. Thread functions must be written by the user so that they take a void pointer as an argument, and then cast to the desired data type inside the function. The OS receives the function pointer and a pointer to the data as arguments. When the OS switches to a new thread, it loads the current register values onto the new thread’s stack. The OS must load the data pointer onto R0 so that the data is passed to the function when the new thread begins.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,1,'2025-07-19_11-28-40','In order to pass an argument to a thread, the user must define a variable containing the data to be passed. Then, the user passes a pointer to that variable to the os in the osCreateThread() function. Thread functions must be written by the user so that they take a void pointer as an argument, and then cast to the desired data type inside the function. The OS receives the function pointer and a pointer to the data as arguments. When the OS switches to a new thread, it loads the current register values onto the new thread’s stack. The OS must load the data pointer onto R0 so that the data is passed to the function when the new thread begins.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',5,1,'2025-07-19_12-12-53','In order to pass an argument to a thread, the user must define a variable containing the data to be passed. Then, the user passes a pointer to that variable to the os in the osCreateThread() function. Thread functions must be written by the user so that they take a void pointer as an argument, and then cast to the desired data type inside the function. The OS receives the function pointer and a pointer to the data as arguments. When the OS switches to a new thread, it loads the current register values onto the new thread’s stack. The OS must load the data pointer onto R0 so that the data is passed to the function when the new thread begins. Specifically, that is because the function calling convention is to have function arguments on registers 0-3.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,1,'2025-07-19_12-43-01','In order to pass an argument to a thread, the user must define a variable containing the data to be passed. Then, the user passes a pointer to that variable to the os in the osCreateThread() function. Thread functions must be written by the user so that they take a void pointer as an argument, and then cast to the desired data type inside the function. The OS receives the function pointer and a pointer to the data as arguments. When the OS switches to a new thread, it loads the current register values onto the new thread’s stack. The OS must load the data pointer onto R0 so that the data is passed to the function when the new thread begins. Specifically, that is because the function calling convention is to have function arguments on registers 0-3.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,2,'2025-07-17_16-59-54','The function osYield() creates a system call, which is executed by SVC_Handler() in assembly, which calls  SVC_Handler_Main() in C. The system call is to pend an interrupt to do the context switch, which is handled by PendSV_Handler. If osYield() were called within any of those functions, it would create an infinite loop of calling system calls for context switches. This is bad because it’s a poor use of system resources and it does not allow for the thread function to run.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',5,2,'2025-07-19_11-28-40','If osYield() was called inside of any of the other above functions, then it would cause a system call inside of an interrupt. A system call is when the user calls the OS to perform a service and there is a switch from user mode to kernel. However, since this system call is made inside of an interrupt, it is already in kernel mode. This would cause a hardware problem. Calling osYield() is meant to create a context switch, but if it’s called inside of a context switch in progress, what would happen instead is a nested interrupt. Every time an interrupt happens, the current register values are saved to the stack. If many nested interrupts occur, the stack could fill up with register values and overflow, which would cause a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,2,'2025-07-19_12-12-53','If osYield() was called inside of any of the other above functions, then it would cause a system call inside of an interrupt. A system call is when the user calls the OS to perform a service and there is a switch from user mode to kernel. However, since this system call is made inside of an interrupt, it is already in kernel mode. This would cause a hardware problem. Calling osYield() is meant to create a context switch, but if it’s called inside of a context switch in progress, what would happen instead is a nested interrupt. Every time an interrupt happens, the current register values are saved to the stack. If many nested interrupts occur, the stack could fill up with register values and overflow, which would cause a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,2,'2025-07-19_12-43-01','If osYield() was called inside of any of the other above functions, then it would cause a system call inside of an interrupt. A system call is when the user calls the OS to perform a service and there is a switch from user mode to kernel. However, since this system call is made inside of an interrupt, it is already in kernel mode. This would cause a hardware problem. Calling osYield() is meant to create a context switch, but if it’s called inside of a context switch in progress, what would happen instead is a nested interrupt. Every time an interrupt happens, the current register values are saved to the stack. If many nested interrupts occur, the stack could fill up with register values and overflow, which would cause a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,3,'2025-07-17_16-59-54','The benefits of setting the fundamental OS clock to a smaller value are that the OS can detect events that are faster than 1ms (higher clock speed), and that the OS can now respond to events with resolution better than 1ms (better clock resolution). Other benefits are faster execution of instructions and therefore better performance. Downsides include increased power consumption, heat generation, or higher cost.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',5,3,'2025-07-19_11-28-40','The benefits of setting the fundamental OS clock to a smaller value are that the OS can detect events that are faster than 1ms, and that the OS can now respond to events with resolution better than 1ms. A smaller tick period means that for tasks that are performed every tick, more tasks will be completed in the same amount of time. Downsides include increased power consumption, heat generation, or higher cost. Shorter OS tick frequency also means that the OS will handle interrupts more frequently, which can be a drain on resources and potentially decrease efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,3,'2025-07-19_12-12-53','The benefits of setting the fundamental OS clock to a smaller value are that the OS can detect events that are faster than 1ms, and that the OS can now respond to events with resolution better than 1ms. A smaller tick period means that for tasks that are performed every tick, more tasks will be completed in the same amount of time. Downsides include increased power consumption, heat generation, or higher cost. Shorter OS tick frequency also means that the OS will handle interrupts more frequently, which can be a drain on resources and potentially decrease efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,3,'2025-07-19_12-43-01','The benefits of setting the fundamental OS clock to a smaller value are that the OS can detect events that are faster than 1ms, and that the OS can now respond to events with resolution better than 1ms. A smaller tick period means that for tasks that are performed every tick, more tasks will be completed in the same amount of time. Downsides include increased power consumption, heat generation, or higher cost. Shorter OS tick frequency also means that the OS will handle interrupts more frequently, which can be a drain on resources and potentially decrease efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,4,'2025-07-17_16-59-54','In co-operative multitasking, tasks voluntarily give up control to the scheduler. In pre-emptive scheduling, the scheduler allows tasks to run for a certain amount of time and then forcibly interrupts them.\nFrom a user perspective, co-operative multitasking means users must write thread functions such that they yield at a certain point by making a system call. The OS’s job is to respond to the system call and perform the context switch. For pre-emptive multitasking, the user does not have to indicate a point to yield in the thread function. Instead, the OS uses a timer to allow a thread to run for a specified amount of time, and then forcibly interrupts the thread to perform the context switch. In pre-emptive multitasking, the time slice and runtime durations can be chosen by either the user or the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,4,'2025-07-19_11-28-40','In co-operative multitasking, tasks voluntarily give up control to the scheduler. In pre-emptive scheduling, the scheduler allows tasks to run for a certain amount of time and then forcibly interrupts them.\nFrom a user perspective, co-operative multitasking means users must write thread functions such that they yield at a certain point by making a system call. The OS’s job is to respond to the system call and perform the context switch. For pre-emptive multitasking, the user does not have to indicate a point to yield in the thread function. Instead, the OS uses a timer to allow a thread to run for a specified amount of time, and then forcibly interrupts the thread to perform the context switch. In pre-emptive multitasking, the time slice and runtime durations can be chosen by either the user or the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,4,'2025-07-19_12-12-53','In co-operative multitasking, tasks voluntarily give up control to the scheduler. In pre-emptive scheduling, the scheduler allows tasks to run for a certain amount of time and then forcibly interrupts them.\nFrom a user perspective, co-operative multitasking means users must write thread functions such that they yield at a certain point by making a system call. The OS’s job is to respond to the system call and perform the context switch. For pre-emptive multitasking, the user does not have to indicate a point to yield in the thread function. Instead, the OS uses a timer to allow a thread to run for a specified amount of time, and then forcibly interrupts the thread to perform the context switch. In pre-emptive multitasking, the time slice and runtime durations can be chosen by either the user or the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,4,'2025-07-19_12-43-01','In co-operative multitasking, tasks voluntarily give up control to the scheduler. In pre-emptive scheduling, the scheduler allows tasks to run for a certain amount of time and then forcibly interrupts them.\nFrom a user perspective, co-operative multitasking means users must write thread functions such that they yield at a certain point by making a system call. The OS’s job is to respond to the system call and perform the context switch. For pre-emptive multitasking, the user does not have to indicate a point to yield in the thread function. Instead, the OS uses a timer to allow a thread to run for a specified amount of time, and then forcibly interrupts the thread to perform the context switch. In pre-emptive multitasking, the time slice and runtime durations can be chosen by either the user or the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,5,'2025-07-17_16-59-54','These two mechanisms work together because the context switching is the same process for both, they simply have different ways of triggering the context switch. In the case of the yielding thread, the thread function requests a system call to do the context switch. In the case of the time-based thread, the OS allows the thread to run for a predetermined amount of time before Sys_Tick_Handler triggers the the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,5,'2025-07-19_11-28-40','These two mechanisms work together because the context switching is the same process for both, they simply have different ways of triggering the context switch. In the case of the yielding thread, the thread function requests a system call to do the context switch. In the case of the time-based thread, the OS allows the thread to run for a predetermined amount of time before Sys_Tick_Handler triggers the the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('arudolph',5,5,'2025-07-19_12-12-53','These two mechanisms work together because the context switching is the same process for both, they simply have different ways of triggering the context switch. In the case of the yielding thread, the thread function requests a system call to do the context switch. In the case of the time-based thread, the OS allows the thread to run for a predetermined amount of time before Sys_Tick_Handler triggers the the context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('arudolph',5,5,'2025-07-19_12-43-01','These two mechanisms work together because the context switching is the same process for both, they simply have different ways of triggering the context switch. In the case of the yielding thread, the thread function requests a system call to do the context switch. In the case of the time-based thread, the OS allows the thread to run for a predetermined amount of time before Sys_Tick_Handler triggers the the context switch.\nFor the yielding function, the context switch is triggered by the function osYield(), which creates a system call handled by SVC_Handler_Main(). Then, SVC_Handler_Main() pends PendSV_Handler(), which actually performs the context switch. For the time-based thread, Sys_Tick_Handler() pends PendSV_Handler() to perform the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',1,1,'2025-05-14_14-00-02','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of pre-written code libraries provided that makes it easier to interact with a microcontroller’s hardware. For Embedded programming this is used to save time, reduce errors and make code easier to read.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',1,1,'2025-06-23_11-23-23','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a layer of code that sits between the hardware of a microcontroller and the application code. It hides the complicated details of the hardware and gives you a simple, consistent way to use things like timers, GPIO pins, or communication features, so you don’t have to mess around with low-level register code. This means programmers can interact with the hardware using easier, high-level functions. Using the HAL helps make the code more portable and reusable across different microcontrollers, because the same HAL functions often work on different devices with little or no change. For example, if you switch from one STM32 chip to another, your code may still work with minimal adjustments. A real-world scenario where HAL saves time is in developing a prototype quickly. For instance, writing code to blink an LED or send data over UART can be done in minutes using HAL, compared to hours if writing all the low-level configuration by hand. This efficiency is especially useful in team projects or when working with complex peripherals.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',1,2,'2025-05-14_14-00-02','The function is used to print out one character at a time from my guess. It\'s important because when we use printf, it breaks everything down into individual characters, and __io_putchar function is what actually sends each character to the console. Without it, printf wouldn’t know how to display anything.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',1,2,'2025-06-23_11-23-23','The __io_putchar function is used to send out one character at a time, usually over a serial connection like UART. It\'s needed because when we use printf, it breaks the message into characters and calls __io_putchar to actually transmit each one. In microcontrollers like the Cortex M4, the UART hardware handles this serial communication. Since printf doesn’t know by default how to send characters out of the chip, we have to implement __io_putchar ourselves to tell it where to send the data—like to a UART port for displaying messages on a serial terminal. That’s why this function is important in embedded projects and often needs to be customized depending on the hardware setup.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',1,3,'2025-05-14_14-00-02','We used the debugger because in embedded programming, printing with printf isn’t always practical. Some systems don’t support printing. Even when they do, it can be really slow, especially if you\'re trying to print a lot of values. The debugger lets us see what’s going on in real time without slowing down the program.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',1,3,'2025-06-23_11-23-23','We used the debugger in this lab because we needed to check the values of specific hardware registers while the program was running. printf can’t show register values directly, and in many embedded systems, it’s either not available or too slow to use, especially if you need to check lots of values or time-sensitive data. The debugger, on the other hand, lets us pause the program and inspect things like register contents, memory addresses, or variable states in real time. This is really helpful in embedded programming, where we often need to see exactly how the hardware is behaving to make sure things are working correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',1,4,'2025-05-14_14-00-02','We start by getting the initial value of the MSP, which points to the highest value of the stack memory. This is important because the MSP is used by default for things like the kernel or exception handling. To get a new value for the PSP, we subtract a certain amount from the MSP value, this gives the PSP its own space in memory, separate from the MSP. We need to know the original MSP value so we can properly allocate space on the stack for both the MSP and the PSP.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',1,4,'2025-06-23_11-23-23','We started by getting the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which points to the top of the stack memory when the system first starts. This value is important because it tells us where the stack begins in memory. The MSP is typically used by the system for exception handling and the OS kernel, so we don’t want to interfere with it. To create a new Process Stack Pointer (PSP) for our own code to use, we subtract a certain number of bytes from the MSP value. This gives us a new location in memory for the PSP, safely below the MSP, so the two stacks don’t overlap. Knowing the initial MSP value is essential because it helps us place the PSP in a valid, unused section of memory. If we just picked a random address, the PSP could end up overlapping with other data or outside of the stack region, which could lead to crashes or unexpected behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',1,5,'2025-05-14_14-00-02','I would guess we would need to set aside a block of memory large enough to hold all the stacks. Since we know the number of threads at compile-time, we can divide that memory into equal parts. Starting from the initial MSP value, we can subtract a fixed space for the MSP and the rest of the stack memory can be allocated for the threads.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',1,5,'2025-06-23_11-23-23','To allocate multiple stacks for different threads, we’d first need to reserve a large enough block of memory to hold all the stacks. Since the number of threads is known at compile time, we can divide this memory into equal-sized chunks, one for each thread’s stack. Each stack would need to have a valid starting address, which on the Cortex-M4 usually means it must be aligned to an 8-byte boundary. This alignment is important because the processor expects the stack pointer to follow certain rules, and misaligned addresses can lead to faults or crashes. We’d start from the initial MSP value, subtract space for the system stack, and then work downward in memory, assigning each thread a chunk of stack space. To make sure the stacks don’t overlap or interfere with other parts of memory, we’d define the start of our stack memory region in the linker script or make sure it\'s inside a reserved RAM section. We could also check each calculated stack address to confirm it\'s aligned and within valid RAM boundaries.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',2,1,'2025-06-23_19-59-01','In this lab, when I made a function pointer to print_continously() function instead of just calling it right away, it jumped straight into that function using low-level assembly instead of a regular function call. Even though the way we got there was different by manually manually calling the function instead of letting the compiler handle it, the result looked exactly the same. As a result the program printed \"Hello, PC\" in an infinite loop. This showed that both methods can run the same code, but using assembly gives us more direct control over how the program flows.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',2,1,'2025-06-24_14-19-30','In this lab, when I changed the Program Counter (PC) to hold the address of the print_continously() function, I was effectively performing a manual function call using assembly. This works because the PC controls the flow of execution by always pointing to the address of the next instruction to execute. So when I directly loaded the function’s address into the PC, the processor immediately began executing the instructions starting at that function, there’s no delay or intermediate step. Although the behavior looked the same as a regular function call, this approach bypasses the usual call stack mechanism, giving us low-level control over program execution. This demonstrates that modifying the PC directly is functionally equivalent to calling a function, but with more precise, manual control over where execution goes.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',2,2,'2025-06-23_19-59-01','A system call (syscall) is a programmatic interface allowing software to request privileged services from the operating system kernel, such as hardware access or process management. It transitions execution from user space to kernel space via interrupts, leveraging the CPU\'s security model (e.g., ring levels) to safely mediate resource access.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',2,2,'2025-06-24_14-19-30','A system call is a special kind of function call that allows a user-level program to request services from the operating system\'s kernel, such as file operations, memory management, or interacting with hardware. It acts as a controlled entry point from user space into kernel space, ensuring that user programs can access critical system resources without compromising security. This transition typically involves an interrupt or trap instruction, which safely shifts execution to a more privileged mode. By framing system calls as function calls that cross a privilege boundary, we can better understand how they serve as the bridge between application code and low-level OS functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',2,3,'2025-06-23_19-59-01','Setting up a thread\'s stack involves allocating memory and initializing the stack pointer to a dedicated region. This creates a LIFO structure for storing register values during execution. Registers must be saved to the stack during context switches (e.g., procedure calls) to preserve the thread\'s state, preventing data corruption when switching between tasks. For instance, callee-saved registers are pushed onto the stack before a function executes and popped afterward to restore the original state\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',2,3,'2025-06-24_14-19-30','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a dedicated memory region that the thread will use to store data like local variables and register values during its execution. This setup happens before the thread starts running and includes writing initial register values such as the program counter and stack pointer to the stack so that the thread knows where to begin execution and how to manage function calls. This is different from what happens during a context switch, where register values are saved and restored to preserve a thread’s state mid-execution. The order in which values are written to the stack during setup is crucial, if registers aren’t stored and restored properly, the thread won’t resume correctly. So, while both setup and context switching involve registers and the stack, setup is about preparing a clean starting point, whereas context switching is about preserving and restoring ongoing execution.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',2,4,'2025-06-23_19-59-01','If the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) isn\'t set via __set_PSP() before thread execution, the CPU would attempt to use an uninitialized stack pointer. This causes a hard fault because the processor lacks valid memory for storing return addresses, local variables, or register states during operations, leading to undefined behavior or crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',2,4,'2025-06-24_14-19-30','On the Cortex-M4, the CPU uses two stack pointers: the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is typically used for handling exceptions and OS-level code, and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is used for running application-level threads. When starting a new thread, it’s essential to switch to the PSP and set it correctly using __set_PSP(). If we don’t do this, the CPU continues using the MSP or an uninitialized PSP, and when the thread begins execution, especially if it tries to push registers or handle a function call, the stack operations access invalid memory. This leads to a hard fault because the processor attempts to read or write to memory it shouldn\'t. Setting the PSP ensures the thread has a valid, isolated stack space to use, which is crucial for correct and safe thread execution.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',2,5,'2025-06-23_19-59-01','If a thread function returned instead of looping, the thread would terminate after its first execution. In systems like POSIX threads, this could cause resource leaks or undefined behavior (e.g., double-printing due to race conditions). In embedded RTOS environments (e.g., mbed OS), the thread would transition to an inactive state, halting further execution and potentially freezing dependent processes.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',2,5,'2025-06-24_14-19-30','In the context of the Cortex-M4, if a thread function returns without looping, the processor expects to pop a valid return address from the stack to continue execution. However, in our setup, the thread’s stack was manually crafted and likely does not contain a meaningful return address. This means when the function tries to return, the CPU fetches a bogus address from the stack, leading to undefined behavior and usually a hard fault. Unlike higher-level OS environments where threads can gracefully exit or be cleaned up, microcontrollers like the Cortex-M4 rely heavily on proper stack setup and control flow.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',3,1,'2025-06-26_20-14-27','The compilation of multiple .c files into a single executable involves preprocessing, compilation, assembly, and linking. Preprocessing handles directives like #include, expanding macros and including headers. Each .c file is compiled independently into an object file (.o), containing machine code and symbols. The linker combines these object files, resolves symbolic references, and merges code sections into a single executable\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',3,1,'2025-06-27_11-09-33','The process of compiling multiple .c files into a single executable follows a three-step sequence: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. In preprocessing, directives like #include and macros are handled. Then, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file containing machine code and unresolved symbols. Finally, the linker combines these object files, resolves symbol references between them, and produces a single executable.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',3,1,'2025-06-27_12-12-41','When multiple .c files are compiled into one executable, each file is first processed by the preprocessor, which replaces #include directives with the actual contents of header files and substitutes macros with their defined values. After this, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file containing machine code, but with unresolved symbols for functions or variables used from other files. These unresolved references are handled during the linking stage, where the linker combines all object files and matches those references with their correct definitions. If a symbol isn’t found during this stage, say, if you forgot to include a file where a function is defined, the linker throws an error. This whole process allows us to split code across multiple files, making it more organized, modular, and easier to maintain. Linking makes this separation possible by resolving the interdependencies among files, so the final program behaves as if it came from one large file.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',3,2,'2025-06-26_20-14-27','Including .c files via #include (e.g., #include <kernel.c>) violates modular design principles. It causes multiple definitions when the included .c file is linked across different compilation units, leading to errors. Header files (.h) should declare interfaces (e.g., function prototypes), while .c files define implementations. This separation ensures safe reuse without duplication.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',3,2,'2025-06-27_11-09-33','Using #include on a .c file, like #include <kernel.c>, is a bad practice because it bypasses the proper compilation process and can lead to multiple definition errors during linking. When a .c file is included this way in multiple translation units, its functions and variables are redefined each time, causing the linker to throw \"multiple definition\" errors. This breaks the modular design where .h files declare interfaces and .c files define implementations. Instead, each .c file should be compiled separately, and the linker should resolve their references to avoid redundancy and ensure clean builds.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',3,2,'2025-06-27_12-12-41','Using #include on a .c file, like #include <kernel.c>, is a bad practice because it bypasses the proper compilation process and can lead to multiple definition errors during linking. When a .c file is included this way in multiple translation units, its functions and variables are redefined each time, causing the linker to throw \"multiple definition\" errors. This breaks the modular design where .h files declare interfaces and .c files define implementations. Instead, each .c file should be compiled separately, and the linker should resolve their references to avoid redundancy and ensure clean builds.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',3,3,'2025-06-26_20-14-27','To set up a thread, you start by defining a function that contains the code the thread will run. If needed, you can also set attributes like stack size or priority to customize the thread’s behavior. Then, you create the thread using a thread creation API like pthread_create(), passing in the function and any attributes. Once the thread is created, the operating system takes over and schedules it to run alongside other threads. If you need to wait for the thread to finish before moving on, you can use synchronization tools like pthread_join() to pause execution until the thread completes.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',3,3,'2025-06-27_11-09-33','To set up and start a thread, you first define the function the thread will execute. Next, you may configure attributes like stack size or priority depending on the system. Once ready, you request the kernel to create and register the thread using a thread creation function provided by your environment (such as pthread_create or an RTOS-specific API). The kernel is responsible for initializing the thread\'s context, assigning system resources, and adding it to the scheduler. After registration, the kernel starts the thread and manages its execution alongside others.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',3,3,'2025-06-27_12-12-41','To set up and start a thread, you begin by initializing the kernel or threading system—this step is essential before any threads can be created, as it sets up the necessary environment and system resources. Then, you define the function that each thread will run and, if needed, configure attributes like stack size or priority. Once everything is set, you create and register each thread using a provided thread creation function, which asks the kernel to allocate resources and prepare the thread\'s execution context. After all threads are created and registered, you must explicitly start the kernel\'s scheduler, which begins running the threads based on the system\'s scheduling policy. Without starting the scheduler, the threads will remain inactive, so this final step is crucial to get the system running.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',3,4,'2025-06-26_20-14-27','A stack pool pre-allocates fixed-size memory blocks for thread stacks. In future labs, it enables multi-threading by assigning each new thread a dedicated stack from this pool during creation. This eliminates runtime allocation overhead, guarantees memory isolation (preventing stack overflows from corrupting other threads), and ensures deterministic memory usage for real-time systems.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',3,4,'2025-06-27_11-09-33','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory made up of fixed-size blocks, each intended to serve as a stack for an individual thread. In future labs, when we start creating multiple threads, the OS can quickly assign one of these pre-allocated stacks to each thread during its creation. This avoids the need to allocate memory at runtime, which can be unpredictable or inefficient, especially in real-time systems. It also helps keep threads isolated, if one overflows its stack, it won’t interfere with others—\\, making the system more stable and easier to manage.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',3,4,'2025-06-27_12-12-41','The stack pool is a dedicated region of memory divided into fixed-size blocks, with each block serving as the stack for a separate thread. When our OS starts supporting multiple threads, it can assign one of these pre-allocated stacks to each thread during creation, avoiding the overhead and unpredictability of allocating memory dynamically at runtime. This is especially useful in systems where performance and timing are critical. In addition to thread stacks, part of the stack pool is typically reserved for the operating system itself, this might include a separate stack for handling exceptions or kernel-level operations. By organizing memory this way, we ensure that both the OS and user-level threads have isolated, predictable memory spaces, which improves stability, helps catch bugs like stack overflows, and makes context switching between threads more straightforward and reliable.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',3,5,'2025-06-26_20-14-27','User-space files contain application logic running with restricted privileges, while kernel-space files handle privileged operations like hardware access and process scheduling. This separation is critical for users: it enforces security (preventing apps from crashing the OS), ensures stability (isolating app failures), and enables fair resource management by the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',3,5,'2025-06-27_11-09-33','The distinction between user and kernel space lies in both access level and file organization: user-space files contain application code that runs with limited privileges, while kernel-space files handle low-level operations like hardware control, memory management, and scheduling. From the user\'s perspective, this separation is important because it simplifies application development, users interact with the kernel through defined APIs or system calls, without needing to manage hardware or low-level resources directly. This not only improves productivity and abstraction but also enhances security and stability, as faults in user programs can’t crash the entire system.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',3,5,'2025-06-27_12-12-41','The distinction between user and kernel space is not just about access level, but also about who writes what. In our OS setup, the user is responsible for writing the application logic. Specifically, the threads that run in user space. These user-space files are written with limited privileges and focus on the actual behavior or functionality of the application, without needing to worry about low-level operations. On the other hand, kernel-space files are written as part of the OS itself and handle critical system-level tasks like scheduling, memory management, and hardware interaction. This separation is important because it keeps the complexity of the OS hidden from the user, letting them focus on building applications rather than dealing with low-level code. It also keeps the system more secure and stable, since bugs in user threads won’t affect the entire OS.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,1,'2025-07-23_17-05-03','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by treating ready tasks in a FIFO (First In, First Out) manner where the scheduler dequeues the task at the front to run it, and when the task\'s time slice expires or it yields, the scheduler enqueues it at the back of the queue. The main benefits of using a queue over an array include natural implementation of the round-robin concept without needing index management or wraparound logic, more efficient dynamic task management, no fixed size limitations since queues can grow dynamically, cleaner and less error-prone code with intuitive enqueue/dequeue operations, and automatic maintenance of scheduling order without additional bookkeeping variables like current task indices.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',4,1,'2025-07-23_17-27-24','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by dequeuing the front task to run and enqueuing it at the back when its time slice expires, maintaining FIFO order among ready tasks. However, this offers limited benefits over an array-based scheduler that already cycles through tasks in order, as both achieve the same round-robin behavior. Using a queue may actually introduce unnecessary overhead through dynamic memory allocation and pointer operations, while an array with simple index management provides equivalent scheduling fairness more efficiently.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,1,'2025-07-23_17-42-38','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by dequeuing the front task to run and enqueuing it at the back when its time slice expires, maintaining FIFO order among ready tasks. However, this offers limited benefits over an array-based scheduler that already cycles through tasks in order, as both achieve the same round-robin behavior. Using a queue may actually introduce unnecessary overhead through dynamic memory allocation and pointer operations, while an array with simple index management provides equivalent scheduling fairness more efficiently.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,2,'2025-07-23_17-05-03','The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch by first saving the current task\'s complete processor state, then storing the current stack pointer in the current task\'s TCB, calling the scheduler function osSched() to determine the next task to run, loading the new task\'s stack pointer from its TCB, restoring the new task\'s manually-saved registers from its stack, and finally allowing the hardware to automatically restore the remaining registers when returning from the interrupt, effectively resuming execution where the new task left off.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',4,2,'2025-07-23_17-27-24','The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch by first manually saving the current task\'s context registers (R4-R11) onto its stack since the Cortex-M hardware automatically saves R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, and PSR upon interrupt entry. It then stores the current stack pointer (PSP) in the current task\'s TCB to preserve the task\'s stack state. Next, it calls the scheduler function osSched() to determine which task should run next based on the scheduling algorithm. The handler then loads the new task\'s stack pointer from its TCB and restores the new task\'s manually-saved registers (R4-R11) from its stack. Finally, when the PendSV handler returns using the special LR value (EXC_RETURN), the Cortex-M hardware automatically restores the remaining registers (R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, PSR) and switches to the new task\'s Program Stack Pointer (PSP), effectively resuming execution where the new task previously left off.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',4,2,'2025-07-23_17-42-38','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first accessing the current task\'s Process Stack Pointer (PSP) and manually pushing registers R4–R11 onto the stack, since only R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR are automatically saved by hardware on exception entry. It then updates the current task’s TCB with the new PSP value to save its context. The scheduler function osSched() is called to select the next ready task. The PSP of the selected task is loaded from its TCB, and registers R4–R11 are restored from its stack. Finally, the handler exits using a special EXC_RETURN value in the LR, prompting the processor to automatically pop the remaining registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) from the stack and resume execution of the new task using its PSP, thus completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,3,'2025-07-23_17-05-03','This priority-based scheduling approach is problematic because it leads to priority starvation where lower priority tasks may never receive CPU time if higher priority tasks are always ready to run, causing system unfairness and potential deadlocks through priority inversion scenarios. Additionally, in embedded systems, even \"low priority\" tasks often have real-time requirements that must be met, and a monopolizing high-priority task could cause critical data loss or system failures. Round-robin scheduling is superior in this context because it ensures fairness by giving each task equal time slices, making system behavior predictable and preventing any task from being starved of processor time.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',4,3,'2025-07-23_17-27-24','This priority-based scheduling approach is problematic because the scheduler will select the highest priority task and then never switch away from it, effectively causing that single task to monopolize the CPU indefinitely. Since our current system lacks preemption mechanisms or time slicing, once the highest priority task is chosen, it will continue running until it voluntarily yields or terminates, meaning all other tasks (regardless of their importance) will never get a chance to execute. This creates a system where only one task ever runs, completely defeating the purpose of having a multitasking operating system and potentially causing critical system functions handled by lower priority tasks to never execute, leading to system failure or unresponsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,3,'2025-07-23_17-42-38','This priority-based scheduling approach is problematic because the scheduler will select the highest priority task and then never switch away from it, effectively causing that single task to monopolize the CPU indefinitely. Since our current system lacks preemption mechanisms or time slicing, once the highest priority task is chosen, it will continue running until it voluntarily yields or terminates, meaning all other tasks (regardless of their importance) will never get a chance to execute. This creates a system where only one task ever runs, completely defeating the purpose of having a multitasking operating system and potentially causing critical system functions handled by lower priority tasks to never execute, leading to system failure or unresponsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,4,'2025-07-23_17-05-03','Using C functions provides significant benefits including improved code maintainability and readability, faster development and debugging cycles, better portability across different architectures, compiler optimizations that often exceed hand-written assembly performance, reduced probability of programming errors through type checking and structured programming, and increased team accessibility since most developers are more comfortable with C than assembly. However, the downsides include performance overhead from function calls, increased stack usage which is critical in memory-constrained embedded systems, less precise control over register usage and instruction timing, potentially larger code size, and increased interrupt latency that may affect real-time performance requirements. The hybrid approach used (assembly for critical context switching, C for higher-level logic) strikes an optimal balance between performance and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,4,'2025-07-23_17-27-24','Using C functions provides significant benefits including improved code maintainability and readability, faster development and debugging cycles, better portability across different architectures, compiler optimizations that often exceed hand-written assembly performance, reduced probability of programming errors through type checking and structured programming, and increased team accessibility since most developers are more comfortable with C than assembly. However, the downsides include performance overhead from function calls, increased stack usage which is critical in memory-constrained embedded systems, less precise control over register usage and instruction timing, potentially larger code size, and increased interrupt latency that may affect real-time performance requirements. The hybrid approach used (assembly for critical context switching, C for higher-level logic) strikes an optimal balance between performance and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,4,'2025-07-23_17-42-38','Using C functions provides significant benefits including improved code maintainability and readability, faster development and debugging cycles, better portability across different architectures, compiler optimizations that often exceed hand-written assembly performance, reduced probability of programming errors through type checking and structured programming, and increased team accessibility since most developers are more comfortable with C than assembly. However, the downsides include performance overhead from function calls, increased stack usage which is critical in memory-constrained embedded systems, less precise control over register usage and instruction timing, potentially larger code size, and increased interrupt latency that may affect real-time performance requirements. The hybrid approach used (assembly for critical context switching, C for higher-level logic) strikes an optimal balance between performance and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,5,'2025-07-23_17-05-03','Yes, the OS will still work but with significant inefficiencies and limitations, the single thread will run, print, yield control to the scheduler, and be immediately rescheduled since it\'s the only available task, creating a continuous cycle. However, this approach wastes substantial resources through unnecessary context switch overhead, scheduler overhead, and eliminates all benefits of multitasking such as concurrent execution, system responsiveness, and efficient resource utilization. While technically functional, this defeats the entire purpose of having an operating system, as it retains all the overhead costs while providing none of the concurrency benefits that justify the complexity of an OS in the first place.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,5,'2025-07-23_17-27-24','Yes, the OS will still work but with significant inefficiencies and limitations, the single thread will run, print, yield control to the scheduler, and be immediately rescheduled since it\'s the only available task, creating a continuous cycle. However, this approach wastes substantial resources through unnecessary context switch overhead, scheduler overhead, and eliminates all benefits of multitasking such as concurrent execution, system responsiveness, and efficient resource utilization. While technically functional, this defeats the entire purpose of having an operating system, as it retains all the overhead costs while providing none of the concurrency benefits that justify the complexity of an OS in the first place.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',4,5,'2025-07-23_17-42-38','Yes, the OS will still work but with significant inefficiencies and limitations, the single thread will run, print, yield control to the scheduler, and be immediately rescheduled since it\'s the only available task, creating a continuous cycle. However, this approach wastes substantial resources through unnecessary context switch overhead, scheduler overhead, and eliminates all benefits of multitasking such as concurrent execution, system responsiveness, and efficient resource utilization. While technically functional, this defeats the entire purpose of having an operating system, as it retains all the overhead costs while providing none of the concurrency benefits that justify the complexity of an OS in the first place.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,1,'2025-07-26_17-51-49','When a user creates a thread, they typically call a thread creation function (like pthread_create or a custom OS function) and pass a pointer to the argument data as one of the parameters. The OS then allocates a new stack for the thread and sets up the thread\'s initial context, including placing the argument pointer in the appropriate register (typically a parameter register like R0) or on the stack depending on the calling convention. When the thread begins execution, the thread function receives this argument pointer as its parameter because the processor\'s calling convention ensures that function parameters are passed through specific registers or stack locations. This works because the OS essentially \"fakes\" a function call by setting up the processor state as if the thread function was called normally with the argument, allowing the thread to seamlessly access its parameters when it starts running.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',5,1,'2025-07-26_20-31-26','When a user wants to pass an argument to a thread, they call osCreateThread, explicitly providing a function pointer and a void *argument. The OS is responsible for creating a new thread control block, allocating a stack for the thread, and initializing the thread’s context. On the Cortex-M4, which follows the ARM EABI (Embedded Application Binary Interface), the first argument to a function is passed in register R0. The OS sets up the initial processor state such that when the thread is scheduled to run, the program counter (PC) points to the thread function, the stack pointer (SP) points to the new stack, and R0 contains the pointer to the user-supplied argument. This setup effectively mimics a regular function call, allowing the thread function to receive and use the argument as expected. The distinction between a calling convention and an ABI is important here: while a calling convention is a set of rules for how functions receive parameters, an ABI like the ARM EABI encompasses not just calling conventions but also stack layout and register usage, critical for low-level context setup during thread creation.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,1,'2025-07-26_20-55-21','When a user wants to pass an argument to a thread, they call osCreateThread, explicitly providing a function pointer and a void *argument. The OS is responsible for creating a new thread control block, allocating a stack for the thread, and initializing the thread’s context. On the Cortex-M4, which follows the ARM EABI (Embedded Application Binary Interface), the first argument to a function is passed in register R0. The OS sets up the initial processor state such that when the thread is scheduled to run, the program counter (PC) points to the thread function, the stack pointer (SP) points to the new stack, and R0 contains the pointer to the user-supplied argument. This setup effectively mimics a regular function call, allowing the thread function to receive and use the argument as expected. The distinction between a calling convention and an ABI is important here: while a calling convention is a set of rules for how functions receive parameters, an ABI like the ARM EABI encompasses not just calling conventions but also stack layout and register usage, critical for low-level context setup during thread creation.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,2,'2025-07-26_17-51-49','You cannot call osYield in context-switching functions because these handlers are already in the middle of performing a context switch, and calling osYield would attempt to trigger another context switch while one is already in progress. If you try to call osYield from these handlers, it would likely trigger a supervisor call (SVC) or set a PendSV exception, but since the system is already handling an exception with elevated privilege, this creates a nested exception scenario that can lead to stack corruption, infinite recursion, or system deadlock. This is bad because the context-switching mechanism relies on atomic operations and consistent system state - interrupting a context switch with another context switch breaks these assumptions and can corrupt thread stacks, processor state, or the scheduler\'s internal data structures, potentially causing the entire system to crash or behave unpredictably.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,2,'2025-07-26_20-31-26','You cannot call osYield in context-switching functions because these handlers are already in the middle of performing a context switch, and calling osYield would attempt to trigger another context switch while one is already in progress. If you try to call osYield from these handlers, it would likely trigger a supervisor call (SVC) or set a PendSV exception, but since the system is already handling an exception with elevated privilege, this creates a nested exception scenario that can lead to stack corruption, infinite recursion, or system deadlock. This is bad because the context-switching mechanism relies on atomic operations and consistent system state - interrupting a context switch with another context switch breaks these assumptions and can corrupt thread stacks, processor state, or the scheduler\'s internal data structures, potentially causing the entire system to crash or behave unpredictably.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,2,'2025-07-26_20-55-21','You cannot call osYield in context-switching functions because these handlers are already in the middle of performing a context switch, and calling osYield would attempt to trigger another context switch while one is already in progress. If you try to call osYield from these handlers, it would likely trigger a supervisor call (SVC) or set a PendSV exception, but since the system is already handling an exception with elevated privilege, this creates a nested exception scenario that can lead to stack corruption, infinite recursion, or system deadlock. This is bad because the context-switching mechanism relies on atomic operations and consistent system state - interrupting a context switch with another context switch breaks these assumptions and can corrupt thread stacks, processor state, or the scheduler\'s internal data structures, potentially causing the entire system to crash or behave unpredictably.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,3,'2025-07-26_17-51-49','Reducing the OS clock period from 1ms to 0.1ms would provide benefits including much finer time resolution for scheduling decisions, more responsive real-time behavior, better support for time-critical applications, and more precise timing for operations that require sub-millisecond accuracy. However, the downsides would be significant: the system would experience 10 times more timer interrupts, dramatically increasing CPU overhead spent on interrupt handling and context switching, which reduces the time available for actual application work. This higher interrupt frequency would also increase power consumption (critical for battery-powered devices), potentially cause priority inversion issues if interrupt handling takes too long, and could overwhelm slower processors that cannot handle the increased interrupt load efficiently. The trade-off between timing precision and system performance means that 0.1ms timing is only justified in hard real-time systems where the precision requirements outweigh the performance costs.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,3,'2025-07-26_20-31-26','Reducing the OS clock period from 1ms to 0.1ms would provide benefits including much finer time resolution for scheduling decisions, more responsive real-time behavior, better support for time-critical applications, and more precise timing for operations that require sub-millisecond accuracy. However, the downsides would be significant: the system would experience 10 times more timer interrupts, dramatically increasing CPU overhead spent on interrupt handling and context switching, which reduces the time available for actual application work. This higher interrupt frequency would also increase power consumption (critical for battery-powered devices), potentially cause priority inversion issues if interrupt handling takes too long, and could overwhelm slower processors that cannot handle the increased interrupt load efficiently. The trade-off between timing precision and system performance means that 0.1ms timing is only justified in hard real-time systems where the precision requirements outweigh the performance costs.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,3,'2025-07-26_20-55-21','Reducing the OS clock period from 1ms to 0.1ms would provide benefits including much finer time resolution for scheduling decisions, more responsive real-time behavior, better support for time-critical applications, and more precise timing for operations that require sub-millisecond accuracy. However, the downsides would be significant: the system would experience 10 times more timer interrupts, dramatically increasing CPU overhead spent on interrupt handling and context switching, which reduces the time available for actual application work. This higher interrupt frequency would also increase power consumption (critical for battery-powered devices), potentially cause priority inversion issues if interrupt handling takes too long, and could overwhelm slower processors that cannot handle the increased interrupt load efficiently. The trade-off between timing precision and system performance means that 0.1ms timing is only justified in hard real-time systems where the precision requirements outweigh the performance costs.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,4,'2025-07-26_17-51-49','From the user perspective, cooperative multitasking requires applications to voluntarily yield control by calling functions like osYield(), meaning that a poorly written or infinite-loop application can freeze the entire system, while preemptive multitasking automatically switches between tasks regardless of application behavior, providing better system responsiveness and preventing any single application from monopolizing the CPU. From the OS perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to implement as the OS only needs to provide yield functions and maintain a task queue, with context switches occurring only when applications explicitly request them, whereas preemptive multitasking requires the OS to set up timer interrupts (like SysTick), implement interrupt handlers that can forcibly interrupt running tasks, save and restore complete processor contexts, and manage more complex scheduling algorithms to decide which task runs next. Preemptive multitasking demands significantly more OS infrastructure but provides better system stability and fairness, while cooperative multitasking relies on application developers to be well-behaved but offers lower overhead and simpler implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,4,'2025-07-26_20-31-26','From the user perspective, cooperative multitasking requires applications to voluntarily yield control by calling functions like osYield(), meaning that a poorly written or infinite-loop application can freeze the entire system, while preemptive multitasking automatically switches between tasks regardless of application behavior, providing better system responsiveness and preventing any single application from monopolizing the CPU. From the OS perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to implement as the OS only needs to provide yield functions and maintain a task queue, with context switches occurring only when applications explicitly request them, whereas preemptive multitasking requires the OS to set up timer interrupts (like SysTick), implement interrupt handlers that can forcibly interrupt running tasks, save and restore complete processor contexts, and manage more complex scheduling algorithms to decide which task runs next. Preemptive multitasking demands significantly more OS infrastructure but provides better system stability and fairness, while cooperative multitasking relies on application developers to be well-behaved but offers lower overhead and simpler implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,4,'2025-07-26_20-55-21','From the user perspective, cooperative multitasking requires applications to voluntarily yield control by calling functions like osYield(), meaning that a poorly written or infinite-loop application can freeze the entire system, while preemptive multitasking automatically switches between tasks regardless of application behavior, providing better system responsiveness and preventing any single application from monopolizing the CPU. From the OS perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to implement as the OS only needs to provide yield functions and maintain a task queue, with context switches occurring only when applications explicitly request them, whereas preemptive multitasking requires the OS to set up timer interrupts (like SysTick), implement interrupt handlers that can forcibly interrupt running tasks, save and restore complete processor contexts, and manage more complex scheduling algorithms to decide which task runs next. Preemptive multitasking demands significantly more OS infrastructure but provides better system stability and fairness, while cooperative multitasking relies on application developers to be well-behaved but offers lower overhead and simpler implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,5,'2025-07-26_17-51-49','Yielding and time-based preemptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately trigger the same underlying context-switching mechanism through different paths - osYield() generates a software interrupt (SVC) that calls the scheduler, while the timer generates a hardware interrupt (SysTick) that also calls the scheduler, and both mechanisms converge on the same scheduler function that decides which thread should run next. The key is that both mechanisms are non-interfering: a thread can voluntarily yield before its time slice expires (cooperative behavior), or if it doesn\'t yield, the timer will eventually force a context switch (preemptive behavior), ensuring that the system maintains responsiveness regardless of thread behavior. This hybrid approach combines the efficiency of cooperative multitasking (threads can yield when they\'re naturally at good stopping points) with the reliability of preemptive multitasking (preventing any thread from monopolizing the CPU), creating a robust system where well-behaved threads can optimize their own context switches while poorly behaved threads are automatically managed by the timer-based preemption.\n',1,'s25'),('as8khan',5,5,'2025-07-26_20-31-26','Yielding and time-based preemptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately trigger the same underlying context-switching mechanism through different paths - osYield() generates a software interrupt (SVC) that calls the scheduler, while the timer generates a hardware interrupt (SysTick) that also calls the scheduler, and both mechanisms converge on the same scheduler function that decides which thread should run next. The key is that both mechanisms are non-interfering: a thread can voluntarily yield before its time slice expires (cooperative behavior), or if it doesn\'t yield, the timer will eventually force a context switch (preemptive behavior), ensuring that the system maintains responsiveness regardless of thread behavior. This hybrid approach combines the efficiency of cooperative multitasking (threads can yield when they\'re naturally at good stopping points) with the reliability of preemptive multitasking (preventing any thread from monopolizing the CPU), creating a robust system where well-behaved threads can optimize their own context switches while poorly behaved threads are automatically managed by the timer-based preemption.\n',0,'s25'),('as8khan',5,5,'2025-07-26_20-55-21','Yielding and time-based preemptive context switching work together by both ultimately triggering the same deferred context-switching mechanism through the PendSV handler. When a thread calls osYield(), it triggers a software interrupt (SVC), and when the time slice expires, the SysTick timer interrupt fires, both cases result in the scheduler determining that a context switch is needed. However, neither SVC nor SysTick performs the context switch directly; instead, they set the PendSV exception as pending. The PendSV handler is the dedicated interrupt used on Cortex-M4 systems to perform the actual context switch because it has the lowest priority and is safely deferred until all higher-priority interrupts are complete. This design allows both cooperative (via osYield()) and preemptive (via SysTick) mechanisms to work harmoniously: they can request a context switch, and the PendSV handler ensures the switch happens at a safe and controlled point. By centralizing the context-switch logic in PendSV, the OS ensures consistency and avoids conflicts between different scheduling triggers.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,1,'2025-05-13_11-22-32','Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) offers a simple way to work with STM32 microcontrollers. Instead of having to deal with low-level code, developers can use HAL’s high-level functions to save time and make code easier to read. It allows projects to work across different STM32 boards, letting developers concentrate on their application logic instead of hardware details.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,1,'2025-05-20_15-21-35','Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) offers a simple way to work with STM32 microcontrollers. Instead of having to deal with low-level code, developers can use HAL’s high-level functions to save time and make code easier to read. It allows projects to work across different STM32 boards, letting developers concentrate on their application logic instead of hardware details.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,1,'2025-05-20_15-56-24','Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) offers a simple way to work with STM32 microcontrollers. Instead of having to deal with low-level code, developers can use HAL’s high-level functions to save time and make code easier to read. It allows projects to work across different STM32 boards, letting developers concentrate on their application logic instead of hardware details.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,2,'2025-05-13_11-22-32','The __io_putchar function sends characters, like those from printf, to a specific output device like a serial port. Since printf on microcontrollers doesn\'t automatically know where to send its output, __io_putchar acts as a bridge sending it to the right place. This setup is useful for seeing debug messages or other output through a serial terminal.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',1,2,'2025-05-20_15-21-35','The __io_putchar function directs output, like printf, to the right hardware interface (such as UART) on a microcontroller. Since microcontrollers don’t have a default output, different projects may use USB, UART, or semihosting. Implementing __io_putchar allows developers to send output via the appropriate interface, making printf more flexible and portable. Without it, printf might not work or would be fixed to a single output, limiting adaptability. This function enables high-level debug output across various embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,2,'2025-05-20_15-56-24','The __io_putchar function directs output, like printf, to the right hardware interface (such as UART) on a microcontroller. Since microcontrollers don’t have a default output, different projects may use USB, UART, or semihosting. Implementing __io_putchar allows developers to send output via the appropriate interface, making printf more flexible and portable. Without it, printf might not work or would be fixed to a single output, limiting adaptability. This function enables high-level debug output across various embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,3,'2025-05-13_11-22-32','We needed to use the debugger here because printf alone doesn’t show us everything the microcontroller is doing, especially with low-level parts like stack pointers (MSP and PSP). Debuggers let us pause the program and step through code. This helps us understand the system and catch issues like stack problems or weird control flow, which printf can’t easily show. While printf is good for simple outputs, it’s not enough for detailed debugging or working with processor features.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',1,3,'2025-05-20_15-21-35','We used the debugger in this lab because it lets us see what\'s happening inside the processor. It shows registers like R0–R12, LR, PC, and stack pointers (MSP and PSP), which is super helpful for things like context switching and checking stack usage. While printf is good for quick output, it can\'t show register values or track the program\'s flow when it’s not running or crashes early. The debugger gives us real-time info on the processor\'s state, making it easier to spot tricky bugs that printf can’t catch.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,3,'2025-05-20_15-56-24','We used the debugger in this lab because it lets us see what\'s happening inside the processor. It shows registers like R0–R12, LR, PC, and stack pointers (MSP and PSP), which is super helpful for things like context switching and checking stack usage. While printf is good for quick output, it can\'t show register values or track the program\'s flow when it’s not running or crashes early. The debugger gives us real-time info on the processor\'s state, making it easier to spot tricky bugs that printf can’t catch.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,4,'2025-05-13_11-22-32','Our stack allocation method involves manually dividing memory between the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). We start by getting the initial MSP value, usually set at the top of RAM during startup. This gives us the starting point for the main stack, called MSP_init_val. To make space for the PSP, we subtract a fixed amount from MSP_init_val, which sets the starting point of the process stack below the main stack. Knowing MSP\'s initial position is key because it marks the top of the stack memory. Without this reference, we couldn\'t safely assign space for the PSP, risking overlap and memory issues. Using MSP_init_val as a baseline helps us allocate both stacks properly and prevent conflicts.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,4,'2025-05-20_15-21-35','Our stack allocation method involves manually dividing memory between the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). We start by getting the initial MSP value, usually set at the top of RAM during startup. This gives us the starting point for the main stack, called MSP_init_val. To make space for the PSP, we subtract a fixed amount from MSP_init_val, which sets the starting point of the process stack below the main stack. Knowing MSP\'s initial position is key because it marks the top of the stack memory. Without this reference, we couldn\'t safely assign space for the PSP, risking overlap and memory issues. Using MSP_init_val as a baseline helps us allocate both stacks properly and prevent conflicts.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,4,'2025-05-20_15-56-24','Our stack allocation method involves manually dividing memory between the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). We start by getting the initial MSP value, usually set at the top of RAM during startup. This gives us the starting point for the main stack, called MSP_init_val. To make space for the PSP, we subtract a fixed amount from MSP_init_val, which sets the starting point of the process stack below the main stack. Knowing MSP\'s initial position is key because it marks the top of the stack memory. Without this reference, we couldn\'t safely assign space for the PSP, risking overlap and memory issues. Using MSP_init_val as a baseline helps us allocate both stacks properly and prevent conflicts.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,5,'2025-05-13_11-22-32','To assign multiple stacks to threads using the same approach, start with MSP_init_val and divide it into equal parts based on the number of threads and stack size. For each thread, we can determine its start address by subtracting (thread_index + 1) times the stack size from MSP_init_val. Store these addresses in an array or separate variables, ensuring each thread has its own non-overlapping stack space.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',1,5,'2025-05-20_15-21-35','To assign multiple stacks to threads using the same approach, start with MSP_init_val and divide it into equal parts based on the number of threads and stack size. For each thread, we can determine its start address by subtracting (thread_index + 1) times the stack size from MSP_init_val. Store these addresses in an array or separate variables, ensuring each thread has its own non-overlapping stack space.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',1,5,'2025-05-20_15-56-24','When assigning multiple stacks to threads, especially when the number of threads is determined at compile-time, a common approach is to divide the memory starting from the initial main stack pointer value, MSP_init_val, into equal segments—each allocated to a different thread. To do this, we calculate each stack\'s start address by subtracting (thread_index + 1) * stack_size from MSP_init_val. This guarantees that stacks are lined up sequentially in memory, growing downward, which aligns naturally with how ARM Cortex-M processors manage stacks. Keep in mind, though, that these stack addresses need to be properly aligned—specifically, 8-byte aligned—to meet the ARM Cortex-M4 architecture\'s requirements. Proper alignment is critical for correct handling of exceptions and smooth context switching. Also, you should verify that the total amount of memory allocated for all thread stacks fits within your available RAM space and doesn\'t interfere with other memory areas like the heap or data regions. Paying attention to these details helps prevent runtime issues and keeps your multithreaded operations reliable and predictable.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,1,'2025-06-01_17-07-00','When we manually set the Program Counter (PC) register to the address of the print_continuously function, the processor began executing that function as if it were the next instruction in sequence. This works because the processor relies on the PC to determine which instruction to run. By directly assigning the PC to the address of our function, we effectively redirected execution to it. As a result, our code continuously prints \'Hello, PC!\' in an infinite loop without explicitly calling the function from main.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,1,'2025-06-01_17-16-37','When we manually set the Program Counter (PC) register to the address of the print_continuously function, the processor began executing that function as if it were the next instruction in sequence. This works because the processor relies on the PC to determine which instruction to run. By directly assigning the PC to the address of our function, we effectively redirected execution to it. As a result, our code continuously prints \'Hello, PC!\' in an infinite loop without explicitly calling the function from main.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,1,'2025-06-01_17-24-39','When we manually set the Program Counter (PC) register to the address of the print_continuously function, the processor began executing that function as if it were the next instruction in sequence. This works because the processor relies on the PC to determine which instruction to run. By directly assigning the PC to the address of our function, we effectively redirected execution to it. As a result, our code continuously prints \'Hello, PC!\' in an infinite loop without explicitly calling the function from main.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,1,'2025-06-01_17-31-45','When we manually set the Program Counter (PC) register to the address of the print_continuously function, the processor began executing that function as if it were the next instruction in sequence. This works because the processor relies on the PC to determine which instruction to run. By directly assigning the PC to the address of our function, we effectively redirected execution to it. As a result, our code continuously prints \'Hello, PC!\' in an infinite loop without explicitly calling the function from main.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,2,'2025-06-01_17-07-00','A system call provides a way for user-level programs to request services from the operating system or privileged components. It typically involves using a software interrupt (such as the SVC instruction in this lab) to safely transition from user mode to handler mode. This process enables the execution of trusted OS functions, like switching between threads or printing messages, while maintaining security and stability. Essentially, system calls help manage hardware and system resources securely, keeping user-level code isolated from direct hardware access.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',2,2,'2025-06-01_17-16-37','A system call is essentially like invoking a function that allows user programs to request specific services from the operating system\'s kernel, such as reading or writing data, or accessing memory. It safely transitions the system from user mode to kernel mode, making sure that hardware and critical resources are protected. Thinking of system calls as function calls helps clarify how user-level code interacts with the kernel through a well-defined, controlled interface.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,2,'2025-06-01_17-24-39','A system call is essentially like invoking a function that allows user programs to request specific services from the operating system\'s kernel, such as reading or writing data, or accessing memory. It safely transitions the system from user mode to kernel mode, making sure that hardware and critical resources are protected. Thinking of system calls as function calls helps clarify how user-level code interacts with the kernel through a well-defined, controlled interface.\n',1,'s25');
INSERT INTO `question_responses` VALUES ('asarker',2,2,'2025-06-01_17-31-45','A system call is essentially like invoking a function that allows user programs to request specific services from the operating system\'s kernel, such as reading or writing data, or accessing memory. It safely transitions the system from user mode to kernel mode, making sure that hardware and critical resources are protected. Thinking of system calls as function calls helps clarify how user-level code interacts with the kernel through a well-defined, controlled interface.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,3,'2025-06-01_17-07-00','To set up a thread\'s stack, we mimic what the stack would look like after an interrupt has saved the thread’s register context. Essentially, we manually place values such as xPSR, PC, LR, and R0–R12 onto the stack in the exact order the processor expects to pop them when restoring a thread. This arrangement tricks the CPU into believing the thread was interrupted and is ready to resume, even though it hasn\'t started executing yet. By saving these register values, we ensure that when the thread begins, it has all the necessary information—like the entry point function and default register states—to assist a smooth and smooth context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',2,3,'2025-06-01_17-16-37','When setting up a thread’s stack, it’s essential to allocate appropriate memory and initialize it with key register values such as xPSR, PC, LR, and others. This process ensures that the CPU can pick up and run the thread smoothly, as if it was already executing. If the memory for the stack isn\'t properly reserved, the CPU might attempt to access invalid memory during a context switch, which could lead to a system crash.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',2,3,'2025-06-01_17-24-39','To initialize the thread’s stack, we begin by reserving a suitable area of memory to are the stack. Next, we load this region with the appropriate register values such as xPSR, PC, and R0–R12, following the precise sequence that the CPU expects. Maintaining this specific order is essential—during a context switch, the CPU retrieves these values in the same sequence. If this order isn’t correct, the thread might fail to start properly or trigger system faults.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',2,3,'2025-06-01_17-31-45','Configuring a thread’s stack involves reserving the appropriate memory and storing the register values—such as xPSR, PC, LR, R12–R0, R11–R4—in a precise sequence. This setup ensures it aligns with what the CPU expects during a context switch. By saving these register states, the thread can pick up right where it left off. Without this step, the CPU wouldn\'t know which code to execute or what data was being used, leading to potential errors or system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,4,'2025-06-01_17-07-00','The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) indicates where the processor should find the current thread\'s stack. If we don\'t explicitly set it to our manually allocated `stackptr`, the processor might attempt to retrieve register values from an invalid or uninitialized memory area during a context switch, which can cause a hard fault. Properly setting the PSP ensures that the processor pops values from the correct stack region where we\'ve carefully saved the thread’s context.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,4,'2025-06-01_17-16-37','The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) indicates where the processor should find the current thread\'s stack. If we don\'t explicitly set it to our manually allocated `stackptr`, the processor might attempt to retrieve register values from an invalid or uninitialized memory area during a context switch, which can cause a hard fault. Properly setting the PSP ensures that the processor pops values from the correct stack region where we\'ve carefully saved the thread’s context.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,4,'2025-06-01_17-24-39','The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) indicates where the processor should find the current thread\'s stack. If we don\'t explicitly set it to our manually allocated `stackptr`, the processor might attempt to retrieve register values from an invalid or uninitialized memory area during a context switch, which can cause a hard fault. Properly setting the PSP ensures that the processor pops values from the correct stack region where we\'ve carefully saved the thread’s context.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,4,'2025-06-01_17-31-45','The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) indicates where the processor should find the current thread\'s stack. If we don\'t explicitly set it to our manually allocated `stackptr`, the processor might attempt to retrieve register values from an invalid or uninitialized memory area during a context switch, which can cause a hard fault. Properly setting the PSP ensures that the processor pops values from the correct stack region where we\'ve carefully saved the thread’s context.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,5,'2025-06-01_17-07-00','If the thread function simply returned instead of looping, the processor would try to continue execution by jumping to the address stored in the Link Register (LR). Since, during setup, we pushed an arbitrary value like 0xA into LR, the processor would attempt to jump to this invalid address. This could lead to a hard fault or unpredictable behavior. This situation emphasizes the importance of making sure that threads either never return or have a proper exit and cleanup process in place.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,5,'2025-06-01_17-16-37','If the thread function simply returned instead of looping, the processor would try to continue execution by jumping to the address stored in the Link Register (LR). Since, during setup, we pushed an arbitrary value like 0xA into LR, the processor would attempt to jump to this invalid address. This could lead to a hard fault or unpredictable behavior. This situation emphasizes the importance of making sure that threads either never return or have a proper exit and cleanup process in place.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',2,5,'2025-06-01_17-24-39','If the thread function simply returned instead of looping, the processor would try to continue execution by jumping to the address stored in the Link Register (LR). Since, during setup, we pushed an arbitrary value like 0xA into LR, the processor would attempt to jump to this invalid address. This could lead to a hard fault or unpredictable behavior. This situation emphasizes the importance of making sure that threads either never return or have a proper exit and cleanup process in place.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',2,5,'2025-06-01_17-31-45','If the thread function simply returned instead of looping, the processor would try to continue execution by jumping to the address stored in the Link Register (LR). Since, during setup, we pushed an arbitrary value like 0xA into LR, the processor would attempt to jump to this invalid address. This could lead to a hard fault or unpredictable behavior. This situation emphasizes the importance of making sure that threads either never return or have a proper exit and cleanup process in place.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',3,1,'2025-06-27_01-12-21','When multiple *.c files are compiled into a single executable, the process generally involves three key steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, each C file undergoes preprocessing, which means expanding directives like #include and #define, removing comments, and converting macros and headers into plain source code. Next, during compilation, each .c file is translated into machine-level object files, where syntax errors are checked and code optimization is performed. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into one executable. It resolves function calls and variable references across files and links in necessary libraries such as printf() from the C standard library. The result is a complete, ready-to-run executable file.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',3,2,'2025-06-27_01-12-21','The #include directive is intended for header files, not source files. When you write #include, you\'re instructing the compiler to copy and paste the entire contents of kernel.c into another C file before compilation. This approach can cause several issues, such as double compilation, where the same code gets compiled twice, potentially leading to duplicate symbols during linking. Besides, including source files breaks proper modularity and can create confusion within the build system.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',3,3,'2025-06-27_01-12-21','1. Initialize the Kernel – start by calling the function responsible for initializing the kernel. This prepares the internal data structures and allocates memory stack space, which are essential steps before creating any threads. 2. Define Your Thread Function – write a function that contains the specific code you want your thread to execute. This function should accept a single void pointer parameter and not return any value. 3. Create and Register the Thread – specify the address of your thread function and invoke the thread creation function. This step communicates to the kernel what code the thread should run. It also sets up the thread’s stack and initial context, so it’s ready to execute. 4. Start the Kernel – call the function that launches the kernel. This action triggers a system call that loads the thread’s stack pointer and begins execution of your thread. 5. Thread Execution – at this point, the thread is running independently, functioning separately from the main program and other threads.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',3,4,'2025-06-27_01-12-21','The stack pool is a dedicated section of memory managed by the operating system. It provides each thread with its own individual stack, which is essential for storing local variables, return addresses during function calls, and saving CPU register states. Essentially, the stack pool ensures thread isolation by allocating a specific memory area to each thread’s stack so that is both controlled and organized, simplifying memory management across multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',3,5,'2025-06-27_01-12-21','The difference between user files and kernel files mainly comes down to their roles within the system. Kernel code, usually found in kernel.c and kernel.h, handles the core functions that keep the operating system running smoothly. This includes managing thread creation, setting up stacks, handling interrupts, and processing system calls. It provides a reliable set of services and abstractions that the rest of the system can depend on. On the other hand, user code, typically written in main.c, contains the application logic that developers want to execute within the environment provided by the operating system. This involves defining thread functions and calling kernel APIs like osCreateThread() and osKernelStart(). For users and developers, understanding this distinction is critical because it offers a clear and straightforward way to interact with the system. They don’t need to worry about the inner workings of the kernel; instead, they can depend on it to manage hardware and low-level operations safely. This separation not only simplifies development but also improves system modularity and security.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,1,'2025-07-31_15-35-35','Currently, the code uses an array to store the thread control blocks (TCBs). If we switch to using a queue for storing these TCBs, the scheduler would dequeue the running thread from the front and, after it yields control, reinsert it at the back of the queue. This makes implementing round robin scheduling straightforward because the scheduler can simply pick the next thread to run by dequeuing the front element. The main advantage of this method is that it simplifies the scheduling logic compared to managing an array with index tracking, especially when threads are dynamically added or removed. The queue naturally maintains the order and cycles through threads without needing manual management of indices or stack pointers. However, this approach does introduce some additional memory overhead compared to using a simple one-dimensional array.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,1,'2025-07-31_15-50-30','Currently, the code uses an array to store the thread control blocks (TCBs). If we switch to using a queue for storing these TCBs, the scheduler would dequeue the running thread from the front and, after it yields control, reinsert it at the back of the queue. This makes implementing round robin scheduling straightforward because the scheduler can simply pick the next thread to run by dequeuing the front element. The main advantage of this method is that it simplifies the scheduling logic compared to managing an array with index tracking, especially when threads are dynamically added or removed. The queue naturally maintains the order and cycles through threads without needing manual management of indices or stack pointers. However, this approach does introduce some additional memory overhead compared to using a simple one-dimensional array.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,1,'2025-07-31_15-59-14','Currently, the code uses an array to store the thread control blocks (TCBs). If we switch to using a queue for storing these TCBs, the scheduler would dequeue the running thread from the front and, after it yields control, reinsert it at the back of the queue. This makes implementing round robin scheduling straightforward because the scheduler can simply pick the next thread to run by dequeuing the front element. The main advantage of this method is that it simplifies the scheduling logic compared to managing an array with index tracking, especially when threads are dynamically added or removed. The queue naturally maintains the order and cycles through threads without needing manual management of indices or stack pointers. However, this approach does introduce some additional memory overhead compared to using a simple one-dimensional array.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,1,'2025-07-31_19-00-59','Currently, the code uses an array to store the thread control blocks (TCBs). If we switch to using a queue for storing these TCBs, the scheduler would dequeue the running thread from the front and, after it yields control, reinsert it at the back of the queue. This makes implementing round robin scheduling straightforward because the scheduler can simply pick the next thread to run by dequeuing the front element. The main advantage of this method is that it simplifies the scheduling logic compared to managing an array with index tracking, especially when threads are dynamically added or removed. The queue naturally maintains the order and cycles through threads without needing manual management of indices or stack pointers. However, this approach does introduce some additional memory overhead compared to using a simple one-dimensional array.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,2,'2025-07-31_15-35-35','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread’s stack. This pointer indicates where the thread’s saved context, such as register values, is stored in memory. It then saves the current thread’s context by pushing the callee-saved registers R4 through R11 onto the stack pointed to by the PSP. Saving these registers is critical because they hold information that needs to be restored when the thread resumes. After securing the current context, the handler calls the osSched() function, which determines the next thread to run based on a round-robin scheduling policy. The osSched() function updates the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack, guiding the processor to load the correct context. The PendSV_Handler then pops R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack using the updated PSP, restoring its state and preparing it for execution. Finally, the handler exits the interrupt, allowing execution to continue smoothly as if the new thread had been running all along.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,2,'2025-07-31_15-50-30','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread’s stack. This pointer indicates where the thread’s saved context, such as register values, is stored in memory. It then saves the current thread’s context by pushing the callee-saved registers R4 through R11 onto the stack pointed to by the PSP. Saving these registers is critical because they hold information that needs to be restored when the thread resumes. After securing the current context, the handler calls the osSched() function, which determines the next thread to run based on a round-robin scheduling policy. The osSched() function updates the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack, guiding the processor to load the correct context. The PendSV_Handler then pops R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack using the updated PSP, restoring its state and preparing it for execution. Finally, the handler exits the interrupt, allowing execution to continue smoothly as if the new thread had been running all along.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,2,'2025-07-31_15-59-14','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread’s stack. This pointer indicates where the thread’s saved context, such as register values, is stored in memory. It then saves the current thread’s context by pushing the callee-saved registers R4 through R11 onto the stack pointed to by the PSP. Saving these registers is critical because they hold information that needs to be restored when the thread resumes. After securing the current context, the handler calls the osSched() function, which determines the next thread to run based on a round-robin scheduling policy. The osSched() function updates the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack, guiding the processor to load the correct context. The PendSV_Handler then pops R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack using the updated PSP, restoring its state and preparing it for execution. Finally, the handler exits the interrupt, allowing execution to continue smoothly as if the new thread had been running all along.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,2,'2025-07-31_19-00-59','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread’s stack. This pointer indicates where the thread’s saved context, such as register values, is stored in memory. It then saves the current thread’s context by pushing the callee-saved registers R4 through R11 onto the stack pointed to by the PSP. Saving these registers is critical because they hold information that needs to be restored when the thread resumes. After securing the current context, the handler calls the osSched() function, which determines the next thread to run based on a round-robin scheduling policy. The osSched() function updates the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack, guiding the processor to load the correct context. The PendSV_Handler then pops R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack using the updated PSP, restoring its state and preparing it for execution. Finally, the handler exits the interrupt, allowing execution to continue smoothly as if the new thread had been running all along.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,3,'2025-07-31_15-35-35','Focusing solely on executing the highest-priority task can be problematic because it can lead to lower-priority tasks being starved of CPU time. When only the top-priority task runs, other important background or maintenance tasks—like logging or communication processes—may never get a chance to execute. This not only compromises fairness across tasks but can also cause system components that rely on those lower priorities to malfunction. Besides, such an approach makes predicting system behavior more difficult, as lower-priority tasks might be indefinitely delayed, which can undermine the reliability of real-time operations.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',4,3,'2025-07-31_15-50-30','Adjusting the scheduler to always prioritize the highest-priority task can unfortunately lead to starvation of lower-priority tasks. This means those tasks might never get a chance to run, resulting in unfair CPU distribution. Important background processes or system tasks could be indefinitely delayed, which can impair overall system performance and responsiveness. Without proper mechanisms like priority inheritance or aging, issues such as priority inversion can also occur. In these cases, lower-priority tasks holding shared resources block higher-priority ones, leading to unpredictable behavior and potential system instability. To prevent these problems, a scheduler needs to strike a balance between respecting task priorities and ensuring fair access to resources for all tasks.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',4,3,'2025-07-31_15-59-14','When the scheduler always selects the task with the highest priority to run, that task will continue executing without interruption. This means that lower-priority tasks might never get a chance to run, leading to a form of starvation. As a result, critical system functions or background processes could be delayed or never executed at all, which can cause system failures or performance issues. Overall, such a scheduling approach is problematic because it fails to ensure fair CPU access among all tasks, compromising system responsiveness and fairness.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,3,'2025-07-31_19-00-59','When the scheduler always selects the task with the highest priority to run, that task will continue executing without interruption. This means that lower-priority tasks might never get a chance to run, leading to a form of starvation. As a result, critical system functions or background processes could be delayed or never executed at all, which can cause system failures or performance issues. Overall, such a scheduling approach is problematic because it fails to ensure fair CPU access among all tasks, compromising system responsiveness and fairness.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,4,'2025-07-31_15-35-35','Using C functions instead of writing everything in assembly greatly enhances the readability and maintainability of the code. C offers higher-level constructs and uses a standard syntax, making it easier to understand and debug. Also, this approach allows for more modular and adaptable code, especially when dealing with different hardware configurations, because C code is more abstract compared to the hardware-specific nature of assembly. However, there are some trade-offs. Calling C functions introduces extra overhead during both compilation and runtime, such as additional instructions and increased stack usage. This is because the abstracted C code needs processing to work with specific hardware, which results in more lines of code, higher memory consumption, and longer execution times. What\'s more, C functions rely on compiler conventions, which can vary, potentially leading to inconsistent behavior across different environments.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,4,'2025-07-31_15-50-30','Using C functions instead of writing everything in assembly greatly enhances the readability and maintainability of the code. C offers higher-level constructs and uses a standard syntax, making it easier to understand and debug. Also, this approach allows for more modular and adaptable code, especially when dealing with different hardware configurations, because C code is more abstract compared to the hardware-specific nature of assembly. However, there are some trade-offs. Calling C functions introduces extra overhead during both compilation and runtime, such as additional instructions and increased stack usage. This is because the abstracted C code needs processing to work with specific hardware, which results in more lines of code, higher memory consumption, and longer execution times. What\'s more, C functions rely on compiler conventions, which can vary, potentially leading to inconsistent behavior across different environments.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,4,'2025-07-31_15-59-14','Using C functions instead of writing everything in assembly greatly enhances the readability and maintainability of the code. C offers higher-level constructs and uses a standard syntax, making it easier to understand and debug. Also, this approach allows for more modular and adaptable code, especially when dealing with different hardware configurations, because C code is more abstract compared to the hardware-specific nature of assembly. However, there are some trade-offs. Calling C functions introduces extra overhead during both compilation and runtime, such as additional instructions and increased stack usage. This is because the abstracted C code needs processing to work with specific hardware, which results in more lines of code, higher memory consumption, and longer execution times. What\'s more, C functions rely on compiler conventions, which can vary, potentially leading to inconsistent behavior across different environments.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,4,'2025-07-31_19-00-59','Using C functions instead of writing everything in assembly greatly enhances the readability and maintainability of the code. C offers higher-level constructs and uses a standard syntax, making it easier to understand and debug. Also, this approach allows for more modular and adaptable code, especially when dealing with different hardware configurations, because C code is more abstract compared to the hardware-specific nature of assembly. However, there are some trade-offs. Calling C functions introduces extra overhead during both compilation and runtime, such as additional instructions and increased stack usage. This is because the abstracted C code needs processing to work with specific hardware, which results in more lines of code, higher memory consumption, and longer execution times. What\'s more, C functions rely on compiler conventions, which can vary, potentially leading to inconsistent behavior across different environments.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,5,'2025-07-31_15-35-35','Yes, the operating system will still function properly, but its performance may be less than optimal. When a thread yields, the scheduler saves the current context, then selects the next thread to run—often the same one—restores its state, and resumes execution. Essentially, this means the scheduler ends up switching back to the same thread, which creates an unnecessary context switch. Each time this happens, it introduces additional overhead, finally making the program less efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,5,'2025-07-31_15-50-30','Yes, the operating system will still function properly, but its performance may be less than optimal. When a thread yields, the scheduler saves the current context, then selects the next thread to run—often the same one—restores its state, and resumes execution. Essentially, this means the scheduler ends up switching back to the same thread, which creates an unnecessary context switch. Each time this happens, it introduces additional overhead, finally making the program less efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,5,'2025-07-31_15-59-14','Yes, the operating system will still function properly, but its performance may be less than optimal. When a thread yields, the scheduler saves the current context, then selects the next thread to run—often the same one—restores its state, and resumes execution. Essentially, this means the scheduler ends up switching back to the same thread, which creates an unnecessary context switch. Each time this happens, it introduces additional overhead, finally making the program less efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',4,5,'2025-07-31_19-00-59','Yes, the operating system will still function properly, but its performance may be less than optimal. When a thread yields, the scheduler saves the current context, then selects the next thread to run—often the same one—restores its state, and resumes execution. Essentially, this means the scheduler ends up switching back to the same thread, which creates an unnecessary context switch. Each time this happens, it introduces additional overhead, finally making the program less efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,1,'2025-07-31_17-50-40','To pass an argument into a thread, the user needs to supply a pointer to the data when calling osCreateThread. The operating system then uses this pointer to initialize the thread\'s stack by storing its value at a specific memory location. When the thread first runs, this memory content is loaded into the R0 register. This approach works because in C, the first argument to any function is placed in the R0 register. When the scheduler starts the thread, it restores the register values from the stack, ensuring R0 contains the pointer, which allows the thread to start executing as if it were a normal function call with that pointer as its argument.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,1,'2025-07-31_18-51-50','To pass an argument into a thread, the user needs to supply a pointer to the data when calling osCreateThread. The operating system then uses this pointer to initialize the thread\'s stack by storing its value at a specific memory location. When the thread first runs, this memory content is loaded into the R0 register. This approach works because in C, the first argument to any function is placed in the R0 register. When the scheduler starts the thread, it restores the register values from the stack, ensuring R0 contains the pointer, which allows the thread to start executing as if it were a normal function call with that pointer as its argument.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,1,'2025-07-31_19-15-33','To pass an argument into a thread, the user needs to supply a pointer to the data when calling osCreateThread. The operating system then uses this pointer to initialize the thread\'s stack by storing its value at a specific memory location. When the thread first runs, this memory content is loaded into the R0 register. This approach works because in C, the first argument to any function is placed in the R0 register. When the scheduler starts the thread, it restores the register values from the stack, ensuring R0 contains the pointer, which allows the thread to start executing as if it were a normal function call with that pointer as its argument.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,2,'2025-07-31_17-50-40','You shouldn’t call osYield from within context switching handlers like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler. These handlers are already a special type of interrupt responsible for managing task switches. Since osYield works by issuing an SVC instruction, which in turn triggers a PendSV interrupt to do the actual context switch, calling osYield from inside one of these handlers would be problematic. It would mean trying to initiate a new context switch while another is still ongoing, potentially causing nested exceptions that could lead to unpredictable behavior, system corruption, or stack issues. Eventually, this could cause the processor to enter a fault state.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,2,'2025-07-31_18-51-50','You shouldn’t call osYield from within context switching handlers like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler. These handlers are already a special type of interrupt responsible for managing task switches. Since osYield works by issuing an SVC instruction, which in turn triggers a PendSV interrupt to do the actual context switch, calling osYield from inside one of these handlers would be problematic. It would mean trying to initiate a new context switch while another is still ongoing, potentially causing nested exceptions that could lead to unpredictable behavior, system corruption, or stack issues. Eventually, this could cause the processor to enter a fault state.\n',0,'s25'),('asarker',5,2,'2025-07-31_19-15-33','It\'s important not to call osYield from within context-switching handlers such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler. These handlers are already responsible for managing task switches. When osYield triggers an SVC interrupt, it could result in another context switch occurring simultaneously—something that could cause problems. Attempting this risks nested exceptions and stack corruption because re-entering the scheduler can overwrite critical data. On ARM Cortex-M processors, such stack issues often lead to a hardfault, which is a serious system error that stops the processor due to invalid memory access or corrupted state.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,3,'2025-07-31_17-50-40','Adjusting the core OS clock from 1 millisecond down to 0.1 milliseconds can considerably enhance system responsiveness by providing a finer time resolution. This allows the operating system to switch tasks more frequently, leading to more precise preemption and quicker responses to external events. However, this improvement comes with a cost: increased overhead. Since the SysTick_Handler would now trigger ten times more often, it would consume considerably more CPU resources just for scheduling purposes. Consequently, less processing power remains available for actual application threads, which could limit the system’s overall throughput. Basically, there’s a clear trade-off between achieving higher precision and maintaining efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,3,'2025-07-31_18-51-50','Adjusting the core OS clock from 1 millisecond down to 0.1 milliseconds can considerably enhance system responsiveness by providing a finer time resolution. This allows the operating system to switch tasks more frequently, leading to more precise preemption and quicker responses to external events. However, this improvement comes with a cost: increased overhead. Since the SysTick_Handler would now trigger ten times more often, it would consume considerably more CPU resources just for scheduling purposes. Consequently, less processing power remains available for actual application threads, which could limit the system’s overall throughput. Basically, there’s a clear trade-off between achieving higher precision and maintaining efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,3,'2025-07-31_19-15-33','Adjusting the core OS clock from 1 millisecond down to 0.1 milliseconds can considerably enhance system responsiveness by providing a finer time resolution. This allows the operating system to switch tasks more frequently, leading to more precise preemption and quicker responses to external events. However, this improvement comes with a cost: increased overhead. Since the SysTick_Handler would now trigger ten times more often, it would consume considerably more CPU resources just for scheduling purposes. Consequently, less processing power remains available for actual application threads, which could limit the system’s overall throughput. Basically, there’s a clear trade-off between achieving higher precision and maintaining efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,4,'2025-07-31_17-50-40','From a user\'s point of view, cooperative multitasking relies on programmers to manage fairness and task switching. Each task needs to explicitly give up control; if a buggy task has an infinite loop, it can halt the entire system. On the other hand, preemptive multitasking offers a more reliable approach because developers don’t need to worry about yielding. The operating system ensures that every task gets a chance to run. Implementing cooperative multitasking is generally simpler since it only requires tasks to voluntarily yield control. In contrast, preemptive multitasking is more complex, as the OS must set up hardware timers—like SysTick—to interrupt tasks at regular intervals, keep track of how long each task runs, and forcibly switch contexts when a task\'s time slice is over.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,4,'2025-07-31_18-51-50','From a user\'s point of view, cooperative multitasking relies on programmers to manage fairness and task switching. Each task needs to explicitly give up control; if a buggy task has an infinite loop, it can halt the entire system. On the other hand, preemptive multitasking offers a more reliable approach because developers don’t need to worry about yielding. The operating system ensures that every task gets a chance to run. Implementing cooperative multitasking is generally simpler since it only requires tasks to voluntarily yield control. In contrast, preemptive multitasking is more complex, as the OS must set up hardware timers—like SysTick—to interrupt tasks at regular intervals, keep track of how long each task runs, and forcibly switch contexts when a task\'s time slice is over.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,4,'2025-07-31_19-15-33','From a user\'s point of view, cooperative multitasking relies on programmers to manage fairness and task switching. Each task needs to explicitly give up control; if a buggy task has an infinite loop, it can halt the entire system. On the other hand, preemptive multitasking offers a more reliable approach because developers don’t need to worry about yielding. The operating system ensures that every task gets a chance to run. Implementing cooperative multitasking is generally simpler since it only requires tasks to voluntarily yield control. In contrast, preemptive multitasking is more complex, as the OS must set up hardware timers—like SysTick—to interrupt tasks at regular intervals, keep track of how long each task runs, and forcibly switch contexts when a task\'s time slice is over.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,5,'2025-07-31_17-50-40','Both yielding and time-based pre-emption can work together smoothly because they rely on the same fundamental mechanism for switching contexts. Whether a thread voluntarily calls osYield(), which triggers an SVC followed by a PendSV, or if a thread is involuntarily interrupted by the SysTick_Handler, which directly triggers PendSV, the PendSV exception is always invoked. The PendSV_Handler acts as the unified process responsible for saving the current thread\'s state and restoring the next thread\'s state. Its function isn\'t dependent on why it\'s called but simply performs the context switch. This design allows yielding and pre-emptive multitasking to operate smoothly in harmony.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,5,'2025-07-31_18-51-50','Both yielding and time-based pre-emption can work together smoothly because they rely on the same fundamental mechanism for switching contexts. Whether a thread voluntarily calls osYield(), which triggers an SVC followed by a PendSV, or if a thread is involuntarily interrupted by the SysTick_Handler, which directly triggers PendSV, the PendSV exception is always invoked. The PendSV_Handler acts as the unified process responsible for saving the current thread\'s state and restoring the next thread\'s state. Its function isn\'t dependent on why it\'s called but simply performs the context switch. This design allows yielding and pre-emptive multitasking to operate smoothly in harmony.\n',1,'s25'),('asarker',5,5,'2025-07-31_19-15-33','Both yielding and time-based pre-emption can work together smoothly because they rely on the same fundamental mechanism for switching contexts. Whether a thread voluntarily calls osYield(), which triggers an SVC followed by a PendSV, or if a thread is involuntarily interrupted by the SysTick_Handler, which directly triggers PendSV, the PendSV exception is always invoked. The PendSV_Handler acts as the unified process responsible for saving the current thread\'s state and restoring the next thread\'s state. Its function isn\'t dependent on why it\'s called but simply performs the context switch. This design allows yielding and pre-emptive multitasking to operate smoothly in harmony.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',1,1,'2025-06-26_16-46-31','The HAL, or Hardware Abstraction Layer is a software layer that between the user or application code and the physical hardware. The HAL provides functions and structures to handle low-level details such as directly manipulating registers, setting bits to configure UART, configuring timers etc. This abstraction reduces the complexity of the user code and makes it easier to read and maintain. Additionally, the code may still work on  other microcontrollers with very little change as the HAL provides a consistent interface across different devices.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',1,1,'2025-06-26_19-59-45','The HAL, or Hardware Abstraction Layer is a software layer that between the user or application code and the physical hardware. The HAL provides functions and structures to handle low-level details such as directly manipulating registers, setting bits to configure UART, configuring timers etc. This abstraction reduces the complexity of the user code and makes it easier to read and maintain. Additionally, the code may still work on  other microcontrollers with very little change as the HAL provides a consistent interface across different devices.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',1,2,'2025-06-26_16-46-31','The function __io_putchar is usually a part of how printf() works in embedded systems. In a normal computer, the operating system (OS) will take care of sending your printf() output to the screen or terminal. In embedded systems, we have to determine where the system should send characters that printf() generates. The __io_putchar() function tells the system how to output each character, and is automatically called by printf() to handle every character in the string. In this lab, the __io_putchar character is used to send characters over UART2, which connects to the PC.\n',0,'s25'),('athiruma',1,2,'2025-06-26_19-59-45','The __io_putchar function tells printf() how to output each character in an embedded system. On a regular computer, printf() is handled by the operating system, which knows how to send output to a terminal or screen. But in embedded systems, there is no built-in output device — we have to define exactly where each character should go.\n__io_putchar acts as a helper that connects printf() to a specific hardware peripheral, like a UART. In this lab, we used __io_putchar to send each character over UART2 to our computer’s serial monitor. Without defining this function, printf() would compile, but nothing would appear because there would be no output destination. This makes __io_putchar critical for adapting printf() to work on different hardware — for example, we could modify it in a future project to print to an LCD, a file, or a radio module just by changing this one function.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',1,3,'2025-06-26_16-46-31','We used the debugger in this lab to verify some of the information that isn’t visible through just printf(). While printf() can print values like variables, it cannot directly access the microcontrollers hardware registers. With the debugger, we were able to observe the vales of the psp and msp hardware registers to ensure that our calculated values were correctly saved.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',1,3,'2025-06-26_19-59-45','We used the debugger in this lab to verify some of the information that isn’t visible through just printf(). While printf() can print values like variables, it cannot directly access the microcontrollers hardware registers. With the debugger, we were able to observe the vales of the psp and msp hardware registers to ensure that our calculated values were correctly saved.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',1,4,'2025-06-26_16-46-31','We are treating the stack memory as one fixed-sized region that is divided into smaller blocks for different purposes. We start by obtaining MSP_INIT_VAL, which is from reading the address 0x0. In ARM Cortex-M processors, the first entry in the vector table (0x0), holds the pointer to the initial MSP value. With the knowledge that the stack grows downward and that the main stack is 0x400 bytes large, we subtract 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL to get PSP_val.  This places the Process Stack directly below the Main Stack in memory. We needed to know the initial value of the MSP because that tells us where the stack starts in memory after reset. Without knowing the MSP’s starting position, we wouldn’t know where to place additional stack regions safely. This ensures that new stacks do not overlap with existing ones.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',1,4,'2025-06-26_19-59-45','We are treating the stack memory as one fixed-sized region that is divided into smaller blocks for different purposes. We start by obtaining MSP_INIT_VAL, which is from reading the address 0x0. In ARM Cortex-M processors, the first entry in the vector table (0x0), holds the pointer to the initial MSP value. With the knowledge that the stack grows downward and that the main stack is 0x400 bytes large, we subtract 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL to get PSP_val.  This places the Process Stack directly below the Main Stack in memory. We needed to know the initial value of the MSP because that tells us where the stack starts in memory after reset. Without knowing the MSP’s starting position, we wouldn’t know where to place additional stack regions safely. This ensures that new stacks do not overlap with existing ones.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',1,5,'2025-06-26_16-46-31','If we wanted to allocate multiple thread stacks using the same method as in the lab, we would need to start with the main stack pointer, MSP_INIT_VAL, then divide the memory into fixed-size regions for each thread. The main stack is of size MAIN_STACK_SIZE, and each thread must have a defined stack size as well, such as THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This establishes that the MSP_INIT_VAL is the starting address of the main stack, we reserve MAIN_STACK_SIZE bytes below it, then place the first thread’s stack immediately below the main stack. Each additional stack is offset by THREAD_SIZE_STACK further down. For each thread j, we can compute the starting address of that thread’s stack using the following formula\nADDRESS_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) × THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nTo make this work in our code, we would need to define a constant number of threads and the size of each stack region. Then, we’d read the initial MSP value like done in the lab already. Then, the base of each thread’s stack should be calculated using the code snippet for ADDRESS_STACK_j listed above.\n',0,'s25'),('athiruma',1,5,'2025-06-26_19-59-45','If we wanted to allocate multiple thread stacks using the same method as in the lab, we would need to start with the main stack pointer, MSP_INIT_VAL, then divide the memory into fixed-size regions for each thread. The main stack is of size MAIN_STACK_SIZE, and each thread must have a defined stack size as well, such as THREAD_STACK_SIZE. These sizes must be multiples of 8 to ensure proper 8-byte alignment, which is required by the Cortex-M4 architecture to avoid a UsageFault. This establishes that the MSP_INIT_VAL is the starting address of the main stack, we reserve MAIN_STACK_SIZE bytes below it, then place the first thread’s stack immediately below the main stack. Since the stack grows downward, each additional stack is offset by THREAD_SIZE_STACK further down. For each thread j, we can compute the starting address of that thread’s stack using the following formula\nADDRESS_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (j * THREAD_STACK_SIZE).\nTo make this work in our code, we would need to define a constant number of threads and the size of each stack region. Then, we’d read the initial MSP value like done in the lab already. Then, the base of each thread’s stack should be calculated using the code snippet for ADDRESS_STACK_j listed above. This formula ensuers that the first thread stack starts immediately after the main stack, and each additional thread’s stack is placed safely below the previous one. By ensuring proper alignment, safe memory usage, and stack separation, this method lets us statically allocate non-overlapping stacks for all threads using only simple compile-time arithmetic.\n',0,'s25'),('athiruma',2,1,'2025-06-27_20-27-02','When I changed the PC register to hold the address of print_continuously, I was manually forcing the processor to start executing that function. Normally, a function call is handled by the compiler and the CPU automatically. In this case, the function address is first stored in R0, then the jumpAssembly function uses the instruction “MOV PC, R0” to move the function address from R0 and into the program counter. Since the program counter tells the CPU where to fetch the next instruction, the CPU begins executing print_contiuously in an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',2,1,'2025-06-27_21-02-31','When I changed the PC register to hold the address of print_continuously, I was manually forcing the processor to start executing that function. Normally, a function call is handled by the compiler and the CPU automatically. In this case, the function address is first stored in R0, then the jumpAssembly function uses the instruction “MOV PC, R0” to move the function address from R0 and into the program counter. Since the program counter tells the CPU where to fetch the next instruction, the CPU begins executing print_contiuously in an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',2,2,'2025-06-27_20-27-02','A system call is a special way for a user-level program to ask the operating system to perform a privileged operation. This service can be something like accessing files, accessing hardware, memory allocation, or I/O operations. Since user programs cannot directly interact with hardware or system-level resources for safety reasons, system calls are used to request these actions in a safe and controlled way. When the system call is triggered, the CPU switches from User mode to Kernel mode and jumps to the OS code that handles the request.\n',0,'s25'),('athiruma',2,2,'2025-06-27_21-02-31','A system call is a special kind of function call that allows a user-level program to request a service from the operating system. These services involve privileged operations like accessing hardware, memory allocation, or I/O operations. Since user programs cannot directly interact with hardware or system-level resources for safety reasons, system calls are used to request these actions in a safe and controlled way. When the system call is triggered, the CPU switches from User mode to Kernel mode and jumps to the OS code that handles the request.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',2,3,'2025-06-27_20-27-02','Setting up a thread’s stack means establishing a new memory region and placing values in it that represent the thread’s CPU state, as If the thread had already been running and was interrupted. In this lab, we manually push values such as program status register (xPSR), program counter (PC), and general-purpose registers (R0-R12) onto the stack in a very specific order. This order is important as when we switch to the thread, the CPU will pop these values off the stack to restore the thread’s execution context. Register values must be stored on the stack to simulate a saved thread state, which is essential for starting the thread correctly and for future context switches. If the values or order are incorrect, the CPU would restore bad data and crash.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',2,3,'2025-06-27_21-02-31','Setting up a thread’s stack means establishing a new memory region and placing values in it that represent the thread’s CPU state, as If the thread had already been running and was interrupted. In this lab, we manually push values such as program status register (xPSR), program counter (PC), and general-purpose registers (R0-R12) onto the stack in a very specific order. This order is important as when we switch to the thread, the CPU will pop these values off the stack to restore the thread’s execution context. Register values must be stored on the stack to simulate a saved thread state, which is essential for starting the thread correctly and for future context switches. If the values or order are incorrect, the CPU would restore bad data and crash.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',2,4,'2025-06-27_20-27-02','We set the process stack pointer (PSP) to point to our manually constructed thread stack because that’s where the CPU expects to find the thread’s saved register values. If the PSP was not set using __set_PSP, the CPU will still try to use the old and likely uninitialized PSP value, which means it will be reading from invalid memory. This would lead to a hard fault, a type of crash that happens when the CPU accesses invalid memory or executes an illegal instruction. Setting the PSP correctly is what lets the thread start safely.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',2,4,'2025-06-27_21-02-31','We set the process stack pointer (PSP) to point to our manually constructed thread stack because that’s where the CPU expects to find the thread’s saved register values. If the PSP was not set using __set_PSP, the CPU will still try to use the old and likely uninitialized PSP value, which means it will be reading from invalid memory. This would lead to a hard fault, a type of crash that happens when the CPU accesses invalid memory or executes an illegal instruction. Setting the PSP correctly is what lets the thread start safely.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',2,5,'2025-06-27_20-27-02','If the thread function didn’t loop forever and instead printed something once and returned, the CPU would try to continue execution at the address stored in the Link Register (LR). But during our stack setup, we pushed a dummy or invalid value into the LR, not a real return address. So when the thread finishes and the CPU tries to return, it will jump to an invalid memory location. This will likely result in a hard fault because the CPU can’t execute from that address.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',2,5,'2025-06-27_21-02-31','If the thread function didn’t loop forever and instead printed something once and returned, the CPU would try to continue execution at the address stored in the Link Register (LR). But during our stack setup, we pushed a dummy or invalid value into the LR, not a real return address. So when the thread finishes and the CPU tries to return, it will jump to an invalid memory location. This will likely result in a hard fault because the CPU can’t execute from that address.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',3,1,'2025-06-27_22-12-16','Each .c file is compiled independently into an object file through a series of steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. During preprocessing, directives like #include and #define are expanded, and comments are removed. The compiler then translates each file into a .o (object) file containing machine code. In the final stage, the linker combines all .o files into a single executable, resolving cross-file references such as function calls and variable definitions. It also links in any necessary standard libraries. The result is a single program made from multiple source files.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',3,1,'2025-06-27_23-20-23','Each .c file is compiled independently into an object file through a series of steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. During preprocessing, directives like #include and #define are expanded, and comments are removed. The compiler then translates each file into a .o (object) file containing machine code. In the final stage, the linker combines all .o files into a single executable, resolving cross-file references such as function calls and variable definitions. It also links in any necessary standard libraries. The result is a single program made from multiple source files.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',3,2,'2025-06-27_22-12-16','Using #include <kernel.c> is incorrect because it forces the compiler to paste the entire source file into another file before compilation, leading to duplicate definitions when both are also compiled separately. This breaks the modular build process and causes errors like \"multiple symbol definitions\" during linking. .c files should be compiled individually and then linked, while only .h header files should be included to share declarations like function prototypes and type definitions.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',3,2,'2025-06-27_23-20-23','Using #include <kernel.c> is incorrect because it forces the compiler to paste the entire source file into another file before compilation, leading to duplicate definitions when both are also compiled separately. This breaks the modular build process and causes errors like \"multiple symbol definitions\" during linking. .c files should be compiled individually and then linked, while only .h header files should be included to share declarations like function prototypes and type definitions.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',3,3,'2025-06-27_22-12-16','To set up and start a thread:\nInitialize the kernel – Call a function to set up the kernel’s internal data and prepare the stack pool.\nWrite a thread function – Define a function that contains the code you want the thread to execute.\nCreate the thread – Use a kernel-provided function to register the thread function, allocate a stack, and prepare it for execution.\nStart the kernel – Call a function that triggers a system call, which sets the thread’s stack pointer and begins running the thread.\nThe thread now runs independently – It executes concurrently, as managed by the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',3,3,'2025-06-27_23-20-23','To set up and start a thread:\nInitialize the kernel – Call a function to set up the kernel’s internal data and prepare the stack pool.\nWrite a thread function – Define a function that contains the code you want the thread to execute.\nCreate the thread – Use a kernel-provided function to register the thread function, allocate a stack, and prepare it for execution.\nStart the kernel – Call a function that triggers a system call, which sets the thread’s stack pointer and begins running the thread.\nThe thread now runs independently – It executes concurrently, as managed by the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',3,4,'2025-06-27_22-12-16','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used by the operating system to allocate private stack space for each thread. Each thread needs its own stack to store local variables, return addresses, and CPU context during execution. By carving out fixed-size chunks of memory from the pool, the OS can create isolated and aligned stack spaces for multiple threads. In future labs, this will allow the OS to manage and switch between several threads safely and efficiently, without memory conflicts.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',3,4,'2025-06-27_23-20-23','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used by the operating system to allocate private stack space for each thread. Each thread needs its own stack to store local variables, return addresses, and CPU context during execution. By carving out fixed-size chunks of memory from the pool, the OS can create isolated and aligned stack spaces for multiple threads. In future labs, this will allow the OS to manage and switch between several threads safely and efficiently, without memory conflicts.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',3,5,'2025-06-27_22-12-16','The kernel code (in kernel.c and kernel.h) contains the core functionality of the OS — things like thread creation, stack management, and handling system calls. The user code (in main.c) contains application-level logic such as thread functions and calls to the kernel API. This separation is important because it provides a clean interface: users can focus on writing application code without needing to understand or modify low-level kernel internals. It also improves modularity, security, and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',3,5,'2025-06-27_23-20-23','The kernel code (in kernel.c and kernel.h) contains the core functionality of the OS — things like thread creation, stack management, and handling system calls. The user code (in main.c) contains application-level logic such as thread functions and calls to the kernel API. This separation is important because it provides a clean interface: users can focus on writing application code without needing to understand or modify low-level kernel internals. It also improves modularity, security, and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,1,'2025-07-29_19-50-38','Round-robin scheduling using a queue involves enqueueing threads at the end and dequeuing them from the front each time a thread yields, or its time slice ends. When a thread finishes its execution or yields, it is placed back into the queue. Thus, the scheduler simply dequeues the next thread to run, making scheduling very straight forward. The primary benefit is efficiency in insertion and removal operations, as queues naturally support O(1) complexity for these operations. In contrast, array operations like shifting elements can take O(n) time.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,1,'2025-07-29_20-28-49','Round-robin scheduling using a queue involves enqueueing threads at the end and dequeuing them from the front each time a thread yields, or its time slice ends. When a thread finishes its execution or yields, it is placed back into the queue. Thus, the scheduler simply dequeues the next thread to run, making scheduling very straight forward. The primary benefit is efficiency in insertion and removal operations, as queues naturally support O(1) complexity for these operations. In contrast, array operations like shifting elements can take O(n) time.\n',0,'s25'),('athiruma',4,1,'2025-07-29_20-35-45','In round-robin scheduling, a queue can be used to manage threads by enqueuing each thread at the end when it\'s created or after it yields, and dequeuing the next thread to run from the front. This approach behaves almost identically to using a simple array with an index, as the order in which threads are stored and accessed still follows a cyclic pattern. In fact, a circular array and a queue both provide the same round-robin behavior, making a queue functionally equivalent to the current array-based scheduler described in the lab. One potential advantage of using a queue, however, is that enqueue and dequeue operations are more intuitive and abstract away manual index management, which can reduce programming errors. Additionally, if the scheduler becomes more complex later (e.g., if threads are inserted or removed dynamically), a queue can help manage that with less overhead and less manual bookkeeping than an array. So while both approaches are equivalent in simple round-robin logic, a queue may scale better and offer slightly cleaner semantics and implementation flexibility.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,2,'2025-07-29_19-50-38','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the state (registers and stack pointer) of the currently running thread and loading the state of the next thread. First, it pushes the registers R4-R11 (the thread context) onto the thread’s own stack (PSP). Then, it calls the osSched() function to determine which thread it should run next. After selecting the next thread, it updates the PSP to the new thread’s stack pointer, pops the saved registers form the stack into CPU registers, and finally resumes execution where the new thread previously left off. Using PendSV ensures the context switch only occurs when no other interrupts or higher priority-tasks are running.\n',0,'s25'),('athiruma',4,2,'2025-07-29_20-28-49','The PendSV_Handler function handles context switches by explicitly managing thread stacks. The process begins by reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the top of the current thread’s stack. This step is crucial because the PSP indicates where the current thread’s context is stored in memory. First, the handler explicitly reads the PSP and uses it to store (push) the contents of registers R4 through R11 onto the thread\'s stack. Saving these registers preserves the thread’s execution context. After the context is saved, the handler then calls the scheduler (osSched) in C to decide which thread will run next. Next, osSched updates the current thread index, selects the next thread’s stack pointer, and returns control to PendSV_Handler. The handler then explicitly updates the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack pointer. Lastly, it pops registers R4 through R11 off the stack (restoring the thread context) and resumes execution at the exact instruction where the newly scheduled thread previously left off. This sequence of explicitly reading and updating the PSP is vital for correctly maintaining thread contexts and ensuring seamless thread switching.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,2,'2025-07-29_20-35-45','The PendSV_Handler function handles context switches by explicitly managing thread stacks. The process begins by reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the top of the current thread’s stack. This step is crucial because the PSP indicates where the current thread’s context is stored in memory. First, the handler explicitly reads the PSP and uses it to store (push) the contents of registers R4 through R11 onto the thread\'s stack. Saving these registers preserves the thread’s execution context. After the context is saved, the handler then calls the scheduler (osSched) in C to decide which thread will run next. Next, osSched updates the current thread index, selects the next thread’s stack pointer, and returns control to PendSV_Handler. The handler then explicitly updates the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack pointer. Lastly, it pops registers R4 through R11 off the stack (restoring the thread context) and resumes execution at the exact instruction where the newly scheduled thread previously left off. This sequence of explicitly reading and updating the PSP is vital for correctly maintaining thread contexts and ensuring seamless thread switching.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,3,'2025-07-29_19-50-38','This is a problematic idea because it can cause lower-prority tasks to experience starvation. Starvation occurs when higher-priority tasks constantly use CPU time, preventing lower-priority tasks from ever getting a chance to execute. If the high-priority tasks never yield or frequently pre-empt lower-priority ones, certain tasks may never run. This negatively impacts system responsiveness and fairness.\n',0,'s25'),('athiruma',4,3,'2025-07-29_20-28-49','If we modify the OS scheduler to always select the task with the highest priority, meaning the largest integer in the TCB priority field, the result would be that only the single highest-priority task would ever run. Lower-priority tasks would never get CPU time unless the highest-priority task voluntarily yields control or finishes execution. In practical scenarios, if the highest-priority task runs indefinitely or does not yield, no other tasks will execute. This causes starvation of all lower-priority tasks and completely prevents multitasking. The issue arises directly from the scheduler\'s behavior of always choosing the highest-priority thread, which makes it impossible for lower-priority threads to run, even if they are important for system functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,3,'2025-07-29_20-35-45','If we modify the OS scheduler to always select the task with the highest priority, meaning the largest integer in the TCB priority field, the result would be that only the single highest-priority task would ever run. Lower-priority tasks would never get CPU time unless the highest-priority task voluntarily yields control or finishes execution. In practical scenarios, if the highest-priority task runs indefinitely or does not yield, no other tasks will execute. This causes starvation of all lower-priority tasks and completely prevents multitasking. The issue arises directly from the scheduler\'s behavior of always choosing the highest-priority thread, which makes it impossible for lower-priority threads to run, even if they are important for system functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,4,'2025-07-29_19-50-38','Using C functions within handlers has clear advantages: readability, maintainability, and easier debugging. C code is typically easier for humans to read, write, and understand. High-level code also abstracts away complex register manipulations, reducing the chance of mistakes. However, calling C functions from assembly adds overhead: there\'s additional time spent calling, managing stacks, and returning from functions. Writing everything directly in assembly might result in more efficient and faster execution, as it allows precise optimization. However, pure assembly code is typically harder to maintain, understand, and debug, making development slower and more error-prone. In practice, using C for most logic and assembly strictly for context-switching tasks strikes an ideal balance between readability and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,4,'2025-07-29_20-28-49','Using C functions within handlers has clear advantages: readability, maintainability, and easier debugging. C code is typically easier for humans to read, write, and understand. High-level code also abstracts away complex register manipulations, reducing the chance of mistakes. However, calling C functions from assembly adds overhead: there\'s additional time spent calling, managing stacks, and returning from functions. Writing everything directly in assembly might result in more efficient and faster execution, as it allows precise optimization. However, pure assembly code is typically harder to maintain, understand, and debug, making development slower and more error-prone. In practice, using C for most logic and assembly strictly for context-switching tasks strikes an ideal balance between readability and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,4,'2025-07-29_20-35-45','Using C functions within handlers has clear advantages: readability, maintainability, and easier debugging. C code is typically easier for humans to read, write, and understand. High-level code also abstracts away complex register manipulations, reducing the chance of mistakes. However, calling C functions from assembly adds overhead: there\'s additional time spent calling, managing stacks, and returning from functions. Writing everything directly in assembly might result in more efficient and faster execution, as it allows precise optimization. However, pure assembly code is typically harder to maintain, understand, and debug, making development slower and more error-prone. In practice, using C for most logic and assembly strictly for context-switching tasks strikes an ideal balance between readability and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,5,'2025-07-29_19-50-38','Yes, the OS will still technically \"work\" because the single thread will yield, causing the scheduler to select the next thread. However, since there is no other thread to switch to, the scheduler will repeatedly select the same thread. Therefore, the thread continually runs, yielding only to itself. The behavior observed from the user\'s perspective would essentially be identical to having no multithreading at all, just a continuous loop of the single thread. There\'s no error or failure, but the main purpose of multitasking, which is to switch between multiple threads, is not demonstrated.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,5,'2025-07-29_20-28-49','Yes, the OS will still technically \"work\" because the single thread will yield, causing the scheduler to select the next thread. However, since there is no other thread to switch to, the scheduler will repeatedly select the same thread. Therefore, the thread continually runs, yielding only to itself. The behavior observed from the user\'s perspective would essentially be identical to having no multithreading at all, just a continuous loop of the single thread. There\'s no error or failure, but the main purpose of multitasking, which is to switch between multiple threads, is not demonstrated.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',4,5,'2025-07-29_20-35-45','Yes, the OS will still technically \"work\" because the single thread will yield, causing the scheduler to select the next thread. However, since there is no other thread to switch to, the scheduler will repeatedly select the same thread. Therefore, the thread continually runs, yielding only to itself. The behavior observed from the user\'s perspective would essentially be identical to having no multithreading at all, just a continuous loop of the single thread. There\'s no error or failure, but the main purpose of multitasking, which is to switch between multiple threads, is not demonstrated.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',5,1,'2025-07-30_20-37-06','To pass an argument into a thread, the user defines their thread function to accept a void* parameter, and then calls osCreateThread with a pointer to the data they want to pass. For example, a pointer to an integer or a struct containing multiple fields. Then, during thread creation, the OS will manually initialize the stack for that thread. As part of this initialization, it places the function pointer into the Program Counter (PC) slot and argument pointer into register R0 on the stack. According to ARM conventions, R0 is there the first argument to a function is expected. When the thread is scheduled and execution starts, the CPU loads the value from R0 and passes it into the thread function. This works because the thread context was carefully set up to mimic what would happen if the thread had been called normally from C code. As a result, when the thread function runs, it receives the argument as expected and can safely cast and use it\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',5,2,'2025-07-30_20-37-06','You can’t call osYield inside the context-switching functions (PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main) because each of these functions is already running in an interrupt context. Calling osYield() would trigger another SVC interrupt, which would lead to nested interrupt handling. This is problematic because the Cortex-M processor does not support pre-emption between system handlers like SVC and PendSV unless priorities are strictly controlled. Calling osYield() from within one of these handlers could corrupt the stack, lead to undefined behaviour, or cause the handler to never finish properly. More importantly, osYield() is meant to simulate a system call from a user thread, not from within the kernel or interrupt code. These handlers already are the context-switching mechanisms, so trying to re-enter them through osYield() creates circular logic and breaks the separation between user-mode and system-mode execution.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',5,3,'2025-07-30_20-37-06','Reducing the OS clock to interval 0.1ms, would increase the tick frequency and consequently increase the responsiveness of the system. The OS could switch threads more frequently and react to timing events with higher resolution, which is useful for real-time applications that require very fine control, such as motor control. However, this also introduces some significant downsides. First, the overhead of handling the SysTick interrupt every 0.1ms is high, the CPU would spend a large portion of time processing ticks rather than running actual threads. This can reduce the overall efficiency and drain power more quickly, especially in embedded systems. Second, smaller time slices increase context switching frequency, which adds overhead from saving and restoring thread states more often. This can lead to cache thrashing and degraded performance for threads that do useful work over longer timespans.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',5,4,'2025-07-30_20-37-06','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires each thread to voluntarily yield the CPU by calling osYield(). If a thread fails to yield, it can monopolize the CPU and prevent other threads from running. Pre-emptive multitasking allows the OS to automatically interrupt a running thread when its time slice is up, without requiring the thread to yield explicitly. For the OS, co-operative multitasking is simpler. It only needs to perform a context switch when osYield() is called and doesn’t need to track time or force switches. Pre-emptive multitasking is more complex, as the OS must set up a timer like SysTick, track how long each thread has been running, and perform context switches automatically when a thread’s time slice expires. This involves additional overhead and careful priority management to avoid interrupt conflicts.\n',1,'s25'),('athiruma',5,5,'2025-07-30_20-37-06','Both mechanisms work together because they share the same context-switching infrastructure. Specifically, they both trigger PendSV_Handler to switch threads. The difference is in who initiates the switch. In the yielding case, the user explicitly calls osYield() which sets a software interrupt flag to request a context switch. The OS then resets the thread’s runtime to its original time slice before switching. In the pre-emptive case, the SysTick_Handler decrements the current thread’s runtime every millisecond. When the runtime reaches zero, the handler resets it and triggers the same PendSV interrupt to force a switch. Because both approaches use the same scheduler and stack-switching logic, they can coexist.  Cooperative threads yield control voluntarily, while pre-emptive threads are automatically switched after a timeout. This allows you to mix responsive, yield-based logic with background tasks that run based on timing constraints.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-10_16-11-03','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is used to that is used to allow the code to interact with the hardware of the device. Without the HAL the code would be unable to\n',0,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-23_16-17-26','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-25_14-07-19','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-25_14-36-49','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-25_15-01-06','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-25_15-25-16','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-26_14-33-06','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-26_15-50-45','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-26_17-35-23','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-26_18-52-48','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-27_10-04-41','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-27_11-29-06','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-27_12-52-57','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-27_13-16-56','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,1,'2025-06-27_13-44-48','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) allows the code to interact with the hardware of the device. It simplifies this interaction by standardizing the code that is used which means that programmers don’t have to learn new syntax when they’re using a new device. Without the HAL writing the code on the STM-32 would be much more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,2,'2025-06-10_16-11-03','The __io_putchar function is meant to send a single character to the uart2 I/O channel. The function then returns the thing so that you know that the function worked correctly and so that the printf function that will be called later.\n',0,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,2,'2025-06-23_16-17-26','The __io_putchar function is meant to send a single character to the uart2 I/O channel. The function then returns this letter so that the printf function can output them as a string to the output channel.\n',0,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,2,'2025-06-25_14-07-19','The __io_putchar function is meant to send a single character to the uart2 I/O channel. The function then returns this letter so that the printf function can output them as a string to the output channel. The __io_putchar function specifically allows the programmer to select which output is used. This allows the programmer to better test that certain features are being used correctly.\n',0,'s25'),('b2lovegr',1,2,'2025-06-25_14-36-49','The __io_putchar function is meant to send a single character to the uart2 I/O channel. The function then returns this letter so that the printf function can output them as a string to the output channel. The __io_putchar function specifically allows the programmer to select which output is used, if this was part of a standard library than the programmer wouldn’t be able to do this. T