HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a set of functions provided by ST that makes it easier to work with the microcontroller\'s hardware like pins, UART, timers, etc. It saves us from writing low-level code and helps our programs work on different STM32 boards without many changes.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',1,1,'2025-06-09_19-50-20','HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a set of functions provided by ST that makes it easier to work with the microcontroller\'s hardware like pins, UART, timers, etc. It saves us from writing low-level code and helps our programs work on different STM32 boards without many changes.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',1,2,'2025-06-09_11-32-07','__io_putchar is a special function that tells the microcontroller where to send the characters printed by printf. We use it to send those characters over UART, so we can see them on a serial monitor. Without it, printf wouldn’t show any output.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',1,2,'2025-06-09_19-50-20','__io_putchar is a special function that tells the microcontroller how to handle characters printed by printf. In our case, it sends each character over UART so we can see the output on a serial monitor. Without it, printf wouldn’t know where to send the data, and we’d see no output. This function is necessary because printf is generic it doesn’t know what hardware it’s running on or how to send output. By defining __io_putchar, we give it a way to work with the specific hardware in our project.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',1,3,'2025-06-09_11-32-07','We used the debugger because printf can’t show everything, especially low-level things like register values or the stack pointer. The debugger lets us pause the program and directly see the values in memory and registers, which is important when dealing with things like stack setup.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',1,3,'2025-06-09_19-50-20','We used the debugger because printf can’t show everything, especially low-level things like register values or the stack pointer. The debugger lets us pause the program and directly see the values in memory and registers, which is important when dealing with things like stack setup.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',1,4,'2025-06-09_11-32-07','We got MSP_init_val from address 0x0, which stores the start of the main stack. Then we calculated PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 from it, since stacks grow downward. We needed MSP_init_val to know where the stack starts, so we could find a safe place below it for the new PSP stack.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',1,4,'2025-06-09_19-50-20','We got MSP_init_val from address 0x0, which stores the start of the main stack. Then we calculated PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 from it, since stacks grow downward. We needed MSP_init_val to know where the stack starts, so we could find a safe place below it for the new PSP stack.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',1,5,'2025-06-09_11-32-07','We’d keep the main stack size and thread stack size fixed. Then for each thread, we’d calculate its stack start using something like\nMSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE\nWe’d repeat this for however many threads we want, making sure they don’t overlap and stay inside the total stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',1,5,'2025-06-09_19-50-20','We’d keep the main stack size and thread stack size fixed. Then for each thread, we’d calculate its stack start using something like\nMSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE\nWe’d repeat this for however many threads we want, making sure they don’t overlap and stay inside the total stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,1,'2025-06-09_19-44-28','When I changed the Program Counter to hold the address of print_continuously, it caused the CPU to jump to that function and start executing it. It’s as if the function was already running, because we manually placed its address into the stack like it had been interrupted. Once the system call restored the context from the stack, the CPU resumed execution starting from that address so it began running the thread function we pointed to\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,1,'2025-06-10_22-20-33','When I changed the Program Counter to hold the address of print_continuously, it caused the CPU to jump to that function and start executing it. It’s as if the function was already running, because we manually placed its address into the stack like it had been interrupted. Once the system call restored the context from the stack, the CPU resumed execution starting from that address so it began running the thread function we pointed to\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,1,'2025-06-10_22-36-59','When I changed the Program Counter (PC) to point to print_continuously, the CPU immediately started executing that function, just as if it was directly called. By assigning the function\'s address to PC, we essentially invoked the function manually, bypassing the usual function call setup (like pushing return addresses). Since PC holds the address of the next instruction to execute, updating it causes the CPU to jump there instantly. In normal operation, additional steps like saving context are handled automatically during function calls or interrupts, but here we directly manipulated PC without that, which is why it immediately started running print_continuously from the start.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,2,'2025-06-09_19-44-28','A system call is a way for regular programs to ask the operating system to do something it can\'t do on its own. In this lab, we used the SVC instruction to make a system call, which let us do things like switch the stack or start a thread, things that normal code isn\'t allowed to do directly.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,2,'2025-06-10_22-20-33','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a user program request a service from the operating system, usually something it can’t do directly like accessing hardware. It works by switching from unprivileged to privileged mode so the kernel can safely take over. In our lab, we used the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction to trigger a system call. This caused an exception that jumped into a special handler in the OS, allowing us to run kernel code. System calls are important because they provide a safe and controlled way to access protected operations and allow code to run the same across different hardware by abstracting away platform-specific details.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,2,'2025-06-10_22-36-59','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a user program request a service from the operating system, usually something it can’t do directly like accessing hardware. It works by switching from unprivileged to privileged mode so the kernel can safely take over. In our lab, we used the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction to trigger a system call. This caused an exception that jumped into a special handler in the OS, allowing us to run kernel code. System calls are important because they provide a safe and controlled way to access protected operations and allow code to run the same across different hardware by abstracting away platform-specific details.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,3,'2025-06-09_19-44-28','We set up the thread’s stack by putting fake values for the CPU’s registers onto the stack in the exact order the CPU expects. This tricks the CPU into thinking the thread was already running and just returning from an interrupt. We store register values on the stack so that when the CPU pops them back, it has all the info it needs to start running the thread correctly.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,3,'2025-06-10_22-20-33','We set up the thread’s stack by reserving a section of RAM for it and setting the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the top of that memory block. Then, we moved the pointer down and manually placed fake register values in the exact order the CPU expects during context switching. This makes the CPU think the thread was already running and just returning from an interrupt. Setting up the stack like this is necessary because it ensures the CPU has all the information it needs to start running the thread correctly. We store register values on the stack so that the CPU can restore its state when switching between threads.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,3,'2025-06-10_22-36-59','We set up the thread’s stack by reserving a section of RAM for it and setting the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the top of that memory block. Then, we moved the pointer down and manually placed fake register values in the exact order the CPU expects during context switching. This makes the CPU think the thread was already running and just returning from an interrupt. Setting up the stack like this is necessary because it ensures the CPU has all the information it needs to start running the thread correctly. We store register values on the stack so that the CPU can restore its state when switching between threads.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,4,'2025-06-09_19-44-28','We use __set_PSP to point to the thread’s stack. If we don’t do this, the CPU will look in the wrong place for the thread’s data like register values and try to run with garbage data. This can cause a crash because the CPU tries to use invalid memory.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,4,'2025-06-10_22-20-33','We use __set_PSP to point to the thread’s stack. If we don’t do this, the CPU will look in the wrong place for the thread’s data like register values and try to run with garbage data. This can cause a crash because the CPU tries to use invalid memory.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,4,'2025-06-10_22-36-59','The ARM architecture uses two stack pointers MSP (Main Stack Pointer) and PSP (Process Stack Pointer). MSP is used during startup and exceptions, while PSP is used for thread-level code. When starting a thread, we must switch to PSP because the thread\'s context (register values, PC, LR etc.) is stored on its own stack. If we don’t call __set_PSP to point PSP to the correct stack, the CPU would pull invalid or garbage values from MSP, leading to unpredictable behavior and likely causing a hard fault. __set_PSP ensures the CPU uses the proper stack prepared for that specific thread execution.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,5,'2025-06-09_19-44-28','If the thread finishes and returns, it will try to go back to the address in the Link Register. But we gave LR a fake value, so the CPU would try to jump to an invalid address. That would cause a crash. That’s why thread functions like this should loop forever or use a special way to end safely.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,5,'2025-06-10_22-20-33','If the thread finishes and returns, the CPU would try to jump to the address stored in the Link Register (LR). But in our setup, LR was given a fake value, and the thread’s stack doesn’t contain a real return address. This would cause the CPU to jump to an invalid or reserved memory address, leading to a hardfault. The CPU expects that threads like this never return, so if they do, they must exit in a controlled way like calling an OS-level thread exit function. Otherwise they’ll cause undefined behavior and crash the system.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',2,5,'2025-06-10_22-36-59','If the thread finishes and returns, the CPU would try to jump to the address stored in the Link Register (LR). But in our setup, LR was given a fake value, and the thread’s stack doesn’t contain a real return address. This would cause the CPU to jump to an invalid or reserved memory address, leading to a hardfault. The CPU expects that threads like this never return, so if they do, they must exit in a controlled way like calling an OS-level thread exit function. Otherwise they’ll cause undefined behavior and crash the system.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,1,'2025-06-27_00-00-56','When a program contains multiple .c source files, each file is compiled individually into an object file by the compiler. These object files contain machine code for only the specific functions and variables defined in that file. After all files are compiled, the linker combines the object files into a single executable format. During this process, it resolves cross-references between files, for example, if one file uses a function defined in another, the linker connects the two. This compilation allows for better organization and reuse of code across large projects.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,1,'2025-06-27_19-12-05','When a program has multiple .c files, the first step is preprocessing. This handles #include, #define, and other directives. It expands headers and macros into the code, so the compiler sees the full picture. After that, each .c file is compiled on its own into an object file that contains machine code. The compiler only processes that file’s content and any included headers. Then, the linker joins all object files into one executable. It connects functions across files, for example, if main.c uses a function from kernel.c, the linker makes sure they’re properly linked. This process helps organize large programs by keeping related code in separate files.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,1,'2025-06-27_21-22-38','When a program has multiple .c files, the first step is preprocessing. This handles #include, #define, and other directives. It expands headers and macros into the code, so the compiler sees the full picture. After that, each .c file is compiled on its own into an object file that contains machine code. The compiler only processes that file’s content and any included headers. Then, the linker joins all object files into one executable. It connects functions across files, for example, if main.c uses a function from kernel.c, the linker makes sure they’re properly linked. This process helps organize large programs by keeping related code in separate files.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,1,'2025-06-27_22-30-55','When a program has multiple .c files, the first step is preprocessing. This handles #include, #define, and other directives. It expands headers and macros into the code, so the compiler sees the full picture. After that, each .c file is compiled on its own into an object file that contains machine code. The compiler only processes that file’s content and any included headers. Then, the linker joins all object files into one executable. It connects functions across files, for example, if main.c uses a function from kernel.c, the linker makes sure they’re properly linked. This process helps organize large programs by keeping related code in separate files.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,2,'2025-06-27_00-00-56','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because .c files should be compiled separately, not pasted into other files. Including a .c file mixes everything into one big file before compiling, which can cause problems like duplicated code or strange errors. Instead, we should use header files (.h) to share function declarations between files. This keeps the code organized and avoids bugs. The compiler is designed to work with .c files on their own, so including them directly breaks the normal build process and makes the project harder to manage.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,2,'2025-06-27_19-12-05','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because .c files should be compiled separately, not pasted into other files. Including a .c file mixes everything into one big file before compiling, which can cause problems like duplicated code or strange errors. Instead, we should use header files (.h) to share function declarations between files. This keeps the code organized and avoids bugs. The compiler is designed to work with .c files on their own, so including them directly breaks the normal build process and makes the project harder to manage.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,2,'2025-06-27_21-22-38','Including a .c file like #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it disrupts the standard compilation and linking process. Normally, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file, and then the linker combines these object files into an executable. If you include a .c file in another source file, its function and variable definitions are copied directly into that file. If this inclusion happens in multiple files, the linker will encounter multiple definitions of the same functions causing linker errors. This is why we use header files to declare functions and variables, while definitions remain in .c files. This approach avoids duplication, keeps code modular, and ensures the build system works as intended.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,2,'2025-06-27_22-30-55','Including a .c file like #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it disrupts the standard compilation and linking process. Normally, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file, and then the linker combines these object files into an executable. If you include a .c file in another source file, its function and variable definitions are copied directly into that file. If this inclusion happens in multiple files, the linker will encounter multiple definitions of the same functions causing linker errors. This is why we use header files to declare functions and variables, while definitions remain in .c files. This approach avoids duplication, keeps code modular, and ensures the build system works as intended.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,3,'2025-06-27_00-00-56','To set up and start a thread as a user, you follow three steps. First, you call osKernelInitialize() to prepare the operating system. This sets up important memory and resets internal data. Second, you call osCreateThread() and give it the function that you want the thread to run. This creates the thread and allocates memory for it. Third, you call osKernelStart() to actually start running the thread you created. Once this is done, your thread takes over and begins running the function you gave it. These steps let you run user code with help from the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,3,'2025-06-27_19-12-05','To set up and start a thread, the user begins by writing the thread function, which contains the specific code they want the thread to run. This is an important step because it defines the thread\'s behavior. Next, the user calls osKernelInitialize() to prepare the kernel\'s internal state and memory. Once the kernel is ready, the user calls osCreateThread() and passes in the thread function they wrote. This sets up the thread and allocates a stack for it. Finally, the user calls osKernelStart() to begin execution, allowing the kernel to switch to thread mode and run the thread. This process gives the user control over what the thread does, while the kernel handles the low-level setup.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,3,'2025-06-27_21-22-38','To set up and start a thread, the user begins by writing the thread function, which contains the specific code they want the thread to run. This is an important step because it defines the thread\'s behavior. Next, the user calls osKernelInitialize() to prepare the kernel\'s internal state and memory. Once the kernel is ready, the user calls osCreateThread() and passes in the thread function they wrote. This sets up the thread and allocates a stack for it. Finally, the user calls osKernelStart() to begin execution, allowing the kernel to switch to thread mode and run the thread. This process gives the user control over what the thread does, while the kernel handles the low-level setup.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,3,'2025-06-27_22-30-55','To set up and start a thread, the user begins by writing the thread function, which contains the specific code they want the thread to run. This is an important step because it defines the thread\'s behavior. Next, the user calls osKernelInitialize() to prepare the kernel\'s internal state and memory. Once the kernel is ready, the user calls osCreateThread() and passes in the thread function they wrote. This sets up the thread and allocates a stack for it. Finally, the user calls osKernelStart() to begin execution, allowing the kernel to switch to thread mode and run the thread. This process gives the user control over what the thread does, while the kernel handles the low-level setup.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,4,'2025-06-27_00-00-56','The stack pool is a big block of memory saved for thread stacks. Every thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls and variables. Instead of creating stacks manually, the kernel uses the stack pool to give each thread its own space. Each new thread gets a fixed-size portion of this pool. In future labs, when we run multiple threads, the kernel will use this pool to give each thread its own memory area, so they don’t interfere with each other. This helps keep the program running safely and lets more threads be added easily.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,4,'2025-06-27_19-12-05','The stack pool is a big block of memory saved for thread stacks. Every thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls and variables. Instead of creating stacks manually, the kernel uses the stack pool to give each thread its own space. Each new thread gets a fixed-size portion of this pool. In future labs, when we run multiple threads, the kernel will use this pool to give each thread its own memory area, so they don’t interfere with each other. This helps keep the program running safely and lets more threads be added easily.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,4,'2025-06-27_21-22-38','The stack pool is a big block of memory saved for thread stacks. Every thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls and variables. Instead of creating stacks manually, the kernel uses the stack pool to give each thread its own space. Each new thread gets a fixed-size portion of this pool. In future labs, when we run multiple threads, the kernel will use this pool to give each thread its own memory area, so they don’t interfere with each other. This helps keep the program running safely and lets more threads be added easily.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,4,'2025-06-27_22-30-55','The stack pool is a dedicated block of memory reserved for allocating stacks to both the operating system and individual threads. Each thread needs its own private stack to store local variables, return addresses, and manage function calls. Similarly, the OS may need stack space during system calls or interrupt handling. Instead of dynamically creating stacks, the kernel divides this stack pool into fixed size chunks and assigns one to each new thread. This design ensures that threads do not overwrite each other’s memory and that the OS has its own reliable stack space for critical operations. In future labs, this pool will be used to support multiple concurrent threads while maintaining system stability. Proper stack pool management is key to safe multitasking and prevents memory corruption between threads and the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,5,'2025-06-27_00-00-56','The user and kernel are kept separate to keep things simple and safe. The user writes code like the main function and thread functions in main.c. The kernel handles low-level tasks like stack allocation and thread management, and this goes into kernel.c. The user only interacts with the kernel by calling functions. This setup is important because it hides complex system details from the user. It also helps prevent bugs and makes it easier to update the kernel later without breaking user programs.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,5,'2025-06-27_19-12-05','The user and kernel are kept separate to keep things simple and safe. The user writes code like the main function and thread functions in main.c. The kernel handles low-level tasks like stack allocation and thread management, and this goes into kernel.c. The user only interacts with the kernel by calling functions. This setup is important because it hides complex system details from the user. It also helps prevent bugs and makes it easier to update the kernel later without breaking user programs.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,5,'2025-06-27_21-22-38','The user and kernel are kept separate to keep things simple and safe. The user writes code like the main function and thread functions in main.c. The kernel handles low-level tasks like stack allocation and thread management, and this goes into kernel.c. The user only interacts with the kernel by calling functions. This setup is important because it hides complex system details from the user. It also helps prevent bugs and makes it easier to update the kernel later without breaking user programs.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',3,5,'2025-06-27_22-30-55','The user and the kernel are kept in separate files to make things simple, safe and organized. User code like main() and thread functions goes in main.c, while kernel code like memory management, stack allocation, and thread control goes in kernel.c. The user only uses the kernel by calling its functions, they don’t need to see or change how it works. This is important because it hides complex details and prevents user mistakes from affecting the system. Also, the kernel can be updated or improved later (like fixing bugs or adding features) without changing any user programs. Even though filenames like kernel.c may vary across systems, the idea stays the same: the kernel handles the system\'s core, and the user focuses only on their own code.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,1,'2025-07-28_22-46-37','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by popping the currently running thread from the front scheduling it and pushing it to the back after it yields. This avoids managing indices manually like in an array. A queue can simplify thread insertion and removal especially for dynamic thread creation or deletion. It\'s also conceptually closer to how some real OS kernels handle task management.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,1,'2025-07-28_23-21-18','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented with a queue, but the current scheduler already behaves like a circular array queue by rotating through the threads in a fixed order. Using a queue data structure would add dynamic flexibility but at the cost of extra overhead in terms of memory usage and pointer management. Arrays offer constant time access and are more predictable for a small and fixed number of threads. So while a queue allows more generalized task management, the simplicity and performance of the array outweigh those benefits. It’s important to evaluate whether added complexity provides meaningful advantages in this constrained environment.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,1,'2025-07-28_23-56-49','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented with a queue, but the current scheduler already behaves like a circular array queue by rotating through the threads in a fixed order. Using a queue data structure would add dynamic flexibility but at the cost of extra overhead in terms of memory usage and pointer management. Arrays offer constant time access and are more predictable for a small and fixed number of threads. So while a queue allows more generalized task management, the simplicity and performance of the array outweigh those benefits. It’s important to evaluate whether added complexity provides meaningful advantages in this constrained environment.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,1,'2025-07-29_00-11-57','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented with a queue, but the current scheduler already behaves like a circular array queue by rotating through the threads in a fixed order. Using a queue data structure would add dynamic flexibility but at the cost of extra overhead in terms of memory usage and pointer management. Arrays offer constant time access and are more predictable for a small and fixed number of threads. So while a queue allows more generalized task management, the simplicity and performance of the array outweigh those benefits. It’s important to evaluate whether added complexity provides meaningful advantages in this constrained environment.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,1,'2025-07-29_00-25-24','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented with a queue, but the current scheduler already behaves like a circular array queue by rotating through the threads in a fixed order. Using a queue data structure would add dynamic flexibility but at the cost of extra overhead in terms of memory usage and pointer management. Arrays offer constant time access and are more predictable for a small and fixed number of threads. So while a queue allows more generalized task management, the simplicity and performance of the array outweigh those benefits. It’s important to evaluate whether added complexity provides meaningful advantages in this constrained environment.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,2,'2025-07-28_22-46-37','PendSV_Handler saves the CPU registers of the current thread to its stack and updates its stack pointer. Then it calls osSched, which switches currentThread and sets the new thread’s PSP. After returning, PendSV loads the new thread’s registers and resumes execution. This lets the processor continue running the next thread as if it had never stopped.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,2,'2025-07-28_23-21-18','The context switch in PendSV_Handler starts by using the PSP to access the current thread’s stack. This is crucial because each thread maintains its own stack and PSP points to where the CPU was interrupted. We save registers R4–R11 onto this stack to preserve the thread’s state. Then, osSched is called to determine the next runnable thread. After that, we load that thread\'s saved PSP value and restore registers R4–R11 from its stack, effectively switching context. Finally, execution resumes by exiting the exception handler using the updated PSP. This mechanism ensures each thread resumes correctly from where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,2,'2025-07-28_23-56-49','The context switch in PendSV_Handler starts by using the PSP to access the current thread’s stack. This is crucial because each thread maintains its own stack and PSP points to where the CPU was interrupted. We save registers R4–R11 onto this stack to preserve the thread’s state. Then, osSched is called to determine the next runnable thread. After that, we load that thread\'s saved PSP value and restore registers R4–R11 from its stack, effectively switching context. Finally, execution resumes by exiting the exception handler using the updated PSP. This mechanism ensures each thread resumes correctly from where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,2,'2025-07-29_00-11-57','The context switch in PendSV_Handler starts by using the PSP to access the current thread’s stack. This is crucial because each thread maintains its own stack and PSP points to where the CPU was interrupted. We save registers R4–R11 onto this stack to preserve the thread’s state. Then, osSched is called to determine the next runnable thread. After that, we load that thread\'s saved PSP value and restore registers R4–R11 from its stack, effectively switching context. Finally, execution resumes by exiting the exception handler using the updated PSP. This mechanism ensures each thread resumes correctly from where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,2,'2025-07-29_00-25-24','The context switch in PendSV_Handler starts by using the PSP to access the current thread’s stack. This is crucial because each thread maintains its own stack and PSP points to where the CPU was interrupted. We save registers R4–R11 onto this stack to preserve the thread’s state. Then, osSched is called to determine the next runnable thread. After that, we load that thread\'s saved PSP value and restore registers R4–R11 from its stack, effectively switching context. Finally, execution resumes by exiting the exception handler using the updated PSP. This mechanism ensures each thread resumes correctly from where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,3,'2025-07-28_22-46-37','If the scheduler always picks the highest-priority task,lower-priority threads may never run this is known as starvation. In our co-operative setup, the high priority thread would keep executing unless it yields, potentially blocking all other threads indefinitely. This breaks fairness and system responsiveness. Priority based scheduling is only safe when paired with pre-emption and aging mechanisms, which we don\'t have yet.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,3,'2025-07-28_23-21-18','Cooperative scheduling relies on threads voluntarily yielding control. If a thread fails to yield due to a bug or long computation, it can starve other threads by using most of the CPU. This makes fairness more difficult to guarantee. Preemptive scheduling addresses this by allowing the OS to force context switches using interrupts like SysTick. While cooperative models are simpler and require less overhead they risk fairness and can be dangerous in real-time system. In contrast, preemptive systems offer better multitasking and responsiveness but require more careful synchronization.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,3,'2025-07-28_23-56-49','A static priority scheduler that always runs the highest priority task can lead to starvation of lower priority tasks. If a high priority thread is always ready to run lower-priority threads may never get CPU time. This becomes a serious problem in systems where background or maintenance tasks are essential for stability. Unlike dynamic systems that adjust priorities based on conditions or aging, static priority schedulers don’t adapt so fairness suffers. In real time systems this may violate deadlines or lead to system failure if critical low-priority tasks are blocked indefinitely.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,3,'2025-07-29_00-11-57','A static priority scheduler that always runs the highest priority task can lead to starvation of lower priority tasks. If a high priority thread is always ready to run lower-priority threads may never get CPU time. This becomes a serious problem in systems where background or maintenance tasks are essential for stability. Unlike dynamic systems that adjust priorities based on conditions or aging, static priority schedulers don’t adapt so fairness suffers. In real time systems this may violate deadlines or lead to system failure if critical low-priority tasks are blocked indefinitely.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,3,'2025-07-29_00-25-24','In a static priority scheduler, the system always runs the highest priority task that is ready. The issue is that if this task never blocks or yields the scheduler will continuously select it ignoring all lower-priority tasks. This rigid behavior means lower-priority threads may never get CPU time resulting in starvation. The scheduler doesn’t evaluate how long a task has been waiting or whether other tasks are being starved, it simply prioritizes based on fixed levels. Without mechanisms like priority aging or dynamic adjustments, the system risks running only one task indefinitely making it unfair and potentially unsafe in multitasking environments.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,4,'2025-07-28_22-46-37','Calling C functions improves code readability, maintainability, and allows easier debugging using standard tools. It makes logic like scheduling or thread creation clearer and more modular. However, C introduces compiler generated code and stack usage, which may reduce predictability in low-level handlers. Assembly offers more precise control over registers and performance but is harder to write and modify. Our hybrid approach balances clarity and low-level access.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,4,'2025-07-28_23-21-18','Using C provides readability, portability and make it easy to develop especially for complex systems. Assembly, gives closer control over registers and execution which is critical for performance and low level hardware interactions. In our case where performance and memory constraints are real concerns a hybrid approach makes sense. C for general logic and assembly for critical sections like interrupt handlers. This trade off helps balance clarity and control. In a real-time embedded system choosing between them depends on context for timing critical paths.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,4,'2025-07-28_23-56-49','Using C provides readability, portability and make it easy to develop especially for complex systems. Assembly, gives closer control over registers and execution which is critical for performance and low level hardware interactions. In our case where performance and memory constraints are real concerns a hybrid approach makes sense. C for general logic and assembly for critical sections like interrupt handlers. This trade off helps balance clarity and control. In a real-time embedded system choosing between them depends on context for timing critical paths.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,4,'2025-07-29_00-11-57','Using C provides readability, portability and make it easy to develop especially for complex systems. Assembly, gives closer control over registers and execution which is critical for performance and low level hardware interactions. In our case where performance and memory constraints are real concerns a hybrid approach makes sense. C for general logic and assembly for critical sections like interrupt handlers. This trade off helps balance clarity and control. In a real-time embedded system choosing between them depends on context for timing critical paths.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,4,'2025-07-29_00-25-24','Using C provides readability, portability and make it easy to develop especially for complex systems. Assembly, gives closer control over registers and execution which is critical for performance and low level hardware interactions. In our case where performance and memory constraints are real concerns a hybrid approach makes sense. C for general logic and assembly for critical sections like interrupt handlers. This trade off helps balance clarity and control. In a real-time embedded system choosing between them depends on context for timing critical paths.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,5,'2025-07-28_22-46-37','The OS would still function, but context switching becomes redundant with only one thread. Each call to osYield() would trigger PendSV and invoke osSched, but it would just restore the same thread’s stack and resume where it left off. While harmless this adds unnecessary overhead. It demonstrates that the OS is functional but highlights that multitasking only adds value when multiple threads are actually present.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,5,'2025-07-28_23-21-18','The OS will still run with only a single thread such as a modified print_continuously function that yields after printing. Each call to osYield() triggers a PendSV interrupt saving and restoring the thread\'s context, but since there’s only one thread, it always just resumes itself. This process works, confirming that the scheduler is functional, but the context switching adds unnecessary overhead with no actual benefit. In a real system this would waste CPU cycles. However, single thread setups can still be useful for testing thread creation or for very simple systems without concurrency needs.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,5,'2025-07-28_23-56-49','The OS will still run with only a single thread such as a modified print_continuously function that yields after printing. Each call to osYield() triggers a PendSV interrupt saving and restoring the thread\'s context, but since there’s only one thread, it always just resumes itself. This process works, confirming that the scheduler is functional, but the context switching adds unnecessary overhead with no actual benefit. In a real system this would waste CPU cycles. However, single thread setups can still be useful for testing thread creation or for very simple systems without concurrency needs.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,5,'2025-07-29_00-11-57','The OS will still run with only a single thread such as a modified print_continuously function that yields after printing. Each call to osYield() triggers a PendSV interrupt saving and restoring the thread\'s context, but since there’s only one thread, it always just resumes itself. This process works, confirming that the scheduler is functional, but the context switching adds unnecessary overhead with no actual benefit. In a real system this would waste CPU cycles. However, single thread setups can still be useful for testing thread creation or for very simple systems without concurrency needs.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',4,5,'2025-07-29_00-25-24','The OS will still run with only a single thread such as a modified print_continuously function that yields after printing. Each call to osYield() triggers a PendSV interrupt saving and restoring the thread\'s context, but since there’s only one thread, it always just resumes itself. This process works, confirming that the scheduler is functional, but the context switching adds unnecessary overhead with no actual benefit. In a real system this would waste CPU cycles. However, single thread setups can still be useful for testing thread creation or for very simple systems without concurrency needs.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,1,'2025-07-28_22-57-51','To pass an argument into a thread, the user creates a struct or variable and passes its pointer when calling osCreateThread. The OS stores this pointer in R0 during stack setup so the thread receives it as a void* argument. Inside the thread function, the user casts and dereferences the pointer to access the actual data. This works because R0 is the standard register for passing the first function argument in ARM. By placing the pointer there, the thread starts with the right data automatically.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,1,'2025-07-28_23-46-12','To pass an argument into a thread, the user creates a struct or variable and passes its pointer when calling osCreateThread. The OS stores this pointer in R0 during stack setup so the thread receives it as a void* argument. Inside the thread function, the user casts and dereferences the pointer to access the actual data. This works because R0 is the standard register for passing the first function argument in ARM. By placing the pointer there, the thread starts with the right data automatically.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,1,'2025-07-29_00-06-18','To pass an argument into a thread, the user creates a struct or variable and passes its pointer when calling osCreateThread. The OS stores this pointer in R0 during stack setup so the thread receives it as a void* argument. Inside the thread function, the user casts and dereferences the pointer to access the actual data. This works because R0 is the standard register for passing the first function argument in ARM. By placing the pointer there, the thread starts with the right data automatically.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,1,'2025-07-29_00-20-18','To pass an argument into a thread the user wraps the needed data into a variable or struct and passes a pointer to it when calling osCreateThread. The OS doesn’t write directly to the R0 register instead it sets up the thread’s initial stack so that when the thread is restored and begins executing, R0 will contain the pointer. This is done by placing the pointer in the correct spot on the stack specifically where R0 would be restored from during exception return. The first function argument is passed via R0, so when the thread function starts running, it sees the pointer as its argument. The user can then cast and dereference it inside the thread function to access the actual data.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,2,'2025-07-28_22-57-51','You can’t call osYield() inside PendSV, SVC, or SysTick handlers because those are already part of the context-switching process. Calling osYield there would trigger another context switch while one is already in progress, which could corrupt the stack or crash the system. It basically creates nested switches, and the OS isn\'t built to handle that safely. You’d likely end up in a hard fault or infinite interrupt loop.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,2,'2025-07-28_23-46-12','osYield() works by triggering an SVC interrupt which leads to a PendSV exception that performs the context switch. If you call osYield() inside SysTick_Handler or PendSV_Handler, you\'re essentially requesting an interrupt while already handling one which causes nested context switches. The CortexM4 supports nested interrupts but initiating one switch inside another can corrupt the stack or leave the processor in an invalid state. This disrupts the consistency of the context-switching process. Instead the switching mechanism should complete before any new switch is initiated to keep thread management safe and consistent.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,2,'2025-07-29_00-06-18','You can’t call osYield() inside PendSV SVC or SysTick handlers because osYield() itself works by triggering an SVC interrupt which leads to a PendSV exception to perform a context switch. If you try to call it from inside one of these handlers you’re effectively starting a context switch while one is already in progress, which violates the expected call structure. This causes nested context switches, which can lead to stack corruption, leave the processor in an inconsistent state and most critically, result in a hard fault. The Cortex-M4’s interrupt nesting rules don’t protect against logic-level misuse like this, so the OS must avoid re-entering critical switching code during its own execution.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,2,'2025-07-29_00-20-18','You can’t call osYield() inside PendSV SVC or SysTick handlers because osYield() itself works by triggering an SVC interrupt which leads to a PendSV exception to perform a context switch. If you try to call it from inside one of these handlers you’re effectively starting a context switch while one is already in progress, which violates the expected call structure. This causes nested context switches, which can lead to stack corruption, leave the processor in an inconsistent state and most critically, result in a hard fault. The Cortex-M4’s interrupt nesting rules don’t protect against logic-level misuse like this, so the OS must avoid re-entering critical switching code during its own execution.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,3,'2025-07-28_22-57-51','Using a 0.1 ms clock would let the OS respond to events with better timing accuracy, which is great for high-speed or real-time tasks. But it also means ten times more interrupts per second, which increases CPU overhead and power usage. It could slow down your threads because the OS keeps switching. For most tasks, 1 ms is already fast enough and much more efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,3,'2025-07-28_23-46-12','Using a 0.1 ms clock would let the OS respond to events with better timing accuracy, which is great for high-speed or real-time tasks. But it also means ten times more interrupts per second, which increases CPU overhead and power usage. It could slow down your threads because the OS keeps switching. For most tasks, 1 ms is already fast enough and much more efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,3,'2025-07-29_00-06-18','Using a 0.1 ms clock would let the OS respond to events with better timing accuracy, which is great for high-speed or real-time tasks. But it also means ten times more interrupts per second, which increases CPU overhead and power usage. It could slow down your threads because the OS keeps switching. For most tasks, 1 ms is already fast enough and much more efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,3,'2025-07-29_00-20-18','Using a 0.1 ms clock would let the OS respond to events with better timing accuracy, which is great for high-speed or real-time tasks. But it also means ten times more interrupts per second, which increases CPU overhead and power usage. It could slow down your threads because the OS keeps switching. For most tasks, 1 ms is already fast enough and much more efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,4,'2025-07-28_22-57-51','In cooperative multitasking, the user controls when a thread gives up the CPU by calling osYield(). The OS just saves state and switches when asked. In preemptive multitasking, the OS handles switching based on time, without the thread’s permission. From the user’s view, cooperative feels more manual, while preemptive “just happens.” The OS has to track time and trigger switches automatically for preemption.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,4,'2025-07-28_23-46-12','In co-operative multitasking threads must voluntarily yield control using osYield(). The OS just waits for this call then performs a context switch using PendSV. The user has full control but a buggy thread can hog the CPU. In contrast, preemptive multitasking relies on interrupts specifically SysTick_Handler, to force a context switch after a time slice expires. The OS tracks how long each thread runs and forces a switch by triggering PendSV when necessary. This ensures fairness even if a thread doesn’t yield. From the user’s view, cooperative multitasking is predictable while preemptive feels more automatic but is backed by precise hardware timing.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,4,'2025-07-29_00-06-18','In co-operative multitasking threads must voluntarily yield control using osYield(). The OS just waits for this call then performs a context switch using PendSV. The user has full control but a buggy thread can hog the CPU. In contrast, preemptive multitasking relies on interrupts specifically SysTick_Handler, to force a context switch after a time slice expires. The OS tracks how long each thread runs and forces a switch by triggering PendSV when necessary. This ensures fairness even if a thread doesn’t yield. From the user’s view, cooperative multitasking is predictable while preemptive feels more automatic but is backed by precise hardware timing.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,4,'2025-07-29_00-20-18','In co-operative multitasking threads must voluntarily yield control using osYield(). The OS just waits for this call then performs a context switch using PendSV. The user has full control but a buggy thread can hog the CPU. In contrast, preemptive multitasking relies on interrupts specifically SysTick_Handler, to force a context switch after a time slice expires. The OS tracks how long each thread runs and forces a switch by triggering PendSV when necessary. This ensures fairness even if a thread doesn’t yield. From the user’s view, cooperative multitasking is predictable while preemptive feels more automatic but is backed by precise hardware timing.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,5,'2025-07-28_22-57-51','These two can work together because the OS checks both the time slice (for preemption) and whether a thread yields voluntarily. If a thread calls osYield(), the OS switches right away. If it doesn’t, the OS still preempts it when its time is up. As long as both mechanisms reset the runtime and trigger a switch properly, they don’t conflict and can run side-by-side.\n',0,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,5,'2025-07-28_23-46-12','Both yielding and preemption ultimately trigger the same context switching logic via PendSV. When a thread calls osYield(), the OS manually sets PendSV to switch threads and resets the runtime counter. Meanwhile, SysTick_Handler fires every 1 ms, and once a thread\'s runtime hits zero, it preemptively triggers PendSV too. What’s going on here is that both mechanisms feed into the same handler so the OS doesn’t care who initiated the switch. Whether the thread gave up control or the OS forcibly took it the transition is seamless. This unified approach is why co-operative and pre-emptive threads can coexist smoothly.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,5,'2025-07-29_00-06-18','Both yielding and preemption ultimately trigger the same context switching logic via PendSV. When a thread calls osYield(), the OS manually sets PendSV to switch threads and resets the runtime counter. Meanwhile, SysTick_Handler fires every 1 ms, and once a thread\'s runtime hits zero, it preemptively triggers PendSV too. What’s going on here is that both mechanisms feed into the same handler so the OS doesn’t care who initiated the switch. Whether the thread gave up control or the OS forcibly took it the transition is seamless. This unified approach is why co-operative and pre-emptive threads can coexist smoothly.\n',1,'s25'),('i2kapur',5,5,'2025-07-29_00-20-18','Both yielding and preemption ultimately trigger the same context switching logic via PendSV. When a thread calls osYield(), the OS manually sets PendSV to switch threads and resets the runtime counter. Meanwhile, SysTick_Handler fires every 1 ms, and once a thread\'s runtime hits zero, it preemptively triggers PendSV too. What’s going on here is that both mechanisms feed into the same handler so the OS doesn’t care who initiated the switch. Whether the thread gave up control or the OS forcibly took it the transition is seamless. This unified approach is why co-operative and pre-emptive threads can coexist smoothly.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',1,1,'2025-05-13_10-01-45','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) contains the code required by the STM32 to setup the components and prepare the microcontroller for running the user-defined code. It is used in general embedded projects because the HAL code can be very abstract, time-consuming, and difficult to program for every new project, so the HAL simplifies the process by doing the hard work for us, making development easier on the microcontroller. In a general embedded project, precise control over the setup code may not be required, making the HAL a useful tool.\n',1,'s25');
INSERT INTO `question_responses` VALUES ('i5garg',1,1,'2025-05-13_10-26-48','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) contains the code required by the STM32 to setup the components and prepare the microcontroller for running the user-defined code. It is used in general embedded projects because the HAL code can be very abstract, time-consuming, and difficult to program for every new project, so the HAL simplifies the process by doing the hard work for us, making development easier on the microcontroller. In a general embedded project, precise control over the setup code may not be required, making the HAL a useful tool.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',1,1,'2025-05-13_10-39-47','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) contains the code required by the STM32 to setup the components and prepare the microcontroller for running the user-defined code. It is used in general embedded projects because the HAL code can be very abstract, time-consuming, and difficult to program for every new project, so the HAL simplifies the process by doing the hard work for us, making development easier on the microcontroller. In a general embedded project, precise control over the setup code may not be required, making the HAL a useful tool.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',1,2,'2025-05-13_10-01-45','The __io_putchar function makes it possible to use printf statements with the microcontroller. This is because the STM32 requires using serial port communication functions – like HAL_UART_Transmit – to send and receive bytes. Thus, the STM32 uses the _io_putchar function to make the printf statement function similarly to how it works outside of embedded systems – it transmits the printf statement’s bytes to the microcontroller and allows them to be printed.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',1,2,'2025-05-13_10-26-48','The __io_putchar function is a helper that enables the printf function to work in the STM32. Since printf by default doesn’t know how to send output to the microcontroller’s serial port, __io_putchar is used to define how individual characters are transmitted using HAL_UART_Transmit. Making __io_putchar a helper function instead of putting hardware-specific code directly into printf keeps the code modular and portable. If the hardware changes – for example, from UART to USB – the implementation of __io_putchar can be updated without changing every printf statement in the code. This approach is part of a hardware abstraction layer (HAL), where low-level hardware details are separated from higher-level application logic. __io_putchar serves as a bridge between standard C library functions and the specific hardware being used, making development easier across embedded platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',1,2,'2025-05-13_10-39-47','The __io_putchar function is a helper that enables the printf function to work in the STM32. Since printf by default doesn’t know how to send output to the microcontroller’s serial port, __io_putchar is used to define how individual characters are transmitted using HAL_UART_Transmit. Making __io_putchar a helper function instead of putting hardware-specific code directly into printf keeps the code modular and portable. If the hardware changes – for example, from UART to USB – the implementation of __io_putchar can be updated without changing every printf statement in the code. This approach is part of a hardware abstraction layer (HAL), where low-level hardware details are separated from higher-level application logic. __io_putchar serves as a bridge between standard C library functions and the specific hardware being used, making development easier across embedded platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',1,3,'2025-05-13_10-01-45','We had to use the debugger and not printf statements because printf affects the stack, meaning that the printed values would not provide the data that we were looking for to confirm the stack worked, given how it would interfere with the results. This is evidenced by the fact that printf uses HAL_UART_Transmit, which essentially requires a buffer to transmit byte by byte – and that uses some memory, thereby requiring the use of the debugger.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',1,3,'2025-05-13_10-26-48','We had to use the debugger because printf cannot access or display the values stored in the processor’s internal registers, such as the stack pointer or program counter. These registers are part of the CPU\'s internal state and are not directly accessible through standard C functions. The debugger, on the other hand, provides a low-level view of the system, allowing us to pause execution and inspect these registers directly – something printf simply can\'t do. Additionally, printf consumes stack memory and can alter the program’s state, especially when testing stack behavior. This interference could mask the very states we are trying to observe. The debugger avoids this by being non-intrusive – it reads the hardware state without modifying it – making it essential for microcontrollers.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',1,3,'2025-05-13_10-39-47','We had to use the debugger because printf cannot access or display the values stored in the processor’s internal registers, such as the stack pointer or program counter. These registers are part of the CPU\'s internal state and are not directly accessible through standard C functions. The debugger, on the other hand, provides a low-level view of the system, allowing us to pause execution and inspect these registers directly – something printf simply can\'t do. Additionally, printf consumes stack memory and can alter the program’s state, especially when testing stack behavior. This interference could mask the very states we are trying to observe. The debugger avoids this by being non-intrusive – it reads the hardware state without modifying it – making it essential for microcontrollers.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',1,4,'2025-05-13_10-01-45','The stack allocation method we used works by using the MSP_init_val and PSP_val to define the memory bounds of the stack; the MSP_init_val defines the start of the stack in memory, and we got the value for PSP_val by subtracting the desired stack size from the initial MSP memory address. This created a block of memory starting at 0x0 for the stack to access. We needed to know the initial location of MSP because the size of the stack is defined by two memory addresses – the initial MSP and the PSP – so the total size of the stack in bytes can be set by knowing the initial memory address – the initial MSP value – and getting the final memory address by subtracting the difference.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',1,4,'2025-05-13_10-26-48','The stack allocation method we used works by using the MSP_init_val and PSP_val to define the memory bounds of the stack; the MSP_init_val defines the start of the stack in memory, and we got the value for PSP_val by subtracting the desired stack size from the initial MSP memory address. This created a block of memory starting at 0x0 for the stack to access. We needed to know the initial location of MSP because the size of the stack is defined by two memory addresses – the initial MSP and the PSP – so the total size of the stack in bytes can be set by knowing the initial memory address – the initial MSP value – and getting the final memory address by subtracting the difference.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',1,4,'2025-05-13_10-39-47','Knowing the initial value of the MSP, or main stack pointer, is essential because it defines the top of the default stack memory region when the STM32 starts up. When allocating a PSP, or process stack pointer, we typically compute it by subtracting the desired stack size from the initial MSP value. This ensures that the PSP points to a valid memory address that lies within a known, valid memory region allocated for stacks. If we didn’t base the PSP on the initial MSP – or used a hardcoded or incorrect value like 0x0 – we could accidentally assign a PSP value outside of valid memory, leading to invalid memory access. Overall, using the initial MSP as a reference helps ensure that the PSP is properly aligned, within valid memory bounds, and doesn\'t overlap with other memory regions or stacks – which is crucial for system correctness.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',1,5,'2025-05-13_10-01-45','To make this work, there would need to be a list of initial MSP memory addresses and each stack’s associated size in bytes in order to keep track of where each one starts and where it ends, as the size of the stack helps indicate where to stop as long as the initial MSP memory address is known.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',1,5,'2025-05-13_10-26-48','We can allocate multiple stacks by starting from a single known base stack address and computing offsets from it. For example, we can define a top-of-stack memory location (like the initial MSP) and calculate the start of each thread’s stack by subtracting N * STACK_SIZE for the Nth stack. To prevent stacks from overlapping or going out of bounds, the total memory allocated for all stacks should be reserved in advance. By calculating each new stack as an offset from the base and checking that the end of the last stack stays within this region, we ensure correctness and memory safety.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',1,5,'2025-05-13_10-39-47','We can allocate multiple stacks by starting from a single known base stack address and computing offsets from it. For example, we can define a top-of-stack memory location (like the initial MSP) and calculate the start of each thread’s stack by subtracting N * STACK_SIZE for the Nth stack. To prevent stacks from overlapping or going out of bounds, the total memory allocated for all stacks should be reserved in advance. By calculating each new stack as an offset from the base and checking that the end of the last stack stays within this region, we ensure correctness and memory safety.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,1,'2025-05-22_22-42-27','When the PC register was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the thread was given context so that when the new thread was started, the CPU immediately popped the new register values and began executing them as if it had only been interrupted – and since PC was loaded with the pointer to the print continuously function, that function began executing.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',2,1,'2025-05-22_23-04-16','When the PC register was set to the address of the print continuously function, the CPU began executing that function as if it had been called normally. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to execute, so changing it directly to point to a function caused the processor to jump to and run that function.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,1,'2025-05-22_23-40-42','When the PC register was set to the address of the print continuously function, the CPU began executing that function as if it had been called normally. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to execute, so changing it directly to point to a function caused the processor to jump to and run that function.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,1,'2025-05-22_23-58-12','When the PC register was set to the address of the print continuously function, the CPU began executing that function as if it had been called normally. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to execute, so changing it directly to point to a function caused the processor to jump to and run that function.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,1,'2025-06-26_23-17-09','When the PC register was set to the address of the print continuously function, the CPU began executing that function as if it had been called normally. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to execute, so changing it directly to point to a function caused the processor to jump to and run that function.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,2,'2025-05-22_22-42-27','A system call is a way for the user to request a service from the operating system, acting like a gatekeeper to protect the OS from potentially malicious low-level software actions.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',2,2,'2025-05-22_23-04-16','A system call is a special kind of function call that allows user-level programs to request services from the operating system\'s kernel. The kernel is the core part of the operating system that acts as a bridge between user applications and hardware, managing the most fundamental system operations. As such, a system call provides a controlled way for user code to access low-level operations, like reading from a file or communicating with hardware, while keeping the kernel protected from unsafe or unauthorized access.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,2,'2025-05-22_23-40-42','A system call is a special kind of function call that allows user-level programs to request services from the operating system\'s kernel. The kernel is the core part of the operating system that acts as a bridge between user applications and hardware, managing the most fundamental system operations. As such, a system call provides a controlled way for user code to access low-level operations, like reading from a file or communicating with hardware, while keeping the kernel protected from unsafe or unauthorized access.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,2,'2025-05-22_23-58-12','A system call is a special kind of function call that allows user-level programs to request services from the operating system\'s kernel. The kernel is the core part of the operating system that acts as a bridge between user applications and hardware, managing the most fundamental system operations. As such, a system call provides a controlled way for user code to access low-level operations, like reading from a file or communicating with hardware, while keeping the kernel protected from unsafe or unauthorized access.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,2,'2025-06-26_23-17-09','A system call is a special kind of function call that allows user-level programs to request services from the operating system\'s kernel. The kernel is the core part of the operating system that acts as a bridge between user applications and hardware, managing the most fundamental system operations. As such, a system call provides a controlled way for user code to access low-level operations, like reading from a file or communicating with hardware, while keeping the kernel protected from unsafe or unauthorized access.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,3,'2025-05-22_22-42-27','The thread’s stack was set up by allocating it memory and then pointing the stack pointer to the start of the memory block so that it can appropriately decrement inside the block to fill all desired registers without overwriting other blocks of memory. “Setting up a thread’s stack” means manually creating the initial state of the CPU registers on the thread’s stack memory, so that when the CPU starts executing the thread, it can \"restore\" these registers and begin running as if it had just been interrupted and resumed — even though it hadn\'t run yet. It is important to store register values on the stack because then it can pause the thread at any time and resume it later by restoring it from where it left off.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',2,3,'2025-05-22_23-04-16','Setting up a thread’s stack involves more than just allocating memory, it means manually writing a specific set of register values (like the Program Counter, Link Register, and general-purpose registers) onto the stack in the exact order that the CPU expects when restoring context. The stack pointer is initialized to point to the top of this prepared stack frame. This setup allows the CPU to \"pop\" the register values off the stack and resume execution as if the thread had been paused, even if it\'s starting for the first time. Storing registers on the stack in the correct order is crucial because the CPU uses a well-defined pattern for context switching. If the values aren\'t placed correctly, the thread may resume with the wrong execution state, leading to crashes or undefined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,3,'2025-05-22_23-40-42','Setting up a thread’s stack involves more than just allocating memory, it means manually writing a specific set of register values (like the Program Counter, Link Register, and general-purpose registers) onto the stack in the exact order that the CPU expects when restoring context. The stack pointer is initialized to point to the top of this prepared stack frame. This setup allows the CPU to \"pop\" the register values off the stack and resume execution as if the thread had been paused, even if it\'s starting for the first time. Storing registers on the stack in the correct order is crucial because the CPU uses a well-defined pattern for context switching. If the values aren\'t placed correctly, the thread may resume with the wrong execution state, leading to crashes or undefined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,3,'2025-05-22_23-58-12','Setting up a thread’s stack involves more than just allocating memory, it means manually writing a specific set of register values (like the Program Counter, Link Register, and general-purpose registers) onto the stack in the exact order that the CPU expects when restoring context. The stack pointer is initialized to point to the top of this prepared stack frame. This setup allows the CPU to \"pop\" the register values off the stack and resume execution as if the thread had been paused, even if it\'s starting for the first time. Storing registers on the stack in the correct order is crucial because the CPU uses a well-defined pattern for context switching. If the values aren\'t placed correctly, the thread may resume with the wrong execution state, leading to crashes or undefined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,3,'2025-06-26_23-17-09','Setting up a thread’s stack involves more than just allocating memory, it means manually writing a specific set of register values (like the Program Counter, Link Register, and general-purpose registers) onto the stack in the exact order that the CPU expects when restoring context. The stack pointer is initialized to point to the top of this prepared stack frame. This setup allows the CPU to \"pop\" the register values off the stack and resume execution as if the thread had been paused, even if it\'s starting for the first time. Storing registers on the stack in the correct order is crucial because the CPU uses a well-defined pattern for context switching. If the values aren\'t placed correctly, the thread may resume with the wrong execution state, leading to crashes or undefined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,4,'2025-05-22_22-42-27','The code would crash in a hard fault because it would not know the start of the memory addressing for the registers if the __set_PSP function did not exist in the handler. By using __set_PSP, the stack is directed to the start of the register memory block so it can pop them in the desired order and in the correct location. Not doing so could load random memory values into the registers and trigger the hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',2,4,'2025-05-22_23-04-16','In the Cortex-M4 architecture, the CPU uses two stack pointers: the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is used by default and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is typically used for running threads. When starting a new thread, we must switch to the PSP and explicitly set it to point to the top of that thread’s stack using __set_PSP. If we don’t do this, the CPU may continue using the MSP during the thread’s execution, which can cause it to pop register values from the wrong location in memory, potentially causing exceptions and triggering faults. By setting the PSP, we ensure the CPU can restore the correct context and run the thread as intended.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',2,4,'2025-05-22_23-40-42','In the Cortex-M4 architecture, the CPU uses two stack pointers: the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is used by default and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is typically used for running threads. When starting a new thread, we must switch to the PSP and explicitly set it to point to the top of that thread’s stack using __set_PSP because the thread’s stack is located in a different memory region than the CPU’s default stack (MSP). If we don’t do this, the CPU may continue using the MSP during the thread’s execution, which can cause it to pop register values from the wrong location in memory, potentially causing exceptions and triggering faults. By setting the PSP, we ensure the CPU can restore the correct context and run the thread as intended.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,4,'2025-05-22_23-58-12','In the Cortex-M4 architecture, the CPU uses two stack pointers: the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is used by default and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is typically used for running threads. When starting a new thread, we must switch to the PSP and explicitly set it to point to the top of that thread’s stack using __set_PSP because the thread’s stack is located in a different memory region than the CPU’s default stack (MSP). If we don’t do this, the CPU may continue using the MSP during the thread’s execution, which can cause it to pop register values from the wrong location in memory, potentially causing exceptions and triggering faults. By setting the PSP, we ensure the CPU can restore the correct context and run the thread as intended.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,4,'2025-06-26_23-17-09','In the Cortex-M4 architecture, the CPU uses two stack pointers: the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is used by default and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is typically used for running threads. When starting a new thread, we must switch to the PSP and explicitly set it to point to the top of that thread’s stack using __set_PSP because the thread’s stack is located in a different memory region than the CPU’s default stack (MSP). If we don’t do this, the CPU may continue using the MSP during the thread’s execution, which can cause it to pop register values from the wrong location in memory, potentially causing exceptions and triggering faults. By setting the PSP, we ensure the CPU can restore the correct context and run the thread as intended.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,5,'2025-05-22_22-42-27','If it did not loop continuously, then the printf statement would execute once, the CPU would try to return to the link register (LR) address since the thread’s actions are technically completed, and it would then crash if LR pointed to a bad location or proceed to LR’s location if it was valid. Since there is no infinite loop in this scenario, the action of exiting the thread – which is essentially being done by printing something once and then returning – would need to be handled to prevent exceptions or crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',2,5,'2025-05-22_23-04-16','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned after printing once, the CPU would attempt to pop the return address from the stack and jump to it using the Link Register (LR). However, as threads are usually designed to run indefinitely using an infinite loop or similar means, returning from the thread would cause the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location stored in LR or on the stack, leading to a hard fault. The address stored in LR is usually invalid because threads are not expected to return. The hard fault occurs because the processor tries to execute code from an invalid or protected memory region.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,5,'2025-05-22_23-40-42','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned after printing once, the CPU would attempt to pop the return address from the stack and jump to it using the Link Register (LR). However, as threads are usually designed to run indefinitely using an infinite loop or similar means, returning from the thread would cause the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location stored in LR or on the stack, leading to a hard fault. The address stored in LR is usually invalid because threads are not expected to return. The hard fault occurs because the processor tries to execute code from an invalid or protected memory region.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',2,5,'2025-05-22_23-58-12','If the thread function returned after printing once, the CPU would try to return by popping the Program Counter (PC) from the stack, expecting a valid return address saved in the Link Register (LR). However, when a thread is created, its stack is manually set up to mimic the state of an interrupted function call, and the LR (effective a return address) is often set to an invalid or placeholder value because threads are not meant to return like regular functions. Unlike a normal function call, which pushes a valid return address onto the stack, a thread’s stack is typically built without this because the thread is expected to run indefinitely. If the CPU attempts to pop a return address that is invalid or missing, it jumps to an illegal memory location, which would trigger a hard fault due to an invalid instruction fetch or possibly an access violation.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',2,5,'2025-06-26_23-17-09','If the thread function returned after printing once, the CPU would try to return by popping the Program Counter (PC) from the stack, expecting a valid return address saved in the Link Register (LR). However, when a thread is created, its stack is manually set up to mimic the state of an interrupted function call, and the LR (effective a return address) is often set to an invalid or placeholder value because threads are not meant to return like regular functions. Unlike a normal function call, which pushes a valid return address onto the stack, a thread’s stack is typically built without this because the thread is expected to run indefinitely. If the CPU attempts to pop a return address that is invalid or missing, it jumps to an illegal memory location, which would trigger a hard fault due to an invalid instruction fetch or possibly an access violation.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,1,'2025-06-26_23-46-35','Each .c file is first compiled separately by the compiler into an object file, .o. These object files contain machine code but still need to be linked together to make them function as a program. After compilation, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into one executable program, resolving references between files; these references may include functional calls or other variables defined in other .c files, making everything work together as a single executable.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',3,1,'2025-06-27_01-21-14','The compilation process has three main steps that happen in order. First, the preprocessing step runs, where directives like #include and #define are handled; this means all included header files are copied into the source file, and macros are replaced. Next, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o), where the code is translated into machine instructions but still contains references to functions or variables defined elsewhere. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable, resolving these references between files so the entire program can work together as one.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,1,'2025-06-27_03-47-12','The compilation process has three main steps that happen in order. First, the preprocessing step runs, where directives like #include and #define are handled; this means all included header files are copied into the source file, and macros are replaced. Next, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o), where the code is translated into machine instructions but still contains references to functions or variables defined elsewhere. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable, resolving these references between files so the entire program can work together as one.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,1,'2025-06-27_11-04-55','The compilation process has three main steps that happen in order. First, the preprocessing step runs, where directives like #include and #define are handled; this means all included header files are copied into the source file, and macros are replaced. Next, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o), where the code is translated into machine instructions but still contains references to functions or variables defined elsewhere. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable, resolving these references between files so the entire program can work together as one.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,1,'2025-06-27_12-36-14','The compilation process has three main steps that happen in order. First, the preprocessing step runs, where directives like #include and #define are handled; this means all included header files are copied into the source file, and macros are replaced. Next, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o), where the code is translated into machine instructions but still contains references to functions or variables defined elsewhere. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable, resolving these references between files so the entire program can work together as one.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,1,'2025-06-27_17-18-32','The compilation process has three main steps that happen in order. First, the preprocessing step runs, where directives like #include and #define are handled; this means all included header files are copied into the source file, and macros are replaced. Next, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o), where the code is translated into machine instructions but still contains references to functions or variables defined elsewhere. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable, resolving these references between files so the entire program can work together as one.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,1,'2025-06-27_18-43-53','The compilation process has three main steps that happen in order. First, the preprocessing step runs, where directives like #include and #define are handled; this means all included header files are copied into the source file, and macros are replaced. Next, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o), where the code is translated into machine instructions but still contains references to functions or variables defined elsewhere. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable, resolving these references between files so the entire program can work together as one.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,2,'2025-06-26_23-46-35','Using #include for .c files cannot be done because #include copies all the code from that file directly into the current file, which can cause definition and compilation errors. This is why #include is meant for header files, .h, to include declarations like function prototypes, data types, or other definitions. Using #include for a .c file also breaks modular compilation where each .c file should be compiled separately and linked together, making the use of #include on .c files a bad practice.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,2,'2025-06-27_01-21-14','Using #include on .c files is a bad idea because it literally copies all the code from that .c file into the current file before compilation. This can cause multiple definition errors during linking since the same functions or variables get defined more than once. It also breaks the normal compilation process where each .c file is compiled independently and then linked. Usually, #include is meant for header files (.h) that only contain declarations, like function prototypes or type definitions, not the actual implementation. Including .c files mixes up interface and implementation and can create confusing and hard-to-debug issues.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,2,'2025-06-27_03-47-12','Using #include on .c files is a bad idea because it literally copies all the code from that .c file into the current file before compilation. This can cause multiple definition errors during linking since the same functions or variables get defined more than once. It also breaks the normal compilation process where each .c file is compiled independently and then linked. Usually, #include is meant for header files (.h) that only contain declarations, like function prototypes or type definitions, not the actual implementation. Including .c files mixes up interface and implementation and can create confusing and hard-to-debug issues.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,2,'2025-06-27_11-04-55','Using #include on .c files is a bad idea because it literally copies all the code from that .c file into the current file before compilation. This can cause multiple definition errors during linking since the same functions or variables get defined more than once. It also breaks the normal compilation process where each .c file is compiled independently and then linked. Usually, #include is meant for header files (.h) that only contain declarations, like function prototypes or type definitions, not the actual implementation. Including .c files mixes up interface and implementation and can create confusing and hard-to-debug issues.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,2,'2025-06-27_12-36-14','Using #include on .c files is a bad idea because it literally copies all the code from that .c file into the current file before compilation. This can cause multiple definition errors during linking since the same functions or variables get defined more than once. It also breaks the normal compilation process where each .c file is compiled independently and then linked. Usually, #include is meant for header files (.h) that only contain declarations, like function prototypes or type definitions, not the actual implementation. Including .c files mixes up interface and implementation and can create confusing and hard-to-debug issues.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,2,'2025-06-27_17-18-32','Using #include on .c files is a bad idea because it literally copies all the code from that .c file into the current file before compilation. This can cause multiple definition errors during linking since the same functions or variables get defined more than once. It also breaks the normal compilation process where each .c file is compiled independently and then linked. Usually, #include is meant for header files (.h) that only contain declarations, like function prototypes or type definitions, not the actual implementation. Including .c files mixes up interface and implementation and can create confusing and hard-to-debug issues.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,2,'2025-06-27_18-43-53','Using #include on .c files is a bad idea because it literally copies all the code from that .c file into the current file before compilation. This can cause multiple definition errors during linking since the same functions or variables get defined more than once. It also breaks the normal compilation process where each .c file is compiled independently and then linked. Usually, #include is meant for header files (.h) that only contain declarations, like function prototypes or type definitions, not the actual implementation. Including .c files mixes up interface and implementation and can create confusing and hard-to-debug issues.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,3,'2025-06-26_23-46-35','To set up and start a thread, the user generally needs to:\nAllocate a stack for the new thread to use, including a stack pointer and size\nInitialize the thread’s stack with initial values, like the function that the thread will run and some register values\nStore the thread’s starting point and stack pointer in a thread control structure\nCall a function to start the kernel, which will then run the thread that was created\nThe kernel manages the scheduling and switching between threads once they are running.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',3,3,'2025-06-27_01-21-14','To set up and start a thread, the user generally needs to:\nWrite the function that the thread will execute; this is the code the thread runs.\nAllocate a stack for the thread’s use, which stores the function calls and local variables when the thread runs.\nInitialize the thread’s stack with starting information, such as the address of the thread function and initial register values.\nRegister the thread with the operating system kernel by creating a thread control structure that holds the thread’s information.\nRequest the kernel to start the thread, after which the kernel scheduler takes over and manages running and switching between threads.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',3,3,'2025-06-27_03-47-12','To set up and start a thread, the user generally needs to carry out several steps, each of which is executed using a function call. The kernel must be initialized before any thread-related operations take place. Here are the steps:\nWrite the function that the thread will execute; this is the code the thread runs.\nInitialize the kernel before any thread-related operations are called.\nAllocate a stack for the thread’s use using a function call. The stack stores function calls and local variables when the thread runs.\nInitialize the stack using another function, setting it up with starting information such as the address of the thread function and initial register values\nIssue a function call\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',3,3,'2025-06-27_11-04-55','To set up and start a thread, the user generally follows a series of steps, all of which are carried out using function calls. The kernel must be initialized before anything else happens, otherwise no thread-related operations will work.\nWrite the function that the thread will execute; this is the code that runs when the thread is scheduled.\nMake sure the kernel is initialized before proceeding; this is a prerequisite for any of the following steps to have any effect.\nAllocate a stack for the thread using a function call. The stack stores local variables and function calls while the thread is running.\nCreate or register the thread with the kernel using a function call. This step sets up internal kernel structures (like a thread control block) and makes the thread visible to the scheduler.\nFinally, issue a function call to start the thread. If this step is skipped, the thread won\'t run. The scheduler only begins managing it once it\'s been explicitly started.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',3,3,'2025-06-27_12-36-14','To set up and start a thread, the user generally follows a set of steps, each done using a function call. These only work if the kernel has already been initialized and started, just initializing the kernel isn’t enough.\nWrite the function that the thread will run. This function\'s address must be known so it can be passed to the kernel as part of thread setup.\nInitialize the kernel using a function call. Without this, the scheduler won\'t run, and no threads will execute. This sets up internal kernel data structures like the scheduler, thread table, and stack pool. No threads run at this point.\nAllocate a stack for the thread using a function. This stack will be used to store local variables, function calls, and context when the thread runs.\nSet up the thread using a function that takes the thread function’s address, assigns it a stack, and registers it with the kernel. This prepares the thread to be scheduled.\nFinally, issue a function call to start the thread. The scheduler only begins managing it once it\'s been explicitly started. This begins running the scheduler, which picks one of the ready threads and starts executing it. If this step is skipped, no thread will run.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',3,3,'2025-06-27_17-18-32','To set up and start a thread, the user generally follows a set of steps, each done using a function call. These steps only work if the kernel is both initialized and explicitly started — just initializing it isn’t enough.\nWrite the function that the thread will run. This function\'s address must be known so it can be passed to the kernel as part of thread setup.\nInitialize the kernel using a function call. Without this, the scheduler won\'t run, and no threads will execute. This sets up internal kernel data structures like the scheduler, thread table, and stack pool. No threads run at this point.\nAllocate a stack for the thread using a function. This stack will be used to store local variables, function calls, and context when the thread runs.\nSet up the thread using a function that takes the thread function’s address, assigns it a stack, and registers it with the kernel. This prepares the thread to be scheduled.\nStart the kernel using a separate function call. This is what actually starts the scheduler and allows threads to begin running. If this step is skipped, nothing executes, even if the threads are registered.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,3,'2025-06-27_18-43-53','To set up and start a thread, the user generally follows a set of steps, each done using a function call. These steps only work if the kernel is both initialized and explicitly started — just initializing it isn’t enough.\nWrite the function that the thread will run. This function\'s address must be known so it can be passed to the kernel as part of thread setup.\nInitialize the kernel using a function call. Without this, the scheduler won\'t run, and no threads will execute. This sets up internal kernel data structures like the scheduler, thread table, and stack pool. No threads run at this point.\nAllocate a stack for the thread using a function. This stack will be used to store local variables, function calls, and context when the thread runs.\nSet up the thread using a function that takes the thread function’s address, assigns it a stack, and registers it with the kernel. This prepares the thread to be scheduled.\nStart the kernel using a separate function call. This is what actually starts the scheduler and allows threads to begin running. If this step is skipped, nothing executes, even if the threads are registered.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,4,'2025-06-26_23-46-35','The stack pool is a reserved area of memory that is used to give each thread its own stack space. Since each thread needs its own stack to store things like function calls and local variables, the stack pool helps manage memory for multiple threads. In future labs, when multiple threads will be created, we will allocate stacks for each thread from this pool so that they do not overwrite each other’s data, ensuring that they run safely at the same time.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,4,'2025-06-27_01-21-14','The stack pool is a dedicated section of memory reserved for thread stacks. Since each thread needs its own stack space to store function calls, local variables, and context, the stack pool provides separate, safe memory regions for each thread’s stack. In future labs, when we create multiple threads, each one will be assigned a stack from this pool, so their data won’t interfere with each other. This lets the operating system run several threads at the same time safely.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,4,'2025-06-27_03-47-12','The stack pool is a dedicated section of memory reserved for thread stacks. Since each thread needs its own stack space to store function calls, local variables, and context, the stack pool provides separate, safe memory regions for each thread’s stack. In future labs, when we create multiple threads, each one will be assigned a stack from this pool, so their data won’t interfere with each other. This lets the operating system run several threads at the same time safely.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,4,'2025-06-27_11-04-55','The stack pool is a dedicated section of memory reserved for thread stacks. Since each thread needs its own stack space to store function calls, local variables, and context, the stack pool provides separate, safe memory regions for each thread’s stack. In future labs, when we create multiple threads, each one will be assigned a stack from this pool, so their data won’t interfere with each other. This lets the operating system run several threads at the same time safely.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',3,4,'2025-06-27_12-36-14','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory used to store stacks for both the operating system and all user threads. Every thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls, local variables, and context during execution, and the OS itself also needs a separate stack for handling exceptions, interrupts, or code that runs outside of any thread. In future labs, when we create multiple threads, each thread will be given a stack from the stack pool. This prevents threads from interfering with each other’s memory and ensures the OS has a stable place to run its own logic. Without a dedicated stack pool, running multiple threads safely wouldn’t be possible.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,4,'2025-06-27_17-18-32','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory used to store stacks for both the operating system and all user threads. Every thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls, local variables, and context during execution, and the OS itself also needs a separate stack for handling exceptions, interrupts, or code that runs outside of any thread. In future labs, when we create multiple threads, each thread will be given a stack from the stack pool. This prevents threads from interfering with each other’s memory and ensures the OS has a stable place to run its own logic. Without a dedicated stack pool, running multiple threads safely wouldn’t be possible.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',3,4,'2025-06-27_18-43-53','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory used to store stacks for both the operating system and all user threads. Every thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls, local variables, and context during execution, and the OS itself also needs a separate stack for handling exceptions, interrupts, or code that runs outside of any thread. In future labs, when we create multiple threads, each thread will be given a stack from the stack pool. This prevents threads from interfering with each other’s memory and ensures the OS has a stable place to run its own logic. Without a dedicated stack pool, running multiple threads safely wouldn’t be possible.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,5,'2025-06-26_23-46-35','The kernel files contain the core operating system code that manages low-level aspects like memory and hardware interaction. The user files are where the program or applications that we write live, which use the kernel’s services. This separation is important because it keeps the system organized and safe. The kernel handles system control, while the user code runs on top without directly messing with the hardware, preventing crashes or security problems.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',3,5,'2025-06-27_01-21-14','Kernel files contain the core operating system code responsible for managing hardware, memory, and low-level system tasks. User files hold the code written by the user, like thread functions or applications, which run on top of the kernel. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows them to focus on writing their own program logic without needing to worry about complex hardware details. The user interacts with the kernel through defined APIs to request services like creating threads or accessing devices, so they don’t have to manage those details themselves. This separation also improves safety and stability by preventing user code from accidentally interfering with critical system operations.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,5,'2025-06-27_03-47-12','Kernel files contain the core operating system code responsible for managing hardware, memory, and low-level system tasks. User files hold the code written by the user, like thread functions or applications, which run on top of the kernel. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows them to focus on writing their own program logic without needing to worry about complex hardware details. The user interacts with the kernel through defined APIs to request services like creating threads or accessing devices, so they don’t have to manage those details themselves. This separation also improves safety and stability by preventing user code from accidentally interfering with critical system operations.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,5,'2025-06-27_11-04-55','Kernel files contain the core operating system code responsible for managing hardware, memory, and low-level system tasks. User files hold the code written by the user, like thread functions or applications, which run on top of the kernel. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows them to focus on writing their own program logic without needing to worry about complex hardware details. The user interacts with the kernel through defined APIs to request services like creating threads or accessing devices, so they don’t have to manage those details themselves. This separation also improves safety and stability by preventing user code from accidentally interfering with critical system operations.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,5,'2025-06-27_12-36-14','Kernel files contain the core operating system code responsible for managing hardware, memory, and low-level system tasks. User files hold the code written by the user, like thread functions or applications, which run on top of the kernel. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows them to focus on writing their own program logic without needing to worry about complex hardware details. The user interacts with the kernel through defined APIs to request services like creating threads or accessing devices, so they don’t have to manage those details themselves. This separation also improves safety and stability by preventing user code from accidentally interfering with critical system operations.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,5,'2025-06-27_17-18-32','Kernel files contain the core operating system code responsible for managing hardware, memory, and low-level system tasks. User files hold the code written by the user, like thread functions or applications, which run on top of the kernel. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows them to focus on writing their own program logic without needing to worry about complex hardware details. The user interacts with the kernel through defined APIs to request services like creating threads or accessing devices, so they don’t have to manage those details themselves. This separation also improves safety and stability by preventing user code from accidentally interfering with critical system operations.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',3,5,'2025-06-27_18-43-53','Kernel files contain the core operating system code responsible for managing hardware, memory, and low-level system tasks. User files hold the code written by the user, like thread functions or applications, which run on top of the kernel. From the user’s perspective, this separation is important because it allows them to focus on writing their own program logic without needing to worry about complex hardware details. The user interacts with the kernel through defined APIs to request services like creating threads or accessing devices, so they don’t have to manage those details themselves. This separation also improves safety and stability by preventing user code from accidentally interfering with critical system operations.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',4,1,'2025-07-10_00-29-29','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a queue by enqueuing each thread control block (TCB) as it is created. When the kernel starts, the first thread can be dequeued and stored in a variable such as active_thread. To perform a context switch, the scheduler can dequeue the next thread in the queue (which is the thread that has waited longest to run), enqueue the previously running active_thread back into the queue and then update active_thread to the newly dequeued thread.\nThis approach has several advantages over using a static array with an index: The queue naturally rotates threads without needing to manually wrap indices. Removing a terminated or blocked thread is simpler with a dynamic queue than shifting elements in an array. Encapsulating thread management in queue operations can lead to cleaner, more modular code.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',4,1,'2025-07-10_00-38-13','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a queue by enqueuing each thread control block (TCB) as it is created. When the kernel starts, the first thread is dequeued and assigned to a variable like active_thread. During a context switch, the scheduler dequeues the next thread in the queue (the one that has waited longest), enqueues the currently running thread back into the queue, and updates active_thread to the new thread. This ensures each thread gets a turn in a cyclical and fair order.\nThis queue-based design has some benefits. It makes the cyclical nature of round-robin scheduling explicit and eliminates the need to manually wrap array indices. It can also simplify removing or skipping threads—for example, when a thread is blocked or terminated—since the scheduler can simply skip enqueuing those threads instead of managing flags or shifting array entries. Encapsulating scheduling behavior in queue operations also helps keep the scheduler code clean and modular.\nHowever, the array-based scheduler used in the lab already behaves similarly to a queue: it iterates through an array of threads in a circular manner using modulo arithmetic. So, in that sense, it already simulates a round-robin queue using simpler data structures.\nAdditionally, using an actual queue structure may introduce overhead. Dynamic queues often require pointer manipulation or memory allocation, which can increase complexity and runtime cost—especially in a small embedded system. In contrast, the array approach has minimal overhead and predictable memory usage, which can be important for performance and determinism in real-time systems.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',4,1,'2025-07-10_00-47-28','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a queue by enqueuing each thread control block (TCB) as it is created. When the kernel starts, the first thread is dequeued and assigned to a variable like active_thread. During a context switch, the scheduler dequeues the next thread in the queue (the one that has waited longest), enqueues the currently running thread back into the queue, and updates active_thread to the new thread. This ensures each thread gets a turn in a cyclical and fair order.\nThis queue-based design has some benefits. It makes the cyclical nature of round-robin scheduling explicit and eliminates the need to manually wrap array indices. It can also simplify removing or skipping threads—for example, when a thread is blocked or terminated—since the scheduler can simply skip enqueuing those threads instead of managing flags or shifting array entries. Encapsulating scheduling behavior in queue operations also helps keep the scheduler code clean and modular.\nHowever, the array-based scheduler used in the lab already behaves similarly to a queue: it iterates through an array of threads in a circular manner using modulo arithmetic. So, in that sense, it already simulates a round-robin queue using simpler data structures.\nAdditionally, using an actual queue structure may introduce overhead. Dynamic queues often require pointer manipulation or memory allocation, which can increase complexity and runtime cost—especially in a small embedded system. In contrast, the array approach has minimal overhead and predictable memory usage, which can be important for performance and determinism in real-time systems.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',4,2,'2025-07-10_00-29-29','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the state of the currently running thread and restoring the state of the next thread to run. When a thread calls osYield() (or another event triggers a context switch), it sets the PendSV interrupt. Once PendSV_Handler runs:\nSave current thread state: The CPU automatically pushes some registers (like R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) onto the thread\'s stack when the exception occurs. PendSV_Handler then manually saves the rest — the callee-saved registers R4–R11 — onto the same stack. This snapshot lets the thread resume later from the exact same point.\nUpdate its stack pointer: The handler stores the current thread’s updated stack pointer (PSP) in its thread control block (threadArray[current_thread].sp).\nSelect next thread: It calls a scheduler function (e.g. osSched()), which updates current_thread to point to the next thread in the round-robin queue.\nRestore next thread state: The handler loads the new thread’s saved stack pointer from threadArray[current_thread].sp into the PSP. It then pops R4–R11 from that stack — manually — and when the handler exits, the CPU automatically restores the rest of the registers (R0–R3, etc.) from the stack as well.\nThis effectively swaps out one thread’s context for another’s, allowing them to take turns on the CPU as if they were running \"in parallel.\" PendSV_Handler is also lower priority than SVC, ensuring that variables do not end up stuck in the stack by allowing SVC to finish. This means that the context switch is only performed when no other handlers are running, ensuring the stack is back to normal before switching to prevent the stack from growing without bounds until the memory is all filled with stuck variables.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',4,2,'2025-07-10_00-38-13','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the state of the currently running thread and restoring the state of the next thread to run. When a thread calls osYield() (or another event triggers a context switch), it sets the PendSV interrupt. Once PendSV_Handler runs:\nSave current thread state: The CPU automatically pushes some registers (like R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) onto the thread\'s stack when the exception occurs. PendSV_Handler then manually saves the rest — the callee-saved registers R4–R11 — onto the same stack. This snapshot lets the thread resume later from the exact same point.\nUpdate its stack pointer: The handler stores the current thread’s updated stack pointer (PSP) in its thread control block (threadArray[current_thread].sp).\nSelect next thread: It calls a scheduler function (e.g. osSched()), which updates current_thread to point to the next thread in the round-robin queue.\nRestore next thread state: The handler loads the new thread’s saved stack pointer from threadArray[current_thread].sp into the PSP. It then pops R4–R11 from that stack — manually — and when the handler exits, the CPU automatically restores the rest of the registers (R0–R3, etc.) from the stack as well.\nThis effectively swaps out one thread’s context for another’s, allowing them to take turns on the CPU as if they were running \"in parallel.\" PendSV_Handler is also lower priority than SVC, ensuring that variables do not end up stuck in the stack by allowing SVC to finish. This means that the context switch is only performed when no other handlers are running, ensuring the stack is back to normal before switching to prevent the stack from growing without bounds until the memory is all filled with stuck variables.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',4,2,'2025-07-10_00-47-28','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the currently running thread’s state and restoring the next thread’s state so that multiple threads can share the CPU.\nWhen osYield() or another event triggers a context switch, the PendSV interrupt is set. Upon entering PendSV_Handler, the first essential step is to read the current Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This pointer indicates where the current thread’s stack frame begins in memory. Knowing the PSP is critical because the thread’s register state is saved on its stack, and this pointer lets the handler know exactly where to save additional registers and where to store the updated stack pointer later.\nAfter reading the PSP, the handler manually saves the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto the current thread’s stack, extending the stack frame. The CPU automatically saved the other registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR) when the exception occurred, so this manual save completes the full context snapshot.\nOnce the full context is saved, the updated PSP value — now pointing to the top of the saved context—is stored in the current thread’s control block (e.g., threadArray[current_thread].sp). This ensures that when the thread resumes later, the system knows exactly where its context is stored.\nNext, the scheduler function (like osSched()) is called to select the next thread to run, updating current_thread accordingly. The handler then loads the new thread’s saved stack pointer from its control block back into the PSP. It manually restores the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) by popping them off the new thread’s stack. When the handler exits, the CPU automatically restores the remaining registers (R0–R3, etc.) from that stack frame. This sequence effectively swaps out the entire CPU state from one thread to another, enabling cooperative multitasking.\nPendSV’s low priority compared to SVC ensures that context switching only occurs when no higher-priority exceptions are active. This prevents interruptions from leaving partial states on the stack, avoiding stack overflows or corrupted context data.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',4,3,'2025-07-10_00-29-29','Modifying the OS scheduler to always choose the thread with the highest priority is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority threads. In such a system, if one or more high-priority threads are always ready to run, the scheduler will never give CPU time to lower-priority threads, even if those threads are important for overall system functionality (e.g., logging, communication, background tasks).\nThis approach ignores fairness and the history of execution — it doesn\'t track how long each thread has waited or whether it has had a recent opportunity to run. For example, with two or more high-priority threads always ready, the system will just switch between those threads indefinitely, and any lower-priority threads will be permanently skipped. This defeats the purpose of multitasking.\nAdditionally, this strategy can cause unexpected system behavior. A lower-priority thread might hold a resource (like a lock or shared buffer) needed by a high-priority thread, but if it never gets CPU time to release it, the high-priority thread will stall — a situation known as priority inversion. A more robust scheduler would use priority alongside fairness mechanisms, like aging or round-robin within each priority level.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',4,3,'2025-07-10_00-38-13','Changing the scheduler to always select the task with the highest priority is a bad idea because it effectively prevents all lower-priority tasks from ever running. If one thread has a higher priority than all others and is always ready to run, the scheduler will choose that same thread every time. As a result, the lower-priority threads are completely starved of CPU time—they are never given a chance to run, regardless of how long they’ve been waiting or whether they are critical to system functionality.\nThis is different from scheduling among high-priority threads. In this case, only the single thread with the highest priority runs, over and over, as long as it remains ready. The system does not alternate between multiple high-priority threads unless they happen to share the exact same priority value. Therefore, if a high-priority thread never blocks or yields, the system becomes effectively single-threaded.\nThis behavior breaks the fairness that round-robin or multi-level scheduling normally provides. It also introduces the risk of priority inversion, where a low-priority thread holds a resource needed by the high-priority thread but never gets a chance to release it. Even essential background tasks like I/O handling, logging, or watchdog resets may be blocked entirely if they don’t share the highest priority.\nTo prevent this, a better approach would combine priority-based selection with fairness mechanisms—such as aging, or round-robin scheduling within each priority level—to ensure that all threads eventually get CPU time.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',4,3,'2025-07-10_00-47-28','Changing the scheduler to always select the task with the highest priority is a bad idea because it effectively prevents all lower-priority tasks from ever running. If one thread has a higher priority than all others and is always ready to run, the scheduler will choose that same thread every time. As a result, the lower-priority threads are completely starved of CPU time—they are never given a chance to run, regardless of how long they’ve been waiting or whether they are critical to system functionality.\nThis is different from scheduling among high-priority threads. In this case, only the single thread with the highest priority runs, over and over, as long as it remains ready. The system does not alternate between multiple high-priority threads unless they happen to share the exact same priority value. Therefore, if a high-priority thread never blocks or yields, the system becomes effectively single-threaded.\nThis behavior breaks the fairness that round-robin or multi-level scheduling normally provides. It also introduces the risk of priority inversion, where a low-priority thread holds a resource needed by the high-priority thread but never gets a chance to release it. Even essential background tasks like I/O handling, logging, or watchdog resets may be blocked entirely if they don’t share the highest priority.\nTo prevent this, a better approach would combine priority-based selection with fairness mechanisms—such as aging, or round-robin scheduling within each priority level—to ensure that all threads eventually get CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',4,4,'2025-07-10_00-29-29','Calling C functions like osSched() in PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main() in SVC_Handler improves readability, maintainability, and development speed. C makes it easier to write and understand logic like scheduling or system call handling, and it allows you to use high-level constructs like loops and conditionals. It\'s also more portable than assembly, making your OS easier to adapt to different hardware platforms.\nHowever, using C introduces some overhead. Function calls add extra instructions for saving registers and setting up the stack, which can matter in performance-critical code like context switching. Assembly, while harder to write and maintain, gives you full control and can be more efficient. There\'s also a risk of the compiler generating unexpected instructions if you\'re not careful with function attributes and register usage.\nIn practice, it\'s best to use assembly for the low-level context save/restore and use C for higher-level logic like scheduling decisions. This balances performance with code clarity and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',4,4,'2025-07-10_00-38-13','Calling C functions like osSched() in PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main() in SVC_Handler improves readability, maintainability, and development speed. C makes it easier to write and understand logic like scheduling or system call handling, and it allows you to use high-level constructs like loops and conditionals. It\'s also more portable than assembly, making your OS easier to adapt to different hardware platforms.\nHowever, using C introduces some overhead. Function calls add extra instructions for saving registers and setting up the stack, which can matter in performance-critical code like context switching. Assembly, while harder to write and maintain, gives you full control and can be more efficient. There\'s also a risk of the compiler generating unexpected instructions if you\'re not careful with function attributes and register usage.\nIn practice, it\'s best to use assembly for the low-level context save/restore and use C for higher-level logic like scheduling decisions. This balances performance with code clarity and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',4,4,'2025-07-10_00-47-28','Calling C functions like osSched() in PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main() in SVC_Handler improves readability, maintainability, and development speed. C makes it easier to write and understand logic like scheduling or system call handling, and it allows you to use high-level constructs like loops and conditionals. It\'s also more portable than assembly, making your OS easier to adapt to different hardware platforms.\nHowever, using C introduces some overhead. Function calls add extra instructions for saving registers and setting up the stack, which can matter in performance-critical code like context switching. Assembly, while harder to write and maintain, gives you full control and can be more efficient. There\'s also a risk of the compiler generating unexpected instructions if you\'re not careful with function attributes and register usage.\nIn practice, it\'s best to use assembly for the low-level context save/restore and use C for higher-level logic like scheduling decisions. This balances performance with code clarity and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',4,5,'2025-07-10_00-29-29','Yes, the OS will still function correctly even if only a single thread is created, such as the print_continuously thread from the previous labs. In this scenario, the thread runs as usual and calls osYield() after printing, which triggers the PendSV interrupt to perform a context switch.\nIn my implementation, osSched() is designed to check whether multiple threads are available before performing a context switch. If only one thread is present, the scheduler recognizes that there are no other runnable threads and simply returns, effectively continuing execution of the same thread. This ensures that the system does not attempt to switch to an invalid or uninitialized context, preserving stability.\nEven if this check were not explicitly implemented, the system would likely still behave correctly in a minimal case. The scheduler would cycle through the thread table and eventually select the same thread again, since it is the only one marked as active. The result would be that the thread yields to itself, resuming execution without any functional change. This would cause slightly unnecessary context switching overhead but wouldn’t break the system.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',4,5,'2025-07-10_00-38-13','Yes, the OS will still function correctly even if only a single thread is created, such as the print_continuously thread from the previous labs. In this scenario, the thread runs as usual and calls osYield() after printing, which triggers the PendSV interrupt to perform a context switch.\nIn my implementation, osSched() is designed to check whether multiple threads are available before performing a context switch. If only one thread is present, the scheduler recognizes that there are no other runnable threads and simply returns, effectively continuing execution of the same thread. This ensures that the system does not attempt to switch to an invalid or uninitialized context, preserving stability.\nEven if this check were not explicitly implemented, the system would likely still behave correctly in a minimal case. The scheduler would cycle through the thread table and eventually select the same thread again, since it is the only one marked as active. The result would be that the thread yields to itself, resuming execution without any functional change. This would cause slightly unnecessary context switching overhead but wouldn’t break the system.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',4,5,'2025-07-10_00-47-28','Yes, the OS will still function correctly even if only a single thread is created, such as the print_continuously thread from the previous labs. In this scenario, the thread runs as usual and calls osYield() after printing, which triggers the PendSV interrupt to perform a context switch.\nIn my implementation, osSched() is designed to check whether multiple threads are available before performing a context switch. If only one thread is present, the scheduler recognizes that there are no other runnable threads and simply returns, effectively continuing execution of the same thread. This ensures that the system does not attempt to switch to an invalid or uninitialized context, preserving stability.\nEven if this check were not explicitly implemented, the system would likely still behave correctly in a minimal case. The scheduler would cycle through the thread table and eventually select the same thread again, since it is the only one marked as active. The result would be that the thread yields to itself, resuming execution without any functional change. This would cause slightly unnecessary context switching overhead but wouldn’t break the system.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',5,1,'2025-07-16_16-50-27','The process starts with the user creating some kind of data structure; this could be something simple like an int, or a pointer to a struct that holds multiple arguments. This pointer is then passed as an argument when creating the thread, using a function like osCreateThread().\nAt thread creation time, the user-provided function and its associated argument pointer are stored using the registers so they can be used when the thread is actually scheduled to run. When the OS starts the thread, it sets up the thread’s initial context, which includes loading registers with the appropriate values. For example, the first four arguments to a function can be passed using registers R0 through R3. So, the OS places the pointer (i.e., the thread argument) into R0 before transferring control to the thread’s entry function.\nBecause the thread function is defined to take a void* argument (or something similar), and the OS placed that pointer in the correct register, the function can access its arguments exactly as expected. This works because the ABI defines how arguments are passed to functions, so as long as both the thread and OS follow the same calling convention, everything lines up.\nIn short: the user prepares the data and passes a pointer, the OS loads that pointer into the expected register when the thread starts, and the ABI ensures that the thread function can access it seamlessly.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',5,1,'2025-07-16_18-19-09','The process begins with the user creating a data structure to hold the thread’s input argument. This could be something simple like an int, or more often, a pointer to a struct that holds multiple values. This pointer is then passed to a thread creation function, like osCreateThread(), along with the thread function itself.\nOnce the thread is created, the OS is responsible for setting up its initial execution context — which includes preparing the stack and initializing the registers so that, when the CPU starts executing the thread, everything behaves as if the function was called normally.\nOn ARM Cortex-M4, this setup is done by manually populating the new thread’s stack with a fake exception frame. This frame includes the initial values for registers R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR. The thread function’s address is placed in the PC field, and the argument pointer is placed in R0 — because R0 is used for the first function argument.\nWhen the thread is scheduled for the first time, the CPU performs an exception return using this pre-populated stack frame. This restores the register values as if the function was entered normally from C code, with R0 already holding the argument.\nIt’s important that the OS puts the argument on the stack (rather than directly in the register) because the CPU won’t execute the thread function immediately — it needs to restore the register context first. And on Cortex-M, this is done by popping registers off the stack, not by writing directly into registers from software. So setting up the stack is the only safe and architecturally correct way to pass in arguments to a new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',5,2,'2025-07-16_16-50-27','You cannot call osYield() inside those handlers because those functions are already in the middle of performing a context switch. Calling osYield asks the OS to start a context switch, so doing this inside a handler that’s already switching contexts creates a conflict.\nIf osYield is called, it sets the PendSV interrupt as pending again. But since you’re already inside the PendSV handler (or another handler that triggers a context switch), the system will finish this current context switch, then immediately try to enter the handler again. This creates unnecessary and repeated context switches, possibly before the scheduler or system state is stable. Worse, if you call osYield repeatedly inside these handlers, you can end up in an infinite loop of context switches or corrupt the stack by interrupting an in-progress one. Even if the loop is finite, it wastes CPU cycles and may destabilize the scheduler. If osYield is called inside something like SVC or SysTick handlers, then PendSV may not get a clean opportunity to run when its supposed to, risking the interleaving of context switches woth other critical OS operations.\nThe key reason why this is bad is because context-switching is carefully designed so that it finishes without being interrupted by another switch request after it starts saving and restoring thread states. Calling osYield during that process violates this rule, leading to undefined behavior like memory corruption, crashes, or scheduler instability.\n',0,'s25'),('i5garg',5,2,'2025-07-16_18-19-09','You cannot call osYield() inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because those functions are already in the middle of a context switch — and osYield() itself is a system call that requests another context switch.\nWhen osYield() is called, it doesn\'t immediately switch threads. Instead, it triggers a system call, typically by generating an SVC interrupt or by explicitly setting PendSV as pending. This causes the processor to enter an interrupt context after the current one finishes. But if you call it from inside an interrupt handler that’s already responsible for performing the context switch — like PendSV_Handler — you’re effectively queuing up a second switch before the first one finishes. This breaks the OS’s assumptions about clean, non-overlapping context switches.\nWorse, calling a system call from within an interrupt handler nests one interrupt within another, which can quickly lead to stack overflow or corruption. ARM Cortex-M cores have limited interrupt nesting depth and small stack sizes; interrupting an already critical handler like PendSV or SysTick risks running out of stack space and hitting a hardfault.\nEven if the system survives, you now have an unstable situation: repeated unnecessary context switches, potential corruption of thread state, and an overworked scheduler. For example, if osYield() is called from within SysTick_Handler, PendSV may never run at the correct time, and scheduling decisions can get interleaved with timing logic — something the OS is not designed to handle safely.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',5,3,'2025-07-16_16-50-27','Reducing the fundamental OS clock tick from 1 ms to 0.1 ms increases the system tick frequency from 1000 Hz to 10000Hz. This change comes with benefits and trade-offs.\nThe benefits include improved responsiveness because the OS can schedule tasks or detect timeouts with 10x higher resolution when using a 0.1 ms clock. This means that threads waiting for a delay or timeout can wake up more precisely, and context switches can occur with finer timing. The 0.1 ms clock also leads to better support for real-time behavior, benefitting systems that require fast polling or high-frequency control loops to meet strict deadlines more reliably.\nThere are some downsides with using a 0.1 ms clock though. It leads to increased CPU overhead, with the SysTick interrupt firing 10 times more often. Each interrupt introduces overhead, consuming a significant portion of the CPU’s time and reducing the efficiency of the code. The 0.1 ms clock may accordingly lead to higher power consumption, as more frequent interrupts keep the CPU active for longer periods. Finally, the 0.1 ms clock presents diminishing returns, as most applications don’t need sub-millisecond timing. If the threads are only doing work on millisecond or longer timescales, increasing tick resolution just wastes CPU cycles without delivering noticeable benefit.\nIn summary, decreasing the OS tick interval from 1 ms to 0.1 ms can improve timing precision and responsiveness for time-critical applications, but it comes at the cost of increased CPU usage, power consumption, and potential scheduler inefficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',5,3,'2025-07-16_18-19-09','Reducing the fundamental OS clock tick from 1 ms to 0.1 ms increases the system tick frequency from 1000 Hz to 10000Hz. This change comes with benefits and trade-offs.\nThe benefits include improved responsiveness because the OS can schedule tasks or detect timeouts with 10x higher resolution when using a 0.1 ms clock. This means that threads waiting for a delay or timeout can wake up more precisely, and context switches can occur with finer timing. The 0.1 ms clock also leads to better support for real-time behavior, benefitting systems that require fast polling or high-frequency control loops to meet strict deadlines more reliably.\nThere are some downsides with using a 0.1 ms clock though. It leads to increased CPU overhead, with the SysTick interrupt firing 10 times more often. Each interrupt introduces overhead, consuming a significant portion of the CPU’s time and reducing the efficiency of the code. The 0.1 ms clock may accordingly lead to higher power consumption, as more frequent interrupts keep the CPU active for longer periods. Finally, the 0.1 ms clock presents diminishing returns, as most applications don’t need sub-millisecond timing. If the threads are only doing work on millisecond or longer timescales, increasing tick resolution just wastes CPU cycles without delivering noticeable benefit.\nIn summary, decreasing the OS tick interval from 1 ms to 0.1 ms can improve timing precision and responsiveness for time-critical applications, but it comes at the cost of increased CPU usage, power consumption, and potential scheduler inefficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',5,4,'2025-07-16_16-50-27','From the user’s perspective, the key difference between the two is how and when the thread gives up control to the CPU. In cooperative multitasking, the user is responsible for explicitly yielding control, like by calling osYield(). If the thread never yields, it can block all other threads from running. This gives the programmer more control but also makes it easier to introduce bugs due to unintentional CPU hogging. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS takes control of scheduling. The user doesn’t need to manually yield – the OS will automatically interrupt the running threads based on time slices or priorities. This makes the system more robust and responsive, especially in real-time applications, and reduces the chance that one thread can starve others.\nFrom the OS’s perspective, the workload differs. In cooperative multitasking, the OS only performs a context switch when the running thread voluntarily yields. This makes the scheduler simpler since context switches happen at predictable points and the OS doesn’t need to rely on timer interrupts for scheduling. However, in pre-emptive multitasking, the OS must use a periodic interrupt like SysTick to forcefully take control and decide when to switch threads. This means the OS needs to track more details like thread runtimes, time slices, and possibly priorities. The added complexity on the OS’s side when using pre-emptive multitasking gives better responsiveness and fairness but requires more resources and handling.\nIn summary, cooperative multitasking is simpler for the OS and gives the user more control but can lead to CPU hogging and blocking. Pre-emptive multitasking requires more resources on both the user’s and OS’s sides but improves responsiveness and ensures that critical threads are able to run.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',5,4,'2025-07-16_18-19-09','From the user’s perspective, the key difference between the two is how and when the thread gives up control to the CPU. In cooperative multitasking, the user is responsible for explicitly yielding control, like by calling osYield(). If the thread never yields, it can block all other threads from running. This gives the programmer more control but also makes it easier to introduce bugs due to unintentional CPU hogging. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS takes control of scheduling. The user doesn’t need to manually yield – the OS will automatically interrupt the running threads based on time slices or priorities. This makes the system more robust and responsive, especially in real-time applications, and reduces the chance that one thread can starve others.\nFrom the OS’s perspective, the workload differs. In cooperative multitasking, the OS only performs a context switch when the running thread voluntarily yields. This makes the scheduler simpler since context switches happen at predictable points and the OS doesn’t need to rely on timer interrupts for scheduling. However, in pre-emptive multitasking, the OS must use a periodic interrupt like SysTick to forcefully take control and decide when to switch threads. This means the OS needs to track more details like thread runtimes, time slices, and possibly priorities. The added complexity on the OS’s side when using pre-emptive multitasking gives better responsiveness and fairness but requires more resources and handling.\nIn summary, cooperative multitasking is simpler for the OS and gives the user more control but can lead to CPU hogging and blocking. Pre-emptive multitasking requires more resources on both the user’s and OS’s sides but improves responsiveness and ensures that critical threads are able to run.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',5,5,'2025-07-16_16-50-27','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately rely on the same underlying mechanism: the OS scheduler. Both methods trigger a context switch, just with different trigger sources.\nWhen a thread yields, like by calling osYield(), it voluntarily gives up the CPU. The OS then looks as the list of ready threads and switches to the next one based on scheduling policy and priorities. With pre-emptive switching, however, a timer interrupt like SysTick fires at regular intervals, and the OS checks whether a higher-priority or ready thread should run instead based on the runtime and time slice. If so, it forces a context switch, even if the current thread didn’t yield voluntarily.\nThese mechanisms are designed to coexist: a thread can yield when its finished doing something, and the scheduler can also use pre-emptive context switching to interrupt the current thread and switch to another one. Both methods trigger a PendSV exception to actually perform the context switch. In short, yielding is voluntary while pre-emption is automatic, and they can coexist because the underlying mechanism is the same, with the same scheduling system.\n',1,'s25'),('i5garg',5,5,'2025-07-16_18-19-09','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately rely on the same underlying mechanism: the OS scheduler. Both methods trigger a context switch, just with different trigger sources.\nWhen a thread yields, like by calling osYield(), it voluntarily gives up the CPU. The OS then looks as the list of ready threads and switches to the next one based on scheduling policy and priorities. With pre-emptive switching, however, a timer interrupt like SysTick fires at regular intervals, and the OS checks whether a higher-priority or ready thread should run instead based on the runtime and time slice. If so, it forces a context switch, even if the current thread didn’t yield voluntarily.\nThese mechanisms are designed to coexist: a thread can yield when its finished doing something, and the scheduler can also use pre-emptive context switching to interrupt the current thread and switch to another one. Both methods trigger a PendSV exception to actually perform the context switch. In short, yielding is voluntary while pre-emption is automatic, and they can coexist because the underlying mechanism is the same, with the same scheduling system.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,1,'2025-06-26_00-06-58','The HAL is a software library written by ST Microelectronics, and it provides a standardized interface between the application code and the low-level hardware of a microcontroller. It is used in a general embedded project to simplify the process of programming embedded systems by offering high level functions to interact with peripherals. Instead of manually configuring hardware registers, the HAL provides pre-written functions that handle this setup behind the scenes. This abstraction makes embedded development faster, easier to maintain, and less error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,1,'2025-06-26_00-42-48','The HAL is a software library written by ST Microelectronics, and it provides a standardized interface between the application code and the low-level hardware of a microcontroller. It is used in a general embedded project to simplify the process of programming embedded systems by offering high level functions to interact with peripherals. Instead of manually configuring hardware registers, the HAL provides pre-written functions that handle this setup behind the scenes. This abstraction makes embedded development faster, easier to maintain, and less error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,1,'2025-06-26_00-51-42','The HAL is a software library written by ST Microelectronics, and it provides a standardized interface between the application code and the low-level hardware of a microcontroller. It is used in a general embedded project to simplify the process of programming embedded systems by offering high level functions to interact with peripherals. Instead of manually configuring hardware registers, the HAL provides pre-written functions that handle this setup behind the scenes. This abstraction makes embedded development faster, easier to maintain, and less error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,2,'2025-06-26_00-06-58','The __io_putchar function’s purpose is to write a single character to the appropriate IO channel, which in this case was UART2. The function also returns the character that was printed, because of what printf needs. This is needed because the printf function repeatedly calls this when we pass in a string to print out.\n',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,2,'2025-06-26_00-42-48','We used the debugger in this lab because some of the information we needed to verify—such as the current values of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), Process Stack Pointer (PSP), and general-purpose registers—is internal to the microcontroller and not easily accessible through printf. While printf is useful for confirming that certain code paths are executed or for displaying variables, it falls short when we need precise, low-level insights into how the processor is managing memory and registers during execution. For example, to verify that our computed PSP value was correctly set and being used by the system, we needed to examine the actual SP register (which should match our PSP value) and observe the value of the SP register to confirm that the processor had switched from using MSP to PSP. These kinds of observations are only possible using a debugger, which gives us real-time access to the processor’s internal state. Additionally, the debugger allowed us to set breakpoints and inspect values at exact moments in the program, such as right before entering the infinite loop in main(). This level of control and visibility cannot be achieved using printf alone, especially since printf itself relies on properly configured peripherals that might not yet be active or correctly set up when we need to gather this diagnostic information.\n',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,2,'2025-06-26_00-51-42','The __io_putchar function defines how a single character is sent to an output device—in this case, UART2. It is required because the standard printf function in C does not know how to interact with the hardware on its own. Instead, printf calls __io_putchar internally to send each character one at a time. In this lab, we implemented __io_putchar using the HAL_UART_Transmit function so that characters printed using printf are transmitted over the serial port. Without this function, printf would compile but produce no visible output, since there would be no connection between the formatted string and the UART hardware. This function bridges that gap by linking standard output to the microcontroller’s UART.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,3,'2025-06-26_00-06-58','We\nWe used the debugger in this lab because some of the information we needed to verify—such as the current values of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), Process Stack Pointer (PSP), and general-purpose registers—is internal to the microcontroller and not easily accessible through printf. While printf is useful for confirming that certain code paths are executed or for displaying variables, it falls short when we need precise, low-level insights into how the processor is managing memory and registers during execution. For example, to verify that our computed PSP value was correctly set and being used by the system, we needed to examine the actual SP register (which should match our PSP value) and observe the value of the SP register to confirm that the processor had switched from using MSP to PSP. These kinds of observations are only possible using a debugger, which gives us real-time access to the processor’s internal state. Additionally, the debugger allowed us to set breakpoints and inspect values at exact moments in the program, such as right before entering the infinite loop in main(). This level of control and visibility cannot be achieved using printf alone, especially since printf itself relies on properly configured peripherals that might not yet be active or correctly set up when we need to gather this diagnostic information.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,3,'2025-06-26_00-42-48','The stack allocation method used in this lab is based on manually managing memory within a predefined stack region by treating the available stack space as a large array of memory. We began by retrieving the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), known as MSP_INIT_VAL, which represents the starting address of the default stack region set up by the microcontroller at boot. This value was obtained by dereferencing the memory address 0x0, which, according to the ARM Cortex-M memory map, contains the initial MSP value used during the reset sequence. Specifically, we declared a pointer with the line uint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0;, which effectively extracts the initial top-of-stack address.\nOnce we had MSP_INIT_VAL, we computed a new value for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), called PSP_val, by subtracting a fixed size from it—specifically 0x400 (1024 bytes), which we designated as the size of the main stack. This was done using the line uint32_t PSP_val = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL - 0x400;. The subtraction reflects the fact that stacks grow downward in memory, so to create a new stack region below the main one, we simply allocate space beneath it.\nKnowing the initial value of the MSP was essential because our entire stack allocation scheme was relative to it. We used MSP_INIT_VAL as the starting point for laying out additional stack regions in memory, such as those needed for threads in a future multitasking OS. Without this known reference point, we would not be able to reliably calculate where each thread\'s stack should begin. Later, we set the PSP to this computed value using the CMSIS function __set_PSP(PSP_val);, and we updated the CONTROL register via __set_CONTROL(0x2); to instruct the processor to use PSP instead of MSP. This method allows us to simulate multithreaded stack behavior by manually partitioning the stack region and explicitly controlling which stack the CPU is using.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,3,'2025-06-26_00-51-42','We used the debugger in this lab because some of the information we needed to verify—such as the current values of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), Process Stack Pointer (PSP), and general-purpose registers—is internal to the microcontroller and not easily accessible through printf. While printf is useful for confirming that certain code paths are executed or for displaying variables, it falls short when we need precise, low-level insights into how the processor is managing memory and registers during execution. For example, to verify that our computed PSP value was correctly set and being used by the system, we needed to examine the actual SP register (which should match our PSP value) and observe the value of the SP register to confirm that the processor had switched from using MSP to PSP. These kinds of observations are only possible using a debugger, which gives us real-time access to the processor’s internal state. Additionally, the debugger allowed us to set breakpoints and inspect values at exact moments in the program, such as right before entering the infinite loop in main(). This level of control and visibility cannot be achieved using printf alone, especially since printf itself relies on properly configured peripherals that might not yet be active or correctly set up when we need to gather this diagnostic information.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,4,'2025-06-26_00-06-58','The stack allocation method used in this lab is based on manually managing memory within a predefined stack region by treating the available stack space as a large array of memory. We began by retrieving the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), known as MSP_INIT_VAL, which represents the starting address of the default stack region set up by the microcontroller at boot. This value was obtained by dereferencing the memory address 0x0, which, according to the ARM Cortex-M memory map, contains the initial MSP value used during the reset sequence. Specifically, we declared a pointer with the line uint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0;, which effectively extracts the initial top-of-stack address.\nOnce we had MSP_INIT_VAL, we computed a new value for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), called PSP_val, by subtracting a fixed size from it—specifically 0x400 (1024 bytes), which we designated as the size of the main stack. This was done using the line uint32_t PSP_val = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL - 0x400;. The subtraction reflects the fact that stacks grow downward in memory, so to create a new stack region below the main one, we simply allocate space beneath it.\nKnowing the initial value of the MSP was essential because our entire stack allocation scheme was relative to it. We used MSP_INIT_VAL as the starting point for laying out additional stack regions in memory, such as those needed for threads in a future multitasking OS. Without this known reference point, we would not be able to reliably calculate where each thread\'s stack should begin. Later, we set the PSP to this computed value using the CMSIS function __set_PSP(PSP_val);, and we updated the CONTROL register via __set_CONTROL(0x2); to instruct the processor to use PSP instead of MSP. This method allows us to simulate multithreaded stack behavior by manually partitioning the stack region and explicitly controlling which stack the CPU is using.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,4,'2025-06-26_00-42-48','To allocate multiple thread stacks, we would divide the available stack memory into fixed-size blocks at compile time. Starting from MSP_INIT_VAL, we subtract MAIN_STACK_SIZE to reserve space for the main stack, then compute each thread stack’s base address using the formula:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - j * THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis formula works because stacks in ARM Cortex-M systems grow downward in memory, so each new stack must be placed at a lower address than the previous one. To ensure correctness, we must guarantee that the total allocated memory (MAIN_STACK_SIZE + num_threads * THREAD_STACK_SIZE) does not exceed the defined stack region (as specified in the linker script). Additionally, stack addresses should be 8-byte aligned to satisfy the Cortex-M architecture\'s alignment requirements and avoid faults. We can ensure this by choosing stack sizes that are multiples of 8 and aligning addresses manually if necessary. By managing offsets carefully and validating that all stack bases stay within the allowed SRAM range, we can reliably assign thread stacks at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,4,'2025-06-26_00-51-42','The stack allocation method used in this lab is based on manually managing memory within a predefined stack region by treating the available stack space as a large array of memory. We began by retrieving the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), known as MSP_INIT_VAL, which represents the starting address of the default stack region set up by the microcontroller at boot. This value was obtained by dereferencing the memory address 0x0, which, according to the ARM Cortex-M memory map, contains the initial MSP value used during the reset sequence. Specifically, we declared a pointer with the line uint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0;, which effectively extracts the initial top-of-stack address.\nOnce we had MSP_INIT_VAL, we computed a new value for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), called PSP_val, by subtracting a fixed size from it—specifically 0x400 (1024 bytes), which we designated as the size of the main stack. This was done using the line uint32_t PSP_val = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL - 0x400;. The subtraction reflects the fact that stacks grow downward in memory, so to create a new stack region below the main one, we simply allocate space beneath it.\nKnowing the initial value of the MSP was essential because our entire stack allocation scheme was relative to it. We used MSP_INIT_VAL as the starting point for laying out additional stack regions in memory, such as those needed for threads in a future multitasking OS. Without this known reference point, we would not be able to reliably calculate where each thread\'s stack should begin. Later, we set the PSP to this computed value using the CMSIS function __set_PSP(PSP_val);, and we updated the CONTROL register via __set_CONTROL(0x2); to instruct the processor to use PSP instead of MSP. This method allows us to simulate multithreaded stack behavior by manually partitioning the stack region and explicitly controlling which stack the CPU is using.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,5,'2025-06-26_00-06-58','To allocate multiple thread stacks using the same method, we would divide the stack memory region into fixed-size blocks, one for each thread, based on constants known at compile time. Starting from MSP_INIT_VAL, we reserve space for the main stack by subtracting MAIN_STACK_SIZE. Then, for each thread stack j, we compute its starting address using the formula: ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. We would define constants such as #define MAIN_STACK_SIZE 0x400 and #define THREAD_STACK_SIZE 0x400, and use simple pointer arithmetic to calculate the base address of each stack. These addresses can be stored or assigned during system initialization. Knowing MSP_INIT_VAL is essential because it serves as the base reference for laying out all stacks in memory without overlap.\n',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',1,5,'2025-06-26_00-51-42','To allocate multiple thread stacks, we would divide the available stack memory into fixed-size blocks at compile time. Starting from MSP_INIT_VAL, we subtract MAIN_STACK_SIZE to reserve space for the main stack, then compute each thread stack’s base address using the formula:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - j * THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis formula works because stacks in ARM Cortex-M systems grow downward in memory, so each new stack must be placed at a lower address than the previous one. To ensure correctness, we must guarantee that the total allocated memory (MAIN_STACK_SIZE + num_threads * THREAD_STACK_SIZE) does not exceed the defined stack region (as specified in the linker script). Additionally, stack addresses should be 8-byte aligned to satisfy the Cortex-M architecture\'s alignment requirements and avoid faults. We can ensure this by choosing stack sizes that are multiples of 8 and aligning addresses manually if necessary. By managing offsets carefully and validating that all stack bases stay within the allowed SRAM range, we can reliably assign thread stacks at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',2,1,'2025-06-26_23-06-06','By manually setting the Program Counter (PC) to the address of print_continuously, I effectively redirected the processor\'s execution flow to that function — mimicking a low-level function call. Specifically, the function pointer to print_continuously is first loaded into register R0. The jumpAssembly function then executes the instruction MOV PC, R0, which transfers the contents of R0 into the PC. Since the PC holds the address of the next instruction to execute, this causes the CPU to immediately begin fetching and executing instructions from the print_continuously function. This behavior is conceptually similar to a high-level function call, but instead of relying on the compiler and call stack, we explicitly manipulate control flow by altering the PC directly. Consequently, the program enters and indefinitely runs the infinite loop defined in print_continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',2,2,'2025-06-26_23-06-06','In this lab, a system call is a controlled mechanism that allows user-level code to request and execute privileged operations within the operating system, such as printing a message or launching a thread. This is achieved through the SVC #n (Supervisor Call) instruction, which triggers a software interrupt that diverts execution to the SVC_Handler. From there, control is passed to a C function, SVC_Handler_Main, which inspects the system call number embedded in the instruction and performs the corresponding operation. This mechanism is crucial for isolating user code from kernel-level functionality, ensuring that sensitive operations are only performed through well-defined, predictable interfaces. In essence, it mirrors how real-world operating systems (like Linux) implement system calls such as write() or fork(), enabling secure transitions between user and privileged modes.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',2,3,'2025-06-26_23-06-06','Setting up a thread’s stack means manually preparing a region of memory to simulate the exact state the CPU would expect if the thread had already been running and was paused mid-execution. In this lab, we create a separate stack (an array in memory) and initialize it with values that represent the saved state of the CPU registers — including general-purpose registers (R0–R12), the Link Register (LR), the Program Counter (PC), and the Program Status Register (xPSR). These values are pushed onto the stack in the specific order that the ARM Cortex-M processor uses during exception handling, because the CPU will later “pop” them off in that same order to restore execution. We need to store register values on the stack so that when the CPU switches to the new thread, it has all the information it needs — such as where to continue execution (PC), current flags (xPSR), and any data that was in the registers — just as if it had paused the thread earlier and is now resuming it. Without this setup, the processor wouldn’t know how to safely begin or resume the thread, and execution would be unpredictable.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',2,4,'2025-06-26_23-06-06','In the SVC_Handler_Main switch statement, we use the __set_PSP function to set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the thread’s pre-configured stack before calling runFirstThread(). This step is critical because when the CPU exits the interrupt handler (triggered by the SVC call), it expects to use the PSP to restore the thread\'s context — including registers like PC and xPSR — from the stack. If we didn’t set PSP to point to the valid memory location where we manually placed these values, the CPU would attempt to pop data from an undefined or invalid location. This would lead to corrupted or illegal values being loaded into the program counter and other registers, resulting in undefined behavior and triggering a hard fault. In other words, without setting PSP correctly, the processor has no idea where to find the simulated thread’s execution context, causing it to crash as it tries to resume execution from an invalid memory address.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',2,5,'2025-06-26_23-06-06','If the thread function did not loop infinitely and instead simply printed a message once and returned, the CPU would attempt to execute the instruction located in the Link Register (LR) — the return address — which was manually set during the thread\'s stack setup (e.g., to an arbitrary value like 0xA). Since this value doesn’t point to a valid or meaningful address in your program’s memory space, the processor would likely attempt to fetch instructions from an invalid location, resulting in a hard fault. This crash occurs because there’s no calling function to return to; the thread wasn’t started via a normal BL (branch with link) instruction but through direct PC manipulation, so the return context is artificial and meaningless. In real systems, threads that finish execution usually either enter a low-power state, are deleted, or return to a scheduler loop. In your case, if the function returns without a valid continuation point, the CPU has no safe execution path forward, so it crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,1,'2025-06-27_14-44-34','The stack pool is just a chunk of memory we set aside for threads to use as their own stacks. Instead of one big shared stack like we’ve used before, now we’re carving up this pool so each thread can have its own private space to store stuff like local variables, return addresses, and function calls. In this lab, we’re only using it for one thread, but later on—when we add more threads—we’ll keep track of where the last stack ended and hand out new chunks below that, kind of like a stack of plates. Each thread gets, say, 0x400 bytes of space, and we keep doing that until we run out of room. The stacks have to be aligned on 8-byte boundaries because that’s what ARM expects. Eventually, this setup will let us switch between threads safely, because each one will have its own stack and won’t mess with the others’.\n',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,1,'2025-06-27_16-11-46','When building an OS project that uses multiple .c files, like main.c, kernel.c, and others, the compiler doesn’t just read them all at once—it processes each file in stages to ultimately produce a single executable. First, during preprocessing, each source file has its #include statements and macros expanded, resulting in a translation unit that the compiler can understand. Next, in the compilation step, each preprocessed .c file is turned into a .o (object) file, which contains machine code but isn’t yet a full program. These object files still don’t know how they fit together—especially if a function is defined in one file and used in another. That’s where linking comes in. The linker scans all the object files and stitches them together into a single executable, resolving references between files using the function and variable declarations shared via .h header files. In a typical build system like Make or STM32CubeIDE, these steps are often hidden behind one command (like make or a button press), but under the hood it’s a multi-step pipeline that transforms modular source files into one cohesive binary.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,1,'2025-06-27_22-25-52','When building an OS project that uses multiple .c files, like main.c, kernel.c, and others, the compiler doesn’t just read them all at once—it processes each file in stages to ultimately produce a single executable. First, during preprocessing, each source file has its #include statements and macros expanded, resulting in a translation unit that the compiler can understand. Next, in the compilation step, each preprocessed .c file is turned into a .o (object) file, which contains machine code but isn’t yet a full program. These object files still don’t know how they fit together—especially if a function is defined in one file and used in another. That’s where linking comes in. The linker scans all the object files and stitches them together into a single executable, resolving references between files using the function and variable declarations shared via .h header files. In a typical build system like Make or STM32CubeIDE, these steps are often hidden behind one command (like make or a button press), but under the hood it’s a multi-step pipeline that transforms modular source files into one cohesive binary.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,2,'2025-06-27_14-44-34','Using #include <kernel.c> or any similar thing where you include a .c source file directly is kinda bad practice. It messes up the normal way C programs are supposed to compile and link stuff. The #include directive is really just for adding declarations—usually from .h header files—not for pasting in full-on implementations. When you include a .c file, the compiler basically pretends you copy-pasted all that code into your file, which breaks modular compilation.\nThis can cause some pretty annoying issues, like functions or global variables being defined more than once (which C really doesn\'t like), messy dependencies, and build errors because stuff gets redefined. As the lab explains, the better way to do it is to declare all your shared stuff—like function prototypes and structs—in a header file (like kernel.h), and then include that where needed. The real code in kernel.c should just get compiled on its own and then linked in with everything else.\nDoing it the proper way makes your code way cleaner, way easier to debug, and also lets you do incremental builds, which saves a lot of time—especially when you\'re building something like a kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,2,'2025-06-27_16-11-46','Using #include <kernel.c> or any similar thing where you include a .c source file directly is kinda bad practice. It messes up the normal way C programs are supposed to compile and link stuff. The #include directive is really just for adding declarations—usually from .h header files—not for pasting in full-on implementations. When you include a .c file, the compiler basically pretends you copy-pasted all that code into your file, which breaks modular compilation.\nThis can cause some pretty annoying issues, like functions or global variables being defined more than once (which C really doesn\'t like), messy dependencies, and build errors because stuff gets redefined. As the lab explains, the better way to do it is to declare all your shared stuff—like function prototypes and structs—in a header file (like kernel.h), and then include that where needed. The real code in kernel.c should just get compiled on its own and then linked in with everything else.\nDoing it the proper way makes your code way cleaner, way easier to debug, and also lets you do incremental builds, which saves a lot of time—especially when you\'re building something like a kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,2,'2025-06-27_22-25-52','Using #include <kernel.c> or any similar thing where you include a .c source file directly is kinda bad practice. It messes up the normal way C programs are supposed to compile and link stuff. The #include directive is really just for adding declarations—usually from .h header files—not for pasting in full-on implementations. When you include a .c file, the compiler basically pretends you copy-pasted all that code into your file, which breaks modular compilation.\nThis can cause some pretty annoying issues, like functions or global variables being defined more than once (which C really doesn\'t like), messy dependencies, and build errors because stuff gets redefined. As the lab explains, the better way to do it is to declare all your shared stuff—like function prototypes and structs—in a header file (like kernel.h), and then include that where needed. The real code in kernel.c should just get compiled on its own and then linked in with everything else.\nDoing it the proper way makes your code way cleaner, way easier to debug, and also lets you do incremental builds, which saves a lot of time—especially when you\'re building something like a kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,3,'2025-06-27_14-44-34','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, the process involves the following steps:\nInitialize the Kernel\nCall the kernel initialization function to prepare all necessary global variables, including stack allocation pointers and thread tracking structures. This must be done before creating any threads.\nDefine a Thread Function\nWrite a thread function that contains the user-defined code to run in the new thread. This function must conform to the expected format (i.e., take and return void).\nCreate the Thread\nUse the provided kernel API function to request the creation of a thread. This function allocates a stack, sets up the initial stack frame for execution, and registers the thread in the OS’s internal structures. If stack space is unavailable, thread creation fails.\nStart the Kernel\nOnce all threads have been created, call the function that starts the kernel. This function switches the processor from the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) to the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) associated with the created thread and begins execution of the user’s thread function.\nThread Runs Independently\nAfter the kernel starts, the thread runs autonomously, and the main function is no longer in control unless explicitly coded for cooperative multitasking or further kernel interaction.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,3,'2025-06-27_16-11-46','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, the process involves the following steps:\nInitialize the Kernel\nCall the kernel initialization function to prepare all necessary global variables, including stack allocation pointers and thread tracking structures. This must be done before creating any threads.\nDefine a Thread Function\nWrite a thread function that contains the user-defined code to run in the new thread. This function must conform to the expected format (i.e., take and return void).\nCreate the Thread\nUse the provided kernel API function to request the creation of a thread. This function allocates a stack, sets up the initial stack frame for execution, and registers the thread in the OS’s internal structures. If stack space is unavailable, thread creation fails.\nStart the Kernel\nOnce all threads have been created, call the function that starts the kernel. This function switches the processor from the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) to the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) associated with the created thread and begins execution of the user’s thread function.\nThread Runs Independently\nAfter the kernel starts, the thread runs autonomously, and the main function is no longer in control unless explicitly coded for cooperative multitasking or further kernel interaction.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,3,'2025-06-27_22-25-52','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, the process involves the following steps:\nInitialize the Kernel\nCall the kernel initialization function to prepare all necessary global variables, including stack allocation pointers and thread tracking structures. This must be done before creating any threads.\nDefine a Thread Function\nWrite a thread function that contains the user-defined code to run in the new thread. This function must conform to the expected format (i.e., take and return void).\nCreate the Thread\nUse the provided kernel API function to request the creation of a thread. This function allocates a stack, sets up the initial stack frame for execution, and registers the thread in the OS’s internal structures. If stack space is unavailable, thread creation fails.\nStart the Kernel\nOnce all threads have been created, call the function that starts the kernel. This function switches the processor from the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) to the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) associated with the created thread and begins execution of the user’s thread function.\nThread Runs Independently\nAfter the kernel starts, the thread runs autonomously, and the main function is no longer in control unless explicitly coded for cooperative multitasking or further kernel interaction.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,4,'2025-06-27_14-44-34','',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,4,'2025-06-27_16-11-46','',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,4,'2025-06-27_22-25-52','The stack pool is a chunk of memory in RAM that\'s set aside just for thread stacks in the OS. It’s defined in the linker script—like where _Min_Stack_Size is supposed to be at least 0x4000—and it’s what lets us give each thread its own stack instead of all the code sharing one big global one. That way, each thread runs with its own private space, so they don’t mess with each other’s stuff.\nFor this lab, we’re only using the stack pool to make space for one thread. We do that by subtracting from the top of the pool, kind of like how we did it back in lab 2. In future labs, we’ll make it more flexible by keeping track of the last spot we allocated, and then giving each new thread a fixed-size chunk—like 0x400 bytes or something. That way we can support multiple threads at once, each with its own stack for local variables, function calls, return addresses, etc.\nSince we’re working with ARM, all the stack addresses have to be aligned to 8-byte boundaries. So the allocator we write has to make sure everything lines up properly. With this setup, the OS can switch between threads safely just by saving and restoring their stack pointers. That’s what’s gonna let us do real multitasking—whether it’s preemptive or cooperative, depending on how we implement it later on.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,5,'2025-06-27_14-44-34','',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,5,'2025-06-27_16-11-46','',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',3,5,'2025-06-27_22-25-52','The \"user\" and the \"kernel\" are two parts of the code that need to be kept separate, and that’s why they go into different files. The user side is where you write your thread functions and call the kernel functions—it’s the stuff you directly interact with, and that all goes in the main file. The kernel is more like the low-level system part that handles things like thread creation, stack allocation, and switching between threads, and that goes in kernel.c, with the function declarations and structs in kernel.h so the user code can use them. This distinction matters a lot because it keeps the code more organized and prevents the user from messing with the kernel’s internal workings by accident. From the user\'s point of view, it makes things simpler—you just use the kernel like a tool without worrying about how it’s built, which is way easier to work with and way less error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,1,'2025-07-29_02-50-54','Round-robin scheduling can be effectively implemented using a queue by treating each task as an element in a circular data structure. When the currently running task finishes its time slice or yields the processor, it is dequeued from the front and enqueued at the back of the queue, ensuring that tasks are scheduled in a fair, first-come-first-served order. This mirrors the natural behavior of round-robin scheduling, which cycles through tasks without bias. Compared to a static array with an index pointer, a queue abstraction encapsulates the rotation logic more intuitively and can simplify certain operations, such as removing tasks from the middle (e.g., on termination) or dynamically adding new tasks. A queue also better enforces the logical idea that the scheduler should always run the \"next\" task, without manual index manipulation. However, arrays are simpler in environments with fixed numbers of tasks and predictable scheduling behavior. Using a queue introduces minimal overhead but increases clarity and modularity, especially in systems aiming for dynamic task management.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,1,'2025-07-29_03-06-48','Round-robin scheduling can be effectively implemented using a queue by treating each task as an element in a circular data structure. When the currently running task finishes its time slice or yields the processor, it is dequeued from the front and enqueued at the back of the queue, ensuring that tasks are scheduled in a fair, first-come-first-served order. This mirrors the natural behavior of round-robin scheduling, which cycles through tasks without bias. Compared to a static array with an index pointer, a queue abstraction encapsulates the rotation logic more intuitively and can simplify certain operations, such as removing tasks from the middle (e.g., on termination) or dynamically adding new tasks. A queue also better enforces the logical idea that the scheduler should always run the \"next\" task, without manual index manipulation. However, arrays are simpler in environments with fixed numbers of tasks and predictable scheduling behavior. Using a queue introduces minimal overhead but increases clarity and modularity, especially in systems aiming for dynamic task management.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,1,'2025-07-30_11-11-39','Round-robin scheduling can be effectively implemented using a queue by treating each task as an element in a circular data structure. When the currently running task finishes its time slice or yields the processor, it is dequeued from the front and enqueued at the back of the queue, ensuring that tasks are scheduled in a fair, first-come-first-served order. This mirrors the natural behavior of round-robin scheduling, which cycles through tasks without bias. Compared to a static array with an index pointer, a queue abstraction encapsulates the rotation logic more intuitively and can simplify certain operations, such as removing tasks from the middle (e.g., on termination) or dynamically adding new tasks. A queue also better enforces the logical idea that the scheduler should always run the \"next\" task, without manual index manipulation. However, arrays are simpler in environments with fixed numbers of tasks and predictable scheduling behavior. Using a queue introduces minimal overhead but increases clarity and modularity, especially in systems aiming for dynamic task management.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,2,'2025-07-29_02-50-54','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for performing a context switch, which involves saving the current thread’s state and restoring the state of the next thread to run. Specifically, it saves the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) of the currently running thread onto its process stack and stores the current stack pointer value in its corresponding TCB. Then it calls a C function, such as osSched, to determine the next thread to run. Once the next TCB is selected, PendSV_Handler restores that thread’s saved stack pointer and pops the saved registers from its stack. The stack pointer is then set to the restored value, so when execution resumes, it picks up exactly where the next thread left off. This ensures seamless task switching. The use of PendSV is crucial because it is a low-priority interrupt that allows safe deferral of the context switch until all other higher-priority interrupts are serviced.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,2,'2025-07-29_03-06-48','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for performing a context switch, which involves saving the current thread’s state and restoring the state of the next thread to run. Specifically, it saves the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) of the currently running thread onto its process stack and stores the current stack pointer value in its corresponding TCB. Then it calls a C function, such as osSched, to determine the next thread to run. Once the next TCB is selected, PendSV_Handler restores that thread’s saved stack pointer and pops the saved registers from its stack. The stack pointer is then set to the restored value, so when execution resumes, it picks up exactly where the next thread left off. This ensures seamless task switching. The use of PendSV is crucial because it is a low-priority interrupt that allows safe deferral of the context switch until all other higher-priority interrupts are serviced.\n',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,2,'2025-07-30_11-11-39','The PendSV_Handler is the core mechanism for performing context switches in the OS. When it is triggered, the first operation it performs is reading the value of the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which identifies the current thread\'s stack. This value is then used to save the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto the current thread\'s stack so the execution state is fully preserved. After saving the registers, the PSP value itself is stored into the current thread’s TCB. Next, a scheduling decision is made—usually via a call to the C function osSched—to select the next TCB to be loaded. The scheduler updates a global pointer to refer to this new thread, from which the PSP is restored. Finally, the saved callee-saved registers for the new thread are popped from its stack, and the system exits the interrupt, effectively resuming the new thread from where it left off. This sequence guarantees that each thread preserves its state independently and that the system can transition smoothly between threads. Being explicit about reading the PSP is critical because it\'s the hardware-managed register that enables each thread to maintain its own stack in a multitasking environment.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,3,'2025-07-29_02-50-54','Modifying the OS scheduler to always pick the thread with the highest priority, without any additional control, leads to priority inversion and potential starvation of lower-priority tasks. If a high-priority thread never yields or blocks, it can monopolize the CPU indefinitely, preventing other threads from executing. This breaks fairness and can render the system unresponsive or deadlocked if the high-priority thread depends on resources managed by lower-priority threads. For example, if a low-priority thread holds a lock needed by the high-priority thread, and the high-priority thread preempts all others, the system could deadlock. Proper priority-based systems typically include mechanisms like priority inheritance or aging to mitigate such issues. Without these, blindly prioritizing the highest-priority task results in fragile and unfair scheduling behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,3,'2025-07-29_03-06-48','Modifying the OS scheduler to always pick the thread with the highest priority, without any additional control, leads to priority inversion and potential starvation of lower-priority tasks. If a high-priority thread never yields or blocks, it can monopolize the CPU indefinitely, preventing other threads from executing. This breaks fairness and can render the system unresponsive or deadlocked if the high-priority thread depends on resources managed by lower-priority threads. For example, if a low-priority thread holds a lock needed by the high-priority thread, and the high-priority thread preempts all others, the system could deadlock. Proper priority-based systems typically include mechanisms like priority inheritance or aging to mitigate such issues. Without these, blindly prioritizing the highest-priority task results in fragile and unfair scheduling behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,3,'2025-07-30_11-11-39','If the scheduler is modified to always select the task with the highest priority, the result is that only the highest-priority task will ever run, completely excluding lower-priority threads from execution. This deterministic behavior fundamentally breaks the fairness and responsiveness expected in a multitasking OS. Lower-priority threads will never be scheduled unless the highest-priority thread blocks or yields, and if it never does, the system effectively becomes single-threaded. This isn\'t merely a risk of starvation—it guarantees it. While priority inversion and deadlocks are valid concerns in priority-based systems, the most immediate and critical flaw in this design is the loss of all but one thread from the scheduling loop. This differs from more nuanced priority scheduling algorithms, which may incorporate time slicing, priority aging, or dynamic boosting to ensure progress for all threads. A priority-only approach, without preemption safeguards or fairness mechanisms, renders the scheduler unusable for real-time systems that depend on predictable behavior across a range of tasks with different priorities.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,4,'2025-07-29_02-50-54','',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,4,'2025-07-29_03-06-48','Calling C functions like osSched or SVC_HandlerMain from within interrupt handlers offers significant benefits in readability, maintainability, and abstraction. Writing the entire logic in assembly would produce faster and more fine-tuned code, but at the cost of complexity and error-proneness. Assembly code is difficult to debug and harder to write correctly, especially for complex control structures or data manipulations. By contrast, C allows modular, type-checked, and higher-level logic that is easier to write, test, and evolve. For educational OS kernels or microcontroller firmware, using C where possible strikes a balance between low-level control and development efficiency. The downside is a small overhead from the context switch between assembly and C, and possibly less precise control over register usage and execution timing. Still, for most embedded systems not operating at real-time nanosecond constraints, the tradeoff heavily favors using C.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,4,'2025-07-30_11-11-39','Calling C functions like osSched or SVC_HandlerMain from within interrupt handlers offers significant benefits in readability, maintainability, and abstraction. Writing the entire logic in assembly would produce faster and more fine-tuned code, but at the cost of complexity and error-proneness. Assembly code is difficult to debug and harder to write correctly, especially for complex control structures or data manipulations. By contrast, C allows modular, type-checked, and higher-level logic that is easier to write, test, and evolve. For educational OS kernels or microcontroller firmware, using C where possible strikes a balance between low-level control and development efficiency. The downside is a small overhead from the context switch between assembly and C, and possibly less precise control over register usage and execution timing. Still, for most embedded systems not operating at real-time nanosecond constraints, the tradeoff heavily favors using C.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,5,'2025-07-29_02-50-54','',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,5,'2025-07-29_03-06-48','If only a single thread is created, such as a modified version of the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, the OS will still function in a limited way. The thread will yield, triggering the scheduler, but since no other threads exist, the scheduler will simply select the same thread again. The context switch will occur, but it will be redundant. This means the OS does not crash, and context-switching logic is still exercised, albeit trivially. However, the benefits of multitasking are lost, and the context switch overhead becomes unnecessary. It effectively becomes a single-threaded program with simulated multitasking infrastructure. This can be useful for testing the correctness of the context switch code or the scheduler in a degenerate case, but offers no practical concurrency or parallelism.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',4,5,'2025-07-30_11-11-39','If only a single thread is created, such as a modified version of the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, the OS will still function in a limited way. The thread will yield, triggering the scheduler, but since no other threads exist, the scheduler will simply select the same thread again. The context switch will occur, but it will be redundant. This means the OS does not crash, and context-switching logic is still exercised, albeit trivially. However, the benefits of multitasking are lost, and the context switch overhead becomes unnecessary. It effectively becomes a single-threaded program with simulated multitasking infrastructure. This can be useful for testing the correctness of the context switch code or the scheduler in a degenerate case, but offers no practical concurrency or parallelism.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,1,'2025-07-29_02-55-47','To pass an argument into a thread in our OS setup, the user must first define a function that takes a void* argument. This allows flexibility, as the user can pass any pointer type (including a pointer to a structure or primitive casted to a pointer). When creating the thread, the user provides the function pointer and the desired argument to the thread creation routine, such as osThreadCreate. Internally, the OS must store this argument in the newly allocated stack for the thread, typically placing it where the ABI expects the first argument—register r0. This is achieved by setting up the thread\'s initial stack frame to mimic a pre-interrupted CPU state, populating registers like r0 with the function argument, and the pc with the function address. When the thread is scheduled, the CPU \"restores\" this state from the stack, seamlessly loading the argument and jumping to the function. It works because the Cortex-M ABI guarantees that r0 is the first argument register, and the thread startup mimics a return from interrupt, triggering the correct execution flow with the right argument in place.\n',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,1,'2025-07-30_22-14-01','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must first define the data the thread will need. This data can be a simple primitive type like an int, or a more complex struct that aggregates multiple values. Crucially, the user must ensure this data resides in memory that persists across context switches—typically global memory or dynamically allocated heap space—so it remains accessible even when the thread is not actively running. Once the data is initialized, the user passes a pointer to it, cast as a void*, into osCreateThread. Inside the OS, during thread creation (as seen in kernel.c), the thread’s exception stack frame is built manually. During this setup, the void* argument pointer is explicitly stored into the R0 register using *(--sp) = (uint32_t)arg;. This step is critical because according to the ARM Architecture Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS), the first argument to a function is expected in R0. When the thread is eventually scheduled to run, the Cortex-M processor restores the saved register context from the stack. Since R0 was loaded with the user’s argument, the thread function receives that same pointer in its parameter, exactly as intended. The thread function, written to accept a void* parameter, can safely cast the pointer back to its original type and use the data. This process works reliably because it follows the calling convention defined for the Cortex-M4 processor. It allows for flexible argument passing—supporting both simple and structured data—while ensuring correctness through hardware and ABI guarantees.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,1,'2025-07-30_22-30-45','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must first create the data that the thread will operate on. This data must be stored in memory that persists throughout the thread’s lifetime, such as global variables or dynamically allocated memory on the heap—not on the stack of the creating function, which may be reclaimed. Once the data is initialized, the user passes a pointer to it—cast to a void*—into osCreateThread. Inside the OS, during the thread’s initialization (as seen in kernel.c), the thread’s exception stack frame is constructed manually. The argument is inserted into the R0 register slot by explicitly writing *(--sp) = (uint32_t)arg;, because under the ARM Architecture Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS), R0 is where the first argument to a function must reside. When the thread begins execution, the Cortex-M processor uses the manually assembled stack frame to restore the context. Since R0 holds the user-provided pointer, the thread function receives it exactly as intended. This mechanism works reliably because it conforms to the AAPCS and the hardware calling conventions of the Cortex-M4. If the function needed to take more arguments, R1–R3 would be used next, and if more were needed, the stack would be used. Failure to follow this calling convention—for example, by not placing the argument in R0—could lead to undefined behavior such as incorrect data being accessed or the thread misinterpreting garbage values.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,2,'2025-07-29_02-55-47','Calling osYield() inside a context-switching handler like PendSV_Handler, SysTick_Handler, or SVC_Handler is dangerous and fundamentally flawed. These handlers are already part of the scheduler\'s internal mechanism, and invoking osYield() from within them re-enters the scheduling logic while it is still in progress—creating recursion or reentrancy problems. This can lead to stack corruption, unexpected behavior, or outright crashes, especially since the OS\'s data structures (like ready queues or context pointers) may be mid-update. Furthermore, handlers are meant to perform quick, atomic operations. Yielding would potentially delay the completion of critical scheduling operations or deadlock the system. It’s also architecturally incoherent: yielding is a request to switch contexts, but you\'re already in a context-switch, so the semantics collapse. It\'s similar to telling a dispatcher mid-transfer to restart the process—confusing and dangerous.\n',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,2,'2025-07-30_22-14-01','Calling osYield from within any of the context-switching interrupt handlers—PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler—is unsafe because osYield is intended to be used only in thread mode, not in handler mode. Internally, osYield sets the PendSV bit to request a context switch, which is valid only when invoked from thread context. If you call osYield inside an interrupt handler, which executes in handler mode using the main stack, you can unintentionally trigger nested exceptions. Each nested interrupt forces the CPU to push another hardware stack frame, increasing the risk of stack overflow or memory corruption. More dangerously, PendSV typically runs at the lowest interrupt priority and will not execute until all higher-priority handlers have returned. If it is re-triggered within an already executing handler, the behavior becomes unpredictable and may result in infinite recursion or a deadlocked system. This misuse breaks the design constraints of ARM’s exception handling model and undermines the real-time operating system’s separation of concerns between thread and interrupt modes. It can lead to corrupted return sequences, inconsistent thread state, and ultimately, a system crash or lock-up.\n',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,2,'2025-07-30_22-30-45','Calling osYield from within an interrupt handler such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler is fundamentally unsafe because osYield is designed to be called in thread mode, not handler mode. Internally, osYield sets the PendSV bit to trigger a context switch—an operation that assumes the CPU is in thread mode using the process stack. If it’s called while already handling an exception, the CPU is using the main stack, and initiating another exception (PendSV) from within a handler can cause nested exceptions. These nested exceptions rapidly consume stack space, risking stack corruption. If this corruption affects the exception return frame, the system will not be able to restore the context correctly. On the Cortex-M4, this typically results in a hardfault, a severe error state that halts execution. Unlike a system “crash” or “lock-up,” a hardfault is a specific exception generated by the hardware when the processor encounters a serious fault condition—such as returning to an invalid address or accessing corrupted stack data. By explicitly naming this outcome as a hardfault, we make clear the nature and severity of the failure, aligning with the rubric’s emphasis on precise terminology and deep understanding of the ARM exception model.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,3,'2025-07-29_02-55-47','Reducing the OS tick interval from 1ms to 0.1ms increases the system timer frequency, offering finer-grained scheduling and more responsive delays or timeouts. This could improve the performance of real-time tasks needing tighter deadlines, such as control loops or audio processing. However, it significantly increases interrupt overhead: the SysTick_Handler would run 10 times more frequently, increasing CPU usage on scheduler bookkeeping. On resource-constrained systems like microcontrollers, this can starve application threads or drain battery life. It also makes debugging harder, as the system state changes more frequently. Unless the application truly benefits from sub-millisecond resolution, the cost in efficiency and complexity often outweighs the gains. You’d likely also need to recalibrate delay functions and review timing assumptions throughout the codebase.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,3,'2025-07-30_22-14-01','Reducing the OS clock tick interval from 1ms to 0.1ms improves timing precision and responsiveness. With a 0.1ms tick, system calls like osDelay(1) now delay for just 0.1ms instead of 1ms, enabling finer control over timing-critical operations such as real-time audio processing, high-speed sensor polling, or motor control. This also allows pre-emptive thread scheduling to occur more frequently, improving fairness and lowering latency for tasks with tight timing constraints. However, this increase in precision introduces significant overhead. A 0.1ms tick requires the SysTick interrupt to fire 10,000 times per second instead of 1,000, leading to a dramatic increase in CPU usage just to handle the timer. Each interrupt triggers context-saving and possibly a context switch, which consumes valuable processing time and reduces the capacity available for user tasks. On slower or lower-power processors, this may result in missed deadlines or even task starvation. In battery-powered systems, more frequent interrupts cause more frequent wake-ups, reducing energy efficiency. Thus, the tradeoff involves balancing timing resolution against CPU overhead and power consumption. For most embedded applications, a 1ms tick provides sufficient accuracy while keeping the system efficient and stable.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,3,'2025-07-30_22-30-45','Reducing the OS clock tick interval from 1ms to 0.1ms improves timing precision and responsiveness. With a 0.1ms tick, system calls like osDelay(1) now delay for just 0.1ms instead of 1ms, enabling finer control over timing-critical operations such as real-time audio processing, high-speed sensor polling, or motor control. This also allows pre-emptive thread scheduling to occur more frequently, improving fairness and lowering latency for tasks with tight timing constraints. However, this increase in precision introduces significant overhead. A 0.1ms tick requires the SysTick interrupt to fire 10,000 times per second instead of 1,000, leading to a dramatic increase in CPU usage just to handle the timer. Each interrupt triggers context-saving and possibly a context switch, which consumes valuable processing time and reduces the capacity available for user tasks. On slower or lower-power processors, this may result in missed deadlines or even task starvation. In battery-powered systems, more frequent interrupts cause more frequent wake-ups, reducing energy efficiency. Thus, the tradeoff involves balancing timing resolution against CPU overhead and power consumption. For most embedded applications, a 1ms tick provides sufficient accuracy while keeping the system efficient and stable.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,4,'2025-07-29_02-55-47','',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,4,'2025-07-30_22-14-01','From the user’s point of view, cooperative multitasking requires each thread to voluntarily yield the processor, typically by calling osYield() or invoking a blocking operation. This gives developers explicit control over task switching, but it also introduces risk: if a thread forgets to yield, it can monopolize the CPU, causing the entire system to freeze. While this model simplifies debugging and is predictable in trusted environments, it relies on all threads behaving correctly. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking uses hardware interrupts—like SysTick—to forcibly interrupt threads at regular intervals and switch control to others. This ensures that no thread can dominate the CPU, improving system responsiveness and fairness. It’s especially valuable in real-time or priority-based applications where certain tasks must run on a tight schedule regardless of other thread behavior. On the OS side, cooperative multitasking is simpler to implement because context switches only occur at known, explicit points. The system doesn’t need to manage timer-based interrupts or track time slices. Pre-emptive multitasking, however, requires a more complex infrastructure: periodic timer interrupts, logic to decrement runtime counters, automatic triggering of context switches, and proper handling of nested interrupts. Both models have their use cases—cooperative multitasking for simple, trusted systems, and pre-emptive multitasking for robust, time-sensitive applications.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,4,'2025-07-30_22-30-45','From the user’s point of view, cooperative multitasking requires each thread to voluntarily yield the processor, typically by calling osYield() or invoking a blocking operation. This gives developers explicit control over task switching, but it also introduces risk: if a thread forgets to yield, it can monopolize the CPU, causing the entire system to freeze. While this model simplifies debugging and is predictable in trusted environments, it relies on all threads behaving correctly. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking uses hardware interrupts—like SysTick—to forcibly interrupt threads at regular intervals and switch control to others. This ensures that no thread can dominate the CPU, improving system responsiveness and fairness. It’s especially valuable in real-time or priority-based applications where certain tasks must run on a tight schedule regardless of other thread behavior. On the OS side, cooperative multitasking is simpler to implement because context switches only occur at known, explicit points. The system doesn’t need to manage timer-based interrupts or track time slices. Pre-emptive multitasking, however, requires a more complex infrastructure: periodic timer interrupts, logic to decrement runtime counters, automatic triggering of context switches, and proper handling of nested interrupts. Both models have their use cases—cooperative multitasking for simple, trusted systems, and pre-emptive multitasking for robust, time-sensitive applications.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,5,'2025-07-29_02-55-47','',0,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,5,'2025-07-30_22-14-01','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching coexist because they both interact with the same scheduler, albeit through different triggers. When a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily relinquishes control, prompting the scheduler to pick another ready thread. On the other hand, pre-emption occurs automatically when a timer interrupt like SysTick_Handler fires and reduces the current thread’s runtime. Once this runtime reaches zero, the OS resets it to the thread’s time slice and initiates a PendSV interrupt to perform the context switch. Though the initiation mechanisms differ—one is software-driven, the other hardware-driven—the end result is identical: the OS saves the current thread’s state and restores the next selected thread. Because both mechanisms rely on the same context-switching infrastructure, they can be used interchangeably and even complement each other within a hybrid scheduling model. This integration allows for fine-grained multitasking control: developers can use osYield() in cooperative scenarios for predictable transitions, while still relying on pre-emptive interrupts to guarantee system responsiveness and fairness. Together, they provide a robust, flexible scheduling environment where threads can both cooperate and be managed forcibly when necessary.\n',1,'s25'),('ixvanden',5,5,'2025-07-30_22-30-45','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching coexist because they both interact with the same scheduler, albeit through different triggers. When a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily relinquishes control, prompting the scheduler to pick another ready thread. On the other hand, pre-emption occurs automatically when a timer interrupt like SysTick_Handler fires and reduces the current thread’s runtime. Once this runtime reaches zero, the OS resets it to the thread’s time slice and initiates a PendSV interrupt to perform the context switch. Though the initiation mechanisms differ—one is software-driven, the other hardware-driven—the end result is identical: the OS saves the current thread’s state and restores the next selected thread. Because both mechanisms rely on the same context-switching infrastructure, they can be used interchangeably and even complement each other within a hybrid scheduling model. This integration allows for fine-grained multitasking control: developers can use osYield() in cooperative scenarios for predictable transitions, while still relying on pre-emptive interrupts to guarantee system responsiveness and fairness. Together, they provide a robust, flexible scheduling environment where threads can both cooperate and be managed forcibly when necessary.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-05-29_09-06-51','The HAL which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, contains a set of IDEs written by ST Microelectronics that allows users to skip the difficult part in writing the setup code for a microcontroller. Utilizing the HAL allows for an easier development because the HAL takes care of all the tedious tasks for users for them to focus solely on the main code.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-05-29_09-12-40','The HAL which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, contains a set of IDEs written by ST Microelectronics that allows users to skip the difficult part in writing the setup code for a microcontroller. Utilizing the HAL allows for an easier development because the HAL takes care of all the tedious tasks for users for them to focus solely on the main code.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-05-29_11-37-17','The HAL which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, contains a set of IDEs written by ST Microelectronics that allows users to skip the difficult part in writing the setup code for a microcontroller. Utilizing the HAL allows for an easier development because the HAL takes care of all the tedious tasks for users for them to focus solely on the main code.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-05-30_09-41-12','The HAL, which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software library developed by ST Microelectronics that contains prewritten functions to simplify the task of getting software programs to coordinate and communicate with various hardware components. It eliminates the need to manually write drivers, functions, etc. for every kind of hardware device, allowing flexibility for users to use the same software program across multiple hardware devices. Thus, utilizing the HAL allows for users to focus solely on the main code.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-05-30_11-13-56','The HAL, which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software library developed by ST Microelectronics that contains prewritten functions to simplify the task of getting software programs to coordinate and communicate with various hardware components. It eliminates the need to manually write drivers, functions, etc. for every kind of hardware device, allowing flexibility for users to use the same software program across multiple hardware devices. Thus, utilizing the HAL allows for users to focus solely on the main code.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-05-30_11-24-27','The HAL, which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software library developed by ST Microelectronics that contains prewritten functions to simplify the task of getting software programs to coordinate and communicate with various hardware components. It eliminates the need to manually write drivers, functions, etc. for every kind of hardware device, allowing flexibility for users to use the same software program across multiple hardware devices. Thus, utilizing the HAL allows for users to focus solely on the main code.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-06-03_20-56-14','The HAL, which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software library developed by ST Microelectronics that contains prewritten functions to simplify the task of getting software programs to coordinate and communicate with various hardware components. It eliminates the need to manually write drivers, functions, etc. for every kind of hardware device, allowing flexibility for users to use the same software program across multiple hardware devices. Thus, utilizing the HAL allows for users to focus solely on the main code.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-06-03_21-23-16','The HAL, which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software library developed by ST Microelectronics that contains prewritten functions to simplify the task of getting software programs to coordinate and communicate with various hardware components. It eliminates the need to manually write drivers, functions, etc. for every kind of hardware device, allowing flexibility for users to use the same software program across multiple hardware devices. Thus, utilizing the HAL allows for users to focus solely on the main code.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-06-22_07-47-41','The HAL, which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software library developed by ST Microelectronics that contains prewritten functions to simplify the task of getting software programs to coordinate and communicate with various hardware components. It eliminates the need to manually write drivers, functions, etc. for every kind of hardware device, allowing flexibility for users to use the same software program across multiple hardware devices. Thus, utilizing the HAL allows for users to focus solely on the main code.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-06-22_23-38-13','The HAL, which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software library developed by ST Microelectronics that contains prewritten functions to simplify the task of getting software programs to coordinate and communicate with various hardware components. It eliminates the need to manually write drivers, functions, etc. for every kind of hardware device, allowing flexibility for users to use the same software program across multiple hardware devices. Thus, utilizing the HAL allows for users to focus solely on the main code.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,1,'2025-06-23_01-19-32','The HAL, which stands for the Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software library developed by ST Microelectronics that contains prewritten functions to simplify the task of getting software programs to coordinate and communicate with various hardware components. It eliminates the need to manually write drivers, functions, etc. for every kind of hardware device, allowing flexibility for users to use the same software program across multiple hardware devices. Thus, utilizing the HAL allows for users to focus solely on the main code.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,2,'2025-05-29_09-06-51','The _io_putchar function is a return function that returns the very same character that was inputted as it’s char parameter. It’s purpose in this project is to take that character and write it into the UART2 IO channel, utilizing the HAL_UART_Transmit function to achieve so. This is a needed function especially when using printf, as the <stdio.h> library is mainly reliant on these helper functions. The printf case in particular, calls the _io_putchar function multiple times to print out the desired string.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,2,'2025-05-29_09-12-40','The _io_putchar function is a return function that returns the very same character that was inputted as it’s char parameter. It’s purpose in this project is to take that character and write it into the UART2 IO channel, utilizing the HAL_UART_Transmit function to achieve so. This is a needed function especially when using printf, as the <stdio.h> library is mainly reliant on these helper functions. The printf case in particular, calls the _io_putchar function multiple times to print out the desired string.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,2,'2025-05-29_11-37-17','The _io_putchar function is a return function that returns the very same character that was inputted as it’s char parameter. It’s purpose in this project is to take that character and write it into the UART2 IO channel, utilizing the HAL_UART_Transmit function to achieve so. This is a needed function especially when using printf, as the <stdio.h> library is mainly reliant on these helper functions. The printf case in particular, calls the _io_putchar function multiple times to print out the desired string.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,2,'2025-05-30_09-41-12','The _io_putchar function is a return function that returns the very same character that was inputted as it’s char parameter. This is a needed function especially when using printf, as it calls the _io_putchar function multiple times to print out the desired input string. Another reason why the _io_putchar function is required to be implemented is because the <stdio.h> library, where the printf function comes from is mainly reliant on these helper functions to allow it to interact with various kinds of hardware that provide different configurations of the output. The _io_putchar function is interpreted differently for each type of hardware so that the printf function can flexibly function.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,2,'2025-05-30_11-13-56','The _io_putchar function is a return function that returns the very same character that was inputted as it’s char parameter. This is a needed function especially when using printf, as it calls the _io_putchar function multiple times to print out the desired input string. Another reason why the _io_putchar function is required to be implemented is because the <stdio.h> library, where the printf function comes from is mainly reliant on these helper functions to allow it to interact with various kinds of hardware that provide different configurations of the output. The _io_putchar function is interpreted differently for each type of hardware so that the printf function can flexibly function.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,2,'2025-05-30_11-24-27','The _io_putchar function is a return function that returns the very same character that was inputted as it’s char parameter. This is a needed function especially when using printf, as it calls the _io_putchar function multiple times to print out the desired input string. Another reason why the _io_putchar function is required to be implemented is because the <stdio.h> library, where the printf function comes from is mainly reliant on these helper functions to allow it to interact with various kinds of hardware that provide different configurations of the output. The _io_putchar function is inter