de at various points of time. Specifically, it investigates data such as values of local and global variables, the order of function calls, and importantly, registers. The debugger uses a concept known as breakpoints, which is placed by the user before pressing the debug button. Its function is to allow the program to run the debugging process and stop at each indicated breakpoint, where the variable and register values can be viewed AT the moment where the code hit the breakpoint. This allows users to identify exactly where errors were made in the program. In this lab, it was important that the debugger tool displayed the values of all the registers at the desired breakpoint. These registers include the registers r0 to r12, sp, pc, and xpsr. In the lab we used the debugger to view the register that stores the address of variables MSP and PSP values to see if they were spaced properly in the stack that was created. Simply using the printf function to check values isn’t as efficient, printf only displays as many outputs at one instance, which doesn’t grant as much flexibility as the debugger with breakpoints. Furthermore, using printf functions can potentially alter the behaviour of the general code.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,3,'2025-06-23_01-19-32','The debugger is a useful tool that acts as a method to “view” the internal process of a code at various points of time. Specifically, it investigates data such as values of local and global variables, the order of function calls, and importantly, registers. The debugger uses a concept known as breakpoints, which is placed by the user before pressing the debug button. Its function is to allow the program to run the debugging process and stop at each indicated breakpoint, where the variable and register values can be viewed AT the moment where the code hit the breakpoint. This allows users to identify exactly where errors were made in the program. In this lab, it was important that the debugger tool displayed the values of all the registers at the desired breakpoint. These registers include the registers r0 to r12, sp, pc, and xpsr. In the lab we used the debugger to view the register that stores the address of variables MSP and PSP values to see if they were spaced properly in the stack that was created. Simply using the printf function to check values isn’t as efficient, printf only displays as many outputs at one instance, which doesn’t grant as much flexibility as the debugger with breakpoints. Furthermore, using printf functions can potentially alter the behaviour of the general code.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,4,'2025-05-29_09-06-51','The stack allocation method can be described starting with the max stack size (denoted by the constant variable MAX_STACK_SIZE), which for the lab was set to 0x4000 bytes. Inside this limited stack space, there is one main stack size which is already defined in the operating system code, and as many desired thread stacks that can fit in the max stack size which is, also defined by the user. Assuming we have the value for MSP_INIT_VAL, the PSP value can simply be determined by subtracting the size of the main stack. It’s important to note that when computing to use the integer value of the MSP_INIT_VAL. It’s important to know the initial location of MSP so that the PSP can be properly inserted into the downward stack.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,4,'2025-05-29_09-12-40','The stack allocation method can be described starting with the max stack size (denoted by the constant variable MAX_STACK_SIZE), which for the lab was set to 0x4000 bytes. Inside this limited stack space, there is one main stack size which is already defined in the operating system code, and as many desired thread stacks that can fit in the max stack size which is, also defined by the user. Assuming we have the value for MSP_INIT_VAL, the PSP value can simply be determined by subtracting the size of the main stack. It’s important to note that when computing to use the integer value of the MSP_INIT_VAL. It’s important to know the initial location of MSP so that the PSP can be properly inserted into the downward stack.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,4,'2025-05-29_11-37-17','The stack allocation method can be described starting with the max stack size (denoted by the constant variable MAX_STACK_SIZE), which for the lab was set to 0x4000 bytes. Inside this limited stack space, there is one main stack size which is already defined in the operating system code, and as many desired thread stacks that can fit in the max stack size which is, also defined by the user. Assuming we have the value for MSP_INIT_VAL, the PSP value can simply be determined by subtracting the size of the main stack. It’s important to note that when computing to use the integer value of the MSP_INIT_VAL. It’s important to know the initial location of MSP so that the PSP can be properly inserted into the downward stack.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,4,'2025-05-30_09-41-12','The stack allocation method can be described starting with the max stack size (denoted by the constant variable MAX_STACK_SIZE), which for the lab was set to 0x4000 bytes. Inside this limited stack space, there is one main stack size which is already defined in the operating system code, and as many desired thread stacks that can fit in the max stack size which is, also defined by the user. Assuming we have the value for MSP_INIT_VAL, the PSP value can simply be determined by subtracting the size of the main stack. It’s important to note that when computing to use the integer value of the MSP_INIT_VAL. It’s important to know the initial location of MSP that indicates the top of the stack base address; it ensures that PSP is always properly aligned in the stack memory is spaced and grown downwards ensuring that it stays in said valid memory space. This is achieved by initializing the address of PSP a set amount under the MSP value.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,4,'2025-05-30_11-13-56','The stack allocation method can be described s