e (denoted by the constant variable MAX_STACK_SIZE), which for the lab was set to 0x4000 bytes. Inside this limited stack space, there is one main stack size which is already defined in the operating system code, and as many desired thread stacks that can fit in the max stack size which is, also defined by the user. Assuming we have the value for MSP_INIT_VAL, the PSP value can simply be determined by subtracting the size of the main stack. It’s important to note that when computing to use the integer value of the MSP_INIT_VAL. It’s important to know the initial location of MSP that indicates the top of the stack base address; it ensures that PSP is always properly aligned in the stack memory is spaced and grown downwards ensuring that it stays in said valid memory space. This is achieved by initializing the address of PSP a set amount under the MSP value.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-05-29_09-06-51','Allocating new stacks for use in multiple threads means that for each new stack created, the code needs to be modified to assign new pointers, referencing the previous pointer referencing the previous stack to determine the spacing address between each stack pointer reference. Since each new stack is created under an existing stack, it is important to keep note of the total memory allocated for these stacks. If the pointer is trying to reference an address that is less than 0, it means that there is not enough space for the stack to be created and stored in the memory.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-05-29_09-12-40','Allocating new stacks for use in multiple threads means that for each new stack created, the code needs to be modified to assign new pointers, referencing the previous pointer referencing the previous stack to determine the spacing address between each stack pointer reference. Since each new stack is created under an existing stack, it is important to keep note of the total memory allocated for these stacks. If the pointer is trying to reference an address that is less than 0, it means that there is not enough space for the stack to be created and stored in the memory.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-05-29_11-37-17','Allocating new stacks for use in multiple threads means that for each new stack created, the code needs to be modified to assign new pointers, referencing the previous pointer referencing the previous stack to determine the spacing address between each stack pointer reference. Since each new stack is created under an existing stack, it is important to keep note of the total memory allocated for these stacks. If the pointer is trying to reference an address that is less than 0, it means that there is not enough space for the stack to be created and stored in the memory.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-05-30_09-41-12','Allocating new stacks for use in multiple threads means that for each new stack created, the code needs to be modified to assign new pointers, referencing the previous pointer referencing the previous stack to determine the spacing address between each stack pointer reference. Since each new stack is created under an existing stack, it is important to keep note of the total memory allocated for these stacks. If the pointer is trying to reference an address that is less than 0, it means that there is not enough space for the stack to be created and stored in the memory.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-05-30_11-13-56','Allocating new stacks for use in multiple threads means that for each new stack created, the code needs to be modified to assign new pointers, referencing the previous pointer referencing the previous stack to determine the spacing address between each stack pointer reference. Since each new stack is created under an existing stack, it is important to keep note of the total memory allocated for these stacks. If the pointer is trying to reference an address that is less than 0, it means that there is not enough space for the stack to be created and stored in the memory.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-05-30_11-24-27','Allocating new stacks for use in multiple threads means that for each new stack created, the code needs to be modified to assign new pointers, referencing the previous pointer referencing the previous stack to determine the spacing address between each stack pointer reference. Since each new stack is created under an existing stack, it is important to keep note of the total memory allocated for these stacks. If the pointer is trying to reference an address that is less than 0, it means that there is not enough space for the stack to be created and stored in the memory.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-06-03_20-56-14','To allocate new stacks for use in multiple threads, a known, valid stack location in the memory is required to properly store said stacks. This can be done simply by referencing address of MSP_INIT_VAL in our code, since it is known to be a valid stack address. Since the number of stacks is known at compile time, it means that the program does not have to be given or calculated by the user, which saves the need to dynamically allocate the stack and compute stack addresses during runtime. A straightforward way to initialize multiple stacks now that the first stack created using the valid address of MSP_INIT_VAL is to create a reference to the bottom of the existing stack, which should be repeated for each stack created as it grows downward. On the Cortex M4, since the stack memory space is predefined, it is important to determine if the number of stacks is valid to be created in said limited memory space. If the pointer is trying to reference an address at the bottom of the allocated memory address, it means that there is not enough space for all the stacks to fit.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-06-03_21-23-16','To allocate new stacks for use in multiple threads, a known, valid stack location in the memory is required to properly store said stacks. This can be done simply by referencing address of MSP_INIT_VAL in our code, since it is known to be a valid stack address. Since the number of stacks is known at compile time, it means that the program does not have to be given or calculated by the user, which saves the need to dynamically allocate the stack and compute stack addresses during runtime. A straightforward way to initialize multiple stacks now that the first stack created using the valid address of MSP_INIT_VAL is to create a reference pointing to the bottom of the existing stack, which should be repeated for each stack created as it grows downward. This can be done by subtracting the address of the top of MSP_INIT_VAL by the nth stack * the stack size. On the Cortex M4, since the stack memory space is predefined, it is important to determine if the number of stacks is valid to be created in said limited memory space. If the pointer is trying to reference an address at the bottom of the allocated memory address, it means that there is not enough space for all the stacks to fit.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-06-22_07-47-41','To allocate new stacks for use in multiple threads, a known, valid stack location in the memory is required to properly store said stacks. This can be done simply by referencing address of MSP_INIT_VAL in our code, since it is known to be a valid stack address. Since the number of stacks is known at compile time, it means that the program does not have to be given or calculated by the user, which saves the need to dynamically allocate the stack and compute stack addresses during runtime. A straightforward way to initialize multiple stacks now that the first stack created using the valid address of MSP_INIT_VAL is to create a reference pointing to the bottom of the existing stack, which should be repeated for each stack created as it grows downward. This can be done by subtracting the address of the top of MSP_INIT_VAL by the nth stack * the stack size. On the Cortex M4, since the stack memory space is predefined, it is important to determine if the number of stacks is valid to be created in said limited memory space. If the pointer is trying to reference an address at the bottom of the allocated memory address, it means that there is not enough space for all the stacks to fit.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-06-22_23-38-13','To allocate new stacks for use in multiple threads, a known, valid stack location in the memory is required to properly store said stacks. This can be done simply by referencing address of MSP_INIT_VAL in our code, since it is known to be a valid stack address. Since the number of stacks is known at compile time, it means that the program does not have to be given or calculated by the user, which saves the need to dynamically allocate the stack and compute stack addresses during runtime. A straightforward way to initialize multiple stacks now that the first stack created using the valid address of MSP_INIT_VAL is to create a reference pointing to the bottom of the existing stack, which should be repeated for each stack created as it grows downward. This can be done by subtracting the address of the top of MSP_INIT_VAL by the nth stack * the stack size. On the Cortex M4, since the stack memory space is predefined, it is important to determine if the number of stacks is valid to be created in said limited memory space. If the pointer is trying to reference an address at the bottom of the allocated memory address, it means that there is not enough space for all the stacks to fit.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',1,5,'2025-06-23_01-19-32','To allocate new stacks for use in multiple threads, a known, valid stack location in the memory is required to properly store said stacks. This can be done simply by referencing address of MSP_INIT_VAL in our code, since it is known to be a valid stack address. Since the number of stacks is known at compile time, it means that the program does not have to be given or calculated by the user, which saves the need to dynamically allocate the stack and compute stack addresses during runtime. A straightforward way to initialize multiple stacks now that the first stack created using the valid address of MSP_INIT_VAL is to create a reference pointing to the bottom of the existing stack, which should be repeated for each stack created as it grows downward. This can be done by subtracting the address of the top of MSP_INIT_VAL by the nth stack * the stack size. On the Cortex M4, since the stack memory space is predefined, it is important to determine if the number of stacks is valid to be created in said limited memory space. If the pointer is trying to reference an address at the bottom of the allocated memory address, it means that there is not enough space for all the stacks to fit.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,1,'2025-06-09_13-20-27','The function pointer does not\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,1,'2025-06-09_13-30-37','Looking at the output of our code, the code still outputs “Hello, PC!” continuously, regardless of if the function was called in main or by manipulation of the PC register. However, these outcomes are vastly different when considered how the function was executed. Before manipulating the PC, the CPU would call the print_continuously function in the main program, pushing a return address onto the stack and executing it, where the PC would automatically be changed through this process. The lab had us use a particular assembly code, a no return function called jumpAssembly, that took the address of our print_continuously function (and consequently being stored into r0), took that value and moved it from the r0 register to the PC register. This causes the program to permanently execute the print_continously function.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,1,'2025-06-22_08-05-14','Looking at the output of our code, the code still outputs “Hello, PC!” continuously, regardless of if the function was called in main or by manipulation of the PC register. However, these outcomes are vastly different when considered how the function was executed. The PC register is one that holds the address of the next instruction that needs to be executed, and so the print_continuously function wouldn’t initially held inside the PC before runtime. Instead, the CPU would call the print_continuously function in the main program, pushing a return address into the stack before jumping (Setting the address into the PC). This provides a way for the program to return to the main program if print_continously function hypothetically had a way to finish its instruction. Conversely, using a no return function called jumpAssembly, taking the address of our print_continuously function (and consequently being stored into r0), and moving it from the r0 register to the PC register does not provide a way to go back to the main program because it wasn’t manually coded in. This causes the program to permanently execute the print_continously function, without a way to go back to the main program.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,1,'2025-06-22_23-44-08','Looking at the output of our code, the code still outputs “Hello, PC!” continuously, regardless of if the function was called in main or by manipulation of the PC register. However, these outcomes are vastly different when considered how the function was executed. The PC register is one that holds the address of the next instruction that needs to be executed, and so the print_continuously function wouldn’t initially held inside the PC before runtime. Instead, the CPU would call the print_continuously function in the main program, pushing a return address into the stack before jumping (Setting the address into the PC). This provides a way for the program to return to the main program if print_continously function hypothetically had a way to finish its instruction. Conversely, using a no return function called jumpAssembly, taking the address of our print_continuously function (and consequently being stored into r0), and moving it from the r0 register to the PC register does not provide a way to go back to the main program because it wasn’t manually coded in. This causes the program to permanently execute the print_continously function, without a way to go back to the main program.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,1,'2025-06-22_23-58-24','Looking at the output of our code, the code still outputs “Hello, PC!” continuously, regardless of if the function was called in main or by manipulation of the PC register, but the behaviour of the program is very different. The PC register is one that holds the address of the next instruction that needs to be executed, and thus the address of print_continuously is not preloaded into the PC at compile time but instead loaded at runtime when the function is called. Instead, the CPU would call the print_continuously function in the main program, pushing a return address into the LR before jumping (Setting the address into the PC). This shows that a proper function call provides a way for the program to return to the main program if print_continously function hypothetically had a way to finish its instruction. Conversely, using a no return function called jumpAssembly, taking the address of our print_continuously function (and consequently being stored into r0), and moving it from the r0 register to the PC register does not provide a way to go back to the main program because a manual jump doesn’t save a return address in the LR. This causes the program to permanently execute the print_continously function, without a way to go back to the main program. In summary, while the two methods are functionally equivalent as they both store the address of the function into the PC, hence the same output, manually setting the PC skips the process of setting a return value and thus can never go back to the main program when done this way.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,1,'2025-06-23_01-13-20','Looking at the output of our code, the code still outputs “Hello, PC!” continuously, regardless of if the function was called in main or by manipulation of the PC register, but the behaviour of the program is very different. The PC register is one that holds the address of the next instruction that needs to be executed, and thus the address of print_continuously is not preloaded into the PC at compile time but instead loaded at runtime when the function is called. Instead, the CPU would call the print_continuously function in the main program, pushing a return address into the LR before jumping (Setting the address into the PC). This shows that a proper function call provides a way for the program to return to the main program if print_continously function hypothetically had a way to finish its instruction. Conversely, using a no return function called jumpAssembly, taking the address of our print_continuously function (and consequently being stored into r0), and moving it from the r0 register to the PC register does not provide a way to go back to the main program because a manual jump doesn’t save a return address in the LR. This causes the program to permanently execute the print_continously function, without a way to go back to the main program. In summary, while the two methods are functionally equivalent as they both store the address of the function into the PC, hence the same output, manually setting the PC skips the process of setting a return value and thus can never go back to the main program when done this way.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,2,'2025-06-09_13-20-27','',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,2,'2025-06-09_13-30-37','A system call is a method that allows the user to pass specific instructions from the program to the OS to either perform certain function or access hardware. In this lab, this is achieved using the SVC instruction to create a system call framework. Using assembly, the line __asm(“SVC” # xx”) triggers this system call, which is like telling the OS to do a certain action. It will pass this into the SVC_Handler function which prepares the stack and calls the C function SVC_Handler. This function controls the actual system call and performs the task based on the number xx passed by the assembly line. This method provided a secure way to handle tasks like I/O or memory management.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,2,'2025-06-22_08-05-14','A system call is a method that allows the user to pass specific instructions from the program to the OS to either perform certain function or access hardware. In this lab, this is achieved using the SVC instruction to create a system call framework. Using assembly, the line __asm(“SVC” # xx”) triggers this system call, which is like telling the OS to do a certain action. It will pass this into the SVC_Handler function which prepares the stack and calls the C function SVC_Handler. This function controls the actual system call and performs the task based on the number xx passed by the assembly line. This method provided a secure way to handle tasks like I/O or memory management.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,2,'2025-06-22_23-44-08','A system call is a method that allows the user to pass specific instructions from the program to the OS to either perform certain function or access hardware. In this lab, this is achieved using the SVC instruction to create a system call framework. Using assembly, the line __asm(“SVC” # xx”) triggers this system call, which is like telling the OS to do a certain action. It will pass this into the SVC_Handler function which prepares the stack and calls the C function SVC_Handler. This function controls the actual system call and performs the task based on the number xx passed by the assembly line. This method provided a secure way to handle tasks like I/O or memory management.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,2,'2025-06-22_23-58-24','A system call is a method that allows the user to pass specific instructions from the program to the OS to either perform certain function or access hardware. In this lab, this is achieved using the SVC instruction to create a system call framework. Using assembly, the line __asm(“SVC” # xx”) triggers this system call, which is like telling the OS to do a certain action. It will pass this into the SVC_Handler function which prepares the stack and calls the C function SVC_Handler. This function controls the actual system call and performs the task based on the number xx passed by the assembly line. A system call is important for the security of hardware access as it provides protection against Untrustworthy or faulty user code. If a user code were to directly be able to access hardware, it would lead to crashes, corruption of hardware components, and/or unwanted access to protected memory; this is why processors today have 2 distinct modes: a user mode (unprivileged access to hardware) and kernel mode (privileged access) for the purpose of mediating access to hardware. User codes run in user mode, which means it does not get access to hardware directly, instead it must utilize a system call to call the OS to perform a requested instruction. The mode bit is then set to kernel mode for the OS to either validate or deny the request. System calls can be seen as a sort of entryway for the user program to safely access the hardware by requesting the OS to validate a task for the user program.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,2,'2025-06-23_01-13-20','A system call is a method that allows the user to pass specific instructions from the program to the OS to either perform certain function or access hardware. In this lab, this is achieved using the SVC instruction to create a system call framework. Using assembly, the line __asm(“SVC” # xx”) triggers this system call, which is like telling the OS to do a certain action. It will pass this into the SVC_Handler function which prepares the stack and calls the C function SVC_Handler. This function controls the actual system call and performs the task based on the number xx passed by the assembly line. A system call is important for the security of hardware access as it provides protection against Untrustworthy or faulty user code. If a user code were to directly be able to access hardware, it would lead to crashes, corruption of hardware components, and/or unwanted access to protected memory; this is why processors today have 2 distinct modes: a user mode (unprivileged access to hardware) and kernel mode (privileged access) for the purpose of mediating access to hardware. User codes run in user mode, which means it does not get access to hardware directly, instead it must utilize a system call to call the OS to perform a requested instruction. The mode bit is then set to kernel mode for the OS to either validate or deny the request. System calls can be seen as a sort of entryway for the user program to safely access the hardware by requesting the OS to validate a task for the user program.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,3,'2025-06-09_13-20-27','',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,3,'2025-06-09_13-30-37','To “Set up a thread’s stack” is to manually set the values of the registers and memory into the stack before the CPU can resume the execution of the thread. This is required as when the CPU resumes a thread, it pops the values in the stack onto it’s registers, so before this happens the register values must be pushed into the stack in the correct order of xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4. In this lab, a stack pointer was already provided by lab based on MSP’s original location, in which the address of PSP was used to point to the stack. This process essentially tricks the CPU into thinking that it is resuming the thread of an already existing stack, when in reality it is running the new stack with all the expected register values inside.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,3,'2025-06-22_08-05-14','Setting up a treads stack means to store all the necessary information required onto a stack, such as the register values (R0 to R12), the PC (what function the CPU wants to run in the thread, and the xPSR, because the CPU doesn’t inherently know these values when starting or resuming a stack. This is important because if these values aren’t properly initialized, then the CPU ends up loading incorrect values into the registers and could lead to unpredictable behaviours and fatal errors. In this lab, the register values must be pushed into the stack in the correct order of xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4. A memory location was already set up in lab 1, where the PSP points to, as PSP is used in thread mode execution and is where the register values were set up for the CPU to “resume” our thread. Knowing that when a CPU pauses a thread it pushes all the register values into a stack and pops it back on the registers when returning from an interrupt, it essentially tricks the CPU into thinking that it is resuming the thread of an already existing stack by popping theses values back into the registers, when it is running a new defined stack with all the expected register values inside.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,3,'2025-06-22_23-44-08','Setting up a treads stack means to store all the necessary information required onto a stack, such as the register values (R0 to R12), the PC (what function the CPU wants to run in the thread, and the xPSR, because the CPU doesn’t inherently know these values when starting or resuming a stack. This is important because if these values aren’t properly initialized, then the CPU ends up loading incorrect values into the registers and could lead to unpredictable behaviours and fatal errors. In this lab, the register values must be pushed into the stack in the correct order of xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4. A memory location was already set up in lab 1, where the PSP points to, as PSP is used in thread mode execution and is where the register values were set up for the CPU to “resume” our thread. Knowing that when a CPU pauses a thread it pushes all the register values into a stack and pops it back on the registers when returning from an interrupt, it essentially tricks the CPU into thinking that it is resuming the thread of an already existing stack by popping theses values back into the registers, when it is running a new defined stack with all the expected register values inside.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,3,'2025-06-22_23-58-24','Setting up a treads stack means to store all the necessary information required onto a stack, such as the register values (R0 to R12), the PC (what function the CPU wants to run in the thread, and the xPSR, because the CPU doesn’t inherently know these values when starting or resuming a stack. This is important because if these values aren’t properly initialized, then the CPU ends up loading incorrect values into the registers and could lead to unpredictable behaviours and fatal errors. In this lab, the register values must be pushed into the stack in the correct order of xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4. A memory location was already set up in lab 1, where the PSP points to, as PSP is used in thread mode execution and is where the register values were set up for the CPU to “resume” our thread. Knowing that when a CPU pauses a thread it pushes all the register values into a stack and pops it back on the registers when returning from an interrupt, it essentially tricks the CPU into thinking that it is resuming the thread of an already existing stack by popping theses values back into the registers, when it is running a new defined stack with all the expected register values inside.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,3,'2025-06-23_01-13-20','Setting up a treads stack means to store all the necessary information required onto a stack, such as the register values (R0 to R12), the PC (what function the CPU wants to run in the thread, and the xPSR, because the CPU doesn’t inherently know these values when starting or resuming a stack. This is important because if these values aren’t properly initialized, then the CPU ends up loading incorrect values into the registers and could lead to unpredictable behaviours and fatal errors. In this lab, the register values must be pushed into the stack in the correct order of xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4. A memory location was already set up in lab 1, where the PSP points to, as PSP is used in thread mode execution and is where the register values were set up for the CPU to “resume” our thread. Knowing that when a CPU pauses a thread it pushes all the register values into a stack and pops it back on the registers when returning from an interrupt, it essentially tricks the CPU into thinking that it is resuming the thread of an already existing stack by popping theses values back into the registers, when it is running a new defined stack with all the expected register values inside.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,4,'2025-06-09_13-20-27','',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,4,'2025-06-09_13-30-37','The __set_PSP function 	sets PSP to the value of stackptr, a pointer to the bottom of the stack. This is important because threads use PSP to prevent overwriting the system stack which uses MSP. If this function is not set, then the CPU ends up trying to pop the register values off potential invalid or uninitialized  addresses, which cause the program to crash as the CPU cannot run the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,4,'2025-06-22_08-05-14','The __set_PSP function 	sets PSP to the value of stackptr, a pointer to the bottom of the stack. This is important because threads use PSP to prevent overwriting the system stack which uses MSP. If this function is not set, then the CPU ends up trying to pop the register values off potential invalid or uninitialized  addresses, which cause the program to crash as the CPU cannot run the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,4,'2025-06-22_23-44-08','The __set_PSP function 	sets PSP to the value of stackptr, a pointer to the bottom of the stack. This is important because threads use PSP to prevent overwriting the system stack which uses MSP. If this function is not set, then the CPU ends up trying to pop the register values off potential invalid or uninitialized  addresses, which cause the program to crash as the CPU cannot run the thread function.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,4,'2025-06-22_23-58-24','The __set_PSP function 	sets PSP to the value of stackptr, a pointer to the bottom of the thread’s stack. This is important because threads use PSP to prevent overwriting the system stack (the one that the MSP is set to point to). This ensures that each thread can have a valid, proper isolated stack space. If this function is not set, then the CPU ends up trying to pop the register values off potential invalid or uninitialized addresses, causing the program to crash as the CPU cannot run the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,4,'2025-06-23_01-13-20','The __set_PSP function 	sets PSP to the value of stackptr, a pointer to the bottom of the thread’s stack. This is important because threads use PSP to prevent overwriting the system stack (the one that the MSP is set to point to). This ensures that each thread can have a valid, proper isolated stack space. If this function is not set, then the CPU ends up trying to pop the register values off potential invalid or uninitialized addresses, causing the program to crash as the CPU cannot run the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,5,'2025-06-09_13-20-27','',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,5,'2025-06-09_13-30-37','If the thread function simply printed once and returned, then the CPU would attempt run another instruction by using the Link Register, however, without a valid return address to go back to (because the arbitrary value of 0xA isn’t a real address in LR), it will try to pop the invalid LR value leading to a potential crash at hard fault. OSes typically never run into this problem because they never return, instead they exit, run continuously, or yield control over to another thread. Our code doesn’t have a proper method of returning the thread to a valid address and so it would simply result in a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,5,'2025-06-22_08-05-14','If the thread function simply printed once and returned, then the CPU would attempt run another instruction by using the Link Register, however, without a valid return address to go back to (because the arbitrary value of 0xA isn’t a real address in LR), it will try to pop the invalid LR value leading to a potential crash at hard fault. OSes typically never run into this problem because they never return, instead they exit, run continuously, or yield control over to another thread. Our code doesn’t have a proper method of returning the thread to a valid address and so it would simply result in a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,5,'2025-06-22_23-44-08','If the thread function simply printed once and returned, then the CPU would attempt run another instruction by using the Link Register, however, without a valid return address to go back to (because the arbitrary value of 0xA isn’t a real address in LR), it will try to pop the invalid LR value leading to a potential crash at hard fault. OSes typically never run into this problem because they never return, instead they exit, run continuously, or yield control over to another thread. Our code doesn’t have a proper method of returning the thread to a valid address and so it would simply result in a crash.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',2,5,'2025-06-22_23-58-24','If the thread function simply printed once and returned, then the CPU would attempt run another instruction by using the Link Register, however, recall that a return address is stored in the thread’s stack where the function was called, which are later set into the PC. Without a valid return address to go back to (because the arbitrary value of 0xA isn’t a real address in LR), it will try to pop the invalid LR value leading to a potential crash at hard fault. OSes generally never run into this problem because they never return, instead they exit, run continuously, or yield control over to another thread. Our code doesn’t have a proper method of returning the thread to a valid address and so it would simply result in a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',2,5,'2025-06-23_01-13-20','If the thread function simply printed once and returned, then the CPU would attempt run another instruction by using the Link Register, however, recall that a return address is stored in the thread’s stack where the function was called, which are later set into the PC. Without a valid return address to go back to (because the arbitrary value of 0xA isn’t a real address in LR), it will try to pop the invalid LR value leading to a potential crash at hard fault. OSes generally never run into this problem because they never return, instead they exit, run continuously, or yield control over to another thread. Our code doesn’t have a proper method of returning the thread to a valid address and so it would simply result in a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',3,1,'2025-06-22_08-15-01','',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,1,'2025-06-24_21-54-14','To best explain how multiple *.c files are created, there are 3 main steps for a user program during the compilation process. The first step is preprocessing, which is where to the *.c files are organized into a form where it is readable for the compiler. This includes things like removing comments, expanding of the inclusion files and macros. Although this process doesn’t include header (*.h) files, it will compile those that are included by and *.c program by copying its contexts during the expansion of includes. Then, the next step involves the actual compiling and assembling of the code. This turns all the *.c files into object files, often incomplete by themselves when multiple *.c files exist, allowing it to be read into machine code that is only understood by the CPU. It should be noted that each of the *.c files undergo these first two steps separately as individual object files, where they are then put together as one single executable, which is the last step, of the compilation process, also referred to linking. This resolves all the function calls made across the different files and allows the program to execute the program in it’s entirety.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',3,1,'2025-06-25_11-34-40','To best explain how multiple *.c files are created, there are 3 main steps for a user program during the compilation process. The first step is preprocessing, which is where to the *.c files are organized into a form where it is readable for the compiler. This includes things like removing comments, expanding of the inclusion files and macros. Although this process doesn’t include header (*.h) files, it will compile those that are included by and *.c program by copying its contexts during the expansion of includes. Then, the next step involves the actual compiling and assembling of the code. This turns all the *.c files into object files, often incomplete by themselves when multiple *.c files exist, allowing it to be read into machine code that is only understood by the CPU. It should be noted that each of the *.c files undergo these first two steps separately as individual object files, where they are then put together as one single executable, which is the last step, of the compilation process, also referred to linking. This resolves all the function calls made across the different files and allows the program to execute the program in it’s entirety.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,1,'2025-06-25_18-44-37','To best explain how multiple *.c files are created, there are 3 main steps for a user program during the compilation process. The first step is preprocessing, which is where to the *.c files are organized into a form where it is readable for the compiler. This includes things like removing comments, expanding of the inclusion files and macros. For any *.h files included using #include, the preprocessor copies the entire code in the file and pastes it into the *.c file that included it. Then, the next step involves the actual compiling and assembling of the code. Compiling turns all the *.c files taken from the preprocessor into *.s machine-specific assembly code, which is just a readable form of machine code. The various assembly codes produced from this step is then turned into object (*.o) files, allowing it to be read into machine code that is only understood by the CPU. These files are often incomplete by themselves when multiple *.c files exist, which is why after each of the *.c files undergo these first two steps separately as individual object files, they are then put together as one single executable, which is the last step, of the compilation process, also referred to linking. This resolves all the function calls made across the different files and allows the program to execute the program in its entirety.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',3,2,'2025-06-22_08-15-01','',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,2,'2025-06-24_21-54-14','the #include function is a preprocessor command that copies the entire code of the specified file into the program that’s trying to include it. Using it to implement a *.c file, especially when multiple .c files exists, causes the compiler to essentially preprocess the entire implementation of the *.c program, which can be both large and irrelevant when it gets translated to machine code and combined during the linking step for the CPU to process (It’s like having duplicate recipes inside a recipe book). It also defeats the whole purpose of header files, which are files that only declare the interface, such as the constants, variables, functions etc. These files are constructed to only hold the interface definitions so that when it is called under #include, the *.c file doesn’t require the entire definitions of these functions and variables, widely reducing the amount of data the compiler needs to process.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',3,2,'2025-06-25_11-34-40','the #include function is a preprocessor command that copies the entire code of the specified file into the program that’s trying to include it. Using it to implement a *.c file, especially when multiple .c files exists, causes the compiler to essentially preprocess the entire implementation of the *.c program, which can be both large and irrelevant when it gets translated to machine code and combined during the linking step for the CPU to process (It’s like having duplicate recipes inside a recipe book). It also defeats the whole purpose of header files, which are files that only declare the interface, such as the constants, variables, functions etc. These files are constructed to only hold the interface definitions so that when it is called under #include, the *.c file doesn’t require the entire definitions of these functions and variables, widely reducing the amount of data the compiler needs to process.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',3,2,'2025-06-25_18-44-37','the #include function is a preprocessor command that copies the entire code of the specified file into the program that’s trying to include it. Using it to implement a *.c file, especially when multiple .c files exists, causes the compiler to essentially preprocess the entire implementation of the *.c program, which can be both large and irrelevant when it gets translated to machine code and combined during the linking step for the CPU to process (It’s like having duplicate recipes inside a recipe book). It also defeats the whole purpose of header files, which are files that only declare the interface, such as the constants, variables, functions etc. These files are constructed to only hold the interface definitions so that when it is called under #include, the *.c file doesn’t require the entire definitions of these functions and variables, widely reducing the amount of data the compiler needs to process.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',3,3,'2025-06-22_08-15-01','',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,3,'2025-06-24_21-54-14','To set up and start a thread, the user must follow these steps:\n1. Initialize the stack pool. The user must allocate stack space to create a location for each of the different threads’ stacks. This means that the user must know how big the stack space will be.\n2. Initialize the thread’s data structure and store metadata values. The values don’t matter as much when first being created, but they should be properly initialized before they can be implemented during code.\n3. Determine a reasonable stack size for each thread. Ideally, this value is divisible by the total stack pool so that there isn’t any wasted memory space.\n4. Create pointers to keep track of the top of the current stack, and the bottom of the entire stack pool. This is crucial to manage during execution, as it allows the stacks to be properly placed and ensures that you can check if the top current stack pointer goes lower than the address of the bottom of the stack pool.\n5. Create the thread, making sure that the stack is properly created by subtracting the address of the top current stack by the stack size and falls in the allocated memory pool. When doing so, the register values are pushed into the newly created stack for the CPU to pop later when starting the thread. It should be noted that the thread’s metadata needs to be updated for that specific thread creation as well.\n6. Start the thread. This is done by setting the PSP value to the thread’s stack pointer address value, then using assembly to request a system call to switch the CPU into thread mode to execute the thread itself\nWhen all the steps above is completed, a thread should be properly implemented.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,3,'2025-06-25_11-34-40','The user plays a big role in setting up a thread, because they define the thread’s logic. Where the kernel sets up the stack and executes the thread, the user provides the actual function of what the thread does.  To set up and start a thread, the user must follow these steps:\n1. Initialize the stack pool. Before any thread can be made, it is important that the kernel is initialized properly, and that means creating the OS’s stack in the stack pool. The user must allocate stack space to create a location for each of the different threads’ stacks. This means that the user must know how big the stack space will be.\n2. Initialize the thread’s data structure and store metadata values. It is crucial to provide values to the stack pool, current pointers, and thread data, where they should be properly initialized before they can be implemented during code. If this part is skipped, then it can result in corruption of memory.\n3. Determine a reasonable stack size for each thread. Ideally, this value is divisible by the total stack pool so that there isn’t any wasted memory space.\n4. Create pointers to keep track of the top of the current stack, and the bottom of the entire stack pool. This is crucial to manage during execution, as it allows the stacks to be properly placed and ensures that you can check if the top current stack pointer goes lower than the address of the bottom of the stack pool.\n5. Create the thread, making sure that the stack is properly created by subtracting the address of the top current stack by the stack size and falls in the allocated memory pool. When doing so, the register values are pushed into the newly created stack for the CPU to pop later when starting the thread. It should be noted that the thread’s metadata needs to be updated for that specific thread creation as well. This is not manually creating our thread; as doing so doesn’t make it executable. Only the kernel can prepare and manage the insertion of register values into the stack.\n6. Start the thread. This is done by setting the PSP value to the thread’s stack pointer address value, then using assembly to request a system call to switch the CPU into thread mode to execute the thread itself. This is critical because not starting a thread means all the effort to create and define the thread and the thread’s stack goes to waste if we never call it. Not starting the kernel means no thread runs.\nWhen all the steps above is completed, a thread should be properly implemented.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,3,'2025-06-25_18-44-37','The user plays a big role in setting up a thread, because they are the ones that provide the function details that the thread implements. Where the kernel sets up the stack and executes the thread, the user is the one that writes the function and informs the kernel to place it inside the PC register of the thread’s stack.  To set up and start a thread, the user must implement three different functions that request the OS to perform in kernel: one that initializes the kernel’s global variables and any stack information, another that creates the actual thread’s stack and stores the values in it’s registers, and one that runs the actual thread by calling it in assembly. Below are steps to ensure that these functions are created properly:\n1. Initialize the kernel global variables and stack info: Before any thread can be made, it is important that the kernel is initialized properly, and that means creating the OS’s stack in the stack pool and the corresponding global variables and pointers that are required to keep track of each thread’s stack location. This is done by setting a global pointer variable to MSP, initializing the first stack address at the very top of the stack pool. The user must also define how much total stack space would be allocated.\n2. Initialize a global thread structure with two parameters: a pointer that indicates a stack pointer address, and a function pointer that points to the address of the function that the user provided.\n4. When creating the function call that creates the thread, a stack must first be allocated into the stack pool. This means subtracting the pointer variable to the current address of the top of the last stack by the stack size. It’s important to compare this value to the bottom address of the stack’s pointer to ensure that a stack isn’t being create outside the range of the stack pool.\n5.Creating the thread itself means that register values must be set into a valid stack via a function call. These register values should be pushed into the newly created stack for the CPU to pop later when starting the thread. It should be noted that the thread’s metadata needs to be updated for that specific thread creation. Calling this function is not equivalent to manually creating our thread, however, as doing so doesn’t make it executable. Only the kernel can prepare and manage the insertion of register values into the stack which is why these all the other two functions are under the kernel file.\n6. Finally, the function call to start the thread must be implemented. This is done by using assembly to request a system call to switch the CPU into thread mode to execute the thread itself. This brings us to the SVC Handler, which identifies the type of system call, in this case would be to resume a thread, and sets the PSP value to the thread’s stack pointer address value before running. This is critical because to not request the OS to starting a thread after setting it in the stack means all the effort to create and define the thread goes to waste if never called.\nWhen all the steps above is completed, a thread should be properly implemented.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',3,4,'2025-06-22_08-15-01','',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,4,'2025-06-24_21-54-14','The stack pool is a block of memory that is specifically allocated as a reserved space that holds the stacks corresponding to different threads. It’s important that a stack pool exists because it avoids the need to dynamically allocate stack memory, as it is predefined before runtime. In the lab, the stack pool was constantly checked each time a new thread’s stack would be created to see if it there was enough allocated stack pool space to create a new thread. This was done by subtracting the address of the last created stack by a chosen stack size, and checks if the address of the top of the new stack is lower (as stacks grow downward) than the address of the bottom of the stack and returns NULL when it is. This idea can be used in a future lab when dealing with multiple threads, because each time a new thread needs to be created, all that needs to happen is to create a new thread’s stack inside the stack pool, granted that there is enough allocated space left in said pool.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,4,'2025-06-25_11-34-40','The stack pool is a block of memory that is specifically allocated as a reserved space that holds the stacks corresponding to different threads. It’s important that a stack pool exists because it avoids the need to dynamically allocate stack memory, as it is predefined before runtime. There are two main stacks that can be found in the stack pool: the OS stack, and as many thread stacks needed from the user. The OS stack appears at the very top of the stack pool, set by the MSP pointer, and its purpose is to handle all the kernel-based operations, which include running the various OS functions and system calls that are called from the program. For thread stacks, the stack pool was constantly checked each time a new thread’s stack would be created to see if it there was enough allocated stack pool space to create a new thread. This was done by subtracting the address of the last created stack by a chosen stack size, and checks if the address of the top of the new stack is lower (as stacks grow downward) than the address of the bottom of the stack and returns NULL when it is. This idea can be used in a future lab when dealing with multiple threads, because each time a new thread needs to be created, all that needs to happen is to create a new thread’s stack inside the stack pool, granted that there is enough allocated space left in said pool.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',3,4,'2025-06-25_18-44-37','The stack pool is a block of memory that is specifically allocated as a reserved space that holds the stacks corresponding to different threads. It’s important that a stack pool exists because it avoids the need to dynamically allocate stack memory, as it is predefined before runtime. There are two main stacks that can be found in the stack pool: the OS stack, and as many thread stacks needed from the user. The OS stack appears at the very top of the stack pool, set by the MSP pointer, and its purpose is to handle all the kernel-based operations, which include running the various OS functions and system calls that are called from the program. For thread stacks, the stack pool was constantly checked each time a new thread’s stack would be created to see if it there was enough allocated stack pool space to create a new thread. This was done by subtracting the address of the last created stack by a chosen stack size, and checks if the address of the top of the new stack is lower (as stacks grow downward) than the address of the bottom of the stack and returns NULL when it is. This idea can be used in a future lab when dealing with multiple threads, because each time a new thread needs to be created, all that needs to happen is to create a new thread’s stack inside the stack pool, granted that there is enough allocated space left in said pool.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',3,5,'2025-06-22_08-15-01','',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,5,'2025-06-24_21-54-14','In our lab, examples of “kernel” functions included that of the SVC Main Handler and the implemented thread functions, while “user” functions were those such as print_continuously() function. The main difference between the two types relate closely to how the computer switches between user and kernel mode when a system call is executed. Kernel functions directly manipulate hardware components, like setting register values, allocating stack memory, or creating threads. These functions have access to privileged data that should not be given access to just any user, hence why all the code that deal with this type of data is stored into the kernel.c file. On the other hand, User functions are those that are created to execute user-specific tasks, or request to the kernel via assembly system calls. Thread functions are considered user functions for this reason, as they require permission from the OS, which would go to kernel mode to, as an example, manipulate a stack when creating a thread. This is why its important to separate these files into a main and kernel file, in order to manage what the user can have access to.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,5,'2025-06-25_11-34-40','In our lab, examples of “kernel” functions included that of the SVC Main Handler and the implemented thread functions, while “user” functions were those such as print_continuously() function. The main difference between the two types relate closely to how the computer switches between user and kernel mode when a system call is executed. Kernel functions directly manipulate hardware components, like setting register values, allocating stack memory, or creating threads. These functions have access to privileged data that should not be given access to just any user, hence why all the code that deal with this type of data is stored into the kernel.c file. On the other hand, the user writes application-level functions such as print_continuously() or print_indefinetly() that define what a thread should do, in the main.c file. These are kernel managed files, but are user defined, and thus do not go in the kernel.c file. The distinction between the two file is important for the user because it promotes code safety from unwanted user access on hardware or memory and prevents bugs in user from corrupting kernel memory or threads. For a programmer’s perspective, it makes the code more organized and easier to interpret as opposed to having everything in 1 c file.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',3,5,'2025-06-25_18-44-37','In our lab, examples of “kernel” functions included that of the SVC Main Handler and the implemented thread functions, while “user” functions were those that the user implements, written outside the kernel, such as print_continuously() function. The main difference between the two types relate closely to how the computer switches between user and kernel mode when a system call is executed. Kernel functions directly manipulate hardware components, like setting register values, allocating stack memory, or creating threads. These functions have access to privileged data that should not be given access to just any user, hence why all the code that deal with this type of data is stored into the kernel.c file. On the other hand, the user writes functions that are defined outside of the kernel, that is, they are thread functions that define what the thread does, and user is responsible for putting these in the thread’s PC register in the main.c file. These are kernel managed files, but are user defined, and thus do not go in the kernel.c file because they are unprivileged and do not access hardware.  The distinction between the two file is important because it makes it easier for users to implement code logic without having to allocate stacks, which are all defined as function calls that the user can simply implement into the main code. Furthermore, by isolating all the kernel functions to the user ones, it minimizes the chance of overwriting memory and protects it from unwanted user access or bugs on hardware or memory as the user simply focuses on the main file. Otherwise, any small error in the kernel could cause hard faults and crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,1,'2025-07-20_20-11-37','Round-robin scheduling works very well with queues, especially with the circular queue method because both methods have a concept of cycling through each of the elements and looping back to the beginning once it reaches the last element. In this lab, round robin scheduling gives all threads an equal amount of runtime, and whenever the last thread is run, it would loop back the first thread created. Circular queues implement well with this idea as a TBC would be dequeued to run from its thread, then it would be enqueued back into the rear end of the queue, then repeats for the next thread, now positioned at the front end of the queue. This method is more beneficial to implement than just a regular array, one of such regions are because arrays have fixed sizes that restrict the number threads available in the round robin method as opposed to the dynamically changeable size of a queue. Furthermore, queues remove the need to manually check the index location to see if it is at the end of a queue to look back or to check if there are extra/empty array slots. Queues are also based on a FIFO algorithm that matches exactly what round robin scheduling does.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',4,1,'2025-07-20_22-01-13','Round-robin scheduling works very well with queues, especially with the circular queue method because both methods have a concept of cycling through each of the elements and looping back to the beginning once it reaches the last element. In this lab, round robin scheduling gives all threads an equal amount of runtime, and whenever the last thread is run, it would loop back the first thread created. Circular queues implement well with this idea as a TBC would be dequeued to run from its thread, then it would be enqueued back into the rear end of the queue, then repeats for the next thread, now positioned at the front end of the queue. This method is more beneficial to implement than just a regular array, one of such regions are because arrays have fixed sizes that restrict the number threads available in the round robin method as opposed to the dynamically changeable size of a queue. Furthermore, queues remove the need to manually check the index location to see if it is at the end of a queue to look back or to check if there are extra/empty array slots. Queues are also based on a FIFO algorithm that matches exactly what round robin scheduling does. So, in hindsight, robin round scheduling is already implementing a queue-based scheduler, it’s just applied with an array and an algorithm that resets the array back to the start once it reaches the end. It could be said that the implementation of a queue-based scheduler might cause some overhead such as added complexity, but it does have a benefit of making it much easier to dynamically add or remove threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,1,'2025-07-21_00-50-54','Round-robin scheduling works very well with queues, especially with the circular queue method because both methods have a concept of cycling through each of the elements and looping back to the beginning once it reaches the last element. In this lab, round robin scheduling gives all threads an equal amount of runtime, and whenever the last thread is run, it would loop back the first thread created. Circular queues implement well with this idea as a time control block would be dequeued to run from its thread, then it would be enqueued back into the rear end of the queue, then repeats for the next thread, now positioned at the front end of the queue. This method is more beneficial to implement than just a regular array, one of such regions are because arrays have fixed sizes that restrict the number threads available in the round robin method as opposed to the dynamically changeable size of a queue. Furthermore, queues remove the need to manually check the index location to see if it is at the end of a queue to look back or to check if there are extra/empty array slots. Queues are also based on a FIFO algorithm that matches exactly what round robin scheduling does. So, in hindsight, robin round scheduling is already implementing a queue-based scheduler, it’s just applied with an array and an algorithm that resets the array back to the start once it reaches the end. It could be said that the implementation of a queue-based scheduler might cause some overhead such as added complexity, but it does have a benefit of making it much easier to dynamically add or remove threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,1,'2025-07-21_02-25-17','Round-robin scheduling works very well with queues, especially with the circular queue method because both methods have a concept of cycling through each of the elements and looping back to the beginning once it reaches the last element. In this lab, round robin scheduling gives all threads an equal amount of runtime, and whenever the last thread is run, it would loop back the first thread created. Circular queues implement well with this idea as a time control block would be dequeued to run from its thread, then it would be enqueued back into the rear end of the queue, then repeats for the next thread, now positioned at the front end of the queue. This method is more beneficial to implement than just a regular array, one of such regions are because arrays have fixed sizes that restrict the number threads available in the round robin method as opposed to the dynamically changeable size of a queue. Furthermore, queues remove the need to manually check the index location to see if it is at the end of a queue to look back or to check if there are extra/empty array slots. Queues are also based on a FIFO algorithm that matches exactly what round robin scheduling does. So, in hindsight, robin round scheduling is already implementing a queue-based scheduler, it’s just applied with an array and an algorithm that resets the array back to the start once it reaches the end. It could be said that the implementation of a queue-based scheduler might cause some overhead such as added complexity, but it does have a benefit of making it much easier to dynamically add or remove threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,2,'2025-07-20_20-11-37','The PendSV_Handler is a low priority interrupt that is responsible for the switching of one thread to another, where in this lab it is mainly called through the osYield wrapper function. When executing a context switch, the function doesn’t technically return; without a return statement the variables in the stack cannot be removed and causes the memory to fill up. The program needs to be able to remove these values off the main stack and store it onto the current thread’s stack for later use. This is what the PendSV_Handler does; it saves the values onto the current thread’s stack, Updates the stack pointer in the TCB, used osSched() to update the thread that needs to be run, setting the PSP value to that thread’s stack pointer, and pops the register values of the next thread. It was important that the PendSV_Handler takes lower priority than the SVC handler, because otherwise the PendSV would execute immediately and prevent SVC from performing the thread’s function.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',4,2,'2025-07-20_22-01-13','The PendSV_Handler is a low priority interrupt that is responsible for the switching of one thread to another, where in this lab it is mainly called through the osYield() wrapper functison. When executing a context switch, the function doesn’t technically return; and without a return statement the register values XPSR, PC, R3-R0, and R4-R11, in the stack cannot be removed, causes the memory to fill up as more threads without a return statement are called. The program needs to be able to remove these values off the main stack, and set PSP’s pointer value to the next thread, so that the OS can then store said register values onto the new current thread’s stack. This is what the PendSV_Handler does; once the osYield() function is called from the thread, it process to saves the values onto the current thread’s stack, Updates PSP in the TCB, uses osSched() to update the thread that needs to be run, setting the PSP value to that thread’s stack pointer, and pops the register values of the next thread. It was important that the PendSV_Handler takes lower priority than the SVC handler, because otherwise the PendSV would execute immediately and prevent SVC from performing the thread’s function.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',4,2,'2025-07-21_00-50-54','The PendSV_Handler is a low priority interrupt that is responsible for the switching of one thread to another, where in this lab it is mainly called through the osYield() wrapper function. When executing a context switch, the function doesn’t technically return; and without a return statement the register values from R4-R11, that are manually saved in the PendSV (The other registers are automatically pushed by the hardware), in the stack cannot be removed, causes the memory to fill up as more threads without a return statement are called. This is why the PendSV_Handler exists; it’s purpose is to manage the switching of the threads either when called by osYield or when another interrupt calls it after completion (aka the SysTick interrupt for the lab). PendSV works by first being called from a thread with osYield() or through the timer interrupt, in which it first retrieves the current value of PSP and stores it into in the thread control block, then, osSched() updates and switches the thread that needs to be run, so that the PSP value to that thread’s stack pointer can be set to the new thread. The registers R4–R11 are then restored from the next thread’s stack. It was important that the PendSV_Handler takes lower priority than the SVC handler, because otherwise the PendSV would execute immediately and prevent SVC from performing the thread’s function.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',4,2,'2025-07-21_02-25-17','The PendSV function is a low priority interrupt in which its purpose is to save the context of one thread and load another thread state, allowing the CPU to “switch” between two different threads. PendSV’s procedure to switch threads starts with manually saving the registers of R4 to R11 onto the current stack thread.  The other 8 registers XPSR, PC, LR, R12, and R0 to R3 are all automatically pushed into the stack by the hardware, and so the PendSV function does not need to deal with those register values. Then, the interrupt saves the new value of PSP into to the thread control block. This is important as the OS needs to know where the next thread’s location is before actually storing the register values, otherwise it could lead to the hardware attempting to store values at invalid addresses which would cause faults. One PSP’s new value is stored, the function osSched() is called, which actually finds the next thread that will be used. PSP is then set to the new thread’s stack pointer, and then finally manually stores the values of R4 to R11.  It was important that the PendSV takes lower priority than the SVC handler, because otherwise the PendSV would execute immediately and prevent SVC from performing the thread’s function.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,3,'2025-07-20_20-11-37','Introducing a high priority system that always chooses the task with highest is not a good idea because it may lead to situations where tasks with a low priority number may never run. Tasks are created with the intention of it being used, thus, if an algorithm prevents certain tasks from every running due to there always being a higher priority task, then it may cause the system to either crash or lose some of its intended functionality.  This was why robin-round scheduling was utilized for this lab: it ensures that every task, even if it has minimal impact, equally has a chance to run, ensuring that no thread is ignored.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,3,'2025-07-20_22-01-13','Introducing a high priority system that always chooses the task with highest is not a good idea because it may lead to situations where tasks with a low priority number may never run. Tasks are created with the intention of it being used, thus, if an algorithm prevents certain tasks from every running due to there always being a higher priority task, then it may cause the system to either crash or lose some of its intended functionality.  This was why robin-round scheduling was utilized for this lab: it ensures that every task, even if it has minimal impact, equally has a chance to run, ensuring that no thread is ignored.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',4,3,'2025-07-21_00-50-54','Tasks are created with the intention of it being used. Introducing a high priority system that always chooses the task with highest is not a good idea because then only the highest-priority task would ever run, preventing lower tasks from ever getting any runtime. Unless there is a separate set of code that handles the tasks to change priorities during runtime, one task will end up being prioritized indefinitely and will always keep running, leaving all the others unused, also known as starvation. The prevention of  certain tasks from ever running due because of the priority system cab lead to either crashes or a loss of  intended functionality within a program.  This was why robin-round scheduling was utilized for this lab: it ensures that every task, even if it has minimal impact, equally has a chance to run, ensuring that no thread is ignored.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,3,'2025-07-21_02-25-17','Tasks are created with the intention of it being used. Introducing a high priority system that always chooses the task with highest is not a good idea because then only the highest-priority task would ever run, preventing lower tasks from ever getting any runtime. Unless there is a separate set of code that handles the tasks to change priorities during runtime, one task will end up being prioritized indefinitely and will always keep running, leaving all the others unused, also known as starvation. The prevention of  certain tasks from ever running due because of the priority system cab lead to either crashes or a loss of  intended functionality within a program.  This was why robin-round scheduling was utilized for this lab: it ensures that every task, even if it has minimal impact, equally has a chance to run, ensuring that no thread is ignored.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,4,'2025-07-20_20-11-37','Utilizing C functions instead of assembly is best practiced when there is an extensive amount of logic behind the function’s purpose, or in other words, when a function requires loops, arrays, or conditions, it is much easier to implement in C rather than assembly. Furthermore, C is much easier to interpret logically and understand to the general user, and typically are much easier to read and modify. C is also a code language that can be reused for many different processors, to which assembly code cannot do because It is processor specific. However, c functions are not as precise as assembly code in terms of having precise control over the CPU: in other words, assembly functions directly deal with hardware and thus are more advantageous when requiring direct control over the CPU and the registers. Futhermore, when dealing with interrupt handlers, where timing and stack usage must be exact, assembly is much more suitable to use thatn C functions because it has direct control over it. Finally, Wwriting C code inside interrupt handlers can be risky if the compiler generates code that uses inappropriate instructions or relies on assumptions not valid in that context.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,4,'2025-07-20_22-01-13','Utilizing C functions instead of assembly is best practiced when there is an extensive amount of logic behind the function’s purpose, or in other words, when a function requires loops, arrays, or conditions, it is much easier to implement in C rather than assembly. Furthermore, C is much easier to interpret logically and understand to the general user, and typically are much easier to read and modify. C is also a code language that can be reused for many different processors, to which assembly code cannot do because it is processor specific. However, c functions are not as precise as assembly code in terms of having precise control over the CPU: in other words, assembly functions directly deal with hardware and thus are more advantageous when requiring direct control over the CPU and the registers. Futhermore, when dealing with interrupt handlers, where timing and stack usage must be exact, assembly is much more suitable to use than C functions because it has direct control over it. Finally, Writing C code inside interrupt handlers can be risky if the compiler generates code that uses inappropriate instructions or relies on assumptions not valid in that context.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,4,'2025-07-21_00-50-54','Utilizing C functions instead of assembly is best practiced when there is an extensive amount of logic behind the function’s purpose, or in other words, when a function requires loops, arrays, or conditions, it is much easier to implement in C rather than assembly. Furthermore, C is much easier to interpret logically and understand to the general user, and typically are much easier to read and modify. C is also a code language that can be reused for many different processors, to which assembly code cannot do because it is processor specific. However, c functions are not as precise as assembly code in terms of having precise control over the CPU: in other words, assembly functions directly deal with hardware and thus are more advantageous when requiring direct control over the CPU and the registers. Futhermore, when dealing with interrupt handlers, where timing and stack usage must be exact, assembly is much more suitable to use than C functions because it has direct control over it. Finally, Writing C code inside interrupt handlers can be risky if the compiler generates code that uses inappropriate instructions or relies on assumptions not valid in that context.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,4,'2025-07-21_02-25-17','Utilizing C functions instead of assembly is best practiced when there is an extensive amount of logic behind the function’s purpose, or in other words, when a function requires loops, arrays, or conditions, it is much easier to implement in C rather than assembly. Furthermore, C is much easier to interpret logically and understand to the general user, and typically are much easier to read and modify. C is also a code language that can be reused for many different processors, to which assembly code cannot do because it is processor specific. However, c functions are not as precise as assembly code in terms of having precise control over the CPU: in other words, assembly functions directly deal with hardware and thus are more advantageous when requiring direct control over the CPU and the registers. Futhermore, when dealing with interrupt handlers, where timing and stack usage must be exact, assembly is much more suitable to use than C functions because it has direct control over it. Finally, Writing C code inside interrupt handlers can be risky if the compiler generates code that uses inappropriate instructions or relies on assumptions not valid in that context.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,5,'2025-07-20_20-11-37','The program would still run as intended, but there would a lot of unnecessary thread “switching” given that the only thread that it can switch to would be the single thread that is being run. Explaining the sequence of events with the print_continuously function, the thread would run and print out a message, then call osYield(), triggering the PendSV interrupt to perform a context switch, storing the register values onto the current stack pointer. At this point, osSched() would be called to switch to the next thread, however with only one thread available the next thread ends up being the print_continously function again. The register values would then be restored onto the stack so that the thread would perform. The context switch in this case was unnecessary and only slows the runtime of the program when compared to just forever looping the print_continuously function. An analogy that represents this behaviour would be to open the door multiple times over and over again to go in; it’s redundant and in the end the same outcome would be achieved.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,5,'2025-07-20_22-01-13','The program would still run as intended, but there would a lot of unnecessary thread “switching” given that the only thread that it can switch to would be the single thread that is being run. Explaining the sequence of events with the print_continuously function, the thread would run and print out a message, then call osYield(), triggering the PendSV interrupt to perform a context switch, storing the register values onto the current stack pointer. At this point, osSched() would be called to switch to the next thread, however with only one thread available the next thread ends up being the print_continously function again. The register values would then be restored onto the stack so that the thread would perform. The context switch in this case was unnecessary and only slows the runtime of the program when compared to just forever looping the print_continuously function. An analogy that represents this behaviour would be to open the door multiple times over and over again to go in; it’s redundant and in the end the same outcome would be achieved.\n',0,'s25'),('j254kim',4,5,'2025-07-21_00-50-54','The program would still run as intended, but there would a lot of unnecessary thread “switching” given that the only thread that it can switch to would be the single thread that is being run. Explaining the sequence of events with the print_continuously function, the thread would run and print out a message, then call osYield(), triggering the PendSV interrupt to perform a context switch, storing the register values onto the current stack pointer. At this point, osSched() would be called to switch to the next thread, however with only one thread available the next thread ends up being the print_continously function again. The register values would then be restored onto the stack so that the thread would perform. This indicates that while there is a lot of redundant context switching, the OS is still working as intended, and in the grand scheme of things the program will output what is expected, however, this does not omit the downsides that context switch causes; in this case it would make a good portion of the program code unnecessary and only slows the runtime of the program when compared to just forever looping the print_continuously function. An analogy that represents this behaviour would be to open the door multiple times repeatedly to go in; it’s redundant and in the end the same outcome would be achieved.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',4,5,'2025-07-21_02-25-17','The program would still run as intended, but there would a lot of unnecessary thread “switching” given that the only thread that it can switch to would be the single thread that is being run. Explaining the sequence of events with the print_continuously function, the thread would run and print out a message, then call osYield(), triggering the PendSV interrupt to perform a context switch, storing the register values onto the current stack pointer. At this point, osSched() would be called to switch to the next thread, however with only one thread available the next thread ends up being the print_continously function again. The register values would then be restored onto the stack so that the thread would perform. This indicates that while there is a lot of redundant context switching, the OS is still working as intended, and in the grand scheme of things the program will output what is expected, however, this does not omit the downsides that context switch causes; in this case it would make a good portion of the program code unnecessary and only slows the runtime of the program when compared to just forever looping the print_continuously function. An analogy that represents this behaviour would be to open the door multiple times repeatedly to go in; it’s redundant and in the end the same outcome would be achieved.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',5,1,'2025-07-20_20-18-14','The process by which an argument gets passed into a thread starts with the user defining a thread function with a parameter. In this lab, as the user may prepare any type of pointer, every thread function would only accept a single void pointer, which would then be turned into an integer or struct* function to be entirely stored in R0. This was done because in a bigger setting as OS designers it is not possible to predict what the user needs, and this simulates that idea by only copying and storage the data of whatever type the parameter was, not the pointer, into a valid type for computation. Once the argument is prepared, a thread function provided, such as osCreateThread() in the lab, would be called by the user to register the thread pointer argument onto the stack. For the OS, once this function was called, it must allocate a new stack and manually restore the register values into the stack to “resume” the thread context. This includes properly storing the pointer to the argument into R0. The reason why this works is because when the thread is properly setup and is started by the OS, it will pop all the values onto the processor’s register, including the PC which holds the function pointer and R0 that holds the manually set argument. Thus, the OS would execute the function in PC using the value of R0 as the function’s argument.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',5,2,'2025-07-20_20-18-14','osYield() is a wrapper function that, when called, gives up control of the CPU and triggers the PendSV interrupt. This causes a context switch to occur after the current interrupt is finished; therefore, the osYield() function was created to only be called from threads. By definition, calling osYield() inside any of the context switching functions causes the program to trigger another PendSV in the middle of an exception handler, in other words, an interrupt would be called while an interrupt would be running. Nested interrupts are bad because they use the same stack; context-switching mid-handler makes the system unable to recover from the correct register state and could end up causing corruption and stack overflows. Furthermore, calling an interrupt inside an exception handler breaks the purpose of the PendSV interrupt, which aims to separate thread-level and OS-level operations. Introducing osYield into these handles violates the design idea that the PendSV waits for an interrupt to finish before execution.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',5,3,'2025-07-20_20-18-14','When the fundamental OS clock was set up to be 1ms, the SysTick timer sends an interrupt every 1 millisecond, decrementing the runtime of the current thread every time, resetting to the timeslice and triggers the PendSV  function for a context switch everytime it reaches 0. Changing this time to 0.1 ms causes these interrupts to occur 10 times faster; an immediate benefit being that it massively speeds up the thread runtime and enables threads to be switched with sub-millisecond precision. Furthermore, any desired timeouts, delays, or delays for periodic tasks are performed much faster due to more frequent interrupts. However, this comes at a cost of a higher load onto the CPU: for instance, increasing the frequency of such interrupts causes the CPU to spend more time servicing the tick interrupt and context switching, reducing the time left for actual thread work. This leads to more power consumption, power efficiency, and more work on the scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',5,4,'2025-07-20_20-18-14','Multitasking for Oses are a useful feature that allows processes and tasks to be processed in parallel, often manipulating/controlling how activities interact with the CPU. From the user’s perspective, pre-emptive multitasking is one of the two types of multitasking where the threads are automatically interrupted by the OS, even if a thread was never set to yield (Such as using an infinite loop). This ensures that all threads have a chance to run despite the chance of some threads not having a way to yield on its own. In this lab, the OS uses the SysTick Timer interrupt to periodically schedule a context switch, where the entire thread’s context must be safely stored before being able to move to the next thread. On the other hand, in co-operative multitasking, the thread switches may only occur through a voluntary yield control; the OS does not initiate the context switching from the running process to another process in this method. In this lab, co-operative multitasking was done by calling the osYield wrapper function, which calls the PendSV handler to manually change the threads without ever touching the timer interrupt. It’s simpler to implement because it doesn’t require the time interrupt to automatically force a switch; For the OS, the switch will only happen when a yield occurs. It is important to know that regardless of which method is used to handle the context switch, because the PendSV file is what’s responsible for actually changing threads that only runs h after all higher-priority interrupts finish, preventing switch context from occurring inside interrupts.\n',1,'s25'),('j254kim',5,5,'2025-07-20_20-18-14','While the two types of multitasking are fundamentally different in how they handle context switches, they are able to work together precisely because they rely on different, independent mechanisms to trigger a thread switch. One of the most crucial concepts that allow coexistence of the two methods is that triggering a yield manually (using osYield) does not affect the runtime of the timer interrupt (the SysTick handler) in any way. If a thread wants to yield early, the PendSV handler will switch the thread regardless of what the runtime of SysTick was. If a thread has no definitive method of yielding, the SysTick handler will still eventually force a context switch for the thread once the runtime reaches 0. It’s important to understand that both methods still end up calling the same PendSV handler, which is a low priority interrupt meaning it will properly initiate the context switch regardless of if it was manually called in or if it was called by the timer interrupt. As such, this allows a system to support both long-running threads (pre-emptive multitasking) and shorter threads with a definitive yield (co-operatuve multitasking), and allow them to coexist without errors.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',1,1,'2025-05-07_14-34-32','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer, and translates from a common language/functions (such as C/C++) to microcontroller-specific operations. This is useful because it allows the user to use pre-existing and general coding knowledge to program many different microcontrollers, rather than having to know the specific workings of each one. Additionally, manufacturer-provided HALs (such as the ones provided by ST) are likely more robust than user-made solutions to interact with the microcontroller.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',1,1,'2025-05-07_14-47-24','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer, and translates from a common language/functions (such as C/C++) to microcontroller-specific operations. This is useful because it allows the user to use pre-existing and general coding knowledge to program many different microcontrollers, rather than having to know the specific workings of each one. Additionally, manufacturer-provided HALs (such as the ones provided by ST) are likely more robust than user-made solutions to interact with the microcontroller.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',1,2,'2025-05-07_14-34-32','The stdio library does not have specific details on how to interact with microcontroller hardware, so it needs helper functions to direct its IO to the correct hardware channels. __io_putchar is one such function which helps printf print to UART2 by printing individual characters to UART2, then returning the character to printf.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',1,2,'2025-05-07_14-47-24','The stdio library does not have specific details on how to interact with microcontroller hardware, so it needs helper functions to direct its IO to the correct hardware channels. __io_putchar is one such function which helps printf print to UART2 by printing individual characters to UART2, then returning the character to printf.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',1,3,'2025-05-07_14-34-32','We needed to view the values in the register as well such as MSP and PSP values, which is not accessible using only printf. To verify that these values were set correctly, we can’t depend just on our calculated variables such as PSP_val, since the values in register are not guaranteed to be the same. The debugger also allows us to see values at a specific point in time.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',1,3,'2025-05-07_14-47-24','We needed to view the values in the register as well such as MSP and PSP values, which is not accessible using only printf. To verify that these values were set correctly, we can’t depend just on our calculated variables such as PSP_val, since the values in register are not guaranteed to be the same. The debugger also allows us to see values at a specific point in time.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',1,4,'2025-05-07_14-34-32','Since we wanted MSP_init_val to be 0x400 bytes in size, and the stack is treated as a single int8 array (i.e. each element is 1 byte), then the start of PSP should be exactly a distance of 0x400 bytes away. Since the stack goes from high to low, the start pointer of PSP should be MSP_init_val – 0x400. The initial location of MSP needs to be known since it is not fixed, so PSP also has to be determined based on the MSP value of each run.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',1,4,'2025-05-07_14-47-24','Since we wanted MSP_init_val to be 0x400 bytes in size, and the stack is treated as a single int8 array (i.e. each element is 1 byte), then the start of PSP should be exactly a distance of 0x400 bytes away. Since the stack goes from high to low, the start pointer of PSP should be MSP_init_val – 0x400. The initial location of MSP needs to be known since it is not fixed, so PSP also has to be determined based on the MSP value of each run.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',1,5,'2025-05-07_14-34-32','Since the stack for each thread will be the same size, we can take the PSP, and subtract the stack size of each thread (in bytes) to get the starting addresses for each thread’s stack.\n',0,'s25'),('j34ye',1,5,'2025-05-07_14-47-24','To allocate multiple stacks for multiple threads, we could start with a fixed base address for this group of stacks. Suppose the stack size of each thread is stackSize. The stack address for the first thread would be the same as the fixed base address, the second would be this address + stackSize, the third would be this address + 2 * stackSize, and so on. This gives each thread a distinct and non-overlapping stack address. The validity of these assignments (if it is properly aligned and within the allocated memory region) can be checked by computing the total stack size, which is the OS stack size + stackSize * N, where N is the number of threads known at compile-time.\nSince the stack for each thread will be the same size, we can take the PSP, and subtract the stack size of each thread (in bytes) to get the starting addresses for each thread’s stack.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',2,1,'2025-05-28_13-05-03','By manually assigning the program counter (PC) to the address of the print_continuously function using assembly (MOV PC, R0), the processor immediately began executing the code at that memory location. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that prints to the console, the system began printing repeatedly and never returned to the original program flow. This experiment demonstrated how controlling the PC register can dictate what code the processor executes next — a foundational concept for context switching in operating systems.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',2,1,'2025-05-28_18-02-01','By manually assigning the program counter (PC) to the address of the print_continuously function using assembly (MOV PC, R0), the processor immediately began executing the code at that memory location. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that prints to the console, the system began printing repeatedly and never returned to the original program flow. This experiment demonstrated how controlling the PC register can dictate what code the processor executes next — a foundational concept for context switching in operating systems.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',2,2,'2025-05-28_13-05-03','A system call is a way for user-level code to request services from the operating system. In this lab, we triggered a system call using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction. When the processor executes SVC, it switches to privileged mode and jumps to a specific handler (SVC_Handler). From there, control is passed to a C function (SVC_Handler_Main) that checks the service number and performs the corresponding action. System calls are essential for abstracting hardware operations and enforcing protection between user and system code.\n',0,'s25'),('j34ye',2,2,'2025-05-28_18-02-01','A system call is a special kind of function call that allows a user program to request services from the operating system. It works by invoking the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction, which triggers a controlled switch from unprivileged user mode to privileged system mode. This transition allows the operating system to safely perform tasks—such as I/O operations or managing memory—that regular user code is not allowed to do directly.\nSystem calls are essential for protecting the system, especially hardware resources, from untrustworthy or faulty user code. By requiring all hardware access to go through the operating system via system calls, we ensure that only verified, safe operations are executed. This prevents user applications from corrupting memory or misusing hardware, preserving the integrity and stability of the entire system.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',2,3,'2025-05-28_13-05-03','Setting up a thread’s stack means pre-loading the stack with values that simulate what would be present if the thread had started running normally. This includes register values like xPSR, PC (pointing to the thread function), LR, and general-purpose registers (R0–R12). We push these onto the stack in a specific order because, when the context switch occurs, the processor will pop these values off in reverse order to restore the CPU state. This setup tricks the processor into \"resuming\" a thread that, in reality, has never been run before. Storing register values on the stack is crucial for saving the execution context, allowing the thread to run independently and resume correctly after interrupts.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',2,3,'2025-05-28_18-02-01','Setting up a thread’s stack means pre-loading the stack with values that simulate what would be present if the thread had started running normally. This includes register values like xPSR, PC (pointing to the thread function), LR, and general-purpose registers (R0–R12). We push these onto the stack in a specific order because, when the context switch occurs, the processor will pop these values off in reverse order to restore the CPU state. This setup tricks the processor into \"resuming\" a thread that, in reality, has never been run before. Storing register values on the stack is crucial for saving the execution context, allowing the thread to run independently and resume correctly after interrupts.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',2,4,'2025-05-28_13-05-03','The processor uses the current stack pointer to determine where to store and retrieve values during execution. If we don’t set the PSP to point to the preconfigured thread stack (stackptr), the processor will attempt to pop register values from an invalid or unexpected location. This causes a hard fault because it accesses memory it shouldn\'t or fails to find critical information like the program counter. By calling __set_PSP, we ensure that the correct stack (containing the prepared context) is used, allowing the thread to start properly and safely.\n',0,'s25'),('j34ye',2,4,'2025-05-28_18-02-01','The processor uses either the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) or the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to determine where to read and write values during execution. By default, the CPU uses MSP, but when running threads in thread mode, we switch to using PSP. This separation is critical: the MSP is used for system-level operations, while the PSP is reserved for user threads.\nIf we don\'t explicitly set the PSP to point to the correct thread stack before running the thread, the CPU will attempt to restore the thread’s context (registers like PC, LR, and others) from an incorrect memory location. This mismatch causes the processor to read invalid values, which leads to a hard fault—a type of fatal error on the Cortex-M4. In short, setting the PSP ensures that the CPU pops the correct data from the thread’s stack, making the thread run as intended without crashing.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',2,5,'2025-05-28_13-05-03','If the thread function returned instead of looping forever, the processor would attempt to use the return address stored in the LR (Link Register) to resume execution. However, since this thread wasn\'t started via a regular function call and the LR may not contain a valid return address, the system would likely jump to an invalid location or attempt to execute arbitrary memory. This would almost certainly lead to a hard fault or undefined behavior. Threads are expected to either loop indefinitely or be terminated cleanly by the OS to prevent this.\n',0,'s25'),('j34ye',2,5,'2025-05-28_18-02-01','If the thread function finished execution and returned, the processor would try to use the value in the Link Register (LR) to determine where to go next. On the Cortex-M4, this return address is expected to be stored on the stack as part of the thread\'s context. However, when we manually set up the thread\'s stack, we didn’t provide a valid return address, because we assumed the thread would never return.\nAs a result, when the function finishes and the CPU pops the return address from the stack, it gets an invalid value. This leads the CPU to jump to an undefined memory location, which causes a hard fault—a serious crash in the system. On Cortex-M4 architecture, a missing or corrupted return address results in the processor executing an instruction from a non-code location, which it cannot recover from. Therefore, threads should either loop forever or terminate in a controlled manner through the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,1,'2025-06-10_14-30-21','Each .c file in a project is first compiled separately into an object file (.o) by the compiler. During this step, the compiler checks the syntax, resolves local references, and translates the code into machine instructions. Once all the .c files are compiled, a linker takes over and combines all the object files into a single executable. The linker resolves cross-file references, such as functions defined in one .c file and used in another. Header files (.h) help this process by sharing function declarations and struct definitions across files. The result is a complete program that can run on the target system.\n',0,'s25'),('j34ye',3,1,'2025-06-10_15-48-04','The process of turning multiple .c files into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking.\nPreprocessing:\nBefore the compiler analyzes the code, the preprocessor handles all lines beginning with #, such as #include and #define. It expands macros, includes the contents of header files, and removes comments. The output is a single, expanded .c file with everything in place for compilation. This step ensures that each .c file has access to necessary declarations and definitions.\nCompiling:\nEach preprocessed .c file is then translated into machine-independent assembly or object code. The compiler checks syntax, converts code into instructions the computer can understand, and produces an object file (.o) for each .c file. These object files still have unresolved references to functions or variables defined elsewhere.\nLinking:\nThe linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable file. It resolves external references—like when a function defined in one .c file is used in another—and arranges everything in memory. It also pulls in any standard libraries needed (e.g., libc), finalizing the program so it’s ready to run.\nBy separating code into multiple .c files and using this three-step process, we can build organized, modular programs efficiently.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,1,'2025-06-10_16-00-43','The process of turning multiple .c files into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking.\nPreprocessing:\nBefore the compiler analyzes the code, the preprocessor handles all lines beginning with #, such as #include and #define. It expands macros, includes the contents of header files, and removes comments. The output is a single, expanded .c file with everything in place for compilation. This step ensures that each .c file has access to necessary declarations and definitions.\nCompiling:\nEach preprocessed .c file is then translated into machine-independent assembly or object code. The compiler checks syntax, converts code into instructions the computer can understand, and produces an object file (.o) for each .c file. These object files still have unresolved references to functions or variables defined elsewhere.\nLinking:\nThe linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable file. It resolves external references—like when a function defined in one .c file is used in another—and arranges everything in memory. It also pulls in any standard libraries needed (e.g., libc), finalizing the program so it’s ready to run.\nBy separating code into multiple .c files and using this three-step process, we can build organized, modular programs efficiently.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,1,'2025-06-11_14-54-53','The process of turning multiple .c files into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking.\nPreprocessing:\nBefore the compiler analyzes the code, the preprocessor handles all lines beginning with #, such as #include and #define. It expands macros, includes the contents of header files, and removes comments. The output is a single, expanded .c file with everything in place for compilation. This step ensures that each .c file has access to necessary declarations and definitions.\nCompiling:\nEach preprocessed .c file is then translated into machine-independent assembly or object code. The compiler checks syntax, converts code into instructions the computer can understand, and produces an object file (.o) for each .c file. These object files still have unresolved references to functions or variables defined elsewhere.\nLinking:\nThe linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable file. It resolves external references—like when a function defined in one .c file is used in another—and arranges everything in memory. It also pulls in any standard libraries needed (e.g., libc), finalizing the program so it’s ready to run.\nBy separating code into multiple .c files and using this three-step process, we can build organized, modular programs efficiently.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,2,'2025-06-10_14-30-21','Including a .c file using #include forces the compiler to insert the entire source file into another file before compilation. This breaks the proper compilation and linking process by effectively duplicating code, which can cause symbol redefinition errors and make debugging harder. It also defeats the purpose of modularity and makes the build system fragile. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately, and only their declarations should be shared via .h files. This keeps code organized, reusable, and easier to manage.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,2,'2025-06-10_15-48-04','Including a .c file using #include forces the compiler to insert the entire source file into another file before compilation. This breaks the proper compilation and linking process by effectively duplicating code, which can cause symbol redefinition errors and make debugging harder. It also defeats the purpose of modularity and makes the build system fragile. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately, and only their declarations should be shared via .h files. This keeps code organized, reusable, and easier to manage.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,2,'2025-06-10_16-00-43','Including a .c file using #include forces the compiler to insert the entire source file into another file before compilation. This breaks the proper compilation and linking process by effectively duplicating code, which can cause symbol redefinition errors and make debugging harder. It also defeats the purpose of modularity and makes the build system fragile. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately, and only their declarations should be shared via .h files. This keeps code organized, reusable, and easier to manage.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,2,'2025-06-11_14-54-53','Including a .c file using #include forces the compiler to insert the entire source file into another file before compilation. This breaks the proper compilation and linking process by effectively duplicating code, which can cause symbol redefinition errors and make debugging harder. It also defeats the purpose of modularity and makes the build system fragile. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately, and only their declarations should be shared via .h files. This keeps code organized, reusable, and easier to manage.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,3,'2025-06-10_14-30-21','To set up and start a thread from the user\'s point of view:\nWrite a function that defines what the thread should do.\nCall osKernelInitialize() to set up the kernel and initialize stack and thread structures.\nCall osCreateThread() and pass it the function you wrote. This allocates stack space and prepares the thread.\nFinally, call osKernelStart() to begin executing the thread.\nEach step ensures the kernel is ready, the thread has memory to run, and the system knows to start executing it.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,3,'2025-06-10_15-48-04','To set up and start a thread from the user\'s point of view:\nWrite a function that defines what the thread should do.\nCall osKernelInitialize() to set up the kernel and initialize stack and thread structures.\nCall osCreateThread() and pass it the function you wrote. This allocates stack space and prepares the thread.\nFinally, call osKernelStart() to begin executing the thread.\nEach step ensures the kernel is ready, the thread has memory to run, and the system knows to start executing it.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,3,'2025-06-10_16-00-43','To set up and start a thread from the user\'s point of view:\nWrite a function that defines what the thread should do.\nCall osKernelInitialize() to set up the kernel and initialize stack and thread structures.\nCall osCreateThread() and pass it the function you wrote. This allocates stack space and prepares the thread.\nFinally, call osKernelStart() to begin executing the thread.\nEach step ensures the kernel is ready, the thread has memory to run, and the system knows to start executing it.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,3,'2025-06-11_14-54-53','To set up and start a thread from the user\'s point of view:\nWrite a function that defines what the thread should do.\nCall osKernelInitialize() to set up the kernel and initialize stack and thread structures.\nCall osCreateThread() and pass it the function you wrote. This allocates stack space and prepares the thread.\nFinally, call osKernelStart() to begin executing the thread.\nEach step ensures the kernel is ready, the thread has memory to run, and the system knows to start executing it.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,4,'2025-06-10_14-30-21','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory from which each thread gets its own stack space. In future labs, when multiple threads are introduced, the kernel will divide this pool into equally sized stacks, one per thread. Each new thread is allocated a section of this pool using a stack allocator, which tracks used and remaining space. This enables the OS to support multiple independent execution contexts without their stacks overlapping, which is critical for stability and proper thread switching.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,4,'2025-06-10_15-48-04','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory from which each thread gets its own stack space. In future labs, when multiple threads are introduced, the kernel will divide this pool into equally sized stacks, one per thread. Each new thread is allocated a section of this pool using a stack allocator, which tracks used and remaining space. This enables the OS to support multiple independent execution contexts without their stacks overlapping, which is critical for stability and proper thread switching.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,4,'2025-06-10_16-00-43','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory from which each thread gets its own stack space. In future labs, when multiple threads are introduced, the kernel will divide this pool into equally sized stacks, one per thread. Each new thread is allocated a section of this pool using a stack allocator, which tracks used and remaining space. This enables the OS to support multiple independent execution contexts without their stacks overlapping, which is critical for stability and proper thread switching.\n',0,'s25'),('j34ye',3,4,'2025-06-11_14-54-53','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory from which each thread gets its own stack space. In future labs, when multiple threads are introduced, the kernel will divide this pool into equally sized stacks, one per thread. Each new thread is allocated a section of this pool using a stack allocator, which tracks used and remaining space. This enables the OS to support multiple independent execution contexts without their stacks overlapping, which is critical for stability and proper thread switching.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,5,'2025-06-10_14-30-21','In this lab, the \"kernel\" refers to core OS functionality such as stack management and thread creation, which goes into kernel.c and kernel.h. The \"user\" refers to application-level code like thread functions and the main() function, which go into the main .c file. From the user\'s perspective, this separation is important because it clearly defines responsibilities: the user writes high-level behavior and interacts with the OS through a defined API, while the kernel handles low-level operations. This improves code clarity, modularity, and prevents accidental misuse of internal OS mechanisms.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,5,'2025-06-10_15-48-04','In this lab, the \"kernel\" refers to core OS functionality such as stack management and thread creation, which goes into kernel.c and kernel.h. The \"user\" refers to application-level code like thread functions and the main() function, which go into the main .c file. From the user\'s perspective, this separation is important because it clearly defines responsibilities: the user writes high-level behavior and interacts with the OS through a defined API, while the kernel handles low-level operations. This improves code clarity, modularity, and prevents accidental misuse of internal OS mechanisms.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,5,'2025-06-10_16-00-43','In this lab, the \"kernel\" refers to core OS functionality such as stack management and thread creation, which goes into kernel.c and kernel.h. The \"user\" refers to application-level code like thread functions and the main() function, which go into the main .c file. From the user\'s perspective, this separation is important because it clearly defines responsibilities: the user writes high-level behavior and interacts with the OS through a defined API, while the kernel handles low-level operations. This improves code clarity, modularity, and prevents accidental misuse of internal OS mechanisms.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',3,5,'2025-06-11_14-54-53','In this lab, the \"kernel\" refers to core OS functionality such as stack management and thread creation, which goes into kernel.c and kernel.h. The \"user\" refers to application-level code like thread functions and the main() function, which go into the main .c file. From the user\'s perspective, this separation is important because it clearly defines responsibilities: the user writes high-level behavior and interacts with the OS through a defined API, while the kernel handles low-level operations. This improves code clarity, modularity, and prevents accidental misuse of internal OS mechanisms.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,1,'2025-06-24_14-29-30','Round-robin scheduling works by giving each thread a turn to run in a fixed, repeating order. With a queue, this can be implemented by always selecting the thread at the front to run, and after it yields, placing it at the back of the queue. This naturally cycles through all threads in a fair and predictable manner.\nUsing a queue instead of an array has some benefits:\nCleaner abstraction: A queue naturally matches the round-robin behavior without requiring manual index tracking or wrap-around logic (e.g., mod operations).\nDynamic scheduling: Queues can more easily support future features like dynamically adding or removing threads during execution.\nScalability: If implemented as a linked list, queues can handle a variable number of threads without a fixed-size limit.\nIn short, while an array works fine for basic round-robin, a queue more elegantly models the scheduling behavior and opens the door to more flexible designs later on.\n',0,'s25'),('j34ye',4,1,'2025-06-24_15-46-27','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using either an array or a queue, but each approach has trade-offs. In the array method, threads are stored in a fixed-size list, and the scheduler keeps track of the current thread with an index. After each thread yields, the index is incremented (modulo the number of threads) to select the next thread.\nWith a queue, the process is slightly different but functionally similar. The currently running thread is removed from the front of the queue after yielding and added back to the rear. This way, the next thread in line always becomes the running thread, and the rotation continues indefinitely.\nComparison and Analysis:\nSimilarity: Both methods achieve the same result—each thread gets an equal chance to run in a circular order.\nBenefits of a Queue: A queue can simplify the logic by removing the need to track indices manually, and it naturally models the round-robin flow. It may also allow easier extensions, such as dynamic addition/removal of threads.\nOverhead and Trade-offs: However, queues can introduce additional overhead, especially if implemented as linked structures (e.g., dynamic memory management or pointer dereferencing). Arrays offer faster indexing with predictable memory layout and no dynamic memory needs.\nIn summary, while queues offer conceptual elegance and flexibility, they may come with minor performance trade-offs compared to arrays. The best choice depends on the specific constraints and goals of the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,1,'2025-06-24_15-56-35','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using either an array or a queue, but each approach has trade-offs. In the array method, threads are stored in a fixed-size list, and the scheduler keeps track of the current thread with an index. After each thread yields, the index is incremented (modulo the number of threads) to select the next thread.\nWith a queue, the process is slightly different but functionally similar. The currently running thread is removed from the front of the queue after yielding and added back to the rear. This way, the next thread in line always becomes the running thread, and the rotation continues indefinitely.\nComparison and Analysis:\nSimilarity: Both methods achieve the same result—each thread gets an equal chance to run in a circular order.\nBenefits of a Queue: A queue can simplify the logic by removing the need to track indices manually, and it naturally models the round-robin flow. It may also allow easier extensions, such as dynamic addition/removal of threads.\nOverhead and Trade-offs: However, queues can introduce additional overhead, especially if implemented as linked structures (e.g., dynamic memory management or pointer dereferencing). Arrays offer faster indexing with predictable memory layout and no dynamic memory needs.\nIn summary, while queues offer conceptual elegance and flexibility, they may come with minor performance trade-offs compared to arrays. The best choice depends on the specific constraints and goals of the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,2,'2025-06-24_14-29-30','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for switching the CPU from one thread to another. It does this by performing a context switch, which means saving the current thread\'s state and restoring the next thread\'s state.\nHere’s how it works:\nSave context: First, it saves the contents of registers R4–R11, which are not automatically saved during exceptions. It also saves the current thread’s stack pointer to the thread’s control block (TCB).\nChoose next thread: It then calls the osSched() function, which selects the next thread to run (usually using round-robin scheduling) and updates the global currentThread variable.\nRestore context: Finally, it loads the next thread\'s stack pointer and restores the saved registers so that execution continues from where that thread last left off.\nThis process allows the OS to pause one thread and resume another seamlessly, enabling multitasking even on a single processor.\n',0,'s25'),('j34ye',4,2,'2025-06-24_15-46-27','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s state and loading the next thread’s state. To do this correctly, it must follow a precise sequence:\nRead the current PSP (Process Stack Pointer): This is done first to determine where the current thread’s stack frame begins.\nAdjust the PSP to make room for registers R4–R11: Since these registers are not automatically saved during an exception, we manually allocate space by subtracting 8 words (32 bytes) from PSP.\nSave registers R4–R11 to the adjusted PSP: This stores the current thread’s context so it can resume later.\nStore the updated PSP in the current thread’s TCB: This records where the thread left off.\nCall osSched(): This function updates the currentThread index to select the next thread, typically using round-robin logic.\nLoad the next thread’s PSP from its TCB: This tells the processor where the next thread\'s stack is located.\nRestore registers R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack: This resumes the context of the next thread.\nBy following this exact order—starting with reading PSP before saving registers—the OS ensures that all data is saved and restored correctly, allowing one thread to pause and another to resume seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,2,'2025-06-24_15-56-35','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s state and loading the next thread’s state. To do this correctly, it must follow a precise sequence:\nRead the current PSP (Process Stack Pointer): This is done first to determine where the current thread’s stack frame begins.\nAdjust the PSP to make room for registers R4–R11: Since these registers are not automatically saved during an exception, we manually allocate space by subtracting 8 words (32 bytes) from PSP.\nSave registers R4–R11 to the adjusted PSP: This stores the current thread’s context so it can resume later.\nStore the updated PSP in the current thread’s TCB: This records where the thread left off.\nCall osSched(): This function updates the currentThread index to select the next thread, typically using round-robin logic.\nLoad the next thread’s PSP from its TCB: This tells the processor where the next thread\'s stack is located.\nRestore registers R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack: This resumes the context of the next thread.\nBy following this exact order—starting with reading PSP before saving registers—the OS ensures that all data is saved and restored correctly, allowing one thread to pause and another to resume seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,3,'2025-06-24_14-29-30','Always choosing the highest-priority thread can lead to starvation—lower-priority threads may never get a chance to run if a high-priority thread is always ready. This breaks fairness and defeats the purpose of cooperative multitasking.\nAdditionally, this makes the system less predictable. Round-robin ensures each thread gets CPU time, but a strict priority scheduler can get stuck running a few high-priority threads endlessly. It also adds complexity: the scheduler must scan all threads for the highest priority every time, which can be inefficient compared to simply rotating through them.\nA better approach is priority aging or priority with round-robin fallback, which keeps the system responsive and fair.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,3,'2025-06-24_15-46-27','Always choosing the highest-priority thread can lead to starvation—lower-priority threads may never get a chance to run if a high-priority thread is always ready. This breaks fairness and defeats the purpose of cooperative multitasking.\nAdditionally, this makes the system less predictable. Round-robin ensures each thread gets CPU time, but a strict priority scheduler can get stuck running a few high-priority threads endlessly. It also adds complexity: the scheduler must scan all threads for the highest priority every time, which can be inefficient compared to simply rotating through them.\nA better approach is priority aging or priority with round-robin fallback, which keeps the system responsive and fair.\n',0,'s25'),('j34ye',4,3,'2025-06-24_15-56-35','Always choosing the highest-priority thread can lead to starvation—lower-priority threads may never get a chance to run if a high-priority thread is always ready. This breaks fairness and defeats the purpose of cooperative multitasking.\nAdditionally, this makes the system less predictable. Round-robin ensures each thread gets CPU time, but a strict priority scheduler can get stuck running a few high-priority threads endlessly. It also adds complexity: the scheduler must scan all threads for the highest priority every time, which can be inefficient compared to simply rotating through them.\nA better approach is priority aging or priority with round-robin fallback, which keeps the system responsive and fair.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,4,'2025-06-24_14-29-30','Benefits of calling C functions:\nReadability and maintainability: C is easier to write, read, and debug than assembly, making the code more accessible to others (and your future self).\nBetter abstraction: Complex logic like choosing the next thread is simpler in C, especially when working with arrays or structs.\nFewer errors: Assembly is error-prone, especially when dealing with low-level stack operations. Using C reduces the chance of subtle bugs.\nDownsides:\nPerformance: C functions may add slight overhead, especially if the compiler inserts extra instructions to preserve registers or set up a stack frame.\nLess control: Assembly gives fine-grained control over the CPU and registers, which may be necessary in critical sections of the OS.\nOverall, combining assembly for low-level mechanics with C for high-level logic strikes a good balance between control and clarity.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,4,'2025-06-24_15-46-27','Benefits of calling C functions:\nReadability and maintainability: C is easier to write, read, and debug than assembly, making the code more accessible to others (and your future self).\nBetter abstraction: Complex logic like choosing the next thread is simpler in C, especially when working with arrays or structs.\nFewer errors: Assembly is error-prone, especially when dealing with low-level stack operations. Using C reduces the chance of subtle bugs.\nDownsides:\nPerformance: C functions may add slight overhead, especially if the compiler inserts extra instructions to preserve registers or set up a stack frame.\nLess control: Assembly gives fine-grained control over the CPU and registers, which may be necessary in critical sections of the OS.\nOverall, combining assembly for low-level mechanics with C for high-level logic strikes a good balance between control and clarity.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,4,'2025-06-24_15-56-35','Benefits of calling C functions:\nReadability and maintainability: C is easier to write, read, and debug than assembly, making the code more accessible to others (and your future self).\nBetter abstraction: Complex logic like choosing the next thread is simpler in C, especially when working with arrays or structs.\nFewer errors: Assembly is error-prone, especially when dealing with low-level stack operations. Using C reduces the chance of subtle bugs.\nDownsides:\nPerformance: C functions may add slight overhead, especially if the compiler inserts extra instructions to preserve registers or set up a stack frame.\nLess control: Assembly gives fine-grained control over the CPU and registers, which may be necessary in critical sections of the OS.\nOverall, combining assembly for low-level mechanics with C for high-level logic strikes a good balance between control and clarity.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,5,'2025-06-24_14-29-30','Yes, the OS will still work with a single thread, but it won\'t be meaningful multitasking. The thread will run, print something, call osYield(), trigger a context switch, and then the scheduler will choose the same thread again because it’s the only one available.\nFunctionally, the OS will go through all the motions—context switch, saving and restoring the same thread—but there will be no visible effect since there\'s no other thread to switch to. It’s like handing off a baton in a relay race where you’re the only runner.\nThis behavior proves the scheduler and context switch mechanism are functioning correctly, even if no real multitasking occurs.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,5,'2025-06-24_15-46-27','Yes, the OS will still work with a single thread, but it won\'t be meaningful multitasking. The thread will run, print something, call osYield(), trigger a context switch, and then the scheduler will choose the same thread again because it’s the only one available.\nFunctionally, the OS will go through all the motions—context switch, saving and restoring the same thread—but there will be no visible effect since there\'s no other thread to switch to. It’s like handing off a baton in a relay race where you’re the only runner.\nThis behavior proves the scheduler and context switch mechanism are functioning correctly, even if no real multitasking occurs.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',4,5,'2025-06-24_15-56-35','Yes, the OS will still work with a single thread, but it won\'t be meaningful multitasking. The thread will run, print something, call osYield(), trigger a context switch, and then the scheduler will choose the same thread again because it’s the only one available.\nFunctionally, the OS will go through all the motions—context switch, saving and restoring the same thread—but there will be no visible effect since there\'s no other thread to switch to. It’s like handing off a baton in a relay race where you’re the only runner.\nThis behavior proves the scheduler and context switch mechanism are functioning correctly, even if no real multitasking occurs.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',5,1,'2025-07-22_16-33-50','Argument pointer is assigned to R0 when thread is created, thread can access argument via arg pointer.\n',0,'s25'),('j34ye',5,1,'2025-07-22_16-45-30','When a user wants to start a new thread they must first prepare any data the thread will need and then call the thread-creation function (for example, osCreateThread) passing two things: a pointer to the thread’s entry function and a void* pointer to the user’s data. Under the hood, the OS allocates a fresh stack for the new thread and builds its initial exception-return frame on that stack. As part of that frame setup the OS writes the user’s data pointer into the saved-R0 slot and writes the entry-function address into the saved-PC slot. When the scheduler invokes the first context switch via the PendSV exception, the hardware pops those saved registers into the CPU’s R0 and PC registers so that R0 contains the argument pointer and execution resumes at the thread’s entry function. This works because the ARM Procedure Call Standard mandates that the first function argument be passed in R0, so by preloading R0 before the thread starts, the user’s data pointer is automatically delivered as the argument to the C function signature void thread_function(void* args).\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',5,2,'2025-07-22_16-33-50','',0,'s25'),('j34ye',5,2,'2025-07-22_16-45-30','Calling osYield from inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler (or its SVC_Handler_Main), or SysTick_Handler is not allowed because those routines are themselves part of the low-level context-switch mechanism and run with interrupts masked or at special priorities. If you invoke osYield there you end up re-pending PendSV or re-entering the supervisor-call logic before the current save/restore sequence has completed. This nested or re-entrant switching corrupts the exception stack frame, clobbers registers that have not yet been saved, and can trigger hard faults or leave the system in an inconsistent state. In short, it violates the atomicity of the context-switch sequence, leads to stack corruption and unpredictable instability.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',5,3,'2025-07-22_16-33-50','',0,'s25'),('j34ye',5,3,'2025-07-22_16-45-30','Reducing the OS tick from 1 ms to 0.1 ms would improve timing resolution so that delays, timeouts, and pre-emptive timeslices can be enforced with 100 µs granularity, which lowers worst-case scheduling jitter for high-priority tasks. However, generating ten times as many SysTick interrupts increases CPU overhead and interrupt handling time, reduces the time available for application code between ticks, worsens peripheral interrupt latency, and prevents the processor from staying in deeper low-power modes. In power-sensitive or CPU-constrained systems the extra interrupt load can significantly degrade performance and battery life.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',5,4,'2025-07-22_16-33-50','',0,'s25'),('j34ye',5,4,'2025-07-22_16-45-30','In cooperative multitasking each thread must explicitly yield or block for the scheduler to switch to another thread. If a thread never yields it monopolizes the CPU indefinitely. From the OS’s point of view, context switches only occur when a thread calls osYield or blocks on a synchronization primitive, so no periodic timer is needed. In pre-emptive multitasking the OS uses a hardware timer (such as SysTick) to interrupt running threads when their timeslice expires or when a higher-priority thread becomes ready. The SysTick handler decrements the running thread’s counter and pends PendSV when it hits zero. This forces a context switch regardless of whether the thread cooperated. Pre-emptive scheduling requires runtime accounting and periodic interrupts but guarantees fairness and responsiveness even if threads never yield.\n',1,'s25'),('j34ye',5,5,'2025-07-22_16-33-50','',0,'s25'),('j34ye',5,5,'2025-07-22_16-45-30','Both voluntary yielding and time-based pre-emptive switching rely on the same PendSV exception to perform the actual context switch. When a thread calls osYield the OS resets its runtime counter to a full timeslice and sets the PendSV-pending bit. When SysTick fires it decrements the counter and, if it reaches zero, resets the counter and sets the same PendSV-pending bit. In either case the low-priority PendSV handler runs, saves the current thread’s registers, invokes the scheduler to choose the next ready thread, and restores that thread’s registers. Because both mechanisms funnel through the identical save-schedule-restore code and share the same thread-state data, yielding and timer-driven pre-emption interoperate seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',1,1,'2025-06-12_00-23-43','The HAL is a library provided by ST Microelectronics which simplifies working with hardware peripherals like UART. It gets rid of the complexity and makes development easier. Instead of manually setting each register, prebuilt functions like HAL_UART_TRANSMIT can be used for the task.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',1,1,'2025-06-12_16-20-06','The HAL is a library provided by ST Microelectronics which simplifies working with hardware peripherals like UART, GPIO. It gets rid of the complexity and makes development easier. Instead of manually setting each register, prebuilt functions like HAL_UART_TRANSMIT can be used for the task. This makes development faster, more readable and help minimize errors. However, sometimes HAL can lead to less efficient code or less control over timing. In some high performance critical systems, bypassing the HAL and directly accessing registers can be more efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',1,2,'2025-06-12_00-23-43','__io_putchar function helps the system to print a single character using the HAL_UART_Transmit function. It is needed because it allows printf to send the output to the desired location, which is our serial port in this case. __io_putchar helps us communicate with the hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',1,2,'2025-06-12_16-20-06','__io_putchar function helps the system to print a single character using the HAL_UART_Transmit function. It is needed because it allows printf to send the output to the desired location, which is our serial port in this case. __io_putchar acts as a bridge between standard C library and the hardware. Without this function being properly implemented, printf would not work correctly on our system as it would have no hardware specific method for output. The function provides flexibility to redirect the output to different interfaces since hardware setups can vary between systems.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',1,3,'2025-06-12_00-23-43','We used the debugger in this lab to find out the values for some internal registers like MSP, PSP, and SP which cannot be directly printed using the printf function. The debugger lets us see how the values of these registers change during different stages of the program and compare them to the expected values.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',1,3,'2025-06-12_16-20-06','We used the debugger in this lab to find out the values for some internal registers like MSP, PSP, and SP which cannot be directly printed using the printf function. Printf is used for debugging logic or variables but cannot access system level registers. However, the debugger gives us a real-time view of memory and register states, which is important for verifying low-level operations like stack manipulation that occur outside the standard I/O.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',1,4,'2025-06-12_00-23-43','We got the initial MSP value by going to the address 0x0 which gives us the location for the top of the main stack. We subtracted 0x400 from that MSP value we found to calculate our new PSP value. We needed to know the MSP’s initial address for this to work to know where the stack starts so we could allocate space for the PSP without overlapping with the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',1,4,'2025-06-12_16-20-06','We got the initial MSP value by going to the address 0x0 which gives us the location for the top of the main stack. We subtracted 0x400 from that MSP value we found to calculate our new PSP value. We needed to know the MSP’s initial address for this to work to know where the stack starts so we could allocate space for the PSP without overlapping with the main stack. This allows for safe and predictable usage of memory.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',1,5,'2025-06-12_00-23-43','We would define how big each stack is and how many threads are needed. Then, we could calculate where each of these stacks would start in our memory based on initial MSP values. Using this method, we can make sure that any overlap is being avoided in between multiple thread stacks.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',1,5,'2025-06-12_16-20-06','We would define how big each stack is and how many threads are needed. Starting from the initial MSP value, we could calculate each stacks starting address by subtracting fixed offset values defined for each stack. Each address must be properly aligned can carefully assigned to avoid overlap with other stacks and other memory areas. For example, thread’s 1 PSP could be MSP – 0x400, thread 2’s could be MSP – 0x800, and so on.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',2,1,'2025-06-25_20-32-43','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, CPU jumps immediately to executing that function as if it was called. The code starts executing resulting in an infinite while loop continuously printing “Hello, PC!’.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',2,1,'2025-06-25_21-04-47','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, CPU jumps immediately to executing that function as if it was called. The code starts executing resulting in an infinite while loop continuously printing “Hello, PC!’. In real code, this can be useful for context switches, but if wrong address is loaded, we will get garbage values or a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',2,1,'2025-06-26_11-47-30','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, CPU jumps immediately to executing that function as if it was called. The code starts executing resulting in an infinite while loop continuously printing “Hello, PC!’. In real code, this can be useful for context switches, but if wrong address is loaded, we will get garbage values or a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',2,2,'2025-06-25_20-32-43','System call is a trap which engages the kernel mode. For example, Instruction like SVC #1 is executed which traps into the SVC Handler of the OS. The handler inspects #1 do decide what action to perform in response to the system call. After responding to the system call, the control is returned to the user code.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',2,2,'2025-06-25_21-04-47','System call is a trap which engages the kernel mode. For example, Instruction like SVC #1 is executed which traps into the SVC Handler of the OS. The handler inspects #1 do decide what action to perform in response to the system call. After responding to the system call, the control is returned to the user code. A system call is a standard mechanism used by the user mode programs to request privileged authorization from the operating system. This protection boundary prevents untrusted code from directly manipulating the hardware or critical data structures.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',2,2,'2025-06-26_11-47-30','System call is conceptually a function call from user code into the kernel that provides access to privileged services. In implementation, issuing SVC #n generates a trap, then the CPU saves user mode registers to the stack, switches to kernel mode, and executes the SVC handler. The handler examines the call number n, performs the requested service, and then returns back to the user mode. By treating the system call as a special function call, with hardware enforcement of privilege levels, it gives user programs a clean API while protecting critical resources.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',2,3,'2025-06-25_20-32-43','Setting up a thread’s stack means loading it with register values in a way the hardware expects. We pushed xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11-R4. The hardware then pops these values into the registers making it look like the thread was suspended right before running.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',2,3,'2025-06-25_21-04-47','Setting up a thread’s stack means loading it with register values in a way the hardware expects. We pushed xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11-R4. The hardware then pops these values into the registers making it look like the thread was suspended right before running. A valid memory region is allocated with PSP pointing to its top. Setting up means pushing that region with the exact exception frame so that the Cortex M4 can pop them in order into the register file. CPU will not be able to restore context correctly without a properly allocated stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',2,3,'2025-06-26_11-47-30','Setting up a thread’s stack means loading it with register values in a way the hardware expects. We pushed xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11-R4. The hardware then pops these values into the registers making it look like the thread was suspended right before running. A valid memory region is allocated with PSP pointing to its top. Setting up means pushing that region with the exact exception frame so that the Cortex M4 can pop them in order into the register file. CPU will not be able to restore context correctly without a properly allocated stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',2,4,'2025-06-25_20-32-43','We use __set_PSP function before we could run the thread because if PSP still points at MSP or any uninitialized address, the system will try to pop from the main stack or invalid address, causing invalid register values to be popped leading to memory corruption resulting in a hard fault. Setting PSP makes sure that CPU pops or un-stacks register values from the right location provided.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',2,4,'2025-06-25_21-04-47','We use __set_PSP function before we could run the thread because if PSP still points at MSP or any uninitialized address, the system will try to pop from the main stack or invalid address, causing invalid register values to be popped leading to memory corruption resulting in a hard fault. Setting PSP makes sure that CPU pops or un-stacks register values from the right location provided. PSP and MSP refer to two different regions in the RAM. Threads use the PSP while the OS uses MSP. If __set_PSP is not called, the hardware would attempt to unstack  our context frame from MSP instead. Setting PSP ensures correct context values are being popped for correct operation.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',2,4,'2025-06-26_11-47-30','We use __set_PSP function before we could run the thread because if PSP still points at MSP or any uninitialized address, the system will try to pop from the main stack or invalid address, causing invalid register values to be popped leading to memory corruption resulting in a hard fault. Setting PSP makes sure that CPU pops or un-stacks register values from the right location provided. PSP and MSP refer to two different regions in the RAM. Threads use the PSP while the OS uses MSP. If __set_PSP is not called, the hardware would attempt to unstack  our context frame from MSP instead. Setting PSP ensures correct context values are being popped for correct operation.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',2,5,'2025-06-25_20-32-43','Threads simply cannot just return and must stay alive. The processor would be returning invalid or garbage values and likely hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',2,5,'2025-06-25_21-04-47','Threads simply cannot just return and must stay alive. The processor would be returning invalid or garbage values and likely hard fault. Normally, a C function returns to the address stored in the link register, but in our context LR does not hold a real address. If our print continuously function exited normally, CPU would interpret that address value as a branch destination resulting in invalid memory and causing a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',2,5,'2025-06-26_11-47-30','If the thread function printed once and then returned normally, the processor would grab its return address from the link register. In our setup LR does not contain a real code address, so on return the CPU would branch into an invalid location. This would result in garbage values triggering a hard fault. Threads generated this way cannot use a standard return and must loop forever or explicitly yield back to the scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',3,1,'2025-06-26_21-36-04','Each .c file gets compiled independently by the compiler first. The unresolved references are then resolved by the compiler as it solves cross references by pulling in any needed library code and lays out the final output. After compiling all these files, a single executable output is produced.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',3,1,'2025-06-27_00-48-05','Each .c file is run through the preprocessor before compilation. All commands like #include and #define are expanded or resolved first. Then, the compiler translates each preprocessed code into an object file (.o), performing syntax checks, optimizations, and generating machine code. Each object file still contains some unresolved symbols for external functions or global variables. Finally, the linker takes all object files with any libraries and resolves cross-file references by matching calls to their definitions, applies address relocation and produces a single executable. The linker’s job is different from the compiler’s as it joins together the pieces into a runnable program.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',3,2,'2025-06-26_21-36-04','Using #include <kernel.c> literally adds the entire kernel.c file to the top of our .c file before compilation, leading to repetitive definitions for different functions or variables. That is why, we like to store only the function prototypes, struct definitions, etc. in kernel.h and compile each of our .c files separately. This avoids duplication and allows for more efficient runtime.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',3,2,'2025-06-27_00-48-05','Using #include <kernel.c> literally adds the entire kernel.c file to the top of our .c file before compilation, leading to repetitive definitions for different functions or variables. That is why, we like to store only the function prototypes, struct definitions, etc. in kernel.h and compile each of our .c files separately. This avoids duplication and allows for more efficient runtime.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',3,3,'2025-06-26_21-36-04','1. The user calls osKernelInitialize to set up the stack allocator in order to initialize the kernel\n2. Creating the thread by passing our function pointer to the kernel using osCreateThread which allocates a stack.\n3. Starting the kernel using osKernelStart loads the PSP with a new thread’s stack, triggering an SVC, and begins executing our thread function.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',3,3,'2025-06-27_00-48-05','1. The user write the thread function in main.c. The function must have been already implemented before osCreateThread is called.\n2. The user calls osKernelInitialize to set up the stack allocator in order to initialize the kernel\n3. Creating the thread by passing our function pointer to the kernel using osCreateThread. The OS uses this address to build the initial CPU context on a fresh stack.\n4. Starting the kernel using osKernelStart loads the PSP with a new thread’s stack, triggering an SVC, and begins executing our thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',3,4,'2025-06-26_21-36-04','The stack pool is a big piece of RAM reserved for thread’s stacks. Each time a new thread is created, a new minimum memory is allocated from this stack pool so each thread can have its own stack. In future labs with multiple threads, the OS simply calls the allocator function repeatedly until each thread has enough of its own memory so that it can run independently.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',3,4,'2025-06-27_00-48-05','The stack pool is a big piece of RAM reserved for thread’s stacks. Each time a new thread is created, a new minimum memory is allocated from this stack pool so each thread can have its own stack. In future labs with multiple threads, the OS simply calls the allocator function repeatedly until each thread has enough of its own memory so that it can run independently. The stack pool is initially set aside at link time (via _Min_Stack_Size) and serves both kernel’s own exception stack and the thread’s stack in user mode. Internally, the OS makes a pointer to the top of the free stack pool. To make a new thread stack, it subtracts a fixed size from that pointer, which is always divisible by 8 bytes, and returns that block. This fixed size subdivision of the stack pool helps make sure that each thread’s stack is isolated from the others.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',3,5,'2025-06-26_21-36-04','User code mainly sits in the main.c file. User code defines application level threads and calls to the OS API. The user can change this file without worrying about low-level details. Whereas, kernel level code sits in kernel.c and kernel.h. It implements low level executions like SVC response, and stack pool allocation. The user usually does not care about what is going on in the kernel level code. This distinction is important so that the user only see the API and not what is going on behind the scenes. This also protects the low level code against corruption and makes the codebase easier to understand and maintain.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',3,5,'2025-06-27_00-48-05','User code mainly sits in the main.c file. User code defines application level threads and calls to the OS API. The user can change this file without worrying about low-level details. Whereas, kernel level code sits in kernel.c and kernel.h. It implements low level executions like SVC response, and stack pool allocation. The user usually does not care about what is going on in the kernel level code. This distinction is important so that the user only see the API and not what is going on behind the scenes. This also protects the low level code against corruption and makes the codebase easier to understand and maintain.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,1,'2025-07-27_00-45-42','In round-robin scheduling using a queue, the currently running thread is at the front of the queue. When osYield() is called, the current thread is popped off the front and pushed to the back. The next thread runs then. The major benefits of using a queue are that there is no need to track current_thread or manually wrap around the array each time. It is also much easier to add or remove threads dynamically and more likely what real OS schedulers use. A queue is more flexible and neater design option for a multitasking system.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,1,'2025-07-27_03-22-33','In round-robin scheduling using a queue, the currently running thread is at the front of the queue. When osYield() is called, the current thread is popped off the front and pushed to the back. The next thread runs then. The major benefits of using a queue are that there is no need to track current_thread or manually wrap around the array each time. It is also much easier to add or remove threads dynamically and more likely what real OS schedulers use. A queue is more flexible and neater design option for a multitasking system. However, a trade-off is that queues add slight overhead in pointer management, especially in limited resource constrained systems. Also, a queue may not work well for some scheduling strategies that need precise timings or deadlines.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,1,'2025-07-27_03-51-08','In round-robin scheduling using a queue, the currently running thread is at the front of the queue. When osYield() is called, the current thread is popped off the front and pushed to the back. The next thread runs then. The major benefits of using a queue are that there is no need to track current_thread or manually wrap around the array each time. It is also much easier to add or remove threads dynamically and more likely what real OS schedulers use. A queue is more flexible and neater design option for a multitasking system. However, a trade-off is that queues add slight overhead in pointer management, especially in limited resource constrained systems. Also, a queue may not work well for some scheduling strategies that need precise timings or deadlines.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,2,'2025-07-27_00-45-42','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s registers(R4-R11) onto the stack, calling osSched() to switch the stack pointer to the next thread, and then restoring that thread’s registers. It finishes by returning to the new thread using PSP. This allows threads to alternate while running.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',4,2,'2025-07-27_03-22-33','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s registers(R4-R11) onto the stack. Then, it calls osSched() to choose the next thread to run and update the PSP to that thread’s stack pointer. After this, it loads the new thread’s registers back from its stack, updates the PSP, and returns from the handler. This ensures that the processor resumes execution in the new thread. This process is needed to preserve and restore each thread’s state during context switching.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',4,2,'2025-07-27_03-51-08','PendSV_starts by using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread’s stack. It saves the thread’s callee-saved registers (R4-R11) onto that stack to preserve its state. Then, it calls osSched() to update the PSP to point to the next thread’s stack, based on the scheduler’s decision. After that, it loads the new thread’s saved registers from its stack, restores the PSP, and exits, resuming the execution in the new thread. This process ensures that each thread can pause and resume correctly without losing data.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,3,'2025-07-27_00-45-42','This might be a bad idea because if one thread has the highest priority, it always runs first and the OS does not provide other threads to run fairly. In this case, the lower priority threads never run, even if they might be important. In a real OS, fairness needs to be balanced with priority where one cannot take over the other.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,3,'2025-07-27_03-22-33','This might be a bad idea because if one thread has the highest priority, it always runs first and the OS does not provide other threads to run fairly. In this case, the lower priority threads never run, even if they might be important. In a real OS, fairness needs to be balanced with priority where one cannot take over the other using priority again or time slicing.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,3,'2025-07-27_03-51-08','This might be a bad idea because if one thread has the highest priority, it always runs first and the OS does not provide other threads to run fairly. In this case, the lower priority threads never run, even if they might be important. In a real OS, fairness needs to be balanced with priority where one cannot take over the other using priority again or time slicing.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,4,'2025-07-27_00-45-42','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_Handler_Main makes the code much easier to read, edit, and debug, especially for logic like handling different SVC cases. The downside is that C functions calls add overhead and can be slower than doing everything directly in low-level assembly code. Also, switching between C and assembly needs to be done carefully to avoid data corruption or crashes. C is easier and safer but assembly is faster and more efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,4,'2025-07-27_03-22-33','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_Handler_Main makes the code much easier to read, edit, and debug, especially for logic like handling different SVC cases. The downside is that C functions calls add overhead and can be slower than doing everything directly in low-level assembly code. Also, switching between C and assembly needs to be done carefully to avoid data corruption or crashes. C is easier and safer for most OS-level logic but assembly is faster and more efficient and is used where precise hardware control is needed.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,4,'2025-07-27_03-51-08','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_Handler_Main makes the code much easier to read, edit, and debug, especially for logic like handling different SVC cases. The downside is that C functions calls add overhead and can be slower than doing everything directly in low-level assembly code. Also, switching between C and assembly needs to be done carefully to avoid data corruption or crashes. C is easier and safer for most OS-level logic but assembly is faster and more efficient and is used where precise hardware control is needed.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,5,'2025-07-27_00-45-42','Yes, the OS will still work if only a single thread like print_continuously that calls osYield() after printing. However, since there are no other threads to switch, the scheduler keeps switching back and forth to the same thread. It doesn’t crash but the context switch is not required so CPU time is wasted to do the same task again and again.\n',0,'s25'),('j3dua',4,5,'2025-07-27_03-22-33','If only a single thread is created that calls osYield(), the OS will still function but no actual context switching will occur because there are no other threads to switch to. When the thread yields, the scheduler runs but just keeps selecting the same thread again. This means the thread will resume exactly where it paused, and the system and continues without crashing, but the call to osYield becomes unnecessary. This highlights the context switches require at least two runnable threads and that in a single threaded system, the scheduler has no effect.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',4,5,'2025-07-27_03-51-08','If only a single thread is created that calls osYield(), the OS will still function but no actual context switching will occur because there are no other threads to switch to. When the thread yields, the scheduler runs but just keeps selecting the same thread again. This means the thread will resume exactly where it paused, and the system and continues without crashing, but the call to osYield becomes unnecessary. This highlights the context switches require at least two runnable threads and that in a single threaded system, the scheduler has no effect.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',5,1,'2025-07-29_01-14-03','To pass an argument into a thread, the user writes their thread function using void* so that it passes in a pointer when calling osCreateThread. The OS builds an initial stack frame for the thread to replicate what the processor expects during a return, which involves storing values like the PC, LR and R0. R0 holds the first function argument. The OS place’s the user’s argument in R0 by pushing it at the correct position on the stack while creating the thread. This works because the first argument is required to be passed in R0, and the processor uses this register to deliver the argument directly when executing.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',5,1,'2025-07-29_03-01-21','To pass an argument into a thread, the user writes their thread function using void* so that it passes in a pointer when calling osCreateThread. The OS builds an initial stack frame for the thread to replicate what the processor expects during a return, which involves storing values like the PC, LR and R0. R0 holds the first function argument. The OS place’s the user’s argument in R0 by pushing it at the correct position on the stack while creating the thread. This works because the first argument is required to be passed in R0, and the processor uses this register to deliver the argument directly when executing. Using a struct gives users flexibility to pass multiple arguments neatly, and the OS needs to handle only one pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',5,2,'2025-07-29_01-14-03','We can’t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because it triggers an SVC interrupt. This breaks the interrupt priority model and may corrupt the stack or crash the system. These handlers should directly request a context switch by setting the PendSV bit. Keeping these minimal ensures safe switching between threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',5,2,'2025-07-29_03-01-21','We can’t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because it triggers an SVC interrupt. This breaks the interrupt priority model and may corrupt the stack or crash the system. These handlers should directly request a context switch by setting the PendSV bit. Keeping these minimal ensures safe switching between threads. Calling osYield from inside these handlers would cause recursive triggering or re-entering interrupts, which may cause the system to crash.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',5,3,'2025-07-29_01-14-03','Setting the clock to 0.1ms would increase the clock speed and resolution allowing the OS to respond faster with more timing control. This can improve the responsiveness for time-sensitive applications. However, it also increases the interrupt load by ten times. This added overhead causes reduced CPU time available and increased power consumption, also might degrade system performance.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',5,3,'2025-07-29_03-01-21','Setting the clock to 0.1ms would increase the clock speed and resolution allowing the OS to respond faster with more timing control. This can improve the responsiveness for time-sensitive applications. However, it also increases the interrupt load by ten times. This added overhead causes reduced CPU time available and increased power consumption, also might degrade system performance.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',5,4,'2025-07-29_01-14-03','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking requires threads to explicitly yield control by calling osYield(), meaning the user is responsible for ensuring fair sharing of CPU. However, pre-emptive multitasking automatically switches threads when their timeslice is over, thus requiring no action from the user. The co-operative multitasking only switches when only when osYield is called thus making it simple and has low overhead. Pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to track the time for each thread using SysTick and perform context switching via interrupts like PendSV when the runtime reaches zero. This adds complexity but makes sure the system is fair and responsive.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',5,4,'2025-07-29_03-01-21','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking requires threads to explicitly yield control by calling osYield(), meaning the user is responsible for ensuring fair sharing of CPU. However, pre-emptive multitasking automatically switches threads when their timeslice is over, thus requiring no action from the user. The co-operative multitasking only switches when only when osYield is called thus making it simple and has low overhead. Pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to track the time for each thread using SysTick and perform context switching via interrupts like PendSV when the runtime reaches zero. This adds complexity but makes sure the system is fair and responsive.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',5,5,'2025-07-29_01-14-03','Yielding and time based pre-emptive switching work together because they both trigger the same context switch mechanism using PendSV. A yielding thread calls osYield which resets its runtime using SVC. The OS monitors each thread’s runtime using SysTick, and when it reaches zero, it triggers a switch itself. Since both methods use the same PendSV infrastructure. They can exist together and can yield themselves or be switched out when their timeslice ends.\n',1,'s25'),('j3dua',5,5,'2025-07-29_03-01-21','Yielding and time based pre-emptive switching work together because they both trigger the same context switch mechanism using PendSV. A yielding thread calls osYield which resets its runtime using SVC. The OS monitors each thread’s runtime using SysTick, and when it reaches zero, it triggers a switch itself. Since both methods use the same PendSV infrastructure. They can exist together and can yield themselves or be switched out when their timeslice ends.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,1,'2025-05-14_15-26-33','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a black box of setup code that lets users go directly to programming on the MCU. It provides a set of APIs to interact with components like the libraries and stacks, which are generic and allow a wide range of uses, letting the user skip having to code this base layer.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,1,'2025-05-21_22-28-24','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a black box of setup code that lets users go directly to programming on the MCU. It provides a set of APIs to interact with components like the libraries and stacks, which are generic and allow a wide range of uses, letting the user skip having to code this base layer.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,1,'2025-06-03_16-51-02','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a black box of setup code that lets users go directly to programming on the MCU. It provides a set of APIs to interact with components like the libraries and stacks, which are generic and allow a wide range of uses, letting the user skip having to code this base layer.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,1,'2025-06-05_15-49-20','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a black box of setup code that lets users go directly to programming on the MCU. It provides a set of APIs to interact with components like the libraries and stacks, which are generic and allow a wide range of uses. Because it is generic, it can be used across many different types of hardware, meaning users can immediately begin coding with the HAL functions without having to worry about how their code interfaces with the hardware – the HAL does that for them, and because it can interact with many devices, user code using the HAL becomes applicable to all of them, which is why it is used in a general embedded project. However, because it is so generic, it is also less optimized compared to an equivalent for a specific device.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,1,'2025-06-21_09-54-06','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a black box of setup code that lets users go directly to programming on the MCU. It provides a set of APIs to interact with components like the libraries and stacks, which are generic and allow a wide range of uses. Because it is generic, it can be used across many different types of hardware, meaning users can immediately begin coding with the HAL functions without having to worry about how their code interfaces with the hardware – the HAL does that for them, and because it can interact with many devices, user code using the HAL becomes applicable to all of them, which is why it is used in a general embedded project. However, because it is so generic, it is also less optimized compared to an equivalent for a specific device.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,1,'2025-06-22_14-45-48','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a black box of setup code that lets users go directly to programming on the MCU. It provides a set of APIs to interact with components like the libraries and stacks, which are generic and allow a wide range of uses. Because it is generic, it can be used across many different types of hardware, meaning users can immediately begin coding with the HAL functions without having to worry about how their code interfaces with the hardware – the HAL does that for them, and because it can interact with many devices, user code using the HAL becomes applicable to all of them, which is why it is used in a general embedded project. However, because it is so generic, it is also less optimized compared to an equivalent for a specific device.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,1,'2025-06-22_21-48-22','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a black box of setup code that lets users go directly to programming on the MCU. It provides a set of APIs to interact with components like the libraries and stacks, which are generic and allow a wide range of uses. Because it is generic, it can be used across many different types of hardware, meaning users can immediately begin coding with the HAL functions without having to worry about how their code interfaces with the hardware – the HAL does that for them, and because it can interact with many devices, user code using the HAL becomes applicable to all of them, which is why it is used in a general embedded project. However, because it is so generic, it is also less optimized compared to an equivalent for a specific device.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,1,'2025-06-22_22-01-37','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a black box of setup code that lets users go directly to programming on the MCU. It provides a set of APIs to interact with components like the libraries and stacks, which are generic and allow a wide range of uses. Because it is generic, it can be used across many different types of hardware, meaning users can immediately begin coding with the HAL functions without having to worry about how their code interfaces with the hardware – the HAL does that for them, and because it can interact with many devices, user code using the HAL becomes applicable to all of them, which is why it is used in a general embedded project. However, because it is so generic, it is also less optimized compared to an equivalent for a specific device.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,1,'2025-06-22_22-19-43','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a black box of setup code that lets users go directly to programming on the MCU. It provides a set of APIs to interact with components like the libraries and stacks, which are generic and allow a wide range of uses. Because it is generic, it can be used across many different types of hardware, meaning users can immediately begin coding with the HAL functions without having to worry about how their code interfaces with the hardware – the HAL does that for them, and because it can interact with many devices, user code using the HAL becomes applicable to all of them, which is why it is used in a general embedded project. However, because it is so generic, it is also less optimized compared to an equivalent for a specific device.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,1,'2025-06-22_22-48-19','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a black box of setup code that lets users go directly to programming on the MCU. It provides a set of APIs to interact with components like the libraries and stacks, which are generic and allow a wide range of uses. Because it is generic, it can be used across many different types of hardware, meaning users can immediately begin coding with the HAL functions without having to worry about how their code interfaces with the hardware – the HAL does that for them, and because it can interact with many devices, user code using the HAL becomes applicable to all of them, which is why it is used in a general embedded project. However, because it is so generic, it is also less optimized compared to an equivalent for a specific device.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,2,'2025-05-14_15-26-33','While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware. The __io_putchar uses a function HAL_URT_Transmit that “puts” a character into the specified output channel (in this case it is UART2, a peripheral set up by the HAL used to print from the MCU to the PC), and is repeatedly called by printf to print all the characters in the string passed to it.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,2,'2025-05-21_22-28-24','While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware. The __io_putchar uses a function HAL_URT_Transmit that “puts” a character into the specified output channel (in this case it is UART2, a peripheral set up by the HAL used to print from the MCU to the PC), and is repeatedly called by printf to print all the characters in the string passed to it.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,2,'2025-06-03_16-51-02','While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware, meaning printf can’t directly interact with hardware by itself. The __io_putchar uses a function HAL_URT_Transmit that “puts” a character into the specified output channel (in this case it is UART2, a peripheral set up by the HAL used to print from the MCU to the PC), and is repeatedly called by printf to print all the characters in the string passed to it. By making this a function, it allows for reusability in the code, so that every time you want to print something you don’t have to call HAL_URT_Transmit each time. A function can also be used across multiple files, so you don’t have to define printf for every file this way.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,2,'2025-06-05_15-49-20','While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware, meaning printf can’t directly interact with hardware by itself. The __io_putchar uses a function HAL_URT_Transmit that “puts” a character into the specified output channel (in this case it is UART2, a peripheral set up by the HAL used to print from the MCU to the PC), and is repeatedly called by printf to print all the characters in the string passed to it. Finally, because the function works using the HAL, the underlying hardware can be changed without needing to change the user code. The HAL provides a generic base to interact with many different types of hardware, which allows this function to still be applicable across many different devices. Without such a function (and without the HAL in general), a hardware-specific version would be needed for each different device, significantly and negatively impacting portability and adaptability.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,2,'2025-06-21_09-54-06','While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware, meaning printf can’t directly interact with hardware by itself. The __io_putchar uses a function HAL_URT_Transmit that “puts” a character into the specified output channel (in this case it is UART2, a peripheral set up by the HAL used to print from the MCU to the PC), and is repeatedly called by printf to print all the characters in the string passed to it. Finally, because the function works using the HAL, the underlying hardware can be changed without needing to change the user code. The HAL provides a generic base to interact with many different types of hardware, which allows this function to still be applicable across many different devices. Without such a function (and without the HAL in general), a hardware-specific version would be needed for each different device, significantly and negatively impacting portability and adaptability.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,2,'2025-06-22_14-45-48','While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware, meaning printf can’t directly interact with hardware by itself. The __io_putchar uses a function HAL_URT_Transmit that “puts” a character into the specified output channel (in this case it is UART2, a peripheral set up by the HAL used to print from the MCU to the PC), and is repeatedly called by printf to print all the characters in the string passed to it. Finally, because the function works using the HAL, the underlying hardware can be changed without needing to change the user code. The HAL provides a generic base to interact with many different types of hardware, which allows this function to still be applicable across many different devices. Without such a function (and without the HAL in general), a hardware-specific version would be needed for each different device, significantly and negatively impacting portability and adaptability.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,2,'2025-06-22_21-48-22','While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware, meaning printf can’t directly interact with hardware by itself. The __io_putchar uses a function HAL_URT_Transmit that “puts” a character into the specified output channel (in this case it is UART2, a peripheral set up by the HAL used to print from the MCU to the PC), and is repeatedly called by printf to print all the characters in the string passed to it. Finally, because the function works using the HAL, the underlying hardware can be changed without needing to change the user code. The HAL provides a generic base to interact with many different types of hardware, which allows this function to still be applicable across many different devices. Without such a function (and without the HAL in general), a hardware-specific version would be needed for each different device, significantly and negatively impacting portability and adaptability.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,2,'2025-06-22_22-01-37','While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware, meaning printf can’t directly interact with hardware by itself. The __io_putchar uses a function HAL_URT_Transmit that “puts” a character into the specified output channel (in this case it is UART2, a peripheral set up by the HAL used to print from the MCU to the PC), and is repeatedly called by printf to print all the characters in the string passed to it. Finally, because the function works using the HAL, the underlying hardware can be changed without needing to change the user code. The HAL provides a generic base to interact with many different types of hardware, which allows this function to still be applicable across many different devices. Without such a function (and without the HAL in general), a hardware-specific version would be needed for each different device, significantly and negatively impacting portability and adaptability.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,2,'2025-06-22_22-19-43','While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware, meaning printf can’t directly interact with hardware by itself. The __io_putchar uses a function HAL_URT_Transmit that “puts” a character into the specified output channel (in this case it is UART2, a peripheral set up by the HAL used to print from the MCU to the PC), and is repeatedly called by printf to print all the characters in the string passed to it. Finally, because the function works using the HAL, the underlying hardware can be changed without needing to change the user code. The HAL provides a generic base to interact with many different types of hardware, which allows this function to still be applicable across many different devices. Without such a function (and without the HAL in general), a hardware-specific version would be needed for each different device, significantly and negatively impacting portability and adaptability.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,2,'2025-06-22_22-48-19','While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware, meaning printf can’t directly interact with hardware by itself. The user must write the __io_putchar function, which serves as an interface between printf and the Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL). The HAL provides a generic set of instructions that can interact with a variety of different hardware – effectively abstracting the hardware from the user. Writing __io_putchar saves you from having to rewrite the HAL functions every time, but it also serves another purpose. Because the HAL works on many different chips, if the underlying hardware were to be changed, the user code wouldn’t need updating, as __io_putchar uses generic HAL functions. If the user instead wrote an implementation of printf specific to the Cortex M4, it would need to be updated every time the hardware changed.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,3,'2025-05-14_15-26-33','The debugger allows us to view many things, such as registers and memory addresses, without the need to add printf statements everywhere. The debugger can also show the changes in memory over time, through the use of breakpoints and steps, which would require even more printf statements to replicate. In this lab we used the debugger to view MSP and PSP (Main and Process Stack Pointers) values, both in variables and in memory.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,3,'2025-05-21_22-28-24','The debugger allows us to view many things, such as registers and memory addresses, without the need to add printf statements everywhere. The debugger can also show the changes in memory over time, through the use of breakpoints and steps, which would require even more printf statements to replicate. In this lab we used the debugger to view MSP and PSP (Main and Process Stack Pointers) values, both in variables and in memory.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,3,'2025-06-03_16-51-02','The debugger allows us to view many things, such as registers and memory addresses, without the need to add printf statements everywhere. The debugger can also show the changes in memory over time, through the use of breakpoints and steps, which would require even more printf statements to replicate. Additionally, as hardware registers are treated differently from regular variables in memory, their values cannot be accessed using printf, and even if it could, it would need to use a register to store the return value of printf, which makes checking the original value of registers using printf impossible. However, the debugger has access to these low-level hardware values such as the hardware registers, and through breakpoints you can see not only their values but also the line-by-line changes, something printf cannot do.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,3,'2025-06-05_15-49-20','The debugger allows us to view many things, such as registers and memory addresses, without the need to add printf statements everywhere. The debugger can also show the changes in memory over time, through the use of breakpoints and steps, which would require even more printf statements to replicate. Additionally, as hardware registers are treated differently from regular variables in memory, their values cannot be accessed using printf, and even if it could, it would need to use a register to store the return value of printf, which makes checking the original value of registers using printf impossible. However, the debugger has access to these low-level hardware values such as the hardware registers, and through breakpoints you can see not only their values but also the line-by-line changes, something printf cannot do.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,3,'2025-06-21_09-54-06','The debugger allows us to view many things, such as registers and memory addresses, without the need to add printf statements everywhere. The debugger can also show the changes in memory over time, through the use of breakpoints and steps, which would require even more printf statements to replicate. Additionally, as hardware registers are treated differently from regular variables in memory, their values cannot be accessed using printf, and even if it could, it would need to use a register to store the return value of printf, which makes checking the original value of registers using printf impossible. However, the debugger has access to these low-level hardware values such as the hardware registers, and through breakpoints you can see not only their values but also the line-by-line changes, something printf cannot do.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,3,'2025-06-22_14-45-48','The debugger allows us to view many things, such as registers and memory addresses, without the need to add printf statements everywhere. The debugger can also show the changes in memory over time, through the use of breakpoints and steps, which would require even more printf statements to replicate. Additionally, as hardware registers are treated differently from regular variables in memory, their values cannot be accessed using printf, and even if it could, it would need to use a register to store the return value of printf, which makes checking the original value of registers using printf impossible. However, the debugger has access to these low-level hardware values such as the hardware registers, and through breakpoints you can see not only their values but also the line-by-line changes, something printf cannot do.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,3,'2025-06-22_21-48-22','The debugger allows us to view many things, such as registers and memory addresses, without the need to add printf st