ntly from regular variables in memory, their values cannot be accessed using printf, and even if it could, it would need to use a register to store the return value of printf, which makes checking the original value of registers using printf impossible. However, the debugger has access to these low-level hardware values such as the hardware registers, and through breakpoints you can see not only their values but also the line-by-line changes, something printf cannot do.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,3,'2025-06-22_22-48-19','The debugger allows us to view many things, such as registers and memory addresses, without the need to add printf statements everywhere. The debugger can also show the changes in memory over time, through the use of breakpoints and steps, which would require even more printf statements to replicate. Additionally, as hardware registers are treated differently from regular variables in memory, their values cannot be accessed using printf, and even if it could, it would need to use a register to store the return value of printf, which makes checking the original value of registers using printf impossible. However, the debugger has access to these low-level hardware values such as the hardware registers, and through breakpoints you can see not only their values but also the line-by-line changes, something printf cannot do.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,4,'2025-05-14_15-26-33','In this lab we assumed the stack is structured with the MSP at the “top” of the stack, taking up some amount of memory, and each of the process stacks (thread stacks) are sequentially below the main stack. Each of the process stacks take up the same amount of memory, which may or may not be the same as the main stack. In this lab we assume that both the main and process stacks take up 0x400 bytes, or 1024 bytes, in memory. As the stack is stored sequentially like an array, by knowing the MSP_init_val, we can add the size of the main stack (0x400) to its start point in memory (MSP_init_val) to find its endpoint, which is also the start point of the first process stack, which is PSP_val.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,4,'2025-05-21_22-28-24','In this lab we assumed the stack is structured with the MSP at the “top” of the stack, taking up some amount of memory, and each of the process stacks (thread stacks) are sequentially below the main stack. Each of the process stacks take up the same amount of memory, which may or may not be the same as the main stack. In this lab we assume that both the main and process stacks take up 0x400 bytes, or 1024 bytes, in memory. As the stack is stored sequentially like an array, by knowing the MSP_init_val, we can add the size of the main stack (0x400) to its start point in memory (MSP_init_val) to find its endpoint, which is also the start point of the first process stack, which is PSP_val.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,4,'2025-06-03_16-51-02','In this lab we assumed the stack is structured with the MSP at the “top” of the stack, taking up some amount of memory, and each of the process stacks (thread stacks) are sequentially below the main stack. Each of the process stacks take up the same amount of memory, which may or may not be the same as the main stack. In this lab we set both the main and process stacks take up 0x400 bytes, or 1024 bytes, in memory. This way, we know the exact size of each stack. As the stack is stored sequentially like an array, by knowing the MSP_init_val, we can add the size of the main stack (0x400) to its start point in memory (MSP_init_val) to find its endpoint, which is also the start point of the first process stack, which is PSP_val.\nFinally, to check if the endpoint value is a valid stack address, you can use __set_PSP and see if the stack pointer register changes to your calculated value.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,4,'2025-06-05_15-49-20','In this lab we assumed the stack is structured with the MSP at the “top” of the stack, taking up some amount of memory, and each of the process stacks (thread stacks) are sequentially below the main stack. Each of the process stacks take up the same amount of memory, which may or may not be the same as the main stack. In this lab we set both the main and process stacks take up 0x400 bytes, or 1024 bytes, in memory. This way, we know the exact size of each stack. As the stack is stored sequentially like an array, by knowing the MSP_init_val, we can add the size of the main stack (0x400) to its start point in memory (MSP_init_val) to find its endpoint, which is also the start point of the first process stack, which is PSP_val.\nFinally, to check if the endpoint value is a valid stack address, you can use __set_PSP and see if the stack pointer register changes to your calculated value.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,4,'2025-06-21_09-54-06','In this lab we assumed the stack is structured with the MSP at the “top” of the stack, taking up some amount of memory, and each of the process stacks (thread stacks) are sequentially below the main stack. Each of the process stacks take up the same amount of memory, which may or may not be the same as the main stack. In this lab we set both the main and process stacks take up 0x400 bytes, or 1024 bytes, in memory. This way, we know the exact size of each stack. As the stack is stored sequentially like an array, by knowing the MSP_init_val, we can add the size of the main stack (0x400) to its start point in memory (MSP_init_val) to find its endpoint, which is also the start point of the first process stack, which is PSP_val.\nFinally, to check if the endpoint value is a valid stack address, you can use __set_PSP and see if the stack pointer register changes to your calculated value.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,4,'2025-06-22_14-45-48','In this lab we assumed the stack is structured with the MSP at the “top” of the stack, taking up some amount of memory, and each of the process stacks (thread stacks) are sequentially below the main stack. Each of the process stacks take up the same amount of memory, which may or may not be the same as the main stack. In this lab we set both the main and process stacks take up 0x400 bytes, or 1024 bytes, in memory. This way, we know the exact size of each stack. As the stack is stored sequentially like an array, by knowing the MSP_init_val, we can add the size of the main stack (0x400) to its start point in memory (MSP_init_val) to find its endpoint, which is also the start point of the first process stack, which is PSP_val.\nFinally, to check if the endpoint value is a valid stack address, you can use __set_PSP and see if the stack pointer register changes to your calculated value.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,4,'2025-06-22_21-48-22','In this lab we assumed the stack is structured with the MSP at the “top” of the stack, taking up some amount of memory, and each of the process stacks (thread stacks) are sequentially below the main stack. Each of the process stacks take up the same amount of memory, which may or may not be the same as the main stack. In this lab we set both the main and process stacks take up 0x400 bytes, or 1024 bytes, in memory. This way, we know the exact size of each stack. As the stack is stored sequentially like an array, by knowing the MSP_init_val, we can add the size of the main stack (0x400) to its start point in memory (MSP_init_val) to find its endpoint, which is also the start point of the first process stack, which is PSP_val.\nFinally, to check if the endpoint value is a valid stack address, you can use __set_PSP and see if the stack pointer register changes to your calculated value.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,4,'2025-06-22_22-01-37','In this lab we assumed the stack is structured with the MSP at the “top” of the stack, taking up some amount of memory, and each of the process stacks (thread stacks) are sequentially below the main stack. Each of the process stacks take up the same amount of memory, which may or may not be the same as the main stack. In this lab we set both the main and process stacks take up 0x400 bytes, or 1024 bytes, in memory. This way, we know the exact size of each stack. As the stack is stored sequentially like an array, by knowing the MSP_init_val, we can add the size of the main stack (0x400) to its start point in memory (MSP_init_val) to find its endpoint, which is also the start point of the first process stack, which is PSP_val.\nFinally, to check if the endpoint value is a valid stack address, you can use __set_PSP and see if the stack pointer register changes to your calculated value.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,4,'2025-06-22_22-19-43','There is a linker file that contains the minimum size of the stack and heap. Suppose you set the minimum size of the main stack to MSS (main stack size). To get a new value for PSP_val, you need to allocate a stack below the main stack, which will become the new process stack. It is important to know the MSP_init_val, as you want this new stack to be directly below, but not overlapping, with the main stack. To get PSP_val, subtract MSS from MSP_init_val. This way, the process stack will not overlap with the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,4,'2025-06-22_22-48-19','There is a linker file that contains the minimum size of the stack and heap. Suppose you set the minimum size of the main stack to MSS (main stack size). To get a new value for PSP_val, you need to allocate a stack below the main stack, which will become the new process stack. It is important to know the MSP_init_val, as you want this new stack to be directly below, but not overlapping, with the main stack. To get PSP_val, subtract MSS from MSP_init_val. This way, the process stack will not overlap with the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,5,'2025-05-14_15-26-33','As each thread stack takes the same amount of memory, you can find the beginning of each of them by adding the size of the main stack to its start point to find the beginning of the thread stacks, which is PSP_val. Since you know the size of each thread stack, you can find the i-th stack by adding (i-1) * (stack memory size) to PSP_val.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,5,'2025-05-21_22-28-24','As each thread stack takes the same amount of memory, you can find the beginning of each of them by adding the size of the main stack to its start point to find the beginning of the thread stacks, which is PSP_val. Since you know the size of each thread stack, you can find the i-th stack by adding (i-1) * (stack memory size) to PSP_val. To check if this is actually one of the process stacks, use the function __set_PSP(PSP_val). This is a function defined by CMSIS (common microcontroller software interface standard), which can be used to manipulate registers. In this case, it sets PSP to the value passed to the function (must be an integer). Finally, switch the active stack from MSP to PSP – to do this, use __set_control(2), another CMSIS function. The “2” parameter represents “10” in binary – in other words, the first bit (the bit that controls the active SP) is set to “1”, which sets the PSP as the active stack. Finally, when you run with the debugger, after this line, the SP register should show the same memory address as your calculated PSP_val – this verifies PSP_val as the correct starting point in memory. Then, to find any stack n from there, you add (n – 1) * (stack memory size).\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,5,'2025-06-03_16-51-02','As each thread stack takes the same amount of memory, you can find the beginning of each of them by adding the size of the main stack to its start point to find the beginning of the thread stacks, which is PSP_val. Since you know the size of each thread stack, you can find the i-th stack by adding (i-1) * (stack memory size) to PSP_val. To check if this is actually one of the process stacks, use the function __set_PSP(PSP_val). This is a function defined by CMSIS (common microcontroller software interface standard), which can be used to manipulate registers. In this case, it sets PSP to the value passed to the function (must be an integer). Finally, switch the active stack from MSP to PSP – to do this, use __set_control(2), another CMSIS function. The “2” parameter represents “10” in binary – in other words, the first bit (the bit that controls the active SP) is set to “1”, which sets the PSP as the active stack. Finally, when you run with the debugger, after this line, the SP register should show the same memory address as your calculated PSP_val – this verifies PSP_val as the correct starting point in memory. Then, to find any stack n from there, you add (n – 1) * (stack memory size).\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,5,'2025-06-05_15-49-20','You can set the minimum size of each of the main and process stacks by editing the linker file. As each thread stack takes the same amount of memory, you can find the beginning of each of them by adding the size of the main stack to its start point to find the beginning of the thread stacks, which is PSP_val. Since you know the size of each thread stack, you can find the i-th stack by adding (i-1) * (stack memory size) to PSP_val. To check if this is actually one of the process stacks, use the function __set_PSP(PSP_val). This is a function defined by CMSIS (common microcontroller software interface standard), which can be used to manipulate registers. In this case, it sets PSP to the value passed to the function (must be an integer). Finally, switch the active stack from MSP to PSP – to do this, use __set_control(2), another CMSIS function. The “2” parameter represents “10” in binary – in other words, the first bit (the bit that controls the active SP) is set to “1”, which sets the PSP as the active stack. Finally, when you run with the debugger, after this line, the SP register should show the same memory address as your calculated PSP_val – this verifies PSP_val as the correct starting point in memory. Then, to find any stack n from there, you add (n – 1) * (stack memory size).\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,5,'2025-06-21_09-54-06','Let N be the number of stacks you want to create. For the Cortex M4, there is a file called “STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld” that sets the minimum stack and heap sizes. Here you can get or set the stack size.\nAs each thread stack takes the same amount of memory, you can find the beginning of each of them by adding the size of the main stack to its start point to find the beginning of the thread stacks, which is PSP_val. Since you know the size of each thread stack, you can find the i-th stack by adding (i-1) * (stack memory size) to PSP_val.\nAt compile time, you can use the known stack size to compute the end of MSP (found by dereferencing the double pointer at 0x0). Compute the start of the N process stack address by adding stack size to the MSP_init_val and store the results in an array, which will be used to check validity. Every time you compute a new stack address, increment your current stack, or change stacks, you can check the array to make sure your address is still within the bounds of the stack you are working on.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,5,'2025-06-22_14-45-48','Let N be the number of stacks you want to create. For the Cortex M4, there is a file called “STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld” that sets the minimum stack and heap sizes. Here you can get and set the stack size. Call the main stack size MSS and the process stack size PSS.\nFirst you need to find the beginning of the main stack by dereferencing the double pointer at 0x0 (call this MSP_INIT_VAL). As each process thread takes the same amount of memory PSS, you can find the pointer to the i-th process stack (ith_PSP) using this equation:\nith_PSP = MSP_INIT_VAL – MSS – i * PSS\nTo ensure that a process stack address is valid, compute the (i+1)th and (i-1)th stack pointer. If the current stack pointer is between the (i+1)th and (i-1)th stack pointers, then you are still in your current stack and working on a valid address.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,5,'2025-06-22_21-48-22','Let N be the number of stacks you want to create. For the Cortex M4, there is a file called “STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld” that sets the minimum stack and heap sizes. Here you can get and set the stack size. Call the main stack size MSS and the process stack size PSS.\nFirst you need to find the beginning of the main stack by dereferencing the double pointer at 0x0 (call this MSP_INIT_VAL). As each process thread takes the same amount of memory PSS, you can find the pointer to the i-th process stack (ith_PSP) using this equation:\nith_PSP = MSP_INIT_VAL – MSS – i * PSS\nTo ensure that a process stack address is valid, at compile time, compute all of your N stack pointer addresses and store them in an array. Then you can check if the current stack pointer i is valid by comparing it with the ith and (i+1)th bounds in the array – since the stack grows upwards, a valid address would be between the start of your array and the start of the next one.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,5,'2025-06-22_22-01-37','Let N be the number of stacks you want to create. For the Cortex M4, there is a file called “STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld” that sets the minimum stack and heap sizes. Here you can get and set the stack size. Call the main stack size MSS and the process stack size PSS.\nFirst you need to find the beginning of the main stack by dereferencing the double pointer at 0x0 (call this MSP_INIT_VAL). As each process thread takes the same amount of memory PSS, you can find the pointer to the i-th process stack (ith_PSP) using this equation:\nith_PSP = MSP_INIT_VAL – MSS – i * PSS\nTo ensure that a process stack address is valid, at compile time, compute all of your N stack pointer addresses and store them in an array. Then you can check if the current stack pointer i is valid by comparing it with the ith and (i+1)th bounds in the array – since the stack grows downwards, a valid address would be between the start of your array and the start of the next one.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',1,5,'2025-06-22_22-19-43','Let N be the number of stacks you want to create. For the Cortex M4, there is a file called “STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld” that sets the minimum stack and heap sizes. Here you can get and set the stack size. Call the main stack size MSS and the process stack size PSS.\nFirst you need to find the beginning of the main stack by dereferencing the double pointer at 0x0 (call this MSP_INIT_VAL). As each process thread takes the same amount of memory PSS, you can find the pointer to the i-th process stack (ith_PSP) using this equation:\nith_PSP = MSP_INIT_VAL – MSS – i * PSS\nTo ensure that a process stack address is valid, at compile time, compute all of your N stack pointer addresses and store them in an array. Then you can check if the current stack pointer i is valid by comparing it with the ith and (i+1)th bounds in the array – since the stack grows downwards, a valid address would be between the start of your array and the start of the next one.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',1,5,'2025-06-22_22-48-19','Let N be the number of stacks you want to create. For the Cortex M4, there is a file called “STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld” that sets the minimum stack and heap sizes. Here you can get and set the stack size. Call the main stack size MSS and the process stack size PSS.\nFirst you need to find the beginning of the main stack by dereferencing the double pointer at 0x0 (call this MSP_INIT_VAL). As each process thread takes the same amount of memory PSS, you can find the pointer to the i-th process stack (ith_PSP) using this equation:\nith_PSP = MSP_INIT_VAL – MSS – i * PSS\nTo ensure that a process stack address is valid, at compile time, compute all of your N stack pointer addresses and store them in an array. Then you can check if the current stack pointer i is valid by comparing it with the ith and (i+1)th bounds in the array – since the stack grows downwards, a valid address would be between the start of your array and the start of the next one.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',2,1,'2025-06-21_10-00-10','The program counter (PC) indicates where a CPU is in its program sequence. Normally it holds the pointer to the next process to be executed in the program sequence, and once a process is completed the PC is incremented. When the PC was changed using an interrupt (supervisor call) to hold the pointer to the print continuously function, the Cortex M4 thought that the instruction running before the interrupt was the print continuously function, and so it immediately ran that instead.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,1,'2025-06-22_14-39-14','The program counter (PC) indicates where a CPU is in its program sequence. Normally it holds the pointer to the next process to be executed in the program sequence, and once a process is completed the PC is incremented. When the PC was changed using an interrupt (supervisor call) to hold the pointer to the print continuously function, the Cortex M4 thought that the instruction running before the interrupt was the print continuously function, and so it immediately ran that instead.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,1,'2025-06-26_14-07-51','The program counter (PC) is used to store the address of the current instruction in memory. Whenever something needs to be run (i.e. a function is called), the address of that instruction will be loaded into PC and the processer will run it. When you manually change PC to hold the pointer to print_continuously, the processor simply executes that instruction – it looks as if you have simply called the function.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',2,1,'2025-06-26_15-35-04','The program counter (PC) is used to store the address of the current instruction in memory. Whenever something needs to be run (i.e. a function is called), the address of that instruction will be loaded into PC and the processer will run it. When you manually change PC to hold the pointer to print_continuously, the processor simply executes that instruction – it looks as if you have simply called the function.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',2,2,'2025-06-21_10-00-10','A system call is a trap made by a program to the Operating System (OS) it is being run on. This may be to access kernel level privileges such as read/write to memory/storage and creation and running of new processes. This will result in a trap (software case for OS interrupt), followed by the OS resolving the interrupt (i.e. completing the read/write request, running a function, etc). When the system call is made, the currently running process is stored in the PC register, and is continued from there when the system call ends, meaning you can change the currently running process by using a system call to edit PC.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,2,'2025-06-22_14-39-14','A system call is a trap made by a program to the Operating System (OS) it is being run on. This may be to access kernel level privileges such as read/write to memory/storage and creation and running of new processes. This will result in a trap (software case for OS interrupt), followed by the OS resolving the interrupt (i.e. completing the read/write request, running a function, etc). When the system call is made, the currently running process is stored in the PC register, and is continued from there when the system call ends, meaning you can change the currently running process by using a system call to edit PC.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,2,'2025-06-26_14-07-51','A system call is a function call triggered when a program requests kernel-level privileges such as disk read/write to the Operating System (OS) it is being run on.\nThis first results in a trap. The OS will prepare to resolve the trap by storing the current state, where the registers, including PC, are saved to the stack, and can be loaded back in to continue the process once the trap is resolved.\nThe OS will then resolve the trap by performing the requested service. This could be a read/write to disk, creation or deletion of files, managing of memory, and more.\nFinally, the OS will load the program context from the stack back into the registers, switch back to user mode, and give control back to the program.\nThis provides a defense for system-level resources, which stops untrusted software from executing without proper permissions.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',2,2,'2025-06-26_15-35-04','A system call is a function call triggered when a program requests kernel-level privileges such as disk read/write to the Operating System (OS) it is being run on.\nThis first results in a trap. The OS will prepare to resolve the trap by storing the current state, where the registers, including PC, are saved to the stack, and can be loaded back in to continue the process once the trap is resolved.\nThe OS will then resolve the trap by performing the requested service. This could be a read/write to disk, creation or deletion of files, managing of memory, and more.\nFinally, the OS will load the program context from the stack back into the registers, switch back to user mode, and give control back to the program.\nThis provides a defense for system-level resources, which stops untrusted software from executing without proper permissions.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',2,3,'2025-06-21_10-00-10','To run a thread, the chip needs to be “tricked” into thinking it had already been running the thread. This requires setting up the thread’s stack and context, which are the values that would have been in the registers had the thread been already running. This is what it means to “set up a thread’s stack,” – to add values to the stack the same way the registers would hold them. Then, the values from this stack can be popped into the hardware registers, and it will be as if the thread had always been running. The stack must have these values pushed on in a particular order, to be popped off into the correct registers.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,3,'2025-06-22_14-39-14','To run a thread, the chip needs to be “tricked” into thinking it had already been running the thread. This requires setting up the thread’s stack and context, which are the values that would have been in the registers had the thread been already running. This is what it means to “set up a thread’s stack,” – to add values to the stack the same way the registers would hold them. Then, the values from this stack can be popped into the hardware registers, and it will be as if the thread had always been running. The stack must have these values pushed on in a particular order, to be popped off into the correct registers.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',2,3,'2025-06-26_14-07-51','To run a thread, the chip needs to be “tricked” into thinking it had already been running the thread. This requires setting up the thread’s stack and context, which are the values that would have been in the registers had the thread been already running. This is what it means to “set up a thread’s stack,” – to add values to the stack the same way the registers would hold them. The stack must have these values pushed on in a particular order, to be popped off into the correct registers – xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and R11 through R4.\nFirst, a new process stack would have to be created, yielding a new value for PSP, which would be filled with the values that would be stored in the registers had the thread already been running. Then, the active stack can be switched to PSP, and a system call can be made to load the values from this stack into the hardware registers, and it will be as if the thread had always been running.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',2,3,'2025-06-26_15-35-04','To run a thread, the chip needs to be “tricked” into thinking it had already been running the thread. This requires setting up the thread’s stack and context, which are the values that would have been in the registers had the thread been already running. This is what it means to “set up a thread’s stack,” – to add values to the stack the same way the registers would hold them. The stack must have these values pushed on in a particular order, to be popped off into the correct registers – xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and R11 through R4.\nFirst, a new process stack would have to be created, yielding a new value for PSP, which would be filled with the values that would be stored in the registers had the thread already been running. Then, the active stack can be switched to PSP, and a system call can be made to load the values from this stack into the hardware registers, and it will be as if the thread had always been running.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',2,4,'2025-06-21_10-00-10','The register values were initially stored in the process stack, which was located by subtracting the size of the main stack from the initial location of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). If PSP wasn’t set as the active stack, the values popped into the registers would’ve been whatever was initialized in the main stack, and with the registers filled with random values, the code would crash.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,4,'2025-06-22_14-39-14','The register values were initially stored in the process stack, which was located by subtracting the size of the main stack from the initial location of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). If PSP wasn’t set as the active stack, the values popped into the registers would’ve been whatever was initialized in the main stack, and with the registers filled with random values, the code would crash.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,4,'2025-06-26_14-07-51','The register values are initialized in the process stack. If PSP wasn’t set as the active stack, the main stack would still be the active stack, and the the values popped into the registers would’ve been whatever was initialized in the main stack. As initialized memory values are not known, the registers would be filled with random values from the main stack, and the code would crash in a hard fault. However, because we set the thread stack as the active stack and set PSP to the first value that needed to be popped into the hardware registers, the registers were filled with the correct values and the thread ran properly.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,4,'2025-06-26_15-35-04','The register values are initialized in the process stack. If PSP wasn’t set as the active stack, the main stack would still be the active stack, with the stack pointer SP still at its current location in the main stack. This would result in our values popped off the stack coming from the main stack rather than what we initialized. Because the main stack is used by the OS, our pop operations performed on that stack would corrupt the memory, resulting in a hard fault. Additionally, even if the code didn’t immediately crash, as initialized memory values are not known, the registers would be filled with random values from the main stack, resulting in a crash.\nHowever, because we set the process stack as the active stack, and set the SP to the first value that needed to be popped into the hardware registers, the registers were filled with the correct values and the thread ran properly.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',2,5,'2025-06-21_10-00-10','The code would hard fault (citation: I tried it). The reason is because we manually changed the PC register to the print_continuously function address, which, to the hardware, is a random address. Since print_continuously only prints once, it returns after, which causes PC to increment normally. However, because it was on a random address which was not part of the actual PC sequence, it increments to an address that doesn’t hold a valid pointer to the next instruction, which results in a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,5,'2025-06-22_14-39-14','The code would hard fault (citation: I tried it). The reason is because we manually changed the PC register to the print_continuously function address, which, to the hardware, is a random address. Since print_continuously only prints once, it returns after, which causes PC to increment normally. However, because it was on a random address which was not part of the actual PC sequence, it increments to an address that doesn’t hold a valid pointer to the next instruction, which results in a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,5,'2025-06-26_14-07-51','The code would hard fault (citation: I tried it). The reason is that when setting up the thread’s context, we set the link register LR to an arbitrary address of 0xA. Normally when a function is called, the function’s address is loaded into PC, and the original address the thread is stored in LR. This way, when the function finishes, the return address in LR can be used to return to the previously running process. However, because we set it to an arbitrary value, when print_continuously finishes, PC is set to an arbitrary value, causing a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',2,5,'2025-06-26_15-35-04','The code would hard fault (citation: I tried it). The reason is that when setting up the thread’s context, we set the value in the stack that would be popped into the link register LR to an arbitrary address of 0xA. Normally when a function is called, the function’s address is loaded into PC, and the original address the thread is stored in LR. This way, when the function finishes, the return address in LR can be used to return to the previously running process. However, when we pop the stack values into the registers to set up the thread context, the LR is set to 0xA, an arbitrary value. Because this return address is not on our active stack, it results in an invalid memory access, causing a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',3,1,'2025-06-27_16-43-47','When the compiler is run, it compiles the individual *.c files, and then the linker links all of the compiled code into one executable. Normally you would have to manually compile and then pass the multiple .c files to the linker, which would link it to main as one executable, but the STM IDE does this automatically.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',3,1,'2025-06-27_19-07-07','There are three main steps in this process: preprocessing, compiling, and linking.\nDuring preprocessing, all commands beginning with a #, such as #define and #include, are processed. For example, with the line #define A, all instances of the variable A are directly text substituted with its defined value. These tokens can also be used to define macros, which are similar to functions, and are also substituted during the processing. A quick note: with #include, angled brackets indicate a system file, and quotation marks indicate a header file from your program.\nNext is compiling, where each individual file is compiled by the compiler, resulting in an assembly language output with extension .s. They are converted by the assembler from assembly into machine code. These files usually have a .o extension.\nFinally, linking is done. This takes all the compiled .o files and links them to resolve external references. For example, if you have a header and source file, the linker will link each header to its definition in source.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',3,1,'2025-06-27_20-18-04','There are three main steps in this process: preprocessing, compiling, and linking.\nDuring preprocessing, all commands beginning with a #, such as #define and #include, are processed. For example, with the line #define A, all instances of the variable A are directly text substituted with its defined value. These tokens can also be used to define macros, which are similar to functions, and are also substituted during the processing. A quick note: with #include, angled brackets indicate a system file, and quotation marks indicate a header file from your program.\nNext is compiling, where each individual file is compiled by the compiler, resulting in an assembly language output with extension .s. Then they are converted by the assembler from assembly into machine code, which is readable by the hardware. These files usually have a .o extension.\nFinally, linking is done. This takes all the compiled .o files and links them to resolve external references. For example, if you have a header and source file, the linker will link each header to its definition in source.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',3,2,'2025-06-27_16-43-47','Ideally a .h file is as light as possible in terms of dependencies. Normally a .c file, the source file, would include the various libraries and utilities it needs to function. However, a .h file includes as little as possible so that when you include the .h in your program, it doesn’t bring modules you don’t want or need, while the source file is compiled separately before linking. This saves memory and time. If you use include in a source file, you also run the risk of multiple definition errors – if one source file A includes another B, and then both are linked to main, there will two definitions of B, one for main and one for A, resulting in a linker error.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',3,2,'2025-06-27_19-07-07','Ideally a .h file is as light as possible in terms of dependencies. Normally a .c file, the source file, would include the various libraries and utilities it needs to function. However, a .h file includes as little as possible so that when you include the .h in your program, it doesn’t bring modules you don’t want or need, while the source file is compiled separately before linking. This saves memory and time. If you use include in a source file, you also run the risk of multiple definition errors – if one source file A includes another B, and then both are linked to main, there will two definitions of B, one for main and one for A, resulting in an error. Because preprocessing directly textually replaces the #include token with the specified code, this will result in the compiler attempting to initialize multiple variables with the same name, or the linker trying to link multiple definitions to the same function name, causing errors. By defining in a .h file and only including that file, you eliminate the risk of duplicate initialization.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',3,2,'2025-06-27_20-18-04','Ideally a .h file is as light as possible in terms of dependencies. Normally a .c file, the source file, would include the various libraries and utilities it needs to function. However, a .h file includes as little as possible so that when you include the .h in your program, it doesn’t bring modules you don’t want or need, while the source file is compiled separately before linking. This saves memory and time. If you use include in a source file, you also run the risk of multiple definition errors – if one source file A includes another B, and then both are linked to main, there will two definitions of B, one for main and one for A, resulting in an error. Because preprocessing directly textually replaces the #include token with the specified code, this will result in the compiler attempting to initialize multiple variables with the same name, or the linker trying to link multiple definitions to the same function name, causing errors. By defining in a .h file and only including that file, you eliminate the risk of duplicate initialization.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',3,3,'2025-06-27_16-43-47','1. Initialize the kernel. This will set up all your needed global variables, such as MSP_INIT_VAL.\n2. Create a thread, including the address of the function you want to run as a parameter. This will set up the context of your thread, which are the values of the registers had it already been running, and will pop them from the stack to the registers in a certain order.\n3. Start the kernel. This will initiate a system call that will run your thread.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',3,3,'2025-06-27_19-07-07','1. Initialize the kernel. This will set up all your needed global variables, such as MSP_INIT_VAL. This can be done with the osKernelInitialize() function, which should be called by the user at the beginning of the program.\n2. Create a thread, including the address of the function you want to run as a parameter. The user must do this using the osCreateThread() function. This will automatically set up the context of your thread, which are the values of the registers had it already been running, and will pop them from the stack to the registers in a certain order.\n3. The user can now start the kernel, using the osKernelStart() function. This will initiate a system call that will run the thread that was just set up by the user.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',3,3,'2025-06-27_20-18-04','The user first needs to write a function to accomplish their task in the main file. Then they only need the address of their function to set up and run a thread – this address will be loaded into PC along with the thread context, which will allow them to run their function on a thread. After writing their function:\n1. Initialize the kernel. This will set up all your needed global variables, such as MSP_INIT_VAL. This can be done with the osKernelInitialize() function, which should be called by the user at the beginning of the program.\n2. Create a thread, including the address of the function you want to run as a parameter. The user must do this using the osCreateThread() function. This will automatically set up the context of your thread, which are the values of the registers had it already been running, and will pop them from the stack to the registers in a certain order.\n3. The user can now start the kernel, using the osKernelStart() function. This will initiate a system call that will run the thread that was just set up by the user.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',3,4,'2025-06-27_16-43-47','The stack pool is a pool of global memory behaving as a stack. This is the pool we allocate our threads to. As each thread is allocated the same amount of space, the size of the process stack, this stack pool can be divided into multiple different “sections,” each containing a different thread. By allocating multiple sections to multiple threads, we can store multiple threads in our OS.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',3,4,'2025-06-27_19-07-07','The stack pool is a pool of dedicated global memory behaving as a stack. The minimum size of the stack pool can be set by the programmer in the linker file. This is the pool we allocate our threads to. As each thread is allocated the same amount of space, the size of the process stack, this stack pool can be divided into multiple different “sections,” each containing a different thread. By allocating multiple sections to multiple threads, we can store multiple threads in our OS. However, the OS itself has a separate stack and heap from the threads it is running on – the main stack is used by the chip, while the process stack is the stack that is divided up to allocate to threads. The OS must keep track of how the process stack is divided, in order to effectively create and allocate new threads to the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',3,4,'2025-06-27_20-18-04','The stack pool is a pool of dedicated global memory behaving as a stack. The minimum size of the stack pool can be set by the programmer in the linker file. This is the pool we allocate our threads to. As each thread is allocated the same amount of space, the size of the process stack, this stack pool can be divided into multiple different “sections,” each containing a different thread. By allocating multiple sections to multiple threads, we can store multiple threads in our OS. However, the OS itself has a separate stack and heap from the threads it is running on – the main stack is used by the chip, while the process stack is the stack that is divided up to allocate to threads. The OS must keep track of how the process stack is divided, in order to effectively create and allocate new threads to the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',3,5,'2025-06-27_16-43-47','The user is only allowed to interact with the stack and set up threads through the functions provided by the kernel file. This provides security, as it stops inexperienced and/or malicious users from editing the memory on their own and possibly corrupting things. It also makes user-side editing much easier, as the kernel provided functions neatly wrap up the code into easy-to-use packages that can be reused.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',3,5,'2025-06-27_19-07-07','The user is only allowed to interact with the stack and set up threads through the functions provided by the kernel file. This provides security, as it stops inexperienced and/or malicious users from editing the memory on their own and possibly corrupting things. It also makes user-side editing much easier, as the kernel provided functions neatly wrap up the code into easy-to-use packages that can be reused.\nThe kernel is able to directly interact with the stack, and indirectly (through assembly) with the hardware – for example, the registers.  The kernel must be able to properly allocate stack memory for threads, and is responsible for doing low-level processes like setting up thread context to run a thread.\nThis separation allows the user to focus on programming and developing applications rather than dealing with issues like memory management, as the kernel takes care of the low-level stuff.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',3,5,'2025-06-27_20-18-04','The user is only allowed to interact with the stack and set up threads through the functions provided by the kernel file. This provides security, as it stops inexperienced and/or malicious users from editing the memory on their own and possibly corrupting things. It also makes user-side editing much easier, as the kernel provided functions neatly wrap up the code into easy-to-use packages that can be reused.\nThe kernel is able to directly interact with the stack, and indirectly (through assembly) with the hardware – for example, the registers.  The kernel must be able to properly allocate stack memory for threads, and is responsible for doing low-level processes like setting up thread context to run a thread.\nThis separation allows the user to focus on programming and developing applications rather than dealing with issues like memory management, as the kernel takes care of the low-level stuff.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,1,'2025-07-28_10-29-04','Round-robin scheduling is implemented so that each thread in the array gets an equal chance at running. When implemented using an array, there must be explicit code to ensure that the thread run order wraps around properly – that is, when the “last” thread runs, the next thread to run is the “first” thread. This can be done using modulus. Implementing this with a queue works well as it removes the need for this extra code. Instead of manually ensuring that the thread run order wraps properly, you can just pop the currently running thread out of the queue, and push it back in when the next thread needs to run. Because of the FIFO properties of the queue, the round-robin order is automatically maintained.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',4,1,'2025-07-28_15-09-29','Round-robin scheduling is implemented so that each thread in the array gets an equal chance at running. When implemented using an array, there must be explicit code to ensure that the thread run order wraps around properly – that is, when the “last” thread runs, the next thread to run is the “first” thread. This can be done using modulus. Implementing this with a queue works well as it removes the need for this extra code. Instead of manually ensuring that the thread run order wraps properly, you can just pop the currently running thread out of the queue, and push it back in when the next thread needs to run. Because of the FIFO properties of the queue, the round-robin order is automatically maintained.\nIn short, if you store your Thread Control Block with an array, you will need to write specific code to make sure that round-robin scheduling wraps properly. On the other hand, if you use a queue, due to the FIFO nature of the queue, you do not need to explicitly implement round-robin scheduling as popping and pushing onto the queue has the exact same behaviour. This allows for slightly simpler code.\nHowever, a queue will require you to define a new class or struct, or another imported library, before you can use it. This will use some extra memory compared to an array. However, because arrays are fixed size and occupy a contiguous memory block, an underfilled array would result in reserved but unused memory. Because queues are dynamic, less memory could be used if you don’t have many threads running, and adding more threads would never be a problem whereas an array would have to resize (which would cause a massive performance and memory hit).\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,1,'2025-07-28_15-44-53','Round-robin scheduling is implemented so that each thread in the array gets an equal chance at running. When implemented using an array, there must be explicit code to ensure that the thread run order wraps around properly – that is, when the “last” thread runs, the next thread to run is the “first” thread. This can be done using modulus. Implementing this with a queue works well as it removes the need for this extra code. Instead of manually ensuring that the thread run order wraps properly, you can just pop the currently running thread out of the queue, and push it back in when the next thread needs to run. Because of the FIFO properties of the queue, the round-robin order is automatically maintained.\nIn short, if you store your Thread Control Block with an array, you will need to write specific code to make sure that round-robin scheduling wraps properly. On the other hand, if you use a queue, due to the FIFO nature of the queue, you do not need to explicitly implement round-robin scheduling as popping and pushing onto the queue has the exact same behaviour. This allows for slightly simpler code.\nHowever, a queue will require you to define a new class or struct, or another imported library, before you can use it. This will use some extra memory compared to an array. However, because arrays are fixed size and occupy a contiguous memory block, an underfilled array would result in reserved but unused memory. Because queues are dynamic, less memory could be used if you don’t have many threads running, and adding more threads would never be a problem whereas an array would have to resize (which would cause a massive performance and memory hit).\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,1,'2025-07-28_16-02-06','Round-robin scheduling is implemented so that each thread in the array gets an equal chance at running. When implemented using an array, there must be explicit code to ensure that the thread run order wraps around properly – that is, when the “last” thread runs, the next thread to run is the “first” thread. This can be done using modulus. Implementing this with a queue works well as it removes the need for this extra code. Instead of manually ensuring that the thread run order wraps properly, you can just pop the currently running thread out of the queue, and push it back in when the next thread needs to run. Because of the FIFO properties of the queue, the round-robin order is automatically maintained.\nIn short, if you store your Thread Control Block with an array, you will need to write specific code to make sure that round-robin scheduling wraps properly. On the other hand, if you use a queue, due to the FIFO nature of the queue, you do not need to explicitly implement round-robin scheduling as popping and pushing onto the queue has the exact same behaviour. This allows for slightly simpler code.\nHowever, a queue will require you to define a new class or struct, or another imported library, before you can use it. This will use some extra memory compared to an array. However, because arrays are fixed size and occupy a contiguous memory block, an underfilled array would result in reserved but unused memory. Because queues are dynamic, less memory could be used if you don’t have many threads running, and adding more threads would never be a problem whereas an array would have to resize (which would cause a massive performance and memory hit).\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,1,'2025-07-30_21-19-55','Round-robin scheduling is implemented so that each thread in the array gets an equal chance at running. When implemented using an array, there must be explicit code to ensure that the thread run order wraps around properly – that is, when the “last” thread runs, the next thread to run is the “first” thread. This can be done using modulus. Implementing this with a queue works well as it removes the need for this extra code. Instead of manually ensuring that the thread run order wraps properly, you can just pop the currently running thread out of the queue, and push it back in when the next thread needs to run. Because of the FIFO properties of the queue, the round-robin order is automatically maintained.\nIn short, if you store your Thread Control Block with an array, you will need to write specific code to make sure that round-robin scheduling wraps properly. On the other hand, if you use a queue, due to the FIFO nature of the queue, you do not need to explicitly implement round-robin scheduling as popping and pushing onto the queue has the exact same behaviour. This allows for slightly simpler code.\nHowever, a queue will require you to define a new class or struct, or another imported library, before you can use it. This will use some extra memory compared to an array. However, because arrays are fixed size and occupy a contiguous memory block, an underfilled array would result in reserved but unused memory. Because queues are dynamic, less memory could be used if you don’t have many threads running, and adding more threads would never be a problem whereas an array would have to resize (which would cause a massive performance and memory hit).\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,2,'2025-07-28_10-29-04','One important thing to note is that the PendSV is set to a lower interrupt priority than SVC. The new asmDump.s file has a definition of PendSV that executes the context switch, and because of the lower priority, it will only do so when SVC is not running.\nSuppose that the variable currentThread stores index of the currently running thread in your global thread array. First, PendSV saves the stack pointer of the current thread in the current thread’s struct. This is the thread struct that was initialized when osCreateThread was called. Next, currentThread is incremented so that it stores the index of the next thread. This can be done by incrementing it by 1, then taking the modulus with the total number of threads (to ensure the index wraps around the array properly). Finally, PSP is set to the new thread’s stack pointer, completing the thread switch. Because the new thread was already created with its context set up in the stack by osCreateThread, the thread context is set up once PSP is switched to that thread’s stack.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',4,2,'2025-07-28_15-09-29','One important thing to note is that the PendSV is set to a lower interrupt priority than SVC. The new asmDump.s file has a definition of PendSV that executes the context switch, and because of the lower priority, it will only do so when SVC is not running.\nSuppose that the variable currentThread stores index of the currently running thread in your global thread array. First, PendSV saves the stack pointer of the current thread in the current thread’s struct, and then pushes the registers R4-R11 onto the stack to save the state of the current thread. This is the thread struct and stack that was initialized when osCreateThread was called. Next, the function osSched is called to ready the next thread. currentThread is incremented so that it stores the index of the next thread. This is done by incrementing it by 1, then taking the modulus with the total number of threads (to ensure the index wraps around the array properly). Finally, PSP is set to the new thread’s stack pointer, and the context registers R4-R11 are popped off the stack into the registers, completing the thread switch.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',4,2,'2025-07-28_15-44-53','One important thing to note is that the PendSV is set to a lower interrupt priority than SVC. The new asmDump.s file has a definition of PendSV that executes the context switch, and because of the lower priority, it will only do so when SVC is not running.\nSuppose that the variable currentThread stores index of the currently running thread in your global thread array. First, PendSV reads PSP and saves the stack pointer of the current thread in the current thread’s struct, and then pushes the registers R4-R11 onto the stack to save the state of the current thread. This is the thread struct and stack that was initialized when osCreateThread was called. Next, the function osSched is called to ready the next thread. currentThread is incremented so that it stores the index of the next thread. This is done by incrementing it by 1, then taking the modulus with the total number of threads (to ensure the index wraps around the array properly). Finally, the new thread’s stack pointer is obtained from its struct and PSP is set to it, and by reading the value of PSP, you can locate the values of the context registers R4-R11 on the stack, which can then be popped off into the hardware registers and the thread run.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,2,'2025-07-28_16-02-06','One important thing to note is that the PendSV is set to a lower interrupt priority than SVC. The new asmDump.s file has a definition of PendSV that executes the context switch, and because of the lower priority, it will only do so when SVC is not running.\nSuppose that the variable currentThread stores index of the currently running thread in your global thread array. First, PendSV reads PSP and saves the stack pointer of the current thread in the current thread’s struct, and then pushes the registers R4-R11 onto the stack to save the state of the current thread. This is the thread struct and stack that was initialized when osCreateThread was called. Next, the function osSched is called to ready the next thread. currentThread is incremented so that it stores the index of the next thread. This is done by incrementing it by 1, then taking the modulus with the total number of threads (to ensure the index wraps around the array properly). Finally, the new thread’s stack pointer is obtained from its struct and PSP is set to it, and by reading the value of PSP, you can locate the values of the context registers R4-R11 on the stack, which can then be popped off into the hardware registers and the thread run.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,2,'2025-07-30_21-19-55','One important thing to note is that the PendSV is set to a lower interrupt priority than SVC. The new asmDump.s file has a definition of PendSV that executes the context switch, and because of the lower priority, it will only do so when SVC is not running.\nSuppose that the variable currentThread stores index of the currently running thread in your global thread array. First, PendSV reads PSP and saves the stack pointer of the current thread in the current thread’s struct, and then pushes the registers R4-R11 onto the stack to save the state of the current thread. This is the thread struct and stack that was initialized when osCreateThread was called. Next, the function osSched is called to ready the next thread. currentThread is incremented so that it stores the index of the next thread. This is done by incrementing it by 1, then taking the modulus with the total number of threads (to ensure the index wraps around the array properly). Finally, the new thread’s stack pointer is obtained from its struct and PSP is set to it, and by reading the value of PSP, you can locate the values of the context registers R4-R11 on the stack, which can then be popped off into the hardware registers and the thread run.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,3,'2025-07-28_10-29-04','If each thread were to have a set priority, the higher priority threads would always be running as the scheduler always chooses them over the lower priority threads. This means that the OS will stay locked running only one thread, while the other threads are created and are taking up memory but unable to run.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,3,'2025-07-28_15-09-29','If each thread were to have a set priority, the higher priority threads would always be running as the scheduler always chooses them over the lower priority threads. This means that the OS will stay locked running only one thread, while the other threads are created and are taking up memory but unable to run. The purpose of round-robin scheduling with multiple threads is to avoid the CPU getting stuck on any one thread (i.e. if one thread is waiting for an input) so that other threads can still progress even if one is stopped. This TCB priority system defeats the purpose of multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,3,'2025-07-28_15-44-53','If each thread were to have a set priority, the higher priority threads would always be running as the scheduler always chooses them over the lower priority threads. This means that the OS will stay locked running only one thread, while the other threads are created and are taking up memory but unable to run. The purpose of round-robin scheduling with multiple threads is to avoid the CPU getting stuck on any one thread (i.e. if one thread is waiting for an input) so that other threads can still progress even if one is stopped. This TCB priority system defeats the purpose of multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,3,'2025-07-28_16-02-06','If each thread were to have a set priority, the higher priority threads would always be running as the scheduler always chooses them over the lower priority threads. This means that the OS will stay locked running only one thread, while the other threads are created and are taking up memory but unable to run. The purpose of round-robin scheduling with multiple threads is to avoid the CPU getting stuck on any one thread (i.e. if one thread is waiting for an input) so that other threads can still progress even if one is stopped. This TCB priority system defeats the purpose of multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,3,'2025-07-30_21-19-55','If each thread were to have a set priority, the higher priority threads would always be running as the scheduler always chooses them over the lower priority threads. This means that the OS will stay locked running only one thread, while the other threads are created and are taking up memory but unable to run. The purpose of round-robin scheduling with multiple threads is to avoid the CPU getting stuck on any one thread (i.e. if one thread is waiting for an input) so that other threads can still progress even if one is stopped. This TCB priority system defeats the purpose of multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,4,'2025-07-28_10-29-04','Assembly is a significantly lower level language than C is, meaning it is closer to machine code than human language and thus is difficult to program in. By calling C functions in the assembly code, we enable ourselves to write these functions in the C language, which is much easier and more convenient. C code is also easier to debug, and is portable to other devices with little to no modification. Additionally, in C you can make use of imported libraries to assist with your project. However, assembly code does execute faster, and it has direct access to things like memory and registers, whereas C has to dereference known preinitialized pointers such as 0x0 and work from there.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,4,'2025-07-28_15-09-29','Assembly is a significantly lower level language than C is, meaning it is closer to machine code than human language and thus is difficult to program in. By calling C functions in the assembly code, we enable ourselves to write these functions in the C language, which is much easier and more convenient. C code is also easier to debug, and is portable to other devices with little to no modification. Additionally, in C you can make use of imported libraries to assist with your project. However, assembly code does execute faster, and it has direct access to things like memory and registers, whereas C has to dereference known preinitialized pointers such as 0x0 and work from there.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,4,'2025-07-28_15-44-53','Assembly is a significantly lower level language than C is, meaning it is closer to machine code than human language and thus is difficult to program in. By calling C functions in the assembly code, we enable ourselves to write these functions in the C language, which is much easier and more convenient. C code is also easier to debug, and is portable to other devices with little to no modification. Additionally, in C you can make use of imported libraries to assist with your project. However, assembly code does execute faster, and it has direct access to things like memory and registers, whereas C has to dereference known preinitialized pointers such as 0x0 and work from there.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,4,'2025-07-28_16-02-06','Assembly is a significantly lower level language than C is, meaning it is closer to machine code than human language and thus is difficult to program in. By calling C functions in the assembly code, we enable ourselves to write these functions in the C language, which is much easier and more convenient. C code is also easier to debug, and is portable to other devices with little to no modification. Additionally, in C you can make use of imported libraries to assist with your project. However, assembly code does execute faster, and it has direct access to things like memory and registers, whereas C has to dereference known preinitialized pointers such as 0x0 and work from there.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,4,'2025-07-30_21-19-55','Assembly is a significantly lower level language than C is, meaning it is closer to machine code than human language and thus is difficult to program in. By calling C functions in the assembly code, we enable ourselves to write these functions in the C language, which is much easier and more convenient. C code is also easier to debug, and is portable to other devices with little to no modification. Additionally, in C you can make use of imported libraries to assist with your project. However, assembly code does execute faster, and it has direct access to things like memory and registers, whereas C has to dereference known preinitialized pointers such as 0x0 and work from there.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,5,'2025-07-28_10-29-04','Yes. The scheduler is designed to run all the threads in order. If there is only one thread, once it has finished PendSV will run and increment the index to store the next thread, however, because of the round-robin scheduling, the new index will be the same as the old one, and the thread will just be run again.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,5,'2025-07-28_15-09-29','Yes, the OS will still work. The scheduler is designed to run all the threads in order. If there is only one thread, once it has finished, PendSV will run and increment the index to store the next thread, however, because of the round-robin scheduling, the new index will be the same as the old one, and the thread will just be run again.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,5,'2025-07-28_15-44-53','Yes, the OS will still work. The scheduler is designed to run all the threads in order. If there is only one thread, once it has finished, PendSV will run and increment the index to store the next thread, however, because of the round-robin scheduling, the new index will be the same as the old one, and the thread will just be run again.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',4,5,'2025-07-28_16-02-06','Yes, the OS will still work. The scheduler is designed to run all the threads in order. If there is only one thread, once it has finished, it will yield, allowing PendSV to run. PendSV will then increment the index to store the next thread, however, because of the round-robin scheduling, the new index will be the same as the old one, and the same thread will just be run again.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',4,5,'2025-07-30_21-19-55','Yes, the OS will still work. The scheduler is designed to run all the threads in order. If there is only one thread, once it has finished, it will yield, allowing PendSV to run. PendSV will then increment the index to store the next thread, however, because of the round-robin scheduling, the new index will be the same as the old one, and the same thread will just be run again.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,1,'2025-07-30_16-10-01','First, the user must prepare their input – if it is a primitive data type, no further preparation is required, but for multiple inputs the user must wrap them in a struct, and get a void pointer that points to this input. Let this struct of inputs be called userParams. Then, the user must create their thread by calling osCreateThread, while passing the pointer to the function they want to run and also the pointer to userParams.\nThe OS runs osCreateThread, creating a thread with the user-specified function pointer, and passes the pointer to the userParams struct to the function, which will dereference it and extract the inputs. Finally, when the thread is run, the parameters will be set up in R0 and the function will run as if the arguments had been passed normally.\nThis works because R0-R3 are registers used to store function arguments, and so if set up with the other registers in the context of a thread, that thread function will run as if the arguments had been normally passed to the function.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',5,1,'2025-07-30_16-43-10','First, the user must prepare their input – if it is a primitive data type, no further preparation is required, but for multiple inputs the user must wrap them in a struct, and get a void pointer that points to this input. Let this struct of inputs be called userParams. Then, the user must create their thread by calling osCreateThread, while passing the pointer to the function they want to run and also the pointer to userParams.\nThe OS runs osCreateThread, creating a thread with the user-specified function pointer, and passes the pointer to the userParams struct to the function, which will dereference it and extract the inputs. Finally, when the thread is run, the OS stores the pointer to userParams in R0 on the stack, which is popped into the correct register, and the function will run as if the arguments had been passed normally.\nThis works because R0-R3 are registers used to store function arguments, and so if set up with the other registers in the context of a thread, that thread function will run as if the arguments had been normally passed to the function.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,1,'2025-07-30_20-26-31','First, the user must prepare their input – if it is a primitive data type, no further preparation is required, but for multiple inputs the user must wrap them in a struct, and get a void pointer that points to this input. Let this struct of inputs be called userParams. Then, the user must create their thread by calling osCreateThread, while passing the pointer to the function they want to run and also the pointer to userParams.\nThe OS runs osCreateThread, creating a thread with the user-specified function pointer, and passes the pointer to the userParams struct to the function, which will dereference it and extract the inputs. Finally, when the thread is run, the OS stores the pointer to userParams in R0 on the stack, which is popped into the correct register, and the function will run as if the arguments had been passed normally.\nThis works because R0-R3 are registers used to store function arguments, and so if set up with the other registers in the context of a thread, that thread function will run as if the arguments had been normally passed to the function.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,2,'2025-07-30_16-10-01','osYield is a function that is called at the end of a thread execution – it triggers the SVC case for the context switch, which is PendSV in our case. Calling osYield would trigger a SVC interrupt, which is a high priority interrupt, and if called inside of a context-switching function, it could interrupt it mid-switch, resulting in incomplete context switching. The osYield interrupt itself would then run, messing up memory further.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',5,2,'2025-07-30_16-43-10','osYield is a function that is called at the end of a thread execution – it triggers the SVC case for the context switch, which is PendSV in our case. Suppose that a context-switch is in progress, and the next thread is being prepared such that some of its register values have been loaded onto the stack. Calling osYield would trigger a SVC interrupt, which is a high priority interrupt, and if called inside of a context-switching function, it could interrupt it mid-switch, resulting in stack corruption. This kind of memory corruption would lead to a hard fault, as once everything has run, invalid values will have been popped into the registers, including the PC register, resulting in the execution of invalid instructions.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,2,'2025-07-30_20-26-31','osYield is a function that is called at the end of a thread execution – it triggers the SVC case for the context switch, which is PendSV in our case. Suppose that a context-switch is in progress, and the next thread is being prepared such that some of its register values have been loaded onto the stack. Calling osYield would trigger a SVC interrupt, which is a high priority interrupt, and if called inside of a context-switching function, it could interrupt it mid-switch, resulting in stack corruption. This kind of memory corruption would lead to a hard fault, as once everything has run, invalid values will have been popped into the registers, including the PC register, resulting in the execution of invalid instructions.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,3,'2025-07-30_16-10-01','Benefits: A higher precision fundamental OS clock means the OS can respond to events more quickly. Additionally, compared to the 1ms clock resolution OS, it can respond to events that last shorter than 1ms, as long as they are longer than 0.1 ms. In a high-speed application such as safety stops, this may be necessary and works better than the 1ms clock OS resolution.\nDrawbacks: An OS with a fundamental clock resolution of 0.1 ms will have 10 times the sysTick interrupts than one with a clock resolution of 1 ms. More interrupts will require more processing time to manage, and so this higher precision comes with the cost of increased processer load.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,3,'2025-07-30_16-43-10','Benefits: A higher precision fundamental OS clock means the OS can respond to events more quickly. Additionally, compared to the 1ms clock resolution OS, it can respond to events that last shorter than 1ms, as long as they are longer than 0.1 ms. In a high-speed application such as safety breaks on a table saw, this may be necessary and works better than the 1ms clock OS resolution.\nDrawbacks: An OS with a fundamental clock resolution of 0.1 ms will have 10 times the sysTick interrupts than one with a clock resolution of 1 ms. More interrupts will require more processing time to manage, and so this higher precision comes with the cost of increased processer load.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,3,'2025-07-30_20-26-31','Benefits: A higher precision fundamental OS clock means the OS can respond to events more quickly. Additionally, compared to the 1ms clock resolution OS, it can respond to events that last shorter than 1ms, as long as they are longer than 0.1 ms. In a high-speed application such as safety breaks on a table saw, this may be necessary and works better than the 1ms clock OS resolution.\nDrawbacks: An OS with a fundamental clock resolution of 0.1 ms will have 10 times the sysTick interrupts than one with a clock resolution of 1 ms. More interrupts will require more processing time to manage, and so this higher precision comes with the cost of increased processer load.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,4,'2025-07-30_16-10-01','In the lab, pre-emptive multitasking is implemented. This assigns either a default or user-specified time value to each thread, and each thread can only run for the duration of this value. If a thread has a duration of, say 5ms, but it finishes its task in 3 ms, it can voluntarily yield to the OS. However, if it has not finished its task within that 5ms, the OS will forcefully pause the thread and load another thread using round-robin scheduling.\nCo-operative multitasking allows threads to decide when and for how long they want to run. The OS no longer schedules thread changes, and threads can occupy processer time for as long as they want. These threads yield once their task is complete or when they want to yield.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,4,'2025-07-30_16-43-10','In the lab, pre-emptive multitasking is implemented. This assigns either a default or user-specified time value to each thread, and each thread can only run for the duration of this value. If a thread has a duration of, say 5ms, but it finishes its task in 3 ms, it can voluntarily yield to the OS. However, if it has not finished its task within that 5ms, the OS will forcefully pause the thread and load another thread using round-robin scheduling. The OS does this through the function osYield, which executes an SVC to trigger pendSV. As this is a low priority interrupt, pendSV runs once no other interrupts are running, and it saves the state of the thread to the thread’s metadata struct and loads in another thread.\nCo-operative multitasking allows threads to decide when and for how long they want to run. The OS no longer schedules thread changes, and threads can occupy processer time for as long as they want. These threads yield once their task is complete or when they want to yield. However, this can result in processer locking, as there is nothing stopping one thread from reserving the processor for an indefinite period of time.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,4,'2025-07-30_20-26-31','In the lab, pre-emptive multitasking is implemented. This assigns either a default or user-specified time value to each thread, and each thread can only run for the duration of this value. If a thread has a duration of, say 5ms, but it finishes its task in 3 ms, it can voluntarily yield to the OS. However, if it has not finished its task within that 5ms, the OS will forcefully pause the thread and load another thread using round-robin scheduling. The OS does this through the function osYield, which executes an SVC to trigger pendSV. As this is a low priority interrupt, pendSV runs once no other interrupts are running, and it saves the state of the thread to the thread’s metadata struct and loads in another thread.\nCo-operative multitasking allows threads to decide when and for how long they want to run. The OS no longer schedules thread changes, and threads can occupy processer time for as long as they want. These threads yield once their task is complete or when they want to yield. However, this can result in processer locking, as there is nothing stopping one thread from reserving the processor for an indefinite period of time.\n',1,'s25'),('j3hou',5,5,'2025-07-30_16-10-01','The yielding thread voluntarily yields when it is done its task, or is forced to yield by the OS due to pre-emptive scheduling. The other thread does not yield and the yielding thread can only run again when the OS forces the context switch after the non-yielding thread’s scheduled time runs out. Because of the pre-emptive context switching in the OS, both types of thread are able to work together.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',5,5,'2025-07-30_16-43-10','The yielding thread voluntarily yields when it is done its task by calling osYield, or is forced to yield by the OS which calls osYield itself. The other thread does not yield and the first thread can only run again when the OS uses osYield forces the context switch after the non-yielding thread’s scheduled time runs out. osYield stores the thread’s current state in the thread metadata struct, and then switches the registers to the next thread to run in round-robin scheduling. Because the thread state is saved, it is essentially paused and can continue running when it is its turn again. Because of the pre-emptive context switching in the OS, both types of thread are able to work together.\n',0,'s25'),('j3hou',5,5,'2025-07-30_20-26-31','The yielding thread voluntarily yields when it is done its task by calling osYield (which triggers pendSV), or is forced to yield by the OS which calls osYield itself. The other thread does not yield and the first thread can only run again when pendSV forces the context switch after the non-yielding thread’s scheduled time runs out. pendSV stores the thread’s current state in the thread metadata struct, and then switches the registers to the next thread to run in round-robin scheduling. Because the thread state is saved, it is essentially paused and can continue running when it is its turn again. Because of the pre-emptive context switching in the OS, both types of thread are able to work together.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,1,'2025-05-15_11-00-18','',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,1,'2025-05-15_11-03-48','The HAL is a Hardware Abstraction Layer. In our case, it is a library provided by STMicroelectronics to simplify the connection between a piece of hardware the the operating system. It does all the mundane and complex tasks such as configuring registers or other hardware and allows the programmer to focus on the high-level abstract details. It also makes it easier to work with peripherals like UART and GPIO, which are communication protocols. It’s kind of like a helper robot that does everything you tell it to do, so you don’t have to know exactly how to do those unnecessary tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,1,'2025-05-17_16-01-30','The HAL is a Hardware Abstraction Layer. In our case, it is a library provided by STMicroelectronics to simplify the connection between a piece of hardware the the operating system. It does all the mundane and complex tasks such as configuring registers or other hardware and allows the programmer to focus on the high-level abstract details. It also makes it easier to work with peripherals like UART and GPIO, which are communication protocols. It’s kind of like a helper robot that does everything you tell it to do, so you don’t have to know exactly how to do those unnecessary tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,1,'2025-05-17_16-36-53','The HAL is a Hardware Abstraction Layer. In our case, it is a library provided by STMicroelectronics to simplify the connection between a piece of hardware the the operating system. It does all the mundane and complex tasks such as configuring registers or other hardware and allows the programmer to focus on the high-level abstract details. It also makes it easier to work with peripherals like UART and GPIO, which are communication protocols. It’s kind of like a helper robot that does everything you tell it to do, so you don’t have to know exactly how to do those unnecessary tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,2,'2025-05-15_11-00-18','',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,2,'2025-05-15_11-03-48','Generally, the __io_putchar function is a function that prints out a singular character to the console, but in our case is for outputting a character to the UART. The function tells the computer how to send each character, one at a time. When the function printf is used on an embedded system, it goes through the io_putchar function. This is done automatically so you don’t have to program the HAL_UART_Transmit function yourself everytime you want to print out a character.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,2,'2025-05-17_16-01-30','Generally, the __io_putchar function is a function that prints out a singular character to the console, but in our case is for outputting a character to the UART. The function tells the computer how to send each character, one at a time. When the function printf is used on an embedded system, it goes through the io_putchar function. This is done automatically so you don’t have to program the HAL_UART_Transmit function yourself everytime you want to print out a character. The printf function is really important for everyday use, both inside and outside the realm of microcontrollers. Having this function allows for printf to work – allowing text to be displayed on a wide variety of devices, controllers, and platforms. If putchar doesn’t work, printf also won’t work.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,2,'2025-05-17_16-36-53','Generally, the __io_putchar function is a function that prints out a singular character to the console, but in our case is for outputting a character to the UART. The function tells the computer how to send each character, one at a time. When the function printf is used on an embedded system, it goes through the io_putchar function. This is done automatically so you don’t have to program the HAL_UART_Transmit function yourself everytime you want to print out a character. The printf function is really important for everyday use, both inside and outside the realm of microcontrollers. Having this function allows for printf to work – allowing text to be displayed on a wide variety of devices, controllers, and platforms. If putchar doesn’t work, printf also won’t work.\nDifferent projects or hardware platforms require different implementations of __io_putchar. For example, one might send characters over UART, while another might use USB or a different communication protocol. If __io_putchar is not correctly implemented for the target hardware, printf won’t be able to display any output, making debugging and communication difficult or impossible.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,3,'2025-05-15_11-00-18','',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,3,'2025-05-15_11-03-48','The debugger was used in this lab to ensure the methodology and the logic of our code works. The debugger allows the user to go through the code line-by-line and see what is going on in the memory. If you just use printf to make sure it worked, you might have the correct final answer, but the steps may not necessarily be correct. In this course, often the result is simple, but ensuring the memory correctly can only be done through the debugger. In our case, we used the debugger to ensure the correct switch from the MSP stack to the PSP stack.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,3,'2025-05-17_16-01-30','The debugger was used in this lab to ensure the methodology and the logic of our code works. The debugger allows the user to go through the code line-by-line and see what is going on in the memory. If you just use printf to make sure it worked, you might have the correct final answer, but the steps may not necessarily be correct. In this course, often the result is simple, but ensuring the memory correctly can only be done through the debugger. In our case, we used the debugger to ensure the correct switch from the MSP stack to the PSP stack, by looking at the register bits on the debugger window.\nThe debugger can be expanded for further applications through the use of registers. Being able to track different functions of a pointer as it changes can be extremely valuable, as compared to printf. This is real-time inspection, or the ability to evaluate a process as it happens, instead of after a brief period of time.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,3,'2025-05-17_16-36-53','The debugger was used in this lab to ensure the methodology and the logic of our code works. The debugger allows the user to go through the code line-by-line and see what is going on in the memory. If you just use printf to make sure it worked, you might have the correct final answer, but the steps may not necessarily be correct. In this course, often the result is simple, but ensuring the memory correctly can only be done through the debugger. In our case, we used the debugger to ensure the correct switch from the MSP stack to the PSP stack, by looking at the register bits on the debugger window.\nThe debugger can be expanded for further applications through the use of registers. Being able to track different functions of a pointer as it changes can be extremely valuable, as compared to printf. This is real-time inspection, or the ability to evaluate a process as it happens, instead of after a brief period of time. Furthermore, printf introduces delay and overhead, which can interfere with timing or alter behavior in embedded systems. The debugger, in contrast, allows us to pause execution, inspect register contents directly, and step through code without altering program flow. This was crucial in verifying that the processor correctly switched from the MSP to the PSP, which can only be confirmed by watching register values update live—not just by checking a final result.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,4,'2025-05-15_11-00-18','',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,4,'2025-05-15_11-03-48','The stack allocation works with the MSP on the top of the stack, and the PSP section below it. Once the value for MSP_init_val is obtained, the PSP_val is obtained by offsetting the memory. This is done explicitly by subtracting the first address of the MSP in the memory by a certain space of memory to reach the PSP address. It is crucial to know the initial location of the MSP to work, as without a reference, you wouldn’t know where to start in the memory. Once you have the MSP, you can use it as a gudie to find the PSP.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,4,'2025-05-17_16-01-30','The stack allocation works with the MSP on the top of the stack, and the PSP section below it. Once the value for MSP_init_val is obtained, the PSP_val is obtained by offsetting the memory. This is done explicitly by subtracting the first address of the MSP in the memory by a fixed size of memory to reach the PSP address. In our case, it is 400 bytes of memory. It is crucial to know the initial location of the MSP to work, as without a reference, you wouldn’t know where to start in the memory. Once you have the MSP, you can use it as a gudie to find the PSP.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,4,'2025-05-17_16-36-53','The stack allocation works with the MSP on the top of the stack, and the PSP section below it. Once the value for MSP_init_val is obtained, the PSP_val is obtained by offsetting the memory. This is done explicitly by subtracting the first address of the MSP in the memory by a fixed size of memory to reach the PSP address. In our case, it is 400 bytes of memory. It is crucial to know the initial location of the MSP to work, as without a reference, you wouldn’t know where to start in the memory. Once you have the MSP, you can use it as a gudie to find the PSP.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,5,'2025-05-15_11-00-18','',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,5,'2025-05-15_11-03-48','If you wanted the same stack allocation method to make new stacks, you would do the same process as you would to find the first PSP stack. Given the number of stacks, you would create new variables (PSP_val2, PSP_val3.. and so on), and you would offset them by the same amount in the memory. In a practical sense, you would subtract the size of the stack from the first memory address to get the starting address of the second stack, and so on. After storing these variables, you have the starting value of the entire stack with the MSP and PSP stacks.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,5,'2025-05-17_16-01-30','If you wanted the same stack allocation method to make new stacks, you would do the same process as you would to find the first PSP stack. Given the number of stacks, you would create new variables (PSP_val2, PSP_val3.. and so on), and you would offset them by the same amount in the memory. In a practical sense, you would subtract the size of the stack from the first memory address to get the starting address of the second stack, and so on. After storing these variables, you have the starting value of the entire stack with the MSP and PSP stacks.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',1,5,'2025-05-17_16-36-53','If you wanted the same stack allocation method to make new stacks, you would do the same process as you would to find the first PSP stack. Given the number of stacks, you would create new variables (PSP_val2, PSP_val3.. and so on), and you would offset them by the same amount in the memory. In a practical sense, you would subtract the size of the stack from the first memory address to get the starting address of the second stack, and so on. After storing these variables, you have the starting value of the entire stack with the MSP and PSP stacks. Stack alignment is critical on Cortex-M4, as each stack must be 8-byte aligned to avoid faults. The stack method must align addresses properly, check bounds, and prevent overlaps.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',2,1,'2025-05-29_10-53-47','PC (Program Counter) is a special register within the CPU that stores the next execution address for the CPU.  When we manipulated the address stored at PC, the computer no longer worked on its own – we forced it to go to the destination we wanted. In this case we wanted to “reroute” it to our own function: print_continuously. We do this by using __asm, through a function called jump_assembly. The code moves the value of the input R0 into PC through the manipulation of the OS. When the program was run, the CPU followed the pointer in PC which pointed to our print_continuously function. So, as a result, the output in the console showed “Hello PC!”, which confirmed that our function pointer was successful.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',2,1,'2025-05-29_20-34-50','PC (Program Counter) is a special register within the CPU that stores the address of the next instruction to execute for the CPU. In the ARM Cortex-M4 architecture, as soon as PC is updated, the CPU immediately begins fetching and executing instructions from the new address. When we manipulated the address stored at PC, the computer no longer worked on its own – we forced it to go to the destination we wanted. In this case we wanted to “reroute” it to our own function: print_continuously. We do this by using (__asm(“MOV PC, R0”), through a function called jump_assembly. The code moves the value of the input R0 into PC through the manipulation of the OS. This directly updates the PC with the address of our function print_continuously. This immediately caused the rerouting of the execution. On the very next cycle, the CPU followed the pointer in PC which pointed to our print_continuously function. So, as a result, the output in the console showed “Hello PC!”, which confirmed that our function pointer was successful.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',2,2,'2025-05-29_10-53-47','In short, a system call is a request made by a program to the OS. This is an interrupt to the program. In most cases this task involves the OS and requests some sort of action from the OS. This is done because the user (us) doesn’t usually have privileged access to the OS. Once we use system calls, we’re able to request tasks from the OS or the kernel, which has privileges and access to things we don’t. In our lab we used SVC_Handler, which is a function that invokes the supervisor call. Once run, the OS follows this call and does what its supposed to. We created a function called print_success, which called “SVC #0” through the main method. The OS follows the SVC instruction and goes to the function SVC Handler to see what to do next. Through a case structure implemented by the user, the 0 case goes to a line which prints “Success!” through printf. This showed how the SVC acted as a middleman between us and the OS, and how we used the SVC for our purposes.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',2,2,'2025-05-29_20-34-50','In short, a system call is a request made by a program to the OS. This is an interrupt to the program. In most cases this task involves the OS and requests some sort of action from the OS. This is done because the user (us) doesn’t usually have privileged access to the OS. In real-life, this mechanism is essential because user programs run in unprivileged mode, which usually restricts their ability to access system resources. The SVC call safely allows the user to take control through a handler (in our case, SVC_Handler_Main), but also has some aspects of protection and stability built into it.\nOnce we use system calls, we’re able to request tasks from the OS or the kernel, which has privileges and access to things we don’t. In our lab we used SVC_Handler, which is a function that invokes the supervisor call. Unlike normal function calls, system calls are implemented using special instructions – in our case we used SVC (Supervisor Call) on ARM Cortex-M4. This triggered a software interrupt which caused the CPU to switch to privileged mode. Once run, the OS follows this call and does what its supposed to.\nWe created a function called print_success, which called “SVC #0” through the main method. The OS follows the SVC instruction and goes to the function SVC Handler to see what to do next. Through a case structure implemented by the user, the 0 case goes to a line which prints “Success!” through printf. This showed how the SVC acted as a middleman between us and the OS, and how we used the SVC for our purposes.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',2,3,'2025-05-29_10-53-47','The thread’s stack was set up with the registers in the correct order. Careful consideration was done to ensure that the stack was almost the same to how it was – just that we added our own function call in between. We push register values onto the stack to make it seem like the CPU just continued where it left off. This simulation allows the CPU to operate as it should, but just on our own terms. Setting up the registers is like laying down the trackwork for a train. The train has a main rail on which it runs, but once we switch the stack pointer or the railroad switch, it follows our own track. Now the train runs on our line, and for someone on board it makes it seem like nothing has changed. This is what the “context” in our lab refers to. The CPU needs registers to run – some more critical than others, like xPSR, LR, or PC. We push these register values onto the stack in the correct order to ensure everything will work correctly. We need to store register values on the stack because the CPU expects a register when it runs, so we push arbitrary or placeholder values onto the stack.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',2,3,'2025-05-29_20-34-50','Before pushing values onto the stack, it’s important to allocate new stack space for the thread. This is done by setting the PSP to a valid memory location to which the registers will be pushed to. The thread’s stack was set up with the registers in the correct order. Careful consideration was done to ensure that the stack was almost the same to how it was – just that we added our own function call in between. We push register values onto the stack to make it seem like the CPU just continued where it left off. This simulation allows the CPU to operate as it should, but just on our own terms. This is the “context” the lab is talking about. These include the registers R0-R12, PC, and xPSR, which are automatically saved or expected during a return.\nSetting up the registers is like laying down the trackwork for a train. The train has a main rail on which it runs, but once we switch the stack pointer or the railroad switch, it follows our own track. Now the train runs on our line, and for someone on board it makes it seem like nothing has changed. This is what the “context” in our lab refers to. The CPU needs registers to run – some more critical than others, like xPSR, LR, or PC. We push these register values onto the stack in the correct order to ensure everything will work correctly. We need to store register values on the stack because the CPU expects a register when it runs, so we push arbitrary or placeholder values onto the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',2,4,'2025-05-29_10-53-47','The __set_PSP function sets the start of the thread stack. In our case, this would be the start of our own custo thread, which includes the context and arbitrary registers we added ourselves. If you don’t set the PSP to the start of our thread, the OS uses a default value for PSP. This PSP doesn’t lead to the correct address, so you would be popping off registers from an invalid location. With no registers actually being popped off, the CPU notices that the registers aren’t as they was. It will output an error and would likely crash in a hard fault. Doing this switch to the PSP stack is essential when you make your own custom threads. Since you’re simulating your own OS, you need to tell it what to do instruction by instruction, and it has to be very precise.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',2,4,'2025-05-29_20-34-50','The __set_PSP function sets the start of the thread stack. In our case, this would be the start of our own custo thread, which includes the context and arbitrary registers we added ourselves. If you don’t set the PSP to the start of our thread, the OS uses an uninitialized or leftover value for PSP. When the OS attempts to execute, PSP doesn’t lead to the correct address. Instead, you would be popping off registers from an invalid or corrupted memory location and would lead to an unwanted process/output. With no registers actually being popped off, the CPU notices that the registers aren’t as they was. It will output an error and would likely crash in a hard fault. This is the OS saying that something went wrong in the code. Doing this switch to the PSP stack is essential when you make your own custom threads. Since you’re simulating your own OS, you need to tell it what to do instruction by instruction, and it has to be very precise. In summary, the PSP tells the Cortex-M4 CPU where the thread stack starts. If it’s not correctly set, the CPU will be reading junk values and will not be doing what the user requests.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',2,5,'2025-05-29_10-53-47','Our infinite loop function print_continously keeps on going without a need for a return. Because of this, there is no need to return back to the stack. However, if print is only done once, there is a need to return back to the stack. When we go back to the registers, we land at the LR or the link register. This register stores the return address after a function call. Because we pushed arbitrary values onto the stack however, it would mean that the value stored in the link register is invalid. The value 0xA we set doesn’t really have any meaning. This would likely cause the system to crash or error in some way. We could fix this by setting the link register to the correct value, but for our purposes, it was left out in the lab.\nReturn to random value, run code as is, but if valid, will run, so not good, dpnt want tp jump in to random place, say I’m done\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',2,5,'2025-05-29_20-34-50','If a thread function like print_continuously() contains an infinite loop, it never returns, so the CPU never tries to use the return address stored in the Link Register (LR). However, if the function completes and returns, the CPU will attempt to pop LR from the thread’s stack and jump to it.\nOur function print_continously keeps on going without a need for a return. This is because it contains an infinite loop and never returns any value. Because of this, the CPU never tries to use the return address stored in the Link Register (LR). However, in the case that print is only done once, there is now a need to return back to the stack. When we go back to the registers, we land at the LR or the link register. This register stores the return address after a function call. Because we pushed arbitrary values onto the stack however, it would mean that the value stored in the link register is invalid. The value 0xA we set doesn’t really have any meaning. This leads the CPU to branch off to an invalid location where the function returns. It might be a reference to some random piece of code, but you never want this functionality to happen. This would likely cause the system to crash or error in some way, causing a hardfault. However, if the address is actually valid, we don’t want it to run other pieces of code as is. We could fix this by setting the link register to the correct value, but for our purposes, it was left out in the lab. Alternatively, another way to make it work would be a stop. This would make it so that the code terminates after the print once function.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,1,'2025-06-24_00-44-01','Multiple .c files are used in this lab. Specifically, there is kernel.c and the main program, main.c. These are compiled together when they are in the same project folder. They are compiled into object files, so in our case main.c would be compiled into main.o. These files contain instructions for the computer to read and understand. They are then linked together by a linker. Additionally, the header files declare the definitions of the structs and functions that the kernel and main program uses.\nAs an example, when the main.c code is run for example, the compiler goes from the top and includes the contents of the .h header file. This .h file tells the compiler what functions exist and if it is valid. The linker comes in next, and links up the main.c file and the kernel.c. That’s when the two files are “attached” together.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,1,'2025-06-24_23-58-04','Multiple .c files are used in this lab. Specifically, there is kernel.c and the main program, main.c. Before the compiling of the program, the CPU does preprocessing. It handles #includes and #defines, and prepares the code for the compilation in the next step.\nAfter all this preprocessing, these files are compiled together. They are compiled into object files, so in our case main.c would be compiled into main.o. These files contain instructions for the computer to read and understand in machine code. The compiler also checks for syntax and applies optimizations in this step.\nThey are then linked together by a linker. The linker takes the compiled object files and puts them together. The result is a single executable file, which is now ready to run.\nAs an example, when the main.c code is run for example, the compiler goes from the top and includes the contents of the .h header file. This .h file tells the compiler what functions exist and if it is valid. The linker comes in next, and links up the main.c file and the kernel.c. That’s when the two files are “attached” together.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,1,'2025-06-26_00-56-22','Multiple .c files are used in this lab. Specifically, there is kernel.c and the main program, main.c. Before the compiling of the program, the CPU does preprocessing. It handles #includes and #defines, and prepares the code for the compilation in the next step.\nAfter all this preprocessing, these files are compiled together. They are compiled into object files, so in our case main.c would be compiled into main.o. These files contain instructions for the computer to read and understand in machine code. The compiler also checks for syntax and applies optimizations in this step.\nThey are then linked together by a linker. The linker takes the compiled object files and puts them together. The result is a single executable file, which is now ready to run.\nAs an example, when the main.c code is run for example, the compiler goes from the top and includes the contents of the .h header file. This .h file tells the compiler what functions exist and if it is valid. The linker comes in next, and links up the main.c file and the kernel.c. That’s when the two files are “attached” together.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,2,'2025-06-24_00-44-01','When you include a .c file to another .c file, it causes some issues, both on the practical side and the logical side of things. When you do include it, you’re effectively pasting the whole .c file into the other .c file. This can cause things like duplicate definitions of functions and can make the program hard to debug. Additionally, the program structure is set up in a way so that each .c file can be compiled into .o object files and then linked up by the linker. There is no need to do this by including the c file on your own. Instead of this, using a .h header file in conjunction with a .c file is a lot more efficient – it simplifies the whole process and makes things better for both the user and the compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,2,'2025-06-24_23-58-04','When you include a .c file to another .c file, it causes some issues, both on the practical side and the logical side of things. When you do include it, you’re effectively pasting the whole .c file into the other .c file. This can cause things like duplicate definitions of functions and can make the program hard to debug. Additionally, the program structure is set up in a way so that each .c file can be compiled into .o object files and then linked up by the linker. There is no need to do this by including the c file on your own. Instead of this, using a .h header file in conjunction with a .c file is a lot more efficient – it simplifies the whole process and makes things better for both the user and the compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,2,'2025-06-26_00-56-22','When you include a .c file to another .c file, it causes some issues, both on the practical side and the logical side of things. When you do include it, you’re effectively pasting the whole .c file into the other .c file. This can cause things like duplicate definitions of functions and can make the program hard to debug. Additionally, the program structure is set up in a way so that each .c file can be compiled into .o object files and then linked up by the linker. There is no need to do this by including the c file on your own. Instead of this, using a .h header file in conjunction with a .c file is a lot more efficient – it simplifies the whole process and makes things better for both the user and the compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,3,'2025-06-24_00-44-01','From the user’s perspective, there are 4 main steps to start a thread. The first step is to initialize the kernel. This is the preparation step before more important things happen. Usually in this step, this looks like clearing any old data from previous runs and resets internal variables. The second step is setting up the actual thread. It should contain the main logic of the code to accomplish a purpose. It should typically loop forever as there’s no return statement. After all this, there is one last thing to setup. The user should create the thread that the kernel runs. This kernel should allocate a new region of memory to use as the stack. Additional context should be added to the register values like the LR and PC. After all this, the user is finally able to start the kernel and execution. This is done through a context switch, switching the stack pointer from the MSP to the PSP. In our case, the initialization is the function osKernelInitialize. The setting up of the actual thread is done in in kernel.c and main.c with the function osCreateThread. Lastly, the actual execution is done by the function osKernelStart, which starts and runs that thread.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,3,'2025-06-24_23-58-04','From the user’s perspective, there are 4 main steps to start a thread. The first step is to initialize the kernel. This is the preparation step before more important things happen. Usually in this step, this looks like clearing any old data from previous runs and resets internal variables. The second step is setting up the actual thread. It should contain the main logic of the code to accomplish a purpose. It is here that the user can define their own function. It should typically loop forever as there’s no return statement needed in threads. After all this, there is one last thing to setup. The user should create the thread that the kernel runs. This kernel should allocate a new region of memory to use as the stack. Additional context should be added to the register values like the LR and PC. After all this, the user is finally able to start the kernel and execution. This is done through a context switch, switching the stack pointer from the MSP to the PSP. In our case, the initialization is the function osKernelInitialize. The setting up of the actual thread is done in in kernel.c and main.c with the function osCreateThread (with a function passed by the user). Lastly, the actual execution is done by the function osKernelStart, which starts and runs that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,3,'2025-06-26_00-56-22','From the user’s perspective, there are 4 main steps to start a thread. The first step is to initialize the kernel. This is the preparation step before more important things happen. Usually in this step, this looks like clearing any old data from previous runs and resets internal variables. The second step is setting up the actual thread. It should contain the main logic of the code to accomplish a purpose. It is here that the user can define their own function. It should typically loop forever as there’s no return statement needed in threads. After all this, there is one last thing to setup. The user should create the thread that the kernel runs. This kernel should allocate a new region of memory to use as the stack. Additional context should be added to the register values like the LR and PC. After all this, the user is finally able to start the kernel and execution. This is done through a context switch, switching the stack pointer from the MSP to the PSP. In our case, the initialization is the function osKernelInitialize. The setting up of the actual thread is done in in kernel.c and main.c with the function osCreateThread (with a function passed by the user). Lastly, the actual execution is done by the function osKernelStart, which starts and runs that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,4,'2025-06-24_00-44-01','The stack pool is a block of memory allocated that the kernel uses to store thread stacks on. Each thread that executes runs on its own special block of memory. This pool of memory can be used for things like local variables and CPU register values. Instead of using memory from the heap, the memory pool allows the use of stacks, which is a lot more efficient. This stack pool is divided into equal sized blocks and is divided up in the setup process. When a thread is created, one of the blocks in the stack pool is picked, and the stack pointer of that thread points to the first address of the memory block. In a future lab, this enables the functionality of multiple threads. Because there are multiple blocks of memory within the pool, each thread will have their own stack from a different space within that pool. To creat multiple threads, the function osCreateThread will be called multiple times. After each thread has been created, the CPU can switch between the created threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,4,'2025-06-24_23-58-04','The stack pool is a block of memory allocated that the kernel uses to store thread stacks on. Each thread that executes runs on its own special block of memory. This pool of memory can be used for things like local variables and CPU register values. Instead of using memory from the heap, the memory pool allows the use of stacks, which is a lot more efficient. This stack pool is divided into equal sized blocks and is divided up in the setup process. When a thread is created, one of the blocks in the stack pool is picked, and the stack pointer of that thread points to the first address of the memory block. In a future lab, this enables the functionality of multiple threads. Because there are multiple blocks of memory within the pool, each thread will have their own stack from a different space within that pool. To creat multiple threads, the function osCreateThread will be called multiple times. After each thread has been created, the CPU can switch between the created threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,4,'2025-06-26_00-56-22','The stack pool is a block of memory allocated that the kernel uses to store thread stacks on. Each thread that executes runs on its own special block of memory. This pool of memory can be used for things like local variables and CPU register values. Instead of using memory from the heap, the memory pool allows the use of stacks, which is a lot more efficient. This stack pool is divided into equal sized blocks and is divided up in the setup process. When a thread is created, one of the blocks in the stack pool is picked, and the stack pointer of that thread points to the first address of the memory block. In a future lab, this enables the functionality of multiple threads. Because there are multiple blocks of memory within the pool, each thread will have their own stack from a different space within that pool. To creat multiple threads, the function osCreateThread will be called multiple times. After each thread has been created, the CPU can switch between the created threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,5,'2025-06-24_00-44-01','The user is responsible for writing main.c. This includes all the functions within the thread. In our case, this would be the function print_continuously. This is where the user implements the functionality they want. In addition, functions to start processes and setup other functions would go here. Again, this would be osKernelInitialize(), osCreateThread(), and osKernelStart() in our case.\nAs the name suggests, the files kernel.c and kernel.h are part of the kernel. These files are the “brain” of the system. They provide the function definitions that the user calls, and include core functionalities of the system like thread switching and SVCs. This distinction is important from the user’s perspective because the separation separates what the user needs to know and what is redundant. The user does not always need to know every single small detail on the OS level. The user should not know every single detail either. This keeps the system safe, as unprivileged users cannot touch the kernel and risk corrupting or destroying the system. The user only has access to what is necessary, keeping the system clean.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,5,'2025-06-24_23-58-04','The user is responsible for writing main.c. This includes all the functions within the thread. In our case, this would be the function print_continuously. This is where the user implements the functionality they want. In addition, functions to start processes and setup other functions would go here. Again, this would be osKernelInitialize(), osCreateThread(), and osKernelStart() in our case.\nAs the name suggests, the files kernel.c and kernel.h are part of the kernel. These files are the “brain” of the system. They provide the function definitions that the user calls, and include core functionalities of the system like thread switching and SVCs. This distinction is important from the user’s perspective because the separation separates what the user needs to know and what is redundant. The user does not always need to know every single small detail on the OS level. The user should not know every single detail either. This keeps the system safe, as unprivileged users cannot touch the kernel and risk corrupting or destroying the system. The user only has access to what is necessary, keeping the system clean.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',3,5,'2025-06-26_00-56-22','The user is responsible for writing main.c. This includes all the functions within the thread. In our case, this would be the function print_continuously. This is where the user implements the functionality they want. In addition, functions to start processes and setup other functions would go here. Again, this would be osKernelInitialize(), osCreateThread(), and osKernelStart() in our case.\nAs the name suggests, the files kernel.c and kernel.h are part of the kernel. These files are the “brain” of the system. They provide the function definitions that the user calls, and include core functionalities of the system like thread switching and SVCs. This distinction is important from the user’s perspective because the separation separates what the user needs to know and what is redundant. The user does not always need to know every single small detail on the OS level. The user should not know every single detail either. This keeps the system safe, as unprivileged users cannot touch the kernel and risk corrupting or destroying the system. The user only has access to what is necessary, keeping the system clean.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,1,'2025-07-02_23-18-00','In the case of this lab, using a queue due to the nature of its sizing. A queue can easily be expanded to accommodate for more threads, whereas an array is not usually very expandable. A queue only takes up space that it needs, but an array needs to be a fixed size and can lead to empty space in the memory. This works well with Round-Robin, as once a thread is run, it can be dequeued and the next thread can run. With an array, you need to constantly shift the index back and forth, which is not very efficient.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,1,'2025-07-02_23-42-29','In the case of this lab, using a queue due to the nature of its sizing. A queue can easily be expanded to accommodate for more threads, whereas an array is not usually very expandable. An array needs to be pre-allocated at the start of the program. A queue only takes up space that it needs, but an array needs to be a fixed size and can lead to empty space in the memory. The head of the queue could hold the address of the next thread to run. When a thread is yielded, it is dequeued and in our case, be put in the back of the queue. This works well with Round-Robin, as once a thread is run, it can be dequeued and the next thread can run. This highlights the circular functionality of the Round-Robin system. With an array, you need to constantly shift the index back and forth, which is not very efficient. A circular queue could be implemented, but it requires more complex implementation than a simple queue.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,1,'2025-07-03_00-30-02','In the case of this lab, using a queue due to the nature of its sizing. A queue can easily be expanded to accommodate for more threads, whereas an array is not usually very expandable. An array needs to be pre-allocated at the start of the program. A queue only takes up space that it needs, but an array needs to be a fixed size and can lead to empty space in the memory. The head of the queue could hold the address of the next thread to run. When a thread is yielded, it is dequeued and in our case, be put in the back of the queue. This works well with Round-Robin, as once a thread is run, it can be dequeued and the next thread can run. This highlights the circular functionality of the Round-Robin system.\nHowever, a queue implementation does have larger memory overhead. Linked queues need dynamic memory and pointers, which could be quite costly. This may not work for small embedded systems like the Cortex M4. Using pointers to implement queues can take longer than a simple increment in an array implementation. In the case of our lab, a queue may not be too beneficial. There is a fixed number of threads, each with a fixed size.\nConceptually and in larger real life examples, it may be better to use a queue even with a larger overhead. However, in the case of our small embedded system, it is more efficient to use an array, which functions like a circular array. A full queue is overkill for such a constrained system like ours.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,1,'2025-07-03_15-37-51','In the case of this lab, using a queue due to the nature of its sizing. A queue can easily be expanded to accommodate for more threads, whereas an array is not usually very expandable. An array needs to be pre-allocated at the start of the program. A queue only takes up space that it needs, but an array needs to be a fixed size and can lead to empty space in the memory. The head of the queue could hold the address of the next thread to run. When a thread is yielded, it is dequeued and in our case, be put in the back of the queue. This works well with Round-Robin, as once a thread is run, it can be dequeued and the next thread can run. This highlights the circular functionality of the Round-Robin system.\nHowever, a queue implementation does have larger memory overhead. Linked queues need dynamic memory and pointers, which could be quite costly. This may not work for small embedded systems like the Cortex M4. Using pointers to implement queues can take longer than a simple increment in an array implementation. In the case of our lab, a queue may not be too beneficial. There is a fixed number of threads, each with a fixed size.\nConceptually and in larger real life examples, it may be better to use a queue even with a larger overhead. However, in the case of our small embedded system, it is more efficient to use an array, which functions like a circular array. A full queue is overkill for such a constrained system like ours.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,1,'2025-07-03_16-17-32','In the case of this lab, using a queue due to the nature of its sizing. A queue can easily be expanded to accommodate for more threads, whereas an array is not usually very expandable. An array needs to be pre-allocated at the start of the program. A queue only takes up space that it needs, but an array needs to be a fixed size and can lead to empty space in the memory. The head of the queue could hold the address of the next thread to run. When a thread is yielded, it is dequeued and in our case, be put in the back of the queue. This works well with Round-Robin, as once a thread is run, it can be dequeued and the next thread can run. This highlights the circular functionality of the Round-Robin system.\nHowever, a queue implementation does have larger memory overhead. Linked queues need dynamic memory and pointers, which could be quite costly. This may not work for small embedded systems like the Cortex M4. Using pointers to implement queues can take longer than a simple increment in an array implementation. In the case of our lab, a queue may not be too beneficial. There is a fixed number of threads, each with a fixed size.\nConceptually and in larger real life examples, it may be better to use a queue even with a larger overhead. However, in the case of our small embedded system, it is more efficient to use an array, which functions like a circular array. A full queue is overkill for such a constrained system like ours.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,2,'2025-07-02_23-18-00','PendSV is a special type of interrupt that runs when there are no other interrupts left to go. It waits for the CPU to finish running all the higher priority threads it needs to and runs after. Once it runs, it saves the thread’s current context like the registers PC and LR. It then has the functionality of switching to another thread. It does this by setting the PSP to the next thread, and loads the new thread’s context and registers. PendSV only runs when everything is safe, and prevents confusion within the system.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,2,'2025-07-02_23-42-29','PendSV is a special type of interrupt that runs when there are no other interrupts left to go. It waits for the CPU to finish running all the higher priority threads it needs to and runs after. Once it runs, it saves the thread’s current context like the registers PC and LR. It then has the functionality of switching to another thread. It does this by setting the PSP to the next thread, telling the CPU where the next thread is. It then loads the new thread’s context and core registers and saves them to the CPU. The additional registers R4-R11 are not saved automatically and must be explicitly saved by the OS. PendSV also calls osSched() function, which selects the next thread to run. PendSV only runs when everything is safe, and prevents confusion within the system. After PendSV exits, the CPU performs an exception return, and restores the new thread’s context for the next thread to be run.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,2,'2025-07-03_00-30-02','PendSV is a special type of interrupt that runs when there are no other interrupts left to go. It waits for the CPU to finish running all the higher priority threads it needs to and runs after. Once it runs, it saves the thread’s current context like the registers PC and LR. It then has the functionality of switching to another thread. It does this by setting the PSP to the next thread, telling the CPU where the next thread is. It then loads the new thread’s context and core registers and saves them to the CPU. The PSP helps to locate these registers in this process. The additional registers R4-R11 are not saved automatically and must be explicitly saved by the OS. These registers are then pushed onto the stack in our case. PendSV also calls osSched() function, which selects the next thread to run. It updates currentThread to point to the next thread to run. The new thread’s registers R4-R11 are popped from the stack, which restores the state that it was previously in. After PendSV exits, the CPU handler performs an exception return, and restores the new thread’s context for the next thread to be run. It does this by popping the rest of the context off of the stack. PendSV only runs when everything is safe, and prevents confusion within the system.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,2,'2025-07-03_15-37-51','PendSV is a special type of interrupt that runs when there are no other interrupts left to go. It waits for the CPU to finish running all the higher priority threads it needs to and is triggered after. PendSV only runs when everything is safe, and prevents confusion within the system.\nOnce it runs, it saves the thread’s current context like the registers PC and LR. This is done through a call, which in our case, is osYield. This tells the CPU that it’s ready to switch threads.\nPendSV then has the functionality of switching to another thread. It first saves the current thread’s context. The hardware pushes the current thread’s context, including the core registers and also R4-R11, which are manually pushed onto the stack. The current thread’s PSP is saved in the TCB so it can be continued at a later time.\nPendSV then calls osSched() function, which selects the next thread to run. It updates currentThread to point to the next thread to run. It sets the PSP to the saved stack pointer of the new thread, so the CPU knows where to resume. After selecting the new thread, its The new thread’s registers R4-R11 are popped from the stack, which restores the state that it was previously in. After PendSV exits, the CPU handler performs an exception return, and restores the new thread’s context for the next thread to be run. It does this by popping the rest of the context off of the stack. This completes the context switch, and the next thread resumes running where it left off.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,2,'2025-07-03_16-17-32','PendSV is a special type of interrupt designed specifically for context switching. It runs only when there are no higher-priority interrupts pending, ensuring that it operates safely without interfering with more urgent tasks.\nWhen a thread voluntarily yields, such as by calling osYield(), PendSV is triggered. This indicates that the current thread is ready to give up the CPU and that a context switch should occur.\nOnce PendSV begins executing, it saves the context of the current thread. The processor automatically pushes certain core registers like the PC, LR, or xPSR onto the thread’s stack. However, registers R4–R11 are not saved by hardware, so the OS must manually push them onto the stack. After this, the current value of the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is stored in the thread\'s TCB so that it can resume later.\nNext, PendSV calls the osSched() function, which selects the next thread to run and updates currentThread accordingly. The PSP is then set to the saved stack pointer of the selected thread. This tells the CPU where to find the new thread’s context.\nBefore returning from PendSV, the saved R4–R11 registers of the new thread are popped from the stack to restore its state. Finally, the exception return mechanism automatically restores the rest of the thread\'s context like the PC or LR from the stack and resumes execution from where the thread left off.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,3,'2025-07-02_23-18-00','This is a bad idea because of starvation. Starvation is when a lower-priority task doesn’t get to run, due to many other higher-priority processes that always go before it. The low-priority task is almost “forgotten”. The system in turn becomes unresponsive to these low-priority tasks, which may affect the core functionality of the system. These things might include background work, or even PendSV in our case.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,3,'2025-07-02_23-42-29','If the scheduler always picks a higher priority task, it can lead to starvation. Starvation is when a lower-priority task doesn’t get to run, due to many other higher-priority processes that always go before it. The low-priority task is almost “forgotten”. The system in turn becomes unresponsive to these low-priority tasks, which may affect the core functionality of the system. These things might include background work, or even PendSV in our case. This loses the multitasking functionality that we implemented. This makes it not fair for the program and other lower-priority tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,3,'2025-07-03_00-30-02','If the scheduler always picks a higher priority task, it can lead to starvation. Starvation is when a lower-priority task doesn’t get to run, due to many other higher-priority processes that always go before it. The low-priority task is almost “forgotten”. The system in turn becomes unresponsive to these low-priority tasks, which may affect the core functionality of the system. These things might include background work, or even PendSV in our case. This loses the multitasking functionality that we implemented. This makes it not fair for the program and other lower-priority tasks.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,3,'2025-07-03_15-37-51','If the scheduler always picks a higher priority task, it can lead to starvation. Starvation is when a lower-priority task doesn’t get to run, due to many other higher-priority processes that always go before it. The low-priority task is almost “forgotten”. The system in turn becomes unresponsive to these low-priority tasks, which may affect the core functionality of the system. These things might include background work, or even PendSV in our case.\nMore importantly, if the highest-priority task is always ready to run, the scheduler will always pick that task. That means that no other program will be given a chance to run. This makes it unfair for the program and other lower-priority tasks. This violates multithreading and defeats the purpose of having multiple threads. An implementation of the highest-priority task scheduling system would make the system unreliable or even fail to function.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,3,'2025-07-03_16-17-32','If the scheduler always picks a higher priority task, it can lead to starvation. Starvation is when a lower-priority task doesn’t get to run, due to many other higher-priority processes that always go before it. The low-priority task is almost “forgotten”. The system in turn becomes unresponsive to these low-priority tasks, which may affect the core functionality of the system. These things might include background work, or even PendSV in our case.\nMore importantly, if the highest-priority task is always ready to run, the scheduler will always pick that task. That means that no other program will be given a chance to run. This makes it unfair for the program and other lower-priority tasks. This violates multithreading and defeats the purpose of having multiple threads. An implementation of the highest-priority task scheduling system would make the system unreliable or even fail to function.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,4,'2025-07-02_23-18-00','The benefits to calling the C functions is that a user can understand the functionality easier. This makes debugging and modifications to the system easier. The downside is that safety is compromised, and a potential malicious user could pose a threat to the system. In addition, assembly code allows for more precise modification of the code. It has control over CPU state and registers, more precise than that coded in C. In our case, writing the interrupt handlers in assembly is also better, as you can handle the stack and threads more accurately.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,4,'2025-07-02_23-42-29','The benefits to calling the C functions is that a user can understand the functionality easier. This makes debugging and modifications to the system easier. The downside is that safety is compromised, and a potential malicious user could pose a threat to the system. In addition, assembly code allows for more precise modification of the code. It has control over CPU state and registers, more precise than that coded in C. In our case, writing the interrupt handlers in assembly is also better, as you can handle the stack and threads more accurately.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,4,'2025-07-03_00-30-02','The benefits to calling the C functions is that a user can understand the functionality easier. This makes debugging and modifications to the system easier. The downside is that safety is compromised, and a potential malicious user could pose a threat to the system. In addition, assembly code allows for more precise modification of the code. It has control over CPU state and registers, more precise than that coded in C. In our case, writing the interrupt handlers in assembly is also better, as you can handle the stack and threads more accurately.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,4,'2025-07-03_15-37-51','The benefits to calling the C functions is that a user can understand the functionality easier. This makes debugging and modifications to the system easier. The downside is that safety is compromised, and a potential malicious user could pose a threat to the system. In addition, assembly code allows for more precise modification of the code. It has control over CPU state and registers, more precise than that coded in C. In our case, writing the interrupt handlers in assembly is also better, as you can handle the stack and threads more accurately.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,4,'2025-07-03_16-17-32','The benefits to calling the C functions is that a user can understand the functionality easier. This makes debugging and modifications to the system easier. The downside is that safety is compromised, and a potential malicious user could pose a threat to the system. In addition, assembly code allows for more precise modification of the code. It has control over CPU state and registers, more precise than that coded in C. In our case, writing the interrupt handlers in assembly is also better, as you can handle the stack and threads more accurately.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,5,'2025-07-02_23-18-00','If you only created a single thread, the functionality would not be as good. It would be similar to lab 3, with no multitasking. The scheduler would always pick the one thread, and no context switch would happen. The cleanliness of the code would also take a hit as well. It is theoretically possible for the OS to work, but it is not good practice. In real life, multithreading allows your program to appear to handle multiple tasks at the same time. Multithreading allows your CPU to not stay idle when one thread is running, and context switches improve the efficiency of the system. In addition, only using one thread also defeats the purpose of the lab.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,5,'2025-07-02_23-42-29','If you only created a single thread, the functionality would not be as good. It would be similar to lab 3, with no multitasking. The scheduler would always pick the one thread, and no context switch would occur because there is no other thread to run. The thread yields, but since there is not other thread to yield to, it just goes reschedules the current thread immediately. The cleanliness of the code would also take a hit as well. The OS still does work and function correctly, but it is not good practice. You waste valuable resources doing a non-essential context switch. In real life, multithreading allows your program to appear to handle multiple tasks at the same time. Multithreading allows your CPU to not stay idle when one thread is running, and context switches improve the efficiency of the system. In addition, only using one thread also defeats the purpose of this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,5,'2025-07-03_00-30-02','If you only created a single thread, the functionality would not be as good. It would be similar to lab 3, with no multitasking. The scheduler would always pick the one thread, and no context switch would occur because there is no other thread to run. The thread yields, but since there is not other thread to yield to, it just goes reschedules the current thread immediately. The cleanliness of the code would also take a hit as well. The OS still does work and function correctly, but it is not good practice. You waste valuable resources doing a non-essential context switch. In real life, multithreading allows your program to appear to handle multiple tasks at the same time. Multithreading allows your CPU to not stay idle when one thread is running, and context switches improve the efficiency of the system. In addition, only using one thread also defeats the purpose of this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,5,'2025-07-03_15-37-51','If you only created a single thread, the functionality would not be as good. It would be similar to lab 3, with no multitasking. The scheduler would always pick the one thread, and no context switch would occur because there is no other thread to run. The thread yields, but since there is not other thread to yield to, it just goes reschedules the current thread immediately. The cleanliness of the code would also take a hit as well. The OS still does work and function correctly, but it is not good practice. You waste valuable resources doing a non-essential context switch. In real life, multithreading allows your program to appear to handle multiple tasks at the same time. Multithreading allows your CPU to not stay idle when one thread is running, and context switches improve the efficiency of the system. In addition, only using one thread also defeats the purpose of this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',4,5,'2025-07-03_16-17-32','If you only created a single thread, the functionality would not be as good. It would be similar to lab 3, with no multitasking. The scheduler would always pick the one thread, and no context switch would occur because there is no other thread to run. The thread yields, but since there is not other thread to yield to, it just goes reschedules the current thread immediately. The cleanliness of the code would also take a hit as well. The OS still does work and function correctly, but it is not good practice. You waste valuable resources doing a non-essential context switch. In real life, multithreading allows your program to appear to handle multiple tasks at the same time. Multithreading allows your CPU to not stay idle when one thread is running, and context switches improve the efficiency of the system. In addition, only using one thread also defeats the purpose of this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,1,'2025-07-19_18-01-30','To get an argument passed into a thread, the user has to define the thread first. The data being passed into the thread is usually passed as a variable. A pointer pointing to the data is then passed into the thread and taken as a parameter.\nNext, the OS takes the request and processes the data. It stores the data passed into the thread, and is passed into R0, the argument pointer.  It remembers the thread function and the argument pointer. It then sets up the thread’s stack, registers, and memory. This is all done to ensure that when the thread starts running, everything runs smoothly.\nThis works because of how functions are designed in C. Depending on the implementation, the function can take arguments. The OS would automatically pass the argument into the thread when it would start. This ensures that no data is left out and every function can have the data it needs to run.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,1,'2025-07-19_19-14-31','To get an argument passed into a thread, the user has to define the thread first. The data being passed into the thread is usually passed as a variable. In our case, the user first defines the thread with a void* parameter to receive such an argument.  A pointer pointing to the data is then passed into the thread and taken as a parameter.\nNext, the OS takes the request and processes the data. It sets up the thread’s initial stack frame. This includes registers like R0, PC, xPSR, and LR. It stores the data passed into the thread, and is passed into R0, the argument pointer.  R0 is then pushed onto the stack. When the thread is context switched in, the processor pops off all the registers and R0 gets loaded with the argument pointer. This is all done to ensure that when the thread starts running, everything runs smoothly.\nThis works because of how functions are designed in C. Depending on the implementation, the function can take arguments. The OS would automatically pass the first argument into R0 of the thread when it would start. So, by placing the correct values on the stack, the OS tricks the CPU into starting the thread as if it were resuming it. This ensures that no data is left out and every function can have the data it needs to run.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,1,'2025-07-19_22-38-21','To get an argument passed into a thread, the user has to allocate and define the data first. The data being passed into the thread is usually passed as a global or static variable. The thread will then receive a pointer to this data and use it when creating the thread. This would be the osCreateThread() function. In our case, the user first defines the thread with a void* parameter to receive such an argument.  A pointer pointing to the data is then passed into the thread and taken as a parameter.\nNext, the OS takes the request and processes the data. It sets up the thread’s initial stack frame. This includes registers like R0, PC, xPSR, and LR. It stores the data passed into the thread, and is passed into R0, the argument pointer. When the thread is context switched in, the processor pops off all the registers and R0 gets loaded with the argument pointer. This is all done to ensure that when the thread starts running, everything runs smoothly.\nThis works because of the ABI. It specifies that the function argument is passed in R0. When the context is loaded in later, the OS pops the stack and R0 into the CPU registers. So, by placing the correct values on the stack, the OS tricks the CPU into starting the thread as if it were resuming it. This ensures that no data is left out and every function can have the data it needs to run.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,2,'2025-07-19_18-01-30','The function osYield() is a system call used to trigger an interrupt. When it is called, the SVC_Handler runs, and tells the OS to perform a context switch. Our context-switching functions are in charge of this: SVC Handler for system calls, PendSV handler for context switching, and Systick Handler for timed interrupts.\nThese handlers are already a part of the thread-switching mechanism. Calling osYield() from inside one of these switching handler functions would cause another SVC call while still in the thread. This would mean you’re switching threads while you’re in the middle of switching threads. This would cause nesting of thread-switching, which is definitely not the intended outcome of the program. The functionality of the entire program would be wasted, as the OS would be stuck switching threads. In extreme cases, this would cause the OS to crash.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,2,'2025-07-19_19-14-31','The function osYield() is a system call used to trigger an interrupt. When it is called, the SVC_Handler runs, and tells the OS to perform a context switch. Our context-switching functions are in charge of this: SVC Handler for system calls, PendSV handler for context switching, and Systick Handler for timed interrupts.\nThese handlers are already a part of the thread-switching mechanism. Calling osYield() from inside one of these switching handler functions would cause another SVC call while still in the thread. This would mean you’re switching threads while you’re in the middle of switching threads. This would cause nesting of thread-switching, which is definitely not the intended outcome of the program. Calling osYield() from inside another handler manually forces another interrupt and breaks the flow of logic. The functionality of the entire program would be wasted, as the OS would be stuck switching threads. In extreme cases, this would cause the OS to crash.\nOn a deeper level, each time an interrupt occurs on Cortex-M4, the hardware pushes registers on the stack. This includes R0-R3, LR, PC, and xPSR. If you call osYield() inside another interrupt, the processor tries to enter another interrupt. The stack frame from the previous interrupt might get overwritten by the new stack frame. After the interrupt returns, the CPU might try to pop corrupted values into the registers, leading to undefined behaviour. The Cortex-M4 also detects whether or not a certain stack frame is valid. If it finds out that the stack frame is invalid, the processor might trigger a hardfault error. This is not what you want.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,2,'2025-07-19_22-38-21','The function osYield() is a system call used to trigger an interrupt. When it is called, the SVC_Handler runs, and tells the OS to perform a context switch. Our context-switching functions are in charge of this: SVC Handler for system calls, PendSV handler for context switching, and Systick Handler for timed interrupts.\nThese handlers are already a part of the thread-switching mechanism. Calling osYield() from inside one of these switching handler functions would cause another SVC call while still in the thread. This would mean you’re switching threads while you’re in the middle of switching threads. This would cause nesting of thread-switching, which is definitely not the intended outcome of the program. Calling osYield() from inside another handler manually forces another interrupt and breaks the flow of logic. The functionality of the entire program would be wasted, as the OS would be stuck switching threads. In extreme cases, this would cause the OS to crash.\nOn a deeper level, each time an interrupt occurs on Cortex-M4, the hardware pushes registers on the stack. This includes R0-R3, LR, PC, and xPSR. If you call osYield() inside another interrupt, the processor tries to enter another interrupt. The stack frame from the previous interrupt might get overwritten by the new stack frame. After the interrupt returns, the CPU might try to pop corrupted values into the registers, leading to undefined behaviour. The Cortex-M4 also detects whether or not a certain stack frame is valid. If it finds out that the stack frame is invalid, the processor might trigger a hardfault error. This is not what you want.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,3,'2025-07-19_18-01-30','Setting the OS clock to be 0.1ms instead of 1ms would make the scheduler tick 10 times faster. This would mean the system would have a faster response time. In a practical sense, this would be threads pre-empting sooner after they exceed their timeslice. This is practical in real life for systems that require strict timelines on processes. Applications like motor controls or high-frequency sampling sensors would benefit highly from a faster response time.\nOn the other side, this would mean an increased CPU overhead. This would increase the amount of context switches, which has some side effects. In an extreme case, more time would be spent doing the actual interrupt versus useful work. Threads might not have the opportunity to run long enough before getting switched off.\nDepending on the application, there are both benefits and downsides to using certain clock times in the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,3,'2025-07-19_19-14-31','Setting the OS clock to be 0.1ms instead of 1ms would make the scheduler tick 10 times faster. This would mean the system would have a faster response time. In a practical sense, this would be threads pre-empting sooner after they exceed their timeslice. This is practical in real life for systems that require strict timelines on processes. Applications like motor controls or high-frequency sampling sensors would benefit highly from a faster response time.\nOn the other side, this would mean an increased CPU overhead. This would increase the amount of context switches, which has some side effects. In an extreme case, more time would be spent doing the actual interrupt versus useful work. Threads might not have the opportunity to run long enough before getting switched off.\nDepending on the application, there are both benefits and downsides to using certain clock times in the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,3,'2025-07-19_22-38-21','Setting the OS clock to be 0.1ms instead of 1ms would make the scheduler tick 10 times faster. This would mean the system would have a faster response time. In a practical sense, this would be threads pre-empting sooner after they exceed their timeslice. This is practical in real life for systems that require strict timelines on processes. Applications like motor controls or high-frequency sampling sensors would benefit highly from a faster response time.\nOn the other side, this would mean an increased CPU overhead. This would increase the amount of context switches, which has some side effects. In an extreme case, more time would be spent doing the actual interrupt versus useful work. Threads might not have the opportunity to run long enough before getting switched off.\nDepending on the application, there are both benefits and downsides to using certain clock times in the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,4,'2025-07-19_18-01-30','Co-operative multitasking requires the user to explicitly call osYield() to give control to other threads. It’s the responsibility of the programmer to explicitly call this function or yield regularly or as desired. If not implemented correctly though, one thread could potentially hog the CPU and other threads won’t run. However, in a practical sense, it is easier to implement. Threads won’t switch without your permission, so you can ensure that the whole program is synchronized. As a result, this means that sharing data is safer. The OS doesn’t have to do much on its own, aside from listening to the programmer’s instructions.\nPre-emptive multitasking gives more control and independence to the OS. Threads are automatically interrupted by the OS after a certain time and its threads are switched automatically. This means that starvation of threads won’t really be an issue – the OS stops and switches threads if one thread runs too long. The downside to this is that it may be hard to implement. There are many different considerations that have to be taken into account, especially if there is shared data between threads. These threads may behave unpredictably if not implemented correctly by the programmer. Mutexes and synchronization has to be done correctly for this to work.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,4,'2025-07-19_19-14-31','Co-operative multitasking requires the user to explicitly call osYield() to give control to other threads. It’s the responsibility of the programmer to explicitly call this function or yield regularly or as desired. If not implemented correctly though, one thread could potentially hog the CPU and other threads won’t run. However, in a practical sense, it is easier to implement. Threads won’t switch without your permission, so you can ensure that the whole program is synchronized. As a result, this means that sharing data is safer. The OS doesn’t have to do much on its own, aside from listening to the programmer’s instructions.\nPre-emptive multitasking gives more control and independence to the OS. Threads are automatically interrupted by the OS after a certain time and its threads are switched automatically. This means that starvation of threads won’t really be an issue – the OS stops and switches threads if one thread runs too long. The downside to this is that it may be hard to implement. There are many different considerations that have to be taken into account, especially if there is shared data between threads. These threads may behave unpredictably if not implemented correctly by the programmer. Mutexes and synchronization has to be done correctly for this to work.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,4,'2025-07-19_22-38-21','Co-operative multitasking requires the user to explicitly call osYield() to give control to other threads. It’s the responsibility of the programmer to explicitly call this function or yield regularly or as desired. If not implemented correctly though, one thread could potentially hog the CPU and other threads won’t run. However, in a practical sense, it is easier to implement. Threads won’t switch without your permission, so you can ensure that the whole program is synchronized. As a result, this means that sharing data is safer. The OS doesn’t have to do much on its own, aside from listening to the programmer’s instructions.\nPre-emptive multitasking gives more control and independence to the OS. Threads are automatically interrupted by the OS after a certain time and its threads are switched automatically. This means that starvation of threads won’t really be an issue – the OS stops and switches threads if one thread runs too long. The downside to this is that it may be hard to implement. There are many different considerations that have to be taken into account, especially if there is shared data between threads. These threads may behave unpredictably if not implemented correctly by the programmer. Mutexes and synchronization has to be done correctly for this to work.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,5,'2025-07-19_18-01-30','Yielding is a thread giving up control of the CPU when the programmer calls something like osYield(). On the other hand, time-based context switching is based on a timer the OS uses. After a certain time slice, the thread is interrupted automatically by the OS.\nThese two processes can work together because they both have the same functionality – switching threads. At the core, they both work in the same way. They both switch threads by saving and restoring CPU registers. Shared variables can be seen by both kinds of thread processes. The operations are the same, but it’s just the terminating condition that changes. Our lab proves that they can work together to achieve a simple task. In real-life, this is used to give the programmer flexibility when designing programs. Sometimes co-operative multitasking works better in some cases and pre-emptive multitasking works better in other applications.\n',0,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,5,'2025-07-19_19-14-31','Yielding is a thread giving up control of the CPU when the programmer calls something like osYield(). The SVC_Handler recognizes the request and sets up the the PendSV interrupt and the PendSV_Handler. When it runs, the actual thread switching occurs.\nOn the other hand, time-based context switching is based on a timer the OS uses. After a certain time slice, the thread is interrupted automatically by the OS. Instead of using SVC_Handler, the OS uses SysTick to to trigger SysTick_Handler. This thread is automatically interrupted through the use of PendSV, with PendSV_Handler performing the context switch.\nThese two processes can work together because they both have the same functionality – switching threads. At the core, they both work in the same way. They both switch threads by saving and restoring CPU registers. Next, it calls the scheduler to select the next runnable thread, and restores the context of the new thread for the CPU. Shared variables can also be seen by these two kinds of thread processes. The operations are the same, but it’s just the terminating condition and scheduling mechanism that changes. They both use the same PendSV handler to avoid conflicts. The Cortex-M4 used in our lab ensures that PendSV runs only when it’s safe to do so. All this functionality ensures that both co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking can occur in the same system.\nOur lab proves that they can work together to achieve a simple task. In real-life, this is used to give the programmer flexibility when designing programs. Sometimes co-operative multitasking works better in some cases and pre-emptive multitasking works better in other applications.\n',1,'s25'),('j3zhuang',5,5,'2025-07-19_22-38-21','Yielding is a thread giving up control of the CPU when the programmer calls something like osYield(). The SVC_Handler recognizes the request and sets up the the PendSV interrupt and the PendSV_Handler. When it runs, the actual thread switching occurs.\nOn the other hand, time-based context switching is based on a timer the OS uses. After a certain time slice, the thread is interrupted automatically by the OS. Instead of using SVC_Handler, the OS uses SysTick to to trigger SysTick_Handler. This thread is automatically interrupted through the use of PendSV, with PendSV_Handler performing the context switch.\nThese two processes can work together because they both have the same functionality – switching threads. At the core, they both work in the same way. They both switch threads by saving and restoring CPU registers. Next, it calls the scheduler to select the next runnable thread, and restores the context of the new thread for the CPU. Shared variables can also be seen by these two kinds of thread processes. The operations are the same, but it’s just the terminating condition and scheduling mechanism that changes. They both use the same PendSV handler to avoid conflicts. The Cortex-M4 used in our lab ensures that PendSV runs only when it’s safe to do so. All this functionality ensures that both co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking can occur in the same system.\nOur lab proves that they can work together to achieve a simple task. In real-life, this is used to give the programmer flexibility when designing programs. Sometimes co-operative multitasking works better in some cases and pre-emptive multitasking works better in other applications.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',1,1,'2025-05-22_13-52-27','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a collection of software libraries that lets us interact with the microcontroller’s hardware (in this case, the Nucleo board) through a unified, high-level API. By abstracting away low-level register manipulations, HAL simplifies development and improves portability across different STM32 families, accelerates prototyping, and reduces bugs that stem from direct hardware configuration mistakes.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',1,2,'2025-05-22_13-52-27','__io_putchar is the hook that reroutes every character produced by printf to a UART transmit routine, one byte at a time. Internally, the standard library’s printf → fputc chain calls __io_putchar; implementing it prevents a linker error and gives us a console over the serial port when the board itself has no display.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',1,3,'2025-05-22_13-52-27','While printf shows that code paths execute, it cannot reveal live register contents or memory layout. The debugger lets us halt at breakpoints, inspect the MSP and PSP registers in real time, and verify that stack pointers match the addresses we computed—insight that plain printf cannot provide (and that printf itself could distort by adding stack usage).\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',1,4,'2025-05-22_13-52-27','We first read the word at address 0x0 to obtain MSP_INIT_VAL, the start of the default stack. Because the stack grows downward, we created a separate process stack by subtracting 0x400 bytes from that address, giving PSP_val = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL – 0x400. We then loaded that value into the CPU with __set_PSP(PSP_val) and flipped bit 1 of the CONTROL register (__set_CONTROL(0x2)) so the core switches from MSP to PSP in privileged thread mode. Knowing the original MSP ensures the new PSP does not overlap the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',1,5,'2025-05-22_13-52-27','For n threads each requiring a stack of size s, we can pre-compute an array of base addresses:\nstackbase[i] = MSP_INIT_VAL – (i + 1) * s for 0 ≤ i < n.\nThis simple arithmetic guarantees every thread i starts with its own non-overlapping stack region, enabling a thread scheduler (added later) to load each base address into PSP before context-switching.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,1,'2025-05-30_11-37-30','Inside the SVC handler I rewrote the saved PC in the thread’s hardware stack frame with the address of print_continuously. When the handler exited, the Cortex-M popped that frame, reloaded its registers, and started fetching instructions at my new PC. Because print_continuously contains an infinite loop, execution remained there, effectively turning the routine into a stand-alone thread.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,1,'2025-06-10_14-35-00','Inside the SVC handler I overwrote the saved program counter in the interrupted thread’s hardware stack frame with the address of print_continuously. When the handler returned, the Cortex-M core restored registers from that frame and began fetching instructions at the new PC, so execution jumped straight into print_continuously. Because this routine contains an infinite loop, the processor stayed there indefinitely, turning it into the only running thread.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,1,'2025-06-24_14-38-59','During the SVC handler I replaced the stored return address with the starting address of print_continuously. When the handler finished, the processor restored its registers from the stack, discovered the new return address, and began fetching instructions from there. Because that routine is an endless loop, the core remained inside it indefinitely, making it the only “thread” that ever runs.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,1,'2025-06-24_15-21-09','During the SVC handler I replaced the stored return address with the starting address of print_continuously. When the handler finished, the processor restored its registers from the stack, discovered the new return address, and began fetching instructions from there. Because that routine is an endless loop, the core remained inside it indefinitely, making it the only “thread” that ever runs.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,2,'2025-05-30_11-37-30','A system call is the safe gateway I use to ask the kernel for a privileged service—anything from scheduling to touching device registers—without letting unprivileged code access those resources directly. I trigger it with the SVC #imm instruction, which raises an SVC exception, switches to handler mode on the main stack, decodes the immediate value to pick the requested service, performs the work with full privileges, and then returns to thread mode.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',2,2,'2025-06-10_14-35-00','A system call is a controlled doorway through which unprivileged code requests a service that requires kernel-mode privileges. On a Cortex-M, executing SVC #imm raises an SVC exception, switches to handler mode on the main stack, lets the kernel decode the immediate value to identify the requested service, performs the privileged work, and then returns to thread mode—granting access to protected resources without exposing them directly.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',2,2,'2025-06-24_14-38-59','A system call is a controlled mechanism that lets un-privileged (user-mode) code request a privileged service from the kernel. A system call is a carefully controlled entryway that lets ordinary-privilege code request a service that requires kernel privilege. On a Cortex-M, executing the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction triggers an exception that automatically moves the processor to handler mode, lets the kernel identify the requested service from the embedded immediate value, carries out the privileged work, and then returns to user code. This keeps sensitive resources protected while still allowing access through well-defined kernel routines.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',2,2,'2025-06-24_15-21-09','A system call is, quite literally, a function call—but one whose implementation lives in the kernel rather than in the user program.\nOn a Cortex-M, the instruction that performs this call is SVC #imm:\nCall site (user code) – The SVC opcode acts as the API stub: the same way a normal C function call pushes a return address and jumps, SVC pushes the user registers and “jumps” to the kernel by raising an SVC exception.\nCall boundary – Hardware automatically switches from thread mode to handler mode, giving the kernel full privilege.\nCall body (kernel service routine) – The SVC handler decodes the immediate value to select which kernel function (read file, write UART, start thread, etc.) the user requested, executes that service, then prepares a return value in R0 just like a normal function.\nReturn – An exception return restores the saved registers and resumes the caller in user mode, mirroring the epilogue of an ordinary function call.\nSo a system call is best understood as a privileged function in the kernel that is invoked through a special instruction rather than the usual BL instruction, providing a clean interface (API) while protecting the underlying resources.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,3,'2025-05-30_11-37-30','To launch a thread I built the exact eight-word frame the hardware expects after an exception: xPSR set to 0x01000000 (Thumb bit high), PC set to the thread’s entry point, LR set to 0xFFFFFFFD so the CPU returns to unprivileged thread mode using PSP, plus R12 and R3-R0 (often passing an argument in R0). Below that I placed the callee-saved registers R4-R11. Pre-loading these values means the CPU can “return” straight into the thread, and saving registers preserves its context so later switches don’t corrupt data.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',2,3,'2025-06-10_14-35-00','Setting up a thread’s stack means pre-building exactly the frame that hardware expects after an exception return so the CPU can “resume” a brand-new thread as if it had merely been pre-empted. I first placed an xPSR value of 0x01000000 (Thumb bit set), followed by the PC pointing at the thread’s entry function and an LR value of 0xFFFFFFFD that tells the core to return to thread mode using the process stack pointer. Next came R12 and R3–R0, with R0 optionally holding the thread’s argument, and finally the callee-saved registers R4–R11. Storing—or pre-initializing—these registers preserves each thread’s independent context so later context switches do not corrupt its state.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',2,3,'2025-06-24_14-38-59','Setting up a thread’s stack means pre-building exactly the register frame that hardware expects to restore when it “returns” to that thread. At the top you place a status word with the Thumb bit set, below it the entry address of the thread, then a special return code that tells the processor to resume in thread mode using the process-stack pointer, followed by space for the remaining core registers. Pre-initialising this frame gives each thread an independent copy of its context so that later context switches can save and restore state without corruption.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',2,3,'2025-06-24_15-21-09','Step 0 – Allocate the stack memory\nBefore anything else, the RTOS reserves a block of RAM (often a fixed-size array or dynamically from a heap) to serve as the thread’s stack. The top of this block becomes the initial Process Stack Pointer (PSP) value.\nStep 1 – Pre-build the hardware frame\nStarting at that top-of-stack address we write, in the exact order the Cortex-M exception return expects:\nProgram-status word with the Thumb bit set,\nThe thread’s entry address,\nA special LR value (0xFFFFFFFD) telling the CPU to return to thread mode and keep using PSP,\nScratch registers R12, R3–R0 (optionally placing a function argument in R0),\nCallee-saved registers R4–R11 so they have known values the first time the task runs.\nWhy store the registers?\nEach thread needs its own copy of every CPU register so that when the scheduler later performs a context switch it can push the outgoing thread’s live registers and pop the incoming thread’s saved ones without corruption. Pre-initialising those slots guarantees the thread can start as though it had merely been pre-empted earlier.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,4,'2025-05-30_11-37-30','Before resuming the thread I called __set_PSP to point PSP at the top of that prepared frame. Because LR = 0xFFFFFFFD tells the CPU to pop registers from PSP, leaving PSP uninitialized would make the exception return read from an invalid address, trigger a memory-management fault, and escalate to a HardFault.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,4,'2025-06-10_14-35-00','LR = 0xFFFFFFFD instructs the CPU to pop registers from the process stack pointer on exception return. If I returned from the SVC handler without first calling __set_PSP, PSP would still be uninitialized, so the pop would read from an invalid address, trigger a memory-management fault, and quickly escalate to a HardFault. Initializing PSP to the top of the prepared frame guarantees that the core restores valid data and resumes execution safely.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',2,4,'2025-06-24_14-38-59','The special return code on the stack instructs the processor to pop registers from the process-stack pointer when the handler exits. If we return without first pointing PSP at the prepared frame, it will read from an invalid address, causing a protection fault that quickly escalates to a HardFault. Initialising PSP beforehand guarantees that valid data are popped and execution resumes safely.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',2,4,'2025-06-24_15-21-09','Two separate stack pointers exist. The core powers up using MSP (Main Stack Pointer). Our new thread, however, is supposed to run on its own stack located at a different RAM address, and the CPU accesses that stack via PSP (Process Stack Pointer).\nThe prepared frame mentioned earlier is the register frame we just wrote at the top of the thread’s stack block. PSP must point exactly there before we attempt the exception return.\nIf we skipped __set_PSP(), PSP would still be zero or some leftover value. When the SVC handler returns, the core would try to pop registers from that bogus address, immediately triggering a memory-management or bus fault, which then escalates to a HardFault.\nInitialising PSP therefore “connects” the return sequence to the correct thread stack, allowing the processor to restore valid registers and start executing the thread safely in thread mode.\n.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,5,'2025-05-30_11-37-30','If the thread printed once and then returned, it would execute BX LR while LR still holds 0xFFFFFFFD. The core would treat that as another exception return, pop whatever random data now lies beneath the current PSP, load a garbage PC, and jump into nowhere raising a Hard Fault almost immediately. Real RTOSes avoid this by never letting a thread’s main function fall through; they loop, block, or call a clean-up stub that terminates the task safely.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,5,'2025-06-10_14-35-00','If the thread’s function printed once and then fell through to BX LR, LR would still contain 0xFFFFFFFD. The core would treat that as another exception return, pop whatever data now lies beneath PSP—almost certainly garbage—load a meaningless PC, and promptly fault, usually ending in a HardFault. Real-time operating systems avoid this by ensuring a thread’s entry routine never returns; instead it loops, blocks, or jumps to a clean-up stub that terminates the task gracefully.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,5,'2025-06-24_14-38-59','If the thread’s function printed once and then tried to return, the link register would still hold the special exception-return value. The processor would treat this as another exception return, pop whatever data happen to lie beneath the current frame—almost certainly garbage—load an unpredictable program counter, and immediately fault. Real operating systems avoid this by ensuring a thread either loops, blocks, or branches to a cleanup stub that deletes the task so it never falls through to a normal return.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',2,5,'2025-06-24_15-21-09','If the thread’s function printed once and then tried to return, the link register would still hold the special exception-return value. The processor would treat this as another exception return, pop whatever data happen to lie beneath the current frame—almost certainly garbage—load an unpredictable program counter, and immediately fault. Real operating systems avoid this by ensuring a thread either loops, blocks, or branches to a cleanup stub that deletes the task so it never falls through to a normal return.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,1,'2025-06-25_12-37-58','Process for compiling several .c files into one executable\nPre-processing: Each source file runs through the pre-processor (#include, #define, conditional compilation) to produce a single translation unit of plain C.\nCompilation: The compiler translates each translation unit independently into machine code, producing one object file (.o) per original .c.\nLinking : The linker collects every .o file plus the required libraries, resolves every external symbol reference (functions or globals defined in one file but used in another), merges the various code and data sections, and assigns final run-time addresses. The result is a single relocatable image (ELF/AXF) or, after one more step, a flashable binary (.bin/.hex).\nStartup wrapping: Finally, a linker script and start-up stub insert the reset vector, memory map, and C runtime initialisation so the microcontroller can jump into main().\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,1,'2025-06-25_15-51-11','Process for compiling several .c files into one executable\nPre-processing: Each source file runs through the pre-processor (#include, #define, conditional compilation) to produce a single translation unit of plain C.\nCompilation: The compiler translates each translation unit independently into machine code, producing one object file (.o) per original .c.\nLinking : The linker collects every .o file plus the required libraries, resolves every external symbol reference (functions or globals defined in one file but used in another), merges the various code and data sections, and assigns final run-time addresses. The result is a single relocatable image (ELF/AXF) or, after one more step, a flashable binary (.bin/.hex).\nStartup wrapping: Finally, a linker script and start-up stub insert the reset vector, memory map, and C runtime initialisation so the microcontroller can jump into main().\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,1,'2025-06-25_16-06-20','Process for compiling several .c files into one executable\nPre-processing: Each source file runs through the pre-processor (#include, #define, conditional compilation) to produce a single translation unit of plain C.\nCompilation: The compiler translates each translation unit independently into machine code, producing one object file (.o) per original .c.\nLinking : The linker collects every .o file plus the required libraries, resolves every external symbol reference (functions or globals defined in one file but used in another), merges the various code and data sections, and assigns final run-time addresses. The result is a single relocatable image (ELF/AXF) or, after one more step, a flashable binary (.bin/.hex).\nStartup wrapping: Finally, a linker script and start-up stub insert the reset vector, memory map, and C runtime initialisation so the microcontroller can jump into main().\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,2,'2025-06-25_12-37-58','#include works by literal text substitution. Pulling a whole .c file into another compilation unit duplicates its function definitions, leading to multiple-definition linker errors. It breaks the “one translation-unit ⇒ one object-file” rule, making incremental builds and separate compilation impossible. Header files declare interfaces; source files contain implementations. Mixing them destroys the clear boundary between API (what other code is allowed to see) and implementation detail (which should remain private). Any small change in the included .c forces a full re-compile of every file that #includes it, slowing the build dramatically. The correct pattern is #include \"kernel.h\" for declarations and compile kernel.c separately.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,2,'2025-06-25_15-51-11','#include works by literal text substitution. Pulling a whole .c file into another compilation unit duplicates its function definitions, leading to multiple-definition linker errors. It breaks the “one translation-unit ⇒ one object-file” rule, making incremental builds and separate compilation impossible. Header files declare interfaces; source files contain implementations. Mixing them destroys the clear boundary between API (what other code is allowed to see) and implementation detail (which should remain private). Any small change in the included .c forces a full re-compile of every file that #includes it, slowing the build dramatically. The correct pattern is #include \"kernel.h\" for declarations and compile kernel.c separately.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,2,'2025-06-25_16-06-20','#include works by literal text substitution. Pulling a whole .c file into another compilation unit duplicates its function definitions, leading to multiple-definition linker errors. It breaks the “one translation-unit ⇒ one object-file” rule, making incremental builds and separate compilation impossible. Header files declare interfaces; source files contain implementations. Mixing them destroys the clear boundary between API (what other code is allowed to see) and implementation detail (which should remain private). Any small change in the included .c forces a full re-compile of every file that #includes it, slowing the build dramatically. The correct pattern is #include \"kernel.h\" for declarations and compile kernel.c separately.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,3,'2025-06-25_12-37-58','Call osKernelInitialize. It lets the kernel record where the global stack pool starts and resets its bookkeeping variables.\nWrite a thread function, a plain C function that never returns (usually a while-loop).\nCreate the thread with osCreateThread passing the function’s address; the kernel carves out a stack from the pool, pre-loads the initial register frame, and stores a pointer to that frame in its thread-control structure.\nStart the scheduler with osKernelStart. It internally raises the SVC that switches the core to Process Stack, loads the first thread’s context, and “returns” into the new thread. From the user’s perspective, control never comes back to main(); the created thread now owns the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,3,'2025-06-25_15-51-11','Call osKernelInitialize. It lets the kernel record where the global stack pool starts and resets its bookkeeping variables.\nWrite a thread function, a plain C function that never returns (usually a while-loop).\nCreate the thread with osCreateThread passing the function’s address; the kernel carves out a stack from the pool, pre-loads the initial register frame, and stores a pointer to that frame in its thread-control structure.\nStart the scheduler with osKernelStart. It internally raises the SVC that switches the core to Process Stack, loads the first thread’s context, and “returns” into the new thread. From the user’s perspective, control never comes back to main(); the created thread now owns the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,3,'2025-06-25_16-06-20','Call osKernelInitialize. It lets the kernel record where the global stack pool starts and resets its bookkeeping variables.\nWrite a thread function, a plain C function that never returns (usually a while-loop).\nCreate the thread with osCreateThread passing the function’s address; the kernel carves out a stack from the pool, pre-loads the initial register frame, and stores a pointer to that frame in its thread-control structure.\nStart the scheduler with osKernelStart. It internally raises the SVC that switches the core to Process Stack, loads the first thread’s context, and “returns” into the new thread. From the user’s perspective, control never comes back to main(); the created thread now owns the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,4,'2025-06-25_12-37-58','The stack pool is a contiguous block of RAM (declared in the linker script) reserved exclusively for thread stacks.\nEvery time the kernel needs a new thread it allocates a slice (e.g., 1 kB) from the pool, moving a “current-top” pointer downward. Because each slice starts on an 8-byte boundary and sizes are uniform, fragmentation is impossible and bookkeeping is trivial. In Lab 4 the scheduler will hold an array/list of thread-control blocks; each entry’s sp field will point somewhere in this pool. Context-switching merely swaps which sp is loaded into PSP, letting many threads coexist without overlapping memory.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,4,'2025-06-25_15-51-11','The stack pool is a contiguous block of RAM (declared in the linker script) reserved exclusively for thread stacks.\nEvery time the kernel needs a new thread it allocates a slice (e.g., 1 kB) from the pool, moving a “current-top” pointer downward. Because each slice starts on an 8-byte boundary and sizes are uniform, fragmentation is impossible and bookkeeping is trivial. In Lab 4 the scheduler will hold an array/list of thread-control blocks; each entry’s sp field will point somewhere in this pool. Context-switching merely swaps which sp is loaded into PSP, letting many threads coexist without overlapping memory.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,4,'2025-06-25_16-06-20','The stack pool is a contiguous block of RAM (declared in the linker script) reserved exclusively for thread stacks.\nEvery time the kernel needs a new thread it allocates a slice (e.g., 1 kB) from the pool, moving a “current-top” pointer downward. Because each slice starts on an 8-byte boundary and sizes are uniform, fragmentation is impossible and bookkeeping is trivial. In Lab 4 the scheduler will hold an array/list of thread-control blocks; each entry’s sp field will point somewhere in this pool. Context-switching merely swaps which sp is loaded into PSP, letting many threads coexist without overlapping memory.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',3,5,'2025-06-25_12-37-58','From the user’s perspective the kernel behaves like a library: they link against its header(s), call its services, and never need to touch or even see the privileged implementation. This clean boundary is essential for reliability, security, and maintainability.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',3,5,'2025-06-25_15-51-11','From the user’s perspective the kernel behaves like a library: they link against its header(s), call its services, and never need to touch or even see the privileged implementation. This clean boundary is essential for reliability, security, and maintainability.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',3,5,'2025-06-25_16-06-20','In this project the kernel is responsible for configuring every hardware peripheral, managing all RAM, switching CPU modes, running the scheduler, maintaining thread stacks, and servicing system calls; only it can touch device registers or adjust stack pointers. The user code, kept in main.c and individual thread files, contains the application’s own logic and interacts with the hardware exclusively by calling the services the kernel exposes in kernel.h, such as osCreateThread or osDelay, never by manipulating registers directly. This strict separation benefits the programmer because it keeps bugs in user code from crashing the entire system, lets developers think in high-level operations instead of low-level details, allows the same application code to be recompiled for a different microcontroller by changing only the kernel drivers, and makes teamwork and debugging easier by drawing a clear line between OS maintenance and feature development.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,1,'2025-07-26_19-32-59','With a queue, each thread control block (TCB) is enqueued when it\'s created and, when a thread yields, its TCB is moved from the front of the queue to the back. The scheduler always runs the thread at the front of the queue. This mimics the circular behavior of round-robin scheduling but using a queue makes the mechanism more explicit and intuitive. The main benefit is that the code for advancing to the next thread is simplified as it can just dequeue and enqueue, rather than tracking indices and wrapping around the array. Additionally, a queue makes it easier to add or remove threads dynamically, which can be more complicated with a fixed-size array.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',4,1,'2025-07-28_12-56-34','Round-robin can use a queue by always running the TCB at the front, then putting it at the back when it yields or its time slice ends. The scheduler just grabs whatever is now at the front next time. Our array plus an index that wraps around already behaves almost the same, so in that sense we’re basically using a circular queue already. A real queue can make the code feel cleaner (enqueue/dequeue calls) and makes adding or removing threads on the fly simpler. The tradeoff is a bit more overhead and possibly worse performance than a fixed-size array, which is very fast and cache friendly when you only have a few threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,1,'2025-07-28_13-07-31','Round-robin can use a queue by always running the TCB at the front, then putting it at the back when it yields or its time slice ends. The scheduler just grabs whatever is now at the front next time. Our array plus an index that wraps around already behaves almost the same, so in that sense we’re basically using a circular queue already. A real queue can make the code feel cleaner (enqueue/dequeue calls) and makes adding or removing threads on the fly simpler. The tradeoff is a bit more overhead and possibly worse performance than a fixed-size array, which is very fast and cache friendly when you only have a few threads.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,2,'2025-07-26_19-32-59','PendSV_Handler is an interrupt handler designed specifically for context switching. When triggered, it saves the current thread’s CPU registers, then calls a scheduler function to update which thread should run next. It then restores the CPU registers from the new thread’s stack and sets the stack pointer to that thread. When the function finishes, the processor resumes execution in the new thread.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',4,2,'2025-07-28_12-56-34','Round-robin can use a queue by always running the TCB at the front, then putting it at the back when it yields or its time slice ends. The scheduler just grabs whatever is now at the front next time. Our array plus an index that wraps around already behaves almost the same, so in that sense we’re basically using a circular queue already. A real queue can make the code feel cleaner (enqueue/dequeue calls) and makes adding or removing threads on the fly simpler. The tradeoff is a bit more overhead and possibly worse performance than a fixed-size array, which is very fast and cache friendly when you only have a few threads.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',4,2,'2025-07-28_13-07-31','PendSV_Handler is the interrupt Cortex-M4 uses to swap from one thread to another. When it triggers, the hardware has already pushed R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR onto the current thread’s stack, which the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) is pointing at. The handler’s first job is to manually save the rest of the callee-saved registers (R4–R11), so it subtracts space from the PSP and stores those there, then writes that new PSP value back into the current thread’s TCB. After the current context is safely parked, PendSV calls osSched, which simply picks the next thread to run and gives back the PSP stored in that thread’s TCB. PendSV then loads R4–R11 from that new stack and updates PSP to point there. When the handler returns, the hardware automatically pops the original set of registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) from the new PSP, so execution continues in the newly selected thread like nothing happened.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,3,'2025-07-26_19-32-59','If the scheduler always picks the highest-priority task, lower-priority threads may never run. If a high-priority task never yields or is always ready to run, it can monopolize the CPU, preventing other tasks from executing at all. This defeats the purpose of multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,3,'2025-07-28_12-56-34','If the scheduler always picks the highest-priority task, lower-priority threads may never run. If a high-priority task never yields or is always ready to run, it can monopolize the CPU, preventing other tasks from executing at all. This defeats the purpose of multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,3,'2025-07-28_13-07-31','If the scheduler always picks the highest-priority task, lower-priority threads may never run. If a high-priority task never yields or is always ready to run, it can monopolize the CPU, preventing other tasks from executing at all. This defeats the purpose of multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,4,'2025-07-26_19-32-59','Calling C functions makes the code much easier to write, read, and debug. However, calling C functions introduces some overhead, and can make the timing less predictable. Assembly offers more direct control over the hardware and can be more efficient, but it’s harder to write, read, and debug.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,4,'2025-07-28_12-56-34','Calling C functions makes the code much easier to write, read, and debug. However, calling C functions introduces some overhead, and can make the timing less predictable. Assembly offers more direct control over the hardware and can be more efficient, but it’s harder to write, read, and debug.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,4,'2025-07-28_13-07-31','Calling C functions makes the code much easier to write, read, and debug. However, calling C functions introduces some overhead, and can make the timing less predictable. Assembly offers more direct control over the hardware and can be more efficient, but it’s harder to write, read, and debug.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,5,'2025-07-26_19-32-59','The OS will still work, but since there’s only one thread, yielding will just return control to the scheduler, which will immediately select the same thread again. So, yielding has no effect except for a slight overhead from the context switch. The output will still show continuous printing.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,5,'2025-07-28_12-56-34','The OS will still work, but since there’s only one thread, yielding will just return control to the scheduler, which will immediately select the same thread again. So, yielding has no effect except for a slight overhead from the context switch. The output will still show continuous printing.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',4,5,'2025-07-28_13-07-31','The OS will still work, but since there’s only one thread, yielding will just return control to the scheduler, which will immediately select the same thread again. So, yielding has no effect except for a slight overhead from the context switch. The output will still show continuous printing.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',5,1,'2025-07-30_11-17-56','To pass data into a thread I first write the thread function so it takes one void* parameter. In main, I make whatever variable or struct I want to share, take its address, and hand that pointer to osCreateThread. Inside the kernel that pointer is copied into R0 when the stack frame for the new thread is built. Because the ARM ABI says R0 holds the first argument, the moment the CPU pops that frame and starts the thread, the pointer shows up in the thread’s own R0 register. I can cast it back to the right type on the thread side and use the data. The whole thing works because both the ABI and the way we laid out the initial stack agree on using R0 for the first parameter.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',5,1,'2025-07-30_11-29-10','To pass data into a thread I first write the thread function so it takes one void* parameter. In main, I make whatever variable or struct I want to share, take its address, and hand that pointer to osCreateThread. Inside the kernel that pointer is copied into R0 when the stack frame for the new thread is built. Because the ARM ABI says R0 holds the first argument, the moment the CPU pops that frame and starts the thread, the pointer shows up in the thread’s own R0 register. I can cast it back to the right type on the thread side and use the data. The whole thing works because both the ABI and the way we laid out the initial stack agree on using R0 for the first parameter.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',5,1,'2025-07-30_11-42-22','The user writes a thread function that takes one parameter (void* arg) and, when creating the thread, passes a pointer to whatever data they need (an int, a struct, etc.). While building the thread’s initial stack frame the kernel places that pointer into register R0 (the first argument register in the ARM AAPCS). The rest of the startup frame (xPSR, PC, LR, R1-R3, R12, R4-R11) is filled in as usual and PSP is pointed at the top of this frame. When the CPU pops this frame during the first BX back into Thread mode, R0 already holds the user’s pointer, so the C function sees the argument exactly as if it had been called in normal code. No extra copying is needed, and any type can be passed because the pointer is just a void*.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',5,2,'2025-07-30_11-17-56','osYield triggers a PendSV interrupt, which itself leads to a context switch. Calling it from inside any of the existing context-switch handlers would queue up another switch while one is already in progress. That means we’d be stacking PendSV requests on top of each other, messing with the stack and possibly losing the saved registers we’re in the middle of pushing or popping. In short, you’d corrupt the current context save/restore and crash.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',5,2,'2025-07-30_11-29-10','Bumping the tick from 1 ms to 0.1 ms would let the OS react about ten times faster, so timeouts and delays could be more precise and short animations would feel smoother. The price is ten times as many SysTick interrupts every second. More interrupts mean more CPU time spent in the handler and more context switches, so less time left for real work and higher power draw. There’s also more chance of jitter in user code if the CPU keeps getting yanked out to run the tick.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',5,2,'2025-07-30_11-42-22','osYield is meant to be used only by regular user threads. When a thread calls it, the function just flips the PendSV bit in the ICSR register and executes an ISB. That tells the CPU, “after you finish what you are doing, run the PendSV interrupt.” PendSV then handles the real context switch by saving the current thread’s registers and loading the next thread’s registers.\nIf you call osYield while you are already inside an interrupt like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler, you cause trouble. Inside these handlers the CPU is running on the MSP stack and part of the register frame is already saved. Setting PendSV pending again means that when the handler exits the CPU will re enter PendSV, but the stack frame now belongs to the interrupt, not to a normal thread. The scheduler treats that frame as if it were a thread context, so when it later pops the registers it gets garbage values and usually crashes into a HardFault because the return address or xPSR is wrong. In short, calling osYield from inside a context switching interrupt nests context switches in a way the kernel cannot handle and corrupts the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',5,3,'2025-07-30_11-17-56','Bumping the tick from 1 ms to 0.1 ms would let the OS react about ten times faster, so timeouts and delays could be more precise and short animations would feel smoother. The price is ten times as many SysTick interrupts every second. More interrupts mean more CPU time spent in the handler and more context switches, so less time left for real work and higher power draw. There’s also more chance of jitter in user code if the CPU keeps getting yanked out to run the tick.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',5,3,'2025-07-30_11-29-10','With co-operative multitasking the thread itself decides when to give up the CPU by calling something like osYield. The OS only switches when a thread asks politely, so the kernel side is simpler and there’s no risk of a thread being half-way through a critical section when it gets swapped out. Pre-emptive multitasking uses a timer; when the slice is up SysTick fires, PendSV runs, and the kernel forces a switch even if the thread wasn’t ready. That’s nicer for fairness and real-time deadlines, but the OS has more bookkeeping, and the user has to protect shared data because a switch can hit at any time.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',5,3,'2025-07-30_11-42-22','If the fundamental tick were shortened from one millisecond to a tenth of a millisecond the operating system could respond to timers and timeouts ten times faster, giving better real-time precision. The price is that the SysTick interrupt would run ten times as often, which means more CPU time lost in interrupt overhead, more context switches, and higher power consumption. Many HAL libraries assume a one-millisecond tick, so they would also need to be reviewed and possibly changed.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',5,4,'2025-07-30_11-17-56','With co-operative multitasking the thread itself decides when to give up the CPU by calling something like osYield. The OS only switches when a thread asks politely, so the kernel side is simpler and there’s no risk of a thread being half-way through a critical section when it gets swapped out. Pre-emptive multitasking uses a timer; when the slice is up SysTick fires, PendSV runs, and the kernel forces a switch even if the thread wasn’t ready. That’s nicer for fairness and real-time deadlines, but the OS has more bookkeeping, and the user has to protect shared data because a switch can hit at any time.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',5,4,'2025-07-30_11-29-10','The pre-emptive timer and manual osYield both end up setting PendSV pending, so they share the same path into PendSV_Handler. If a thread calls osYield, it resets its runtime counter and triggers PendSV immediately. Otherwise the counter keeps ticking down in SysTick; when it hits zero, SysTick also sets PendSV. Since both roads lead to the same handler, the two schemes mix without stepping on each other.\n',0,'s25'),('j46an',5,4,'2025-07-30_11-42-22','With co-operative multitasking a thread keeps the processor until it calls osYield or blocks on I/O. The operating system only switches tasks when the running thread explicitly asks. With pre-emptive multitasking every thread is given a time slice counted by the SysTick interrupt. When the slice expires the kernel forces a context switch even if the thread never yielded. From the user’s point of view co-operative code must remember to give up control, while pre-emptive code can run freely, trusting the scheduler to interrupt it at regular intervals. From the OS point of view co-operative switching is simpler because it is triggered by a software interrupt, while pre-emptive switching adds the need to keep a runtime counter in each TCB and to service SysTick regularly.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',5,5,'2025-07-30_11-17-56','The pre-emptive timer and manual osYield both end up setting PendSV pending, so they share the same path into PendSV_Handler. If a thread calls osYield, it resets its runtime counter and triggers PendSV immediately. Otherwise the counter keeps ticking down in SysTick; when it hits zero, SysTick also sets PendSV. Since both roads lead to the same handler, the two schemes mix without stepping on each other.\n',1,'s25'),('j46an',5,5,'2025-07-30_11-42-22','Both voluntary yields and timer expirations end up doing the same thing, which is to set the PendSV pending bit so that PendSV_Handler can run. A yielding thread sets the bit directly with osYield, while a pre-emptive slice end sets the bit from inside SysTick_Handler. Because they converge on exactly the same handler, the scheduler can mix threads that call osYield with threads that rely on the timer and still switch between them safely.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,1,'2025-05-18_17-01-17','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. Essentially it is software provided by the manufacturer of boards that simplifies writing code for the microcontroller by automatically including necessary references between the C code and the actual hardware of the controller.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,1,'2025-05-18_17-56-41','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. Essentially it is software provided by the manufacturer of boards that simplifies writing code for the microcontroller by automatically including necessary references between the C code and the actual hardware of the controller.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,1,'2025-05-18_18-09-19','HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. Essentially it is software provided by the manufacturer of boards that simplifies writing code for the microcontroller by automatically including necessary references between the C code and the actual hardware of the controller.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,2,'2025-05-18_17-01-17','The _io_putchar function assigns where character outputs should be displayed, whether on a debugging console on your computer or on a display connected to the microcontroller.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,2,'2025-05-18_17-56-41','The _io_putchar function assigns where character outputs should be displayed, whether on a debugging console on your computer or on a display connected to the microcontroller. It allows high-level functions like printf to send characters to hardware-specific outputs, typically UART, in embedded systems. __io_putchar is defined by the developer to transmit one character at a time through a specific output interface. This is crucial because printf internally outputs characters individually and depends on a function like __io_putchar to handle the actual transmission. By customizing __io_putchar, developers can easily redirect output to various hardware devices. This makes it an essential tool for flexible and hardware-independent console output in embedded applications.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,2,'2025-05-18_18-09-19','The _io_putchar function assigns where character outputs should be displayed, whether on a debugging console on your computer or on a display connected to the microcontroller. It allows high-level functions like printf to send characters to hardware-specific outputs, typically UART, in embedded systems. __io_putchar is defined by the developer to transmit one character at a time through a specific output interface. This is crucial because printf internally outputs characters individually and depends on a function like __io_putchar to handle the actual transmission. By customizing __io_putchar, developers can easily redirect output to various hardware devices. This makes it an essential tool for flexible and hardware-independent console output in embedded applications.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,3,'2025-05-18_17-01-17','You could have hypothetically printed out the memory locations of variables in the code then done math to determine if the memory was allocated correctly, however the debugger simplifies this by allowing us to directly see where memory is allocated in order to compare if variables were assigned to the correct locations.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,3,'2025-05-18_17-56-41','You could have hypothetically printed out the memory locations of variables in the code then done math to determine if the memory was allocated correctly. However, the debugger simplifies this by allowing us to directly see where memory is allocated to compare if variables were assigned to the correct locations throughout code execution. The debugger’s ability to directly access and display CPU register values allows developers to examine register contents throughout program execution. This is important because registers often hold temporary values, that influence code behavior. Understanding the state of these values is essential for diagnosing issues. printf is not a suitable alternative for this task because it only accesses and displays data that resides in memory—typically variables—and it operates at the application level, far removed from the CPU’s internal registers. There are register values that cannot be accessed by printf, which necessitates the use of the debugger.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,3,'2025-05-18_18-09-19','You could have hypothetically printed out the memory locations of variables in the code then done math to determine if the memory was allocated correctly. However, the debugger simplifies this by allowing us to directly see where memory is allocated to compare if variables were assigned to the correct locations throughout code execution. The debugger’s ability to directly access and display CPU register values allows developers to examine register contents throughout program execution. This is important because registers often hold temporary values, that influence code behavior. Understanding the state of these values is essential for diagnosing issues. printf is not a suitable alternative for this task because it only accesses and displays data that resides in memory—typically variables—and it operates at the application level, far removed from the CPU’s internal registers. There are register values that cannot be accessed by printf, which necessitates the use of the debugger.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,4,'2025-05-18_17-01-17','The stack grows towards 0 from the higher edge. So essentially from the MSP_init_val, we get a new value for PSP_Val by going 400 bytes down the stack. We needed to know the initial location of MSP so that when we assigned a value for PSP we knew we weren’t overwriting over values or using memory that wasn’t available.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,4,'2025-05-18_17-56-41','The stack grows towards 0 from the higher edge. So essentially from the MSP_init_val, we get a new value for PSP_Val by going 400 bytes down the stack. We needed to know the initial location of MSP so that when we assigned a value for PSP we knew we weren’t overwriting over values or using memory that wasn’t available.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,4,'2025-05-18_18-09-19','The stack grows towards 0 from the higher edge. So essentially from the MSP_init_val, we get a new value for PSP_Val by going 400 bytes down the stack from MSP_init_val. 400 bytes was chosen as a fixed offset between each new stack. We needed to know the initial location of MSP so that when we assigned a value for PSP we knew we weren’t overwriting over values or using memory that wasn’t available. By carefully calculating each PSP value relative to MSP, we can safely allocate multiple stacks within the available memory region, avoiding conflicts and ensuring correct thread behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,5,'2025-05-18_17-01-17','If we wanted to allocate a number of new stacks, in our code we would need to have a function that tracked the last stacks starting and go down 400 bytes from that value each time a new stack is assigned.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,5,'2025-05-18_17-56-41','If we wanted to allocate a number of new stacks, and the number is known at compile-time, you could define a statically allocated 2D array. Each stack starts at a known memory location, and since the layout is fixed at compile-time we can determine each stack’s start (or top, if the stack grows downward) using a function based on the size of each stack. This ensures all stack addresses are properly spaced, correctly aligned, and within RAM boundaries.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',1,5,'2025-05-18_18-09-19','If we wanted to allocate a number of new stacks, and the number is known at compile-time, you could define a statically allocated 2D array. Each stack starts at a known memory location, and since the layout is fixed at compile-time we can determine each stack’s start (or top, if the stack grows downward) using a function based on the size of each stack. This ensures all stack addresses are properly spaced, correctly aligned, and within RAM boundaries.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,1,'2025-05-27_12-53-02','',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,1,'2025-05-27_13-27-25','',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,1,'2025-05-27_15-10-07','When we changed the Program Counter (PC) to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to that function and began executing it. Since print_continuously loops forever, the function ran continuously, printing \"Hello PC\" without returning. This works because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to execute, so by assigning it a new function address, we redirected the program\'s execution to that function\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,2,'2025-05-27_12-53-02','A system call is a way for a program to request a service from the operating system, such as reading a file, creating a process, or accessing hardware. These tasks require special privileges, so they can\'t be done directly through normal instructions. Instead, the program uses a system call to switch from user mode to kernel mode, allowing the OS to safely perform the requested operation.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,2,'2025-05-27_13-27-25','A system call is a special kind of function call that a user program uses to request services from the operating system—such as reading from a file, allocating memory, or interacting with hardware. Unlike regular function calls, a system call triggers a controlled transition from user mode (where the program has limited privileges) to kernel mode (where the operating system has full access to hardware and memory). This switch is crucial because it allows user programs to interact with the kernel safely, without exposing sensitive system resources to potentially faulty or malicious code. System calls act as the secure interface between user applications and the kernel, ensuring that only trusted code in the OS can directly access or manage hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,2,'2025-05-27_15-10-07','A system call is a special kind of function call that a user program uses to request services from the operating system—such as reading from a file, allocating memory, or interacting with hardware. Unlike regular function calls, a system call triggers a controlled transition from user mode (where the program has limited privileges) to kernel mode (where the operating system has full access to hardware and memory). This switch is crucial because it allows user programs to interact with the kernel safely, without exposing sensitive system resources to potentially faulty or malicious code. System calls act as the secure interface between user applications and the kernel, ensuring that only trusted code in the OS can directly access or manage hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,3,'2025-05-27_12-53-02','The stack was set up by allocating 16 locations on the PSP stack, one for the xPSR, one for the function return address and 14 for the registers. The register values need tot be stored on the stack because prior to the system running, there needs to be fake thread to trick the system into running the function. “Setting up a thread’s stack” means preparing that memory so the thread can start running like it’s mid-way through a function call. The system fakes it—placing values on the stack and setting registers so that, when the thread is scheduled, it jumps into a function (usually a thread start function) as if it were called naturally.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,3,'2025-05-27_13-27-25','The stack was set up by allocating 16 locations on the PSP stack, one for the xPSR, one for the function return address and 14 for the registers. The register values need tot be stored on the stack because prior to the system running, there needs to be fake thread to trick the system into running the function. “Setting up a thread’s stack” means preparing that memory so the thread can start running like it’s mid-way through a function call. The system fakes it—placing values on the stack and setting registers so that, when the thread is scheduled, it jumps into a function (usually a thread start function) as if it were called naturally.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,3,'2025-05-27_15-10-07','The stack was set up by allocating 16 locations on the PSP stack, one for the xPSR, one for the function return address and 14 for the registers. The register values need tot be stored on the stack because prior to the system running, there needs to be fake thread to trick the system into running the function. “Setting up a thread’s stack” means preparing that memory so the thread can start running like it’s mid-way through a function call. The system fakes it—placing values on the stack and setting registers so that, when the thread is scheduled, it jumps into a function (usually a thread start function) as if it were called naturally.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,4,'2025-05-27_12-53-02','The code would crash with a hard fault because the CPU would try to use an invalid stack pointer when running the thread. By default, the processor uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is shared with the operating system. Threads are supposed to use the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to keep their stacks separate. If we don’t set the PSP before switching to thread mode, the CPU tries to push data onto an undefined or wrong location in memory, causing a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,4,'2025-05-27_13-27-25','Before a thread can run, the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) must be set to the top of the thread’s stack in memory—a valid and properly aligned stack region that was allocated for that thread. The __set_PSP function assigns this specific memory location to the PSP. If we skip this step, the CPU has no valid stack to use when switching to thread mode. It will attempt to access or modify memory through an uninitialized or incorrect PSP value, which leads to a hard fault. This happens because the processor tries to push or pop values (like register contents) to a location that may not be mapped or aligned, violating memory safety. Setting the PSP ensures that each thread uses its own dedicated stack space, preventing corruption and misalignment that would otherwise crash the system.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,4,'2025-05-27_15-10-07','Before a thread can run, the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) must be set to the top of the thread’s stack in memory—a valid and properly aligned stack region that was allocated for that thread. The __set_PSP function assigns this specific memory location to the PSP. If we skip this step, the CPU has no valid stack to use when switching to thread mode. It will attempt to access or modify memory through an uninitialized or incorrect PSP value, which leads to a hard fault. This happens because the processor tries to push or pop values (like register contents) to a location that may not be mapped or aligned, violating memory safety. Setting the PSP ensures that each thread uses its own dedicated stack space, preventing corruption and misalignment that would otherwise crash the system.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,5,'2025-05-27_12-53-02','If the thread function returned instead of looping continuously, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the stack to resume execution—but since the thread wasn’t started like a normal function call, there’s no valid return address. This would cause the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location and trigger a hard fault, crashing the program.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,5,'2025-05-27_13-27-25','If the thread function returned instead of looping continuously, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the stack to resume execution—but since the thread wasn’t started like a normal function call, there’s no valid return address. This would cause the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location and trigger a hard fault, crashing the program.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',2,5,'2025-05-27_15-10-07','If the thread function returned instead of looping continuously, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the stack to resume execution—but since the thread wasn’t started like a normal function call, there’s no valid return address. This would cause the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location and trigger a hard fault, crashing the program.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,1,'2025-06-26_18-32-51','Each .c file is compiled separately into machine code. Then the linker combines the machine code into one executable, taking into account references between each of the different files such as function calls/variables. Startup code is also added by the linker into the executable file.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,1,'2025-06-27_11-38-25','The process has three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, the preprocessor handles directives like #include and #define, producing a single expanded source file. Then the compiler translates this into an object file (.o) with machine code. Finally, the linker takes all the object files from different .c files and combines them—resolving function and variable references—into a single executable file, adding in startup code if needed.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,1,'2025-06-27_11-52-39','The process has three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, the preprocessor handles directives like #include and #define, producing a single expanded source file. Then the compiler translates this into an object file (.o) with machine code. Finally, the linker takes all the object files from different .c files and combines them—resolving function and variable references—into a single executable file, adding in startup code if needed.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,1,'2025-06-27_13-26-47','The process has three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, the preprocessor handles directives like #include and #define, producing a single expanded source file. Then the compiler translates this into an object file (.o) with machine code. Finally, the linker takes all the object files from different .c files and combines them—resolving function and variable references—into a single executable file, adding in startup code if needed.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,2,'2025-06-26_18-32-51','Using #include on a .c file is a bad idea because it copies the entire implementation into the current file, which can lead to multiple definitions of functions or global variables when the same .c file is included in more than one place. This breaks the modular design of C programs, causes linker errors (like \"multiple definition\" errors), and makes code harder to maintain. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only their corresponding .h headers should be included to expose needed declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,2,'2025-06-27_11-38-25','Using #include on a .c file is a bad idea because it copies the entire implementation into the current file, which can lead to multiple definitions of functions or global variables when the same .c file is included in more than one place. This breaks the modular design of C programs, causes linker errors (like \"multiple definition\" errors), and makes code harder to maintain. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only their corresponding .h headers should be included to expose needed declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,2,'2025-06-27_11-52-39','Using #include on a .c file is a bad idea because it copies the entire implementation into the current file, which can lead to multiple definitions of functions or global variables when the same .c file is included in more than one place. This breaks the modular design of C programs, causes linker errors (like \"multiple definition\" errors), and makes code harder to maintain. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only their corresponding .h headers should be included to expose needed declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,2,'2025-06-27_13-26-47','Using #include on a .c file is a bad idea because it copies the entire implementation into the current file, which can lead to multiple definitions of functions or global variables when the same .c file is included in more than one place. This breaks the modular design of C programs, causes linker errors (like \"multiple definition\" errors), and makes code harder to maintain. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only their corresponding .h headers should be included to expose needed declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,3,'2025-06-26_18-32-51','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, you generally follow these steps:\nInitialize the kernel – This sets up internal data structures and captures the starting memory location of the main stack to use for allocating thread stacks.\nCreate the thread – You provide the function the thread will run. During this step, memory (usually a chunk of the stack) is allocated specifically for the thread, and its stack pointer is set up with values the CPU expects (like xPSR, PC, LR, etc.), so the thread \"thinks\" it was already running and can resume correctly.\nStart the kernel – This triggers an SVC (Supervisor Call), which sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to the thread’s stack and jumps into the thread’s function as if it had always been running.\nLet the thread execute – Once the setup is complete, the CPU switches context and the thread runs its assigned function in its own stack space.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,3,'2025-06-27_11-38-25','From the user\'s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves calling specific functions provided by the kernel. First, the user calls osKernelInitialize() to initialize the kernel, which sets up internal data structures and stores the starting point of the main stack for future use. Next, the user calls osCreateThread() and passes in the function the thread should run; during this step, a stack is allocated for the thread and initialized with values like xPSR and PC so the thread appears \"ready to run.\" Then, the user calls osKernelStart(), which triggers a software interrupt (SVC) that sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) and jumps into the thread function. From that point on, the thread runs in its own context using the memory that was allocated for it.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,3,'2025-06-27_11-52-39','From the user\'s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves calling specific functions provided by the kernel. First, the user calls osKernelInitialize() to initialize the kernel, which sets up internal data structures and stores the starting point of the main stack for future use. Next, the user calls osCreateThread() and passes in the function the thread should run; during this step, a stack is allocated for the thread and initialized with values like xPSR and PC so the thread appears \"ready to run.\" Then, the user calls osKernelStart(), which triggers a software interrupt (SVC) that sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) and jumps into the thread function. From that point on, the thread runs in its own context using the memory that was allocated for it.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,3,'2025-06-27_13-26-47','From the user\'s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves calling specific functions provided by the kernel. First, the user writes the function that the thread will run—this is a user-defined function that contains the code the thread will execute. Then, the user calls osKernelInitialize() to initialize the kernel, which sets up internal data structures and stores the starting point of the main stack for future thread stack allocation. Next, the user calls osCreateThread() and passes in the function they wrote; during this step, a private stack is allocated for the thread and initialized with values like xPSR and PC so the thread appears \"ready to run.\" Finally, the user calls osKernelStart(), which triggers a software interrupt (SVC) to set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) and jump into the thread. The thread now runs in its own memory space, managed by the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,4,'2025-06-26_18-32-51','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory used to hold the stacks for all threads created by the OS. Each thread needs its own stack space to store local variables, function call information, and CPU state during context switches. In future labs, we’ll use the stack pool to allocate a separate stack for each thread. This allows multiple threads to run \"concurrently\" (in a time-sliced manner) without interfering with each other’s data, because each one operates in its own section of the stack pool.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,4,'2025-06-27_11-38-25','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory used to hold the stacks for all threads created by the OS. Each thread needs its own stack space to store local variables, function call information, and CPU state during context switches. In future labs, we’ll use the stack pool to allocate a separate stack for each thread. This allows multiple threads to run \"concurrently\" (in a time-sliced manner) without interfering with each other’s data, because each one operates in its own section of the stack pool.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,4,'2025-06-27_11-52-39','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory used to hold the stacks for all threads created by the OS. Each thread needs its own stack space to store local variables, function call information, and CPU state during context switches. In future labs, we’ll use the stack pool to allocate a separate stack for each thread. This allows multiple threads to run \"concurrently\" (in a time-sliced manner) without interfering with each other’s data, because each one operates in its own section of the stack pool.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,4,'2025-06-27_13-26-47','The stack pool is a dedicated region of memory reserved by the operating system to hold the stacks for both the kernel and any user-created threads. Each thread is allocated its own private section of the stack pool to store local variables, return addresses, and saved CPU registers during context switches. The OS itself also uses a portion of the stack pool for managing system-level tasks and handling interrupts. In future labs, we’ll dynamically allocate different parts of the stack pool to different threads so that they can run concurrently (through time slicing), while keeping their execution contexts isolated and protected.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,5,'2025-06-26_18-32-51','The user refers to code written by the application developer — like main.c — where threads are created and behaviors are defined. The kernel refers to the low-level OS code — like kernel.c — that manages thread scheduling, stack allocation, and system calls. This separation is important because the user doesn\'t need to worry about how threads are implemented or how the hardware is configured; they just use clean interfaces provided by the kernel. It makes the system easier to understand, maintain, and expand without breaking core functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,5,'2025-06-27_11-38-25','The user refers to code written by the application developer — like main.c — where threads are created and behaviors are defined. The kernel refers to the low-level OS code — like kernel.c — that manages thread scheduling, stack allocation, and system calls. This separation is important because the user doesn\'t need to worry about how threads are implemented or how the hardware is configured; they just use clean interfaces provided by the kernel. It makes the system easier to understand, maintain, and expand without breaking core functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,5,'2025-06-27_11-52-39','The user refers to code written by the application developer — like main.c — where threads are created and behaviors are defined. The kernel refers to the low-level OS code — like kernel.c — that manages thread scheduling, stack allocation, and system calls. This separation is important because the user doesn\'t need to worry about how threads are implemented or how the hardware is configured; they just use clean interfaces provided by the kernel. It makes the system easier to understand, maintain, and expand without breaking core functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',3,5,'2025-06-27_13-26-47','The user refers to code written by the application developer — like main.c — where threads are created and behaviors are defined. The kernel refers to the low-level OS code — like kernel.c — that manages thread scheduling, stack allocation, and system calls. This separation is important because the user doesn\'t need to worry about how threads are implemented or how the hardware is configured; they just use clean interfaces provided by the kernel. It makes the system easier to understand, maintain, and expand without breaking core functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,1,'2025-07-03_12-22-36','The scheduler would remove the TCB a the front of the queue, run that thread for the designated amount of time, then add it back to the queue. Then the next TCB at the front of the queue is removed from the front of the queue, ran for its designated amount of time, and then added back to the queue. This process then repeats for each of the TCBs in the queue. The benefits of using a queue are that it is a simpler process to switch between the thread that is running compared to an array, as it a queue naturally follows the First-In First-Out structure of roundrobin scheduling.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,1,'2025-07-03_12-39-04','In round-robin scheduling, a queue can be used to manage TCBs by removing the TCB at the front of the queue, running that thread for a fixed time slice, and then re-enqueuing it at the back. This process naturally rotates through all threads in a fair, First-In First-Out (FIFO) manner, which aligns well with the goals of round-robin scheduling.\nHowever, it\'s worth noting that the current scheduler implementation may already be using a queue-like approach, even if the underlying data structure is an array. For example, by maintaining an index that wraps around using modulo arithmetic, the scheduler can simulate queue behavior (i.e., rotating through threads in order), so switching to an actual queue structure may not change functionality significantly.\nAdditionally, using a true queue (such as a linked list or dynamic structure) introduces extra overhead, such as memory allocation, pointer management, or more complex enqueue/dequeue operations. In contrast, an array-based implementation can be faster and more memory-efficient in embedded systems where resources are limited.\nSo while using a queue for TCBs aligns well conceptually with round-robin scheduling, the actual benefit over an array-based system depends on the design constraints and the existing scheduler’s logic. It’s an \"interesting\" idea, but not necessarily a more efficient one.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,1,'2025-07-03_12-45-59','In round-robin scheduling, a queue can be used to manage TCBs by removing the TCB at the front of the queue, running that thread for a fixed time slice, and then re-enqueuing it at the back. This process naturally rotates through all threads in a fair, First-In First-Out (FIFO) manner, which aligns well with the goals of round-robin scheduling.\nHowever, it\'s worth noting that the current scheduler implementation may already be using a queue-like approach, even if the underlying data structure is an array. For example, by maintaining an index that wraps around using modulo arithmetic, the scheduler can simulate queue behavior (i.e., rotating through threads in order), so switching to an actual queue structure may not change functionality significantly.\nAdditionally, using a true queue (such as a linked list or dynamic structure) introduces extra overhead, such as memory allocation, pointer management, or more complex enqueue/dequeue operations. In contrast, an array-based implementation can be faster and more memory-efficient in embedded systems where resources are limited.\nSo while using a queue for TCBs aligns well conceptually with round-robin scheduling, the actual benefit over an array-based system depends on the design constraints and the existing scheduler’s logic. It’s an \"interesting\" idea, but not necessarily a more efficient one.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,2,'2025-07-03_12-22-36','The PendSV_Handler is triggered when a context switch is needed, not necessarily when a thread finishes running. It saves the CPU state (registers) of the current thread onto its stack and stores its stack pointer in its TCB. Then, the osched() function selects the next thread to run. The PendSV_Handler then loads the stack pointer of the next thread, restores its saved registers, and the CPU resumes execution from where that thread left off.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,2,'2025-07-03_12-39-04','The PendSV_Handler is triggered when a context switch is required, such as when a thread yields or its time slice expires. To perform the switch, the handler first reads the PSP (Process Stack Pointer), which points to the current thread\'s stack. Using this stack pointer, it saves the remaining callee-saved registers—specifically, R4 to R11—onto the current thread\'s stack. These registers are not saved automatically by the hardware during an exception, so the OS must save them manually to preserve the thread\'s state.\nNext, the updated PSP value is stored into the thread’s TCB, so the OS knows where to resume the thread later. The scheduler function, osSched(), is then called to determine the next thread to run.\nAfter the next thread is chosen, the OS loads that thread’s saved PSP from its TCB and sets it as the current PSP. It then restores the R4 to R11 registers from that thread’s stack. When the PendSV_Handler finishes, the CPU automatically restores the remaining registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) and returns to the new thread’s execution point, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,2,'2025-07-03_12-45-59','The PendSV_Handler is triggered when a context switch is required, such as when a thread yields or its time slice expires. To perform the switch, the handler first reads the PSP (Process Stack Pointer), which points to the current thread\'s stack. Using this stack pointer, it saves the remaining callee-saved registers—specifically, R4 to R11—onto the current thread\'s stack. These registers are not saved automatically by the hardware during an exception, so the OS must save them manually to preserve the thread\'s state.\nNext, the updated PSP value is stored into the thread’s TCB, so the OS knows where to resume the thread later. The scheduler function, osSched(), is then called to determine the next thread to run.\nAfter the next thread is chosen, the OS loads that thread’s saved PSP from its TCB and sets it as the current PSP. It then restores the R4 to R11 registers from that thread’s stack. When the PendSV_Handler finishes, the CPU automatically restores the remaining registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) and returns to the new thread’s execution point, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,3,'2025-07-03_12-22-36','This is a bad idea because always picking the highest-priority task can lead to starvation of lower-priority threads. If there are always higher-priority threads ready to run, the low-priority ones may never get CPU time. This can break essential background functions, cause system instability, or lead to unresponsiveness. In real-world systems, we need fairness—scheduling policies must ensure that all threads get a chance to run, even if their priority is low. This is why systems often use techniques like priority aging or priority-based round-robin instead of strict greedy scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,3,'2025-07-03_12-39-04','This is a bad idea because always picking the highest-priority task can lead to starvation of lower-priority threads. If there are always higher-priority threads ready to run, the low-priority ones may never get CPU time. This can break essential background functions, cause system instability, or lead to unresponsiveness. In real-world systems, we need fairness—scheduling policies must ensure that all threads get a chance to run, even if their priority is low. This is why systems often use techniques like priority aging or priority-based round-robin instead of strict greedy scheduling.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,3,'2025-07-03_12-45-59','This is a bad idea because always picking the highest-priority task can lead to starvation of lower-priority threads. If there are always higher-priority threads ready to run, the low-priority ones may never get CPU time. This can break essential background functions, cause system instability, or lead to unresponsiveness. In real-world systems, we need fairness—scheduling policies must ensure that all threads get a chance to run, even if their priority is low. This is why systems often use techniques like priority aging or priority-based round-robin instead of strict greedy scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,4,'2025-07-03_12-22-36','Calling C functions from PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler makes the code easier to read, write, and debug. It also helps with development speed and maintainability. However, it comes at the cost of lower control and potential performance overhead. For low-level operations like saving and restoring context, a small amount of carefully written assembly is still necessary. The best approach is usually a hybrid: use assembly for the truly low-level parts, and C for everything else.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,4,'2025-07-03_12-39-04','Calling C functions from PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler makes the code easier to read, write, and debug. It also helps with development speed and maintainability. However, it comes at the cost of lower control and potential performance overhead. For low-level operations like saving and restoring context, a small amount of carefully written assembly is still necessary. The best approach is usually a hybrid: use assembly for the truly low-level parts, and C for everything else.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,4,'2025-07-03_12-45-59','Calling C functions from PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler makes the code easier to read, write, and debug. It also helps with development speed and maintainability. However, it comes at the cost of lower control and potential performance overhead. For low-level operations like saving and restoring context, a small amount of carefully written assembly is still necessary. The best approach is usually a hybrid: use assembly for the truly low-level parts, and C for everything else.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,5,'2025-07-03_12-22-36','Yes, the OS will still function correctly with only a single thread. When the thread yields, the context switch mechanism will save and restore its state, and the scheduler will simply pick the same thread again. However, this is inefficient: there’s no need for scheduling or context switching if there’s only one thread. The overhead of the OS is unnecessary in this case, and the system could be simplified by just running the thread directly without an operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,5,'2025-07-03_12-39-04','Yes, the OS will still function correctly with only a single thread. When the thread yields, the context switch mechanism will save and restore its state, and the scheduler will simply pick the same thread again. However, this is inefficient: there’s no need for scheduling or context switching if there’s only one thread. The overhead of the OS is unnecessary in this case, and the system could be simplified by just running the thread directly without an operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',4,5,'2025-07-03_12-45-59','Yes, the OS will still function correctly with only a single thread. When the thread yields, the context switch mechanism will save and restore its state, and the scheduler will simply pick the same thread again. However, this is inefficient: there’s no need for scheduling or context switching if there’s only one thread. The overhead of the OS is unnecessary in this case, and the system could be simplified by just running the thread directly without an operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',5,1,'2025-07-17_10-59-44','To pass an argument to a thread, the user must provide a void* argument when creating the thread. This pointer typically points to a structure or variable containing all the data the thread function needs. In C, the thread function is defined to accept a void* parameter, allowing any data type to be passed after casting.\nWhen the OS creates the thread, it sets up the new thread\'s stack and context. As part of this context, the OS loads the argument pointer into register R0, following the ARM Procedure Call Standard, which specifies that the first argument to a function is passed in R0. This ensures that when the thread starts running, the pointer is already in the correct register for the function to access it.\nIt works because the thread function is called like any other function with arguments, and the OS sets up the initial state (including R0) so that execution begins with the expected parameters already in place.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',5,1,'2025-07-17_11-13-20','To pass an argument to a thread, the user must provide a void* argument when creating the thread. This pointer typically points to a structure or variable containing all the data the thread function needs. In C, the thread function is defined to accept a void* parameter, allowing any data type to be passed after casting.\nWhen the OS creates the thread, it sets up the new thread\'s stack and context. As part of this context, the OS loads the argument pointer into register R0, following the ARM Procedure Call Standard, which specifies that the first argument to a function is passed in R0. This ensures that when the thread starts running, the pointer is already in the correct register for the function to access it.\nIt works because the thread function is called like any other function with arguments, and the OS sets up the initial state (including R0) so that execution begins with the expected parameters already in place.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',5,2,'2025-07-17_10-59-44','You can\'t call osYield() from within PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because osYield() itself triggers a context switch by setting the PendSV interrupt pending. Calling it from within these handlers, which are already in the middle of a context switch, can cause nested or recursive context switches, which is unsafe.\nIf you do call osYield() from one of these handlers, it can corrupt the stack, lead to unpredictable behavior, or cause the system to crash. This is because the OS might not complete the current context switch properly before starting another, violating the atomicity of the context switch process.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',5,2,'2025-07-17_11-13-20','You cannot call osYield() inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler (and SVC_Handler_Main), or SysTick_Handler because osYield() is a system call that triggers a PendSV interrupt to initiate a context switch. Calling it from within one of these handlers would attempt to nest a system call inside an interrupt context, which is not allowed. The OS is already in the middle of handling a context switch when it\'s inside one of these handlers, so triggering another system call would result in nested context switching, leading to corruption of the stack or thread state. This can cause a hardfault, which crashes the system. To avoid these critical failures, system calls like osYield() must only be made from thread context—not from within interrupt handlers where the OS is already performing low-level context-switching operations.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',5,3,'2025-07-17_10-59-44','Reducing the OS clock tick interval from 1 ms to 0.1 ms means the system can perform finer-grained time slicing, which allows for more responsive multitasking. This is especially beneficial for real-time systems requiring high-frequency updates or very short response times. However, the downside is increased overhead. A timer interrupt will now occur 10 times more often, increasing the number of context switches and reducing the CPU time available for actual thread execution. It also consumes more power and can lead to timing jitter or missed deadlines if the system cannot handle the increased interrupt load efficiently.\n',0,'s25'),('j4bakker',5,3,'2025-07-17_11-13-20','Reducing the OS clock tick interval from 1 ms to 0.1 ms means the system can perform finer-grained time slicing, which allows for more responsive multitasking. This is especially beneficial for real-time systems requiring high-frequency updates or very short response times. However, the downside is increased overhead. A timer interrupt will now occur 10 times more often, increasing the number of context switches and reducing the CPU time available for actual thread execution. It also consumes more power and can lead to timing jitter or missed deadlines if the system cannot handle the increased interrupt load efficiently.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',5,4,'2025-07-17_10-59-44','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking differ in how and when threads give up control of the CPU. In co-operative multitasking, threads must explicitly yield control by calling a function like osYield(), meaning the user decides when context switches happen. This approach is simpler for the OS to manage but relies on the programmer to ensure fairness between threads. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking uses a hardware timer (e.g., SysTick) to interrupt threads at fixed intervals, automatically switching between them regardless of what they are doing. From the OS’s perspective, pre-emptive multitasking requires maintaining timers, tracking time slices, and handling interrupts to ensure each thread gets a fair share of CPU time. While more complex, pre-emption provides better responsiveness and robustness in systems where thread cooperation cannot be guaranteed.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',5,4,'2025-07-17_11-13-20','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking differ in how and when threads give up control of the CPU. In co-operative multitasking, threads must explicitly yield control by calling a function like osYield(), meaning the user decides when context switches happen. This approach is simpler for the OS to manage but relies on the programmer to ensure fairness between threads. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking uses a hardware timer (e.g., SysTick) to interrupt threads at fixed intervals, automatically switching between them regardless of what they are doing. From the OS’s perspective, pre-emptive multitasking requires maintaining timers, tracking time slices, and handling interrupts to ensure each thread gets a fair share of CPU time. While more complex, pre-emption provides better responsiveness and robustness in systems where thread cooperation cannot be guaranteed.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',5,5,'2025-07-17_10-59-44','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching can work together seamlessly because they both ultimately lead to the same outcome: triggering a context switch. Yielding is voluntary—when a thread calls osYield(), it signals the OS to switch to another thread immediately. Pre-emptive switching, on the other hand, is automatic and time-based, typically using a timer interrupt (like SysTick) to switch threads once their time slice has expired. Since both mechanisms invoke the same low-level switching logic (often through PendSV), they can coexist in a system. This hybrid approach allows for flexibility: real-time responsiveness through pre-emption and controlled scheduling through yielding, which can be useful when specific thread timing behavior is required.\n',1,'s25'),('j4bakker',5,5,'2025-07-17_11-13-20','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching can work together seamlessly because they both ultimately lead to the same outcome: triggering a context switch. Yielding is voluntary—when a thread calls osYield(), it signals the OS to switch to another thread immediately. Pre-emptive switching, on the other hand, is automatic and time-based, typically using a timer interrupt (like SysTick) to switch threads once their time slice has expired. Since both mechanisms invoke the same low-level switching logic (often through PendSV), they can coexist in a system. This hybrid approach allows for flexibility: real-time responsiveness through pre-emption and controlled scheduling through yielding, which can be useful when specific thread timing behavior is required.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,1,'2025-06-25_23-06-30','HAL, which stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer is a layer of software that allows embedded developers to interact with hardware of a microcontroller with high level function calls instead of accessing registers directly. The layer helps simplify development by providing standardized APIs for peripherals which make the code much easier to maintain across different microcontrollers. It’s often used in embedded projects to help speed up prototyping, reduce complexity and support a consistent code structure.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,1,'2025-06-26_18-17-37','HAL, which stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer is a layer of software that allows embedded developers to interact with hardware of a microcontroller with high level function calls instead of accessing registers directly. The layer helps simplify development by providing standardized APIs for peripherals which make the code much easier to maintain across different microcontrollers. It’s often used in embedded projects to help speed up prototyping, reduce complexity and support a consistent code structure.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,1,'2025-06-26_19-20-33','HAL, which stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer is a layer of software that allows embedded developers to interact with hardware of a microcontroller with high level function calls instead of accessing registers directly. The layer helps simplify development by providing standardized APIs for peripherals which make the code much easier to maintain across different microcontrollers. It’s often used in embedded projects to help speed up prototyping, reduce complexity and support a consistent code structure.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,1,'2025-06-26_20-51-30','HAL, which stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer is a layer of software that allows embedded developers to interact with hardware of a microcontroller with high level function calls instead of accessing registers directly. The layer helps simplify development by providing standardized APIs for peripherals which make the code much easier to maintain across different microcontrollers. It’s often used in embedded projects to help speed up prototyping, reduce complexity and support a consistent code structure.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,2,'2025-06-25_23-06-30','The __io_putchar function is used to redirect output from functions (ex. printf) to a specific output device. For embedded systems, printf(), for example, doesn’t know where it should be sending its output by default (there is no screen or console on a computer), and so by defining __io_putchar, you’re essentially telling the system how to handle each character that printf() wats to print. The function is needed to make standard C functions work properly on microcontrollers.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,2,'2025-06-26_18-17-37','The __io_putchar function is used to redirect output from functions (ex. printf) to a specific output device. For embedded systems, printf(), for example, doesn’t know where it should be sending its output by default (there is no screen or console on a computer), and so by defining __io_putchar, you’re essentially telling the system how to handle each character that printf() wats to print. The function is needed to make standard C functions work properly on microcontrollers.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,2,'2025-06-26_19-20-33','The __io_putchar function is used to direct output from standard C functions like printf() to a specific hardware interface, such as the UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver-Transmitter). In embedded systems, there is no default output device like a screen or console, so printf() doesn’t know where to send its output. By implementing __io_putchar, you define how each character should be transmitted, typically by sending it over the UART to a connected serial console. This function is essential for enabling debug output or logging on microcontrollers where standard output streams are not available by default.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,2,'2025-06-26_20-51-30','The __io_putchar function acts as a hardware-specific implementation of how characters are output when using functions like printf() in embedded systems. Since microcontrollers do not have a default output console, printf() needs to be redirected to a physical interface such as UART.\nThis function is where the developer defines how each character is sent, depending on the hardware in use. For example, if UART is being used, __io_putchar would transmit characters through the UART transmit register. If the project used USB instead, the implementation would be completely different.\nBecause the output hardware can vary between projects, the programmer must adapt __io_putchar to match the specific peripheral. It is essential for enabling debugging and output in systems where printf() would otherwise have no effect.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,3,'2025-06-25_23-06-30','In this lab, the debugger was used because printf() is limited in what it can show and where it an be used. Therefore, debuggers allow us to inspect the exact state of the microcontroller (ex. registers, memory, variable values etc.) at any point in time during the execution, while printf() only works after certain systems are set up and cannot show low level information like the stack pointer and core register.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,3,'2025-06-26_18-17-37','In this lab, the debugger was used because printf() is limited in what it can show and where it an be used. Therefore, debuggers allow us to inspect the exact state of the microcontroller (ex. registers, memory, variable values etc.) at any point in time during the execution, while printf() only works after certain systems are set up and cannot show low level information like the stack pointer and core register.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,3,'2025-06-26_19-20-33','In this lab, the debugger was used because printf() is limited in what it can show and where it an be used. Therefore, debuggers allow us to inspect the exact state of the microcontroller (ex. registers, memory, variable values etc.) at any point in time during the execution, while printf() only works after certain systems are set up and cannot show low level information like the stack pointer and core register.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,3,'2025-06-26_20-51-30','In this lab, the debugger was used because printf() is limited in what it can show and where it an be used. Therefore, debuggers allow us to inspect the exact state of the microcontroller (ex. registers, memory, variable values etc.) at any point in time during the execution, while printf() only works after certain systems are set up and cannot show low level information like the stack pointer and core register.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,4,'2025-06-25_23-06-30','In this lab we used manual stack allocation, where we set up the PSP ourselves. We started with the MSP_init_val (initial value of MSP which typically points to top of the RAM), which tells us where the stack begins in the memory. To get the new value of PSP_val, we subtracted a fixed number of bytes from MSP_init_val, which created a new, lower section in RAM that we manually assign to be used by the PSP. The region then acts like a second stack which we needed to simulate switching from main stack to the process stack.\nWe needed to know the initial location of the MSP because it allowed us to choose a safe, unused area in the memory for the PSP without overlapping with the existing stack or other memory regions and avoid risking corrupting data or crashing the system.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,4,'2025-06-26_18-17-37','In this lab we used manual stack allocation, where we set up the PSP ourselves. We started with the MSP_init_val (initial value of MSP which typically points to top of the RAM), which tells us where the stack begins in the memory. To get the new value of PSP_val, we subtracted a fixed number of bytes from MSP_init_val, which created a new, lower section in RAM that we manually assign to be used by the PSP. The region then acts like a second stack which we needed to simulate switching from main stack to the process stack.\nWe needed to know the initial location of the MSP because it allowed us to choose a safe, unused area in the memory for the PSP without overlapping with the existing stack or other memory regions and avoid risking corrupting data or crashing the system.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,4,'2025-06-26_19-20-33','In this lab we used manual stack allocation, where we set up the PSP ourselves. We started with the MSP_init_val (initial value of MSP which typically points to top of the RAM), which tells us where the stack begins in the memory. To get the new value of PSP_val, we subtracted a fixed number of bytes from MSP_init_val, which created a new, lower section in RAM that we manually assign to be used by the PSP. The region then acts like a second stack which we needed to simulate switching from main stack to the process stack.\nWe needed to know the initial location of the MSP because it allowed us to choose a safe, unused area in the memory for the PSP without overlapping with the existing stack or other memory regions and avoid risking corrupting data or crashing the system.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,4,'2025-06-26_20-51-30','In this lab we used manual stack allocation, where we set up the PSP ourselves. We started with the MSP_init_val (initial value of MSP which typically points to top of the RAM), which tells us where the stack begins in the memory. To get the new value of PSP_val, we subtracted a fixed number of bytes from MSP_init_val, which created a new, lower section in RAM that we manually assign to be used by the PSP. The region then acts like a second stack which we needed to simulate switching from main stack to the process stack.\nWe needed to know the initial location of the MSP because it allowed us to choose a safe, unused area in the memory for the PSP without overlapping with the existing stack or other memory regions and avoid risking corrupting data or crashing the system.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,5,'2025-06-25_23-06-30','In order to allocate multiple stacks for multiple threads using the same method, we would need to divide the free RAM into separate regions so that they do not overlap (one for each thread’s stack). In the code, we would then have to firstly define a total stack region size and divide it by the number of threads to get the size per stack. Then, subtract fixed offsets from the initial MSP_init_val to get the base for each stack. And then store each stack base address in an array so that they can be assigned to PSP or used later.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,5,'2025-06-26_18-17-37','In order to allocate multiple stacks for multiple threads using the same method, we would need to divide the free RAM into separate regions so that they do not overlap (one for each thread’s stack). In the code, we would then have to firstly define a total stack region size and divide it by the number of threads to get the size per stack. Then, subtract fixed offsets from the initial MSP_init_val to get the base for each stack. And then store each stack base address in an array so that they can be assigned to PSP or used later.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,5,'2025-06-26_19-20-33','In order to allocate multiple stacks for multiple threads using the same method, we would need to divide the free RAM into separate regions so that they do not overlap (one for each thread’s stack). In the code, we would then have to firstly define a total stack region size and divide it by the number of threads to get the size per stack. Then, subtract fixed offsets from the initial MSP_init_val to get the base for each stack. And then store each stack base address in an array so that they can be assigned to PSP or used later.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',1,5,'2025-06-26_20-51-30','In order to allocate multiple stacks for multiple threads using the same method, we would need to divide the free RAM into separate regions so that they do not overlap (one for each thread’s stack). In the code, we would then have to firstly define a total stack region size and divide it by the number of threads to get the size per stack. Then, subtract fixed offsets from the initial MSP_init_val to get the base for each stack. And then store each stack base address in an array so that they can be assigned to PSP or used later.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,1,'2025-06-26_19-11-10','When I changed the PC register to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately began to execute that function, just like if I had called it normally in C. This was the case because the PC tells the processor the instruction to run next and by directly moving the function address into PC with inline assembly, I was able to redirect the processor’s flow of execution to that function. As a result, the microcontroller jumped into print_continuously, and it started printing “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,1,'2025-06-26_20-42-58','When I changed the PC register to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately began to execute that function, just like if I had called it normally in C. This was the case because the PC tells the processor the instruction to run next and by directly moving the function address into PC with inline assembly, I was able to redirect the processor’s flow of execution to that function. As a result, the microcontroller jumped into print_continuously, and it started printing “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,1,'2025-06-27_07-52-38','When I changed the PC register to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately began to execute that function, just like if I had called it normally in C. This was the case because the PC tells the processor the instruction to run next and by directly moving the function address into PC with inline assembly, I was able to redirect the processor’s flow of execution to that function. As a result, the microcontroller jumped into print_continuously, and it started printing “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,1,'2025-06-27_08-07-49','When I changed the PC register to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately began to execute that function, just like if I had called it normally in C. This was the case because the PC tells the processor the instruction to run next and by directly moving the function address into PC with inline assembly, I was able to redirect the processor’s flow of execution to that function. As a result, the microcontroller jumped into print_continuously, and it started printing “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,2,'2025-06-26_19-11-10','A system call is a way for a program that is running in user mode to request a service from the OS, usually something that cannot be done directly for safety or abstraction purposes. Since user programs are restricted from directly performing sensitive operations, they can use system calls to transition into a privileged mode, where the OS can safely handle the request.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,2,'2025-06-26_20-42-58','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets user programs request services from the operating system, especially actions that access hardware or manage resources. In most systems, programs run in user mode and cannot directly control hardware to avoid bugs or malicious code causing damage. Instead, they use system calls, which temporarily switch the CPU into privileged mode (also called kernel mode) so the OS can safely perform the requested task. This protects the hardware and other programs by ensuring only trusted code—inside the OS—can execute sensitive operations. For example, instead of writing directly to a device, a user program makes a system call like write(), and the OS handles the actual hardware access securely.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,2,'2025-06-27_07-52-38','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets user programs request services from the operating system, especially actions that access hardware or manage resources. In most systems, programs run in user mode and cannot directly control hardware to avoid bugs or malicious code causing damage. Instead, they use system calls, which temporarily switch the CPU into privileged mode (also called kernel mode) so the OS can safely perform the requested task. This protects the hardware and other programs by ensuring only trusted code—inside the OS—can execute sensitive operations. For example, instead of writing directly to a device, a user program makes a system call like write(), and the OS handles the actual hardware access securely.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,2,'2025-06-27_08-07-49','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets user programs request services from the operating system, especially actions that access hardware or manage resources. In most systems, programs run in user mode and cannot directly control hardware to avoid bugs or malicious code causing damage. Instead, they use system calls, which temporarily switch the CPU into privileged mode (also called kernel mode) so the OS can safely perform the requested task. This protects the hardware and other programs by ensuring only trusted code—inside the OS—can execute sensitive operations. For example, instead of writing directly to a device, a user program makes a system call like write(), and the OS handles the actual hardware access securely.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,3,'2025-06-26_19-11-10','Setting up a thread’s stack means to prepare the memory area that the thread will use to store its execution context, including register values and return addresses. Before the thread runs, we simulate the state it would have if it had been running all along through pushing the initial register values and program counter onto its stack in the order the processor expects. Then, when the thread is switched to, the processor can pop those values off the stack and restore the thread’s context. It’s important to store register values on the stack because registers hold the thread’s execution state and switching between threads needs saving/restoring these states.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,3,'2025-06-26_20-42-58','Setting up a thread’s stack means to prepare the memory area that the thread will use to store its execution context, including register values and return addresses. Before the thread runs, we simulate the state it would have if it had been running all along through pushing the initial register values and program counter onto its stack in the order the processor expects. Then, when the thread is switched to, the processor can pop those values off the stack and restore the thread’s context. It’s important to store register values on the stack because registers hold the thread’s execution state and switching between threads needs saving/restoring these states.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,3,'2025-06-27_07-52-38','Setting up a thread’s stack means to prepare the memory area that the thread will use to store its execution context, including register values and return addresses. Before the thread runs, we simulate the state it would have if it had been running all along through pushing the initial register values and program counter onto its stack in the order the processor expects. Then, when the thread is switched to, the processor can pop those values off the stack and restore the thread’s context. It’s important to store register values on the stack because registers hold the thread’s execution state and switching between threads needs saving/restoring these states.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,3,'2025-06-27_08-07-49','Setting up a thread’s stack means to prepare the memory area that the thread will use to store its execution context, including register values and return addresses. Before the thread runs, we simulate the state it would have if it had been running all along through pushing the initial register values and program counter onto its stack in the order the processor expects. Then, when the thread is switched to, the processor can pop those values off the stack and restore the thread’s context. It’s important to store register values on the stack because registers hold the thread’s execution state and switching between threads needs saving/restoring these states.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,4,'2025-06-26_19-11-10','We must set the PSP to point to the thread’s stack before running the thread because the processor uses PSP to access the current thread’s stack during execution. If PSP is not set correctly, the processor may try to access an invalid/uninitialized memory region when popping registers or handling interrupts, which would lead to undefined behavior and a hard fault. Without setting PSP, the hardware stack operations would fail because the stack pointer would not point to a valid stack for the thread, causing the program to crash.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,4,'2025-06-26_20-42-58','We must set the PSP to point to the thread’s stack before running the thread because the processor uses PSP to access the current thread’s stack during execution. If PSP is not set correctly, the processor may try to access an invalid/uninitialized memory region when popping registers or handling interrupts, which would lead to undefined behavior and a hard fault. Without setting PSP, the hardware stack operations would fail because the stack pointer would not point to a valid stack for the thread, causing the program to crash.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,4,'2025-06-27_07-52-38','We must set the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to point to the thread’s stack before running the thread because, in ARM Cortex-M systems, the PSP is the designated stack pointer used by threads, while the MSP (Main Stack Pointer) is used by the OS and interrupt handlers. Each thread’s stack is located at a different memory address than the MSP. If we don’t set the PSP properly, the processor will attempt to pop register values or push return addresses to an invalid or uninitialized memory region when the thread starts executing. This will almost certainly result in a hard fault, since the processor cannot reliably read or write from a stack that hasn’t been correctly assigned. Setting the PSP ensures that the thread runs with its own private stack space, and the processor knows where to fetch the saved execution context from.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,4,'2025-06-27_08-07-49','We must set the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to the starting address of the thread’s stack before running the thread because the PSP is the stack pointer that the processor uses while executing in thread mode. Unlike the MSP (Main Stack Pointer), which is used by the OS and during exceptions, the PSP is what the thread relies on for stack operations like pushing and popping register values. If the PSP is not explicitly initialized to the thread’s stack address, the processor will try to use an uninitialized or incorrect memory location as the stack. This will cause invalid memory accesses when the processor attempts to restore the thread\'s context, leading directly to a hard fault. Therefore, assigning the thread’s stack address to the PSP using __set_PSP is a required step before the thread can safely begin executing. Without this, the processor cannot manage the thread’s execution context correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,5,'2025-06-26_19-11-10','If the thread function printed once and returned, the thread would complete execution and attempt to return to an address stored in the link register. However, since this is a simple single-thread system without a scheduler to manage thread termination/switching back to a parent context, the return address might be invalid. This would most likely cause the program to crash or behave unpredictably as the processor would try to execute instructions from an invalid location.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,5,'2025-06-26_20-42-58','If the thread function printed once and then returned, the processor would try to use the Link Register (LR) to return to a location—but we never stored a valid return address in the thread’s stack. Since this return address is not initialized, the processor would attempt to execute code at an invalid location.\nThis results in a hardfault, a Cortex M4 exception triggered by illegal memory access or instruction fetch. Our minimal OS doesn’t include a scheduler or termination handler, so threads aren’t designed to return—they’re expected to run indefinitely.\nProper thread termination would require storing a safe return address or switching control back to the OS, which we haven’t implemented yet.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,5,'2025-06-27_07-52-38','If the thread function printed once and then returned, the processor would try to use the Link Register (LR) to return to a location—but we never stored a valid return address in the thread’s stack. Since this return address is not initialized, the processor would attempt to execute code at an invalid location.\nThis results in a hardfault, a Cortex M4 exception triggered by illegal memory access or instruction fetch. Our minimal OS doesn’t include a scheduler or termination handler, so threads aren’t designed to return—they’re expected to run indefinitely.\nProper thread termination would require storing a safe return address or switching control back to the OS, which we haven’t implemented yet.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',2,5,'2025-06-27_08-07-49','If the thread function printed once and then returned, the processor would try to use the Link Register (LR) to return to a location—but we never stored a valid return address in the thread’s stack. Since this return address is not initialized, the processor would attempt to execute code at an invalid location.\nThis results in a hardfault, a Cortex M4 exception triggered by illegal memory access or instruction fetch. Our minimal OS doesn’t include a scheduler or termination handler, so threads aren’t designed to return—they’re expected to run indefinitely.\nProper thread termination would require storing a safe return address or switching control back to the OS, which we haven’t implemented yet.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,1,'2025-06-26_21-05-28','Each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o) by the compiler. Then, the linker combines all these object files along with necessary libraries into a single executable file. This process allows the program to be modular, letting different parts of the code be developed and compiled independently.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,1,'2025-06-27_07-48-26','Before any C code is compiled, the preprocessor runs first. It handles directives such as #include, #define, and #ifdef, which may add headers, substitute macros, or include conditional code. After preprocessing, each .c file is independently compiled into an object file (.o) by the compiler. These object files contain machine code for each part of the program. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them—resolving function calls and memory references—into a single executable. This step also includes linking any required standard libraries. This entire process allows for modular development, where changes in one file don’t require recompiling the whole project.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,1,'2025-06-27_08-28-02','Before any C code is compiled, the preprocessor runs first. It handles directives such as #include, #define, and #ifdef, which may add headers, substitute macros, or include conditional code. After preprocessing, each .c file is independently compiled into an object file (.o) by the compiler. These object files contain machine code for each part of the program. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them—resolving function calls and memory references—into a single executable. This step also includes linking any required standard libraries. This entire process allows for modular development, where changes in one file don’t require recompiling the whole project.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,1,'2025-06-27_09-34-38','Before any C code is compiled, the preprocessor runs first. It handles directives such as #include, #define, and #ifdef, which may add headers, substitute macros, or include conditional code. After preprocessing, each .c file is independently compiled into an object file (.o) by the compiler. These object files contain machine code for each part of the program. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them—resolving function calls and memory references—into a single executable. This step also includes linking any required standard libraries. This entire process allows for modular development, where changes in one file don’t require recompiling the whole project.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,1,'2025-06-27_10-19-37','Before any C code is compiled, the preprocessor runs first. It handles directives such as #include, #define, and #ifdef, which may add headers, substitute macros, or include conditional code. After preprocessing, each .c file is independently compiled into an object file (.o) by the compiler. These object files contain machine code for each part of the program. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them—resolving function calls and memory references—into a single executable. This step also includes linking any required standard libraries. This entire process allows for modular development, where changes in one file don’t require recompiling the whole project.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,2,'2025-06-26_21-05-28','Including .c files with #include causes the entire source file to be pasted into another, which can lead to multiple definitions of the same functions and variables during linking. This breaks modularity and can cause compilation errors. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only headers (.h files) should be included to provide function declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,2,'2025-06-27_07-48-26','Including .c files with #include causes the entire source file to be pasted into another, which can lead to multiple definitions of the same functions and variables during linking. This breaks modularity and can cause compilation errors. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only headers (.h files) should be included to provide function declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,2,'2025-06-27_08-28-02','Including .c files with #include causes the entire source file to be pasted into another, which can lead to multiple definitions of the same functions and variables during linking. This breaks modularity and can cause compilation errors. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only headers (.h files) should be included to provide function declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,2,'2025-06-27_09-34-38','Including .c files with #include causes the entire source file to be pasted into another, which can lead to multiple definitions of the same functions and variables during linking. This breaks modularity and can cause compilation errors. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only headers (.h files) should be included to provide function declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,2,'2025-06-27_10-19-37','Including .c files with #include causes the entire source file to be pasted into another, which can lead to multiple definitions of the same functions and variables during linking. This breaks modularity and can cause compilation errors. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only headers (.h files) should be included to provide function declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,3,'2025-06-26_21-05-28','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves first writing the function that the thread will execute. Then, the user calls a kernel function to create the thread, passing the function as an argument. The kernel handles allocating stack space for the thread and preparing its execution context. After the thread is successfully created, the user starts the kernel scheduler, which begins running the thread. This process allows the thread to run independently alongside other threads managed by the OS.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,3,'2025-06-27_07-48-26','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread begins with initializing the kernel using a provided kernel function. This prepares the OS to track threads and allocate stack memory. After initialization, the user writes the thread function that defines what the thread will do when it runs. The user then calls another kernel function to create the thread, passing that thread function as an argument. The kernel allocates stack space and sets up the thread’s execution context. Finally, the user starts the kernel scheduler, which begins executing the created thread. Once started, the thread runs independently and continuously, as managed by the OS.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,3,'2025-06-27_08-28-02','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves the following steps:\nInitialize the kernel using the provided function. This step is essential and must happen before any threads are created. It prepares the kernel’s internal data structures and the stack pool.\nWrite the thread function, which defines the code the thread will execute when it runs.\nCreate the thread by calling a kernel function that registers the thread with the OS and sets up its stack and initial register values.\nStart the kernel using a separate function that begins the OS’s thread scheduling and context switching.\nOnce these steps are complete, the thread begins executing its function and runs independently as part of the multitasking system.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,3,'2025-06-27_09-34-38','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread on a Cortex-M4 microcontroller involves the following steps:\nInitialize the kernel using a provided function. This is the first step and prepares the kernel’s internal thread management structures and stack pool. Without this, the OS cannot track or run any threads.\nWrite the thread function, which defines the behavior of the thread—what the thread will do once it starts executing.\nCreate the thread by calling a kernel function that registers the thread and sets up its initial execution context. This includes assigning it a stack and placing simulated register values (like the program counter and xPSR) on that stack.\nStart the kernel by calling the kernel’s start function. This triggers the scheduler and causes the processor to switch context to the first thread, typically by setting the PSP and performing a context restore.\nOnce started, the thread begins executing the function you wrote, and the OS takes over managing which thread runs and when. The user does not need to manually manage the context switching after the kernel is started.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,3,'2025-06-27_10-19-37','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread on the Cortex-M4 involves a clear sequence of steps:\nInitialize the kernel using the provided kernel initialization function. This step prepares the kernel’s internal data structures, such as the stack pool and thread table, and must be done before creating any threads.\nWrite a thread function that defines what the thread will do. The user is responsible for writing this function. It contains the logic the thread will execute and is typically written in main.c.\nCreate the thread by calling a kernel-provided function that takes the user’s thread function as an argument. This step sets up the thread’s stack with simulated register values, including a program counter (PC) and xPSR, so the processor knows how to start executing it.\nStart the kernel using another kernel function. This activates the OS scheduler, sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to the correct address, and performs a context switch to begin executing the thread.\nOnce these steps are completed, the thread runs its function, and the OS handles the scheduling and switching. The user doesn\'t need to manually manage register values or stack pointers—these are handled by the kernel functions under the hood.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,4,'2025-06-26_21-05-28','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used to allocate individual stacks for each thread. By carving this memory into separate stack spaces, the OS can support multiple threads, each with its own stack, allowing them to run without interfering with each other’s data. Future labs will manage these allocations dynamically to enable true multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,4,'2025-06-27_07-48-26','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used to allocate individual stacks for each thread. By carving this memory into separate stack spaces, the OS can support multiple threads, each with its own stack, allowing them to run without interfering with each other’s data. Future labs will manage these allocations dynamically to enable true multitasking.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,4,'2025-06-27_08-28-02','The stack pool is a dedicated region of memory that the operating system uses to allocate stacks for all threads and for itself. Each thread needs its own stack to store register values, return addresses, and local variables during execution. The OS also needs its own stack (typically using the MSP) to handle exceptions and internal tasks. The stack pool provides all of these stacks from a shared memory space, which is divided up as needed. In future labs, the OS will dynamically manage this pool to support multiple threads, ensuring each one has a separate stack so they can run independently without interfering with each other or the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,4,'2025-06-27_09-34-38','The stack pool is a dedicated region of memory that the operating system uses to allocate stacks for all threads and for itself. Each thread needs its own stack to store register values, return addresses, and local variables during execution. The OS also needs its own stack (typically using the MSP) to handle exceptions and internal tasks. The stack pool provides all of these stacks from a shared memory space, which is divided up as needed. In future labs, the OS will dynamically manage this pool to support multiple threads, ensuring each one has a separate stack so they can run independently without interfering with each other or the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,4,'2025-06-27_10-19-37','The stack pool is a dedicated region of memory that the operating system uses to allocate stacks for all threads and for itself. Each thread needs its own stack to store register values, return addresses, and local variables during execution. The OS also needs its own stack (typically using the MSP) to handle exceptions and internal tasks. The stack pool provides all of these stacks from a shared memory space, which is divided up as needed. In future labs, the OS will dynamically manage this pool to support multiple threads, ensuring each one has a separate stack so they can run independently without interfering with each other or the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,5,'2025-06-26_21-05-28','Kernel files (kernel.c and kernel.h) contain low-level system code that manages core OS functions like thread scheduling and stack allocation. User files (main.c) contain application-level code such as thread functions and the main program logic. This separation keeps the kernel stable and secure while allowing users to develop applications without needing to modify the core OS.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,5,'2025-06-27_07-48-26','In an operating system, the kernel is responsible for managing low-level operations like memory, thread control, and hardware interaction. The user space, on the other hand, contains application-level code that uses the services provided by the kernel. In code structure, this means files like kernel.c and kernel.h contain system-level logic such as stack allocation, SVC handling, and kernel initialization. In contrast, main.c is the user’s file where thread functions are written and the kernel API is called. This separation is important because it keeps the kernel code isolated and secure, so users can build applications without accidentally modifying or depending on the internal workings of the OS. It also ensures modularity and scalability in system design.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,5,'2025-06-27_08-28-02','In an operating system, the kernel is responsible for managing low-level operations like memory, thread control, and hardware interaction. The user space, on the other hand, contains application-level code that uses the services provided by the kernel. In code structure, this means files like kernel.c and kernel.h contain system-level logic such as stack allocation, SVC handling, and kernel initialization. In contrast, main.c is the user’s file where thread functions are written and the kernel API is called. This separation is important because it keeps the kernel code isolated and secure, so users can build applications without accidentally modifying or depending on the internal workings of the OS. It also ensures modularity and scalability in system design.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,5,'2025-06-27_09-34-38','In an operating system, the kernel is responsible for managing low-level operations like memory, thread control, and hardware interaction. The user space, on the other hand, contains application-level code that uses the services provided by the kernel. In code structure, this means files like kernel.c and kernel.h contain system-level logic such as stack allocation, SVC handling, and kernel initialization. In contrast, main.c is the user’s file where thread functions are written and the kernel API is called. This separation is important because it keeps the kernel code isolated and secure, so users can build applications without accidentally modifying or depending on the internal workings of the OS. It also ensures modularity and scalability in system design.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',3,5,'2025-06-27_10-19-37','In an operating system, the kernel is responsible for managing low-level operations like memory, thread control, and hardware interaction. The user space, on the other hand, contains application-level code that uses the services provided by the kernel. In code structure, this means files like kernel.c and kernel.h contain system-level logic such as stack allocation, SVC handling, and kernel initialization. In contrast, main.c is the user’s file where thread functions are written and the kernel API is called. This separation is important because it keeps the kernel code isolated and secure, so users can build applications without accidentally modifying or depending on the internal workings of the OS. It also ensures modularity and scalability in system design.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,1,'2025-07-28_14-20-21','Round-robin scheduling works naturally with a queue because each thread takes a turn, one after the other. Using a queue instead of an array allows you to enqueue the currently running thread at the back after its time slice (or after yielding) and dequeue the next thread to run from the front. This maintains a clear order of execution and makes thread switching more intuitive. A benefit of using a queue is improved modularity and scalability, adding or removing threads becomes easier since the scheduler doesn\'t need to track thread indices or wrap around. It also allows for future enhancements like time-based reordering or dynamic prioritization.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,1,'2025-07-28_14-47-24','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using either a simple array or a queue to track threads. Our current scheduler already uses an array with an index (currentThread) to iterate through threads in a circular manner, which is functionally similar to queue behavior. Switching to an explicit queue would involve dequeuing the running thread after it yields and enqueuing it back at the end, which mirrors the same round-robin effect. However, using a queue introduces additional overhead in memory and computation due to enqueue/dequeue operations, especially in a system with a fixed number of threads. It may not provide significant performance improvements over the current array-based approach. That said, a queue-based system offers better modularity and extensibility—it can support dynamic thread creation and deletion more naturally and opens the door for priority-based scheduling strategies in future versions. But for a fixed, round-robin scheduler, the array-based implementation is simpler and more efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,1,'2025-07-28_15-28-20','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using either a simple array or a queue to track threads. Our current scheduler already uses an array with an index (currentThread) to iterate through threads in a circular manner, which is functionally similar to queue behavior. Switching to an explicit queue would involve dequeuing the running thread after it yields and enqueuing it back at the end, which mirrors the same round-robin effect. However, using a queue introduces additional overhead in memory and computation due to enqueue/dequeue operations, especially in a system with a fixed number of threads. It may not provide significant performance improvements over the current array-based approach. That said, a queue-based system offers better modularity and extensibility—it can support dynamic thread creation and deletion more naturally and opens the door for priority-based scheduling strategies in future versions. But for a fixed, round-robin scheduler, the array-based implementation is simpler and more efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,2,'2025-07-28_14-20-21','The PendSV_Handler is triggered to perform a context switch. It saves the current thread’s CPU context (like registers and stack pointer) into its TCB so that it can resume later. Then it calls osSched() to decide which thread should run next. The scheduler selects the next thread (e.g. round-robin) and updates the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to that thread’s saved stack location. Finally, the handler restores the new thread’s context and exits, allowing the new thread to resume from where it left off.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,2,'2025-07-28_14-47-24','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s CPU state and restoring the next thread’s state. First, it reads the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the current thread’s stack. It then stores registers R4–R11 onto that stack, as these are the callee-saved registers that need to be preserved across context switches. Next, it saves the updated PSP into the current thread’s TCB (threadArray[currentThread].stackPointer), so the thread can resume from this point later. The handler then calls the C function osSched(), which selects the next thread to run and updates currentThread. After that, the handler loads the new thread’s saved PSP from its TCB and restores R4–R11 from the stack. It updates the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack and exits the handler with bx lr, completing the switch. This ensures that the CPU resumes execution in the new thread exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,2,'2025-07-28_15-28-20','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s CPU state and restoring the next thread’s state. First, it reads the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the current thread’s stack. It then stores registers R4–R11 onto that stack, as these are the callee-saved registers that need to be preserved across context switches. Next, it saves the updated PSP into the current thread’s TCB (threadArray[currentThread].stackPointer), so the thread can resume from this point later. The handler then calls the C function osSched(), which selects the next thread to run and updates currentThread. After that, the handler loads the new thread’s saved PSP from its TCB and restores R4–R11 from the stack. It updates the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack and exits the handler with bx lr, completing the switch. This ensures that the CPU resumes execution in the new thread exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,3,'2025-07-28_14-20-21','Always running the highest-priority thread can lead to starvation—lower-priority threads might never get CPU time if a higher-priority thread is always ready. This breaks fairness and makes the system non-responsive to background tasks. In embedded systems, all threads may have important but different roles (e.g., logging, UI, communications), so starving some tasks can cause system failure. A better approach would be priority with aging or priority round-robin, which balances urgency with fairness.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,3,'2025-07-28_14-47-24','Always running the highest-priority thread can lead to starvation—lower-priority threads might never get CPU time if a higher-priority thread is always ready. This breaks fairness and makes the system non-responsive to background tasks. In embedded systems, all threads may have important but different roles (e.g., logging, UI, communications), so starving some tasks can cause system failure. A better approach would be priority with aging or priority round-robin, which balances urgency with fairness.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,3,'2025-07-28_15-28-20','If the scheduler is changed to always select the task with the highest priority, then only that thread will run as long as it is ready. The scheduler will repeatedly return the same highest-priority thread, ignoring any lower-priority threads that are also ready to run. This leads to starvation, where lower-priority threads never get scheduled because they are perpetually preempted by a higher-priority task. In embedded systems, this is especially dangerous because even low-priority threads may perform essential background operations like logging, maintenance, or communications. If they are starved, the system can appear unresponsive or even fail. This behavior also breaks the fairness typically expected in multitasking systems. A better approach would be to combine priority with fairness mechanisms—such as priority aging, where a waiting thread’s priority increases over time, or priority-based round-robin, which cycles among threads of equal priority. These methods ensure all threads get CPU time while still respecting urgency.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,4,'2025-07-28_14-20-21','Calling C functions improves code readability, maintainability, and modularity. It\'s easier to write, debug, and update logic in C than in assembly. It also lets us reuse standard C constructs and libraries. However, calling C from an interrupt handler can have overhead—extra stack usage and function call latency. Assembly is more efficient and low-level, but harder to understand and modify. So using C in handlers is a good tradeoff for a learning lab, though in production systems with strict timing, performance-critical parts may still be written in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,4,'2025-07-28_14-47-24','Calling C functions improves code readability, maintainability, and modularity. It\'s easier to write, debug, and update logic in C than in assembly. It also lets us reuse standard C constructs and libraries. However, calling C from an interrupt handler can have overhead—extra stack usage and function call latency. Assembly is more efficient and low-level, but harder to understand and modify. So using C in handlers is a good tradeoff for a learning lab, though in production systems with strict timing, performance-critical parts may still be written in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,4,'2025-07-28_15-28-20','Calling C functions improves code readability, maintainability, and modularity. It\'s easier to write, debug, and update logic in C than in assembly. It also lets us reuse standard C constructs and libraries. However, calling C from an interrupt handler can have overhead—extra stack usage and function call latency. Assembly is more efficient and low-level, but harder to understand and modify. So using C in handlers is a good tradeoff for a learning lab, though in production systems with strict timing, performance-critical parts may still be written in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,5,'2025-07-28_14-20-21','Yes, the OS will still technically work, but context switching won\'t be meaningful. Since there\'s only one thread, yielding will trigger the scheduler, but it will just select the same thread again. So osSched() and context switch still happen, but the effect is redundant. This setup can be useful to verify that context switching code works without interference from multiple threads, but it doesn’t demonstrate multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,5,'2025-07-28_14-47-24','Yes, the OS will still technically work, but context switching won\'t be meaningful. Since there\'s only one thread, yielding will trigger the scheduler, but it will just select the same thread again. So osSched() and context switch still happen, but the effect is redundant. This setup can be useful to verify that context switching code works without interference from multiple threads, but it doesn’t demonstrate multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',4,5,'2025-07-28_15-28-20','Yes, the OS will still technically work, but context switching won\'t be meaningful. Since there\'s only one thread, yielding will trigger the scheduler, but it will just select the same thread again. So osSched() and context switch still happen, but the effect is redundant. This setup can be useful to verify that context switching code works without interference from multiple threads, but it doesn’t demonstrate multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,1,'2025-07-29_09-58-44','The user must provide a pointer to the data they want to pass into the thread when calling the thread creation function (e.g., osCreateThread). This pointer typically points to a variable or struct that contains the argument data.\nThe OS, during thread creation, sets up the thread’s stack so that the initial value of register R0 contains this pointer. This is done by placing the pointer into the saved context part of the thread’s stack frame at the location corresponding to R0. When the thread starts executing, the processor restores the registers from the stack, so R0 is automatically loaded with the user’s pointer argument. The thread function receives this pointer as its parameter and can dereference it to access the passed-in data. This works because, according to the ARM calling convention (ABI), the first function argument is passed in register R0. By preloading R0 with the argument pointer on the thread’s stack before the thread runs, the thread function naturally receives the correct input when execution begins.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,1,'2025-07-29_10-09-26','The user must provide a pointer to the data they want to pass into the thread when calling the thread creation function (e.g., osCreateThread). This pointer typically points to a variable or struct that contains the argument data.\nThe OS, during thread creation, sets up the thread’s stack so that the initial value of register R0 contains this pointer. This is done by placing the pointer into the saved context part of the thread’s stack frame at the location corresponding to R0. When the thread starts executing, the processor restores the registers from the stack, so R0 is automatically loaded with the user’s pointer argument. The thread function receives this pointer as its parameter and can dereference it to access the passed-in data. This works because, according to the ARM calling convention (ABI), the first function argument is passed in register R0. By preloading R0 with the argument pointer on the thread’s stack before the thread runs, the thread function naturally receives the correct input when execution begins.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,1,'2025-07-29_17-43-53','The user must provide a pointer to the data they want to pass into the thread when calling the thread creation function (e.g., osCreateThread). This pointer typically points to a variable or struct that contains the argument data.\nThe OS, during thread creation, sets up the thread’s stack so that the initial value of register R0 contains this pointer. This is done by placing the pointer into the saved context part of the thread’s stack frame at the location corresponding to R0. When the thread starts executing, the processor restores the registers from the stack, so R0 is automatically loaded with the user’s pointer argument. The thread function receives this pointer as its parameter and can dereference it to access the passed-in data. This works because, according to the ARM calling convention (ABI), the first function argument is passed in register R0. By preloading R0 with the argument pointer on the thread’s stack before the thread runs, the thread function naturally receives the correct input when execution begins.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,2,'2025-07-29_09-58-44','Calling osYield inside the context-switching functions (PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler) can cause recursive or nested context switches. These handlers are themselves triggered by interrupts or system calls to perform context switching. If osYield is called within these handlers, it would request another context switch while one is already in progress, which can corrupt the processor’s state or stack, cause unpredictable behavior, or lead to deadlocks. It is bad because the OS’s context switching code is not re-entrant or designed to handle being interrupted by another context switch request. This can cause stack overflows, corrupted data, or the system becoming unstable or crashing.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,2,'2025-07-29_10-09-26','osYield is implemented as a system call that triggers an SVC interrupt to request a context switch. When called inside context-switching functions such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, it effectively attempts to trigger another interrupt while already handling one.Since interrupts are running in interrupt context, calling osYield here causes nested or recursive interrupts. This can lead to hardfaults due to stack corruption because the interrupt stack frames can overwrite each other. The processor’s state and the call stack become inconsistent and corrupted, resulting in undefined behavior or system crashes.It is bad because the OS’s context switching code is not designed to be re-entrant or handle being interrupted by itself. Nested context switches destabilize the system, cause memory corruption, and can lead to deadlocks or watchdog resets. Therefore, osYield must only be called from thread (non-interrupt) context, never from within interrupt handlers that perform scheduling\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,2,'2025-07-29_17-43-53','osYield is implemented as a system call that triggers an SVC interrupt to request a context switch. When called inside context-switching functions such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, it effectively attempts to trigger another interrupt while already handling one.Since interrupts are running in interrupt context, calling osYield here causes nested or recursive interrupts. This can lead to hardfaults due to stack corruption because the interrupt stack frames can overwrite each other. The processor’s state and the call stack become inconsistent and corrupted, resulting in undefined behavior or system crashes.It is bad because the OS’s context switching code is not designed to be re-entrant or handle being interrupted by itself. Nested context switches destabilize the system, cause memory corruption, and can lead to deadlocks or watchdog resets. Therefore, osYield must only be called from thread (non-interrupt) context, never from within interrupt handlers that perform scheduling\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,3,'2025-07-29_09-58-44','Benefits of decreasing the OS clock tick to 0.1 ms include higher timing resolution and the ability to respond more quickly to time-critical events. This finer granularity allows threads to be scheduled more precisely and can improve real-time responsiveness. Downsides include increased overhead because the SysTick interrupt would fire 10 times more frequently. This increases CPU time spent handling interrupts, reducing time available for thread execution and potentially causing higher power consumption. It also adds complexity in timer management and can make the system less efficient if the application does not require such fine timing. Overall, while increasing clock frequency improves responsiveness, it can negatively affect system performance and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,3,'2025-07-29_10-09-26','Benefits of decreasing the OS clock tick to 0.1 ms include higher timing resolution and the ability to respond more quickly to time-critical events. This finer granularity allows threads to be scheduled more precisely and can improve real-time responsiveness. Downsides include increased overhead because the SysTick interrupt would fire 10 times more frequently. This increases CPU time spent handling interrupts, reducing time available for thread execution and potentially causing higher power consumption. It also adds complexity in timer management and can make the system less efficient if the application does not require such fine timing. Overall, while increasing clock frequency improves responsiveness, it can negatively affect system performance and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,3,'2025-07-29_17-43-53','Benefits of decreasing the OS clock tick to 0.1 ms include higher timing resolution and the ability to respond more quickly to time-critical events. This finer granularity allows threads to be scheduled more precisely and can improve real-time responsiveness. Downsides include increased overhead because the SysTick interrupt would fire 10 times more frequently. This increases CPU time spent handling interrupts, reducing time available for thread execution and potentially causing higher power consumption. It also adds complexity in timer management and can make the system less efficient if the application does not require such fine timing. Overall, while increasing clock frequency improves responsiveness, it can negatively affect system performance and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,4,'2025-07-29_09-58-44','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking requires each thread to explicitly yield control (via a call like osYield) so that other threads can run. If a thread never yields, it monopolizes the CPU and blocks other threads. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS interrupts threads automatically based on a timer interrupt, regardless of whether the thread yields. This ensures fair CPU sharing even if a thread never voluntarily yields. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking only switches context when a thread calls yield, so the OS waits passively. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS uses a hardware timer (SysTick) to trigger interrupts regularly, decrements thread runtimes, and forces context switches via interrupts when a thread’s timeslice expires.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,4,'2025-07-29_10-09-26','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking requires each thread to explicitly yield control (via a call like osYield) so that other threads can run. If a thread never yields, it monopolizes the CPU and blocks other threads. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS interrupts threads automatically based on a timer interrupt, regardless of whether the thread yields. This ensures fair CPU sharing even if a thread never voluntarily yields. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking only switches context when a thread calls yield, so the OS waits passively. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS uses a hardware timer (SysTick) to trigger interrupts regularly, decrements thread runtimes, and forces context switches via interrupts when a thread’s timeslice expires.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,4,'2025-07-29_17-43-53','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking requires each thread to explicitly yield control (via a call like osYield) so that other threads can run. If a thread never yields, it monopolizes the CPU and blocks other threads. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS interrupts threads automatically based on a timer interrupt, regardless of whether the thread yields. This ensures fair CPU sharing even if a thread never voluntarily yields. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking only switches context when a thread calls yield, so the OS waits passively. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS uses a hardware timer (SysTick) to trigger interrupts regularly, decrements thread runtimes, and forces context switches via interrupts when a thread’s timeslice expires.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,5,'2025-07-29_09-58-44','These two mechanisms work together by allowing threads to voluntarily yield the CPU at any time, while also having the OS enforce maximum runtime via pre-emptive scheduling. When a thread calls osYield, the OS performs a context switch immediately and resets the thread’s runtime, allowing other threads to run sooner if desired. If a thread does not yield, the SysTick timer decrements its runtime and triggers a pre-emptive context switch once its timeslice expires. This combination ensures cooperative threads can voluntarily give up CPU time, while non-cooperative threads are still pre-emptively interrupted to maintain fairness and system responsiveness.\n',0,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,5,'2025-07-29_10-09-26','Both yielding and pre-emptive context switching rely on the same core context-switching mechanism implemented in the PendSV handler. The OS uses PendSV to perform actual thread switching regardless of the trigger. When a thread calls osYield, it triggers an SVC interrupt, which eventually requests a PendSV context switch and resets the thread’s runtime, allowing other threads to run cooperatively. Separately, the SysTick timer interrupt decrements the currently running thread’s runtime. When this runtime expires, SysTick triggers a PendSV context switch automatically, pre-emptively switching to the next thread without requiring the thread to yield. Because both mechanisms funnel through the same PendSV context switch logic, they coexist seamlessly. The OS can handle cooperative yields and forced pre-emptive switches uniformly, ensuring fair scheduling whether or not threads voluntarily yield CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('j7jiao',5,5,'2025-07-29_17-43-53','Both yielding and pre-emptive context switching rely on the same core context-switching mechanism implemented in the PendSV handler. The OS uses PendSV to perform actual thread switching regardless of the trigger. When a thread calls osYield, it triggers an SVC interrupt, which eventually requests a PendSV context switch and resets the thread’s runtime, allowing other threads to run cooperatively. Separately, the SysTick timer interrupt decrements the currently running thread’s runtime. When this runtime expires, SysTick triggers a PendSV context switch automatically, pre-emptively switching to the next thread without requiring the thread to yield. Because both mechanisms funnel through the same PendSV context switch logic, they coexist seamlessly. The OS can handle cooperative yields and forced pre-emptive switches uniformly, ensuring fair scheduling whether or not threads voluntarily yield CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',1,1,'2025-06-25_19-44-04','The HAL(Hardware abstraction layer) is a library used in stm to simplify access to hardware and makes certain configurations easier. It allows higher-level application code to communicate with a microcontroller or CPU more consistently and portablely by abstracting the hardware details. The HAL helps to better organize and simplify code when working on a general embedded project.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',1,1,'2025-06-26_22-17-01','The HAL(Hardware abstraction layer) is a library used in stm to simplify access to hardware and makes certain configurations easier. It allows higher-level application code to communicate with a microcontroller or CPU more consistently and portablely by abstracting the hardware details. The HAL helps to better organize and simplify code when working on a general embedded project.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',1,2,'2025-06-25_19-44-04','It is a function that allows the printf function  to output a character to a particular port. The __io_putchar definition instructs the system on how to deliver each character produced by printf(). Without it the program would compile but no output would be seen.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',1,2,'2025-06-26_22-17-01','It is a function that allows the printf function  to output a character to the UART output console. The __io_putchar definition instructs the system on how to deliver each character produced by printf(). Without it the program would compile but no output would be seen and also, this function is necessary because different projects may require different output configurations, and the __io_putchar is a way to standardize it.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',1,3,'2025-06-25_19-44-04','The debugger allows us to look at the registers and see how they change at different points of the program. Unlike printf it allows for real time debugging and is much faster and more efficient to use the debugger rather than printf. With the debugger you can make alterations to your code as you will be able to see what happens as it is runs through each step.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',1,3,'2025-06-26_22-17-01','The debugger allows us to look at the registers and see how they change at different points of the program. Unlike printf it allows for real time debugging and is much faster and more efficient to use the debugger rather than printf. With the debugger you can make alterations to your code as you will be able to see what happens as it is runs through each step.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',1,4,'2025-06-25_19-44-04','The size of the main stack was decided to be 0x400. We subtract 0x400 from our msp_init_val to get the location of our psp_val. Doing this allows our stack size to be 0x400. We needed to know the location of the MSP because that is where the main stack starts. All other stack are relative to the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',1,4,'2025-06-26_22-17-01','The size of the main stack was decided to be 0x400. We subtract 0x400 from our msp_init_val to get the location of our psp_val. Doing this allows our stack size to be 0x400. We needed to know the location of the MSP because that is where the main stack starts. All other stack are relative to the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',1,5,'2025-06-25_19-44-04','We would need to know the stack sizes,the number of stacks, and the psp pointers for each stack. By looking at the psp values and the msp value you will be able to know where each stack is, in the memory. With the stack sizes you can increment from msp to get the location of the stacks depending on their sizes.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',1,5,'2025-06-26_22-17-01','To allocate multiple stacks for threads at compile time, we would reserve a block of memory and divide it into fixed sized, properly aligned stack regions. We would ensure that each stack has a unique starting point. By knowing the number of stacks and the size of each stack , we can calculate each stack’s base address using MSP - i * STACK_SIZE, where i is the stack index. To prevent overlap and ensure validity, we must ensure that the total memory used which is the number of stacks times the stack size fits within the bounds of available RAM. Each thread’s Process Stack Pointer would be set to the top of its assigned stack since the stack grows downwards. Because the layout is determined at compile-time, we can assign stack addresses statically, avoiding the need to inspect stack pointers at runtime and ensuring deterministic and safe memory use.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,1,'2025-06-26_16-56-51','The function printed “Hello world” to the console in an infinite loop. Since we put the pointer in the program counter register the processor runs the function by moving what is stored in R0 to the PC register every time the function loops.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,1,'2025-06-26_23-51-06','When we changed to the program counter to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor runs the function which prints hello world to the console continuously at that address. The PC tells the processor where to get the next instruction. Once it was set to the function’s address, the processor started executing the function from the beginning. As the function runs, the PC automatically updates to point to the next instruction, so there\'s no need to manually change it after the initial assignment. By directly placing the function address into the PC, we do not need to do normal function calling, causing the processor to jump straight into the function and stay there.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,1,'2025-06-27_13-30-53','When we changed to the program counter to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor runs the function which prints hello world to the console continuously at that address. The PC tells the processor where to get the next instruction. Once it was set to the function’s address, the processor started executing the function from the beginning. As the function runs, the PC automatically updates to point to the next instruction, so there\'s no need to manually change it after the initial assignment. By directly placing the function address into the PC, we do not need to do normal function calling, causing the processor to jump straight into the function and stay there.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,2,'2025-06-26_16-56-51','It is a method to directly manipulate or request something from the operating system. It allows for direct communication that would only be possible with higher privileged access.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,2,'2025-06-26_23-51-06','A system call is a unique kind of function or process that enables a user program to request services from the kernel of the operating system for . A system call functions at a lower level than a regular function call. It initiates a controlled switch from user mode to kernel mode, allowing the OS to safely carry out privileged operations on behalf of the program, such as reading a file, allocating memory, or starting a process.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,2,'2025-06-27_13-30-53','A system call is a unique kind of function or process that enables a user program to request services from the kernel of the operating system for . A system call functions at a lower level than a regular function call. It initiates a controlled switch from user mode to kernel mode, allowing the OS to safely carry out privileged operations on behalf of the program, such as reading a file, allocating memory, or starting a process. System calls protect hardware access from untrustworthy user code. The main reason the operating system must shield hardware from user code is to maintain system integrity, security, and stability. Without security, malicious or bug-ridden programs could get direct access to devices, memory, or CPU instructions, which could lead to system crashes or data compromise.  Systems establish a distinction between user mode and kernel mode to handle this: only the operating system functions in kernel mode, whilst user mode limits access to essential resources. System calls are necessary when user programs need to communicate with hardware. These system calls safely convert the CPU to kernel mode, verify the request, and, if allowed, carry out the action. This system makes sure that access to hardware is strictly regulated and only permitted via monitored, trusted channels.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,3,'2025-06-26_16-56-51','The threads stack was set up by moving the registers from the stack to the PC register. Firstly, we got the new stack pointer for PSP by decrementing it from msp then for the other stacks pointers they were decremented from each other, all with the same sizes. To set up a thread stack is to allocate memory for the stack. We need to store register values in a stack so when there is an interrupt the state of the thread will be saved. It is also done to make sure that when a register is popped unto the stack the thread executes in the proper state.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,3,'2025-06-26_23-51-06','A block of memory that represents the CPU\'s saved state must be prepared in order for a thread to start or properly resume execution. This process is known as setting up the thread stack. Allocating space for the stack and arranging the initial register values in the proper sequence at the top of that space is the first step in the process. The registers R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR are all included in this. After that, PSP is configured to point to the prepared stack frame\'s base. This arrangement is crucial because the CPU anticipates finding the register values in a particular configuration when it executes a context switch. The CPU may crash or restore the incorrect state if the order is off. Storing registers on the stack ensures that when an interrupt occurs or a thread is paused, its exact state can be saved and later restored. Thread switching may require software to save and restore additional context, and the stack is the central structure used for this. By maintaining strict stack structure and pointer management, we ensure that threads execute safely, independently, and resume exactly where they left off.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,3,'2025-06-27_13-30-53','A block of memory that represents the CPU\'s saved state must be prepared in order for a thread to start or properly resume execution. This process is known as setting up the thread stack. Allocating space for the stack and arranging the initial register values in the proper sequence at the top of that space is the first step in the process. The registers R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR are all included in this. After that, PSP is configured to point to the prepared stack frame\'s base. This arrangement is crucial because the CPU anticipates finding the register values in a particular configuration when it executes a context switch. The CPU may crash or restore the incorrect state if the order is off. Storing registers on the stack ensures that when an interrupt occurs or a thread is paused, its exact state can be saved and later restored. Thread switching may require software to save and restore additional context, and the stack is the central structure used for this. By maintaining strict stack structure and pointer management, we ensure that threads execute safely, independently, and resume exactly where they left off.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,4,'2025-06-26_16-56-51','The operating system will make the thread run on the main stack. When the SVC then tries to run the first thread it would run it with an improper value. This causes the code to accesss memory it was not surposed to, leading to a crash.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,4,'2025-06-26_23-51-06','The Main Stack Pointer, is used by default,during reset and for handling interrupts. The Process Stack Pointer is used by individual threads in an operating system. When a thread is created, its stack is prepared separately from the main stack, and it is expected to use the PSP. If we do not explicitly set the PSP to point to the top of the thread’s stack using __set_PSP, the CPU continues to use the MSP, which may not point to valid memory for that thread. When the thread begins execution, popping of the stack or function calls expect valid stack memory at the PSP, and if it\'s not set correctly, the CPU may try to access an invalid or corrupted memory address. This results in a hard fault, which is an error that crashes the system. So, setting the PSP ensures that the thread runs with its own isolated stack, allowing it to operate safely and independently from the main stack and other threads.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,4,'2025-06-27_13-30-53','The Main Stack Pointer, is used by default,during reset and for handling interrupts. The Process Stack Pointer is used by individual threads in an operating system. When a thread is created, its stack is prepared separately from the main stack, and it is expected to use the PSP. If we do not explicitly set the PSP to point to the top of the thread’s stack using __set_PSP, the CPU continues to use the MSP, which may not point to valid memory for that thread. When the thread begins execution, popping of the stack or function calls expect valid stack memory at the PSP, and if it\'s not set correctly, the CPU may try to access an invalid or corrupted memory address. This results in a hard fault, which is an error that crashes the system. So, setting the PSP ensures that the thread runs with its own isolated stack, allowing it to operate safely and independently from the main stack and other threads.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,5,'2025-06-26_16-56-51','The thread fineshes exucutiong then swithes to the main stack to finish executing.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,5,'2025-06-26_23-51-06','The CPU would try to pop a return address from the thread\'s stack to figure out where to go next if the thread function printed once and then returned rather than looping endlessly. Since the OS-created thread was never called from another function, the stack frame is manually constructed and lacks a suitable return address. This means that the CPU jumps to a random or constrained memory location when the thread attempts to return by inserting an invalid or missing address into the program counter. Because it is unable to execute from an invalid address, the system consequently causes a hard fault. Because it instructs the CPU where to continue execution, the return address is essential in both standard function calls and interrupt handling. However, returning from a thread is dangerous since, unless specifically handled, there is no caller to return to.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',2,5,'2025-06-27_13-30-53','The CPU would try to pop a return address from the thread\'s stack to figure out where to go next if the thread function printed once and then returned rather than looping endlessly. Since the OS-created thread was never called from another function, the stack frame is manually constructed and lacks a suitable return address. This means that the CPU jumps to a random or constrained memory location when the thread attempts to return by inserting an invalid or missing address into the program counter. Because it is unable to execute from an invalid address, the system consequently causes a hard fault. Because it instructs the CPU where to continue execution, the return address is essential in both standard function calls and interrupt handling. However, returning from a thread is dangerous since, unless specifically handled, there is no caller to return to.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,1,'2025-06-26_22-50-28','Firstly, each .c file is first compiled into .o files which are object files that help translate c code to machine code. Then the header files help share code between the .c files. The linker then combines all .o files into one executable. The linker also resolves local variables and functions between the files. In the end the file can be run directly in a single executable file.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,1,'2025-06-27_14-39-46','Preprocessing, compiling, and linking are the three primary proceses involved in combining several .c files into a single executable. First, the compiler replaces macros and copies the contents of included header files into the source code to process directives like #include and #define during preprocessing. This facilitates the sharing of code between .c files, such as constants and function declarations. Each.c file is then converted into a .o (object) file, which contains machine code that the computer can comprehend, during the compilation stage. The linker then merges all of the .o files into a single executable during the linking process. Additionally, it fixes file references, such as ensuring that a function specified in one file can be used appropriately in another. The result is a single executable file that can be run directly.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,1,'2025-06-27_16-01-17','Preprocessing, compiling, and linking are the three primary proceses involved in combining several .c files into a single executable. First, the compiler replaces macros and copies the contents of included header files into the source code to process directives like #include and #define during preprocessing. This facilitates the sharing of code between .c files, such as constants and function declarations. Each.c file is then converted into a .o (object) file, which contains machine code that the computer can comprehend, during the compilation stage. The linker then merges all of the .o files into a single executable during the linking process. Additionally, it fixes file references, such as ensuring that a function specified in one file can be used appropriately in another. The result is a single executable file that can be run directly.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,2,'2025-06-26_22-50-28','There are a couple of problems. Firstly, it causes duplicate files. Like with the given example kernell.c is included in kernel .h so including kernel.h would create a duplicate. It negatively affects the linking process. Each .c file is meant t be independently compiled into .o files, you are  bypassing the build process and combining everything into a single translation unit if you include a .c file. This makes it more difficult to debug and maintain your program. It makes it harder to reuse code in multiple programs and avoid name conflicts.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,2,'2025-06-27_14-39-46','There are a couple of problems. Firstly, it causes duplicate files. Like with the given example kernell.c is included in kernel .h so including kernel.h would create a duplicate. It negatively affects the linking process. Each .c file is meant t be independently compiled into .o files, you are  bypassing the build process and combining everything into a single translation unit if you include a .c file. This makes it more difficult to debug and maintain your program. It makes it harder to reuse code in multiple programs and avoid name conflicts.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,2,'2025-06-27_16-01-17','There are a couple of problems. Firstly, it causes duplicate files. Like with the given example kernell.c is included in kernel .h so including kernel.h would create a duplicate. It negatively affects the linking process. Each .c file is meant t be independently compiled into .o files, you are  bypassing the build process and combining everything into a single translation unit if you include a .c file. This makes it more difficult to debug and maintain your program. It makes it harder to reuse code in multiple programs and avoid name conflicts.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,3,'2025-06-26_22-50-28','Firstly, you select the task you want a thread to perform. After that, you create a thread object and instruct it on which function to execute in order to set up the thread. When you finally start the thread, it starts to execute that function concurrently with your main program.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,3,'2025-06-27_14-39-46','A thread function, which specifies the work the thread will accomplish and typically accepts a void* argument and returns nothing, must be defined before a thread can be set up and started in an RTOS. The RTOS itself needs to be initialized using a certain function, such as osKernelInitialize(), before any threads are created. When the system is prepared, you call a thread creation function, to create the thread. You supply the function pointer, any required arguments, and configuration information, such as stack size or priority. The thread is registered with the OS in this phase. But until the kernel is explicitly started using a function like osKernelStart(), the thread won\'t start running. Only after this call does the RTOS begin scheduling and running the registered threads, allowing them to execute concurrently with each other and with the main program if it\'s still active.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,3,'2025-06-27_16-01-17','A thread function, which specifies the work the thread will accomplish and typically accepts a void* argument and returns nothing, must be defined before a thread can be set up and started in an RTOS. The RTOS itself needs to be initialized using a certain function, such as osKernelInitialize(), before any threads are created. When the system is prepared, you call a thread creation function, to create the thread. You supply the function pointer, any required arguments, and configuration information, such as stack size or priority. The thread is registered with the OS in this phase. But until the kernel is explicitly started using a function like osKernelStart(), the thread won\'t start running. Only after this call does the RTOS begin scheduling and running the registered threads, allowing them to execute concurrently with each other and with the main program if it\'s still active.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,4,'2025-06-26_22-50-28','The stack pool is a region of reserved memory which holds the stack for each thread in the operating system. Each thread needs its own stack to store information on function calls, return addresses and local variables. When running multiple threads each thread should have its own stack to keep track of its execution independently.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,4,'2025-06-27_14-39-46','The operating system uses a specific area of memory called the stack pool to hold the separate stacks that each thread requires. To handle function calls, return addresses, and local variables, each thread needs its own stack. The stack pool facilitates multithreading by offering a centralized location from which these stacks can be distributed. The OS can allocates parts of the stack pool to threads as they are generated because it is dynamically allocated rather than pre-assigning a fixed memory space to each thread. As a result, memory may be used more effectively and a variable number of threads can be supported without wasting space. In future labs, we can use this pool to dynamically assign stacks to new threads when they are registered, allowing for multiple threads to run at the same time while they maintain their own isolated execution contexts.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,4,'2025-06-27_16-01-17','The operating system uses a specific area of memory called the stack pool to hold the separate stacks that each thread requires. To handle function calls, return addresses, and local variables, each thread needs its own stack. The stack pool facilitates multithreading by offering a centralized location from which these stacks can be distributed. The OS can allocates parts of the stack pool to threads as they are generated because it is dynamically allocated rather than pre-assigning a fixed memory space to each thread. As a result, memory may be used more effectively and a variable number of threads can be supported without wasting space. In future labs, we can use this pool to dynamically assign stacks to new threads when they are registered, allowing for multiple threads to run at the same time while they maintain their own isolated execution contexts.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,5,'2025-06-26_22-50-28','The programs you write and execute, such as instructions or apps, are accessed in user mode. Files in user space, such as main.c, are accessed in this mode. From the perspective of the user, these files specify what the software does. The operating system\'s kernel code controls memory, hardware, processes, and system calls. Files in kernel mode are files like kernel.c. This separation is key for safety, security, stability, and keeping the system well-organized and secure from the user’s perspective.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,5,'2025-06-27_14-39-46','User mode is represented by files like main.c, where application logic is defined and the user specifies what their program or thread should accomplish. With restricted access to system and hardware resources, these files operate in user mode. The kernel mode, on the other hand, is home to files like kernel.c and is responsible for low-level functions like scheduling, memory management, hardware control, and system call processing. System calls safely hand over control to the kernel when user programs need to ask the OS for services, such as starting a thread or accessing a timer. These should not be mistaken for API calls, which are higher-level function calls that have the potential to internally call system calls. In addition to being essential for security and stability, this division helps the user by streamlining development by letting the user concentrate on what their program should accomplish, while the kernel manages the system\'s backend operations. Writing safer, more dependable programs that utilize system resources appropriately is made easier for the user when they are aware of this difference.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',3,5,'2025-06-27_16-01-17','User mode is represented by files like main.c, where application logic is defined and the user specifies what their program or thread should accomplish. With restricted access to system and hardware resources, these files operate in user mode. The kernel mode, on the other hand, is home to files like kernel.c and is responsible for low-level functions like scheduling, memory management, hardware control, and system call processing. System calls safely hand over control to the kernel when user programs need to ask the OS for services, such as starting a thread or accessing a timer. These should not be mistaken for API calls, which are higher-level function calls that have the potential to internally call system calls. In addition to being essential for security and stability, this division helps the user by streamlining development by letting the user concentrate on what their program should accomplish, while the kernel manages the system\'s backend operations. Writing safer, more dependable programs that utilize system resources appropriately is made easier for the user when they are aware of this difference.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',4,1,'2025-07-27_14-55-29','Round robin scheduling works well with a queue due to the fact that they are both first in first out. Each process is enqueued as it comes in, runs, then either exits when finished or is placed at the back of the queue if more time is required to finish. Compared to using an array which requires manual management like shifting elements or tracking the indexes of elements, a queue alloform more efficient process scheduling. This improves the implementation\'s usability, maintainability, and suitability for workloads that are dynamic and may involve processes that enter or leave the system frequently.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',4,1,'2025-07-30_17-00-14','Round-robin scheduling works well with a queue because both follow a First-In-First-Out approach. Each thread runs for a time slice and then advances to the back of the queue if it isn\'t finished so they operate well together. The scheduler successfully simulates queue behavior by iteratively cycling over thread indices. Because a queue naturally manages thread additions and deletions without the need for intricate index monitoring, it may increase flexibility, particularly in situations where threads are dynamically allocated or removed.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',4,2,'2025-07-27_14-55-29','The system sets a flag to initiate the PendSV interrupt when a context switch is required. The state of the current thread, which includes crucial registers like the program counter , stack pointer , and general-purpose registers, is initially saved into the thread\'s stack or into a memory structure by PendSV_Handler when it executes. The thread that is now active is then indicated by updating a pointer. In order to resume where it left off, it then loads the context that was saved for the subsequent thread back into the CPU registers. The handler then ends, and the CPU continues to run as if nothing had changed except that it was now executing a separate thread.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',4,2,'2025-07-30_17-00-14','In order to perform a context switch, the PendSV_Handler first locates the stack of the active thread using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). It stores the CPU context into the thread\'s stack, namely registers R4 through R11, which are not routinely preserved by hardware. In order to update the current thread pointer and choose which thread should execute next, it then invokes osSched. It recovers the preserved context by removing R4–R11 from the stack using the PSP for the new thread. Ultimately, the handler ends and the modified PSP is written back, enabling the CPU to continue running where the new thread left off. This procedure allows for seamless task switching without compromising the stability of the system as a whole.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',4,3,'2025-07-27_14-55-29','This introduces the problem of starvation. Even if they are crucial to the operation of the system as a whole, lower-priority processes may never receive CPU time under this configuration if a high-priority task is always ready to start. System instability or failure may result from this, particularly if lower-priority tasks are in charge of crucial background functions like memory freeing or I/O handling. Furthermore, this rigid priority-based scheduling can lead to unpredictable system behavior if it is not accompanied with a fair scheduling technique, like time slicing or aging. Priority-based scheduling with preemption and protections such priority aging, which progressively raises the priority of lower-priority tasks over time, might be a superior strategy.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',4,3,'2025-07-30_17-00-14','This introduces the problem of starvation. Even if they are crucial to the operation of the system as a whole, lower-priority processes may never receive CPU time under this configuration if a high-priority task is always ready to start. System instability or failure may result from this, particularly if lower-priority tasks are in charge of crucial background functions like memory freeing or I/O handling. Furthermore, this rigid priority-based scheduling can lead to unpredictable system behavior if it is not accompanied with a fair scheduling technique, like time slicing or aging. Priority-based scheduling with preemption and protections such priority aging, which progressively raises the priority of lower-priority tasks over time, might be a superior strategy.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',4,4,'2025-07-27_14-55-29','When it comes to complicated logic, such as scheduling decisions or managing system calls, C code is far simpler to comprehend, develop, and debug than assembly. Additionally, it enables programmers to utilize high-level features like conditionals, loops, and data structures, which makes the code more modular and simpler to update or expand later. But there are drawbacks. Because C functions adhere to rigid calling rules, the compiler may produce extra instructions to manage the stack or save and restore registers. This can increase overhead and reduce performance, particularly in interrupt handlers where accuracy and speed are crucial. If you don\'t use the right compiler settings and carefully handle function characteristics, you run the danger of the compiler introducing unexpected behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',4,4,'2025-07-30_17-00-14','When it comes to complicated logic, such as scheduling decisions or managing system calls, C code is far simpler to comprehend, develop, and debug than assembly. Additionally, it enables programmers to utilize high-level features like conditionals, loops, and data structures, which makes the code more modular and simpler to update or expand later. But there are drawbacks. Because C functions adhere to rigid calling rules, the compiler may produce extra instructions to manage the stack or save and restore registers. This can increase overhead and reduce performance, particularly in interrupt handlers where accuracy and speed are crucial. If you don\'t use the right compiler settings and carefully handle function characteristics, you run the danger of the compiler introducing unexpected behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',4,5,'2025-07-27_14-55-29','True multithreading won\'t happen, but the OS will still function. The OS will try to move to another thread when the scheduler runs and the thread yields, but since there are no other threads, it will just return to the original thread. This indicates that while the system still operates and is operationally correct, multithreading\'s advantages are gone. Concurrency, context switching between processes, and the ability to share the CPU across several separate functions are all absent. In fact, it turns into a cooperative single-tasking system in which one thread gives way but restarts right away. Because there is only one runnable thread, the OS is underutilized even though it doesn\'t malfunction its scheduling and context switching algorithms are exerting unnecessary effort for no actual benefit.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',4,5,'2025-07-30_17-00-14','True multithreading won\'t happen, but the OS will still function. The OS will try to move to another thread when the scheduler runs and the thread yields, but since there are no other threads, it will just return to the original thread. This indicates that while the system still operates and is operationally correct, multithreading\'s advantages are gone. Concurrency, context switching between processes, and the ability to share the CPU across several separate functions are all absent. In fact, it turns into a cooperative single-tasking system in which one thread gives way but restarts right away. Because there is only one runnable thread, the OS is underutilized even though it doesn\'t malfunction its scheduling and context switching algorithms are exerting unnecessary effort for no actual benefit.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',5,1,'2025-07-27_18-38-24','To pass an argument to the a thread the user first has to define the thread function and initialize the necessary argument. When the thread is created using the thread library, the library collects the function and its arguments to generate a system call to create the thread. The operating system sets up the thread\'s execution context, which includes allocating a new stack and setting a pointer to the specified function. The passed argument are now placed in the threads context. This argument is accessed from the shared memory space when the thread first starts. Because threads in a process share the same address space, they can use pointers or references to access common data structures without having to transfer the data, which is how this process operates.\n',0,'s25'),('jagagbo',5,1,'2025-07-30_17-50-00','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must pass a pointer to the desired data when calling a thread creation function like osCreateThread. Because threads can dereference shared memory and share the same address space, it is crucial that the OS receives this pointer rather than the actual raw data. The operating system saves this pointer in the thread\'s initial context, more precisely in register R0, which is the register for a function\'s first argument according to the ARM calling convention. The CPU loads its context, which includes the program counter and registers like R0, when the thread is first scheduled and starts running. The thread function then reads its input from R0. This works correctly because the ABI ensures that arguments are passed this way, and the OS correctly prepares the stack and register state before launching the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',5,2,'2025-07-27_18-38-24','Because osYield() itself cause an exception and because context-switching functions already operate in the exception context, you cannot use it inside of them. This problem results from calling osYield() inside one of these handlers, which initiates a new context switch request while another is already underway. This is problematic because the processor is already handling thread switching and context saving and restoration. Depending on priority levels, if another exception is pending at this period, it may be handled during or after the current one. This could potentially corrupt the context or lead to hard faults, stack overflows, or unpredictable behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',5,2,'2025-07-30_17-50-00','Because osYield() itself cause an exception and because context-switching functions already operate in the exception context, you cannot use it inside of them. This problem results from calling osYield() inside one of these handlers, which initiates a new context switch request while another is already underway. This is problematic because the processor is already handling thread switching and context saving and restoration. Depending on priority levels, if another exception is pending at this period, it may be handled during or after the current one. This could potentially corrupt the context or lead to hard faults, stack overflows, or unpredictable behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',5,3,'2025-07-27_18-38-24','Setting the OS clock (SysTick) to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms means the system tick interrupt will fire 10 times more frequently. This would improve the timing resolution and responsiveness of each program. and the scheduler can make more frequent decisions. The OS can handle delays, timeouts, and task scheduling with finer granularity and  the scheduler can make more frequent decisions. There are downsides. Because the processor must service the SysTick interrupt every 0.1 ms, more frequent interrupts result in increased CPU overhead because there is less time available for task execution. By increasing the frequency of context switches, more CPU cycles are used for state saving and restoration. Additionally, it increases the strain on the bus and memory system and may result in higher power usage.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',5,3,'2025-07-30_17-50-00','Setting the OS clock (SysTick) to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms means the system tick interrupt will fire 10 times more frequently. This would improve the timing resolution and responsiveness of each program. and the scheduler can make more frequent decisions. The OS can handle delays, timeouts, and task scheduling with finer granularity and  the scheduler can make more frequent decisions. There are downsides. Because the processor must service the SysTick interrupt every 0.1 ms, more frequent interrupts result in increased CPU overhead because there is less time available for task execution. By increasing the frequency of context switches, more CPU cycles are used for state saving and restoration. Additionally, it increases the strain on the bus and memory system and may result in higher power usage.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',5,4,'2025-07-27_18-38-24','From the perspective of the user, the process and timing of task switching distinguishes cooperative multitasking from preemptive multitasking. Cooperative multitasking involves executing tasks until they give up control. This implies that the user is in charge of ensuring that processes periodically release the CPU so that others can operate. The system as a whole may become blocked if one task fails to yield. Preemptive multitasking allows the operating system to automatically pause tasks at predetermined intervals and choose which task to execute next, independent of the current process\'s activity. From the perspective of the user, this makes the system more responsive and dependable because poorly behaving tasks are unable to use the CPU. Cooperative multitasking is easier from the OS\'s point of view since the OS only switches tasks when specifically instructed to do so by the task that is currently running. Unless a task freely yields, it does not need to save or restore context or manage interruptions for scheduling. The OS must manage timer interrupts, determine which task should execute next, and automatically preserve and restore complete task contexts during each changeover, making preemptive multitasking more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',5,4,'2025-07-30_17-50-00','From the perspective of the user, the process and timing of task switching distinguishes cooperative multitasking from preemptive multitasking. Cooperative multitasking involves executing tasks until they give up control. This implies that the user is in charge of ensuring that processes periodically release the CPU so that others can operate. The system as a whole may become blocked if one task fails to yield. Preemptive multitasking allows the operating system to automatically pause tasks at predetermined intervals and choose which task to execute next, independent of the current process\'s activity. From the perspective of the user, this makes the system more responsive and dependable because poorly behaving tasks are unable to use the CPU. Cooperative multitasking is easier from the OS\'s point of view since the OS only switches tasks when specifically instructed to do so by the task that is currently running. Unless a task freely yields, it does not need to save or restore context or manage interruptions for scheduling. The OS must manage timer interrupts, determine which task should execute next, and automatically preserve and restore complete task contexts during each changeover, making preemptive multitasking more difficult.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',5,5,'2025-07-27_18-38-24','Because they ultimately activate the same processes, yielding and time-based preemptive context switching can work together. The PendSV_Handler typically handles a context switch. The OS configures the PendSV interrupt to execute a context switch regardless of whether a task invokes the cooperative osYield() or a preemptive timer interrupt, which is SysTick, fires. PendSV is directly triggered when osYield() is called, requesting a context switch. The OS\'s tick handler may determine that a higher-priority task is prepared when SysTick fires, in which case PendSV may be triggered once more. The context switch function chooses the next task to execute, restores the context of the current task, and preserves its state in both scenarios. The OS handles the context switch in the same manner regardless of the reason for the request. This makes multitasking responsive and effective by enabling the system to take advantage of the flexibility of cooperative yielding and the deterministic nature of pre-emptive swithing.\n',1,'s25'),('jagagbo',5,5,'2025-07-30_17-50-00','Because they ultimately activate the same processes, yielding and time-based preemptive context switching can work together. The PendSV_Handler typically handles a context switch. The OS configures the PendSV interrupt to execute a context switch regardless of whether a task invokes the cooperative osYield() or a preemptive timer interrupt, which is SysTick, fires. PendSV is directly triggered when osYield() is called, requesting a context switch. The OS\'s tick handler may determine that a higher-priority task is prepared when SysTick fires, in which case PendSV may be triggered once more. The context switch function chooses the next task to execute, restores the context of the current task, and preserves its state in both scenarios. The OS handles the context switch in the same manner regardless of the reason for the request. This makes multitasking responsive and effective by enabling the system to take advantage of the flexibility of cooperative yielding and the deterministic nature of pre-emptive swithing.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,1,'2025-05-17_23-05-31','The HAL is a software layer that provides general libraries and functions that allows you to directly work with hardware, so we don’t need to deal with hardware settings and low-level code for registers. For example, if you want to work GPIO or timers, you can call a high-level HAL function instead of figuring out individual register values.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,1,'2025-05-17_23-43-59','The HAL is a software layer that provides general libraries and functions that allows you to directly work with hardware, so we don’t need to deal with hardware settings and low-level code for registers. For example, if you want to work GPIO or timers, you can call a high-level HAL function instead of figuring out individual register values.\n',0,'s25'),('jbluo',1,1,'2025-05-18_00-00-18','The HAL, Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software layer that provides general libraries and functions that allows you to directly work with hardware (such as components like the GPIO, timers, and UART), so we don’t need to deal with hardware settings and low-level code for registers. For example, if you want to work GPIO or timers, you can call a high-level HAL function instead of figuring out individual register values. Another benefit is portability and accessibility. HAL functions are very consistent across different chips in the same family, so you’re able to move code to other compatible devices with little to no changes. And due to the nature of simplification, there are less chances for bugs to occur as you skip much of the manual low-level code.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,2,'2025-05-17_23-05-31','The function lets you send one character over UART. When printf is used, it breaks your input into characters and sends the message one character at a time. The reason this is used is because microcontrollers don’t have screens or standard outputs to output. Thus, we need to define how and where to send the output; for example, allowing the output to be viewable on our computer. Seeing the output is important, especially when doing validation or debugging on the code.\n',0,'s25'),('jbluo',1,2,'2025-05-17_23-43-59','The function lets you send one character over UART. When printf is used, it breaks your input into characters and sends the message one character at a time. The reason this is used is because microcontrollers don’t have screens or standard outputs to output. The reason it is important to define a function like __io_putchar is because it tells the system how to send over the characters; without this, there is no output. Different hardware setups use different output hardware. The role that the function plays in handling these differences is that it allows the bridge for printf to the preferred output method. To adjust for each of the setups, we must tweak the code to match the setup, so that printf can work regardless of setup and allow us to the see the output. Seeing the output is important, especially when doing validation or debugging on the code.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,2,'2025-05-18_00-00-18','The function lets you send one character over UART. When printf is used, it breaks your input into characters and sends the message one character at a time. The reason this is used is because microcontrollers don’t have screens or standard outputs to output. The reason it is important to define a function like __io_putchar is because it tells the system how to send over the characters; without this, there is no output. Different hardware setups use different output hardware. The role that the function plays in handling these differences is that it allows the bridge for printf to the preferred output method. To adjust for each of the setups, we must tweak the code to match the setup, so that printf can work regardless of setup and allow us to the see the output. Seeing the output is important, especially when doing validation or debugging on the code.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,3,'2025-05-17_23-05-31','The reason we used the debugger in this lab is because it allows us to pause the code at specific points to check register values and variable changes; in the case of this code, it’s at the while loop. The use of the debugger allows us to see the change throughout the code, before and after the stopping points. If we just used printf, then we would not be able to determine at what point in the code variable values or register values change. With the debugger, we can look at the stack pointer and memory before and after pause to see how the code is setting up and using memory while it runs.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,3,'2025-05-17_23-43-59','The reason we used the debugger in this lab is because it allows us to pause the code at specific points to check register values and variable changes; in the case of this code, it’s at the while loop. The use of the debugger allows us to see the change throughout the code, before and after the stopping points. If we just used printf, then we would not be able to determine at what point in the code variable values or register values change. With the debugger, we can look at the stack pointer and memory before and after pause to see how the code is setting up and using memory while it runs.\n',0,'s25'),('jbluo',1,3,'2025-05-18_00-00-18','The reason we used the debugger in this lab is because it allows us to pause the code at specific points to check register values and variable changes; in the case of this code, it’s at the while loop. The use of the debugger allows us to see the change throughout the code, before and after the stopping points. If we just used printf, then we would not be able to determine at what point in the code variable values or register values change. With the debugger, we can look at the stack pointer and memory before and after pause to see how the code is setting up and using memory while it runs. In this case, printf cannot be used to view register values in real-time. In this case, printf is limited to values that are explicitly sent to the output. It is not capable of seeing which lines of code changes the values of variables or registers. On the other hand, debugger allows us to pause the program at any point and view the state of the system. For example, it is useful for checking the change in value for MSP_INIT_VAL over time.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,4,'2025-05-17_23-05-31','The stack grows downward in memory, so to get the new value for PSP_val, we took the initial value, MSP_init_val, and subtracted by 0x400 bytes. We need to know the initial MSP value for this to work because it prevents us from dangerously placing PSP. By knowing where the initial value is, we can determine a set byte jump to prevent PSP from being placed too close or overlapping with the main stack. If placed incorrectly, this can lead to bugs, memory corruption or crashing.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,4,'2025-05-17_23-43-59','The stack grows downward in memory, so to get the new value for PSP_val, we took the initial value, MSP_init_val, and subtracted by 0x400 bytes. We need to know the initial MSP value for this to work because it prevents us from dangerously placing PSP. By knowing where the initial value is, we can determine a set byte jump to prevent PSP from being placed too close or overlapping with the main stack. If placed incorrectly, this can lead to bugs, memory corruption or crashing.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,4,'2025-05-18_00-00-18','The stack grows downward in memory, so to get the new value for PSP_val, we took the initial value, MSP_init_val, and subtracted by 0x400 bytes. We need to know the initial MSP value for this to work because it prevents us from dangerously placing PSP. By knowing where the initial value is, we can determine a set byte jump to prevent PSP from being placed too close or overlapping with the main stack. If placed incorrectly, this can lead to bugs, memory corruption or crashing.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,5,'2025-05-17_23-05-31','First you need to define the stack sizes. For example, defining a main stack size and thread stack size. In our case too, we need to ensure that the sizes are aligned to 8-bytes. This is addressed in the initial definition of sizes. Start with MSP_init_val. From this value, subtract the main stack size. This will allocate space for the main stack. Then, you start allocating for each thread stack. From the value found after the main calculation, allocate your defined thread stack size by subtracting by the defined size. From this, you can allocate for the next stack as you now know the ending point after the last calculation. Continue on by repeating the steps. This, along with proper definition and alignment with 8-byte requirement ensures that the thread stacks are placed correctly to prevent overlap.\n',0,'s25'),('jbluo',1,5,'2025-05-17_23-43-59','First you need to define the stack sizes. For example, defining a main stack size and thread stack size. In our case too, we need to ensure that the sizes are aligned to 8-bytes, to ensure address validity for Cortex-M4. This is addressed in the initial definition of sizes. Start with MSP_init_val. From this value, subtract the main stack size. This will allocate space for the main stack. Here, the pattern begins. Once you subtract the fixed size from the top, you will get the next stack’s starting address. From there, subtract the defined thread stack size from the previous calculation. You can continue this for as many stacks as you want to define. This method uses the pattern for the main stack and thread stacks in which you subtract a fixed size to move downward in memory and find the starting address for the next calculation. As stated above, to ensure the address is valid, the stack sizes must be aligned to 8-bytes (a multiple of 8). Ensuring this makes each stack exactly the defined stack size below the previous one, ensuring that they are placed to prevent overlap. By subtracting the stack size from previous address, we create a new region that does not overlap with others. This works because we have a defined a size that is aligned to 8-bytes, the next position is found by subtracting a known, fixed amount that meets the memory alignment requirements of the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',1,5,'2025-05-18_00-00-18','First you need to define the stack sizes. For example, defining a main stack size and thread stack size. In our case too, we need to ensure that the sizes are aligned to 8-bytes, to ensure address validity for Cortex-M4. This is addressed in the initial definition of sizes. Start with MSP_init_val. From this value, subtract the main stack size. This will allocate space for the main stack. Here, the pattern begins. Once you subtract the fixed size from the top, you will get the next stack’s starting address. From there, subtract the defined thread stack size from the previous calculation. You can continue this for as many stacks as you want to define. This method uses the pattern for the main stack and thread stacks in which you subtract a fixed size to move downward in memory and find the starting address for the next calculation. As stated above, to ensure the address is valid, the stack sizes must be aligned to 8-bytes (a multiple of 8). Ensuring this makes each stack exactly the defined stack size below the previous one, ensuring that they are placed to prevent overlap. By subtracting the stack size from previous address, we create a new region that does not overlap with others. This works because we have a defined a size that is aligned to 8-bytes, the next position is found by subtracting a known, fixed amount that meets the memory alignment requirements of the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',2,1,'2025-06-25_22-51-24','When PC was set to the address of the print_continuously function, it began to run the function. The function has an infinite loop for printing, so it’d continue to print “Hello, PC!”. This happens because the PC always points to the next instruction that needs to be executed. When we swap it to the print function, the processor will continue to run this function because it never ends. Understanding how PC works is important because it shows how PC control modification is connected with context switching and thread management, and shows how multitasking is done.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',2,1,'2025-06-25_23-16-54','When PC was set to the address of the print_continuously function, it began to run the function. The function has an infinite loop for printing, so it’d continue to print “Hello, PC!”. This happens because the PC always points to the next instruction that needs to be executed. When we swap it to the print function, the processor will continue to run this function because it never ends. Understanding how PC works is important because it shows how PC control modification is connected with context switching and thread management, and shows how multitasking is done.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',2,2,'2025-06-25_22-51-24','A system call is a way for the user to interact with the OS. Because users don’t have direct access to the protected (kernel) section of memory, the use of system calls is necessary to do things like read files and create processes. In the lab, SVC (supervisor call) were used to get the processor to access privileged files to run certain operations. The use of system calls ensures safety, as it keeps the boundary between the user and the privileged data.\n',0,'s25'),('jbluo',2,2,'2025-06-25_23-16-54','A system call is a special type of function call that allows user programs to request specific services from the OS kernel such as file reading, memory allocation and process creation. Because user programs run in the unprivileged section, they cannot access hardware directly or critical system resources. The system call is supposed to allow the user to safely interact with the kernel, which has the rights to do these actions. In this lab, the system calls were made with the SVC function (supervisor call). This function would cause the processor to switch from user mode to privileged mode and jump to a predefined handler in the kernel. The handler will use a system call number to determine which function is being requested, and then execute that function. The importance of system call is that it allows for easy functionality. It’s the bridge between user programs and privilege state. This also provides protection. By having a controlled interface between the user and the kernel, it prevents access to sensitive data and resources, reducing crashes and security breaches.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',2,3,'2025-06-25_22-51-24','The thread’s stack was setup by preparing a section of memory in which the read can store data such as local data and register values during code execution. In this lab, we made a stack to hold important values like the PC, LR, xPSR and general register values. The importance of this is that it allows the CPU to return a state after being interrupted. When an interrupt happens, we store all the values of the current state of the stack, and return a pointer to the stack When the interrupt is completed, we pop the values off the stack back to restore the state. The importance of this is that it allows restoration of exact states of threads, meaning we can pause and resume threads reliably.\n',0,'s25'),('jbluo',2,3,'2025-06-25_23-16-54','Setting up a thread’s stack means that we create a specific section of memory where the thread can store its variables and saved register values during execution. In this lab, we manually set up the stack with values for key registers such as the program counter (PC), link register (LR), xPSR, and general purpose registers like R0-R12. This simulates what happens usually during context switching, or interrupts. The reason we store the values is to let the CPU restore a thead’s state after it has been paused. Without this, the processor does not know where to continue execution after pause. The CPU also expects the registers to be laid out in stack in a certain order when returning from interrupt. If the order is incorrect, wrong values will be fed to wrong registers and the output is not predictable. For example, if an interrupt happens, the state will be saved onto the stack by saving all the values of registers there. When the interrupt is dealt with, the values must be popped off by the processor in the proper order so that the state can be restored from before the interrupt. This is important for proper context switching, as it allows the same processor to run multiple threads without affecting capabilities.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',2,4,'2025-06-25_22-51-24','If we don’t use the function to assign PSP before running, the processor will use MSP. MSP is not meant for user threads; it’s used for OS and handling exceptions. Since we need to manually set up the thread’s stack in a separate memory intended for PSP, MSP would cause the CPU to access the wrong memory. Accessing the wrong memory can lead to garbage values or uninitialized data, which can cause hard fault. Setting the PSP ensures that the thread runs the proper stack that we want, which prevents memory errors and keeps thread execution stable and isolated from OS stack.\n',0,'s25'),('jbluo',2,4,'2025-06-25_23-16-54','The __set_PSP function is used to assign the address of the thread’s stack to the process stack pointer (PSP). This is critical because the processor will use the PSP to access the thread stack while it runs. Without calling the __set_PSP function, the CPU will default to use the MSP, which is reserved for the OS and exception handling. Even though we manually allocated memory for the thread stack, it won’t be used unless the PSP explicitly points to it. If skipped, the CPU will try to use MSP, which will cause it to access the wrong memory area. Since the thread stack is prepared in a different region, it will cause the CPU to load invalid or non-initialized data, which will cause a hard fault. By using the function, we make sure that the processor is using the correct stack for the thread, and ensure that it doesn’t interfere with the main default stack. This is essential for ensuring correctness and stability and proper execution of user threads.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',2,5,'2025-06-25_22-51-24','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned after printing once, the processor would try to continue to execute using the return address of the stack. In this lab, the thread’s stack didn’t include a valid return address in the place that the link register would normally store. As a result, the processor would jump to an invalid or undefined memory location, which would cause a hard fault. This happens because the Cotex-M4 expects a valid return address to continue to execute the code. Without it, the system crashes. In other systems like FreeRTOS, threads are meant to run forever, or exit off special system calls, and not supposed to return like normal functions.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',2,5,'2025-06-25_23-16-54','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned after printing once, the processor would try to continue to execute using the return address of the stack. In this lab, the thread’s stack didn’t include a valid return address in the place that the link register would normally store. As a result, the processor would jump to an invalid or undefined memory location, which would cause a hard fault. This happens because the Cotex-M4 expects a valid return address to continue to execute the code. Without it, the system crashes. In other systems like FreeRTOS, threads are meant to run forever, or exit off special system calls, and not supposed to return like normal functions.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',3,1,'2025-06-26_16-40-54','Firstly, there is preprocessing. This is where the preprocessor includes header files, expands macros and handles conditional compilation using directives like #ifdef. The end result of this stage is a full C source file ready for the compiler. Secondly, is compilation. Each file is converted into an object file (.o). This fie contains machine code and symbol table with information about functions and variables defined. Some compilers will internally generate assembly code, but this step is still part of the compilation process. Finally there is linking. This is where the linker will connect all the object files, resolve references between them, and assign final memory locations and make an executable.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',3,2,'2025-06-26_16-40-54','Using #include on the .c file is a bad idea because it pastes the entire contents of the file wherever the directive is used, which will cause all its function definitions to be duplicated in every file that includes it. This will lead to linker errors due to multiple definitions of the same symbol. Instead, it is better for only declarations like function prototypes, type definitions and macros to be placed in .h files, which can safely be included in multiple source files. Each .c file will be compiled separately, which ensures that every function and variable is defined only once and the linker can bring them together into one executable with no issue.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',3,3,'2025-06-26_16-40-54','To setup and start a thread, you first write the thread function in main.c. This function should accept a void* parameter and contain the code that you want the thread to run After initializing the HAL, you call osKernelInitialize() to setup the kernel’s internal state and prepare the stack pool. After, you call osCreateThread() with your function as the input so that the kernel will allocate space for the stack, setup the startup frame and store the thread’s context. Finally you call osKernelStart() to switch the CPU to thread mode and you load the new thread stack pointer to begin executing the function. After this, the thread has control and the kernel infrastructure is ready to support multitasking in the future.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',3,4,'2025-06-26_16-40-54','Thee stack pool is a dedicated section of RAM that is reserved for thread stacks. When osCreateThread is called, the kernel will allocate a size for stack (usually 0x400 bytes) from the top of this pool. It will assign it to a new thread and update pointer to track the next open space. Each thread will receie its own slice of memory. This ensures that local variables and return addresses and data don’t interfere with each other. In future labs, when the kernel wants to support multiple threads using an array of thread descriptors, this pool will let them all (stacks) to eexist side-by-side. The scheduler will then be able to switch between threads by updating thee processor’s PSP to point to the right stack between context switches. This will help ultimately the the capability for multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',3,5,'2025-06-26_16-40-54','The kernel and user code are separated to enforce responsibilities and ensure stability and privacy. Kernel code is located in Core/Src and Core/Inc, and runs with full privileges and is responsible for low-leevel operations like stack management, context switching, system call attendance, and maintaining global OS data. On the other hand, user code will handle application logic and define thread functions, but can only interact with the kernel through its public API. This separation is important from the user’s perspective because it improves safety, keeps the system modular and makes the codebase easier to understand and maintain.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',4,1,'2025-07-10_18-25-18','Using a quene for round-robin scheduling works by enqueueing each of the TCB after it yields and always scheduling the thread at front of quene. This will ensure that the threads are executed in the order they arrive, and moved back into the quene. This is beneficial because compared to an array, a quene makes insertion and removal a lot more efficient, especially when you deal with threads that have dynamic lifetimes. It will also avoid tracking the current index as it will simply go in order. This can support more priority based, or scheduling.\n',0,'s25'),('jbluo',4,1,'2025-07-10_18-54-35','Using a quene for round-robin scheduling works but enqueneing a thread after it yields and always scheduling the thread at the front of the quene. This enforces strict FIFO behaviour.. However, the implementation already behaves like a queen by using an array and a current_thread index that wraps around. This mimics enquene-dequene without needing a full quene data structure. However, to use a real quene will add overhead memory and complexity, but can support more dynamic thread creation and destruction. So the array method can be simpler and faster, but the quene system will offer more flexibility but at the cost of performance and implementation effort.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',4,2,'2025-07-10_18-25-18','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current thread’s CPU registers onto the stack, then using the osSched() function to switch the stack pointer to the next thread. It then restores the next thread’s saved registers so execution can continue as if it never paused. This will allow the processor to switch cleanly between threads without losing their states.\n',0,'s25'),('jbluo',4,2,'2025-07-10_18-54-35','The PendSV_Handler performs a context swith by using the PSP to access the current thread’s stack. It will push the registers R4-R11 onto the current stack so the thread’s state is saved. Then it will call osSched() to update the current_thread index and set the PSP to the saved stack pointer of the next thread. The PendSV_Handler then pops R4-R11 from the new stack to restore the context so it can continue to execute as if it never paused. This switch is clean and efficient, which allows to the OS to rotate between threads without  data loss.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',4,3,'2025-07-10_18-25-18','This is a bad idea because if one task has highest priority and never finishes, then it will run forever and prevent lower-priority tasks from running well. This will defeat the purpose of multitasking and can cause important background tasks to not be able to run. This will cause the system to become unstable or unresponsive to lower-priority work.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',4,3,'2025-07-10_18-54-35','This is a bad idea because if one task has highest priority and never finishes, then it will run forever and prevent lower-priority tasks from running well. This will defeat the purpose of multitasking and can cause important background tasks to not be able to run. This will cause the system to become unstable or unresponsive to lower-priority work.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',4,4,'2025-07-10_18-25-18','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code easier to read, write and debug. C is a higher-level and less error-prone than assembly, especially or complex logic like thread management or scheduling. The downside though is that calling C from an interrupt requires more stack usage and can introduce overhead. Assembly gives you more control and allows you to be more efficient in what you want to do, but it’s harder to maintain, use and more prone to small bugs.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',4,4,'2025-07-10_18-54-35','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code easier to read, write and debug. C is a higher-level and less error-prone than assembly, especially or complex logic like thread management or scheduling. The downside though is that calling C from an interrupt requires more stack usage and can introduce overhead. Assembly gives you more control and allows you to be more efficient in what you want to do, but it’s harder to maintain, use and more prone to small bugs.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',4,5,'2025-07-10_18-25-18','Yes, the OS would still work but yielding would not have any effect. With only one thread, osYield() would still trigger a context switch,, but osSched() would switch back to the same thread. The system would function correctly and act like a single-threaded system. The benefits of multithreading will be lost, but the output and the logic would still remain proper and right.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',4,5,'2025-07-10_18-54-35','Yes, the OS would still work but yielding would not have any effect. With only one thread, osYield() would still trigger a context switch,, but osSched() would switch back to the same thread. The system would function correctly and act like a single-threaded system. The benefits of multithreading will be lost, but the output and the logic would still remain proper and right.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',5,1,'2025-07-22_21-19-10','The user needs to define a thread function that accepts a single argument, usually by type void*. This allows the passing of any kind of data. Before creating the thread, the user allocates and initializes a struct with all the necessary data fields and passes a pointer to this struct when calling the thread creation function like osCreateThread. The OS must then place the pointer to the struct into the thread’s initial stack frame in the correct register (usually R0). This means that when thread begins execution, it will see the argument in R0 and use it in the thread function. This works because the interface defines that the first argument to a function is passed via register R0. By loading R0 with the struct pointer when the thread is initialized, the OS ensures the thread receives the data as expected, allowing it to access many values through the struct fields.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',5,1,'2025-07-23_15-59-30','The user needs to define a thread function that accepts a single argument, usually by type void*. This allows the passing of any kind of data. Before creating the thread, the user allocates and initializes a struct with all the necessary data fields and passes a pointer to this struct when calling the thread creation function like osCreateThread. The OS must then place the pointer to the struct into the thread’s initial stack frame in the correct register (usually R0). This means that when thread begins execution, it will see the argument in R0 and use it in the thread function. This works because the interface defines that the first argument to a function is passed via register R0. By loading R0 with the struct pointer when the thread is initialized, the OS ensures the thread receives the data as expected, allowing it to access many values through the struct fields.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',5,2,'2025-07-22_21-19-10','You can’t call osYield in any context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main or SysTick_Handler because these handlers are already in the middle of performing or scheduling a context switch. osYield is designed to trigger a context switch by setting PendSV pending. If you call it in the context-switch handler, then it’ll result in nested context switches, which will corrupt the stack or interrupt state. This is bad because it’ll cause stack corruption, infinite handler loops or bad behaviour. The system can also enter fault or crash entirely because of corrupted thread state.\n',0,'s25'),('jbluo',5,2,'2025-07-23_15-59-30','You can’t call osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main or SysTick_Handler because osYield works by triggering a supervisor call, which is a software generated interrupt used to request a context switch from thread mode. The SVC instruction is meant to be used from thread mode, not from another exception handler. If you call osYield inside an interrupt handler, the CPU is already handling an exception. Since SVC has lower priority than other exceptions, it cannot pre-empt the current handlers, and will be delayed until all other handlers return. This is bad because is violates the expected interrupt nesting rules, leading to non-deterministic behaviour. The SVC doesn’t run immediately, breaking the logic of osYield and possibly leaving the scheduler in an inconsistent state. It can result in stack corruption, missed context switches, or even system crashes in time-sensitive applications.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',5,3,'2025-07-22_21-19-10','Benefits:\nThere is higher timing resolution. This means that threads can sleep or delay for more precise intervals. There’s also faster response time. The OS can switch between threads or handle delays more quickly. Finally, there’s also improved real-time behaviour. This is good for applications that need find-grained control, like motor control or audio processing.\nDownside:\nThere’s increased overhead. The OS has to handle ten times more SysTick interrupts per second, which will consume more CPU time. There’s also higher power consumption. Frequent context switches and interrupt handling can drain battery life faster. There’s also reduced thread execution efficiency. With more frequent interrupts, threads may not get enough time to finish meaningful work before being pre-empted.\nOverall, it improves responsiveness, but at the cost of efficiency and system overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',5,3,'2025-07-23_15-59-30','Benefits:\nThere is higher timing resolution. This means that threads can sleep or delay for more precise intervals. There’s also faster response time. The OS can switch between threads or handle delays more quickly. Finally, there’s also improved real-time behaviour. This is good for applications that need find-grained control, like motor control or audio processing.\nDownside:\nThere’s increased overhead. The OS has to handle ten times more SysTick interrupts per second, which will consume more CPU time. There’s also higher power consumption. Frequent context switches and interrupt handling can drain battery life faster. There’s also reduced thread execution efficiency. With more frequent interrupts, threads may not get enough time to finish meaningful work before being pre-empted.\nOverall, it improves responsiveness, but at the cost of efficiency and system overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',5,4,'2025-07-22_21-19-10','From the user’s perspective: co-operative multitasking is where the user is must manually call osYield() or similar functions to give up control. If a thread doesn’t yield, then other threads won’t run. The user is responsible for fairness. Pre-emptive multitasking is where the OS automatically switches between threads, usually a timer (Systick). The user won’t worry about yielding because threads are switched based on timing and priority.\nFrom the OS perspective: co-operative multitasking is where the OS only switches context when a thread explicitly calls a yield function. Context switching is simpler and more predictable but relies on well-behaved threads. Pre-emptive is where the OS uses hardware interrupts (like SysTick) to force context switches. It must save and restore context more carefully to avoid corruption, and scheduling is more complex but robust and responsive.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',5,4,'2025-07-23_15-59-30','From the user’s perspective: co-operative multitasking is where the user is must manually call osYield() or similar functions to give up control. If a thread doesn’t yield, then other threads won’t run. The user is responsible for fairness. Pre-emptive multitasking is where the OS automatically switches between threads, usually a timer (Systick). The user won’t worry about yielding because threads are switched based on timing and priority.\nFrom the OS perspective: co-operative multitasking is where the OS only switches context when a thread explicitly calls a yield function. Context switching is simpler and more predictable but relies on well-behaved threads. Pre-emptive is where the OS uses hardware interrupts (like SysTick) to force context switches. It must save and restore context more carefully to avoid corruption, and scheduling is more complex but robust and responsive.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',5,5,'2025-07-22_21-19-10','Both yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching work together because they share a common mechanism for triggering a context switch – setting the PendSV interrupt. When a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily triggers a context switch by setting PendSV pending. When SysTick timer fires, it also sets PendSV pending to enforce a time-based switch. In both cases, the actual context switch happens in the PendSV_Handler which is responsible for saving the current thread’s context and restoring the next one. Because both mechanisms converge on the same handler, they can coexist. One is voluntary (cooperative), the other is automatic (preemptive), but both follow the same switch logic, ensuring seamless integration.\n',1,'s25'),('jbluo',5,5,'2025-07-23_15-59-30','Both yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching work together because they share a common mechanism for triggering a context switch – setting the PendSV interrupt. When a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily triggers a context switch by setting PendSV pending. When SysTick timer fires, it also sets PendSV pending to enforce a time-based switch. In both cases, the actual context switch happens in the PendSV_Handler which is responsible for saving the current thread’s context and restoring the next one. Because both mechanisms converge on the same handler, they can coexist. One is voluntary (cooperative), the other is automatic (preemptive), but both follow the same switch logic, ensuring seamless integration.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,1,'2025-05-15_13-23-07','The HAL provides a lot of the setup code that required to properly program on the microcontroller. It provides this as a default setup that is typically sufficient for writing general embedded C projects and is a much easier and more convenient option than writing this setup code manually.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,1,'2025-06-01_10-08-25','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) ensures that the software running on the computer is compatible with the computer’s hardware by allowing the program to interact with the hardware at a high level, commonly referred to as “abstraction”. This typically enables an operating system to use similar startup code to interact with computer hardware regardless of the model/design of the physical computer components. For general embedded C projects, the HAL is handy as it eliminates the need for the software engineer to manually write the initial low-level code that would otherwise be required to interface with the computer.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,1,'2025-06-01_11-33-14','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) ensures that the software running on the computer is compatible with the computer’s hardware by allowing the program to interact with the hardware at a high level, commonly referred to as “abstraction”. This typically enables an operating system to use similar startup code to interact with computer hardware regardless of the model/design of the physical computer components. For general embedded C projects, the HAL is handy as it eliminates the need for the software engineer to manually write the initial low-level code that would otherwise be required to interface with the computer.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,1,'2025-06-01_12-19-18','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) ensures that the software running on the computer is compatible with the computer’s hardware by allowing the program to interact with the hardware at a high level, commonly referred to as “abstraction”. This typically enables an operating system to use similar startup code to interact with computer hardware regardless of the model/design of the physical computer components. For general embedded C projects, the HAL is handy as it eliminates the need for the software engineer to manually write the initial low-level code that would otherwise be required to interface with the computer.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,2,'2025-05-15_13-23-07','This function enables the printing of single characters to the UART2 channel when using the “printf” function. This is needed because the “printf” function, by default, does not write to a specific channel, and therefore needs the “__io_putchar” function to declare the UART2 channel as the desired channel to write to.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,2,'2025-06-01_10-08-25','The “__io_putchar” function allows the software engineer to control how data is outputted when working with different hardware. It is useful for converting a “printf” output into a signal that is in accordance with a desired communication protocol such as UART, USB, or LCD, depending on the application. By default, the “printf” function does not write to a specific channel for communication and thus requires the “__io_putchar” function to be passed in order to declare the UART2 channel as the desired channel to write to. Without the “__io_putchar” function, the result of “printf” would not appear on the UART2 channel.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,2,'2025-06-01_11-33-14','The “__io_putchar” function allows the software engineer to control how data is outputted when working with different hardware. It is useful for converting a “printf” output into a signal that is in accordance with a desired communication protocol such as UART, USB, or LCD, depending on the application. By default, the “printf” function does not write to a specific channel for communication and thus requires the “__io_putchar” function to be passed in order to declare the UART2 channel as the desired channel to write to. Without the “__io_putchar” function, the result of “printf” would not appear on the UART2 channel.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,2,'2025-06-01_12-19-18','The “__io_putchar” function allows the software engineer to control how data is outputted when working with different hardware. It is useful for converting a “printf” output into a signal that is in accordance with a desired communication protocol such as UART, USB, or LCD, depending on the application. By default, the “printf” function does not write to a specific channel for communication and thus requires the “__io_putchar” function to be passed in order to declare the UART2 channel as the desired channel to write to. Without the “__io_putchar” function, the result of “printf” would not appear on the UART2 channel.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,3,'2025-05-15_13-23-07','The debugger lets us see what the value size of the stack is at all points during the program’s lifetime; this value may be different than the initial value we computed for the stack. This let’s us catch any potential errors (bugs) in our code because we can step through each step of the program as it runs to see if our stack implementation works as desired. We can expect our stack size value to be close to the initial value, but if this value differs by a lot at any step of the program while it is running, then we know that something is wrong with our design.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,3,'2025-06-01_10-08-25','The debugger lets us view the data held by the computer’s registers at all points during the program’s execution. Certain registers, such as SP, MSP, and PSP, are useful to watch during the debugging our stack implementation code; if their values change unexpectedly after the execution of a certain line in the program (determined by stepping through the code with the debugger tool) then we know that that line is causing an error. Using a “printf” function to simply output the desired register values is not an ideal method of debugging because it requires calling the function at one or more points in the program. This may limit the memory available in a small embedded/real-time system and can potentially slow it down, as we would be running inefficient temporary code.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,3,'2025-06-01_11-33-14','The debugger lets us view the data held by the computer’s registers at all points during the program’s execution. Certain registers, such as SP, MSP, and PSP, are useful to watch during the debugging our stack implementation code; if their values change unexpectedly after the execution of a certain line in the program (determined by stepping through the code with the debugger tool) then we know that that line is causing an error. Using a “printf” function to simply output the desired register values is not an ideal method of debugging because it requires calling the function at one or more points in the program. This may limit the memory available in a small embedded/real-time system and can potentially slow it down, as we would be running inefficient temporary code.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,3,'2025-06-01_12-19-18','The debugger lets us view the data held by the computer’s registers at all points during the program’s execution. Certain registers, such as SP, MSP, and PSP, are useful to watch during the debugging our stack implementation code; if their values change unexpectedly after the execution of a certain line in the program (determined by stepping through the code with the debugger tool) then we know that that line is causing an error. Using a “printf” function to simply output the desired register values is not an ideal method of debugging because it requires calling the function at one or more points in the program. This may limit the memory available in a small embedded/real-time system and can potentially slow it down, as we would be running inefficient temporary code.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,4,'2025-05-15_13-23-07','We wanted the MSP and PSP stacks to be adjacent in position in the microcontroller’s memory. In order to determine the ideal location of the start of the PSP stack in memory, we needed to know the position of the start of the MSP stack in memory in addition to the desired size for our PSP stack. The location of PSP is then found by subtracting the size of the PSP stack from the address in memory of the start of the MSP stack to find our new value for the PSP stack.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,4,'2025-06-01_10-08-25','We wanted the MSP and PSP stacks to be adjacent in position in the microcontroller’s memory. In order to determine the ideal location of the start of the PSP stack in memory, we needed to know the position of the start of the MSP stack in memory in addition to the desired size for our PSP stack. The location of PSP is then found by subtracting the size of the PSP stack from the address in memory of the start of the MSP stack to find our new value for the PSP stack.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,4,'2025-06-01_11-33-14','We wanted the MSP and PSP stacks to be adjacent in position in the microcontroller’s memory, with the PSP stack placed below MSP. In order to determine the ideal location of the start of the PSP stack in memory, we needed to know the position of the start of the MSP stack in memory in addition to the desired size for our PSP stack. The MSP stack location is crucial to determine in order to ensure that the allocated space in memory for the stack does not overlap with other memory regions; disregarding this could lead to the overwriting of other memory regions that are crucial for other computer functions. The location of PSP is then found by subtracting the size of the PSP stack from the address in memory of the start of the MSP stack to find our new value for the PSP stack. We place the PSP stack below the MSP stack because it allows for us to have flexibility in what we determine to be the size of the stack; typically, the MSP address is at a location without enough space above it to implement a proper stack for multithreading.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,4,'2025-06-01_12-19-18','We wanted our MSP stack to be a size of 0x400 in memory and wanted our PSP stack to be allocated directly below MSP according to the mathematical relationship:\nPSP_Value = MSP_Initial_Value  –  0x400\nIn other words, the starting location of PSP is found by subtracting the size of the MSP stack from the address in memory of the start of MSP. The MSP stack location is crucial to determine because it ensures that there is space in memory for the desired max stack size (which includes MSP and PSP), so that the stack does not overlap with other memory regions. The MSP address in this case is used as the reference point, mathematically, for assigning memory space to PSP; without knowing the initial value of MSP, PSP would not be able to be calculated properly and otherwise trying to do so may lead to it being assigned to an already occupied space in memory. We also place the PSP stack below the MSP stack because it allows for us to have flexibility in what we determine to be the size of the stack; typically, the MSP address is at a location without enough space above it to implement a proper stack for multithreading.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,5,'2025-05-15_13-23-07','In the case of multiple threads, each stack will be the same size. This means that given the location in memory of the first stack (eg. MSP), the starting location in memory of any other stack can be found by adding a positive integer multiple of the stack size (eg. N*0x400 bytes) to the original stack address, so long as the main stack is large enough to contain an Nth stack.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,5,'2025-06-01_10-08-25','In the case of multiple threads, each stack will be the same size. This means that given the location in memory of the first stack (eg. MSP), the starting location in memory of any other stack can be found by adding a positive integer multiple of the stack size (eg. N*0x400 bytes) to the original stack address, so long as the Nth stack does not have an address greater than the address “MSP_INIT_VAL” plus the predetermined “MAX_STACK_SIZE” (the total memory allocated for stacks) minus 0x400 bytes (the size of the thread stack). Setting this condition in the program will ensure that new threads will only be created back-to-back in memory and will not be created if the stack is full, thus preventing overlap with other memory regions.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,5,'2025-06-01_11-33-14','In the case of multiple threads, each stack will be the same size. This means that after reserving the location in memory of MSP, the starting location in memory of the next thread that we want to run can be found 0x400 bytes after either MSP or the last thread address. This can be repeated N times so long as the Nth stack does not have an address greater than the address “MSP_INIT_VAL” plus the predetermined “MAX_STACK_SIZE” (the total memory allocated for stacks) minus 0x400 bytes (the size of a thread). Setting this condition in the program will ensure that new threads will only be created back-to-back in memory and will not be created if the stack is full, thus preventing overlap with other memory regions. This back-to-back alignment of stacks in memory ensures efficient use of memory space while also allowing us to index each thread at known even intervals of 0x400 bytes.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',1,5,'2025-06-01_12-19-18','In the case of multiple threads, each stack will be the same size. This means that after reserving the location in memory of MSP, the starting location in memory of the next thread that we want to run can be found 0x400 bytes after either MSP or the last thread address. This can be repeated N times so long as the Nth stack does not have an address greater than the address “MSP_INIT_VAL” plus the predetermined “MAX_STACK_SIZE” (the total memory allocated for stacks) minus 0x400 bytes (the size of a thread). Setting this condition in the program will ensure that new threads will only be created back-to-back in memory and will not be created if the stack is full, thus preventing overlap with other memory regions. This back-to-back alignment of stacks in memory ensures efficient use of memory space while also allowing us to index each thread at known even intervals of 0x400 bytes.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,1,'2025-06-01_14-41-10','The data stored in the program counter (PC) determines the next instruction that the computer will execute. By assigning the print continuously function (an infinite loop) to PC, we created an environment in which the program would run indefinitely, which is one of the primary steps involved in setting up a thread.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,1,'2025-06-01_16-16-36','The data stored in the program counter (PC) determines the next instruction that the computer will execute. By assigning a function pointer to PC that points to the print continuously function (an infinite loop), the operating system (OS) immediately goes to the address of the pointer in the next step of the program and executes the function. This creates an environment in which the program runs indefinitely.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,1,'2025-06-01_16-36-40','The data stored in the program counter (PC) determines the next instruction that the computer will execute. By assigning a function pointer to PC that points to the print continuously function (an infinite loop), the operating system (OS) immediately goes to the address of the pointer in the next step of the program and executes the function. This creates an environment in which the program runs indefinitely.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,1,'2025-06-01_16-51-33','The data stored in the program counter (PC) determines the next instruction that the computer will execute. By assigning a function pointer to PC that points to the print continuously function (an infinite loop), the operating system (OS) immediately goes to the address of the pointer in the next step of the program and executes the function. This creates an environment in which the program runs indefinitely.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,1,'2025-06-01_17-01-01','The data stored in the program counter (PC) determines the next instruction that the computer will execute. By assigning a function pointer to PC that points to the print continuously function (an infinite loop), the operating system (OS) immediately goes to the address of the pointer in the next step of the program and executes the function. This creates an environment in which the program runs indefinitely.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,1,'2025-06-24_15-51-30','The data stored in the program counter (PC) determines the next instruction that the computer will execute. By assigning a function pointer to PC that points to the print continuously function (an infinite loop), the CPU will go to the address of the pointer immediately after the previous instruction finishes, and execute the print continuously function. This is functionally equivalent to calling a function in code, but this time we can have the function be executed by the CPU directly, without involvement from the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,1,'2025-06-24_16-21-36','The data stored in the program counter (PC) determines the next instruction that the computer will execute. By assigning a function pointer to PC that points to the print continuously function (an infinite loop), the CPU will go to the address of the pointer immediately after the previous instruction finishes, and execute the print continuously function. This is functionally equivalent to calling a function in code, but this time we can have the function be executed by the CPU directly, without involvement from the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,1,'2025-06-25_13-01-11','The data stored in the program counter (PC) determines the next instruction that the computer will execute. By assigning a function pointer to PC that points to the print continuously function (an infinite loop), the CPU will go to the address of the pointer immediately after the previous instruction finishes, and execute the print continuously function. This is functionally equivalent to calling a function in code, but this time we can have the function be executed by the CPU directly, without involvement from the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,1,'2025-06-25_14-46-14','The data stored in the program counter (PC) determines the next instruction that the computer will execute. By assigning a function pointer to PC that points to the print continuously function (an infinite loop), the CPU will go to the address of the pointer immediately after the previous instruction finishes, and execute the print continuously function. This is functionally equivalent to calling a function in code, but this time we can have the function be executed by the CPU directly, without involvement from the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,2,'2025-06-01_14-41-10','A system call is a special type of function that requests the operating system’s (OS) kernel to perform a task. Normally, user-written programs and kernel tasks operate independently in order to safeguard the operating system from being affected at a low level by other programs. But sometimes, a program is required to access OS data, resources, or files, and executing system calls allow for this to happen while providing a mode of security between the two environments.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,2,'2025-06-01_16-16-36','A system call is a special type of function that requests the OS’s kernel to perform a task. Normally, user-written programs and kernel tasks operate independently in order to safeguard the operating system from being affected at a low level by other programs. But sometimes, a program is required to access OS data, resources, or files, and executing system calls allow for this to happen while providing a mode of security between the two environments.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,2,'2025-06-01_16-36-40','A system call is a special type of function that requests the OS’s kernel to perform a task. Normally, user-written programs and kernel tasks operate independently in order to safeguard the operating system from being affected at a low level by other programs. But sometimes, a program is required to access OS data, resources, or files, and executing system calls allow for this to happen while providing a mode of security between the two environments.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,2,'2025-06-01_16-51-33','A system call is a special type of function that requests the OS’s kernel to perform a task. Normally, user-written programs and kernel tasks operate independently in order to safeguard the operating system from being affected at a low level by other programs. But sometimes, a program is required to access OS data, resources, or files, and executing system calls allow for this to happen while providing a mode of security between the two environments.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,2,'2025-06-01_17-01-01','A system call is a special type of function that requests the OS’s kernel to perform a task. Normally, user-written programs and kernel tasks operate independently in order to safeguard the operating system from being affected at a low level by other programs. But sometimes, a program is required to access OS data, resources, or files, and executing system calls allow for this to happen while providing a mode of security between the two environments.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,2,'2025-06-24_15-51-30','A system call is a special type of function that is used by a user program to request a specific service from the kernel, such as performing file operations or reading/writing to memory, functions that the user program does not have the permission to execute. Normally, user-written programs and kernel tasks operate independently in order to safeguard the operating system from being affected at a low level by other programs. Sometimes, a program is required to access certain computer data, resources, or files, and executing system calls allow for this to happen while providing a mode of security between the two environments. What we don’t want is user programs to affect the computer’s memory without first consulting the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,2,'2025-06-24_16-21-36','A system call is a special type of function that is used by a user program to request a specific service from the kernel, such as performing file operations or reading/writing to memory, functions that the user program does not have the permission to execute. Normally, user-written programs and kernel tasks operate independently in order to safeguard the operating system from being affected at a low level by other programs. Sometimes, a program is required to access certain computer data, resources, or files, and executing system calls allow for this to happen while providing a mode of security between the two environments. What we don’t want is user programs to affect the computer’s memory without first consulting the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,2,'2025-06-25_13-01-11','A system call is a special type of function that is used by a user program to request a specific service from the kernel, such as performing file operations or reading/writing to memory, functions that the user program does not have the permission to execute. Normally, user-written programs and kernel tasks operate independently in order to safeguard the operating system from being affected at a low level by other programs. Sometimes, a program is required to access certain computer data, resources, or files, and executing system calls allow for this to happen while providing a mode of security between the two environments. What we don’t want is user programs to affect the computer’s memory without first consulting the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,2,'2025-06-25_14-46-14','A system call is a special type of function that is used by a user program to request a specific service from the kernel, such as performing file operations or reading/writing to memory, functions that the user program does not have the permission to execute. Normally, user-written programs and kernel tasks operate independently in order to safeguard the operating system from being affected at a low level by other programs. Sometimes, a program is required to access certain computer data, resources, or files, and executing system calls allow for this to happen while providing a mode of security between the two environments. What we don’t want is user programs to affect the computer’s memory without first consulting the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,3,'2025-06-01_14-41-10','In order to set up the thread’s stack, we need to predict what the OS environment would look like as if the thread had already been running, so that is has a foundation to continue running. What this means is that we need to manually assign the current and next states of the program to the program status (xPSR) and program counter (PC) registers respectively, as if they were assigned there by an existing thread. In our case, the current state is set to a 32-bit value, and our next state is our print continuously function. We also choose to set the rest of the registers to some arbitrary known value, so that we can view them in the debugger to ensure the thread is working. By forcing the OS into this state, we enable our thread to continue running indefinitely from the current state once the function is called to run it.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,3,'2025-06-01_16-16-36','A thread runs using registers and a predefined area of the stack to read and write data in memory. To prepare a thread for running, we need to imitate what this environment would look like as if the thread had already been running, so that it has a foundation to continue running once loaded. This means we need to push register values to the stack in the order that they will be popped when the thread is loaded for the first time, values that will enable the thread to execute properly. We will do this by first manually pushing the desired current and next states of the program—what will later be popped to (xPSR) and program counter (PC) registers respectively. In our case, the current state is set to a 32-bit value, and our next state is our print Following this, we also choose to push 14 more arbitrary known values to the stack continuously function, which will fill the rest of the registers. We do this so that we can view them in the debugger to ensure the thread is working. If we don’t push these preferred register values to the stack in this order, then they will not be popped into the correct registers, causing the thread to fail.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,3,'2025-06-01_16-36-40','A thread runs using registers and a predefined area of the stack to read and write data in memory. To prepare a thread for running, we need to imitate what this environment would look like as if the thread had already been running, so that it has a foundation to continue running once loaded. This means we need to push register values to the stack in the order that they will be popped when the thread is loaded for the first time, values that will enable the thread to execute properly. We will do this by first manually pushing the desired current and next states of the program—what will later be popped to (xPSR) and program counter (PC) registers respectively. In our case, the current state is set to a 32-bit value, and our next state is our print Following this, we also choose to push 14 more arbitrary known values to the stack continuously function, which will fill the rest of the registers. We do this so that we can view them in the debugger to ensure the thread is working. If we don’t push these preferred register values to the stack in this order, then they will not be popped into the correct registers, causing the thread to fail.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,3,'2025-06-01_16-51-33','A thread runs using registers and the stack to read and write data in memory. To prepare a thread for running, we need to imitate what this environment would look like as if the thread had already been running, so that it has a foundation to continue running once loaded. This means we need to first reserve a location in PSP to ensure that there is enough space in memory for our thread to run. Next, we need to push register values to this part of the stack in the order that they will be popped when the thread is loaded for the first time, values that will enable the thread to execute properly. We will do this by first manually pushing the desired current and next states of the program—what will later