spectively. In our case, the current state is set to a 32-bit value, and our next state is our print Following this, we also choose to push 14 more arbitrary known values to the stack continuously function, which will fill the rest of the registers. We do this so that we can view them in the debugger to ensure the thread is working. If we don’t push these preferred register values to the stack in this order, then they will not be popped into the correct registers, causing the thread to fail.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,3,'2025-06-25_13-01-11','A thread runs using registers and the stack to read and write data in memory. To prepare a thread for running, we need to imitate what this environment would look like as if the thread had already been running, so that it has a foundation to continue running once loaded. This means we need to first reserve a location in PSP to ensure that there is enough space in memory for our thread to run. Next, we need to push register values to this part of the stack in the order that they will be popped when the thread is loaded for the first time, values that will enable the thread to execute properly. We will do this by first manually pushing the desired current and next states of the program—what will later be popped to (xPSR) and program counter (PC) registers respectively. In our case, the current state is set to a 32-bit value, and our next state is our print Following this, we also choose to push 14 more arbitrary known values to the stack continuously function, which will fill the rest of the registers. We do this so that we can view them in the debugger to ensure the thread is working. If we don’t push these preferred register values to the stack in this order, then they will not be popped into the correct registers, causing the thread to fail.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,3,'2025-06-25_14-46-14','A thread runs using registers and the stack to read and write data in memory. To prepare a thread for running, we need to imitate what this environment would look like as if the thread had already been running, so that it has a foundation to continue running once loaded. This means we need to first reserve a location in PSP to ensure that there is enough space in memory for our thread to run. Next, we need to push register values to this part of the stack in the order that they will be popped when the thread is loaded for the first time, values that will enable the thread to execute properly. We will do this by first manually pushing the desired current and next states of the program—what will later be popped to (xPSR) and program counter (PC) registers respectively. In our case, the current state is set to a 32-bit value, and our next state is our print Following this, we also choose to push 14 more arbitrary known values to the stack continuously function, which will fill the rest of the registers. We do this so that we can view them in the debugger to ensure the thread is working. If we don’t push these preferred register values to the stack in this order, then they will not be popped into the correct registers, causing the thread to fail.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,4,'2025-06-01_14-41-10','Threads run in the stack, which we have defined as PSP. We need to define where PSP is in memory in order to assign a thread to run in it. We do this using the “__set_PSP” function to assign the start of PSP to a specific memory address. Since we are only running a single thread, this address will also be the address for our thread. If we didn’t set the PSP address before calling the function that runs our first thread, the program would crash as the thread would attempt to run at a memory address that is likely invalid, which would subsequently cause a hard fault crash.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,4,'2025-06-01_16-16-36','Threads run in the thread stack (PSP), which must be at a different location in memory than the default stack (MSP) because MSP is reserved for kernel operations. We need to define where PSP is in memory in order to assign a thread to run in it. We do this using the “__set_PSP” function to assign our thread stack to start at a specific memory address indicated by the stack pointer, which in our case is pointing to the desired location for PSP that we calculated earlier. Since we are only running a single thread, this address will also be the address for our thread. If we didn’t set the PSP address before calling the function that runs our first thread, the thread would attempt to run at a memory address that is likely invalid, which would subsequently cause a hard fault crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,4,'2025-06-01_16-36-40','Threads run in the thread stack (PSP), which must be at a different location in memory than the default stack (MSP) because MSP is reserved for kernel operations. We need to define where PSP is in memory in order to assign a thread to run in it. We do this using the “__set_PSP” function to assign our thread stack to start at a specific memory address indicated by the stack pointer, which in our case is pointing to the desired location for PSP that we calculated earlier. Since we are only running a single thread, this address will also be the address for our thread. If we didn’t set the PSP address before calling the function that runs our first thread, the thread would attempt to run at a memory address that is likely invalid, which would subsequently cause a hard fault crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,4,'2025-06-01_16-51-33','Threads run in the thread stack (PSP), which must be at a different location in memory than the default stack (MSP) because MSP is reserved for kernel operations. We need to define where PSP is in memory in order to assign a thread to run in it. We do this using the “__set_PSP” function to assign our thread stack to start at a specific memory address indicated by the stack pointer, which in our case is pointing to the desired location for PSP that we calculated earlier. Since we are only running a single thread, this address will also be the address for our thread. If we didn’t set the PSP address before calling the function that runs our first thread, the thread would attempt to run at a memory address that is likely invalid, which would subsequently cause a hard fault crash.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,4,'2025-06-01_17-01-01','Threads run in the thread stack (PSP), which must be at a different location in memory than the default stack (MSP) because MSP is reserved for kernel operations. We need to define where PSP is in memory in order to assign a thread to run in it. We do this using the “__set_PSP” function to assign our thread stack to start at a specific memory address indicated by the stack pointer, which in our case is pointing to the desired location for PSP that we calculated earlier. Since we are only running a single thread, this address will also be the address for our thread. If we didn’t set the PSP address before calling the function that runs our first thread, the thread would attempt to run at a memory address that is likely invalid, which would subsequently cause a hard fault crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,4,'2025-06-24_15-51-30','Threads run in the thread stack (PSP), which must be at a different location in memory than the default stack (MSP) because MSP is reserved for kernel operations. We need to define where PSP is in memory in order to assign a thread to run in it. We do this using the “__set_PSP” function to assign our stack pointer to the specific memory address where we want to run our thread, which in our case is pointing to the desired location for PSP that we calculated earlier. We need to set the PSP address this way because the CPU cannot, on its own, determine which stack to use. If we didn’t set the stack pointer to be our PSP address before calling the function that runs our first thread, the thread would attempt to run at an address that is likely invalid, which would subsequently cause a hard fault crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,4,'2025-06-24_16-21-36','Threads run in the thread stack (PSP), which must be at a different location in memory than the default stack (MSP) because MSP is reserved for kernel operations. We need to define where PSP is in memory in order to assign a thread to run in it. We do this using the “__set_PSP” function to assign our stack pointer to the specific memory address where we want to run our thread, which in our case is pointing to the desired location for PSP that we calculated earlier. We need to set the PSP address this way because the CPU cannot, on its own, determine which stack to use. If we didn’t set the stack pointer to be our PSP address before calling the function that runs our first thread, the thread would attempt to run at an address that is likely invalid, which would subsequently cause a hard fault crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,4,'2025-06-25_13-01-11','Threads run in the thread stack (PSP), which must be at a different location in memory than the default stack (MSP) because MSP is reserved for kernel operations. We need to define where PSP is in memory in order to assign a thread to run in it. We do this using the “__set_PSP” function to assign our stack pointer to the specific memory address where we want to run our thread, which in our case is pointing to the desired location for PSP that we calculated earlier. We need to set the PSP address this way because the CPU cannot, on its own, determine which stack to use. If we didn’t set the stack pointer to be our PSP address before calling the function that runs our first thread, the thread would attempt to run at an address that is likely invalid, which would subsequently cause a hard fault crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,4,'2025-06-25_14-46-14','Threads run in the thread stack (PSP), which must be at a different location in memory than the default stack (MSP) because MSP is reserved for kernel operations. We need to define where PSP is in memory in order to assign a thread to run in it. We do this using the “__set_PSP” function to assign our stack pointer to the specific memory address where we want to run our thread, which in our case is pointing to the desired location for PSP that we calculated earlier. We need to set the PSP address this way because the CPU cannot, on its own, determine which stack to use. If we didn’t set the stack pointer to be our PSP address before calling the function that runs our first thread, the thread would attempt to run at an address that is likely invalid, which would subsequently cause a hard fault crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,5,'2025-06-01_14-41-10','Our thread function loops continuously and never returns, meaning that the OS runs indefinitely. If our thread function instead executed a single command and returned, the thread would subsequently shut down, as there would be no future instructions that would need to be executed according to the program counter (PC) register. Without any threads in operation, our OS would no longer be operating and would shut down in response.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,5,'2025-06-01_16-16-36','Our thread function loops continuously and never returns, meaning that the OS runs indefinitely. If our thread function instead executed a single command and returned (in this case without a return address), the program would crash in a hard fault, as the program would not have an address in memory to return to.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,5,'2025-06-01_16-36-40','Our thread function loops continuously and never returns, meaning that the OS runs indefinitely. If our thread function instead executed a single command and returned (in this case without a return address), the program would crash in a hard fault, as the program would not have an address in memory to return to.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,5,'2025-06-01_16-51-33','Our thread function loops continuously and never returns, meaning that the OS runs indefinitely. If our thread function instead executed a single command and returned (in this case without a return address), the program would crash in a hard fault, as the program would not have a valid address in memory to return to receive instructions. For the program to properly shut down or transition to executing the next instruction, there must be a return address matching the location of the desired instruction to be run next at the top of the stack for the OS to pop to the PC register following the completion of the current function being run.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,5,'2025-06-01_17-01-01','Our thread function loops continuously and never returns, meaning that the OS runs indefinitely. If our thread function instead executed a single command and returned (in this case without a return address), the program would crash in a hard fault, as the program would not have a valid address in memory to return to receive instructions. For the program to properly shut down or transition to executing the next instruction, there must be a return address matching the location of the desired instruction to be run next at the top of the stack for the OS to pop to the PC register following the completion of the current function being run.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,5,'2025-06-24_15-51-30','Our thread function loops continuously and never returns, meaning that the OS runs indefinitely. If our thread function instead executed a single command and returned (in this case without a return address), the program would crash in a hard fault, as the program would not have a valid address in memory to return to receive instructions. Having a return address in the LR register, however, allows the OS to pop it to the PC register following the completion of the current function being run. For the program to run the proper shut down procedure or transition to executing the next instruction, there must be a return address stored in this register that matches the location of this next instruction to be run.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,5,'2025-06-24_16-21-36','Our thread function loops continuously and never returns, meaning that the OS runs indefinitely. If our thread function instead executed a single command and returned (in this case without a return address), the program would crash in a hard fault. It crashes because the Cortex M4 does not store the return address on the thread’s stack, and as a result, the program does not have a valid address in memory to return to receive instructions after the thread returns. Having a return address in the LR register, however, allows the OS to pop it to the PC register following the completion of the current function being run. For the program to run the proper shut down procedure or transition to executing the next instruction, there must be a return address stored in this register that matches the location of this next instruction to be run.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,5,'2025-06-25_13-01-11','Our thread function loops continuously and never returns, meaning that the OS runs indefinitely. If our thread function instead executed a single command and returned (in this case without a return address), the program would crash in a hard fault. It crashes because the Cortex M4 does not store the return address on the thread’s stack, and as a result, the program does not have a valid address in memory to return to receive instructions after the thread returns. Having a return address in the LR register, however, allows the OS to pop it to the PC register following the completion of the current function being run. For the program to run the proper shut down procedure or transition to executing the next instruction, there must be a return address stored in this register that matches the location of this next instruction to be run.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',2,5,'2025-06-25_14-46-14','Our thread function loops continuously and never returns, meaning that the OS runs indefinitely. If our thread function instead executed a single command and returned (in this case without a return address), the program would crash in a hard fault. It crashes because the Cortex M4 does not store the return address on the thread’s stack, and as a result, the program does not have a valid address in memory to return to receive instructions after the thread returns. Having a return address in the LR register, however, allows the OS to pop it to the PC register following the completion of the current function being run. For the program to run the proper shut down procedure or transition to executing the next instruction, there must be a return address stored in this register that matches the location of this next instruction to be run.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,1,'2025-06-25_13-54-14','First, an object file is created for each c file, then these object files are combined into a single executable using a linker.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,1,'2025-06-25_14-32-21','First, an object file is created for each c file, then these object files are combined into a single executable using a linker.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,1,'2025-06-26_14-19-29','There are three main processes that go into compiling multiple c files into a single executable. Firstly, the c files undergo preprocessing, which involves writing the content from any included headers or macros directly to the file instead of simply referencing them; this makes them able to be compiled into machine code, which is done in the next step. The preprocessed c file code is translated directly into machine code and compiled into an object file. The resulting set of object files finally undergoes linking, where references to functions, variables, and libraries across multiple files are resolved and all files are combined into a single executable file.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,1,'2025-06-26_15-40-43','There are three main processes that go into compiling multiple c files into a single executable. Firstly, the c files undergo preprocessing, which involves writing the content from any included headers or macros directly to the file instead of simply referencing them; this makes them able to be compiled into machine code, which is done in the next step. The preprocessed c file code is translated directly into machine code and compiled into an object file. The resulting set of object files finally undergoes linking, where references to functions, variables, and libraries across multiple files are resolved and all files are combined into a single executable file.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,1,'2025-06-26_17-21-28','There are three main processes that go into compiling multiple c files into a single executable. Firstly, the c files undergo preprocessing, which involves writing the content from any included headers or macros directly to the file instead of simply referencing them; this makes them able to be compiled into machine code, which is done in the next step. The preprocessed c file code is translated directly into machine code and compiled into an object file. The resulting set of object files finally undergoes linking, where references to functions, variables, and libraries across multiple files are resolved and all files are combined into a single executable file.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,2,'2025-06-25_13-54-14','When compiling a file, any #includes that are called in that file will cause the included files to also require compilation. It is a bad idea to include c files because they typically don’t need to be compiled every time the main program is changed, and are typically very large files, requiring long compile times—if the compiler is even able to handle them. It is better practice to include header files instead of c files. A header file only contains the definitions of the functions and/or structures that are in its respective c file, which contains the implementations of those functions and/or structures. Because of this, it is much easier for the compiler to compile the header files.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,2,'2025-06-25_14-32-21','When compiling a file, any #includes that are called in that file will cause the included files to also require compilation. It is a bad idea to include c files because they typically don’t need to be compiled every time the main program is changed, and are typically very large files, requiring long compile times—if the compiler is even able to handle them. It is better practice to include header files instead of c files. A header file only contains the definitions of the functions and/or structures that are in its respective c file, which contains the implementations of those functions and/or structures. Because of this, it is much easier for the compiler to compile the header files.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,2,'2025-06-26_14-19-29','A c file typically contains the definitions of functions and/or variables, which includes their actual implementation in code. This is different from a file (commonly a header file) that simply contains the declaration of the respective functions/variables. When compiling a file, any #includes that are called in that file will cause the included files to also require compilation. When you include a c file in more than one source file, you copy its full definitions across multiple compilations. This causes an error during linking, as the linker can only create an executable of all the c files if there is only one definition for each function/variable across each file. If the files can’t be linked, then we won’t be able to create our executable nor run our code. This error can be avoided by including header files instead of c files.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,2,'2025-06-26_15-40-43','A c file typically contains the definitions of functions and/or variables, which includes their actual implementation in code. This is different from a file (commonly a header file) that simply contains the declaration of the respective functions/variables. When compiling a file, any #includes that are called in that file will cause the included files to also require compilation. When you include a c file in more than one source file, you copy its full definitions across multiple compilations. This causes an error during linking, as the linker can only create an executable of all the c files if there is only one definition for each function/variable across each file. If the files can’t be linked, then we won’t be able to create our executable nor run our code. This error can be avoided by including header files instead of c files.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,2,'2025-06-26_17-21-28','A c file typically contains the definitions of functions and/or variables, which includes their actual implementation in code. This is different from a file (commonly a header file) that simply contains the declaration of the respective functions/variables. When compiling a file, any #includes that are called in that file will cause the included files to also require compilation. When you include a c file in more than one source file, you copy its full definitions across multiple compilations. This causes an error during linking, as the linker can only create an executable of all the c files if there is only one definition for each function/variable across each file. If the files can’t be linked, then we won’t be able to create our executable nor run our code. This error can be avoided by including header files instead of c files.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,3,'2025-06-25_13-54-14','A thread is set up, in general terms, in the following way:\nThe location of MSP and PSP are computed, and a pointer to the stack is declared that points to the desired location that we want to run our thread.\nThe required values for the registers to run the thread are pushed, in order, to the stack, which includes our thread function.\nA system call is declared to pop our desired register values from the stack and push them to the registers, thus initializing the thread to start running.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,3,'2025-06-25_14-32-21','A thread is set up, in general terms, in the following way:\nThe location of MSP and PSP are computed, and a pointer to the stack is declared that points to the desired location that we want to run our thread.\nThe required values for the registers to run the thread are pushed, in order, to the stack, which includes our thread function.\nA system call is declared to pop our desired register values from the stack and push them to the registers, thus initializing the thread to start running.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,3,'2025-06-26_14-19-29','A thread runs a function, which is written by the user to make the computer do some predefined task. Before any threads can be created, however, the kernel must be set up along with its ability to manage the stack and any threads that are allocated to it, which in our case is done by calling the osKernelnitialize function. Next, the user needs to set up the thread by calling the osCreateThread function and passing the function they want to run in that thread as the parameter. This loads the thread into memory and prepares it to be run. All that is left to do is to run the kernel with the function osKernelStart, and the thread will begin to run since, in our case, it is the only thread scheduled to run.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,3,'2025-06-26_15-40-43','A thread runs a function, which is written by the user to make the computer do some predefined task. Before any threads can be created, however, the kernel must be set up along with its ability to manage the stack and any threads that are allocated to it, which in our case is done by calling the osKernelnitialize function. Next, the user needs to set up the thread by calling the osCreateThread function and passing the function they want to run in that thread as the parameter. This loads the thread into memory and prepares it to be run. All that is left to do is to run the kernel with the function osKernelStart, and the thread will begin to run since, in our case, it is the only thread scheduled to run.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,3,'2025-06-26_17-21-28','A thread runs a function, which is written by the user to make the computer do some predefined task. Before any threads can be created, however, the kernel must be set up along with its ability to manage the stack and any threads that are allocated to it, which in our case is done by calling the osKernelnitialize function. Next, the user needs to set up the thread by calling the osCreateThread function and passing the function they want to run in that thread as the parameter. This loads the thread into memory and prepares it to be run. All that is left to do is to run the kernel with the function osKernelStart, and the thread will begin to run since, in our case, it is the only thread scheduled to run.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,4,'2025-06-25_13-54-14','The stack pool is the allocated space for our entire stack. Often, threads in the stack are set up to be the same size (eg. 0x400 bytes) so that it is easy to determine the address of any given thread in the stack, so that each new thread will be allocated 0x400 bytes in memory below the last thread. The OS needs to know where the last stack allocation was for the thread, so we typically define a pointer to this address in the stack.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,4,'2025-06-25_14-32-21','The stack pool is the allocated space for our entire stack. Often, threads in the stack are set up to be the same size (eg. 0x400 bytes) so that it is easy to determine the address of any given thread in the stack, so that each new thread will be allocated 0x400 bytes in memory below the last thread. The OS needs to know where the last stack allocation was for the thread, so we typically define a pointer to this address in the stack.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,4,'2025-06-26_14-19-29','The stack pool is the region in memory where the individual stacks for any threads are stored. This region is divided into memory chunks of equal size (eg. 0x400 bytes) where threads of 0x400 bytes or less can be stored. Each new thread is assigned its own chunk, which is necessary so that a thread doesn’t overwrite another thread’s data. This also allows threads to be indexed easily, as they are always allocated 0x400 bytes in memory below the last thread.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,4,'2025-06-26_15-40-43','The stack pool is the region in memory where the individual stacks for any threads are stored. This region is divided into memory chunks of equal size (eg. 0x400 bytes) where threads of 0x400 bytes or less can be stored. Each new thread is assigned its own chunk, which is necessary so that a thread doesn’t overwrite another thread’s data. This also allows threads to be indexed easily, as they are always allocated 0x400 bytes in memory below the last thread.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,4,'2025-06-26_17-21-28','The stack pool is the region in memory where the operating system’s stack is stored (MSP), as well as the individual stacks for any created threads (PSP). This region is divided into memory chunks of equal size (eg. 0x400 bytes) where threads of 0x400 bytes or less can be stored. Each new thread is assigned its own chunk, which is necessary so that a thread doesn’t overwrite another thread’s data. This also allows threads to be indexed easily, as they are always allocated 0x400 bytes in memory below the last thread.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,5,'2025-06-25_13-54-14','Methods and members that interact or interface directly with the computer’s memory go in the kernel files, whereas methods and members used by user programs go in the user files.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,5,'2025-06-25_14-32-21','Methods and members that interact or interface directly with the computer’s memory go in the kernel files, whereas methods and members used by user programs go in the user files.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,5,'2025-06-26_14-19-29','The kernel is defined as the set of files that contain methods and members that interact or interface directly with the computer’s hardware, memory, and other system resources critical for the functionality of the OS. The user, however, is defined as the set of files that contain the code for running programs and other applications, for instance threads. In order for the user to interact with the computer hardware, it must request the kernel to perform any such services; this ensures that the user can’t directly (or accidentally) modify the computer hardware, providing a level of security between operations that run a program, and operations that affect core system resources.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,5,'2025-06-26_15-40-43','The kernel is defined as the set of files that contain methods and members that interact or interface directly with the computer’s hardware, memory, and other system resources critical for the functionality of the OS. The user, however, is defined as the set of files that contain the code for running programs and other applications, for instance threads. In order for the user to interact with the computer hardware, it must request the kernel to perform any such services; this ensures that the user can’t directly (or accidentally) modify the computer hardware, providing a level of security between operations that run a program, and operations that affect core system resources. This separation between the user and the kernel simplifies the process for the user when writing application-level code, as the user doesn’t have to worry about interacting directly with computer hardware when writing programs.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',3,5,'2025-06-26_17-21-28','The kernel is defined as the set of files that contain methods and members that interact or interface directly with the computer’s hardware, memory, and other system resources critical for the functionality of the OS. The user, however, is defined as the set of files that contain the code for running programs and other applications, for instance threads. In order for the user to interact with the computer hardware, it must request the kernel to perform any such services; this ensures that the user can’t directly (or accidentally) modify the computer hardware, providing a level of security between operations that run a program, and operations that affect core system resources. This separation between the user and the kernel simplifies the process for the user when writing application-level code, as the user doesn’t have to worry about interacting directly with computer hardware when writing programs.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,1,'2025-07-03_13-25-06','The concept of round-robin scheduling involves allowing an ordered list of tasks to run in a rotating order for a short, but equal amount of time. At the end of this time, if a task has not completed, it is paused, sent to the back of the list, and the next task begins to run. This process can be implemented with a queue, which uses the heap to address each task dynamically in memory (using pointers) to enqueue and dequeue tasks as they run in a rotating schedule. Scheduling with an array (static memory), however, requires manual indexing and shifting of task positions in a list, which can be a more complex process in terms of processing time, allocated memory, and code needed for implementation. Using a queue for a rotating schedule of tasks is more beneficial in this case as it allows for flexible queue sizes and more straightforward written implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,1,'2025-07-07_11-30-39','The concept of round-robin scheduling involves allowing an ordered list of tasks to run in a rotating order for a short, but equal amount of time. At the end of this time, if a task has not completed, it is paused, sent to the back of the list, and the next task begins to run. This process can be implemented with a queue, which uses the heap to address each task dynamically in memory (using pointers) to enqueue and dequeue tasks as they run in a rotating schedule. Scheduling with an array (static memory), however, requires manual indexing and shifting of task positions in a list, which can be a more complex process in terms of processing time, allocated memory, and code needed for implementation. Using a queue for a rotating schedule of tasks is more beneficial in this case as it allows for flexible queue sizes and more straightforward written implementation.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,1,'2025-07-07_12-30-47','The concept of round-robin scheduling involves allowing an ordered list of tasks to run in a rotating order for a short, but equal amount of time. At the end of this time, if a task has not completed, it is paused, sent to the back of the list, and the next task begins to run. This process can be implemented with a queue, which uses the heap to address each task dynamically in memory (using pointers) to enqueue and dequeue tasks as they run in a rotating schedule. Scheduling with an array (static memory), however, requires manual indexing and shifting of task positions in a list, which can be a more complex process in terms of processing time, allocated memory, and code needed for implementation. The current scheduler doesn’t behave like a queue, because it has a fixed array size and doesn’t shift its members depending on where tasks are enqueued or dequeued. Using a queue for a rotating schedule of tasks instead of the current model is more beneficial in this case as it allows for flexible queue sizes and more efficient memory usage.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,1,'2025-07-08_13-24-01','The concept of round-robin scheduling involves allowing a list of tasks to loop in a rotating order for a short, but equal amount of time. This process can be implemented with a queue, where each task is enqueued and dequeued in a rotating order. Typically, a queue is implemented using pointers and dynamic memory allocation/deallocation. However, this introduces overhead to the system and complex heap management software that is not standard on this microcontroller, whereas we already have software for managing the stack. This means that much more code would have to be written to properly set up a heap management system, putting constraints on memory usage and processing time. In our case, however, the array implementation already acts like a simple queue, where the index of the current and next stack pointer is tracked and the list of threads is circularly looped through in order using the array. Implementing the scheduler as a static array is a more memory efficient alternative in this case, preventing potential fragmentation while requiring minimal code to implement, which is ideal for a small embedded system such as the Cortex M4, which has limited memory resource availability.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,2,'2025-07-03_13-25-06','A context switch is required when we want to pause the currently executing thread to run a different thread. The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the context of the current thread; this includes saving the state of the registers to the thread’s stack, and the thread’s stack pointer to the data structure responsible for scheduling (eg. a queue or an array). After this, the kernel function osSched is called, which chooses the next thread to be run, using the round-robin scheduling method in this case. The context of this next thread is then popped from its stack to the registers so that this new thread can begin running.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,2,'2025-07-07_11-30-39','A context switch is required when we want to pause the currently executing thread to run a different thread. The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the thread’s CPU register values to the thread’s stack using its PSP address. We have to manually program how to save registers R4-R11 to the stack, while the R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR registers are all saved automatically by the computer’s hardware; the thread’s stack pointer is also stored in the data structure responsible for scheduling (eg. a queue or an array). After this, the kernel function osSched is called, which chooses the next thread to be run, using the round-robin scheduling method in this case. The stack pointer for this thread and the state for its R4-R11 registers are restored manually, and the remaining registers are restored automatically. With the full context of this next thread loaded, the new thread can begin or resume execution.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,2,'2025-07-07_12-30-47','A context switch is required when we want to pause the currently executing thread to run a different thread. The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the thread’s CPU register values to the thread’s stack using its PSP address. We have to manually program how to save registers R4-R11 to the stack, while the R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR registers are all saved automatically by the computer’s hardware; the thread’s stack pointer is also stored in the data structure responsible for scheduling (eg. a queue or an array). After this, the kernel function osSched is called, which chooses the next thread to be run, using the round-robin scheduling method in this case. The stack pointer for this thread and the state for its R4-R11 registers are restored manually, and the remaining registers are restored automatically. With the full context of this next thread loaded, the new thread can begin or resume execution.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,2,'2025-07-08_13-24-01','A context switch is required when we want to pause the currently executing thread to run a different thread. The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the thread’s CPU register values to the thread’s stack using its PSP address. We have to manually program how to save registers R4-R11 to the stack, while the R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR registers are all saved automatically by the computer’s hardware; the thread’s stack pointer is also stored in the data structure responsible for scheduling (eg. a queue or an array). After this, the kernel function osSched is called, which chooses the next thread to be run, using the round-robin scheduling method in this case. The stack pointer for this thread and the state for its R4-R11 registers are restored manually, and the remaining registers are restored automatically. With the full context of this next thread loaded, the new thread can begin or resume execution.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,3,'2025-07-03_13-25-06','There are several reasons for why scheduling tasks by their designated priority is a bad idea, listed below:\nFor tasks that never yield or return, either by design or by error, any tasks that are a lower priority than those running indefinitely will never get a chance to run. Using a round-robin scheduler, every task at least gets a chance to run, even if partially.\nNew tasks that get assigned to the list may not immediately run even if they are assigned a higher priority than the currently running task. A high-priority task should immediately interrupt a lower priority one, but this would not happen given the very simple priority implementation outlined in the question; the higher priority task would only run once the lower priority one finishes or yields.\nThe behaviour of such a simple priority queue would be unpredictable and require more complex methods of task management in order to have a functioning scheduler. Using a round-robin scheduler allows for a very simple queue or array to manage the task schedule.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,3,'2025-07-07_11-30-39','There are several reasons for why scheduling tasks by their designated priority is a bad idea. Firstly, the scheduler will only ever run the highest priority task, and if this task runs indefinitely, such as in an always true while loop, no other task that is a lower priority will ever be allowed to run. This also means that new tasks that get assigned to the list may not immediately run even if they are assigned a higher priority than the currently running task, because the scheduler will wait until the present task completes, which in this case is never; a high-priority task should immediately interrupt a lower priority one, which is the basis of the concept of pre-emption. The consequence of this is that any long-running high priority tasks have the potential to lock out the system and guarantee that no other tasks can run. Using a round-robin scheduler instead allows for a very simple queue or array to manage the task schedule so that every task is guaranteed a small amount of time to run, even if partially, to allow for fair and equal computation time for all tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,3,'2025-07-07_12-30-47','There are several reasons for why scheduling tasks by their designated priority is a bad idea. Firstly, the scheduler will only ever run the highest priority task, and if this task runs indefinitely, such as in an always true while loop, no other task that is a lower priority will ever be allowed to run. This also means that new tasks that get assigned to the list may not immediately run even if they are assigned a higher priority than the currently running task, because the scheduler will wait until the present task completes, which in this case is never; a high-priority task should immediately interrupt a lower priority one, which is the basis of the concept of pre-emption. The consequence of this is that any long-running high priority tasks have the potential to lock out the system and guarantee that no other tasks can run. Using a round-robin scheduler instead allows for a very simple queue or array to manage the task schedule so that every task is guaranteed a small amount of time to run, even if partially, to allow for fair and equal computation time for all tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,3,'2025-07-08_13-24-01','There are several reasons for why scheduling tasks by their designated priority is a bad idea. Firstly, the scheduler will only ever run the highest priority task, and if this task runs indefinitely, such as in an always true while loop, no other task that is a lower priority will ever be allowed to run. This also means that new tasks that get assigned to the list may not immediately run even if they are assigned a higher priority than the currently running task, because the scheduler will wait until the present task completes, which in this case is never; a high-priority task should immediately interrupt a lower priority one, which is the basis of the concept of pre-emption. The consequence of this is that any long-running high priority tasks have the potential to lock out the system and guarantee that no other tasks can run. Using a round-robin scheduler instead allows for a very simple queue or array to manage the task schedule so that every task is guaranteed a small amount of time to run, even if partially, to allow for fair and equal computation time for all tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,4,'2025-07-03_13-25-06','Writing and calling C functions like osSched in our handlers is beneficial because it is easier to understand and debug compared to raw assembly code. It also allows us to have the flexibility to call the same C functions in other regions of our code if desired, instead of having to hard code the same function in assembly multiple times. The downsides to using C functions, however, are that we use more memory than we would with an assembly implementation. This can cause a decrease in real-time performance as well as fill up our stack more, which may limit us when using very small microcontrollers. In this lab however, highly-efficient performance is not a priority so it is much more convenient to use C functions.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,4,'2025-07-07_11-30-39','Writing and calling C functions like osSched in our handlers is beneficial because it is easier to understand and debug compared to raw assembly code. It also allows us to have the flexibility to call the same C functions in other regions of our code if desired, instead of having to hard code the same function in assembly multiple times. The downsides to using C functions, however, are that we use more memory than we would with an assembly implementation. This can cause a decrease in real-time performance as well as fill up our stack more, which may limit us when using very small microcontrollers. In this lab however, highly-efficient performance is not a priority so it is much more convenient to use C functions.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,4,'2025-07-07_12-30-47','Writing and calling C functions like osSched in our handlers is beneficial because it is easier to understand and debug compared to raw assembly code. It also allows us to have the flexibility to call the same C functions in other regions of our code if desired, instead of having to hard code the same function in assembly multiple times. The downsides to using C functions, however, are that we use more memory than we would with an assembly implementation. This can cause a decrease in real-time performance as well as fill up our stack more, which may limit us when using very small microcontrollers. In this lab however, highly-efficient performance is not a priority so it is much more convenient to use C functions.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,4,'2025-07-08_13-24-01','Writing and calling C functions like osSched in our handlers is beneficial because it is easier to understand and debug compared to raw assembly code. It also allows us to have the flexibility to call the same C functions in other regions of our code if desired, instead of having to hard code the same function in assembly multiple times. The downsides to using C functions, however, are that we use more memory than we would with an assembly implementation. This can cause a decrease in real-time performance as well as fill up our stack more, which may limit us when using very small microcontrollers. In this lab however, highly-efficient performance is not a priority so it is much more convenient to use C functions.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,5,'2025-07-03_13-25-06','Creating only one thread in our case would still allow the OS to run, the only difference being that there would be no real context switching executed while the OS is running, as there would be no alterative threads to switch to. After the osYield function is called, triggering osSched, our schedule can only re-select the same single thread. This means that the context is still saved (popped from the registers to the stack), but it is immediately loaded back into the registers afterwards, triggering the thread to continue running.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,5,'2025-07-07_11-30-39','Creating only one thread in our case would still allow the OS to run, the only difference being that there would be no real context switching executed while the OS is running, as there would be no alterative threads to switch to. After the osYield function is called, triggering osSched, our schedule can only re-select the same single thread. This means that the context is still saved (popped from the registers to the stack), but it is immediately loaded back into the registers afterwards, triggering the thread to continue running.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,5,'2025-07-07_12-30-47','Creating only one thread in our case would still allow the OS to run, the only difference being that there would be no real context switching executed while the OS is running, as there would be no alterative threads to switch to. After the osYield function is called, triggering osSched, our schedule can only re-select the same single thread. This means that the context is still saved (popped from the registers to the stack), but it is immediately loaded back into the registers afterwards, triggering the thread to continue running.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',4,5,'2025-07-08_13-24-01','Creating only one thread in our case would still allow the OS to run, the only difference being that there would be no real context switching executed while the OS is running, as there would be no alterative threads to switch to. After the osYield function is called, triggering osSched, our schedule can only re-select the same single thread. This means that the context is still saved (popped from the registers to the stack), but it is immediately loaded back into the registers afterwards, triggering the thread to continue running.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',5,1,'2025-07-10_18-30-08','To pass an argument into a thread, the user has to call a kernel function such as osCreateThread and pass in pointers to both the thread function and the argument that they want to be passed in the thread. After this, the OS allocates a stack for the thread as normal, but during the process of pushing register values onto the stack, the argument that the user passed is put into the position in the stack frame that holds the value of R0. R0 is one of the registers that is meant to hold an argument pointer for passing into the function pointed to in PC, so manually assigning the argument pointer to R0 simulates calling the thread function and its associated arguments in code (eg. thread_function(argument_1);).\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',5,1,'2025-07-10_19-06-34','To pass an argument into a thread, the user has to call a kernel function such as osCreateThread and pass in pointers to both the thread function and the argument that they want to be passed in the thread. After this, the OS allocates a stack for the thread as normal, but during the process of pushing register values onto the stack, the argument that the user passed is put into the position in the stack frame that holds the value of R0. R0 is one of the registers that is meant to hold an argument pointer for passing into the function pointed to in PC, so manually assigning the argument pointer to R0 simulates calling the thread function and its associated arguments in code (eg. thread_function(argument_1);).\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',5,2,'2025-07-10_18-30-08','osYield is a function that triggers a system call and is meant for user programs that need to request a specific interrupt from the kernel. Our context-switching functions, however, are kernel programs, so they don’t require system calls to perform certain tasks. Calling osYield or any other system calls inside one of these kernel handler functions may cause a nest of interrupts (handlers calling other handlers) and may lead to unexpected kernel behaviour, faults, or crashes. But we can instead simply call the low-level functions we want to run instead of risking such behaviour, because we don’t need system calls when we are in kernel handlers.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',5,2,'2025-07-10_19-06-34','osYield is a function that triggers a system call and is meant for user programs that need to request a specific interrupt from the kernel. Our context-switching functions, however, are kernel programs, so they don’t require system calls to perform certain tasks. Calling osYield or any other system calls inside one of these kernel handler functions may cause a nest of interrupts (handlers calling other handlers) and may lead to unexpected kernel behaviour, such as faults or crashes. This happens because when the kernel is handling an exception, it is saving context from registers to the stack. But, if another exception is called during this process, the data being handled in the first exception may be corrupted as the second exception is handled. The Cortex M4 is designed to only run one handler process at a time. But we can instead simply call the low-level functions we want to run instead of risking such corruption, because we don’t need system calls when we are in kernel handlers.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',5,3,'2025-07-10_18-30-08','A higher resolution OS clock is useful for precision timing and scheduling, allowing us to have shorter and/or more accurate deadlines. This is useful if we want to better simulate real-time behaviour as threads and other programs can run with better responsiveness. On the other hand, such a clock will increase the frequency of interrupts generated from context switches and the SysTick handler itself, potentially slowing down performance by reducing the time available for threads to run. Because of this, it is important to balance the efficiency of the OS with the desire to be as precise as possible when deciding on a system clock speed.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',5,3,'2025-07-10_19-06-34','A higher resolution OS clock is useful for precision timing and scheduling, allowing us to have shorter and/or more accurate deadlines. This is useful if we want to better simulate real-time behaviour as threads and other programs can run with better responsiveness. On the other hand, such a clock will increase the frequency of interrupts generated from context switches and the SysTick handler itself, potentially slowing down performance by reducing the time available for threads to run. Because of this, it is important to balance the efficiency of the OS with the desire to be as precise as possible when deciding on a system clock speed.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',5,4,'2025-07-10_18-30-08','In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS is instead designed to reference a timer and the function’s runtime when running a thread to trigger a context switch; no action is needed from the user to execute a context switch except for when the user wants to manually define a runtime for the thread function, which requires the user to call the osCreateThreadWithDeadline function and passing the runtime argument value instead of the osCreateThread function, which uses a default runtime. In co-operative multitasking, the user needs to ensure that the thread function they want to run calls osYield manually, as the OS will only switch context when this system call is made. This form of multitasking is much easier to set up, as timing trackers are not needed to determine context switches.\n',0,'s25'),('jdoehler',5,4,'2025-07-10_19-06-34','In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS is instead designed to reference a timer and the function’s runtime and time slice when running a thread to periodically trigger a context switch if the time slice runs out; no action is needed from the user to execute a context switch except for when the user wants to manually define a time slice for the thread function, which requires the user to call the osCreateThreadWithDeadline function and passing the runtime argument value instead of the osCreateThread function, which uses a default runtime. This makes sure that no thread can run indefinitely even if osYield is not called. In co-operative multitasking, the user needs to ensure that the thread function they want to run calls osYield manually, as the OS will only switch context when this system call is made. This form of multitasking is much easier to implement, as timing trackers are not needed to determine context switches, but leaves the responsibility of calling a context switch within an appropriate (short) time entirely up to the user.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',5,5,'2025-07-10_18-30-08','Both methods of context switching trigger the PendSV handler to actually make the context switch, despite using different methods to determine when the context switch was desired. Co-operative switching requires manual implementation, whereas pre-emptive switching is automatically implemented by the OS using timers. Neither method is actually involved in the process of context switching; they only determine when it happens. This allows for both methods to be used interchangeably without causing any issues in scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('jdoehler',5,5,'2025-07-10_19-06-34','Both methods of context switching trigger the PendSV handler to actually make the context switch, despite using different methods to determine when the context switch was desired. Co-operative switching requires manual implementation, whereas pre-emptive switching is automatically implemented by the OS using timers. Neither method is actually involved in the process of context switching; they only determine when it happens. This allows for both methods to be used interchangeably without causing any issues in scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,1,'2025-05-15_12-16-47','The Hardware Abstraction Layer, or HAL, is a software component that allows developers to write code that is independent from the hardware. The HAL is used in a general embedded project because it allows the same software application to be used to develop software for different embedded systems without overhauling the IDE.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',1,1,'2025-05-27_16-03-14','The Hardware Abstraction Layer, or HAL, is a software component that allows developers to write code that is independent from the hardware. “This abstraction layer allows software programs to communicate with hardware devices using a high-level application programming interface (API), rather than having to deal with the intricacies of device-specific drivers and protocols” [1]. HAL is used in a general embedded project because HAL can provide a standardized interface to write drivers to a consistent specification [1]. This allows drivers created for a specific HAL interface to be used with other hardware that supports this same HAL interface [1].\nReferences:\n[1] “What is a hardware abstraction layer and how does it work?,” What is a Hardware Abstraction Layer and How Does it Work? | Lenovo CA, https://www.lenovo.com/ca/en/glossary/hardware-abstraction-layer/?orgRef=https%253A%252F%252Fwww.google.com%252F&srsltid=AfmBOopfGGCNitNKH-A0NPj5h89C9tR2bDxp_VfAXla0opGCid4dkkLV (accessed May 27, 2025).\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,1,'2025-05-29_15-56-19','The Hardware Abstraction Layer, or HAL, is a software component that allows developers to write code that is independent from the hardware. “This abstraction layer allows software programs to communicate with hardware devices using a high-level application programming interface (API), rather than having to deal with the intricacies of device-specific drivers and protocols” [1]. HAL is used in a general embedded project because HAL can provide a standardized interface to write drivers to a consistent specification [1]. This allows drivers created for a specific HAL interface to be used with other hardware that supports this same HAL interface [1].\nReferences:\n[1] “What is a hardware abstraction layer and how does it work?,” What is a Hardware Abstraction Layer and How Does it Work? | Lenovo CA, https://www.lenovo.com/ca/en/glossary/hardware-abstraction-layer/?orgRef=https%253A%252F%252Fwww.google.com%252F&srsltid=AfmBOopfGGCNitNKH-A0NPj5h89C9tR2bDxp_VfAXla0opGCid4dkkLV (accessed May 27, 2025).\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,1,'2025-06-23_14-20-43','The Hardware Abstraction Layer, or HAL, is a software component that allows developers to write code that is independent from the hardware. “This abstraction layer allows software programs to communicate with hardware devices using a high-level application programming interface (API), rather than having to deal with the intricacies of device-specific drivers and protocols” [1]. HAL is used in a general embedded project because HAL can provide a standardized interface to write drivers to a consistent specification [1]. This allows drivers created for a specific HAL interface to be used with other hardware that supports this same HAL interface [1].\nReferences:\n[1] “What is a hardware abstraction layer and how does it work?,” What is a Hardware Abstraction Layer and How Does it Work? | Lenovo CA, https://www.lenovo.com/ca/en/glossary/hardware-abstraction-layer/?orgRef=https%253A%252F%252Fwww.google.com%252F&srsltid=AfmBOopfGGCNitNKH-A0NPj5h89C9tR2bDxp_VfAXla0opGCid4dkkLV (accessed May 27, 2025).\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,1,'2025-06-23_14-43-02','The Hardware Abstraction Layer, or HAL, is a software component that allows developers to write code that is independent from the hardware. “This abstraction layer allows software programs to communicate with hardware devices using a high-level application programming interface (API), rather than having to deal with the intricacies of device-specific drivers and protocols” [1]. HAL is used in a general embedded project because HAL can provide a standardized interface to write drivers to a consistent specification [1]. This allows drivers created for a specific HAL interface to be used with other hardware that supports this same HAL interface [1].\nReferences:\n[1] “What is a hardware abstraction layer and how does it work?,” What is a Hardware Abstraction Layer and How Does it Work? | Lenovo CA, https://www.lenovo.com/ca/en/glossary/hardware-abstraction-layer/?orgRef=https%253A%252F%252Fwww.google.com%252F&srsltid=AfmBOopfGGCNitNKH-A0NPj5h89C9tR2bDxp_VfAXla0opGCid4dkkLV (accessed May 27, 2025).\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,2,'2025-05-15_12-16-47','The io_putchar function writes a character to the IO channel specified in the HAL_UART_Transmit function call. The io_putchar function is needed because it is repeatedly called any time the printf function is called to individually print each character in the string of characters passed to the printf function.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',1,2,'2025-05-27_16-03-14','The io_putchar function writes a character to the IO channel specified in the HAL_UART_Transmit function call. The io_putchar function is needed because it is repeatedly called any time the printf function is called to individually print each character in the string of characters passed to the printf function. While io_putchar can be used in the printf function, it can also be used to supply characters to any variable assignment or function input in an embedded system.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',1,2,'2025-05-29_15-56-19','The io_putchar function writes a character to the IO channel specified in the HAL_UART_Transmit function call. The io_putchar function is needed because it is repeatedly called any time the printf function is called to individually print each character in the string of characters passed to the printf function. While io_putchar can be used in the printf function, it can also be used to supply characters to any variable assignment or function input in an embedded system. It is important to have a function like io_putchar when working with different types of hardware or output devices because the stdio library that contains the printf function needs helper functions, like io_putchar, to allow it to interact with hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',1,2,'2025-06-23_14-20-43','The io_putchar function writes a character to the IO channel specified in the HAL_UART_Transmit function call. The io_putchar function is needed because it is repeatedly called any time the printf function is called to individually print each character in the string of characters passed to the printf function. It is important to have a function like io_putchar when working with hardware or output devices because the stdio library that contains the printf function needs helper functions, like io_putchar, to allow it to interact with hardware. When working with different types of hardware, it is important to have a consistent function that can be implemented to facilitate the printf function in each type of hardware, allowing multiple different sets of hardware to communicate with one another. By building a custom helper function that can work across multiple hardware devices, or be slightly modified from device to device, we can simplify our development process and the interaction between different hardware devices.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,2,'2025-06-23_14-43-02','The io_putchar function writes a character to the IO channel specified in the HAL_UART_Transmit function call. The io_putchar function is needed because it is repeatedly called any time the printf function is called to individually print each character in the string of characters passed to the printf function. It is important to have a function like io_putchar when working with hardware or output devices because the stdio library that contains the printf function needs helper functions, like io_putchar, to allow it to interact with hardware. When working with different types of hardware, it is important to have a consistent function that can be implemented to facilitate the printf function in each type of hardware, allowing multiple different sets of hardware to communicate with one another. By building a custom helper function that can work across multiple hardware devices, or be slightly modified from device to device, we can simplify our development process and the interaction between different hardware devices.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,3,'2025-05-15_12-16-47','We had to use the debugger in this lab to view the register values. We didn’t just use printf to output the values we needed because register values cannot be directly output using printf. There is a longer process required to make a register value able to be output, all of which is unnecessary if we just use the debugger.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,3,'2025-05-27_16-03-14','We had to use the debugger in this lab to view the register values. We didn’t just use printf to output the values we needed because register values cannot be directly output using printf. There is a longer process required to make a register value able to be output, all of which is unnecessary if we just use the debugger.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,3,'2025-05-29_15-56-19','We had to use the debugger in this lab to view the register values. We didn’t just use printf to output the values we needed because register values cannot be directly output using printf. There is a longer process required to make a register value able to be output, all of which is unnecessary if we just use the debugger.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,3,'2025-06-23_14-20-43','We had to use the debugger in this lab to view the register values. We didn’t just use printf to output the values we needed because register values cannot be directly output using printf. There is a longer process required to make a register value able to be output, all of which is unnecessary if we just use the debugger.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,3,'2025-06-23_14-43-02','We had to use the debugger in this lab to view the register values. We didn’t just use printf to output the values we needed because register values cannot be directly output using printf. There is a longer process required to make a register value able to be output, all of which is unnecessary if we just use the debugger.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,4,'2025-05-15_12-16-47','The stack allocation method we used works by initially setting a maximum amount of stack memory, 0x4000 (or 16 KB), starting at that memory value, and working downwards in memory values. MSP_init_val is an initially large number. By subtracting 0x400 from the address of MSP_inti_val, we can calculate a new address to have PSP_val point to. We need to know the initial location of MSP for this to work since the allocation of PSP_val is dependent on the initial value of MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,4,'2025-05-27_16-03-14','The stack allocation method we used works by initially setting a maximum amount of stack memory, 0x4000 (or 16 KB), starting at that memory value, and working downwards in memory values. MSP_init_val is an initially large number. By subtracting 0x400 from the address of MSP_inti_val, we can calculate a new address to have PSP_val point to. We need to know the initial location of MSP for this to work since the allocation of PSP_val is dependent on the initial value of MSP.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',1,4,'2025-05-29_15-56-19','The stack allocation method we used works by initially setting a maximum amount of stack memory, 0x4000 (or 16 KB), starting at that memory value, and working downwards in memory values. MSP_init_val is an initially large number. By subtracting 0x400 from the address of MSP_inti_val, we can calculate a new address to have PSP_val point to. This offset of the initial MSP and PSP values is done so that there are no memory overlaps between MSP and PSP. We need to know the initial location of MSP for this to work since the allocation of PSP_val is dependent on the initial value of MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,4,'2025-06-23_14-20-43','The stack allocation method we used works by initially setting a maximum amount of stack memory, 0x4000 (or 16 KB), starting at that memory value, and working downwards in memory values. MSP_init_val is an initially large number. By subtracting 0x400 from the address of MSP_inti_val, we can calculate a new address to have PSP_val point to. This offset of the initial MSP and PSP values is done so that there are no memory overlaps between MSP and PSP. We need to know the initial location of MSP for this to work since the allocation of PSP_val is dependent on the initial value of MSP.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',1,4,'2025-06-23_14-43-02','The stack allocation method we used works by initially setting a maximum amount of stack memory, 0x4000 (or 16 KB), starting at that memory register value, and working downwards in memory register values. The address of MSP_init_val is an initially large number. By subtracting 0x400 from the address of MSP_inti_val, we can calculate a new address to have PSP_val point to. This offset of the initial MSP and PSP values is done so that there are no memory overlaps between MSP and PSP. We need to know the initial location of MSP for this to work since the allocation of PSP_val is dependent on the initial value of MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,5,'2025-05-15_12-16-47','To make this work, we would need to have multiple MSP_init_val’s, each a certain address value from one another based on the required size of the stacks. For example, if we want to have two stacks of size five, we would need to have MSP_init_val_1’s memory address be 0x400 * 5 values higher in the memory than MSP_init_val_2’s memory address. By doing this, we can then create a PSP_val_1 that can count down 5 times by a value of 0x400 before hitting MSP_init_val_2’s memory address, allowing us to have multiple stacks with completely separate groups of memory addresses.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',1,5,'2025-05-27_16-03-14','To make this work, we would need to have multiple MSP_init_val’s, each a certain address value from one another based on the required size of the stacks. For example, if we want to have two stacks of size five, we will need to have MSP_init_val_1’s memory address be 0x400 * 5 values higher in the memory than MSP_init_val_2’s memory address. This difference in memory by a value of 0x400 * 5 is there to avoid overlap between MSP_init_val_1 and MSP_init_val_2’s memory. We can then create a PSP_val_1 that can count down 5 times by a value of 0x400 before hitting MSP_init_val_2’s memory address, allowing us to have multiple stacks with separate groups of memory addresses.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',1,5,'2025-05-29_15-56-19','To make this work, we would need to have multiple MSP_init_val’s, each with a different address value from one another by a difference based on the required size of the stacks. The appropriate offset between stacks can be determined by multiplying the size of a stack by the amount of memory required for each value in the stack. For example, if we want to have two stacks of size five, we will need to have MSP_init_val_1’s memory address be a value equal to five times 0x400 higher in the memory than MSP_init_val_2’s memory address. This difference in memory by a value of five times 0x400 prevents overlap between MSP_init_val_1 and MSP_init_val_2’s memory. We can then create a PSP_val_1 that can count down 5 times by a value of 0x400 before hitting MSP_init_val_2’s memory address, allowing us to have multiple stacks with separate groups of memory addresses.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,5,'2025-06-23_14-20-43','To make this work, we would need to have multiple MSP_init_val’s, each with a different address value from one another by a difference based on the required size of the stacks. The appropriate offset between stacks can be determined by multiplying the size of a stack by the amount of memory required for each value in the stack. For example, if we want to have two stacks of size five, we will need to have MSP_init_val_1’s memory address be a value equal to five times 0x400 higher in the memory than MSP_init_val_2’s memory address. This difference in memory by a value of five times 0x400 prevents overlap between MSP_init_val_1 and MSP_init_val_2’s memory. We can then create a PSP_val_1 that can count down 5 times by a value of 0x400 before hitting MSP_init_val_2’s memory address, allowing us to have multiple stacks with separate groups of memory addresses.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',1,5,'2025-06-23_14-43-02','To make this work, we would need to have multiple MSP_init_val’s, each with a different address value from one another by a difference based on the required size of the stacks. The appropriate offset between stacks can be determined by multiplying the size of a stack by the amount of memory required for each value in the stack. For example, if we want to have two stacks of size five, we will need to have MSP_init_val_1’s memory address be a value equal to five times 0x400 higher in the memory than MSP_init_val_2’s memory address. This difference in memory by a value of five times 0x400 prevents overlap between MSP_init_val_1 and MSP_init_val_2’s memory. We can then create a PSP_val_1 that can count down 5 times by a value of 0x400 before hitting MSP_init_val_2’s memory address, allowing us to have multiple stacks with separate groups of memory addresses.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',2,1,'2025-06-26_15-14-24','The output to the command shell did not change when PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function. The output remained a continuous output stream of “Hello, PC!” However, the place in memory that the print continuously function runs in changes. When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the jumpAssembly function was called with an input argument of the address of the print continuously function, which was R0. This stored function was then moved by the assembly code from R0 to PC and then run just as before.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',2,1,'2025-06-26_18-22-16','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the jumpAssembly function was called with an input argument of the address of the print continuously function, which was R0. As a result, PC was updated to hold a function pointer to the print continuously function and since PC holds the address of the next instruction, setting PC causes a jump to a new function and its immediate execution.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',2,1,'2025-06-26_22-40-40','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the jumpAssembly function was called with an input argument of the address of the print continuously function, which was R0. As a result, PC was updated to hold a function pointer to the print continuously function and since PC holds the address of the next instruction, setting PC causes a jump to a new function and its immediate execution.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',2,2,'2025-06-26_15-14-24','A system call is the way that a program can go about requesting help from the operating system to complete a task that it cannot on its own. Without system calls, individual programs would be incapable of performing tasks that require the operating system’s help. For example, if a program needs to alter a file, it must perform 4 individual system calls. One to open the file, one to read the file, one to write to the file, and one to close the file.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',2,2,'2025-06-26_18-22-16','A system call is the way that a program can go about requesting help from the operating system to complete a task that it cannot on its own. In other words, system calls let user programs access privileged OS functionality. The actual work (eg. File I/O) required to perform system calls is done by the kernel. Systems calls also help to enforce security by restricting direct hardware access, instead routing requests through the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',2,2,'2025-06-26_22-40-40','A system call is the way that a program can go about requesting help from the operating system to complete a task that it cannot on its own. In other words, system calls let user programs access privileged OS functionality. The actual work (eg. File I/O) required to perform system calls is done by the kernel. Systems calls also help to enforce security by restricting direct hardware access, instead routing requests through the kernel. In ARM architecture, a system call is triggered using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction. This results in an exception, switching the processor from unprivileged (aka “user”) mode to privileged (aka “handler”) mode. Control is then passed to the SVC_Handler function, where the kernel inspects the requested service and executes the appropriate privileged operation.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',2,3,'2025-06-26_15-14-24','The thread’s stack was set up by first setting up xPSR and PC. Once these registers had been set up, 14 stack frames were created in the process of setting registers R0, R1, R2, and more to an arbitrary value, 0xA. Once these stack frames were created, RUN_FIRST_THREAD is called using an SVC call. In doing so, PSP is set to stackptr, and runFirstThread is called, forcing a manual jump to the PC register, triggering the stackptr to point to PC and allowing print continuously to run.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',2,3,'2025-06-26_18-22-16','The thread’s stack is set up by allocating memory to the key registers (R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR). By setting up all these registers, we can simulate an interrupted context, allowing the thread to start as if resuming once we call RunFirstThread. We need to store register values on the stack to enable context switching, allowing us to pause and resume threads.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',2,3,'2025-06-26_22-40-40','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a memory area (the stack) with the values the CPU expects when restoring thread context. This includes storing register values such as R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR, in the order expected by the exception return mechanism. These values are not allocated to the registers but written into memory so that, when the thread starts (via RunFirstThread), the processor can pop these values into the actual CPU registers. This simulates the state of the CPU after an interrupt, enabling the thread to start as if it were resumed mid-execution. Correct order is critical to avoid misinterpreting register values and crashing the system.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',2,4,'2025-06-26_15-14-24','Our processor (the Cortex M4) inherently has two stack pointers, MSP and PSP. MSP is always initially set to some default value, meaning that we can always reference it, even if the value is useless. However, PSP has no default value and therefore must be explicitly set. As a result, when the CPU tries to access the address pointed to by PSP and PSP has not been explicitly set, it finds nothing and returns a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',2,4,'2025-06-26_18-22-16','The Cortext-M4 processor uses MSP for system code and PSP for running threads. Before running a thread, PSP must point to valid stack memory or the CPU will attempt to access invalid memory and crash, leading to a hard fault. By setting PSP prior to running the thread, the CPU will access valid memory, preventing a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',2,4,'2025-06-26_22-40-40','The Cortext-M4 processor uses two stack pointers. First, the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is the default and responsible for exception and interrupt handlers. Second, the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is used for running application threads. Running threads using PSP allows their stacks to be isolated from system stacks. Before running a thread, PSP must point to valid stack memory. If PSP does not point to valid stack memory, the CPU will attempt to access invalid memory and crash, leading to a hard fault. By setting PSP prior to running the thread, the CPU will access valid memory, preventing a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',2,5,'2025-06-26_15-14-24','If the thread function did not loop continuously and instead printed something once, there would be zero output to the console. This is because the function, when running continuously, runs as a thread in memory. However, the print function starts running before the __set_PSP function is called to have PSP point to the stack frame running the print function. While running continuously, the function continues to run after PSP has been assigned to its spot in memory. However, with only one function call the print function returns before PSP is assigned to it, preventing any printed messages from showing up at all.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',2,5,'2025-06-26_18-22-16','Threads are intended to run forever or until terminated by the OS. They are not designed to return. Returning involves looking for an address (via LR) that does not exist or is invalid. The CPU then attempts to jump to this address and hard faults because it does not exist or is invalid.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',2,5,'2025-06-26_22-40-40','Threads are intended to run forever or until terminated by the OS. They are not designed to return. Returning involves looking for an address (via LR) that does not exist or is invalid. The CPU then attempts to jump to this address and hard faults because it does not exist or is invalid. In our thread setup, we created a stack frame containing the registers required to start execution, including PC and xPSR, but we did not store a valid return address (LR). If the thread function tries to return, the CPU will try to pop LR and jump to its address, but since the thread’s stack does not have a valid return address, the CPU tries to access an undefined address, resulting in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',3,1,'2025-06-27_00-52-41','Multiple *.c files can get compiled into a single executable file using header files and includes. We can declare the required functions to be used in a *.c file in a *.h file, include that *.h file in the *.c file, and then define those functions in the *.c file. This allows the compiler to process all of the *.h files included in the main.c file as a part of the *.h files that individually include all of *.h files.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',3,1,'2025-06-27_02-00-06','The process of building a single executable from multiple *.c files involves three main stages. First, there is preprocessing, where each *.c file is run through the C preprocessor. In this process, the preprocessor handles all #include, #define, and conditional compilation directives. For example, header files like kernel.h get copied into each of the source files that they are included in. Second, there is compilation, where each *.c file is compiled separately into an object file (a file that contains machine code, but is not combined into a full program). Third, there is linking, in which the linker combines all the object files into a single executable. During linking, the references between functions and variables located in different files are resolved.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',3,2,'2025-06-27_00-52-41','Each *.c file is supposed to be compiled separately into an object file because including a *.c file effectively includes all its code as if it were copied and pasted into the top of the file it is included in. Including a *.c file is a bad idea because including multiple *.c files can lead to defining the same functions and variables multiple times, resulting in duplicate symbol errors.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',3,2,'2025-06-27_02-00-06','Each *.c file is supposed to be compiled separately into an object file because including a *.c file effectively includes all its code as if it were copied and pasted into the top of the file it is included in. Including a *.c file is a bad idea because including multiple *.c files can lead to defining the same functions and variables multiple times, resulting in duplicate symbol errors.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',3,3,'2025-06-27_00-52-41','From the user’s perspective, setting up a thread involves the following four steps:\nWrite the thread function\nThe user must write a function to define what the thread will do when it runs\nInitialize the thread system\nPrior to using the thread, the kernel’s thread management system must be initialized\nRegister the thread\nThe user provides the function and any needed parameters to a thread creation API which allocates stack space and prepares the thread’s context\nStart the scheduler\nOnce all threads have been created, the scheduler must be started\nOnce started, the scheduler begins executing threads according to priority or a scheduling algorithm\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',3,3,'2025-06-27_02-00-06','From the user’s perspective, setting up a thread involves the following four steps:\nWrite the thread function\nThe user must write a function to define what the thread will do when it runs\nInitialize the thread system\nPrior to using the thread, the kernel’s thread management system must be initialized\nRegister the thread\nThe user provides the function and any needed parameters to a thread creation API which allocates stack space and prepares the thread’s context\nStart the scheduler\nOnce all threads have been created, the scheduler must be started\nOnce started, the scheduler begins executing threads according to priority or a scheduling algorithm\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',3,4,'2025-06-27_00-52-41','The stack pool is a region of memory that is reserved exclusively for thread stacks. Each thread needs its own private stack where it can store local variables, function call return addresses, and register values during context switches. To avoid multiple threads sharing the same stack and overwriting one another, one must allocate a chunk of RAM to be used as a pool of stack space. In its current state, our code can only support one thread by using the global stack pointer. To enable multiple threads, each thread needs its own stack pointer and its own reserved chunk of memory, which we can do with a stack pool.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',3,4,'2025-06-27_02-00-06','The stack pool is a region of memory that is reserved exclusively for thread stacks. Each thread needs its own private stack where it can store local variables, function call return addresses, and register values during context switches. To avoid multiple threads sharing the same stack and overwriting one another, one must allocate a chunk of RAM to be used as a pool of stack space. In its current state, our code can only support one thread by using the global stack pointer. To enable multiple threads, each thread needs its own stack pointer and its own reserved chunk of memory, which we can do with a stack pool.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',3,5,'2025-06-27_00-52-41','User files are code that is written by the user of the operating system (OS) such as the main function and user threads. This code uses the OS to do work such as running applications and/or threads. Kernel files are OS code such as system calls, stack setup, thread management, and interrupt handlers. All this code implements the OS itself through scheduling, memory, and system calls. This matters to the user because the user does not need to understand the complex internal functions like __set_PSP. They only need to be able to call high level functions like osCreateThread. In addition, the kernel runs in privileged mode, whereas the user code runs in unprivileged mode. This means that the kernel prevents accidental or malicious access to hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',3,5,'2025-06-27_02-00-06','User files are code that is written by the user of the operating system (OS) such as the main function and user threads. This code uses the OS to do work such as running applications and/or threads. Kernel files are OS code such as system calls, stack setup, thread management, and interrupt handlers. All this code implements the OS itself through scheduling, memory, and system calls. This matters to the user because the user does not need to understand the complex internal functions like __set_PSP. They only need to be able to call high level functions like osCreateThread. In addition, the kernel runs in privileged mode, whereas the user code runs in unprivileged mode. This means that the kernel prevents accidental or malicious access to hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,1,'2025-07-28_21-14-23','Round-robin scheduling can use a queue instead of an array by rotating threads: the currently running thread is removed from the front of the queue and re-added to the back after yielding. While an array is simple and fast, a queue offers more flexibility for managing dynamic thread lifecycles (e.g., termination, sleeping) and scales better as the OS grows.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',4,1,'2025-07-28_21-26-51','The array-based round-robin scheduler already acts like a queue by cycling through threads in order using an index. When a thread yields, the scheduler moves to the next thread, similar to dequeuing and enqueuing. While a real queue makes it easier to add or remove threads dynamically, it adds complexity and some overhead. Since this lab has a fixed, small number of threads, the simpler and faster array method is usually better. So, although queues fit round-robin scheduling conceptually, their benefits may not justify the extra complexity here.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,1,'2025-07-28_21-40-44','The array-based round-robin scheduler already acts like a queue by cycling through threads in order using an index. When a thread yields, the scheduler moves to the next thread, similar to dequeuing and enqueuing. While a real queue makes it easier to add or remove threads dynamically, it adds complexity and some overhead. Since this lab has a fixed, small number of threads, the simpler and faster array method is usually better. So, although queues fit round-robin scheduling conceptually, their benefits may not justify the extra complexity here.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,2,'2025-07-28_21-14-23','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current thread’s CPU state (register values) to its stack and loading the next thread’s saved state from its stack. More specifically, the PendSV_Handler function saves context by pushing registers R4-R11 onto the current thread’s stack. Next, it stores the stack pointer (the current value of PSP) in the thread’s metadata so it can resume later. Then, it calls osSched() which updates the current thread and sets PSP to the next thread’s saved stack pointer. After, it restores the context by popping R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack, restoring its state. Finally, it returns from interrupt by restoring R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,2,'2025-07-28_21-26-51','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current thread’s CPU state (register values) to its stack and loading the next thread’s saved state from its stack. More specifically, the PendSV_Handler function saves context by pushing registers R4-R11 onto the current thread’s stack. Next, it stores the stack pointer (the current value of PSP) in the thread’s metadata so it can resume later. Then, it calls osSched() which updates the current thread and sets PSP to the next thread’s saved stack pointer. After, it restores the context by popping R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack, restoring its state. Finally, it returns from interrupt by restoring R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR, completing the context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',4,2,'2025-07-28_21-40-44','PendSV_Handler starts by reading the current Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the active thread’s stack. It saves registers R4 to R11 onto this stack to preserve the thread’s state. Then, it updates the current thread’s metadata with the new PSP value to remember where the stack is.\nNext, it calls osSched(), which chooses the next thread to run and updates the current thread index. osSched() sets the PSP to the stack pointer of the selected thread.\nFinally, PendSV_Handler restores the next thread’s registers R4 to R11 by popping them from its stack. When the interrupt ends, the processor restores the rest of the registers and status, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,3,'2025-07-28_21-14-23','Changing the scheduler to always run the highest-priority task can lead to problems like starvation, where lower-priority tasks never get a chance to run. If a high-priority task is always ready, it can completely block other tasks from executing. This is unfair and can cause important background or support tasks to be skipped, potentially breaking parts of the system. A better solution would be to use priority scheduling with techniques like aging or time slices to ensure all tasks eventually get CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,3,'2025-07-28_21-26-51','Changing the scheduler to always run the highest-priority task can lead to problems like starvation, where lower-priority tasks never get a chance to run. If a high-priority task is always ready, it can completely block other tasks from executing. This is unfair and can cause important background or support tasks to be skipped, potentially breaking parts of the system. A better solution would be to use priority scheduling with techniques like aging or time slices to ensure all tasks eventually get CPU time.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',4,3,'2025-07-28_21-40-44','When a scheduler always chooses the highest-priority task to run, that task will keep executing as long as it’s ready, which prevents any lower-priority tasks from running. This causes the system to effectively run only that one task, completely ignoring others. This issue is more serious than simple starvation—it leads to total task monopolization. To avoid this, schedulers typically use techniques like priority aging or time slicing to guarantee all tasks receive CPU time and maintain system fairness.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,4,'2025-07-28_21-14-23','Using C for osSched and SVC_HandlerMain improves code clarity and maintainability, which is ideal for learning and most systems work. Assembly is still essential for critical, low-level operations (like saving/restoring registers), but offloading control logic to C strikes a good balance.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,4,'2025-07-28_21-26-51','Using C for osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code clearer and easier to maintain, which is helpful for most system tasks. However, C can add overhead and offers less precise control compared to assembly, which is crucial for timing-sensitive operations like context switching.\nAssembly is better suited for low-level tasks that require direct hardware control but is harder to write and maintain. Using assembly for critical parts and C for higher-level logic strikes a good balance between efficiency and readability.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,4,'2025-07-28_21-40-44','Using C for osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code clearer and easier to maintain, which is helpful for most system tasks. However, C can add overhead and offers less precise control compared to assembly, which is crucial for timing-sensitive operations like context switching.\nAssembly is better suited for low-level tasks that require direct hardware control but is harder to write and maintain. Using assembly for critical parts and C for higher-level logic strikes a good balance between efficiency and readability.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,5,'2025-07-28_21-14-23','Yes, the OS will still work if only a single thread is created. In this case, when the thread calls osYield(), the context switch mechanism is triggered, but it simply saves and restores the state of the same thread since no others exist. This adds some unnecessary overhead but does not break the system. It demonstrates that the multitasking infrastructure is functioning correctly, even though it isn\'t being fully utilized.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,5,'2025-07-28_21-26-51','Yes, the OS will still work if only a single thread is created. In this case, when the thread calls osYield(), the context switch mechanism is triggered, but it simply saves and restores the state of the same thread since no others exist. This adds some unnecessary overhead but does not break the system. It demonstrates that the multitasking infrastructure is functioning correctly, even though it isn\'t being fully utilized.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',4,5,'2025-07-28_21-40-44','Yes, the OS will still work if only a single thread is created. In this case, when the thread calls osYield(), the context switch mechanism is triggered, but it simply saves and restores the state of the same thread since no others exist. This adds some unnecessary overhead but does not break the system. It demonstrates that the multitasking infrastructure is functioning correctly, even though it isn\'t being fully utilized.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,1,'2025-07-29_01-51-54','To pass an argument to a thread, the user prepares the data—usually in a struct—and passes a pointer to it when creating the thread. The OS places this pointer in register R0 on the thread’s stack, following the ARM calling convention. When the thread starts, the CPU loads the value from R0 into the function, allowing it to receive the argument as if it were called normally. Using a void* makes the function flexible for any input type.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,1,'2025-07-29_02-00-27','To pass an argument to a thread, the user prepares the data—usually in a struct—and passes a pointer to it when creating the thread. The OS places this pointer in register R0 on the thread’s stack, following the ARM calling convention. When the thread starts, the CPU loads the value from R0 into the function, allowing it to receive the argument as if it were called normally. Using a void* makes the function flexible for any input type.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,1,'2025-07-29_02-09-30','To pass an argument to a thread, the user prepares the data—usually in a struct—and passes a pointer to it when creating the thread. The OS places this pointer in register R0 on the thread’s stack, following the ARM calling convention. When the thread starts, the CPU loads the value from R0 into the function, allowing it to receive the argument as if it were called normally. Using a void* makes the function flexible for any input type.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,2,'2025-07-29_01-51-54','You shouldn\'t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because they\'re already handling a context switch. Calling it again can interrupt that process, leading to stack corruption or unstable behavior. These handlers need to run without interruption to safely manage thread state.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',5,2,'2025-07-29_02-00-27','You shouldn’t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because these functions are already handling a context switch triggered by an interrupt or system call. Calling osYield again during this process would start another context switch before the current one finishes, causing nested switches that can corrupt the stack and CPU registers. This interference may lead to hardware faults or unpredictable crashes. Since osYield triggers a software interrupt to request a context switch, calling it while switching threads disrupts the saving and restoring of thread state. Therefore, these handlers must run to completion without interruption to keep the system stable.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,2,'2025-07-29_02-09-30','You shouldn’t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because these functions are already handling a context switch triggered by an interrupt or system call. Calling osYield again during this process would start another context switch before the current one finishes, causing nested switches that can corrupt the stack and CPU registers. This interference may lead to hardware faults or unpredictable crashes. Since osYield triggers a software interrupt to request a context switch, calling it while switching threads disrupts the saving and restoring of thread state. Therefore, these handlers must run to completion without interruption to keep the system stable.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,3,'2025-07-29_01-51-54','Setting the system’s OS clock to 0.1ms would increase the system’s timing resolution, leading to the following benefits and downsides:\nBenefits:\nThe precision with which the OS can respond to events would greatly increase\nThis is useful in high-speed or real-time applications where everything needs to be very precise\nThe responsiveness of the system would increase, leading to a more precise resuming of threads that are waiting on timeouts or delays\nDownsides:\nThe time spent by the CPU handling interrupts would greatly increase, leading to the SysTick interrupt happening about 10 times more often\nSpending more time handling interrupts means spending less time handling the rest of the code, such as running threads, hurting overall performance\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,3,'2025-07-29_02-00-27','Setting the system’s OS clock to 0.1ms would increase the system’s timing resolution, leading to the following benefits and downsides:\nBenefits:\nThe precision with which the OS can respond to events would greatly increase\nThis is useful in high-speed or real-time applications where everything needs to be very precise\nThe responsiveness of the system would increase, leading to a more precise resuming of threads that are waiting on timeouts or delays\nDownsides:\nThe time spent by the CPU handling interrupts would greatly increase, leading to the SysTick interrupt happening about 10 times more often\nSpending more time handling interrupts means spending less time handling the rest of the code, such as running threads, hurting overall performance\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,3,'2025-07-29_02-09-30','Setting the system’s OS clock to 0.1ms would increase the system’s timing resolution, leading to the following benefits and downsides:\nBenefits:\nThe precision with which the OS can respond to events would greatly increase\nThis is useful in high-speed or real-time applications where everything needs to be very precise\nThe responsiveness of the system would increase, leading to a more precise resuming of threads that are waiting on timeouts or delays\nDownsides:\nThe time spent by the CPU handling interrupts would greatly increase, leading to the SysTick interrupt happening about 10 times more often\nSpending more time handling interrupts means spending less time handling the rest of the code, such as running threads, hurting overall performance\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,4,'2025-07-29_01-51-54','User Perspective:\nCooperative Multitasking:\nUser’s thread must explicitly yield control (via osYield) to allow other threads to run\nThis can lead to a thread hogging the CPU indefinitely\nPre-emptive Multitasking:\nAfter a fixed amount of time (determined by timeslice), the OS automatically interrupts and switches threads, regardless of whether the thread yields\nThe user’s thread can run normally without  worrying about giving up the CPU\nOS Perspective:\nCooperative Multitasking:\nThe OS switches context when a thread calls osYield\nDoesn’t need extra timer interrupts\nContext switches happen on explicit user requests\nPre-emptive Multitasking:\nOS uses a hardware timer (such as SysTick) to generate periodic interrupts, decrementing each thread’s runtime\nWhen a thread’s timeslice expires, the OS triggers a context switch automatically using PendSV, without any action from the thread\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,4,'2025-07-29_02-00-27','User Perspective:\nCooperative Multitasking:\nUser’s thread must explicitly yield control (via osYield) to allow other threads to run\nThis can lead to a thread hogging the CPU indefinitely\nPre-emptive Multitasking:\nAfter a fixed amount of time (determined by timeslice), the OS automatically interrupts and switches threads, regardless of whether the thread yields\nThe user’s thread can run normally without  worrying about giving up the CPU\nOS Perspective:\nCooperative Multitasking:\nThe OS switches context when a thread calls osYield\nDoesn’t need extra timer interrupts\nContext switches happen on explicit user requests\nPre-emptive Multitasking:\nOS uses a hardware timer (such as SysTick) to generate periodic interrupts, decrementing each thread’s runtime\nWhen a thread’s timeslice expires, the OS triggers a context switch automatically using PendSV, without any action from the thread\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,4,'2025-07-29_02-09-30','User Perspective:\nCooperative Multitasking:\nUser’s thread must explicitly yield control (via osYield) to allow other threads to run\nThis can lead to a thread hogging the CPU indefinitely\nPre-emptive Multitasking:\nAfter a fixed amount of time (determined by timeslice), the OS automatically interrupts and switches threads, regardless of whether the thread yields\nThe user’s thread can run normally without  worrying about giving up the CPU\nOS Perspective:\nCooperative Multitasking:\nThe OS switches context when a thread calls osYield\nDoesn’t need extra timer interrupts\nContext switches happen on explicit user requests\nPre-emptive Multitasking:\nOS uses a hardware timer (such as SysTick) to generate periodic interrupts, decrementing each thread’s runtime\nWhen a thread’s timeslice expires, the OS triggers a context switch automatically using PendSV, without any action from the thread\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,5,'2025-07-29_01-51-54','Yielding lets a thread voluntarily give up the CPU before its time slice ends, while pre-emptive switching forces a context switch when the thread’s time slice runs out. The OS handles both yield calls and timer interrupts as signals to switch threads. This allows cooperative yielding and pre-emptive multitasking to work together seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('jflepida',5,5,'2025-07-29_02-00-27','Yielding lets a thread voluntarily give up the CPU before its time slice ends, while pre-emptive switching forces a context switch when the thread’s time slice runs out. The OS handles both yield calls and timer interrupts as signals to switch threads. This allows cooperative yielding and pre-emptive multitasking to work together seamlessly.\n',0,'s25'),('jflepida',5,5,'2025-07-29_02-09-30','In the final exercise, yielding and time-based pre-emptive switching work together because both use the same context-switching mechanism. When a thread calls osYield, it triggers a software interrupt that tells the OS to switch tasks. When a thread’s time slice runs out, the SysTick timer interrupt signals the OS to switch tasks. In both cases, the OS uses the PendSV interrupt handler to perform the actual context switch, so voluntary and pre-emptive switches share the same code and operate smoothly together.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',1,1,'2025-06-26_19-30-34','HAL, short for Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software layer that acts as an intermediary between high-level software and the underlying hardware. If HAL is not used, it would raise a challenge in software development since switching platforms or upgrading hardware will become more error prone and time consuming since developers would need to rewrite low-level code for every device. The abstraction provided by the HAL can improve the code reusability by allowing the same high-level logic to work across multiple systems, simplifying debugging and enabling faster development cycles.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',1,1,'2025-06-26_21-40-52','HAL, short for Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a software layer that acts as an intermediary between high-level software and the underlying hardware. If HAL is not used, it would raise a challenge in software development since switching platforms or upgrading hardware will become more error prone and time consuming since developers would need to rewrite low-level code for every device. The abstraction provided by the HAL can improve the code reusability by allowing the same high-level logic to work across multiple systems, simplifying debugging and enabling faster development cycles.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',1,2,'2025-06-26_19-30-34','A project might require a custom implementation of __io_putchar because different hardware platforms have different methods for handling output, such as sending data over UART, USB, or writing to a display. Since standard C functions like printf need a low-level way to output characters, __io_putchar acts as the link between the high-level function and the hardware-specific output. The output destination can vary—for example, one board might use a serial port while another uses a debug console or an LCD. This variation is important to consider because without customizing __io_putchar to match the hardware, output functions won’t work correctly. Customizing it ensures that data is sent to the right place, making debugging and communication possible.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',1,2,'2025-06-26_21-40-52','A project might require a custom implementation of __io_putchar because different hardware platforms handle output differently—such as through UART, USB, or an LCD. The standard C function printf formats strings at a high level, but it does not know how to actually send characters to the hardware. Instead, it relies on a low-level function like __io_putchar to transmit each character. When printf is called, it breaks the string into individual characters and calls __io_putchar repeatedly to output them one by one. If __io_putchar is not defined correctly for the target hardware, printf will not produce any visible output. Therefore, implementing __io_putchar ensures that printf can route its output to the appropriate hardware interface, making debugging and communication through serial or other channels possible.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',1,3,'2025-06-26_19-30-34','We used the debugger in this lab because printf is limited in what it can show, especially when working close to the hardware like we are with the Cortex M4. Examining register values is important because it lets us see exactly how the processor is interacting with peripherals and executing instructions, which can’t be done through printf. A debugger allows us to pause the program, inspect CPU and peripheral register values, step through code line by line, and watch how values change in real time. In contrast, printf only shows what we explicitly print—it\'s slower, can affect timing, and often isn’t available early in program execution or in interrupt routines. That’s why the debugger is a more powerful and precise tool for understanding low-level behavior in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',1,3,'2025-06-26_21-40-52','We used the debugger in this lab because printf is limited in what it can show, especially when working close to the hardware like we are with the Cortex M4. Examining register values is important because it lets us see exactly how the processor is interacting with peripherals and executing instructions, which can’t be done through printf. A debugger allows us to pause the program, inspect CPU and peripheral register values, step through code line by line, and watch how values change in real time. In contrast, printf only shows what we explicitly print—it\'s slower, can affect timing, and often isn’t available early in program execution or in interrupt routines. That’s why the debugger is a more powerful and precise tool for understanding low-level behavior in embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',1,4,'2025-06-26_19-30-34','The stack allocation method we used involves reserving a separate region of memory for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by calculating its value from the initial Main Stack Pointer (MSP). Once we have the value of MSP_init_val, we set the PSP using the relationship PSP_val = MSP_init_val - offset, where the offset (e.g., 0x400 bytes) defines how much space is allocated for the main stack. This offset is chosen based on how much stack space the main thread is expected to need. Since stacks grow downward in memory, subtracting the offset ensures that the PSP starts below the MSP, avoiding overlap. If the PSP were not properly offset from the MSP, the two stacks could collide, leading to memory corruption, unexpected behavior, or system crashes. Knowing the initial location of the MSP is essential because it serves as the reference point for allocating safe, non-overlapping stack space for the PSP.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',1,4,'2025-06-26_21-40-52','The stack allocation method we used involves reserving a separate region of memory for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by calculating its value from the initial Main Stack Pointer (MSP). Once we have the value of MSP_init_val, we set the PSP using the relationship PSP_val = MSP_init_val - offset, where the offset (e.g., 0x400 bytes) defines how much space is allocated for the main stack. This offset is chosen based on how much stack space the main thread is expected to need. Since stacks grow downward in memory, subtracting the offset ensures that the PSP starts below the MSP, avoiding overlap. If the PSP were not properly offset from the MSP, the two stacks could collide, leading to memory corruption, unexpected behavior, or system crashes. Knowing the initial location of the MSP is essential because it serves as the reference point for allocating safe, non-overlapping stack space for the PSP.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',1,5,'2025-06-26_19-30-34','To allocate multiple stacks at compile time, we define a static memory region for thread stacks and calculate each stack’s starting address using a formula like PSP_val[n] = THREAD_STACK_BASE + ((n + 1) * STACK_SIZE). This method ensures each stack is properly aligned (e.g., 8-byte aligned for Cortex M4) and avoids overlap with the main stack (MSP), which is used for system-level tasks. Using compile-time addresses is important in embedded systems like the Cortex M4 because memory layout must be predictable and efficient. Clear separation between the MSP and thread stacks helps maintain system stability and supports multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',1,5,'2025-06-26_21-40-52','To allocate multiple stacks at compile time, we define a static memory region for thread stacks and calculate each stack’s starting address using a formula like PSP_val[n] = THREAD_STACK_BASE + ((n + 1) * STACK_SIZE). This method ensures each stack is properly aligned (e.g., 8-byte aligned for Cortex M4) and avoids overlap with the main stack (MSP), which is used for system-level tasks. Using compile-time addresses is important in embedded systems like the Cortex M4 because memory layout must be predictable and efficient. Clear separation between the MSP and thread stacks helps maintain system stability and supports multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,1,'2025-06-26_22-05-38','When I changed the Program Counter (PC) to hold the address of the print_continuously function, I redirected the processor’s execution flow directly to that function. The PC register determines which instruction the processor executes next, so by manually loading it with the address of print_continuously, the processor jumped straight to that code. As a result, the function began running immediately, entering its infinite loop that repeatedly prints \"Hello, PC!\". This bypassed the usual method of calling functions through the call stack and showed how directly manipulating the PC gives low-level control over execution. It’s similar to how an operating system performs context switching by updating the PC and other registers to jump between tasks. This lab helped demonstrate how changing the PC allows us to begin executing a function without a traditional call, which is fundamental to understanding how OS-level scheduling and multitasking work.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,1,'2025-06-27_00-10-16','When I changed the Program Counter (PC) to hold the address of the print_continuously function, I redirected the processor’s execution flow directly to that function. The PC register determines which instruction the processor executes next, so by manually loading it with the address of print_continuously, the processor jumped straight to that code. As a result, the function began running immediately, entering its infinite loop that repeatedly prints \"Hello, PC!\". This bypassed the usual method of calling functions through the call stack and showed how directly manipulating the PC gives low-level control over execution. It’s similar to how an operating system performs context switching by updating the PC and other registers to jump between tasks. This lab helped demonstrate how changing the PC allows us to begin executing a function without a traditional call, which is fundamental to understanding how OS-level scheduling and multitasking work.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,1,'2025-06-27_17-13-23','When I changed the Program Counter (PC) to hold the address of the print_continuously function, I redirected the processor’s execution flow directly to that function. The PC register determines which instruction the processor executes next, so by manually loading it with the address of print_continuously, the processor jumped straight to that code. As a result, the function began running immediately, entering its infinite loop that repeatedly prints \"Hello, PC!\". This bypassed the usual method of calling functions through the call stack and showed how directly manipulating the PC gives low-level control over execution. It’s similar to how an operating system performs context switching by updating the PC and other registers to jump between tasks. This lab helped demonstrate how changing the PC allows us to begin executing a function without a traditional call, which is fundamental to understanding how OS-level scheduling and multitasking work.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,2,'2025-06-26_22-05-38','A system call is a mechanism that allows a user-mode program to request a service from the operating system\'s kernel, which runs in a more privileged mode. It acts as a secure interface for programs to access protected resources or perform tasks they aren’t allowed to handle directly—such as interacting with hardware, managing files, or creating processes. To make a system call, a program executes a special instruction (like SVC on ARM processors), which triggers a switch from user mode to kernel mode. The operating system then performs the requested operation with the necessary privileges and returns control to the user program, usually with a result or status code. In our lab, we used system calls to invoke kernel-level functions from our application code. For instance, by issuing SVC instructions with different values, we were able to trigger specific actions like printing messages, demonstrating how system calls provide controlled access to privileged operations while maintaining system integrity.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,2,'2025-06-27_00-10-16','A system call is a mechanism that allows a user-mode program to request a service from the operating system\'s kernel, which runs in a more privileged mode. It acts as a secure interface for programs to access protected resources or perform tasks they aren’t allowed to handle directly—such as interacting with hardware, managing files, or creating processes. To make a system call, a program executes a special instruction (like SVC on ARM processors), which triggers a switch from user mode to kernel mode. The operating system then performs the requested operation with the necessary privileges and returns control to the user program, usually with a result or status code. In our lab, we used system calls to invoke kernel-level functions from our application code. For instance, by issuing SVC instructions with different values, we were able to trigger specific actions like printing messages, demonstrating how system calls provide controlled access to privileged operations while maintaining system integrity.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,2,'2025-06-27_17-13-23','A system call is a mechanism that allows a user-mode program to request a service from the operating system\'s kernel, which runs in a more privileged mode. It acts as a secure interface for programs to access protected resources or perform tasks they aren’t allowed to handle directly—such as interacting with hardware, managing files, or creating processes. To make a system call, a program executes a special instruction (like SVC on ARM processors), which triggers a switch from user mode to kernel mode. The operating system then performs the requested operation with the necessary privileges and returns control to the user program, usually with a result or status code. In our lab, we used system calls to invoke kernel-level functions from our application code. For instance, by issuing SVC instructions with different values, we were able to trigger specific actions like printing messages, demonstrating how system calls provide controlled access to privileged operations while maintaining system integrity.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,3,'2025-06-26_22-05-38','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing the memory and register context that a thread needs to execute independently of other threads. In our lab, this involved allocating a block of memory for the thread and assigning its address to a new stack pointer. We then set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to this new value so that the thread would use its own private stack space. Next, we manually pushed specific values onto the stack in a precise order: starting with the xPSR (set to a default value), then the PC (set to the address of the thread’s function like print_continuously), followed by the LR and general-purpose registers R12 through R0. This created a fake context that mimics the state the processor would expect if the thread had just been interrupted and was now resuming. When the processor pops these values off the stack during a context switch, it loads them into the appropriate registers, effectively starting the thread. This process is crucial because storing register values ensures the thread’s execution context can be fully restored. Each thread must have its own set of registers to avoid conflicts and maintain proper execution. Setting the PC and xPSR ensures the thread begins at the right function with the correct execution state. Overall, stack setup enables multitasking by allowing threads to be paused and resumed seamlessly, giving the illusion of parallel execution.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,3,'2025-06-27_00-10-16','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing the memory and register context that a thread needs to execute independently of other threads. In our lab, this involved allocating a block of memory for the thread and assigning its address to a new stack pointer. We then set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to this new value so that the thread would use its own private stack space. Next, we manually pushed specific values onto the stack in a precise order: starting with the xPSR (set to a default value), then the PC (set to the address of the thread’s function like print_continuously), followed by the LR and general-purpose registers R12 through R0. This created a fake context that mimics the state the processor would expect if the thread had just been interrupted and was now resuming. When the processor pops these values off the stack during a context switch, it loads them into the appropriate registers, effectively starting the thread. This process is crucial because storing register values ensures the thread’s execution context can be fully restored. Each thread must have its own set of registers to avoid conflicts and maintain proper execution. Setting the PC and xPSR ensures the thread begins at the right function with the correct execution state. Overall, stack setup enables multitasking by allowing threads to be paused and resumed seamlessly, giving the illusion of parallel execution.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,3,'2025-06-27_17-13-23','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing the memory and register context that a thread needs to execute independently of other threads. In our lab, this involved allocating a block of memory for the thread and assigning its address to a new stack pointer. We then set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to this new value so that the thread would use its own private stack space. Next, we manually pushed specific values onto the stack in a precise order: starting with the xPSR (set to a default value), then the PC (set to the address of the thread’s function like print_continuously), followed by the LR and general-purpose registers R12 through R0. This created a fake context that mimics the state the processor would expect if the thread had just been interrupted and was now resuming. When the processor pops these values off the stack during a context switch, it loads them into the appropriate registers, effectively starting the thread. This process is crucial because storing register values ensures the thread’s execution context can be fully restored. Each thread must have its own set of registers to avoid conflicts and maintain proper execution. Setting the PC and xPSR ensures the thread begins at the right function with the correct execution state. Overall, stack setup enables multitasking by allowing threads to be paused and resumed seamlessly, giving the illusion of parallel execution.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,4,'2025-06-26_22-05-38','Setting the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) using the __set_PSP function is essential because it defines which memory region the processor will use as the stack during thread execution. If we skip this step, the PSP would either remain uninitialized or point to an incorrect location in memory. When the processor attempts to restore the thread’s context—by popping values such as the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and program status register (xPSR) from the stack—it would read from an invalid or undefined memory region. This leads to critical errors like accessing illegal memory addresses or executing unintended instructions, ultimately causing a hard fault and crashing the system. In our lab, the SVC handler relies on the PSP to locate the stack frame we manually set up for the thread. Without assigning the PSP to the correct address of that thread’s stack, the processor has no way of retrieving the saved context, resulting in undefined behavior. Therefore, setting the PSP correctly ensures that the processor has a valid stack to work with, allowing the thread to start safely and function as intended.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,4,'2025-06-27_00-10-16','Setting the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) using the __set_PSP function is essential because it defines which memory region the processor will use as the stack during thread execution. If we skip this step, the PSP would either remain uninitialized or point to an incorrect location in memory. When the processor attempts to restore the thread’s context—by popping values such as the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and program status register (xPSR) from the stack—it would read from an invalid or undefined memory region. This leads to critical errors like accessing illegal memory addresses or executing unintended instructions, ultimately causing a hard fault and crashing the system. In our lab, the SVC handler relies on the PSP to locate the stack frame we manually set up for the thread. Without assigning the PSP to the correct address of that thread’s stack, the processor has no way of retrieving the saved context, resulting in undefined behavior. Therefore, setting the PSP correctly ensures that the processor has a valid stack to work with, allowing the thread to start safely and function as intended.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,4,'2025-06-27_17-13-23','Setting the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) using the __set_PSP function is essential because it defines which memory region the processor will use as the stack during thread execution. If we skip this step, the PSP would either remain uninitialized or point to an incorrect location in memory. When the processor attempts to restore the thread’s context—by popping values such as the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and program status register (xPSR) from the stack—it would read from an invalid or undefined memory region. This leads to critical errors like accessing illegal memory addresses or executing unintended instructions, ultimately causing a hard fault and crashing the system. In our lab, the SVC handler relies on the PSP to locate the stack frame we manually set up for the thread. Without assigning the PSP to the correct address of that thread’s stack, the processor has no way of retrieving the saved context, resulting in undefined behavior. Therefore, setting the PSP correctly ensures that the processor has a valid stack to work with, allowing the thread to start safely and function as intended.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,5,'2025-06-26_22-05-38','If the thread function did not loop continuously and instead printed something once and returned, the system would likely encounter serious issues. Since we manually configured the thread’s stack, the Link Register (LR)—which determines where the function returns to—might not contain a valid return address. As a result, when the function finishes and attempts to return, the processor could jump to an invalid or undefined memory location, leading to unpredictable behavior or a system crash. Additionally, without a proper mechanism to terminate the thread, the system wouldn’t recognize that the thread has completed, potentially causing problems for the scheduler or leading to resource leaks. Because our operating system is simple and lacks a cleanup process like a thread exit function, returning from the thread leaves the system in an unstable state. This situation underscores the importance of handling thread termination explicitly, especially in systems programming where every step of execution must be carefully managed.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,5,'2025-06-27_00-10-16','If the thread function did not loop continuously and instead printed something once and returned, the system would likely encounter serious issues, including a hardfault. Since we manually set up the thread’s stack, the Link Register (LR)—which holds the return address—may not contain a valid value. When the function tries to return, the processor uses this invalid address, potentially jumping to undefined memory. This can trigger a hardfault, a type of exception that occurs when the processor encounters an error such as accessing invalid memory or executing an illegal instruction. Without a valid return path or a mechanism to cleanly terminate the thread, the system cannot properly handle the end of the thread’s execution. In our simple operating system, where no thread exit routine exists, this can lead to instability, resource leaks, or unpredictable behavior. This example highlights the importance of both correctly setting up the execution context and using precise terminology, like “hardfault,” to describe such critical failures in low-level programming.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',2,5,'2025-06-27_17-13-23','If the thread function did not loop continuously and instead printed something once and returned, the system would likely encounter serious issues, including a hardfault. Since we manually set up the thread’s stack, the Link Register (LR)—which holds the return address—may not contain a valid value. When the function tries to return, the processor uses this invalid address, potentially jumping to undefined memory. This can trigger a hardfault, a type of exception that occurs when the processor encounters an error such as accessing invalid memory or executing an illegal instruction. Without a valid return path or a mechanism to cleanly terminate the thread, the system cannot properly handle the end of the thread’s execution. In our simple operating system, where no thread exit routine exists, this can lead to instability, resource leaks, or unpredictable behavior. This example highlights the importance of both correctly setting up the execution context and using precise terminology, like “hardfault,” to describe such critical failures in low-level programming.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,1,'2025-06-27_17-03-03','Each .c file in a project is first compiled individually into an object file (.o), where the compiler translates the source code into machine code while preserving symbols for functions and variables. During the linking phase, the linker combines all object files—and any used libraries—into a single executable, resolving symbol references between files. If a symbol, like a function or variable, is referenced but not defined, the linker throws an error. In larger projects or embedded systems like those for the Cortex M4, managing this process is crucial because memory and performance are limited, and specific startup files, linker scripts, and interrupt vector tables must be included correctly for the system to function.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,1,'2025-06-27_18-08-05','The process of turning multiple .c files into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, during preprocessing, directives like #include, #define, and #ifdef are handled—this step expands macros and includes the contents of header files, producing a “cleaned” version of the code ready for compilation. Next, each .c file is compiled individually into an object file (.o), where the compiler translates the source code into machine code while preserving symbol information. Finally, the linker combines all object files—and any used libraries—into a single executable, resolving symbol references between files. If a function or variable is used but not defined, the linker throws an error. This process is especially important in larger projects or on embedded systems like the Cortex M4, where memory is limited and setup files like the interrupt vector table and startup code must be carefully included.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,1,'2025-06-27_18-52-31','The process of turning multiple .c files into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, during preprocessing, directives like #include, #define, and #ifdef are handled—this step expands macros and includes the contents of header files, producing a “cleaned” version of the code ready for compilation. Next, each .c file is compiled individually into an object file (.o), where the compiler translates the source code into machine code while preserving symbol information. Finally, the linker combines all object files—and any used libraries—into a single executable, resolving symbol references between files. If a function or variable is used but not defined, the linker throws an error. This process is especially important in larger projects or on embedded systems like the Cortex M4, where memory is limited and setup files like the interrupt vector table and startup code must be carefully included.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,1,'2025-06-27_22-45-18','The process of turning multiple .c files into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, during preprocessing, directives like #include, #define, and #ifdef are handled—this step expands macros and includes the contents of header files, producing a “cleaned” version of the code ready for compilation. Next, each .c file is compiled individually into an object file (.o), where the compiler translates the source code into machine code while preserving symbol information. Finally, the linker combines all object files—and any used libraries—into a single executable, resolving symbol references between files. If a function or variable is used but not defined, the linker throws an error. This process is especially important in larger projects or on embedded systems like the Cortex M4, where memory is limited and setup files like the interrupt vector table and startup code must be carefully included.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,2,'2025-06-27_17-03-03','Including a .c file with #include copies its contents directly into another file during preprocessing, which can lead to duplicate definitions and multiple symbols with external linkage—causing linker errors. Unlike header files, which declare interfaces with extern functions or variables, .c files define them. This breaks modularity and can hide bugs that don’t appear until link time or runtime. For example, including kernel.c might not fail initially but could lead to problems if it defines a function already present elsewhere. Proper design uses header files for declarations and compiles .c files separately to maintain clean, reliable symbol resolution during linking.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,2,'2025-06-27_18-08-05','Including a .c file with #include copies its contents directly into another file during preprocessing, which can lead to duplicate definitions and multiple symbols with external linkage—causing linker errors. Unlike header files, which declare interfaces with extern functions or variables, .c files define them. This breaks modularity and can hide bugs that don’t appear until link time or runtime. For example, including kernel.c might not fail initially but could lead to problems if it defines a function already present elsewhere. Proper design uses header files for declarations and compiles .c files separately to maintain clean, reliable symbol resolution during linking.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,2,'2025-06-27_18-52-31','Including a .c file with #include copies its contents directly into another file during preprocessing, which can lead to duplicate definitions and multiple symbols with external linkage—causing linker errors. Unlike header files, which declare interfaces with extern functions or variables, .c files define them. This breaks modularity and can hide bugs that don’t appear until link time or runtime. For example, including kernel.c might not fail initially but could lead to problems if it defines a function already present elsewhere. Proper design uses header files for declarations and compiles .c files separately to maintain clean, reliable symbol resolution during linking.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,2,'2025-06-27_22-45-18','Including a .c file with #include copies its contents directly into another file during preprocessing, which can lead to duplicate definitions and multiple symbols with external linkage—causing linker errors. Unlike header files, which declare interfaces with extern functions or variables, .c files define them. This breaks modularity and can hide bugs that don’t appear until link time or runtime. For example, including kernel.c might not fail initially but could lead to problems if it defines a function already present elsewhere. Proper design uses header files for declarations and compiles .c files separately to maintain clean, reliable symbol resolution during linking.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,3,'2025-06-27_17-03-03','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective:\nDefine the function that the thread will run.\nAllocate a stack for the thread—this may come from a predefined stack pool.\nRegister the thread with the kernel by providing the function pointer and stack pointer.\nOptionally set priority or scheduling parameters.\nStart the OS or call the scheduler to begin executing threads.\nThis setup allows the kernel to manage context switching and ensure the thread runs when scheduled.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,3,'2025-06-27_18-08-05','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective:\nInitialize the thread system—this sets up internal data structures the kernel needs to manage threads.\nDefine the function that the thread will run.\nAllocate a stack for the thread—this may come from a predefined stack pool.\nRegister the thread with the kernel by providing the function pointer and stack pointer.\nOptionally set priority or scheduling parameters.\nStart the OS or call the scheduler to begin executing threads.\nInitializing the thread system is critical because, without it, the kernel may not have the memory or structures needed to track and manage threads properly.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,3,'2025-06-27_18-52-31','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective:\nCall osKernelInit() to initialize the thread system—this sets up the internal data structures the kernel needs to manage threads.\nDefine the function that the thread will run.\nCall osCreateThread() and pass the thread function pointer as an argument—this allocates a stack (from a predefined stack pool) and registers the thread with the kernel.\nOptionally, use other configuration function calls if priority or scheduling settings are supported.\nFinally, call osKernelStart() to begin executing threads.\nEach step relies on clearly defined kernel API function calls to interact with the OS. Explicitly calling these functions ensures proper initialization, resource allocation, and thread startup, which is critical for system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,3,'2025-06-27_22-45-18','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective:\nCall osKernelInit() to initialize the thread system—this sets up the internal data structures the kernel needs to manage threads.\nDefine the function that the thread will run.\nCall osCreateThread() and pass the thread function pointer as an argument—this allocates a stack (from a predefined stack pool) and registers the thread with the kernel.\nOptionally, use other configuration function calls if priority or scheduling settings are supported.\nFinally, call osKernelStart() to begin executing threads.\nEach step relies on clearly defined kernel API function calls to interact with the OS. Explicitly calling these functions ensures proper initialization, resource allocation, and thread startup, which is critical for system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,4,'2025-06-27_17-03-03','The stack pool is a statically allocated region of memory divided into fixed-size chunks, with each chunk serving as an isolated stack for a thread. In future labs, this pool allows us to support multiple threads by giving each one a dedicated, non-overlapping stack. This prevents stack collisions and enables safe context switching. Since stacks grow downward, we calculate each stack’s starting point from the top of its chunk using an offset formula. Efficient use of the stack pool improves system safety, and reallocating it can be helpful if thread count or stack sizes change in more advanced implementations.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,4,'2025-06-27_18-08-05','The stack pool is a statically allocated region of memory divided into fixed-size chunks, with each chunk serving as an isolated stack for a thread. In future labs, this pool allows us to support multiple threads by giving each one a dedicated, non-overlapping stack. This prevents stack collisions and enables safe context switching. Since stacks grow downward, we calculate each stack’s starting point from the top of its chunk using an offset formula. Efficient use of the stack pool improves system safety, and reallocating it can be helpful if thread count or stack sizes change in more advanced implementations.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,4,'2025-06-27_18-52-31','The stack pool is a statically allocated region of memory divided into fixed-size chunks, with each chunk serving as an isolated stack for a thread. In future labs, this pool allows us to support multiple threads by giving each one a dedicated, non-overlapping stack. This prevents stack collisions and enables safe context switching. Since stacks grow downward, we calculate each stack’s starting point from the top of its chunk using an offset formula. Efficient use of the stack pool improves system safety, and reallocating it can be helpful if thread count or stack sizes change in more advanced implementations.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,4,'2025-06-27_22-45-18','The stack pool is a statically allocated region of memory divided into fixed-size chunks, with each chunk serving as an isolated stack for a thread. In future labs, this pool allows us to support multiple threads by giving each one a dedicated, non-overlapping stack. This prevents stack collisions and enables safe context switching. Since stacks grow downward, we calculate each stack’s starting point from the top of its chunk using an offset formula. Efficient use of the stack pool improves system safety, and reallocating it can be helpful if thread count or stack sizes change in more advanced implementations.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,5,'2025-06-27_17-03-03','The \"user\" code (e.g., main.c) contains application-level logic that interacts with the OS through specific API functions, like thread creation or delays. The \"kernel\" code handles low-level operations such as context switching, stack setup, and responding to SVC calls. Separating these ensures the user code stays clean and abstracted from hardware details, while the kernel maintains system stability and security. This distinction is especially important when debugging or expanding the OS, as it keeps responsibilities clear and allows the user to write code without accidentally disrupting core system behavior. It also helps maintain a safe boundary for privileged operations.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,5,'2025-06-27_18-08-05','The \"user\" code (e.g., main.c) contains application-level logic that interacts with the OS through specific API functions, like thread creation or delays. The \"kernel\" code handles low-level operations such as context switching, stack setup, and responding to SVC calls. Separating these ensures the user code stays clean and abstracted from hardware details, while the kernel maintains system stability and security. This distinction is especially important when debugging or expanding the OS, as it keeps responsibilities clear and allows the user to write code without accidentally disrupting core system behavior. It also helps maintain a safe boundary for privileged operations.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,5,'2025-06-27_18-52-31','The \"user\" code (e.g., main.c) contains application-level logic that interacts with the OS through specific API functions, like thread creation or delays. The \"kernel\" code handles low-level operations such as context switching, stack setup, and responding to SVC calls. Separating these ensures the user code stays clean and abstracted from hardware details, while the kernel maintains system stability and security. This distinction is especially important when debugging or expanding the OS, as it keeps responsibilities clear and allows the user to write code without accidentally disrupting core system behavior. It also helps maintain a safe boundary for privileged operations.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',3,5,'2025-06-27_22-45-18','The \"user\" code (e.g., main.c) contains application-level logic that interacts with the OS through specific API functions, like thread creation or delays. The \"kernel\" code handles low-level operations such as context switching, stack setup, and responding to SVC calls. Separating these ensures the user code stays clean and abstracted from hardware details, while the kernel maintains system stability and security. This distinction is especially important when debugging or expanding the OS, as it keeps responsibilities clear and allows the user to write code without accidentally disrupting core system behavior. It also helps maintain a safe boundary for privileged operations.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,1,'2025-07-21_00-35-58','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling works by enqueuing all TCBs (Thread Control Blocks) in the order they should run. The scheduler dequeues the thread at the front (i.e., the current thread), runs it for one time slice, and then re-enqueues it at the back of the queue. This cycle repeats, giving each thread an equal opportunity to run.\nThe main benefit of using a queue is that it naturally models the round-robin behavior — each thread gets processed in a FIFO (First In, First Out) order. This makes the scheduling logic simpler and more intuitive. In contrast, using an array often requires additional index tracking and shifting logic, which can be more error-prone and harder to maintain.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,1,'2025-07-21_11-22-29','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling works by enqueuing all TCBs (Thread Control Blocks) in the order they should run. The scheduler dequeues the thread at the front (i.e., the current thread), runs it for one time slice, and then re-enqueues it at the back of the queue. This cycle repeats, giving each thread an equal opportunity to run.\nThis approach closely resembles what the current scheduler already does, even if it uses an array or a circular buffer — it effectively cycles through threads in a round-robin fashion. However, using an explicit queue data structure makes the logic more intuitive and aligns well with FIFO (First In, First Out) behavior.\nOne trade-off to consider is the potential overhead. A queue may introduce additional dynamic memory or pointer management, especially in systems without native support for linked data structures. In contrast, arrays offer faster indexing and simpler memory access patterns but may require more manual tracking (like maintaining a current index or wrapping around). Overall, while a queue simplifies the round-robin logic conceptually, its practical benefit depends on implementation details and system constraints.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,1,'2025-07-21_13-01-15','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling works by enqueuing all TCBs (Thread Control Blocks) in the order they should run. The scheduler dequeues the thread at the front (i.e., the current thread), runs it for one time slice, and then re-enqueues it at the back of the queue. This cycle repeats, giving each thread an equal opportunity to run.\nThis approach closely resembles what the current scheduler already does, even if it uses an array or a circular buffer — it effectively cycles through threads in a round-robin fashion. However, using an explicit queue data structure makes the logic more intuitive and aligns well with FIFO (First In, First Out) behavior.\nOne trade-off to consider is the potential overhead. A queue may introduce additional dynamic memory or pointer management, especially in systems without native support for linked data structures. In contrast, arrays offer faster indexing and simpler memory access patterns but may require more manual tracking (like maintaining a current index or wrapping around). Overall, while a queue simplifies the round-robin logic conceptually, its practical benefit depends on implementation details and system constraints.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,2,'2025-07-21_00-35-58','The PendSV_Handler is a special interrupt handler used for performing context switches in ARM Cortex-M processors. When it runs, it saves the current thread\'s context (CPU register values) onto its stack, updates the stack pointer in the TCB, selects the next thread to run (via the scheduler), loads that thread’s context from its stack, and restores the processor registers so that execution resumes in the new thread. This allows the OS to pause one thread and resume another as if it had never stopped.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,2,'2025-07-21_11-22-29','The PendSV_Handler is a special interrupt handler used to perform context switches on ARM Cortex-M processors. When it is triggered, it first saves the current thread’s context by pushing registers R4–R11 onto the stack. These are the callee-saved registers that are not automatically saved by the hardware during an exception, so the OS must handle them explicitly.\nAfter saving the context, the OS stores the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) value into the thread’s TCB, allowing it to later resume execution from the same point. The function osSched is then called to select the next thread to run based on the scheduling policy.\nOnce a new thread is selected, the PSP is loaded from that thread’s TCB, and the values of registers R4–R11 are popped from its stack to restore its context. Control then returns to the new thread, which resumes execution as though it had never been paused. This detailed save-and-restore mechanism enables seamless switching between threads.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,2,'2025-07-21_13-01-15','The PendSV_Handler is a special interrupt handler used to perform context switches on ARM Cortex-M processors. When triggered, it begins by reading the current Process Stack Pointer (PSP) into a temporary register. This step is critical because it tells the OS where the current thread’s stack is located and ensures that the context is saved in the correct place.\nNext, the OS uses that PSP value to push registers R4–R11 onto the current thread’s stack. These are callee-saved registers that the hardware does not automatically save during exceptions, so the OS must explicitly store them to preserve the thread’s execution state.\nAfter the registers are saved, the updated PSP is stored in the thread’s TCB, allowing the OS to later restore the thread’s context from that same location. The scheduler function, osSched, is then called to determine the next thread to run.\nOnce the next thread is selected, its PSP is loaded from its TCB, and the OS pops registers R4–R11 from that thread’s stack to restore its saved context. Finally, the PSP is updated, and control is returned to the new thread, which resumes execution as if it had never been interrupted.\nThis precise ordering ensures that each thread\'s state is saved and restored correctly, enabling reliable and seamless context switching.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,3,'2025-07-21_00-35-58','Always choosing the highest priority task can cause lower priority tasks to starve — they may never get a chance to run if a high-priority task is always ready. This violates fairness and can prevent essential background tasks (like I/O handling or logging) from executing. It also makes it hard to debug timing issues and creates unpredictability in the system\'s behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,3,'2025-07-21_11-22-29','If the scheduler is modified to always select the task with the highest priority, then only the highest-priority task would ever run, as long as it remains ready. Since the scheduler always picks the highest-priority option and does not rotate or share CPU time, all lower-priority tasks would be completely ignored, regardless of whether they’re ready to run.\nThis is a serious problem because it leads to starvation — lower-priority tasks may never get CPU time at all. As a result, essential background operations like I/O handling, logging, or even yielding-based timing logic may never execute. This not only creates unfairness in task scheduling, but also leads to unpredictable system behavior, making the system difficult to debug, inefficient, and potentially unusable.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,3,'2025-07-21_13-01-15','If the scheduler is modified to always select the task with the highest priority, then only the highest-priority task would ever run, as long as it remains ready. Since the scheduler always picks the highest-priority option and does not rotate or share CPU time, all lower-priority tasks would be completely ignored, regardless of whether they’re ready to run.\nThis is a serious problem because it leads to starvation — lower-priority tasks may never get CPU time at all. As a result, essential background operations like I/O handling, logging, or even yielding-based timing logic may never execute. This not only creates unfairness in task scheduling, but also leads to unpredictable system behavior, making the system difficult to debug, inefficient, and potentially unusable.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,4,'2025-07-21_00-35-58','Calling C functions improves readability, maintainability, and portability of the code. C is easier to understand and debug, especially for complex logic like scheduling. However, switching between C and assembly can introduce some overhead due to context preservation and function calling conventions. Writing everything in assembly can be more efficient in terms of performance and control, but it is harder to write, error-prone, and less portable across platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,4,'2025-07-21_11-22-29','Calling C functions improves readability, maintainability, and portability of the code. C is easier to understand and debug, especially for complex logic like scheduling. However, switching between C and assembly can introduce some overhead due to context preservation and function calling conventions. Writing everything in assembly can be more efficient in terms of performance and control, but it is harder to write, error-prone, and less portable across platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,4,'2025-07-21_13-01-15','Calling C functions improves readability, maintainability, and portability of the code. C is easier to understand and debug, especially for complex logic like scheduling. However, switching between C and assembly can introduce some overhead due to context preservation and function calling conventions. Writing everything in assembly can be more efficient in terms of performance and control, but it is harder to write, error-prone, and less portable across platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,5,'2025-07-21_00-35-58','Yes, the OS would still work, but the benefits of multithreading would be lost. With only one thread, context switching becomes unnecessary — the thread will just yield and then resume again, since there are no other threads to switch to. The scheduler will keep selecting the same thread. This setup could still be useful for testing and understanding the scheduler\'s behavior, but it doesn’t demonstrate multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,5,'2025-07-21_11-22-29','Yes, the OS would still work, but the benefits of multithreading would be lost. With only one thread, context switching becomes unnecessary — the thread will just yield and then resume again, since there are no other threads to switch to. The scheduler will keep selecting the same thread. This setup could still be useful for testing and understanding the scheduler\'s behavior, but it doesn’t demonstrate multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',4,5,'2025-07-21_13-01-15','Yes, the OS would still work, but the benefits of multithreading would be lost. With only one thread, context switching becomes unnecessary — the thread will just yield and then resume again, since there are no other threads to switch to. The scheduler will keep selecting the same thread. This setup could still be useful for testing and understanding the scheduler\'s behavior, but it doesn’t demonstrate multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',5,1,'2025-07-21_00-43-29','The user passes an argument to a thread by including a pointer to the argument when calling the thread creation function (e.g., osThreadNew(myThread, &myData);). The OS stores this pointer in the thread\'s TCB and arranges for it to be loaded into the appropriate CPU register (usually r0) when the thread first starts executing. This setup works because, by convention, the first argument to a function in ARM architecture is passed in register r0, so when the thread begins, it receives the correct argument just like a regular function call.\n',0,'s25'),('jhamidal',5,1,'2025-07-21_11-28-15','The user passes an argument to a thread by supplying a pointer to that argument when calling the thread creation function (e.g., osThreadNew(myThread, &myData);).\nThe OS stores this pointer in the thread\'s TCB and, when setting up the thread’s initial stack frame, it places the pointer at the specific stack location that corresponds to register R0. This is because when the thread is first started, the processor uses the stack to restore the initial context — including registers — and the value at the R0 slot is automatically loaded into the actual R0 register, which is how arguments are passed according to the ARM procedure call standard (ABI).\nThis mechanism works because the thread’s startup context is carefully crafted by the OS to match what the CPU expects during exception return. As a result, when the thread begins execution, it receives the correct argument in R0 just like a regular C function call.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',5,2,'2025-07-21_00-43-29','Calling osYield() inside any context-switching function is bad because osYield() triggers a PendSV interrupt to request a context switch. If you call it from within PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, you may end up nesting or triggering another PendSV while one is already in progress. This can cause unpredictable behavior, re-entrancy issues, stack corruption, or even crash the system. These handlers are already part of the scheduling mechanism, so calling osYield() is redundant and unsafe.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',5,2,'2025-07-21_11-28-15','Calling osYield() inside any context-switching function is bad because osYield() triggers a PendSV interrupt to request a context switch. If you call it from within PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, you may end up nesting or triggering another PendSV while one is already in progress. This can cause unpredictable behavior, re-entrancy issues, stack corruption, or even crash the system. These handlers are already part of the scheduling mechanism, so calling osYield() is redundant and unsafe.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',5,3,'2025-07-21_00-43-29','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms (10 kHz tick rate) would allow for finer time resolution, meaning threads could delay or sleep with higher precision, and the system could respond to events more quickly. However, the downside is increased overhead — the CPU would spend more time handling the SysTick interrupt and performing context switches, leaving less time for actual thread work. This could hurt performance and power efficiency, especially in resource-constrained systems.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',5,3,'2025-07-21_11-28-15','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms (10 kHz tick rate) would allow for finer time resolution, meaning threads could delay or sleep with higher precision, and the system could respond to events more quickly. However, the downside is increased overhead — the CPU would spend more time handling the SysTick interrupt and performing context switches, leaving less time for actual thread work. This could hurt performance and power efficiency, especially in resource-constrained systems.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',5,4,'2025-07-21_00-43-29','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily yield control (using osYield()), so poorly behaved threads can block others by not yielding. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS interrupts threads automatically (e.g., using a timer) to enforce fairness.\nFrom the OS’s perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler — context switches only happen when explicitly requested. Pre-emptive multitasking requires more complex interrupt handling (e.g., through SysTick) and saving/restoring thread context without relying on the thread’s cooperation.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',5,4,'2025-07-21_11-28-15','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily yield control (using osYield()), so poorly behaved threads can block others by not yielding. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS interrupts threads automatically (e.g., using a timer) to enforce fairness.\nFrom the OS’s perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler — context switches only happen when explicitly requested. Pre-emptive multitasking requires more complex interrupt handling (e.g., through SysTick) and saving/restoring thread context without relying on the thread’s cooperation.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',5,5,'2025-07-21_00-43-29','Yielding and time-based pre-emption work together because they both ultimately trigger the same context-switching mechanism — typically via the PendSV interrupt. osYield() triggers it voluntarily, while the OS clock (SysTick) triggers it on a schedule. Since both paths lead to PendSV_Handler, they integrate naturally. This allows threads to give up control when they’re done, while still ensuring fairness through pre-emption if they run too long.\n',1,'s25'),('jhamidal',5,5,'2025-07-21_11-28-15','Yielding and time-based pre-emption work together because they both ultimately trigger the same context-switching mechanism — typically via the PendSV interrupt. osYield() triggers it voluntarily, while the OS clock (SysTick) triggers it on a schedule. Since both paths lead to PendSV_Handler, they integrate naturally. This allows threads to give up control when they’re done, while still ensuring fairness through pre-emption if they run too long.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',1,1,'2025-05-29_13-44-56','The HAL is the hardware abstraction layer which is the code that is essentially behind the scenes and preforms all of the set up which is difficult to write. Typically the IDE is able to write this code and makes us as the programmers life significantly easier. Using the HAL for general embedded projects is good since a large chunk of the work for setup is already completed and done for the programmer.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',1,1,'2025-05-29_17-31-17','The HAL is the hardware abstraction layer which is the code that is essentially behind the scenes and preforms all of the set up which is difficult to write. Typically the IDE is able to write this code and makes us as the programmer’s life significantly easier. Using the HAL for general embedded projects is good since a large chunk of the work for setup is already completed and done for the programmer.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',1,2,'2025-05-29_13-44-56','The __io_putchar function is used to redirect character output for embedded systems. It sends one singular character over UART2 using the HAL_UART_Transmit function and is cast to a uint8_t* which is a byte pointer to be transmitted as a single byte. The function then waits indefinitely for the transmission to complete the HAL_MAX_DELAY. And finally, it will return the same character after transmitting. It is needed to allow for functions such as printf() to work for embedded systems. This is only because these types of functions need a physical way to output the data. This function tells the embedded system how to do that using UART communication protocols.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',1,2,'2025-05-29_17-31-17','The __io_putchar function is used to redirect character output for embedded systems. It sends one singular character over UART2 using the HAL_UART_Transmit function and is cast to a uint8_t* which is a byte pointer to be transmitted as a single byte. The function then waits indefinitely for the transmission to complete the HAL_MAX_DELAY. And finally, it will return the same character after transmitting. It is needed to allow for functions such as printf() to work for embedded systems. This is only because these types of functions need a physical way to output the data. This function tells the embedded system how to do that using UART communication protocols. It is a hardware abstraction layer that separates typical I/O functions from the physical communication interface. For example, switching the communication protocol to another such as I2C or USB only the the __io_putchar function would need to change, while the printf will still work. This flexibility allows for this same application code to support various hardware setups using different protocols, by again just modifying one low-level function.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',1,3,'2025-05-29_13-44-56','In this lab we needed to use the debugger because we were manipulating the values of particular registers. We wanted to be able to tell which stack was currently be used by the STM32 in different parts of the program. To do so the debugger was required to go line by line and step over each function call. If we were to print out the value of either __get PSP() or __get_CONTROL () it wouldn’t be able to tell us that at that particular moment the processor is actually using PSP as the active SP.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',1,3,'2025-05-29_17-31-17','In this lab we needed to use the debugger because we were manipulating the values of particular registers. We wanted to be able to tell which stack was currently be used by the STM32 in different parts of the program. To do so the debugger was required to go line by line and step over each function call. If we were to print out the value of either __get PSP() or __get_CONTROL () it wouldn’t be able to tell us that at that particular moment the processor is actually using PSP as the active SP.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',1,4,'2025-05-29_13-44-56','We got a new value for PSP_val by casting the PSP_INIT_val to a uint32_t which is just an interger and then subtracting 0x400 from it. Which is decimal is 1024. We needed to know the initial location of MSP for this to work because by reading the MSP from the vector table at address 0x0. This ensured the PSP was placed in a non-overlapping memory region which was below it. This prevents stack corruption and allows us to switch safely from the MSP to the PSP.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',1,4,'2025-05-29_17-31-17','We got a new value for PSP_val by casting the MSP_INIT_val to a uint32_t which is just an integer and then subtracting 0x400 from it. Which in decimal is 1024. We needed to know the initial location of MSP for this to work because by reading the MSP from the vector table at address 0x0. This ensured the PSP was placed in a non-overlapping memory region which was below it. This prevents stack corruption and allows us to switch safely from the MSP to the PSP.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',1,5,'2025-05-29_13-44-56','Each thread needs its own separate stack space in memory. In order to achieve this the number of threads must be predefined. Then we need to pre-allocate a fixed sized block for each thread stack. Finally, we need to compute the base of each stack statically at the time of completion. This will allow for us to avoid overlaps. In this part we would need to compute the base (top) of each stack. Since the STM 32 processor grows downward it would be needed to use the end of each array as the stack start position. This ensures that each stack is properly aligned, non-overlapping, and its location is known statically at compile time.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',1,5,'2025-05-29_17-31-17','Each thread needs its own separate stack space in memory. In order to achieve this the number of threads must be predefined. Then we need to pre-allocate a fixed sized block for each thread stack. To avoid overlaps, we must computer the base (top) address of each of the stacks statically at compile time. Since the STM 32 processor grows downward, the starting address of of each stack should be set to the end of its own corresponding block. If we define a known base address for the entire stack region, we can then computer the starting point of each of thread’s stacks using an offsetting formula. stack_base_i = STACK_REGION_BASE - i * STACK_SIZE. This is for where i, the thread index and STACK_SIZE is the fixed size allocated per thread. This ensures that each stack is properly aligned, non-overlapping, and its location is known statically at compile time. Proper alignment (e.g., 8-byte or 16-byte boundaries) should also be considered to meet hardware requirements. This can be done by ensuring STACK_SIZE is a multiple of the required alignment, or by using compiler directives to enforce it. This approach allows for it to be scalable and safe for multithreading in an embedded environment.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,1,'2025-06-24_23-35-58','When I changed the value of PC in the thread’s stack to point to my print_continuously function, I was telling the processor to begin executing that function when the thread starts. This worked because during the system call, the processor switches to the prepared thread stack (stackptr), and then pops all the saved register values off the stack—including PC—into the actual hardware registers. By setting PC to the address of print_continuously, the processor starts executing that function as if it had been interrupted and is now resuming. This approach effectively boots up the thread in the correct execution context, and confirms that the stack was set up correctly. Without properly setting PC, the processor would jump to an invalid address, leading to a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,1,'2025-06-25_00-12-42','When I changed the value of PC in the thread’s stack to point to my print_continuously function, I was telling the processor to begin executing that function when the thread starts. This worked because during the system call, the processor switches to the prepared thread stack (stackptr), and then pops all the saved register values off the stack—including PC—into the actual hardware registers. By setting PC to the address of print_continuously, the processor starts executing that function as if it had been interrupted and is now resuming. This approach effectively boots up the thread in the correct execution context, and confirms that the stack was set up correctly. Without properly setting PC, the processor would jump to an invalid address, leading to a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,1,'2025-06-25_10-56-15','When I changed the value of PC in the thread’s stack to point to my print_continuously function, I was telling the processor to begin executing that function when the thread starts. This worked because during the system call, the processor switches to the prepared thread stack (stackptr), and then pops all the saved register values off the stack—including PC—into the actual hardware registers. By setting PC to the address of print_continuously, the processor starts executing that function as if it had been interrupted and is now resuming. This approach effectively boots up the thread in the correct execution context, and confirms that the stack was set up correctly. Without properly setting PC, the processor would jump to an invalid address, leading to a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,2,'2025-06-24_23-35-58','A system call is the mechanism a user-level program uses to request services from the operating system (OS). These services can include actions like reading from a file, writing to a display, allocating memory, or accessing devices. Since user programs do not have permission to directly access hardware or critical system resources, they must ask the OS to perform these tasks on their behalf using system calls.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,2,'2025-06-25_00-12-42','A system call is the mechanism a user-level program uses to request services from the operating system (OS). These services can include actions like reading from a file, writing to a display, allocating memory, or accessing devices. Since user programs do not have permission to directly access hardware or critical system resources, they must ask the OS to perform these tasks on their behalf using system calls.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,2,'2025-06-25_10-56-15','A system call is a special type of function call that allows a user-level program to request services from the kernel, the core part of the operating system that manages hardware and system resources. Because user programs run in a restricted environment without direct access to these resources, they rely on system calls to perform actions like reading from a file, writing to a display, allocating memory, or interacting with devices. When a system call is made, the CPU switches from user mode to kernel mode, allowing the kernel to safely execute the requested operation. The kernel identifies the specific service using a system call number, checks the arguments, performs the action, and returns the result to the user program. Referring to system calls as function calls and specifying that they interact with the kernel improves clarity by highlighting both their familiar structure and their critical role in enabling safe communication between user programs and low-level system operations.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,3,'2025-06-24_23-35-58','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing the memory so the CPU can begin executing the thread as if it had already been running. This involves manually pushing register values, like xPSR, PC (set to the thread function’s address), LR, and general-purpose registers (R0–R12), onto the stack in the exact order expected during an interrupt. This setup is needed because when the system call is made, the OS switches to the thread’s stack and restores these values into the CPU’s registers. Storing register values on the stack allows the CPU to recover the thread’s full execution context. Once this is done, calling runFirstThread() pops the values off the stack, loads them into the actual hardware registers, and the thread starts running at its entry function. This setup ensures the CPU treats the thread as if it had already been interrupted and is now resuming execution.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,3,'2025-06-25_00-12-42','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating and initializing a dedicated memory region that will act as the stack for that specific thread. This memory must be properly allocated beforehand to ensure it’s a valid area where the CPU can safely store and retrieve data. Once allocated, the stack is manually populated with the values that the CPU expects to find when resuming execution—such as xPSR, PC (pointing to the thread function), LR, and registers R0–R12—mimicking the layout created during an exception.\nThis preparation allows the operating system to switch context to the new thread by updating the stack pointer to this memory region. When runFirstThread() is called, it loads the CPU registers from this prepared stack, allowing the thread to begin execution as if it were returning from an interrupt. Without a properly allocated stack, the system would attempt to access invalid memory, leading to unpredictable behavior or a crash. Thus, setting up the stack correctly is critical for enabling the CPU to fully restore the thread’s context and begin execution seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,3,'2025-06-25_10-56-15','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating and initializing a dedicated memory region that will act as the stack for that specific thread. This memory must be properly allocated beforehand to ensure it’s a valid area where the CPU can safely store and retrieve data. Once allocated, the stack is manually populated with the values that the CPU expects to find when resuming execution—such as xPSR, PC (pointing to the thread function), LR, and registers R0–R12—mimicking the layout created during an exception.\nThis preparation allows the operating system to switch context to the new thread by updating the stack pointer to this memory region. When runFirstThread() is called, it loads the CPU registers from this prepared stack, allowing the thread to begin execution as if it were returning from an interrupt. Without a properly allocated stack, the system would attempt to access invalid memory, leading to unpredictable behavior or a crash. Thus, setting up the stack correctly is critical for enabling the CPU to fully restore the thread’s context and begin execution seamlessly.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,4,'2025-06-24_23-35-58','If we didn’t call __set_PSP(stackptr) before running the thread, the processor would still be using the previous stack (likely the main thread’s or an uninitialized value), which means it would try to pop register values from an invalid or incorrect memory location. Since runFirstThread() triggers the CPU to restore register values from the current Process Stack Pointer (PSP), an incorrect PSP would lead to the processor loading garbage or accessing restricted memory, causing a hard fault. By setting the PSP to stackptr, we ensure the CPU uses the correctly prepared stack where we manually placed the thread’s initial context (like xPSR, PC, and registers). This prevents invalid memory access and ensures the thread starts cleanly with the correct execution state.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,4,'2025-06-25_00-12-42','If we didn’t call __set_PSP(stackptr) before running the thread, the processor would still be using the previous stack (likely the main thread’s or an uninitialized value), which means it would try to pop register values from an invalid or incorrect memory location. Since runFirstThread() triggers the CPU to restore register values from the current Process Stack Pointer (PSP), an incorrect PSP would lead to the processor loading garbage or accessing restricted memory, causing a hard fault. By setting the PSP to stackptr, we ensure the CPU uses the correctly prepared stack where we manually placed the thread’s initial context (like xPSR, PC, and registers). This prevents invalid memory access and ensures the thread starts cleanly with the correct execution state.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,4,'2025-06-25_10-56-15','If we don’t call __set_PSP(stackptr) before running the thread, the processor continues using the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) instead of switching to the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is expected in unprivileged thread mode. Since runFirstThread() performs a context restore by popping register values from the stack currently pointed to by the PSP, failing to set the PSP would result in loading register values from an incorrect or uninitialized memory location—typically leading to a hard fault due to invalid memory access. On the ARM Cortex-M4, the distinction between MSP and PSP is critical: the MSP is used by the OS or exception handlers in privileged mode, while the PSP is used for thread-level execution in unprivileged mode. By calling __set_PSP(stackptr), we ensure the processor switches to the correct thread stack that we manually initialized with the proper context, including key values like xPSR, PC, and general-purpose registers. These initial values simulate the state the CPU expects after an interrupt, allowing runFirstThread() to restore the correct context and start the thread safely. Without this setup, the CPU would not know where to resume execution, resulting in unpredictable behavior or a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,5,'2025-06-24_23-35-58','If the thread function only printed once and then returned, the processor would attempt to continue execution from the address stored in the link register (LR), which we manually set to 0xFFFFFFFD. This value tells the processor to return to thread mode using the process stack, but since there is no valid return context prepared beyond the thread function, the processor would be left executing an undefined or invalid instruction. As a result, the system would likely crash and enter a hard fault. In bare-metal embedded systems, there is no underlying OS to manage thread cleanup or schedule a new task, so it is critical that thread functions do not return. Instead, they should remain in an infinite loop to keep the system stable and prevent unintended behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,5,'2025-06-25_00-12-42','If the thread function only printed once and then returned, the processor would attempt to continue execution from the address stored in the link register (LR), which we manually set to 0xFFFFFFFD. This value tells the processor to return to thread mode using the process stack, but since there is no valid return context prepared beyond the thread function, the processor would be left executing an undefined or invalid instruction. As a result, the system would likely crash and enter a hard fault. In bare-metal embedded systems, there is no underlying OS to manage thread cleanup or schedule a new task, so it is critical that thread functions do not return. Instead, they should remain in an infinite loop to keep the system stable and prevent unintended behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',2,5,'2025-06-25_10-56-15','If the thread function only printed once and then returned, the processor would attempt to continue execution from the address in the link register (LR), which we set to 0xFFFFFFFD to indicate an exception return to thread mode using the process stack. However, unlike during a normal exception, no return address was ever pushed onto the thread’s stack—meaning the processor has no valid address to return to. When it executes the return, it tries to pop a program counter (PC) value from an empty or invalid location, resulting in the execution of garbage data and triggering a hard fault. In a bare-metal system without an operating system to manage thread termination, it is critical that thread functions do not return. Instead, they should loop infinitely, ensuring the processor never attempts to perform a return sequence that depends on stack data that was never set up.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',3,1,'2025-06-27_02-21-03','When working with multiple .c files in a C project, each file is first compiled separately into what’s called an object file (.o). This process is known as separate compilation. For example, if you have main.c and kernel.c, each file is compiled independently by the compiler into main.o and kernel.o. These object files contain the compiled machine code for the functions defined in those files, as well as metadata about unresolved symbols—such as function calls or global variables that are defined in other files. To make this process work, shared function declarations (like osKernelInitialize() or osCreateThread()) are placed in header files (e.g., kernel.h) and included in each .c file using #include. This tells the compiler what functions exist and how to call them, even though the actual definitions are elsewhere.\nAfter compilation, the linker takes over. Its job is to combine all the object files into a single executable (in embedded development, this is typically an .elf file). During linking, it matches up the symbols—resolving function calls in main.o to their definitions in kernel.o, for example—and lays out the final memory layout of the program. This step uses a linker script, which is especially important in embedded systems like STM32. The linker script defines the memory regions (FLASH, RAM) and where code and data should be placed, including the initial stack pointer and interrupt vector table. Once the linking is complete, the resulting .elf file can be flashed onto the microcontroller.\nIn the context of your STM32 lab, main.c is responsible for calling high-level kernel setup functions like osKernelInitialize, osCreateThread, and osKernelStart, while kernel.c implements those functions. The compiler compiles each file separately, and the linker binds them together. This modular approach allows you to keep related code in distinct files, makes your codebase easier to manage, and ensures that only changed files are recompiled, which improves build efficiency.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',3,1,'2025-06-27_08-36-03','When building a C program with multiple .c files, the process involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. In the preprocessing stage, the compiler handles directives such as #include, #define, and #ifdef. For example, when main.c includes kernel.h, the preprocessor replaces that line with the actual contents of the header file, making function declarations and macros available to the source file. After preprocessing, the compilation step translates each .c file independently into an object file (.o). This is called separate compilation. The compiler converts the preprocessed code into machine code, and any references to functions or variables defined in other files are marked as unresolved symbols to be resolved later. Finally, during the linking stage, the linker combines all the object files into a single executable—typically an .elf file in embedded systems like STM32. It resolves all cross-file references and uses a linker script to define the final memory layout, such as where to place code in FLASH or data in RAM. This modular approach allows for better code organization, faster builds, and clear separation between files like main.c, which calls high-level OS functions, and kernel.c, which implements them.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',3,2,'2025-06-27_02-21-03','Using an #include directive on a .c file like #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it disrupts the modular compilation model that C is designed for. In a proper build system, each .c source file is compiled independently into an object file (.o), and then all object files are linked together to form the final executable. When you #include a .c file, you are directly pasting its contents into another .c file before compilation. This can cause multiple definition errors, as functions or variables defined in the included file may be defined again when the original .c file is compiled separately. It also leads to tightly coupled code, makes debugging harder, and violates separation of concerns. In our lab, we followed the correct structure: placing all source files in Core/Src and all headers in Core/Inc. For example, kernel logic like SVC_Handler_Main belongs in kernel.c, while user-level thread functions such as thread1_start should remain in main.c. These files communicate through header files like kernel.h, which expose declarations but not definitions. This organization ensures each component is compiled once, avoids duplication, and allows for a cleaner, more maintainable project. It also aligns with how embedded IDEs like STM32CubeIDE expect files to be organized, ensuring all files are visible to the project and correctly included in the build.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',3,2,'2025-06-27_08-36-03','Using an #include directive on a .c file like #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it disrupts the modular compilation model that C is designed for. In a proper build system, each .c source file is compiled independently into an object file (.o), and then all object files are linked together to form the final executable. When you #include a .c file, you are directly pasting its contents into another .c file before compilation. This can cause multiple definition errors, as functions or variables defined in the included file may be defined again when the original .c file is compiled separately. It also leads to tightly coupled code, makes debugging harder, and violates separation of concerns. In our lab, we followed the correct structure: placing all source files in Core/Src and all headers in Core/Inc. For example, kernel logic like SVC_Handler_Main belongs in kernel.c, while user-level thread functions such as thread1_start should remain in main.c. These files communicate through header files like kernel.h, which expose declarations but not definitions. This organization ensures each component is compiled once, avoids duplication, and allows for a cleaner, more maintainable project. It also aligns with how embedded IDEs like STM32CubeIDE expect files to be organized, ensuring all files are visible to the project and correctly included in the build.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',3,3,'2025-06-27_02-21-03','Call osKernelInitialize function\nThis prepares the kernel environment. It resets internal variables, stack allocation pointers, and thread structures, ensuring a clean slate before creating any threads.\nCall osCreateThread function\nYou pass your thread function (usually defined in the main c file) to this function. The OS handles allocating a stack and setting up the necessary CPU context for the thread to run.\nCall osKernelStart function\nThis starts the kernel by issuing a system call. The kernel switches to the thread’s stack, enters unprivileged thread mode, and jumps to the start of the thread function. Your thread begins executing from here.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',3,3,'2025-06-27_08-36-03','Call osKernelInitialize function\nThis prepares the kernel environment. It resets internal variables, stack allocation pointers, and thread structures, ensuring a clean slate before creating any threads.\nCall osCreateThread function\nYou pass your thread function (usually defined in the main c file) to this function. The OS handles allocating a stack and setting up the necessary CPU context for the thread to run.\nCall osKernelStart function\nThis starts the kernel by issuing a system call. The kernel switches to the thread’s stack, enters unprivileged thread mode, and jumps to the start of the thread function. Your thread begins executing from here.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',3,4,'2025-06-27_02-21-03','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory used by the operating system to manage the stack space needed for each thread. Every thread in a system must have its own dedicated stack, which is a region of memory used to store temporary data like function parameters, return addresses, and the CPU\'s saved register values during function calls or context switches. If threads shared a single stack, their data would overlap and corrupt each other, so isolated stacks are essential for correctness and reliability.\nIn this lab, the stack pool is manually implemented by predefining a block of memory (for example, starting at the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer, MSP) and then carving out fixed-size chunks (e.g., 0x400 bytes) from the top of that region each time a new thread is created. This process is managed by a global pointer (like next_stack_addr) which keeps track of the next free memory location in the pool. When osCreateThread() is called, it uses this pointer to assign a stack to the new thread, sets up an initial context frame at the top of the stack, and updates the pointer for the next allocation.\nIn future labs, this stack pool becomes especially important when adding support for multiple threads. For each new thread, the OS will allocate a unique stack region from the pool, store the starting address in a thread control block (like kernel_thread.sp), and use it to restore or switch between threads during context switching. As threads execute and the OS preempts or suspends them, their stack pointers are saved, and the OS loads the saved stack of the next thread to resume it exactly where it left off.\nThis mechanism is the foundation for building a cooperative or preemptive multitasking system, allowing multiple threads to run seemingly in parallel on a single CPU core. The stack pool ensures that each thread can operate independently without interfering with the execution context of others.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',3,4,'2025-06-27_08-36-03','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory used by the operating system to manage the stack space needed for each thread. Every thread in a system must have its own dedicated stack, which is a region of memory used to store temporary data like function parameters, return addresses, and the CPU\'s saved register values during function calls or context switches. If threads shared a single stack, their data would overlap and corrupt each other, so isolated stacks are essential for correctness and reliability.\nIn this lab, the stack pool is manually implemented by predefining a block of memory (for example, starting at the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer, MSP) and then carving out fixed-size chunks (e.g., 0x400 bytes) from the top of that region each time a new thread is created. This process is managed by a global pointer (like next_stack_addr) which keeps track of the next free memory location in the pool. When osCreateThread() is called, it uses this pointer to assign a stack to the new thread, sets up an initial context frame at the top of the stack, and updates the pointer for the next allocation.\nIn future labs, this stack pool becomes especially important when adding support for multiple threads. For each new thread, the OS will allocate a unique stack region from the pool, store the starting address in a thread control block (like kernel_thread.sp), and use it to restore or switch between threads during context switching. As threads execute and the OS preempts or suspends them, their stack pointers are saved, and the OS loads the saved stack of the next thread to resume it exactly where it left off.\nThis mechanism is the foundation for building a cooperative or preemptive multitasking system, allowing multiple threads to run seemingly in parallel on a single CPU core. The stack pool ensures that each thread can operate independently without interfering with the execution context of others.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',3,5,'2025-06-27_02-21-03','In an embedded operating system, the distinction between the \"user\" and the \"kernel\" refers to the separation between application-level logic and low-level system functionality. The kernel is responsible for managing core services like thread creation, stack allocation, and system calls. In our lab, these functionalities are implemented in kernel.c and declared in kernel.h, which are placed in the Core/Src and Core/Inc directories respectively. On the other hand, user code refers to the functions written by the application developer—such as thread behavior definitions and the main() function—which remain in main.c. This separation is important because it provides a clean and well-defined interface for users to interact with the operating system without needing to understand or modify its internals. It also promotes better modularity, easier debugging, and greater code reusability. For example, by keeping thread definitions in main.c and system functionality in the kernel files, the user can focus on what their application should do, while relying on the kernel to handle how it gets done. This mirrors how real operating systems are structured, where user code operates in unprivileged mode and accesses kernel services through system calls.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',3,5,'2025-06-27_08-36-03','In an embedded operating system, the distinction between the \"user\" and the \"kernel\" refers to the separation between application-level logic and low-level system functionality. The kernel is responsible for managing core services like thread creation, stack allocation, and system calls. In our lab, these functionalities are implemented in kernel.c and declared in kernel.h, which are placed in the Core/Src and Core/Inc directories respectively. On the other hand, user code refers to the functions written by the application developer—such as thread behavior definitions and the main() function—which remain in main.c. This separation is important because it provides a clean and well-defined interface for users to interact with the operating system without needing to understand or modify its internals. It also promotes better modularity, easier debugging, and greater code reusability. For example, by keeping thread definitions in main.c and system functionality in the kernel files, the user can focus on what their application should do, while relying on the kernel to handle how it gets done. This mirrors how real operating systems are structured, where user code operates in unprivileged mode and accesses kernel services through system calls.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,1,'2025-07-12_20-14-58','Using a queue to store Thread Control Blocks (TCBs) in round-robin scheduling provides a more natural and flexible structure compared to using a simple array. In round-robin scheduling, each thread takes a turn running, then yields and waits its turn again. A queue models this behavior perfectly by always selecting the thread at the front and placing it at the back after it runs. This simplifies the scheduling logic, eliminates the need to manually manage array indices or wraparounds, and makes it easier to skip inactive threads. It also allows for more dynamic thread management, as threads can be added or removed from the queue without shifting elements. Overall, a queue enhances readability, maintainability, and scalability of the scheduler, making it a cleaner and more modular solution than using a static array.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,1,'2025-07-12_20-25-26','Using a queue to store Thread Control Blocks (TCBs) in round-robin scheduling aligns closely with the functional behavior of the array-based scheduler already used in the lab, where a wraparound index cycles through threads. Both approaches implement the core idea of giving each thread a fair, time-sliced opportunity to run. However, a queue offers a more explicit and modular way to express this logic—threads are dequeued when scheduled and enqueued again after yielding, naturally reflecting the “first-in, first-out” behavior of round-robin scheduling. That said, switching to a queue-based approach may introduce unnecessary overhead. For instance, managing a linked-list queue or even a circular buffer involves extra pointer operations and memory usage, which can be inefficient in memory-constrained embedded systems. In contrast, the existing array-based approach with an index and modulo arithmetic is lightweight and performs well for a fixed number of threads. Therefore, while a queue may improve code clarity and extensibility, especially in more complex schedulers, the current array method is already efficient and functionally equivalent for basic round-robin use cases.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,1,'2025-07-22_19-53-35','Using a queue to manage Thread Control Blocks (TCBs) in round-robin scheduling aligns naturally with the core principle of giving each thread a fair, time-sliced turn to execute. In this model, threads are dequeued when scheduled and re-enqueued after yielding, which clearly reflects the “first-in, first-out” behavior central to round-robin logic. This approach offers conceptual clarity and modularity, making the scheduling process easier to understand and extend. However, in memory-constrained embedded systems like the one used in the lab, a queue-based implementation—especially one using dynamic memory or pointers—can introduce unnecessary overhead. The existing array-based scheduler already achieves the same effect using a wraparound index and modulo arithmetic, offering a simpler and more efficient solution for a fixed number of threads. While a queue may be more flexible for future enhancements or more complex scheduling policies, the array-based approach remains optimal for basic round-robin scheduling in this context.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,1,'2025-07-22_20-01-03','To demonstrate my understanding of the topic, I should have included a detailed explanation of how round-robin scheduling works, specifically how using a queue supports its \"first-in, first-out\" behavior. I also needed to evaluate the potential benefits and drawbacks of using a queue compared to the array-based method used in the lab. Providing a complete response is important because it shows that I understand not just the definition of a concept, but how it applies in context—especially in embedded systems where efficiency and simplicity matter. It also helps the instructor assess my comprehension and provide meaningful feedback. To ensure future submissions meet expectations, I will carefully reread the prompt, double-check formatting requirements (such as using proper XML tags), and make sure my answer directly responds to all parts of the question. Reviewing the rubric before submitting can also help me confirm that I’ve met the required criteria.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,1,'2025-07-26_19-04-45','Round-robin scheduling gives each thread a fixed time slice to run before switching to the next, and using a queue to store Thread Control Blocks (TCBs) naturally supports this behavior through its first-in, first-out (FIFO) structure. When a thread’s time slice ends, its TCB is removed from the front of the queue and reinserted at the back, allowing the next thread in line to run. This cycle continues, ensuring fair and predictable scheduling. Compared to using an array, which requires manual index management and can become complex when adding or removing threads, a queue offers greater flexibility and simplicity. In embedded systems where efficiency and clarity are essential, queues simplify scheduling logic and make dynamic thread management easier. Their built-in FIFO behavior also encapsulates the core idea of round-robin scheduling, reducing the amount of code needed to manage thread order. Overall, a queue can make the implementation of a round-robin scheduler more intuitive, maintainable, and adaptable than a static array-based approach.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,1,'2025-07-26_20-43-47','Round-robin scheduling gives each thread a fixed time slice to run before switching to the next, and a queue supports this behavior well through its first-in, first-out (FIFO) structure. When a thread’s time slice ends, its TCB is dequeued from the front and re-enqueued at the back, cycling execution in a fair and predictable manner. The current array-based scheduler mimics this behavior by incrementing an index and wrapping around—functionally similar to a circular queue. However, arrays require manual index tracking and make dynamic thread addition or removal more cumbersome.\nUsing an actual queue introduces some overhead, such as additional memory usage for dynamic data structures or more CPU cycles spent on enqueue/dequeue operations. On resource-constrained systems like the Cortex-M4, these trade-offs must be considered carefully. However, the benefits, like clearer logic, easier management of dynamic threads, and encapsulated FIFO behavior, may outweigh the overhead in more complex or modular RTOS designs. Overall, while both approaches support round-robin scheduling, queues offer greater flexibility and maintainability, especially when thread lifetimes vary or grow during runtime.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,1,'2025-07-27_08-01-48','Round-robin scheduling gives each thread a fixed time slice to run before switching to the next, and a queue supports this behavior well through its first-in, first-out (FIFO) structure. When a thread’s time slice ends, its TCB is dequeued from the front and re-enqueued at the back, cycling execution in a fair and predictable manner. The current array-based scheduler mimics this behavior by incrementing an index and wrapping around—functionally similar to a circular queue. However, arrays require manual index tracking and make dynamic thread addition or removal more cumbersome.\nUsing an actual queue introduces some overhead, such as additional memory usage for dynamic data structures or more CPU cycles spent on enqueue/dequeue operations. On resource-constrained systems like the Cortex-M4, these trade-offs must be considered carefully. However, the benefits, like clearer logic, easier management of dynamic threads, and encapsulated FIFO behavior, may outweigh the overhead in more complex or modular RTOS designs. Overall, while both approaches support round-robin scheduling, queues offer greater flexibility and maintainability, especially when thread lifetimes vary or grow during runtime.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,2,'2025-07-12_20-14-58','The PendSV_Handler function enables context switching in ARM Cortex-M systems by saving the state of the currently running thread and restoring the state of the next scheduled thread. When the operating system decides that a context switch is needed, it triggers the PendSV interrupt. Inside the PendSV_Handler, the CPU first saves the current thread’s context, this includes general-purpose registers that aren’t automatically saved during an interrupt, onto its stack. Then, the scheduler selects the next thread to run and updates the current thread pointer. The saved context of the new thread, including its stack pointer and registers, is then restored. Once this is complete, the handler exits, and execution resumes in the newly selected thread exactly where it left off. This mechanism allows multiple threads to share the CPU efficiently without interfering with each other’s execution.\n',0,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,2,'2025-07-12_20-25-26','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the context of the currently running thread and restoring the context of the next scheduled thread. When a context switch is triggered, the CPU is already using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the stack of the current thread. This is important because the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is reserved for exception handling, so using the PSP ensures each thread has its own independent stack. Inside PendSV_Handler, the OS first saves the current thread’s context by manually pushing the non-privileged registers—R4 to R11—onto the current thread’s stack. These registers are not automatically saved during an exception, so they must be preserved explicitly. The updated PSP value is then stored in the thread’s Thread Control Block (TCB).\nNext, the handler calls the osSched function, which is responsible for determining which thread to run next by updating a global pointer to the current TCB. After the new thread is selected, its PSP is loaded from its TCB, and its saved R4–R11 registers are restored by popping them off the new stack. The updated PSP is written back using the MSR PSP, R0 instruction. When PendSV_Handler exits, the processor automatically restores the remaining registers—R0 to R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR—from the new stack, completing the context switch and resuming execution of the next thread. This structured saving and restoring of context ensures that each thread can run independently and resume exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,2,'2025-07-22_19-53-35','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the context of the currently running thread and restoring the context of the next scheduled thread. When a context switch is triggered, the CPU is already using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the stack of the current thread. This is important because the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is reserved for exception handling, so using the PSP ensures each thread has its own independent stack. Inside PendSV_Handler, the OS first saves the current thread’s context by manually pushing the non-privileged registers—R4 to R11—onto the current thread’s stack. These registers are not automatically saved during an exception, so they must be preserved explicitly. The updated PSP value is then stored in the thread’s Thread Control Block (TCB).\nNext, the handler calls the osSched function, which is responsible for determining which thread to run next by updating a global pointer to the current TCB. After the new thread is selected, its PSP is loaded from its TCB, and its saved R4–R11 registers are restored by popping them off the new stack. The updated PSP is written back using the MSR PSP, R0 instruction. When PendSV_Handler exits, the processor automatically restores the remaining registers—R0 to R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR—from the new stack, completing the context switch and resuming execution of the next thread. This structured saving and restoring of context ensures that each thread can run independently and resume exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,2,'2025-07-22_20-01-03','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the context of the currently running thread and restoring the context of the next scheduled thread. When a context switch is triggered, the CPU is already using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the stack of the current thread. This is important because the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is reserved for exception handling, so using the PSP ensures each thread has its own independent stack. Inside PendSV_Handler, the OS first saves the current thread’s context by manually pushing the non-privileged registers—R4 to R11—onto the current thread’s stack. These registers are not automatically saved during an exception, so they must be preserved explicitly. The updated PSP value is then stored in the thread’s Thread Control Block (TCB).\nNext, the handler calls the osSched function, which is responsible for determining which thread to run next by updating a global pointer to the current TCB. After the new thread is selected, its PSP is loaded from its TCB, and its saved R4–R11 registers are restored by popping them off the new stack. The updated PSP is written back using the MSR PSP, R0 instruction. When PendSV_Handler exits, the processor automatically restores the remaining registers—R0 to R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR—from the new stack, completing the context switch and resuming execution of the next thread. This structured saving and restoring of context ensures that each thread can run independently and resume exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,2,'2025-07-26_19-04-45','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the context of the currently running thread and restoring the context of the next scheduled thread. When a context switch is triggered, the CPU is already using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the stack of the current thread. This is important because the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is reserved for exception handling, so using the PSP ensures each thread has its own independent stack. Inside PendSV_Handler, the OS first saves the current thread’s context by manually pushing the non-privileged registers—R4 to R11—onto the current thread’s stack. These registers are not automatically saved during an exception, so they must be preserved explicitly. The updated PSP value is then stored in the thread’s Thread Control Block (TCB).\nNext, the handler calls the osSched function, which is responsible for determining which thread to run next by updating a global pointer to the current TCB. After the new thread is selected, its PSP is loaded from its TCB, and its saved R4–R11 registers are restored by popping them off the new stack. The updated PSP is written back using the MSR PSP, R0 instruction. When PendSV_Handler exits, the processor automatically restores the remaining registers—R0 to R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR—from the new stack, completing the context switch and resuming execution of the next thread. This structured saving and restoring of context ensures that each thread can run independently and resume exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,2,'2025-07-26_20-43-47','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the context of the currently running thread and restoring the context of the next scheduled thread. When a context switch is triggered, the CPU is already using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the stack of the current thread. This is important because the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is reserved for exception handling, so using the PSP ensures each thread has its own independent stack. Inside PendSV_Handler, the OS first saves the current thread’s context by manually pushing the non-privileged registers—R4 to R11, onto the current thread’s stack. These registers are not automatically saved during an exception, so they must be preserved explicitly. The updated PSP value is then stored in the thread’s Thread Control Block (TCB).\nNext, the handler calls the osSched function, which is responsible for determining which thread to run next by updating a global pointer to the current TCB. After the new thread is selected, its PSP is loaded from its TCB, and its saved R4 to R11 registers are restored by popping them off the new stack. The updated PSP is written back using the MSR PSP, R0 instruction. When PendSV_Handler exits, the processor automatically restores the remaining registers—R0 to R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR, from the new stack, completing the context switch and resuming execution of the next thread. This structured saving and restoring of context ensures that each thread can run independently and resume exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,2,'2025-07-27_08-01-48','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the context of the currently running thread and restoring the context of the next scheduled thread. When a context switch is triggered, the CPU is already using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the stack of the current thread. This is important because the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is reserved for exception handling, so using the PSP ensures each thread has its own independent stack. Inside PendSV_Handler, the OS first saves the current thread’s context by manually pushing the non-privileged registers—R4 to R11, onto the current thread’s stack. These registers are not automatically saved during an exception, so they must be preserved explicitly. The updated PSP value is then stored in the thread’s Thread Control Block (TCB).\nNext, the handler calls the osSched function, which is responsible for determining which thread to run next by updating a global pointer to the current TCB. After the new thread is selected, its PSP is loaded from its TCB, and its saved R4 to R11 registers are restored by popping them off the new stack. The updated PSP is written back using the MSR PSP, R0 instruction. When PendSV_Handler exits, the processor automatically restores the remaining registers—R0 to R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR, from the new stack, completing the context switch and resuming execution of the next thread. This structured saving and restoring of context ensures that each thread can run independently and resume exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,3,'2025-07-12_20-14-58','Adding a static priority field to each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the task with the highest priority, without any further checks, is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority tasks. If a high-priority task is always ready to run (e.g., it never blocks or yields), then all lower-priority tasks will never get CPU time. This makes the system unfair and can cause important background tasks like I/O handling, logging, or communication (which often run at lower priority) to be completely ignored. Additionally, this approach removes any support for priority inversion handling or real-time responsiveness, both of which are crucial in embedded systems. A better design would involve preemptive scheduling with priority aging, time slicing, or blocking awareness, ensuring fairness and system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,3,'2025-07-12_20-25-26','Adding a static priority field to each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the task with the highest priority, without any further checks, is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority tasks. If a high-priority task is always ready to run (e.g., it never blocks or yields), then all lower-priority tasks will never get CPU time. This makes the system unfair and can cause important background tasks like I/O handling, logging, or communication (which often run at lower priority) to be completely ignored. Additionally, this approach removes any support for priority inversion handling or real-time responsiveness, both of which are crucial in embedded systems. A better design would involve preemptive scheduling with priority aging, time slicing, or blocking awareness, ensuring fairness and system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,3,'2025-07-22_19-53-35','Adding a static priority field to each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the task with the highest priority, without any further checks, is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority tasks. If a high-priority task is always ready to run (e.g., it never blocks or yields), then all lower-priority tasks will never get CPU time. This makes the system unfair and can cause important background tasks like I/O handling, logging, or communication (which often run at lower priority) to be completely ignored. Additionally, this approach removes any support for priority inversion handling or real-time responsiveness, both of which are crucial in embedded systems. A better design would involve preemptive scheduling with priority aging, time slicing, or blocking awareness, ensuring fairness and system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,3,'2025-07-22_20-01-03','Adding a static priority field to each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the task with the highest priority, without any further checks, is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority tasks. If a high-priority task is always ready to run (e.g., it never blocks or yields), then all lower-priority tasks will never get CPU time. This makes the system unfair and can cause important background tasks like I/O handling, logging, or communication (which often run at lower priority) to be completely ignored. Additionally, this approach removes any support for priority inversion handling or real-time responsiveness, both of which are crucial in embedded systems. A better design would involve preemptive scheduling with priority aging, time slicing, or blocking awareness, ensuring fairness and system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,3,'2025-07-26_19-04-45','Adding a static priority field to each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the task with the highest priority, without any further checks, is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority tasks. If a high-priority task is always ready to run (e.g., it never blocks or yields), then all lower-priority tasks will never get CPU time. This makes the system unfair and can cause important background tasks like I/O handling, logging, or communication (which often run at lower priority) to be completely ignored. Additionally, this approach removes any support for priority inversion handling or real-time responsiveness, both of which are crucial in embedded systems. A better design would involve preemptive scheduling with priority aging, time slicing, or blocking awareness, ensuring fairness and system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,3,'2025-07-26_20-43-47','Adding a static priority field to each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the task with the highest priority, without any further checks, is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority tasks. If a high-priority task is always ready to run (e.g., it never blocks or yields), then all lower-priority tasks will never get CPU time. This makes the system unfair and can cause important background tasks like I/O handling, logging, or communication (which often run at lower priority) to be completely ignored. Additionally, this approach removes any support for priority inversion handling or real-time responsiveness, both of which are crucial in embedded systems. A better design would involve preemptive scheduling with priority aging, time slicing, or blocking awareness, ensuring fairness and system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,3,'2025-07-27_08-01-48','Adding a static priority field to each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the task with the highest priority, without any further checks, is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority tasks. If a high-priority task is always ready to run (e.g., it never blocks or yields), then all lower-priority tasks will never get CPU time. This makes the system unfair and can cause important background tasks like I/O handling, logging, or communication (which often run at lower priority) to be completely ignored. Additionally, this approach removes any support for priority inversion handling or real-time responsiveness, both of which are crucial in embedded systems. A better design would involve preemptive scheduling with priority aging, time slicing, or blocking awareness, ensuring fairness and system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,4,'2025-07-12_20-14-58','Calling C functions like osSched in PendSV_Handler and SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler offers several important benefits, especially in the context of an educational or maintainable embedded OS. The main advantage is readability and maintainability: C code is far easier to read, understand, and debug compared to assembly, making it much more approachable for development, especially when logic becomes complex (e.g., managing thread queues or priority comparisons). C also allows for better abstraction and modularity, enabling the reuse of functions and integration with other C code, such as libraries or user applications.\nHowever, there are also downsides. Calling C functions from an exception handler adds overhead, as the compiler generates prologue/epilogue code to save/restore registers and manage the stack, which can make context switches slightly slower. Additionally, C doesn\'t give direct control over hardware-specific operations like precise register manipulation or stack frame layout, which are sometimes necessary in low-level systems programming. These tasks are more efficiently and precisely handled in assembly.\nIn summary, using C improves development speed, clarity, and maintainability, while assembly offers maximum control and efficiency. A hybrid approach—using assembly for low-level, performance-critical parts (like saving/restoring registers), and C for higher-level logic (like scheduling)—provides a practical and balanced solution, as seen in your lab.\n',1,'s25');
