ach—using assembly for low-level, performance-critical parts (like saving/restoring registers), and C for higher-level logic (like scheduling)—provides a practical and balanced solution, as seen in your lab.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,4,'2025-07-26_20-43-47','Calling C functions like osSched in PendSV_Handler and SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler offers several important benefits, especially in the context of an educational or maintainable embedded OS. The main advantage is readability and maintainability: C code is far easier to read, understand, and debug compared to assembly, making it much more approachable for development, especially when logic becomes complex (e.g., managing thread queues or priority comparisons). C also allows for better abstraction and modularity, enabling the reuse of functions and integration with other C code, such as libraries or user applications.\nHowever, there are also downsides. Calling C functions from an exception handler adds overhead, as the compiler generates prologue/epilogue code to save/restore registers and manage the stack, which can make context switches slightly slower. Additionally, C doesn\'t give direct control over hardware-specific operations like precise register manipulation or stack frame layout, which are sometimes necessary in low-level systems programming. These tasks are more efficiently and precisely handled in assembly.\nIn summary, using C improves development speed, clarity, and maintainability, while assembly offers maximum control and efficiency. A hybrid approach—using assembly for low-level, performance-critical parts (like saving/restoring registers), and C for higher-level logic (like scheduling)—provides a practical and balanced solution, as seen in your lab.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,4,'2025-07-27_08-01-48','Calling C functions like osSched in PendSV_Handler and SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler offers several important benefits, especially in the context of an educational or maintainable embedded OS. The main advantage is readability and maintainability: C code is far easier to read, understand, and debug compared to assembly, making it much more approachable for development, especially when logic becomes complex (e.g., managing thread queues or priority comparisons). C also allows for better abstraction and modularity, enabling the reuse of functions and integration with other C code, such as libraries or user applications.\nHowever, there are also downsides. Calling C functions from an exception handler adds overhead, as the compiler generates prologue/epilogue code to save/restore registers and manage the stack, which can make context switches slightly slower. Additionally, C doesn\'t give direct control over hardware-specific operations like precise register manipulation or stack frame layout, which are sometimes necessary in low-level systems programming. These tasks are more efficiently and precisely handled in assembly.\nIn summary, using C improves development speed, clarity, and maintainability, while assembly offers maximum control and efficiency. A hybrid approach—using assembly for low-level, performance-critical parts (like saving/restoring registers), and C for higher-level logic (like scheduling)—provides a practical and balanced solution, as seen in your lab.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,5,'2025-07-12_20-14-58','Yes, the OS will still work if you create only a single thread, such as the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, but its behavior will be limited. Since there\'s only one thread, the context switch mechanism will still function technically, but it will always \"switch\" back to the same thread. When the thread calls osYield(), the scheduler runs, but because there are no other runnable threads, it will simply resume the same thread. This means the system will repeatedly save and restore the same context, which is inefficient but not incorrect. The OS will still run and follow the intended flow, but the key benefit of multithreading,concurrent or interleaved execution of multiple tasks, won’t be realized. In short, the OS will function, but with unnecessary overhead and no real task switching.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,5,'2025-07-12_20-25-26','Yes, the OS will still work if you create only a single thread, such as the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, but its behavior will be limited. Since there\'s only one thread, the context switch mechanism will still function technically, but it will always \"switch\" back to the same thread. When the thread calls osYield(), the scheduler runs, but because there are no other runnable threads, it will simply resume the same thread. This means the system will repeatedly save and restore the same context, which is inefficient but not incorrect. The OS will still run and follow the intended flow, but the key benefit of multithreading,concurrent or interleaved execution of multiple tasks, won’t be realized. In short, the OS will function, but with unnecessary overhead and no real task switching.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,5,'2025-07-22_19-53-35','Yes, the OS will still work if you create only a single thread, such as the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, but its behavior will be limited. Since there\'s only one thread, the context switch mechanism will still function technically, but it will always \"switch\" back to the same thread. When the thread calls osYield(), the scheduler runs, but because there are no other runnable threads, it will simply resume the same thread. This means the system will repeatedly save and restore the same context, which is inefficient but not incorrect. The OS will still run and follow the intended flow, but the key benefit of multithreading,concurrent or interleaved execution of multiple tasks, won’t be realized. In short, the OS will function, but with unnecessary overhead and no real task switching.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,5,'2025-07-22_20-01-03','Yes, the OS will still work if you create only a single thread, such as the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, but its behavior will be limited. Since there\'s only one thread, the context switch mechanism will still function technically, but it will always \"switch\" back to the same thread. When the thread calls osYield(), the scheduler runs, but because there are no other runnable threads, it will simply resume the same thread. This means the system will repeatedly save and restore the same context, which is inefficient but not incorrect. The OS will still run and follow the intended flow, but the key benefit of multithreading,concurrent or interleaved execution of multiple tasks, won’t be realized. In short, the OS will function, but with unnecessary overhead and no real task switching.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,5,'2025-07-26_19-04-45','Yes, the OS will still work if you create only a single thread, such as the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, but its behavior will be limited. Since there\'s only one thread, the context switch mechanism will still function technically, but it will always \"switch\" back to the same thread. When the thread calls osYield(), the scheduler runs, but because there are no other runnable threads, it will simply resume the same thread. This means the system will repeatedly save and restore the same context, which is inefficient but not incorrect. The OS will still run and follow the intended flow, but the key benefit of multithreading,concurrent or interleaved execution of multiple tasks, won’t be realized. In short, the OS will function, but with unnecessary overhead and no real task switching.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,5,'2025-07-26_20-43-47','Yes, the OS will still work if you create only a single thread, such as the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, but its behavior will be limited. Since there\'s only one thread, the context switch mechanism will still function technically, but it will always \"switch\" back to the same thread. When the thread calls osYield(), the scheduler runs, but because there are no other runnable threads, it will simply resume the same thread. This means the system will repeatedly save and restore the same context, which is inefficient but not incorrect. The OS will still run and follow the intended flow, but the key benefit of multithreading,concurrent or interleaved execution of multiple tasks, won’t be realized. In short, the OS will function, but with unnecessary overhead and no real task switching.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',4,5,'2025-07-27_08-01-48','Yes, the OS will still work if you create only a single thread, such as the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, but its behavior will be limited. Since there\'s only one thread, the context switch mechanism will still function technically, but it will always \"switch\" back to the same thread. When the thread calls osYield(), the scheduler runs, but because there are no other runnable threads, it will simply resume the same thread. This means the system will repeatedly save and restore the same context, which is inefficient but not incorrect. The OS will still run and follow the intended flow, but the key benefit of multithreading,concurrent or interleaved execution of multiple tasks, won’t be realized. In short, the OS will function, but with unnecessary overhead and no real task switching.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',5,1,'2025-07-29_21-59-23','To pass an argument into a thread in Lab 5, the user calls osCreateThread(thread_func, args), where args is a pointer to any data they want to pass in. Internally, the OS is responsible for setting up the thread’s stack in a way that mimics what the CPU expects when a function is called. Specifically, the OS allocates a block of memory to act as the thread’s private stack, and manually pushes values onto it in the exact format that the ARM Cortex-M4 architecture requires when restoring thread context. This includes loading general-purpose registers (R4–R11) and, crucially, placing the argument pointer into register R0, which is where the ARM calling convention expects the first function parameter to be. The Program Counter (PC) is set to point to thread_func, and the stack also includes a special value for the Program Status Register (xPSR) to ensure the CPU runs in Thumb mode. When the thread is scheduled (via PendSV or SVC), the CPU performs a context restore, popping all these values off the stack. Because R0 was preloaded with the argument pointer and the PC was set to the thread function, execution begins as if the function was naturally called with that argument. It works because the Cortex-M4 hardware is designed to resume execution from a stack frame formatted in this way, and by manually setting up that frame, we effectively \"trick\" the CPU into starting our thread with the correct argument.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',5,2,'2025-07-29_21-59-23','You can’t call osYield from within PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these functions are already part of the low-level context switching process and are running in handler mode, not thread mode. The purpose of osYield is to request a context switch by triggering an SVC exception, which only works correctly when called from thread mode. If you call osYield from within one of these handlers, the system will attempt to trigger another exception while it is already handling one, which is not allowed for low-priority exceptions like SVC and PendSV. This can lead to unexpected behavior, such as the request being ignored or, worse, the system crashing due to corrupted stack state or register values. Since these handlers are already performing sensitive operations like saving and restoring thread context, interrupting them mid-process can break the scheduler entirely. Therefore, osYield must only be used from regular thread code where it is safe to initiate a context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',5,3,'2025-07-29_21-59-23','In Lab 5, we set the OS clock tick to 1ms, meaning the SysTick interrupt fires every millisecond to manage software timers and trigger context switches. If we reduced the tick interval to 0.1ms, the main benefit would be increased responsiveness and finer-grained control over timing, which could be useful for high-precision tasks or real-time applications that require tighter scheduling accuracy. However, the downside is that the SysTick interrupt would fire ten times more often, which increases CPU overhead significantly. This can lead to more time spent handling interrupts and context switches instead of running actual thread code, reducing overall efficiency and possibly causing missed deadlines or system instability. In most embedded applications like ours, a 1ms tick strikes a good balance between timing precision and system performance, so going to 0.1ms is rarely worth the added cost unless extremely fine timing is absolutely necessary.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',5,4,'2025-07-29_21-59-23','From the user\'s perspective, cooperative multitasking means that threads must voluntarily give up control by calling osYield, so the user is responsible for making sure each thread yields at appropriate times to allow other threads to run. In contrast, preemptive multitasking automatically switches between threads at regular intervals based on the OS clock, such as the 1ms SysTick timer in Lab 5, so the user does not need to manage when threads yield. From the OS perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler because the OS only performs a context switch when a thread explicitly calls osYield, usually by triggering an SVC exception. In preemptive multitasking, the OS must handle periodic interrupts from SysTick, check if the current thread\'s timeslice has expired, and then trigger a context switch using PendSV. This makes preemptive multitasking more complex to implement, but it provides better fairness and responsiveness without relying on threads to yield properly.\n',1,'s25'),('jmangaro',5,5,'2025-07-29_21-59-23','In the final exercise of Lab 5, we mixed cooperative and preemptive multitasking, and they work together because both mechanisms ultimately rely on the same context switching infrastructure, specifically the PendSV interrupt. In cooperative multitasking, when a thread calls osYield, it triggers an SVC interrupt, which runs SVC_Handler_Main in handler mode. This function then sets the PendSV pending bit to request a context switch. In preemptive multitasking, the SysTick_Handler runs every 1ms and manages software timers. If a thread’s time slice has expired, it also sets the PendSV pending bit. Regardless of the trigger, the actual context switch always happens in PendSV_Handler, which safely saves the current thread’s state and restores the next thread’s state. Because both cooperative and preemptive approaches feed into the same low-level handler, they can be used together without interference. This unified handling allows threads that yield voluntarily and threads that are preempted by the OS to coexist in the same system, enabling flexibility, fairness, and responsiveness while keeping the scheduler logic centralized and consistent.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',1,1,'2025-05-13_11-49-24','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer, and is comprised of header files and functions which interact with the device hardware directly. This allows you to use it as an intermediary when writing software, where you use the built-in HAL functions and variables to perform hardware actions. It makes development much easier because you do not have to code this low-level software yourself, and can simply call its functions when you need them.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',1,1,'2025-05-13_14-53-54','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer, and is comprised of header files and functions which interact with the device hardware directly. This allows you to use it as an intermediary when writing software, where you use the built-in HAL functions and variables to perform hardware actions. It makes development much easier because you do not have to code this low-level software yourself, and can simply call its functions when you need them.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',1,1,'2025-05-13_17-54-35','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer, and is comprised of header files and functions which interact with the device hardware directly. This allows you to use it as an intermediary when writing software, where you use the built-in HAL functions and variables to perform hardware actions. It makes development much easier because you do not have to code this low-level software yourself, and can simply call its functions when you need them.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',1,2,'2025-05-13_11-49-24','The function __io_putchar does two things. Firstly, you pass a character into the function, and it transmits this character as a single byte out of the UART2 channel. Secondly, it returns the character you passed into the function. The __io_putchar function is needed for using printf, as printf repeatedly calls __io_putchar when you pass it a string.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',1,2,'2025-05-13_14-53-54','The function __io_putchar does two things. Firstly, you pass a character into the function, and it transmits this character as a single byte out of the UART2 channel. Secondly, it returns the character you passed into the function. The __io_putchar function is needed for using printf, as printf repeatedly calls __io_putchar when you pass it a string. Moreover, it allows the flexibility for different output methods depending on your hardware implementation. You can define __io_putchar to be different depending on hardware implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',1,2,'2025-05-13_17-54-35','The function __io_putchar does two things. Firstly, you pass a character into the function, and it transmits this character as a single byte out of the UART2 channel. Secondly, it returns the character you passed into the function. The __io_putchar function is needed for using printf, as printf repeatedly calls __io_putchar when you pass it a string. Moreover, it allows the flexibility for different output methods depending on your hardware implementation. You can define __io_putchar to be different depending on hardware implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',1,3,'2025-05-13_11-49-24','We used the debugger to monitor the variables stored by the program in stack, at a certain instance in the program. For example, it let us see the msp and psp values before the infinite loop ran, which contain addresses to the current msp and first psp memory locations. The reason using printf to see these variables is bad practise is because it is first of all more tedious. But secondly, using printf can change the pointer locations, causing them to read different values than what you expect.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',1,3,'2025-05-13_14-53-54','We used the debugger to monitor the variables stored in CPU registers by the program in stack, at a certain instance in the program. For example, it let us see the msp and psp register values before the infinite loop ran, which contain addresses to the current msp and first psp memory locations. The reason using printf to see these variables is bad practise is because it is first of all more tedious. But secondly, using printf can change the pointer locations and register values, causing them to read different values than what you expect.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',1,3,'2025-05-13_17-54-35','We used the debugger to monitor the variables stored in CPU registers by the program in stack, at a certain instance in the program. For example, it let us see the msp and psp register values before the infinite loop ran, which contain addresses to the current msp and first psp memory locations. The reason using printf to see these variables is bad practise is because it is first of all more tedious. But secondly, using printf can change the pointer locations and register values, causing them to read different values than what you expect.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',1,4,'2025-05-13_11-49-24','Our stack allocation method divides the stack memory into two sections, one for the main stack of a certain size and the other for thread specific stacks, each of the same size. The main stack contains setup, interrupts, and other OS-specific memory, whereas the thread stacks will have memory which is used when running different threads. We got the new value for PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 from MSP_init_val, and so we needed to know the initial location of MSP to do so. You would not want to set PSP_val to the address of a random memory location, and so it is better to set it a certain distance from MSP_init_val.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',1,4,'2025-05-13_14-53-54','Our stack allocation method divides the stack memory into two sections, one for the main stack of a certain size and the other for thread specific stacks, each of the same size. The main stack contains setup, interrupts, and other OS-specific memory, whereas the thread stacks will have memory which is used when running different threads. We got the new value for PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 from MSP_init_val, and so we needed to know the initial location of MSP to do so. You would not want to set PSP_val to the address of a random memory location, and so it is better to set it a certain distance from MSP_init_val.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',1,4,'2025-05-13_17-54-35','Our stack allocation method divides the stack memory into two sections, one for the main stack of a certain size and the other for thread specific stacks, each of the same size. The main stack contains setup, interrupts, and other OS-specific memory, whereas the thread stacks will have memory which is used when running different threads. We got the new value for PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 from MSP_init_val, and so we needed to know the initial location of MSP to do so. Knowing the exact MSP starting address is crucial—without it, the PSP might point to an invalid or unsafe memory region. The offset must match the thread stack size to ensure proper memory separation and avoid issues like stack overlap or corruption, maintaining system stability and memory safety.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',1,5,'2025-05-13_11-49-24','To allocate n new stacks, you would first find out how much memory each stack needs to have. To do this, divide the total thread stack size by n and round to the nearest byte – call this number m. Next, you would change the existing code by adding a new pointer for every new thread, where each new pointer points to the memory location m away from the previous pointer. This would set up pointers pointing to the first memory location of each thread stack, allowing you to locate each stack efficiently. For example, the first new thread would be a pointer pointing to PSP_val – n.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',1,5,'2025-05-13_14-53-54','To allocate new stacks, you would first set a fixed stack size which you would make all your new stacks – call this size n in bytes. This ensures that stacks cannot overlap as you will be sure to keep within the stack size throughout the program. Next, you would change the existing code by adding a new pointer for every new thread, where each new pointer points to the memory location n away from the previous pointer. This would set up pointers pointing to the first memory location of each thread stack, allowing you to locate each stack efficiently. For example, the first new thread would be a pointer pointing to PSP_val – n.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',1,5,'2025-05-13_17-54-35','To allocate new stacks, you would first set a fixed stack size which you would make all your new stacks – call this size n in bytes. Since stacks typically grow downward in memory, each new stack would be positioned at a lower address than the previous one. For example, if the first thread\'s stack starts at PSP_val, the second would start at PSP_val - n, the third at PSP_val - 2n, and so on. This approach maintains memory safety and organization by respecting stack growth direction and ensuring no overlap or interference with other memory regions like the heap or system memory. Next, you would change the existing code by adding a new pointer for every new thread, where each new pointer points to the memory location n away from the previous pointer. This would set up pointers pointing to the first memory location of each thread stack, allowing you to locate each stack efficiently. For example, the first new thread would be a pointer pointing to PSP_val – n, the second PSP_val – 2n, and so on.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',2,1,'2025-06-02_11-27-16','The PC holds the address of the next function to run, and so when you pass the print continuously pointer into R0, the processor jumps to that function and executes it. This results in the infinite loop as defined in the print continuously function.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',2,1,'2025-06-02_12-32-06','The PC holds the address of the next function to run, and so when you pass the print continuously pointer into R0, the processor jumps to that function immediately and executes it. This results in the infinite loop as defined in the print continuously function.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',2,2,'2025-06-02_11-27-16','A system call allows a program to perform restricted actions that are not available in the default unprivileged mode. When performing a system call, a special interrupt makes the processor switch to privileged mode, where a handler is then able to perform actions such as interacting with the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',2,2,'2025-06-02_12-32-06','A system call is a function call that allows a program to perform restricted actions that are not available in the default unprivileged mode. When performing a system call, a special interrupt makes the processor switch to privileged mode, where a handler is then able to perform actions such as interacting with the kernel. The purpose of a system call is to request services from the kernel, as well as protecting hardware from untrustworthy code.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',2,3,'2025-06-02_11-27-16','When switching to a thread, the CPU attempt to “restore” the thread’s previous state – meaning the values stored in its registers previously. These values, if the thread had been running before, would have been pushed onto the thread’s stack, and subsequently been popped off into the CPU’s actual registers when the thread resumes once again. But, since we did not have a thread running previously that means that no register values were pushed onto the thread’s stack – meaning we had to put them there ourselves. The thread’s stack was set up by setting the PSP to a predetermined location in stack that would be used only for the thread. To “set up a thread’s stack”, as stated, means pushing register values onto the thread’s stack before beginning the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',2,3,'2025-06-02_12-32-06','When switching to a thread, the CPU attempt to “restore” the thread’s previous state – meaning the values stored in its registers previously. These values, if the thread had been running before, would have been pushed onto the thread’s stack, and subsequently been popped off into the CPU’s actual registers when the thread resumes once again. But, since we did not have a thread running previously that means that no register values were pushed onto the thread’s stack – meaning we had to put them there ourselves. The thread’s stack was set up by setting the PSP to a predetermined location in stack that would be used only for the thread. To “set up a thread’s stack”, as stated, means pushing register values onto the thread’s stack before beginning the thread. The order of this stack matters, as the processor pops off register values from the stack one at a time, starting from the top of the stack and putting them in registers in order beginning with xPSR.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',2,4,'2025-06-02_11-27-16','When switching to a thread, you need to tell the CPU where in the stack memory the “thread’s stack” is located. This prevents the CPU from using stack memory that is used in the main program, or other parts of the code. To do this, you set the PSP to hold the highest memory location of the predetermined “thread stack” by using __set_PSP. If we didn’t do this, the code would crash because the CPU would try to pop values off of wherever the PSP points by default, accessing random or invalid memory locations.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',2,4,'2025-06-02_12-32-06','When switching to a thread, you need to tell the CPU where in the stack memory the “thread’s stack” is located. This prevents the CPU from using stack memory that is used in the main program, or other parts of the code. To do this, you set the PSP to hold the highest memory location of the predetermined “thread stack” by using __set_PSP. If we didn’t do this, the code would crash because the CPU would try to pop values off of wherever the PSP points by default, accessing random or invalid memory locations.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',2,5,'2025-06-02_11-27-16','When the thread returns, it pops the link register value which holds the return address, and attempts to resume the main program from there. However, when we set up the thread’s stack and then began the thread, we put a garbage value in the LR register – 0xA. So when the thread returns, it attempts to jump to an invalid location, 0xA, and faults.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',2,5,'2025-06-02_12-32-06','When the thread returns, it pops the link register value from the CPU link register which holds the return address, and attempts to resume the main program from there. However, when we set up the thread’s stack and then began the thread, we put a garbage value in the LR register – 0xA. So when the thread returns, it attempts to jump to an invalid location, 0xA, and hardfaults.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',3,1,'2025-06-25_14-56-18','First, each file gets compiled into separate machine code files, or object files. During this step, the compiler also uses the corresponding header files to recognize function declarations and global variables in other files. Then, the linker connects function calls and global variables across files, producing a single executable.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',3,1,'2025-06-25_17-23-34','First the preprocessor runs, and handles macros and #includes. These are simply text-based operations, where macros are replaced with their respective values, and header files are placed in the same file as the .c file. Next, each file gets compiled into separate machine code files, or object files. During this step, the compiler uses the corresponding header file declarations to recognize function declarations and global variables in other files. Then, the linker connects function calls and global variables across files, producing a single executable. It matches function prototypes to the actual function declaration in other files, and also pulls code from libraries.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',3,1,'2025-06-25_18-09-10','First the preprocessor runs, and handles macros and #includes. These are simply text-based operations, where macros are replaced with their respective values, and header files are placed in the same file as the .c file. Next, each file gets compiled into separate machine code files, or object files. During this step, the compiler uses the corresponding header file declarations to recognize function declarations and global variables in other files. Then, the linker connects function calls and global variables across files, producing a single executable. It matches function prototypes to the actual function declaration in other files, and also pulls code from libraries.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',3,2,'2025-06-25_14-56-18','#include directives are supposed to be used for header files only. If you use it to include a .c file, the code in the .c file will be copied and compiled into all machine code files that include that .c file. However, if this occurs in multiple files, the compiler will see a function or variable declared more than once, resulting in a compilation error.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',3,2,'2025-06-25_17-23-34','#include directives are supposed to be used for header files only. If you use it to include a .c file, the code in the .c file will be copied and compiled into all machine code files that include that .c file. However, if this occurs in multiple files, the compiler will see a function or variable declared more than once, resulting in a compilation error.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',3,2,'2025-06-25_18-09-10','#include directives are supposed to be used for header files only. If you use it to include a .c file, the code in the .c file will be copied and compiled into all machine code files that include that .c file. However, if this occurs in multiple files, the compiler will see a function or variable declared more than once, resulting in a compilation error.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',3,3,'2025-06-25_14-56-18','Firstly, a thread must have memory allocated for it on the stack\nThen, its stack must be initialized with register values to simulate a thread which has just been interrupted. This must contain things like the thread’s stack pointer and function pointer.\nThen, to begin running a thread you need access to the kernel, and so need to initiate a system call which will pop the register values off the stack and place them in the CPU registers.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',3,3,'2025-06-25_17-23-34','The user must write 3 functions as follows, and call them in order:\nA function which initializes the kernel with values like stack pool size, thread size, number of threads, and the MSP start location.\nA function which allocates memory for a thread on the stack, initializing it with register values to simulate a thread which has just been interrupted. This must contain things like the thread’s stack pointer and function pointer.\nThen, to begin running a thread you need access to the kernel, and so need a function to initiate a system call which will pop the register values off the stack and place them in the CPU registers. This essentially starts the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',3,3,'2025-06-25_18-09-10','The user must write 3 functions as follows, and call them in order:\nA function which initializes the kernel with values like stack pool size, thread size, number of threads, and the MSP start location.\nA function which allocates memory for a thread in the kernel stack, initializing it with register values to simulate a thread which has just been interrupted. This must contain things like the thread’s stack pointer and function pointer. This function pointer contains the address of the thread function, which the user defines and is the function which runs immediately after the thread begins.\nThen, to begin running a thread you need access to the kernel, and so need a function to initiate a system call which will pop the register values off the stack and place them in the CPU registers. This essentially starts the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',3,4,'2025-06-25_14-56-18','The stack pool is the total pool of memory which is used for stacks, including the main stack and stacks for individual threads. Each stack needs its own space allocated so that they do not overlap. In a future lab, additional thread stacks can be allocated by keeping track of how many stacks have been allocated already, and finding the next available block of memory to fit a new stack.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',3,4,'2025-06-25_17-23-34','The stack pool is the total pool of memory which is used for stacks, including the main stack and stacks for individual threads. Each stack needs its own space allocated so that they do not overlap. In a future lab, additional thread stacks can be allocated by keeping track of how many stacks have been allocated already, and finding the next available block of memory to fit a new stack.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',3,4,'2025-06-25_18-09-10','The stack pool is the total pool of memory which is used for stacks, including the main stack and stacks for individual threads. Each stack needs its own space allocated so that they do not overlap. In a future lab, additional thread stacks can be allocated by keeping track of how many stacks have been allocated already, and finding the next available block of memory to fit a new stack.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',3,5,'2025-06-25_14-56-18','The kernel code is important to separate from user code, as it is very sensitive and interacts directly with memory and the CPU. The kernel file contains code managing threads, and controlling system calls, and if used improperly can cause crashes. The user code uses the kernel code to provide a layer of abstraction between the user and the low-level implementation, making it easier for the user to develop their program.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',3,5,'2025-06-25_17-23-34','The kernel code is important to separate from user code, as it is very sensitive and interacts directly with memory and the CPU. The kernel file contains code managing threads, and controlling system calls, and if used improperly can cause crashes. The user only interacts with the user code, and only calls functions from the kernel code. The user code uses the kernel code to provide a layer of abstraction between the user and the low-level implementation, making it easier for the user to develop their program as they do not need to worry about register and stack usage.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',3,5,'2025-06-25_18-09-10','The kernel code is important to separate from user code, as it is very sensitive and is responsible for controlling access to hardware and memory. The kernel file contains code managing threads and controlling system calls, and if used improperly can cause crashes. The user only interacts with user code, and is responsible for writing threads and using the kernel’s API. The user code uses the kernel code to provide a layer of abstraction between the user and the kernel implementation, making it easier for the user to develop their application as they do not need to worry about register and stack usage.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,1,'2025-07-29_17-15-35','A queue can be used to implement round-robin scheduling by storing the threads in a queue. The first thread is dequeued, ran, and afterwards it is enqueued to the end of the list. This repeats for all threads, creating a loop. The benefits of this stem from not having to use an array data structure. Compared to an array format, the queue does not need to have as much space allocated if you are not running the maximum number of threads. Additionally a queue does not need to worry about indexing in an array and potentially having gaps in the array.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',4,1,'2025-07-29_23-37-48','A queue can be used to implement round-robin scheduling by storing the threads in a queue. The first thread is dequeued, ran, and afterwards it is enqueued to the end of the list. This repeats for all threads, creating a loop. The benefits of this stem from not having to use an array data structure. Compared to an array format, the queue does not need to have as much space allocated if you are not running the maximum number of threads. Additionally a queue does not need to worry about indexing in an array and potentially having gaps in the array. However, a queue would potentially need more overhead to run, due to dynamic memory allocation and pointer manipulation.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,1,'2025-07-30_00-27-57','A queue can be used to implement round-robin scheduling by storing the threads in a queue. The first thread is dequeued, ran, and afterwards it is enqueued to the end of the list. This repeats for all threads, creating a loop. The benefits of this stem from not having to use an array data structure. Compared to an array format, the queue does not need to have as much space allocated if you are not running the maximum number of threads. Additionally a queue does not need to worry about indexing in an array and potentially having gaps in the array. However, a queue would potentially need more overhead to run, due to dynamic memory allocation and pointer manipulation.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,1,'2025-07-30_11-35-50','A queue can be used to implement round-robin scheduling by storing the threads in a queue. The first thread is dequeued, ran, and afterwards it is enqueued to the end of the list. This repeats for all threads, creating a loop. The benefits of this stem from not having to use an array data structure. Compared to an array format, the queue does not need to have as much space allocated if you are not running the maximum number of threads. Additionally a queue does not need to worry about indexing in an array and potentially having gaps in the array. However, a queue would potentially need more overhead to run, due to dynamic memory allocation and pointer manipulation.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,2,'2025-07-29_17-15-35','The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch firstly by saving the current state of the registers onto the current thread’s stack. Then, it calls osSched() to update the thread data structure’s elements, like the stack pointer and current thread index. Then, it pops the new thread’s register values from its stack, and sets PSP. Then it runs the new thread.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',4,2,'2025-07-29_23-37-48','The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch firstly by saving the current state of the registers onto the current thread’s stack. R4-11 are pushed onto the stack in PendSV_Handler, and the rest of the registers are automatically pushed onto the stack by hardware. Then, it calls osSched() to update the thread data structure’s elements, like the stack pointer and current thread index. The PSP points to the current top of the thread’s stack, so it is important to keep track of its location so that when resuming the thread, the stack structure is maintained. Then, it locates the new thread’s stack and pops the new thread’s register values from it, and sets PSP. Then it runs the new thread.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',4,2,'2025-07-30_00-27-57','The process begins when the user triggers an interrupt which is handled by PendSV_Handler. The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch firstly by saving the current state of the registers onto the current thread’s stack. R4-11 are pushed onto the stack in PendSV_Handler, and the rest of the registers are automatically pushed onto the stack by hardware. Then, it calls osSched() to update the thread data structure’s elements, like the stack pointer and current thread index. The PSP points to the current top of the thread’s stack, so it is important to keep track of its location so that when resuming the thread, the stack structure is maintained. osSched() also selects a new thread by setting the PSP. Then, PendSV_Handler locates the new thread’s stack using PSP, and pops the new thread’s register values from it. Then it runs the new thread.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',4,2,'2025-07-30_11-35-50','The process begins when the user triggers an interrupt which is handled by PendSV_Handler. The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch firstly by reading the PSP to locate the thread’s stack, then saving the current state of the registers onto the current thread’s stack. R4-11 are pushed onto the stack in PendSV_Handler, and the rest of the registers are automatically pushed onto the stack by hardware. Then, it calls osSched() to update the thread data structure’s elements: it updates the stack pointer element, and then increments the current thread number. The PSP points to the current top of the thread’s stack, so it is important to keep track of its location so that when resuming the thread, the stack structure is maintained. osSched() then selects a new thread by setting the PSP. Then, PendSV_Handler locates the new thread’s stack using PSP, and pops the new thread’s register values from it. Then it runs the new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,3,'2025-07-29_17-15-35','Adding a priority feature to the TCB would mean that the scheduler always runs the thread with the highest priority. However, this is a bad idea because it can cause starvation. Essentially, threads with lower priority may never get a chance to run, because the scheduler keeps running the higher priority thread.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,3,'2025-07-29_23-37-48','Adding a priority feature to the TCB would mean that the scheduler always runs the thread with the highest priority. However, this is a bad idea because it can cause starvation. Essentially, threads with lower priority may never get a chance to run, because the scheduler keeps running the higher priority thread.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,3,'2025-07-30_00-27-57','Adding a priority feature to the TCB would mean that the scheduler always runs the thread with the highest priority. However, this is a bad idea because it can cause starvation. Essentially, threads with lower priority may never get a chance to run, because the scheduler keeps running the higher priority thread.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,3,'2025-07-30_11-35-50','Adding a priority feature to the TCB would mean that the scheduler always runs the thread with the highest priority. However, this is a bad idea because it can cause starvation. Essentially, threads with lower priority may never get a chance to run, because the scheduler keeps running the higher priority thread.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,4,'2025-07-29_17-15-35','Firstly the benefits of calling C functions are as follows. C is much more maintainable and readable, meaning that development is a lot quicker and less error-prone. On the other hand, the advantages to using assembly are as follows. Assembly, if written effectively, can be quicker than C code as there will be less overhead. Additionally, in assembly you can control exactly what happens to each register.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,4,'2025-07-29_23-37-48','Firstly the benefits of calling C functions are as follows. C is much more maintainable and readable, meaning that development is a lot quicker and less error-prone. On the other hand, the advantages to using assembly are as follows. Assembly, if written effectively, can be quicker than C code as there will be less overhead. Additionally, in assembly you can control exactly what happens to each register.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,4,'2025-07-30_00-27-57','Firstly the benefits of calling C functions are as follows. C is much more maintainable and readable, meaning that development is a lot quicker and less error-prone. On the other hand, the advantages to using assembly are as follows. Assembly, if written effectively, can be quicker than C code as there will be less overhead. Additionally, in assembly you can control exactly what happens to each register.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,4,'2025-07-30_11-35-50','Firstly the benefits of calling C functions are as follows. C is much more maintainable and readable, meaning that development is a lot quicker and less error-prone. On the other hand, the advantages to using assembly are as follows. Assembly, if written effectively, can be quicker than C code as there will be less overhead. Additionally, in assembly you can control exactly what happens to each register.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,5,'2025-07-29_17-15-35','Yes, the OS will still work. When the thread calls osYield(), the SVC_Handler is triggered, calling the PendSV_Handler. It saves the thread’s context, then calls osSched() which will simply cycle the thread back to itself, then restores the same thread’s context. Then the thread resumes, running the same thread again.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,5,'2025-07-29_23-37-48','Yes, the OS will still work. When the thread calls osYield(), the SVC_Handler is triggered, calling the PendSV_Handler. It saves the thread’s context, then calls osSched() which will simply cycle the thread back to itself, then restores the same thread’s context. Then the thread resumes, running the same thread again.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,5,'2025-07-30_00-27-57','Yes, the OS will still work. When the thread calls osYield(), the SVC_Handler is triggered, calling the PendSV_Handler. It saves the thread’s context, then calls osSched() which will simply cycle the thread back to itself, then restores the same thread’s context. Then the thread resumes, running the same thread again.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',4,5,'2025-07-30_11-35-50','Yes, the OS will still work. When the thread calls osYield(), the SVC_Handler is triggered, calling the PendSV_Handler. It saves the thread’s context, then calls osSched() which will simply cycle the thread back to itself, then restores the same thread’s context. Then the thread resumes, running the same thread again.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,1,'2025-07-30_11-48-00','Firstly, the user defines a thread function that can take in a void* parameter. Then, the user creates the data to pass into the thread, and passes in its address. On the OS side, the kernel allocates a new stack for the thread, and sets up the register context making sure to place the argument into R0. Then, when the scheduler runs the thread, R0 is popped off the stack, and the function is executed while using the address in R0 as its argument.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,1,'2025-07-30_13-50-44','Firstly, the user defines a thread function that can take in a void* parameter. Then, the user creates the data to pass into the thread, and passes in its address. On the OS side, the kernel allocates a new stack for the thread, and sets up the register context making sure to place the argument into R0. Then, when the scheduler runs the thread, R0 is popped off the stack, and the function is executed while using the address in R0 as its argument.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',5,1,'2025-07-30_14-25-14','Firstly, the user defines a thread function that can take in a void* parameter. Then, the user creates the data to pass into the thread, and passes in its address. On the OS side, the kernel allocates a new stack for the thread, and sets up the register context making sure to place the argument into the R0 location in stack. Then, when the scheduler runs the thread, R0 is popped off the stack, and the function is executed while using the address in R0 as its argument. This all works because the stack is set up according to the ABI convention, and so register values are popped off of the stack in the correct order such that they are placed into their corresponding registers.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,1,'2025-07-30_15-26-26','Firstly, the user defines a thread function that can take in a void* parameter. Then, the user creates the data to pass into the thread, and passes in its address. On the OS side, the kernel allocates a new stack for the thread, and sets up the register context making sure to place the argument into the R0 location in stack. Then, when the scheduler runs the thread, R0 is popped off the stack, and the function is executed while using the address in R0 as its argument. This all works because the stack is set up according to the ABI convention, and so register values are popped off of the stack in the correct order such that they are placed into their corresponding registers.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,2,'2025-07-30_11-48-00','Calling osYield in any handlers which are involved in context switching can cause problems. Firstly, calling it in any of the functions which are run after calling osYield can cause an infinite loop. When osYield is called, it triggers SVC_Handler, which then calls SVC_Handler_Main, running the case which schedules PendSV_Handler which switches context. Therefore, if osYield is triggered in SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or PendSV_Handler, it will run osYield again, resulting in an infinite loop. However, if osYield is called in SysTick_Handler, it will not result in an infinite loop, but will have unwanted behavior. Since SysTick_Handler is not called when osYield is ran, there will be no infinite loop. However SysTick_Handler is called every 1ms, and is used as a counter to trigger a context switch if a thread runs for too long. So, if osYield is called in SysTick_Handler, it will cause the context to switch before the counter reaches 0, resulting in a premature context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',5,2,'2025-07-30_13-50-44','Calling osYield in any handlers which are involved in context switching can cause problems. Firstly, calling it in any of the functions which are run after calling osYield can cause an infinite loop, resulting in stack corruption and risk of a hard fault. When osYield is called, it triggers SVC_Handler, which then calls SVC_Handler_Main, running the case which schedules PendSV_Handler which switches context. Therefore, if osYield is triggered in SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or PendSV_Handler, it will run osYield again, resulting in an infinite loop. This infinite loop will keep adding to the stack, which will cause it to corrupt and potentially hard fault. However, if osYield is called in SysTick_Handler, it will not result in an infinite loop, but will have unwanted behavior. Since SysTick_Handler is not called when osYield is ran, there will be no infinite loop. However SysTick_Handler is called every 1ms, and is used as a counter to trigger a context switch if a thread runs for too long. So, if osYield is called in SysTick_Handler, it will cause the context to switch before the counter reaches 0, resulting in a premature context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,2,'2025-07-30_14-25-14','Calling osYield in any handlers which are involved in context switching can cause problems. Firstly, calling it in any of the functions which are run after calling osYield can cause an infinite loop, resulting in stack corruption and risk of a hard fault. When osYield is called, it triggers SVC_Handler, which then calls SVC_Handler_Main, running the case which schedules PendSV_Handler which switches context. Therefore, if osYield is triggered in SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or PendSV_Handler, it will run osYield again, resulting in an infinite loop. This infinite loop will keep adding to the stack, which will cause it to corrupt and potentially hard fault. However, if osYield is called in SysTick_Handler, it will not result in an infinite loop, but will have unwanted behavior. Since SysTick_Handler is not called when osYield is ran, there will be no infinite loop. However SysTick_Handler is called every 1ms, and is used as a counter to trigger a context switch if a thread runs for too long. So, if osYield is called in SysTick_Handler, it will cause the context to switch before the counter reaches 0, resulting in a premature context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',5,2,'2025-07-30_15-26-26','Calling osYield in any handlers which are involved in context switching can cause problems. Firstly, calling it in any of the functions which are run after calling osYield can cause an infinite loop, resulting in stack corruption and risk of a hard fault. When osYield is called, it triggers SVC_Handler, which then calls SVC_Handler_Main, running the case which schedules PendSV_Handler which switches context. Therefore, if osYield is triggered in SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or PendSV_Handler, it will run osYield again, resulting in an infinite loop. This infinite loop will keep adding to the stack, which will cause stack corruption or a hard fault. However, if osYield is called in SysTick_Handler, it will not result in an infinite loop, but will have unwanted behavior. Since SysTick_Handler is not called when osYield is ran, there will be no infinite loop. However SysTick_Handler is called every 1ms, and is used as a counter to trigger a context switch if a thread runs for too long. So, if osYield is called in SysTick_Handler, it will cause the context to switch before the counter reaches 0, resulting in a premature context switch. Essentially, you should never execute a system call from an interrupt handler, as they assume that they are being called from unprivileged user code.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,3,'2025-07-30_11-48-00','Firstly, the benefits are as follows. Thread scheduling and delays would be more precise, as there is a finer time scale. This would benefit systems which require many fast context switches. However, there would be downsides as follows. There would be more overhead, as the SysTick_Handler would be called every 0.1 ms instead of every 1 ms.\n',0,'s25'),('jsgort',5,3,'2025-07-30_13-50-44','Firstly, the benefits are as follows. Thread scheduling and delays would be more precise, as there is a finer time scale. This would benefit systems which require many fast context switches. Additionally, the system would have more reactivity because context switches can occur more frequently. However, there would be downsides as follows. There would be more overhead, as the SysTick_Handler would be called every 0.1 ms instead of every 1 ms. Additionally, there would be higher power consumption because the increased interrupt activity would prevent the CPU from entering lower-power modes.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,3,'2025-07-30_14-25-14','Firstly, the benefits are as follows. Thread scheduling and delays would be more precise, as there is a finer time scale. This would benefit systems which require many fast context switches. Additionally, the system would have more reactivity because context switches can occur more frequently. However, there would be downsides as follows. There would be more overhead, as the SysTick_Handler would be called every 0.1 ms instead of every 1 ms. Additionally, there would be higher power consumption because the increased interrupt activity would prevent the CPU from entering lower-power modes.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,3,'2025-07-30_15-26-26','Firstly, the benefits are as follows. Thread scheduling and delays would be more precise, as there is a finer time scale. This would benefit systems which require many fast context switches. Additionally, the system would have more reactivity because context switches can occur more frequently. However, there would be downsides as follows. There would be more overhead, as the SysTick_Handler would be called every 0.1 ms instead of every 1 ms. Additionally, there would be higher power consumption because the increased interrupt activity would prevent the CPU from entering lower-power modes.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,4,'2025-07-30_11-48-00','From the user perspective, implementing co-operative multitasking means that the user must call osYield to switch context to another thread when the current thread is done running. If osYield is never called, then the current thread will run forever, and so co-operative multitasking requires more responsibility for the user. Pre-emptive multitasking means the threads are automatically interrupted after a certain period of time, and so the user does not need to call any function to switch contexts, it is done automatically. Therefore this option is safer, and ensures all threads get CPU time. \n\nFrom the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler as context switching only happens when the current thread yields (when osYield is called). The OS does not need to keep track of anything, and simply saves the thread’s state and loads the next thread when the current thread yields. However, for pre-emptive multitasking, interrupts need to be generated at regular intervals to decrement a time counter, and the OS must force a context switch once the counter reaches 0. This requires the OS to keep track of more things.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,4,'2025-07-30_13-50-44','From the user perspective, implementing co-operative multitasking means that the user must call osYield to switch context to another thread when the current thread is done running. If osYield is never called, then the current thread will run forever, and so co-operative multitasking requires more responsibility for the user. Pre-emptive multitasking means the threads are automatically interrupted after a certain period of time, and so the user does not need to call any function to switch contexts, it is done automatically. Therefore this option is safer, and ensures all threads get CPU time. \n\nFrom the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler as context switching only happens when the current thread yields (when osYield is called). The OS does not need to keep track of anything, and simply saves the thread’s state and loads the next thread when the current thread yields. However, for pre-emptive multitasking, interrupts need to be generated at regular intervals to decrement a time counter, and the OS must force a context switch once the counter reaches 0. This requires the OS to keep track of more things.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,4,'2025-07-30_14-25-14','From the user perspective, implementing co-operative multitasking means that the user must call osYield to switch context to another thread when the current thread is done running. If osYield is never called, then the current thread will run forever, and so co-operative multitasking requires more responsibility for the user. Pre-emptive multitasking means the threads are automatically interrupted after a certain period of time, and so the user does not need to call any function to switch contexts, it is done automatically. Therefore this option is safer, and ensures all threads get CPU time. \n\nFrom the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler as context switching only happens when the current thread yields (when osYield is called). The OS does not need to keep track of anything, and simply saves the thread’s state and loads the next thread when the current thread yields. However, for pre-emptive multitasking, interrupts need to be generated at regular intervals to decrement a time counter, and the OS must force a context switch once the counter reaches 0. This requires the OS to keep track of more things.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,4,'2025-07-30_15-26-26','From the user perspective, implementing co-operative multitasking means that the user must call osYield to switch context to another thread when the current thread is done running. If osYield is never called, then the current thread will run forever, and so co-operative multitasking requires more responsibility for the user. Pre-emptive multitasking means the threads are automatically interrupted after a certain period of time, and so the user does not need to call any function to switch contexts, it is done automatically. Therefore this option is safer, and ensures all threads get CPU time. \n\nFrom the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler as context switching only happens when the current thread yields (when osYield is called). The OS does not need to keep track of anything, and simply saves the thread’s state and loads the next thread when the current thread yields. However, for pre-emptive multitasking, interrupts need to be generated at regular intervals to decrement a time counter, and the OS must force a context switch once the counter reaches 0. This requires the OS to keep track of more things.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,5,'2025-07-30_11-48-00','This works, because both threads have a mechanism to switch contexts, even though the methods in which they do so are different. Both pre-emptive and time-based context switches call PendSV_Handler to switch contexts, however they trigger it differently. Pre-emptive context switching calls PendSV_Handler through osYield when the thread is done running. However time-based context switching occurs through SysTick_Handler which is called every 1ms. When the timer reaches 0, PendSV_Handler is called to switch contexts. In one of the threads, both pre-emptive and time-based context switching is enabled, however the timeslice used is large compared to the time it takes the thread to get to osYield, and so it yields before a time-based context switch can be made. In the other thread, it switches contexts after running for a certain time, and does not call osYield ever. It is possible for all this to work together, as it does not matter by which method a thread switches context, only that it does switch context eventually.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,5,'2025-07-30_13-50-44','This works, because both threads have a mechanism to switch contexts, even though the methods in which they do so are different. Both pre-emptive and time-based context switches call PendSV_Handler to switch contexts, however they trigger it differently. Pre-emptive context switching calls PendSV_Handler through osYield when the thread is done running. However time-based context switching occurs through SysTick_Handler which is called every 1ms. When the timer reaches 0, PendSV_Handler is called to switch contexts. In one of the threads, both pre-emptive and time-based context switching is enabled, however the timeslice used is large compared to the time it takes the thread to get to osYield, and so it yields before a time-based context switch can be made. In the other thread, it switches contexts after running for a certain time, and does not call osYield ever. It is possible for all this to work together, as it does not matter by which method a thread switches context, only that it does switch context eventually.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,5,'2025-07-30_14-25-14','This works, because both threads have a mechanism to switch contexts, even though the methods in which they do so are different. Both pre-emptive and time-based context switches call PendSV_Handler to switch contexts, however they trigger it differently. Pre-emptive context switching calls PendSV_Handler through osYield when the thread is done running. However time-based context switching occurs through SysTick_Handler which is called every 1ms. When the timer reaches 0, PendSV_Handler is called to switch contexts. In one of the threads, both pre-emptive and time-based context switching is enabled, however the timeslice used is large compared to the time it takes the thread to get to osYield, and so it yields before a time-based context switch can be made. In the other thread, it switches contexts after running for a certain time, and does not call osYield ever. It is possible for all this to work together, as it does not matter by which method a thread switches context, only that it does switch context eventually.\n',1,'s25'),('jsgort',5,5,'2025-07-30_15-26-26','This works, because both threads have a mechanism to switch contexts, even though the methods in which they do so are different. Both pre-emptive and time-based context switches call PendSV_Handler to switch contexts, however they trigger it differently. Pre-emptive context switching calls PendSV_Handler through osYield when the thread is done running. However time-based context switching occurs through SysTick_Handler which is called every 1ms. When the timer reaches 0, PendSV_Handler is called to switch contexts. In one of the threads, both pre-emptive and time-based context switching is enabled, however the timeslice used is large compared to the time it takes the thread to get to osYield, and so it yields before a time-based context switch can be made. In the other thread, it switches contexts after running for a certain time, and does not call osYield ever. It is possible for all this to work together, as it does not matter by which method a thread switches context, only that it does switch context eventually.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',1,1,'2025-06-25_23-42-39','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) provides a standardized interface between the application code and the hardware of the system. It is used for general embedded projects because a lot of setup code is needed for an embedded program, so tools like HAL are used to make that easier.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',1,2,'2025-06-25_23-42-39','The __io_putchar function handles the transmission of individual characters to a specific hardware output, typically UART in embedded systems. Since printf is part of the standard C library and doesn’t inherently know how to interface with hardware, __io_putchar acts as a bridge, directing each character from printf to the appropriate peripheral. In this lab, it\'s used to send output to a serial console via UART. If we wanted to use a different output like USB or an LCD, we would modify __io_putchar accordingly. This function is essential for redirecting standard library output to hardware-specific interfaces in embedded applications.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',1,3,'2025-06-25_23-42-39','I used the debugger because printf can’t access or show low-level processor details like the stack pointer values (MSP, PSP) in real-time. Debuggers provide direct access to processor registers and memory, which is necessary for verifying correct stack behaviour. printf is limited to user-defined output and doesn’t give insight into internal CPU states, which is why it’s insufficient for this kind of debugging.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',1,4,'2025-06-25_23-42-39','The stack allocation method assumes a fixed block of memory reserved for stacks. We begin with MSP_INIT_VAL, the base address of the main stack. To set up the process stack (PSP), we subtract a known offset (e.g., 0x400 bytes) from the main stack’s starting point. This works because stacks grow downward in memory, so moving to a lower address allocates a new stack. Knowing MSP_INIT_VAL is critical to ensure the PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area and doesn’t intrude on system-reserved regions. This separation is necessary for safe multitasking and avoids stack corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',1,5,'2025-06-25_23-42-39','To allocate stacks for multiple threads, we treat the stack region as a block of memory split into fixed-size segments. Starting from MSP_INIT_VAL, we add the size of the main stack (MAIN_STACK_SIZE) and then assign each thread a stack of size THREAD_STACK_SIZE. The formula for the start of the j-th thread’s stack is:\nADDRESS_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) × THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nAll stack sizes must be multiples of 8 for proper alignment, as required by the Cortex-M4. We also need to make sure the total allocated stack memory doesn’t exceed the available RAM. This setup ensures that each thread gets a dedicated, aligned, non-overlapping stack region.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',2,1,'2025-06-27_15-18-02','By assigning the address of print_continuously to the PC, I forced the CPU to start executing that function directly. This was done by placing the function’s address into register R0, then using inline assembly to move R0 into PC. Since PC controls the instruction flow, the processor immediately jumped to the print_continuously function. This mimics a function call, but instead of using call instructions, we redirected execution manually at the hardware level. The result was that the CPU entered an infinite loop, printing \"Hello, PC!\" continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',2,2,'2025-06-27_15-18-02','A system call is a controlled interface that allows user programs to request services from the operating system, such as file I/O, memory allocation, or process control. It triggers a software interrupt (using the SVC instruction), which switches the CPU from user mode to kernel mode, ensuring that privileged operations are executed safely. This mechanism prevents direct access to critical system resources, maintaining stability and security. For example, instead of writing directly to hardware, a program uses a system call like write() to let the OS handle the operation correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',2,3,'2025-06-27_15-18-02','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing the memory to simulate a state as if the thread had already been running. This involves manually pushing values like the xPSR, PC (the function address), LR, and dummy values for R0–R12 onto a new stack. This stack is then assigned to the PSP. When the thread starts, the CPU pops these values into the hardware registers, allowing the thread to begin execution properly. This setup is essential for context switching, where the OS needs to save and restore thread states. If the values are pushed in the wrong order, the CPU would restore incorrect data into the wrong registers, causing crashes or undefined behavior. The exact stack layout is critical for correct execution.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',2,4,'2025-06-27_15-18-02','If we don\'t set the PSP using __set_PSP, the CPU has no valid stack to use for the thread. The PSP must point to the thread\'s stack, which holds the saved context including PC, xPSR, and other registers. Without this, when the CPU attempts to restore the thread’s context, it reads from an invalid or uninitialized memory location, leading to a hard fault. The fault occurs because the processor can’t safely access or interpret garbage memory as valid instruction data or register values.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',2,5,'2025-06-27_15-18-02','If the thread function returned after executing once, the CPU would attempt to jump to the address stored in the Link Register (LR). Since LR was set to an arbitrary placeholder during setup, it wouldn’t contain a valid return address. As a result, the CPU would likely branch to an invalid or restricted memory region, triggering a hard fault. This demonstrates the importance of ensuring either that the thread never returns, or that the LR points to a valid location for proper control flow.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',3,1,'2025-06-27_16-58-21','Multiple .c files are compiled through three main stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, each .c file is preprocessed to expand macros, include headers, and remove comments, resulting in pure source code. Next, each file is compiled individually into object files, where the compiler checks syntax and optimizes the code. Finally, the linker combines all the object files into one executable, resolving references between functions and variables across files, and linking in necessary libraries like the C standard library.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',3,2,'2025-06-27_16-58-21','#include is intended for header files, not source files. Including a .c file literally copies its entire content into another source file before compilation. This leads to multiple definitions of the same code, causing duplicate symbol errors during linking. It also breaks proper code modularity and complicates the build process.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',3,3,'2025-06-27_16-58-21','Initialize the kernel to set up internal data structures and reserve stack space for threads.\nWrite the thread function containing the code you want the thread to run; it should have a void return type and no arguments.\nCreate the thread by passing the thread function’s address to a kernel thread creation function, which sets up the thread’s stack and context.\nStart the kernel, which begins executing the thread by loading its stack pointer and jumping to its function.\nThe thread then runs independently from the main program.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',3,4,'2025-06-27_16-58-21','The stack pool is a reserved memory area from which the OS allocates individual stacks for each thread. Each thread needs its own stack to store local variables, return addresses, and CPU state. The stack pool enables controlled and organized allocation of these stacks, ensuring thread isolation and proper memory management, which is essential for running multiple threads concurrently.\n',0,'s25'),('jstadnyk',3,5,'2025-06-27_16-58-21','Kernel files (e.g., kernel.c, kernel.h) contain low-level OS functions like thread management, stack setup, and system calls. User files (e.g., main.c) hold the application logic, including thread functions and calls to kernel APIs. This separation matters because it abstracts complexity from the user. The user does not need to modify kernel internals and can safely rely on the kernel to manage hardware and core operations, which improves modularity, security, and ease of development.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',4,1,'2025-07-30_11-00-35','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented with a queue by treating the queue itself as the ready list: when a task finishes its time slice, its Task Control Block (TCB) is removed from the head of the queue, appended to the tail (unless it has blocked), and the scheduler simply runs the task now at the head. Using a queue instead of a fixed array means you do not waste time searching for the next runnable slot or compacting gaps left by blocked tasks, so insertion and removal remain constant-time operations. It also makes it easy to extend the design—for example, you can maintain multiple queues of different priorities and still keep the core scheduler logic simple and fair.\n',0,'s25'),('jstadnyk',4,1,'2025-07-30_16-37-23','In the lab’s current implementation we already achieve a “queue-like” effect by storing TCBs in an array and advancing a circular index; this behaves as a logical FIFO without actually moving any data. Replacing that implicit queue with an explicit linked or circular queue would change little in terms of scheduling behaviour, but it could simplify the code that removes or re-inserts tasks when they block, because you would physically detach and re-enqueue the corresponding TCB node instead of leaving holes in the array. The trade-off is overhead: queue operations incur pointer dereferencing and, on small MCUs, a few extra bytes per node for next-pointers, whereas an array plus index costs only arithmetic on contiguous memory that is cache-friendly. Whenever the set of ready tasks is small and mostly stable—as in this lab—the array solution is just as fast and simpler; a real queue starts to pay off only when tasks frequently block, unblock, or change priority and you need O(1) insert/remove without scanning for empty slots.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',4,2,'2025-07-30_11-00-35','When PendSV_Handler runs, the processor has already pushed the volatile registers onto the current task’s stack, and the handler (or a C helper such as osSched) immediately pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 so that every register context is preserved. It then records the resulting process-stack pointer in the outgoing task’s TCB, chooses the next ready TCB according to the scheduler’s policy, loads that task’s saved stack pointer into the PSP register, and pops R4–R11 from the new task’s stack. Finally, when the handler returns, the CPU automatically pops the rest of the saved context, restoring PC and xPSR, so execution resumes inside the newly selected task as though it had never been interrupted.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',4,2,'2025-07-30_16-37-23','When PendSV_Handler runs, the processor has already pushed the volatile registers onto the current task’s stack, and the handler (or a C helper such as osSched) immediately pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 so that every register context is preserved. It then records the resulting process-stack pointer in the outgoing task’s TCB, chooses the next ready TCB according to the scheduler’s policy, loads that task’s saved stack pointer into the PSP register, and pops R4–R11 from the new task’s stack. Finally, when the handler returns, the CPU automatically pops the rest of the saved context, restoring PC and xPSR, so execution resumes inside the newly selected task as though it had never been interrupted.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',4,3,'2025-07-30_11-00-35','If the scheduler always selects the highest numeric priority, a thread of very high priority that never blocks or yields can monopolize the CPU and permanently starve every lower-priority thread. Such starvation is especially dangerous in embedded systems where background maintenance or watchdog tasks must run periodically. In addition, strict priority scheduling magnifies priority-inversion problems: a low-priority task that owns a shared resource can prevent a high-priority task from making progress if one or more medium-priority tasks keep pre-empting the low-priority one before it can release the resource.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',4,3,'2025-07-30_16-37-23','If the scheduler always selects the highest numeric priority, a thread of very high priority that never blocks or yields can monopolize the CPU and permanently starve every lower-priority thread. Such starvation is especially dangerous in embedded systems where background maintenance or watchdog tasks must run periodically. In addition, strict priority scheduling magnifies priority-inversion problems: a low-priority task that owns a shared resource can prevent a high-priority task from making progress if one or more medium-priority tasks keep pre-empting the low-priority one before it can release the resource.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',4,4,'2025-07-30_11-00-35','Handing control from the minimal assembly wrapper to C helper functions makes the implementation far easier to read, maintain, and port, because complex list manipulations and state updates are expressed in a high-level language instead of cryptic assembly. The same C scheduler can also be reused by different interrupt handlers or even on a different Cortex-M part without rewriting it. The cost is a few extra cycles for the C function prologue and epilogue and a strict dependence on the compiler’s calling convention—if the ABI changes or the handler fails to preserve required registers, the context switch will break—yet, for most microcontroller applications, the clarity and portability gains outweigh the small performance penalty.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',4,4,'2025-07-30_16-37-23','Handing control from the minimal assembly wrapper to C helper functions makes the implementation far easier to read, maintain, and port, because complex list manipulations and state updates are expressed in a high-level language instead of cryptic assembly. The same C scheduler can also be reused by different interrupt handlers or even on a different Cortex-M part without rewriting it. The cost is a few extra cycles for the C function prologue and epilogue and a strict dependence on the compiler’s calling convention—if the ABI changes or the handler fails to preserve required registers, the context switch will break—yet, for most microcontroller applications, the clarity and portability gains outweigh the small performance penalty.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',4,5,'2025-07-30_11-00-35','If the operating system is started with only a single thread—for example, a modified print_continuously task that calls osYield after every print—the OS will still function. The first SVC launches that lone thread, and each time it yields the scheduler runs but finds that the ready list contains only the same TCB, so it saves that task’s context, immediately chooses the same task again, restores the identical context, and continues printing. The system therefore incurs some unnecessary context-switch overhead but remains fully operational, because round-robin scheduling gracefully degenerates to executing a single runnable thread if no others exist.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',4,5,'2025-07-30_16-37-23','If the operating system is started with only a single thread—for example, a modified print_continuously task that calls osYield after every print—the OS will still function. The first SVC launches that lone thread, and each time it yields the scheduler runs but finds that the ready list contains only the same TCB, so it saves that task’s context, immediately chooses the same task again, restores the identical context, and continues printing. The system therefore incurs some unnecessary context-switch overhead but remains fully operational, because round-robin scheduling gracefully degenerates to executing a single runnable thread if no others exist.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',5,1,'2025-07-30_16-31-59','To give a thread an argument, the application code first allocates or populates some data (perhaps a simple integer or a whole struct) and passes the address of that data into osCreateThread. Inside that call the kernel builds the thread’s initial stack frame: among other saved registers it writes the pointer value into R0, the register the ABI uses for a function’s first parameter . Because every user thread is written with the signature void thread(void* args), the value that appears in R0 when the CPU eventually pops that frame is exactly the args pointer the user expected . Once the scheduler selects the thread, PendSV restores the registers; the function begins executing with R0 already loaded, so a simple cast gives the user code immediate access to its data. The whole scheme works because ARM’s procedure-call standard guarantees that the first argument lives in R0 and because the kernel faithfully recreated the stack layout the hardware expects.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',5,2,'2025-07-30_16-31-59','osYield triggers an SVC exception so that a thread can voluntarily hand control to the scheduler. Calling it from PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler would mean starting a second context switch while one is already in progress and while the processor is still in Handler mode. At that moment the thread’s stack frame is only half-saved, kernel data structures may be in an inconsistent state, and interrupts are masked. A nested yield therefore risks corrupting the stack, losing register contents, or dead-locking the scheduler; on most Cortex-M parts it will actually fault because SVC cannot be executed from another exception context. In short, yielding from inside any of the context-switch pathways breaks the atomicity the kernel relies on and can crash the system.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',5,3,'2025-07-30_16-31-59','Moving the fundamental OS tick from 1 ms to 0.1 ms would give ten-times-finer timing resolution. Tasks that sleep or time-out could react about 900 µs sooner, and deadlines could be scheduled with correspondingly tighter granularity. The price is that the SysTick interrupt would now fire 10 000 times per second instead of 1 000. Every tick forces an interrupt entry, an update of software timers, and often a context switch, so CPU time lost to overhead and power consumption both rise sharply. With very short computational bursts the processor may spend more time bookkeeping than doing useful work, and some peripherals (like UART configured by the HAL) might need retuning. The lab notes already warn that at 1 ms “our OS can never respond to events faster than 1 ms” and that this interval sets the resolution of all timing services ; shrinking the tick improves that resolution but at a real efficiency cost.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',5,4,'2025-07-30_16-31-59','In co-operative multitasking a thread keeps the processor until it explicitly calls osYield; from the programmer’s point of view the code runs uninterrupted except at those deliberate yield points. The kernel is therefore simple: it only needs to save context when SVC fires and then pick the next ready thread. In pre-emptive multitasking the user may write the same code, but the OS can pre-empt it after its time slice expires; no yield call is required, though it is still allowed . To make that possible the kernel arms SysTick, tracks a per-thread runtime counter, and, in its interrupt handler, decrements that counter and triggers PendSV when it reaches zero. Thus the user gains responsiveness without adding yield calls, while the OS gains the responsibility of handling a periodic interrupt and more complex scheduling rules.\n',1,'s25'),('jstadnyk',5,5,'2025-07-30_16-31-59','Both mechanisms coexist because they converge on the same context-switch pathway. When a running thread voluntarily calls osYield, the kernel resets that thread’s remaining runtime to its original time-slice and invokes PendSV; when the SysTick interrupt notices the runtime has fallen to zero, it likewise reloads the counter and invokes PendSV. The decision to switch is different, but the code that actually saves registers, updates the ready queue, and restores the next thread is shared. The lab instructions capture this unity by noting that if a thread yields, the runtime must be reset before the normal switch proceeds . Because both paths meet at the same scheduler, voluntary and forced context switches interleave smoothly, letting co-operative tasks and pre-emptive tasks run together.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',1,1,'2025-05-20_14-59-55','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a layer containing functions to help communicate with the hardware, in this case, an STM32 microcontroller. First, to understand why it’s used in embedded projects, we must understand the basics of an embedded system. Embedded projects are projects that require writing code or instructions for a microcontroller to perform a specific function. Using HAL functions include setting GPIO pins or initializing UART like HAL_UART_Init(&huart) as seen in this lab. Essentially, it is used to bypass the user from low-level programming, which involves assigning and locating memory, registers, and assemblies. Through HAL, it makes coding easier through accessible functions.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',1,1,'2025-05-27_13-03-23','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer\nIt does the hardwork so we don’t have to write low-level code\nIt’s like a library that contains all the C functions provided by STM\nEmbedded projects where you are communicating with electronics essentially through signals, you don’t have to write low-level programming\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',1,1,'2025-05-27_16-49-07','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a layer containing functions to help communicate with the hardware, in this case, an STM32 microcontroller. First, to understand why it’s used in embedded projects, we must understand the basics of an embedded system. Embedded projects are projects that require writing code or instructions for a microcontroller to perform a specific function. Using HAL functions include setting GPIO pins or initializing UART like HAL_UART_Init(&huart) as seen in this lab. Essentially, it is used to bypass the user from low-level programming, which involves assigning and locating memory, registers, and assemblies. Through HAL, it makes coding easier through accessible functions.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',1,2,'2025-05-20_14-59-55','__io_putchar is a function that directs a signal to an appropriate IO channel, directing how characters are being sent – in this case, through UART2 to STM32 board. It is similar to the printf function, but rather than simply printing it out on a screen, each character must be sent to the microcontroller. In this lab setup, when we use printf, it repeatedly calls the __io_putchar() function.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',1,2,'2025-05-27_13-03-23','__io_putchar is a function that directs a singal to an appropriate IO channel (this case is UART2)\nIt is put above the main function so that the printf function can repeatly call it when printing a string out which requires multiple characters to be written\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',1,2,'2025-05-27_16-49-07','__io_putchar is a function that directs a signal to an appropriate IO channel, directing how characters are being sent – in this case, through UART2 to STM32 board. It is similar to the printf function, but rather than simply printing it out on a screen, each character must be sent to the microcontroller. In this lab setup, when we use printf, it repeatedly calls the __io_putchar() function.\nThis function is necessary because the standard printf function doesn’t know how to send charaters to specific hardware, so __io_putchar acts as a hardware-specific wrapper that directs output to the correct communication channel, allowing printf to work on embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',1,3,'2025-05-20_14-59-55','A debugger is used to view a program while it’s running, with features like pausing, resuming, stepping into or over functions, and setting breakpoints. It allows the programmer to see the behaviour and flow of the code, rather than only its output. For example, when inspecting how memory is being changed, we often care about how and when the values change rather than the actual values itself. Using printf for this can be tedious and inefficient, especially when tracking function calls or value changes. This can result in cluttered code and make it more prone to error in larger-scale projects. Therefore, debugging provides a clearer, faster, and more accurate way of pinpointing issues, while giving the programmer a better sense of the behaviour of its operation.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',1,3,'2025-05-27_13-03-23','',0,'s25'),('k52kim',1,3,'2025-05-27_16-49-07','A debugger is used to view a program while it’s running, with features like pausing, resuming, stepping into or over functions, and setting breakpoints. It allows the programmer to see the behaviour and flow of the code, rather than only its output. For example, when inspecting how memory is being changed, we often care about how and when the values change rather than the actual values itself. Using printf for this can be tedious and inefficient, especially when tracking function calls or value changes. This can result in cluttered code and make it more prone to error in larger-scale projects. Therefore, debugging provides a clearer, faster, and more accurate way of pinpointing issues, while giving the programmer a better sense of the behaviour of its operation.\nObserving register values is especially important on the Cortex M4 because register hold important control and status information that directly affects the processor’s operation such as the current stack pointer or program counter. Unlike memory or code flow, which shows a broader program state, registers show immediate hardware context and essential for low-level programming.\nUsing printf in an embedded context is limiting due inhibiting factors such as UART transmission speed, which can disrupt real-time performance. This is why printf is impractical for detailed debugging where timing is critical. With a debugger, it provides direct, real-time access to registers, system state, without interfering with the operation.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',1,4,'2025-05-20_14-59-55','MSP_init_val stands for “Main Stack Pointer”. This points to the starting address of a region in the memory chip for stack allocation. The MSP_init_val starts at the top of the stack and grows downwards. By placing MSP at a higher memory address and growing downwards, it reduces the risk of overwriting other variables.\nPSP stands for “Process Stack Pointer”. Similarly, the stack grows downwards (into lower memory addresses). To declare a PSP means to declare a separate stack. We must know where MSP’s initial location is and define PSP relative to that address to ensure that these respective stack spaces do not overlap and get overwritten. It is important to have two stack pointers to put data until it reaches an upper limit before stack overflow occurs.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',1,4,'2025-05-27_13-03-23','',0,'s25'),('k52kim',1,4,'2025-05-27_16-49-07','MSP_init_val stands for “Main Stack Pointer”. This points to the starting address of a region in the memory chip for stack allocation. MSP_init_value is important to mark the upper boundary of the main stack’s memory usage. Without this, we can’t allocate a new stack below it. When we say PSP is defined relative to MSP, it means PSP is intentionally positioned as a fixed offset below MSP, not any random amount below it. This ensures predictable and safe memory allocation.\nPSP stands for “Process Stack Pointer”. Similarly, the stack grows downwards (into lower memory addresses). To calculate PSP_val, we subtract a specific offset from MSP_init_val to reserve a separate memory region for each stack to avoid overlap and ensure each stack has enough space to not collide with the main stack. When defining PSP, it is also important to ensure that it is placed within a valid memory range within stack operations.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',1,5,'2025-05-20_14-59-55','If we wanted to allocate new stacks for the use of several threads, we would need to separate the total amount of memory to be allocated for the number of stacks needed for multiple threads. Assuming that we know the total amount of memory and the threads needed, and that each stack is divided equally, the location of each stack will be determined by dividing the total memory by the number of threads. To add on, if we wanted to change memory to use a different chunk (stacks) of memory, we would need to change the stack pointer (such as MSP for the main stack or PSP for the processing stack) to its corresponding memory address.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',1,5,'2025-05-27_13-03-23','',0,'s25'),('k52kim',1,5,'2025-05-27_16-49-07','If we wanted to allocate new stacks for the use of several threads, we would need to separate the total amount of memory to be allocated for the number of stacks needed for multiple threads. Assuming that we know the total amount of memory and the threads needed, and that each stack is divided equally, the location of each stack will be determined by dividing the total memory by the number of threads. To add on, if we wanted to change memory to use a different chunk (stacks) of memory, we would need to change the stack pointer (such as MSP for the main stack or PSP for the processing stack) to its corresponding memory address.\nTo allocate multiple stacks, we start from a known valid top-of-memory address, like MSP_init_val and subtract a fixed offset for each thread based on a specific required stack size. For example, in this lab, we started with MSP_init_val = 0x20020000 and allocated a new stack region by subtracting fixed offsets of 0x400 bytes per stack. In the first PSP_val, we calculated 0x20020000 - 0x400 = 0x2001FC00, and so on. This offsetting ensures each stack is in a unique region. We also have to ensure that each stack’s starting address is aligned, typically to an 8-byte or 32-byte boundary on Cortex’s hardware requirements. Not doing this can cause faults or undefined behaviour. Each stack pointer for a thread (PSP values_ would be assigned to these calculated started addresses to manage thread-local stack usage safely\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',2,1,'2025-06-23_11-17-42','When we change the PC to hold the function “print_continuously()”, it repeatedly calls this function within the jumpAssembly function. What this does it moves the specific argument into a register R0 of the PC.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',2,1,'2025-06-25_22-29-53','When we changed the PC (Program Counter) to hold the address of the print_continuously() function, the processor began executing that function immediately. The PC is a special-purpose register that always holds the memory address of the next instruction to execute. Unlike general-purpose registers like R0, which can hold data or function arguments, the PC controls the execution flow of the program. In this lab, we passed the address of print_continuously into R0, and then used the assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 to copy that address into the PC. As a result, the processor began executing print_continuously() as if it had jumped to that point in the program. This is different from calling a function repeatedly — we didn’t return to the original code. Instead, execution transferred to the new address and remained there, continuously running the instructions inside print_continuously().\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',2,2,'2025-06-23_11-17-42','System calls are a way to interrupt the kernel to switch the CPU from user mode to kernel mode and run privileged operations by requesting a service from the operating kernel system. In this lab, this is specifically done with “__asm(“SVC #”)”. After it is triggered using the SVC assembly instruction, it creates an interrupt handled by the SVC_Handler, which is then processed by the function in SVC_Handler_Main.\nSystem calls are useful because they’re able to switch between user and kernel modes safely, manage processes by stopping, starting, or controlling other programs, allocate memory from the system, and allow it to communicate with various devices and programs.\nSystem calls can help load data into registers as part of setting up a thread’s context. System calls load data into registers to communicate between the user and kernel programs.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',2,2,'2025-06-25_22-29-53','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a program in user mode request a service from the operating system kernel, which runs in privileged mode. It acts as a controlled interface between user code and the kernel, allowing access to hardware or system resources that user programs can\'t access directly for safety and stability.\nIn this lab, we triggered system calls using the __asm(\"SVC #\") instruction. This raises a software interrupt, which causes the processor to switch from user mode to kernel mode and execute the SVC_Handler, eventually calling the SVC_Handler_Main() function in C to process the request based on the passed SVC number.\nSystem calls are essential for safely managing processes, allocating memory, controlling devices, and setting up threads. They also ensure that user programs cannot directly perform potentially harmful actions, as all privileged operations must go through this secure interface.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',2,3,'2025-06-23_11-17-42','To set up a thread’s stack, we prepare a special area in memory with “fake” register values that the thread will use. This is stored on the stack so when the thread starts, the chip loads them into its registers and run correctly. We place values in the thread stack in a specific order (xPSR, PC, LR, then other registers). This is done by moving the stack pointer down and storing these values. This prepares the chip to load data when the thread begins.\nIn this lab, the thread’s stack was set up with a PSP for the stack pointer. First, we created a new stack pointer to use for PSP, then set up the thread stack and information, then initiated system calls to load registers, then switch the stack pointer to PSP, then return.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',2,3,'2025-06-25_22-29-53','To set up a thread’s stack, we manually prepare a region of memory to simulate what the processor would expect if the thread had been running and was interrupted. This involves placing initial values for key registers onto the stack in a specific order: starting with xPSR, then the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and then the general-purpose registers. This setup is necessary because when the thread is started, the processor performs a context restore by popping these values off the stack into the actual CPU registers.\nIn this lab, we allocated a separate stack for the thread and set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the top of that prepared stack. By decrementing the stack pointer as we placed each register value, we ensured the memory layout matched the expectations of the hardware. The correct order and alignment are critical — if the registers are not stored properly, the CPU could load incorrect values and crash. After setting up the stack, we used a system call to switch the active stack pointer to PSP and transfer execution to the thread, allowing it to start with the correct context.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',2,4,'2025-06-23_11-17-42','Before running a thread, we set PSP to point to the thread’s prepared stack (stackptr). This tells the CPU where the find the thread’s saved register values so we can load them and start the thread. If we didn’t set PSP using __set_PSP, the CPU would not know where to get the thread’s context (the registered values) from. When it tries to load registers from an invalid or wrong memory location, it causes a hard fault, and crashes the system. Therefore, it is crucial so that the CPU can retrieve the correct saved data and state of the thread. Without PSP, the CPU may access incorrect memory.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',2,4,'2025-06-25_22-29-53','Before running a thread, we set PSP to point to the thread’s prepared stack (stackptr). This tells the CPU where the find the thread’s saved register values so we can load them and start the thread. If we didn’t set PSP using __set_PSP, the CPU would not know where to get the thread’s context (the registered values) from. When it tries to load registers from an invalid or wrong memory location, it causes a hard fault, and crashes the system. Therefore, it is crucial so that the CPU can retrieve the correct saved data and state of the thread. Without PSP, the CPU may access incorrect memory.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',2,5,'2025-06-23_11-17-42','If the thread function is printed once and returned, the thread would finish running, and there would be no instructions for the CPU to execute on that thread. Since we didn’t set up anything to handle this situation, the system would crash or behave unpredictably.\nOverall, when a thread ends without proper cleanup or switching to another thread, the CPU can try to run invalid instructions or access invalid memory, causing a hard fault, errors, and crashes. This is why generally, threads run loops or have a way to safetly stop.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',2,5,'2025-06-25_22-29-53','If the thread function printed something once and then returned, the system would likely crash. This is because, in a thread’s context, there is usually no valid return address set up on the stack — threads aren’t meant to return like normal function calls. When the thread ends and tries to return, the CPU would pop an invalid value into the Program Counter (PC) from the stack and attempt to jump to it. This often leads to a hard fault, as the CPU ends up executing invalid or non-existent instructions.\nIn an operating system, threads are typically expected to loop indefinitely or be explicitly terminated and cleaned up by the scheduler. Allowing a thread to return without that cleanup leads to unpredictable behaviour, memory corruption, or crashes. That’s why in this lab — and in real operating systems — thread functions usually loop or hand control back to the OS rather than trying to return.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',3,1,'2025-06-27_15-23-17','Using #include <kernel.c> or including any .c file directly is a bad practice because it disrupts the proper structure of the C build process. Each .c file is meant to be compiled separately into an object file, and then linked with other object files to create the final executable. Including a .c file in another source file causes the compiler to compile the same code multiple times, leading to linker errors such as multiple symbol definitions. This also eliminates modularity, making the codebase harder to manage and debug. Instead, developers should use header files (.h) to share declarations like function prototypes or struct definitions, and keep the actual implementations in .c files, which are compiled separately. This approach keeps the build process clean, avoids duplication, and supports better organization of large projects.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',3,1,'2025-06-27_18-23-12','When a C program with multiple .c files gets compiled, each file goes through a series of steps. First, in the preprocessing step, the compiler handles all #include directives, macros (#define), and conditional compilations (#ifdef/endif). Second, the compiler takes the preprocessed code and checks for syntax and type errors, then translates the C code into assembly instructions (MOV, LDR, STR), which are .o files. Each .c file is compiled separately this way, producing multiple object files. Lastly, a linker combines these object files into a single executable (.exe) and references/connects code in between. For example, when a function defined in one file is called by the other. This linking step can connect external libraries as well. Keeping .c files separate and letting the compiler and linker handle them is a way of keeping the code organized and ensures that the final .exe includes everything in a modular file so that the processor can run.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',3,2,'2025-06-27_15-23-17','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because this will cause the file you’re copying all of its code directly into another file before compilation. This can cause the same functions or variables to be compiled multiple times leading to multiple defintions. It makes the code messy and hard to manage, because you’re mixing implementation files together instead of keeping them separate and orgiized. The peroper way would be to #include “kernel.h” to share function headers and defintions. the .c file would be compiled separately and links at the end.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',3,2,'2025-06-27_18-23-12','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because this will cause the file you’re copying all of its code directly into another file before compilation. This can cause the same functions or variables to be compiled multiple times leading to multiple defintions. It makes the code messy and hard to manage, because you’re mixing implementation files together instead of keeping them separate and orgiized. The proper way would be to #include “kernel.h” to share function headers and definitions. the .c file would be compiled separately and links at the end.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',3,3,'2025-06-27_15-23-17','From the user’s perspective for setting up and starting a kernel involves a few steps. First, the kernel needs to be initialized (osKernelInitialize), which sets up the kernel’s internal data structures like assigning MSP_INIT_VAL (the address of the stack) and referencing it with another pointer (stackptr) to track the start of the stack pool. This makes sure that the OS is ready to manage threads. Second, the user can implement their thread function – this is similar to a regular C function that contains code to run a thread (in the lab, this was print_continuously). Third, the user must create a thread by calling osCreateThread. This function allocates the block of stack memory and sets up the thread’s context, storing information in the kernel’s structure. Lastly, the user must start the kernel by calling osKernelStart, which switches the system into thread and enter the kernel mode through a system call.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',3,3,'2025-06-27_18-23-12','From the user’s perspective for setting up and starting a kernel involves a few steps. First, the kernel needs to be initialized (osKernelInitialize), which sets up the kernel’s internal data structures like assigning MSP_INIT_VAL (the address of the stack) and referencing it with another pointer (stackptr) to track the start of the stack pool. This makes sure that the OS is ready to manage threads. Second, the user can implement their thread function – this is similar to a regular C function that contains code to run a thread (in the lab, this was print_continuously). Third, the user must create a thread by calling osCreateThread. This function allocates the block of stack memory and sets up the thread’s context, storing information in the kernel’s structure. Lastly, the user must start the kernel by calling osKernelStart, which switches the system into thread and enter the kernel mode through a system call.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',3,4,'2025-06-27_15-23-17','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory in the RAM specifically set aside for thread stacks. Each thread in the OS has its own separate stack to store information like local variables, return addresses, and register values. In lab 3, the MIN_STACK_SIZE determines that enough space is available for multiple threads. In the future lab, the stack pool can be divided into equal-sized chunks, with each thread having its own chunk. When a new thread is created, the OS can allocate this stack from an unused part of the pool and use a pointer to track this thread. Having the stack pool allows the system to manage multiple threads as each of them has its own designated stack space, preventing overlap and allowing proper switching for multitasks.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',3,4,'2025-06-27_18-23-12','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory in the RAM specifically set aside for thread stacks. Each thread in the OS has its own separate stack to store information like local variables, return addresses, and register values. In lab 3, the MIN_STACK_SIZE determines that enough space is available for multiple threads. In the future lab, the stack pool can be divided into equal-sized chunks, with each thread having its own chunk. When a new thread is created, the OS can allocate this stack from an unused part of the pool and use a pointer to track this thread. Having the stack pool allows the system to manage multiple threads as each of them has its own designated stack space, preventing overlap and allowing proper switching for multitasks.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',3,5,'2025-06-27_15-23-17','The distinction between “user” and “kernel” can be defined by their privileges. It is a way of separating code written by the user, such as threads, main(), or other functions, from the OS code that involves interrupt handling (e.g.,SVC_Handler_Main), system calls, thread management (such as allocation), system timers, and clocks. The kernel gives privilege to this OS logic to give an additional layer of security in case the user accidentally changes an important OS logic without understanding its impact. The kernel-related code goes into the kernel.c and kernel.h. The .c file holds the actual implementations while the .h contains definitions/headers. Kernels are a type of abstraction since the user doesn’t need to know how a kernel works internally, only how to call its functions. From the user’s perspective, this makes the system easier to understand and safer to use.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',3,5,'2025-06-27_18-23-12','The distinction between “user” and “kernel” is about separating what the application code does from how the OS system actually works. The “user” code includes main() function and any thread functions that go in main.c and uses kernel calls like osCreateThread() or osKernelStart() to request services from the OS and enter this privileged mode. The “kernel” code is composed of kernel.c and kernel.h files. It contains low-level logic that makes these services possible. Interrupt handlers like SVC_Handler_Main, system calls, stack management, and hardware timers are all examples of code in the kernel.\nA system call is a trigger that requests the system to switch from CPU user mode to privileged kernel mode to perform protected operations. From the user’s perspective, this separation is a type of abstraction, meaning the user doesn’t have to know exactly how context switching works but just be able to call its function. Furthermore, it keeps the user from accidentally changing a critical OS logic that can corrupt the system.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,1,'2025-07-18_00-27-29','In round-robin scheduling, using a queue to store TCBs instead of an array allows threads to be managed more simply. In a queue, the currently running thread is always at the front. When a thread yields, it is removed from the front of the queue and placed at the back, and then the next thread is scheduled to run. This method eliminates the need for index tracking, which would be required in arrays. It simplifies the logic of rotating between threads in multithreading. Furthermore, queues are easier to extend in more advanced systems where they may need to be dynamically allocated or removed. While using an array is sufficient and simpler for smaller systems like in this lab, a queue structure is better as it offers more flexibility and maintenance.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,1,'2025-07-22_20-21-11','In round-robin scheduling, using a queue to store TCBs instead of an array allows threads to be managed more simply. In a queue, the currently running thread is always at the front. When a thread yields, it is removed from the front of the queue and placed at the back, and then the next thread is scheduled to run. This method eliminates the need for index tracking, which would be required in arrays. It simplifies the logic of rotating between threads in multithreading. Furthermore, queues are easier to extend in more advanced systems where they may need to be dynamically allocated or removed. While using an array is sufficient and simpler for smaller systems like in this lab, a queue structure is better as it offers more flexibility and maintenance.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',4,1,'2025-07-22_21-13-25','In round-robin scheduling, using a queue to store TCBs instead of an array allows threads to be managed more simply. In a queue, the currently running thread is always at the front. When a thread yields, it is removed from the front of the queue and placed at the back, and then the next thread is scheduled to run. This is similar to how array-based schedulers work, which also cycles through threads in a fixed order. The current implementation is similar to a queue functionality. The queue method eliminates the need for index tracking, which would be required in arrays. It simplifies the logic of rotating between threads in multithreading. Furthermore, queues are easier to extend in more advanced systems where they may need to be dynamically allocated or removed. While using an array is sufficient and simpler for smaller systems like in this lab, a queue structure is better as it offers more flexibility and maintenance. However, implementing this would lead to more CPU overhead, especially for a resource-constrained microcontroller like the Cortex M4, since dynamic memory operations and pointers are more complex and CPU-intensive. Though queue can be more flexible, the array-based approach is simpler and it’s efficient when working with a smaller number of threads, such as this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,2,'2025-07-18_00-27-29','In the SVC_Handler_Main, two variables are put into the stack – a pointer and the SVC number. When a context switch occurs, this function never returns, as seen in the previous lab. Normally, a return statement removes local variables from the stack, but if this doesn’t happen, the stack continues to grow and eventually leads to memory overflow. This is why context switching cannot occur outside an interrupt – the Cortex-M architecture restricts the user from fully accessing certain registers in function calls. To work around this, PendSV is used. It is set to a lower priority than SVC, which means it only runs after all high-priority interrupts have been completed. Inside the SVC_Handler, PendSV is prepared to run so that once SVC finishes and the stack is restored, PendSV triggers and performs the actual context switch. Essentially, PendSV is an interrupt, but it is “pending” until the higher priority interrupt is done.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',4,2,'2025-07-22_20-21-11','The PendSV_Handler is responsible for performing context switches between threads in a cooperative multitasking system. When a thread calls osYield(), the SVC_Handler sets the PendSV interrupt as “pending”, but does not perform the context switch itself. It must finish the system call handling, then restore the slack, before the lower-priority PendSV_Handler takes over. After it’s triggered, the context switch begins by saving the current thread’s CPU state using PSP to access the stack and push registers R4 through R11. Then, the PendSV_Hander calls osSched() function, selecting the next thread to run based on the scheduling algorithm (round-robin in this lab). osSched() updates the global currentThread index and loads the next thread’s stack pointer into the PSP. Then, PendSV_Handler restores R4-R11 registers to continue where it last left off. The responsibilities between SVC_handler and PendSV_Handler separate the setup and actual switch. This is crucial as context switches must occur in an interrupt context, and the Cortex M architecture restricts access to only certain registers outside of interrupts. Using PendSV ensures that the stack remains consistent, and that context switching is done safely and efficiently.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,2,'2025-07-22_21-13-25','The PendSV_Handler is responsible for performing context switches between threads in a cooperative multitasking system. When a thread calls osYield(), the SVC_Handler sets the PendSV interrupt as “pending”, but does not perform the context switch itself. It must finish the system call handling, then restore the slack, before the lower-priority PendSV_Handler takes over. After it’s triggered, the context switch begins by saving the current thread’s CPU state using PSP to access the stack and push registers R4 through R11. Then, the PendSV_Hander calls osSched() function, selecting the next thread to run based on the scheduling algorithm (round-robin in this lab). osSched() updates the global currentThread index and loads the next thread’s stack pointer into the PSP. Then, PendSV_Handler restores R4-R11 registers to continue where it last left off. The responsibilities between SVC_handler and PendSV_Handler separate the setup and actual switch. This is crucial as context switches must occur in an interrupt context, and the Cortex M architecture restricts access to only certain registers outside of interrupts. Using PendSV ensures that the stack remains consistent, and that context switching is done safely and efficiently.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,3,'2025-07-18_00-27-29','If we modify the OS to always run the thread with the highest priority logic first, this may lead to a concept called “starvation,” which is seen in some scheduling algorithms. Starvation would mean that when there are multiple threads, the scheduler will choose the higher-priority processes. In the case that higher priorities continue to come in, the lower priority tasks may never get the chance to run are continuously waiting. This is unfair and can lead to lower priority tasks (such as background tasks or  IO handling) being completely ignored. This algorithm is different from the round-robin scheduling implemented in this lab, which ensures every thread eventually runs based on time slicing (a specific allocated time for each task). Thus, this is a bad idea as this algorithm can lead to unbalanced systems where only a selection of threads are running.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',4,3,'2025-07-22_20-21-11','Modifying the OS to always run the thread with the highest priority is not the best solution because it leads to issues like starvation. Starvation occurs when lower-priority threads are never given CPU time because higher-priority threads always take precedence. If there is a continuous influx of high-priority tasks, lower-priority tasks can be indefinitely postponed, even if they are ready and waiting. Even when new tasks aren’t being introduced, the scheduler will select threads with the highest priority and that one will only ever run. All other threads, even if they are important, will remain idle simply due to their priority. This is different from a round-robin scheduling, as implemented in the lab, which ensures fairness by giving each thread to run a turn to run, regardless of priority. Therefore, while priority-based scheduling can be useful, using it without additional safeguards – such as aging or time slicing – can make the system unfair, unbalanced, and even completely neglect lower priority tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,3,'2025-07-22_21-13-25','Modifying the OS to always run the thread with the highest priority is not the best solution because it leads to issues like starvation. Starvation occurs when lower-priority threads are never given CPU time because higher-priority threads always take precedence. If there is a continuous influx of high-priority tasks, lower-priority tasks can be indefinitely postponed, even if they are ready and waiting. Even when new tasks aren’t being introduced, the scheduler will select threads with the highest priority and that one will only ever run. All other threads, even if they are important, will remain idle simply due to their priority. This is different from a round-robin scheduling, as implemented in the lab, which ensures fairness by giving each thread to run a turn to run, regardless of priority. Therefore, while priority-based scheduling can be useful, using it without additional safeguards – such as aging or time slicing – can make the system unfair, unbalanced, and even completely neglect lower priority tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,4,'2025-07-18_00-27-29','In this lab, PendSV Handler calls osSched, and SVC_Handler calls SVC_Handler_Main. There are benefits and downsides to calling these C functions rather than writing everything in assembly.\nFirst, the advantage of C functions is its readability. Writing scheduling logic and system call handling in C code is a lot easier to understand, debug, and modify than assembly code for the user. As the OS gets more complicated, the C functions provide higher-level abstractions, better compiler support, and easier access to structs, arrays, and variables.\nNow, the disadvantages of calling a C function from an interrupt handler introduces more overhead. More registers are needed, stacks need to be properly implemented and processed, and calling them correctly. This leads to more instructions, code, and effort. Assembly code is efficient—it provides finer control over the CPU and is desired for applications that prioritize performance.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,4,'2025-07-22_20-21-11','In this lab, PendSV Handler calls osSched, and SVC_Handler calls SVC_Handler_Main. There are benefits and downsides to calling these C functions rather than writing everything in assembly.\nFirst, the advantage of C functions is its readability. Writing scheduling logic and system call handling in C code is a lot easier to understand, debug, and modify than assembly code for the user. As the OS gets more complicated, the C functions provide higher-level abstractions, better compiler support, and easier access to structs, arrays, and variables.\nNow, the disadvantages of calling a C function from an interrupt handler introduces more overhead. More registers are needed, stacks need to be properly implemented and processed, and calling them correctly. This leads to more instructions, code, and effort. Assembly code is efficient—it provides finer control over the CPU and is desired for applications that prioritize performance.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,4,'2025-07-22_21-13-25','In this lab, PendSV Handler calls osSched, and SVC_Handler calls SVC_Handler_Main. There are benefits and downsides to calling these C functions rather than writing everything in assembly.\nFirst, the advantage of C functions is its readability. Writing scheduling logic and system call handling in C code is a lot easier to understand, debug, and modify than assembly code for the user. As the OS gets more complicated, the C functions provide higher-level abstractions, better compiler support, and easier access to structs, arrays, and variables.\nNow, the disadvantages of calling a C function from an interrupt handler introduces more overhead. More registers are needed, stacks need to be properly implemented and processed, and calling them correctly. This leads to more instructions, code, and effort. Assembly code is efficient—it provides finer control over the CPU and is desired for applications that prioritize performance.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,5,'2025-07-18_00-27-29','Yes, this will work if only a single thread is used and osYield() is still called. However, as seen in previous labs, single threading doesn’t need functions like osYield() since there is no other thread to “yield” to. The purpose of osYield() is to have the scheduler save the current thread’s state and transition to the next thread to run. In a single-thread system, this is wrapped around the same thread. The context switch will still happen, but it will just be returned to itself, thus, unnecessary. Therefore, the logic will work (it will not crash), but it adds overhead as it is not useful in a single-thread system.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,5,'2025-07-22_20-21-11','Yes, this will work if only a single thread is used and osYield() is still called. However, as seen in previous labs, single threading doesn’t need functions like osYield() since there is no other thread to “yield” to. The purpose of osYield() is to have the scheduler save the current thread’s state and transition to the next thread to run. In a single-thread system, this is wrapped around the same thread. The context switch will still happen, but it will just be returned to itself, thus, unnecessary. Therefore, the logic will work (it will not crash), but it adds overhead as it is not useful in a single-thread system.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',4,5,'2025-07-22_21-13-25','Yes, this will work if only a single thread is used and osYield() is still called. However, as seen in previous labs, single threading doesn’t need functions like osYield() since there is no other thread to “yield” to. The purpose of osYield() is to have the scheduler save the current thread’s state and transition to the next thread to run. In a single-thread system, this is wrapped around the same thread. The context switch will still happen, but it will just be returned to itself, thus, unnecessary. Therefore, the logic will work (it will not crash), but it adds overhead as it is not useful in a single-thread system.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',5,1,'2025-07-22_21-07-51','To pass an argument through a thread, the user must define the thread function that accepts the void* parameter. The user is allowed to pass any data type by casting it into a pointer, typically wrapping multiple values into structs if needed. Then, the user must prepare the argument by declaring it as an integer value or struct, and pass it through the thread creation function – this is stored in the R0 register through the thread’s stack initialization. When the thread is later scheduled and executing, the CPU will pop R0 from the stack and place it into the function’s input, passing the argument to the thread function. In Cortex-M4 architecture, the function’s arguments are placed in R0-R3 and by preloading R0 with the desired pointer during the setup, the thread receives the argument like it were a normal function call.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',5,1,'2025-07-23_00-53-44','To pass an argument through a thread, the user must define the thread function that accepts the void* parameter. The user is allowed to pass any data type by casting it into a pointer, typically wrapping multiple values into structs if needed. Then, the user must prepare the argument by declaring it as an integer value or struct, and pass it through the thread creation function – this is stored in the R0 register through the thread’s stack initialization. When the thread is later scheduled and executing, the CPU will pop R0 from the stack and place it into the function’s input, passing the argument to the thread function. In Cortex-M4 architecture, the function’s arguments are placed in R0-R3 and by preloading R0 with the desired pointer during the setup, the thread receives the argument like it were a normal function call.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',5,2,'2025-07-22_21-07-51','Calling osYield inside any context switching functions (PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, SysTick_Handler) is not possible because these functions are already a part of the context-switching mechanism and are executed in handler mode. Calling osYield within them would lead to nested context switches and recursive/endless triggering of the PendSV, which can corrupt the stack or cause undefined behaviour due to improper saving and restoration of thread states. Furthermore, osYield triggers PendSV explicitly, so calling it within a handler that’s already in the middle of a context switch leads to redundant, overlapping switches. This can lead to destabilizing the OS and crashing.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',5,2,'2025-07-23_00-53-44','Calling osYield inside any context switching functions (PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, SysTick_Handler) is not allowed because these functions are already a part of the context-switching mechanism and are executed in handler mode. osYield is designed to be a system call that triggers a context switch by setting the PendSV interrupt, which is meant to occur from thread mode, not from within another interrupt. Calling osYield within them would lead to nested context switches and recursive triggering of the PendSV, which can corrupt the stack or cause undefined behaviour due to improper saving and restoration of thread states. Since system calls rely on returning cleanly to the thread mode, making one from within an interrupt can violate this assumption and lead to a hard fault. Furthermore, osYield triggers PendSV explicitly, so calling it within a handler that’s already in the middle of a context switch leads to redundant, overlapping switches. This can lead to destabilizing the OS and crashing.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',5,3,'2025-07-22_21-07-51','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1ms instead of 1ms would increase the timing resolution of the system. This would allow the OS to respond more quickly to time-based events and allow for fine-grained scheduling and delays. It would be useful in applications where high precision or reaction times are prioritized, such as real-time signal processing. However, the downsides of this is that a faster clock increases the frequency of SysTick_Handler interrupts, leading to more CPU overhead and more context switches and interrupt handling. This reduces the time for actual thread execution, increases power consumption, and can cause timing instability. In extreme cases, the OS may spend more time running interrupts than running useful tasks, which degrades its performance. Therefore, with higher resolution improving its responsiveness, there is a trade-off of overhead and power.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',5,3,'2025-07-23_00-53-44','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1ms instead of 1ms would increase the timing resolution of the system. This would allow the OS to respond more quickly to time-based events and allow for fine-grained scheduling and delays. It would be useful in applications where high precision or reaction times are prioritized, such as real-time signal processing. However, the downsides of this is that a faster clock increases the frequency of SysTick_Handler interrupts, leading to more CPU overhead and more context switches and interrupt handling. This reduces the time for actual thread execution, increases power consumption, and can cause timing instability. In extreme cases, the OS may spend more time running interrupts than running useful tasks, which degrades its performance. Therefore, with higher resolution improving its responsiveness, there is a trade-off of overhead and power.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',5,4,'2025-07-22_21-07-51','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily yield control back to the OS using a function like osYield in this lab’s example. This means that the user designs their threads to share the CPU’s time fairly. The downside is, if one thread forgets to yield or runs past the time slice, it will block all. On the other hand, pre-emptive multitasking doesn’t rely on the user to yield – threads are interrupted automatically by the OS based on a timer (SysTick) as soon as its called, and control is forcibly switched after a certain time slice, regardless of what it was doing.\nFrom an OS perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to implement. The OS performs the context switch after osYield is called, meaning less consideration for timing constraints and definitions. Pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to manage time tracking, interrupt handling, and forced context switching. This involves SysTick to track remaining runtime of each thread, calling PendSV interrupt when thread’s time slice is finished, and more. However, though it is more complex, pre-emptive multitasking offers better control and fairness overall.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',5,4,'2025-07-23_00-53-44','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily yield control back to the OS using a function like osYield in this lab’s example. This means that the user designs their threads to share the CPU’s time fairly. The downside is, if one thread forgets to yield or runs past the time slice, it will block all. On the other hand, pre-emptive multitasking doesn’t rely on the user to yield – threads are interrupted automatically by the OS based on a timer (SysTick) as soon as its called, and control is forcibly switched after a certain time slice, regardless of what it was doing.\nFrom an OS perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to implement. The OS performs the context switch after osYield is called, meaning less consideration for timing constraints and definitions. Pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to manage time tracking, interrupt handling, and forced context switching. This involves SysTick to track remaining runtime of each thread, calling PendSV interrupt when thread’s time slice is finished, and more. However, though it is more complex, pre-emptive multitasking offers better control and fairness overall.\n',1,'s25'),('k52kim',5,5,'2025-07-22_21-07-51','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switches can work together because they trigger the same context switch mechanism by setting the PendSV interrupt. The difference in these two is how and when the context switch occurs. In cooperative multitasking, the user calls osYield, resetting the thread’s runtime, and a voluntary context switch happens. In pre-emptive multitasking, the SysTick timer interrupt decrements the thread’s runtime every millisecond and once it reaches zero (or the task finishes), the OS resets runtime and triggers a switch automatically. Though different, these two mechanisms are compatible. The OS tracks runtime and handles timer-based pre-emption, but it also allows user threads to yield or timeout; thus, both approaches can coexist and still ensure fair scheduling. This model will give users flexibility while still maintaining the system’s responsiveness.\n',0,'s25'),('k52kim',5,5,'2025-07-23_00-53-44','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switches can work together because they both trigger the same context switch mechanism by setting the PendSV interrupt. The difference between them is in how and when the context switch is triggered. In cooperative multitasking, the user calls osYield, which resets the thread’s runtime and voluntarily triggers PendSV. In pre-emptive multitasking, the SysTick timer interrupt decrements the thread’s runtime every millisecond, and once it reaches zero, the OS resets the runtime and also triggers PendSV. In both cases, the actual context switch is performed by the same PendSV handler code, which saves the current thread’s context and restores the next thread’s context. PendSV is reused, and no matter how the switch is triggered, the logic is the same. Therefore, both mechanisms are compatible and can coexist, allowing the OS to manage thread scheduling fairly while giving users flexibility and maintaining system responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',1,1,'2025-06-25_23-37-45','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a set of standardized functions and APIs that let us communicate with hardware, such as an STM32 board, without needing to write low-level code. This helps make it faster to create and develop embedded applications and tools. It is also useful because instead of writing complex code to set bits in registers, I can use simple high-level HAL function calls to control LEDs and buttons, configure timers or even communicate over UART. This saves a lot of time and makes the code very readable and can work with similar microcontrollers with limited modifications.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',1,1,'2025-06-26_00-12-22','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a set of standardized functions and APIs that let us communicate with hardware, such as an STM32 board, without needing to write low-level code. This helps make it faster to create and develop embedded applications and tools. It is also useful because instead of writing complex code to set bits in registers, I can use simple high-level HAL function calls to control LEDs and buttons, configure timers or even communicate over UART. This saves a lot of time and makes the code very readable and can work with similar microcontrollers with limited modifications.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',1,2,'2025-06-25_23-37-45','The _io_putchar function tells the program how to send out one character by connecting the standard C library to the microcontroller’s output hardware. In this case, it was using a UART serial connection, however, since this function is hardware dependent, the function would need to be rewritten and modified to match the new peripheral system. Whenever using the printf function in code, the io_putchar function takes the message and breaks it into individual characters, sending each one. Without this, printf function would not be able to actually display anything in the console.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',1,2,'2025-06-26_00-12-22','The _io_putchar function tells the program how to send out one character by connecting the standard C library to the microcontroller’s output hardware. In this case, it was using a UART serial connection, however, since this function is hardware dependent, the function would need to be rewritten and modified to match the new peripheral system. Whenever using the printf function in code, the io_putchar function takes the message and breaks it into individual characters, sending each one. Without this, printf function would not be able to actually display anything in the console.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',1,3,'2025-06-25_23-37-45','We couldn’t use the printf right away because the microcontroller hardware needs to be setup beforehand. Since in this lab, we were testing code that runs before the hardware is setup, the debugger lets us essentially pause the program and look at the memory to check each register values to see the code’s behaviour without there being an output. Debugging is the only tool that allows us to do this such a low level.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',1,3,'2025-06-26_00-12-22','We couldn’t use the printf right away because the microcontroller hardware needs to be setup beforehand. Since in this lab, we were testing code that runs before the hardware is setup, the debugger lets us essentially pause the program and look at the memory to check each register values to see the code’s behaviour without there being an output. Debugging is the only tool that allows us to do this such a low level.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',1,4,'2025-06-25_23-37-45','Every program needs memory for a stack because the stack stores things like local variables and function calls. The system defaults to using a MSP and since we assume to already have obtained that value, in order to make a second stack (PSP), we need to take the MSP value and subtract some memory space from it. Now this empty space is for the PSP to use. We needed to know the initial location of MSP so we don’t accidentally overwrite or overlap the stack. By having the initial MSP location, it ensures that each stack is safe in the memory and won’t be wiped out.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',1,4,'2025-06-26_00-12-22','Every program needs memory for a stack because the stack stores things like local variables and function calls. The system defaults to using a MSP and since we assume to already have obtained that value, in order to make a second stack (PSP), we need to take the MSP value and subtract some memory space from it. Now this empty space is for the PSP to use. We needed to know the initial location of MSP so we don’t accidentally overwrite or overlap the stack. By having the initial MSP location, it ensures that each stack is safe in the memory and won’t be wiped out.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',1,5,'2025-06-25_23-37-45','If we want to make multiple stacks for multiple threads, we would need to allocate a block of memory for each one. Since this number is known at compile time, we can calculate where each stack starts. We would take the initial MSP value and then subtract the size of the stack for the number of stacks that we need. This way, each stack would have enough of its own space in memory and we would know the exact location of where each one starts.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',1,5,'2025-06-26_00-12-22','If we want to make multiple stacks for multiple threads, we would need to allocate a separate block of memory for each one. Since the number of stacks is known at compile time, we can pre-calculate where each stack starts by subtracting fixed offsets from a known reference point, such as the initial MSP value. Each offset must account for the size of a stack and ensure proper memory alignment to avoid faults or undefined behavior. It’s also important to reserve a large enough memory region ahead of time to fit all the stacks without overlapping with each other or other data. By doing this, we can ensure that each stack has its own dedicated space in memory, and we know exactly where each one begins.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,1,'2025-06-27_16-52-43','When we changed the PC (Program Counter) to hold our print_continuously function, we essentially got the processor to start executing and performing instructions from that function. Since the PC decides and controls which instructions run next, making this change caused it to jump directly to the thread code instead of through the main program. This allowed the core to follow the thread’s instructions and the function repeated the output as expected.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',2,1,'2025-06-27_19-26-01','When we changed the PC (Program Counter) to hold our print_continuously function, we essentially got the processor to start immediately executing and performing instructions from that function. Since the PC decides and controls which instructions run next, as soon as we set it to the address of our thread function, control is transferred without delay. This causes the CPU to jump directly to the thread code instead of through the main program, allowing it to follow the thread’s instructions right away and repeat the output as expected.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,1,'2025-06-27_19-51-52','When we changed the PC (Program Counter) to hold our print_continuously function, we essentially got the processor to start immediately executing and performing instructions from that function. Since the PC decides and controls which instructions run next, as soon as we set it to the address of our thread function, control is transferred without delay. This causes the CPU to jump directly to the thread code instead of through the main program, allowing it to follow the thread’s instructions right away and repeat the output as expected.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,2,'2025-06-27_16-52-43','A system call is a way for a program that is running in the user mode to request a service from the operating system or kernel using privileged information in a private/privileged mode. This is usually done using a supervisor call (SVC) instruction that triggers an exception and switches to handler code so it can safely perform takss like accessing hardware or starting a new thread. System calls help keep user code separate from sensitive system operations to ensure safety and control over the entire system.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',2,2,'2025-06-27_19-26-01','A system call is a specific type of trap that allows a program that is running in the user mode to request a service from the operating system or kernel using privileged information in a private/privileged mode. This is usually done using a supervisor call (SVC) instruction that triggers an exception and switches to handler code so it can safely perform tasks like accessing hardware or starting a new thread. System calls are important because they protect access to hardware and critical system resources. Instead of letting untrusted or incorrect user code access hardware directly, they provide a safe way to perform privileged operations through a safe and secure interface.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',2,2,'2025-06-27_19-51-52','A system call is a specific type of trap and function call that allows a program that is running in the user mode to request a service from the operating system or kernel using privileged information in a private/privileged mode. This is usually done using a supervisor call (SVC) instruction that triggers an exception and switches to handler code so it can safely perform tasks like accessing hardware or starting a new thread. System calls are important because they protect access to hardware and critical system resources. Instead of letting untrusted or incorrect user code access hardware directly, they provide a safe way to perform privileged operations through a safe and secure interface.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,3,'2025-06-27_16-52-43','Setting up a thread stack is creating a block of memory that holds the initial values that CPU needs to start running the threads. This includes the PC, Link registers and general registers. We need to place these in the correct order on the stack to mimic the CPU’s expectation when it returns from an exception. Storing these calues is important because when the CPU switches to the thread, it can essentially “restore” the context by popping values off the stack allowing it to start with the correct size.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,3,'2025-06-27_19-26-01','Setting up a thread stack is creating a block of memory that holds the initial values that CPU needs to start running the threads. This includes the PC, Link registers and general registers. We need to place these in the correct order on the stack to mimic the CPU’s expectation when it returns from an exception. Storing these calues is important because when the CPU switches to the thread, it can essentially “restore” the context by popping values off the stack allowing it to start with the correct size.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,3,'2025-06-27_19-51-52','Setting up a thread stack is creating a block of memory that holds the initial values that CPU needs to start running the threads. This includes the PC, Link registers and general registers. We need to place these in the correct order on the stack to mimic the CPU’s expectation when it returns from an exception. Storing these calues is important because when the CPU switches to the thread, it can essentially “restore” the context by popping values off the stack allowing it to start with the correct size.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,4,'2025-06-27_16-52-43','The PSP’s (Process Stack Pointer) purpose is to tell the CPU where the thread’s stack is located. If we don’t set the PSP before running the thread, then the CPU will try to use the wrong memory location when restoring the registers for that thread. This can cause it to misread or write to the invalid memory causing there to be a hard fault. By using switch/case and the __set_PSP function, we can ensure that CPU has a valid pointer to the stack before starting execution.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,4,'2025-06-27_19-26-01','The PSP’s (Process Stack Pointer) purpose is to tell the CPU where the thread’s stack is located. If we don’t set the PSP before running the thread, then the CPU will try to use the wrong memory location when restoring the registers for that thread. This can cause it to misread or write to the invalid memory causing there to be a hard fault. By using switch/case and the __set_PSP function, we can ensure that CPU has a valid pointer to the stack before starting execution.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',2,4,'2025-06-27_19-51-52','The PSP’s (Process Stack Pointer) purpose is to tell the CPU where the thread’s stack is located, specifically for thread-level (unprivileged) execution, while the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is used by the system and exception handlers. When we switch from system code to a thread, the CPU expects the PSP to be set to a valid memory address that holds the thread\'s stack. If we don’t explicitly set the PSP before running the thread, then the CPU may continue using the MSP or an undefined value, leading it to load or store registers to the wrong place in memory. This can cause it to misread or write to the invalid memory, causing there to be a hard fault. By using switch/case and the __set_PSP function, we can ensure that the thread has its own valid and separate stack space before execution begins, avoiding these faults and ensuring proper context switching\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,5,'2025-06-27_16-52-43','If the thread simply returned instead of looping continuously, the CPU would try and pop the return address from the stack to continue executing the instructions, however, since the thread isn’t meant to be returned and does not have a correct return address, the CPU will likely jump to an invalid memory space causing the program to crash or creating a hard fault. To avoid hard faults and crashes, thread functions should either be in an infinite loop or call a termination routine..\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,5,'2025-06-27_19-26-01','If the thread simply returned instead of looping continuously, the CPU would try and pop the return address from the stack to continue executing the instructions, however, since the thread isn’t meant to be returned and does not have a correct return address, the CPU will likely jump to an invalid memory space causing the program to crash or creating a hard fault. To avoid hard faults and crashes, thread functions should either be in an infinite loop or call a termination routine..\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',2,5,'2025-06-27_19-51-52','If the thread simply returned instead of looping continuously, the CPU would try and pop the return address from the stack to continue executing the instructions, however, since the thread isn’t meant to be returned and does not have a correct return address, the CPU will likely jump to an invalid memory space causing the program to crash or creating a hard fault. To avoid hard faults and crashes, thread functions should either be in an infinite loop or call a termination routine..\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',3,1,'2025-06-27_21-11-49','When multiple .c files are compiled into a single program, each file is first compiled separately into an object file (.o) During this step, each file is translated into machine code, and the compiler also records data like functions or variables that will be needed from or provided to other files. Once all object files are generated, the linker then takes them and combines them into one executable. The linker matches references between files and connects them. For example, if main.c calls a function defined in utils.c, the linker will connect the two. The final output is a single binary that includes all the code and data.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',3,1,'2025-06-27_21-57-19','The process of turning multiple .c files into a single executable involves three main stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. Preprocessing happens first, it handles all #include, #define, and other preprocessor directives. It expands macros and copies the contents of included header files into the source file and it removes comments. After preprocessing, you get a “pure” C file with everything inlined. Each file is then compiled separately into an object file (.o). During this step, each file is translated into machine code, and the compiler also records data like functions or variables that will be needed from or provided to other files. The compiler checks for syntax errors and converts the code into machine-level instructions. Once all object files are generated, the linker then takes them and combines them into one executable. The linker matches references between files and connects them. For example, if main.c calls a function defined in utils.c, the linker will connect the two. The result is one final executable file that can run on the system.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',3,2,'2025-06-27_21-11-49','Using #include <kernel.c> or any .c file is a bad idea because it breaks the normal compile-link process. The #include is meant for header (.h) files that declare functions and structures, not for source files with actual code. Including a .c file copies all its code into the current file before compilation, which can cause duplicate definitions, makes debugging harder, and leads to bad organization. It also removes the benefits of using the linker and of compiling files separately.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',3,2,'2025-06-27_21-57-19','Using #include <kernel.c> or any .c file is a bad idea because it breaks the normal compile-link process. The #include is meant for header (.h) files that declare functions and structures, not for source files with actual code. Including a .c file copies all its code into the current file before compilation, which can cause duplicate definitions, makes debugging harder, and leads to bad organization. It also removes the benefits of using the linker and of compiling files separately.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',3,3,'2025-06-27_21-11-49','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves the following steps:\nAllocate memory for the stack from a shared stack pool.\nPrepare the thread’s initial stack and make sure to set up the Program Counter, Link Register, and other registers.\nStore the thread’s stack pointer so the CPU can later use it to restore the thread’s context.\nUse a system call to tell the OS to start the thread.\nThe CPU switches to the thread by loading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) and restoring the thread’s registers.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',3,3,'2025-06-27_21-57-19','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves the following steps:\nInitialize the kernel: Call a setup function that prepares the OS, including memory structures like the stack pool. This must happen before creating any threads.\nDefine your thread function: Write the function that contains the code your thread will run. It must match the required signature (typically takes no arguments and returns nothing).\nCreate the thread: Tell the kernel about your thread by providing the address of your function. This registers the thread with the OS and allocates stack memory from the stack pool.\nCall the function that begins execution of the kernel. This triggers a system call that selects a thread, loads its context, and begins executing it.\nOnce the kernel starts, your thread runs independently, executing the code you defined.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',3,4,'2025-06-27_21-11-49','The stack pool is a block of pre-allocated memory reserved for thread stacks. Since each thread needs its own private stack to store local variables and register values, the stack pool provides a way to manage multiple stacks in one place. In future labs, the stack pool can be used to assign stack memory to each new thread. This would allow the operating system to track and separate each thread’s execution context, making it possible for multiple threads to run safely and independently from one another on the same system.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',3,4,'2025-06-27_21-57-19','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used by the operating system to manage stacks for both the kernel and each thread. Every thread needs its own stack to store local variables, return addresses, and CPU register values during execution. Similarly, the OS needs a dedicated stack for handling exceptions, system calls, and other privileged operations. The stack pool provides a centralized and organized way to manage all these stacks. In future labs, we can create multiple threads, and the OS will allocate a portion of the stack pool for each one. This setup ensures memory isolation between threads and makes it easier for the OS to switch between them safely. Including the OS’s stack in the pool also helps simplify memory management and keeps all stack-related data in one controlled area.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',3,5,'2025-06-27_21-11-49','From the user’s perspective, the “user” code refers to application-level logic, which is typically functions that run in unprivileged mode and perform specific tasks like printing or computing. This code goes in files like user.c. The “kernel” contains system-level code that handles scheduling, memory management, and privileged operations. This code belongs in kernel.c or similar files. By separating these, it helps enforce clear boundaries so users can\'t directly access or modify protected resources. By limiting access, the system is stable and secure. It also makes it easier to maintain and test each part of the system independently.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',3,5,'2025-06-27_21-57-19','From the user’s perspective, the “user” code refers to application-level logic, which is typically functions that run in unprivileged mode and perform specific tasks like printing or computing. This code goes in files like user.c. The “kernel” contains system-level code that handles scheduling, memory management, and privileged operations. This code belongs in kernel.c or similar files. By separating these, it helps enforce clear boundaries so users can\'t directly access or modify protected resources. By limiting access, the system is stable and secure. It also makes it easier to maintain and test each part of the system independently.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',4,1,'2025-07-30_18-34-47','Round-robin scheduling can work using a queue instead of an array to allow for a fair and more natural thread execution model. The thread at the front of the queue is always the next one to be run. Once the thread finishes its time slice, it is moved to the back of the queue, allowing the threads to run in order of how they entered. The benefit of using a queue over an array is that it simplifies the scheduling logic because it removes tracking and incrementing using a separate index, which you need in an array. The queue also helps with scalability by making dynamically adding or removing threads easier compared to static arrays of fixed size.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',4,1,'2025-07-30_19-13-08','Round-robin scheduling can work using a queue instead of an array to allow for a fair and more natural thread execution model. The thread at the front of the queue is always the next one to run, and once it finishes or yields, it’s moved to the back of the queue so the next thread can take its turn. While this makes the logic feel simpler, our current implementation using an array with a currentThread index and modulo already works like a circular queue. Switching to a proper queue doesn’t actually improve fairness, but it could make the design more flexible for adding and removing threads. However, using a queue comes with trade-offs because it adds memory overhead and could be slower, especially on a small system like ours because it doesn’t have a lot of processing power or memory. So while a queue might be interesting, the current array-based approach is already efficient and suitable for a simple round-robin scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',4,2,'2025-07-30_18-34-47','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for doing the actual context switch task between the OS and the threads. When it is triggered, it saves the thread’s current state by pushing R4 to R11 onto the thread’s stack, and these preserve the states so the thread can resume later. Using the os scheduling function, it determines the next thread that should run by updating the current thread index and setting the PSP to the new thread’s saved stack pointer. After the thread is selected, the handler restores the new thread’s registers (R4 to R11) by popping them from the stack and then it exits the interrupt. Once this happens the rest of the registers get restored from the stack and the new thread resumes like before. This lets multiple threads run without interfering with each other.\n',0,'s25'),('k63patel',4,2,'2025-07-30_19-13-08','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for doing the actual context switch between threads in the OS. When it gets triggered, the first thing it does is read the PSP, which points to the current thread’s stack, and then it saves that thread’s context by pushing registers R4 to R11 onto the stack. These registers hold important values that need to be preserved for the thread to continue running later. After that, the handler calls the osSched function by name, which updates the current thread index and sets the PSP to the stack pointer of the next thread that should run. This is the step where the OS actually chooses a new thread. Once the PSP is set to the new thread’s stack, PendSV_Handler function pops R4 to R11 from that stack, restoring the state of the new thread. When the interrupt exits, the processor finishes the context switch by automatically restoring the rest of the registers like R0 to R3, LR, and the others. This lets the new thread start running exactly where it left off and it allows multiple threads to take turns running on the processor without interfering with each other.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',4,3,'2025-07-30_18-34-47','Modifying it to always choose the highest priority thread is dangerous and can lead to problems with the OS. This happens because the threads with lower priority may never run. It could also result in a high-priority thread continuously yielding and being selected, which can prevent lower threads from making progress, and it dominates the CPU. This can cause I/O handlers or cleanup tasks to break unless fairness mechanisms like aging are included, resulting in the system becoming unreliable and unbalanced. While this method, or scheduling by priority, is common in real-time systems, its implementation must be done carefully to ensure that all threads run and the low-priority ones don’t get skipped.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',4,3,'2025-07-30_19-13-08','Modifying it to always choose the highest priority thread is dangerous and can lead to problems with the OS. This happens because the threads with lower priority may never run. It could also result in a high-priority thread continuously yielding and being selected, which can prevent lower threads from making progress, and it dominates the CPU. This can cause I/O handlers or cleanup tasks to break unless fairness mechanisms like aging are included, resulting in the system becoming unreliable and unbalanced. While this method, or scheduling by priority, is common in real-time systems, its implementation must be done carefully to ensure that all threads run and the low-priority ones don’t get skipped.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',4,4,'2025-07-30_18-34-47','Calling these C functions provides a lot of benefits compared to writing everything in assembly. C code is much easier to read, understand, and maintain, which is especially important in small or large-scale systems and projects. Using C lets developers use various high-level constructs like arrays, loops and structures, making the code less prone to errors because it\'s easier to understand. By using these C functions, it is easier to split parts and make it modular to debug or scale in the future by allowing parts like the scheduler to be updated without modifying low-level interrupts. However, calling C from assembly introduces overhead and limits control over exact timing and registers. Depending on the context of the situation, for real-time systems, the overhead may not be acceptable. Another con is that compilers may optimize C code in different ways, which may lead to less precise control over execution, but overall the pros outweigh the cons and C functions are considered a good choice for long-term management and clarity.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',4,4,'2025-07-30_19-13-08','Calling these C functions provides a lot of benefits compared to writing everything in assembly. C code is much easier to read, understand, and maintain, which is especially important in small or large-scale systems and projects. Using C lets developers use various high-level constructs like arrays, loops and structures, making the code less prone to errors because it\'s easier to understand. By using these C functions, it is easier to split parts and make it modular to debug or scale in the future by allowing parts like the scheduler to be updated without modifying low-level interrupts. However, calling C from assembly introduces overhead and limits control over exact timing and registers. Depending on the context of the situation, for real-time systems, the overhead may not be acceptable. Another con is that compilers may optimize C code in different ways, which may lead to less precise control over execution, but overall the pros outweigh the cons and C functions are considered a good choice for long-term management and clarity.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',4,5,'2025-07-30_18-34-47','If only one thread is created and it yields, the OS will still work, but the yield is ineffective. The scheduler will save and restore the same thread’s context, adding overhead without switching to a different thread, which is very inefficient. In real systems, schedulers often detect this and avoid unnecessary switching when no other threads are available.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',4,5,'2025-07-30_19-13-08','If only one thread is created and it yields, the OS will still work, but the yield is ineffective. The scheduler will save and restore the same thread’s context, adding overhead without switching to a different thread, which is very inefficient. In real systems, schedulers often detect this and avoid unnecessary switching when no other threads are available.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',5,1,'2025-07-31_14-51-30','To pass an argument to a thread, the user first needs to define the thread function so it accepts a void* parameter. Then, when calling osCreateThread, the user provides the function and the argument pointer. On the OS side, the argument is stored in the correct spot during thread setup, so it ends up in register R0 which is where the ARM calling convention expects the first argument. This is done by manually placing the argument in the thread’s stack frame as if R0 were preloaded with it. When the context switch occurs and the thread starts running, the processor pops that value automatically into R0 . This works because the Cortex expects function arguments to be passed this way, and our manual setup matches what the hardware expects when returning from an interrupt.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',5,2,'2025-07-31_14-51-30','You can’t call osYield from any of the context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because they are already running inside an interrupt. osYield triggers another context switch by setting a bit in the ICSR register, which then tries to start PendSV again, but if you’re already in PendSV or any handler that’s controlling context switching, doing this can cause a nested interrupt and overlap with the stack frame. This will corrupt the thread’s stored state or cause the scheduler to run in an inconsistent state. In specific cases, the request will simply be ignored, but it can also crash the system in other cases. These handlers are low-level and need to fully finish before any new switching is allowed, which is why osYield should only be called from regular thread code, not inside the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',5,3,'2025-07-31_14-51-30','Changing the OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms would make the system more responsive and allow smaller time granularity. Threads could be scheduled more precisely, and short delays would be more accurate. This could help with applications that need tighter timing or more real-time behaviour. But the downside is that the system now has to handle 10 times as many SysTick interrupts per second. That increases the CPU’s interrupt load and leaves less time for running actual thread code. It also increases context switching overhead, which is especially bad on small systems like the one we are using. In some cases, this could hurt performance more than it helps. So while 0.1ms gives more control, it only makes sense if the system really needs that level of precision, otherwise, 1ms is a better balance between speed and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',5,4,'2025-07-31_14-51-30','From the user’s point of view, co-operative multitasking means threads are in control and they need to call osYield when they want to let other threads run. For example, if a thread forgets to yield or gets stuck, the system can’t switch away from it. However, in pre-emptive multitasking, the OS handles switching using a timer like SysTick, so the user doesn’t have to do anything. The OS interrupts threads and gives others a chance to run, even if the thread doesn’t yield on its own. For the OS, co-operative multitasking is easier because context switching only happens when explicitly requested, but with pre-emptive multitasking, the OS has to automatically save the thread state when the timer goes and handle switching safely regardless of what the thread is doing. That makes pre-emptive harder to implement, but it’s more reliable for fairness and responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('k63patel',5,5,'2025-07-31_14-51-30','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching both end up calling the same logic to switch threads, so they can work together without conflict. osYield is triggered by the user manually and forces a context switch, while SysTick is triggered by a timer and causes the OS to pre-empt the current thread. Both methods cause PendSV to run which is where the actual switching happens, but since the PendSV handler doesn’t care how it was triggered, it works the same way for both. This means threads that yield early don’t waste their whole timeslice, and threads that don’t yield still get interrupted eventually. The two mechanisms just provide different ways to reach the same context switch, and together they help balance responsiveness and control.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',1,1,'2025-06-26_20-20-14','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a set of pre-made functions that makes it easier to control a microcontroller’s hardware without having to mess with low-level register code. It sits between your code and the hardware and lets you do things like turn on LEDs or use sensors in a more readable and consistent way. The big benefit is that your code becomes more portable, if you switch to a different microcontroller from the same family, you usually only need to adjust the HAL, not rewrite everything. It saves time, reduces bugs, and makes your code easier to understand and update.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',1,2,'2025-06-26_20-20-14','On bare-metal microcontrollers, there’s no built-in way for printf to know how to send output, because there’s no default console like on a PC. That’s why we need to write the __io_putchar function. It connects printf to the actual hardware—in our case, by using HAL_UART_Transmit to send each character over serial. This function is specific to the microcontroller and must be customized so printf knows how to print. Without it, printf won’t show anything or might even hang if it tries to use semihosting without a debugger.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',1,3,'2025-06-26_20-20-14','We used the debugger in this lab because it gives us much more detailed and accurate control over what\'s happening in the program while it runs. Unlike printf, which just prints output, the debugger lets us pause the program exactly where we want using breakpoints, and inspect things like memory, registers, and the stack. This was especially important for seeing how the stack grows or checking the exact value of the stack pointer—things printf can’t really show. Also, printf can change how the program behaves or slow it down, which matters on small embedded systems. The debugger gave us a clearer and more reliable way to see what was really going on.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',1,4,'2025-06-26_20-20-14','We got the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) from address 0x0, since that’s where the reset vector stores the starting location of the main stack. This value is important because it tells us where RAM starts being used for the stack. To get a new value for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), we picked a size for the main stack (0x400 bytes) and subtracted it from the MSP value, since the stack grows downward in memory. This gave us a new memory location for PSP, safely below the main stack. We needed the MSP’s starting value so we knew where to begin dividing up RAM. Without it, we wouldn’t know where to place other stacks like PSP without risking overlap or memory issues.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',1,5,'2025-06-26_20-20-14','To allocate multiple thread stacks on a Cortex-M4, we start from the initial MSP value and reserve a block of memory below it by subtracting N times the thread stack size. This gives us a base address marking the bottom of the reserved space. For each thread i, the stack pointer is set to base_address minus (i + 1) times the thread stack size. This way, each thread has its own stack space, the stacks don’t overlap, and they grow downward correctly. Since all stacks are based on a single aligned starting point, we know they’re properly spaced and safe to use. When switching threads, we just set PSP to the correct value and enable PSP mode using __set_CONTROL(2).\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',2,1,'2025-06-26_20-47-13','When we changed the PC to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the program jumped directly to that function and started running it, even though we didn’t call it in the usual way. This worked because we moved the function pointer into R0 and then used MOV PC, R0 in assembly. That loaded the PC with the function’s address, so the processor started executing it as if it had been called normally. As a result, we saw “Hello, PC!” printed continuously, showing that the CPU was now fully focused on running that function. This was a simple example of how changing the PC lets us control program execution manually, which is useful when learning how an operating system runs threads.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',2,1,'2025-06-27_00-58-04','When we changed the PC to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the program jumped directly to that function and started running it, even though we didn’t call it in the usual way. This worked because we moved the function pointer into R0 and then used MOV PC, R0 in assembly. That loaded the PC with the function’s address, so the processor started executing it as if it had been called normally. As a result, we saw “Hello, PC!” printed continuously, showing that the CPU was now fully focused on running that function. This was a simple example of how changing the PC lets us control program execution manually, which is useful when learning how an operating system runs threads.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',2,2,'2025-06-26_20-47-13','A system call is a way for a program to ask the operating system to do something it normally doesn’t have permission to do on its own, like accessing hardware or switching threads. It’s like raising your hand and asking the OS to step in and handle something special. In embedded systems, we trigger a system call using a special instruction like SVC, which causes an interrupt and runs a specific handler function that deals with the request.\n',0,'s25'),('kaqamar',2,2,'2025-06-27_00-58-04','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a user program request a service from the operating system’s kernel. It provides a controlled and safe way for user code to interact with hardware or perform privileged operations, like accessing I/O devices, managing memory, or switching threads. This is important because user programs are not allowed to access these resources directly, they must go through the kernel to prevent errors or security issues. In embedded systems, system calls are usually triggered with a special instruction like SVC, which causes an interrupt and transfers control to a handler in the kernel. The kernel then processes the request and safely returns control to the user program. This separation ensures that only trusted code in the kernel can access critical hardware or system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',2,3,'2025-06-26_20-47-13','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a block of memory so that when the thread starts running, the CPU knows exactly where to begin and how to restore its state. Since the CPU expects certain values to be on the stack during an interrupt or context switch, like the program counter (PC), xPSR, and registers, we push those values onto the stack ahead of time.\nIn this lab, we manually filled the thread’s stack starting with xPSR (a special status register), then the address of the function we want to run (PC), and then placeholder values for the rest of the registers (like R0–R12, LR). We stored them in the exact order the CPU expects when restoring a thread.\nWe need to store register values on the stack because when a context switch happens (like jumping to a new thread), the CPU loads its working state, like where it left off, which variables it was using, and where to go next, from the stack. Without this setup, the CPU wouldn’t know how to start the thread or what context it should be in.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',2,3,'2025-06-27_00-58-04','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a block of memory so that when the thread starts running, the CPU knows exactly where to begin and how to restore its state. Since the CPU expects certain values to be on the stack during an interrupt or context switch, like the program counter (PC), xPSR, and registers, we push those values onto the stack ahead of time.\nIn this lab, we manually filled the thread’s stack starting with xPSR (a special status register), then the address of the function we want to run (PC), and then placeholder values for the rest of the registers (like R0–R12, LR). We stored them in the exact order the CPU expects when restoring a thread.\nWe need to store register values on the stack because when a context switch happens (like jumping to a new thread), the CPU loads its working state, like where it left off, which variables it was using, and where to go next, from the stack. Without this setup, the CPU wouldn’t know how to start the thread or what context it should be in.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',2,4,'2025-06-26_20-47-13','We had to use __set_PSP to set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) before running the thread because the CPU needs a valid stack to pop register values from when it starts executing the thread. If we didn’t set PSP, it would still be zero or some random value, so when the system call tried to return and restore the thread’s context (like PC, xPSR, and registers), it would pop from an invalid memory address. This causes a hard fault because the CPU tries to access memory it’s not allowed to, or that doesn’t exist, leading to a crash. Setting PSP correctly ensures the CPU can safely load the thread’s saved context and start running it without error.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',2,4,'2025-06-27_00-58-04','We had to use __set_PSP to set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) before running the thread because the CPU needs a valid stack to pop register values from when it starts executing the thread. If we didn’t set PSP, it would still be zero or some random value, so when the system call tried to return and restore the thread’s context (like PC, xPSR, and registers), it would pop from an invalid memory address. This causes a hard fault because the CPU tries to access memory it’s not allowed to, or that doesn’t exist, leading to a crash. Setting PSP correctly ensures the CPU can safely load the thread’s saved context and start running it without error.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',2,5,'2025-06-26_20-47-13','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead just printed once and returned, the program would crash. That’s because when the function returns, it tries to pop a return address from the stack and jump back to it. But in our case, we didn’t set up a valid return address (LR) on the thread’s stack, just a placeholder value like 0xA. So when the function tries to return to that fake address, it jumps to an invalid location in memory, which leads to a hard fault or unexpected behavior. In short, the thread finishes, but there’s nowhere safe to return to, so the system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',2,5,'2025-06-27_00-58-04','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead just printed once and returned, the program would crash. That’s because when the function returns, it tries to pop a return address from the stack and jump back to it. But in our case, we didn’t set up a valid return address (LR) on the thread’s stack, just a placeholder value like 0xA. So when the function tries to return to that fake address, it jumps to an invalid location in memory, which leads to a hard fault or unexpected behavior. In short, the thread finishes, but there’s nowhere safe to return to, so the system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',3,1,'2025-06-27_20-57-56','When you build a program with multiple .c files, the process starts with preprocessing. This is where the compiler handles things like #include and #define, it pulls in the code from header files and replaces macros. After that, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o), which contains machine code and references to functions or variables. Then comes linking: the linker takes all the object files and combines them into one executable. It connects everything together by matching up function calls and variable references across files so the final program works as a whole.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',3,2,'2025-06-27_20-57-56','Using #include <kernel.c> (or any .c file) is a bad practice because it goes against how separate compilation is supposed to work. When you include a .c file, it’s like copy-pasting all its code into your current file. This can cause problems like multiple definitions of functions or global variables, which leads to compiler errors. It also ruins modularity, each .c file should be compiled on its own into an object file, and then everything should be linked together later. Including .c files skips that process, makes builds slower, increases the chance of bugs, and tightly couples your code. The right approach is to include the matching .h file instead, which just contains declarations and shared info, so each .c file can compile cleanly on its own.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',3,3,'2025-06-27_20-57-56','To start a thread, you first write the function that the thread will run—this usually takes no arguments and might loop forever if it needs to keep running. Then you initialize the OS kernel using a setup function that gets things ready, like preparing the stack memory. After that, you create the thread by calling a function and passing in the address of the thread’s function so the OS knows what to run. Finally, you start the kernel, which switches to the thread’s stack and begins running the thread function from the beginning.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',3,4,'2025-06-27_20-57-56','The stack pool is a chunk of memory that the operating system sets aside to hold the stacks for the kernel and all its threads. Each thread needs its own stack to handle things like function calls and register storage while it\'s running. The kernel uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) for its stack, while each thread gets its own section of the stack pool accessed through the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). When a new thread is created, it’s given part of the stack pool as its private stack space. This keeps memory organized and avoids overlap between threads. Later on, the OS will use the stack pool to switch between threads by saving and loading their stacks. If the pool isn’t managed carefully, it could lead to memory errors or data getting overwritten, since the kernel and all threads share this same pool.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',3,5,'2025-06-27_20-57-56','The kernel takes care of low-level tasks like managing stacks, handling threads, and controlling hardware. Its code is stored separately in dedicated kernel files. Meanwhile, the user writes their program logic—like what each thread should do—in the main file. Users don’t interact with the kernel directly; instead, they use API functions provided by the kernel to do things like create threads, set up the system, and start execution. This separation keeps everything clean and organized. Users don’t need to mess with the kernel code, which protects it from accidental changes, and the kernel makes sure that memory and hardware are handled safely. This way, users can focus on their own programs while the kernel runs things smoothly in the background.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',4,1,'2025-07-26_19-50-30','Round-robin scheduling works naturally with a queue because a queue is a FIFO (first-in-first-out) data structure. Each time the scheduler runs, it can dequeue the thread at the front (the current thread that just finished its time slice or called osYield) and enqueue it at the back. Then the scheduler simply runs the new thread at the front of the queue. This makes the scheduling flow super clean because you don’t have to track an index like you do with an array.\nThe benefit of a queue is that it can be more flexible if threads are dynamically created and destroyed. With an array, if one thread ends you might have to \"compact\" or deal with gaps. With a queue, threads naturally cycle, and you can easily remove or add them without re-indexing. It’s also conceptually closer to how round-robin works because the threads \"line up\" and wait their turn.\n',0,'s25'),('kaqamar',4,1,'2025-07-27_17-10-10','A queue fits naturally with round-robin scheduling because it’s a FIFO (first-in-first-out) structure. When the scheduler runs, it would dequeue the current thread from the front after its time slice (or yield) and enqueue it at the back. The next thread at the front of the queue then runs. This models round-robin perfectly because threads literally “line up” and take turns.\nThere are benefits:\nFlexibility: Threads can be easily added or removed at runtime without worrying about gaps in an array.\nConceptual alignment: The scheduler logic matches how a queue behaves, so the design is simpler and easier to understand.\nHowever, there is some overhead compared to using an array index. Enqueue and dequeue operations may involve additional pointer updates or dynamic memory operations, while an array just uses (currentThread + 1) % numThreadsRunning to find the next thread, which is very fast.\nAlso, our current array-based round-robin logic already behaves a lot like a queue. By incrementing the index and wrapping around, it “rotates” through the threads in order. Switching to a queue may not change behavior, but it might make dynamic thread management easier at the cost of slightly more overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',4,2,'2025-07-26_19-50-30','PendSV_Handler is triggered after osYield() requests a context switch. Inside PendSV_Handler, the current thread’s context (R4–R11 registers) is saved onto its stack (because the CPU already pushed R0–R3, LR, PC, and xPSR automatically during the exception). Then, the handler calls osSched() in C, which updates the scheduler’s data structures, picks the next thread to run, and sets PSP to point to that thread’s saved stack. Finally, PendSV_Handler restores R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack and returns from the exception, which causes the CPU to pop the rest of the registers and resume the new thread exactly where it left off.\nSo basically, PendSV_Handler saves the current thread, updates which thread should run, and restores the next thread, making the context switch seamless.\n',0,'s25'),('kaqamar',4,2,'2025-07-27_17-10-10','PendSV_Handler is triggered when osYield() (or a preemption request) signals that the CPU should switch threads. The first thing it does is read the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to find the exact location of the current thread’s stack. This is critical because the PSP points to where the current thread’s context is stored.\nNext, PendSV_Handler pushes the remaining registers R4–R11 onto that stack, since the CPU already automatically saved R0–R3, LR, PC, and xPSR when the interrupt fired. At this point, the full state of the current thread is preserved.\nThen the handler calls osSched(), which updates the scheduler’s data structures to select the next thread to run and updates the PSP to point to that thread’s saved stack pointer.\nFinally, PendSV_Handler restores the next thread’s context: it pops R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack, and when it exits, the CPU restores the remaining registers automatically from the stack. The new thread resumes exactly where it left off.\nReading and updating the PSP at the start and end is what allows the OS to precisely locate and restore each thread’s stack during the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',4,3,'2025-07-26_19-50-30','This can lead to starvation. If one or more threads have the highest priority, the scheduler will always pick those threads, and lower-priority threads may never get CPU time. In a simple system like ours with cooperative scheduling, this is even worse because if the highest-priority thread never yields, no other thread will ever run. We’d also lose fairness, which is one of the key benefits of round-robin scheduling.\nIn class we talked about priority scheduling needing extra mechanisms like aging or time-based priority changes to avoid starvation, but that’s much more complicated than what we’re doing now.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',4,3,'2025-07-27_17-10-10','This can lead to starvation. If one or more threads have the highest priority, the scheduler will always pick those threads, and lower-priority threads may never get CPU time. In a simple system like ours with cooperative scheduling, this is even worse because if the highest-priority thread never yields, no other thread will ever run. We’d also lose fairness, which is one of the key benefits of round-robin scheduling.\nIn class we talked about priority scheduling needing extra mechanisms like aging or time-based priority changes to avoid starvation, but that’s much more complicated than what we’re doing now.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',4,4,'2025-07-26_19-50-30','The main benefit of calling the C functions is readability and maintainability. It’s much easier to write and understand scheduling logic in C than in assembly. We can also reuse code and easily modify data structures like the thread array. Writing everything in assembly would be much harder to debug and way more error-prone, especially when managing stacks and pointers.\nThe downside is a bit of overhead. Each time we call the C function from assembly, we have to follow the ARM procedure call standard, which may push and pop extra registers. It might be slightly slower than doing everything in assembly. But for our OS, the trade-off is worth it because the scheduling logic is clearer and we’re less likely to break things.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',4,4,'2025-07-27_17-10-10','The main benefit of calling the C functions is readability and maintainability. It’s much easier to write and understand scheduling logic in C than in assembly. We can also reuse code and easily modify data structures like the thread array. Writing everything in assembly would be much harder to debug and way more error-prone, especially when managing stacks and pointers.\nThe downside is a bit of overhead. Each time we call the C function from assembly, we have to follow the ARM procedure call standard, which may push and pop extra registers. It might be slightly slower than doing everything in assembly. But for our OS, the trade-off is worth it because the scheduling logic is clearer and we’re less likely to break things.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',4,5,'2025-07-26_19-50-30','Yes, the OS would still technically work, but it would behave the same as before. When the single thread calls osYield(), the scheduler would try to switch to the \"next\" thread, but since there’s only one, it would just pick that same thread again. The PendSV context switch would save and restore the same thread’s context.\nThis means you’d still see “Hello PC!” printed continuously, but the yielding wouldn’t actually do anything useful because there are no other threads to run. So the OS is functioning correctly; it’s just that the scheduler only has one option, so nothing changes.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',4,5,'2025-07-27_17-10-10','Yes, the OS would still technically work, but it would behave the same as before. When the single thread calls osYield(), the scheduler would try to switch to the \"next\" thread, but since there’s only one, it would just pick that same thread again. The PendSV context switch would save and restore the same thread’s context.\nThis means you’d still see “Hello PC!” printed continuously, but the yielding wouldn’t actually do anything useful because there are no other threads to run. So the OS is functioning correctly; it’s just that the scheduler only has one option, so nothing changes.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',5,1,'2025-07-30_07-33-46','From the user’s point of view, passing an argument into a thread is straightforward. When creating the thread, the user calls osCreateThread() and passes in the function pointer for the thread as well as a pointer to the argument they want it to receive. For example, osCreateThread(myThread, &myStruct) sends the address of myStruct to the thread function. The thread function itself must be written to accept a void* argument so it can receive any type of data.\nFrom the OS side, the argument gets placed into the new thread’s initial stack frame. When osCreateThread() is setting up the stack, it reserves space for all the registers that the processor will expect to pop off the stack when the thread starts running. One of these is register R0, which is where function arguments are stored by the ARM calling convention. The OS writes the pointer to the user’s data into the correct position for R0 in the stack frame. Then, when the thread is first scheduled and the CPU restores its context, the value in R0 is set to that pointer automatically.\nThis works because the ARM Cortex-M architecture always expects the first argument to a function to be in R0. By preloading R0 in the saved stack frame, the OS tricks the CPU into thinking the argument was passed in normally when the thread starts. The thread function can then just cast the void* pointer to the correct type and use it.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',5,2,'2025-07-30_07-33-46','osYield() itself triggers a context switch by setting the PendSV interrupt. If you call it from inside a context-switching function like PendSV_Handler or SVC_Handler, you end up asking the processor to context switch while it’s already in the middle of one. This can cause serious problems because these handlers are already saving and restoring stack frames and updating the current thread pointer. If osYield() interrupts that process, you can corrupt the stack or lose track of which thread is running.\nThe end result is usually a hard fault or the system just crashing unpredictably because the stack pointers get messed up. It’s essentially a form of recursion at the hardware interrupt level, which the system isn’t designed to handle. That’s why context-switching functions should complete their work first, and any requests for yielding should just set flags or trigger PendSV after the current switch finishes.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',5,3,'2025-07-30_07-33-46','Lowering the OS tick interval to 0.1ms means the scheduler can respond to events and pre-empt threads much faster. This is good for systems where latency is critical, because threads that need more immediate attention won’t have to wait as long. It also makes timers in the OS more precise since they’re checked 10 times as often.\nThe downside is that the CPU will now spend much more time handling interrupts and context switches. Each tick causes the SysTick interrupt to fire, and each context switch has overhead from saving and restoring registers. At 0.1ms, you’re interrupting the processor 10,000 times per second, which can take away a significant portion of processing time from the actual threads. It can also increase power consumption since the CPU is constantly waking up. So it’s a trade-off between responsiveness and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',5,4,'2025-07-30_07-33-46','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires the thread to give up the CPU voluntarily using something like osYield(). If a thread never yields, it will hog the processor and other threads won’t run. With pre-emptive multitasking, the OS interrupts threads automatically at fixed intervals (or based on priorities) so that each one gets CPU time, regardless of whether it wants to yield.\nFrom the OS side, co-operative multitasking is simpler. The scheduler only runs when a thread explicitly yields or blocks, so it doesn’t need a timer or hardware interrupt to force a context switch. Pre-emptive multitasking is more complex because the OS must set up a clock (SysTick in our case) to interrupt threads periodically and run the scheduler. It also has to save and restore the full CPU state at arbitrary points because the thread could be in the middle of anything when it gets pre-empted.\n',1,'s25'),('kaqamar',5,5,'2025-07-30_07-33-46','Both mechanisms ultimately trigger the same context-switching logic in the OS, which is why they work together. When a thread calls osYield(), it sets the PendSV interrupt to run the scheduler. Similarly, when the SysTick timer expires, the OS decrements the current thread’s runtime and sets PendSV if the time slice is up. PendSV is the common place where the OS saves the current thread’s context, picks the next thread, and restores its context.\nBecause they use the same underlying mechanism, it doesn’t matter whether the switch was requested voluntarily or forced by the timer. If a thread yields before its time slice is over, the scheduler just moves on to the next one. If it doesn’t yield, the timer will eventually pre-empt it when its runtime expires. This allows cooperative and pre-emptive threads to coexist without interfering with each other.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,1,'2025-05-14_18-31-47','HAL or the Hardware Abstraction Layer that allows embedded software code to communicate with the hardware. It is usually a set of predefined functions that work with the registers written by the producer to allow the user to implement higher-level software onto hardware without having to write the code using complex registers. This allows for simpler and faster development.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,1,'2025-05-14_20-26-26','HAL or the Hardware Abstraction Layer that allows embedded software code to communicate with the hardware. It is usually a set of predefined functions that work with the registers written by the producer to allow the user to implement higher-level software onto hardware without having to write the code using complex registers. This allows for simpler and faster development. Other benefits of abstraction including resubaility within code as well as portability to other hardware devices.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,1,'2025-05-14_21-01-13','HAL or the Hardware Abstraction Layer that allows embedded software code to communicate with the hardware. It is usually a set of predefined functions that work with the registers written by the producer to allow the user to implement higher-level software onto hardware without having to write the code using complex registers. This allows for simpler and faster development. Other benefits of abstraction including resubaility within code as well as portability to other hardware devices.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,1,'2025-05-15_19-03-13','HAL or the Hardware Abstraction Layer that allows embedded software code to communicate with the hardware. It is usually a set of predefined functions that work with the registers written by the producer to allow the user to implement higher-level software onto hardware without having to write the code using complex registers. This allows for simpler and faster development. Other benefits of abstraction including resubaility within code as well as portability to other hardware devices.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,1,'2025-06-27_21-42-57','HAL or the Hardware Abstraction Layer that allows embedded software code to communicate with the hardware. It is usually a set of predefined functions that work with the registers written by the producer to allow the user to implement higher-level software onto hardware without having to write the code using complex registers. This allows for simpler and faster development. Other benefits of abstraction including resubaility within code as well as portability to other hardware devices.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,2,'2025-05-14_18-31-47','__io_putchar function receives a char in integer form from the ‘printf’ function. In the case of a string, the printf function take the string and sends a character within the string one at a time. Using the HAL_UART_Transmit function, it prints out the character on the console output. This function is needed because the library for printf, the stdio.h library needs helper functions for hardware interactions. Since the hardware system being used does not have a default output, a wrapper function is required to define the output, which, in this case, is the UART port.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,2,'2025-05-14_20-26-26','__io_putchar function receives a char in integer form from the ‘printf’ function. In the case of a string, the printf function take the string and sends a character within the string one at a time. Using the HAL_UART_Transmit function, it prints out the character on the console output. This function is needed because the library for printf, the stdio.h library needs helper functions for hardware interactions as it cannot assume where the output is being used. Since the hardware system being used does not have a default output, a wrapper function is required to define the output, which, in this case, is the UART port. This function can be used and modified in different scenarios and other output methods based on the specifications of the project .\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,2,'2025-05-14_21-01-13','__io_putchar function receives a char in integer form from the ‘printf’ function. In the case of a string, the printf function take the string and sends a character within the string one at a time. Using the HAL_UART_Transmit function, it prints out the character on the console output. This function is needed because the library for printf, the stdio.h library needs helper functions for hardware interactions as it cannot assume where the output is being used. Since the hardware system being used does not have a default output, a wrapper function is required to define the output, which, in this case, is the UART port. This function can be used and modified in different scenarios and other output methods based on the specifications of the project .\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,2,'2025-05-15_19-03-13','__io_putchar function receives a char in integer form from the ‘printf’ function. In the case of a string, the printf function take the string and sends a character within the string one at a time. Using the HAL_UART_Transmit function, it prints out the character on the console output. This function is needed because the library for printf, the stdio.h library needs helper functions for hardware interactions as it cannot assume where the output is being used. Since the hardware system being used does not have a default output, a wrapper function is required to define the output, which, in this case, is the UART port. This function can be used and modified in different scenarios and other output methods based on the specifications of the project .\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,2,'2025-06-27_21-42-57','__io_putchar function receives a char in integer form from the ‘printf’ function. In the case of a string, the printf function take the string and sends a character within the string one at a time. Using the HAL_UART_Transmit function, it prints out the character on the console output. This function is needed because the library for printf, the stdio.h library needs helper functions for hardware interactions as it cannot assume where the output is being used. Since the hardware system being used does not have a default output, a wrapper function is required to define the output, which, in this case, is the UART port. This function can be used and modified in different scenarios and other output methods based on the specifications of the project .\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,3,'2025-05-14_18-31-47','The debugger allows us to gain further insight within the code. When using the printf statement, the code runs fully and takes a long time, whereas the code debugger allows users to stop the code at specific points and look further into the values and registers. This allows faster debugging as it allows suers to draw more detailed conclusions.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,3,'2025-05-14_20-26-26','The debugger allows us to gain further insight into the code. When using the printf statement, the code runs fully and takes a long time, whereas the code debugger allows users to stop the code at specific points and look further into the values and registers. This allows faster debugging as it allows suers to draw more detailed conclusions. When using the debugger, the register values and variable values are displayed right after the line, during changes, versus using the printf, where the value might be slightly different because it accesses the value after changes. Furthermore, the printf takes those values in their final state rather than as they are changing, thus it is hard to differentiate the different values of registers due to their transient nature.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,3,'2025-05-14_21-01-13','The debugger allows us to gain further insight into the code. When using the printf statement, the code runs fully and takes a long time, whereas the code debugger allows users to stop the code at specific points and look further into the values and registers. This allows faster debugging as it allows suers to draw more detailed conclusions. When using the debugger, the register values and variable values are displayed right after the line, during changes, versus using the printf, where the value might be slightly different because it accesses the value after changes. Furthermore, the printf takes those values in their final state rather than as they are changing, thus it is hard to differentiate the different values of registers due to their transient nature.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,3,'2025-05-15_19-03-13','The debugger allows us to gain further insight into the code. When using the printf statement, the code runs fully and takes a long time, whereas the code debugger allows users to stop the code at specific points and look further into the values and registers. This allows faster debugging as it allows suers to draw more detailed conclusions. When using the debugger, the register values and variable values are displayed right after the line, during changes, versus using the printf, where the value might be slightly different because it accesses the value after changes. Furthermore, the printf takes those values in their final state rather than as they are changing, thus it is hard to differentiate the different values of registers due to their transient nature.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,3,'2025-06-27_21-42-57','The debugger allows us to gain further insight into the code. When using the printf statement, the code runs fully and takes a long time, whereas the code debugger allows users to stop the code at specific points and look further into the values and registers. This allows faster debugging as it allows suers to draw more detailed conclusions. When using the debugger, the register values and variable values are displayed right after the line, during changes, versus using the printf, where the value might be slightly different because it accesses the value after changes. Furthermore, the printf takes those values in their final state rather than as they are changing, thus it is hard to differentiate the different values of registers due to their transient nature.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,4,'2025-05-14_18-31-47','Once the MSP_init_val has been obtained, the next thing that must be done is use the value to computer the value of the PSP. This is how the new value is obtained; the value is subtracted from the address of the MSP_INIT_VAL and assigned to the PSP value variable. This value is then used in a function called ‘__set_PSP()’ This is defined by the Common Microcontroller Software Interface Standard (CMSIS).Then, using the ___set_CONTROL(), the active stack or the stack being used will be changed from the mSP stack to the PSP stack pointe by inputting ‘2’ as the parameter.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,4,'2025-05-14_20-26-26','Once the MSP_init_val has been obtained, the next thing that must be done is to use the value to compute the value of the PSP. This is how the new value is obtained; the value is subtracted from the address of the MSP_INIT_VAL and assigned to the PSP value variable. Knowing the initial MSP value is important as it provides a reference point as to where the memory is and assign the PSP accordingly. Furthermore, it points the program to a part of the memory specifically made for the program. Without this, the PSP data might be assigned to unknown areas or be corrupted.  Additionally, this is another reason why the value needs to be offset. It allows the PSP to be assigned to an area after the main stack as the main stack is necessary and takes priority over the PSP. This value is then used in a function called ‘__set_PSP()’ This is defined by the Common Microcontroller Software Interface Standard (CMSIS). Then, using the ___set_CONTROL(), the active stack or the stack being used will be changed from the MSP  stack to the PSP stack pointer by inputting ‘2’ as the parameter.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,4,'2025-05-14_21-01-13','Once the MSP_init_val has been obtained, the next thing that must be done is to use the value to compute the value of the PSP. This is how the new value is obtained; the value is subtracted from the address of the MSP casted as an integer, MSP_INIT_VAL and assigned to the PSP_val variable. Knowing the initial MSP value is important as it provides a reference point as to where the memory is and offset and assign the PSP_val accordingly. Furthermore, it points the program to a part of the memory specifically made for the program. Without this, the PSP data might be assigned to unknown areas or be corrupted.  Additionally, this is another reason why the value needs to be offset. It allows the PSP to be assigned to an area after the main stack as the main stack is necessary and takes priority over the PSP. This value is then used in a function called ‘__set_PSP()’ This is defined by the Common Microcontroller Software Interface Standard (CMSIS). Then, using the ___set_CONTROL(), the active stack or the stack being used will be changed from the MSP  stack to the PSP stack pointer by inputting ‘2’ as the parameter.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,4,'2025-05-15_19-03-13','Once the MSP_init_val has been obtained, the next thing that must be done is to use the value to compute the value of the PSP. This is how the new value is obtained; the value of PSP_val is obtained by subtracting MSP_init_val by 0x400 or 1024 bytes. Knowing the initial MSP value is important as it provides a reference point as to where the memory is, and offset the PSP_val accordingly. Furthermore, it points the program to a part of the memory specifically made for the program. Without this, the PSP data might be assigned to unknown areas or be corrupted.  Additionally, this is another reason why the value needs to be offset. It allows the PSP to be assigned to an area after the main stack as the main stack is necessary and takes priority over the PSP. This value is then used in a function called ‘__set_PSP()’ This is defined by the Common Microcontroller Software Interface Standard (CMSIS). Then, using the ___set_CONTROL(), the active stack or the stack being used will be changed from the MSP  stack to the PSP stack pointer by inputting ‘2’ as the parameter.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,4,'2025-06-27_21-42-57','Once the MSP_init_val has been obtained, the next thing that must be done is to use the value to compute the value of the PSP. This is how the new value is obtained; the value of PSP_val is obtained by subtracting MSP_init_val by 0x400 or 1024 bytes. Knowing the initial MSP value is important as it provides a reference point as to where the memory is, and offset the PSP_val accordingly. Furthermore, it points the program to a part of the memory specifically made for the program. Without this, the PSP data might be assigned to unknown areas or be corrupted.  Additionally, this is another reason why the value needs to be offset. It allows the PSP to be assigned to an area after the main stack as the main stack is necessary and takes priority over the PSP. This value is then used in a function called ‘__set_PSP()’ This is defined by the Common Microcontroller Software Interface Standard (CMSIS). Then, using the ___set_CONTROL(), the active stack or the stack being used will be changed from the MSP  stack to the PSP stack pointer by inputting ‘2’ as the parameter.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,5,'2025-05-14_18-31-47','The code will be changed by doing the process multiple times for each of the stacks except the starting point of the stack will change each time. The order will then become starting with the main stack and then adding the threads after each of the previous threads ends.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,5,'2025-05-14_20-26-26','The code will be changed by doing the process multiple times for each of the stacks, except the starting point of the stack will change each time, as it will be after each thread. The order will then become starting with the main stack and then adding the threads after each of the previous threads ends. Similar to the current code, each thread must be offset to after the previous thread. This allows the memory to be uncorrupted and assigned in a logical manner. Furthermore, each stack should have enough memory, which can be done if the memory is assigned in an organized method. Assuming the value of the stack is the same, as mentioned in the lab manual, the following formula can be used: “ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE” to assign the offset value. In the scenario that the thread size is different, ‘THREAD_STACK_SIZE” will need to be changed to the size of the stack of the previous thread to ensure the offset is correct and the memory is not overwritten by the new thread or there is empty memory in between.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,5,'2025-05-14_21-01-13','The code will be changed by doing the process multiple times for each of the stacks, except the starting point of the stack will change each time, as it will be after each thread. The order will then become starting with the main stack and then adding the threads after each of the previous threads ends. Similar to the current code, each thread must be offset to after the previous thread. This allows the memory to be uncorrupted and assigned in a logical manner. Furthermore, each stack should have enough memory, which can be done if the memory is assigned in an organized method. Assuming the value of the stack is the same, as mentioned in the lab manual, the following formula can be used: “ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE” to assign the offset value. In the scenario that the thread size is different, ‘THREAD_STACK_SIZE” will need to be changed to the size of the stack of the previous thread to ensure the offset is correct and the memory is not overwritten by the new thread or there is empty memory in between.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,5,'2025-05-15_19-03-13','The code will be changed by doing the process multiple times for each of the stacks, except the starting point of the stack will change each time, as it will be after each thread. The order will then become starting with the main stack and then adding the threads after each of the previous threads ends. Similar to the current code, each thread must be offset to after the previous thread. This allows the memory to be uncorrupted and assigned in a logical manner. Furthermore, each stack should have enough memory, which can be done if the memory is assigned in an organized method. Assuming the value of the stack is the same, as mentioned in the lab manual, the following formula can be used: “ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE” to assign the offset value. In the scenario that the thread size is different, ‘THREAD_STACK_SIZE” will need to be changed to the size of the stack of the previous thread to ensure the offset is correct and the memory is not overwritten by the new thread or there is empty memory in between.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',1,5,'2025-06-27_21-42-57','The code will be changed by doing the process multiple times for each of the stacks, except the starting point of the stack will change each time, as it will be after each thread. The order will then become starting with the main stack and then adding the threads after each of the previous threads ends. Similar to the current code, each thread must be offset to after the previous thread. This allows the memory to be uncorrupted and assigned in a logical manner. Furthermore, each stack should have enough memory, which can be done if the memory is assigned in an organized method. Assuming the value of the stack is the same, as mentioned in the lab manual, the following formula can be used: “ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE” to assign the offset value. In the scenario that the thread size is different, ‘THREAD_STACK_SIZE” will need to be changed to the size of the stack of the previous thread to ensure the offset is correct and the memory is not overwritten by the new thread or there is empty memory in between.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,1,'2025-05-27_10-42-39','When changing the PC to hold the function pointer, R0, the register associated with the input argument during the function call, moves into the PC. Once this occurs, the PC stores function location. Since the PC(program counter) is the register that contains the location of the next program instruction, the next instruction becomes the print continuously function. Since the PC now has the location of the print_continuously function in the PC R0 location, it becomes the first instruction, causing the function to be ran and produce the output of “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop, as defined in the function.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,1,'2025-05-27_13-20-12','When changing the PC to hold the function pointer, R0 moves into the PC. Register 0, R0, is the input argument of the function call. Once this occurs, the PC stores function location. Since the PC is the register that contains the location of the next program instruction, the next instruction becomes the print continuously function as it is in the R0 position This causes the function to be ran and produce the output of “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop, as defined in the function.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,1,'2025-06-26_00-32-12','When changing the PC to hold the function pointer, R0 moves into the PC. Register 0, R0, is the input argument of the function call. Once this occurs, the PC stores function location. Since the PC is the register that contains the location of the next program instruction, the next instruction becomes the print continuously function as it is in the R0 position This causes the function to be ran and produce the output of “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop, as defined in the function.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,1,'2025-06-26_11-23-30','When changing the PC to hold the function pointer, R0 moves into the PC. Register 0, R0, is the input argument of the function call. Once this occurs, the PC stores function location. Since the PC is the register that contains the location of the next program instruction, the next instruction becomes the print continuously function as it is in the R0 position This causes the function to be ran and produce the output of “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop, as defined in the function.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,1,'2025-06-26_13-27-41','When changing the PC to hold the function pointer, R0 moves into the PC. Register 0, R0, is the input argument of the function call. Once this occurs, the PC stores the function location. This essentially calling the function. Since the PC is the register that contains the location of the next program instruction, the next instruction becomes the print continuously function as it is in the R0 position This causes the function to be run and produce the output of “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop, as defined in the function as that is the incruction contained in the register.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,1,'2025-06-26_22-10-55','When changing the PC to hold the function pointer, R0 moves into the PC. Register 0, R0, is the input argument of the function call. Once this occurs, the PC stores the function location. This essentially tells the PC where the next instruction is by giving it the function pointer of where the actual function instructions begin. It then jumps to that address and begins the execution of the function as the next instruction becomes the print continuously function as it is in the R0 position. This causes the function to be run and produce the output of “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop, as defined in the function.  This occurs because the assembly instruction MOV PC used in the jumpAssembly instructs the CPU to set the program counter to the function and the CPU directly runs it.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,1,'2025-06-26_23-25-38','When changing the PC to hold the function pointer, R0 moves into the PC. Register 0, R0, is the input argument of the function call. Once this occurs, the PC stores the function location. This essentially tells the PC where the next instruction is by giving it the function pointer of where the actual function instructions begin. It then jumps to that address and begins the execution of the function as the next instruction becomes the print continuously function as it is in the R0 position. This causes the function to be run and produce the output of “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop, as defined in the function.  This occurs because the assembly instruction MOV PC used in the jumpAssembly instructs the CPU to set the program counter to the function and the CPU directly runs it.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,1,'2025-06-27_10-24-23','When changing the PC to hold the function pointer, R0 moves into the PC. Register 0, R0, is the input argument of the function call. Once this occurs, the PC stores the function location. This essentially tells the PC where the next instruction is by giving it the function pointer of where the actual function instructions begin. It then jumps to that address and begins the execution of the function as the next instruction becomes the print continuously function as it is in the R0 position. This causes the function to be run and produce the output of “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop, as defined in the function.  This occurs because the assembly instruction MOV PC used in the jumpAssembly instructs the CPU to set the program counter to the function and the CPU directly runs it.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,1,'2025-06-27_11-19-10','When changing the PC to hold the function pointer, R0 moves into the PC. Register 0, R0, is the input argument of the function call. Once this occurs, the PC stores the function location. This essentially tells the PC where the next instruction is by giving it the function pointer of where the actual function instructions begin. It then jumps to that address and begins the execution of the function as the next instruction becomes the print continuously function as it is in the R0 position. This causes the function to be run and produce the output of “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop, as defined in the function.  This occurs because the assembly instruction MOV PC used in the jumpAssembly instructs the CPU to set the program counter to the function and the CPU directly runs it.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,1,'2025-06-27_21-27-47','When changing the PC to hold the function pointer, R0 moves into the PC. Register 0, R0, is the input argument of the function call. Once this occurs, the PC stores the function location. This essentially tells the PC where the next instruction is by giving it the function pointer of where the actual function instructions begin. It then jumps to that address and begins the execution of the function as the next instruction becomes the print continuously function as it is in the R0 position. This causes the function to be run and produce the output of “Hello PC!” in an infinite loop, as defined in the function.  This occurs because the assembly instruction MOV PC used in the jumpAssembly instructs the CPU to set the program counter to the function and the CPU directly runs it.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,2,'2025-05-27_10-42-39','A system call is a call from the program indicating to the OS that is needs to perform a task it cannot. This is because the OS has restrictions on resource access and memories. As a result, the system call must be used to gain access to these resources. It also allows the OS to allocate and manage the memory. In this lab, this works by calling different functions using the __asm() and print statements accordingly.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,2,'2025-05-27_13-20-12','A system call is a function call from the user program requesting to the OS that it needs to perform a task the program cannot do due to the OS having restrictions on resource access and memory to protect the hardware from unknown code. As a result, the system call must be used to gain access to these resources. The system call allows the program to contact the OS through the kernel to request a service that it cannot perform. The OS then performs the operations required and returns to the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,2,'2025-06-26_00-32-12','A system call is a function call from the user program requesting to the OS that it needs to perform a task the program cannot do due to the OS having restrictions on resource access and memory to protect the hardware from unknown code. As a result, the system call must be used to gain access to these resources. The system call allows the program to contact the OS through the kernel to request a service that it cannot perform. The OS then performs the operations required and returns to the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,2,'2025-06-26_11-23-30','A system call is a function call from the user program requesting to the OS that it needs to perform a task the program cannot do due to the OS having restrictions on resource access and memory to protect the hardware from unknown code. As a result, the system call must be used to gain access to these resources. The system call allows the program to contact the OS through the kernel to request a service that it cannot perform. The OS then performs the operations required and returns to the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,2,'2025-06-26_13-27-41','A system call is a function call from the user program requesting to the OS that it needs to perform a task the program cannot do due to the OS having restrictions on resource access and memory to protect the hardware from unknown code. As a result, the system call must be used to gain access to these resources. The system call allows the program to contact the OS through the kernel to request a service that it cannot perform. The OS then performs the operations required and returns to the program.\