values need to be pushed onto the thread’s stack. This shows us what it means to set up a thread’s stack.” It means to load all the required registers to run the thread. These values need to be on the stack so the CPU knows what instructions to execute. This is because the CPU simply runs programs; it does not assign values. Therefore, if there is a new thread with a new stack,  the CPU will not have any instructions to run it. Thus, when running the new thread, it is as if it were running before, and the CPU is just restoring the state. To restore the state, it must have values to restore. Additionally, the value restoration order must be correct to ensure that the CPU assign the correct values to the correct registers.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,3,'2025-06-27_11-19-10','The stack was set up by first navigating to the memory address of the PSP, which becomes the start of the stack for the thread. The pointer to the stack must be declared and set to the address obtained. The stack is then allocated from that value in the memory. Next, the register values need to be pushed onto the thread’s stack. This shows us what it means to set up a thread’s stack.” It means to load all the required registers to run the thread. These values need to be on the stack so the CPU knows what instructions to execute. This is because the CPU simply runs programs; it does not assign values. Therefore, if there is a new thread with a new stack,  the CPU will not have any instructions to run it. Thus, when running the new thread, it is as if it were running before, and the CPU is just restoring the state. To restore the state, it must have values to restore. Additionally, the value restoration order must be correct to ensure that the CPU assign the correct values to the correct registers.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,3,'2025-06-27_21-27-47','The stack was set up by first navigating to the memory address of the PSP, which becomes the start of the stack for the thread. The pointer to the stack must be declared and set to the address obtained. The stack is then allocated from that value in the memory. Next, the register values need to be pushed onto the thread’s stack. This shows us what it means to set up a thread’s stack.” It means to load all the required registers to run the thread. These values need to be on the stack so the CPU knows what instructions to execute. This is because the CPU simply runs programs; it does not assign values. Therefore, if there is a new thread with a new stack,  the CPU will not have any instructions to run it. Thus, when running the new thread, it is as if it were running before, and the CPU is just restoring the state. To restore the state, it must have values to restore. Additionally, the value restoration order must be correct to ensure that the CPU assign the correct values to the correct registers.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,4,'2025-05-27_10-42-39','The code would crash because it would cause the thread to run on memory that is undefined. For example it could be overwriting other memory or be assigned to an unknown location. By setting the PSP, it also indicates that the PSP is the set of instructions to run and the instructions to run are determined by the SVC_Handler_Main. Without this, there would be no instructions to run because the system does not know where to locate them.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,4,'2025-05-27_13-20-12','The code would crash because it would cause the thread to run on memory that is undefined. For example, it could be overwriting other memory or be assigned to an unknown location. By setting the stack pointer to the address of PSP, it ensures that the MSP values are not overwritten as they are important to the OS and the memory is allocated in an organized manner to ensure memory remains uncorrupted. Without this, there would be no instructions to run because the system does not know where to locate them.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,4,'2025-06-26_00-32-12','The code would crash because it would cause the thread to run on memory that is undefined. For example, it could be overwriting other memory or be assigned to an unknown location. By setting the stack pointer to the address of PSP, it ensures that the MSP values are not overwritten as they are important to the OS and the memory is allocated in an organized manner to ensure memory remains uncorrupted. Without this, there would be no instructions to run because the system does not know where to locate them.\nThere are two types of stack pointers, MSP and PSP. The MSP is for overall system instructions and data. The PSP, on the other hand, is used for user threads. It is for the user programs. There are two main reasons why the __set_PSP needs to be used. The first is that it allocates separate memory for the thread and allows maintaining the safety of the memory, ensuring the integrity is maintained. Secondly, it is important to ensure that the user programs only have access to certain memory, also to ensure data integrity is maintained. Without the __set_PSP(), there may be confusion on which memory to use, causing overwriting of important MSP data. Additionally, if there is different information than what the CPU expected in a location due to overwriting, the system will throw an error. Also, it also gives PSP access to privileged parts of the memory.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,4,'2025-06-26_11-23-30','There are two types of stack pointers, MSP and PSP. The MSP is for overall system instructions and data. The PSP, on the other hand, is used for user threads. It is for the user programs. There are two main reasons why the __set_PSP needs to be used. The first is that it allocates separate memory for the thread and allows maintaining the safety of the memory, ensuring the integrity is maintained. Secondly, it is important to ensure that the user programs only have access to certain memory, also to ensure data integrity is maintained. Without the __set_PSP(), there may be confusion on which memory to use, causing overwriting of important MSP data. Additionally, if there is different information than what the CPU expected in a location due to overwriting, the system will throw an error. Additionally, using __set_PSP() also ensures that the CPU knows where the data corresponding to that thread is. Which is important, as the CPU can then properly allocate data and access instructions without pushing or pulling from an undefined or invalid memory location. If __set_PSP() is used the CPU also knows that there is more memory for this thread and it will use that memory instead of the MSP memory, which is important to ensure the right procedure uses the right data. By using __set_PSP(), the CPU knows to use the PSP data, maintaining the integrity of memory.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,4,'2025-06-26_13-27-41','There are two types of stack pointers, MSP and PSP. The MSP is for overall system instructions and data. The PSP, on the other hand, is used for user threads. It is for the user programs. There are two main reasons why the __set_PSP needs to be used. The first is that it allocates separate memory for the thread and allows maintaining the safety of the memory for each process and thread, ensuring the integrity is maintained. Secondly, it is important to ensure that the user programs only have access to certain memory, also to ensure data integrity is maintained. Without the __set_PSP(), there may be confusion on which memory to use, causing overwriting of important MSP data. Additionally, if there is different information than what the CPU expected in a location due to overwriting, the system will throw an error. Using __set_PSP() also ensures that the CPU knows where the data corresponding to that thread is, which is important, as the CPU can then properly allocate data and access instructions without pushing or pulling from an undefined or invalid memory location. If __set_PSP() is used, the CPU also knows that there is more memory for this thread and it will use that memory instead of the MSP memory, which is important to ensure the right procedure uses the right data. By using __set_PSP(), the CPU knows to use the PSP data, maintaining the integrity of memory. This prevents a crash because the CPU knows what memory to use to ensure that the MSP data isn’t used, and the memory dedicated for threads is used. If the CPU keeps using the MSP, the data could be overwritten by the PSP, causing issues as the MSP has data required to run the kernel. This ensures that the MSP data integrity is maintained, which is required for the OS, while ensuring that the thread can get the memory it needs for its instructions and processes and notifying the CPU to use this memory for all the corresponding thread maintenance.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,4,'2025-06-26_22-10-55','There are two types of stack pointers, MSP and PSP. The MSP is for overall system instructions and data. The PSP, on the other hand, is used for user threads. It is for the user programs. The main reason why the __set_PSP needs to be used relates to locating where the thread and user programs in the memory. The CPU needs to know where the PSP stack begins to understand where to go to run the thread. This is especially important because the MSP is used by default. In the scenario that a thread needs to be used and the CPU thinks it needs to use the MSP stack, this might lead to the overwriting of data of the important instructions located in the MSP. The MSP and other threads must have their own memory to ensure that they can store their instructions and data safely. Additionally, the CPU must know the difference between when to use the MSP and when to use PSP for maintaining the integrity of the data. By using __set_PSP, it is ensured that the CPU know where to go instead of locating memory with invalid or undefined spaces, causing a hard fault as the CPU is finding values that are not correct.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,4,'2025-06-26_23-25-38','There are two types of stack pointers, MSP and PSP. The MSP is for overall system instructions and data. The PSP, on the other hand, is used for user threads. It is for the user programs. The main reason why the __set_PSP needs to be used relates to locating where the thread and user programs in the memory. It must be used to set the thread stack at a different mamory address than the system stack. The CPU needs to know where the PSP stack begins to understand where to go to run the thread. This is especially important because the MSP is used by default. In the scenario that a thread needs to be used and the CPU thinks it needs to use the MSP stack, this might lead to the overwriting of data of the important instructions located in the MSP. The MSP and other threads must have their own memory to ensure that they can store their instructions and data safely. Additionally, the CPU must know the difference between when to use the MSP and when to use PSP for maintaining the integrity of the data. By using __set_PSP, it is ensured that the CPU know where to go instead of locating memory with invalid or undefined spaces, causing a hard fault as the CPU is finding values that are not correct.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,4,'2025-06-27_10-24-23','There are two types of stack pointers, MSP and PSP. The MSP is for overall system instructions and data. The PSP, on the other hand, is used for user threads. It is for the user programs. The main reason why the __set_PSP needs to be used relates to setting the PSP refiter to the pointer addres of the threadès stack. This is especially important ebcuase the CPU uses the MSP by default. If the CPU is trying to use a thread and is using the MSP stack instead of the designated thread stack, it might lead to undefined behaviour or a hard fault. Thus, by setting the __setPSP(), the CPU knows where the stack it needs to use for the thread is.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,4,'2025-06-27_11-19-10','There are two types of stack pointers, MSP and PSP. The MSP is for overall system instructions and data. The PSP, on the other hand, is used for user threads. It is for the user programs. The main reason why the __set_PSP needs to be used relates to setting the PSP register to the pointer address of the thread’s stack. This is especially important because the CPU uses the MSP by default and thread stack addresses must be set to the PSP register. If the CPU is trying to use a thread and is using the MSP stack instead of the designated thread stack, it might lead to undefined behaviour or a hard fault. Thus, by setting the __setPSP(), the CPU knows where the stack it needs to use for the thread is as otherwise it will use the incorrect stack.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,4,'2025-06-27_21-27-47','There are two types of stack pointers, MSP and PSP. The MSP is for overall system instructions and data. The PSP, on the other hand, is used for user threads. It is for the user programs. The main reason why the __set_PSP needs to be used relates to setting the PSP register to the pointer address of the thread’s stack. This is especially important because the CPU uses the MSP by default and thread stack addresses must be set to the PSP register. If the CPU is trying to use a thread and is using the MSP stack instead of the designated thread stack, it might lead to undefined behaviour or a hard fault. Thus, by setting the __setPSP(), the CPU knows where the stack it needs to use for the thread is as otherwise it will use the incorrect stack.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,5,'2025-05-27_10-42-39','If the function simply printed the statement once and returned, it would cause the PC to move to the next instruction on the next register R1. This would cause the system to be confused because there is no function defined there.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,5,'2025-05-27_13-20-12','If the function simply printed the statement once and returned, the PC would have no return address as it is not defined. As a result, when resuming the program after the function call, the CPU would have a return address that does not produce valid results, causing the CPU to locate the memory that is garbage or running an invalid instruction. This could lead to a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,5,'2025-06-26_00-32-12','If the function simply printed the statement once and returned, the PC would have no return address, as it is not defined in the stack of the thread. As a result, when resuming the program after the function call, the CPU attempts to reach a return address that does not exist, leading to a hard fault as it does not know what to do.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,5,'2025-06-26_11-23-30','If the function simply printed the statement once and returned, the PC would have no return address, as it is not defined in the stack of the thread. As a result, when resuming the program after the function call, the CPU attempts to reach a return address that does not exist, leading to a hard fault as it does not know what to do.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,5,'2025-06-26_13-27-41','If the function simply printed the statement once and returned, the PC would have no return address, as it is not defined in the stack of the thread. As a result, when resuming the program after the function call, the CPU attempts to reach a return address that does not exist, leading to a hard fault as it does not know what to do.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,5,'2025-06-26_22-10-55','If the function simply printed the statement once and returned, the PC would have no return address, as it is not defined in the stack of the thread. As a result, when resuming the program after the function call, the CPU attempts to reach a return address that does not exist, leading to a hard fault as it does not know what to do.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,5,'2025-06-26_23-25-38','If the function simply printed the statement once and returned, the PC would have no return address, as it is not defined in the stack of the thread. As a result, when resuming the program after the function call, the CPU attempts to reach a return address that does not exist, leading to a hard fault as it will load the wrong or incorrect return address from the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,5,'2025-06-27_10-24-23','If the function simply printed the statement once and returned, the PC would have no return address, as it is not defined in the stack of the thread. As a result, when resuming the program after the function call, the CPU attempts to reach a return address that does not exist, leading to a hard fault as it will load the wrong or incorrect return address from the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,5,'2025-06-27_11-19-10','If the function simply printed the statement once and returned, the PC would have no return address, as it is not defined in the stack of the thread. As a result, when resuming the program after the function call, the CPU attempts to reach a return address that does not exist, leading to a hard fault as it will load the wrong or incorrect return address from the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',2,5,'2025-06-27_21-27-47','If the function simply printed the statement once and returned, the PC would have no return address, as it is not defined in the stack of the thread. As a result, when resuming the program after the function call, the CPU attempts to reach a return address that does not exist, leading to a hard fault as it will load the wrong or incorrect return address from the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,1,'2025-06-24_23-46-41','The process is begins by taking each file and compiling it into an object file. These files are put into an executable. This process can be more broken down into four steps: pre-processing, compilation, assembly and linking. In the preprocessing step, all the c++ predefined functions and definitions become more solidified into the file by being expanded and further defined using c++ processing. In compilation, the file gets turned into assembly. In the third step, assembly, the assembly turns into object code. In the Linker step, the linker takes all the files classified as objects and connects all the file, references, and libraries into a file that is executable by the operating system.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,1,'2025-06-26_00-36-52','The overall process of compiling multiple *.c files into a single executable is turning them into a single object file. However, this process can be further broken down into three steps: pre-processing, compilation and assembly. The first step is compiling. Here, the compiler expands each “predefined lines” within a each file. In this context, a “predefined line,” refers to any references made to external files or libraries that are defined somewhere else and require information outside of the file to properly understand the code and logic. This could include #include directives, macros and references to libraires. Essentially, these lines and commands must be preprocessed as context to references made in the file to define any undefined behivaour in the file. When this is being done, the compiler with replace those lines by expanding the instructions and inputing them.  The next step in the process is the compiler step. In this step,  the compiler takes the code and translates it into assembly meant for the processor. In the next step, assembly, it will take the code and further translated into machine code which is a set of binary instructions for the processor. In the Linker stage, it will take the outputted object files and libraries and create a usually executable file while understanding references to symbols and assigning final addresses to procedures.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,1,'2025-06-26_14-13-01','The overall process of compiling multiple *.c files into a single executable is turning them into a single object file. However, this process can be further broken down into three steps: pre-processing, compilation/assembly and linker. The first step is preprocessing. Here, the preprocessor expands each directive within a each file. This refers to any references made to external files or libraries that are defined somewhere else and require information outside of the file to properly understand the code and logic. This could include #include directives, macros and references to libraires. Essentially, these lines and commands must be preprocessed as context to references made in the file to define any undefined behaviour in the file. When this is being done, the compiler with replace those lines by expanding the instructions and inputing them.  The next step in the process is the compiler step. In this step,  the compiler takes the code and translates it into assembly meant for the processor. Then, the code will be further translated into machine code, which is a set of binary instructions for the processor, outputting object files. In the Linker stage, it takes the outputted object files and libraries and creates anexecutable file, understanding references to symbols and assigning final addresses to procedures.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,1,'2025-06-26_22-25-27','The overall process of compiling multiple *.c files into a single executable is turning them into a single object file. However, this process can be further broken down into three steps: pre-processing, compilation and linker.\nThe first step is preprocessing. Here, the preprocessor expands each directive within each file. This refers to any references made to external files or libraries that are defined somewhere else and require information outside of the file to properly understand the code and logic. This could include #include directives, macros and references to libraires. Essentially, these lines and commands must be preprocessed as context to references made in the file to define any undefined behaviour in the file. When this is being done, the preproessor will replace those lines by expanding the instructions and inputting them into the file.\nThe next step in the process is the compiler step. In this step, the code will be translated into machine code, which is a set of binary instructions for the processor, outputting object files.\nIn the Linker stage, it takes the outputted object files and libraries and creates an executable file. It will reference symbols and assign. final addresses to procedures. It also links external files that are referenced in the source file.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,1,'2025-06-27_21-32-26','The overall process of compiling multiple *.c files into a single executable is turning them into a single object file. However, this process can be further broken down into three steps: pre-processing, compilation and linker.\nThe first step is preprocessing. Here, the preprocessor expands each directive within each file. This refers to any references made to external files or libraries that are defined somewhere else and require information outside of the file to properly understand the code and logic. This could include #include directives, macros and references to libraires. Essentially, these lines and commands must be preprocessed as context to references made in the file to define any undefined behaviour in the file. When this is being done, the preproessor will replace those lines by expanding the instructions and inputting them into the file.\nThe next step in the process is the compiler step. In this step, the code will be translated into machine code, which is a set of binary instructions for the processor, outputting object files.\nIn the Linker stage, it takes the outputted object files and libraries and creates an executable file. It will reference symbols and assign. final addresses to procedures. It also links external files that are referenced in the source file.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,1,'2025-06-27_22-39-51','The overall process of compiling multiple *.c files can be  broken down into three steps: pre-processing, compilation and linker.\nThe first step is preprocessing. Here, the preprocessor expands each directive within each file. This refers to any references made to external files or libraries that are defined somewhere else and require information outside of the file to properly understand the code and logic. This could include #include directives, macros and references to libraires. Essentially, these lines and commands must be preprocessed as context for references made to external files and libraries in the file. When this is being done, the preprocessor will replace those lines by expanding the instructions and inputting them into the file.\nThe next step in the process is the compiler step. In this step, the code will be translated into machine code, which is a set of binary instructions for the processor, outputting object files.\nIn the Linker stage, it takes the outputted object files and libraries and creates an executable file. It will reference symbols and assign final addresses to procedures. It also links all the required files.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,2,'2025-06-24_23-46-41','These standards are due to the process of compilation. Since the compiler unpacks these include directives and places the entire file before the actual file. This would cause an issue where including these files could cause issues with the actual files where there may be competing names or recourses or overwriting that could cause issues with the data and the whole program.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,2,'2025-06-26_00-36-52','These standards are due to the process of compilation. In the first step of pre-processing, all directives, including references to any *.c files, are unpacked and expanded. This may be fine in this step. However, in the Linker stage, this could cause an error. This is because when the Linker is trying to define values for symbols, and in the scenario that referenced *.c  files and the actual file both have that definition, it is confusing to consider which one to use. This is why header files are used as they only provide prototypes to ensure that one definition only needs to be written once rather than in each file.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,2,'2025-06-26_14-13-01','These standards are due to the process of compilation. In the first step of pre-processing, all directives, including references to any *.c files, are unpacked and expanded. This may be fine in this step. However, in the Linker stage, this could cause an error. This is because when the Linker is trying to define values for symbols, and in the scenario that referenced *.c  files and the actual file both have that definition, it is confusing to consider which one to use. This is why header files are used as they only provide prototypes to ensure that one definition only needs to be written once rather than in each file.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,2,'2025-06-26_22-25-27','These standards are due to the process of compilation. In the first step of pre-processing, all directives, including references to any *.c files, are unpacked and expanded. This may be fine in this step. However, in the Linker stage, this could cause an error. This is because when the Linker is trying to define values for symbols, and in the scenario that referenced *.c  files and the actual file both have that definition, it is confusing to consider which one to use. This is why header files are used as they only provide prototypes to ensure that one definition only needs to be written once rather than in each file.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,2,'2025-06-27_21-32-26','These standards are due to the process of compilation. In the first step of pre-processing, all directives, including references to any *.c files, are unpacked and expanded. This may be fine in this step. However, in the Linker stage, this could cause an error. This is because when the Linker is trying to define values for symbols, and in the scenario that referenced *.c  files and the actual file both have that definition, it is confusing to consider which one to use. This is why header files are used as they only provide prototypes to ensure that one definition only needs to be written once rather than in each file.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,2,'2025-06-27_22-39-51','These standards are due to the process of compilation. In the first step of pre-processing, all directives, including references to any *.c files, are unpacked and expanded. This may be fine in this step. However, in the Linker stage, this could cause an error. This is because when the Linker is trying to define values for symbols, and in the scenario that referenced *.c  files and the actual file both have that definition, it is confusing to consider which one to use. This is why header files are used as they only provide prototypes to ensure that one definition only needs to be written once rather than in each file.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,3,'2025-06-24_23-46-41','In terms of setting up a thread,\nOpen Program or code\nInitialize the Kernel\nCreate the thread by allocating memory\nDefine the process and the system call to the kernel for this process\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,3,'2025-06-26_00-36-52','Step 1: The user should define the logic of the thread, which is writing code to understand what the thread does. This is similar to writing code for a function and the logic within it.\nStep 2: The user needs to define properties of the thread, including the name, priority of the thread, and how much memory is required. This allows the kernel to decide and manage it within it’s other realm of processes, threads and memory.\nStep 3: The kernel must be initialized using osKernelInitialize. It will locate the variables such as the start of the MSP/\nStep 4: In the context of this lab, the user needs to call osCreateThread, which actually allocates and sets up the stack for the thread. It calls the stack allocation, sets up the stack and the stack pointer.\nStep 5: The osKernelStart needs to be called to actually start the kernel and allow the thread to start and run.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,3,'2025-06-26_14-13-01','Step 1: The user should define the logic of the thread, which is writing code to understand what the thread does. This is similar to writing code for a function and the logic within it.\nStep 2: The user needs to define properties of the thread, including the name, priority of the thread, and how much memory is required. This allows the kernel to decide and manage it within it’s other realm of processes, threads and memory.\nStep 3: The kernel must be initialized using osKernelInitialize. It will locate the variables such as the start of the MSP/\nStep 4: In the context of this lab, the user needs to call osCreateThread, which actually allocates and sets up the stack for the thread. It calls the stack allocation, sets up the stack and the stack pointer.\nStep 5: The osKernelStart needs to be called to actually start the kernel and allow the thread to start and run.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,3,'2025-06-26_22-25-27','Step 1: The user should define the logic of the thread, which is writing code to understand what the thread does. This is similar to writing code for a function and the logic within it.\nStep 2: The user needs to define properties of the thread, including the name, priority of the thread, and how much memory is required. This allows the kernel to decide and manage it within it’s other realm of processes, threads and memory.\nStep 3: The kernel must be initialized using osKernelInitialize. It will locate the variables such as the start of the MSP/\nStep 4: In the context of this lab, the user needs to call osCreateThread, which actually allocates and sets up the stack for the thread. It calls the stack allocation, sets up the stack and the stack pointer.\nStep 5: The osKernelStart needs to be called to actually start the kernel and allow the thread to start and run.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,3,'2025-06-27_21-32-26','Step 1: The user should define the logic of the thread, which is writing code to understand what the thread does. This is similar to writing code for a function and the logic within it.\nStep 2: The user needs to define properties of the thread, including the name, priority of the thread, and how much memory is required. This allows the kernel to decide and manage it within it’s other realm of processes, threads and memory.\nStep 3: The kernel must be initialized using osKernelInitialize. It will locate the variables such as the start of the MSP/\nStep 4: In the context of this lab, the user needs to call osCreateThread, which actually allocates and sets up the stack for the thread. It calls the stack allocation, sets up the stack and the stack pointer.\nStep 5: The osKernelStart needs to be called to actually start the kernel and allow the thread to start and run.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,3,'2025-06-27_22-39-51','Step 1: The user should define the logic of the thread, which is writing code to understand what the thread does. This is similar to writing code for a function and the logic within it.\nStep 2: The user needs to define properties of the thread, including the name, priority of the thread, and how much memory is required. This allows the kernel to decide and manage it within it’s other realm of processes, threads and memory.\nStep 3: The kernel must be initialized using osKernelInitialize. It will locate the variables such as the start of the MSP/\nStep 4: In the context of this lab, the user needs to call osCreateThread, which actually allocates and sets up the stack for the thread. It calls the stack allocation, sets up the stack and the stack pointer.\nStep 5: The osKernelStart needs to be called to actually start the kernel and allow the thread to start and run.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,4,'2025-06-24_23-46-41','It is a tool that is a memory pool to manage the memory of the stack. It allows managing allocation and acts as a pool literally. When a thread is created and requires memory, it will take a chunk and then release it back when it\'s done. This will be helpful for future labs when there are more threads that are built and need to allocate memory for multiple threads.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,4,'2025-06-26_00-36-52','The stack pool is important to ensure that multiple threads can run. In terms of the OS, it allows the kernel to properly allocate space and manage switching bet ween threads. The memory required for these processes comes from the stack pool. Furthermore, the thread also must be able to have their own memory to tore their instructions and data. This memory also comes from the stack pool. The stack pool is essentially pool of memory that is divided into equal pieces. Each piece is assigned to a thread by the OS when required This is useful as when there are multiple threads, each thread can receive their own memory which allows safe use of memory. Having this pool also allows the kernel to have enough memory to ensure it does its management properly and allow each thread to have its own space, which is also to ensure safety. It also allows the dynamic deallocation and allocation of memory, to ensure that there is enough space for used and unused threads.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,4,'2025-06-26_14-13-01','The stack pool is important to ensure that multiple threads can run. The stack pool is memory that is dedicated to giving threads the memory they need to execute, in terms of calling them, switching threads, and thread data. Since the stack pool is dedicated to managing multitasking and is managed by the OS, it differentiates it from a general-purpose memory pool. In terms of the OS, it allows the kernel to properly allocate space and manage switching between threads. The memory required for these processes comes from the stack pool. Furthermore, the thread must also be able to have its own memory to store its instructions and data. This memory also comes from the stack pool. The stack pool is essentially a pool of memory that is divided into equal pieces. Each piece is assigned to a thread by the OS when required. This is useful as when there are multiple threads, each thread can receive its own memory, which allows safe use of memory. Having this pool also allows the kernel to have enough memory to ensure it does its management properly and allows each thread to have its own space, which also ensures safety. It also allows the dynamic deallocation and allocation of memory to ensure that there is enough space for used and unused threads.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,4,'2025-06-26_22-25-27','The stack pool is important to ensure that multiple threads can run. The stack pool is memory that is dedicated to giving threads the memory they need to execute, in terms of calling them, switching threads, and thread data. Since the stack pool is dedicated to managing multitasking and is managed by the OS, it differentiates it from a general-purpose memory pool. In terms of the OS, it allows the kernel to properly allocate space and manage switching between threads. The memory required for these processes comes from the stack pool. Furthermore, the thread must also be able to have its own memory to store its instructions and data. This memory also comes from the stack pool. The stack pool is essentially a pool of memory that is divided into equal pieces. Each piece is assigned to a thread by the OS when required. This is useful as when there are multiple threads, each thread can receive its own memory, which allows safe use of memory. Having this pool also allows the kernel to have enough memory to ensure it does its management properly and allows each thread to have its own space, which also ensures safety. It also allows the dynamic deallocation and allocation of memory to ensure that there is enough space for used and unused threads.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,4,'2025-06-27_21-32-26','The stack pool is important to ensure that multiple threads can run. The stack pool is memory that is dedicated to giving threads the memory they need to execute, in terms of calling them, switching threads, and thread data. Since the stack pool is dedicated to managing multitasking and is managed by the OS, it differentiates it from a general-purpose memory pool. In terms of the OS, it allows the kernel to properly allocate space and manage switching between threads. The memory required for these processes comes from the stack pool. Furthermore, the thread must also be able to have its own memory to store its instructions and data. This memory also comes from the stack pool. The stack pool is essentially a pool of memory that is divided into equal pieces. Each piece is assigned to a thread by the OS when required. This is useful as when there are multiple threads, each thread can receive its own memory, which allows safe use of memory. Having this pool also allows the kernel to have enough memory to ensure it does its management properly and allows each thread to have its own space, which also ensures safety. It also allows the dynamic deallocation and allocation of memory to ensure that there is enough space for used and unused threads.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,4,'2025-06-27_22-39-51','The stack pool is important to ensure that multiple threads can run. The stack pool is memory that is dedicated to giving threads the memory they need to execute, in terms of calling them, switching threads, and thread data. Since the stack pool is dedicated to managing multitasking and is managed by the OS, it differentiates it from a general-purpose memory pool. In terms of the OS, it allows the kernel to properly allocate space and manage switching between threads. The memory required for these processes comes from the stack pool. Furthermore, the thread must also be able to have its own memory to store its instructions and data. This memory also comes from the stack pool. The stack pool is essentially a pool of memory that is divided into equal pieces. Each piece is assigned to a thread by the OS when required. This is useful as when there are multiple threads, each thread can receive its own memory, which allows safe use of memory. Having this pool also allows the kernel to have enough memory to ensure it does its management properly and allows each thread to have its own space, which also ensures safety. It also allows the dynamic deallocation and allocation of memory to ensure that there is enough space for used and unused threads.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,5,'2025-06-24_23-46-41','The distinction between user and kernel is basically what the user programs are and what the system needs to do. The content in the user files is the actual programs themselves, whereas the kernel is what goes on at a more hardware level in terms of memory and processes. This distinction is important to ensure that security is maintained. This means that the memory and hardware can be protected from unauthorized users by acting as a barrier between the memory, hardware, processes and the user programs.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,5,'2025-06-26_00-36-52','In terms of files for the users, this includes code defining functions and threads, registering them, using the kernel and system calls to get the code running. The user only has to define the logic and the flow, while the actual kernel does the hardware-level services. In a kernel file, the threads and memory are managed along with assigning system resources, scheduling and thread switching. It is important for the user to understand the distinction to understand what the user requires to write in the code and what they don’t. For example, the code must define the functions, though it does not and cannot access the hardware-level memory. This is done to make running code easier for the user as it ensures that the system calls and what the kernel does are more deterministic. Additionally, it will ensure that safety is maintained. By only giving kernel files certain access, data security can be maintained.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,5,'2025-06-26_14-13-01','In terms of files for the users, this includes code defining functions and threads, registering them, using the kernel and system calls to get the code running. The user only has to define the logic and the flow, while the actual kernel does the hardware-level services. In a kernel file, the threads and memory are managed along with assigning system resources, scheduling and thread switching. It is important for the user to understand the distinction to understand what the user requires to write in the code and what they don’t. For example, the code must define the functions, though it does not and cannot access the hardware-level memory. This is done to make running code easier for the user as it ensures that the system calls and what the kernel does are more deterministic. Additionally, it will ensure that safety is maintained. By only giving kernel files certain access, data security can be maintained.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,5,'2025-06-26_22-25-27','In terms of files for the users, this includes code defining functions and threads, registering them, using the kernel and system calls to get the code running. The user only has to define the logic and the flow, while the actual kernel does the hardware-level services. In a kernel file, the threads and memory are managed along with assigning system resources, scheduling and thread switching. It is important for the user to understand the distinction to understand what the user requires to write in the code and what they don’t. For example, the code must define the functions, though it does not and cannot access the hardware-level memory. This is done to make running code easier for the user as it ensures that the system calls and what the kernel does are more deterministic. Additionally, it will ensure that safety is maintained. By only giving kernel files certain access, data security can be maintained.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,5,'2025-06-27_21-32-26','In terms of files for the users, this includes code defining functions and threads, registering them, using the kernel and system calls to get the code running. The user only has to define the logic and the flow, while the actual kernel does the hardware-level services. In a kernel file, the threads and memory are managed along with assigning system resources, scheduling and thread switching. It is important for the user to understand the distinction to understand what the user requires to write in the code and what they don’t. For example, the code must define the functions, though it does not and cannot access the hardware-level memory. This is done to make running code easier for the user as it ensures that the system calls and what the kernel does are more deterministic. Additionally, it will ensure that safety is maintained. By only giving kernel files certain access, data security can be maintained.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',3,5,'2025-06-27_22-39-51','In terms of files for the users, this includes code defining functions and threads, registering them, using the kernel and system calls to get the code running. The user only has to define the logic and the flow, while the actual kernel does the hardware-level services. In a kernel file, the threads and memory are managed along with assigning system resources, scheduling and thread switching. It is important for the user to understand the distinction to understand what the user requires to write in the code and what they don’t. For example, the code must define the functions, though it does not and cannot access the hardware-level memory. This is done to make running code easier for the user as it ensures that the system calls and what the kernel does are more deterministic. Additionally, it will ensure that safety is maintained. By only giving kernel files certain access, data security can be maintained.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,1,'2025-07-01_17-50-38','A round-robin scheduling can work with queues because they are both based on FIFO. As the process is created and ready to be executed, it can be added to the queue. As the processes get executed, they can be dequeued and enqueued as required based on the completion state of the process. This could be beneficial if there is an incoming of different processes as it can allow all of them to have a turn. For example, processes one and two are added in the first 5 time units and continuously switch between each other. A third process is added at 20 time units. Since a queue is being used, it can be enqueued after the last task and process one and two will be enqueued after task three. This ensures that task three does not starve and it is easier to add elements to a queue than it is to an array.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,1,'2025-07-01_19-09-57','A round-robin scheduling can work with queues because they are both based on FIFO. As the process is created and ready to be executed, it can be added to the queue. As the processes get executed, they can be dequeued and enqueued as required based on the completion state of the process. This could be beneficial if there is an incoming of different processes as it can allow all of them to have a turn. For example, processes one and two are added in the first 5 time units and continuously switch between each other. A third process is added at 20 time units. Since a queue is being used, it can be enqueued after the last task and process one and two will be enqueued after task three. This ensures that task three does not starve. Additionally, process scheduling often requires dynamic scheduling, which can be easier to achieve using a queue. Though it is faster to access elements using an array, making storage and retrieval of data more efficient. This could be beneficial as it is just as important to ensure fast switching between data. Therefore, both a queue and an array could be useful. One is more useful for sequencing the threads dynamically, while the other is more efficient at managing the threads and the switching.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,1,'2025-07-02_09-20-10','A round-robin scheduling can work with queues because they are both based on FIFO. As the process is created and ready to be executed, it can be added to the queue. As the processes get executed, they can be dequeued and enqueued as required based on the completion state of the process. This could be beneficial if there is an incoming of different processes as it can allow all of them to have a turn. For example, processes one and two are added in the first 5 time units and continuously switch between each other. A third process is added at 20 time units. Since a queue is being used, it can be enqueued after the last task and process one and two will be enqueued after task three. This ensures that task three does not starve. Additionally, process scheduling often requires dynamic scheduling, which can be easier to achieve using a queue. Though it is faster to access elements using an array, making storage and retrieval of data more efficient. This could be beneficial as it is just as important to ensure fast switching between data. Therefore, both a queue and an array could be useful. One is more useful for sequencing the threads dynamically, while the other is more efficient at managing the threads and the switching.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,1,'2025-07-02_09-58-15','A round-robin scheduling can work with queues because they are both based on FIFO. As the process is created and ready to be executed, it can be added to the queue. As the processes get executed, they can be dequeued and enqueued as required based on the completion state of the process. This could be beneficial if there is an incoming of different processes as it can allow all of them to have a turn. For example, processes one and two are added in the first 5 time units and continuously switch between each other. A third process is added at 20 time units. Since a queue is being used, it can be enqueued after the last task and process one and two will be enqueued after task three. This ensures that task three does not starve. Additionally, process scheduling often requires dynamic scheduling, which can be easier to achieve using a queue. Though, it is faster to access elements using an array, making storage and retrieval of data more efficient. This could be beneficial as it is just as important to ensure fast switching between data. Additionally, the array can schedule tasks using round-robin scheduling without the overhead introduced by using the queue, including pointer manipulation or memory allocation. Both a queue and an array could be useful. One is more useful for sequencing the threads dynamically, while the other is more efficient at managing the threads and the switching. In terms of the context of this lab, an array is a valid choice as it performs the same as a queue in terms of choosing which task to execute next.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,1,'2025-07-02_10-18-41','A round-robin scheduling can work with queues because they are both based on FIFO. As the process is created and ready to be executed, it can be added to the queue. As the processes get executed, they can be dequeued and enqueued as required based on the completion state of the process. This could be beneficial if there is an incoming of different processes as it can allow all of them to have a turn. For example, processes one and two are added in the first 5 time units and continuously switch between each other. A third process is added at 20 time units. Since a queue is being used, it can be enqueued after the last task and process one and two will be enqueued after task three. This ensures that task three does not starve. Additionally, process scheduling often requires dynamic scheduling, which can be easier to achieve using a queue. Though, it is faster to access elements using an array, making storage and retrieval of data more efficient. This could be beneficial as it is just as important to ensure fast switching between data. Additionally, the array can schedule tasks using round-robin scheduling without the overhead introduced by using the queue, including pointer manipulation or memory allocation. Both a queue and an array could be useful. One is more useful for sequencing the threads dynamically, while the other is more efficient at managing the threads and the switching. In terms of the context of this lab, an array is a valid choice as it performs the same as a queue in terms of choosing which task to execute next.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,2,'2025-07-01_17-50-38','When the SVC function is performing a function, the PendSV is set to be ready. Once SVC is complete, PendSV is run. When it is run, it sends an interrupt. During this time, it will save the current thread stack pointer into the thread’s struct. Then it will switch out the current thread registers to the next process registers. After this, it will tell the CPU to resume. Since the registers are reloaded, the next thread will run.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,2,'2025-07-01_19-09-57','When the SVC function is performing a function, the PendSV is set to be ready. Once SVC is complete, PendSV is run. When it is run, it sends an interrupt. During this time, it will save the current thread stack pointer into the thread’s struct and pop the current registers of the thread. It will then create room for registers R4 through R11 for context switching. Then, it will switch out the current thread registers to the next process registers. It will set the PSP to the stack pointer of the new thread. After this, it will tell the CPU to resume. Since the registers are reloaded, the next thread will run.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,2,'2025-07-02_09-20-10','The PendSV executes a context switch as follows:\nWhen the SVC function is performing a function, the PendSV is set to be ready.\nOnce SVC is complete, PendSV is run.\nWhen it is run, it sends an interrupt.\nThe PendSV saves the current thread stack pointer into the thread’s struct by saving the PSP value to R0. It will also save the registers of the current thread, specifically R4 to R11.\nPendSV should pop registers R4 to R11 of the current thread.\nIt will set the PSP to point to the stack pointer of the new thread.\nIt will push the registers of the new thread.\nIt will tell the CPU to resume. Since the registers are reloaded with the new thread, the next thread will run.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,2,'2025-07-02_09-58-15','The PendSV executes a context switch as follows:\nThe PendSV is set to ready as the SVC performs a function\nOnce SVC is complete, PendSV runs and sends an interrupt\nThe current thread stack pointer will be saved onto its thread stack by saving the PSP value to R0. The registers R4 to R11 will also be saved to the current thread stack and popped. This is where osSched will run to save the stack pointer of the current thread in that thread’s struct.\nSwitch the index of the thread that is running to the next thread.\nPendSV will set the PSP to point to the stack pointer of the next thread.\nThe PendSV is complete, the CPU resumes with registers that are reloaded with the context of the new thread, causing the execution of that thread.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,2,'2025-07-02_10-18-41','The PendSV executes a context switch as follows:\nPendSV sends an interrupt\nUse PSP to locate the current thread stack.\nThe current thread stack pointer will be saved onto its thread stack by saving the PSP value to R0. The registers R4 to R11 will also be saved by pushing them to the current thread stack and then popped for the next thread.\nosSched is called and the new thread is chosen.\nPendSV will set the PSP to point to the stack pointer of the next thread, and the registers R4 to R11 will be popped from the new stack.\nThe PendSV is complete, the CPU resumes with registers that are reloaded with the context of the new thread, causing the execution of that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,3,'2025-07-01_17-50-38','If the OS is set to consider the highest priority as the highest integer, this could be an issue in multiple ways. Firstly, the number of integers will constantly grow as high priority tasks come in. Furthermore, the CPU will have to spend time to figure out which is the highest priority by comparing the priority integers of each task. Additionally, this number could become infinitely large, as theoretically, the highest priority could be a very large number. Tasks can also just set themselves to a higher priority each time creating a larger number each time. This number could also take a lot of memory to store.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,3,'2025-07-01_19-09-57','If the OS is set to consider the highest priority as the highest integer, this could be an issue in multiple ways. Firstly, the number of integers will constantly grow as high priority tasks come in. Furthermore, the CPU will have to spend time to figure out which is the highest priority by comparing the priority integers of each task. Additionally, this number could become infinitely large, as theoretically, the highest priority could be a very large number. Tasks can also just set themselves to a higher priority each time creating a larger number each time. This number could also take a lot of memory to store. Also, when using a schedule that runs the highest priority task, lower priority tasks may starve.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,3,'2025-07-02_09-20-10','If the OS is set to consider the highest priority as the highest integer, this could be an issue in multiple ways. Firstly, the number of the highest integer will grow as higher priority tasks come in. It will be difficult for the CPU to decide which integer is the highest, and it will have to spend time figuring out which one is the highest by comparing the priority of the tasks.  Additionally, this number could become infinitely large, as theoretically, the highest priority could be a very large number. Tasks can also just set themselves to a higher priority each time, creating a larger number each time. Also, when using a schedule that runs the highest priority task, lower priority tasks may starve. Without looking at the status, the scheduler will always choose the highest priority. It will continue to run indefinitely as its highest priority status will remain even if the status of the thread is complete.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,3,'2025-07-02_09-58-15','If the OS is set to consider the highest priority as the highest integer, this could be an issue in multiple ways. Firstly, the number of the highest integer will grow as higher priority tasks come in. It will be difficult for the CPU to decide which integer is the highest, and it will have to spend time figuring out which one is the highest by comparing the priority of the tasks. Tasks can also just set themselves to a higher priority each time, creating a larger number each time. Also, when using a schedule that runs the highest priority task, lower priority tasks may starve. Without looking at the status, the scheduler will always choose the highest priority. It will continue to run the highest priority task indefinitely as its highest priority status will remain even if the status of the thread is complete. The scheduler will never run other functions as they will never have the highest integer.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,3,'2025-07-02_10-18-41','If the OS is set to consider the highest priority as the highest integer, this could be an issue in multiple ways. Firstly, the number of the highest integer will grow as higher priority tasks come in. It will be difficult for the CPU to decide which integer is the highest, and it will have to spend time figuring out which one is the highest by comparing the priority of the tasks. Tasks can also just set themselves to a higher priority each time, creating a larger number each time. Also, when using a schedule that runs the highest priority task, lower priority tasks may starve. Without looking at the status, the scheduler will always choose the highest priority. It will continue to run the highest priority task indefinitely as its highest priority status will remain even if the status of the thread is complete. The scheduler will never run other functions as they will never have the highest integer.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,4,'2025-07-01_17-50-38','The benefits of doing it in C could include the readability and the user friendliness. Additionally, C can be used on multiple different systems as its compilation process is what allows it to be executed on a specific architecture. In assembly, on the other hand, the code is tied to the architecture. This makes the code more reusable, especially because it can also incorporate different code in functions and libraries. Assembly could be beneficial in situations where optimization is more important than readability and portability.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,4,'2025-07-01_19-09-57','The benefits of doing it in C could include the readability and the user friendliness. Additionally, C can be used on multiple different systems as its compilation process is what allows it to be executed on a specific architecture. In assembly, on the other hand, the code is tied to the architecture. This makes the code more reusable, especially because it can also incorporate different code in functions and libraries. Assembly could be beneficial in situations where optimization is more important than readability and portability.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,4,'2025-07-02_09-20-10','The benefits of doing it in C could include the readability and the user friendliness. Additionally, C can be used on multiple different systems as its compilation process is what allows it to be executed on a specific architecture. In assembly, on the other hand, the code is tied to the architecture. This makes the code more reusable, especially because it can also incorporate different code in functions and libraries. Assembly could be beneficial in situations where optimization is more important than readability and portability.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,4,'2025-07-02_09-58-15','The benefits of doing it in C could include the readability and the user friendliness. Additionally, C can be used on multiple different systems as its compilation process is what allows it to be executed on a specific architecture. In assembly, on the other hand, the code is tied to the architecture. This makes the code more reusable, especially because it can also incorporate different code in functions and libraries. Assembly could be beneficial in situations where optimization is more important than readability and portability.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,4,'2025-07-02_10-18-41','The benefits of doing it in C could include the readability and the user friendliness. Additionally, C can be used on multiple different systems as its compilation process is what allows it to be executed on a specific architecture. In assembly, on the other hand, the code is tied to the architecture. This makes the code more reusable, especially because it can also incorporate different code in functions and libraries. Assembly could be beneficial in situations where optimization is more important than readability and portability.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,5,'2025-07-01_17-50-38','Yes, the OS will still work. The way the osSched works is through taking the incrementation of the index in the thread array and then the modulus of the number of threads. This implements a round robin scheduling of the threads. Each thread will run one after the other, and the overall cycle will wrap into a circle. If there is only one thread, there will only be one step in the cycle, meaning that it will just come back to itself and continue to run. In terms of reality, this makes sense. There may be scenarios where is only one thread that is being run and there are no other threads to be run.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,5,'2025-07-01_19-09-57','Yes, the OS will still work. The way the osSched works is through taking the incrementation of the index in the thread array and then the modulus of the number of threads. This implements a round robin scheduling of the threads. Each thread will run one after the other, and the overall cycle will wrap into a circle. If there is only one thread, there will only be one step in the cycle, meaning that it will just come back to itself and continue to run. In terms of reality, this makes sense. There may be scenarios where is only one thread that is being run and there are no other threads to be run.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,5,'2025-07-02_09-20-10','Yes, the OS will still work. The way the osSched works is through taking the incrementation of the index in the thread array and then the modulus of the number of threads. This implements a round robin scheduling of the threads. Each thread will run one after the other, and the overall cycle will wrap into a circle. If there is only one thread, there will only be one step in the cycle, meaning that it will just come back to itself and continue to run. In terms of reality, this makes sense. There may be scenarios where is only one thread that is being run and there are no other threads to be run.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,5,'2025-07-02_09-58-15','Yes, the OS will still work. The way the osSched works is through taking the incrementation of the index in the thread array and then the modulus of the number of threads. This implements a round robin scheduling of the threads. Each thread will run one after the other, and the overall cycle will wrap into a circle. If there is only one thread, there will only be one step in the cycle, meaning that the task will yield, the scheduler reschedules the same task, and the task comes back to itself and continues to run. In terms of reality, this makes sense. There may be scenarios where there is only one thread that is being run and there are no other threads to be run.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',4,5,'2025-07-02_10-18-41','Yes, the OS will still work. The way the osSched works is through taking the incrementation of the index in the thread array and then the modulus of the number of threads. This implements a round robin scheduling of the threads. Each thread will run one after the other, and the overall cycle will wrap into a circle. If there is only one thread, there will only be one step in the cycle, meaning that the task will yield, the scheduler reschedules the same task, and the task comes back to itself and continues to run. In terms of reality, this makes sense. There may be scenarios where there is only one thread that is being run and there are no other threads to be run.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,1,'2025-07-29_12-16-38','The first step for the user is to write the function that must be executed. Next, the user will use the osCreateThread function to pass the location of the function to be executed. The user will pass an argument with reference to the function that gets passed to the function osCreateThread. If there is a specific deadline, it will be passed to the function osCreateThreadwithDeadline. The user will also pass any required user data. Next, the kernel will use the arguments passed to create a stack for the user. This also includes allocating the user data passed to the proper register (R3). It will also put the thread definitions into a struct that will then go into an array of threads. The properties include the location of the thread (stackptr), the thread function to be executed, the thread number, the timeslice and the current runtime. This works because the user is able to create the processes it need to be executed with enough customization to ensure things run properly while the kernel handles the necessary allocation and organization to ensure all user programs are properly allocated in a safe manner while keeping track of the threads.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,1,'2025-07-29_16-32-46','The first step for the user is to write the function that must be executed and initialize data for the argument. Next, the user will use the osCreateThread function to pass the pointer of the function to be executed and argument data. If there is a specific deadline, it will be passed to the function osCreateThreadwithDeadline. The OS will use the arguments passed to create a stack for the user. This also includes allocating the user data passed to the proper register (R0) in the stack. It will also put the thread properties into a struct that will then go into an array of threads. The properties include the location of the thread (stackptr), the thread function to be executed, the thread number, the timeslice and the current runtime. This works because the user is able to create the processes it need to be executed with enough customization to ensure things run properly while the kernel handles the necessary allocation and organization to ensure all user programs are properly allocated in a safe manner while keeping track of the threads. This follows the AAPCS standard.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,1,'2025-07-29_17-21-15','The first step for the user is to write the function that must be executed and initialize data for the argument. Next, the user will use the osCreateThread function to pass the pointer of the function to be executed and argument data. If there is a specific deadline, it will be passed to the function osCreateThreadwithDeadline. The OS will use the arguments passed to create a stack for the user. This also includes allocating the user data passed to the proper register (R0) in the stack. It will also put the thread properties into a struct that will then go into an array of threads. The properties include the location of the thread (stackptr), the thread function to be executed, the thread number, the timeslice and the current runtime. This works because the user is able to create the processes it need to be executed with enough customization to ensure things run properly while the kernel handles the necessary allocation and organization to ensure all user programs are properly allocated in a safe manner while keeping track of the threads. This follows the AAPCS standard.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,1,'2025-07-30_18-18-06','The first step for the user is to write the function that must be executed and initialize data for the argument. Next, the user will use the osCreateThread function to pass the pointer of the function to be executed and argument data. If there is a specific deadline, it will be passed to the function osCreateThreadwithDeadline. The OS will use the arguments passed to create a stack for the user. This also includes allocating the user data passed to the proper register (R0) in the stack. It will also put the thread properties into a struct that will then go into an array of threads. The properties include the location of the thread (stackptr), the thread function to be executed, the thread number, the timeslice and the current runtime. This works because the user is able to create the processes it need to be executed with enough customization to ensure things run properly while the kernel handles the necessary allocation and organization to ensure all user programs are properly allocated in a safe manner while keeping track of the threads. This follows the AAPCS standard.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,2,'2025-07-29_12-16-38','The function osYield is to trigger a context switch. If the context switching functions are already triggered, retriggering them could lead to issues such as the data being misplaced since it is in the middle of saving or loading a new thread. This could cause hard faults or behaviour that is undefined. Additionally, if the function is called within these function, the kernel may be stuck in a loop as it keeps switching contexts in the middle of nowhere, not allowing the kernel to properly organize stack data and choose the right thread. The other processes may never get the chance to run.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,2,'2025-07-29_16-32-46','The function osYield is to trigger a context switch voluntarily and stop it’s CPU use for time-being. It will trigger a system call. If the context switching functions are already triggered, retriggering them could lead to issues such as the stack data being misplaced since it is in the middle of using the thread context. Additionally, this could corrupt the data in way that leads to hard faults or undefined behaviour.  This could cause hard faults or behaviour that is undefined. Additionally, if the function is called within these function, the OS  may be stuck in a loop as it keeps switching contexts in the middle of nowhere, not allowing the kernel to properly organize stack data and choose the right thread. As a result, the OS will be stuck in this loop and the other processes may never get the chance to run. The saved data will also constantly get changed, leading to corrupted data. It could also overflow the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,2,'2025-07-29_17-21-15','The function osYield is to trigger a context switch voluntarily and stop it’s CPU use for time-being. It will trigger a system call. If the context switching functions are already triggered, retriggering them could lead to issues such as the stack data being misplaced since it is in the middle of using the thread context. Additionally, this could corrupt the data in way that leads to hard faults or undefined behaviour.  This could cause hard faults or behaviour that is undefined. Additionally, if the function is called within these function, the OS  may be stuck in a loop as it keeps switching contexts in the middle of nowhere, not allowing the kernel to properly organize stack data and choose the right thread. As a result, the OS will be stuck in this loop and the other processes may never get the chance to run. The saved data will also constantly get changed, leading to corrupted data. It could also overflow the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,2,'2025-07-30_18-18-06','The function osYield is to trigger a context switch voluntarily and stop it’s CPU use for time-being. It will trigger a system call. If the context switching functions are already triggered, retriggering them could lead to issues such as the stack data being misplaced since it is in the middle of using the thread context. Additionally, this could corrupt the data in way that leads to hard faults or undefined behaviour.  This could cause hard faults or behaviour that is undefined. Additionally, if the function is called within these function, the OS  may be stuck in a loop as it keeps switching contexts in the middle of nowhere, not allowing the kernel to properly organize stack data and choose the right thread. As a result, the OS will be stuck in this loop and the other processes may never get the chance to run. The saved data will also constantly get changed, leading to corrupted data. It could also overflow the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,3,'2025-07-29_12-16-38','The benefits of having a smaller fundamental OS clock time would mean that timing-based tasks would be more accurate. This could be useful in tasks that require high precision and accuracy. However, it would mean that the power consumption would increase and the CPU would need to be used more to handle the increased number of timer interrupts. This would take power and CPU time from the threads and other processes. Thus, having a smaller clock time could be useful in scenarios where accuracy it the most important factor, whereas in areas where there are more threads or processes, a larger clock time would be beneficial.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,3,'2025-07-29_16-32-46','The benefits of having a smaller fundamental OS clock time would mean that timing-based tasks would be more accurate. This could be useful in tasks that require high precision and accuracy. However, it would mean that the power consumption would increase and the CPU would need to be used more to handle the increased number of timer interrupts. This would take power and CPU time from the threads and other processes. Thus, having a smaller clock time could be useful in scenarios where accuracy it the most important factor, whereas in areas where there are more threads or processes, a larger clock time would be beneficial.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,3,'2025-07-29_17-21-15','The benefits of having a smaller fundamental OS clock time would mean that timing-based tasks would be more accurate. This could be useful in tasks that require high precision and accuracy. However, it would mean that the power consumption would increase and the CPU would need to be used more to handle the increased number of timer interrupts. This would take power and CPU time from the threads and other processes. Thus, having a smaller clock time could be useful in scenarios where accuracy it the most important factor, whereas in areas where there are more threads or processes, a larger clock time would be beneficial.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,3,'2025-07-30_18-18-06','The benefits of having a smaller fundamental OS clock time would mean that timing-based tasks would be more accurate. This could be useful in tasks that require high precision. The higher number sof ticks and precision would also increase the responsiveness of the system as it can respond within a tick of 0.1ms rather than 1ms. However, it would mean that the power consumption would increase and the CPU would need to be used more to handle the increased number of timer interrupts. This would take power and CPU time from the threads and other processes. Thus, having a smaller clock time could be useful in scenarios where accuracy it the most important factor, whereas in areas where there are more threads or processes, a larger clock time would be beneficial.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,4,'2025-07-29_12-16-38','The difference between cooperative and preemptive multitasking from the user perspective is that in cooperative multitasking, the task can voluntarily yield to the next task. However, a task can also never yield, meaning that all the CPU time goes to that task. The user must be careful in terms of choosing the timing of a task. In pre-emptive, the OS starts the interrupts and handles the context switching, meaning that the user does not have to worry about task switching or ensuring that tasks get fair access to the CPU. However, this also means that it is less predictable from the user side, as it does not know when the context may be switched. In terms of the OS, co-operative multitasking means that the OS only needs to worry about context switching when a context-switching function is called by the user. On the other hand, pre-emptive is where the OS must first save the task and then choose the next thread. This also means that in cooperative multitasking, the overhead only occurs when the switch is called, versus in pre-emptive multitasking, the overhead is higher as the OS must also account for when the task needs to be switched. In cooperative multitasking, it also means that the OS does not necessarily have control over tasks in the scenario that a task takes too much CPU time. In preemptive multitasking, the OS can force a task to switch to ensure that all tasks get fair access to the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,4,'2025-07-29_16-32-46','The difference between cooperative and preemptive multitasking from the user perspective is that in cooperative multitasking, the task can voluntarily yield to the next task. However, a task can also never yield, meaning that all the CPU time goes to that task. The user must be careful in terms of choosing the timing of a task. In pre-emptive, the OS starts the interrupts and handles the context switching, meaning that the user does not have to worry about task switching or ensuring that tasks get fair access to the CPU. However, this also means that it is less predictable from the user side, as it does not know when the context may be switched. In terms of the OS, co-operative multitasking means that the OS only needs to worry about context switching when a context-switching function is called by the user. On the other hand, pre-emptive is where the OS must first save the task and then choose the next thread. This also means that in cooperative multitasking, the overhead only occurs when the switch is called, versus in pre-emptive multitasking, the overhead is higher as the OS must also account for when the task needs to be switched. In cooperative multitasking, it also means that the OS does not necessarily have control over tasks in the scenario that a task takes too much CPU time. In preemptive multitasking, the OS can force a task to switch to ensure that all tasks get fair access to the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,4,'2025-07-29_17-21-15','The difference between cooperative and preemptive multitasking from the user perspective is that in cooperative multitasking, the task can voluntarily yield to the next task. However, a task can also never yield, meaning that all the CPU time goes to that task. The user must be careful in terms of choosing the timing of a task. In pre-emptive, the OS starts the interrupts and handles the context switching, meaning that the user does not have to worry about task switching or ensuring that tasks get fair access to the CPU. However, this also means that it is less predictable from the user side, as it does not know when the context may be switched. In terms of the OS, co-operative multitasking means that the OS only needs to worry about context switching when a context-switching function is called by the user. On the other hand, pre-emptive is where the OS must first save the task and then choose the next thread. This also means that in cooperative multitasking, the overhead only occurs when the switch is called, versus in pre-emptive multitasking, the overhead is higher as the OS must also account for when the task needs to be switched. In cooperative multitasking, it also means that the OS does not necessarily have control over tasks in the scenario that a task takes too much CPU time. In preemptive multitasking, the OS can force a task to switch to ensure that all tasks get fair access to the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,4,'2025-07-30_18-18-06','The difference between cooperative and preemptive multitasking from the user perspective is that in cooperative multitasking, the task can voluntarily yield to the next task. However, a task can also never yield, meaning that all the CPU time goes to that task. The user must be careful in terms of choosing the timing of a task. In pre-emptive, the OS starts the interrupts and handles the context switching, meaning that the user does not have to worry about task switching or ensuring that tasks get fair access to the CPU. However, this also means that it is less predictable from the user side, as it does not know when the context may be switched. In terms of the OS, co-operative multitasking means that the OS only needs to worry about context switching when a context-switching function is called by the user. On the other hand, pre-emptive is where the OS must first save the task and then choose the next thread. This also means that in cooperative multitasking, the overhead only occurs when the switch is called, versus in pre-emptive multitasking, the overhead is higher as the OS must also account for when the task needs to be switched. In cooperative multitasking, it also means that the OS does not necessarily have control over tasks in the scenario that a task takes too much CPU time. In preemptive multitasking, the OS can force a task to switch to ensure that all tasks get fair access to the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,5,'2025-07-29_12-16-38','They can work together as the OS allots the desired timeslice for the yielding task and a pre-decided timeslice for the pre-emptive task. This means that the yielding tasks can run for as long as it needs, as it will call a context switch on is own, and the OS does not have to worry about the context switch. The OS just needs to properly save and load the next task. The OS will handle the context switch on its own for the pre-emptive context switching alongside saving and loading the next task. Both tasks still context switch and have fair access to the PCU time. The only difference is what the OS handles.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,5,'2025-07-29_16-32-46','They can work together as the OS allots the desired timeslice for the yielding task and a pre-decided timeslice for the pre-emptive task. This means that the yielding tasks can run for as long as it needs, as it will call a context switch on is own, and the OS does not have to worry about the context switch. The OS just needs to properly save and load the next task. The OS will handle the context switch on its own for the pre-emptive context switching alongside saving and loading the next task. Both tasks still context switch and have fair access to the PCU time. The only difference is what the OS handles. The reason this works is because of PendSV. PendSV does the actual context switching, and both types of processes will eventually call this trigger. Having this trigger defined is important to ensure that the trigger is called as the lowest priority and does not interrupt other interrupts.\n',0,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,5,'2025-07-29_17-21-15','They can work together as the OS allots the desired timeslice for the yielding task and a pre-decided timeslice for the pre-emptive task. This means that the yielding tasks can run for as long as it needs, as it will call a context switch on is own and the OS will force the pre-emptive task to end when it ‘s timeslice has reached zero. Both tasks still context switch and have fair access to the CPU time. The reason this works is because of PendSV. PendSV does the actual context switching, and both types of processes will eventually call this trigger. After the yielding task yields, it’ll cause a trigger that will eventually call PendSV which switches the context. PendSV will also be called once the pre-emptive task is forced to switch. Having this trigger defined is important to ensure that the trigger is called as the lowest priority and does not interrupt other interrupts. It also ensures that context is switched regardless of how to trigger is called. By defining it in a manner that both types of tasks can use the same code, it ensures that the trigger does the same thing and the trigger is eventually used by either task.\n',1,'s25'),('kbsavla',5,5,'2025-07-30_18-18-06','They can work together as the OS allots the desired timeslice for the yielding task and a pre-decided timeslice for the pre-emptive task. This means that the yielding tasks can run for as long as it needs, as it will call a context switch on is own and the OS will force the pre-emptive task to end when it ‘s timeslice has reached zero. Both tasks still context switch and have fair access to the CPU time. The reason this works is because of PendSV. PendSV does the actual context switching, and both types of processes will eventually call this trigger. After the yielding task yields, it’ll cause a trigger that will eventually call PendSV which switches the context. PendSV will also be called once the pre-emptive task is forced to switch. Having this trigger defined is important to ensure that the trigger is called as the lowest priority and does not interrupt other interrupts. It also ensures that context is switched regardless of how to trigger is called. By defining it in a manner that both types of tasks can use the same code, it ensures that the trigger does the same thing and the trigger is eventually used by either task.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',1,1,'2025-06-23_23-15-41','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a software layer that acts as an interface between the application code and the hardware registers of the microcontroller. It provides a collection of functions that make it easier to interact with the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',1,1,'2025-06-24_17-59-26','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a software layer that acts as an interface between the application code and the hardware registers of the microcontroller. It provides a collection of functions that make it easier to interact with the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',1,2,'2025-06-23_23-15-41','The __io_putchar function is used to write a single character to the appropriate IO channel. In the case of this lab, it is UART2. This function is needed in the printf function, which calls it multiple times to print a string to the console.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',1,2,'2025-06-24_17-59-26','The __io_putchar function is used to write a single character to the appropriate IO channel. In the case of this lab, it is UART2. This function is used in the printf function, which calls it multiple times to print a string to the console. This function is necessary because printf is part of the C standard library, which is platform independent; it does not know how to interact with the specific platform hardware. __io_putchar acts as the bridge between generic C code and hardware I/O interaction to successfully print. This method of implementation allows for printf to remain abstracted and portable between systems since you only need to change the implementation of __io_putchar.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',1,3,'2025-06-23_23-15-41','The debugger allows us to inspect the value of variables and registers at different points in the program. For this lab, the debugger was used to make sure that the MSP and PSP values were set properly. It is better to use the debugger instead of printf because it provides more debug information, and allows for the usage of breakpoints, allowing us to stop the code in the middle for inspection. Additionally, using printf is tedious, and can cause additional errors as you are introducing new code into the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',1,3,'2025-06-24_17-59-26','The debugger allows us to inspect the value of variables and registers at different points in the program. For this lab, the debugger was used to make sure that the MSP and PSP values were set properly. It is better to use the debugger instead of printf because it provides more debug information, and allows for the usage of breakpoints, allowing us to stop the code in the middle for inspection. Additionally, using printf is tedious, and can cause additional errors as you are introducing new code into the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',1,4,'2025-06-23_23-15-41','The value for MSP_INIT_VAL is the start of the main stack, and we know this value grows downwards as items are pushed. In essence, MSP_INIT_VAL is the bottom of the main stack. We know that the size of the main stack is 0x400, and that we want PSP to be after the main stack. Therefore to get the value for PSP_VAL, we must take the value of MSP_INIT_VAL, and subtract 0x400.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',1,4,'2025-06-24_17-59-26','The method used for stack allocation works by allocating the next stack’s starting address at a location that is known to be outside the addresses used by other stacks. In the case of this lab, we know the value for MSP_INIT_VAL, and the size of the MSP stack, which is 0x400. Therefore, to get the starting address of PSP_VAL, we take MSP_INIT_VAL and subtract 0x400 since the stack address grows downwards. Doing this guarantees that the addresses allocated for the PSP do not overlap with the addresses allocated for the MSP. If MSP_INIT_VAL was not known, or we used an incorrect value, we could end up overlapping stacks, which can cause data corruption, undefined behaviour, or crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',1,5,'2025-06-23_23-15-41','To allocate more stacks, you would do the same process of subtracting the stack size from the MSP_INIT_VAL value, assuming you knew the size of each stack you want to allocate. For example, we determined the value of PSP_VAL by subtracting 0x400, the size of the main stack, from MSP_INIT_VAL, the bottom of the main stack. Let’s say the size of all the stacks are the same size of the main stack, which is 0x400. If you wanted another stack to be allocated after PSP_VAL, you would subtract two times 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, or 0x400 from PSP_VAL. This process continues for each stack you want to allocate.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',1,5,'2025-06-24_17-59-26','To allocate more stacks, you could follow the same allocation process used for allocating the PSP stack. For example, stack number N is allocated as:\nmyStackN = MSP_INIT_VAL – N*0x400;\nAssuming that the size of each stack is the same of 0x400. If the stacks have a different size, you would start at MSP_INIT_VAL and subtract the size of each stack that precedes it. So if you wanted to allocate a stack after stacks of size 0x400 and 0x200, you would subtract 0x600. This guarantees that new allocated stacks will never overlap with any other stacks because the address is outside of the stacks’ maximum size.\nAllocating multiple stacks also requires that you don’t end up in an address outside of the RAM space after allocation, or else undefined behaviour may occur. You can do this by taking MSP_INIT_VAL and subtracting N*0x400, (the size of all the stacks combined,) and seeing if that address is beyond the bounds of the RAM. The RAM has a known start and end which can be found in a data sheet.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,1,'2025-06-25_11-19-29','In the call to the assembly function, we input a pointer to our print continuously function. This is placed into R0, which is the typical register for function parameters. The assembly code then moves the function pointer from R0 to PC. PC is the program counter, and it holds the address of what will run next in the program. Since the print continuously function is placed in the PC, it will be run and “Hello World!” is continuously printed.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,1,'2025-06-25_11-58-00','In the call to the assembly function, we input a pointer to our print continuously function. This is placed into R0, which is the typical register for function parameters. The assembly code then moves the function pointer from R0 to PC. PC is the program counter, and it holds the address of what will run next in the program. Since the print continuously function is placed in the PC, it will be run and “Hello World!” is continuously printed.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,1,'2025-06-25_15-15-05','In the call to the assembly function, we input a pointer to our print continuously function. This is placed into R0, which is the typical register for function parameters. The assembly code then moves the function pointer from R0 to PC. PC is the program counter, and it holds the address of what will run next in the program. Since the print continuously function is placed in the PC, it will be run and “Hello World!” is continuously printed.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',2,1,'2025-06-25_22-56-25','In the call to the assembly function, we pass a pointer to our print continuously function. The pointer is placed into register R0, which is the standard register for passing the first argument in the CortexM4 core. The function’s assembly code then moves the function pointer from R0 to the Program Counter (PC). The PC holds the address of the next instruction to be run in the program. Since the print_continuously function is loaded in the PC, execution jumps to that location in memory and starts the print_contiunously function. This is effectively the same as making a function call to print_continuously at the assembly level, and thus the program continuously prints “Hello World!” in an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,1,'2025-06-26_18-02-57','In the call to the assembly function, we pass a pointer to our print continuously function. The pointer is placed into register R0, which is the standard register for passing the first argument in the CortexM4 core. The function’s assembly code then moves the function pointer from R0 to the Program Counter (PC). The PC holds the address of the next instruction to be run in the program. Since the print_continuously function is loaded in the PC, execution jumps to that location in memory and starts the print_contiunously function. This is effectively the same as making a function call to print_continuously at the assembly level, and thus the program continuously prints “Hello World!” in an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,2,'2025-06-25_11-19-29','A system call is a software generated interrupt that allows for user programs to interact with the kernel of the OS. For safety and security reasons, the kernel has elevated permissions and must be used to perform elevated access tasks.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',2,2,'2025-06-25_11-58-00','A system call is a software generated interrupt that allows for user programs to interact with the kernel of the OS. For safety and security reasons, the kernel has elevated permissions and must be used to perform elevated access tasks.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',2,2,'2025-06-25_15-15-05','A system call is a software generated interrupt that allows for user programs to interact with the kernel of the OS. For safety and security reasons, the kernel has elevated permissions and must be used to perform elevated access tasks.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',2,2,'2025-06-25_22-56-25','A system call is a software generated interrupt that allows for user programs to interact with the kernel of the OS by triggering a transition from the user mode to kernel mode. They are a special type of function call provided by the OS. The purpose of switching to the kernel mode is that it has elevated permissions that allow the user to perform elevated access tasks. These types of tasks are only accessible by the kernel for safety and security reasons. For example, system calls can be used to allocate memory, create processes, and write files. Besides acting as a method of requesting elevated access, it helps abstract low level access to hardware. The user does not need to concern themselves with how write() is implemented. System calls also allow the OS to manage resources such as CPU time, memory, Disk I/O, across processes.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,2,'2025-06-26_18-02-57','A system call is a software generated interrupt that allows for user programs to interact with the kernel of the OS by triggering a transition from the user mode to kernel mode. They are a special type of function call provided by the OS. The purpose of switching to the kernel mode is that it has elevated permissions that allow the user to perform elevated access tasks. These types of tasks are only accessible by the kernel for safety and security reasons. For example, system calls can be used to allocate memory, create processes, and write files. Besides acting as a method of requesting elevated access, it helps abstract low level access to hardware. The user does not need to concern themselves with how write() is implemented. System calls also allow the OS to manage resources such as CPU time, memory, Disk I/O, across processes.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,3,'2025-06-25_11-19-29','Firstly, the MSP_INIT_VAL was found, and 0x400 was subtracted to get the initial value of the thread stack. This is done to ensure that the address dedicated for the thread stack does not overlap with the MSP stack, which could cause undefine behaviour. Once the initial value of the thread stack was defined, the thread stack was “set up” by pushing register values to the stack. First was the xPSR, then the PC, then the rest of the values. Register values need to be stored on the stack so that they can be popped and stored into the CPU’s registers when the thread is running so that the thread has the proper context. For example, the stack included the function print_continuously which is what we wanted to run.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,3,'2025-06-25_11-58-00','Firstly, the MSP_INIT_VAL was found, and 0x400 was subtracted to get the initial value of the thread stack. This is done to ensure that the address dedicated for the thread stack does not overlap with the MSP stack, which could cause undefine behaviour. Once the initial value of the thread stack was defined, the thread stack was “set up” by pushing register values to the stack. First was the xPSR, then the PC, then the rest of the values. Register values need to be stored on the stack so that they can be popped and stored into the CPU’s registers when the thread is running so that the thread has the proper context. For example, the stack included the function print_continuously which is what we wanted to run.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,3,'2025-06-25_15-15-05','Firstly, the MSP_INIT_VAL was found, and 0x400 was subtracted to get the initial value of the thread stack. This is done to ensure that the address dedicated for the thread stack does not overlap with the MSP stack, which could cause undefine behaviour. Once the initial value of the thread stack was defined, the thread stack was “set up” by pushing register values to the stack. First was the xPSR, then the PC, then the rest of the values. Register values need to be stored on the stack so that they can be popped and stored into the CPU’s registers when the thread is running so that the thread has the proper context. For example, the stack included the function print_continuously which is what we wanted to run.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,3,'2025-06-25_22-56-25','Firstly, the MSP_INIT_VAL was found, and 0x400 was subtracted to get the initial value of the thread stack. This is done to ensure that the address dedicated for the thread stack does not overlap with the MSP stack, which could cause undefine behaviour. Once the initial value of the thread stack was defined, the thread stack was “set up” by pushing register values to the stack. First was the xPSR, then the PC, then the rest of the values. Register values need to be stored on the stack so that they can be popped and stored into the CPU’s registers when the thread is running so that the thread has the proper context. For example, the stack included the function print_continuously which is what we wanted to run.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,3,'2025-06-26_18-02-57','Firstly, the MSP_INIT_VAL was found, and 0x400 was subtracted to get the initial value of the thread stack. This is done to ensure that the address dedicated for the thread stack does not overlap with the MSP stack, which could cause undefine behaviour. Once the initial value of the thread stack was defined, the thread stack was “set up” by pushing register values to the stack. First was the xPSR, then the PC, then the rest of the values. Register values need to be stored on the stack so that they can be popped and stored into the CPU’s registers when the thread is running so that the thread has the proper context. For example, the stack included the function print_continuously which is what we wanted to run.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,4,'2025-06-25_11-19-29','Before running the thread, you need to trick the chip into believing it is already running the thread. To do that you need to use __Set_PSP to set the address of the PSP to the stack we defined, so that the PSP thinks that it’s running the thread we want. After __Set_PSP is called, the system will pop the registers off the stack into the actual hardware registers, setting up the context for the thread we want to run.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,4,'2025-06-25_11-58-00','Before running the thread, you need to trick the chip into believing it is already running the thread. To do that you need to use __Set_PSP to set the address of the PSP to the stack we defined, so that the PSP thinks that it’s running the thread we want. After __Set_PSP is called, the system will pop the registers off the stack into the actual hardware registers, setting up the context for the thread we want to run.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',2,4,'2025-06-25_15-15-05','Before running the thread, you need to trick the chip into believing it is already running the thread. To do that you need to use __Set_PSP to set the address of the PSP to the stack we defined, so that the PSP thinks that it’s running the thread we want. After __Set_PSP is called, the system will pop the registers off the stack into the actual hardware registers, setting up the context for the thread we want to run.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',2,4,'2025-06-25_22-56-25','Before running a thread, you must initialize the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the correct stack for the thread. The CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) for system level code such as interrupt service routines, kernels and privileged code, and it uses the PSP to run threads. Therefore, the PSP must be set properly to run a thread.\nIf the PSP isn’t set using __set_PSP() before switching to the thread, the CPU will attempt to pop the saved context into the registers from an uninitialized or incorrect stack location. This can cause the CPU to access invalid memory, which triggers a hard fault exception, or in other words a crash.\nSetting the PSP ensures that when the thread starts executing it has a valid stack context to restore the registers from so that the CPU can safety run the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,4,'2025-06-26_18-02-57','Before running a thread, you must initialize the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the correct stack for the thread. The CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) for system level code such as interrupt service routines, kernels and privileged code, and it uses the PSP to run threads. Therefore, the PSP must be set properly to run a thread.\nIf the PSP isn’t set using __set_PSP() before switching to the thread, the CPU will attempt to pop the saved context into the registers from an uninitialized or incorrect stack location. This can cause the CPU to access invalid memory, which triggers a hard fault exception, or in other words a crash.\nSetting the PSP ensures that when the thread starts executing it has a valid stack context to restore the registers from so that the CPU can safety run the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,5,'2025-06-25_11-19-29','If this is the case, this means that the thread’s function completed and is ready to return to whatever address is stored in the Link Register (LR). The LR must be defined but since it was not in the case of this lab, it may hold a garbage value. Thus once the thread completes, the system will experience undefined behaviour and potentially crash because it does not know where to resume.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',2,5,'2025-06-25_11-58-00','If this is the case, this means that the thread’s function completed and is ready to return to whatever address is stored in the Link Register (LR). The LR must be defined but since it was not in the case of this lab, it may hold a garbage value. Thus once the thread completes, the system will experience undefined behaviour and potentially crash because it does not know where to resume.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',2,5,'2025-06-25_15-15-05','If this is the case, this means that the thread’s function completed and is ready to return to whatever address is stored in the Link Register (LR). The LR must be defined but since it was not in the case of this lab, it may hold a garbage value. Thus once the thread completes, the system will experience undefined behaviour and potentially crash because it does not know where to resume.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',2,5,'2025-06-25_22-56-25','If the thread function simply printed once and returned, the CPU would attempt to return to the address stored in the Link Register (LR), which holds the return address of where the program will resume. However, in this lab, the LR was not properly initialized, so it may contain an invalid or garbage value. When the thread tries to return using this corrupted LR value, the CPU attempts to load an invalid address which leads to a hard fault exception, or in other words, a crash. This happens because the process cannot correctly restore the program counter (PC) from the corrupted returned address, causing the system to crash or enter an undefined state.\nThus, without a proper return address saved in the thread’s stack or LR, the system experiences a fault due to the failure to correctly resume execution after the thread function completes.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',2,5,'2025-06-26_18-02-57','If the thread function simply printed once and returned, the CPU would attempt to return to the address stored in the Link Register (LR), which holds the return address of where the program will resume. However, in this lab, the LR was not properly initialized, so it may contain an invalid or garbage value. When the thread tries to return using this corrupted LR value, the CPU attempts to load an invalid address which leads to a hard fault exception, or in other words, a crash. This happens because the process cannot correctly restore the program counter (PC) from the corrupted returned address, causing the system to crash or enter an undefined state.\nThus, without a proper return address saved in the thread’s stack or LR, the system experiences a fault due to the failure to correctly resume execution after the thread function completes.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',3,1,'2025-06-26_21-55-47','In C, multiple .c files are compiled into a single executable in three steps.\nThe first step Is preprocessing, where lines starting with # are delt with. #include is replaced with the content of the header files specified. #define macros are expanded and every occurrence of the macro is replaced, etc.\nThe second step in compilation and the second is linking. In the compliation step, each .c file is independently into an object file indicated by .o. This is done because .c contains human readable code and it needs to be converted into machine readable code which is put into .o files.\nAfterwards, as the name suggests, the linker links to .o files together into a single executable. It does this by resolving references between files, so that a function in one .c file can be used in a function in another .c file. Essentially, the linker is compiling all the .o files into one big final executable.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',3,1,'2025-06-27_11-58-04','In C, multiple .c files are compiled into a single executable in three steps.\nThe first step Is preprocessing, where lines starting with # are delt with. #include is replaced with the content of the header files specified. #define macros are expanded and every occurrence of the macro is replaced, etc.\nThe second step in compilation and the second is linking. In the compliation step, each .c file is independently into an object file indicated by .o. This is done because .c contains human readable code and it needs to be converted into machine readable code which is put into .o files.\nAfterwards, as the name suggests, the linker links to .o files together into a single executable. It does this by resolving references between files, so that a function in one .c file can be used in a function in another .c file. Essentially, the linker is compiling all the .o files into one big final executable.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',3,2,'2025-06-26_21-55-47','The .h file acts as the interface for a .c file. It includes function declarations, macros, and structs. This allows other .c files to know things about each other without duplicating code. During compilation, including a .h file gives each .c file a copy of the declarations only, which is safe because declarations don’t generate code. Including .c files would duplicate code during compilation because every .c file that includes it would get a copy of its full contents, leading to multiple function and global variable definitions which can cause errors.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',3,2,'2025-06-27_11-58-04','The .h file acts as the interface for a .c file. It includes function declarations, macros, and structs. This allows other .c files to know things about each other without duplicating code. During compilation, including a .h file gives each .c file a copy of the declarations only, which is safe because declarations don’t generate code. Including .c files would duplicate code during compilation because every .c file that includes it would get a copy of its full contents, leading to multiple function and global variable definitions which can cause errors.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',3,3,'2025-06-26_21-55-47','To start a thread, you first must need to initialize your kernel space. This involves locating the memory of the MSP so that the program knows where to allocate the thread’s stack without overlapping with any other allocated space, and not exceeding the total space allocated for thread stacks which could cause undefined behaviour. Once an appropriate memory space is identified, it can be allocated to your thread finishing the initialization process.\nAfterwards, you need to setup the context of thread by initializing and creating the thread. In this lab, this is done with the osCreateThread function, where the function pointer is passed to it and the stack is initialized. (Note that the in the case of the lab, the thread is created at compilation time so the user does not have to worry about it.)\nFinally, the user calls osKernelStart which starts a system call that pops the thread’s stack into the CPU’s registers, running the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',3,3,'2025-06-27_11-58-04','Write the thread function: First, decide what your thread should do, and write a function that completes those tasks. This function must follow the correct format expected by the system, (in this case taking no arguments and returning nothing.) You as the user are responsible for defining this since the thread’s sole purpose is to run a task that you want.\nInitialize the kernel: Before any threads can run, you need to initialize the kernel. This step prepares the system and memory so that thread stacks can be allocated without overlapping or exceeding memory limits, which can cause a hard fault. This is done by calling osInitializeKernel().\nCreate the thread: You then create the thread by passing your function’s pointer to osCreateThread. This allocates a stack to your pointer which holds your function’s pointer and other register values that will be popped to the CPU’s registers once the thread is ready to run.\nStart the kernel: Once the thread is created, you start the kernel using osKernelStart(). This launches the system and begins executing your thread by loading its context into the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',3,4,'2025-06-26_21-55-47','The stack pool is a reserved space in memory for the stack space. This is useful for creating multiple threads because we know how much space we have for thread stacks. The boundaries of the space are defined by MSP_INIT_VAL on one end, plus MSP_INIT_VAL minus the stack space size on the other. Note that the stack space size is subtracted because address decrement in the CortexM4. To allocate stacks, we can keep track of the last allocated stack’s address, (which starts at MSP_INIT_VAL when nothing has been allocated,) and subtract the size of the stack to get the starting value of the next stack. In the case of this lab, it is 0x400. To allocate the next stack, you simply take the last allocated stack’s starting address and subtract again. Since we know the minimum allowable address, we can always check that the next allocation will not pass this limit. This system ensures that no stack allocation will overlap with another one’s, nor will it exceed the stack pool. Both issues could cause undefined behaviour or a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',3,4,'2025-06-27_11-58-04','The stack pool is a reserved space in memory for the stack space. This is useful for creating multiple threads because we know how much space we have for thread stacks. The boundaries of the space are defined by MSP_INIT_VAL on one end, plus MSP_INIT_VAL minus the stack space size on the other. Note that the stack space size is subtracted because address decrement in the CortexM4. To allocate stacks, we can keep track of the last allocated stack’s address, (which starts at MSP_INIT_VAL when nothing has been allocated,) and subtract the size of the stack to get the starting value of the next stack. In the case of this lab, it is 0x400. To allocate the next stack, you simply take the last allocated stack’s starting address and subtract again. Since we know the minimum allowable address, we can always check that the next allocation will not pass this limit. This system ensures that no stack allocation will overlap with another one’s, nor will it exceed the stack pool. Both issues could cause undefined behaviour or a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',3,5,'2025-06-26_21-55-47','The “user” related code goes into main.c, and the “kernel” related code goes into kernel.h and kernel.c. This is important to isolate code from the user so that they cannot break anything. For example, the kernel files deal with a lot of memory allocation so if the user touches that they could trigger a hard fault should they try to read a garbage value.\nThe user is only able to do is create the function they want to run, and to initialize, create, and run a thread. The user should not have access to any resources or low level operations. Along with security, this also helps abstract the code which makes development easier and more portable. If the code was moved to another architecture, you’d only have to change kernel.c’s code which is centralized, instead of jumping all over main.c to change code.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',3,5,'2025-06-27_11-58-04','The “user” related code goes into main.c, and the “kernel” related code goes into kernel.h and kernel.c. This is important to isolate code from the user so that they cannot break anything. For example, the kernel files deal with a lot of memory allocation so if the user touches that they could trigger a hard fault should they try to read a garbage value.\nThe user is only able to do is create the function they want to run, and to initialize, create, and run a thread. The user should not have access to any resources or low level operations. Along with security, this also helps abstract the code which makes development easier and more portable. If the code was moved to another architecture, you’d only have to change kernel.c’s code which is centralized, instead of jumping all over main.c to change code.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',4,1,'2025-07-15_09-21-17','Currently, the way the scheduler works is it increments through the indices of the TCB array to run each thread, wrapping back to the start again once the array is exhausted, which is why the osSched uses the modulo function. With a queue TCB, you pop the thread from the front of the queue. Once it yields, you can place it in the back of the queue.\nBenefits:\n- Using a queue data structure more naturally matches the round-robin logic. Additionally, this allows you to remove the integer that keeps track of the current running thread. You know that the next thread to run is at the front of your queue data structure.\n-Since an array is static, you are stuck with a fixed amount of memory allocated for your threads. With a queue, you can dynamically allocate/deallocate nodes as necessary as your threads change.\n-Using a queue makes thread destruction easier. In an array, if you removed a thread in a middle index, you’d have to shift all subsequent thread indices back one index to fill in the gap. With a queue, once your thread is done running for the last time, you simply omit readding the thread to the back of the queue.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',4,1,'2025-07-15_17-51-06','Currently, the way the scheduler works is it increments through the indices of the TCB array to run each thread, wrapping back to the start again once the array is exhausted, which is why the osSched uses the modulo function. With a queue TCB, you pop the thread from the front of the queue. Once it yields, you can place it in the back of the queue.\nBenefits:\n- Using a queue data structure more naturally matches the round-robin logic. Additionally, this allows you to remove the integer that keeps track of the current running thread. You know that the next thread to run is at the front of your queue data structure.\n-Since an array is static, you are stuck with a fixed amount of memory allocated for your threads. With a queue, you can dynamically allocate/deallocate nodes as necessary as your threads change.\n-Using a queue makes thread destruction easier. In an array, if you removed a thread in a middle index, you’d have to shift all subsequent thread indices back one index to fill in the gap. With a queue, once your thread is done running for the last time, you simply omit readding the thread to the back of the queue.\nTrade-offs:\n-While a queue is more representative of the round robin logic, it introduces additional overhead. Queueing and dequeuing requires pointer manipulation and potentially dynamic memory allocation. Additionally, more metadata needs to be stored for each thread such as the pointer to the next thread in the queue.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',4,2,'2025-07-15_09-21-17','To perform a context switch, PendSV_Handler, (“The Handler”), first saves the context of the current thread. The current PSP is located into register R0 using: MRS R0, PSP. Then, the registers of the thread R4-R11 are pushed onto the stack using: STMD R0!, {R4-R11}. Now the stack contains the full context of the running thread. Afterwards, BL osSched is called, which is the round robin scheduler that updates runningThread and sets the PSP to the new thread’s stack. The remaining code is used to load the next thread’s stack.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',4,2,'2025-07-15_17-51-06','To perform a context switch, the PendSV_Handler first saves the context of the currently running thread. It does this by copying the current PSP into register 0 using MRS R0, PSP. Then, the callee-saved registers R4 to R11 are stored onto the thread’s stack using STMDB R0!, {R4-R11}. This effectively saves the thread’s context so it can be resumed later. Now, the stack contains the full context of the running thread. Afterwards, BL osSched is called, which is the round robin scheduler that updates runningThread and sets the PSP to the new thread’s stack. After returning from osSched, the final step is to restore the context of the new thread. This is done by loading the new thread’s saved registers (R4 to R11) from its stack using LDMIA R0!, {R4-R11} and updating the PSP again with MSR PSP, R0. These restored registered ensure the new thread resumes excecution with the correct context. Finally, BX LR is used to return from the handler which triggers the processor to pop the remaining registers automatically, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',4,3,'2025-07-15_09-21-17','This is a bad idea for a few reasons:\n-If a high-priority task is always ready, it will always run. This causes starvation because the low-priority tasks may never have the chance to run which leads to system unresponsiveness.\n-There’s nothing in the design that deals with priority inversion yet. If a higher priority task runs but then becomes blocked by a lower priority task, there’s no mechanism implemented that will allow the lower priority task to run to unblock the higher priority task.\n-We haven’t added pre-emption to our design yet. If a high priority task runs and takes a very long time or becomes blocked, it can hog the CPU forever. There’s no method for another thread to rob the blocking thread’s resources.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',4,3,'2025-07-15_17-51-06','This is a bad idea for a few reasons:\n-If a high-priority task is always ready, it will always run. This causes starvation because the low-priority tasks may never have the chance to run which leads to system unresponsiveness.\n-There’s nothing in the design that deals with priority inversion yet. If a higher priority task runs but then becomes blocked by a lower priority task, there’s no mechanism implemented that will allow the lower priority task to run to unblock the higher priority task.\n-We haven’t added pre-emption to our design yet. If a high priority task runs and takes a very long time or becomes blocked, it can hog the CPU forever. There’s no method for another thread to rob the blocking thread’s resources.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',4,4,'2025-07-15_09-21-17','Benefits of using C:\n-Using C is more readable and easier to maintain that raw C since it uses language that humans are more used to.\n-A lot of tools/extensions exist for C in IDEs and debuggers that help with type checking, debugging, and code navigation that makes development easier.\n-Using C abstracts the implementation, making development more streamlined. Additionally, assembly can change based on the hardware so using C makes the code more portable.\nDownsides of using C:\n-Using C requires more performance overhead.\n-Using C abstracts away the exact memory layout, so you can’t guarantee which registers are used and preserved.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',4,4,'2025-07-15_17-51-06','Benefits of using C:\n-Using C is more readable and easier to maintain than raw assembly since it uses language that humans are more used to.\n-A lot of tools/extensions exist for C in IDEs and debuggers that help with type checking, debugging, and code navigation that makes development easier.\n-Using C abstracts the implementation, making development more streamlined. Additionally, assembly can change based on the hardware so using C makes the code more portable.\nDownsides of using C:\n-Using C requires more performance overhead.\n-Using C abstracts away the exact memory layout, so you can’t guarantee which registers are used and preserved.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',4,5,'2025-07-15_09-21-17','The OS will still work but a lot of the features implemented become trivial, and that extra overhead used to make the OS work with multiple threads is wasted. osKernelStart() will still switch into the single thread using the SVC handler and call runFirstThread(). The thread will run and print continuously. Calling osYield will trigger PendSV_Handler, which switches context. This context switch will call osSched(), which will just return the same running thread (since we only have one thread.) Essentially that’s the root of why it works but also why it’s redundant. osSched() will always set the running thread to the same thing.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',4,5,'2025-07-15_17-51-06','The OS will still work but a lot of the features implemented become trivial, and that extra overhead used to make the OS work with multiple threads is wasted. osKernelStart() will still switch into the single thread using the SVC handler and call runFirstThread(). The thread will run and print continuously. Calling osYield will trigger PendSV_Handler, which switches context. This context switch will call osSched(), which will just return the same running thread (since we only have one thread.) Essentially that’s the root of why it works but also why it’s redundant. osSched() will always set the running thread to the same thing.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',5,1,'2025-07-29_12-11-06','Firstly, the user must create a pointer to the piece of data they want, then pass that pointer into the osCreateThread functions. A void pointer is used instead of the actual data itself for several reasons. Firstly, it allows the user to input any type they want. If the input argument was hardcoded to be an int (or int*), then the osThreadCreate function would be less useful since it can only accept one type of data. Secondly, using a pointer allows you to pass multiple pieces of data, since the user can pass a pointer to a struct. Finally, (as mentioned in the lab instructions,) using a pointer standardizes the thread functions so that the OS designer does not need to guess what the user wants.\nOnce the create thread function has the pointer to the data, it is initialized into R0 of the thread’s stack, along with the pointer to the desired function into PC. This is because R0 to R3 of the stack is reserved for function arguments.\nTherefore when the thread gets its CPU time, it pops the argument from R0 and uses that for the function placed into the PC address.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',5,1,'2025-07-29_17-38-41','Firstly, the user must create a pointer to the piece of data they want, then pass that pointer into the osCreateThread functions. A void pointer is used instead of the actual data itself for several reasons. Firstly, it allows the user to input any type they want. If the input argument was hardcoded to be an int (or int*), then the osThreadCreate function would be less useful since it can only accept one type of data. Secondly, using a pointer allows you to pass multiple pieces of data, since the user can pass a pointer to a struct. Finally, (as mentioned in the lab instructions,) using a pointer standardizes the thread functions so that the OS designer does not need to guess what the user wants.\nOnce the create thread function has the pointer to the data, it is initialized into R0 of the thread’s stack, along with the pointer to the desired function into PC. This is because R0 to R3 of the stack is reserved for function arguments.\nTherefore when the thread gets its CPU time, it pops the argument from R0 and uses that for the function placed into the PC address.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',5,2,'2025-07-29_12-11-06','osYield is used to call the context switching functions, so calling osYield in them is essentially calling a context switch inside a context switch. osYield serves to abstract that code, so calling it inside of the context-switch breaks the abstraction and causes circular reasoning. If you put osYield in these functions, you can cause infinite recursion, stack corruption, or other undefined behaviour leading to a fault.\n',0,'s25'),('kdqi',5,2,'2025-07-29_17-38-41','You can’t call osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler etc. because osYield is itself a system call. It triggers an SVC exception to initiate a context switch. Calling osYield inside of these handlers essentially attempts to initiate a context switch from within an active interrupt handler.\nOn the Cortex-M4, system calls like osYield rely on exceptions and the MSP which is also used during interrupt handling. Calling osYield inside one of these handlers would cause the processor to try and enter another exception while it is already handling one. Since calling an exception inside an exception uses the same stack, it can lead to stack corruption or infinite recursion, which can lead to a hard fault.\nAt a higher level, the purpose of osYield is to abstract away the context-switching functions. Using osYield inside of these would be breaking the abstraction.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',5,3,'2025-07-29_12-11-06','Upsides:\n-A finer time slice allows for improved parallelism.\n-Can feel more responsive, since threads can be pre-empted sooner by higher priority tasks\n-More suitable for high frequency applications such as robotics or high speed communication.\nDownsides:\n-More overhead. Every single time a thread is pre-empted it requires context switching. Calling PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, etc.\n-Uses more power. The more time the CPU spends context switching, the less time it has to be in power optimized states. This can also have mechanical implications since a higher power consumption also produces more heat.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',5,3,'2025-07-29_17-38-41','Upsides:\n-A finer time slice allows for improved parallelism.\n-Can feel more responsive, since threads can be pre-empted sooner by higher priority tasks\n-More suitable for high frequency applications such as robotics or high speed communication.\nDownsides:\n-More overhead. Every single time a thread is pre-empted it requires context switching. Calling PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, etc.\n-Uses more power. The more time the CPU spends context switching, the less time it has to be in power optimized states. This can also have mechanical implications since a higher power consumption also produces more heat.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',5,4,'2025-07-29_12-11-06','For the user, co-operative multitasking requires that the functions designed voluntarily yields the CPU resources back to the scheduler. In the case of this lab this is done with osYield. osYield is required in each function or else it will hog the CPU forever. The benefit of co-operative multitasking is that it has more predictable behaviour, since you always know where the function will be interrupted. Pre-emptive multitasking forces the threads to yield control of the CPU to the scheduler. For the user, this can feel more responsive since timeslicing can be used to yield/schedule, giving each thread equal CPU time. Additionally, pre-emptive multitasking won’t hog the CPU forever. However, the downside is that switching is more unpredictable, and can cause problems if it occurs during a task that should be uninterrupted/atomic. More code is required to ensure this doesn’t happen.\nFrom the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is easier to implement. The scheduler just waits for osYield to be called. For pre-emptive multitasking, the OS needs to keep track of the timeslice and the amount of time the current thread’s run time. In this lab, SysTick_Handler is used to keep track of the runtime and if it’s up, it runs the scheduling routine. This additional code also requires additional overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',5,4,'2025-07-29_17-38-41','For the user, co-operative multitasking requires that the functions designed voluntarily yields the CPU resources back to the scheduler. In the case of this lab this is done with osYield. osYield is required in each function or else it will hog the CPU forever. The benefit of co-operative multitasking is that it has more predictable behaviour, since you always know where the function will be interrupted. Pre-emptive multitasking forces the threads to yield control of the CPU to the scheduler. For the user, this can feel more responsive since timeslicing can be used to yield/schedule, giving each thread equal CPU time. Additionally, pre-emptive multitasking won’t hog the CPU forever. However, the downside is that switching is more unpredictable, and can cause problems if it occurs during a task that should be uninterrupted/atomic. More code is required to ensure this doesn’t happen.\nFrom the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is easier to implement. The scheduler just waits for osYield to be called. For pre-emptive multitasking, the OS needs to keep track of the timeslice and the amount of time the current thread’s run time. In this lab, SysTick_Handler is used to keep track of the runtime and if it’s up, it runs the scheduling routine. This additional code also requires additional overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',5,5,'2025-07-29_12-11-06','In the final lab exercise, cooperative and preemtive multitasking work by relying on the same scheduler and context switch mechanism. Although cooperative threads yield voluntarily using osYield and preemptive threads are switched out by the SysTick timer interrupt, both end up triggering the same context switch using PendSV. This shared mechanism allows the OS to manage both types of threads.\n',1,'s25'),('kdqi',5,5,'2025-07-29_17-38-41','In the final lab exercise, cooperative and preemtive multitasking work by relying on the same scheduler and context switch mechanism. Although cooperative threads yield voluntarily using osYield and preemptive threads are switched out by the SysTick timer interrupt, both end up triggering the same context switch using PendSV. This shared mechanism allows the OS to manage both types of threads.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',1,1,'2025-06-25_19-10-07','HAL is a collection driver code that is prewritten and sits between the application and the microcontroller’s registers, presenting them as simple while also high-level C functions and data structures. This abstraction dramatically accelerates development, since it allows the focus to shift to application logic, and keeps the code portable across MCU, and reduces the chances of subtle hardware bugs that can arise from manual register manipulation. In summary, a HAL trades a little performance and transparency for faster, safer, and far more maintainable embedded projects\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',1,1,'2025-06-26_12-52-51','HAL is a collection driver code that is prewritten and sits between the application and the microcontroller’s registers, presenting them as simple while also high-level C functions and data structures. This abstraction dramatically accelerates development, since it allows the focus to shift to application logic, and keeps the code portable across MCU, and reduces the chances of subtle hardware bugs that can arise from manual register manipulation. In summary, a HAL trades a little performance and transparency for faster, safer, and far more maintainable embedded projects\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',1,2,'2025-06-25_19-10-07','__io_putchar is the tiny adapter that allows standard‐library functions like printf work on a microcontroller that has no default “console.” Whenever printf needs to emit a single character, it calls __io_putchar; the implementation then sends that byte out through a real I/O channel that has been chosen, which is typically a UART, SWO, or semihosting interface. Basically, the function bridges the gap between libc’s expectation of an abstract stdout stream and the concrete peripheral registers of the board, its possible to keep using familiar, portable print statements without having to use low-level transmit code throughout your application.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',1,2,'2025-06-26_12-52-51','__io_putchar is the tiny adapter that allows standard‐library functions like printf work on a microcontroller that has no default “console.” Whenever printf needs to emit a single character, it calls __io_putchar; the implementation then sends that byte out through a real I/O channel that has been chosen, which is typically a UART, SWO, or semihosting interface. Basically, the function bridges the gap between libc’s expectation of an abstract stdout stream and the concrete peripheral registers of the board, its possible to keep using familiar, portable print statements without having to use low-level transmit code throughout your application.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',1,3,'2025-06-25_19-10-07','A debugger was essential in this lab because I was working at a stage where the microcontroller’s run-time environment wasn’t fully initialized, so there was literally nowhere for formatted text to go. With the debugger it was possible to stop execution at any instruction, inspect and modify registers (like PC, LR, MSP, PSP), single-step through exception handlers, and watch memory locations change in real time, these are capabilities that a printf-only approach simply can’t provide. Even if I had managed to bring up a serial console, sprinkling printf calls into startup code or time-critical ISRs would have distorted timing, masked subtle bugs, and been useless once the CPU jumped to an invalid address or hit a hard fault. In short, the debugger allows to observe and control the system from the outside without altering its behavior, making it the only reliable way to verify low-level assembly and context-switching logic in this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',1,3,'2025-06-26_12-52-51','A debugger was essential in this lab because I was working at a stage where the microcontroller’s run-time environment wasn’t fully initialized, so there was literally nowhere for formatted text to go. With the debugger it was possible to stop execution at any instruction, inspect and modify registers (like PC, LR, MSP, PSP), single-step through exception handlers, and watch memory locations change in real time, these are capabilities that a printf-only approach simply can’t provide. Even if I had managed to bring up a serial console, sprinkling printf calls into startup code or time-critical ISRs would have distorted timing, masked subtle bugs, and been useless once the CPU jumped to an invalid address or hit a hard fault. In short, the debugger allows to observe and control the system from the outside without altering its behavior, making it the only reliable way to verify low-level assembly and context-switching logic in this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',1,4,'2025-06-25_19-10-07','Because the Cortex-M stacks grow downward from the top of SRAM, a second track has been carved out by sliding a pointer lower in memory. After reading MSP_init_val (the reset value pre-loaded into the Main Stack Pointer and usually equal to the first address just below the RAM top), it can be decided how much space we wanted for the Process Stack—say 0x400 bytes—then formed PSP_val = MSP_init_val – 0x400, rounding down to an 8-byte boundary for alignment. Loading that result into the PSP register instantly reserved a private block of RAM for thread-mode code without touching any linker scripts. Knowing the original MSP address was critical, because only then is it possible to be sure that the subtraction kept the new stack inside valid SRAM, and the two stacks would never collide—if an offset was blindly picked, we might overwrite global data or the main stack itself and crash the system when the two regions eventually met.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',1,4,'2025-06-26_12-52-51','Because the Cortex-M stacks grow downward from the top of SRAM, a second track has been carved out by sliding a pointer lower in memory. After reading MSP_init_val (the reset value pre-loaded into the Main Stack Pointer and usually equal to the first address just below the RAM top), it can be decided how much space we wanted for the Process Stack—say 0x400 bytes—then formed PSP_val = MSP_init_val – 0x400, rounding down to an 8-byte boundary for alignment. Loading that result into the PSP register instantly reserved a private block of RAM for thread-mode code without touching any linker scripts. Knowing the original MSP address was critical, because only then is it possible to be sure that the subtraction kept the new stack inside valid SRAM, and the two stacks would never collide—if an offset was blindly picked, we might overwrite global data or the main stack itself and crash the system when the two regions eventually met.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',1,5,'2025-06-25_19-10-07','Right now we subtract one fixed offset from MSP_init_val and call the result PSP_val; to carve out N threads we’d parameterise that step. Define two compile-time constants—NUM_THREADS and STACK_SIZE_BYTES (aligned to 8 bytes)—then either (a) reserve an array of raw memory (uint8_t thread_stack[NUM_THREADS][STACK_SIZE_BYTES] __attribute__((aligned(8)));) or (b) walk downward from MSP_init_val, subtracting STACK_SIZE_BYTES for each thread. In both cases we populate an array such as uint32_t stack_top[NUM_THREADS], where stack_top[i] is &thread_stack[i][STACK_SIZE_BYTES] (or MSP_init_val - (i+1)*STACK_SIZE_BYTES in method b). The only code you really change is the one-line subtraction: wrap it in a loop or macro that repeats it NUM_THREADS times and stores each resulting address. Because the sizes are known at compile time, the compiler/linker can verify the total space (NUM_THREADS × STACK_SIZE_BYTES) fits in SRAM, and the rest of your context-switching logic can later pick the appropriate entry from stack_top[] when a thread is created.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',1,5,'2025-06-26_12-52-51','Right now we subtract one fixed offset from MSP_init_val and call the result PSP_val; to carve out N threads we’d parameterise that step. Define two compile-time constants—NUM_THREADS and STACK_SIZE_BYTES (aligned to 8 bytes)—then either (a) reserve an array of raw memory (uint8_t thread_stack[NUM_THREADS][STACK_SIZE_BYTES] __attribute__((aligned(8)));) or (b) walk downward from MSP_init_val, subtracting STACK_SIZE_BYTES for each thread. In both cases we populate an array such as uint32_t stack_top[NUM_THREADS], where stack_top[i] is &thread_stack[i][STACK_SIZE_BYTES] (or MSP_init_val - (i+1)*STACK_SIZE_BYTES in method b). The only code you really change is the one-line subtraction: wrap it in a loop or macro that repeats it NUM_THREADS times and stores each resulting address. Because the sizes are known at compile time, the compiler/linker can verify the total space (NUM_THREADS × STACK_SIZE_BYTES) fits in SRAM, and the rest of your context-switching logic can later pick the appropriate entry from stack_top[] when a thread is created.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,1,'2025-06-25_22-04-15','When I moved the address of print_continuously into the PC, I essentially took over the processor’s control flow which allowed the argument I passed to the tiny jumpAssembly stub to be copied into R0, the inline assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 loaded that value into the PC, and the CPU immediately treated it as the “next instruction” to run. Because the PC now pointed to the first instruction of print_continuously, execution jumped straight into that function exactly as if I had branched to it at the machine-code level, bypassing the usual C call/return mechanics and stack setup. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that repeatedly prints to the console, the processor stayed inside that loop and never returned to the original context, demonstrating how directly manipulating the PC lets me redirect execution flow without any higher-level call semantics.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,1,'2025-06-26_15-02-40','When I moved the address of print_continuously into the PC, I essentially took over the processor’s control flow which allowed the argument I passed to the tiny jumpAssembly to be copied into R0, the inline assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 loaded that value into the PC, and the CPU immediately treated it as the “next instruction” to run. Because the PC now pointed to the first instruction of print_continuously, execution jumped straight into that function exactly as if I had branched to it at the machine-code level, bypassing the usual C call/return mechanics and stack setup. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that repeatedly prints to the console, the processor stayed inside that loop and never returned to the original context, demonstrating how directly manipulating the PC lets me redirect execution flow without any higher-level call semantics.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,1,'2025-06-26_16-22-31','When I moved the address of print_continuously into the PC, I essentially took over the processor’s control flow which allowed the argument I passed to the tiny jumpAssembly to be copied into R0, the inline assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 loaded that value into the PC, and the CPU immediately treated it as the “next instruction” to run. Because the PC now pointed to the first instruction of print_continuously, execution jumped straight into that function exactly as if I had branched to it at the machine-code level, bypassing the usual C call/return mechanics and stack setup. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that repeatedly prints to the console, the processor stayed inside that loop and never returned to the original context, demonstrating how directly manipulating the PC lets me redirect execution flow without any higher-level call semantics.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,1,'2025-06-26_21-00-32','When I moved the address of print_continuously into the PC, I essentially took over the processor’s control flow which allowed the argument I passed to the tiny jumpAssembly to be copied into R0, the inline assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 loaded that value into the PC, and the CPU immediately treated it as the “next instruction” to run. Because the PC now pointed to the first instruction of print_continuously, execution jumped straight into that function exactly as if I had branched to it at the machine-code level, bypassing the usual C call/return mechanics and stack setup. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that repeatedly prints to the console, the processor stayed inside that loop and never returned to the original context, demonstrating how directly manipulating the PC lets me redirect execution flow without any higher-level call semantics.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,1,'2025-06-26_22-35-34','When I moved the address of print_continuously into the PC, I essentially took over the processor’s control flow which allowed the argument I passed to the tiny jumpAssembly to be copied into R0, the inline assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 loaded that value into the PC, and the CPU immediately treated it as the “next instruction” to run. Because the PC now pointed to the first instruction of print_continuously, execution jumped straight into that function exactly as if I had branched to it at the machine-code level, bypassing the usual C call/return mechanics and stack setup. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that repeatedly prints to the console, the processor stayed inside that loop and never returned to the original context, demonstrating how directly manipulating the PC lets me redirect execution flow without any higher-level call semantics.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,1,'2025-06-27_07-06-02','When I moved the address of print_continuously into the PC, I essentially took over the processor’s control flow which allowed the argument I passed to the tiny jumpAssembly to be copied into R0, the inline assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 loaded that value into the PC, and the CPU immediately treated it as the “next instruction” to run. Because the PC now pointed to the first instruction of print_continuously, execution jumped straight into that function exactly as if I had branched to it at the machine-code level, bypassing the usual C call/return mechanics and stack setup. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that repeatedly prints to the console, the processor stayed inside that loop and never returned to the original context, demonstrating how directly manipulating the PC lets me redirect execution flow without any higher-level call semantics.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,1,'2025-06-27_07-19-11','When I moved the address of print_continuously into the PC, I essentially took over the processor’s control flow which allowed the argument I passed to the tiny jumpAssembly to be copied into R0, the inline assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 loaded that value into the PC, and the CPU immediately treated it as the “next instruction” to run. Because the PC now pointed to the first instruction of print_continuously, execution jumped straight into that function exactly as if I had branched to it at the machine-code level, bypassing the usual C call/return mechanics and stack setup. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that repeatedly prints to the console, the processor stayed inside that loop and never returned to the original context, demonstrating how directly manipulating the PC lets me redirect execution flow without any higher-level call semantics.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,2,'2025-06-25_22-04-15','A system call is the formal way that the application code asks the operating system to do something it can’t and or shouldn’t handle directly, this could be something such as accessing hardware, allocating memory, or talking to a peripheral. Instead of doing it myself (which could break other tasks or violate protection rules), I trigger a special instruction like SVC that causes the CPU to switch into privileged mode and jump to a kernel-side handler. That handler performs the requested service with full authority, packages up any return value, and then safely hands control back to my user-mode code.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,2,'2025-06-26_15-02-40','A system call is the formal way that the application code asks the operating system to do something it can’t and or shouldn’t handle directly, this could be something such as accessing hardware, allocating memory, or talking to a peripheral. Instead of doing it myself (which could break other tasks or violate protection rules), I trigger a special instruction like SVC that causes the CPU to switch into privileged mode and jump to a kernel-side handler. That handler performs the requested service with full authority, packages up any return value, and then safely hands control back to my user-mode code.\n',0,'s25'),('kmizania',2,2,'2025-06-26_16-22-31','Setting up a thread’s stack means reserving a part of the RAM and its memory for that thread and pre-filling the top of that part with initial register values that the CPU expects to find when it resumes execution. We pushed a valid xPSR, the thread’s entry address as the would-be PC, a dummy LR (or cleanup handler), and any initial R0–R3 arguments, then pointed the process-stack pointer (PSP) just below this block. When the scheduler later loads that PSP, the core pops the frame and starts running the thread as if it had always been active. Saving these register values on the stack is crucial because registers are reused by every thread; without stashing them, each context switch would destroy the suspended thread’s state.\n',0,'s25'),('kmizania',2,2,'2025-06-26_21-00-32','Setting up a thread’s stack means reserving a part of the RAM and its memory for that thread and pre-filling the top of that part with initial register values that the CPU expects to find when it resumes execution. We pushed a valid xPSR, the thread’s entry address as the would-be PC, a dummy LR (or cleanup handler), and any initial R0–R3 arguments, then pointed the process-stack pointer (PSP) just below this block. When the scheduler later loads that PSP, the core pops the frame and starts running the thread as if it had always been active. Saving these register values on the stack is crucial because registers are reused by every thread; without stashing them, each context switch would destroy the suspended thread’s state.\n',0,'s25'),('kmizania',2,2,'2025-06-26_22-35-34','A system call is a type of function call made by user-mode code into the operating-system kernel. When an application needs a privileged service, such as reading a hardware register, allocating physical memory, or writing to a device, it cannot perform that operation directly because user mode lacks the necessary rights. Instead, the program issues a designated trap instruction (on Cortex-M we use SVC, but other CPUs have other equivalents) that supports the system-call mechanism by safely switching the CPU into privileged mode and transferring control to a predefined kernel entry point. The kernel-side handler decodes the requested service number, executes the operation with full authority, places any return value where the calling convention expects it, and then restores the saved user context before returning to user mode. This arrangement enforces protection, keeps hardware access orderly, and provides a clear, function-call-like interface between untrusted application code and trusted kernel code.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,2,'2025-06-27_07-06-02','A system call is a type of function call made by user-mode code into the operating-system kernel. When an application needs a privileged service, such as reading a hardware register, allocating physical memory, or writing to a device, it cannot perform that operation directly because user mode lacks the necessary rights. Instead, the program issues a designated trap instruction (on Cortex-M we use SVC, but other CPUs have other equivalents) that supports the system-call mechanism by safely switching the CPU into privileged mode and transferring control to a predefined kernel entry point. The kernel-side handler decodes the requested service number, executes the operation with full authority, places any return value where the calling convention expects it, and then restores the saved user context before returning to user mode. This arrangement enforces protection, keeps hardware access orderly, and provides a clear, function-call-like interface between untrusted application code and trusted kernel code.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,2,'2025-06-27_07-19-11','A system call is a type of function call made by user-mode code into the operating-system kernel. When an application needs a privileged service, such as reading a hardware register, allocating physical memory, or writing to a device, it cannot perform that operation directly because user mode lacks the necessary rights. Instead, the program issues a designated trap instruction (on Cortex-M we use SVC, but other CPUs have other equivalents) that supports the system-call mechanism by safely switching the CPU into privileged mode and transferring control to a predefined kernel entry point. The kernel-side handler decodes the requested service number, executes the operation with full authority, places any return value where the calling convention expects it, and then restores the saved user context before returning to user mode. This arrangement enforces protection, keeps hardware access orderly, and provides a clear, function-call-like interface between untrusted application code and trusted kernel code.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,3,'2025-06-25_22-04-15','Setting up a thread’s stack means reserving a part of the RAM and its memory for that thread and pre-filling the top of that part with initial register values that the CPU expects to find when it resumes execution. We pushed a valid xPSR, the thread’s entry address as the would-be PC, a dummy LR (or cleanup handler), and any initial R0–R3 arguments, then pointed the process-stack pointer (PSP) just below this block. When the scheduler later loads that PSP, the core pops the frame and starts running the thread as if it had always been active. Saving these register values on the stack is crucial because registers are reused by every thread; without stashing them, each context switch would destroy the suspended thread’s state.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,3,'2025-06-26_15-02-40','Setting up a thread’s stack means reserving a part of the RAM and its memory for that thread and pre-filling the top of that part with initial register values that the CPU expects to find when it resumes execution. We pushed a valid xPSR, the thread’s entry address as the would-be PC, a dummy LR (or cleanup handler), and any initial R0–R3 arguments, then pointed the process-stack pointer (PSP) just below this block. When the scheduler later loads that PSP, the core pops the frame and starts running the thread as if it had always been active. Saving these register values on the stack is crucial because registers are reused by every thread; without stashing them, each context switch would destroy the suspended thread’s state.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,3,'2025-06-26_16-22-31','Once the function fell out of its last instruction and executed BX LR, the core would load whatever value we pre-stuffed into the thread’s link register. In our setup that LR is just a sentinel (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFD or 0xDEADDEAD), not a real code address, so the CPU would branch to an unmapped or mis-aligned location, immediately triggering a fault that escalates into a HardFault. Because the scheduler assumes threads never “return” the normal C way, letting one do so leaves the processor with nowhere valid to go and crashes the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,3,'2025-06-26_21-00-32','Once the function fell out of its last instruction and executed BX LR, the core would load whatever value we pre-stuffed into the thread’s link register. In our setup that LR is just a sentinel (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFD or 0xDEADDEAD), not a real code address, so the CPU would branch to an unmapped or mis-aligned location, immediately triggering a fault that escalates into a HardFault. Because the scheduler assumes threads never “return” the normal C way, letting one do so leaves the processor with nowhere valid to go and crashes the program.\n',0,'s25'),('kmizania',2,3,'2025-06-26_22-35-34','Setting up a thread’s stack means reserving a part of the RAM and its memory for that thread and pre-filling the top of that part with initial register values that the CPU expects to find when it resumes execution. We pushed a valid xPSR, the thread’s entry address as the would-be PC, a dummy LR (or cleanup handler), and any initial R0–R3 arguments, then pointed the process-stack pointer (PSP) just below this block. When the scheduler later loads that PSP, the core pops the frame and starts running the thread as if it had always been active. Saving these register values on the stack is crucial because registers are reused by every thread; without stashing them, each context switch would destroy the suspended thread’s state.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,3,'2025-06-27_07-06-02','Setting up a thread’s stack means reserving a part of the RAM and its memory for that thread and pre-filling the top of that part with initial register values that the CPU expects to find when it resumes execution. We pushed a valid xPSR, the thread’s entry address as the would-be PC, a dummy LR (or cleanup handler), and any initial R0–R3 arguments, then pointed the process-stack pointer (PSP) just below this block. When the scheduler later loads that PSP, the core pops the frame and starts running the thread as if it had always been active. Saving these register values on the stack is crucial because registers are reused by every thread; without stashing them, each context switch would destroy the suspended thread’s state.\n',0,'s25'),('kmizania',2,3,'2025-06-27_07-19-11','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a region of RAM for that thread and pre-loading it with a complete exception frame so the CPU can restore the thread exactly as if it had been pre-empted earlier (this means pre-filling the top of that part with initial register values that the CPU expects to find when it resumes execution). On Cortex-M cores this frame must be written in the exact sequence the hardware will later pop it: xPSR, then PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and (for full context switching) R11–R4 beneath that. The processor’s exception-return logic assumes this precise ordering; if even one word is out of place, the restored registers are scrambled, the PC may point to invalid code, and a HardFault occurs before the thread ever runs. After pushing the frame, the process-stack pointer (PSP) is set to the word just below the saved registers. When the scheduler triggers an exception return, the core pops the frame, reloads every saved register, and begins executing at the thread’s entry address with the correct status flags. Storing registers in this ordered frame preserves each thread’s working state across context switches and prevents different threads from corrupting one another’s data.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,4,'2025-06-25_22-04-15','When we exit the SVC handler with the magic return code 0xFFFFFFFD, the CPU expects the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to already reference a correctly aligned, eight-word “exception frame” (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0) for the thread we’re about to resume. If we skip __set_PSP, that register still holds zero or stale data, so on the way out of Handler mode the core tries to pop those eight words from an invalid or mis-aligned address. The attempt triggers a MemManage or BusFault that escalates into a HardFault, and the program dies before the thread ever executes. Properly seeding PSP ensures the unstacking hardware reads valid memory and the thread starts cleanly.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,4,'2025-06-26_15-02-40','When we exit the SVC handler with the magic return code 0xFFFFFFFD, the CPU expects the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to already reference a correctly aligned, eight-word “exception frame” (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0) for the thread we’re about to resume. If we skip __set_PSP, that register still holds zero or stale data, so on the way out of Handler mode the core tries to pop those eight words from an invalid or mis-aligned address. The attempt triggers a MemManage or BusFault that escalates into a HardFault, and the program dies before the thread ever executes. Properly seeding PSP ensures the unstacking hardware reads valid memory and the thread starts cleanly.\n',0,'s25'),('kmizania',2,4,'2025-06-26_22-35-34','During the SVC handler we are on the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), but the thread we are about to start will run in Thread mode using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which must point to that thread’s own stack block in RAM; before exiting the handler we therefore call __set_PSP(...) to load PSP with the top-of-stack address where we have pre-built an eight-word exception frame (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0). When we then return with the special value 0xFFFFFFFD, the Cortex-M core switches to Thread mode, pops those eight words from the address in PSP, restores the registers, sets PC to the thread’s entry point, and begins execution. If we skipped __set_PSP, PSP would still hold its reset (often zero) or some stale value, so the unstacking hardware would read from an invalid address, trigger a MemManage or BusFault, and escalate to a HardFault before the thread could run therefore explicitly setting PSP is essential for a clean, fault-free launch.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,4,'2025-06-27_07-06-02','During the SVC handler we are on the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), but the thread we are about to start will run in Thread mode using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which must point to that thread’s own stack block in RAM; before exiting the handler we therefore call __set_PSP(...) to load PSP with the top-of-stack address where we have pre-built an eight-word exception frame (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0). When we then return with the special value 0xFFFFFFFD, the Cortex-M core switches to Thread mode, pops those eight words from the address in PSP, restores the registers, sets PC to the thread’s entry point, and begins execution. If we skipped __set_PSP, PSP would still hold its reset (often zero) or some stale value, so the unstacking hardware would read from an invalid address, trigger a MemManage or BusFault, and escalate to a HardFault before the thread could run therefore explicitly setting PSP is essential for a clean, fault-free launch.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,4,'2025-06-27_07-19-11','During the SVC handler we are on the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), but the thread we are about to start will run in Thread mode using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which must point to that thread’s own stack block in RAM; before exiting the handler we therefore call __set_PSP(...) to load PSP with the top-of-stack address where we have pre-built an eight-word exception frame (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0). When we then return with the special value 0xFFFFFFFD, the Cortex-M core switches to Thread mode, pops those eight words from the address in PSP, restores the registers, sets PC to the thread’s entry point, and begins execution. If we skipped __set_PSP, PSP would still hold its reset (often zero) or some stale value, so the unstacking hardware would read from an invalid address, trigger a MemManage or BusFault, and escalate to a HardFault before the thread could run therefore explicitly setting PSP is essential for a clean, fault-free launch.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,5,'2025-06-25_22-04-15','Once the function fell out of its last instruction and executed BX LR, the core would load whatever value we pre-stuffed into the thread’s link register. In our setup that LR is just a sentinel (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFD or 0xDEADDEAD), not a real code address, so the CPU would branch to an unmapped or mis-aligned location, immediately triggering a fault that escalates into a HardFault. Because the scheduler assumes threads never “return” the normal C way, letting one do so leaves the processor with nowhere valid to go and crashes the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,5,'2025-06-26_15-02-40','Once the function fell out of its last instruction and executed BX LR, the core would load whatever value we pre-stuffed into the thread’s link register. In our setup that LR is just a sentinel (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFD or 0xDEADDEAD), not a real code address, so the CPU would branch to an unmapped or mis-aligned location, immediately triggering a fault that escalates into a HardFault. Because the scheduler assumes threads never “return” the normal C way, letting one do so leaves the processor with nowhere valid to go and crashes the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,5,'2025-06-26_22-35-34','Once the function fell out of its last instruction and executed BX LR, the core would load whatever value we pre-stuffed into the thread’s link register. In our setup that LR is just a sentinel (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFD or 0xDEADDEAD), not a real code address, so the CPU would branch to an unmapped or mis-aligned location, immediately triggering a fault that escalates into a HardFault. Because the scheduler assumes threads never “return” the normal C way, letting one do so leaves the processor with nowhere valid to go and crashes the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,5,'2025-06-27_07-06-02','Once the function fell out of its last instruction and executed BX LR, the core would load whatever value we pre-stuffed into the thread’s link register. In our setup that LR is just a sentinel (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFD or 0xDEADDEAD), not a real code address, so the CPU would branch to an unmapped or mis-aligned location, immediately triggering a fault that escalates into a HardFault. Because the scheduler assumes threads never “return” the normal C way, letting one do so leaves the processor with nowhere valid to go and crashes the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',2,5,'2025-06-27_07-19-11','Once the function fell out of its last instruction and executed BX LR, the core would load whatever value we pre-stuffed into the thread’s link register. In our setup that LR is just a sentinel (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFD or 0xDEADDEAD), not a real code address, so the CPU would branch to an unmapped or mis-aligned location, immediately triggering a fault that escalates into a HardFault. Because the scheduler assumes threads never “return” the normal C way, letting one do so leaves the processor with nowhere valid to go and crashes the program.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',3,1,'2025-06-25_22-14-15','When building a program spread across several *.c files, the compiler first runs each source file through the pre-processor to expand macros and pull in header contents, then translates the result into machine-independent object code, producing one *.o file per *.c file. These object files contain each translation unit’s machine instructions plus symbol tables that mark which functions or global variables they define and which external symbols they still need. Next, the linker gathers all the object files, resolves those external symbols so every call or reference points to exactly one matching definition, merges code and data sections into contiguous memory segments, lays out a runtime start-up routine, and writes relocation information if needed. Finally, it emits a single executable image with a defined entry point so the loader can jump straight into main, turning the independent *.c modules into one cohesive program.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',3,1,'2025-06-26_17-06-55','When building a program spread across several *.c files, the compiler first runs each source file through the pre-processor to expand macros and pull in header contents, then translates the result into machine-independent object code, producing one *.o file per *.c file. These object files contain each translation unit’s machine instructions plus symbol tables that mark which functions or global variables they define and which external symbols they still need. Next, the linker gathers all the object files, resolves those external symbols so every call or reference points to exactly one matching definition, merges code and data sections into contiguous memory segments, lays out a runtime start-up routine, and writes relocation information if needed. Finally, it emits a single executable image with a defined entry point so the loader can jump straight into main, turning the independent *.c modules into one cohesive program.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',3,2,'2025-06-25_22-14-15','#include literally pastes the referenced file’s text into whatever translation unit is being compiled, so if #include <kernel.c> is inside several other source files you end up duplicating every function and global variable defined in kernel.c across multiple compilation units. The linker then sees multiple definitions of the same symbols, throws “multiple definition” errors, and your normal benefit of separate compilation—being able to rebuild only the files that changed—disappears because everything that transitively includes kernel.c must be re-compiled. By contrast, header files declare interfaces (prototypes, structs, macros) that can safely appear in many translation units without producing duplicate code. Implementation details belong in a single *.c file that is compiled once and linked in, while headers give other files just enough information to call into that implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',3,2,'2025-06-26_17-06-55','#include literally pastes the referenced file’s text into whatever translation unit is being compiled, so if #include <kernel.c> is inside several other source files you end up duplicating every function and global variable defined in kernel.c across multiple compilation units. The linker then sees multiple definitions of the same symbols, throws “multiple definition” errors, and your normal benefit of separate compilation—being able to rebuild only the files that changed—disappears because everything that transitively includes kernel.c must be re-compiled. By contrast, header files declare interfaces (prototypes, structs, macros) that can safely appear in many translation units without producing duplicate code. Implementation details belong in a single *.c file that is compiled once and linked in, while headers give other files just enough information to call into that implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',3,3,'2025-06-25_22-14-15','Pick the work to run. Decide which function (and any argument values) is wanted for the new thread to execute; that function becomes the thread’s entry point.\nReserve stack space. Allocate a private stack for the thread (either let the OS pick a default size or specify one) so it has room for local variables and saved registers.\nCreate the thread object. Call the OS’s thread-creation routine, passing the entry-point function, its arguments, the stack pointer/size, and any attributes such as priority or scheduling policy.\nRegister with the scheduler. The OS adds the new thread to its ready list, giving it an initial state (usually “ready” but not yet “running”).\nStart or resume scheduling. Once the scheduler gets CPU time it loads the thread’s stack pointer and program counter and begins executing the entry function.\nLet the thread run or terminate cleanly. From this point the thread executes concurrently with others until it either loops forever, calls the thread-exit API, or the program shuts down; simply “returning” without one of those actions is undefined and usually crashes the system.\n',0,'s25'),('kmizania',3,3,'2025-06-26_17-06-55','First must initialize the kernel so it captures the main stack pointer, set up its data structures, and prepares its scheduler. Then have to choose the function that will serve as the thread’s entry point, telling the kernel what code to run. After that we have to allocate a private stack, either by asking the kernel for one or providing a pointer to suitably sized memory. Then by calling the kernel’s create-thread routine, passing the entry function, its argument, the stack pointer/size, and any attributes such as priority. After that the kernel adds the new thread to its ready list, marking it ready but not yet running. Then start the kernel scheduler; the kernel performs the first context switch, loads the thread’s stack pointer and program counter, and begins executing the entry function. And at the end the thread runs concurrently until it loops forever, invokes the kernel’s thread-exit API, or the system shuts down; returning without the exit call is undefined and can crash the system.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',3,4,'2025-06-25_22-14-15','The stack pool is like part of the RAM that is reserved exclusively for thread stacks. Instead of letting every new thread take a space out of the general heap (risking fragmentation and accidental overlap), the OS pre-allocates one big, contiguous chunk of memory and divides it into equal-size “slots,” each large enough to hold a thread’s private stack frames, local variables, and saved registers. When a thread is created, the scheduler simply hands it the next free slot in the pool and records the slot’s start address in the thread-control block. By pre-allocating and tracking stacks this way, it guarantees isolation between threads, avoid runtime memory allocation inside critical contexts, and make the kernel’s context-switch logic as simple as “pop a stack pointer from the pool, push it back when the thread exits.”\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',3,4,'2025-06-26_17-06-55','The stack pool is like part of the RAM that is reserved exclusively for thread stacks. Instead of letting every new thread take a space out of the general heap (risking fragmentation and accidental overlap), the OS pre-allocates one big, contiguous chunk of memory and divides it into equal-size “slots,” each large enough to hold a thread’s private stack frames, local variables, and saved registers. When a thread is created, the scheduler simply hands it the next free slot in the pool and records the slot’s start address in the thread-control block. By pre-allocating and tracking stacks this way, it guarantees isolation between threads, avoid runtime memory allocation inside critical contexts, and make the kernel’s context-switch logic as simple as “pop a stack pointer from the pool, push it back when the thread exits.”\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',3,5,'2025-06-25_22-14-15','Kernel files hold the privileged OS logic, interrupt handlers, device register access, and the functions that service system-call numbers. They compile once, run in privileged mode, and must stay stable and trusted. User files contain the work that you would actually want to run, such as printing, algorithms, blinking LEDs, and this interacts with the kernel only through its published API declared in header files. Keeping them separate enforces a clean boundary: which means that it is possible to rebuild or replace user code without touching the scheduler, avoid duplicate symbol definitions, and prevent bugs in user code from corrupting the OS because it runs without elevated privileges.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',3,5,'2025-06-26_17-06-55','Kernel files hold the privileged OS logic, interrupt handlers, device register access, and the functions that service system-call numbers. They compile once, run in privileged mode, and must stay stable and trusted. User files contain the work that you would actually want to run, such as printing, algorithms, blinking LEDs, and this interacts with the kernel only through its published API declared in header files. Keeping them separate enforces a clean boundary: which means that it is possible to rebuild or replace user code without touching the scheduler, avoid duplicate symbol definitions, and prevent bugs in user code from corrupting the OS because it runs without elevated privileges.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',4,1,'2025-07-29_21-42-04','Round-robin maps cleanly to a FIFO ready queue of TCB pointers. The kernel always runs the TCB at the front of the queue; when that thread calls yield the kernel saves its context, dequeues its TCB from the front, and if it remains READY enqueues it at the back. The new front element becomes the next running thread. When a thread blocks (I/O, semaphore, sleep), its TCB is removed from the ready queue; upon unblock it is pushed to the back to preserve fairness. Compared with a plain array+mod-index, a queue (often a fixed-size ring buffer) keeps operations O(1), avoids “holes” and validity scans when threads block/exit, and makes the policy self-documenting (“front runs → goes to back”). The small head/tail bookkeeping cost is constant and cache-friendly; under heavy blocking/unblocking it is typically lower overhead than compacting or scanning arrays. The structure also generalizes: one FIFO per priority enables round-robin within levels, while moving TCBs between queues cleanly implements sleep/wake. Arrays are fine for tiny, static sets of threads; once dynamism appears, an explicit ready queue yields simpler invariants and fewer bookkeeping bugs without sacrificing determinism.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',4,2,'2025-07-29_21-42-04','PendSV runs at the lowest interrupt priority, so switches happen tail-end with no pre-emption by other ISRs. On entry, Cortex-M hardware has already auto-stacked {r0–r3, r12, lr, pc, xPSR} on the PSP of the current thread. The handler first reads PSP, then saves the callee-saved registers {r4–r11} onto that stack and writes the resulting PSP back into the current thread’s TCB. It then calls the C scheduler (e.g., osSched()), which selects the next READY TCB and returns its saved PSP. The handler writes that value to PSP, restores {r4–r11}, sets LR to an EXC_RETURN value that returns to Thread mode using PSP, and branches to LR; the exception return causes hardware to unstack {r0–r3, r12, lr, pc, xPSR}, resuming the chosen thread exactly where it last ran. Saving {r4–r11} specifically matches the AAPCS callee-saved set, minimizing work the hardware doesn’t do; using PSP isolates thread stacks while MSP remains reserved for handlers, improving robustness. The TCB is the anchor that records each thread’s PSP and metadata, allowing the scheduler to treat a switch as swapping pointers rather than copying state.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',4,3,'2025-07-29_21-42-04','This is a bad idea because a pure “pick the highest-priority READY task” scheduler eliminates fairness and guarantees starvation: once any high-priority thread is READY, no lower-priority thread will ever run unless the high-priority one blocks. The direct effect is CPU monopolization by a single task; the secondary effects include unbounded latency for background work and priority inversion when a high-priority task waits on a lock held by a low-priority task that never gets CPU time due to mid-priority activity. If priorities are needed, use per-priority ready queues with round-robin within each level and ensure tasks block rather than spin; add priority inheritance/ceiling for shared resources. Otherwise, basic round-robin provides bounded wait times and predictable behavior. In short, the proposed change converts the system into a “run the top task forever” design whenever that task is READY.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',4,4,'2025-07-29_21-42-04','An assembly wrapper and C core is the right trade-off here. Assembly keeps the CPU-specific prologue/epilogue tight (choose MSP/PSP, save/restore {r4–r11}, set EXC_RETURN, insert barriers), while C holds policy and data-structure logic (ready queues, TCB operations, priorities). Benefits: clearer code, type safety, symbolized debugging, unit-testable scheduling logic, and easier portability across Cortex-M variants and future changes (the same C scheduler can run on M0/M3/M4; only the tiny wrapper is core-specific). Downsides: a small call/return overhead and strict ABI discipline inside an ISR; on tight deadlines (e.g., a 100 µs tick) those extra dozens of cycles may matter, and all-assembly gives absolute control over barriers and lazy FPU stacking. The hybrid still usually hits timing while remaining maintainable. The same pattern pays off in other paths—e.g., a HardFault handler stub in assembly that captures registers and then jumps to a C decoder for diagnostics—keeping critical entry/exit precise and complex logic readable.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',4,5,'2025-07-29_21-42-04','The OS still works, but round-robin degenerates. With one READY TCB, each yield triggers PendSV; the scheduler “rotates” the ready queue and immediately selects the same TCB. The system pays a context-switch cost yet resumes the same thread, so behavior appears single-threaded while ISRs and the tick still fire normally. Two important caveats: firstly, if that lone thread blocks or exits, the ready queue becomes empty; without an idle thread the system has nothing to schedule (best case it executes WFI, worst case it hits an error path). Secondly the switch overhead buys no parallel progress, so throughput is lower than a tight loop. With additional threads, the scheduler time-slices them fairly; with one, progress depends entirely on that thread’s yield/block behavior. A minimal idle task (low priority, runs WFI) keeps the kernel well-defined when nothing else is READY and avoids busy-spinning or stalls between interrupts.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,1,'2025-07-29_21-51-13','To pass an argument into a thread, the user writes the thread function with the standard lab signature void thread_function(void* args) and supplies a pointer to the desired input when creating the thread. Inside the thread, they cast and dereference that pointer to obtain a local copy of the input (e.g., copy an int, or unpack a struct if many fields are needed). This pattern works for both simple scalars and arbitrarily large inputs (wrap them in a struct). The OS side makes this work by how it initializes the thread’s stack frame and how the Cortex-M calling convention is used. When the kernel builds a new thread, it lays out the initial stack in the order xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, then R11–R4, and it places the argument pointer in the R0 slot of that frame. Later, when the scheduler selects the thread, the context-switch path (runFirstThread/PendSV) loads the thread’s PSP and performs an exception return so that the CPU pops R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR from that stack; because the ARM EABI passes a function’s first argument in R0, the thread function receives the user’s pointer in args exactly as if it had been called normally. Using a single void*is sufficient because a pointer fits entirely in R0; if more data are needed, the user passes a pointer to a struct and unpacks it in the thread. That’s why this mechanism is both simple and general.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,1,'2025-07-30_11-53-10','The user declares the thread entry with the lab’s signature void thread_fn(void *arg) and supplies a pointer at creation time, often a struct bundling multiple fields. The OS fabricates the initial exception-return frame on the new thread’s PSP exactly as Cortex-M expects: PC is set to the entry address, R0 to the argument pointer, LR to a safe exit trampoline, and xPSR with the Thumb bit set, leaving space for {R4–R11} to be saved later; the PSP and metadata are recorded in the TCB and the return path is arranged to use PSP in Thread mode with proper 8-byte stack alignment. When the scheduler first dispatches the thread, PendSV loads PSP and triggers an exception return so hardware un-stacks the crafted frame, restoring registers so that thread_fn observes the argument in R0 per the ARM EABI. This works because it matches the architecture’s calling convention and exception semantics; if a different ABI were used, only the register assignments in the fabricated frame would change while the user-facing void* contract remains valid.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,1,'2025-07-30_17-43-59','The user declares the thread entry with the lab’s signature void thread_fn(void *arg) and supplies a pointer at creation time, often a struct bundling multiple fields. The OS fabricates the initial exception-return frame on the new thread’s PSP exactly as Cortex-M expects: PC is set to the entry address, R0 to the argument pointer, LR to a safe exit trampoline, and xPSR with the Thumb bit set, leaving space for {R4–R11} to be saved later; the PSP and metadata are recorded in the TCB and the return path is arranged to use PSP in Thread mode with proper 8-byte stack alignment. When the scheduler first dispatches the thread, PendSV loads PSP and triggers an exception return so hardware un-stacks the crafted frame, restoring registers so that thread_fn observes the argument in R0 per the ARM EABI. This works because it matches the architecture’s calling convention and exception semantics; if a different ABI were used, only the register assignments in the fabricated frame would change while the user-facing void* contract remains valid.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,2,'2025-07-29_21-51-13','In this lab’s design, pre-emptive switches are initiated by SysTick_Handler, it decrements the running thread’s runtimeeach millisecond and, when that counter reaches zero, it pends PendSV with _ICSR |= 1<<28; __asm(\"isb\"); so that the actual context switch happens in PendSV, not inside SysTick itself. If a thread cooperatively yields, the SVC path likewise pends PendSV and returns; PendSV then performs the switch. This separation is explicit here, (SysTick decrements runtime, resets it to timeslice, and pends PendSV; yielding still does a “normal” switch), and the two-line pend sequence belongs in the handler, not a call to osYield from there. Calling osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler/Main, or SysTick_Handler is harmful because osYieldraises SVC, creating nested exceptions and breaking the intended priority/ordering between SVC and PendSV. The lab materials stress that SVC should pend PendSV and return, and that PendSV must run only after the current handler completes; otherwise, the “pending” semantics are defeated and PendSV can run immediately or out of order. That is why SVC is assigned a higher priority than PendSV, and why the kernel never switches in SVC itself—SVC needs to finish so its locals are popped, and the stack unwinds cleanly before the switch. Violating this contract risks double save/restore, stranded stack frames, stack growth, and hard-to-debug faults. Proper behavior is: SysTick and SVC pend PendSV; PendSV alone executes the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,2,'2025-07-30_11-53-10','osYield raises SVC to request a cooperative switch, so invoking it from within SysTick, SVC, or PendSV nests exceptions and breaks the intended priority ordering where SVC (higher) only pends PendSV (lower) and then returns, with the actual save/restore happening once in PendSV_Handler. Re-entering scheduling logic from an active handler risks double-saving {R4–R11}, interleaving or misordering the SVC/PendSV pair, corrupting the PSP/MSP split, and losing a valid EXC_RETURN, which manifests as erratic preemption latency, stack frames that never unwind, spurious HardFaults, or scheduler lockups under load. The correct pattern is disciplined and single-pointed: SysTick_Handlerperforms time accounting and pends PendSV on slice expiry; osYield() triggers SVC, whose handler pends PendSV; and only PendSV_Handler executes the context switch after the current handler has unwound, preserving stack discipline and deterministic priorities.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,2,'2025-07-30_17-43-59','osYield raises SVC to request a cooperative switch, so invoking it from within SysTick, SVC, or PendSV nests exceptions and breaks the intended priority ordering where SVC (higher) only pends PendSV (lower) and then returns, with the actual save/restore happening once in PendSV_Handler. Re-entering scheduling logic from an active handler risks double-saving {R4–R11}, interleaving or misordering the SVC/PendSV pair, corrupting the PSP/MSP split, and losing a valid EXC_RETURN, which manifests as erratic preemption latency, stack frames that never unwind, spurious HardFaults, or scheduler lockups under load. The correct pattern is disciplined and single-pointed: SysTick_Handlerperforms time accounting and pends PendSV on slice expiry; osYield() triggers SVC, whose handler pends PendSV; and only PendSV_Handler executes the context switch after the current handler has unwound, preserving stack discipline and deterministic priorities.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,3,'2025-07-29_21-51-13','Moving the fundamental OS clock from 1 ms to 0.1 ms (10 kHz) improves responsiveness and timing resolution. With a shorter tick, the kernel can pre-empt sooner and schedule with finer granularity, so software timers and time slices can be expressed in 0.1 ms units rather than full milliseconds. This directly lowers the best-case reaction time, and the timestamp resolution limits that exist when the OS tick is 1 ms, where the manual notes the system “can never respond to events faster than 1 ms” nor with resolution better than 1 ms; tightening the tick lifts those limits. In contexts that need tighter control (e.g., short CPU quanta or precise periodic work), a 0.1 ms tick makes pre-emptive switching and timer expirations more precise. The downsides are primarily overhead and integration risk. SysTick currently fires every 1 ms as set up by the HAL, and the lab builds pre-emption by decrementing a thread’s runtime on each tick and pended-switching via PendSV; increasing tick frequency by 10× means running the interrupt path 10× as often, which steals CPU time from threads, increases power, and amplifies any fixed work already performed in SysTick_Handler (the manual cautions not to remove existing code there or printf breaks). It also forces a retuning of time-based semantics: defaults like the “round-robin timeout” (often ~5 ms) and per-thread timeslice/runtime counters must be rescaled to preserve behavior, or every timeslice will effectively shrink by 10×. Finally, because the HAL configures SysTick for 1 ms and the lab explicitly “does not mess with that,” moving to 0.1 ms can complicate or break HAL-based timing unless the broader system is reconfigured consistently. In short, a 0.1 ms tick buys precision at the cost of ISR load, energy, and potential HAL/driver coupling issues that the lab’s 1 ms design deliberately avoids.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,3,'2025-07-30_11-53-10','Moving to a 0.1 ms tick yields ten-fold finer granularity for delays, timeouts, and software timers, with lower worst-case preemption latency and smaller jitter windows for tick-driven work, which can materially improve responsiveness for short-deadline tasks. The cost is that ISR workload scales with tick frequency: SysTick_Handler runs at 10 kHz, so any fixed bookkeeping inside the tick (including HAL maintenance) consumes a much larger share of CPU and energy; for example, a 1–2 µs tick path becomes a 10–20% CPU tax at 10 kHz. All time-based semantics must be retuned (timeslices, runtime counters, timer wheels) to avoid unintended 10× shrinkage in quanta; otherwise threads may be preempted earlier than designed. There is also ecosystem coupling because libraries and HAL components often assume a 1 ms system tick, so divergence without coordinated reconfiguration can break delays and peripherals, and the higher interrupt rate can increase cache churn and latency for other ISRs. Precision and latency improve, but throughput, power, and integration complexity worsen unless mitigations like tickless idle or per-thread hardware timers are introduced.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,3,'2025-07-30_17-43-59','Moving to a 0.1 ms tick yields ten-fold finer granularity for delays, timeouts, and software timers, with lower worst-case preemption latency and smaller jitter windows for tick-driven work, which can materially improve responsiveness for short-deadline tasks. The cost is that ISR workload scales with tick frequency: SysTick_Handler runs at 10 kHz, so any fixed bookkeeping inside the tick (including HAL maintenance) consumes a much larger share of CPU and energy; for example, a 1–2 µs tick path becomes a 10–20% CPU tax at 10 kHz. All time-based semantics must be retuned (timeslices, runtime counters, timer wheels) to avoid unintended 10× shrinkage in quanta; otherwise threads may be preempted earlier than designed. There is also ecosystem coupling because libraries and HAL components often assume a 1 ms system tick, so divergence without coordinated reconfiguration can break delays and peripherals, and the higher interrupt rate can increase cache churn and latency for other ISRs. Precision and latency improve, but throughput, power, and integration complexity worsen unless mitigations like tickless idle or per-thread hardware timers are introduced.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,4,'2025-07-29_21-51-13','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking means each thread must voluntarily give up the CPU, threads are written to “do some work, then yield,” typically by calling osYield() inside their loop. If a thread forgets to yield, it can monopolize the processor and others won’t run until it does. This is exactly how Lab 4’s co-operative round-robin is used: write two threads, have each print and then call osYield(), and the scheduler alternates them. In pre-emptive multitasking , threads no longer need to yield; the OS timer interrupt (SysTick) fires every 1 ms and forces a switch when a thread’s time slice expires, improving responsiveness and eliminating reliance on well-behaved user code. The 1 ms tick also sets the best-case reaction time and timestamp resolution for the system. From the OS’s perspective, co-operative switching is simple: osYield() traps to SVC, and the SVC handler merely pends PendSV; the actual context switch happens in PendSV_Handler (save current PSP, pick next thread, restore PSP), preserving stack discipline and keeping the handler small. In pre-emptive mode the kernel augments each TCB with a timeslice and a runtime counter; SysTick_Handler decrements the running thread’s runtime every tick, and when it reaches zero the handler resets it to the timeslice and pends PendSV to switch. The lab also cautions to keep existing HAL work inside SysTick_Handler (needed for printf) and only add the pre-emption logic around it. In conclusion, co-operative multitasking relies on threads to yield; pre-emptive multitasking relies on the timer to enforce fairness and latency guarantees.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,4,'2025-07-30_11-53-10','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking means each thread must voluntarily give up the CPU, threads are written to “do some work, then yield,” typically by calling osYield() inside their loop. If a thread forgets to yield, it can monopolize the processor and others won’t run until it does. This is exactly how Lab 4’s co-operative round-robin is used: write two threads, have each print and then call osYield(), and the scheduler alternates them. In pre-emptive multitasking , threads no longer need to yield; the OS timer interrupt (SysTick) fires every 1 ms and forces a switch when a thread’s time slice expires, improving responsiveness and eliminating reliance on well-behaved user code. The 1 ms tick also sets the best-case reaction time and timestamp resolution for the system. From the OS’s perspective, co-operative switching is simple: osYield() traps to SVC, and the SVC handler merely pends PendSV; the actual context switch happens in PendSV_Handler (save current PSP, pick next thread, restore PSP), preserving stack discipline and keeping the handler small. In pre-emptive mode the kernel augments each TCB with a timeslice and a runtime counter; SysTick_Handler decrements the running thread’s runtime every tick, and when it reaches zero the handler resets it to the timeslice and pends PendSV to switch. The lab also cautions to keep existing HAL work inside SysTick_Handler (needed for printf) and only add the pre-emption logic around it. In conclusion, co-operative multitasking relies on threads to yield; pre-emptive multitasking relies on the timer to enforce fairness and latency guarantees.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,4,'2025-07-30_17-43-59','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking means each thread must voluntarily give up the CPU, threads are written to “do some work, then yield,” typically by calling osYield() inside their loop. If a thread forgets to yield, it can monopolize the processor and others won’t run until it does. This is exactly how Lab 4’s co-operative round-robin is used: write two threads, have each print and then call osYield(), and the scheduler alternates them. In pre-emptive multitasking , threads no longer need to yield; the OS timer interrupt (SysTick) fires every 1 ms and forces a switch when a thread’s time slice expires, improving responsiveness and eliminating reliance on well-behaved user code. The 1 ms tick also sets the best-case reaction time and timestamp resolution for the system. From the OS’s perspective, co-operative switching is simple: osYield() traps to SVC, and the SVC handler merely pends PendSV; the actual context switch happens in PendSV_Handler (save current PSP, pick next thread, restore PSP), preserving stack discipline and keeping the handler small. In pre-emptive mode the kernel augments each TCB with a timeslice and a runtime counter; SysTick_Handler decrements the running thread’s runtime every tick, and when it reaches zero the handler resets it to the timeslice and pends PendSV to switch. The lab also cautions to keep existing HAL work inside SysTick_Handler (needed for printf) and only add the pre-emption logic around it. In conclusion, co-operative multitasking relies on threads to yield; pre-emptive multitasking relies on the timer to enforce fairness and latency guarantees.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,5,'2025-07-29_21-51-13','They work together because both paths funnel into the same PendSV-based context-switch but are triggered by different events. In this lab, pre-emption is timer-driven: each 1 ms SysTick_Handler decrements the running thread’s runtime; when it hits zero, the handler resets runtime to the thread’s timeslice and pends PendSV to switch away. Threads may still yield, but they no longer have to yielding simply takes the “co-operative” path that also ends by pended PendSV, so the actual switch mechanism is identical in both cases. This keeps the kernel simple: one switch routine (PendSV) services two initiation mechanisms (timer expiry or explicit yield). The two mechanisms interoperate by maintaining correct accounting: when a thread yields before its slice expires, the lab requires resetting its runtime back to its timeslice so it won’t be immediately pre-empted on return, preserving fair quanta while still allowing voluntary hand-offs for responsiveness. Conversely, a non-yielding thread is forcibly switched when runtime reaches zero. The final exercise explicitly asks to run one yielding thread alongside one non-yielding (pre-empted) thread and show they operate concurrently, demonstrating that cooperative yields and time-slice pre-emption coexist cleanly via the common PendSV switch path.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,5,'2025-07-30_11-53-10','The mechanisms differ only in triggers but share a single switching path: a voluntary yield goes through SVC and a time-slice expiry is detected in SysTick, both merely pend PendSV so that PendSV_Handler alone performs save/select/restore. With consistent accounting, if a thread yields early its runtime is reset to its timeslice so it does not resume and get immediately preempted, while a non-yielding thread is forced to switch at expiry, preserving fairness; priority ordering (SVC above PendSV) prevents re-entrancy, and keeping one locus for the switch avoids conflicting saves/restores and timing anomalies. Recording the trigger cause in the TCB or a scheduler trace aids debugging and tuning, but the core invariants remain: distinct triggers, unified switch logic, and disciplined priorities that keep stacks correct and behavior predictable.\n',1,'s25'),('kmizania',5,5,'2025-07-30_17-43-59','The mechanisms differ only in triggers but share a single switching path: a voluntary yield goes through SVC and a time-slice expiry is detected in SysTick, both merely pend PendSV so that PendSV_Handler alone performs save/select/restore. With consistent accounting, if a thread yields early its runtime is reset to its timeslice so it does not resume and get immediately preempted, while a non-yielding thread is forced to switch at expiry, preserving fairness; priority ordering (SVC above PendSV) prevents re-entrancy, and keeping one locus for the switch avoids conflicting saves/restores and timing anomalies. Recording the trigger cause in the TCB or a scheduler trace aids debugging and tuning, but the core invariants remain: distinct triggers, unified switch logic, and disciplined priorities that keep stacks correct and behavior predictable.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',1,1,'2025-06-27_03-10-39','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer and it makes it easier for people to interact with the microcontrollers hardware. It allows us to use functions that are built in instead of writing low level code to set up UART or toggle pins. It saves time and makes code easier to read and write.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',1,1,'2025-06-27_03-42-25','The __io_putchar function is responsible for handling the actual transmission of characters to an output device, usually over a UART interface. When a program uses printf, it internally breaks the output string into single characters and calls __io_putchar to send each character one at a time. This function must be defined by the developer because printf alone does not know how to communicate with specific hardware. The implementation of __io_putchar varies depending on which peripheral is being used and how it is configured. Without it, printf would not be able to display any output on the console. Defining this function is what makes printf portable across different hardware platforms, since it connects the high-level function to the low-level hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',1,2,'2025-06-27_03-10-39','__io_putchar is a function that is used when we call printf. It sends one character out at a time to the io over UART. Printf doesn’t actually output anything to the console on its own so we need to define __io_putchar so it can actually display the characters. Without this printf wouldn’t work.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',1,2,'2025-06-27_03-42-25','The __io_putchar function is responsible for handling the actual transmission of characters to an output device, usually over a UART interface. When a program uses printf, it internally breaks the output string into single characters and calls __io_putchar to send each character one at a time. This function must be defined by the developer because printf alone does not know how to communicate with specific hardware. The implementation of __io_putchar varies depending on which peripheral is being used and how it is configured. Without it, printf would not be able to display any output on the console. Defining this function is what makes printf portable across different hardware platforms, since it connects the high-level function to the low-level hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',1,3,'2025-06-27_03-10-39','We used the debugger because we want to see what is inside the registers and what the stack pointer is. The debugger lets you stop the program at a specific point and evaluate the values of the registers before it starts printing to the console. This lets you see the state of the registers.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',1,3,'2025-06-27_03-42-25','We used the debugger in this lab because it allows us to pause the program and inspect the internal state of the microcontroller, including register values and stack pointers. printf cannot be used to view these values accurately because it only displays text output after code has already executed, and by that point, the values in registers may have changed. Additionally, printf does not have access to internal CPU registers unless they are first stored in variables, which can distort the actual timing and behavior of the program. The debugger, on the other hand, provides a real-time snapshot of memory and register values at specific points during execution. This makes it a much more reliable tool for verifying that stack setup and register loading are working correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',1,4,'2025-06-27_03-10-39','We start by getting the initial value of the MSP (main stack pointer). This tells us where the stack starts originally. Since we don’t want to change the value of the MSP since it is used by the system, we create out own stack by taking some value away from the MSP where there is free space without overlapping the MSP. We need the initial value of MSP because it tells us that we are allocating memory in the correct space in RAM without messing with other memory.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',1,4,'2025-06-27_03-42-25','We started by obtaining the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which tells us where the system’s main stack begins in RAM. To create a new stack for a thread, we subtracted a fixed size from the MSP value to calculate a new address for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This ensures the new stack is placed in a valid, unused section of memory. Knowing the original MSP value is important because it lets us avoid overlap between the system stack and the new thread’s stack. This method also assumes that the MSP is aligned and located at the top of the available RAM, which guarantees that subtracting a stack size from it places the PSP within a safe and aligned memory region. Without knowing where MSP starts, we would risk placing the PSP in invalid or conflicting memory areas, which could cause unpredictable behavior or crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',1,5,'2025-06-27_03-10-39','To create multiple stacks, we would need to just keep subtracting a fixed size from the original MSP value. To do this, we need one value to store where the last allocation occurred and then use that as the new starting value to subtract the fixed size from. We do this until MSP is empty or cannot fit another stack.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',1,5,'2025-06-27_03-42-25','To support multiple stacks using this method, we would need to track the top of the stack pool using a global variable and subtract a fixed stack size each time a new stack is allocated. Each thread’s stack would start at a unique address, calculated by subtracting n × stack_size from the original MSP_init_val, where n is the number of stacks already allocated. We must ensure that each new stack address is aligned to an 8-byte boundary to satisfy the ARM Cortex-M4 alignment requirements. In addition, we need to check that the new address stays within the allocated stack pool and does not go below the minimum valid RAM address. This prevents the thread’s stack from overlapping with other data or exceeding the available memory. By keeping track of the last allocated address and updating it after each allocation, we can systematically assign safe and valid memory regions for each thread’s stack.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,1,'2025-06-27_02-55-25','When we loaded the address of print_continuously() into the PC, we basically forced the processor to jump straight to that function. The PC (program counter) always holds the address of the next instruction to run, so putting the function’s address there made the CPU start executing that code immediately. It’s like calling a function, but without the usual overhead like saving a return address. This gives you direct control over where the code goes next, though it skips the normal safety net of regular function calls.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,1,'2025-06-27_03-15-47','In this lab, we directly assigned the address of print_continuously() to the program counter (PC), which caused the CPU to immediately jump to that function and begin executing it. The PC always holds the address of the next instruction to execute, so manually setting it to the function’s start address overrides the normal program flow and transfers control there. This method is similar to a function call, but it skips the usual steps like saving the return address onto the stack. Because we bypass the standard calling convention, this approach gives more direct control but also removes the ability to return to the original location in the code. It was a useful way to understand how execution flow works at the hardware level and how function calls are managed under the hood.',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,1,'2025-06-27_03-28-36','When we manually loaded the address of the print_continuously() function into the Program Counter (PC), we effectively redirected the processor to start executing that function immediately. The PC holds the memory address of the next instruction the CPU will execute. By assigning it the address of a function, we’re telling the CPU to jump directly to that code without going through the usual process of a function call. Normally, when a function is called, the return address is pushed onto the stack so the program knows where to return afterward. In this case, by directly assigning the PC, we bypass that mechanism. This gives us low-level control over the instruction flow, which is useful in an OS context, but it also means the thread doesn’t have a return path, so it must either run indefinitely or enter a safe state when finished.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,2,'2025-06-27_02-55-25','A system call is how a user program asks the operating system to do something that it can\'t do on its own — like talking to hardware or managing memory. It works by using a special instruction (like SVC) to switch the processor into a higher privilege level so it can safely access protected resources. Basically, it’s the bridge between regular application code and the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,2,'2025-06-27_03-15-47','A system call is a special kind of function call that allows a user program to request services from the operating system’s kernel. It acts as a controlled entry point where the processor switches from user mode to kernel mode, giving the application temporary access to privileged operations like working with hardware or managing memory. System calls are triggered using software interrupts, such as the SVC instruction on ARM Cortex processors. This mechanism ensures that all access to protected system resources goes through the kernel, which acts as a gatekeeper to prevent user code from directly manipulating hardware. By wrapping privileged operations in system calls, the operating system can maintain control, enforce rules, and protect against crashes or unsafe behavior from untrusted code.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,2,'2025-06-27_03-28-36','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets user code request services from the kernel, like accessing hardware or managing memory. Since user applications run in a lower privilege mode for safety, they can’t directly access protected resources. A system call, triggered by an instruction like SVC (Supervisor Call), switches the CPU into a higher privilege level so the kernel can safely take over. This process is how user code communicates with the operating system without breaking hardware isolation or security. On the Cortex-M4, this typically involves storing register values and branching to a predefined handler in kernel space, where the request is processed.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,3,'2025-06-27_02-55-25','Setting up the thread’s stack means preparing the memory area the thread will use when it runs. This includes putting important values like the program counter (PC), xPSR, and other register values onto the stack in the right order. That way, when the processor starts the thread, it can \"pop\" those values and begin running it correctly. We do this so the thread has everything it needs to start fresh — kind of like giving it a pre-packed backpack. This is different from context switching, which is about saving and restoring progress when switching between multiple running threads.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,3,'2025-06-27_03-15-47','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a section of memory that the thread will use while it runs. This involves placing specific values on the stack, such as the initial program counter (PC), xPSR, and general-purpose registers, in the exact order the processor expects when returning from an interrupt. These values form the thread’s context and simulate the state the CPU would be in if it had paused and was about to resume the thread. We do this because when the thread starts, it needs a valid environment that tells the processor where to begin and what values to load into the registers. Unlike a context switch, which saves and restores a thread’s state during multitasking, stack setup happens only once before the thread begins. This process ensures the thread can start running correctly and independently.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,3,'2025-06-27_03-28-36','Setting up a thread’s stack means preloading a specific region of memory with the data the processor expects when the thread starts running. This includes values like the xPSR register, the function pointer (which gets loaded into PC), and default values for other CPU registers. These values are pushed in the order that the processor will later pop them off to restore context. The reason we do this is because the CPU expects a specific structure when switching to a thread’s stack—if the stack is missing these values or they’re in the wrong order, the thread will not start correctly. This setup is different from context switching, which saves and restores live data. Here, we are creating a clean environment for the thread’s first execution, ensuring it behaves as if it had always been running in that space.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,4,'2025-06-27_02-55-25','We have to call __set_PSP() to set up the thread’s stack pointer (PSP), because threads use a different stack than the main system. If we don’t set the PSP correctly, the CPU might try to use an invalid memory location for the stack, which leads to a crash (a hard fault). The PSP needs to point to the top of the stack we set up for the thread — otherwise, the thread has nowhere safe to store its data or return addresses.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,4,'2025-06-27_03-15-47','The Cortex-M4 processor has two stack pointers: the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). By default, the MSP is used for system-level tasks like interrupts, while the PSP is meant for user-level threads. Before running a thread, we must call __set_PSP() to assign the PSP to point to the top of the thread’s stack. If we skip this step, the processor might use an uninitialized or invalid address as the stack, which would lead to memory corruption and eventually cause a hard fault. Setting the PSP ensures that the thread operates on a valid stack in a safe area of memory, isolated from the system stack. This is essential to prevent crashes and to allow user threads to run reliably.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,4,'2025-06-27_03-28-36','We needed to call __set_PSP() to give the thread a valid stack pointer before execution began. Threads use the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which must be explicitly initialized to point to the top of the thread’s stack region in RAM. If this is skipped, the processor might try to use a random or null address for the stack, and the first push or function call in the thread would write to invalid memory. This triggers a hard fault, which is the CPU’s response to serious issues like illegal memory access. Setting the PSP correctly ensures the thread operates in its own isolated and safe stack space, separate from the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is used by the kernel and exceptions.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,5,'2025-06-27_02-55-25','If the thread function runs once and returns, the CPU will try to pop a return address from the stack — but our thread’s stack doesn’t have a valid return address. Since we manually set up the stack, there’s no proper place to return to. So the CPU ends up jumping to a garbage memory address, which almost always causes a crash (a hard fault). In embedded systems, threads are usually designed to run forever or go into a controlled state when done — not return like a normal function.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,5,'2025-06-27_03-15-47','If the thread function ran once and then returned, the processor would try to pop a return address from the stack to continue execution. However, since we manually created the thread’s stack and did not include a valid return address, the CPU would retrieve a meaningless value and jump to it. This usually causes the program to crash with a hard fault. In a typical embedded system without an operating system to manage thread termination, threads are not supposed to return like regular functions. Instead, they should loop forever, enter a low-power state, or be explicitly managed by the kernel. Without handling the end of the thread properly, the system cannot guarantee safe or predictable behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',2,5,'2025-06-27_03-28-36','If the thread function finished and returned, the processor would try to use a return address from the stack to determine where to go next. However, in this lab, we manually set up the thread’s stack and didn’t include a valid return address. As a result, the CPU would pop a garbage value into the Program Counter, attempting to jump to an invalid location in memory. This usually results in a hard fault. In real operating systems, the kernel would clean up after a thread finishes, or use mechanisms like idle loops or exit handlers. But on a bare-metal system like this one, threads should be designed to run indefinitely or explicitly enter a safe state. Returning like a normal function without a valid return target is unsafe and causes the system to crash.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',3,1,'2025-06-27_03-01-21','When working with multiple .c files in a project, each source file is compiled on its own by the compiler into an object file. This object file contains machine code but is not a complete program by itself. After all .c files are compiled into their respective object files, a separate tool called the linker is used to combine them. The linker connects function calls, variable references, and symbols between files to make sure everything fits together. The result is a single executable file that includes code from all the .c files and can be loaded onto the microcontroller. This process makes large programs more manageable and allows developers to organize their code across multiple files.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',3,1,'2025-06-27_03-33-12','When building a project with multiple .c files, the first step is preprocessing. During this stage, the compiler processes all #include and #define directives, replacing them with actual content or values. Next comes the compilation step, where each individual .c file is translated into an object file containing machine code. However, these object files are not complete programs on their own. The final step is linking, where a separate tool called the linker combines all the object files into a single executable. The linker resolves all cross-references between files, such as function calls or variable definitions. This whole process allows developers to split their code into logical parts while ensuring it all comes together into one working program. Skipping or misunderstanding the preprocessing step could lead to missing definitions or macros, which would cause errors during compilation or unexpected behavior in the final program.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',3,2,'2025-06-27_03-01-21','Using #include to bring in a .c file, such as writing #include <kernel.c>, is not appropriate because it bypasses the standard compile-link process. Instead of compiling each source file separately and linking them properly, the preprocessor simply copies the entire contents of the .c file into the file where the directive appears. This can lead to duplicate definitions, name conflicts, and errors during compilation. It also makes the code harder to read and maintain. The correct approach is to include a header file like kernel.h, which contains function declarations, and let the build system compile kernel.c separately. This keeps the project organized and avoids unnecessary problems.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',3,2,'2025-06-27_03-33-12','Using #include with a .c file is considered bad practice because it skips the normal compilation process. Instead of compiling kernel.c separately, the preprocessor copies its entire contents directly into the file that included it. This can cause multiple definitions of the same functions or variables if the .c file is included in more than one place, which leads to compilation errors or undefined behavior. Proper C programming separates function declarations (which go in .h files) from function definitions (which go in .c files). The build system is then responsible for compiling each .c file independently and linking the object files together. Including .c files directly breaks this model and makes the project harder to debug, maintain, and scale.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',3,3,'2025-06-27_03-01-21','To start a thread from the user’s point of view, the first step is to write a function that defines what the thread should do. This function contains the code that will run when the thread is launched. Next, the user calls a kernel function that is responsible for creating the thread. This function sets up a stack for the thread and stores information about it in the kernel. Once all the desired threads have been created, the user calls another kernel function to start the system. This function triggers the scheduler or startup routine to begin running threads. At this point, the kernel takes over and begins executing threads using the stacks that were prepared earlier.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',3,3,'2025-06-27_03-33-12','To start a thread in the system, the user must follow a few key steps in the correct order. First, the user must initialize the kernel using a function provided by the OS. This prepares the system’s internal data, including the stack pool. Next, the user defines a function that contains the behavior they want the thread to perform. Once the thread function is written, the user calls another kernel function to create the thread, which allocates stack memory and sets up the thread’s metadata. After all threads have been created, the user then calls the kernel’s start function to begin execution. This final call switches the system from setup mode into running mode, launching the first thread.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',3,4,'2025-06-27_03-01-21','The stack pool is a dedicated section of RAM that is reserved for thread stacks. Each thread in the operating system needs its own private stack to store local variables, function call data, and saved register values. In this lab, we allocated stack space for one thread manually, but in a future lab, the stack pool will allow us to create multiple threads by dividing it into separate regions. Each region can be assigned to a thread by subtracting a fixed size from the top of the pool, ensuring that stacks do not overlap. This approach gives each thread a safe area of memory to use and is essential for supporting multitasking in the operating system.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',3,4,'2025-06-27_03-33-12','The stack pool is a reserved portion of RAM used to allocate stacks for individual threads in the operating system. Each thread needs its own private stack space to store temporary values like local variables, return addresses, and register contents. The pool starts at a known location, usually the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer, and stack memory is allocated by subtracting fixed sizes for each new thread. In future labs, this stack pool will allow the system to support multiple threads by assigning each one a unique portion of the pool. By tracking how much of the pool is used, the system ensures that stacks do not overlap. This organized approach makes it possible to safely run multiple threads without corrupting memory.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',3,5,'2025-06-27_03-01-21','In an embedded operating system, the kernel refers to the low-level code that manages system resources, while the user refers to the application code that performs specific tasks. Code that belongs to the kernel goes into files like kernel.c and includes things such as stack allocation, thread management, and system calls. Code that belongs to the user goes into files like main.c and includes thread functions and setup code written by the programmer. This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on writing application logic without having to deal with the complexity of how the OS works internally. It also keeps the codebase organized and makes it easier to reuse or update parts of the kernel without changing the user\'s code.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',3,5,'2025-06-27_03-33-12','The user and the kernel represent two distinct layers of code in an embedded operating system. The kernel contains low-level code that manages system resources such as stack allocation, thread scheduling, and hardware access. This code goes into files like kernel.c and is not meant to be edited by the application developer. On the other hand, the user code contains the specific functions that define the behavior of the program, like what each thread does, and goes into files like main.c. This separation is important because it keeps the kernel stable and reusable, while allowing the user to focus on writing application logic. From the user’s perspective, this modularity also makes the system easier to understand and maintain, since they can interact with the kernel through a defined interface without needing to worry about how it works internally.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,1,'2025-07-29_02-14-38','Round-robin scheduling works naturally with a queue because a queue is designed to handle things in order first in, first out. So when a thread finishes its time slice or calls osYield, we can just take it off the front of the queue and push it to the back. Then, the thread at the front becomes the new one that runs. This lets the scheduler easily move through all the threads in a loop, just like round-robin is supposed to.\nThe main benefit of using a queue over an array is that the logic for scheduling becomes simpler and cleaner you don’t have to track indexes or wrap around with modulo arithmetic. Also, if you ever decide to support features like thread termination or dynamic creation/destruction of threads at runtime, queues make it easier to remove or add threads on the fly without shifting a bunch of data like you’d have to with an array.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,1,'2025-07-30_21-25-13','Round-robin scheduling works well with a queue because queues naturally process items in a first-in, first-out (FIFO) order. When a thread yields or finishes its time slice, the scheduler can simply pop it from the front of the queue and push it to the back. This allows the next thread in line to be scheduled, creating a smooth, automatic rotation through all available threads.\nIn our current implementation using an array, we’re already simulating this queue-like behavior by incrementing an index and wrapping around with modulo arithmetic. So while a queue makes this logic cleaner and easier to understand, the array approach can be just as effective with some extra logic.\nThat said, using a queue can introduce some overhead, especially if we’re dealing with linked list-based queues or dynamic memory operations. These could slightly impact performance compared to using a fixed-size array. However, the flexibility of a queue becomes more valuable in systems where threads can be created or destroyed dynamically — it allows for easy insertion and removal without needing to shift elements or recalculate indices.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,1,'2025-07-30_22-04-03','Round-robin scheduling works well with a queue because queues naturally process items in a first-in, first-out (FIFO) order. When a thread yields or finishes its time slice, the scheduler can simply pop it from the front of the queue and push it to the back. This allows the next thread in line to be scheduled, creating a smooth, automatic rotation through all available threads.\nIn our current implementation using an array, we’re already simulating this queue-like behavior by incrementing an index and wrapping around with modulo arithmetic. So while a queue makes this logic cleaner and easier to understand, the array approach can be just as effective with some extra logic.\nThat said, using a queue can introduce some overhead, especially if we’re dealing with linked list-based queues or dynamic memory operations. These could slightly impact performance compared to using a fixed-size array. However, the flexibility of a queue becomes more valuable in systems where threads can be created or destroyed dynamically — it allows for easy insertion and removal without needing to shift elements or recalculate indices.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,2,'2025-07-29_02-14-38','PendSV_Handler is the part of the system that actually performs the context switch between threads. When osYield is called, we trigger PendSV by setting a bit in a special interrupt register. Since PendSV is a low-priority interrupt, it only runs when all other interrupts are done, which is important to avoid weird timing bugs.\nInside the handler (which is assembly code), it first saves the current thread’s registers (R4–R11) onto the current thread’s stack. Then it calls osSched, a C function, which updates the current thread index and sets the stack pointer to the next thread’s stack. After that, it pops the new thread’s registers off its stack and sets the processor’s stack pointer to match. When it exits, the CPU starts running the new thread like nothing happened. So even though we switched threads, it’s totally seamless — the CPU has no idea a switch happened.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,2,'2025-07-30_21-25-13','PendSV_Handler handles the actual context switch between threads. When osYield is called, it sets a bit in a special interrupt control register to trigger PendSV. Because PendSV is the lowest-priority interrupt, it waits until all other interrupts are finished before running, which helps avoid timing conflicts.\nInside PendSV_Handler (written in assembly), the first step is to read the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This tells the OS where the current thread’s stack is. The handler then saves the current thread’s registers (R4–R11) onto that stack.\nAfter saving the registers, PendSV_Handler calls the osSched C function. This function selects the next thread to run and updates the stack pointer to point to the next thread’s stack. Back in assembly, the new thread’s saved registers are restored from its stack, and the PSP is updated accordingly.\nWhen PendSV_Handler exits, the CPU continues execution as if nothing happened — it’s running a new thread, but seamlessly, because the registers and stack pointer were all updated behind the scenes.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,2,'2025-07-30_22-04-03','PendSV_Handler handles the actual context switch between threads. When osYield is called, it sets a bit in a special interrupt control register to trigger PendSV. Because PendSV is the lowest-priority interrupt, it waits until all other interrupts are finished before running, which helps avoid timing conflicts.\nInside PendSV_Handler (written in assembly), the first step is to read the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This tells the OS where the current thread’s stack is. The handler then saves the current thread’s registers (R4–R11) onto that stack.\nAfter saving the registers, PendSV_Handler calls the osSched C function. This function selects the next thread to run and updates the stack pointer to point to the next thread’s stack. Back in assembly, the new thread’s saved registers are restored from its stack, and the PSP is updated accordingly.\nWhen PendSV_Handler exits, the CPU continues execution as if nothing happened — it’s running a new thread, but seamlessly, because the registers and stack pointer were all updated behind the scenes.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,3,'2025-07-29_02-14-38','The problem with always choosing the highest priority thread is that lower-priority threads might never get to run. If a high-priority thread is always ready, it’ll keep getting picked again and again, and everything else will be starved — this is called starvation. That means even if a lower-priority thread is important, like updating the display or saving data, it might never get a chance to run because the scheduler keeps ignoring it.\nRound-robin doesn’t have this issue since every thread gets a fair turn. If you really want to include priorities, you need a better strategy like priority aging or dynamic priority adjustment so low-priority threads aren’t stuck forever. But just going with “always run the highest” is too simple and causes more harm than good.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,3,'2025-07-30_21-25-13','The problem with always choosing the highest-priority thread is that it can lead to starvation — lower-priority threads might never get to run if a high-priority one is always ready. That could cause serious issues, especially if those lower-priority threads handle important background tasks like saving data or refreshing a display.\nRound-robin scheduling avoids this by giving each thread a fair time slice, regardless of priority. If you really want to factor in priorities, you need a smarter approach — like priority aging, which gradually boosts the priority of waiting threads so they eventually get scheduled.\nOther strategies like multi-level feedback queues or priority ceilings can also help balance fairness and responsiveness. But simply picking the highest-priority thread every time is too simplistic and leads to unfairness and inefficiency.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,3,'2025-07-30_22-04-03','The core issue with always picking the highest-priority thread is that only that thread will ever run, and no other threads will get a chance to execute — even once. This happens because as long as a high-priority thread stays in the ready state, the scheduler continuously selects it, completely ignoring all lower-priority threads.\nThis isn’t just a case of eventual starvation — it’s worse. It’s a flaw in the scheduler’s logic: the design guarantees that no other thread can ever run unless the highest-priority thread is somehow removed or blocked. That means tasks like updating the display, saving logs, or running background checks — even if they’re necessary — will be completely skipped if they have lower priority.\nIn the long run, this causes the system to behave unfairly and potentially malfunction, especially if those lower-priority tasks are critical for stability or user interaction. This is very different from round-robin scheduling, where each thread is guaranteed CPU time, regardless of its importance.\nIf priorities are needed, the scheduler should include mechanisms like priority aging or predefined time slices for lower-priority threads. These approaches help ensure that all tasks eventually get CPU time, even in the presence of high-priority tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,4,'2025-07-29_02-14-38','Calling a C function from assembly makes the code way easier to write, read, and debug. C is much more readable than assembly, especially when dealing with stuff like array indexing, updating variables, or doing math. You can also reuse code and call other C functions without worrying about low-level register management. It’s just cleaner and faster to work with.\nThe downside is that calling C from assembly adds a bit of overhead. The CPU has to follow calling conventions, push arguments, and maybe save some extra registers. Also, you need to be careful that your C function doesn’t mess with registers the assembly expects to stay the same.\nBut honestly, for a lab or small OS like this, the benefits far outweigh the downsides. It keeps things way more manageable. Writing everything in assembly might save a few CPU cycles, but it would make development way more painful.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,4,'2025-07-30_21-25-13','Calling a C function from within assembly — like osSched in PendSV_Handler or SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler — makes the code much more manageable. C is more readable and easier to maintain than assembly, especially when dealing with logic-heavy tasks like choosing the next thread or updating scheduling variables.\nThis approach also helps with debugging and reusing code, since we can take advantage of existing C functions instead of writing everything from scratch in assembly.\nThe downside is that calling C from assembly introduces some overhead. The CPU has to follow calling conventions, which means saving and restoring certain registers and managing stack frames carefully. There’s also a small performance cost due to the function call itself.\nStill, for a small OS like ours, this trade-off is worth it. The slight performance hit is outweighed by the clarity, flexibility, and maintainability you get by writing more of your logic in C rather than low-level assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,4,'2025-07-30_22-04-03','Calling a C function from assembly makes the code way easier to write, read, and debug. C is much more readable than assembly, especially when dealing with stuff like array indexing, updating variables, or doing math. You can also reuse code and call other C functions without worrying about low-level register management. It’s just cleaner and faster to work with.\nThe downside is that calling C from assembly adds a bit of overhead. The CPU has to follow calling conventions, push arguments, and maybe save some extra registers. Also, you need to be careful that your C function doesn’t mess with registers the assembly expects to stay the same.\nBut honestly, for a lab or small OS like this, the benefits far outweigh the downsides. It keeps things way more manageable. Writing everything in assembly might save a few CPU cycles, but it would make development way more painful.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,5,'2025-07-29_02-14-38','Yeah, the OS would still work technically, but you wouldn’t really notice the multitasking part. Since there’s only one thread, it would yield, trigger a context switch, and then just switch back to itself. So you’d go through the motions of switching threads, but it’d always be the same one getting picked. It’s kind of like pressing next on a playlist that only has one song — it works, but it’s pointless.\nThe print would still show up, and you’d see the output just like in Lab 3, so from a user point of view, nothing’s broken. But behind the scenes, all the context switch overhead is still there even though you’re not actually switching between different threads. So it’s not wrong, it’s just not doing much.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,5,'2025-07-30_21-25-13','If you only create a single thread — like the print_continuously thread from previous labs — the OS will still work just fine. When the thread yields, the scheduler gets triggered and does a full context switch, but since there\'s only one thread in the system, it just ends up selecting itself again.\nSo the switching mechanism still runs, but it’s basically switching from the thread… to itself. It’s like a playlist with only one song — you can hit “next” as much as you want, but you’ll always hear the same thing.\nFrom a performance perspective, the system still incurs the context switch overhead, which includes saving and restoring registers and updating the stack pointer, even though no actual switch occurs. This adds a bit of inefficiency, but it doesn’t break anything. It just makes the multitasking part redundant since there\'s nothing to switch to.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',4,5,'2025-07-30_22-04-03','Yeah, the OS would still work technically, but you wouldn’t really notice the multitasking part. Since there’s only one thread, it would yield, trigger a context switch, and then just switch back to itself. So you’d go through the motions of switching threads, but it’d always be the same one getting picked. It’s kind of like pressing next on a playlist that only has one song — it works, but it’s pointless.\nThe print would still show up, and you’d see the output just like in Lab 3, so from a user point of view, nothing’s broken. But behind the scenes, all the context switch overhead is still there even though you’re not actually switching between different threads. So it’s not wrong, it’s just not doing much.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',5,1,'2025-07-29_03-13-39','From the user’s side, when they want to create a thread and pass in an argument, they just call osAddThread() and give it the thread function and the argument as parameters. So, for example, osAddThread(myThread, 0, (void*) someValue); is what they’d write. That third parameter is the actual argument they want to pass into the thread.\nBehind the scenes, the OS is responsible for taking that argument and putting it in the right place so the thread can actually access it when it starts running. Specifically, the OS puts the argument in register r0 of the new thread\'s stack frame because that’s where arguments go according to the ARM calling convention. When the thread starts running, the CPU expects the first function argument to be in r0, and since the OS placed it there during stack setup, everything lines up.\nThis works because when the thread is initialized, we simulate the stack frame that would’ve been there if the thread had been started normally. That includes faking the r0 value so it holds the argument we want. The function then just uses that value like any other parameter. So overall, the user just gives us the value, the OS sets it up in the stack properly, and it works because the CPU follows a known convention when calling functions.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',5,1,'2025-07-30_21-30-26','From the user’s side, when they want to create a thread and pass in an argument, they just call osAddThread() and give it the thread function and the argument as parameters. So, for example, osAddThread(myThread, 0, (void*) someValue); is what they’d write. That third parameter is the actual argument they want to pass into the thread.\nBehind the scenes, the OS is responsible for taking that argument and putting it in the right place so the thread can actually access it when it starts running. Specifically, the OS puts the argument in register r0 of the new thread\'s stack frame because that’s where arguments go according to the ARM calling convention. When the thread starts running, the CPU expects the first function argument to be in r0, and since the OS placed it there during stack setup, everything lines up.\nThis works because when the thread is initialized, we simulate the stack frame that would’ve been there if the thread had been started normally. That includes faking the r0 value so it holds the argument we want. The function then just uses that value like any other parameter. So overall, the user just gives us the value, the OS sets it up in the stack properly, and it works because the CPU follows a known convention when calling functions.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',5,2,'2025-07-29_03-13-39','You can’t call osYield() inside any of the context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because those functions are already in the middle of switching context. osYield() itself triggers a context switch by setting the PendSV flag, but if you’re already inside a context switch, doing that again creates a big problem — it’s like calling another switch while one is already happening.\nIf you try to do it anyway, what happens is that the system gets stuck in a weird loop of trying to switch contexts endlessly. You might end up corrupting the stack or jumping to unexpected parts of memory, and your whole OS can crash or just freeze. That’s because you\'re messing with the CPU state mid-transition, which is unstable. Basically, calling osYield() from within a context-switching function breaks the assumption that only one switch happens at a time, and the OS just can’t handle that.\n',0,'s25'),('kmorshed',5,2,'2025-07-30_21-30-26','You can\'t call osYield() from inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these functions are already deep inside a context switch. osYield() works by setting the PendSV interrupt flag, which tells the system to trigger a context switch as soon as possible. Under the hood, this is done by writing to a special register that requests a PendSV interrupt, moving from user mode to privileged mode via an interrupt event.\nBut if you’re already in one of these handlers — which means the system is already in privileged mode and inside an interrupt — trying to request another context switch can break everything. It leads to nested or overlapping context switches that the OS isn’t prepared to handle. This can corrupt the stack (since it’s already saving/restoring registers), or cause unpredictable jumps and even hardfaults, where the CPU locks up trying to deal with an invalid or corrupted state.\nThe key rule here is that only one context switch should happen at a time, and osYield() is a tool for user-level code to ask for a switch — not something to use when the OS is already in the middle of performing one.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',5,3,'2025-07-29_03-13-39','If we make the OS clock tick every 0.1ms instead of 1ms, the biggest benefit is better responsiveness. Threads that wait for time-based delays (like sleeping for a few milliseconds) will be woken up more precisely, and the scheduler can react to time-based events more quickly. This might be useful in real-time systems where timing is super important.\nBut the downside is that it puts a lot more load on the CPU. Every clock tick triggers an interrupt, and handling those interrupts takes time — even if there’s nothing to do. So now the CPU has to handle 10 times more interrupts than before, which eats into the time available for actual thread work. It can slow things down overall and drain more power too, which is a big deal in embedded systems. So unless you really need that extra precision, it’s usually not worth the trade-off.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',5,3,'2025-07-30_21-30-26','If we make the OS clock tick every 0.1ms instead of 1ms, the biggest benefit is better responsiveness. Threads that wait for time-based delays (like sleeping for a few milliseconds) will be woken up more precisely, and the scheduler can react to time-based events more quickly. This might be useful in real-time systems where timing is super important.\nBut the downside is that it puts a lot more load on the CPU. Every clock tick triggers an interrupt, and handling those interrupts takes time — even if there’s nothing to do. So now the CPU has to handle 10 times more interrupts than before, which eats into the time available for actual thread work. It can slow things down overall and drain more power too, which is a big deal in embedded systems. So unless you really need that extra precision, it’s usually not worth the trade-off.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',5,4,'2025-07-29_03-13-39','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking means that threads have to be “nice” and give up control by calling osYield() themselves. So it’s on the programmer to make sure threads don’t hog the CPU. If a thread doesn’t yield, the others might not get a chance to run, which can be a problem.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS doesn’t wait for threads to yield — it just interrupts them after a certain amount of time (like every 1ms) and switches to the next thread. From the user’s point of view, this feels more automatic and fair, because each thread gets time whether it cooperates or not.\nOn the OS side, co-operative multitasking is easier to implement. The OS just needs to switch contexts when a thread calls osYield(), which is predictable and safe. For pre-emptive multitasking, the OS has to use hardware timers (like SysTick), and it needs to be ready to save and restore thread state in the middle of whatever the thread was doing. That makes it more complex, but it’s also more powerful because it prevents any one thread from blocking the system.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',5,4,'2025-07-30_21-30-26','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking means that threads have to be “nice” and give up control by calling osYield() themselves. So it’s on the programmer to make sure threads don’t hog the CPU. If a thread doesn’t yield, the others might not get a chance to run, which can be a problem.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS doesn’t wait for threads to yield — it just interrupts them after a certain amount of time (like every 1ms) and switches to the next thread. From the user’s point of view, this feels more automatic and fair, because each thread gets time whether it cooperates or not.\nOn the OS side, co-operative multitasking is easier to implement. The OS just needs to switch contexts when a thread calls osYield(), which is predictable and safe. For pre-emptive multitasking, the OS has to use hardware timers (like SysTick), and it needs to be ready to save and restore thread state in the middle of whatever the thread was doing. That makes it more complex, but it’s also more powerful because it prevents any one thread from blocking the system.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',5,5,'2025-07-29_03-13-39','Yielding and time-based switching work together because they both use the same context-switching mechanism — they both trigger PendSV to run. Whether a thread calls osYield() manually, or the SysTick handler triggers a switch every 1ms, the result is the same: PendSV runs and swaps the thread.\nWhat makes them able to mix is that the scheduler doesn’t care who asked for the switch — it just checks which thread should go next and does the swap. That means a thread can yield when it’s done its work early, but if it forgets or takes too long, the timer still steps in to force a switch after 1ms.\nSo the system ends up being a combo: if threads are polite, things switch smoothly with cooperative yielding. If not, the timer keeps things fair. They don’t conflict because they both lead to the same scheduler behavior — they just differ in when and why the switch happens.\n',1,'s25'),('kmorshed',5,5,'2025-07-30_21-30-26','Yielding and time-based switching work together because they both use the same context-switching mechanism — they both trigger PendSV to run. Whether a thread calls osYield() manually, or the SysTick handler triggers a switch every 1ms, the result is the same: PendSV runs and swaps the thread.\nWhat makes them able to mix is that the scheduler doesn’t care who asked for the switch — it just checks which thread should go next and does the swap. That means a thread can yield when it’s done its work early, but if it forgets or takes too long, the timer still steps in to force a switch after 1ms.\nSo the system ends up being a combo: if threads are polite, things switch smoothly with cooperative yielding. If not, the timer keeps things fair. They don’t conflict because they both lead to the same scheduler behavior — they just differ in when and why the switch happens.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,1,'2025-05-13_10-54-03','The HAL is a base layer of functionality and functions that we can use to develop and implement functionality onto hardware. The HAL helps us communicate with our STM32 and setup our code so that we can create a general embedded project in a timely manner.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,1,'2025-05-13_11-27-21','The HAL is an acronym for hardware abstraction layer and it is a base layer of functionality and functions that we can use to develop and implement functionality onto hardware. The HAL operate as a medium between which the OS and the hardware. The HAL helps us communicate with our STM32 and setup our code so that we can create a general embedded project in a timely manner.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,1,'2025-05-13_15-14-44','The HAL is an acronym for hardware abstraction layer and it is a base layer of functionality and functions that we can use to develop and implement functionality onto hardware. The HAL operate as a medium between which the OS and the hardware. The HAL helps us communicate with our STM32 and setup our code so that we can create a general embedded project in a timely manner.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,1,'2025-05-14_12-33-12','The HAL is an acronym for hardware abstraction layer and it is a base layer of functionality and functions that we can use to develop and implement functionality onto hardware. The HAL operate as a medium between the OS and the hardware. The HAL helps us communicate with our STM32 and setup our code so that we can create a general embedded project in a timely manner. Using the HAL built in functions allows for easy flexibility when changing between hardware. For example using the HAL_transmit function on different hardware requires nothing more than changing the output channel. The overall functionality of the code remains the same.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,1,'2025-05-27_09-38-48','The HAL is an acronym for hardware abstraction layer and it is a base layer of functionality and functions that we can use to develop and implement functionality onto hardware. The HAL operate as a medium between the OS and the hardware. The HAL helps us communicate with our STM32 and setup our code so that we can create a general embedded project in a timely manner. Using the HAL built in functions allows for easy flexibility when changing between hardware. For example using the HAL_transmit function on different hardware requires nothing more than changing the output channel. The overall functionality of the code remains the same.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,1,'2025-06-10_08-50-36','The HAL is an acronym for hardware abstraction layer and it is a base layer of functionality and functions that we can use to develop and implement functionality onto hardware. The HAL operate as a medium between the OS and the hardware. The HAL helps us communicate with our STM32 and setup our code so that we can create a general embedded project in a timely manner. Using the HAL built in functions allows for easy flexibility when changing between hardware. For example using the HAL_transmit function on different hardware requires nothing more than changing the output channel. The overall functionality of the code remains the same.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,2,'2025-05-13_10-54-03','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that takes in a character input and using the HAL, transmits the character to an IO channel (in our case, UART2). This function is then used by multiple other functions, notably printf, to output characters or strings.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,2,'2025-05-13_11-27-21','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that takes in a character input and using the HAL, transmits the character to an IO channel (in our case, UART2). This function is then used by multiple other functions, notably printf, to output characters or strings. The reason we need to write our own putchar function is that we do not always want to output the character to the same channel. Each time we write a putchar function, we can choose exactly where the character is transmitted using HAL_Transmit. This ensures that the printf will function no matter what hardware platform we are developing for. Without the putchar function, printf would not know where to print to, causing errors.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,2,'2025-05-13_15-14-44','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that takes in a character input and using the HAL, transmits the character to an IO channel (in our case, UART2). This function is then used by multiple other functions, notably printf, to output characters or strings. The reason we need to write our own putchar function is that we do not always want to output the character to the same channel. Each time we write a putchar function, we can choose exactly where the character is transmitted using HAL_Transmit. This ensures that the printf will function no matter what hardware platform we are developing for. Without the putchar function, printf would not know where to print to, causing errors.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,2,'2025-05-14_12-33-12','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that takes in a character input and using the HAL, transmits the character to an IO channel (in our case, UART2). This function is then used by multiple other functions, notably printf, to output characters or strings. The reason we need to write our own putchar function is that we do not always want to output the character to the same channel. Each time we write a putchar function, we can choose exactly where the character is transmitted using HAL_Transmit. This ensures that the printf will function no matter what hardware platform we are developing for. Without the putchar function, printf would not know where to print to, causing errors.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,2,'2025-05-27_09-38-48','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that takes in a character input and using the HAL, transmits the character to an IO channel (in our case, UART2). This function is then used by multiple other functions, notably printf, to output characters or strings. The reason we need to write our own putchar function is that we do not always want to output the character to the same channel. Each time we write a putchar function, we can choose exactly where the character is transmitted using HAL_Transmit. This ensures that the printf will function no matter what hardware platform we are developing for. Without the putchar function, printf would not know where to print to, causing errors.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,2,'2025-06-10_08-50-36','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that takes in a character input and using the HAL, transmits the character to an IO channel (in our case, UART2). This function is then used by multiple other functions, notably printf, to output characters or strings. The reason we need to write our own putchar function is that we do not always want to output the character to the same channel. Each time we write a putchar function, we can choose exactly where the character is transmitted using HAL_Transmit. This ensures that the printf will function no matter what hardware platform we are developing for. Without the putchar function, printf would not know where to print to, causing errors.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,3,'2025-05-13_10-54-03','We needed to use the debugger in this lab to view the values of the registers and of the stack itself in a neat and organized way. Using printf to instrument our code is more time consuming and can affect the registers themselves.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,3,'2025-05-13_11-27-21','We needed to use the debugger in this lab to view the values of the registers and of the stack itself in a neat and organized way. Using printf to instrument our code is more time consuming and can affect the registers themselves.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,3,'2025-05-13_15-14-44','We needed to use the debugger in this lab to view the values of the registers and of the stack itself in a neat and organized way. Using printf to instrument our code is more time consuming and can affect the registers themselves due to it being a function that exists on the stack. Furthermore, printf cannot directly access the registers whereas the debugger can. The debugger does not interact with the code, rather, it acts as an observer whereas printf directly interacts with the code in order to look at the registers.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,3,'2025-05-14_12-33-12','We needed to use the debugger in this lab to view the values of the registers and of the stack itself in a neat and organized way. Using printf to instrument our code is more time consuming and can affect the registers themselves due to it being a function that exists on the stack. Furthermore, printf cannot directly access the registers whereas the debugger can. The debugger does not interact with the code, rather, it acts as an observer whereas printf directly interacts with the code in order to look at the registers.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,3,'2025-05-27_09-38-48','We needed to use the debugger in this lab to view the values of the registers and of the stack itself in a neat and organized way. Using printf to instrument our code is more time consuming and can affect the registers themselves due to it being a function that exists on the stack. Furthermore, printf cannot directly access the registers whereas the debugger can. The debugger does not interact with the code, rather, it acts as an observer whereas printf directly interacts with the code in order to look at the registers.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,3,'2025-06-10_08-50-36','We needed to use the debugger in this lab to view the values of the registers and of the stack itself in a neat and organized way. Using printf to instrument our code is more time consuming and can affect the registers themselves due to it being a function that exists on the stack. Furthermore, printf cannot directly access the registers whereas the debugger can. The debugger does not interact with the code, rather, it acts as an observer whereas printf directly interacts with the code in order to look at the registers.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,4,'2025-05-13_10-54-03','The way our stack allocation method works is by taking an initial value for the beginning of our stack, MSP_init_val and beginning with our main stack. This main stack houses our setup and other OS-specific things required for the program to run. Below that are the stacks that we allocate ourselves. For example if we wanted our main stack to be 0x600 bytes, we would start our first thread at MSP_init_val – 0x600 since our stack grows downwards and we are now giving the main stack 0x600 bytes of space. This stack allocation method is why we needed MSP_init_val to get a new value for PSP_val. Without MSP_init_val, we could not ensure the size of our main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,4,'2025-05-13_11-27-21','The way our stack allocation method works is by taking an initial value for the beginning of our stack, MSP_init_val and beginning with our main stack. This main stack houses our setup and other OS-specific things required for the program to run. Below that are the stacks that we allocate ourselves. For example if we wanted our main stack to be 0x600 bytes, we would start our first thread at MSP_init_val – 0x600 since our stack grows downwards and we are now giving the main stack 0x600 bytes of space. This stack allocation method is why we needed MSP_init_val to get a new value for PSP_val. Without MSP_init_val, we could not ensure the size of our main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,4,'2025-05-13_15-14-44','The way our stack allocation method works is by taking an initial value for the beginning of our stack, MSP_init_val and beginning with our main stack. This main stack houses our setup and other OS-specific things required for the program to run. Below that are the stacks that we allocate ourselves. For example if we wanted our main stack to be 0x600 bytes, we would start our first thread at MSP_init_val – 0x600 since our stack grows downwards and we are now giving the main stack 0x600 bytes of space. This stack allocation method is why we needed MSP_init_val to get a new value for PSP_val. Without MSP_init_val, we could not ensure the size of our main stack.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,4,'2025-05-14_12-33-12','The way our stack allocation method works is by taking an initial value for the beginning of our stack, MSP_init_val and beginning with our main stack. This main stack houses our setup and other OS-specific things required for the program to run. Below that are the stacks that we allocate ourselves. For example if we wanted our main stack to be 0x600 bytes, we would start our first thread at MSP_init_val – 0x600 since our stack grows downwards and we are now giving the main stack 0x600 bytes of space. This stack allocation method is why we needed MSP_init_val to get a new value for PSP_val. Without MSP_init_val, we could not ensure the size of our main stack or that the location we are setting for PSP_val is a valid spot in memory. Without knowing MSP_init_val, PSP_val might overwrite memory that is already in use, causing unexpected functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,4,'2025-05-27_09-38-48','The way our stack allocation method works is by taking an initial value for the beginning of our stack, MSP_init_val and beginning with our main stack. This main stack houses our setup and other OS-specific things required for the program to run. Below that are the stacks that we allocate ourselves. For example if we wanted our main stack to be 0x600 bytes, we would start our first thread at MSP_init_val – 0x600 since our stack grows downwards and we are now giving the main stack 0x600 bytes of space. This stack allocation method is why we needed MSP_init_val to get a new value for PSP_val. Without MSP_init_val, we could not ensure the size of our main stack or that the location we are setting for PSP_val is a valid spot in memory. Without knowing MSP_init_val, PSP_val might overwrite memory that is already in use, causing unexpected functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,4,'2025-06-10_08-50-36','The way our stack allocation method works is by taking an initial value for the beginning of our stack, MSP_init_val and beginning with our main stack. This main stack houses our setup and other OS-specific things required for the program to run. Below that are the stacks that we allocate ourselves. For example if we wanted our main stack to be 0x600 bytes, we would start our first thread at MSP_init_val – 0x600 since our stack grows downwards and we are now giving the main stack 0x600 bytes of space. This stack allocation method is why we needed MSP_init_val to get a new value for PSP_val. Without MSP_init_val, we could not ensure the size of our main stack or that the location we are setting for PSP_val is a valid spot in memory. Without knowing MSP_init_val, PSP_val might overwrite memory that is already in use, causing unexpected functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,5,'2025-05-13_10-54-03','To use the same stack allocation method to make a number, n, new stacks, we would do the following. After defining our main stack size using MSP_init_val – MAIN_STACK_SIZE, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = MSP_init_val - MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (n-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE if all threads were the same size. If the threads were not all the same size, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = MSP_init_val - MAIN_STACK_SIZE –  num_bytes_allocated where num_bytes_allocated is an ever incrementing value storing the sum of all thread sizes.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,5,'2025-05-13_11-27-21','To use the same stack allocation method to make a number, n, new stacks, we would do the following. After defining our main stack size using MSP_init_val – MAIN_STACK_SIZE, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = MSP_init_val - MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (n-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE if all threads were the same size. If the threads were not all the same size, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = STACK_(N-1) – STACK_SIZE(N-1) where stack 0 is the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,5,'2025-05-13_15-14-44','To use the same stack allocation method to make a number, n, new stacks, we would do the following. After defining our main stack size using MSP_init_val – MAIN_STACK_SIZE, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = MSP_init_val - MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (n-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE if all threads were the same size. If the threads were not all the same size, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = STACK_(N-1) – STACK_SIZE(N-1) where stack 0 is the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,5,'2025-05-14_12-33-12','To use the same stack allocation method to make a number, n, new stacks, we would do the following. After defining our main stack size using MSP_init_val – MAIN_STACK_SIZE, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = MSP_init_val - MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (n-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE if all threads were the same size. If the threads were not all the same size, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = STACK_(N-1) – STACK_SIZE(N-1) where stack 0 is the main stack.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,5,'2025-05-27_09-38-48','To use the same stack allocation method to make a number, n, new stacks, we would do the following. After defining our main stack size using MSP_init_val – MAIN_STACK_SIZE, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = MSP_init_val - MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (n-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE if all threads were the same size. If the threads were not all the same size, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = STACK_(N-1) – STACK_SIZE(N-1) where stack 0 is the main stack. If we cannot assume that the stack sizes are a multiple of 8, to assure memory alignment we may wish to increment the address of STACK_N until STACK_N % 8 == 0. These formulas will ensure that stacks do not overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',1,5,'2025-06-10_08-50-36','To use the same stack allocation method to make a number, n, new stacks, we would do the following. After defining our main stack size using MSP_init_val – MAIN_STACK_SIZE, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = MSP_init_val - MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (n-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE if all threads were the same size. If the threads were not all the same size, the address of STACK_N would be defined as STACK_N = STACK_(N-1) – STACK_SIZE(N-1) where stack 0 is the main stack. If we cannot assume that the stack sizes are a multiple of 8, to assure memory alignment we may wish to increment the address of STACK_N until STACK_N % 8 == 0. These formulas will ensure that stacks do not overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,1,'2025-06-23_13-21-47','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to print_continuously, the function was executed. The reasoning behind why print_continuously was run when the pointer was passed to PC is that PC acts as a guide to what instructions should be executed by storing the address of the current instruction. When we force the address of print_continuously to be stored in PC using jumpAssembly, we are creating a jump where we explicitly tell the PC what happens next as opposed to it incrementing through memory addresses linearly.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,1,'2025-06-23_13-46-54','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to print_continuously, the function was executed. The reasoning behind why print_continuously was run when the pointer was passed to PC is that PC acts as a guide to what instructions should be executed by storing the address of the current instruction. When we force the address of print_continuously to be stored in PC using jumpAssembly, we are creating a jump where we explicitly tell the PC what happens next as opposed to it incrementing through memory addresses linearly. This is the same as calling the function normally in main; the PC will jump to the address of print_continuously and will execute the function much the same way as us jumping to the print_continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,1,'2025-06-23_14-58-13','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to print_continuously, the function was executed. The reasoning behind why print_continuously was run when the pointer was passed to PC is that PC acts as a guide to what instructions should be executed by storing the address of the current instruction. When we force the address of print_continuously to be stored in PC using jumpAssembly, we are creating a jump where we explicitly tell the PC what happens next as opposed to it incrementing through memory addresses linearly. This is the same as calling the function normally in main; the PC will jump to the address of print_continuously and will execute the function much the same way as us jumping to the print_continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,1,'2025-06-25_12-51-56','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to print_continuously, the function was executed. The reasoning behind why print_continuously was run when the pointer was passed to PC is that PC acts as a guide to what instructions should be executed by storing the address of the current instruction. When we force the address of print_continuously to be stored in PC using jumpAssembly, we are creating a jump where we explicitly tell the PC what happens next as opposed to it incrementing through memory addresses linearly. This is the same as calling the function normally in main; the PC will jump to the address of print_continuously and will execute the function much the same way as us jumping to the print_continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,1,'2025-06-25_17-19-02','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to print_continuously, the function was executed. The reasoning behind why print_continuously was run when the pointer was passed to PC is that PC acts as a guide to what instructions should be executed by storing the address of the current instruction. When we force the address of print_continuously to be stored in PC using jumpAssembly, we are creating a jump where we explicitly tell the PC what happens next as opposed to it incrementing through memory addresses linearly. This is the same as calling the function normally in main; the PC will jump to the address of print_continuously and will execute the function much the same way as us jumping to the print_continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,1,'2025-06-26_13-07-56','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer to print_continuously, the function was executed. The reasoning behind why print_continuously was run when the pointer was passed to PC is that PC acts as a guide to what instructions should be executed by storing the address of the current instruction. When we force the address of print_continuously to be stored in PC using jumpAssembly, we are creating a jump where we explicitly tell the PC what happens next as opposed to it incrementing through memory addresses linearly. This is the same as calling the function normally in main; the PC will jump to the address of print_continuously and will execute the function much the same way as us jumping to the print_continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,2,'2025-06-23_13-21-47','A system call is a type of interrupt where the program calls for the operating system to perform a specific task. When the OS receives the interrupt, it switches to kernel mode, executes the required process, and returns to user mode to give control back to the program.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,2,'2025-06-23_13-46-54','A system call is a type of interrupt where the program calls for the operating system to perform a specific task such as writing to a file or a console. These system calls are the barebones of the higher-level function calls that we use. When the OS receives the interrupt, it switches to kernel mode, executes the required process, and returns to user mode to give control back to the program. This allows the program to use restricted code without posing a security risk by forcing the user program to ask the OS for permission to use the restricted code. The OS can now perform the requested task if it is deemed safe, or reject the request if it would cause issues.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,2,'2025-06-23_14-58-13','A system call is a type of interrupt where the program calls for the operating system to perform a specific task such as writing to a file or a console. These system calls are the bridge between the user program and the OS. Though we rarely need to explicitly make a system call ourselves as the functions in the standard libraries act as system call wrappers, most functions will involve system calls. When the OS receives the interrupt, it switches to kernel mode, executes the required process, and returns to user mode to give control back to the program. This allows the program to use restricted code without posing a security risk by forcing the user program to ask the OS for permission to use the restricted code. The OS can now perform the requested task if it is deemed safe or reject the request if it would cause issues.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,2,'2025-06-25_12-51-56','A system call is a function call that a user program makes to request a service from the operating system’s kernel. It acts as a well-defined interface (or API) through which user-level applications can perform privileged operations, such as reading from a file, writing to the console, or allocating memory—operations that are not allowed directly in user mode for security and stability reasons.\nWhen a program makes a system call—such as write() to output text to the screen—it invokes a special function provided by a standard library (like libc in C). This function executes a predefined sequence that includes triggering a software interrupt or trap, which signals the CPU to switch from user mode to kernel mode. This switch allows the operating system to safely carry out the requested action on behalf of the user program.\nIt\'s important to distinguish between the system call itself (which is the function call made by the program) and the interrupt mechanism (which is the underlying hardware-level process that facilitates the switch to kernel mode). The system call is the programmer-facing part; the interrupt is part of what happens behind the scenes to carry it out securely.\nOnce the OS completes the task, it returns control to the user program, resuming in user mode. This process ensures that user programs can access essential OS services while maintaining strict boundaries to protect system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,2,'2025-06-25_17-19-02','A system call is a function call that a user program makes to request a service from the operating system’s kernel. It acts as a well-defined interface (or API) through which user-level applications can perform privileged operations, such as reading from a file, writing to the console, or allocating memory—operations that are not allowed directly in user mode for security and stability reasons.\nWhen a program makes a system call—such as write() to output text to the screen—it invokes a special function provided by a standard library (like libc in C). This function executes a predefined sequence that includes triggering a software interrupt or trap, which signals the CPU to switch from user mode to kernel mode. This switch allows the operating system to safely carry out the requested action on behalf of the user program.\nIt\'s important to distinguish between the system call itself (which is the function call made by the program) and the interrupt mechanism (which is the underlying hardware-level process that facilitates the switch to kernel mode). The system call is the programmer-facing part; the interrupt is part of what happens behind the scenes to carry it out securely.\nOnce the OS completes the task, it returns control to the user program, resuming in user mode. This process ensures that user programs can access essential OS services while maintaining strict boundaries to protect system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,2,'2025-06-26_13-07-56','A system call is a function call that a user program makes to request a service from the operating system’s kernel. It acts as a well-defined interface (or API) through which user-level applications can perform privileged operations, such as reading from a file, writing to the console, or allocating memory—operations that are not allowed directly in user mode for security and stability reasons.\nWhen a program makes a system call—such as write() to output text to the screen—it invokes a special function provided by a standard library (like libc in C). This function executes a predefined sequence that includes triggering a software interrupt or trap, which signals the CPU to switch from user mode to kernel mode. This switch allows the operating system to safely carry out the requested action on behalf of the user program.\nIt\'s important to distinguish between the system call itself (which is the function call made by the program) and the interrupt mechanism (which is the underlying hardware-level process that facilitates the switch to kernel mode). The system call is the programmer-facing part; the interrupt is part of what happens behind the scenes to carry it out securely.\nOnce the OS completes the task, it returns control to the user program, resuming in user mode. This process ensures that user programs can access essential OS services while maintaining strict boundaries to protect system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,3,'2025-06-23_13-21-47','The thread’s stack was set up by creating a stack pointer to the stack and by setting the values of the thread’s registers so that the computer thinks that the thread was already there. This way the thread will pop the register values into the hardware registers and will run the thread. If we do not store register values on the stack, the hardware will not pop the values onto the hardware registers, preventing the thread from executing.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,3,'2025-06-23_13-46-54','The thread’s stack was set up by creating a stack pointer to the stack and by setting the values of the thread’s registers so that the computer thinks that the thread was already there. This way the thread will pop the register values into the hardware registers and will run the thread. If we do not store register values on the stack, the hardware will not pop the values onto the hardware registers, preventing the thread from executing. The way the registers are set up is the first entry on the stack is xPSR, the second is PC, then the link register and the general registers follow. If this order is not properly set, the thread may exhibit unexpected qualities or hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,3,'2025-06-23_14-58-13','The thread’s stack was set up by creating a stack pointer to the stack and by setting the values of the thread’s registers so that the computer thinks that the thread was already there. This way the thread will pop the register values into the hardware registers and will run the thread. If we do not store register values on the stack, the hardware will not pop the values onto the hardware registers, preventing the thread from executing. The way the registers are set up is the first entry on the stack is xPSR, the second is PC, then the link register and the general registers follow. If this order is not properly set, the thread may exhibit unexpected qualities or hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,3,'2025-06-25_12-51-56','Setting up a thread’s stack involves more than just assigning a stack pointer—it requires allocating a valid memory region for the stack, usually in RAM, where the thread\'s local variables, return addresses, and saved register values will be stored. This memory must be properly reserved and aligned to ensure the stack operates correctly during execution.\nOnce a valid stack space is allocated, the stack pointer is initialized to point to the top (highest address) of that stack region. Then, the initial values for key CPU registers are pushed onto the stack in a specific order: typically starting with xPSR, followed by PC (the address of the thread\'s entry point), the link register (LR), and then general-purpose registers like R0–R12. This setup mimics the state the CPU expects after an interrupt or context switch.\nThe reason we store register values on the stack is to simulate a running thread so that when the scheduler selects this thread to run, the CPU can simply \"pop\" these values off the stack into the actual hardware registers. This enables the thread to begin executing as if it had already been running. If these values are missing or out of order, the thread could crash, misbehave, or trigger faults like a hard fault.\nThis careful setup ensures the thread starts cleanly and that the CPU behaves predictably during thread context switches.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,3,'2025-06-25_17-19-02','Setting up a thread’s stack involves more than just assigning a stack pointer—it requires allocating a valid memory region for the stack, usually in RAM, where the thread\'s local variables, return addresses, and saved register values will be stored. This memory must be properly reserved and aligned to ensure the stack operates correctly during execution.\nOnce a valid stack space is allocated, the stack pointer is initialized to point to the top (highest address) of that stack region. Then, the initial values for key CPU registers are pushed onto the stack in a specific order: typically starting with xPSR, followed by PC (the address of the thread\'s entry point), the link register (LR), and then general-purpose registers like R0–R12. This setup mimics the state the CPU expects after an interrupt or context switch.\nThe reason we store register values on the stack is to simulate a running thread so that when the scheduler selects this thread to run, the CPU can simply \"pop\" these values off the stack into the actual hardware registers. This enables the thread to begin executing as if it had already been running. If these values are missing or out of order, the thread could crash, misbehave, or trigger faults like a hard fault.\nThis careful setup ensures the thread starts cleanly and that the CPU behaves predictably during thread context switches.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,3,'2025-06-26_13-07-56','Setting up a thread’s stack involves more than just assigning a stack pointer—it requires allocating a valid memory region for the stack, usually in RAM, where the thread\'s local variables, return addresses, and saved register values will be stored. This memory must be properly reserved and aligned to ensure the stack operates correctly during execution.\nOnce a valid stack space is allocated, the stack pointer is initialized to point to the top (highest address) of that stack region. Then, the initial values for key CPU registers are pushed onto the stack in a specific order: typically starting with xPSR, followed by PC (the address of the thread\'s entry point), the link register (LR), and then general-purpose registers like R0–R12. This setup mimics the state the CPU expects after an interrupt or context switch.\nThe reason we store register values on the stack is to simulate a running thread so that when the scheduler selects this thread to run, the CPU can simply \"pop\" these values off the stack into the actual hardware registers. This enables the thread to begin executing as if it had already been running. If these values are missing or out of order, the thread could crash, misbehave, or trigger faults like a hard fault.\nThis careful setup ensures the thread starts cleanly and that the CPU behaves predictably during thread context switches.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,4,'2025-06-23_13-21-47','We had to set PSP using the __set_PSP function before we could run the thread because by where the stack pointer was, the register values we pushed onto the stack would get popped into the hardware registers and our thread would execute instead of crashing with a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,4,'2025-06-23_13-46-54','We had to set PSP using the __set_PSP function before we could run the thread because by where the stack pointer was, the register values we pushed onto the stack would get popped into the hardware registers and our thread would execute instead of crashing with a hard fault. If we did not use the __set_PSP function, we would still be using our MSP value, which is protected memory reserved for the OS. If we try to use this memory, the resulting hard fault is a security measure designed to prevent unauthorized access to protected areas.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,4,'2025-06-23_14-58-13','We had to set PSP using the __set_PSP function before we could run the thread because by where the stack pointer was, the register values we pushed onto the stack would get popped into the hardware registers and our thread would execute instead of crashing with a hard fault. If we did not use the __set_PSP function, we would still be using our MSP value, which is protected memory reserved for the OS. If we try to use this memory, the resulting hard fault is a security measure designed to prevent unauthorized access to protected areas. In general, MSP is meant for use by the kernel whereas PSP is meant for use by individual tasks/threads. Setting PSP to the stack address allows for the context to be given to the stack as we fill the registers and allows for the PSP to know where the stack begins.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,4,'2025-06-25_12-51-56','We must use __set_PSP() to explicitly set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to the thread’s stack address before running the thread because, by default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is reserved for kernel-level operations like handling interrupts or exceptions. The PSP, on the other hand, is intended for user threads.\nIf we don’t set the PSP, the CPU will continue using the MSP during thread execution. Since the thread’s stack was initialized in a separate memory region (not where the MSP points), any attempt by the CPU to pop register values or access the stack would refer to the wrong memory location—potentially a protected or invalid area. This mismatch leads to incorrect memory access and ultimately causes a hard fault.\nBy calling __set_PSP() with the address of the thread’s stack, we ensure that the CPU uses the correct stack region when the thread is restored and starts executing. This allows the thread’s context (registers like xPSR, PC, LR, etc.) to be correctly popped off the stack, preventing a fault and allowing proper execution to begin.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,4,'2025-06-25_17-19-02','We must use __set_PSP() to explicitly set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to the thread’s stack address before running the thread because, by default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is reserved for kernel-level operations like handling interrupts or exceptions. The PSP, on the other hand, is intended for user threads.\nIf we don’t set the PSP, the CPU will continue using the MSP during thread execution. Since the thread’s stack was initialized in a separate memory region (not where the MSP points), any attempt by the CPU to pop register values or access the stack would refer to the wrong memory location—potentially a protected or invalid area. This mismatch leads to incorrect memory access and ultimately causes a hard fault.\nBy calling __set_PSP() with the address of the thread’s stack, we ensure that the CPU uses the correct stack region when the thread is restored and starts executing. This allows the thread’s context (registers like xPSR, PC, LR, etc.) to be correctly popped off the stack, preventing a fault and allowing proper execution to begin.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,4,'2025-06-26_13-07-56','We must use __set_PSP() to explicitly set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to the thread’s stack address before running the thread because, by default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is reserved for kernel-level operations like handling interrupts or exceptions. The PSP, on the other hand, is intended for user threads.\nIf we don’t set the PSP, the CPU will continue using the MSP during thread execution. Since the thread’s stack was initialized in a separate memory region (not where the MSP points), any attempt by the CPU to pop register values or access the stack would refer to the wrong memory location—potentially a protected or invalid area. This mismatch leads to incorrect memory access and ultimately causes a hard fault.\nBy calling __set_PSP() with the address of the thread’s stack, we ensure that the CPU uses the correct stack region when the thread is restored and starts executing. This allows the thread’s context (registers like xPSR, PC, LR, etc.) to be correctly popped off the stack, preventing a fault and allowing proper execution to begin.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,5,'2025-06-23_13-21-47','If the thread function did not loop continuously the thread would terminate as soon as the function terminated and would result in a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,5,'2025-06-23_13-46-54','If the thread function did not loop continuously the thread would terminate as soon as the function terminated and would result in a hard fault due to a lack of a return address. When the processor attempts to return to an address that does not exist, a hard fault occurs to protect the system and to stop the processor from going to a random area of memory.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,5,'2025-06-23_14-58-13','If the thread function did not loop continuously the thread would terminate as soon as the function terminated and would result in a hard fault due to a lack of a return address. When the processor attempts to return to an address that does not exist, a hard fault occurs to protect the system and to stop the processor from going to a random area of memory. The return address would be stored on the link register and because we manually set that register to 0x0A, the address we are trying to acces is within our OS’ stack and as such, is protected.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,5,'2025-06-25_12-51-56','If the thread function did not loop continuously the thread would terminate as soon as the function terminated and would result in a hard fault due to a lack of a return address. When the processor attempts to return to an address that does not exist, a hard fault occurs to protect the system and to stop the processor from going to a random area of memory. The return address would be stored on the link register at the point when print_continuously was first called, and would tell the thread what to do afterwards, because the link register is not updated properly, it would hard fault when print_continuously terminates\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,5,'2025-06-25_17-19-02','If the thread function did not loop continuously the thread would terminate as soon as the function terminated and would result in a hard fault due to a lack of a return address. When the processor attempts to return to an address that does not exist, a hard fault occurs to protect the system and to stop the processor from going to a random area of memory. The return address would be stored on the link register at the point when print_continuously was first called, and would tell the thread what to do afterwards, because the link register is not updated properly, it would hard fault when print_continuously terminates\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',2,5,'2025-06-26_13-07-56','If the thread function does not loop continuously and instead reaches the end of the function, the thread will terminate and the processor will attempt to return to an address stored on the stack—not the link register (LR). In the Cortex-M4 architecture, when a thread is launched, its stack is preloaded with a fake context that includes values for registers such as xPSR, PC, and a return address.\nThis return address is critical because, when the thread finishes executing, the CPU pops this value off the stack and attempts to return to it. However, since thread functions in our OS are not meant to return (they’re usually written as infinite loops), this return address is often invalid or not set at all. If the stack does not contain a valid return address, the processor will try to jump to an undefined or restricted memory location, which triggers a hard fault.\nUnlike traditional function calls where the LR stores the return address, Cortex-M4 threads rely on the stack to simulate the context of a preempted thread. The stack must contain all the necessary information for the CPU to resume execution, and if the return address is missing or corrupted, the processor cannot safely continue—hence the hard fault.\nThis is why thread functions must loop or otherwise never return—so the CPU never attempts to pop an invalid return address from the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,1,'2025-06-25_13-12-02','When the files are placed in the same project folder, the files are capable are accessing each other using the #include command. When this happens, the linker will link the files together into a single .exe.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,1,'2025-06-25_17-54-16','When the files are placed in the same project folder, the files are capable are accessing each other using the #include command. When this happens, the linker will link the files together into a single .exe.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,1,'2025-06-26_15-23-59','The process of compiling multiple .c files into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compilation, and linking.\nPreprocessing:\nEach .c file is first passed through the preprocessor. The preprocessor handles all directives that start with #, such as #include, #define, and #ifdef. For example, #include inserts the contents of header files directly into the source file. The result is a \"pure\" C file with all macros expanded and included files copied in.\nCompilation:\nAfter preprocessing, each individual source file is compiled by the compiler into an object file (.o or .obj). The compiler translates C code into machine code, but at this point, each object file contains only part of the program and may include unresolved symbols (like function calls or variables defined in other files).\nLinking:\nFinally, all the object files from the compiled .c files are passed to the linker. The linker’s job is to resolve these references between files—for instance, connecting a function call in one file to the function definition in another. It combines all the object files into a single executable file (e.g., .exe or .elf), ready to be run by the system.\nAlthough placing .c files in the same project folder makes them easier to organize and find, what really matters is how they’re listed in the build configuration (e.g., makefile or IDE project settings). Simply being in the same folder doesn’t automatically link them—each file must be compiled and explicitly included in the linking step.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,2,'2025-06-25_13-12-02','Including a .c file will essentially copy that file into another file. This will result in functions being defined twice and the compiler will likely throw an error.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,2,'2025-06-25_17-54-16','Including a .c file will essentially copy that file into another file. This will result in functions being defined twice and the compiler will likely throw an error.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,2,'2025-06-26_15-23-59','Including a .c file will essentially copy that file into another file. This will result in functions being defined twice and the compiler will likely throw an error.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,3,'2025-06-25_13-12-02','The user creates a function that they want to run on a thread. The user then calls osCreateThread and passes their function into the thread. The user then calls kernelstart\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,3,'2025-06-25_17-54-16','The user creates a function that they want to run on a thread. The user then calls osCreateThread and passes their function into the thread. The user then calls kernelstart\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,3,'2025-06-26_15-23-59','To set up and start a thread in the system, the user follows these general steps:\nInitialize the kernel:\nBefore doing anything else, the user must initialize the kernel. This sets up internal data structures and resources the operating system needs to manage threads. Without this step, thread creation will not work properly.\nWrite a thread function:\nThe user creates a function that contains the code they want to run in the thread. This function should typically loop or run continuously, since threads are not meant to return like normal functions.\nRegister the thread with the kernel:\nThe user uses a thread creation function (e.g., osCreateThread) to register their thread function with the kernel. This step allocates stack space for the thread, sets up its initial context (like register values and stack pointer), and adds it to the kernel’s thread list.\nStart the kernel:\nOnce all threads have been created, the user calls a function like kernelStart. This begins thread execution, typically by launching the first thread and enabling context switching.\nThis process ensures that threads are correctly set up, registered, and managed by the kernel, enabling multitasking in the system.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,4,'2025-06-25_13-12-02','The stack pool is a designated region of memory set aside to hold the individual stacks for multiple threads in the operating system. Each thread requires its own separate stack to store its local variables, function return addresses, and saved register values during context switches. Without separate stacks, threads would overwrite each other’s data, leading to crashes or unpredictable behavior.\nIn a future lab, the stack pool will allow us to dynamically allocate stack space for each new thread we create. For example, when we create a new thread, we can assign it a unique section of memory from the stack pool and initialize its stack with the correct register values and entry point. We then set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to that thread’s stack region before execution.\nBy organizing memory this way, the OS can support multiple threads running independently. The stack pool ensures that each thread has an isolated memory space for its execution context, enabling safe and efficient multitasking in our system.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,4,'2025-06-25_17-54-16','The stack pool is a designated region of memory set aside to hold the individual stacks for multiple threads in the operating system. Each thread requires its own separate stack to store its local variables, function return addresses, and saved register values during context switches. Without separate stacks, threads would overwrite each other’s data, leading to crashes or unpredictable behavior.\nIn a future lab, the stack pool will allow us to dynamically allocate stack space for each new thread we create. For example, when we create a new thread, we can assign it a unique section of memory from the stack pool and initialize its stack with the correct register values and entry point. We then set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to that thread’s stack region before execution.\nBy organizing memory this way, the OS can support multiple threads running independently. The stack pool ensures that each thread has an isolated memory space for its execution context, enabling safe and efficient multitasking in our system.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,4,'2025-06-26_15-23-59','The stack pool is a designated region of memory set aside to hold the individual stacks for multiple threads in the operating system. Each thread requires its own separate stack to store its local variables, function return addresses, and saved register values during context switches. Without separate stacks, threads would overwrite each other’s data, leading to crashes or unpredictable behavior.\nIn a future lab, the stack pool will allow us to dynamically allocate stack space for each new thread we create. For example, when we create a new thread, we can assign it a unique section of memory from the stack pool and initialize its stack with the correct register values and entry point. We then set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to that thread’s stack region before execution.\nBy organizing memory this way, the OS can support multiple threads running independently. The stack pool ensures that each thread has an isolated memory space for its execution context, enabling safe and efficient multitasking in our system.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,5,'2025-06-25_13-12-02','The kernel contains the core operating system code. This includes low-level components such as thread management, context switching, interrupt handling, and privileged operations. Kernel code is placed in kernel.c and runs in privileged (kernel) mode, meaning it has full access to the hardware and system resources.\nThe user code contains the application logic or threads that the developer writes to perform specific tasks. This code goes into main.c and runs in unprivileged (user) mode, which restricts access to critical system features and hardware.\nThis provides safety for the user, simplifies programming and supports multithreading.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,5,'2025-06-25_17-54-16','The kernel contains the core operating system code. This includes low-level components such as thread management, context switching, interrupt handling, and privileged operations. Kernel code is placed in kernel.c and runs in privileged (kernel) mode, meaning it has full access to the hardware and system resources.\nThe user code contains the application logic or threads that the developer writes to perform specific tasks. This code goes into main.c and runs in unprivileged (user) mode, which restricts access to critical system features and hardware.\nThis provides safety for the user, simplifies programming and supports multithreading.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',3,5,'2025-06-26_15-23-59','The kernel contains the core operating system code. This includes low-level components such as thread management, context switching, interrupt handling, and privileged operations. Kernel code is placed in kernel.c and runs in privileged (kernel) mode, meaning it has full access to the hardware and system resources.\nThe user code contains the application logic or threads that the developer writes to perform specific tasks. This code goes into main.c and runs in unprivileged (user) mode, which restricts access to critical system features and hardware.\nThis provides safety for the user, simplifies programming and supports multithreading.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',4,1,'2025-07-27_13-18-27','Round-robin scheduling can work using a circular queue instead of an array by making a linked list storing pointers to each thread and having the tail of the queue point back to the head. This has the benefit of making deleting threads that are not the most recent easier as the queue can easily remove an element whereas in an array, the new list would have to be copied to a new array.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',4,1,'2025-07-27_13-43-11','Round-robin scheduling works well with a queue because a queue naturally supports first-in, first-out (FIFO) behavior, which matches the logic of giving each thread a fair and equal share of CPU time. In this model, each thread control block (TCB) is enqueued when it\'s ready to run and dequeued when it\'s its turn to execute. After using its time slice, it’s re-enqueued at the end of the queue if it\'s still runnable. This cycle continues, allowing the scheduler to rotate through the threads in the order they arrived, maintaining fairness and predictability.\nUsing a queue instead of an array can reduce overhead because it eliminates the need to shift elements around to maintain order. For example, in an array, removing a thread from the front (after it runs) would typically require shifting all other elements forward to fill the gap. A queue structure avoids this by adjusting pointers, making enqueue and dequeue operations more efficient. This supports the round-robin model’s goal of low scheduling overhead and functional simplicity.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',4,2,'2025-07-27_13-18-27','PendSV executes a context switch by triggering a pendable interrupt, meaning it will run when no other interrupt is running and will actually perform the context switch using the pointer passed to it inside an interrupt, which is crucial as the Cortex M4 cannot perform a context switch outside of an interrupt.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',4,2,'2025-07-27_13-43-11','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the current thread\'s execution state and restoring the state of the next scheduled thread. This is done using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which holds the location of the current thread’s stack in memory. Since user threads in an RTOS typically run using the PSP (not the Main Stack Pointer), the handler uses this register to access and manipulate the thread’s context.\nWhen the PendSV_Handler is triggered, it first saves the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto the current thread’s stack. These registers are manually pushed because the processor doesn’t automatically save them during an interrupt. The updated PSP value (which now points to the saved context) is then stored in the thread’s TCB so it can be resumed later.\nNext, the handler calls the osSched function, which is the scheduler. This function selects the next thread to run by updating a global pointer to point to the TCB of the next ready thread.\nOnce the new thread is selected, the handler loads its saved PSP from its TCB and restores the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) by popping them from the new thread’s stack. When the handler exits, the processor automatically restores the caller-saved registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) from the new thread’s stack—these were saved automatically during the exception entry.\nBy carefully managing the PSP and saving/restoring registers, PendSV_Handler allows seamless switching between threads, preserving the full CPU state of each one.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',4,3,'2025-07-27_13-18-27','This would be more inefficient as with lower numbers being higher priority, the scheduler can begin looking for tasks at priority 0 and increasing the priority value until it matches a task. With higher number being higher priority, the scheduler must know the priority value of all tasks and compare them with each other to determine the highest priority.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',4,3,'2025-07-27_13-43-11','Modifying the scheduler to always choose the highest-priority task (with higher integers meaning higher priority) is a bad idea because it can cause starvation. If a high-priority task is always ready to run and never yields or blocks, then lower-priority tasks may never get CPU time at all. This results in an unfair system where only the top-priority task runs, even if other tasks are important but simply ranked lower.\nIn contrast, round-robin scheduling gives each task a fair time slice in a fixed, repeating order, regardless of priority. Every task gets a turn to execute, which ensures that no task is permanently ignored. This fairness helps maintain system responsiveness, especially in systems with multiple background tasks or user-level processes that must all make progress over time.\nWhile priority-based scheduling can be useful in real-time systems to ensure urgent tasks run first, it must be combined with mechanisms like aging or preemption limits to avoid starving lower-priority tasks. Round-robin avoids this problem by design, trading strict priority for fairness and predictability.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',4,4,'2025-07-27_13-18-27','Calling C functions increases the readability of our code by putting it into a higher level language as well as making it more accessible for developers who do not have assembly knowledge to modify and iterate on top of the code.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',4,4,'2025-07-27_13-43-11','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain instead of writing everything in assembly has several benefits:\nImproved readability and maintainability: C is a high-level language with clearer syntax, making the code easier to read and understand, especially for developers unfamiliar with low-level assembly instructions.\nEasier development and debugging: Writing in C allows us to take advantage of compiler features, type checking, and standard debugging tools, which greatly simplify the development process compared to debugging raw assembly.\nPortability and reuse: C code is more portable across different architectures and microcontrollers. If we wanted to move to a different ARM core or even a different processor entirely, the C functions could likely be reused with minimal changes, whereas assembly code is usually tightly tied to the instruction set of a specific CPU.\nOne downside, however, is that calling C functions from within an exception handler like PendSV_Handler or SVC_Handler introduces some overhead, both in terms of CPU cycles and stack usage. Assembly is more efficient for fine-grained control, so using C might slightly impact performance or responsiveness in timing-critical applications.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',4,5,'2025-07-27_13-18-27','It would work as the OS_yield function ensures pendSV runs and a context switch can be completed, exiting SVC_handler and allowing the local variables to be freed.\n',0,'s25'),('ktcollin',4,5,'2025-07-27_13-43-11','Yes, the OS will still work if only a single thread is created, such as a modified print_continuously thread that yields after printing. When OS_Yield() is called, it triggers a PendSV interrupt to request a context switch. The scheduler (osSched) is then invoked to choose the next thread to run.\nHowever, since there is only one thread in the system, the scheduler will always select that same thread again. As a result, the context switch will occur, but it will effectively switch from the thread back to itself. This may seem redundant, but the mechanism still functions correctly, and the thread will continue running in a loop, yielding and being rescheduled over and over.\nIn this setup, the OS demonstrates that its context switching and scheduling infrastructure works, even though it\'s always switching to the same thread. There is no error or failure; the system remains stable. However, no multitasking or true concurrency occurs because there\'s only one runnable task.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',5,1,'2025-07-27_17-16-34','1. User Responsibilities\nBefore the OS ever runs your thread, you must:\nDefine a struct (or primitive) that holds every piece of data your thread needs.\nAllocate or declare that struct in a scope that outlives the thread (global, static, or heap).\nPopulate its fields with the values you want to pass in.\nCall the RTOS thread‐creation API, passing your thread function pointer and the address of your struct as the void* argument.\n2. What the OS Does Under the Hood\nOnce you request a new thread, the kernel:\nAllocates a Thread Control Block (TCB) and its dedicated stack region.\nBuilds an initial “exception stack frame” on that new stack. In Cortex-M this frame contains, in order:\nxPSR\nReturn Address (PC = your thread function)\nLR (EXC_RETURN marker)\nR12, R3, R2, R1\nR0 – loaded with the void* argument you passed\nSets the thread’s Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to that frame.\nMarks the new thread Ready and, on the next scheduling event (PendSV or Systick), switches context to it.\n3. Why It Works\nARM’s Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS) dictates that the first function parameter arrives in register R0.\nBy initializing R0 in the exception stack frame, the OS hands your thread function exactly the pointer you provided.\nYour thread function can safely cast that void* back to your struct type and dereference it.\nAs long as the storage for your struct remains valid, each thread sees the correct data in its own first argument.\nWhen the scheduler performs a context switch:\nIt saves the outgoing thread’s CPU registers back onto its stack.\nIt loads the incoming thread’s PSP, pops the saved registers (including R0–R12, LR, PC), and performs an exception return.\nThe hardware exception return mechanism automatically jumps to the PC it popped—in this case, your thread function—with R0 already loaded.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',5,2,'2025-07-27_17-16-34','When you call osYield you’re invoking a thread-level API that assumes it’s running in normal thread context, not inside an interrupt handler. Internally osYield is implemented by setting the PendSV pending bit. Both SVC_Handler and PendSV_Handler execute in handler mode using the main stack pointer, so mixing a thread-level call into that environment breaks the clear separation between “a thread asking to be rescheduled” and “the kernel doing its reschedule work.”\nIf you force osYield from within PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler you end up with nested exceptions. Each exception pushes a new hardware stack frame (registers, LR, PC) onto the main stack, so repeated nesting can overflow or corrupt the stack. At the same time, Cortex-M schedulers typically configure PendSV at the lowest interrupt priority so it only runs when no other handler is active. Triggering it again from within an active handler can prevent the pending bit from ever clearing, leaving you stuck in the handler forever or crashing unpredictably.\nThis is bad design because it violates the RTOS’s single, controlled context-switch sequence. You won’t return cleanly from the original exception, exception returns won’t unwind properly, and interrupt timing becomes non-deterministic. It also breaks the ARM procedure call standard by mixing up handler and thread semantics.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',5,3,'2025-07-27_17-16-34','Changing the fundamental OS clock tick from 1ms to 0.1ms makes your system ten times more responsive, but there are both advantages and disadvantages.\nOn the benefit side, the RTOS becomes more responsive. Time-based functions like delays, timeouts, and periodic threads now operate with finer granularity. For example, a delay of one tick now lasts 0.1ms instead of 1ms. This also means high-priority tasks can be scheduled more frequently, reducing latency. A smaller tick interval also improves timing accuracy for applications that require precise measurements, such as motor control, signal sampling, or tight synchronization. Additionally, if your system includes animations or visual updates, smoother transitions are possible because timing resolution improves.\nHowever, this comes with costs. A faster tick rate means more frequent interrupts—ten thousand per second instead of one thousand. Each of these consumes CPU cycles, leading to increased overhead. In battery-powered or low-power applications, this increased activity reduces efficiency because the processor wakes more often. Even in performance-focused systems, excessive context switching can hurt throughput, as the processor spends more time switching tasks than doing useful work. On slower microcontrollers, it may even be impossible to keep up with the tick rate.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',5,4,'2025-07-27_17-16-34','From the user’s point of view, cooperative multitasking means that tasks voluntarily give up control of the CPU. Each thread runs until it chooses to yield or call a blocking function like delay or wait. The user has to be careful to ensure no thread hogs the CPU or forgets to yield, or else other tasks won\'t get a chance to run. It offers predictability and simpler debugging, but places more responsibility on the programmer.\nWith pre-emptive multitasking, the operating system has more control. The OS uses a hardware timer, like SysTick, to interrupt running threads and decide if a higher-priority one should run instead. The user doesn’t have to insert yields manually—context switching happens automatically at regular intervals or when priorities demand it. This makes the system more responsive and fair, especially when tasks have unpredictable run times or bugs that prevent them from yielding.\nFrom the OS’s perspective, cooperative multitasking is easier. It doesn’t need to handle interrupts or save and restore contexts unless a thread requests a switch. The scheduler just waits for threads to yield and then decides which one to run next.\nPre-emptive multitasking is more complex. The OS must set up a periodic interrupt, usually with SysTick, to trigger time-based context switches. It needs an efficient scheduler that can track priorities, manage stack frames, and perform safe transitions between threads. It also must handle the delicate timing of switching in and out of thread and handler modes without corrupting the processor state.\nBoth models have strengths. Cooperative multitasking works well in simple systems where timing isn’t critical. Pre-emptive multitasking suits real-time systems that need guaranteed responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('ktcollin',5,5,'2025-07-27_17-16-34','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they’re complementary scheduling strategies managed by the same RTOS kernel. Pre-emptive context switching uses a hardware timer like SysTick to interrupt running threads at fixed intervals, allowing the OS to compare thread priorities and switch to a higher-priority task if needed. This happens automatically and ensures fairness and responsiveness, especially when tasks run long or misbehave.\nYielding, on the other hand, is cooperative. A thread voluntarily calls a function like osYield to signal it’s done using the CPU for now. The OS then looks at its list of ready threads and picks the most suitable one to resume. This provides extra flexibility and allows threads to give control explicitly when they know they’re done with something.\nThe RTOS maintains a scheduler that handles both cases. On a SysTick interrupt, the scheduler checks priorities and flags to decide if a context switch is needed. When a thread yields, the scheduler is triggered manually and performs the same logic. In both cases, the RTOS saves the current thread’s context and restores the new thread’s state before resuming execution.\nThe key is that both mechanisms use the same switching infrastructure: saving CPU registers, managing stacks, and updating the active thread pointer. Yielding gives programmers manual control when it makes sense, and time-based pre-emption ensures consistent system behavior even when that control isn’t used. By mixing both, you get predictability and responsiveness in one design.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',1,1,'2025-06-24_22-59-41','The HAL is a layer of code that provides code for most of the hardware setup while providing an interface to interact with the hardware. Instead of writing low-level code for the microcontroller, we can use the HAL to access hardware features. It is used in general embedded projects since the user does not need specific hardware setups such as configuring timers and handling I/O operations, and the HAL setup is sufficient for the project\'s purpose, thus saving time and effort. The HAL also improves the code’s portability and maintainability since the same code can often be applied across different microcontrollers or hardware platforms with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',1,1,'2025-06-24_23-17-54','The HAL is a layer of code that provides code for most of the hardware setup while providing an interface to interact with the hardware. Instead of writing low-level code for the microcontroller, we can use the HAL to access hardware features. It is used in general embedded projects since the user does not need specific hardware setups such as configuring timers and handling I/O operations, and the HAL setup is sufficient for the project\'s purpose, thus saving time and effort. The HAL also improves the code’s portability and maintainability since the same code can often be applied across different microcontrollers or hardware platforms with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',1,2,'2025-06-24_22-59-41','The io_putchar function takes a character from the printf function and then transmits it through UART to be displayed on the console. This function is necessary since the printf function does not know how to communicate with hardware on its own despite being a part of the standard C library. In embedded systems, printf needs to be defined how it will output data by relating it to a hardware-specific function like __io_putchar. These functions allow us to take characters from printf to the desired peripheral output. If we wanted to display the characters to a USB interface or an LCD screen instead of UART, the __io_putchar function would need to be changed to match the desired hardware, showing how the __io_putchar function is hardware-dependent.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',1,2,'2025-06-24_23-17-54','The io_putchar function takes a character from the printf function and then transmits it through UART to be displayed on the console. This function is necessary since the printf function does not know how to communicate with hardware on its own despite being a part of the standard C library. In embedded systems, printf needs to be defined how it will output data by relating it to a hardware-specific function like __io_putchar. These functions allow us to take characters from printf to the desired peripheral output. If we wanted to display the characters to a USB interface or an LCD screen instead of UART, the __io_putchar function would need to be changed to match the desired hardware, showing how the __io_putchar function is hardware-dependent.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',1,3,'2025-06-24_22-59-41','The debugger is used in this lab since it gives us more control and insight into what the code is doing as it runs. It allows us to pause execution, inspect variables, and find errors in our code. Some info such as the actual values of the stack pointers cannot be observed using just printf. Printf does not give us live updates to the stack pointers without call the function multiple times. This makes using the debugger to access the MSP and PSP register values much more accurate and efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',1,3,'2025-06-24_23-17-54','The debugger is used in this lab since it gives us more control and insight into what the code is doing as it runs. It allows us to pause execution, inspect variables, and find errors in our code. Some info such as the actual values of the stack pointers cannot be observed using just printf. Printf does not give us live updates to the stack pointers without call the function multiple times. This makes using the debugger to access the MSP and PSP register values much more accurate and efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',1,4,'2025-06-24_22-59-41','The stack allocation method that we used works by treating the stack memory as a fixed-size region and then obtaining the MSP_INIT_VAL and PSP_VAL. The MSP_INIT_VAL tells use where the main stack starts in memory and it is important as it’s initial value ensures that the PSP points to a valid memory address allocated for stack usage. If the initial MSP value is unknown, the PSP could point to an invalid or reserved memory region, causing faults. Knowing the MSP value is also important to ensure that the PSP stack does not overlap with the main stack. We can determine the value of the PSP by subtracting a fixed amount of memory (0x400 bytes in our case) from the main stack’s initial address. We need to subtract the memory address because the stack grows downward in memory, which means moving down to a lower address.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',1,4,'2025-06-24_23-17-54','The stack allocation method that we used works by treating the stack memory as a fixed-size region and then obtaining the MSP_INIT_VAL and PSP_VAL. The MSP_INIT_VAL tells use where the main stack starts in memory and it is important as it’s initial value ensures that the PSP points to a valid memory address allocated for stack usage. If the initial MSP value is unknown, the PSP could point to an invalid or reserved memory region, causing faults. Knowing the MSP value is also important to ensure that the PSP stack does not overlap with the main stack. We can determine the value of the PSP by subtracting a fixed amount of memory (0x400 bytes in our case) from the main stack’s initial address. We need to subtract the memory address because the stack grows downward in memory, which means moving down to a lower address.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',1,5,'2025-06-24_22-59-41','In order to allocate multiple stacks for use in multiple threads using the method in this lab, we need to start with the initial MSP pointer value and divide that stack memory into fixed-sized blocks. Each thread stack has a defined size as well as the main stack. This method works because we can treat the stack as a large block of memory and assign each thread its own space above the main stack. The Cortex-M4 architecture requires the thread stack and main stack size to be a multiple of 8 to ensure 8-byte alignment in order for the CPU to not enter a fault. Additionally, we should also check that the total memory allocated for all the stacks does not exceed the max stack size available and it stays within a valid RAM memory region.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',1,5,'2025-06-24_23-17-54','In order to allocate multiple stacks for use in multiple threads using the method in this lab, we need to start with the initial MSP pointer value and divide that stack memory into fixed-sized blocks. Each thread stack has a defined size (THREAD_SIZE) as well as the main stack (MAIN_SIZE). The starting address for the thread stack, i, (where i starts at 1) can be calculated using the formula:\nADDRESS_STACK_i = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_SIZE + (i – 1) x THREAD_SIZE\nThis method works because we can treat the stack as a large block of memory and assign each thread its own space above the main stack. The Cortex-M4 architecture requires the thread stack and main stack size to be a multiple of 8 to ensure 8-byte alignment in order for the CPU to not enter a fault. Additionally, we should also check that the total memory allocated for all the stacks does not exceed the max stack size available and it stays within a valid RAM memory region.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,1,'2025-06-23_17-56-39','Changing the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function made it so that the processor would immediately start to execute my print continuously function. Since that function is designed to print “Hello, PC!” infinitely without returning anything, the program stayed inside the loop and effectively stopped the execution of any other part of the program. The only way to break out of the infinite loop would be to use some kind of interrupt or manual reset.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,1,'2025-06-24_18-07-15','Changing the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function made it so that the processor would immediately call the print continuously function. After the jump assembly function would move the function pointer address to the R0 register, and since the PC register tells what program to run next, the CPU would start to run the print continuously function, which is an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,1,'2025-06-25_00-08-03','Changing the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function made it so that the processor would immediately call the print continuously function. After the jump assembly function would move the function pointer address to the R0 register, and since the PC register tells what program to run next, the CPU would start to run the print continuously function, which is an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,1,'2025-06-25_00-29-12','Changing the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function made it so that the processor would immediately call the print continuously function. After the jump assembly function would move the function pointer address to the R0 register, and since the PC register tells what program to run next, the CPU would start to run the print continuously function, which is an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,1,'2025-06-25_01-14-39','Changing the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function made it so that the processor would immediately call the print continuously function. After the jump assembly function would move the function pointer address to the R0 register, and since the PC register tells what program to run next, the CPU would start to run the print continuously function, which is an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,1,'2025-06-26_00-16-56','Changing the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function made it so that the processor would immediately call the print continuously function. After the jump assembly function would move the function pointer address to the R0 register, and since the PC register tells what program to run next, the CPU would start to run the print continuously function, which is an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,2,'2025-06-23_17-56-39','A system call is a way for a program to request some service from the OS. It acts as the interface between user applications and the core functionality of the OS (kernel).\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',2,2,'2025-06-24_18-07-15','A system call is a way for a program to request some service from the OS, such as accessing files, performing I/O operations, and allocating memory. It acts as the interface between user applications and the core functionality of the OS. However, a system call differs from a function since it switches the CPU from user mode (less privileged) to kernel mode (more privileged) through a trap (software-driven interrupt) in order to execute privileged operations. They provide controlled access to both hardware and system resources to ensure the stability of the CPU. An example of a system call is write (), which programs use to request the OS to safely write to the disk. Without it, data corruption can occur.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',2,2,'2025-06-25_00-08-03','A system call is a way for a program to request some service from the OS, such as accessing files, performing I/O operations, and allocating memory. It acts as the interface between user applications and the core functionality of the OS. However, a system call differs from a function since it switches the CPU from user mode (less privileged) to kernel mode (more privileged) through a trap (software-driven interrupt) in order to execute privileged operations. They provide controlled access to both hardware and system resources to ensure the stability of the CPU. An example of a system call is write (), which programs use to request the OS to safely write to the disk. Without it, data corruption can occur.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',2,2,'2025-06-25_00-29-12','A system call is a way for a program to request some service from the OS, such as accessing files, performing I/O operations, and allocating memory. It acts as the interface between user applications and the core functionality of the OS. However, a system call differs from a function since it switches the CPU from user mode (less privileged) to kernel mode (more privileged) through a trap (software-driven interrupt) in order to execute privileged operations on behalf of the program. Like regular function calls, system calls have parameters, a defined interface, and return a result. They provide controlled access to both hardware and system resources to ensure the stability of the CPU. Once the trap is triggered, control is transferred to a predefined handler in the kernel, which then executes the requested operation. Once the request is finished, the CPU then returns to user mode and restores the previous user mode state. An example of a system call is write (), which programs use to request the OS to safely write to the disk. Without it, data corruption can occur.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',2,2,'2025-06-25_01-14-39','A system call is a special type of function call that allows a user program to request services from the operating system, such as file access, memory allocation, or performing I/O operations. Like a regular function call, it follows a defined interface, accepts parameters, and returns results. However, it differs in a fundamental way: a system call causes a controlled transition from user mode to kernel mode—a mechanism known as a trap or software interrupt. This transition is necessary because the operations requested often require privileged access to hardware or protected system resources that user-mode code cannot access directly.\nOnce the trap is executed, control is passed to a predefined system call handler in the kernel. The kernel performs the requested operation on behalf of the user program, after which it restores the previous user-mode context and returns control to the application. This ensures both security and stability, as it prevents user programs from interfering with critical system operations.\nFor example, the write() system call enables programs to request that the OS write data to a file or device. Without using a system call, direct hardware access could lead to system crashes or data corruption. Therefore, while a system call behaves like a function in terms of structure, its implementation involves mode switching and hardware protection mechanisms that set it apart from ordinary function calls.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,2,'2025-06-26_00-16-56','A system call is a special type of function call that allows a user program to request services from the operating system, such as file access, memory allocation, or performing I/O operations. Like a regular function call, it follows a defined interface, accepts parameters, and returns results. However, it differs in a fundamental way: a system call causes a controlled transition from user mode to kernel mode—a mechanism known as a trap or software interrupt. This transition is necessary because the operations requested often require privileged access to hardware or protected system resources that user-mode code cannot access directly.\nOnce the trap is executed, control is passed to a predefined system call handler in the kernel. The kernel performs the requested operation on behalf of the user program, after which it restores the previous user-mode context and returns control to the application. This ensures both security and stability, as it prevents user programs from interfering with critical system operations.\nFor example, the write() system call enables programs to request that the OS write data to a file or device. Without using a system call, direct hardware access could lead to system crashes or data corruption. Therefore, while a system call behaves like a function in terms of structure, its implementation involves mode switching and hardware protection mechanisms that set it apart from ordinary function calls.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,3,'2025-06-23_17-56-39','The thread’s stack was set up by placing initial register values into memory determined by the MSP. Things such as the PC, xPSR, LR, R12 and core registers (R0-R3) were set. Then the PSP was set to point to the top of the stack so that the thread has all the information of the stack during execution. To “set up a thread’s stack” means to preload the stack with the initial register values so that the CPU can expect when it begins to execute a thread and when the thread’s state is restored.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',2,3,'2025-06-24_18-07-15','The thread’s stack was set up by placing initial register values into memory determined by the MSP. Things such as the PC, xPSR, LR, R12 and core registers (R0-R3) were set. Then the PSP was set to point to the top of the stack so that the thread has all the information of the stack during execution. During execution, the stack will begin to “pop” these values off the stack. Storing initial values for the registers is important so that the CPU can save and restore a thread’s state during switching. If the register values were stored in the wrong order, the CPU would not save/restore the data in the correct register, which could lead to a crash or system failure. The order is crucial because it directly affects how the CPU restores the thread state to resume properly.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,3,'2025-06-25_00-08-03','The thread’s stack was set up by placing initial register values into memory determined by the MSP. Things such as the PC, xPSR, LR, R12 and core registers (R0-R3) were set. Then the PSP was set to point to the top of the stack so that the thread has all the information of the stack during execution. During execution, the stack will begin to “pop” these values off the stack. Storing initial values for the registers is important so that the CPU can save and restore a thread’s state during switching. If the register values were stored in the wrong order, the CPU would not save/restore the data in the correct register, which could lead to a crash or system failure. The order is crucial because it directly affects how the CPU restores the thread state to resume properly.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,3,'2025-06-25_00-29-12','The thread’s stack was set up by placing initial register values into memory determined by the MSP. Things such as the PC, xPSR, LR, R12 and core registers (R0-R3) were set. Then the PSP was set to point to the top of the stack so that the thread has all the information of the stack during execution. During execution, the stack will begin to “pop” these values off the stack. Storing initial values for the registers is important so that the CPU can save and restore a thread’s state during switching. If the register values were stored in the wrong order, the CPU would not save/restore the data in the correct register, which could lead to a crash or system failure. The order is crucial because it directly affects how the CPU restores the thread state to resume properly.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,3,'2025-06-25_01-14-39','The thread’s stack was set up by placing initial register values into memory determined by the MSP. Things such as the PC, xPSR, LR, R12 and core registers (R0-R3) were set. Then the PSP was set to point to the top of the stack so that the thread has all the information of the stack during execution. During execution, the stack will begin to “pop” these values off the stack. Storing initial values for the registers is important so that the CPU can save and restore a thread’s state during switching. If the register values were stored in the wrong order, the CPU would not save/restore the data in the correct register, which could lead to a crash or system failure. The order is crucial because it directly affects how the CPU restores the thread state to resume properly.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,3,'2025-06-26_00-16-56','The thread’s stack was set up by placing initial register values into memory determined by the MSP. Things such as the PC, xPSR, LR, R12 and core registers (R0-R3) were set. Then the PSP was set to point to the top of the stack so that the thread has all the information of the stack during execution. During execution, the stack will begin to “pop” these values off the stack. Storing initial values for the registers is important so that the CPU can save and restore a thread’s state during switching. If the register values were stored in the wrong order, the CPU would not save/restore the data in the correct register, which could lead to a crash or system failure. The order is crucial because it directly affects how the CPU restores the thread state to resume properly.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,4,'2025-06-23_17-56-39','If the PSP was not set before running the thread, the processor would not know where the thread’s stack is located. Then, if the thread tries to execute the program and use the PSP for pushing/popping registers, etc, it would try to access some invalid location in memory, causing a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,4,'2025-06-24_18-07-15','If the PSP was not set before running the thread, the processor would not know where the thread’s stack is located. Then, if the thread tries to execute the program and use the PSP for pushing/popping registers, etc, it would try to access some invalid and uninitialized location in memory. This leads to a hard fault, which is the processor’s way of catching a fatal error such as accessing uninitialized memory.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',2,4,'2025-06-25_00-08-03','If the PSP was not set before running the thread, the processor would not know where the thread’s stack is located. Then, if the thread tries to execute the program and use the PSP for pushing/popping registers, etc, it would try to access some invalid and uninitialized location in memory. This leads to a hard fault, which is the processor’s way of catching a fatal error such as accessing uninitialized memory.\nOn the Cortex-M4, the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is used by default and automatically during certain cases: reset (startup), interrupts and exceptions, privileged execution, etc. Meanwhile, threads or tasks are typically run in unprivileged mode and use the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This separation is by design to isolate application (thread) code from system-level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,4,'2025-06-25_00-29-12','If the PSP was not set before running the thread, the processor would not know where the thread’s stack is located. Then, if the thread tries to execute the program and use the PSP for pushing/popping registers, etc, it would try to access some invalid and uninitialized location in memory. This leads to a hard fault, which is the processor’s way of catching a fatal error such as accessing uninitialized memory.\nOn the Cortex-M4, the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is used by default and automatically during certain cases: reset (startup), interrupts and exceptions, privileged execution, etc. Meanwhile, threads or tasks are typically run in unprivileged mode and use the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This separation is by design to isolate application (thread) code from system-level operations.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,4,'2025-06-25_01-14-39','If the PSP was not set before running the thread, the processor would not know where the thread’s stack is located. Then, if the thread tries to execute the program and use the PSP for pushing/popping registers, etc, it would try to access some invalid and uninitialized location in memory. This leads to a hard fault, which is the processor’s way of catching a fatal error such as accessing uninitialized memory.\nOn the Cortex-M4, the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is used by default and automatically during certain cases: reset (startup), interrupts and exceptions, privileged execution, etc. Meanwhile, threads or tasks are typically run in unprivileged mode and use the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This separation is by design to isolate application (thread) code from system-level operations.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',2,4,'2025-06-26_00-16-56','If the PSP was not set before running the thread, the processor would not know where the thread’s stack is located. Then, if the thread tries to execute the program and use the PSP for pushing/popping registers, etc, it would try to access some invalid and uninitialized location in memory. This leads to a hard fault, which is the processor’s way of catching a fatal error such as accessing uninitialized memory.\nOn the Cortex-M4, the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is used by default and automatically during certain cases: reset (startup), interrupts and exceptions, privileged execution, etc. Meanwhile, threads or tasks are typically run in unprivileged mode and use the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). This separation is by design to isolate application (thread) code from system-level operations.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',2,5,'2025-06-23_17-56-39','If the thread function did not loop continuously, the system would likely crash or enter a hard fault due to there being no parent function to return to. Since the thread was created manually by setting up the stack and the PC, it was not created by some parent thread.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',2,5,'2025-06-24_18-07-15','If the thread function did not loop continuously, the system would likely crash or enter a hard fault due to there being no parent function to return to. Since the thread was created manually by setting up the stack and the PC, it was not created by some parent thread. Additionally, if the thread returned, the CPU will attempt to continue through the stack and try to execute whatever is stored in the LR register. But since the LR was manually set as a dummy value during setup, the CPU would again most likely move to an invalid memory location. This also results in a hard fault since the processor cannot execute any instructions from that invalid memory.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',2,5,'2025-06-25_00-08-03','If the thread function did not loop continuously, the system would likely crash or enter a hard fault due to there being no parent function to return to. Since the thread was created manually by setting up the stack and the PC, it was not created by some parent thread. As a thread ends, its return value needs to be manually set by the user back to the stack by using the PSP. Additionally, if the thread is returned, the CPU will attempt to continue through the stack and execute whatever is stored in the LR register. But since the LR was manually set as a dummy value during setup, the CPU would again most likely move to an invalid memory location. This also results in a hard fault since the processor cannot execute any instructions from that invalid memory.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,5,'2025-06-25_00-29-12','If the thread function did not loop continuously, the system would likely crash or enter a hard fault due to there being no parent function to return to. Since the thread was created manually by setting up the stack and the PC, it was not created by some parent thread. As a thread ends, its return value needs to be manually set by the user back to the stack by using the PSP. Additionally, if the thread is returned, the CPU will attempt to continue through the stack and execute whatever is stored in the LR register. But since the LR was manually set as a dummy value during setup, the CPU would again most likely move to an invalid memory location. This also results in a hard fault since the processor cannot execute any instructions from that invalid memory.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,5,'2025-06-25_01-14-39','If the thread function did not loop continuously, the system would likely crash or enter a hard fault due to there being no parent function to return to. Since the thread was created manually by setting up the stack and the PC, it was not created by some parent thread. As a thread ends, its return value needs to be manually set by the user back to the stack by using the PSP. Additionally, if the thread is returned, the CPU will attempt to continue through the stack and execute whatever is stored in the LR register. But since the LR was manually set as a dummy value during setup, the CPU would again most likely move to an invalid memory location. This also results in a hard fault since the processor cannot execute any instructions from that invalid memory.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',2,5,'2025-06-26_00-16-56','If the thread function did not loop continuously, the system would likely crash or enter a hard fault due to there being no parent function to return to. Since the thread was created manually by setting up the stack and the PC, it was not created by some parent thread. As a thread ends, its return value needs to be manually set by the user back to the stack by using the PSP. Additionally, if the thread is returned, the CPU will attempt to continue through the stack and execute whatever is stored in the LR register. But since the LR was manually set as a dummy value during setup, the CPU would again most likely move to an invalid memory location. This also results in a hard fault since the processor cannot execute any instructions from that invalid memory.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',3,1,'2025-06-26_00-22-07','The process of compiling multiple *.c files into a single executable can be broken down into 3 different processes: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, in the preprocessing phase, each C file is preprocessed where directives like #define and #include are expanded, as well as comments are removed. This results in a pure C source file with all macros and headers inlined. In the compiling phase, each .c file is converted into machine-level object files where errors and syntax is checked. Optimization is also performed in this stage. Finally, the linking phase then combines all .o files into a single executable. It does this by connecting all function calls and variable references across files as well as linking standard libraries like printf() from the C standard library.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',3,2,'2025-06-26_00-22-07','The #include directive is meant for header files instead of sources files (*.c files). The line #include <kernel.c> is telling the compiler to copy and paste the entire content of the kernel.c file into another source file before compilation. Do so can lead to problems such as double compilation where the same set of code might get compiled twice, leading to duplicate symbols during linking. This also breaks modularity and builds system confusion.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',3,3,'2025-06-26_00-22-07','Initialize the kernel – call a function to initialize the kernel which will prepare the internal data structure and sets up the necessary memory stack so threads can be created\nDefine the thread function – create a function that contains the code that you want the thread to run. The function should take a single void function as an argument and have no return.\nProvide the thread function’s address and create the thread – calling a thread creation function and passing it the address to the thread function will create the thread. This allows the kernel to know what code the new thread should execute while also setting up the thread’s stack and initial values to prepare it for execution.\nStarting the kernel – by calling the function that starts the kernel, a system call will be triggered which loads the thread’s stack pointer and jumps to the thread’s execution.\nThread should now be running independently.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',3,4,'2025-06-26_00-22-07','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory in the CPU, and it is used to provide space for the individual stacks from each thread that the OS manages. The purpose of the stack pool is to provide thread isolation where each thread will need its own stack to store local variables, save CPU registers and function return addresses. It is also used for memory management as it allows the OS to allocate a portion of memory to each thread’s stack in a controlled and organized manner.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',3,5,'2025-06-26_00-22-07','The kernel code is typically found in the kernel.c and kernel.h files and it contains the low-level functionality that makes the OS work. This includes thread creation, stack setup, iterrupts and system call handling. This code also provides a set of service and abstraction that the user code can rely on.\nThe user code is usually found in the main.c file and contains the application logic that the developer wants to run using the OS. This includes defining thread functions and calling kernel-provided APIs.\nThe distinction between the user and kernel files mainly lies in their roles and responsibilities and it is important because it provides a clear and simplified interface for interacting with the system. The user does not need ot understand or modify the kernel code; it just needs to simply rely on the kernel to manage the hardware and low-level functionality safely and correctly. This distinction improves modularity, security, and ease of development.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',4,1,'2025-07-31_16-30-09','Using a queue in round-robin scheduling to store TCBs provides an efficient structure for implementation. The currently running thread is moved to the back of the queue after its time slice, and the next thread is selected to run, which is very similar to the first-in, first-out structure of a queue. This means that a queue eliminates the need to manually manage array indices or rotate elements, reducing the likelihood of errors and improving efficiency. Furthermore, queues make it easier to add or remove threads dynamically, offering more flexibility and scalability. They provide a cleaner and more maintainable scheduling for dynamic kernel designs, while arrays may be sufficient for systems with a fixed number of threads and memory constraints.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',4,1,'2025-07-31_17-36-40','Using a queue in round-robin scheduling to store TCBs provides an efficient structure for implementation. The currently running thread is moved to the back of the queue after its time slice, and the next thread is selected to run, which is very similar to the first-in, first-out structure of a queue. That said, a queue does offer advantages in more dynamic systems. It simplifies the management of TCBs when threads need to be added or removed during runtime, which is less straightforward with a statically sized array. This makes queues more suitable for scalable or modular kernel designs. In contrast, arrays might still be the better choice for simpler systems with a fixed number of threads and strict memory constraints, where dynamic thread creation is not required. They provide a cleaner and more maintainable scheduling for dynamic kernel designs, while arrays may be sufficient for systems with a fixed number of threads and memory constraints. If the existing scheduler already uses an array and a rotating index to cycle through threads in a fixed order, it effectively achieves the same round-robin behaviour as a queue. In such a case, replacing the array with an actual queue may not yield significant functional improvements and could introduce additional overhead in terms of memory allocation and pointer management, especially in resource-constrained embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',4,1,'2025-07-31_17-45-35','Using a queue in round-robin scheduling to store TCBs provides an efficient structure for implementation. The currently running thread is moved to the back of the queue after its time slice, and the next thread is selected to run, which is very similar to the first-in, first-out structure of a queue. That said, a queue does offer advantages in more dynamic systems. It simplifies the management of TCBs when threads need to be added or removed during runtime, which is less straightforward with a statically sized array. This makes queues more suitable for scalable or modular kernel designs. In contrast, arrays might still be the better choice for simpler systems with a fixed number of threads and strict memory constraints, where dynamic thread creation is not required. They provide a cleaner and more maintainable scheduling for dynamic kernel designs, while arrays may be sufficient for systems with a fixed number of threads and memory constraints. If the existing scheduler already uses an array and a rotating index to cycle through threads in a fixed order, it effectively achieves the same round-robin behaviour as a queue. In such a case, replacing the array with an actual queue may not yield significant functional improvements and could introduce additional overhead in terms of memory allocation and pointer management, especially in resource-constrained embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',4,2,'2025-07-31_16-30-09','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for performing a context switch reliably on ARM Cortex-M processors. When a context switch is needed, such as during an SVC, it cannot be safely done immediately because we are still stuck inside an interrupt (SVC_Handler_Main) which may still have local variables on the stack. If the context switch were done, the local variables on the stack would never get cleaned up, causing the stack to grow uncontrollably. Thus, the PendSV function is used as a special interrupt designed for context switching. We set the PendSV interrupt as pending inside SVC_Handler_Main, and we do not trigger it right away. Instead, we let SVC_Handler_Main finish its process, which allows the processor to clean the stack properly, and when the SVC handler is done, the CPU sees that PendSV is still pending, thus executing PendSV_Handler. Since PendSV is an interrupt, it runs in a context where we are allowed to modify key registers and switch stack pointers without interference, allowing us to save the state of the current thread and restore the state of the next thread without risking stack corruption or violating processor rules.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',4,2,'2025-07-31_17-36-40','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for performing a context switch reliably on ARM Cortex-M processors. When a context switch is needed, such as during an SVC, it cannot be safely done immediately because we are still stuck inside an interrupt (SVC_Handler_Main) which may still have local variables on the stack. If the context switch were done, the local variables on the stack would never get cleaned up, causing the stack to grow uncontrollably. Thus, the PendSV function is used as a special interrupt designed for context switching. We set the PendSV interrupt as pending inside SVC_Handler_Main, and we do not trigger it right away. Instead, we let SVC_Handler_Main finish its process, which allows the processor to clean the stack properly, and when the SVC handler is done, the CPU sees that PendSV is still pending, thus executing PendSV_Handler. Since PendSV is an interrupt, it runs in a context where we are allowed to modify key registers and switch stack pointers without interference, allowing us to save the state of the current thread and restore the state of the next thread without risking stack corruption or violating processor rules. nce PendSV_Handler is triggered, the context switch begins. The first step is to read the current thread’s stack pointer by accessing the PSP (Process Stack Pointer), which points to the active thread\'s stack. The PendSV_Handler then saves the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) to this stack. These registers are not automatically saved during exception entry, so saving them manually is essential to preserve the thread’s execution state. Once the next thread has been selected, its stack pointer is loaded from the corresponding TCB, and the R4–R11 registers for that thread are restored from its stack. Finally, the updated PSP is written back so that when the exception returns, the processor resumes execution in the newly selected thread, with all registers and stack state restored as if it had never been interrupted.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',4,2,'2025-07-31_17-45-35','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for safely performing a context switch on ARM Cortex-M processors. When a context switch is requested—such as during an SVC—it cannot be performed immediately within SVC_Handler_Main because the handler is still using its own stack frame, and switching at that point could lead to stack corruption or memory leaks. Instead, the PendSV interrupt is set to pending inside SVC_Handler_Main, and the handler is allowed to return normally, ensuring its stack is properly cleaned up. Once the PendSV interrupt is triggered, PendSV_Handler reads the current thread’s stack pointer from the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) and saves the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) to that stack. Then, the osSched() function is called to select the next thread by updating the global current_tcb pointer. The new thread’s stack pointer is loaded from its TCB, its R4–R11 registers are restored from its stack, and the updated PSP is written back to the processor. When the interrupt returns, execution resumes in the new thread with its full context restored, enabling safe and efficient multitasking.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',4,3,'2025-07-31_16-30-09','If the OS scheduler is modified to always choose the task with the highest priority, starvation will become an issue. If one or more tasks have the highest or higher priority compared to the rest, they will constantly be chosen to run, and lower priority tasks may never get any CPU time. This could mean that important background or system tasks (ie. handling input, updating displays, managing time, etc.) could be indefinitely delayed or completely blocked. Without a mechanism like priority aging or time slicing, the lower priority tasks are effectively ignored, thus making the system unstable or unresponsive. In systems where responsiveness and priority fairness are critical, this type of scheduler is not desirable.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',4,3,'2025-07-31_17-36-40','If the OS scheduler is modified to always choose the task with the highest priority, starvation will become an issue. This deterministic behaviour means that once a high-priority task becomes ready, it will always be selected to run, and all lower-priority tasks will be completely ignored, regardless of how long they’ve been waiting. As a result, starvation becomes a significant issue. Lower-priority tasks may never get CPU time, even if they are essential for system operation—such as handling inputs, updating displays, or performing background maintenance. These tasks could be indefinitely delayed or never executed at all, leading to an unresponsive or unstable system. Without a mechanism like priority aging or time slicing, the lower priority tasks are effectively ignored, thus making the system unstable or unresponsive. In systems where responsiveness and priority fairness are critical, this type of scheduler is not desirable.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',4,3,'2025-07-31_17-45-35','If the OS scheduler is modified to always choose the task with the highest priority, starvation will become an issue. This deterministic behaviour means that once a high-priority task becomes ready, it will always be selected to run, and all lower-priority tasks will be completely ignored, regardless of how long they’ve been waiting. As a result, starvation becomes a significant issue. Lower-priority tasks may never get CPU time, even if they are essential for system operation—such as handling inputs, updating displays, or performing background maintenance. These tasks could be indefinitely delayed or never executed at all, leading to an unresponsive or unstable system. Without a mechanism like priority aging or time slicing, the lower priority tasks are effectively ignored, thus making the system unstable or unresponsive. In systems where responsiveness and priority fairness are critical, this type of scheduler is not desirable.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',4,4,'2025-07-31_16-30-09','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_Handler_Main instead of writing everything in assembly make the code much easier to read, understand and maintain. High-level C code is much more accessible to a broader range of developers which speeds up development and simplifies debugging. It also improves portability since C code can be compiled for different architectures with minimal changes, whereas assembly is specific to the processor it is running on. Additionally, C is faster and less error-prone compared to assembly, which is more verbose and lower-level.\nIn contrast, calling C functions from handlers may introduce some drawbacks. There is added performance overhead, especially in critical sections like context switching, where every cycle counts. C functions may use the stack inefficiently or push unnecessary registers, leading to slower execution. Additionally, C does not provide the low-level control required for certain operations such as precise register and stack manipulation, which are often necessary in OS internals. Furthermore, relying on a compiler to translate C code efficiently can sometimes produce suboptimal machine code, whereas hand-written assembly gives the programmer full control over performance and behaviour.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',4,4,'2025-07-31_17-36-40','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_Handler_Main instead of writing everything in assembly make the code much easier to read, understand and maintain. High-level C code is much more accessible to a broader range of developers which speeds up development and simplifies debugging. It also improves portability since C code can be compiled for different architectures with minimal changes, whereas assembly is specific to the processor it is running on. Additionally, C is faster and less error-prone compared to assembly, which is more verbose and lower-level.\nIn contrast, calling C functions from handlers may introduce some drawbacks. There is added performance overhead, especially in critical sections like context switching, where every cycle counts. C functions may use the stack inefficiently or push unnecessary registers, leading to slower execution. Additionally, C does not provide the low-level control required for certain operations such as precise register and stack manipulation, which are often necessary in OS internals. Furthermore, relying on a compiler to translate C code efficiently can sometimes produce suboptimal machine code, whereas hand-written assembly gives the programmer full control over performance and behaviour.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',4,4,'2025-07-31_17-45-35','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_Handler_Main instead of writing everything in assembly make the code much easier to read, understand and maintain. High-level C code is much more accessible to a broader range of developers which speeds up development and simplifies debugging. It also improves portability since C code can be compiled for different architectures with minimal changes, whereas assembly is specific to the processor it is running on. Additionally, C is faster and less error-prone compared to assembly, which is more verbose and lower-level.\nIn contrast, calling C functions from handlers may introduce some drawbacks. There is added performance overhead, especially in critical sections like context switching, where every cycle counts. C functions may use the stack inefficiently or push unnecessary registers, leading to slower execution. Additionally, C does not provide the low-level control required for certain operations such as precise register and stack manipulation, which are often necessary in OS internals. Furthermore, relying on a compiler to translate C code efficiently can sometimes produce suboptimal machine code, whereas hand-written assembly gives the programmer full control over performance and behaviour.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',4,5,'2025-07-31_16-30-09','If only a single thread was created, such as the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, the OS will technically still “work” in the sense that it will boot, initialize, and run he thread. However, once that thread yields, the scheduler will have no other threads to switch to. Since there are no additional threads in the ready queue, the scheduler will likely return control to the same thread again, assuming the OS is designed to handle this case. This setup reveals limitations in the scheduler design, and a well-designed scheduler should detect that only one thread exists and continue running it even after it yields. If the OS does not account for this case, it might get stuck in an idle loop, or worse, crash because it assumes there will be another thread to switch to. The OS is not doing “multi-threading” anymore, it is just running a single thread with extra overhead, thus the OS will lose the benefits of multi-threading and some parts of the kernel, like context switching, may not be exercised fully, making it harder to test or debug.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',4,5,'2025-07-31_17-36-40','If only a single thread was created, such as the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, the OS will technically still “work” in the sense that it will boot, initialize, and run he thread. However, once that thread yields, the scheduler will have no other threads to switch to. Since there are no additional threads in the ready queue, the scheduler will likely return control to the same thread again, assuming the OS is designed to handle this case. This setup reveals limitations in the scheduler design, and a well-designed scheduler should detect that only one thread exists and continue running it even after it yields. If the OS does not account for this case, it might get stuck in an idle loop, or worse, crash because it assumes there will be another thread to switch to. The OS is not doing “multi-threading” anymore, it is just running a single thread with extra overhead, thus the OS will lose the benefits of multi-threading and some parts of the kernel, like context switching, may not be exercised fully, making it harder to test or debug.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',4,5,'2025-07-31_17-45-35','If only a single thread was created, such as the print_continuously thread that yields after printing, the OS will technically still “work” in the sense that it will boot, initialize, and run he thread. However, once that thread yields, the scheduler will have no other threads to switch to. Since there are no additional threads in the ready queue, the scheduler will likely return control to the same thread again, assuming the OS is designed to handle this case. This setup reveals limitations in the scheduler design, and a well-designed scheduler should detect that only one thread exists and continue running it even after it yields. If the OS does not account for this case, it might get stuck in an idle loop, or worse, crash because it assumes there will be another thread to switch to. The OS is not doing “multi-threading” anymore, it is just running a single thread with extra overhead, thus the OS will lose the benefits of multi-threading and some parts of the kernel, like context switching, may not be exercised fully, making it harder to test or debug.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',5,1,'2025-07-31_17-57-31','In order to pass an argument into a thread, the user writes a thread function that accepts a void *arg parameter, prepares the argument, and calls the thread creation function with the function pointer and argument. The OS or kernel then creates a TCB, allocates a stack, and sets up the initial CPU context so that the PC points to the thread function and the register R0 holds the argument pointer. Once the thread starts running, the function receives the argument as if it were called normally in C. This mechanism works because the OS manually sets up the context to match what the CPU expects when calling a function, allowing seamless argument passing to newly created threads.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',5,1,'2025-07-31_18-10-13','In order to pass an argument into a thread, the user writes a thread function that accepts a void *arg parameter, prepares the argument, and calls the thread creation function with the function pointer and argument. The OS or kernel then creates a TCB, allocates a stack, and sets up the initial CPU context so that the PC points to the thread function and the register R0 holds the argument pointer. Once the thread starts running, the function receives the argument as if it were called normally in C. This mechanism works because the OS manually sets up the context to match what the CPU expects when calling a function, allowing seamless argument passing to newly created threads. This setup includes initializing the PC to point to the thread function and placing the argument point in the correct register according to the ABI. The ABI defines the conventions for how functions receive their arguments—such as which CPU registers or stack locations hold the first, second, and subsequent arguments. The OS uses these ABI rules to correctly place the argument pointer (e.g., in register R0 on ARM architectures) when it initializes the thread’s context. By doing this, when the thread starts executing, the CPU’s state matches the ABI’s expectations, making the thread function receive its argument just like a normal function call in C. When the OS creates a thread, it sets up the thread’s stack frame so that the saved context will load R0 with the argument pointer before jumping to the thread function. This ensures that when the CPU begins executing the thread function, the argument is already in R0, perfectly matching the ABI\'s calling convention and allowing seamless argument passing.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',5,2,'2025-07-31_17-57-31','You cannot call osYield() inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because osYield() triggers a context switch by setting the PendSV interrupt as pending, which only works correctly when called from thread mode. Calling it from within an interrupt (handler mode) causes the system to request a new context switch while one is already in progress, leading to redundant or recursive PendSV triggers. This can result in unpredictable behavior, such as infinite context switch loops, stack corruption, or system crashes. It violates the kernel’s scheduling flow, which assumes that context switches are requested from thread mode and executed only once in PendSV_Handler, making such usage unsafe and fundamentally incorrect.\n',0,'s25'),('l28tang',5,2,'2025-07-31_18-10-13','You cannot call osYield() inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because osYield() triggers a context switch by setting the PendSV interrupt as pending, which only works correctly when called from thread mode. Calling it from within an interrupt (handler mode) causes the system to request a new context switch while one is already in progress, leading to redundant or recursive PendSV triggers. However, calling osYield() from within an interrupt handler—such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler—violates Cortex-M4 interrupt nesting rules. Inside an interrupt (handler) mode, interrupts can be nested only according to their priority levels, and PendSV is meant to run when no other interrupt is active. Setting PendSV pending from within an interrupt causes the hardware to queue a PendSV exception while the processor is still handling the current interrupt. This can lead to interrupt nesting conflicts, where PendSV preempts or is re-triggered before the previous context switch completes.The direct consequence of this misuse can be severe: it may cause stack corruption due to repeated saving and restoring of CPU context in an unexpected manner, or even an infinite context switch loop if PendSV continually triggers itself. Moreover, it can cause a HardFault or BusFault when the processor attempts to execute invalid or corrupted instructions or access corrupted memory, because the processor state becomes inconsistent. This happens because the kernel\'s scheduling logic assumes context switches are requested only from thread mode and handled once per PendSV invocation. Calling osYield() inside an interrupt breaks this assumption, making the system unstable and prone to faults.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',5,3,'2025-07-31_17-57-31','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms instead of 1ms increases the timer resolution, allowing the system to respond more quickly to events and enabling finer-grained timing and more precise scheduling of tasks, which can improve real-time responsiveness and reduce latency for time-critical operations. However, this higher resolution comes with downsides: the timer interrupt will occur ten times more frequently, increasing CPU overhead and power consumption due to more frequent context switches and interrupt handling. This can reduce overall system efficiency, especially if many interrupts happen but the workload per interrupt remains low. Additionally, the increased interrupt rate may complicate timing calculations and require more careful handling of timing-related software to avoid jitter or missed deadlines. Thus, while a 0.1ms tick offers better timing precision, it must be balanced against the greater processing cost and potential impact on system performance.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',5,3,'2025-07-31_18-10-13','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms instead of 1ms increases the timer resolution, allowing the system to respond more quickly to events and enabling finer-grained timing and more precise scheduling of tasks, which can improve real-time responsiveness and reduce latency for time-critical operations. However, this higher resolution comes with downsides: the timer interrupt will occur ten times more frequently, increasing CPU overhead and power consumption due to more frequent context switches and interrupt handling. This can reduce overall system efficiency, especially if many interrupts happen but the workload per interrupt remains low. Additionally, the increased interrupt rate may complicate timing calculations and require more careful handling of timing-related software to avoid jitter or missed deadlines. Thus, while a 0.1ms tick offers better timing precision, it must be balanced against the greater processing cost and potential impact on system performance.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',5,4,'2025-07-31_17-57-31','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking means that each running task voluntarily yields control back to the OS, so a misbehaving or long-running task can block others by not yielding, potentially causing the system to become unresponsive. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking allows the OS to interrupt and switch tasks at any time, ensuring that all tasks get CPU time fairly regardless of their behavior, which generally results in smoother and more responsive multitasking.\nFrom the OS perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to implement because the OS relies on tasks to explicitly call yield functions to perform context switches; it doesn’t need to forcibly interrupt tasks, so the scheduler runs only when tasks allow. On the other hand, pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to use hardware timer interrupts or other mechanisms to forcibly pause running tasks, save their state, and switch context automatically. This means the OS must handle more complex interrupt-driven context switching and ensure safe saving/restoring of all task states without relying on task cooperation.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',5,4,'2025-07-31_18-10-13','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking means that each running task voluntarily yields control back to the OS, so a misbehaving or long-running task can block others by not yielding, potentially causing the system to become unresponsive. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking allows the OS to interrupt and switch tasks at any time, ensuring that all tasks get CPU time fairly regardless of their behavior, which generally results in smoother and more responsive multitasking.\nFrom the OS perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to implement because the OS relies on tasks to explicitly call yield functions to perform context switches; it doesn’t need to forcibly interrupt tasks, so the scheduler runs only when tasks allow. On the other hand, pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to use hardware timer interrupts or other mechanisms to forcibly pause running tasks, save their state, and switch context automatically. This means the OS must handle more complex interrupt-driven context switching and ensure safe saving/restoring of all task states without relying on task cooperation.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',5,5,'2025-07-31_17-57-31','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they complement each other: yielding allows tasks to voluntarily give up the CPU when they finish or reach a logical pause point, while the timer-based pre-emptive mechanism ensures that no task can hog the CPU indefinitely. When a task calls osYield(), it explicitly signals the scheduler to switch to another task, enabling cooperative multitasking. Meanwhile, the timer interrupt triggers a PendSV exception at regular intervals, forcing the OS to pre-empt the running task and switch context if its time slice expires. By combining these, the system benefits from efficient voluntary context switches while still maintaining fairness and responsiveness through forced, time-driven pre-emption—so even if a task never yields, the timer ensures other tasks still get CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('l28tang',5,5,'2025-07-31_18-10-13','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they complement each other: yielding allows tasks to voluntarily give up the CPU when they finish or reach a logical pause point, while the timer-based pre-emptive mechanism ensures that no task can hog the CPU indefinitely. When a task calls osYield(), it explicitly signals the scheduler to switch to another task, enabling cooperative multitasking. Meanwhile, the timer interrupt triggers a PendSV exception at regular intervals, forcing the OS to pre-empt the running task and switch context if its time slice expires. By combining these, the system benefits from efficient voluntary context switches while still maintaining fairness and responsiveness through forced, time-driven pre-emption—so even if a task never yields, the timer ensures other tasks still get CPU time.\n',0,'s25'),('ldachner',1,1,'2025-06-27_00-05-14','The HAL is a collection of software routines that acts as a bridge between application code and the physical hardware of the device. The HAL’s provided low level standardized functions hide the complexity of having to writer low level code, making it easier and quicker to develop low level applications. It also makes it possible to move your code to another microcontroller that also supports the same HAL.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',1,2,'2025-06-27_00-05-14','The __io_putchar function is used to communicate with a specific hardware output, allowing text to be displayed on a serial console. It is necessary in addition to just printf in order to print a single character (in our case ‘m’) as printf doesn’t know how to communicate with the hardware. By linking it to a hardware specific function like __io_putchar we can match each printf character to the desired peripheral.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',1,3,'2025-06-27_00-05-14','We needed the debugger to see intermediate values such as the values of our stack pointers which we wouldn’t necessarily be able to observe with printf. printf also cannot show what is happening within the processor while it runs. We can also view registers such as MSP and PSP using the debugger.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',1,4,'2025-06-27_00-05-14','The stack allocation method we used treats the stack as a fixed block of memory. We start by the initial value of the main stakc pointer (MSP_INIT_VAL) which then tells us where the main stack memory begins.\nAfter finding this starting point, we can set the process stack pointer (PSP) to a safe valid address that is reserved for stack use. If the MSP’s initial location is not known, the PSP could possibly point to a restricted or overlapping area of memory, which could lead to crashes. In our method, we subtracted 0x400 bytes from the main stacks starting address and then allocated space for a new stack by moving to lower memory addresses (since stack grows downwards). This process keeps the main stack and process stack from overlapping.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',1,5,'2025-06-27_00-05-14','To allocate multiple stacks at compile-time for use with multiple threads, you would first define a constant THREAD_STACK_SIZE representing the size of each stack and a NUM_THREADS constant for the total number of threads you plan to support. Using the initial main stack pointer value (MSP_INIT_VAL) as the starting point, you would then calculate the starting address of each stack by subtracting THREAD_STACK_SIZE multiplied by the thread’s index from MSP_INIT_VAL. This creates an array of stack pointers, with each entry corresponding to a unique, non-overlapping memory region allocated for a thread’s stack. For example, the starting address of stack j would be calculated as MSP_INIT_VAL - (THREAD_STACK_SIZE * j), ensuring each thread’s stack is allocated sequentially in memory, growing downward, without any overlap. This approach relies solely on pointer arithmetic at compile-time and guarantees each stack has its own dedicated space within the available RAM, as long as the total stack memory fits within the system’s memory constraints.\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('ldachner',2,1,'2025-06-26_23-36-48','When I changed the program counter (PC) to hold the address of my print_continuously function in Lab 2, I was essentially forcing the microcontroller to jump directly into that function by manually setting where the CPU should execute next. By moving the function pointer into PC, I bypassed the usual call and return mechanisms that C uses, and the processor immediately began running print_continuously as if it had always been there. As a result, the microcontroller entered an infinite loop printing “Hello, PC!” over and over. This demonstrated that by directly manipulating PC, I could take full control over the CPU’s execution flow, which is the basic idea behind how context switching works in an operating system, switching from one task’s context to another by changing the register values, especially PC.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',2,1,'2025-06-27_10-34-15','When I changed the program counter (PC) to hold the address of my print_continuously function in Lab 2, I was essentially forcing the microcontroller to jump directly into that function by manually setting where the CPU should execute next. By moving the function pointer into PC, I bypassed the usual call and return mechanisms that C uses, and the processor immediately began running print_continuously as if it had always been there. As a result, the microcontroller entered an infinite loop printing “Hello, PC!” over and over. This demonstrated that by directly manipulating PC, I could take full control over the CPU’s execution flow, which is the basic idea behind how context switching works in an operating system, switching from one task’s context to another by changing the register values, especially PC.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',2,2,'2025-06-26_23-36-48','A system call is a way for a running program to ask the operating system to do something it can’t do by itself, like interacting with hardware, managing memory, or performing privileged operations. It’s like a controlled door between user code and the OS: when the program makes a system call, it uses a special instruction (like SVC on ARM) that switches the CPU to a more privileged mode, allowing the OS to safely execute the requested operation. This keeps programs from directly messing with sensitive parts of the system while still giving them a way to request services they need.\n',0,'s25'),('ldachner',2,2,'2025-06-27_10-34-15','A system call is a special kind of function call that a program uses to request a service from the operating system’s kernel. Programs use system calls to perform tasks they can’t do directly, like accessing hardware, reading or writing files, or managing memory. When a program makes a system call, it calls a predefined function provided by the OS. This triggers a special CPU instruction (for example, SVC on ARM) that switches the processor from user mode to a privileged mode, allowing the kernel to safely execute the requested operation. This controlled interface prevents programs from directly interfering with critical system resources while still giving them access to essential services.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',2,3,'2025-06-26_23-36-48','Setting up the thread’s stack meant preparing a chunk of memory so the CPU could start running the new thread as if it had been interrupted and was now resuming. We did this by manually pushing the values of key CPU registers—like xPSR, PC, LR, and general-purpose registers—onto the thread’s stack in the exact order the CPU expects when exiting an interrupt. By placing the thread’s function address into the PC slot on the stack, we told the processor where to start executing when it switched to this new thread. Setting up a thread’s stack like this is crucial because it gives the CPU the complete context it needs: when the CPU pops these values back into the registers, it restores the thread’s environment so it can start running normally. Without saving and restoring these register values, the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread left off or how to resume it correctly, which is essential for multitasking systems where the CPU switches between threads.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',2,3,'2025-06-27_10-34-15','Setting up the thread’s stack meant preparing a chunk of memory so the CPU could start running the new thread as if it had been interrupted and was now resuming. We did this by manually pushing the values of key CPU registers—like xPSR, PC, LR, and general-purpose registers—onto the thread’s stack in the exact order the CPU expects when exiting an interrupt. By placing the thread’s function address into the PC slot on the stack, we told the processor where to start executing when it switched to this new thread. Setting up a thread’s stack like this is crucial because it gives the CPU the complete context it needs: when the CPU pops these values back into the registers, it restores the thread’s environment so it can start running normally. Without saving and restoring these register values, the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread left off or how to resume it correctly, which is essential for multitasking systems where the CPU switches between threads.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',2,4,'2025-06-26_23-36-48','If we didn’t set the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack before running the thread, the CPU would keep using whatever stack pointer was active before the system call—usually the main stack pointer (MSP), which is already in use by the main program. When we tried to return from the interrupt to start the thread, the CPU would pop register values from the wrong place in memory, since it would be using MSP instead of the thread’s prepared stack. This would cause it to load garbage data into critical registers like PC and xPSR, leading the CPU to jump to an invalid address or execute invalid instructions. As a result, the microcontroller would crash immediately with a hard fault. Setting PSP to the thread’s stack ensures the CPU pops register values from the correct, preconfigured memory region, restoring the new thread’s context safely and allowing it to start running properly.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',2,4,'2025-06-27_10-34-15','If we didn’t set the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack before running the thread, the CPU would keep using whatever stack pointer was active before the system call—usually the main stack pointer (MSP), which is already in use by the main program. When we tried to return from the interrupt to start the thread, the CPU would pop register values from the wrong place in memory, since it would be using MSP instead of the thread’s prepared stack. This would cause it to load garbage data into critical registers like PC and xPSR, leading the CPU to jump to an invalid address or execute invalid instructions. As a result, the microcontroller would crash immediately with a hard fault. Setting PSP to the thread’s stack ensures the CPU pops register values from the correct, preconfigured memory region, restoring the new thread’s context safely and allowing it to start running properly.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',2,5,'2025-06-26_23-36-48','If the thread function only printed something once and then returned, the CPU would try to pop the return address from the stack to figure out where to go next. But in our setup, the fake stack we built for the thread didn’t include a valid return address—because we assumed the thread would never exit. So when the thread’s function finished and tried to execute the BX LR or similar return instruction, it would jump to whatever random or uninitialized value was in LR, which almost certainly points to an invalid memory address. This would cause the CPU to start executing garbage instructions or jump to a restricted area, leading to an immediate crash with a hard fault. Essentially, because our fake thread stack wasn’t prepared to handle the function returning, letting it return would break the program. That’s why real operating systems either never let threads return or have a cleanup mechanism in place.\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('ldachner',2,5,'2025-06-27_10-34-15','If the thread function only printed something once and then returned, the CPU would try to pop the return address from the stack to figure out where to go next. But in our setup, the fake stack we built for the thread didn’t include a valid return address—because we assumed the thread would never exit. So when the thread’s function finished and tried to execute the BX LR or similar return instruction, it would jump to whatever random or uninitialized value was in LR, which almost certainly points to an invalid memory address. This would cause the CPU to start executing garbage instructions or jump to a restricted area, leading to an immediate crash with a hard fault. Essentially, because our fake thread stack wasn’t prepared to handle the function returning, letting it return would break the program. That’s why real operating systems either never let threads return or have a cleanup mechanism in place.\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('ldachner',3,1,'2025-06-26_23-21-14','When building a program from multiple .c files, each source file is first compiled separately into an object file (.o), which contains machine code for the functions and data defined in that file. These object files are then passed to the linker, which combines them into a single executable by resolving references between functions or variables across files, assigning memory addresses, and organizing code and data into sections according to the linker script. The result is a complete executable file, like an .elf, that can be loaded onto the target hardware. This process allows large programs to be split into manageable, modular source files that are compiled independently but work together as a unified program.\n',0,'s25'),('ldachner',3,1,'2025-06-27_09-54-37','When building a program from multiple .c files, the process begins with preprocessing, where each source file is scanned by the preprocessor to handle directives like #include and #define. This step expands macros, includes header files, and removes comments, resulting in a complete source file ready for compilation. Next, in the compilation step, each preprocessed source file is compiled separately into an object file (.o), which contains the machine code for the functions and data defined in that file. Finally, the linking step combines all the object files into a single executable by resolving references between functions or variables across files, assigning memory addresses, and organizing code and data into sections according to the linker script. The result is a complete executable file (e.g. .elf) that can be loaded onto the target hardware. This three-step process, preprocessing, compiling, and linking—allows large programs to be split into manageable, modular source files that are compiled independently but work together as a unified program.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',3,2,'2025-06-26_23-21-14','Including a .c file directly with #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it copies the entire contents of that source file into the place you included it, just like text copy-paste. This causes all the functions, variables, and definitions in that .c file to be recompiled as if they were part of the including file. As a result, you’ll end up with multiple definitions of the same functions or global variables across different compilation units, leading to linking errors. Instead, one should only #include header files (.h files), which contain function declarations and struct definitions, these tell the compiler what exists without duplicating the actual code. The correct way is to compile each .c file separately, then link them together, rather than merging source files with #include.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',3,2,'2025-06-27_09-54-37','Including a .c file directly with #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because it copies the entire contents of that source file into the place you included it, just like text copy-paste. This causes all the functions, variables, and definitions in that .c file to be recompiled as if they were part of the including file. As a result, you’ll end up with multiple definitions of the same functions or global variables across different compilation units, leading to linking errors. Instead, one should only #include header files (.h files), which contain function declarations and struct definitions, these tell the compiler what exists without duplicating the actual code. The correct way is to compile each .c file separately, then link them together, rather than merging source files with #include.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',3,3,'2025-06-26_23-21-14','To set up and start a thread, you begin by writing your thread function, which contains the code you want the thread to execute. Next, you initialize the kernel by calling a function that sets up the necessary data structures and prepares the operating system to manage threads. After initialization, you create your thread by calling a kernel function that registers the thread, allocates its stack, and stores information about it. Finally, you start the kernel by calling another function that launches the thread and begins its execution. This process ensures that your thread is properly set up and the kernel is ready to manage its operation.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',3,3,'2025-06-27_09-54-37','To set up and start a thread, you begin by writing your thread function, which contains the code you want the thread to execute. Next, you initialize the kernel by calling a function that sets up the necessary data structures and prepares the operating system to manage threads. After initialization, you create your thread by calling a kernel function that registers the thread, allocates its stack, and stores information about it. Finally, you start the kernel by calling another function that launches the thread and begins its execution. This process ensures that your thread is properly set up and the kernel is ready to manage its operation.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',3,4,'2025-06-26_23-21-14','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory set aside specifically to provide each thread with its own private stack space. Since every thread needs a separate stack to keep track of its function calls, local variables, and return addresses, the stack pool allows the operating system to divide memory into fixed-size stacks for multiple threads. By systematically allocating chunks of this pool to new threads, the OS can ensure each thread has a safe, isolated area to operate without corrupting other threads’ data. In future labs, the stack pool will enable the OS to support multiple threads at once by dynamically assigning stack space to each thread as it’s created, allowing them to run independently while sharing the same system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',3,4,'2025-06-27_09-54-37','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory set aside specifically to provide each thread with its own private stack space. Since every thread needs a separate stack to keep track of its function calls, local variables, and return addresses, the stack pool allows the operating system to divide memory into fixed-size stacks for multiple threads. By systematically allocating chunks of this pool to new threads, the OS can ensure each thread has a safe, isolated area to operate without corrupting other threads’ data. In future labs, the stack pool will enable the OS to support multiple threads at once by dynamically assigning stack space to each thread as it’s created, allowing them to run independently while sharing the same system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',3,5,'2025-06-26_23-21-14','The distinction between the user and the kernel in your project separates responsibilities and keeps your code organized. The kernel includes all the core operating system functionality, such as managing stacks, creating threads, and handling context switching, which belongs in the kernel.c and kernel.h files. In contrast, the user side contains application-specific code, like the main function and thread functions you write to perform tasks, which go in main.c. This separation is important because it allows you, as the user, to focus only on writing your application code without worrying about the complex internals of the kernel. It also makes the kernel reusable and modular, so you can create new applications simply by changing your user code while keeping the kernel stable and consistent.\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('ldachner',3,5,'2025-06-27_09-54-37','The distinction between the user and the kernel in your project separates responsibilities and keeps your code organized. The kernel includes all the core operating system functionality, such as managing stacks, creating threads, and handling context switching, which belongs in the kernel.c and kernel.h files. In contrast, the user side contains application-specific code, like the main function and thread functions you write to perform tasks, which go in main.c. This separation is important because it allows you, as the user, to focus only on writing your application code without worrying about the complex internals of the kernel. It also makes the kernel reusable and modular, so you can create new applications simply by changing your user code while keeping the kernel stable and consistent.\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,1,'2025-07-26_20-25-01','A round-robin scheduler can just as easily be built on a queue of thread-control blocks (TCBs) as on an indexed array. When a task’s time slice ends, or it cooperatively calls yield(), the scheduler simply dequeues the TCB at the head of the ready queue, runs that task, and, when the task yields, enqueues the same TCB at the tail. Because a queue is naturally first-in/first-out, the head always represents “who runs next,” so no index arithmetic or wrap-around logic is required. The principal benefit shows up when threads are created or destroyed dynamically: inserting or removing a node in a linked queue is O(1) and never leaves “holes,” whereas an array often needs compaction or carries unused slots. A queue also dovetails nicely with more sophisticated schemes, such as maintaining one FIFO per priority level, while keeping the round-robin policy inside each level. The trade-off is a slight increase in pointer storage and, in some micros, a few extra cache misses, but the conceptual clarity and dynamic flexibility usually make a queue the cleaner structure.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,1,'2025-07-27_13-30-06','A round-robin scheduler can just as easily be built on a queue of thread-control blocks (TCBs) as on an indexed array. When a task’s time slice ends, or it cooperatively calls yield(), the scheduler simply dequeues the TCB at the head of the ready queue, runs that task, and, when the task yields, enqueues the same TCB at the tail. Because a queue is naturally first-in/first-out, the head always represents “who runs next,” so no index arithmetic or wrap-around logic is required. The principal benefit shows up when threads are created or destroyed dynamically: inserting or removing a node in a linked queue is O(1) and never leaves “holes,” whereas an array often needs compaction or carries unused slots. A queue also dovetails nicely with more sophisticated schemes, such as maintaining one FIFO per priority level, while keeping the round-robin policy inside each level. The trade-off is a slight increase in pointer storage and, in some micros, a few extra cache misses, but the conceptual clarity and dynamic flexibility usually make a queue the cleaner structure.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,1,'2025-07-27_14-00-30','A round-robin scheduler can just as easily be built on a queue of thread-control blocks (TCBs) as on an indexed array. When a task’s time slice ends, or it cooperatively calls yield(), the scheduler simply dequeues the TCB at the head of the ready queue, runs that task, and, when the task yields, enqueues the same TCB at the tail. Because a queue is naturally first-in/first-out, the head always represents “who runs next,” so no index arithmetic or wrap-around logic is required. The principal benefit shows up when threads are created or destroyed dynamically: inserting or removing a node in a linked queue is O(1) and never leaves “holes,” whereas an array often needs compaction or carries unused slots. A queue also dovetails nicely with more sophisticated schemes, such as maintaining one FIFO per priority level, while keeping the round-robin policy inside each level. The trade-off is a slight increase in pointer storage and, in some micros, a few extra cache misses, but the conceptual clarity and dynamic flexibility usually make a queue the cleaner structure.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,2,'2025-07-26_20-25-01','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the complete CPU state of the outgoing thread, selecting a new thread, and restoring that new thread’s state before returning. When PendSV fires, the Cortex-M hardware has already pushed the core registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) for the current thread onto its Process Stack Pointer (PSP). The handler-running on the Main Stack in privileged mode-first pushes the remaining callee-saved registers R4–R11 onto the same PSP and then stores that final PSP value into the outgoing thread’s TCB. It calls the C routine osSched(), which chooses the next runnable TCB and returns a pointer to its saved PSP. The handler loads this PSP into the processor, pops R4–R11 for the incoming thread, and exits the exception using the special EXC_RETURN code. On that exception return, the hardware automatically pops the core register set from the incoming PSP. Because both register sets and the stack pointer have now been replaced, execution resumes in thread mode exactly where the new task last left off, achieving a full context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('ldachner',4,2,'2025-07-27_13-30-06','PendSV_Handler first needs to know exactly where the current thread’s stack frame lives, so the very first instruction in most implementations is an MRS R0, PSP (or the intrinsic __get_PSP()), which reads the Process Stack Pointer and copies its value into a general-purpose register. With that address in hand, the handler pushes the remaining callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto the same stack, then writes the updated PSP back into the outgoing thread’s TCB, fully preserving its context. Next it calls the C routine osSched(), which returns the PSP saved in the TCB of the thread that should run next. The handler loads this new PSP into the CPU (via MSR PSP, R0), pops R4–R11 for the incoming task, and exits the exception with the special EXC_RETURN value. On that exception return the hardware automatically pops R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR from the new PSP. Because the PSP was explicitly read at the start and explicitly reloaded before exit, the Cortex-M resumes execution in thread mode exactly where the new task last yielded, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,2,'2025-07-27_14-00-30','PendSV_Handler first needs to know exactly where the current thread’s stack frame lives, so the very first instruction in most implementations is an MRS R0, PSP (or the intrinsic __get_PSP()), which reads the Process Stack Pointer and copies its value into a general-purpose register. With that address in hand, the handler pushes the remaining callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto the same stack, then writes the updated PSP back into the outgoing thread’s TCB, fully preserving its context. Next it calls the C routine osSched(), which returns the PSP saved in the TCB of the thread that should run next. The handler loads this new PSP into the CPU (via MSR PSP, R0), pops R4–R11 for the incoming task, and exits the exception with the special EXC_RETURN value. On that exception return the hardware automatically pops R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR from the new PSP. Because the PSP was explicitly read at the start and explicitly reloaded before exit, the Cortex-M resumes execution in thread mode exactly where the new task last yielded, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,3,'2025-07-26_20-25-01','Always selecting the task with the numerically highest priority is attractive in theory but disastrous in practice when combined with cooperative yielding. A single highest-priority task can monopolise the CPU simply by failing to call yield(), starving every lower-priority task indefinitely. Even if the system is modified to pre-empt on a timer, strict “run the highest” scheduling introduces fairness and latency problems: lower-priority housekeeping or I/O tasks may wait unbounded amounts of time, potentially violating real-time deadlines. The policy also creates opportunities for priority inversion, where a low-priority task holds a shared resource needed by a high-priority task, blocking the latter unless extra priority-inheritance mechanisms are added. For these reasons, real systems either combine priority with round-robin time slices inside each level or employ ageing algorithms that gradually boost the priority of long-waiting tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,3,'2025-07-27_13-30-06','Always selecting the task with the numerically highest priority is attractive in theory but disastrous in practice when combined with cooperative yielding. A single highest-priority task can monopolise the CPU simply by failing to call yield(), starving every lower-priority task indefinitely. Even if the system is modified to pre-empt on a timer, strict “run the highest” scheduling introduces fairness and latency problems: lower-priority housekeeping or I/O tasks may wait unbounded amounts of time, potentially violating real-time deadlines. The policy also creates opportunities for priority inversion, where a low-priority task holds a shared resource needed by a high-priority task, blocking the latter unless extra priority-inheritance mechanisms are added. For these reasons, real systems either combine priority with round-robin time slices inside each level or employ ageing algorithms that gradually boost the priority of long-waiting tasks.\n',0,'s25'),('ldachner',4,3,'2025-07-27_14-00-30','Always picking the highest-priority TCB each time the scheduler runs creates starvation even when every task is perfectly well-behaved. Because the algorithm is deterministically biased toward the numerically largest priority value, the moment two ready lists exist (e.g., priority 5 and priority 2), the level-5 task is chosen on every scheduling decision, immediately returns to the head of the ready set after its time slice, and is chosen again. Lower-priority tasks never reach the CPU, regardless of whether the high-priority thread yields cooperatively or is pre-empted by a timer. In other words, the unfairness is baked into the scheduler’s fixed comparison logic, not into the threads’ code. This differs from careless task behaviour (such as a thread that forgets to call yield()): even a courteous, well-written low-priority task is starved, because the scheduler itself guarantees it will never be the “highest.” The design also amplifies classic priority-inversion hazards—if a low-priority task holds a mutex needed by a high-priority task, the high-priority task spins forever while an intermediate priority thread can still pre-empt the low one. Modern kernels therefore blend priority with round-robin slices or dynamic ageing so that the ready list is eventually exhausted across all levels, preventing starvation that is otherwise intrinsic to a “strict highest-wins” policy.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,4,'2025-07-26_20-25-01','Splitting the exception handlers so that they push and pop registers in assembly but delegate policy decisions to C strikes a pragmatic balance between control and clarity. Writing the scheduler and SVC logic in C makes the code readable, maintainable, and easy to debug—variables appear naturally in watch windows, and porting to other architectures or running unit tests on a desktop host is straightforward. The price is modest: each function call inserts a prologue and epilogue, increasing interrupt latency by a few dozen cycles and expanding the Main Stack frame. A pure-assembly implementation could shave those cycles and minimise stack usage by tailoring every push/pop exactly, but would be harder to modify or review and more error-prone for novice developers. In an educational lab the extra overhead is negligible, whereas the didactic value of legible C logic is substantial.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,4,'2025-07-27_13-30-06','Splitting the exception handlers so that they push and pop registers in assembly but delegate policy decisions to C strikes a pragmatic balance between control and clarity. Writing the scheduler and SVC logic in C makes the code readable, maintainable, and easy to debug—variables appear naturally in watch windows, and porting to other architectures or running unit tests on a desktop host is straightforward. The price is modest: each function call inserts a prologue and epilogue, increasing interrupt latency by a few dozen cycles and expanding the Main Stack frame. A pure-assembly implementation could shave those cycles and minimise stack usage by tailoring every push/pop exactly, but would be harder to modify or review and more error-prone for novice developers. In an educational lab the extra overhead is negligible, whereas the didactic value of legible C logic is substantial.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,4,'2025-07-27_14-00-30','Splitting the exception handlers so that they push and pop registers in assembly but delegate policy decisions to C strikes a pragmatic balance between control and clarity. Writing the scheduler and SVC logic in C makes the code readable, maintainable, and easy to debug—variables appear naturally in watch windows, and porting to other architectures or running unit tests on a desktop host is straightforward. The price is modest: each function call inserts a prologue and epilogue, increasing interrupt latency by a few dozen cycles and expanding the Main Stack frame. A pure-assembly implementation could shave those cycles and minimise stack usage by tailoring every push/pop exactly, but would be harder to modify or review and more error-prone for novice developers. In an educational lab the extra overhead is negligible, whereas the didactic value of legible C logic is substantial.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,5,'2025-07-26_20-25-01','If the application creates only a single thread e.g. print_continuously that calls osYield() after each message, the operating system still initializes correctly and the thread runs. Each yield() raises SVC #4, which sets the PendSV pending bit; PendSV then saves the thread’s context, calls the scheduler, and finds that the sole entry in the ready list is the same TCB it just saved. Consequently it restores that context and returns, so execution continues in exactly the same task. Functionally nothing breaks: the console output appears, and the kernel’s machinery churns harmlessly in the background. The only consequence is unnecessary overhead from context-switch bookkeeping that accomplishes no real scheduling, making the system behave almost as if no OS were present—just slightly slower.\n\n\n\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,5,'2025-07-27_13-30-06','If the application creates only a single thread e.g. print_continuously that calls osYield() after each message, the operating system still initializes correctly and the thread runs. Each yield() raises SVC #4, which sets the PendSV pending bit; PendSV then saves the thread’s context, calls the scheduler, and finds that the sole entry in the ready list is the same TCB it just saved. Consequently it restores that context and returns, so execution continues in exactly the same task. Functionally nothing breaks: the console output appears, and the kernel’s machinery churns harmlessly in the background. The only consequence is unnecessary overhead from context-switch bookkeeping that accomplishes no real scheduling, making the system behave almost as if no OS were present—just slightly slower.\n\n\n\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('ldachner',4,5,'2025-07-27_14-00-30','If the application creates only a single thread e.g. print_continuously that calls osYield() after each message, the operating system still initializes correctly and the thread runs. Each yield() raises SVC #4, which sets the PendSV pending bit; PendSV then saves the thread’s context, calls the scheduler, and finds that the sole entry in the ready list is the same TCB it just saved. Consequently it restores that context and returns, so execution continues in exactly the same task. Functionally nothing breaks: the console output appears, and the kernel’s machinery churns harmlessly in the background. The only consequence is unnecessary overhead from context-switch bookkeeping that accomplishes no real scheduling, making the system behave almost as if no OS were present—just slightly slower.\n\n\n\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,1,'2025-07-26_19-27-31','From the user’s point of view, supplying an argument is trivial: they declare their thread entry as void myTask(void *arg) and call osCreateThread(myTask, pointerToData). Internally, the kernel must copy that pointer into the new thread’s startup stack frame so that, when the CPU finally branches to myTask, register R0 already contains the user-supplied value. During thread creation the OS allocates a fresh stack, pre-loads the eight registers that the hardware automatically pops on an exception return (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1 and R0), and then tucks away dummy values for R4-R11. Crucially, the last of those “auto-popped” slots is R0, so by writing the pointer there the OS guarantees that the very first instruction executed inside myTask sees its argument in the normal ARM calling position. Because the ARM Cortex-M exception return sequence restores registers directly from the stack, no extra code is needed at run-time; the value simply appears in R0 exactly as if the function had been called in the usual C way.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,1,'2025-07-27_13-24-12','From the user’s point of view, supplying an argument is trivial: they declare their thread entry as void myTask(void *arg) and call osCreateThread(myTask, pointerToData). Internally, the kernel must copy that pointer into the new thread’s startup stack frame so that, when the CPU finally branches to myTask, register R0 already contains the user-supplied value. During thread creation the OS allocates a fresh stack, pre-loads the eight registers that the hardware automatically pops on an exception return (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1 and R0), and then tucks away dummy values for R4-R11. Crucially, the last of those “auto-popped” slots is R0, so by writing the pointer there the OS guarantees that the very first instruction executed inside myTask sees its argument in the normal ARM calling position. Because the ARM Cortex-M exception return sequence restores registers directly from the stack, no extra code is needed at run-time; the value simply appears in R0 exactly as if the function had been called in the usual C way.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,1,'2025-07-30_15-50-57','From the user’s point of view, supplying an argument is trivial: they declare their thread entry as void myTask(void *arg) and call osCreateThread(myTask, pointerToData). Internally, the kernel must copy that pointer into the new thread’s startup stack frame so that, when the CPU finally branches to myTask, register R0 already contains the user-supplied value. During thread creation the OS allocates a fresh stack, pre-loads the eight registers that the hardware automatically pops on an exception return (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1 and R0), and then tucks away dummy values for R4-R11. Crucially, the last of those “auto-popped” slots is R0, so by writing the pointer there the OS guarantees that the very first instruction executed inside myTask sees its argument in the normal ARM calling position. Because the ARM Cortex-M exception return sequence restores registers directly from the stack, no extra code is needed at run-time; the value simply appears in R0 exactly as if the function had been called in the usual C way.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,1,'2025-07-30_16-48-28','From the user’s point of view, supplying an argument is trivial: they declare their thread entry as void myTask(void *arg) and call osCreateThread(myTask, pointerToData). Internally, the kernel must copy that pointer into the new thread’s startup stack frame so that, when the CPU finally branches to myTask, register R0 already contains the user-supplied value. During thread creation the OS allocates a fresh stack, pre-loads the eight registers that the hardware automatically pops on an exception return (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1 and R0), and then tucks away dummy values for R4-R11. Crucially, the last of those “auto-popped” slots is R0, so by writing the pointer there the OS guarantees that the very first instruction executed inside myTask sees its argument in the normal ARM calling position. Because the ARM Cortex-M exception return sequence restores registers directly from the stack, no extra code is needed at run-time; the value simply appears in R0 exactly as if the function had been called in the usual C way.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,2,'2025-07-26_19-27-31','osYield() itself triggers a supervisor call (SVC #4) whose whole purpose is to request a context switch from thread mode. If you invoke it while already inside an exception handler such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, you nest one privileged exception inside another. That causes two problems. First, you waste cycles: the processor will finish the current handler, push yet another stack frame and immediately enter SVC again only to discover that it is already in privileged mode. Second—and worse—doing so can corrupt the scheduling invariants: osYield() assumes the callee is a thread with a valid process stack pointer (PSP); in an ISR the processor is using the main stack (MSP), so the scheduler would record the wrong SP in the TCB, leading to an eventual hard fault when the task is restored. In short, calling osYield() inside any of the OS’s own handlers causes redundant privilege transitions and is likely to scramble the bookkeeping that keeps each task’s stack isolated.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,2,'2025-07-27_13-24-12','osYield() itself triggers a supervisor call (SVC #4) whose whole purpose is to request a context switch from thread mode. If you invoke it while already inside an exception handler such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, you nest one privileged exception inside another. That causes two problems. First, you waste cycles: the processor will finish the current handler, push yet another stack frame and immediately enter SVC again only to discover that it is already in privileged mode. Second—and worse—doing so can corrupt the scheduling invariants: osYield() assumes the callee is a thread with a valid process stack pointer (PSP); in an ISR the processor is using the main stack (MSP), so the scheduler would record the wrong SP in the TCB, leading to an eventual hard fault when the task is restored. In short, calling osYield() inside any of the OS’s own handlers causes redundant privilege transitions and is likely to scramble the bookkeeping that keeps each task’s stack isolated.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,2,'2025-07-30_15-50-57','osYield() itself triggers a supervisor call (SVC #4) whose whole purpose is to request a context switch from thread mode. If you invoke it while already inside an exception handler such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, you nest one privileged exception inside another. That causes two problems. First, you waste cycles: the processor will finish the current handler, push yet another stack frame and immediately enter SVC again only to discover that it is already in privileged mode. Second—and worse—doing so can corrupt the scheduling invariants: osYield() assumes the callee is a thread with a valid process stack pointer (PSP); in an ISR the processor is using the main stack (MSP), so the scheduler would record the wrong SP in the TCB, leading to an eventual hard fault when the task is restored. In short, calling osYield() inside any of the OS’s own handlers causes redundant privilege transitions and is likely to scramble the bookkeeping that keeps each task’s stack isolated.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,2,'2025-07-30_16-48-28','osYield() itself triggers a supervisor call (SVC #4) whose whole purpose is to request a context switch from thread mode. If you invoke it while already inside an exception handler such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, you nest one privileged exception inside another. That causes two problems. First, you waste cycles: the processor will finish the current handler, push yet another stack frame and immediately enter SVC again only to discover that it is already in privileged mode. Second—and worse—doing so can corrupt the scheduling invariants: osYield() assumes the callee is a thread with a valid process stack pointer (PSP); in an ISR the processor is using the main stack (MSP), so the scheduler would record the wrong SP in the TCB, leading to an eventual hard fault when the task is restored. In short, calling osYield() inside any of the OS’s own handlers causes redundant privilege transitions and is likely to scramble the bookkeeping that keeps each task’s stack isolated.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,3,'2025-07-26_19-27-31','Shortening the kernel tick to 0.1 ms gives much finer temporal resolution. Latency-sensitive tasks—think audio processing or high-rate motor control—could be pre-empted and rescheduled an order of magnitude faster, reducing worst-case response times. Smaller quanta also make round-robin timeslicing appear “smoother” because individual bursts of CPU usage become less perceptible. The price is CPU overhead: every tick triggers SysTick, runs osTick(), possibly PendSV, and executes at least a partial context switch. Ten times more ticks therefore spend roughly ten times more cycles inside the kernel, shrinking the time left for real work and increasing power consumption. High interrupt rates can also starve low-priority peripherals that rely on DMA completion or slower ISRs. Finally, application software that assumes a 1-ms heartbeat (common in embedded timing loops) would all need revisiting. So a 0.1-ms tick is justified only when sub-millisecond deadlines genuinely dominate the workload.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,3,'2025-07-27_13-24-12','Shortening the kernel tick to 0.1 ms gives much finer temporal resolution. Latency-sensitive tasks—think audio processing or high-rate motor control—could be pre-empted and rescheduled an order of magnitude faster, reducing worst-case response times. Smaller quanta also make round-robin timeslicing appear “smoother” because individual bursts of CPU usage become less perceptible. The price is CPU overhead: every tick triggers SysTick, runs osTick(), possibly PendSV, and executes at least a partial context switch. Ten times more ticks therefore spend roughly ten times more cycles inside the kernel, shrinking the time left for real work and increasing power consumption. High interrupt rates can also starve low-priority peripherals that rely on DMA completion or slower ISRs. Finally, application software that assumes a 1-ms heartbeat (common in embedded timing loops) would all need revisiting. So a 0.1-ms tick is justified only when sub-millisecond deadlines genuinely dominate the workload.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,3,'2025-07-30_15-50-57','Shortening the kernel tick to 0.1 ms gives much finer temporal resolution. Latency-sensitive tasks—think audio processing or high-rate motor control—could be pre-empted and rescheduled an order of magnitude faster, reducing worst-case response times. Smaller quanta also make round-robin timeslicing appear “smoother” because individual bursts of CPU usage become less perceptible. The price is CPU overhead: every tick triggers SysTick, runs osTick(), possibly PendSV, and executes at least a partial context switch. Ten times more ticks therefore spend roughly ten times more cycles inside the kernel, shrinking the time left for real work and increasing power consumption. High interrupt rates can also starve low-priority peripherals that rely on DMA completion or slower ISRs. Finally, application software that assumes a 1-ms heartbeat (common in embedded timing loops) would all need revisiting. So a 0.1-ms tick is justified only when sub-millisecond deadlines genuinely dominate the workload.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,3,'2025-07-30_16-48-28','Shortening the kernel tick to 0.1 ms gives much finer temporal resolution. Latency-sensitive tasks—think audio processing or high-rate motor control—could be pre-empted and rescheduled an order of magnitude faster, reducing worst-case response times. Smaller quanta also make round-robin timeslicing appear “smoother” because individual bursts of CPU usage become less perceptible. The price is CPU overhead: every tick triggers SysTick, runs osTick(), possibly PendSV, and executes at least a partial context switch. Ten times more ticks therefore spend roughly ten times more cycles inside the kernel, shrinking the time left for real work and increasing power consumption. High interrupt rates can also starve low-priority peripherals that rely on DMA completion or slower ISRs. Finally, application software that assumes a 1-ms heartbeat (common in embedded timing loops) would all need revisiting. So a 0.1-ms tick is justified only when sub-millisecond deadlines genuinely dominate the workload.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,4,'2025-07-26_19-27-31','To a user-level programmer, co-operative multitasking means “your thread keeps running until you voluntarily call osYield() or perform a blocking operation.” The programmer must sprinkle yields or delays at points where the task can safely be paused; otherwise one misbehaving loop can freeze the whole system. In pre-emptive multitasking, the kernel forcibly interrupts tasks after a configurable slice—no consent required—so each task merely appears to run concurrently.\nFrom the OS’s perspective the difference is in who decides when to switch. In a co-operative design the scheduler runs only when a thread asks for it, so context switches happen in a known, controlled state; no interrupt-time bookkeeping of CPU usage is needed. Pre-emption requires a periodic hardware timer (SysTick) that fires in the background, decrements a runtime counter, and, when it expires, pends PendSV to perform an involuntary context switch. The kernel must track per-task accounting, handle nested interrupts, and ensure that a task can be interrupted at any point without corrupting shared data—often demanding mutexes or critical sections. Thus pre-emption adds responsiveness and fault-tolerance at the cost of greater kernel complexity and potential for race conditions.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,4,'2025-07-27_13-24-12','To a user-level programmer, co-operative multitasking means “your thread keeps running until you voluntarily call osYield() or perform a blocking operation.” The programmer must sprinkle yields or delays at points where the task can safely be paused; otherwise one misbehaving loop can freeze the whole system. In pre-emptive multitasking, the kernel forcibly interrupts tasks after a configurable slice—no consent required—so each task merely appears to run concurrently.\nFrom the OS’s perspective the difference is in who decides when to switch. In a co-operative design the scheduler runs only when a thread asks for it, so context switches happen in a known, controlled state; no interrupt-time bookkeeping of CPU usage is needed. Pre-emption requires a periodic hardware timer (SysTick) that fires in the background, decrements a runtime counter, and, when it expires, pends PendSV to perform an involuntary context switch. The kernel must track per-task accounting, handle nested interrupts, and ensure that a task can be interrupted at any point without corrupting shared data—often demanding mutexes or critical sections. Thus pre-emption adds responsiveness and fault-tolerance at the cost of greater kernel complexity and potential for race conditions.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,4,'2025-07-30_15-50-57','To a user-level programmer, co-operative multitasking means “your thread keeps running until you voluntarily call osYield() or perform a blocking operation.” The programmer must sprinkle yields or delays at points where the task can safely be paused; otherwise one misbehaving loop can freeze the whole system. In pre-emptive multitasking, the kernel forcibly interrupts tasks after a configurable slice—no consent required—so each task merely appears to run concurrently.\nFrom the OS’s perspective the difference is in who decides when to switch. In a co-operative design the scheduler runs only when a thread asks for it, so context switches happen in a known, controlled state; no interrupt-time bookkeeping of CPU usage is needed. Pre-emption requires a periodic hardware timer (SysTick) that fires in the background, decrements a runtime counter, and, when it expires, pends PendSV to perform an involuntary context switch. The kernel must track per-task accounting, handle nested interrupts, and ensure that a task can be interrupted at any point without corrupting shared data—often demanding mutexes or critical sections. Thus pre-emption adds responsiveness and fault-tolerance at the cost of greater kernel complexity and potential for race conditions.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,4,'2025-07-30_16-48-28','To a user-level programmer, co-operative multitasking means “your thread keeps running until you voluntarily call osYield() or perform a blocking operation.” The programmer must sprinkle yields or delays at points where the task can safely be paused; otherwise one misbehaving loop can freeze the whole system. In pre-emptive multitasking, the kernel forcibly interrupts tasks after a configurable slice—no consent required—so each task merely appears to run concurrently.\nFrom the OS’s perspective the difference is in who decides when to switch. In a co-operative design the scheduler runs only when a thread asks for it, so context switches happen in a known, controlled state; no interrupt-time bookkeeping of CPU usage is needed. Pre-emption requires a periodic hardware timer (SysTick) that fires in the background, decrements a runtime counter, and, when it expires, pends PendSV to perform an involuntary context switch. The kernel must track per-task accounting, handle nested interrupts, and ensure that a task can be interrupted at any point without corrupting shared data—often demanding mutexes or critical sections. Thus pre-emption adds responsiveness and fault-tolerance at the cost of greater kernel complexity and potential for race conditions.\n',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,5,'2025-07-26_19-27-31','\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',0,'s25'),('ldachner',5,5,'2025-07-27_13-24-12','Yield-based and timer-based context switching coexist because both ultimately funnel through the same mechanism: the Cortex-M4’s PendSV handler, which is the one and only place the OS performs the full context switch—saving R4-R11 of the outgoing task, writing its updated PSP to its TCB, selecting the next runnable TCB, restoring that task’s PSP and registers, and returning to thread mode. A co-operative switch begins when a running task calls osYield(), which issues SVC #4; inside SVC_Handler_Main the kernel does nothing more than set the “pend” bit for PendSV and exit, leaving the heavy lifting to PendSV. Pre-emptive switching starts in SysTick_Handler, where osTick() decrements the running task’s runtime_ms; when it reaches zero the timer code pends PendSV in exactly the same way. Thus, whether a thread voluntarily yields or the 1-ms slice expires, the processor ends up in PendSV and executes the identical context-switching code path, ensuring consistency, eliminating duplication, and allowing both co-operative and pre-emptive policies to operate harmoniously.\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,5,'2025-07-30_15-50-57','Yield-based and timer-based context switching coexist because both ultimately funnel through the same mechanism: the Cortex-M4’s PendSV handler, which is the one and only place the OS performs the full context switch—saving R4-R11 of the outgoing task, writing its updated PSP to its TCB, selecting the next runnable TCB, restoring that task’s PSP and registers, and returning to thread mode. A co-operative switch begins when a running task calls osYield(), which issues SVC #4; inside SVC_Handler_Main the kernel does nothing more than set the “pend” bit for PendSV and exit, leaving the heavy lifting to PendSV. Pre-emptive switching starts in SysTick_Handler, where osTick() decrements the running task’s runtime_ms; when it reaches zero the timer code pends PendSV in exactly the same way. Thus, whether a thread voluntarily yields or the 1-ms slice expires, the processor ends up in PendSV and executes the identical context-switching code path, ensuring consistency, eliminating duplication, and allowing both co-operative and pre-emptive policies to operate harmoniously.\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('ldachner',5,5,'2025-07-30_16-48-28','Yield-based and timer-based context switching coexist because both ultimately funnel through the same mechanism: the Cortex-M4’s PendSV handler, which is the one and only place the OS performs the full context switch—saving R4-R11 of the outgoing task, writing its updated PSP to its TCB, selecting the next runnable TCB, restoring that task’s PSP and registers, and returning to thread mode. A co-operative switch begins when a running task calls osYield(), which issues SVC #4; inside SVC_Handler_Main the kernel does nothing more than set the “pend” bit for PendSV and exit, leaving the heavy lifting to PendSV. Pre-emptive switching starts in SysTick_Handler, where osTick() decrements the running task’s runtime_ms; when it reaches zero the timer code pends PendSV in exactly the same way. Thus, whether a thread voluntarily yields or the 1-ms slice expires, the processor ends up in PendSV and executes the identical context-switching code path, ensuring consistency, eliminating duplication, and allowing both co-operative and pre-emptive policies to operate harmoniously.\n\nCitation: AI was one of the tools used to help generate and refine code, proof read, refine and research answers in this report as is consistent and allowed in the course outline.',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',1,1,'2025-05-14_19-14-57','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer and is code (a library) written by ST Microelectronics that contains a lot of code needed to use with the microcontroller. For example, they include drivers and other functions that are bases needed to use the microcontroller, just like the HAL_UART_Transmit() function we used earlier in the lab to do serial output.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',1,2,'2025-05-14_19-14-57','__io_putchar is a wrapper function that is called by the printf function. Although printf is included in the stdio.h library, it uses other functions to help it actually use the hardware to perform its function. This helper function need to be written individually since it may vary depending on the project. In our case, it needs to use HAL_UART_Transmit to convert each letter of our string to be printable.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',1,3,'2025-05-14_19-14-57','Although printf can be used to output many things at different points so that it is visible to us, using a debugger can allow us to see the values in the stack as the program runs. It’s difficult to see what’s contained in the register by just using printf, so in this way the debugger is especially helpful.\nI also think that using printf for everything is incredibly inefficient for debugging. At my last co-op, we were working on a code base that didn’t have an easily usable debugger, possibly since the builds relied on libraries from so many code bases. So I would use output whenever I needed to debug and find where a value was changed incorrectly. This took forever since there was no way to have breakpoints and step through, I just had to make sure I put all the print statements in all the right places. If I missed a place or printed the wrong thing, I had to add it back in and rebuild over again.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',1,4,'2025-05-14_19-14-57','There is a certain amount of stack memory. Of that, some is needed for the main stack – this can be used for interrupts, setup and other things like knowing when the storage is full. For most of the rest of the stack, it can be used for threading and managing processes, which is why we want to have a PSP_val as well. We get this just by subtracting the amount of stack we need (in our case 0x400 bytes) for the main stack. It’s important to know the initial location of MSP so that we can make sure that our PSP_val points to the correct location since it is based off the initial location of the MSP. This way, those 0x400 bytes are reserved for the main stack and the PSP_val now holds the address of the start of the part of the stack we can use for threading and running current processes without stealing from the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',1,5,'2025-05-14_19-14-57','You would know where each thread stack starts in memory since we said we want each thread stack to be a certain size (this is defined by us). Similarly to how we decided that the main stack needs 0x400 bytes and subtracted that from the initial value of MSP to get the location of the PSP, we would just subtract the thread stack size (in the lab, we said this would also be 0x400) in increments to get the locations of all the thread stacks. If we just know how many we want, we would divide the available space (after subtracting the size needed for the main stack) into the number of threads we want, and then subtract that size from the PSP value in increments.\nBasically:\nTHREAD_LOCATION_j = MSP_INIT_VAL – MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (j+1)(THREAD_STACK_SIZE)\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',2,1,'2025-06-27_08-45-13','The PC (program counter) stores the address of the next instruction. When we change it to hold the function pointer of the print continuously function, it is basically storing the next instruction as the print continuously function. Since the print continuously function prints the print statement continuously in a loop, the program does exactly that and that’s why we get the same output as when we called the print continuously function from main() directly.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',2,1,'2025-06-27_09-38-31','The PC (Program Counter) holds the memory address of the next instruction to execute. When the PC is updated to point to the address of our print_continuously function, the processor immediately begins executing instructions from that address. On a Cortex-M processor, the PC is directly tied to the instruction fetch stage — as soon as it’s changed, the processor loads the instruction at the new address and begins executing it without any delay or additional function call. This is why execution jumps straight into the print_continuously loop: the updated PC causes the processor to treat the new address as the next step in the control flow. In contrast to just referencing a function pointer, updating the PC transfers control and triggers immediate execution from that memory location.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',2,2,'2025-06-27_08-45-13','Since there are some things that only the OS (in kernel mode) can do, like reading and writing from memory, starting processes, network communication, and more, a user program must request the OS (kernel mode) to do these things if it wants them to be done. In this case a system call is used – a call to the OS to perform a service for the user program. When the system call is executed, a software interrupt (or trap) is issued to the OS, the CPU switches from user mode to kernel mode, the OS looks up the call number to see which specific service is requested, and then executes the service. Once it’s finished, it might return a result if there is any to the user program, and the CPU switches back to user mode (OS gives control back to user program).\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',2,2,'2025-06-27_09-38-31','Since there are some things that only the OS (in kernel mode) can do, like reading and writing from memory, starting processes, network communication, and more, a user program must request the OS (kernel mode) to do these things if it wants them to be done. In this case a system call is used – a call to the OS to perform a service for the user program. When the system call is executed, a software interrupt (or trap) is issued to the OS, the CPU switches from user mode to kernel mode, the OS looks up the call number to see which specific service is requested, and then executes the service. Once it’s finished, it might return a result if there is any to the user program, and the CPU switches back to user mode (OS gives control back to user program).\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',2,3,'2025-06-27_08-45-13','The thread’s stack was set up by allocating space in the stack for the thread. First, a variable stackptr was assigned a value based on the MSP initial value, giving us a new stack pointer. Then the stack, starting from where our new stack pointer was, was populated with register values starting from xPSR up to R0. Since the stack pointer grows downwards, we had to keep decrementing each time we populate a register value. Since after all that, the stack pointer was at the end of the registers, ready to pop and operate, and store as needed with the register values. The reason we need to store register values on the stack is because in order to start the thread, we need to trick the chip/system into thinking it’s already running a thread – if it was, the register values would be found in the stack, therefore we put them into the stack to make it seem like it is already running a thread.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',2,3,'2025-06-27_09-38-31','The thread’s stack was set up by allocating space in the stack for the thread. First, a variable stackptr was assigned a value based on the MSP initial value, giving us a new stack pointer. Then the stack, starting from where our new stack pointer was, was populated with register values starting from xPSR up to R0. Since the stack pointer grows downwards, we had to keep decrementing each time we populate a register value. Since after all that, the stack pointer was at the end of the registers, ready to pop and operate, and store as needed with the register values. The reason we need to store register values on the stack is because in order to start the thread, we need to trick the chip/system into thinking it’s already running a thread – if it was, the register values would be found in the stack, therefore we put them into the stack to make it seem like it is already running a thread.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',2,4,'2025-06-27_08-45-13','If we didn’t set PSP, the PSP would still be uninitialized, likely containing whatever garbage value that memory held previously, if any. Since the runFirstThread() function pops and operates on the register values that are supposed to be stored in the thread stack pointed to by the PSP, if it tried to operate using the uninitialized PSP, the thread stack wouldn’t exist in the perspective of the runFirstThread(). Any of the POP, MOV, etc. operations with the uninitialized PSP would result in a hard fault and crash, because that space is not allocated or defined for the system at that point. In short, if we didn’t set the PSP before running the thread, the PSP wouldn’t be in the right position to manage the stack allocated specifically for the thread, leading to a fault.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',2,4,'2025-06-27_09-38-31','The CPU starts with the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is used for system-level operations like exception handling and initialization. However, threads are expected to use the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is completely separate and located in a different memory region to isolate thread execution from the kernel. If we don’t set the PSP before running a thread, it remains uninitialized and likely holds a garbage value, pointing to invalid memory. Unlike the default Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is used during reset and for exception handling, the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is specifically used for thread execution to keep thread stacks separate from the system stack. Since runFirstThread() relies on PSP to access the thread\'s stack, failing to set PSP means the CPU will try to pop register values from an undefined location, leading to a hard fault. The PSP must be explicitly set to the top of the memory region allocated for the thread\'s stack to ensure that context restoration works properly. Without this setup, the system tries to operate on an invalid stack during the thread startup, which crashes the program.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',2,5,'2025-06-27_08-45-13','I did actually try this out. When looking with the debugger, past the breakpoint I put on the while loop at the end of main, the register values are not populated. I think this means, as I suspected, that since the thread is created for the purpose of the function, once the function is run and the PC (program counter)  does not hold any further instruction, the space in the stack allocated to the thread is returned and reset.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',2,5,'2025-06-27_09-38-31','If the thread function simply runs once and returns, the processor will attempt to pop a return address from the thread\'s stack so it knows where to go next. However, unlike a regular function call, a thread\'s stack typically does not contain a valid return address, because the thread was never called by another function — it was launched by the OS. As a result, when the function returns, the CPU tries to branch to an invalid or uninitialized memory location. On a Cortex-M4, this triggers a hardfault, because the processor attempts to execute an instruction from an illegal address. To avoid this, thread functions must never return — they should either loop indefinitely or call a termination handler that properly cleans up the thread context.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',3,1,'2025-06-27_09-26-16','When building an embedded application, each .c file is compiled separately into an object file (.o). These object files contain machine code but are not yet linked together. The linker then combines all the object files into a single executable (.elf) file. During this linking stage, it resolves function calls and variable references across different files. This process allows for modular development and easier debugging and maintenance.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',3,1,'2025-06-27_10-39-08','When building an embedded application, each .c file first goes through preprocessing, where directives like #include and #define are expanded and replaced to produce a single, expanded source file. This preprocessed file is then compiled into an object file (.o), which contains machine code but is not yet a complete program. Each .c file is compiled separately into its own object file. After that, the linker combines all object files into a single executable (.elf) file by resolving function calls, variable references, and memory addresses across files. This full process — preprocessing, compiling, and linking — enables modular development and allows different parts of the codebase to be organized across multiple source files.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',3,2,'2025-06-27_09-26-16','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because #include is meant for header files (.h), not source files. Including a .c file directly causes its contents to be copied into another .c file before compilation, leading to multiple definitions of the same functions or variables. This breaks the separation of compilation units and can cause linker errors. It also prevents independent compilation, which defeats modularity. Instead, you should include only .h files and compile .c files separately.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',3,2,'2025-06-27_10-39-08','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad idea because #include is meant for header files (.h), not source files. Including a .c file directly causes its contents to be copied into another .c file before compilation, leading to multiple definitions of the same functions or variables. This breaks the separation of compilation units and can cause linker errors. It also prevents independent compilation, which defeats modularity. Instead, you should include only .h files and compile .c files separately.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',3,3,'2025-06-27_09-26-16','To set up and start a thread, the user typically follows these steps:\nDefine a function that the thread will run.\nAllocate a stack for the thread, either manually or through a thread-creation API.\nCall a function (often provided by the OS) to create the thread, passing it the function and stack.\nSet the thread’s priority and other parameters if needed.\nThe OS will schedule and run the thread once it is created.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',3,3,'2025-06-27_10-39-08','Generally, the user does not directly set up and start a thread on their own. Instead the user calls on the kernel to do it. The kernel is the part of the OS code that should have the ability to set up and start threads. In our case, the kernel code includes a method for kernel initialization (osKernelInitialization()) and a method to start (osKernelStart()), as well as a method for creating a thread (osCreateThread()). To set up and start a thread, from the user’s perspective:\nDefine a function that the thread will run.\nCall the OS kernel initialization method (initialize the kernel)\nCall the OS kernel method to create a thread, passing in the function that the thread will run\nCall the OS kernel method to start – this will actually start the thread\nFor the user, the specific process of setting up the thread and starting it is abstracted and as long as the user calls the correct initialization, thread-creation and start methods for the kernel, it does not have to worry about the specifics. For extra information, though, the steps the kernel must follow for thread set up and start are as follows:\nAllocate a stack for the thread (we used a thread-creation method which then did the stack allocation)\nPush the initial register values onto the thread’s stack\nCall the correct SVC to start the thread – this will run a different function with assembly instructions through the SVC handler\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',3,4,'2025-06-27_09-26-16','The stack pool is a reserved area of memory that provides space for thread stacks. Each thread in an operating system needs its own stack to keep track of local variables, return addresses, and function calls. In future labs, we can allocate space from the stack pool when creating new threads. This ensures that multiple threads can run independently without overwriting each other\'s stack data. Proper management of the stack pool is critical for thread stability and system reliability.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',3,4,'2025-06-27_10-39-08','The stack pool is a reserved area of memory that provides space for thread stacks. Each thread in an operating system needs its own stack to keep track of local variables, return addresses, and function calls. In future labs, we can allocate space from the stack pool when creating new threads. This ensures that multiple threads can run independently without overwriting each other\'s stack data. Proper management of the stack pool is critical for thread stability and system reliability.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',3,5,'2025-06-27_09-26-16','The “user” refers to the application code written to use the OS (user programs), while the “kernel” refers to the OS code that manages hardware and resources. From a file structure perspective, main.c and main.h contain user code, while kernel.c and kernel.h implement the OS functions. This abstraction/separation ensures that the user can use kernel services without needing to understand or modify the kernel’s internals. It also allows the kernel to be reused across different applications. From the user’s perspective, this distinction makes the system easier to use, and test.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',3,5,'2025-06-27_10-39-08','The “user” refers to the application code written to use the OS (user programs), while the “kernel” refers to the OS code that manages hardware and resources. From a file structure perspective, main.c and main.h contain user code, while kernel.c and kernel.h implement the OS functions. This abstraction/separation ensures that the user can use kernel services without needing to understand or modify the kernel’s internals. It also allows the kernel to be reused across different applications. From the user’s perspective, this distinction makes the system easier to use, and test.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',4,1,'2025-07-31_01-14-33','Using a queue instead of just an array to store threads is interesting because queues are first-in-first-out data structures. This means there is no indexing required if there are pointers to the head and tail of the queue. The round-robin scheduling implemented in the lab with an array would work similarly with a queue instead of the array, but instead of using an index to add, you can just enqueue. Benefits would be that there are less numbers to keep track of (no need for indexing).\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',4,1,'2025-07-31_01-47-08','Using a queue to store TCBs is interesting because it naturally supports round-robin scheduling through its FIFO behavior: the thread at the front (head) of the queue runs next, and once it finishes or yields, it can be moved to the back. This eliminates the need for manual indexing, as the scheduler always runs the thread at the head and rotates it automatically. While the lab’s current array-based scheduler mimics this behavior using a circular index, a queue makes this behavior more explicit and potentially easier to manage conceptually. The benefit is that there’s less to keep track of in terms of indices, but it comes with trade-offs such as managing more pointers, which may introduce additional overhead compared to using a simple array with known size.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',4,2,'2025-07-31_01-14-33','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s CPU state (such as registers and stack pointer) onto its stack. It then calls the scheduler to select the next thread to run and loads that thread’s saved context. This allows the CPU to resume execution from where the new thread left off. Because PendSV is a pendable interrupt and has the lowest priority (waits for all higher priority interrupts to be done), it ensures the context switch happens only when no other interrupts are active.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',4,2,'2025-07-31_01-47-08','The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch by first reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread’s stack. It saves the thread’s CPU context by pushing registers R4–R11 onto the stack, as these are not automatically saved by the hardware. It then calls osSched to select the next thread to run and updates the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack. The new thread’s context is restored by popping R4–R11 from its stack, allowing execution to resume precisely where it left off. Because PendSV has the lowest interrupt priority, it ensures the context switch occurs only when all higher-priority interrupts have completed.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',4,3,'2025-07-31_01-14-33','This is an issue because if the task with the highest priority is always chosen, a task with extremely low priority may never get done.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',4,3,'2025-07-31_01-47-08','This is an issue because if the task with the highest priority is always chosen, a task with extremely low priority may never get done.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',4,4,'2025-07-31_01-14-33','First of all, assembly is less readable than C functions, so this helps keep the code organized. There is abstraction between what we are controlling directly and the very granular tasks that have to be done in assembly, making the code easier to work with. Although having the abstraction makes the code easier to work with, it also makes it more difficult to debug errors that could happen.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',4,4,'2025-07-31_01-47-08','First of all, assembly is less readable than C functions, so this helps keep the code organized. There is abstraction between what we are controlling directly and the very granular tasks that have to be done in assembly, making the code easier to work with. Although having the abstraction makes the code easier to work with, it also makes it more difficult to debug errors that could happen.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',4,5,'2025-07-31_01-14-33','While the OS will likely still work, having just the single print_continuously thread will not allow for any other tasks to be done by the OS. Having the yield will not do anything since there is no other thread to yield for, and so the single thread will continue running. The PendSV interrupt that happens when osYield is called will not actually be able to switch contexts since there is no other context to switch to. However, it will still save the status of the current (and only) thread onto its stack.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',4,5,'2025-07-31_01-47-08','If only a single thread like print_continuously is created and it yields after printing, the OS will still function correctly, but no actual context switch will occur. When osYield is called, the thread’s state is saved and the PendSV interrupt is triggered, prompting the scheduler to select the next thread. Since only one thread exists, the scheduler will return the same thread, and its saved state will be restored, allowing it to continue execution. This shows that the OS is operational—performing context save, scheduler call, and context restore—even though there are no other threads to switch to. The system effectively loops through the yield mechanism, but all control remains with the single thread.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',5,1,'2025-07-31_03-02-24','To pass an argument into a thread, the user should call the osCreateThread (or whatever thread creation function that is being used in the OS), passing in the thread function as well as the argument to be passed to the thread. The OS has to ensure that osCreateThread can handle the arguments properly – the function osCreateThread should be able to take in the arguments as a void pointer and then set R0 and whatever other registers required to hold the arguments so they can be ready for the function. When the thread is created with the function, and the registers hold the arguments to be passed into the thread function, the thread creation is all set. Now, when the thread is started, the registers hold the correct values to perform the thread function with the arguments.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',5,1,'2025-07-31_03-17-44','To pass an argument into a thread, the user calls osCreateThread, providing both the thread function and a pointer to the argument (typically as a void*). The OS then prepares the initial stack frame for the new thread so that, when it starts executing, the argument is placed in register R0, following the ARM Cortex-M4 Procedure Call Standard (part of the ABI). This is done by preloading R0 in the saved context on the stack, so when the thread is first restored and begins execution, it behaves as if the function was called normally with the argument. The stack is essential here, as it holds the initial context including the program counter, xPSR, and argument registers, making the thread start seamlessly. This works because the ABI defines how arguments are passed (e.g., first argument in R0), allowing the OS to construct the stack so that the function receives the argument correctly\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',5,2,'2025-07-31_03-02-24','Our function osYield actually calls the PendSV_Handler call located in SVC_Handler_Main through the _asm(“SVC #3”) call. This means that if you were to call osYield within any of the context-switching functions, the function would be calling itself over and over again and it would be stuck in a loop forever. Also, the context_switching functions deal with assembly code that has to get done anyways, so calling osYield from within would be unhelpful since it is essentially calling the slightly higher level version of the same thing which ends up having to do the lower level (assembly) code anyways.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',5,2,'2025-07-31_03-17-44','Calling osYield inside a context-switching function like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler is unsafe because it triggers an SVC exception, which the CPU tries to handle by starting a new interrupt while already in an interrupt context. On the Cortex-M4, nested exceptions are allowed, but only under specific priority rules—calling osYield inside an active handler can break these rules, potentially leading to a hardfault. This can result in stack corruption because each exception pushes state onto the stack, and repeated or recursive triggering without returning may overflow the stack or corrupt saved context. Moreover, these handlers are already part of the low-level mechanism used by osYield, so calling it from within creates a recursive loop that disrupts the intended control flow of the scheduler. Therefore, calling osYield in these functions is both logically redundant and structurally unsafe.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',5,3,'2025-07-31_03-02-24','Having the fundamental OS clock to be 1ms means that changes get clocked every 1ms. If we changed it to be 0.1ms, changes would happen that much faster. Benefits to this would be if something needed to be very accurately timed, and interrupts for reactions to follow up on the change, it would be much more precise and quick (higher resolution response). Downsides would be if the change needed to be seen, there would be no point in reducing the fundamental OS clock to be 0.1ms since someone watching would not be able to witness the change as well as if the fundamental OS clock was larger.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',5,3,'2025-07-31_03-17-44','Reducing the fundamental OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms increases the system’s timing resolution, allowing for more precise task scheduling, faster response to timeouts, and improved accuracy in real-time applications. It’s particularly beneficial for applications requiring high-frequency sampling or tight control loops. However, this also increases the number of SysTick interrupts the CPU must handle—ten times more frequent in this case—leading to higher CPU overhead and potentially reducing the time available for actual thread execution. It may also increase power consumption and strain shared resources like memory or buses due to more frequent context switching. Therefore, while the increased timing precision is valuable, it comes with significant system-level trade-offs in performance and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',5,4,'2025-07-31_03-02-24','From the user perspective, pre-emptive multitasking requires an input for the threads for the amount of time the thread should be run before getting reset. For co-operative multitasking, the user does not need to concern themselves with the timing of the threads. For pre-emptive multitasking, the OS needs to take in the user-input time parameter and set the runtime and timeslice to be that. It can use the SysTick_Handler, as we did in the lab to keep track of the time passing and decrementing the runtime left for the thread. Once the runtime runs out, the thread will not continue unless reset to the timeslice value and the PendSV (pendable interrupt) call must be called for context-switching, same as the yielding in the co-operative multitasking. For co-operative multitasking, the OS does not need to concern itself with the runtime, timeslice, or SysTick_Handler, only calling the PendSV context-switching call when osYield is called.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',5,4,'2025-07-31_03-17-44','In co-operative multitasking, the user (programmer) is responsible for explicitly calling osYield() within each thread to allow the OS to switch to another thread; if the user forgets to do this, that thread will run indefinitely and block all others. In contrast, with pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically interrupts threads after a fixed time slice using a timer interrupt like SysTick_Handler, so the user doesn’t need to manage yielding manually. For pre-emptive multitasking, the OS tracks each thread’s runtime and timeslice, and once the time expires, it triggers a context switch via PendSV_Handler. In co-operative multitasking, the OS does not track time or enforce switching—it only performs a context switch when osYield() is explicitly called. So, co-operative multitasking gives the user more control but also more responsibility, while pre-emptive multitasking relies on the OS for fairness and responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('lkuruvil',5,5,'2025-07-31_03-02-24','The final exercise of this lab really demonstrates how yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can be used at the same time. In my example, I had a yielding thread that was performing simple addition and multiplication operations on a value, as well as a pre-emptive thread that was printing the value. The pre-emptive printing thread was created with a 2000ms timeslice. What happens is that the printing will keep happening until its 2000ms are up at which point the context switching happens and the calculation thread is able to run, yield, and the context switch happens again to go back to the printing. This can be seen clearly in the proof photos.\n',0,'s25'),('lkuruvil',5,5,'2025-07-31_03-17-44','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because both ultimately trigger the same mechanism for switching threads: the PendSV_Handler. In cooperative multitasking, a thread voluntarily calls osYield(), which sets the PendSV interrupt to request a context switch. In pre-emptive multitasking, the SysTick_Handler fires after a thread\'s timeslice expires and also sets the PendSV interrupt. In both cases, the OS uses the same context switch logic inside PendSV_Handler to save the current thread\'s state, call the scheduler, and restore the next thread\'s context. This unified use of PendSV allows both cooperative and pre-emptive threads to coexist smoothly within the same system.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',1,1,'2025-06-25_17-39-12','The HAL, or Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a set of code provided by the chip manufacturer (STMicroelectronics in this case) that makes it easier to control hardware like timers, UART, and GPIO without needing to write low-level code yourself. It handles the complex setup behind the scenes, so I can focus on writing higher-level code for my project. Using the HAL saves time and avoids errors since I don\'t have to manually configure all the registers.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',1,1,'2025-06-25_22-19-22','The HAL, or Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a set of code provided by the chip manufacturer (STMicroelectronics in this case) that makes it easier to control hardware like timers, UART, and GPIO without needing to write low-level code yourself. It handles the complex setup behind the scenes, so I can focus on writing higher-level code for my project. Using the HAL saves time and avoids errors since I don\'t have to manually configure all the registers.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',1,2,'2025-06-25_17-39-12','The __io_putchar function is used to send a single character over a serial connection, usually through UART. It’s important because the printf function depends on it to print output to a terminal. Without this function, printf wouldn’t know how to actually send characters to the computer. It acts like a bridge between printf and the UART hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',1,2,'2025-06-25_22-19-22','The __io_putchar function is used to send a single character over a serial connection, usually through UART. It’s important because the printf function depends on it to print output to a terminal. Without this function, printf wouldn’t know how to actually send characters to the computer. It acts like a bridge between printf and the UART hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',1,3,'2025-06-25_17-39-12','We used the debugger because it lets us look at the internal state of the microcontroller, like register values and memory, which printf can’t do. Also, some things happen before we can even use printf, like stack setup, so we need a way to pause and inspect what’s going on at those early stages. The debugger helped confirm if the stack values were correct and let us verify that MSP and PSP were set properly.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',1,3,'2025-06-25_22-19-22','We used the debugger because it lets us look at the internal state of the microcontroller, like register values and memory, which printf can’t do. Also, some things happen before we can even use printf, like stack setup, so we need a way to pause and inspect what’s going on at those early stages. The debugger helped confirm if the stack values were correct and let us verify that MSP and PSP were set properly.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',1,4,'2025-06-25_17-39-12','We started by reading the initial value of MSP from address 0x0. That gave us the top of the default stack. Then, since we wanted the main stack to be 0x400 bytes, we subtracted 0x400 from the MSP value to get the start of PSP. We needed the MSP value because the stack memory comes from one big block, so we had to calculate where PSP starts by knowing where MSP ends. This lets us avoid overlaps and keep each thread’s stack separate.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',1,4,'2025-06-25_22-19-22','We needed the initial value of the MSP because it tells us where the stack memory begins in RAM. Since stacks grow downward in memory, this value acts as our reference point for laying out all other stacks, like the PSP. If we didn\'t know the starting point of the MSP, we would have no idea where the current stack ends, and we might accidentally place the PSP in the same memory region, which could lead to overlapping stacks or corrupted data.\nIf the MSP value was incorrect or unknown, we could end up assigning the PSP to a memory location that’s already being used by the main stack or other system data. That would cause hard-to-find bugs and possibly crash the program. The layout of stacks depends on knowing exactly where each one starts, and MSP gives us that first anchor point. Without it, we couldn’t safely split the memory into separate stack spaces for different threads or tasks.\nSo overall, the MSP is essential because it defines the \"top\" of our stack region. All other stack pointers, like PSP, are calculated relative to it. That’s why the MSP value must be correct and known—it guarantees that the PSP points to a safe, non-overlapping address in RAM.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',1,5,'2025-06-25_17-39-12','We’d need to set a fixed size for each thread’s stack, like THREAD_STACK_SIZE, and then figure out where each stack starts by adding the correct offset to the end of the main stack. For example, the start of stack 1 would be MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE, stack 2 would be that plus another THREAD_STACK_SIZE, and so on. We’d use a formula like:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE\nThis way we can calculate all the stack start addresses at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',1,5,'2025-06-25_22-19-22','We’d need to set a fixed size for each thread’s stack, like THREAD_STACK_SIZE, and then figure out where each stack starts by adding the correct offset to the end of the main stack. For example, the start of stack 1 would be MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE, stack 2 would be that plus another THREAD_STACK_SIZE, and so on. We’d use a formula like:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE\nThis way we can calculate all the stack start addresses at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,1,'2025-06-25_22-41-13','A system call is a way for a user program to safely ask the OS to do something privileged, like printing or managing memory. It uses the SVC instruction to switch the CPU into kernel mode. In this lab, we used system calls to trigger custom functions (e.g., printing messages or starting a thread) by passing call numbers into the SVC handler.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,1,'2025-06-26_12-07-44','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a user program ask the operating system to do something it doesn’t have permission to do on its own—like writing to a file, accessing memory, or communicating with hardware. System calls are important because they act as a security barrier between user code and hardware. Instead of letting any program talk to the hardware directly (which could be dangerous or buggy), the OS controls those actions. So, when a program makes a system call, it’s basically saying, “Hey OS, please do this for me,” and the OS decides if it’s safe and allowed. This protects the system by making sure only trusted code in the kernel can actually interact with critical parts of the computer. The system call works like a gateway that gives limited, controlled access to powerful features.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,1,'2025-06-26_12-21-25','The program counter (PC) is the register that tells the CPU where to fetch the next instruction. By manually changing PC to hold the address of the print_continuously function, we redirected the CPU to begin executing that function immediately. This simulates a function call at the hardware level — instead of using a call instruction, we directly inserted the function\'s address into PC. As a result, the CPU started executing print_continuously as if it had been called normally, without ever returning. This shows how controlling PC allows us to change the flow of execution, which is a key concept for implementing thread context switches in an OS.',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,1,'2025-06-26_12-45-10','The program counter (PC) is the register that tells the CPU where to fetch the next instruction. By manually changing PC to hold the address of the print_continuously function, we redirected the CPU to begin executing that function immediately. This simulates a function call at the hardware level — instead of using a call instruction, we directly inserted the function\'s address into PC. As a result, the CPU started executing print_continuously as if it had been called normally, without ever returning. This shows how controlling PC allows us to change the flow of execution, which is a key concept for implementing thread context switches in an OS.',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,1,'2025-06-26_13-53-09','The program counter (PC) is the register that tells the CPU where to fetch the next instruction. By manually changing PC to hold the address of the print_continuously function, we redirected the CPU to begin executing that function immediately. This simulates a function call at the hardware level — instead of using a call instruction, we directly inserted the function\'s address into PC. As a result, the CPU started executing print_continuously as if it had been called normally, without ever returning. This shows how controlling PC allows us to change the flow of execution, which is a key concept for implementing thread context switches in an OS.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,1,'2025-06-26_16-35-49','The program counter (PC) is the register that tells the CPU where to fetch the next instruction. By manually changing PC to hold the address of the print_continuously function, we redirected the CPU to begin executing that function immediately. This simulates a function call at the hardware level — instead of using a call instruction, we directly inserted the function\'s address into PC. As a result, the CPU started executing print_continuously as if it had been called normally, without ever returning. This shows how controlling PC allows us to change the flow of execution, which is a key concept for implementing thread context switches in an OS.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,2,'2025-06-25_22-41-13','I set up the thread’s stack by manually pushing fake register values (like xPSR, PC, LR, R0–R12) onto it in the exact order the CPU expects. This tricks the CPU into thinking the thread was already running. Setting up the stack like this is important because it lets the CPU pop values into the correct registers and safely begin execution when the thread is \"resumed.\"\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,2,'2025-06-26_12-07-44','Before we can push register values onto a stack for a new thread, we need to make sure that the stack has a valid location in memory. This means we must first allocate memory space for the stack, usually by calculating where it should go based on the available stack region. Once we have that memory location, we can start placing (pushing) the values of the CPU registers onto it, like R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR. This creates a fake context so that when the CPU starts the thread, it pops those values off the stack and begins running the thread as if it had been running already. Allocating a valid memory location is critical because if we pushed values to the wrong spot, we could overwrite other important data or cause the program to crash. It’s like making sure you have a desk before putting papers on it—without the desk, your stuff just ends up on the floor. So, allocating stack memory is an essential step in setting up a thread’s environment properly.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,2,'2025-06-26_12-21-25','The __set_PSP function sets the Process Stack Pointer to the memory location we prepared for the thread’s stack. Without this step, the CPU wouldn’t know where to find the saved context when trying to run the thread. Instead, it might use an uninitialized or incorrect stack, causing it to pop invalid values into critical registers like PC or LR. This results in a hard fault — the CPU\'s way of signaling a serious error due to accessing invalid memory. Setting PSP ensures the CPU loads the correct context from the correct location, allowing the thread to start safely.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,2,'2025-06-26_12-45-10','When a function returns, it uses the value stored in the Link Register (LR) to know where to jump back to. In a typical function call, this return address is saved on the stack by the caller, ensuring a valid path back. But in our lab, we manually created the thread\'s stack and didn’t include a real return address — we just inserted a placeholder like 0xA into LR. The root cause of the hard fault is that the return address was never saved onto the thread’s stack, which means the CPU has no valid place to return to when the function ends. Instead, it uses the invalid LR value, tries to jump to that address, and crashes. This demonstrates why threads that don’t loop forever must have a proper return mechanism or be safely cleaned up by the OS — otherwise, the absence of a valid return address leads directly to a fault.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,2,'2025-06-26_13-53-09','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a user program request a service from the operating system. It acts as a controlled gateway between user code and hardware by switching the CPU from user mode to privileged kernel mode. This protects the system from faulty or malicious code because user programs can\'t directly access hardware or sensitive resources. Instead, they must go through system calls, which enforce access control. In our lab, we used the SVC instruction to trigger system calls, allowing user-level code to run privileged actions safely and in a structured way.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,2,'2025-06-26_16-35-49','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a user program request a service from the operating system. It acts as a controlled gateway between user code and hardware by switching the CPU from user mode to privileged kernel mode. This protects the system from faulty or malicious code because user programs can\'t directly access hardware or sensitive resources. Instead, they must go through system calls, which enforce access control. In our lab, we used the SVC instruction to trigger system calls, allowing user-level code to run privileged actions safely and in a structured way.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,3,'2025-06-25_22-41-13','Without calling __set_PSP, the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread’s stack is. Then, when the thread is started, it would pop values from the wrong location, leading to a crash (hard fault). The PSP must point to the stack we built so the CPU can restore the context correctly.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,3,'2025-06-26_12-07-44','Without calling __set_PSP, the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread’s stack is. Then, when the thread is started, it would pop values from the wrong location, leading to a crash (hard fault). The PSP must point to the stack we built so the CPU can restore the context correctly.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,3,'2025-06-26_12-21-25','If the thread function didn’t loop and returned instead, the CPU would try to return to the address in the Link Register (LR), which we manually set to a dummy value like 0xA. Since that’s not a valid return address, the CPU would jump to an invalid memory location and crash with a hard fault. In real systems, threads that finish either clean up and exit safely or are designed to run continuously. In our case, the infinite loop prevents the CPU from trying to return to an invalid address.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,3,'2025-06-26_13-53-09','Before setting up a thread’s stack, we first allocate a valid memory region to serve as the stack — in this lab, we used an array for that purpose. With a safe location ready, we then pushed values onto it that represent what the CPU expects when resuming execution: xPSR, PC (the function to run), LR, and general-purpose registers like R0–R12. This process prepares a fake CPU state so the processor can “resume” the thread as if it had been running. Storing registers on the stack is essential for preserving the thread’s context, and having a valid memory location ensures we aren’t writing to or reading from unsafe or undefined memory, which could lead to crashes or hard faults.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,3,'2025-06-26_16-35-49','Before setting up a thread’s stack, we first allocate a valid memory region to serve as the stack — in this lab, we used an array for that purpose. With a safe location ready, we then pushed values onto it that represent what the CPU expects when resuming execution: xPSR, PC (the function to run), LR, and general-purpose registers like R0–R12. This process prepares a fake CPU state so the processor can “resume” the thread as if it had been running. Storing registers on the stack is essential for preserving the thread’s context, and having a valid memory location ensures we aren’t writing to or reading from unsafe or undefined memory, which could lead to crashes or hard faults.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,4,'2025-06-25_22-41-13','If the thread didn’t loop and returned, the CPU would try to jump to whatever value we put in the LR slot (usually 0xA), which isn’t a valid address. This would cause a hard fault. The infinite loop prevents this by keeping the CPU in a known, safe state instead of trying to return somewhere invalid.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,4,'2025-06-26_12-07-44','If the thread didn’t loop and returned, the CPU would try to jump to whatever value we put in the LR slot (usually 0xA), which isn’t a valid address. This would cause a hard fault. The infinite loop prevents this by keeping the CPU in a known, safe state instead of trying to return somewhere invalid.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,4,'2025-06-26_13-53-09','By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is shared by the system and not meant for individual threads. However, in this lab, we prepared a separate stack in memory specifically for our thread. Since this new stack exists in a different memory location than MSP, we must explicitly switch to using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is designated for thread-level execution. The __set_PSP function updates the CPU to use our thread’s stack by pointing PSP to the top of the manually prepared context. Without switching to PSP, the CPU would continue using MSP and attempt to pop register values from the wrong location. This mismatch causes invalid data to be loaded into critical registers like PC or LR, leading to a hard fault. Switching to PSP ensures the CPU accesses the correct stack for the thread and avoids execution from uninitialized memory.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,4,'2025-06-26_16-35-49','By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is shared by the system and not meant for individual threads. However, in this lab, we prepared a separate stack in memory specifically for our thread. Since this new stack exists in a different memory location than MSP, we must explicitly switch to using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is designated for thread-level execution. The __set_PSP function updates the CPU to use our thread’s stack by pointing PSP to the top of the manually prepared context. Without switching to PSP, the CPU would continue using MSP and attempt to pop register values from the wrong location. This mismatch causes invalid data to be loaded into critical registers like PC or LR, leading to a hard fault. Switching to PSP ensures the CPU accesses the correct stack for the thread and avoids execution from uninitialized memory.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',2,5,'2025-06-26_13-53-09','By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is shared by the system and not meant for individual threads. However, in this lab, we prepared a separate stack in memory specifically for our thread. Since this new stack exists in a different memory location than MSP, we must explicitly switch to using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is designated for thread-level execution. The __set_PSP function updates the CPU to use our thread’s stack by pointing PSP to the top of the manually prepared context. Without switching to PSP, the CPU would continue using MSP and attempt to pop register values from the wrong location. This mismatch causes invalid data to be loaded into critical registers like PC or LR, leading to a hard fault. Switching to PSP ensures the CPU accesses the correct stack for the thread and avoids execution from uninitialized memory.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',2,5,'2025-06-26_16-35-49','If the thread function finishes executing and tries to return, the CPU will look for the return address in the Link Register (LR), as it does with any regular function call. Normally, this return address is placed on the stack automatically when a function is called, so that when it returns, the CPU knows where to go next. However, in our lab, we manually set up the thread\'s stack and did not store a valid return address on it. Instead, we pushed a placeholder (like 0xA) into the LR slot. Because there was no proper function call, the thread never saved a real return address, so the CPU has no valid instruction address to return to when the function ends. As a result, when the thread returns and the CPU uses the invalid LR value as a return address, it jumps to a location that doesn’t contain valid code. This causes a hard fault, which is the CPU’s way of handling serious errors like jumping to undefined memory. This is why thread functions either loop forever or must be explicitly managed by the OS when they end — otherwise, the missing return address leads to a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',3,1,'2025-06-27_19-16-43','Each .c file goes through a few steps: first, the preprocessor expands all macros and includes headers. Then the compiler turns each .c file into an object file with machine code. Once all the object files are ready, the linker combines them together. It connects functions and variables between files, and includes any system libraries we need (like for printf). The end result is one executable file that runs everything.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',3,2,'2025-06-27_19-16-43','You’re only supposed to #include header files (.h), not source files (.c). If you include a .c file, the compiler ends up copying that code into another file before compiling. This can cause the same code to be compiled twice, leading to errors and duplicate symbols. It also defeats the purpose of organizing code properly and makes it harder to find bugs.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',3,3,'2025-06-27_19-16-43','Call osKernelInitialize() to set up the kernel and prepare the stack space.\nWrite the thread function (it should be a void function with a void* argument).\nCreate the thread using osCreateThread() and pass it your function.\nStart the thread by calling osKernelStart() — this switches to the thread and runs it.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',3,4,'2025-06-27_19-16-43','The stack pool is a big block of memory reserved for thread stacks. Each thread needs its own private stack to store things like local variables and CPU registers. When the kernel creates a new thread, it carves out a section of the stack pool for it. In future labs, the kernel can repeat this process for multiple threads so they all have their own separate stacks to run safely.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',3,5,'2025-06-27_19-16-43','The kernel is where all the low-level stuff happens: stack setup, system calls, and scheduling. The user just writes the thread functions and uses kernel functions like osCreateThread() and osKernelStart(). From the user\'s perspective, this is helpful because they don’t need to deal with hardware or CPU details. It keeps things clean and safe — the kernel handles the hard parts and the user just uses its API.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',4,1,'2025-07-29_10-53-05','A queue can implement round-robin scheduling by always taking the first thread in line to run, and when it yields, putting it back at the end. This automatically cycles through all threads in order without needing index counters or modulo math. One benefit is that it’s very easy to add or remove threads while the system is running, because queues handle dynamic resizing better than arrays. It also makes the scheduling code straightforward: you just pop from the front and push to the back. This approach is often used in larger operating systems where threads are created and destroyed frequently.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',4,2,'2025-07-29_10-53-05','PendSV_Handler performs the actual context switch by saving the current thread’s registers onto its stack, updating the PSP value in its thread control block, then selecting the next thread and loading its PSP and registers. When it finishes, the CPU is running exactly where that new thread left off. Because PendSV is a “pendable” interrupt that runs after SVC finishes, it ensures the SVC handler’s stack frame is cleaned up first, preventing corrupted local variables and keeping the stack balanced. This separation is why context switching works reliably on Cortex-M processors.\n',0,'s25'),('lramloga',4,3,'2025-07-29_10-53-05','If the scheduler always runs the highest priority thread, lower-priority ones can be starved and never execute. In our cooperative OS, where threads only switch when they yield, a high-priority thread could completely block every other task unless it’s carefully written to yield often. This defeats the purpose of having multiple threads. Round-robin avoids this by sharing CPU time evenly so every thread runs in turn. For a simple embedded OS without timers or complex scheduling, fairness and predictability are more important than priority levels.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',4,4,'2025-07-29_10-53-05','Using C functions like osSched and SVC_Handler_Main keeps the code much easier to write and debug compared to pure assembly. You can use normal C variables, reuse code, and make changes without rewriting low-level register handling. The tradeoff is that C adds some overhead and gives you less direct control of the exact instructions, so the code might be slightly slower. In production kernels, critical parts like context saving are often hand-written in assembly for maximum speed. For our lab OS, using C is a good balance between simplicity and functionality, especially while learning the concepts.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',4,5,'2025-07-29_10-53-05','If there’s only one thread and it calls osYield(), the OS will still run correctly but the scheduler will just save and restore the same stack pointer. With no other threads to switch to, the system effectively behaves like a single-threaded program. You would still see the thread’s output, but there would be no visible context switching. This shows that the kernel’s scheduling mechanism works even if there’s just one thread, but it also highlights that multitasking only makes a difference when multiple threads share the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',5,1,'2025-07-30_19-53-43','The user passes a void* pointer when creating a thread, often pointing to a struct or variable. The OS takes this pointer and places it in the R0 register during stack initialization because R0 is where function arguments are passed in the ARM ABI. When the thread starts, its function receives the pointer as the arg parameter. The user casts it to the correct type and dereferences it to access data. This works because the Cortex-M calling convention expects the first argument in R0, so the OS sets up the stack exactly as if the function had been called normally.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',5,2,'2025-07-30_19-53-43','You can’t call osYield inside PendSV, SVC, or SysTick handlers because they are already part of the context-switching mechanism. Calling it again would trigger another SVC or PendSV while one is already running, causing nested context switches. This can corrupt the stack pointers or lose the current thread state entirely. The OS depends on these handlers running to completion without interruption. If you do it, the system risks entering an unrecoverable state or hard faulting.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',5,3,'2025-07-30_19-53-43','Reducing the OS clock to 0.1 ms increases the resolution of timing and allows faster task switching. This is good for applications needing very precise real-time responses. However, it means the SysTick interrupt fires ten times more often, which increases overhead and reduces CPU time available for threads. Power consumption also goes up due to the higher interrupt rate. The 1 ms tick is a common compromise between responsiveness and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',5,4,'2025-07-30_19-53-43','In cooperative multitasking, the user must explicitly call osYield to give up CPU time, so the OS just switches when asked. In preemptive multitasking, the OS uses SysTick to force a context switch after a timeslice, even if the thread never yields. From the user’s perspective, cooperative requires careful placement of yields, while preemptive requires no extra code but can interrupt threads at any time. The OS must track and reset runtimes in preemptive mode, while cooperative only rotates threads on yield. Preemptive scheduling adds timing logic, while cooperative is simpler but relies on user discipline.\n',1,'s25'),('lramloga',5,5,'2025-07-30_19-53-43','Mixing yielding and time-based preemption works because both ultimately trigger the same context-switch mechanism. A yielding thread voluntarily calls osYield, which resets its runtime and lets the scheduler pick the next thread. A preemptive thread runs until its runtime expires and SysTick forces a switch. The OS maintains separate logic: yield-triggered switches reset the timeslice, and SysTick-triggered switches happen when the runtime hits zero. Both mechanisms can coexist because they share the same PendSV-based context switch routine.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,1,'2025-06-09_16-08-33','The HAL or hardware abstraction layer is a simplified interface purpose made for interacting with low-level hardware peripherals. Some examples of said peripherals are UART, SPI, I2C, GPIOs, timers, and more. This is used in a general embedded project because writing embedded code directly in hardware registers is very error-prone and can be hardware specific. As such, the HAL provides C functions that can help developers decrease development time, reduce errors, and focus on system logic instead of low-level logic.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,1,'2025-06-09_16-19-07','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a standardized software interface that allows developers to interact with microcontroller hardware without needing to directly access hardware registers. It provides convenient C functions to configure and use peripherals like UART, SPI, I2C, GPIOs, ADCs, and timers. HAL is used in embedded projects to simplify development, reduce errors, and avoid writing device-specific code.\nUsing HAL also improves code portability, since the same application code can often be reused across different microcontroller platforms with minimal changes. Additionally, HAL simplifies collaboration among developers, allowing some team members to focus on application logic while others manage hardware configurations.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,1,'2025-06-09_16-25-21','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a standardized software interface that allows developers to interact with microcontroller hardware without needing to directly access hardware registers. It provides convenient C functions to configure and use peripherals like UART, SPI, I2C, GPIOs, ADCs, and timers. HAL is used in embedded projects to simplify development, reduce errors, and avoid writing device-specific code.\nUsing HAL also improves code portability, since the same application code can often be reused across different microcontroller platforms with minimal changes. Additionally, HAL simplifies collaboration among developers, allowing some team members to focus on application logic while others manage hardware configurations.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,1,'2025-06-09_16-30-34','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a standardized software interface that allows developers to interact with microcontroller hardware without needing to directly access hardware registers. It provides convenient C functions to configure and use peripherals like UART, SPI, I2C, GPIOs, ADCs, and timers. HAL is used in embedded projects to simplify development, reduce errors, and avoid writing device-specific code.\nUsing HAL also improves code portability, since the same application code can often be reused across different microcontroller platforms with minimal changes. Additionally, HAL simplifies collaboration among developers, allowing some team members to focus on application logic while others manage hardware configurations.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,1,'2025-06-09_16-35-25','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a standardized software interface that allows developers to interact with microcontroller hardware without needing to directly access hardware registers. It provides convenient C functions to configure and use peripherals like UART, SPI, I2C, GPIOs, ADCs, and timers. HAL is used in embedded projects to simplify development, reduce errors, and avoid writing device-specific code.\nUsing HAL also improves code portability, since the same application code can often be reused across different microcontroller platforms with minimal changes. Additionally, HAL simplifies collaboration among developers, allowing some team members to focus on application logic while others manage hardware configurations.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,1,'2025-06-09_16-40-55','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a standardized software interface that allows developers to interact with microcontroller hardware without needing to directly access hardware registers. It provides convenient C functions to configure and use peripherals like UART, SPI, I2C, GPIOs, ADCs, and timers. HAL is used in embedded projects to simplify development, reduce errors, and avoid writing device-specific code.\nUsing HAL also improves code portability, since the same application code can often be reused across different microcontroller platforms with minimal changes. Additionally, HAL simplifies collaboration among developers, allowing some team members to focus on application logic while others manage hardware configurations.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,2,'2025-06-09_16-08-33','The __io_putchar function is a low-level write function used by printf() to send characters. This is needed in embedded systems so that printf() can call the function to output one character at a time. In the lab, we used the function to send characters one at a time over UART2, which printf would not be able to do (as it would have nowhere to send its output) without __io_putchar.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',1,2,'2025-06-09_16-19-07','The __io_putchar function is a low-level implementation that printf() uses to send characters one at a time to an output device. In embedded systems, this function must be defined to direct output to a specific peripheral, such as a UART port. Without it, printf() would have no default output destination.\nThis function is important because it allows printf() to work across different hardware platforms—by changing only __io_putchar, the developer can redirect output to UART, USB, an LCD, or another interface. It provides an abstraction that decouples the standard I/O functions from specific hardware details.\nFor example, if we move our program to a board with a different UART setup, redefining __io_putchar ensures all printf() statements still work without modifying the main application code.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,2,'2025-06-09_16-25-21','The __io_putchar function is a low-level implementation that printf() uses to send characters one at a time to an output device. In embedded systems, this function must be defined to direct output to a specific peripheral, such as a UART port. Without it, printf() would have no default output destination.\nThis function is important because it allows printf() to work across different hardware platforms—by changing only __io_putchar, the developer can redirect output to UART, USB, an LCD, or another interface. It provides an abstraction that decouples the standard I/O functions from specific hardware details.\nFor example, if we move our program to a board with a different UART setup, redefining __io_putchar ensures all printf() statements still work without modifying the main application code.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,2,'2025-06-09_16-30-34','The __io_putchar function is a low-level implementation that printf() uses to send characters one at a time to an output device. In embedded systems, this function must be defined to direct output to a specific peripheral, such as a UART port. Without it, printf() would have no default output destination.\nThis function is important because it allows printf() to work across different hardware platforms—by changing only __io_putchar, the developer can redirect output to UART, USB, an LCD, or another interface. It provides an abstraction that decouples the standard I/O functions from specific hardware details.\nFor example, if we move our program to a board with a different UART setup, redefining __io_putchar ensures all printf() statements still work without modifying the main application code.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,2,'2025-06-09_16-35-25','The __io_putchar function is a low-level implementation that printf() uses to send characters one at a time to an output device. In embedded systems, this function must be defined to direct output to a specific peripheral, such as a UART port. Without it, printf() would have no default output destination.\nThis function is important because it allows printf() to work across different hardware platforms—by changing only __io_putchar, the developer can redirect output to UART, USB, an LCD, or another interface. It provides an abstraction that decouples the standard I/O functions from specific hardware details.\nFor example, if we move our program to a board with a different UART setup, redefining __io_putchar ensures all printf() statements still work without modifying the main application code.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,2,'2025-06-09_16-40-55','The __io_putchar function is a low-level implementation that printf() uses to send characters one at a time to an output device. In embedded systems, this function must be defined to direct output to a specific peripheral, such as a UART port. Without it, printf() would have no default output destination.\nThis function is important because it allows printf() to work across different hardware platforms—by changing only __io_putchar, the developer can redirect output to UART, USB, an LCD, or another interface. It provides an abstraction that decouples the standard I/O functions from specific hardware details.\nFor example, if we move our program to a board with a different UART setup, redefining __io_putchar ensures all printf() statements still work without modifying the main application code.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,3,'2025-06-09_16-08-33','The debugger was used instead of printf statements because printf cannot reliably print the values of registers such as SP, MSP, PSP during runtime operation. As such, breaks were needed in the code using the debugging tool to see the values of the registers as the code was stepped through.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,3,'2025-06-09_16-19-07','We used the debugger in this lab because printf() cannot access or print the values of hardware registers like SP, MSP, and PSP in real-time. These registers are part of the processor\'s core and not directly accessible by high-level functions.\nThe debugger allows us to pause program execution, inspect register values, and step through code line by line. This gives us precise visibility into what the processor is doing, which is not possible with printf() alone. Furthermore, using a debugger avoids the risk of interfering with timing-critical or low-level code, which can happen if printf() adds too much overhead.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',1,3,'2025-06-09_16-25-21','We used the debugger in this lab because printf() cannot access or print the values of hardware registers like SP, MSP, and PSP in real-time. These registers are part of the processor\'s core and not directly accessible by high-level functions.\nThe debugger allows us to pause program execution, inspect register values, and step through code line by line. This gives us precise visibility into what the processor is doing, which is not possible with printf() alone. Furthermore, using a debugger avoids the risk of interfering with timing-critical or low-level code, which can happen if printf() adds too much overhead.\nIn conclusion, the debugger was used instead of printf statements because printf cannot reliably print the values of registers such as SP, MSP, PSP during runtime operation. As such, breaks were needed in the code using the debugging tool to see the values of the registers as the code was stepped through.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',1,3,'2025-06-09_16-30-34','The debugger was used instead of printf statements because printf cannot reliably print the values of registers such as SP, MSP, PSP during runtime operation. As such, breaks were needed in the code using the debugging tool to see the values of the registers as the code was stepped through.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',1,3,'2025-06-09_16-35-25','The debugger was used instead of printf statements because printf cannot reliably print the values of registers such as SP, MSP, PSP during runtime operation. As such, breaks were needed in the code using the debugging tool to see the values of the registers as the code was stepped through.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',1,3,'2025-06-09_16-40-55','The debugger was used instead of printf statements because printf cannot reliably print the values of registers such as SP, MSP, PSP during runtime operation. As such, breaks were needed in the code using the debugging tool to see the values of the registers as the code was stepped through.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,4,'2025-06-09_16-08-33','First, the initial stack pointer, MSP stored at 0x0 was obtained and casted to an integer. We got a new value for PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 bytes from the MSP because the stack grows downward in memory. The MSP was needed as it marks the location of the top of the stack. With this value, we allocate memory below it safely without an overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,4,'2025-06-09_16-19-07','Once we retrieved the initial Main Stack Pointer value (MSP_init_val) from address 0x0, we allocated a new region for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by subtracting 0x400 bytes from it. This works because the stack in ARM Cortex-M grows downward in memory, so subtracting allocates space without overwriting what\'s already above it.\nKnowing the MSP’s initial location is critical because it defines the top of the available stack memory. Without this reference point, we could unknowingly assign the PSP to an address that overlaps with other memory, leading to stack corruption. This method ensures safe, manual allocation of stack space in RAM.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,4,'2025-06-09_16-25-21','Once we retrieved the initial Main Stack Pointer value (MSP_init_val) from address 0x0, we allocated a new region for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by subtracting 0x400 bytes from it. This works because the stack in ARM Cortex-M grows downward in memory, so subtracting allocates space without overwriting what\'s already above it.\nKnowing the MSP’s initial location is critical because it defines the top of the available stack memory. Without this reference point, we could unknowingly assign the PSP to an address that overlaps with other memory, leading to stack corruption. This method ensures safe, manual allocation of stack space in RAM.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,4,'2025-06-09_16-30-34','Once we retrieved the initial Main Stack Pointer value (MSP_init_val) from address 0x0, we allocated a new region for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by subtracting 0x400 bytes from it. This works because the stack in ARM Cortex-M grows downward in memory, so subtracting allocates space without overwriting what\'s already above it.\nKnowing the MSP’s initial location is critical because it defines the top of the available stack memory. Without this reference point, we could unknowingly assign the PSP to an address that overlaps with other memory, leading to stack corruption. This method ensures safe, manual allocation of stack space in RAM.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,4,'2025-06-09_16-35-25','Once we retrieved the initial Main Stack Pointer value (MSP_init_val) from address 0x0, we allocated a new region for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by subtracting 0x400 bytes from it. This works because the stack in ARM Cortex-M grows downward in memory, so subtracting allocates space without overwriting what\'s already above it.\nKnowing the MSP’s initial location is critical because it defines the top of the available stack memory. Without this reference point, we could unknowingly assign the PSP to an address that overlaps with other memory, leading to stack corruption. This method ensures safe, manual allocation of stack space in RAM.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,4,'2025-06-09_16-40-55','Once we retrieved the initial Main Stack Pointer value (MSP_init_val) from address 0x0, we allocated a new region for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by subtracting 0x400 bytes from it. This works because the stack in ARM Cortex-M grows downward in memory, so subtracting allocates space without overwriting what\'s already above it.\nKnowing the MSP’s initial location is critical because it defines the top of the available stack memory. Without this reference point, we could unknowingly assign the PSP to an address that overlaps with other memory, leading to stack corruption. This method ensures safe, manual allocation of stack space in RAM.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,5,'2025-06-09_16-08-33','To allocate multiple new stacks for use in multiple threads, we must compute the starting address of each stack, giving them enough space each in the stack per thread. Once the MSP is known, space is divided for the main and each thread stack. The main stack would start at the top of the available stack memory and extend down to the max stack size. After that, thread stacks would be placed adjacent to each other, allocating as many as known at compile time. This is done so that each stack occupies a predictable region in memory and accidental stack collisions are not possible.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',1,5,'2025-06-09_16-19-07','To allocate multiple new stacks, we would need to divide a memory block into multiple, non-overlapping regions—one for each thread. Starting from the initial MSP value, we would compute the start of each thread\'s stack by subtracting a fixed offset (e.g., 0x400 bytes) per thread.\nFor example, if thread 1’s stack starts at PSP1 = MSP_init_val - 0x400, then thread 2’s could be PSP2 = MSP_init_val - 0x800, and so on. This offset-based approach ensures each thread stack is aligned, sized correctly, and does not overlap with others.\nAdditionally, stack sizes should be aligned to 8 or 16 bytes (depending on the platform) to meet processor alignment requirements and avoid memory faults.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,5,'2025-06-09_16-25-21','To allocate multiple new stacks, we would need to divide a memory block into multiple, non-overlapping regions—one for each thread. Starting from the initial MSP value, we would compute the start of each thread\'s stack by subtracting a fixed offset (e.g., 0x400 bytes) per thread.\nFor example, if thread 1’s stack starts at PSP1 = MSP_init_val - 0x400, then thread 2’s could be PSP2 = MSP_init_val - 0x800, and so on. This offset-based approach ensures each thread stack is aligned, sized correctly, and does not overlap with others.\nAdditionally, stack sizes should be aligned to 8 or 16 bytes (depending on the platform) to meet processor alignment requirements and avoid memory faults.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,5,'2025-06-09_16-30-34','To allocate multiple new stacks, we would need to divide a memory block into multiple, non-overlapping regions—one for each thread. Starting from the initial MSP value, we would compute the start of each thread\'s stack by subtracting a fixed offset (e.g., 0x400 bytes) per thread.\nFor example, if thread 1’s stack starts at PSP1 = MSP_init_val - 0x400, then thread 2’s could be PSP2 = MSP_init_val - 0x800, and so on. This offset-based approach ensures each thread stack is aligned, sized correctly, and does not overlap with others.\nAdditionally, stack sizes should be aligned to 8 or 16 bytes (depending on the platform) to meet processor alignment requirements and avoid memory faults.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,5,'2025-06-09_16-35-25','To allocate multiple new stacks, we would need to divide a memory block into multiple, non-overlapping regions—one for each thread. Starting from the initial MSP value, we would compute the start of each thread\'s stack by subtracting a fixed offset (e.g., 0x400 bytes) per thread.\nFor example, if thread 1’s stack starts at PSP1 = MSP_init_val - 0x400, then thread 2’s could be PSP2 = MSP_init_val - 0x800, and so on. This offset-based approach ensures each thread stack is aligned, sized correctly, and does not overlap with others.\nAdditionally, stack sizes should be aligned to 8 or 16 bytes (depending on the platform) to meet processor alignment requirements and avoid memory faults.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',1,5,'2025-06-09_16-40-55','To allocate multiple new stacks, we would need to divide a memory block into multiple, non-overlapping regions—one for each thread. Starting from the initial MSP value, we would compute the start of each thread\'s stack by subtracting a fixed offset (e.g., 0x400 bytes) per thread.\nFor example, if thread 1’s stack starts at PSP1 = MSP_init_val - 0x400, then thread 2’s could be PSP2 = MSP_init_val - 0x800, and so on. This offset-based approach ensures each thread stack is aligned, sized correctly, and does not overlap with others.\nAdditionally, stack sizes should be aligned to 8 or 16 bytes (depending on the platform) to meet processor alignment requirements and avoid memory faults.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,1,'2025-06-23_11-27-56','Changing the Program Counter (PC) allows the programmer to control what the processor does next. Normally, the PC is automatically changed by the processor as the clock ticks, pointing to the next instruction to execute; however, in this lab, we changed the PC ourselves to hold the function pointer to our print continuously function.\nThe address of the print continuously function was passed as an argument to the jumpAssembly function which put it into the PC. As such, the PC points to the print continuously function and the processor executes it next.\nThis function had an infinite loop that printed “Hello PC!” to the console. As such, the “Hello, PC!” message was printed on repeat infinitely. As mentioned in the lab, you would never normally change the PC directly in a real-world program, but in this lab it was done to show how the PC register works.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,1,'2025-06-23_16-06-27','Changing the Program Counter (PC) allows the programmer to control what the processor does next. Normally, the PC is automatically changed by the processor as the clock ticks, pointing to the next instruction to execute; however, in this lab, we changed the PC ourselves to hold the function pointer to our print continuously function.\nThe address of the print continuously function was passed as an argument to the jumpAssembly function which put it into the PC. As such, the PC points to the print continuously function and the processor executes it next.\nThis function had an infinite loop that printed “Hello PC!” to the console. As such, the “Hello, PC!” message was printed on repeat infinitely. As mentioned in the lab, you would never normally change the PC directly in a real-world program, but in this lab it was done to show how the PC register works.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,1,'2025-06-23_16-57-50','Changing the Program Counter (PC) allows the programmer to control what the processor does next. Normally, the PC is automatically changed by the processor as the clock ticks, pointing to the next instruction to execute; however, in this lab, we changed the PC ourselves to hold the function pointer to our print continuously function.\nThe address of the print continuously function was passed as an argument to the jumpAssembly function which put it into the PC. As such, the PC points to the print continuously function and the processor executes it next.\nThis function had an infinite loop that printed “Hello PC!” to the console. As such, the “Hello, PC!” message was printed on repeat infinitely. As mentioned in the lab, you would never normally change the PC directly in a real-world program, but in this lab it was done to show how the PC register works.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,2,'2025-06-23_11-27-56','A system call is a method for user programs to request access to hardware from the operating system. For example, if you are making a program that wants access to read a file, save a file, or access hardware such as a peripheral or memory, you will need to make a system call. The program is not privileged to directly access and control these things as it is in “user mode”. This is a restricted mode that ensures the safety of the system.\nAs such, the program must ask the OS to do these things with a request called a system call. The system call will alert the OS, who runs in kernel mode with full control to perform the task. In the lab, we used the Supervisor Call (SVC) to make a system call which alerts the operating system that the program needs something, and the system can respond by handling the request with the SVC_Handler function.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',2,2,'2025-06-23_16-06-27','A system call is a method for user programs to request access to hardware from the operating system. For example, if you are making a program that wants access to read a file, save a file, or access hardware such as a peripheral or memory, you will need to make a system call. The program is not privileged to directly access and control these things as it is in “user mode”. This is a restricted mode that ensures the safety of the system. As such, the program must ask the OS to do these things with a request called a system call.\nAt the hardware level, system calls are typically invoked using a special instruction—in our case, the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction. This instruction causes the processor to trap into a special mode called handler mode, switching from user mode to kernel mode. This transition allows the processor to safely hand over control to the operating system so it can handle the request on behalf of the user program. Essentially, the SVC instruction triggers an interrupt, and the processor jumps to a predefined SVC handler routine to process the system call.\nThis mechanism is similar in appearance to a function call, but function calls stay within the same privilege level and do not cause any context switches or changes in access permissions. In contrast, system calls involve entering a protected mode of execution, isolating user programs from directly manipulating system resources. In our lab, calling SVC caused the CPU to invoke the SVC_Handler function, which acted as the interface between the user code and our simulated kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',2,2,'2025-06-23_16-57-50','A system call is a method for user programs to request access to hardware or privileged resources from the operating system. For example, if a program wants to read or write a file, access a peripheral, or allocate memory, it cannot do so directly because it runs in user mode, a restricted execution level designed to protect system stability and security. To perform these operations, the program must make a system call, which is a formal request for the OS—running in kernel mode—to carry out the action on its behalf.\nImportantly, a system call is a special kind of function call. Like a regular function call, it transfers control from one part of the code to another and may pass arguments and receive a return value. However, what makes a system call distinct is that it also triggers a switch in privilege level. In the Cortex-M4, this is achieved using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction. When the processor encounters this instruction, it performs an exception—similar to an interrupt—and branches to the SVC_Handler function in kernel mode. This allows the processor to safely handle the request with elevated permissions, while isolating the user program from direct hardware access.\nIn our lab, this mechanism was illustrated by triggering a system call with the SVC instruction, which directed the processor to the SVC_Handler to carry out the requested service. While system calls are more secure and controlled than standard function calls, they still follow the same basic structure: they are invoked by name or number, they execute a defined routine, and they return control to the caller. Thus, even though they involve low-level hardware mechanisms and privilege transitions, they are still fundamentally function calls—just ones that cross the user-kernel boundary.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,3,'2025-06-23_11-27-56','Each thread gets its own stack to remember the values of its registers and what instruction its executing in the PC. To set up the stack, we must load the stack with all the values it needs to start. As such, we push the register values such as xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R1, R0 and R11 down to R4 onto the stack in this specific order to provide the thread all the information it needs when it starts up.\nIf threads need to be switched between, the CPU must store and restore each threads state which can be done using the stack. These values such as registers can be pushed onto the stack to store important information while paused and then popped back so the thread can pick back up from where it left off.\nIn the lab, we pushed the registers xPSR, PC, LR and others listed above onto the thread’s stack. This was needed as we were switching to a new thread and the processor needs to load the threads registers from the stack to provide context for the thread. Without this context, the thread would not be able to continue execution as it would have too little information.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',2,3,'2025-06-23_16-06-27','Each thread gets its own stack to remember the values of its registers and what instruction it’s executing in the PC. To set up the stack, we must load the stack with all the values it needs to start. As such, we push the register values such as xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R1, R0 and R11 down to R4 onto the stack in this specific order to provide the thread all the information it needs when it starts up. If threads need to be switched between, the CPU must store and restore each thread\'s state, which can be done using the stack. These values, such as registers, can be pushed onto the stack to store important information while paused and then popped back so the thread can pick back up from where it left off.\nHowever, before registers can be written, the stack itself must be properly allocated in memory. This means that each thread needs a reserved block of memory that will act as its stack, typically created as an array or a section of static memory. The top of this memory block is used to initialize the thread’s stack pointer, which tells the processor where the first value will be pushed. If this memory region is not correctly defined or aligned, the processor might overwrite other data or access invalid memory, leading to hard faults. In our lab, we ensured the thread\'s stack was prepared by statically assigning memory and placing the register values in the right order, so the thread could begin with all the required context in place.\nThis setup is essential because when the processor switches context to a thread, it expects a specific layout on the stack that matches the exception return mechanism. If that layout is incomplete or points to garbage memory, the processor cannot correctly restore the thread\'s context. Therefore, both stack allocation and register value setup must be done carefully to ensure reliable thread startup and switching.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,3,'2025-06-23_16-57-50','Each thread gets its own stack to remember the values of its registers and what instruction it’s executing in the PC. To set up the stack, we must load the stack with all the values it needs to start. As such, we push the register values such as xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R1, R0 and R11 down to R4 onto the stack in this specific order to provide the thread all the information it needs when it starts up. If threads need to be switched between, the CPU must store and restore each thread\'s state, which can be done using the stack. These values, such as registers, can be pushed onto the stack to store important information while paused and then popped back so the thread can pick back up from where it left off.\nHowever, before registers can be written, the stack itself must be properly allocated in memory. This means that each thread needs a reserved block of memory that will act as its stack, typically created as an array or a section of static memory. The top of this memory block is used to initialize the thread’s stack pointer, which tells the processor where the first value will be pushed. If this memory region is not correctly defined or aligned, the processor might overwrite other data or access invalid memory, leading to hard faults. In our lab, we ensured the thread\'s stack was prepared by statically assigning memory and placing the register values in the right order, so the thread could begin with all the required context in place.\nThis setup is essential because when the processor switches context to a thread, it expects a specific layout on the stack that matches the exception return mechanism. If that layout is incomplete or points to garbage memory, the processor cannot correctly restore the thread\'s context. Therefore, both stack allocation and register value setup must be done carefully to ensure reliable thread startup and switching.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,4,'2025-06-23_11-27-56','This is done because each thread needs a stack pointer to tell the processor where in memory to find the thread’s stack. In the lab, PSP is the pointer for the thread’s stack which points to the location in memory where the stack can be found.\nIf this was not set, the processor would not know where to find the thread’s stack and would crash in a hard fault when we try to run the thread as it would be trying to access invalid memory.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',2,4,'2025-06-23_16-06-27','This is done because each thread needs a stack pointer to tell the processor where in memory to find the thread’s stack. In the lab, PSP is the pointer for the thread’s stack which points to the location in memory where the stack can be found. If this was not set, the processor would not know where to find the thread’s stack and would crash in a hard fault when we try to run the thread, as it would be trying to access invalid memory.\nHowever, simply assigning a value to the PSP isn’t enough—the CPU must also actively switch from using the default stack pointer (MSP), which it uses by default during reset and interrupt handling, to using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) during thread execution. The Cortex-M4 processor maintains two stack pointers: MSP (Main Stack Pointer) and PSP (Process Stack Pointer). While the MSP is used in handler mode (e.g., during exceptions), the PSP is used when the CPU is running in thread mode, which is the context we use for application threads.\nThe __set_PSP function does more than assign a stack address; it prepares the CPU to use that address as the new active stack pointer for thread-level code. But unless we also configure the CONTROL register to tell the CPU to switch from MSP to PSP, the processor will continue using the MSP—even if PSP is set—which leads to memory corruption or accessing uninitialized memory. In the lab, failing to switch the active stack pointer meant the processor would execute the thread using the wrong stack, which ultimately caused a hard fault when it tried to access invalid stack memory.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,4,'2025-06-23_16-57-50','This is done because each thread needs a stack pointer to tell the processor where in memory to find the thread’s stack. In the lab, PSP is the pointer for the thread’s stack which points to the location in memory where the stack can be found. If this was not set, the processor would not know where to find the thread’s stack and would crash in a hard fault when we try to run the thread, as it would be trying to access invalid memory.\nHowever, simply assigning a value to the PSP isn’t enough—the CPU must also actively switch from using the default stack pointer (MSP), which it uses by default during reset and interrupt handling, to using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) during thread execution. The Cortex-M4 processor maintains two stack pointers: MSP (Main Stack Pointer) and PSP (Process Stack Pointer). While the MSP is used in handler mode (e.g., during exceptions), the PSP is used when the CPU is running in thread mode, which is the context we use for application threads.\nThe __set_PSP function does more than assign a stack address; it prepares the CPU to use that address as the new active stack pointer for thread-level code. But unless we also configure the CONTROL register to tell the CPU to switch from MSP to PSP, the processor will continue using the MSP—even if PSP is set—which leads to memory corruption or accessing uninitialized memory. In the lab, failing to switch the active stack pointer meant the processor would execute the thread using the wrong stack, which ultimately caused a hard fault when it tried to access invalid stack memory.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,5,'2025-06-23_11-27-56','In a real-world application, a well-configured system would have the OS handle cleaning up the computer’s resources when a thread is finished; however, our simplified lab model does not have these systems in place.\nAs such, if our thread did not loop infinitely and instead returned, the Cortex M4 would expect a valid return address found on the stack. Since our simplified model does not include this, the processor will not know where to return once the thread completes its execution. This will cause the Cortex M4 to try to load an invalid address onto the PC, leading to the processor attempting to execute a random, invalid instruction which will result in a hard fault.\nAs mentioned above, in a well-defined OS, the kernel would manage this, but since we do not have proper handling, the system would crash. This is because the return address is missing from the stack which causes the processor to encounter an invalid memory address upon trying to return after a thread has completed execution.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,5,'2025-06-23_16-06-27','In a real-world application, a well-configured system would have the OS handle cleaning up the computer’s resources when a thread is finished; however, our simplified lab model does not have these systems in place.\nAs such, if our thread did not loop infinitely and instead returned, the Cortex M4 would expect a valid return address found on the stack. Since our simplified model does not include this, the processor will not know where to return once the thread completes its execution. This will cause the Cortex M4 to try to load an invalid address onto the PC, leading to the processor attempting to execute a random, invalid instruction which will result in a hard fault.\nAs mentioned above, in a well-defined OS, the kernel would manage this, but since we do not have proper handling, the system would crash. This is because the return address is missing from the stack which causes the processor to encounter an invalid memory address upon trying to return after a thread has completed execution.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',2,5,'2025-06-23_16-57-50','In a real-world application, a well-configured system would have the OS handle cleaning up the computer’s resources when a thread is finished; however, our simplified lab model does not have these systems in place.\nAs such, if our thread did not loop infinitely and instead returned, the Cortex M4 would expect a valid return address found on the stack. Since our simplified model does not include this, the processor will not know where to return once the thread completes its execution. This will cause the Cortex M4 to try to load an invalid address onto the PC, leading to the processor attempting to execute a random, invalid instruction which will result in a hard fault.\nAs mentioned above, in a well-defined OS, the kernel would manage this, but since we do not have proper handling, the system would crash. This is because the return address is missing from the stack which causes the processor to encounter an invalid memory address upon trying to return after a thread has completed execution.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',3,1,'2025-06-26_17-45-41','The process by which multiple *.c files get complied into a single executable can be broken into four steps defined as preprocessing, compilation, linking, and generating the final executable. First, preprocessing prepares the source code for compilation by handling all directives. These include, #define substitution of macros and #include to insert the contents of header files as well as other conditional compilation directives like #ifdef or #ifndef. Preprocessing expands the source code by replacing macros and including all included files so that the compiler has one cohesive, error free source code input.\nSecond, compilation involves taking each .c file and converting them into a .o object file with machine code. The compiler will check each file for syntax errors and will translate the variables and functions in it into a low-level code with placeholders for references and external definitions. These placeholders are dealt with by the linker in the next step in which the linker combines these object files. It is the linker’s role to connect placeholders to actual definitions and integrate any system libraries to ensure all parts of the program can work together.\nLast, the unified executable file is generated and can be run by the operating system. In the lab, we used a modular design to break up our code into main.c, kernel.c, and kernel.h files. This approach was adapted because it provides higher modularity, simplifying updates to individual parts of code while maintaining a structured system.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',3,1,'2025-06-26_19-06-04','The process by which multiple *.c files get complied into a single executable can be broken into four steps defined as preprocessing, compilation, linking, and generating the final executable. First, preprocessing prepares the source code for compilation by handling all directives. These include, #define substitution of macros and #include to insert the contents of header files as well as other conditional compilation directives like #ifdef or #ifndef. Preprocessing expands the source code by replacing macros and including all included files so that the compiler has one cohesive, error free source code input.\nSecond, compilation involves taking each .c file and converting them into a .o object file with machine code. The compiler will check each file for syntax errors and will translate the variables and functions in it into a low-level code with placeholders for references and external definitions. These placeholders are dealt with by the linker in the next step in which the linker combines these object files. It is the linker’s role to connect placeholders to actual definitions and integrate any system libraries to ensure all parts of the program can work together.\nLast, the unified executable file is generated and can be run by the operating system. In the lab, we used a modular design to break up our code into main.c, kernel.c, and kernel.h files. This approach was adapted because it provides higher modularity, simplifying updates to individual parts of code while maintaining a structured system.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',3,2,'2025-06-26_17-45-41','You cannot include another .c file in a .c file as the #include function copies the contents of the file during preprocessing which can result in duplicate functions and break modularity. If multiple files include the same .c file, code within that .c file will appear in multiple places of the final executable, resulting in duplicate functions. Additionally, .c files are complied independently into object files in C, but by including nested .c files, this modularity is broken which requires everything to be recompiled if something is changed.\nInstead, we include .h files that include only the function declarations so that the linker can match the declarations in the .h file to the definitions in the compiled .c files. Directly including .c files breaks this layer of separation and can cause linker errors.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',3,2,'2025-06-26_19-06-04','You cannot include another .c file in a .c file as the #include function copies the contents of the file during preprocessing which can result in duplicate functions and break modularity. If multiple files include the same .c file, code within that .c file will appear in multiple places of the final executable, resulting in duplicate functions. Additionally, .c files are complied independently into object files in C, but by including nested .c files, this modularity is broken which requires everything to be recompiled if something is changed.\nInstead, we include .h files that include only the function declarations so that the linker can match the declarations in the .h file to the definitions in the compiled .c files. Directly including .c files breaks this layer of separation and can cause linker errors.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',3,3,'2025-06-26_17-45-41','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread can be broken down into the following list:\nDefine the function that the thread will execute which should include any tasks the user wants the thread to perform.\nInitialize the kernel to prepare the necessary resources and global variables needed for the kernel to manage the upcoming threads.\nUsing the kernel’s functions like osCreateThread() to create a new thread. The user must pass in the address of the function they want the thread to execute and the kernel will allocate the stack space and store any required information for the thread.\nStart the kernel using a function like osKernelStart() which will call the SVC handler and run the thread the user defined which will run the thread function based on the kernel’s scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',3,3,'2025-06-26_19-06-04','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread can be broken down into the following list:\nDefine the function that the thread will execute which should include any tasks the user wants the thread to perform.\nInitialize the kernel to prepare the necessary resources and global variables needed for the kernel to manage the upcoming threads.\nUsing the kernel’s functions like osCreateThread() to create a new thread. The user must pass in the address of the function they want the thread to execute, and the kernel will allocate the stack space and store any required information for the thread.\nStart the kernel using a function like osKernelStart() which will call the SVC handler and run the thread the user defined which will run the thread function based on the kernel’s scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',3,4,'2025-06-26_17-45-41','The stack pool is used to designate a part of memory for the thread stacks. In this lab, we only allocated one thread with one stack which needed memory to store function calls, local variables, and the return address; however, in future labs we will be running multiple threads which each need their own stack. As such, the stack pool will be divided into fixed size segments giving each thread its own portion of the stack pool for its stack.\nThe stack allocator function in the kernel can be used by the OS to manage where each thread’s stack begins and ends within the stack pool. This is done so that there are no overlaps between threads. Furthermore, the allocator function tracks the available space left in the stack pool, and if there is too little space remaining, it will not allocate space for the stack and return an error.\nIn multithreaded systems, the OS will often switch between threads which requires it to sae the state and restore the state of each thread by pushing and popping data from the thread’s stack. This ensures each thread can pick back up from where it left off after resuming execution.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',3,4,'2025-06-26_19-06-04','The stack pool is used to designate a part of memory for the thread stacks. In this lab, we only allocated one thread with one stack which needed memory to store function calls, local variables, and the return address; however, in future labs we will be running multiple threads which each need their own stack. As such, the stack pool will be divided into fixed size segments giving each thread its own portion of the stack pool for its stack.\nThe stack allocator function in the kernel can be used by the OS to manage where each thread’s stack begins and ends within the stack pool. This is done so that there are no overlaps between threads. Furthermore, the allocator function tracks the available space left in the stack pool, and if there is too little space remaining, it will not allocate space for the stack and return an error.\nIn multithreaded systems, the OS will often switch between threads which requires it to sae the state and restore the state of each thread by pushing and popping data from the thread’s stack. This ensures each thread can pick back up from where it left off after resuming execution.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',3,5,'2025-06-26_17-45-41','The distinction between the “user” and “kernel” was established in this lab to make the code more modular and separate the responsibilities in our OS.\nFirst up, user code has high-level functions and logic like the thread function we want to execute and the main function. This code defines what functions the user wants the OS to do and is separate from the kernel code to simplify the user’s role when writing high-level code without worrying about low-level system management.\nOn the other hand, kernel code has the low level core functionality of our OS like managing the global variables, system functions and resources for threads like stacks and thread structs. The low-level resource management here allows the OS to control and execute the user’s requested tasks safely without giving the user too much privilege to access functions like stack allocation and thread creation. This also allows for higher efficiency.\nThis distinction is important as it allows the users to write application-level code without worrying about the low-level thread management. It is also modular which allows for ease of scalability as the OS now has the framework to support multiple threads without needing to change the user’s code. Kernel code also provides safety by isolating sensitive operations from the user who could accidentally cause unwanted errors.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',3,5,'2025-06-26_19-06-04','The distinction between the “user” and “kernel” was established in this lab to make the code more modular and separate the responsibilities in our OS.\nFirst up, user code has high-level functions and logic like the thread function we want to execute and the main function. This code defines what functions the user wants the OS to do and is separate from the kernel code to simplify the user’s role when writing high-level code without worrying about low-level system management.\nUser code interacts with the kernel through a defined set of API calls. For example, we used osKernelInitialize() to set up the stack pool, alloc_stack() to request stack space for a thread, and an SVC instruction (SVC #3) to safely switch to thread mode and start execution. These function calls serve as the bridge between user-level logic and the low-level kernel operations, allowing the user to request services from the OS without directly accessing hardware or protected memory.\nOn the other hand, kernel code has the low-level core functionality of our OS like managing the global variables, system functions and resources for threads like stacks and thread structs. The low-level resource management here allows the OS to control and execute the user’s requested tasks safely without giving the user too much privilege to access functions like stack allocation and thread creation. This also allows for higher efficiency.\nThis distinction is important as it allows the users to write application-level code without worrying about the low-level thread management. It is also modular which allows for ease of scalability as the OS now has the framework to support multiple threads without needing to change the user’s code. Kernel code also provides safety by isolating sensitive operations from the user who could accidentally cause unwanted errors.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',4,1,'2025-07-28_00-47-45','Instead of an array, a queue can be used to store the Task Control Blocks and is a better fit for the round-robin scheduling method. Round robin scheduling gives each task a fixed time slice to execute and if it does not complete the task in that time, control is passed to the next task in line. In the lab, we used an array to store the TCBs, but this is not ideal as the programmer has to manage the indices of the array carefully, ensuring sound wrap-around logic and is limited to a fixed size of array.\nAlternatively, queues work well with the round-robin scheduling method as they are First-In-First-Out. In round-robin, if a task reaches its timeslice, it can be dequeued from the front of the queue and enqueued onto the back of the queue. This is a much simpler and more efficient implementation than with arrays and has improved scalability over fixed size arrays.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',4,1,'2025-07-28_01-04-26','Instead of an array, a queue can be used to store the Task Control Blocks (TCBs) and is a better conceptual fit for round-robin scheduling. Round-robin gives each task a fixed time slice to execute, and if the task doesn\'t complete, it is moved to the back of the line. In the lab, we used an array to hold all TCBs, but this requires careful index management, wrap-around logic, and is limited by a fixed size.\nUsing a queue simplifies the flow: once a task’s timeslice expires, it is dequeued from the front and re-enqueued at the back. This naturally models round-robin behavior with less manual index tracking.\nHowever, this approach isn\'t free of cost. Compared to arrays, queues—especially dynamic or pointer-based ones—introduce overhead through pointer manipulation and possibly dynamic memory management. Arrays, on the other hand, offer constant-time access and predictable memory layout, which is important in embedded systems with tight constraints.\nFunctionally, a queue-based implementation and the current array-based one are nearly equivalent in behavior. The scheduler still picks the \"next\" thread in line; the difference lies in how that \"next\" is tracked and managed. This means that unless we are hitting limits in readability or scalability, there may not be a strong need to change the scheduler\'s implementation just for this. But conceptually, queues better represent the round-robin idea.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',4,2,'2025-07-28_00-47-45','The PendSV_Handler function is an interrupt handler specific to the ARM Cortex M4 which was purpose made for executing context switches between threads in real-time multitasking operating systems. The interrupt is triggered when PendSV is pended by a function such as osYield used in the lab (used to yield control of the processor). Once triggered, the handler will push the relevant context for the thread stored in registers onto the thread’s stack to save them. This will allow the relevant registers to be popped off the stack when the thread is ready to resume. Now, it is the job of the scheduler to determine which thread to run next, this can be done with a round-robin schedule for example. The handler will update its stack pointer to this new thread and restore its context by popping the registers off the stack and setting the CPUs pointer to the new thread. This will run the new thread, allowing it to pick back up from where it left off. In conclusion, the PendSV handler saves and restores the states of threads which allows for seamless switching between tasks in a multitasking system.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',4,2,'2025-07-28_01-04-26','The PendSV_Handler is a special interrupt handler on the ARM Cortex-M4 specifically designed for performing context switches in real-time operating systems. It is triggered when the OS explicitly requests a context switch using a function like osYield().\nWhen the interrupt fires, the current thread\'s context must be saved. The hardware automatically saves a subset of registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR) when entering the exception. The handler then manually saves additional registers—specifically R4 to R11—onto the current thread’s stack to preserve the full state of the CPU. This is done using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is the stack pointer used for threads running in unprivileged mode.\nOnce the current context is saved, the osSched() function is called to determine which thread should run next. This scheduler could use round-robin or deadline-based logic to make its decision. It sets curr_stck_ptr to point to the new thread’s stack.\nThe new thread\'s saved context (R4–R11) is then restored from its stack using the PSP, and finally control is returned to the new thread by loading its stack and executing BX LR, effectively resuming it from where it left off. This precise register handling and PSP manipulation enable seamless switching between threads.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',4,3,'2025-07-28_00-47-45','If we add an integer “priority” to the task control block and change the scheduler to a highest priority period algorithm where it will always choose the highest priority tasks, there will be many unwanted issues. The first issue is a vulnerability to starvation where if a higher priority task is always present, a low priority task may never get a chance to run. This can lead to parts of the system failing or becoming frozen / unresponsive if critical sections are stuck waiting to run indefinitely. These long wait times for low priority tasks lead to a poor response time of the system itself. This algorithm is also unfair as high priority tasks are always favoured which can prevent low priority tasks from receiving enough CPU cycles. If CPU time is hogged by the high priority tasks, the stability and predictability of the system decrease which is not ideal in real-time multitasking operating systems.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',4,3,'2025-07-28_01-04-26','If we add an integer \"priority\" to the TCB and modify the scheduler to always run the task with the highest priority, we introduce a serious flaw: only the highest-priority task will ever run. If a high-priority task is always ready to execute (which is often the case), lower-priority tasks will be completely ignored and never get CPU time.\nThis causes starvation, where lower-priority threads are indefinitely blocked from running. These may include background tasks or even essential system functions. The result is an unfair system with poor responsiveness and potentially serious functional issues.\nFurthermore, this monopolization of the CPU by a single task breaks the fairness and predictability expected in real-time systems. It leads to long delays or deadlocks in parts of the system waiting for CPU access. To avoid this, real-time schedulers typically use techniques like priority aging or deadline-based scheduling, which allow all tasks a chance to run while still respecting urgency.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',4,4,'2025-07-28_00-47-45','In the labs we called C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain instead of handling the required tasks in assembly which has its benefits and downsides. For the benefits, C code is more well known, readable, and maintainable. C code the most popular industry standard and is easier read, write, and maintain than assembly. Especially, for non-trivial code like done in the lab, C is more intuitive, concise, and easier to debug. C code supports debuggers for troubleshooting code which are extremely helpful and well supported. Additionally, assembly is a lower-level code which is hardware specific such that code written for one processor might not work on another. Whereas C code is not hardware specific and can be written to run on multiple different processors. For the downsides, by calling our C functions using an interrupt handler we introduce additional overhead needed to handle the stacks used to save context when switching from C to assembly and vice versa. If CPU cycles are limited in a critical section of an OS, these cycles could be saved by directly completing the tasks in assembly instead. Additionally, as C is a higher-level code, it may not optimize all the hardware specific low-level details which could be done is assembly to achieve higher performance on a specific processor.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',4,4,'2025-07-28_01-04-26','In the labs we called C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain instead of handling the required tasks in assembly which has its benefits and downsides. For the benefits, C code is more well known, readable, and maintainable. C code the most popular industry standard and is easier read, write, and maintain than assembly. Especially, for non-trivial code like done in the lab, C is more intuitive, concise, and easier to debug. C code supports debuggers for troubleshooting code which are extremely helpful and well supported. Additionally, assembly is a lower-level code which is hardware specific such that code written for one processor might not work on another. Whereas C code is not hardware specific and can be written to run on multiple different processors. For the downsides, by calling our C functions using an interrupt handler we introduce additional overhead needed to handle the stacks used to save context when switching from C to assembly and vice versa. If CPU cycles are limited in a critical section of an OS, these cycles could be saved by directly completing the tasks in assembly instead. Additionally, as C is a higher-level code, it may not optimize all the hardware specific low-level details which could be done is assembly to achieve higher performance on a specific processor.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',4,5,'2025-07-28_00-47-45','If we created a single thread which modifed the print_continuously thread to yield once it has printed, the OS would still work, but it would not prove that the OS is working properly as a multitasking real-time OS. Once osYield is called, it invokes the PendSV_Handler to provide a context switch, but because there are no other threads, the scheduler function osSched will simply schedule the same thread. As such, the same thread will be repeatedly run and the modified print_continuously function will yield and then get run again indefinitely. The OS will still work as the context switching is fully functional, but it does not prove that the OS can switch between multiple threads to share the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',4,5,'2025-07-28_01-04-26','If we created a single thread which modifed the print_continuously thread to yield once it has printed, the OS would still work, but it would not prove that the OS is working properly as a multitasking real-time OS. Once osYield is called, it invokes the PendSV_Handler to provide a context switch, but because there are no other threads, the scheduler function osSched will simply schedule the same thread. As such, the same thread will be repeatedly run and the modified print_continuously function will yield and then get run again indefinitely. The OS will still work as the context switching is fully functional, but it does not prove that the OS can switch between multiple threads to share the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',5,1,'2025-07-28_01-47-52','The process by which an arguments gets passed into a thread starts with the user defining the argument which is passed into the thread. The user will define a thread function that takes in a void pointer as its argument which is done as it allows the user to decide whatever data they want to pass. This can be done by creating a struct which contains all the relevant data and passing a pointer to the struct.\nNext, the OS will manage the thread’s creation by allocating a stack for the thread and filling it with the relevant registers such as the link register, program counter, xPSR, and R0-R12. The OS will place the thread’s argument into the R0 register so that upon execution, the thread has the argument information available immediately.\nThis system works as it uses the flexibility of the void* and thread’s stack to seamlessly pass any arguments to a thread. The void* can point to any type which allows for flexibility in the type of data being passed to the threads while still being efficient. This data can be accessed by dereferencing the pointer located in R0 and allows for threads to receive arguments passed by a user which is very powerful in a real-time multitasking system.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',5,1,'2025-07-28_11-46-08','The process by which an argument gets passed into a thread starts with the user defining the argument to be used by the thread function. The thread function is written to take in a void* argument, which allows for flexibility in the type of data the user passes in. The user can pass any kind of data—such as a struct containing multiple fields—by simply passing a pointer to it when calling osCreateThread.\nOnce the thread is created, it becomes the OS’s responsibility to initialize it properly. The OS allocates a stack for the new thread and pre-fills it with the default context required to start execution. This includes pushing registers like xPSR, PC (the address of the thread function), LR, and the general-purpose registers R4–R11. Most importantly, the OS places the user’s argument directly into the R0 register—the standard register used for passing the first argument in ARM’s procedure call standard.\nThis works because ARM Cortex-M4 devices follow the AAPCS (ARM Architecture Procedure Call Standard), which is a part of the ARM EABI (Embedded Application Binary Interface). According to this ABI, the first argument to any function is passed in R0, the second in R1, and so on. Since the thread function is just a C function with a void* argument, and since the OS starts the thread by jumping to that function with R0 already loaded, the system behaves predictably.\nThis convention ensures that even though the thread is starting in a low-level, manually initialized state, the C function receives its argument exactly as expected. The use of void* adds flexibility and the EABI ensures consistency, making the system portable, reliable, and efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',5,2,'2025-07-28_01-47-52','You can’t call osYield in any of our context-switching function slike PendSV_Handler, SV_Handler, SV_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler as it can cause the system to switch contexts in an infinite loop. The handlers above are used to switch contexts and handle interrupts while running a multitasking OS. The function osYield invokes a context switch by triggering the PendSV interrupt and if it was invoked while inside one of the listed handlers, it could cause an infinite loop of context switches. The system would repeatedly try to switch contexts, interrupting either from switching. This can have negative effects such as corrupting data on the stack which will not be properly managed during this time by overwriting important values of key registers. This could cause unwanted behaviour such as system errors and crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('lsky',5,2,'2025-07-28_11-46-08','You cannot call osYield from within our context-switching functions such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because osYield is implemented as a system call—specifically, it triggers the PendSV interrupt to initiate a context switch. If you call osYield while already inside one of these handlers, you\'re essentially trying to pend another context switch while a context switch is already happening, which the processor isn’t designed to handle.\nThe ARM Cortex-M4 supports nested interrupts, but PendSV has the lowest priority by design so that it only runs when no other higher-priority interrupts are pending. If osYield is called from within a currently executing PendSV_Handler, the processor may attempt to re-enter the same handler before it completes, leading to nested PendSV execution, which is unsafe.\nAt the hardware level, this can cause stack corruption because each interrupt assumes it has a clean stack to work with. Since context-switching handlers manipulate the stack (saving and restoring registers like R4–R11 using the PSP), re-entering them mid-execution may overwrite in-progress data. The processor detects this kind of low-level corruption or invalid memory access and responds with a HardFault—a serious exception that halts the system.\nUnlike a software-level issue like an infinite loop (which just keeps running), a HardFault is a hardware-level crash indicating a violation of fundamental rules like stack alignment or invalid memory access. This makes calling osYield from within these critical handlers not just a logic error, but a fatal one that causes the entire system to fail.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',5,3,'2025-07-28_01-47-52','Changing the clock from 1ms to being 0.1ms will have its benefits and downsides on the system. For the benefits, the finer clock resolution will result in improved precision during time-sensitive operations. For example, systems that require high-frequency scheduling for real-time control will achieve higher precision in switching between tasks at high speed. This can reduce latency and improve the responsiveness of the system.\nFor the downsides, the higher the clock frequency, the more overhead is required for the clock interrupts. Due to there being 10 times more interrupts per second, the CPU will need to dedicate more resources to the overhead associated with the clock. This can be detrimental if the CPU spends more time handling interrupts and context switches than doing meaningful work. This can lead to higher power usage for a less efficient solution. As such, while a 0.1ms clock could be beneficial for certain high frequency, high precision applications, it may also be computationally inefficient due to the large overhead for systems that don’t require such fine granularity of time management.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',5,3,'2025-07-28_11-46-08','Changing the clock from 1ms to being 0.1ms will have its benefits and downsides on the system. For the benefits, the finer clock resolution will result in improved precision during time-sensitive operations. For example, systems that require high-frequency scheduling for real-time control will achieve higher precision in switching between tasks at high speed. This can reduce latency and improve the responsiveness of the system.\nFor the downsides, the higher the clock frequency, the more overhead is required for the clock interrupts. Due to there being 10 times more interrupts per second, the CPU will need to dedicate more resources to the overhead associated with the clock. This can be detrimental if the CPU spends more time handling interrupts and context switches than doing meaningful work. This can lead to higher power usage for a less efficient solution. As such, while a 0.1ms clock could be beneficial for certain high frequency, high precision applications, it may also be computationally inefficient due to the large overhead for systems that don’t require such fine granularity of time management.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',5,4,'2025-07-28_01-47-52','From the perspective of the user, cooperative and pre-emptive multitasking differ in how control of the CPU is transferred between tasks. In cooperative multitasking, control of the CPU is relinquished by the task in control voluntarily. This can be done through an explicit call like osYield, but if a task does not yield control, it can monopolize the CPU. This can cause other tasks to wait on it indefinitely. Alternatively, in pre-emptive multitasking, the operating system controls when tasks yield control by giving each task a timeslice which defines the maximum amount of CPU time they get or priorities. Tasks are interrupted by the OS and are pre-emptively switched between without needing the running task to yield access to the CPU. This is done so that no task can monopolize the CPU and leave other tasks waiting indefinitely.\nFrom the perspective of the OS, cooperative multitasking requires very little work, whereas pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS’s attention frequently. Cooperative multitasking is simple as the OS only needs to switch between tasks when one task signals that it is done with the CPU. Pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to monitor the running task, keeping track of how long it has run using timers like the system tick timer in the lab so that when its runtime is over, control is switched to the next waiting task. The OS needs to manage timeslices and ensure that higher priority tasks get scheduled which more complex to implement than cooperative multitasking, but offers higher fairness and responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',5,4,'2025-07-28_11-46-08','From the perspective of the user, cooperative and pre-emptive multitasking differ in how control of the CPU is transferred between tasks. In cooperative multitasking, control of the CPU is relinquished by the task in control voluntarily. This can be done through an explicit call like osYield, but if a task does not yield control, it can monopolize the CPU. This can cause other tasks to wait on it indefinitely. Alternatively, in pre-emptive multitasking, the operating system controls when tasks yield control by giving each task a timeslice which defines the maximum amount of CPU time they get or priorities. Tasks are interrupted by the OS and are pre-emptively switched between without needing the running task to yield access to the CPU. This is done so that no task can monopolize the CPU and leave other tasks waiting indefinitely.\nFrom the perspective of the OS, cooperative multitasking requires very little work, whereas pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS’s attention frequently. Cooperative multitasking is simple as the OS only needs to switch between tasks when one task signals that it is done with the CPU. Pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to monitor the running task, keeping track of how long it has run using timers like the system tick timer in the lab so that when its runtime is over, control is switched to the next waiting task. The OS needs to manage timeslices and ensure that higher priority tasks get scheduled which more complex to implement than cooperative multitasking, but offers higher fairness and responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',5,5,'2025-07-28_01-47-52','In the lab, we used yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching which worked together because they both use the PendSV interrupt to handle context switches reliably. The osYield function calls the PendSV handler to push the current tasks data to the stack, update the scheduler, and switch to the next task which is waiting. The time-based pre-emptive switching method uses a timer like SysTick to periodically interrupt the task and call the PendSV handler to save the state of the task, update the scheduler, and switch to the next task. Thus, both methods use the same handler to ensure that context switching is not only efficient, but reliable. This consistency between the two methods allows for fairness by preventing tasks from monopolizing the CPU and allowing for voluntary yields so that tasks that don’t need the CPU can give it to others immediately. This allows for an efficient and reliable real-time multitasking system.\n',1,'s25'),('lsky',5,5,'2025-07-28_11-46-08','In the lab, we used yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching which worked together because they both use the PendSV interrupt to handle context switches reliably. The osYield function calls the PendSV handler to push the current tasks data to the stack, update the scheduler, and switch to the next task which is waiting. The time-based pre-emptive switching method uses a timer like SysTick to periodically interrupt the task and call the PendSV handler to save the state of the task, update the scheduler, and switch to the next task. Thus, both methods use the same handler to ensure that context switching is not only efficient, but reliable. This consistency between the two methods allows for fairness by preventing tasks from monopolizing the CPU and allowing for voluntary yields so that tasks that don’t need the CPU can give it to others immediately. This allows for an efficient and reliable real-time multitasking system.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',1,1,'2025-06-26_18-57-49','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a software interface that standardizes interaction between application code and hardware peripherals. Rather than requiring low-level register manipulation, HAL functions allow configuration and control of features such as timers, digital I/O, and communication protocols. This abstraction simplifies firmware development, enhances portability across different microcontroller platforms, and improves code maintainability by isolating hardware-specific details.\n',0,'s25'),('m2dang',1,1,'2025-06-26_20-29-09','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a software interface that standardizes interaction between application code and hardware peripherals. Rather than requiring low-level register manipulation, HAL functions allow configuration and control of features such as timers, digital I/O, and communication protocols. This abstraction simplifies firmware development, enhances portability across different microcontroller platforms, and improves code maintainability by isolating hardware-specific details.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',1,2,'2025-06-26_18-57-49','The __io_putchar function defines how the standard printf function outputs characters to a hardware interface, commonly UART. While printf is part of the C standard library, it lacks direct hardware support. By implementing __io_putchar, character output can be routed to specific peripherals. In this lab, the function enabled communication with a serial terminal via UART. Modifying this function allows redirection of output to other devices, such as USB or an LCD, making it an essential component in adapting standard output functions for embedded environments.\n',0,'s25'),('m2dang',1,2,'2025-06-26_20-29-09','The __io_putchar function defines how the standard printf function outputs characters to a hardware interface, commonly UART. While printf is part of the C standard library, it lacks direct hardware support. By implementing __io_putchar, character output can be routed to specific peripherals. In this lab, the function enabled communication with a serial terminal via UART. Modifying this function allows redirection of output to other devices, such as USB or an LCD, making it an essential component in adapting standard output functions for embedded environments.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',1,3,'2025-06-26_18-57-49','The debugger was used to access processor-specific information, such as register values and stack pointers, which are not visible through printf. Registers like MSP and PSP require direct inspection for validation and cannot be reliably output via software printing. The debugger provides a real-time view of the internal processor state, enabling accurate analysis of memory usage, function behavior, and hardware-level execution. In contrast, printf is limited to displaying user-defined variables and lacks the resolution needed for detailed system-level observation.\n',0,'s25'),('m2dang',1,3,'2025-06-26_20-29-09','The debugger was used to access processor-specific information, such as register values and stack pointers, which are not visible through printf. Registers like MSP and PSP require direct inspection for validation and cannot be reliably output via software printing. The debugger provides a real-time view of the internal processor state, enabling accurate analysis of memory usage, function behavior, and hardware-level execution. In contrast, printf is limited to displaying user-defined variables and lacks the resolution needed for detailed system-level observation.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',1,4,'2025-06-26_18-57-49','The stack allocation method defined memory as a fixed-size region and used the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) as a reference point. The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) was assigned by subtracting 0x400 bytes from the MSP value, creating a non-overlapping region in lower memory. This approach aligns with the downward growth of stacks in ARM architecture. Knowledge of the MSP’s initial location ensures the PSP is placed in valid memory, preventing conflicts or access violations. Without this reference, improper stack allocation could result in undefined behavior or system faults.\n',0,'s25'),('m2dang',1,4,'2025-06-26_20-29-09','The stack allocation method defined memory as a fixed-size region and used the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) as a reference point. The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) was assigned by subtracting 0x400 bytes from the MSP value, creating a non-overlapping region in lower memory. This approach aligns with the downward growth of stacks in ARM architecture. Knowledge of the MSP’s initial location ensures the PSP is placed in valid memory, preventing conflicts or access violations. Without this reference, improper stack allocation could result in undefined behavior or system faults.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',1,5,'2025-06-26_18-57-49','To allocate multiple thread stacks, the memory above the main stack is divided into fixed-size regions, one per thread. Given the main stack size and thread stack size, the base address for each thread’s stack is calculated using:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) × THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis ensures each thread receives a distinct, non-overlapping memory region. All stack sizes must be aligned to 8 bytes to satisfy ARM architecture requirements and avoid alignment faults. Additionally, total memory usage must remain within the bounds of available RAM to prevent overflow. These calculations provide a reliable and deterministic layout for multitasking systems.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',1,5,'2025-06-26_20-29-09','To allocate multiple thread stacks, the memory above the main stack is divided into fixed-size regions, one per thread. Given the main stack size and thread stack size, the base address for each thread’s stack is calculated using:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) × THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis ensures each thread receives a distinct, non-overlapping memory region. All stack sizes must be aligned to 8 bytes to satisfy ARM architecture requirements and avoid alignment faults. Additionally, total memory usage must remain within the bounds of available RAM to prevent overflow. These calculations provide a reliable and deterministic layout for multitasking systems.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',2,1,'2025-06-26_20-37-46','When PC was changed to hold the function pointer, program execution immediately redirected to that function. This occurred because the PC determines the next instruction to execute, and altering its value caused the processor to begin fetching and executing instructions from the new address. As a result, the system continuously printed “Hello, PC!” in an infinite loop. This experiment demonstrated that direct manipulation of PC can be used to control execution flow which is an essential concept in implementing thread switching and low-level operating system behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',2,2,'2025-06-26_20-37-46','A system call is a way for user programs to access functions that run in a more privileged part of the system, like the operating system kernel. Since user code isn’t allowed to directly perform actions like hardware access or managing memory, it uses system calls to request these operations safely. In this lab, system calls were triggered using the SVC instruction, which caused the processor to jump to a special handler that interpreted the request and executed the corresponding code. This setup allows the system to stay protected while still giving programs access to critical functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',2,3,'2025-06-26_20-37-46','Setting up a thread’s stack involves preparing the memory layout to match the CPU’s expected register state at the start of execution. In this lab, the stack was manually populated with values in the order the processor would normally restore them during an exception return: xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, followed by R11 to R4. The PC was set to the address of the print_continuously function, and xPSR was configured to enable Thumb mode. This process is necessary because, during a context switch, the processor loads register values from the stack to resume execution. Without this setup, the processor would not have the correct context to begin executing the thread\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',2,4,'2025-06-26_20-37-46','The __set_PSP function sets the Process Stack Pointer to the top of the thread’s prepared stack. This step is required before running the thread because the processor uses the PSP to restore register values during the exception return that follows a system call. If PSP is not set, the processor will attempt to pop values such as PC and xPSR from an undefined or invalid memory location, leading to a hard fault. Properly setting PSP ensures that the processor restores the thread’s context from a valid stack, allowing execution to begin without crashing.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',2,5,'2025-06-26_20-37-46','If the thread function did not loop and instead returned, the processor would attempt to branch to the address stored in the Link Register (LR). In this lab, the LR was manually set to an arbitrary value during stack setup and does not point to valid code. As a result, the processor would jump to an invalid memory location, causing a hard fault. In a proper operating system, threads are not expected to return normally; instead, they either loop indefinitely or terminate through a defined mechanism such as a system call or scheduler cleanup routine.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',3,1,'2025-06-27_22-50-11','Each .c file is compiled individually into an object file (.o) by the compiler. These object files contain machine code but are not complete programs on their own. After all .c files are compiled, the linker combines the object files into a single executable by resolving symbols—such as function and variable references—across the files. The final output is a single binary that contains all necessary code and data to run the program.\n',0,'s25'),('m2dang',3,1,'2025-06-27_23-30-43','The process of compiling multiple .c files into a single executable involves three major steps: preprocessing, compilation, and linking. During preprocessing, each source file has its macros expanded and header files inlined through directives like #include and #define, while comments are stripped out. This results in a complete C source with all dependencies resolved. Next, in the compilation stage, each .c file is independently converted into an object file (.o) containing machine code. This step checks for syntax errors and may include compiler optimizations. Finally, during linking, all object files are combined into a single executable. The linker resolves references between files, such as function calls and global variables, and brings in standard libraries like the C runtime. The final output is a self-contained executable binary ready to run on the target system.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',3,2,'2025-06-27_22-50-11','Including a .c file with #include causes its contents to be copied directly into the file being compiled. This results in the same code being compiled multiple times if the .c file is also compiled separately, leading to duplicate symbol errors during linking. Proper compilation requires each .c file to be compiled once and included through its corresponding header file (.h) to share declarations without duplicating implementations.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',3,2,'2025-06-27_23-30-43','Using the #include directive on a .c file, such as #include <kernel.c>, is considered poor practice and leads to serious problems. The #include directive is designed for header files, which typically contain declarations, not actual code. When a source file is included this way, its entire contents are pasted directly into another file before compilation. This causes duplicate definitions when the same .c file is also compiled normally, leading to linker errors due to multiple symbol definitions. It also breaks the modular structure of a project by tightly coupling implementation files, making the build system harder to maintain and understand. Proper compilation involves compiling .c files separately and linking them, using header files to share declarations between modules.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',3,3,'2025-06-27_22-50-11','First, call the kernel initialization function to prepare internal data structures. Then, write the thread function that defines what the thread will do. Next, pass the thread function to the thread creation function provided by the kernel. Finally, once all threads are created, call the kernel start function to begin execution of the first thread.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',3,3,'2025-06-27_23-30-43','To set up and start a thread from a user’s perspective, the process begins by initializing the kernel. This step sets up internal data structures and allocates memory to prepare for thread creation. After that, the user writes a thread function containing the logic that the new thread will execute. This function must take no arguments and return no value. Once the function is defined, the user calls a thread creation function, passing the address of the thread function. This allows the kernel to set up the necessary stack and execution context. Finally, the user starts the kernel using a dedicated function that performs a system call to switch the processor into thread mode. At this point, the thread begins running independently, and the user’s code continues under the control of the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',3,4,'2025-06-27_22-50-11','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory from which stacks for individual threads are allocated. Each thread needs its own stack to store local variables and CPU state during execution. By managing the stack pool and assigning separate regions to each thread, the OS can support multiple threads without stack conflicts. In future labs, this will allow threads to run independently and safely as the scheduler switches between them.\n',0,'s25'),('m2dang',3,4,'2025-06-27_23-30-43','The stack pool is a reserved area of memory used by the operating system to provide each thread with its own stack. Every thread in a multitasking system requires a separate stack to store its local variables, function return addresses, and saved CPU registers during context switches. The stack pool ensures that this memory is allocated in an organized and isolated way, preventing threads from overwriting each other’s data. In future labs, this mechanism will enable the creation of multiple threads by dividing the stack pool into fixed-size chunks, one per thread. This approach ensures predictable memory use and allows the kernel to manage multiple execution contexts safely and efficiently.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',3,5,'2025-06-27_22-50-11','Kernel files (kernel.c and kernel.h) contain low-level OS functionality, such as thread creation, stack management, and system control. User files (main.c) contain the application code, including thread functions and calls to kernel APIs. This separation ensures that user code interacts with the OS only through well-defined interfaces, improving modularity, preventing accidental misuse of kernel internals, and making the system easier to maintain and extend.\n',0,'s25'),('m2dang',3,5,'2025-06-27_23-30-43','The user and kernel portions of the OS are separated by their purpose and the type of code they contain. Kernel code, typically found in files like kernel.c and kernel.h, handles low-level operating system responsibilities. This includes creating threads, managing stacks, handling interrupts, and performing system calls. In contrast, the user code—typically in main.c—contains the high-level application logic written by the developer. This is where thread functions are defined and where the kernel’s API functions are called to manage threads and start the OS. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important because it abstracts away complex hardware-level behavior. The user can focus on writing application code without needing to understand or interact with the internals of the kernel, which improves modularity, code clarity, and system reliability.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',4,1,'2025-07-27_21-59-55','In round-robin scheduling, using a queue to manage thread control blocks (TCBs) offers a conceptually clean and efficient approach. Threads are enqueued in the order they are scheduled to run, and after each yields or finishes its time slice, it is moved to the back of the queue. This ensures that all threads receive CPU time in a fair and predictable manner. Compared to an array with manual index tracking and modulo operations, a queue simplifies the scheduling logic by naturally supporting insertion and removal, which becomes especially useful when dealing with blocking, terminating, or dynamically created threads. While queues may introduce slight overhead due to pointer manipulation or dynamic memory management, they improve the system’s scalability, maintainability, and support for more advanced scheduling policies.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',4,1,'2025-07-27_22-09-43','In round-robin scheduling, using a queue to manage thread control blocks (TCBs) offers a conceptually clean and efficient approach. Threads are enqueued in the order they are scheduled to run, and after each yields or finishes its time slice, it is moved to the back of the queue. This ensures that all threads receive CPU time in a fair and predictable manner. Compared to an array with manual index tracking and modulo operations, a queue simplifies the scheduling logic by naturally supporting insertion and removal, which becomes especially useful when dealing with blocking, terminating, or dynamically created threads. While queues may introduce slight overhead due to pointer manipulation or dynamic memory management, they improve the system’s scalability, maintainability, and support for more advanced scheduling policies.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',4,2,'2025-07-27_21-59-55','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current thread’s CPU state and restoring the next thread’s state from its stack. It begins by reading the PSP (Process Stack Pointer), which points to the top of the current thread’s stack, and pushes registers R4–R11 onto the stack to preserve the thread’s context. Then, it calls the osSched() function to select the next thread to run, typically using a round-robin scheduling strategy. After the scheduler updates the current thread index and sets the PSP to the new thread’s stack, PendSV_Handler pops R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack, effectively restoring its CPU state. Finally, it exits the handler and resumes execution in the newly selected thread.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',4,2,'2025-07-27_22-09-43','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current thread’s CPU state and restoring the next thread’s state from its stack. It begins by reading the PSP (Process Stack Pointer), which points to the top of the current thread’s stack, and pushes registers R4–R11 onto the stack to preserve the thread’s context. Then, it calls the osSched() function to select the next thread to run, typically using a round-robin scheduling strategy. After the scheduler updates the current thread index and sets the PSP to the new thread’s stack, PendSV_Handler pops R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack, effectively restoring its CPU state. Finally, it exits the handler and resumes execution in the newly selected thread.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',4,3,'2025-07-27_21-59-55','Modifying the scheduler to always select the task with the highest priority can lead to a problem called starvation. In this setup, if a high-priority task is always ready to run and frequently yields or never blocks, lower-priority tasks may never get scheduled. This creates an unfair system where some threads are perpetually ignored, even if they perform important background or maintenance functions. Over time, this can lead to degraded system performance, missed deadlines for critical low-priority tasks, and unpredictable behavior. A more balanced approach, such as priority-based scheduling with aging or time slicing, is needed to ensure fairness and system stability.\n',0,'s25'),('m2dang',4,3,'2025-07-27_22-09-43','Modifying the scheduler to always select the highest-priority task causes lower-priority tasks to be indefinitely postponed, a problem known as starvation. The most direct consequence is that if a high-priority thread is constantly ready to run, it will dominate the CPU, and lower-priority threads may never be scheduled at all. This is not just a fairness issue; it is a flaw in the scheduling mechanism itself. Fair scheduling requires that all runnable tasks get execution time, but strict priority-based selection prevents this by design. This can lead to critical background or maintenance tasks being entirely blocked from execution, even if they are essential for system stability. To prevent starvation while still honoring task priority, schedulers often implement techniques such as priority aging or multi-level feedback queues, which balance responsiveness with fairness.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',4,4,'2025-07-27_21-59-55','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain instead of writing everything in assembly offers several benefits. C code is more readable, maintainable, and easier to debug, especially when working with complex logic or data structures like arrays or queues. It allows developers to write high-level logic without manually managing registers or memory, reducing the chance of low-level bugs. Additionally, using C improves portability across platforms. However, this approach has downsides: calling C from assembly introduces overhead, requires adherence to calling conventions, and offers less control over low-level operations like precise register usage. In performance-critical or timing-sensitive parts of the OS, pure assembly may still be preferred.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',4,4,'2025-07-27_22-09-43','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain instead of writing everything in assembly offers several benefits. C code is more readable, maintainable, and easier to debug, especially when working with complex logic or data structures like arrays or queues. It allows developers to write high-level logic without manually managing registers or memory, reducing the chance of low-level bugs. Additionally, using C improves portability across platforms. However, this approach has downsides: calling C from assembly introduces overhead, requires adherence to calling conventions, and offers less control over low-level operations like precise register usage. In performance-critical or timing-sensitive parts of the OS, pure assembly may still be preferred.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',4,5,'2025-07-27_21-59-55','Yes, the OS will still function correctly even with only a single thread. When that thread, such as print_continuously, calls osYield(), the scheduler will perform a context switch as usual. However, since there are no other threads in the system, the scheduler will select the same thread again, effectively yielding back to itself. The context switch will still save and restore the thread’s state, introducing some overhead, but functionally the system will behave like a cooperative single-threaded application. This demonstrates that the OS can handle edge cases gracefully, even though its multitasking capabilities are not being utilized. It also confirms the correctness of the scheduler\'s logic, as it does not assume multiple threads must be present to operate safely.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',4,5,'2025-07-27_22-09-43','Yes, the OS will still function correctly even with only a single thread. When that thread, such as print_continuously, calls osYield(), the scheduler will perform a context switch as usual. However, since there are no other threads in the system, the scheduler will select the same thread again, effectively yielding back to itself. The context switch will still save and restore the thread’s state, introducing some overhead, but functionally the system will behave like a cooperative single-threaded application. This demonstrates that the OS can handle edge cases gracefully, even though its multitasking capabilities are not being utilized. It also confirms the correctness of the scheduler\'s logic, as it does not assume multiple threads must be present to operate safely.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',5,1,'2025-07-28_14-08-12','To pass an argument to a thread, the user calls osCreateThread() or osCreateThreadWithDeadline(), supplying both a function pointer and a void* argument. This argument can point to any user-defined structure or primitive type, enabling flexible parameterization. The OS handles this by setting up the thread’s initial stack frame to comply with the ARM calling convention, specifically placing the argument value into register R0. When the CPU branches to the thread’s entry function, it automatically uses R0 as the first parameter, effectively delivering the argument without requiring further user action. This mechanism is robust because it leverages the hardware’s native function call behavior, ensuring compatibility and efficiency without runtime overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',5,2,'2025-07-28_14-08-12','osYield() invokes a software interrupt (SVC) to request a context switch. However, calling it from within interrupt handlers like PendSV_Handler, SysTick_Handler, or SVC_Handler_Main is invalid because the processor is already operating in handler mode. Attempting to execute another exception while already servicing one can result in nested faults or corruption of the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). More critically, SVC relies on predictable exception entry and return behavior, which breaks down if the OS attempts to interrupt itself recursively. This leads to undefined behavior, including stack misalignment, context corruption, and potentially permanent processor faults. For these reasons, context-switching functions must never trigger new system calls—they must only save state and transition between threads deterministically.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',5,3,'2025-07-28_14-08-12','Setting the OS tick interval to 0.1ms instead of 1ms increases the frequency of the system’s timing interrupt by a factor of ten. This offers improved scheduling precision and enables finer control over thread execution timing, which is especially beneficial for time-sensitive tasks in real-time systems. However, it also dramatically raises interrupt overhead. The CPU must enter and exit the SysTick_Handler far more frequently, leaving less time for actual thread execution. This can lead to decreased throughput, increased power consumption, and higher jitter in thread behavior. On resource-constrained embedded systems like the STM32F4, such high-frequency interrupts may overwhelm the scheduler and compromise real-time guarantees, making this tradeoff unsuitable unless sub-millisecond accuracy is a strict requirement.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',5,4,'2025-07-28_14-08-12','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires threads to explicitly call osYield() to allow other threads to execute. This offers predictable behavior and is simpler to implement, but it relies entirely on well-behaved threads voluntarily yielding control. A single uncooperative thread can block the entire system. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking uses the system tick to forcibly interrupt threads after a defined time slice, regardless of their cooperation. The OS must manage timers, interrupt vectors, and thread state transitions automatically. It performs context switches in response to timer expiry, requiring more complex synchronization and exception handling. While harder to implement, pre-emption ensures fairness and responsiveness, especially in systems where timing and fault isolation are critical.\n',1,'s25'),('m2dang',5,5,'2025-07-28_14-08-12','The lab’s final exercise demonstrates that cooperative and pre-emptive multitasking can be integrated effectively by leveraging a common context-switching mechanism. Pre-emptive switching occurs when the SysTick timer expires, triggering a PendSV interrupt that invokes the scheduler. In parallel, a cooperative thread may voluntarily call osYield(), which issues an SVC to request a context switch. Both mechanisms ultimately converge on the same osSched() function, which handles stack saving and restoration. This unified infrastructure allows both styles to coexist, enabling developers to write responsive cooperative code that yields when convenient, while still enforcing fairness through time-based pre-emption. The result is a flexible multitasking model that accommodates both structured and time-sensitive thread behaviors.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,1,'2025-05-14_10-12-18','The HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. It sits underneath the operating system, and it provides the interface needed for the operating system to interact with directly with the hardware resources. The layer is “abstract” as it leaves out complex hardware details. This abstraction makes the HAL useful with working with embedded systems, as it generalizes the OS to hardware interface so that the software is not specific to any hardware resources.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,1,'2025-05-14_10-51-10','The HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. It sits underneath the operating system, and it provides the interface needed for the operating system to interact with directly with the hardware resources. The layer is “abstract” as it leaves out complex hardware details. This abstraction makes the HAL useful with working with embedded systems, as it generalizes the OS to hardware interface so that the software is not specific to any hardware resources.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',1,1,'2025-05-14_13-20-22','The HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. It sits underneath the operating system, and it provides the interface needed for the operating system to interact with directly with the hardware resources. The layer is “abstract” as it leaves out complex hardware details that may be specific to certain vendors and models. This abstraction greatly reduces the code complexity by leaving out lower-level details and allows the code to be ported across different embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,1,'2025-05-19_12-27-01','The HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. It sits underneath the operating system, and it provides the interface needed for the operating system to interact with directly with the hardware resources. The layer is “abstract” as it leaves out complex hardware details that may be specific to certain vendors and models. This abstraction greatly reduces the code complexity by leaving out lower-level details and allows the code to be ported across different embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,1,'2025-05-19_12-39-55','The HAL stands for hardware abstraction layer. It sits underneath the operating system, and it provides the interface needed for the operating system to interact with directly with the hardware resources. The layer is “abstract” as it leaves out complex hardware details that may be specific to certain vendors and models. This abstraction greatly reduces the code complexity by leaving out lower-level details and allows the code to be ported across different embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,2,'2025-05-14_10-12-18','The io_putchar function is used to write a character, hence the name “put char”, to the specified IO channel. When printing a value, such as in the case with this lab, it is essential because the printf function is part of the stdio library, which relies on “intermediate” functions in order to interface with the hardware. So when printf is called from our program, io_putchar will be called in the background in order to interface with the hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',1,2,'2025-05-14_10-51-10','The io_putchar function is used to write a character, hence the name “put char”, to the specified IO channel. When printing a value, such as in the case with this lab, it is essential because the printf function is part of the stdio library, which relies on “intermediate” functions in order to interface with the hardware. Since there is no single interface for each embedded system, these functions must be defined manually by the programmer to ensure compatibility. In the case of this lab, we define io_putchar to call the HAL_UART_Transmit function. The HAL_UART_Transmit function transmits data over the second UART (UART2), which is a hardware component on the microcontroller responsible for transmitting data serially. Other microcontrollers, however, a different number of UART components, or none at all, which is why this manually specification is essential.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,2,'2025-05-14_13-20-22','The io_putchar function is used to write a character, hence the name “put char”, to the specified IO channel. When printing a value, such as in the case with this lab, it is essential because the printf function is part of the stdio library, which relies on “intermediate” functions in order to interface with the hardware. Since there is no single interface for each embedded system, these functions must be defined manually by the programmer to ensure compatibility. In the case of this lab, we define io_putchar to call the HAL_UART_Transmit function. The HAL_UART_Transmit function transmits data over the second UART (UART2), which is a hardware component on the microcontroller responsible for transmitting data serially. Other microcontrollers, however, a different number of UART components, or none at all, which is why this manually specification is essential.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,2,'2025-05-19_12-27-01','The io_putchar function is used to write a character, hence the name “put char”, to the specified IO channel. When printing a value, such as in the case with this lab, it is essential because the printf function is part of the stdio library, which relies on “intermediate” functions in order to interface with the hardware. Since there is no single interface for each embedded system, these functions must be defined manually by the programmer to ensure compatibility. In the case of this lab, we define io_putchar to call the HAL_UART_Transmit function. The HAL_UART_Transmit function transmits data over the second UART (UART2), which is a hardware component on the microcontroller responsible for transmitting data serially. Other microcontrollers, however, a different number of UART components, or none at all, which is why this manually specification is essential.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,2,'2025-05-19_12-39-55','The io_putchar function is used to write a character, hence the name “put char”, to the specified IO channel. When printing a value, such as in the case with this lab, it is essential because the printf function is part of the stdio library, which relies on “intermediate” functions in order to interface with the hardware. Since there is no single interface for each embedded system, these functions must be defined manually by the programmer to ensure compatibility. In the case of this lab, we define io_putchar to call the HAL_UART_Transmit function. The HAL_UART_Transmit function transmits data over the second UART (UART2), which is a hardware component on the microcontroller responsible for transmitting data serially. Other microcontrollers, however, a different number of UART components, or none at all, which is why this manually specification is essential.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,3,'2025-05-14_10-12-18','We must use the debugger in this lab because we must pause the execution of the program. The program constantly pushes and pops data to and from the stack. As a result, the memory location of the stack is continuously changing. The printf function will most likely not work to validate our code as we need to compare the memory locations of multiple registers whose locations may change during the execution of printf. By using the debugger to freeze the execution, we can ensure that the memory locations of the registers shown by the debugger are all from the exact same point in the execution, so we can accurately compare them.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,3,'2025-05-14_10-51-10','We must use the debugger in this lab because we must pause the execution of the program. The program constantly pushes and pops data to and from the stack. As a result, the memory location of the stack is continuously changing. The printf function will most likely not work to validate our code as we need to compare the memory locations of multiple registers whose locations may change during the execution of printf. By using the debugger to freeze the execution, we can ensure that the memory locations of the registers shown by the debugger are all from the exact same point in the execution, so we can accurately compare them.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,3,'2025-05-14_13-20-22','We must use the debugger in this lab because we must pause the execution of the program. The program constantly pushes and pops data to and from the stack. As a result, the memory location of the stack is continuously changing. The printf function will most likely not work to validate our code as we need to compare the memory locations of multiple registers whose locations may change during the execution of printf. By using the debugger to freeze the execution, we can ensure that the memory locations of the registers shown by the debugger are all from the exact same point in the execution, so we can accurately compare them.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',1,3,'2025-05-19_12-27-01','The debugger must be used in this lab because the printf function cannot be used to directly access and display register values, which is required to verify that the code worked.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,3,'2025-05-19_12-39-55','The debugger must be used in this lab because the printf function cannot be used to directly access and display register values, which is required to verify that the code worked.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,4,'2025-05-14_10-12-18','The stack allocation method we used works by splitting up the stack into substacks. These substacks consist of one main stack, which holds setup and OS data, and any number of thread stacks, which are each dedicated to a line of execution. The MSP_init_val is a double pointer. It points to the address 0x0, which stores the memory address of start of the main stack. To get PSP_val, which will point to a start of a thread stack, we simply take MSP_init_val and subtract the maximum stack size. We must know the initial MSP value so that we can shift the maximum stack size bytes and end up with the memory location of the start of the subsequent thread stack.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,4,'2025-05-14_10-51-10','The stack allocation method we used works by splitting up the stack into substacks. These substacks consist of one main stack, which holds setup and OS data, and any number of thread stacks, which are each dedicated to a line of execution. The MSP_init_val is a double pointer. It points to the address 0x0, which stores the memory address of start of the main stack. To get PSP_val, which will point to a start of a thread stack, we simply take MSP_init_val and subtract the maximum stack size. We must know the initial MSP value so that we can shift the maximum stack size bytes and end up with the memory location of the start of the subsequent thread stack.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',1,4,'2025-05-14_13-20-22','The stack allocation method we used works by splitting up the stack into substacks. These substacks consist of one main stack, which holds setup and OS data, and any number of thread stacks, which are each dedicated to a line of execution. By subtracting 0x400 from the MSP_init_val, we can correctly obtain the memory location of the start of the next stack and ensure that the two stacks do not overlap.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',1,4,'2025-05-19_12-27-01','The stack allocation method we used works by subtracting 0x400 from the MSP_init_val, and then assigning the result to the PSP_val. This ensures that memory is allocate for the PSP stack correctly, ensuring that it does not overlap with the MSP stack.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',1,4,'2025-05-19_12-39-55','The stack allocation method we used works by subtracting 0x400 from the MSP_init_val, and then assigning the result to the PSP_val. Knowing the initial MSP value is required to ensure that memory is allocated for the PSP stack correctly and that it does not overlap with the MSP stack.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,5,'2025-05-14_10-12-18','If we know the initial location of the main stack, as well as the maximum stack size, we can create new thread stacks and know exactly where they start in memory. This is because the memory allocation for each stack will be padded to the maximum stack size, so all we must do to obtain the memory location for the start of any desired stack is to shift the initial MSP location by a number of bytes equal to the maximum stack size times the number of stacks we need to traverse.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',1,5,'2025-05-14_10-51-10','If we know the initial location of the main stack, as well as the maximum stack sizes, we can create new thread stacks and know exactly where they start in memory. The Cortex M4 architecture requires that all stack pointers must be 8-bit aligned, so we must set the maximum stack size for both the main stack and the thread stack to a multiple of 8. All we must do to obtain the memory location for the start of any desired stack is to shift the initial MSP location by a number of bytes equal to the maximum stack size times the number of stacks we need to traverse. To further verify that we have reached the memory location of the start of a stack, since the start of all stacks will be 8-bit aligned, the last three bits of the address must be either 000 or 100, which we can check.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',1,5,'2025-05-14_13-20-22','If we know the initial location of the main stack, we can create new thread stacks and know exactly where they start in memory. Since stacks are arranged as an array in memory, we can repeatedly subtract 0x400 from the initial MSP value to obtain the memory location for each of the new stacks. To reserve memory for each stack and ensure that they don’t overlap, we must store pointers to these memory locations in an array. Doing so requires that the number of stacks needed is known at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',1,5,'2025-05-19_12-27-01','If we know the initial location of the main stack, we can create new thread stacks and know exactly where they start in memory. Since stacks are arranged as an array in memory, we can repeatedly subtract 0x400 from the initial MSP value to obtain the memory location for each of the new stacks. To reserve memory for each stack and ensure that they don’t overlap, we must store pointers to these memory locations in an array. Doing so requires that the number of stacks needed is known at compile time.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',1,5,'2025-05-19_12-39-55','If we know the initial location of the main stack, we can create new thread stacks and know exactly where they start in memory. Since stacks are arranged as an array in memory, we can repeatedly subtract 0x400 from the initial MSP value to obtain the memory location for each of the new stacks. 0x400 is subtracted because the Cortex-M4 architecture requires that the memory address of all stacks be a multiple of 8 bits. To reserve memory for each stack and ensure that they don’t overlap, we must store pointers to these memory locations in an array. Doing so requires that the number of stacks needed is known at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',2,1,'2025-06-05_12-01-24','In this lab, we implemented the jump assembly function to move the memory address of the print continuously function from the R0 register to the program counter (PC) register. Once this switch is made, the CPU will move to the memory address defined in the PC register, which in this case if the print continuously function, and begin execution.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',2,1,'2025-06-05_12-47-03','In this lab, we implemented the jump assembly function to move the memory address of the print continuously function from the R0 register to the program counter (PC) register. Once this switch is made, the CPU will move to the memory address defined in the PC register, which in this case if the print continuously function, and begin execution.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',2,1,'2025-06-05_13-13-45','In this lab, we implemented the jump assembly function to move the memory address of the print continuously function from the R0 register to the program counter (PC) register. Once this switch is made, the CPU will move to the memory address defined in the PC register, which in this case if the print continuously function, and begin execution.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',2,2,'2025-06-05_12-01-24','A system call is a request made by a program to the operating system (OS). It is the primary way that user-level programs can interact with hardware, processes, and other functionality that are managed by the OS. In this lab, we wrote system call functions that were warppers of the __asm function. The __asm function makes a request to the OS with a system call number. We then use a case block within a switch statement to map system call numbers to our desired functionality for each.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',2,2,'2025-06-05_12-47-03','A system call is a request made by a program to the kernel of the operating system (OS). It is the primary way that user-level programs can interact with hardware and processes that are managed by the kernel and that they don’t have direct access to. These system calls maintain security while still providing user-level programs with the low-level functionality they need. In this lab, the __asm function makes a request to the OS with a system call number. We then use a case block within a switch statement to map system call numbers to our desired functionality for each.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',2,2,'2025-06-05_13-13-45','A system call is a function call invoked by a user-level program that requests a service from the kernel that the program cannot access directly. It is the primary way that user-level programs can interact with hardware and processes that are managed by the kernel and that they don’t have direct access to. These system calls maintain security while still providing user-level programs with the low-level functionality they need. In this lab, the __asm function makes a request to the OS with a system call number. We then use a case block within a switch statement to map system call numbers to our desired functionality for each.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',2,3,'2025-06-05_12-01-24','To set up a “thread’s stack” means to properly allocate space in memory with all required information so that the CPU can run the thread properly. First, we obtain a new stack pointer. We do this by initializing the stack pointer, and then continuously decrementing until we’ve pushed all the registers into the stack, thus setting up the stack’s context. Storing the register values in the stack as the thread’s context allows for maintain our threads. If we chose to come back to this thread later in our code, we can then load the thread context into the registers and the thread ca resume where it left off. Once this is done, we perform a system call which will use the __set_PSP function to switch the PSP’s current stack to our new thread stack, and the runFirstThread function will pop the register values from the thread stack and move them into the hardware registers.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',2,3,'2025-06-05_12-47-03','To set up a “thread’s stack” means to properly allocate space in memory with all required information so that the CPU can run the thread properly. First, we obtain a new stack pointer. We do this by initializing the stack pointer, and then continuously decrementing until we’ve pushed all the registers into the stack, thus setting up the stack’s context. Storing the register values in the stack as the thread’s context allows for maintain our threads. If we chose to come back to this thread later in our code, we can then load the thread context into the registers and the thread ca resume where it left off. Once this is done, we perform a system call which will use the __set_PSP function to switch the PSP’s current stack to our new thread stack, and the runFirstThread function will pop the register values from the thread stack and move them into the hardware registers.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',2,3,'2025-06-05_13-13-45','To set up a “thread’s stack” means to properly allocate space in memory with all required information so that the CPU can run the thread properly. First, we obtain a new stack pointer. We do this by initializing the stack pointer, and then continuously decrementing until we’ve pushed all the registers into the stack, thus setting up the stack’s context. Once this is done, we perform a system call which will use the __set_PSP function to switch the PSP’s current stack to our new thread stack, and the runFirstThread function will pop the register values from the thread stack and move them into the hardware registers. The order in which we push the registers into the stack is vital so that when the thread is resumed, it can properly restore the values into the hardware registers and continue from where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',2,4,'2025-06-05_12-01-24','We have found the memory location required to store the thread’s context and stored it into stackptr. Calling __set_PSP and passing stackptr to it is required to tell the microcontroller that this is the stack to use. If we don’t do this, the stack to use will be unknown and the code would be unable to setup the new stack, thus crashing with a fatal error.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',2,4,'2025-06-05_12-47-03','We have found the memory location required to store the thread’s context and stored it into stackptr. The Cortex M4 architecture requires that threads use the process stack pointer and not the main stack pointer to keep thread contexts and the system context separated. As such, calling __set_PSP and passing stackptr to it is required to tell the microcontroller that this is the stack to use. If we don’t do this, the stack pointer may point to an invalid memory location, triggering a hard fault in the CPU as it tries to access memory at an invalid address.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',2,4,'2025-06-05_13-13-45','We have found the memory location required to store the thread’s context and stored it into stackptr. The Cortex M4 architecture requires that threads use the process stack pointer and not the main stack pointer to keep thread contexts and the system context separated. As such, calling __set_PSP and passing stackptr to it is required to tell the microcontroller that this is the stack to use. If we don’t do this, the stack pointer may point to an invalid memory location, triggering a hard fault in the CPU as it tries to access memory at an invalid address.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',2,5,'2025-06-05_12-01-24','What would happen after the print continuously function returned would depend on what the function returned. The CPU expects a return address from the function that tells it where to go next. However, since the print continuously function returns nothing, the CPU does not know where to return, which can result in a corrupted stack or code crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',2,5,'2025-06-05_12-47-03','Normally when a process finishes, the CPU will look to the link register for where to go next. In the case of this lab, a value was not specifically put into the LR. If the function printed once and then returned, the CPU would attempt to return to an invalid address causing a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',2,5,'2025-06-05_13-13-45','Normally when a process finishes, the CPU will look to the link register for where to go next. In the case of thread execution, the LR should point to where the CPU before it began running the thread. In the scenario presented in the lab, a value was not specifically put into the LR because the function looped continuously and the LR was not intended to be reached. If the function instead returned after printing once, the CPU would attempt to return to an invalid address which would trigger a hard faut.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',3,1,'2025-06-26_16-30-39','The processing in which multiple files are compiled into a single executable is called linking. Linking is the last step in the process from code to executable. Linking is a complex process but put simply it involves ensuring all symbol references point to the correct memory space, merging sections such as text and data and aligning them in memory, bringing functions from external libraries into the executable, and then translating the result into machine code.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',3,1,'2025-06-27_13-50-17','This process is three-fold. First there is preprocessing, where the preprocessor prepares .c files a prepared for compilation by taking steps such as replacing #includes with the actual content, removing comments, and error and warning generation. Next is the actual compilation, where .c files are converted to object files (.o) (i.e. machine code). Finally, a program called the linker combines these object files into a single executable file by taking steps such as resolving symbols and mapping out memory.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',3,2,'2025-06-26_16-30-39','Using #include for a *.c file should not be done for a number of reasons. Most severely, it can lead to compilation errors because the linker will encounter repeat declarations of functions and variables if the *.c file is “included” multiple times.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',3,2,'2025-06-27_13-50-17','Using #include for a *.c file should not be done for a number of reasons. Most severely, it can lead to compilation errors because the linker will encounter repeat declarations of functions and variables if the *.c file is “included” multiple times.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',3,3,'2025-06-26_16-30-39','Step 0 – Call osKernelInitialize\nBefore I began to set up my thread, I make a call to osKernelInitialize, which sets up the kernel so that it can properly run my thread when I tell it to.\nStep 1 – Write Thread Function\nI write the function that I want my thread to run in the main file.\nStep 2 – Call osCreateThread\nI then make a call to the osCreateThread function and pass it the function I previously created. This will perform the necessary steps, like stack allocation to set up my thread.\nStep 2 – Call osKernelStart\nFinally, I make a call to osKernelStart, which will run the thread, and thus my function, I previously created.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',3,3,'2025-06-27_13-50-17','Step 0 – Call osKernelInitialize\nBefore I began to set up my thread, I make a call to osKernelInitialize, which sets up the kernel so that it can properly run my thread when I tell it to.\nStep 1 – Write Thread Function\nI write the function that I want my thread to run in the main file.\nStep 2 – Call osCreateThread\nI then make a call to the osCreateThread function and pass it the memory address of the function I previously created. This will perform the necessary steps, like stack allocation to set up my thread. The memory address of my function is passed to osCreateThread and subsequently pushed onto my thread’s stack so that once my thread starts running, the memory address will be loaded into the program counter (PC) and the kernel will be able to access my function.\nStep 3 – Call osKernelStart\nFinally, I make a call to osKernelStart, which will run the thread, and thus my function, I previously created.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',3,4,'2025-06-26_16-30-39','A certain amount of memory is allocated for the stacks of threads. This reserved memory forms the “stack pool”. Individual thread stacks of a fixed size are then allocated in this pool. Having a stack pool will make it easier to manage multiple threads in future labs, as we can quickly assign new thread stacks and access existing threads by simply shifting the stack pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',3,4,'2025-06-27_13-50-17','A certain amount of memory is allocated for the stacks of threads. This reserved memory forms the “stack pool”. Individual thread stacks of a fixed size are then allocated in this pool. Having a stack pool will make it easier to manage multiple threads in future labs, as we can quickly assign new thread stacks and access existing threads by simply shifting the stack pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',3,5,'2025-06-26_16-30-39','As a user, we are not concerned with how functions are run, and we assume that it is being run properly. We should not have to write our functions in the same file that has functions responsible for running or functions (i.e. kernel level functionality) as this makes organization difficult and it also runs the risk that users accidently mess with kernel code they don’t understand, which can have catastrophic consequences. This is why it is important that there are distinct files so that user code and kernel code can be stored separate from each other.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',3,5,'2025-06-27_13-50-17','User-level code refers to the implementation of the function we want our thread to run, as well as API calls made to the kernel that tell it to set up and run our thread. Kernel-level code takes care of low-level requirements to get our thread going, such as stack allocation and interfacing with the actual hardware registers to new a few. This distinction is important because as a user, we are not concerned with the complex kernel-level tasks such as the ones above and thus should not have to work in and around kernel-level code to implement our user-level code. We separate user and kernel code into their own separate files so that the user can write clean code that is easy to manage and so that they don’t run the risk of accidently modifying kernel-level logic.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,1,'2025-07-08_13-19-54','Round-robin involves cycling through the threads in a circular pattern so that each thread gets an equal amount of time to run. It is quite simple to implement round-robin using a queue. Suppose we are running the first thread. All we must do to cycle through the threads is to dequeue the thread at the front and begin running it while requeuing the thread we just finished running so that it will eventually reach the front of the queue and run again. Using a queue as opposed to an array to implement round-robin is beneficial because round-robin is inherently queue based. All we must do is use the queue as we normally would. While some pointer management is required, this is the case for any queue regardless of application. With an array, since it is not a fundamentally FIFO-based data structure, we must implement additional logic to increment the array and then cycle back, which involves some more variables and computations. Another benefit is that queues are more dynamic. Queues can grow and shrink as we add or remove more threads, whereas arrays are of fixed size so we must know exactly how many threads we need before runtime.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,1,'2025-07-14_13-06-22','Round-robin involves cycling through the threads in a circular pattern so that each thread gets an equal amount of time to run. It is quite simple to implement round-robin using a queue. Suppose we are running the first thread. All we must do to cycle through the threads is to dequeue the thread at the front and begin running it while requeuing the thread we just finished running so that it will eventually reach the front of the queue and run again. Using a queue as opposed to an array to implement round-robin is beneficial because round-robin is inherently queue based. All we must do is use the queue as we normally would. While some pointer management is required, this is the case for any queue regardless of application. With an array, since it is not a fundamentally FIFO-based data structure, we must implement additional logic to increment the array and then cycle back, which involves some more variables and computations. Another benefit is that queues are more dynamic. Queues can grow and shrink as we add or remove more threads, whereas arrays are of fixed size so we must know exactly how many threads we need before runtime.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,1,'2025-07-28_14-37-33','Round-robin involves cycling through the threads in a circular pattern so that each thread gets an equal amount of time to run. It is quite simple to implement round-robin using a queue. Suppose we are running the first thread. All we must do to cycle through the threads is to dequeue the thread at the front and begin running it while requeuing the thread we just finished running so that it will eventually reach the front of the queue and run again. Using a queue as opposed to an array to implement round-robin is beneficial because round-robin is inherently queue based. All we must do is use the queue as we normally would. While some pointer management is required, this is the case for any queue regardless of application. With an array, since it is not a fundamentally FIFO-based data structure, we must implement additional logic to increment the array and then cycle back, which involves some more variables and computations. Another benefit is that queues are more dynamic. Queues can grow and shrink as we add or remove more threads, whereas arrays are of fixed size so we must know exactly how many threads we need before runtime.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,2,'2025-07-08_13-19-54','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch in their main steps. Firstly, it reads the PSP value to identify the location of the current thread’s stack, and then pushes all the registers onto the stack, effectively saving the current thread’s context. Next, it makes a call to osSched, which we have implemented to identify the next thread to run. Finally, the PSP is set to the stack location of the thread selected by osSched, and the thread’s registers are popped off the stack and into the CPU registers.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',4,2,'2025-07-14_13-06-22','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch in three main steps. Firstly, it reads the PSP value to identify the location of the current thread’s stack, and then pushes the R4-R11 registers onto the stack, effectively saving the current thread’s context. Next, it makes a call to osSched, which we have implemented to identify the next thread to run using the round-robin scheduling algorithm. Finally, the PSP is set to the stack location of the thread selected by osSched, and the thread’s R4-R11 registers are popped off the stack and into the CPU registers to restore the thread’s context before the CPU resumes its execution.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,2,'2025-07-28_14-37-33','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch in three main steps. Firstly, it reads the PSP value to identify the location of the current thread’s stack, and then pushes the R4-R11 registers onto the stack, effectively saving the current thread’s context. Next, it makes a call to osSched, which we have implemented to identify the next thread to run using the round-robin scheduling algorithm. Finally, the PSP is set to the stack location of the thread selected by osSched, and the thread’s R4-R11 registers are popped off the stack and into the CPU registers to restore the thread’s context before the CPU resumes its execution.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,3,'2025-07-08_13-19-54','This is a bad idea because it can lead to poor fairness and starvation. If there are frequent high priority tasks, they could “hog” the CPUs resources, and processes of a lower priority may never end up running (i.e. starvation).\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,3,'2025-07-14_13-06-22','This is a bad idea because it can lead to poor fairness and starvation. If there are frequent high priority tasks, they could “hog” the CPUs resources, and processes of a lower priority may never end up running (i.e. starvation).\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',4,3,'2025-07-28_14-37-33','This is a bad idea because it can lead to poor fairness and starvation. If there are frequent high priority tasks, they could “hog” the CPUs resources. Regardless of its importance to the system, a task that never has the highest integer priority of all tasks will never get selected to run.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,4,'2025-07-08_13-19-54','The benefits and downsides of writing and calling C functions as opposed to everything doing everything in assembly are akin to the trade-offs of low-level versus high-level programming languages. Writing and calling C functions improves readability and is easier to manage. We can also define higher-level and more complicated data structures (such as arrays and queues) without worrying too much about what they look like in the registers and memory. On the other hand, we have less control over those lower-level details such as register and memory management, making it more difficult to optimize for performance-critical system tasks. Ultimately, we chose to handle thread scheduling and management in C by calling functions like osSched and SVX_HandlerMain.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,4,'2025-07-14_13-06-22','The benefits and downsides of writing and calling C functions as opposed to everything doing everything in assembly are akin to the trade-offs of low-level versus high-level programming languages. Writing and calling C functions improves readability and is easier to manage. We can also define higher-level and more complicated data structures (such as arrays and queues) without worrying too much about what they look like in the registers and memory. On the other hand, we have less control over those lower-level details such as register and memory management, making it more difficult to optimize for performance-critical system tasks. Ultimately, we chose to handle thread scheduling and management in C by calling functions like osSched and SVX_HandlerMain.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,4,'2025-07-28_14-37-33','The benefits and downsides of writing and calling C functions as opposed to everything doing everything in assembly are akin to the trade-offs of low-level versus high-level programming languages. Writing and calling C functions improves readability and is easier to manage. We can also define higher-level and more complicated data structures (such as arrays and queues) without worrying too much about what they look like in the registers and memory. On the other hand, we have less control over those lower-level details such as register and memory management, making it more difficult to optimize for performance-critical system tasks. Ultimately, we chose to handle thread scheduling and management in C by calling functions like osSched and SVX_HandlerMain.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,5,'2025-07-08_13-19-54','Yes, the OS will still work because we have set up our scheduler so that it can still work with one thread. When osSched is called, it will increment the current_thread integer and then mod it by the number of threads, leading to 1 mod 1 which equals 0, so the current_thread integer remains unchanged. The same thread will be run again.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',4,5,'2025-07-14_13-06-22','Yes, the OS will still work because we have set up our scheduler so that it can still work with one thread. The thread will being running and print once before calling osYield() which triggers a context switch. As only one thread has been created, the scheduler will select this thread again, creating a continuous loop where this thread and the print_continously() function run repeatedly.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',4,5,'2025-07-28_14-37-33','Yes, the OS will still work because we have set up our scheduler so that it can still work with one thread. The thread will being running and print once before calling osYield() which triggers a context switch. As only one thread has been created, the scheduler will select this thread again, creating a continuous loop where this thread and the print_continously() function run repeatedly.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,1,'2025-07-28_14-59-16','First, the user must create a pointer to the data they wish to pass. They then provide this pointer as an input when calling osCreateThread or osCreateThreadWithDeadlone. The OS uses the pointer in the TCB when setting up the threads initial stack. The OS places this pointer in the current location in the stack so that R0 is loaded with the argument during the context switch. This works because conventionally, the ARM Cortex expects that the first function arguments be loaded in R0.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,1,'2025-07-28_15-22-12','First, the user must create a pointer to the data they wish to pass to the thread and supply this pointer as an argument when calling osCreateThread or osCreateThreadWithDeadline. The OS stores this pointer in the thread control block (TCB) and uses it when initializing the thread’s stack. Specifically, the OS arranges the initial stack frame such that the value pointed to by the user is placed at the offset corresponding to register R0 in the context-switch frame. This ensures that when the thread begins execution, R0 is preloaded with the argument. This mechanism works because the ARM architecture requires that the first argument to a function must be passed in R0.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,1,'2025-07-28_15-39-27','First, the user must create a pointer to the data they wish to pass to the thread and supply this pointer as an argument when calling osCreateThread or osCreateThreadWithDeadline. The OS stores this pointer in the thread control block (TCB) and uses it when initializing the thread’s stack. Specifically, the OS arranges the initial stack frame such that the value pointed to by the user is placed at the offset corresponding to register R0 in the context-switch frame. This ensures that when the thread begins execution, R0 is preloaded with the argument. This mechanism works because the ARM architecture requires that the first argument to a function must be passed in R0.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,1,'2025-07-28_15-57-28','First, the user must create a pointer to the data they wish to pass to the thread and supply this pointer as an argument when calling osCreateThread or osCreateThreadWithDeadline. The OS stores this pointer in the thread control block (TCB) and uses it when initializing the thread’s stack. Specifically, the OS arranges the initial stack frame such that the value pointed to by the user is placed at the offset corresponding to register R0 in the context-switch frame. This ensures that when the thread begins execution, R0 is preloaded with the argument. This mechanism works because the ARM architecture requires that the first argument to a function must be passed in R0.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,1,'2025-07-29_13-38-10','First, the user must create a pointer to the data they wish to pass to the thread and supply this pointer as an argument when calling osCreateThread or osCreateThreadWithDeadline. The OS stores this pointer in the thread control block (TCB) and uses it when initializing the thread’s stack. Specifically, the OS arranges the initial stack frame such that the value pointed to by the user is placed at the offset corresponding to register R0 in the context-switch frame. This ensures that when the thread begins execution, R0 is preloaded with the argument. This mechanism works because the ARM architecture requires that the first argument to a function must be passed in R0.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',5,1,'2025-07-29_13-49-28','First, the user must create a pointer to the data they wish to pass to the thread and supply this pointer as an argument when calling osCreateThread or osCreateThreadWithDeadline. The OS stores this pointer in the thread control block (TCB) and uses it when initializing the thread’s stack. Specifically, the OS arranges the initial stack frame such that the value pointed to by the user is placed at the offset corresponding to register R0 in the context-switch frame. This ensures that when the thread begins execution, R0 is preloaded with the argument. This mechanism works because the ARM architecture requires that the first argument to a function must be passed in R0.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,2,'2025-07-28_14-59-16','Calling osYield in any of the context-switching functions is a bad idea and potentially dangerous. When these context-switching functions are running, a context switch is actively being performed and calling osYield would attempt to trigger a nested context switch. This can corrupt the interrupt stack that the CPU is currently using, potentially resulting in a hard fault or system crash.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',5,2,'2025-07-28_15-22-12','Calling osYield inside any of the context-switching functions is a bad idea and dangerous because it attempts to initiate a new context switch while one is already in progress. At a low level, osYield works by setting the PendSV bit, which triggers the PendSV exception to perform a context switch. However, if the processor is already executing in an exception handler it is in Handler mode and already using the interrupt stack. Triggering another PendSV interrupt during this time does not cause an immediate interrupt, but it defers execution until after the current handler finishes. But if osYield is misused recursively or repeatedly in this context, it can corrupt the exception stack, break the integrity of the context save/restore process, and lead to unpredictable behavior, such as a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,2,'2025-07-28_15-39-27','Calling osYield inside any of the context-switching functions is a bad idea and dangerous because it attempts to initiate a new context switch while one is already in progress. At a low level, osYield works by setting the PendSV bit, which triggers the PendSV exception to perform a context switch. However, if the processor is already executing in an exception handler it is in Handler mode and already using the interrupt stack. Triggering another PendSV interrupt during this time does not cause an immediate interrupt, but it defers execution until after the current handler finishes. But if osYield is misused recursively or repeatedly in this context, it can corrupt the exception stack, break the integrity of the context save/restore process, and lead to unpredictable behavior, such as a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,2,'2025-07-28_15-57-28','Calling osYield inside any of the context-switching functions is a bad idea and dangerous because it attempts to initiate a new context switch while one is already in progress. At a low level, osYield works by setting the PendSV bit, which triggers the PendSV exception to perform a context switch. However, if the processor is already executing in an exception handler it is in Handler mode and already using the interrupt stack. Triggering another PendSV interrupt during this time does not cause an immediate interrupt, but it defers execution until after the current handler finishes. But if osYield is misused recursively or repeatedly in this context, it can corrupt the exception stack, break the integrity of the context save/restore process, and lead to unpredictable behavior, such as a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,2,'2025-07-29_13-38-10','Calling osYield inside any of the context-switching functions is a bad idea and dangerous because it attempts to initiate a new context switch while one is already in progress. At a low level, osYield works by setting the PendSV bit, which triggers the PendSV exception to perform a context switch. However, if the processor is already executing in an exception handler it is in Handler mode and already using the interrupt stack. Triggering another PendSV interrupt during this time does not cause an immediate interrupt, but it defers execution until after the current handler finishes. But if osYield is misused recursively or repeatedly in this context, it can corrupt the exception stack, break the integrity of the context save/restore process, and lead to unpredictable behavior, such as a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,2,'2025-07-29_13-49-28','Calling osYield inside any of the context-switching functions is a bad idea and dangerous because it attempts to initiate a new context switch while one is already in progress. At a low level, osYield works by setting the PendSV bit, which triggers the PendSV exception to perform a context switch. However, if the processor is already executing in an exception handler it is in Handler mode and already using the interrupt stack. Triggering another PendSV interrupt during this time does not cause an immediate interrupt, but it defers execution until after the current handler finishes. But if osYield is misused recursively or repeatedly in this context, it can corrupt the exception stack, break the integrity of the context save/restore process, and lead to unpredictable behavior, such as a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,3,'2025-07-28_14-59-16','The upside of changing the fundamental OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms is that system responsiveness would be improved. We will have a better response time for taking care of interrupts, and we can adjust or thread runtime/timeslice to be even finer. The drawback is the increase in CPU overhead. Taking care of interrupts and context switches more frequently means less time for actual thread execution, potentially reducing system efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,3,'2025-07-28_15-22-12','Reducing the fundamental OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms increases system responsiveness, allowing the OS to handle interrupts and perform context switches with finer granularity. This enables more precise control over thread timeslices and can benefit latency-sensitive applications, such as those in real-time control systems or audio processing. However, this comes at the cost of significantly higher CPU overhead. More frequent timer interrupts mean the processor spends more time handling context switches and less time executing application threads, which can degrade overall system efficiency. In systems with limited processing power or battery life, this trade-off might not be worth it.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',5,3,'2025-07-28_15-39-27','In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS is fully responsible for managing context switches. The user does not need to include any explicit yielding in thread code. Instead, the OS uses a hardware timer (like SysTick) to periodically interrupt the running thread and forcibly trigger a context switch, even if the thread is not cooperating. This ensures that all threads get CPU time, regardless of their behavior. In contrast, co-operative multitasking relies on the threads themselves to yield control. The user must manually insert calls to osYield in the thread code to indicate when a context switch should occur. If a co-operative thread never calls osYield, it can block all other threads, making the system unresponsive. Thus, in co-operative systems, fairness and responsiveness depend on well-behaved threads, while in pre-emptive systems, the OS enforces them.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',5,3,'2025-07-28_15-57-28','In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS is fully responsible for managing context switches. The user does not need to include any explicit yielding in thread code. Instead, the OS uses a hardware timer (like SysTick) to periodically interrupt the running thread and forcibly trigger a context switch, even if the thread is not cooperating. This ensures that all threads get CPU time, regardless of their behavior. In contrast, co-operative multitasking relies on the threads themselves to yield control. The user must manually insert calls to osYield in the thread code to indicate when a context switch should occur. If a co-operative thread never calls osYield, it can block all other threads, making the system unresponsive. Thus, in co-operative systems, fairness and responsiveness depend on well-behaved threads, while in pre-emptive systems, the OS enforces them.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',5,3,'2025-07-29_13-38-10','Testing testing 1,2, 3.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',5,3,'2025-07-29_13-49-28','Reducing the fundamental OS clock from 1 ms to 0.1 ms increases system responsiveness, allowing the OS to handle interrupts and context switches with finer granularity. This provides more concise control over thread time slices, which can be beneficial for latency-sensitive and real-time applications. However, this comes at the cost of higher CPU overhead. More frequency timer interrupts means that the CPU spends more time handling context switches and less time on actual thread execution, which can degrade overall system efficiency. In systems with limited processing power or battery life, this trade-off might not be worthwhile.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,4,'2025-07-28_14-59-16','Pre-emptive multitasking is taken care of entirely by the OS. The user does not have to build context switches into the thread functionality, and instead the OS will trigger a context switch according to the threads specified timeslice (or the default timeslice if not specified). In co-operative multitasking, context switches are requested by the thread and explicitly built-in to the thread functionality. Co-operative threads will call the osYield function, which triggers and interrupt telling the OS to perform a context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('m6riley',5,4,'2025-07-28_15-22-12','In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS is fully responsible for managing context switches. The user does not need to include any explicit yielding in thread code. Instead, the OS uses a hardware timer (like SysTick) to periodically interrupt the running thread and forcibly trigger a context switch, even if the thread is not cooperating. This ensures that all threads get CPU time, regardless of their behavior. In contrast, co-operative multitasking relies on the threads themselves to yield control. The user must manually insert calls to osYield in the thread code to indicate when a context switch should occur. If a co-operative thread never calls osYield, it can block all other threads, making the system unresponsive. Thus, in co-operative systems, fairness and responsiveness depend on well-behaved threads, while in pre-emptive systems, the OS enforces them.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,4,'2025-07-28_15-39-27','These two mechanisms work together because both use the same unified context-switching pathway: the PendSV interrupt. Whether a thread’s timeslice expires (pre-emptive) or it explicitly calls osYield (co-operative), the system sets the PendSV bit to trigger the PendSV_Handler, which performs the context switch. This shared handler ensures consistent behavior and simplifies the OS design by centralizing context-switching logic. While the trigger method differs, the end result is the same. This allows the system to balance responsiveness and fairness: threads can voluntarily yield when appropriate, while the OS still guarantees progress by pre-empting threads that don\'t.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,4,'2025-07-28_15-57-28','These two mechanisms work together because both use the same unified context-switching pathway: the PendSV interrupt. Whether a thread’s timeslice expires (pre-emptive) or it explicitly calls osYield (co-operative), the system sets the PendSV bit to trigger the PendSV_Handler, which performs the context switch. This shared handler ensures consistent behavior and simplifies the OS design by centralizing context-switching logic. While the trigger method differs, the end result is the same. This allows the system to balance responsiveness and fairness: threads can voluntarily yield when appropriate, while the OS still guarantees progress by pre-empting threads that don\'t.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,4,'2025-07-29_13-38-10','In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS is fully responsible for managing context switches. The user does not need to include any explicit yielding in thread code. Instead, the OS uses a hardware timer (like SysTick) to periodically interrupt the running thread and forcibly trigger a context switch, even if the thread is not cooperating. This ensures that all threads get CPU time, regardless of their behavior. In contrast, co-operative multitasking relies on the threads themselves to yield control. The user must manually insert calls to osYield in the thread code to indicate when a context switch should occur. If a co-operative thread never calls osYield, it can block all other threads, making the system unresponsive. Thus, in co-operative systems, fairness and responsiveness depend on well-behaved threads, while in pre-emptive systems, the OS enforces them.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,4,'2025-07-29_13-49-28','In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS is fully responsible for managing context switches. The user does not need to include any explicit yielding in thread code. Instead, the OS uses a hardware timer (like SysTick) to periodically interrupt the running thread and forcibly trigger a context switch, even if the thread is not cooperating. This ensures that all threads get CPU time, regardless of their behavior. In contrast, co-operative multitasking relies on the threads themselves to yield control. The user must manually insert calls to osYield in the thread code to indicate when a context switch should occur. If a co-operative thread never calls osYield, it can block all other threads, making the system unresponsive. Thus, in co-operative systems, fairness and responsiveness depend on well-behaved threads, while in pre-emptive systems, the OS enforces them.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,5,'2025-07-28_14-59-16','These two mechanisms can work together because they were built such that they both trigger a context switch via the PendSV interrupt. If either the threads runtime reaches its timeslice, or the it explicitly triggers osYield, the PendSV interrupt will occur which results in PendSV_Handler to be run which performs the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,5,'2025-07-28_15-22-12','These two mechanisms work together because both use the same unified context-switching pathway: the PendSV interrupt. Whether a thread’s timeslice expires (pre-emptive) or it explicitly calls osYield (co-operative), the system sets the PendSV bit to trigger the PendSV_Handler, which performs the context switch. This shared handler ensures consistent behavior and simplifies the OS design by centralizing context-switching logic. While the trigger method differs, the end result is the same. This allows the system to balance responsiveness and fairness: threads can voluntarily yield when appropriate, while the OS still guarantees progress by pre-empting threads that don\'t.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,5,'2025-07-29_13-38-10','These two mechanisms work together because both use the same unified context-switching pathway: the PendSV interrupt. Whether a thread’s timeslice expires (pre-emptive) or it explicitly calls osYield (co-operative), the system sets the PendSV bit to trigger the PendSV_Handler, which performs the context switch. This shared handler ensures consistent behavior and simplifies the OS design by centralizing context-switching logic. While the trigger method differs, the end result is the same. This allows the system to balance responsiveness and fairness: threads can voluntarily yield when appropriate, while the OS still guarantees progress by pre-empting threads that don\'t.\n',1,'s25'),('m6riley',5,5,'2025-07-29_13-49-28','These two mechanisms work together because both use the same unified context-switching pathway: the PendSV interrupt. Whether a thread’s timeslice expires (pre-emptive) or it explicitly calls osYield (co-operative), the system sets the PendSV bit to trigger the PendSV_Handler, which performs the context switch. This shared handler ensures consistent behavior and simplifies the OS design by centralizing context-switching logic. While the trigger method differs, the end result is the same. This allows the system to balance responsiveness and fairness: threads can voluntarily yield when appropriate, while the OS still guarantees progress by pre-empting threads that don\'t.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',1,1,'2025-06-27_10-29-37','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a library provided by STMicroelectronics that simplifies hardware interactions by abstracting away low-level register programming. It acts as setup code for the microcontroller and provides standardized, reusable functions to configure and use peripherals like GPIOs, UARTs, and timers. This reduces complexity, accelerates development, and ensures portability across different STM32 boards by allowing developers to write code using familiar, high-level APIs instead of dealing with hardware-specific details.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',1,1,'2025-06-27_10-55-21','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a library provided by STMicroelectronics that simplifies hardware interactions by abstracting away low-level register programming. It acts as setup code for the microcontroller and provides standardized, reusable functions to configure and use peripherals like GPIOs, UARTs, and timers. This reduces complexity, accelerates development, and ensures portability across different STM32 boards by allowing developers to write code using familiar, high-level APIs instead of dealing with hardware-specific details.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',1,2,'2025-06-27_10-29-37','__io_putchar is a low-level function that connects printf to a hardware output, like UART. Since embedded systems have no built-in console, printf needs help to send characters to a terminal. By defining __io_putchar to use HAL_UART_Transmit, each character printed by printf is transmitted over the serial port. Without this, printf would either do nothing or rely on semihosting, which can hang if no debugger is attached. This function bridges high-level formatted output to low-level serial hardware in a portable way.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',1,2,'2025-06-27_10-55-21','__io_putchar is a low-level function that connects printf to a hardware output, like UART. Since embedded systems have no built-in console, printf needs help to send characters to a terminal. By defining __io_putchar to use HAL_UART_Transmit, each character printed by printf is transmitted over the serial port. Without this, printf would either do nothing or rely on semihosting, which can hang if no debugger is attached. This function bridges high-level formatted output to low-level serial hardware in a portable way.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',1,3,'2025-06-27_10-29-37','We used the debugger to inspect register values like MSP and PSP directly, without modifying the system state. Using printf to print these values isn’t reliable -- printf itself pushes data onto the stack and only works after UART setup, potentially altering the values we’re trying to observe. The debugger allows precise control with breakpoints and lets us view internal states like stack pointers before and after stack changes, making it the right tool to verify our stack allocation logic.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',1,3,'2025-06-27_10-55-21','We used the debugger to inspect register values like MSP and PSP directly, without modifying the system state. Using printf to print these values isn’t reliable -- printf itself pushes data onto the stack and only works after UART setup, potentially altering the values we’re trying to observe. The debugger allows precise control with breakpoints and lets us view internal states like stack pointers before and after stack changes, making it the right tool to verify our stack allocation logic.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',1,4,'2025-06-27_10-29-37','We obtained the value of MSP_init_val by dereferencing address 0x0, which holds the initial top of the main stack. Since stacks grow downward, we calculated PSP_val by subtracting a fixed stack size (e.g., 0x400) from MSP_init_val. This ensures PSP starts in valid memory below the main stack. Knowing MSP_init_val is crucial -- it defines the usable top of RAM, and incorrect values could result in stack overlap, corruption, or undefined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',1,4,'2025-06-27_10-55-21','We obtained the value of MSP_init_val by dereferencing address 0x0, which holds the initial top of the main stack. Since stacks grow downward, we calculated PSP_val by subtracting a fixed stack size (e.g., 0x400) from MSP_init_val. This ensures PSP starts in valid memory below the main stack. Knowing MSP_init_val is crucial -- it defines the usable top of RAM, and incorrect values could result in stack overlap, corruption, or undefined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',1,5,'2025-06-27_10-29-37','To allocate multiple thread stacks, we would first define a THREAD_STACK_SIZE and use the known MSP_init_val as a base reference. From there, each stack’s start address would be calculated as:\nPSP_values[i] = MSP_init_val - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (i + 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE;\nThis ensures stacks are non-overlapping and aligned, since each thread gets its own memory region offset from a fixed origin. We would store these PSP values in an array and assign them using __set_PSP() when needed. The CONTROL register can be updated to switch to PSP mode with __set_CONTROL(2).\n',0,'s25'),('mamara',1,5,'2025-06-27_10-55-21','To allocate multiple thread stacks at compile-time, we must first define THREAD_STACK_SIZE and determine the initial MSP value using the dereference method:\nuint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0;\nGiven a fixed MAIN_STACK_SIZE, we offset each new stack downwards from the initial MSP value. For thread i, the starting address of its stack would be:\nstack[i] = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (i + 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE;\nThis ensures each stack is placed in a distinct region of memory, prevents overlap, and respects the downward-growing nature of ARM Cortex-M stacks. Additionally, we must guarantee that each stack address is word-aligned (4-byte boundary) and within the valid RAM range (e.g., 0x20000000–0x20018000 for 96KB RAM). Failure to check these could lead to stack corruption or faults. Thus, our code must verify that:\nstack[i] >= RAM_START && stack[i] + THREAD_STACK_SIZE <= RAM_END\nwhere RAM_START = 0x20000000 and RAM_END = 0x20018000.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',2,1,'2025-06-27_15-56-07','When we changed the Program Counter (PC) to point to our print_continuously function, the CPU began executing that function as if it were the next instruction. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop with printf statements, the system entered that loop and continuously printed messages. This simulates switching control to a new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',2,1,'2025-06-27_16-06-53','When we changed the Program Counter (PC) to point to the print_continuously function, the CPU began executing instructions starting at that function\'s memory address. Since that function contains an infinite loop calling printf, the system entered that loop and continuously printed to the serial terminal. This demonstrates how changing PC allows us to transfer control to a new thread-like context. The CPU treated this function pointer as the next instruction to execute, which is equivalent to a jump. Because the function never returns, the CPU remains trapped in that loop.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',2,2,'2025-06-27_15-56-07','A system call is a way for a user-level program to request a service or privileged operation from the operating system or kernel. In this lab, we used the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction to trigger a software interrupt, which transferred control to a handler function (SVC_Handler_Main) that executed specific logic depending on the system call number.\n',0,'s25'),('mamara',2,2,'2025-06-27_16-06-53','A system call is a special kind of function that allows unprivileged user code to request services from privileged kernel code, like accessing hardware or performing memory operations. In embedded systems, this is typically done using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction, which triggers a software interrupt and switches execution to a predefined handler. This mechanism enforces hardware protection by ensuring only trusted, kernel-level code can directly interact with sensitive resources. Without system calls, user code might compromise system stability or security.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',2,3,'2025-06-27_15-56-07','To set up a thread’s stack, we manually placed the expected register values onto the stack in reverse order, including the function’s return address, xPSR, and general-purpose registers. This mimics how the processor expects a thread’s context to appear before execution. Storing these values is essential so that when the CPU switches to the new thread and loads from this stack, it can restore the correct execution state and begin running the new function seamlessly.\n',0,'s25'),('mamara',2,3,'2025-06-27_16-06-53','Setting up a thread’s stack involves two main steps: first, allocating a specific memory region for that thread (its stack space), and second, preloading that stack with a fake CPU context that mimics what the processor expects during an exception return. This includes pushing values like xPSR, the thread function address (as if it were a return address), and initial values for registers like R0–R3, LR, and PC. This setup is required so that when we set PSP to point to this stack and perform an exception return, the CPU can pop all these values back into registers and begin executing the thread normally. This is how context switching works safely.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',2,4,'2025-06-27_15-56-07','If we didn’t set the PSP (Process Stack Pointer), the CPU would try to load the thread’s register context from an undefined or incorrect memory location. Since the PSP is used in thread mode to manage stack operations, failing to initialize it properly leads to stack corruption or invalid memory access, causing a hard fault crash.\n',0,'s25'),('mamara',2,4,'2025-06-27_16-06-53','If we don’t set the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to the base of the thread’s stack before switching to thread mode, the CPU will attempt to pop register values from whatever address PSP happens to hold—which may be zero or an invalid address. This leads to a hard fault due to an illegal memory access. The MSP (Main Stack Pointer) is used by default during startup, but threads are designed to use the PSP. To ensure the thread runs from its own stack, we must explicitly set the PSP to point to the prepared memory region containing the thread’s context.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',2,5,'2025-06-27_15-56-07','If the thread function returned after executing, the CPU would try to pop a return address (LR) from the stack and continue executing from there. However, since the return address and execution context weren’t set up for a clean return to another function, this would likely lead to jumping to an invalid memory location, causing unpredictable behavior or a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('mamara',2,5,'2025-06-27_16-06-53','If the thread function returned instead of looping, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the thread’s stack to resume execution. However, in our setup, the thread’s stack does not include a valid return address—only a dummy xPSR and the address of the thread function (as if it were the return address). Since no real caller exists and no valid return address was stored, returning from the thread would cause the CPU to jump to an invalid memory address. This results in a hard fault because the processor attempts to execute instructions from an undefined or restricted area of memory.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',3,1,'2025-06-27_16-39-07','Each .c file in a project is compiled separately into an object file (.o). During compilation, the compiler translates each source file into machine code but doesn’t yet combine them. Once all .c files are compiled, the linker takes all the .o files and links them together, resolving references between functions and variables across files. The result is a single executable binary that can run on the microcontroller.\n',0,'s25'),('mamara',3,1,'2025-06-27_16-48-54','The process begins with preprocessing, where the compiler handles all #include, #define, and other preprocessor directives. This step effectively expands the source code by including headers and replacing macros. Next is compilation, where each .c file is independently translated into machine-level object code (.o files). After all source files are compiled, the linker combines the resulting object files into a single executable by resolving function calls and shared symbols between files. This modular process allows different .c files to interact while keeping code organized and reusable.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',3,2,'2025-06-27_16-39-07','Including a .c file using #include causes its contents to be copy-pasted into another file before compilation, leading to duplicate definitions and compilation errors. This defeats the purpose of separate compilation and breaks modularity. Instead, we use #include for header (.h) files, which contain declarations, while .c files are compiled independently.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',3,2,'2025-06-27_16-48-54','Including a .c file using #include causes its contents to be copy-pasted into another file before compilation, leading to duplicate definitions and compilation errors. This defeats the purpose of separate compilation and breaks modularity. Instead, we use #include for header (.h) files, which contain declarations, while .c files are compiled independently.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',3,3,'2025-06-27_16-39-07','Write the function you want the thread to run.\nCall a function to initialize the kernel.\nCall another function to create the thread using the function you wrote.\nFinally, call a function to start the kernel and begin running the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',3,3,'2025-06-27_16-48-54','Write the function you want the thread to run.\nCall a function to initialize the kernel.\nCall another function to create the thread using the function you wrote.\nFinally, call a function to start the kernel and begin running the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',3,4,'2025-06-27_16-39-07','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used to allocate stacks for threads. Instead of giving all threads the same stack, each thread gets a separate portion of the stack pool. In future labs, we will allocate one stack per thread from this pool, allowing multiple threads to have independent execution contexts and enabling multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',3,4,'2025-06-27_16-48-54','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory used to allocate stacks for threads. Instead of giving all threads the same stack, each thread gets a separate portion of the stack pool. In future labs, we will allocate one stack per thread from this pool, allowing multiple threads to have independent execution contexts and enabling multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',3,5,'2025-06-27_16-39-07','The kernel contains the core OS logic like thread management and stack allocation, and is implemented in kernel.c. The user code includes the main function and thread functions, and lives in main.c. This separation is important because it keeps the system modular—users can create and run threads without needing to know or change how the kernel works. It also makes debugging and future expansion easier.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',3,5,'2025-06-27_16-48-54','The kernel contains the core OS logic like thread management and stack allocation, and is implemented in kernel.c. The user code includes the main function and thread functions, and lives in main.c. This separation is important because it keeps the system modular—users can create and run threads without needing to know or change how the kernel works. It also makes debugging and future expansion easier.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',4,1,'2025-07-30_19-43-07','In round-robin scheduling with a queue, each thread’s TCB (thread control block) gets placed into the queue in the order it’s supposed to run. When a thread finishes its turn or yields, the scheduler dequeues it, updates its stack pointer, and enqueues it again at the back of the queue. This automatically cycles through threads in a fair loop. Compared to an array, a queue means I don’t have to manually manage the currentThread index and do modulus math — the data structure handles ordering for me. It also makes adding or removing threads easier, since I don’t have to worry about leaving unused spaces in the array. The downside is a little extra overhead from enqueue/dequeue operations, but functionally it behaves the same as my current array-based system, just more flexible if thread counts change.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',4,2,'2025-07-30_19-43-07','When PendSV_Handler runs, it first looks at the PSP (process stack pointer) for the current thread. It saves that thread’s registers (R4–R11) onto its stack so the thread’s state isn’t lost. Then it calls my osSched() function in C, which updates currentThread to the next thread and sets the PSP to that new thread’s saved stack pointer. After that, PendSV_Handler restores the new thread’s registers from its stack. Once everything is loaded, the processor continues executing the new thread right where it paused earlier. This lets my kernel switch between threads smoothly, even though only one is running at a time.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',4,3,'2025-07-30_19-43-07','If the scheduler always runs the highest-priority thread, then that thread will hog the CPU whenever it’s ready. Lower-priority threads would basically never run, which is called starvation. That breaks fairness and can leave important work undone just because the scheduler never gives time to anything except the “top” thread. Real OS schedulers that use priorities combine it with things like time-slicing or priority aging to make sure even low-priority tasks eventually get CPU time. Without that, the OS is no longer multitasking — it just becomes a single-thread system for the highest-priority job.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',4,4,'2025-07-30_19-43-07','Using C functions makes the kernel logic much easier to write and read. For example, my osSched() in C clearly handles updating currentThread and PSP without me having to write all of that in assembly. It also makes debugging and extending the code simpler. The downside is that calling from assembly into C adds some overhead, and I give up some low-level control. If I wrote everything in pure assembly, it might run slightly faster and be more predictable. But it would also be harder to maintain or change later, so using C strikes a good balance for this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',4,5,'2025-07-30_19-43-07','The OS would still run, but yielding wouldn’t actually change anything. Since there’s only one thread in the thread array, the scheduler would just select the same thread again every time. The context switch logic would still happen (PendSV, osSched, etc.), but there would be no visible alternation like with two threads. Basically, it would behave like a regular infinite loop program, just with some extra overhead from the kernel. It doesn’t break the OS, but it also doesn’t give any benefit from multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,1,'2025-07-30_20-43-24','The user starts by creating the data they want a thread to use — for example, an integer or a struct — and then passes a pointer to it when calling osCreateThread. Inside the OS, that pointer is stored in the thread’s stack frame so that when the CPU begins running the thread, it shows up in the first function argument. On the Cortex-M4, this is handled by loading the pointer into the R0 register, following the standard calling convention. Because our kernel builds the stack in this format, the thread automatically receives the pointer argument when it starts executing.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,1,'2025-07-30_20-54-41','The user starts by creating the data they want a thread to use — for example, an integer or a struct — and then passes a pointer to it when calling osCreateThread. Inside the OS, that pointer is stored in the thread’s stack frame so that when the CPU begins running the thread, it shows up in the first function argument. On the Cortex-M4, this is handled by loading the pointer into the R0 register, following the standard calling convention. Because our kernel builds the stack in this format, the thread automatically receives the pointer argument when it starts executing.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,1,'2025-07-30_22-09-19','The user starts by creating the data they want a thread to use — for example, an integer or a struct — and then passes a pointer to it when calling osCreateThread. Inside the OS, that pointer is stored in the thread’s stack frame so that when the CPU begins running the thread, it shows up in the first function argument. On the Cortex-M4, this is handled by loading the pointer into the R0 register, following the standard calling convention. Because our kernel builds the stack in this format, the thread automatically receives the pointer argument when it starts executing.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,2,'2025-07-30_20-43-24','osYield works by triggering a supervisor call (SVC), which is itself an interrupt. If we try to call osYield from inside a handler like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler, we end up asking for a new interrupt while one is already being serviced. That causes nested context switches, corrupting the stack frame and potentially breaking the scheduler. Since these handlers are in the middle of saving or restoring registers, interrupting them can overwrite critical state, leading to unpredictable behavior or a hard fault. In short, calling osYield inside those handlers risks crashing the system because they are not re-entrant.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,2,'2025-07-30_20-54-41','osYield works by triggering a supervisor call (SVC), which is itself an interrupt. If we try to call osYield from inside a handler like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler, we end up asking for a new interrupt while one is already being serviced. That causes nested context switches, corrupting the stack frame and potentially breaking the scheduler. Since these handlers are in the middle of saving or restoring registers, interrupting them can overwrite critical state, leading to unpredictable behavior or a hard fault. In short, calling osYield inside those handlers risks crashing the system because they are not re-entrant.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,2,'2025-07-30_22-09-19','osYield works by triggering a supervisor call (SVC), which is itself an interrupt. If we try to call osYield from inside a handler like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler, we end up asking for a new interrupt while one is already being serviced. That causes nested context switches, corrupting the stack frame and potentially breaking the scheduler. Since these handlers are in the middle of saving or restoring registers, interrupting them can overwrite critical state, leading to unpredictable behavior or a hard fault. In short, calling osYield inside those handlers risks crashing the system because they are not re-entrant.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,3,'2025-07-30_20-43-24','If we ran the SysTick every 0.1 ms, our OS would react much faster to timing events. Threads waiting on delays or real-time updates would resume sooner, giving the system better responsiveness. But the trade-off is a lot more overhead: the CPU would be handling ten times more interrupts per second, leaving less time for the threads themselves to run. That also increases power use and may reduce overall efficiency. So while shorter ticks give finer timing control, the cost is heavier interrupt load and reduced throughput for actual work.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,3,'2025-07-30_20-54-41','If we ran the SysTick every 0.1 ms, our OS would react much faster to timing events. Threads waiting on delays or real-time updates would resume sooner, giving the system better responsiveness. But the trade-off is a lot more overhead: the CPU would be handling ten times more interrupts per second, leaving less time for the threads themselves to run. That also increases power use and may reduce overall efficiency. So while shorter ticks give finer timing control, the cost is heavier interrupt load and reduced throughput for actual work.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,3,'2025-07-30_22-09-19','If we ran the SysTick every 0.1 ms, our OS would react much faster to timing events. Threads waiting on delays or real-time updates would resume sooner, giving the system better responsiveness. But the trade-off is a lot more overhead: the CPU would be handling ten times more interrupts per second, leaving less time for the threads themselves to run. That also increases power use and may reduce overall efficiency. So while shorter ticks give finer timing control, the cost is heavier interrupt load and reduced throughput for actual work.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,4,'2025-07-30_20-43-24','For the user, co-operative multitasking means their threads must explicitly call osYield to let others run. If a thread never yields, it can block the rest. Pre-emptive multitasking, on the other hand, uses the SysTick interrupt to automatically switch threads after a set time, so users don’t need to manage sharing the CPU. For the OS, co-operative scheduling is simpler since it only swaps contexts when told to. Pre-emptive scheduling is more complex because the OS must handle periodic interrupts, decrement runtime counters, and force a switch when a thread’s timeslice expires — but it guarantees fairness.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,4,'2025-07-30_20-54-41','For the user, co-operative multitasking means their threads must explicitly call osYield to let others run. If a thread never yields, it can block the rest. Pre-emptive multitasking, on the other hand, uses the SysTick interrupt to automatically switch threads after a set time, so users don’t need to manage sharing the CPU. For the OS, co-operative scheduling is simpler since it only swaps contexts when told to. Pre-emptive scheduling is more complex because the OS must handle periodic interrupts, decrement runtime counters, and force a switch when a thread’s timeslice expires — but it guarantees fairness.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,4,'2025-07-30_22-09-19','For the user, co-operative multitasking means their threads must explicitly call osYield to let others run. If a thread never yields, it can block the rest. Pre-emptive multitasking, on the other hand, uses the SysTick interrupt to automatically switch threads after a set time, so users don’t need to manage sharing the CPU. For the OS, co-operative scheduling is simpler since it only swaps contexts when told to. Pre-emptive scheduling is more complex because the OS must handle periodic interrupts, decrement runtime counters, and force a switch when a thread’s timeslice expires — but it guarantees fairness.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,5,'2025-07-30_20-43-24','They work together because both methods use the same context-switching mechanism. When a thread voluntarily calls osYield, the scheduler picks the next ready thread. When SysTick interrupts for a pre-emptive switch, the OS also uses the same save/restore routine to swap threads. By combining them, we allow threads to give up control when they want to, while still ensuring no thread can hog the CPU for too long. This mix makes the system both responsive and fair without either approach conflicting with the other.\n',0,'s25'),('mamara',5,5,'2025-07-30_20-54-41','Both approaches can work together because they rely on the same context-switching mechanism. A thread that calls osYield voluntarily gives up control, and the scheduler immediately chooses the next thread. Meanwhile, SysTick decrements the current thread’s runtime, and once it hits zero, the OS triggers a PendSV exception. PendSV is the handler that actually saves the current state and restores the next thread, performing the context switch. So yielding and pre-emption complement each other: one is voluntary, the other enforced, but both end up using PendSV to do the actual switch.\n',1,'s25'),('mamara',5,5,'2025-07-30_22-09-19','Both approaches can work together because they rely on the same context-switching mechanism. A thread that calls osYield voluntarily gives up control, and the scheduler immediately chooses the next thread. Meanwhile, SysTick decrements the current thread’s runtime, and once it hits zero, the OS triggers a PendSV exception. PendSV is the handler that actually saves the current state and restores the next thread, performing the context switch. So yielding and pre-emption complement each other: one is voluntary, the other enforced, but both end up using PendSV to do the actual switch.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,1,'2025-06-04_11-13-53','HAL stands for “hardware abstraction layer”. It simplifies the programming of the MCU by allowing the user to access and modify the various features of the processer (i.e. peripherals, registers)  without requiring knowledge on the hardware specific details that control them.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,1,'2025-06-05_20-04-46','HAL stands for “hardware abstraction layer”. It simplifies the programming of the MCU by allowing the user to access and modify the various features of the processer (i.e. peripherals, registers)  without requiring knowledge on the hardware specific details that control them.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,1,'2025-06-05_23-18-12','HAL stands for “hardware abstraction layer”. It simplifies the programming of the MCU by allowing the user to access and modify the various features of the processer (i.e. peripherals, registers)  without requiring knowledge on the hardware specific details that control them.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,1,'2025-06-05_23-31-45','HAL stands for “hardware abstraction layer”. It simplifies the programming of the MCU by allowing the user to access and modify the various features of the processer (i.e. peripherals, registers)  without requiring knowledge on the hardware specific details that control them.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,2,'2025-06-04_11-13-53','__io_putchar(int char); is a function that transmits a single byte representing a character through UART. Since UART is a serial protocol, data has to be sent through it byte by byte. __io_putchar thus has to be declared since it is called by printf() for each character in its input string.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',1,2,'2025-06-05_20-04-46','__io_putchar(int char); is a function that transmits a single byte representing a character through UART. It has to be defined since functions that belong to the <studio.h> library require helper functions if they are to interact with hardware.\nThe way a given peripheral (i.e. UART) is implemented differs between each embedded system in terms of pin assignment, the drivers and HAL libraries that are present, etc. Therefore, the helper function must be explicitly declare since it cannot work universally for all embedded systems.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',1,2,'2025-06-05_23-18-12','__io_putchar(int char) is a function that transmits a single byte representing character through an IO channel (which in our case is UART). It is called repeatedly by printf when it is in the process of displaying a string. It has to be defined since functions that belong to the <studio.h> library require helper functions if they are to interact with hardware.\nThe way a given peripheral (i.e. UART) is implemented differs between each embedded system in terms of pin assignment, the drivers and HAL libraries that are present, etc. Therefore, the helper function must be explicitly declare since it cannot work universally for all embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,2,'2025-06-05_23-31-45','__io_putchar(int char) is a function that transmits a single byte representing character through an IO channel (which in our case is UART). It is called repeatedly by printf when it is in the process of displaying a string. It has to be defined since functions that belong to the <studio.h> library require helper functions if they are to interact with hardware.\nThe way a given peripheral (i.e. UART) is implemented differs between each embedded system in terms of pin assignment, the drivers and HAL libraries that are present, etc. Therefore, the helper function must be explicitly declare since it cannot work universally for all embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,3,'2025-06-04_11-13-53','The debugger allows us to see the precise value for any register within the MCU. This can be helpful to see the value during a specific time period (before processes or your code affect the value). Additionally, if printf was not implemented properly, or it was called during the wrong place in the code, it could lead to erroneous values.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',1,3,'2025-06-05_20-04-46','The debugger allows us to see the precise value for any register within the MCU. This can be helpful to see the value during a specific period of execution (i.e. before background processes or your code mutate the value that’s being analyzed).\nOn the other hand, printf()is a function that requests the use of a hardware peripheral. This would add a stack frame and lead to the registers within the M4 cortex’s registers will be modified to execute the function. Other processes stemming from the function call like interrupts from UART or memory allocation could further mutate the value that is being observed.\nTherefore, it may be difficult to debug low level registers, since they will be modified while the printf is being executed.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,3,'2025-06-05_23-18-12','The debugger allows us to see the precise value for any register within the MCU. This can be helpful to see the value during a specific period of execution (i.e. before background processes or your code mutate the value that’s being analyzed).\nOn the other hand, printf()is a function that requests the use of a hardware peripheral. This would add a stack frame and lead to the registers within the M4 cortex’s registers will be modified to execute the function. Other processes stemming from the function call like interrupts from UART or memory allocation could further mutate the value that is being observed.\nTherefore, it may be difficult to debug low level registers, since they will be modified while the printf is being executed.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,3,'2025-06-05_23-31-45','The debugger allows us to see the precise value for any register within the MCU. This can be helpful to see the value during a specific period of execution (i.e. before background processes or your code mutate the value that’s being analyzed).\nOn the other hand, printf()is a function that requests the use of a hardware peripheral. This would add a stack frame and lead to the registers within the M4 cortex’s registers will be modified to execute the function. Other processes stemming from the function call like interrupts from UART or memory allocation could further mutate the value that is being observed.\nTherefore, it may be difficult to debug low level registers, since they will be modified while the printf is being executed.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,4,'2025-06-04_11-13-53','The address of the PSP value is the MSP address subtracted an offset of 0x400. We must know the initial location of the MSP, since it represents the beginning of the all of the stacks in memory, and its value does not change during the lifetime of the program. As such, the address of all the thread stacks will always be relative to the initial MSP value.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,4,'2025-06-05_20-04-46','The address of the PSP value is the MSP address subtracted an offset of 0x400. We must know the initial location of the MSP, since it represents the beginning of the all of the stacks in memory, and its value does not change during the lifetime of the program. As such, the address of all the thread stacks will always be relative to the initial MSP value.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,4,'2025-06-05_23-18-12','The address of the PSP value is the MSP address subtracted an offset of 0x400. We must know the initial location of the MSP, since it represents the beginning of the all of the stacks in memory, and its value does not change during the lifetime of the program. As such, the address of all the thread stacks will always be relative to the initial MSP value.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,4,'2025-06-05_23-31-45','The address of the PSP value is the MSP address subtracted an offset of 0x400. We must know the initial location of the MSP, since it represents the beginning of the all of the stacks in memory, and its value does not change during the lifetime of the program. As such, the address of all the thread stacks will always be relative to the initial MSP value.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,5,'2025-06-04_11-13-53','The address for the beginning of the Nth thread’s stack would equal to the initial address for the MSP minus the size allocated the main stack, which is then subtracted with thread stack size multiplied by N. This allows us to create multiple “stacks”, where the first element of each thread’s stack are spaced an equal distance apart following the space allocated to the main stack.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',1,5,'2025-06-05_20-04-46','Say that the number of threads we know we want to create is equal to N.\nBefore any memory is allocated to these stacks, we should ensure that the requested memory does not exceed the MAX_STACK_SIZE determined by the programmer or the system.\nWe should then declare compile-time constants that determine the size of each type of stack (i.e. MAIN_STACK_SIZE, PROCESS_STACK_SIZE).\nIt would also be good to keep track of the sizes of all of the stacks in a list of size N (e.g. thread_stack_sizes[]). This is so that we don’t index to the bottom of a non-empty stack when switching between threads.\nAssuming we have found the initial MSP via dereferencing the double pointer at 0x00 (uint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0;) we can find calculate the pointer for the top of the i-th thread’s stack with the equation:\nith_thread_ptr = MSP_INIT_VAL – MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (i*PROCESS_STACK_SIZE) – thread_size[i]\nHere we are indexing the threads starting from zero. Also note that we are subtracting the value from the main stack’s address, so that it can more effectively share the memory space with the heap, which grows upwards.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',1,5,'2025-06-05_23-18-12','Say that the number of threads we know we want to create is equal to N.\nBefore any memory is allocated to these stacks, we should ensure that the requested memory does not exceed the MAX_STACK_SIZE determined by the programmer or the system.\nWe should then declare compile-time constants that determine the size of each type of stack (i.e. MAIN_STACK_SIZE, PROCESS_STACK_SIZE).\nIt would also be good to keep track of the sizes of all of the stacks in a list of size N (e.g. thread_stack_sizes[]). This is so that we don’t index to the bottom of a non-empty stack when switching between threads.\nAssuming we have found the initial MSP via dereferencing the double pointer at 0x00 (uint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0;) we can find calculate the pointer for the top of the i-th thread’s stack with the equation:\nith_thread_ptr = MSP_INIT_VAL – MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (i*PROCESS_STACK_SIZE) – thread_size[i]\nHere we are indexing the threads starting from zero. Also note that we are subtracting the value from the main stack’s address, so that it can more effectively share the memory space with the heap, which grows upwards.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',1,5,'2025-06-05_23-31-45','Say that the number of threads we know we want to create is equal to N.\nBefore any memory is allocated to these stacks, we should ensure that the requested memory does not exceed the MAX_STACK_SIZE determined by the programmer or the system.\nWe should then declare compile-time constants that determine the size of each type of stack (i.e. MAIN_STACK_SIZE, PROCESS_STACK_SIZE).\nIt would also be good to keep track of the sizes of all of the stacks in a list of size N (e.g. thread_stack_sizes[]). This is so that we don’t index to the bottom of a non-empty stack when switching between threads.\nAssuming we have found the initial MSP via dereferencing the double pointer at 0x00 (uint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0;) we can find calculate the pointer for the top of the i-th thread’s stack with the equation:\nith_thread_ptr = MSP_INIT_VAL – MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (i*PROCESS_STACK_SIZE) – thread_size[i]\nHere we are indexing the threads starting from zero. Also note that we are subtracting the value from the main stack’s address, so that it can more effectively share the memory space with the heap, which grows upwards.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,1,'2025-06-07_17-34-02','Changing the program counter as was done in the lab essentially tells the OS directly where it will be reading instructions. As a result, the PC jumps directly to where the machine code for the print_continuously function in memory.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',2,1,'2025-06-12_11-59-00','Changing the program counter as was done in the lab essentially tells the OS directly where it will be reading instructions. As a result, the PC jumps directly to where the machine code for the print_continuously function in memory.\nThis leads to the print_continously function being immediately executed without the , since the PC registers dictates what instruction in the code the CPU is executing.\nThink of it like reaching a superscript footnote when reading a book. Even though there are words after the footnote marker, many readers will instead jump to the text of the footnote immediately.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,1,'2025-06-12_12-51-40','Changing the program counter as was done in the lab essentially tells the OS directly where it will be reading instructions. As a result, the PC jumps directly to where the machine code for the print_continuously function in memory.\nThis leads to the print_continously function being immediately executed without the , since the PC registers dictates what instruction in the code the CPU is executing.\nThink of it like reaching a superscript footnote when reading a book. Even though there are words after the footnote marker, many readers will instead jump to the text of the footnote immediately.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,1,'2025-06-12_12-57-43','Changing the program counter as was done in the lab essentially tells the OS directly where it will be reading instructions. As a result, the PC jumps directly to where the machine code for the print_continuously function in memory.\nThis leads to the print_continously function being immediately executed without the , since the PC registers dictates what instruction in the code the CPU is executing.\nThink of it like reaching a superscript footnote when reading a book. Even though there are words after the footnote marker, many readers will instead jump to the text of the footnote immediately.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',2,1,'2025-06-13_10-51-18','Changing the program counter as was done in the lab essentially tells the OS directly where it will be reading instructions. As a result, the PC jumps directly to where the machine code for the print_continuously function in memory.\nThis leads to the print_continously function being immediately executed without the , since the PC registers dictates what instruction in the code the CPU is executing.\nThink of it like reaching a superscript footnote when reading a book. Even though there are words after the footnote marker, many readers will instead jump to the text of the footnote immediately.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,2,'2025-06-07_17-34-02','A system call is a request (typically an interrupt) by a program that asks the OS to switch the CPU from “user mode” to “privilege mode” so that it can executed functions that can only be accessed by the Kernel, like managing hardware devices, writing/reading to files, or handling processes.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',2,2,'2025-06-12_11-59-00','A system call is a function call that interfaces with the hardware that he OS can control.\nMore specifically, It is a request (typically an interrupt) by a program that asks the OS to switch the CPU from “user mode” to “privilege mode” so that it can executed functions that can only be accessed by the Kernel, like managing hardware devices, writing/reading to files, or handling processes. This adds an extra layer of security because it prevents the user or malicious entities from accessing hardware directly.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,2,'2025-06-12_12-51-40','A system call is a function call that interfaces with the hardware that he OS can control.\nMore specifically, It is a request (typically an interrupt) by a program that asks the OS to switch the CPU from “user mode” to “privilege mode” so that it can executed functions that can only be accessed by the Kernel, like managing hardware devices, writing/reading to files, or handling processes. This adds an extra layer of security because it prevents the user or malicious entities from accessing hardware directly.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,2,'2025-06-12_12-57-43','A system call is a function call that interfaces with the hardware that he OS can control.\nMore specifically, It is a request (typically an interrupt) by a program that asks the OS to switch the CPU from “user mode” to “privilege mode” so that it can executed functions that can only be accessed by the Kernel, like managing hardware devices, writing/reading to files, or handling processes. This adds an extra layer of security because it prevents the user or malicious entities from accessing hardware directly.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,2,'2025-06-13_10-51-18','A system call is a function call that interfaces with the hardware that he OS can control.\nMore specifically, It is a request (typically an interrupt) by a program that asks the OS to switch the CPU from “user mode” to “privilege mode” so that it can executed functions that can only be accessed by the Kernel, like managing hardware devices, writing/reading to files, or handling processes. This adds an extra layer of security because it prevents the user or malicious entities from accessing hardware directly.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,3,'2025-06-07_17-34-02','Setting up a thread’s stack involves first getting a pointer to the thread stack we want to switch to (lets call it thread_ptr). We then push the thread’s context into the stack (which in our case are the register values for the CortexM4) before we actually switch our PSP value to that stack pointer.\nWe push the context to the stack by dereferencing thread_ptr and setting it to an appropriate value. Since the stack grows downwards, we decrement thread_ptr to move the address of the top of the stack.\nIt is crucial to push the new register values into the stack in the correct order (xPSR, then PC, then LR, etc),  so that the SVC_Handler can pop them off the stack and store them in the correct register and in the right order.\nOnce everything is in order in the stack, we then set the PSP value to thread_ptr, and call the external system call runFirstThread(), which handles the actual process of switching threads. Part of this involves popping the top element of the stack and feeding it into one of the registers after returning from an interrupt.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,3,'2025-06-12_11-59-00','Setting up a thread’s stack involves first getting a pointer to the thread stack we want to switch to (lets call it thread_ptr). We then push the thread’s context into the stack (which in our case are the register values for the CortexM4) before we actually switch our PSP value to that stack pointer.\nWe push the context to the stack by dereferencing thread_ptr and setting it to an appropriate value. Since the stack grows downwards, we decrement thread_ptr to move the address of the top of the stack.\nIt is crucial to push the new register values into the stack in the correct order (xPSR, then PC, then LR, etc),  so that the SVC_Handler can pop them off the stack and store them in the correct register and in the right order.\nOnce everything is in order in the stack, we then set the PSP value to thread_ptr, and call the external system call runFirstThread(), which handles the actual process of switching threads. Part of this involves popping the top element of the stack and feeding it into one of the registers after returning from an interrupt.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,3,'2025-06-12_12-51-40','Setting up a thread’s stack involves first getting a pointer to the thread stack we want to switch to (lets call it thread_ptr). We then push the thread’s context into the stack (which in our case are the register values for the CortexM4) before we actually switch our PSP value to that stack pointer.\nWe push the context to the stack by dereferencing thread_ptr and setting it to an appropriate value. Since the stack grows downwards, we decrement thread_ptr to move the address of the top of the stack.\nIt is crucial to push the new register values into the stack in the correct order (xPSR, then PC, then LR, etc),  so that the SVC_Handler can pop them off the stack and store them in the correct register and in the right order.\nOnce everything is in order in the stack, we then set the PSP value to thread_ptr, and call the external system call runFirstThread(), which handles the actual process of switching threads. Part of this involves popping the top element of the stack and feeding it into one of the registers after returning from an interrupt.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,3,'2025-06-12_12-57-43','Setting up a thread’s stack involves first getting a pointer to the thread stack we want to switch to (lets call it thread_ptr). We then push the thread’s context into the stack (which in our case are the register values for the CortexM4) before we actually switch our PSP value to that stack pointer.\nWe push the context to the stack by dereferencing thread_ptr and setting it to an appropriate value. Since the stack grows downwards, we decrement thread_ptr to move the address of the top of the stack.\nIt is crucial to push the new register values into the stack in the correct order (xPSR, then PC, then LR, etc),  so that the SVC_Handler can pop them off the stack and store them in the correct register and in the right order.\nOnce everything is in order in the stack, we then set the PSP value to thread_ptr, and call the external system call runFirstThread(), which handles the actual process of switching threads. Part of this involves popping the top element of the stack and feeding it into one of the registers after returning from an interrupt.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,3,'2025-06-13_10-51-18','Setting up a thread’s stack involves first getting a pointer to the thread stack we want to switch to (lets call it thread_ptr). We then push the thread’s context into the stack (which in our case are the register values for the CortexM4) before we actually switch our PSP value to that stack pointer.\nWe push the context to the stack by dereferencing thread_ptr and setting it to an appropriate value. Since the stack grows downwards, we decrement thread_ptr to move the address of the top of the stack.\nIt is crucial to push the new register values into the stack in the correct order (xPSR, then PC, then LR, etc),  so that the SVC_Handler can pop them off the stack and store them in the correct register and in the right order.\nOnce everything is in order in the stack, we then set the PSP value to thread_ptr, and call the external system call runFirstThread(), which handles the actual process of switching threads. Part of this involves popping the top element of the stack and feeding it into one of the registers after returning from an interrupt.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,4,'2025-06-07_17-34-02','Upon calling the RUN_FIRST_STACK_TRHEAD case we wrote within the SVC_Handler_Main, it will pop the values from the current stack pointer and store them into the cortexM4 registers. However, without setting the stack pointer to be at the desired PSP value, that means that the current program stack will still be in the MSP.  As such, whatever is on the main stack will be transferred into the registers while it is executing code. This can lead to undefined behaviour, especially since the main stack handles important OS processes like interrupts. Since SVC is an interrupt, the registers that the MSP uses must be clean so that they can be handled by the main stack.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',2,4,'2025-06-12_11-59-00','Upon calling the RUN_FIRST_STACK_TRHEAD case we wrote within the SVC_Handler_Main, it will pop the values from the current stack pointer and store them into the cortexM4 registers.\nIn our code we primed the thread switching by pushing in the desired register values into the PSP pointer.\nHowever, without actually setting the stack pointer to be at our PSP value, that means that all the effort we took to setting the register values was all for nought, and the program stack will still be in the MSP.  As such, whatever is on the main stack will be transferred into the registers while it is executing code. This can lead to undefined behaviour, especially since the main stack handles important OS processes like interrupts. Since SVC is an interrupt, the registers that the MSP uses must be clean so that they can be handled by the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,4,'2025-06-12_12-51-40','Upon calling the RUN_FIRST_STACK_TRHEAD case we wrote within the SVC_Handler_Main, it will pop the values from the current stack pointer and store them into the cortexM4 registers.\nIn our code we primed the thread switching by pushing in the desired register values into the PSP pointer.\nHowever, without actually setting the stack pointer to be at our PSP value, that means that all the effort we took to setting the register values was all for nought, and the program stack will still be in the MSP.  As such, whatever is on the main stack will be transferred into the registers while it is executing code. This can lead to undefined behaviour, especially since the main stack handles important OS processes like interrupts. Since SVC is an interrupt, the registers that the MSP uses must be clean so that they can be handled by the main stack.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',2,4,'2025-06-12_12-57-43','Upon calling the RUN_FIRST_STACK_TRHEAD case we wrote within the SVC_Handler_Main, it will pop the values from the current stack pointer and store them into the cortexM4 registers.\nIn our code we primed the thread switching by pushing in the desired register values into the PSP pointer.\nHowever, without actually setting the stack pointer to be at our PSP value, that means that all the effort we took to setting the register values was all for nought, and the program stack will still be in the MSP.  As such, whatever is on the main stack will be transferred into the registers while it is executing code. This can lead to undefined behaviour, especially since the main stack handles important OS processes like interrupts. Since SVC is an interrupt, the registers that the MSP uses must be clean so that they can be handled by the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,4,'2025-06-13_10-51-18','Upon calling the RUN_FIRST_STACK_TRHEAD case we wrote within the SVC_Handler_Main, it will pop the values from the current stack pointer and store them into the cortexM4 registers.\nIn our code we primed the thread switching by pushing in the desired register values into the PSP pointer.\nHowever, without actually setting the stack pointer to be at our PSP value, that means that all the effort we took to setting the register values was all for nought, and the program stack will still be in the MSP.  As such, whatever is on the main stack will be transferred into the registers while it is executing code. This can lead to undefined behaviour, especially since the main stack handles important OS processes like interrupts. Since SVC is an interrupt, the registers that the MSP uses must be clean so that they can be handled by the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,5,'2025-06-07_17-34-02','Upon a return, a function on the stack would be popped, and it would go back to executing the function that called it. However, because the thread does not have any previous functions in the stack, it will hardfault.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',2,5,'2025-06-12_11-59-00','When a function is called, a value called the return address is pushed into the stack. This value tell the program where the program counter should return once the function has finished execution (i.e. it returns to the method that invoked the function call). However, because the thread does not have any previous functions in the stack (as we set the program counter ), when it comes time for the CPU to pop the stack for the return address a hard fault will occur due to the stack being empty.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',2,5,'2025-06-12_12-51-40','When a function is called, a value called the return address is pushed into the stack. This value tell the program where the program counter should return once the function has finished execution (i.e. it returns to the method that invoked the function call). However, because the return address is not stored in the stack (since there was no written code that called the function since PC was set directly) when it comes time for the CPU to pop the stack for the return address, a hard fault will occur due to the stack being empty.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,5,'2025-06-12_12-57-43','When a function is called, a value called the return address is pushed into the stack. This value tell the program where the program counter should return once the function has finished execution (i.e. it returns to the method that invoked the function call). However, because the return address is not stored in the stack (since there was no written code that called the function since PC was set directly) when it comes time for the CPU to pop the stack for the return address, a hard fault will occur due to the stack being empty.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',2,5,'2025-06-13_10-51-18','When a function is called, a value called the return address is pushed into the stack. This value tell the program where the program counter should return once the function has finished execution (i.e. it returns to the method that invoked the function call). However, because the return address is not stored in the stack (since there was no written code that called the function since PC was set directly) when it comes time for the CPU to pop the stack for the return address, a hard fault will occur due to the stack being empty.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,1,'2025-06-23_19-43-12','The compilation process happens in four steps. First, the preprocessor passes through the code and modifies the code wherever there’s a preprocessor directive (i.e. macro definition, conditional compilation). Second, each file containing high level code are compiled into machine code and transformed into individual object files. Lastly, it is the job of the linker to take all of the independent object files containing code and libraries, and package them into a single executable file that can be run by the system.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',3,1,'2025-06-25_10-38-54','The compilation process happens in three steps. First, the preprocessor passes through all of the .c files and modifies the code wherever there’s a preprocessor directive (i.e. macro definition, conditional compilation). Second, each file containing high level code are converted into machine code that the CPU can understand and transformed into individual object files by the compiler.\nTypically, it is convenient to divide code between multiple files for organization, to control function and variable visibility, and to facilitate code reuse. As such, during the last step it is the job of the linker to consolidate all the independent object files containing code and libraries, by packaging them into a single executable file that can be run by the system. It does this by adding code between each file which essentially\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,1,'2025-06-25_11-40-55','The compilation process happens in three steps. First, the preprocessor passes through all of the .c files and modifies the code wherever there’s a pr