g include on a .c file can duplicate function definition errors.\nSince #include on a c file will copy everything in the function (rather than just the definition as would be the case for including a header file), if the c file in question is included in multiple places, then it will end up being defined in multiple files, which is not allowed by the c compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,2,'2025-06-25_10-38-54','Doing include on a .c file can duplicate function definition errors.\nSince #include on a c file will copy everything in the function (rather than just the definition as would be the case for including a header file), if the c file in question is included in multiple places, then it will end up being defined in multiple files, which is not allowed by the c compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,2,'2025-06-25_11-40-55','Doing include on a .c file can duplicate function definition errors.\nSince #include on a c file will copy everything in the function (rather than just the definition as would be the case for including a header file), if the c file in question is included in multiple places, then it will end up being defined in multiple files, which is not allowed by the c compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,2,'2025-06-25_12-26-00','Doing include on a .c file can duplicate function definition errors.\nSince #include on a c file will copy everything in the function (rather than just the definition as would be the case for including a header file), if the c file in question is included in multiple places, then it will end up being defined in multiple files, which is not allowed by the c compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,2,'2025-06-25_21-42-04','Doing include on a .c file can duplicate function definition errors.\nSince #include on a c file will copy everything in the function (rather than just the definition as would be the case for including a header file), if the c file in question is included in multiple places, then it will end up being defined in multiple files, which is not allowed by the c compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,2,'2025-06-25_21-53-22','Doing include on a .c file can duplicate function definition errors.\nSince #include on a c file will copy everything in the function (rather than just the definition as would be the case for including a header file), if the c file in question is included in multiple places, then it will end up being defined in multiple files, which is not allowed by the c compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,2,'2025-06-25_22-23-52','Doing include on a .c file can duplicate function definition errors.\nSince #include on a c file will copy everything in the function (rather than just the definition as would be the case for including a header file), if the c file in question is included in multiple places, then it will end up being defined in multiple files, which is not allowed by the c compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,2,'2025-06-25_23-01-19','Doing include on a .c file can duplicate function definition errors.\nSince #include on a c file will copy everything in the function (rather than just the definition as would be the case for including a header file), if the c file in question is included in multiple places, then it will end up being defined in multiple files, which is not allowed by the c compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,2,'2025-06-25_23-56-38','Doing include on a .c file can duplicate function definition errors.\nSince #include on a c file will copy everything in the function (rather than just the definition as would be the case for including a header file), if the c file in question is included in multiple places, then it will end up being defined in multiple files, which is not allowed by the c compiler.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,3,'2025-06-23_19-43-12','From the user’s perspective, switching or creating a new thread should be seamless and abstracted.  As such, creating a new thread should look something like this:\nUser creates function for thread to run\nUser requests to create a new thread and the thread function, perhaps also explicitly defining its\nKernel notifies the user whether the creation of the new thread was successful\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',3,3,'2025-06-25_10-38-54','From the user’s perspective, switching or creating a new thread should be seamless and abstracted.  As such, creating a new thread should look something like this:\nUser creates function/context for the new thread\nUser uses a system call to request for the kernel to allocate a new thread and switch the current thread of execution\nKernel notifies the user whether the creation of the new thread was successful\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',3,3,'2025-06-25_11-40-55','From the user’s perspective, switching or creating a new thread should be seamless and abstracted.  As such, creating a new thread should look something like this:\nUser creates function/context for the new thread\nUser uses a system call to request for the kernel to allocate a new thread and switch the current thread of execution\nKernel notifies the user whether the creation of the new thread was successful\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',3,3,'2025-06-25_12-26-00','From the user’s perspective, switching or creating a new thread should be seamless and abstracted.  As such, creating a new thread should look something like this:\nUser creates function/context for the new thread\nUser uses a system call to request for the kernel to allocate a new thread and switch the current thread of execution\nKernel notifies the user whether the creation of the new thread was successful\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',3,3,'2025-06-25_21-42-04','From the user’s perspective, switching or creating a new thread should be seamless and abstracted.  As such, creating a new thread should look something like this:\nUser creates function/context for the new thread\nUser initialize kernel to allow it to create new threads (i.e. kernel finds initial MSP value)\nUser uses a system call to request for the kernel to allocate a new thread and switch the current thread of execution\nKernel notifies the user whether the creation of the new thread was successful\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',3,3,'2025-06-25_21-53-22','From the user’s perspective, switching or creating a new thread should be seamless and abstracted.  As such, creating a new thread should look something like this:\nUser creates function for new thread\nUser initialize kernel to allow it to create new threads (i.e. kernel finds initial MSP value)\nUser passes function address and thread context to kernel\nUser uses a system call to request for the kernel to allocate a new thread and switch the current thread of execution\nKernel notifies the user whether the creation of the new thread was successful\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',3,3,'2025-06-25_22-23-52','From the user’s perspective, switching or creating a new thread should be seamless and abstracted.  As such, creating a new thread should look something like this:\nUser creates function for new thread\nUser starts the kernel by calling the function to initialize it (i.e. kernel initializer finds initial MSP value)\nUser passes function address and thread context to kernel\nUser uses a system call to request for the kernel to allocate a new thread and switch the current thread of execution\nKernel notifies the user whether the creation of the new thread was successful\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,3,'2025-06-25_23-01-19','From the user’s perspective, switching or creating a new thread should be seamless and abstracted.  As such, creating a new thread should look something like this:\nUser creates function for new thread\nUser starts the kernel by calling the function to initialize it (i.e. kernel initializer finds initial MSP value)\nUser passes function address and thread context to kernel\nUser uses a system call to request for the kernel to allocate a new thread and switch the current thread of execution\nKernel notifies the user whether the creation of the new thread was successful\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,3,'2025-06-25_23-56-38','From the user’s perspective, switching or creating a new thread should be seamless and abstracted.  As such, creating a new thread should look something like this:\nUser creates function for new thread\nUser starts the kernel by calling the function to initialize it (i.e. kernel initializer finds initial MSP value)\nUser passes function address and thread context to kernel\nUser uses a system call to request for the kernel to allocate a new thread and switch the current thread of execution\nKernel notifies the user whether the creation of the new thread was successful\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',3,4,'2025-06-23_19-43-12','The stack pool is the segment of memory where all of the threads stacks are stored. The allotted stack memory can be divided between multiple threads, so that they each have their own individual stack that they can use.\nThis is useful because it allows stacks to essentially have a memory workspace where they don’t have to worry about it being overwritten or modified by other processes or when they are not currently executing any instructions.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',3,4,'2025-06-25_10-38-54','The stack pool is the segment of memory where all of the threads stacks are stored. The allotted stack memory can be divided between multiple threads, so that they each have their own individual stack that they can use.\nThis is useful because it allows stacks to essentially have a memory workspace where they don’t have to worry about it being overwritten or modified by other processes or when they are not currently executing any instructions. Furthermore, if each thread stack is the same size, then a given thread’s stack pointer offset can be easily computer by multiplying the thread number by the thread stack size, and adding the stack size for that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,4,'2025-06-25_11-40-55','The stack pool is the segment of memory where all of the threads stacks are stored. The allotted stack memory can be divided between multiple threads, so that they each have their own individual stack that they can use.\nThis is useful because it allows stacks to essentially have a memory workspace where they don’t have to worry about it being overwritten or modified by other processes or when they are not currently executing any instructions. Furthermore, if each thread stack is the same size, then a given thread’s stack pointer offset can be easily computer by multiplying the thread number by the thread stack size, and adding the stack size for that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,4,'2025-06-25_12-26-00','The stack pool is the segment of memory where all of the threads stacks are stored. The allotted stack memory can be divided between multiple threads, so that they each have their own individual stack that they can use.\nThis is useful because it allows stacks to essentially have a memory workspace where they don’t have to worry about it being overwritten or modified by other processes or when they are not currently executing any instructions. Furthermore, if each thread stack is the same size, then a given thread’s stack pointer offset can be easily computer by multiplying the thread number by the thread stack size, and adding the stack size for that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,4,'2025-06-25_21-42-04','The stack pool is the segment of memory where all of the threads stacks are stored. The allotted stack memory can be divided between multiple threads, so that they each have their own individual stack that they can use.\nThis is useful because it allows stacks to essentially have a memory workspace where they don’t have to worry about it being overwritten or modified by other processes or when they are not currently executing any instructions. Furthermore, if each thread stack is the same size, then a given thread’s stack pointer offset can be easily computer by multiplying the thread number by the thread stack size, and adding the stack size for that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,4,'2025-06-25_21-53-22',