er by multiplying the thread number by the thread stack size, and adding the stack size for that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,4,'2025-06-25_23-56-38','The stack pool is the segment of memory where all of the threads stacks are stored. The allotted stack memory can be divided between multiple threads, so that they each have their own individual stack that they can use.\nThis is useful because it allows stacks to essentially have a memory workspace where they don’t have to worry about it being overwritten or modified by other processes or when they are not currently executing any instructions. Furthermore, if each thread stack is the same size, then a given thread’s stack pointer offset can be easily computer by multiplying the thread number by the thread stack size, and adding the stack size for that thread.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,5,'2025-06-23_19-43-12','The user should only have access and be able to access things that are relevant to the function of their program. As a result, the only functions that are in the “user’s” file (i.e. main.c) include those that the threads will be running, and the system calls that create or switch between threads.\nAs for initializing or managing the memory of thread, or creating a new pointer for a stack, it is inconvenient for the user to do all of that themselves, so from their perspective that’s the job of the kernel. This also prevents the user from messing up their threads since they cannot directly access their pointers.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',3,5,'2025-06-25_10-38-54','The user should only have access and be able to access things that are relevant to the function of their program. As a result, the only functions that are in the “user’s” file (i.e. main.c) include those that the threads will be running, and the system calls that create or switch between threads.\nAs for initializing or managing the memory of thread, or creating a new pointer for a stack, it is inconvenient for the user to do all of that themselves, so from their perspective that’s the job of the kernel. By offloading all the resource management to the kernel, the user can put more time into working on the logic of their program.\nAny files pertaining to the computer’s hardware and peripherals (i.e. drivers) should also only be handled by the kernel. Since the hardware is closely tied to the stable and consistent operation of the computer, functions related to accessing hardware should only be accessed by the kernel to prevent the user from messing anything up.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,5,'2025-06-25_11-40-55','The user should only have access and be able to access things that are relevant to the function of their program. As a result, the only functions that are in the “user’s” file (i.e. main.c) include those that the threads will be running, and the system calls that create or switch between threads.\nAs for initializing or managing the memory of thread, or creating a new pointer for a stack, it is inconvenient for the user to do all of that themselves, so from their perspective that’s the job of the kernel. By offloading all the resource management to the kernel, the user can put more time into working on the logic of their program.\nAny files pertaining to the computer’s hardware and peripherals (i.e. drivers) should also only be handled by the kernel. Since the hardware is closely tied to the stable and consistent operation of the computer, functions related to accessing hardware should only be accessed by the kernel to prevent the user from messing anything up.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,5,'2025-06-25_12-26-00','The user should only have access and be able to access things that are relevant to the function of their program. As a result, the only functions that are in the “user’s” file (i.e. main.c) include those that the threads will be running, and the system calls that create or switch between threads.\nAs for initializing or managing the memory of thread, or creating a new pointer for a stack, it is inconveni