or initializing or managing the memory of thread, or creating a new pointer for a stack, it is inconvenient for the user to do all of that themselves, so from their perspective that’s the job of the kernel. By offloading all the resource management to the kernel, the user can put more time into working on the logic of their program.\nAny files pertaining to the computer’s hardware and peripherals (i.e. drivers) should also only be handled by the kernel. Since the hardware is closely tied to the stable and consistent operation of the computer, functions related to accessing hardware should only be accessed by the kernel to prevent the user from messing anything up.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',3,5,'2025-06-25_23-56-38','The user should only have access and be able to access things that are relevant to the function of their program. As a result, the only functions that are in the “user’s” file (i.e. main.c) include those that the threads will be running, and the system calls that create or switch between threads.\nAs for initializing or managing the memory of thread, or creating a new pointer for a stack, it is inconvenient for the user to do all of that themselves, so from their perspective that’s the job of the kernel. By offloading all the resource management to the kernel, the user can put more time into working on the logic of their program.\nAny files pertaining to the computer’s hardware and peripherals (i.e. drivers) should also only be handled by the kernel. Since the hardware is closely tied to the stable and consistent operation of the computer, functions related to accessing hardware should only be accessed by the kernel to prevent the user from messing anything up.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,1,'2025-07-26_18-22-48','When using a queue to implement round robin, every time a thread is instantiated, it is enqueued. When the OS wants to schedule a thread, it will dequeue the oldest thread (since a queue is FIFO) and execute it for its allotted time. Once it has finished executing the thread, it will then enqueue it once more (assuming it still has not be released). The process then repeats when the OS schedules the next thread that is first in the queue.\nUsing a queue for round robin scheduling has some advantages.\nThis automatic nature of queue means we don’t have to keep track of the order thread that the threads need to be executed since the first thread in the queue will always be the one who has gone the longest from being serviced.\nIf the queue is implemented as a linked list, it also gives us a way to easy delete threads and dynamically increase the size of the queue\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,1,'2025-07-26_23-51-39','When using a queue to implement round robin, every time a thread is instantiated, it is enqueued. When the OS wants to schedule a thread, it will dequeue the oldest thread (since a queue is FIFO) and execute it for its allotted time. Once it has finished executing the thread, it will then enqueue it once more (assuming it still has not be released). The process then repeats when the OS schedules the next thread that is first in the queue.\nUsing a queue for round robin scheduling has some advantages.\nThis automatic nature of queue means we don’t have to keep track of the order thread that the threads need to be executed since the first thread in the queue will always be the one who has gone the longest from being serviced.\nIf the queue is implemented as a linked list, it also gives us a way to easy delete threads and dynamically increase the size of the queue\nHowever, using a queue for scheduling can add some overhead due the execution time required for the enqueue and dequeue operations, as well as the additional memory needed for node pointers for each thread (if linked list queue is being used).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,1,'2025-07-27_00-07-58','When using a queue to implement round robin, every time a thread is instantiated, it is enqueued. When the OS wants to schedule a thread, it will dequeue the oldest thread (since a queue is FIFO) and execute it for its allotted time. Once it has finished executing the thread, it will then enqueue it once more (assuming it still has not be released). The process then repeats when the OS schedules the next thread that is first in the queue.\nUsing a queue for round robin scheduling has some advantages.\nThis automatic nature of queue means we don’t have to keep track of the order thread that the threads need to be executed since the first thread in the queue will always be the one who has gone the longest from being serviced.\nIf the queue is implemented as a linked list, it also gives us a way to easy delete threads and dynamically increase the size of the queue\nHowever, using a queue for scheduling can add some overhead due the execution time required for the enqueue and dequeue operations, as well as the additional memory needed for node pointers for each thread (if linked list queue is being used).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,1,'2025-07-27_23-55-58','When using a queue to implement round robin, every time a thread is instantiated, it is enqueued. When the OS wants to schedule a thread, it will dequeue the oldest thread (since a queue is FIFO) and execute it for its allotted time. Once it has finished executing the thread, it will then enqueue it once more (assuming it still has not be released). The process then repeats when the OS schedules the next thread that is first in the queue.\nUsing a queue for round robin scheduling has some advantages.\nThis automatic nature of queue means we don’t have to keep track of the order thread that the threads need to be executed since the first thread in the queue will always be the one who has gone the longest from being serviced.\nIf the queue is implemented as a linked list, it also gives us a way to easy delete threads and dynamically increase the size of the queue\nHowever, using a queue for scheduling can add some overhead due the execution time required for the enqueue and dequeue operations, as well as the additional memory needed for node pointers for each thread (if linked list queue is being used).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,1,'2025-07-28_10-57-46','When using a queue to implement round robin, every time a thread is instantiated, it is enqueued. When the OS wants to schedule a thread, it will dequeue the oldest thread (since a queue is FIFO) and execute it for its allotted time. Once it has finished executing the thread, it will then enqueue it once more (assuming it still has not be released). The process then repeats when the OS schedules the next thread that is first in the queue.\nUsing a queue for round robin scheduling has some advantages.\nThis automatic nature of queue means we don’t have to keep track of the order thread that the threads need to be executed since the first thread in the queue will always be the one who has gone the longest from being serviced.\nIf the queue is implemented as a linked list, it also gives us a way to easy delete threads and dynamically increase the size of the queue\nHowever, using a queue for scheduling can add some overhead due the execution time required for the enqueue and dequeue operations, as well as the additional memory needed for node pointers for each thread (if linked list queue is being used).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,1,'2025-07-28_11-18-03','When using a queue to implement round robin, every time a thread is instantiated, it is enqueued. When the OS wants to schedule a thread, it will dequeue the oldest thread (since a queue is FIFO) and execute it for its allotted time. Once it has finished executing the thread, it will then enqueue it once more (assuming it still has not be released). The process then repeats when the OS schedules the next thread that is first in the queue.\nUsing a queue for round robin scheduling has some advantages.\nThis automatic nature of queue means we don’t have to keep track of the order thread that the threads need to be executed since the first thread in the queue will always be the one who has gone the longest from being serviced.\nIf the queue is implemented as a linked list, it also gives us a way to easy delete threads and dynamically increase the size of the queue\nHowever, using a queue for scheduling can add some overhead due the execution time required for the enqueue and dequeue operations, as well as the additional memory needed for node pointers for each thread (if linked list queue is being used).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,1,'2025-07-28_11-38-33','When using a queue to implement round robin, every time a thread is instantiated, it is enqueued. When the OS wants to schedule a thread, it will dequeue the oldest thread (since a queue is FIFO) and execute it for its allotted time. Once it has finished executing the thread, it will then enqueue it once more (assuming it still has not be released). The process then repeats when the OS schedules the next thread that is first in the queue.\nUsing a queue for round robin scheduling has some advantages.\nThis automatic nature of queue means we don’t have to keep track of the order thread that the threads need to be executed since the first thread in the queue will always be the one who has gone the longest from being serviced.\nIf the queue is implemented as a linked list, it also gives us a way to easy delete threads and dynamically increase the size of the queue\nHowever, using a queue for scheduling can add some overhead due the execution time required for the enqueue and dequeue operations, as well as the additional memory needed for node pointers for each thread (if linked list queue is being used).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,1,'2025-07-28_17-19-05','When using a queue to implement round robin, every time a thread is instantiated, it is enqueued. When the OS wants to schedule a thread, it will dequeue the oldest thread (since a queue is FIFO) and execute it for its allotted time. Once it has finished executing the thread, it will then enqueue it once more (assuming it still has not be released). The process then repeats when the OS schedules the next thread that is first in the queue.\nUsing a queue for round robin scheduling has some advantages.\nThis automatic nature of queue means we don’t have to keep track of the order thread that the threads need to be executed since the first thread in the queue will always be the one who has gone the longest from being serviced.\nIf the queue is implemented as a linked list, it also gives us a way to easy delete threads and dynamically increase the size of the queue\nHowever, using a queue for scheduling can add some overhead due the execution time required for the enqueue and dequeue operations, as well as the additional memory needed for node pointers for each thread (if linked list queue is being used).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,2,'2025-07-26_18-22-48','PendSV_Handler is the function that services the pendable interrupt PendSV. A pendable interrupt only executes when there is no other interrupt currently being serviced.\nWhen performing a context switch, we need to ensure that the context switch function returns, so that local variables on the stack do not stay in memory indefinitely. PendSV allows us to perform the context switch without using the stack, preventing the issue of local variables from a SVC call wasting stack memory. However, PendSV must wait until the SVC call has concluded so that the kernel is not interrupted when it handles the SVC.\nHence, PendSV_Handler has to be invoked within the SVC call so that it is serviced immediately after the SVC call is completed. Once the SVC call is done, then PendSV_Handler performs the actual context switch once the system call has concluded.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,2,'2025-07-26_23-51-39','PendSV_Handler is the function that services the pendable interrupt PendSV. A pendable interrupt only executes when there is no other interrupt currently being serviced.\nWhen performing a context switch, we need to ensure that the context switch function returns, so that local variables on the stack do not stay in memory indefinitely. PendSV allows us to perform the context without halting switch without using the stack, preventing the issue of local variables from a SVC call wasting stack memory. However, PendSV must wait until the SVC call has concluded so that the kernel is not interrupted when it handles the SVC.\nHence, PendSV_Handler services the PendSV interrupt whenever we want to switch context without halting another interrupt. Since PendSV is often the lowest priority it possibly can be, it will wait for all other interrupts to conclude before it is service. Only then will the actual context switch happen.\nDoing the context switch involves saving the registers of the current thread, pushing the next thread’s context onto the stack (i.e. registers), setting the PSP to the stack pointer of the next thread, and then finally invoking the system call to switch context.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,2,'2025-07-27_00-07-58','The process of the context switch begins first begins with the scheduler being invoked by the OS. Once it choses a thread to go to, it finds its stack pointer, and then inpoves the PendSV interrupt.\nPendSV_Handler is the function that services the pendable interrupt PendSV. A pendable interrupt only executes when there is no other interrupt currently being serviced.\nWhen performing a context switch, we need to ensure that the context switch function returns, so that local variables on the stack do not stay in memory indefinitely. PendSV allows us to perform the context without halting switch without using the stack, preventing the issue of local variables from a SVC call wasting stack memory. However, PendSV must wait until the SVC call has concluded so that the kernel is not interrupted when it handles the SVC.\nHence, PendSV_Handler services the PendSV interrupt whenever we want to switch context without halting another interrupt. Since PendSV is often the lowest priority it possibly can be, it will wait for all other interrupts to conclude before it is service. Only then will the actual context switch happen.\nDoing the context switch involves saving the registers of the current thread, pushing the next thread’s context onto the stack (i.e. R4-R11, PC) and in the correct order, setting the PSP to the stack pointer of the next thread, and then finally invoking the system call to switch context.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,2,'2025-07-27_23-55-58','The process of the context switch begins first begins when the OS invokes the PendSV trap. It is an interrupt designed invoke a context switch as soon as all other currently running interrupts are finished being serviced.\nOnce the PendSV_Handler is able to service the PendSV interrupt, then the context switch can begin. First, the current running thread saves their state by pushing their context (i.e. working registers R4-R11, program counter, xPSR state) to the stack.\nNext, the new thread is selected through the osSched function. The scheduler will return the thread’s identifier and the current location of its stack pointer. The specific thread it returns is dependent on the scheduling algorithm the OS is using.\nAt the location of memory of the new stack pointer, the new thread’s previous state and registers are stored within it. These values are popped in the correct order and placed in their respective registers. Finally, the PSP is then changed to the current location of the stack pointer. The new thread is now restored in the same state as it was when it yielded.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,2,'2025-07-28_10-57-46','The process of the context switch begins first begins when the OS invokes the PendSV trap. It is an interrupt designed invoke a context switch as soon as all other currently running interrupts are finished being serviced.\nOnce the PendSV_Handler is able to service the PendSV interrupt, then the context switch can begin. First, the current running thread saves their state by pushing their context (i.e. working registers R4-R11, program counter, xPSR state) to the stack.\nNext, the new thread is selected through the osSched function. The scheduler will return the thread’s identifier and the current location of its stack pointer. The specific thread it returns is dependent on the scheduling algorithm the OS is using.\nAt the location of memory of the new stack pointer, the new thread’s previous state and registers are stored within it. These values are popped in the correct order and placed in their respective registers. Finally, the PSP is then changed to the current location of the stack pointer. The new thread is now restored in the same state as it was when it yielded.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,2,'2025-07-28_11-18-03','The process of the context switch begins first begins when the OS invokes the PendSV trap. It is an interrupt designed invoke a context switch as soon as all other currently running interrupts are finished being serviced.\nOnce the PendSV_Handler is able to service the PendSV interrupt, then the context switch can begin. First, the current running thread saves their state by pushing their context (i.e. working registers R4-R11, program counter, xPSR state) to the stack.\nNext, the new thread is selected through the osSched function. The scheduler will return the thread’s identifier and the current location of its stack pointer that has been stored in memory. The specific thread it returns is dependent on the scheduling algorithm the OS is using.\nAt the location of memory of the new stack pointer, the new thread’s previous context and registers will be stored within it if it previously had osYield invoked upon it. These values are popped in the correct order and placed in the respective R4-R11 registers. If instead the new thread has yet to run, then its context has to be set manually to the appropriate values for the task at hand. Finally, the PSP is then changed to the current location of the stack pointer. The new thread is now restored in the same state as it was when it yielded.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,2,'2025-07-28_11-38-33','The process of the context switch begins first begins when the OS invokes the PendSV trap. It is an interrupt designed invoke a context switch as soon as all other currently running interrupts are finished being serviced.\nOnce the PendSV_Handler is able to service the PendSV interrupt, then the context switch can begin. First, the current thread’s context needs to be saved by pushing their context (i.e. working registers R4-R11, program counter, xPSR state) to the stack. The OS can locate the current thread’s stack pointer can be found within the PSP register.\nNext, the new thread is selected through the osSched function. The scheduler will return the thread’s identifier and the current location of its stack pointer that has been stored in memory. The specific thread it returns is dependent on the scheduling algorithm the OS is using.\nThe new thread’s context is restored by popping its registers R4-R11 off of its stack, where they were previously set. These values are then placed within the M4 Cortex’s registers. Finally, the PSP is then changed to the current location of the stack pointer. The new thread is now restored in the same state as it was when it yielded.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,2,'2025-07-28_17-19-05','The process of the context switch begins first begins when the OS invokes the PendSV trap. It is an interrupt designed invoke a context switch as soon as all other currently running interrupts are finished being serviced.\nOnce the PendSV_Handler is able to service the PendSV interrupt, then the context switch can begin. First, the current thread’s state needs to be saved by storing its context in memory. The OS can locate the current thread’s stack pointer can be found within the PSP register. Some of these registers are automatically saved by the cortex M4 processor, like the PC, R0-R3, and xPSR. Registers R4-R11 however, must be explicitly saved by having the OS push them to the thread’s stack.\nNext, the new thread is selected through the osSched function. The scheduler will return the thread’s identifier and the current location of its stack pointer that has been stored in memory. The specific thread it returns is dependent on the scheduling algorithm the OS is using.\nThe new thread’s context is restored by popping its registers R4-R11 off of its stack, where they were previously set. These values are then placed within the M4 Cortex’s registers. Finally, the PSP is then changed to the current location of the stack pointer. From this point the CPU handles loading the hardware stacked context into their corresponding registers (R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR). The new thread is now restored in the same state as it was when it yielded.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,3,'2025-07-26_18-22-48','Though it depends on the application of the OS, scheduling threads purely on priority is typically a bad idea because threads that are of low priority may never get executed if threads of higher priority are perpetually created by the user. This is called starvation, and it is bad since important (but not necessarily urgent) tasks will never receive any CPU time.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,3,'2025-07-26_23-51-39','Though it depends on the application of the OS, scheduling threads purely on priority is typically a bad idea because threads that are of low priority may never get executed if threads of higher priority that will take precedence are perpetually created by the user. These higher threads will proceed to monopolize the CPU’s resources, especially because priority will be fixed. This will lead to starvation, as low priority threads won’t have an opportunity to have their priority increased due to not receiving any CPU slices for an extended period of time. This it is bad since important (but not necessarily urgent) tasks will never receive any CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,3,'2025-07-27_00-07-58','Though it depends on the application of the OS, scheduling threads purely on priority is typically a bad idea because threads that are of low priority may never get executed if threads of higher priority that will take precedence are perpetually created by the user. These higher threads will proceed to monopolize the CPU’s resources, especially because priority will be fixed. This will lead to starvation, as low priority threads won’t have an opportunity to have their priority increased due to not receiving any CPU slices for an extended period of time. This it is bad since important (but not necessarily urgent) tasks will never receive any CPU time.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,3,'2025-07-27_23-55-58','Though it depends on the application of the OS, scheduling threads purely on priority is typically a bad idea because threads that are of low priority may never get executed if threads of higher priority that will take precedence are perpetually created by the user. These higher threads will proceed to monopolize the CPU’s resources, especially because priority will be fixed. This will lead to starvation, as low priority threads won’t have an opportunity to have their priority increased due to not receiving any CPU slices for an extended period of time. This it is bad since important (but not necessarily urgent) tasks will never receive any CPU time.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,3,'2025-07-28_10-57-46','Though it depends on the application of the OS, scheduling threads purely on priority is typically a bad idea because threads that are of low priority may never get executed if threads of higher priority that will take precedence are perpetually created by the user. These higher threads will proceed to monopolize the CPU’s resources, especially because priority will be fixed. This will lead to starvation, as low priority threads won’t have an opportunity to have their priority increased due to not receiving any CPU slices for an extended period of time. This it is bad since important (but not 