priority increased due to not receiving any CPU slices for an extended period of time. This it is bad since important (but not necessarily urgent) tasks will never receive any CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,4,'2025-07-26_18-22-48','Writing code in C (or any high level language for that matter) results in code that is more readable and easier to write. This is because the compiler abstracts much of the inner workings of the CPU. As such, the programmer does not require knowledge of the devices instruction set, ABI, registers, etc for the CPU that they are working with.\nHowever, writing in assembly may also have some performance benefits too, depending on if there are inefficiencies with the compiler. Additionally, there may be some specific instructions/system calls that the programming language or compiler does not support, resulting in some inline assembly being used (i.e. how we needed ASM to invoke system calls or switch threads).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,4,'2025-07-26_23-51-39','Writing code in C (or any high level language for that matter) results in code that is more readable and easier to write. This is because the compiler abstracts much of the inner workings of the CPU. As such, the programmer does not require knowledge of the devices instruction set, ABI, registers, etc for the CPU that they are working with.\nHowever, writing in assembly may also have some performance benefits too, depending on if there are inefficiencies with the compiler. Additionally, there may be some specific instructions/system calls that the programming language or compiler does not support, resulting in some inline assembly being used (i.e. how we needed ASM to invoke system calls or switch threads).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,4,'2025-07-27_00-07-58','Writing code in C (or any high level language for that matter) results in code that is more readable and easier to write. This is because the compiler abstracts much of the inner workings of the CPU. As such, the programmer does not require knowledge of the devices instruction set, ABI, registers, etc for the CPU that they are working with.\nHowever, writing in assembly may also have some performance benefits too, depending on if there are inefficiencies with the compiler. Additionally, there may be some specific instructions/system calls that the programming language or compiler does not support, resulting in some inline assembly being used (i.e. how we needed ASM to invoke system calls or switch threads).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,4,'2025-07-27_23-55-58','Writing code in C (or any high level language for that matter) results in code that is more readable and easier to write. This is because the compiler abstracts much of the inner workings of the CPU. As such, the programmer does not require knowledge of the devices instruction set, ABI, registers, etc for the CPU that they are working with.\nHowever, writing in assembly may also have some performance benefits too, depending on if there are inefficiencies with the compiler. Additionally, there may be some specific instructions/system calls that the programming language or compiler does not support, resulting in some inline assembly being used (i.e. how we needed ASM to invoke system calls or switch threads).\n',1,'s25');
INSERT INTO `question_responses` VALUES ('memilne',4,4,'2025-07-28_10-57-46','Writing code in C (or any high level language for that matter) results in code that is more readable and easier to write. This is because the compiler abstracts much of the inner workings of the CPU. As such, the programmer does not require knowledge of the devices instruction set, ABI, registers, etc for the CPU that they are working with.\nHowever, writing in assembly may also have some performance benefits too, depending on if there are inefficiencies with the compiler. Additionally, there may be some specific instructions/system calls that the programming language or compiler does not support, resulting in some inline assembly being used (i.e. how we needed ASM to invoke system calls or switch threads).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,4,'2025-07-28_11-18-03','Writing code in C (or any high level language for that matter) results in code that is more readable and easier to write. This is because the compiler abstracts much of the inner workings of the CPU. As such, the programmer does not require knowledge of the devices instruction set, ABI, registers, etc for the CPU that they are working with.\nHowever, writing in assembly may also have some performance benefits too, depending on if there are inefficiencies with the compiler. Additionally, there may be some specific instructions/system calls that the programming language or compiler does not support, resulting in some inline assembly being used (i.e. how we needed ASM to invoke system calls or switch threads).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,4,'2025-07-28_11-38-33','Writing code in C (or any high level language for that matter) results in code that is more readable and easier to write. This is because the compiler abstracts much of the inner workings of the CPU. As such, the programmer does not require knowledge of the devices instruction set, ABI, registers, etc for the CPU that they are working with.\nHowever, writing in assembly may also have some performance benefits too, depending on if there are inefficiencies with the compiler. Additionally, there may be some specific instructions/system calls that the programming language or compiler does not support, resulting in some inline assembly being used (i.e. how we needed ASM to invoke system calls or switch threads).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,4,'2025-07-28_17-19-05','Writing code in C (or any high level language for that matter) results in code that is more readable and easier to write. This is because the compiler abstracts much of the inner workings of the CPU. As such, the programmer does not require knowledge of the devices instruction set, ABI, registers, etc for the CPU that they are working with.\nHowever, writing in assembly may also have some performance benefits too, depending on if there are inefficiencies with the compiler. Additionally, there may be some specific instructions/system calls that the programming language or compiler does not support, resulting in some inline assembly being used (i.e. how we needed ASM to invoke system calls or switch threads).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,5,'2025-07-26_18-22-48','Yes. If how we track the number of threads running has been implemented correctly, then the round robin will just repeatedly schedule the singular thread. Assuming that we begin indexing our threads at the 0th element of the thread array, next thread = (current thread + 1) % num_threads running will give (1 % 1) == 0, and thus the 0th thread will be scheduled.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,5,'2025-07-26_23-51-39','Yes. If how we track the number of threads running has been implemented correctly, then the round robin will just repeatedly schedule the singular thread since the singular thread still yields to the OS. Assuming that we begin indexing our threads at the 0th element of the thread array, next thread = (current thread + 1) % num_threads running will give (1 % 1) == 0, and thus the 0th thread will be scheduled.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,5,'2025-07-27_00-07-58','Yes. If how we track the number of threads running has been implemented correctly, then the round robin will just repeatedly schedule the singular thread since the singular thread still yields to the OS. Assuming that we begin indexing our threads at the 0th element of the thread array, next thread = (current thread + 1) % num_threads running will give (1 % 1) == 0, and thus the 0th thread will be scheduled.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',4,5,'2025-07-27_23-55-58','Yes. If how we track the number of threads running has been implemented correctly, then the round robin will just repeatedly schedule the singular thread since the singular thread still yields to the OS. Assuming that we begin indexing our threads at the 0th element of the thread array, next thread = (current thread + 1) % num_threads running will give (1 % 1) == 0, and thus the 0th thread will be scheduled.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,5,'2025-07-28_10-57-46','Yes. If how we track the number of threads running has been implemented correctly, then the round robin will just repeatedly schedule the singular thread since the singular thread still yields to the OS. Assuming that we begin indexing our threads at the 0th element of the thread array, next thread = (current thread + 1) % num_threads running will give (1 % 1) == 0, and thus the 0th thread will be scheduled.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,5,'2025-07-28_11-18-03','Yes. If how we track the number of threads running has been implemented correctly, then the round robin will just repeatedly schedule the singular thread since the singular thread still yields to the OS. Assuming that we begin indexing our threads at the 0th element of the thread array, next thread = (current thread + 1) % num_threads running will give (1 % 1) == 0, and thus the 0th thread will be scheduled.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,5,'2025-07-28_11-38-33','Yes. If how we track the number of threads running has been implemented correctly, then the round robin will just repeatedly schedule the singular thread since the singular thread still yields to the OS. Assuming that we begin indexing our threads at the 0th element of the thread array, next thread = (current thread + 1) % num_threads running will give (1 % 1) == 0, and thus the 0th thread will be scheduled.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',4,5,'2025-07-28_17-19-05','Yes. If how we track the number of threads running has been implemented correctly, then the round robin will just repeatedly schedule the singular thread since the singular thread still yields to the OS. Assuming that we begin indexing our threads at the 0th element of the thread array, next thread = (current thread + 1) % num_threads running will give (1 % 1) == 0, and thus the 0th thread will be scheduled.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,1,'2025-07-26_18-58-09','User first passes a pointer into the createThread function. Because the args parameter of the createThread function is a void pointer type, a pointer to any data type will be accepted.\nWhen the OS is setting up the thread’s context, it stores the pointer of (void* args) into R0, since Application binary interface dictates that value of a functions first parameter into R0. For simplicity, we are only considering functions that have a single parameter.\nNow, when the thread function is running, it is up to the discretion of the function what to do with the void pointer that was passed into it. Because it could be anything, it must cast it into whatever type it\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,1,'2025-07-26_23-57-55','User first passes a pointer into the createThread function. Because the args parameter of the createThread function is a void pointer type, a pointer to any data type will be accepted.\nWhen the OS is setting up the thread’s context, it stores the pointer of (void* args) into R0, since Application binary interface dictates that value of a functions first parameter into R0. For simplicity, we are only considering functions that have a single parameter.\nNow, when the thread function is running, it is up to the discretion of the function what to do with the void pointer that was passed into it. Because it could be anything, it must cast it into whatever type it\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,1,'2025-07-28_00-01-48','User first passes a pointer into the createThread function. Because the args parameter of the createThread function is a void pointer type, a pointer to any data type will be accepted.\nWhen the OS is setting up the thread’s context, it stores the pointer of (void* args) into R0, since Application binary interface dictates that value of a functions first parameter into R0. For simplicity, we are only considering functions that have a single parameter.\nNow, when the thread function is running, it is up to the discretion of the function what to do with the void pointer that was passed into it. Because it could be anything, it must cast it into whatever type it\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,1,'2025-07-28_12-00-20','User first passes a pointer into the createThread function. Because the args parameter of the createThread function is a void pointer type, a pointer to any data type will be accepted.\nWhen the OS is setting up the thread’s context, it stores the pointer of (void* args) into R0, since Application binary interface dictates that value of a functions first parameter into R0. For simplicity, we are only considering functions that have a single parameter.\nNow, when the thread function is running, it is up to the discretion of the function what to do with the void pointer that was passed into it. Because it could be anything, it must cast it into whatever type it\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,1,'2025-07-28_18-25-51','The user first creates a pointer of a specified type (i.e. int, struct) that they want to pass into the thread function.\nThe user then passes this pointer into the createThread(void* args) function. Because the args parameter of the createThread function is a void pointer type, a pointer to any data type will be accepted.\nWhen the OS is setting up the new thread’s context, it pushes all of that thread’s registers into its stack, where they will be popped once the scheduler switches to the new thread. The OS stores the pointer of (void* args) into the part of the stack that corresponds to the R0, since Application binary interface dictates that value of a functions first parameter into R0. For simplicity, we are only considering functions that have a single parameter.\nNow, when the thread function is running, it is up to the discretion of the function what to do with the void pointer that was passed into it. Because it could be anything, it must cast it into whatever type it\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,1,'2025-07-28_18-53-11','The user first creates a pointer of a specified type (i.e. int, struct) that they want to pass into the thread function.\nThe user then passes this pointer into the createThread(void* args) function. Because the args parameter of the createThread function is a void pointer type, a pointer to any data type will be accepted.\nWhen the OS is setting up the new thread’s context, it pushes all of that thread’s registers into its stack, where they will be popped once the scheduler switches to the new thread. The OS stores the pointer of (void* args) into the part of the stack that corresponds to the R0, since Application binary interface dictates that value of a functions first parameter into R0. For simplicity, we are only considering functions that have a single parameter.\nNow, when the thread function is running, it is up to the discretion of the function what to do with the void pointer that was passed into it. Because it could be anything, it must cast it into whatever type it\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,2,'2025-07-26_18-58-09','OS yield is invoked by a thread whenever it wants to hand control to the scheduler to switch context to a new thread. If yielding happens within these context-switching functions, then the context-switch will never conclude since OSyield essentially starts the process from the beginning.\nDepending on where the OSyield is placed in the context-switching functions, it can result in an infinite loop that will never exit, or a hardfault if it is called while the CPU is executing sensitive code or through a stack overflow.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,2,'2025-07-26_23-57-55','OS yield is invoked by a thread whenever it wants to hand control to the scheduler to switch context to a new thread. If yielding happens within these context-switching functions, then the context-switch will never conclude since OSyield essentially starts the process from the beginning.\nDepending on where the OSyield is placed in the context-switching functions, it can result in an infinite loop that will never exit, or a hardfault if it is called while the CPU is executing sensitive code or through a stack overflow.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,2,'2025-07-28_00-01-48','OS yield is invoked by a thread whenever it wants to hand control to the scheduler to switch context to a new thread. If yielding happens within these context-switching functions, then the context-switch will never conclude since OSyield essentially starts the process from the beginning.\nDepending on where the OSyield is placed in the context-switching functions, it can result in an infinite loop that will never exit, or a hardfault if it is called while the CPU is executing sensitive code or through a stack overflow.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,2,'2025-07-28_12-00-20','OS yield is invoked by a thread whenever it wants to hand control to the scheduler to switch context to a new thread. If yielding happens within these context-switching functions, then the context-switch will never conclude since OSyield essentially starts the process from the beginning.\nDepending on where the OSyield is placed in the context-switching functions, it can result in an infinite loop that will never exit, or a hardfault if it is called while the CPU is executing sensitive code or through a stack overflow.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,2,'2025-07-28_18-25-51','The osYield system call is invoked by a thread whenever it wants to hand control to the scheduler to switch context to a new thread. If yielding happens within these context-switching functions, then the context-switch will never conclude since OSyield essentially starts the process from the beginning.\nBecause osYield invokes the PendSV interrupt, it will not get serviced until the current system call handling the context switch completes its interrupt service routine. This can lead to inefficiencies, since the thread that is being switched to will immediately yield once the context switch is complete, leading to redundant thread-swapping.\nDepending on where the OSyield is placed in the context-switching functions, it could also result in an infinite loop that will never exit due to the osYield service call being repeatedly invoked. A hardfault may even occur if the OS doesn’t switch from the MSP to the PSP once the pended interrupt is serviced.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,2,'2025-07-28_18-53-11','The osYield system call is invoked by a thread whenever it wants to hand control to the scheduler to switch context to a new thread. If yielding happens within these context-switching functions, then the context-switch will never conclude since OSyield essentially starts the process from the beginning.\nBecause osYield invokes the PendSV interrupt, it will not get serviced until the current system call handling the context switch completes its interrupt service routine. This can lead to inefficiencies if a higher priority interrupt is currently being serviced, since the thread that is being switched to will immediately yield once the context switch is complete, leading to redundant thread-swapping.\nIf no other interrupt is ongoing when osYield is invoked, it will be serviced immediately, which can lead to stack corruption, i.e. current thread’s context is pushed to stack multiple times, possibly leading to stack overflow.\nDepending on where the OSyield is placed in the context-switching functions, it could also result in an infinite loop that will never exit due to the osYield service call being repeatedly invoked. A hardfault may even occur if the OS doesn’t switch from the MSP to the PSP once the pended interrupt is serviced.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,3,'2025-07-26_18-58-09','Pro: Having a higher clock speed and resolution will result in an OS that is more responsive, and can complete tasks in  response time will lead to a more responsive operating system\nCon: Adjusting the OS clock speed requires configuring SysTick, which requires reading up on the documentation. Additionally, some of our code will have to be adjusted, (i.e. scale each value corresponding to time by the clock divisor).\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,3,'2025-07-26_23-57-55','Pro: Having a higher clock speed and resolution will result in an OS that is more responsive, and can complete tasks in  response time will lead to a more responsive operating system\nCon: Adjusting the OS clock speed requires configuring SysTick, which requires reading up on the documentation. Additionally, some of our code will have to be adjusted, (i.e. scale each value corresponding to time by the clock divisor).\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,3,'2025-07-28_00-01-48','Pro: Having a higher clock speed and resolution will result in an OS that is more responsive, and can complete tasks in  response time will lead to a more responsive operating system\nCon: Adjusting the OS clock speed requires configuring SysTick, which requires reading up on the documentation. Additionally, some of our code will have to be adjusted, (i.e. scale each value corresponding to time by the clock divisor).\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,3,'2025-07-28_12-00-20','Pro: Having a higher clock speed and resolution will result in an OS that is more responsive, and can complete tasks in response time will lead to a more responsive operating system\nFor instance, having a higher resolution clock allows the CPU to discern the order in which two interrupts were received if they were both sent with little time between them.\nIf a CPU can run much faster than it’s clock speed, then it can get bottlenecked by multiple interrupts if several of them get queued up within, say, 1ms. Though the CPU might be able to service them all within a fraction of a millisecond, it now has a backlog of interrupts it will have to respond to. This would be quite bad for time critical systems.\nCon: Adjusting the OS clock speed requires configuring SysTick, which requires reading up on the documentation. Additionally, some of our code will have to be adjusted, (i.e. scale each value corresponding to time by the clock divisor).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,3,'2025-07-28_18-25-51','Pro: Having a higher clock speed and resolution will result in an OS that is more responsive, and can complete tasks in response time will lead to a more responsive operating system\nFor instance, having a higher resolution clock allows the CPU to discern the order in which two interrupts were received if they were both sent with little time between them.\nIf a CPU can run much faster than it’s clock speed, then it can get bottlenecked by multiple interrupts if several of them get queued up within, say, 1ms. Though the CPU might be able to service them all within a fraction of a millisecond, it now has a backlog of interrupts it will have to respond to. This would be quite bad for time critical systems.\nCon: Adjusting the OS clock speed requires configuring SysTick, which requires reading up on the documentation. Additionally, some of our code will have to be adjusted, (i.e. scale each value corresponding to time by the clock divisor).\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,3,'2025-07-28_18-53-11','Pro: Having a higher clock speed and resolution will result in an OS that is more responsive, and can complete tasks in response time will lead to a more responsive operating system\nFor instance, having a higher resolution clock allows the CPU to discern the order in which two interrupts were received if they were both sent with little time between them.\nIf a CPU can run much faster than it’s clock speed, then it can get bottlenecked by multiple interrupts if several of them get queued up within, say, 1ms. Though the CPU might be able to service them all within a fraction of a millisecond, it now has a backlog of interrupts it will have to respond to. This would be quite bad for time critical systems.\nCon: Adjusting the OS clock speed requires configuring SysTick, which requires reading up on the documentation. Additionally, some of our code will have to be adjusted, (i.e. scale each value corresponding to time by the clock divisor).\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,4,'2025-07-26_18-58-09','Cooperative; threads communicate to each other as to who will be scheduled next. Tasks give up control voluntarily\nTo the user, it will appear though as if each process has to complete, or require user input before the next process will execute\nPremptive;  OS has the authority to interrupt threads and context switch\nTo user, it will look like time slicing, allowing for the appearance of multiple processes being computed simultaneously\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,4,'2025-07-26_23-57-55','Cooperative; threads communicate to each other as to who will be scheduled next. Tasks give up control voluntarily\nTo the user, it will appear though as if each process has to complete, or require user input before the next process will execute\nPremptive;  OS has the authority to interrupt threads and context switch\nTo user, it will look like time slicing, allowing for the appearance of multiple processes being computed simultaneously\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,4,'2025-07-28_00-01-48','Cooperative; threads communicate to each other as to who will be scheduled next. Tasks give up control voluntarily\nTo the user, it will appear though as if each process has to complete, or require user input before the next process will execute\nPremptive;  OS has the authority to interrupt threads and context switch\nTo user, it will look like time slicing, allowing for the appearance of multiple processes being computed simultaneously\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,4,'2025-07-28_12-00-20','Cooperative; threads communicate to each other as to who will be scheduled next. Tasks give up control voluntarily\nTo the user, it will appear though as if each process has to complete, or require user input before the next process will execute\nPremptive;  OS has the authority to interrupt threads and context switch. The OS forces the scheduler to context switch to a new thread.\nOccurs via an interrupt that triggers when a thread does not give up control voluntarily due to a certain condition being met (i.e. higher priority thread exists, time sensitivity, or because the current thread has used up it’s whole time slice.)\nTo user, it will look like time slicing, allowing for the appearance of multiple processes being computed simultaneously\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,4,'2025-07-28_18-25-51','Cooperative; threads communicate to each other as to who will be scheduled next. Tasks give up control voluntarily\nTo the user, it will appear though as if each process has to complete, or require user input before the next process will execute\nPremptive;  OS has the authority to interrupt threads and context switch. The OS forces the scheduler to context switch to a new thread.\nOccurs via an interrupt that triggers when a thread does not give up control voluntarily due to a certain condition being met (i.e. higher priority thread exists, time sensitivity, or because the current thread has used up it’s whole time slice.)\nTo user, it will look like time slicing, allowing for the appearance of multiple processes being computed simultaneously\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,4,'2025-07-28_18-53-11','Cooperative; threads communicate to each other as to who will be scheduled next. Tasks give up control voluntarily\nTo the user, it will appear though as if each process has to complete, or require user input before the next process will execute\nPremptive;  OS has the authority to interrupt threads and context switch. The OS forces the scheduler to context switch to a new thread.\nOccurs via an interrupt that triggers when a thread does not give up control voluntarily due to a certain condition being met (i.e. higher priority thread exists, time sensitivity, or because the current thread has used up it’s whole time slice.)\nTo user, it will look like time slicing, allowing for the appearance of multiple processes being computed simultaneously\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,5,'2025-07-26_18-58-09','The time based thread will never yield. Instead, it will invoke a context switch once its allotted execution time in finished. It will repeatedly loop until this time runs out. Because it will still invoke OS yield when the thread has received its allotted execution time, both the premptivly and cooperatively scheduled threads can run in tandem.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,5,'2025-07-26_23-57-55','The time based thread will never yield. Instead, it will invoke a context switch once its allotted execution time in finished. It will repeatedly loop until this time runs out. Because it will still invoke OS yield when the thread has received its allotted execution time, both the premptivly and cooperatively scheduled threads can run in tandem.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,5,'2025-07-28_00-01-48','The time based thread will never yield. Instead, it will invoke a context switch once its allotted execution time in finished. It will repeatedly loop until this time runs out. Because it will still invoke OS yield when the thread has received its allotted execution time, both the premptivly and cooperatively scheduled threads can run in tandem.\n',0,'s25'),('memilne',5,5,'2025-07-28_12-00-20','These two mechanisms can work together because they will both eventually invoke the osYield interrupt. A co-operatively multitasking thread will call osYield at the end of its thread function. On the other hand, the pre-emptively multitasking thread does not yield within its function. Rather, the OS keeps track of the number of ticks that have passed since a given thread has been running.\nEach thread also has a struct member for the max time it can be running for before it must yield. After each tick, it compares the thread’s max runtime to the number of ticks that have been elapsed. If the runtime has exceeded the max value stored within the thread struct, then the Sys_tick_handler will invoke the osYield interrupt to force a context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,5,'2025-07-28_18-25-51','These two mechanisms can work together because they will both eventually invoke the osYield interrupt. A co-operatively multitasking thread will call osYield at the end of its thread function. On the other hand, the pre-emptively multitasking thread does not yield within its function. Rather, the OS keeps track of the number of ticks that have passed since a given thread has been running.\nEach thread also has a struct member for the max time it can be running for before it must yield. After each tick, it compares the thread’s max runtime to the number of ticks that have been elapsed. If the runtime has exceeded the max value stored within the thread struct, then the Sys_tick_handler will invoke the osYield interrupt to force a context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('memilne',5,5,'2025-07-28_18-53-11','These two mechanisms can work together because they will both eventually invoke the osYield interrupt. A co-operatively multitasking thread will call osYield at the end of its thread function. On the other hand, the pre-emptively multitasking thread does not yield within its function. Rather, the OS keeps track of the number of ticks that have passed since a given thread has been running.\nEach thread also has a struct member for the max time it can be running for before it must yield. After each tick, it compares the thread’s max runtime to the number of ticks that have been elapsed. If the runtime has exceeded the max value stored within the thread struct, then the Sys_tick_handler will invoke the osYield interrupt to force a context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,1,'2025-06-24_14-55-13','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a collection of STMicroelectronics libraries that simplifies programming on STM32 microcontrollers. The HAL serves as an intermediary between the application code and the low-level hardware registers. As the name suggests, the HAL allows developers to focus on application logic and makes writing code to peripherals like GPIOs, timers or UART easier. Users can use HAL functions which are easier to understand and implement.\nIn general, embedded projects, using the HAL can accelerate development, reduce bugs, and improve portability across different STM32 devices. Although the added abstraction may introduce some overhead, the trade-off is typically worthwhile due to improved readability, maintainability, and faster prototyping.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,1,'2025-06-24_16-04-50','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a collection of STMicroelectronics libraries that simplifies programming on STM32 microcontrollers. The HAL serves as an intermediary between the application code and the low-level hardware registers. As the name suggests, the HAL allows developers to focus on application logic and makes writing code to peripherals like GPIOs, timers or UART easier. Users can use HAL functions which are easier to understand and implement.\nIn general, embedded projects, using the HAL can accelerate development, reduce bugs, and improve portability across different STM32 devices. Although the added abstraction may introduce some overhead, the trade-off is typically worthwhile due to improved readability, maintainability, and faster prototyping.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,1,'2025-06-27_13-07-47','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a collection of STMicroelectronics libraries that simplifies programming on STM32 microcontrollers. The HAL serves as an intermediary between the application code and the low-level hardware registers. As the name suggests, the HAL allows developers to focus on application logic and makes writing code to peripherals like GPIOs, timers or UART easier. Users can use HAL functions which are easier to understand and implement.\nIn general, embedded projects, using the HAL can accelerate development, reduce bugs, and improve portability across different STM32 devices. Although the added abstraction may introduce some overhead, the trade-off is typically worthwhile due to improved readability, maintainability, and faster prototyping.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,2,'2025-06-24_14-55-13','The __ioputchar is a low-level function defines exactly what should happen when a character is outputted. This is important in embedded systems because unlike desktop systems, the compiler does not always know how to output characters unless explicitly defined without functions like __ioputchar.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,2,'2025-06-24_16-04-50','In embedded systems, the __io_putchar function defines how individual characters are sent to an output device, such as a UART. It acts as a low-level hook that the C standard library uses when higher-level functions like printf try to output characters. Unlike desktop systems, embedded environments don’t have a default console, so we must explicitly define how and where characters should be sent.\nBy implementing __io_putchar, we enable printf to work by routing its output to a specific peripheral, usually a serial interface like UART, which can then send characters to a terminal or debug console. Without defining this function, calls to printf will either fail silently or have no effect, since the system doesn\'t know how to handle character output.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,2,'2025-06-27_13-07-47','In embedded systems, the __io_putchar function defines how individual characters are sent to an output device, such as a UART. It acts as a low-level hook that the C standard library uses when higher-level functions like printf try to output characters. Unlike desktop systems, embedded environments don’t have a default console, so we must explicitly define how and where characters should be sent.\nBy implementing __io_putchar, we enable printf to work by routing its output to a specific peripheral, usually a serial interface like UART, which can then send characters to a terminal or debug console. Without defining this function, calls to printf will either fail silently or have no effect, since the system doesn\'t know how to handle character output.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,3,'2025-06-24_14-55-13','In low-level embedded development, the printf function is not always reliable or even working at some stages of development. Because we are working on a low-level, printf may not show us the errors or root errors in our development and may fail to a combination of reasons like misconfigured clocks or uninitialized UART peripherals. The debugger, on the other hand, provides more insight into the internal state of the microcontroller. By going through the code line by line we can inspect registers at key values and monitor memory/stack in real time. This is especially important for verifying behaviour of key information like the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and Process Stack Pointer (PSP).\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,3,'2025-06-24_16-04-50','In low-level embedded development, the printf function is not always reliable or even working at some stages of development. Because we are working on a low-level, printf may not show us the errors or root errors in our development and may fail to a combination of reasons like misconfigured clocks or uninitialized UART peripherals. The debugger, on the other hand, provides more insight into the internal state of the microcontroller. By going through the code line by line we can inspect registers at key values and monitor memory/stack in real time. This is especially important for verifying behaviour of key information like the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and Process Stack Pointer (PSP).\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,3,'2025-06-27_13-07-47','In low-level embedded development, the printf function is not always reliable or even working at some stages of development. Because we are working on a low-level, printf may not show us the errors or root errors in our development and may fail to a combination of reasons like misconfigured clocks or uninitialized UART peripherals. The debugger, on the other hand, provides more insight into the internal state of the microcontroller. By going through the code line by line we can inspect registers at key values and monitor memory/stack in real time. This is especially important for verifying behaviour of key information like the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and Process Stack Pointer (PSP).\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,4,'2025-06-24_14-55-13','The stack allocation method uses the manual control of both the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), two separate stacks provided byt the ARM Cortex-M microcontrollers.\nWe start with an initial MSP value (MSP_init_val), which points to the top of the stack of the main stack. Then we allocate space for the PSP by subtracting a fixed size from MSP_initi_val. We will then have safely allocated a non-overlapping location dedicated for PSP which is important for context switching and multithreading.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,4,'2025-06-24_16-04-50','The stack allocation method involves manually assigning values to both the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which are separate hardware-supported stacks on ARM Cortex-M microcontrollers.\nWe begin with MSP_init_val, which is the initial value of the MSP and typically points to the top of a valid region of RAM. To allocate space for the PSP, we subtract a fixed size from MSP_init_val. This offset defines the amount of memory reserved for the PSP’s stack. It must be large enough to support expected function calls and interrupt handling for the thread using it. Additionally, the resulting PSP_val must be aligned to an 8-byte boundary, as required by the ARM Cortex-M architecture for stack operations. Misalignment can lead to faults or unpredictable behavior during execution.\nKnowing the initial value of the MSP ensures that the PSP is placed in a valid and unused region of RAM. If the PSP were not properly aligned or placed outside of valid memory, the program could crash, overwrite important data, or behave incorrectly during context switching. This is why both the size and position of the PSP must be calculated from a known valid stack location.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,4,'2025-06-27_13-07-47','The stack allocation method involves manually assigning values to both the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which are separate hardware-supported stacks on ARM Cortex-M microcontrollers.\nWe begin with MSP_init_val, which is the initial value of the MSP and typically points to the top of a valid region of RAM. To allocate space for the PSP, we subtract a fixed size from MSP_init_val. This offset defines the amount of memory reserved for the PSP’s stack. It must be large enough to support expected function calls and interrupt handling for the thread using it. Additionally, the resulting PSP_val must be aligned to an 8-byte boundary, as required by the ARM Cortex-M architecture for stack operations. Misalignment can lead to faults or unpredictable behavior during execution.\nKnowing the initial value of the MSP ensures that the PSP is placed in a valid and unused region of RAM. If the PSP were not properly aligned or placed outside of valid memory, the program could crash, overwrite important data, or behave incorrectly during context switching. This is why both the size and position of the PSP must be calculated from a known valid stack location.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,5,'2025-06-24_14-55-13','To allocate multiple stacks for multiple threads, we would need to start with a known valid stack address, usually MSP_initi_val, then reserve a fixed stack for each new thread. We would do so by starting with MSP_init_val then subtract increasing offsets for as many additional stacks we need. We must make sure that there is no overlap and that each stack is placed directly below the previous on. Additionally, we must ensure that all the stacks stay within the RAM memory bounds and each stack must also be aligned to 8 bytes for ARM Cortex-M.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,5,'2025-06-24_16-04-50','To allocate multiple stacks for multiple threads, we would need to start with a known valid stack address, usually MSP_initi_val, then reserve a fixed stack for each new thread. We would do so by starting with MSP_init_val then subtract increasing offsets for as many additional stacks we need. We must make sure that there is no overlap and that each stack is placed directly below the previous on. Additionally, we must ensure that all the stacks stay within the RAM memory bounds and each stack must also be aligned to 8 bytes for ARM Cortex-M.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',1,5,'2025-06-27_13-07-47','To allocate multiple stacks for multiple threads, we would need to start with a known valid stack address, usually MSP_initi_val, then reserve a fixed stack for each new thread. We would do so by starting with MSP_init_val then subtract increasing offsets for as many additional stacks we need. We must make sure that there is no overlap and that each stack is placed directly below the previous on. Additionally, we must ensure that all the stacks stay within the RAM memory bounds and each stack must also be aligned to 8 bytes for ARM Cortex-M.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,1,'2025-06-25_16-00-54','When I changed PC to hold the function pointer to your print continuously function, the processor began to execute it in an infinite loop. This is because the function’s address was stored in the R0 register and the PC was updated to point to this very address with the jumpAssembly function. As a result, the processor jumped to start reading and executing instructions from print_continuously which is effectively what occurs during a context switch in operating systems. Manipulating PC changes the order of instructions being read and executed and is important for process management.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,1,'2025-06-25_17-59-11','When I changed PC to hold the function pointer to your print continuously function, the processor began to execute it in an infinite loop. This is because the function’s address was stored in the R0 register and the PC was updated to point to this very address with the jumpAssembly function. As a result, the processor jumped to start reading and executing instructions from print_continuously which is effectively what occurs during a context switch in operating systems. Manipulating PC changes the order of instructions being read and executed and is important for process management.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,1,'2025-06-27_13-11-50','When I changed PC to hold the function pointer to your print continuously function, the processor began to execute it in an infinite loop. This is because the function’s address was stored in the R0 register and the PC was updated to point to this very address with the jumpAssembly function. As a result, the processor jumped to start reading and executing instructions from print_continuously which is effectively what occurs during a context switch in operating systems. Manipulating PC changes the order of instructions being read and executed and is important for process management.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,2,'2025-06-25_16-00-54','A system call is a service request made by a program to the operating system. The program is in user mode that has limited low-level access whereas the operating system runs in kernel mode which has unrestricted access to all system resources. Because user programs are unable to access hardware or execute certain operations like switching threads, a service call is used to “ask” the OS to do so.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,2,'2025-06-25_17-59-11','A system call is a service request made by a program to the operating system. The program is in user mode that has limited low-level access whereas the operating system runs in kernel mode which has unrestricted access to all system resources. Because user programs are unable to access hardware or execute certain operations like switching threads, a service call is used to “ask” the OS to do so.\nSystem calls are important because they provide a controlled and secure intermediary between untrusted user programs and the trusted core of the operating system. Without system calls, user code could directly manipulate hardware, which could lead to crashes or security vulnerabilities.\nIn this lab, we used the SVC instruction to trigger system calls, which let our thread interact with the OS to print messages or start execution. This is also how threads and processes request things like file access, memory allocation, or context switching.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,2,'2025-06-27_13-11-50','A system call is a service request made by a program to the operating system. The program is in user mode that has limited low-level access whereas the operating system runs in kernel mode which has unrestricted access to all system resources. Because user programs are unable to access hardware or execute certain operations like switching threads, a service call is used to “ask” the OS to do so.\nSystem calls are important because they provide a controlled and secure intermediary between untrusted user programs and the trusted core of the operating system. Without system calls, user code could directly manipulate hardware, which could lead to crashes or security vulnerabilities.\nIn this lab, we used the SVC instruction to trigger system calls, which let our thread interact with the OS to print messages or start execution. This is also how threads and processes request things like file access, memory allocation, or context switching.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,3,'2025-06-25_16-00-54','We manually created a “fake” stack frame that tricks the CPU into seeing it as if the thread had already been running. To do this, we allocate memory for the stack using stackptr, then push values onto it in a specific order. These values begin with xPSR, followed by the address of the thread function (which goes into PC), then LR, and finally the remaining registers in the order: R12, R3–R0, and R11–R4.\n“Setting up a thread’s stack” means preparing the stack so that, when it’s loaded during a context switch, all the necessary register values are restored correctly and execution starts at the right point. This is essential because in context switching, the CPU’s state, including its registers, must be saved and restored across threads. If we don’t do this, the thread would crash or behave unpredictably. In effect, this setup fools the CPU into thinking the thread was previously paused and is now resuming, even though it’s actually running for the first time.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,3,'2025-06-25_17-59-11','We manually created a “fake” stack frame that tricks the CPU into seeing it as if the thread had already been running. To do this, we allocate memory for the stack using stackptr, then push values onto it in a specific order. These values begin with xPSR, followed by the address of the thread function (which goes into PC), then LR, and finally the remaining registers in the order: R12, R3–R0, and R11–R4.\n“Setting up a thread’s stack” means preparing the stack so that, when it’s loaded during a context switch, all the necessary register values are restored correctly and execution starts at the right point. This is essential because in context switching, the CPU’s state, including its registers, must be saved and restored across threads. If we don’t do this, the thread would crash or behave unpredictably. In effect, this setup fools the CPU into thinking the thread was previously paused and is now resuming, even though it’s actually running for the first time.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,3,'2025-06-27_13-11-50','We manually created a “fake” stack frame that tricks the CPU into seeing it as if the thread had already been running. To do this, we allocate memory for the stack using stackptr, then push values onto it in a specific order. These values begin with xPSR, followed by the address of the thread function (which goes into PC), then LR, and finally the remaining registers in the order: R12, R3–R0, and R11–R4.\n“Setting up a thread’s stack” means preparing the stack so that, when it’s loaded during a context switch, all the necessary register values are restored correctly and execution starts at the right point. This is essential because in context switching, the CPU’s state, including its registers, must be saved and restored across threads. If we don’t do this, the thread would crash or behave unpredictably. In effect, this setup fools the CPU into thinking the thread was previously paused and is now resuming, even though it’s actually running for the first time.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,4,'2025-06-25_16-00-54','If we didn’t set the PSP before running the thread, the processor would still try to pop register values from the stack as usual — but it would start from whatever value PSP was already holding, which might be uninitialized or point to invalid memory. The CPU expects a properly ordered stack after returning from an interrupt, so if PSP isn\'t set correctly, it will read garbage data. This can cause incorrect register values to be loaded, leading to a hard fault. Setting PSP ensures the CPU starts with the correct stack and valid data, allowing the thread to resume (or start) safely.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,4,'2025-06-25_17-59-11','If we didn’t set the PSP before running the thread, the processor would still try to pop register values from wherever the PSP was pointing. By default, PSP is either uninitialized or pointing to an unrelated memory location, because the default stack used by the system is MSP, not PSP.\nWhen we create a thread, we manually allocate a separate stack in memory for it, which lives at a different memory address than the default stack. This separation is intentional and important; it keeps each thread’s context isolated. So for the CPU to use this custom thread stack, we must explicitly update the PSP to point to it.\nIf we skip this step, the CPU will pop invalid register values from the wrong place during the context switch (after the system call), leading to undefined behavior or a hard fault. Setting PSP correctly ensures the processor uses the correct memory region containing the thread’s prepared stack frame, allowing it to run safely.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,4,'2025-06-27_13-11-50','If we didn’t set the PSP before running the thread, the processor would still try to pop register values from wherever the PSP was pointing. By default, PSP is either uninitialized or pointing to an unrelated memory location, because the default stack used by the system is MSP, not PSP.\nWhen we create a thread, we manually allocate a separate stack in memory for it, which lives at a different memory address than the default stack. This separation is intentional and important; it keeps each thread’s context isolated. So for the CPU to use this custom thread stack, we must explicitly update the PSP to point to it.\nIf we skip this step, the CPU will pop invalid register values from the wrong place during the context switch (after the system call), leading to undefined behavior or a hard fault. Setting PSP correctly ensures the processor uses the correct memory region containing the thread’s prepared stack frame, allowing it to run safely.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,5,'2025-06-25_16-00-54','Without the infinite loop, the CPU would attempt to continue executing by popping the return address from LR and jumping to it. However, since we manually created the thread’s stack, LR was not set to a valid return address. As a result, the CPU would try to jump to an invalid memory location after the function returns, causing a hard fault or crash. This happens because there is no proper return context -the thread wasn’t called like a normal function, so the CPU is essentially left “hanging” after executing the function once.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,5,'2025-06-25_17-59-11','If the thread function printed something once and then returned, the processor would attempt to pop a return address from the stack to continue execution. However, unlike a regular function call, our thread was not called through standard C code, and its stack was manually constructed. That means there is no real return address saved on the stack by default. As a result, when the thread returns, the CPU will pop a meaningless value from the stack and try to jump to it, resulting in a hard fault or crash. The key issue is that the stack lacks a valid return address, which is normally pushed during a function call. Without it, the CPU doesn\'t know where to go next after finishing the thread, and ultimately fails.\nThis highlights an important difference: in real function calls, the return address is automatically placed on the stack, but in thread setup, we must manually set everything, and returning from a thread that wasn’t truly “called” leads to undefined behavior without proper setup.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',2,5,'2025-06-27_13-11-50','If the thread function printed something once and then returned, the processor would attempt to pop a return address from the stack to continue execution. However, unlike a regular function call, our thread was not called through standard C code, and its stack was manually constructed. That means there is no real return address saved on the stack by default. As a result, when the thread returns, the CPU will pop a meaningless value from the stack and try to jump to it, resulting in a hard fault or crash. The key issue is that the stack lacks a valid return address, which is normally pushed during a function call. Without it, the CPU doesn\'t know where to go next after finishing the thread, and ultimately fails.\nThis highlights an important difference: in real function calls, the return address is automatically placed on the stack, but in thread setup, we must manually set everything, and returning from a thread that wasn’t truly “called” leads to undefined behavior without proper setup.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',3,1,'2025-06-27_12-31-27','Each .c file is first compiled separately into an object file, which contains machine code for that file. After all files are compiled, the linker combines all these object files into a single executable by resolving references between them (like function calls or variable access across files) and arranging the code and data in memory.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',3,1,'2025-06-27_15-44-50','The process involves three main steps:\nPreprocessing:\nEach .c file is first passed through the preprocessor, which handles directives like #include, #define, and conditional compilation. This stage produces a translation unit by replacing includes with the contents of header files and expanding macros. The result is a .i file containing plain C code.\nCompiling:\nThe preprocessed code is then compiled into machine code for that file, stored in an object file (.o). This step translates the C code into low-level instructions, but references to external symbols (like functions defined in other files) are left unresolved.\nLinking:\nFinally, the linker combines all object files and libraries into a single executable. It resolves references between files (e.g., if main.c calls a function in kernel.c), lays out memory, and generates a final binary that can be loaded to run on the microcontroller.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',3,2,'2025-06-27_12-31-27','Including .c files causes the compiler to copy the entire source code into another file, leading to multiple definitions of the same functions and variables. This causes errors during linking. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and .h header files used to share declarations and interfaces between files.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',3,2,'2025-06-27_15-44-50','Including .c files causes the compiler to copy the entire source code into another file, leading to multiple definitions of the same functions and variables. This causes errors during linking. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and .h header files used to share declarations and interfaces between files.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',3,3,'2025-06-27_12-31-27','Write the function that the thread will execute.\nCall the kernel’s thread creation function, passing it your thread function.\nThe kernel allocates stack space for the thread and prepares the context.\nAfter creating the thread, start the kernel scheduler to run the thread.\nThe kernel switches the processor context to begin executing the thread’s function.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',3,3,'2025-06-27_15-44-50','Initialize the kernel by calling the kernel initialization function. This sets up internal kernel data, including the starting point for stack allocation and any thread tracking structures.\nWrite the function that defines the work the thread should perform. This is a regular C function written by the user.\nCreate the thread by calling the kernel’s thread creation function, passing the function you just wrote.\nThe kernel allocates a stack for the new thread, sets up the initial register values, and stores the thread information in internal structures.\nStart the kernel, which begins executing the first thread by switching the processor to use the new thread\'s context.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',3,4,'2025-06-27_12-31-27','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory dedicated to thread stacks. Each thread needs its own stack space to store local variables and function call information. The stack pool allows the OS to allocate separate, non-overlapping stacks to multiple threads, enabling the system to run multiple threads safely by keeping their execution contexts isolated.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',3,4,'2025-06-27_15-44-50','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory dedicated to thread stacks. Each thread needs its own stack space to store local variables and function call information. The stack pool allows the OS to allocate separate, non-overlapping stacks to multiple threads, enabling the system to run multiple threads safely by keeping their execution contexts isolated.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',3,5,'2025-06-27_12-31-27','The kernel contains core OS functionality like thread management, context switching, and hardware interaction. User code includes application-specific logic such as thread functions and the main program. This separation keeps the OS modular and secure, making it easier to maintain and develop user applications without risking the stability of the core system.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',3,5,'2025-06-27_15-44-50','The kernel contains core OS functionality like thread management, context switching, and hardware interaction. User code includes application-specific logic such as thread functions and the main program. This separation keeps the OS modular and secure, making it easier to maintain and develop user applications without risking the stability of the core system.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,1,'2025-07-18_23-30-29','Round-robin scheduling using a queue works by always executing the task at the front of the queue, then moving it to the back after it yields or completes its time slice. This creates a cycle where each thread gets a turn in order, just like round-robin with an array.\nThe benefit of using a queue over an array is that it naturally models the behavior of round-robin scheduling without needing to track indices manually. With a queue, you can easily enqueue and dequeue tasks, which makes it easier to extend to more complex scheduling models (e.g., priority queues). It also improves code readability and modularity, especially if we later implement dynamic task creation or deletion.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,1,'2025-07-18_23-48-49','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a queue by executing the thread at the front of the queue, then moving it to the back once it finishes its time slice or yields. This process repeats in a cycle, giving each task an equal share of CPU time.\nHowever, it’s important to note that even when an array is used to store TCBs, the scheduler may already simulate queue-like behavior by maintaining an index that rotates through the array in a round-robin fashion. This means that functionally, the system might already be behaving like a queue without explicitly using one.\nUsing an explicit queue data structure can improve clarity and modularity, particularly in systems that support dynamic task creation and deletion. However, it can also introduce overhead: maintaining a dynamic queue can increase memory usage (especially with pointer-based implementations) and potentially slow down scheduling due to enqueue/dequeue operations. Arrays, on the other hand, offer predictable access times and lower overhead for small, fixed-size systems.\nTherefore, while a queue provides conceptual elegance and flexibility, it may add unnecessary complexity in systems where tasks are statically defined and predictable scheduling performance is critical.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,1,'2025-07-20_23-13-00','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a queue by executing the thread at the front of the queue, then moving it to the back once it finishes its time slice or yields. This process repeats in a cycle, giving each task an equal share of CPU time.\nHowever, it’s important to note that even when an array is used to store TCBs, the scheduler may already simulate queue-like behavior by maintaining an index that rotates through the array in a round-robin fashion. This means that functionally, the system might already be behaving like a queue without explicitly using one.\nUsing an explicit queue data structure can improve clarity and modularity, particularly in systems that support dynamic task creation and deletion. However, it can also introduce overhead: maintaining a dynamic queue can increase memory usage (especially with pointer-based implementations) and potentially slow down scheduling due to enqueue/dequeue operations. Arrays, on the other hand, offer predictable access times and lower overhead for small, fixed-size systems.\nTherefore, while a queue provides conceptual elegance and flexibility, it may add unnecessary complexity in systems where tasks are statically defined and predictable scheduling performance is critical.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,2,'2025-07-18_23-30-29','The PendSV_Handler function is triggered when the processor is idle or finishes a higher-priority interrupt. It performs the context switch by first saving the current thread’s CPU context (registers R4–R11) onto that thread’s stack and updating its stack pointer. It then calls osSched(), which selects the next thread to run and sets the processor’s PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to that thread’s stack pointer. Finally, PendSV_Handler restores the CPU context (R4–R11) of the new thread from its stack, effectively transferring execution to the new thread.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,2,'2025-07-18_23-48-49','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by following a structured sequence of operations when triggered:\nIt begins by reading the current Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the current thread\'s stack.\nIt saves the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) of the running thread onto its own stack, updating the PSP to reflect the new top of the stack.\nIt stores this updated PSP value back into the current thread\'s TCB so the OS knows where to resume this thread later.\nThen, it calls osSched() to select the next thread to run. The scheduler returns a pointer to the next thread\'s TCB.\nThe PSP is updated to the stack pointer stored in the new thread’s TCB.\nThe handler then restores the new thread’s R4–R11 registers from its stack using the updated PSP.\nFinally, control is returned to the new thread, resuming execution where it left off.\nBy using the PSP and carefully managing register and stack state, PendSV_Handler ensures a seamless switch between threads.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,2,'2025-07-20_23-13-00','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by following a structured sequence of operations when triggered:\nIt begins by reading the current Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the current thread\'s stack.\nIt saves the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) of the running thread onto its own stack, updating the PSP to reflect the new top of the stack.\nIt stores this updated PSP value back into the current thread\'s TCB so the OS knows where to resume this thread later.\nThen, it calls osSched() to select the next thread to run. The scheduler returns a pointer to the next thread\'s TCB.\nThe PSP is updated to the stack pointer stored in the new thread’s TCB.\nThe handler then restores the new thread’s R4–R11 registers from its stack using the updated PSP.\nFinally, control is returned to the new thread, resuming execution where it left off.\nBy using the PSP and carefully managing register and stack state, PendSV_Handler ensures a seamless switch between threads.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,3,'2025-07-18_23-30-29','Always choosing the highest-priority thread without preemption leads to starvation of lower-priority threads. If a high-priority thread never yields or is constantly ready to run, the scheduler will never choose lower-priority threads. This violates fairness and can prevent essential lower-priority tasks (like logging or background cleanup) from ever executing. In real-time systems, this can lead to missed deadlines or system instability.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,3,'2025-07-18_23-48-49','Modifying the scheduler to always pick the highest-priority task without preemption or fairness mechanisms causes it to always select the same thread if that thread remains ready. This means the scheduler will repeatedly return the same high-priority task every time it runs, and lower-priority tasks will never get CPU time unless the high-priority task blocks or yields.\nThis leads to starvation of lower-priority threads. For example, if the high-priority thread performs a loop and never yields, other tasks - such as background cleanup or system logging - may never execute.\nThe core issue is not the use of priorities themselves, but rather that without proper preemption, aging, or time-slicing, the system becomes unfair and potentially unstable. In real-time systems, this can result in missed deadlines and unpredictable behavior, especially if lower-priority threads are responsible for critical background functions.\nThus, a priority-only scheduling policy, without safeguards for fairness, creates serious system-level issues.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,3,'2025-07-20_23-13-00','Modifying the scheduler to always pick the highest-priority task without preemption or fairness mechanisms causes it to always select the same thread if that thread remains ready. This means the scheduler will repeatedly return the same high-priority task every time it runs, and lower-priority tasks will never get CPU time unless the high-priority task blocks or yields.\nThis leads to starvation of lower-priority threads. For example, if the high-priority thread performs a loop and never yields, other tasks - such as background cleanup or system logging - may never execute.\nThe core issue is not the use of priorities themselves, but rather that without proper preemption, aging, or time-slicing, the system becomes unfair and potentially unstable. In real-time systems, this can result in missed deadlines and unpredictable behavior, especially if lower-priority threads are responsible for critical background functions.\nThus, a priority-only scheduling policy, without safeguards for fairness, creates serious system-level issues.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,4,'2025-07-18_23-30-29','Benefits of calling C functions:\nReadability and maintainability: C is much easier to read and write than assembly.\nPortability: C code can be reused or ported to other architectures with fewer changes.\nFaster development: Writing logic like thread selection or bookkeeping is faster and less error-prone in C.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,4,'2025-07-18_23-48-49','Benefits of calling C functions:\nReadability and maintainability: C is much easier to read and write than assembly.\nPortability: C code can be reused or ported to other architectures with fewer changes.\nFaster development: Writing logic like thread selection or bookkeeping is faster and less error-prone in C.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,4,'2025-07-20_23-13-00','Benefits of calling C functions:\nReadability and maintainability: C is much easier to read and write than assembly.\nPortability: C code can be reused or ported to other architectures with fewer changes.\nFaster development: Writing logic like thread selection or bookkeeping is faster and less error-prone in C.\n',0,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,5,'2025-07-18_23-30-29','Yes, the OS will still work, but context switching becomes trivial. Since there is only one thread, every time it calls osYield, the scheduler will \"switch\" back to the same thread. The PendSV handler will save and restore the same context, essentially doing extra work for no change. This demonstrates that the scheduler and context switch mechanism are functioning, but there\'s no actual multitasking happening since there’s only one thread in the system.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,5,'2025-07-18_23-48-49','Yes, the OS will still work, but context switching becomes trivial. Since there is only one thread, every time it calls osYield, the scheduler will \"switch\" back to the same thread. The PendSV handler will save and restore the same context, essentially doing extra work for no change. This demonstrates that the scheduler and context switch mechanism are functioning, but there\'s no actual multitasking happening since there’s only one thread in the system.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',4,5,'2025-07-20_23-13-00','Yes, the OS will still work, but context switching becomes trivial. Since there is only one thread, every time it calls osYield, the scheduler will \"switch\" back to the same thread. The PendSV handler will save and restore the same context, essentially doing extra work for no change. This demonstrates that the scheduler and context switch mechanism are functioning, but there\'s no actual multitasking happening since there’s only one thread in the system.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,1,'2025-07-18_23-36-22','From the user side, to pass an argument into a thread, the user must define a void* argument and pass it when calling osCreateThread (or osCreateThreadWithDeadline). For example, if the user wants to pass two integers, they can define a struct with those fields, initialize it, and pass a pointer to that struct.\nOn the OS side, when creating the thread, the kernel sets up the initial stack frame for that thread. It places the pointer to the argument in register R0 of the stack frame, since R0 is used by the ARM calling convention to pass the first argument to functions.\nWhen the thread is eventually run, the processor pops R0 from the stack as part of restoring the context. Since the thread function is defined as void thread(void* args), it receives the pointer properly. The user can then cast it to the appropriate type inside the thread and use the data.\nThis works because we are aligning with the standard function call ABI (Application Binary Interface), which expects R0 to hold the first argument. By manually placing the argument in R0 during thread creation, the thread function receives the correct input.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,1,'2025-07-21_19-50-36','From the user side, to pass an argument into a thread, the user must define a void* argument and pass it when calling osCreateThread (or osCreateThreadWithDeadline). For example, if the user wants to pass two integers, they can define a struct with those fields, initialize it, and pass a pointer to that struct.\nOn the OS side, when creating the thread, the kernel sets up the initial stack frame for that thread. It places the pointer to the argument in register R0 of the stack frame, since R0 is used by the ARM calling convention to pass the first argument to functions.\nWhen the thread is eventually run, the processor pops R0 from the stack as part of restoring the context. Since the thread function is defined as void thread(void* args), it receives the pointer properly. The user can then cast it to the appropriate type inside the thread and use the data.\nThis works because we are aligning with the standard function call ABI (Application Binary Interface), which expects R0 to hold the first argument. By manually placing the argument in R0 during thread creation, the thread function receives the correct input.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,1,'2025-07-27_23-06-48','From the user side, to pass an argument into a thread, the user must define a void* argument and pass it when calling osCreateThread (or osCreateThreadWithDeadline). For example, if the user wants to pass two integers, they can define a struct with those fields, initialize it, and pass a pointer to that struct.\nOn the OS side, when creating the thread, the kernel sets up the initial stack frame for that thread. It places the pointer to the argument in register R0 of the stack frame, since R0 is used by the ARM calling convention to pass the first argument to functions.\nWhen the thread is eventually run, the processor pops R0 from the stack as part of restoring the context. Since the thread function is defined as void thread(void* args), it receives the pointer properly. The user can then cast it to the appropriate type inside the thread and use the data.\nThis works because we are aligning with the standard function call ABI (Application Binary Interface), which expects R0 to hold the first argument. By manually placing the argument in R0 during thread creation, the thread function receives the correct input.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,2,'2025-07-18_23-36-22','Calling osYield inside any context-switching function is unsafe because it attempts to trigger a context switch while already performing one, leading to recursive and unpredictable behavior. osYield sets the PendSV bit and executes an isb (instruction synchronization barrier), which tells the processor to schedule a context switch after the current exception ends.\nIf you call osYield from inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, you\'re still inside an exception context. Scheduling another context switch before the current one has finished restoring the stack and registers can corrupt the stack, cause infinite nesting, or break the saved processor state.\nMoreover, it defeats the purpose of PendSV, which is to pend and delay the context switch until all other interrupts are done. Calling osYield inside these handlers creates reentrancy and race conditions, leading to undefined behavior and system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,2,'2025-07-21_19-50-36','Calling osYield inside any context-switching function is unsafe because it attempts to trigger a context switch while already performing one, leading to recursive and unpredictable behavior. osYield sets the PendSV bit and executes an isb (instruction synchronization barrier), which tells the processor to schedule a context switch after the current exception ends.\nIf you call osYield from inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, you\'re still inside an exception context. Scheduling another context switch before the current one has finished restoring the stack and registers can corrupt the stack, cause infinite nesting, or break the saved processor state.\nMoreover, it defeats the purpose of PendSV, which is to pend and delay the context switch until all other interrupts are done. Calling osYield inside these handlers creates reentrancy and race conditions, leading to undefined behavior and system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,2,'2025-07-27_23-06-48','Calling osYield inside any context-switching function is unsafe because it attempts to trigger a context switch while already performing one, leading to recursive and unpredictable behavior. osYield sets the PendSV bit and executes an isb (instruction synchronization barrier), which tells the processor to schedule a context switch after the current exception ends.\nIf you call osYield from inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, you\'re still inside an exception context. Scheduling another context switch before the current one has finished restoring the stack and registers can corrupt the stack, cause infinite nesting, or break the saved processor state.\nMoreover, it defeats the purpose of PendSV, which is to pend and delay the context switch until all other interrupts are done. Calling osYield inside these handlers creates reentrancy and race conditions, leading to undefined behavior and system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,3,'2025-07-18_23-36-22','Benefits:\nFaster responsiveness: A 0.1ms clock increases the OS\'s ability to respond quickly to time-based events or deadlines.\nFiner granularity: It allows for higher timing resolution, which is useful in real-time applications that require precise control.\nDownsides:\nHigher CPU overhead: More frequent timer interrupts (10x more per second) mean the CPU spends more time handling SysTick, leaving less time for user threads.\nIncreased power consumption: The system wakes up more often, leading to more switching and higher energy use, which is problematic for embedded or battery-powered devices.\nLower throughput: Excessive context switching reduces actual useful work done, since more CPU time is spent in overhead.\nReduced reliability: If the interrupt load becomes too high, lower-priority tasks might starve or timing jitter can increase unpredictably.\nIn short, reducing the tick interval improves precision but increases system burden. The trade-off must be carefully considered based on the application.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,3,'2025-07-21_19-50-36','Benefits:\nFaster responsiveness: A 0.1ms clock increases the OS\'s ability to respond quickly to time-based events or deadlines.\nFiner granularity: It allows for higher timing resolution, which is useful in real-time applications that require precise control.\nDownsides:\nHigher CPU overhead: More frequent timer interrupts (10x more per second) mean the CPU spends more time handling SysTick, leaving less time for user threads.\nIncreased power consumption: The system wakes up more often, leading to more switching and higher energy use, which is problematic for embedded or battery-powered devices.\nLower throughput: Excessive context switching reduces actual useful work done, since more CPU time is spent in overhead.\nReduced reliability: If the interrupt load becomes too high, lower-priority tasks might starve or timing jitter can increase unpredictably.\nIn short, reducing the tick interval improves precision but increases system burden. The trade-off must be carefully considered based on the application.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,3,'2025-07-27_23-06-48','Benefits:\nFaster responsiveness: A 0.1ms clock increases the OS\'s ability to respond quickly to time-based events or deadlines.\nFiner granularity: It allows for higher timing resolution, which is useful in real-time applications that require precise control.\nDownsides:\nHigher CPU overhead: More frequent timer interrupts (10x more per second) mean the CPU spends more time handling SysTick, leaving less time for user threads.\nIncreased power consumption: The system wakes up more often, leading to more switching and higher energy use, which is problematic for embedded or battery-powered devices.\nLower throughput: Excessive context switching reduces actual useful work done, since more CPU time is spent in overhead.\nReduced reliability: If the interrupt load becomes too high, lower-priority tasks might starve or timing jitter can increase unpredictably.\nIn short, reducing the tick interval improves precision but increases system burden. The trade-off must be carefully considered based on the application.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,4,'2025-07-18_23-36-22','From the user’s perspective:\nCo-operative multitasking means the thread must manually call osYield() to give up control. If a thread doesn’t yield, it will hog the CPU and prevent others from running.\nPre-emptive multitasking doesn’t require the user to do anything. The OS automatically interrupts the thread when its time slice expires and switches to another thread.\nFrom the OS perspective:\nCo-operative multitasking is simpler to implement. The OS only switches context when explicitly asked by the running thread.\nPre-emptive multitasking is more complex. The OS must track time slices, decrement runtime on each SysTick interrupt, and trigger a PendSV interrupt when time runs out - even if the thread does not yield.\nPre-emption gives better fairness and responsiveness but requires interrupt-based context switching and more bookkeeping by the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,4,'2025-07-21_19-50-36','From the user’s perspective:\nCo-operative multitasking means the thread must manually call osYield() to give up control. If a thread doesn’t yield, it will hog the CPU and prevent others from running.\nPre-emptive multitasking doesn’t require the user to do anything. The OS automatically interrupts the thread when its time slice expires and switches to another thread.\nFrom the OS perspective:\nCo-operative multitasking is simpler to implement. The OS only switches context when explicitly asked by the running thread.\nPre-emptive multitasking is more complex. The OS must track time slices, decrement runtime on each SysTick interrupt, and trigger a PendSV interrupt when time runs out - even if the thread does not yield.\nPre-emption gives better fairness and responsiveness but requires interrupt-based context switching and more bookkeeping by the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,4,'2025-07-27_23-06-48','From the user’s perspective:\nCo-operative multitasking means the thread must manually call osYield() to give up control. If a thread doesn’t yield, it will hog the CPU and prevent others from running.\nPre-emptive multitasking doesn’t require the user to do anything. The OS automatically interrupts the thread when its time slice expires and switches to another thread.\nFrom the OS perspective:\nCo-operative multitasking is simpler to implement. The OS only switches context when explicitly asked by the running thread.\nPre-emptive multitasking is more complex. The OS must track time slices, decrement runtime on each SysTick interrupt, and trigger a PendSV interrupt when time runs out - even if the thread does not yield.\nPre-emption gives better fairness and responsiveness but requires interrupt-based context switching and more bookkeeping by the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,5,'2025-07-18_23-36-22','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking both ultimately trigger the same context switch mechanism - the PendSV interrupt. Whether a thread voluntarily yields (osYield) or is forcibly pre-empted (via SysTick when its runtime hits zero), the OS performs the same steps: save the current thread\'s context and switch to the next one.\nThey work together because they are compatible ways to invoke the same process. The key difference is who initiates the switch: the thread itself (co-operative), or the OS timer (pre-emptive). As long as the thread’s runtime is reset properly in both cases, and context switches go through PendSV, the system can seamlessly mix both types.\nThis hybrid approach gives flexibility: some threads can yield when convenient, and others can be pre-empted if they run too long.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,5,'2025-07-21_19-50-36','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking both ultimately trigger the same context switch mechanism - the PendSV interrupt. Whether a thread voluntarily yields (osYield) or is forcibly pre-empted (via SysTick when its runtime hits zero), the OS performs the same steps: save the current thread\'s context and switch to the next one.\nThey work together because they are compatible ways to invoke the same process. The key difference is who initiates the switch: the thread itself (co-operative), or the OS timer (pre-emptive). As long as the thread’s runtime is reset properly in both cases, and context switches go through PendSV, the system can seamlessly mix both types.\nThis hybrid approach gives flexibility: some threads can yield when convenient, and others can be pre-empted if they run too long.\n',1,'s25'),('mjetzler',5,5,'2025-07-27_23-06-48','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking both ultimately trigger the same context switch mechanism - the PendSV interrupt. Whether a thread voluntarily yields (osYield) or is forcibly pre-empted (via SysTick when its runtime hits zero), the OS performs the same steps: save the current thread\'s context and switch to the next one.\nThey work together because they are compatible ways to invoke the same process. The key difference is who initiates the switch: the thread itself (co-operative), or the OS timer (pre-emptive). As long as the thread’s runtime is reset properly in both cases, and context switches go through PendSV, the system can seamlessly mix both types.\nThis hybrid approach gives flexibility: some threads can yield when convenient, and others can be pre-empted if they run too long.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',1,1,'2025-06-25_14-12-19','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. Its purpose is to simplify interaction with the hardware by providing easier-to-use libraries of code that programmers can use to interface with the hardware. It is typically written by the manufacturer of the chip so that applications programmers do not need to spend as much time getting their code up and running.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',1,1,'2025-08-13_10-26-51','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. Its purpose is to simplify interaction with the hardware by providing easier-to-use libraries of code that programmers can use to interface with the hardware. It is typically written by the manufacturer of the chip so that applications programmers do not need to spend as much time getting their code up and running.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',1,2,'2025-06-25_14-12-19','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that enables printing to the UART or console. The function is used by printf to put a single character to the output device, which in our case was a UART. It is required because different projects may require different hardware for printing. Therefore, it is not possible to write printf without it, since it is unknown which output device will be used in every project. Therefore, the programmer has to write it themselves once the output device has been chosen.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',1,2,'2025-08-13_10-26-51','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that enables printing to the UART or console. The function is used by printf to put a single character to the output device, which in our case was a UART. It is required because different projects may require different hardware for printing. Therefore, it is not possible to write printf without it, since it is unknown which output device will be used in every project. Therefore, the programmer has to write it themselves once the output device has been chosen.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',1,3,'2025-06-25_14-12-19','The debugger was required in order to examine the values stored in the registers. Since printf is a function that may use the registers, if we were to try to access them and print out their values we would not be certain whether the values we are seeing are due to printf’s temporary storage needs or our own code.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',1,3,'2025-08-13_10-26-51','The debugger was required in order to examine the values stored in the registers. Since printf is a function that may use the registers, if we were to try to access them and print out their values we would not be certain whether the values we are seeing are due to printf’s temporary storage needs or our own code.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',1,4,'2025-06-25_14-12-19','The new value for PSP is computed by offsetting the value of MSP_init_val by a value that we have chosen for the stack size. We subtracted this value from the initial value of the MSP, since in the Cortex M the stack grows downward. If we did not know the initial value for MSP, we could not be sure that the value for PSP would be a valid stack address. We had to have a known, valid starting address.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',1,4,'2025-08-13_10-26-51','The new value for PSP is computed by offsetting the value of MSP_init_val by a value that we have chosen for the stack size. We subtracted this value from the initial value of the MSP, since in the Cortex M the stack grows downward. If we did not know the initial value for MSP, we could not be sure that the value for PSP would be a valid stack address. We had to have a known, valid starting address.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',1,5,'2025-06-25_14-12-19','If we were to add multiple new stacks, we would use the same basic method of offsetting the address from a known, valid stack address. We would have to make sure that each new stack is offset using a rule that ensures that the new stack address is also valid, or there should be an error if no more memory is available. The easiest way to do this would be to keep track of the last allocated stack address, and then when a new stack is required, to offset by the stack size from that last address.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',1,5,'2025-08-13_10-26-51','If we were to add multiple new stacks, we would use the same basic method of offsetting the address from a known, valid stack address. We would have to make sure that each new stack is offset using a rule that ensures that the new stack address is also valid, or there should be an error if no more memory is available. The easiest way to do this would be to keep track of the last allocated stack address, and then when a new stack is required, to offset by the stack size from that last address.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',3,1,'2025-07-29_10-04-59','There are three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking, which happen in that order. In preprocessing, the C file is first processed to resolve things like #include directives, remove comments and so on, to get the file ready for compilation. In compilation, each individual C file is compiled into a separate object file, which is a file that contains machine instructions and potentially unresolved links to symbols in other files. Finally, during linking, these object files are put into a single executable, the links are resolved, and the code contains the instructions and data required to run.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',3,2,'2025-07-29_10-04-59','This can potentially cause multiple inclusion errors, where it is possible that multiple definitions of the same symbols are present. When compilation and linking occurs, these multiple definitions will cause errors that prevent the executable from being compiled.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',3,3,'2025-07-29_10-04-59','The user must know the thread function or its address. Usually, the user would write the thread function themselves. The user must initialize the kernel and then create or register the threads with the kernel. Finally, the kernel must be started. We must use kernel function calls to do this so  that the kernel gets set up and initialized with the appropriate information.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',3,4,'2025-07-29_10-04-59','The thread stack pool is a region in memory where the stacks for the operating system and its threads will be placed. The stack pool gets subdivided into stacks for the kernel and the threads, and these subdivided pieces are allocated to the threads.\n',1,'s25'),('mstachow',3,5,'2025-07-29_10-04-59','The user is responsible for writing the threads and interacting with the kernel via its functions or API calls. The kernel is then responsible for controlling and enabling access to the hardware or other things, such as memory. The separation between the user and the kernel is important because it simplifies the user’s job and lets them focus on their application, rather than low-level programming.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,1,'2025-06-01_19-57-49','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a software layer provided by microcontroller vendors that sits between your application code and the low-level hardware. It allows the programmer to control hardware without having to change very low-level details such as writing to registers. It also allows code to be reused across a family of microcontrollers.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,1,'2025-06-24_12-29-26','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a software layer provided by microcontroller vendors that sits between your application code and the low-level hardware. It allows the programmer to control hardware without having to change very low-level details such as writing to registers. It also allows code to be reused across a family of microcontrollers.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,1,'2025-06-24_13-33-45','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a software layer provided by microcontroller vendors that sits between your application code and the low-level hardware. It allows the programmer to control hardware without having to change very low-level details such as writing to registers. It also allows code to be reused across a family of microcontrollers.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,2,'2025-06-01_19-57-49','The __io_putchar() function is a special low-level function that\'s used to redirect character output from standard C library functions like printf() to a specific output device, such as a serial port. Printf() calls the __io_putchar() fuction each time it wants to print a character, and it is the user’s job to define putchar() to print the character however they like(or how their hardware permits).\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,2,'2025-06-24_12-29-26','The __io_putchar() function is a special low-level function that\'s used to redirect character output from standard C library functions like printf() to a specific output device, such as a serial port. Printf() calls the __io_putchar() fuction each time it wants to print a character, and it is the user’s job to define putchar() to print the character however they like(or how their hardware permits).\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,2,'2025-06-24_13-33-45','The __io_putchar() function is a special low-level function that\'s used to redirect character output from standard C library functions like printf() to a specific output device, such as a serial port. Printf() calls the __io_putchar() fuction each time it wants to print a character, and it is the user’s job to define putchar() to print the character however they like(or how their hardware permits).\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,3,'2025-06-01_19-57-49','We used the debugger since you can’t print the active stack pointer (sp) directly, as it changes during runtime, and depends on the processor mode. Also, the debugger makes it easy to go through the code step-by-step and directly see all of the register values.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,3,'2025-06-24_12-29-26','We used the debugger since you can’t print the active stack pointer (sp) directly, as it changes during runtime, and depends on the processor mode. Also, the debugger makes it easy to go through the code step-by-step and directly see all of the register values.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,3,'2025-06-24_13-33-45','We used the debugger since you can’t print the active stack pointer (sp) directly, as it changes during runtime, and depends on the processor mode. Also, the debugger makes it easy to go through the code step-by-step and directly see all of the register values.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,4,'2025-06-01_19-57-49','In this lab, we allocated a new stack for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by first obtaining the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). We calculated a new PSP value by subtracting an offset of 0x400 from the initial MSP value to reserve a separate region of memory for the PSP. This ensured that the PSP had its own dedicated stack space, avoiding any overlap with the MSP. Knowing the initial MSP value was important because it helped us position the PSP in a contiguous location to the MSP, at a fixed distance that determined the amount of memory allocated to the MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,4,'2025-06-24_12-29-26','In this lab, we allocated a new stack for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by first obtaining the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). We calculated a new PSP value by subtracting an offset of 0x400 from the initial MSP value to reserve a separate region of memory for the PSP. This ensured that the PSP had its own dedicated stack space, avoiding any overlap with the MSP. Knowing the initial MSP value was important because it helped us position the PSP in a contiguous location to the MSP, at a fixed distance that determined the amount of memory allocated to the MSP.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,4,'2025-06-24_13-33-45','In this lab, we allocated a new stack for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by first obtaining the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). We calculated the new PSP value by subtracting an offset (e.g., 0x400) from the initial MSP value, which reserved a separate region of memory for the PSP stack. This method ensured that the PSP pointed to a valid memory location—one that was properly aligned, within an allocated RAM region, and did not overlap with the MSP’s stack. Knowing the initial MSP value was essential because it gave us a safe and predictable reference point within valid memory. Without it, placing the PSP arbitrarily could lead to using an invalid or overlapping address, which might cause memory corruption or hard faults. By deriving the PSP from the initial MSP, we ensure that both stack pointers stay within bounds and follow a predictable layout, preserving system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,5,'2025-06-01_19-57-49','First, we would decide the amount of memory that each stack requires. Then, for each stack, we would calculate its starting address by repeatedly subtracting the size of the previous stack from MSP’s initial value. For example, the first new stack would be at stack0_INIT = MSP_INIT_VAL – msp_stack_size, and the next one would be at stack1_INIT = stack0_INIT – stack0_stack_size, and so on until you reach the desired number of stacks. It is important to also make sure that _Min_Stack_Size defined in the linker script is large enough to fit all of the stacks.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,5,'2025-06-24_12-29-26','First, we would determine the amount of memory each stack requires, ensuring that each stack size is aligned to an 8-byte boundary to meet the CPU’s alignment requirements. To find the starting address of each stack, we would subtract the size of each new stack from the previous one, starting from the initial value of MSP. For example, stack0_INIT = MSP_INIT_VAL - stack_size, stack1_INIT = stack0_INIT -stack_size, and so on. To prevent stack overlap, we must ensure that the total size of all allocated stacks does not exceed the size of the memory region reserved for stacks. This region should be explicitly defined in the linker script (e.g., by increasing _Min_Stack_Size or creating a dedicated stack pool section) so that all allocated stack addresses fall within a valid, non-overlapping memory range.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',1,5,'2025-06-24_13-33-45','First, we would determine the amount of memory each stack requires, ensuring that each stack size is aligned to an 8-byte boundary to meet the CPU’s alignment requirements. To find the starting address of each stack, we would subtract the size of each new stack from the previous one, starting from the initial value of MSP. For example, stack0_INIT = MSP_INIT_VAL - stack_size, stack1_INIT = stack0_INIT -stack_size, and so on. To prevent stack overlap, we must ensure that the total size of all allocated stacks does not exceed the size of the memory region reserved for stacks. This region should be explicitly defined in the linker script (e.g., by increasing _Min_Stack_Size or creating a dedicated stack pool section) so that all allocated stack addresses fall within a valid, non-overlapping memory range.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,1,'2025-06-23_11-49-15','When I changed PC to hold the pointer to print_continuously, the CPU began executing this function. It contains an infinite loop that prints “Hello, PC!” repeatedly, so that is what was done.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,1,'2025-06-23_17-51-36','When I changed PC to hold the pointer to print_continuously, the CPU began executing this function. It contains an infinite loop that prints “Hello, PC!” repeatedly, so that is what was done.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,1,'2025-06-24_12-19-36','When I changed the PC to hold the pointer to print_continuously, I was loading the PC register with the address of the first instruction of that function. Since the Program Counter (PC) determines the next instruction the CPU executes, this caused the CPU to jump to the start of print_continuously and begin running it. Because the function contains an infinite loop that repeatedly prints “Hello, PC!”, that is exactly what the CPU did.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,1,'2025-06-24_13-28-55','When I changed the PC to hold the pointer to print_continuously, I was loading the PC register with the address of the first instruction of that function. Since the Program Counter (PC) determines the next instruction the CPU executes, this caused the CPU to jump to the start of print_continuously and begin running it. Because the function contains an infinite loop that repeatedly prints “Hello, PC!”, that is exactly what the CPU did.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,2,'2025-06-23_11-49-15','A system call, also known as a trap is a way for a program to request the operating system to do something that it cannot do itself without kernel privileges, such as printing to console or reading from a disk. When a system call is made, the CPU switches to kernel mode, carries out the instruction, then switches back to user mode and hands control back to the program.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,2,'2025-06-23_17-51-36','A system call is a special type of function call that allows a user program to request a service from the operating system kernel. It provides a controlled way for user code to interact with kernel code in order to access privileged operations, like writing to the console or reading from a disk. When a system call is made, the CPU safely switches from user mode to kernel mode so the OS can perform the requested task, then switches back to user mode to return control to the program. This mechanism protects critical system resources and hardware by ensuring that untrusted user programs cannot access them directly, maintaining both stability and security.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,2,'2025-06-24_12-19-36','A system call is a special type of function call that allows a user program to request a service from the operating system kernel. It provides a controlled way for user code to interact with kernel code in order to access privileged operations, like writing to the console or reading from a disk. When a system call is made, the CPU safely switches from user mode to kernel mode so the OS can perform the requested task, then switches back to user mode to return control to the program. This mechanism protects critical system resources and hardware by ensuring that untrusted user programs cannot access them directly, maintaining both stability and security.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,2,'2025-06-24_13-28-55','A system call is a special type of function call that allows a user program to request a service from the operating system kernel. It provides a controlled way for user code to interact with kernel code in order to access privileged operations, like writing to the console or reading from a disk. When a system call is made, the CPU safely switches from user mode to kernel mode so the OS can perform the requested task, then switches back to user mode to return control to the program. This mechanism protects critical system resources and hardware by ensuring that untrusted user programs cannot access them directly, maintaining both stability and security.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,3,'2025-06-23_11-49-15','When a new thread is started, there need to be values in it’s stack to pop on the registers, so that it can begin functioning. To make sure this happens, all of the register values are put into a new stack, beginning from xPSR, then PC(R15), then R14-R1. The stack pointer is then set to the top of the new stack (i.ee where the R1 value is stored). This way, when the thread pops values off of the stack, it will start with R1 and put them all onto the registers in the correct order.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,3,'2025-06-23_17-51-36','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a valid block of memory specifically reserved to hold that thread’s stack, which is used to store the thread’s local data and register states. This memory must be separate from other threads’ stacks to avoid conflicts. To prepare the stack for a new thread, we place initial register values onto this reserved memory area in a specific order, starting with xPSR at the bottom, followed by the program counter (PC), and then registers like LR (R14) down to R1. These values simulate the state the CPU expects when returning from an exception, allowing the thread to start running the specified function. Finally, we set the stack pointer to point to the top of this prepared stack so that when the CPU pops values from the stack into the registers, it restores the thread’s context correctly and execution begins smoothly.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,3,'2025-06-24_12-19-36','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a valid block of memory specifically reserved to hold that thread’s stack, which is used to store the thread’s local data and register states. This memory must be separate from other threads’ stacks to avoid conflicts. To prepare the stack for a new thread, we place initial register values onto this reserved memory area in a specific order, starting with xPSR at the bottom, followed by the program counter (PC), and then registers like LR (R14) down to R1. These values simulate the state the CPU expects when returning from an exception, allowing the thread to start running the specified function. Finally, we set the stack pointer to point to the top of this prepared stack so that when the CPU pops values from the stack into the registers, it restores the thread’s context correctly and execution begins smoothly.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,3,'2025-06-24_13-28-55','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a valid block of memory specifically reserved to hold that thread’s stack, which is used to store the thread’s local data and register states. This memory must be separate from other threads’ stacks to avoid conflicts. To prepare the stack for a new thread, we place initial register values onto this reserved memory area in a specific order, starting with xPSR at the bottom, followed by the program counter (PC), and then registers like LR (R14) down to R1. These values simulate the state the CPU expects when returning from an exception, allowing the thread to start running the specified function. Finally, we set the stack pointer to point to the top of this prepared stack so that when the CPU pops values from the stack into the registers, it restores the thread’s context correctly and execution begins smoothly.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,4,'2025-06-23_11-49-15','If we don’t set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) using __set_PSP before running a new thread, the CPU won’t know where that thread’s stack is located. Since every function relies on the stack for things like storing return addresses and local variables, using an uninitialized or incorrect PSP causes the CPU to access invalid memory. This triggers a hard fault, which is the processor’s way of handling critical errors like invalid memory access. Setting the PSP ensures the new thread has a valid stack to operate on, preventing the system from crashing.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,4,'2025-06-23_17-51-36','If we don’t set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) using __set_PSP before running a new thread, the CPU won’t know where that thread’s stack is located. Since every function relies on the stack for things like storing return addresses and local variables, using an uninitialized or incorrect PSP causes the CPU to access invalid memory. This triggers a hard fault, which is the processor’s way of handling critical errors like invalid memory access. Setting the PSP ensures the new thread has a valid stack to operate on, preventing the system from crashing.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,4,'2025-06-24_12-19-36','If we don’t set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) using __set_PSP before running a new thread, the CPU won’t know where that thread’s stack is located. Since every function relies on the stack for things like storing return addresses and local variables, using an uninitialized or incorrect PSP causes the CPU to access invalid memory. This triggers a hard fault, which is the processor’s way of handling critical errors like invalid memory access. Setting the PSP ensures the new thread has a valid stack to operate on, preventing the system from crashing.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,4,'2025-06-24_13-28-55','If we don’t set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) using __set_PSP before running a new thread, the CPU won’t know where that thread’s stack is located. When running a thread, the CPU switches from using MSP to PSP, since MSP is only used for kernel functions, and MSP is meant to be used in user mode. When you switch to PSP, you must make sure that it is pointing to the top of the thread you want to run’s stack. Since every function relies on the stack for things like storing return addresses and local variables, using an uninitialized or incorrect PSP causes the CPU to access invalid memory. This triggers a hard fault, which is the processor’s way of handling critical errors like invalid memory access. Setting the PSP ensures the new thread has a valid stack to operate on, preventing the system from crashing.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,5,'2025-06-23_11-49-15','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead printed something once and returned, the system would crash, most likely resulting in a hard fault. This is because when the thread returns, the CPU tries to pop a return address from the stack to continue execution. But since we just set the R14 Link register to an arbitrary value, there’s no valid return address on its stack. The CPU ends up jumping to an invalid or unexpected location, causing a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,5,'2025-06-23_17-51-36','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead printed something once and returned, the system would crash, most likely resulting in a hard fault. This is because when the thread returns, the CPU tries to pop a return address from the stack to continue execution. But since we just set the R14 Link register to an arbitrary value, there’s no valid return address on its stack. The CPU ends up jumping to an invalid or unexpected location, causing a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,5,'2025-06-24_12-19-36','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead printed something once and returned, the system would crash, most likely resulting in a hard fault. This is because when the thread returns, the CPU tries to pop a return address from the stack to continue execution. But since we just set the R14 Link register to an arbitrary value, there’s no valid return address on its stack. The CPU ends up jumping to an invalid or unexpected location, causing a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',2,5,'2025-06-24_13-28-55','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead printed something once and returned, the system would crash, most likely resulting in a hard fault. This is because when the thread returns, the CPU tries to pop a return address from the stack to continue execution. But since we just set the R14 Link register to an arbitrary value, there’s no valid return address on its stack. The CPU ends up jumping to an invalid or unexpected location, causing a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,1,'2025-06-23_17-34-20','When you compile a C program made up of multiple .c files, each file is first compiled independently into an object file (.o) using a compiler like gcc. This step translates the C code into machine code without yet resolving references to functions or variables defined in other files. After all the .c files are compiled, the linker combines the resulting .o files into a single executable, resolving all inter-file references and linking in necessary libraries like the C standard library. The final output is a standalone exe file that contains all the compiled code from the original source files, ready to be run on your system.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,1,'2025-06-23_18-07-18','When compiling multiple .c files into a single executable, the process starts with preprocessing, where the compiler handles directives like #include and #define. During this step, all included files are copied into the source code, and macros are expanded, producing an intermediate file that’s ready for compilation. Next, each .c file is compiled independently into an object file (.o), translating the preprocessed C code into machine code but without yet resolving references between files. Finally, the linker combines all the .o files into one executable, resolving external function and variable references and linking in any required libraries like the C standard library. The order of these steps is essential because preprocessing must happen first to produce valid source code for compilation, and linking must occur last to combine all compiled code into a runnable program.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,1,'2025-06-24_12-15-18','When compiling multiple .c files into a single executable, the process starts with preprocessing, where the compiler handles directives like #include and #define. During this step, all included files are copied into the source code, and macros are expanded, producing an intermediate file that’s ready for compilation. Next, each .c file is compiled independently into an object file (.o), translating the preprocessed C code into machine code but without yet resolving references between files. Finally, the linker combines all the .o files into one executable, resolving external function and variable references and linking in any required libraries like the C standard library. The order of these steps is essential because preprocessing must happen first to produce valid source code for compilation, and linking must occur last to combine all compiled code into a runnable program.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,1,'2025-06-24_14-03-35','When compiling multiple .c files into a single executable, the process starts with preprocessing, where the compiler handles directives like #include and #define. During this step, all included files are copied into the source code, and macros are expanded, producing an intermediate file that’s ready for compilation. Next, each .c file is compiled independently into an object file (.o), translating the preprocessed C code into machine code but without yet resolving references between files. Finally, the linker combines all the .o files into one executable, resolving external function and variable references and linking in any required libraries like the C standard library. The order of these steps is essential because preprocessing must happen first to produce valid source code for compilation, and linking must occur last to combine all compiled code into a runnable program.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,2,'2025-06-23_17-34-20','Using #include <kernel.c> (or any .c file) is a bad idea because the #include directive simply copies and pastes the contents of the file into the current one before compilation. If you do this, the function in kernel.c would be defined twice, in the actual kernel.c file and the file that it is being included in. Instead, we include the header file so that the declaration is included, but there is still only one definition, allowing multiple source files to share information without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,2,'2025-06-23_18-07-18','Using #include <kernel.c> (or any .c file) is a bad idea because the #include directive simply copies and pastes the contents of the file into the current one before compilation. If you do this, the function in kernel.c would be defined twice, in the actual kernel.c file and the file that it is being included in. Instead, we include the header file so that the declaration is included, but there is still only one definition, allowing multiple source files to share information without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,2,'2025-06-24_12-15-18','Using #include <kernel.c> (or any .c file) is a bad idea because the #include directive simply copies and pastes the contents of the file into the current one before compilation. If you do this, the function in kernel.c would be defined twice, in the actual kernel.c file and the file that it is being included in. Instead, we include the header file so that the declaration is included, but there is still only one definition, allowing multiple source files to share information without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,2,'2025-06-24_14-03-35','Using #include <kernel.c> (or any .c file) is a bad idea because the #include directive simply copies and pastes the contents of the file into the current one before compilation. If you do this, the function in kernel.c would be defined twice, in the actual kernel.c file and the file that it is being included in. Instead, we include the header file so that the declaration is included, but there is still only one definition, allowing multiple source files to share information without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,3,'2025-06-23_17-34-20','Prepare the thread\'s stack by placing it at a fixed offset from other thread stacks. Fill it with the initial register values the CPU expects on exception return: start with xPSR at the bottom, then the address of the function to run (PC), followed by other registers like LR, R12, down to R0. This creates a fake exception frame so the CPU can \"return\" into the thread correctly.\nSet PSP to point to the top of the new stack. Make sure the control register is set to use PSP, not MSP, which is usually reserved for kernel use.\nStart the thread by triggering an exception return (handled by the OS). The CPU will restore the register values from the stack and begin executing the thread’s function at the address placed in the PC slot.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,3,'2025-06-23_18-07-18','Write the thread function that defines what the thread will do when it runs. This is the code the thread will execute.\nRequest the OS to create a new thread by calling a function that sets up the thread’s execution context. From the user’s perspective, this involves specifying the thread function and letting the OS handle the details.\nInternally, the OS allocates a valid memory area for the thread’s stack (separate from other threads) and initializes the stack with the values the CPU expects on an exception return. This setup includes placing the initial processor state in order—starting with xPSR, then the program counter (PC) set to the thread function’s address, followed by other registers like LR and R13-R1.\nThe OS then sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the top of this prepared stack and configures the CPU to use PSP instead of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is typically reserved for the kernel.\nFinally, the user triggers the start of the thread via an OS call or scheduler action, and the CPU restores the register values from the stack and begins executing the thread function.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,3,'2025-06-24_12-15-18','Write the thread function that defines the behavior of the thread. This is the code the thread will execute when it runs.\nInitialize the kernel by calling a setup function provided by the system. This step prepares the kernel’s internal data structures, enables scheduling, and gets the system ready to manage threads.\nCreate the thread by calling a kernel-provided function and passing it the address of the thread function. From the user’s perspective, this is where they request the kernel to create a new thread and prepare it to run.\nBehind the scenes, the kernel allocates a valid block of memory for the thread’s stack and fills it with an initial register context in the correct order, starting with xPSR, followed by PC (pointing to the thread function), then LR, and other registers like R12 through R1. This forms a fake exception frame so the CPU can start the thread properly.\nThe kernel then sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to the top of this stack and configures the CPU to use PSP (rather than the MSP, which is reserved for kernel use).\nFinally, the user starts the kernel’s scheduler, which begins executing threads. When the new thread is selected to run, the CPU restores the prepared register values from the stack and begins executing the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,3,'2025-06-24_14-03-35','Write the thread function that defines the behavior of the thread. This is the code the thread will execute when it runs.\nInitialize the kernel by calling a setup function provided by the system. This step prepares the kernel’s internal data structures, enables scheduling, and gets the system ready to manage threads.\nCreate the thread by calling a kernel-provided function and passing it the address of the thread function. From the user’s perspective, this is where they request the kernel to create a new thread and prepare it to run.\nBehind the scenes, the kernel allocates a valid block of memory for the thread’s stack and fills it with an initial register context in the correct order, starting with xPSR, followed by PC (pointing to the thread function), then LR, and other registers like R12 through R1. This forms a fake exception frame so the CPU can start the thread properly.\nThe kernel then sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to the top of this stack and configures the CPU to use PSP (rather than the MSP, which is reserved for kernel use).\nFinally, the user starts the kernel’s scheduler, which begins executing threads. When the new thread is selected to run, the CPU restores the prepared register values from the stack and begins executing the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,4,'2025-06-23_17-34-20','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory set aside to hold the stacks for multiple threads in an operating system. Each thread needs its own stack to store local variables, function call information, and return addresses. In future labs, when we create multiple threads, the stack pool will be used to allocate a separate section of memory to each thread as its own stack space. By assigning each thread a unique starting point within the stack pool and setting the processor’s PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to that address when switching threads, we ensure that each thread operates independently without corrupting another thread’s stack. This is essential for safe and reliable multitasking in our OS.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,4,'2025-06-23_18-07-18','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory set aside to hold the stacks for multiple threads in an operating system. Each thread needs its own stack to store local variables, function call information, and return addresses. In future labs, when we create multiple threads, the stack pool will be used to allocate a separate section of memory to each thread as its own stack space. By assigning each thread a unique starting point within the stack pool and setting the processor’s PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to that address when switching threads, we ensure that each thread operates independently without corrupting another thread’s stack. This is essential for safe and reliable multitasking in our OS.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,4,'2025-06-24_12-15-18','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory set aside to hold the stacks for multiple threads in an operating system. Each thread needs its own stack to store local variables, function call information, and return addresses. In future labs, when we create multiple threads, the stack pool will be used to allocate a separate section of memory to each thread as its own stack space. By assigning each thread a unique starting point within the stack pool and setting the processor’s PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to that address when switching threads, we ensure that each thread operates independently without corrupting another thread’s stack. This is essential for safe and reliable multitasking in our OS.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,4,'2025-06-24_14-03-35','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory set aside to hold the stacks for multiple threads in an operating system. Each thread needs its own stack to store local variables, function call information, and return addresses. In future labs, when we create multiple threads, the stack pool will be used to allocate a separate section of memory to each thread as its own stack space. By assigning each thread a unique starting point within the stack pool and setting the processor’s PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to that address when switching threads, we ensure that each thread operates independently without corrupting another thread’s stack. This is essential for safe and reliable multitasking in our OS.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,5,'2025-06-23_17-34-20','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” in our OS project is reflected in how code is organized into main.c and kernel.c. Code in main.c represents the user-level program, which is what the user wants to run, like creating and running threads or printing output. In contrast, kernel.c contains the operating system code that manages low-level tasks such as context switching, scheduling, and stack management. This separation is important because it allows the user to write programs without worrying about the complex inner workings of the OS. It also keeps the system modular and safer, as bugs in user code don’t directly affect kernel behavior, and the kernel can enforce boundaries to prevent misuse of system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,5,'2025-06-23_18-07-18','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” in our OS project is reflected in how code is organized into main.c and kernel.c. Code in main.c represents the user-level program, which is what the user wants to run, like creating and running threads or printing output. In contrast, kernel.c contains the operating system code that manages low-level tasks such as context switching, scheduling, and stack management. This separation is important because it allows the user to write programs without worrying about the complex inner workings of the OS. It also keeps the system modular and safer, as bugs in user code don’t directly affect kernel behavior, and the kernel can enforce boundaries to prevent misuse of system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,5,'2025-06-24_12-15-18','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” in our OS project is reflected in how code is organized into main.c and kernel.c. Code in main.c represents the user-level program, which is what the user wants to run, like creating and running threads or printing output. In contrast, kernel.c contains the operating system code that manages low-level tasks such as context switching, scheduling, and stack management. This separation is important because it allows the user to write programs without worrying about the complex inner workings of the OS. It also keeps the system modular and safer, as bugs in user code don’t directly affect kernel behavior, and the kernel can enforce boundaries to prevent misuse of system resources.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',3,5,'2025-06-24_14-03-35','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” in our OS project is reflected in how code is organized into main.c and kernel.c. Code in main.c represents the user-level program, which includes writing thread functions and using API calls provided by the kernel to create and start threads or print output. In contrast, kernel.c contains the kernel code responsible for low-level operations like context switching, scheduling, and stack management It also contains definitions for the API calls made in the user program. This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on application logic without needing to understand or modify the internal workings of the kernel. From the user’s perspective, interacting with the kernel through well-defined API calls simplifies development and enforces a boundary that improves system safety. The kernel also plays a critical role in controlling access to hardware and memory, ensuring that user code cannot directly access devices or corrupt memory used by other threads or the kernel itself. This protects the system from crashes and unintended behavior, making the separation both practical and secure.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',4,1,'2025-07-26_18-46-19','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using either an array or a queue, but using a queue offers some advantages. In a typical array-based implementation, all TCBs are stored in a fixed-size array, and the scheduler cycles through them using an index. While this is simple and efficient for systems with a fixed number of tasks, it can slow down when tasks are created or terminated dynamically. The array must be manually managed to skip over blocked or inactive tasks, which can add complexity.\nIn contrast, when using a queue, each task is placed in a queue, and the scheduler pops the task at the front, runs it, and then either places it at the back of the queue if it is still runnable or removes it if it has completed or is blocked. This structure automatically cycles through only the active tasks, avoiding the need to manually skip over inactive ones. It also makes it easier to handle tasks that are added or removed at runtime, since new tasks can simply be enqueued and terminated tasks can be removed without affecting the rest of the structure.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',4,1,'2025-07-29_15-02-06','The round-robin scheduler in the lab already works in a way that\'s similar to a queue, even though it uses an array of TCBs and cycles through them with an index. It checks if each task is runnable and skips over any that are blocked, which mimics how a queue would only work with active tasks.\nUsing an actual queue structure could make this behavior more explicit. In a queue-based version, the scheduler would take the task from the front of the queue, run it, and then put it back at the end if it\'s still active. If a task is blocked or finished, it just wouldn’t be added back. This makes the scheduler automatically cycle through only the tasks that can run, without having to manually check or skip over others.\nThere are some trade-offs, though. Implementing a queue might involve using pointers or dynamic memory, especially in C. That adds some complexity and potential for bugs. The array-based method used in the lab is simple, predictable, and works well when the number of tasks is small and fixed, which is usually the case in embedded systems.\nOverall, both methods support round-robin scheduling. The array approach is more straightforward and fits well with the lab’s setup, while a queue can be more flexible and cleaner to manage, especially in systems where tasks are created and removed more often.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',4,2,'2025-07-26_18-46-19','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch safely by taking advantage of the Cortex-M interrupt system. When a context switch is requested in SVC_Handler_Main, local variables are pushed onto the stack. If we switched context immediately, the function would never return, and those variables would stay on the stack, eventually causing overflow. Also, modifying key CPU registers for a context switch can only be done safely within an interrupt.\nTo solve this, SVC_Handler_Main sets the PendSV interrupt to pending and then returns, which clears the stack properly. Once SVC_Handler_Main finishes, PendSV runs at its lower priority and the PendSV_Handler function performs the actual context switch. This ensures the stack is restored and the processor state is safely managed during the switch.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',4,2,'2025-07-29_15-02-06','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for performing the actual context switch between threads. It runs at the lowest priority so that it doesn’t interrupt more important work and only runs when nothing else is happening.\nWhen a context switch is requested, for example by SVC_Handler_Main, the switch isn’t done right away. Instead, PendSV is set to pending, and SVC_Handler_Main returns. This allows the current function to finish and clean up its stack frame before switching, which helps avoid stack corruption or overflow.\nOnce PendSV_Handler starts running, it saves the current thread’s context by pushing the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto that thread’s stack. This is important because these registers aren’t automatically saved by the hardware during interrupts, so we need to do it manually.\nThe current value of the PSP is saved in the thread’s TCB so the thread can resume later. Then, the scheduler function osSched() is called to pick the next thread to run. This function returns a pointer to the next thread’s TCB. In this lab, the threads just cycle through in a fixed order.\nThe new thread’s stack pointer is loaded from its TCB, and the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) are popped from that stack. At this point, the CPU state has been fully restored for the new thread, and the handler exits. The processor uses the updated PSP and the remaining register values (automatically saved during the interrupt) to continue execution in the new thread.\nThis process makes sure that each thread resumes exactly where it left off, with its own set of register values and stack contents, allowing multitasking to work correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',4,3,'2025-07-26_18-46-19','Modifying the scheduler to always choose the highest priority task defeats the purpose of multithreading because lower-priority tasks may never get a chance to run. If the highest-priority task is always ready, it will monopolize the CPU, leading to starvation of lower-priority tasks. This violates fairness and can break important system functionality, especially if low-priority tasks handle critical operations.\nAdditionally, dynamically changing priorities just to force a context switch is inefficient and error-prone. It adds unnecessary complexity and can introduce bugs such as priority inversion or integer overflows if priorities are not carefully managed.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',4,3,'2025-07-29_15-02-06','Modifying the scheduler to always choose the highest priority task defeats the purpose of multithreading because lower-priority tasks may never get a chance to run. If the highest-priority task is always ready, it will monopolize the CPU, leading to starvation of lower-priority tasks. This violates fairness and can break important system functionality, especially if low-priority tasks handle critical operations.\nAdditionally, dynamically changing priorities just to force a context switch is inefficient and error-prone. It adds unnecessary complexity and can introduce bugs such as priority inversion or integer overflows if priorities are not carefully managed.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',4,4,'2025-07-26_18-46-19','Calling C functions like osSched in PendSV_Handler and SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler has both advantages and disadvantages compared to writing everything in assembly. One major benefit is readability and maintainability. C code is easier to understand, debug, and modify, especially for complex logic such as deciding which task to schedule next. It also allows us to reuse existing C libraries and take advantage of the compiler’s optimizations. However, calling C functions from assembly introduces overhead, such as saving and restoring registers and managing the function call stack, which can affect performance. Additionally, low-level operations like directly manipulating CPU registers or performing a precise context switch are harder or sometimes not possible in C, so some assembly is still necessary. In general, using C for higher-level logic while keeping low-level operations in assembly provides a good balance between efficiency and clarity.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',4,4,'2025-07-29_15-02-06','Calling C functions like osSched in PendSV_Handler and SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler has both advantages and disadvantages compared to writing everything in assembly. One major benefit is readability and maintainability. C code is easier to understand, debug, and modify, especially for complex logic such as deciding which task to schedule next. It also allows us to reuse existing C libraries and take advantage of the compiler’s optimizations. However, calling C functions from assembly introduces overhead, such as saving and restoring registers and managing the function call stack, which can affect performance. Additionally, low-level operations like directly manipulating CPU registers or performing a precise context switch are harder or sometimes not possible in C, so some assembly is still necessary. In general, using C for higher-level logic while keeping low-level operations in assembly provides a good balance between efficiency and clarity.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',4,5,'2025-07-26_18-46-19','Yes, the OS would still work with only a single thread. Since there is only one thread in the system, when osYield() is called, the scheduler would simply select the same thread again. This means the thread would continue running as usual, just with an unnecessary context switch each time it yields. While this adds a small performance overhead due to saving and restoring the same context, the system would still function correctly. The context switch would be redundant, but it would not break the behavior of the OS or the thread itself.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',4,5,'2025-07-29_15-02-06','Yes, the OS would still work with only a single thread. Since there is only one thread in the system, when osYield() is called, the scheduler would simply select the same thread again. This means the thread would continue running as usual, just with an unnecessary context switch each time it yields. While this adds a small performance overhead due to saving and restoring the same context, the system would still function correctly. The context switch would be redundant, but it would not break the behavior of the OS or the thread itself.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,1,'2025-07-29_15-06-53','The input arguments to a thread are stored on R0-R4. Before the thread runs, the user puts the input argument on the thread’s stack, in the right position so that when the thread runs, it restores the stack frame and pops this value from the stack into the R0 register, then runs the thread function(the pointer to which is in the PC register) with the value in R0 as the input argument. Usually, the input argument is a pointer to the actual input variable rather than the variable itself. This allows larger data structures such as structs to be used as inputs. In this case, the user-written thread function must reference the argument in order to access the desired value.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,1,'2025-07-29_18-30-02','To pass an argument into a thread, the user provides a pointer to the input data (often a struct or variable) and sets up the thread’s initial stack manually in osCreateThread(). As part of this setup, the user places the argument pointer into the correct position on the stack so that it will be loaded into register R0 when the thread starts running.\nThe stack is initialized to mimic what the CPU would expect after an exception return — a full register frame including xPSR, PC, LR, and R0–R3, among others. The user fills in this frame manually. The PC is set to the thread function, and R0 is set to the argument pointer.\nWhen the thread is later scheduled, the OS sets the PSP (process stack pointer) to point to this prepared stack. Then, during the context switch, the CPU automatically pops this frame off the stack and loads the registers. As a result, the thread begins executing at the function given in PC, with the value in R0 passed as its argument.\nThis works because of the ARM calling convention, which specifies that function arguments are passed through R0–R3. By placing the argument pointer in the R0 slot of the initial stack frame, the thread starts exactly as if it had been called normally, and can access the argument directly in its parameter list.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,1,'2025-07-30_13-36-54','To pass an argument into a thread on the Cortex-M4, the user starts by creating a variable in memory, often a struct or an integer. Instead of passing the value directly, the user passes a pointer to this variable when calling osCreateThread(). This allows the thread to access or modify the data without copying large values through the limited number of registers.\nThe user provides three things to osCreateThread(): the function pointer to the thread, the pointer to the argument, and the starting location of the thread\'s stack. The user does not directly modify the stack contents. Instead, the operating system is responsible for building the thread\'s initial stack frame.\nInside osCreateThread(), the OS creates a fake exception frame on the stack. This frame contains values for the xPSR, PC (the thread function), LR, and registers R0 to R3, along with R12. According to the ARM Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS), function arguments are passed in registers R0 to R3. The OS places the pointer to the user’s variable in the R0 slot of the stack frame.\nWhen the thread is first scheduled, the OS sets the process stack pointer (PSP) to point to the top of this prepared frame. During the context switch, the CPU pops the register values from the stack. This loads the thread function into the PC and the argument pointer into R0. As a result, when the thread begins executing, it receives the argument through its parameter list as if it had been called in normal C code.\nThis process works because it follows the ARM AAPCS, which defines how parameters are passed during function calls. By carefully placing the argument pointer into the R0 register during stack setup, the OS ensures the thread starts with the correct input, without needing any special handling inside the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,1,'2025-07-30_14-08-35','To pass an argument into a thread on the Cortex-M4, the user starts by creating a variable in memory, often a struct or an integer. Instead of passing the value directly, the user passes a pointer to this variable when calling osCreateThread(). This allows the thread to access or modify the data without copying large values through the limited number of registers.\nThe user provides three things to osCreateThread(): the function pointer to the thread, the pointer to the argument, and the starting location of the thread\'s stack. The user does not directly modify the stack contents. Instead, the operating system is responsible for building the thread\'s initial stack frame.\nInside osCreateThread(), the OS creates a fake exception frame on the stack. This frame contains values for the xPSR, PC (the thread function), LR, and registers R0 to R3, along with R12. According to the ARM Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS), function arguments are passed in registers R0 to R3. The OS places the pointer to the user’s variable in the R0 slot of the stack frame.\nWhen the thread is first scheduled, the OS sets the process stack pointer (PSP) to point to the top of this prepared frame. During the context switch, the CPU pops the register values from the stack. This loads the thread function into the PC and the argument pointer into R0. As a result, when the thread begins executing, it receives the argument through its parameter list as if it had been called in normal C code.\nThis process works because it follows the ARM AAPCS, which defines how parameters are passed during function calls. By carefully placing the argument pointer into the R0 register during stack setup, the OS ensures the thread starts with the correct input, without needing any special handling inside the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,1,'2025-07-30_21-41-48','To pass an argument into a thread on the Cortex-M4, the user starts by creating a variable in memory, often a struct or an integer. Instead of passing the value directly, the user passes a pointer to this variable when calling osCreateThread(). This allows the thread to access or modify the data without copying large values through the limited number of registers.\nThe user provides three things to osCreateThread(): the function pointer to the thread, the pointer to the argument, and the starting location of the thread\'s stack. The user does not directly modify the stack contents. Instead, the operating system is responsible for building the thread\'s initial stack frame.\nInside osCreateThread(), the OS creates a fake exception frame on the stack. This frame contains values for the xPSR, PC (the thread function), LR, and registers R0 to R3, along with R12. According to the ARM Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS), function arguments are passed in registers R0 to R3. The OS places the pointer to the user’s variable in the R0 slot of the stack frame.\nWhen the thread is first scheduled, the OS sets the process stack pointer (PSP) to point to the top of this prepared frame. During the context switch, the CPU pops the register values from the stack. This loads the thread function into the PC and the argument pointer into R0. As a result, when the thread begins executing, it receives the argument through its parameter list as if it had been called in normal C code.\nThis process works because it follows the ARM AAPCS, which defines how parameters are passed during function calls. By carefully placing the argument pointer into the R0 register during stack setup, the OS ensures the thread starts with the correct input, without needing any special handling inside the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,2,'2025-07-29_15-06-53','The purpose of OsYield is to trigger an SVC interrupt, which in turn triggers a pending PendSV interrupt. If PendSV_Handler or SVC_Handler/SVC_Handler_Main called OsYield, they would effectively be calling themselves recursively, causing an infinite loop. If you called OsYield from SysTick_Handler, it would trigger an SVC call interrupt, which would then perform a context switch, without letting SysTick_Handler clear its stack, causing a memory leak.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,2,'2025-07-29_18-30-02','You should not call osYield() from inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because it can lead to re-entrancy and stack corruption.\nosYield() triggers an SVC interrupt to request a context switch. But on the Cortex-M4, interrupts are not allowed to preempt other interrupts of the same or lower priority. If you call osYield() inside SVC_Handler, for example, it tries to trigger another SVC while one is already in progress, which won’t run until the current one finishes. If the handler keeps trying to trigger itself, this leads to an infinite loop or a hard fault.\nCalling osYield() from SysTick_Handler is especially dangerous. It could cause a context switch before the SysTick handler finishes. Since the CPU hasn\'t yet restored the full stack frame, the interrupted thread’s context may be saved incorrectly. This results in stack corruption, where return addresses or register values are overwritten or lost.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,2,'2025-07-30_13-36-54','The purpose of osYield() is to allow a running thread to voluntarily give up the CPU so that the operating system can switch to another ready thread. It does this by triggering an SVC (Supervisor Call) interrupt, which sets the PendSV (Pendable Service) interrupt as pending. The actual context switch happens in PendSV_Handler.\nYou cannot safely call osYield() from within PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these are already running in handler mode on the Cortex-M4. If you call osYield() from within one of these functions, it tries to trigger another SVC interrupt while the current one is still active. Since the Cortex-M4 interrupt controller does not allow an interrupt to preempt itself or any higher-priority interrupt, the new SVC request cannot be handled immediately. This can cause the system to repeatedly request the same interrupt without progress, leading to infinite loops or a hard fault.\nCalling osYield() inside SysTick_Handler is especially risky. SysTick_Handler is a periodic interrupt used to drive preemptive scheduling. If osYield() is called before SysTick_Handler finishes, it may cause a context switch while the stack frame is still incomplete. In this case, the OS could overwrite part of the stack or return to the wrong location, resulting in stack corruption. This is not just a memory leak, as it compromises the integrity of the running program and can lead to unpredictable behavior, crashes, or lost data.\nFrom a scheduling perspective, calling osYield() at the wrong time interferes with the clean separation between thread and interrupt modes. Context switching depends on that separation to manage registers, the stack, and scheduling decisions safely. Breaking this flow can corrupt the CPU state and make the system unstable or unresponsive.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,2,'2025-07-30_14-08-35','The purpose of osYield() is to allow a running thread to voluntarily give up the CPU so that the operating system can switch to another ready thread. It does this by triggering an SVC (Supervisor Call) interrupt, which sets the PendSV (Pendable Service) interrupt as pending. The actual context switch happens in PendSV_Handler.\nYou cannot safely call osYield() from within PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these are already running in handler mode on the Cortex-M4. If you call osYield() from within one of these functions, it tries to trigger another SVC interrupt while the current one is still active. Since the Cortex-M4 interrupt controller does not allow an interrupt to preempt itself or any higher-priority interrupt, the new SVC request cannot be handled immediately. This can cause the system to repeatedly request the same interrupt without progress, leading to infinite loops or a hard fault.\nCalling osYield() inside SysTick_Handler is especially risky. SysTick_Handler is a periodic interrupt used to drive preemptive scheduling. If osYield() is called before SysTick_Handler finishes, it may cause a context switch while the stack frame is still incomplete. In this case, the OS could overwrite part of the stack or return to the wrong location, resulting in stack corruption. This is not just a memory leak, as it compromises the integrity of the running program and can lead to unpredictable behavior, crashes, or lost data.\nFrom a scheduling perspective, calling osYield() at the wrong time interferes with the clean separation between thread and interrupt modes. Context switching depends on that separation to manage registers, the stack, and scheduling decisions safely. Breaking this flow can corrupt the CPU state and make the system unstable or unresponsive.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,2,'2025-07-30_21-41-48','The purpose of osYield() is to allow a running thread to voluntarily give up the CPU so that the operating system can switch to another ready thread. It does this by triggering an SVC (Supervisor Call) interrupt, which sets the PendSV (Pendable Service) interrupt as pending. The actual context switch happens in PendSV_Handler.\nYou cannot safely call osYield() from within PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these are already running in handler mode on the Cortex-M4. If you call osYield() from within one of these functions, it tries to trigger another SVC interrupt while the current one is still active. Since the Cortex-M4 interrupt controller does not allow an interrupt to preempt itself or any higher-priority interrupt, the new SVC request cannot be handled immediately. This can cause the system to repeatedly request the same interrupt without progress, leading to infinite loops or a hard fault.\nCalling osYield() inside SysTick_Handler is especially risky. SysTick_Handler is a periodic interrupt used to drive preemptive scheduling. If osYield() is called before SysTick_Handler finishes, it may cause a context switch while the stack frame is still incomplete. In this case, the OS could overwrite part of the stack or return to the wrong location, resulting in stack corruption. This is not just a memory leak, as it compromises the integrity of the running program and can lead to unpredictable behavior, crashes, or lost data.\nFrom a scheduling perspective, calling osYield() at the wrong time interferes with the clean separation between thread and interrupt modes. Context switching depends on that separation to manage registers, the stack, and scheduling decisions safely. Breaking this flow can corrupt the CPU state and make the system unstable or unresponsive.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,3,'2025-07-29_15-06-53','The benefits would be a greater OS clock speed and resolution, meaning that the OS can respond to events faster, and do things such as context switching more frequently. This can be important in time-critical applications where different threads need to update different values very rapidly. However, this would also introduce significant overhead, as the SysTick interrupt would be triggered 10 times more frequently, meaning that SysTick_Handler would have to run 10 times more.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,3,'2025-07-29_18-30-02','The benefits would be a greater OS clock speed and resolution, meaning that the OS can respond to events faster, and do things such as context switching more frequently. This can be important in time-critical applications where different threads need to update different values very rapidly. However, this would also introduce significant overhead, as the SysTick interrupt would be triggered 10 times more frequently, meaning that SysTick_Handler would have to run 10 times more.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,3,'2025-07-30_13-36-54','The benefits would be a greater OS clock speed and resolution, meaning that the OS can respond to events faster, and do things such as context switching more frequently. This can be important in time-critical applications where different threads need to update different values very rapidly. However, this would also introduce significant overhead, as the SysTick interrupt would be triggered 10 times more frequently, meaning that SysTick_Handler would have to run 10 times more.\n',0,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,3,'2025-07-30_14-08-35','Reducing the OS clock interval from 1 ms to 0.1 ms increases the temporal resolution of the system. This allows for more precise timing and faster response to delays, which is useful in applications that require frequent updates or fine-grained control, such as real-time sensor sampling or motor control. The OS can also preempt tasks more accurately and react to scheduling events more quickly.\nHowever, this comes with trade-offs. First, the SysTick interrupt will now fire 10 times more often, increasing the time spent in interrupt handling and context switching. This raises CPU overhead and leaves less time for actual thread execution. Second, the increased interrupt frequency can negatively impact system resources like the instruction and data cache. Constant switching between threads may lead to more cache invalidations and memory bus contention, which lowers overall system efficiency. Additionally, higher interrupt rates can also lead to increased power consumption, which is critical in embedded systems running on battery power.\nIn summary, while a shorter OS tick improves timing precision and responsiveness, it also increases CPU overhead and can reduce overall system efficiency due to more frequent context switches and greater resource contention. The choice of tick rate must balance precision against these costs.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,3,'2025-07-30_21-41-48','Reducing the OS clock interval from 1 ms to 0.1 ms increases the temporal resolution of the system. This allows for more precise timing and faster response to delays, which is useful in applications that require frequent updates or fine-grained control, such as real-time sensor sampling or motor control. The OS can also preempt tasks more accurately and react to scheduling events more quickly.\nHowever, this comes with trade-offs. First, the SysTick interrupt will now fire 10 times more often, increasing the time spent in interrupt handling and context switching. This raises CPU overhead and leaves less time for actual thread execution. Second, the increased interrupt frequency can negatively impact system resources like the instruction and data cache. Constant switching between threads may lead to more cache invalidations and memory bus contention, which lowers overall system efficiency. Additionally, higher interrupt rates can also lead to increased power consumption, which is critical in embedded systems running on battery power.\nIn summary, while a shorter OS tick improves timing precision and responsiveness, it also increases CPU overhead and can reduce overall system efficiency due to more frequent context switches and greater resource contention. The choice of tick rate must balance precision against these costs.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,4,'2025-07-29_15-06-53','From the user’s perspective, the key difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking is control. In co-operative multitasking, threads run until they explicitly choose to give up the CPU by calling a function like osYield(). This means the user must make sure every thread yields control regularly, or else other threads may not get a chance to run.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically forces a context switch at regular intervals (e.g., using a timer interrupt), so each thread gets a fair share of CPU time. The user doesn’t have to worry about calling yield functions, since the OS handles it in the background.\nFrom the OS side, co-operative multitasking is simpler. The OS only switches context when a thread calls a yield function, so it doesn\'t need to manage interrupts or track time slices. Context switching happens in a controlled, predictable way.\nPre-emptive multitasking is more complex for the OS. It needs to set up a timer interrupt (like SysTick), handle the interrupt to trigger a context switch, and ensure that switching happens safely, even if the thread is in the middle of something.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,4,'2025-07-29_18-30-02','From the user’s perspective, the key difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking is control. In co-operative multitasking, threads run until they explicitly choose to give up the CPU by calling a function like osYield(). This means the user must make sure every thread yields control regularly, or else other threads may not get a chance to run.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically forces a context switch at regular intervals (e.g., using a timer interrupt), so each thread gets a fair share of CPU time. The user doesn’t have to worry about calling yield functions, since the OS handles it in the background.\nFrom the OS side, co-operative multitasking is simpler. The OS only switches context when a thread calls a yield function, so it doesn\'t need to manage interrupts or track time slices. Context switching happens in a controlled, predictable way.\nPre-emptive multitasking is more complex for the OS. It needs to set up a timer interrupt (like SysTick), handle the interrupt to trigger a context switch, and ensure that switching happens safely, even if the thread is in the middle of something.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,4,'2025-07-30_13-36-54','From the user’s perspective, the key difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking is control. In co-operative multitasking, threads run until they explicitly choose to give up the CPU by calling a function like osYield(). This means the user must make sure every thread yields control regularly, or else other threads may not get a chance to run.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically forces a context switch at regular intervals (e.g., using a timer interrupt), so each thread gets a fair share of CPU time. The user doesn’t have to worry about calling yield functions, since the OS handles it in the background.\nFrom the OS side, co-operative multitasking is simpler. The OS only switches context when a thread calls a yield function, so it doesn\'t need to manage interrupts or track time slices. Context switching happens in a controlled, predictable way.\nPre-emptive multitasking is more complex for the OS. It needs to set up a timer interrupt (like SysTick), handle the interrupt to trigger a context switch, and ensure that switching happens safely, even if the thread is in the middle of something.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,4,'2025-07-30_14-08-35','From the user’s perspective, the key difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking is control. In co-operative multitasking, threads run until they explicitly choose to give up the CPU by calling a function like osYield(). This means the user must make sure every thread yields control regularly, or else other threads may not get a chance to run.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically forces a context switch at regular intervals (e.g., using a timer interrupt), so each thread gets a fair share of CPU time. The user doesn’t have to worry about calling yield functions, since the OS handles it in the background.\nFrom the OS side, co-operative multitasking is simpler. The OS only switches context when a thread calls a yield function, so it doesn\'t need to manage interrupts or track time slices. Context switching happens in a controlled, predictable way.\nPre-emptive multitasking is more complex for the OS. It needs to set up a timer interrupt (like SysTick), handle the interrupt to trigger a context switch, and ensure that switching happens safely, even if the thread is in the middle of something.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,4,'2025-07-30_21-41-48','From the user’s perspective, the key difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking is control. In co-operative multitasking, threads run until they explicitly choose to give up the CPU by calling a function like osYield(). This means the user must make sure every thread yields control regularly, or else other threads may not get a chance to run.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically forces a context switch at regular intervals (e.g., using a timer interrupt), so each thread gets a fair share of CPU time. The user doesn’t have to worry about calling yield functions, since the OS handles it in the background.\nFrom the OS side, co-operative multitasking is simpler. The OS only switches context when a thread calls a yield function, so it doesn\'t need to manage interrupts or track time slices. Context switching happens in a controlled, predictable way.\nPre-emptive multitasking is more complex for the OS. It needs to set up a timer interrupt (like SysTick), handle the interrupt to trigger a context switch, and ensure that switching happens safely, even if the thread is in the middle of something.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,5,'2025-07-29_15-06-53','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive switching can work together because they both trigger the same core mechanism for a context switch, just in different ways. In the lab, a thread can voluntarily call osYield() to give up the CPU, which sets the PendSV interrupt to trigger a switch. At the same time, a hardware timer like SysTick can interrupt a running thread at regular intervals, also setting PendSV to trigger a switch it if the thread’s time slice is over.\nBoth methods rely on the OS scheduling logic in osSched() to decide which thread runs next, and both ultimately lead to the same context-switching code in PendSV_Handler. The difference is just in who initiates the switch: the thread itself (co-operative) or the system timer (pre-emptive).\nBy combining the two, the system stays responsive. Pre-emption ensures no thread can hog the CPU, while yielding lets threads give up control early when they’re done or waiting on something. The OS handles both cases in a unified way, so they don’t conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,5,'2025-07-29_18-30-02','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive switching can work together because they both trigger the same core mechanism for a context switch, just in different ways. In the lab, a thread can voluntarily call osYield() to give up the CPU, which sets the PendSV interrupt to trigger a switch. At the same time, a hardware timer like SysTick can interrupt a running thread at regular intervals, also setting PendSV to trigger a switch it if the thread’s time slice is over.\nBoth methods rely on the OS scheduling logic in osSched() to decide which thread runs next, and both ultimately lead to the same context-switching code in PendSV_Handler. The difference is just in who initiates the switch: the thread itself (co-operative) or the system timer (pre-emptive).\nBy combining the two, the system stays responsive. Pre-emption ensures no thread can hog the CPU, while yielding lets threads give up control early when they’re done or waiting on something. The OS handles both cases in a unified way, so they don’t conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,5,'2025-07-30_13-36-54','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive switching can work together because they both trigger the same core mechanism for a context switch, just in different ways. In the lab, a thread can voluntarily call osYield() to give up the CPU, which sets the PendSV interrupt to trigger a switch. At the same time, a hardware timer like SysTick can interrupt a running thread at regular intervals, also setting PendSV to trigger a switch it if the thread’s time slice is over.\nBoth methods rely on the OS scheduling logic in osSched() to decide which thread runs next, and both ultimately lead to the same context-switching code in PendSV_Handler. The difference is just in who initiates the switch: the thread itself (co-operative) or the system timer (pre-emptive).\nBy combining the two, the system stays responsive. Pre-emption ensures no thread can hog the CPU, while yielding lets threads give up control early when they’re done or waiting on something. The OS handles both cases in a unified way, so they don’t conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,5,'2025-07-30_14-08-35','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive switching can work together because they both trigger the same core mechanism for a context switch, just in different ways. In the lab, a thread can voluntarily call osYield() to give up the CPU, which sets the PendSV interrupt to trigger a switch. At the same time, a hardware timer like SysTick can interrupt a running thread at regular intervals, also setting PendSV to trigger a switch it if the thread’s time slice is over.\nBoth methods rely on the OS scheduling logic in osSched() to decide which thread runs next, and both ultimately lead to the same context-switching code in PendSV_Handler. The difference is just in who initiates the switch: the thread itself (co-operative) or the system timer (pre-emptive).\nBy combining the two, the system stays responsive. Pre-emption ensures no thread can hog the CPU, while yielding lets threads give up control early when they’re done or waiting on something. The OS handles both cases in a unified way, so they don’t conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('mvasudev',5,5,'2025-07-30_21-41-48','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive switching can work together because they both trigger the same core mechanism for a context switch, just in different ways. In the lab, a thread can voluntarily call osYield() to give up the CPU, which sets the PendSV interrupt to trigger a switch. At the same time, a hardware timer like SysTick can interrupt a running thread at regular intervals, also setting PendSV to trigger a switch it if the thread’s time slice is over.\nBoth methods rely on the OS scheduling logic in osSched() to decide which thread runs next, and both ultimately lead to the same context-switching code in PendSV_Handler. The difference is just in who initiates the switch: the thread itself (co-operative) or the system timer (pre-emptive).\nBy combining the two, the system stays responsive. Pre-emption ensures no thread can hog the CPU, while yielding lets threads give up control early when they’re done or waiting on something. The OS handles both cases in a unified way, so they don’t conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,1,'2025-06-04_20-51-12','For a microcontroller the Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of files and libraries that is prewritten but the company who developed the microcontroller (in this case ST Microelectronics) for the NUCLEO-F401RE. The hardware abstraction layer contains all the files and libraries to setup the microcontroller so it can then run code. The hardware abstraction layer makes it easier to use the microcontroller right after opening it from the box because it has all the code necessary to initialize and setup the microcontroller on a lower-level interface, and someone can start programming right away in C or C++, without having to go through the long and difficult process of writing the equivalent code for the HAL themselves. That is why ST Microelectronics includes the prewritten HAL because it allows users to start development right away in the programming language they choose (C or C++) without having to worry about the setup of the microcontroller.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,1,'2025-06-04_21-33-11','For a microcontroller the Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of files and libraries that is prewritten but the company who developed the microcontroller (in this case ST Microelectronics) for the NUCLEO-F401RE. The hardware abstraction layer contains all the files and libraries to setup the microcontroller so it can then run code. The hardware abstraction layer makes it easier to use the microcontroller right after opening it from the box because it has all the code necessary to initialize and setup the microcontroller on a lower-level interface, and someone can start programming right away in C or C++, without having to go through the long and difficult process of writing the equivalent code for the HAL themselves. That is why ST Microelectronics includes the prewritten HAL because it allows users to start development right away in the programming language they choose (C or C++) without having to worry about the setup of the microcontroller.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,1,'2025-06-04_22-58-01','For a microcontroller 