-12','The __io_putchar() function takes one character as an argument and then writes it to an IO channel on the microcontroller (in this case the channel written to was UART2 on the microcontroller). The function also has a return type of int, and the function returns the same char that was feed in as a argument.\nThe reason why the __io_putchar function is needed is because printf calls the __io_putchar function, and without that printf would not work. The printf function code is contained in the <stdio.h> library, and that library requires using helper functions to allow it to interact with the hardware. So, the __io_putchar function was created as a helper function to allow printf to work. When printf is called with a string argument, the printf function repeatedly calls the __io_putchar function.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',1,2,'2025-06-04_21-33-11','The __io_putchar() function takes one character as an argument and then writes it to an IO channel on the microcontroller (in this case the channel written to was UART2 on the microcontroller). The function also has a return type of int, and the function returns the same char that was feed in as a argument.\nThe reason why the __io_putchar function is needed is because printf calls the __io_putchar function, and without that printf would not work. The printf function code is contained in the <stdio.h> library, and that library requires using helper functions to allow it to interact with the hardware. So, the __io_putchar function was created as a helper function to allow printf to work. When printf is called with a string argument, the printf function repeatedly calls the __io_putchar function. Additionally, the __io_putchar function allows the printf function to be adaptable to a wide variety of different microcontrollers. Each microcontroller has different hardware and a processor. For example, one microcontroller may use UART2, whereas another one may use UART3. So, to use the printf statement, the coder could simply add and modify certain parts of the helper function (__io_putchar) in this case, so they could get the printf function to work on the specific hardware and microcontroller that they are using for their project.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,2,'2025-06-04_22-58-01','The __io_putchar() function takes one character as an argument and then writes it to an IO channel on the microcontroller (in this case the channel written to was UART2 on the microcontroller). The function also has a return type of int, and the function returns the same char that was feed in as a argument.\nThe reason why the __io_putchar function is needed is because printf calls the __io_putchar function, and without that printf would not work. The printf function code is contained in the <stdio.h> library, and that library requires using helper functions to allow it to interact with the hardware. So, the __io_putchar function was created as a helper function to allow printf to work. When printf is called with a string argument, the printf function repeatedly calls the __io_putchar function. Additionally, the __io_putchar function allows the printf function to be adaptable to a wide variety of different microcontrollers. Each microcontroller has different hardware and a processor. For example, one microcontroller may use UART2, whereas another one may use UART3. So, to use the printf statement, the coder could simply add and modify certain parts of the helper function (__io_putchar) in this case, so they could get the printf function to work on the specific hardware and microcontroller that they are using for their project.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,2,'2025-06-05_00-08-20','The __io_putchar() function takes one character as an argument and then writes it to an IO channel on the microcontroller (in this case the channel written to was UART2 on the microcontroller). The function also has a return type of int, and the function returns the same char that was feed in as a argument.\nThe reason why the __io_putchar function is needed is because printf calls the __io_putchar function, and without that printf would not work. The printf function code is contained in the <stdio.h> library, and that library requires using helper functions to allow it to interact with the hardware. So, the __io_putchar function was created as a helper function to allow printf to work. When printf is called with a string argument, the printf function repeatedly calls the __io_putchar function. Additionally, the __io_putchar function allows the printf function to be adaptable to a wide variety of different microcontrollers. Each microcontroller has different hardware and a processor. For example, one microcontroller may use UART2, whereas another one may use UART3. So, to use the printf statement, the coder could simply add and modify certain parts of the helper function (__io_putchar) in this case, so they could get the printf function to work on the specific hardware and microcontroller that they are using for their project.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,3,'2025-06-04_20-51-12','The debugger was used in the lab, so the actual value of the stack could be seen while the program was running. In addition, the debugger allowed us to a breakpoint code in the code just before the main while loop, and that breakpoint paused the code execution at that location. That was important because pausing the code execution allowed for us to check the values on the stack (MSP_init_val, MSP, and PSP) and verify that the values stored in memory and on the registers were correct. Also, using the debug mode gave us access to the registers window in the IDE, and we could use that to verify the data stored in the registers.\nThe reason why we did not just use printf to test the code was because adding more printf statements to check the values held in variables would affect the stack itself and cause a discrepancy between the expected values and the values that would be printed to the console.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,3,'2025-06-04_21-33-11','The debugger was used in the lab, so the actual value of the stack could be seen while the program was running. In addition, the debugger allowed us to a breakpoint code in the code just before the main while loop, and that breakpoint paused the code execution at that location. That was important because pausing the code execution allowed for us to check the values on the stack (MSP_init_val, MSP, and PSP) and verify that the values stored in memory and on the registers were correct. Also, using the debug mode gave us access to the registers window in the IDE, and we could use that to verify the data stored in the registers.\nThe reason why we did not just use printf to test the code was because adding more printf statements to check the values held in variables would affect the stack itself and cause a discrepancy between the expected values and the values that would be printed to the console.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,3,'2025-06-04_22-58-01','The debugger was used in the lab, so the actual value of the stack could be seen while the program was running. In addition, the debugger allowed us to a breakpoint code in the code just before the main while loop, and that breakpoint paused the code execution at that location. That was important because pausing the code execution allowed for us to check the values on the stack (MSP_init_val, MSP, and PSP) and verify that the values stored in memory and on the registers were correct. Also, using the debug mode gave us access to the registers window in the IDE, and we could use that to verify the data stored in the registers.\nThe reason why we did not just use printf to test the code was because adding more printf statements to check the values held in variables would affect the stack itself and cause a discrepancy between the expected values and the values that would be printed to the console.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,3,'2025-06-05_00-08-20','The debugger was used in the lab, so the actual value of the stack could be seen while the program was running. In addition, the debugger allowed us to a breakpoint code in the code just before the main while loop, and that breakpoint paused the code execution at that location. That was important because pausing the code execution allowed for us to check the values on the stack (MSP_init_val, MSP, and PSP) and verify that the values stored in memory and on the registers were correct. Also, using the debug mode gave us access to the registers window in the IDE, and we could use that to verify the data stored in the registers.\nThe reason why we did not just use printf to test the code was because adding more printf statements to check the values held in variables would affect the stack itself and cause a discrepancy between the expected values and the values that would be printed to the console.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,4,'2025-06-04_20-51-12','The stack allocation method used in the lab is based on the stack growing downwards. In the code we obtained the value for MSP-init_val which is a pointer to the start of the stack in memory.  Then, to obtain the value for PSP, MSP-init_val was casted to an int, then 0x400 was subtracted for it, which gave the value of PSP. It was required to know the initial value for MSP, because each new place in the stack is based on the starting location (which is held in MSP), and without knowing MSP, it would be impossible to traverse and add new locations in the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,4,'2025-06-04_21-33-11','The stack allocation method used in the lab is based on the stack growing downwards. In the code we obtained the value for MSP-init_val which is a pointer to the start of the stack in memory.  Then, to obtain the value for PSP, MSP-init_val was casted to an int, then 0x400 was subtracted for it, which gave the value of PSP. It was required to know the initial value for MSP, because each new place in the stack is based on the starting location (which is held in MSP), and without knowing MSP, it would be impossible to traverse and add new locations in the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,4,'2025-06-04_22-58-01','The stack allocation method used in the lab is based on the stack growing downwards. In the code we obtained the value for MSP-init_val which is a pointer to the start of the stack in memory.  Then, to obtain the value for PSP, MSP-init_val was casted to an int, then 0x400 was subtracted for it, which gave the value of PSP. It was required to know the initial value for MSP, because each new place in the stack is based on the starting location (which is held in MSP), and without knowing MSP, it would be impossible to traverse and add new locations in the stack.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',1,4,'2025-06-05_00-08-20','The stack allocation method used in the lab is based on the stack growing downwards. In the code we obtained the value for MSP-init_val which is a pointer to the start of the stack in memory.  Then, to obtain the value for PSP, MSP-init_val was casted to an int, then 0x400 was subtracted for it, which gave the value of PSP. It was required to know the initial value for MSP, because each new place in the stack is based on the starting location (which is held in MSP), and without knowing MSP, it would be impossible to traverse and add new locations in the stack.\nIt is important for PSP to be valid stack address in memory, because PSP is used for running threads, and it is important to maintain a valid memory address for PSP. To make PSP a valid address, it is calculated using MSP-init_val as a reference. In our lab, we obtain PSP using the formula PSP = MSP-init_val – offset, where offset was equal to 0x400. This allowed the value for PSP to be valid, and follow the proper memory alignment rules, and since the maximum stack size was not exceeded, PSP was in a valid memory region.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,5,'2025-06-04_20-51-12','To allocate multiple new stacks two items are required to be known before, the number of stacks to be created, and the size of a single stack (THREAD_STACK_SIZE). Then, then formula\nSTACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE, where j is the stack index.\ncan be used to calculate the address in memory of each stack.  Then, a loop could be used to create each new stack using the formula above, and in addition each memory address could be stored in an array or linked list, which allows for the memory address to be easily accessible for future use.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,5,'2025-06-04_21-33-11','To allocate multiple new stacks two items are required to be known before, the number of stacks to be created, and the size of a single stack (THREAD_STACK_SIZE). Then, then formula\nSTACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE, where j is the stack index.\ncan be used to calculate the address in memory of each stack.  Then, a loop could be used to create each new stack using the formula above, and in addition each memory address could be stored in an array or linked list, which allows for the memory address to be easily accessible for future use.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',1,5,'2025-06-04_22-58-01','To allocate multiple new stacks some items are required to be known before, which are the number of stacks to be created, and the size of a single stack (THREAD_STACK_SIZE), and the MAX_STACK_SIZE which is the maximum amount of memory the stack uses, and . Then, then formula\nSTACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE, where j is the stack index.\ncan be used to calculate the address in memory of each stack. Using this approach, we will take the stack and treat it like an array of integers. By subdividing it, we will then keep track of multiple stacks. There will be one for the main stack (to store OS related stuff) and several other thread stacks.\nUsing MSP_INIT_VAL as a starting reference, we can calculate the locations of the new thread stacks that have to be created, and in addition, we can also include error checking, to make sure the maximum stack size is not exceeded. It is important to have error checking, to make sure the maximum stack size is not exceeded, because that will allow the code to operate as predicted.\nThen, a loop could be used to create each new stack using the formula above and there would also be a condition to check that the maximum stack size is not exceeded. In addition, each memory address could be stored in an array or linked list, which allows for the memory address to be easily accessible for future use.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',1,5,'2025-06-05_00-08-20','To allocate multiple new stacks some items are required to be known before, which are the number of stacks to be created, and the size of a single stack (THREAD_STACK_SIZE), and the MAX_STACK_SIZE which is the maximum amount of memory the stack uses, and . Then, then formula\nSTACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE, where j is the stack index.\ncan be used to calculate the address in memory of each stack. Using this approach, we will take the stack and treat it like an array of integers. By subdividing it, we will then keep track of multiple stacks. There will be one for the main stack (to store OS related stuff) and several other thread stacks.\nUsing MSP_INIT_VAL as a starting reference, we can calculate the locations of the new thread stacks that have to be created, and in addition, we can also include error checking, to make sure the maximum stack size is not exceeded. It is important to have error checking, to make sure the maximum stack size is not exceeded, because that will allow the code to operate as predicted.\nThen, a loop could be used to create each new stack using the formula above and there would also be a condition to check that the maximum stack size is not exceeded. In addition, each memory address could be stored in an array or linked list, which allows for the memory address to be easily accessible for future use.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',2,1,'2025-06-26_21-20-06','In part one of the lab, we wrote code in assembly to manipulate the PC (program counter register). This was done so the PC would call the print continuously function.\nThe function of the PC register on the STM32 board is to tell the processor (CPU) what instructions it should preform next in execution. To do that, the PC register holds the memory address of the instruction to run next. Then, when the next clock cycle occurs, the memory address held in the PC register is decoded and then executed by the CPU, (which follows along with the CPU’s normal decoding cycle)\nThen, when the jumpAssmebly() function was called in main, the argument we passed to it was a pointer to the print continuously function (ie the memory address of the print continuously function).\nThe jumpAssembly() function, then in turn moved the data in r0 to PC. Since the value held in r0 was the memory address of the print continuously function, it’s address was moved directly into the PC register. Finally, at the next clock cycle, then CPU decoded the instructions and executed the print continuous function.\nThen, main goal of part one of the lab, was to show by directly manipulating the PC register, a programmer can manipulate/control, what the CPU does next in execution.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',2,1,'2025-06-26_23-11-08','In part one of the lab, we wrote code in assembly to manipulate the PC (program counter register). This was done so the PC would call the print continuously function.\nThe function of the PC register on the STM32 board is to tell the processor (CPU) what instructions it should preform next in execution. To do that, the PC register holds the memory address of the instruction to run next. Then, when the next clock cycle occurs, the memory address held in the PC register is decoded and then executed by the CPU, (which follows along with the CPU’s normal decoding cycle)\nThen, when the jumpAssmebly() function was called in main, the argument we passed to it was a pointer to the print continuously function (ie the memory address of the print continuously function).\nThe jumpAssembly() function, then in turn moved the data in r0 to PC. Since the value held in r0 was the memory address of the print continuously function, it’s address was moved directly into the PC register. Finally, at the next clock cycle, then CPU decoded the instructions and executed the print continuous function.\nThen, main goal of part one of the lab, was to show by directly manipulating the PC register, a programmer can manipulate/control, what the CPU does next in execution.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',2,2,'2025-06-26_21-20-06','A system calls is a request sent to the OS Kernel, in which a running user program tells the OS Kernel, that it needs the OS Kernel to preform a certain action (like handle an error, allow access to a certain part of the hardware, or provide some Kernel service).\nSystem calls are present on the majority of operating systems, and their main purpose is to give userspace programs the ability to request a Kernel service. The Kernel may either allow or deny the request, depending on what service is requested, and the current state of the operating system.\nIn code, a system calls is a function, usually accessed through an interrupt call. The system call function does not get compiled into user code, and instead the system call function is stored in a fixed memory place.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',2,2,'2025-06-26_23-11-08','A system calls is a request sent to the OS Kernel, in which a running user program tells the OS Kernel, that it needs the OS Kernel to preform a certain action (like handle an error, allow access to a certain part of the hardware, or provide some Kernel service).\nSystem calls are present on the majority of operating systems, and their main purpose is to give userspace programs the ability to request a Kernel service. The Kernel may either allow or deny the request, depending on what service is requested, and the current state of the operating system. The benefit of this method is that using system calls protect the computers hardware from direct access by user code (which may be potentially malicious). Since user code operates in User mode, user programs do not have direct access to memory or hardware. When, a user programs makes a system call, it is basically asking the OS to preform a specific operation, that the user program can’t do directly (for example creating or opening a file). The system call provides a controlled interface from user programs to the OS, which ensures security, by preventing direct hardware access by user programs.\nIn code, a system call is a function, usually accessed through an interrupt call. The system call function does not get compiled into user code, and instead the system call function is stored in a fixed memory place.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',2,3,'2025-06-26_21-20-06','For lab 2, we used a uint32_t* called stackptr as a pointer to the stack that would be used by the thread we were going to run. First, stackptr was set to the value of PSP_val. After the starting address of the stack was assigned, the next goal was to push values onto the stack for xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4.\nThe two lines of code below, pushed values on to the stack for xPSR and then the address of the print_continuously function.\n*(--stackptr) = 1<<24;\n*(--stackptr) = (uint32_t)print_continuously;\nThen, the line of code below was written 14 times, and it’s purpose was is to store the value 0XA (and during testing, we could check the rest of the register values while the thread was being run to see if they were being set to 0XA for debugging purposes).\n*(--stackptr) = 0xA; //An arbitrary number\nAlso, the part “*(--stackptr)” decrements, then dereferences stackptr, which allows the stack to grown downward as more values are pushed onto the stack. Finally, then stackptr ends up in the appropriate location that it needs to be, so that when we return from the interrupt, we can pop from the stack.\nWhat it means to “set up a thread’s stack” and why we need to store register values on the stack:\nThe meaning of “set up a thread’s stack”, is to create a stack that holds future register values that will be popped off the stack and put into the actual hardware registers of the CPU.\nTo make a thread run, we need to “trick” the CPU into thinking it is already executing the thread before it is actually run. This is done by creating a thread stack, and then we push on to the thread stack (the future) register values that would exist if the thread’s function was called. Then, when a system call is made to run the thread, it will then pop off values from the thread stack and put them into the actual hardware registers. After that is complete, the hardware registers will be set up to have all the correct values to enable the thread function to run on the CPU\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',2,3,'2025-06-26_23-11-08','For lab 2, we used a uint32_t* called stackptr as a pointer to the stack that would be used by the thread we were going to run. First, stackptr was set to the value of PSP_val. After the starting address of the stack was assigned, the next goal was to push values onto the stack for xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and then R11 through R4.\nThe two lines of code below, pushed values on to the stack for xPSR and then the address of the print_continuously function.\n*(--stackptr) = 1<<24;\n*(--stackptr) = (uint32_t)print_continuously;\nThen, the line of code below was written 14 times, and it’s purpose was is to store the value 0XA (and during testing, we could check the rest of the register values while the thread was being run to see if they were being set to 0XA for debugging purposes).\n*(--stackptr) = 0xA; //An arbitrary number\nAlso, the part “*(--stackptr)” decrements, then dereferences stackptr, which allows the stack to grown downward as more values are pushed onto the stack. Finally, then stackptr ends up in the appropriate location that it needs to be, so that when we return from the interrupt, we can pop from the stack.\nWhat it means to “set up a thread’s stack” and why we need to store register values on the stack:\nThe meaning of “set up a thread’s stack”, is to create a stack that holds future register values that will be popped off the stack and put into the actual hardware registers of the CPU.\nTo make a thread run, we need to “trick” the CPU into thinking it is already executing the thread before it is actually run. This is done by creating a thread stack, and then we push on to the thread stack (the future) register values that would exist if the thread’s function was called. Then, when a system call is made to run the thread, it will then pop off values from the thread stack and put them into the actual hardware registers. After that is complete, the hardware registers will be set up to have all the correct values to enable the thread function to run on the CPU\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',2,4,'2025-06-26_21-20-06','If PSP was not set using the __set_PSP function in the switch/case statements, the code would crash and result in a hard fault.\n__set_PSP is a CMSIS function that takes an integer argument and sets the value of the actual PSP to that argument. In our case, PSP was set to the value of stackptr. If PSP was not set, the code would crash, because PSP would have a null value. That would case problems when the runFirstThread() function is called, which executes the assembly code in “asmDump.s”. The code in “asmDump.s” stakes the values from PSP, pops them off the stack and loads them into the actual hardware registers. The big issues, is that if PSP is not assigned, then the code will attempt to pop values off the stack, when it does not have the right pointer to the top of the stack. Therefore, there would be an error trying to pop of values from the stack, and the code would crash and hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',2,4,'2025-06-26_23-11-08','If PSP was not set using the __set_PSP function in the switch/case statements, the code would crash and result in a hard fault.\n__set_PSP is a CMSIS function that takes an integer argument and sets the value of the actual PSP to that argument. In our case, PSP was set to the value of stackptr. If PSP was not set, the code would crash, because PSP would have a null/wrong value. That would cause problems when the runFirstThread() function is called, which executes the assembly code in “asmDump.s”. The code in “asmDump.s” takes the values from the thread’s stack, pops them off the stack and loads them into the actual hardware registers. The big issue is that if PSP is the wrong value, then the code will attempt to pop values off the stack, when it does not have the right pointer to the top of the stack. Therefore, incorrect memory locations would be accessed, potentially corrupting memory, and there would be an error trying to pop off values from the stack and load them into the registers. That would results in a crash and hard fault for the microcontroller.\nWhen running threads on a microcontroller, it is crucial that PSP is used and managed correctly. When running a thread, the OS needs to save the thread’s state and stack pointer and load the values into hardware registers. Using PSP gives a separate stack pointer for the thread and allows the OS to quickly switch to that threads stack. If PSP is not set correctly before running a thread, then processor will likely not be able to access the thread’s variables and stack, which leads to undefined behaviour and program execution, and likely a crash. This is because the processor will use/manipulate incorrect memory locations when running the thread instructions, likely leading to a crash.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',2,5,'2025-06-26_21-20-06','If the thread function printed once and returned, there would only be one console output for “Hello, PC!”. After the print statement, the CPU, would then execute the next instructions held in the PC register, and the code would continue as normal. However, for the lab, we never saved the state of the CPU registers before executing the thread, and as a result, we can not restore the hardware registers to the previous state before the thread executed, so that will result in the microcontroller pausing, and not being able to continue in code execution.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',2,5,'2025-06-26_23-11-08','If the thread function printed once and returned, it would be likely that the microcontroller code would crash and there would be a hard fault. First, when we pushed values onto the threads stack, we did not push a value for the return address, (so essentially, the code has no idea where to go after the thread execution is finished). Therefore, when the thread is finished execution, there would be no location specified as a return address, which would likely cause the system to crash and hard fault.\nReturn addresses in functions calls are important (especially when dealing with threads). The return address allows the program execution to continue right were it left off, just before the function call. This is important when running threads, because after we make a system call to run a thread, it is important to specify the return address, so the program knows where to continue, after the thread has finished.\nIn the code, we did not push a return address onto the threads stack, so after the thread is finished execution, there would be no return address to load into the PC register. As a result, the system would have unexpected behaviour, with a crash and hard fault being likely to occur.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',3,1,'2025-06-27_17-43-32','When there are multiple “*.c” files in a project, the compiler uses two steps (compilation, then linking) to make all files into a single executable. First, the compiler goes through each “.c” file and compiles them in to an object “.o” file. Then, then compiler takes all the object files and any libraries, and liks each of them together. Finally, the compiler takes all the object files and emrges them into a single executable file, like a .exe file.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',3,1,'2025-06-27_22-06-32','There are three important steps to compiling code written in c language, which are preprocessing, compiling, and linking. Once a programmer has finished making a project with multiple “.c” files, the first part of compilation is the preprocessing step. In the preprocessing step, the source code (code written by the programmer) is scanned by the compiler, and the compiler manages directives, like “#define” and “#include”, in addition the compiler will expand macros, and include header files. The overall goal in preprocessing step is to prepare the source code.\nIn the next stage called compiling, the compiler will take the user written code after it has finished with preprocessing, and it then converts it into assembly code. During this stage, it also checks for syntax errors in the c code. After the code has been converted into assembly code, it is then converted into machine code (which is just binary ones and zeros). After converting to machine code, this produces object files with a “.o” or “.obj” extension.\nThe final stage is called linking, where object files and libraries are combined into a single executable file. In this stage, any external references are resolved, and the final memory addresses are assigned to variables and functions. The linker plays an important role in this stage, and the role of the linker is to combine multiple object files and libraries into a single executable. After the linking process is done, we are left with a single executable file that can be run on system.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',3,2,'2025-06-27_17-43-32','Because it will include extra libraries, and only .h header files can be included\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',3,2,'2025-06-27_22-06-32','The scenario described for “including a .c file in another .c file” is not good programming practice.\nFirst, using an “# include” statement in that manner can lead to multiple function and/or variable definitions (within the same “.c” file). Basically, using include, is like copy and pasting the contents of one file withing another. This can cause a problem, because it may result in functions and/or variables being defined multiple times, which would cause compilation errors.\nA C language compiler can only handle one definition per symbol (function, variable, constant, etc). As a result, including a “.c” file within another “.c” file, may result in the compiler seeing multiple definitions of the same symbol (in code), which would cause a compilation error.\nThere is an important difference between “.c” and “.h” files. For “.c” files, they are code that is directly compliable (and has the actual implementation of variables and functions). In contrast, “.h” files are header files (that contain variable declarations and function prototype’s). Specifically, “.h” files are meant to be included in other “.c” files at the time of linking. However, including a “.c” files within another “.c” file does not follow that process, and doing that makes the code behaviour less predictable.\nIn addition, there would be a specific problem that would occur at the time of linking. Including a “.c” file within another “.c” file, may result in multiple definitions of the same symbol (function, variable, etc). This would specifically cause an error for the linker because the linker would see multiple definitions of the same symbol (and would cause a duplicate symbol error). Since, the role of the linker is to resolve differences in the code and then combine multiple object files into a single executable, when the linker finds multiple definitions for the same symbol, the linker will not be able to choose which definitions to use, and that will lead to an error in the linking process.\nIn summary, that is why it is not a good programming process to “include a .c file in another .c file”.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',3,3,'2025-06-27_17-43-32','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective:\n•	First, the user has to request the kernel service to allocate a new stack, (which can be used as the thread stack). If the kernel accepts the user request, the OS Kernel will return a pointer to the top of the newly created stacked. In our case, the user made a call to the stack allocator function to accomplish that step.\n•	Second, the user has to make a request for the Kernel to create a thread. In this step, the user must provide a function pointer, to the function that will be executed in the thread. The user should also provide any other necessary variables that will be used will running the stack. If the request is accepted the by OS Kernel, it will setup the thread stack to be executed latter on.\n•	Third, the user can then make a request to the Kernel to start the thread, for that.\n•	Third, the user should call the SVC Kernel function, to request that the kernel start the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',3,3,'2025-06-27_22-06-32','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective:\nFirst, the Kernel must be correctly initialized, before any threads can be run (basically the OS Kernel must setup main memory and MSP correctly).\nSecond, the user has to request the kernel service to allocate a new stack, (which can be used as the thread stack). If the kernel accepts the user request, the OS Kernel will return a pointer to the top of the newly created stacked. In our case, the user made a call to the stack allocator function to accomplish that step.\nThird, the user has to make a request for the Kernel to create a thread. In this step, the user must provide a function pointer, to the function that will be executed in the thread. It is important to note that the user is responsible for writing the function to be run on the thread, and the user must provide a pointer to the address of that function to the Kernel at this stage. The user should also provide any other necessary variables that will be used will running the thread stack. If the request is accepted the by OS Kernel, it will setup the thread stack to be executed latter on in the thread.\nFourth, the user can then make a request to the Kernel to start the thread. If the OS Kernel accepts the request, the thread will start its execution.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',3,4,'2025-06-27_17-43-32','The stack pool is a space in the RAM, where the Knerel can allocate memory. Specifically, the kernel can create multiple individuals stacks, that can be used for threads.\nThe block of memory in contiguous, and it has a maximum specified sized (determined in a setup file for the microcontroller).\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',3,4,'2025-06-27_22-06-32','The stack pool is a space in the RAM, where the OS Kernel can allocate memory. Specifically, the OS Kernel can create the main stack (used by the OS for critical system operations), and it can create multiple individual stacks, that can be used for thread stacks. The stack pool is basically a block of contiguous memory, and it has a maximum specified size (determine the the programmer in a setup file for the microcontroller). Typically, MSP will point to the start of the stack pool, and MSP is used by the OS Kernel for critical OS tasks. When we have to run multiple threads, we can split the stack pool into smaller blocks, and those blocks can be used as individual thread stacks for all the threads we want to run. We can then us PSP to point to the individual thread stack when a thread is running. In summary, that is how the stack pool works.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',3,5,'2025-06-27_17-43-32','In user files, all individual functions created by the user, and and user code is stored in that file. In lab 3, the user code was held in “main.c”.\nIn contrast, Kernel files contain all the code used by the OS kernel. Since the OS Kernel has\nThe separation between “user” and “kernel” files/code is important in making a fully functional os.  User code has restricted access, because we want to protect the system hardware and memory from any malicious user code. In contrast, the OS Kernel has full access to manipulate system hardware and memory. So, we want user code to interface with the OS Kernel code.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',3,5,'2025-06-27_22-06-32','In user files, all individual functions created by the user, and and user code is stored in those files. In lab 3, the user code was held in “main.c” and “main.h”. In contrast, Kernel files contain all the code used by the OS kernel. Since the OS Kernel has full access to the system hardware and memory, that access must be managed carefully, to ensure the system operates reliably, and memory integrity is maintained.\nThe separation between “user” and “kernel” files/code is important in making a fully functional OS. User code has restricted access, because we want to protect the system hardware and memory from any (potentially malicious) user code. In contrast, the OS Kernel has full access to manipulate system hardware and memory. The interface between user code and the OS Kernel plays a key role in how the operating system works.\nUser code uses system calls, and the main purpose of systems calls is to give user programs the ability to request a Kernel service (similar to an API). The Kernel may either allow or deny the request, depending on what service is requested, and the current state of the operating system. The benefit of this method is that using system calls protect the computers hardware from direct access by user code (which may be potentially malicious). Since user code operates in User mode, user programs do not have direct access to memory or hardware. When, a user programs makes a system call, it is basically asking the OS to preform a specific operation, that the user program can’t do directly (for example creating or opening a file). The system call provides a controlled interface from user programs to the OS Kernel, which ensures security, by preventing direct hardware access by user programs.\nIn addition, separating user code and OS Kernel code simplifies the tasks of writing user code. Separating user code provides a level of abstraction and makes the writing user code easier. This is because hardware complexity is abstracted away from user code, and dealing with the hardware is left to the OS Kernel. This benefits the user, because writing user code is then easier (since the user does not have to deal with the system hardware and only has to make requests to the OS Kernel).\nIn addition, this way of separating user and OS Kernel code, allows users to focus directly on the application logic. For example, if a user program wants to make a thread, the user program just has to write the thread function. Then, the user code, and make a request to the OS kernel to allocate a new thread stack space, then the user code can pass the function to run for the thread. Finally, the user code can the request the OS Kernel to start the thread, and if the request is accepted, the OS Kernel with start the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,1,'2025-07-31_03-48-33','It would be simple to implement round robin scheduling using a queue. To do that, each new processor on arrival would be enqueued into the queue. As a result, the process that arrives first will be at the front of the queue and the process that arrives the last will be at the back of the queue. Then, the OS Kernel could assign a time slice (also called time quantum) that each process will use. The process at the start of the queue will be run first. If the first process finishes within the time quantum, then it will be removed from the queue, and the next process (second in line) in the queue will run. However, if the first process in the queue does not finish within the time quantum, it will then be stopped and moved to the back of the queue, while the second process in the quantum then gets to run and use the CPU. This cycle will keep on occurring, and it allows each process to get an equal share of run time and usage with the processor.\nThere are some benefits to using specifically a circular queue over an array for round robin scheduling. First, circular queues have dynamically allocated memory space. The dynamic size for a circular queue is more efficient to use because it allows the circular queue to grow or shrink to fit the amount of thread control blocks the circular queue data structure needs to hold. In contrast, arrays are statically allocated so this requires declaring more memory at the start, In addition, dynamically resizing arrays during program execution (by making a new array, copying all the element over to the new array, and then deleting the original array) is inefficient and uses more memory. As a result, a circular queue is better than an array when it comes to memory management and memory overhead.\nIn addition, a circular queue offers better performance in terms of enqueue/dequeue operations, then shifting elements in an array. When, a new thread has a request to run a process, the thread control block can be added to a circular queue in O(1) operations. This is also the same when a thread has finished executing, and the thread control block (for the thread that is finished executing), can be dequeued (Removed) in O(1) operations. However, this is not the case for an array because in the worst-case scenario when a thread is finished executing, removing the thread control block from the array and then shifting the data held in other array indices would in the worst case scenario be O(n) operations. As a result, a circular queue has a better run time performance when adding or removing thread controls blocks (from the thread metadata data structure) compared to an array.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,1,'2025-07-31_04-34-43','It would be simple to implement round robin scheduling using a queue. To do that, each new processor on arrival would be enqueued into the queue. As a result, the process that arrives first will be at the front of the queue and the process that arrives the last will be at the back of the queue. Then, the OS Kernel could assign a time slice (also called time quantum) that each process will use. The process at the start of the queue will be run first. If the first process finishes within the time quantum, then it will be removed from the queue, and the next process (second in line) in the queue will run. However, if the first process in the queue does not finish within the time quantum, it will then be stopped and moved to the back of the queue, while the second process in the quantum then gets to run and use the CPU. This cycle will keep on occurring, and it allows each process to get an equal share of run time and usage with the processor.\nThere are some benefits to using specifically a circular queue over an array for round robin scheduling. First, circular queues have dynamically allocated memory space. The dynamic size for a circular queue is more efficient to use because it allows the circular queue to grow or shrink to fit the amount of thread control blocks the circular queue data structure needs to hold. In contrast, arrays are statically allocated so this requires declaring more memory at the start, In addition, dynamically resizing arrays during program execution (by making a new array, copying all the element over to the new array, and then deleting the original array) is inefficient and uses more memory. As a result, a circular queue is better than an array when it comes to memory management and memory overhead.\nIn addition, a circular queue offers better performance in terms of enqueue/dequeue operations, then shifting elements in an array. When, a new thread has a request to run a process, the thread control block can be added to a circular queue in O(1) operations. This is also the same when a thread has finished executing, and the thread control block (for the thread that is finished executing), can be dequeued (Removed) in O(1) operations. However, this is not the case for an array because in the worst-case scenario when a thread is finished executing, removing the thread control block from the array and then shifting the data held in other array indices would in the worst-case scenario be O(n) operations. As a result, a circular queue has a better run time performance when adding or removing thread controls blocks (from the thread metadata data structure) compared to an array.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',4,1,'2025-07-31_05-10-45','It would be simple to implement round robin scheduling using a queue. To do that, each new processor on arrival would be enqueued into the queue. As a result, the process that arrives first will be at the front of the queue and the process that arrives the last will be at the back of the queue. Then, the OS Kernel could assign a time slice (also called time quantum) that each process will use. The process at the start of the queue will be run first. If the first process finishes within the time quantum, then it will be removed from the queue, and the next process (second in line) in the queue will run. However, if the first process in the queue does not finish within the time quantum, it will then be stopped and moved to the back of the queue, while the second process in the quantum then gets to run and use the CPU. This cycle will keep on occurring, and it allows each process to get an equal share of run time and usage with the processor.\nFirstly, a round robin scheduling algorithm is very similar to a queue data structure. Both uses a first in first out (FIFO) process, where the first arrived task gets access to the CPU resources first.\nThere are some benefits to using specifically a circular queue over an array for round robin scheduling. First, circular queues have dynamically allocated memory space. The dynamic size for a circular queue is more efficient to use because it allows the circular queue to grow or shrink to fit the amount of thread control blocks the circular queue data structure needs to hold. In contrast, arrays are statically allocated so this requires declaring more memory at the start, In addition, dynamically resizing arrays during program execution (by making a new array, copying all the element over to the new array, and then deleting the original array) is inefficient and uses more memory. As a result, a circular queue is better than an array when it comes to memory management and memory overhead.\nIn addition, a circular queue offers better performance in terms of enqueue/dequeue operations, then shifting elements in an array. When, a new thread has a request to run a process, the thread control block can be added to a circular queue in O(1) operations. This is also the same when a thread has finished executing, and the thread control block (for the thread that is finished executing), can be dequeued (Removed) in O(1) operations. However, this is not the case for an array because in the worst-case scenario when a thread is finished executing, removing the thread control block from the array and then shifting the data held in other array indices would in the worst-case scenario be O(n) operations. As a result, a circular queue has a better run time performance when adding or removing thread controls blocks (from the thread metadata data structure) compared to an array.\nIt is important to note that the current implementation of round robin scheduling in this lab (using an array) then incrementing the index and using the modulo (%) with the total amount of threads, is very much like using a queue. As a result, for this lab, there would be no real benefit gained by switching to using a queue, and switching to a queue would require more work and overhead, with no real improvement in code efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,1,'2025-07-31_18-03-22','It would be simple to implement round robin scheduling using a queue. To do that, each new processor on arrival would be enqueued into the queue. As a result, the process that arrives first will be at the front of the queue and the process that arrives the last will be at the back of the queue. Then, the OS Kernel could assign a time slice (also called time quantum) that each process will use. The process at the start of the queue will be run first. If the first process finishes within the time quantum, then it will be removed from the queue, and the next process (second in line) in the queue will run. However, if the first process in the queue does not finish within the time quantum, it will then be stopped and moved to the back of the queue, while the second process in the quantum then gets to run and use the CPU. This cycle will keep on occurring, and it allows each process to get an equal share of run time and usage with the processor.\nFirstly, a round robin scheduling algorithm is very similar to a queue data structure. Both uses a first in first out (FIFO) process, where the first arrived task gets access to the CPU resources first.\nThere are some benefits to using specifically a circular queue over an array for round robin scheduling. First, circular queues have dynamically allocated memory space. The dynamic size for a circular queue is more efficient to use because it allows the circular queue to grow or shrink to fit the amount of thread control blocks the circular queue data structure needs to hold. In contrast, arrays are statically allocated so this requires declaring more memory at the start, In addition, dynamically resizing arrays during program execution (by making a new array, copying all the element over to the new array, and then deleting the original array) is inefficient and uses more memory. As a result, a circular queue is better than an array when it comes to memory management and memory overhead.\nIn addition, a circular queue offers better performance in terms of enqueue/dequeue operations, then shifting elements in an array. When, a new thread has a request to run a process, the thread control block can be added to a circular queue in O(1) operations. This is also the same when a thread has finished executing, and the thread control block (for the thread that is finished executing), can be dequeued (Removed) in O(1) operations. However, this is not the case for an array because in the worst-case scenario when a thread is finished executing, removing the thread control block from the array and then shifting the data held in other array indices would in the worst-case scenario be O(n) operations. As a result, a circular queue has a better run time performance when adding or removing thread controls blocks (from the thread metadata data structure) compared to an array.\nIt is important to note that the current implementation of round robin scheduling in this lab (using an array) then incrementing the index and using the modulo (%) with the total amount of threads, is very much like using a queue. As a result, for this lab, there would be no real benefit gained by switching to using a queue, and switching to a queue would require more work and overhead, with no real improvement in code efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,1,'2025-07-31_18-24-48','It would be simple to implement round robin scheduling using a queue. To do that, each new processor on arrival would be enqueued into the queue. As a result, the process that arrives first will be at the front of the queue and the process that arrives the last will be at the back of the queue. Then, the OS Kernel could assign a time slice (also called time quantum) that each process will use. The process at the start of the queue will be run first. If the first process finishes within the time quantum, then it will be removed from the queue, and the next process (second in line) in the queue will run. However, if the first process in the queue does not finish within the time quantum, it will then be stopped and moved to the back of the queue, while the second process in the quantum then gets to run and use the CPU. This cycle will keep on occurring, and it allows each process to get an equal share of run time and usage with the processor.\nFirstly, a round robin scheduling algorithm is very similar to a queue data structure. Both uses a first in first out (FIFO) process, where the first arrived task gets access to the CPU resources first.\nThere are some benefits to using specifically a circular queue over an array for round robin scheduling. First, circular queues have dynamically allocated memory space. The dynamic size for a circular queue is more efficient to use because it allows the circular queue to grow or shrink to fit the amount of thread control blocks the circular queue data structure needs to hold. In contrast, arrays are statically allocated so this requires declaring more memory at the start, In addition, dynamically resizing arrays during program execution (by making a new array, copying all the element over to the new array, and then deleting the original array) is inefficient and uses more memory. As a result, a circular queue is better than an array when it comes to memory management and memory overhead.\nIn addition, a circular queue offers better performance in terms of enqueue/dequeue operations, then shifting elements in an array. When, a new thread has a request to run a process, the thread control block can be added to a circular queue in O(1) operations. This is also the same when a thread has finished executing, and the thread control block (for the thread that is finished executing), can be dequeued (Removed) in O(1) operations. However, this is not the case for an array because in the worst-case scenario when a thread is finished executing, removing the thread control block from the array and then shifting the data held in other array indices would in the worst-case scenario be O(n) operations. As a result, a circular queue has a better run time performance when adding or removing thread controls blocks (from the thread metadata data structure) compared to an array.\nIt is important to note that the current implementation of round robin scheduling in this lab (using an array) then incrementing the index and using the modulo (%) with the total amount of threads, is very much like using a queue. As a result, for this lab, there would be no real benefit gained by switching to using a queue, and switching to a queue would require more work and overhead, with no real improvement in code efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,2,'2025-07-31_03-48-33','The PendSV_Handler function has two main purposes:\nFirst, the PendSV_Handler must allow SVC_Handler_Main to finish it’s current tasks and restore values on the stack. Second, the PendSV_Handler then performs the context switch in an interrupt.\nThe overall steps that the PendSV_Handler function takes to makes a context switch is that it first saves the current tasks register values to the respective thread stack (i.e., form the thread context for this first thread). Then, updates PSP and makes a call to the osSched() function and receives the function pointer for the next thread to run. Lastly, the PendSV_Handler function restores the register values from that thread (which the OS Kernel scheduler had decided to run).\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',4,2,'2025-07-31_04-34-43','The PendSV_Handler function has two main purposes:\nFirst, the PendSV_Handler must allow SVC_Handler_Main to finish its current tasks and restore values on the stack. Second, the PendSV_Handler then performs the context switch in an interrupt.\nThe overall steps that the PendSV_Handler function takes to makes a context switch is that it first saves the current tasks register values to the respective thread stack (i.e., form the thread context for this first thread). Then, the function makes a call to the osSched() function and receives the stack pointed for the next thread to run and stores the value in R0. Lastly, the PendSV_Handler function restores the register values for the new task by popping of the values from the thread stack and putting them on the registers, which allows the scheduled task to be run.\nAt the first step, the hardware automatically takes the values from R0 – R3 and pushes them onto the current thread’s stack. Then PendSV_Handler function takes the current values saved on the remaining general-purpose registers R4 – R11 and pushes those onto the current threads stack. After this is done, all register values for the current executing task have been correctly saved and pushed onto the thread stack.\nThen nest step involves updating the stack pointer (PSP), and the OS Kernel scheduling function is called which updates the PSP value. Then, the PendSV_Handler function takes the thread stack address held in PSP and moves it to R0. Then, then PendSV_Handler function sets up the registers by taking the context stored in the tread stack, popping it off the stack, and putting the values on R4 – R11, at which point the context switch is complete and the new thread can be run by the CPU.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',4,2,'2025-07-31_05-10-45','The PendSV_Handler function has two main purposes:\nFirst, the PendSV_Handler must allow SVC_Handler_Main to finish its current tasks and restore values on the stack. Second, the PendSV_Handler then performs the context switch in an interrupt.\nThe overall steps that the PendSV_Handler function takes to makes a context switch is that it first saves the current tasks register values to the respective thread stack (i.e., form the thread context for this first thread). Then it reads the PSP value to full saved the values on the thread stack. Then, the function makes a call to the osSched() function and receives the stack pointed for the next thread to run and reads it and stores the value in R0. Lastly, the PendSV_Handler function restores the register values for the new task by popping of the values from the thread stack and putting them on the registers, which allows the scheduled task to be run.\nAt the first step, the hardware automatically takes the values from R0 – R3 and pushes them onto the current thread’s stack. Then PendSV_Handler function takes the current values saved on the remaining general-purpose registers R4 – R11 and pushes those onto the current threads stack. Then, the PSP value is read again, so that the data values are fully saved on the thread stack in memory. After this is done, all register values for the current executing task have been correctly saved and pushed onto the thread stack.\nThen nest step involves getting the new (PSP), and the OS Kernel scheduling function is called which gets a new PSP value (corresponding to the thread stack for the next task chosen). Then, the PendSV_Handler function takes the thread stack address held in PSP reads it and moves it to R0. Then, then PendSV_Handler function sets up the registers by taking the context stored in the tread stack, popping it off the stack, and putting the values on R4 – R11, at which point the context switch is complete and the new thread can be run by the CPU.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',4,2,'2025-07-31_18-03-22','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch in the steps listed below:\nFirst, the line (MRS R0, PSP) is executed, which copies the value of PSP into the general-purpose register (R0). Then the line (STMDB R0!,{R4-R11}) is executed which stores the values held in R4 to R11 to the memory address pointed to be R0 (which is the PSP). These two steps above serve the function of reading the value of PSP, and then it saves the current values in R4 to R11 to the thread stack pointed to by PSP.\nThen, the line (BL osSched) is run which calls the osSched() function in c, and the osSched() function determines the new task to run (using round robin scheduling) and then sets the new value of PSP to the stack pointer (of the function scheduled by the round robin scheduling algorithm).\nThen, the line (MRS R0, PSP) is executed, which copies the newly updated value of PSP into the general-purpose register (R0). Next, the line (MOV LR, #0xFFFFFFFD) is executed, which stores the value (#0xFFFFFFFD) in the link register, and that will be the return address, after the current thread is finished.\nThen, the line (LDMIA R0!,{R4-R11}) is executed which takes the values from the current thread stack, pops them off, and puts them on the registers R4 to R11. This line also updates the values held in R0 and increments it by (4*8 = 32). Next the line (MSR PSP, R0) is executed, which sets the value of PSP to the value held in R0. At this point, then context for the new thread has been loaded onto the registers, and this task can execute now. After the task is finished, the line (BX LR) is executed which goes to the return address held in the load register (LR), and this allows the current task to end and go back to the specified return address.\nThe steps outlined above show how the PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,2,'2025-07-31_18-24-48','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch in the steps listed below:\nFirst, the line (MRS R0, PSP) is executed, which copies the value of PSP into the general-purpose register (R0). Then the line (STMDB R0!,{R4-R11}) is executed which stores the values held in R4 to R11 to the memory address pointed to be R0 (which is the PSP). These two steps above serve the function of reading the value of PSP, and then it saves the current values in R4 to R11 to the thread stack pointed to by PSP.\nThen, the line (BL osSched) is run which calls the osSched() function in c, and the osSched() function determines the new task to run (using round robin scheduling) and then sets the new value of PSP to the stack pointer (of the function scheduled by the round robin scheduling algorithm).\nThen, the line (MRS R0, PSP) is executed, which copies the newly updated value of PSP into the general-purpose register (R0). Next, the line (MOV LR, #0xFFFFFFFD) is executed, which stores the value (#0xFFFFFFFD) in the link register, and that will be the return address, after the current thread is finished.\nThen, the line (LDMIA R0!,{R4-R11}) is executed which takes the values from the current thread stack, pops them off, and puts them on the registers R4 to R11. This line also updates the values held in R0 and increments it by (4*8 = 32). Next the line (MSR PSP, R0) is executed, which sets the value of PSP to the value held in R0. At this point, then context for the new thread has been loaded onto the registers, and this task can execute now. After the task is finished, the line (BX LR) is executed which goes to the return address held in the load register (LR), and this allows the current task to end and go back to the specified return address.\nThe steps outlined above show how the PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,3,'2025-07-31_03-48-33','This is a bad idea because it could possibly result in lower priority tasks being starved, while higher priority tasks consume all the CPU resources. For example, there could be multiple low priority tasks that have a small competition time and could be completed in 2 time quantum’s. In addition, there is one highest priority tasks, but it takes a very long time to complete. Based on the scheduling scenario in the question, the highest priority tasks would consume all cpu resources for a long time and leave the other small tasks starved, which is not desirable. In contrast, a round robin scheduling algorithm would be better for this, because it would all all the smaller tasks to finish quickly, while the CPU resources are equally shared among the current tasks, and would not cause any starvation in that case.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,3,'2025-07-31_04-34-43','This is a bad idea because it could possibly result in lower priority tasks being starved, while higher priority tasks consume all the CPU resources. For example, there could be multiple low priority tasks that have a small competition time and could be completed in 2 time quantum’s. In addition, there is one highest priority tasks, but it takes a very long time to complete. Based on the scheduling scenario in the question, the highest priority tasks would consume all CPU resources for a long time and leave the other small tasks starved, which is not desirable. In contrast, a round robin scheduling algorithm would be better for this, because it would all the smaller tasks to finish quickly, while the CPU resources are equally shared among the current tasks, and would not cause any starvation in that case.\nA second issue that could occur in the scenario described is priority inversion. Priority inversion is when a higher priority task needs a resource that is currently being held by a lower priority task. In the scheduling algorithm described in the question, is would be possibly for the lower priority tasks to be pre-empted and then suffer from starvation. As a result, the lower priority tasks would not finish executing and therefore would maintain usage of the resources. In turn, this would cause the higher priority tasks to not run, and it would be blocked for a long time (until the lower priority tasks finishes and frees the resource) at which point the higher priority task could run.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,3,'2025-07-31_05-10-45','This is a bad idea because it could possibly result in lower priority tasks being starved, while higher priority tasks consume all the CPU resources. For example, there could be multiple low priority tasks that have a small competition time and could be completed in 2 time quantum’s. In addition, there is one highest priority tasks, but it takes a very long time to complete. Based on the scheduling scenario in the question, the highest priority tasks would consume all CPU resources for a long time and leave the other small tasks starved, which is not desirable. In contrast, a round robin scheduling algorithm would be better for this, because it would all the smaller tasks to finish quickly, while the CPU resources are equally shared among the current tasks, and would not cause any starvation in that case.\nA second issue that could occur in the scenario described is priority inversion. Priority inversion is when a higher priority task needs a resource that is currently being held by a lower priority task. In the scheduling algorithm described in the question, is would be possibly for the lower priority tasks to be pre-empted and then suffer from starvation. As a result, the lower priority tasks would not finish executing and therefore would maintain usage of the resources. In turn, this would cause the higher priority tasks to not run, and it would be blocked for a long time (until the lower priority tasks finishes and frees the resource) at which point the higher priority task could run.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,3,'2025-07-31_18-03-22','This is a bad idea because it could possibly result in lower priority tasks being starved, while higher priority tasks consume all the CPU resources. For example, there could be multiple low priority tasks that have a small competition time and could be completed in 2 time quantum’s. In addition, there is one highest priority tasks, but it takes a very long time to complete. Based on the scheduling scenario in the question, the highest priority tasks would consume all CPU resources for a long time and leave the other small tasks starved, which is not desirable. In contrast, a round robin scheduling algorithm would be better for this, because it would all the smaller tasks to finish quickly, while the CPU resources are equally shared among the current tasks, and would not cause any starvation in that case.\nA second issue that could occur in the scenario described is priority inversion. Priority inversion is when a higher priority task needs a resource that is currently being held by a lower priority task. In the scheduling algorithm described in the question, is would be possibly for the lower priority tasks to be pre-empted and then suffer from starvation. As a result, the lower priority tasks would not finish executing and therefore would maintain usage of the resources. In turn, this would cause the higher priority tasks to not run, and it would be blocked for a long time (until the lower priority tasks finishes and frees the resource) at which point the higher priority task could run.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',4,3,'2025-07-31_18-24-48','This is a bad idea because it could possibly result in lower priority tasks being starved, while higher priority tasks consume all the CPU resources. For example, there could be multiple low priority tasks that have a small competition time and could be completed in 2 time quantum’s. In addition, there is one highest priority tasks, but it takes a very long time to complete. Based on the scheduling scenario in the question, the highest priority tasks would consume all CPU resources for a long time and leave the other small tasks starved, which is not desirable. In contrast, a round robin scheduling algorithm would be better for this, because it would all the smaller tasks to finish quickly, while the CPU resources are equally shared among the current tasks, and would not cause any starvation in that case.\nIn the scenario described in the question, only the highest-priority task would ever run, which is not desirable, because it leads to starvation for lower priority tasks. The highest-priority task would take all the processor resources and never yields control, which causes other smaller and lower priority tasks to be starved of processing time. This could cause the system to appear unresponsive/frozen because other critical tasks for managing the OS are blocked.\nA second issue that could occur in the scenario described is priority inversion. Priority inversion is when a higher priority task needs a resource that is currently being held by a lower priority task. In the scheduling algorithm described in the question, is would be possibly for the lower priority tasks to be pre-empted and then suffer from starvation. As a result, the lower priority tasks would not finish executing and therefore would maintain usage of the resources. In turn, this would cause the higher priority tasks to not run, and it would be blocked for a long time (until the lower priority tasks finishes and frees the resource) at which point the higher priority task could run.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,4,'2025-07-31_03-48-33','The main benefit to writing some functions in c and some functions in assembly, is that it makes the code, more system agnostic and adaptable to other microcontrollers and stm 32 boards.\nIt is totally possible to write everything in assembly, however the downside to this is that the CPU on each microcontroller is a bit different, and the assembly code that each CPU support is a bit different. So, if we want to take our code and use it on others microcontrollers, there would be a large amount of work to modify the assembly code for each new microcontroller used. However, (the way we wrote the code in this lab), by writing assembly code for manipulating data saved in the registers and thread stacks, and using C for wrapper functions and OS Kernel functions is advantageous because it makes the code more reusable. First, the assembly code is only for manipulating the data saved in registers, which is the same for most stm32 microcontroller. Then, it is much easier to write the wrapper functions and OS Kernal functions in C, as the C code can be run on all systems. In addition, writing the code in c gives more abstraction, and is easier to interpret and debug, rather that writing all of the code in assembly which is a lower-level language.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,4,'2025-07-31_04-34-43','The main benefit to writing some functions in c and some functions in assembly, is that it makes the code, more system agnostic and adaptable to other microcontrollers and stm 32 boards.\nIt is totally possible to write everything in assembly, however the downside to this is that the CPU on each microcontroller is a bit different, and the assembly code that each CPU support is a bit different. So, if we want to take our code and use it on other microcontrollers, there would be a large amount of work to modify the assembly code for each new microcontroller used. However, (the way we wrote the code in this lab), by writing assembly code for manipulating data saved in the registers and thread stacks, and using C for wrapper functions and OS Kernel functions is advantageous because it makes the code more reusable. First, the assembly code is only for manipulating the data saved in registers, which is the same for most stm32 microcontroller. Then, it is much easier to write the wrapper functions and OS Kernal functions in C, as the C code can be run on all systems. In addition, writing the code in c gives more abstraction, and is easier to interpret and debug, rather that writing all of the code in assembly which is a lower-level language.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,4,'2025-07-31_05-10-45','The main benefit to writing some functions in c and some functions in assembly, is that it makes the code, more system agnostic and adaptable to other microcontrollers and stm 32 boards.\nIt is totally possible to write everything in assembly, however the downside to this is that the CPU on each microcontroller is a bit different, and the assembly code that each CPU support is a bit different. So, if we want to take our code and use it on other microcontrollers, there would be a large amount of work to modify the assembly code for each new microcontroller used. However, (the way we wrote the code in this lab), by writing assembly code for manipulating data saved in the registers and thread stacks, and using C for wrapper functions and OS Kernel functions is advantageous because it makes the code more reusable. First, the assembly code is only for manipulating the data saved in registers, which is the same for most stm32 microcontroller. Then, it is much easier to write the wrapper functions and OS Kernal functions in C, as the C code can be run on all systems. In addition, writing the code in c gives more abstraction, and is easier to interpret and debug, rather that writing all of the code in assembly which is a lower-level language.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,4,'2025-07-31_18-03-22','The main benefit to writing some functions in c and some functions in assembly, is that it makes the code, more system agnostic and adaptable to other microcontrollers and stm 32 boards.\nIt is totally possible to write everything in assembly, however the downside to this is that the CPU on each microcontroller is a bit different, and the assembly code that each CPU support is a bit different. So, if we want to take our code and use it on other microcontrollers, there would be a large amount of work to modify the assembly code for each new microcontroller used. However, (the way we wrote the code in this lab), by writing assembly code for manipulating data saved in the registers and thread stacks, and using C for wrapper functions and OS Kernel functions is advantageous because it makes the code more reusable. First, the assembly code is only for manipulating the data saved in registers, which is the same for most stm32 microcontroller. Then, it is much easier to write the wrapper functions and OS Kernal functions in C, as the C code can be run on all systems. In addition, writing the code in c gives more abstraction, and is easier to interpret and debug, rather that writing all of the code in assembly which is a lower-level language.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,4,'2025-07-31_18-24-48','The main benefit to writing some functions in c and some functions in assembly, is that it makes the code, more system agnostic and adaptable to other microcontrollers and stm 32 boards.\nIt is totally possible to write everything in assembly, however the downside to this is that the CPU on each microcontroller is a bit different, and the assembly code that each CPU support is a bit different. So, if we want to take our code and use it on other microcontrollers, there would be a large amount of work to modify the assembly code for each new microcontroller used. However, (the way we wrote the code in this lab), by writing assembly code for manipulating data saved in the registers and thread stacks, and using C for wrapper functions and OS Kernel functions is advantageous because it makes the code more reusable. First, the assembly code is only for manipulating the data saved in registers, which is the same for most stm32 microcontroller. Then, it is much easier to write the wrapper functions and OS Kernal functions in C, as the C code can be run on all systems. In addition, writing the code in c gives more abstraction, and is easier to interpret and debug, rather that writing all of the code in assembly which is a lower-level language.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,5,'2025-07-31_03-48-33','The answer to the question described is that yes, the OS will still work. Since there is only one thread created and stored in the threadArray[] data structure, it means that this process will be scheduled each time to run by the osSched() function.\nThe program flow would be that the single thread would execute first, and print, then it would call the osYield() function. Since, that tasks is the only task waiting it would be scheduled again by the round robin algorithm, as can be seen in the line of code below (where the starting value of current_Thread_Index is 0)\ncurrent_Thread_Index = (current_Thread_Index + 1)%total_Thread_Structs_Amount;\nAfter the line of code is executed, current_Thread_Index will still have the value of 0 (i.e., the value of current_Thread_Index remains unchanged). As a result, the same tasks will be scheduled to run again by the round robin algorithm, and therefore that task would receive all of the CPU resources.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,5,'2025-07-31_04-34-43','The answer to the question described is that yes, the OS will still work. Since there is only one thread created and stored in the threadArray[] data structure, it means that this process will be scheduled each time to run by the osSched() function.\nThe program flow would be that the single thread would execute first, and print, then it would call the osYield() function. Since, that tasks is the only task waiting it would be scheduled again by the round robin algorithm, as can be seen in the line of code below (where the starting value of current_Thread_Index is 0)\ncurrent_Thread_Index = (current_Thread_Index + 1)%total_Thread_Structs_Amount;\nAfter the line of code is executed, current_Thread_Index will still have the value of 0 (i.e., the value of current_Thread_Index remains unchanged). As a result, the same tasks will be scheduled to run again by the round robin algorithm, and therefore that task would receive all of the CPU resources.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,5,'2025-07-31_05-10-45','The answer to the question described is that yes, the OS will still work. Since there is only one thread created and stored in the threadArray[] data structure, it means that this process will be scheduled each time to run by the osSched() function.\nThe program flow would be that the single thread would execute first, and print, then it would call the osYield() function. Since, that tasks is the only task waiting it would be scheduled again by the round robin algorithm, as can be seen in the line of code below (where the starting value of current_Thread_Index is 0)\ncurrent_Thread_Index = (current_Thread_Index + 1)%total_Thread_Structs_Amount;\nAfter the line of code is executed, current_Thread_Index will still have the value of 0 (i.e., the value of current_Thread_Index remains unchanged). As a result, the same tasks will be scheduled to run again by the round robin algorithm, and therefore that task would receive all of the CPU resources.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,5,'2025-07-31_18-03-22','The answer to the question described is that yes, the OS will still work. Since there is only one thread created and stored in the threadArray[] data structure, it means that this process will be scheduled each time to run by the osSched() function.\nThe program flow would be that the single thread would execute first, and print, then it would call the osYield() function. Since, that tasks is the only task waiting it would be scheduled again by the round robin algorithm, as can be seen in the line of code below (where the starting value of current_Thread_Index is 0)\ncurrent_Thread_Index = (current_Thread_Index + 1)%total_Thread_Structs_Amount;\nAfter the line of code is executed, current_Thread_Index will still have the value of 0 (i.e., the value of current_Thread_Index remains unchanged). As a result, the same tasks will be scheduled to run again by the round robin algorithm, and therefore that task would receive all of the CPU resources.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',4,5,'2025-07-31_18-24-48','The answer to the question described is that yes, the OS will still work. Since there is only one thread created and stored in the threadArray[] data structure, it means that this process will be scheduled each time to run by the osSched() function.\nThe program flow would be that the single thread would execute first, and print, then it would call the osYield() function. Since, that tasks is the only task waiting it would be scheduled again by the round robin algorithm, as can be seen in the line of code below (where the starting value of current_Thread_Index is 0)\ncurrent_Thread_Index = (current_Thread_Index + 1)%total_Thread_Structs_Amount;\nAfter the line of code is executed, current_Thread_Index will still have the value of 0 (i.e., the value of current_Thread_Index remains unchanged). As a result, the same tasks will be scheduled to run again by the round robin algorithm, and therefore that task would receive all of the CPU resources.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,1,'2025-07-31_22-59-24','An argument is passed into a thread through a three-step process that involves the user and the operating system.\nFirst, the user must define their thread function to accept a single argument: a `void` pointer. This design choice is flexible, allowing users to pass simple data types like an `int` or wrap multiple arguments into a `struct’.  If a `struct` is used, the user must define it to contain all the necessary. Inside the thread function, the user will then cast the `void` pointer to the appropriate data type and dereference it to access the data.\nNext, the operating system\'s role is to facilitate this process by modifying the `osCreateThread` function. The OS fills the R0 register with a pointer to the user\'s data. This is possible because a `void` pointer can be cast to an integer and stored entirely within R0. The ABI (Application Binary Interface) dictates that function input arguments are passed via registers R0 through R3, so using R0 for the `void` pointer is consistent with this convention.\nThis approach works effectively because it provides a standardized way to pass arguments without the OS needing to predict the user\'s specific data needs. The single `void` pointer argument provides a flexible mechanism, allowing the user to pass either a single value or a pointer to a more complex data structure. When the thread begins execution, the data pointer is already in the R0 register, making it immediately available for the user\'s function to cast and use.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,1,'2025-07-31_23-21-07','An argument is passed into a thread through a three-step process that involves the user and the operating system.\nFirst, the user must define their thread function to accept a single argument: a `void` pointer. This design choice is flexible, allowing users to pass simple data types like an `int` or wrap multiple arguments into a `struct’.  If a `struct` is used, the user must define it to contain all the necessary. Inside the thread function, the user will then cast the `void` pointer to the appropriate data type and dereference it to access the data.\nNext, the operating system\'s role is to facilitate this process by modifying the `osCreateThread` function. The OS fills the R0 register with a pointer to the user\'s data. This is possible because a `void` pointer can be cast to an integer and stored entirely within R0. The ABI (Application Binary Interface) dictates that function input arguments are passed via registers R0 through R3, so using R0 for the `void` pointer is consistent with this convention.\nThis approach works effectively because it provides a standardized way to pass arguments without the OS needing to predict the user\'s specific data needs. The single `void` pointer argument provides a flexible mechanism, allowing the user to pass either a single value or a pointer to a more complex data structure. When the thread begins execution, the data pointer is already in the R0 register, making it immediately available for the user\'s function to cast and use.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',5,1,'2025-07-31_23-56-10','An argument is passed into a thread through a three-step process that involves the user and the operating system.\nFirst, the user must define their thread function to accept a single argument: a `void` pointer. This design choice is flexible, allowing users to pass simple data types like an `int` or wrap multiple arguments into a `struct’.  If a `struct` is used, the user must define it to contain all the necessary. In this step the used also has to allocate the memory for the struct (that will be passed to the function. This may include calling the malloc() function or statically declaring the struct and assigning values. Then, Inside the thread function, the user will then cast the `void` pointer to the appropriate data type and dereference it to access the data.\nNext, the operating system\'s role is to facilitate this process by modifying the `osCreateThread` function. The OS fills the R0 register with a pointer to the user\'s data. This is possible because a `void` pointer can be cast to an integer and stored entirely within R0. The ABI (Application Binary Interface) dictates that function input arguments are passed via registers R0 through R3, so using R0 for the `void` pointer is consistent with this convention. In this step, the OS uses the PSP values to set up R0 value.\nThis approach works effectively because it provides a standardized way to pass arguments without the OS needing to predict the user\'s specific data needs. The single `void` pointer argument provides a flexible mechanism, allowing the user to pass either a single value or a pointer to a more complex data structure. When the thread begins execution, the data pointer is already in the R0 register, making it immediately available for the user\'s function to cast and use.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,2,'2025-07-31_22-59-24','You cannot call osYield within any of the context-switching functions, such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, because it would lead to a recursive and problematic chain of context switches.\nosYield is designed to be called by a thread to voluntarily give up control of the CPU. When osYield is called, it triggers a context switch by setting a flag to activate the PendSV interrupt. If osYield were to be called from within one of the handlers that are already part of the context-switching mechanism, such as PendSV_Handler, it would initiate another context switch before the current one is complete. This creates a nested or recursive call to the context-switching logic. The system would attempt to save the state of a thread and then immediately try to save it again, leading to a corrupt and unpredictable state. The stack pointers would be incorrect, and the system would likely crash or enter an infinite loop of context switching, as it would be unable to properly restore the state of any thread. This is why a function like osYield, which initiates a context switch, must be called from within a thread\'s normal execution and not from within the low-level interrupt handlers (like (PendSV_Handler, and SVC_Handler) that are responsible for managing those switches.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,2,'2025-07-31_23-21-07','You cannot call osYield within any of the context-switching functions, such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, because it would lead to a recursive and problematic chain of context switches.\nosYield is designed to be called by a thread to voluntarily give up control of the CPU. When osYield is called, it triggers a context switch by setting a flag to activate the PendSV interrupt. If osYield were to be called from within one of the handlers that are already part of the context-switching mechanism, such as PendSV_Handler, it would initiate another context switch before the current one is complete. This creates a nested or recursive call to the context-switching logic. The system would attempt to save the state of a thread and then immediately try to save it again, leading to a corrupt and unpredictable state. The stack pointers would be incorrect, and the system would likely crash or enter an infinite loop of context switching, as it would be unable to properly restore the state of any thread. This is why a function like osYield, which initiates a context switch, must be called from within a thread\'s normal execution and not from within the low-level interrupt handlers (like (PendSV_Handler, and SVC_Handler) that are responsible for managing those switches.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',5,2,'2025-07-31_23-56-10','You cannot call osYield within any of the context-switching functions, such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, because it would lead to a recursive and chain of context switches (i.e., nested interrupts), which would cause the system to hard fault. The hard fault is due to stack corruption, and the recursive interrupt calls, that would occur.\nAt the code level, the osYield() function, is a wrapper function that then executes the line (__asm(\"SVC #4\")), which makes a call to the SVC interrupt function, and in that it goes to the yield case, which triggers the code in “asmDump.s” to run.\nosYield is designed to be called by a thread to voluntarily give up control of the CPU. When osYield is called, it triggers a context switch by setting a flag to activate the PendSV interrupt. If osYield were to be called from within one of the handlers that are already part of the context-switching mechanism, such as PendSV_Handler, it would initiate another context switch before the current one is complete. This creates a nested or recursive call to the context-switching logic. The system would attempt to save the state of a thread and then immediately try to save it again, leading to a corrupt and unpredictable state. The stack pointers would be incorrect, and the system would likely crash or enter an infinite loop of context switching, as it would be unable to properly restore the state of any thread. This is why a function like osYield, which initiates a context switch, must be called from within a thread\'s normal execution and not from within the low-level interrupt handlers (like (PendSV_Handler, and SVC_Handler) that are responsible for managing those switches.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,3,'2025-07-31_22-59-24','SysTick_Handler interrupt every one millisecond. This means that the OS\'s clock speed and resolution are both 1ms. If we wanted to change the fundamental OS clock to 0.1ms, we would need to reconfigure the SysTick timer.\nThere are both benefits and downsides to decreasing the OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms. The primary benefit would be an increase in the OS\'s responsiveness and resolution. A 0.1ms clock speed would allow the OS to respond to events up to ten times faster than with a 1ms clock. This higher resolution would be advantageous for applications requiring more precise timing, as the OS could respond to events and perform context switches with a much finer granularity. For example, pre-emptive multitasking could be more efficient, and deadlines for threads could be set and met with greater accuracy.\nHowever, there are significant downsides to increasing the clock speed. A 0.1ms clock would mean that the SysTick_Handler interrupt would trigger ten times more frequently than before. This would lead to a much higher interrupt overhead. Every time the interrupt triggers, the OS must save the current thread\'s state, run the handler\'s logic (such as decrementing a software timer), and then restore the thread\'s state. This constant context switching and interrupt processing would consume a larger portion of the CPU\'s time, leaving less processing power available for the actual user threads. The increased overhead could potentially degrade the overall performance of the system, making it less efficient despite its higher timing resolution.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',5,3,'2025-07-31_23-21-07','SysTick_Handler interrupt every one millisecond. This means that the OS\'s clock speed and resolution are both 1ms. If we wanted to change the fundamental OS clock to 0.1ms, we would need to reconfigure the SysTick timer.\nThere are both benefits and downsides to decreasing the OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms. The primary benefit would be an increase in the OS\'s responsiveness and resolution. A 0.1ms clock speed would allow the OS to respond to events up to ten times faster than with a 1ms clock. This higher resolution would be advantageous for applications requiring more precise timing, as the OS could respond to events and perform context switches with a much finer granularity. For example, pre-emptive multitasking could be more efficient, and deadlines for threads could be set and met with greater accuracy. In addition, from a user’s perspective, the 0.1ms OS clock would lead to quicker response times for user interactions and users tasks, which would make the OS be more responsive.\nA 0.1ms clock offers benefits beyond basic responsiveness and resolution, particularly in high-precision real-time systems. It allows for finer-grained control over timing-sensitive operations, enabling more accurate synchronization, reduced jitter in time-critical tasks, and potentially improved system stability in applications like robotics and financial trading. This level of precision can also be crucial in scenarios where even minor timing discrepancies can lead to significant errors, such as in scientific experiments or high-frequency data acquisition.\nAnother consideration is that a faster clock speed in a computer\'s processor (CPU) generally leads to increased power consumption and heat generation, but also potentially improves the system\'s ability to handle user tasks by executing instructions faster. The faster clock speed allows the CPU to process more instructions per second, leading to quicker task completion and potentially better responsiveness. However, this increased speed comes at the cost of higher power usage and more heat, which may require improved cooling solutions to maintain stable operation.\nHowever, there are significant downsides to increasing the clock speed. A 0.1ms clock would mean that the SysTick_Handler interrupt would trigger ten times more frequently than before. This would lead to a much higher interrupt overhead. Every time the interrupt triggers, the OS must save the current thread\'s state, run the handler\'s logic (such as decrementing a software timer), and then restore the thread\'s state. This constant context switching and interrupt processing would consume a larger portion of the CPU\'s time, leaving less processing power available for the actual user threads. The increased overhead could potentially degrade the overall performance of the system, making it less efficient despite its higher timing resolution.\n',0,'s25'),('nruprai',5,3,'2025-07-31_23-56-10','There are both benefits and downsides to decreasing the OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms. The primary benefit would be an increase in the OS\'s responsiveness and resolution. A 0.1ms clock speed would allow the OS to respond to events up to ten times faster than with a 1ms clock. This higher resolution would be advantageous for applications requiring more precise timing, as the OS could respond to events and perform context switches with a much finer granularity. For example, pre-emptive multitasking could be more efficient, and deadlines for threads could be set and met with greater accuracy. In addition, from a user’s perspective, the 0.1ms OS clock would lead to quicker response times for user interactions and users tasks, which would make the OS be more responsive.\nA 0.1ms clock offers benefits beyond basic responsiveness and resolution, particularly in high-precision real-time systems. It allows for finer-grained control over timing-sensitive operations, enabling more accurate synchronization, reduced jitter in time-critical tasks, and potentially improved system stability in applications like robotics and financial trading. This level of precision can also be crucial in scenarios where even minor timing discrepancies can lead to significant errors, such as in scientific experiments or high-frequency data acquisition.\nHigher clock speeds mean the CPU consumes more power and generates more heat. This can be a significant concern in battery-powered devices and systems where thermal management is critical.\nWhile faster clock speeds can improve overall performance, the RTOS itself still has overhead. If the RTOS services are invoked frequently, a faster clock speed might not translate to a proportional increase in performance for the application due to increased overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,4,'2025-07-31_22-59-24','From a user\'s perspective, the primary difference lies in control. In co-operative multitasking, a thread must voluntarily give up control of the CPU by calling a function like osYield. The user, therefore, has complete control over when a context switch occurs. This requires the user to be mindful of thread execution times and to strategically place osYield calls to prevent any single thread from monopolizing the CPU. In contrast, with pre-emptive multitasking, the user does not need to explicitly yield. The OS will automatically switch threads based on a timed schedule, even if a thread does not yield. While a user can still choose to yield, the OS will handle the switching if the thread\'s allocated time slice expires.\nFrom the OS\'s perspective, the difference is in the triggering mechanism for a context switch. In co-operative multitasking, the OS simply waits for a thread to call osYield, which then triggers a context switch using the PendSV interrupt. The OS\'s role is reactive; it only performs a switch when a thread requests it.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS takes an active role in managing thread execution. It uses a hardware timer, the SysTick timer, to trigger an interrupt called SysTick_Handler at a regular interval (e.g., every one millisecond). This handler decrements a software timer for the currently executing thread. When this runtime reaches zero, the OS proactively triggers a context switch using PendSV. The OS\'s role is proactive and scheduled, ensuring that no single thread runs indefinitely, thus providing a fair distribution of CPU resources.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,4,'2025-07-31_23-21-07','From a user\'s perspective, the primary difference lies in control. In co-operative multitasking, a thread must voluntarily give up control of the CPU by calling a function like osYield. The user, therefore, has complete control over when a context switch occurs. This requires the user to be mindful of thread execution times and to strategically place osYield calls to prevent any single thread from monopolizing the CPU. In contrast, with pre-emptive multitasking, the user does not need to explicitly yield. The OS will automatically switch threads based on a timed schedule, even if a thread does not yield. While a user can still choose to yield, the OS will handle the switching if the thread\'s allocated time slice expires.\nFrom the OS\'s perspective, the difference is in the triggering mechanism for a context switch. In co-operative multitasking, the OS simply waits for a thread to call osYield, which then triggers a context switch using the PendSV interrupt. The OS\'s role is reactive; it only performs a switch when a thread requests it.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS takes an active role in managing thread execution. It uses a hardware timer, the SysTick timer, to trigger an interrupt called SysTick_Handler at a regular interval (e.g., every one millisecond). This handler decrements a software timer for the currently executing thread. When this runtime reaches zero, the OS proactively triggers a context switch using PendSV. The OS\'s role is proactive and scheduled, ensuring that no single thread runs indefinitely, thus providing a fair distribution of CPU resources.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,4,'2025-07-31_23-56-10','User perspective:\nFrom a user\'s perspective, the primary difference lies in control. In co-operative multitasking, a thread must voluntarily give up control of the CPU by calling a function like osYield. The user, therefore, has complete control over when a context switch occurs. This requires the user to be mindful of thread execution times and to strategically place osYield calls to prevent any single thread from monopolizing the CPU. In contrast, with pre-emptive multitasking, the user does not need to explicitly yield. The OS will automatically switch threads based on a timed schedule, even if a thread does not yield. While a user can still choose to yield, the OS will handle the switching if the thread\'s allocated time slice expires.\nOS perspective:\nFrom the OS\'s perspective, the difference is in the triggering mechanism for a context switch. In co-operative multitasking, the OS simply waits for a thread to call osYield, which then triggers a context switch using the PendSV interrupt. The OS\'s role is reactive; it only performs a switch when a thread requests it.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS takes an active role in managing thread execution. It uses a hardware timer, the SysTick timer, to trigger an interrupt called SysTick_Handler at a regular interval (e.g., every one millisecond). This handler decrements a software timer for the currently executing thread. When this runtime reaches zero, the OS proactively triggers a context switch using PendSV. The OS\'s role is proactive and scheduled, ensuring that no single thread runs indefinitely, thus providing a fair distribution of CPU resources.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,5,'2025-07-31_22-59-24','It is possible for both yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching to work together because they both ultimately achieve the same goal—triggering a context switch—but are initiated by different mechanisms. In both cases, the final step for a context switch is to trigger the PendSV interrupt.\nThe key difference lies in what causes the context switch to be initiated. For a co-operative, (or yielding), thread, the context switch is triggered by the thread itself calling osYield. This function sets a flag to activate the PendSV interrupt, which then performs the context switch. This is a user-initiated event.\nFor a pre-emptive thread, the context switch is initiated by the OS. The SysTick_Handler interrupt, which is triggered every one millisecond, decrements a software timer for the currently running thread. When this runtime reaches zero, the OS resets the runtime and then triggers a context switch using PendSV.\nThe two mechanisms work together because the OS is designed to handle both scenarios. The pre-emptive mechanism ensures that no thread can run indefinitely, as the OS will always regain control after a certain period. The yielding mechanism allows a thread to voluntarily give up the CPU, which can be beneficial if a thread finishes its task early, or is waiting for an event. The OS\'s context-switching logic, which is handled by PendSV, is the same regardless of whether the switch was triggered by a yielding thread or a timer expiration. The system can therefore support both types of threads running concurrently, with the pre-emptive mechanism acting as a safety net to prevent a co-operative thread from locking up the system if it fails to yield.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,5,'2025-07-31_23-21-07','It is possible for both yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching to work together because they both ultimately achieve the same goal—triggering a context switch—but are initiated by different mechanisms. In both cases, the final step for a context switch is to trigger the PendSV interrupt.\nThe key difference lies in what causes the context switch to be initiated. For a co-operative, (or yielding), thread, the context switch is triggered by the thread itself calling osYield. This function sets a flag to activate the PendSV interrupt, which then performs the context switch. This is a user-initiated event.\nFor a pre-emptive thread, the context switch is initiated by the OS. The SysTick_Handler interrupt, which is triggered every one millisecond, decrements a software timer for the currently running thread. When this runtime reaches zero, the OS resets the runtime and then triggers a context switch using PendSV.\nThe two mechanisms work together because the OS is designed to handle both scenarios. The pre-emptive mechanism ensures that no thread can run indefinitely, as the OS will always regain control after a certain period. The yielding mechanism allows a thread to voluntarily give up the CPU, which can be beneficial if a thread finishes its task early, or is waiting for an event. The OS\'s context-switching logic, which is handled by PendSV, is the same regardless of whether the switch was triggered by a yielding thread or a timer expiration. The system can therefore support both types of threads running concurrently, with the pre-emptive mechanism acting as a safety net to prevent a co-operative thread from locking up the system if it fails to yield.\n',1,'s25'),('nruprai',5,5,'2025-07-31_23-56-10','It is possible for both yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching to work together because they both ultimately achieve the same goal—triggering a context switch—but are initiated by different mechanisms. In both cases, the final step for a context switch is to trigger the PendSV interrupt.\nThe key difference lies in what causes the context switch to be initiated. For a co-operative, (or yielding), thread, the context switch is triggered by the thread itself calling osYield. This function sets a flag to activate the PendSV interrupt, which then performs the context switch. This is a user-initiated event.\nFor a pre-emptive thread, the context switch is initiated by the OS. The SysTick_Handler interrupt, which is triggered every one millisecond, decrements a software timer for the currently running thread. When this runtime reaches zero, the OS resets the runtime and then triggers a context switch using PendSV.\nThe two mechanisms work together because the OS is designed to handle both scenarios. The pre-emptive mechanism ensures that no thread can run indefinitely, as the OS will always regain control after a certain period. The yielding mechanism allows a thread to voluntarily give up the CPU, which can be beneficial if a thread finishes its task early, or is waiting for an event. The OS\'s context-switching logic, which is handled by PendSV, is the same regardless of whether the switch was triggered by a yielding thread or a timer expiration. The system can therefore support both types of threads running concurrently, with the pre-emptive mechanism acting as a safety net to prevent a co-operative thread from locking up the system if it fails to yield.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',1,1,'2025-05-29_14-50-51','The HAL, also known as the Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a set of libraries that’s provided by STM. It simplifies interaction with the microcontroller’s hardware. HAL hides the low-level register manipulations and makes it user-friendly for the user to control peripherals like UART and timers. HAL helps reduce development time and allows the user to write code without the need to manage hardware details.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',1,1,'2025-05-29_15-35-19','The HAL, also known as the Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a set of libraries that’s provided by STM. It simplifies interaction with the microcontroller’s hardware. HAL hides the low-level register manipulations and makes it user-friendly for the user to control peripherals like UART and timers. HAL helps reduce development time and allows the user to write code without the need to manage hardware details.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',1,2,'2025-05-29_14-50-51','The __io_putchar function sends a single character over the UART. It\'s needed because the standard printf function in C doesn\'t know how to output text on an embedded system, so it relies on this helper function to send characters to corret output device. In this case, its implemented by using io_putchar to transmit characters over UART2, enabling printf so the user can see the output on the console.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',1,2,'2025-05-29_15-35-19','The __io_putchar function sends one character at a time to the UART so that printf can display text on a serial console. It\'s necessary because printf is a generic C function that doesn\'t know anything about the specific hardware it\'s running on. Different embedded systems use different peripherals for output, so each project needs its own version of __io_putchar to connect printf to the correct device, like UART, USB, or an LCD. In this case, we use it to send characters through UART2, allowing printf output to appear on the computer console.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',1,3,'2025-05-29_14-50-51','The reason we used the debugger instead of the printf is because the printf is only able to show values after the program runs. Printf cannot display hardware specific information, such as the values of CPU registers (SP, MSP, etc) in real time. The debugger allows the user to pause the program and directly inspect the registers, which is necessary to verify the stack pointers were set correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',1,3,'2025-05-29_15-35-19','The reason we used the debugger instead of the printf is because the printf is only able to show values after the program runs. Printf cannot display hardware specific information, such as the values of CPU registers (SP, MSP, etc) in real time. The debugger allows the user to pause the program and directly inspect the registers, which is necessary to verify the stack pointers were set correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',1,4,'2025-05-29_14-50-51','Firstly, we got the MSP_INIT_VAL by reading memory at address 0x0, which hold the initial Stack Pointer value. Since stacks grow downward in memory, we computed a new PSP value by subtracting 0x400 (1024 bytes) from MSP_INIT_VAL. This would give us a new location in memory to use as the process stack pointer. We needed the MSP location so we can make sure the PSP stack would not overlap with MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',1,4,'2025-05-29_15-35-19','Firstly, we got the MSP_INIT_VAL by reading memory at address 0x0, which hold the initial Stack Pointer value. Since stacks grow downward in memory, we computed a new PSP value by subtracting 0x400 (1024 bytes) from MSP_INIT_VAL. This would give us a new location in memory to use as the process stack pointer. We needed the MSP location so we can make sure the PSP stack would not overlap with MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',1,5,'2025-05-29_14-50-51','In order to support multiple thread stacks, we would divide the stack memory into equal-sized sections, so we can have one for each thread. Starting after the main stack, each thread’s stack would begin at the end of the previous one. As long as we know how many threads there are and how big each stack needs to be, we can calculate where each one starts to make sure they don’t overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',1,5,'2025-05-29_15-35-19','In order to support multiple thread stacks, we would divide the stack memory into equal-sized sections, so we can have one for each thread. Starting after the main stack, each thread’s stack would begin at the end of the previous one. As long as we know how many threads there are and how big each stack needs to be, we can calculate where each one starts to make sure they don’t overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,1,'2025-06-25_17-33-48','In this lab, when I set the Program Counter (PC) to the address of my print_continuously function, I was simulating a thread switch by telling the CPU to start executing that function. I built a fake stack frame with register values, including the PC, and used a system call to switch to it. When the CPU restored this context using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), it jumped to print_continuously, effectively running it like a new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,1,'2025-06-25_18-19-07','In this lab, when I set the Program Counter (PC) to the address of my print_continuously function, I was simulating a thread switch by telling the CPU to start executing that function. I built a fake stack frame with register values, including the PC, and used a system call to switch to it. When the CPU restored this context using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), it jumped to print_continuously, effectively running it like a new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,1,'2025-06-25_18-54-44','In this lab, when I set the Program Counter (PC) to the address of my print_continuously function, I was simulating a thread switch by telling the CPU to start executing that function. I built a fake stack frame with register values, including the PC, and used a system call to switch to it. When the CPU restored this context using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), it jumped to print_continuously, effectively running it like a new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,1,'2025-06-26_13-43-01','In this lab, when I set the Program Counter (PC) to the address of my print_continuously function, I was simulating a thread switch by telling the CPU to start executing that function. I built a fake stack frame with register values, including the PC, and used a system call to switch to it. When the CPU restored this context using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), it jumped to print_continuously, effectively running it like a new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,1,'2025-06-26_15-56-01','In this lab, when I set the Program Counter (PC) to the address of my print_continuously function, I was simulating a thread switch by telling the CPU to start executing that function. I built a fake stack frame with register values, including the PC, and used a system call to switch to it. When the CPU restored this context using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), it jumped to print_continuously, effectively running it like a new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,1,'2025-06-27_18-03-31','In this lab, when I set the Program Counter (PC) to the address of my print_continuously function, I was simulating a thread switch by telling the CPU to start executing that function. I built a fake stack frame with register values, including the PC, and used a system call to switch to it. When the CPU restored this context using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), it jumped to print_continuously, effectively running it like a new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,2,'2025-06-25_17-33-48','A system call is a way for a program to ask the operating system to do something it can’t do by itself, like printing to the screen or switching threads. It works by using a special instruction (like SVC) that tells the processor to pause the program and run trusted code in the OS instead, which handles the request safely.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,2,'2025-06-25_18-19-07','A system call is a way for a program to ask the operating system to do something it can’t do by itself, like printing to the screen or switching threads. It works by using a special instruction (like SVC) that tells the processor to pause the program and run trusted code in the OS instead, which handles the request safely.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',2,2,'2025-06-25_18-54-44','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a user program safely request services from the operating system’s kernel, such as printing to a screen or creating threads. Since user programs are not allowed to directly access hardware or privileged operations, system calls use the SVC instruction to trigger a software interrupt, which switches the processor from user mode to privileged (kernel) mode. Once in kernel mode, the OS can carry out the requested operation in a controlled and secure way. This protects the system from bugs or malicious behavior in user code while still allowing access to essential system resources through a well-defined interface.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,2,'2025-06-26_13-43-01','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a user program safely request services from the operating system’s kernel, such as printing to a screen or creating threads. Since user programs are not allowed to directly access hardware or privileged operations, system calls use the SVC instruction to trigger a software interrupt, which switches the processor from user mode to privileged (kernel) mode. Once in kernel mode, the OS can carry out the requested operation in a controlled and secure way. This protects the system from bugs or malicious behavior in user code while still allowing access to essential system resources through a well-defined interface.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,2,'2025-06-26_15-56-01','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a user program safely request services from the operating system’s kernel, such as printing to a screen or creating threads. Since user programs are not allowed to directly access hardware or privileged operations, system calls use the SVC instruction to trigger a software interrupt, which switches the processor from user mode to privileged (kernel) mode. Once in kernel mode, the OS can carry out the requested operation in a controlled and secure way. This protects the system from bugs or malicious behavior in user code while still allowing access to essential system resources through a well-defined interface.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,2,'2025-06-27_18-03-31','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets a user program safely request services from the operating system’s kernel, such as printing to a screen or creating threads. Since user programs are not allowed to directly access hardware or privileged operations, system calls use the SVC instruction to trigger a software interrupt, which switches the processor from user mode to privileged (kernel) mode. Once in kernel mode, the OS can carry out the requested operation in a controlled and secure way. This protects the system from bugs or malicious behavior in user code while still allowing access to essential system resources through a well-defined interface.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,3,'2025-06-25_17-33-48','Setting up a thread’s stack means creating a fake version of what the CPU expects to see when it switches to a new thread. In this lab, we manually pushed values like the program counter (PC), xPSR, and general-purpose registers onto a chunk of memory that will act as the thread’s private stack. This is important because when the thread starts running, the CPU will \"pop\" these values off the stack to restore its context, as if the thread had been paused before. We store register values on the stack so that the CPU knows where to resume execution, what function to call, and what the previous state of the thread was.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',2,3,'2025-06-25_18-19-07','Setting up a thread’s stack means manually preparing the memory layout so that when the CPU starts or resumes the thread, it can restore the thread’s context exactly as if it had been running before. In this lab, we created a new stack below the main stack and pushed values onto it in a specific order: starting with xPSR, then the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and registers R0 through R12, including R4–R11 which are normally saved during context switching. This exact order is critical because the CPU expects the stack to follow a strict layout when popping values back into the registers during an exception return. If the order were incorrect, the CPU would place the wrong values into the wrong registers, leading to unpredictable behavior or a system crash. Storing these values allows the CPU to \"resume\" the thread in a consistent state, making context switching possible. Without this setup, thread execution could not begin safely or correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,3,'2025-06-25_18-54-44','Setting up a thread’s stack means manually preparing the memory layout so that when the CPU starts or resumes the thread, it can restore the thread’s context exactly as if it had been running before. In this lab, we created a new stack below the main stack and pushed values onto it in a specific order: starting with xPSR, then the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and registers R0 through R12, including R4–R11 which are normally saved during context switching. This exact order is critical because the CPU expects the stack to follow a strict layout when popping values back into the registers during an exception return. If the order were incorrect, the CPU would place the wrong values into the wrong registers, leading to unpredictable behavior or a system crash. Storing these values allows the CPU to \"resume\" the thread in a consistent state, making context switching possible. Without this setup, thread execution could not begin safely or correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,3,'2025-06-26_13-43-01','Setting up a thread’s stack means manually preparing the memory layout so that when the CPU starts or resumes the thread, it can restore the thread’s context exactly as if it had been running before. In this lab, we created a new stack below the main stack and pushed values onto it in a specific order: starting with xPSR, then the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and registers R0 through R12, including R4–R11 which are normally saved during context switching. This exact order is critical because the CPU expects the stack to follow a strict layout when popping values back into the registers during an exception return. If the order were incorrect, the CPU would place the wrong values into the wrong registers, leading to unpredictable behavior or a system crash. Storing these values allows the CPU to \"resume\" the thread in a consistent state, making context switching possible. Without this setup, thread execution could not begin safely or correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,3,'2025-06-26_15-56-01','Setting up a thread’s stack means manually preparing the memory layout so that when the CPU starts or resumes the thread, it can restore the thread’s context exactly as if it had been running before. In this lab, we created a new stack below the main stack and pushed values onto it in a specific order: starting with xPSR, then the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and registers R0 through R12, including R4–R11 which are normally saved during context switching. This exact order is critical because the CPU expects the stack to follow a strict layout when popping values back into the registers during an exception return. If the order were incorrect, the CPU would place the wrong values into the wrong registers, leading to unpredictable behavior or a system crash. Storing these values allows the CPU to \"resume\" the thread in a consistent state, making context switching possible. Without this setup, thread execution could not begin safely or correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,3,'2025-06-27_18-03-31','Setting up a thread’s stack means manually preparing the memory layout so that when the CPU starts or resumes the thread, it can restore the thread’s context exactly as if it had been running before. In this lab, we created a new stack below the main stack and pushed values onto it in a specific order: starting with xPSR, then the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and registers R0 through R12, including R4–R11 which are normally saved during context switching. This exact order is critical because the CPU expects the stack to follow a strict layout when popping values back into the registers during an exception return. If the order were incorrect, the CPU would place the wrong values into the wrong registers, leading to unpredictable behavior or a system crash. Storing these values allows the CPU to \"resume\" the thread in a consistent state, making context switching possible. Without this setup, thread execution could not begin safely or correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,4,'2025-06-25_17-33-48','If we didn’t set the PSP using __set_PSP, the processor wouldn’t know where the new thread’s stack is located. When runFirstThread() runs, it expects the process stack (PSP) to point to a valid stack with all the saved register values. Without this setup, the CPU tries to pop values (like PC and xPSR) from an uninitialized or incorrect memory location, leading to invalid instruction execution or memory access — which causes a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,4,'2025-06-25_18-19-07','If we didn’t set the PSP using __set_PSP, the processor wouldn’t know where the new thread’s stack is located. When runFirstThread() runs, it expects the process stack (PSP) to point to a valid stack with all the saved register values. Without this setup, the CPU tries to pop values (like PC and xPSR) from an uninitialized or incorrect memory location, leading to invalid instruction execution or memory access — which causes a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,4,'2025-06-25_18-54-44','If we didn’t set the PSP using __set_PSP, the processor wouldn’t know where the new thread’s stack is located. When runFirstThread() runs, it expects the process stack (PSP) to point to a valid stack with all the saved register values. Without this setup, the CPU tries to pop values (like PC and xPSR) from an uninitialized or incorrect memory location, leading to invalid instruction execution or memory access — which causes a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,4,'2025-06-26_13-43-01','If we didn’t set the PSP using __set_PSP, the processor wouldn’t know where the new thread’s stack is located. When runFirstThread() runs, it expects the process stack (PSP) to point to a valid stack with all the saved register values. Without this setup, the CPU tries to pop values (like PC and xPSR) from an uninitialized or incorrect memory location, leading to invalid instruction execution or memory access — which causes a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',2,4,'2025-06-26_15-56-01','The Cortex-M4 processor uses two stack pointers: the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is used by default during startup and exceptions, and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is meant for user or thread-level code. When running a new thread, the CPU must explicitly switch to the PSP so it can use the thread’s private stack. If we skip the __set_PSP call, the CPU will continue using the MSP, which doesn’t point to the thread’s stack. As a result, when runFirstThread() executes and the CPU tries to restore register values from the wrong location, it ends up reading invalid data or accessing memory it shouldn\'t. This mismatch leads directly to a hard fault, since the processor can’t continue safely without a valid stack pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,4,'2025-06-27_18-03-31','The Cortex-M4 processor uses two stack pointers: the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is used by default during startup and exceptions, and the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is meant for user or thread-level code. When running a new thread, the CPU must explicitly switch to the PSP so it can use the thread’s private stack. If we skip the __set_PSP call, the CPU will continue using the MSP, which doesn’t point to the thread’s stack. As a result, when runFirstThread() executes and the CPU tries to restore register values from the wrong location, it ends up reading invalid data or accessing memory it shouldn\'t. This mismatch leads directly to a hard fault, since the processor can’t continue safely without a valid stack pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,5,'2025-06-25_17-33-48','If the thread function didn’t loop and simply returned, the processor would try to pop the return address (LR) from the stack and jump there. But since the thread wasn’t started by a normal function call, the return address isn’t meaningful. This would likely make the CPU jump to an invalid address or corrupted instruction, causing a crash or a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,5,'2025-06-25_18-19-07','If the thread function didn’t loop and simply returned, the processor would try to pop the return address (LR) from the stack and jump there. But since the thread wasn’t started by a normal function call, the return address isn’t meaningful. This would likely make the CPU jump to an invalid address or corrupted instruction, causing a crash or a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',2,5,'2025-06-25_18-54-44','If the thread function printed something once and then returned, the CPU would try to pop the return address from the stack using the link register (LR). However, in our setup, we manually set LR to a placeholder value because the thread was not started by a normal function call. Since this return address doesn’t point to valid code, the CPU would jump to an invalid or restricted memory location, which would trigger a hard fault—a serious system crash in the Cortex-M4 processor. This happens because the processor can\'t recover from executing instructions at an undefined address, and it highlights why thread functions must not return unless a proper exit path is set up.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',2,5,'2025-06-26_13-43-01','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned after printing, the CPU would attempt to return by popping a return address from the stack and jumping to it using the link register (LR). However, because the thread was not started using a regular function call, no valid return address was ever pushed onto the thread’s stack. Instead, we manually placed a placeholder value in LR, and the stack contains no real return path. When the CPU tries to return using this fake LR, it ends up jumping to an invalid memory location, which results in a hard fault. This fault occurs because the processor depends on a properly structured stack to resume execution safely, and without a valid return address, it cannot continue correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,5,'2025-06-26_15-56-01','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned after printing, the CPU would attempt to return by popping a return address from the stack and jumping to it using the link register (LR). However, because the thread was not started using a regular function call, no valid return address was ever pushed onto the thread’s stack. Instead, we manually placed a placeholder value in LR, and the stack contains no real return path. When the CPU tries to return using this fake LR, it ends up jumping to an invalid memory location, which results in a hard fault. This fault occurs because the processor depends on a properly structured stack to resume execution safely, and without a valid return address, it cannot continue correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',2,5,'2025-06-27_18-03-31','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned after printing, the CPU would attempt to return by popping a return address from the stack and jumping to it using the link register (LR). However, because the thread was not started using a regular function call, no valid return address was ever pushed onto the thread’s stack. Instead, we manually placed a placeholder value in LR, and the stack contains no real return path. When the CPU tries to return using this fake LR, it ends up jumping to an invalid memory location, which results in a hard fault. This fault occurs because the processor depends on a properly structured stack to resume execution safely, and without a valid return address, it cannot continue correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',3,1,'2025-06-27_18-13-22','Each .c file in a project goes through a series of steps to be combined into a single executable. First, the preprocessor expands macros and #include directives, producing a complete C source file. Next, the compiler converts each .c file into a machine-readable .o (object) file, while checking for syntax errors and optimizing the code. Finally, during linking, all the object files are stitched together into one executable. The linker resolves cross-file references such as function calls and variable access, and includes standard libraries like printf(). This results in a fully linked program ready to be loaded onto a microcontroller or system.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',3,1,'2025-06-27_19-02-26','Each .c file in a project goes through a series of steps to be combined into a single executable. First, the preprocessor expands macros and #include directives, producing a complete C source file. Next, the compiler converts each .c file into a machine-readable .o (object) file, while checking for syntax errors and optimizing the code. Finally, during linking, all the object files are stitched together into one executable. The linker resolves cross-file references such as function calls and variable access, and includes standard libraries like printf(). This results in a fully linked program ready to be loaded onto a microcontroller or system.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',3,2,'2025-06-27_18-13-22','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad practice because it tells the compiler to copy the entire source code of kernel.c into another .c file before compiling, instead of compiling it separately. This leads to problems such as duplicate symbols during the linking stage because the same functions and variables might now appear in multiple object files. It also breaks the modular structure of the program and confuses the build system, making the code harder to manage, test, and maintain. Instead, .h files should be included, and .c files should be compiled separately and linked properly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',3,2,'2025-06-27_19-02-26','Using #include <kernel.c> is a bad practice because it tells the compiler to copy the entire source code of kernel.c into another .c file before compiling, instead of compiling it separately. This leads to problems such as duplicate symbols during the linking stage because the same functions and variables might now appear in multiple object files. It also breaks the modular structure of the program and confuses the build system, making the code harder to manage, test, and maintain. Instead, .h files should be included, and .c files should be compiled separately and linked properly.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',3,3,'2025-06-27_18-13-22','Initialize the kernel – Call a function to set up memory and data structures needed for thread management.\nWrite the thread function – Define a function with no parameters and no return value that contains the code the thread will run.\nCreate the thread – Call a thread creation function, passing it the address of the thread function. This allocates a stack and prepares the thread\'s context.\nStart the kernel – Call a function to start the OS, which triggers a system call to load the thread\'s stack and jump to it.\nThread begins execution – The thread now runs independently as part of the system\'s execution flow.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',3,3,'2025-06-27_19-02-26','Initialize the kernel – Call a function to set up memory and data structures needed for thread management.\nWrite the thread function – Define a function with no parameters and no return value that contains the code the thread will run.\nCreate the thread – Call a thread creation function, passing it the address of the thread function. This allocates a stack and prepares the thread\'s context.\nStart the kernel – Call a function to start the OS, which triggers a system call to load the thread\'s stack and jump to it.\nThread begins execution – The thread now runs independently as part of the system\'s execution flow.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',3,4,'2025-06-27_18-13-22','The stack pool is a pre-allocated block of memory dedicated to thread stacks. Its purpose is to provide isolated, fixed-size stack spaces for each thread the OS runs. Each thread needs its own stack for storing local variables, function calls, and saved CPU registers. In future labs, when we extend the OS to support multiple concurrent threads, the stack pool will allow the OS to dynamically assign stack memory from this shared region to each new thread in a controlled, efficient, and aligned way. This enables proper context switching and thread isolation without memory corruption.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',3,4,'2025-06-27_19-02-26','The stack pool is a pre-allocated block of memory dedicated to thread stacks. Its purpose is to provide isolated, fixed-size stack spaces for each thread the OS runs. Each thread needs its own stack for storing local variables, function calls, and saved CPU registers. In future labs, when we extend the OS to support multiple concurrent threads, the stack pool will allow the OS to dynamically assign stack memory from this shared region to each new thread in a controlled, efficient, and aligned way. This enables proper context switching and thread isolation without memory corruption. In addition to thread stacks, the stack pool also includes a portion for the operating system\'s own stack, which is used during system calls and context switches. Including the OS stack in the same memory region ensures that all stack usage is managed centrally and predictably by the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',3,5,'2025-06-27_18-13-22','The kernel contains the low-level code that manages hardware and core OS functions. This includes thread management, stack setup, and interrupt/system call handling, and is typically stored in kernel.c and kernel.h. The user code, written in main.c, defines the application\'s logic—such as thread behavior and when to start the OS—while interacting with the kernel through well-defined APIs like osCreateThread(). This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on application logic without worrying about the internal OS mechanisms. It also promotes modularity, reusability, and system security, as kernel code is centralized and protected from accidental modification.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',3,5,'2025-06-27_19-02-26','The kernel contains the low-level code that manages hardware and core OS functions. This includes thread management, stack setup, and interrupt/system call handling, and is typically stored in kernel.c and kernel.h. The user code, written in main.c, defines the application\'s logic—such as thread behavior and when to start the OS—while interacting with the kernel through well-defined APIs like osCreateThread(). This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on application logic without worrying about the internal OS mechanisms. It also promotes modularity, reusability, and system security, as kernel code is centralized and protected from accidental modification.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,1,'2025-07-28_19-02-27','In the lab, we used an array to hold multiple Thread Control Blocks (TCBs), but a queue offers a more natural mapping to round-robin scheduling. When a thread becomes ready, its TCB is enqueued at the tail; when the scheduler runs, it dequeues the head TCB, dispatches that thread, and then—if the thread remains runnable—re-enqueues it at the tail. This “take your turn and go to the back of the line” approach can be implemented with O(1) enqueue and dequeue operations, avoiding the need to shift array elements. A queue also supports dynamic sizing: you can grow or shrink the queue at runtime to match exactly the number of ready threads. Finally, removing a thread from the middle of a queue when it blocks (for example on I/O) and re-enqueuing it when it unblocks is straightforward and does not disturb the positions of other TCBs. These benefits—constant-time operations, dynamic resizing, and easy blocking/unblocking—make a queue an appealing alternative to a fixed-size array for a production-quality scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,1,'2025-07-28_22-52-13','In the lab, we used an array to hold multiple Thread Control Blocks (TCBs), but a queue offers a more natural mapping to round-robin scheduling. When a thread becomes ready, its TCB is enqueued at the tail; when the scheduler runs, it dequeues the head TCB, dispatches that thread, and then—if the thread remains runnable—re-enqueues it at the tail. This “take your turn and go to the back of the line” approach can be implemented with O(1) enqueue and dequeue operations, avoiding the need to shift array elements. A queue also supports dynamic sizing: you can grow or shrink the queue at runtime to match exactly the number of ready threads. Finally, removing a thread from the middle of a queue when it blocks (for example on I/O) and re-enqueuing it when it unblocks is straightforward and does not disturb the positions of other TCBs. These benefits—constant-time operations, dynamic resizing, and easy blocking/unblocking—make a queue an appealing alternative to a fixed-size array for a production-quality scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,1,'2025-07-28_23-11-24','In the lab, we used an array to hold multiple Thread Control Blocks (TCBs), but a queue offers a more natural mapping to round-robin scheduling. When a thread becomes ready, its TCB is enqueued at the tail; when the scheduler runs, it dequeues the head TCB, dispatches that thread, and then—if the thread remains runnable—re-enqueues it at the tail. This “take your turn and go to the back of the line” approach can be implemented with O(1) enqueue and dequeue operations, avoiding the need to shift array elements. A queue also supports dynamic sizing: you can grow or shrink the queue at runtime to match exactly the number of ready threads. Finally, removing a thread from the middle of a queue when it blocks (for example on I/O) and re-enqueuing it when it unblocks is straightforward and does not disturb the positions of other TCBs. These benefits—constant-time operations, dynamic resizing, and easy blocking/unblocking—make a queue an appealing alternative to a fixed-size array for a production-quality scheduler.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',4,1,'2025-07-28_23-40-03','In the lab, we used an array to hold multiple Thread Control Blocks (TCBs), but a queue offers a more natural mapping to round-robin scheduling. When a thread becomes ready, its TCB is enqueued at the tail; when the scheduler runs, it dequeues the head TCB, dispatches that thread, and then—if the thread remains runnable—re-enqueues it at the tail. This “take your turn and go to the back of the line” approach can be implemented with O(1) enqueue and dequeue operations, avoiding the need to shift array elements. A queue also supports dynamic sizing: you can grow or shrink the queue at runtime to match exactly the number of ready threads. Finally, removing a thread from the middle of a queue when it blocks (for example on I/O) and re-enqueuing it when it unblocks is straightforward and does not disturb the positions of other TCBs. These benefits—constant-time operations, dynamic resizing, and easy blocking/unblocking—make a queue an appealing alternative to a fixed-size array for a production-quality scheduler.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',4,1,'2025-07-28_23-51-39','In the lab, we used an array to hold multiple Thread Control Blocks (TCBs), but a queue offers a more natural mapping to round-robin scheduling. When a thread becomes ready, its TCB is enqueued at the tail; when the scheduler runs, it dequeues the head TCB, dispatches that thread, and then—if the thread remains runnable—re-enqueues it at the tail. This “take your turn and go to the back of the line” approach can be implemented with O(1) enqueue and dequeue operations, avoiding the need to shift array elements. A queue also supports dynamic sizing: you can grow or shrink the queue at runtime to match exactly the number of ready threads. Finally, removing a thread from the middle of a queue when it blocks (for example on I/O) and re-enqueuing it when it unblocks is straightforward and does not disturb the positions of other TCBs. These benefits—constant-time operations, dynamic resizing, and easy blocking/unblocking—make a queue an appealing alternative to a fixed-size array for a production-quality scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,1,'2025-07-29_00-00-54','In the lab, we used an array to hold multiple Thread Control Blocks (TCBs), but a queue offers a more natural mapping to round-robin scheduling. When a thread becomes ready, its TCB is enqueued at the tail; when the scheduler runs, it dequeues the head TCB, dispatches that thread, and then—if the thread remains runnable—re-enqueues it at the tail. This “take your turn and go to the back of the line” approach can be implemented with O(1) enqueue and dequeue operations, avoiding the need to shift array elements. A queue also supports dynamic sizing: you can grow or shrink the queue at runtime to match exactly the number of ready threads. Finally, removing a thread from the middle of a queue when it blocks (for example on I/O) and re-enqueuing it when it unblocks is straightforward and does not disturb the positions of other TCBs. These benefits—constant-time operations, dynamic resizing, and easy blocking/unblocking—make a queue an appealing alternative to a fixed-size array for a production-quality scheduler.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',4,1,'2025-07-29_00-29-56','In the lab, we used an array to hold multiple TCBs and implemented round-robin scheduling by advancing a currentThread index modulo numThreadsRunning. This behavior is functionally equivalent to a circular queue: each READY thread gets its turn in order, and the scheduler wraps around once it reaches the end. So even though we used an array, the existing scheduler already behaves like a queue in how it cycles through threads fairly.\nExplicitly using a queue—such as a linked list or ring buffer—can make this behavior more intuitive. When a thread yields, blocks, or becomes ready again, its TCB can be easily removed or inserted without disturbing others. Queues also support dynamic resizing and constant-time enqueue/dequeue operations, which can simplify management in more advanced systems.\nHowever, this added abstraction introduces overhead. Managing pointers, handling memory for a dynamic queue, and dealing with edge cases (e.g., full or empty conditions) adds complexity. In a resource-constrained embedded OS, this overhead may not justify the functional gains, especially since the array already provides efficient and fair round-robin behavior.\nSo while a queue can be conceptually cleaner and more flexible, the array-based design we used is already functionally equivalent for our purposes and achieves the same result with lower complexity and overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,2,'2025-07-28_19-02-27','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch in two phases. First, when the PendSV exception fires, the Cortex-M hardware has already automatically pushed registers R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR onto the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) of the currently running thread. The assembly stub in asmDump.s then explicitly pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 onto that same PSP, giving a full 16-register snapshot. Next, the C function osSched is invoked. It calls __get_PSP() to read the current PSP, subtracts 8×4 bytes to account for the R4–R11 space, and stores the result in threadArray[currentThread].stackPointer. It then advances currentThread in round-robin fashion, modulo numThreadsRunning, and loads the PSP of the next thread using __set_PSP(). When the handler returns, the processor automatically pops R4–R11, then R0–xPSR, restoring the new thread’s context and resuming execution exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,2,'2025-07-28_22-52-13','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch in two phases. First, when the PendSV exception fires, the Cortex-M hardware has already automatically pushed registers R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR onto the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) of the currently running thread. The assembly stub in asmDump.s then explicitly pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 onto that same PSP, giving a full 16-register snapshot. Next, the C function osSched is invoked. It calls __get_PSP() to read the current PSP, subtracts 8×4 bytes to account for the R4–R11 space, and stores the result in threadArray[currentThread].stackPointer. It then advances currentThread in round-robin fashion, modulo numThreadsRunning, and loads the PSP of the next thread using __set_PSP(). When the handler returns, the processor automatically pops R4–R11, then R0–xPSR, restoring the new thread’s context and resuming execution exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,2,'2025-07-28_23-11-24','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch in two phases. First, when the PendSV exception fires, the Cortex-M hardware has already automatically pushed registers R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR onto the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) of the currently running thread. The assembly stub in asmDump.s then explicitly pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 onto that same PSP, giving a full 16-register snapshot. Next, the C function osSched is invoked. It calls __get_PSP() to read the current PSP, subtracts 8×4 bytes to account for the R4–R11 space, and stores the result in threadArray[currentThread].stackPointer. It then advances currentThread in round-robin fashion, modulo numThreadsRunning, and loads the PSP of the next thread using __set_PSP(). When the handler returns, the processor automatically pops R4–R11, then R0–xPSR, restoring the new thread’s context and resuming execution exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,2,'2025-07-28_23-40-03','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch in two phases. First, when the PendSV exception fires, the Cortex-M hardware has already automatically pushed registers R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR onto the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) of the currently running thread. The assembly stub in asmDump.s then explicitly pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 onto that same PSP, giving a full 16-register snapshot. Next, the C function osSched is invoked. It calls __get_PSP() to read the current PSP, subtracts 8×4 bytes to account for the R4–R11 space, and stores the result in threadArray[currentThread].stackPointer. It then advances currentThread in round-robin fashion, modulo numThreadsRunning, and loads the PSP of the next thread using __set_PSP(). When the handler returns, the processor automatically pops R4–R11, then R0–xPSR, restoring the new thread’s context and resuming execution exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,2,'2025-07-28_23-51-39','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch in two phases. First, when the PendSV exception fires, the Cortex-M hardware has already automatically pushed registers R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR onto the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) of the currently running thread. The assembly stub in asmDump.s then explicitly pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 onto that same PSP, giving a full 16-register snapshot. Next, the C function osSched is invoked. It calls __get_PSP() to read the current PSP, subtracts 8×4 bytes to account for the R4–R11 space, and stores the result in threadArray[currentThread].stackPointer. It then advances currentThread in round-robin fashion, modulo numThreadsRunning, and loads the PSP of the next thread using __set_PSP(). When the handler returns, the processor automatically pops R4–R11, then R0–xPSR, restoring the new thread’s context and resuming execution exactly where it left off.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',4,2,'2025-07-29_00-00-54','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch in two phases. First, when the PendSV exception fires, the Cortex-M hardware has already automatically pushed registers R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR onto the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) of the currently running thread. The assembly stub in asmDump.s then explicitly pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 onto that same PSP, giving a full 16-register snapshot. Next, the C function osSched is invoked. It calls __get_PSP() to read the current PSP, subtracts 8×4 bytes to account for the R4–R11 space, and stores the result in threadArray[currentThread].stackPointer. It then advances currentThread in round-robin fashion, modulo numThreadsRunning, and loads the PSP of the next thread using __set_PSP(). When the handler returns, the processor automatically pops R4–R11, then R0–xPSR, restoring the new thread’s context and resuming execution exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,2,'2025-07-29_00-29-56','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch in two phases. First, when the PendSV exception fires, the Cortex-M hardware has already automatically pushed registers R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR onto the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) of the currently running thread. The assembly stub in asmDump.s then explicitly pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 onto that same PSP, giving a full 16-register snapshot. Next, the C function osSched is invoked. It calls __get_PSP() to read the current PSP, subtracts 8×4 bytes to account for the R4–R11 space, and stores the result in threadArray[currentThread].stackPointer. It then advances currentThread in round-robin fashion, modulo numThreadsRunning, and loads the PSP of the next thread using __set_PSP(). When the handler returns, the processor automatically pops R4–R11, then R0–xPSR, restoring the new thread’s context and resuming execution exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,3,'2025-07-28_19-02-27','Introducing a “priority” integer into each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the highest-priority thread may seem reasonable, but it leads to starvation. A runnable high-priority thread will continuously preempt all lower-priority threads, which may never get CPU time. Without additional mechanisms such as aging (gradually boosting the priority of waiting tasks) or priority inheritance (to avoid deadlocks on shared resources), low-priority threads could be starved indefinitely. This lack of fairness and potential for unbounded waiting make a pure priority-based scheduler a poor choice for general-purpose multitasking.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',4,3,'2025-07-28_22-52-13','Introducing a “priority” integer into each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the highest-priority thread may seem reasonable, but it leads to starvation. A runnable high-priority thread will continuously preempt all lower-priority threads, which may never get CPU time. Without additional mechanisms such as aging (gradually boosting the priority of waiting tasks) or priority inheritance (to avoid deadlocks on shared resources), low-priority threads could be starved indefinitely. This lack of fairness and potential for unbounded waiting make a pure priority-based scheduler a poor choice for general-purpose multitasking.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',4,3,'2025-07-28_23-11-24','Introducing a “priority” integer into each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the highest-priority thread may seem reasonable, but it leads to starvation. A runnable high-priority thread will continuously preempt all lower-priority threads, which may never get CPU time. Without additional mechanisms such as aging (gradually boosting the priority of waiting tasks) or priority inheritance (to avoid deadlocks on shared resources), low-priority threads could be starved indefinitely. This lack of fairness and potential for unbounded waiting make a pure priority-based scheduler a poor choice for general-purpose multitasking.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',4,3,'2025-07-28_23-40-03','Introducing a “priority” integer into each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the highest-priority thread may seem reasonable, but it leads to starvation. A runnable high-priority thread will continuously preempt all lower-priority threads, which may never get CPU time. Without additional mechanisms such as aging (gradually boosting the priority of waiting tasks) or priority inheritance (to avoid deadlocks on shared resources), low-priority threads could be starved indefinitely. This lack of fairness and potential for unbounded waiting make a pure priority-based scheduler a poor choice for general-purpose multitasking.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',4,3,'2025-07-28_23-51-39','Introducing a “priority” integer into each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the highest-priority thread may seem reasonable, but it leads to starvation. A runnable high-priority thread will continuously preempt all lower-priority threads, which may never get CPU time. Without additional mechanisms such as aging (gradually boosting the priority of waiting tasks) or priority inheritance (to avoid deadlocks on shared resources), low-priority threads could be starved indefinitely. This lack of fairness and potential for unbounded waiting make a pure priority-based scheduler a poor choice for general-purpose multitasking.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',4,3,'2025-07-29_00-00-54','Introducing a “priority” integer into each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the highest-priority thread may seem reasonable, but it leads to starvation. A runnable high-priority thread will continuously preempt all lower-priority threads, which may never get CPU time. Without additional mechanisms such as aging (gradually boosting the priority of waiting tasks) or priority inheritance (to avoid deadlocks on shared resources), low-priority threads could be starved indefinitely. This lack of fairness and potential for unbounded waiting make a pure priority-based scheduler a poor choice for general-purpose multitasking.\n',0,'s25'),('osajid',4,3,'2025-07-29_00-29-56','Introducing a priority integer into each TCB and modifying the scheduler to always run the thread with the highest priority seems reasonable, but it leads to broken scheduler behavior. In this design, the scheduler always picks the highest-priority thread from the READY list—every single time it runs. If that thread never blocks or yields, it will be the only task that ever runs. The scheduler will never perform a context switch to any lower-priority thread, no matter how long it has been waiting.\nThis is a direct result of the scheduler’s logic. There is nothing in this scheme to rotate between tasks, demote priorities, or enforce fairness. Lower-priority threads are left in the READY state indefinitely, but are completely ignored, causing total starvation. Only one task is ever scheduled, and all others are effectively frozen out of execution.\nReal operating systems solve this by augmenting pure priority scheduling with mechanisms like aging, where a task’s priority increases the longer it waits, and priority inheritance, which prevents low-priority threads from indefinitely blocking higher-priority ones. Without such mechanisms, this system prevents all task switching, starves every lower-priority thread, and violates the fundamental goal of a multitasking scheduler: to share the CPU fairly across tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,4,'2025-07-28_19-02-27','We placed our complex context-switch logic in assembly stubs, but then called high-level C functions (osSched from PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main from SVC_Handler) to perform most of the work. This hybrid approach brings several benefits: the C code is more readable and maintainable, compiler checks and type safety help avoid subtle bugs, and porting to a different ARM core or compiler often requires only small C modifications rather than a full rewrite in assembly. It also lets us step through scheduling logic in a source-level debugger. On the downside, C function calls incur prologue and epilogue overhead, use more stack space, and can make precise timing analysis harder because the compiler may reorder or inline code unpredictably. A fully assembly-based scheduler would be smaller, faster, and more deterministic, but it would be far harder to write, debug, and port.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,4,'2025-07-28_22-52-13','We placed our complex context-switch logic in assembly stubs, but then called high-level C functions (osSched from PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main from SVC_Handler) to perform most of the work. This hybrid approach brings several benefits: the C code is more readable and maintainable, compiler checks and type safety help avoid subtle bugs, and porting to a different ARM core or compiler often requires only small C modifications rather than a full rewrite in assembly. It also lets us step through scheduling logic in a source-level debugger. On the downside, C function calls incur prologue and epilogue overhead, use more stack space, and can make precise timing analysis harder because the compiler may reorder or inline code unpredictably. A fully assembly-based scheduler would be smaller, faster, and more deterministic, but it would be far harder to write, debug, and port.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,4,'2025-07-28_23-11-24','We placed our complex context-switch logic in assembly stubs, but then called high-level C functions (osSched from PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main from SVC_Handler) to perform most of the work. This hybrid approach brings several benefits: the C code is more readable and maintainable, compiler checks and type safety help avoid subtle bugs, and porting to a different ARM core or compiler often requires only small C modifications rather than a full rewrite in assembly. It also lets us step through scheduling logic in a source-level debugger. On the downside, C function calls incur prologue and epilogue overhead, use more stack space, and can make precise timing analysis harder because the compiler may reorder or inline code unpredictably. A fully assembly-based scheduler would be smaller, faster, and more deterministic, but it would be far harder to write, debug, and port.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,4,'2025-07-28_23-40-03','We placed our complex context-switch logic in assembly stubs, but then called high-level C functions (osSched from PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main from SVC_Handler) to perform most of the work. This hybrid approach brings several benefits: the C code is more readable and maintainable, compiler checks and type safety help avoid subtle bugs, and porting to a different ARM core or compiler often requires only small C modifications rather than a full rewrite in assembly. It also lets us step through scheduling logic in a source-level debugger. On the downside, C function calls incur prologue and epilogue overhead, use more stack space, and can make precise timing analysis harder because the compiler may reorder or inline code unpredictably. A fully assembly-based scheduler would be smaller, faster, and more deterministic, but it would be far harder to write, debug, and port.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,4,'2025-07-28_23-51-39','We placed our complex context-switch logic in assembly stubs, but then called high-level C functions (osSched from PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main from SVC_Handler) to perform most of the work. This hybrid approach brings several benefits: the C code is more readable and maintainable, compiler checks and type safety help avoid subtle bugs, and porting to a different ARM core or compiler often requires only small C modifications rather than a full rewrite in assembly. It also lets us step through scheduling logic in a source-level debugger. On the downside, C function calls incur prologue and epilogue overhead, use more stack space, and can make precise timing analysis harder because the compiler may reorder or inline code unpredictably. A fully assembly-based scheduler would be smaller, faster, and more deterministic, but it would be far harder to write, debug, and port.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,4,'2025-07-29_00-00-54','We placed our complex context-switch logic in assembly stubs, but then called high-level C functions (osSched from PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main from SVC_Handler) to perform most of the work. This hybrid approach brings several benefits: the C code is more readable and maintainable, compiler checks and type safety help avoid subtle bugs, and porting to a different ARM core or compiler often requires only small C modifications rather than a full rewrite in assembly. It also lets us step through scheduling logic in a source-level debugger. On the downside, C function calls incur prologue and epilogue overhead, use more stack space, and can make precise timing analysis harder because the compiler may reorder or inline code unpredictably. A fully assembly-based scheduler would be smaller, faster, and more deterministic, but it would be far harder to write, debug, and port.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,4,'2025-07-29_00-29-56','We placed our complex context-switch logic in assembly stubs, but then called high-level C functions (osSched from PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main from SVC_Handler) to perform most of the work. This hybrid approach brings several benefits: the C code is more readable and maintainable, compiler checks and type safety help avoid subtle bugs, and porting to a different ARM core or compiler often requires only small C modifications rather than a full rewrite in assembly. It also lets us step through scheduling logic in a source-level debugger. On the downside, C function calls incur prologue and epilogue overhead, use more stack space, and can make precise timing analysis harder because the compiler may reorder or inline code unpredictably. A fully assembly-based scheduler would be smaller, faster, and more deterministic, but it would be far harder to write, debug, and port.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,5,'2025-07-28_19-02-27','If you create only a single thread—say the print_continuously thread modified to call osYield() after each print—the OS still functions correctly. Each call to osYield() triggers SVC, which pends PendSV, saves the thread’s context into its sole TCB, and then the scheduler picks the only runnable thread (itself) and restores its context. In effect, the system context-switches to the same thread repeatedly. The OS remains stable and does not crash, but you gain no concurrency benefit; you simply pay the overhead of saving and restoring context without ever executing any other task.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,5,'2025-07-28_22-52-13','If you create only a single thread—say the print_continuously thread modified to call osYield() after each print—the OS still functions correctly. Each call to osYield() triggers SVC, which pends PendSV, saves the thread’s context into its sole TCB, and then the scheduler picks the only runnable thread (itself) and restores its context. In effect, the system context-switches to the same thread repeatedly. The OS remains stable and does not crash, but you gain no concurrency benefit; you simply pay the overhead of saving and restoring context without ever executing any other task.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,5,'2025-07-28_23-11-24','If you create only a single thread—say the print_continuously thread modified to call osYield() after each print—the OS still functions correctly. Each call to osYield() triggers SVC, which pends PendSV, saves the thread’s context into its sole TCB, and then the scheduler picks the only runnable thread (itself) and restores its context. In effect, the system context-switches to the same thread repeatedly. The OS remains stable and does not crash, but you gain no concurrency benefit; you simply pay the overhead of saving and restoring context without ever executing any other task.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,5,'2025-07-28_23-40-03','If you create only a single thread—say the print_continuously thread modified to call osYield() after each print—the OS still functions correctly. Each call to osYield() triggers SVC, which pends PendSV, saves the thread’s context into its sole TCB, and then the scheduler picks the only runnable thread (itself) and restores its context. In effect, the system context-switches to the same thread repeatedly. The OS remains stable and does not crash, but you gain no concurrency benefit; you simply pay the overhead of saving and restoring context without ever executing any other task.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,5,'2025-07-28_23-51-39','If you create only a single thread—say the print_continuously thread modified to call osYield() after each print—the OS still functions correctly. Each call to osYield() triggers SVC, which pends PendSV, saves the thread’s context into its sole TCB, and then the scheduler picks the only runnable thread (itself) and restores its context. In effect, the system context-switches to the same thread repeatedly. The OS remains stable and does not crash, but you gain no concurrency benefit; you simply pay the overhead of saving and restoring context without ever executing any other task.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,5,'2025-07-29_00-00-54','If you create only a single thread—say the print_continuously thread modified to call osYield() after each print—the OS still functions correctly. Each call to osYield() triggers SVC, which pends PendSV, saves the thread’s context into its sole TCB, and then the scheduler picks the only runnable thread (itself) and restores its context. In effect, the system context-switches to the same thread repeatedly. The OS remains stable and does not crash, but you gain no concurrency benefit; you simply pay the overhead of saving and restoring context without ever executing any other task.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',4,5,'2025-07-29_00-29-56','If you create only a single thread—say the print_continuously thread modified to call osYield() after each print—the OS still functions correctly. Each call to osYield() triggers SVC, which pends PendSV, saves the thread’s context into its sole TCB, and then the scheduler picks the only runnable thread (itself) and restores its context. In effect, the system context-switches to the same thread repeatedly. The OS remains stable and does not crash, but you gain no concurrency benefit; you simply pay the overhead of saving and restoring context without ever executing any other task.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',5,1,'2025-07-29_03-45-26','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must first define the data that the thread will need. This can be a primitive (like an int) or a struct that aggregates multiple values. The user then allocates that variable in a memory region that persists across context switches (such as global memory or the heap), populates it with data, and passes its pointer as a void* to the osCreateThread function.\nThe OS receives this pointer and places it into the R0 register during the construction of the thread’s exception stack frame. According to the ARM Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS), R0 is where the first function argument is expected. When the thread is eventually scheduled and context-switched in, the CPU uses the stack frame to restore the register values, including R0. This means the thread function receives the user’s data pointer exactly as expected.\nBecause the thread function is written to accept a single void* parameter, it can cast this pointer back to the original type and safely use the data inside. This approach is flexible, supports both simple and complex data, and works reliably due to strict adherence to the Cortex-M4 calling convention.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',5,2,'2025-07-29_03-45-26','Calling osYield from within any context-switching interrupt handler — such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler — is incorrect because osYield is a thread-level API designed to run in thread mode. Internally, osYield sets the PendSV bit to request a context switch, which is only safe when done from thread context.\nIf you invoke it within an interrupt handler (which runs in handler mode using the main stack), you risk triggering nested exceptions. Each nested exception causes the processor to push another hardware stack frame, which can overflow the stack or corrupt system state. More critically, PendSV is typically assigned the lowest interrupt priority and won\'t execute until all other handlers complete. Calling it recursively or while another handler is still executing may result in undefined behavior or a stuck system.\nThis violates the design principles of real-time operating systems and breaks ARM’s defined context separation between thread mode and handler mode. It leads to nondeterministic behavior, corrupted return sequences, and a fundamentally unstable scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',5,3,'2025-07-29_03-45-26','Reducing the OS clock tick from 1ms to 0.1ms increases system responsiveness and scheduling granularity. For instance, osDelay(1) would now pause for just 0.1ms, enabling tighter control over timing-sensitive tasks such as high-frequency sensor sampling, real-time audio, or motor control. Pre-emptive threads also switch more quickly, reducing latency and improving fairness among tasks with shorter deadlines.\nHowever, this precision comes at a cost. A 0.1ms tick means the SysTick interrupt triggers 10,000 times per second instead of 1,000. Each interrupt consumes CPU cycles, and the overhead of frequent context switches reduces the time available for actual work. On slower processors, this may lead to missed ticks or starvation. In power-sensitive applications, the energy cost of frequent wakeups can shorten battery life. The tradeoff is between timing accuracy and efficiency — for many embedded applications, 1ms is a more practical balance.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',5,4,'2025-07-29_03-45-26','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires threads to explicitly call osYield() or a blocking function to give up CPU time. This gives the programmer full control but also responsibility — a misbehaving thread that forgets to yield can monopolize the CPU, freezing the rest of the system. It’s easier to debug, but less robust in large or complex systems.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS uses hardware timers (like SysTick) to automatically interrupt running threads and trigger context switches. This eliminates the need for explicit yielding and ensures that no single thread can block others indefinitely. It enables better responsiveness and fairness, especially for real-time or mixed-priority workloads.\nFrom the OS’s side, cooperative multitasking is simpler: context switches only occur when the thread voluntarily yields, so the OS doesn’t need timer interrupts or context preemption logic. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking requires periodic interrupts, saving and restoring thread contexts, and a more complex scheduler to manage time slices and priorities.\nBoth models are useful. Cooperative multitasking suits simpler systems or trusted code, while pre-emptive multitasking is essential for robust, real-time performance.\n',1,'s25'),('osajid',5,5,'2025-07-29_03-45-26','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching work together because they both feed into the same scheduler, but are triggered differently. Yielding is initiated by the thread itself using osYield(), while pre-emption is initiated by the OS through a timer interrupt like SysTick.\nWhen a thread yields, it voluntarily gives up control, and the scheduler selects the next ready thread. When the SysTick_Handler fires, it decrements the current thread’s runtime counter. If the counter reaches zero, the OS resets it to the thread’s timeslice and triggers a PendSV interrupt for a context switch.\nBoth mechanisms result in the same process: saving the current thread’s state and restoring another’s. Because they share this infrastructure, mixing the two is seamless. This hybrid model allows developers to use osYield when appropriate for cooperative logic, while relying on the OS to enforce fairness and prevent starvation via timed pre-emption.\nThis design enables predictable, flexible multitasking — yielding gives manual control, and pre-emption guarantees forward progress even if a thread misbehaves.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,1,'2025-05-15_11-35-41','The HAL is a hardware abstraction layer. It is useful in connecting the software of the computer, in this case the IDE, with the hardware of the Nucleo microcontroller. It is used within a general embedded project to control machines through transmitting information. For instance, in this lab, numbers were transmitted through a function involving HAL between the hardware and the software. The HAL is not necessarily a necessity, but rather a convenience to operate low-level functions within the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,1,'2025-05-15_17-01-48','The HAL is a hardware abstraction layer. It is useful in connecting the software of the computer, in this case the IDE, with the hardware of the Nucleo microcontroller. It is used within a general embedded project to control machines through transmitting information. For instance, in this lab, numbers were transmitted through a function involving HAL between the hardware and the software. The HAL is not necessarily a necessity, but rather a convenience to operate low-level functions within the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,1,'2025-05-15_17-38-23','The HAL is a hardware abstraction layer. It is useful in connecting the software of the computer, in this case the IDE, with the hardware of the Nucleo microcontroller. It is used within a general embedded project to control machines through transmitting information. For instance, in this lab, numbers were transmitted through a function involving HAL between the hardware and the software. The HAL is not necessarily a necessity, but rather a convenience to operate low-level functions within the hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',1,1,'2025-05-16_09-38-17','The HAL is a hardware abstraction layer. It is useful in connecting the software of the computer, in this case the IDE, with the hardware of the Nucleo microcontroller. It is used within a general embedded project to control machines through transmitting information. For instance, in this lab, numbers were transmitted through a function involving HAL between the hardware and the software. The HAL is not necessarily a necessity, but rather a convenience to operate low-level functions within the hardware. Abstraction is very useful within embedded systems because it utilises a simplified interface rather than accessing specific memory locations in the hardware to retrieve information from the peripherals. However,  HAL may limit understandings of the microcontroller by reducing flexibility with accessing the microcontroller’s full functionality. Nevertheless, for beginners HAL provides a variety of options to get into hardware programming.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,2,'2025-05-15_11-35-41','The __io_putchar function is a function that takes an integer to be output. It converts the information to an 8 bit unsigned integer, from 0 to 255. This is sent as 1 byte over the UART port to eventually show the print statement on the software.  This is needed for debugging to verify that the output from the microcontroller is as expected.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',1,2,'2025-05-15_17-01-48','The __io_putchar function is a function that takes an integer to be output. It converts the information to an 8 bit unsigned integer, from 0 to 255. This is sent as 1 byte over the UART port to eventually show the print statement on the software.  For printf, every individual character goes through the __io_putchar function and is transmitted over the UART serial port. The __io_putchar function also creates consistency among different microcontrollers such as STM32. This is because printf may require additional functionality to operate for certain microcontrollers, however the __io_putchar function takes in the character from the serial port without relying on the printf function. The HAL is connected through the __io_putchar function which connects the microcontroller to the software of the computer highlighting the function’s necessity.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',1,2,'2025-05-15_17-38-23','The __io_putchar function is a function that takes an integer to be output. It converts the information to an 8 bit unsigned integer, from 0 to 255. This is sent as 1 byte over the UART port to eventually show the print statement on the software.  For printf, every individual character goes through the __io_putchar function and is transmitted over the UART serial port. The __io_putchar function also creates consistency among different microcontrollers such as STM32. This is because printf may require additional functionality to operate for certain microcontrollers, however the __io_putchar function takes in the character from the serial port without relying on the printf function. The HAL is connected through the __io_putchar function which connects the microcontroller to the software of the computer highlighting the function’s necessity. The programmer must be able to implement the __io_putchar function for themselves as there may be different system requirements depending on the hardware used. As an example, huart2 within the function is an instance of the UART meaning that the hardware may impact which huart should be accessed for HAL initialization. Another thing that can affect the __io_putchar function from a hardware aspect is the size of information where the microcontroller may differ from how many bytes of data can be sent at a time. Hardware requirements will change based on the project being worked on, so understanding how functions like __io_putchar can still access data is vital.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,2,'2025-05-16_09-38-17','The __io_putchar function is a function that takes an integer to be output. It converts the information to an 8 bit unsigned integer, from 0 to 255. This is sent as 1 byte over the UART port to eventually show the print statement on the software.  For printf, every individual character goes through the __io_putchar function and is transmitted over the UART serial port. The __io_putchar function also creates consistency among different microcontrollers such as STM32. This is because printf may require additional functionality to operate for certain microcontrollers, however the __io_putchar function takes in the character from the serial port without relying on the printf function. The HAL is connected through the __io_putchar function which connects the microcontroller to the software of the computer highlighting the function’s necessity. The programmer must be able to implement the __io_putchar function for themselves as there may be different system requirements depending on the hardware used. As an example, huart2 within the function is an instance of the UART meaning that the hardware may impact which huart should be accessed for HAL initialization. Another thing that can affect the __io_putchar function from a hardware aspect is the size of information where the microcontroller may differ from how many bytes of data can be sent at a time. Hardware requirements will change based on the project being worked on, so understanding how functions like __io_putchar can still access data is vital.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,3,'2025-05-15_11-35-41','Debuggers help to get step-by-step throughout the code and find what and where the errors are. For instance, break points as used on the while statement would tell the debugger how far to look for errors. Just using a printf statement will either show a correct result, wrong result, or an error. To add on, a printf statement may be lost in transmitting from the microcontroller not allowing the user to check if there is an error in code execution. A debugger is also much more useful for fixing issues in the code at various points.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',1,3,'2025-05-15_17-01-48','Debuggers are needed to examine register values. Debuggers help to get step-by-step throughout the code and find what and where the errors are. For instance, break points as used on the while statement would tell the debugger how far to look for errors. Just using a printf statement will either show a correct result, wrong result, or an error as it operates at a high-level. Prinf cannot find errors in the transmitting of information from the serial port and cannot access register values as register values are part of the hardware. Printf will just take information from memory. A debugger is also much more useful for fixing issues in the code at various points.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,3,'2025-05-15_17-38-23','Debuggers are needed to examine register values. Debuggers help to get step-by-step throughout the code and find what and where the errors are. For instance, break points as used on the while statement would tell the debugger how far to look for errors. Just using a printf statement will either show a correct result, wrong result, or an error as it operates at a high-level. Prinf cannot find errors in the transmitting of information from the serial port and cannot access register values as register values are part of the hardware. Printf will just take information from memory. A debugger is also much more useful for fixing issues in the code at various points.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,3,'2025-05-16_09-38-17','Debuggers are needed to examine register values. Debuggers help to get step-by-step throughout the code and find what and where the errors are. For instance, break points as used on the while statement would tell the debugger how far to look for errors. Just using a printf statement will either show a correct result, wrong result, or an error as it operates at a high-level. Prinf cannot find errors in the transmitting of information from the serial port and cannot access register values as register values are part of the hardware. Printf will just take information from memory. A debugger is also much more useful for fixing issues in the code at various points.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,4,'2025-05-15_11-35-41','The stack allocation method works by using the main stack for introduction processes like interrupts. The thread 1 stacks are based on context and for storing information given by the users. Based on the code, the new value for PSP_val is subtracting 0x400 from MSP_init_val. The initial location of MSP as a register is required so that the data can be accessed to calculate the PSP value.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',1,4,'2025-05-15_17-01-48','The stack allocation method works by using the main stacks and thread stacks to store information given by the users. Based on the code, the new value for PSP_val is subtracting 0x400 from MSP_init_val. If the offset is present, but MSP_init_val is not in a valid location, the PSP_val may replace other important information in memory leading to memory corruption. The initial location of MSP as a register is required so that the data can be accessed to calculate the PSP value. Nevertheless, the offset can be useful in setting PSP_val in a location that utilises the stack efficiently to allow for other registers to be pushed onto the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,4,'2025-05-15_17-38-23','The stack allocation method works by using the main stacks and thread stacks to store information given by the users. Based on the code, the new value for PSP_val is subtracting 0x400 from MSP_init_val. If the offset is present, but MSP_init_val is not in a valid location, the PSP_val may replace other important information in memory leading to memory corruption. The initial location of MSP as a register is required so that the data can be accessed to calculate the PSP value. Nevertheless, the offset can be useful in setting PSP_val in a location that utilises the stack efficiently to allow for other registers to be pushed onto the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,4,'2025-05-16_09-38-17','The stack allocation method works by using the main stacks and thread stacks to store information given by the users. Based on the code, the new value for PSP_val is subtracting 0x400 from MSP_init_val. If the offset is present, but MSP_init_val is not in a valid location, the PSP_val may replace other important information in memory leading to memory corruption. The initial location of MSP as a register is required so that the data can be accessed to calculate the PSP value. Nevertheless, the offset can be useful in setting PSP_val in a location that utilises the stack efficiently to allow for other registers to be pushed onto the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',1,5,'2025-05-15_11-35-41','The size in each stack will differ depending on the information involved with the threads. After finding out the size, sum the sizes up to get the total amount of memory required to hold all the stacks. The memory should then be allocated for the threads. Since the threads may contain a variety of information such as int which takes up 4 bytes, padding and alignment of memory should be utilised to ensure correct starting of new stacks.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',1,5,'2025-05-15_17-01-48','The first step for stack allocation is defining a valid base address for the stack region. The size in each stack, compile-time, will differ depending on the information involved with the threads. After finding out the size, sum the sizes up to get the total amount of memory required to hold all the stacks. Each additional stack can be offset from the base stack to store the information. For instance, one could subtract 0x400 from each additional stack keeping in mind that each stack will not have information in bytes greater than 0x400. The base stack must be a valid location as this will allow the offset stacks to also be valid and use memory efficiently.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',1,5,'2025-05-15_17-38-23','The first step for stack allocation is defining a valid base address for the stack region. The size in each stack, compile-time, will differ depending on the information involved with the threads. After finding out the size, sum the sizes up to get the total amount of memory required to hold all the stacks. Each additional stack can be offset from the base stack to store the information. For instance, one could subtract 0x400 from each additional stack by ensuring that the memory size of the largest stack is less than 0x400. The base stack should be at the highest point in memory and further stack pointers should be placed downwards for accessing lower addresses. The base stack must be a valid location as this will allow the offset stacks to also be valid and use memory efficiently. If the stack size varies too much, dynamic memory allocation can be alternative by expanding to prevent stack overflow through the malloc() function. Every stack should be aligned to multiples of bytes and the stack addresses should not interfere with other essential processes like interrupts.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',1,5,'2025-05-16_09-38-17','The first step for stack allocation is defining a valid base address for the stack region. The size in each stack will differ depending on the information involved with the threads. After finding out the size, sum the sizes up to get the total amount of memory required to hold all the stacks. Each additional stack can be offset from the base stack to store the information. The offset can be calculated to match byte-alignment and taking the largest stack size into consideration. For instance, one could find an offset value such as 0x400 if the memory size of the largest stack is 0x400. Additional stacks would be 2 times 0x400 subtracted from the base stack address, then 3 times, and so forth until all stacks are accounted for. The base stack should be at the highest point in memory and further stack pointers should be placed downwards for accessing lower addresses. The base stack must be a valid location as this will allow the offset stacks to also be valid and use memory efficiently. Since, the stacks are known at compile-time, a contiguous block of memory with a size large enough to fit all the stacks can be allocated for effective memory usage. In general, the stack should be aligned to multiples of bytes and the stack addresses should not interfere with other essential processes like interrupts.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,1,'2025-05-27_18-06-23','When PC held the print continuously function, the function still operated as expected without a direct call to the print continuously function. The “MOV” aspect changed the register of which the value is stored from R0 to PC. This is because R0 contains the output from calling the function directly, however since the value is null through being a comment in the code, the R0 memory is still not freed. In the jumpAssembly function the void* helps to change the memory address to be able to call the print continuously function when wrapped inside the jumpAssembly function.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',2,1,'2025-05-27_20-38-38','When PC held the print continuously function, the function still operated as expected without a direct call to the print continuously function. PC is set to hold the address of what was inside R0 to then start executing the print continuously function provided within the parameter without a return path available. The function is then run immediately from the PC register. The “MOV” aspect changed the register of which the value is stored from R0 to PC. The information being retrieved from the PC register means that no returns would be possible as at the PC register, the R0 instructions are being run. A call frame is not produced for the function and no additional memory is created, the PC just accesses the instructions already stored.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',2,1,'2025-05-27_21-26-12','When PC held the print continuously function, the function still operated as expected without a direct call to the print continuously function. A function seeks to separate chunks of logic where a function call will execute logic or in some cases can output values. PC is set to hold the address of what was inside R0 to then start executing the print continuously function provided without a return path available. This is beneficial for testing a certain amount of logic within a program promptly as the function is then run immediately from the PC register instead of calling the function directly. The “MOV” aspect changed the register of which the value is stored from R0 to PC. A call frame is not produced for the function and no additional memory is created, the PC just accesses the instructions already stored.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,1,'2025-05-28_11-32-35','When PC held the print continuously function, the function still operated as expected without a direct call to the print continuously function. A function seeks to separate chunks of logic where a function call will execute logic or in some cases can output values. PC is set to hold the address of what was inside R0 to then executing the print continuously function provided without a return path available. This is beneficial for testing a certain amount of logic within a program promptly as the function is then run immediately from the PC register instead of calling the function directly. In comparison, a function call will take more time, but there is a return path. The “MOV” aspect changed the register of which the value is stored from R0 to PC through copying the data over. A call frame is not produced for the function and no additional memory is created, the PC just accesses the instructions already stored.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',2,1,'2025-05-28_11-56-35','When PC held the print continuously function, the function still operated as expected without a 