rations. To expand, the kernel can check for misuse or illegal operations. System call functions includes a parameter that allows for the associated set of instructions to be executed. From there, an interrupt is set forth to trigger a pause in normal execution of code to then opt for a different operation, in this case, system-level operation. For instance, when “SVC #3”, Case 3 will allow for the printing of a statement. Since it is a system call, the system pauses the other requests being processed to put its priority to print the statement.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,2,'2025-05-29_13-34-47','A system call is a form of a function call but accesses privileged operations instead of general user mode functions. A system call accesses the kernel of the computer through switching to supervision mode and acting as a controlled bridge from user programs. System calls are necessary to utilise interrupts and access hardware through requesting services from the kernel. Services are requested through a trap instruction like SVC which performs the operations. Interrupts are useful for scheduling tasks so that a specific request is completed first by means of the SVC Handler function. A resulting issue could be the computer being stuck running a program that will continuously overwrite memory and cause harm to the OS. In general, system calls allow the CPU to manage resources effectively to enforce security. An example of a system call increasing security is through the system call only using trusted code to perform critical operations. To expand, the kernel can check for misuse or illegal operations. System call functions includes a parameter that allows for the associated set of instructions to be executed. From there, an interrupt is set forth to trigger a pause in normal execution of code to then opt for a different operation, in this case, system-level operation. For instance, when “SVC #3”, Case 3 will allow for the printing of a statement. Since it is a system call, the system pauses the other requests being processed to put its priority to print the statement.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,3,'2025-05-27_18-06-23','The thread’s stack was set up where the top of the stack to the bottom is xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3-0, R11-4. Setting up a thread’s stack involves assigning values to all of the registers listed above for expected use. Hence, 14 dummy values are assigned to majority of the registers. xPSR represents the top of the stack and is set for validity to be popped from the stack. Next, PC points to the function being called which is the infinite print statement for this code. The stackptr points to the different registers to assign values to in the thread stack. It is crucial to store register values on the stack, so the information is not replaced and the CPU does not deviate from the normal execution of the thread stack.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',2,3,'2025-05-27_20-38-38','The thread’s stack was set up where the top of the stack to the bottom is xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3-0, R11-4. Setting up a thread’s stack involves allocating a valid memory location and then using an offset to store and push the register information in the thread stack. Hence, 14 dummy values are assigned to majority of the registers. xPSR represents the top of the stack and is set for validity to be popped from the stack. Next, PC points to the function being called which is the infinite print statement for this code. The stackptr points to the different registers to assign values to the thread stack. It is crucial to store register values on the stack, so the information is not replaced and the CPU does not deviate from the normal execution of the thread stack. To elaborate, the execution of the full state of the thread stack can be resumed depending on the priority of tasks where no data or information will be lost. As an example, there can be interrupts with system calls that focuses on the printing of a statement first, then may want to resume the thread stack afterwards.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,3,'2025-05-27_21-26-12','The thread’s stack was set up where the top of the stack to the bottom is xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3-0, R11-4. Setting up a thread’s stack involves allocating a valid memory location and then using an offset to store and push the register information in the thread stack. Hence, 14 dummy values are assigned to majority of the registers. xPSR represents the top of the stack and is set for validity to be popped from the stack. Next, PC points to the function being called which is the infinite print statement for this code. The stackptr points to the different registers to assign values to the thread stack. It is crucial to store register values on the stack, so the information is not replaced and the CPU does not deviate from the normal execution of the thread stack. To elaborate, the execution of the full state of the thread stack can be resumed depending on the priority of tasks where no data or information will be lost. As an example, there can be interrupts with system calls that focuses on the printing of a statement first, then may want to resume the thread stack afterwards.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,3,'2025-05-28_11-32-35','The thread’s stack was set up where the top of the stack to the bottom is xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3-0, R11-4. Setting up a thread’s stack involves allocating a valid memory location and then using an offset to store and push the register information in the thread stack. Hence, 14 dummy values are assigned to majority of the registers. xPSR represents the top of the stack and is set for validity to be popped from the stack. Next, PC points to the function being called which is the infinite print statement for this code. The stackptr points to the different registers to assign values to the thread stack. It is crucial to store register values on the stack, so the information is not replaced and the CPU does not deviate from the normal execution of the thread stack. To elaborate, the execution of the full state of the thread stack can be resumed depending on the priority of tasks where no data or information will be lost. As an example, there can be interrupts with system calls that focuses on the printing of a statement first, then may want to resume the thread stack afterwards.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,3,'2025-05-28_11-56-35','The thread’s stack was set up where the top of the stack to the bottom is xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3-0, R11-4. Setting up a thread’s stack involves allocating a valid memory location and then using an offset to store and push the register information in the thread stack. Hence, 14 dummy values are assigned to majority of the registers. xPSR represents the top of the stack and is set for validity to be popped from the stack. Next, PC points to the function being called which is the infinite print statement for this code. The stackptr points to the different registers to assign values to the thread stack. It is crucial to store register values on the stack, so the information is not replaced and the CPU does not deviate from the normal execution of the thread stack. To elaborate, the execution of the full state of the thread stack can be resumed depending on the priority of tasks where no data or information will be lost. As an example, there can be interrupts with system calls that focuses on the printing of a statement first, then may want to resume the thread stack afterwards.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,3,'2025-05-28_18-18-00','The thread’s stack was set up where the top of the stack to the bottom is xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3-0, R11-4. Setting up a thread’s stack involves allocating a valid memory location and then using an offset to store and push the register information in the thread stack. Hence, 14 dummy values are assigned to majority of the registers. xPSR represents the top of the stack and is set for validity to be popped from the stack. Next, PC points to the function being called which is the infinite print statement for this code. The stackptr points to the different registers to assign values to the thread stack. It is crucial to store register values on the stack, so the information is not replaced and the CPU does not deviate from the normal execution of the thread stack. To elaborate, the execution of the full state of the thread stack can be resumed depending on the priority of tasks where no data or information will be lost. As an example, there can be interrupts with system calls that focuses on the printing of a statement first, then may want to resume the thread stack afterwards.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,3,'2025-05-29_13-34-47','The thread’s stack was set up where the top of the stack to the bottom is xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3-0, R11-4. Setting up a thread’s stack involves allocating a valid memory location and then using an offset to store and push the register information in the thread stack. Hence, 14 dummy values are assigned to majority of the registers. xPSR represents the top of the stack and is set for validity to be popped from the stack. Next, PC points to the function being called which is the infinite print statement for this code. The stackptr points to the different registers to assign values to the thread stack. It is crucial to store register values on the stack, so the information is not replaced and the CPU does not deviate from the normal execution of the thread stack. To elaborate, the execution of the full state of the thread stack can be resumed depending on the priority of tasks where no data or information will be lost. As an example, there can be interrupts with system calls that focuses on the printing of a statement first, then may want to resume the thread stack afterwards.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,4,'2025-05-27_18-06-23','If PSP is not set to the stackptr, then the PSP will be accessing invalid memory which can corrupt data and crash the code. Since stackptr allows the code to iterate through the different register values, if PSP is not set to it, the values cannot be assigned to the thread stack. This leads to an invalid thread stack that deviates from expected use as PSP helps to point to the top of the stack. The runFirstThread function relies on PSP to utilise the thread stack, so when it is called directly after the line to set PSP to stackptr, the code could also crash from that point.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',2,4,'2025-05-27_20-38-38','To understand the utility of PSP, the difference between PSP and MSP is that the MSP is used in kernel mode, while PSP is for user mode tasks. Therefore, for managing values in a thread stack PSP is essential as MSP deals with system-level operations. If PSP is not set to the stack pointer, then the PSP will be accessing invalid memory which can corrupt data and crash the code or cause a hardfault. If PSP is not initialised, garbage from previous operations can still be stored in the memory affecting the expected output. Since the stack pointer allows the code to iterate through the different register values, if PSP is not set to it, the values cannot be assigned to the memory address of the thread stack. This leads to an invalid thread stack that deviates from expected use as PSP helps to point to the top of the stack. The runFirstThread function relies on PSP to utilise the thread stack, so when it is called directly after the line to set PSP to the stack pointer, the code could also crash from that point.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,4,'2025-05-27_21-26-12','To understand the utility of PSP, the difference between PSP and MSP is that the MSP is used in kernel mode, while PSP is for user mode tasks. Therefore, for managing values in a thread stack PSP is essential as MSP deals with system-level operations. If PSP is not set to the stack pointer, then the PSP will be accessing invalid memory which can corrupt data and crash the code or cause a hardfault. If PSP is not initialised, garbage from previous operations can still be stored in the memory affecting the expected output. Since the stack pointer allows the code to iterate through the different register values, if PSP is not set to it, the values cannot be assigned to the memory address of the thread stack. This leads to an invalid thread stack that deviates from expected use as PSP helps to point to the top of the stack. The runFirstThread function relies on PSP to utilise the thread stack, so when it is called directly after the line to set PSP to the stack pointer, the code could also crash from that point.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',2,4,'2025-05-28_11-32-35','To understand the utility of PSP, the difference between PSP and MSP is that the MSP is used in kernel mode, while PSP is for user mode tasks. Therefore, for managing values in a thread stack, PSP is essential as MSP deals with system-level operations. It is important to distinguish between the memory location of the CPU’s stack (MSP) and the thread’s stack so that unintended system-level operations do not occur. If PSP is not set to the stack pointer, then the PSP will be accessing invalid memory which can corrupt data and crash the code or cause a hardfault. If PSP is not initialised, garbage from previous operations can still be stored in the memory affecting the expected output. Since the stack pointer allows the code to iterate through the different register values, if PSP is not set to it, the values cannot be assigned to the memory address of the thread stack. This leads to an invalid thread stack that deviates from expected use as PSP helps to point to the top of the stack highlighting the necessity of PSP initialization. The runFirstThread function relies on PSP to utilise the thread stack, so when it is called directly after the line to set PSP to the stack pointer, the code could also crash from that point.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,4,'2025-05-28_11-56-35','To understand the utility of PSP, the difference between PSP and MSP is that the MSP is used in kernel mode, while PSP is for user mode tasks. Therefore, for managing values in a 