to an invalid thread stack that deviates from expected use as PSP helps to point to the top of the stack highlighting the necessity of PSP initialization. The runFirstThread function relies on PSP to utilise the thread stack, so when it is called directly after the line to set PSP to the stack pointer, the code could also crash from that point.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,4,'2025-05-29_13-34-47','To understand the utility of PSP, the difference between PSP and MSP is that the MSP is used in kernel mode, while PSP is for user mode tasks. Therefore, for managing values in a thread stack, PSP is essential as MSP deals with system-level operations. It is important to distinguish between the memory location of the CPU’s stack (MSP) and the thread’s stack so that unintended system-level operations do not occur. If PSP is not set to the stack pointer, then the PSP will be accessing invalid memory which can corrupt data and crash the code or cause a hardfault. If PSP is not initialised, garbage from previous operations can still be stored in the memory affecting the expected output. Since the stack pointer allows the code to iterate through the different register values, if PSP is not set to it, the values cannot be assigned to the memory address of the thread stack. This leads to an invalid thread stack that deviates from expected use as PSP helps to point to the top of the stack highlighting the necessity of PSP initialization. The runFirstThread function relies on PSP to utilise the thread stack, so when it is called directly after the line to set PSP to the stack pointer, the code could also crash from that point.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,5,'2025-05-27_18-06-23','The code would crash because of the 14 dummy values set to the registers besides PC and xPSR. If printing something and returning once, xPSR would be popped, then PC would be popped for the execution of the function. After that, there will be just 14 dummy values remaining in the thread stack. In the infinite loop, the stack was stuck on PC as the function never returned anything, so the dummy values in the other registers never had to be accessed. LR, next in stack after PC, utilises 0xA as an address, but there is no set of instructions assigned at memory address leading to a fault in code.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',2,5,'2025-05-27_20-38-38','The code would crash because of the 14 dummy values set to the registers besides PC and xPSR. If printing something and returning once, xPSR would be popped, then PC would be popped for the execution of the function. After that, there will be just 14 dummy values remaining in the thread stack. In the infinite loop, the stack was stuck on PC as the function never returned anything, so the dummy values in the other registers never had to be accessed. LR, next in stack after PC, utilises 0xA as an address, but there is no set of instructions assigned at memory address leading to a hardfault. Hardfault is an execution-based error where it is likely to occur on a Cortex-M4 from strict memory address guidelines for alignment. To add on, there is no return address stored on the thread’s stack so the execution of the stack cannot continue. The return address is missing as a thread either runs forever or exits, meaning that LR, the next register after PC, will not be accessed.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,5,'2025-05-27_21-26-12','The code would crash because of the 14 dummy values set to the registers besides PC and xPSR. If printing something and returning once, xPSR would be popped, then PC would be popped for the execution of the function. After that, there will be just 14 dummy values remaining in the thread stack. In the infinite loop, the stack was stuck on PC as the function never returned anything, so the dummy values in the other registers never had to be accessed. LR, next in stack after PC, utilises 0xA as an address, but there is no set of instructions assigned at memory address leading to a hardfault. Hardfault is an execution-based error where it is likely to occur on a Cortex-M4 from strict memory address guidelines for alignment. To add on, there is no return address stored on the thread’s stack so the execution of the stack cannot continue. The return address is missing as a thread either runs forever or exits, meaning that LR, the next register after PC, will not be accessed.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,5,'2025-05-28_11-32-35','The code would crash because of the 14 dummy values set to the registers besides PC and xPSR. If printing something and returning once, xPSR would be popped, then PC would be popped for the execution of the function. After that, there will be just 14 dummy values of 0xA remaining in the thread stack. In the infinite loop, the stack was stuck on PC as the function never returned anything, so the dummy values in the other registers never had to be accessed. LR, next in stack after PC, utilises 0xA as an address, but there is no set of instructions assigned at memory address leading to a hardfault. Hardfault is an execution-based error where it is likely to occur on a Cortex-M4 from strict memory address guidelines for alignment. To add on, there is no return address stored on the thread’s stack so the execution of the stack cannot continue. The return address is missing as a thread either runs forever or exits, meaning that LR, the next register after PC, will not be accessed.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,5,'2025-05-28_11-56-35','The code would crash because of the 14 dummy values set to the registers besides PC and xPSR. If printing something and returning once, xPSR would be popped, then PC would be popped for the execution of the function. After that, there will be just 14 dummy values of 0xA remaining in the thread stack. In the infinite loop, the stack was stuck on PC as the function never returned anything, so the dummy values in the other registers never had to be accessed. LR, next in stack after PC, utilises 0xA as an address, but there is no set of instructions assigned at memory address leading to a hardfault. Hardfault is an execution-based error where it is likely to occur on a Cortex-M4 from strict memory address guidelines for alignment. To add on, there is no return address stored on the thread’s stack so the execution of the stack cannot continue. The return address is missing as a thread either runs forever or exits, meaning that LR, the next register after PC, will not be accessed.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,5,'2025-05-28_18-18-00','The code would crash because of the 14 dummy values set to the registers besides PC and xPSR. If printing something and returning once, xPSR would be popped, then PC would be popped for the execution of the function. After that, there will be just 14 dummy values of 0xA remaining in the thread stack. In the infinite loop, the stack was stuck on PC as the function never returned anything, so the dummy values in the other registers never had to be accessed. LR, next in stack after PC, utilises 0xA as an address, but there is no set of instructions assigned at memory address leading to a hardfault. Hardfault is an execution-based error where it is likely to occur on a Cortex-M4 from strict memory address guidelines for alignment. To add on, there is no return address stored on the thread’s stack so the execution of the stack cannot continue. The return address is missing as a thread either runs forever or exits, meaning that LR, the next register after PC, will not be accessed.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',2,5,'2025-05-29_13-34-47','The code would crash because of the 14 dummy values set to the registers besides PC and xPSR. If printing something and returning once, xPSR would be popped, then PC would be popped for the execution of the function. After that, there will be just 14 dummy values of 0xA remaining in the thread stack. In the infinite loop, the stack was stuck on PC as the function never returned anything, so the dummy values in the other registers never had to be accessed. LR, next in stack after PC, utilises 0xA as an address, but there is no set of instructions assigned at memory address leading to a hardfault. Hardfault is an execution-based error where it is likely to occur on a Cortex-M4 from strict memory address guidelines for alignment. To add on, there is no return address stored on the thread’s stack so the execution of the stack cannot continue. The return address is missing as a thread either runs forever or exits, meaning that LR, the next register after PC, will not be accessed.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,1,'2025-06-24_14-23-48','Multiple c files will have its individually associated header files with declarations of the functions along with definitions. Each c file will be compiled into an object file. Additionally, there are two memory-based files, sysmem.c along with the .ld script for RAM. These help to manage the memory of the information given to the system, including variables and constants. The .ld is a linker file to define the memory layout and handle symbols between files.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',3,1,'2025-06-24_18-48-00','Before actual compilation begins, the preprocessor handles all lines beginning with #, such as #include, #define, and conditional compilation directives like #ifndef. It expands macros, inserts header file contents, and prepares the code for compilation. Multiple c files will have its individually associated header files with declarations of the functions along with definitions. Then, each c file will be compiled into their corresponding object files. These help to manage the memory of the information given to the system, including variables and constants. Finally, the linker file takes all the object files and combines it into a single executable. It resolves external references and handles memory addresses for functions and global variables. It also incorporates standard libraries and uses a linker script with .ld to define the memory layout of the executable.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,1,'2025-06-24_19-19-24','Before actual compilation begins, the preprocessor handles all lines beginning with #, such as #include, #define, and conditional compilation directives like #ifndef. It expands macros, inserts header file contents, and prepares the code for compilation. Multiple c files will have its individually associated header files to have both definitions and declarations of functions. Then, each c file will be compiled into their corresponding object files. These help to manage the memory of the information given to the system, including variables and constants. Finally, the linker file takes all the object files and combines it into a single executable. It resolves external references and handles memory addresses for functions and global variables. It also incorporates standard libraries and uses a linker script with .ld to define the memory layout of the executable.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,1,'2025-06-25_16-10-58','Before actual compilation begins, the preprocessor handles all lines beginning with #, such as #include, #define, and conditional compilation directives like #ifndef. It expands macros, inserts header file contents, and prepares the code for compilation. Multiple c files will have its individually associated header files to have both definitions and declarations of functions. Then, each c file will be compiled into their corresponding object files. These help to manage the memory of the information given to the system, including variables and constants. Finally, the linker file takes all the object files and combines it into a single executable. It resolves external references and handles memory addresses for functions and global variables. It also incorporates standard libraries and uses a linker script with .ld to define the memory layout of the executable.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,2,'2025-06-24_14-23-48','Since multiple c files get compiled into a single executable, #include for the same kernel files could cause code disruption. There would also be no benefit in calling the c file as the point of the code organisation is to separate the kernel c files and the user c files. If wanting to use #include <kernel.c>, the code from that file might as well be placed in the main.c file. The c files are solely meant for definitions and not declarations, it is just a place to store instructions to be used automatically through the object file.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',3,2,'2025-06-24_18-48-00','Since multiple c files get compiled into a single executable through the combination of object files, #include for the same kernel files could cause code disruption.  To add on, this practice can result in multiple definitions for the same function leading to linker errors. For instance, a linker error could be that the linker does not know which definition of the function to utilise as there are multiple definitions for the same symbol. The c files are solely meant for definitions and not declarations. The definitions are the instructions to be implemented when a function is called and can allocate storage. On the other hand, declaration informs the compiler about the existence of the function generally through a header file.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,2,'2025-06-24_19-19-24','Since multiple c files get compiled into a single executable through the combination of object files, #include for the same kernel files could cause code disruption.  To add on, this practice can result in multiple definitions for the same function leading to linker errors. For instance, a linker error could be that the linker does not know which definition of the function to utilise as there are multiple definitions for the same symbol. The c files are solely meant for definitions and not declarations. The definitions are the instructions to be implemented when a function is called and can allocate storage. On the other hand, declaration informs the compiler about the existence of the function generally through a header file.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,2,'2025-06-25_16-10-58','Since multiple c files get compiled into a single executable through the combination of object files, #include for the same kernel files could cause code disruption.  To add on, this practice can result in multiple definitions for the same function leading to linker errors. For instance, a linker error could be that the linker does not know which definition of the function to utilise as there are multiple definitions for the same symbol. The c files are solely meant for definitions and not declarations. The definitions are the instructions to be implemented when a function is called and can allocate storage. On the other hand, declaration informs the compiler about the existence of the function generally through a header file.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,3,'2025-06-24_14-23-48','To set up and start a thread, a stack pointer must first be created	 with reference to a valid start location. For instance, starting with an offset from MSP initial value. From there, a thread struct should be set up and initialized including a stack pointer and somewhere to point to the function being executed. After that, the thread should be checked to see if it can be successfully created. If it can be successfully created, then inputs should be added for the xPSR, PC, and 14 other registers. The struct should be updated after this. Finally, a thread should be run by means of an SVC handler and an environment to run the thread such as stack operations that can navigate through the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',3,3,'2025-06-24_18-48-00','To start off with, the address of the function the thread should execute should be given. This is because the PC register would be set to this address for execution. The developer should be writing the function to be executed. To set up and start a thread, a stack pointer must be created with reference to a valid start location. For instance, starting with an offset from MSP initial value. From there, the kernel will manage the lifecycle of the thread. To register the thread with the kernel, the kernel must store its Thread Control Block in a scheduling structure. A thread struct should be set up and initialized including a stack pointer and somewhere to point to the function being executed. After that, the thread should be checked to see if it can be successfully created. If it can be successfully created, then inputs should be added for the xPSR, PC, and 14 other registers. The struct should be updated after this. Finally, a thread should be run by means of an SVC handler and an environment to run the thread such as stack operations that can navigate through the thread. Utilising the SVC Handler corresponds to calling the RunFirstThread() function. In general, the 3 main functions for the ease of thread implementation are initialization, creation, and running. These are called by the user to activate the thread stack and retrieve the desired results from it.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',3,3,'2025-06-24_19-19-24','To start off with, the address of the function the thread should execute should be given. This is because the PC register would be set to this address for execution. The developer should be writing the function to be executed. The kernel will manage the lifecycle of the thread. To register the thread with the kernel, the kernel must store its Thread Control Block in a scheduling structure. The initialization of the kernel’s internal data structures should be done first. If the thread can be successfully created, a kernel API requests the kernel create a new thread to run the function, where the call includes the function’s address and stack sizes. A thread should be run by means of an SVC handler and an environment where the kernel can manage and execute threads. In general, the 3 main functions called by the user for the ease of thread implementation are initialization, creation, and running. For threads, the user simply writes thread logic and interacts with the system through high-level function calls, while the kernel handles low-level details like handling registers.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',3,3,'2025-06-25_16-10-58','To start off with, the address of the function the thread should execute should be given. This is because the PC register would be set to this address for execution. The developer should be writing the function to be executed. The kernel will manage the lifecycle of the thread. To register the thread with the kernel, the kernel must store its Thread Control Block in a scheduling structure. The initialization of the kernel’s internal data structures should be done first. If the thread can be successfully created, a function requests the kernel create a new thread to run the function, where the call includes the function’s address and stack sizes. A thread should be run by means of an SVC handler and an environment where the kernel can manage and execute threads which is started by a kernel_start() function. This specific  function call starts the kernel through a system call to run the thread. In general, the 3 main functions called by the user for the ease of thread implementation are initialization, creation, and running. For threads, the user simply writes thread logic and interacts with the system through high-level function calls, while the kernel handles low-level details like handling registers.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,4,'2025-06-24_14-23-48','Stack pool is defined as the RAM space available within the system. In this lab, it was considered as 0x4000, while a thread only takes up 0x400. It can be used in a future lab to keep it as a maximum when creating new threads that are being added through a stack pointer consistently pointing downwards. 0x4000 acts as the maximum space for the multiple threads to be added and the available space can be iteratively deducted from 0x4000, to ensure the threads will not take up more space than capacity.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',3,4,'2025-06-24_18-48-00','The stack pool refers to a reserved region of RAM that the operating system (OS) uses to allocate individual stacks for threads. In this lab, it was considered as 0x4000 bytes, while each thread has its private stack space of 0x400 bytes. The OS is responsible for managing this stack pool by assigning a unique stack region to each thread during thread creation. This process can be done using static allocation, where fixed-size blocks are assigned from the pool, or dynamic allocation, where the OS tracks used and free memory regions to fit stack sizes as needed. In this scenario, a static allocation approach is used where each new thread receives the next available chunk of memory, moving downward. The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is used by each thread to keep track of its current stack position. The stack typically grows downward, meaning the stack pointer assigned to PSP starts at the top of the thread\'s allocated stack space and decreases as data is pushed onto the stack. The OS can track a base address for the stack pool and subtract fixed offsets of 0x400 bytes to assign each new thread a safe, non-overlapping stack region. 0x4000 bytes acts as the maximum space for the multiple threads to be added and the available space can be iteratively deducted from 0x4000 bytes, to ensure that the new stacks do not exceed the total pool space.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,4,'2025-06-24_19-19-24','The stack pool refers to a reserved region of RAM that the operating system (OS) uses to allocate individual stacks for threads. In this lab, it was considered as 0x4000 bytes, while each thread has its private stack space of 0x400 bytes. The OS is responsible for managing this stack pool by assigning a unique stack region to each thread during thread creation. This process can be done using static allocation, where fixed-size blocks are assigned from the pool, or dynamic allocation, where the OS tracks used and free memory regions to fit stack sizes as needed. In this scenario, a static allocation approach is used where each new thread receives the next available chunk of memory, moving downward. The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is used by each thread to keep track of its current stack position. The stack typically grows downward, meaning the stack pointer assigned to PSP starts at the top of the thread\'s allocated stack space and decreases as data is pushed onto the stack. The OS can track a base address for the stack pool and subtract fixed offsets of 0x400 bytes to assign each new thread a safe, non-overlapping stack region. 0x4000 bytes acts as the maximum space for the multiple threads to be added and the available space can be iteratively deducted from 0x4000 bytes, to ensure that the new stacks do not exceed the total pool space.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,4,'2025-06-25_16-10-58','The stack pool refers to a reserved region of RAM that the operating system (OS) uses to allocate individual stacks for threads. In this lab, it was considered as 0x4000 bytes, while each thread has its private stack space of 0x400 bytes. The OS is responsible for managing this stack pool by assigning a unique stack region to each thread during thread creation. This process can be done using static allocation, where fixed-size blocks are assigned from the pool, or dynamic allocation, where the OS tracks used and free memory regions to fit stack sizes as needed. In this scenario, a static allocation approach is used where each new thread receives the next available chunk of memory, moving downward. The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is used by each thread to keep track of its current stack position. The stack typically grows downward, meaning the stack pointer assigned to PSP starts at the top of the thread\'s allocated stack space and decreases as data is pushed onto the stack. The OS can track a base address for the stack pool and subtract fixed offsets of 0x400 bytes to assign each new thread a safe, non-overlapping stack region. 0x4000 bytes acts as the maximum space for the multiple threads to be added and the available space can be iteratively deducted from 0x4000 bytes, to ensure that the new stacks do not exceed the total pool space.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,5,'2025-06-24_14-23-48','Kernel is a more privileged system and is used for system call implementations. Hence, the SVC handler function goes into the kernel files. Additionally, the kernel should know what each thread stack looks like through its memory allocation with stack pointers. Therefore, the three functions, involving initialising the thread, creating the thread, and running the thread are all placed within the kernel files. On the other hand, user files are for code that does not directly access hardware or protected memory. For instance, the printf function can be placed within the main.c, user file. This distinction is important because it can harm the system in using the kernel in a dangerous way. Since, it is extremely privileged, only in certain operations should the kernel be utilised as memory can be accidentally corrupted.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',3,5,'2025-06-24_18-48-00','Kernel is a more privileged system and is used for system call implementations. Hence, the SVC handler function goes into the kernel files. Additionally, the kernel should know what each thread stack looks like through its memory allocation with stack pointers. Therefore, the three functions, involving initialising the thread, creating the thread, and running the thread are all placed within the kernel files. These functions must interact directly with hardware, manage memory, and control processor modes which all  require elevated permissions. On the other hand, user files are for code that does not directly access hardware or protected memory. From the user\'s perspective, their primary responsibility is to write the thread functions and interact with the kernel through defined API calls. The distinction between kernel and user is important because it can harm the system by using the kernel in a dangerous way. This separation between user and kernel space is crucial. For example, it simplifies application development by keeping complex, hardware-specific operations separately through kernel files. It also protects system stability and security, since user code cannot accidentally lead to system corruption. The distinction is essential to ensure higher-level developers are not working on low-level code and can focus on desired operations without increasing the risk of bugs.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,5,'2025-06-24_19-19-24','Kernel is a more privileged system and is used for system call implementations. Hence, the SVC handler function goes into the kernel files. Additionally, the kernel should know what each thread stack looks like through its memory allocation with stack pointers. Therefore, the three functions, involving initialising the thread, creating the thread, and running the thread are all placed within the kernel files. These functions must interact directly with hardware, manage memory, and control processor modes which all  require elevated permissions. On the other hand, user files are for code that does not directly access hardware or protected memory. From the user\'s perspective, their primary responsibility is to write the thread functions and interact with the kernel through defined API calls. The distinction between kernel and user is important because it can harm the system by using the kernel in an improper way such as hardfaults through invalid memory access. This separation between user and kernel space is crucial. For example, it simplifies application development by keeping complex, hardware-specific operations separately through kernel files. It also protects system stability and security, since user code cannot accidentally lead to system corruption. The distinction is essential to ensure higher-level developers are not working on low-level code and can focus on desired operations without increasing the risk of bugs.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',3,5,'2025-06-25_16-10-58','Kernel is a more privileged system and is used for system call implementations. Hence, the SVC handler function goes into the kernel files. Additionally, the kernel should know what each thread stack looks like through its memory allocation with stack pointers. Therefore, the three functions, involving initialising the thread, creating the thread, and running the thread are all placed within the kernel files. These functions must interact directly with hardware, manage memory, and control processor modes which all  require elevated permissions. On the other hand, user files are for code that does not directly access hardware or protected memory. From the user\'s perspective, their primary responsibility is to write the thread functions and interact with the kernel through defined API calls. The distinction between kernel and user is important because it can harm the system by using the kernel in an improper way such as hardfaults through invalid memory access. This separation between user and kernel space is crucial. For example, it simplifies application development by keeping complex, hardware-specific operations separately through kernel files. It also protects system stability and security, since user code cannot accidentally lead to system corruption. The distinction is essential to ensure higher-level developers are not working on low-level code and can focus on desired operations without increasing the risk of bugs.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',4,1,'2025-07-03_13-38-45','Round-robin scheduling can work to from the start of the queue to the end based on the first-in first-out concept. The TCBs can still occur in a cyclic manner where it will pop, then immediately after push the same thread to push the thread to the back of the queue. A benefit of it compared to an array is that there is no need to keep track of the indices and the size. This is because the pushing and popping will allow for the cyclic nature of the round-robin scheduling to take place. There is also a lower time complexity because of this.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',4,1,'2025-07-03_16-12-34','The current scheduler is equivalent to a queue without the direct implementation of enqueue and dequeue. Round-robin scheduling can work to cycle from the start of the queue to the end based on the first-in first-out concept. The current scheduler increments through the indices of an array and retrieves the thread context for the index cyclically.  A queue to store TCBs is the same as the current scheduler where it can still occur in a cyclic manner, but will dequeue, then immediately after enqueue the same thread to push the thread to the back of the queue. A benefit of it compared to an array is that there is no need to keep track of the indices and the size. This is because the enqueueing and dequeuing will allow for the cyclic nature of the round-robin scheduling to take place. There is also a lower time complexity because of this. To explain, for performance, a properly implemented queue has O(1) time complexity, while a queue from an array has additional memory overhead. A queue supports the logical structure of round-robin scheduling and can simplify some implementation details. However, it can introduce a trade-off of minimal overhead depending on implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',4,2,'2025-07-03_13-38-45','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch through saving the state of the current task through its registers. It then calls the scheduler to move on to access the context in the next thread. After the scheduler function is ran, PSP will then point to the top of the next thread. Then, the registers for the next thread are popped to return its context. This will keep occurring for new threads in a cyclic manner where current registers are popped for execution and pushed when moving on to the new threads.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',4,2,'2025-07-03_16-12-34','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by first reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread stack. This allows saving the state of the current thread through its R4-R11 registers. It then calls the osSched function to move on to access the context in the next thread. After the osSched function is ran and the context is saved, PSP will then point to the top of the next thread. This is done by using __get_PSP() to have the same value as the global stack pointer. Then, the R4-11 registers for the next thread are popped to return its context. This loads the previously saved state of the thread to resume execution. In general, this process ensures that each thread’s context is preserved and restored correctly during each context switch, allowing the system to alternate between threads in a round-robin fashion.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',4,3,'2025-07-03_13-38-45','There will be a starvation of lower-priority tasks as they may never be run or will be run after a long time reducing its effectiveness. It also can lead to a priority inversion where the low priority task may hold a resource a high priority task requires. If the high-priority task is also not utilising the CPU well, it may restrict the system from completing other tasks at the same time. This overall creates a lack of fairness between different tasks heavily reducing the efficiency of the system through poor time management. This can be fixed through a Round-Robin system which gives each task an equal chance of being completed.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',4,3,'2025-07-03_16-12-34','A scheduler that chooses the task with highest priority means that the highest priority task will always run before the lower priority tasks regardless of if the lower priority task was waiting longer. There will be a starvation of lower-priority tasks as they may never be run or will be run after a long time reducing its effectiveness. It also can lead to a priority inversion where the low priority task may hold a resource a high priority task requires. Nevertheless, for the TCBs, the different threads are not sharing resources, and the priorities are static meaning that priority inversion is not a valid argument. To fix the issue of starvation, priorities can be dynamically adjusted. This can be incorporated where tasks that are waiting longer will gradually have higher priorities. Round-robin scheduling does not use a priority system and instead gives each task a fixed time slice. While Round-Robin ensures fairness, it doesn’t distinguish between critical and non-critical tasks, which may be inefficient for systems with real-time constraints. Overall, priority scheduling is an effective scheduler for focusing on important tasks first but suffers from a risk of starvation which can be fixed through dynamically adjusting priorities.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',4,4,'2025-07-03_13-38-45','Assembly is a more low-level language compared to C. Therefore, many more lines of code will need to be written to perform the same functionality as calling C functions. C is also easier to debug making it faster to achieve the required functionality. C compilers could have high-level optimizations that may not be available in assembly. However, using assembly grants greater control over register values.  Another downside with C functions is that calling a C function involves worse time complexities in regarding to passing arguments and adjusting the stack frame. C functions are more high-level compared to using assembly so they may make assumptions in handlers that are not desired. For instance, SVC_HandlerMain should not perform long or blocking tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',4,4,'2025-07-03_16-12-34','Assembly is a more low-level language compared to C. Therefore, many more lines of code will need to be written to perform the same functionality as calling C functions. C is also easier to debug making it faster to achieve the required functionality. C compilers could have high-level optimizations that may not be available in assembly. However, using assembly grants greater control over register values.  Another downside with C functions is that calling a C function involves worse time complexities in regarding to passing arguments and adjusting the stack frame. C functions are more high-level compared to using assembly so they may make assumptions in handlers that are not desired. For instance, SVC_HandlerMain should not perform long or blocking tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',4,5,'2025-07-03_13-38-45','The OS will still work but it creates overhead costs through the context switching. To explain, the scheduler will help with cycling with the same function infinitely. However, it is ineffective because you are essentially popping the registers, moving the stack pointer, and pushing the same registers indefinitely. This may create time delays to run this functionality in between iterations but will still produce the desired output.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',4,5,'2025-07-03_16-12-34','The OS will still work but it creates overhead costs through the redundant context switching. To explain, the osYield function yields control back to the scheduler, and as a result, the scheduler reschedules the same thread again. The role of the scheduler is to update the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to the next thread stack by finding which thread stack should be run next. This creates a necessity with the scheduler as it ensures that the global stack pointer is always updated and the PSP points to the top of the stack of the selected thread. Yet, this is ineffective because the OS saves the R4-R11 registers, updates the stack pointer, and reloads the exact same registers indefinitely. This creates overhead without providing the benefits of multitasking. In summary, this may slow down execution to run this functionality and introduce greater inefficiency but will still retrieve the desired output.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,1,'2025-07-28_12-32-33','The initial process involves the user defining a thread function such as a print function that takes a void* argument. After that, a struct is created containing the necessary data, initializes it, then passes a pointer to it into the osCreateThread function. Then the osCreateThread function creates a thread stack involving the different registers simulating the CPU state. Since, PC is used for printing the output, the pointer to the thread function is placed there in Register R0’s position. When the thread is scheduled to run, the OS sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the thread stack and triggers a context switch where the CPU loads the values from the stack into the appropriate registers through decrementing a stack pointer. Therefore, R0 will be assigned the correct function of which the CPU can execute. This process and behaviour lead to arguments being able to be passed into threads effectively.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',5,1,'2025-07-28_13-20-05','The initial process involves the user defining a thread function such as a print function that takes a void* argument. After that, a struct is created containing the necessary data, initializes it, then passes a pointer to it into the osCreateThread function. Then the osCreateThread function creates a thread stack involving the different registers like PC, LR, R0-3, simulating the CPU state. Since, PC holds the address of the thread function, the thread function is placed in Register R0’s position. Placing the argument in R0 works because of the calling convention defined by the system\'s Application Binary Interface (ABI) on ARM-based systems. This convention is specifically the ARM Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS) on ARM-based systems. The convention applies as when a function is called, its first argument must be placed in register R0. When the thread is scheduled to run, the OS sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the thread stack and triggers a context switch where the CPU loads the values from the stack into the appropriate registers through decrementing a stack pointer. Therefore, R0 will be assigned the correct function of which the CPU can execute. This process and behaviour lead to arguments being able to be passed into threads effectively.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,1,'2025-07-28_14-01-34','The initial process involves the user defining a thread function such as a print function that takes a void* argument. After that, a struct is created containing the necessary data, initializes it, then passes a pointer to it into the osCreateThread function. Then the osCreateThread function creates a thread stack involving the different registers like PC, LR, R0-3, simulating the CPU state. Since, PC holds the address of the thread function, the thread function is placed in Register R0’s position. Placing the argument in R0 works because of the calling convention defined by the system\'s Application Binary Interface (ABI) on ARM-based systems. This convention is specifically the ARM Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS) on ARM-based systems. The convention applies as when a function is called, its first argument must be placed in register R0. When the thread is scheduled to run, the OS sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the thread stack and triggers a context switch where the CPU loads the values from the stack into the appropriate registers through decrementing a stack pointer. Therefore, R0 will be assigned the correct function of which the CPU can execute. This process and behaviour lead to arguments being able to be passed into threads effectively.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',5,1,'2025-07-29_13-32-21','The initial process involves the user defining a thread function such as a print function that takes a void* argument. After that, a struct is created containing the necessary data, initializes it, then passes a pointer to it into the osCreateThread function. Then the osCreateThread function creates a thread stack involving the different registers like PC, LR, R0-3, simulating the CPU state. Since, PC holds the address of the thread function, the CPU knows what code to execute when the thread starts. To simulate passing an argument to the thread function, the OS places the argument in register R0. Placing the argument in R0 works because of the calling convention defined by the system\'s Application Binary Interface (ABI) on ARM-based systems. This convention is specifically the ARM Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS) on ARM-based systems. The convention applies as when a function is called, its first argument must be placed in register R0. When the thread is scheduled to run, the OS sets the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to point to the thread stack and triggers a context switch where the CPU loads the values from the stack into the appropriate registers through decrementing a stack pointer. Therefore, R0 already holds the expected argument of which the CPU can execute. This process and behaviour lead to arguments being able to be passed into threads effectively.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,2,'2025-07-28_12-32-33','All the functions listed including PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler and utilised when an interrupt triggered. However, osYield is another interrupt which seeks to context switch to the other thread. Therefore, this leads to nested interrupts which can cause stack overflows or system crashes. This can also consume too much stack space as even more states are being saved due to the extra context switches. In a constrained embedded environment, this can lead to undefined behaviour. The scheduler is disturbed heavily by the interrupt usage as the logic is now confusing on what the next performed task should be.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',5,2,'2025-07-28_13-20-05','All the functions listed including PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler and utilised when an interrupt triggered. However, osYield is another interrupt which seeks to context switch to the other thread.  When an interrupt occurs, the Cortex M4 Hardware automatically saves key CPU states to the hardware. Calling osYield leads to nested interrupts which can then cause stack overflows through overwritten states or cause system crashes. Nested interrupts are handled through priority numbers where certain tasks may be interrupted by higher priority ones. The greater the number of interrupts, the more stack will be used. This overall mechanism can be detailed as follows where the Cortex-M4 initially uses the stack for every exception, including context switches. Then, calling osYield() inside an ISR delays PendSV, but still consumes stack later. If the stack overflows, the CPU will use corrupted values during return affecting PC leading to a hardfault. In a constrained embedded environment, this can lead to undefined behaviour. The scheduler is disturbed heavily by the interrupt usage as the logic is now confusing on what the next performed task should be.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,2,'2025-07-28_14-01-34','All the functions listed including PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler and utilised when an interrupt triggered. However, osYield is another interrupt which seeks to context switch to the other thread.  When an interrupt occurs, the Cortex M4 Hardware automatically saves key CPU states to the hardware. Calling osYield leads to nested interrupts which can then cause stack overflows through overwritten states or cause system crashes. Nested interrupts are handled through priority numbers where certain tasks may be interrupted by higher priority ones. The greater the number of interrupts, the more stack will be used. This overall mechanism can be detailed as follows where the Cortex-M4 initially uses the stack for every exception, including context switches. Then, calling osYield() inside an ISR delays PendSV, but still consumes stack later. If the stack overflows, the CPU will use corrupted values during return affecting PC leading to a hardfault. In a constrained embedded environment, this can lead to undefined behaviour. The scheduler is disturbed heavily by the interrupt usage as the logic is now confusing on what the next performed task should be.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,2,'2025-07-29_13-32-21','All the functions listed including PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler and utilised when an interrupt triggered. However, osYield is another interrupt which seeks to context switch to the other thread.  When an interrupt occurs, the Cortex M4 Hardware automatically saves key CPU states to the hardware. Calling osYield leads to nested interrupts which can then cause stack overflows through overwritten states or cause system crashes. Nested interrupts are handled through priority numbers where certain tasks may be interrupted by higher priority ones. The greater the number of interrupts, the more stack will be used. This overall mechanism can be detailed as follows where the Cortex-M4 initially uses the stack for every exception, including context switches. Then, calling osYield() inside an ISR delays PendSV, but still consumes stack later. If the stack overflows, the CPU will use corrupted values during return affecting PC leading to a hardfault. In a constrained embedded environment, this can lead to undefined behaviour. The scheduler is disturbed heavily by the interrupt usage as the logic is now confusing on what the next performed task should be.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,3,'2025-07-28_12-32-33','This would increase the system ticks by a factor of 10 from 1000 times a second to 10000 times a second. The benefits including quicker response times where the threads can be scheduled with greater precision. To add on, delays would also be more accurate in the scheduling process. However, there are also many downsides including the cost of operation. For instance, the overhead would be worse due to increased power consumption and stack usage. Over long durations, this may lead to performance instability where interrupts are no longer working as effectively.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,3,'2025-07-28_13-20-05','This would increase the system ticks by a factor of 10 from 1000 times a second to 10000 times a second. The benefits including quicker response times where the threads can be scheduled with greater precision. To add on, delays would also be more accurate in the scheduling process. However, there are also many downsides including the cost of operation. For instance, the overhead would be worse due to increased power consumption and stack usage due to saving the current state much more frequently. Over long durations, this may lead to performance instability where interrupts are no longer working as effectively. For instance, the CPU may become overloaded with interrupt handling, leaving less time for actual thread execution.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,3,'2025-07-28_14-01-34','This would increase the system ticks by a factor of 10 from 1000 times a second to 10000 times a second. The benefits including quicker response times where the threads can be scheduled with greater precision. To add on, delays would also be more accurate in the scheduling process. However, there are also many downsides including the cost of operation. For instance, the overhead would be worse due to increased power consumption and stack usage due to saving the current state much more frequently. Over long durations, this may lead to performance instability where interrupts are no longer working as effectively. For instance, the CPU may become overloaded with interrupt handling, leaving less time for actual thread execution.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,3,'2025-07-29_13-32-21','This would increase the system ticks by a factor of 10 from 1000 times a second to 10000 times a second. The benefits including quicker response times where the threads can be scheduled with greater precision. To add on, delays would also be more accurate in the scheduling process. However, there are also many downsides including the cost of operation. For instance, the overhead would be worse due to increased power consumption and stack usage due to saving the current state much more frequently. Over long durations, this may lead to performance instability where interrupts are no longer working as effectively. For instance, the CPU may become overloaded with interrupt handling, leaving less time for actual thread execution.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,4,'2025-07-28_12-32-33','Co-operative multitasking is through the osYield function where the user chooses when the thread should be switched. This leads to an issue where if a thread yields or runs too long, the other threads may be starved. A solution to this is pre-emptive multitasking is controlled by the system through a system timer. The OS automatically interrupts threads based on a timer to ensure fairness. These points are from the user perspective where it may be easier for the OS to control multitasking. In the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is easier where a context switch only needs to be performed when a thread explicitly yields. To add on, managing forced context switches is a difficult process especially when one thread is not ready to yield.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,4,'2025-07-28_13-20-05','Co-operative multitasking is through the osYield function where the user chooses when the thread should be switched. This leads to an issue where if a thread yields or runs too long, the other threads may be starved. A solution to this is pre-emptive multitasking is controlled by the system through a system timer. The OS automatically interrupts threads based on a timer to ensure fairness. These points are from the user perspective where it may be easier for the OS to control multitasking. In the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is easier where a context switch only needs to be performed when a thread explicitly yields. To add on, managing forced context switches is a difficult process especially when one thread is not ready to yield.\n',0,'s25'),('p64jain',5,4,'2025-07-28_14-01-34','Co-operative multitasking is through the osYield function where the user chooses when the thread should be switched. This leads to an issue where if a thread yields or runs too long, the other threads may be starved. A solution to this is pre-emptive multitasking which is controlled by the system to forcibly interrupt a thread. This ensures that no single thread can monopolize the CPU. For instance, the OS can automatically interrupt threads based on a timer to ensure fairness. A hardware timer is set to trigger at regular intervals and when the timer fires, a context switch may be initiated by the OS scheduler. To explain, the OS saves the state of the currently running thread and switches to another ready thread from the queue. This process allows the OS to guarantee CPU time sharing among threads, even when some threads are not cooperative. These points are from the user perspective where it may be easier for the OS to control multitasking. In the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is easier where a context switch only needs to be performed when a thread explicitly yields. To add on, managing forced context switches is a difficult process especially when one thread is not ready to yield.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,4,'2025-07-29_13-32-21','Co-operative multitasking is through the osYield function where the user chooses when the thread should be switched. This leads to an issue where if a thread yields or runs too long, the other threads may be starved. A solution to this is pre-emptive multitasking which is controlled by the system to forcibly interrupt a thread. This ensures that no single thread can monopolize the CPU. For instance, the OS can automatically interrupt threads based on a timer to ensure fairness. A hardware timer is set to trigger at regular intervals and when the timer fires, a context switch may be initiated by the OS scheduler. To explain, the OS saves the state of the currently running thread and switches to another ready thread from the queue. This process allows the OS to guarantee CPU time sharing among threads, even when some threads are not cooperative. These points are from the user perspective where it may be easier for the OS to control multitasking. In the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is easier where a context switch only needs to be performed when a thread explicitly yields. To add on, managing forced context switches is a difficult process especially when one thread is not ready to yield.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,5,'2025-07-28_12-32-33','They can work together through the use of the PendSV interrupt. Whether using, the osYield function or the SysTick_Handler, both mechanisms signal the OS to switch to another thread. When PendSV_Handler is ran, the states of the different threads are saved regardless of how the switch is requested. Therefore, thread stack operations get resumed for the different stacks effectively. This scheduler of the OS figures out which task should be ran next. The use of both time slices and voluntary yielding works as long as the logic for context switching is consistent and can be applied to both systems.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,5,'2025-07-28_13-20-05','They can work together through the use of the PendSV interrupt. Whether using, the osYield function or the SysTick_Handler, both mechanisms signal the OS to switch to another thread. When PendSV_Handler is ran, the states of the different threads are saved regardless of how the switch is requested. Therefore, thread stack operations get resumed for the different stacks effectively. This scheduler of the OS figures out which task should be ran next. The use of both time slices and voluntary yielding works as long as the logic for context switching is consistent and can be applied to both systems.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,5,'2025-07-28_14-01-34','They can work together using the PendSV interrupt. Whether using the osYield function or the SysTick_Handler, both mechanisms signal the OS to switch to another thread. When PendSV_Handler is run, the states of the different threads are saved regardless of how the switch is requested. This is because the PendSV_Handler saves the state of the currently running thread and restores the state for the next scheduled thread. Therefore, thread stack operations get resumed for the different stacks effectively. This scheduler of the OS figures out which task should be run next. The use of both time slices and voluntary yielding works if the logic for context switching is consistent and can be applied to both systems. This logic centralization in PendSV_Handler leads to code reuse and maintainability to support both co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('p64jain',5,5,'2025-07-29_13-32-21','They can work together using the PendSV interrupt. Whether using the osYield function or the SysTick_Handler, both mechanisms signal the OS to switch to another thread. When PendSV_Handler is run, the states of the different threads are saved regardless of how the switch is requested. This is because the PendSV_Handler saves the state of the currently running thread and restores the state for the next scheduled thread. Therefore, thread stack operations get resumed for the different stacks effectively. This scheduler of the OS figures out which task should be run next. The use of both time slices and voluntary yielding works if the logic for context switching is consistent and can be applied to both systems. This logic centralization in PendSV_Handler leads to code reuse and maintainability to support both co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',1,1,'2025-05-21_13-37-13','The hardware abstraction layer (HAL) is a programming component which permits interactions between an operating system and hardware devices at a general level, obscuring the intricacies of the hardware. HAL is widely utilized in embedded projects since it simplifies the coding process by eradicating the need to write device-specific code, instead allowing the same software to be applied across various systems.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',1,1,'2025-05-22_11-59-27','The hardware abstraction layer (HAL) is a programming component which permits interactions between an operating system and hardware devices at a general level, obscuring the intricacies of the hardware. HAL is widely utilized in embedded projects since it simplifies the coding process by eradicating the need to write device-specific code, instead allowing the same software to be applied across various systems. The abstraction provided by HAL promotes code reusability as it detaches application logic from hardware implementation, a modular approach that simplifies transitions between hardware platforms while drastically reducing development time.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',1,2,'2025-05-21_13-37-13','The __io_putchar function is used in embedded systems, taking a single character as input and physically sending it to an output stream, the device. It is a low-level function, meaning it operates closer to a hardware level and handles tasks related directly to hardware or OS, and is employed by high-level functions like printf as an output mechanism.\n',0,'s25'),('pkotta',1,2,'2025-05-22_11-59-27','The __io_putchar function is used in embedded systems, taking a single character as input and physically sending it to an output stream, the device. It is a low-level function, meaning it operates closer to a hardware level and handles tasks related directly to hardware or OS, and is employed by high-level functions like printf as an output mechanism. This is significant in embedded projects since different platforms may employ varying communication interfaces so __io_putchar must be implemented accordingly to ensure the output is accurately sent out. In fact, custom implementations may be required to match the target hardware’s configuration and abilities. Ultimately, the absence of this function would create ambiguity with regards to how I/O functions release their output.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',1,3,'2025-05-21_13-37-13','While printf statements can allow for the perusal of variable values at different stages of the code, the debugger facilitates interaction with the program, to examine all variables at any point in execution, along with their specific memory location, regardless of whether they are in scope. A major difference is that debugging does not modify the original code in the slightest, rather it is a function that can be used to identify and fix issues or runtime errors that are not always revealed by a mere call of printf.\n',0,'s25'),('pkotta',1,3,'2025-05-22_11-59-27','While printf statements allow for the perusal of variable values at different stages of the code, the debugger facilitates interaction with the program, to examine all variables at any point in execution, along with their specific memory location, regardless of whether they are in scope. A major difference is that debugging does not modify the original code in the slightest, rather it is a function that can be used to identify and fix issues or runtime errors that are not always revealed by a mere call of printf, including interrupt handling issues or corruption of the stack. Examining the registers that control core processor functions is only possible through the debugger, which provides a complete snapshot of the system. This is a better tool for identifying subtle bugs, diagnosing hardware-level issues, and gaining insight into how the processor is executing instructions.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',1,4,'2025-05-21_13-37-13','In the stack allocation process, the initial MSP value was obtained from address 0x0, which points to the top of the main stack. After dereferencing the 0x0 twice, its address was retrieved, and knowledge of the initial location of MSP was necessary since the stack expands downwards in memory. The PSP value was subsequent to MSP, stored in a different memory address relative by an offset of 0x400 bytes.\n',0,'s25'),('pkotta',1,4,'2025-05-22_11-59-27','In the stack allocation process, the initial Main Stack Pointer (MSP) value was obtained from address 0x0, which points to the top of the main stack. After dereferencing the 0x0 twice, its address was retrieved, and knowledge of the initial location of MSP was necessary since the stack expands downwards in memory, towards loward addresses. The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) value was positioned subsequent to MSP, stored in a different memory address relative by an offset of 0x400 bytes, with the relation PSP_val = MSP_init_vsl - 0x400. The offset reserves space for the main stack, whilst ensuring that PSP has a separate region in memory, preventing overlap between the stacks. This is necessary as stack memory regions must not interfere with one another, to avoid the possibility of stack corruptions, crashes, and other unpredictable behaviours which would disrupt system integrity when using the stacks simultaneously.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',1,5,'2025-05-21_13-37-13','To allocate multiple stacks for threads when the number of threads is known at compile-time, retrieve the initial stack pointer, which points to the top of the main stack. Each thread\'s stack has a fixed size, and the starting address of each thread’s stack by adding the size of the main stack and an increment based on the thread’s position, ensuring that each thread’s stack starts at a unique memory location. This method prevents any overlap between the stacks, and since the number of threads is determined at compile-time, the memory addresses for all the thread stacks can be predetermined, allowing the OS to assign the appropriate memory locations.\n',0,'s25'),('pkotta',1,5,'2025-05-22_11-59-27','To allocate multiple stacks for threads when the number of threads is known at compile-time, retrieve the initial stack pointer which points to the top of the main stack. Each thread\'s stack has a fixed size, and the starting address can be computed by adding the size of the main stack and an increment based on the thread’s position, ensuring that each thread’s stack starts at a unique memory location. For instance, calculate the placement of each thread’s stack by: stack_start[i] = thread_stack_base + (i*stack_size) , where i is the thread index and stack_size is a constant that is a multiple of 8 bytes to align with Cortex-M4\'s stack alignment requirements. This method prevents any overlap between the stacks as the thread stack region is separated from the system stack, and since the number of threads is determined at compile-time, the memory addresses for all the thread stacks can be predetermined, allowing the OS to assign the appropriate memory locations in a predictable layout.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,1,'2025-06-26_13-22-41','By changing the program counter (PC) to store the address of the print continuously function, the processor was instructed to jump to that location in memory and begin executing the function. The PC register automatically increments when instructions are executed and determines which task to work with next. Directly setting it to the function address assigned the CPU to running that specific function immediately. This illustrates how the PC can be manually manipulated, ultimately changing the flow of the processor’s execution, an action that occurs frequently when the OS switches between threads or creates new ones. While this direct manipulation is not applied often in real world situations due to security concerns, it demonstrates the ability for context switching.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,1,'2025-06-26_21-25-35','By changing the program counter (PC) to store the address of the print continuously function, the processor was instructed to jump to that location in memory and begin executing the function. The PC register automatically increments when instructions are executed and determines which task to work with next. Directly setting it to the function address assigned the CPU to running that specific function immediately. This illustrates how the PC can be manually manipulated, ultimately changing the flow of the processor’s execution, an action that occurs frequently when the OS switches between threads or creates new ones. While this direct manipulation is not applied often in real world situations due to security concerns, it demonstrates the ability for context switching.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,1,'2025-06-27_00-22-30','By changing the program counter (PC) to store the address of the print continuously function, the processor was instructed to jump to that location in memory and begin executing the function. The PC register automatically increments when instructions are executed and determines which task to work with next. Directly setting it to the function address assigned the CPU to running that specific function immediately. This illustrates how the PC can be manually manipulated, ultimately changing the flow of the processor’s execution, an action that occurs frequently when the OS switches between threads or creates new ones. While this direct manipulation is not applied often in real world situations due to security concerns, it demonstrates the ability for context switching.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,2,'2025-06-26_13-22-41','System calls are mechanisms that allow user-level programs to request services from the operating system’s kernel to perform privileged access tasks such as accessing hardware or managing processes. Typically, programs cannot directly access these resources on their own due to their limited permissions, utilizing system calls to safely make requests to the OS. The Cortex M4 processor avails of the SVC function, which is a supervisor call instruction that triggers a special exception and takes an argument as an identifier (an integer), and performs a task based on the assigned meaning of that number. User programs interact with system calls via APIs that wrap the SVC instruction.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,2,'2025-06-26_21-25-35','System calls are mechanisms that allow user-level programs to request services from the operating system’s kernel to perform privileged access tasks such as accessing hardware or managing processes. Typically, programs cannot directly access these resources on their own due to their limited permissions, utilizing system calls to safely make requests to the OS. The Cortex M4 processor avails of the SVC function, which is a supervisor call instruction that triggers a special exception and takes an argument as an identifier (an integer), and performs a task based on the assigned meaning of that number. User programs interact with system calls via APIs that wrap the SVC instruction.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,2,'2025-06-27_00-22-30','System calls are mechanisms that allow user-level programs to request services from the operating system’s kernel to perform privileged access tasks such as accessing hardware or managing processes. Typically, programs cannot directly access these resources on their own due to their limited permissions, utilizing system calls to safely make requests to the OS. The Cortex M4 processor avails of the SVC function, which is a supervisor call instruction that triggers a special exception and takes an argument as an identifier (an integer), and performs a task based on the assigned meaning of that number. User programs interact with system calls via APIs that wrap the SVC instruction.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,3,'2025-06-26_13-22-41','Setting up the thread’s stack involved manually arranging the memory used by the thread when it begins execution to ensure the processor believes it is already running that thread. This was simulated in the lab by pushing register values onto the stack in the exact hardware-defined context order expected by the CPU during an interrupt; xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, followed by R11 to R4. Register values must be stored on the stack because this practice allows the CPU the save and restore the state of execution. This acts as a temporary storage defining the actions of the thread, meaning that during a context switch such as a system call, the CPU can seamlessly pause and resume the thread, a fundamental aspect of the operating system’s multitasking behaviour.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,3,'2025-06-26_21-25-35','Setting up the thread’s stack involved manually arranging the memory used by the thread when it begins execution to ensure the processor believes it is already running that thread. This was simulated in the lab by pushing register values onto the stack in the exact hardware-defined context order expected by the CPU during an interrupt; xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, followed by R11 to R4. Register values must be stored on the stack because this practice allows the CPU the save and restore the state of execution. This acts as a temporary storage defining the actions of the thread, meaning that during a context switch such as a system call, the CPU can seamlessly pause and resume the thread, a fundamental aspect of the operating system’s multitasking behaviour.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,3,'2025-06-27_00-22-30','Setting up the thread’s stack involved manually arranging the memory used by the thread when it begins execution to ensure the processor believes it is already running that thread. This was simulated in the lab by pushing register values onto the stack in the exact hardware-defined context order expected by the CPU during an interrupt; xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, followed by R11 to R4. Register values must be stored on the stack because this practice allows the CPU the save and restore the state of execution. This acts as a temporary storage defining the actions of the thread, meaning that during a context switch such as a system call, the CPU can seamlessly pause and resume the thread, a fundamental aspect of the operating system’s multitasking behaviour.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,4,'2025-06-26_13-22-41','In the SVC_Handler_Main function, the process stack pointer was set using __set_PSP(stackptr) prior to launching the thread since that matches the thread’s configuration, it is not meant to run using MSP. Without setting the PSP to point to the thread’s initialized stack, which includes expected register values, the processor would not have a valid stack to avail of during exception return, which means it may attempt to pop register values from an invalid memory region. The hard fault originates from this misconfiguration, as the processor encounters corrupted context. Essentially, incorrect assignment of PSP would prevent the restoration of the thread’s context, therefore the CPU crashed since it will attempt to execute as though the thread is already in operation.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,4,'2025-06-26_21-25-35','In the SVC_Handler_Main function, the process stack pointer was set using __set_PSP(stackptr) prior to launching the thread since that matches the thread’s configuration, it is not meant to run using MSP. Without setting the PSP to point to the thread’s initialized stack, which includes expected register values, the processor would not have a valid stack to avail of during exception return, which means it may attempt to pop register values from an invalid memory region. The hard fault originates from this misconfiguration, as the processor encounters corrupted context. Essentially, incorrect assignment of PSP would prevent the restoration of the thread’s context, therefore the CPU crashed since it will attempt to execute as though the thread is already in operation.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,4,'2025-06-27_00-22-30','In the SVC_Handler_Main function, the process stack pointer was set using __set_PSP(stackptr) prior to launching the thread since that matches the thread’s configuration, it is not meant to run using MSP. Without setting the PSP to point to the thread’s initialized stack, which includes expected register values, the processor would not have a valid stack to avail of during exception return, which means it may attempt to pop register values from an invalid memory region. The hard fault originates from this misconfiguration, as the processor encounters corrupted context. Essentially, incorrect assignment of PSP would prevent the restoration of the thread’s context, therefore the CPU crashed since it will attempt to execute as though the thread is already in operation.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',2,5,'2025-06-26_13-22-41','If the thread function only printed once and then returned as opposed to continuously looping, the thread would terminate immediately after execution. Since the thread’s context and stack were initialized to run this function as an independent thread, returning from the function would prompt the processor to attempt executing any instructions that follow the thread function’s return address on the stack. However, the stack and context assumed indefinite operation, and no valid next instructions are set up, meaning this would likely trigger a fault, crash, or other undefined behaviour. In other words, the system is designed to expect that the thread keeps running, or that a proper thread exit mechanism handles cleanup and switching; without that, simply returning leaves the CPU with no valid next task, causing a failure.\n',0,'s25'),('pkotta',2,5,'2025-06-26_21-25-35','If a thread function prints once and returns instead of looping, its behavior depends on the threading system in use. In most standard operating systems or threading libraries, returning from the thread function causes the thread to exit cleanly and release its resources. However, in custom or low-level implementations, simply returning might not be handled as expected. The return address is stored on the stack and tells the CPU where to continue execution after the function finishes. If this address is invalid or not set up for thread cleanup, the CPU will attempt to execute code at the wrong place. This usually results in a fault, which is an exception indicating something went wrong. If the fault is not caught, it can escalate to a hard fault (an unrecoverable error) triggering a system crash or reset. Therefore, proper thread termination mechanisms are necessary to ensure safe and predictable behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('pkotta',2,5,'2025-06-27_00-22-30','In a standard function call, the return address is pushed onto the stack, so when the function finishes executing, the CPU knows where to continue execution. For thread functions, however, the return address is not stored at all on the thread’s stack. Instead, the thread is expected to exit via a system-managed function (like pthread_exit in POSIX or ExitThread in Windows). This is a critical difference because when a thread simply returns after performing some task (like printing), there is no valid return address for the CPU to jump to. Without this return address, the CPU has no direction for where to continue execution, which can cause it to attempt to execute an invalid instruction or access invalid memory. The absence of the return address on the thread\'s stack means that, when the thread finishes executing, it will lead directly to a hardfault. A hardfault occurs when the CPU encounters an invalid instruction or tries to jump to an invalid memory location, often because the thread function’s exit wasn\'t handled properly by the system. This is a serious error that can crash the system or trigger a reset. In other words, the missing return address is a direct cause of the hardfault, and without proper termination handling, the thread’s exit can lead to system instability. Therefore, thread functions must exit using proper termination methods to avoid these types of errors.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',3,1,'2025-06-25_18-04-12','When compiling a project with multiple *.c files, each file is individually compiled into an object file (with a .o or .obj extension), each containing machine code for the functions and variables defined in each source file. The linker combines all the object files, resolves references between them (ie. function calls from one file to another), and links in any necessary libraries. The end result is a single executable that contains all the code from the separate *.c files, ready to be run on the target system. In the context of this lab, the main.c and kernel.c files are compiled and linked together to produce the final program for the microcontroller.\n',0,'s25'),('pkotta',3,1,'2025-06-26_13-18-26','When building a project with multiple *.c files, the process involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. During preprocessing, each source file is scanned for directives such as #include and #define. Header files are included in place, and macros are expanded, resulting in a complete source file that is ready for compilation. Next, the compiler processes each preprocessed file individually, converting it into an object file (.o or .obj) that contains machine code for the specific functions and variables defined in that file. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable, resolving references between them (ie. function calls or shared variables) across different source files. It also links in any necessary external libraries. In the context of this lab, files like main.c and kernel.c undergo this process to produce a single executable program suitable for running on the target.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',3,2,'2025-06-25_18-04-12','Including a *.c file with the #include directive is not advisable since it can lead to multiple definitions of the same functions/variables if the included *.c file is referenced in multiple places. When the compiler processes each .c file, it creates an object file. If the same code (such as kernel.c) is included in multiple source files, the linker views multiple definitions of the same functions, causing errors. Instead, header files (.h) are used to declare functions and variables, while *.c files define them. For instance, this lab avails of kernel.h to declare functions and kernel.c to define them, keeping the code organized while avoiding linker errors.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',3,2,'2025-06-26_13-18-26','Including a *.c file with the #include directive is not advisable since it can lead to multiple definitions of the same functions/variables if the included *.c file is referenced in multiple places. When the compiler processes each .c file, it creates an object file. If the same code (such as kernel.c) is included in multiple source files, the linker views multiple definitions of the same functions, causing errors. Instead, header files (.h) are used to declare functions and variables, while *.c files define them. For instance, this lab avails of kernel.h to declare functions and kernel.c to define them, keeping the code organized while avoiding linker errors.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',3,3,'2025-06-25_18-04-12','Write a function in main.c to be the entry point for the thread\nCall an initialization function (such as osKernelInitialize) to prepare the kernel and set up any necessary data structures\nCreate a thread by calling functions such as osCreateThread, to allocate a stack for the thread and sets up its initial/original state\nStart the kernel by calling a function like osKernelStart to begin execution, it runs the thread and the user’s function begins executing as a thread\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',3,3,'2025-06-26_13-18-26','Write a function in main.c to be the entry point for the thread\nCall an initialization function (such as osKernelInitialize) to prepare the kernel and set up any necessary data structures\nCreate a thread by calling functions such as osCreateThread, to allocate a stack for the thread and sets up its initial/original state\nStart the kernel by calling a function like osKernelStart to begin execution, it runs the thread and the user’s function begins executing as a thread\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',3,4,'2025-06-25_18-04-12','The stack pool is a region of memory reserved for use as the stacks of multiple threads. Each thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls, local variables, and context during execution. By allocating a large memory pool (for example, by setting the minimum stack size to 0x4000 in the linker script), the OS can assign smaller stacks for each thread from this pool. In order to run multiple threads in future labs, allocate a new stack from the pool for each thread, ensuring they have independent memory for their execution. This approach ensures the OS can manage multiple threads efficiently and safely, as each thread’s stack is separately stored from the others.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',3,4,'2025-06-26_13-18-26','The stack pool is a region of memory reserved for use as the stacks of multiple threads. Each thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls, local variables, and context during execution. By allocating a large memory pool (for example, by setting the minimum stack size to 0x4000 in the linker script), the OS can assign smaller stacks for each thread from this pool. In order to run multiple threads in future labs, allocate a new stack from the pool for each thread, ensuring they have independent memory for their execution. This approach ensures the OS can manage multiple threads efficiently and safely, as each thread’s stack is separately stored from the others.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',3,5,'2025-06-25_18-04-12','The distinction between “user” and “kernel” code revolves around separating responsibilities and organization. The kernel code (in kernel.c and kernel.h) contains low-level functions and data structures that manage threads, stacks, and system calls. The user code (in main.c) contains the actual thread functions and the main program logic that uses the kernel’s services. This dissociation keeps the kernel code clean, modular, and easy to maintain, while the user code can exclusively focus on application logic. From the user’s perspective, this means only writing thread functions and using the kernel’s provided functions, without harbouring concerns regarding the internal details of thread/stack management. This separation also makes it easier to expand the operating system in future labs, as new kernel features can be added without changing the user code\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',3,5,'2025-06-26_13-18-26','The distinction between “user” and “kernel” code revolves around separating responsibilities and organization. The kernel code (in kernel.c and kernel.h) contains low-level functions and data structures that manage threads, stacks, and system calls. The user code (in main.c) contains the actual thread functions and the main program logic that uses the kernel’s services. This dissociation keeps the kernel code clean, modular, and easy to maintain, while the user code can exclusively focus on application logic. From the user’s perspective, this means only writing thread functions and using the kernel’s provided functions, without harbouring concerns regarding the internal details of thread/stack management. This separation also makes it easier to expand the operating system in future labs, as new kernel features can be added without changing the user code\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',4,1,'2025-07-21_22-40-42','Using a queue to store Thread Control Blocks (TCBs) offers an alternative to an array for implementing round-robin scheduling, primarily due to the queue\'s inherent FIFO nature. In this model, when a thread yields the processor, its TCB is added to the back of the queue. The scheduler then simply retrieves the thread at the front of the queue to determine which thread runs next. This cyclic behavior ensures that all threads eventually get a chance to execute, providing equal treatment. A key benefit of using a queue, especially a dynamic one, is its ability to more easily manage the creation and termination of threads without requiring a fixed maximum number of threads, unlike a statically sized array. This makes it more memory-efficient in scenarios where the number of active threads fluctuates. Furthermore, for round-robin, the logic for selecting the next thread is simplified to enqueuing the current thread and dequeuing the next, which can be more intuitive and potentially less error-prone than managing array indices and modulo operations. While the lab manual notes that arrays can be memory-inefficient and don\'t lend themselves well to fast scheduling algorithms in production OSes, a queue can be a suitable intermediate step towards more complex scheduling algorithms without the rigid memory allocation of a fixed array.\n',0,'s25'),('pkotta',4,1,'2025-07-21_23-21-04','Using a queue to store Thread Control Blocks (TCBs) offers an alternative to an array for implementing round-robin scheduling, primarily due to the queue\'s inherent FIFO nature. In this model, when a thread yields the processor, its TCB is added to the back of the queue. The scheduler then simply retrieves the thread at the front of the queue to determine which thread runs next. This cyclic behavior ensures that all threads eventually get a chance to execute, providing equal treatment. A key benefit of using a queue, especially a dynamic one, is its ability to more easily manage the creation and termination of threads without requiring a fixed maximum number of threads, unlike a statically sized array. This makes it more memory-efficient in scenarios where the number of active threads fluctuates. Furthermore, for round-robin, the logic for selecting the next thread is simplified to enqueuing the current thread and dequeuing the next, which can be more intuitive and potentially less error-prone than managing array indices and modulo operations. However, using a queue can introduce some overhead due to the dynamic memory allocations and deallocations often involved, which might be less efficient than simply incrementing an index in a pre-allocated array. This overhead could be a critical consideration for real-time systems where consistent and minimal scheduling latency is paramount. While the lab manual notes that arrays can be memory-inefficient and don\'t lend themselves well to fast scheduling algorithms in production OSes, a queue can be a suitable intermediate step towards more complex scheduling algorithms without the rigid memory allocation of a fixed array.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',4,2,'2025-07-21_22-40-42','The PendSV_Handler function is central to performing the actual context switching between threads. Its operation hinges on the PendSV interrupt, a \"pendable interrupt\" designed to execute only when no other interrupts are active, assuming correct priority settings. This design allows higher-priority operations, such as the SVC_Handler_Main (which initiates the context switch), to complete and restore their stack before PendSV takes over. The process begins when a context switch is requested (e.g., via an osYield system call), leading the SVC_Handler_Main to \"pend\" the PendSV interrupt by setting a specific bit in the ICSR register. An instruction synchronization barrier (asm(\"isb\");) is typically used to ensure the instruction pipeline clears. After SVC_Handler_Main finishes and restores its stack, the PendSV interrupt, no longer blocked, triggers. Inside PendSV_Handler, the current thread\'s context, specifically registers R4 through R11, is saved onto its stack, and the current Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is then stored in the current thread\'s Thread Control Block (TCB). Following this, PendSV_Handler calls the C function osSched. The osSched function\'s role is to determine the next thread to run; in a round-robin scheduler, it increments the currentThread index and uses the modulo operator to cycle through available threads. Upon osSched\'s return, PendSV_Handler loads the PSP with the stack pointer of the newly selected thread and then restores that thread\'s context by popping the saved registers from its stack. Finally, PendSV_Handler returns, and the processor resumes execution from the point where the new thread was interrupted, thereby completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',4,2,'2025-07-21_23-21-04','The PendSV_Handler function is central to performing the actual context switching between threads. Its operation hinges on the PendSV interrupt, a \"pendable interrupt\" designed to execute only when no other interrupts are active, assuming correct priority settings. This design allows higher-priority operations, such as the SVC_Handler_Main (which initiates the context switch), to complete and restore their stack before PendSV takes over. The process begins when a context switch is requested (e.g., via an osYield system call), leading the SVC_Handler_Main to \"pend\" the PendSV interrupt by setting a specific bit in the ICSR register. An instruction synchronization barrier (asm(\"isb\");) is typically used to ensure the instruction pipeline clears. After SVC_Handler_Main finishes and restores its stack, the PendSV interrupt, no longer blocked, triggers. Inside PendSV_Handler, the current thread\'s context, specifically registers R4 through R11, is saved onto its stack, and the current Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is then stored in the current thread\'s Thread Control Block (TCB). Following this, PendSV_Handler calls the C function osSched. The osSched function\'s role is to determine the next thread to run; in a round-robin scheduler, it increments the currentThread index and uses the modulo operator to cycle through available threads. Upon osSched\'s return, PendSV_Handler loads the PSP with the stack pointer of the newly selected thread and then restores that thread\'s context by popping the saved registers from its stack. Finally, PendSV_Handler returns, and the processor resumes execution from the point where the new thread was interrupted, thereby completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',4,3,'2025-07-21_22-40-42','Modifying the OS to include an integer priority in the TCB, where a higher integer signifies a higher priority, and then altering the scheduler to consistently select the task with the highest priority, is a problematic approach. This is primarily because it can lead to thread starvation. If a high-priority task is continuously ready to run, any lower-priority tasks may never get an opportunity to execute, as the scheduler will perpetually select the highest-priority task, effectively neglecting all others. This system lacks the fairness inherent in round-robin scheduling, which ensures all threads receive equal treatment and a chance to run. In contrast, a purely priority-based scheduler can indefinitely postpone lower-priority tasks, regardless of their waiting time. While not a direct deadlock, the continuous starvation of critical lower-priority tasks could indirectly lead to a system-wide stall if those tasks are responsible for releasing resources or producing outputs necessary for higher-priority tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',4,3,'2025-07-21_23-21-04','Modifying the OS to include an integer priority in the TCB, where a higher integer signifies a higher priority, and then altering the scheduler to consistently select the task with the highest priority, is a problematic approach. This is primarily because it can lead to thread starvation. If a high-priority task is continuously ready to run, any lower-priority tasks may never get an opportunity to execute, as the scheduler will perpetually select the highest-priority task, effectively neglecting all others. This system lacks the fairness inherent in round-robin scheduling, which ensures all threads receive equal treatment and a chance to run. In contrast, a purely priority-based scheduler can indefinitely postpone lower-priority tasks, regardless of their waiting time. While not a direct deadlock, the continuous starvation of critical lower-priority tasks could indirectly lead to a system-wide stall if those tasks are responsible for releasing resources or producing outputs necessary for higher-priority tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',4,4,'2025-07-21_22-40-42','Choosing to call C functions like osSched from assembly-based handlers such as PendSV_Handler offers significant advantages over writing everything in assembly, primarily enhancing readability, maintainability, portability, and development speed. C code is much easier to understand and debug, making kernel development more efficient and sustainable, and modern C compilers often generate highly optimized machine code. While an all-assembly approach might offer slightly more direct control over hardware and potentially more compact code in niche cases, it introduces complexity, reduces portability, and slows down development. The trade-off leans heavily towards C for the bulk of OS logic due to its higher-level abstractions and compiler optimizations, reserving assembly for the most critical, performance-sensitive sections that demand direct hardware manipulation.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',4,4,'2025-07-21_23-21-04','Choosing to call C functions like osSched from assembly-based handlers such as PendSV_Handler offers significant advantages over writing everything in assembly, primarily enhancing readability, maintainability, portability, and development speed. C code is much easier to understand and debug, making kernel development more efficient and sustainable, and modern C compilers often generate highly optimized machine code. While an all-assembly approach might offer slightly more direct control over hardware and potentially more compact code in niche cases, it introduces complexity, reduces portability, and slows down development. The trade-off leans heavily towards C for the bulk of OS logic due to its higher-level abstractions and compiler optimizations, reserving assembly for the most critical, performance-sensitive sections that demand direct hardware manipulation.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',4,5,'2025-07-21_22-40-42','If, instead of creating multiple threads, only a single print_continuously thread (modified to call osYield after printing) were created, the operating system would still technically function without crashing. The osKernelInit would proceed to initialize the kernel, and osThreadCreate would correctly set up the single print_continuously thread\'s stack and its Thread Control Block (TCB) within the global metadata structure. Subsequently, osKernelStart would begin the execution of this sole thread. During its execution, the print_continuously thread would print its message, for example, Thread 1. Following the print statement, it would call osYield(). This call would trigger an SVC interrupt, specifically the YIELD system call. Inside the SVC_Handler_Main function, the YIELD case would be processed, which would pend the PendSV interrupt by setting a bit in the ICSR register (ICSR |= 1<<28;). After SVC_Handler_Main completes its execution, the PendSV_Handler would then execute. Within PendSV_Handler, the context of the currently running thread (the only thread) would be saved. The osSched function would then be called to determine the next thread. However, because there is only one thread, osSched would effectively select the same thread to run next, as the calculation (currentThread + 1) % 1 would always resolve to 0. Consequently, PendSV_Handler would restore the context of this very same thread. The single print_continuously thread would then resume its execution from immediately after the osYield() call, looping back to print its message again. Therefore, while the OS infrastructure for context switching would be engaged and functional, the user would observe a continuous stream of print statements from Thread 1 without any alternation, as there would be no other threads to switch to.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',4,5,'2025-07-21_23-21-04','If, instead of creating multiple threads, only a single print_continuously thread (modified to call osYield after printing) were created, the operating system would still technically function without crashing. The osKernelInit would proceed to initialize the kernel, and osThreadCreate would correctly set up the single print_continuously thread\'s stack and its Thread Control Block (TCB) within the global metadata structure. Subsequently, osKernelStart would begin the execution of this sole thread. During its execution, the print_continuously thread would print its message, for example, Thread 1. Following the print statement, it would call osYield(). This call would trigger an SVC interrupt, specifically the YIELD system call. Inside the SVC_Handler_Main function, the YIELD case would be processed, which would pend the PendSV interrupt by setting a bit in the ICSR register (ICSR |= 1<<28;). After SVC_Handler_Main completes its execution, the PendSV_Handler would then execute. Within PendSV_Handler, the context of the currently running thread (the only thread) would be saved. The osSched function would then be called to determine the next thread. However, because there is only one thread, osSched would effectively select the same thread to run next, as the calculation (currentThread + 1) % 1 would always resolve to 0. Consequently, PendSV_Handler would restore the context of this very same thread. The single print_continuously thread would then resume its execution from immediately after the osYield() call, looping back to print its message again. Therefore, while the OS infrastructure for context switching would be engaged and functional, the user would observe a continuous stream of print statements from Thread 1 without any alternation, as there would be no other threads to switch to.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',5,1,'2025-07-21_22-46-55','Passing arguments into a thread is a collaborative effort between the user\'s code and the operating system to correctly position data for the thread\'s execution. From the user\'s side, they must define their thread function to accept a void pointer (e.g., void thread_function(void* args)), offering flexibility for various data types, including structs for multiple arguments. The user is then responsible for casting and dereferencing this pointer within the thread to access the actual data. The OS, specifically in osCreateThread, plays its part by populating the R0 register on the new thread\'s stack with the argument\'s address, leveraging the ARM ABI which uses R0 for the first function argument. This works because the thread\'s stack is initialized with these register values, allowing the processor to automatically treat the value in R0 as the thread\'s argument when it begins execution, thus enabling versatile data input without the OS needing to predict specific user data needs.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',5,2,'2025-07-21_22-46-55','Calling osYield within kernel context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler (including SVC_Handler_Main), or SysTick_Handler is strictly forbidden because it would lead to severe system instability, including interrupt handling issues, stack corruption, and reentrancy problems. Such a call would attempt to trigger a context switch while another one is already in progress or about to complete. For instance, if osYield were called in SVC_Handler_Main, it would immediately re-pend or execute PendSV_Handler before SVC_Handler_Main has finished restoring its stack, causing nested interrupt execution. This nested call would corrupt the stack and thread contexts, as the mechanisms are not designed for reentrancy, ultimately leading to unpredictable program flow, data loss, and system crashes. The careful priority setup and \"pendable\" nature of PendSV are specifically designed to avoid such recursive context switching and ensure clean thread transitions.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',5,3,'2025-07-21_22-46-55','A finer OS clock of 0.1ms offers significant benefits, including a higher temporal resolution for precise timing in demanding applications like high-speed data acquisition, enabling faster responses and reduced latency. This increased resolution also promotes improved fairness in dynamic systems by ensuring quicker context switches, preventing any single task from monopolizing the CPU. However, a major downside is the dramatically increased overhead of context switching, as SysTick_Handler would trigger ten times more frequently, consuming more CPU cycles for saving and restoring thread contexts and reducing overall system throughput. This heightened activity also leads to increased power consumption, a critical factor for energy-sensitive embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',5,4,'2025-07-21_22-46-55','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking are distinct OS models for managing concurrent execution, differing in how CPU control is relinquished. From the user\'s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires threads to explicitly call functions like osYield() to give up the CPU, putting the onus on the user to prevent starvation. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking frees the user from explicit yielding, as the OS automatically interrupts threads after a set \"timeslice.\" From the OS\'s perspective, co-operative multitasking involves providing a mechanism for threads to voluntarily trigger a context switch, while pre-emptive multitasking involves the OS actively using a hardware timer (e.g., SysTick) to periodically force context switches by decrementing a thread\'s runtime and triggering the PendSV interrupt when the timeslice expires, ensuring fairness and responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('pkotta',5,5,'2025-07-21_22-46-55','Cooperative (yielding) and pre-emptive (time-based) context switching seamlessly coexist by leveraging the same underlying PendSV_Handler for the actual context switch, which centralizes the logic for saving and restoring thread states. When a thread cooperatively yields via osYield(), it triggers an SVC call that pends the PendSV interrupt. Similarly, when a thread\'s timeslice expires due to a hardware timer like SysTick_Handler, the OS also triggers the PendSV interrupt. In both scenarios, PendSV_Handler takes over, saves the current thread\'s context, selects the next thread via osSched, and restores the new thread\'s context. This unified approach, coupled with careful interrupt priority management (e.g., SVC having higher priority than PendSV), ensures a robust and efficient multitasking system that supports both voluntary and forced thread transitions.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',1,1,'2025-05-13_10-44-28','The HAL allows us to interface with easily with the hardware by acting as an interface between the two.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',1,1,'2025-05-13_11-17-41','The HAL which stands for the hardware abstraction layer allows us to interface with easily with the hardware by acting as an interface between the two. This makes working with hardware easier as it allows for porting. Since different devices might have different hardware registers or implementations of similar functions even within the same chipset, the HAL allows for simplification by creating identical wrapper functions which abstract the specific hardware assignment.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',1,2,'2025-05-13_10-44-28','The io_putchar function sets up a character for transmission by UART to the Nucleo board using the HAL. It is a helper function required to implement the printf function. It needs to be defined by us because every embedded project and hardware is different.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',1,2,'2025-05-13_11-17-41','The io_putchar function sets up a character for transmission by UART to the Nucleo board using the HAL. It is a helper function required to implement the printf function. It needs to be defined by us because every embedded project and hardware is different.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',1,3,'2025-05-13_10-44-28','We couldn’t use printf and were forced to use the debugger because we were trying to look for the values of the MSP and running functions like printf changes the value of the MSP. So if we used printf we would be changing the value of MSP as we were running the code.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',1,3,'2025-05-13_11-17-41','We couldn’t use printf and were forced to use the debugger because we were trying to look for the values of the MSP and running functions like printf changes the value of the MSP. So if we used printf we would be changing the value of MSP as we were running the code.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',1,4,'2025-05-13_10-44-28','In order to get a new value for PSP_val we subtracted 0x400 which was the size we set for the main stack. Essentially, our allocation method is to take the entire stack memory and break it into sections, with one section reserved for the main stack and the rest broken up into thread stacks. The position of open memory in the main stack is held by msp and thread stacks open position is stored in psp. The MSP initial value tells us where the memory assignment for the stack begins from. In order to properly assign values for PSP based on the size of the main stack, we needed to know at the address at which is starts which is why we needed MSP_init_val.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',1,4,'2025-05-13_11-17-41','In order to get a new value for PSP_val we subtracted 0x400 which was the size we set for the main stack. Essentially, our allocation method is to take the entire stack memory and break it into sections, with one section reserved for the main stack and the rest broken up into thread stacks. The position of open memory in the main stack is held by msp and thread stacks open position is stored in psp. The MSP initial value tells us where the memory assignment for the stack begins from. In order to properly assign values for PSP based on the size of the main stack, we needed to know at the address at which is starts which is why we needed MSP_init_val. It is important to ensure that PSP when assigned points to a valid memory address, as otherwise the program may crash or access values which are not supposed to be accessible during runtime. This might occur if PSP is calculated from a non known starting value instead of MSP. Using MSP as a starting point we can check if PSP has been assigned to a invalid location since it will point to an address which is less than 0x0.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',1,5,'2025-05-13_10-44-28','In order to use the same allocation method for multiple new stacks, we would have to create new pointers in order to hold the position of start of each stacks and subtract the size of each stack from the previous pointer address to get the new start position starting from the msp pointer. At the creation of each new stack, a check would have to be done by comparing the previous pointer address subtracting the new stack size and comparing it to 0, to ensure that the stack size specified is not greater than the amount of memory assigned to the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',1,5,'2025-05-13_11-17-41','In order to use the same allocation method for multiple new stacks, we would have to create new pointers in order to hold the position of start of each stacks and subtract the size of each stack from the previous pointer address to get the new start position starting from the msp pointer. At the creation of each new stack, a check would have to be done by comparing the previous pointer address subtracting the new stack size and comparing it to 0, to ensure that the stack size specified is not greater than the amount of memory assigned to the stack.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,1,'2025-05-27_10-49-54','When you change the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, we essentially said to the program to jump to the print continuously function. This is because the program uses the program counter to keep track of which instruction was running in order to tell what line of code to run next.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,1,'2025-05-27_13-12-40','When you change the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, we essentially said to the program to jump to the print continuously function. This is because the program counter holds the memory address of the next instruction which will be run and increments sequentially every time an instruction is fetched. So once the PC value is changed to match print continuously and that instruction is fetched the program executes right away.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,1,'2025-05-27_17-14-20','When you change the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, we essentially tell the program to jump to the print continuously function which is equivalent to calling the function to run. This is because the program counter holds the memory address of the next instruction which will be run and increments sequentially every time an instruction is fetched. So once the PC memory address holds the memory location of the print continuously function and that instruction is fetched from memory, it runs instantly.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,1,'2025-05-27_18-13-14','When you change the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, we essentially tell the program to jump to the print continuously function which is equivalent to calling the function to run. This is because the program counter holds the memory address of the next instruction which will be run and increments sequentially every time an instruction is fetched. So once the PC memory address holds the memory location of the print continuously function and that instruction is fetched from memory, it runs instantly.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,1,'2025-05-27_18-56-20','When you change the PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, we essentially tell the program to jump to the print continuously function which is equivalent to calling the function to run. This is because the program counter holds the memory address of the next instruction which will be run and increments sequentially every time an instruction is fetched. So once the PC memory address holds the memory location of the print continuously function and that instruction is fetched from memory, it runs instantly.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,2,'2025-05-27_10-49-54','A system call is the program gives when it wants something to be handled by the operating system which it does not have access to. In a real computer this could be something like manipulating the main memory or reading from the hard drive. Since we don’t want to give the program direct power to access these things unsafely, a system call allows the operating system to expose some functionality to the programs in a way that is safe instead.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,2,'2025-05-27_13-12-40','A system call provides an interface between processes which have user access and the kernel which has much wider privileges to access and change things on the computer. In order to ensure that programs can safely and consistently access hardware without having to always implement the functionality themselves the programs use system calls to the kernel which then is able to handle the access instead safely and consistently.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,2,'2025-05-27_17-14-20','A system call is a special function call that allows user-level programs to request services from the operating system kernel, which has higher privileges. It provides a safe and consistent way for applications to access hardware or perform tasks like file operations without handling low-level details themselves.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,2,'2025-05-27_18-13-14','A system call is a special function call that allows user-level programs to request services from the operating system kernel, which has higher privileges. The system call ensures that only trusted code can access resources by giving the application a standardized functions through the system call to access hardware resources. This is important as if the users have direct access to higher level hardware such as memory access could allow programs to access invalid memory locations resulting in crashes. It also means that each program doesn’t have to implement its own access functions for the more dangerous functionality, ensuring program safety through standardization of functions used to access the hardware.\nIt provides a safe and consistent way for applications to access hardware or perform tasks like file operations without handling low-level details themselves.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,2,'2025-05-27_18-56-20','A system call is a special function call that allows user-level programs to request services from the operating system kernel, which has higher privileges. The system call ensures that only trusted code can access resources by giving the application a standardized functions through the system call to access hardware resources. This is important as if the users have direct access to higher level hardware such as memory access could allow programs to access invalid memory locations resulting in crashes. It also means that each program doesn’t have to implement its own access functions for the more dangerous functionality, ensuring program safety through standardization of functions used to access the hardware.\nIt provides a safe and consistent way for applications to access hardware or perform tasks like file operations without handling low-level details themselves.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,3,'2025-05-27_10-49-54','The threads stack was setup by taking spaces starting from PSP and working downwards and storing the register values we want to set, which in this case was just tens. Setting up a thread’s stack means to store in the values which would exist if the thread were already running and this needs to be done such that when the thread is run, the register values are moved from that stack to the actual registers to get the program to run the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,3,'2025-05-27_13-12-40','The threads stack was setup by taking spaces starting from PSP and working downwards and storing the register values we want to set, which in this case was just tens. The sequence of the values that is stored in the stack corresponds to the order in which the order these values are popped from the PSP and loaded into the hardware registers. This order must match the order of that the CPU inputs into the registers in order to restore the correct state before running the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,3,'2025-05-27_17-14-20','The threads stack was setup by taking spaces starting from PSP and working downwards and storing the register values we want to set, which in this case was just tens. The sequence of the values that is stored in the stack corresponds to the order in which the order these values are popped from the PSP and loaded into the hardware registers. This order must match the order of that the CPU inputs into the registers in order to restore the correct state before running the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,3,'2025-05-27_18-13-14','The threads stack was setup by taking spaces starting from PSP and working downwards and storing the register values we want to set, which in this case was just tens. It is required to store register values on the stack so that the context for running a particular thread state is ready before the thread is called to run. The program counter position to run a particular function as well as the values of the other registers are stored provide context for the thread when starting operation.\nThe sequence of the values that is stored in the stack corresponds to the order in which the order these values are popped from the PSP and loaded into the hardware registers. This order must match the order of that the CPU inputs into the registers in order to restore the correct state before running the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,3,'2025-05-27_18-56-20','The threads stack was setup by taking spaces starting from PSP and working downwards and storing the register values we want to set, which in this case was just tens. It is required to store register values on the stack so that the context for running a particular thread state is ready before the thread is called to run. The program counter position to run a particular function as well as the values of the other registers are stored provide context for the thread when starting operation.\nThe sequence of the values that is stored in the stack corresponds to the order in which the order these values are popped from the PSP and loaded into the hardware registers. This order must match the order of that the CPU inputs into the registers in order to restore the correct state before running the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,4,'2025-05-27_10-49-54','The run first thread function uses the psp value to decide where to start populating values for the registers from. If we did not use the set psp function, the run thread would not know where to start in order to populate values to the register and so would crash in a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,4,'2025-05-27_13-12-40','The run first thread function uses the psp value to decide where to start populating values for the registers from. The PSP value points to the position of the thread stack which has only application-level access to the hardware. The MSP points to the position of the OS kernel stack which has much higher-level access to the hardware. It is important to differentiate between the two because we want to restrict application code access by running it in the PSP stack and not the MSP stack.\nIt is required to switch from the MSP stack to the PSP stack because the PSP is the one which holds the values for the state of execution while the MSP stack as the kernel stack controls the flow of the program. Not setting PSP correctly leads to a crash because it means that the program counter is set to a random value which means a random memory address instruction is executed resulting in a crash.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,4,'2025-05-27_17-14-20','In SVC_Handler_Main, setting the PSP with __set_PSP is essential because it points to the thread’s stack, which holds the saved CPU context (register values). If we don’t set the PSP correctly before running the thread, the CPU tries to restore registers from an invalid stack location, leading to corrupted execution state—especially an invalid program counter—causing a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,4,'2025-05-27_18-13-14','In SVC_Handler_Main, setting the PSP with __set_PSP is essential because it points to the thread’s stack, which holds the saved CPU context (register values). If we don’t set the PSP correctly before running the thread, the CPU tries to restore registers from an invalid stack location, leading to corrupted execution state—especially an invalid program counter—causing a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,4,'2025-05-27_18-56-20','In SVC_Handler_Main, setting the PSP with __set_PSP is essential because it points to the thread’s stack, which holds the saved CPU context (register values). If we don’t set the PSP correctly before running the thread, the CPU tries to restore registers from an invalid stack location, leading to corrupted execution state—especially an invalid program counter—causing a hard fault. The thread stack is located at a different address than the default stack pointer in order to separate it from the main thread register values and provide space to store the updated context of the registers. This difference in location affects the behaviour of the CPU when restoring context because the CPU thinks that when it switches context it has been running that thread the whole time. If the PSP is not updated, the correct context is not loaded into the registers resulting in a crash when the thread tries to access memory addresses illegally.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,5,'2025-05-27_10-49-54','If our thread function didn’t loop continuously and only ran once, it would hard fault. This is because since we haven’t given a new place for the program counter to return it would increment by one and move to the next value after the previous line pc which is a random value.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,5,'2025-05-27_13-12-40','If our thread function didn’t loop continuously and only ran once, it would hard fault. This is because the thread’s link register which stores the place that the thread should return to once the thread ends. In this case we had filled all the register values with 10 including the link register, so the thread returned to the address of 10 causing a hard fault, which could be caused because the memory at address of 10 is empty or invalid so the program doesn’t know how to proceed.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,5,'2025-05-27_17-14-20','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned after printing, it would cause a hard fault. This is because the thread’s link register has no valid return address, so returning from the function leads the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location, crashing the system. Threads are not meant to return; they should run continuously or yield control explicitly.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,5,'2025-05-27_18-13-14','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned after printing, it would cause a hard fault. This is because the thread’s link register has no valid return address, so returning from the function leads the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location, crashing the system. Threads are not meant to return; they should run continuously or yield control explicitly.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',2,5,'2025-05-27_18-56-20','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned after printing, it would cause a hard fault. This is because the thread’s link register has no valid return address, so returning from the function leads the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location, crashing the system. Threads are not meant to return; they should run continuously or yield control explicitly.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,1,'2025-06-21_14-11-19','Multiple *.c files are compiled into a single executable through a multistage process. First the code is pre-processed, where comment is removed and header files are copied over into the file at this stage. Next the file is compiled to produce a. s file, which contains assembly code for the computer. Next the code is assembled and turned into a .o file which contains machine level instructions. The last step is linking, where all functions calls are linked with their definitions at which point the file becomes an executable file.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,1,'2025-06-22_07-53-44','Multiple *.c files are compiled into a single executable through a three stage process. First the code is pre-processed, where comment is removed and header files are copied over into the file at this stage. Next the code is assembled and turned into a .o file which contains machine level instructions. The last step is linking, where all functions calls are linked with their definitions, which means the computer searches where each of the functions are defined and pastes in their function definitions in the code at which point the file becomes an executable file.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,1,'2025-06-22_08-49-10','Multiple *.c files are compiled into a single executable through a three-stage process. First the code is pre-processed, where comment is removed and header files are copied over into the file at this stage. Next the code is compiled and turned into a .s file which contains assembly language instructions. The last step is linking, where all functions calls are linked with their definitions, which means the computer searches where each of the functions are defined and pastes in their function definitions in the code at which point the file becomes an executable file.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,1,'2025-06-24_01-29-13','Multiple *.c files are compiled into a single executable through a three-stage process. First the code is pre-processed. Here comments are removed from the code and any include files are found and copied in place of the #include tag that marked them. Additionally, macros which are not active are deleted from the code in this step. Next the code is compiled and turned into a .o file which is an object file. The last step is linking, where all functions calls are linked with their definitions, which means the computer searches where each of the functions are defined and pastes in their function definitions in the code at which point the file becomes an executable file. The linker essentially takes all the object files and combines them into one executable file that can be run by the computer.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,1,'2025-06-24_08-53-12','Multiple *.c files are compiled into a single executable through a three-stage process. First the code is pre-processed. Here comments are removed from the code and any include files are found and copied in place of the #include tag that marked them. Additionally, macros which are not active are deleted from the code in this step. Next the code is compiled and turned into a .o file which is an object file. The last step is linking, where all functions calls are linked with their definitions, which means the computer searches where each of the functions are defined and pastes in their function definitions in the code at which point the file becomes an executable file. The linker essentially takes all the object files and combines them into one executable file that can be run by the computer.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,1,'2025-06-24_09-03-35','Multiple *.c files are compiled into a single executable through a three-stage process. First the code is pre-processed. Here comments are removed from the code and any include files are found and copied in place of the #include tag that marked them. Additionally, any defined macros found and their definition is substituted into the place the macro was used at this step. Next the code is compiled and turned into a .o file which is an object file. The last step is linking, where all functions calls are linked with their definitions, which means the computer searches where each of the functions are defined and pastes in their function definitions in the code at which point the file becomes an executable file. The linker essentially takes all the object files and combines them into one executable file that can be run by the computer.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,2,'2025-06-21_14-11-19','It’s a bad idea to use an include directive on a .c file for multiple reasons. The first is that it might result in multiple definitions of a function or object type if it has already been used in the file the .c is being included into. It also increases the compiler workload since every single time the file has to be compiled, it will have to compile the entire .c file instead of just the functions prototypes which are in the .h header file.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,2,'2025-06-22_07-53-44','It’s a bad idea to use an include directive on a .c file for multiple reasons. The first is that it might result in multiple definitions of a function or object type if it has already been used in the file the .c is being included into. It also increases the compiler workload since every single time the file has to be compiled, it will have to compile the entire .c file instead of just the functions prototypes which are in the .h header file.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,2,'2025-06-22_08-49-10','It’s a bad idea to use an include directive on a .c file for multiple reasons. The first is that it might result in multiple definitions of a function or object type if it has already been used in the file the .c is being included into. It also increases the compiler workload since every single time the file has to be compiled, it will have to compile the entire .c file instead of just the functions prototypes which are in the .h header file.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,2,'2025-06-24_01-29-13','It’s a bad idea to use an include directive on a .c file for multiple reasons. The first is that it might result in multiple definitions of a function or object type if it has already been used in the file the .c is being included into. It also increases the compiler workload since every single time the file has to be compiled, it will have to compile the entire .c file instead of just the functions prototypes which are in the .h header file.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,2,'2025-06-24_08-53-12','It’s a bad idea to use an include directive on a .c file for multiple reasons. The first is that it might result in multiple definitions of a function or object type if it has already been used in the file the .c is being included into. It also increases the compiler workload since every single time the file has to be compiled, it will have to compile the entire .c file instead of just the functions prototypes which are in the .h header file.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,2,'2025-06-24_09-03-35','It’s a bad idea to use an include directive on a .c file for multiple reasons. The first is that it might result in multiple definitions of a function or object type if it has already been used in the file the .c is being included into. It also increases the compiler workload since every single time the file has to be compiled, it will have to compile the entire .c file instead of just the functions prototypes which are in the .h header file.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,3,'2025-06-21_14-11-19','From a user’s perspective, to start a thread they have to initialize the kernel and then call the create thread function, passing it the function/process that they want to run. Once the OS finishes setting up the thread, they use the run thread function to start the thread running.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,3,'2025-06-22_07-53-44','From a user’s perspective they must first write the function which they want to run in the thread.  Once that is done, they have to initialize the kernel by calling the relevant kernel_init() or os_start() function. This allows the kernel to start tracking the number of threads and to set the MSP accordingly to prepare to run threads. Then they have to call the create thread function, passing it the function they want to run as a parameter. This function allows the kernel to allocate stack space for the thread and to load the correct context into the registers in order to prepare to run the thread. Lastly a function to start the thread executing such as start_thread() has to be used. This tells the kernel to load the context into the registers and begin execution of the process thread.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,3,'2025-06-22_08-49-10','From a user’s perspective they must first write the function which they want to run in the thread.  Once that is done, they have to initialize the kernel by calling the relevant kernel_init() or os_start() function. This allows the kernel to start tracking the number of threads and to set the MSP accordingly to prepare to run threads. Then they have to call the create thread function, passing it the function they want to run as a parameter. This function allows the kernel to allocate stack space for the thread and to load the correct context into the registers in order to prepare to run the thread. Lastly a function to start the thread executing such as start_thread() has to be used. This tells the kernel to load the context into the registers and begin execution of the process thread.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,3,'2025-06-24_01-29-13','From a user’s perspective they must first write the function which they want to run in the thread.  Once that is done, they have to initialize the kernel by calling the relevant kernel_init() or os_start() function. This allows the kernel to start tracking the number of threads and to set the MSP accordingly to prepare to run threads. Then they have to call the create thread function, passing it the function they want to run as a parameter. This function allows the kernel to allocate stack space for the thread and to load the correct context into the registers in order to prepare to run the thread. Lastly a function to start the thread executing such as start_thread() has to be used. This tells the kernel to load the context into the registers and begin execution of the process thread.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,3,'2025-06-24_08-53-12','From a user’s perspective they must first write the function which they want to run in the thread.  Once that is done, they have to initialize the 