n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,4,'2025-06-22_07-53-44','The stack pool is a collection of stack memory which is assigned to allow running of threads. The stack pool is subdivided into fixed blocks that are then assigned to each thread to allow them to store their current state of the registers and pointer to the function that they will run in the thread. The operating system also uses the stack pool to store it’s state when switching to another thread so that once that thread finishes it can restore the operating system again.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,4,'2025-06-22_08-49-10','The stack pool is a collection of stack memory which is assigned to allow running of threads. The stack pool is subdivided into fixed blocks that are then assigned to each thread to allow them to store their current state of the registers and pointer to the function that they will run in the thread. The operating system also uses the stack pool to store it’s state when switching to another thread so that once that thread finishes it can restore the operating system again.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,4,'2025-06-24_01-29-13','The stack pool is a collection of stack memory which is assigned to allow running of threads. The stack pool is subdivided into fixed blocks that are then assigned to each thread to allow them to store their current state of the registers and pointer to the function that they will run in the thread. The operating system also uses the stack pool to store it’s state when switching to another thread so that once that thread finishes it can restore the operating system again.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,4,'2025-06-24_08-53-12','The stack pool is a collection of stack memory which is assigned to allow running of threads. The stack pool is subdivided into fixed blocks that are then assigned to each thread to allow them to store their current state of the registers and pointer to the function that they will run in the thread. The operating system also uses the stack pool to store it’s state when switching to another thread so that once that thread finishes it can restore the operating system again.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,4,'2025-06-24_09-03-35','The stack pool is a collection of stack memory which is assigned to allow running of threads. The stack pool is subdivided into fixed blocks that are then assigned to each thread to allow them to store their current state of the registers and pointer to the function that they will run in the thread. The operating system also uses the stack pool to store it’s state when switching to another thread so that once that thread finishes it can restore the operating system again.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,5,'2025-06-21_14-11-19','The kernel handles all privileged access. This means that all the direct memory manipulation such as assigning the PSP as well as registers as well as executing and using the system calls for the functions are done in the kernel. In the user file, there are only calls to 3 functions which provide a generic API to create a thread, the kernel initialization function, the thread create function, and the thread start function, as well as the program function which will be run by the thread. This distinction is important from a user’s perspective because it means that the user doesn’t have to care about the implementation and just has a generic API of functions to access to run functions.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,5,'2025-06-22_07-53-44','The kernel handles all privileged access. This means that all the direct memory manipulation such as assigning the PSP as well as registers as well as executing and using the system calls for the functions are done in the kernel. In the user file, there are only calls to 3 functions which provide a generic API to create a thread, the kernel initialization function, the thread create function, and the thread start function, as well as the program function which will be run by the thread. This allows the user who is defining the tasks (threads) that the program will execute through functions, doesn’t have to worry about hardware or memory management since that is taken care of by the kernel once they call each of the API functions to create and start the thread, making it easier for the user to focus on what their application needs to do and not how to deal with the hardware in order to make it function.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,5,'2025-06-22_08-49-10','The kernel handles all privileged access. This means that all the direct memory manipulation such as assigning the PSP as well as registers as well as executing and using the system calls for the functions are done in the kernel. In the user file, there are only calls to 3 functions which provide a generic API to create a thread, the kernel initialization function, the thread create function, and the thread start function, as well as the program function which will be run by the thread. This allows the user who is defining the tasks (threads) that the program will execute through functions, doesn’t have to worry about hardware or memory management since that is taken care of by the kernel once they call each of the API functions to create and start the thread, making it easier for the user to focus on what their application needs to do and not how to deal with the hardware in order to make it function.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,5,'2025-06-24_01-29-13','The kernel handles all privileged access. This means that all the direct memory manipulation such as assigning the PSP as well as registers as well as executing and using the system calls for the functions are done in the kernel. In the user file, there are only calls to 3 functions which provide a generic API to create a thread, the kernel initialization function, the thread create function, and the thread start function, as well as the program function which will be run by the thread. This allows the user who is defining the tasks (threads) that the program will execute through functions, doesn’t have to worry about hardware or memory management since that is taken care of by the kernel once they call each of the API functions to create and start the thread, making it easier for the user to focus on what their application needs to do and not how to deal with the hardware in order to make it function.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,5,'2025-06-24_08-53-12','The kernel handles all privileged access. This means that all the direct memory manipulation such as assigning the PSP as well as registers as well as executing and using the system calls for the functions are done in the kernel. In the user file, there are only calls to 3 functions which provide a generic API to create a thread, the kernel initialization function, the thread create function, and the thread start function, as well as the program function which will be run by the thread. This allows the user who is defining the tasks (threads) that the program will execute through functions, doesn’t have to worry about hardware or memory management since that is taken care of by the kernel once they call each of the API functions to create and start the thread, making it easier for the user to focus on what their application needs to do and not how to deal with the hardware in order to make it function.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',3,5,'2025-06-24_09-03-35','The kernel handles all privileged access. This means that all the direct memory manipulation such as assigning the PSP as well as registers as well as executing and using the system calls for the functions are done in the kernel. In the user file, there are only calls to 3 functions which provide a generic API to create a thread, the kernel initialization function, the thread create function, and the thread start function, as well as the program function which will be run by the thread. This allows the user who is defining the tasks (threads) that the program will execute through functions, doesn’t have to worry about hardware or memory management since that is taken care of by the kernel once they call each of the API functions to create and start the thread, making it easier for the user to focus on what their application needs to do and not how to deal with the hardware in order to make it function.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,1,'2025-07-09_15-32-35','Using a queue to implement round robin scheduling can be done by having the threads be enqueued into the queue with the queue holding the functions stack pointer and function pointer for each thread in order. When a thread has to be run, it is dequeued from the front of the queue and runs until the task finishes or the interrupt is generated. If the thread is not yet finished, it is enqueued at the back of the queue, and if it is finished, can be simply discarded from the queue. The advantage of the queue over an array is that once tasks are finished, the order of tasks are automatically moved up instead of having to do individual access on each array element in order to update the order.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,1,'2025-07-09_15-59-49','Using a queue to implement round robin scheduling can be done by having the threads be enqueued into the queue with the queue holding the functions stack pointer and function pointer for each thread in order. When a thread has to be run, it is dequeued from the front of the queue and runs until the task finishes or the interrupt is generated. If the thread is not yet finished, it is enqueued at the back of the queue, and if it is finished, can be simply discarded from the queue. The advantage of the queue over an array is that once tasks are finished, the order of tasks are automatically moved up instead of having to do individual access on each array element in order to update the order. The current round-robin scheduler already in a way behaves like a queue since it moves from the first to last elements in the array structure over and over again. A queue is simply a more organised and efficient data structure to implement this ordering behaviour. In addition a queue is better suited to the task we need since we only need access to the first thread in the queue to run and the last position in the queue to enqueue new threads, so the extra overhead of an array which allows you to access any element is not required.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,1,'2025-07-09_16-25-52','Using a queue to implement round robin scheduling can be done by having the threads be enqueued into the queue with the queue holding the functions stack pointer and function pointer for each thread in order. When a thread has to be run, it is dequeued from the front of the queue and runs until the task finishes or the interrupt is generated. If the thread is not yet finished, it is enqueued at the back of the queue, and if it is finished, can be simply discarded from the queue. The advantage of the queue over an array is that once tasks are finished, the order of tasks are automatically moved up instead of having to do individual access on each array element in order to update the order. The current round-robin scheduler already in a way behaves like a queue since it moves from the first to last elements in the array structure over and over again. A queue is simply a more organised and efficient data structure to implement this ordering behaviour. In addition a queue is better suited to the task we need since we only need access to the first thread in the queue to run and the last position in the queue to enqueue new threads, so the extra overhead of an array which allows you to access any element is not required.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,1,'2025-07-21_15-40-09','Using a queue to implement round robin scheduling can be done by having the threads be enqueued into the queue with the queue holding the functions stack pointer and function pointer for each thread in order. When a thread has to be run, it is dequeued from the front of the queue and runs until the task finishes or the interrupt is generated. If the thread is not yet finished, it is enqueued at the back of the queue, and if it is finished, can be simply discarded from the queue. The advantage of the queue over an array is that once tasks are finished, the order of tasks are automatically moved up instead of having to do individual access on each array element in order to update the order. The current round-robin scheduler already in a way behaves like a queue since it moves from the first to last elements in the array structure over and over again. A queue is simply a more organised and efficient data structure to implement this ordering behaviour. In addition, a queue is better suited to the task we need since we only need access to the first thread in the queue to run and the last position in the queue to enqueue new threads, so the extra overhead of an array which allows you to access any element is not required.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,1,'2025-07-21_15-43-34','Using a queue to implement round robin scheduling can be done by having the threads be enqueued into the queue with the queue holding the functions stack pointer and function pointer for each thread in order. When a thread has to be run, it is dequeued from the front of the queue and runs until the task finishes or the interrupt is generated. If the thread is not yet finished, it is enqueued at the back of the queue, and if it is finished, can be simply discarded from the queue. The advantage of the queue over an array is that once tasks are finished, the order of tasks are automatically moved up instead of having to do individual access on each array element in order to update the order. The current round-robin scheduler already in a way behaves like a queue since it moves from the first to last elements in the array structure over and over again. A queue is simply a more organised and efficient data structure to implement this ordering behaviour. In addition, a queue is better suited to the task we need since we only need access to the first thread in the queue to run and the last position in the queue to enqueue new threads, so the extra overhead of an array which allows you to access any element is not required.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,1,'2025-07-21_18-35-59','Using a queue to implement round robin scheduling can be done by having the threads be enqueued into the queue with the queue holding the functions stack pointer and function pointer for each thread in order. When a thread has to be run, it is dequeued from the front of the queue and runs until the task finishes or the interrupt is generated. If the thread is not yet finished, it is enqueued at the back of the queue, and if it is finished, can be simply discarded from the queue. The advantage of the queue over an array is that once tasks are finished, the order of tasks are automatically moved up instead of having to do individual access on each array element in order to update the order. The current round-robin scheduler already in a way behaves like a queue since it moves from the first to last elements in the array structure over and over again. A queue is simply a more organised and efficient data structure to implement this ordering behaviour. In addition, a queue is better suited to the task we need since we only need access to the first thread in the queue to run and the last position in the queue to enqueue new threads, so the extra overhead of an array which allows you to access any element is not required.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,2,'2025-07-09_15-32-35','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch when triggered by the pendable interrupt by loading the registers into physical memory to save the context of the previous thread, then executing the Scheduling function to queue up the next thread and load in it’s context into the registers to allow the next thread to be ready to run.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,2,'2025-07-09_15-59-49','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch when triggered by the pendable interrupt by loading the registers into physical memory to save the context of the previous thread, then executing the Scheduling function to queue up the next thread and pushing its content onto the stack to allow the next thread to be ready to run. The stack pointer is used to locate a thread’s stack in the Cortex M4, and so to context switch, the stack pointer of the program has to be updated to the stack pointer of the next running thread’s stack. During a context switch the registers R1 to R14 are saved and restored, and they should be stored in the stack of the thread for which they provide context. The function which determines the next thread to run is OSsched() or the scheduler.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,2,'2025-07-09_16-25-52','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch when triggered by the pendable interrupt. The first step it executes is located the stack location of the current running thread, through looking for the PSP of the current running thread. It then stores the registers R4-R11 of the current thread, which contain the context for the thread into memory. Next it calls the scheduling function osSched(). This function determines the next thread that should be run and sets the PSP to match the stack pointer of the next thread which will be run. Next using the new updated value of PSP, the context for the new thread is popped into registers R4-R11 on the stack. This finishes the context switch that then allows the next thread to run.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,2,'2025-07-21_15-40-09','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch when triggered by the pendable interrupt. The first step it executes is located the stack location of the current running thread, through reading the PSP of the current running thread. It then stores the registers R4-R11 of the current thread, which contain the context for the thread are pushed onto the stack to be stored. Next it calls the scheduling function osSched(). This function determines the next thread that should be run. Then it locates the new thread’s stack and sets the PSP to match the stack pointer of the next new thread which will be run. Next using the new updated value of PSP, the context for the new thread is popped into registers R4-R11 on the stack. This finishes the context switch that then allows the next thread to run.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,2,'2025-07-21_15-43-34','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch when triggered by the pendable interrupt. The first step it executes is located the stack location of the current running thread, through reading the PSP of the current running thread. It then stores the registers R4-R11 of the current thread, which contain the context for the thread are pushed onto the stack to be stored. Next it calls the scheduling function osSched(). This function determines the next thread that should be run. Then it locates the new thread’s stack and sets the PSP to match the stack pointer of the next new thread which will be run. Next using the new updated value of PSP, the context for the new thread is popped into registers R4-R11 on the stack. This finishes the context switch that then allows the next thread to run.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,2,'2025-07-21_18-35-59','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch when triggered by the pendable interrupt. The first step it executes is located the stack location of the current running thread, through reading the PSP of the current running thread. It then stores the registers R4-R11 of the current thread, which contain the context for the thread are pushed onto the stack to be stored. Next it calls the scheduling function osSched(). This function determines the next thread that should be run. Then it locates the new thread’s stack and sets the PSP to match the stack pointer of the next new thread which will be run. Next using the new updated value of PSP, the context for the new thread is popped into registers R4-R11 on the stack. This finishes the context switch that then allows the next thread to run.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,3,'2025-07-09_15-32-35','It is a bad idea to add an integer priority to the TCB because if these high priority tasks take a large amount of CPU bursts to run, the higher priority tasks can be queued to run before the tasks are finished. This leaves lower priority tasks starved for CPU time as they are never the highest priority task in the TCB.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,3,'2025-07-09_15-59-49','It is a bad idea to add an integer priority to the TCB because if these high priority tasks take a large amount of CPU bursts to run, the higher priority tasks can be queued to run before the tasks are finished. This leaves lower priority tasks starved for CPU time as they are never the highest priority task in the TCB. This would mean that only the highest priority task would ever run. Since with priority, no task can ever pre-empt the current running task, the highest priority task would be able to always use all the CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,3,'2025-07-09_16-25-52','It is a bad idea to add an integer priority to the TCB because if these high priority tasks take a large amount of CPU bursts to run, the higher priority tasks can be queued to run before the tasks are finished. This leaves lower priority tasks starved for CPU time as they are never the highest priority task in the TCB. This would mean that only the highest priority task would ever run. Since with priority, no task can ever pre-empt the current running task, the highest priority task would be able to always use all the CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,3,'2025-07-21_15-40-09','It is a bad idea to add an integer priority to the TCB because if these high priority tasks take a large amount of CPU bursts to run, the higher priority tasks can be queued to run before the tasks are finished. This leaves lower priority tasks starved for CPU time as they are never the highest priority task in the TCB. This would mean that only the highest priority task would ever run. Since with priority, no task can ever pre-empt the current running task, the highest priority task would be able to always use all the CPU time.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,3,'2025-07-21_15-43-34','It is a bad idea to add an integer priority to the TCB because if these high priority tasks take a large amount of CPU bursts to run, the higher priority tasks can be queued to run before the tasks are finished. This leaves lower priority tasks starved for CPU time as they are never the highest priority task in the TCB. This would mean that only the highest priority task would ever run. Since with priority, no task can ever pre-empt the current running task, the highest priority task would be able to always use all the CPU time.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,3,'2025-07-21_18-35-59','It is a bad idea to add an integer priority to the TCB because if these high priority tasks take a large amount of CPU bursts to run, the higher priority tasks can be queued to run before the tasks are finished. This leaves lower priority tasks starved for CPU time as they are never the highest priority task in the TCB. This would mean that only the highest priority task would ever run. Since with priority, no task can ever pre-empt the current running task, the highest priority task would be able to always use all the CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,4,'2025-07-09_15-32-35','The use of C functions makes it easier for the operating system programmer to implement more complicated scheduling algorithms as required, which is harder to implement in low level languages like assembly. The advantage of using assembly is for startup code, since assembly doesn’t need a existing stack setup to run while using C functions does. It also allows us greater control over the register directly as opposed to using C functions.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,4,'2025-07-09_15-59-49','The use of C functions makes it easier for the operating system programmer to implement more complicated scheduling algorithms as required, which is harder to implement in low level languages like assembly. The advantage of using assembly is for startup code, since assembly doesn’t need a existing stack setup to run while using C functions does. It also allows us greater control over the register directly as opposed to using C functions.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,4,'2025-07-09_16-25-52','The use of C functions makes it easier for the operating system programmer to implement more complicated scheduling algorithms as required, which is harder to implement in low level languages like assembly. The advantage of using assembly is for startup code, since assembly doesn’t need a existing stack setup to run while using C functions does. It also allows us greater control over the register directly as opposed to using C functions.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,4,'2025-07-21_15-40-09','The use of C functions makes it easier for the operating system programmer to implement more complicated scheduling algorithms as required, which is harder to implement in low level languages like assembly. The advantage of using assembly is for startup code, since assembly doesn’t need a existing stack setup to run while using C functions does. It also allows us greater control over the register directly as opposed to using C functions.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,4,'2025-07-21_15-43-34','The use of C functions makes it easier for the operating system programmer to implement more complicated scheduling algorithms as required, which is harder to implement in low level languages like assembly. The advantage of using assembly is for startup code, since assembly doesn’t need a existing stack setup to run while using C functions does. It also allows us greater control over the register directly as opposed to using C functions.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,4,'2025-07-21_18-35-59','The use of C functions makes it easier for the operating system programmer to implement more complicated scheduling algorithms as required, which is harder to implement in low level languages like assembly. The advantage of using assembly is for startup code, since assembly doesn’t need a existing stack setup to run while using C functions does. It also allows us greater control over the register directly as opposed to using C functions.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,5,'2025-07-09_15-32-35','The OS would still work with a single thread. This is because the scheduler function OS schedule always circularly deploys the threads in which are queued. With only 1 thread, the next scheduled thread would be thread+1 modulo 1 and so will only run the first thread.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,5,'2025-07-09_15-59-49','The OS would still work with a single thread. This is because once the print_continiously thread yielded to the OS, the scheduler would start the next thread in the queue to run and reschedule the current thread. However, since there is only no other threads, the only one present in the queue would be print_continiously which has been rescheduled. As a result print_continiously would run continuously which is equivalent to the behaviour when not running multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,5,'2025-07-09_16-25-52','The OS would still work with a single thread. This is because once the print_continiously thread yielded to the OS, the scheduler would start the next thread in the queue to run and reschedule the current thread. However, since there is only no other threads, the only one present in the queue would be print_continiously which has been rescheduled. As a result print_continiously would run continuously which is equivalent to the behaviour when not running multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,5,'2025-07-21_15-40-09','The OS would still work with a single thread. This is because once the print_continiously thread yielded to the OS, the scheduler would start the next thread in the queue to run and reschedule the current thread. However, since there is only no other threads, the only one present in the queue would be print_continiously which has been rescheduled. As a result print_continiously would run continuously which is equivalent to the behaviour when not running multiple threads.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,5,'2025-07-21_15-43-34','The OS would still work with a single thread. This is because once the print_continiously thread yielded to the OS, the scheduler would start the next thread in the queue to run and reschedule the current thread. However, since there is only no other threads, the only one present in the queue would be print_continiously which has been rescheduled. As a result print_continiously would run continuously which is equivalent to the behaviour when not running multiple threads.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',4,5,'2025-07-21_18-35-59','The OS would still work with a single thread. This is because once the print_continiously thread yielded to the OS, the scheduler would start the next thread in the queue to run and reschedule the current thread. However, since there is only no other threads, the only one present in the queue would be print_continiously which has been rescheduled. As a result print_continiously would run continuously which is equivalent to the behaviour when not running multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',5,1,'2025-07-26_19-21-22','In order to pass an argument into a thread, the user has to first specify an argument into the create thread function in order to pass an argument. This argument takes the form of a void* pointer within the function which is then cast to uint32_t and pushed to the thread stack at the location which will be filled into R0. This pushing of values on the thread stack is handled by the O.S. Next the O.S is able to load the pointer into the thread stack at R_0 when the kernel start function is called and the thread is scheduled to run. This method can accommodate multiple input arguments since even though say an array of arguments will take up multiple memory locations, the pointer to an array only takes up 1 memory location, allowing them to be stored in only one register R0.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',5,1,'2025-07-26_21-01-49','In order to pass an argument into a thread, the user has to first specify an argument into the create thread function in order to pass an argument. This argument takes the form of a void* pointer within the function which is then cast to uint32_t and pushed to the thread stack at the location which will be filled into R0. This pushing of values on the thread stack is handled by the O.S. Next the O.S is able to load the pointer into the thread stack at R_0 when the kernel start function is called and the thread is scheduled to run. This method can accommodate multiple input arguments since even though say an array of arguments will take up multiple memory locations, the pointer to an array only takes up 1 memory location, allowing them to be stored in only one register R0. This method works because the Application Binary Interface which defines how functions are called at the binary level. They determine which registers are used for passing arguments and how many arguments can be passed in registers before it spills over to the stack. This acts as a common contract between the compiled code and the execution environment allow them to work together. Additionally, the environment enforces this through the compiler which creates code that follows the ABI for the target platform as well as system call interfaces which uses a ABI defined mechanism to pass arguments to the kernel. It’s important to understand the ABI when passing arguments between the user and the OS in order to format system call arguments in a format expected by the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',5,2,'2025-07-26_19-21-22','OSYield cannot be called in any of the context-switching functions because that would cause an infinite loop. This is because the osYield itself triggers the PendSV_Handler and the SVC_Handler. This is bad because once a thread calls to yield, it will loop, and no other thread will be able to run. This is because PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler and associated functions are used in order to setup the context switch by scheduling the next thread to run and prompting the pend able interrupt for the switching. So putting osYield in these functions means that the functions would never be able to complete the context switch before another switch is called for, creating the loop.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',5,2,'2025-07-26_21-01-49','OSYield cannot be called in any of the context-switching functions because that would cause an infinite loop, ending in a stack overflow and hard fault. In order to initiate a context switch, osYield uses a system call interrupt in order to trigger the PendSV_Handler to initiate a context switch for the thread. If an interrupt is called while already inside the interrupt handler, it would mean that the handler could never complete the context switch, as it would be continuously interrupted. Additionally, if the interrupt handler is re-entered before it completes, the stack may become corrupted due to overlapping stack frame saves, or overflow as more and more duplicate thread stacks are pushed to the stack. This will eventually lead to stack overflow and a hard fault. Additionally, since any context-switching functions such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, and SVC_Handler_Main, and SysTick_Handler are all involved in context switching, placing a call to osYield within any of these functions will lead to triggering an interrupt within the interrupt handler chain.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',5,3,'2025-07-26_19-21-22','The benefits of having a fundamental OS clock speed at 0.1ms means that threads can be switched out faster since the maximum that a interrupt can be switched out depends on the fundamental clock speed. However, this also increases the processor overhead since it will be receiving 10 times as many interrupts from the O.S clock as it was when it was 1ms.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',5,3,'2025-07-26_21-01-49','The benefits of having a fundamental OS clock speed at 0.1ms has several benefits. In an RTOS system being able to meet deadlines is a critical functionality. Increasing the clock speed increases the rate at which the system can respond to inputs, increasing responsiveness. This is especially true for allowing more chances for higher-priority threads to pre-empt lower priority ones, reducing latency. It also increases the CPU utilization of the system, since if a thread blocks for I/O input, it can be switched out faster for another thread that requires CPU bursts.  However, it also has downsides. An increase in clock speed means that that the processor overhead increases since it will have to deal with 10times as many interrupts from the O.S clock as before. Additionally, since the CPU is being used much more intensely, the power consumption of the system will also increase proportionately to the clock speed increase. Additionally, for systems which are primarily I/O bound as opposed to CPU bound, then an increase in fundamental clock speed may not come with any large increase in processor throughput at all.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',5,4,'2025-07-26_19-21-22','From the user’s perspective there are differences between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking. For a user coding a co-operative thread function, they have to ensure that once their thread task completes, they call the OSyield functionality in order to signal to the OS that the task is done and another one can be scheduled. In pre-emptive multitasking, the user is not required to call OSYield, only instead set the runtime which will be given to the thread. Once the time allotted to a thread is complete, the timer interrupt will run, and the thread will be swapped out for the next thread to run. For the OS in co-operative multithreading, the O.S. only needs to wait for the thread function to call the yield function. For pre-emptive multitasking, the O.S waits until the timer interrupt is finished before pre-emptively swapping the current running thread. In order to do that the O.S must save the current state of execution of the thread being interrupted in case it will run again.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',5,4,'2025-07-26_21-01-49','From the user’s perspective there are differences between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking. For a user coding a co-operative thread function, they have to ensure that once their thread task completes, they call the OSyield functionality in order to signal to the OS that the task is done and another one can be scheduled. In pre-emptive multitasking, the user is not required to call OSYield, only instead set the runtime which will be given to the thread. Once the time allotted to a thread is complete, the timer interrupt will run, and the thread will be swapped out for the next thread to run. For the OS in co-operative multithreading, the O.S. only needs to wait for the thread function to call the yield function. For pre-emptive multitasking, the O.S waits until the timer interrupt is finished before pre-emptively swapping the current running thread. In order to do that the O.S must save the current state of execution of the thread being interrupted in case it will run again.\n',1,'s25'),('pmahabal',5,5,'2025-07-26_19-21-22','It is possible for yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching to work together simply by allowing the time-based pre-emptive context switching to have precedence. By having the timer interrupt always running, if the thread tries to run for longer than the timeout time, it will be switched by the timer interrupt. However, it yields willingly then it will call the PendSV handler interrupt and be switched out. This way there will always be a maximum time bound on how a thread can run.\n',0,'s25'),('pmahabal',5,5,'2025-07-26_21-01-49','It is possible for yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching to work together simply by allowing the time-based pre-emptive context switching to have precedence. By having the timer interrupt always running, if the thread tries to run for longer than the timeout time, it will be switched by the timer interrupt. However, it yields willingly then it will call the PendSV handler interrupt and be switched out. This way there will always be a maximum time bound on how a thread can run. This is possible because yielding and time-based pre-emption ultimately lead to the use of the same handler and interrupt the PendSV handler in order to perform the context switch.  The timer interrupt doesn’t directly perform the context switch but instead signals an interrupt to the PendSV handler to trigger a pend able interrupt. In this way the trigger for a context switch differs from yielding where the thread allows to be switched willingly vs time based where the thread is pre-empted by the interrupt, but uses the same mechanism of the PendSV handler.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-23_11-34-03','HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer is a set of high level functions that simplifies communication with microcontroller hardware which the IDE usually does for us.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-23_12-05-03','HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer is a set of high level functions that simplifies communication with microcontroller hardware which the IDE usually does for us.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-23_12-18-43','HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer is a set of high level functions that simplifies communication with microcontroller hardware which the IDE usually does for us.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-24_14-11-32','HAL, or Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a collection of high-level functions provided by the microcontroller vendor to simplify low-level hardware control. It is not part of the IDE, but rather works with it. HAL helps developers avoid writing direct register-level code by offering reusable functions for hardware tasks. In addition, HAL ensures portability across different microcontrollers from the same vendor because it abstracts away the hardware-specific details. This allows developers to focus on functionality rather than spending time learning each peripheral\'s register map.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-24_17-15-35','HAL, or Hardware Abstraction Layer, is a collection of high-level functions provided by the microcontroller vendor to simplify low-level hardware control. It is not part of the IDE, but rather works with it. HAL helps developers avoid writing direct register-level code by offering reusable functions for hardware tasks. In addition, HAL ensures portability across different microcontrollers from the same vendor because it abstracts away the hardware-specific details. This allows developers to focus on functionality rather than spending time learning each peripheral\'s register map. Using HAL also helps standardize development workflows and speeds up prototyping, especially when transitioning between different STM32 devices. It’s particularly helpful for teams working on large embedded systems, where hardware abstraction improves code maintainability.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-24_17-26-44','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations. Furthermore, knowing the initial MSP value allows us to maintain memory safety by ensuring no overlap between stacks, preventing potential data corruption or unexpected behavior when switching between handler and thread modes. This method of calculating the PSP is foundational for context switching in an RTOS, where multiple tasks require independent stack spaces. Knowing MSP_INIT_VAL gives a reliable starting point for partitioning memory safely.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-24_19-01-02','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations. Furthermore, knowing the initial MSP value allows us to maintain memory safety by ensuring no overlap between stacks, preventing potential data corruption or unexpected behavior when switching between handler and thread modes. This method of calculating the PSP is foundational for context switching in an RTOS, where multiple tasks require independent stack spaces. Knowing MSP_INIT_VAL gives a reliable starting point for partitioning memory safely.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-24_19-07-53','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of vendor-provided software routines that simplify hardware interaction by abstracting low-level register access. It allows developers to control peripherals like timers, GPIOs, and communication interfaces without writing direct hardware code. HAL integrates with development tools but is separate from the IDE. Its abstraction promotes portability across devices in the same microcontroller family, improving code readability, maintainability, and development speed. By standardizing APIs, HAL also helps prevent hardware-specific bugs and streamlines embedded system design across different platforms.',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-24_19-15-01','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of vendor-provided software routines that simplify hardware interaction by abstracting low-level register access. It allows developers to control peripherals like timers, GPIOs, and communication interfaces without writing direct hardware code. HAL integrates with development tools but is separate from the IDE. Its abstraction promotes portability across devices in the same microcontroller family, improving code readability, maintainability, and development speed. By standardizing APIs, HAL also helps prevent hardware-specific bugs and streamlines embedded system design across different platforms.',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-24_19-25-27','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of vendor-provided software routines that simplify hardware interaction by abstracting low-level register access. It allows developers to control peripherals like timers, GPIOs, and communication interfaces without writing direct hardware code. HAL integrates with development tools but is separate from the IDE. Its abstraction promotes portability across devices in the same microcontroller family, improving code readability, maintainability, and development speed. By standardizing APIs, HAL also helps prevent hardware-specific bugs and streamlines embedded system design across different platforms.',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,1,'2025-06-24_19-32-07','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of vendor-provided software routines that simplify hardware interaction by abstracting low-level register access. It allows developers to control peripherals like timers, GPIOs, and communication interfaces without writing direct hardware code. HAL integrates with development tools but is separate from the IDE. Its abstraction promotes portability across devices in the same microcontroller family, improving code readability, maintainability, and development speed. By standardizing APIs, HAL also helps prevent hardware-specific bugs and streamlines embedded system design across different platforms.',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,2,'2025-06-23_11-34-03','The function basically just writes one character to the appropriate I/O channel, which printf uses.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,2,'2025-06-23_12-05-03','__io_putchar tells printf how to send characters over UART. It\'s needed so that printf can work on embedded systems, since it doesn’t know how to output by default.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,2,'2025-06-23_12-18-43','__io_putchar tells printf how to send characters over UART. It\'s needed so that printf can work on embedded systems, since it doesn’t know how to output by default.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,2,'2025-06-24_14-11-32','__io_putchar tells printf how to send output, such as over UART. It’s required because embedded systems don’t have a default output device, so this function maps standard output to the specific hardware being used, making the code more flexible and portable. Moreover, this redirection allows standard C functions like printf() to be used in embedded contexts, which improves code readability and debugging efficiency without altering the behavior of core C libraries.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,2,'2025-06-24_17-15-35','__io_putchar tells printf how to send output, such as over UART. It’s required because embedded systems don’t have a default output device, so this function maps standard output to the specific hardware being used, making the code more flexible and portable. Moreover, this redirection allows standard C functions like printf() to be used in embedded contexts, which improves code readability and debugging efficiency without altering the behavior of core C libraries. This function is a low-level bridge between software and hardware, ensuring that output from printf() actually gets routed to the appropriate peripheral, such as USART2. It’s one of the key ways to debug in systems where traditional stdout doesn’t exist.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,2,'2025-06-24_19-07-53','The __io_putchar function defines how characters output by printf are transmitted on an embedded system, which lacks a standard output device. It typically redirects output to a UART peripheral, such as USART2. Each time printf is called, it sends characters to __io_putchar, which then handles the low-level transmission. Without this function, printf wouldn’t work because there would be no defined path to send data. Implementing __io_putchar allows developers to use standard C I/O functions for debugging and logging while keeping the hardware-specific details isolated and the code more portable and readable.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,2,'2025-06-24_19-15-01','The __io_putchar function defines how characters output by printf are transmitted on an embedded system, which lacks a standard output device. It typically redirects output to a UART peripheral, such as USART2. Each time printf is called, it sends characters to __io_putchar, which then handles the low-level transmission. Without this function, printf wouldn’t work because there would be no defined path to send data. Implementing __io_putchar allows developers to use standard C I/O functions for debugging and logging while keeping the hardware-specific details isolated and the code more portable and readable.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,2,'2025-06-24_19-25-27','The __io_putchar function defines how characters output by printf are transmitted on an embedded system, which lacks a standard output device. It typically redirects output to a UART peripheral, such as USART2. Each time printf is called, it sends characters to __io_putchar, which then handles the low-level transmission. Without this function, printf wouldn’t work because there would be no defined path to send data. Implementing __io_putchar allows developers to use standard C I/O functions for debugging and logging while keeping the hardware-specific details isolated and the code more portable and readable.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,2,'2025-06-24_19-32-07','The __io_putchar function defines how characters output by printf are transmitted on an embedded system, which lacks a standard output device. It typically redirects output to a UART peripheral, such as USART2. Each time printf is called, it sends characters to __io_putchar, which then handles the low-level transmission. Without this function, printf wouldn’t work because there would be no defined path to send data. Implementing __io_putchar allows developers to use standard C I/O functions for debugging and logging while keeping the hardware-specific details isolated and the code more portable and readable.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,3,'2025-06-23_11-34-03','We used the debugger because printf only shows what we explicitly output, but the debugger lets us pause the program, inspect register values, memory, and execution flow in real time.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,3,'2025-06-23_12-05-03','We used the debugger because printf only shows what we explicitly output, but the debugger lets us pause the program, inspect register values, memory, and execution flow in real time.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,3,'2025-06-23_12-18-43','We used the debugger because printf only shows what we explicitly output, but the debugger lets us pause the program, inspect register values, memory, and execution flow in real time.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,3,'2025-06-24_14-11-32','We used the debugger because it allows us to observe what’s happening inside the microcontroller in real time, like register values and execution flow. printf only shows pre-defined outputs, but the debugger helps find problems we didn’t expect. Additionally, the debugger lets us set breakpoints, inspect memory and variables, and step through code execution line-by-line. This level of control is essential for diagnosing issues like incorrect register values or invalid memory access that printf cannot reveal.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,3,'2025-06-24_17-15-35','We used the debugger because it allows us to observe what’s happening inside the microcontroller in real time, like register values and execution flow. printf only shows pre-defined outputs, but the debugger helps find problems we didn’t expect. Additionally, the debugger lets us set breakpoints, inspect memory and variables, and step through code execution line-by-line. This level of control is essential for diagnosing issues like incorrect register values or invalid memory access that printf cannot reveal. In this lab, the debugger was especially useful for verifying stack pointer behavior and checking register assignments, which would’ve been nearly impossible to observe using only printf statements.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,3,'2025-06-24_19-07-53','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations. Furthermore, knowing the initial MSP value allows us to maintain memory safety by ensuring no overlap between stacks, preventing potential data corruption or unexpected behavior when switching between handler and thread modes. This method of calculating the PSP is foundational for context switching in an RTOS, where multiple tasks require independent stack spaces. Knowing MSP_INIT_VAL gives a reliable starting point for partitioning memory safely.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,3,'2025-06-24_19-15-01','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations. Furthermore, knowing the initial MSP value allows us to maintain memory safety by ensuring no overlap between stacks, preventing potential data corruption or unexpected behavior when switching between handler and thread modes. This method of calculating the PSP is foundational for context switching in an RTOS, where multiple tasks require independent stack spaces. Knowing MSP_INIT_VAL gives a reliable starting point for partitioning memory safely.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,3,'2025-06-24_19-25-27','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations. Furthermore, knowing the initial MSP value allows us to maintain memory safety by ensuring no overlap between stacks, preventing potential data corruption or unexpected behavior when switching between handler and thread modes. This method of calculating the PSP is foundational for context switching in an RTOS, where multiple tasks require independent stack spaces. Knowing MSP_INIT_VAL gives a reliable starting point for partitioning memory safely.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,3,'2025-06-24_19-32-07','We used a debugger because it gives direct visibility into the microcontroller’s internal operations, including the contents of registers, the stack, and control flow. Unlike printf, which only outputs specific values to a console and depends on functional I/O, the debugger lets us pause the program, inspect memory, and step through execution. This is especially useful in embedded systems where certain bugs—like misconfigured registers or incorrect stack usage—cannot be detected with print statements alone. The debugger enables thorough investigation of low-level issues that would otherwise be hidden, making it an essential tool for development and debugging.',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,4,'2025-06-23_11-34-03','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,4,'2025-06-23_12-05-03','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,4,'2025-06-23_12-18-43','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,4,'2025-06-24_14-11-32','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations. Furthermore, knowing the initial MSP value allows us to maintain memory safety by ensuring no overlap between stacks, preventing potential data corruption or unexpected behavior when switching between handler and thread modes.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,4,'2025-06-24_17-15-35','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations. Furthermore, knowing the initial MSP value allows us to maintain memory safety by ensuring no overlap between stacks, preventing potential data corruption or unexpected behavior when switching between handler and thread modes. This method of calculating the PSP is foundational for context switching in an RTOS, where multiple tasks require independent stack spaces. Knowing MSP_INIT_VAL gives a reliable starting point for partitioning memory safely.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,4,'2025-06-24_19-07-53','To create multiple valid thread stacks, we start from a known base address—typically the initial Main Stack Pointer (MSP)—and subtract a fixed offset for each thread. For instance, if we reserve 0x400 bytes per stack, the first thread would use MSP_INIT - 0x400, the second MSP_INIT - 0x800, and so on. This method ensures proper separation between stacks, prevents overlap, and maintains predictable memory layout. It’s important to ensure 8-byte alignment (as required by ARM Cortex-M) and to verify that all stack addresses fall within valid memory regions. By defining an array of stack pointers at compile time using these calculated offsets, we can safely manage multiple threads and enable basic context switching, even in systems without a full RTOS. This technique makes thread management simple, reliable, and memory-safe.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,4,'2025-06-24_19-15-01','To create multiple valid thread stacks, we start from a known base address—typically the initial Main Stack Pointer (MSP)—and subtract a fixed offset for each thread. For instance, if we reserve 0x400 bytes per stack, the first thread would use MSP_INIT - 0x400, the second MSP_INIT - 0x800, and so on. This method ensures proper separation between stacks, prevents overlap, and maintains predictable memory layout. It’s important to ensure 8-byte alignment (as required by ARM Cortex-M) and to verify that all stack addresses fall within valid memory regions. By defining an array of stack pointers at compile time using these calculated offsets, we can safely manage multiple threads and enable basic context switching, even in systems without a full RTOS. This technique makes thread management simple, reliable, and memory-safe.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,4,'2025-06-24_19-25-27','To create multiple valid thread stacks, we start from a known base address—typically the initial Main Stack Pointer (MSP)—and subtract a fixed offset for each thread. For instance, if we reserve 0x400 bytes per stack, the first thread would use MSP_INIT - 0x400, the second MSP_INIT - 0x800, and so on. This method ensures proper separation between stacks, prevents overlap, and maintains predictable memory layout. It’s important to ensure 8-byte alignment (as required by ARM Cortex-M) and to verify that all stack addresses fall within valid memory regions. By defining an array of stack pointers at compile time using these calculated offsets, we can safely manage multiple threads and enable basic context switching, even in systems without a full RTOS. This technique makes thread management simple, reliable, and memory-safe.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,4,'2025-06-24_19-32-07','We first read MSP_INIT_VAL from address 0x0, which tells us where the main stack starts. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted 0x400 from MSP_INIT_VAL, giving us a separate region in memory to use for the PSP. We needed the initial MSP value to ensure that PSP points to a valid, non-overlapping area in RAM for thread-level stack operations. Furthermore, knowing the initial MSP value allows us to maintain memory safety by ensuring no overlap between stacks, preventing potential data corruption or unexpected behavior when switching between handler and thread modes. This method of calculating the PSP is foundational for context switching in an RTOS, where multiple tasks require independent stack spaces. Knowing MSP_INIT_VAL gives a reliable starting point for partitioning memory safely.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,5,'2025-06-23_11-34-03','We would need to subtract a fixed size (like 0x400) for each new stack from the initial MSP value.\nWe would start with the initial MSP value and create each new stack by moving down in memory by a fixed amount for each one. Since we know how many stacks we need, we can decide the start of each stack at compile-time by repeatedly subtracting the same amount of space. This way, each stack has its own space in memory.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',1,5,'2025-06-23_12-05-03','We would need to subtract a fixed size (like 0x400) for each new stack from the initial MSP value.\nWe would start with the initial MSP value and create each new stack by moving down in memory by a fixed amount for each one. Since we know how many stacks we need, we can decide the start of each stack at compile-time by repeatedly subtracting the same amount of space. This way, each stack has its own space in memory.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,5,'2025-06-23_12-18-43','We would need to subtract a fixed size (like 0x400) for each new stack from the initial MSP value.\nWe would start with the initial MSP value and create each new stack by moving down in memory by a fixed amount for each one. Since we know how many stacks we need, we can decide the start of each stack at compile-time by repeatedly subtracting the same amount of space. This way, each stack has its own space in memory.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,5,'2025-06-24_14-11-32','We’d subtract a fixed size like 0x400 from the initial MSP value for each thread and assign each new stack that way. Since we know the number of stacks at compile time, we can predefine the start of each one using fixed memory offsets to ensure they don’t overlap and follow alignment rules. To expand, we could define an array of stack pointers, where each entry is computed as MSP_INIT_VAL - (i+1)*0x400 for i from 0 to num_threads - 1. This deterministic allocation allows safe stack switching and can be verified at compile time for correct memory layout and alignment (typically 8-byte aligned for ARM Cortex-M).\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,5,'2025-06-24_17-15-35','We’d subtract a fixed size like 0x400 from the initial MSP value for each thread and assign each new stack that way. Since we know the number of stacks at compile time, we can predefine the start of each one using fixed memory offsets to ensure they don’t overlap and follow alignment rules. To expand, we could define an array of stack pointers, where each entry is computed as MSP_INIT_VAL - (i+1)*0x400 for i from 0 to num_threads - 1. This deterministic allocation allows safe stack switching and can be verified at compile time for correct memory layout and alignment (typically 8-byte aligned for ARM Cortex-M). By setting up this array and keeping each stack pointer offset predictable, it becomes easier to implement thread management and simulate context switches, even if we are not yet performing true multitasking.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',1,5,'2025-06-24_19-32-07','To create multiple valid thread stacks, we start from a known base address—typically the initial Main Stack Pointer (MSP)—and subtract a fixed offset for each thread. For instance, if we reserve 0x400 bytes per stack, the first thread would use MSP_INIT - 0x400, the second MSP_INIT - 0x800, and so on. This method ensures proper separation between stacks, prevents overlap, and maintains predictable memory layout. It’s important to ensure 8-byte alignment (as required by ARM Cortex-M) and to verify that all stack addresses fall within valid memory regions. By defining an array of stack pointers at compile time using these calculated offsets, we can safely manage multiple threads and enable basic context switching, even in systems without a full RTOS. This technique makes thread management simple, reliable, and memory-safe.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-23_12-00-24','By changing PC to the address of print_continuously(), the CPU started the function as if it were resuming from an interrupt, using a completely custom stack.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-23_12-23-48','By changing PC to the address of the print_continuously function, the CPU immediately jumped to and began executing that function. This works because setting PC directly causes the CPU to continue execution from the new address.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-23_12-49-34','By changing PC to the address of the print_continuously function, the CPU immediately jumped to and began executing that function. This works because setting PC directly causes the CPU to continue execution from the new address.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-24_14-08-15','By changing PC to the address of the print_continuously function, the CPU immediately jumped to and began executing that function. This works because setting PC directly causes the CPU to continue execution from the new address. Additionally, in normal program execution, the PC automatically increments to fetch the next instruction. However, manually assigning a value to the PC interrupts this normal control flow and causes a jump, bypassing typical function call behavior like pushing the return address to the stack. In more complex systems, this form of control transfer can skip stack frames or context-saving steps, which is useful in low-level thread launching but dangerous if misused.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-24_17-23-04','By changing PC to the address of the print_continuously function, the CPU immediately jumped to and began executing that function. This works because setting PC directly causes the CPU to continue execution from the new address. Additionally, in normal program execution, the PC automatically increments to fetch the next instruction. However, manually assigning a value to the PC interrupts this normal control flow and causes a jump, bypassing typical function call behavior like pushing the return address to the stack. In more complex systems, this form of control transfer can skip stack frames or context-saving steps, which is useful in low-level thread launching but dangerous if misused.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-24_17-43-23','By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). However, threads should run using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to ensure proper separation of kernel and user contexts. In this lab, we used __set_PSP(stackptr) to assign a valid memory region to the PSP before launching a thread. If we didn’t do this, the CPU would use the default PSP value (often 0 or uninitialized), and when it tries to pop registers during exception return, it would access invalid memory, leading to a hard fault. The PSP is critical for thread execution because it allows each thread to maintain its own private stack. If we used MSP for everything, thread execution could overwrite OS or interrupt stack data, resulting in corruption or crashes. In real-time operating systems (RTOS), stack separation is fundamental to safety and multitasking. Setting the PSP correctly ensures that context switching works safely and that the CPU has a known, valid location for all stack-based operations.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-24_17-49-09','By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). However, threads should run using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to ensure proper separation of kernel and user contexts. In this lab, we used __set_PSP(stackptr) to assign a valid memory region to the PSP before launching a thread. If we didn’t do this, the CPU would use the default PSP value (often 0 or uninitialized), and when it tries to pop registers during exception return, it would access invalid memory, leading to a hard fault. The PSP is critical for thread execution because it allows each thread to maintain its own private stack. If we used MSP for everything, thread execution could overwrite OS or interrupt stack data, resulting in corruption or crashes. In real-time operating systems (RTOS), stack separation is fundamental to safety and multitasking. Setting the PSP correctly ensures that context switching works safely and that the CPU has a known, valid location for all stack-based operations.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-24_18-03-49','By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). However, threads should run using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to ensure proper separation of kernel and user contexts. In this lab, we used __set_PSP(stackptr) to assign a valid memory region to the PSP before launching a thread. If we didn’t do this, the CPU would use the default PSP value (often 0 or uninitialized), and when it tries to pop registers during exception return, it would access invalid memory, leading to a hard fault. The PSP is critical for thread execution because it allows each thread to maintain its own private stack. If we used MSP for everything, thread execution could overwrite OS or interrupt stack data, resulting in corruption or crashes. In real-time operating systems (RTOS), stack separation is fundamental to safety and multitasking. Setting the PSP correctly ensures that context switching works safely and that the CPU has a known, valid location for all stack-based operations.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-24_18-13-08','The Program Counter (PC) in the Cortex-M4 is responsible for holding the address of the next instruction the CPU will execute. When we manually loaded the PC with the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to that address and began executing the instructions at that location. This is because the PC directly controls the flow of execution—loading it with any memory address causes the CPU to fetch and run instructions from there. Unlike a regular function call that automatically stores the return address in the Link Register (LR) and updates the stack for context switching, this manual override skips all of that. As a result, there’s no return path and no saved context, which makes the execution non-reversible unless we manually set things up. This technique is powerful for starting threads or jumping to exception handlers, but it must be used carefully, because improper use—like loading an invalid or misaligned address—can cause undefined behavior or system faults.The Program Counter (PC) in the Cortex-M4 is responsible for holding the address of the next instruction the CPU will execute. When we manually loaded the PC with the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to that address and began executing the instructions at that location. This is because the PC directly controls the flow of execution—loading it with any memory address causes the CPU to fetch and run instructions from there. Unlike a regular function call that automatically stores the return address in the Link Register (LR) and updates the stack for context switching, this manual override skips all of that. As a result, there’s no return path and no saved context, which makes the execution non-reversible unless we manually set things up. This technique is powerful for starting threads or jumping to exception handlers, but it must be used carefully, because improper use—like loading an invalid or misaligned address—can cause undefined behavior or system faults.',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-24_18-23-55','The Program Counter (PC) in the Cortex-M4 is responsible for holding the address of the next instruction the CPU will execute. When we manually loaded the PC with the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to that address and began executing the instructions at that location. This is because the PC directly controls the flow of execution—loading it with any memory address causes the CPU to fetch and run instructions from there. Unlike a regular function call that automatically stores the return address in the Link Register (LR) and updates the stack for context switching, this manual override skips all of that. As a result, there’s no return path and no saved context, which makes the execution non-reversible unless we manually set things up. This technique is powerful for starting threads or jumping to exception handlers, but it must be used carefully, because improper use—like loading an invalid or misaligned address—can cause undefined behavior or system faults.The Program Counter (PC) in the Cortex-M4 i