ause undefined behavior or system faults.The Program Counter (PC) in the Cortex-M4 is responsible for holding the address of the next instruction the CPU will execute. When we manually loaded the PC with the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to that address and began executing the instructions at that location. This is because the PC directly controls the flow of execution—loading it with any memory address causes the CPU to fetch and run instructions from there. Unlike a regular function call that automatically stores the return address in the Link Register (LR) and updates the stack for context switching, this manual override skips all of that. As a result, there’s no return path and no saved context, which makes the execution non-reversible unless we manually set things up. This technique is powerful for starting threads or jumping to exception handlers, but it must be used carefully, because improper use—like loading an invalid or misaligned address—can cause undefined behavior or system faults.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,1,'2025-06-24_18-43-08','The Program Counter (PC) in the Cortex-M4 is responsible for holding the address of the next instruction the CPU will execute. When we manually loaded the PC with the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to that address and began executing the instructions at that location. This is because the PC directly controls the flow of execution—loading it with any memory address causes the CPU to fetch and run instructions from there. Unlike a regular function call that automatically stores the return address in the Link Register (LR) and updates the stack for context switching, this manual override skips all of that. As a result, there’s no return path and no saved context, which makes the execution non-reversible unless we manually set things up. This technique is powerful for starting threads or jumping to exception handlers, but it must be used carefully, because improper use—like loading an invalid or misaligned address—can cause undefined behavior or system faults.The Program Counter (PC) in the Cortex-M4 is responsible for holding the address of the next instruction the CPU will execute. When we manually loaded the PC with the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to that address and began executing the instructions at that location. This is because the PC directly controls the flow of execution—loading it with any memory address causes the CPU to fetch and run instructions from there. Unlike a regular function call that automatically stores the return address in the Link Register (LR) and updates the stack for context switching, this manual override skips all of that. As a result, there’s no return path and no saved context, which makes the execution non-reversible unless we manually set things up. This technique is powerful for starting threads or jumping to exception handlers, but it must be used carefully, because improper use—like loading an invalid or misaligned address—can cause undefined behavior or system faults.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-23_12-00-24','System call basically requests a service from OS from user mode\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-23_12-23-48','A system call is a special instruction that lets user-mode code request services from the kernel. It switches the processor to privileged mode so it can safely access hardware or kernel-only resources.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-23_12-49-34','A system call is a special instruction that lets user-mode code request services from the kernel. It switches the processor to privileged mode so it can safely access hardware or kernel-only resources. Additionally, system calls act like function calls that pass control from untrusted user programs to trusted OS code. This separation protects the system by preventing user code from directly accessing sensitive hardware or memory, ensuring stability and security.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-24_14-08-15','A system call is a special instruction that lets user-mode code request services from the kernel. It switches the processor to privileged mode so it can safely access hardware or kernel-only resources. Additionally, system calls act like function calls that pass control from untrusted user programs to trusted OS code. This separation protects the system by preventing user code from directly accessing sensitive hardware or memory, ensuring stability and security. They also enforce privilege boundaries by letting the kernel validate inputs, which makes them critical for system security. Without system calls, user programs could interfere with critical operations or corrupt hardware. To be more precise, a system call is a controlled software interrupt (often using the SVC instruction) that causes the CPU to enter handler mode and begin execution at a privileged exception vector. This acts like a function call to a trusted interface, and the CPU manages the privilege transition and stack frame handling automatically.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-24_17-23-04','A system call is a special instruction that lets user-mode code request services from the kernel. It switches the processor to privileged mode so it can safely access hardware or kernel-only resources. Additionally, system calls act like function calls that pass control from untrusted user programs to trusted OS code. This separation protects the system by preventing user code from directly accessing sensitive hardware or memory, ensuring stability and security. They also enforce privilege boundaries by letting the kernel validate inputs, which makes them critical for system security. Without system calls, user programs could interfere with critical operations or corrupt hardware. To be more precise, a system call is a controlled software interrupt (often using the SVC instruction) that causes the CPU to enter handler mode and begin execution at a privileged exception vector. This acts like a function call to a trusted interface, and the CPU manages the privilege transition and stack frame handling automatically.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-24_17-49-09','If the thread function simply printed something once and then returned, the CPU would attempt to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. However, because we manually set up the thread’s stack in this lab, we didn’t include a valid return address in the correct location. There was no actual caller that pushed a proper Link Register (LR) value, so when the function returns, the CPU jumps to an unpredictable memory location, often resulting in a hard fault. This is especially dangerous in embedded systems like ARM Cortex-M, where stack format and register values must follow strict conventions. Without a valid return path, the processor fetches invalid instructions and crashes. To prevent this, thread functions should either loop indefinitely or terminate through a system-defined thread exit mechanism, like those found in RTOS environments. This ensures that control never returns to an invalid address. Diagnosing such issues typically involves inspecting the stack and LR contents at runtime using a debugger. It’s also important to ensure that when manually crafting a thread\'s stack, you either supply a valid return address (if supported) or design the thread to never return. Understanding and managing this behavior is critical for writing safe and reliable embedded software.',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-24_18-03-49','If the thread function simply printed something once and then returned, the CPU would attempt to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. However, because we manually set up the thread’s stack in this lab, we didn’t include a valid return address in the correct location. There was no actual caller that pushed a proper Link Register (LR) value, so when the function returns, the CPU jumps to an unpredictable memory location, often resulting in a hard fault. This is especially dangerous in embedded systems like ARM Cortex-M, where stack format and register values must follow strict conventions. Without a valid return path, the processor fetches invalid instructions and crashes. To prevent this, thread functions should either loop indefinitely or terminate through a system-defined thread exit mechanism, like those found in RTOS environments. This ensures that control never returns to an invalid address. Diagnosing such issues typically involves inspecting the stack and LR contents at runtime using a debugger. It’s also important to ensure that when manually crafting a thread\'s stack, you either supply a valid return address (if supported) or design the thread to never return. Understanding and managing this behavior is critical for writing safe and reliable embedded software.',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-24_18-13-08','By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). However, threads should run using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to ensure proper separation of kernel and user contexts. In this lab, we used __set_PSP(stackptr) to assign a valid memory region to the PSP before launching a thread. If we didn’t do this, the CPU would use the default PSP value (often 0 or uninitialized), and when it tries to pop registers during exception return, it would access invalid memory, leading to a hard fault. The PSP is critical for thread execution because it allows each thread to maintain its own private stack. If we used MSP for everything, thread execution could overwrite OS or interrupt stack data, resulting in corruption or crashes. In real-time operating systems (RTOS), stack separation is fundamental to safety and multitasking. Setting the PSP correctly ensures that context switching works safely and that the CPU has a known, valid location for all stack-based operations.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-24_18-23-55','By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). However, threads should run using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to ensure proper separation of kernel and user contexts. In this lab, we used __set_PSP(stackptr) to assign a valid memory region to the PSP before launching a thread. If we didn’t do this, the CPU would use the default PSP value (often 0 or uninitialized), and when it tries to pop registers during exception return, it would access invalid memory, leading to a hard fault. The PSP is critical for thread execution because it allows each thread to maintain its own private stack. If we used MSP for everything, thread execution could overwrite OS or interrupt stack data, resulting in corruption or crashes. In real-time operating systems (RTOS), stack separation is fundamental to safety and multitasking. Setting the PSP correctly ensures that context switching works safely and that the CPU has a known, valid location for all stack-based operations.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-24_18-29-13','By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). However, threads should run using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to ensure proper separation of kernel and user contexts. In this lab, we used __set_PSP(stackptr) to assign a valid memory region to the PSP before launching a thread. If we didn’t do this, the CPU would use the default PSP value (often 0 or uninitialized), and when it tries to pop registers during exception return, it would access invalid memory, leading to a hard fault. The PSP is critical for thread execution because it allows each thread to maintain its own private stack. If we used MSP for everything, thread execution could overwrite OS or interrupt stack data, resulting in corruption or crashes. In real-time operating systems (RTOS), stack separation is fundamental to safety and multitasking. Setting the PSP correctly ensures that context switching works safely and that the CPU has a known, valid location for all stack-based operations.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,2,'2025-06-24_18-43-08','A system call is a controlled way for user-level programs to request services from the operating system kernel, functioning similarly to a regular function call but with a crucial difference—it involves a privilege level change. While a regular function call jumps to another section of user code, a system call transfers execution from unprivileged user mode to privileged kernel mode using a software interrupt, often through the SVC instruction in ARM Cortex-M processors. This transition allows the kernel to safely execute sensitive operations like accessing hardware, managing memory, or performing I/O, which user code cannot do directly. System calls serve as a secure gateway between user applications and the system’s core functions, enabling communication while enforcing strict access control. By acting as intermediaries, system calls validate inputs and isolate user code from direct hardware interaction, helping to prevent bugs or malicious actions from compromising system stability or security. This protective mechanism is essential in embedded systems where reliability and safety are critical.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,3,'2025-06-23_12-00-24','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing memory so the CPU knows where to start executing the thread. We simulate a saved context by pushing fake register values (like xPSR, PC, and others) onto the stack. This tricks the CPU into thinking the thread was already running, so it can “resume” it properly. Storing registers on the stack is essential to restore execution state during context switches.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,3,'2025-06-23_12-23-48','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing memory so the CPU knows where to start executing the thread. We simulate a saved context by pushing fake register values (like xPSR, PC, and others) onto the stack. This tricks the CPU into thinking the thread was already running, so it can “resume” it properly. Storing registers on the stack is essential to restore execution state during context switches.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,3,'2025-06-23_12-49-34','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing memory so the CPU knows where to start executing the thread. We simulate a saved context by pushing fake register values (like xPSR, PC, and others) onto the stack. This tricks the CPU into thinking the thread was already running, so it can “resume” it properly. Storing registers on the stack is essential to restore execution state during context switches. It’s important to choose a valid memory location for the thread’s stack to avoid crashes or memory corruption. Also, the register values must be pushed in the correct order, matching the order the CPU expects during exception return so that it can restore execution correctly. “Fake” register values just means we pre-fill the stack with realistic defaults like a valid PC and xPSR, even though the thread hasn’t run yet.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,3,'2025-06-24_14-08-15','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing memory so the CPU knows where to start executing the thread. We simulate a saved context by pushing fake register values (like xPSR, PC, and others) onto the stack. This tricks the CPU into thinking the thread was already running, so it can “resume” it properly. Storing registers on the stack is essential to restore execution state during context switches. It’s important to choose a valid memory location for the thread’s stack to avoid crashes or memory corruption. Also, the register values must be pushed in the correct order, matching the order the CPU expects during exception return so that it can restore execution correctly. “Fake” register values just means we pre-fill the stack with realistic defaults like a valid PC and xPSR, even though the thread hasn’t run yet. Choosing the correct order and alignment is important because the CPU expects a specific format when returning from an interrupt. If the memory location or register layout is incorrect, the CPU might crash or misinterpret values. Valid memory ensures that all stack accesses during execution are safe. Furthermore, registers like R0–R3, R12, LR (R14), PC (R15), and xPSR are expected in a specific order by the ARM exception return mechanism. Failing to match this order would prevent proper restoration and lead to unpredictable behavior. Setting up the stack this way allows us to mimic the effect of an interrupted thread and resume it, even though it hasn’t run before.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,3,'2025-06-24_17-23-04','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing memory so the CPU knows where to start executing the thread. We simulate a saved context by pushing fake register values (like xPSR, PC, and others) onto the stack. This tricks the CPU into thinking the thread was already running, so it can “resume” it properly. Storing registers on the stack is essential to restore execution state during context switches. It’s important to choose a valid memory location for the thread’s stack to avoid crashes or memory corruption. Also, the register values must be pushed in the correct order, matching the order the CPU expects during exception return so that it can restore execution correctly. “Fake” register values just means we pre-fill the stack with realistic defaults like a valid PC and xPSR, even though the thread hasn’t run yet. Choosing the correct order and alignment is important because the CPU expects a specific format when returning from an interrupt. If the memory location or register layout is incorrect, the CPU might crash or misinterpret values. Valid memory ensures that all stack accesses during execution are safe. Furthermore, registers like R0–R3, R12, LR (R14), PC (R15), and xPSR are expected in a specific order by the ARM exception return mechanism. Failing to match this order would prevent proper restoration and lead to unpredictable behavior. Setting up the stack this way allows us to mimic the effect of an interrupted thread and resume it, even though it hasn’t run before.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,3,'2025-06-24_18-13-08','If the thread function simply printed something once and then returned, the CPU would attempt to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. However, because we manually set up the thread’s stack in this lab, we didn’t include a valid return address in the correct location. There was no actual caller that pushed a proper Link Register (LR) value, so when the function returns, the CPU jumps to an unpredictable memory location, often resulting in a hard fault. This is especially dangerous in embedded systems like ARM Cortex-M, where stack format and register values must follow strict conventions. Without a valid return path, the processor fetches invalid instructions and crashes. To prevent this, thread functions should either loop indefinitely or terminate through a system-defined thread exit mechanism, like those found in RTOS environments. This ensures that control never returns to an invalid address. Diagnosing such issues typically involves inspecting the stack and LR contents at runtime using a debugger. It’s also important to ensure that when manually crafting a thread\'s stack, you either supply a valid return address (if supported) or design the thread to never return. Understanding and managing this behavior is critical for writing safe and reliable embedded software.',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,3,'2025-06-24_18-23-55','If the thread function simply printed something once and then returned, the CPU would attempt to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. However, because we manually set up the thread’s stack in this lab, we didn’t include a valid return address in the correct location. There was no actual caller that pushed a proper Link Register (LR) value, so when the function returns, the CPU jumps to an unpredictable memory location, often resulting in a hard fault. This is especially dangerous in embedded systems like ARM Cortex-M, where stack format and register values must follow strict conventions. Without a valid return path, the processor fetches invalid instructions and crashes. To prevent this, thread functions should either loop indefinitely or terminate through a system-defined thread exit mechanism, like those found in RTOS environments. This ensures that control never returns to an invalid address. Diagnosing such issues typically involves inspecting the stack and LR contents at runtime using a debugger. It’s also important to ensure that when manually crafting a thread\'s stack, you either supply a valid return address (if supported) or design the thread to never return. Understanding and managing this behavior is critical for writing safe and reliable embedded software.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,3,'2025-06-24_18-29-13','If the thread function simply printed something once and then returned, the CPU would attempt to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. However, because we manually set up the thread’s stack in this lab, we didn’t include a valid return address in the correct location. There was no actual caller that pushed a proper Link Register (LR) value, so when the function returns, the CPU jumps to an unpredictable memory location, often resulting in a hard fault. This is especially dangerous in embedded systems like ARM Cortex-M, where stack format and register values must follow strict conventions. Without a valid return path, the processor fetches invalid instructions and crashes. To prevent this, thread functions should either loop indefinitely or terminate through a system-defined thread exit mechanism, like those found in RTOS environments. This ensures that control never returns to an invalid address. Diagnosing such issues typically involves inspecting the stack and LR contents at runtime using a debugger. It’s also important to ensure that when manually crafting a thread\'s stack, you either supply a valid return address (if supported) or design the thread to never return. Understanding and managing this behavior is critical for writing safe and reliable embedded software.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,3,'2025-06-24_18-43-08','Setting up a thread’s stack involves preparing a block of memory with a fake context frame so that when the CPU begins execution, it can \"resume\" the thread as if it had been preempted. The key components include choosing a valid memory region for the stack, aligning it properly (typically 8-byte aligned for Cortex-M), and preloading it with default values for registers such as R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR. The order of these registers is critical because the ARM Cortex-M exception return mechanism expects them in a specific sequence; any deviation can cause unpredictable behavior or faults when the CPU tries to restore context. The purpose of storing these register values is to give the CPU all the necessary state information to begin execution from the new thread’s entry point, with valid initial values for the program counter and processor status. This approach allows the thread to start executing seamlessly as if it had been previously interrupted, enabling cooperative multitasking or context switching without traditional function calls.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,4,'2025-06-23_12-00-24','If we didn’t call __set_PSP(stackptr), the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread\'s stack is. When the thread starts, it would try to pop register values from an invalid or zero PSP, leading to a hard fault because it tries to access memory it shouldn\'t. Setting PSP ensures the thread has a valid stack to start from.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,4,'2025-06-23_12-23-48','If we didn’t call __set_PSP(stackptr), the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread\'s stack is. When the thread starts, it would try to pop register values from an invalid or zero PSP, leading to a hard fault because it tries to access memory it shouldn\'t. Setting PSP ensures the thread has a valid stack to start from.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,4,'2025-06-23_12-49-34','If we didn’t call __set_PSP(stackptr), the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread\'s stack is. When the thread starts, it would try to pop register values from an invalid or zero PSP, leading to a hard fault because it tries to access memory it shouldn\'t. Setting PSP ensures the thread has a valid stack to start from.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,4,'2025-06-24_14-08-15','If we didn’t call __set_PSP(stackptr), the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread\'s stack is. When the thread starts, it would try to pop register values from an invalid or zero PSP, leading to a hard fault because it tries to access memory it shouldn\'t. Setting PSP ensures the thread has a valid stack to start from. Also, since the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) by default, explicitly switching to the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is essential when running threads. Without this, the CPU would be using the wrong stack space for thread execution, which could overwrite other important memory. In addition, MSP is usually reserved for OS-level or interrupt-level contexts. Threads running in unprivileged mode must use the PSP to keep stack separation and prevent unintended overwrites. Calling __set_PSP(stackptr) and then triggering a context switch causes the CPU to start using PSP, making the transition safe and compliant with the thread model.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,4,'2025-06-24_17-23-04','If we didn’t call __set_PSP(stackptr), the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread\'s stack is. When the thread starts, it would try to pop register values from an invalid or zero PSP, leading to a hard fault because it tries to access memory it shouldn\'t. Setting PSP ensures the thread has a valid stack to start from. Also, since the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) by default, explicitly switching to the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is essential when running threads. Without this, the CPU would be using the wrong stack space for thread execution, which could overwrite other important memory. In addition, MSP is usually reserved for OS-level or interrupt-level contexts. Threads running in unprivileged mode must use the PSP to keep stack separation and prevent unintended overwrites. Calling __set_PSP(stackptr) and then triggering a context switch causes the CPU to start using PSP, making the transition safe and compliant with the thread model.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,4,'2025-06-24_18-43-08','In the switch/case of SVC_Handler_Main, we needed to call __set_PSP(stackptr) to assign the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) before starting the thread. Without this, the CPU would default to using an uninitialized PSP, which typically holds a zero value. When the thread attempts to pop registers from this invalid location, it causes a hard fault due to illegal memory access. The PSP is essential because, in a multi-threaded system, each thread needs its own stack space, and the PSP manages this for thread-level execution. If we don’t explicitly set it, the CPU may use the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is reserved for interrupt or OS-level code, risking memory corruption. Properly setting the PSP ensures the thread starts with a valid stack that was pre-filled with a fake context (registers like PC, xPSR, etc.). This stack separation is critical in real-time systems to maintain safety and prevent interference between user-level and system-level execution contexts.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,5,'2025-06-23_12-00-24','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. But since we manually set up the thread\'s stack with dummy values (like 0xA), the return address would be invalid. This would likely cause the program to jump to an undefined location and crash with a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',2,5,'2025-06-23_12-23-48','If the thread function returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. But we didn’t store a valid return address in the thread’s stack. Since it’s missing entirely, the CPU would jump to an unpredictable location, likely crashing with a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,5,'2025-06-23_12-49-34','If the thread function returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. But we didn’t store a valid return address in the thread’s stack. Since it’s missing entirely, the CPU would jump to an unpredictable location, likely crashing with a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,5,'2025-06-24_14-08-15','If the thread function returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. But we didn’t store a valid return address in the thread’s stack. Since it’s missing entirely, the CPU would jump to an unpredictable location, likely crashing with a hard fault. Additionally, the return path assumes that a calling function pushed a valid LR (Link Register) value, but in our case, the thread was launched manually by restoring context from a synthetic stack frame. Since no caller exists, returning from the thread is undefined behavior. To avoid this, thread functions should either loop forever or call a proper thread exit routine provided by an RTOS or scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,5,'2025-06-24_17-23-04','If the thread function returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. But we didn’t store a valid return address in the thread’s stack. Since it’s missing entirely, the CPU would jump to an unpredictable location, likely crashing with a hard fault. Additionally, the return path assumes that a calling function pushed a valid LR (Link Register) value, but in our case, the thread was launched manually by restoring context from a synthetic stack frame. Since no caller exists, returning from the thread is undefined behavior. To avoid this, thread functions should either loop forever or call a proper thread exit routine provided by an RTOS or scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',2,5,'2025-06-24_18-43-08','If the thread function did not loop and instead returned, the CPU would attempt to pop a return address from the stack to determine where to continue execution. However, in our setup, we never placed a valid return address in the thread’s stack frame because the thread was launched manually using a crafted context. This would result in the CPU jumping to an undefined or garbage address, almost certainly triggering a hard fault. Normally, during a regular function call, the Link Register (LR) is used to store the return address, but our thread didn’t have a caller, so no LR value was pushed. As a result, returning from the thread is undefined behavior. To prevent this issue, thread functions in bare-metal or RTOS systems are typically written to either loop infinitely or explicitly call a thread exit handler provided by the scheduler. Without this, the CPU’s attempt to return leads to a crash, highlighting the importance of managing control flow explicitly when dealing with manually constructed threads.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,1,'2025-06-27_02-40-50','When working with multiple .c files in a C project, each source file is first compiled individually into an object file (.o) using a compiler. This step translates the human-readable C code into machine code while keeping references to external functions or variables unresolved. After all .c files are compiled, the linker takes over and combines these object files into a single executable file. The linker’s job is to resolve all references between files—ensuring that functions or variables used in one file are correctly matched with their definitions in others. Once everything is linked successfully, the final result is an executable program that can be run by the system. This process allows code to be modular and organized across multiple files, improving maintainability and reusability.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',3,1,'2025-06-27_03-24-22','When working with multiple .c files in a C project, the build process involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. During preprocessing, directives like #include and #define are handled—header files are inserted, and macros are expanded. Next, in the compilation step, each .c file is translated into a machine-readable object file (.o) with unresolved references. Finally, the linker combines all object files into a single executable, resolving cross-file references such as function and variable definitions. This process enables modular, maintainable code across multiple files.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,1,'2025-06-27_03-56-54','When working with multiple .c files in a C project, the build process involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. During preprocessing, directives like #include and #define are handled—header files are inserted, and macros are expanded. Next, in the compilation step, each .c file is translated into a machine-readable object file (.o) with unresolved references. Finally, the linker combines all object files into a single executable, resolving cross-file references such as function and variable definitions. This process enables modular, maintainable code across multiple files.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,1,'2025-06-27_18-33-48','When working with multiple .c files in a C project, the build process involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. During preprocessing, directives like #include and #define are handled—header files are inserted, and macros are expanded. Next, in the compilation step, each .c file is translated into a machine-readable object file (.o) with unresolved references. Finally, the linker combines all object files into a single executable, resolving cross-file references such as function and variable definitions. This process enables modular, maintainable code across multiple files.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,2,'2025-06-27_02-40-50','Using #include <kernel.c> or including any .c file directly with #include is a bad idea because it bypasses the proper compilation and linking process. When you include a .c file, you\'re essentially copying all its contents into the current file before compilation. This can lead to multiple definition errors, especially for functions or global variables, since the compiler might see the same code more than once. It also makes your code harder to manage and debug, breaking modularity. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately, and their function declarations should be shared through header files (.h). This keeps code clean, modular, and compiler-friendly.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,2,'2025-06-27_03-24-22','Using #include <kernel.c> or including any .c file directly with #include is a bad idea because it bypasses the proper compilation and linking process. When you include a .c file, you\'re essentially copying all its contents into the current file before compilation. This can lead to multiple definition errors, especially for functions or global variables, since the compiler might see the same code more than once. It also makes your code harder to manage and debug, breaking modularity. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately, and their function declarations should be shared through header files (.h). This keeps code clean, modular, and compiler-friendly.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,2,'2025-06-27_03-56-54','Using #include <kernel.c> or including any .c file directly with #include is a bad idea because it bypasses the proper compilation and linking process. When you include a .c file, you\'re essentially copying all its contents into the current file before compilation. This can lead to multiple definition errors, especially for functions or global variables, since the compiler might see the same code more than once. It also makes your code harder to manage and debug, breaking modularity. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately, and their function declarations should be shared through header files (.h). This keeps code clean, modular, and compiler-friendly.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,2,'2025-06-27_18-33-48','Using #include <kernel.c> or including any .c file directly with #include is a bad idea because it bypasses the proper compilation and linking process. When you include a .c file, you\'re essentially copying all its contents into the current file before compilation. This can lead to multiple definition errors, especially for functions or global variables, since the compiler might see the same code more than once. It also makes your code harder to manage and debug, breaking modularity. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately, and their function declarations should be shared through header files (.h). This keeps code clean, modular, and compiler-friendly.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,3,'2025-06-27_02-40-50','To set up and start a thread from the user\'s perspective, the process begins by initializing the kernel. This step prepares the system by setting up necessary internal structures such as the initial stack pointer and any global variables required for thread management. Next, the user defines a thread function, which contains the code they want the thread to execute. After writing the function, the user creates the thread by calling a kernel function designed for thread creation. This function allocates a new stack for the thread and sets up the processor context so the thread can run properly. Finally, once the thread has been created, the user starts the kernel by calling the appropriate function, which triggers a system call to begin execution of the first thread. This marks the transition from the main application into a multi-threaded execution environment managed by the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',3,3,'2025-06-27_03-24-22','The stack pool is a dedicated memory region reserved for storing stacks used by both user threads and the operating system itself. Each thread requires its own stack to manage function calls, local variables, and saved CPU state during execution. By preallocating this pool, the OS can dynamically assign isolated stack space to each thread, preventing memory overlap and ensuring reliable execution. Importantly, part of the stack pool is also used by the kernel to maintain its own stack, which is essential for handling system calls, interrupts, and performing context switches. This dual-purpose design supports safe multitasking and consistent system behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',3,3,'2025-06-27_03-56-54','The stack pool is a dedicated memory region reserved for storing stacks used by both user threads and the operating system itself. Each thread requires its own stack to manage function calls, local variables, and saved CPU state during execution. By preallocating this pool, the OS can dynamically assign isolated stack space to each thread, preventing memory overlap and ensuring reliable execution. Importantly, part of the stack pool is also used by the kernel to maintain its own stack, which is essential for handling system calls, interrupts, and performing context switches. This dual-purpose design supports safe multitasking and consistent system behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',3,3,'2025-06-27_18-33-48','To start a thread, the user first initializes the kernel by calling a function that sets up the necessary memory and data structures for managing threads. Next, they define the thread function—a function with no parameters and no return value that contains the code the thread will execute. Then, they create the thread by calling a kernel-provided function and passing it the address of the thread function. This step allocates a stack for the thread and prepares its execution context. After that, the user starts the kernel, which issues a system call to load the thread’s stack and begin execution. Once this is complete, the thread runs independently as part of the system’s execution flow.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,4,'2025-06-27_02-40-50','The stack pool is a dedicated region of memory reserved specifically for storing the stacks of multiple threads. Each thread in an operating system needs its own stack to keep track of function calls, local variables, and processor state during execution. By preallocating a pool of memory (the stack pool), we can ensure there is a reliable and separate memory space for each thread’s stack. In future labs, this pool allows the OS to dynamically allocate a portion of memory from the pool to each new thread during creation. This enables multiple threads to coexist and run independently, while preventing them from interfering with each other\'s memory space, which is critical for system stability and predictable behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',3,4,'2025-06-27_03-24-22','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” is crucial for organizing an operating system. From a user’s perspective, the user code (typically in main.c) contains application-specific logic like creating threads, defining what those threads do, and starting the OS. It’s where the developer writes what they want the system to accomplish. On the other hand, the kernel code (in kernel.c and kernel.h) handles low-level operations like setting up stacks, managing thread switching, and responding to SVC calls. This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on high-level behavior without worrying about how threads are managed underneath. It also keeps the kernel modular and reusable, enabling multiple applications to rely on the same stable system core.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,4,'2025-06-27_03-56-54','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” is crucial for organizing an operating system. From a user’s perspective, the user code (typically in main.c) contains application-specific logic like creating threads, defining what those threads do, and starting the OS. It’s where the developer writes what they want the system to accomplish. On the other hand, the kernel code (in kernel.c and kernel.h) handles low-level operations like setting up stacks, managing thread switching, and responding to SVC calls. This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on high-level behavior without worrying about how threads are managed underneath. It also keeps the kernel modular and reusable, enabling multiple applications to rely on the same stable system core.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,4,'2025-06-27_18-33-48','The stack pool is a dedicated memory region reserved for storing stacks used by both user threads and the operating system itself. Each thread requires its own stack to manage function calls, local variables, and saved CPU state during execution. By preallocating this pool, the OS can dynamically assign isolated stack space to each thread, preventing memory overlap and ensuring reliable execution. Importantly, part of the stack pool is also used by the kernel to maintain its own stack, which is essential for handling system calls, interrupts, and performing context switches. This dual-purpose design supports safe multitasking and consistent system behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,5,'2025-06-27_02-40-50','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” is crucial for organizing an operating system. From a user’s perspective, the user code (typically in main.c) contains application-specific logic like creating threads, defining what those threads do, and starting the OS. It’s where the developer writes what they want the system to accomplish. On the other hand, the kernel code (in kernel.c and kernel.h) handles low-level operations like setting up stacks, managing thread switching, and responding to SVC calls. This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on high-level behavior without worrying about how threads are managed underneath. It also keeps the kernel modular and reusable, enabling multiple applications to rely on the same stable system core.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',3,5,'2025-06-27_18-33-48','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” is crucial for organizing an operating system. From a user’s perspective, the user code (typically in main.c) contains application-specific logic like creating threads, defining what those threads do, and starting the OS. It’s where the developer writes what they want the system to accomplish. On the other hand, the kernel code (in kernel.c and kernel.h) handles low-level operations like setting up stacks, managing thread switching, and responding to SVC calls. This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on high-level behavior without worrying about how threads are managed underneath. It also keeps the kernel modular and reusable, enabling multiple applications to rely on the same stable system core.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',4,1,'2025-07-18_13-06-25','A queue-based round-robin scheduler works by placing each ready thread at the back of a queue and always running the thread at the front. When a thread yields or is paused, it moves to the end of the queue, ensuring every thread gets a turn without complex arithmetic or scanning through an array. Threads that block or finish simply stay out of the queue, so the scheduler never wastes time on inactive entries. Queue operations are fast and uniform, and the same structure can easily support extensions like different priority levels or separate queues for I/O without rewriting the core scheduler logic.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',4,1,'2025-07-19_11-35-22','A queue-based round-robin scheduler still delivers exactly the same FIFO fairness as our simple array-plus-index approach, but trades a tiny bit of per-switch overhead for greater adaptability. With an array scheduler you merely bump an integer and wrap it around—almost zero work per context switch—but you must still check or skip reserved slots for threads that have blocked or exited. By contrast, a queue only ever holds live, ready threads, so you never waste cycles scanning inactive entries; enqueue and dequeue each incur a small cost in pointer updates, but the structure grows or shrinks automatically as threads block, terminate, or are created, and it can even be extended to multiple priority or I/O-wait queues without touching the core scheduler logic.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',4,2,'2025-07-18_13-06-25','When the PendSV interrupt fires, the handler first uses the process stack pointer (PSP) to find the current thread’s stack and pushes its callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto that stack. It then calls the osSched() function, which records the old thread’s stack pointer in its thread control block, picks the next thread to run, and sets PSP to the new thread’s saved stack pointer. Returning from osSched(), PendSV pops R4–R11 off the new thread’s stack and updates PSP again. Finally, by branching to the return address in LR, the processor performs an exception return that restores the remaining registers (including PC and xPSR) from the new thread’s stack frame, so execution resumes inside that thread’s code.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',4,2,'2025-07-19_11-35-22','When the PendSV interrupt fires, the handler first uses the process stack pointer (PSP) to find the current thread’s stack and pushes its callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto that stack. It then calls the osSched() function, which records the old thread’s stack pointer in its thread control block, picks the next thread to run, and sets PSP to the new thread’s saved stack pointer. Returning from osSched(), PendSV pops R4–R11 off the new thread’s stack and updates PSP again. Finally, by branching to the return address in LR, the processor performs an exception return that restores the remaining registers (including PC and xPSR) from the new thread’s stack frame, so execution resumes inside that thread’s code.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',4,3,'2025-07-18_13-06-25','Always running the single highest-priority task means any lower-priority work could never get the CPU—if a high-priority thread never yields or keeps re-raising its priority, all others starve. You also introduce classic priority inversion: a medium-priority task could preempt a low-priority thread that holds a lock needed by the high-priority thread, effectively blocking the highest priority one. Finally, constantly scanning every TCB to find the maximum adds scheduling overhead. In practice you need time-slicing or priority-aging so that every thread eventually makes progress and resources don’t get permanently locked out.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',4,3,'2025-07-19_11-35-22','Always running the single highest-priority task means any lower-priority work could never get the CPU—if a high-priority thread never yields or keeps re-raising its priority, all others starve. You also introduce classic priority inversion: a medium-priority task could preempt a low-priority thread that holds a lock needed by the high-priority thread, effectively blocking the highest priority one. Finally, constantly scanning every TCB to find the maximum adds scheduling overhead. In practice you need time-slicing or priority-aging so that every thread eventually makes progress and resources don’t get permanently locked out.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',4,4,'2025-07-18_13-06-25','Always running the single highest-priority task means any lower-priority work could never get the CPU—if a high-priority thread never yields or keeps re-raising its priority, all others starve. You also introduce classic priority inversion: a medium-priority task could preempt a low-priority thread that holds a lock needed by the high-priority thread, effectively blocking the highest priority one. Finally, constantly scanning every TCB to find the maximum adds scheduling overhead. In practice you need time-slicing or priority-aging so that every thread eventually makes progress and resources don’t get permanently locked out.\n',0,'s25'),('r242huan',4,4,'2025-07-19_11-35-22','Always running the highest-priority thread starves lower-priority ones, risks priority inversion, and wastes time scanning every TCB—so real systems use time-slicing or priority aging to ensure fairness. Similarly, writing SVC/PendSV handlers entirely in C makes them easier to read and maintain but incurs function-call overhead and obscures exact register handling, whereas pure assembly offers cycle-perfect control at the cost of complexity. In practice, RTOS kernels use a tiny “naked” assembly stub to set up the stack and then branch into a compact C routine, balancing precision with clarity.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',4,5,'2025-07-18_13-06-25','If you create only one thread the scheduler still runs but always picks that same thread again. At startup the OS will load your single print loop and begin executing it. Each time the thread calls yield the PendSV handler saves its stack pointer then invokes the scheduler which selects the next ready thread. Because there is only one ready thread the scheduler restores the same stack pointer and returns into the print loop. The result is that your thread continues printing and yielding in a never ending cycle. The kernel remains alive and never stalls but you do not gain any real multitasking since only one task ever runs.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',4,5,'2025-07-19_11-35-22','If you create only one thread the scheduler still runs but always picks that same thread again. At startup the OS will load your single print loop and begin executing it. Each time the thread calls yield the PendSV handler saves its stack pointer then invokes the scheduler which selects the next ready thread. Because there is only one ready thread the scheduler restores the same stack pointer and returns into the print loop. The result is that your thread continues printing and yielding in a never ending cycle. The kernel remains alive and never stalls but you do not gain any real multitasking since only one task ever runs.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,1,'2025-07-27_13-12-49','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must define their thread function to accept a single void* parameter and then call osCreateThread, passing both the function and a pointer to the desired argument (such as a struct or integer). The OS is responsible for preparing the thread’s stack such that the pointer is loaded into register R0, which is the ARM calling convention\'s register for the first argument. Specifically, during thread initialization, the OS builds the thread’s stack frame with all necessary registers, placing the argument into the correct position for R0 before the thread starts executing. This works because when the OS switches context to this new thread using PSP (Process Stack Pointer), the processor pops the prepared registers off the stack, automatically loading R0 with the user’s argument before jumping to the thread’s function. As a result, when the thread begins running, it receives the user’s input just like a normal function call.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,1,'2025-07-28_12-20-17','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must define their thread function to accept a single void* parameter and then call osCreateThread, passing both the function and a pointer to the desired argument (such as a struct or integer). The OS is responsible for preparing the thread’s stack such that the pointer is loaded into register R0, which is the ARM calling convention\'s register for the first argument. Specifically, during thread initialization, the OS builds the thread’s stack frame with all necessary registers, placing the argument into the correct position for R0 before the thread starts executing. This works because when the OS switches context to this new thread using PSP (Process Stack Pointer), the processor pops the prepared registers off the stack, automatically loading R0 with the user’s argument before jumping to the thread’s function. As a result, when the thread begins running, it receives the user’s input just like a normal function call.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,1,'2025-07-28_12-50-48','To pass an argument into a thread, the user must define their thread function to accept a single void* parameter and then call osCreateThread, passing both the function and a pointer to the desired argument (such as a struct or integer). The OS is responsible for preparing the thread’s stack such that the pointer is loaded into register R0, which is the ARM calling convention\'s register for the first argument. Specifically, during thread initialization, the OS builds the thread’s stack frame with all necessary registers, placing the argument into the correct position for R0 before the thread starts executing. This works because when the OS switches context to this new thread using PSP (Process Stack Pointer), the processor pops the prepared registers off the stack, automatically loading R0 with the user’s argument before jumping to the thread’s function. As a result, when the thread begins running, it receives the user’s input just like a normal function call.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,2,'2025-07-27_13-12-49','You can’t call osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because osYield itself triggers a software interrupt (SVC) to initiate a context switch. Calling it inside those handlers would cause nested interrupts or reentrant behavior, leading to stack corruption, undefined execution, or even a system crash. These handlers already handle context switching directly by saving and restoring thread state, so re-entering the switching logic mid-handler breaks the execution flow and violates the expected call-return structure. Essentially, you’d be asking the processor to interrupt itself while it’s already in a critical section of context management, which is unsafe and unpredictable.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,2,'2025-07-28_12-20-17','You can’t call osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because osYield itself triggers a software interrupt (SVC) to initiate a context switch. Calling it inside those handlers would cause nested interrupts or reentrant behavior, leading to stack corruption, undefined execution, or even a system crash. These handlers already handle context switching directly by saving and restoring thread state, so re-entering the switching logic mid-handler breaks the execution flow and violates the expected call-return structure. Essentially, you’d be asking the processor to interrupt itself while it’s already in a critical section of context management, which is unsafe and unpredictable.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,2,'2025-07-28_12-50-48','You can’t call osYield inside context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because osYield itself triggers a software interrupt (SVC) to initiate a context switch. Calling it inside those handlers would cause nested interrupts or reentrant behavior, leading to stack corruption, undefined execution, or even a system crash. These handlers already handle context switching directly by saving and restoring thread state, so re-entering the switching logic mid-handler breaks the execution flow and violates the expected call-return structure. Essentially, you’d be asking the processor to interrupt itself while it’s already in a critical section of context management, which is unsafe and unpredictable.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,3,'2025-07-27_13-12-49','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms would increase the system\'s timing resolution, allowing for finer control over thread scheduling and more responsive multitasking, especially for time-critical applications like motor control or high-frequency sensor sampling. However, the downside is a significant increase in overhead: the CPU will enter the SysTick_Handler interrupt 10 times more often, consuming more processing time just handling context switches and timer management. This reduces the time available for actual thread execution and can degrade overall system performance. It may also increase power consumption, which is critical in embedded or battery-powered systems. So while you gain precision, you sacrifice efficiency and run a higher risk of interrupt saturation.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,3,'2025-07-28_12-20-17','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms would increase the system\'s timing resolution, allowing for finer control over thread scheduling and more responsive multitasking, especially for time-critical applications like motor control or high-frequency sensor sampling. However, the downside is a significant increase in overhead: the CPU will enter the SysTick_Handler interrupt 10 times more often, consuming more processing time just handling context switches and timer management. This reduces the time available for actual thread execution and can degrade overall system performance. It may also increase power consumption, which is critical in embedded or battery-powered systems. So while you gain precision, you sacrifice efficiency and run a higher risk of interrupt saturation.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,3,'2025-07-28_12-50-48','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms would increase the system\'s timing resolution, allowing for finer control over thread scheduling and more responsive multitasking, especially for time-critical applications like motor control or high-frequency sensor sampling. However, the downside is a significant increase in overhead: the CPU will enter the SysTick_Handler interrupt 10 times more often, consuming more processing time just handling context switches and timer management. This reduces the time available for actual thread execution and can degrade overall system performance. It may also increase power consumption, which is critical in embedded or battery-powered systems. So while you gain precision, you sacrifice efficiency and run a higher risk of interrupt saturation.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,4,'2025-07-27_13-12-49','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking differ both in how the user writes code and in what the operating system must handle. In co-operative multitasking, the user is responsible for explicitly yielding control by calling a function like osYield() within each thread, which means the system relies on the user to ensure all threads get a chance to run. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking requires no such action from the user—threads are automatically interrupted and switched based on a timer managed by the OS, making it more convenient and reliable in practice. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler because context switches only occur when requested. However, pre-emptive multitasking is more complex, as it requires configuring a hardware timer, tracking each thread’s runtime, and forcibly switching threads when their timeslice expires. While pre-emptive systems are harder to implement, they offer better fairness and responsiveness, especially when user threads don’t or can’t yield properly.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,4,'2025-07-28_12-20-17','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking differ both in how the user writes code and in what the operating system must handle. In co-operative multitasking, the user is responsible for explicitly yielding control by calling a function like osYield() within each thread, which means the system relies on the user to ensure all threads get a chance to run. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking requires no such action from the user—threads are automatically interrupted and switched based on a timer managed by the OS, making it more convenient and reliable in practice. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler because context switches only occur when requested. However, pre-emptive multitasking is more complex, as it requires configuring a hardware timer, tracking each thread’s runtime, and forcibly switching threads when their timeslice expires. While pre-emptive systems are harder to implement, they offer better fairness and responsiveness, especially when user threads don’t or can’t yield properly.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,4,'2025-07-28_12-50-48','Co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking differ both in how the user writes code and in what the operating system must handle. In co-operative multitasking, the user is responsible for explicitly yielding control by calling a function like osYield() within each thread, which means the system relies on the user to ensure all threads get a chance to run. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking requires no such action from the user—threads are automatically interrupted and switched based on a timer managed by the OS, making it more convenient and reliable in practice. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler because context switches only occur when requested. However, pre-emptive multitasking is more complex, as it requires configuring a hardware timer, tracking each thread’s runtime, and forcibly switching threads when their timeslice expires. While pre-emptive systems are harder to implement, they offer better fairness and responsiveness, especially when user threads don’t or can’t yield properly.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,5,'2025-07-27_13-12-49','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately trigger the same context switch mechanism, just at different times and under different conditions. When a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily asks the OS to switch context, whereas in pre-emptive switching, the OS uses a timer (like SysTick) to force a context switch after a thread’s timeslice expires. In both cases, the OS performs the same actions: saving the current thread’s state, selecting the next thread, and restoring its state. Because these mechanisms share the same context-switching infrastructure (e.g., via PendSV), they are compatible and can coexist. This allows cooperative threads to yield when appropriate, while still ensuring fairness and responsiveness through pre-emption if they don’t.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,5,'2025-07-28_12-20-17','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately trigger the same context switch mechanism, just at different times and under different conditions. When a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily asks the OS to switch context, whereas in pre-emptive switching, the OS uses a timer (like SysTick) to force a context switch after a thread’s timeslice expires. In both cases, the OS performs the same actions: saving the current thread’s state, selecting the next thread, and restoring its state. Because these mechanisms share the same context-switching infrastructure (e.g., via PendSV), they are compatible and can coexist. This allows cooperative threads to yield when appropriate, while still ensuring fairness and responsiveness through pre-emption if they don’t.\n',1,'s25'),('r242huan',5,5,'2025-07-28_12-50-48','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately trigger the same context switch mechanism, just at different times and under different conditions. When a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily asks the OS to switch context, whereas in pre-emptive switching, the OS uses a timer (like SysTick) to force a context switch after a thread’s timeslice expires. In both cases, the OS performs the same actions: saving the current thread’s state, selecting the next thread, and restoring its state. Because these mechanisms share the same context-switching infrastructure (e.g., via PendSV), they are compatible and can coexist. This allows cooperative threads to yield when appropriate, while still ensuring fairness and responsiveness through pre-emption if they don’t.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,1,'2025-05-13_10-59-04','HAL acts as the layer that directly configures the hardware and provides them as libraries and functions for us to use, so instead of directly working with the hardware, we can now simply call a function to help us do so using HAL.\nQuestion\nIn your own words, explain what the __io_putchar function does and why it is needed.',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,1,'2025-05-13_11-12-25','HAL acts as the layer that directly configures the hardware and provides them as libraries and functions for us to use, so instead of directly working with the hardware, we can now simply call a function to help us do so using HAL. The consist of high level functions that lets developers interact with hardware components like GPIO, UART and timers, without the need to configure the hardware registers.\nQuestion\nIn your own words, explain what the __io_putchar function does and why it is needed.',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,1,'2025-05-13_11-36-58','HAL acts as the layer that directly configures the hardware and provides them as libraries and functions for us to use, so instead of directly working with the hardware, we can now simply call a function to help us do so using HAL. The consist of high level functions that lets developers interact with hardware components like GPIO, UART and timers, without the need to configure the hardware registers.\nQuestion\nIn your own words, explain what the __io_putchar function does and why it is needed.',0,'s25'),('r36dong',1,1,'2025-05-27_10-12-56','HAL acts as the layer that directly configures the hardware and provides them as libraries and functions for us to use, so instead of directly working with the hardware, we can now simply call a function to help us do so using HAL. The consist of high level functions that lets developers interact with hardware components like GPIO, UART and timers, without the need to configure the hardware registers.\nThe HAL is a software layer that sits between your application code and the microcontroller’s hardware registers. It provides a set of standardized libraries and functions—such as HAL_GPIO_WritePin(), HAL_UART_Transmit(), or HAL_TIM_Base_Start()—so you never have to manipulate control registers or bitfields directly. In short, the HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) abstracts away the nitty-gritty of register setup and lets embedded developers focus on application logic rather than low-level hardware details.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,1,'2025-05-27_10-20-06','HAL acts as the layer that directly configures the hardware and provides them as libraries and functions for us to use, so instead of directly working with the hardware, we can now simply call a function to help us do so using HAL. The consist of high level functions that lets developers interact with hardware components like GPIO, UART and timers, without the need to configure the hardware registers.\nThe HAL is a software layer that sits between your application code and the microcontroller’s hardware registers. It provides a set of standardized libraries and functions—such as HAL_GPIO_WritePin(), HAL_UART_Transmit(), or HAL_TIM_Base_Start()—so you never have to manipulate control registers or bitfields directly. In short, the HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) abstracts away the nitty-gritty of register setup and lets embedded developers focus on application logic rather than low-level hardware details.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,2,'2025-05-13_10-59-04','This function is a custom and specified function that lets the prinf function sends one character over UART\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',1,2,'2025-05-13_11-12-25','This function is a custom and specified function that lets the printf function sends one character over UART. It is called automatically everything a printf function is called, without it the computer would not know how to send characters to the terminal. Microcontrollers don’t have standard outputs, so we need to define how and where to send the data to. This function connects the C standard library\'s printf() to the hardware, enabling us to see program output for debugging or monitoring.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,2,'2025-05-13_11-36-58','This function is a custom and specified function that lets the printf function sends one character over UART. It is called automatically everything a printf function is called, without it the computer would not know how to send characters to the terminal. Microcontrollers don’t have standard outputs, so we need to define how and where to send the data to. This function connects the C standard library\'s printf() to the hardware, enabling us to see program output for debugging or monitoring.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,2,'2025-05-27_10-12-56','This function is a custom and specified function that lets the printf function sends one character over UART. It is called automatically everything a printf function is called, without it the computer would not know how to send characters to the terminal. Microcontrollers don’t have standard outputs, so we need to define how and where to send the data to. This function connects the C standard library\'s printf() to the hardware, enabling us to see program output for debugging or monitoring.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,2,'2025-05-27_10-20-06','This function is a custom and specified function that lets the printf function sends one character over UART. It is called automatically everything a printf function is called, without it the computer would not know how to send characters to the terminal. Microcontrollers don’t have standard outputs, so we need to define how and where to send the data to. This function connects the C standard library\'s printf() to the hardware, enabling us to see program output for debugging or monitoring.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,3,'2025-05-13_10-59-04','Debugger stops the code midway for us to look at the registers and variables. This shows the value of MSP_INITIAL_VAL changing throughout the code, prior to run and after.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',1,3,'2025-05-13_11-12-25','Debugger stops the code midway for us to look at the registers and variables. This shows the value of MSP_INITIAL_VAL changing throughout the code, prior to run and after. When just running printf, we would not be able to carefully see which lines of code changes the values of certain variables or register values. With the debugger, we can inspect the stack pointer values and verify changes before reaching the while(1) loop. This gives us precise visibility into how the stack is set up and how memory is being allocated during execution\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,3,'2025-05-13_11-36-58','Debugger stops the code midway for us to look at the registers and variables. This shows the value of MSP_INITIAL_VAL changing throughout the code, prior to run and after. When just running printf, we would not be able to carefully see which lines of code changes the values of certain variables or register values. With the debugger, we can inspect the stack pointer values and verify changes before reaching the while(1) loop. This gives us precise visibility into how the stack is set up and how memory is being allocated during execution\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,3,'2025-05-27_10-12-56','Debugger stops the code midway for us to look at the registers and variables. This shows the value of MSP_INITIAL_VAL changing throughout the code, prior to run and after. When just running printf, we would not be able to carefully see which lines of code changes the values of certain variables or register values. With the debugger, we can inspect the stack pointer values and verify changes before reaching the while(1) loop. This gives us precise visibility into how the stack is set up and how memory is being allocated during execution\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',1,3,'2025-05-27_10-20-06','We relied on a hardware debugger because unlike printf, which merely spits out formatted data over a UART or semihosting channel (and only once your clocks, bus and I/O are up), a debugger lets you halt the CPU at an exact instruction, freeze all registers—including memory-mapped system registers like MSP_INITIAL_VAL or the Program Status Register—and inspect or even overwrite them on the spot without adding hundreds of bytes of library code or millisecond-scale I/O delays that would violate real-time deadlines. In contrast, every printf call not only assumes your I/O is initialized (so it often can’t run early in startup), but it also perturbs timing, masks which instruction changed a value, and can’t target non-RAM registers at all. Only with breakpoints, single-step execution, and direct register/memory access can you see precisely how the stack pointer and other registers evolve before reaching while(1), making a debugger indispensable for low-level embedded bring-up and register-level debugging.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,4,'2025-05-13_10-59-04','We got the new value of PSP by subtracting from the MSP_init_val and this allocates space for psp. We need to know the address of the starting point in order to subtract certain value from it.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',1,4,'2025-05-13_11-12-25','We got the new value of PSP by subtracting from the MSP_init_val and this reserves a portion of the memory for the main stack and places the psp portion under the main stack. We needed to know the initial MSP value so we could calculate where to place the PSP safely, ensuring it doesn\'t overlap with the main stack. This approach gives us manual control over how stack memory is split between the main execution and other contexts like threads.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',1,4,'2025-05-13_11-36-58','We got the new value of PSP by subtracting a fixed amount of bytes — in this case 0x400 — from MSP_init_val. This value was chosen because we wanted to set aside exactly 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since stacks grow downward in memory, subtracting from the initial MSP moves us further down the stack space to create room for a new stack. This new PSP value marks the start of the thread’s stack. We need to know the initial MSP value because that tells us where the top of the stack memory begins. Without knowing this, we wouldn\'t know where to safely place the PSP. If the PSP is placed too close to the MSP or overlaps with it, the two stacks could overwrite each other during execution, which would cause bugs or crashes. By carefully calculating the PSP location based on the known MSP value and subtracting the main stack size, we make sure the stacks do not overlap and are placed correctly in memory.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,4,'2025-05-27_10-12-56','We got the new value of PSP by subtracting a fixed amount of bytes — in this case 0x400 — from MSP_init_val. This value was chosen because we wanted to set aside exactly 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since stacks grow downward in memory, subtracting from the initial MSP moves us further down the stack space to create room for a new stack. This new PSP value marks the start of the thread’s stack. We need to know the initial MSP value because that tells us where the top of the stack memory begins. Without knowing this, we wouldn\'t know where to safely place the PSP. If the PSP is placed too close to the MSP or overlaps with it, the two stacks could overwrite each other during execution, which would cause bugs or crashes. By carefully calculating the PSP location based on the known MSP value and subtracting the main stack size, we make sure the stacks do not overlap and are placed correctly in memory.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,4,'2025-05-27_10-20-06','We got the new value of PSP by subtracting a fixed amount of bytes — in this case 0x400 — from MSP_init_val. This value was chosen because we wanted to set aside exactly 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since stacks grow downward in memory, subtracting from the initial MSP moves us further down the stack space to create room for a new stack. This new PSP value marks the start of the thread’s stack. We need to know the initial MSP value because that tells us where the top of the stack memory begins. Without knowing this, we wouldn\'t know where to safely place the PSP. If the PSP is placed too close to the MSP or overlaps with it, the two stacks could overwrite each other during execution, which would cause bugs or crashes. By carefully calculating the PSP location based on the known MSP value and subtracting the main stack size, we make sure the stacks do not overlap and are placed correctly in memory.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,5,'2025-05-13_10-59-04','We could just do a for loop and each time subtracts certain amount of memory space starting from the top. The starting pointer address would just be a multiple of the space wee allocated starting from 0.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',1,5,'2025-05-13_11-12-25','We could define a main stack size and thread stack size. We could just do a for loop and each time subtracts certain amount of memory space starting from the top, like starting from MSP_init_val, we subtract the MAIN_STACK_SIZE to find the end of the main stack.  The starting pointer address would just be a multiple of the space wee allocated starting from 0. Then, each thread gets its own non-overlapping block of memory for its stack. This method allows us to preallocate memory for each thread’s stack in a predictable, organized way.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',1,5,'2025-05-13_11-36-58','We could define a fixed MAIN_STACK_SIZE and THREAD_STACK_SIZE and start from MSP_init_val, which gives us the top of the stack memory. First, we subtract the MAIN_STACK_SIZE to reserve space for the main stack. Then, for each thread, we subtract additional blocks of size THREAD_STACK_SIZE to calculate the starting address for each thread\'s stack. To make sure each thread stack address is valid on the Cortex-M4, we have to make sure the addresses are 8-byte aligned, because the stack pointer must always be aligned to 8 bytes for correct operation. If we don\'t align the addresses properly, the processor might trigger a fault or behave unpredictably. We can ensure alignment by choosing stack sizes that are multiples of 8 and by verifying that the resulting stack addresses are also divisible by 8. This method gives each thread its own non-overlapping block of memory in a predictable way while also satisfying the memory alignment requirements of the hardware. It allows us to avoid stack overlap and ensures that all stack regions are valid for use by the processor.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,5,'2025-05-27_10-12-56','We could define a fixed MAIN_STACK_SIZE and THREAD_STACK_SIZE and start from MSP_init_val, which gives us the top of the stack memory. First, we subtract the MAIN_STACK_SIZE to reserve space for the main stack. Then, for each thread, we subtract additional blocks of size THREAD_STACK_SIZE to calculate the starting address for each thread\'s stack. To make sure each thread stack address is valid on the Cortex-M4, we have to make sure the addresses are 8-byte aligned, because the stack pointer must always be aligned to 8 bytes for correct operation. If we don\'t align the addresses properly, the processor might trigger a fault or behave unpredictably. We can ensure alignment by choosing stack sizes that are multiples of 8 and by verifying that the resulting stack addresses are also divisible by 8. This method gives each thread its own non-overlapping block of memory in a predictable way while also satisfying the memory alignment requirements of the hardware. It allows us to avoid stack overlap and ensures that all stack regions are valid for use by the processor.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',1,5,'2025-05-27_10-20-06','We could define a fixed MAIN_STACK_SIZE and THREAD_STACK_SIZE and start from MSP_init_val, which gives us the top of the stack memory. First, we subtract the MAIN_STACK_SIZE to reserve space for the main stack. Then, for each thread, we subtract additional blocks of size THREAD_STACK_SIZE to calculate the starting address for each thread\'s stack. To make sure each thread stack address is valid on the Cortex-M4, we have to make sure the addresses are 8-byte aligned, because the stack pointer must always be aligned to 8 bytes for correct operation. If we don\'t align the addresses properly, the processor might trigger a fault or behave unpredictably. We can ensure alignment by choosing stack sizes that are multiples of 8 and by verifying that the resulting stack addresses are also divisible by 8. This method gives each thread its own non-overlapping block of memory in a predictable way while also satisfying the memory alignment requirements of the hardware. It allows us to avoid stack overlap and ensures that all stack regions are valid for use by the processor.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',2,1,'2025-05-27_10-30-35','When the address of print_continuously is loaded, it basically forced the PC to immediately jump to that address instead of the next instruction sequentially that was supposed to run next. As a result, execution began inside print_continuously, which contains an infinite loop calling printf(\"Hello, PC!\"). From that point on, only the print function ever ran, and continuous printing, because it basically just hijacked the normal sequence of flow by writing directly into the PC register.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',2,2,'2025-05-27_10-30-35','A system call is a controlled trap from unprivileged “user” code into a privileged “kernel” or OS layer to request services (like I/O or thread switching) that user code isn’t allowed to perform directly. In Cortex-M it is implemented by issuing an SVC #n instruction: the immediate value n identifies which service is being requested. The CPU then vectors to the SVC exception handler, which examines that number and dispatches to the appropriate handler routine in C.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',2,3,'2025-05-27_10-30-35','In the code, setting up the thread’s stack means carving out a fresh region of memory and then pre-loading it with exactly the registers the processor will need when that thread starts. In main() the code read the initial MSP value from address 0x0, subtract 0x400 bytes to reserve space, and set stackptr to that new top‐of‐stack, then it uses the *(--stackptr) = … pattern to push, in reverse order, first the xPSR (with the Thumb bit set), then the PC pointing at print_continously, and fourteen zeros to stand in for LR, R12, R3–R0, and R11–R4—exactly matching the Cortex-M hardware’s exception frame layout. When it invoke the start() wrapper (SVC #3), the handler does __set_PSP((uint32_t)stackptr), switching the Process Stack Pointer to the prepared buffer, and then runFirstThread. Because it stored all the register values in the precise order the hardware expects, the processor can restore a valid execution context and jump straight into the thread function without crashing. We push fourteen zeros into the stack frame because we need to reserve space for every register the CPU expects to restore when we switch into the new thread—even those registers that don’t carry any meaningful startup value. On Cortex-M, an exception return will automatically pop R0–R3, R12, LR, PC and xPSR from the stack; any additional registers (R4–R11) are typically saved and restored by your runtime or RTOS. By laying out exactly sixteen words (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0 and then R11…R4) you guarantee that every pop or manual restore finds a well-defined value at the correct offset. By “setting up the stack” this way, it is ensured that every register the new thread needs is already in place, which is why storing those values on the stack is essential for a successful context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',2,4,'2025-05-27_10-30-35','When CONTROL is switched so that thread-mode uses PSP, all subsequent stack operations—whether they’re the hardware popping the pre-built exception frame or the compiler’s function-entry pushes—will use PSP rather than MSP. If PSP still contains its reset or garbage value, the very first push or pop will address an invalid RAM location. That invalid access triggers a bus fault, which escalates into a hard fault. Calling __set_PSP((uint32_t)stackptr) ensures PSP points into the valid region, so every register save or restore lands in defined memory and avoids the fault.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',2,5,'2025-05-27_10-30-35','If the thread entry were to return instead of looping, execution would attempt to pop the return address (LR) from the stack frame and jump there. In the provided setup only xPSR and PC are explicitly initialized—the next slot, reserved for LR, was filled with zero when the sixteen‐word frame was built (for (…)=0). Thus a single call to the thread function would return into address 0x00000000, which is not valid program code. Fetching an instruction from address 0 would trigger a bus fault (or usage fault) that escalates into a hard fault. Because no valid LR was stored, there is no safe place for execution to continue after the thread returns, so a crash is inevitable.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',3,1,'2025-06-10_12-23-10','Preprocessing: Each .c file is fed into the C pre-processor, in which the header files are passed in, macros are expanded, and conditional compile block are stripped out\nCompile: The compiler translates each resulting unit into machine independent object code for which every function or global variable defined in that file is recorded in the object’s file’s symbol table.\nAssemble: The complier emits assemble and an assembler turn it into relocatable machine code inside the object file\nLink: the linker combines all object files into one final binary file where it matches up symbol definitions with symbol uses and it fills in the address of functions and global as well as lays out code and data in memory sections.\nOutput: For an STM32 Project, the linker write an ELF executable for debugging\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',3,1,'2025-06-10_12-36-49','When you build a program made of several .c files, the tool-chain goes through three logical stages. First comes pre-processing: the pre-processor pastes in header files, expands macros, and removes or keeps code according to #ifdef directives, leaving you with a complete C translation unit for each source file. Next is compilation: the compiler itself reads that translation unit and translates it directly into a machine-specific, relocatable object file (.o). All functions and global variables in the file are recorded in the object file’s symbol table. (Some compilers briefly emit assembly code and immediately assemble it, but that assembling is an internal convenience, not a separate step in the formal model—the compiler is still considered to have produced the object code.) Finally, during linking, the linker gathers every object file (plus any libraries you requested), resolves all symbol references, assigns final memory addresses, applies relocations, and emits a single executable image—for an STM32 project this is usually an ELF file for debugging and a stripped .bin or .hex for flashing.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',3,2,'2025-06-10_12-23-10','#include is a textual paste where it dumps the entire contents of the file into the current translation unit before compilation. If you include a .c file, all of its function definitions end up duplicated in every file that also includes it, leading to “multiple definition” linker errors. The common practice is to place only declarations (prototypes, typedefs, #defines) in .h headers. Each .c file then compiles exactly once, producing exactly one set of definitions for the linker to merge.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',3,2,'2025-06-10_12-36-49','#include is a textual paste where it dumps the entire contents of the file into the current translation unit before compilation. If you include a .c file, all of its function definitions end up duplicated in every file that also includes it, leading to “multiple definition” linker errors. The common practice is to place only declarations (prototypes, typedefs, #defines) in .h headers. Each .c file then compiles exactly once, producing exactly one set of definitions for the linker to merge.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',3,3,'2025-06-10_12-23-10','Write the thread function: In main, define a function that takes a void * argument and contains the code that we want to run.\nInitialize the kernel: Call osKernelInitialize, once, right after we bring up the HAL.\nCreate the thread: Call osCreateThread, the kernel allocates a stack, builds the startup frame, and stores it in its thread table.\nStart the scheduler: Call osKernelStart, the kernel loads the new thread’s stack pointer, switches to thread mode, and begins executing your function. From this moment the thread takes over the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',3,3,'2025-06-10_12-36-49','Write the thread function: In main, define a function that takes a void * argument and contains the code that we want to run.\nInitialize the kernel: Call osKernelInitialize, once, right after we bring up the HAL.\nCreate the thread: Call osCreateThread, the kernel allocates a stack, builds the startup frame, and stores it in its thread table.\nStart the scheduler: Call osKernelStart, the kernel loads the new thread’s stack pointer, switches to thread mode, and begins executing your function. From this moment the thread takes over the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',3,4,'2025-06-10_12-23-10','The stack pool is a block of RAM reserved exclusively for thread stacks. Each time osCreateThread is called, the kernel carves 0x400 from the top of that pool, hands its top address to the thread, and updates a next free space pointer. Because every thread gets its own slice, their local variables and return addresses never collide. In future labs, once we extend the kernel to keep an array of thread descriptors, the allocator will let many threads coexist; the scheduler can then swap the processor’s PSP between those stacks whenever it switches context.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',3,4,'2025-06-10_12-36-49','The stack pool is a block of RAM reserved exclusively for thread stacks. Each time osCreateThread is called, the kernel carves 0x400 from the top of that pool, hands its top address to the thread, and updates a next free space pointer. Because every thread gets its own slice, their local variables and return addresses never collide. In future labs, once we extend the kernel to keep an array of thread descriptors, the allocator will let many threads coexist; the scheduler can then swap the processor’s PSP between those stacks whenever it switches context.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',3,5,'2025-06-10_12-23-10','Kernel code lives in Core→Src/Inc but is compiled with full privilege. It manages stacks, performs context switches, services SVC calls, and stores global OS data. User code contains thread functions and high-level logic. It must use the kernel’s public API instead of touching hardware registers or privileged state directly. The reason why it is designed like this is because for safety, clarity and maintainability purposes, like bugs in user code cannot corrupt kernal data, we do not need to read or modify the low level scheduler code and the kernal can be updated without changes in other user modules.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',3,5,'2025-06-10_12-36-49','Kernel code lives in Core→Src/Inc but is compiled with full privilege. It manages stacks, performs context switches, services SVC calls, and stores global OS data. User code contains thread functions and high-level logic. It must use the kernel’s public API instead of touching hardware registers or privileged state directly. The reason why it is designed like this is because for safety, clarity and maintainability purposes, like bugs in user code cannot corrupt kernal data, we do not need to read or modify the low level scheduler code and the kernal can be updated without changes in other user modules.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,1,'2025-07-10_18-36-03','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling works by treating the queue as a circular data structure where threads are dequeued from the front and enqueued at the back after their time slice expires. When a thread yields or its time slice ends, it is dequeued from the front and the newly dequeued thread runs. When that thread yields, it gets enqueued at the back of the queue, and the next thread at the front automatically becomes the next to run. This approach offers several benefits over arrays including but not limited to dynamic sizing allows adding or removing threads without wasting fixed array slots, the queue operations inherently implement the circular scheduling pattern making the code more intuitive, memory efficiency is improved since space is only allocated for active threads, thread lifecycle management becomes easier with simple enqueue or dequeue operations for adding new threads or removing terminated ones, and queue implementations can be more cache-friendly than indexing into arrays.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',4,1,'2025-07-10_19-02-16','Our present round-robin scheduler already mimics a queue: the TCBs sit in a fixed array and we simply bump an index modulo the thread count, which is equivalent to removing the “front” thread and appending it to the “back.” Replacing that array-plus-index with an explicit queue would give true dynamic sizing—nodes exist only for threads that are alive—so no array slots lie idle and terminated threads disappear with a single dequeue. It also makes insertion and removal of threads conceptually cleaner: create -> enqueue, exit -> dequeue. The trade-off is extra pointer chasing and bookkeeping on every context switch, plus heap or pool management code, all of which introduce CPU cycles and can scatter TCBs in memory, harming cache locality. On a small MCU with a known hard limit on threads, the simple array is usually faster and simpler; a queue becomes worthwhile only when threads are created and destroyed frequently or the upper bound on thread count is unknown.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,1,'2025-07-10_20-23-47','Our present round-robin scheduler already mimics a queue: the TCBs sit in a fixed array and we simply bump an index modulo the thread count, which is equivalent to removing the “front” thread and appending it to the “back.” Replacing that array-plus-index with an explicit queue would give true dynamic sizing—nodes exist only for threads that are alive—so no array slots lie idle and terminated threads disappear with a single dequeue. It also makes insertion and removal of threads conceptually cleaner: create -> enqueue, exit -> dequeue. The trade-off is extra pointer chasing and bookkeeping on every context switch, plus heap or pool management code, all of which introduce CPU cycles and can scatter TCBs in memory, harming cache locality. On a small MCU with a known hard limit on threads, the simple array is usually faster and simpler; a queue becomes worthwhile only when threads are created and destroyed frequently or the upper bound on thread count is unknown.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,1,'2025-07-10_20-31-28','Our present round-robin scheduler already mimics a queue: the TCBs sit in a fixed array and we simply bump an index modulo the thread count, which is equivalent to removing the “front” thread and appending it to the “back.” Replacing that array-plus-index with an explicit queue would give true dynamic sizing—nodes exist only for threads that are alive—so no array slots lie idle and terminated threads disappear with a single dequeue. It also makes insertion and removal of threads conceptually cleaner: create -> enqueue, exit -> dequeue. The trade-off is extra pointer chasing and bookkeeping on every context switch, plus heap or pool management code, all of which introduce CPU cycles and can scatter TCBs in memory, harming cache locality. On a small MCU with a known hard limit on threads, the simple array is usually faster and simpler; a queue becomes worthwhile only when threads are created and destroyed frequently or the upper bound on thread count is unknown.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,2,'2025-07-10_18-36-03','The PendSV_Handler performs context switching through a carefully orchestrated sequence of steps. First, it saves the current thread\'s context by using the STMDB (store multiple, decrement before) instruction to push registers R4-R11 onto the current thread\'s stack, preserving the CPU state. Next, it calls the osSched() function which saves the current PSP to the current thread\'s TCB and updates the currentThread variable to point to the next thread in round-robin order. After osSched() returns, the handler restores the next thread\'s context by loading the new PSP value and using LDMIA (load multiple, increment after) to restore registers R4-R11 from the new thread\'s stack. Finally, it returns to the new thread by setting LR to 0xFFFFFFFD, which tells the processor to return to thread mode using PSP, effectively jumping to where the new thread left off. The key insight is that PendSV has the lowest interrupt priority, so it only runs when no other interrupts are pending, ensuring clean context switches without interference from other system events.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',4,2,'2025-07-10_19-02-16','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch in a fixed order. When the handler starts, the processor has already pushed registers R0 to R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR onto the current thread’s stack using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). The handler immediately copies that PSP value into a general-purpose register and then pushes the remaining callee-saved registers, R4 through R11, onto the same stack. With every register now saved, it stores this updated PSP value into the current thread’s control block so the kernel can find the thread’s exact resume point later. Next, the handler calls osSched, a C routine that advances the scheduler and returns the saved PSP of the thread that should run next. Back in assembly, the handler loads this PSP into the processor, pops R4 through R11 from the new thread’s stack to restore its state and finally executes a branch-and-exchange instruction with a special link-register value (0xFFFFFFFD). That value tells the processor to return to thread mode and to finish popping the auto-saved registers, so execution resumes exactly where the new thread last yielded. PendSV is set to the lowest interrupt priority, which guarantees that nothing pre-empts this save-and-restore sequence.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',4,2,'2025-07-10_20-23-47','PendSV_Handler is the special interrupt routine the ARM core runs whenever the kernel wants to hand the CPU from one thread to another. When it fires, the processor has already pushed a basic register set (r0 to r3, r12, lr, pc, xPSR) onto the current thread’s stack, which is addressed through the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). The handler’s first job is to save the rest of the callee-saved registers, r4 through r11, by pushing them onto the same stack. It then writes the resulting PSP value into the current thread’s control block so the kernel can later restore that exact point of execution. Next, PendSV_Handler calls the C routine osSched, which selects the next runnable thread and returns that thread’s saved PSP. The handler loads this new PSP into the hardware, pops r4 through r11 from the new thread’s stack to rebuild its register state, and then executes the return instruction that tells the core to pop the automatically-saved registers and resume execution in thread mode. Because PendSV is configured at the lowest interrupt priority, no higher-priority interrupt can interrupt this sequence, ensuring the old thread is fully saved and the new thread is fully restored without interference.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',4,2,'2025-07-10_20-31-28','PendSv_Handler is the special interrupt routine the ARM core runs whenever the kernel wants to hand the CPU from one thread to another. When it fires, the processor has already pushed a basic register set (r0 to r3, r12, lr, pc, xPSR) onto the current thread’s stack, which is addressed through the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). The handler’s first job is to save the rest of the callee-saved registers, r4 through r11, by pushing them onto the same stack. It then writes the resulting PSP value into the current thread’s control block so the kernel can later restore that exact point of execution. Next, PendSV_Handler calls the C routine osSched, which selects the next runnable thread and returns that thread’s saved PSP. The handler loads this new PSP into the hardware, pops r4 through r11 from the new thread’s stack to rebuild its register state, and then executes the return instruction that tells the core to pop the automatically-saved registers and resume execution in thread mode. Because PendSV is configured at the lowest interrupt priority, no higher-priority interrupt can interrupt this sequence, ensuring the old thread is fully saved and the new thread is fully restored without interference.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,3,'2025-07-10_18-36-03','This priority-based scheduling approach is fundamentally flawed because it leads to starvation, where lower priority threads never get a chance to run if high-priority threads are always ready to execute. The system becomes inherently unfair as some threads may never receive CPU time, potentially causing critical low-priority tasks to be indefinitely postponed. This creates a serious resource deadlock potential where if a low-priority thread holds a resource that a high-priority thread needs, the high-priority thread will wait forever since the low-priority thread never gets scheduled to release the resource. Additionally, system responsiveness suffers as critical system maintenance tasks might be starved if user applications have higher priorities. Unlike round-robin scheduling where threads cooperatively yield and share CPU time fairly, priority scheduling can lead to monopolization of CPU resources by a few high-priority threads. A better approach would incorporate priority scheduling with aging mechanisms that gradually increase the priority of waiting threads over time, or implement time-slicing even for high-priority threads to ensure fairness and prevent starvation while still respecting priority differences\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',4,3,'2025-07-10_19-02-16','If the scheduler always chooses the runnable task with the highest numeric priority and never relinquishes the CPU, the system effectively collapses into a single-task environment. Once a thread with the top priority exists, every call to the scheduler returns that same thread, so lower-priority tasks never run—they starve forever. Any resource that a low-priority thread holds and a high-priority thread needs can never be released, leading to priority-inversion deadlock. Fairness and real-time predictability disappear unless the design adds aging, time-slicing, or explicit blocking to force the CPU away from the top-priority thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,3,'2025-07-10_20-23-47','If the scheduler always chooses the runnable task with the highest numeric priority and never relinquishes the CPU, the system effectively collapses into a single-task environment. Once a thread with the top priority exists, every call to the scheduler returns that same thread, so lower-priority tasks never run—they starve forever. Any resource that a low-priority thread holds and a high-priority thread needs can never be released, leading to priority-inversion deadlock. Fairness and real-time predictability disappear unless the design adds aging, time-slicing, or explicit blocking to force the CPU away from the top-priority thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,3,'2025-07-10_20-31-28','If the scheduler always chooses the runnable task with the highest numeric priority and never relinquishes the CPU, the system effectively collapses into a single-task environment. Once a thread with the top priority exists, every call to the scheduler returns that same thread, so lower-priority tasks never run—they starve forever. Any resource that a low-priority thread holds and a high-priority thread needs can never be released, leading to priority-inversion deadlock. Fairness and real-time predictability disappear unless the design adds aging, time-slicing, or explicit blocking to force the CPU away from the top-priority thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,4,'2025-07-10_18-36-03','Calling C functions from assembly handlers like PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler provides significant benefits compared to writing everything in assembly. The primary advantages include improved code readability and maintainability since C code is much easier to understand, debug, and modify than complex assembly routines. Development time is greatly reduced as implementing complex logic like thread scheduling algorithms, data structure manipulation, and conditional branching is far more straightforward in C. The code becomes more portable across different ARM architectures since the C compiler handles architecture-specific optimizations and instruction selection. Additionally, C provides access to standard libraries, debugging tools, and allows for better integration with the rest of the kernel codebase. However, there are some downsides to this approach. There is a slight performance overhead due to function call overhead and the need to preserve additional registers when transitioning between assembly and C. The stack usage increases since C functions may use more stack space for local variables and function calls. There\'s also less precise control over timing-critical operations, and the developer must ensure that the C functions don\'t inadvertently modify processor state in ways that could interfere with the context switching process. Despite these minor drawbacks, the benefits of using C for complex logic while keeping only the essential low-level register manipulation in assembly represents a good balance between performance and maintainability\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,4,'2025-07-10_19-02-16','Calling C functions from assembly handlers like PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler provides significant benefits compared to writing everything in assembly. The primary advantages include improved code readability and maintainability since C code is much easier to understand, debug, and modify than complex assembly routines. Development time is greatly reduced as implementing complex logic like thread scheduling algorithms, data structure manipulation, and conditional branching is far more straightforward in C. The code becomes more portable across different ARM architectures since the C compiler handles architecture-specific optimizations and instruction selection. Additionally, C provides access to standard libraries, debugging tools, and allows for better integration with the rest of the kernel codebase. However, there are some downsides to this approach. There is a slight performance overhead due to function call overhead and the need to preserve additional registers when transitioning between assembly and C. The stack usage increases since C functions may use more stack space for local variables and function calls. There\'s also less precise control over timing-critical operations, and the developer must ensure that the C functions don\'t inadvertently modify processor state in ways that could interfere with the context switching process. Despite these minor drawbacks, the benefits of using C for complex logic while keeping only the essential low-level register manipulation in assembly represents a good balance between performance and maintainability\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,4,'2025-07-10_20-23-47','Calling C functions from assembly handlers like PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler provides significant benefits compared to writing everything in assembly. The primary advantages include improved code readability and maintainability since C code is much easier to understand, debug, and modify than complex assembly routines. Development time is greatly reduced as implementing complex logic like thread scheduling algorithms, data structure manipulation, and conditional branching is far more straightforward in C. The code becomes more portable across different ARM architectures since the C compiler handles architecture-specific optimizations and instruction selection. Additionally, C provides access to standard libraries, debugging tools, and allows for better integration with the rest of the kernel codebase. However, there are some downsides to this approach. There is a slight performance overhead due to function call overhead and the need to preserve additional registers when transitioning between assembly and C. The stack usage increases since C functions may use more stack space for local variables and function calls. There\'s also less precise control over timing-critical operations, and the developer must ensure that the C functions don\'t inadvertently modify processor state in ways that could interfere with the context switching process. Despite these minor drawbacks, the benefits of using C for complex logic while keeping only the essential low-level register manipulation in assembly represents a good balance between performance and maintainability\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,4,'2025-07-10_20-31-28','Calling C functions from assembly handlers like PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler provides significant benefits compared to writing everything in assembly. The primary advantages include improved code readability and maintainability since C code is much easier to understand, debug, and modify than complex assembly routines. Development time is greatly reduced as implementing complex logic like thread scheduling algorithms, data structure manipulation, and conditional branching is far more straightforward in C. The code becomes more portable across different ARM architectures since the C compiler handles architecture-specific optimizations and instruction selection. Additionally, C provides access to standard libraries, debugging tools, and allows for better integration with the rest of the kernel codebase. However, there are some downsides to this approach. There is a slight performance overhead due to function call overhead and the need to preserve additional registers when transitioning between assembly and C. The stack usage increases since C functions may use more stack space for local variables and function calls. There\'s also less precise control over timing-critical operations, and the developer must ensure that the C functions don\'t inadvertently modify processor state in ways that could interfere with the context switching process. Despite these minor drawbacks, the benefits of using C for complex logic while keeping only the essential low-level register manipulation in assembly represents a good balance between performance and maintainability\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,5,'2025-07-10_18-36-03','If only a single thread were created (such as the print_continuously thread modified to yield after printing), the OS would still function correctly but would exhibit some interesting behavior. The system would work because all the fundamental OS mechanisms remain intact: the kernel initialization would succeed, the single thread would be properly created with its stack and TCB entry, and osKernelStart would successfully launch the thread. When the single thread calls osYield, it would trigger the SVC #4 handler which sets the PendSV pending bit, causing PendSV_Handler to execute and call osSched. The osSched function would dutifully save the current thread\'s context, increment currentThread from 0 to 1, then perform modulo operation with numThreadsRunning (which is 1), resulting in currentThread becoming 0 again - effectively scheduling the same thread. The context would then be restored and execution would continue from where the thread yielded. From an external perspective, the thread would appear to pause momentarily during the yield operation due to the context switch overhead, but would then resume normal execution. This demonstrates that the OS infrastructure is robust and can handle edge cases gracefully, though it also highlights that the context switching overhead in a single-threaded scenario provides no benefit and only wastes CPU cycles. The behavior would be functionally equivalent to the thread simply continuing without yielding, but with added latency from the unnecessary context switch operations\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,5,'2025-07-10_19-02-16','If only a single thread were created (such as the print_continuously thread modified to yield after printing), the OS would still function correctly but would exhibit some interesting behavior. The system would work because all the fundamental OS mechanisms remain intact: the kernel initialization would succeed, the single thread would be properly created with its stack and TCB entry, and osKernelStart would successfully launch the thread. When the single thread calls osYield, it would trigger the SVC #4 handler which sets the PendSV pending bit, causing PendSV_Handler to execute and call osSched. The osSched function would dutifully save the current thread\'s context, increment currentThread from 0 to 1, then perform modulo operation with numThreadsRunning (which is 1), resulting in currentThread becoming 0 again - effectively scheduling the same thread. The context would then be restored and execution would continue from where the thread yielded. From an external perspective, the thread would appear to pause momentarily during the yield operation due to the context switch overhead, but would then resume normal execution. This demonstrates that the OS infrastructure is robust and can handle edge cases gracefully, though it also highlights that the context switching overhead in a single-threaded scenario provides no benefit and only wastes CPU cycles. The behavior would be functionally equivalent to the thread simply continuing without yielding, but with added latency from the unnecessary context switch operations\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,5,'2025-07-10_20-23-47','If only a single thread were created (such as the print_continuously thread modified to yield after printing), the OS would still function correctly but would exhibit some interesting behavior. The system would work because all the fundamental OS mechanisms remain intact: the kernel initialization would succeed, the single thread would be properly created with its stack and TCB entry, and osKernelStart would successfully launch the thread. When the single thread calls osYield, it would trigger the SVC #4 handler which sets the PendSV pending bit, causing PendSV_Handler to execute and call osSched. The osSched function would dutifully save the current thread\'s context, increment currentThread from 0 to 1, then perform modulo operation with numThreadsRunning (which is 1), resulting in currentThread becoming 0 again - effectively scheduling the same thread. The context would then be restored and execution would continue from where the thread yielded. From an external perspective, the thread would appear to pause momentarily during the yield operation due to the context switch overhead, but would then resume normal execution. This demonstrates that the OS infrastructure is robust and can handle edge cases gracefully, though it also highlights that the context switching overhead in a single-threaded scenario provides no benefit and only wastes CPU cycles. The behavior would be functionally equivalent to the thread simply continuing without yielding, but with added latency from the unnecessary context switch operations\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',4,5,'2025-07-10_20-31-28','If only a single thread were created (such as the print_continuously thread modified to yield after printing), the OS would still function correctly but would exhibit some interesting behavior. The system would work because all the fundamental OS mechanisms remain intact: the kernel initialization would succeed, the single thread would be properly created with its stack and TCB entry, and osKernelStart would successfully launch the thread. When the single thread calls osYield, it would trigger the SVC #4 handler which sets the PendSV pending bit, causing PendSV_Handler to execute and call osSched. The osSched function would dutifully save the current thread\'s context, increment currentThread from 0 to 1, then perform modulo operation with numThreadsRunning (which is 1), resulting in currentThread becoming 0 again - effectively scheduling the same thread. The context would then be restored and execution would continue from where the thread yielded. From an external perspective, the thread would appear to pause momentarily during the yield operation due to the context switch overhead, but would then resume normal execution. This demonstrates that the OS infrastructure is robust and can handle edge cases gracefully, though it also highlights that the context switching overhead in a single-threaded scenario provides no benefit and only wastes CPU cycles. The behavior would be functionally equivalent to the thread simply continuing without yielding, but with added latency from the unnecessary context switch operations\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,1,'2025-07-20_16-04-10','When the user invokes the OS function osCreateThread(thread_function, argument) or osCreateThreadWithDeadline(thread_function, argument, deadline), the process of passing arguments into threads starts. Usually, the argument is a pointer to data that the thread will require while it is running. Since the thread will access this data asynchronously after creation, the user must make sure this argument points to valid memory that will be accessible for the duration of the thread, such as a global variable, static variable, or dynamically allocated memory. When the OS receives this thread creation request, it performs several critical steps to ensure the argument reaches the thread correctly. First, the OS allocates a stack for the new thread using alloc_stack(), then constructs an initial stack frame that mimics what the ARM Cortex-M processor expects when entering a function. This stack frame contains 16 registers worth of data, with the most crucial being the Program Counter (PC) set to the thread function\'s address, and importantly, register R0 set to the argument value using *(--sp) = (uint32_t)arg. The OS then stores this prepared stack pointer in the thread\'s control block along with the timeslice and other metadata, making the thread ready for execution. The ARM Architecture Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS), which stipulates that the first function argument is always passed in register R0, is what makes the mechanism function. The hardware automatically loads all of the saved registers from the thread\'s stack, including R0, which contains the argument value, when the thread is finally scheduled and the processor executes a context switch. The function receives this R0 value as its first parameter as the processor branches to the thread function\'s entry point, just like if it had been called normally by another function. Through the standard void* arg parameter, the thread function can instantly access its argument thanks to the OS\'s stack frame construction and the processor\'s calling convention, allowing threads to receive initialization data, configuration structures.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,1,'2025-07-21_14-45-18','When the user invokes the OS function osCreateThread(thread_function, argument) or osCreateThreadWithDeadline(thread_function, argument, deadline), the process of passing arguments into threads starts. Usually, the argument is a pointer to data that the thread will require while it is running. Since the thread will access this data asynchronously after creation, the user must make sure this argument points to valid memory that will be accessible for the duration of the thread, such as a global variable, static variable, or dynamically allocated memory. When the OS receives this thread creation request, it performs several critical steps to ensure the argument reaches the thread correctly. First, the OS allocates a stack for the new thread using alloc_stack(), then constructs an initial stack frame that mimics what the ARM Cortex-M processor expects when entering a function. This stack frame contains 16 registers worth of data, with the most crucial being the Program Counter (PC) set to the thread function\'s address, and importantly, register R0 set to the argument value using *(--sp) = (uint32_t)arg. The OS then stores this prepared stack pointer in the thread\'s control block along with the timeslice and other metadata, making the thread ready for execution. The ARM Architecture Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS), which stipulates that the first function argument is always passed in register R0, is what makes the mechanism function. The hardware automatically loads all of the saved registers from the thread\'s stack, including R0, which contains the argument value, when the thread is finally scheduled and the processor executes a context switch. The function receives this R0 value as its first parameter as the processor branches to the thread function\'s entry point, just like if it had been called normally by another function. Through the standard void* arg parameter, the thread function can instantly access its argument thanks to the OS\'s stack frame construction and the processor\'s calling convention, allowing threads to receive initialization data, configuration structures.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,1,'2025-07-21_14-48-03','When the user invokes the OS function osCreateThread(thread_function, argument) or osCreateThreadWithDeadline(thread_function, argument, deadline), the process of passing arguments into threads starts. Usually, the argument is a pointer to data that the thread will require while it is running. Since the thread will access this data asynchronously after creation, the user must make sure this argument points to valid memory that will be accessible for the duration of the thread, such as a global variable, static variable, or dynamically allocated memory. When the OS receives this thread creation request, it performs several critical steps to ensure the argument reaches the thread correctly. First, the OS allocates a stack for the new thread using alloc_stack(), then constructs an initial stack frame that mimics what the ARM Cortex-M processor expects when entering a function. This stack frame contains 16 registers worth of data, with the mos