he duration of the thread, such as a global variable, static variable, or dynamically allocated memory. When the OS receives this thread creation request, it performs several critical steps to ensure the argument reaches the thread correctly. First, the OS allocates a stack for the new thread using alloc_stack(), then constructs an initial stack frame that mimics what the ARM Cortex-M processor expects when entering a function. This stack frame contains 16 registers worth of data, with the most crucial being the Program Counter (PC) set to the thread function\'s address, and importantly, register R0 set to the argument value using *(--sp) = (uint32_t)arg. The OS then stores this prepared stack pointer in the thread\'s control block along with the timeslice and other metadata, making the thread ready for execution. The ARM Architecture Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS), which stipulates that the first function argument is always passed in register R0, is what makes the mechanism function. The hardware automatically loads all of the saved registers from the thread\'s stack, including R0, which contains the argument value, when the thread is finally scheduled and the processor executes a context switch. The function receives this R0 value as its first parameter as the processor branches to the thread function\'s entry point, just like if it had been called normally by another function. Through the standard void* arg parameter, the thread function can instantly access its argument thanks to the OS\'s stack frame construction and the processor\'s calling convention, allowing threads to receive initialization data, configuration structures.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,2,'2025-07-20_16-04-10','We do not call osYield from within context-switching functions because it leads to infinite recursion and a deadlocked system. When osYield is invoked, it makes SVC #4 system call, which calls SVC_Handler_Main, which sets the PendSV pending bit, which invokes the PendSV_Handler. If any of those handlers make an call to osYield again... you guessed it... infinite recursion leads to a deadlocked system. What happens if you do: The system hangs instantly because the processor is trapped in an infinite loop of interrupt handlers calling each other. The stack will overflow due to endless recursive function calls and no thread has ever really switched because context switch never completes. Why is this bad: Context-switching functions need to be atomic functions - meaning it finishes its work and returns control to a thread. Context-switching functions are the mechanism that implements yielding (they don\'t consume yielding). They consume, not yield. The function that is called causes osYield to be called, which causes osYield to be called by PendSV or SVC which violates atomically switching threads - that all needs to be blurred together. Now the system is unusable because the user threads never get CPU time - the processor has spent all its time in interrupt handlers\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,2,'2025-07-21_14-45-18','We do not call osYield from within context-switching functions because it leads to infinite recursion and a deadlocked system. This creates nested interrupts, which the Cortex-M4 cannot handle safely when SVC (synchronous exception) is called from within an asynchronous interrupt handler. When osYield is invoked, it makes SVC #4 system call, which calls SVC_Handler_Main, which sets the PendSV pending bit, which invokes the PendSV_Handler. If any of those handlers make a call to osYield again, infinite recursion leads to a deadlocked system. What happens if you do: The system hangs instantly because the processor is trapped in an infinite loop of interrupt handlers calling each other. The stack will overflow due to endless recursive function calls as the Cortex-M4 attempts to stack additional exception frames on top of existing interrupt frames, but cannot properly manage multiple exception stack contexts, leading to stack corruption and HardFault. No thread switching occurs because the context switch never completes. Why is this bad: Context-switching functions need to be atomic functions that complete their work and return control to threads. They are the mechanism that implements yielding, not consumers of it. Calling osYield from within these handlers violates the fundamental principle of atomic thread switching because the NVIC cannot safely transition from our low-priority PendSV (0xFE) to higher-priority SVC (0xFD) since the processor\'s exception return mechanism becomes confused about which stack frames belong to which exception level. The system becomes unusable because the processor spends all its time trying to handle nested exceptions that result in HardFault crashes, never returning control to user threads.',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,2,'2025-07-21_14-48-03','We do not call osYield from within context-switching functions because it leads to infinite recursion and a deadlocked system. This creates nested interrupts, which the Cortex-M4 cannot handle safely when SVC (synchronous exception) is called from within an asynchronous interrupt handler. When osYield is invoked, it makes SVC #4 system call, which calls SVC_Handler_Main, which sets the PendSV pending bit, which invokes the PendSV_Handler. If any of those handlers make a call to osYield again, infinite recursion leads to a deadlocked system. What happens if you do: The system hangs instantly because the processor is trapped in an infinite loop of interrupt handlers calling each other. The stack will overflow due to endless recursive function calls as the Cortex-M4 attempts to stack additional exception frames on top of existing interrupt frames, but cannot properly manage multiple exception stack contexts, leading to stack corruption and HardFault. No thread switching occurs because the context switch never completes. Why is this bad: Context-switching functions need to be atomic functions that complete their work and return control to threads. They are the mechanism that implements yielding, not consumers of it. Calling osYield from within these handlers violates the fundamental principle of atomic thread switching because the NVIC cannot safely transition from our low-priority PendSV (0xFE) to higher-priority SVC (0xFD) since the processor\'s exception return mechanism becomes confused about which stack frames belong to which exception level. The system becomes unusable because the processor spends all its time trying to handle nested exceptions that result in HardFault crashes, never returning control to user threads.',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,2,'2025-07-21_15-31-26','We do not call osYield from within context-switching functions because it leads to infinite recursion and a deadlocked system. This creates nested interrupts, which the Cortex-M4 cannot handle safely when SVC (synchronous exception) is called from within an asynchronous interrupt handler. When osYield is invoked, it makes SVC #4 system call, which calls SVC_Handler_Main, which sets the PendSV pending bit, which invokes the PendSV_Handler. If any of those handlers make a call to osYield again, infinite recursion leads to a deadlocked system. What happens if you do: The system hangs instantly because the processor is trapped in an infinite loop of interrupt handlers calling each other. The stack will overflow due to endless recursive function calls as the Cortex-M4 attempts to stack additional exception frames on top of existing interrupt frames, but cannot properly manage multiple exception stack contexts, leading to stack corruption and HardFault. No thread switching occurs because the context switch never completes. Why is this bad: Context-switching functions need to be atomic functions that complete their work and return control to threads. They are the mechanism that implements yielding, not consumers of it. Calling osYield from within these handlers violates the fundamental principle of atomic thread switching because the NVIC cannot safely transition from our low-priority PendSV (0xFE) to higher-priority SVC (0xFD) since the processor\'s exception return mechanism becomes confused about which stack frames belong to which exception level. The system becomes unusable because the processor spends all its time trying to handle nested exceptions that result in HardFault crashes, never returning control to user threads.',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,2,'2025-07-21_23-06-43','We do not call osYield from within context-switching functions because it leads to infinite recursion and a deadlocked system. This creates nested interrupts, which the Cortex-M4 cannot handle safely when SVC (synchronous exception) is called from within an asynchronous interrupt handler. When osYield is invoked, it makes SVC #4 system call, which calls SVC_Handler_Main, which sets the PendSV pending bit, which invokes the PendSV_Handler. If any of those handlers make a call to osYield again, infinite recursion leads to a deadlocked system. What happens if you do: The system hangs instantly because the processor is trapped in an infinite loop of interrupt handlers calling each other. The stack will overflow due to endless recursive function calls as the Cortex-M4 attempts to stack additional exception frames on top of existing interrupt frames, but cannot properly manage multiple exception stack contexts, leading to stack corruption and HardFault. No thread switching occurs because the context switch never completes. Why is this bad: Context-switching functions need to be atomic functions that complete their work and return control to threads. They are the mechanism that implements yielding, not consumers of it. Calling osYield from within these handlers violates the fundamental principle of atomic thread switching because the NVIC cannot safely transition from our low-priority PendSV (0xFE) to higher-priority SVC (0xFD) since the processor\'s exception return mechanism becomes confused about which stack frames belong to which exception level. The system becomes unusable because the processor spends all its time trying to handle nested exceptions that result in HardFault crashes, never returning control to user threads.',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,2,'2025-07-21_23-26-39','We do not call osYield from within context-switching functions because it leads to infinite recursion and a deadlocked system. This creates nested interrupts, which the Cortex-M4 cannot handle safely when SVC (synchronous exception) is called from within an asynchronous interrupt handler. When osYield is invoked, it makes SVC #4 system call, which calls SVC_Handler_Main, which sets the PendSV pending bit, which invokes the PendSV_Handler. If any of those handlers make a call to osYield again, infinite recursion leads to a deadlocked system. What happens if you do: The system hangs instantly because the processor is trapped in an infinite loop of interrupt handlers calling each other. The stack will overflow due to endless recursive function calls as the Cortex-M4 attempts to stack additional exception frames on top of existing interrupt frames, but cannot properly manage multiple exception stack contexts, leading to stack corruption and HardFault. No thread switching occurs because the context switch never completes. Why is this bad: Context-switching functions need to be atomic functions that complete their work and return control to threads. They are the mechanism that implements yielding, not consumers of it. Calling osYield from within these handlers violates the fundamental principle of atomic thread switching because the NVIC cannot safely transition from our low-priority PendSV (0xFE) to higher-priority SVC (0xFD) since the processor\'s exception return mechanism becomes confused about which stack frames belong to which exception level. The system becomes unusable because the processor spends all its time trying to handle nested exceptions that result in HardFault crashes, never returning control to user threads.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,3,'2025-07-20_16-04-10','Benefits:\n- You have greater timing precision - threads can be given timeslices as small as 0.1ms instead of 1ms, giving you finer granularity on CPU allocation.\n- You have a more responsive system - high-priority tasks get CPU time mostly quicker because the maximum amount of time before the system takes that CPU time away drops from 1ms to 0.1ms\n- You have better real-time performance - since time-sensitive operations have tighter timing guarantees.\nDownsides:\n- You create more CPU overhead - when SysTick interrupts occur 10x more frequently, that is 10x more CPU cycles spent handling interrupts.\n- You create more power consumption - the processor wakes up more often to service timer interrupts\n- You create significant context switching overhead - When threads require more than the period (0.1ms) to do something useful, they have to spend more time switching context than doing any work\n- You introduced timer resolution that exceeds hardware capabilities - not all ARM processors can issue reliable 0.1ms interrupts.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,3,'2025-07-21_14-45-18','Benefits:\n- You have greater timing precision - threads can be given timeslices as small as 0.1ms instead of 1ms, giving you finer granularity on CPU allocation.\n- You have a more responsive system - high-priority tasks get CPU time mostly quicker because the maximum amount of time before the system takes that CPU time away drops from 1ms to 0.1ms\n- You have better real-time performance - since time-sensitive operations have tighter timing guarantees.\nDownsides:\n- You create more CPU overhead - when SysTick interrupts occur 10x more frequently, that is 10x more CPU cycles spent handling interrupts.\n- You create more power consumption - the processor wakes up more often to service timer interrupts\n- You create significant context switching overhead - When threads require more than the period (0.1ms) to do something useful, they have to spend more time switching context than doing any work\n- You introduced timer resolution that exceeds hardware capabilities - not all ARM processors can issue reliable 0.1ms interrupts.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,3,'2025-07-21_14-48-03','Benefits:\n- You have greater timing precision - threads can be given timeslices as small as 0.1ms instead of 1ms, giving you finer granularity on CPU allocation.\n- You have a more responsive system - high-priority tasks get CPU time mostly quicker because the maximum amount of time before the system takes that CPU time away drops from 1ms to 0.1ms\n- You have better real-time performance - since time-sensitive operations have tighter timing guarantees.\nDownsides:\n- You create more CPU overhead - when SysTick interrupts occur 10x more frequently, that is 10x more CPU cycles spent handling interrupts.\n- You create more power consumption - the processor wakes up more often to service timer interrupts\n- You create significant context switching overhead - When threads require more than the period (0.1ms) to do something useful, they have to spend more time switching context than doing any work\n- You introduced timer resolution that exceeds hardware capabilities - not all ARM processors can issue reliable 0.1ms interrupts.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,3,'2025-07-21_15-31-26','Benefits:\n- You have greater timing precision - threads can be given timeslices as small as 0.1ms instead of 1ms, giving you finer granularity on CPU allocation.\n- You have a more responsive system - high-priority tasks get CPU time mostly quicker because the maximum amount of time before the system takes that CPU time away drops from 1ms to 0.1ms\n- You have better real-time performance - since time-sensitive operations have tighter timing guarantees.\nDownsides:\n- You create more CPU overhead - when SysTick interrupts occur 10x more frequently, that is 10x more CPU cycles spent handling interrupts.\n- You create more power consumption - the processor wakes up more often to service timer interrupts\n- You create significant context switching overhead - When threads require more than the period (0.1ms) to do something useful, they have to spend more time switching context than doing any work\n- You introduced timer resolution that exceeds hardware capabilities - not all ARM processors can issue reliable 0.1ms interrupts.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,3,'2025-07-21_23-06-43','Benefits:\n- You have greater timing precision - threads can be given timeslices as small as 0.1ms instead of 1ms, giving you finer granularity on CPU allocation.\n- You have a more responsive system - high-priority tasks get CPU time mostly quicker because the maximum amount of time before the system takes that CPU time away drops from 1ms to 0.1ms\n- You have better real-time performance - since time-sensitive operations have tighter timing guarantees.\nDownsides:\n- You create more CPU overhead - when SysTick interrupts occur 10x more frequently, that is 10x more CPU cycles spent handling interrupts.\n- You create more power consumption - the processor wakes up more often to service timer interrupts\n- You create significant context switching overhead - When threads require more than the period (0.1ms) to do something useful, they have to spend more time switching context than doing any work\n- You introduced timer resolution that exceeds hardware capabilities - not all ARM processors can issue reliable 0.1ms interrupts.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,3,'2025-07-21_23-26-39','Benefits:\n- You have greater timing precision - threads can be given timeslices as small as 0.1ms instead of 1ms, giving you finer granularity on CPU allocation.\n- You have a more responsive system - high-priority tasks get CPU time mostly quicker because the maximum amount of time before the system takes that CPU time away drops from 1ms to 0.1ms\n- You have better real-time performance - since time-sensitive operations have tighter timing guarantees.\nDownsides:\n- You create more CPU overhead - when SysTick interrupts occur 10x more frequently, that is 10x more CPU cycles spent handling interrupts.\n- You create more power consumption - the processor wakes up more often to service timer interrupts\n- You create significant context switching overhead - When threads require more than the period (0.1ms) to do something useful, they have to spend more time switching context than doing any work\n- You introduced timer resolution that exceeds hardware capabilities - not all ARM processors can issue reliable 0.1ms interrupts.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,4,'2025-07-20_16-04-10','User Viewpoint:\nIn order to permit other threads to operate during cooperative multitasking, the user must specifically invoke osYield() within their thread functions. A thread monopolizes the CPU and totally starves other threads if it persists in an endless loop. The user is solely responsible for maintaining system fairness, but they also have complete control over when their thread relinquishes the processor.\nBecause the OS automatically interrupts threads after their timeslice expires and switches to other threads, pre-emptive multitasking allows the user to write thread functions without worrying about yielding. The OS forcibly removes threads from the CPU, so even poorly written threads that never yield cannot starve the system. The user loses fine-grained control over scheduling choices but gains automatic fairness.\nOS Implementation:\nThe OS only needs to use the SVC mechanism to enable voluntary context switching for cooperative multitasking. The OS sets the PendSV pending bit to execute a context switch via PendSV_Handler and osSched() when osYield() initiates SVC #4. The OS does not change threads unless specifically asked to do so.\nThe OS must actively control thread execution time with the SysTick timer in order to perform preemptive multitasking. The OS automatically starts PendSV for an involuntary context switch when the current thread\'s runtime counter hits zero, which is decremented by SysTick_Handler every 1 ms. In order to preserve cooperative and preemptive scheduling at the same time, the OS also resets thread runtime values when threads yield or start. In contrast to cooperative scheduling, this necessitates timer-driven interrupt handling and extra thread metadata (timeslice, runtime).\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,4,'2025-07-21_14-45-18','User Viewpoint:\nIn order to permit other threads to operate during cooperative multitasking, the user must specifically invoke osYield() within their thread functions. A thread monopolizes the CPU and totally starves other threads if it persists in an endless loop. The user is solely responsible for maintaining system fairness, but they also have complete control over when their thread relinquishes the processor.\nBecause the OS automatically interrupts threads after their timeslice expires and switches to other threads, pre-emptive multitasking allows the user to write thread functions without worrying about yielding. The OS forcibly removes threads from the CPU, so even poorly written threads that never yield cannot starve the system. The user loses fine-grained control over scheduling choices but gains automatic fairness.\nOS Implementation:\nThe OS only needs to use the SVC mechanism to enable voluntary context switching for cooperative multitasking. The OS sets the PendSV pending bit to execute a context switch via PendSV_Handler and osSched() when osYield() initiates SVC #4. The OS does not change threads unless specifically asked to do so.\nThe OS must actively control thread execution time with the SysTick timer in order to perform preemptive multitasking. The OS automatically starts PendSV for an involuntary context switch when the current thread\'s runtime counter hits zero, which is decremented by SysTick_Handler every 1 ms. In order to preserve cooperative and preemptive scheduling at the same time, the OS also resets thread runtime values when threads yield or start. In contrast to cooperative scheduling, this necessitates timer-driven interrupt handling and extra thread metadata (timeslice, runtime).\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,4,'2025-07-21_14-48-03','User Viewpoint:\nIn order to permit other threads to operate during cooperative multitasking, the user must specifically invoke osYield() within their thread functions. A thread monopolizes the CPU and totally starves other threads if it persists in an endless loop. The user is solely responsible for maintaining system fairness, but they also have complete control over when their thread relinquishes the processor.\nBecause the OS automatically interrupts threads after their timeslice expires and switches to other threads, pre-emptive multitasking allows the user to write thread functions without worrying about yielding. The OS forcibly removes threads from the CPU, so even poorly written threads that never yield cannot starve the system. The user loses fine-grained control over scheduling choices but gains automatic fairness.\nOS Implementation:\nThe OS only needs to use the SVC mechanism to enable voluntary context switching for cooperative multitasking. The OS sets the PendSV pending bit to execute a context switch via PendSV_Handler and osSched() when osYield() initiates SVC #4. The OS does not change threads unless specifically asked to do so.\nThe OS must actively control thread execution time with the SysTick timer in order to perform preemptive multitasking. The OS automatically starts PendSV for an involuntary context switch when the current thread\'s runtime counter hits zero, which is decremented by SysTick_Handler every 1 ms. In order to preserve cooperative and preemptive scheduling at the same time, the OS also resets thread runtime values when threads yield or start. In contrast to cooperative scheduling, this necessitates timer-driven interrupt handling and extra thread metadata (timeslice, runtime).\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,4,'2025-07-21_15-31-26','User Viewpoint:\nIn order to permit other threads to operate during cooperative multitasking, the user must specifically invoke osYield() within their thread functions. A thread monopolizes the CPU and totally starves other threads if it persists in an endless loop. The user is solely responsible for maintaining system fairness, but they also have complete control over when their thread relinquishes the processor.\nBecause the OS automatically interrupts threads after their timeslice expires and switches to other threads, pre-emptive multitasking allows the user to write thread functions without worrying about yielding. The OS forcibly removes threads from the CPU, so even poorly written threads that never yield cannot starve the system. The user loses fine-grained control over scheduling choices but gains automatic fairness.\nOS Implementation:\nThe OS only needs to use the SVC mechanism to enable voluntary context switching for cooperative multitasking. The OS sets the PendSV pending bit to execute a context switch via PendSV_Handler and osSched() when osYield() initiates SVC #4. The OS does not change threads unless specifically asked to do so.\nThe OS must actively control thread execution time with the SysTick timer in order to perform preemptive multitasking. The OS automatically starts PendSV for an involuntary context switch when the current thread\'s runtime counter hits zero, which is decremented by SysTick_Handler every 1 ms. In order to preserve cooperative and preemptive scheduling at the same time, the OS also resets thread runtime values when threads yield or start. In contrast to cooperative scheduling, this necessitates timer-driven interrupt handling and extra thread metadata (timeslice, runtime).\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,4,'2025-07-21_23-06-43','User Viewpoint:\nIn order to permit other threads to operate during cooperative multitasking, the user must specifically invoke osYield() within their thread functions. A thread monopolizes the CPU and totally starves other threads if it persists in an endless loop. The user is solely responsible for maintaining system fairness, but they also have complete control over when their thread relinquishes the processor.\nBecause the OS automatically interrupts threads after their timeslice expires and switches to other threads, pre-emptive multitasking allows the user to write thread functions without worrying about yielding. The OS forcibly removes threads from the CPU, so even poorly written threads that never yield cannot starve the system. The user loses fine-grained control over scheduling choices but gains automatic fairness.\nOS Implementation:\nThe OS only needs to use the SVC mechanism to enable voluntary context switching for cooperative multitasking. The OS sets the PendSV pending bit to execute a context switch via PendSV_Handler and osSched() when osYield() initiates SVC #4. The OS does not change threads unless specifically asked to do so.\nThe OS must actively control thread execution time with the SysTick timer in order to perform preemptive multitasking. The OS automatically starts PendSV for an involuntary context switch when the current thread\'s runtime counter hits zero, which is decremented by SysTick_Handler every 1 ms. In order to preserve cooperative and preemptive scheduling at the same time, the OS also resets thread runtime values when threads yield or start. In contrast to cooperative scheduling, this necessitates timer-driven interrupt handling and extra thread metadata (timeslice, runtime).\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,4,'2025-07-21_23-26-39','User Viewpoint:\nIn order to permit other threads to operate during cooperative multitasking, the user must specifically invoke osYield() within their thread functions. A thread monopolizes the CPU and totally starves other threads if it persists in an endless loop. The user is solely responsible for maintaining system fairness, but they also have complete control over when their thread relinquishes the processor.\nBecause the OS automatically interrupts threads after their timeslice expires and switches to other threads, pre-emptive multitasking allows the user to write thread functions without worrying about yielding. The OS forcibly removes threads from the CPU, so even poorly written threads that never yield cannot starve the system. The user loses fine-grained control over scheduling choices but gains automatic fairness.\nOS Implementation:\nThe OS only needs to use the SVC mechanism to enable voluntary context switching for cooperative multitasking. The OS sets the PendSV pending bit to execute a context switch via PendSV_Handler and osSched() when osYield() initiates SVC #4. The OS does not change threads unless specifically asked to do so.\nThe OS must actively control thread execution time with the SysTick timer in order to perform preemptive multitasking. The OS automatically starts PendSV for an involuntary context switch when the current thread\'s runtime counter hits zero, which is decremented by SysTick_Handler every 1 ms. In order to preserve cooperative and preemptive scheduling at the same time, the OS also resets thread runtime values when threads yield or start. In contrast to cooperative scheduling, this necessitates timer-driven interrupt handling and extra thread metadata (timeslice, runtime).\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,5,'2025-07-20_16-04-10','The two mechanisms work together because they utilize the same underlying context switching infrastructure but trigger via different pathways. Both cooperative yielding and pre-emptive switching eventually result in the same PendSV_Handler and osSched() functions that perform the actual thread switch - the only difference is the reason the switch is triggered. Calling osYield() for a thread sets the SVC #4 which sets the PendSV pending bit, and resets the threads runtime to its maximum timeslice value, which is how threads cooperate to yield the remaining time to the other thread or threads. When the SysTick timer expires a thread\'s timeslice the SysTick timer calls the same function that sets the PendSV pending bit along with resetting the runtime, but this is being done involuntarily by the thread that was pre-empted. The important take away here is both mechanisms reset a thread\'s runtime to its timeslice value regardless of whether it was voluntary or involuntary. Thus, a thread that yields before its run is finished gets to start with a fresh timeslice on the next time it runs. Similarly when a thread gets pre-empted it also starts with a full timeslice will be ready when it runs the next time. The scheduler does not care how a thread lost the CPU and treats all threads equally.\nThis hybrid approach provides the best of both worlds: well-behaved threads can yield immediately when they finish their work (improving system responsiveness), while misbehaving threads that never yield still get forcibly switched after their timeslice expires (maintaining system fairness). The OS doesn\'t need separate scheduling queues or different context switching mechanisms - it simply uses different triggers for the same underlying thread switching process.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,5,'2025-07-21_14-45-18','The two mechanisms work together because they utilize the same underlying context switching infrastructure but trigger via different pathways. Both cooperative yielding and pre-emptive switching eventually result in the same PendSV_Handler and osSched() functions that perform the actual thread switch - the only difference is the reason the switch is triggered. Calling osYield() for a thread sets the SVC #4 which sets the PendSV pending bit, and resets the threads runtime to its maximum timeslice value, which is how threads cooperate to yield the remaining time to the other thread or threads. When the SysTick timer expires a thread\'s timeslice the SysTick timer calls the same function that sets the PendSV pending bit along with resetting the runtime, but this is being done involuntarily by the thread that was pre-empted. The important take away here is both mechanisms reset a thread\'s runtime to its timeslice value regardless of whether it was voluntary or involuntary. Thus, a thread that yields before its run is finished gets to start with a fresh timeslice on the next time it runs. Similarly when a thread gets pre-empted it also starts with a full timeslice will be ready when it runs the next time. The scheduler does not care how a thread lost the CPU and treats all threads equally.\nThis hybrid approach provides the best of both worlds: well-behaved threads can yield immediately when they finish their work (improving system responsiveness), while misbehaving threads that never yield still get forcibly switched after their timeslice expires (maintaining system fairness). The OS doesn\'t need separate scheduling queues or different context switching mechanisms - it simply uses different triggers for the same underlying thread switching process.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,5,'2025-07-21_14-48-03','The two mechanisms work together because they utilize the same underlying context switching infrastructure but trigger via different pathways. Both cooperative yielding and pre-emptive switching eventually result in the same PendSV_Handler and osSched() functions that perform the actual thread switch - the only difference is the reason the switch is triggered. Calling osYield() for a thread sets the SVC #4 which sets the PendSV pending bit, and resets the threads runtime to its maximum timeslice value, which is how threads cooperate to yield the remaining time to the other thread or threads. When the SysTick timer expires a thread\'s timeslice the SysTick timer calls the same function that sets the PendSV pending bit along with resetting the runtime, but this is being done involuntarily by the thread that was pre-empted. The important take away here is both mechanisms reset a thread\'s runtime to its timeslice value regardless of whether it was voluntary or involuntary. Thus, a thread that yields before its run is finished gets to start with a fresh timeslice on the next time it runs. Similarly when a thread gets pre-empted it also starts with a full timeslice will be ready when it runs the next time. The scheduler does not care how a thread lost the CPU and treats all threads equally.\nThis hybrid approach provides the best of both worlds: well-behaved threads can yield immediately when they finish their work (improving system responsiveness), while misbehaving threads that never yield still get forcibly switched after their timeslice expires (maintaining system fairness). The OS doesn\'t need separate scheduling queues or different context switching mechanisms - it simply uses different triggers for the same underlying thread switching process.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,5,'2025-07-21_15-31-26','The two mechanisms work together because they utilize the same underlying context switching infrastructure but trigger via different pathways. Both cooperative yielding and pre-emptive switching eventually result in the same PendSV_Handler and osSched() functions that perform the actual thread switch - the only difference is the reason the switch is triggered. Calling osYield() for a thread sets the SVC #4 which sets the PendSV pending bit, and resets the threads runtime to its maximum timeslice value, which is how threads cooperate to yield the remaining time to the other thread or threads. When the SysTick timer expires a thread\'s timeslice the SysTick timer calls the same function that sets the PendSV pending bit along with resetting the runtime, but this is being done involuntarily by the thread that was pre-empted. The important take away here is both mechanisms reset a thread\'s runtime to its timeslice value regardless of whether it was voluntary or involuntary. Thus, a thread that yields before its run is finished gets to start with a fresh timeslice on the next time it runs. Similarly when a thread gets pre-empted it also starts with a full timeslice will be ready when it runs the next time. The scheduler does not care how a thread lost the CPU and treats all threads equally.\nThis hybrid approach provides the best of both worlds: well-behaved threads can yield immediately when they finish their work (improving system responsiveness), while misbehaving threads that never yield still get forcibly switched after their timeslice expires (maintaining system fairness). The OS doesn\'t need separate scheduling queues or different context switching mechanisms - it simply uses different triggers for the same underlying thread switching process.\n',0,'s25'),('r36dong',5,5,'2025-07-21_23-06-43','The two mechanisms work together because they utilize the same underlying context switching infrastructure but trigger via different pathways. Both cooperative yielding and pre-emptive switching eventually result in the same PendSV_Handler and osSched() functions that perform the actual thread switch - the only difference is the reason the switch is triggered. Calling osYield() for a thread sets the SVC #4 which sets the PendSV pending bit, and resets the threads runtime to its maximum timeslice value, which is how threads cooperate to yield the remaining time to the other thread or threads. When the SysTick timer expires a thread\'s timeslice the SysTick timer calls the same function that sets the PendSV pending bit along with resetting the runtime, but this is being done involuntarily by the thread that was pre-empted. The important take away here is both mechanisms reset a thread\'s runtime to its timeslice value regardless of whether it was voluntary or involuntary. Thus, a thread that yields before its run is finished gets to start with a fresh timeslice on the next time it runs. Similarly when a thread gets pre-empted it also starts with a full timeslice will be ready when it runs the next time. The scheduler does not care how a thread lost the CPU and treats all threads equally.\nThis hybrid approach provides the best of both worlds: well-behaved threads can yield immediately when they finish their work (improving system responsiveness), while misbehaving threads that never yield still get forcibly switched after their timeslice expires (maintaining system fairness). The OS doesn\'t need separate scheduling queues or different context switching mechanisms - it simply uses different triggers for the same underlying thread switching process.\n',1,'s25'),('r36dong',5,5,'2025-07-21_23-26-39','The two mechanisms work together because they utilize the same underlying context switching infrastructure but trigger via different pathways. Both cooperative yielding and pre-emptive switching eventually result in the same PendSV_Handler and osSched() functions that perform the actual thread switch - the only difference is the reason the switch is triggered. Calling osYield() for a thread sets the SVC #4 which sets the PendSV pending bit, and resets the threads runtime to its maximum timeslice value, which is how threads cooperate to yield the remaining time to the other thread or threads. When the SysTick timer expires a thread\'s timeslice the SysTick timer calls the same function that sets the PendSV pending bit along with resetting the runtime, but this is being done involuntarily by the thread that was pre-empted. The important take away here is both mechanisms reset a thread\'s runtime to its timeslice value regardless of whether it was voluntary or involuntary. Thus, a thread that yields before its run is finished gets to start with a fresh timeslice on the next time it runs. Similarly when a thread gets pre-empted it also starts with a full timeslice will be ready when it runs the next time. The scheduler does not care how a thread lost the CPU and treats all threads equally.\nThis hybrid approach provides the best of both worlds: well-behaved threads can yield immediately when they finish their work (improving system responsiveness), while misbehaving threads that never yield still get forcibly switched after their timeslice expires (maintaining system fairness). The OS doesn\'t need separate scheduling queues or different context switching mechanisms - it simply uses different triggers for the same underlying thread switching process.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,1,'2025-05-16_09-32-02','The hardware abstraction layer (HAL) is a software component used to interface with hardware devices on a ‘general’ level. It is often used in general embedded projects as a simple way to interact with hardware through programming interfaces.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,1,'2025-06-24_13-38-04','The hardware abstraction layer (HAL) is a software component used to interface with hardware devices on a ‘general’ level. It is often used in general embedded projects as a simple way to interact with hardware through programming interfaces.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,1,'2025-06-24_16-31-04','The hardware abstraction layer (HAL) is a software component used to interface with hardware devices on a ‘general’ level. It is often used in general embedded projects as a simple way to interact with hardware through programming interfaces.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',1,1,'2025-06-25_13-49-14','The hardware abstraction layer (HAL) is a software component used to interface with hardware devices on a ‘general’ level. It is often used in general embedded projects as a simple way to interact with hardware through programming interfaces.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',1,1,'2025-06-26_15-19-46','The hardware abstraction layer (HAL) is a software component used to interface with hardware devices on a ‘general’ level. It is often used in general embedded projects as a simple way to interact with hardware through programming interfaces. Since it abstracts the complexities of various hardware dependencies, software can easily be reused and ported to other hardware platforms without many changes. This reduces debugging and spending time on low-level programming, which allows embedded developers to focus on coding higher-level applications.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,2,'2025-05-16_09-32-02','The __io_putchar function writes a character to an IO channel, which is UART2 for this lab. The putchar function acts as a necessary wrapper function for printf to output each character. Since the io_putchar is not always predefined, sometimes an implementation of the function must be written to link the output function (printf) with the actual hardware, which can vary in terms of capabilities.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,2,'2025-06-24_13-38-04','The __io_putchar function writes a character to an IO channel, which is UART2 for this lab. The putchar function acts as a necessary wrapper function for printf to output each character. Since the io_putchar is not always predefined, sometimes an implementation of the function must be written to link the output function (printf) with the actual hardware, which can vary in terms of capabilities.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,2,'2025-06-24_16-31-04','The __io_putchar function writes a character to an IO channel, which is UART2 for this lab. The putchar function acts as a necessary wrapper function for printf to output each character. Since the io_putchar is not always predefined, sometimes an implementation of the function must be written to link the output function (printf) with the actual hardware, which can vary in terms of capabilities.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,2,'2025-06-25_13-49-14','The __io_putchar function writes a character to an IO channel, which is UART2 for this lab. The putchar function acts as a necessary wrapper function for printf to output each character. Since the io_putchar is not always predefined, sometimes an implementation of the function must be written to link the output function (printf) with the actual hardware, which can vary in terms of capabilities.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,2,'2025-06-26_15-19-46','The __io_putchar function writes a character to an IO channel, which is UART2 for this lab. The putchar function acts as a necessary wrapper function for printf to output each character. Since the io_putchar is not always predefined, sometimes an implementation of the function must be written to link the output function (printf) with the actual hardware, which can vary in terms of capabilities.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,3,'2025-05-16_09-32-02','Using the debugger means that the register values of psp, msp, and sp—which can’t be printed to terminal or console—can be simultaneously tracked for each line of code, which helps resolve logic or runtime errors.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,3,'2025-06-24_13-38-04','Using the debugger means that the register values of psp, msp, and sp—which can’t be printed to terminal or console—can be simultaneously tracked for each line of code, which helps resolve logic or runtime errors.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,3,'2025-06-24_16-31-04','Using the debugger means that the register values of psp, msp, and sp—which can’t be printed to terminal or console—can be simultaneously tracked for each line of code, which helps resolve logic or runtime errors.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',1,3,'2025-06-25_13-49-14','Using the debugger means that the register values of psp, msp, and sp—which can’t be printed to terminal or console—can be simultaneously tracked for each line of code, which helps resolve logic or runtime errors.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,3,'2025-06-26_15-19-46','Using the debugger means that the register values of psp, msp, and sp—which can’t be printed to terminal or console—can be simultaneously tracked for each line of code, which helps resolve logic or runtime errors. Furthermore, printf has no direct access to CPU registers since it operates in user space, and it is limited to data stored by variables in the RAM or the CPU. Also, it does not show real-time changes (e.g. register values might change before printing) whereas all of these problems are avoided by viewing the register values through the debugger.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,4,'2025-05-16_09-32-02','Based on the equations given in the lab manual, the initial starting position of MSP can be calculated using MSP_init_val. At startup, the MSP is set to a predefined initial value that is defined in a system startup file or specified by the hardware. MSP_init_val points to the top of the main stack region in memory. Next, the size of the stack required for the new thread/process needs to be chosen, and the stack size must be aligned according to the system’s requirements (4-byte or 8-byte). Since the stack is growing downwards, the starting address of MSP is found by subtracting the maximum stack size and subtracting the previous thread’s stack size from MSP_init_val, if there was a previous thread. PSP_val is found using the same principle, where our main stack (MSP) size is 0x400 bytes.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',1,4,'2025-06-24_13-38-04','Based on the equations given in the lab manual, the initial starting position of MSP can be calculated using MSP_init_val. At startup, the MSP is set to a predefined initial value that is defined in a system startup file or specified by the hardware. MSP_init_val points to the top of the main stack region in memory; without it, we would not know where the stack begins. Next, the size of the stack required for the new thread/process needs to be chosen, and the stack size must be aligned according to the system’s requirements (4-byte or 8-byte). Since the stack is growing downwards, the starting address of MSP is found by subtracting the maximum stack size and subtracting the previous thread’s stack size from MSP_init_val, if there was a previous thread. PSP_val is found using the same principle, where our main stack (MSP) size is 0x400 bytes, or as written in th program, “uint32_t PSP_VAL = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL  - 0x400.”\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',1,4,'2025-06-24_16-31-04','Based on the equations given in the lab manual, the initial starting position of MSP can be calculated using MSP_init_val. At startup, the MSP is set to a predefined initial value that is defined in a system startup file or specified by the hardware. MSP_init_val points to the known address of the top of the stack in memory. If MSP is not known, then the offset could point anywhere and could cause PSP to be outside of the memory; this could result in stack corruption and hard faults.\nNext, the size of the stack required for the new thread/process needs to be chosen, and the stack size must be aligned according to the system’s requirements (4-byte or 8-byte). Since the stack is growing downwards, the starting address of MSP is found by subtracting the maximum stack size and subtracting the previous thread’s stack size from MSP_init_val, if there was a previous thread. PSP_val is found using the same principle, where our main stack (MSP) size is 0x400 bytes, or as written in the program, “uint32_t PSP_VAL = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL  - 0x400.”\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,4,'2025-06-25_13-49-14','Based on the equations given in the lab manual, the initial starting position of MSP can be calculated using MSP_init_val. At startup, the MSP is set to a predefined initial value that is defined in a system startup file or specified by the hardware. MSP_init_val points to the known address of the top of the stack in memory. If MSP is not known, then the offset could point anywhere and could cause PSP to be outside of the memory; this could result in stack corruption and hard faults.\nNext, the size of the stack required for the new thread/process needs to be chosen, and the stack size must be aligned according to the system’s requirements (4-byte or 8-byte). Since the stack is growing downwards, the starting address of MSP is found by subtracting the maximum stack size and subtracting the previous thread’s stack size from MSP_init_val, if there was a previous thread. PSP_val is found using the same principle, where our main stack (MSP) size is 0x400 bytes, or as written in the program, “uint32_t PSP_VAL = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL  - 0x400.”\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,4,'2025-06-26_15-19-46','Based on the equations given in the lab manual, the initial starting position of MSP can be calculated using MSP_init_val. At startup, the MSP is set to a predefined initial value that is defined in a system startup file or specified by the hardware. MSP_init_val points to the known address of the top of the stack in memory. If MSP is not known, then the offset could point anywhere and could cause PSP to be outside of the memory; this could result in stack corruption and hard faults.\nNext, the size of the stack required for the new thread/process needs to be chosen, and the stack size must be aligned according to the system’s requirements (4-byte or 8-byte). Since the stack is growing downwards, the starting address of MSP is found by subtracting the maximum stack size and subtracting the previous thread’s stack size from MSP_init_val, if there was a previous thread. PSP_val is found using the same principle, where our main stack (MSP) size is 0x400 bytes, or as written in the program, “uint32_t PSP_VAL = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL  - 0x400.”\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,5,'2025-05-16_09-32-02','The main stack will still have the highest address. For each thread stack, keep subtracting the size of the stack using the same method until all stacks are done. Since each thread has separate PSP and MSP, setControl can be modified to switch between the two.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',1,5,'2025-06-24_13-38-04','The main stack will still have the highest address. For each thread stack, keep subtracting the size of the stack using the same method until all stacks are done. Since each thread has separate PSP and MSP, setControl can be modified to switch between the two.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',1,5,'2025-06-24_16-31-04','The main stack will still have the highest address. For each thread stack, keep subtracting the size of the stack using the same method until all stacks are done. Since each thread has separate PSP and MSP, setControl can be modified to switch between the two. While doing so, all of the stack pointers must be 8-bit aligned and the stack address must be within a valid RAM address without overlapping other sections, such as peripherals or other system stacks. It would also be preferable to have enough space for ‘guard’ space, which is used to detect overflow, in addition to the total amount of memory required for the stacks.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',1,5,'2025-06-25_13-49-14','The main stack will still have the highest address. For each thread stack, keep subtracting the size of the stack using the same method until all stacks are done. Since each thread has separate PSP and MSP, setControl can be modified to switch between the two. While doing so, all of the stack pointers must be 8-bit aligned and the stack address must be within a valid RAM address without overlapping other sections, such as peripherals or other system stacks. It would also be preferable to have enough space for ‘guard’ space, which is used to detect overflow, in addition to the total amount of memory required for the stacks.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',1,5,'2025-06-26_15-19-46','The main stack will still have the highest address. For each thread stack, keep subtracting the size of the stack using the same method until all stacks are done. Since each thread has separate PSP and MSP, setControl can be modified to switch between the two. While doing so, all of the stack pointers must be 8-bit aligned and the stack address must be within a valid RAM address without overlapping other sections, such as peripherals or other system stacks. It would also be preferable to have enough space for ‘guard’ space, which is used to detect overflow, in addition to the total amount of memory required for the stacks.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,1,'2025-06-05_15-29-23','In order to switch to the print continuously function, a wrapper function to call the provided Assembly code had to be created. The jumpAssembly wrapper function takes the address of the target function (the ‘print_continuously’ function in this case) to move the value held in R0 to the PC. Then, the computer stores the location of the print continuously function before it begins printing infinitely to the console.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,1,'2025-06-24_13-11-23','In order to switch to the print continuously function, a wrapper function to call the provided Assembly code had to be created. The jumpAssembly wrapper function takes the address of the target function (the ‘print_continuously’ function in this case) to move the value held in R0 to the PC. Then, the computer stores the location of the print continuously function before it begins printing infinitely to the console. Changing the PC allows the CPU to jump to another part of the program and to fetch and execute the instruction at the new address.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,1,'2025-06-24_13-42-43','In order to switch to the print continuously function, a wrapper function to call the provided Assembly code had to be created. The jumpAssembly wrapper function takes the address of the target function (the ‘print_continuously’ function in this case) to move the value held in R0 to the PC. Then, the computer stores the address of the print continuously function to stack before it begins printing infinitely to the console. Changing the PC allows the CPU to jump to another part of the program and to fetch and execute the instruction at the new address, so changing PC to point to the print_continuously function essentially instructs the CPU to run the function.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,1,'2025-06-25_14-25-48','In order to switch to the print continuously function, a wrapper function to call the provided Assembly code had to be created. The jumpAssembly wrapper function takes the address of the target function (the ‘print_continuously’ function in this case) to move the value held in R0 to the PC. Then, the computer stores the address of the print continuously function to stack before it begins printing infinitely to the console. Changing the PC allows the CPU to jump to another part of the program and to fetch and execute the instruction at the new address, so changing PC to point to the print_continuously function essentially instructs the CPU to run the function.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,1,'2025-06-26_15-28-32','In order to switch to the print continuously function, a wrapper function to call the provided Assembly code had to be created. The jumpAssembly wrapper function takes the address of the target function (the ‘print_continuously’ function in this case) to move the value held in R0 to the PC. Then, the computer stores the address of the print continuously function to stack before it begins printing infinitely to the console. Changing the PC allows the CPU to jump to another part of the program and to fetch and execute the instruction at the new address, so changing PC to point to the print_continuously function essentially instructs the CPU to run the function.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,1,'2025-06-26_18-47-14','In order to switch to the print continuously function, a wrapper function to call the provided Assembly code had to be created. The jumpAssembly wrapper function takes the address of the target function (the ‘print_continuously’ function in this case) to move the value held in R0 to the PC. Then, the computer stores the address of the print continuously function to stack before it begins printing infinitely to the console. Changing the PC allows the CPU to jump to another part of the program and to fetch and execute the instruction at the new address, so changing PC to point to the print_continuously function essentially instructs the CPU to run the function.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,2,'2025-06-05_15-29-23','A system call is a request made by a process to an operating system’s kernel, thus permitting user-level processes to access the services of the operating system. This is used by all system programs that require resources. Another possible reason for needing to use a system call is having to perform operations that need authorization.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,2,'2025-06-24_13-11-23','A system call is a request made by a process to an operating system’s kernel, thus permitting user-level processes to access the services of the operating system (e.g. read/write commands). This is used by all system programs that require resources, which protects against unauthorized access by user programs that could damage hardware or other system components.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,2,'2025-06-24_13-42-43','A system call is a request made by a process to an operating system’s kernel, thus permitting user-level processes to access the services of the operating system (e.g. read/write commands). This is used by all system programs that require resources, which protects against unauthorized access by user programs that could damage hardware or other system components.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,2,'2025-06-25_14-25-48','A system call is a request made by a process to an operating system’s kernel, thus permitting user-level processes to access the services of the operating system (e.g. read/write commands). This is used by all system programs that require resources, which protects against unauthorized access by user programs that could damage hardware or other system components.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,2,'2025-06-26_15-28-32','A system call, or a function call, is a request made by a process to an operating system’s kernel for a service that the application can’t directly perform, which typically involves a mode switch in order to access the services of the operating system (e.g. read/write commands). It is used by all system programs that require resources, which protects against unauthorized access by user programs that could damage hardware or other system components. This is different from a regular call, which typically runs without having to change a mode and is performed by the application itself. A function call is initiated similarly to a regular function call, but as stated above, it requires a mode switch.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,2,'2025-06-26_18-47-14','A system call, or a function call, is a request made by a process to an operating system’s kernel for a service that the application can’t directly perform, which typically involves a mode switch in order to access the services of the operating system (e.g. read/write commands). It is used by all system programs that require resources, which protects against unauthorized access by user programs that could damage hardware or other system components. This is different from a regular call, which typically runs without having to change a mode and is performed by the application itself. A function call is initiated similarly to a regular function call, but as stated above, it requires a mode switch.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,3,'2025-06-05_15-29-23','This thread’s stack was set up by first creating a new pointer to point at PSP, then immediately setting the values of xPSR and PC. Following this, the remaining registers in the stack were recorded as 0xA; the stack itself was traversed by decrementing the index so that the stack can be popped immediately after the interrupt is completed. Storing these registers allows the thread to have all the necessary data to execute when loaded, facilitating context switching between threads without losing the execution state of any thread.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,3,'2025-06-24_13-11-23','Firstly, space for this stack was allocated in memory by the operating system or the program. Then, this thread’s stack was set up by creating a new pointer to point at PSP, then immediately setting the values of xPSR and PC. Following this, the remaining registers in the stack were recorded as 0xA; the stack itself was traversed by decrementing the index so that the stack can be popped immediately after the interrupt is completed. A thread’s stack grows downwards, which is why the index must be decremented. Storing these registers allows the thread to have all the necessary data to execute when loaded, facilitating context switching between threads without losing the execution state of any thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,3,'2025-06-24_13-42-43','Firstly, space for this stack was allocated in memory by the operating system or the program. Then, this thread’s stack was set up by creating a new pointer to point at PSP, then immediately setting the values of xPSR and PC. Following this, the remaining registers in the stack were recorded as 0xA; the stack itself was traversed by decrementing the index so that the stack can be popped immediately after the interrupt is completed. A thread’s stack grows downwards, which is why the index must be decremented. Storing these registers allows the thread to have all the necessary data to execute when loaded, facilitating context switching between threads without losing the execution state of any thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,3,'2025-06-25_14-25-48','Firstly, space for this stack was allocated in memory by the operating system or the program. Then, this thread’s stack was set up by creating a new pointer to point at PSP, then immediately setting the values of xPSR and PC. Following this, the remaining registers in the stack were recorded as 0xA; the stack itself was traversed by decrementing the index so that the stack can be popped immediately after the interrupt is completed. A thread’s stack grows downwards, which is why the index must be decremented. Storing these registers allows the thread to have all the necessary data to execute when loaded, facilitating context switching between threads without losing the execution state of any thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,3,'2025-06-26_15-28-32','Firstly, space for this stack was allocated in memory by the operating system or the program. Then, this thread’s stack was set up by creating a new pointer to point at PSP, then immediately setting the values of xPSR and PC. Following this, the remaining registers in the stack were recorded as 0xA; the stack itself was traversed by decrementing the index so that the stack can be popped immediately after the interrupt is completed. A thread’s stack grows downwards, which is why the index must be decremented. Storing these registers allows the thread to have all the necessary data to execute when loaded, facilitating context switching between threads without losing the execution state of any thread.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,3,'2025-06-26_18-47-14','Firstly, space for this stack was allocated in memory by the operating system or the program. Then, this thread’s stack was set up by creating a new pointer to point at PSP, then immediately setting the values of xPSR and PC. Following this, the remaining registers in the stack were recorded as 0xA; the stack itself was traversed by decrementing the index so that the stack can be popped immediately after the interrupt is completed. A thread’s stack grows downwards, which is why the index must be decremented. When a thread is interrupted or switched out, the processor must save its current state (register values) to memory (usually the thread’s stack). Later, when the thread is resumed, the processor restores these registers from memory. If the order of storing is not preserved, the wrong values will be restored into the wrong registers, which leads to unpredictable behavior, corruption, or crashes. Storing these registers in proper order allows the thread to have all the necessary data to execute when loaded, facilitating context switching between threads without losing the execution state of any thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,4,'2025-06-05_15-29-23','Setting the PSP using __set_PSP in the SVC_Handler_Main function ensures that each thread has a correctly configured stack pointer. Without this, the system would attempt to run threads with incorrect stack configurations, leading to stack corruption, invalid memory access, and ultimately, a hard fault. When switching between threads, the system must save the current thread\'s context (including the stack pointer) and load the context of the next thread to run so that each thread can resume execution as if it had never been interrupted. Managing the PSP is essential for the stability, safety, and reliability of a multitasking system.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,4,'2025-06-24_13-11-23','Setting the PSP using __set_PSP in the SVC_Handler_Main function ensures that each thread has a correctly configured stack pointer. Without this, the system would attempt to run threads with incorrect stack configurations, leading to stack corruption, invalid memory access, and ultimately, a hard fault. When switching between threads, the system must save the current thread\'s context (including the stack pointer) and load the context of the next thread to run so that each thread can resume execution as if it had never been interrupted. Managing the PSP is essential for the stability, safety, and reliability of a multitasking system.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,4,'2025-06-24_13-42-43','Setting the PSP using __set_PSP in the SVC_Handler_Main function ensures that each thread has a correctly configured stack pointer. Without this, the system would attempt to run threads with incorrect stack configurations, leading to stack corruption, invalid memory access, and ultimately, a hard fault. When switching between threads, the system must save the current thread\'s context (including the stack pointer) and load the context of the next thread to run so that each thread can resume execution as if it had never been interrupted. Managing the PSP is essential for the stability, safety, and reliability of a multitasking system.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,4,'2025-06-25_14-25-48','Setting the PSP using __set_PSP in the SVC_Handler_Main function ensures that each thread has a correctly configured stack pointer. Without this, the system would attempt to run threads with incorrect stack configurations, leading to stack corruption, invalid memory access, and ultimately, a hard fault. When switching between threads, the system must save the current thread\'s context (including the stack pointer) and load the context of the next thread to run so that each thread can resume execution as if it had never been interrupted. Managing the PSP is essential for the stability, safety, and reliability of a multitasking system.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,4,'2025-06-26_15-28-32','Setting the PSP using __set_PSP in the SVC_Handler_Main function ensures that each thread has a correctly configured stack pointer. If PSP is not properly initialized, then any attempt to use it will result in memory access violation and, ultimately, a hard fault. When switching between threads, the system must save the current thread\'s context (including the stack pointer) and load the context of the next thread to run so that each thread can resume execution as if it had never been interrupted. If PSP is not set to the correct thread address, then the processor might overwrite the kernel’s stack or another thread’s stack, which would cause data corruption and unpredictable behaviour. Managing the PSP is essential for the stability, safety, and reliability of a multitasking system.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,4,'2025-06-26_18-47-14','The threads stack is located at a different address than the CPU’s default stack pointer, the main stack pointer (MSP). The PSP is used to enable separate stacks for each application thread while the MSP is used for the operating system kernel and the interrupt handlers. Since PSP is mainly used for applications and MSP is used for internal kernel operations, this is similar to the actual role and protection level required for the kernel stacks and the thread stacks. Setting the PSP using __set_PSP in the SVC_Handler_Main function ensures that each thread has a correctly configured stack pointer. If PSP is not properly initialized, then any attempt to use it will result in memory access violation and, ultimately, a hard fault. When switching between threads, the system must save the current thread\'s context (including the stack pointer) and load the context of the next thread to run so that each thread can resume execution as if it had never been interrupted. If PSP is not set to the correct thread address, then the processor might overwrite the kernel’s stack or another thread’s stack, which would cause data corruption and unpredictable behaviour. Managing the PSP is essential for the stability, safety, and reliability of a multitasking system.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,5,'2025-06-05_15-29-23','In order for a thread to return one value and terminate, the infinite loop should be removed, and all shared resources should be freed. The thread should also be removed from the stack to prevent unnecessary memory accumulation.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,5,'2025-06-24_13-11-23','In order for a thread to return one value and terminate, the infinite loop should be removed, and all shared resources should be freed. The thread should also be removed from the stack to prevent unnecessary memory accumulation. Typically, the return address is stored in the stack frame. After printing, if this thread attempts to return to the unsaved execution context, then the system attempts to return the thread from a context where there is no valid return address, which will cause a hard fault. This is handled by the Cortex M4, which will typically return an error\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,5,'2025-06-24_13-42-43','In order for a thread to return one value and terminate, the infinite loop should be removed, and all shared resources should be freed. The thread should also be removed from the stack to prevent unnecessary memory accumulation. Typically, the return address is stored in the stack frame. After printing, if this thread attempts to return to the unsaved execution context, then the system attempts to return the thread from a context where there is no valid return address, which will cause a hard fault. This is handled by the Cortex M4, which will typically return an error\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,5,'2025-06-25_14-25-48','In order for a thread to return one value and terminate, the infinite loop should be removed, and all shared resources should be freed. The thread should also be removed from the stack to prevent unnecessary memory accumulation. Typically, the return address is stored in the stack frame. After printing, if this thread attempts to return to the unsaved execution context, then the system attempts to return the thread from a context where there is no valid return address, which will cause a hard fault. This is handled by the Cortex M4, which will typically return an error.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',2,5,'2025-06-26_15-28-32','In order for a thread to return one value and terminate, the infinite loop should be removed, and all shared resources should be freed. The thread should also be removed from the stack to prevent unnecessary memory accumulation. Typically, the return address is stored in the stack frame. After printing, if this thread attempts to return to the unsaved execution context, then the system attempts to return the thread from a context where there is no valid return address, which will cause a hard fault. This is handled by the Cortex M4, which will typically return an error.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',2,5,'2025-06-26_18-47-14','In order for a thread to return one value and terminate, the infinite loop should be removed, and all shared resources should be freed. The thread should also be removed from the stack to prevent unnecessary memory accumulation. Typically, the return address is stored in the stack frame, but not on the thread stack. After printing, if this thread attempts to return to the unsaved execution context, then the system attempts to return the thread from a context where there is no valid return address. If the return address is corrupted, overwritten, or missing (e.g., due to a buffer overflow or other memory corruption), the CPU will not know where to return to, and this will trigger a fault or a trap. The OS will likely terminate the program to prevent further damage to the system, which will cause a hard fault. This is handled by the Cortex M4, which will typically return an error.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,1,'2025-06-26_12-30-58','.c files are first translated into .o (object) files, which are basically incomprehensible to humans, and then the .o files are linked together by the linker and turned into a .exe file, which is the executable. The first step during compilation is pre-processing, during which any #include or # define statements are executed and macros and header files are included with the main code. Following this step, each source file is then compiled into .o files (object files), which are created when the C code is translated into machine code (binary code and symbol tables). All the object files and any references like function calls or access to variables files are combined by the linker, which also links necessary libraries (such as standard r custom libraries). This results in a single executable file that can be run on the target system.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,1,'2025-06-26_13-12-41','.c files are first translated into .o (object) files, which are basically incomprehensible to humans, and then the .o files are linked together by the linker and turned into a .exe file, which is the executable. The first step during compilation is pre-processing, during which any #include or # define statements are executed and macros and header files are included with the main code. Following this step, each source file is then compiled into .o files (object files), which are created when the C code is translated into machine code (binary code and symbol tables). All the object files and any references like function calls or access to variables files are combined by the linker, which also links necessary libraries (such as standard r custom libraries). This results in a single executable file that can be run on the target system.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,1,'2025-06-27_12-08-03','.c files are first translated into .o (object) files, which are basically incomprehensible to humans, and then the .o files are linked together by the linker and turned into a .exe file, which is the executable. The first step during compilation is pre-processing, during which any #include or # define statements are executed and macros and header files are included with the main code. Following this step, each source file is then compiled into .o files (object files), which are created when the C code is translated into machine code (binary code and symbol tables). All the object files and any references like function calls or access to variables files are combined by the linker, which also links necessary libraries (such as standard r custom libraries). This results in a single executable file that can be run on the target system.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,1,'2025-06-27_12-41-02','.c files are first translated into .o (object) files, which are basically incomprehensible to humans, and then the .o files are linked together by the linker and turned into a .exe file, which is the executable. The first step during compilation is pre-processing, during which any #include or # define statements are executed and macros and header files are included with the main code. Following this step, each source file is then compiled into .o files (object files), which are created when the C code is translated into machine code (binary code and symbol tables). All the object files and any references like function calls or access to variables files are combined by the linker, which also links necessary libraries (such as standard r custom libraries). This results in a single executable file that can be run on the target system.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,2,'2025-06-26_12-30-58','When #include is used, this will cause both files to be compiled together and pre-processed, which could potentially cause functions in the kernel.c file to be defined twice (once when compiling the file itself and again when the file containing the #include directive is processed). Essentially, the code will be compiled as if the contents of kernel.c were part of the code that it was included in. Due to the many possible conflict errors, it is generally a safer idea to compile the files separately and then link them together.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,2,'2025-06-26_13-12-41','When #include is used, this will cause both files to be compiled together and pre-processed, which could potentially cause functions in the kernel.c file to be defined twice (once when compiling the file itself and again when the file containing the #include directive is processed). Essentially, the code will be compiled as if the contents of kernel.c were part of the code that it was included in. Due to the many possible conflict errors, it is generally a safer idea to compile the files separately and then link them together.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,2,'2025-06-27_12-08-03','When #include is used, this will cause both files to be compiled together and pre-processed, which could potentially cause functions in the kernel.c file to be defined twice (once when compiling the file itself and again when the file containing the #include directive is processed). Essentially, the code will be compiled as if the contents of kernel.c were part of the code that it was included in. Due to the many possible conflict errors, it is generally a safer idea to compile the files separately and then link them together.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,2,'2025-06-27_12-41-02','When #include is used, this will cause both files to be compiled together and pre-processed, which could potentially cause functions in the kernel.c file to be defined twice (once when compiling the file itself and again when the file containing the #include directive is processed). Essentially, the code will be compiled as if the contents of kernel.c were part of the code that it was included in. Due to the many possible conflict errors, it is generally a safer idea to compile the files separately and then link them together.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,3,'2025-06-26_12-30-58','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, the necessary variables (e.g. the initial value of MSP and the default size of a stack) and thread structs must be first initialized followed by a check for sufficient stack space (in this case, 0x400 for the size of the stack). Next, a target function is passed to the thread, and then the method to start the thread is called to execute the previously passed task in a new concurrently running thread.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',3,3,'2025-06-26_13-12-41','Threads are units of execution within a process, while the kernel is the core of the operating system that manages everything; as such, the kernel must be started first so that there is an environment in which processes and threads can run. The kernel must be initialized, then the thread must be registered, and then the kernel must be started. Then, to set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, the necessary variables (e.g. the initial value of MSP and the default size of a stack) and thread structs must be first initialized followed by a check for sufficient stack space (in this case, 0x400 for the size of the stack). Next, a target function is passed to the thread, and then the method to start the thread is called to execute the previously passed task in a new concurrently running thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,3,'2025-06-27_12-08-03','Threads are units of execution within a process, while the kernel is the core of the operating system that manages everything; as such, the kernel must be started first so that there is an environment in which processes and threads can run. The kernel must be initialized, then the thread must be registered, and then the kernel must be started. Then, to set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, the necessary variables (e.g. the initial value of MSP and the default size of a stack) and thread structs must be first initialized followed by a check for sufficient stack space (in this case, 0x400 for the size of the stack). Next, a target function is passed to the thread, and then the method to start the thread is called to execute the previously passed task in a new concurrently running thread.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',3,3,'2025-06-27_12-41-02','The user initiates threading by writing the code (in this lab, a function) that a thread will execute, then explicitly calling the appropriate API to create and start the thread. Threads are units of execution within a process, while the kernel is the core of the operating system that manages everything; as such, the kernel must be started first so that there is an environment in which processes and threads can run. The kernel must be initialized, then the thread must be registered, and then the kernel must be started. Then, to set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, the necessary variables (e.g. the initial value of MSP and the default size of a stack) and thread structs must be first initialized followed by a check for sufficient stack space (in this case, 0x400 for the size of the stack). Next, the target user-created function is passed to the thread, and then the method to start the thread is called to execute the previously passed task in a new concurrently running thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,4,'2025-06-26_12-30-58','The stack pool is typically used to manage allocation/deallocation of threads by reusing stack space after a thread is terminated, which allows the OS to run multiple threads concurrently and prevents one thread from corrupting the stack of another thread. When switching between threads, the current thread pointer is saved and the next thread’s context is loaded; when a thread is stopped, the OS deallocates it from the stack and recycles the stack space.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',3,4,'2025-06-26_13-12-41','The stack pool is typically used to manage allocation/deallocation of threads by reusing stack space after a thread is terminated, which allows the OS to run multiple threads concurrently and prevents one thread from corrupting the stack of another thread. It is essentially a pre-allocated block of memory that allocates/deallocates using LIFO (last-in first-out). Within it, each thread has its own private stack, which is a memory region used for storing local variables, function call information, and execution context. This isolates and protects separate parts of a process’ memory so that threads do not interfere with each other. When switching between threads, the current thread pointer is saved and the next thread’s context is loaded; when a thread is stopped, the OS deallocates it from the stack and recycles the stack space.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,4,'2025-06-27_12-08-03','The stack pool is typically used to manage allocation/deallocation of threads by reusing stack space after a thread is terminated, which allows the OS to run multiple threads concurrently and prevents one thread from corrupting the stack of another thread. It is essentially a pre-allocated block of memory that allocates/deallocates using LIFO (last-in first-out). Within it, each thread has its own private stack, which is a memory region used for storing local variables, function call information, and execution context. This isolates and protects separate parts of a process’ memory so that threads do not interfere with each other. When switching between threads, the current thread pointer is saved and the next thread’s context is loaded; when a thread is stopped, the OS deallocates it from the stack and recycles the stack space.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,4,'2025-06-27_12-41-02','The stack pool is typically used to manage allocation/deallocation of threads by reusing stack space after a thread is terminated, which allows the OS to run multiple threads concurrently and prevents one thread from corrupting the stack of another thread. It is essentially a pre-allocated block of memory that allocates/deallocates using LIFO (last-in first-out). Within it, each thread has its own private stack, which is a memory region used for storing local variables, function call information, and execution context. This isolates and protects separate parts of a process’ memory so that threads do not interfere with each other. When switching between threads, the current thread pointer is saved and the next thread’s context is loaded; when a thread is stopped, the OS deallocates it from the stack and recycles the stack space.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,5,'2025-06-26_12-30-58','User space is the memory area where application programs and some system utilities run. It is separate from the kernel space to protect the operating system from potentially harmful user applications. Kernel space is the memory area where the core of the operating system, the kernel, executes. The kernel has complete control over the system and can execute any CPU instruction and reference any memory address. Since the “user” and the “kernel” have different permission levels with regards to OS access, this improves the overall safety and performance of the system (e.g. user application bugs/crashes do not directly impact the stability of the entire system).\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',3,5,'2025-06-26_13-12-41','User space is the memory area where application programs and some system utilities run. It is separate from the kernel space to protect the operating system from potentially harmful user applications. Kernel space is the memory area where the core of the operating system, the kernel, executes. The kernel has complete control over the system and can execute any CPU instruction and reference any memory address. Since the “user” and the “kernel” have different permission levels with regards to OS access, this improves the overall safety and performance of the system (e.g. user application bugs/crashes do not directly impact the stability of the entire system). To provide protection for the kernel, the kernel’s API acts as an intermediary, which prevents the user applications from directly accessing/modifying the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',3,5,'2025-06-27_12-08-03','User space is the memory area where application programs and some system utilities run. It is separate from the kernel space to protect the operating system from potentially harmful user applications. Kernel space is the memory area where the core of the operating system, the kernel, executes. The kernel has complete control over the system and can execute any CPU instruction and reference any memory address. It provides an API that application developers use to create and manage system-level features like threads, synchronization, and memory, but it’s the user’s responsibility to call the appropriate API functions to perform these tasks. Since the “user” and the “kernel” have different permission levels with regards to OS access, this improves the overall safety and performance of the system (e.g. user application bugs/crashes do not directly impact the stability of the entire system). To provide protection for the kernel, the kernel’s API acts as an intermediary, which prevents the user applications from directly accessing/modifying the kernel. This essentially abstracts the complexity of kernel-level thread management, allowing the user to focus on designing the application logic while relying on the kernel’s API to handle low-level details. Some examples would be using malloc() to allocate memory or osCreateThread() (from this lab) to create a new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',3,5,'2025-06-27_12-41-02','User space is the memory area where application programs and some system utilities run. It is separate from the kernel space to protect the operating system from potentially harmful user applications. Kernel space is the memory area where the core of the operating system, the kernel, executes. The kernel has complete control over the system and can execute any CPU instruction and reference any memory address. It provides an API that application developers use to create and manage system-level features like threads, synchronization, and memory, but it’s the user’s responsibility to call the appropriate API functions to perform these tasks. Since the “user” and the “kernel” have different permission levels with regards to OS access, this improves the overall safety and performance of the system (e.g. user application bugs/crashes do not directly impact the stability of the entire system). To provide protection for the kernel, the kernel’s API acts as an intermediary, which prevents the user applications from directly accessing/modifying the kernel. This essentially abstracts the complexity of kernel-level thread management, allowing the user to focus on designing the application logic while relying on the kernel’s API to handle low-level details. Some examples would be using malloc() to allocate memory or osCreateThread() (from this lab) to create a new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',4,1,'2025-07-26_20-14-21','Round-robin scheduling completes tasks in the order in which they arrive, so the queue’s first-in first-out structure works well since the next processes (new or unfinished) will always be at the front of the queue, and the completed processes will not be requeued. Unlike arrays, queues are dynamic (do not need a predefined size), and enqueueing/dequeuing only takes O(1) time, which is relatively efficient.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',4,1,'2025-07-28_14-32-11','Round-robin scheduling completes tasks in the order in which they arrive; when there is a context switch or if a task’s allocated time expires, the task is moved to the back of the line. This ensures fairness and efficiency and avoids starvation by preventing a task from taking up too many resources.\nTo implement this algorithm, a queue’s first-in first-out structure works well since the next processes (new or unfinished) will always be at the front of the queue, and the completed processes will not be requeued. Unlike arrays, queues are dynamic (do not need a predefined size), and enqueueing/dequeuing only takes O(1) time, which is relatively efficient.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',4,2,'2025-07-26_20-14-21','When a context switch occurs, the SVC main function doesn’t return, which clears local variables from the stack. Without the return statement, the memory stack will fill up with variables, and no context switching can occur outside of an interrupt because of the Cortex M4’s restrictions on accessing internal registers. In this case, PendSV—which has to stop and wait for the current interrupt to complete—is required to perform context switching without using the stack and to allow SVC_Handler_Main to complete its task and restore the stack. Though it is still an interrupt, it’s set to a lower priority than SVC_Handler_Main, and it will trigger following the completion and stack restoration of SVC_Handler_Main.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',4,2,'2025-07-28_14-32-11','When a context switch occurs, the CPU\'s user registers, including the program counter, stack pointer, and status register, must be saved, along with any co-processor registers used by the thread or process. These values are typically stored in the kernel stack associated with the interrupted process or thread or within the Process Control Block. In addition, the SVC main function doesn’t return, which clears local variables from the stack. Without the return statement, the memory stack will fill up with variables, and no context switching can occur outside of an interrupt because of the Cortex M4’s restrictions on accessing internal registers. In this case, PendSV—which has to stop and wait for the current interrupt to complete—is required to perform context switching without using the stack and to allow SVC_Handler_Main to complete its task and restore the stack. Though it is still an interrupt, it’s set to a lower priority than SVC_Handler_Main, and it will trigger following the completion and stack restoration of SVC_Handler_Main.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',4,3,'2025-07-26_20-14-21','This priority system means CPU, space, and other resources will be monopolized by high-priority tasks, but low-priority tasks may never have a chance to run (starvation). This leads to further problems if a high-priority task may need resources that are occupied by a low-priority task that has not been run. On a system where multiple users may be attempting to run processes, priority scheduling would cause unresponsiveness and would not be fair; furthermore, maintaining priority queues would be much more difficult than round-robin scheduling, which is already well-supported by the default structure of a queue. Overall, numbering TCBs by priority would decrease performance and efficiency of a system, especially on multi-user systems where fairness is essential.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',4,3,'2025-07-28_14-32-11','This priority system means CPU, space, and other resources will be monopolized by high-priority tasks, but low-priority tasks may never have a chance to run (starvation). This leads to further problems if a high-priority task may need resources that are occupied by a low-priority task that has not been run. On a system where multiple users may be attempting to run processes, priority scheduling would cause unresponsiveness and would not be fair; furthermore, maintaining priority queues would be much more difficult than round-robin scheduling, which is already well-supported by the default structure of a queue. Overall, numbering TCBs by priority would decrease performance and efficiency of a system, especially on multi-user systems where fairness is essential; the scheduler will always choose the highest-priority task to run without interruption, thus causing the other tasks to starve.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',4,4,'2025-07-26_20-14-21','Compared to Assembly, C is much more readable, reliable, and easy to develop, maintain, and port. C also has many libraries to support development of complex functions, and it’s much easier to debug, handle, and fix errors in C. Unfortunately, C requires more stack space due to local variables and function calls, and it doesn’t offer the precise, low-level control over hardware and code execution that Assembly does.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',4,4,'2025-07-28_14-32-11','Compared to Assembly, C is much more readable, reliable, and easy to develop, maintain, and port. C also has many libraries to support development of complex functions, and it’s much easier to debug, handle, and fix errors in C. Unfortunately, C requires more stack space due to local variables and function calls, and it doesn’t offer the precise, low-level control over hardware and code execution that Assembly does.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',4,5,'2025-07-26_20-14-21','Theoretically, the OS should still work; the scheduler will save the context of the print thread after it yields before queuing and executing the same run thread once again. Since there is only the print_continuously thread, the OS will not be multitasking (no other threads to run), but it will not be running or responding to other tasks. However, the OS will still be able to respond to system calls and interrupts, but because it is running only one thread, it won’t be multitasking or making effective use of system resources and it will be slow to respond to inputs or system events.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',4,5,'2025-07-28_14-32-11','Theoretically, the OS should still work; the scheduler will save the context of the print thread after it yields before queuing and executing the same run thread once again. Since there is only the print_continuously thread, the OS will not be multitasking (no other threads to run), but it will not be running or responding to other tasks. However, the OS will still be able to respond to system calls and interrupts, but because it is running only one thread, it won’t be multitasking or making effective use of system resources and it will be slow to respond to inputs or system events.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',5,1,'2025-07-26_18-51-38','First, the OS is requested to create a new thread by allocating resources such as a stack, CPU register state, and thread control block (TCB). The TCB maintains all the state information about a thread, including its stack pointer, program counter, and register state, allowing the OS to manage and switch between threads seamlessly. Then, the system sets up the environment for the thread to execute by placing the arguments in the appropriate location, such as the stack or registers, depending on the OS and architecture. By ensuring that the thread\'s stack is initialized correctly, the thread function can access its arguments once it starts. Finally, the scheduler determines when the new thread gets CPU time. The thread is put into the runnable state and scheduled according to the system\'s scheduling policy.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',5,2,'2025-07-26_18-51-38','osYield itself may trigger a context switch and/or another interrupt and calling it can lead to nested or recursive interrupt handling. Nesting can result in stack overflow or other resource exhaustion issues, as each nested interrupt requires additional stack space and handling overhead. Context switches should be predictable and deterministic but calling osYield in context-switching handlers can make the timing and sequence of task switches unpredictable, which can lead to race conditions, deadlocks, or inconsistent system states. Furthermore, osYield might not be designed to be re-entrant—it cannot safely be called from within an interrupt context where another context switch is already being processed—so re-entrancy issues can corrupt the system state if the context-switching mechanism can’t handle concurrent modifications. The responsibility of handlers like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, and SysTick_Handler is to manage specific system events and trigger context switches in a controlled manner, but introducing osYield disrupts their designated roles and any errors caused using osYield can be difficult to debug due to unpredictable behaviour caused by such calls. In addition, since real-time operating systems are designed with specific practices for task management and context switching, calling osYield in inappropriate places undermines the RTOS\'s integrity and reliability.\n',0,'s25'),('r42liu',5,3,'2025-07-26_18-51-38','A smaller tick interval means that the OS can switch between tasks more frequently. This can lead to better responsiveness, particularly for real-time applications where tasks need to react to events promptly. With a 0.1ms tick, tasks can have more precise timing controls, which is beneficial for applications requiring high-resolution timing, such as data acquisition systems. Lower tick intervals reduce the latency for high-priority tasks to be scheduled, which is crucial for real-time systems where meeting deadlines is critical. The OS can provide more accurate time measurements and delays, which can improve the precision of time-dependent operations.\nHowever, a smaller tick interval means the SysTick timer interrupt occurs more frequently, which increases the CPU\'s interrupt handling overhead, potentially reducing the time available for actual task execution. More frequent task switches can lead to higher context switching overhead, which can degrade overall system performance if the tasks are relatively short. This increased overhead from handling frequent interrupts and context switches can make the system less efficient in executing tasks. Frequent interrupts and context switches can also lead to higher power consumption, which is a concern for battery-powered or energy-sensitive applications. More frequent interrupts can cause greater timer drift if not managed properly, lowering the accuracy of time-dependent operations, and tasks that require longer execution times might experience more frequent pre-emption, which can lead to resource starvation or inefficiencies.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',5,4,'2025-07-26_18-51-38','In co-operative multitasking, the user (programmer) is responsible for ensuring that each task periodically yields control back to the OS or other tasks. This is typically done through explicit calls to yield functions or by completing the task\'s execution. Users have precise control over when context switches occur, making the system behavior predictable if all tasks cooperate correctly. If a task does not yield control, it can block the entire system, leading to unresponsive behavior, and if a poorly designed task fails to yield, it can cause other tasks to starve. Since the timing of context switches is more predictable and controlled by the application, debugging timing-related issues can be simpler. The OS scheduler is simpler because it relies on tasks to yield control voluntarily and it doesn’t need to rely on the OS to handle interrupts for context switching or forcibly save and restore task states.\nIn pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically manages task switching, so the user does not need to manually yield control. This leads to a more balanced use of CPU time among tasks. The system can respond more promptly to external events and interrupts, making it more suitable for real-time applications. Even if a task enters an infinite loop or becomes unresponsive, the OS can pre-empt it and switch to other tasks, preventing the entire system from hanging. Since context switches can occur at any time, timing-related issues, such as race conditions and deadlocks, can be more challenging to debug. The OS requires a more complex scheduler to manage priorities, handle interrupts (e.g., from a system tick timer like SysTick, which will periodically pre-empt the running task and switch to another task) and ensure fair CPU time distribution among tasks, but it will incur overhead from saving and restoring task states more frequently, handling interrupts, and managing the task control blocks (TCBs) to ensure high-priority tasks are given preference.\n',1,'s25'),('r42liu',5,5,'2025-07-26_18-51-38','Yielding involves a task voluntarily giving up control of the CPU by calling a specific function, such as osYield(), taskYield(), or similar. When a task calls a yield function, it signals the OS scheduler to perform a context switch, allowing another task to run. The OS marks the yielding task as ready to run and selects another ready task to execute. Then, the context switch occurs, saving the state of the current task and loading the state of the next task.\nTime-based pre-emptive context switching relies on a hardware timer (such as the SysTick timer) to generate periodic interrupts. When the timer interrupt occurs, the OS scheduler is invoked to determine if the currently running task should be pre-empted to allow another task to run. The OS interrupt handler (e.g., SysTick_Handler) saves the state of the current task and invokes the scheduler. Next, the scheduler selects the highest-priority ready task, and the context switch occurs, loading the state of the selected task.\nBoth switching methods rely on the same OS scheduler to maintain a list of tasks and their states (e.g., running, ready, waiting) and to perform context switches. When a task yields, it explicitly transitions to the ready state, allowing the scheduler to pick another task. When a timer interrupt occurs, the OS determines if the running task should be pre-empted based on priority and scheduling policies. The timer interrupt handler (e.g., SysTick_Handler) and the yield function both lead to invoking the scheduler, which decides the next task to run.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',1,1,'2025-05-06_12-33-08','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is code that has been written as a “base” for embedded projects that is hardware specific and made to simplify the control by a user. It essentially provides functions and starting code that users build on top of so that they do not have to start from scratch – and they don’t have to write extremely low-level code to get the board working. As the name suggests, it abstracts the hardware layer so that a user does not have to fully grasp the details of the system to be able to code. For example, in the lab we used the function HAL_UART_Transmit() which allowed us to send data through the UART without us having to directly manipulate hardware registers, which made development significantly easier.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',1,2,'2025-05-06_12-33-08','The __io_putchar function simply writes a single char (which is a single byte) to the UART peripheral of the microcontroller. It is needed because the printf function included with the stdio.h header relies on low level functions to interact with hardware. The __io_putchar function could not have come directly from stdio.h because it is hardware specific - this function would have to be different for a different microcontroller. Stdio.h provides a base to use inputs/outputs that are generalized amongst operating systems, and the function we write at a low level helps the stdio functions to do what is expected. When using printf, it repeatedly calls __io_putchar for each character in the string. __io_putchar acts as a bridging function between the standard c library and the specific hardware we use.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',1,3,'2025-05-06_12-33-08','The debugger was necessary in the lab because it allows real-time examination of system registers and stack pointers without disturbing them. Using printf to simply print these values to examine them is problematic because printing uses the stack, which would change the values it is trying to see in the first place. To observe things at a low level, debugging allows pausing to directly view register values, such as when we inspected MSP and PSP values. Additionally, because we are manipulating core registers such as CONTROL and switching between stack pointers, errors could have easily been made and would cause instability/crash before printf could execute. Debugging allows us to examine the values and ensure they are correct without affecting the system state.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',1,4,'2025-05-06_12-33-08','The stack allocation method we used works by dividing the total stack into sections of memory to be used for distinct executions – which would allow for multithreading. MSP_init_val is the initial stack pointer value (0x0). PSP_val was calculated by subtracting the size of the main stack, which was 0x400 or 1024 bytes. This value is subtracted because the stack grows downward in memory, thus a new stack section would start at a lower address than the previous one. So, the PSP can be allocated at 1024 bytes downward from the MSP_init_val, ensuring that the new process stack will be in unused memory directly below the main stack. It was necessary to know the initial MSP location since it serves as our reference point for the top of all available stack memory.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',1,5,'2025-05-06_12-33-08','This would be done with a line like ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE where j is the thread number starting from 1. There would be defined constants for MAIN_STACK_SIZE and THREAD_STACK_SIZE (in our case we used 0x400) to track the stack locations. This method allows as many stacks as needed (so long as it does not exceed the total stack size) that will each have its own safe and distinct section of memory to perform the thread’s tasks. These sections will simply continue to be added below the previous. To take it further, these stack locations could be kept in an array to make it easier to index easily, though this would only be more optimal if many more threads are desired.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',2,1,'2025-06-02_09-34-41','This example showed the effects of manipulating the PC, which was able to control program flow at a very low level. By moving the function address to the PC register, it essentially forced the processor to begin executing that function. Normally, the processor updates PC automatically with what to do, but by manually inserting the address of the function in the PC, the CPU jumps to that function and executes it forever (since the function never stops).\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',2,1,'2025-06-02_09-50-32','This example showed the effects of manipulating the PC, which was able to control program flow at a very low level. By moving the function address to the PC register, it essentially forced the processor to begin executing that function. Normally, the processor updates PC automatically with what to do, but by manually inserting the address of the function in the PC, the CPU jumps to that function and executes it forever (since the function never stops).\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',2,2,'2025-06-02_09-34-41','A system call is a way for user level permissions to request a service from the OS which can then be executed in Kernel/Supervisor mode. It is triggered using special assembly instructions which is the SVC and automatically tells the processor to enter kernel mode and jump to a handler function. The system call passes a number that tells the handler what service is being requested (eg. Printing, switch stacks).\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',2,2,'2025-06-02_09-50-32','A system call is a special type of function that allows user-level rpograms to request services from the operating system. It is a controlled way for programs to interact with privileged hardware operations without accessing them directly. This provides added security, as it prevents the user from even accidentally breaking the hardware interface, it allows the OS to have full control and determine whether the operation is safe. System calls are invoked using the SVC which is a supervisor call. It switches the processor to privileged mode and transfers control to a handler function in the OS. This mechanism organizes program to OS communication and ensures security and isolation.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',2,3,'2025-06-02_09-34-41','To set up the threads stack, a valid memory location had to be initialized first (below the initial MSP), where fake values were pushed to the stack that represent what the CPU would normally store during a function call. This included program counter (PC), program status registers (xPSR, and all the general-purpose registers. To “Set up a threads stack” it means to prepare memory so that when the CPU starts using it, it can pop the values in the real registers and begin running the thread as if it had always been active. Essentially, we trick the chip into believing it is already running the thread before we run it. These values are stored so the CPU knows where to return, what data the thread had, and which function to execute.\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',2,3,'2025-06-02_09-50-32','Setting up a thread’s stack means to prepare a specific memory area that will be used as the thread’s private stack. In the lab, memory just below the MSP is used to simulate a new stack and this memory was denoted as PSP. Then, register values were manually pushed onto the stack in a very specific order: xPSR (program status register), PC (program counter), LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, followed by R11 through R4. This order was critical because the CPU pops these values in exact reverse order when restoring the thread’s context during execution. By preloading these values, it is simulating what would happen if the thread had been running and was interrupted. This gives the thread all the data it needs, including function address, return address, arguments, and working registers, so the CPU can resume execution as if the thread had never been stopped or created artificially.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',2,4,'2025-06-02_09-34-41','If PSP was not set, the processor would try to use an undefined or incorrect stack pointer for the thread and continue to pop register values from an unknown and bad memory address. This results in memory access violations, or corrupted PC values which lead to a hard fault. Setting PSP correctly ensures the processor uses a safe, valid stack for the new thread’s context.\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',2,4,'2025-06-02_09-50-32','Each thread requires its own stack, and that stack must reside at a different memory address than the default main stack pointer (MSP). In ARM processors, the PSP is specifically used for thread execution, while the MSP is reserved for system level operations. If PSP was not set, the processor would try to use an undefined or incorrect stack pointer for the thread and continue to pop register values from an unknown and bad memory address. This results in memory access violations, or corrupted PC values which lead to a hard fault. Calling __set_PSP correctly ensures the processor uses a safe, valid stack for the new thread’s context.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',2,5,'2025-06-02_09-34-41','If the thread returned, the CPU would attempt to pop the return address from the stack and jump to it. Since this is not a normal function call and we did not set up proper return address, it would likely jump to an invalid location or execute garbage instructions, causing a hard fault. Threads are not meant to return; they are meant to run indefinitely until explicitly terminated by the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',2,5,'2025-06-02_09-50-32','If the thread returned, the CPU would attempt to pop the return address from the stack and jump to it. Since this is not a normal function call and we did not set up proper return address, it would likely jump to an invalid location or execute garbage instructions, causing a hard fault. Threads are not meant to return; they are meant to run indefinitely until explicitly terminated by the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',3,1,'2025-06-24_23-06-53','Each .c file is compiled individually by the compiler into an obj file .o. The object files contain machine code but are not yet linked together. The linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable, resolving any references between files (such as extern variables, or references between files and headers).\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',3,1,'2025-06-24_23-32-07','Preprocessing: Before compilation, each .c file is sent through the preprocessor which handles all the preprocessor directives (things that start with #, such as #include or #define). It replaces header file includes with their contents, expands macros, and removes comments. The output is then a fully expanded version of the source code.\nCompiling: After preprocessing, the compiler translates each preprocessed c file into an object file .o. This converts the human readable code into machine code, whilst checking for syntax or semantic errors. The result is still not complete because it contains unresolved references to functions or variables in other files.\nLinking: Linker takes all object files and combines them into a single executable, resolving any references between files (such as extern variables, or references between files and headers). Linker ensures that all function calls, global variables, and symbols are matched up correctly so the executable can run.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',3,2,'2025-06-24_23-06-53','The macro #include copies the entire content of the file before compilation. This can often lead to duplicated definitions, linker errors, and breaks the principle of modularity design. Each source file should be compiled separately and only communication through shared declarations in header files. Instead, only the header file should be included while the actual logic remains in c files to be individually compiled.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',3,2,'2025-06-24_23-32-07','The macro #include copies the entire content of the file before compilation. This can often lead to duplicated definitions, linker errors, and breaks the principle of modularity design. Each source file should be compiled separately and only communication through shared declarations in header files. Instead, only the header file should be included while the actual logic remains in c files to be individually compiled.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',3,3,'2025-06-24_23-06-53','Prepare OS: setup internal data structures to keep track of available memory and stack space for threads\nDefine thread behaviour: write a function describing what the thread will do when it runs\nRegister thread with system: request the system to create new thread using the defined behaviour. As long as there is enough stack space available, the system can set up necessary context and saves info about the thread.\nRun thread: System runs thread based on setup earlier\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',3,3,'2025-06-24_23-32-07','Initialize the kernel: Set up all internal kernel data, such as where the thread stacks will be placed and how the kernel will manage threads. This is done by calling a specific kernel function to initialize the system.\nDefine thread behaviour: write a function describing what the thread will do when it runs. The function address must be know for the kernel to use it to setup the thread correctly.\nCreate thread: User calls kernel function to take thread function as input. Kernel allocates memory for the threads stack, prepares the threads initial state, and stores all the necessary information (stack ptr, function address) in its internal records.\nStart kernel: After one thread is created, user can call another function to start the kernel, in which it begins execution by switching to the thread and running its function\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',3,4,'2025-06-24_23-06-53','Stack pool is a large memory region reserved for allocating stacks for all threads. Each thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls, local variables, and CPU context. When a new thread is created, the kernel carves out a fixed size portion of the stack pool for it. In future labs, this will allow the OS to manage multiple threads by giving each a separate stack and switching between them safely.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',3,4,'2025-06-24_23-32-07','Stack pool is a large memory region reserved for allocating stacks for all threads. Each thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls, local variables, and CPU context. When a new thread is created, the kernel carves out a fixed size portion of the stack pool for it. In future labs, this will allow the OS to manage multiple threads by giving each a separate stack and switching between them safely.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',3,5,'2025-06-24_23-06-53','The kernel code (both the header and c file) contains the OS internals such as stack management, thread control, and global variables. The user code (main) defines the thread behaviour and calls kernel functions in order to access OS services. This separation ensures the user doesn’t directly interfere with low-level OS mechanisms, making development safer and more modular. It allows the OS to be reused without changing the user code.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',3,5,'2025-06-24_23-32-07','The kernel code (both the header and c file) contains the OS internals such as stack management, thread control, and global variables. The user code (main) defines the thread behaviour and calls kernel functions in order to access OS services. This separation ensures the user doesn’t directly interfere with low-level OS mechanisms, making development safer and more modular. It allows the OS to be reused without changing the user code.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,1,'2025-07-14_12-52-38','Using a queue is better than using just an array since it aligns well with the FIFO methodology. When a task yields or is pre-empted, its TCB is placed at the back of the queue and the scheduler selects the next TCB from the front. This simplifies rotation logic as the queue inherently provides the necessary order of execution. Compared to using an array, which would require manual indexing and cycling, a queue reduces the user overhead and the necessary work needed to implement the idea. It also avoids errors such as index overflow. Additionally, queues make it easier to dynamically add or remove tasks, which increases flexibility and scalability.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,1,'2025-07-14_13-01-42','Using a queue is better than using just an array since it aligns well with the FIFO methodology. When a task yields or is pre-empted, its TCB is placed at the back of the queue and the scheduler selects the next TCB from the front. This simplifies rotation logic as the queue inherently provides the necessary order of execution. Compared to using an array, which would require manual indexing and cycling, a queue reduces the user overhead and the necessary work needed to implement the idea. It also avoids errors such as index overflow. Additionally, queues make it easier to dynamically add or remove tasks, which increases flexibility and scalability.\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',4,1,'2025-07-14_13-11-05','Using a queue is better than using just an array since it aligns well with the FIFO methodology. When a task yields or is pre-empted, its TCB is placed at the back of the queue and the scheduler selects the next TCB from the front. This simplifies rotation logic as the queue inherently provides the necessary order of execution. Compared to using an array, which would require manual indexing and cycling, a queue reduces the user overhead and the necessary work needed to implement the idea. It also avoids errors such as index overflow. Additionally, queues make it easier to dynamically add or remove tasks, which increases flexibility and scalability.\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',4,1,'2025-07-14_13-19-07','In round robin scheduling, tasks are selected in a fixed, repeating order. The scheduler implemented in the lab already behaves like a queue, it cycles through TCBs in a circular array moving to the next index each time a task yields. This mimics the behaviour of a FIFO queue. Using a queue is better than using just an array since it aligns well with the FIFO methodology. When a task yields or is pre-empted, its TCB is placed at the back of the queue and the scheduler selects the next TCB from the front. This simplifies rotation logic as the queue inherently provides the necessary order of execution. Compared to using an array, which would require manual indexing and cycling, a queue reduces the user overhead and the necessary work needed to implement the idea. It also avoids errors such as index overflow. Additionally, queues make it easier to dynamically add or remove tasks, which increases flexibility and scalability. In short, while both structures can support round-robin scheduling, the array offers simpler, faster access patterns, whereas a queue provides flexibility and clearer semantics at the cost of added complexity.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,1,'2025-07-14_13-27-46','In round robin scheduling, tasks are selected in a fixed, repeating order. The scheduler implemented in the lab already behaves like a queue, it cycles through TCBs in a circular array moving to the next index each time a task yields. This mimics the behaviour of a FIFO queue. Using a queue is better than using just an array since it aligns well with the FIFO methodology. When a task yields or is pre-empted, its TCB is placed at the back of the queue and the scheduler selects the next TCB from the front. This simplifies rotation logic as the queue inherently provides the necessary order of execution. Compared to using an array, which would require manual indexing and cycling, a queue reduces the user overhead and the necessary work needed to implement the idea. It also avoids errors such as index overflow. Additionally, queues make it easier to dynamically add or remove tasks, which increases flexibility and scalability. In short, while both structures can support round-robin scheduling, the array offers simpler, faster access patterns, whereas a queue provides flexibility and clearer semantics at the cost of added complexity.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,2,'2025-07-14_12-52-38','The PendSV handler is triggered to perform a context switch. It saves the context including registers and stack pointers of the currently running task and stores it in the tasks TCB. Then it calls the scheduler to select the next task to run. After choosing the next task, it loads that tasks saved context from its TCB, restoring the cpu state so execution can resume from where that task left off. This switching is what allows multiple tasks to appear to run simultaneously on a single CPU.\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',4,2,'2025-07-14_13-01-42','The PendSV handler is triggered to perform a context switch, and saves the current task’s CPU state and loads the next task’s state. When the handler runs, it saves the current task context (registers R0-R3 and R12 and LR PC xPSR all pushed via the PSP, handler manually pushes R4-R11 cause they are callee-saved registers, and current PSP value is saved to task’s TCB), then selects the next taskusing osSched and that PSP value is loaded from its TCB, and finally the handler restores the next task context by popping R4-R11 from the stack. The hardware automatically restores R0-R3 R12 LR PC and xPSR.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,2,'2025-07-14_13-11-05','The PendSV handler is triggered to perform a context switch, and saves the current task’s CPU state and loads the next task’s state. When the handler runs, it saves the current task context (registers R0-R3 and R12 and LR PC xPSR all pushed via the PSP, handler manually pushes R4-R11 cause they are callee-saved registers, and current PSP value is saved to task’s TCB), then selects the next taskusing osSched and that PSP value is loaded from its TCB, and finally the handler restores the next task context by popping R4-R11 from the stack. The hardware automatically restores R0-R3 R12 LR PC and xPSR.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,2,'2025-07-14_13-19-07','The PendSV handler is triggered to perform a context switch, and saves the current task’s CPU state and loads the next task’s state. When the handler runs, it saves the current task context (registers R0-R3 and R12 and LR PC xPSR all pushed via the PSP, handler manually pushes R4-R11 cause they are callee-saved registers, and current PSP value is saved to task’s TCB), then selects the next taskusing osSched and that PSP value is loaded from its TCB, and finally the handler restores the next task context by popping R4-R11 from the stack. The hardware automatically restores R0-R3 R12 LR PC and xPSR.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,2,'2025-07-14_13-27-46','The PendSV handler is triggered to perform a context switch, and saves the current task’s CPU state and loads the next task’s state. When the handler runs, it saves the current task context (registers R0-R3 and R12 and LR PC xPSR all pushed via the PSP, handler manually pushes R4-R11 cause they are callee-saved registers, and current PSP value is saved to task’s TCB), then selects the next taskusing osSched and that PSP value is loaded from its TCB, and finally the handler restores the next task context by popping R4-R11 from the stack. The hardware automatically restores R0-R3 R12 LR PC and xPSR.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,3,'2025-07-14_12-52-38','Choosing the highest priority task leads to starvation of lower priority tasks, since they may never get to run if there is always a higher priority task. This destroys fairness and makes the system unresponsive to important lower priority tasks. It can sometimes also complicate the predictability and timing, especially in real time systems. A better approach would be priority based scheduling with aging or preemptive multilevel queues.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,3,'2025-07-14_13-01-42','Choosing the highest priority task leads to starvation of lower priority tasks, since they may never get to run if there is always a higher priority task. This destroys fairness and makes the system unresponsive to important lower priority tasks. It can sometimes also complicate the predictability and timing, especially in real time systems. A better approach would be priority based scheduling with aging or preemptive multilevel queues.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,3,'2025-07-14_13-11-05','Choosing the highest priority task leads to starvation of lower priority tasks, since they may never get to run if there is always a higher priority task. This destroys fairness and makes the system unresponsive to important lower priority tasks. It can sometimes also complicate the predictability and timing, especially in real time systems. A better approach would be priority based scheduling with aging or preemptive multilevel queues.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,3,'2025-07-14_13-19-07','Choosing the highest priority task leads to starvation of lower priority tasks, since they may never get to run if there is always a higher priority task. This destroys fairness and makes the system unresponsive to important lower priority tasks. It can sometimes also complicate the predictability and timing, especially in real time systems. A better approach would be priority based scheduling with aging or preemptive multilevel queues.\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',4,3,'2025-07-14_13-27-46','This approach is problematic because the scheduler will always choose the task with the highest priority that is ready to run, regardless of how long it has already been running. If that task never blocks or yields, the scheduler will repeatedly select it, causing it to run continuously while lower-priority tasks are never given CPU time. This behavior results in starvation for all other tasks. The core issue is that the scheduler lacks fairness—it prioritizes one task unconditionally without considering whether other tasks need to run. To avoid this, a priority-based scheduler should also include mechanisms like aging, preemption limits, or fair scheduling to ensure that all tasks eventually get CPU access.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,4,'2025-07-14_12-52-38','A fundamental of C is functions and modularity. This is for purposes of code readability, maintainability, and portability. C code is easier to write, debug, and understand than low level assembly, however calling C from an interrupt handler has some overhead, and the correct context must be saved and restored. Writing everything in assembly can be more efficient and precise, its more prone to errors and harder to maintain. The hybrid approach of using assembly for context switch and C for scheduling balances performance and ease of development.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,4,'2025-07-14_13-01-42','A fundamental of C is functions and modularity. This is for purposes of code readability, maintainability, and portability. C code is easier to write, debug, and understand than low level assembly, however calling C from an interrupt handler has some overhead, and the correct context must be saved and restored. Writing everything in assembly can be more efficient and precise, its more prone to errors and harder to maintain. The hybrid approach of using assembly for context switch and C for scheduling balances performance and ease of development.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,4,'2025-07-14_13-11-05','A fundamental of C is functions and modularity. This is for purposes of code readability, maintainability, and portability. C code is easier to write, debug, and understand than low level assembly, however calling C from an interrupt handler has some overhead, and the correct context must be saved and restored. Writing everything in assembly can be more efficient and precise, its more prone to errors and harder to maintain. The hybrid approach of using assembly for context switch and C for scheduling balances performance and ease of development.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,4,'2025-07-14_13-19-07','A fundamental of C is functions and modularity. This is for purposes of code readability, maintainability, and portability. C code is easier to write, debug, and understand than low level assembly, however calling C from an interrupt handler has some overhead, and the correct context must be saved and restored. Writing everything in assembly can be more efficient and precise, its more prone to errors and harder to maintain. The hybrid approach of using assembly for context switch and C for scheduling balances performance and ease of development.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,4,'2025-07-14_13-27-46','A fundamental of C is functions and modularity. This is for purposes of code readability, maintainability, and portability. C code is easier to write, debug, and understand than low level assembly, however calling C from an interrupt handler has some overhead, and the correct context must be saved and restored. Writing everything in assembly can be more efficient and precise, its more prone to errors and harder to maintain. The hybrid approach of using assembly for context switch and C for scheduling balances performance and ease of development.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,5,'2025-07-14_12-52-38','The OS would still work but not multitask. Since there is only one thread, the context switch will save and restore the same thread, and the scheduler will be redundant. The yield call will result in the same thread being selected again. The system will appear to run normally as the single thread executes and yields, but no real scheduling or multitasking occurs.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,5,'2025-07-14_13-01-42','The OS would still work but not multitask. Since there is only one thread, the context switch will save and restore the same thread, and the scheduler will be redundant. The yield call will result in the same thread being selected again. The system will appear to run normally as the single thread executes and yields, but no real scheduling or multitasking occurs.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,5,'2025-07-14_13-11-05','The OS would still work but not multitask. Since there is only one thread, the context switch will save and restore the same thread, and the scheduler will be redundant. The yield call will result in the same thread being selected again. The system will appear to run normally as the single thread executes and yields, but no real scheduling or multitasking occurs.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,5,'2025-07-14_13-19-07','The OS would still work but not multitask. Since there is only one thread, the context switch will save and restore the same thread, and the scheduler will be redundant. The yield call will result in the same thread being selected again. The system will appear to run normally as the single thread executes and yields, but no real scheduling or multitasking occurs.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',4,5,'2025-07-14_13-27-46','The OS would still work but not multitask. Since there is only one thread, the context switch will save and restore the same thread, and the scheduler will be redundant. The yield call will result in the same thread being selected again. The system will appear to run normally as the single thread executes and yields, but no real scheduling or multitasking occurs.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,1,'2025-07-14_15-00-06','When creating threds, the user specifies an function and an argument to pass to it. The OS sets up the thread’s stack to simulate the state expected when returning from an interrupt, placing the functions argument in register r0. When the thread begins execution, it returns to the function with r0 already containing the argument, so the function starts as if it had been normally called, making the mechanism seamless and reliable.\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',5,1,'2025-07-14_15-11-59','When a user wants to pass an argument to a thread function, they typically allocate the argument (often as a pointer to a structure or primitive type) and pass that pointer as a parameter to the thread creation function, such as OS_AddThread. The OS then sets up an initial stack frame for the new thread that mimics the state expected after an interrupt return, in accordance with the ARM Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS). This includes placing the argument into register r0—done by arranging the value in the correct position on the stack so that when the processor performs an exception return, it automatically loads r0 with the argument value. This setup ensures that when the thread begins executing, it behaves exactly as if the function had been called normally from C code, with arguments in the correct registers, thanks to adherence to the ABI, which guarantees compatibility with standard function calling conventions.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,1,'2025-07-14_15-18-42','When a user wants to pass an argument to a thread function, they typically allocate the argument (often as a pointer to a structure or primitive type) and pass that pointer as a parameter to the thread creation function, such as OS_AddThread. The OS then sets up an initial stack frame for the new thread that mimics the state expected after an interrupt return, in accordance with the ARM Procedure Call Standard (AAPCS). This includes placing the argument into register r0—done by arranging the value in the correct position on the stack so that when the processor performs an exception return, it automatically loads r0 with the argument value. This setup ensures that when the thread begins executing, it behaves exactly as if the function had been called normally from C code, with arguments in the correct registers, thanks to adherence to the ABI, which guarantees compatibility with standard function calling conventions.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,2,'2025-07-14_15-00-06','Calling osYield() inside any context-switching function (like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler) is dangerous because it sets the PendSV interrupt again, triggering a new context switch while one is already in progress. This can lead to stack corruption, undefined behavior, or system crashes, as it violates the OS\'s assumptions about the atomicity and order of context-switching operations. Therefore, yielding must be avoided in these critical sections to maintain OS stability.\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',5,2,'2025-07-14_15-11-59','Calling osYield() from within context-switching handlers like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler is problematic because osYield() typically triggers a software interrupt using the SVC instruction, which enters SVC_Handler, not directly PendSV. The system call mechanism (SVC) is used to safely request a context switch from thread mode by raising privilege and invoking SVC_Handler_Main, which sets the PendSV bit to defer the actual switch. However, invoking this mechanism from within an existing interrupt or context switch handler—where you’re already in privileged mode and possibly mid-switch—results in nested or redundant PendSV triggers, risking stack corruption, re-entrancy issues, and unpredictable behavior. Thus, context-switching logic must remain isolated and cannot safely invoke osYield() or any mechanism that leads back into the context-switch pipeline.\n',0,'s25'),('r57luo',5,2,'2025-07-14_15-18-42','Calling osYield() inside an interrupt context such as from PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler is unsafe because osYield() typically triggers a supervisor call (SVC), which is only meant to be executed in thread mode, not from within another exception. On a Cortex-M4 processor, attempting to execute an SVC while already handling an interrupt can result in improper exception stacking. This can lead to misaligned or corrupted stack frames, especially if the processor tries to push a new exception frame onto a stack that\'s already mid-transition. In such cases, the hardware detects an invalid state (e.g., corrupted stack alignment or improper return address) and raises a HardFault exception, which is a critical system failure used to indicate unrecoverable processor errors. This outcome is particularly dangerous because it can crash the system or lead to unpredictable behavior. Therefore, invoking osYield() and any SVC from within context-switching handlers is prohibited to avoid triggering these low-level architectural failures.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,3,'2025-07-14_15-00-06','Reducing the OS clock interval from 1ms to 0.1ms increases timing resolution and improves responsiveness, especially for real-time or delay-sensitive tasks. However, it also results in ten times more SysTick interrupts, significantly increasing CPU overhead, power consumption, and potentially causing timing instability or missed deadlines for operations that don\'t complete quickly enough. This tradeoff must be carefully considered, especially on resource-constrained embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,3,'2025-07-14_15-11-59','Reducing the OS clock interval from 1ms to 0.1ms increases timing resolution and improves responsiveness, especially for real-time or delay-sensitive tasks. However, it also results in ten times more SysTick interrupts, significantly increasing CPU overhead, power consumption, and potentially causing timing instability or missed deadlines for operations that don\'t complete quickly enough. This tradeoff must be carefully considered, especially on resource-constrained embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,3,'2025-07-14_15-18-42','Reducing the OS clock interval from 1ms to 0.1ms increases timing resolution and improves responsiveness, especially for real-time or delay-sensitive tasks. However, it also results in ten times more SysTick interrupts, significantly increasing CPU overhead, power consumption, and potentially causing timing instability or missed deadlines for operations that don\'t complete quickly enough. This tradeoff must be carefully considered, especially on resource-constrained embedded systems.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,4,'2025-07-14_15-00-06','Co-operative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily yield control (e.g., using osYield()), so poorly written threads can block others if they don’t yield. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking relies on a timer interrupt (like SysTick) to periodically force context switches, ensuring all threads get CPU time regardless of their behavior. From the OS perspective, co-operative switching is simpler as it happens at known points, while pre-emption requires the OS to handle asynchronous context saving and restoring, which is more complex and error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,4,'2025-07-14_15-11-59','Co-operative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily yield control (e.g., using osYield()), so poorly written threads can block others if they don’t yield. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking relies on a timer interrupt (like SysTick) to periodically force context switches, ensuring all threads get CPU time regardless of their behavior. From the OS perspective, co-operative switching is simpler as it happens at known points, while pre-emption requires the OS to handle asynchronous context saving and restoring, which is more complex and error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,4,'2025-07-14_15-18-42','Co-operative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily yield control (e.g., using osYield()), so poorly written threads can block others if they don’t yield. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking relies on a timer interrupt (like SysTick) to periodically force context switches, ensuring all threads get CPU time regardless of their behavior. From the OS perspective, co-operative switching is simpler as it happens at known points, while pre-emption requires the OS to handle asynchronous context saving and restoring, which is more complex and error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,5,'2025-07-14_15-00-06','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching work together because both ultimately trigger the same mechanism: setting the PendSV interrupt to perform a context switch. When a thread yields voluntarily or is preempted by the SysTick timer, the OS sets PendSV, and PendSV_Handler does the actual switching. Since both methods use the same infrastructure, they integrate seamlessly, allowing the system to support both cooperative and pre-emptive multitasking in a unified manner.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,5,'2025-07-14_15-11-59','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching work together because both ultimately trigger the same mechanism: setting the PendSV interrupt to perform a context switch. When a thread yields voluntarily or is preempted by the SysTick timer, the OS sets PendSV, and PendSV_Handler does the actual switching. Since both methods use the same infrastructure, they integrate seamlessly, allowing the system to support both cooperative and pre-emptive multitasking in a unified manner.\n',1,'s25'),('r57luo',5,5,'2025-07-14_15-18-42','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching work together because both ultimately trigger the same mechanism: setting the PendSV interrupt to perform a context switch. When a thread yields voluntarily or is preempted by the SysTick timer, the OS sets PendSV, and PendSV_Handler does the actual switching. Since both methods use the same infrastructure, they integrate seamlessly, allowing the system to support both cooperative and pre-emptive multitasking in a unified manner.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',1,1,'2025-06-04_15-14-47','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a collection of pre-written functions provided by STMicroelectronics that simplifies access to hardware components on a microcontroller. It abstracts away low-level hardware details, allowing developers to interact with peripherals like UART or GPIO without writing complex register-level code. This makes development faster and less error prone.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',1,2,'2025-06-04_15-14-47','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that sends a single character to the UART. It is required to connect the printf function to actual hardware output. Since printf is part of the standard C library and doesn\'t know how to talk to microcontroller hardware, it relies on __io_putchar to handle character-by-character output to the correct communication channel (UART2 in our case).\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',1,3,'2025-06-04_15-14-47','We used the debugger because some internal register values like MSP and PSP cannot be observed accurately through printf, especially before the code starts running fully. The debugger allows us to pause execution, inspect the state of registers, and validate memory configurations in real time. printf is limited to runtime output and doesn’t help in debugging low-level initialization issues.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',1,4,'2025-06-04_15-14-47','The stack allocation method treats the total stack memory as a large array. After obtaining the initial MSP value from address 0x0, we calculated a new PSP value by subtracting a predefined offset (0x400) to allocate space for the main stack. We needed the MSP initial value so we could determine the top of the stack region and place the PSP at a safe offset to avoid overlap. This ensures thread stacks can be spaced apart properly.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',1,5,'2025-06-04_15-14-47','We would need to define constants for THREAD_STACK_SIZE and compute each thread’s stack base address using the formula:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1)*THREAD_STACK_SIZE\nWe’d create an array or use macros to compute these addresses at compile-time. This allows each thread to get a unique stack starting point without overlapping, assuming the total memory is sufficient.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,1,'2025-06-25_15-45-35','When I set the PC register to the address of the print_continuously function using the jumpAssembly(void* fcn)function, execution immediately jumped to that function. This worked because the address of the function was passed into R0, and the assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 set the PC to that value. As a result, the processor began executing the print function continuously, demonstrating how changing PC directly can transfer control to any desired function.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,1,'2025-06-25_16-30-36','When I set the PC register to the address of the print_continuously function using the jumpAssembly(void* fcn)function, execution immediately jumped to that function. This worked because the address of the function was passed into R0, and the assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 set the PC to that value. As a result, the processor began executing the print function continuously, demonstrating how changing PC directly can transfer control to any desired function.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,1,'2025-06-25_19-15-29','When I set the PC register to the address of the print_continuously function using the jumpAssembly(void* fcn)function, execution immediately jumped to that function. This worked because the address of the function was passed into R0, and the assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 set the PC to that value. As a result, the processor began executing the print function continuously, demonstrating how changing PC directly can transfer control to any desired function.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,1,'2025-06-25_19-52-50','When I set the PC register to the address of the print_continuously function using the jumpAssembly(void* fcn)function, execution immediately jumped to that function. This worked because the address of the function was passed into R0, and the assembly instruction MOV PC, R0 set the PC to that value. As a result, the processor began executing the print function continuously, demonstrating how changing PC directly can transfer control to any desired function.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,2,'2025-06-25_15-45-35','A system call is a controlled way for user-level code to request services from the operating system. In this lab, system calls were triggered using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction with an 8-bit number identifying the request. This caused an exception, jumping to the SVC handler, where the specific call number was extracted and the appropriate C function was executed. It’s a safe and standardized way for switching into privileged mode to execute sensitive operations like starting a thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,2,'2025-06-25_16-30-36','A system call is a controlled way for user-level code to request services from the operating system. In this lab, system calls were triggered using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction with an 8-bit number identifying the request. This caused an exception, jumping to the SVC handler, where the specific call number was extracted and the appropriate C function was executed. It’s a safe and standardized way for switching into privileged mode to execute sensitive operations like starting a thread.\n',0,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,2,'2025-06-25_19-15-29','A system call is a special type of function call that allows user-level code to request services from the operating system running in privileged mode. Unlike regular function calls, a system call uses the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction to trigger an exception, which switches the processor into a higher-privileged mode to safely perform restricted tasks such as managing memory, handling I/O, or starting threads.\nIn this lab, we used system call functions in C that internally invoked the SVC instruction with a unique identifier. These acted as wrappers, abstracting away the low-level instruction and allowing us to call them like normal functions from user code. This structure highlights how system calls are function calls designed specifically to bridge user and kernel code securely.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,2,'2025-06-25_19-52-50','A system call is a special type of function call that allows user-level code to request services from the operating system running in privileged mode. Unlike regular function calls, a system call uses the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction to trigger an exception, which switches the processor into a higher-privileged mode to safely perform restricted tasks such as managing memory, handling I/O, or starting threads.\nIn this lab, we used system call functions in C that internally invoked the SVC instruction with a unique identifier. These acted as wrappers, abstracting away the low-level instruction and allowing us to call them like normal functions from user code. This structure highlights how system calls are function calls designed specifically to bridge user and kernel code securely.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,3,'2025-06-25_15-45-35','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing it with the expected values for all the CPU registers, as if the thread had already been running. This includes placing values like xPSR, PC, LR, and general-purpose registers in the correct order so that when the CPU pops them off during context restoration, it enters the thread function seamlessly. Register values are stored on the stack so that the processor can restore a thread’s exact execution context, which is essential for multitasking and thread switching.\n',0,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,3,'2025-06-25_16-30-36','Before setting up a thread’s stack, we must first allocate valid memory for it. This typically involves declaring a fixed-size array in memory (e.g., uint32_t threadStack[STACK_SIZE];) which serves as the thread’s private stack space. We then initialize a stack pointer (stackptr) to the top of this array, since the ARM Cortex-M stack grows downward.\nOnce we have this valid stack location, we begin \"setting up the thread’s stack\" by manually writing the values that the CPU would expect if the thread were resuming from an interrupt. This includes pushing special registers like xPSR and PC, followed by LR, general-purpose registers R12 to R0, and finally R11 through R4 in the correct order. These values simulate the CPU state and enable the processor to load the thread’s context during the first switch. Without allocating memory properly at the start, any attempt to write these values would lead to invalid memory access and unpredictable behavior.\nIn summary, stack setup begins with allocating a memory block, positioning the stack pointer, and then filling it with the simulated CPU context to allow the thread to start as though it had always been running.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,3,'2025-06-25_19-15-29','Before setting up a thread’s stack, we must first allocate valid memory for it. This typically involves declaring a fixed-size array in memory (e.g., uint32_t threadStack[STACK_SIZE];) which serves as the thread’s private stack space. We then initialize a stack pointer (stackptr) to the top of this array, since the ARM Cortex-M stack grows downward.\nOnce we have this valid stack location, we begin \"setting up the thread’s stack\" by manually writing the values that the CPU would expect if the thread were resuming from an interrupt. This includes pushing special registers like xPSR and PC, followed by LR, general-purpose registers R12 to R0, and finally R11 through R4 in the correct order. These values simulate the CPU state and enable the processor to load the thread’s context during the first switch. Without allocating memory properly at the start, any attempt to write these values would lead to invalid memory access and unpredictable behavior.\nIn summary, stack setup begins with allocating a memory block, positioning the stack pointer, and then filling it with the simulated CPU context to allow the thread to start as though it had always been running.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,3,'2025-06-25_19-52-50','Before setting up a thread’s stack, we must first allocate valid memory for it. This typically involves declaring a fixed-size array in memory (e.g., uint32_t threadStack[STACK_SIZE];) which serves as the thread’s private stack space. We then initialize a stack pointer (stackptr) to the top of this array, since the ARM Cortex-M stack grows downward.\nOnce we have this valid stack location, we begin \"setting up the thread’s stack\" by manually writing the values that the CPU would expect if the thread were resuming from an interrupt. This includes pushing special registers like xPSR and PC, followed by LR, general-purpose registers R12 to R0, and finally R11 through R4 in the correct order. These values simulate the CPU state and enable the processor to load the thread’s context during the first switch. Without allocating memory properly at the start, any attempt to write these values would lead to invalid memory access and unpredictable behavior.\nIn summary, stack setup begins with allocating a memory block, positioning the stack pointer, and then filling it with the simulated CPU context to allow the thread to start as though it had always been running.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,4,'2025-06-25_15-45-35','If we don’t set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) before running the thread, the CPU doesn’t know where the thread’s stack is located. This leads to invalid memory accesses when the system tries to restore register values or execute from an undefined address, resulting in a hard fault. By using __set_PSP(stackptr), we ensure that the thread uses its correctly initialized stack, allowing the CPU to load the context safely and start the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,4,'2025-06-25_16-30-36','If we don’t set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) before running the thread, the CPU doesn’t know where the thread’s stack is located. This leads to invalid memory accesses when the system tries to restore register values or execute from an undefined address, resulting in a hard fault. By using __set_PSP(stackptr), we ensure that the thread uses its correctly initialized stack, allowing the CPU to load the context safely and start the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,4,'2025-06-25_19-15-29','If we don’t set the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) before running the thread, the CPU doesn’t know where the thread’s stack is located. This leads to invalid memory accesses when the system tries to restore register values or execute from an undefined address, resulting in a hard fault. By using __set_PSP(stackptr), we ensure that the thread uses its correctly initialized stack, allowing the CPU to load the context safely and start the thread function.\n',0,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,4,'2025-06-25_19-52-50','In the Cortex-M4 thread execution model, it is essential that threads use the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) instead of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). The MSP is reserved for privileged code such as the operating system or exception handlers, while the PSP is specifically designed for user threads. This separation protects kernel operations from being corrupted by thread behavior and enforces privilege boundaries.\nWhen a thread is created, a region of memory (i.e., a thread stack) is allocated, and its stack is manually initialized with the required register values. For the thread to use this memory properly during execution, the PSP must be explicitly set to point to the top of this initialized thread stack using __set_PSP(stackptr). This ensures that when the CPU starts executing the thread in thread mode, it accesses the correct memory region for local variables, function calls, and returns.\nWithout setting the PSP to the thread\'s stack address, the thread would either crash or operate on an invalid memory region. Thus, properly configuring the PSP is critical for correct thread execution and safe separation between the OS (using MSP) and user threads (using PSP).\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,5,'2025-06-25_15-45-35','If the thread function returned, the processor would attempt to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. However, because the thread was not started using a traditional function call (it was set up manually), there is no valid return address. This would lead to undefined behavior and likely cause the processor to fetch an invalid instruction, resulting in a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,5,'2025-06-25_16-30-36','If the thread function returned, the processor would attempt to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. However, because the thread was not started using a traditional function call (it was set up manually), there is no valid return address. This would lead to undefined behavior and likely cause the processor to fetch an invalid instruction, resulting in a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,5,'2025-06-25_19-15-29','If the thread function returned, the processor would attempt to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. However, because the thread was not started using a traditional function call (it was set up manually), there is no valid return address. This would lead to undefined behavior and likely cause the processor to fetch an invalid instruction, resulting in a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',2,5,'2025-06-25_19-52-50','If the thread function returned, the processor would attempt to pop a return address from the stack and jump to it. However, because the thread was not started using a traditional function call (it was set up manually), there is no valid return address. This would lead to undefined behavior and likely cause the processor to fetch an invalid instruction, resulting in a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,1,'2025-06-25_19-48-28','Each .c file is compiled individually into an object file (.o) by the compiler. These object files contain machine code and metadata, but not yet a complete program. Once all .c files are compiled, the linker combines the object files into a single executable. The linker resolves function calls, global variables, and memory addresses across files to ensure everything is connected properly. The result is a binary that can be loaded onto the target hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,1,'2025-06-25_20-16-01','The process of building a program from multiple .c files involves three key steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking.\nPreprocessing: Before compilation, each .c file is passed through the preprocessor. This step handles all preprocessor directives such as #include, #define, and #ifdef, effectively expanding macros and inserting the contents of header files. It generates a \"pure\" C version of the code, with all directives resolved.\nCompiling: The preprocessed C code is then compiled into an object file (.o), which contains machine code and symbol information, but it is not yet executable on its own. Each .c file is compiled separately.\nLinking: Finally, the linker combines all object files into a single executable. It resolves references between files, such as function calls or global variables declared in one file and used in another. The result is a complete binary ready to be loaded and executed on the microcontroller.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,1,'2025-06-26_13-38-28','The process of building a program from multiple .c files involves three key steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking.\nPreprocessing: Before compilation, each .c file is passed through the preprocessor. This step handles all preprocessor directives such as #include, #define, and #ifdef, effectively expanding macros and inserting the contents of header files. It generates a \"pure\" C version of the code, with all directives resolved.\nCompiling: The preprocessed C code is then compiled into an object file (.o), which contains machine code and symbol information, but it is not yet executable on its own. Each .c file is compiled separately.\nLinking: Finally, the linker combines all object files into a single executable. It resolves references between files, such as function calls or global variables declared in one file and used in another. The result is a complete binary ready to be loaded and executed on the microcontroller.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,2,'2025-06-25_19-48-28','Including a .c file directly using #include causes the compiler to copy-paste its contents into the current file, which can lead to multiple definitions of the same function or variable if used in multiple places. This breaks modularity and defeats the purpose of separate compilation. Instead, .h header files should be included to expose function declarations and shared constants, while the .c files are compiled separately and linked together later.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,2,'2025-06-25_20-16-01','Including a .c file directly using #include causes the compiler to copy-paste its contents into the current file, which can lead to multiple definitions of the same function or variable if used in multiple places. This breaks modularity and defeats the purpose of separate compilation. Instead, .h header files should be included to expose function declarations and shared constants, while the .c files are compiled separately and linked together later.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,2,'2025-06-26_13-38-28','Including a .c file directly using #include causes the compiler to copy-paste its contents into the current file, which can lead to multiple definitions of the same function or variable if used in multiple places. This breaks modularity and defeats the purpose of separate compilation. Instead, .h header files should be included to expose function declarations and shared constants, while the .c files are compiled separately and linked together later.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,3,'2025-06-25_19-48-28','To set up and start a thread as a user:\nCall osKernelInitialize() to prepare the kernel and stack pool.\nUse osCreateThread() and pass in your thread function. This sets up the stack and prepares the thread context.\nOnce all threads are created, call osKernelStart() to start executing the first thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,3,'2025-06-25_20-16-01','To set up and start a thread as a user:\nCall osKernelInitialize() to prepare the kernel and stack pool.\nUse osCreateThread() and pass in your thread function. This sets up the stack and prepares the thread context.\nOnce all threads are created, call osKernelStart() to start executing the first thread.\n',0,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,3,'2025-06-26_13-38-28','To set up and start a thread from the user\'s perspective:\nWrite a thread function: The user must create a function that contains the code they want the thread to run. This function should not take any arguments or return anything.\nInitialize the kernel: Call osKernelInitialize() to prepare the system for thread creation. This sets up the internal stack pool and thread management structures.\nCreate the thread: Call osCreateThread() and pass in the thread function you wrote. This tells the kernel to allocate stack space and prepare the thread\'s execution context.\nStart the kernel: Call osKernelStart() to begin thread execution. The kernel will switch context and start running the first thread.\nBy following these steps, the user provides both the logic (in the thread function) and the instructions to the kernel for how and when to run that logic.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,4,'2025-06-25_19-48-28','The stack pool is a block of preallocated memory that the kernel uses to assign each thread its own private stack. When a new thread is created, the kernel takes a portion of the stack pool and assigns it to that thread. In future labs, this allows the OS to support multiple threads by allocating a separate stack for each one from the shared pool, enabling safe and independent execution for each thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,4,'2025-06-25_20-16-01','The stack pool is a block of preallocated memory that the kernel uses to assign each thread its own private stack. When a new thread is created, the kernel takes a portion of the stack pool and assigns it to that thread. In future labs, this allows the OS to support multiple threads by allocating a separate stack for each one from the shared pool, enabling safe and independent execution for each thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,4,'2025-06-26_13-38-28','The stack pool is a block of preallocated memory that the kernel uses to assign each thread its own private stack. When a new thread is created, the kernel takes a portion of the stack pool and assigns it to that thread. In future labs, this allows the OS to support multiple threads by allocating a separate stack for each one from the shared pool, enabling safe and independent execution for each thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,5,'2025-06-25_19-48-28','The \"user\" code consists of application-level logic — what the programmer writes to run on top of the OS — and belongs in main.c. The \"kernel\" code contains low-level OS functionality such as thread management, stack setup, and system call handling, and belongs in kernel.c and kernel.h. This separation is important because it keeps the user interface simple and consistent, hides the complexity of the OS, and allows the kernel to evolve without affecting user applications.\n',0,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,5,'2025-06-25_20-16-01','The user is responsible for writing application-level code, such as thread functions and calls to kernel APIs. This code goes into main.c and uses functions like osKernelInitialize, osCreateThread, and osKernelStart to set up and start threads. The user focuses only on the logic of what the program should do.\nThe kernel, on the other hand, manages low-level operations like thread scheduling, stack management, context switching, and interfacing with hardware. This code belongs in kernel.c and kernel.h and is hidden from the user to simplify usage and prevent errors.\nThis separation is important because it allows users to build applications without needing to understand or modify the complex internals of the OS. It enforces modularity and abstraction — users rely on clean, well-defined kernel APIs, while the kernel safely handles all the low-level operations behind the scenes.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',3,5,'2025-06-26_13-38-28','The user is responsible for writing application-level code, such as thread functions and calls to kernel APIs. This code goes into main.c and uses functions like osKernelInitialize, osCreateThread, and osKernelStart to set up and start threads. The user focuses only on the logic of what the program should do.\nThe kernel, on the other hand, manages low-level operations like thread scheduling, stack management, context switching, and interfacing with hardware. This code belongs in kernel.c and kernel.h and is hidden from the user to simplify usage and prevent errors.\nThis separation is important because it allows users to build applications without needing to understand or modify the complex internals of the OS. It enforces modularity and abstraction — users rely on clean, well-defined kernel APIs, while the kernel safely handles all the low-level operations behind the scenes.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',4,1,'2025-07-30_16-16-12','In a round-robin scheduler using a queue, each time a thread yields, it is removed from the front of the queue and reinserted at the back. The next thread to run is always the one at the front of the queue. This matches the round-robin model perfectly since it cycles through threads in the order they were added. Compared to an array, a queue automatically handles wrapping around, reducing the need for index management. Queues are also more flexible for dynamic thread insertion and removal, which is useful for more advanced schedulers.\n',0,'s25'),('r8rahman',4,1,'2025-07-30_16-26-45','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by placing each thread into the back of the queue when it\'s created or after it yields. When a context switch occurs, the scheduler removes the thread at the front of the queue (i.e., the currently running thread), executes it, and then reinserts it at the back after it yields. This ensures all threads get equal time in a fair, cyclic order.\nInterestingly, the array-based round-robin approach we used in Lab 4 already behaves like a queue: it cycles through threads in order by incrementing an index and wrapping around using modulo. So functionally, both implementations achieve the same outcome.\nHowever, switching to an actual queue introduces additional overhead. It requires dynamic memory allocation or pointer manipulation for enqueue/dequeue operations, and managing linked nodes or circular buffer pointers adds complexity. While a queue may offer better support for dynamic thread creation/deletion in more complex systems, for simple round-robin scheduling with a fixed number of threads, an array is more efficient and less error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',4,2,'2025-07-30_16-16-12','The PendSV_Handler is an interrupt that is triggered when a context switch is needed. When it runs, it first saves the current thread’s context—specifically the values in registers R4–R11 and the current stack pointer (PSP)—into its corresponding thread control block (TCB). Then, it updates the currentThread index to point to the next thread in the scheduling order and loads the next thread\'s saved stack pointer and registers. This effectively restores the new thread\'s context and allows it to resume execution from where it last yielded.\n',0,'s25'),('r8rahman',4,2,'2025-07-30_16-26-45','The PendSV_Handler performs the actual context switch between threads. It starts by reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread’s active stack, which contains the saved register values (R4–R11). These registers are pushed onto the stack by the assembly stub before PendSV_Handler is invoked.\nThe function then saves the PSP value (minus space for the pushed registers) into the current thread’s TCB (Thread Control Block). This tells the OS where to resume this thread from in the future.\nNext, the osSched() function is called. This scheduler is responsible for selecting the next thread to run, typically using a round-robin algorithm. It updates currentThread to point to the next thread in the thread array.\nOnce the next thread is chosen, the PSP is set to that thread’s saved stack pointer from its TCB. This prepares the processor to resume execution from where the thread last yielded or started.\nFinally, when the handler returns, the pushed R4–R11 values are popped from the new thread’s stack, and execution resumes within the selected thread.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',4,3,'2025-07-30_16-16-12','This approach causes starvation for lower-priority tasks because the highest-priority thread will continuously run as long as it’s ready. In a cooperative multitasking system, if a high-priority thread never yields, the others never get a chance to execute. Without preemption or fairness controls, priority-based scheduling can make the system unfair and unresponsive to less critical, but still necessary, threads.\n',0,'s25'),('r8rahman',4,3,'2025-07-30_16-26-45','Modifying the scheduler to always pick the highest-priority task causes the scheduler to become biased. Once it selects the thread with the highest priority, it will continue to choose that same thread every time, even if that thread yields. That’s because the scheduler logic no longer rotates between threads—it only compares priorities and picks the maximum.\nAs a result, lower-priority threads are starved: they are never chosen by the scheduler because a higher-priority thread is always ready to run. The issue isn’t just about cooperative yielding anymore—it\'s that the scheduler\'s logic is fundamentally flawed for fairness, because it ignores all threads except the one with the maximum value. This violates the principle of fair resource allocation and breaks multitasking, especially if the top-priority thread never blocks.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',4,4,'2025-07-30_16-16-12','Calling C functions improves readability and maintainability. Writing complex logic in C is easier and less error-prone than in assembly, and it allows the use of higher-level abstractions and type checking. However, the downside is that C functions may introduce overhead or unintended side effects due to compiler optimizations or calling conventions. Assembly offers more control and is sometimes necessary for precise low-level operations like context switching. Overall, using a mix—as we did—provides a good balance between clarity and control.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',4,4,'2025-07-30_16-26-45','Calling C functions improves readability and maintainability. Writing complex logic in C is easier and less error-prone than in assembly, and it allows the use of higher-level abstractions and type checking. However, the downside is that C functions may introduce overhead or unintended side effects due to compiler optimizations or calling conventions. Assembly offers more control and is sometimes necessary for precise low-level operations like context switching. Overall, using a mix—as we did—provides a good balance between clarity and control.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',4,5,'2025-07-30_16-16-12','Yes, the OS would still work, but it would only run that one thread. When osYield is called, the context switch mechanism is triggered, but since there are no other threads to switch to, it would just resume the same thread. The round-robin scheduler would cycle back to the same thread, making the yielding redundant. This setup is functionally correct but doesn\'t demonstrate multitasking since there\'s only one active task.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',4,5,'2025-07-30_16-26-45','Yes, the OS would still work, but it would only run that one thread. When osYield is called, the context switch mechanism is triggered, but since there are no other threads to switch to, it would just resume the same thread. The round-robin scheduler would cycle back to the same thread, making the yielding redundant. This setup is functionally correct but doesn\'t demonstrate multitasking since there\'s only one active task.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',5,1,'2025-07-30_17-27-38','The user creates a thread function with a void* argument and prepares input data—either a struct or primitive type—to pass into the thread. When calling osCreateThread, the user passes a pointer to this data. The OS is responsible for placing this pointer into register R0 of the thread’s initial stack frame during setup. This works because R0 is the standard register used for the first function argument in the ARM Procedure Call Standard (APCS), so when the thread starts running, the pointer is automatically passed into the function. Inside the thread, the user can safely cast and dereference it to access the original data.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',5,1,'2025-07-30_17-55-01','The user creates a thread function with a void* argument and prepares input data—either a struct or primitive type—to pass into the thread. When calling osCreateThread, the user passes a pointer to this data. The OS is responsible for placing this pointer into register R0 of the thread’s initial stack frame during setup. This works because R0 is the standard register used for the first function argument in the ARM Procedure Call Standard (APCS), so when the thread starts running, the pointer is automatically passed into the function. Inside the thread, the user can safely cast and dereference it to access the original data.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',5,2,'2025-07-30_17-27-38','Calling osYield within a context-switching handler would cause nested or recursive context switches. osYield triggers a system call, which initiates SVC_Handler and schedules a PendSV interrupt. If osYield is called again within SVC_Handler or PendSV_Handler, it can corrupt the stack and disrupt the deterministic nature of context switching. This leads to unpredictable behavior, such as stack overflows, missed interrupts, or threads resuming in invalid states. These handlers are part of the low-level scheduling mechanism and must remain atomic and minimal.\n',0,'s25'),('r8rahman',5,2,'2025-07-30_17-55-01','Calling osYield inside any of the context-switching handlers such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler is dangerous because it triggers another system call, which initiates a nested interrupt. Since these handlers are already operating within an interrupt context, a nested call to osYield leads to recursive use of the stack before the previous context has been fully saved or restored.\nIn an ARM Cortex-M4 system, this type of nested interrupt behavior can corrupt the stack and overflow it, especially since ISR stacks are limited. This stack corruption can lead to a hardfault, which is a critical system-level fault that occurs when the processor encounters an unrecoverable error, such as accessing invalid memory or exceeding stack bounds.\nUnlike vague “unpredictable behavior,” a hardfault is a well-defined, terminal exception on ARM systems that halts the program and requires a manual reset. Mentioning it is essential because it highlights the severity of the consequence—this isn\'t just instability; it\'s a total failure of the system’s execution environment.\nTherefore, osYield must never be called within any context-switching interrupt handler. These handlers should remain minimal, atomic, and should only manipulate already-established scheduling logic.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',5,3,'2025-07-30_17-27-38','Reducing the clock interval to 0.1 ms increases system responsiveness and allows finer-grained scheduling, which is useful for time-sensitive applications like audio processing or robotics. However, it comes at a cost. The CPU spends more time handling SysTick interrupts, increasing overhead and reducing time available for actual thread work. It may also introduce jitter and instability if the system can\'t complete thread operations between interrupts. Additionally, such fine timing isn\'t needed for most applications and makes debugging harder due to faster context switches.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',5,3,'2025-07-30_17-55-01','Reducing the clock interval to 0.1 ms increases system responsiveness and allows finer-grained scheduling, which is useful for time-sensitive applications like audio processing or robotics. However, it comes at a cost. The CPU spends more time handling SysTick interrupts, increasing overhead and reducing time available for actual thread work. It may also introduce jitter and instability if the system can\'t complete thread operations between interrupts. Additionally, such fine timing isn\'t needed for most applications and makes debugging harder due to faster context switches.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',5,4,'2025-07-30_17-27-38','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires the programmer to explicitly call osYield() in their thread to allow others to run. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking automatically switches threads after a certain time slice, even if the thread doesn’t yield. From the OS side, cooperative multitasking is simpler—it only switches context when the user requests it. Pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to track run times using a system timer (SysTick_Handler) and automatically trigger a context switch via PendSV_Handler when a thread\'s time slice expires. Pre-emption adds complexity but improves fairness and responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',5,4,'2025-07-30_17-55-01','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires the programmer to explicitly call osYield() in their thread to allow others to run. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking automatically switches threads after a certain time slice, even if the thread doesn’t yield. From the OS side, cooperative multitasking is simpler—it only switches context when the user requests it. Pre-emptive multitasking requires the OS to track run times using a system timer (SysTick_Handler) and automatically trigger a context switch via PendSV_Handler when a thread\'s time slice expires. Pre-emption adds complexity but improves fairness and responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',5,5,'2025-07-30_17-27-38','Both cooperative (osYield) and pre-emptive (timer-based) context switches use the same core mechanism—triggering the PendSV_Handler to perform the actual context switch. The distinction lies in how the switch is requested: osYieldmanually sets the PendSV interrupt, while SysTick_Handler does so based on a countdown timer. Because they both ultimately call the same scheduler and restore stack context via PendSV_Handler, they can coexist. A thread can yield voluntarily, or it can be pre-empted when its time slice expires. The OS resets the runtime counter in both cases, ensuring fair scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('r8rahman',5,5,'2025-07-30_17-55-01','Both cooperative (osYield) and pre-emptive (timer-based) context switches use the same core mechanism—triggering the PendSV_Handler to perform the actual context switch. The distinction lies in how the switch is requested: osYieldmanually sets the PendSV interrupt, while SysTick_Handler does so based on a countdown timer. Because they both ultimately call the same scheduler and restore stack context via PendSV_Handler, they can coexist. A thread can yield voluntarily, or it can be pre-empted when its time slice expires. The OS resets the runtime counter in both cases, ensuring fair scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,1,'2025-05-14_18-27-16','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction layer, and works to provide an interface between the actual hardware (in this case, the microprocessor), and the code that is being written to control the microprocessor. So allowing the higher level of code to interact with and control the hardware. It is used in a general embedded project so that the code being written does not have to written for the hardware specifically. HAL also provides some built in functions to be used too and makes the development process much faster.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,1,'2025-05-15_10-33-53','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction layer, and works to provide an interface between the actual hardware (in this case, the microprocessor), and the code that is being written to control the microprocessor. So allowing the higher level of code to interact with and control the hardware. It is used in a general embedded project so that the code being written does not have to written for the hardware specifically. HAL also provides some built in functions to be used too and makes the development process much faster.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,1,'2025-05-15_11-28-36','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction layer, and works to provide an interface between the actual hardware (in this case, the microprocessor), and the code that is being written to control the microprocessor. So allowing the higher level of code to interact with and control the hardware. It is used in a general embedded project so that the code being written does not have to written for the hardware specifically. HAL also provides some built in functions to be used too and makes the development process much faster.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,1,'2025-05-15_14-51-35','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction layer, and works to provide an interface between the actual hardware (in this case, the microprocessor), and the code that is being written to control the microprocessor. So allowing the higher level of code to interact with and control the hardware. It is used in a general embedded project so that the code being written does not have to written for the hardware specifically. HAL also provides some built in functions to be used too and makes the development process much faster.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,2,'2025-05-14_18-27-16','The function __io_putchar is used to print the input from the printf function. Using C on a computer, and calling printf, prints the input onto the screen/console. However, in the embedded system of the microprocessor, there is no screen or console, so the system does not know where to print the input. This function is used as the in between to allow the input of printf to be printed. The function is called for each character in the printf input and sends the character to the specified port (in this case UART2), to be printed.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,2,'2025-05-15_10-33-53','The function __io_putchar is used to print the input from the printf function. Using C on a computer, and calling printf, prints the input onto the screen/console. However, in the embedded system of the microprocessor, there is no screen or console, so the system does not know where to print the input. This function is used as the in between to allow the input of printf to be printed. The function is called for each character in the printf input and sends the character to the specified port (in this case UART2), to be printed. A developer may need to change this function as it refers to the way that the hardware (microprocessor) can send information to the connect computer. Having this helper function is beneficial as it allows the use of a variety of hardware rather than relying on a specific one.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,2,'2025-05-15_11-28-36','The function __io_putchar is used to print the input from the printf function. Using C on a computer, and calling printf, prints the input onto the screen/console. However, in the embedded system of the microprocessor, there is no screen or console, so the system does not know where to print the input. This function is used as the in between to allow the input of printf to be printed. The function is called for each character in the printf input and sends the character to the specified port (in this case UART2), to be printed. A developer may need to change this function as it refers to the way that the hardware (microprocessor) can send information to the connect computer. Having this helper function is beneficial as it allows the use of a variety of hardware rather than relying on a specific one.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,2,'2025-05-15_14-51-35','The function __io_putchar is used to print the input from the printf function. Using C on a computer, and calling printf, prints the input onto the screen/console. However, in the embedded system of the microprocessor, there is no screen or console, so the system does not know where to print the input. This function is used as the in between to allow the input of printf to be printed. The function is called for each character in the printf input and sends the character to the specified port (in this case UART2), to be printed. A developer may need to change this function as it refers to the way that the hardware (microprocessor) can send information to the connect computer. Having this helper function is beneficial as it allows the use of a variety of hardware rather than relying on a specific one.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,3,'2025-05-14_18-27-16','The debugger allows the value actual value of the stack to be observed, rather than just the end value. This is beneficial to understand what exactly is happening behind the scenes to achieve the end goal. In this lab we are looking to see the in between steps rather than the end value. Printf would only just provide the end value\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,3,'2025-05-15_10-33-53','The debugger allows the value actual value of the stack to be observed, rather than just the end value. The registers window shows this in more depth. In this window, registers can be seen representing specific memory, and using the debugger allows the user to see what is being stored in that memory location at a specified break point. This is beneficial to understand what exactly is happening behind the scenes to achieve the end goal. In this lab we are looking to see the in between steps rather than the end value. Printf would only just provide the end value. This can be compared to using the registers, where the inbetween steps of the memory is not seen from printf. For example, if the variable value got overwritten somewhere in the code, and by using printf this only prints the overwritten value, but by using debugger and registers, it allows the user to find exactly where their mistake occurred.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,3,'2025-05-15_11-28-36','The debugger allows the value actual value of the stack to be observed, rather than just the end value. The registers window shows this in more depth. In this window, registers can be seen representing specific memory, and using the debugger allows the user to see what is being stored in that memory location at a specified break point. This is beneficial to understand what exactly is happening behind the scenes to achieve the end goal. In this lab we are looking to see the in between steps rather than the end value. Printf would only just provide the end value. This can be compared to using the registers, where the inbetween steps of the memory is not seen from printf. For example, if the variable value got overwritten somewhere in the code, and by using printf this only prints the overwritten value, but by using debugger and registers, it allows the user to find exactly where their mistake occurred.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,3,'2025-05-15_14-51-35','The debugger allows the value actual value of the stack to be observed, rather than just the end value. The registers window shows this in more depth. In this window, registers can be seen representing specific memory, and using the debugger allows the user to see what is being stored in that memory location at a specified break point. This is beneficial to understand what exactly is happening behind the scenes to achieve the end goal. In this lab we are looking to see the in between steps rather than the end value. Printf would only just provide the end value. This can be compared to using the registers, where the inbetween steps of the memory is not seen from printf. For example, if the variable value got overwritten somewhere in the code, and by using printf this only prints the overwritten value, but by using debugger and registers, it allows the user to find exactly where their mistake occurred.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,4,'2025-05-14_18-27-16','In this specific case, the stack allocation grows downwards. Starting the stack at the initial value of MSP_init_val and continues by growing the stack. Since we have the value for MSP_init_val and know the main stack size, this gives us enough information to calculate PSP_val (the next stack value). This can be done by taking the MSP_init_val and minus the main stack size (since the stack grows downward). The initial location of MSP is needed, as depending on the use it may be defined differently depending on the use.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,4,'2025-05-15_10-33-53','In this specific case, the stack allocation grows downwards. Starting the stack at the initial value of MSP_init_val and continues by growing the stack. Since we have the value for MSP_init_val and know the main stack size, this gives us enough information to calculate PSP_val (the next stack value). This can be done by taking the MSP_init_val and minus the main stack size (since the stack grows downward). The initial location of MSP is needed, as depending on the use it may be defined differently depending on the use. MSP_init_val is important in ensuring that no overlapping regions are being defined for PSP, as MSP_init_val is a fixed value. Hardware automatically sets up MSP_init_val on reset.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,4,'2025-05-15_11-28-36','In this specific case, the stack allocation grows downwards. Starting the stack at the initial value of MSP_init_val and continues by growing the stack. Since we have the value for MSP_init_val and know the main stack size, this gives us enough information to calculate PSP_val (the next stack value). This can be done by taking the MSP_init_val and minus the main stack size (since the stack grows downward). The initial location of MSP is needed, as depending on the use it may be defined differently depending on the use. MSP_init_val is important in ensuring that no overlapping regions are being defined for PSP, as MSP_init_val is a fixed value. Hardware automatically sets up MSP_init_val on reset.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,4,'2025-05-15_14-51-35','In this specific case, the stack allocation grows downwards. Starting the stack at the initial value of MSP_init_val and continues by growing the stack. Since we have the value for MSP_init_val and know the main stack size, this gives us enough information to calculate PSP_val (the next stack value). This can be done by taking the MSP_init_val and minus the main stack size (since the stack grows downward). The initial location of MSP is needed, as depending on the use it may be defined differently depending on the use. MSP_init_val is important in ensuring that no overlapping regions are being defined for PSP, as MSP_init_val is a fixed value. Hardware automatically sets up MSP_init_val on reset.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,5,'2025-05-14_18-27-16','To make this work, the code would need to allocate memory to the stack for the number of new stacks desired. The PSP_val would have to be defined as the starting point for each individual new stack. This allows for the desired number of stacks to be created and defines them each to a unique memory address, so they can be accessed when needed.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,5,'2025-05-15_10-33-53','To make this work, the code would need to allocate memory to the stack for the number of new stacks desired. A fixed value would be needed (MSP_init_val), allowing each stack to be properly defined and aligned. We also need NUM_STACK for the number of desired stacks and then STACK_SIZE for the size of each stack. The STACK_START(1) = MSP_init_val  - ((i+1) * STACK_SIZE. The stack would start at the STACK_START(i) beginning memory address. As long as MSP_init_val is a multiple of the STACK_SIZE byte, then all the stacks will be properly aligned.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,5,'2025-05-15_11-28-36','To make this work, the code would need to allocate memory to the stack for the number of new stacks desired. A fixed value would be needed (MSP_init_val), allowing each stack to be properly defined and aligned. We also need NUM_STACK for the number of desired stacks and then STACK_SIZE for the size of each stack. The same formula used in the lab can be slightly modified, as the original formula, provides the memory based on the starting value and if there is any previously defined memory allocated in the stack. This formula can be modified to instead of defining a memory in the stack, to defining different thread stack. This means the defining of the variable to represent, is not representing just one stack, but a thread stack. This carves out memory for each individually defined thread stack since all stacks are created this way, a maximum value of the thread stack can be defined (referenced in the original formula), so that when allocating memory in the thread stack, a check can be performed to ensure that the thread stack does not exceed the maximum specified. If the thread stack is full while trying to allocating more memory, a potential solution is adding to the next available space in the following defined thread stack, or even just returning a potential statement saying “This thread stack is full.” To inform the user.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',1,5,'2025-05-15_14-51-35','To make this work, the code would need to allocate memory to the stack for the number of new stacks desired. A fixed value would be needed (MSP_init_val), allowing each stack to be properly defined and aligned. We also need NUM_STACK for the number of desired stacks and then STACK_SIZE for the size of each stack. The same formula used in the lab can be slightly modified, as the original formula, provides the memory based on the starting value and if there is any previously defined memory allocated in the stack. This formula can be modified to instead of defining a memory in the stack, to defining different thread stack. This means the defining of the variable to represent, is not representing just one stack, but a thread stack. By using this method, it offsets the newly defined stack location in respect to the existing stack locations and thus creates memory locations for each newly defined stack location. By offsetting by the same amount as the maximum stack size, this ensures that every single stack is properly aligned by the multiple of the maximum stack size. This carves out memory for each individually defined thread stack since all stacks are created this way, a maximum value of the thread stack can be defined (referenced in the original formula), so that when allocating memory in the thread stack, a check can be performed to ensure that the thread stack does not exceed the maximum specified. If the thread stack is full while trying to allocating more memory, a potential solution is adding to the next available space in the following defined thread stack, or even just returning a potential statement saying “This thread stack is full.” To inform the user.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,1,'2025-06-01_13-56-54','After changing PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the output was actually the same as just calling the print continuously function directly. However, there’s more to it than meets the surface. The function jumpAssembly takes the input argument of the address of the print continuously function. The jumpAssembly then takes this address, stores it in a local variable and then moves it into the PC, which then provides the continuous output.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,1,'2025-06-01_14-34-36','After changing PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the output was actually the same as just calling the print continuously function directly. However, there’s more to it than meets the surface. The function jumpAssembly takes the input argument of the address of the print continuously function. The jumpAssembly then takes this address, stores it in a local variable and then moves it into the PC, which then provides the continuous output. In retrospect to directly changing the instructions to hold the PC address, allows the processor to immediately carry out this instruction, instead of having waiting time in between to allow for the process of fetching the information, storing and moving. In this specific case, the time difference between both methods, are insignificant, but with larger codes/processors, the time difference may be a significant element to be considered.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,1,'2025-06-01_14-54-07','After changing PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the output was actually the same as just calling the print continuously function directly. However, there’s more to it than meets the surface. The function jumpAssembly takes the input argument of the address of the print continuously function. The jumpAssembly then takes this address, stores it in a local variable and then moves it into the PC, which then provides the continuous output. In retrospect to directly changing the instructions to hold the PC address, allows the processor to immediately carry out this instruction, instead of having waiting time in between to allow for the process of fetching the information, storing and moving. In this specific case, the time difference between both methods, are insignificant, but with larger codes/processors, the time difference may be a significant element to be considered.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,1,'2025-06-02_10-49-30','After changing PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the output was actually the same as just calling the print continuously function directly. However, there’s more to it than meets the surface. The function jumpAssembly takes the input argument of the address of the print continuously function. The jumpAssembly then takes this address, stores it in a local variable and then moves it into the PC, which then provides the continuous output. In retrospect to directly changing the instructions to hold the PC address, allows the processor to immediately carry out this instruction, instead of having waiting time in between to allow for the process of fetching the information, storing and moving. In this specific case, the time difference between both methods, are insignificant, but with larger codes/processors, the time difference may be a significant element to be considered.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,1,'2025-06-02_11-00-49','After changing PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, the output was actually the same as just calling the print continuously function directly. However, there’s more to it than meets the surface. The function jumpAssembly takes the input argument of the address of the print continuously 