e SVC instruction then must be specifically defined and told what the purpose and what happens after being called. A benefit to using this, is to be able to name the function a useful and appropriate name, while the SVC instruction is an arbitrary number value. So every time that SVC instruction needs to be called, instead of having to just know what number what does, the function that describes what it does can be used. System calls can be used at the bridge between the kernel and user code, by making users use the system call rather than directly using the SVC instruction. This is done to protect hardware and security and provide support in trouble shooting or changing instructions. In terms of hardware and security, it ensures that users are not making code that directly references hardware, and thus protects the actual processor. It also helps when trouble shooting or changing the instructions, as it would just have to be changed in the system call, rather than going to every line of code and changing that.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,3,'2025-06-01_13-56-54','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a piece of memory to represent that stack. This is done by assigning a pointer to that memory region, and manually adding register values onto the stack that are needed/expected by the stack. By doing this, it tricks the processor into thinking that the stack was already running, and it just needs to be resumed rather than actually setting up. The register values are needed on the stack to make sure the processor has all the required information from the “state prior to the pause” to continue running onwards. This includes the instruction that is to be printed next, the current values in the registers and the status.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,3,'2025-06-01_14-34-36','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a piece of memory to represent that stack. This is done by assigning a pointer to that memory region, and manually adding register values onto the stack that are needed/expected by the stack. By doing this, it “tricks” the processor into thinking that the stack was already running, and it just needs to be resumed rather than actually setting up. This means that the processor is setting up the thread’s stack, by making the processor think it is just resuming, by having all the required variables manually defined and then starting the processor where it is being told to continue on, using the manually defined variables. The register values are needed on the stack to make sure the processor has all the required information from the “state prior to the pause” to continue running onwards. This includes the instruction that is to be printed next, the current values in the registers and the status. The order of the register values is incredibility important, as the thread pops values from the stack into the specific registers in a specific order which is outlined in the lab. If the values are popped off in the wrong order (meaning that they were inputted into the stack in the wrong order), the registors will not be assigned their respective correct values.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,3,'2025-06-01_14-54-07','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a piece of memory to represent that stack. This is done by assigning a pointer to that memory region, and manually adding register values onto the stack that are needed/expected by the stack. By doing this, it “tricks” the processor into thinking that the stack was already running, and it just needs to be resumed rather than actually setting up. This means that the processor is setting up the thread’s stack, by making the processor think it is just resuming, by having all the required variables manually defined and then starting the processor where it is being told to continue on, using the manually defined variables. The register values are needed on the stack to make sure the processor has all the required information from the “state prior to the pause” to continue running onwards. This includes the instruction that is to be printed next, the current values in the registers and the status. The order of the register values is incredibility important, as the thread pops values from the stack into the specific registers in a specific order which is outlined in the lab. If the values are popped off in the wrong order (meaning that they were inputted into the stack in the wrong order), the registors will not be assigned their respective correct values.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,3,'2025-06-02_10-49-30','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a piece of memory to represent that stack. This is done by assigning a pointer to that memory region, and manually adding register values onto the stack that are needed/expected by the stack. By doing this, it “tricks” the processor into thinking that the stack was already running, and it just needs to be resumed rather than actually setting up. This means that the processor is setting up the thread’s stack, by making the processor think it is just resuming, by having all the required variables manually defined and then starting the processor where it is being told to continue on, using the manually defined variables. The register values are needed on the stack to make sure the processor has all the required information from the “state prior to the pause” to continue running onwards. This includes the instruction that is to be printed next, the current values in the registers and the status. The order of the register values is incredibility important, as the thread pops values from the stack into the specific registers in a specific order which is outlined in the lab. If the values are popped off in the wrong order (meaning that they were inputted into the stack in the wrong order), the registors will not be assigned their respective correct values.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,3,'2025-06-02_11-00-49','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a piece of memory to represent that stack. This is done by