ssor has all the required information from the “state prior to the pause” to continue running onwards. This includes the instruction that is to be printed next, the current values in the registers and the status. The order of the register values is incredibility important, as the thread pops values from the stack into the specific registers in a specific order which is outlined in the lab. If the values are popped off in the wrong order (meaning that they were inputted into the stack in the wrong order), the registors will not be assigned their respective correct values.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,3,'2025-06-03_11-32-36','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a piece of memory to represent that stack. This is done by assigning a pointer to that memory region, and manually adding register values onto the stack that are needed/expected by the stack. By doing this, it “tricks” the processor into thinking that the stack was already running, and it just needs to be resumed rather than actually setting up. This means that the processor is setting up the thread’s stack, by making the processor think it is just resuming, by having all the required variables manually defined and then starting the processor where it is being told to continue on, using the manually defined variables. The register values are needed on the stack to make sure the processor has all the required information from the “state prior to the pause” to continue running onwards. This includes the instruction that is to be printed next, the current values in the registers and the status. The order of the register values is incredibility important, as the thread pops values from the stack into the specific registers in a specific order which is outlined in the lab. If the values are popped off in the wrong order (meaning that they were inputted into the stack in the wrong order), the registors will not be assigned their respective correct values.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,4,'2025-06-01_13-56-54','The __set_PSP function tells the processor where the new thread is located in its memory. If this function was not run, then the processor would result in a hard fault, as the rest of the code is referring to memory that has not yet been allocated (invalid memory addresses) and high chance of the memory being misaligned if some memory has been assumed.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,4,'2025-06-01_14-34-36','The __set_PSP function tells the processor where the new thread is located in its memory, by setting where the top (address) of the top of the stack is allocated, and then to use this memory address for building the rest of the stack. If this function was not run, then the processor would result in a hard fault, as the rest of the code is referring to memory that has not yet been allocated (invalid memory addresses) and high chance of the memory being misaligned if some memory has been assumed. By using the __set_PSP function, this allows the thread’s stack to have a private stack that is not related to the MSP value and all the variables/stacks/memory that is located there. Using the __set_PSP function ensures that the stack starts from a memory address that fits all requirements to ensure no crashes, this includes (but not limited to); properly aligned memory, not overwriting existing memory, etc. By using the wrong stack pointers, these elements are not confirmed, and thus could result in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,4,'2025-06-01_14-54-07','The __set_PSP function tells the processor where the new thread is located in its memory, by setting where the top (address) of the top of the stack is allocated, and then to use this memory address for building the rest of the stack. If this function was not run, then the processor would result in a hard fault, as the rest of the code is referring to memory that has not yet been allocated (invalid memory addresses) and high chance of the memory being misaligned if some memory has been assumed. By using the __set_PSP function, this allows the thread’s stack to have a private stack that is not related to the MSP value and all the variables/stacks/memory that is located there. Using the __set_PSP function ensures that the stack starts from a memory address that fits all requirements to ensure no crashes, this includes (but not limited to); properly aligned memory, not overwriting existing memory, etc. By using the wrong stack pointers, these elements are not confirmed, and thus could result in a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,4,'2025-06-02_10-49-30','The __set_PSP function tells the processor where the new thread is located in its memory, by setting where the top (address) of the top of the stack is allocated, and then to use this memory address for building the rest of the stack. If this function was not run, then the processor would result in a hard fault, as the rest of the code is referring to memory that has not yet been allocated (invalid memory addresses) and high chance of the memory being misaligned if some memory has been assumed. By using the __set_PSP function, this allows the thread’s stack to have a private stack that is not related to the MSP value and all the variables/stacks/memory that is located there. Using the __set_PSP function ensures that the stack starts from a memory address that fits all requirements to ensure no crashes, this includes (but not limited to); properly aligned memory, not overwriting existing memory, etc. By using the wrong stack pointers, these elements are not confirmed, and thus could result in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,4,'2025-06-02_11-00-49','The __set_PSP function tells the processor where the new thread is located in its memory, by setting where the top (address) of the top of the stack is allocated, and then to use this memory address for building the rest of the stack. If this function was not run, then the processor would result in a hard fault, as the rest of the code is referring to memory that has not yet been 