his function was not run, then the processor would result in a hard fault, as the rest of the code is referring to memory that has not yet been allocated (invalid memory addresses) and high chance of the memory being misaligned if some memory has been assumed. By using the __set_PSP function, this allows the thread’s stack to have a private stack that is not related to the MSP value and all the variables/stacks/memory that is located there. Using the __set_PSP function ensures that the stack starts from a memory address that fits all requirements to ensure no crashes, this includes (but not limited to); properly aligned memory, not overwriting existing memory, etc. By using the wrong stack pointers, these elements are not confirmed, and thus could result in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,5,'2025-06-01_13-56-54','Our thread function would most likely crash or result in a hard fault, this is due to the set up. The thread function is set up in a way where the function is told where to go once the thread function has finished (back to beginning). The thread function has no set up on what to do for the return, and thus will return to either an invalid location or instruction.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,5,'2025-06-01_14-34-36','Our thread function would most likely crash or result in a hard fault, this is due to the set up. The thread function is set up in a way where the function is told where to go once the thread function has finished (back to beginning). In this specific case, the return address is pointing towards the stack to ensure that the function loops continuously. If this specific case is removed, which removes the continuously looping, the thread function has no set up on what to do for the return, and thus once the thread function has finished, it will have no instructions on the return and what to do afterwards and then results in a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,5,'2025-06-01_14-54-07','Our thread function would most likely crash or result in a hard fault by only printing once instead of continuously, due to the way the thread function is set up. The thread function is set up in a way where the function is told where to go once the thread function has finished (back to beginning). In this specific case, the return address (which is stored in the LR register) is pointing towards the beginning of the stack to ensure that the function loops continuously. This is what causes the thread function to loop continuously, now if in this specific case is removed, which removes the continuously looping. The thread function has no set up on what to do for the return, and thus once the thread function has finished, it will have no instructions on the return and what to do afterwards and then results in a hard fault. The processor will try to branch to an undefined or invalid memory address after the function, meaning that the CPU will be attempting to follow instructions from an illegal action and since this is not possible, a hard fault will occur. If valid instructions are provided on the return of the thread function, then the CPU would be able follow through on these instructions and thus result in not a hard fault, but that is not the case in this specific example/question.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,5,'2025-06-02_10-49-30','Our thread function would most likely crash or result in a hard fault by only printing once instead of continuously, due to the way the thread function is set up. The thread function is set up in a way where the function is told where to go once the thread function has finished (back to beginning). In this specific case, the return address (which is stored in the LR register) is pointing towards the beginning of the stack to ensure that the function loops continuously. This is what causes the thread function to loop continuously, now if in this specific case is removed, which removes the continuously looping. The thread function has no set up on what to do for the return, and thus once the thread function has finished, it will have no instructions on the return and what to do afterwards and then results in a hard fault. The processor will try to branch to an undefined or invalid memory address after the function, meaning that the CPU will be attempting to follow instructions from an illegal action and since this is not possible, a hard fault will occur. If valid instructions are provided on the return of the thread function, then the CPU would be able follow through on these instructions and thus result in not a hard fault, but that is not the case in this specific example/question.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,5,'2025-06-02_11-00-49','Our thread function would most likely crash or result in a hard fault by only printing once instead of continuously, due to the way the thread function is set up. The thread function is set up in a way where the function is told where to go once the thread function has finished (back to beginning). In this specific case, the return address (which is stored in the LR register) is pointing towards the beginning of the stack to ensure that the function loops continuously. This is what causes the thread function to loop continuously, now if in this specific case is removed, which removes the continuously looping. The thread function has no set up on what to do for the return, and thus once the thread function has finished, it will have no instructions on the return and what to do afterwards and then results in a hard fault. The processor will try to branch to an undefined or invalid memory address after the function, meaning that the CPU will be attempting to follow instructions from an illegal action and since this is not possible, a hard fault will occur. If valid instructions are provided on the return of the thread function, then the CPU would be able follow through on these instructions and thus result in not a hard fault, but that is not the case in this specific example/question.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,5,'2025-06-02_11-13-36','Our thread function would most likely crash or result in a hard fault by only printing once instead of continuously, due to the way the thread function is set up. The thread function is set up in a way where the function is told where to go once the thread function has finished (back to beginning). In this specific case, the return address (which is stored in the LR register) is pointing towards the beginning of the stack to ensure that the function loops continuously. This is what causes the thread function to loop continuously, now if in this specific case is removed, which removes the continuously looping. The thread function has no set up on what to do for the return (the return address is not being stored in the thread’s stack), and thus once the thread function has finished, it will have no instructions on the return and what to do afterwards and then results in a hard fault. The processor will try to branch to an undefined or invalid memory address after the function, meaning that the CPU will be attempting to follow instructions from an illegal action and since this is not possible, a hard fault will occur. If valid instructions are provided on the return of the thread function, then the CPU would be able follow through on these instructions and thus result in not a hard fault, but that is not the case in this specific example/question.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',2,5,'2025-06-03_11-32-36','Our thread function would most likely crash or result in a hard fault by only printing once instead of continuously, due to the way the thread function is set up. The thread function is set up in a way where the function is told where to go once the thread function has finished (back to beginning). In this specific case, the return address (which is stored in the LR register) is pointing towards the beginning of the stack to ensure that the function loops continuously. This is what causes the thread function to loop continuously, now if in this specific case is removed, which removes the continuously looping. The thread function has no set up on what to do for the return (the return address is not being stored in the thread’s stack), and thus once the thread function has finished, it will have no instructions on the return and what to do afterwards and then results in a hard fault. The processor will try to branch to an undefined or invalid memory address after the function, meaning that the CPU will be attempting to follow instructions from an illegal action and since this is not possible, a hard fault will occur. If valid instructions are provided on the return of the thread function, then the CPU would be able follow through on these instructions and thus result in not a hard fault, but that is not the case in this specific example/question.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,1,'2025-06-24_13-57-40','Logic is written in multiple *.c files to keep the code organized. However to run the logic, it doesn’t make sense to have to run each *.c file individually, this is why they get compiled into a single executable. To make this happen, each individual *.c file is compiled into a *.o file (object file). This is done individually to check for errors and to prevent the need to recompile all the *.c files and only to recompile the individual files that are changed/required. Once each file has been compiled into a *.o file, they are then linked together to form one executable. By linking together, this means combining the code from all the files, resolving all function calls between files and linking system files. The result is a executable that runs the entirety of the logic.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,1,'2025-06-25_11-28-49','Logic is written in multiple *.c files to keep the code organized. However to run the logic, it doesn’t make sense to have to run each *.c file individually, this is why they get compiled into a single executable. To prepare the compilation, each file is preprocessed to load the #include and #define lines. Where this expands macros and adds the contents of the header file. Leading to the file becoming ready to be compiled. To make this happen, each individual *.c file is compiled into a *.o file (object file), a *.o file is an assembly level file (a low level). This is done individually to check for errors and to prevent the need to recompile all the *.c files and only to recompile the individual files that are changed/required. Once each file has been compiled into a *.o file, they are then linked together to form one executable. By linking together, this means combining the code from all the files, resolving all function calls between files and linking system files. The result is an executable that runs the entirety of the logic.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,1,'2025-06-25_16-58-42','Logic is written in multiple *.c files to keep the code organized. However to run the logic, it doesn’t make sense to have to run each *.c file individually, this is why they get compiled into a single executable. To prepare the compilation, each file is preprocessed to load the #include and #define lines. Where this expands macros and adds the contents of the header file. Leading to the file becoming ready to be compiled. To make this happen, each individual *.c file is compiled into a *.o file (object file), a *.o file is an assembly level file (a low level). This is done individually to check for errors and to prevent the need to recompile all the *.c files and only to recompile the individual files that are changed/required. Once each file has been compiled into a *.o file, they are then linked together to form one executable. By linking together, this means combining the code from all the files, resolving all function calls between files and linking system files. The result is an executable that runs the entirety of the logic.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,1,'2025-06-25_18-29-19','Logic is written in multiple *.c files to keep the code organized. However to run the logic, it doesn’t make sense to have to run each *.c file individually, this is why they get compiled into a single executable. To prepare the compilation, each file is preprocessed to load the #include and #define lines. Where this expands macros and adds the contents of the header file. Leading to the file becoming ready to be compiled. To make this happen, each individual *.c file is compiled into a *.o file (object file), a *.o file is an assembly level file (a low level). This is done individually to check for errors and to prevent the need to recompile all the *.c files and only to recompile the individual files that are changed/required. Once each file has been compiled into a *.o file, they are then linked together to form one executable. By linking together, this means combining the code from all the files, resolving all function calls between files and linking system files. The result is an executable that runs the entirety of the logic.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,1,'2025-06-26_16-17-23','Logic is written in multiple *.c files to keep the code organized. However to run the logic, it doesn’t make sense to have to run each *.c file individually, this is why they get compiled into a single executable. To prepare the compilation, each file is preprocessed to load the #include and #define lines. Where this expands macros and adds the contents of the header file. Leading to the file becoming ready to be compiled. To make this happen, each individual *.c file is compiled into a *.o file (object file), a *.o file is an assembly level file (a low level). This is done individually to check for errors and to prevent the need to recompile all the *.c files and only to recompile the individual files that are changed/required. Once each file has been compiled into a *.o file, they are then linked together to form one executable. By linking together, this means combining the code from all the files, resolving all function calls between files and linking system files. The result is an executable that runs the entirety of the logic.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,2,'2025-06-24_13-57-40','As mentioned in the response for ',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,2,'2025-06-25_11-28-49','As mentioned in the response for ',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,2,'2025-06-25_16-58-42','As mentioned in the response for ',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,2,'2025-06-25_18-29-19','As mentioned in the response for ',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,2,'2025-06-26_16-17-23','As mentioned in the response for ',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,3,'2025-06-24_13-57-40','Decide the purpose of the thread\nThis means deciding what set of instructions the thread is carrying out, this is to be a very specific goal where it can be run independently\nCreate a function for the thread\nThis function entails the purpose of the thread. This is the function that will carry out the task. The function is called and use during the runtime/usage of the thread.\nSet up the thread\nCreate a thread object and assign the function that the thread will use. Can also initialize input arguments depending on the purpose of the function\nStart the thread\nCall a method to start the thread! This will tell the system to start running the thread while running other programs in the background\nLet the thread run\nThe thread will run in the background independently\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,3,'2025-06-25_11-28-49','Decide the purpose of the thread\nThis means deciding what set of instructions the thread is carrying out, this is to be a very specific goal where it can be run independently\nCreate a function for the thread\nThis function entails the purpose of the thread. This is the function that will carry out the task. The function is called and use during the runtime/usage of the thread.\nInitialize the Kernel\nThe kernel needs to be initialized if it has not been set up prior\nSet up the thread\nCreate a thread object and assign the function that the thread will use. Can also initialize input arguments depending on the purpose of the function\nStart the thread\nCall a method to start the thread! This will tell the system to start running the thread while running other programs in the background\nStart the kernel\nThis begins thread execution and then hands over control to the operating system, so the thread continues to run\nLet the thread run\nThe thread will run in the background independently\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,3,'2025-06-25_16-58-42','Decide the purpose of the thread\nThis means deciding what set of instructions the thread is carrying out, this is to be a very specific goal where it can be run independently\nCreate a function for the thread\nThis function entails the purpose of the thread. This is the function that will carry out the task. The function is called and use during the runtime/usage of the thread.\nInitialize the Kernel\nThe kernel needs to be initialized if it has not been set up prior\nSet up the thread\nCreate a thread object and assign the function that the thread will use. Can also initialize input arguments depending on the purpose of the function\nStart the thread\nCall a method to start the thread! This will tell the system to start running the thread while running other programs in the background\nStart the kernel\nThis begins thread execution and then hands over control to the operating system, so the thread continues to run\nLet the thread run\nThe thread will run in the background independently\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,3,'2025-06-25_18-29-19','Decide the purpose of the thread\nThis means deciding what set of instructions the thread is carrying out, this is to be a very specific goal where it can be run independently\nCreate a function for the thread\nThis function entails the purpose of the thread. This is the function that will carry out the task. The function is called and use during the runtime/usage of the thread.\nInitialize the Kernel\nThe kernel needs to be initialized if it has not been set up prior\nCreate and register the thread\nCreate the thread object and register it with the kernel, during this process the function is specified that the thread will run along with any input arguments that the function needs\nStart the kernel\nThis begins thread execution and then hands over control to the operating system, so the thread continues to run\nKernel is responsible for switching between threads when needed, so no individual thread should be started prior to this point\nLet the thread run\nThe thread will run in the background independently\nOnce the kernel is running, it automatically prioritizes scheduling and processes, which allows the user to not have to manually do this\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,3,'2025-06-26_16-17-23','Decide the purpose of the thread\nThis means deciding what set of instructions the thread is carrying out, this is to be a very specific goal where it can be run independently\nCreate a function for the thread\nThis function entails the purpose of the thread. This is the function that will carry out the task. The function is called and use during the runtime/usage of the thread.\nInitialize the Kernel\nThe kernel needs to be initialized if it has not been set up prior\nCreate and register the thread (and set up thread)\nCreate the thread object and register it with the kernel, during this process the function is specified that the thread will run along with any input arguments that the function needs\nThis is done with the kernel creating the thread. To do this the kernel needs the initial value of the MSP stack (which is from step 3, initializing the kernel). From the user’s perspective this happens automatically when the kernel is initialized through calling a function.\nTo actually set the thread’s stack pointer, the kernel subtracts the known stack size of the thread (offset) from the MSP stack point, and this becomes the new address. This only happens if there is enough space in the stack for the thread’s stack size. The user has control over this by setting the stack size to work around.\nThe kernel then creates the registers within the thread’s stack, by using the stack pointer to reach the address of each register (starting at xPSR to R0), this is done without direct instructions from the user\nStart the kernel\nThis begins thread execution and then hands over control to the operating system, so the thread continues to run. The start of the thread is controlled by the user by calling a case function that entails the value that triggers the thread to run.\nKernel is responsible for switching between threads when needed, so no individual thread should be started prior to this point\nLet the thread run\nThe thread will run in the background independently\nOnce the kernel is running, it automatically prioritizes scheduling and processes, which allows the user to not have to manually do this\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,4,'2025-06-24_13-57-40','A stack pool is a section of memory that is specifically for storing stack space for multiple threads. To properly function, a thread needs to have local variables, function calls, and information to switch between threads. To store this information, each thread is assigned and allocated a stack, where this memory is within the stack pool. In a future lab, to enable multiple threads, each thread is assigned a section of memory from the stack pool (assigned an initial value and this is set at the pointer to the thread and instructions are given to assign future values of the stack, i.e growing upwards or downwards). This allows each thread to have its own stack with its own personal information so no threads overwrite instructions for another thread. This also allow switching between threads, as the OS can save the stack pointer in the registers and restore once the thread wants to be resumed.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,4,'2025-06-25_11-28-49','A stack pool is a section of memory that is specifically for storing stack space for multiple threads. To properly function, a thread needs to have local variables, function calls, and information to switch between threads. To store this information, each thread is assigned and allocated a stack, where this memory is within the stack pool. In a future lab, to enable multiple threads, each thread is assigned a section of memory from the stack pool (assigned an initial value and this is set at the pointer to the thread and instructions are given to assign future values of the stack, i.e growing upwards or downwards). This allows each thread to have its own stack with its own personal information so no threads overwrite instructions for another thread. This also allow switching between threads, as the OS can save the stack pointer in the registers and restore once the thread wants to be resumed.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,4,'2025-06-25_16-58-42','A stack pool is a section of memory that is specifically for storing stack space for multiple threads. To properly function, a thread needs to have local variables, function calls, and information to switch between threads. To store this information, each thread is assigned and allocated a stack, where this memory is within the stack pool. In a future lab, to enable multiple threads, each thread is assigned a section of memory from the stack pool (assigned an initial value and this is set at the pointer to the thread and instructions are given to assign future values of the stack, i.e growing upwards or downwards). This allows each thread to have its own stack with its own personal information so no threads overwrite instructions for another thread. This also allow switching between threads, as the OS can save the stack pointer in the registers and restore once the thread wants to be resumed.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,4,'2025-06-25_18-29-19','A stack pool is a section of memory that is specifically for storing stack space for multiple threads. To properly function, a thread needs to have local variables, function calls, and information to switch between threads. To store this information, each thread is assigned and allocated a stack, where this memory is within the stack pool. In a future lab, to enable multiple threads, each thread is assigned a section of memory from the stack pool (assigned an initial value and this is set at the pointer to the thread and instructions are given to assign future values of the stack, i.e growing upwards or downwards). This allows each thread to have its own stack with its own personal information so no threads overwrite instructions for another thread. This also allow switching between threads, as the OS can save the stack pointer in the registers and restore once the thread wants to be resumed.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,4,'2025-06-26_16-17-23','A stack pool is a section of memory that is specifically for storing stack space for multiple threads. To properly function, a thread needs to have local variables, function calls, and information to switch between threads. To store this information, each thread is assigned and allocated a stack, where this memory is within the stack pool. In a future lab, to enable multiple threads, each thread is assigned a section of memory from the stack pool (assigned an initial value and this is set at the pointer to the thread and instructions are given to assign future values of the stack, i.e growing upwards or downwards). This allows each thread to have its own stack with its own personal information so no threads overwrite instructions for another thread. This also allow switching between threads, as the OS can save the stack pointer in the registers and restore once the thread wants to be resumed.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,5,'2025-06-24_13-57-40','Kernel is a core part of the operating system, where the kernel is a more privileged mode with access to a larger amount of controls that the user. This includes directly accessing hardware, memory, CPU etc, and handles low level logic. This includes files like kernel.c, thread.c etc. On the otherhand, user files run on top of the operating system and don’t have access to a lot of the controls that the kernel does. To run these commands, the user must request the kernel through a system call. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important as it provides clarity and protects the user. Clarity to show where code should be written depending on the purpose and output of the code. Protects harming the kernel by running programs on top of the operating system, where if the program crashes, it won’t harm the kernel as the kernel is isolated. This also helps to prevent bugs from spreading into the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,5,'2025-06-25_11-28-49','Kernel is a core part of the operating system, where the kernel is a more privileged mode with access to a larger amount of controls that the user. This includes directly accessing hardware, memory, CPU etc, and handles low level logic. This includes files like kernel.c, thread.c etc. On the otherhand, user files run on top of the operating system and don’t have access to a lot of the controls that the kernel does. To run these commands, the user must request the kernel through a system call. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important as it provides clarity and protects the user. Clarity to show where code should be written depending on the purpose and output of the code. Protects harming the kernel by running programs on top of the operating system, where if the program crashes, it won’t harm the kernel as the kernel is isolated. This also helps to prevent bugs from spreading into the kernel. Additionally, the user is responsible for writing threads or the actual tasks in the programs. This includes writing threads in kernel and writing instructions for tasks in a program in the user mode. Knowing the purpose and capabilities of both the kernel and user mode allows the user to make a decision on which mode to do to carry out a certain task.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,5,'2025-06-25_16-58-42','Kernel is a core part of the operating system, where the kernel is a more privileged mode with access to a larger amount of controls that the user. This includes directly accessing hardware, memory, CPU etc, and handles low level logic. This includes files like kernel.c, thread.c etc. On the otherhand, user files run on top of the operating system and don’t have access to a lot of the controls that the kernel does. To run these commands, the user must request the kernel through a system call. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important as it provides clarity and protects the user. Clarity to show where code should be written depending on the purpose and output of the code. Protects harming the kernel by running programs on top of the operating system, where if the program crashes, it won’t harm the kernel as the kernel is isolated. This also helps to prevent bugs from spreading into the kernel. Additionally, the user is responsible for writing threads or the actual tasks in the programs. This includes writing threads in kernel and writing instructions for tasks in a program in the user mode. Knowing the purpose and capabilities of both the kernel and user mode allows the user to make a decision on which mode to do to carry out a certain task.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,5,'2025-06-25_18-29-19','Kernel is a core part of the operating system, where the kernel is a more privileged mode with access to a larger amount of controls that the user. This includes directly accessing hardware, memory, CPU etc, and handles low level logic. This includes files like kernel.c, thread.c etc. On the otherhand, user files run on top of the operating system and don’t have access to a lot of the controls that the kernel does. The user mode is a API or interface that is made from the kernel to provide a secondary environment to run programs. To run these commands, the user must request the kernel through a system call. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important as it provides clarity and protects the user. Clarity to show where code should be written depending on the purpose and output of the code. Protects harming the kernel by running programs on top of the operating system, where if the program crashes, it won’t harm the kernel as the kernel is isolated. This also helps to prevent bugs from spreading into the kernel. Additionally, the user is responsible for writing threads or the actual tasks in the programs. This includes writing threads in kernel and writing instructions for tasks in a program in the user mode. Writing threads include writing the logic in the user mode and then using system calls to the kernel to execute. Knowing the purpose and capabilities of both the kernel and user mode allows the user to make a decision on which mode to do to carry out a certain task. For example, when downloading an application, this makes the user’s job extremely simple by using the user mode, in comparsion to installing an operating system when has to be down in the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',3,5,'2025-06-26_16-17-23','Kernel is a core part of the operating system, where the kernel is a more privileged mode with access to a larger amount of controls that the user. This includes directly accessing hardware, memory, CPU etc, and handles low level logic. This includes files like kernel.c, thread.c etc. On the otherhand, user files run on top of the operating system and don’t have access to a lot of the controls that the kernel does. The user mode is a API or interface that is made from the kernel to provide a secondary environment to run programs. To run these commands, the user must request the kernel through a system call. From the user’s perspective, this distinction is important as it provides clarity and protects the user. Clarity to show where code should be written depending on the purpose and output of the code. Protects harming the kernel by running programs on top of the operating system, where if the program crashes, it won’t harm the kernel as the kernel is isolated. This also helps to prevent bugs from spreading into the kernel. Additionally, the user is responsible for writing threads or the actual tasks in the programs. This includes writing threads in kernel and writing instructions for tasks in a program in the user mode. Writing threads include writing the logic in the user mode and then using system calls to the kernel to execute. Knowing the purpose and capabilities of both the kernel and user mode allows the user to make a decision on which mode to do to carry out a certain task. For example, when downloading an application, this makes the user’s job extremely simple by using the user mode, in comparsion to installing an operating system when has to be down in the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,1,'2025-07-17_12-32-30','Using round-robin scheduling with a queue would work in a similar manner. Where the scheduler would remove the TCB at the front of the queue, runs the thread until either it yields or it gets timed out. After, the TCB gets added to the end of the queue and the scheduler picks up from the start of the queue once again. This follows the First in First Out system. There are some benefits to using a queue, this has a natural FIFO behaviour, very simple queue and scheduler logic, very simple to remove or add a task.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,1,'2025-07-17_23-41-43','Using round-robin scheduling with a queue would work in a similar manner. Where the scheduler would remove the TCB at the front of the queue, runs the thread until either it yields or it gets timed out. After, the TCB gets added to the end of the queue and the scheduler picks up from the start of the queue once again. This follows the First in First Out system. There are some benefits to using a queue, this has a natural FIFO behaviour, very simple queue and scheduler logic, very simple to remove or add a task. Overall, a queue makes the process simpler, easier to use and implement, more flexible to changes and maintainability. This allows each thread to have an equal CPU time in a rotating manner. The queue allows a more dynamic and modular to control and manage TCBs. Queues already have an associated order for the threads/contents. This eliminates the need for index arithmetic and is easier to navigate.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,1,'2025-07-18_12-01-36','Using round-robin scheduling with a queue would work in a similar manner. Where the scheduler would remove the TCB at the front of the queue, runs the thread until either it yields or it gets timed out. After, the TCB gets added to the end of the queue and the scheduler picks up from the start of the queue once again. This follows the First in First Out system. There are some benefits to using a queue, this has a natural FIFO behaviour, very simple queue and scheduler logic, very simple to remove or add a task. Overall, a queue makes the process simpler, easier to use and implement, more flexible to changes and maintainability. This allows each thread to have an equal CPU time in a rotating manner. The queue allows a more dynamic and modular to control and manage TCBs. Queues already have an associated order for the threads/contents. This eliminates the need for index arithmetic and is easier to navigate. In the lab manual the scheduler is implemented using an array of TCBs with an index variable to access a specific one. After each time slice or yield, the scheduler increments this index to initiate the next thread to run. Array is similar in the sense that it cycles through its content however, it requires the index variable and static memory allocation. Queue can have some downsides, with overhead and functionality. Overhead can be caused by allocating and deallocating memory, where a queue may go into overhead with linked structures. While arrays are more memory efficient and have constant time indexing. Functionality, a queue can be better as it doesn’t require an index variable or having to keep track of where it is during the queue. As a queue just cycles through the entire list, where an array requires that index variable and continuously updating it as well.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,1,'2025-07-18_12-08-43','Using round-robin scheduling with a queue would work in a similar manner. Where the scheduler would remove the TCB at the front of the queue, runs the thread until either it yields or it gets timed out. After, the TCB gets added to the end of the queue and the scheduler picks up from the start of the queue once again. This follows the First in First Out system. There are some benefits to using a queue, this has a natural FIFO behaviour, very simple queue and scheduler logic, very simple to remove or add a task. Overall, a queue makes the process simpler, easier to use and implement, more flexible to changes and maintainability. This allows each thread to have an equal CPU time in a rotating manner. The queue allows a more dynamic and modular to control and manage TCBs. Queues already have an associated order for the threads/contents. This eliminates the need for index arithmetic and is easier to navigate. In the lab manual the scheduler is implemented using an array of TCBs with an index variable to access a specific one. After each time slice or yield, the scheduler increments this index to initiate the next thread to run. Array is similar in the sense that it cycles through its content however, it requires the index variable and static memory allocation. Queue can have some downsides, with overhead and functionality. Overhead can be caused by allocating and deallocating memory, where a queue may go into overhead with linked structures. While arrays are more memory efficient and have constant time indexing. Functionality, a queue can be better as it doesn’t require an index variable or having to keep track of where it is during the queue. As a queue just cycles through the entire list, where an array requires that index variable and continuously updating it as well.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,1,'2025-07-18_13-15-54','Using round-robin scheduling with a queue would work in a similar manner. Where the scheduler would remove the TCB at the front of the queue, runs the thread until either it yields or it gets timed out. After, the TCB gets added to the end of the queue and the scheduler picks up from the start of the queue once again. This follows the First in First Out system. There are some benefits to using a queue, this has a natural FIFO behaviour, very simple queue and scheduler logic, very simple to remove or add a task. Overall, a queue makes the process simpler, easier to use and implement, more flexible to changes and maintainability. This allows each thread to have an equal CPU time in a rotating manner. The queue allows a more dynamic and modular to control and manage TCBs. Queues already have an associated order for the threads/contents. This eliminates the need for index arithmetic and is easier to navigate. In the lab manual the scheduler is implemented using an array of TCBs with an index variable to access a specific one. After each time slice or yield, the scheduler increments this index to initiate the next thread to run. Array is similar in the sense that it cycles through its content however, it requires the index variable and static memory allocation. Queue can have some downsides, with overhead and functionality. Overhead can be caused by allocating and deallocating memory, where a queue may go into overhead with linked structures. While arrays are more memory efficient and have constant time indexing. Functionality, a queue can be better as it doesn’t require an index variable or having to keep track of where it is during the queue. As a queue just cycles through the entire list, where an array requires that index variable and continuously updating it as well.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,2,'2025-07-17_12-32-30','PendSV_Handler executes a context switch with an interrupt within SVC. SVC is first triggered which has a higher priority number for an interrupt in comparison to the Pend which has a lower priority number. This causes the Pend to wait until the SVC is finished, where the SVC is triggered, runs and then uses the registers to restore to the previous state prior to the interrupt and then the Pend is triggered. Pend is still within the interrupt though which is what allows for the context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,2,'2025-07-17_23-41-43','PendSV_Handler executes a context switch with an interrupt within SVC. SVC is first triggered which has a higher priority number for an interrupt in comparison to the Pend which has a lower priority number. This causes the Pend to wait until the SVC is finished, where the SVC is triggered, runs and then uses the registers to restore to the previous state prior to the interrupt and then the Pend is triggered. Pend is still within the interrupt though which is what allows for the context switch.\nPendSV identifies and access the current thread’s stack, this is done by using the PSP registers which contains the stack pointer for the current running thread. R4-R11 are pushed onto the current thread’s stack as they are not manually saved. This needs to be saved as the values in the registers are important to when the thread will resume.\nThe scheduler then is used to determine which thread should run next. This makes the scheduler update the global variable of TCB to the next thread. Which triggers the PSP to be updated to the pointer of the stack of the next thread. R4-R11 are restored for that desired stack.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,2,'2025-07-18_12-01-36','PendSV_Handler executes a context switch with an interrupt within SVC. SVC is first triggered which has a higher priority number for an interrupt in comparison to the Pend which has a lower priority number. This causes the Pend to wait until the SVC is finished, where the SVC is triggered, runs and then uses the registers to restore to the previous state prior to the interrupt and then the Pend is triggered. Pend is still within the interrupt though which is what allows for the context switch.\nPendSV identifies and access the current thread’s stack, this is done by using the PSP registers which contains the stack pointer for the current running thread. R4-R11 are pushed onto the current thread’s stack as they are not manually saved. This needs to be saved as the values in the registers are important to when the thread will resume.\nThe scheduler then is used to determine which thread should run next. This makes the scheduler update the global variable of TCB to the next thread. Which triggers the PSP to be updated to the pointer of the stack of the next thread. R4-R11 are restored for that desired stack.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,2,'2025-07-18_12-08-43','PendSV_Handler executes a context switch with an interrupt within SVC. SVC is first triggered which has a higher priority number for an interrupt in comparison to the Pend which has a lower priority number. This causes the Pend to wait until the SVC is finished, where the SVC is triggered, runs and then uses the registers to restore to the previous state prior to the interrupt and then the Pend is triggered. Pend is still within the interrupt though which is what allows for the context switch.\nPendSV identifies and access the current thread’s stack, this is done by using the PSP registers which contains the stack pointer for the current running thread. R4-R11 are pushed onto the current thread’s stack as they are not manually saved. This needs to be saved as the values in the registers are important to when the thread will resume.\nThe scheduler then is used to determine which thread should run next. This makes the scheduler update the global variable of TCB to the next thread. Which triggers the PSP to be updated to the pointer of the stack of the next thread. R4-R11 are restored for that desired stack.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,2,'2025-07-18_13-15-54','PendSV_Handler executes a context switch with an interrupt within SVC. SVC is first triggered which has a higher priority number for an interrupt in comparison to the Pend which has a lower priority number. This causes the Pend to wait until the SVC is finished, where the SVC is triggered, runs and then uses the registers to restore to the previous state prior to the interrupt and then the Pend is triggered. Pend is still within the interrupt though which is what allows for the context switch.\nPendSV identifies and access the current thread’s stack, this is done by using the PSP registers which contains the stack pointer for the current running thread. R4-R11 are pushed onto the current thread’s stack as they are not manually saved. This needs to be saved as the values in the registers are important to when the thread will resume.\nThe scheduler then is used to determine which thread should run next. This makes the scheduler update the global variable of TCB to the next thread. Which triggers the PSP to be updated to the pointer of the stack of the next thread. R4-R11 are restored for that desired stack.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,3,'2025-07-17_12-32-30','This is bad for a couple of reasons. If there is a long queue of high priority items, then the low priority may never see the light of day and be stuck behind the huge list of high priority items. This could mean the low priority items get significantly delayed or never get executed. Low priority may also be essential to the upkeep and running of the system, tasks such as, clean up routines, I/O handles may never get executed as they can be considered low priority but are still essential to the system. This also violates timely responses, as this method no longer can guarantee a response time, as low priority items can continuously be delayed with a continuous stream of high priority items.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,3,'2025-07-17_23-41-43','This is bad for a couple of reasons. If there is a long queue of high priority items, then the low priority may never see the light of day and be stuck behind the huge list of high priority items. This could mean the low priority items get significantly delayed or never get executed. Low priority may also be essential to the upkeep and running of the system, tasks such as, clean up routines, I/O handles may never get executed as they can be considered low priority but are still essential to the system. This also violates timely responses, as this method no longer can guarantee a response time, as low priority items can continuously be delayed with a continuous stream of high priority items. These are just consequences, but the actual scheduling itself is a bad idea. This type of scheduling only allows ONE task to be selected at the time, which is the highest priority task. Without mechanisms or logic in place like priority aging or scheduling, this fails to allows other asks to run. This violates real-time constraints and system responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,3,'2025-07-18_12-01-36','This is bad for a couple of reasons. If there is a long queue of high priority items, then the low priority may never see the light of day and be stuck behind the huge list of high priority items. This could mean the low priority items get significantly delayed or never get executed. Low priority may also be essential to the upkeep and running of the system, tasks such as, clean up routines, I/O handles may never get executed as they can be considered low priority but are still essential to the system. This also violates timely responses, as this method no longer can guarantee a response time, as low priority items can continuously be delayed with a continuous stream of high priority items. These are just consequences, but the actual scheduling itself is a bad idea. This type of scheduling only allows ONE task to be selected at the time, which is the highest priority task. Without mechanisms or logic in place like priority aging or scheduling, this fails to allows other asks to run. This violates real-time constraints and system responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,3,'2025-07-18_12-08-43','This is bad for a couple of reasons. If there is a long queue of high priority items, then the low priority may never see the light of day and be stuck behind the huge list of high priority items. This could mean the low priority items get significantly delayed or never get executed. Low priority may also be essential to the upkeep and running of the system, tasks such as, clean up routines, I/O handles may never get executed as they can be considered low priority but are still essential to the system. This also violates timely responses, as this method no longer can guarantee a response time, as low priority items can continuously be delayed with a continuous stream of high priority items. These are just consequences, but the actual scheduling itself is a bad idea. This type of scheduling only allows ONE task to be selected at the time, which is the highest priority task. Without mechanisms or logic in place like priority aging or scheduling, this fails to allows other asks to run. This violates real-time constraints and system responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,3,'2025-07-18_13-15-54','This is bad for a couple of reasons. If there is a long queue of high priority items, then the low priority may never see the light of day and be stuck behind the huge list of high priority items. This could mean the low priority items get significantly delayed or never get executed. Low priority may also be essential to the upkeep and running of the system, tasks such as, clean up routines, I/O handles may never get executed as they can be considered low priority but are still essential to the system. This also violates timely responses, as this method no longer can guarantee a response time, as low priority items can continuously be delayed with a continuous stream of high priority items. These are just consequences, but the actual scheduling itself is a bad idea. This type of scheduling only allows ONE task to be selected at the time, which is the highest priority task. Without mechanisms or logic in place like priority aging or scheduling, this fails to allows other asks to run. This violates real-time constraints and system responsiveness.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,4,'2025-07-17_12-32-30','Benefits of calling the C functions:\nTransferable: The function is not specific to a system, writing in assembly is specific to a system and the hardware that is being used. By writing in C, this allows the person who is coding to apply more widely known knowledge and as well use the same code for multiple systems that may work with different systems. This also allows others to read the code and maintain it since C is a more widely known language.\nCompiler Tools: Using C allows for many different compilers that can help with debugging and optimization, this also helps to increase safety\nDownsides:\nControl: Since assembly talks directly to the hardware/system, this provides the user with more control on what is happening with the harder in comparison to using C. C also provides more delay in the system in comparison to assembly, which may be of importance depending on the context and purpose of the system. In the case that the system is acting in real time, it may be important to have as little delay as possible.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,4,'2025-07-17_23-41-43','Benefits of calling the C functions:\nTransferable: The function is not specific to a system, writing in assembly is specific to a system and the hardware that is being used. By writing in C, this allows the person who is coding to apply more widely known knowledge and as well use the same code for multiple systems that may work with different systems. This also allows others to read the code and maintain it since C is a more widely known language.\nCompiler Tools: Using C allows for many different compilers that can help with debugging and optimization, this also helps to increase safety\nDownsides:\nControl: Since assembly talks directly to the hardware/system, this provides the user with more control on what is happening with the harder in comparison to using C. C also provides more delay in the system in comparison to assembly, which may be of importance depending on the context and purpose of the system. In the case that the system is acting in real time, it may be important to have as little delay as possible.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,4,'2025-07-18_12-01-36','Benefits of calling the C functions:\nTransferable: The function is not specific to a system, writing in assembly is specific to a system and the hardware that is being used. By writing in C, this allows the person who is coding to apply more widely known knowledge and as well use the same code for multiple systems that may work with different systems. This also allows others to read the code and maintain it since C is a more widely known language.\nCompiler Tools: Using C allows for many different compilers that can help with debugging and optimization, this also helps to increase safety\nDownsides:\nControl: Since assembly talks directly to the hardware/system, this provides the user with more control on what is happening with the harder in comparison to using C. C also provides more delay in the system in comparison to assembly, which may be of importance depending on the context and purpose of the system. In the case that the system is acting in real time, it may be important to have as little delay as possible.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,4,'2025-07-18_12-08-43','Benefits of calling the C functions:\nTransferable: The function is not specific to a system, writing in assembly is specific to a system and the hardware that is being used. By writing in C, this allows the person who is coding to apply more widely known knowledge and as well use the same code for multiple systems that may work with different systems. This also allows others to read the code and maintain it since C is a more widely known language.\nCompiler Tools: Using C allows for many different compilers that can help with debugging and optimization, this also helps to increase safety\nDownsides:\nControl: Since assembly talks directly to the hardware/system, this provides the user with more control on what is happening with the harder in comparison to using C. C also provides more delay in the system in comparison to assembly, which may be of importance depending on the context and purpose of the system. In the case that the system is acting in real time, it may be important to have as little delay as possible.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,4,'2025-07-18_13-15-54','Benefits of calling the C functions:\nTransferable: The function is not specific to a system, writing in assembly is specific to a system and the hardware that is being used. By writing in C, this allows the person who is coding to apply more widely known knowledge and as well use the same code for multiple systems that may work with different systems. This also allows others to read the code and maintain it since C is a more widely known language.\nCompiler Tools: Using C allows for many different compilers that can help with debugging and optimization, this also helps to increase safety\nDownsides:\nControl: Since assembly talks directly to the hardware/system, this provides the user with more control on what is happening with the harder in comparison to using C. C also provides more delay in the system in comparison to assembly, which may be of importance depending on the context and purpose of the system. In the case that the system is acting in real time, it may be important to have as little delay as possible.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,5,'2025-07-17_12-32-30','Short answer, yes the OS will still work. However, the overall purpose of the OS will not be useful. Since the purpose of this was multiple threads and by only using one thread it arguably loses the point of the RTOS along with wasting the resources that are being used to run it. The scheduler is working but is just switching to the same thread that is running. The system would also be in a stable state, but no other tasks can be completed either. This is the because the OS is stuck with “switching” to the same thread and thus can get out of the thread loop and can’t run any other tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,5,'2025-07-17_23-41-43','Short answer, yes the OS will still work. However, the overall purpose of the OS will not be useful. Since the purpose of this was multiple threads and by only using one thread it arguably loses the point of the RTOS along with wasting the resources that are being used to run it. The scheduler is working but is just switching to the same thread that is running. The system would also be in a stable state, but no other tasks can be completed either. This is the because the OS is stuck with “switching” to the same thread and thus can get out of the thread loop and can’t run any other tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,5,'2025-07-18_12-01-36','Short answer, yes the OS will still work. However, the overall purpose of the OS will not be useful. Since the purpose of this was multiple threads and by only using one thread it arguably loses the point of the RTOS along with wasting the resources that are being used to run it. The scheduler is working but is just switching to the same thread that is running. The system would also be in a stable state, but no other tasks can be completed either. This is the because the OS is stuck with “switching” to the same thread and thus can get out of the thread loop and can’t run any other tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,5,'2025-07-18_12-08-43','Short answer, yes the OS will still work. However, the overall purpose of the OS will not be useful. Since the purpose of this was multiple threads and by only using one thread it arguably loses the point of the RTOS along with wasting the resources that are being used to run it. The scheduler is working but is just switching to the same thread that is running. The system would also be in a stable state, but no other tasks can be completed either. This is the because the OS is stuck with “switching” to the same thread and thus can get out of the thread loop and can’t run any other tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',4,5,'2025-07-18_13-15-54','Short answer, yes the OS will still work. However, the overall purpose of the OS will not be useful. Since the purpose of this was multiple threads and by only using one thread it arguably loses the point of the RTOS along with wasting the resources that are being used to run it. The scheduler is working but is just switching to the same thread that is running. The system would also be in a stable state, but no other tasks can be completed either. This is the because the OS is stuck with “switching” to the same thread and thus can get out of the thread loop and can’t run any other tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,1,'2025-07-21_13-59-36','The pass an argument to a thread, the user must define the argument and either create a system enclosing this argument or directly input into the setUpThread function depending on what the argument is. Where the argument may not be accepted by the setupThread function. This is the work that is to be done by the user. Once the function or argument is passed into the setupthread function, the work now gets passed into the OS. Where the OS extracts the value directly from the argument or from the function enclosing the desired argument. Calling this function triggers the initial creation of the thread setting up initial registers and values within the thread as well. The OS can then set the initial registers, and input the argument that is wanted to get passed into the thread. This works because the work is split between what is done between the user and the OS. Where the user is defining the functions that are being triggered/called and defining the arguments passed into each one. While the OS is doing the work of setting up the thread and following through of the functions that are called by the user.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,1,'2025-07-23_14-40-37','The pass an argument to a thread, the user must define the argument and either create a system enclosing this argument or directly input into the osCreateThread function depending on what the argument is. Where the argument may not be accepted by the osCreateThread function. This is the work that is to be done by the user. The user creates the function and passes a pointer to the argument of osCreateThread. Once the function or argument is passed into the osCreateThread function, the work now gets passed into the OS. Where the OS extracts the value directly from the argument or from the function enclosing the desired argument. Calling this function triggers the initial creation of the thread setting up initial registers and values within the thread as well. The OS can then set the initial registers, and input the argument that is wanted to get passed into the thread. This works because the work is split between what is done between the user and the OS. Where the user is defining the functions that are being triggered/called and defining the arguments passed into each one. While the OS is doing the work of setting up the thread and following through of the functions that are called by the user. This works because of the calling convention defined by the ABI, where the first argument of any function is passed to register R0. By placing the desired pointer in this register during the initial set up, the OS can ensure that the thread function receives it correctly when it runs.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,1,'2025-07-23_14-56-44','The pass an argument to a thread, the user must define the argument and either create a function enclosing (where the function takes the argument and may output it as well depending the type allowed into osCreateThread) this argument or directly input into the osCreateThread function depending on what the argument is. Where the argument may not be accepted by the osCreateThread function. This is the work that is to be done by the user. The user creates the function and passes a pointer to the argument of osCreateThread. Once the function or argument is passed into the osCreateThread function, the work now gets passed into the OS. Where the OS extracts the value directly from the argument or from the function enclosing the desired argument. Calling this function triggers the initial creation of the thread setting up initial registers and values within the thread as well. The OS can then set the initial registers, and input the argument that is wanted to get passed into the thread. Specifically, the OS passes the pointer to the argument into the memory location on the stack that corresponds to register R0. This works because the work is split between what is done between the user and the OS. Where the user is defining the functions that are being triggered/called and defining the arguments passed into each one. While the OS is doing the work of setting up the thread and following through of the functions that are called by the user. This works because of the calling convention defined by the ABI, where the first argument of any function is passed to register R0. By placing the desired pointer in this register during the initial set up, the OS can ensure that the thread function receives it correctly when it runs.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,2,'2025-07-21_13-59-36','In simple terms, the context-switching functions are already part of the OS’s context switching and by calling osYield within these functions would result in a nested loop. This means that if the context-switching functions have osYield within them, it would result in requesting another context-switch while one is already in progress. This would lead to nested PendSV_handler calls, as one is already in progress and then another one is triggered. The cortex processor may not be able to support this action and may behaviour unpredictably or even crash. This can also lead to corrupting the thread stack.\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,2,'2025-07-23_14-40-37','In simple terms, the context-switching functions are already part of the OS’s context switching and by calling osYield within these functions would result in a nested loop. First understanding what osYield does, osYield triggers a system call which then triggers an interrupt. This interrupt is what triggers the context-switch function to occur. This means that if the context-switching functions have osYield within them, it would result in requesting another context-switch while one is already in progress. This would lead to nested PendSV_handler calls, as one is already in progress and then another one is triggered. The cortex processor may not be able to support this action and may behaviour unpredictably, crash or result in a hardfault. The hardfault is referring to a critical error saying that the system has entered into an unrecoverable state due to unsafe operations at the operating level. This can also lead to corrupting the thread stack.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,2,'2025-07-23_14-56-44','In simple terms, the context-switching functions are already part of the OS’s context switching and by calling osYield within these functions would result in a nested loop. First understanding what osYield does, osYield triggers a system call which then triggers an interrupt. This interrupt is what triggers the context-switch function to occur. This means that if the context-switching functions have osYield within them, it would result in requesting another context-switch while one is already in progress. This would lead to nested PendSV_handler calls, as one is already in progress and then another one is triggered. The cortex processor may not be able to support this action and may behaviour unpredictably, crash or result in a hardfault. The hardfault is referring to a critical error saying that the system has entered into an unrecoverable state due to unsafe operations at the operating level. This can also lead to corrupting the thread stack.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,3,'2025-07-21_13-59-36','Changing the fundamental OS clock increases the frequency at which the OS performs its operations. Some benefits include:\nHigher resolution\nThis allows tasks timings to be more precise\nThis also allows modelling of real time systems to be accurate as there is a smaller delay between signals\nFaster context switching\nThe fundamental OS clock controls the delay between context switching and by having shorter increments of times, this allows for smaller delays between the switch, Improving responsiveness\nBut there are some down sides:\nIncreased overhead\nSince the timer interrupt is occurring more often the OS spends overall more time handling the interrupts and context switching\nThis also leads to more power consumption\nReduced efficiency\nA lot of programs won’t necessarily benefit from high resolution time, this can be a waste of GPU\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,3,'2025-07-23_14-40-37','Changing the fundamental OS clock increases the frequency at which the OS performs its operations. Some benefits include:\nHigher resolution\nThis allows tasks timings to be more precise\nThis also allows modelling of real time systems to be accurate as there is a smaller delay between signals\nFaster context switching\nThe fundamental OS clock controls the delay between context switching and by having shorter increments of times, this allows for smaller delays between the switch, Improving responsiveness\nBut there are some down sides:\nIncreased overhead\nSince the timer interrupt is occurring more often the OS spends overall more time handling the interrupts and context switching\nThis also leads to more power consumption\nReduced efficiency\nA lot of programs won’t necessarily benefit from high resolution time, this can be a waste of GPU\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,3,'2025-07-23_14-56-44','Changing the fundamental OS clock increases the frequency at which the OS performs its operations. Some benefits include:\nHigher resolution\nThis allows tasks timings to be more precise\nThis also allows modelling of real time systems to be accurate as there is a smaller delay between signals\nFaster context switching\nThe fundamental OS clock controls the delay between context switching and by having shorter increments of times, this allows for smaller delays between the switch, Improving responsiveness\nBut there are some down sides:\nIncreased overhead\nSince the timer interrupt is occurring more often the OS spends overall more time handling the interrupts and context switching\nThis also leads to more power consumption\nReduced efficiency\nA lot of programs won’t necessarily benefit from high resolution time, this can be a waste of GPU\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,4,'2025-07-21_13-59-36','The difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking is mostly relevant to the control that the OS and user have of the tasks. Context switching for co-operative is normally done with yielding and is done with the timer interrupt for pre-emptive. The user in this case can control when to give up control for a certain task, while pre-emptive multitasking is controlled by the OS. For scheduling, co-operative only happens once a task has been completed, while pre-emptive is a more smooth running where the tasks automatically run. This may lead to the user feeling that the system is unresponsive if a certain task doesn’t yield for co-operative. While for pre-emptive even if one task does not perform well, the system will continue to run.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,4,'2025-07-23_14-40-37','The difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking is mostly relevant to the control that the OS and user have of the tasks. Context switching for co-operative is normally done with yielding and is done with the timer interrupt for pre-emptive. The user in this case can control when to give up control for a certain task, while pre-emptive multitasking is controlled by the OS. For scheduling, co-operative only happens once a task has been completed, while pre-emptive is a more smooth running where the tasks automatically run. This may lead to the user feeling that the system is unresponsive if a certain task doesn’t yield for co-operative. While for pre-emptive even if one task does not perform well, the system will continue to run.\n',1,'s25');
INSERT INTO `question_responses` VALUES ('rbharadi',5,4,'2025-07-23_14-56-44','The difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking is mostly relevant to the control that the OS and user have of the tasks. Context switching for co-operative is normally done with yielding and is done with the timer interrupt for pre-emptive. The user in this case can control when to give up control for a certain task, while pre-emptive multitasking is controlled by the OS. For scheduling, co-operative only happens once a task has been completed, while pre-emptive is a more smooth running where the tasks automatically run. This may lead to the user feeling that the system is unresponsive if a certain task doesn’t yield for co-operative. While for pre-emptive even if one task does not perform well, the system will continue to run.\n',1,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,5,'2025-07-21_13-59-36','This is possible due to the way that the functions are set up. The lab instructed us to create two different OScreatethread, one where it uses the time-based pre-emptive context and one where the user would be able to input the timeslice and runtime. In practice the two mechanisms should not work together but in this lab the logic is set up to run both. Where yielding is called for a particular thread and the time-based pre-emptive context switching is called for the other. These are able to work together as the context switch has been defined for each individual thread. So the thread that is using the yielding is defined in the function that is the argument into the OscreateThread. Even though OsCreateThread has a predetermined and set timeslice and runtime, since the argument function has the yielding defined, this takes presumptive action over the default timeslice and runtime. Then the thread that is using the time based function is defined to have the timeslice and runtime. And this is run through into OsCreateThreadWithDeadline, showing that the thread set up is expecting an input from the user\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,5,'2025-07-23_14-40-37','This is possible due to the way that the functions are set up but still rely on the same method of PendSV interrupt. The lab instructed us to create two different OScreatethread, one where it uses the time-based pre-emptive context and one where the user would be able to input the timeslice and runtime. In practice the two mechanisms should not work together but in this lab the logic is set up to run both. Where yielding is called for a particular thread and the time-based pre-emptive context switching is called for the other. These are able to work together as the context switch has been defined for each individual thread. So the thread that is using the yielding is defined in the function that is the argument into the OscreateThread. When osYield is called it triggers the PendSV interrupt to request the context switching. The thread that is using the time based function is defined to have the timeslice and runtime, where the Systick interrupt is already firing at regular intervals and checks certain conditions against the function and then triggers PendSV interrupt for the context switching. And this is run through into OsCreateThreadWithDeadline, showing that the thread set up is expecting an input from the user\n',0,'s25'),('rbharadi',5,5,'2025-07-23_14-56-44','This is possible due to the way that the functions are set up but still rely on the same method of PendSV interrupt. The lab instructed us to create two different OScreatethread, one where it uses the time-based pre-emptive context and one where the user would be able to input the timeslice and runtime. In practice the two mechanisms should not work together but in this lab the logic is set up to run both. Where yielding is called for a particular thread and the time-based pre-emptive context switching is called for the other. These are able to work together as the context switch has been defined for each individual thread. So the thread that is using the yielding is defined in the function that is the argument into the OscreateThread. When osYield is called it triggers the PendSV interrupt to request the context switching. The thread that is using the time based function is defined to have the timeslice and runtime, where the Systick interrupt is already firing at regular intervals and checks certain conditions against the function and then triggers PendSV interrupt for the context switching. And this is run through into OsCreateThreadWithDeadline, showing that the thread set up is expecting an input from the user. In both cases, each method is calling the SAME PendSV interrupt, that is defined with the same logic. While the implementation of the context switching is different, (osYield vs. SysTick), both methods trigger the PendSV interrupt for the context switching. The logic for PendSV interrupt is shared between this yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching. For both methods, the PendSV interrupt is used, meaning that this interrupt is consistent between methods\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,1,'2025-06-25_10-49-02','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a layer of abstraction to help embedded developers interface with the hardware without needing to worry about the complexities of the hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',1,1,'2025-06-25_13-06-26','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a layer of abstraction to help embedded developers interface with the hardware without needing to worry about the complexities of the hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',1,1,'2025-06-25_15-35-09','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a layer of abstraction to help embedded developers interface with the hardware without needing to worry about the complexities of the hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',1,1,'2025-06-25_16-26-06','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a layer of abstraction to help embedded developers interface with the hardware without needing to worry about the complexities of the hardware. Utilizing a HAL can lead to better code portability, scalability, and safety across platforms, allowing the user to show the main logic of their code without bloating it with the complexities of accounting for hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,2,'2025-06-25_10-49-02','The __io__putchar function is used to allow for character data to be serially outputted on an embedded device. Without this, printf would not work on the embedded device.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',1,2,'2025-06-25_13-06-26','The __io__putchar function is used to allow for character data to be serially outputted on an embedded device. Without this, printf would not work on the embedded device.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',1,2,'2025-06-25_15-35-09','The __io__putchar function is used to allow for character data to be serially outputted on an embedded device. Without this, printf would not work on the embedded device.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',1,2,'2025-06-25_16-26-06','The __io__putchar function is used to allow for character data to be serially outputted on an embedded device. Without this, printf would not work on the embedded device. Because of varying architectures, embedded systems do not have a default printing method; __io__putchar abstracts away these differences, allowing the user to write clean code that is not bloated with the complexities of accounting for varying hardware platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,3,'2025-06-25_10-49-02','We use the debugger to observe register activity, which cannot be reliably observed with printf.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,3,'2025-06-25_13-06-26','We use the debugger to observe register activity, which cannot be reliably observed with printf.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',1,3,'2025-06-25_15-35-09','We use the debugger to observe register activity, which cannot be reliably observed with printf.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,3,'2025-06-25_16-26-06','We use the debugger to observe register activity, which cannot be reliably observed with printf.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,4,'2025-06-25_10-49-02','We read the first word of the vector table at address 0x0, then subtracted 0x400 from this initial value to allocate a new stack for PSP. Knowing the initial location of MSP allows us to ensure that the two stacks do not overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,4,'2025-06-25_13-06-26','We read the first word of the vector table at address 0x0, then subtracted 0x400 from this initial value to allocate a new stack for PSP. Knowing the initial location of MSP allows us to ensure that the two stacks do not overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,4,'2025-06-25_15-35-09','We read the first word of the vector table at address 0x0, then subtracted 0x400 from this initial value to allocate a new stack for PSP. Knowing the initial location of MSP allows us to ensure that the two stacks do not overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,4,'2025-06-25_16-26-06','We read the first word of the vector table at address 0x0, then subtracted 0x400 from this initial value to allocate a new stack for PSP. Knowing the initial location of MSP allows us to ensure that the two stacks do not overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,5,'2025-06-25_10-49-02','We can use ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE to compute the new stack address and ensure that each thread\'s stack is directly below the main stack without overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',1,5,'2025-06-25_13-06-26','We can use ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE to compute the new stack address and ensure that each thread\'s stack is directly below the main stack without overlap.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',1,5,'2025-06-25_15-35-09','We can use ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE to compute the new stack address and ensure that each thread\'s stack is directly below the main stack without overlap.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',1,5,'2025-06-25_16-26-06','We can use the MAIN_STACK_SIZE and THREAD_STACK_SIZE in ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (j * THREAD_STACK_SIZE) to compute each thread\'s starting address and ensure that each thread\'s stack is placed below the main stack without overlap, and within the available RAM space.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,1,'2025-06-25_23-22-02','When the PC register was altered to be set to the address of print_continuously, the CPU began executing print_continuously in an infinite sequential manner because to the CPU, this function was the next instruction after each cycle. Because this function contained an infinite loop of printed outputs, the program repeatedly printed \"Hello, PC!\" unless disengaged.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,1,'2025-06-26_21-31-23','When the PC register was altered to be set to the address of print_continuously, the CPU began executing print_continuously in an infinite sequential manner because to the CPU, this function was the next instruction after each cycle. Because this function contained an infinite loop of printed outputs, the program repeatedly printed \"Hello, PC!\" unless disengaged.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,1,'2025-06-27_12-17-46','When the PC register was altered to be set to the address of print_continuously, the CPU began executing print_continuously in an infinite sequential manner because to the CPU, this function was the next instruction after each cycle. Because this function contained an infinite loop of printed outputs, the program repeatedly printed \"Hello, PC!\" unless disengaged.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,1,'2025-06-27_22-35-29','When the PC register was altered to be set to the address of print_continuously, the CPU began executing print_continuously in an infinite sequential manner because to the CPU, this function was the next instruction after each cycle. Because this function contained an infinite loop of printed outputs, the program repeatedly printed \"Hello, PC!\" unless disengaged.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,2,'2025-06-25_23-22-02','A system call is a way for user programs to request services from the OS-managed hardware resources. These services are usually associated with hardware resources, process creation/scheduling, and other kernel-level duties.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',2,2,'2025-06-26_21-31-23','A system call is a way for user programs to request services from the OS-managed hardware resources. These services are usually associated with hardware resources, process creation/scheduling, and other kernel-level duties. It acts as an interface between the user space and kernel space, making sure that user programs cannot directly access the hardware resources. This makes the system more secure and stable by providing an interface that reviews and mediates requests instead of allowing access to any program.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',2,2,'2025-06-27_12-17-46','A system call is a special kind of function used by user programs to request services from the OS-managed hardware resources. These services are usually associated with accessing hardware resources, process creation/scheduling, performing I/O, and other kernel-level duties. It acts as an interface between the user space and kernel space, using software interrupts to make sure that user programs cannot directly access the hardware resources. This makes the system more secure and stable by providing a function-calling interface that reviews and mediates requests instead of allowing access to any program (via all accesses going through predefined, validated kernel functions).\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,2,'2025-06-27_22-35-29','A system call is a special kind of function used by user programs to request services from the OS-managed hardware resources. These services are usually associated with accessing hardware resources, process creation/scheduling, performing I/O, and other kernel-level duties. It acts as an interface between the user space and kernel space, using software interrupts to make sure that user programs cannot directly access the hardware resources. This makes the system more secure and stable by providing a function-calling interface that reviews and mediates requests instead of allowing access to any program (via all accesses going through predefined, validated kernel functions).\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,3,'2025-06-25_23-22-02','To set up the thread\'s stack, I initialized it with values of registers that the CPU expects (xPSR, PC, LR, General Purpose Registers). These values are stored so that when a thread is switched to, it can pop the values of the hardware registers and execute the thread, allowing the system to quickly stop and resume execution of various threads.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',2,3,'2025-06-26_21-31-23','To set up the thread\'s stack, I initialized it with values of registers that the CPU expects (xPSR, PC, LR, General Purpose Registers). These values are stored so that when a thread is switched to, it can pop the values of the hardware registers and execute the thread, allowing the system to quickly stop and resume execution of various threads. This ensures that registers are stored in the correct order - if not, unpredictable behaviour could occur when switching between threads and restoring context. Furthermore, the system requires a valid memory location for the stack to eliminate the risk of hard faults or trying to access other memory locations that are reserved for other resources.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,3,'2025-06-27_12-17-46','To set up the thread\'s stack, I initialized it with values of registers that the CPU expects (xPSR, PC, LR, General Purpose Registers). These values are stored so that when a thread is switched to, it can pop the values of the hardware registers and execute the thread, allowing the system to quickly stop and resume execution of various threads. This ensures that registers are stored in the correct order - if not, unpredictable behaviour could occur when switching between threads and restoring context. Furthermore, the system requires a valid memory location for the stack to eliminate the risk of hard faults or trying to access other memory locations that are reserved for other resources.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,3,'2025-06-27_22-35-29','To set up the thread\'s stack, I initialized it with values of registers that the CPU expects (xPSR, PC, LR, General Purpose Registers). These values are stored so that when a thread is switched to, it can pop the values of the hardware registers and execute the thread, allowing the system to quickly stop and resume execution of various threads. This ensures that registers are stored in the correct order - if not, unpredictable behaviour could occur when switching between threads and restoring context. Furthermore, the system requires a valid memory location for the stack to eliminate the risk of hard faults or trying to access other memory locations that are reserved for other resources.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,4,'2025-06-25_23-22-02','PSP was set with the __set_PSP function so that the processor used a valid stack pointer to restore the state of the thread. If we did not do this, the CPU would try to access an invalid location in memory, which would cause the hard fault crash.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',2,4,'2025-06-26_21-31-23','PSP was set with the __set_PSP function so that the processor used a valid stack pointer to restore the state of the thread. If we did not do this, the CPU would try to access an invalid location in memory, which would cause the hard fault crash. The CPU uses the MSP by default, but threads should use the PSP to separate between system and thread stacks. Having this partition between the two stacks ensures that threads and stacks use their own respective memory space. Before running each thread, we should set the PSP to point to the thread\'s stack so that there is a valid location for the CPU to store register state - if not, the CPU could use an invalid stack location and hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,4,'2025-06-27_12-17-46','PSP was set with the __set_PSP function so that the processor used a valid stack pointer to restore the state of the thread. If we did not do this, the CPU would try to access an invalid location in memory, which would cause the hard fault crash. The CPU uses the MSP by default, but threads should use the PSP to separate between system and thread stacks. Having this partition between the two stacks ensures that threads and stacks use their own respective memory space. Before running each thread, we should set the PSP to point to the thread\'s stack so that there is a valid location for the CPU to store register state - if not, the CPU could use an invalid stack location and hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',2,4,'2025-06-27_22-35-29','By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which points to a memory region reserved for system-level operations like interrupt handling. However, each thread should use its own stack located in a separate memory region, accessed via the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). We use __set_PSP to assign the PSP to the base of the thread’s stack so the CPU can correctly restore each register\'s value and use that stack during thread execution. If we don’t set the PSP, the CPU will continue using the MSP or an undefined location, which can lead to a hard fault when it tries to access invalid memory. (Answer was rephrased better using AI).\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,5,'2025-06-25_23-22-02','This would likely cause a hard fault or crash because the CPU would try to pop the return address off the stack and jump to it despite the thread not being meant to return, causing the previously mentioned hard fault or crash.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',2,5,'2025-06-26_21-31-23','This would likely cause a hard fault or crash because the CPU would try to pop the return address off the stack and jump to it despite the thread not being meant to return, causing the previously mentioned hard fault or crash. If the function returns, the CPU will try to pop a return address from the thread\'s stack, which was never placed there in the first place. When the CPU jumps to this location, it could access an invalid memory location, which would cause a hard fault. This scenario exhibits how threads are not meant to return like normal functions until terminated by the Operating System.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,5,'2025-06-27_12-17-46','This would likely cause a hard fault or crash because the CPU would try to pop the return address off the stack and jump to it despite the thread not being meant to return, causing the previously mentioned hard fault or crash. If the function returns, the CPU will try to pop a return address from the thread\'s stack, which was never placed there in the first place. When the CPU jumps to this location, it could access an invalid memory location, which would cause a hard fault. This scenario exhibits how threads are not meant to return like normal functions until terminated by the Operating System.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',2,5,'2025-06-27_22-35-29','This would likely cause a hard fault or crash because the CPU would try to pop the return address off the stack and jump to it despite the thread not being meant to return, causing the previously mentioned hard fault or crash. If the function returns, the CPU will try to pop a return address from the thread\'s stack, which was never placed there in the first place. When the CPU jumps to this location, it could access an invalid memory location, which would cause a hard fault. This scenario exhibits how threads are not meant to return like normal functions until terminated by the Operating System.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',3,1,'2025-06-27_22-58-59','Each .c file is run through the preprocessing step, which expands headers and macros to produce a \"self-contained translation unit\". Then, the compiler converts each \"unit\" to a \".o\" (object) file, where the C code is interpreted into assembly and converted into machine code that the system can execute. Finally, the linker links together all of the object files and any libraries used, resolves any cross-referenced files, and combines everything into a singular executable. If any symbols are not defined, the linker will report any as errors.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',3,2,'2025-06-27_22-58-59','This is because the preprocessing step of compilation expands any included headers, meaning that including a .c file with #include will expand the entire implementation into the current translation unit, which will defeat separate compilation and may result in duplicate-definition errors when that same code is compiled in another location. This also results in longer compile times and makes it hard to reason about which .o (object) file actually owns each function. Because of this, it is best practice to only put declarations in header files and compile each .c function into its respective .o file and let the linker fix any discrepancies. This promotes modularity compilation, avoids clashes, and lets builds stay efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',3,3,'2025-06-27_22-58-59','Initialize the kernel: Call the kernel initialization function to save the CPU\'s stack pointer and reset kernel data structures.\nWrite Thread Function: Provide a function that details the instructions that a specific thread should follow.\nCreate the Thread: Call the kernel\'s \"create thread\" API, passing the user\'s function as a pointer, which will allocate a private thread\'s stack and start execution of its program.\nStart Kernel: Call the kernel\'s \"Start\" function, which may use a supervisor call to switch the context to the new thread\'s stack and start execution\nRun Thread: The kernel handler will respond to the supervisor call by loading the thread\'s respective stack pointer and jumping into the user\'s thread function and execute until termination or switching to execute another thread.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',3,4,'2025-06-27_22-58-59','The stack pool is a section of RAM that is reserved for all thread stacks and separated from the processors main stack region. It is divided into 8-byte aligned blocks of equal size to ensure each thread gets its own reserved space in memory. When the thread-creation API is called, the kernel\'s stack allocator will return the next free block in the pool and return its address. This could be extended in the future to support multiple blocks, allowing the system to create several independent threads that can be ran or scheduled asynchronously.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',3,5,'2025-06-27_22-58-59','From the user\'s perspective, application-level logic (main() setup, I/O, independent thread functions) belongs exclusively in user code files. Kernel files (kernel.h and kernel.c) help encapsulate low-level OS duties like stack-pool management, the supervisor call handler, thread structures, and APIs used like osCreateThread and osKernelStart. Keeping these duties serparated ensures that the user can call kernel services through a mediated interface while keeping these resources safe and not risking OS functionality corruption. This also lets user applications to be operated on (recompiled, modified) without worrying about the kernel, which helps for more efficient build times, maintainability, and scalability. As our OS evolves, this partition keeps user code independent of internal changes and helps make the system safer.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',4,1,'2025-07-30_22-52-05','The scheduler dequeues the next TCB in the queue, runs it for an allotted timeslice, and then enqueues it at the tail. This allows all operations to be O(1) when carrying out scheduling operations. The queue natively supports dynamic thread creation and termination, simplifies indexing logic and decouples the relation of TCBs to a fixed size array. Using a queue replaces the manual index searching that would have been needed if an array was used. The downside of this approach is the extra pointer management needed, which might be disadvantageous for low-latency applications.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',4,1,'2025-07-31_12-00-04','While our current circular-buffer scheduler already behaves like a bounded queue—bumping a head index, running that TCB, then bumping the tail index—using an explicit linked‐list queue decouples you from a fixed-size array and eliminates wrap-around logic. You still get O(1) enqueue/dequeue but pay extra pointer‐chasing and potential cache misses, which in a deeply embedded or real-time system could outweigh the simplicity gains. In use cases with frequent dynamic thread creation or termination (e.g., USB stacks or network servers), that flexibility justifies the overhead; otherwise, a compact circular buffer may be more efficient.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',4,2,'2025-07-30_22-52-05','PendSV_Handler uses the hardware-based exception stack frame (R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR) to begin the context save by first reading the PSP to locate the tasks\'s stack, the pushes R4-R11 onto the stack. Then, it inserts the new PSP into the task\'s TCB, loads the new task\'s PSP from the TCB, and pop R4-R11 from the stack. Updating PSP and executing the exception return allow the processor to natively unstack the stack frame and switch contexts.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',4,2,'2025-07-31_12-00-04','When entered, PendSV_Handler uses the PSP to locate the exiting task\'s exception stack frame, pushes R4–R11, and sets up the new PSP in the current TCB. It then calls osSched(), which returns the next available TCB and its saved PSP. The PendSV_Handler finally loads that PSP, pops R4–R11 from the new stack and issues the exception return so the hardware will automatically reload R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR for the selected thread.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',4,3,'2025-07-30_22-52-05','This is a bad idea because the scheduler would likely get stuck in a cycle of only running high-priority tasks and letting low-priority tasks wait for an obscene amount of time, or \"starve\". This often happened when a high-priority task runs long or infrequently blocks. Under the assumption of static priorities, the \"fairness\" of the scheduler is degraded heavily. Without mechanisms to mitigate this risk (dynamic adjustment, dynamic priorities), lower-priority tasks may never run.\n',0,'s25'),('rfudge',4,3,'2025-07-31_12-00-04','With a strict \"always select highest-priority\" scheduler, one runnable high-priority task will be chosen every dispatch and therefore no lower-priority tasks ever execute—effectively only that one task ever executes. Because it keeps the CPU occupied (until it blocks or terminates), all other tasks are starved forever. Without priority aging, timeslicing between levels, or dynamic adjustment, you remove fairness and prevent any other work from progressing.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',4,4,'2025-07-30_22-52-05','Writing the “osSched” and “SVC_HandlerMain” functions in C ensures that each function is maintainable, scalable, portable, and takes advantage of compiler optimizations. The downside of this is that it is inherently slower than Assembly because each function call pushes R4-R11 (callee-saved registers) and updates the PSP, which increases latency and bloats the code. In assembly, these functions could likely be optimized heavily, but this is very complex, hard to maintain and make changes to, and reduces code readability.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',4,4,'2025-07-31_12-00-04','Writing the “osSched” and “SVC_HandlerMain” functions in C ensures that each function is maintainable, scalable, portable, and takes advantage of compiler optimizations. The downside of this is that it is inherently slower than Assembly because each function call pushes R4-R11 (callee-saved registers) and updates the PSP, which increases latency and bloats the code. In assembly, these functions could likely be optimized heavily, but this is very complex, hard to maintain and make changes to, and reduces code readability.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',4,5,'2025-07-30_22-52-05','The OS would still work - on startup, the individual thread runs until it hits its first yield, saving its context. The scheduler only sees that one thread ready, so it almost instantaneously restores the context and resumes the thread once again. With just the individual thread, the scheduling process just becomes a loop of the same task. This causes a loss of concurrency but preserves the control flow of the operating system. The context is switched on every yield regardless of the fact that there is only one thread. The scheduler is stil used for handling interrupts or future threads, if introduced.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',4,5,'2025-07-31_12-00-04','The OS would still work - on startup, the individual thread runs until it hits its first yield, saving its context. The scheduler only sees that one thread ready, so it almost instantaneously restores the context and resumes the thread once again. With just the individual thread, the scheduling process just becomes a loop of the same task. This causes a loss of concurrency but preserves the control flow of the operating system. The context is switched on every yield regardless of the fact that there is only one thread. The scheduler is stil used for handling interrupts or future threads, if introduced.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',5,1,'2025-07-30_23-36-40','When calling osCreateThread, a pointer to the thread function and the argument pointer to be received is passed. The operating system then allocates a fresh stack for the new thread, constructs an initial exception stack frame on that stack, and then stores this argument in the ABI slot (R0) and sets the PSP to point at the current frame. When the scheduler switches to the thread, the hardware\'s exception-return feature pops the frame and branches to the function. Since the OS setup and the processor\'s unstacking are EABI-compliant, the thread will see the correct function argument.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',5,2,'2025-07-30_23-36-40','This cannot be done because osYield will attempt a context-switch exception (by SVC or PendSV) while the OS is already mid-exception. This nested exception before the hardware has finished saving the current context will corrupt the PSP or processor-pushed registers. This can cause an infinite recursive call or a hard fault, so the stack should be manipulated directly instead of calling osYield.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',5,3,'2025-07-30_23-36-40','Running the OS tick at 0.1ms instead of 1ms provides 10 times more precision. This allows for more precise delays, faster response, and tighter control loops. This response in a full order of magnitude more SysTick interrupts, which increases processing overhead and can starve processes and miss deadlines. This will also result in higher power consumption and a larger share of interrupts during execution. In general, the cons to this surmount the possible benefits.\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',5,4,'2025-07-30_23-36-40','In co-operative multitasking, a task must call a function such as osYield to free the CPU. From the user\'s perspective, yields are at safe points, and the OS switches context upon request.Pre-emptive multitasking uses a \"periodic\" interrupt (like SysTick) to queue a context switch, so the user doesn\'t have to manually yield, but the OS should save and restore its contexts on each tick. Co-operative scheduling is general simpler and has lower interrupt overhead but can stall if a task does not yield. Pre-emptive scheduling promises response with the downside of interrupt and context-switch overhead\n',1,'s25'),('rfudge',5,5,'2025-07-30_23-36-40','Both osYield and SysTick pend the PendSV exception, funneling into the same context-switch handler. osYield causes the handler flag to be instantaneously set, while SysTick\'s ISR sets that same flag on the periodic tick. PendSV then gets executed, saving the initial thread\'s context, scheduling, and restoring another thread\'s context. This happens regardless of the chosen method of switching between tasks, allowing for portability and continuity between different approaches.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',1,1,'2025-05-14_16-10-40','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) for STM32Cube is a software library that simplifies the interaction between the code and hardware in STM32 microcontrollers. It provides high level access to the microcontroller\'s peripherals and acts as an abstraction layer.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',1,1,'2025-05-19_19-21-02','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) for STM32Cube is a software library that simplifies the interaction between the code and hardware in STM32 microcontrollers. It provides high level access to the microcontroller\'s peripherals and acts as an abstraction layer.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',1,2,'2025-05-14_16-10-40','It connects the printf function to the UART hardware. In embedded systems, there is no screen. It sends the characters to the UART using HAL.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',1,2,'2025-05-19_19-21-02','The __io_putchar function connects printf to the UART by defining how characters get sent out. Since embedded systems don’t have standard outputs like screens, we need a way to redirect printf to something like UART. On its own, printf doesn’t know how to talk to hardware—it relies on __io_putchar to do that.\nThis setup gives flexibility, letting programmers adapt printf for different outputs like USB or displays. It’s up to the programmer to write __io_putchar to match the hardware and use the right HAL functions to send data where it needs to go.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',1,3,'2025-05-14_16-10-40','We used the debugger in this lab because it lets us pause the program and directly see what\'s happening inside the processor. It shows the values of the registers; printf runs after the code has executed and can’t show the low level details.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',1,3,'2025-05-19_19-21-02','We used the debugger in this lab because it lets us pause the program and directly see what\'s happening inside the processor. It shows the values of the registers; printf runs after the code has executed and can’t show the low level details.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',1,4,'2025-05-14_16-10-40','The stack allocation method we used works by first identifying the starting location of the main stack, called MSP_init_val at address 0x0 and points to the top of the main stack. We created a new Process Stack Pointer (PSP) value by subtracting the main stack (0x400 bytes) from MSP_init_val. This ensures the thread PSP stack starts just below the MSP stack, avoiding overlap.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',1,4,'2025-05-19_19-21-02','We set up the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by starting with the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), called MSP_init_val, which is stored at address 0x0 and points to the top of the main stack. To create space for the PSP, we simply subtracted 0x400 bytes from MSP_init_val, placing it just below the main stack to avoid any overlap.\nBut it’s not just about avoiding overlap—using MSP_init_val also makes sure the PSP ends up in a valid memory region that’s properly aligned and safe to use. If we picked a random address instead, even if it didn’t overlap, we might run into memory faults or unexpected behavior. So, using the initial MSP value helps keep the PSP setup reliable and safe.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',1,5,'2025-05-14_16-10-40','To allocate multiple stacks, we first reserve space for the main stack starting from MSP_init_val. For each new thread, we calculate its stack start address by adding the main stack size and an offset increment  thread number with a standard thread stack size. For example, stack j starts at MSP_init_val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This way, we can set up fixed stack locations in memory for all threads when compiling.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',1,5,'2025-05-19_19-21-02','To allocate multiple stacks, we first reserve space for the main stack starting from MSP_init_val. For each new thread, we calculate its stack start address by adding the main stack size and an offset increment  thread number with a standard thread stack size. For example, stack j starts at MSP_init_val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This way, we can set up fixed stack locations in memory for all threads when compiling.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',2,1,'2025-06-04_22-36-51','By setting print_continuously\'s location into PC (MOV PC, R0), we caused the CPU\'s next instruction to be fetched from that function instead. Instead of resuming at main, it dropped directly into the infinite loop in print_continuously, and \"Hello, PC!\" was printed over and over again. That is, by setting PC manually, the call/return cycle was broken and control transferred directly to our print function.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',2,1,'2025-06-04_22-52-34','By setting print_continuously\'s location into PC (MOV PC, R0), we caused the CPU\'s next instruction to be fetched from that function instead. Instead of resuming at main, it dropped directly into the infinite loop in print_continuously, and \"Hello, PC!\" was printed over and over again. That is, by setting PC manually, the call/return cycle was broken and control transferred directly to our print function.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',2,1,'2025-06-04_23-10-13','By setting print_continuously\'s location into PC (MOV PC, R0), we caused the CPU\'s next instruction to be fetched from that function instead. Instead of resuming at main, it dropped directly into the infinite loop in print_continuously, and \"Hello, PC!\" was printed over and over again. That is, by setting PC manually, the call/return cycle was broken and control transferred directly to our print function.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',2,1,'2025-06-04_23-19-19','By setting print_continuously\'s location into PC (MOV PC, R0), we caused the CPU\'s next instruction to be fetched from that function instead. Instead of resuming at main, it dropped directly into the infinite loop in print_continuously, and \"Hello, PC!\" was printed over and over again. That is, by setting PC manually, the call/return cycle was broken and control transferred directly to our print function.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',2,2,'2025-06-04_22-36-51','A system call is a controlled switch between unprivileged \"user\" and privileged \"kernel\" (or supervisor) mode to allow the program to perform operations it is otherwise not allowed to perform directly. Calling SVC #n on Cortex-M causes an exception: the processor jumps to the SVC handler (which executes in supervisor mode), retrieves call number n, and jumps to the related function (e.g., I/O or context switch). In short, a system call is the mechanism by which application code solicits the OS (or our supervisor code) to take some action on its behalf.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',2,2,'2025-06-04_22-52-34','A system call is a controlled switch from unprivileged “user” code into privileged “kernel” (or supervisor) code so the program can perform operations it isn’t allowed to do directly. On Cortex-M, executing SVC #n triggers an exception: the CPU vectors to the SVC handler (running in privileged mode), reads the call number n, and dispatches to the appropriate routine (e.g., I/O or context switching). In short, a system call is the mechanism by which application code asks the OS (or our supervisor code) to do something on its behalf.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',2,2,'2025-06-04_23-10-13','A system call (also called a supervisor call or software trap) is a controlled transition from unprivileged thread code into privileged handler code, triggered by executing SVC #n. This generates a software interrupt—the CPU switches into Supervisor mode, vectors to the SVC handler, and extracts the call number n from the instruction stream. That number indexes into a kernel-style dispatch routine, which performs operations (e.g., I/O, memory management, or context switching) on behalf of the user thread. By forcing all hardware-sensitive or security-sensitive actions through this trap mechanism, the OS enforces privilege separation: unprivileged code cannot directly access protected peripherals or memory regions. Instead, every request goes through the SVC handler, which checks the call number and arguments before allowing or denying access.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',2,2,'2025-06-04_23-19-19','A system call (also called a supervisor call or software trap) is a controlled transition from unprivileged thread code into privileged handler code, triggered by executing SVC #n. This generates a software interrupt—the CPU switches into Supervisor mode, vectors to the SVC handler, and extracts the call number n from the instruction stream. That number indexes into a kernel-style dispatch routine, which performs operations (e.g., I/O, memory management, or context switching) on behalf of the user thread. By forcing all hardware-sensitive or security-sensitive actions through this trap mechanism, the OS enforces privilege separation: unprivileged code cannot directly access protected peripherals or memory regions. Instead, every request goes through the SVC handler, which checks the call number and arguments before allowing or denying access.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',2,3,'2025-06-04_22-36-51','',0,'s25'),('rruan',2,3,'2025-06-04_22-52-34','',0,'s25'),('rruan',2,3,'2025-06-04_23-10-13','On Cortex-M, there are two stack pointers: MSP (Main Stack Pointer) used in Handler mode (exceptions/interrupts), and PSP (Process Stack Pointer) used in Thread mode (regular code). When the SVC handler finishes, it executes an exception return that tells the hardware to switch into Thread mode and use PSP. If we don’t call __set_PSP(stackptr) first, PSP remains either zero or whatever leftover value it had—not pointing at our prepared context frame. Consequently, the exception-return sequence will pop register values from an invalid address, causing a HardFault. By explicitly setting PSP to our thread’s stack base, we ensure that upon exiting the SVC handler, the CPU loads R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR, etc., from the correct memory region. In short, PSP must be pointed at the thread’s own stack frame rather than MSP’s memory, because only that stack holds the fake context we built; otherwise, the CPU cannot restore a valid state and crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',2,3,'2025-06-04_23-19-19','On Cortex-M, there are two stack pointers: MSP (Main Stack Pointer) used in Handler mode (exceptions/interrupts), and PSP (Process Stack Pointer) used in Thread mode (regular code). When the SVC handler finishes, it executes an exception return that tells the hardware to switch into Thread mode and use PSP. If we don’t call __set_PSP(stackptr) first, PSP remains either zero or whatever leftover value it had—not pointing at our prepared context frame. Consequently, the exception-return sequence will pop register values from an invalid address, causing a HardFault. By explicitly setting PSP to our thread’s stack base, we ensure that upon exiting the SVC handler, the CPU loads R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR, etc., from the correct memory region. In short, PSP must be pointed at the thread’s own stack frame rather than MSP’s memory, because only that stack holds the fake context we built; otherwise, the CPU cannot restore a valid state and crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',2,4,'2025-06-04_22-36-51','If we had not called __set_PSP(stackptr) before, PSP would be pointing to whichever invalid or residual value it held before, which is often zero. When the exception return then tried to pop the registers it had stacked off of PSP, it would be reading off of an invalid location and faulting with a HardFault. By setting PSP to our setup stack (with a valid dummy exception frame), we permit the CPU to pop valid register values; without this, the CPU faults instantly because it can’t restore a valid context off of an invalid PSP.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',2,4,'2025-06-04_22-52-34','If we didn’t call __set_PSP(stackptr) first, PSP would still point to whatever invalid or leftover value it had (often zero). Then, when the exception return tried to pop the saved registers from PSP, it would read from an invalid location, causing a HardFault. By setting PSP to our prepared stack (with a valid fake exception frame), we ensure the CPU pops correct register values; otherwise, the CPU would fault immediately because it can’t restore a valid context from a bogus PSP.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',2,4,'2025-06-04_23-10-13','If the thread function had printed and returned, the CPU would reload LR from the stored stack frame—but since we added a dummy value (0xA) to LR, that “return address” is bogus. The CPU would attempt to run instructions at the bogus address and fault within microseconds. In a real OS, you’d usually set LR to be the address of cleanup or thread-exit function; since we did not, normal return crashes with HardFault.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',2,4,'2025-06-04_23-19-19','If the thread function had printed and returned, the CPU would reload LR from the stored stack frame—but since we added a dummy value (0xA) to LR, that “return address” is bogus. The CPU would attempt to run instructions at the bogus address and fault within microseconds. In a real OS, you’d usually set LR to be the address of cleanup or thread-exit function; since we did not, normal return crashes with HardFault.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',2,5,'2025-06-04_22-36-51','If the thread function had printed and returned, the CPU would reload LR from the stored stack frame—but since we added a dummy value (0xA) to LR, that “return address” is bogus. The CPU would attempt to run instructions at the bogus address and fault within microseconds. In a real OS, you’d usually set LR to be the address of cleanup or thread-exit function; since we did not, normal return crashes with HardFault.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',2,5,'2025-06-04_22-52-34','If the thread function had printed and returned, the CPU would reload LR from the stored stack frame—but since we added a dummy value (0xA) to LR, that “return address” is bogus. The CPU would attempt to run instructions at the bogus address and fault within microseconds. In a real OS, you’d usually set LR to be the address of cleanup or thread-exit function; since we did not, normal return crashes with HardFault.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',3,1,'2025-06-27_23-05-06','Each C file is compiled separately into an object file by the compiler. These object files contain machine code but are not complete programs. The linker then combines all the object files together, connecting all the pieces, such as functions and variables that are used across files. The final result is a single executable program that the computer can run.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',3,2,'2025-06-27_23-05-06','Including a C file directly means copying all of its contents into another file before compilation. This can cause problems like defining the same function or variable more than once. It also makes the program harder to understand and manage. The proper way is to include only header files and let each C file be compiled on its own. Then the linker connects them correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',3,3,'2025-06-27_23-05-06','First, the user writes a function that contains the thread\'s behavior.\nSecond, the user calls a thread creation function, giving it the function to run and any needed information.\nThird, the user calls a kernel initialization function to prepare the system.\nFourth, the user starts the kernel to begin running threads.\nFrom then on, the system takes control and manages the execution of the threads.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',3,4,'2025-06-27_23-05-06','The stack pool is a section of memory set aside for thread stacks. Each thread needs its own private stack space to keep track of its own function calls and local variables. In a future lab, each new thread will be assigned a part of the stack pool. This way, when the system switches from one thread to another, each thread keeps its own memory safe and separate.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',3,5,'2025-06-27_23-05-06','The kernel contains the core parts of the operating system, like thread management and scheduling, and is written in kernel dot c and kernel dot h. The user code includes the application logic that uses the kernel services, and this goes into the main file.\nThis separation is important because it allows the user to write applications without touching the core operating system code. It keeps the code organized and makes it easier to reuse and expand the system.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',4,1,'2025-07-29_12-22-35','Round-robin scheduling can utilize a queue by keeping each thread\'s control block in a first-in, first-out data structure. If a thread yields or completes a turn, its control block is taken from the front end and then pushed to the tail after saving its state. The scheduler then executes the next thread from the front end of the queue. It allows dynamic additions or deletions to be easier and cycle through tasks naturally without having to keep array indices by hand.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',4,1,'2025-07-29_13-21-40','Round-robin with a queue stores TCBs in FIFO; on yield, the head moves to the tail and the next head runs. Your array-based scheduler is already queue-like (a circular buffer), so behavior is equivalent. A real queue helps when threads join/exit at runtime: O(1) enqueue/dequeue without shifting elements. Trade-offs: extra pointer fields and pointer-chasing (slight RAM/latency) versus an array’s locality and a single modulo increment. For fixed threads use an array; for dynamic sets the queue’s flexibility can justify the tiny overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',4,2,'2025-07-29_12-22-35','The PendSV handler does a context switch by storing away a thread\'s current processor state and loading its next. It pushes its own registers up onto its own stack and increments its stored stack pointer. It then gives a call to its own scheduler to get its next, sets up its stack pointer to its new one, and restores registers so it picks up where it left off. That way, it\'s very easy to switch between threads.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',4,2,'2025-07-29_13-21-40','The PendSV handler switches context using the process stack pointer (PSP), while the main stack (MSP) is reserved for handlers. On entry, hardware pushes R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR to the PSP; the handler saves R4–R11, stores the PSP in the current TCB, calls the scheduler, loads the next thread’s PSP, and restores R4–R11. The exception return then pops the hardware frame and resumes that thread. PSP is key because it isolates per-thread stacks, and ARM’s ABI requires preserving R4–R11 across calls.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',4,3,'2025-07-29_12-22-35','If a scheduler simply takes a highest priority thread, then lower priority threads might even starve and never get a scheduling opportunity. There might be a high priority thread that just runs or yields back to itself so no other threads are even given a chance to make progress. This causes biased scheduling and might even dead lock background or lower priority work.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',4,3,'2025-07-29_13-21-40','If a scheduler simply takes a highest priority thread, then lower priority threads might even starve and never get a scheduling opportunity. There might be a high priority thread that just runs or yields back to itself so no other threads are even given a chance to make progress. This causes biased scheduling and might even dead lock background or lower priority work.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',4,4,'2025-07-29_12-22-35','Coding with a call to a C routine as a scheduler rather than hardcoding with assembly makes code more readable, comprehensible, and maintainable. It\'s quite easy to do complex things such as, oh, say, manipulate arrays or queues of thread blocks with C. The disadvantages are a loss of speed by shifting from assembly to C or from a higher-level language to assembly since there\'s overhead and register saving. Programs coded with assembly by themselves are faster but less maintainable, less transportable.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',4,4,'2025-07-29_13-21-40','Coding with a call to a C routine as a scheduler rather than hardcoding with assembly makes code more readable, comprehensible, and maintainable. It\'s quite easy to do complex things such as, oh, say, manipulate arrays or queues of thread blocks with C. The disadvantages are a loss of speed by shifting from assembly to C or from a higher-level language to assembly since there\'s overhead and register saving. Programs coded with assembly by themselves are faster but less maintainable, less transportable.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',4,5,'2025-07-29_12-22-35','If just a single thread is generated that yields during the print, then a system still runs but becomes a rudimentary infinite loop. In every call to yield, a context switch will be attempted by the scheduler but will just switch to the same one every time because there are no other threads to switch to. The net effect being that a system still runs but does unnecessary context switch with no real multitasking to offer.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',4,5,'2025-07-29_13-21-40','If just a single thread is generated that yields during the print, then a system still runs but becomes a rudimentary infinite loop. In every call to yield, a context switch will be attempted by the scheduler but will just switch to the same one every time because there are no other threads to switch to. The net effect being that a system still runs but does unnecessary context switch with no real multitasking to offer.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,1,'2025-07-29_13-43-18','Package the thread’s inputs into one structure, pass a single pointer to it, and have the thread immediately copy out the contents on start. The OS creates the new thread’s initial stack frame and places that pointer where the first argument is expected so the function begins with it ready to use. This works because the calling convention defines how first parameters are delivered; by matching that at thread launch, a thread starts like a normal function call while one pointer can represent any amount of data.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',5,1,'2025-07-29_14-14-32','From the user side, you package the data the thread needs and pass the address of that package to osCreateThread as the thread’s argument. The OS then builds the thread’s initial stack frame exactly as a Cortex-M4 expects on first entry: it sets the program counter to the thread function, prepares the status and link registers, and—crucially—places the argument pointer in the saved R0 slot of that startup frame. When the scheduler finally launches the thread (by returning from PendSV into Thread mode using the process stack), the core restores registers from that frame so the function begins with R0 already holding the user’s argument pointer. This works because the ARM calling convention defines that a function’s first argument is passed in R0, so pre-loading R0 in the prepared frame makes the first call into the thread indistinguishable from a normal C function call.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,1,'2025-07-29_14-22-39','From the user side, you package the data the thread needs and pass the address of that package to osCreateThread as the thread’s argument. The OS then builds the thread’s initial stack frame exactly as a Cortex-M4 expects on first entry: it sets the program counter to the thread function, prepares the status and link registers, and—crucially—places the argument pointer in the saved R0 slot of that startup frame. When the scheduler finally launches the thread (by returning from PendSV into Thread mode using the process stack), the core restores registers from that frame so the function begins with R0 already holding the user’s argument pointer. This works because the ARM calling convention defines that a function’s first argument is passed in R0, so pre-loading R0 in the prepared frame makes the first call into the thread indistinguishable from a normal C function call.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,1,'2025-07-29_14-53-43','From the user side, you package the data the thread needs and pass the address of that package to osCreateThread as the thread’s argument. The OS then builds the thread’s initial stack frame exactly as a Cortex-M4 expects on first entry: it sets the program counter to the thread function, prepares the status and link registers, and—crucially—places the argument pointer in the saved R0 slot of that startup frame. When the scheduler finally launches the thread (by returning from PendSV into Thread mode using the process stack), the core restores registers from that frame so the function begins with R0 already holding the user’s argument pointer. This works because the ARM calling convention defines that a function’s first argument is passed in R0, so pre-loading R0 in the prepared frame makes the first call into the thread indistinguishable from a normal C function call.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,1,'2025-07-30_11-23-25','From the user side, you package the data the thread needs and pass the address of that package to osCreateThread as the thread’s argument. The OS then builds the thread’s initial stack frame exactly as a Cortex-M4 expects on first entry: it sets the program counter to the thread function, prepares the status and link registers, and—crucially—places the argument pointer in the saved R0 slot of that startup frame. When the scheduler finally launches the thread (by returning from PendSV into Thread mode using the process stack), the core restores registers from that frame so the function begins with R0 already holding the user’s argument pointer. This works because the ARM calling convention defines that a function’s first argument is passed in R0, so pre-loading R0 in the prepared frame makes the first call into the thread indistinguishable from a normal C function call.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,2,'2025-07-29_13-43-18','These handlers already run in privileged exception code that either performs a switch, dispatches a system call, or advances the tick. Calling a thread-level yield inside them mixes control paths and can cause re-entrancy and nested switch requests. The tick handler should remain minimal—do hardware work, update time, and request a switch—rather than invoke yield. If you do call it there, you risk recursion, corrupted stacks, missed ticks, broken printing, and unstable scheduling.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',5,2,'2025-07-29_14-14-32','You can’t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler (or its main), or SysTick_Handler because osYield requests a system call by triggering SVC, which is intended to be invoked from thread context, not while you are already in an interrupt. Calling it inside a handler nests an SVC within existing handler mode, mixing assumptions about which stack is active (handlers use MSP while threads use PSP), re-entering the scheduler while its own critical sections are in flight, and often re-pending PendSV while a switch is already being arranged. The result can be recursive exception chains, corrupted save/restore frames, missed ticks, and ultimately HardFaults—all rooted in the fact that the yield path’s SVC protocol assumes it’s running from a thread, not from within the context-switch machinery itself.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,2,'2025-07-29_14-22-39','You can’t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler (or its main), or SysTick_Handler because osYield requests a system call by triggering SVC, which is intended to be invoked from thread context, not while you are already in an interrupt. Calling it inside a handler nests an SVC within existing handler mode, mixing assumptions about which stack is active (handlers use MSP while threads use PSP), re-entering the scheduler while its own critical sections are in flight, and often re-pending PendSV while a switch is already being arranged. The result can be recursive exception chains, corrupted save/restore frames, missed ticks, and ultimately HardFaults—all rooted in the fact that the yield path’s SVC protocol assumes it’s running from a thread, not from within the context-switch machinery itself.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,2,'2025-07-29_14-53-43','You can’t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler (or its main), or SysTick_Handler because osYield requests a system call by triggering SVC, which is intended to be invoked from thread context, not while you are already in an interrupt. Calling it inside a handler nests an SVC within existing handler mode, mixing assumptions about which stack is active (handlers use MSP while threads use PSP), re-entering the scheduler while its own critical sections are in flight, and often re-pending PendSV while a switch is already being arranged. The result can be recursive exception chains, corrupted save/restore frames, missed ticks, and ultimately HardFaults—all rooted in the fact that the yield path’s SVC protocol assumes it’s running from a thread, not from within the context-switch machinery itself.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,2,'2025-07-30_11-23-25','You can’t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler (or its main), or SysTick_Handler because osYield requests a system call by triggering SVC, which is intended to be invoked from thread context, not while you are already in an interrupt. Calling it inside a handler nests an SVC within existing handler mode, mixing assumptions about which stack is active (handlers use MSP while threads use PSP), re-entering the scheduler while its own critical sections are in flight, and often re-pending PendSV while a switch is already being arranged. The result can be recursive exception chains, corrupted save/restore frames, missed ticks, and ultimately HardFaults—all rooted in the fact that the yield path’s SVC protocol assumes it’s running from a thread, not from within the context-switch machinery itself.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,3,'2025-07-29_13-43-18','Changing the base period to 0.1 ms improves timing resolution and responsiveness, but increases the interrupt rate tenfold. More time is spent in the tick, context switches rise, power use can go up, and jitter appears if work can’t finish between ticks. You must also retune timeouts and slices to avoid thrashing, and reconfigure the hardware and timer setup that were designed around a 1 ms tick to prevent side effects.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,3,'2025-07-29_14-14-32','Changing the base period to 0.1 ms improves timing resolution and responsiveness, but increases the interrupt rate tenfold. More time is spent in the tick, context switches rise, power use can go up, and jitter appears if work can’t finish between ticks. You must also retune timeouts and slices to avoid thrashing, and reconfigure the hardware and timer setup that were designed around a 1 ms tick to prevent side effects.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,3,'2025-07-29_14-22-39','Changing the base period to 0.1 ms improves timing resolution and responsiveness, but increases the interrupt rate tenfold. More time is spent in the tick, context switches rise, power use can go up, and jitter appears if work can’t finish between ticks. You must also retune timeouts and slices to avoid thrashing, and reconfigure the hardware and timer setup that were designed around a 1 ms tick to prevent side effects.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,3,'2025-07-29_14-53-43','Changing the base period to 0.1 ms improves timing resolution and responsiveness, but increases the interrupt rate tenfold. More time is spent in the tick, context switches rise, power use can go up, and jitter appears if work can’t finish between ticks. You must also retune timeouts and slices to avoid thrashing, and reconfigure the hardware and timer setup that were designed around a 1 ms tick to prevent side effects.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,3,'2025-07-30_11-23-25','Changing the base period to 0.1 ms improves timing resolution and responsiveness, but increases the interrupt rate tenfold. More time is spent in the tick, context switches rise, power use can go up, and jitter appears if work can’t finish between ticks. You must also retune timeouts and slices to avoid thrashing, and reconfigure the hardware and timer setup that were designed around a 1 ms tick to prevent side effects.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,4,'2025-07-29_13-43-18','For users, cooperative multitasking requires threads to yield at good points; in pre-emptive multitasking they don’t have to, since the OS time-slices automatically (they can still yield to be courteous). For the OS, pre-emptive mode tracks a time slice and remaining budget per thread; each tick decrements the budget and, on zero, requests a switch and refreshes the budget. Cooperative mode switches only on yield or blocking; if a pre-emptive thread yields, it’s handled like a normal switch and the slice is refreshed.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,4,'2025-07-29_14-14-32','For users, cooperative multitasking requires threads to yield at good points; in pre-emptive multitasking they don’t have to, since the OS time-slices automatically (they can still yield to be courteous). For the OS, pre-emptive mode tracks a time slice and remaining budget per thread; each tick decrements the budget and, on zero, requests a switch and refreshes the budget. Cooperative mode switches only on yield or blocking; if a pre-emptive thread yields, it’s handled like a normal switch and the slice is refreshed.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,4,'2025-07-29_14-22-39','For users, cooperative multitasking requires threads to yield at good points; in pre-emptive multitasking they don’t have to, since the OS time-slices automatically (they can still yield to be courteous). For the OS, pre-emptive mode tracks a time slice and remaining budget per thread; each tick decrements the budget and, on zero, requests a switch and refreshes the budget. Cooperative mode switches only on yield or blocking; if a pre-emptive thread yields, it’s handled like a normal switch and the slice is refreshed.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,4,'2025-07-29_14-53-43','For users, cooperative multitasking requires threads to yield at good points; in pre-emptive multitasking they don’t have to, since the OS time-slices automatically (they can still yield to be courteous). For the OS, pre-emptive mode tracks a time slice and remaining budget per thread; each tick decrements the budget and, on zero, requests a switch and refreshes the budget. Cooperative mode switches only on yield or blocking; if a pre-emptive thread yields, it’s handled like a normal switch and the slice is refreshed.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',5,4,'2025-07-30_11-23-25','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires threads to give up the processor at sensible points, while in pre-emptive multitasking they don’t have to because the operating system will time-slice automatically. Under the hood, pre-emption is enforced by a periodic hardware timer interrupt (the system tick). On each tick, the OS decrements the running thread’s remaining time budget; when that budget reaches zero—or when a higher-priority thread becomes ready—the tick handler marks a software context-switch exception (commonly the PendSV interrupt) to run at the lowest priority. After the tick completes, that exception saves the current context, restores the next thread’s context, and resumes execution there. This interrupt-driven path forces a switch even if a thread never yields. In cooperative mode, by contrast, no forced switch is scheduled by the timer; the OS only changes threads when a running one calls yield or blocks, and the scheduler simply picks the next runnable thread at that moment.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,5,'2025-07-29_13-43-18','They work together because the scheduler supports two compatible triggers that feed the same switch path: an explicit yield from a thread and an automatic hand-off when the time slice expires. The tick path resets the budget on expiry, while the yield path resets it when a thread gives up early. Since both converge on the same save/restore and accounting, a yielding thread and a pre-empted thread interleave cleanly.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,5,'2025-07-29_14-14-32','They work together because the scheduler supports two compatible triggers that feed the same switch path: an explicit yield from a thread and an automatic hand-off when the time slice expires. The tick path resets the budget on expiry, while the yield path resets it when a thread gives up early. Since both converge on the same save/restore and accounting, a yielding thread and a pre-empted thread interleave cleanly.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',5,5,'2025-07-29_14-22-39','They work together because the scheduler supports two compatible triggers that feed the same switch path: an explicit yield from a thread and an automatic hand-off when the time slice expires. The tick path resets the budget on expiry, while the yield path resets it when a thread gives up early. Since both converge on the same save/restore and accounting, a yielding thread and a pre-empted thread interleave cleanly.\n',0,'s25'),('rruan',5,5,'2025-07-29_14-53-43','Co-operative yielding and time-based pre-emption work together because both paths ultimately request the same context-switch mechanism: the PendSV exception. On the tick path, the timer interrupt decrements a thread’s remaining budget and, when it hits zero, sets PendSV so the switch happens on interrupt exit. On the yield path, a thread calls the yield routine, which raises a system call (SVC); the SVC handler decides to reschedule and likewise sets PendSV, so the actual save/restore runs in PendSV_Handler for both cases. Because both triggers converge on PendSV and a single switch routine and accounting model, a yielding thread and a pre-empted thread interleave cleanly with consistent state. If you don’t name and centralize this mechanism—i.e., if code tries to “switch” directly from SVC or the tick handler—you risk duplicated or re-entrant switch logic, mixed stacks, broken invariants, and hard-to-debug faults; making PendSV the one, explicit switch trigger avoids that.\n',1,'s25'),('rruan',5,5,'2025-07-30_11-23-25','Co-operative yielding and time-based pre-emption work together because both paths ultimately request the same context-switch mechanism: the PendSV exception. On the tick path, the timer interrupt decrements a thread’s remaining budget and, when it hits zero, sets PendSV so the switch happens on interrupt exit. On the yield path, a thread calls the yield routine, which raises a system call (SVC); the SVC handler decides to reschedule and likewise sets PendSV, so the actual save/restore runs in PendSV_Handler for both cases. Because both triggers converge on PendSV and a single switch routine and accounting model, a yielding thread and a pre-empted thread interleave cleanly with consistent state. If you don’t name and centralize this mechanism—i.e., if code tries to “switch” directly from SVC or the tick handler—you risk duplicated or re-entrant switch logic, mixed stacks, broken invariants, and hard-to-debug faults; making PendSV the one, explicit switch trigger avoids that.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',1,1,'2025-06-26_22-21-11','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is an intermediate layer of code with functions that makes it easier to interact with the hardware of the microcontroller, such as registers, GPIO, timers, etc. It is used in general embedded products to ensure that code written by developers can work across multiple different hardware set-ups, such as different STM32 models. Without it, the developer’s code would have to be much-more hardware specific, making it more time consuming to write, and also making it more difficult to adapt to different hardware setups (ie. CPUs). The HAL simplifies this by giving pre-written functions that handle all the low-level details. For example, instead of manually configuring UART registers, we can call HAL_UART_Transmit() to send data over serial.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',1,1,'2025-06-27_00-32-50','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is an intermediate layer of code with functions that makes it easier to interact with the hardware of the microcontroller, such as registers, GPIO, timers, etc. It is used in general embedded products to ensure that code written by developers can work across multiple different hardware set-ups, such as different STM32 models. Without it, the developer’s code would have to be much-more hardware specific, making it more time consuming to write, and also making it more difficult to adapt to different hardware setups (ie. CPUs). The HAL simplifies this by giving pre-written functions that handle all the low-level details. For example, instead of manually configuring UART registers, we can call HAL_UART_Transmit() to send data over serial.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',1,2,'2025-06-26_22-21-11','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that tells the STM32 how to output a single character to a specified I/O channel, such as a particular UART channel. It is needed because the standard printf() function does not provide any information to the CPU on how to send data to the hardware. Without this information, the STM32 would not know where or how to “print”. However, the __io_putchar does provide these hardware-specific details, because inside this function, there is a call to HAL_UART_Transmit() or another similar function which specifies the specific port to be printed to. Without the __io_putchar function, printf() would compile, but nothing would show up in the serial console because it wouldn’t know how to send its output anywhere.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',1,2,'2025-06-27_00-32-50','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that tells the STM32 how to output a single character to a specified I/O channel, such as a particular UART channel. It is needed because the standard printf() function does not provide any information to the CPU on how to send data to the hardware. Without this information, the STM32 would not know where or how to “print”. However, the __io_putchar does provide these hardware-specific details, because inside this function, there is a call to HAL_UART_Transmit() or another similar function which specifies the specific port to be printed to. Without the __io_putchar function, printf() would compile, but nothing would show up in the serial console because it wouldn’t know how to send its output anywhere.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',1,3,'2025-06-26_22-21-11','The debugger had to be used in this lab because it allowed us to observe how certain values changed at different points during the program, which would otherwise not be possible using the printf function. For example, we needed to see live values of registers, such as MSP and PSP, at different points in the execution of the program to understand how they were changing. This would be difficult to do with printf because it would introduce additional lines of code, and it would also not be possible to easily determine when the register values change. By using the debugger, we can pause the execution of the program and execute code line-by-line, which means we can easily track how the register values change, without introducing new lines of code.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',1,3,'2025-06-27_00-32-50','The debugger had to be used in this lab because it allowed us to observe how certain values changed at different points during the program, which would otherwise not be possible using the printf function. For example, we needed to see live values of registers, such as MSP and PSP, at different points in the execution of the program to understand how they were changing. This would be difficult to do with printf because it would introduce additional lines of code, and it would also not be possible to easily determine when the register values change. By using the debugger, we can pause the execution of the program and execute code line-by-line, which means we can easily track how the register values change, without introducing new lines of code.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',1,4,'2025-06-26_22-21-11','In this lab, we used a stack allocation method that starts by obtaining the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) from memory address 0x0, which stores the starting location of the main stack region set during the microcontroller’s reset process. This value, called MSP_init_val, is important because it marks the beginning of the total stack space available in RAM. Once we have this starting point, we can calculate the address for a new Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by offsetting from MSP_init_val. We do this by adding the size of the main stack and the size of any previous thread stacks to ensure each thread has its own separate stack space. In this way, the stack grows downwards, meaning successive addresses are lower. Knowing the initial MSP location is essential because it allows us to avoid overlapping memory regions and enables safe, predictable allocation of stack space.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',1,4,'2025-06-27_00-32-50','In this lab, we used a stack allocation method that starts by obtaining the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) from memory address 0x0, which stores the starting location of the main stack region set during the microcontroller’s reset process. This value, called MSP_init_val, is important because it marks the beginning of the total stack space available in RAM. Once we have this starting point, we can calculate the address for a new Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by offsetting from MSP_init_val. We do this by adding the size of the main stack and the size of any previous thread stacks to ensure each thread has its own separate stack space. In this way, the stack grows downwards, meaning successive addresses are lower. Knowing the initial MSP location is essential because it allows us to avoid overlapping memory regions and enables safe, predictable allocation of stack space.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',1,5,'2025-06-26_22-21-11','If we wanted to use the same stack allocation method to allocate multiple stacks for threads, and the number of threads is known at compile-time, we would need to make a few changes to the code to calculate the starting address for each thread’s stack. First, we would have to define a constant for the number of threads along with the sizes of each stack, so that the main stack, which starts at MSP_INIT_VAL_ can be divided into multiple blocks. With these constants, we can compute the starting address for each thread stack using a loop or by hardcoding the formula for each thread:\nstack_j = MSP_init_val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE, where j ranges from 1 to the number of threads. This would give a separate memory location for each thread’s stack, spaced evenly in memory. Additionally, we could make an array or table of stack pointers, like uint32_t* stack_tops[NUM_THREADS], and populate it using the formula above during initialization. This way, we can keep track of where each thread\'s stack begins in memory, even before using them in the OS. No runtime stack allocation would be needed since all addresses are computed statically based on the known number of threads.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',1,5,'2025-06-27_00-32-50','If we wanted to use the same stack allocation method to allocate multiple stacks for threads, and the number of threads is known at compile-time, we would need to make a few changes to the code to calculate the starting address for each thread’s stack. First, we would have to define a constant for the number of threads along with the sizes of each stack, so that the main stack, which starts at MSP_INIT_VAL_ can be divided into multiple blocks. This division must be done in a way that avoids overlapping addresses and ensures that all of the addresses are valid, which is why the essential constraints of MAIN_STACK_SIZE and THREAD_STACK_SIZE must be known. With these constraints, we can compute the starting address for each thread stack using a loop or by hardcoding the formula for each thread: stack_j = MSP_init_val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE, where j ranges from 1 to the number of threads. This would give a separate memory location for each thread’s stack, aligned one after another so that the starting address of each thread’s stack can be computed. Additionally, we could make an array or table of stack pointers, like uint32_t* stack_tops[NUM_THREADS], and populate it using the formula above during initialization. This way, we can keep track of where each thread\'s stack begins in memory, even before using them in the OS. No runtime stack allocation would be needed since all addresses are computed statically based on the known number of threads.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,1,'2025-06-24_22-48-35','When I changed the PC register to hold the function pointer, I manually instructed the processor to begin executing the print continuously function. This is because the PC register is responsible for telling the processor what instructions to execute next, so by setting it to the function pointer, I forced it to begin executing the continuous printing function that was stored at the address being pointed to.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,1,'2025-06-25_12-11-53','When I changed the PC register to hold the function pointer, I manually instructed the processor to begin executing the print continuously function. This is because the PC register is responsible for telling the processor what instructions to execute next, so by setting it to the function pointer, I forced it to begin executing the continuous printing function that was stored at the address being pointed to.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,1,'2025-06-25_12-46-52','When I changed the PC register to hold the function pointer, I manually instructed the processor to begin executing the print continuously function. This is because the PC register is responsible for telling the processor what instructions to execute next, so by setting it to the function pointer, I forced it to begin executing the continuous printing function that was stored at the address being pointed to.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,1,'2025-06-26_13-48-10','When I changed the PC register to hold the function pointer, I manually instructed the processor to begin executing the print continuously function. This is because the PC register is responsible for telling the processor what instructions to execute next, so by setting it to the function pointer, I forced it to begin executing the continuous printing function that was stored at the address being pointed to.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,1,'2025-06-26_19-45-17','When I changed the PC register to hold the function pointer, I manually instructed the processor to begin executing the print continuously function. The pointer is initially stored in R0, and then the jump assembly function moves it directly into the PC register. The PC register is responsible for telling the processor what instructions to execute next, so by setting it to the function pointer, I forced it to begin executing the continuous printing function that was stored at the address being pointed to.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',2,1,'2025-06-27_01-16-33','When I changed the PC register to hold the function pointer, I manually instructed the processor to begin executing the print continuously function. The pointer is initially stored in R0, and then the jump assembly function moves it directly into the PC register. The PC register is responsible for telling the processor what functions to call next, so by setting it to the function pointer, I forced it to call the continuous printing function that was stored at the address being pointed to.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,1,'2025-06-27_14-03-41','When I changed the PC register to hold the function pointer, I manually instructed the processor to begin executing the print continuously function. The pointer is initially stored in R0, and then the jump assembly function moves it directly into the PC register. The PC register is responsible for telling the processor what functions to call next, so by setting it to the function pointer, I forced it to call the continuous printing function that was stored at the address being pointed to.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,2,'2025-06-24_22-48-35','A system call is how a program requests a hardware specific function that is controlled by the OS. It is needed because programs run in user mode, which has very limited functions. As such, the program wants to perform a privileged function, such as reading from a file, it generates a system call to ask (or “interrupt”) the OS, since the OS runs in kernel mode and has full access to the system.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,2,'2025-06-25_12-11-53','A system call is how a program requests a hardware specific function that is controlled by the OS. It is needed because programs run in user mode, which has very limited functions. As such, the program wants to perform a privileged function, such as reading from a file, it generates a system call to ask (or “interrupt”) the OS, since the OS runs in kernel mode and has full access to the system.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',2,2,'2025-06-25_12-46-52','A system call is how a program requests a hardware specific function that is controlled by the OS. It is needed because programs run in user mode, which has very limited functions. As such, the program wants to perform a privileged function, such as reading from a file, it generates a system call to ask (or “interrupt”) the OS, since the OS runs in kernel mode and has full access to the system.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,2,'2025-06-26_13-48-10','A system call is how a program requests a hardware specific function that is controlled by the OS. It is needed because programs run in user mode, which has very limited functions. As such, the program wants to perform a privileged function, such as reading from a file, it generates a system call to ask (or “interrupt”) the OS, since the OS runs in kernel mode and has full access to the system.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',2,2,'2025-06-26_19-45-17','A system call a type of function call where a program requests a hardware specific function that is controlled by the OS. It is needed because programs run in user mode, which has very limited functions. As such, the program wants to perform a privileged function call, such as reading from a file, it generates a system call to ask (or “interrupt”) the OS, since the OS runs in kernel mode and has full access to the system.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,2,'2025-06-27_01-16-33','A system call a type of function call where a program requests a hardware specific function that is controlled by the OS. It is needed because programs run in user mode, which has very limited functions. As such, the program wants to perform a privileged function call, such as reading from a file, it generates a system call to ask (or “interrupt”) the OS, since the OS runs in kernel mode and has full access to the system.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,2,'2025-06-27_14-03-41','A system call a type of function call where a program requests a hardware specific function that is controlled by the OS. It is needed because programs run in user mode, which has very limited functions. As such, the program wants to perform a privileged function call, such as reading from a file, it generates a system call to ask (or “interrupt”) the OS, since the OS runs in kernel mode and has full access to the system.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,3,'2025-06-24_22-48-35','In this lab, the thread’s stack was set up by writing the register values to the stack in a specific order (XPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, R11, R10, R9, R8, R7, R6, R5, R4). The order was important so that these values could be popped off in the same order when the thread was executed.\nSetting up a thread’s stack means allocating a dedicated space in memory where the thread will be able to store it’s temporary data, like variables and register values. It also includes filling up this stack with an initial set of register values so that when the thread is executed, these values can be popped and the system can proceed as if the thread had always been running.\nRegister values must be stored (or pushed) on the stack because it allows a processor to pause a thread, handle an interrupt, then pop the register values off the stack and resume the thread. When a thread is manually created, the initial fake values in the stack are popped off, allowing the thread to be executed.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,3,'2025-06-25_12-11-53','In this lab, the thread’s stack was set up by writing the register values to the stack in a specific order (XPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, R11, R10, R9, R8, R7, R6, R5, R4). The order was important so that these values could be popped off in the same order when the thread was executed.\nSetting up a thread’s stack means allocating a dedicated space in memory where the thread will be able to store it’s temporary data, like variables and register values. It also includes filling up this stack with an initial set of register values so that when the thread is executed, these values can be popped and the system can proceed as if the thread had always been running.\nRegister values must be stored (or pushed) on the stack because it allows a processor to pause a thread, handle an interrupt, then pop the register values off the stack and resume the thread. When a thread is manually created, the initial fake values in the stack are popped off, allowing the thread to be executed.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,3,'2025-06-25_12-46-52','In this lab, the thread’s stack was set up by writing the register values to the stack in a specific order (XPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, R11, R10, R9, R8, R7, R6, R5, R4). The order was important so that these values could be popped off in the same order when the thread was executed.\nSetting up a thread’s stack means allocating a dedicated space in memory where the thread will be able to store it’s temporary data, like variables and register values. It also includes filling up this stack with an initial set of register values so that when the thread is executed, these values can be popped and the system can proceed as if the thread had always been running.\nRegister values must be stored (or pushed) on the stack because it allows a processor to pause a thread, handle an interrupt, then pop the register values off the stack and resume the thread. When a thread is manually created, the initial fake values in the stack are popped off, allowing the thread to be executed.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,3,'2025-06-26_13-48-10','In this lab, the thread’s stack was set up by writing the register values to the stack in a specific order (XPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, R11, R10, R9, R8, R7, R6, R5, R4). The order was important so that these values could be popped off in the same order when the thread was executed.\nSetting up a thread’s stack means allocating a dedicated space in memory where the thread will be able to store it’s temporary data, like variables and register values. It also includes filling up this stack with an initial set of register values so that when the thread is executed, these values can be popped and the system can proceed as if the thread had always been running.\nRegister values must be stored (or pushed) on the stack because it allows a processor to pause a thread, handle an interrupt, then pop the register values off the stack and resume the thread. When a thread is manually created, the initial fake values in the stack are popped off, allowing the thread to be executed.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,3,'2025-06-26_19-45-17','In this lab, the thread’s stack was set up by writing the register values to the stack in a specific order (XPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, R11, R10, R9, R8, R7, R6, R5, R4). The order was important so that these values could be popped off in the same order when the thread was executed.\nSetting up a thread’s stack means allocating a dedicated space in memory where the thread will be able to store it’s temporary data, like variables and register values. It also includes filling up this stack with an initial set of register values so that when the thread is executed, these values can be popped and the system can proceed as if the thread had always been running.\nRegister values must be stored (or pushed) on the stack because it allows a processor to pause a thread, handle an interrupt, then pop the register values off the stack and resume the thread. When a thread is manually created, the initial fake values in the stack are popped off, allowing the thread to be executed.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,3,'2025-06-27_01-16-33','In this lab, the thread’s stack was set up by writing the register values to the stack in a specific order (XPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, R11, R10, R9, R8, R7, R6, R5, R4). The order was important so that these values could be popped off in the same order when the thread was executed.\nSetting up a thread’s stack means allocating a dedicated space in memory where the thread will be able to store it’s temporary data, like variables and register values. It also includes filling up this stack with an initial set of register values so that when the thread is executed, these values can be popped and the system can proceed as if the thread had always been running.\nRegister values must be stored (or pushed) on the stack because it allows a processor to pause a thread, handle an interrupt, then pop the register values off the stack and resume the thread. When a thread is manually created, the initial fake values in the stack are popped off, allowing the thread to be executed.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,3,'2025-06-27_14-03-41','In this lab, the thread’s stack was set up by writing the register values to the stack in a specific order (XPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, R11, R10, R9, R8, R7, R6, R5, R4). The order was important so that these values could be popped off in the same order when the thread was executed.\nSetting up a thread’s stack means allocating a dedicated space in memory where the thread will be able to store it’s temporary data, like variables and register values. It also includes filling up this stack with an initial set of register values so that when the thread is executed, these values can be popped and the system can proceed as if the thread had always been running.\nRegister values must be stored (or pushed) on the stack because it allows a processor to pause a thread, handle an interrupt, then pop the register values off the stack and resume the thread. When a thread is manually created, the initial fake values in the stack are popped off, allowing the thread to be executed.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,4,'2025-06-24_22-48-35','If we didn’t set the PSP address using the __set_PSP function, the processor would assume that PSP was already pointing to a valid stack with the register values for a thread. However, since PSP was not initialized to anything, it would actually point to a garbage address, and thus the processor would not be able to pop the register values needed to resume thread execution, triggering a hard fault. Calling __set_PSP ensures that the PSP points to a valid address that the processor can resume the thread from.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,4,'2025-06-25_12-11-53','If we didn’t set the PSP address using the __set_PSP function, the processor would assume that PSP was already pointing to a valid stack with the register values for a thread. However, since PSP was not initialized to anything, it would actually point to a garbage address, and thus the processor would not be able to pop the register values needed to resume thread execution, triggering a hard fault. Calling __set_PSP ensures that the PSP points to a valid address that the processor can resume the thread from.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',2,4,'2025-06-25_12-46-52','If we didn’t set the PSP address using the __set_PSP function, the processor would assume that PSP was already pointing to a valid stack with the register values for a thread. However, since PSP was not initialized to anything, it would actually point to a garbage address, and thus the processor would not be able to pop the register values needed to resume thread execution, triggering a hard fault. Calling __set_PSP ensures that the PSP points to a valid address that the processor can resume the thread from.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',2,4,'2025-06-26_13-48-10','If we didn’t set the PSP address using the __set_PSP function, the processor would assume that PSP was already pointing to a valid stack with the register values for a thread. However, since PSP was not initialized to anything, it would actually point to a garbage address (the default values), and thus the processor would not be able to pop the register values needed to resume thread execution, triggering a hard fault. Instead, the PSP must be explicitly set to the thread’s stack address because that ensures that when the CPU begins executing the thread, it knows exactly where to pop the necessary register values from. As such, calling __set_PSP ensures that the PSP points to a valid address that the processor can resume the thread from.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',2,4,'2025-06-26_19-45-17','The Cortex-M4 uses the PSP to point to the thread’s stack, which is where the data required for thread execution is stored. This is different from the MSP, which points to a different memory address with the CPU’s stack. If we didn’t set the PSP address using the __set_PSP function, the processor would assume that PSP was already pointing to a valid stack with the register values for a thread. However, since PSP was not initialized to anything, it would actually point to a garbage address (the default values), and thus the processor would not be able to pop the register values needed to resume thread execution, triggering a hard fault. Instead, the PSP must be explicitly set to the thread’s stack address because that ensures that when the CPU begins executing the thread, it knows exactly where to pop the necessary register values from. As such, calling __set_PSP ensures that the PSP points to a valid address that the processor can resume the thread from.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,4,'2025-06-27_01-16-33','The Cortex-M4 uses the PSP to point to the thread’s stack, which is where the data required for thread execution is stored. This is different from the MSP, which points to a different memory address with the CPU’s stack. If we didn’t set the PSP address using the __set_PSP function, the processor would assume that PSP was already pointing to a valid stack with the register values for a thread. However, since PSP was not initialized to anything, it would actually point to a garbage address (the default values), and thus the processor would not be able to pop the register values needed to resume thread execution, triggering a hard fault. Instead, the PSP must be explicitly set to the thread’s stack address because that ensures that when the CPU begins executing the thread, it knows exactly where to pop the necessary register values from. As such, calling __set_PSP ensures that the PSP points to a valid address that the processor can resume the thread from.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,4,'2025-06-27_14-03-41','The Cortex-M4 uses the PSP to point to the thread’s stack, which is where the data required for thread execution is stored. This is different from the MSP, which points to a different memory address with the CPU’s stack. If we didn’t set the PSP address using the __set_PSP function, the processor would assume that PSP was already pointing to a valid stack with the register values for a thread. However, since PSP was not initialized to anything, it would actually point to a garbage address (the default values), and thus the processor would not be able to pop the register values needed to resume thread execution, triggering a hard fault. Instead, the PSP must be explicitly set to the thread’s stack address because that ensures that when the CPU begins executing the thread, it knows exactly where to pop the necessary register values from. As such, calling __set_PSP ensures that the PSP points to a valid address that the processor can resume the thread from.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,5,'2025-06-24_22-48-35','If the thread function didn\'t loop continuously and instead printed something once and then returned, the program would crash. This is because when a function returns, the processor tries to jump to the address stored in the link register. Since we manually set up the thread\'s stack and filled in a dummy value for LR, when the thread finishes executing and attempts to return, the processor jumps to the dummy LR address. However, this address is not a valid or properly aligned code address, so the processor triggers a fault and crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',2,5,'2025-06-25_12-11-53','If the thread function did not loop and instead returned after executing only once, the program would trigger a hardfault and crash. In normal function calls, the processor automatically saves the return address to the thread’s stack so it knows where to return after the function finishes. However, in this lab, we manually set up the thread’s stack and did not store a valid return address. Instead, we placed a dummy value for the LR, which means that no valid return address is ever stored on the stack for the thread to return to. As a result, when the function returns, the CPU tries to branch to this invalid address. Because there is no proper return address stored on the stack and LR holds an invalid value, this causes the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location and results in a hardfault, which is an exception indicating a serious error in the CPU commands.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,5,'2025-06-25_12-46-52','If the thread function did not loop and instead returned after executing only once, the program would trigger a hardfault and crash. In normal function calls, the processor automatically saves the return address to the thread’s stack so it knows where to return after the function finishes. However, in this lab, we manually set up the thread’s stack and did not store a valid return address. Instead, we placed a dummy value for the LR, which means that no valid return address is ever stored on the stack for the thread to return to. As a result, when the function returns, the CPU tries to branch to this invalid address. Because there is no proper return address stored on the stack and LR holds an invalid value, this causes the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location and results in a hardfault, which is an exception indicating a serious error in the CPU commands.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,5,'2025-06-26_13-48-10','If the thread function did not loop and instead returned after executing only once, the program would trigger a hardfault and crash. In normal function calls, the processor automatically saves the return address to the thread’s stack so it knows where to return after the function finishes. However, in this lab, we manually set up the thread’s stack and did not store a valid return address. Instead, we placed a dummy value for the LR, which means that no valid return address is ever stored on the stack for the thread to return to. As a result, when the function returns, the CPU tries to branch to this invalid address. Because there is no proper return address stored on the stack and LR holds an invalid value, this causes the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location and results in a hardfault, which is an exception indicating a serious error in the CPU commands.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,5,'2025-06-26_19-45-17','If the thread function did not loop and instead returned after executing only once, the program would trigger a hardfault and crash. In normal function calls, the processor automatically saves the return address to the thread’s stack so it knows where to return after the function finishes. However, in this lab, we manually set up the thread’s stack and did not store a valid return address. Instead, we placed a dummy value for the LR, which means that no valid return address is ever stored on the stack for the thread to return to. As a result, when the function returns, the CPU tries to branch to this invalid address. Because there is no proper return address stored on the stack and LR holds an invalid value, this causes the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location and results in a hardfault, which is an exception indicating a serious error in the CPU commands.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,5,'2025-06-27_01-16-33','If the thread function did not loop and instead returned after executing only once, the program would trigger a hardfault and crash. In normal function calls, the processor automatically saves the return address to the thread’s stack so it knows where to return after the function finishes. However, in this lab, we manually set up the thread’s stack and did not store a valid return address. Instead, we placed a dummy value for the LR, which means that no valid return address is ever stored on the stack for the thread to return to. As a result, when the function returns, the CPU tries to branch to this invalid address. Because there is no proper return address stored on the stack and LR holds an invalid value, this causes the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location and results in a hardfault, which is an exception indicating a serious error in the CPU commands.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',2,5,'2025-06-27_14-03-41','If the thread function did not loop and instead returned after executing only once, the program would trigger a hardfault and crash. In normal function calls, the processor automatically saves the return address to the thread’s stack so it knows where to return after the function finishes. However, in this lab, we manually set up the thread’s stack and did not store a valid return address. Instead, we placed a dummy value for the LR, which means that no valid return address is ever stored on the stack for the thread to return to. As a result, when the function returns, the CPU tries to branch to this invalid address. Because there is no proper return address stored on the stack and LR holds an invalid value, this causes the CPU to jump to an invalid memory location and results in a hardfault, which is an exception indicating a serious error in the CPU commands.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',3,1,'2025-06-27_13-54-57','The process by which multiple .c files are compiled into a single executable begins with preprocessing of each C file individually. During preprocessing, the code is prepared for compilation by removing comments and bringing the macros and headers inline. Then, next step is compilation, which is when each file is translated into machine code separately to produce a set of object files. These object files are verified for syntax errors, and additional optimization occurs to reduce the total execution steps (ie. lines of code). Once all .c files are compiled into object files, the next step is linking. The linker is responsible for combining all the object files into a single executable. It resolves any references between files, such as functional calls, definitions, variables, and standard libraries. It also sets up the program\'s memory layout and results in a single executable file that can be run.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',3,2,'2025-06-27_13-54-57','Using #include on a .c file can cause problems during compilation because the #include directive directly pastes the contents of the file into the place where it’s included. This means that if you #include a .c file, its entire code, including variables and function definitions are pasted in the main file. This can lead to linker errors if there are multiple definitions of the same functions or variables. It would also make the code less modular since the .c files would be dependent on each other. However, the #include directive is fine for header files because they only contain declarations of function prototypes, variables, and structures, without actually defining them. As such, there is no risk of multiple definitions being included. This is why header files are used for common declarations and then use in individual .c files, which are then compiled separately.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',3,3,'2025-06-27_13-54-57','First, initialize the kernel. Before any threads can be created, the user has to call a function that initializes the operating system\'s kernel. This sets up internal data structures, like the memory stack, which are needed for thread creation.\nSecond, define a thread function: The user has to write a function that contains the code they want the thread to run. This function should call a function with a void* argument and which also returns void.\nThird, specify the function address and create a thread: The user then calls the kernel thread creation function and passes it the address of the thread function. This function sets up the stack and registers for the new thread and also tells the kernel what function the thread will execute.\nFourth, start the kernel: The last step is for the user to call the function that that starts the kernel. This initiates a system call that points the processor to the new thread\'s stack and begins executing the thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',3,4,'2025-06-27_13-54-57','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory specifically set aside for use by thread stacks. Since each thread needs its own stack to store register values, local variables, and return addresses, the stack pool provides a space from which these stacks can be allocated. In this lab, we only used one stack for a single thread, but it was allocated from the stack pool. In future labs, this will be extended to support multiple threads by dividing the stack pool into equal-sized stack regions. Each time a new thread is created, a chunk of the stack pool could be allocated for it. Then, the kernel would be able to keep track of how much of the pool has been used and where each thread’s stack starts, which is how the OS would manage the memory of the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',3,5,'2025-06-27_13-54-57','The “kernel” code deals with the part of the operating system that manages hardware, threads, memory, and system calls. Code related to the kernel, such as thread creation, stack management, and system call handling goes into kernel.c and kernel.h. On the other hand, the “user” code interacts with the kernel but does not directly manage system resources. This includes the user’s thread functions (like osKernelStart) and the main function, which go into main.c.\nThe distinction is important because it allows for separation between system-level operations and user-level behavior, which simplifies things from the user’s perspective. This is because the user only needs to worry about writing thread functions and making simple kernel calls (like  osKernelStart), without needing to understand or modify the complex internal structures of the kernel. It also ensures security, since low level hardware functions cannot be directly accessed by any user.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,1,'2025-07-03_13-01-27','If a queue is used to store the thread control blocks, the currently running queue would be dequeued from the front, and after it yields, it would be requeued at the back. This would allow for the implementation of round robin scheduling because the scheduler would just pick the next thread to run by dequeuing the front of the queue. The benefit to this approach is that the scheduling logic would be simpler than an array with index tracking, especially if threads were to be added or removed. This is because the queue automatically handles order preservation and cycling, and it avoids the need for manual index and stack pointer management.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,1,'2025-07-03_13-11-40','If a queue is used to store the thread control blocks, the currently running queue would be dequeued from the front, and after it yields, it would be requeued at the back. This would allow for the implementation of round robin scheduling because the scheduler would just pick the next thread to run by dequeuing the front of the queue. The benefit to this approach is that the scheduling logic would be simpler than an array with index tracking, especially if threads were to be added or removed. This is because the queue automatically handles order preservation and cycling, and it avoids the need for manual index and stack pointer management.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',4,1,'2025-07-03_13-49-50','The current code uses an array to store the TCBs. If a queue is used to store the thread control blocks, the currently running queue would be dequeued from the front, and after it yields, it would be requeued at the back. This would allow for the implementation of round robin scheduling because the scheduler would just pick the next thread to run by dequeuing the front of the queue. The benefit to this approach is that the scheduling logic would be simpler than an array with index tracking, especially if threads were to be added or removed. This is because the queue automatically handles order preservation and cycling, and it avoids the need for manual index and stack pointer management. However, the trade-off to using a queue is that there is more memory overhead compared to a 1D array.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,1,'2025-07-03_14-12-02','The current code uses an array to store the TCBs. If a queue is used to store the thread control blocks, the currently running queue would be dequeued from the front, and after it yields, it would be requeued at the back. This would allow for the implementation of round robin scheduling because the scheduler would just pick the next thread to run by dequeuing the front of the queue. The benefit to this approach is that the scheduling logic would be simpler than an array with index tracking, especially if threads were to be added or removed. This is because the queue automatically handles order preservation and cycling, and it avoids the need for manual index and stack pointer management. However, the trade-off to using a queue is that there is more memory overhead compared to a 1D array.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,2,'2025-07-03_13-01-27','The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch by first saving the state of the current thread by pushing the registers saved by the callee (R4–R11) onto the current thread’s stack. It then calls osSched() to update the kernel’s internal tracking by saving the updated stack pointer of the current thread and setting the PSP to the stack of the next thread. After that, PendSV_Handler pops R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack, which restores its context and sets it up for execution. Finally, it returns from the interrupt and the new thread begins executing using the restored context. This ensures that each thread resumes in its own state with its own stack.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',4,2,'2025-07-03_13-11-40','The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch by first reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread’s stack. This pointer tells the system where the thread\'s context (register values, etc.) is stored in memory. Next, the handler saves the context of the current thread by pushing the registers saved by the callee (R4–R11) onto the stack pointed to by the PSP. These registers are important to save because they contain values that need to be restored when the thread resumes later. After saving the context, the handler calls the osSched() function. This function is responsible for selecting the next thread to run based on the round-robin scheduling policy. Once called, osSched() sets the PSP to the stack pointer of the next thread, so the processor knows where to load the next thread’s context from. Then, PendSV_Handler pops R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack using the updated PSP, which restores its context and sets it up for execution. Finally, the handler then returns from the interrupt, and execution continues as if the new thread had always been running.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,2,'2025-07-03_13-49-50','The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch by first reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread’s stack. This pointer tells the system where the thread\'s context (register values, etc.) is stored in memory. Next, the handler saves the context of the current thread by pushing the registers saved by the callee (R4–R11) onto the stack pointed to by the PSP. These registers are important to save because they contain values that need to be restored when the thread resumes later. After saving the context, the handler calls the osSched() function. This function is responsible for selecting the next thread to run based on the round-robin scheduling policy. Once called, osSched() sets the PSP to the stack pointer of the next thread, so the processor knows where to load the next thread’s context from. Then, PendSV_Handler pops R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack using the updated PSP, which restores its context and sets it up for execution. Finally, the handler then returns from the interrupt, and execution continues as if the new thread had always been running.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,2,'2025-07-03_14-12-02','The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch by first reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate the current thread’s stack. This pointer tells the system where the thread\'s context (register values, etc.) is stored in memory. Next, the handler saves the context of the current thread by pushing the registers saved by the callee (R4–R11) onto the stack pointed to by the PSP. These registers are important to save because they contain values that need to be restored when the thread resumes later. After saving the context, the handler calls the osSched() function. This function is responsible for selecting the next thread to run based on the round-robin scheduling policy. Once called, osSched() sets the PSP to the stack pointer of the next thread, so the processor knows where to load the next thread’s context from. Then, PendSV_Handler pops R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack using the updated PSP, which restores its context and sets it up for execution. Finally, the handler then returns from the interrupt, and execution continues as if the new thread had always been running.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,3,'2025-07-03_13-01-27','Always choosing the highest-priority task is a bad idea because it leads to starvation of lower-priority tasks. This means that if a high-priority task never yields or if new high-priority tasks keep arriving, lower-priority threads may never get a chance to run. This violates fairness and can break programs that depend on background or maintenance tasks (e.g., logging, communications) which might typically be assigned a lower priority, but are still essential. It also makes it difficult to predict the system behaviour since the lower priority tasks will keep getting pushed back, which can make real-time behavior unreliable.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,3,'2025-07-03_13-11-40','Always choosing the highest-priority task is a bad idea because it leads to starvation of lower-priority tasks. This means that if a high-priority task never yields or if new high-priority tasks keep arriving, lower-priority threads may never get a chance to run. This violates fairness and can break programs that depend on background or maintenance tasks (e.g., logging, communications) which might typically be assigned a lower priority, but are still essential. It also makes it difficult to predict the system behaviour since the lower priority tasks will keep getting pushed back, which can make real-time behavior unreliable.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',4,3,'2025-07-03_13-49-50','Always choosing the highest-priority task is a bad idea because it leads to starvation of lower-priority tasks. This means that if a high-priority task never yields or if new high-priority tasks keep arriving, no other task would get the chance to run. This violates fairness and can break programs that depend on background or maintenance tasks (e.g., logging, communications) which might typically be assigned a lower priority, but are still essential. It also makes it difficult to predict the system behaviour since the lower priority tasks will keep getting pushed back and may never run, which can make real-time behavior unreliable.\n',0,'s25'),('s25saha',4,3,'2025-07-03_14-12-02','Always choosing the highest-priority task is a bad idea because it leads to starvation of lower-priority tasks, since only the task with the highest priority would ever run. The direct consequence of this is that no other task would get the chance to run because the CPU time would be dominated by the highest priority task. This violates fairness and can break programs that depend on background or maintenance tasks (e.g., logging, communications) which might typically be assigned a lower priority, but are still essential. It also makes it difficult to predict the system behaviour since the lower priority tasks will keep getting pushed back and may never run, which can make real-time behavior unreliable.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,4,'2025-07-03_13-01-27','Calling C functions makes the code much more readable and maintainable than writing everything in assembly. This is because C provides higher-level constructs and is written with standard vocabulary/syntax, which make it easy to understand the code and debug it. It would also be easier to make it modular and adapt it to different hardware configurations because the code is more abstract than assembly (which is much more hardware-specific). However, the downsides to calling C functions over assembly are the additional overhead during compilation and program execution, both in terms of extra instructions and stack usage. This is because to make the abstract C functions applicable to the hardware, they must be processed, which adds additional lines of code, memory usage, and time. The C functions would also be dependent on compiler conventions, which are not always consistent, and thus it could lead to inconsistent behaviour.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,4,'2025-07-03_13-11-40','Calling C functions makes the code much more readable and maintainable than writing everything in assembly. This is because C provides higher-level constructs and is written with standard vocabulary/syntax, which make it easy to understand the code and debug it. It would also be easier to make it modular and adapt it to different hardware configurations because the code is more abstract than assembly (which is much more hardware-specific). However, the downsides to calling C functions over assembly are the additional overhead during compilation and program execution, both in terms of extra instructions and stack usage. This is because to make the abstract C functions applicable to the hardware, they must be processed, which adds additional lines of code, memory usage, and time. The C functions would also be dependent on compiler conventions, which are not always consistent, and thus it could lead to inconsistent behaviour.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,4,'2025-07-03_13-49-50','Calling C functions makes the code much more readable and maintainable than writing everything in assembly. This is because C provides higher-level constructs and is written with standard vocabulary/syntax, which make it easy to understand the code and debug it. It would also be easier to make it modular and adapt it to different hardware configurations because the code is more abstract than assembly (which is much more hardware-specific). However, the downsides to calling C functions over assembly are the additional overhead during compilation and program execution, both in terms of extra instructions and stack usage. This is because to make the abstract C functions applicable to the hardware, they must be processed, which adds additional lines of code, memory usage, and time. The C functions would also be dependent on compiler conventions, which are not always consistent, and thus it could lead to inconsistent behaviour.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,4,'2025-07-03_14-12-02','Calling C functions makes the code much more readable and maintainable than writing everything in assembly. This is because C provides higher-level constructs and is written with standard vocabulary/syntax, which make it easy to understand the code and debug it. It would also be easier to make it modular and adapt it to different hardware configurations because the code is more abstract than assembly (which is much more hardware-specific). However, the downsides to calling C functions over assembly are the additional overhead during compilation and program execution, both in terms of extra instructions and stack usage. This is because to make the abstract C functions applicable to the hardware, they must be processed, which adds additional lines of code, memory usage, and time. The C functions would also be dependent on compiler conventions, which are not always consistent, and thus it could lead to inconsistent behaviour.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,5,'2025-07-03_13-01-27','Yes, the OS will still function correctly, but the behavior will be inefficient. When the single thread yields, the scheduler will save its context, then pick the \"next\" thread (which is just the same one), and finally restore its state. In other words, the scheduler would just be switching back to itself. This unnecessary context switch would occur each time the thread yields, which adds unnecessary overhead, making the program inefficient.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,5,'2025-07-03_13-11-40','Yes, the OS will still function correctly, but the behavior will be inefficient. When the single thread yields, the scheduler will save its context, then pick the \"next\" thread (which is just the same one), and finally restore its state. In other words, the scheduler would just be switching back to itself. This unnecessary context switch would occur each time the thread yields, which adds unnecessary overhead, making the program inefficient.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,5,'2025-07-03_13-49-50','Yes, the OS will still function correctly, but the behavior will be inefficient. When the single thread yields, the scheduler will save its context, then pick the \"next\" thread (which is just the same one), and finally restore its state. In other words, the scheduler would just be switching back to itself. This unnecessary context switch would occur each time the thread yields, which adds unnecessary overhead, making the program inefficient.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',4,5,'2025-07-03_14-12-02','Yes, the OS will still function correctly, but the behavior will be inefficient. When the single thread yields, the scheduler will save its context, then pick the \"next\" thread (which is just the same one), and finally restore its state. In other words, the scheduler would just be switching back to itself. This unnecessary context switch would occur each time the thread yields, which adds unnecessary overhead, making the program inefficient.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',5,1,'2025-07-28_18-16-05','To pass an argument into a thread, the user has to provide a pointer to the data when calling osCreateThread. The OS takes this pointer and then initielizes the thread\'s stack by putting its value in the specific memory location that will be loaded into the R0 register when the thread first runs. This works because the first argument to any C function is passed via the R0 register. When the scheduler starts the thread, it restores the registers from the stack, so R0 receives the pointer value and the thread begins execution as if it were a normal function called with that pointer as its argument.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',5,2,'2025-07-28_18-16-05','You can’t call osYield from within any context switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because these handlers are already a special type of interrupt which are responsible for managing task switching. osYield works by triggering an SVC instruction, which then pends a PendSV interrupt to perform the actual switch. Calling it from within a handler would mean trying to start a new context switch while another is already in progress. This would lead to nested exception entries that would be unpredictable and corrupt the system\'s state and the stack. The processor would likely end up in a fault state.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',5,3,'2025-07-28_18-16-05','Changing the fundamental OS clock from 1ms to 0.1ms would make the system more responsive by making it’s time-resolution more fine. The OS could switch between tasks more frequently, making pre-emption more precise and allowing the system to react faster to external events. The downside of this is that it comes at the cost of increased overhead. This is because SysTick_Handler would be triggered ten times as often, consuming significantly more CPU time just for scheduling operations. This leaves less processing time for the actual application threads, potentially reducing the overall work the system can accomplish. This means that there is a direct trade-off between precision and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',5,4,'2025-07-28_18-16-05','From a user\'s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires the programmer to be responsible for fairness/task swtiching. Each task must explicitly yield control, and a buggy task with an infinite loop can freeze the entire system. Pre-emptive multitasking is more robust because users can write code without worrying about yielding, as the OS guarantees every task gets a turn. In terms of the OS, co-operative multitasking is simpler to implement since it only requires a way for tasks to yield voluntarily. Pre-emptive multitasking is more complex, as the OS must configure a hardware timer (like SysTick) to periodically interrupt tasks, track their runtimes, and forcibly trigger a context switch when their time slice is up.\n',1,'s25'),('s25saha',5,5,'2025-07-28_18-16-05','Yielding and time-based pre-emption can work together seamlessly because they both use the same basic context-switching mechanism. Regardless of whether a thread voluntarily calls osYield() (triggering an SVC and then PendSV) or if a thread is involuntarily interrupted by the SysTick_Handler (triggering PendSV directly), the PendSV exception is always triggered. The PendSV_Handler is the single, unified mechanism that saves the state of the current thread and restores the state of the next. It’s function doesn’t depend on why it was called. Instead, it just performs the switch, allowing both yielding and pre-emptive context switching mechanisms to work together.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',1,1,'2025-06-24_20-25-08','HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a collection of complex code designed by STMicroelectronics to abstract hardware complexity. It functions as the board\'s setup code, similar to a lightweight operating system. HAL reduces the necessity for low-level programming and the need to learn and apply multiple coding structures to different hardware platforms. It allows users to modify setup parameters to match project requirements, and it has built-in functions that can be used to create simpler, higher-level code. The user then creates application code and provides some basic functions to interface with the board using conventional C syntax. HAL provides direct routines for interacting with board features such as timers, interrupts, and GPIOs, simplifying access and development.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',1,1,'2025-06-24_20-51-30','HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a collection of complex code designed by STMicroelectronics to abstract hardware complexity. It functions as the board\'s setup code, similar to a lightweight operating system. HAL reduces the necessity for low-level programming and the need to learn and apply multiple coding structures to different hardware platforms. It allows users to modify setup parameters to match project requirements, and it has built-in functions that can be used to create simpler, higher-level code. The user then creates application code and provides some basic functions to interface with the board using conventional C syntax. HAL provides direct routines for interacting with board features such as timers, interrupts, and GPIOs, simplifying access and development.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',1,2,'2025-06-24_20-25-08','On bare-metal microcontrollers, there is no default console device, so the printf function from the standard C stdio library needs low-level support to output data. Without it, printf is unable to communicate with the board. There is no built-in printf destination on HAL-enabled embedded systems, so __io_putchar is used to bridge high-level I/O with hardware. It usually uses HAL_UART_Transmit to transfer characters over UART for serial output. This function must be redefined for each MCU or peripheral configuration to allow for hardware variations. Both __io_putchar and printf are included in stdio.h and are internally linked; each call to printf invokes __io_putchar for each character. This function allows developers to specify whether the transmission is blocking or non-blocking, if data is buffered, and whether the communication is interrupt-based or polling. Without __io_putchar, there would be no output and the behavior would be unknown. If it is absent, the output often falls back to semihosting, causing the system to hang unless a debugger is present.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',1,2,'2025-06-24_20-51-30','On bare-metal microcontrollers, there is no default console device, so the printf function from the standard C stdio library needs low-level support to output data. Without it, printf is unable to communicate with the board. There is no built-in printf destination on HAL-enabled embedded systems, so __io_putchar is used to bridge high-level I/O with hardware. It usually uses HAL_UART_Transmit to transfer characters over UART for serial output. This function must be redefined for each MCU or peripheral configuration to allow for hardware variations. Both __io_putchar and printf are included in stdio.h and are internally linked; each call to printf invokes __io_putchar for each character. This function allows developers to specify whether the transmission is blocking or non-blocking, if data is buffered, and whether the communication is interrupt-based or polling. Without __io_putchar, there would be no output and the behavior would be unknown. If it is absent, the output often falls back to semihosting, causing the system to hang unless a debugger is present.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',1,3,'2025-06-24_20-25-08','We used the debugger because it allows us to pause execution at any instruction line and check the system\'s state, particularly registers such as MSP, PSP, and SP without changing their contents.  This is not possible with printf, which utilizes the stack, puts data into it, and invokes the stdio library.  These activities rely on initialized values and change the exact numbers we want to investigate, which can lead to inaccurate or incorrect data.  Additionally, printf may not even operate properly unless the UART is already configured.  In contrast, utilizing the debugger with breakpoints and step-by-step execution provides an accurate, live picture of the system\'s state, indicating how and when each task is completed with no side effects.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',1,3,'2025-06-24_20-51-30','We used the debugger because it allows us to pause execution at any instruction line and check the system\'s state, particularly registers such as MSP, PSP, and SP without changing their contents.  This is not possible with printf, which utilizes the stack, puts data into it, and invokes the stdio library.  These activities rely on initialized values and change the exact numbers we want to investigate, which can lead to inaccurate or incorrect data.  Additionally, printf may not even operate properly unless the UART is already configured.  In contrast, utilizing the debugger with breakpoints and step-by-step execution provides an accurate, live picture of the system\'s state, indicating how and when each task is completed with no side effects.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',1,4,'2025-06-24_20-25-08','To find a valid PSP_val, we begin with MSP_init_val, which is the top of the main stack. We then deduct the known size of the main stack from it to go downward in memory and get a safe value for PSP_val, which we place at the beginning of a free stack region beneath the main stack. This assures that the process stack pointer (PSP) refers to a valid and unused memory location. If we had estimated the PSP value or used the wrong one, it may have ended up outside the stack or in a non-free memory region, resulting in issues. Knowing the exact initial location of the MSP is critical to ensure that the newly calculated PSP is valid and that it aligns with memory where registers and stack data can be properly accessed.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',1,4,'2025-06-24_20-51-30','To find a valid PSP_val, we begin with MSP_init_val, which is the top of the main stack. We then deduct the known size of the main stack from it to go downward in memory and get a safe value for PSP_val, which we place at the beginning of a free stack region beneath the main stack. This assures that the process stack pointer (PSP) refers to a valid and unused memory location. If we had estimated the PSP value or used the wrong one, it may have ended up outside the stack or in a non-free memory region, resulting in issues. Knowing the exact initial location of the MSP is critical to ensure that the newly calculated PSP is valid and that it aligns with memory where registers and stack data can be properly accessed.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',1,5,'2025-06-24_20-25-08','To allocate several thread stacks in the same way (on a Cortex-M4, stacks must grow downward and be 8-byte aligned), we begin with a known top-of-stack address—MSP_init_val. To reserve a memory space just below it, remove N × Thread_stack_size, where N represents the number of threads. This returns a base address that is guaranteed to be in the stack space. For each thread i (where 0 ≤ i < N), we calculate the stack pointer as follows: PSP_values[i] = base_address - (i+1) × Thread_stack_size.\nThis assigns each thread its own stack partition, and because the stacks expand downward, the offsetting prevents them from overlapping. Although this topic does not address context switching, the actual switching would take place using __set_PSP(PSP_values[i]) and activating PSP mode with __set_CONTROL(2). Because each stack pointer is derived from a single validated base address and employs a fixed-size stride, all stack regions are guaranteed to be valid and unique.\n',0,'s25'),('s2alrifa',1,5,'2025-06-24_20-51-30','To allocate several thread stacks in the same way (on a Cortex-M4, stacks must grow downward and must be aligned to an 8-byte boundary), we begin with a known top-of-stack address MSP_init_val. To reserve a memory space just below it, remove N × Thread_stack_size, where N represents the number of threads. This returns a base address that is guaranteed to be in the stack space. For each thread i (where 0 ≤ i < N), we calculate the stack pointer as follows: PSP_values[i] = base_address - (i+1) × Thread_stack_size.\nThis assigns each thread its own stack partition, and because the stacks expand downward, the offsetting prevents them from overlapping. However, Thread_stack_size must be a multiple of 8 to ensure that each resulting PSP_values[i] is 8-byte aligned. Misaligned stack addresses on the Cortex-M4 can result in faults or undefined behavior during exception entry/exit and context transitions.\n\nThe base address and each computed stack pointer can be aligned explicitly using bit masking or alignment properties, such as:\nPSP_values[i] = (base_address - (i + 1) × Thread_stack_size). This masks the final three bits and aligns to the nearest lower 8-byte boundary. Although this topic does not address context switching, the actual switching would take place using __set_PSP(PSP_values[i]) and activating PSP mode with __set_CONTROL(2). Because each stack pointer is derived from a single validated base address with a fixed-size, properly aligned stride, all stack regions are guaranteed to be valid, unique, and Cortex-M4 compliant.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,1,'2025-06-24_22-53-55','When we directly provided the address of the print_continuously function to the PC register, the processor instantly began executing that function, resulting in an infinite loop that repeatedly printed \"Hello, PC!\". This happened because the program counter (PC) always contains the address of the next instruction to be executed. By setting it the address of a function, we effectively drove the CPU to execute that function without a formal call. Although this avoids the standard function call procedure (which includes recording a return address), the end result is the same: the processor begins performing the function. This low-level control over PC functions similar to a direct function call, however it occurs at the register level.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,1,'2025-06-25_11-06-27','When we directly provided the address of the print_continuously function to the PC register, the processor instantly began executing that function, resulting in an infinite loop that repeatedly printed \"Hello, PC!\". This happened because the program counter (PC) always contains the address of the next instruction to be executed. By setting it the address of a function, we effectively drove the CPU to execute that function without a formal call. Although this avoids the standard function call procedure (which includes recording a return address), the end result is the same: the processor begins performing the function. This low-level control over PC functions similar to a direct function call, however it occurs at the register level.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,1,'2025-06-25_12-31-21','When we directly provided the address of the print_continuously function to the PC register, the processor instantly began executing that function, resulting in an infinite loop that repeatedly printed \"Hello, PC!\". This happened because the program counter (PC) always contains the address of the next instruction to be executed. By setting it the address of a function, we effectively drove the CPU to execute that function without a formal call. Although this avoids the standard function call procedure (which includes recording a return address), the end result is the same: the processor begins performing the function. This low-level control over PC functions similar to a direct function call, however it occurs at the register level.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,1,'2025-06-25_12-56-47','When we directly provided the address of the print_continuously function to the PC register, the processor instantly began executing that function, resulting in an infinite loop that repeatedly printed \"Hello, PC!\". This happened because the program counter (PC) always contains the address of the next instruction to be executed. By setting it the address of a function, we effectively drove the CPU to execute that function without a formal call. Although this avoids the standard function call procedure (which includes recording a return address), the end result is the same: the processor begins performing the function. This low-level control over PC functions similar to a direct function call, however it occurs at the register level.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,1,'2025-06-25_20-05-26','When we directly provided the address of the print_continuously function to the PC register, the processor instantly began executing that function, resulting in an infinite loop that repeatedly printed \"Hello, PC!\". This happened because the program counter (PC) always contains the address of the next instruction to be executed. By setting it the address of a function, we effectively drove the CPU to execute that function without a formal call. Although this avoids the standard function call procedure (which includes recording a return address), the end result is the same: the processor begins performing the function. This low-level control over PC functions similar to a direct function call, however it occurs at the register level.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,2,'2025-06-24_22-53-55','A system call is a request made by a program to the operating system for a task that the application does not have direct authority to perform typically anything that requires privileged access, such as hardware control or memory management. In ARM-based systems, this request is sent using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction, which transfers control to the operating system, allowing it to handle the task in kernel mode.\n',0,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,2,'2025-06-25_11-06-27','A system call is a request made by a program to the operating system for a task that the application does not have direct authority to perform, typically anything that requires privileged access, such as hardware control or memory management. It is a special kind of function call that allows a user program to transition into kernel mode to safely request operating system services. In ARM-based systems, this request is sent using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction, which transfers control to the operating system, allowing it to handle the task in kernel mode. This mechanism ensures access control and enforces system security by restricting privileged operations to the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,2,'2025-06-25_12-31-21','A system call is a request made by a program to the operating system for a task that the application does not have direct authority to perform, typically anything that requires privileged access, such as hardware control or memory management. It is a special kind of function call that allows a user program to transition into kernel mode to safely request operating system services. In ARM-based systems, this request is sent using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction, which transfers control to the operating system, allowing it to handle the task in kernel mode. This mechanism ensures access control and enforces system security by restricting privileged operations to the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,2,'2025-06-25_12-56-47','A system call is a request made by a program to the operating system for a task that the application does not have direct authority to perform, typically anything that requires privileged access, such as hardware control or memory management. It is a special kind of function call that allows a user program to transition into kernel mode to safely request operating system services. In ARM-based systems, this request is sent using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction, which transfers control to the operating system, allowing it to handle the task in kernel mode. This mechanism ensures access control and enforces system security by restricting privileged operations to the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,2,'2025-06-25_20-05-26','A system call is a request made by a program to the operating system for a task that the application does not have direct authority to perform, typically anything that requires privileged access, such as hardware control or memory management. It is a special kind of function call that allows a user program to transition into kernel mode to safely request operating system services. In ARM-based systems, this request is sent using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction, which transfers control to the operating system, allowing it to handle the task in kernel mode. This mechanism ensures access control and enforces system security by restricting privileged operations to the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,3,'2025-06-24_22-53-55','Setting up a thread\'s stack involves preparing a specific memory region so that the processor can treat the thread as if it were already active. We start by pointing stackptr to a valid stack memory location. Then, we manually add register values onto that stack in a specific order, beginning with xPSR and PC, then LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11 to R4. This order is critical because, upon an exception return or context transition, the CPU recovers registers from the stack in the exact sequence. Preloading the stack with values in the correct layout allows the CPU to \"resume\" the thread as if it had been interrupted previously. This setup ensures that the thread runs the correct function with known register values. If the order is not correct then the thread will misbehave or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,3,'2025-06-25_11-06-27','Setting up a thread\'s stack involves preparing a specific memory region so that the processor can treat the thread as if it were already active. We start by pointing stackptr to a valid stack memory location. Then, we manually add register values onto that stack in a specific order, beginning with xPSR and PC, then LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11 to R4. This order is critical because, upon an exception return or context transition, the CPU recovers registers from the stack in the exact sequence. Preloading the stack with values in the correct layout allows the CPU to \"resume\" the thread as if it had been interrupted previously. This setup ensures that the thread runs the correct function with known register values. If the order is not correct then the thread will misbehave or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,3,'2025-06-25_12-31-21','Setting up a thread\'s stack involves preparing a specific memory region so that the processor can treat the thread as if it were already active. We start by pointing stackptr to a valid stack memory location. Then, we manually add register values onto that stack in a specific order, beginning with xPSR and PC, then LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11 to R4. This order is critical because, upon an exception return or context transition, the CPU recovers registers from the stack in the exact sequence. Preloading the stack with values in the correct layout allows the CPU to \"resume\" the thread as if it had been interrupted previously. This setup ensures that the thread runs the correct function with known register values. If the order is not correct then the thread will misbehave or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,3,'2025-06-25_12-56-47','Setting up a thread\'s stack involves preparing a specific memory region so that the processor can treat the thread as if it were already active. We start by pointing stackptr to a valid stack memory location. Then, we manually add register values onto that stack in a specific order, beginning with xPSR and PC, then LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11 to R4. This order is critical because, upon an exception return or context transition, the CPU recovers registers from the stack in the exact sequence. Preloading the stack with values in the correct layout allows the CPU to \"resume\" the thread as if it had been interrupted previously. This setup ensures that the thread runs the correct function with known register values. If the order is not correct then the thread will misbehave or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,3,'2025-06-25_20-05-26','Setting up a thread\'s stack involves preparing a specific memory region so that the processor can treat the thread as if it were already active. We start by pointing stackptr to a valid stack memory location. Then, we manually add register values onto that stack in a specific order, beginning with xPSR and PC, then LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11 to R4. This order is critical because, upon an exception return or context transition, the CPU recovers registers from the stack in the exact sequence. Preloading the stack with values in the correct layout allows the CPU to \"resume\" the thread as if it had been interrupted previously. This setup ensures that the thread runs the correct function with known register values. If the order is not correct then the thread will misbehave or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,4,'2025-06-24_22-53-55','If we hadn\'t called __set_PSP() before switching to the thread, the processor would continue to use the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) rather than the new thread stack we created. Since the thread\'s context (register values) was pushed onto its own stack, not the MSP, the CPU attempted to retrieve those data from the incorrect memory address. This would result in corrupted register values and undefined behavior, most likely producing a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,4,'2025-06-25_11-06-27','If we hadn’t called __set_PSP() before switching to the thread, the processor would continue to use the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) rather than the new thread stack we created. Since the thread\'s context (register values) was pushed onto its own stack, not the MSP, the CPU would attempt to retrieve those values from the wrong memory location, resulting in corrupted data and potentially a hard fault. In ARM Cortex-M systems, thread execution is intended to use the Processor Stack Pointer (PSP), which must be explicitly configured using __set_PSP() before the thread starts running. By setting PSP to point to the top of the thread’s stack, we ensure that the processor pops register values from the correct location during the context switch. This fixes the mismatch between the stack being used and the stack that was prepared for the thread, and allows the thread to execute properly.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,4,'2025-06-25_12-31-21','If we hadn’t called __set_PSP() before switching to the thread, the processor would continue to use the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) rather than the new thread stack we created. Since the thread\'s context (register values) was pushed onto its own stack, not the MSP, the CPU would attempt to retrieve those values from the wrong memory location, resulting in corrupted data and potentially a hard fault. In ARM Cortex-M systems, thread execution is intended to use the Processor Stack Pointer (PSP), which must be explicitly configured using __set_PSP() before the thread starts running. By setting PSP to point to the top of the thread’s stack, we ensure that the processor pops register values from the correct location during the context switch. This fixes the mismatch between the stack being used and the stack that was prepared for the thread, and allows the thread to execute properly.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,4,'2025-06-25_12-56-47','If we hadn’t called __set_PSP() before switching to the thread, the processor would continue to use the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) rather than the new thread stack we created. Since the thread\'s context (register values) was pushed onto its own stack, not the MSP, the CPU would attempt to retrieve those values from the wrong memory location, resulting in corrupted data and potentially a hard fault. In ARM Cortex-M systems, thread execution is intended to use the Processor Stack Pointer (PSP), which must be explicitly configured using __set_PSP() before the thread starts running. By setting PSP to point to the top of the thread’s stack, we ensure that the processor pops register values from the correct location during the context switch. This fixes the mismatch between the stack being used and the stack that was prepared for the thread, and allows the thread to execute properly.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,4,'2025-06-25_20-05-26','If we hadn’t called __set_PSP() before switching to the thread, the processor would continue to use the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) rather than the new thread stack we created. Since the thread\'s context (register values) was pushed onto its own stack, not the MSP, the CPU would attempt to retrieve those values from the wrong memory location, resulting in corrupted data and potentially a hard fault. In ARM Cortex-M systems, thread execution is intended to use the Processor Stack Pointer (PSP), which must be explicitly configured using __set_PSP() before the thread starts running. By setting PSP to point to the top of the thread’s stack, we ensure that the processor pops register values from the correct location during the context switch. This fixes the mismatch between the stack being used and the stack that was prepared for the thread, and allows the thread to execute properly.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,5,'2025-06-24_22-53-55','If the thread function didn\'t loop and instead returned after printing once, the application would most likely crash or throw a hard error. This is due to the fact that the thread stack we created had placeholder values such as 0xA for registers, including the link register. When the thread returns, the processor attempts to utilize LR to leap back to a return address, but because LR was not set to a valid instruction address, the CPU jumps to an invalid memory location, resulting in a crash.\n',0,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,5,'2025-06-25_11-06-27','If the thread function did not contain an infinite loop and instead returned after completing execution, the processor would likely trigger a hardfault. This occurs because the thread\'s stack does not include a valid return address, which would normally be placed there by the compiler during a regular function call. In our manual stack setup, we used placeholder values such as 0xA, including for the Link Register (LR). When the function returns, the CPU attempts to branch to the value in LR, but since it does not point to a valid memory address, the processor jumps to an invalid location, causing a fault. Without a proper return address in the thread\'s stack frame, the exception return process fails, resulting in a hardfault.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,5,'2025-06-25_12-31-21','If the thread function did not contain an infinite loop and instead returned after completing execution, the processor would likely trigger a hardfault. This occurs because the thread\'s stack does not include a valid return address, which would normally be placed there by the compiler during a regular function call. In our manual stack setup, we used placeholder values such as 0xA, including for the Link Register (LR). When the function returns, the CPU attempts to branch to the value in LR, but since it does not point to a valid memory address, the processor jumps to an invalid location, causing a fault. Without a proper return address in the thread\'s stack frame, the exception return process fails, resulting in a hardfault.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,5,'2025-06-25_12-56-47','If the thread function did not contain an infinite loop and instead returned after completing execution, the processor would likely trigger a hardfault. This occurs because the thread\'s stack does not include a valid return address, which would normally be placed there by the compiler during a regular function call. In our manual stack setup, we used placeholder values such as 0xA, including for the Link Register (LR). When the function returns, the CPU attempts to branch to the value in LR, but since it does not point to a valid memory address, the processor jumps to an invalid location, causing a fault. Without a proper return address in the thread\'s stack frame, the exception return process fails, resulting in a hardfault.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',2,5,'2025-06-25_20-05-26','If the thread function did not contain an infinite loop and instead returned after completing execution, the processor would likely trigger a hardfault. This occurs because the thread\'s stack does not include a valid return address, which would normally be placed there by the compiler during a regular function call. In our manual stack setup, we used placeholder values such as 0xA, including for the Link Register (LR). When the function returns, the CPU attempts to branch to the value in LR, but since it does not point to a valid memory address, the processor jumps to an invalid location, causing a fault. Without a proper return address in the thread\'s stack frame, the exception return process fails, resulting in a hardfault.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',3,1,'2025-06-25_20-10-36','When a program is split into multiple .c files, the compiler compiles each one independently into an object file (.o), which contains machine code but is not yet complete. These object files are then sent to a linker, which converts them into a single executable file. During linking, the linker resolves any references between files, such as when one file calls a function or variable specified in another. This step ensures that everything is properly connected. The result is a single binary executable that can be run on the intended hardware, such as an STM32 board.\n',0,'s25'),('s2alrifa',3,1,'2025-06-26_14-03-03','When a program is split into multiple .c files, the compiler compiles each one independently into an object file (.o), which contains machine code but is not yet complete. Before compilation, the preprocessor handles tasks like expanding macros and including header files, which sets up the source code for the compilation phase. These object files are then sent to a linker, which converts them into a single executable file. During linking, the linker resolves any references between files, such as when one file calls a function or variable specified in another. It replaces placeholders in the object files with the correct addresses of functions and variables. This step ensures that everything is properly connected. The result is a single binary executable that can be run on the intended hardware, such as an STM32 board.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',3,2,'2025-06-25_20-10-36','Using #include is a bad idea because the #include statement is meant for header files (.h) rather than source files. Including a .c file effectively pastes its whole content into another file at compilation time, which might cause issues such as multiple function or variable definitions. This disrupts the standard compilation process, in which each .c file is compiled separately into an object file and then linked. Including .c files undermines modularity, creates duplicate code concerns, and complicates debugging and maintaining the code. Instead, we should declare shared functions or variables in a .h file and then include them in .c files.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',3,2,'2025-06-26_14-03-03','Using #include is a bad idea because the #include statement is meant for header files (.h) rather than source files. Including a .c file effectively pastes its whole content into another file at compilation time, which might cause issues such as multiple function or variable definitions. This disrupts the standard compilation process, in which each .c file is compiled separately into an object file and then linked. Including .c files undermines modularity, creates duplicate code concerns, and complicates debugging and maintaining the code. Instead, we should declare shared functions or variables in a .h file and then include them in .c files.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',3,3,'2025-06-25_20-10-36','To create and start a thread, first initialize the kernel by invoking a procedure that prepares the operating system\'s internal structures, such as setting global variables and defining the stack pool\'s starting point.  This initialization phase must be completed before any threads are created.  Next, the user must create the thread function, which is the code that the thread will execute.  It normally accepts a void* argument and returns void, even when no arguments are supplied. After writing the thread function, the user establishes the thread by using a kernel-provided function that configures the thread\'s stack and stores the thread information in the operating system. This phase may fail if there is insufficient memory, thus it is a good idea to test for success. Finally, after the kernel has been initialized and at least one thread has been established, the user invokes the kernel by invoking a method that initiates thread execution. This function instructs the processor to use the thread\'s stack and passes control from the main program to the thread. From this point forward, the thread operates independently on the OS infrastructure.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',3,3,'2025-06-26_14-03-03','To create and start a thread, first initialize the kernel by invoking a procedure that prepares the operating system\'s internal structures, such as setting global variables and defining the stack pool\'s starting point.  This initialization phase must be completed before any threads are created.  Next, the user must create the thread function, which is the code that the thread will execute.  It normally accepts a void* argument and returns void, even when no arguments are supplied. After writing the thread function, the user establishes the thread by using a kernel-provided function that configures the thread\'s stack and stores the thread information in the operating system. This phase may fail if there is insufficient memory, thus it is a good idea to test for success. Finally, after the kernel has been initialized and at least one thread has been established, the user invokes the kernel by invoking a method that initiates thread execution. This function instructs the processor to use the thread\'s stack and passes control from the main program to the thread. From this point forward, the thread operates independently on the OS infrastructure.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',3,4,'2025-06-25_20-10-36','The stack pool is a memory block dedicated exclusively for thread stacks in the operating system.  Each thread in the OS requires its own private stack space to hold local variables, return addresses, and register values while running.  Instead of assigning stack space at random or dynamically from the heap, the operating system allocates stack memory in a regulated and predictable manner using the stack pool. The stack pool begins at a known memory location (usually the beginning value of the Main Stack Pointer) and is divided into fixed-sized chunks, with each new thread receiving its own chunk.  In future experiments, this method will allow the OS to manage numerous threads efficiently by assigning each one to its own piece of the stack pool, minimizing stack overlap and ensuring reliable context switching between them.  By monitoring how much of the stack pool is being used, the operating system can properly allocate stacks to new threads and prohibit thread creation if there is insufficient memory.\n',0,'s25'),('s2alrifa',3,4,'2025-06-26_14-03-03','The stack pool is a memory block shared by both the operating system and threads, not exclusively for thread stacks. Each thread in the OS requires its own private stack space to hold local variables, return addresses, and register values while running. Additionally, the OS itself also relies on the stack pool to manage its own stack, which is necessary for handling interrupts and managing system-level tasks. Instead of assigning stack space at random or dynamically from the heap, the operating system allocates stack memory in a regulated and predictable manner using the stack pool. The stack pool begins at a known memory location (usually the beginning value of the Main Stack Pointer) and is divided into fixed-sized chunks, with each new thread receiving its own chunk. The Main Stack Pointer (MSP) is used for the operating system\'s stack, while the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is used for individual thread stacks. This separation ensures that the OS can maintain its stack independently of thread stacks, and each thread operates in its own stack space.\nIn future labs, this method will allow the OS to manage numerous threads efficiently by assigning each one to its own piece of the stack pool, minimizing stack overlap and ensuring reliable context switching between them. By monitoring how much of the stack pool is being used, the operating system can properly allocate stacks to new threads and prohibit thread creation if there is insufficient memory.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',3,5,'2025-06-25_20-10-36','The \"kernel\" is the basic component of the operating system that handles low-level processes including stack allocation, thread management, and context switching.  The kernel.c and kernel.h files contain kernel-related code.  These files describe and implement the operating system\'s underlying logic and data structures.  In contrast, the \"user\" is the programmer who interacts with the kernel by writing application-level code, such as thread functions and the main program.  The user-level code is located in main.c. From the user\'s perspective, this separation is significant because it allows them to generate and manage threads using a simple and clean interface without having to learn or modify the underlying OS technology.  The kernel hides the complexities of memory management and context switching, leaving the user to focus solely on writing thread behavior and invoking methods such as osCreateThread() and osKernelStart().  This modular form makes the code easier to read, maintain, and grow in subsequent laboratories.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',3,5,'2025-06-26_14-03-03','The \"kernel\" is the basic component of the operating system that handles low-level processes including stack allocation, thread management, and context switching.  The kernel.c and kernel.h files contain kernel-related code.  These files describe and implement the operating system\'s underlying logic and data structures.  In contrast, the \"user\" is the programmer who interacts with the kernel by writing application-level code, such as thread functions and the main program.  The user-level code is located in main.c. From the user\'s perspective, this separation is significant because it allows them to generate and manage threads using a simple and clean interface without having to learn or modify the underlying OS technology.  The kernel hides the complexities of memory management and context switching, leaving the user to focus solely on writing thread behavior and invoking methods such as osCreateThread() and osKernelStart().  This modular form makes the code easier to read, maintain, and grow in subsequent laboratories.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',4,1,'2025-07-22_15-34-54','Round-robin scheduling might be implemented with the use of a queue wherein the TCB of every thread sits in a circular queue. Its TCB is removed from the front of the queue upon yielding and is later inserted into the rear end of the queue, while scheduling of the newly arrived node is undertaken. This is a fair and relatively predictable way to groom the competing threads while scrambling for the same CPU: each designation gets one turn. Compared to an array and an index, where they were used in the lab context, this approach offers additional abstraction and modularity that can help, for instance, if the OS has to scale to support features such as dynamic thread creation or priority-based scheduling. Of course, more flexibility sometimes equals a bit of extra cost. Consider a queue structure wherein you have to enqueue and dequeue  it may be an overhead in terms of complexity or slight slowness, whereas direct array indexing requires none. Given the nature of a small embedded system, such as the one involved with the lab, simpler and faster is probably the more practical option. The queue offers more flexibility and is better for systems that have to expand or accommodate advanced features; for now, it is probably more than what is really required.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',4,2,'2025-07-22_15-34-54','PendSV_Handler is what enables a thread switch to actually take place. When osYield() is called from within a thread, a special bit is set in the interrupt control register, requesting that the PendSV interrupt be served but only after all other higher-priority interrupts finish. When the PendSV interrupt starts, it gets the current value of the PSP and, with that, the location of the current thread’s stack. Then, it saves registers R4–R11 onto this stack, since those are not saved automatically. The thread’s TCB is then updated with the current value of the PSP so it knows where to continue later. Next, osSched() is called to determine the next thread to run. The PSP is set to the new thread\'s saved stack, and the registers that were saved earlier are restored so the new thread can resume exactly where it left off. This whole process allows one thread to be suspended while another picks up right where it left off, with its full state intact.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',4,3,'2025-07-22_15-34-54','At first glance, letting threads have priorities can sound like a nice improvement — the obvious thing to do would be to allow the most important tasks to run first. However, problems really occur if the one whose priority is highest is always chosen to run. Should the highest priority thread always be kept ready to run, then it may just steal away the whole CPU, with the low-priority threads left waiting forever. This whole thing is called starvation, and it defeats the whole purpose of multitasking. Instead of sharing the processor justifiably amongst tasks, the system ends up favoring only one. This also sets the stage for a priority inversion problem: a low-priority thread is holding a resource needed by the high-priority thread, and the system cannot proceed without extra mechanisms such as priority inheritance. In simple cooperative systems such as the one we made in this lab, introducing priority logic without some advanced scheduling tools just forces unfairness and complexity. Round-robin scheduling isn\'t anything fancy, but it makes sure that every thread gets a chance to run, which more often than not in a lightweight embedded OS is what matter most.',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',4,4,'2025-07-22_15-34-54','Calling C functions like osSched() and SVC_HandlerMain() from within our interrupt handlers makes the code a lot easier to read, understand, and work with. Writing logic like picking the next thread or managing stack pointers is much simpler in C, especially when you\'re trying to debug or modify things later. It\'s more beginner-friendly—the teaching lab needs it to be so. Contrarily, there is definitely some overhead in calling C from assembly since you need to respect calling conventions, but with that comes a bit of overhead regarding compiler-generated function call instructions and register saving. With all coding in pure assembly, you will have more control-therefore, more power optimization potential-a slight drawback is the difficulty in writing and maintaining it in due course. So, in this lab, assembly code is kept at the barest bone level of things saving and restoring registers, and the C code takes care of scheduling, which is almost an ideal fit between performance and clarity.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',4,5,'2025-07-22_15-34-54','The effects of context switching won\'t be noticeable if there is only one thread running, such as a modified print continuously thread that calls osYield() after each print. When a thread yields, the PendSV interrupt will be triggered normally, and the scheduler will be contacted to choose the next thread to execute. However, the scheduler will just pick the same thread again because there aren\'t any other threads in the system. This indicates that, practically as if no switch had occurred at all, the thread picks up where it left off. Although this negates the primary goal of multitasking, the system is unaffected. Behind the scenes, the OS continues to perform its duties, and its reasoning is sound even when only one thread is used. The system operates as intended; it simply does not utilize the multitasking capabilities that are intended to manage multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,1,'2025-07-22_21-59-33','Before passing an argument to a thread, the user must first define the thread function to take one void* argument. When the user calls osCreateThread, they give it a pointer to the input they want. This could be a pointer to a more complex structure with multiple fields or just an int. By correctly casting and dereferencing the argument inside the thread function, we can get the original data. The operating system sets up the thread\'s first stack frame so that this pointer is stored in register R0. This is because the ARM Application Binary Interface (ABI) says that the first function argument must be passed in R0. Pulling this value from R0, the thread receives the argument correctly when it first starts up. This method is flexible because a void* can point to any data type, and it ensures consistency by always using the same register for input. It also keeps thread creation modular and generic, regardless of what specific data the user wants to pass in.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,1,'2025-07-22_22-06-26','The user must first make the thread function accept a single void* argument before passing an argument to a thread. The user should then pass a pointer to the value or structure they wish the thread to use when osCreateThread is used to create the thread. This could be a multi-field structure or something as basic as an int. To retrieve the original data, void* is cast back to the original type in the thread. According to the ARM ABI convention, which states that the first function parameter must go in R0, the OS takes that pointer and places it in register R0 when it is creating the thread. Thus, when the thread begins to run, the data is already  in the correct register. This is due to the fact that void* can point to anything, and the ABI ensures that the thread function will always get its argument in the same way.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,1,'2025-07-23_11-52-02','The user must first make the thread function accept a single void* argument before passing an argument to a thread. The user should then pass a pointer to the value or structure they wish the thread to use when osCreateThread is used to create the thread. This could be a multi-field structure or something as basic as an int. To retrieve the original data, void* is cast back to the original type in the thread. According to the ARM ABI convention, which states that the first function parameter must go in R0, the OS takes that pointer and places it in register R0 when it is creating the thread. Thus, when the thread begins to run, the data is already  in the correct register. This is due to the fact that void* can point to anything, and the ABI ensures that the thread function will always get its argument in the same way.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,1,'2025-07-23_12-01-30','The user must declare the thread function to take a single void* argument before passing an argument to the thread. They then pass a pointer to the value, or to a structure of values, that they would like the thread to work with when creating the thread with osCreateThread. This may be a multi-field structure, or it may be as simple as an int. They will cast void* back to the original type in their thread in order to retrieve the original data. The ARM ABI calls for the first function parameter to be put in R0. The OS takes that pointer and puts it in register R0 when it is creating the thread. So when the thread begins to run, the data is already in the right register. This is because void* can point to anything and, as the ABI is ensuring the thread function will always receive its argument the same way, it can make no assumptions about what the function argument actually is. That works, but using void* also gives up type safety, so the programmer must take care to cast it back properly. For certain ARM versions, or in certain calling contexts, arguments may be passed on the stack as well, so knowing both can be useful. It is also technically possible to pass arguments via global memory, or have the user place them in a known place in the thread\'s control block or something similar, but that\'s rarer.',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,2,'2025-07-22_21-59-33','Since PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, and SysTick_Handler are already included in the operating system\'s context-switching system, you cannot call osYield from any function that switches contexts. Recursive context switching, which is the attempt to initiate a new context switch while one is already underway, may occur if osYield is called inside of them. This causes stack corruption and behavior that is hard to predict because the thread state being saved and restored could be interrupted or overwritten. This kind of recursive or re-entrant context switching breaks the atomicity of these important routines and can make the whole system unstable. So, to make sure that threads are handled correctly and the system is stable, osYield should only be used in normal thread code and never from inside interrupt or handler contexts.\n',0,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,2,'2025-07-22_22-06-26','It is not allowed to invoke osYield in context-switching procedures like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these procedures are already in the act of processing a context switch. To call osYield in one of them is effectively asking the system to execute another context switch when it\'s still engaged in processing one, and this is also called recursive or re-entrant context switching. This can cause serious issues like stack corruption as the system would try to save or restore a thread\'s state when it is halfway through doing so. It breaks the integrity and sequence of thread execution and can take down the whole system. Such handler routines need to finish uninterrupted, so osYield has to be called by normal thread code but never in such handlers.\n',0,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,2,'2025-07-23_11-52-02','It is not allowed to invoke osYield in context-switching procedures like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these procedures are already in the act of processing a context switch. To call osYield in one of them is effectively asking the system to execute another context switch when it\'s still engaged in processing one, and this is also called recursive or re-entrant context switching. This can cause serious issues like stack corruption as the system would try to save or restore a thread\'s state when it is halfway through doing so. It breaks the integrity and sequence of thread execution and can take down the whole system. Such handler routines need to finish uninterrupted, so osYield has to be called by normal thread code but never in such handlers.\n',0,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,2,'2025-07-23_12-01-30','It is not allowed to invoke osYield in context-switching procedures like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these procedures are already in the act of processing a context switch. To call osYield in one of them is effectively asking the system to execute another context switch when it\'s still engaged in processing one, which is known as recursive or re-entrant context switching. This can lead to problems like stack corruption or a hardfault, as the system may try to save or restore thread context while it is in an unstable or incomplete state. More importantly, osYield is a system call it is implemented using the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction, which causes a software triggered exception. Calling a system call like this from inside an interrupt handler can trigger a new interrupt while another is already being serviced, which breaks normal interrupt sequencing and can overwhelm the stack. On Cortex-M4, this can interfere with tail-chaining and the processor\'s interrupt priority handling, causing unpredictable behavior. Because interrupt handlers need to run to completion without being interrupted by new system calls, osYield must only be called from thread-level code, not from within another interrupt context.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,3,'2025-07-22_21-59-33','The operating system\'s timing resolution would be improved by lowering the fundamental OS clock from 1 millisecond to 0.1 milliseconds, which would enable it to react to time-sensitive events more rapidly and accurately. In real-time applications like motor control or high-frequency signal sampling, where a finer granularity is required, this might be advantageous. But there are also serious drawbacks to this change. Most significantly, it causes a significant overhead on the system by increasing the number of SysTick interrupts per second by a factor of 10. There would be less time for the processor to run actual thread code since it would be spending more time managing interrupts and switching contexts. Power performance and overall system efficiency may suffer as a result. Furthermore, sub-millisecond resolution is not necessary for many embedded applications, so the price of higher precision may not be justifiable. For general use, a 1 ms clock offers a practical balance between responsiveness and system overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,3,'2025-07-22_22-06-26','Adjusting the OS clock to 0.1 ms would actually make the system responsive due to the fact that it increases the timing resolution. What this means is that it now can handle short timeouts or brief delays more precisely, which is beneficial to time-critical applications. The downside is that it also increases the interrupts by a factor of 10. The processor would be spending far more time dealing with SysTick interrupts and context switching, which imposes overhead and can slow down your actual threads. It also takes more power and might be unnecessary for the majority of embedded systems where 1 ms is near enough already. So while 0.1 ms improves precision, it can degrade efficiency and isn\'t always a worthwhile enough compromise.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,3,'2025-07-23_11-52-02','Adjusting the OS clock to 0.1 ms would actually make the system responsive due to the fact that it increases the timing resolution. What this means is that it now can handle short timeouts or brief delays more precisely, which is beneficial to time-critical applications. The downside is that it also increases the interrupts by a factor of 10. The processor would be spending far more time dealing with SysTick interrupts and context switching, which imposes overhead and can slow down your actual threads. It also takes more power and might be unnecessary for the majority of embedded systems where 1 ms is near enough already. So while 0.1 ms improves precision, it can degrade efficiency and isn\'t always a worthwhile enough compromise.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,3,'2025-07-23_12-01-30','Adjusting the OS clock to 0.1 ms would actually make the system responsive due to the fact that it increases the timing resolution. What this means is that it now can handle short timeouts or brief delays more precisely, which is beneficial to time-critical applications. The downside is that it also increases the interrupts by a factor of 10. The processor would be spending far more time dealing with SysTick interrupts and context switching, which imposes overhead and can slow down your actual threads. It also takes more power and might be unnecessary for the majority of embedded systems where 1 ms is near enough already. Therefore while 0.1 ms improves precision, it can degrade efficiency and isn\'t always a worthwhile enough compromise. For example, if the system is just reading sensors every few milliseconds or updating a display, the added resolution may be wasted. But for systems with fine motor control or high-speed communication, that extra resolution could make a difference.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,4,'2025-07-22_21-59-33','Cooperative multitasking requires threads to be fair by independently calling osYield to allow others to run. A thread that refuses to yield may wind up using up all of the CPU and preventing the system from functioning. Because of this, it is particularly crucial that the programmer write thoughtful, well-behaved code. A different strategy is used by pre-emptive multitasking, which uses a timer such as SysTick to automatically interrupt threads after a predetermined amount of time, regardless of what the thread is doing. This makes the system more reliable and responsive because it doesn’t have to depend on the user to manage sharing. Because context switches only happen at predictable points where threads yield, co-operative multitasking is easier to implement from the OS\'s point of view. Preemptive multitasking is more complicated; the operating system must keep track of timers, monitor thread runtime, and trigger interrupts to enforce switching. Despite the extra effort, pre-emptive multitasking ensures fair CPU usage and better responsiveness, especially when combining multiple independently operating threads.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,4,'2025-07-22_22-06-26','In cooperative multitasking, threads voluntarily decide to give up the CPU by calling osYield. If a thread does not yield, it runs and other threads are blocked waiting, so the programmer needs to guarantee that all the threads are getting their fair shot. It\'s easier for the OS to handle since context switches only happen when threads ask for them to happen. Pre-emptive multitasking works differently the OS uses a timer like SysTick to interrupt threads periodically and make them switch after a predetermined time period. This makes the system more responsive and does not let any one thread hog the CPU, but it\'s harder to implement because the OS needs to handle time and interrupts correctly. Pre-emption is complex, but it keeps things running nicer in general.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,4,'2025-07-23_11-52-02','In cooperative multitasking, threads voluntarily decide to give up the CPU by calling osYield. If a thread does not yield, it runs and other threads are blocked waiting, so the programmer needs to guarantee that all the threads are getting their fair shot. It\'s easier for the OS to handle since context switches only happen when threads ask for them to happen. Pre-emptive multitasking works differently the OS uses a timer like SysTick to interrupt threads periodically and make them switch after a predetermined time period. This makes the system more responsive and does not let any one thread hog the CPU, but it\'s harder to implement because the OS needs to handle time and interrupts correctly. Pre-emption is complex, but it keeps things running nicer in general.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,4,'2025-07-23_12-01-30','In cooperative multitasking, threads voluntarily decide to give up the CPU by calling osYield. If a thread does not yield, it runs and other threads are blocked waiting, so the programmer needs to guarantee that all the threads are getting their fair shot. It\'s easier for the OS to handle since context switches only happen when threads ask for them to happen. Pre-emptive multitasking works differently the OS uses a timer like SysTick to interrupt threads periodically and make them switch after a predetermined time period. This makes the system more responsive and does not let any one thread hog the CPU, but it\'s harder to implement because the OS needs to handle time and interrupts correctly. Pre-emption is complex, but it keeps things running nicer in general. When the timer fires, the OS has to save the context of the current thread and pick the next one to run whether it wanted to yield or not, and that’s how the OS ensures every thread gets fair CPU time, even if they don’t cooperate.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,5,'2025-07-22_21-59-33','The last lab exercise combined preemptive context switching with yielding in a single system. Because both approaches ultimately rely on the same underlying mechanism using the PendSV interrupt to trigger a context switch this combination works. The OS instantly resets its runtime and starts a switch to the next thread when a thread voluntarily calls osYield. In order to reduce the amount of runtime left in the currently running thread, the system timer independently calls the SysTick_Handler every millisecond. The OS resets this runtime to zero and preempts the thread by instructing PendSV to switch the context. Both cooperative and preemptive threads can easily share the CPU because the runtime is reset after each switch, regardless of how it was triggered. This hybrid strategy allows threads to either yield manually or be interrupted by the system, enabling more flexible and fair multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,5,'2025-07-22_22-06-26','Both of these methods can be employed because they are both accomplishing a context switch using the same technique making PendSV pending. When a thread calls osYield, it causes the OS to switch threads by making PendSV pending. The pre-emptive system does the same: every millisecond, SysTick_Handler checks how long ago the thread has run and, if it is its turn, it also makes PendSV pending. Either way, the OS is invoking the same function to switch between threads. That\'s why both pre-emptive and cooperative switching can be used at the same time it doesn\'t matter how the switch is triggered, the OS does it in the same manner.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,5,'2025-07-23_11-52-02','Both of these methods can be employed because they are both accomplishing a context switch using the same technique making PendSV pending. When a thread calls osYield, it causes the OS to switch threads by making PendSV pending. The pre-emptive system does the same: every millisecond, SysTick_Handler checks how long ago the thread has run and, if it is its turn, it also makes PendSV pending. Either way, the OS is invoking the same function to switch between threads. That\'s why both pre-emptive and cooperative switching can be used at the same time it doesn\'t matter how the switch is triggered, the OS does it in the same manner.\n',1,'s25'),('s2alrifa',5,5,'2025-07-23_12-01-30','Both of these methods can be employed because they are both accomplishing a context switch using the same technique making PendSV pending. When a thread calls osYield, it causes the OS to switch threads by making PendSV pending. The pre-emptive system does the same: every millisecond, SysTick_Handler checks how long ago the thread has run and, if it is its turn, it also makes PendSV pending. Either way, the OS is invoking the same function to switch between threads. That\'s why both pre-emptive and cooperative switching can be used at the same time it doesn\'t matter how the switch is triggered, the OS does it in the same manner. PendSV_Handler does the actual switching, so both methods just use different triggers to reach the same point. This shared mechanism makes it simple for the OS to support both models at the same time without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,1,'2025-06-09_11-36-30','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a low-level structure of code that defines how the user code can interact with the hardware (i.e. digital and analog GPIO, UART and other I/O interfaces). This layer is important to enable the user to write code that can be understood by humans while performing hardware functionality. Abstraction is a very important concept in software development, as it enables developers to create defined interfaces that can be integrated into higher-level runtimes.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,1,'2025-06-10_12-29-56','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a low-level structure of code that defines how the user code can interact with the hardware (i.e. digital and analog GPIO, UART and other I/O interfaces). This layer is important to enable the user to write code that can be understood by humans while performing hardware functionality. Abstraction is a very important concept in software development, as it enables developers to create defined interfaces that can be integrated into higher-level runtimes.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,1,'2025-06-25_16-35-19','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a middleware structure of code that defines how the user code can interact with the hardware (i.e. digital and analog GPIO, UART and other I/O interfaces). This layer is important to enable the user to write code that can be understood by humans while performing hardware functionality. Abstraction is a very important concept in software development, as it enables developers to create defined interfaces that can be integrated into higher-level runtimes.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,1,'2025-06-26_19-25-26','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a middleware structure of code that defines how the user code can interact with the hardware (i.e. digital and analog GPIO, UART and other I/O interfaces). This layer is important to enable the user to write code that can be understood by humans while performing hardware functionality. Abstraction is a very important concept in software development, as it enables developers to create defined interfaces that can be integrated into higher-level runtimes.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,1,'2025-06-27_01-07-45','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a middleware structure of code that defines how the user code can interact with the hardware (i.e. digital and analog GPIO, UART and other I/O interfaces). This layer is important to enable the user to write code that can be understood by humans while performing hardware functionality. Abstraction is a very important concept in software development, as it enables developers to create defined interfaces that can be integrated into higher-level runtimes.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,2,'2025-06-09_11-36-30','The __io_putchar function takes a character input (a 1-byte variable representing any value defined in the ASCII table of codes, characters, and symbols) and internally calls the HAL_UART_Transmit function to send that character over the Nucleo board’s primary UART interface. This is achieved by passing &huart2 as the UART handler to the HAL_UART_Transmit function. Since the STM platform offers multiple serial I/O interfaces, this function effectively encapsulates the process of transmitting a single character over the serial bus, and it is used by the printf function to transmit data that gets displayed on the serial console. It serves as a modular and user-friendly building block that can be extended to transmit entire strings and further sequences of characters over UART I/O.\n',0,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,2,'2025-06-10_12-29-56','The __io_putchar function takes a character input (a 1-byte variable representing any value defined in the ASCII table of codes, characters, and symbols) and internally calls the HAL_UART_Transmit function to send that character over the Nucleo board’s primary UART interface. This is achieved by passing &huart2 as the UART handler to the HAL_UART_Transmit function. Since the STM platform offers multiple serial I/O interfaces, this function effectively encapsulates the process of transmitting a single character over the serial bus, and it is used by the printf function to transmit data that gets displayed on the serial console. It serves as a modular and user-friendly building block that can be extended to transmit entire strings and further sequences of characters over UART I/O.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,2,'2025-06-25_16-35-19','The __io_putchar function takes a character input (a 1-byte variable representing any value defined in the ASCII table of codes, characters, and symbols) and internally calls the HAL_UART_Transmit function to send that character over the Nucleo board’s primary UART interface. This is achieved by passing &huart2 as the UART handler to the HAL_UART_Transmit function. Since the STM platform offers multiple serial I/O interfaces, this function effectively encapsulates the process of transmitting a single character over the serial bus, and it is used by the printf function to transmit data that gets displayed on the serial console. It serves as a modular and user-friendly building block that can be extended to transmit entire strings and further sequences of characters over UART I/O.\n',0,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,2,'2025-06-26_19-25-26','The __io_putchar function takes a character input (a 1-byte variable representing any value defined in the ASCII table of codes, characters, and symbols) and internally calls the HAL_UART_Transmit function to send that character over the Nucleo board’s primary UART interface. This is achieved by passing &huart2 as the UART handler to the HAL_UART_Transmit function. Since the STM platform offers multiple serial I/O interfaces, this function effectively encapsulates the process of transmitting a single character over the serial bus, and it is used by the printf function to transmit data that gets displayed on the serial console. It serves as a modular and user-friendly building block that can be extended to transmit entire strings and further sequences of characters over UART I/O.\nThe reason __io_putchar is needed in the first place is because standard printf does not inherently know how to send characters to a specific hardware output. On embedded systems, the standard output must be manually mapped to a physical interface like UART. Defining __io_putchar allows us to redirect printf output to the correct serial port, depending on the hardware. If __io_putchar is not implemented, printf has no valid path to transmit characters, so any printf calls would either do nothing or cause a runtime error. This function provides the link between high-level output functions and low-level hardware, making the code portable and adaptable across different platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,2,'2025-06-27_01-07-45','The __io_putchar function takes a character input (a 1-byte variable representing any value defined in the ASCII table of codes, characters, and symbols) and internally calls the HAL_UART_Transmit function to send that character over the Nucleo board’s primary UART interface. This is achieved by passing &huart2 as the UART handler to the HAL_UART_Transmit function. Since the STM platform offers multiple serial I/O interfaces, this function effectively encapsulates the process of transmitting a single character over the serial bus, and it is used by the printf function to transmit data that gets displayed on the serial console. It serves as a modular and user-friendly building block that can be extended to transmit entire strings and further sequences of characters over UART I/O.\nThe reason __io_putchar is needed in the first place is because standard printf does not inherently know how to send characters to a specific hardware output. On embedded systems, the standard output must be manually mapped to a physical interface like UART. Defining __io_putchar allows us to redirect printf output to the correct serial port, depending on the hardware. If __io_putchar is not implemented, printf has no valid path to transmit characters, so any printf calls would either do nothing or cause a runtime error. This function provides the link between high-level output functions and low-level hardware, making the code portable and adaptable across different platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,3,'2025-06-09_11-36-30','We used the debugger to observe the Cortex-M4’s live memory while the program was running. In this lab, it allowed us to examine the actual contents of the stack at each step of code execution. This level of visibility is crucial because functions like printf are complex and can significantly alter the stack by the time they execute. As a result, relying on printf to inspect the stack could lead to misleading or overwritten data. By setting breakpoints, we were able to pause execution at precise moments and inspect the immediate memory state.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,3,'2025-06-10_12-29-56','We used the debugger to observe the Cortex-M4’s live memory while the program was running. In this lab, it allowed us to examine the actual contents of the stack at each step of code execution. This level of visibility is crucial because functions like printf are complex and can significantly alter the stack by the time they execute. As a result, relying on printf to inspect the stack could lead to misleading or overwritten data. By setting breakpoints, we were able to pause execution at precise moments and inspect the immediate memory state.\n',0,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,3,'2025-06-25_16-35-19','We used the debugger to observe the Cortex-M4’s live memory and register values while the program was running. In the lab, that meant we could examine the actual contents of the stack and key registers like the stack pointer (SP) and link register (LR) at each step of code execution. This was important for understanding how the stack was being used and how the program was flowing through function calls.\nThis level of visibility is critical in systems-level debugging, where understanding the control flow and precise memory usage matters. The SP, for instance, lets us verify that stack frames are being allocated and deallocated correctly, while the LR can help trace where functions will return after a call.\nUsing printf wouldn’t have worked here because it adds its own overhead, which can change the state of the stack and registers by the time it runs. The debugger allowed us to pause execution at specific points and inspect the exact memory and register state without interfering with it. By setting breakpoints, we were able to pause execution at precise moments and inspect the immediate memory state.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,3,'2025-06-26_19-25-26','We used the debugger to observe the Cortex-M4’s live memory and register values while the program was running. In the lab, that meant we could examine the actual contents of the stack and key registers like the stack pointer (SP) and link register (LR) at each step of code execution. This was important for understanding how the stack was being used and how the program was flowing through function calls.\nThis level of visibility is critical in systems-level debugging, where understanding the control flow and precise memory usage matters. The SP, for instance, lets us verify that stack frames are being allocated and deallocated correctly, while the LR can help trace where functions will return after a call.\nUsing printf wouldn’t have worked here because it adds its own overhead, which can change the state of the stack and registers by the time it runs. The debugger allowed us to pause execution at specific points and inspect the exact memory and register state without interfering with it. By setting breakpoints, we were able to pause execution at precise moments and inspect the immediate memory state.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,3,'2025-06-27_01-07-45','We used the debugger to observe the Cortex-M4’s live memory and register values while the program was running. In the lab, that meant we could examine the actual contents of the stack and key registers like the stack pointer (SP) and link register (LR) at each step of code execution. This was important for understanding how the stack was being used and how the program was flowing through function calls.\nThis level of visibility is critical in systems-level debugging, where understanding the control flow and precise memory usage matters. The SP, for instance, lets us verify that stack frames are being allocated and deallocated correctly, while the LR can help trace where functions will return after a call.\nUsing printf wouldn’t have worked here because it adds its own overhead, which can change the state of the stack and registers by the time it runs. The debugger allowed us to pause execution at specific points and inspect the exact memory and register state without interfering with it. By setting breakpoints, we were able to pause execution at precise moments and inspect the immediate memory state.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,4,'2025-06-09_11-36-30','We manually implemented stack allocation by determining the starting address of the PSP. First, we obtained the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), MSP_init_Val, which points to the top of the allocated stack space in memory. Since the stack grows downward and the PSP is positioned below the MSP, we calculated the starting address of the PSP by subtracting the chosen MAIN_STACK_SIZE of 0x400 from MSP_init_Val. This method allowed us to manually assign a value to PSP_Val.\n',0,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,4,'2025-06-10_12-29-56','We manually implemented stack allocation by determining the starting address of the PSP. First, we obtained the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), MSP_init_Val, which points to the top of the allocated stack space in memory. Since the stack grows downward and the PSP is positioned below the MSP, we calculated the starting address of the PSP by subtracting the chosen MAIN_STACK_SIZE of 0x400 from MSP_init_Val. This method allowed us to manually assign a value to PSP_Val.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,4,'2025-06-25_16-35-19','We manually implemented stack allocation by determining the starting address of the PSP. First, we obtained the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), MSP_init_Val, which points to the top of the allocated stack space in memory. Since the stack grows downward and the PSP is positioned below the MSP, we calculated the starting address of the PSP by subtracting the chosen MAIN_STACK_SIZE of 0x400 from MSP_init_Val. This method allowed us to manually assign a value to PSP_Val.\n',0,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,4,'2025-06-26_19-25-26','We manually implemented stack allocation by determining the starting address of the PSP. First, we obtained the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), MSP_init_Val, which points to the top of the allocated stack space in memory. Since the stack grows downward and the PSP is positioned below the MSP, we calculated the starting address of the PSP by subtracting the chosen MAIN_STACK_SIZE of 0x400 from MSP_init_Val. This method allowed us to manually assign a value to PSP_Val.\nKnowing the initial value of the MSP was essential because it defined the upper boundary of the stack memory region allocated at startup. By referencing it, we ensured that the calculated PSP value still pointed to a valid location within the same memory region. If we chose an arbitrary base address for the PSP without reference to the MSP, we could end up placing the Process Stack in an invalid or overlapping memory region, which could cause stack collisions or unpredictable behavior during execution.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,4,'2025-06-27_01-07-45','We manually implemented stack allocation by determining the starting address of the PSP. First, we obtained the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), MSP_init_Val, which points to the top of the allocated stack space in memory. Since the stack grows downward and the PSP is positioned below the MSP, we calculated the starting address of the PSP by subtracting the chosen MAIN_STACK_SIZE of 0x400 from MSP_init_Val. This method allowed us to manually assign a value to PSP_Val.\nKnowing the initial value of the MSP was essential because it defined the upper boundary of the stack memory region allocated at startup. By referencing it, we ensured that the calculated PSP value still pointed to a valid location within the same memory region. If we chose an arbitrary base address for the PSP without reference to the MSP, we could end up placing the Process Stack in an invalid or overlapping memory region, which could cause stack collisions or unpredictable behavior during execution.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,5,'2025-06-09_11-36-30','We can allocate multiple process stacks by referencing the _Min_Stack_Size defined in the STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld linker script, along with our values for MSP_Init_Val, MAIN_STACK_SIZE, and THREAD_STACK_SIZE, given we’ve defined that all threads have the same stack size in this method. The _Min_Stack_Size variable effectively sets the total available memory space for both the Main Stack and all Process (thread) stacks. Therefore, the combined size of the main stack and all thread stacks must not exceed this value of our global stack pool. To determine where each thread stack starts in memory, we can use the formula:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_Init_Val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE,\nwhere j is the thread index and satisfies:\nj ≤ (_Min_Stack_Size - MAIN_STACK_SIZE) / THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis calculation ensures each thread stack starts at a unique, properly aligned location in memory.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,5,'2025-06-10_12-29-56','We can allocate multiple process stacks by referencing the _Min_Stack_Size defined in the STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld linker script, along with our values for MSP_Init_Val, MAIN_STACK_SIZE, and THREAD_STACK_SIZE, given we’ve defined that all threads have the same stack size in this method. The _Min_Stack_Size variable effectively sets the total available memory space for both the Main Stack and all Process (thread) stacks. Therefore, the combined size of the main stack and all thread stacks must not exceed this value of our global stack pool. To determine where each thread stack starts in memory, we can use the formula:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_Init_Val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE,\nwhere j is the thread index and satisfies:\nj ≤ (_Min_Stack_Size - MAIN_STACK_SIZE) / THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis calculation ensures each thread stack starts at a unique, properly aligned location in memory.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,5,'2025-06-25_16-35-19','We can allocate multiple process stacks by referencing the _Min_Stack_Size defined in the STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld linker script, along with our values for MSP_Init_Val, MAIN_STACK_SIZE, and THREAD_STACK_SIZE, given we’ve defined that all threads have the same stack size in this method. The _Min_Stack_Size variable effectively sets the total available memory space for both the Main Stack and all Process (thread) stacks. Therefore, the combined size of the main stack and all thread stacks must not exceed this value of our global stack pool. To determine where each thread stack starts in memory, we can use the formula:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_Init_Val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE,\nwhere j is the thread index and satisfies:\nj ≤ (_Min_Stack_Size - MAIN_STACK_SIZE) / THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis calculation ensures each thread stack starts at a unique, properly aligned location in memory.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,5,'2025-06-26_19-25-26','We can allocate multiple process stacks by referencing the _Min_Stack_Size defined in the STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld linker script, along with our values for MSP_Init_Val, MAIN_STACK_SIZE, and THREAD_STACK_SIZE, given we’ve defined that all threads have the same stack size in this method. The _Min_Stack_Size variable effectively sets the total available memory space for both the Main Stack and all Process (thread) stacks. Therefore, the combined size of the main stack and all thread stacks must not exceed this value of our global stack pool. To determine where each thread stack starts in memory, we can use the formula:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_Init_Val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE,\nwhere j is the thread index and satisfies:\nj ≤ (_Min_Stack_Size - MAIN_STACK_SIZE) / THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis calculation ensures each thread stack starts at a unique, properly aligned location in memory.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',1,5,'2025-06-27_01-07-45','We can allocate multiple process stacks by referencing the _Min_Stack_Size defined in the STM32F401RETX_RAM.ld linker script, along with our values for MSP_Init_Val, MAIN_STACK_SIZE, and THREAD_STACK_SIZE, given we’ve defined that all threads have the same stack size in this method. The _Min_Stack_Size variable effectively sets the total available memory space for both the Main Stack and all Process (thread) stacks. Therefore, the combined size of the main stack and all thread stacks must not exceed this value of our global stack pool. To determine where each thread stack starts in memory, we can use the formula:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_Init_Val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE,\nwhere j is the thread index and satisfies:\nj ≤ (_Min_Stack_Size - MAIN_STACK_SIZE) / THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis calculation ensures each thread stack starts at a unique, properly aligned location in memory.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',2,1,'2025-06-27_01-25-31','Directly assigning the PC register to hold the address of print_continuously caused the processor to begin executing that function immediately. Since the Program Counter (PC) controls the flow of execution by always pointing to the next instruction, writing the function pointer into PC effectively jumped to the beginning of print_continuously. As a result, the processor began executing the loop within that function, which continuously outputs text over serial. This demonstrates that PC can be directly manipulated to control execution flow — a technique that’s key for thread launching and context switching, even though it’s not safe for use in real systems outside of controlled instructional environments.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',2,1,'2025-06-27_02-50-48','Directly assigning the PC register to hold the address of print_continuously caused the processor to begin executing that function immediately. Since the Program Counter (PC) controls the flow of execution by always pointing to the next instruction, writing the function pointer into PC effectively jumped to the beginning of print_continuously. As a result, the processor began executing the loop within that function, which continuously outputs text over serial. This demonstrates that PC can be directly manipulated to control execution flow — a technique that’s key for thread launching and context switching, even though it’s not safe for use in real systems outside of controlled instructional environments.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',2,2,'2025-06-27_01-25-31','A system call is a controlled entry point into kernel-level functionality that allows user-space programs to request services such as I/O operations, register access, or memory manipulation. It provides a safe, structured way to elevate privilege and interact with hardware or protected OS functions. In our lab, we implemented system calls using the SVC instruction, which triggers an exception that routes control to the SVC_Handler. Instead of directly writing complex assembly, we abstracted system call behavior by defining void functions in C that invoke SVC #<number>, each number mapping to a task in SVC_Handler_Main. This framework allowed us to perform operations like thread launching from user code without breaking the abstraction barrier between user and kernel mode.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',2,2,'2025-06-27_02-50-48','A system call is a controlled entry point into kernel-level functionality that allows user-space programs to request services such as I/O operations, register access, or memory manipulation. It provides a safe, structured way to elevate privilege and interact with hardware or protected OS functions. In our lab, we implemented system calls using the SVC instruction, which triggers an exception that routes control to the SVC_Handler. Instead of directly writing complex assembly, we abstracted system call behavior by defining void functions in C that invoke SVC #<number>, each number mapping to a task in SVC_Handler_Main. This framework allowed us to perform operations like thread launching from user code without breaking the abstraction barrier between user and kernel mode.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',2,3,'2025-06-27_01-25-31','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a memory region so that, when context is restored, the CPU behaves as if the thread had been running normally. For this lab, we created a stack by manually allocating memory below the initial MSP value. We then pushed the register values expected when our simple thread is resumed from interrupt into the R0-R12, xPSR, PC, and LR CPU resgisters. These values simulate the CPU’s state during execution. By storing them in the correct order, we enabled our system call handler to load these values back into the processor’s registers during context restoration. Without doing this, the CPU wouldn\'t know where to return, which arguments to pass, or what function to run. This setup is foundational for context switching and multithreading in any RTOS.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',2,3,'2025-06-27_02-50-48','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a memory region so that, when context is restored, the CPU behaves as if the thread had been running normally. For this lab, we created a stack by manually allocating memory below the initial MSP value. We then pushed the register values expected when our simple thread is resumed from interrupt into the R0-R12, xPSR, PC, and LR CPU resgisters. These values simulate the CPU’s state during execution. By storing them in the correct order, we enabled our system call handler to load these values back into the processor’s registers during context restoration. Without doing this, the CPU wouldn\'t know where to return, which arguments to pass, or what function to run. This setup is foundational for context switching and multithreading in any RTOS.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',2,4,'2025-06-27_01-25-31','The assembly instructions MRS R0, PSP followed by LDMIA R0!, {R4-R11} effectively load register values from the memory pointed to by the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). Specifically, MRS copies the PSP into R0, and LDMIA then loads the values from that memory into registers R4 through R11. This operation restores the context we manually injected into the thread\'s stack. If we do not set the PSP using __set_PSP before this operation, the PSP will contain an undefined or invalid value. As a result, LDMIA would attempt to read from an invalid memory region, causing a hard fault. Setting the PSP ensures it points to the correct stack frame we prepared, allowing the context restore to succeed without fault.\n',0,'s25'),('s3quadri',2,4,'2025-06-27_02-50-48','We set the PSP using __set_PSP so that the processor knows which stack to use when restoring the context of the new thread. By default, the Cortex-M4 uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is intended for system-level code and exception handling. However, in this lab, we manually created a thread stack in memory and intended to use the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to manage it.\nThe context restore in runFirstThread uses MRS R0, PSP followed by LDMIA R0!, {R4–R11} to load saved register values from the thread’s stack. If we don’t set the PSP beforehand, it still contains its default or uninitialized value, so the LDMIA instruction attempts to read from an invalid memory location. This is what causes the hard fault.\nUsing MSP in this situation would also lead to corruption, because the thread\'s context was not stored on the main stack, and we would be restoring from the wrong region. Setting PSP to the base of the thread\'s prepared stack ensures the CPU reads the correct context and continues execution safely. Overall, setting PSP aligns the processor with the memory layout we manually created for the thread, preventing mismatches between where the stack pointer is pointing and where valid context data actually resides, allowing the context restore to succeed without fault.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',2,5,'2025-06-27_01-25-31','If the thread function did not loop continuously and instead returned, control would flow back to the address stored in the link register (LR). In our context switch mechanism, LR is explicitly set to 0xFFFFFFFD in the runFirstThread assembly function. This is a special EXC_RETURN value used by ARM Cortex-M processors, which tells the CPU to return to Thread mode using the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) and to resume execution from the instruction following the system call that triggered the context switch.\nThis means that after the thread function returns, the processor would automatically exit exception context and resume executing the next instruction after the SVC that launched the thread — which, in our case, brings it back to the main function. Since the only code after execute_thread(); is an infinite loop, the program would simply enter that loop and remain idle. Rather than crashing, returning from the thread would simply cause the program to resume normal execution in main. However, since there is no thread scheduler or any post-thread handling logic yet, this behavior is somewhat unintended — a returned thread would not re-enter the RTOS context unless explicitly handled.\n',0,'s25'),('s3quadri',2,5,'2025-06-27_02-50-48','If the thread function returned instead of looping forever, the processor would attempt to pop a return address from the stack and branch to it, as is standard behavior for any C function return. However, the thread\'s stack was manually constructed, and while it includes register values such as xPSR, PC, and LR, it was not set up to support a normal function return. That means there is no valid return address on the stack for the thread function to use when it finishes executing.\nEven though LR was set to 0xFFFFFFFD in runFirstThread to trigger an exception return using the PSP, this works only once — during the initial transition into thread mode. If the thread later returns, the CPU will look for a return address in the thread’s stack, expecting the stack to contain a valid calling context (as would be present if the function had been called normally). Since we never pushed such a context, the CPU ends up trying to pop and branch to a garbage address or misaligned memory location, which causes a hard fault or undefined behavior.\nAlthough entering the thread works correctly, returning from it leads to a crash because the thread function was never meant to return. Our example implementation of context switching lacks the call frame needed for a clean exit. This illustrates why thread functions in RTOS environments are typically written to either loop forever or explicitly terminate by invoking a thread exit handler that can clean up or remove the thread from the scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',3,1,'2025-06-27_23-35-08','Each .c file in a C project is compiled independently by the compiler into an object file (.o). These object files contain machine code and symbol information but are not yet linked together. Once all source files are compiled, the linker takes over. It combines the object files, resolves symbol references across them (such as function calls or global variables defined in one file but used in another), and produces a single .elf executable. The .elf file contains all the compiled instructions and memory mappings needed to run on the target system. This separation between compiling and linking is what allows us to modularize our code into separate .c files for the kernel, user threads, and HAL drivers while still ending up with one complete program.\n',0,'s25'),('s3quadri',3,1,'2025-06-27_23-55-32','Each .c file in a C project is compiled independently by the compiler into an object file (.o). These object files contain machine code and symbol information but are not yet linked together. The build process for a C program happens in three major stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking.\nPreprocessing is the first step. During this stage, all preprocessor directives like #include and #define are handled. Header files are pasted into the source files, macros are expanded, and conditional compilation directives are evaluated. This step transforms the code into a single, translation-unit-ready file that the compiler can work with.\nCompiling is the second step. Each preprocessed .c file is translated into machine code and stored in a .o object file. These object files contain instructions and symbol references, but are not yet connected to each other.\nLinking is the final step. The linker combines all object files and resolves symbol references across them. For example, connecting a function call in one file to a definition in another. Once linking succeeds, the result is a complete .elf executable that can be loaded onto the target hardware.\nUnderstanding this sequence is critical because it explains why we can organize large projects into multiple .c files. Each file can be built separately, and the linker resolves the final structure of the program.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',3,2,'2025-06-27_23-35-08','Including a .c file using #include causes the preprocessor to paste the contents of that source file directly into the file doing the including. This bypasses the normal compile-link separation and results in multiple definitions of functions and variables across translation units. Since .c files contain function and variable definitions—not just declarations—this creates redundancy and linker errors when the project is built. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately, and only the corresponding .h files should be included, which provide the necessary declarations to resolve external symbols during linking without duplicating code.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',3,2,'2025-06-27_23-55-32','Using #include on a .c file is a bad idea because it causes the entire source file to be pasted into the including file before compilation. Since .c files contain function and variable definitions, not just declarations, this will lead to multiple definitions if the same .c file is included elsewhere. The compiler will allow it, but the linker will throw an error because it sees the same symbol defined more than once.\nThis breaks the standard build model, where each .c file is compiled into its own object file. Only header files (.h) should be included, because they contain declarations, not full implementations. This separation allows the linker to resolve external references safely and avoids code duplication. Misusing #include like this not only leads to technical errors but also makes the project harder to maintain and reason about.\nA similar issue can occur if header files themselves contain definitions (like non-static global variables). Including them across multiple files would cause the same linker conflict for the same reason.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',3,3,'2025-06-27_23-35-08','To set up and start a thread, the process looks like this from the user’s side:\nWrite your thread function in main.c. This function must match the format expected by the kernel (i.e., it takes a void* and returns nothing).\nCall osKernelInitialize() to set up internal kernel variables, like stack boundaries and thread metadata.\nCall osCreateThread() and pass it your thread function. The kernel will allocate a stack for the thread and prepare its context to run correctly.\nCall osKernelStart() to begin thread execution. This uses a system call to switch the CPU to thread mode and start running the first thread.\nIf everything has been initialized right with no errors (e.g) hitting the thread stack limit or some other edge case that can either be handled by our OS or cause a hard fault etc. The thread will begin running as expected.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',3,3,'2025-06-27_23-55-32','To set up and start a thread, the process looks like this from the user’s side:\nWrite a thread function that matches the required format that takes in a single void* argument and returns nothing.\nInitialize the kernel by calling a setup function to prepare internal variables, including those used to manage thread stacks.\nCreate a thread by passing the thread function into the thread creation API. If successful, this allocates stack space for the thread and sets up its context so the CPU can begin executing it.\nStart the kernel by calling the start function, which uses a system call to switch the processor into thread mode and begin running the first scheduled thread.\nEach of these steps abstracts away the low-level details, allowing the user to focus on writing application logic without directly manipulating registers or stack pointers. If everything has been initialized right with no errors (e.g) hitting the thread stack limit or some other edge case that can either be handled by our OS or cause a hard fault etc. The thread will begin running as expected.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',3,4,'2025-06-27_23-35-08','The stack pool is a reserved region of memory, carved out of the system RAM, specifically designated for thread stacks. It acts as a managed memory space from which each thread receives a private, fixed-size chunk to store its local variables and CPU context during execution. In Lab 3, we set up a mechanism that allocates these chunks sequentially, subtracting from the initial MSP downward. In future labs, this same pool will enable multiple threads to coexist by giving each one a dedicated stack region. By tracking how much of the stack pool has been used and aligning each allocation on 8-byte boundaries, the kernel can avoid overlap between stacks, prevent corruption, and support context switching between threads reliably.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',3,4,'2025-06-27_23-55-32','The stack pool is a reserved region of RAM dedicated to holding the stacks for all threads created by the OS. Each thread needs its own stack space to store local variables and register values during context switches. In Lab 3, the stack pool is allocated by subtracting stack blocks from the initial MSP location, moving downward in memory. In future labs, the stack pool enables multiple threads to exist simultaneously, since the kernel can allocate a private stack for each thread from this pool. As new threads are created, the kernel tracks the next available space and ensures that each allocation respects alignment constraints and avoids overlapping memory.\nIt’s also worth noting that while each thread gets its own stack from the pool, the kernel itself may continue to use the MSP. This distinction ensures that system-level operations remain isolated from thread-level execution. Proper management of the pool, including tracking how much space is used is critical. If the kernel loses track of allocations or overlaps stacks, it could cause corruption or hard faults that are difficult to debug.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',3,5,'2025-06-27_23-35-08','The \"user\" refers to the application-level code that defines thread functions and uses kernel services, while the \"kernel\" manages privileged operations like stack management, scheduling, and context switching. In terms of file organization, main.c holds the user code and thread logic, while kernel.c and kernel.h encapsulate the kernel\'s internal implementation and public interface, respectively. This separation ensures that user code can request services from the kernel without directly manipulating low-level resources like stack pointers or registers. From the user’s perspective, this is important because it allows threads to be written safely and portably without needing to understand or access the hardware-specific logic handled by the kernel. It also lays the groundwork for enforcing privilege boundaries in a real RTOS.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',3,5,'2025-06-27_23-55-32','The “user” refers to code that is application-level. The threads and logic written by the programmer to solve a specific task. This code belongs in main.c, and it interacts with the system by calling exposed kernel functions like osCreateThread. The “kernel” is responsible for handling privileged and hardware-specific operations like stack allocation, context setup, and thread launching. These are defined internally in kernel.c and exposed to the user via declarations in kernel.h.\nThis separation is important because it provides structure, safety, and modularity. From the user’s perspective, it means they don’t need to manage CPU state or memory directly, they only need to follow the kernel\'s API. It also improves portability, since the kernel can be reused across projects while user code stays focused on the application logic.\nIn real systems, this separation is enforced by hardware and privilege levels, but in this lab environment, we enforce it by keeping internal mechanisms private to the kernel. This separation also helps with debugging, since a clean interface means fewer cross-cutting changes and more predictable behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',4,1,'2025-07-31_11-29-10','Implementing a queue to store thread metadata is more interesting than using an array because a queue is FIFO (First In, First Out), which aligns well with round-robin scheduling where threads are run in the order they were created. In a regular queue structure, round-robin could be implemented by enqueueing threads as they are initialized and rotating them by dequeuing and re-enqueueing after each run. However, this becomes much cleaner with a circular queue: each thread simply points to the next, and osSched advances using current_thread = current_thread->next. This avoids even minor overhead from using array-based modulo arithmetic like currentThread = (currentThread + 1) % numThreadsRunning, making the implementation slightly more efficient. Circular queues also support dynamic memory allocation, allowing threads to be added without fixed size limits, which improves flexibility and memory efficiency in constrained systems.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',4,2,'2025-07-31_11-29-10','PendSV_Handler is a low-level mechanism that performs the actual thread context switch. It starts by grabbing the current process stack pointer (PSP) into R0 and manually pushes the callee-saved registers R4–R11 onto that thread’s stack in the line (STMDB R0!, {R4-R11}), preserving its execution state. It then calls osSched to schedule the next thread to run, (that’s simply implemented using current_thread = current_thread->next using a circular queue or currentThread = (currentThread + 1) % numThreadsRunning in the thread array implementation). After osSched updates which thread is current, the handler reloads the new PSP into R0, sets LR to the special exception return value (0xFFFFFFFD) to indicate returning to Thread mode using PSP, restores R4–R11 from the new thread’s stack in (LDMIA R0!, {R4-R11}), writes the updated stack pointer back to PSP, and branches to LR. That sequence transparently switches the CPU’s visible context from the old thread to the new one, relying on the pre-built stack frame (xPSR/PC/etc.) and the saved registers to resume execution as if the new thread had been running all along.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',4,3,'2025-07-31_11-29-10','This modification would involve defining our thread structure with a developer-assigned priority field and scheduling strictly by “highest priority runs first.” While this aligns with the naive Highest-Priority-Only scheduling model discussed in lectures, it deviates from common RTOS practice where priorities are often combined with time slicing or aging mechanisms to maintain fairness. Although easy to implement, always selecting the highest-priority ready thread leads to starvation. If high-priority tasks are constantly runnable, lower-priority threads may never execute. It also risks priority inversion, where a low-priority thread holding a resource blocks a medium-priority thread, and a high-priority thread preempts both, indirectly delaying its own progress. Without techniques such as priority inheritance, aging, or round-robin time slicing, the system loses fairness and predictability. In practice, this means real-time guarantees for lower-priority tasks collapse, and overall system responsiveness suffers despite having “priority” metadata. Modern schedulers therefore combine priority levels with mechanisms like round-robin queues or dynamic boosting to avoid these pitfalls.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',4,4,'2025-07-31_11-29-10','Calling into C keeps most of the scheduler logic portable, readable, and maintainable. High-level policies (e.g., advancing the circular queue, updating metadata, handling state) are easier and safer to express in C than in dense assembly, reducing bugs and speeding iteration. It also allows the assembly stub to remain minimal, just preserving/restoring registers and delegating policy to C, so that the boundary between fast context switch glue and flexible scheduling logic is clear. Debugging, extending (e.g., adding timeslices or new thread fields), and reasoning about correctness are far simpler in C.\nOn the other hand, crossing from assembly into C incurs calling convention overhead: pushing arguments, potential additional register saves, and slight latency compared to an entirely inlined assembly routine. It also requires careful coordination about ABI and stack alignment to avoid subtle bugs in privilege/exception return semantics. In extremely constrained or ultra-low-latency systems, a fully hand-crafted assembly scheduler might shave off cycles, but for this lab-level RTOS, the clarity and flexibility of splitting core context switch glue in assembly and policy in C overwhelmingly outweigh that cost.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',4,5,'2025-07-31_11-29-10','Yes, the OS will still work, but its behavior simplifies. With only one thread in the circular queue, osSched will repeatedly select that same thread (currentThread = (currentThread + 1) % numThreadsRunning is itself), and the context switch becomes effectively useless while redundantly saving and restoring its original state. Each time the thread yields, PendSV_Handler still runs, saves its registers, calls osSched (which leaves current_thread unchanged), and restores its state so execution continues. We would see the expected output which is the same as previous labs where print_continously outputs forever. This implementation would interleave some scheduler overhead that wouldn’t necessarily be noticeable to the user. There would be no real multitasking since there’s nothing else to switch to. This is a valid degenerate case and is why the original single-thread lab used a single global thread struct without a scheduler; the infrastructure generalizes gracefully from one to many via the circular queue.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',5,1,'2025-07-31_16-56-23','From the user’s perspective, passing an argument to a thread means providing a void* pointer as the second parameter of osCreateThread or osCreateThreadWithDeadline. This can be a pointer to a variable, a heap-allocated object, or a struct containing multiple fields. (e.g., int num = 121; osCreateThread (thread3, &num); where the pointer to the integer num is passed into osCreateThread).\nFrom the OS perspective, during thread creation we must store that pointer in R0 in the thread’s initial exception frame on its stack. This is because, by the ARM Cortex-M calling convention (AAPCS), the first argument to a function is always passed in R0. When the thread is first scheduled, the CPU returns from the PendSV exception using that stack frame, automatically loading R0 with the user’s argument. This works because the hardware’s exception return sequence restores registers from the prepared stack frame.\nWhen the thread function executes for the first time, the args parameter in void threadFuncX(void* args) directly reflects the value in R0, which the OS set to the pointer the user provided when calling osCreateThread. The thread function’s internal implementation either ignores this pointer or directly casts the void type argument for use as its expected type. Any additional user data can be accessed by dereferencing that pointer. This is an excellent, lean and generalized way of passing smaller and large sets of user data as arguments into various thread functions.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',5,2,'2025-07-31_16-56-23','I initially considered calling osYield inside SysTick_Handler since it seems like a lean way to trigger a context switch when a thread’s runtime reaches zero. It performs a similar function of initiating a context switch under the right conditions.\nHowever, osYield works by setting the PendSV bit in the ICSR to request a context switch, which is only safe to do from Thread Mode. Calling it inside PendSV_Handler, SysTick_Handler, or SVC_Handler_Main is unsafe because these functions are already running in exception context, and PendSV may already be active or pending. Triggering a yield at that moment risks re-entrant scheduling or stack corruption, as the OS is in the middle of saving/restoring PSP and callee-saved registers. At best, the call would be redundant; at worst, it could lead to a nested exception return on the same PSP frame, causing HardFaults or corrupted scheduler state. For this reason, osYield is only safe to call from a running thread, not from interrupt handlers already managing context switches.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',5,3,'2025-07-31_16-56-23','Modifying the HAL to reduce the fundamental OS clock interval from 1 ms to 0.1 ms provides finer timing granularity for pre-emptive scheduling. Threads can be switched more frequently, reducing worst-case response time for high-priority or latency-sensitive tasks, and software timers, delays, and timeouts gain higher resolution. This is especially useful for high-speed hardware interactions or control loops that need microsecond-level responsiveness rather than millisecond-level.\nOn the downside, SysTick will now fire 10× more frequently, increasing interrupt overhead and consuming more CPU cycles, which can reduce effective throughput for real work. Frequent wakeups also increase power consumption in the Nucleo or similar embedded targets. Higher context-switch frequency means more time spent saving and restoring registers in the round-robin scheduler or circular queue instead of executing useful work. Additionally, shorter ticks can introduce visible jitter in I/O-heavy functions like printf or UART if preemption occurs mid-transmission.\nFinally, modifying the fundamental clock affects HAL timing functions (like HAL_Delay or HAL_IncTick), which are based on the SysTick tick rate. Reducing the tick period without adjusting their scaling would make standard delays 10× shorter than expected, breaking timing assumptions elsewhere in the firmware. In embedded RTOS design, 1 ms is a common compromise between responsiveness, scheduler overhead, and HAL function reliability.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',5,4,'2025-07-31_16-56-23','From the user’s perspective:\nCo-operative multitasking requires threads to explicitly yield (call osYield) after fully running for a defined number of iterations to allow other threads to run. If one thread never yields, it can block all others. While pre-emptive multitasking occurs automatically: the OS timer interrupts (via SysTick) force a context switch after a timeslice expires, so threads that can run without manually yielding.\nFrom the OS perspective:\nWith cooperative multitasking, the scheduler is only invoked on voluntary yields. Simpler, less frequent context switches, but relies on thread discipline. Pre-emptive multitasking sees that the OS must handle timer interrupts, decrement runtime counters, and trigger PendSV for context switches, ensuring no single thread can monopolize the CPU for longer than its allowed timeslice.\n',1,'s25'),('s3quadri',5,5,'2025-07-31_16-56-23','Yielding or cooperative multitasking requires that a running thread voluntarily calls osYield(), resetting its runtime to timeslice and immediately moving the scheduler to the next thread.\nIn Pre-emption or time based multitasking, SysTick_Handler decrements the current thread’s runtime every time the Sys_Tick interrupt is triggered (every milli-second of the Nucleo’s clock time), and when it reaches zero, a PendSV is triggered to switch to the next thread automatically.\nThey work together because the thread state and PSP frame management are unified. Whether the switch is voluntary or forced, the OS saves R4–R11, updates the current_thread pointer to the next node in the circular queue, and restores the new thread’s context. This allows high responsiveness under pre-emption while still letting threads yield early to improve system throughput or responsiveness. Importantly, the two mechanisms are complementary because they operate on the same scheduling framework / thread function types (the circular queue of TCBs) but are triggered differently: All threads can decide to yield on their own through cooperative multitasking, otherwise pre-emptive multitasking would still switch tasks based on the default or an assigned time slice.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',1,1,'2025-05-14_10-26-46','The HAL is the Hardware Abstract Layer, a software component generally understood to be an interface that one can use between hardware and an operating system, in short, it makes it easy to access hardware. The HAL is used in a general embedded project because of these properties, simply because it provides a layer of separation between the software and hardware portions of a project, making it easier to access hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',1,1,'2025-05-14_20-35-12','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer, a software component, code written by ST Microelectronics, generally understood to be an interface that one can use between hardware and an operating system, in short, it makes it easy to access hardware by abstracting away specific details of the hardware from the software running on top of it. The HAL is used in a general embedded project because of these properties, simply because it provides a layer of separation between the software and hardware portions of a project, making it easier to access hardware. Using a Hardware Abstraction Layer holds several benefits, including allowing software to interact with different hardware without needing specific details, enabling parallel development of hardware and software, allowing virtualization, and makes software more portable and less hardware dependant. This allows developers to use one set of code across different platforms, reducing development time and promoting code reuse, reducing the new to rewrite code per device.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',1,2,'2025-05-14_10-26-46','__io_putchar is a function that writes a single character into an appropriate IO channel. For our purposes, this was needed as it writes to the UART2 IO channel and returns the character that was printed, enabling us to use the printf function.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',1,2,'2025-05-14_20-35-12','__io_putchar is a function that writes a single character into an appropriate IO channel, it acts as an interface between standard C library functions and the specific hardware platform’s output mechanism. For our purposes, this was needed as it writes to the UART2 IO channel and returns the character that was printed, enabling us to use the printf function. Furthermore, the __io_putchar function is actually necessary. The printf function and other standard C library functions rely on low-level out functions to write characters to output streams, however on embedded systems there isn’t always a standard console to output to. As such, __io_putchar provides an interface for the standard library functions to write characters to UART, allowing us to see debugging info or apply outputs. By providing this interface, it allows developers to also write code that’s independent of hardware or operating systems, lower complexity and making it easier to port code between different hardware platforms. Moreover, for programming flexibility itself, having this function allows C library functions to be directed to various output devices without modification to the function code themselves.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',1,3,'2025-05-14_10-26-46','Using the debugger allowed us to directly view the actual value of the stack as the program runs, allowing a deeper analysis of the code as we execute. If we had used printf instead, we would be unable to view the values of the stack as the program runs since the two cannot perfectly simultaneously occur, inducing potential error or inaccuracies in our assessment of the code.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',1,3,'2025-05-14_20-35-12','Using the debugger allowed us to directly view the actual values and registers of the stack as the program runs, allowing a deeper analysis of the code as we execute which is crucial for debugging. To add, the registers are able to show us immediate in-moment states of the CPU processing while the stack provides context to the flow of functions and local data management. If we had used printf instead, we would be unable to view the values of the stack as the program runs since the two cannot perfectly simultaneously occur, inducing potential error or inaccuracies in our assessment of the code. As printf is a sequential operation, the CPU must spend time transmitting each characters which takes time relative to the constraints of a real-time system, reducing or blocking the system until transmitted which can cause real-time tasks to miss deadlines and potentially lead to system failures. Moreover, the printf function, as apart of the standard C library, would pull in a substantial overhead to memory-constrained Cortex-M4 systems as it uses a buffer on the stack for formatting the output string, reducing available space for code and data.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',1,4,'2025-05-14_10-26-46','Assuming we have an obtained value for the MSP_init_val, the way we then got a new value for the PSP_val was by subtracting 0x400 since that was the size of our main stack. The reason we needed to know the location of the MSP for this to work was that, without the MSP_init_val, we wouldn’t have a reference point in the address to then subtract the size of the main stack in order to find the PSP_val.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',1,4,'2025-05-14_20-35-12','Assuming we have an obtained value for the MSP_init_val, the way we then got a new value for the PSP_val was by subtracting 0x400 since that was the size of our main stack. The reason we needed to know the location of the MSP for this to work was that, without the MSP_init_val, we wouldn’t have a reference point in the address to then subtract the size of the main stack in order to find the PSP_val.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',1,5,'2025-05-14_10-26-46','In order to allocate a number of new stacks for use in multiple threads, assuming this number is known at compile time, we would need to change our code to also subtract the THREAD_STACK_SIZE multiplied by the respective thread number that we are looking for in the memory. For example, in addition to the code we wrote, for a Thread N Stack, we would also need to subtract in the address by the THREAD_STACK_SIZE multiplied by N to know where this stack starts in the memory, relative to the MSP.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',1,5,'2025-05-14_20-35-12','In order to allocate a number of new stacks for use in multiple threads, assuming this number is known at compile time, we would need to change our code to also subtract the THREAD_STACK_SIZE multiplied by the respective thread number that we are looking for in the memory. Moreover, to ensure that the stack address is valid in the target architecture, we would have to ensure that it falls within the stack’s allocated memory, is aligned to the proper byte multiple, and grows in the proper direction as required by the architecture. For example, in addition to the code we wrote, for a Thread N Stack, we would also need to subtract in the address by the THREAD_STACK_SIZE multiplied by N to know where this stack starts in the memory, relative to the MSP. To verify that the stack addresses don’t fall outside the allocated memory we can check that the N stack plus its size doesn’t exceed the stack end. Plus, to ensure stack alignment, for example for a stack that requires 4-byte alignment, we can allocate (N/4)*4 bytes and ensure that each item pushed is a multiple of the required alignment. Finally, being mindful of overlapping stacks, we can prevent this by statically allocating non-overlapping regions, as mentioned above, since the THREAD_STACK_SIZE ensures that enough space is allocated per Thread N Stack, there should be no overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,1,'2025-06-10_21-10-15','When I changed PC to hold the function point to my print continuously function, I was providing an input. From what we’ve learned in class, this argument goes into R0. The assembly code then moved the value stored in R0 into PC. As soon as that happened, PC stored the location of this function and proceeded to output \"Hello PC!\" in an infinite loop as expected.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,1,'2025-06-12_12-38-18','When I changed PC to hold the function point to my print continuously function, I was providing an input. From what we’ve learned in class, this argument goes into R0. The assembly code then moved the value stored in R0 into PC. As soon as that happened, PC stored the location of this function and proceeded to output \"Hello PC!\" in an infinite loop as expected.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',2,1,'2025-06-12_13-24-34','When I changed PC to hold the function point to my print continuously function, I was providing an input. From what we’ve learned in class, this argument goes into R0. The assembly code then moved the value stored in R0 into PC. As soon as that happened, PC stored the location of this function and proceeded to output \"Hello PC!\" in an infinite loop as expected.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,1,'2025-06-12_13-37-42','When I changed PC to hold the function point to my print continuously function, I was providing an input. From what we’ve learned in class, this argument goes into R0. The assembly code then moved the value stored in R0 into PC. As soon as that happened, PC stored the location of this function and proceeded to output \"Hello PC!\" in an infinite loop as expected.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',2,1,'2025-06-12_15-11-13','When I changed PC to hold the function point to my print continuously function, I was providing an input. From what we’ve learned in class, this argument goes into R0. The assembly code then moved the value stored in R0 into PC. As soon as that happened, PC stored the location of this function and proceeded to output \"Hello PC!\" in an infinite loop as expected. Doing this relates to the idea of a function call as it involves involving a subroutine that performs a specific task, whilst jumping with the function entry point into the PC. There is also significance in the CPU executing instructions at the new address as it signifies a transfer of control, modularity, abstraction, stack and context usage, and even handling that occurs within program flow.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,2,'2025-06-10_21-10-15','A system call is a software-generated interrupt that is intentionally generated by a user program request in which the Operating System, OS, takes over in order to perform a task that requires privileged access or kernel-level functionality. This allows a user-mode program to interact with system resources such as files, devices, and other processes.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',2,2,'2025-06-12_12-38-18','A system call is a software-generated interrupt that is intentionally generated by a user program request in which the Operating System, OS, takes over in order to perform a task that requires privileged access or kernel-level functionality. This allows a user-mode program to interact with system resources such as files, devices, and other processes. In terms of its role in program structure, it is a gateway operation that through which applications ask the OS to perform operations that they can not that include resource management, process management, and system information. Moreover, it provides a transition from user mode to kernel mode, and it forms a crucial abstraction layer that shields used program from complexity and hardware specific details of the system. System calls are also synchronous in that they wait for the kernel to complete operation before returning control to the user program. It is important to note that system calls enforce security boundaries, as they are the only way that user mode code accesses and manipulates system resources. Without this protection, malicious programs could access or corrupt other programs, programs would not be isolated from one another, the kernel memory and critical data structures would be at risk to manipulation, and programs could bypass system-wide policies.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,2,'2025-06-12_13-24-34','A system call is a software-generated interrupt that is intentionally generated by a user program request in which the Operating System, OS, takes over in order to perform a task that requires privileged access or kernel-level functionality. This allows a user-mode program to interact with system resources such as files, devices, and other processes. In terms of its role in program structure, it is a gateway operation that through which applications ask the OS to perform operations that they can not that include resource management, process management, and system information. Moreover, it provides a transition from user mode to kernel mode, and it forms a crucial abstraction layer that shields used program from complexity and hardware specific details of the system. System calls are also synchronous in that they wait for the kernel to complete operation before returning control to the user program. It is important to note that system calls enforce security boundaries, as they are the only way that user mode code accesses and manipulates system resources. Without this protection, malicious programs could access or corrupt other programs, programs would not be isolated from one another, the kernel memory and critical data structures would be at risk to manipulation, and programs could bypass system-wide policies.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',2,2,'2025-06-12_13-37-42','A system call is a software-generated interrupt that is intentionally generated by a user program request in which the Operating System, OS, takes over in order to perform a task that requires privileged access or kernel-level functionality. This allows a user-mode program to interact with system resources such as files, devices, and other processes. In terms of its role in program structure, it is a gateway operation that through which applications ask the OS to perform operations that they can not that include resource management, process management, and system information. Moreover, it provides a transition from user mode to kernel mode, and it forms a crucial abstraction layer that shields used program from complexity and hardware specific details of the system. System calls are also synchronous in that they wait for the kernel to complete operation before returning control to the user program. It is important to note that system calls enforce security boundaries, as they are the only way that user mode code accesses and manipulates system resources. Without this protection, malicious programs could access or corrupt other programs, programs would not be isolated from one another, the kernel memory and critical data structures would be at risk to manipulation, and programs could bypass system-wide policies.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',2,2,'2025-06-12_15-11-13','A system call is a software-generated interrupt, or function call, that is intentionally generated by a user program request in which the Operating System, OS, takes over in order to perform a task that requires privileged access or kernel-level functionality. This allows a user-mode program to interact with system resources such as files, devices, and other processes. In terms of its role in program structure, it is a gateway operation that through which applications ask the OS to perform operations that they can not that include resource management, process management, and system information. Moreover, it provides a transition from user mode to kernel mode, and it forms a crucial abstraction layer that shields used program from complexity and hardware specific details of the system. System calls are also synchronous in that they wait for the kernel to complete operation before returning control to the user program. It is important to note that system calls enforce security boundaries, as they are the only way that user mode code accesses and manipulates system resources. Without this protection, malicious programs could access or corrupt other programs, programs would not be isolated from one another, the kernel memory and critical data structures would be at risk to manipulation, and programs could bypass system-wide policies. Within system calls, it’s important to note that the abstraction of a system call is that it is a function call that gives user mode programs access or a gateway to resources, while it’s implementation mainly involves a software interrupt that stores a context, calls the CPU, passes an argument, runs a program based on the interrupt, and then returns to user mode and the relevant context.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,3,'2025-06-10_21-10-15','The thread’s stack was set up by first getting a new stack pointer to use for the PSP and then setting up the thread’s stack. To do this, we used the code from Lab 1 to create a new stack pointer from the MSP’s original location. Once we have this, we decrement each time to grow the stack pointe downward, setting the values of xPSR and PC immediately before setting the rest of the values to arbitrary values to verify on the debugger. In the context of our lab, we needed to store the register values on the stack and so our goal was to set the register values on the stack as if the thread we want to run had been running the whole time and thus chip will proceed to run the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,3,'2025-06-12_12-38-18','The thread’s stack was set up by first getting a new stack pointer to use for the PSP and then setting up the thread’s stack. To do this, we used the code from Lab 1 to create a new stack pointer from the MSP’s original location. Once we have this, we decrement each time to grow the stack pointe downward, setting the values of xPSR and PC immediately before setting the rest of the values to arbitrary values to verify on the debugger. In the context of our lab, we needed to store the register values on the stack and so our goal was to set the register values on the stack as if the thread we want to run had been running the whole time and thus chip will proceed to run the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,3,'2025-06-12_13-24-34','The thread’s stack was set up by first getting a new stack pointer to use for the PSP and then setting up the thread’s stack. To do this, we used the code from Lab 1 to create a new stack pointer from the MSP’s original location. Once we have this, we decrement each time to grow the stack pointe downward, setting the values of xPSR and PC immediately before setting the rest of the values to arbitrary values to verify on the debugger. In the context of our lab, we needed to store the register values on the stack and so our goal was to set the register values on the stack as if the thread we want to run had been running the whole time and thus chip will proceed to run the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,3,'2025-06-12_13-37-42','The thread’s stack was set up by first getting a new stack pointer to use for the PSP and then setting up the thread’s stack. To do this, we used the code from Lab 1 to create a new stack pointer from the MSP’s original location. Once we have this, we decrement each time to grow the stack pointe downward, setting the values of xPSR and PC immediately before setting the rest of the values to arbitrary values to verify on the debugger. In the context of our lab, we needed to store the register values on the stack and so our goal was to set the register values on the stack as if the thread we want to run had been running the whole time and thus chip will proceed to run the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,3,'2025-06-12_15-11-13','The thread’s stack was set up by first getting a new stack pointer to use for the PSP and then setting up the thread’s stack. To do this, we used the code from Lab 1 to create a new stack pointer from the MSP’s original location. Once we have this, we decrement each time to grow the stack pointe downward, setting the values of xPSR and PC immediately before setting the rest of the values to arbitrary values to verify on the debugger. In the context of our lab, we needed to store the register values on the stack and so our goal was to set the register values on the stack as if the thread we want to run had been running the whole time and thus chip will proceed to run the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,4,'2025-06-10_21-10-15','This is because the SVC_Handler_Main is an exception handler that executes in privileged mode and uses the MSP, more importantly, after handling SVC it needs to be able to return to a user-mode. Without __set_PSP, we would be unable to return to the context of the active system before the interrupt occurred. __set_PSP would have stored the context that is active into the stack before the interrupt in order to switch to a kernel mode to handle the SVC, but without it, attempting to return to the previous context would result in the previous thread stack before the one that we are looking for or an invalid/uninitialized value. Either way, this would result in either an access violation and a Hard Fault or an invalid/old memory address/incorrect stack that would also result in a Hard Fault.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',2,4,'2025-06-12_12-38-18','This is because the SVC_Handler_Main is an exception handler that executes in privileged mode and uses the MSP, more importantly, after handling SVC it needs to be able to return to a user-mode. Since the thread’s stack is located at a different memory address than the CPU\'s default stack pointer (MSP), the PSP must be set to match the thread’s stack address. Without __set_PSP, we would be unable to return to the context of the active system before the interrupt occurred. __set_PSP would have stored the context that is active into the stack before the interrupt in order to switch to a kernel mode to handle the SVC, but without it, attempting to return to the previous context would result in the previous thread stack before the one that we are looking for or an invalid/uninitialized value. Either way, this would result in either an access violation and a Hard Fault or an invalid/old memory address/incorrect stack that would also result in a Hard Fault. Moreover, it is critical to note that MSP and PSP serve distinct purposes, in which MSP is privileged and considered the kernel stack while the PSP is used by user mode programs. Such a separation is necessary to maintain the access and privilege of different programs, makes the kernel robust and isolated, makes the context switching more efficient as it can use the PSP to save the context instead of the MSP, and it makes the program more stable overall. Adding on to issues that may occur if the PSP is not set correctly to the thread’s stack address, the Hard Fault that will arise is due to the fact that the CPU will attempt to read an invalid value if the PSP is not set correctly. This will be because the memory location is invalid.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,4,'2025-06-12_13-24-34','This is because the SVC_Handler_Main is an exception handler that executes in privileged mode and uses the MSP, more importantly, after handling SVC it needs to be able to return to a user-mode. Since the thread’s stack is located at a different memory address than the CPU\'s default stack pointer (MSP), the PSP must be set to match the thread’s stack address. Without __set_PSP, we would be unable to return to the context of the active system before the interrupt occurred. __set_PSP would have stored the context that is active into the stack before the interrupt in order to switch to a kernel mode to handle the SVC, but without it, attempting to return to the previous context would result in the previous thread stack before the one that we are looking for or an invalid/uninitialized value. Either way, this would result in either an access violation and a Hard Fault or an invalid/old memory address/incorrect stack that would also result in a Hard Fault. Moreover, it is critical to note that MSP and PSP serve distinct purposes, in which MSP is privileged and considered the kernel stack while the PSP is used by user mode programs. Such a separation is necessary to maintain the access and privilege of different programs, makes the kernel robust and isolated, makes the context switching more efficient as it can use the PSP to save the context instead of the MSP, and it makes the program more stable overall. Adding on to issues that may occur if the PSP is not set correctly to the thread’s stack address, the Hard Fault that will arise is due to the fact that the CPU will attempt to read an invalid value if the PSP is not set correctly. This will be because the memory location is invalid.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,4,'2025-06-12_13-37-42','This is because the SVC_Handler_Main is an exception handler that executes in privileged mode and uses the MSP, more importantly, after handling SVC it needs to be able to return to a user-mode. Since the thread’s stack is located at a different memory address than the CPU\'s default stack pointer (MSP), the PSP must be set to match the thread’s stack address. Without __set_PSP, we would be unable to return to the context of the active system before the interrupt occurred. __set_PSP would have stored the context that is active into the stack before the interrupt in order to switch to a kernel mode to handle the SVC, but without it, attempting to return to the previous context would result in the previous thread stack before the one that we are looking for or an invalid/uninitialized value. Either way, this would result in either an access violation and a Hard Fault or an invalid/old memory address/incorrect stack that would also result in a Hard Fault. Moreover, it is critical to note that MSP and PSP serve distinct purposes, in which MSP is privileged and considered the kernel stack while the PSP is used by user mode programs. Such a separation is necessary to maintain the access and privilege of different programs, makes the kernel robust and isolated, makes the context switching more efficient as it can use the PSP to save the context instead of the MSP, and it makes the program more stable overall. Adding on to issues that may occur if the PSP is not set correctly to the thread’s stack address, the Hard Fault that will arise is due to the fact that the CPU will attempt to read an invalid value if the PSP is not set correctly. This will be because the memory location is invalid.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,4,'2025-06-12_15-11-13','This is because the SVC_Handler_Main is an exception handler that executes in privileged mode and uses the MSP, more importantly, after handling SVC it needs to be able to return to a user-mode. Since the thread’s stack is located at a different memory address than the CPU\'s default stack pointer (MSP), the PSP must be set to match the thread’s stack address. Without __set_PSP, we would be unable to return to the context of the active system before the interrupt occurred. __set_PSP would have stored the context that is active into the stack before the interrupt in order to switch to a kernel mode to handle the SVC, but without it, attempting to return to the previous context would result in the previous thread stack before the one that we are looking for or an invalid/uninitialized value. Either way, this would result in either an access violation and a Hard Fault or an invalid/old memory address/incorrect stack that would also result in a Hard Fault. Moreover, it is critical to note that MSP and PSP serve distinct purposes, in which MSP is privileged and considered the kernel stack while the PSP is used by user mode programs. Such a separation is necessary to maintain the access and privilege of different programs, makes the kernel robust and isolated, makes the context switching more efficient as it can use the PSP to save the context instead of the MSP, and it makes the program more stable overall. Adding on to issues that may occur if the PSP is not set correctly to the thread’s stack address, the Hard Fault that will arise is due to the fact that the CPU will attempt to read an invalid value if the PSP is not set correctly. This will be because the memory location is invalid.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,5,'2025-06-10_21-10-15','Given that the thread was designed to loop continuously, we would likely see the system attempt to return to a previous context by popping it from the stack. However, given that we didn’t make provisions to handle this termination, this would likely result in a Hard Fault given that there shouldn’t be a valid context for the system to return to.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',2,5,'2025-06-12_12-38-18','Given that the thread was designed to loop continuously, we would likely see the system attempt to return to a previous context by popping it from the stack. However, given that we didn’t make provisions to handle this termination, this would likely result in a Hard Fault given that there shouldn’t be a valid context for the system to return to. This is an important point, given that if there isn’t a return address to return to from the thread, the program would get lost and by attempting to pop a value from the PSP that was never stored it would result in an invalid memory address. To add on to this, typically the return address is stored in the PSP, to make the context switching more efficient from the MSP. However, if this return address is missing, as mentioned before, the CPU will attempt to pop a garbage or arbitrary value that is invalid, resulting in a Hard Fault. Moreover, regarding consequences of missing or invalid return addresses, this could result in either a Hard Fault, as mentioned before, an Unpredictable Behaviour, if the return address coincidentally happens to be valid but the instruction was not intended for execution at that time, or even System Instability, the RTOS schedules won’t have the right context, resulting in an error or an unresponsive system as the CPU attempts to execute garbage.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,5,'2025-06-12_13-24-34','Given that the thread was designed to loop continuously, we would likely see the system attempt to return to a previous context by popping it from the stack. However, given that we didn’t make provisions to handle this termination, this would likely result in a Hard Fault given that there shouldn’t be a valid context for the system to return to. This is an important point, given that if there isn’t a return address to return to from the thread, the program would get lost and by attempting to pop a value from the PSP that was never stored it would result in an invalid memory address. To add on to this, typically the return address is stored in the PSP, to make the context switching more efficient from the MSP. However, if this return address is missing, as mentioned before, the CPU will attempt to pop a garbage or arbitrary value that is invalid, resulting in a Hard Fault. Moreover, regarding consequences of missing or invalid return addresses, this could result in either a Hard Fault, as mentioned before, an Unpredictable Behaviour, if the return address coincidentally happens to be valid but the instruction was not intended for execution at that time, or even System Instability, the RTOS schedules won’t have the right context, resulting in an error or an unresponsive system as the CPU attempts to execute garbage.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,5,'2025-06-12_13-37-42','Given that the thread was designed to loop continuously, we would likely see the system attempt to return to a previous context by popping it from the stack. However, given that we didn’t make provisions to handle this termination, this would likely result in a Hard Fault given that there shouldn’t be a valid context for the system to return to. This is an important point, given that if there isn’t a return address to return to from the thread, the program would get lost and by attempting to pop a value from the PSP that was never stored it would result in an invalid memory address. To add on to this, typically the return address is stored in the PSP, to make the context switching more efficient from the MSP. However, if this return address is missing, as mentioned before, the CPU will attempt to pop a garbage or arbitrary value that is invalid, resulting in a Hard Fault. Moreover, regarding consequences of missing or invalid return addresses, this could result in either a Hard Fault, as mentioned before, an Unpredictable Behaviour, if the return address coincidentally happens to be valid but the instruction was not intended for execution at that time, or even System Instability, the RTOS schedules won’t have the right context, resulting in an error or an unresponsive system as the CPU attempts to execute garbage.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',2,5,'2025-06-12_15-11-13','Given that the thread was designed to loop continuously, we would likely see the system attempt to return to a previous context by popping it from the stack. However, given that we didn’t make provisions to handle this termination, this would likely result in a Hard Fault given that there shouldn’t be a valid context for the system to return to. This is an important point, given that if there isn’t a return address to return to from the thread, the program would get lost and by attempting to pop a value from the PSP that was never stored it would result in an invalid memory address. To add on to this, typically the return address is stored in the PSP, to make the context switching more efficient from the MSP. However, if this return address is missing, as mentioned before, the CPU will attempt to pop a garbage or arbitrary value that is invalid, resulting in a Hard Fault. Moreover, regarding consequences of missing or invalid return addresses, this could result in either a Hard Fault, as mentioned before, an Unpredictable Behaviour, if the return address coincidentally happens to be valid but the instruction was not intended for execution at that time, or even System Instability, the RTOS schedules won’t have the right context, resulting in an error or an unresponsive system as the CPU attempts to execute garbage.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,1,'2025-06-25_18-39-33','The process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable is first by sending the *.c files into a preprocessor, then a compiler, then an assembler, before being processed as object files. At this point, the linker then combines the object files with various library objects before compiling everything into a single executable for execution.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',3,1,'2025-06-26_18-37-57','The process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable is a three-step process of preprocessing, compiling, and then linking everything into an executable. First, in the preprocessing, textual transformations are made on the source code before passing it to the compiler, this includes the macro expansion of replacing occurrences of defined macros with the their corresponding text replacement, the inclusion of specified header file content when specified in each file and merging sources to be filed into a single translation unit, and the conditional compilation of directives in tandem with defined macros to predetermine if conditions are met, resulting in a .i file type. Next, in the compiler the preprocessed source code is transformed into machine-independent intermediate code, a lower-level representation that a computer’s processer can eventually understand and execute. This step usually involves breaking down the code into a stream of tokens, checking them against the language’s grammar rules, checking for errors, translating into a intermediate representation, optimization, and then the generation of that code into assembly language instructions specific to the CPU architecture, usually in .s or .asm format or as object files. Finally, the linker takes the compiled object files and external libraries, resolves cross-references, and produces the final executable, creating a complete programs using the multiple object files.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,1,'2025-06-27_09-49-19','The process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable is a three-step process of preprocessing, compiling, and then linking everything into an executable. First, in the preprocessing, textual transformations are made on the source code before passing it to the compiler, this includes the macro expansion of replacing occurrences of defined macros with the their corresponding text replacement, the inclusion of specified header file content when specified in each file and merging sources to be filed into a single translation unit, and the conditional compilation of directives in tandem with defined macros to predetermine if conditions are met, resulting in a .i file type. Next, in the compiler the preprocessed source code is transformed into machine-independent intermediate code, a lower-level representation that a computer’s processer can eventually understand and execute. This step usually involves breaking down the code into a stream of tokens, checking them against the language’s grammar rules, checking for errors, translating into a intermediate representation, optimization, and then the generation of that code into assembly language instructions specific to the CPU architecture, usually in .s or .asm format or as object files. Finally, the linker takes the compiled object files and external libraries, resolves cross-references, and produces the final executable, creating a complete programs using the multiple object files.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,1,'2025-06-27_11-43-28','The process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable is a three-step process of preprocessing, compiling, and then linking everything into an executable. First, in the preprocessing, textual transformations are made on the source code before passing it to the compiler, this includes the macro expansion of replacing occurrences of defined macros with the their corresponding text replacement, the inclusion of specified header file content when specified in each file and merging sources to be filed into a single translation unit, and the conditional compilation of directives in tandem with defined macros to predetermine if conditions are met, resulting in a .i file type. Next, in the compiler the preprocessed source code is transformed into machine-independent intermediate code, a lower-level representation that a computer’s processer can eventually understand and execute. This step usually involves breaking down the code into a stream of tokens, checking them against the language’s grammar rules, checking for errors, translating into a intermediate representation, optimization, and then the generation of that code into assembly language instructions specific to the CPU architecture, usually in .s or .asm format or as object files. Finally, the linker takes the compiled object files and external libraries, resolves cross-references, and produces the final executable, creating a complete programs using the multiple object files.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,1,'2025-06-27_13-03-06','The process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable is a three-step process of preprocessing, compiling, and then linking everything into an executable. First, in the preprocessing, textual transformations are made on the source code before passing it to the compiler, this includes the macro expansion of replacing occurrences of defined macros with the their corresponding text replacement, the inclusion of specified header file content when specified in each file and merging sources to be filed into a single translation unit, and the conditional compilation of directives in tandem with defined macros to predetermine if conditions are met, resulting in a .i file type. Next, in the compiler the preprocessed source code is transformed into machine-independent intermediate code, a lower-level representation that a computer’s processer can eventually understand and execute. This step usually involves breaking down the code into a stream of tokens, checking them against the language’s grammar rules, checking for errors, translating into a intermediate representation, optimization, and then the generation of that code into assembly language instructions specific to the CPU architecture, usually in .s or .asm format or as object files. Finally, the linker takes the compiled object files and external libraries, resolves cross-references, and produces the final executable, creating a complete programs using the multiple object files.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,2,'2025-06-25_18-39-33','The #include directive cannot be used on a *.c file because it would give a file access to code that it may not necessarily should have access to. Moreover, with regards to the line #include <kernel.c> it would give access to the kernel code to any file regardless of hierarchy which is very dangerous, user applications or other code and functions in general should not have access to kernel level code. Without this level of abstraction between kernel and user modes there is no level of security or control over all parts of the code overall.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',3,2,'2025-06-26_18-37-57','First of all, it should be noted that the primary purpose of a #include directive in C is to incorporate the contest of once source file, usually a header file, directly into another source file at the point where the directive appears.  Towards the compilation process, the #include directive is handled by the preprocessor where it locates the specified files and preforms a textual substitution. The difference in doing this with a .c file as opposed to a .h file is that in terms of content and treatment, .h files only usually have declarations and are only ever preprocessed into .c files, the compiler does not actually compile the .h files into an object files. This is contrary to a .c file where each file is compiled independently by the compiler which then reads the preprocessed output and translates it to machine code along side their independent object file generation for each .c file. Thus, if a .c file has been used in a #include directive in another .c file, it will lead to a compilation or linking error. This is because doing so will copy the entirety of the .c file into the other during the preprocessing phase, and when the compilation phase is reached there will be multiple object files for each .c file instance that used the #include directive, which will each then have identical functions and variables across multiple object files. When the linker attempts to combine the object files into a program it will encounter multiple definitions for the exact same symbols. This will create a multiple definition error as it is the linker’s job to ensure that each function or global variable only has exactly one definition across all object files in the final program. Moreover, with regards to the line #include <kernel.c> it would give access to the kernel code to any file regardless of hierarchy which is very dangerous, user applications or other code and functions in general should not have access to kernel level code. Without this level of abstraction between kernel and user modes there is no level of security or control over all parts of the code overall.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,2,'2025-06-27_09-49-19','First of all, it should be noted that the primary purpose of a #include directive in C is to incorporate the contest of once source file, usually a header file, directly into another source file at the point where the directive appears.  Towards the compilation process, the #include directive is handled by the preprocessor where it locates the specified files and preforms a textual substitution. The difference in doing this with a .c file as opposed to a .h file is that in terms of content and treatment, .h files only usually have declarations and are only ever preprocessed into .c files, the compiler does not actually compile the .h files into an object files. This is contrary to a .c file where each file is compiled independently by the compiler which then reads the preprocessed output and translates it to machine code along side their independent object file generation for each .c file. Thus, if a .c file has been used in a #include directive in another .c file, it will lead to a compilation or linking error. This is because doing so will copy the entirety of the .c file into the other during the preprocessing phase, and when the compilation phase is reached there will be multiple object files for each .c file instance that used the #include directive, which will each then have identical functions and variables across multiple object files. When the linker attempts to combine the object files into a program it will encounter multiple definitions for the exact same symbols. This will create a multiple definition error as it is the linker’s job to ensure that each function or global variable only has exactly one definition across all object files in the final program. Moreover, with regards to the line #include <kernel.c> it would give access to the kernel code to any file regardless of hierarchy which is very dangerous, user applications or other code and functions in general should not have access to kernel level code. Without this level of abstraction between kernel and user modes there is no level of security or control over all parts of the code overall.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,2,'2025-06-27_11-43-28','First of all, it should be noted that the primary purpose of a #include directive in C is to incorporate the contest of once source file, usually a header file, directly into another source file at the point where the directive appears.  Towards the compilation process, the #include directive is handled by the preprocessor where it locates the specified files and preforms a textual substitution. The difference in doing this with a .c file as opposed to a .h file is that in terms of content and treatment, .h files only usually have declarations and are only ever preprocessed into .c files, the compiler does not actually compile the .h files into an object files. This is contrary to a .c file where each file is compiled independently by the compiler which then reads the preprocessed output and translates it to machine code along side their independent object file generation for each .c file. Thus, if a .c file has been used in a #include directiv