it to machine code along side their independent object file generation for each .c file. Thus, if a .c file has been used in a #include directive in another .c file, it will lead to a compilation or linking error. This is because doing so will copy the entirety of the .c file into the other during the preprocessing phase, and when the compilation phase is reached there will be multiple object files for each .c file instance that used the #include directive, which will each then have identical functions and variables across multiple object files. When the linker attempts to combine the object files into a program it will encounter multiple definitions for the exact same symbols. This will create a multiple definition error as it is the linker’s job to ensure that each function or global variable only has exactly one definition across all object files in the final program. Moreover, with regards to the line #include <kernel.c> it would give access to the kernel code to any file regardless of hierarchy which is very dangerous, user applications or other code and functions in general should not have access to kernel level code. Without this level of abstraction between kernel and user modes there is no level of security or control over all parts of the code overall.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,3,'2025-06-25_18-39-33','To set up a thread from the user’s perspective, one simply needs to make a function that the thread would run and then initialize the kernel, make a call to create the thread for the function the user has created, and then start the kernel to run the function as required through its created thread.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,3,'2025-06-26_18-37-57','To set up a thread from the user’s perspective, one simply needs to make a function that the thread would run and then initialize the kernel, make a call to create the thread for the function the user has created, and then start the kernel to run the function as required through its created thread.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,3,'2025-06-27_09-49-19','To set up a thread from the user’s perspective, one simply needs to make a function that the thread would run and then initialize the kernel, make a call to create the thread for the function the user has created, and then start the kernel to run the function as required through its created thread.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,3,'2025-06-27_11-43-28','To set up a thread from the user’s perspective, one simply needs to make a function that the thread would run and then initialize the kernel, make a call to create the thread for the function the user has created, and then start the kernel to run the function as required through its created thread.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,3,'2025-06-27_13-03-06','To set up a thread from the user’s perspective, one simply needs to make a function that the thread would run and then initialize the kernel, make a call to create the thread for the function the user has created, and then start the kernel to run the function as required through its created thread.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,4,'2025-06-25_18-39-33','The stack pool is for the arrangement of all memory that it to be used for the stack. In other words, the stack pool refers to all the memory available for the stack. In future labs, this would be useful as any future threads can have stacks allocated from this stack pool, which then enables the ability to have multiple threads each with their own stack to exist within our OS.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',3,4,'2025-06-26_18-37-57','The stack pool is a specific region in memory, or a contiguous pre-allocated region of RAM from which individual fixed-size stack segments are reserved for distinct threads. In future labs, this would be useful as this enables a multi-threaded environment and allows for the system’s memory management to fulfill relevant stack allocation requests. It should be further noted that the stack pool is subdivided into fixed-sized segments, this is important for thread allocation as it makes the allocation process more deterministic, the segments of stacks for each thread are isolated and protected from one another, and it simplifies the stack allocation and memory management system through a standardized memory block system.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,4,'2025-06-27_09-49-19','The stack pool is a specific region in memory, or a contiguous pre-allocated region of RAM from which individual fixed-size stack segments are reserved for distinct threads. In future labs, this would be useful as this enables a multi-threaded environment and allows for the system’s memory management to fulfill relevant stack allocation requests. It should be further noted that the stack pool is subdivided into fixed-sized segments, this is important for thread allocation as it makes the allocation process more deterministic, the segments of stacks for each thread are isolated and protected from one another, and it simplifies the stack allocation and memory management system through a standardized memory block system.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,4,'2025-06-27_11-43-28','The stack pool is a specific region in memory, or a contiguous pre-allocated region of RAM from which individual fixed-size stack segments are reserved for distinct threads. In future labs, this would be useful as this enables a multi-threaded environment and allows for the system’s memory management to fulfill relevant stack allocation requests. It should be further noted that the stack pool is subdivided into fixed-sized segments, this is important for thread allocation as it makes the allocation process more deterministic, the segments of stacks for each thread are isolated and protected from one another, and it simplifies the stack allocation and memory management system through a standardized memory block system.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,4,'2025-06-27_13-03-06','The stack pool is a specific region in memory, or a contiguous pre-allocated region of RAM from which individual fixed-size stack segments are reserved for distinct threads. In future labs, this would be useful as this enables a multi-threaded environment and allows for the system’s memory management to fulfill relevant stack allocation requests. It should be further noted that the stack pool is subdivided into fixed-sized segments, this is important for thread allocation as it makes the allocation process more deterministic, the segments of stacks for each thread are isolated and protected from one another, and it simplifies the stack allocation and memory management system through a standardized memory block system.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,5,'2025-06-25_18-39-33','The distinction between the user and the kernel in terms of what goes into which file is that user items often go into the main where they can place any function they want in order to accomplish any goal, but with calls to the kernel that would be required to fulfill and privileged requests that would require system resources or memory. The kernel, in comparison to the user, would be the most privileged code structure and code in general that would enable the user access to the resources it requests if it deems it as a valid request. In general, the distinction is important from the user perspective because it offers and abstract interface to interact with system resources that prevent misuse of said resources as regulated by the kernel mode.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',3,5,'2025-06-26_18-37-57','The distinction between the user and the kernel in terms of what goes into which file is that user items often go into the main where they can place any function they want in order to accomplish any goal, but with calls to the kernel that would be required to fulfill and privileged requests that would require system resources or memory. The kernel, in comparison to the user, would be the most privileged code structure and code in general that would enable the user access to the resources it requests if it deems it as a valid request. In general, the distinction is important from the user perspective because it offers and abstract interface to interact with system resources that prevent misuse of said resources as regulated by the kernel mode.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',3,5,'2025-06-27_09-49-19','The distinction between the user and the kernel in terms of what goes into which file is that user items often go into the main where they can place any function they want in order to accomplish any goal, but with calls to the kernel that would be required to fulfill and privileged requests that would require system resources or memory. The kernel, in comparison to the user, would be the most privileged code structure and code in general that would enable the user access to the resources it requests if it deems it as a valid request. In general, the distinction is important from the user perspective because it offers and abstract interface to interact with system resources that prevent misuse of said resources as regulated by the kernel mode.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',3,5,'2025-06-27_11-43-28','The distinction between the user and the kernel in terms of what goes into which file is that user items often go into the main where they can place any function they want in order to accomplish any goal, but with calls to the kernel that would be required to fulfill and privileged requests that would require system resources or memory. The kernel, in comparison to the user, would be the most privileged code structure and code in general that would enable the user access to the resources it requests if it deems it as a valid request. In general, the distinction and separation is important from the user perspective because it offers and abstract interface to interact with system resources that prevent misuse of said resources as regulated by the kernel mode.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',3,5,'2025-06-27_13-03-06','The distinction between the user and the kernel in terms of what goes into which file is that user items such as writing threads and their associated functions often go into the main where they can place any function they want in order to accomplish any goal, but with calls to the kernel that would be required to fulfill and privileged requests that would require system resources or memory. The kernel, in comparison to the user, would be the most privileged code structure and contains code that in general that would enable the user access to the resources it requests if it deems it as a valid request. In general, the distinction and separation of the user and kernel is important from the user perspective because it offers an abstract interface to interact with system resources that prevent misuse of said resources as regulated by the kernel mode. Moreover,  it simplifies their job and allows the user to focus on application logic rather than low-level system details related to the usage of the computer system resources. Expanding on the simplification for the user, this separation enables the user to focus on application logic and leaves the complication of hardware expertise to simplified system calls, offers protection from other programs by providing their own protected spaces and prevents code with bugs from corrupting the ore operating system, and enables better security for the user as this structure protects the operating system from malicious or faulty programs as well as enables policy enforcement from the kernel across all user programs.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,1,'2025-07-28_13-12-48','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling instead of just an array will place all TCBs into a ready queue, upon each scheduling decision the scheduler will take the TCB from the front of the ready queue and load the context of this thread upon execution. Upon the timer interrupt or the thread yielding the CPU, the TCB is saved and placed in the back of the ready queue, allowing the next TCB in the queue to run as required. In contrast with an array, this eliminates the need to have a pointer or index to the currently running thread in the array, logic to increment the index cyclically, and logic to skip threads that have blocked or terminated. The benefits of using the queue also include the fact that the logic required is a natural fit for FIFO and no index management is required, its very easy as well as dynamic to add and remove new threads, dequeue and enqueue is very seamless, the ready queue is very efficient an typically can be done in a O(1) operation time with no iteration, and it offers a flexible foundation for other scheduling techniques along with reduced code complexity.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',4,1,'2025-07-28_16-07-38','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling instead of just an array will place all TCBs into a ready queue, upon each scheduling decision the scheduler will take the TCB from the front of the ready queue and load the context of this thread upon execution. Upon the timer interrupt or the thread yielding the CPU, the TCB is saved and placed in the back of the ready queue, allowing the next TCB in the queue to run as required. In contrast with an array, this eliminates the need to have a pointer or index to the currently running thread in the array, logic to increment the index cyclically, and logic to skip threads that have blocked or terminated. The benefits of using the queue also include the fact that the logic required is a natural fit for FIFO and no index management is required, its very easy as well as dynamic to add and remove new threads, dequeue and enqueue is very seamless, the ready queue is very efficient an typically can be done in a O(1) operation time with no iteration, and it offers a flexible foundation for other scheduling techniques along with reduced code complexity. It should be noted that the current round-robin scheduler in the lab already uses a structure similar to a queue as it holds the threads in an array that is cyclically looped using the array, thus in a manner that is similar to a queue in terms of order. It should also be noted that the potential overheads of using a queue as opposed to an array are higher than an array since, in addition to the TCB itself, memory will be needed for each node to have a pointer each to the next and previous nodes. In terms of performance and complexity, this will increase the amount of RAM required, which in a memory-constrained system can be critical, as well as increase complexity, as the underlying data structure of a queue is more complex to implement than an array.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,1,'2025-07-28_16-17-26','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling instead of just an array will place all TCBs into a ready queue, upon each scheduling decision the scheduler will take the TCB from the front of the ready queue and load the context of this thread upon execution. Upon the timer interrupt or the thread yielding the CPU, the TCB is saved and placed in the back of the ready queue, allowing the next TCB in the queue to run as required. In contrast with an array, this eliminates the need to have a pointer or index to the currently running thread in the array, logic to increment the index cyclically, and logic to skip threads that have blocked or terminated. The benefits of using the queue also include the fact that the logic required is a natural fit for FIFO and no index management is required, its very easy as well as dynamic to add and remove new threads, dequeue and enqueue is very seamless, the ready queue is very efficient an typically can be done in a O(1) operation time with no iteration, and it offers a flexible foundation for other scheduling techniques along with reduced code complexity. It should be noted that the current round-robin scheduler in the lab already uses a structure similar to a queue as it holds the threads in an array that is cyclically looped using the array, thus in a manner that is similar to a queue in terms of order. It should also be noted that the potential overheads of using a queue as opposed to an array are higher than an array since, in addition to the TCB itself, memory will be needed for each node to have a pointer each to the next and previous nodes. In terms of performance and complexity, this will increase the amount of RAM required, which in a memory-constrained system can be critical, as well as increase complexity, as the underlying data structure of a queue is more complex to implement than an array.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,2,'2025-07-28_13-12-48','In order to switch from one thread to another the following will occur. First, the context switch is triggered by a request from the RTOS scheduler either from a timer interrupt, a thread yielding or blocking the CPU, or a higher-priority thread becoming ready to run. Upon this, the system enters a handler mode where then switches from the current thread’s stack PSP to the MSP, pushing the subset of CPU registers into the PSP. Afterwards, the remaining registers are saved and the old thread’s TCB is updated along with it’s PSP. Next, the next thread is chosen by the RTOS scheduler and the new thread’s context is loaded onto the CPU’s registers. Finally, the system exits from the handler mode to the thread mode where the new thread is now free to be executed as if nothing ever happened.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',4,2,'2025-07-28_16-07-38','In order to switch from one thread to another the following will occur. First, the PSP must be read to locate the thread’s stack, this is important as it is the anchor point for saving the rest of the current thread’s context and to restore it later as well. Thus, the context switch is triggered by a request from the RTOS scheduler either from a timer interrupt, a thread yielding or blocking the CPU, or a higher-priority thread becoming ready to run. Upon this, the system enters a handler mode where then switches from the current thread’s stack PSP to the MSP, pushing the subset of CPU registers, R4-R11, into the thread’s own PSP. Naming these registers specifically is important in an embedded systems context as it is important when dealing with direct hardware interaction, architecture specific knowledge, understanding the ABI, and debugging. Afterwards, the remaining registers are saved and the old thread’s TCB is updated along with it’s PSP. Next, the next thread is chosen by the function osSched and the new thread’s context is loaded onto the CPU’s registers, R4-R11, by being popped from the stack. Finally, the system exits from the handler mode to the thread mode, switching back to the PSP, where the new thread is now free to be executed as if nothing ever happened.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,2,'2025-07-28_16-17-26','In order to switch from one thread to another the following will occur. First, the PSP must be read to locate the thread’s stack, this is important as it is the anchor point for saving the rest of the current thread’s context and to restore it later as well. Thus, the context switch is triggered by a request from the RTOS scheduler either from a timer interrupt, a thread yielding or blocking the CPU, or a higher-priority thread becoming ready to run. Upon this, the system enters a handler mode where then switches from the current thread’s stack PSP to the MSP, pushing the subset of CPU registers, R4-R11, into the thread’s own PSP. Naming these registers specifically is important in an embedded systems context as it is important when dealing with direct hardware interaction, architecture specific knowledge, understanding the ABI, and debugging. Afterwards, the remaining registers are saved and the old thread’s TCB is updated along with it’s PSP. Next, the next thread is chosen by the function osSched and the new thread’s context is loaded onto the CPU’s registers, R4-R11, by being popped from the stack. Finally, the system exits from the handler mode to the thread mode, switching back to the PSP, where the new thread is now free to be executed as if nothing ever happened.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,3,'2025-07-28_13-12-48','This is indeed a bad idea, one of the first reasons that comes to mind is that of starvation, if a continuous stream of high-priority tasks is gained or a single high-priority task never finishes, and is constantly in ready stated, then lower-priority tasks never get a chance to execute and are starved of CPU time. Even if something as low level as a background maintenance task or a sensor data check ever gets run, then it could lead to data loss or system degradation over time. Another issue with this idea is the lack of fairness, such a strict priority scheduling system inherently sacrifices fairness for responsiveness to higher priorities. While this is sometimes acceptable for critical tasks, it means that low-priority tasks will make very little progress. One other issue is also that of complexity of priority assignment, determining the correct priority for every task is very difficult and is these priorities are assigned incorrectly, it could result in starvation of background processes or bottlenecks for system tuning and maintenance. Finally, another issue is that of priority inversion, which is a major real-time problem, where a high-priority task relying on information from a low-priority task, may never run due to another task with higher priority than the low priority task, thus inverting the priorities. This is a huge problem that would lead to unpredictable delays, missed timelines, system instability, or even system crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',4,3,'2025-07-28_16-07-38','This is indeed a bad idea, one of the first reasons that comes to mind is that of starvation, if a continuous stream of high-priority tasks is gained or a single high-priority task never finishes, and is constantly in ready stated, then lower-priority tasks never get a chance to execute and are starved of CPU time. Even if something as low level as a background maintenance task or a sensor data check ever gets run, then it could lead to data loss or system degradation over time. As a direct results of the scheduler’s design, only one task with the highest priority would ever be scheduled to run. Moreover, the primary behaviour of such a system would be that of strict pre-emptive prioritization, in which is the highest priority task is always selected, even when other tasks are ready to run, these other tasks would be starved of CPU time and thus not be allowed to run due to a lack of priority. Another issue with this idea is the lack of fairness, such a strict priority scheduling system inherently sacrifices fairness for responsiveness to higher priorities. While this is sometimes acceptable for critical tasks, it means that low-priority tasks will make very little progress. One other issue is also that of complexity of priority assignment, determining the correct priority for every task is very difficult and is these priorities are assigned incorrectly, it could result in starvation of background processes or bottlenecks for system tuning and maintenance. Finally, another issue is that of priority inversion, which is a major real-time problem, where a high-priority task relying on information from a low-priority task, may never run due to another task with higher priority than the low priority task, thus inverting the priorities. This is a huge problem that would lead to unpredictable delays, missed timelines, system instability, or even system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,3,'2025-07-28_16-17-26','This is indeed a bad idea, one of the first reasons that comes to mind is that of starvation, if a continuous stream of high-priority tasks is gained or a single high-priority task never finishes, and is constantly in ready stated, then lower-priority tasks never get a chance to execute and are starved of CPU time. Even if something as low level as a background maintenance task or a sensor data check ever gets run, then it could lead to data loss or system degradation over time. As a direct results of the scheduler’s design, only one task with the highest priority would ever be scheduled to run. Moreover, the primary behaviour of such a system would be that of strict pre-emptive prioritization, in which is the highest priority task is always selected, even when other tasks are ready to run, these other tasks would be starved of CPU time and thus not be allowed to run due to a lack of priority. Another issue with this idea is the lack of fairness, such a strict priority scheduling system inherently sacrifices fairness for responsiveness to higher priorities. While this is sometimes acceptable for critical tasks, it means that low-priority tasks will make very little progress. One other issue is also that of complexity of priority assignment, determining the correct priority for every task is very difficult and is these priorities are assigned incorrectly, it could result in starvation of background processes or bottlenecks for system tuning and maintenance. Finally, another issue is that of priority inversion, which is a major real-time problem, where a high-priority task relying on information from a low-priority task, may never run due to another task with higher priority than the low priority task, thus inverting the priorities. This is a huge problem that would lead to unpredictable delays, missed timelines, system instability, or even system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,4,'2025-07-28_13-12-48','There are certainly some benefits to calling the C functions compared to writing them in assembly. For one, it assists with readability and maintainability, allows the code to be more expressive and understandable while also reducing development time. In addition, there is the benefit of having portability as the logic can remain the same with a different ARM core or Cortex-M variant, just by having from minor changes in the assembly wrapper. Plus, by calling a C function, there is access to C library features, optimizations made by the compiler, and easier tools for debugging as well. That all being said, there are some downsides. These include the fact that there is overhead to calling a C function that would involve saving caller-saved registers, parameter passing, and restoring the caller-saved registers as well, which over high-frequency or volume can add up in overhead. In addition, there is also the downside of stack usage implications, where by calling a C function uses more stack space for each function called, this becomes a problem for RTOS with constrained RAM or has deeply nested C calls from within its handlers. One other possible downside is that of loss of absolute control, where by coding in assembly ensures you have precise byte-level control over every instruction and register manipulation, but using a C function relies on the compiler to generate the assembly. This might only matter if the system is performance-sensitive and requires a high degree of optimization.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,4,'2025-07-28_16-07-38','There are certainly some benefits to calling the C functions compared to writing them in assembly. For one, it assists with readability and maintainability, allows the code to be more expressive and understandable while also reducing development time. In addition, there is the benefit of having portability as the logic can remain the same with a different ARM core or Cortex-M variant, just by having from minor changes in the assembly wrapper. Plus, by calling a C function, there is access to C library features, optimizations made by the compiler, and easier tools for debugging as well. That all being said, there are some downsides. These include the fact that there is overhead to calling a C function that would involve saving caller-saved registers, parameter passing, and restoring the caller-saved registers as well, which over high-frequency or volume can add up in overhead. In addition, there is also the downside of stack usage implications, where by calling a C function uses more stack space for each function called, this becomes a problem for RTOS with constrained RAM or has deeply nested C calls from within its handlers. One other possible downside is that of loss of absolute control, where by coding in assembly ensures you have precise byte-level control over every instruction and register manipulation, but using a C function relies on the compiler to generate the assembly. This might only matter if the system is performance-sensitive and requires a high degree of optimization.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,4,'2025-07-28_16-17-26','There are certainly some benefits to calling the C functions compared to writing them in assembly. For one, it assists with readability and maintainability, allows the code to be more expressive and understandable while also reducing development time. In addition, there is the benefit of having portability as the logic can remain the same with a different ARM core or Cortex-M variant, just by having from minor changes in the assembly wrapper. Plus, by calling a C function, there is access to C library features, optimizations made by the compiler, and easier tools for debugging as well. That all being said, there are some downsides. These include the fact that there is overhead to calling a C function that would involve saving caller-saved registers, parameter passing, and restoring the caller-saved registers as well, which over high-frequency or volume can add up in overhead. In addition, there is also the downside of stack usage implications, where by calling a C function uses more stack space for each function called, this becomes a problem for RTOS with constrained RAM or has deeply nested C calls from within its handlers. One other possible downside is that of loss of absolute control, where by coding in assembly ensures you have precise byte-level control over every instruction and register manipulation, but using a C function relies on the compiler to generate the assembly. This might only matter if the system is performance-sensitive and requires a high degree of optimization.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,5,'2025-07-28_13-12-48','The OS would certainly still work as there wouldn’t be a reason for the system to crash due to a fundamental flaw in the scheduling logic. However, the system behaviour would be very limited and wouldn’t demonstrate the benefits of a multi-threaded OS as the scheduler would just be endlessly switching back to the same print_continously thread in a loop indefinitely, creating the overhead of context switching without any benefit.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',4,5,'2025-07-28_16-07-38','The OS would certainly still work as there wouldn’t be a reason for the system to crash due to a fundamental flaw in the scheduling logic. However, the system behaviour would be very limited and wouldn’t demonstrate the benefits of a multi-threaded OS as the scheduler would just be endlessly switching back to the same print_continously thread in a loop indefinitely, creating the overhead of context switching without any benefit.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',4,5,'2025-07-28_16-17-26','The OS would certainly still work as there wouldn’t be a reason for the system to crash due to a fundamental flaw in the scheduling logic, the thread would still be chosen by osSched and granted CPU time to execute its instructions. However, the system behaviour would be very limited and wouldn’t demonstrate the benefits of a multi-threaded OS as osSched would just be re-selecting the same thread of print_continously in a loop indefinitely, creating the overhead of context switching without any benefit.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',5,1,'2025-07-29_11-53-13','In order to pass an argument into a thread, the user must define the thread function signature, the prepare the argument data, and then call the thread creation API. Afterwards, the OS must store the argument in the TCB and initialize the thread’s stack. This works because of the strict adherence to the ABI and CPU’s context restoration mechanism where there’s a given ABI standard for how function arguments are passed, plus, the setup of the initial thread stack enables the CPU to be already in the state required to directly execute the thread with the arguments loaded. As well, it should be mentioned that when the context switch is complete, the new thread’s PSP is loaded with the address of the prepared stack frame and the registers are automatically popped from this stack frame, with the value for the argument popped directly into r0 and the address from the thread function passed into PC. As soon as the CPU finished this restoration, the PC already points to the thread function in the first argument is already in the r0 register, just as the compiler expects, allowing the CPU to proceed with the function as if it has been called normally with that argument, enabling it to cast and use data.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',5,1,'2025-07-29_12-29-39','In order to pass an argument into a thread, the user must define the thread function signature, the prepare the argument data, and then call the thread creation API. Afterwards, the OS must store the argument in the TCB and initialize the thread’s stack. This works because of the strict adherence to the ABI and CPU’s context restoration mechanism where there’s a given ABI standard for how function arguments are passed, plus, the setup of the initial thread stack enables the CPU to be already in the state required to directly execute the thread with the arguments loaded. As well, it should be mentioned that when the context switch is complete, the new thread’s PSP is loaded with the address of the prepared stack frame and the registers are automatically popped from this stack frame, with the value for the argument popped directly into r0 and the address from the thread function passed into PC. As soon as the CPU finished this restoration, the PC already points to the thread function in the first argument is already in the r0 register, just as the compiler expects, allowing the CPU to proceed with the function as if it has been called normally with that argument, enabling it to cast and use data.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',5,2,'2025-07-29_11-53-13','In SVC_Handler and PendSV_Handler, a call to osYield would request a context switch by triggering PendSV or the SVC. However, in the case of the PendSV_Handler, this is bad as it would mean that you’re calling for a context switch while one is taking place, as is the purpose of these handlers, creating a recursive call to the context switching mechanism and resulting in stack corruption, infinite loops, or a hard fault. For the SVC_Handler, this is bad as it would mean yielding the current context to a thread context, which can lead to the kernel MSP being overwritten and the CPU state becoming inconsistent or a double context switch. In the SysTick_Handler, a call to osYield would bypass the proper deferral mechanism and attempt to trigger an immediate, possibly conflicting, context switch from within an interrupt which is bad practice. Moreover, this is also conflicting since the SysTick_Handler itself is responsible for telling the scheduler that a switch might be needed.\n',0,'s25'),('s6ke',5,2,'2025-07-29_12-29-39','In SVC_Handler and PendSV_Handler, a call to osYield itself triggers a system call or interrupt by triggering PendSV or the SVC. However, in the case of the PendSV_Handler, this is bad as it would mean that you’re calling for a context switch while one is taking place, as is the purpose of these handlers, creating a recursive call to the context switching mechanism and resulting in stack corruption, infinite loops, or a hard fault. For the SVC_Handler, this is bad as it would mean yielding the current context to a thread context, which can lead to the kernel MSP being overwritten and the CPU state becoming inconsistent or a double context switch. In the SysTick_Handler, a call to osYield also triggers a system call or interrupt that would bypass the proper deferral mechanism and attempt to trigger an immediate, possibly conflicting, context switch from within an interrupt which is bad practice. Moreover, this is also conflicting since the SysTick_Handler itself is responsible for telling the scheduler that a switch might be needed.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',5,3,'2025-07-29_11-53-13','Some benefits of decreasing the fundamental OS clock to 0.1ms would include increasing the responsiveness and granularity for time-critical tasks, since there would be lower latency for delays and timeouts as well as more precise scheduling, having a higher resolution for time-based operations, resulting in more accurate event timestamps and finer control of delays, and improving the concurrency for CPU-bound tasks, meaning that each task gets a slice of CPU more frequently and making the system feel more responsive. However, some downsides would include increased CPU overhead for SysTick Interrupts, owing to a higher interrupt frequency, increased context switch rate, and more time spent on OS overhead owing to the frequency increase, increased power consumption, due to more work being evaluated at any given time, and increased stack usage and thrashing, since more frames of stacks may be needed or nested rapidly and the time spent switching between tasks could eventually become larger that the time spent on tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',5,3,'2025-07-29_12-29-39','Some benefits of decreasing the fundamental OS clock to 0.1ms would include increasing the responsiveness and granularity for time-critical tasks, since there would be lower latency for delays and timeouts as well as more precise scheduling, having a higher resolution for time-based operations, resulting in more accurate event timestamps and finer control of delays, and improving the concurrency for CPU-bound tasks, meaning that each task gets a slice of CPU more frequently and making the system feel more responsive. However, some downsides would include increased CPU overhead for SysTick Interrupts, owing to a higher interrupt frequency, increased context switch rate, and more time spent on OS overhead owing to the frequency increase, increased power consumption, due to more work being evaluated at any given time, and increased stack usage and thrashing, since more frames of stacks may be needed or nested rapidly and the time spent switching between tasks could eventually become larger that the time spent on tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',5,4,'2025-07-29_11-53-13','From the user perspective, having co-operative multitasking enables the used to be in control of yielding, allows for voluntary CPU relinquishment, offers a simpler mental model, and ensure that you won’t have to worry about automatic pre-emption. For co-operative multitasking, the OS must rely on tasks for control transfer, the scheduling is yield driven, the scheduling logic is simpler, and there are no timer interrupts for scheduling. On the other hand, with pre-emptive multitasking, from the user perspective the OS is in control of scheduling, tasks are time-sliced, there is apparent concurrency as the tasks seem to run in parallel, there is a need for synchronization since tasks can be interrupted at any point, and it is more robust for bad tasks should a task get stuck in an infinite loop. For pre-emptive multitasking, the OS performs active scheduling with a hardware timer, pre-emptions are forced, there is more sophisticated scheduler logic, there is guaranteed progress for ready tasks, and the scheduling is interrupt driven.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',5,4,'2025-07-29_12-29-39','From the user perspective, having co-operative multitasking enables the used to be in control of yielding, allows for voluntary CPU relinquishment, offers a simpler mental model, and ensure that you won’t have to worry about automatic pre-emption. For co-operative multitasking, the OS must rely on tasks for control transfer, the scheduling is yield driven, the scheduling logic is simpler, and there are no timer interrupts for scheduling. On the other hand, with pre-emptive multitasking, from the user perspective the OS is in control of scheduling, tasks are time-sliced, there is apparent concurrency as the tasks seem to run in parallel, there is a need for synchronization since tasks can be interrupted at any point, and it is more robust for bad tasks should a task get stuck in an infinite loop. For pre-emptive multitasking, the OS performs active scheduling with a hardware timer, pre-emptions are forced, there is more sophisticated scheduler logic, there is guaranteed progress for ready tasks, and the scheduling is interrupt driven.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',5,5,'2025-07-29_11-53-13','It is possible for these two mechanisms to work together since there are complementary tools that a thread can use to manage CPU allocation, with the ultimate goal of achieving a context switch with different methods. To start, they have a shared goal of relinquishing the CPU, where osYield voluntarily gives up the CPU and the time-based pre-emption uses the OS to involuntarily take the PCU away from the thread. Plus, the scheduler is the central decider regardless of whether yielding or pre-emption triggered the scheduler. Where they may differ is in the pathways to the scheduler, where yielding will result in a SVC call or direct PendSV exception that saves the current context and loads the next, and the time-based pre-emption will run to check if a thread’s quantum time has expired and thus pends the PendSV exception in the same way if it has. As such, there systems are able to complement each other by yielding for cooperative pauses within pre-emption, using pre-emption as a safety next, and managing task state transitions.\n',1,'s25'),('s6ke',5,5,'2025-07-29_12-29-39','It is possible for these two mechanisms to work together since there are complementary tools that a thread can use to manage CPU allocation, with the ultimate goal of achieving a context switch with different methods. To start, they have a shared goal of relinquishing the CPU, where osYield voluntarily gives up the CPU and the time-based pre-emption uses the OS to involuntarily take the PCU away from the thread. Plus, the scheduler is the central decider regardless of whether yielding or pre-emption triggered the scheduler. Where they may differ is in the pathways to the scheduler, where yielding will result in a SVC call or direct PendSV exception that saves the current context and loads the next, and the time-based pre-emption will run to check if a thread’s quantum time has expired and thus pends the PendSV exception in the same way if it has. As such, there systems are able to complement each other by yielding for cooperative pauses within pre-emption, using pre-emption as a safety next, and managing task state transitions.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',1,1,'2025-06-24_13-52-59','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a collection of software libraries that sit between the application code and the low-level hardware of the microcontroller. It is provided by STMicroelectronics for STM32s to simplify controlling and interacting with microcontroller hardware and prevents the need to dive into complicated low-level code.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',1,1,'2025-06-24_14-34-35','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a collection of software libraries that sit between the application code and the low-level hardware of the microcontroller. It is provided by STMicroelectronics for STM32s to simplify controlling and interacting with microcontroller hardware and prevents the need to dive into complicated low-level code.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',1,2,'2025-06-24_13-52-59','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that tells the program how to actually send a character out of the microcontroller. __io_putchar is required in this case as print does not know how to actually communicate with the hardware by itself. The printf function sends the message one character at a time, and for each character it calls __io_putchar(ch). In this lab, we used the UART interface to display the text on the serial console.\n',0,'s25'),('s96zhang',1,2,'2025-06-24_14-34-35','The __io_putchar function is a helper function that tells the program how to actually send a character out of the microcontroller. __io_putchar is required in this case as print does not know how to actually communicate with the hardware by itself. The printf function sends the message one character at a time, and for each character it calls __io_putchar(ch). In this lab, we used the UART interface to display the text on the serial console. The __io_purchar function acts as a bridge between the printf function and the hardware specific output mechanism. In embedded systems, printf is a general purpose standard library function that doesn’t know how to send data to any particular device and relies on functions such as __io_putchar to define how and where characters should be output. Printf is hardware-agnostic and doesn’t know how to send outpu to a specific device by default. Different projects may use different output peripherals such as UART (in this lab) or USB or SPI, and __io_putchar provides the link between standard C output and the chosen hardware. This is a common approach on many embedded platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',1,3,'2025-06-24_13-52-59','We used the debugger in this lab as some of the things we needed to verify, such as the exact values of the MSP and PSP registers, or whether the stack pointer itself changed cannot be actually checked using printf. Debugger provides easy and accurate access to register values in the processor.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',1,3,'2025-06-24_14-34-35','We used the debugger in this lab as some of the things we needed to verify, such as the exact values of the MSP and PSP registers, or whether the stack pointer itself changed cannot be actually checked using printf. Debugger provides easy and accurate access to register values in the processor.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',1,4,'2025-06-24_13-52-59','We read the initial MSP value to figure out where the top of the stack memory is. Then, we subtracted a certain amount (0x400 bytes) to create a new region of memory for the PSP. The stack grows downwards in memory, and subtracting a certain amount moves to a lower address. We required the initial MSP value so we could place the PSP stack safely in memory and avoid any allocation conflicts and memory corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',1,4,'2025-06-24_14-34-35','We read the initial MSP value to figure out where the top of the stack memory is. Then, we subtracted a certain amount (0x400 bytes) to create a new region of memory for the PSP. The stack grows downwards in memory, and subtracting a certain amount moves to a lower address. We required the initial MSP value so we could place the PSP stack safely in memory and avoid any allocation conflicts and memory corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',1,5,'2025-06-24_13-52-59','To allocate multiple stacks for threads using the same stack allocation method outlined in the lab manual, we can use the same definitions, where the initial main stack pointer value is MSP_INIT_VAL and divided into fixed-sized blocks. And each thread stack is defined with a fixed size of THREAD_STACK_SIZE. Finally, the main stack is also defined to have its own size of MAIN_STACK_SIZE. Again, any thread’s starting address for its stack would be ADDR_STACK_i = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE+(j-1)*THREAD_STACK_SIZE where we start at 1 and returns the starting address in bytes. Using a loop, we can calculate each thread stack’s starting address. Two main checks we need for check for in this case is alignment and total size. Both THREAD_STACK_SIZE and MAIN_STACK_SIZE should be multiples of 8 bytes in order to meet the Cortex-M4’s alignment requirements. To check for total size, we need to make sure that the sum of the MAIN_STACK_SIZE and the number of threads multiplied by the THREAD_STACK_SIZE, is less than the MAX_STACK_SIZE as to not exceed the total amount of RAM.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',1,5,'2025-06-24_14-34-35','To allocate multiple stacks for threads using the same stack allocation method outlined in the lab manual, we can use the same definitions, where the initial main stack pointer value is MSP_INIT_VAL and divided into fixed-sized blocks. And each thread stack is defined with a fixed size of THREAD_STACK_SIZE. Finally, the main stack is also defined to have its own size of MAIN_STACK_SIZE. Again, any thread’s starting address for its stack would be ADDR_STACK_i = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE+(j-1)*THREAD_STACK_SIZE where we start at 1 and returns the starting address in bytes. Using a loop, we can calculate each thread stack’s starting address. Two main checks we need for check for in this case is alignment and total size. Both THREAD_STACK_SIZE and MAIN_STACK_SIZE should be multiples of 8 bytes in order to meet the Cortex-M4’s alignment requirements. To check for total size, we need to make sure that the sum of the MAIN_STACK_SIZE and the number of threads multiplied by the THREAD_STACK_SIZE, is less than the MAX_STACK_SIZE as to not exceed the total amount of RAM.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',2,1,'2025-06-25_23-51-36','When we changed the Program Counter (PC) to hold the address of the print_continuously function, we changed the flow of program execution. Normally during operations the CPU updates the PC to point to the next instruction, but in this case changing the program counter to hold the function pointer to the print_continuously makes the processor immediately jump to the print_continuously function and start to execute it as if it was called normally. This is basically manually setting the PC to a new address which is the start of the function we wrote. And finally, since the function is an infinite loop, the CPU continued to print “Hello PC!” forever and no code is run afterwards. In this way, we controlled exactly where the CU goes to next.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',2,2,'2025-06-25_23-51-36','A system call is a special way for a program to ask the operating system to do something. This can include accessing memory or printing to the console. In this lab, we used the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction to trigger a system call, which caused the CPU to pause what it was doing and start a special function, which is SVC_Handler. This special function handled the request based on the number we passed into the function. Basically, a system is like a software trap that lets user code request services from the OS/privileged kernel code and provided a more controlled access to system hardware and resources and making sure there is no corruption and faults.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',2,3,'2025-06-25_23-51-36','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing memory so that the CPU can load the thread’s information and start executing the thread correctly. We push values like xPSR, PC, LR, and registers from R0 to R12 onto the stack in the order the CPU expects. This process simulates what the stack would look like if the thread had already been running. The register values are stored on the stack so the CPU can restore them and resume execution as if it never stopped. This is important as it allows the system to\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',2,4,'2025-06-25_23-51-36','If the PSP was not set using the __set_PSP function, the CPU would not know where the thread’s stack is located. Then, when the CPU tries to pop register values like PC and xPSR from an uninitialized or wrong stack location, it will access invalid memory, leading to hard fault. Setting the PSP makes sure that the CPU will be reading from the properly prepared stack for the new thread, which will allow it to restore context and run safely with no issues.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',2,5,'2025-06-25_23-51-36','If the thread function did not loop continuously and simply printed something once and returned, the CPU would try to pop the return address from the stack and execute whatever is stored already in the LR (Link Register). But because of the fact that the thread wasn’t called through a normal function call, the return address of this operation might be invalid or missing, and lead to access to a restricted memory region. This will cause a hard fault. Therefore, it is very important for the thread’s stack to be initialized correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',3,1,'2025-06-27_00-27-57','The process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable involves three main steps – preprocessing, compiling, and linking. During the preprocessing stage, #include and #DEFINE directives and macros are expanded. This creates pure C files. Then, for the compiling stage, each .c file is translated into an object file which contains machine code, and this is the stage where syntax errors are checked. Then, for the final stage of linking, the linker takes all the object files and combines then, resolving references between them such as function calls across different files, which produces a single executable that can run on the system.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',3,1,'2025-06-27_10-00-01','The process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable involves three main steps – preprocessing, compiling, and linking. During the preprocessing stage, #include and #DEFINE directives and macros are expanded. This creates pure C files. Then, for the compiling stage, each .c file is translated into an object file which contains machine code, and this is the stage where syntax errors are checked. Then, for the final stage of linking, the linker takes all the object files and combines then, resolving references between them such as function calls across different files, which produces a single executable that can run on the system.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',3,2,'2025-06-27_00-27-57','We cannot use an #includee directive on a *.c file such as using #include <kernel.c> because it breaks the purpose of the separate compilation process. In this case, instead of compiling each *.c file independently and linking them later on, including a *.c file forces the entire content of the file to be added into another file during preprocessing stage, before compilation. This can lead to cases where there are multiple definitions of the same functions or variables if the file is included more than once, causing double compilation. This also makes the entire build process harder to manage and debug. The correct way to share code between files is to use header files for declarations and compile each *.c file separately.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',3,2,'2025-06-27_10-00-01','We cannot use an #includee directive on a *.c file such as using #include <kernel.c> because it breaks the purpose of the separate compilation process. In this case, instead of compiling each *.c file independently and linking them later on, including a *.c file forces the entire content of the file to be added into another file during preprocessing stage, before compilation. This can lead to cases where there are multiple definitions of the same functions or variables if the file is included more than once, causing double compilation. This also makes the entire build process harder to manage and debug. The correct way to share code between files is to use header files for declarations and compile each *.c file separately.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',3,3,'2025-06-27_00-27-57','Define the thread function – writing a function that contains the code that is intended for the thread to run.\nInitialize the kernel – before creating any threads, need to call a function that prepares the kernel’s internal state, includes actions such as setting up the stack memory.\nCreate the thread – call a function to register the thread with the kernel, which sets up the thread’s stack and prepares it to be run soon.\nStart the kernel – call a function to begin the execution of the first thread. Starting the kernel triggers a system call that loads the thread’s stack pointer. The processor switches into thread mode and jumps to thread function.\nThread runs independently from rest of program\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',3,3,'2025-06-27_10-00-01','Define the thread function – writing a function that contains the code that is intended for the thread to run.\nInitialize the kernel – before creating any threads, need to call a function that prepares the kernel’s internal state, includes actions such as setting up the stack memory.\nCreate the thread – call a function to register the thread with the kernel, which sets up the thread’s stack and prepares it to be run soon.\nStart the kernel – call a function to begin the execution of the first thread. Starting the kernel triggers a system call that loads the thread’s stack pointer. The processor switches into thread mode and jumps to thread function.\nThread runs independently from rest of program\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',3,4,'2025-06-27_00-27-57','The stack pool is a reserved memory region used to allocate individual stacks for each thread. Stack pool allows multiple threads to exist by giving each thread its own reserved separate stack space, which prevents overlap and allows for safe context switching. This allows for the kernel to manage memory dynamically and supports the creation and deletion of threads during execution.\n',0,'s25'),('s96zhang',3,4,'2025-06-27_10-00-01','The stack pool is a reserved memory region used to allocate individual stacks for each thread and OS stacks that are used for system calls, interrupt handling, and privileged execution. Stack pool allows multiple threads to exist by giving each thread its own reserved separate stack space, which prevents overlap and allows for safe context switching. This allows for the kernel to manage memory dynamically and supports the creation and deletion of threads during execution. The stack pool also includes space reserved for the OS’s own stacks, and including OS stacks ensures that both user threads and kernel-level operations can run safely and independently in managed memory.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',3,5,'2025-06-27_00-27-57','The difference between the “user” and the “kernel” is that kernel code handles the low-level setup and management of the system, whereas the “user” code defines what the program should do. The “kernel” handles tasks such as stack initialization, interrupts, thread switching, and system calls. Whereas the “user” completes things like thread functions and calls to create or start threads. In terms of what goes into which file, usually the “user” code goes into files like main.c, and the “kernel” code goes into files like kernel.c and kernel.h. This distinction is important from the user’s perspective because it keeps the user interface simple, and users can create and run threads without needing to understand or work with the complex kernel internals. Overall, this makes the system more modular and easier to maintain.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',3,5,'2025-06-27_10-00-01','The difference between the “user” and the “kernel” is that kernel code handles the low-level setup and management of the system, whereas the “user” code defines what the program should do. The “kernel” handles tasks such as stack initialization, interrupts, thread switching, and system calls. Whereas the “user” completes things like thread functions and calls to create or start threads. In terms of what goes into which file, usually the “user” code goes into files like main.c, and the “kernel” code goes into files like kernel.c and kernel.h. This distinction is important from the user’s perspective because it keeps the user interface simple, and users can create and run threads without needing to understand or work with the complex kernel internals. Overall, this makes the system more modular and easier to maintain.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,1,'2025-07-26_21-08-31','In a queue-based system, the scheduler would remove the current thread from the front of the queue and perform a context switch, then it will place the thread at the back. Round-robin behaviour would natural emerge from this kind of rotation. When compared to an array with an index, a queue can make queueing and dequeuing more clean and explicit. This also makes it better when combined with other functions like removing threads since adding and removing from a queue is easier than manipulating array numbers.\n',0,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,1,'2025-07-26_23-19-36','In a queue-based system, the scheduler would remove the current thread from the front of the queue and perform a context switch, then it will place the thread at the back. Round-robin behaviour would natural emerge from this kind of rotation. When compared to an array with an index, a queue can make queueing and dequeuing more clean and explicit. This also makes it better when combined with other functions like removing threads since adding and removing from a queue is easier than manipulating array numbers. However, the added abstraction may introduce overhead in terms of memory and performance due to dynamic memory usage and pointer manipulation. This may not offer significant functional improvements.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,1,'2025-07-30_11-06-49','In a queue-based system, the scheduler would remove the current thread from the front of the queue and perform a context switch, then it will place the thread at the back. Round-robin behaviour would natural emerge from this kind of rotation. When compared to an array with an index, a queue can make queueing and dequeuing more clean and explicit. This also makes it better when combined with other functions like removing threads since adding and removing from a queue is easier than manipulating array numbers. However, the added abstraction may introduce overhead in terms of memory and performance due to dynamic memory usage and pointer manipulation. This may not offer significant functional improvements.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,2,'2025-07-26_21-08-31','PendSV_Handler is triggered when the operating system decides to switch threads. First, it saves the current thread’s context (R4-R11) onto the stack. Then, it calls the osSched() function to update the currentThread index and set the PSP (process stack pointer) to the new thread’s stake pointer. Finally, it would restore the new thread’s saved context (R4-R11) from its stack and returns execution to that thread.\n',0,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,2,'2025-07-26_23-19-36','PendSV_Handler is triggered when the operating system decides to switch threads. First, it reads the current process stack pointer (PSP), this is important because the PSP points to where the thread’s context will be saved. Secondly, it pushes the callee-saved registers (R4-R11) of the current thread onto its stack using the PSP. This makes sure the context is preserved. Third, it saves the update PSP value back to the current thread so the OS knows where its stack ends. Fourth, the osSched() function is called to updated and point to where the next thread to run is. Fifth, it loads the new thread’s saved PSP and restores the new threads R4-R11 registers from its stack. Finally, it updates the PSP register and returns from the exception, and resume execution in the new thread. This structured process makes sure that each thread can resume where it left off and allows for smooth context switching.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,2,'2025-07-30_11-06-49','PendSV_Handler is triggered when the operating system decides to switch threads. First, it reads the current process stack pointer (PSP), this is important because the PSP points to where the thread’s context will be saved. Secondly, it pushes the callee-saved registers (R4-R11) of the current thread onto its stack using the PSP. This makes sure the context is preserved. Third, it saves the update PSP value back to the current thread so the OS knows where its stack ends. Fourth, the osSched() function is called to updated and point to where the next thread to run is. Fifth, it loads the new thread’s saved PSP and restores the new threads R4-R11 registers from its stack. Finally, it updates the PSP register and returns from the exception, and resume execution in the new thread. This structured process makes sure that each thread can resume where it left off and allows for smooth context switching.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,3,'2025-07-26_21-08-31','This is a bad idea because if the scheduler is designed such that it always chooses the highest priority task, then only the highest priority tasks would ever be run. This can cause starvation which means that lower priority threads may never be run if higher priority threads are never finished. This breaks fairness and the system would become non-responsive to other lower priority tasks. Ultimately, one high priority task will dominate the CPU indefinitely.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,3,'2025-07-26_23-19-36','This is a bad idea because if the scheduler is designed such that it always chooses the highest priority task, then only the highest priority tasks would ever be run. This can cause starvation which means that lower priority threads may never be run if higher priority threads are never finished. This breaks fairness and the system would become non-responsive to other lower priority tasks. Ultimately, one high priority task will dominate the CPU indefinitely.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,3,'2025-07-30_11-06-49','This is a bad idea because if the scheduler is designed such that it always chooses the highest priority task, then only the highest priority tasks would ever be run. This can cause starvation which means that lower priority threads may never be run if higher priority threads are never finished. This breaks fairness and the system would become non-responsive to other lower priority tasks. Ultimately, one high priority task will dominate the CPU indefinitely.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,4,'2025-07-26_21-08-31','Calling C functions makes the code much easier to read and debug, for example with logic like indexing and stack pointer manipulation. Also, C allows for the use of higher-level data structures lik arrays and queues without having to manually manipulate memory. However, there are some downsides of using C such has having more performance overhead than assembly and not having as much precise control over low level registers and memory layout. Assembly offers more control but is easier to make errors with it and hard to write.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,4,'2025-07-26_23-19-36','Calling C functions makes the code much easier to read and debug, for example with logic like indexing and stack pointer manipulation. Also, C allows for the use of higher-level data structures lik arrays and queues without having to manually manipulate memory. However, there are some downsides of using C such has having more performance overhead than assembly and not having as much precise control over low level registers and memory layout. Assembly offers more control but is easier to make errors with it and hard to write.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,4,'2025-07-30_11-06-49','Calling C functions makes the code much easier to read and debug, for example with logic like indexing and stack pointer manipulation. Also, C allows for the use of higher-level data structures lik arrays and queues without having to manually manipulate memory. However, there are some downsides of using C such has having more performance overhead than assembly and not having as much precise control over low level registers and memory layout. Assembly offers more control but is easier to make errors with it and hard to write.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,5,'2025-07-26_21-08-31','Yes, the OS will still work but thread switching would have no effect. With having only one thread, it would rotate to the next thread which will be the same thread again repeatedly. No other thread will run so the scheduler has nothing to switch to. Functionally, the system would behave like a single thread program but still go through the process of multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,5,'2025-07-26_23-19-36','Yes, the OS will still work but thread switching would have no effect. With having only one thread, it would rotate to the next thread which will be the same thread again repeatedly. No other thread will run so the scheduler has nothing to switch to. Functionally, the system would behave like a single thread program but still go through the process of multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',4,5,'2025-07-30_11-06-49','Yes, the OS will still work but thread switching would have no effect. With having only one thread, it would rotate to the next thread which will be the same thread again repeatedly. No other thread will run so the scheduler has nothing to switch to. Functionally, the system would behave like a single thread program but still go through the process of multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',5,1,'2025-07-27_10-24-59','To pass argument into a thread, the user must first define a thread function that accepts a (void* arg) parameter, then calls osCreateThread() or osCreateThreadWithDeadline() with a pointer to their argument.\nInternally, the OS stores this argument pointer into the new stack for the thread and manually constructs the initial stack during initialization. It writes the argument point into the R0 slot of this frame. When the thread is launched using an assembly routine like runFirstThread(), the CPU performs an exception return which restores the saved registers.\nThis works because in the ARM calling convention/the Cortex-M4 hardware, register values are automatically restored from the stack during exception return, which allows the OS to preload R0 and simulate a clean function call into the thread. Thus, by placing the argument on the stack at the right location during the setup of the thread, the thread receives it correctly as if it was called normally in C.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',5,2,'2025-07-27_10-24-59','osYield() cannot be called in context switching functions because osYield() is designed to trigger a supervisor call (PendSV) interrupt to perform a context switch, this causes an exception while already handling an exception. This would lead to nested exceptions or recursive context switching, leading to corrupting the stack and undefined behaviour of the system. This would also violate the fact that osYield() should only be called from thread-level code, and not from the OS kernel’s interrupt context.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',5,3,'2025-07-27_10-24-59','Reducing the OS tick interval from 1ms to something like 0.1ms would increase the time resolution of the scheduler, which allows for finer control over time-slicing and potentially more responsive multitasking, as well as increased responsiveness. But, this would come with downsides as the SysTick interrupt will now occur 10 times more frequently, increasing significantly CPU overhead from context switching and timer handling and decreasing system performance. Every tick would require the OS to run the SysTick_Handler, consequently reducing the time actual work can get done.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',5,4,'2025-07-27_10-24-59','From a user perspective, cooperative multitasking requires the thread to manually yield control (for example calling osYield()), and the user must decide when to give up CPU time. On the other hand, pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts threads after a fixed timeslice by using timer interrupts. From an OS perspective, cooperative scheduling is more simple. In this case the OS switches context only when told so. In preemptive scheduling, the OS must use a hardware timer like SysTick and set up interrupts to force a context switch. The OS also needs to make sure that the context is safely saved and restored later if a thread isn’t expecting to be interrupted.\n',1,'s25'),('s96zhang',5,5,'2025-07-27_10-24-59','Cooperative and pre-emptive context switching can work together because they use different triggers to call the same context switching mechanism - PendSV. For cooperative threads, osYield uses a supervisor call and sets PendSV bit manually. In pre-emptive threads, a hardware timer interrupt (SysTick) calls PendSV. Then, both lead to the pendSV_Handler which then performs the actual context switching. This allows them to coexist safely.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,1,'2025-05-15_21-52-16','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It provides an interface between the hardware (STM32 in this lab) and the software (the development IDE). HAL provides portability and simplicity to embedded project since HAL functions can be used directly to complete tasks like configure pins and read sensors, instead of developers writing low-level code for the microcontrollers.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,1,'2025-05-15_22-04-28','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It provides an interface between the hardware (STM32 in this lab) and the software (the development IDE). HAL provides portability and simplicity to embedded project since HAL functions can be used directly to complete tasks like configure pins and read sensors, instead of developers writing low-level code for the microcontrollers.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,1,'2025-05-15_22-11-26','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It provides an interface between the hardware (STM32 in this lab) and the software (the development IDE). HAL provides portability and simplicity to embedded project since HAL functions can be used directly to complete tasks like configure pins and read sensors, instead of developers writing low-level code for the microcontrollers.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,1,'2025-05-15_22-17-37','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It provides an interface between the hardware (STM32 in this lab) and the software (the development IDE). HAL provides portability and simplicity to embedded project since HAL functions can be used directly to complete tasks like configure pins and read sensors, instead of developers writing low-level code for the microcontrollers.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,2,'2025-05-15_21-52-16','The function redirects a typical software console outputting like “printf()”, to UART (serial port). Since embedded systems works different from software development, instead of just printing to the console, the embedded project program needs to tell where the output is going. In this case, it’s UART. The function takes in characters and transmitting those characters to the UART, and allow developers to see the output of the program in the IDE. It is needed because it allows standard output functions like “printf()” to work in embedded system programs. It defines how “printf()” sends characters to UART and connects this standard software output function to real hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',1,2,'2025-05-15_22-04-28','The __io_putchar function redirects standard output functions like printf() to a specific hardware interface, such as UART. In embedded systems, unlike traditional software environments, there is no default console output—so the program must explicitly define where output should go. This function takes each character from printf() and transmits it over UART, allowing developers to see output in a terminal connected to the device. It\'s necessary because it bridges the gap between standard C library functions and the specific hardware in use. Importantly, the way __io_putchar is implemented can vary between projects depending on the hardware—some systems may use UART, while others may use USB, semihosting, or other interfaces. This hardware variability means developers must customize the function to match the output method available on their particular device.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',1,2,'2025-05-15_22-11-26','Debugger is a clearer way to demonstrate low-level processor features like manipulating PSP and MSP. Since those are core parts of the CPU’s internal state, they don’t necessary produce visible outputs for functions like “printf()” to accept and show on the console. More importantly, debugger allows developers to stop at a certain instant of the program. Running “printf()” might potentially configures the memory settings and therefore changes the ideal output developers want when changing MSP and PSP. Since this lab is about changing MSP to PSP, using debugger provides a better view by pausing the program before the infinite while loop and allow us to see the actual memory address changes for MSP and PSP.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',1,2,'2025-05-15_22-17-37','The __io_putchar function redirects standard output functions like printf() to a specific hardware interface, such as UART. In embedded systems, unlike traditional software environments, there is no default console output—so the program must explicitly define where output should go. This function takes each character from printf() and transmits it over UART, allowing developers to see output in a terminal connected to the device. It\'s necessary because it bridges the gap between standard C library functions and the specific hardware in use. Importantly, the way __io_putchar is implemented can vary between projects depending on the hardware—some systems may use UART, while others may use USB, semihosting, or other interfaces. This hardware variability means developers must customize the function to match the output method available on their particular device.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,3,'2025-05-15_21-52-16','Debugger is a clearer way to demonstrate low-level processor features like manipulating PSP and MSP. Since those are core parts of the CPU’s internal state, they don’t necessary produce visible outputs for functions like “printf()” to accept and show on the console. More importantly, debugger allows developers to stop at a certain instant of the program. Running “printf()” might potentially configures the memory settings and therefore changes the ideal output developers want when changing MSP and PSP. Since this lab is about changing MSP to PSP, using debugger provides a better view by pausing the program before the infinite while loop and allow us to see the actual memory address changes for MSP and PSP.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,3,'2025-05-15_22-04-28','Debugger is a clearer way to demonstrate low-level processor features like manipulating PSP and MSP. Since those are core parts of the CPU’s internal state, they don’t necessary produce visible outputs for functions like “printf()” to accept and show on the console. More importantly, debugger allows developers to stop at a certain instant of the program. Running “printf()” might potentially configures the memory settings and therefore changes the ideal output developers want when changing MSP and PSP. Since this lab is about changing MSP to PSP, using debugger provides a better view by pausing the program before the infinite while loop and allow us to see the actual memory address changes for MSP and PSP.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,3,'2025-05-15_22-11-26','We get the new value for PSP_val by subtracting our assumed size of main stack size and threads stack size (0x400 bytes) from the MSP_init_val. This works because stacks grow from higher memory address from lower ones, meaning the top of the stack, which is MSP, is at a higher memory address and subtracting it allows us to obtain the correct PSP_val. MSP defines the address of the start of the whole stack region. Without MSP it becomes impossible to know where the useable RAM for stacks begins.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,3,'2025-05-15_22-17-37','Debugger is a clearer way to demonstrate low-level processor features like manipulating PSP and MSP. Since those are core parts of the CPU’s internal state, they don’t necessary produce visible outputs for functions like “printf()” to accept and show on the console. More importantly, debugger allows developers to stop at a certain instant of the program. Running “printf()” might potentially configures the memory settings and therefore changes the ideal output developers want when changing MSP and PSP. Since this lab is about changing MSP to PSP, using debugger provides a better view by pausing the program before the infinite while loop and allow us to see the actual memory address changes for MSP and PSP.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,4,'2025-05-15_21-52-16','We get the new value for PSP_val by subtracting our assumed size of main stack size and threads stack size (0x400 bytes) from the MSP_init_val. This works because stacks grow from higher memory address from lower ones, meaning the top of the stack, which is MSP, is at a higher memory address and subtracting it allows us to obtain the correct PSP_val. MSP defines the address of the start of the whole stack region. Without MSP it becomes impossible to know where the useable RAM for stacks begins.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,4,'2025-05-15_22-04-28','We get the new value for PSP_val by subtracting our assumed size of main stack size and threads stack size (0x400 bytes) from the MSP_init_val. This works because stacks grow from higher memory address from lower ones, meaning the top of the stack, which is MSP, is at a higher memory address and subtracting it allows us to obtain the correct PSP_val. MSP defines the address of the start of the whole stack region. Without MSP it becomes impossible to know where the useable RAM for stacks begins.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',1,4,'2025-05-15_22-11-26','To allocate multiple stacks at compile-time, we divide the total stack region—starting from MSP_init_val into equal-sized blocks, one for each thread. For each stack, we compute its starting address by subtracting an offset based on its index and stack size, such as “PSP_val = MSP_init_val – (i + 1) * STACK_SIZE”. This ensures that each stack has a dedicated, non-overlapping space in memory. To meet the Cortex-M4\'s requirement for 8-byte alignment, we must ensure that STACK_SIZE is a multiple of 8. Additionally, we need to confirm that the total memory consumed by all stacks does not exceed the allocated stack region—this means NUM_THREADS * STACK_SIZE must be less than or equal to the total available space between MSP_init_val and the bottom of the stack area. Since the stack grows downward, it’s also important to maintain correct stack direction and alignment, respecting any architecture-specific constraints to avoid unpredictable behavior or faults.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,4,'2025-05-15_22-17-37','We get the new value for PSP_val by subtracting our assumed size of main stack size and threads stack size (0x400 bytes) from the MSP_init_val. This works because stacks grow from higher memory address from lower ones, meaning the top of the stack, which is MSP, is at a higher memory address and subtracting it allows us to obtain the correct PSP_val. MSP defines the address of the start of the whole stack region. Without MSP it becomes impossible to know where the useable RAM for stacks begins.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,5,'2025-05-15_21-52-16','To allocate multiple stacks at compile-time, we would divide the total stack space starting from MSP_init_val into equal-sized regions, one for each thread. By subtracting fixed offsets for each thread, such as “PSP_val = MSP_init_val – (i + 1) * STACK_SIZE”, we can determine the starting address of each stack in advance and ensure they don’t overlap.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',1,5,'2025-05-15_22-04-28','To allocate multiple stacks at compile-time, we divide the total stack region—starting from MSP_init_val into equal-sized blocks, one for each thread. For each stack, we compute its starting address by subtracting an offset based on its index and stack size, such as “PSP_val = MSP_init_val – (i + 1) * STACK_SIZE”. This ensures that each stack has a dedicated, non-overlapping space in memory. To meet the Cortex-M4\'s requirement for 8-byte alignment, we must ensure that STACK_SIZE is a multiple of 8. Additionally, we need to confirm that the total memory consumed by all stacks does not exceed the allocated stack region—this means NUM_THREADS * STACK_SIZE must be less than or equal to the total available space between MSP_init_val and the bottom of the stack area. Since the stack grows downward, it’s also important to maintain correct stack direction and alignment, respecting any architecture-specific constraints to avoid unpredictable behavior or faults.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',1,5,'2025-05-15_22-17-37','To allocate multiple stacks at compile-time, we divide the total stack region—starting from MSP_init_val into equal-sized blocks, one for each thread. For each stack, we compute its starting address by subtracting an offset based on its index and stack size, such as “PSP_val = MSP_init_val – (i + 1) * STACK_SIZE”. This ensures that each stack has a dedicated, non-overlapping space in memory. To meet the Cortex-M4\'s requirement for 8-byte alignment, we must ensure that STACK_SIZE is a multiple of 8. Additionally, we need to confirm that the total memory consumed by all stacks does not exceed the allocated stack region—this means NUM_THREADS * STACK_SIZE must be less than or equal to the total available space between MSP_init_val and the bottom of the stack area. Since the stack grows downward, it’s also important to maintain correct stack direction and alignment, respecting any architecture-specific constraints to avoid unpredictable behavior or faults.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,1,'2025-05-28_19-59-40','In this lab the program passes the continuously printing function’s memory address to the “jumpAssembly” function. This contrasts to a traditional function call where it goes through the Link Register, setting up the stack frame, passing in the parameter in R0 – R3 and the function returns using BX LR. This assembly directly bypasses by copying the R0 value directly into PC. So, there is not Link register saving, it directly manipulates the PC, there is no stack operation involved and there isn’t any return path like normal function calls.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',2,1,'2025-05-28_20-14-03','When the PC is directly set to a function\'s address, program execution immediately jumps to that memory location and begins executing instructions there. This completely bypasses normal function call mechanisms. In this context, \"invoking\" the function isn\'t really a function call at all - it\'s a direct control transfer. Unlike traditional calls that use BL to save return addresses in LR and set up stack frames, MOV PC, R0 simply copies the function pointer directly into the Program Counter. There\'s no parameter setup beyond what\'s already in registers, no stack manipulation, and critically, no return mechanism. The immediate consequence is that execution permanently transfers to the target function. Once MOV PC, R0 executes, the processor starts running instructions at the new address with no memory of where it came from. This is why the continuous printing function works - execution gets trapped there indefinitely since there\'s no way to return to the original calling point. It\'s essentially a one-way go to rather than a proper function call.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,1,'2025-05-28_20-23-44','When the PC is directly set to a function\'s address, program execution immediately jumps to that memory location and begins executing instructions there. This completely bypasses normal function call mechanisms. In this context, \"invoking\" the function isn\'t really a function call at all - it\'s a direct control transfer. Unlike traditional calls that use BL to save return addresses in LR and set up stack frames, MOV PC, R0 simply copies the function pointer directly into the Program Counter. There\'s no parameter setup beyond what\'s already in registers, no stack manipulation, and critically, no return mechanism. The immediate consequence is that execution permanently transfers to the target function. Once MOV PC, R0 executes, the processor starts running instructions at the new address with no memory of where it came from. This is why the continuous printing function works - execution gets trapped there indefinitely since there\'s no way to return to the original calling point. It\'s essentially a one-way go to rather than a proper function call.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,2,'2025-05-28_19-59-40','A system call is a controlled interface for user programs to request services from the operating system kernel. Modern systems have privilege levels: user applications run in restricted \"user mode\" while the OS kernel operates in \"kernel mode\" with full hardware access. When your program needs kernel-level operations—file I/O, memory allocation, network access, process creation—it can\'t perform these directly. Instead, it makes a system call: a formal request where the program sets up parameters, triggers a special processor instruction, and transfers control to the OS. The processor switches from user mode to kernel mode, the kernel validates and executes the request, then returns results and switches back to user mode. Common examples include read()/write() for files, malloc() for memory, fork() for processes.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',2,2,'2025-05-28_20-14-03','A system call functions as a special type of function call that bridges user applications and the kernel through controlled privilege escalation. Like regular function calls, system calls accept parameters, execute specific operations, and return results, but they operate across privilege boundaries between user mode and kernel mode. This function-call abstraction is crucial because it provides programmers with familiar interfaces (like read(), write(), malloc()) that hide the complex privilege-switching mechanics underneath. At the low level, specific processor instructions like ARM\'s SVC (Supervisor Call) or x86\'s INT 0x80 trigger the transition from user to kernel mode, similar to how BL triggers regular function calls. The processor automatically saves the current context, switches privilege levels, and transfers control to kernel handlers that process the request. Parameters are passed through standard calling conventions (R0-R3 in ARM), and return values follow the same patterns as normal functions. This abstraction allows developers to write system-level code using familiar function call semantics while the hardware and OS handle the underlying privilege management, making system programming more intuitive and maintainable.',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,2,'2025-05-28_20-23-44','A system call functions as a special type of function call that bridges user applications and the kernel through controlled privilege escalation. Like regular function calls, system calls accept parameters, execute specific operations, and return results, but they operate across privilege boundaries between user mode and kernel mode. This function-call abstraction is crucial because it provides programmers with familiar interfaces (like read(), write(), malloc()) that hide the complex privilege-switching mechanics underneath. At the low level, specific processor instructions like ARM\'s SVC (Supervisor Call) or x86\'s INT 0x80 trigger the transition from user to kernel mode, similar to how BL triggers regular function calls. The processor automatically saves the current context, switches privilege levels, and transfers control to kernel handlers that process the request. Parameters are passed through standard calling conventions (R0-R3 in ARM), and return values follow the same patterns as normal functions. This abstraction allows developers to write system-level code using familiar function call semantics while the hardware and OS handle the underlying privilege management, making system programming more intuitive and maintainable.',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,3,'2025-05-28_19-59-40','Setting up a thread\'s stack means creating a fake execution context that tricks the processor into believing it\'s resuming an already-running thread when it\'s starting for the first time. We manually push register values onto the thread\'s stack in the exact order ARM processors expect during interrupt returns: xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3-R0, then R11-R4. The critical values are xPSR (processor status) and PC (set to our thread function address). Everything else gets arbitrary values for debugging. We store register values on the stack because that\'s how ARM handles context switching. When our system calls switches to the thread\'s stack and performs an interrupt return, it pops our pre-arranged values into hardware registers. When PC gets popped with our function address, the processor thinks it\'s continuing interrupted execution but starts our thread. This exploits the normal interrupt mechanism in reverse, bootstrapping thread execution by mimicking the processor\'s own context-switching behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,3,'2025-05-28_20-14-03','Setting up a thread\'s stack means creating a fake execution context that tricks the processor into believing it\'s resuming an already-running thread when it\'s starting for the first time. We manually push register values onto the thread\'s stack in the exact order ARM processors expect during interrupt returns: xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3-R0, then R11-R4. The critical values are xPSR (processor status) and PC (set to our thread function address). Everything else gets arbitrary values for debugging. We store register values on the stack because that\'s how ARM handles context switching. When our system calls switches to the thread\'s stack and performs an interrupt return, it pops our pre-arranged values into hardware registers. When PC gets popped with our function address, the processor thinks it\'s continuing interrupted execution but starts our thread. This exploits the normal interrupt mechanism in reverse, bootstrapping thread execution by mimicking the processor\'s own context-switching behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',2,3,'2025-05-28_20-23-44','Thread stack allocation involves several critical steps: first, allocating a dedicated memory region (typically from heap or static arrays), then setting the stack pointer to the top of this region since ARM stacks grow downward. We ensure validity by using proper memory boundaries, aligning addresses to word boundaries (4-byte alignment for ARM), and avoiding overlap with other memory regions. The allocated memory must be writable and within valid address space. We then manually populate this stack with register context in the exact order ARM expects during interrupt returns: xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3-R0, then R11-R4. Critical values include xPSR (processor status) and PC (thread function address), while others get debugging values. Without proper stack allocation, the thread would experience stack overflow, corrupt other memory regions, or crash with hard faults when trying to push/pop data. Invalid stack pointers cause the processor to access restricted memory during context switches, leading to system instability or complete crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,4,'2025-05-28_19-59-40','If we didn\'t call __set_PSP() before running the thread, we\'d get a hard fault because the processor would try to pop register values from the wrong stack. Here\'s what happens: when runFirstThread() executes the interrupt return sequence, it expects to pop the thread\'s context (our carefully arranged register values) from the current stack pointer. Without setting PSP, the processor would still be using MSP (Main Stack Pointer), which points to a completely different memory location that doesn\'t contain our thread setup. The processor would attempt to pop random memory values into critical registers like PC and xPSR. Since PC would get garbage data instead of our function address, the processor would jump to an invalid memory location, causing a hard fault when it tries to execute whatever random bits happen to be there. Setting PSP ensures the interrupt return mechanism pops from our prepared stack containing the correct register values, especially the crucial PC value pointing to our thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,4,'2025-05-28_20-14-03','If we didn\'t call __set_PSP() before running the thread, we\'d get a hard fault because the processor would try to pop register values from the wrong stack. Here\'s what happens: when runFirstThread() executes the interrupt return sequence, it expects to pop the thread\'s context (our carefully arranged register values) from the current stack pointer. Without setting PSP, the processor would still be using MSP (Main Stack Pointer), which points to a completely different memory location that doesn\'t contain our thread setup. The processor would attempt to pop random memory values into critical registers like PC and xPSR. Since PC would get garbage data instead of our function address, the processor would jump to an invalid memory location, causing a hard fault when it tries to execute whatever random bits happen to be there. Setting PSP ensures the interrupt return mechanism pops from our prepared stack containing the correct register values, especially the crucial PC value pointing to our thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,4,'2025-05-28_20-23-44','If we didn\'t call __set_PSP() before running the thread, we\'d get a hard fault because the processor would try to pop register values from the wrong stack. Here\'s what happens: when runFirstThread() executes the interrupt return sequence, it expects to pop the thread\'s context (our carefully arranged register values) from the current stack pointer. Without setting PSP, the processor would still be using MSP (Main Stack Pointer), which points to a completely different memory location that doesn\'t contain our thread setup. The processor would attempt to pop random memory values into critical registers like PC and xPSR. Since PC would get garbage data instead of our function address, the processor would jump to an invalid memory location, causing a hard fault when it tries to execute whatever random bits happen to be there. Setting PSP ensures the interrupt return mechanism pops from our prepared stack containing the correct register values, especially the crucial PC value pointing to our thread function.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,5,'2025-05-28_19-59-40','If the thread function just printed once and returned, the system would crash with a hard fault. Here\'s why: when a normal function returns, it pops the return address from the stack and jumps back to wherever it was called from. But our thread wasn\'t actually \"called\" by anything - we faked its context and tricked the processor into thinking it was already running. When the thread tries to return, it would pop whatever garbage value we put in the LR register (remember, we set it to 0xA) and try to jump there. Since 0xA isn\'t a valid code address, the processor would attempt to execute invalid instructions or access restricted memory, triggering a hard fault. In a real operating system, threads either run forever in loops, call an exit system call that properly handles cleanup, or have special thread termination code that switches back to the scheduler. You can\'t just let them \"fall off the end\" like regular functions because there\'s nowhere valid to return to. That\'s why our print_continuously function has an infinite loop - it never tries to return.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,5,'2025-05-28_20-14-03','If the thread function just printed once and returned, the system would crash with a hard fault. Here\'s why: when a normal function returns, it pops the return address from the stack and jumps back to wherever it was called from. But our thread wasn\'t actually \"called\" by anything - we faked its context and tricked the processor into thinking it was already running. When the thread tries to return, it would pop whatever garbage value we put in the LR register (remember, we set it to 0xA) and try to jump there. Since 0xA isn\'t a valid code address, the processor would attempt to execute invalid instructions or access restricted memory, triggering a hard fault. In a real operating system, threads either run forever in loops, call an exit system call that properly handles cleanup, or have special thread termination code that switches back to the scheduler. You can\'t just let them \"fall off the end\" like regular functions because there\'s nowhere valid to return to. That\'s why our print_continuously function has an infinite loop - it never tries to return.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',2,5,'2025-05-28_20-23-44','If the thread function just printed once and returned, the system would crash with a hard fault. Here\'s why: when a normal function returns, it pops the return address from the stack and jumps back to wherever it was called from. But our thread wasn\'t actually \"called\" by anything - we faked its context and tricked the processor into thinking it was already running. When the thread tries to return, it would pop whatever garbage value we put in the LR register (remember, we set it to 0xA) and try to jump there. Since 0xA isn\'t a valid code address, the processor would attempt to execute invalid instructions or access restricted memory, triggering a hard fault. In a real operating system, threads either run forever in loops, call an exit system call that properly handles cleanup, or have special thread termination code that switches back to the scheduler. You can\'t just let them \"fall off the end\" like regular functions because there\'s nowhere valid to return to. That\'s why our print_continuously function has an infinite loop - it never tries to return.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',3,1,'2025-06-27_11-14-19','The compilation process for multiple C files involves several stages. First, each .c file is individually compiled by the compiler into an object file (.o), which contains the machine code for that specific source file but with unresolved references to functions or variables defined in other files. The compiler uses header files (.h) to understand the function prototypes and data structures from other modules. After all object files are created, the linker combines them together, resolving all the cross-references between files - for example, when main.c calls a function defined in kernel.c, the linker connects these references. Finally, the linker produces a single executable file containing all the compiled code with all references properly resolved and memory addresses assigned.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',3,1,'2025-06-27_14-48-03','The compilation process for multiple C files involves three distinct stages. First, the preprocessor processes each .c file by handling all preprocessor directives - it expands #include statements by literally copying header file contents into the source file, replaces #define macros with their values, and processes conditional compilation directives like #ifdef. This preprocessing step is crucial because it creates a single, complete translation unit from multiple files and resolves all textual substitutions before actual compilation begins. For example, when kernel.c includes kernel.h, the preprocessor inserts all the function prototypes and struct definitions from kernel.h directly into the kernel.c text. Second, the compiler takes each preprocessed file and converts it into an object file (.o) containing machine code, but with unresolved references to functions or variables defined in other translation units. Finally, the linker combines all object files together, resolving cross-references between modules (like when main.c calls functions defined in kernel.c) and producing a single executable with all memory addresses properly assigned. The preprocessing step is essential because it allows modular programming - without it, each .c file would need to contain all function definitions and wouldn\'t be able to reference code from other files.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',3,2,'2025-06-27_11-14-19','Including a .c file directly with #include <kernel.c> is problematic because it violates the fundamental principle of separate compilation. When you include a .c file, the preprocessor literally copies its entire contents into the including file, which means the same code gets compiled multiple times if multiple files include it. This leads to duplicate symbol errors during linking because functions and variables get defined multiple times. Instead, we use header files (.h) to share declarations (function prototypes, struct definitions, extern variables) while keeping implementations in .c files that are compiled separately. This approach ensures each function is compiled only once, reduces compilation time, and maintains proper code organization.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',3,2,'2025-06-27_14-48-03','Including a .c file directly with #include <kernel.c> is problematic because it violates the fundamental principle of separate compilation. When you include a .c file, the preprocessor literally copies its entire contents into the including file, which means the same code gets compiled multiple times if multiple files include it. This leads to duplicate symbol errors during linking because functions and variables get defined multiple times. Instead, we use header files (.h) to share declarations (function prototypes, struct definitions, extern variables) while keeping implementations in .c files that are compiled separately. This approach ensures each function is compiled only once, reduces compilation time, and maintains proper code organization.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',3,3,'2025-06-27_11-14-19','From the user\'s perspective, creating and running a thread involves three simple steps: First, initialize the kernel by calling osKernelInitialize(), which sets up all the internal data structures and prepares the system for thread management. Second, Write your thread function that takes a void pointer parameter and contains the code you want to run (typically an infinite loop for embedded systems). Third, Create the thread by calling osCreateThread() and passing your thread function as an argument - this allocates stack space and prepares the thread for execution. Lastly, Start the kernel by calling osKernelStart(), which begins executing your thread. The kernel handles all the complex details like stack allocation, register initialization, and context switching, so the user only needs to focus on writing their application logic.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',3,3,'2025-06-27_14-48-03','From the user\'s perspective, creating and running a thread involves three simple steps: First, initialize the kernel by calling osKernelInitialize(), which sets up all the internal data structures and prepares the system for thread management. Second, Write your thread function that takes a void pointer parameter and contains the code you want to run (typically an infinite loop for embedded systems). Third, Create the thread by calling osCreateThread() and passing your thread function as an argument - this allocates stack space and prepares the thread for execution. Lastly, Start the kernel by calling osKernelStart(), which begins executing your thread. The kernel handles all the complex details like stack allocation, register initialization, and context switching, so the user only needs to focus on writing their application logic.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',3,4,'2025-06-27_11-14-19','The stack pool is a reserved region of RAM (defined by _Min_Stack_Size in the linker script, typically 0x4000 bytes) that serves as the memory reservoir for all thread stacks in the system. Currently, we allocate individual stack spaces from this pool each time a thread is created, with each thread getting its own dedicated stack space (like 0x400 bytes). This design enables multiple threads to coexist because each thread maintains its own execution context on its private stack, including local variables, function call history, and saved register values. In future labs, this stack pool will be crucial for implementing a multi-threaded OS where we can create an array of thread control blocks, each pointing to its own allocated stack within the pool, allowing the scheduler to switch between multiple threads by simply changing which stack pointer the processor uses.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',3,4,'2025-06-27_14-48-03','The stack pool is a reserved region of RAM (defined by _Min_Stack_Size in the linker script, typically 0x4000 bytes) that serves as the memory reservoir for all thread stacks in the system. Currently, we allocate individual stack spaces from this pool each time a thread is created, with each thread getting its own dedicated stack space (like 0x400 bytes). This design enables multiple threads to coexist because each thread maintains its own execution context on its private stack, including local variables, function call history, and saved register values. In future labs, this stack pool will be crucial for implementing a multi-threaded OS where we can create an array of thread control blocks, each pointing to its own allocated stack within the pool, allowing the scheduler to switch between multiple threads by simply changing which stack pointer the processor uses.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',3,5,'2025-06-27_11-14-19','The distinction between \"user\" and \"kernel\" files reflects a fundamental separation of concerns in operating system design. The kernel files (kernel.c and kernel.h) contain all the low-level system management code including thread management, stack allocation, context switching, and SVC handlers - essentially the \"engine\" of the OS that users shouldn\'t need to modify or understand in detail. The user files (main.c) contain the application-specific code including thread functions, hardware initialization, and business logic. This separation is important from the user\'s perspective because it provides a clean, simple API (like osCreateThread() and osKernelStart()) that hides complex implementation details, allows the kernel to be updated or improved without changing user code, and prevents users from accidentally breaking critical system functionality. Users can focus on writing their application logic without worrying about stack management, context switching, or other OS internals.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',3,5,'2025-06-27_14-48-03','The distinction between \"user\" and \"kernel\" files reflects a fundamental separation of concerns in operating system design. The kernel files (kernel.c and kernel.h) contain all the low-level system management code including thread management, stack allocation, context switching, and SVC handlers - essentially the \"engine\" of the OS that users shouldn\'t need to modify or understand in detail. The user files (main.c) contain the application-specific code including thread functions, hardware initialization, and business logic. This separation is important from the user\'s perspective because it provides a clean, simple API (like osCreateThread() and osKernelStart()) that hides complex implementation details, allows the kernel to be updated or improved without changing user code, and prevents users from accidentally breaking critical system functionality. Users can focus on writing their application logic without worrying about stack management, context switching, or other OS internals.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,1,'2025-07-28_20-39-54','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by treating the queue as a circular data structure where threads are dequeued from the front and enqueued at the back. When a context switch occurs, the currently running thread is moved to the rear of the queue, and the thread at the front becomes the next to run. This naturally implements the round-robin \"take turns\" behavior without needing index arithmetic or wraparound logic. The benefits are significant: First, adding and removing threads becomes O(1) operations - you simply enqueue new threads and dequeue terminated ones without shifting array elements or managing gaps. Second, the queue inherently handles the circular nature of round-robin scheduling, eliminating the need for modulo operations and current thread index tracking. Third, dynamic thread management becomes cleaner since you don\'t need to search for empty slots in a fixed-size array. Fourth, priority scheduling extensions become easier - you could maintain separate queues for different priority levels. Finally, the code becomes more intuitive and less error-prone since the data structure naturally matches the scheduling algorithm\'s behavior, reducing the likelihood of off-by-one errors or incorrect wraparound handling that can occur with array-based implementations.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,1,'2025-07-28_21-06-09','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by treating the queue as a circular data structure where threads are dequeued from the front and enqueued at the back. When a context switch occurs, the currently running thread is moved to the rear of the queue, and the thread at the front becomes the next to run. This naturally implements the round-robin \"take turns\" behavior without needing index arithmetic or wraparound logic. The benefits are significant: First, adding and removing threads becomes O(1) operations - you simply enqueue new threads and dequeue terminated ones without shifting array elements or managing gaps. Second, the queue inherently handles the circular nature of round-robin scheduling, eliminating the need for modulo operations and current thread index tracking. Third, dynamic thread management becomes cleaner since you don\'t need to search for empty slots in a fixed-size array. Fourth, priority scheduling extensions become easier - you could maintain separate queues for different priority levels. Finally, the code becomes more intuitive and less error-prone since the data structure naturally matches the scheduling algorithm\'s behavior, reducing the likelihood of off-by-one errors or incorrect wraparound handling that can occur with array-based implementations.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',4,1,'2025-07-28_21-19-37','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by treating the queue as a circular data structure where threads are dequeued from the front and enqueued at the back. When a context switch occurs, the currently running thread is moved to the rear of the queue, and the thread at the front becomes the next to run. This naturally implements the round-robin \"take turns\" behavior without needing index arithmetic or wraparound logic. However, critical analysis reveals that the benefits may be largely superficial. The current array-based scheduler with a circular index already behaves functionally like a queue - it cycles through threads in order and wraps around automatically. The array approach achieves O(1) thread switching by simply incrementing an index with modulo arithmetic, which is computationally cheaper than queue enqueue/dequeue operations that typically involve pointer manipulation and potential memory allocation. The queue implementation would actually introduce overhead that doesn\'t exist in the current system. Queue operations require additional memory management for node structures, pointer updates, and potentially dynamic memory allocation if implemented as a linked list. The array-based approach uses a fixed amount of memory and simple arithmetic operations. For thread creation and deletion, while queues offer O(1) insertion/removal, our current system rarely creates/destroys threads dynamically during normal operation - threads are typically created at startup and run throughout the system\'s lifetime. The supposed benefits like \"cleaner dynamic thread management\" and \"easier priority extensions\" are mostly stylistic rather than functional improvements. The current array-based system can handle thread states (active/inactive) just as effectively, and priority scheduling can be implemented with arrays using priority-based indexing or separate arrays per priority level. In essence, while a queue-based approach would work correctly, it would add complexity and overhead without providing meaningful functional advantages over the current efficient array-based implementation that already achieves the same round-robin behavior with simpler, faster operations.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,2,'2025-07-28_20-39-54','The PendSV_Handler orchestrates a complete context switch through a carefully choreographed sequence of register saves, C function calls, and register restores. When triggered by setting the PendSV pending bit, the handler first saves the current thread\'s context by storing registers R4-R11 onto the current PSP stack using STMDB R0!,{R4-R11}. These are the \"callee-saved\" registers that the ARM ABI requires to be preserved across function calls. Next, it calls the C function osSched(), which performs the high-level scheduling logic - determining which thread should run next, updating the current thread index, and setting PSP to point to the new thread\'s stack. This separation allows complex scheduling algorithms to be implemented in readable C code rather than assembly. Finally, the handler restores the new thread\'s context by loading registers R4-R11 from the new thread\'s stack using LDMIA R0!,{R4-R11}. The magic happens with the BX LR instruction - since LR was set to the special value 0xFFFFFFFD during thread creation, this triggers the processor\'s automatic context restoration mechanism, which pops the remaining registers (R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) from the PSP stack and jumps to the new thread\'s PC address, effectively resuming execution where that thread left off\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',4,2,'2025-07-28_21-06-09','The PendSV_Handler orchestrates a complete context switch through a carefully choreographed sequence of register saves, C function calls, and register restores. When triggered by setting the PendSV pending bit, the handler must first read the current value of the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to locate the current thread\'s stack in memory. This step is critical because the PSP contains the address where the current thread\'s context must be saved. Once the PSP value is obtained, the handler saves the current thread\'s context by storing registers R4-R11 onto this stack using STMDB R0!,{R4-R11}. These are the \"callee-saved\" registers that the ARM ABI requires to be preserved across function calls. Next, it calls the C function osSched(), which performs the high-level scheduling logic - determining which thread should run next, updating the current thread index, and setting PSP to point to the new thread\'s stack. This separation allows complex scheduling algorithms to be implemented in readable C code rather than assembly. Finally, the handler restores the new thread\'s context by loading registers R4-R11 from the new thread\'s stack using LDMIA R0!,{R4-R11}. The magic happens with the BX LR instruction - since LR was set to the special value 0xFFFFFFFD during thread creation, this triggers the processor\'s automatic context restoration mechanism, which pops the remaining registers (R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) from the PSP stack and jumps to the new thread\'s PC address, effectively resuming execution where that thread left off.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,2,'2025-07-28_21-19-37','The PendSV_Handler orchestrates a complete context switch through a carefully choreographed sequence of register saves, C function calls, and register restores. When triggered by setting the PendSV pending bit, the handler must first read the current value of the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to locate the current thread\'s stack in memory. This step is critical because the PSP contains the address where the current thread\'s context must be saved. Once the PSP value is obtained, the handler saves the current thread\'s context by storing registers R4-R11 onto this stack using STMDB R0!,{R4-R11}. These are the \"callee-saved\" registers that the ARM ABI requires to be preserved across function calls. Next, it calls the C function osSched(), which performs the high-level scheduling logic - determining which thread should run next, updating the current thread index, and setting PSP to point to the new thread\'s stack. This separation allows complex scheduling algorithms to be implemented in readable C code rather than assembly. Finally, the handler restores the new thread\'s context by loading registers R4-R11 from the new thread\'s stack using LDMIA R0!,{R4-R11}. The magic happens with the BX LR instruction - since LR was set to the special value 0xFFFFFFFD during thread creation, this triggers the processor\'s automatic context restoration mechanism, which pops the remaining registers (R0-R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) from the PSP stack and jumps to the new thread\'s PC address, effectively resuming execution where that thread left off.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,3,'2025-07-28_20-39-54','This priority-only scheduling approach suffers from the critical problem of starvation, where low-priority threads may never execute if high-priority threads are always ready to run. This creates an unfair system where important but lower-priority tasks (like background maintenance, logging, or user interface updates) could be indefinitely postponed, potentially causing system instability or poor user experience. Additionally, this approach is vulnerable to priority inversion, where a high-priority thread becomes blocked waiting for a resource held by a low-priority thread, while medium-priority threads continue running and prevent the low-priority thread from completing and releasing the resource. This can cause unpredictable delays and system deadlocks. The system also becomes prone to priority inheritance problems and convoy effects, where the execution order becomes heavily dependent on priority assignments that may not reflect actual system needs. Real-time systems require more sophisticated approaches like priority aging (gradually increasing priority of waiting threads), time-slicing within priority levels, or hybrid algorithms that combine priority with fairness mechanisms. Furthermore, pure priority scheduling can lead to thrashing if priorities are poorly assigned, causing excessive context switching between competing high-priority threads while starving lower-priority system maintenance tasks that are essential for long-term stability.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',4,3,'2025-07-28_21-06-09','This priority-only scheduling approach is fundamentally flawed because it would result in only one task ever running - the highest-priority task. Once the scheduler identifies the thread with the highest priority value, it will continuously select that same thread for execution, regardless of whether other threads are ready to run. This means that as long as the highest-priority thread remains in a runnable state, no context switching will occur to other threads, effectively turning the multitasking system into a single-threaded system. The scheduler\'s \"always choose highest priority\" logic creates a situation where lower-priority threads are completely starved - they will never get CPU time even if they are ready to execute and have important work to do. This defeats the entire purpose of having a multitasking operating system, as multiple threads cannot share the CPU. The system becomes unable to provide the concurrent execution that multitasking systems are designed to deliver. Even critical system threads like those handling user input, system maintenance, or background tasks would be indefinitely blocked if they have lower priority than the currently running thread. This scheduling policy essentially breaks the multitasking capability of the OS, making it impossible for multiple threads to coexist and share processor time effectively.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,3,'2025-07-28_21-19-37','This priority-only scheduling approach is fundamentally flawed because it would result in only one task ever running - the highest-priority task. Once the scheduler identifies the thread with the highest priority value, it will continuously select that same thread for execution, regardless of whether other threads are ready to run. This means that as long as the highest-priority thread remains in a runnable state, no context switching will occur to other threads, effectively turning the multitasking system into a single-threaded system. The scheduler\'s \"always choose highest priority\" logic creates a situation where lower-priority threads are completely starved - they will never get CPU time even if they are ready to execute and have important work to do. This defeats the entire purpose of having a multitasking operating system, as multiple threads cannot share the CPU. The system becomes unable to provide the concurrent execution that multitasking systems are designed to deliver. Even critical system threads like those handling user input, system maintenance, or background tasks would be indefinitely blocked if they have lower priority than the currently running thread. This scheduling policy essentially breaks the multitasking capability of the OS, making it impossible for multiple threads to coexist and share processor time effectively.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,4,'2025-07-28_20-39-54','Calling C functions from assembly handlers provides substantial maintainability and development benefits. C code is far more readable and debuggable than assembly, allowing complex scheduling algorithms, system call logic, and data structure manipulation to be implemented with standard programming constructs like loops, conditionals, and function calls. This makes the codebase more accessible to developers who aren\'t assembly experts and significantly reduces development time for implementing new features or debugging issues. The modularity benefit is crucial - separating low-level context switching (assembly) from high-level policy decisions (C) creates clean interfaces and allows scheduling algorithms to be modified without touching the delicate assembly code. C also provides type safety, compiler optimizations, and access to standard libraries and debugging tools. However, there are performance downsides: function calls introduce overhead through stack frame setup, parameter passing, and potential register spilling. In interrupt contexts, this extra latency can impact real-time performance. Assembly implementations can be more deterministic with predictable execution times, which is crucial for hard real-time systems. The hybrid approach we used strikes an optimal balance - assembly handles the time-critical, hardware-specific register manipulation that must be precisely controlled, while C handles the higher-level logic that benefits from readability and maintainability. This separation of concerns maximizes both performance and code quality, making the system both efficient and maintainable.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,4,'2025-07-28_21-06-09','Calling C functions from assembly handlers provides substantial maintainability and development benefits. C code is far more readable and debuggable than assembly, allowing complex scheduling algorithms, system call logic, and data structure manipulation to be implemented with standard programming constructs like loops, conditionals, and function calls. This makes the codebase more accessible to developers who aren\'t assembly experts and significantly reduces development time for implementing new features or debugging issues. The modularity benefit is crucial - separating low-level context switching (assembly) from high-level policy decisions (C) creates clean interfaces and allows scheduling algorithms to be modified without touching the delicate assembly code. C also provides type safety, compiler optimizations, and access to standard libraries and debugging tools. However, there are performance downsides: function calls introduce overhead through stack frame setup, parameter passing, and potential register spilling. In interrupt contexts, this extra latency can impact real-time performance. Assembly implementations can be more deterministic with predictable execution times, which is crucial for hard real-time systems. The hybrid approach we used strikes an optimal balance - assembly handles the time-critical, hardware-specific register manipulation that must be precisely controlled, while C handles the higher-level logic that benefits from readability and maintainability. This separation of concerns maximizes both performance and code quality, making the system both efficient and maintainable.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,4,'2025-07-28_21-19-37','Calling C functions from assembly handlers provides substantial maintainability and development benefits. C code is far more readable and debuggable than assembly, allowing complex scheduling algorithms, system call logic, and data structure manipulation to be implemented with standard programming constructs like loops, conditionals, and function calls. This makes the codebase more accessible to developers who aren\'t assembly experts and significantly reduces development time for implementing new features or debugging issues. The modularity benefit is crucial - separating low-level context switching (assembly) from high-level policy decisions (C) creates clean interfaces and allows scheduling algorithms to be modified without touching the delicate assembly code. C also provides type safety, compiler optimizations, and access to standard libraries and debugging tools. However, there are performance downsides: function calls introduce overhead through stack frame setup, parameter passing, and potential register spilling. In interrupt contexts, this extra latency can impact real-time performance. Assembly implementations can be more deterministic with predictable execution times, which is crucial for hard real-time systems. The hybrid approach we used strikes an optimal balance - assembly handles the time-critical, hardware-specific register manipulation that must be precisely controlled, while C handles the higher-level logic that benefits from readability and maintainability. This separation of concerns maximizes both performance and code quality, making the system both efficient and maintainable.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,5,'2025-07-28_20-39-54','The OS would technically still function, but it would operate in a degenerate state that defeats the purpose of having a multitasking operating system. When the single thread calls osYield(), the scheduler\'s osSched() function would execute its round-robin algorithm, but since there\'s only one active thread, it would simply \"switch\" back to the same thread after going through the scheduling logic. This creates unnecessary overhead - every yield triggers a full context switch including PendSV interrupt handling, register saving/restoring, and scheduler execution, only to return to the identical thread. The system would experience performance degradation compared to a simple bare-metal loop because of this context switching overhead with no benefit. From a system state perspective, all the OS data structures (thread array, current thread index, etc.) would remain valid, and the timing mechanisms (SysTick, preemptive scheduling) would continue operating normally. If the thread\'s timeslice expires, the preemptive scheduler would also \"switch\" to the same thread. However, this configuration wastes resources and provides no concurrency benefits. The system would be functionally equivalent to a single-threaded program but with significantly more complexity and overhead. It demonstrates that while the OS infrastructure can handle edge cases gracefully, the real value of multitasking comes from having multiple threads that can share CPU time and provide true concurrent execution of different tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,5,'2025-07-28_21-06-09','The OS would technically still function, but it would operate in a degenerate state that defeats the purpose of having a multitasking operating system. When the single thread calls osYield(), the scheduler\'s osSched() function would execute its round-robin algorithm, but since there\'s only one active thread, it would simply \"switch\" back to the same thread after going through the scheduling logic. This creates unnecessary overhead - every yield triggers a full context switch including PendSV interrupt handling, register saving/restoring, and scheduler execution, only to return to the identical thread. The system would experience performance degradation compared to a simple bare-metal loop because of this context switching overhead with no benefit. From a system state perspective, all the OS data structures (thread array, current thread index, etc.) would remain valid, and the timing mechanisms (SysTick, preemptive scheduling) would continue operating normally. If the thread\'s timeslice expires, the preemptive scheduler would also \"switch\" to the same thread. However, this configuration wastes resources and provides no concurrency benefits. The system would be functionally equivalent to a single-threaded program but with significantly more complexity and overhead. It demonstrates that while the OS infrastructure can handle edge cases gracefully, the real value of multitasking comes from having multiple threads that can share CPU time and provide true concurrent execution of different tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',4,5,'2025-07-28_21-19-37','The OS would technically still function, but it would operate in a degenerate state that defeats the purpose of having a multitasking operating system. When the single thread calls osYield(), the scheduler\'s osSched() function would execute its round-robin algorithm, but since there\'s only one active thread, it would simply \"switch\" back to the same thread after going through the scheduling logic. This creates unnecessary overhead - every yield triggers a full context switch including PendSV interrupt handling, register saving/restoring, and scheduler execution, only to return to the identical thread. The system would experience performance degradation compared to a simple bare-metal loop because of this context switching overhead with no benefit. From a system state perspective, all the OS data structures (thread array, current thread index, etc.) would remain valid, and the timing mechanisms (SysTick, preemptive scheduling) would continue operating normally. If the thread\'s timeslice expires, the preemptive scheduler would also \"switch\" to the same thread. However, this configuration wastes resources and provides no concurrency benefits. The system would be functionally equivalent to a single-threaded program but with significantly more complexity and overhead. It demonstrates that while the OS infrastructure can handle edge cases gracefully, the real value of multitasking comes from having multiple threads that can share CPU time and provide true concurrent execution of different tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',5,1,'2025-07-28_20-46-04','The user passes an argument pointer to osCreateThread() or osCreateThreadWithDeadline(). The OS stores this pointer in the thread\'s stack frame during creation, specifically placing it in register R0\'s position on the stack. When the thread starts, the ARM processor automatically loads R0 from the stack, making the argument available as the function parameter. This works because ARM\'s calling convention uses R0 for the first function parameter. The OS essentially \"fakes\" a function call by pre-loading the stack with the correct register values, including the argument pointer in R0\'s slot, so when the thread begins execution, it appears as if it was called normally with the argument.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',5,1,'2025-07-28_21-01-09','The user must first allocate memory for the data they want to pass as an argument, ensuring the data exists at a valid memory location that will remain accessible throughout the thread\'s lifetime. Only after allocating this memory can the user obtain a pointer to it and pass this pointer to osCreateThread() or osCreateThreadWithDeadline(). The user cannot simply pass an uninitialized pointer - they must ensure the pointer references actual allocated data. The OS then stores this argument pointer in the thread\'s stack frame during creation, specifically placing it in register R0\'s position on the stack. When the thread starts, the ARM processor automatically loads R0 from the stack, making the argument available as the function parameter. This works because ARM\'s calling convention uses R0 for the first function parameter. The OS essentially \"fakes\" a function call by pre-loading the stack with the correct register values, including the argument pointer in R0\'s slot, so when the thread begins execution, it appears as if it was called normally with the argument. The key requirement is that the user must have properly allocated memory for the argument before passing its pointer, otherwise the thread would receive a pointer to invalid or unallocated memory.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',5,2,'2025-07-28_20-46-04','Calling osYield() in context-switching functions creates infinite recursion and stack overflow. osYield() triggers an SVC interrupt, which calls SVC_Handler_Main, which triggers PendSV for context switching. If you call osYield() within these handlers, you\'re essentially calling the same interrupt handler from within itself. This creates a nested interrupt scenario where each call adds another stack frame without ever returning, eventually exhausting stack space. Additionally, ARM processors have interrupt priority rules that prevent lower-priority interrupts from interrupting higher-priority ones, potentially causing the system to hang. Context-switching functions must complete their work and return naturally to avoid these issues.\n',0,'s25'),('s9chow',5,2,'2025-07-28_21-01-09','Calling osYield() within these context-switching functions causes immediate stack corruption and system hardfault. The problem occurs because osYield() triggers an SVC interrupt, but when you\'re already executing inside an interrupt handler (like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler), attempting to trigger another SVC interrupt while the interrupt context is still active corrupts the processor\'s stack state. Specifically, the ARM processor expects to use the current stack to save the interrupt context for the new SVC call, but the stack is already in use by the currently executing interrupt handler. This stack corruption leads directly to a hardfault because the processor cannot properly manage the overlapping interrupt contexts. The system becomes unstable because the stack pointer and saved register values become corrupted, making it impossible for the processor to return properly from either interrupt. Unlike normal nested interrupts that are handled by hardware priority mechanisms, calling osYield() from within an interrupt creates an artificial interrupt trigger that the hardware cannot manage safely, resulting in immediate system failure due to stack corruption and subsequent hardfault.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',5,3,'2025-07-28_20-46-04','Benefits:0.1ms clock provides 10x better timing resolution and faster response to events. The OS can react to changes within 0.1ms instead of 1ms, enabling more precise real-time behavior and smoother multitasking with finer-grained time slices. Downsides: 10x more frequent interrupts consume significantly more CPU overhead, reducing available processing time for actual work. Higher interrupt frequency increases power consumption, reduces battery life, and may cause timing-sensitive operations to be interrupted more frequently. The system becomes less efficient overall, with more time spent in interrupt handlers rather than executing user threads. Context switching overhead becomes a larger percentage of total CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',5,3,'2025-07-28_21-01-09','Benefits:0.1ms clock provides 10x better timing resolution and faster response to events. The OS can react to changes within 0.1ms instead of 1ms, enabling more precise real-time behavior and smoother multitasking with finer-grained time slices. Downsides: 10x more frequent interrupts consume significantly more CPU overhead, reducing available processing time for actual work. Higher interrupt frequency increases power consumption, reduces battery life, and may cause timing-sensitive operations to be interrupted more frequently. The system becomes less efficient overall, with more time spent in interrupt handlers rather than executing user threads. Context switching overhead becomes a larger percentage of total CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',5,4,'2025-07-28_20-46-04','User perspective: Cooperative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily call osYield() to share CPU time - poorly written threads can monopolize the processor. Pre-emptive multitasking automatically switches threads based on time slices, ensuring fair CPU sharing regardless of thread behavior. OS perspective: Cooperative switching only occurs when threads call osYield(), triggering SVC interrupts. The OS simply responds to voluntary yield requests. Pre-emptive switching requires the OS to actively monitor each thread\'s runtime using SysTick interrupts, decrementing timers every millisecond and forcing context switches when time expires. Pre-emptive requires more OS overhead but provides better system responsiveness and prevents thread starvation.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',5,4,'2025-07-28_21-01-09','User perspective: Cooperative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily call osYield() to share CPU time - poorly written threads can monopolize the processor. Pre-emptive multitasking automatically switches threads based on time slices, ensuring fair CPU sharing regardless of thread behavior. OS perspective: Cooperative switching only occurs when threads call osYield(), triggering SVC interrupts. The OS simply responds to voluntary yield requests. Pre-emptive switching requires the OS to actively monitor each thread\'s runtime using SysTick interrupts, decrementing timers every millisecond and forcing context switches when time expires. Pre-emptive requires more OS overhead but provides better system responsiveness and prevents thread starvation.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',5,5,'2025-07-28_20-46-04','Both mechanisms use the same underlying context-switching infrastructure (PendSV_Handler) but trigger it differently. Cooperative switching occurs when threads voluntarily call osYield(), which triggers an SVC interrupt that sets the PendSV pending bit. Pre-emptive switching occurs when SysTick_Handler detects a thread\'s timeslice has expired and automatically sets the same PendSV pending bit. Both paths converge at PendSV_Handler, which performs identical context switching regardless of the trigger source. The key is that both reset the new thread\'s runtime to its full timeslice, ensuring consistent timing behavior. This unified approach allows threads to mix yielding and time-based switching seamlessly within the same system.\n',1,'s25'),('s9chow',5,5,'2025-07-28_21-01-09','Both mechanisms use the same underlying context-switching infrastructure (PendSV_Handler) but trigger it differently. Cooperative switching occurs when threads voluntarily call osYield(), which triggers an SVC interrupt that sets the PendSV pending bit. Pre-emptive switching occurs when SysTick_Handler detects a thread\'s timeslice has expired and automatically sets the same PendSV pending bit. Both paths converge at PendSV_Handler, which performs identical context switching regardless of the trigger source. The key is that both reset the new thread\'s runtime to its full timeslice, ensuring consistent timing behavior. This unified approach allows threads to mix yielding and time-based switching seamlessly within the same system.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',1,1,'2025-05-18_13-35-53','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is used to make interaction with the hardware simpler as it handles specific register addresses, interrupt handling and timing requirements. The HAL provides a standard set of functions for accessing hardware resources, making it easier to write portable code.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',1,1,'2025-05-26_14-24-27','The HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is used to make interaction with the hardware simpler as it handles specific register addresses, interrupt handling and timing requirements. The HAL provides a standard set of functions for accessing hardware resources, making it easier to write portable code.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',1,2,'2025-05-18_13-35-53','This function writes a character to the specified IO channel. It is needed for running basic output operations since the ‘printf’ function calls the __io_putchar function multiple times to output a string as individual characters.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',1,2,'2025-05-26_14-24-27','This function writes a character to the specified IO device, in this case UART2. It is needed for running basic output operations, the ‘printf’ function calls the __io_putchar function multiple times to output a string as individual characters. This is needed because the entire stdio library relies on helper functions to make it interact with the hardware. The __io_putchar function is basically a wrapper function that allows the programmer to interact with the hardware and output data to the UART2 pin. Since not all hardware setups are identical, different projects may utilise different ports, e.g. UART1. This can be adjusted in the __io_putchar function definition so the device knows exactly where to direct the data to.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',1,3,'2025-05-18_13-35-53','Using a debugger allows us to directly view the register values rather than having to add code. Using the printf function may also cause register values to be altered, rendering the data uselss.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',1,3,'2025-05-26_14-24-27','Using a debugger allows us to directly view the register values rather than having to add code. Using the printf function may also cause register values to be altered, rendering the data useless. The printf function can be very useful in debugging when we want to check which section of code is being executed or may be causing issues. Simple printf statements can be added to the function/section being debugged, if the statement is not output we know that there is an issue there.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',1,4,'2025-05-18_13-35-53','We know that the main stack size is 0x400, the stack grows downward, and that the PSP is right after the main stack. Knowing the initial location of the MSP gives us the starting point, then we need to move 0x400 bytes downwards (-0x400) to get the new value for PSP_val.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',1,4,'2025-05-26_14-24-27','We know that the main stack size is 0x400, the stack grows downward, and that the PSP is right after the main stack. Knowing the initial location of the MSP gives us the starting point, then we need to move 0x400 bytes downwards (-0x400) to get the new value for PSP_val.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',1,5,'2025-05-18_13-35-53','First, we need to ensure that there is enough space in the stack for the number of new stacks needed otherwise the MAX_STACK_SIZE would need to be adjusted. Now we can get the address of each stack by using the following formula:\nWhere j is the number of the stack we want to access, starting with 1. We subtract from the initial position because stack progress downwards.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',1,5,'2025-05-26_14-24-27','First, we need to ensure that there is enough space in the stack for the number of new stacks needed otherwise the MAX_STACK_SIZE would need to be adjusted. Now we can get the address of each stack by using the following formula:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL – MAIN_STACK_SIZE – (j-1)THREAD_STACK_SIZE\nWhere j is the number of the stack we want to access, starting with 1. We subtract from the initial position, MSP_INIT_VAL, because stack progress downwards. This value is at 0x0 by default.\nWe would need to define the size of each stack needed, depending on the requirements, this could be a variable, THREAD_STACK_SIZE or just a value, e.g. 0x400.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,1,'2025-06-22_16-33-59','The PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next. When the PC is changed to hold the function pointer, it stores the address of the print continuously function. Consequently, the processor assumes that the next instruction to run is at the address of the print function and executes it, resulting in “Hello, PC!” being printed continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,1,'2025-06-22_20-09-15','The PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next. When the PC is changed to hold the function pointer, it stores the address of the print continuously function. Consequently, the processor assumes that the next instruction to run is at the address of the print function and executes it, resulting in “Hello, PC!” being printed continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,1,'2025-06-22_22-32-16','The PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next. When the PC is changed to hold the function pointer, it stores the address of the print continuously function. Consequently, the processor assumes that the next instruction to run is at the address of the print function and executes it, resulting in “Hello, PC!” being printed continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,1,'2025-06-22_22-53-36','The PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next. When the PC is changed to hold the function pointer, it stores the address of the print continuously function. Consequently, the processor assumes that the next instruction to run is at the address of the print function and executes it, resulting in “Hello, PC!” being printed continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,1,'2025-06-23_14-13-29','The PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next. When the PC is changed to hold the function pointer, it stores the address of the print continuously function. Consequently, the processor assumes that the next instruction to run is at the address of the print function and executes it, resulting in “Hello, PC!” being printed continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,1,'2025-06-23_16-34-47','The PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next. When the PC is changed to hold the function pointer, it stores the address of the print continuously function. Consequently, the processor assumes that the next instruction to run is at the address of the print function and executes it, resulting in “Hello, PC!” being printed continuously.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',2,1,'2025-06-23_16-52-21','The PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next. When the PC is changed to hold the function pointer, it stores the address of the print continuously function.  Consequently, the processor assumes that the next instruction to run is at the address of the print function and executes it, resulting in “Hello, PC!” being printed continuously. By manually assigning the function’s address to PC, we simulated a function call but without the usual context-saving. In a cormal function call, the CPU updates the PC and stores the return address in the LR so that the program knows where to return after the function completes. In contrast, by updating the PC manually, we skip saving any return information and assign an arbitrary address to LR (where the return point is usually stored) since the function is a continuous loop and we do not intend for it to ever return. So if the function were to return from our function, it would result in a hardfault. Therefore, directly assigning the PC to a function pointer is functionally equivalent to an unstable function call, where execution begins at the specified address but has no valid return path.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,2,'2025-06-22_16-33-59','A system call enables userspace programs to request the kernel to perform tasks. It is a function that lives in a fixed space in memory and does not get compiled into the userspace program’s code. Userspace program’s have limited privileges, however a system call can be used to carry out tasks that require special privileges by the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',2,2,'2025-06-22_20-09-15','A system call enables userspace programs to request the kernel to perform tasks. It is a function that lives in a fixed space in memory and does not get compiled into the userspace program’s code. Userspace program’s have limited privileges, however a system call can be used to carry out tasks that require special privileges by the kernel. This allows us to run functions like runfirstthread where the register values are manipulated to run a function rather than calling it within the program itself. The system calls preserve security whilst allowing us to safely access memory, invalid instructions can cause faults but will not crash the system. Without system calls userspace programs would be able to directly access memory, since all the code will have the same privileges; a bug in any one part of the code may lead to system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,2,'2025-06-22_22-32-16','A system call enables userspace programs to request the kernel to perform tasks. It is a function that lives in a fixed space in memory and does not get compiled into the userspace program’s code. Userspace program’s have limited privileges, however a system call can be used to carry out tasks that require special privileges by the kernel. This allows us to run functions like runfirstthread where the register values are manipulated to run a function rather than calling it within the program itself. The system calls preserve security whilst allowing us to safely access memory, invalid instructions can cause faults but will not crash the system. Without system calls userspace programs would be able to directly access memory, since all the code will have the same privileges; a bug in any one part of the code may lead to system crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',2,2,'2025-06-22_22-53-36','A system call enables user code to request the kernel to perform tasks. It is a function that lives in a fixed space in memory and does not get compiled into the code. User code has limited privileges; however, a system call can be used to carry out tasks that require special privileges by the kernel by temporarily switching the processor to kernel mode. This allows us to run functions where the register values need to be manipulated. The system calls preserve security whilst allowing us to safely access memory, where invalid instructions can cause faults but will not crash the system. Without system calls, user’s code would be able to directly access memory without safety measures in place, since all the code will have the same privileges; a bug in any one part of the code may lead to system crashes.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',2,2,'2025-06-23_14-13-29','A system call acts as an interface between the user code and the kernel. Its purpose is to allow user space programs to ask the kernel to do something. It allows user programs to request access to low-level functions such as working on memory. The kernel can either refuse the request or carry out the task requested by the user space program depending on how it is written and what the system call is. A system call is essentially a function, accessed via an interrupt. The function defining the system call though is not compiled into the user program’s code. It rather lives in a fixed place in memory. So when a system call is triggered, we only need to share the address and call the function pointer stored there. Without system calls and the appropriate security in place, user programs would be able to directly access memory and corrupt system data, leading to the system crashing or a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,2,'2025-06-23_16-34-47','A system call acts as an interface between the user code and the kernel. Its purpose is to allow user space programs to ask the kernel to do something. It allows user programs to request access to low-level functions such as working on memory. The kernel can either refuse the request or carry out the task requested by the user space program depending on how it is written and what the system call is. A system call is essentially a function, accessed via an interrupt. The function defining the system call though is not compiled into the user program’s code. It rather lives in a fixed place in memory. So when a system call is triggered, we only need to share the address and call the function pointer stored there. Without system calls and the appropriate security in place, user programs would be able to directly access memory and corrupt system data, leading to the system crashing or a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,2,'2025-06-23_16-52-21','A system call acts as an interface between the user code and the kernel. Its purpose is to allow user space programs to ask the kernel to do something. It allows user programs to request access to low-level functions such as working on memory. The kernel can either refuse the request or carry out the task requested by the user space program depending on how it is written and what the system call is. A system call is essentially a function, accessed via an interrupt. The function defining the system call though is not compiled into the user program’s code. It rather lives in a fixed place in memory. So when a system call is triggered, we only need to share the address and call the function pointer stored there. Without system calls and the appropriate security in place, user programs would be able to directly access memory and corrupt system data, leading to the system crashing or a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,3,'2025-06-22_16-33-59','The thread’s stack was set up by pushing values to registers, which would exist if the thread was already running. The stack is set up by first obtaining a new stack pointer that will be used for the PSP. We assigned values to the stack in the same order as they would be popped off by the interrupt handler to the registers so that PC holds the address of the thread’s function. Once the interrupt is done, the next instruction will be to run that function.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',2,3,'2025-06-22_20-09-15','Thread’s stack was set up by manually placing key register values onto the stack, in the exact order that they would be popped off. Once the stack is ready and the PSP is pointing to it, the system is made to believe that it is “resuming” operations from the thread as if it were interrupted from it. This is done by setting a specific value for the xPSR to enable Thumb mode and the address of the function to the PC. Values for the other registers are also provided since the CPU is expecting them and will attempt to pop them from the stack regardless. If dummy values for those registers are not provided, it could result in the system crashing or a hard fault. This allows the system to fully restore the execution context of the thread, without which the program flow would be interrupted. We are essentially forming a synthetic snapshot for the CPU to restore from it and begin execution from the specified function. This is needed for enabling thread switching, ensuring that the program continues correctly as if the thread had always been running.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,3,'2025-06-22_22-32-16','Thread’s stack was set up by manually placing key register values onto the stack, in the exact order that they would be popped off. Once the stack is ready and the PSP is pointing to it, the system is made to believe that it is “resuming” operations from the thread as if it were interrupted from it. This is done by setting a specific value for the xPSR to enable Thumb mode and the address of the function to the PC. Values for the other registers are also provided since the CPU is expecting them and will attempt to pop them from the stack regardless. If dummy values for those registers are not provided, it could result in the system crashing or a hard fault. This allows the system to fully restore the execution context of the thread, without which the program flow would be interrupted. We are essentially forming a synthetic snapshot for the CPU to restore from it and begin execution from the specified function. This is needed for enabling thread switching, ensuring that the program continues correctly as if the thread had always been running.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,3,'2025-06-22_22-53-36','Thread’s stack was set up by manually placing key register values onto the stack, in the exact order that they would be popped off. Once the stack is ready and the PSP is pointing to it, the system is made to believe that it is “resuming” operations from the thread as if it were interrupted from it. This is done by setting a specific value for the xPSR to enable Thumb mode and the address of the function to the PC. Values for the other registers are also provided since the CPU is expecting them and will attempt to pop them from the stack regardless. If dummy values for those registers are not provided, it could result in the system crashing or a hard fault. This allows the system to fully restore the execution context of the thread, without which the program flow would be interrupted. We are essentially forming a synthetic snapshot for the CPU to restore from it and begin execution from the specified function. This is needed for enabling thread switching, ensuring that the program continues correctly as if the thread had always been running.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',2,3,'2025-06-23_14-13-29','Thread’s stack was set up by manually placing key register values onto the stack, in the exact order that they would be popped off. Once the stack is ready and the PSP is pointing to it, the system is made to believe that it is “resuming” operations from the thread as if it were interrupted from it. This is done by setting a specific value for the xPSR to enable Thumb mode and the address of the function to the PC. Values for the other registers are also provided since the CPU is expecting them and will attempt to pop them from the stack regardless. If dummy values for those registers are not provided, it could result in the system crashing or a hard fault since key register values would get swapped and the processor would attempt to access invalid addresses. The threads stack allows the system to fully restore the context of the thread, without which the program flow would be interrupted. We are essentially forming a synthetic snapshot for the CPU to restore from it and begin execution from the specified function. This is needed for enabling thread switching, ensuring that the program continues correctly as if the thread had always been running.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',2,3,'2025-06-23_16-34-47','Thread’s stack was set up by manually placing key register values onto the stack, in the exact order that they would be popped off. For each stack, 1KB of memory is allocated, since stacks move downwards in STM, the starting address of the previous stack is noted and we subtract 0x400 from it to obtain the new stack address. The stack set up involves providing a specific value for the xPSR to enable Thumb mode and the address of the function to the PC. Values for the other registers are also provided since the CPU is expecting them and will attempt to pop them from the stack regardless. If dummy values for those registers are not provided, it could result in the system crashing or a hard fault since key register values would get swapped and the processor would attempt to access invalid addresses. Once the stack is set up, the system is made to believe that it is “resuming” operations from the thread’s stack as if it were interrupted from it. The threads stack allows the system to fully restore the context of the thread, without which the program flow would be interrupted. We are essentially forming a synthetic snapshot for the CPU to restore from it and begin execution from the specified function. This is needed for enabling thread switching, ensuring that the program continues correctly as if the thread had always been running. If we didn’t reserve stack memory beforehand and just pushed register values onto an undefined region, the processor might overwrite critical data or access invalid addresses, leading to stack corruption, hard faults, or system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,3,'2025-06-23_16-52-21','Thread’s stack was set up by manually placing key register values onto the stack, in the exact order that they would be popped off. For each stack, 1KB of memory is allocated, since stacks move downwards in STM, the starting address of the previous stack is noted and we subtract 0x400 from it to obtain the new stack address. The stack set up involves providing a specific value for the xPSR to enable Thumb mode and the address of the function to the PC. Values for the other registers are also provided since the CPU is expecting them and will attempt to pop them from the stack regardless. If dummy values for those registers are not provided, it could result in the system crashing or a hard fault since key register values would get swapped and the processor would attempt to access invalid addresses. Once the stack is set up, the system is made to believe that it is “resuming” operations from the thread’s stack as if it were interrupted from it. The threads stack allows the system to fully restore the context of the thread, without which the program flow would be interrupted. We are essentially forming a synthetic snapshot for the CPU to restore from it and begin execution from the specified function. This is needed for enabling thread switching, ensuring that the program continues correctly as if the thread had always been running. If we didn’t reserve stack memory beforehand and just pushed register values onto an undefined region, the processor might overwrite critical data or access invalid addresses, leading to stack corruption, hard faults, or system crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,4,'2025-06-22_16-33-59','At this point, the stackptr is as at the location needed to store the thread’s context into the registers. The PSP needs to be set to the stackptr so that when the runFirstThread function is called, the popping of the values initiates from that address. If we did not do this, the values stored in the stack we setup will not be correctly popped off and incorrect values will be assigned to the registers with the next instruction address (PC) being an undefined address, causing the code to crash.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,4,'2025-06-22_20-09-15','At this point, the stackptr is as at the location needed to store the thread’s context into the registers. The PSP needs to be set to the stackptr so that when the runFirstThread function is called, the popping of the values initiates from that address. If we did not do this, the values stored in the stack we setup will not be correctly popped off and incorrect values will be assigned to the registers with the next instruction address (PC) being an undefined address, causing the code to crash.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',2,4,'2025-06-22_22-32-16','At this point, the stackptr is as at the location needed to store the thread’s context into the registers. The PSP needs to be set to the stackptr so that when the runFirstThread function is called, the popping of the values initiates from that address. If we did not do this, the values stored in the stack we setup will not be correctly popped off and incorrect values will be assigned to the registers with the next instruction address (PC) being an undefined address, causing the code to crash. The CPU’s default stack and the thread’s stack need to be independent, if not, they can interfere with each other and corrupt system-level data, leading to crashes and faults. The MSP is used by the OS, in privileged mode, during system resets, exception handling, etc. while the PSP is used in thread mode. This prevents the system-level colliding with the userspace code, allowing us to switch contexts safely when using the “synthetic” stack frame. We can clearly see that the code crashes when the PSP is not set by noticing overwritten variables from unrelated parts of the code, in debug mode contents of the stack being corrupted and overlapping since they are using the same stack.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,4,'2025-06-22_22-53-36','At this point, the stackptr is as at the location needed to store the thread’s context into the registers. The PSP needs to be set to the stackptr so that when the runFirstThread function is called, the popping of the values initiates from that address. If we did not do this, the values stored in the stack we setup will not be correctly popped off and incorrect values will be assigned to the registers with the next instruction address (PC) being an undefined address, causing the code to crash. The CPU’s default stack and the thread’s stack need to be independent, if not, they can interfere with each other and corrupt system-level data, leading to crashes and faults. The MSP is used by the OS, in privileged mode, during system resets, exception handling, etc. while the PSP is used in thread mode. This prevents the system-level colliding with the userspace code, allowing us to switch contexts safely when using the “synthetic” stack frame. We can clearly see that the code crashes when the PSP is not set by noticing overwritten variables from unrelated parts of the code, in debug mode contents of the stack being corrupted and overlapping since they are using the same stack.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,4,'2025-06-23_14-13-29','At this point, the stackptr is as at the location needed to store the thread’s context into the registers. The PSP needs to be set to the stackptr so that when the runFirstThread function is called, the popping of the values initiates from that address. If we did not do this, the values stored in the stack we setup will not be correctly popped off and incorrect values will be assigned to the registers with the next instruction address (PC) being an undefined address, causing the code to crash. The CPU’s default stack and the thread’s stack need to be independent, if not, they can interfere with each other and corrupt system-level data, leading to crashes and faults. The MSP is used by the OS, in privileged mode, during system resets, exception handling, etc. while the PSP is used in thread mode. This prevents the system-level colliding with the userspace code, allowing us to switch contexts safely when using the “synthetic” stack frame. We can clearly see that the code crashes when the PSP is not set by noticing overwritten variables from unrelated parts of the code, in debug mode contents of the stack being corrupted and overlapping since they are using the same stack.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,4,'2025-06-23_16-34-47','At this point, the stackptr is as at the location needed to store the thread’s context into the registers. The PSP needs to be set to the stackptr so that when the runFirstThread function is called, the popping of the values initiates from that address. If we did not do this, the values stored in the stack we setup will not be correctly popped off and incorrect values will be assigned to the registers with the next instruction address (PC) being an undefined address, causing the code to crash. The CPU’s default stack and the thread’s stack need to be independent, if not, they can interfere with each other and corrupt system-level data, leading to crashes and faults. The MSP is used by the OS, in privileged mode, during system resets, exception handling, etc. while the PSP is used in thread mode. This prevents the system-level colliding with the userspace code, allowing us to switch contexts safely when using the “synthetic” stack frame. We can clearly see that the code crashes when the PSP is not set by noticing overwritten variables from unrelated parts of the code, in debug mode contents of the stack being corrupted and overlapping since they are using the same stack.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,4,'2025-06-23_16-52-21','At this point, the stackptr is as at the location needed to store the thread’s context into the registers. The PSP needs to be set to the stackptr so that when the runFirstThread function is called, the popping of the values initiates from that address. If we did not do this, the values stored in the stack we setup will not be correctly popped off and incorrect values will be assigned to the registers with the next instruction address (PC) being an undefined address, causing the code to crash. The CPU’s default stack and the thread’s stack need to be independent, if not, they can interfere with each other and corrupt system-level data, leading to crashes and faults. The MSP is used by the OS, in privileged mode, during system resets, exception handling, etc. while the PSP is used in thread mode. This prevents the system-level colliding with the userspace code, allowing us to switch contexts safely when using the “synthetic” stack frame. We can clearly see that the code crashes when the PSP is not set by noticing overwritten variables from unrelated parts of the code, in debug mode contents of the stack being corrupted and overlapping since they are using the same stack.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,5,'2025-06-22_16-33-59','If the function returns, the code will likely crash. Since the thread was manually setup to run the function, the LR is holding a garbage/arbitrary value. The LR is used to hold the address of the next instruction to be carried out after the instruction stored at the address defined by the PC, since the LR in our context is arbitrary, the system will attempt to the carry out the instruction at an invalid address, resulting in a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',2,5,'2025-06-22_20-09-15','If the function returns, the code will likely crash. The thread was manually setup to run the print function; in which we assigned an invalid address to LR (return address). If the function did not loop continuously, the CPU would attempt to go to an invalid address, stored in LR, and crash the system. If, however, the function was called by a normal call, a real address would be stored in LR and the system would be able to access that address normally and resume operations from the valid return address without crashing. The root issue is that the thread function is not meant to return, if they do return, the CPU tries to continue execution from a meaningless LR, leading to fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,5,'2025-06-22_22-32-16','If the function returns, the code will likely crash. The thread was manually setup to run the print function; in which we assigned an invalid address to LR (return address). If the function did not loop continuously, the CPU would attempt to go to an invalid address, stored in LR, and crash the system. If, however, the function was called by a normal call, a real address would be stored in LR and the system would be able to access that address normally and resume operations from the valid return address without crashing. The root issue is that the thread function is not meant to return, if they do return, the CPU tries to continue execution from a meaningless LR, leading to fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,5,'2025-06-22_22-53-36','If the function returns, the code will likely crash. The thread was manually setup to run the print function; in which we assigned an invalid address to LR (return address). If the function did not loop continuously, the CPU would attempt to go to an invalid address, stored in LR, and crash the system. If, however, the function was called by a normal call, a real address would be stored in LR and the system would be able to access that address normally and resume operations from the valid return address without crashing. The root issue is that the thread function is not meant to return, if they do return, the CPU tries to continue execution from a meaningless LR, leading to fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,5,'2025-06-23_14-13-29','If the function returns, the code will likely crash. The thread was manually setup to run the print function; in which we assigned an invalid address to LR (return address). If the function did not loop continuously, the CPU would attempt to go to an invalid address, stored in LR, and crash the system. If, however, the function was called by a normal call, a real address would be stored in LR and the system would be able to access that address normally and resume operations from the valid return address without crashing. The root issue is that the thread function is not meant to return, if they do return, the CPU tries to continue execution from a meaningless LR, leading to fault.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',2,5,'2025-06-23_16-34-47','If the function does not loop continuously and instead returns, the code will result in a hardfault. This happens because the thread was manually setup to execute the function; as a result there is no valid return address (LR is an invalid address assigned from the thread’s stack). So when the processor attempts to go execute code from LR, it leads to a hardfault. If, however, the function was called by a normal call, the CPU would have automatically saved the return address in LR and the system would be able to access that address normally, resuming operations from the return address without crashing. The root issue is that the thread function is not meant to return, if they do return, the CPU tries to continue execution from a meaningless LR, leading to fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',2,5,'2025-06-23_16-52-21','If the function does not loop continuously and instead returns, the code will result in a hardfault. This happens because the thread was manually setup to execute the function; as a result there is no valid return address (LR is an invalid address assigned from the thread’s stack). So when the processor attempts to go execute code from LR, it leads to a hardfault. If, however, the function was called by a normal call, the CPU would have automatically saved the return address in LR and the system would be able to access that address normally, resuming operations from the return address without crashing. The root issue is that the thread function is not meant to return, if they do return, the CPU tries to continue execution from a meaningless LR, leading to fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',3,1,'2025-06-26_13-33-23','Each *.c file gets compiled into object files. These object files are then combined into a single executable file by the linker, resolving references between them.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',3,1,'2025-06-26_17-16-46','Before compilation begins, the preprocessor replaces macros with the corresponding values or code. For instance, the #include directive referring to header to files is basically replaced with the contents of the header file itself. #define macros are replaced with the actual values assigned to them. Any comments from the code are eliminated since they serve no purpose to the compiler. Then each *.c file gets compiled into individual object files, however these cannot yet be executed by the processor. These object files need to get combined into a single executable file by the linker. The linker takes the different sections from different object files and combines them along with any libraries required, assigning everything a location in memory. This resolves references between object files, such as global variables, all compiled into a single executable file.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',3,2,'2025-06-26_13-33-23','Since *.c files get compiled into object files, using an #include directive causes the code to be duplicated in the file it is included in. This leads to multiple definitions of functions and variables, causing errors. Only header files should be included to share function prototypes and definitions between files since they are not independently compiled.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',3,2,'2025-06-26_17-16-46','Since *.c files get compiled into object files, using an #include directive causes the code to be duplicated in the file it is included in. This leads to multiple definitions of functions and variables, causing errors. Only header files should be included to share function prototypes and definitions between files since they are not independently compiled.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',3,3,'2025-06-26_13-33-23','Define the thread function in main.c which continuously loops and has no return.\nIntialize the kernel by calling a function to prepare a stack space.\nCreate the thread by passing the thread function to the stack space via the kernel’s thread creation function.\nStart the os to load the context from the stack to the appropriate registers.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',3,3,'2025-06-26_17-16-46','Define the thread function in main.c which continuously loops and has no return.\nIntialize the kernel by calling a function to prepare a stack space.\nCreate the thread by passing the thread function to the stack space via the kernel’s thread creation function.\nStart the os to load the context from the stack to the appropriate registers.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',3,4,'2025-06-26_13-33-23','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory for implementing threads via stacks. The pool gets divided into equal portions, each assigned to a single thread. This allows for multiple stacks to be used for multiple threads, enabling safe thread switching.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',3,4,'2025-06-26_17-16-46','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory for implementing threads via stacks. The pool gets divided into equal portions, each assigned to a single thread. This allows for multiple stacks to be used for multiple threads, enabling safe thread switching.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',3,5,'2025-06-26_13-33-23','The kernel is responsible for handling low-level operations such as stack allocation, thread creation and system calls. The user utilises kernel functions to create and run threads by calling the kernel’s API and passing parameters to it. This distinction is important because it allows the kernel to be modular and reusable, the user only needs to focus on the writing thread logic while direct memory operations are handled by the kernel.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',3,5,'2025-06-26_17-16-46','The kernel is responsible for handling low-level operations such as setting up a stack, thread creation and system calls because they involve memory management (assigning values to registers and finding stack addresses) and hardware control. The user utilises kernel functions to create and run threads by calling the kernel’s API and passing parameters to it. This distinction is important because it allows the kernel to be modular and reusable, making the process of running threads simpler for the user, as they only need to focus on the writing thread logic while the specific memory operations are handled by the kernel. This reduces the risk of system crashes and hardfaults since the user is only responsible for the high-level operations such as calling functions and passing parameters while the kernel already has predefined functions responsible for handling memory.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,1,'2025-07-26_18-29-33','Using a queue would require us to manipulate the start and end pointers of the queue rather than having to update the total number of stacks and current stack counters. Implementing round-robin scheduling would be more aligned with the purpose of a queue. Queues function on the First In First Out (FIFO) principle, which is similar to round-robin scheduling since one a task/instruction is run the next instruction will be run and the previous instruction will only run after the others have gotten a chance.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,1,'2025-07-26_21-34-13','Using a queue would require us to manipulate the start and end pointers of the queue rather than having to update the total number of stacks and current stack counters. Implementing round-robin scheduling would be more aligned with the purpose of a queue. Queues function on the First In First Out (FIFO) principle, which is similar to round-robin scheduling since one a task/instruction is run the next instruction will be run and the previous instruction will only run after the others have gotten a chance.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,1,'2025-07-28_12-07-25','Using a queue would require us to manipulate the start and end pointers of the queue rather than having to update the total number of stacks and current stack counters. Implementing round-robin scheduling would be more aligned with the purpose of a queue. Queues function on the First In First Out (FIFO) principle, which is similar to round-robin scheduling since one a task/instruction is run the next instruction will be run and the previous instruction will only run after the others have gotten a chance.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,1,'2025-07-28_13-26-37','Round-robin can be naturally implemented using a queue because the head of the queue always points to the thread currently running. When the current thread’s time slice expired (or it yields), it is dequeued from the head and enqueued at the tail, automatically moving the next thread into the head position to be executed. This rotation perfectly matches the round-robin concept of giving each thread an equal turn. Compared to using an array: an array based scheduler requires tracking the current index and manually incrementing in with (current_thread + 1) % total_thread; a queue eliminates this requirement since enqueue and dequeue operations inherently maintain this order of execution. There is no need to shift elements or calculate indices when threads are added or removed using a queue, unlike with an array. A queue offers cleaner scheduling logic since its First In First Out behavior aligns with round-robin, adding or removing threads is simpler and does not require additional steps such as index management (as explained above) and makes the implementation intuitive as the head of the queue will always represent the next thread to run. Arrays may be slightly faster for fixed-size threads due to their efficiency as a data structure, queues provide a more flexible and straightforward approach to implement round-robin scheduling.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,1,'2025-07-28_13-55-58','Round-robin can be naturally implemented using a queue because the head of the queue always points to the thread currently running. When the current thread’s time slice expired (or it yields), it is dequeued from the head and enqueued at the tail, automatically moving the next thread into the head position to be executed. This rotation perfectly matches the round-robin concept of giving each thread an equal turn. Compared to using an array: an array based scheduler requires tracking the current index and manually incrementing in with (current_thread + 1) % total_thread; a queue eliminates this requirement since enqueue and dequeue operations inherently maintain this order of execution. There is no need to shift elements or calculate indices when threads are added or removed using a queue, unlike with an array. A queue offers cleaner scheduling logic since its First In First Out behavior aligns with round-robin, adding or removing threads is simpler and does not require additional steps such as index management (as explained above) and makes the implementation intuitive as the head of the queue will always represent the next thread to run. Arrays may be slightly faster for fixed-size threads due to their efficiency as a data structure, queues provide a more flexible and straightforward approach to implement round-robin scheduling.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,1,'2025-07-28_15-04-40','Round-robin can be effectively implemented using a queue because the head of the queue always represents the currently running thread, and once the thread’s time slice expires or call osYield, it is dequeued from the head and re-enqueued at the tail. This automatically cycles through all threads in a fair, First In First Out (FIFO) order without requiring manual index management. Benefits: Automatic rotation, a queue naturally handles the rotation of threads by enqueue and dequeue operations, whereas an array-based scheduler required maintaining a current_thread index and manually calculating the next position; adding or removing threads is easier with a queue since you do not need to shift elements or recalculate positions as you might with arrays; the FIFO behavior of a queue directly aligns with round-robin principles, making the implementation more intuitive and clean. While arrays are slightly more efficient for fixed-size, static scheduling due to direct indexing and minimal overhead, they lack flexibility. A queue may introduce minor overhead from its pointer manipulation but is more adaptable, especially if threads are frequently added or removed. Additionally, the current array-based system already mimics a queue by rotating an index, so switching to a true queue will not yield significant benefits unless the dynamic thread management is required.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,2,'2025-07-26_18-29-33','PendSV is a special type interrupt, that only rubs when no other interrupts need to be run. If we were to use the SVC_Hangler function to perform context switching and a function was run, the variables left on the stack by the previous function will be stuck and the stack will keep growing until memory is full. The PendSV_Handler is set to a lower priority than SVC, so only after SVC is completed and the stack is restored, will the PendSV be triggered. This ensures that an interrupt is being used for context switches and the stack is restored.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,2,'2025-07-26_21-34-13','PendSV is a special type interrupt, that only rubs when no other interrupts need to be run. If we were to use the SVC_Hangler function to perform context switching and a function was run, the variables left on the stack by the previous function will be stuck and the stack will keep growing until memory is full. The PendSV_Handler is set to a lower priority than SVC, so only after SVC is completed and the stack is restored, will the PendSV be triggered. This ensures that an interrupt is being used for context switches and the stack is restored.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,2,'2025-07-28_12-07-25','PendSV is a special type interrupt, that only rubs when no other interrupts need to be run. If we were to use the SVC_Hangler function to perform context switching and a function was run, the variables left on the stack by the previous function will be stuck and the stack will keep growing until memory is full. The PendSV_Handler is set to a lower priority than SVC, so only after SVC is completed and the stack is restored, will the PendSV be triggered. This ensures that an interrupt is being used for context switches and the stack is restored.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,2,'2025-07-28_13-26-37','The PendSV_Handler function is a special type of interrupt designed for context switching, by having the lowest priority. This ensures that all other interrupts complete before switching threads. The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first reading the PSP to locate the current thread’s stack. Then the registers R4-R11 are pushed onto the stack to save the full CPU state. Next osSched is called to update the current thread pointer and sets the PSP to the stack of the next thread to run. Finally, the handler pops R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack, restoring its state so it can resume execution exactly where it was interrupted at.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,2,'2025-07-28_13-55-58','The PendSV_Handler function is a special type of interrupt designed for context switching, by having the lowest priority. This ensures that all other interrupts complete before switching threads. The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first reading the PSP to locate the current thread’s stack. Then the registers R4-R11 are pushed onto the stack to save the full CPU state. Next osSched is called to update the current thread pointer and sets the PSP to the stack of the next thread to run. Finally, the handler pops R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack, restoring its state so it can resume execution exactly where it was interrupted at.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,2,'2025-07-28_15-04-40','The PendSV_Handler function is a special type of interrupt designed for context switching, by having the lowest priority. This ensures that all other interrupts complete before switching threads. The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first reading the PSP to locate the current thread’s stack. Then the registers R4-R11 are pushed onto the stack to save the full CPU state. Next osSched is called to update the current thread pointer and sets the PSP to the stack of the next thread to run. Finally, the handler pops R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack, restoring its state so it can resume execution exactly where it was interrupted at.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,3,'2025-07-26_18-29-33','If the higher priority task is always chosen, lower priority tasks will keep getting ignored and may never be run if there is always a task with a higher priority.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,3,'2025-07-26_21-34-13','If the higher priority task is always chosen, lower priority tasks will keep getting ignored and may never be run if there is always a task with a higher priority.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,3,'2025-07-28_12-07-25','If the higher priority task is always chosen, lower priority tasks will keep getting ignored and may never be run if there is always a task with a higher priority.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,3,'2025-07-28_13-26-37','If the higher priority task is always chosen, lower priority tasks will experience starvation, they might never get CPU time if a higher-priority task is always ready to run. This results in unfair scheduling, where some tasks never progress, potentially causing system failures if those tasks are important.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,3,'2025-07-28_13-55-58','If the scheduler always selects the highest-priority task, then as long as that task is ready to run, it will completely monopolise the CPU. This means that no other tasks, regardless of their importance, will ever get a chance to execute. Instead of true multitasking, the system will effectively become single-tasked as only one thread will ever run. This not only causes guaranteed starvation of lower priority tasks but also defeats the purpose of having a scheduler designed to manage multiple tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,3,'2025-07-28_15-04-40','If the scheduler always selects the highest-priority task, then as long as that task is ready to run, it will completely monopolise the CPU. This means that no other tasks, regardless of their importance, will ever get a chance to execute. Instead of true multitasking, the system will effectively become single-tasked as only one thread will ever run. This not only causes guaranteed starvation of lower priority tasks but also defeats the purpose of having a scheduler designed to manage multiple tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,4,'2025-07-26_18-29-33','It is much easier to read and write in C rather than having to write everything in assembly, C is a higher-level language and closer to human language. Calling the C functions makes the code more readable since assembly language does not need to be understood every time, only a simple function is called. This also aids in debugging since only the function will need to be debugged if an error exists rather than having to the go through the entire program in assembly.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,4,'2025-07-26_21-34-13','It is much easier to read and write in C rather than having to write everything in assembly, C is a higher-level language and closer to human language. Calling the C functions makes the code more readable since assembly language does not need to be understood every time, only a simple function is called. This also aids in debugging since only the function will need to be debugged if an error exists rather than having to the go through the entire program in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,4,'2025-07-28_12-07-25','It is much easier to read and write in C rather than having to write everything in assembly, C is a higher-level language and closer to human language. Calling the C functions makes the code more readable since assembly language does not need to be understood every time, only a simple function is called. This also aids in debugging since only the function will need to be debugged if an error exists rather than having to the go through the entire program in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,4,'2025-07-28_13-26-37','It is much easier to read and write in C rather than having to write everything in assembly, C is a higher-level language and closer to human language. Calling the C functions makes the code more readable since assembly language does not need to be understood every time, only a simple function is called. This also aids in debugging since only the function will need to be debugged if an error exists rather than having to the go through the entire program in assembly.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,4,'2025-07-28_13-55-58','It is much easier to read and write in C rather than having to write everything in assembly since C is a higher-level language that abstracts low-level details and is closer to human language. Calling the C functions makes the code more readable since assembly language does not need to be understood every time, only a simple function is called. This also aids in debugging since only the function will need to be debugged if an error exists rather than having to the go through the entire program in assembly. Additionally, C allows for better portability and quicker development compared to assembly. However, using assembly, we have direct control over the hardware and registers, which can lead to more optimised and faster code. Calling C functions introduces additional overhead, such as saving and restoring registers during function calls, and may generate less efficient machine code. Furthermore, certain low-level tasks or previse timing operations might be harder to implement reliably in C compared to assembly. C provides maintainability and readability at the cost of performance and precise hardware control.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,4,'2025-07-28_15-04-40','It is much easier to read and write in C rather than having to write everything in assembly since C is a higher-level language that abstracts low-level details and is closer to human language. Calling the C functions makes the code more readable since assembly language does not need to be understood every time, only a simple function is called. This also aids in debugging since only the function will need to be debugged if an error exists rather than having to the go through the entire program in assembly. Additionally, C allows for better portability and quicker development compared to assembly. However, using assembly, we have direct control over the hardware and registers, which can lead to more optimised and faster code. Calling C functions introduces additional overhead, such as saving and restoring registers during function calls, and may generate less efficient machine code. Furthermore, certain low-level tasks or previse timing operations might be harder to implement reliably in C compared to assembly. C provides maintainability and readability at the cost of performance and precise hardware control.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,5,'2025-07-26_18-29-33','Yes, it will still be functional, but with no purpose. After printing once, the thread will yield and attempt to perform a context switch, however, it will end up simply reloading the same values back into the registers. The function will simply be called again, so while it does not need to “switch context” it will do so to only switch back to itself and resume operations.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,5,'2025-07-26_21-34-13','Yes, it will still be functional, but with no purpose. After printing once, the thread will yield and attempt to perform a context switch, however, it will end up simply reloading the same values back into the registers. The function will simply be called again, so while it does not need to “switch context” it will do so to only switch back to itself and resume operations.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,5,'2025-07-28_12-07-25','Yes, it will still be functional, but with no purpose. After printing once, the thread will yield and attempt to perform a context switch, however, it will end up simply reloading the same values back into the registers. The function will simply be called again, so while it does not need to “switch context” it will do so to only switch back to itself and resume operations.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',4,5,'2025-07-28_13-26-37','Yes, it will still be functional, but the context switch will have no real effect. After printing, the thread will yield and attempt to perform a context switch by calling osYield. The scheduler, osSched, will be invoked to select the next thread to run. Since there is only one thread in the system, the scheduler will simply choose the same thread again and reload its saved context from the stack. As a result, the thread will resume execution exactly where it left off, making the context switch redundant but still functional.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,5,'2025-07-28_13-55-58','Yes, it will still be functional, but the context switch will have no real effect. After printing, the thread will yield and attempt to perform a context switch by calling osYield. The scheduler, osSched, will be invoked to select the next thread to run. Since there is only one thread in the system, the scheduler will simply choose the same thread again and reload its saved context from the stack. As a result, the thread will resume execution exactly where it left off, making the context switch redundant but still functional.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',4,5,'2025-07-28_15-04-40','Yes, it will still be functional, but the context switch will have no real effect. After printing, the thread will yield and attempt to perform a context switch by calling osYield. The scheduler, osSched, will be invoked to select the next thread to run. Since there is only one thread in the system, the scheduler will simply choose the same thread again and reload its saved context from the stack. As a result, the thread will resume execution exactly where it left off, making the context switch redundant but still functional.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',5,1,'2025-07-26_20-49-05','The user is only required to pass a void pointer. The OS will then dereference the pointer so that the data stored at the pointer address can be accessed. A void pointer is used because it may be cast to an integer to be stored within R0 or if multiple arguments need to be input, they can be wrapped into a struct and passed.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',5,1,'2025-07-28_12-45-07','The user must create the data they want to pass, could be an integer or struct, and provide a pointer to it when calling osCreateThread. This data must remain valid in memory for the lifetime of the thread, ideally a global variable. When the thread’s stack is initialised, the OS places the pointer, provided by the user, into the R0 register as part of the initial stack frame. Function arguments are passed through R0-R3, so the thread’s function received the pointer as its argument when its starts executing. The OS sets up the stack and ensures that when the thread is scheduled, R0 hold the user-provided pointer. This works because ARM Application Binary Interface (ABI) specifies that the first argument is passed to R0. By preloading R0 with the pointer during the stack’s initialisation, the thread function starts with he correct argument. If multiple arguments need to be passed, they can be placed in a struct and the pointer to the struct is passed instead.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',5,2,'2025-07-26_20-49-05','osYield cannot be called in any of the context-switching functions because they are all interrupt context functions. This means that when osYield attempts to trigger a context switch while inside an interrupt (SysTick_Handler or PendSV_Handler), the hardware has already pushed a set of registers to the stack, rendering osYield confused as it is not designed to work with this modified stack. If this is done, we will essentially be triggering a context switch during another context switch. This can corrupt the stack because stack values will not correspond to the right registers anymore, and may lead to hard faults, due to invalid return addresses.\n',0,'s25'),('sana',5,2,'2025-07-28_12-45-07','osYield cannot be called in any of the context-switching functions (PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler and SVC_Handler_Main or SysTick_Handler) because it relies on trigerring a system call (SVC) to initiate a context switch. These handlers are already executing in an interrupt context, meaning the CPU has already pushed a stack frame and is in the middle of saving/restoring registers. Calling osYield within them would attempt to trigger another SVC interrupt on top of the current one, leading ot nested context switching. This is bad because it causes stack corruption, the stack frame being built for the current interrupt would be overwritten or confused with another and break the saved context; unpredictable behavior, switching threads while already performing a switch can lead to invalid return addresses and hard faults; a redundant mechanism, those handlers already manage context switching directly, so calling osYield inside them is not required and harmful.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',5,3,'2025-07-26_20-49-05','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1ms would result in a finer timing resolution, enabling the OS to respond to events with a precision of 0.1ms, this would be better for real-time tasks which require more accurate scheduling, such as for precise actuator control or sensor sampling. Moreover, round-robin will move quicker between tasks since task slices will be shorter, making the system appear more responsive in the context of multiple threads. However, this also has some downsides. Since the OS will be switching 10 times more often, there will also be a significant increase in CPU time being spent on saving and restoring registers rather than running the program.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',5,3,'2025-07-28_12-45-07','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1ms would result in a finer timing resolution, enabling the OS to respond to events with a precision of 0.1ms, this would be better for real-time tasks which require more accurate scheduling, such as for precise actuator control or sensor sampling. Moreover, round-robin will move quicker between tasks since task slices will be shorter, making the system appear more responsive in the context of multiple threads. However, this also has some downsides. Since the OS will be switching 10 times more often, there will also be a significant increase in CPU time being spent on saving and restoring registers rather than running the program.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',5,4,'2025-07-26_20-49-05','Co-operative multitasking is simple, the OS is only responsible for saving and restoring thread context when explicitly told to do so by the user, whereas in pre-emptive multitasking the OS has to setup a hardware timer (SysTick) to trigger interrupts and perform context switching. This means that there is less overhead in co-operative multitasking since no periodic interrupts are required to enforce scheduling. Pre-emptive multitasking is more complex since the\n',1,'s25'),('sana',5,4,'2025-07-28_12-45-07','Co-operative multitasking is simple, the OS is only responsible for saving and restoring thread context when explicitly told to do so by the user calling osYield. The user must design their code to periodically give up the CPU or else no other can run. In pre-emptive multitasking the OS has to setup a hardware timer (SysTick) to trigger interrupts and perform context switching. This means that there is less overhead in co-operative multitasking since periodic interrupts are not required as frequently to enforce scheduling. Pre-emptive multitasking is more complex since the OS needs to manage timer itnerrupts, keep track of time slices and handle more frequent context switches. Pre-emptive multitasking gives the user the impression that tasks run “in parallel” because no single thread is able to monopolise the CPU.\n',1,'s25'),('sana',5,5,'2025-07-26_20-49-05','',0,'s25'),('sana',5,5,'2025-07-28_12-45-07','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching work together because they rely on the same context-switching mechanism, the PendSV_Handler. In cooperative multitasking, a thread calls osYield to voluntarily give up the CPU, which triggers a context switch via PendSV. In pre-emptive multitasking, the SysTick_handler automatically triggers PendSV when a thread’s time slice expires, forcing a switch even if the thread does not yield. Since both methods ultimately call the same scheduler, osSched, and contex-switch routine, they can seamlessly coexist, allowing threads to either yield voluntarily or be pre-empted by the OS timer.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,1,'2025-05-16_09-05-21','The hardware abstraction layer provides a standardized way for programs to interact with hardware devices without having to know details about the device. This allows the programmer to write code that does not depend on the device being used. It works by abstracting the low-level details of the device being used. It is useful because it saves time as the programmer does not have to know the details of the device being interfaced. It also makes the code more portable since it can be used for other hardware devices with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,1,'2025-05-16_09-20-37','The hardware abstraction layer provides a standardized way for programs to interact with hardware devices without having to know details about the device. This allows the programmer to write code that does not depend on the device being used. It works by abstracting the low-level details of the device being used. It is useful because it saves time as the programmer does not have to know the details of the device being interfaced. It also makes the code more portable since it can be used for other hardware devices with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,1,'2025-05-17_16-19-46','The hardware abstraction layer provides a standardized way for programs to interact with hardware devices without having to know details about the device. This allows the programmer to write code that does not depend on the device being used. It works by abstracting the low-level details of the device being used. It is useful because it saves time as the programmer does not have to know the details of the device being interfaced. It also makes the code more portable since it can be used for other hardware devices with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,1,'2025-05-17_16-27-38','The hardware abstraction layer provides a standardized way for programs to interact with hardware devices without having to know details about the device. This allows the programmer to write code that does not depend on the device being used. It works by abstracting the low-level details of the device being used. It is useful because it saves time as the programmer does not have to know the details of the device being interfaced. It also makes the code more portable since it can be used for other hardware devices with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,1,'2025-05-17_16-42-51','The hardware abstraction layer provides a standardized way for programs to interact with hardware devices without having to know details about the device. This allows the programmer to write code that does not depend on the device being used. It works by abstracting the low-level details of the device being used. It is useful because it saves time as the programmer does not have to know the details of the device being interfaced. It also makes the code more portable since it can be used for other hardware devices with minimal changes.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,1,'2025-05-18_15-25-29','The HAL is a hardware abstraction layer that provides a standardized way for programs to interact with hardware devices without having to know details about the device. This allows the programmer to write code that does not depend on the device being used. It works by abstracting the low-level details of the device being used. It is useful because it saves time as the programmer does not have to know the details of the device being interfaced. It also makes the code more portable since it can be used for other hardware devices with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,1,'2025-05-18_15-31-58','The HAL is a hardware abstraction layer that provides a standardized way for programs to interact with hardware devices without having to know details about the device. This allows the programmer to write code that does not depend on the device being used. It works by abstracting the low-level details of the device being used. It is useful because it saves time as the programmer does not have to know the details of the device being interfaced. It also makes the code more portable since it can be used for other hardware devices with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,1,'2025-06-22_08-22-58','The HAL is a hardware abstraction layer that provides a standardized way for programs to interact with hardware devices without having to know details about the device. This allows the programmer to write code that does not depend on the device being used. It works by abstracting the low-level details of the device being used. It is useful because it saves time as the programmer does not have to know the details of the device being interfaced. It also makes the code more portable since it can be used for other hardware devices with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,2,'2025-05-16_09-05-21','The __io_putchar function is used to write single characters to the right IO channel. The printf calls this function multiple times when a string is printed out. It is used because the standard printf() function does not know where to send characters on the device. The characters are sent using UART.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,2,'2025-05-16_09-20-37','The __io_putchar function is used to write single characters to the right IO channel. The printf calls this function multiple times when a string is printed out. It is used because the standard printf() function does not know where to send characters on the device. The characters are sent using UART.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,2,'2025-05-17_16-19-46','The __io_putchar function is used to write single characters to the right IO channel. The printf calls this function multiple times when a string is printed out. It is used because the standard printf() function does not know where to send characters on the device. The characters are sent using UART.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,2,'2025-05-17_16-27-38','The __io_putchar function is used to write single characters to the right IO channel. The printf calls this function multiple times when a string is printed out. It is used because the standard printf() function does not know where to send characters on the device. The characters are sent using UART.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,2,'2025-05-17_16-42-51','The __io_putchar function is used to write single characters to the correct IO channel. The function is used to tell the program how to send the character. It is used to provide information about which communication protocol to use and where to send the character. The printf calls this function multiple times when a string is printed out. It is used because the standard printf() function does not know where to send characters on the device. The characters are sent using UART as specified by the function.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,2,'2025-05-18_15-25-29','The __io_putchar function is used to write single characters to the correct IO channel. The function is used to tell the program how to send the character. It is used to provide information about which communication protocol to use and where to send the character. The printf calls this function multiple times when a string is printed out. This function is needed because the standard printf() function does not know how to physically send data on a specific piece of hardware. Different embedded systems might use different output methods — such as UART, USB, or even LCD screens — and __io_putchar provides a way to define how and where characters should be sent. By customizing this function, developers can ensure that printf() works correctly regardless of the hardware being used. The characters are sent using UART as specified by the function.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,2,'2025-05-18_15-31-58','The __io_putchar function is used to write single characters to the correct IO channel. The function is used to tell the program how to send the character. It is used to provide information about which communication protocol to use and where to send the character. The printf calls this function multiple times when a string is printed out. This function is needed because the standard printf() function does not know how to physically send data on a specific piece of hardware. Different embedded systems might use different output methods — such as UART, USB, or even LCD screens — and __io_putchar provides a way to define how and where characters should be sent. By customizing this function, developers can ensure that printf() works correctly regardless of the hardware being used. The characters are sent using UART as specified by the function.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,2,'2025-06-22_08-22-58','The __io_putchar function is used to write single characters to the correct IO channel. The function is used to tell the program how to send the character. It is used to provide information about which communication protocol to use and where to send the character. The printf calls this function multiple times when a string is printed out. This function is needed because the standard printf() function does not know how to send data on a specific type of hardware. Embedded systems use different output methods like UART, USB, or even LCD screens.  __io_putchar provides a way to define how and where characters should be sent. By using this function, we can ensure that printf() works correctly regardless of the specific hardware being used. The characters are sent using UART as specified by the function.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,3,'2025-05-16_09-05-21','The debugger allows us to pause the program using the breakpoints. This allows us to look at memory and registers live as the values are changed. Printf only shows the end result without any information about when variables are changed or updated.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,3,'2025-05-16_09-20-37','The debugger allows us to pause the program using the breakpoints. This allows us to look at memory and registers live as the values are changed. Printf only shows the end result without any information about when variables are changed or updated.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,3,'2025-05-17_16-19-46','The debugger allows us to pause the program using the breakpoints. This allows us to look at memory and registers live as the values are changed. Printf only shows the end result without any information about when variables are changed or updated.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,3,'2025-05-17_16-27-38','The debugger allows us to pause the program using the breakpoints. This allows us to look at memory and registers live as the values are changed. Printf only shows the end result without any information about when variables are changed or updated.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,3,'2025-05-17_16-42-51','The debugger allows us to pause the program using the breakpoints. This allows us to look at memory and registers live as the values are changed. Printf only shows the end result without any information about when variables are changed or updated.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,3,'2025-05-18_15-25-29','We used the debugger in this lab because it allows us to observe program variables and hardware registers in real time. In embedded systems, variables are values stored in RAM and managed by the program, while hardware registers are special memory-mapped locations used to control and monitor the behavior of hardware components, like timers, GPIO, or communication peripherals. A tool like printf can only display values that the software can access and is often limited to program variables. It usually cannot access or display hardware registers directly, especially if they require specific timing or access patterns. Additionally, printf only shows values after the fact and introduces delays that can interfere with timing-sensitive operations. In contrast, a debugger lets us pause the program at any point using breakpoints and inspect the current state of both variables and hardware registers. This provides much deeper insight into how the system is behaving, especially when we’re trying to understand low-level interactions between software and hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,3,'2025-05-18_15-31-58','We used the debugger in this lab because it allows us to observe program variables and hardware registers in real time. In embedded systems, variables are values stored in RAM and managed by the program, while hardware registers are special memory-mapped locations used to control and monitor the behavior of hardware components, like timers, GPIO, or communication peripherals. A tool like printf can only display values that the software can access and is often limited to program variables. It usually cannot access or display hardware registers directly, especially if they require specific timing or access patterns. Additionally, printf only shows values after the fact and introduces delays that can interfere with timing-sensitive operations. In contrast, a debugger lets us pause the program at any point using breakpoints and inspect the current state of both variables and hardware registers. This provides much deeper insight into how the system is behaving, especially when we’re trying to understand low-level interactions between software and hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,3,'2025-06-22_08-22-58','We used the debugger in this lab because it allows us to see program variables and registers in real time.  printf can only display values that the software can access and is limited to program variables. It cannot access the registers directly. Additionally, printf only shows values after the changes have been made or instructions have been executed. A debugger lets us pause the program at any point using breakpoints and see the current state of both variables and registers. This gives us a better understanding of how the system and program is behaving.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,4,'2025-05-16_09-05-21','The MSP_init_val gives us the starting address of the main stack. We reserved 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since the stack grows downwards, we subtract 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL and move the PSP down in memory, leaving space for the main stack on top. Once we get PSP_VAL, we set PSP to the new location. We need to know the initial location of MSP because we want to ensure that PSP does not overwrite the main stack. If we randomly chose a PSP value, we could end up putting the thread stack inside the main stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,4,'2025-05-16_09-20-37','The MSP_init_val gives us the starting address of the main stack. We reserved 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since the stack grows downwards, we subtract 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL and move the PSP down in memory, leaving space for the main stack on top. Once we get PSP_VAL, we set PSP to the new location. We need to know the initial location of MSP because we want to ensure that PSP does not overwrite the main stack. If we randomly chose a PSP value, we could end up putting the thread stack inside the main stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,4,'2025-05-17_16-19-46','The MSP_init_val gives us the starting address of the main stack. We reserved 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since the stack grows downwards, we subtract 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL and move the PSP down in memory, leaving space for the main stack on top. Once we get PSP_VAL, we set PSP to the new location. We need to know the initial location of MSP because we want to ensure that PSP does not overwrite the main stack. If we randomly chose a PSP value, we could end up putting the thread stack inside the main stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,4,'2025-05-17_16-27-38','The MSP_init_val gives us the starting address of the main stack. We reserved 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since the stack grows downwards, we subtract 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL and move the PSP down in memory, leaving space for the main stack on top. Once we get PSP_VAL, we set PSP to the new location. We need to know the initial location of MSP because we want to ensure that PSP does not overwrite the main stack. If we randomly chose a PSP value, we could end up putting the thread stack inside the main stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,4,'2025-05-17_16-42-51','The MSP_init_val gives us the starting address of the main stack. We reserved 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since the stack grows downwards, we subtract 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL and move the PSP down in memory, leaving space for the main stack on top. Once we get PSP_VAL, we set PSP to the new location. We need to know the initial location of MSP because we want to ensure that PSP does not overwrite the main stack. If we randomly chose a PSP value, we could end up putting the thread stack inside the main stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,4,'2025-05-18_15-25-29','The MSP_init_val gives us the starting address of the main stack. We reserved 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since the stack grows downwards, we subtract 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL and move the PSP down in memory, leaving space for the main stack on top. Once we get PSP_VAL, we set PSP to the new location. We need to know the initial location of MSP because we want to ensure that PSP does not overwrite the main stack. If we randomly chose a PSP value, we could end up putting the thread stack inside the main stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,4,'2025-05-18_15-31-58','The MSP_init_val gives us the starting address of the main stack. We reserved 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since the stack grows downwards, we subtract 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL and move the PSP down in memory, leaving space for the main stack on top. Once we get PSP_VAL, we set PSP to the new location. We need to know the initial location of MSP because we want to ensure that PSP does not overwrite the main stack. If we randomly chose a PSP value, we could end up putting the thread stack inside the main stack area.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,4,'2025-06-22_08-22-58','The MSP_init_val gives us the starting address of the main stack. We reserved 0x400 bytes for the main stack. Since the stack grows downwards, we subtract 0x400 bytes from MSP_INIT_VAL and move the PSP down in memory effectively leaving space for the main stack on top. Once we get PSP_VAL, we set PSP to the new location. We need to know the initial location of MSP because we want to ensure that PSP does not overwrite the main stack. If we chose a PSP value randomly, we can mistakenly put the thread stack inside the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,5,'2025-05-16_09-05-21','After reserving space for the main stack, we would need a loop that iterates a number of times equal to the number of threads. Within the loop, we would move down the stack by the number of bytes needed by each thread. We would store the top of each thread’s stack in an array. Then we would know where each stack starts.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,5,'2025-05-16_09-20-37','After reserving space for the main stack, we would need a loop that iterates a number of times equal to the number of threads. Within the loop, we would move down the stack by the number of bytes needed by each thread. We would store the top of each thread’s stack in an array. Then we would know where each stack starts. It is important to note that initially, we would need to move down in memory by a number of bytes equal to the size of the main stack. This would prevent us from overwriting the main stack’s memory. We should also ensure that we are not taking up more memory than was allocated for the main stack and threads (0x4000 bytes).\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,5,'2025-05-17_16-19-46','First, we would need to move down in memory to the end of the main stack. Then we would need to create new variables to store the locations of each new stack (eg. STACK1, STACK2, STACK3). Then we would need to move down in memory by N bytes. N represents the size of each new stack. We would save the location of the top of each stack with the variables created.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,5,'2025-05-17_16-27-38','First, we would need to move down in memory to the end of the main stack. Then we would need to create new variables to store the locations of each new stack (eg. STACK1, STACK2, STACK3). Then we would need to move down in memory by N bytes. N represents the size of each new stack. We would save the location of the top of each stack with the variables created.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,5,'2025-05-17_16-42-51','First, we would need to move down in memory to the end of the main stack and ensure that the offset does not cause memory overlap with the main stack. Then we would need to create new variables to store the locations of each new stack (eg. STACK1, STACK2, STACK3). Then we would need to move down in memory by N bytes. N represents the size of each new stack. We would save the location of the top of each stack with the variables created. It must be noted that the main stack and additional stacks should not use more memory than was allocated at compile time and this should be checked before allocating memory for another stack.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,5,'2025-05-18_15-25-29','If we want to allocate multiple stacks for threads and we already know how many stacks we need at compile time, we should identify a fixed, known location in memory to start placing those stacks. The starting location should be away from other parts of the program like the main stack or the heap to avoid overlap. From this known starting point, we can calculate the location of each thread’s stack by subtracting a fixed size for each one. For example, the first stack might start right below the starting point (the end of the main stack), the second one below that, and so on. Each stack needs to be placed at a specific memory address that is properly spaced from the others. It\'s also important to make sure each stack address is aligned according to the processor’s rules. For a Cortex-M4, stack addresses must be aligned to 8 bytes. This alignment ensures the processor operates correctly and avoids faults. To maintain alignment, we must choose stack sizes and positions that are multiples of 8 bytes. Since the number of stacks is known in advance, we can calculate and assign all of their locations ahead of time. We want to ensure that all stack locations are fixed and predictable before the program even runs. Finally, we should describe the stack locations as specific memory positions based on a known reference point, with consistent spacing for each one such that the stacks do not take more memory than was allocated.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,5,'2025-05-18_15-31-58','If we want to allocate multiple stacks for threads and we already know how many stacks we need at compile time, we should identify a fixed, known location in memory to start placing those stacks. The starting location should be away from other parts of the program like the main stack or the heap to avoid overlap. From this known starting point, we can calculate the location of each thread’s stack by subtracting a fixed size for each one. For example, the first stack might start right below the starting point (the end of the main stack), the second one below that, and so on. Each stack needs to be placed at a specific memory address that is properly spaced from the others. It\'s also important to make sure each stack address is aligned according to the processor’s rules. For a Cortex-M4, stack addresses must be aligned to 8 bytes. This alignment ensures the processor operates correctly and avoids faults. To maintain alignment, we must choose stack sizes and positions that are multiples of 8 bytes. Since the number of stacks is known in advance, we can calculate and assign all of their locations ahead of time. We want to ensure that all stack locations are fixed and predictable before the program even runs. Finally, we should describe the stack locations as specific memory positions based on a known reference point, with consistent spacing for each one such that the stacks do not take more memory than was allocated.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',1,5,'2025-06-22_08-22-58','If we need to allocate multiple stacks for threads and we know how many stacks we need at compile-time, we should determine a fixed location in memory to start placing the stacks. The starting location should be away from the main stack or the heap to avoid overlap. From the starting point, we can calculate the location of each thread’s stack by subtracting a fixed size for each one. The first stack starts right below the end of the main stack, the second one below that, and so on. Each stack needs to be placed at a specific memory address that is spaced appropriately away from the others. To ensure alignment, stack sizes and positions must be chosen such that they are multiples of 8 bytes. Since the number of stacks is known, we can calculate and assign all of their locations. We want to ensure that all stack locations are fixed before the program runs. We should describe the stack locations as specific memory positions based on a known reference point, with consistent spacing for each one such that the stacks do not take more memory than was allocated.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,1,'2025-06-22_07-59-43','When I changed PC to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor jumped to the address in PC and the print continuously function was executed. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to be executed. For this reason, changing the contents of PC, changes the next instruction to be executed. In this case, the PC was changed to execute the print continuously function next. The function’s address was put into R0 and R0’s contents were moved using assembly into PC.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,1,'2025-06-22_14-09-03','When I changed PC to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor jumped to the address in PC and the print continuously function was executed. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to be executed. For this reason, changing the contents of PC, changes the next instruction to be executed. In this case, the PC was changed to execute the print continuously function next. The function’s address was put into R0 and R0’s contents were moved using assembly into PC.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,1,'2025-06-22_14-32-58','When I changed PC to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor jumped to the address in PC and the print continuously function was executed. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to be executed. For this reason, changing the contents of PC, changes the next instruction to be executed. In this case, the PC was changed to execute the print continuously function next. The function’s address was put into R0 and R0’s contents were moved using assembly into PC.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,1,'2025-06-22_14-50-29','When I changed PC to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor jumped to the address in PC and the print continuously function was executed. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to be executed. For this reason, changing the contents of PC, changes the next instruction to be executed. In this case, the PC was changed to execute the print continuously function next. The function’s address was put into R0 and R0’s contents were moved using assembly into PC.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,1,'2025-06-23_09-41-29','When I changed PC to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor jumped to the address in PC and the print continuously function was executed. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to be executed. For this reason, changing the contents of PC, changes the next instruction to be executed. In this case, the PC was changed to execute the print continuously function next. The function’s address was put into R0 and R0’s contents were moved using assembly into PC.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,1,'2025-06-23_09-56-34','When I changed PC to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor jumped to the address in PC and the print continuously function was executed. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to be executed. For this reason, changing the contents of PC, changes the next instruction to be executed. In this case, the PC was changed to execute the print continuously function next. The function’s address was put into R0 and R0’s contents were moved using assembly into PC.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,1,'2025-06-24_16-53-29','When I changed PC to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor jumped to the address in PC and the print continuously function was executed. This is because the PC holds the address of the next instruction to be executed. For this reason, changing the contents of PC, changes the next instruction to be executed. In this case, the PC was changed to execute the print continuously function next. The function’s address was put into R0 and R0’s contents were moved using assembly into PC.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,2,'2025-06-22_07-59-43','System calls give userspace programs the ability to ask the kernel to do something. However, the kernel might refuse depending on what the system call is. A system call is a function that is usually accessed by an interrupt but the function does not get compiled into the user’s program code. It is fixed in memory.\nWhen a system call is triggered, the system call handler is called in to provide a kernel service. The system call handler is always at the same place in memory hence the mechanism by which we trigger a system call just needs to know the memory location and then call the function pointer stored there.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,2,'2025-06-22_14-09-03','System calls give userspace programs the ability to ask the kernel to do something. However, the kernel might refuse depending on what the system call is. A system call is a function that is usually accessed by an interrupt but the function does not get compiled into the user’s program code. It is fixed in memory.\nWhen a system call is triggered, the system call handler is called in to provide a kernel service. The system call handler is always at the same place in memory hence the mechanism by which we trigger a system call just needs to know the memory location and then call the function pointer stored there.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,2,'2025-06-22_14-32-58','System calls are a kind of function which give userspace programs the ability to ask the kernel to do something. Other functions don’t have the ability to do this. However, the kernel might refuse depending on what the system call is. A system call is a function that is usually accessed by an interrupt but the function does not get compiled into the user’s program code. It is fixed in memory.\nWhen a system call is triggered, the system call handler is called in to provide a kernel service. The system call handler is always at the same place in memory hence the mechanism by which we trigger a system call just needs to know the memory location and then call the function pointer stored there. Since user code can’t run protected/privileged instructions directly, every sensitive operation must be handled through a system call.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,2,'2025-06-22_14-50-29','System calls are a kind of function which give userspace programs the ability to ask the kernel to do something. Other functions don’t have the ability to do this. However, the kernel might refuse depending on what the system call is. A system call is a function that is usually accessed by an interrupt but the function does not get compiled into the user’s program code. It is fixed in memory.\nWhen a system call is triggered, the system call handler is called in to provide a kernel service. The system call handler is always at the same place in memory hence the mechanism by which we trigger a system call just needs to know the memory location and then call the function pointer stored there. Since user code can’t run protected/privileged instructions directly, every sensitive operation must be handled through a system call.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,2,'2025-06-23_09-41-29','System calls are a kind of function which give userspace programs the ability to ask the kernel to do something. Other functions don’t have the ability to do this. However, the kernel might refuse depending on what the system call is. A system call is a function that is usually accessed by an interrupt but the function does not get compiled into the user’s program code. It is fixed in memory.\nWhen a system call is triggered, the system call handler is called in to provide a kernel service. The system call handler is always at the same place in memory hence the mechanism by which we trigger a system call just needs to know the memory location and then call the function pointer stored there. Since user code can’t run protected/privileged instructions directly, every sensitive operation must be handled through a system call.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,2,'2025-06-23_09-56-34','System calls are a kind of function which give userspace programs the ability to ask the kernel to do something. They are the main method used by a user program to request services from the operating system. Other functions don’t have the ability to do this. However, the kernel might refuse depending on what the system call is. A system call is a function that is usually accessed by an interrupt but the function does not get compiled into the user’s program code. It is fixed in memory. For example, a system call is needed when opening a file from memory.\nWhen a system call is triggered, the system call handler is called in to provide a kernel service. The system call handler is always at the same place in memory hence the mechanism by which we trigger a system call just needs to know the memory location and then call the function pointer stored there. Since user code can’t run protected/privileged instructions directly, every sensitive operation must be handled through a system call.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,2,'2025-06-24_16-53-29','System calls are a kind of function which give userspace programs the ability to ask the kernel to do something. They are the main method used by a user program to request services from the operating system. Other functions don’t have the ability to do this. However, the kernel might refuse depending on what the system call is. A system call is a function that is usually accessed by an interrupt but the function does not get compiled into the user’s program code. It is fixed in memory. For example, a system call is needed when opening a file from memory.\nWhen a system call is triggered, the system call handler is called in to provide a kernel service. The system call handler is always at the same place in memory hence the mechanism by which we trigger a system call just needs to know the memory location and then call the function pointer stored there. Since user code can’t run protected/privileged instructions directly, every sensitive operation must be handled through a system call.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,3,'2025-06-22_07-59-43','What it means to set up a thread’s stack is that we need to use a stack pointer to point to the top of the thread’s stack and we need to populate the registers of the thread’s stack. First to set up the thread’s stack, a stack pointer will be needed to point to the top of it. This is done by referring to MSP’s location. Then we need to set up the context of the stack. What this means is that we need to populate the registers 0 through R12, LR, PC, and XPSR. We need to set up the registers as though the thread had been running the whole time, and the chip will proceed to run the thread. In this way, we trick the chip into believing that it is already running the thread before we actually run it. We need to store register values on the stack because during a context switch the kernel must later pop the data into the real registers so that the CPU continues the thread with the same state it would have if it was running all along.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,3,'2025-06-22_14-09-03','Setting up a stack for a thread means building the stack frame in memory that the hardware expects to see when it returns into that thread. We dedicated memory for the thread’s stack and use PSP to point to the top of the stack’s region. This is done by referring to MSP’s location. During a context switch, the kernel saves R4-R11 for the outgoing thread on its stack then restores them back for the incoming thread. This lets the CPU continue exactly from where it left off.\nWe need to populate the registers 0 through R12, LR, PC, and XPSR in that order because it is a stack. We need to set up the registers as though the thread had been running the whole time, and the chip will proceed to run the thread. In this way, we trick the chip into believing that it is already running the thread before we actually run it. We need to store register values on the stack because during a context switch the kernel must later pop the data into the real registers so that the CPU continues the thread with the same state it would have if it was running all along.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,3,'2025-06-22_14-32-58','Setting up a stack for a thread means building the stack frame in memory that the hardware expects to see when it returns into that thread. We dedicated memory for the thread’s stack and use PSP to point to the top of the stack’s region. This is done by referring to MSP’s location. During a context switch, the kernel saves R4-R11 for the outgoing thread on its stack then restores them back for the incoming thread. This lets the CPU continue exactly from where it left off.\nWe need to populate the registers 0 through R12, LR, PC, and XPSR in that order because it is a stack. We need to set up the registers as though the thread had been running the whole time, and the chip will proceed to run the thread. In this way, we trick the chip into believing that it is already running the thread before we actually run it. We need to store register values on the stack because during a context switch the kernel must later pop the data into the real registers so that the CPU continues the thread with the same state it would have if it was running all along.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,3,'2025-06-22_14-50-29','Setting up a stack for a thread means building the stack frame in memory that the hardware expects to see when it returns into that thread. We dedicated memory for the thread’s stack and use PSP to point to the top of the stack’s region. This is done by referring to MSP’s location. During a context switch, the kernel saves the content of the registers for the outgoing thread on its stack then restores them back for the incoming thread. This lets the CPU continue exactly from where it left off. Additionally, stack setup happens once during thread creation whereas context saving happens during every switch.\nWe need to populate the registers 0 through R12, LR, PC, and XPSR in that order because it is a stack. We need to set up the registers as though the thread had been running the whole time, and the chip will proceed to run the thread. In this way, we trick the chip into believing that it is already running the thread before we actually run it. We need to store register values on the stack because during a context switch the kernel must later pop the data into the real registers so that the CPU continues the thread with the same state it would have if it was running all along.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,3,'2025-06-23_09-41-29','Setting up a stack for a thread means building the stack frame in memory that the hardware expects to see when it returns into that thread. We dedicated memory for the thread’s stack and use PSP to point to the top of the stack’s region. This is done by referring to MSP’s location. During a context switch, the kernel saves the content of the registers for the outgoing thread on its stack then restores them back for the incoming thread. This lets the CPU continue exactly from where it left off. Additionally, stack setup happens once during thread creation whereas context saving happens during every switch.\nWe need to populate the registers 0 through R12, LR, PC, and XPSR in that order because it is a stack. We need to set up the registers as though the thread had been running the whole time, and the chip will proceed to run the thread. In this way, we trick the chip into believing that it is already running the thread before we actually run it. We need to store register values on the stack because during a context switch the kernel must later pop the data into the real registers so that the CPU continues the thread with the same state it would have if it was running all along.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,3,'2025-06-23_09-56-34','Setting up a stack for a thread means building the stack frame in memory that the hardware expects to see when it returns into that thread. We dedicated memory for the thread’s stack and use PSP to point to the top of the stack’s region. This is done by referring to MSP’s location. During a context switch, the kernel saves the content of the registers for the outgoing thread on its stack then restores them back for the incoming thread. This lets the CPU continue exactly from where it left off. Additionally, stack setup happens once during thread creation whereas context saving happens during every switch.\nWe need to populate the registers 0 through R12, LR, PC, and XPSR in that order because it is a stack. We need to set up the registers as though the thread had been running the whole time, and the chip will proceed to run the thread. This makes it seem like the thread was interrupted in the middle of execution and can now resume. We need to store register values on the stack because during a context switch the kernel must later pop the data into the real registers so that the CPU continues the thread with the same state it would have if it was running all along.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,3,'2025-06-24_16-53-29','Setting up a stack for a thread means building the stack frame in memory that the hardware expects to see when it returns into that thread. We dedicated memory for the thread’s stack and use PSP to point to the top of the stack’s region. This is done by referring to MSP’s location. During a context switch, the kernel saves the content of the registers for the outgoing thread on its stack then restores them back for the incoming thread. This lets the CPU continue exactly from where it left off. Additionally, stack setup happens once during thread creation whereas context saving happens during every switch.\nWe need to populate the registers 0 through R12, LR, PC, and XPSR in that order because it is a stack. We need to set up the registers as though the thread had been running the whole time, and the chip will proceed to run the thread. This makes it seem like the thread was interrupted in the middle of execution and can now resume. We need to store register values on the stack because during a context switch the kernel must later pop the data into the real registers so that the CPU continues the thread with the same state it would have if it was running all along.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,4,'2025-06-22_07-59-43','If we did not set PSP then the wrong registers would be read while the program runs causing an invalid address or alignment error and resulting in a hard fault. This is because the PC’s contents would be different and thus the print continuously function would not run. Additionally, the other registers will be overwritten which will result in a hard fault. If we did not set PSP, the CPU would still be using the MSP.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,4,'2025-06-22_14-09-03','If __set_PSP(stackptr) is not included in the code, the SVC still tries to pop the stack frame but it does so from MSP which is the wrong stack to be popped. Garbage values are loaded into the registers such as PC, LR and xPSR. The value in PC is garbage hence the CPU raises a hard fault.\nIf we did not set PSP then the wrong registers would be read while the program runs causing an invalid address or alignment error and resulting in a hard fault. This is because the PC’s contents would be different and thus the print continuously function would not run. Additionally, the other registers will be overwritten which will result in a hard fault. If we did not set PSP, the CPU would still be using the MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,4,'2025-06-22_14-32-58','If __set_PSP(stackptr) is not included in the code, the SVC still tries to pop the stack frame but it does so from MSP which is the wrong stack to be popped. Garbage values are loaded into the registers such as PC, LR and xPSR. The value in PC is garbage hence the CPU raises a hard fault.\nIf we did not set PSP then the wrong registers would be read while the program runs causing an invalid address or alignment error and resulting in a hard fault. This is because the PC’s contents would be different and thus the print continuously function would not run. Additionally, the other registers will be overwritten which will result in a hard fault. If we did not set PSP, the CPU would still be using the MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,4,'2025-06-22_14-50-29','If __set_PSP(stackptr) is not included in the code, the SVC still tries to pop the stack frame but it does so from MSP which is the wrong stack to be popped. Garbage values are loaded into the registers such as PC, LR and xPSR. The value in PC is garbage hence the CPU raises a hard fault.\nIf we did not set PSP then the wrong registers would be read while the program runs causing an invalid address or alignment error and resulting in a hard fault. This is because the PC’s contents would be different and thus the print continuously function would not run. Additionally, the other registers will be overwritten which will result in a hard fault. If we did not set PSP, the CPU would still be using the MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,4,'2025-06-23_09-41-29','If __set_PSP(stackptr) is not included in the code, the SVC still tries to pop the stack frame but it does so from MSP which is the wrong stack to be popped. Garbage values are loaded into the registers such as PC, LR and xPSR. The value in PC is garbage hence the CPU raises a hard fault.\nIf we did not set PSP then the wrong registers would be read while the program runs causing an invalid address or alignment error and resulting in a hard fault. This is because the PC’s contents would be different and thus the print continuously function would not run. Additionally, the other registers will be overwritten which will result in a hard fault. If we did not set PSP, the CPU would still be using the MSP.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,4,'2025-06-23_09-56-34','The processor uses MSP by default which is reserved for kernel level code. Threads must use the PSP so that each one has a private stack area in memory. The use of PSP ensures that thread operations don’t disturb the kernel stack which is pointed to by MSP. If __set_PSP(stackptr) is not included in the code, the SVC still tries to pop the stack frame but it does so from MSP which is the wrong stack to be popped. Garbage values are loaded into the registers such as PC, LR and xPSR. The value in PC is garbage hence the CPU raises a hard fault.\nIf we did not set PSP then the wrong registers would be read while the program runs causing an invalid address or alignment error and resulting in a hard fault. This is because the PC’s contents would be different and thus the print continuously function would not run. Additionally, the other registers will be overwritten which will result in a hard fault. If we did not set PSP, the CPU would still be using the MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,4,'2025-06-24_16-53-29','The processor uses MSP by default which is reserved for kernel level code. Threads must use the PSP so that each one has a private stack area in memory. The use of PSP ensures that thread operations don’t disturb the kernel stack which is pointed to by MSP. If __set_PSP(stackptr) is not included in the code, the SVC still tries to pop the stack frame but it does so from MSP which is the wrong stack to be popped. Garbage values are loaded into the registers such as PC, LR and xPSR. The value in PC is garbage hence the CPU raises a hard fault.\nIf we did not set PSP then the wrong registers would be read while the program runs causing an invalid address or alignment error and resulting in a hard fault. This is because the PC’s contents would be different and thus the print continuously function would not run. Additionally, the other registers will be overwritten which will result in a hard fault. If we did not set PSP, the CPU would still be using the MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,5,'2025-06-22_07-59-43','The link register was given a (0XA) address. When the print_continuously function returns after printing once, the CPU will jump to the (0XA) address which does not have any significance and was just arbitrarily chosen. The vector table has no value there so a hard fault is raised.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,5,'2025-06-22_14-09-03','The link register was given a (0XA) address. When the print_continuously function returns after printing once, the CPU will jump to the (0XA) address as it was loaded into PC from LR. Address 0xA is not a valid memory location so the next fetch results in a hard fault. The top thread is not called so it contains no return address. For this reason we used an arbitrary address (0XA).\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,5,'2025-06-22_14-32-58','In function calls, the link register contains the return address. After the function finishes, it returns to the caller of the function. The return address is kept on the stack. But in a top thread, there is no function caller hence there is no valid return address. For this reason, nothing is pushed onto the stack to indicate where to return. Henceforth, if the thread function executes a return, the value in LR will be treated as the return address and moved into PC. The link register was given an arbitrary address (0XA). When the print_continuously function returns after printing once, the CPU will jump to the (0XA) address as it was loaded into PC from LR. Address 0xA is not a valid memory location so the next fetch results in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,5,'2025-06-22_14-50-29','In function calls, the link register contains the return address. After the function finishes, it returns to the caller of the function. The return address is kept on the stack. But in a top thread, there is no function caller hence there is no valid return address. For this reason, nothing is pushed onto the stack to indicate where to return. Henceforth, if the thread function executes a return, the value in LR will be treated as the return address and moved into PC. The link register was given an arbitrary address (0XA). When the print_continuously function returns after printing once, the CPU will jump to the (0XA) address as it was loaded into PC from LR. Address 0xA is not a valid memory location so the next fetch results in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,5,'2025-06-23_09-41-29','In function calls, the link register contains the return address. After the function finishes, it returns to the caller of the function. The return address is kept on the stack. But in a top thread, there is no function caller hence there is no valid return address. For this reason, nothing is pushed onto the stack to indicate where to return. Henceforth, if the thread function executes a return, the value in LR will be treated as the return address and moved into PC. The link register was given an arbitrary address (0XA). When the print_continuously function returns after printing once, the CPU will jump to the (0XA) address as it was loaded into PC from LR. Address 0xA is not a valid memory location so the next fetch results in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,5,'2025-06-23_09-56-34','In function calls, the link register contains the return address. After the function finishes, it returns to the caller of the function. The return address is kept on the stack. But in a top thread, there is no function caller hence there is no valid return address. For this reason, nothing is pushed onto the stack to indicate where to return. Henceforth, if the thread function executes a return, the value in LR will be treated as the return address and moved into PC. The link register was given an arbitrary address (0XA). When the print_continuously function returns after printing once, the CPU will jump to the (0XA) address as it was loaded into PC from LR. Address 0xA is not a valid memory location so the next fetch results in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',2,5,'2025-06-24_16-53-29','In function calls, the link register contains the return address. After the function finishes, it returns to the caller of the function. The return address is kept on the stack. But in a top thread, there is no function caller hence there is no valid return address. For this reason, nothing is pushed onto the stack to indicate where to return. Henceforth, if the thread function executes a return, the value in LR will be treated as the return address and moved into PC. The link register was given an arbitrary address (0XA). When the print_continuously function returns after printing once, the CPU will jump to the (0XA) address as it was loaded into PC from LR. Address 0xA is not a valid memory location so the next fetch results in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,1,'2025-06-24_16-58-45','When multiple *.c files get compiled, each file is first compiled separately into an object file. In this step, the code is turned into machine code. Then, when all the files are compiled, the linker takes the object files and combines them into one executable. The linker is responsible for handling the references between files such as the functions and variables.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,1,'2025-06-24_17-34-18','Before compilation, each file is preprocessed to handle directives such as #include and macros such as #define. When multiple *.c files get compiled, each file is first compiled separately into an object file. In this step, the code is turned into machine code. Then, when all the files are compiled, the linker takes the object files and combines them into one executable. The linker is responsible for handling the references between files such as the functions and variables.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,1,'2025-07-02_17-32-48','Before compilation, each file is preprocessed to handle directives such as #include and macros such as #define. When multiple *.c files get compiled, each file is first compiled separately into an object file. In this step, the code is turned into machine code. Then, when all the files are compiled, the linker takes the object files and combines them into one executable. The linker is responsible for handling the references between files such as the functions and variables.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,2,'2025-06-24_16-58-45','Using an include directive on a *.c file is bad because it copies .c files into others. This can lead to multiple definitions of functions and variables which could lead to an unexpected error. It also messes up the compilation method described in the answer for ',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,2,'2025-06-24_17-34-18','Using an include directive on a *.c file is bad because it copies .c files into others. This can lead to multiple definitions of functions and variables which could lead to an unexpected error. It also messes up the compilation method described in the answer for ',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,2,'2025-07-02_17-32-48','Using an include directive on a *.c file is bad because it copies .c files into others. This can lead to multiple definitions of functions and variables which could lead to an unexpected error. It also messes up the compilation method described in the answer for ',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,3,'2025-06-24_16-58-45','Firstly, the user must write a function that dictates what the thread will do when it runs. This function will be written in main.c as threads are not kernel functions\nThe second step is to call the osKernelInitialize function to set up the kernel’s variables\nThird, a stack must be allocated for the thread using the osCreateThread and stack_allocator functions and passing in the thread function.\nFinally, we can call osKernelStart as this will launch the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,3,'2025-06-24_17-34-18','Firstly, the user must write a function that dictates what the thread will do when it runs. This function will be written in main.c as threads are not kernel functions\nThe second step is to call the osKernelInitialize function to set up the kernel’s variables\nThird, a stack must be allocated for the thread using the osCreateThread and stack_allocator functions and passing in the thread function. The user does not manage the stack allocation directly as it is handled by the kernel. The user only provides the function pointer and the kernel is responsible for setting up the stack.\nFinally, we can call osKernelStart as this will launch the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,3,'2025-07-02_17-32-48','Firstly, the user must write a function that dictates what the thread will do when it runs. This function will be written in main.c as threads are not kernel functions\nThe second step is to call the osKernelInitialize function to set up the kernel’s variables\nThird, a stack must be allocated for the thread using the osCreateThread and stack_allocator functions and passing in the thread function. The user does not manage the stack allocation directly as it is handled by the kernel. The user only provides the function pointer and the kernel is responsible for setting up the stack.\nFinally, we can call osKernelStart as this will launch the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,4,'2025-06-24_16-58-45','The stack pool is a space in memory reserved to allocate stacks for threads. Every thread needs it’s own stack space to store variables and function calls. In future labs, the stack pool will be used to reserve space for stack frames to be used by other threads when we enable multiple threads to exist in our OS. This allows the threads to work without overwriting each other’s data.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,4,'2025-06-24_17-34-18','The stack pool is a space in memory reserved to allocate stacks for threads. Every thread needs it’s own stack space to store variables and function calls. Rather than each thread having a completely separate stack, the OS subdivides the pool into equally sized spaces for each thread. In future labs, the stack pool will be used to reserve space for stack frames to be used by other threads when we enable multiple threads to exist in our OS. This allows the threads to work without overwriting each other’s data.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,4,'2025-07-02_17-32-48','The stack pool is a space in memory reserved to allocate stacks for threads. Every thread needs it’s own stack space to store variables and function calls. Rather than each thread having a completely separate stack, the OS subdivides the pool into equally sized spaces for each thread. In future labs, the stack pool will be used to reserve space for stack frames to be used by other threads when we enable multiple threads to exist in our OS. This allows the threads to work without overwriting each other’s data.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,5,'2025-06-24_16-58-45','The kernel is the code that is responsible for handling threads, memory and low level tasks in general. Kernel functions and data are put in kernel.h and kernel.c. Meanwhile, the user is the code written on the OS such as thread functions and the main function in main.c. The distinction is important because it prevents users from mistakenly or intentionally modifying core system code which could lead to broken core OS logic. It also allows users to rely on clean and error-free functions without having to understand the logic behind it.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,5,'2025-06-24_17-34-18','The kernel is the code that is responsible for handling threads, memory and low level tasks in general. Kernel functions and data are put in kernel.h and kernel.c. Meanwhile, the user is the code written on the OS such as thread functions and the main function in main.c. The distinction is important because it prevents users from mistakenly or intentionally modifying core system code which could lead to broken core OS logic. Mixing user logic and kernel logic can crash the system if critical operations such as memory management is altered. Keeping a distinction between user and kernel allows users to rely on clean and error-free functions without having to understand the logic behind it.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',3,5,'2025-07-02_17-32-48','The kernel is the code that is responsible for handling threads, memory and low level tasks in general. Kernel functions and data are put in kernel.h and kernel.c. Meanwhile, the user is the code written on the OS such as thread functions and the main function in main.c. The distinction is important because it prevents users from mistakenly or intentionally modifying core system code which could lead to broken core OS logic. Mixing user logic and kernel logic can crash the system if critical operations such as memory management is altered. Keeping a distinction between user and kernel allows users to rely on clean and error-free functions without having to understand the logic behind it.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,1,'2025-07-02_21-44-13','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by treating the queue as a rotating list of runnable threads. Each time a thread yields, the scheduler removes its TCB from the front of the queue and puts it in the back of the queue. The new thread in the front of the queue then runs. As a result, the threads are cycled through in order.\nWhen a thread finishes or is created, we can push back or erase from the queue in constant time without having to leaves gaps between elements of the queue. We would not have to shift the elements to fill in the gaps either. In an array, we have to either waste slots due to empty spaces between elements or use a O(n) time complexity to compact the array. Additionally, the use of a queue means that we do not have to keep track of indices as the front of the queue always contains the next thread to be run. As a result, we do not have to calculate the next thread to be run.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,1,'2025-07-02_22-00-46','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by treating the queue as a rotating list of runnable threads. Each time a thread yields, the scheduler removes its TCB from the front of the queue and puts it in the back of the queue. The new thread in the front of the queue then runs. As a result, the threads are cycled through in order.\nWhen a thread finishes or is created, we can push back or erase from the queue in constant time without having to leaves gaps between elements of the queue. We would not have to shift the elements to fill in the gaps either. In an array, we have to either waste slots due to empty spaces between elements or use a O(n) time complexity to compact the array. Additionally, the use of a queue means that we do not have to keep track of indices as the front of the queue always contains the next thread to be run. As a result, we do not have to calculate the next thread to be run.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,1,'2025-07-02_22-12-41','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by treating the queue as a rotating list of runnable threads. Each time a thread yields, the scheduler removes its TCB from the front of the queue and puts it in the back of the queue. The new thread in the front of the queue then runs. As a result, the threads are cycled through in order.\nWhen a thread finishes or is created, we can push back or erase from the queue in constant time without having to leaves gaps between elements of the queue. We would not have to shift the elements to fill in the gaps either. In an array, we have to either waste slots due to empty spaces between elements or use a O(n) time complexity to compact the array. Additionally, the use of a queue means that we do not have to keep track of indices as the front of the queue always contains the next thread to be run. As a result, we do not have to calculate the next thread to be run.\nQueues also introduce some overhead. Each TCB needs an extra pointer if the queue is implemented as a linked list and dynamic memory management may be required. Compared to a fixed array, this adds memory and also performance costs.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,1,'2025-07-02_22-20-40','Round-robin scheduling with a queue works by treating the queue as a rotating list of runnable threads. Each time a thread yields, the scheduler removes its TCB from the front of the queue and puts it in the back of the queue. The new thread in the front of the queue then runs. As a result, the threads are cycled through in order.\nWhen a thread finishes or is created, we can push back or erase from the queue in constant time without having to leaves gaps between elements of the queue. We would not have to shift the elements to fill in the gaps either. In an array, we have to either waste slots due to empty spaces between elements or use a O(n) time complexity to compact the array. Additionally, the use of a queue means that we do not have to keep track of indices as the front of the queue always contains the next thread to be run. As a result, we do not have to calculate the next thread to be run.\nQueues also introduce some overhead. Each TCB needs an extra pointer if the queue is implemented as a linked list and dynamic memory management may be required. Compared to a fixed array, this adds memory and also performance costs.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,2,'2025-07-02_21-44-13','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current state of the thread being run and loading the state of the new/next thread to be run. When it runs, the registers R4-R11 of the current thread are saved on the thread’s stack. Then osSched() is called. This function updates the current thread index and loads the stack pointer of the next thread with __set_PSP() function. PendSV_Handler restores R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack and exits. Control is given to the next thread. As a result, the CPU continues execution as if the new thread had always been running. It is important to note that the context switch is done in an interrupt.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,2,'2025-07-02_22-00-46','First the PendSV_Handler read the Process Stack Pointer to get the location of the currently running thread. This is essential because all the saved registers (R4-R11) are to be pushed onto that thread’s stack. By reading the PSP first, the handler is able to identify where to store the content.\nThe PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current state of the thread being run and loading the state of the new/next thread to be run. When it runs, the registers R4-R11 of the current thread are saved on the thread’s stack. Then osSched() is called. This function updates the current thread index and loads the stack pointer of the next thread with __set_PSP() function. PendSV_Handler restores R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack and exits. Control is given to the next thread. As a result, the CPU continues execution as if the new thread had always been running. It is important to note that the context switch is done in an interrupt.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,2,'2025-07-02_22-12-41','First the PendSV_Handler read the Process Stack Pointer to get the location of the currently running thread. This is essential because all the saved registers (R4-R11) are to be pushed onto that thread’s stack. By reading the PSP first, the handler is able to identify where to store the content.\nThe PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current state of the thread being run and loading the state of the new/next thread to be run. When it runs, the registers R4-R11 of the current thread are saved on the thread’s stack. Then osSched() is called. This function updates the current thread index and loads the stack pointer of the next thread with __set_PSP() function. PendSV_Handler restores R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack and exits. Control is given to the next thread. As a result, the CPU continues execution as if the new thread had always been running. It is important to note that the context switch is done in an interrupt.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,2,'2025-07-02_22-20-40','First the PendSV_Handler read the Process Stack Pointer to get the location of the currently running thread. This is essential because all the saved registers (R4-R11) are to be pushed onto that thread’s stack. By reading the PSP first, the handler is able to identify where to store the content.\nThe PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current state of the thread being run and loading the state of the new/next thread to be run. When it runs, the registers R4-R11 of the current thread are saved on the thread’s stack. Then osSched() is called. This function updates the current thread index and loads the stack pointer of the next thread with __set_PSP() function. PendSV_Handler restores R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack and exits. Control is given to the next thread. As a result, the CPU continues execution as if the new thread had always been running. It is important to note that the context switch is done in an interrupt.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,3,'2025-07-02_21-44-13','This is a bad idea because if the highest priority task never yields, it will run forever, and all the other tasks will starve. In the same way, if there are always high priority tasks to be run, the low priority tasks will never run. A Round-robin scheduler ensures that every thread will be run by the CPU for some time.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,3,'2025-07-02_22-00-46','This is a bad idea because if the highest priority task never yields, it prevents fairness as it will run forever, and all the other tasks will starve. In the same way, if there are always high priority tasks to be run, the low priority tasks will never run. A Round-robin scheduler ensures that every thread will be run by the CPU for some time.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,3,'2025-07-02_22-12-41','This is a bad idea because if the highest priority task never yields, it prevents fairness as it will run forever, and all the other tasks will starve. In the same way, if there are always high priority tasks to be run, the low priority tasks will never run. A Round-robin scheduler ensures that every thread will be run by the CPU for some time.\n',0,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,3,'2025-07-02_22-20-40','This is a bad idea because if the highest priority task never yields, it prevents fairness as it will run forever, and all the other tasks will starve. If the scheduler always picks the task with the highest priority, then only the highest-priority thread will run regardless of whether other threads are waiting or how long they have waited. If there are always high priority tasks to be run, the low priority tasks will never run. A Round-robin scheduler ensures that every thread will be run by the CPU for some time.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,4,'2025-07-02_21-44-13','Writing code in C makes it easier to read, write and debug. It allows us to use variables, loops and conditions in our code logic. The disadvantage is that calling C from an interrupt handler requires that the C functions do not interfere with the processor state. In parts such as saving or restoring registers, assembly is still needed. Assembly is error-prone and tedious to bug.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,4,'2025-07-02_22-00-46','Writing code in C makes it easier to read, write and debug. It allows us to use variables, loops and conditions to convey logic more clearly. The disadvantage is that calling C from an interrupt handler requires that the C functions do not interfere with the processor state. In parts such as saving or restoring registers, assembly is still needed. Assembly code is more error-prone, tedious to debug and tedious to write.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,4,'2025-07-02_22-12-41','Writing code in C makes it easier to read, write and debug. It allows us to use variables, loops and conditions to convey logic more clearly. The disadvantage is that calling C from an interrupt handler requires that the C functions do not interfere with the processor state. In parts such as saving or restoring registers, assembly is still needed. Assembly code is more error-prone, tedious to debug and tedious to write.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,4,'2025-07-02_22-20-40','Writing code in C makes it easier to read, write and debug. It allows us to use variables, loops and conditions to convey logic more clearly. The disadvantage is that calling C from an interrupt handler requires that the C functions do not interfere with the processor state. In parts such as saving or restoring registers, assembly is still needed. Assembly code is more error-prone, tedious to debug and tedious to write.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,5,'2025-07-02_21-44-13','The OS will still work because after the thread prints and calls osYield(), the osSched() function will try to switch to the next thread which happens to be the exact same thread. The round-robin calculation for the index of the next thread to be run will still result in the index of the same thread. As a result, the thread yields to itself.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,5,'2025-07-02_22-00-46','The OS will still work because after the thread prints and calls osYield(), the osSched() function will try to switch to the next thread which happens to be the exact same thread. The round-robin calculation for the index of the next thread to be run will still result in the index of the same thread. As a result, since there is only one thread, osYield() results in the same thread being selected again.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,5,'2025-07-02_22-12-41','The OS will still work because after the thread prints and calls osYield(), the osSched() function will try to switch to the next thread which happens to be the exact same thread. The round-robin calculation for the index of the next thread to be run will still result in the index of the same thread. As a result, since there is only one thread, osYield() results in the same thread being selected again.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',4,5,'2025-07-02_22-20-40','The OS will still work because after the thread prints and calls osYield(), the osSched() function will try to switch to the next thread which happens to be the exact same thread. The round-robin calculation for the index of the next thread to be run will still result in the index of the same thread. As a result, since there is only one thread, osYield() results in the same thread being selected again.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',5,1,'2025-07-16_20-40-50','The user creates some data in a struct and passes a pointer to the data when osCreateThread() is called. The pointer is what the thread will receive as its argument when it begins execution. In the osCreateThread() function, the OS prepares the stack for the thread by placing an exception stack frame on top of the stack memory. The stack is what the CPU distributes into registers when the thread is scheduled to run. When the CPU switches context and runs the thread, it performs an exception return which tells the hardware to pop the values from the stack into the correct registers and being execution of the thread. Basically, the user passes a pointer to existing data to osCreateThread() and the OS allocates a new stack for the thread but does not copy the data onto it. The OS places the pointer in the right spot on the stack so that the CPU will load it into R0. When the thread starts, the function gets the pointer and can use the data.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',5,2,'2025-07-16_20-40-50','It is dangerous to call osYield() inside PendSV_Handler because PendSV is the context switch. If osYield() is called inside PendSV_Handler, another SVC is triggered which triggers another PendSV. This creates an infinite loop of nested exceptions and the system will crash. In SVC_Handler, the same problem arises. osYield() issues another SVC which means that we are inside an SVC calling another SVC. The CPU doesn’t re-enter the same exception properly and registers are corrupted. If osYield() was placed inside the SysTick_Handler, we would be pending an SVC from inside an interrupt which would trigger PendSV again. This may cause context switches mid-interrupt. These handlers are already part of the context-switch mechanism and should use internal scheduling logic instead of triggering more exceptions.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',5,3,'2025-07-16_20-40-50','In the lab, the OS clock which is driven by SysTick_Handler fires once every 1ms. This gives the kernel consistent time base for pre-emption, delays and time-slicing. Changing it to 0.1ms means the timer  now fires ten times more frequently. The benefits of doing this include higher timer precision as well as more responsive pre-emption. Higher timing precision means that threads can be scheduled with more miniscule and precise timings such as 0.2ms. This is useful for RTOS that need low latency responses. More responsive pre-emption means that threads that hog the CPU can be pre-empted more quickly. However, changing the OS clock to be 1ms means that there is higher CPU overhead as the SysTick_Handler runs 10x more, effectively taking up more of the CPU’s time. Additionally, there is increased power consumption as the CPU wakes up more often. This is bad for systems where energy efficiency is important.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',5,4,'2025-07-16_20-40-50','From the user’s perspective, in co-operative multitasking, the user must call osYield() in the thread code to allow another thread to run while in pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically pre-empts threads when their timeslice expires. No action is need from the user. In co-operative multitasking, the programmer has full control over when the switching happens while in pre-emptive multitasking, the OS takes control of timing and switching. From the OS’s perspective, the OS only switches when the current thread calls osYield() in co-operative multitasking. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS uses a hardware timer interrupt (SysTick) to decide when to switch. In Co-operative multitasking, the OS only needs to save/restore context when a thread yields. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS must be able to save/restore thread state from any point in execution.\n',1,'s25'),('sbhatnag',5,5,'2025-07-16_20-40-50','Both osYield() and the timer interrupt SysTick_Handler trigger a context switch by pend-ing the PendSV interrupt. In both co-operative and pre-emptive context switching, PendSV is the only function that actually performs the context switch. Hence the shared path allows the two styles of multitasking to work together. If a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily gives up the CPU regardless of whether its timeslice is over. If a thread doesn’t yield but runs too long, the OS forcibly switches it out through pre-emption. Since both methods update the same scheduler state, they can work together without conflict.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',1,1,'2025-06-27_12-03-09','It’s the Hardware Abstraction Layer which contains most of the setup code which is difficult to write thus acting as a safety rail between the code and registers. It’s used because it eases maintenance and accelerates development.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',1,1,'2025-06-27_20-46-36','It’s the Hardware Abstraction Layer which contains most of the setup code which is difficult to write thus acting as a safety rail between the code and registers. It’s used because it eases maintenance and accelerates development.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',1,2,'2025-06-27_12-03-09','The printf function in embedded c can’t communicate directly with UART, by using __io_putchar, printf sends every character it wants to get printed to USART2. Without this printf would would either do nothing or write to some undefined console channel\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',1,2,'2025-06-27_20-46-36','Printf() in C library’s are designed for desktop environments and it doesn’t know about the boards UART, USB or display hardware. By providing a function like the “__io_putchar(int ch)” it’s able to retarget printf() to any I/O channel you choose. Because printf internally calls a single character output, using “__io_putchar(int ch)” allows you to decouple your application code from a specific peripheral, increase reusability and ensures portability as different boards and peripherals can reuse the same printf based debug/logging code.\nWithout this retargeting, printf() wouldn’t do anything or it’d attempt to write to an undefined output channel.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',1,3,'2025-06-27_12-03-09','It’s not possible to print out the MSP register or the exact stack pointer values with printf alone because they are located in CPU registers and not variables. The debugger allows us to see all the register values and they were the actual loaded values which is why it was possible for us to verify our calls to set psp and control. Printf can’t read into the CPUs’ core registers.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',1,3,'2025-06-27_20-46-36','It’s not possible to print out the MSP register or the exact stack pointer values with printf alone because they are located in CPU registers and not variables. The debugger allows us to see all the register values and they were the actual loaded values which is why it was possible for us to verify our calls to set psp and control. Printf can’t read into the CPUs’ core registers.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',1,4,'2025-06-27_12-03-09','It starts off by reading the initial MSP value at 0x0. After which we compute the new PSP value and because we want to start 0x400 bytes below the top we subtract the MSP_INIT_VAL by 0x400. Lastly we load PSP and switch to it.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',1,4,'2025-06-27_20-46-36','It starts by reading the main stack top from the vector table using “uint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0” this line gives the address of where the hardware initially set MSP on reset.\nWe then compute the process stack base by subtracting the desired stack size (0x400) “uint32_t PSP_val = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL – 0x400;” because stacks grow downwards what this line does is that it reserves a spot 0x400 bytes below the main stack for the process stack.\nLastly we load and enable the PSP with CMSIS intrinsics and a pipeline barrier.\nKnowing that “MSP_INIT_VAL” is essential because it anchors us to the actual memory region allocated for the main stack. Without a reference we won’t know where it’s safe to place the second stack so there would be a possibility of a collision with the heap, data sections or maybe even corrupt the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',1,5,'2025-06-27_12-03-09','Firstly we need to choose the main stack size, the thread stack size and the number of equal sized stacks, we then allocate an array of Number of equal sized stacks x thread stack size bytes in RAM. Then we compute the start of a threads stack and lastly we initialise each threads psp. It’s essentially the same logic as the subtract and offset we did previously.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',1,5,'2025-06-27_20-46-36','If we needed “N” thread stacks of the size of each specified by “THREAD_STACK_SIZE”, known at compile time.\nWe would start by allocating a buffer in RAM that must be contiguous (static uint32_t threadStacks[N_threads][THREAD_STACK_SIZE/4];).\nAfter which we would compute each stack base relative to “MSP_INIT_VAL” and “MAIN_STACK_SIZE” (uint32_t* stack_j = (uint32_t*)(MSP_INIT_VAL - MAIN_STACK_SIZE - (j+1)*THREAD_STACK_SIZE);)\nThe next step would be to enforce alignment but it’s important to note that on the Cortex-M4 you must maintain 8-byte alignment which is already done if the “THREAD_STACK_SIZE” is a multiple of 8.\nLastly, overlap must be prevented by spacing each stakc in its own “THREAD_STACK_SIZE” chunk, making sure the buffers won’t be able to collide.\nIn code we could get by with just indexing into the 2D “threadStacks” array or by calculating the pointer arithmetic. This works by applying the same subtract and offset logic as we had learned previously.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',2,1,'2025-06-27_14-24-08','When “jumpAssembly((void*)print_continuously);” was executed the compiler ha dplaced the addresss of “print_continuously” into the register R0. Inside “jumpAssembly” the instruction “MOV PC, R0” works by copying the value in the PC and since the PC always points to the next instruction to fetch, the core began fetching from the start of “print_continuously” rather than going back to “main()” because “print_continuously” has an infinite loop.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',2,1,'2025-06-27_20-13-11','When “jumpAssembly((void*)print_continuously);” was executed the compiler had placed the address of “print_continuously” into the register R0. Inside “jumpAssembly” the instruction “MOV PC, R0” works by copying the 32-bit value from R0 into the Program counters. Since the Program Counter always points at the next instruction to fetch, overwriting it with the function’s entry address causes the CPU to immediately jump to and begin executing “print_continuously” which in turn performs an unconditional branch to that function because “print_continuously” contains an infinite loop, execution never return to “main()”.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',2,2,'2025-06-27_14-24-08','A system call is a way for application code to ask the OS to perform service. In our case we can issue system calls by executing a SVC instruction which triggers the processor.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',2,2,'2025-06-27_20-13-11','A system call is a controlled interface by which application code requests services from the OS’s privileged kernel. Instead of letting the user code directly modify hardware or system resources the application invokes a thin wrapper which in turn causes a software interrupt that transitions the execution into the “SVC_Handler” in kernel space. The kernel will then examine the call number, it’ll perform the requested operation and return control while making sure that only safe routines are able to access critical hardware or OS data structures.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',2,3,'2025-06-27_14-24-08','“setup a thread’s stack” means allocating a fresh memory region and loading it with the exact register context the CPU would expect if the thread had been interrupted. It’s setup by allocation a buffer and making “stackptr” point to the high address. After which a push is executed by decrementing the “stackptr”. Lastly in the SVC handler case for “RUN_FIRST_THREAD” we then do “__set_PSP(stackptr)”.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',2,3,'2025-06-27_20-13-11','Setting up a thread’s stack means allocating a fresh memory region and pre-filling it with the exact register context the CPU expects to see upon an exception return.\nWe specifically allocate a buffer and position a pointer at the top (stackptr).\nWe then push the registers in the precise order the Cortex-M pops them on exceptrion return; xPSR (thumb bit set), PC (entry address of the thread function), LR, R12, R3, R2, R2, R0 followed by R11 to R4 if needed.\nIn the “RUN_FIRST_THREAD” case, we would then call “__set_PSP(stackptr)”, switch into thread mode and return from the SVC exception.\nUpon exception return, the CPU pops the values back into hardware registers and resumes execution at PC as if the thread had been running the entire time.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',2,4,'2025-06-27_14-24-08','If we don’t do this then the process stack pointer remains at 0. When the SVC handler returns using “EXC_RETURN”, the CPU will try to pop the saved context from the address in PSP however PSP is 0 thus pointing at an unmapped region so attempting to read other registers from the invalid address will cause a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',2,4,'2025-06-27_20-13-11','The CPU uses MSP on reset by default. However, our thread must run the PSP with its own stack region. If we remove “__set_PSP(stackptr)”, PSP will remain zero or pointing at the MSP region. When the SVC handler returns using “EXC_RETURN” , the CPU will try to pop the saved context from the address in PSP however PSP is 0 thus pointing at an unmapped region so attempting to read other registers from the invalid address will cause a hard fault before the thread even begins.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',2,5,'2025-06-27_14-24-08','If it printed something once and then returned, the processor would pop the next value off PSP into PC. Since we never pushed a valid return address for a non-existent caller the popped value wouldn’t be useful as it would be pointing to unmapped or invalid memory thus fetching an instruction from that address would cause unwanted behaviour.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',2,5,'2025-06-27_20-13-11','If the thread function executed only one printf() and then returned, the exception return sequence would pop whatever value was stacked into the “return address” slot directly into the Program Counter. Because that slot had never been initialised with a valid address, the CPU would attempt to fetch an instruction from invalid memory. The bus fault this causes triggers the HardFault exception, landing in “HardFault_Handler” and stopping execution.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,1,'2025-06-27_15-07-49','It starts off with each *.c file in Core>Src being compiled independently by the compiler into an object file, each object file contains its own symbol table. The linker then takes all the object files, startup code and library objects and resolves all the undefined symbols by finding exactly one definition, performs address relocation and returns a single image (binary or hex).\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,1,'2025-06-27_19-41-06','The process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable begins with preprocessing. This is the step in which the compiler handles all directives such as #include, #define and conditional compilation, this is done by copying the contents of the header files and expanding macros thus producing a complete source file for each *.c file that is ready for compilation. The next phase is the compilation phase in which each *.c file in Core>Src is being compiled independently by the compiler into an object file, each object file contains its own symbol table. The linker then takes all the object files, startup code and library objects and resolves all the undefined symbols by finding exactly one definition, performs address relocation and returns a single image (binary or hex).\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,1,'2025-06-27_21-06-57','The process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable begins with preprocessing. This is the step in which the compiler handles all directives such as #include, #define and conditional compilation, this is done by copying the contents of the header files and expanding macros thus producing a complete source file for each *.c file that is ready for compilation. The next phase is the compilation phase in which each *.c file in Core>Src is being compiled independently by the compiler into an object file, each object file contains its own symbol table. The linker then takes all the object files, startup code and library objects and resolves all the undefined symbols by finding exactly one definition, performs address relocation and returns a single image (binary or hex).\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,1,'2025-06-27_22-16-47','The process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable begins with preprocessing. This is the step in which the compiler handles all directives such as #include, #define and conditional compilation, this is done by copying the contents of the header files and expanding macros thus producing a complete source file for each *.c file that is ready for compilation. The next phase is the compilation phase in which each *.c file in Core>Src is being compiled independently by the compiler into an object file, each object file contains its own symbol table. The linker then takes all the object files, startup code and library objects and resolves all the undefined symbols by finding exactly one definition, performs address relocation and returns a single image (binary or hex).\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,2,'2025-06-27_15-07-49','We can’t use “#include” on a *.c file because of the possibility of duplicating definitions, if the *.c file is included into another file various function and variable definitions will appear twice when the files are compiled which would lead to link errors.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,2,'2025-06-27_19-41-06','We can’t use “#include” on a *.c file because of the possibility of duplicating definitions, if the *.c file is included into another file various function and variable definitions will appear twice when the files are compiled which would lead to link errors.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,2,'2025-06-27_21-06-57','We can’t use “#include” on a *.c file because of the possibility of duplicating definitions, if the *.c file is included into another file various function and variable definitions will appear twice when the files are compiled which would lead to link errors.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,2,'2025-06-27_22-16-47','We can’t use “#include” on a *.c file because of the possibility of duplicating definitions, if the *.c file is included into another file various function and variable definitions will appear twice when the files are compiled which would lead to link errors.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,3,'2025-06-27_15-07-49','Write the thread function in the file main.c\nInitialize the kernel by calling “osKernelInitialize()”, this will setup the stack pool base and global kernel data.\nCreate the thread with “osCreateThread(thread_function)”, this allocates a tack from the pool and builds the necessary initial register content in order for the thread to run.\nStart the kernel by calling “osKernelStart()”\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,3,'2025-06-27_19-41-06','Write the thread function in the file main.c\nInitialize the kernel by calling “osKernelInitialize()”, this will setup the stack pool base and global kernel data.\nCreate the thread with “osCreateThread(thread_function)”, this allocates a tack from the pool and builds the necessary initial register content in order for the thread to run.\nStart the kernel by calling “osKernelStart()”\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,3,'2025-06-27_21-06-57','Write the thread function in the file main.c\nInitialize the kernel by calling “osKernelInitialize()”, this will setup the stack pool base and global kernel data.\nCreate the thread with “osCreateThread(thread_function)”, this allocates a tack from the pool and builds the necessary initial register content in order for the thread to run.\nStart the kernel by calling “osKernelStart()”\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,3,'2025-06-27_22-16-47','We must define the threads entry route. In the main source file, we must implement a standalone C function that contains the code we want the thread to run.\nWe initialise the kernel. Before creating any threads the OS’s initialization routine must be invoked, this is what configures the stack pool and internal kernel data structures.\nRegister the thread with kernel. We hand the kernel the threads entry function. This is what carves out a fixed size stack from the pool and preloads the initial register context so the CPU can resume into that function.\nStart the scheduler. Finally we can invoke the kernels start mechanism which switches the processor into thread mode and begins executing the newly created thread.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,4,'2025-06-27_15-07-49','The stack pool is a region of the RAM which is reserved for the sole purpose of storing thread stacks. It can be used in future labs because it will allow the kernel to manage the stacks for various threads seamlessly.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,4,'2025-06-27_19-41-06','The stack pool is a block of memory reserved by the OS to store the stacks that are used by the kernel and its threads. The kernel uses its stack (normally accessed through the MSP) for system level operations while each thread uses a portion of the stack pool via the PSP. Each thread will need its own dedicated stack in order to keep track of function calls and register values during execution. This organized allocation helps ensure stability, if the stack pool isn’t managed properly the system could run out of memory or overwrite existing data.\nIn future labs the stack pool will allow the OS to switch between threads and this will be done by saving and restoring their stack contents.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,4,'2025-06-27_21-06-57','At the time of compilation a contiguous region of RAM is reserved through “_Min_Stack_Size”, this area is the stack pool. It’s positioned immediately below the initial MSP location and is set aside to be used exclusively for all kernel and thread stacks. Rather than using one large stack the kernel divides the pool into equal sized chunks with each of these chunks being the size of one threads’ stack. The bump pointer is used to track the next free address in the stack pool (the bump pointer is a global pointer and is stored in the kernel).\nThe allocation mechanism works by first initialising on “osKernelInitialize()”, by doing this the bump pointer is set to the top of the pool. Then it checks per thread allocation, with each call to “osCreateThread()” subtracts “THREAD_STACK_SIZE” from that bump pointer thus giving us a new top of stack address. Then there’s the alignment check which basically works by making both the pool base and “THREAD_STACK_SIZE” a multiple of 8 so that every stack is 8-byte aligned. Lastly there’s exhaustion handling. If subtracting one more “THREAD_STACK_SIZE” would move it below the bottom of the pool, the allocator will return NULL and “osCreateThread()” fails preventing overlap.\nThe stack pool is important for future labs as it’s what enables multiple threads. By calling this allocator for each thread, the OS can hand out independent stacks to up to “_Min_Stack_Size” / ”THREAD_STACK_SIZE” threads. In future labs the kernel can use these “per thread” stack pointers to switch context safely between multiple threads without the risk one threads’ stack overwriting anothers.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,4,'2025-06-27_22-16-47','At the time of compilation a contiguous region of RAM is reserved through “_Min_Stack_Size”, this area is the stack pool. It’s positioned immediately below the initial MSP location and is set aside to be used exclusively for all kernel and thread stacks. Rather than using one large stack the kernel divides the pool into equal sized chunks with each of these chunks being the size of one threads’ stack. The bump pointer is used to track the next free address in the stack pool (the bump pointer is a global pointer and is stored in the kernel).\nThe allocation mechanism works by first initialising on “osKernelInitialize()”, by doing this the bump pointer is set to the top of the pool. Then it checks per thread allocation, with each call to “osCreateThread()” subtracts “THREAD_STACK_SIZE” from that bump pointer thus giving us a new top of stack address. Then there’s the alignment check which basically works by making both the pool base and “THREAD_STACK_SIZE” a multiple of 8 so that every stack is 8-byte aligned. Lastly there’s exhaustion handling. If subtracting one more “THREAD_STACK_SIZE” would move it below the bottom of the pool, the allocator will return NULL and “osCreateThread()” fails preventing overlap.\nThe stack pool is important for future labs as it’s what enables multiple threads. By calling this allocator for each thread, the OS can hand out independent stacks to up to “_Min_Stack_Size” / ”THREAD_STACK_SIZE” threads. In future labs the kernel can use these “per thread” stack pointers to switch context safely between multiple threads without the risk one threads’ stack overwriting anothers.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,5,'2025-06-27_15-07-49','The user would be main.c which is the file containing application level logic, the main functions and calls to the kernels API.\nThe kernel (kernel.c or kernel.h files) contain the internal mechanism of the OS such as global variables to manage stacks, the SVC handler and various routines such as the ones to setup and start a thread.\nThis distinction is important because it hides complex systems from the users such as thread management which in turn makes the system easier to understand and maintain. This distinctions keeps developers from dealing with low level code so they can focus on dealing with documented kernel functions.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,5,'2025-06-27_19-41-06','The user would be main.c which is the file containing application level logic, the main functions and calls to the kernels API.\nThe kernel (kernel.c or kernel.h files) contain the internal mechanism of the OS such as global variables to manage stacks, the SVC handler and various routines such as the ones to setup and start a thread.\nThis distinction is important because it hides complex systems from the users such as thread management which in turn makes the system easier to understand and maintain. This distinctions keeps developers from dealing with low level code so they can focus on dealing with documented kernel functions.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,5,'2025-06-27_21-06-57','The user would be main.c which is the file containing application level logic, the main functions and calls to the kernels API.\nThe kernel (kernel.c or kernel.h files) contain the internal mechanism of the OS such as global variables to manage stacks, the SVC handler and various routines such as the ones to setup and start a thread.\nThis distinction is important because it hides complex systems from the users such as thread management which in turn makes the system easier to understand and maintain. This distinctions keeps developers from dealing with low level code so they can focus on dealing with documented kernel functions.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',3,5,'2025-06-27_22-16-47','The user would be main.c which is the file containing application level logic, the main functions and calls to the kernels API.\nThe kernel (kernel.c or kernel.h files) contain the internal mechanism of the OS such as global variables to manage stacks, the SVC handler and various routines such as the ones to setup and start a thread.\nThis distinction is important because it hides complex systems from the users such as thread management which in turn makes the system easier to understand and maintain. This distinctions keeps developers from dealing with low level code so they can focus on dealing with documented kernel functions.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,1,'2025-07-26_19-10-41','Using a multilevel queue will allow you to break the ready queue into several smaller queues however a process can be in only one of the queues, it’s assigned based on some attribute of the process.\nUsing a queue is interesting because it allows for cleaner abstraction which matches the logic of round robin more closely. It’s also easier to manage as you add or remove threads making it easier to maintain.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,1,'2025-07-27_15-38-02','Using an array needs us to manually keep track of the thread index, wrapping around when reaching the end and handling thread additions/removals more carefully. The array data structure is less flexible compared to queues, if threads are removed then other elements need to be used which makes it harder to keep the scheduling in order.\nWhereas when using a queue to store TCBs in round robin scheduling aligns automatically because of how the scheduling mechanism works. Round robin works by giving each task/process a time slice, after the time slice is completed the process can be moved to the back of the queue and the next thread dequeued from the front and scheduled. This logic is more intuitive and cleaner than using an array.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,1,'2025-07-27_16-03-05','Using an array needs us to manually keep track of the thread index, wrapping around when reaching the end and handling thread additions/removals more carefully. The array data structure is less flexible compared to queues, if threads are removed then other elements need to be used which makes it harder to keep the scheduling in order.\nWhereas when using a queue to store TCBs in round robin scheduling aligns automatically because of how the scheduling mechanism works. Round robin works by giving each task/process a time slice, after the time slice is completed the process can be moved to the back of the queue and the next thread dequeued from the front and scheduled. This logic is more intuitive and cleaner than using an array another reason a queue is more ideal is because a queue provides a higher level of abstraction.\nHowever there are a few trade offs to get this abstraction. Depending on the way the queue is implemented (implements using a linked list or circular buffer), enqueuing and dequeuing can involve additional memory overheads or pointer manipulation which will impact performance. On the other hand arrays provide constant time access by index making it more efficition. Additionally dynamic memory allocation in the queue can cause fragmentation which isn’t ideal for real time systems.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,1,'2025-07-28_11-32-37','Using an array needs us to manually keep track of the thread index, wrapping around when reaching the end and handling thread additions/removals more carefully. The array data structure is less flexible compared to queues, if threads are removed then other elements need to be used which makes it harder to keep the scheduling in order.\nWhereas when using a queue to store TCBs in round robin scheduling aligns automatically because of how the scheduling mechanism works. Round robin works by giving each task/process a time slice, after the time slice is completed the process can be moved to the back of the queue and the next thread dequeued from the front and scheduled. This logic is more intuitive and cleaner than using an array another reason a queue is more ideal is because a queue provides a higher level of abstraction.\nHowever there are a few trade offs to get this abstraction. Depending on the way the queue is implemented (implements using a linked list or circular buffer), enqueuing and dequeuing can involve additional memory overheads or pointer manipulation which will impact performance. On the other hand arrays provide constant time access by index making it more efficition. Additionally dynamic memory allocation in the queue can cause fragmentation which isn’t ideal for real time systems.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,1,'2025-07-30_21-14-24','Using an array needs us to manually keep track of the thread index, wrapping around when reaching the end and handling thread additions/removals more carefully. The array data structure is less flexible compared to queues, if threads are removed then other elements need to be used which makes it harder to keep the scheduling in order.\nWhereas when using a queue to store TCBs in round robin scheduling aligns automatically because of how the scheduling mechanism works. Round robin works by giving each task/process a time slice, after the time slice is completed the process can be moved to the back of the queue and the next thread dequeued from the front and scheduled. This logic is more intuitive and cleaner than using an array.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,1,'2025-07-30_21-36-41','Using an array needs us to manually keep track of the thread index, wrapping around when reaching the end and handling thread additions/removals more carefully. The array data structure is less flexible compared to queues, if threads are removed then other elements need to be used which makes it harder to keep the scheduling in order.\nWhereas when using a queue to store TCBs in round robin scheduling aligns automatically because of how the scheduling mechanism works. Round robin works by giving each task/process a time slice, after the time slice is completed the process can be moved to the back of the queue and the next thread dequeued from the front and scheduled. This logic is more intuitive and cleaner than using an array.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,1,'2025-07-30_21-47-17','It’s possible to implement round-robin  scheduling with a queue of TCBs using the following approach. The TCB at the front of the queue is always going to be the next thread to run, when the time slice of the current TCB which was being run is stopped and dequeued from the head and is enqueued to the back of the queue. This approach will maintain fairness and it’s the expected behaviour of round robin because the next task is always at the front of the queue.\nOn the other hand, with an array it’s essential that you keep a separate current index and “wrap” it at the end and on the removal of a thread you need to shift the other elements of the array. This is very different, because of it’s enqueue and dequeue  operations which each have a time complexity of O(1) and can handle the addition or removals without any index arithmetic.\nIt is possible to modify the array to make it work similarly to a fixed size queue by using a circular array but queue abstraction just makes it more intentional. There will be a few performance trade offs, if a linked list based queue is used, it has a point over head for each TCB and every enqueue and dequeue touches two pointers. If a circular buffer queue in an array doesn’t need any extra memory and it’s pretty much just as fast as index arithmetic would be.\nThere are a few benefits of implementing a queue in real life scenarios such as how it helps make it easier to maintain the code and improve clarity because the process of dequeuing then running and then enqueuing matches exactly to the description of methods which use time slices. Using queues will allow you to add or remove threads will running without the need of shifting an array just to enqueue new TCBs or drop the old. Lastly it’s possible for us to swap in priority queues later on with the need of rewriting the scheduler loop, all that needs to be changed are the data structures and not the algorithm itself.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,2,'2025-07-26_19-10-41','It works by assuming that the user has setup the priorities correctly and will only run when no other interrupts need to be run.\nIt performs context switching by saving the values for register R4 to R11 of the current thread to its stack, after which it calls osSched(). osSched() saves the current thread’s process stack pointer and then selects the next thread and loads the saved process stack pointer. After this it restores the values of R4 to R11 from the new thread’s stack. It then lets the interrupt return\nThis process completes the context switch by jumping directly into the next thread’s content.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,2,'2025-07-27_15-38-02','The pendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current CPU state of the thread and restoring the state of the next thread using the process stack pointer. The process stack pointer points to the location of the thread stack, the process stack pointer is also used to keep track of that threads context.\nPendSV_Handler begins a context switch by saving the register values in R4 to R11 for the current thread to its stack. The handler has push them using the process stack pointer (this is because the hardware doesn’t automatically save the register values). After saving the registers, the osSched() function is called. Within this function the OS saves the current threads process stack pointer value in the TCB. After which round robin is used to select the next thread, it then loads the process stack pointer value from the next threads TCB which points to its respective saved stack.\nAfter running the osSched() function, the PendSV_handler restores the values of R4 to R11 by popping them from the new threads stack using the updated process stack pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,2,'2025-07-27_16-03-05','The pendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current CPU state of the thread and restoring the state of the next thread using the process stack pointer. The process stack pointer points to the location of the thread stack, the process stack pointer is also used to keep track of that threads context.\nPendSV_Handler begins a context switch by saving the register values in R4 to R11 for the current thread to its stack. The handler has push them using the process stack pointer (this is because the hardware doesn’t automatically save the register values). After saving the registers, the osSched() function is called. Within this function the OS saves the current threads process stack pointer value in the TCB. After which round robin is used to select the next thread, it then loads the process stack pointer value from the next threads TCB which points to its respective saved stack.\nAfter running the osSched() function, the PendSV_handler restores the values of R4 to R11 by popping them from the new threads stack using the updated process stack pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,2,'2025-07-28_11-32-37','The pendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current CPU state of the thread and restoring the state of the next thread using the process stack pointer. The process stack pointer points to the location of the thread stack, the process stack pointer is also used to keep track of that threads context.\nPendSV_Handler begins a context switch by saving the register values in R4 to R11 for the current thread to its stack. The handler has push them using the process stack pointer (this is because the hardware doesn’t automatically save the register values). After saving the registers, the osSched() function is called. Within this function the OS saves the current threads process stack pointer value in the TCB. After which round robin is used to select the next thread, it then loads the process stack pointer value from the next threads TCB which points to its respective saved stack.\nAfter running the osSched() function, the PendSV_handler restores the values of R4 to R11 by popping them from the new threads stack using the updated process stack pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,2,'2025-07-30_21-14-24','The pendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current CPU state of the thread and restoring the state of the next thread using the process stack pointer. The process stack pointer points to the location of the thread stack, the process stack pointer is also used to keep track of that threads context.\nPendSV_Handler begins a context switch by saving the register values in R4 to R11 for the current thread to its stack. The handler has push them using the process stack pointer (this is because the hardware doesn’t automatically save the register values). After saving the registers, the osSched() function is called. Within this function the OS saves the current threads process stack pointer value in the TCB. After which round robin is used to select the next thread, it then loads the process stack pointer value from the next threads TCB which points to its respective saved stack.\nAfter running the osSched() function, the PendSV_handler restores the values of R4 to R11 by popping them from the new threads stack using the updated process stack pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,2,'2025-07-30_21-36-41','The pendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current CPU state of the thread and restoring the state of the next thread using the process stack pointer. The process stack pointer points to the location of the thread stack, the process stack pointer is also used to keep track of that threads context.\nPendSV_Handler begins a context switch by saving the register values in R4 to R11 for the current thread to its stack. The handler has push them using the process stack pointer (this is because the hardware doesn’t automatically save the register values). After saving the registers, the osSched() function is called. Within this function the OS saves the current threads process stack pointer value in the TCB. After which round robin is used to select the next thread, it then loads the process stack pointer value from the next threads TCB which points to its respective saved stack.\nAfter running the osSched() function, the PendSV_handler restores the values of R4 to R11 by popping them from the new threads stack using the updated process stack pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,2,'2025-07-30_21-47-17','The pendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by saving the current CPU state of the thread and restoring the state of the next thread using the process stack pointer. The process stack pointer points to the location of the thread stack, the process stack pointer is also used to keep track of that threads context.\nPendSV_Handler begins a context switch by saving the register values in R4 to R11 for the current thread to its stack. The handler has push them using the process stack pointer (this is because the hardware doesn’t automatically save the register values). After saving the registers, the osSched() function is called. Within this function the OS saves the current threads process stack pointer value in the TCB. After which round robin is used to select the next thread, it then loads the process stack pointer value from the next threads TCB which points to its respective saved stack.\nAfter running the osSched() function, the PendSV_handler restores the values of R4 to R11 by popping them from the new threads stack using the updated process stack pointer.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,3,'2025-07-26_19-10-41','It’s a bad idea because there will always be a task with a higher priority than the low priority task which can cause starvation because the low priority task my never be executed. This can make the OS unresponsive to background tasks and more vulnerable to bugs in high priority code.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,3,'2025-07-27_15-38-02','Making the scheduler select the task with the highest priority would lead to the same highest priority thread to run non-stop as long as it’s ready to be executed. The problem with this is that it prevents any lower priority threads from being scheduled even if they’re ready to run. This results with the system becoming unfair and lower priority block will be completely blocked from running which can cause problems. For example background tasks or maintenance tasks may never get CPU time which in turn can cause system instability, there’s also a chance that the high priority thread contains a bug which can cause the entire system to lock up.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,3,'2025-07-27_16-03-05','Making the scheduler select the task with the highest priority would lead 