',4,3,'2025-07-30_21-36-41','Making the scheduler select the task with the highest priority would lead to the same highest priority thread to run non-stop as long as it’s ready to be executed. The problem with this is that it prevents any lower priority threads from being scheduled even if they’re ready to run. This results with the system becoming unfair and lower priority block will be completely blocked from running which can cause problems. For example background tasks or maintenance tasks may never get CPU time which in turn can cause system instability, there’s also a chance that the high priority thread contains a bug which can cause the entire system to lock up.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,3,'2025-07-30_21-47-17','Making the scheduler select the task with the highest priority would lead to the same highest priority thread to run non-stop as long as it’s ready to be executed. The problem with this is that it prevents any lower priority threads from being scheduled even if they’re ready to run. This results with the system becoming unfair and lower priority block will be completely blocked from running which can cause problems. For example background tasks or maintenance tasks may never get CPU time which in turn can cause system instability, there’s also a chance that the high priority thread contains a bug which can cause the entire system to lock up.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,4,'2025-07-26_19-10-41','Some of the benefits of using C includes the fact that it is more readable and easier to maintain as a consequence of being easier to debug. The same C code can be used across multiple microcontrollers which can also help with improving the speed of development, another thing which makes it faster to develop using C is that writing control logic is much faster and safer in C.\nAlthough these are all good, there are still a few issues such as overheads which add extra instructions and stack usage. You have less control compared to assembly and there are also a number of compiler limitations.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,4,'2025-07-27_15-38-02','Some of the benefits of using C includes the fact that it is more readable and easier to maintain as a consequence of being easier to debug. The same C code can be used across multiple microcontrollers which can also help with improving the speed of development, another thing which makes it faster to develop using C is that writing control logic is much faster and safer in C.\nAlthough these are all good, there are still a few issues such as overheads which add extra instructions and stack usage. You have less control compared to assembly and there are also a number of compiler limitations.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,4,'2025-07-27_16-03-05','Some of the benefits of using C includes the fact that it is more readable and easier to maintain as a consequence of being easier to debug. The same C code can be used across multiple microcontrollers which can also help with improving the speed of development, another thing which makes it faster to develop using C is that writing control logic is much faster and safer in C.\nAlthough these are all good, there are still a few issues such as overheads which add extra instructions and stack usage. You have less control compared to assembly and there are also a number of compiler limitations.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,4,'2025-07-28_11-32-37','Some of the benefits of using C includes the fact that it is more readable and easier to maintain as a consequence of being easier to debug. The same C code can be used across multiple microcontrollers which can also help with improving the speed of development, another thing which makes it faster to develop using C is that writing control logic is much faster and safer in C.\nAlthough these are all good, there are still a few issues such as overheads which add extra instructions and stack usage. You have less control compared to assembly and there are also a number of compiler limitations.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,4,'2025-07-30_21-14-24','Some of the benefits of using C includes the fact that it is more readable and easier to maintain as a consequence of being easier to debug. The same C code can be used across multiple microcontrollers which can also help with improving the speed of development, another thing which makes it faster to develop using C is that writing control logic is much faster and safer in C.\nAlthough these are all good, there are still a few issues such as overheads which add extra instructions and stack usage. You have less control compared to assembly and there are also a number of compiler limitations.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,4,'2025-07-30_21-36-41','Some of the benefits of using C includes the fact that it is more readable and easier to maintain as a consequence of being easier to debug. The same C code can be used across multiple microcontrollers which can also help with improving the speed of development, another thing which makes it faster to develop using C is that writing control logic is much faster and safer in C.\nAlthough these are all good, there are still a few issues such as overheads which add extra instructions and stack usage. You have less control compared to assembly and there are also a number of compiler limitations.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,4,'2025-07-30_21-47-17','Some of the benefits of using C includes the fact that it is more readable and easier to maintain as a consequence of being easier to debug. The same C code can be used across multiple microcontrollers which can also help with improving the speed of development, another thing which makes it faster to develop using C is that writing control logic is much faster and safer in C.\nAlthough these are all good, there are still a few issues such as overheads which add extra instructions and stack usage. You have less control compared to assembly and there are also a number of compiler limitations.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,5,'2025-07-26_19-10-41','The OS will still technically work however after initializing the thread, running it and the thread will yield multiple times, osSched() will run on each yield however there won’t be any other threads to switch to. So even if it calls osYield() the scheduler will switch back to the same thread\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,5,'2025-07-27_15-38-02','The OS will still technically work however after initializing the thread, running it and the thread will yield multiple times, osSched() will run on each yield however there won’t be any other threads to switch to. So even if it calls osYield() the scheduler will switch back to the same thread\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,5,'2025-07-27_16-03-05','The OS will still technically still work if only one thread is made like the print_continuosly thread. After intitializing and starting the thread, it’ll run as expected but after this is when problems start. When the thread calls the osYield() function a context switch is triggered which means osSched() is called. The scheduler examines the list of runnable threads but because there’s only one thread the scheduler will select the same thread again.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,5,'2025-07-28_11-32-37','The OS will still technically still work if only one thread is made like the print_continuosly thread is made. After initializing and running the thread, it begins the execution just as expected. When osYield() is called the thread voluntarily gives up the CPU which triggers a context switch in turn calling osSched() which goes through the list of threads. However since there’s only one thread in the system which is why the scheduler doesn’t have choice but to choose the same thread again. After PendSV_Handler completes the context switch the thread (same one as before) will go back to executing from where it left off continuing after the osYield() call.\nThis will repeat constantly and while it introduces unnecessary context switching over head the OS will still operate as expected. The important thing to note is that the thread gets selected again by the scheduler but it also continues execution without interruption which allows the system to function.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,5,'2025-07-30_21-14-24','The OS will still technically work however after initializing the thread, running it and the thread will yield multiple times, osSched() will run on each yield however there won’t be any other threads to switch to. So even if it calls osYield() the scheduler will switch back to the same thread\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,5,'2025-07-30_21-36-41','The OS will still technically work however after initializing the thread, running it and the thread will yield multiple times, osSched() will run on each yield however there won’t be any other threads to switch to. So even if it calls osYield() the scheduler will switch back to the same thread\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',4,5,'2025-07-30_21-47-17','The OS will still technically work however after initializing the thread, running it and the thread will yield multiple times, osSched() will run on each yield however there won’t be any other threads to switch to. So even if it calls osYield() the scheduler will switch back to the same thread\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,1,'2025-07-27_15-16-00','',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,1,'2025-07-27_15-44-22','In order to write the thread the user must write it in the following form:\nvoid myThread(void* args) {\nmyStruct* data = (myStruct*)args;\nand the user creates the thread with something like:\nmyStruct myData = {1, 2};\nosCreateThread(myThread, &myData);\nThe OS has to do the following, during the thread creation it sets up the initial stack. The R0 register is used to pass the first argument so the oS places the function pointer in the program counter field.\nThis works because it schedules the thread, the CPU then pops the initial stack frame, this will load R0 with its argument and the thread function starts with the right pointer. Since the function will expect a void pointer the user can cast it into any form.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,1,'2025-07-28_11-53-10','To pass an argument into a thread the user needs to define the thread function a standard format which takes a single void* parameter like the example below:\nvoid myThread(void* args) {\nmyStruct* data = (myStruct*)args;\nIn order to create the user needs to provide both the thread function and a pointer to the data they want to pass something like the example below:\nmyStruct myData = {1, 2};\nosCreateThread(myThread, &myData);\nThe OS has to do the following, during the thread creation it sets up the threads initial stack frame. This stack frame included the values of important registers ins the CPU like the program counter and registers R0 to R3. The OS places the thread function’s address in the PC of the stack frame so that the CPU knows where to go to. Then places the argument from the user into R0.\nWhen the thread is scheduled to run, the CPU will load the initial stack fram which sets the PC to the thread functions address and R0 will be set to the users argument pointer. This follows the application binary interface, the purpose of which is to ensure the thread functions starts executing by receiving the correct argument in R0.\nThis works because both the user and OS follow similar conventions with the user writing the thread to accept a void argument and the OS works by arranging the stack to satisfy the ARM convention. This allows easy argument passing at runtime.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,1,'2025-07-30_23-27-04','To pass an argument into a thread the user needs to define the thread function a standard format which takes a single void* parameter like the example below:\nvoid myThread(void* args) {\nmyStruct* data = (myStruct*)args;\nIn order to create the user needs to provide both the thread function and a pointer to the data they want to pass something like the example below:\nmyStruct myData = {1, 2};\nosCreateThread(myThread, &myData);\nThe OS has to do the following, during the thread creation it sets up the threads initial stack frame. This stack frame included the values of important registers ins the CPU like the program counter and registers R0 to R3. The OS places the thread function’s address in the PC of the stack frame so that the CPU knows where to go to. Then places the argument from the user into R0.\nWhen the thread is scheduled to run, the CPU will load the initial stack fram which sets the PC to the thread functions address and R0 will be set to the users argument pointer. This follows the application binary interface, the purpose of which is to ensure the thread functions starts executing by receiving the correct argument in R0.\nThis works because both the user and OS follow similar conventions with the user writing the thread to accept a void argument and the OS works by arranging the stack to satisfy the ARM convention. This allows easy argument passing at runtime.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,1,'2025-07-31_22-28-31','To pass an argument into a thread the user needs to define the thread function a standard format which takes a single void* parameter like the example below:\nvoid myThread(void* args) {\nmyStruct* data = (myStruct*)args;\nIn order to create the user needs to provide both the thread function and a pointer to the data they want to pass something like the example below:\nmyStruct myData = {1, 2};\nosCreateThread(myThread, &myData);\nThe OS has to do the following, during the thread creation it sets up the threads initial stack frame. This stack frame included the values of important registers ins the CPU like the program counter and registers R0 to R3. The OS places the thread function’s address in the PC of the stack frame so that the CPU knows where to go to. Then places the argument from the user into R0.\nWhen the thread is scheduled to run, the CPU will load the initial stack fram which sets the PC to the thread functions address and R0 will be set to the users argument pointer. This follows the application binary interface, the purpose of which is to ensure the thread functions starts executing by receiving the correct argument in R0.\nThis works because both the user and OS follow similar conventions with the user writing the thread to accept a void argument and the OS works by arranging the stack to satisfy the ARM convention. This allows easy argument passing at runtime.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,2,'2025-07-27_15-16-00','',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,2,'2025-07-27_15-44-22','This is because the mentioned functions trigger/perform  a context switch calling osYield inside them will cause a nested context switch and this is undefined behaviour. If this is done then a few issues can rise such as stack over flow or corruption, unexpected crashing and PendSV being triggered before it even finished.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,2,'2025-07-28_11-53-10','This is because doing so would break the interrupt handling rules of the Cortex-M4 which can cause undefined behaviour.\nCalling osYield will trigger a software interrupt by executing SVC #YIELD which in turn will cause the process to enter SVC_Handler (which is an interrupt handler). If you do the same thing (calling osYield()) from within another interrupt context like PendSV_Handler it will try to trigger an interrupt while another interrupt is already being handled. Interrupt nesting is allowed on the Cortex-M4 but that’s only if the new interrupt has higher priority than the current interrupt being serviced. The issue is that PendSV and SVC are both low priority which means the new interrupt can end up being ignored or improperly handled depending on timing.\nThis can cause issues such as nested context switches which the OS isn’t designed to handle. Stack corruption since each interrupt will push its data to the stack and improper nesting can mean that clean up will be skipped. There will be crashes and other unpredictable behaviour as the interrupt return can restore and inconsistent processor state.\nThese are among many reasons as to why osYield() must be called from thread level code where it can cause controlled context switch. Calling it from within an interrupt handler will break the flow and compromise the systems stability.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,2,'2025-07-30_23-27-04','This is because doing so would break the interrupt handling rules of the Cortex-M4 which can cause undefined behaviour.\nCalling osYield will trigger a software interrupt by executing SVC #YIELD which in turn will cause the process to enter SVC_Handler (which is an interrupt handler). If you do the same thing (calling osYield()) from within another interrupt context like PendSV_Handler it will try to trigger an interrupt while another interrupt is already being handled. Interrupt nesting is allowed on the Cortex-M4 but that’s only if the new interrupt has higher priority than the current interrupt being serviced. The issue is that PendSV and SVC are both low priority which means the new interrupt can end up being ignored or improperly handled depending on timing.\nThis can cause issues such as nested context switches which the OS isn’t designed to handle. Stack corruption since each interrupt will push its data to the stack and improper nesting can mean that clean up will be skipped. There will be crashes and other unpredictable behaviour as the interrupt return can restore and inconsistent processor state.\nThese are among many reasons as to why osYield() must be called from thread level code where it can cause controlled context switch. Calling it from within an interrupt handler will break the flow and compromise the systems stability.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,2,'2025-07-31_22-28-31','This is because doing so would break the interrupt handling rules of the Cortex-M4 which can cause undefined behaviour.\nCalling osYield will trigger a software interrupt by executing SVC #YIELD which in turn will cause the process to enter SVC_Handler (which is an interrupt handler). If you do the same thing (calling osYield()) from within another interrupt context like PendSV_Handler it will try to trigger an interrupt while another interrupt is already being handled. Interrupt nesting is allowed on the Cortex-M4 but that’s only if the new interrupt has higher priority than the current interrupt being serviced. The issue is that PendSV and SVC are both low priority which means the new interrupt can end up being ignored or improperly handled depending on timing.\nThis can cause issues such as nested context switches which the OS isn’t designed to handle. Stack corruption since each interrupt will push its data to the stack and improper nesting can mean that clean up will be skipped. There will be crashes and other unpredictable behaviour as the interrupt return can restore and inconsistent processor state.\nThese are among many reasons as to why osYield() must be called from thread level code where it can cause controlled context switch. Calling it from within an interrupt handler will break the flow and compromise the systems stability.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,3,'2025-07-27_15-16-00','',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,3,'2025-07-27_15-44-22','Some key benefits would be that we would have higher resolution timing because the OS can respond with 0.1ms precision which in turn makes it more responsive, this allows pre-emptive scheduling can happen more quicker allowing faster task switching.\nSome of the benefits include the increased CPU overhead because SysTick_Handler runs 10 times more often which would increase the interrupt handling load, the more frequent interrupts will drain more energy and this isn’t ideal for low power systems. There will be less time for actual work because the threads will spend less time executing and more time being interrupted.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,3,'2025-07-28_11-53-10','Some key benefits would be that we would have a higher timing resolution, a 0.1 ms clock will allow the OS to respond with 10 times more precision as compared to when using a 1 ms clock which is good for time sensitive application like control systems. Another benefit is that the 0.1 ms clock will be more responsive because of its higher tick rate, pre-emptive scheduling can occur more often. The 0.1 ms clock will also allowe for finer control over timeouts and delays.\nHowever there are  a few issues with using a 0.1 ms OS clock, one of which is the increased CPU overhead, the SysTick_Handler interrupt will run 10 times more and this will increase how often context switches happen and the time spent servicing interrupts. Another issue is that the CPU will not be as available to execute threads because of more frequent interrupts the threads have less uninterrupted CPU time. The frequent interrupts and context switching will increase CPU activity and this will drain more power which is far from ideal for low power systems.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,3,'2025-07-30_23-27-04','Some key benefits would be that we would have a higher timing resolution, a 0.1 ms clock will allow the OS to respond with 10 times more precision as compared to when using a 1 ms clock which is good for time sensitive application like control systems. Another benefit is that the 0.1 ms clock will be more responsive because of its higher tick rate, pre-emptive scheduling can occur more often. The 0.1 ms clock will also allowe for finer control over timeouts and delays.\nHowever there are  a few issues with using a 0.1 ms OS clock, one of which is the increased CPU overhead, the SysTick_Handler interrupt will run 10 times more and this will increase how often context switches happen and the time spent servicing interrupts. Another issue is that the CPU will not be as available to execute threads because of more frequent interrupts the threads have less uninterrupted CPU time. The frequent interrupts and context switching will increase CPU activity and this will drain more power which is far from ideal for low power systems.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,3,'2025-07-31_22-28-31','Some key benefits would be that we would have a higher timing resolution, a 0.1 ms clock will allow the OS to respond with 10 times more precision as compared to when using a 1 ms clock which is good for time sensitive application like control systems. Another benefit is that the 0.1 ms clock will be more responsive because of its higher tick rate, pre-emptive scheduling can occur more often. The 0.1 ms clock will also allowe for finer control over timeouts and delays.\nHowever there are  a few issues with using a 0.1 ms OS clock, one of which is the increased CPU overhead, the SysTick_Handler interrupt will run 10 times more and this will increase how often context switches happen and the time spent servicing interrupts. Another issue is that the CPU will not be as available to execute threads because of more frequent interrupts the threads have less uninterrupted CPU time. The frequent interrupts and context switching will increase CPU activity and this will drain more power which is far from ideal for low power systems.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,4,'2025-07-27_15-16-00','',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,4,'2025-07-27_15-44-22','In co-operative multitasking the threads are responsible for giving up control of the CPU by call the osYield() function. From the users perspective this means that a thread which behaves as expected must have a yield call within its execution loop otherwise the thread will run indefinitely. The only responsibility of the OS will only perform a context switch when a thread voluntarily yields so the scheduler doesn’t have much control.\nPre-emptive multitasking on the other hand doesn’t need the threads to yield manually instead the OS can use a hardware timer to track how long a thread has been running. When it runs for the full time slice the OS will interrupt the execution and this triggers context switching by using PendSV which give the OS more control over the scheduling and this allows for each thread to get an equal amount of CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,4,'2025-07-28_11-53-10','In co-operative multitasking the threads are responsible for giving up control of the CPU by call the osYield() function. From the users perspective this means that a thread which behaves as expected must have a yield call within its execution loop otherwise the thread will run indefinitely. The only responsibility of the OS will only perform a context switch when a thread voluntarily yields so the scheduler doesn’t have much control.\nPre-emptive multitasking on the other hand doesn’t need the threads to yield manually instead the OS can use a hardware timer to track how long a thread has been running. When it runs for the full time slice the OS will interrupt the execution and this triggers context switching by using PendSV which give the OS more control over the scheduling and this allows for each thread to get an equal amount of CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,4,'2025-07-30_23-27-04','In co-operative multitasking the threads are responsible for giving up control of the CPU by call the osYield() function. From the users perspective this means that a thread which behaves as expected must have a yield call within its execution loop otherwise the thread will run indefinitely. The only responsibility of the OS will only perform a context switch when a thread voluntarily yields so the scheduler doesn’t have much control.\nPre-emptive multitasking on the other hand doesn’t need the threads to yield manually instead the OS can use a hardware timer to track how long a thread has been running. When it runs for the full time slice the OS will interrupt the execution and this triggers context switching by using PendSV which give the OS more control over the scheduling and this allows for each thread to get an equal amount of CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,4,'2025-07-31_22-28-31','In co-operative multitasking the threads are responsible for giving up control of the CPU by call the osYield() function. From the users perspective this means that a thread which behaves as expected must have a yield call within its execution loop otherwise the thread will run indefinitely. The only responsibility of the OS will only perform a context switch when a thread voluntarily yields so the scheduler doesn’t have much control.\nPre-emptive multitasking on the other hand doesn’t need the threads to yield manually instead the OS can use a hardware timer to track how long a thread has been running. When it runs for the full time slice the OS will interrupt the execution and this triggers context switching by using PendSV which give the OS more control over the scheduling and this allows for each thread to get an equal amount of CPU time.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,5,'2025-07-27_15-16-00','',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,5,'2025-07-27_15-44-22','It’s possible for both mechanisms to work together by using two separate triggers for context switching. There’s yielding which is when the thread calls osYield and this causes the runtime to reset to tis timeslice.\nThere’s pre-emptive switching which is automatic, it runs a thread for it total runtime or until it has fully executed (whichever comes first) and then it triggers PendSV for the context switching.\nThis all works because both the approaches use PendSV for context switching so it doesn’t matter whether the switch was triggered manually or by the timer because the logic in osSched() will still select the next thread properly.\n',0,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,5,'2025-07-28_11-53-10','It’s possible for both mechanisms to work together because both the mechanisms rely on the same context switch handler (PendSV_Handler), to execute switches. Even though both the triggers are different their switching logic is the same.\nWhen a thread calls osYield() it gives up its CPU time. This causes a software interrupt SVC which then leads to the PendSV interrupt being scheduled, once triggered it saves the current threads context and calls to select the next thread. It’s important to note that yielding doesn’t reset the threads runtime, instead the OS (in osSched() or SysTick_Handler) will decide whether and how the runtime will be reset and this is based on the scheduling logic.\nOn the other hand, pre-emptive switching is triggered automatically because of the SysTick timer. Every 1 ms, SysTick_Handler will decrement the running threads runtime and eventually when it reaches 0 the OS triggers PendSV to do a context switch with osSched() being used to choose the next thread to run.\nThese two can work together because the both use PendSV to handle the context switch and they will call the same scheduling logic of osSched() for consistent and fair thread selections.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,5,'2025-07-30_23-27-04','It’s possible for both mechanisms to work together because both the mechanisms rely on the same context switch handler (PendSV_Handler), to execute switches. Even though both the triggers are different their switching logic is the same.\nWhen a thread calls osYield() it gives up its CPU time. This causes a software interrupt SVC which then leads to the PendSV interrupt being scheduled, once triggered it saves the current threads context and calls to select the next thread. It’s important to note that yielding doesn’t reset the threads runtime, instead the OS (in osSched() or SysTick_Handler) will decide whether and how the runtime will be reset and this is based on the scheduling logic.\nOn the other hand, pre-emptive switching is triggered automatically because of the SysTick timer. Every 1 ms, SysTick_Handler will decrement the running threads runtime and eventually when it reaches 0 the OS triggers PendSV to do a context switch with osSched() being used to choose the next thread to run.\nThese two can work together because the both use PendSV to handle the context switch and they will call the same scheduling logic of osSched() for consistent and fair thread selections.\n',1,'s25'),('sffarhan',5,5,'2025-07-31_22-28-31','It’s possible for both mechanisms to work together because both the mechanisms rely on the same context switch handler (PendSV_Handler), to execute switches. Even though both the triggers are different their switching logic is the same.\nWhen a thread calls osYield() it gives up its CPU time. This causes a software interrupt SVC which then leads to the PendSV interrupt being scheduled, once triggered it saves the current threads context and calls to select the next thread. It’s important to note that yielding doesn’t reset the threads runtime, instead the OS (in osSched() or SysTick_Handler) will decide whether and how the runtime will be reset and this is based on the scheduling logic.\nOn the other hand, pre-emptive switching is triggered automatically because of the SysTick timer. Every 1 ms, SysTick_Handler will decrement the running threads runtime and eventually when it reaches 0 the OS triggers PendSV to do a context switch with osSched() being used to choose the next thread to run.\nThese two can work together because the both use PendSV to handle the context switch and they will call the same scheduling logic of osSched() for consistent and fair thread selections.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,1,'2025-05-13_10-22-42','HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is setup code written by ST Microelectronics to simplify the code needed for working with a microcontroller by providing a constant interface for software to interact with hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,1,'2025-05-23_11-03-50','HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is library available on setup written by ST Microelectronics to simplify the code needed for working with a microcontroller by providing a constant interface for software to interact with hardware. This reduces the need to write low-level, device-specific code.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,1,'2025-05-23_11-12-48','HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is library available on setup written by ST Microelectronics to simplify the code needed for working with a microcontroller by providing a constant interface for software to interact with hardware. This reduces the need to write low-level, device-specific code.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,2,'2025-05-13_10-22-42','__io_putchar is used to redirect printf to a specific IO device of your choosing.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',1,2,'2025-05-23_11-03-50','__io_putchar is used to redirect printf to a specific output device, such as a UART, USB, or debug console. the standard output used by printf is not defined by default because these systems typically lack an operating system or standard I/O streams like a desktop computer. By implementing __io_putchar, developers can define how characters are sent to the appropriate hardware peripheral. For example, if you want printf to send data over UART, __io_putchar would include the necessary code to transmit each character via the UART interface.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,2,'2025-05-23_11-12-48','__io_putchar is used to redirect printf to a specific output device, such as a UART, USB, or debug console. the standard output used by printf is not defined by default because these systems typically lack an operating system or standard I/O streams like a desktop computer. By implementing __io_putchar, developers can define how characters are sent to the appropriate hardware peripheral. For example, if you want printf to send data over UART, __io_putchar would include the necessary code to transmit each character via the UART interface.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,3,'2025-05-13_10-22-42','With the debugger we can access variables and view effects of the code without fully running the entire code, this simplifies the process of debugging and removes the need to write extra printf statements to view important values.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',1,3,'2025-05-23_11-03-50','We used the debugger in this lab because it allows us to directly inspect memory, variable states, and most importantly, hardware register values—which are critical for understanding the behavior of peripherals on embedded systems like the Cortex M4. Registers control low-level hardware functions, and examining them helps verify whether configurations (e.g., for GPIO or timers) have been applied correctly. Using printf alone is not sufficient in this environment for several reasons. First, embedded systems often lack standard I/O by default, and redirecting output (e.g., via UART using __io_putchar) adds complexity and may not be configured at early stages of development. Second, printf introduces overhead and may interfere with timing-sensitive operations or resource-constrained systems. Lastly, printf only shows you the values you explicitly choose to print—whereas a debugger provides real-time access to all variables and hardware states without modifying your code.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,3,'2025-05-23_11-12-48','We used the debugger in this lab because it allows us to directly inspect memory, variable states, and most importantly, hardware register values—which are critical for understanding the behavior of peripherals on embedded systems like the Cortex M4. Registers control low-level hardware functions, and examining them helps verify whether configurations (e.g., for GPIO or timers) have been applied correctly. Using printf alone is not sufficient in this environment for several reasons. First, embedded systems often lack standard I/O by default, and redirecting output (e.g., via UART using __io_putchar) adds complexity and may not be configured at early stages of development. Second, printf introduces overhead and may interfere with timing-sensitive operations or resource-constrained systems. Lastly, printf only shows you the values you explicitly choose to print—whereas a debugger provides real-time access to all variables and hardware states without modifying your code.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,4,'2025-05-13_10-22-42','The PSP_val start address is the MSP start address subtracted by 0x400. We find this address by casting the MSP_init_val as an integer, subtracting 0x400 from it and assigning this new address value to the PSP_val. We need to know the initial location of the MSP so we can properly allocate space for the PSP that does not impact or override the MSP.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,4,'2025-05-23_11-03-50','The stack allocation method we used involves calculating a value for PSP_val (Process Stack Pointer) by offsetting from MSP_init_val (Main Stack Pointer initial value). We first obtain MSP_init_val, typically from the value stored at address 0x0 in the vector table. We then cast it to an integer, subtract 0x400 from it (which is 1024 bytes), and assign this result as the start of the PSP. This sets the PSP to a memory location lower than the MSP, effectively reserving a separate region of memory for the process stack. Knowing the initial location of the MSP is crucial because it ensures that we allocate a PSP region that does not overlap with the main stack. If they did overlap, stack data could become corrupted, leading to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',1,4,'2025-05-23_11-12-48','The stack allocation method we used involves calculating a value for PSP_val (Process Stack Pointer) by offsetting from MSP_init_val (Main Stack Pointer initial value). We first obtain MSP_init_val, typically from the value stored at address 0x0 in the vector table. We then cast it to an integer, subtract 0x400 from it (which is 1024 bytes), and assign this result as the start of the PSP. This sets the PSP to a memory location lower than the MSP, effectively reserving a separate region of memory for the process stack.\nKnowing the initial location of the MSP is crucial because it ensures that we allocate a PSP region that does not overlap with the main stack. If they did overlap, stack data could become corrupted, leading to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,5,'2025-05-13_10-22-42','If we know the size of each stack then we can follow the same process for setting up the PSP. By decrementing the initial location of each stack by the defined size, we can ensure that each stack has their own allocated location and memory and does not overlap.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',1,5,'2025-05-23_11-03-50','If we know the number of stacks and their sizes at compile-time, we can allocate them by starting from a valid base address in RAM—such as the initial MSP value—and subtracting fixed-size offsets for each new stack. Each stack start address would be calculated by decrementing the previous one by the defined stack size, ensuring that stacks do not overlap. To guarantee validity, each address must fall within the available RAM and be aligned according to the processor\'s requirements (typically 8-byte alignment on Cortex-M). This method provides distinct, non-overlapping stack regions suitable for multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',1,5,'2025-05-23_11-12-48','If we know the number of stacks and their sizes at compile-time, we can allocate them by starting from a valid base address in RAM—such as the initial MSP value—and subtracting fixed-size offsets for each new stack. Each stack start address would be calculated by decrementing the previous one by the defined stack size, ensuring that stacks do not overlap. To guarantee validity, each address must fall within the available RAM and be aligned according to the processor\'s requirements (typically 8-byte alignment on Cortex-M). This method provides distinct, non-overlapping stack regions suitable for multiple threads.\n.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',2,1,'2025-05-27_10-56-23','When I the program counter to hold the function pointer I am manually telling the PC that this is the next function to run. When I make the PC hold this print continuously pointer it begins to run that function.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',2,1,'2025-06-10_11-41-17','When I set the Program Counter (PC) to the address of the print_continuously function, I was directly telling the CPU to start executing that function immediately. Unlike a regular function call (which pushes a return address onto the stack and updates the PC automatically), manually assigning a value to the PC bypasses the call/return mechanism and control flow jumps instantly to the new function. This means the processor begins executing instructions from that address right away, with no return path unless manually handled. This direct manipulation of the PC is powerful, but it requires careful setup to avoid unexpected behavior like missing return addresses or incorrect stack state.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',2,1,'2025-06-22_16-39-20','When I set the Program Counter (PC) to the address of the print_continuously function, I was directly telling the CPU to start executing that function immediately. Unlike a regular function call (which pushes a return address onto the stack and updates the PC automatically), manually assigning a value to the PC bypasses the call/return mechanism and control flow jumps instantly to the new function. This means the processor begins executing instructions from that address right away, with no return path unless manually handled. This direct manipulation of the PC is powerful, but it requires careful setup to avoid unexpected behavior like missing return addresses or incorrect stack state.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',2,2,'2025-05-27_10-56-23','A system call is a request to the kernel to carried out a pre specified command.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',2,2,'2025-06-10_11-41-17','A system call is a special mechanism that allows a user program to request a service from the operating system’s kernel, such as reading a file or creating a thread. It is typically invoked using a software interrupt (like SVC in ARM), which safely switches execution from user mode to privileged kernel mode. This controlled transition lets the kernel perform operations that the user code isn’t allowed to do directly, maintaining system security and stability.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',2,2,'2025-06-22_16-39-20','A system call is a controlled entry point into the kernel that provides an interface between user space and kernel space. It allows a user program to request services from the operating system’s kernel, such as reading from a file, allocating memory, or creating a thread. System calls are typically invoked using a special instruction like SVC on ARM, which triggers a software interrupt and causes the processor to switch from user mode to privileged kernel mode. This transition ensures that critical operations are performed securely and that user programs cannot directly access sensitive system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',2,3,'2025-05-27_10-56-23','The threads stack was set up by replicating how the stored values in the register would look while running that specific thread within said stack. These registers are stored in a stack and then popped into the real registers when that specific thread are ready to run.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',2,3,'2025-06-10_11-41-17','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a section of memory to simulate the processor’s register state as if the thread had been paused mid-execution. This includes pushing the values for key registers like r0–r3, r12, lr, pc, and xPSR in the correct order onto the thread’s stack memory. This setup ensures that when the thread is resumed, these values can be popped into the CPU’s registers to begin or continue execution correctly. We store register values on the stack because the CPU expects to retrieve them from the stack during context switches, ensuring that each thread resumes with the exact state it needs.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',2,3,'2025-06-22_16-39-20','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a section of memory to simulate the processor’s register state as if the thread had been paused mid-execution. This includes pushing the values for key registers like r0–r3, r12, lr, pc, and xPSR in the correct order onto the thread’s stack memory. This setup ensures that when the thread is resumed, these values can be popped into the CPU’s registers to begin or continue execution correctly. We store register values on the stack because the CPU expects to retrieve them from the stack during context switches, ensuring that each thread resumes with the exact state it needs.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',2,4,'2025-05-27_10-56-23','If the PSP stack isn’t explicitly set then the CPU attempts to pop the registers from a wrong unspecified stack, chances are the MSP. This results in garbage values being popped and a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',2,4,'2025-06-10_11-41-17','The CPU uses the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) when executing thread-level code. If we don\'t explicitly set the PSP to point to the top of the thread’s allocated stack, the CPU defaults to using the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) or an invalid address, which can cause it to pop incorrect or garbage values into registers during context switching. This results in unpredictable behavior and usually triggers a hard fault. Setting the PSP correctly ensures that the CPU accesses the right stack for that thread, maintaining data integrity and allowing the thread to run safely\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',2,4,'2025-06-22_16-39-20','The CPU uses the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) when executing thread-level code. If we don\'t explicitly set the PSP to point to the top of the thread’s allocated stack, the CPU defaults to using the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) or an invalid address, which can cause it to pop incorrect or garbage values into registers during context switching. This results in unpredictable behavior and usually triggers a hard fault. Setting the PSP correctly ensures that the CPU accesses the right stack for that thread, maintaining data integrity and allowing the thread to run safely\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',2,5,'2025-05-27_10-56-23','As the function completes and returns the thread ends, but the CPU has no place to return to specified as the Link register has not been set with a proper and explicit value in the code. At this point the CPU is essentially lost and hard faults due to this.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',2,5,'2025-06-10_11-41-17','As the function completes and returns the thread ends, but the CPU has no place to return to specified as the Link register has not been set with a proper and explicit value in the code. At this point the CPU is essentially lost and hard faults due to this.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',2,5,'2025-06-22_16-39-20','If the thread function simply printed something and then returned, the thread would terminate. However, since the thread was not created using a standard function call (like bl or blx) from another function, the Link Register (LR) does not contain a valid return address. In a properly structured function call, the return address is stored in LR and also pushed onto the stack so it can be restored later.\nBut in this case, the thread was likely created by manually setting up its stack and program counter (PC), and no valid return address was ever pushed to the thread\'s stack. When the function returns, it tries to pop the return address from the stack or jump to the LR value, but since neither is valid, the CPU doesn’t know where to return to. This causes a hard fault as the processor attempts to execute an invalid or undefined memory address.\nThus, the missing return address on the stack and the invalid LR together prevent a safe return, and the CPU crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,1,'2025-06-10_11-45-58','Each .c file is first compiled separately into an object file (.o) by the compiler. These object files contain machine code, but they are not yet combined into a complete program. Once all the necessary .c files have been compiled, a linker takes over. The linker combines all the object files into a single executable, resolving references between functions and variables that are defined in one file but used in another. This process ensures that the final program has all its components properly connected and ready to run on the target hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',3,1,'2025-06-22_16-44-38','When multiple .c files are used in a program, each file first goes through preprocessing, where the preprocessor handles directives like #include and #define, expands macros, and inserts the contents of header files, preparing the source code for compilation. Next is the compilation step, where each preprocessed file is converted into an object file (.o) containing machine code, though references to functions or variables in other files remain unresolved. Finally, during linking, the linker combines all the object files into a single executable, resolving cross-file references and ensuring that all parts of the program are properly connected and ready to run.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,1,'2025-06-22_16-51-29','When multiple .c files are used in a program, each file first goes through preprocessing, where the preprocessor handles directives like #include and #define, expands macros, and inserts the contents of header files, preparing the source code for compilation. Next is the compilation step, where each preprocessed file is converted into an object file (.o) containing machine code, though references to functions or variables in other files remain unresolved. Finally, during linking, the linker combines all the object files into a single executable, resolving cross-file references and ensuring that all parts of the program are properly connected and ready to run.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,1,'2025-06-23_19-50-09','When multiple .c files are used in a program, each file first goes through preprocessing, where the preprocessor handles directives like #include and #define, expands macros, and inserts the contents of header files, preparing the source code for compilation. Next is the compilation step, where each preprocessed file is converted into an object file (.o) containing machine code, though references to functions or variables in other files remain unresolved. Finally, during linking, the linker combines all the object files into a single executable, resolving cross-file references and ensuring that all parts of the program are properly connected and ready to run.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,2,'2025-06-10_11-45-58','Using #include to bring in a .c file is a bad idea because it bypasses the compiler\'s normal compilation and linking process. Instead of compiling each .c file separately and then linking them, including a .c file copies all its contents into the current file before compilation. This can lead to problems like duplicate definitions, increased compilation times, and loss of modularity. It also breaks the separation of concerns—.c files should be compiled into object files on their own, and only .h header files should be included to share declarations (not definitions) between source files.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,2,'2025-06-22_16-44-38','Using #include to bring in a .c file is a bad idea because it bypasses the compiler\'s normal compilation and linking process. Instead of compiling each .c file separately and then linking them, including a .c file copies all its contents into the current file before compilation. This can lead to problems like duplicate definitions, increased compilation times, and loss of modularity. It also breaks the separation of concerns—.c files should be compiled into object files on their own, and only .h header files should be included to share declarations (not definitions) between source files.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,2,'2025-06-22_16-51-29','Using #include to bring in a .c file is a bad idea because it bypasses the compiler\'s normal compilation and linking process. Instead of compiling each .c file separately and then linking them, including a .c file copies all its contents into the current file before compilation. This can lead to problems like duplicate definitions, increased compilation times, and loss of modularity. It also breaks the separation of concerns—.c files should be compiled into object files on their own, and only .h header files should be included to share declarations (not definitions) between source files.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,2,'2025-06-23_19-50-09','Using #include to bring in a .c file is a bad idea because it bypasses the compiler\'s normal compilation and linking process. Instead of compiling each .c file separately and then linking them, including a .c file copies all its contents into the current file before compilation. This can lead to problems like duplicate definitions, increased compilation times, and loss of modularity. It also breaks the separation of concerns—.c files should be compiled into object files on their own, and only .h header files should be included to share declarations (not definitions) between source files.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,3,'2025-06-10_11-45-58','To set up and start a thread, the user typically does the following: Define the thread function: Write a function that contains the code the thread will run. Allocate stack memory: Reserve space in memory to serve as the thread’s private stack. Initialize the stack: Set up the stack with the initial values for the thread’s registers, including the function address (PC), and necessary status bits. Set up a thread control structure: Create or fill in a data structure that holds the thread’s state, such as its stack pointer and status (e.g., ready or running). Register the thread with the OS: Add the thread to the OS’s scheduler so it can be selected to run. Start the scheduler: When the OS starts running, it picks one of the threads and begins executing it using its stack and function.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',3,3,'2025-06-22_16-44-38','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, you first need to initialize the kernel using a function like os_kernel_init(), which prepares the system to manage threads. Next, you define the thread function—the code that the thread will run. Then, you create the thread by calling a function such as os_thread_create(), providing it with the thread’s stack, stack size, and a pointer to the function. If needed, you can repeat this step to create additional threads. Finally, you start the kernel using os_kernel_start(), which launches the scheduler and begins executing the created threads.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,3,'2025-06-22_16-51-29','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, you first need to initialize the kernel using a function like os_kernel_init(), which prepares the system to manage threads. Next, you define the thread function—the code that the thread will run. Then, you create the thread by calling a function such as os_thread_create(), providing it with the thread’s stack, stack size, and a pointer to the function. If needed, you can repeat this step to create additional threads. Finally, you start the kernel using os_kernel_start(), which launches the scheduler and begins executing the created threads.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,3,'2025-06-23_19-50-09','To set up and start a thread from the user’s perspective, you first need to initialize the kernel using a function like os_kernel_init(), which prepares the system to manage threads. Next, you define the thread function—the code that the thread will run. Then, you create the thread by calling a function such as os_thread_create(), providing it with the thread’s stack, stack size, and a pointer to the function. If needed, you can repeat this step to create additional threads. Finally, you start the kernel using os_kernel_start(), which launches the scheduler and begins executing the created threads.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,4,'2025-06-10_11-45-58','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory used to store stacks for multiple threads. Instead of manually allocating stack space for each new thread, the OS can assign each thread a unique portion of the stack pool. This allows multiple threads to have their own separate stacks in a predictable way. In future labs, the stack pool makes it easy to dynamically create threads, since the OS can simply select an unused region from the pool, initialize it, and assign it to a new thread. This enables efficient thread management and supports scalability in a multi-threaded OS.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,4,'2025-06-22_16-44-38','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory used to store stacks for multiple threads. Instead of manually allocating stack space for each new thread, the OS can assign each thread a unique portion of the stack pool. This allows multiple threads to have their own separate stacks in a predictable way. In future labs, the stack pool makes it easy to dynamically create threads, since the OS can simply select an unused region from the pool, initialize it, and assign it to a new thread. This enables efficient thread management and supports scalability in a multi-threaded OS.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',3,4,'2025-06-22_16-51-29','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory used by the operating system to provide stack space for both the OS itself and for each thread it manages. Every thread, including the main OS execution context, requires its own stack to track function calls, local variables, and return addresses. Instead of allocating stack memory ad hoc, the OS uses the stack pool to divide memory into fixed-size regions, assigning one to each thread or system-level task. In future labs, this design allows the OS to efficiently create and manage multiple threads by selecting unused stack regions from the pool, ensuring that each thread operates independently and safely. This also simplifies context switching, as each thread’s execution state is isolated in its dedicated stack within the pool.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,4,'2025-06-23_19-50-09','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory used by the operating system to provide stack space for both the OS itself and for each thread it manages. Every thread, including the main OS execution context, requires its own stack to track function calls, local variables, and return addresses. Instead of allocating stack memory ad hoc, the OS uses the stack pool to divide memory into fixed-size regions, assigning one to each thread or system-level task. In future labs, this design allows the OS to efficiently create and manage multiple threads by selecting unused stack regions from the pool, ensuring that each thread operates independently and safely. This also simplifies context switching, as each thread’s execution state is isolated in its dedicated stack within the pool.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,5,'2025-06-10_11-45-58','the kernel contains the core functions of the operating system—like scheduling, context switching, and managing hardware access. These are implemented in kernel.c and related files. The user, on the other hand, writes application code (such as thread functions or main programs), which lives in separate files like main.c. This separation is important because it isolates privileged operations in the kernel, preventing user code from accidentally or maliciously modifying core system behavior. From the user’s perspective, this structure makes the system easier to use, safer, and more modular. The user writes their application logic without needing to understand or alter how the OS itself works.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,5,'2025-06-22_16-44-38','the kernel contains the core functions of the operating system—like scheduling, context switching, and managing hardware access. These are implemented in kernel.c and related files. The user, on the other hand, writes application code (such as thread functions or main programs), which lives in separate files like main.c. This separation is important because it isolates privileged operations in the kernel, preventing user code from accidentally or maliciously modifying core system behavior. From the user’s perspective, this structure makes the system easier to use, safer, and more modular. The user writes their application logic without needing to understand or alter how the OS itself works.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',3,5,'2025-06-22_16-51-29','the kernel contains the core functions of the operating system—like scheduling, context switching, and managing hardware access. These are implemented in kernel.c and related files. The user, on the other hand, writes application code (such as thread functions or main programs), which lives in separate files like main.c. This separation is important because it isolates privileged operations in the kernel, preventing user code from accidentally or maliciously modifying core system behavior. From the user’s perspective, this structure makes the system easier to use, safer, and more modular. The user writes their application logic without needing to understand or alter how the OS itself works.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',3,5,'2025-06-23_19-50-09','The kernel contains the core, privileged functions of the operating system, including scheduling, context switching, managing hardware access, and crucially, memory management. These kernel functions are implemented in files like kernel.c. Memory management involves allocating and protecting memory spaces for different programs to ensure safe and efficient use of the system’s RAM, which is different from hardware access because it controls how memory is shared and isolated rather than how physical devices are accessed.\nThe user writes application code (such as thread functions or main programs) in separate files like main.c. User code cannot directly access hardware or manage memory; instead, it interacts with the kernel through well-defined interfaces such as system calls or API functions (e.g., creating threads, reading files, or allocating memory). These function calls request services from the kernel, which performs the privileged operations on behalf of the user.\nThis separation is important because it protects the core system from accidental or malicious interference by user programs, maintains system stability, and simplifies application development. From the user’s perspective, it means they can write application logic without needing to understand or control low-level system details, relying on the kernel to manage resources safely and efficiently.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',4,1,'2025-07-03_12-17-00','Using a queue for TCBs (Thread Control Blocks) allows the scheduler to easily implement round-robin scheduling by simply removing the current task from the front of the queue and re-inserting it at the back after it yields. This creates a natural rotation among tasks. Compared to an array, a queue simplifies thread switching logic and avoids the need to track an index manually. It also makes dynamic thread management easier—threads can be added or removed without shifting array elements.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',4,1,'2025-07-04_10-29-51','In our current lab implementation, the scheduler already behaves like a queue—it cycles through the TCBs in a fixed order and wraps around, effectively acting like a circular buffer. Using an explicit queue data structure would make this behavior more explicit: the thread at the front is dequeued (i.e., it runs), and after yielding, it is enqueued at the back. This makes the rotation clearer and aligns well with the natural semantics of round-robin scheduling. However, this approach can introduce additional overhead compared to using an array and an index, especially in embedded systems where minimizing memory and processing is critical. In practice, the array-based approach with an index may offer similar functionality with lower cost, so the queue doesn\'t provide significant functional benefits unless dynamic thread creation/removal is required.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',4,2,'2025-07-03_12-17-00','PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by first saving the current thread’s CPU state (registers) onto its stack, then updating the stack pointer in the current TCB. It then calls the scheduler to choose the next thread to run, loads that thread’s stack pointer, and restores its saved CPU state from its stack, effectively switching execution to that thread.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',4,2,'2025-07-04_10-29-51','The PendSV_Handler executes a context switch by first reading the current Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which points to the current thread’s stack. It then pushes the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto this stack to save the current thread’s context. Next, it stores the updated PSP back into the current thread’s TCB. The scheduler is then called to choose the next thread to run. Once the new thread is selected, its PSP is loaded from its TCB, and the registers R4–R11 are popped from its stack to restore its state. Finally, the PSP is updated so that the CPU resumes execution from the new thread as if it had never stopped.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',4,3,'2025-07-03_12-17-00','Always choosing the highest-priority task can cause starvation of lower-priority tasks—if a high-priority task is always ready, lower-priority tasks may never run. This violates fairness and can lead to system instability if critical low-priority tasks (like background logging or cleanup) never execute.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',4,3,'2025-07-04_10-29-51','If the scheduler always selects the highest-priority task, then only that task will ever run—lower-priority tasks will never be chosen, regardless of how long they’ve been waiting. This causes starvation, where low-priority tasks are permanently blocked. In other words, the scheduler becomes unfair and non-preemptive in practice, breaking the round-robin model. Critical but low-priority tasks like background services or maintenance threads might never execute, leading to unpredictable or unstable system behavior. Therefore, such a scheduler design undermines fairness and overall system reliability.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',4,4,'2025-07-03_12-17-00','Calling C functions improves readability, maintainability, and portability, making it easier to write, debug, and update the code. However, it introduces some overhead due to function calls and the need to manage the C calling convention. Writing everything in assembly can be more efficient, but it\'s harder to understand, error-prone, and less flexible for future changes.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',4,4,'2025-07-04_10-29-51','Calling C functions improves readability, maintainability, and portability, making it easier to write, debug, and update the code. However, it introduces some overhead due to function calls and the need to manage the C calling convention. Writing everything in assembly can be more efficient, but it\'s harder to understand, error-prone, and less flexible for future changes.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',4,5,'2025-07-03_12-17-00','Yes, the OS will still work, but it will effectively behave like a bare-metal loop. Since there\'s only one thread, the scheduler will always pick the same one after it yields. Yielding becomes redundant because there\'s no other thread to switch to. The system remains functional but does not demonstrate multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',4,5,'2025-07-04_10-29-51','Yes, the OS will still work, but it will effectively behave like a bare-metal loop. Since there\'s only one thread, the scheduler will always pick the same one after it yields. Yielding becomes redundant because there\'s no other thread to switch to. The system remains functional but does not demonstrate multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',5,1,'2025-07-15_15-03-34','The user passes an argument to a thread by placing the value (usually a pointer to a structure or variable) as a parameter in the thread creation function (e.g., osCreateThread(myThreadFunction)). Internally, the OS is responsible for storing that argument in the location where the thread will expect it upon execution. Specifically, the OS places the argument in R0, the first argument register according to the ARM calling convention. This is done when setting up the initial thread stack in osCreateThread() or osCreateThreadWithDeadline(), right before the thread starts running. It works because when the thread begins executing, the processor automatically pops values from the stack into the correct registers, including R0, so the thread receives the correct argument as if it were called normally.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',5,1,'2025-07-17_11-32-43','The user passes an argument to a thread by placing the value (usually a pointer to a structure or variable) as a parameter in the thread creation function (e.g., osCreateThread(myThreadFunction)). Internally, the OS is responsible for storing that argument in the location where the thread will expect it upon execution. Specifically, the OS places the argument in R0, the first argument register according to the ARM calling convention. This is done when setting up the initial thread stack in osCreateThread() or osCreateThreadWithDeadline(), right before the thread starts running. It works because when the thread begins executing, the processor automatically pops values from the stack into the correct registers, including R0, so the thread receives the correct argument as if it were called normally.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',5,2,'2025-07-15_15-03-34','Calling osYield() in any of the context-switching handlers is dangerous because osYield itself triggers a context switch using PendSV. If you call it inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, you risk re-entrant PendSV interrupts, which can lead to corrupting the thread state or crashing the system. Since PendSV is already being handled, calling osYield would re-trigger it before the current context switch completes, breaking the assumptions the OS makes about interrupt nesting and stack consistency. It can also overflow the stack or cause infinite recursion.\n',0,'s25'),('smiske',5,2,'2025-07-17_11-32-43','Calling osYield() from within an interrupt handler such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler causes serious problems due to how context switching and interrupts work on the Cortex-M4. osYield() triggers a software interrupt (usually by setting the PendSV bit), which is fine in thread mode but unsafe during an active interrupt. If invoked inside a handler, it can cause nested interrupts, interrupting the very process of saving or restoring thread context, which risks stack corruption. This corrupted stack may contain invalid return addresses or register values, potentially leading to a HardFault, which is a fatal exception on ARM processors. Moreover, because osYield() uses SVC, calling it within the SVC_Handler would re-trigger the same interrupt, causing infinite recursion and eventually a stack overflow. These effects are catastrophic in embedded systems, where memory is limited and safety is critical. For these reasons, osYield() must only be called from thread context, not from within an interrupt.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',5,3,'2025-07-15_15-03-34','Setting the OS tick to 0.1ms increases the timer resolution, which means the OS can make more frequent decisions about task switching and timing. Benefits; Finer control over time-based scheduling. Better responsiveness in real-time applications. More accurate delay/timing mechanisms. Downsides; Higher overhead: the CPU spends more time handling SysTick interrupts. Reduces available time for actual thread execution. Increases power consumption. Can overwhelm slower threads with too-frequent context switches\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',5,3,'2025-07-17_11-32-43','Setting the OS tick to 0.1ms increases the timer resolution, which means the OS can make more frequent decisions about task switching and timing. Benefits; Finer control over time-based scheduling. Better responsiveness in real-time applications. More accurate delay/timing mechanisms. Downsides; Higher overhead: the CPU spends more time handling SysTick interrupts. Reduces available time for actual thread execution. Increases power consumption. Can overwhelm slower threads with too-frequent context switches\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',5,4,'2025-07-15_15-03-34','Co-operative multitasking relies on each thread voluntarily yielding control back to the OS by calling osYield(). From the user\'s perspective, the thread must be written to yield periodically. The OS simply switches context when asked. Pre-emptive multitasking, on the other hand, uses a timer (like SysTick) to interrupt threads automatically and switch to another one. The user doesn’t have to write any yielding logic. The OS must set up and handle periodic interrupts, manage thread runtime, and trigger PendSV for context switching automatically.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',5,4,'2025-07-17_11-32-43','Co-operative multitasking relies on each thread voluntarily yielding control back to the OS by calling osYield(). From the user\'s perspective, the thread must be written to yield periodically. The OS simply switches context when asked. Pre-emptive multitasking, on the other hand, uses a timer (like SysTick) to interrupt threads automatically and switch to another one. The user doesn’t have to write any yielding logic. The OS must set up and handle periodic interrupts, manage thread runtime, and trigger PendSV for context switching automatically.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',5,5,'2025-07-15_15-03-34','Both mechanisms work because they trigger the same context-switching mechanism, typically via PendSV. osYield() explicitly sets the PendSV pending bit, while pre-emptive scheduling (e.g., from SysTick) does so when a thread’s runtime expires. Since the actual context switch happens in PendSV_Handler, both cooperative and preemptive switches are funneled through the same safe code path. This allows threads to yield voluntarily without waiting for the timer, while also ensuring fairness and responsiveness through automatic preemption.\n',1,'s25'),('smiske',5,5,'2025-07-17_11-32-43','Both mechanisms work because they trigger the same context-switching mechanism, typically via PendSV. osYield() explicitly sets the PendSV pending bit, while pre-emptive scheduling (e.g., from SysTick) does so when a thread’s runtime expires. Since the actual context switch happens in PendSV_Handler, both cooperative and preemptive switches are funneled through the same safe code path. This allows threads to yield voluntarily without waiting for the timer, while also ensuring fairness and responsiveness through automatic preemption.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',1,1,'2025-06-26_14-57-08','The HAL is a let of libraries provided by the manufacturer that helps to simplify low level tasks. The HAL provides easier functions to use instead of having to make complex code to control things like timers, GPIOs, etc. This helps speed up development and make the code more portable, and reduce errors.\n',0,'s25'),('sturetke',1,1,'2025-06-26_20-55-57','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is software that offers a standard interface between application code and the hardware. It simplifies development by hiding the complexity of low-level, device-specific programming. Instead of writing detailed hardware code, developers use high-level HAL functions for tasks like configuring timers, handling input/output, or UART communication. This abstraction speeds up development and makes code more portable and maintainable, allowing the same application to run on different microcontrollers or platforms with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',1,2,'2025-06-26_14-57-08','The __io_putchar function sends a character over UART so it can be printed to a serial console. It is needed because the printf function uses llower level functions for it’s output. In embedded systems, there is no screen by default, so we have to define how characters are sent. By using __io_putchar we can connect printf to the UART so it knows what to do\n',0,'s25'),('sturetke',1,2,'2025-06-26_20-55-57','The __io_putchar function handles sending one character at a time from printf to a specific hardware output—in this case, the UART interface, which enables displaying text on a serial console. Since printf itself doesn’t know how to interact with hardware, embedded systems require this function to link printf’s output to actual hardware. Each character printed by printf passes through __io_putchar, which routes it to the correct peripheral. The exact implementation depends on the hardware used; for instance, if output were redirected to USB or an LCD screen instead of UART, __io_putchar would need to be rewritten accordingly. This shows how embedded programming demands flexible, hardware-specific customization.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',1,3,'2025-06-26_14-57-08','We used the debugger because printf is limited to certain things such as the value of variables at certain points. But when working with something like a stack pointer, we need to know what the processor is doing, such as the value of the MSP register. The debugger allows you to pause the program, inspect memory, and view registers directly. This is important for verifying that the processor actually switched stacks\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',1,3,'2025-06-26_20-55-57','We used the debugger because printf is limited to certain things such as the value of variables at certain points. But when working with something like a stack pointer, we need to know what the processor is doing, such as the value of the MSP register. The debugger allows you to pause the program, inspect memory, and view registers directly. This is important for verifying that the processor actually switched stacks\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',1,4,'2025-06-26_14-57-08','Once we had MSP_INIT_VAL, which tells where the main stack starts in memory, we calculated a new PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 from it. This gave a new memory location below the main stack that could be used as the starting point for the process stack. We needed the initial MSP location so we could allocate a new stack region without overlapping the main one. It also allows us to treat the stack space as a large block of memory to be divided into different chunks.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',1,4,'2025-06-26_20-55-57','Once we had MSP_INIT_VAL, which tells where the main stack starts in memory, we calculated a new PSP_val by subtracting 0x400 from it. This gave a new memory location below the main stack that could be used as the starting point for the process stack. We needed the initial MSP location so we could allocate a new stack region without overlapping the main one. It also allows us to treat the stack space as a large block of memory to be divided into different chunks.\n',0,'s25'),('sturetke',1,5,'2025-06-26_14-57-08','If we know how many threads there are at compile time, we can predefine a fixed size for each thread’s stack and use a formula to calculate the starting address of each one. Starting from MSP_INIT_VAL, we would first skip the space for the main stack, then assign each new stack by offsetting further. We would define constants for main stack size and thread stack size, and loop through the number of threads to store each stack’s start address in an array\n',0,'s25'),('sturetke',1,5,'2025-06-26_20-55-57','To allocate multiple stacks for threads as described in the lab, you start with the initial main stack pointer value (MSP_INIT_VAL) and divide the stack memory into fixed-size blocks. Each thread’s stack has a fixed size (THREAD_STACK_SIZE), and the main stack has its own size (MAIN_STACK_SIZE). The starting address for thread j’s stack (with j starting at 1) is calculated as MSP_INIT_VAL plus MAIN_STACK_SIZE plus (j minus 1) times THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis method treats the entire stack memory as a big block, assigning each thread its own dedicated space right after the main stack. To comply with Cortex-M4 architecture requirements, THREAD_STACK_SIZE and MAIN_STACK_SIZE must be multiples of 8 to ensure 8-byte alignment, preventing processor faults. Additionally, the total allocated stack memory should not exceed MAX_STACK_SIZE and must fit within a valid RAM region. Following these guidelines guarantees that each thread’s stack address is safe, properly aligned, and non-overlapping, all computed at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',2,1,'2025-06-26_14-46-51','When I set the program counter (PC) to the address of print_continuously, I was basically telling the CPU to start executing that function. The address was first stored in register R0, and then the jumpAssembly function moved that value into the PC. Since the PC controls which instruction is executed next, the CPU immediately jumped to the instructions at print_continuously. This is similar to calling a function in a high-level language, except we manually redirected the flow by changing the PC. As a result, the CPU started running print_continuously, which is an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',2,2,'2025-06-26_14-46-51','A system call is a special kind of function that lets a user program ask the operating system to perform tasks like file access, memory allocation, or I/O operations. Unlike regular functions, system calls trigger a software interrupt (called a trap) that switches the CPU from user mode to kernel mode, allowing safe execution of privileged actions. This controlled access helps maintain system stability—for instance, rather than letting a program write directly to the disk (which could cause data loss), it uses a system call like write() to let the OS handle the operation securely and properly.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',2,3,'2025-06-26_14-46-51','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a dedicated memory space that the CPU will use when running that thread. This includes placing values onto the stack that represent what the CPU expects to see when it starts running a function, things like the PC, LR and general-purpose registers. In our setup, we manually pushed these values onto the stack in reverse order, mimicking what the hardware does during an actual context switch or function call. The stack pointer was then set to this memory location, and the CPU was told to start using it through the PSP. We store register values on the stack because when switching between threads or starting a new one, we need to restore the CPU to the correct state. This allows the thread to run independently with its own variables and return addresses, without interfering with other threads or the main program.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',2,4,'2025-06-26_14-46-51','We set the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) using __set_PSP before running the thread because the CPU needs to know where to find the thread’s stack. When a thread starts running, it expects the PSP to point to a valid stack that contains saved register values like the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and general-purpose registers. If we don’t set the PSP, the CPU might try to load this data from an invalid or uninitialized memory location during the context switch. This leads to unpredictable behavior or memory access violations, which causes a hard fault. By setting the PSP to point to our prepared thread stack, we ensure the CPU can safely restore the thread’s context and start executing the thread code correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',2,5,'2025-06-26_14-46-51','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead just printed something once and returned, the program would crash or trigger a hard fault. That’s because when the function returns, it tries to pop a return address from the stack (using the link register, LR) to know where to go next. But in our thread setup, there\'s no valid return address because this thread was never meant to return. Since the return address was either fake or missing, the CPU would jump to an invalid memory location or execute garbage instructions, leading to a crash. In thread-based systems like this, thread functions are typically designed to run forever or be explicitly terminated by the scheduler—not return like normal functions.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',3,1,'2025-06-26_20-33-35','The process of turning multiple C source files into a single executable involves three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, each C file goes through preprocessing, where directives such as #include and #define are expanded, and comments are removed, resulting in a pure C source file with all macros and headers included. Next, during compilation, each source file is translated into machine-level object files, where the code is checked for syntax errors and optimized. Finally, the linker combines all the object files into one executable, resolving function calls and variable references between files and linking in standard libraries like printf(). This results in one complete executable file.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',3,2,'2025-06-26_20-33-35','The #include directive is designed for header files, not source files. When you write #include<kernel.c>, you instruct the compiler to copy the entire contents of kernel.c into another .c file before compilation. This is problematic because it can cause the same code to be compiled multiple times, resulting in duplicate symbols during linking. Additionally, it disrupts modularity and can confuse the build system.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',3,3,'2025-06-26_20-33-35','Initialize the kernel by calling a function that prepares internal data structures and sets up the stack memory so threads can be created.\nDefine your thread function—a void function with no return—that contains the code the thread will run.\nPass the function’s address to the thread creation function. This tells the kernel which code the new thread will execute and sets up its stack and initial context for execution.\nStart the kernel by calling the function that triggers a system call to load the thread’s stack pointer and begin running the thread.\nThe thread then runs independently from the main program.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',3,4,'2025-06-26_20-33-35','The stack pool is a reserved memory area in the operating system that provides separate stacks for each thread it manages. Its main purpose is thread isolation—each thread requires its own stack to store local variables, return addresses, and saved CPU registers. Additionally, the stack pool helps with memory management by allowing the OS to allocate stack memory to threads in a controlled and organized way.\n',0,'s25'),('sturetke',3,5,'2025-06-26_20-33-35','User and kernel files differ mainly in their roles and responsibilities. Kernel files, like kernel.c and kernel.h, contain the low-level code that makes the operating system function—handling thread creation, stack setup, interrupts, and system calls. They provide essential services and abstractions that user code depends on.\nUser code, usually in main.c, contains the application logic written by the developer. This includes defining thread functions and calling kernel APIs like osCreateThread() and osKernelStart().\nThis separation is important because it gives users a clear, simple interface to interact with the system without needing to understand or modify the kernel internals. It improves modularity, security, and makes development easier by isolating hardware management and low-level operations within the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',4,1,'2025-07-30_18-57-16','In round robin scheduling, each thread takes turns executing in a fixed order. A queue is often more intuitive than an array for this pattern. With a queue, the current thread is dequeued when it finishes and is queued back in the rear, which ensures the next thread is always at the front of the queue. This queue structure naturally follows the FIFO behavior that round robin scheduling is based on. A benefit of using a queue is that it allows for more advanced scheduling, such as dynamic thread removal/insertion. It also improves readability and helps to separate the logic from the physical memory layout.\n',0,'s25'),('sturetke',4,1,'2025-07-30_20-33-04','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented with either an array or a queue, and both can achieve the same fundamental behavior: cycling through threads in a fixed order. Using a queue doesn\'t change how the scheduler behaves but provides a different way to manage the thread control blocks (TCBs). With a queue, the currently running thread is dequeued and re-enqueued at the back, which naturally follows the FIFO pattern that round-robin relies on. While this approach can improve code readability and flexibility such as supporting dynamic thread addition or removal, it may introduce slight overhead compared to an array, which uses simple index manipulation. Overall, the queue-based method is functionally equivalent but could trade a bit of performance for clarity or extensibility, depending on the implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',4,2,'2025-07-30_18-57-16','The pendSV_handler function executes a context switch by changing which thread the processor is currently running. When a thread calls osTield, it doesn’t switch directly to another thread, instead it first sets a special flag that causes the PendSV_handler to run once higher priority operations are finished. Then, the function osSched is called, which executes the context switch. The stack pointer is saved for later use, while the next thread’s stack pointer is loaded to resume execution. Switching the stack pointer effectively switches all the context required, allowing the CPU to jump between threads without losing their state.\n',0,'s25'),('sturetke',4,2,'2025-07-30_20-33-04','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread\'s execution state and restoring the next thread\'s state so that it can resume where it left off. It begins by using the PSP (Process Stack Pointer), which holds the stack location of the currently running thread. The handler first saves the callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto the current thread’s stack so none of its state is lost. Then, it stores the current PSP into the thread’s TCB to preserve its context. After that, it calls osSched, which updates the currentThread pointer to point to the next thread that should run. The PSP is then updated to the new thread’s saved stack pointer, and the callee-saved registers are restored from that thread’s stack. This process ensures that the CPU registers and stack are fully swapped out for the new thread, allowing it to continue execution as if it was never interrupted. Simply switching the stack pointer wouldn’t be enough, restoring the registers is critical to maintaining the execution flow.\n',0,'s25'),('sturetke',4,3,'2025-07-30_18-57-16','Modifying the OS to always run the highest-priority thread leads to a major issue. If a high-priority thread is always ready, lower-priority threads may never get a chance to run. This can block important background tasks and eventually destabilize the system. The approach also removes fairness from scheduling and makes the system less predictable unless additional mechanisms like time-slicing or priority aging are introduced which defeats the simplicity of the original idea. Overall this modification would not be a good idea to implement to the OS.\n',0,'s25'),('sturetke',4,3,'2025-07-30_20-33-04','If the OS scheduler is modified to always choose the thread with the highest priority, then whenever a high-priority thread is ready to run, it will be selected every single time the scheduler is invoked. This creates a situation known as starvation, where lower-priority threads are completely blocked from execution because they are never chosen. The scheduler logic would constantly return the same high-priority thread, effectively turning the system into a single-threaded environment despite multiple threads being present. This flaw leads to unfairness, prevents critical lower-priority tasks like background updates or I/O from ever executing, and breaks the cooperative nature of multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',4,4,'2025-07-30_18-57-16','Calling C functions instead of writing all tasks in assembly has many clear benefits, especially for readability and maintainability. C code far is easier to write, debug, and understand, especially when dealing with logic like indexing threads or updating pointers. It also makes the OS to expand or modify later. However, there are some minor downsides. Calling C from assembly adds some overhead due to function call setup and register handling, which might slightly impact performance in time-critical systems. Additionally, low-level operations like context saving/restoring are more precise and efficient in assembly. Overall, the use of C and assembly strikes a good balance between both approaches\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',4,4,'2025-07-30_20-33-04','Calling C functions instead of writing all tasks in assembly has many clear benefits, especially for readability and maintainability. C code far is easier to write, debug, and understand, especially when dealing with logic like indexing threads or updating pointers. It also makes the OS to expand or modify later. However, there are some minor downsides. Calling C from assembly adds some overhead due to function call setup and register handling, which might slightly impact performance in time-critical systems. Additionally, low-level operations like context saving/restoring are more precise and efficient in assembly. Overall, the use of C and assembly strikes a good balance between both approaches\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',4,5,'2025-07-30_18-57-16','If only a single thread is created and it calls yield() after printing, the OS will still technically work, but the call to yield() won’t have any meaningful effect. Since there\'s only one thread in the system, the scheduler will save its context and then switch right back to the same thread. This results in some unnecessary overhead from the context switch, but functionally, the thread will continue running as if it had never yielded. So in theory the OS will run fine, but the multitasking mechanism becomes redundant when there\'s only one thread to schedule.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',4,5,'2025-07-30_20-33-04','If only a single thread is created and it calls yield() after printing, the OS will still technically work, but the call to yield() won’t have any meaningful effect. Since there\'s only one thread in the system, the scheduler will save its context and then switch right back to the same thread. This results in some unnecessary overhead from the context switch, but functionally, the thread will continue running as if it had never yielded. So in theory the OS will run fine, but the multitasking mechanism becomes redundant when there\'s only one thread to schedule.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',5,1,'2025-07-30_18-52-03','To pass an argument into a thread, the user writes a thread function that takes a void* parameter and passes in a pointer to a struct or variable when creating the thread. The OS stores this pointer when setting up the thread’s context, usually placing it in a register like r0 or on the stack so that when the thread begins execution, it receives the argument as expected. This works because the calling convention for ARM Cortex-M (and most architectures) specifies that the first argument to a function is passed via a specific register. Therefore, by initializing the thread\'s stack or context to mimic what it would look like at the start of a regular function call with the argument properly placed, the CPU naturally delivers it when the thread is launched.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',5,1,'2025-07-30_20-21-11','To pass an argument into a thread, the user writes a thread function that takes a void* parameter and passes in a pointer to a struct or variable when creating the thread. The OS stores this pointer when setting up the thread’s context, usually placing it in a register like r0 or on the stack so that when the thread begins execution, it receives the argument as expected. This works because the calling convention for ARM Cortex-M (and most architectures) specifies that the first argument to a function is passed via a specific register. Therefore, by initializing the thread\'s stack or context to mimic what it would look like at the start of a regular function call with the argument properly placed, the CPU naturally delivers it when the thread is launched.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',5,2,'2025-07-30_18-52-03','You can’t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because these are already context-switching functions. The functions are running in special exception modes with elevated privileges and modified stack states. Calling osYield inside them would trigger another SVC interrupt, leading to nested exceptions or recursive context switches, which corrupts the stack and can cause undefined behavior or system crashes. Essentially, you’d be trying to context-switch while already in the middle of a context switch, which breaks the carefully controlled flow the OS relies on to manage threads safely.\n',0,'s25'),('sturetke',5,2,'2025-07-30_20-21-11','You can’t call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because these are already running in special exception modes on the Cortex-M4, where the processor is using a modified stack and elevated privileges to safely manage context switching. Calling osYield during one of these handlers triggers another SVC interrupt, which leads to nested exceptions while the stack is already in an inconsistent state. This can corrupt the stack and cause a hardfault, which is the specific fault the Cortex-M4 throws when the processor detects invalid stack operations during an exception. Since the OS relies on a strict sequence of context switching using PendSV and SVC, breaking this flow by invoking osYield inside those handlers undermines the system\'s stability and predictability.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',5,3,'2025-07-30_18-52-03','Reducing the OS clock interval to 0.1 ms increases the system’s responsiveness and allows for finer-grained time slicing, which can benefit real-time performance and improve the precision of task scheduling. However, this comes at a cost: the CPU will spend significantly more time handling SysTick interrupts, which increases overhead and can degrade overall system efficiency. Threads will have less uninterrupted execution time, making context switches more frequent and potentially starving longer tasks. It also adds pressure to ensure all interrupt service routines (ISRs) are extremely short and efficient, or the system could become unstable.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',5,3,'2025-07-30_20-21-11','Reducing the OS clock interval to 0.1 ms increases the system’s responsiveness and allows for finer-grained time slicing, which can benefit real-time performance and improve the precision of task scheduling. However, this comes at a cost: the CPU will spend significantly more time handling SysTick interrupts, which increases overhead and can degrade overall system efficiency. Threads will have less uninterrupted execution time, making context switches more frequent and potentially starving longer tasks. It also adds pressure to ensure all interrupt service routines (ISRs) are extremely short and efficient, or the system could become unstable.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',5,4,'2025-07-30_18-52-03','From the user\'s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily yield control using a function like osYield, meaning that a thread can block all others if it forgets to yield. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking allows the OS to automatically interrupt a running thread based on a timer, ensuring that all threads get CPU time regardless of whether they yield. For the OS, cooperative multitasking is simpler since context switches only happen when explicitly requested. Pre-emptive multitasking is more complex because the OS must configure timer interrupts, handle context saving and restoring during interrupts, and manage potential race conditions from forced switches.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',5,4,'2025-07-30_20-21-11','From the user\'s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires threads to voluntarily yield control using a function like osYield, meaning that a thread can block all others if it forgets to yield. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking allows the OS to automatically interrupt a running thread based on a timer, ensuring that all threads get CPU time regardless of whether they yield. For the OS, cooperative multitasking is simpler since context switches only happen when explicitly requested. Pre-emptive multitasking is more complex because the OS must configure timer interrupts, handle context saving and restoring during interrupts, and manage potential race conditions from forced switches.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',5,5,'2025-07-30_18-52-03','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because both ultimately trigger the same underlying mechanism, the PendSV interrupt. When a thread calls osYield, it voluntarily requests a context switch by setting the PendSV bit, whereas the system timer triggers a context switch automatically when a thread\'s time slice expires. In both cases, the OS saves the current thread’s context and restores the next thread’s context in the PendSV handler. Since both methods use the same context-switching infrastructure, they can coexist seamlessly, allowing the system to support both cooperative and pre-emptive multitasking within the same framework.\n',1,'s25'),('sturetke',5,5,'2025-07-30_20-21-11','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because both ultimately trigger the same underlying mechanism, the PendSV interrupt. When a thread calls osYield, it voluntarily requests a context switch by setting the PendSV bit, whereas the system timer triggers a context switch automatically when a thread\'s time slice expires. In both cases, the OS saves the current thread’s context and restores the next thread’s context in the PendSV handler. Since both methods use the same context-switching infrastructure, they can coexist seamlessly, allowing the system to support both cooperative and pre-emptive multitasking within the same framework.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',1,1,'2025-06-25_14-16-16','HAL simplifies programming the hardware and makes it easier to access hardware. In a general embedded project, HAL saves time and makes the code easier to read and maintain.\n',0,'s25'),('t3jang',1,1,'2025-06-25_14-51-47','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer. It is a collection of software routines provided by STMicroelectronics that helps developers interact with the microcontroller hardware more easily. Instead of writing low-level code to access peripherals like timers, UARTs, or GPIOs directly, you can use HAL functions that handle the setup and communication for you. This is especially helpful in embedded projects because it speeds up development, reduces errors, and makes code easier to read and maintain. Clearly defining acronyms like HAL is important because it ensures that anyone reading the explanation, even if they’re new to the topic, can follow along without confusion.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',1,2,'2025-06-25_14-16-16','It prints a single character to the console using UART. The function connects printf to the UART hardware so that characters can actually be sent to the computer.\n',0,'s25'),('t3jang',1,2,'2025-06-25_14-51-47','The __io_putchar function is needed to make the standard C printf function work with embedded hardware, like a microcontroller’s UART. While printf handles formatting, it doesn’t know how to send characters to hardware — it relies on a low-level output function to do that. Since different embedded systems use different output methods (like UART, USB, or LCD), __io_putchar acts as a custom bridge between printf and the specific hardware you’re using. By defining this function ourselves, we give printf a way to send each character to the correct device. This approach provides flexibility across platforms, because we can change only this one function to support different hardware, without rewriting our whole program. It’s a good example of abstraction — separating what we want to do (print a character) from how it actually happens on the hardware.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',1,3,'2025-06-25_14-16-16','The debugger in the lab is used to step through the code and check what is happening inside the microcontroller, since printf cannot show everything.\n',0,'s25'),('t3jang',1,3,'2025-06-25_14-51-47','We used the debugger in this lab because it allows us to inspect specific hardware components, like the MSP and PSP register values, which printf alone cannot access. While printf is useful for displaying general program output, it has major limitations in embedded systems like the Cortex M4 — it doesn’t provide direct access to internal processor registers, and standard I/O isn’t available by default. The debugger lets us pause execution at specific lines using breakpoints, step through instructions, and view the contents of memory and CPU registers in real time. This was especially important in this lab when we needed to verify the exact stack pointer locations to confirm our stack setup was working correctly. Without the debugger, we’d be guessing based on printed output, which isn’t enough for low-level hardware validation.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',1,4,'2025-06-25_14-16-16','The stack allocation splits a large block of memory into smaller chunks for different uses. One we obtained the value for MSP_init_val, we can get a new value for PSP_val by subtracting the size of the main stack (0x400) from it. We needed the initial location of MSP so that we know where the main stack ends and thus can figure out where it is safe to place the PSP.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',1,4,'2025-06-25_14-51-47','The stack allocation splits a large block of memory into smaller chunks for different uses. One we obtained the value for MSP_init_val, we can get a new value for PSP_val by subtracting the size of the main stack (0x400) from it. We needed the initial location of MSP so that we know where the main stack ends and thus can figure out where it is safe to place the PSP.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',1,5,'2025-06-25_14-16-16','If we wanted to allocate multiple new stacks (for multiple threads) using the same method, we would define the constants for the main stack size, thread stack size, and number of threads (known at compile time). Starting from the initial MSP value (MSP_init_val), we calculate each thread’s stack starting address. Knowing MSP_init_val is important because it tells us where the main stack ends, which is the reference point for placing the rest of the stacks in memory.\n',0,'s25'),('t3jang',1,5,'2025-06-25_14-51-47','If we want to allocate multiple stacks for threads and the number of threads is known at compile time, we can use a static memory layout. First, we define constants for MAIN_STACK_SIZE, THREAD_STACK_SIZE, and NUM_THREADS. After obtaining the initial main stack pointer value (MSP_init_val), we compute each thread’s stack starting address using the formula:\nADDR_STACK_j = MSP_init_val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE,\nwhere j is the thread number starting from 1. This ensures each thread stack is placed sequentially in memory, directly after the main stack. Knowing MSP_init_val is essential because it marks the base address from which we calculate all other stack locations. Since the number of threads is fixed, we can precompute and statically assign each stack location at compile time. We also need to ensure that the stack addresses are word-aligned (typically 4-byte aligned on Cortex-M) and that we don’t exceed the maximum available stack space (MAX_STACK_SIZE) defined in the linker script.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',2,1,'2025-06-26_14-28-03','When I set the PC (program counter) to point to my print_continuously function, the processor started executing that function as if it were the main program. This caused the MCU to repeatedly run the instructions in print_continuously, which printed \"Hello, PC!\" in an infinite loop. By directly modifying the PC, I effectively jumped into the thread\'s execution context.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',2,1,'2025-06-26_15-45-49','When I set the PC (program counter) to point to my print_continuously function, the processor started executing that function as if it were the main program. This caused the MCU to repeatedly run the instructions in print_continuously, which printed \"Hello, PC!\" in an infinite loop. By directly modifying the PC, I effectively jumped into the thread\'s execution context.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',2,2,'2025-06-26_14-28-03','A system call is a way for a program to request a service from the operating system or low-level system code. In this lab, we used the SVC (Supervisor Call) instruction to trigger the SVC_Handler, which allowed us to switch context or print messages by choosing different cases based on the system call number.\n',0,'s25'),('t3jang',2,2,'2025-06-26_15-45-49','A system call is a special kind of function call that allows user-level code to request services from the kernel. Since user programs aren’t allowed to directly access hardware or perform privileged operations (like context switching or managing memory), they need a controlled way to interact with the operating system — this is where system calls come in.\nWhen a system call is made, it causes the processor to switch from user mode to kernel mode using an interrupt (typically a SVC instruction in ARM). This safely transfers control to the kernel, which can then perform the requested service, like starting a thread or yielding the CPU. After handling the request, the processor switches back to user mode to resume the user’s program.\nSystem calls also enforce security and stability by keeping the kernel isolated from potentially buggy or malicious user code. This separation helps protect the system while still enabling users to access necessary low-level functions.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',2,3,'2025-06-26_14-28-03','Setting up a thread’s stack means preloading the stack with the values the processor expects when starting execution. This includes values for registers like the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and general-purpose registers. We stored these on the stack so that when the thread is started, the runFirstThread function can restore them and begin execution properly. This setup mimics what the CPU would normally do during an interrupt or context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('t3jang',2,3,'2025-06-26_15-45-49','Setting up a thread’s stack begins by finding a valid memory location to use — this is usually a chunk of memory from a designated stack pool. Each thread needs its own private section of the stack to avoid interfering with others. Once the memory location is selected, the initial values of key CPU registers are manually pushed onto the stack in a specific order to mimic what the CPU expects during an exception return. This includes the program counter (PC), link register (LR), and general-purpose registers like R0–R12. The order matters because the CPU expects to pop the values in a specific sequence when switching context. If the registers are not stored in the correct order, the processor could restore incorrect data, causing the thread to crash or behave unpredictably. Following the ARM architecture’s calling convention ensures that the thread starts executing correctly and maintains system stability.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',2,4,'2025-06-26_14-28-03','The __set_PSP function sets the Process Stack Pointer to our thread’s stack. If we didn’t do this before running the thread, the processor would continue using the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which could corrupt the main program’s stack or cause a fault. Since the thread’s context expects its own stack space, failing to switch to PSP would lead to invalid memory access and a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',2,4,'2025-06-26_15-45-49','The __set_PSP function sets the Process Stack Pointer to our thread’s stack. If we didn’t do this before running the thread, the processor would continue using the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which could corrupt the main program’s stack or cause a fault. Since the thread’s context expects its own stack space, failing to switch to PSP would lead to invalid memory access and a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',2,5,'2025-06-26_14-28-03','If the thread function returned instead of looping forever, the processor would try to pop the return address from the stack and jump to it. But since this thread was manually set up and didn\'t have a valid return address or calling context, it would likely jump to garbage memory and crash, resulting in a hard fault or undefined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',2,5,'2025-06-26_15-45-49','If the thread function returned instead of looping forever, the processor would try to pop the return address from the stack and jump to it. But since this thread was manually set up and didn\'t have a valid return address or calling context, it would likely jump to garbage memory and crash, resulting in a hard fault or undefined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,1,'2025-06-26_14-42-18','Each .c file is first compiled separately into an object file (.o) by the compiler. These object files contain machine code but are not yet a complete program. Then, the linker takes all these object files and combines them into a single executable file. The linker also resolves references between files, like when one file uses a function or variable defined in another. This whole process allows you to break your program into modular pieces.\n',0,'s25'),('t3jang',3,1,'2025-06-26_16-01-31','When multiple .c files are compiled into a single executable, the process happens in several ordered steps:\nPreprocessing: Each .c file is first passed through the preprocessor, which handles #include, #define, and other directives. This step expands macros and inserts the content of header files, so the compiler has a complete view of each source file.\nCompilation: The preprocessed code is then compiled into object files (.o or .obj). Each file is translated independently into machine code, but unresolved symbols (like external function or variable names) are left as placeholders.\nLinking: The linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable. During this step, it resolves those previously unknown references across files (for example, connecting a function defined in kernel.c to a call in main.c). It also connects in standard library code and sets up the entry point of the program.\nOutput: After successful linking, the final product is an executable file (.elf for embedded systems) that the microcontroller can load and run.\nThis ordered sequence ensures that dependencies between files are handled correctly. If the steps are skipped or misordered (e.g. trying to link without compiling), the build will fail. Each step plays a vital role in making sure your code goes from human-readable to machine-executable.\nEach .c file is first compiled separately into an object file (.o) by the compiler. These object files contain machine code but are not yet a complete program. Then, the linker takes all these object files and combines them into a single executable file. The linker also resolves references between files, like when one file uses a function or variable defined in another. This whole process allows you to break your program into modular pieces.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,1,'2025-06-26_17-57-49','When multiple .c files are compiled into a single executable, the process happens in several ordered steps:\nPreprocessing: Each .c file is first passed through the preprocessor, which handles #include, #define, and other directives. This step expands macros and inserts the content of header files, so the compiler has a complete view of each source file.\nCompilation: The preprocessed code is then compiled into object files (.o or .obj). Each file is translated independently into machine code, but unresolved symbols (like external function or variable names) are left as placeholders.\nLinking: The linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable. During this step, it resolves those previously unknown references across files (for example, connecting a function defined in kernel.c to a call in main.c). It also connects in standard library code and sets up the entry point of the program.\nOutput: After successful linking, the final product is an executable file (.elf for embedded systems) that the microcontroller can load and run.\nThis ordered sequence ensures that dependencies between files are handled correctly. If the steps are skipped or misordered (e.g. trying to link without compiling), the build will fail. Each step plays a vital role in making sure your code goes from human-readable to machine-executable.\nEach .c file is first compiled separately into an object file (.o) by the compiler. These object files contain machine code but are not yet a complete program. Then, the linker takes all these object files and combines them into a single executable file. The linker also resolves references between files, like when one file uses a function or variable defined in another. This whole process allows you to break your program into modular pieces.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,2,'2025-06-26_14-42-18','Including a .c file with #include copies its contents directly into another file, which leads to duplicate definitions when the compiler sees both the included .c file and its corresponding object file. This breaks the modular compilation process and causes errors like multiple definitions of functions or variables. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only the corresponding .h headers should be included for declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,2,'2025-06-26_16-01-31','Including a .c file with #include copies its contents directly into another file, which leads to duplicate definitions when the compiler sees both the included .c file and its corresponding object file. This breaks the modular compilation process and causes errors like multiple definitions of functions or variables. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only the corresponding .h headers should be included for declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,2,'2025-06-26_17-57-49','Including a .c file with #include copies its contents directly into another file, which leads to duplicate definitions when the compiler sees both the included .c file and its corresponding object file. This breaks the modular compilation process and causes errors like multiple definitions of functions or variables. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and only the corresponding .h headers should be included for declarations.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,3,'2025-06-26_14-42-18','To set up and start a thread:\nInitialize the kernel using osKernelInit().\nCreate the thread by passing your function to osCreateThread().\nStart the thread using osKernelStart().\nAfter that, the thread begins running and takes over the processor using the thread stack we’ve set up.\n',0,'s25'),('t3jang',3,3,'2025-06-26_16-01-31','Write the thread function: You must first create the function that the thread will run. This function must have the correct format (no arguments and no return value for this lab).\nInitialize the kernel: Before creating any threads, you call a function like osKernelInit() to prepare the kernel’s internal structures and stack pool.\nCreate the thread: You use a function like osCreateThread() and pass in a pointer to your thread function. Behind the scenes, this sets up the thread’s stack and stores the function pointer, so the OS knows what to run.\nStart the kernel: Once all threads are created, calling osKernelStart() launches the first thread. This transitions the system into thread-based execution.\nThe user is explicitly responsible for writing the thread function and calling the setup APIs in the right order. The OS handles low-level details like stack setup and context switching, but the user must follow the setup steps exactly to get a working multithreaded system.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,3,'2025-06-26_17-57-49','Write the thread function: You must first create the function that the thread will run. This function must have the correct format (no arguments and no return value for this lab).\nInitialize the kernel: Before creating any threads, you call a function like osKernelInit() to prepare the kernel’s internal structures and stack pool.\nCreate the thread: You use a function like osCreateThread() and pass in a pointer to your thread function. Behind the scenes, this sets up the thread’s stack and stores the function pointer, so the OS knows what to run.\nStart the kernel: Once all threads are created, calling osKernelStart() launches the first thread. This transitions the system into thread-based execution.\nThe user is explicitly responsible for writing the thread function and calling the setup APIs in the right order. The OS handles low-level details like stack setup and context switching, but the user must follow the setup steps exactly to get a working multithreaded system.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,4,'2025-06-26_14-42-18','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory that holds stack space for each thread. Since each thread needs its own private stack to store local variables and register values, the pool is divided so each thread gets a separate chunk. In future labs, we can expand our OS to support multiple threads by assigning a different stack from the pool to each one, and then switching between them using context switching.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,4,'2025-06-26_16-01-31','The stack pool is a reserved section of memory that holds stack space for each thread. Since each thread needs its own private stack to store local variables and register values, the pool is divided so each thread gets a separate chunk. In future labs, we can expand our OS to support multiple threads by assigning a different stack from the pool to each one, and then switching between them using context switching.\n',0,'s25'),('t3jang',3,4,'2025-06-26_17-57-49','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory set aside specifically for stack usage in our operating system. It is shared by both the operating system and all the threads that get created. Each thread in the OS needs its own private stack so it can store local variables, function return addresses, and register values during execution. When a new thread is created, a portion of the stack pool is carved out and assigned to that thread. This ensures that threads don’t overwrite each other’s data.  The operating system itself also uses a portion of the stack pool to handle system-level tasks like interrupts and scheduling. For example, when a system call is made or the OS switches threads, it needs space to save registers and manage control flow. That stack space comes from the same overall pool. In future labs where we’ll create multiple threads, this stack pool ensures each thread has enough memory to run independently while still sharing space efficiently. The OS must keep track of how much of the pool has been used and where new stack frames can safely be allocated. By including both the OS and the threads as users of this memory region, we make sure the system stays stable and that stack overflows or memory corruption don’t happen due to bad allocation.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,5,'2025-06-26_14-42-18','The user code contains the application logic — the functions the user writes and wants to run in a thread. This goes into main.c. The kernel code handles low-level operations like thread creation, stack setup, and switching to the thread — it lives in kernel.c. This separation keeps the system modular: users can create and run threads without needing to touch the kernel’s internal logic. It also improves maintainability and safety by keeping system-level operations separate from user-defined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,5,'2025-06-26_16-01-31','The user code contains the application logic — the functions the user writes and wants to run in a thread. This goes into main.c. The kernel code handles low-level operations like thread creation, stack setup, and switching to the thread — it lives in kernel.c. This separation keeps the system modular: users can create and run threads without needing to touch the kernel’s internal logic. It also improves maintainability and safety by keeping system-level operations separate from user-defined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',3,5,'2025-06-26_17-57-49','The user code contains the application logic — the functions the user writes and wants to run in a thread. This goes into main.c. The kernel code handles low-level operations like thread creation, stack setup, and switching to the thread — it lives in kernel.c. This separation keeps the system modular: users can create and run threads without needing to touch the kernel’s internal logic. It also improves maintainability and safety by keeping system-level operations separate from user-defined behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',4,1,'2025-07-28_14-14-06','Round‑robin scheduling with a queue works by treating your thread control blocks exactly like items in a FIFO line. When a thread is created its TCB is enqueued at the back of the queue. The scheduler then simply dequeues the head TCB, lets that thread run for one time slice, and as soon as it yields, it re‑enqueues its TCB at the tail. In this way each thread naturally cycles through in order without any modulo arithmetic or explicit wrap‑around logic, therefore it keeps popping and pushing until the queue is empty or the system shuts down.\nUsing a queue instead of a fixed array has some benefits. One advantage is that it makes thread creation and teardown uniform, so adding or removing a thread is just an enqueue or dequeue operation, with no need to shift array entries or maintain a “hole” list. Another advantage is that both enqueue and dequeue are constant‑time operations in a well‑implemented ring buffer or linked list, so there’s no extra cost as thread count grows. Furthermore, it’s straightforward to implement more advanced policies and blocking a thread omits its enqueue step, and you can even layer multiple queues for priority or feedback scheduling without rewriting the core logic. Overall, a simple FIFO queue keeps the scheduler code cleaner, more flexible, and easier to extend.\n',0,'s25'),('t3jang',4,1,'2025-07-28_14-53-31','In a queue‑based round‑robin scheduler, each new thread’s TCB is enqueued at the tail of a FIFO. The scheduler loop simply dequeues the head TCB, runs that thread for one time slice, and when it yields re‑enqueues its TCB at the back. This gives the same “each thread in turn” behavior without any explicit modulo arithmetic or wrap‑around logic.\nIn fact, our fixed‑size array plus a current = (current+1)%num_threads index is really just an implicit ring buffer queue: incrementing and wrapping the index achieves the same push/pop semantics with almost zero overhead. Switching to an explicit queue (whether a linked list or dynamically‑sized ring buffer) brings flexibility—threads can be inserted or removed on the fly, blocking simply skips an enqueue, and you can chain multiple queues for priority or feedback scheduling.\nThe trade‑off is extra cost: a linked‑list queue needs next pointers, and pointer dereferences on each enqueue/dequeue (more RAM and cycles), and a dynamic ring buffer needs head/tail indices and potential resizing logic. In a small, embedded OS with a fixed set of tasks, the simple array/index method is faster, more predictable, and uses less memory. But once you need true dynamic creation, deletion, or multiple priority queues, an explicit FIFO queue can keep your scheduler logic cleaner—despite the slight overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',4,2,'2025-07-28_14-14-06','When PendSV fires, the processor first pushes the “hardware” registers (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0) onto the process stack. The PendSV handler stub then pushes the rest of the context (R4–R11), so that all of the thread’s registers live on its PSP. Inside that handler we branch into our C function osSched(), which does the actual switch: it calls __get_PSP() to grab the current stack pointer, backs it up by eight words to include R4–R11, and writes that pointer into the outgoing thread’s control‐block. Next it picks the next thread (by bumping current_thread modulo num_threads), reads that thread’s saved PSP from its TCB, and does __set_PSP() to load it. When PendSV returns, the CPU automatically pops R4–R11 (from our stub) and then “unwinds” the hardware-stacked registers, resuming execution right where the new thread last yielded.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',4,2,'2025-07-28_14-53-31','When PendSV fires, the processor first pushes the “hardware” registers (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0) onto the process stack. The PendSV handler stub then pushes the rest of the context (R4–R11), so that all of the thread’s registers live on its PSP. Inside that handler we branch into our C function osSched(), which does the actual switch: it calls __get_PSP() to grab the current stack pointer, backs it up by eight words to include R4–R11, and writes that pointer into the outgoing thread’s control‐block. Next it picks the next thread (by bumping current_thread modulo num_threads), reads that thread’s saved PSP from its TCB, and does __set_PSP() to load it. When PendSV returns, the CPU automatically pops R4–R11 (from our stub) and then “unwinds” the hardware-stacked registers, resuming execution right where the new thread last yielded.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',4,3,'2025-07-28_14-14-06','Always running the highest‐priority TCB is tempting, but without any fairness or aging mechanism it quickly leads to starvation: a never‐ending high‐priority task will keep getting rescheduled and lower‐priority tasks will never run. You also lose predictability, since any new or interrupt‐driven high‐priority work can indefinitely postpone background processing. In other words, pure “pick the max priority” scheduling destroys basic liveness guarantees and makes it almost impossible to reason about whether—and when—each task will actually get CPU ti\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',4,3,'2025-07-28_14-53-31','Always running the highest‐priority TCB is tempting, but without any fairness or aging mechanism it quickly leads to starvation: a never‐ending high‐priority task will keep getting rescheduled and lower‐priority tasks will never run. You also lose predictability, since any new or interrupt‐driven high‐priority work can indefinitely postpone background processing. In other words, pure “pick the max priority” scheduling destroys basic liveness guarantees and makes it almost impossible to reason about whether—and when—each task will actually get CPU ti\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',4,4,'2025-07-28_14-14-06','Calling out to small C routines from an assembly stub strikes a good balance between control and convenience. On the upside, you can write most of your scheduler logic in C—where it’s easier to read, maintain, and debug—while keeping just the minimal entry/exit glue in assembly. You get type checking, compiler optimizations, and portability to other Cortex-M chips without rewriting all of your exception-handling code. On the downside there’s a tiny function-call overhead and you have to obey the compiler’s calling conventions (stack alignment, pushed registers, etc.), which is why we keep the assembly stubs “naked” and only branch into C once we’ve saved everything manually. If you had written the entire context switch in straight assembly you’d avoid that call/return overhead and have absolute control over every instruction, but you’d also end up with longer, harder-to-audit code that’s brittle to changes in toolchains or core revisions.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',4,4,'2025-07-28_14-53-31','Calling out to small C routines from an assembly stub strikes a good balance between control and convenience. On the upside, you can write most of your scheduler logic in C—where it’s easier to read, maintain, and debug—while keeping just the minimal entry/exit glue in assembly. You get type checking, compiler optimizations, and portability to other Cortex-M chips without rewriting all of your exception-handling code. On the downside there’s a tiny function-call overhead and you have to obey the compiler’s calling conventions (stack alignment, pushed registers, etc.), which is why we keep the assembly stubs “naked” and only branch into C once we’ve saved everything manually. If you had written the entire context switch in straight assembly you’d avoid that call/return overhead and have absolute control over every instruction, but you’d also end up with longer, harder-to-audit code that’s brittle to changes in toolchains or core revisions.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',4,5,'2025-07-28_14-14-06','If you only ever create one thread—say your print_continuously loop that does a printf and then osYield()—the OS still runs just fine. Each time that thread yields, PendSV fires, osSched saves its context, bumps current_thread = (0+1)%1 = 0, then restores the same thread’s stack pointer. In other words you context-switch right back into the only thread you have. You won’t see any other “tasks” run, but nothing breaks: your printf keeps happening, the yield/syscall machinery still works, and the scheduler remains valid. The only downside is the extra overhead of saving and restoring exactly the same context every time, but functionally the OS still does its job.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',4,5,'2025-07-28_14-53-31','If you only ever create one thread—say your print_continuously loop that does a printf and then osYield()—the OS still runs just fine. Each time that thread yields, PendSV fires, osSched saves its context, bumps current_thread = (0+1)%1 = 0, then restores the same thread’s stack pointer. In other words you context-switch right back into the only thread you have. You won’t see any other “tasks” run, but nothing breaks: your printf keeps happening, the yield/syscall machinery still works, and the scheduler remains valid. The only downside is the extra overhead of saving and restoring exactly the same context every time, but functionally the OS still does its job.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',5,1,'2025-07-28_18-31-50','When a user wants to pass an argument into a new thread, they begin by allocating or defining that data in their code—often a small struct or variable—and then calling the OS’s thread‑creation API with a pointer to both the thread function and the data. Internally, the OS reserves a separate stack for the new thread and builds an initial exception frame on that stack. Among the registers it initializes in this frame is R0, which the ARM exception return sequence will restore into the thread’s first function call. By placing the user’s pointer into that slot, the processor automatically delivers the data address as the thread’s argument when it performs the SVC return into Thread mode. In this way, the user simply passes a pointer at creation time, and the OS’s stack‑frame setup and the ARM hardware work together to hand that pointer to the thread function when it starts.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',5,2,'2025-07-28_18-31-50','You must never call osYield() (which itself triggers an SVC) from inside any of the low‑level context‑switching handlers—SVC_Handler, PendSV_Handler, or SysTick_Handler—because doing so would attempt to issue a software interrupt while already servicing an exception. That misuses the ARM exception model by nesting an SVC on top of itself, corrupting the exception return context and mixing up which stack (MSP vs. PSP) is in use. Instead, these handlers simply set the PendSV pending bit and execute an instruction‑synchronization barrier; when the handler exits, the processor will then take PendSV at the correct priority and perform the actual context switch cleanly. Issuing another SVC from within the handler would bypass this pended mechanism, leading to stack corruption, unpredictable failures, or a hard lockup.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',5,3,'2025-07-28_18-31-50','Shrinking the OS clock tick from 1 ms to 0.1 ms would give your system tenfold finer timing granularity—and faster pre‑emptive switches—so threads can be scheduled with much tighter jitter and delays. This benefits real‑time tasks that require sub‑millisecond responsiveness or very precise periodic execution. However, the downside is significantly higher interrupt overhead: the CPU would be interrupted ten times more often, stealing more cycles for the SysTick handler and context‑switch checks, which increases overall interrupt latency for other peripherals and reduces the time available for actual application code. Moreover, the power consumption cost goes up because each tick wakes the processor. In short, you gain resolution and responsiveness at the expense of CPU utilization, increased interrupt load, and potentially higher power draw.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',5,4,'2025-07-28_18-31-50','From the user’s perspective, cooperative multitasking requires each thread to voluntarily call a yield function when it reaches a safe point; until then, it monopolizes the CPU. The OS’s job in that model is simply to swap in the next thread when osYield() is called, with no background timer or forced preemption. In contrast, pre‑emptive multitasking frees the user from having to explicitly yield—threads run until the OS’s timer interrupt (SysTick) counts down their timeslice and automatically triggers a context switch via PendSV. Implementing this requires the OS to maintain per‑thread runtime counters, decrement them on each tick, and pend a context switch without relying on the thread’s cooperation. Thus, cooperative only needs a manual yield call, while pre‑emptive demands hardware timers, interrupt handlers, and careful priority management to enforce fairness.\n',1,'s25'),('t3jang',5,5,'2025-07-28_18-31-50','In this lab, cooperative yields and time‑based preemption are simply two ways to invoke the same context‑switch machinery (PendSV). A thread that calls osYield() triggers an SVC, which in turn pends PendSV after resetting its timeslice, handing control back to the scheduler. Meanwhile, the SysTick interrupt periodically decrements the running thread’s remaining runtime and, when it reaches zero, also pends PendSV to force a switch. Because both mechanisms ultimately queue the PendSV exception—and because each switch resets the thread’s runtime to its timeslice—threads can voluntarily relinquish the CPU or be preempted on a timer tick without any conflict. The scheduler doesn’t care whether it was invoked by an explicit yield or by the hardware timer; it simply saves the current thread’s state, picks the next thread, restores its state, and resumes execution. Thus, cooperative and pre‑emptive switching coexist seamlessly atop the same low‑level context‑switch routine.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',1,1,'2025-05-23_11-41-29','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it is a software layer that works between the code and the microcontroller’s raw registers. Instead of manipulating bits directly, you call HAL functions that take care of all device-specific register details. This makes it easier to make, read and maintain projects for several applications.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',1,1,'2025-05-23_15-13-35','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it is a software layer that works between the code and the microcontroller’s raw registers. Instead of manipulating bits directly, you call HAL functions that take care of all device-specific register details. This makes it easier to make, read and maintain projects for several applications.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',1,2,'2025-05-23_11-41-29','__io_putchar() is what printf() calls every time it needs to emit one character. Implementing __io_putchar() forwards each byte to UART so that higher-level printing functions can work for bare-metal microcontrollers, where there is no default console. Without this function, printf() has nowhere to send its data.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',1,2,'2025-05-23_15-13-35','__io_putchar() is what printf() calls every time it needs to emit one character. Implementing __io_putchar() forwards each byte to UART so that higher-level printing functions can work for bare-metal microcontrollers, where there is no default console. Without this function, printf() has nowhere to send its data.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',1,3,'2025-05-23_11-41-29','The debugger is useful for tracing the values of any variables or registers simultaneously, and can stop at any point of the code. Using printf() only displays certain values and is limited in its ability to show where possible errors could be.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',1,3,'2025-05-23_15-13-35','The debugger is useful for tracing the values of any variables or registers simultaneously, and can stop at any point of the code. Using printf() only displays certain values and is limited in its ability to show where possible errors could be.\nThe debugger runs outside the code. The user has the ability to halt the core at any instruction, peer directly into the CPU and peripheral registers and RAM,  and set break or watch points without executing extra instructions. By contrast, printf() is inside the code; it inserts additional instructions that must execute, and can only emit the values that are explicitly passed to it after those instructions are run. Because printf() operates through the C library I/O layer, it has no direct path to read CPU or peripheral registers. Instead, to print a register its value would need to be copied into a variable. A debugger is better for examining registers and real-time view of the system state, while printf() is best for quick views of certain values.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',1,4,'2025-05-23_11-41-29','Once the MSP_init_val value is read, the entire stack region is treated as one long array. The PSP value is 0x400 offset from the MSP_init_val, so it is below that address in the array by the main stack size. The main stack size is 0x400 in this case. Knowing the initial location of the MSP is essential because it is the only reliable that guarantees every new PSP block starts in free RAM and never overlaps the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',1,4,'2025-05-23_15-13-35','Once the MSP_init_val value is read, the entire stack region is treated as one long array. The PSP value is 0x400 offset from the MSP_init_val, so it is below that address in the array by the main stack size. The main stack size is 0x400 in this case. Knowing the initial location of the MSP is essential because it is the only reliable that guarantees every new PSP block starts in free RAM and never overlaps the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',1,5,'2025-05-23_11-41-29','Since the number of threads is now fixed, all the arithmetic can be replaced with compile-time constants. At start-up, MSP_init_val is still read, but the array is immediately filled, where entry j is computed once with the same formula as provided, ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_init_val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This tells the rest of the code where every stack begins with no further calculation needed. It also guarantees that the blocks do not overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',1,5,'2025-05-23_15-13-35','Since the number of threads is now fixed, all the arithmetic can be replaced with compile-time constants. At start-up, MSP_init_val is still read, but the array is immediately filled, where entry j is computed once with the same formula as provided, ADDR_STACK_j = MSP_init_val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j-1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This tells the rest of the code where every stack begins with no further calculation needed. It also guarantees that the blocks do not overlap.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,1,'2025-06-23_18-55-38','When the PC was changed to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to and began executing that function. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that continuously prints to the console, the CPU just stayed there, repeatedly printing the message and never returning.\nThis worked because the jumpAssembly function used the assembly instruction MOV PC, R0, which tells the CPU to set the program counter to whatever value is in register R0. Since R0 held the address of print_continuously, the CPU essentially \"jumped\" to that function and began executing it as if it were called directly.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,1,'2025-06-24_09-20-19','When the PC was changed to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to and began executing that function. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that continuously prints to the console, the CPU just stayed there, repeatedly printing the message and never returning.\nThis worked because the jumpAssembly function used the assembly instruction MOV PC, R0, which tells the CPU to set the program counter to whatever value is in register R0. Since R0 held the address of print_continuously, the CPU essentially \"jumped\" to that function and began executing it as if it were called directly.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,1,'2025-06-24_09-37-22','When the PC was changed to hold the address of the print_continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to and began executing that function. Since print_continuously contains an infinite loop that continuously prints to the console, the CPU just stayed there, repeatedly printing the message and never returning.\nThis worked because the jumpAssembly function used the assembly instruction MOV PC, R0, which tells the CPU to set the program counter to whatever value is in register R0. Since R0 held the address of print_continuously, the CPU essentially \"jumped\" to that function and began executing it as if it were called directly.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,2,'2025-06-23_18-55-38','A system call is a way for a program to request a service from the operating system. For example, if the program wants to read a file or send data over the internet, it can’t do it on its own because it doesn’t have the required permissions or direct access to hardware. Instead, a system call will allow the operating system to take over and performs the task.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',2,2,'2025-06-24_09-20-19','A system call is a special kind of function call that a program uses to request services from the kernel, which is the core part of the operating system responsible for managing hardware and system resources. Unlike regular function calls that stay within the boundaries of user code, a system call transitions the program from user mode to kernel mode, allowing it to perform privileged operations that regular code isn\'t allowed to do directly. For example, if a program wants to allocate memory, it can\'t access the hardware or system resources on its own due to protection mechanisms enforced by the OS. Instead, it makes a system call like malloc(). The system call acts as a controlled gateway that allows the kernel to carry out the requested task on behalf of the program, while maintaining security and stability.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,2,'2025-06-24_09-37-22','A system call is a special kind of function call that a program uses to request services from the kernel, which is the core part of the operating system responsible for managing hardware and system resources. Unlike regular function calls that stay within the boundaries of user code, a system call transitions the program from user mode to kernel mode, allowing it to perform privileged operations that regular code isn\'t allowed to do directly. For example, if a program wants to allocate memory, it can\'t access the hardware or system resources on its own due to protection mechanisms enforced by the OS. Instead, it makes a system call like malloc(). The system call acts as a controlled gateway that allows the kernel to carry out the requested task on behalf of the program, while maintaining security and stability.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,3,'2025-06-23_18-55-38','To run the thread, the thread’s stack was manually prepared. The register values R0-R12, LR, PC and xPSR had to be pushed to the stack in reverse order since the stack grows downwards. When the processer resumes the thread, it can pop these values back into the registers in the right order. Setting up a thread’s stack means to allocate space for the stack. Storing registers on the stack is done because the CPU uses the stack to remember the state of the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,3,'2025-06-24_09-20-19','To run the thread, the thread’s stack was manually prepared. The register values R0-R12, LR, PC and xPSR had to be pushed to the stack in reverse order since the stack grows downwards. When the processer resumes the thread, it can pop these values back into the registers in the right order. Setting up a thread’s stack means to allocate space for the stack. Storing registers on the stack is done because the CPU uses the stack to remember the state of the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,3,'2025-06-24_09-37-22','To run the thread, the thread’s stack was manually prepared. The register values R0-R12, LR, PC and xPSR had to be pushed to the stack in reverse order since the stack grows downwards. When the processer resumes the thread, it can pop these values back into the registers in the right order. Setting up a thread’s stack means to allocate space for the stack. Storing registers on the stack is done because the CPU uses the stack to remember the state of the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,4,'2025-06-23_18-55-38','If the PSP is not set to point to the thread’s stack before trying to run the thread, the processor will try to use an uninitialized or invalid stack pointer or return from the system call. When the runFirstThread() function is called, the processor expects a valid, correctly ordered stack at the PSP location. If PSP is not set, the CPU will ultimately and hard fault by performing an illegal operation. Setting PSP ensures that the processor has a valid stack to pop the simulated thread context from.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,4,'2025-06-24_09-20-19','If the PSP is not set to point to the thread’s stack before trying to run the thread, the processor will try to use an uninitialized or invalid stack pointer or return from the system call. When the runFirstThread() function is called, the processor expects a valid, correctly ordered stack at the PSP location. If PSP is not set, the CPU will ultimately and hard fault by performing an illegal operation. Setting PSP ensures that the processor has a valid stack to pop the simulated thread context from.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',2,4,'2025-06-24_09-37-22','If the PSP is not set to point to the thread’s stack before attempting to run the thread, the system will encounter a hard fault. This happens because the processor relies on the PSP to locate the thread’s stack during exception return, which is how the system begins executing the thread. By default, the processor uses the Main Stack Pointer for operations like interrupt handling and initialization. However, in a multithreading environment, each thread needs its own stack, and for this purpose, the processor can use the PSP, which is specifically designed to serve as the stack pointer for user threads.\nIn the lab setup, the thread was manually prepared on a stack. But this stack is not usable unless PSP is explicitly pointed to it. If we try to start the thread without setting PSP, the processor will still attempt to perform a context switch using whatever PSP currently points to, which is likely garbage or uninitialized memory. As a result, when the processor tries to pop register values from an invalid location during runFirstThread(), it performs an illegal operation and triggers a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,5,'2025-06-23_18-55-38','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead just printed something once and returned, then the thread would simply exit or terminate after running that one line of code. This happens because threads are often used to perform ongoing tasks, so if it doesn’t loop or wait for something, it will just end.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',2,5,'2025-06-24_09-20-19','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead just printed something once and returned, the thread would not exit cleanly. Instead, it would cause a hard fault on the system. This is because threads are typically launched by manually setting up their stack to simulate what the CPU expects when returning from a function. However, when setting up a thread manually in this lab, there was not a valid return address for the thread function; there was only the necessary context to begin execution. So, if the thread function tries to return, it will attempt to jump to an invalid or arbitrary value. Since this value is not a valid memory address or doesn\'t point to executable code, the processor triggers a hard fault exception, which effectively crashes the program. In a usual system, threads aren\'t expected to return; they should either loop indefinitely or be terminated by the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',2,5,'2025-06-24_09-37-22','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead just printed something once and returned, the thread would not exit cleanly. Instead, it would cause a hard fault on the system. This is because threads are typically launched by manually setting up their stack to simulate what the CPU expects when returning from a function. However, when setting up a thread manually in this lab, there was not a valid return address for the thread function; there was only the necessary context to begin execution. So, if the thread function tries to return, it will attempt to jump to an invalid or arbitrary value. Since this value is not a valid memory address or doesn\'t point to executable code, the processor triggers a hard fault exception, which effectively crashes the program. In a usual system, threads aren\'t expected to return; they should either loop indefinitely or be terminated by the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,1,'2025-06-25_17-43-49','Each .c file in the project is compiled separately into an object file. These object files contain machine code but are not yet executable. The compiler compiles each file based on the function and struct declarations it sees, provided by the #include statements from header files. After compilation, the linker takes all the object files and links them together into a single executable.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',3,1,'2025-06-25_19-25-48','The process of compiling multiple .c files into a single executable involves three distinct stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. In the preprocessing stage, the preprocessor handles all lines beginning with #, such as #include and #define. This means it replaces include directives with the actual contents of the header files. The result is a fully expanded C file with all dependencies ready for compilation. During the compiling stage, each preprocessed .c file is compiled independently into an object file. The compiler translates the C code into machine code instructions, but at this point, the program is still not executable. Lastly, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable program. It resolves all external references by matching function calls and variable accesses across files, assigns final memory addresses, and includes any required runtime libraries. The linker also ensures there is only one definition for each symbol and will raise errors if any references remain unresolved or if duplicates exist.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,1,'2025-06-25_19-57-27','The process of compiling multiple .c files into a single executable involves three distinct stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. In the preprocessing stage, the preprocessor handles all lines beginning with #, such as #include and #define. This means it replaces include directives with the actual contents of the header files. The result is a fully expanded C file with all dependencies ready for compilation. During the compiling stage, each preprocessed .c file is compiled independently into an object file. The compiler translates the C code into machine code instructions, but at this point, the program is still not executable. Lastly, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable program. It resolves all external references by matching function calls and variable accesses across files, assigns final memory addresses, and includes any required runtime libraries. The linker also ensures there is only one definition for each symbol and will raise errors if any references remain unresolved or if duplicates exist.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,2,'2025-06-25_17-43-49','Including a .c file using #include essentially copies and pastes its contents into your file before compilation. This bypasses the proper compilation process. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and their interfaces should be declared in corresponding .h files. This separation ensures clean compilation and prevents unintended interactions between files.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,2,'2025-06-25_19-25-48','Including a .c file using #include essentially copies and pastes its contents into your file before compilation. This bypasses the proper compilation process. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately and their interfaces should be declared in corresponding .h files. This separation ensures clean compilation and prevents unintended interactions between files.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',3,2,'2025-06-25_19-57-27','Including a .c file using #include is problematic because it effectively copies and pastes the entire contents of that file into another before compilation, bypassing the proper build process. While this may seem harmless, it leads to serious issues during linking. Specifically, anything global defined in the included .c file are now duplicated in every file that includes it. When the compiler generates object files, each will contain its own copy of these definitions.\nDuring the linking phase, the linker attempts to merge all object files into a single executable. If the same function or variable is defined in more than one object file due to multiple #include statements of the same .c file, the linker will encounter duplicate errors and fail. Instead, .c files should be compiled separately, and their functions or variables should be made accessible using declarations in .h header files.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,3,'2025-06-25_17-43-49','From the user\'s point of view, setting up and starting a thread involves the following steps:\nWrite the thread function: This is the actual task or behavior the thread performs.\nInitialize the kernel: Call a function to prepare the OS, including stack tracking and thread structures.\nCreate the thread: Use a kernel-provided function and pass the thread function to it. This allocates stack space and sets up the thread\'s execution context.\nStart the kernel: Call a function to begin running the thread. This sets the stack pointer and triggers execution.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,3,'2025-06-25_19-25-48','From the user\'s point of view, setting up and starting a thread involves the following steps:\nWrite the thread function: This is the actual task or behavior the thread performs.\nInitialize the kernel: Call a function to prepare the OS, including stack tracking and thread structures.\nCreate the thread: Use a kernel-provided function and pass the thread function to it. This allocates stack space and sets up the thread\'s execution context.\nStart the kernel: Call a function to begin running the thread. This sets the stack pointer and triggers execution.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,3,'2025-06-25_19-57-27','From the user\'s point of view, setting up and starting a thread involves the following steps:\nWrite the thread function: This is the actual task or behavior the thread performs.\nInitialize the kernel: Call a function to prepare the OS, including stack tracking and thread structures.\nCreate the thread: Use a kernel-provided function and pass the thread function to it. This allocates stack space and sets up the thread\'s execution context.\nStart the kernel: Call a function to begin running the thread. This sets the stack pointer and triggers execution.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,4,'2025-06-25_17-43-49','The stack pool is a reserved region of RAM used to store the stacks for all threads. Each thread needs its own stack space to function correctly. In future labs, when multiple threads are introduced, the kernel will divide this stack pool into equal-sized chunks. A stack allocator function will manage this pool, handing out memory blocks for each new thread. This way, each thread has its own area to store local variables, return addresses, and context, enabling safe multitasking without interference.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',3,4,'2025-06-25_19-25-48','The stack pool is a reserved region of RAM specifically designated for storing the stacks required by both the operating system kernel and each individual user thread. Every thread needs its own private stack space to hold local variables, return addresses, and saved register values during context switches. Likewise, the operating system itself needs dedicated stack space for handling system-level operations such as interrupts, exceptions, and system calls. All of these stacks are carved out of the same global stack pool. In future labs, when the OS begins supporting multiple threads, the kernel will divide the stack pool into equal-sized blocks to allocate a separate stack for every new thread. A stack allocator function within the kernel will keep track of how much of the stack pool has been used and will hand out memory blocks as new threads are created. This systematic management of stack memory is essential to ensuring that each thread operates in isolation, without interfering with the memory of others, and that the kernel itself has a reliable stack to carry out critical system functions. Omitting the kernel’s use of the stack pool would leave out a fundamental aspect of multitasking support, where both thread execution and kernel reliability depend on this shared, carefully managed memory space.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,4,'2025-06-25_19-57-27','The stack pool is a reserved region of RAM specifically designated for storing the stacks required by both the operating system kernel and each individual user thread. Every thread needs its own private stack space to hold local variables, return addresses, and saved register values during context switches. Likewise, the operating system itself needs dedicated stack space for handling system-level operations such as interrupts, exceptions, and system calls. All of these stacks are carved out of the same global stack pool. In future labs, when the OS begins supporting multiple threads, the kernel will divide the stack pool into equal-sized blocks to allocate a separate stack for every new thread. A stack allocator function within the kernel will keep track of how much of the stack pool has been used and will hand out memory blocks as new threads are created. This systematic management of stack memory is essential to ensuring that each thread operates in isolation, without interfering with the memory of others, and that the kernel itself has a reliable stack to carry out critical system functions. Omitting the kernel’s use of the stack pool would leave out a fundamental aspect of multitasking support, where both thread execution and kernel reliability depend on this shared, carefully managed memory space.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,5,'2025-06-25_17-43-49','The user code contains application-level logic; what the threads do, how they interact, and the order in which the user starts and stops them. The kernel code contains all the low-level OS functionality; this includes stack allocation, thread management, system calls, and hardware setup. This separation is important because it keeps the system organized and modular. From the user’s perspective, it means they don’t need to understand or modify the complex kernel logic to create or run threads. They can just call the kernel API. It also makes debugging and development easier, since bugs in user logic won’t directly interfere with the kernel and vice versa.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,5,'2025-06-25_19-25-48','The user code contains application-level logic; what the threads do, how they interact, and the order in which the user starts and stops them. The kernel code contains all the low-level OS functionality; this includes stack allocation, thread management, system calls, and hardware setup. This separation is important because it keeps the system organized and modular. From the user’s perspective, it means they don’t need to understand or modify the complex kernel logic to create or run threads. They can just call the kernel API. It also makes debugging and development easier, since bugs in user logic won’t directly interfere with the kernel and vice versa.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',3,5,'2025-06-25_19-57-27','The user code contains application-level logic; what the threads do, how they interact, and the order in which the user starts and stops them. The kernel code contains all the low-level OS functionality; this includes stack allocation, thread management, system calls, and hardware setup. This separation is important because it keeps the system organized and modular. From the user’s perspective, it means they don’t need to understand or modify the complex kernel logic to create or run threads. They can just call the kernel API. It also makes debugging and development easier, since bugs in user logic won’t directly interfere with the kernel and vice versa.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',4,1,'2025-07-02_23-10-46','Using a queue to store TCBs makes round-robin scheduling more intuitive and efficient. In round-robin scheduling, each thread is given a fixed time slice to execute before it yields and is replaced by the next thread. With a queue, the currently running thread can be dequeued and then enqueued again at the end, ensuring that all threads get equal access to the CPU in FIFO manner. A queue also supports more dynamic behavior, making it easier to add or remove threads during runtime. In contrast, using an array would require tracking the index of the current thread, which adds complexity. Overall, the use of a queue simplifies scheduler logic and better models fair, cyclic thread execution.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',4,1,'2025-07-02_23-36-22','Using a queue to store TCBs can make round-robin scheduling both intuitive and structured. In this scheduling strategy, the thread at the front of the queue is always the one selected to run next. After it completes its time slice or yields, it is moved to the back of the queue, and the next thread at the front is selected—creating a cyclic and fair rotation among all active threads. This behavior is FIFO, making it a natural fit for round-robin systems. One of the key advantages of using a queue over an array is that it takes away the need to manually track and update an index, which is required when using an array to cycle through threads. This not only reduces complexity in the scheduler but also allows for more dynamic behavior, such as easier insertion or removal of threads at runtime. However, implementing a queue introduces some overhead. For instance, enqueue and dequeue operations may involve pointer updates or memory management logic, especially if implemented using linked structures. These operations may not be as fast as simple index arithmetic in an array-based implementation.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',4,2,'2025-07-02_23-10-46','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for performing a context switch, allowing the OS to switch from one thread to another. When a context switch is needed, such as when a thread yields or its time slice ends, the PendSV interrupt is triggered. Inside the handler, the current thread’s CPU state is saved onto its stack. The current PSP is saved into that thread\'s TCB so that its execution context can be restored later. After saving the current thread\'s context, the handler calls osSched() to determine which thread should run next. The chosen thread’s PSP is then loaded, and its saved registers are restored from its stack. The handler then returns, and the processor resumes execution in the new thread.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',4,2,'2025-07-02_23-36-22','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s state and restoring the state of the next thread to run. When a context switch is needed, such as when a thread calls osYield() or its time slice ends, the PendSV interrupt is triggered. The handler begins by reading the current PSP, which points to the location in memory where the current thread’s stack is stored. This is crucial because all context saving and restoring operations are performed using the PSP, not the MSP. Next, the handler pushes registers R4–R11 onto the current thread’s stack. These registers are preserved across function calls. Once these registers are saved, the updated PSP, which now reflects the reduced stack after the push operations, is stored into the thread’s TCB so the thread\'s exact execution context can be restored later. After saving the current thread’s state, the handler calls osSched(), which selects the next thread to run. Once the next thread has been chosen, the scheduler updates the global pointer to reference it. The handler then loads the next thread’s PSP from its TCB and pops registers R4–R11 from its stack, effectively restoring that thread’s saved CPU context. Finally, the PSP is updated to reflect the new thread’s position in memory, and the PendSV_Handler exits. When control returns from the handler, the processor resumes execution at the instruction where the newly selected thread last left off.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',4,3,'2025-07-02_23-10-46','The main issue is starvation; if a high-priority thread is always ready to run and never yields or blocks, lower-priority threads may never get a chance to execute. This can prevent essential background or system maintenance tasks from running at all. Additionally, the system becomes less predictable. Unlike round-robin scheduling, which ensures fairness, strict priority-based scheduling can lead to instability and unresponsiveness. Using the logic of having a larger number be assigned to a higher priority also means that the scheduler may never know if the number it sees is the highest priority.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',4,3,'2025-07-02_23-36-22','The main problem with modifying the scheduler to always select the highest-priority task is that it allows only that task to run, potentially excluding all others indefinitely. The scheduler does not consider whether other tasks are ready to run; it simply picks the task with the numerically highest priority each time it is called. This design means that if the highest-priority task is always in the ready state and never yields or blocks, no lower-priority tasks will ever get CPU time, regardless of their importance or readiness. This leads to task starvation, where lower-priority threads, may never be executed. Over time, this can cause critical system functions to stall or fail. It also removes the predictability and fairness that round-robin scheduling guarantees, where all threads get a chance to run. The flaw lies in the scheduler’s strict policy of always choosing the highest-priority thread without considering fairness or whether other threads deserve CPU time. Priority-based scheduling can be useful, but it must be combined with fairness mechanisms to ensure that lower-priority tasks are not indefinitely blocked.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',4,4,'2025-07-02_23-10-46','Calling C functions from within the assembly-based handlers like PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler offers a balance between low-level control and higher-level readability. Writing logic like scheduling or handling system calls in C makes the code easier to read, maintain, and debug. It allows the use of standard C syntax, structures, and compiler optimizations. It also keeps the assembly code short and focused only on the hardware-specific operations, like register saving and stack manipulation. However, calling C functions from assembly comes with some overhead. Critical sections requiring precise register or memory control are often better handled in assembly. While doing everything in assembly might result in slightly faster code, it would be harder to write, understand, and modify.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',4,4,'2025-07-02_23-36-22','Calling C functions from within the assembly-based handlers like PendSV_Handler and SVC_Handler offers a balance between low-level control and higher-level readability. Writing logic like scheduling or handling system calls in C makes the code easier to read, maintain, and debug. It allows the use of standard C syntax, structures, and compiler optimizations. It also keeps the assembly code short and focused only on the hardware-specific operations, like register saving and stack manipulation. However, calling C functions from assembly comes with some overhead. Critical sections requiring precise register or memory control are often better handled in assembly. While doing everything in assembly might result in slightly faster code, it would be harder to write, understand, and modify.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',4,5,'2025-07-02_23-10-46','If only a single thread is created, the OS will still function, but with some limitations. Each time the thread yields, the scheduler is triggered and selects the same thread again, effectively performing a context switch back to itself. While this works, it introduces unnecessary overhead since there is no real switching happening. If the thread fails to yield, the scheduler is never called, and the OS behaves in a situation where the single thread controls the CPU entirely. This behavior is valid but doesn’t use the benefits of a multitasking OS. While technically correct, using a single thread limits the system\'s capabilities and does not demonstrate the usefulness of the scheduler and context switching mechanisms.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',4,5,'2025-07-02_23-36-22','If only a single thread is created, the OS will still function, but with some limitations. Each time the thread yields, the scheduler is triggered and selects the same thread again, effectively performing a context switch back to itself. While this works, it introduces unnecessary overhead since there is no real switching happening. If the thread fails to yield, the scheduler is never called, and the OS behaves in a situation where the single thread controls the CPU entirely. This behavior is valid but doesn’t use the benefits of a multitasking OS. While technically correct, using a single thread limits the system\'s capabilities and does not demonstrate the usefulness of the scheduler and context switching mechanisms.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,1,'2025-07-27_04-48-29','To pass an argument into a thread, the user begins by writing their thread function to accept a void pointer argument. This allows the user to pass any type of data by casting it appropriately inside the thread function. For example, if the user wants to pass a struct, they define and populate it, then create the thread by passing both the function and a pointer to the struct. From the user’s perspective, this works just like passing an argument to a regular function. Behind the scenes, the operating system must prepare the new thread’s stack so that the CPU can start executing the thread function with the correct argument. This involves manually pushing values onto the stack in the order the CPU expects; starting with the xPSR register, followed by the PC, LR, and then registers R12 through R0. The OS places the address of the thread function in the PC slot so that when the CPU starts the thread, it knows where to begin execution. It also places the pointer to the user’s argument in the R0 slot, as R0 is the register used for the first argument. When the CPU restores the context and starts running the thread, it loads the thread function into the PC and the argument pointer into R0, just as it would in a normal function call. As a result, when the thread function begins executing, the argument appears correctly in its parameter. This approach works as a way to pass data into threads without requiring the OS to know anything about the specific data being passed.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,1,'2025-07-27_12-59-01','To pass an argument into a thread, the user begins by writing their thread function to accept a void pointer argument. This allows the user to pass any type of data by casting it appropriately inside the thread function. For example, if the user wants to pass a struct, they define and populate it, then create the thread by passing both the function and a pointer to the struct. From the user’s perspective, this works just like passing an argument to a regular function. Behind the scenes, the operating system must prepare the new thread’s stack so that the CPU can start executing the thread function with the correct argument. This involves manually pushing values onto the stack in the order the CPU expects; starting with the xPSR register, followed by the PC, LR, and then registers R12 through R0. The OS places the address of the thread function in the PC slot so that when the CPU starts the thread, it knows where to begin execution. It also places the pointer to the user’s argument in the R0 slot, as R0 is the register used for the first argument. When the CPU restores the context and starts running the thread, it loads the thread function into the PC and the argument pointer into R0, just as it would in a normal function call. As a result, when the thread function begins executing, the argument appears correctly in its parameter. This approach works as a way to pass data into threads without requiring the OS to know anything about the specific data being passed.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,1,'2025-07-27_15-51-13','To pass an argument into a thread, the user begins by writing their thread function to accept a void pointer argument. This allows the user to pass any type of data by casting it appropriately inside the thread function. For example, if the user wants to pass a struct, they define and populate it, then create the thread by passing both the function and a pointer to the struct. From the user’s perspective, this works just like passing an argument to a regular function. Behind the scenes, the operating system must prepare the new thread’s stack so that the CPU can start executing the thread function with the correct argument. This involves manually pushing values onto the stack in the order the CPU expects; starting with the xPSR register, followed by the PC, LR, and then registers R12 through R0. The OS places the address of the thread function in the PC slot so that when the CPU starts the thread, it knows where to begin execution. It also places the pointer to the user’s argument in the R0 slot, as R0 is the register used for the first argument. When the CPU restores the context and starts running the thread, it loads the thread function into the PC and the argument pointer into R0, just as it would in a normal function call. As a result, when the thread function begins executing, the argument appears correctly in its parameter. This approach works as a way to pass data into threads without requiring the OS to know anything about the specific data being passed.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,2,'2025-07-27_04-48-29','osYield() cannot be called from within any of the context-switching functions because it interferes with what is used to switch between threads. The osYield() function works by triggering a context switch by setting a specific bit in the ICSR to request the PendSV exception, which is responsible for performing the actual thread switch. However, if osYield() is called from within one of these handlers, the system is already in the middle of handling an interrupt or performing a context switch. Calling osYield() in this state would re-trigger a new context switch before the current one finishes, effectively causing a nested or recursive exception. This leads to undefined behavior, which results in system crashes or hard faults. For this reason, osYield() can only be called by threads running in regular thread mode, not during the execution of exception handlers.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,2,'2025-07-27_12-59-01','osYield() cannot be called from within any of the context-switching functions because it interferes with what is used to switch between threads. The osYield() function works by triggering a context switch by setting a specific bit in the ICSR to request the PendSV exception, which is responsible for performing the actual thread switch. However, if osYield() is called from within one of these handlers, the system is already in the middle of handling an interrupt or performing a context switch. Calling osYield() in this state would re-trigger a new context switch before the current one finishes, effectively causing a nested or recursive exception. This leads to undefined behavior, which results in system crashes or hard faults. For this reason, osYield() can only be called by threads running in regular thread mode, not during the execution of exception handlers.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',5,2,'2025-07-27_15-51-13','osYield() cannot be called from within any of the context-switching functions, such as PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler, because it functions as a system call that explicitly triggers an interrupt. Specifically, osYield() sets the PendSV interrupt pending bit in the ICSR to request a context switch. This action causes the processor to schedule a new exception return into the PendSV handler, which is responsible for saving and restoring thread contexts. While this behavior is correct when initiated from thread mode, calling osYield() from within an active interrupt handler creates a situation where the system attempts to nest a context switch inside another. This is problematic because the processor has already partially pushed the current context onto the stack. Triggering another exception at this point violates the expected stack and control flow behavior, potentially leading to overlapping or corrupted stack frames. Such corruption can result in incorrect register restoration, invalid return addresses, and ultimately, hard faults or system crashes. Calling osYield() from within a context-switching handler disrupts the atomic, deterministic nature of the exception handling process, causing the OS to lose track of where it was in the middle of switching threads. To avoid this, osYield() must only be called from thread mode, where it safely requests a context switch by deferring control to the PendSV handler after the current instruction finishes.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,3,'2025-07-27_04-48-29','In this lab, the fundamental OS clock was configured to tick every 1 millisecond, meaning the operating system checks and handles timing-related events at that interval. If we were to reduce the clock period to 0.1 milliseconds, one potential benefit would be improved responsiveness and finer timing resolution. Threads could be scheduled with greater precision, and the OS could react to events more quickly, which may be useful for applications requiring real-time behavior with tight timing constraints. However, a shorter tick interval significantly increases the number of interrupts the processor must handle per second, introducing considerable overhead. This frequent interruption can degrade overall system performance, as the CPU spends more time servicing SysTick interrupts and less time executing useful thread work. The increased context-switching frequency can also lead to stack overflows and timing issues if the system is not designed to handle such high rates. Therefore, while a 0.1 ms OS clock could offer better timing granularity, it would also reduce efficiency and reliability.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,3,'2025-07-27_12-59-01','In this lab, the fundamental OS clock was configured to tick every 1 millisecond, meaning the operating system checks and handles timing-related events at that interval. If we were to reduce the clock period to 0.1 milliseconds, one potential benefit would be improved responsiveness and finer timing resolution. Threads could be scheduled with greater precision, and the OS could react to events more quickly, which may be useful for applications requiring real-time behavior with tight timing constraints. However, a shorter tick interval significantly increases the number of interrupts the processor must handle per second, introducing considerable overhead. This frequent interruption can degrade overall system performance, as the CPU spends more time servicing SysTick interrupts and less time executing useful thread work. The increased context-switching frequency can also lead to stack overflows and timing issues if the system is not designed to handle such high rates. Therefore, while a 0.1 ms OS clock could offer better timing granularity, it would also reduce efficiency and reliability.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',5,3,'2025-07-27_15-51-13','In this lab, the OS clock was configured to tick every 1 millisecond, which sets the base timing resolution for scheduling, timeouts, and thread management. Reducing the clock period to 0.1 milliseconds would increase the OS\'s timing resolution, allowing it to respond to events with much finer granularity. This could be beneficial in applications that require high-precision timing, such as motor control, where accurate timing can lead to better control performance or responsiveness. A finer clock also improves the accuracy of timeouts and delays within the OS, enabling more predictable behavior for time-sensitive tasks. Additionally, it allows tighter control over thread runtime enforcement and scheduling decisions, which can be useful in safety-critical real-time systems where timing constraints must be strictly observed. However, a 0.1 ms clock would result in ten times as many SysTick interrupts per second, dramatically increasing the number of context switches and interrupt entries. This frequent interruption introduces considerable overhead, reducing the amount of CPU time available for actual thread execution. The constant saving and restoring of thread contexts may lead to increased power consumption as well. It can also put pressure on stack usage due to more frequent exception nesting, increasing the risk of stack overflows or corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,4,'2025-07-27_04-48-29','From the user\'s perspective, the difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking depends on how and when control is given up by a running thread. In co-operative multitasking, threads must voluntarily yield control back to the operating system by explicitly calling a function like osYield(). This gives the user direct control over when their thread stops running, which can make timing more predictable but also puts the responsibility on the user to ensure that all threads yield fairly. If one thread forgets to yield, it can monopolize the CPU and starve other threads. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking allows the operating system to automatically interrupt a running thread after a fixed amount of time and switch to another thread. This means the user does not need to worry about yielding manually, and all threads are guaranteed a chance to run, assuming fair scheduling. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler to implement because context switches only occur when a thread calls osYield(). The OS doesn’t need to track time or handle timer-based interrupts to manage switching. Pre-emptive multitasking, on the other hand, requires the OS to keep track of each thread’s remaining runtime using a timer, trigger context switches automatically using interrupts, and ensure that the switch happens safely even if a thread is in the middle of execution. While this adds complexity, it provides better fairness and responsiveness, especially in systems with time-sensitive tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,4,'2025-07-27_12-59-01','From the user\'s perspective, the difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking depends on how and when control is given up by a running thread. In co-operative multitasking, threads must voluntarily yield control back to the operating system by explicitly calling a function like osYield(). This gives the user direct control over when their thread stops running, which can make timing more predictable but also puts the responsibility on the user to ensure that all threads yield fairly. If one thread forgets to yield, it can monopolize the CPU and starve other threads. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking allows the operating system to automatically interrupt a running thread after a fixed amount of time and switch to another thread. This means the user does not need to worry about yielding manually, and all threads are guaranteed a chance to run, assuming fair scheduling. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler to implement because context switches only occur when a thread calls osYield(). The OS doesn’t need to track time or handle timer-based interrupts to manage switching. Pre-emptive multitasking, on the other hand, requires the OS to keep track of each thread’s remaining runtime using a timer, trigger context switches automatically using interrupts, and ensure that the switch happens safely even if a thread is in the middle of execution. While this adds complexity, it provides better fairness and responsiveness, especially in systems with time-sensitive tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,4,'2025-07-27_15-51-13','From the user\'s perspective, the difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking depends on how and when control is given up by a running thread. In co-operative multitasking, threads must voluntarily yield control back to the operating system by explicitly calling a function like osYield(). This gives the user direct control over when their thread stops running, which can make timing more predictable but also puts the responsibility on the user to ensure that all threads yield fairly. If one thread forgets to yield, it can monopolize the CPU and starve other threads. In contrast, pre-emptive multitasking allows the operating system to automatically interrupt a running thread after a fixed amount of time and switch to another thread. This means the user does not need to worry about yielding manually, and all threads are guaranteed a chance to run, assuming fair scheduling. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler to implement because context switches only occur when a thread calls osYield(). The OS doesn’t need to track time or handle timer-based interrupts to manage switching. Pre-emptive multitasking, on the other hand, requires the OS to keep track of each thread’s remaining runtime using a timer, trigger context switches automatically using interrupts, and ensure that the switch happens safely even if a thread is in the middle of execution. While this adds complexity, it provides better fairness and responsiveness, especially in systems with time-sensitive tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,5,'2025-07-27_04-48-29','These two mechanisms can work together because they both ultimately trigger the same outcome: a context switch managed by the operating system. In co-operative multitasking, the thread explicitly calls a function like osYield(), which signals the OS to switch to another thread. In pre-emptive multitasking, the SysTick timer interrupt decrements the currently running thread’s runtime, and once it reaches zero, the OS automatically initiates a context switch using the PendSV interrupt. Although the triggers are different, one is manual, the other is timer-driven, the context switch mechanism is shared. As long as the OS keeps track of each thread’s timeslice and resets it appropriately after a switch, both methods can coexist. This hybrid approach allows threads that are aware of the OS to yield when appropriate while still ensuring fairness and responsiveness through time-based pre-emption when a thread does not yield voluntarily.\n',0,'s25'),('tookoro',5,5,'2025-07-27_12-59-01','In the final exercise of this lab, a hybrid multitasking system that supports both co-operative and pre-emptive context switching was implemented. These two mechanisms work together because, although they are triggered differently, they both ultimately rely on the same context switch logic carried out by the PendSV_Handler. In co-operative multitasking, a thread voluntarily gives up the CPU by calling osYield(), which sets the PendSV interrupt pending bit (ICSR |= 1 << 28) to trigger the PendSV exception. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS uses the SysTick_Handler to decrement the currently running thread’s runtime every millisecond. When the runtime reaches zero, the OS also sets the PendSV pending bit, again invoking the PendSV_Handler. In both cases, whether initiated manually or by timer, the same PendSV_Handler is executed to perform the context switch. This saves the current thread’s state, updates scheduling information, and restores the context of the next thread. This shared use of the PendSV handler ensures consistent and safe switching between threads, regardless of the trigger. By centralizing the context switch in one handler, the OS avoids duplicated logic and maintains a single, reliable entry point for switching control between threads. This design enables co-operative and pre-emptive threads to coexist, giving responsive time-based switching while still allowing user-controlled yielding where appropriate.\n',1,'s25'),('tookoro',5,5,'2025-07-27_15-51-13','In the final exercise of this lab, a hybrid multitasking system that supports both co-operative and pre-emptive context switching was implemented. These two mechanisms work together because, although they are triggered differently, they both ultimately rely on the same context switch logic carried out by the PendSV_Handler. In co-operative multitasking, a thread voluntarily gives up the CPU by calling osYield(), which sets the PendSV interrupt pending bit (ICSR |= 1 << 28) to trigger the PendSV exception. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS uses the SysTick_Handler to decrement the currently running thread’s runtime every millisecond. When the runtime reaches zero, the OS also sets the PendSV pending bit, again invoking the PendSV_Handler. In both cases, whether initiated manually or by timer, the same PendSV_Handler is executed to perform the context switch. This saves the current thread’s state, updates scheduling information, and restores the context of the next thread. This shared use of the PendSV handler ensures consistent and safe switching between threads, regardless of the trigger. By centralizing the context switch in one handler, the OS avoids duplicated logic and maintains a single, reliable entry point for switching control between threads. This design enables co-operative and pre-emptive threads to coexist, giving responsive time-based switching while still allowing user-controlled yielding where appropriate.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',1,1,'2025-06-09_16-14-44','The Hardware Abstraction Layer is a set of prewritten functions provided by ST Microelectronics. HAL simplifies the interactions with the microcontroller’s hardware, allowing us to write low-level register code through a higher-level interface. The HAL makes embedded development faster and easier for the STM devices.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',1,1,'2025-06-09_16-45-12','The Hardware Abstraction Layer is a set of prewritten functions provided by ST Microelectronics. HAL simplifies the interactions with the microcontroller’s hardware, allowing us to write low-level register code through a higher-level interface. The HAL makes embedded development faster and easier for the STM devices.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',1,2,'2025-06-09_16-14-44','The __io_putchar function controls how characters are outputted using functions like printf. It essentially serves as a bridge between standard C I/O functions and the microcontroller’s hardware, redirecting individual characters into the desired output (such as a serial port or console).\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',1,2,'2025-06-09_16-45-12','The __io_putchar function controls how individual characters are transmitted using functions like printf. It acts as a bridge between standard C I/O functions and the specific microcontroller’s hardware, typically redirecting characters to a serial interface. This function must be written explicitly by the programmer because different hardware platforms and projects use different output peripherals. Without defining __io_putchar, the compiler would not know where to send the output, and printf would produce no visible result. By customizing __io_putchar, we can connect high-level software to low-level hardware behavior for our specific embedded setup.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',1,3,'2025-06-09_16-14-44','We used the debugger because printf doesn’t show us all the low-level behaviour of our program, such as register values and stack pointers. The debugger allows for a direct inspection of CPU registers so that we could see the values of SP, PSP, and MSP in real-time. This was important in ensuring that pointer calculations worked correctly, whereas printf would only show us the final output.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',1,3,'2025-06-09_16-45-12','We used the debugger because printf doesn’t show us all the low-level behaviour of our program, such as register values and stack pointers. The debugger allows for a direct inspection of CPU registers so that we could see the values of SP, PSP, and MSP in real-time. This was important in ensuring that pointer calculations worked correctly, whereas printf would only show us the final output.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',1,4,'2025-06-09_16-14-44','After obtaining MSP_init_val, the starting address of the main stack, we subtracted the fixed size of 0x400 bytes to calculate PSP_val. PSP_val is the starting address for the process stack, and we had to subtract 0x400 bytes to get this value because the stack grows downward in memory, and subtracting reserves space for the main stack. We needed to know the initial MSP location to ensure that the new PSP would not overlap with the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',1,4,'2025-06-09_16-45-12','After obtaining MSP_init_val, the starting address of the main stack, we subtracted the fixed size of 0x400 bytes to calculate PSP_val. PSP_val is the starting address for the process stack, and we had to subtract 0x400 bytes to get this value because the stack grows downward in memory, and subtracting reserves space for the main stack. We needed to know the initial MSP location to ensure that the new PSP would not overlap with the main stack.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',1,5,'2025-06-09_16-14-44','To allocate several stacks for use in multiple threads, we would need to define constants for the number of threads and the size of each thread’s stack. We would start from MSP_INIT_VAL and subtract MAIN_STACK_SIZE, then allocate each thread’s stack by further subtracting THREAD_STACK_SIZE x j for the j-th thread. This ensures each thread gets its own stack space without overlap. We would also need to use something like an array to keep track of each stack’s starting address.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',1,5,'2025-06-09_16-45-12','To allocate several stacks for use in multiple threads, we would need to define constants for the number of threads and the size of each thread’s stack. We would start from MSP_INIT_VAL and subtract MAIN_STACK_SIZE, then allocate each thread’s stack by further subtracting THREAD_STACK_SIZE x j for the j-th thread. This ensures each thread gets its own stack space without overlap.\nOn our target architecture, stack pointers must be 8-byte aligned. This means every stack’s starting address should be a multiple of 8. Misaligned stack pointers can lead to faults or unpredictable behaviour. To ensure that the allocated stacks do not overlap with the main stack, we would need to calculate each thread’s stack start address based on a fixed offset from a known base (e.g. MSP_INIT_VAL – MAIN_STACK_SIZE – THREAD-STACK_SIZE x (j-1)). Each thread should have a reserved region of memory so that it doesn’t overlap with any other stack range. We would also need to use something like an array to keep track of each stack’s starting address.\nTo verify that the total memory required for all stacks is available and fits within the memory region, at startup we should compute the total memory needed, using something like:\nTOTAL_STACK_USAGE = MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (NUM_THREADS X THREAD_STACK_SIZE)\nThen, we would need to compare it to the available RAM (SRAM_END – SRAM_START). If TOTAL_STACK_USAGE exceeds this size, we would need to reduce the number of threads or stack sizes.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,1,'2025-06-23_12-59-41','When I set the program counter register to point to the print continuously function, the CPU began executing the function like it was the next instruction. PC determines what code is going to be executed, and changing the code execution location to our thread function made the processor jump into that code first. This allowed our thread to begin running from the start of the print continuously function.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,1,'2025-06-23_15-54-57','When I set the program counter register to point to the print continuously function, the CPU began executing the function like it was the next instruction. PC determines what code is going to be executed, and changing the code execution location to our thread function made the processor jump into that code first. This allowed our thread to begin running from the start of the print continuously function.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',2,1,'2025-06-23_17-07-31','When I set the program counter register to point to the print continuously function, the CPU immediately begins executing instructions from this address. Since PC determines what code is going to be next executed, changing it essentially redirects the flow of execution. This is almost equivalent to calling the function, because by placing its address in the PC we cause the CPU to jump into the function’s code and begin executing it. However, it is different from a traditional function call because no return address is saved. Jumping to a block of code transfers control without saving the return address, so there’s no way to return to the original location. Function calls are different because they push a return address onto the stack, which allows the CPU to resume where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,1,'2025-06-23_18-13-01','When I set the program counter register to point to the print continuously function, the CPU immediately begins executing instructions from this address. Since PC determines what code is going to be next executed, changing it essentially redirects the flow of execution. This is almost equivalent to calling the function, because by placing its address in the PC we cause the CPU to jump into the function’s code and begin executing it. However, it is different from a traditional function call because no return address is saved. Jumping to a block of code transfers control without saving the return address, so there’s no way to return to the original location. Function calls are different because they push a return address onto the stack, which allows the CPU to resume where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,1,'2025-06-25_17-28-53','When I set the program counter register to point to the print continuously function, the CPU immediately begins executing instructions from this address. Since PC determines what code is going to be next executed, changing it essentially redirects the flow of execution. This is almost equivalent to calling the function, because by placing its address in the PC register, we caused the CPU to jump into the function’s code and begin executing it. However, it is different from a traditional function call because no return address is saved. Jumping to a block of code transfers control without saving the return address, so there’s no way to return to the original location. Function calls are different because they push a return address onto the stack, which allows the CPU to resume where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,1,'2025-06-25_18-14-45','When I set the program counter register to point to the print continuously function, the CPU immediately begins executing instructions from this address. Since PC determines what code is going to be next executed, changing it essentially redirects the flow of execution. This is almost equivalent to calling the function, because by placing its address in the PC register, we caused the CPU to jump into the function’s code and begin executing it. However, it is different from a traditional function call because no return address is saved. Jumping to a block of code transfers control without saving the return address, so there’s no way to return to the original location. Function calls are different because they push a return address onto the stack, which allows the CPU to resume where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,1,'2025-06-25_23-27-27','When I set the program counter register to point to the print continuously function, the CPU immediately begins executing instructions from this address. Since PC determines what code is going to be next executed, changing it essentially redirects the flow of execution. This is almost equivalent to calling the function, because by placing its address in the PC register, we caused the CPU to jump into the function’s code and begin executing it. However, it is different from a traditional function call because no return address is saved. Jumping to a block of code transfers control without saving the return address, so there’s no way to return to the original location. Function calls are different because they push a return address onto the stack, which allows the CPU to resume where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,2,'2025-06-23_12-59-41','A system call is a way for user level code to request a service or resource from the operating system or kernel level, such as starting a new thread or accessing specific hardware. In our case, this is typically done using the SVC instruction, which switches the CPU to a privileged mode (kernel) to safely executive safety-sensitive operations.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',2,2,'2025-06-23_15-54-57','A system call is a specific type of function call that allows user level code to request services from the operating system or kernel. System calls act as a controlled entry point into privileged mode for the system to perform critical/sensitive operations. This allows the system to do things like create threads or access hardware on behalf of the user program. In our case, this is typically done using the SVC instruction, which switches the CPU to a privileged mode (kernel) to safely executive safety-sensitive operations.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,2,'2025-06-23_17-07-31','A system call is a specific type of function call that allows user level code to request services from the operating system or kernel. System calls act as a controlled entry point into privileged mode for the system to perform critical/sensitive operations. This allows the system to do things like create threads or access hardware on behalf of the user program. In our case, this is typically done using the SVC instruction, which switches the CPU to a privileged mode (kernel) to safely executive safety-sensitive operations.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',2,2,'2025-06-23_18-13-01','A system call is a specific type of function call that allows user level code to request services from the operating system or kernel. System calls act as a controlled entry point into privileged mode for the system to perform critical or sensitive operations, such as creating threads, on behalf of the user program. In our case, this transition is typically done using the SVC instruction, which switches the CPU to a privileged mode (kernel) to safely executive safety-sensitive operations.\nSystem calls are important because they protect the system from potentially unsafe user code directly accessing the hardware or kernel privileges. Without this protection, user code would have unrestricted hardware access, which would increase the risk of data corruption, crashes, and security breaches. Privileged mode ensures that only trusted kernel code can interact with the hardware, and the system calls acts as a gatekeeper to ensure that only trusted code can perform critical tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,2,'2025-06-25_17-28-53','A system call is a specific type of function call that allows user level code to request services from the operating system or kernel. System calls act as a controlled entry point into privileged mode for the system to perform critical or sensitive operations, such as creating threads, on behalf of the user program. In our case, this transition is typically done using the SVC instruction, which switches the CPU to a privileged mode (kernel) to safely executive safety-sensitive operations.\nSystem calls are important because they protect the system from potentially unsafe user code directly accessing the hardware or kernel privileges. Without this protection, user code would have unrestricted hardware access, which would increase the risk of data corruption, crashes, and security breaches. Privileged mode ensures that only trusted kernel code can interact with the hardware, and the system calls act as a gatekeeper to ensure that only trusted code can perform critical tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,2,'2025-06-25_18-14-45','A system call is a specific type of function call that allows user level code to request services from the operating system or kernel. System calls act as a controlled entry point into privileged mode for the system to perform critical or sensitive operations, such as creating threads, on behalf of the user program. In our case, this transition is typically done using the SVC instruction, which switches the CPU to a privileged mode (kernel) to safely executive safety-sensitive operations.\nSystem calls are important because they protect the system from potentially unsafe user code directly accessing the hardware or kernel privileges. Without this protection, user code would have unrestricted hardware access, which would increase the risk of data corruption, crashes, and security breaches. Privileged mode ensures that only trusted kernel code can interact with the hardware, and the system calls act as a gatekeeper to ensure that only trusted code can perform critical tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,2,'2025-06-25_23-27-27','A system call is a specific type of function call that allows user level code to request services from the operating system or kernel. System calls act as a controlled entry point into privileged mode for the system to perform critical or sensitive operations, such as creating threads, on behalf of the user program. In our case, this transition is typically done using the SVC instruction, which switches the CPU to a privileged mode (kernel) to safely executive safety-sensitive operations.\nSystem calls are important because they protect the system from potentially unsafe user code directly accessing the hardware or kernel privileges. Without this protection, user code would have unrestricted hardware access, which would increase the risk of data corruption, crashes, and security breaches. Privileged mode ensures that only trusted kernel code can interact with the hardware, and the system calls act as a gatekeeper to ensure that only trusted code can perform critical tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,3,'2025-06-23_12-59-41','To set up a thread’s stack, we must manually push values onto it that the CPU expects when starting execution, such as the xPSR, PC, and LR register. We also reserve additional space for the callee-saved registers. This is important because the stack provides the threads working memory and saved register states. Storing registers on the stack allows the thread to resume exactly where it left off after a context switch for something like an interrupt or trap.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',2,3,'2025-06-23_15-54-57','Stack setup begins by reserving dedicated memory space for the thread, often from a predefined stack pool of dynamically allocated memory. This region must not overlap with other stacks, and it typically grows downwards from a known top pointer. Then, we must manually push values onto it that the CPU expects when starting execution, such as the xPSR, PC, and LR register. We also reserve additional space for the callee-saved registers. This is important because the stack provides the threads working memory and saved register states. Storing registers on the stack allows the thread to resume exactly where it left off after a context switch for something like an interrupt or trap.\nThe order in which register values are stored is critical because the CPU expects a specific layout when restoring context during a context switch. If the registers aren’t in the expected order, the CPU may restore incorrect values, leading to hard faults or unexpected behaviour. We store these register values so that the CPU can correctly restore the thread’s state when beginning/resuming execution. This allows the thread to function independently, not interfering with other threads in the main program.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,3,'2025-06-23_17-07-31','Stack setup begins by reserving dedicated memory space for the thread, often from a predefined stack pool of dynamically allocated memory. This region must not overlap with other stacks, and it typically grows downwards from a known top pointer. Then, we must manually push values onto it that the CPU expects when starting execution, such as the xPSR, PC, and LR register. We also reserve additional space for the callee-saved registers. This is important because the stack provides the threads working memory and saved register states. Storing registers on the stack allows the thread to resume exactly where it left off after a context switch for something like an interrupt or trap.\nThe order in which register values are stored is critical because the CPU expects a specific layout when restoring context during a context switch. If the registers aren’t in the expected order, the CPU may restore incorrect values, leading to hard faults or unexpected behaviour. We store these register values so that the CPU can correctly restore the thread’s state when beginning/resuming execution. This allows the thread to function independently, not interfering with other threads in the main program.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,3,'2025-06-23_18-13-01','Stack setup begins by reserving dedicated memory space for the thread, often from a predefined stack pool of dynamically allocated memory. This region must not overlap with other stacks, and it typically grows downwards from a known top pointer. Then, we must manually push values onto it that the CPU expects when starting execution, such as the xPSR, PC, and LR register. We also reserve additional space for the callee-saved registers. This is important because the stack provides the threads working memory and saved register states. Storing registers on the stack allows the thread to resume exactly where it left off after a context switch for something like an interrupt or trap.\nThe order in which register values are stored is critical because the CPU expects a specific layout when restoring context during a context switch. If the registers aren’t in the expected order, the CPU may restore incorrect values, leading to hard faults or unexpected behaviour. We store these register values so that the CPU can correctly restore the thread’s state when beginning/resuming execution. This allows the thread to function independently, not interfering with other threads in the main program.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,3,'2025-06-25_17-28-53','Stack setup begins by reserving dedicated memory space for the thread, often from a predefined stack pool of dynamically allocated memory. This region must not overlap with other stacks, and it typically grows downwards from a known top pointer. Then, we must manually push values onto it that the CPU expects when starting execution, such as the xPSR, PC, and LR register. We also reserve additional space for the callee-saved registers. This is important because the stack provides the threads working memory and saved register states. Storing registers on the stack allows the thread to resume exactly where it left off after a context switch for something like an interrupt or trap.\nThe order in which register values are stored is critical because the CPU expects a specific layout when restoring context during a context switch. If the registers aren’t in the expected order, the CPU may restore incorrect values, leading to hard faults or unexpected behaviour. We store these register values so that the CPU can correctly restore the thread’s state when beginning/resuming execution. This allows the thread to function independently, not interfering with other threads in the main program.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,3,'2025-06-25_18-14-45','Stack setup begins by reserving dedicated memory space for the thread, often from a predefined stack pool of dynamically allocated memory. This region must not overlap with other stacks, and it typically grows downwards from a known top pointer. Then, we must manually push values onto it that the CPU expects when starting execution, such as the xPSR, PC, and LR register. We also reserve additional space for the callee-saved registers. This is important because the stack provides the threads working memory and saved register states. Storing registers on the stack allows the thread to resume exactly where it left off after a context switch for something like an interrupt or trap.\nThe order in which register values are stored is critical because the CPU expects a specific layout when restoring context during a context switch. If the registers aren’t in the expected order, the CPU may restore incorrect values, leading to hard faults or unexpected behaviour. We store these register values so that the CPU can correctly restore the thread’s state when beginning/resuming execution. This allows the thread to function independently, not interfering with other threads in the main program.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,3,'2025-06-25_23-27-27','Stack setup begins by reserving dedicated memory space for the thread, often from a predefined stack pool of dynamically allocated memory. This region must not overlap with other stacks, and it typically grows downwards from a known top pointer. Then, we must manually push values onto it that the CPU expects when starting execution, such as the xPSR, PC, and LR register. We also reserve additional space for the callee-saved registers. This is important because the stack provides the threads working memory and saved register states. Storing registers on the stack allows the thread to resume exactly where it left off after a context switch for something like an interrupt or trap.\nThe order in which register values are stored is critical because the CPU expects a specific layout when restoring context during a context switch. If the registers aren’t in the expected order, the CPU may restore incorrect values, leading to hard faults or unexpected behaviour. We store these register values so that the CPU can correctly restore the thread’s state when beginning/resuming execution. This allows the thread to function independently, not interfering with other threads in the main program.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,4,'2025-06-23_12-59-41','The process stack pointer (PSP) tells the CPU where to find the thread’s stack. If we don’t set it before the thread runs, the CPU may use an invalid stack address, leading to access violations or corruptions, triggering a hard fault. This results in a hard fault because the CPU is trying to execute something that is unsafe. Therefore, it is important to use the __set_PSP function to ensure that the CPU uses the correct stack area that has been prepared for the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',2,4,'2025-06-23_15-54-57','The process stack pointer (PSP) tells the CPU where to find the thread’s stack. The CPU starts with the MSP by default, which is used for exceptions/system level code by the Kernel. However, process stack pointers (PSPs) are used for individual tasks, which in our case are each thread’s separate stack. The PSP points to the thread’s private stack memory space, isolating the thread from system-level operations handled by the MSP.  If we don’t set the PSP before the thread runs, the CPU may use an invalid stack address, leading to access violations or corruptions, triggering a hard fault. This results in a hard fault because the CPU is trying to execute something that is unsafe. Therefore, it is important to use the __set_PSP function to ensure that the CPU uses the correct stack area that has been prepared for the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,4,'2025-06-23_17-07-31','The process stack pointer (PSP) tells the CPU where to find the thread’s stack. The CPU starts with the MSP by default, which is used for exceptions/system level code by the Kernel. However, process stack pointers (PSPs) are used for individual tasks, which in our case are each thread’s separate stack. The PSP points to the thread’s private stack memory space, isolating the thread from system-level operations handled by the MSP.  If we don’t set the PSP before the thread runs, the CPU may use an invalid stack address, leading to access violations or corruptions, triggering a hard fault. This results in a hard fault because the CPU is trying to execute something that is unsafe. Therefore, it is important to use the __set_PSP function to ensure that the CPU uses the correct stack area that has been prepared for the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,4,'2025-06-23_18-13-01','The process stack pointer (PSP) tells the CPU where to find the thread’s stack. The CPU starts with the MSP by default, which is used for exceptions/system level code by the Kernel. However, process stack pointers (PSPs) are used for individual tasks, which in our case are each thread’s separate stack. The PSP points to the thread’s private stack memory space, isolating the thread from system-level operations handled by the MSP.  If we don’t set the PSP before the thread runs, the CPU may use an invalid stack address, leading to access violations or corruptions, triggering a hard fault. This results in a hard fault because the CPU is trying to execute something that is unsafe. Therefore, it is important to use the __set_PSP function to ensure that the CPU uses the correct stack area that has been prepared for the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,4,'2025-06-25_17-28-53','The process stack pointer (PSP) tells the CPU where to find the thread’s stack. The CPU starts with the MSP by default, which is used for exceptions/system level code by the Kernel. However, process stack pointers (PSPs) are used for individual tasks, which in our case are each thread’s separate stack. The PSP points to the thread’s private stack memory space, isolating the thread from system-level operations handled by the MSP.  If we don’t set the PSP before the thread runs, the CPU may use an invalid stack address, leading to access violations or corruptions, triggering a hard fault. This results in a hard fault because the CPU is trying to execute something that is unsafe. Therefore, it is important to use the __set_PSP function to ensure that the CPU uses the correct stack area that has been prepared for the thread.\n',1,'s25'),