thread was never meant to return and wasn’t setup for this, the return address would likely be invalid or could point to some random memory. This would likely cause a hard fault, and this is why threads should have an explicit termination handler if the if it doesn’t loop continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,5,'2025-06-23_15-54-57','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously, and instead printing something and returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the thread’s stack to continue execution. Since the thread was never meant to return and wasn’t setup for this, the return address would likely be invalid or could point to some random memory. This would likely cause a hard fault, and this is why threads should have an explicit termination handler if the if it doesn’t loop continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,5,'2025-06-23_17-07-31','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously, and instead printing something and returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the thread’s stack to continue execution. Since the thread was never meant to return and wasn’t setup for this, the return address would likely be invalid or could point to some random memory. This would likely cause a hard fault, and this is why threads should have an explicit termination handler if the if it doesn’t loop continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,5,'2025-06-23_18-13-01','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously, and instead printing something and returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the thread’s stack to continue execution. Since the thread was never meant to return and wasn’t setup for this, the return address would likely be invalid or could point to some random memory. This would likely cause a hard fault, and this is why threads should have an explicit termination handler if the if it doesn’t loop continuously.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',2,5,'2025-06-25_17-28-53','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously, and instead printed something and returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the thread’s stack to continue execution. Since the thread was never meant to return and wasn’t setup for this, the return address would likely be invalid or could point to some random memory. This would likely cause a hard fault, and this is why threads should have an explicit termination handler if the if it doesn’t loop continuously.\nIn proper thread-based system, threads are not meant to loop indefinitely and should have some kind of exit strategy to deal with this, such as jumping to the thread termination handler. Without allowing a thread to return, undefined behaviour may occur, since the CPU expects a valid return context. This shows the importance of designing thread stacks and termination logic.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,5,'2025-06-25_18-14-45','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously, and instead printed something and returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the thread’s stack to continue execution. Since the thread was never meant to return and wasn’t setup for this, the return address would likely be invalid or could point to some random memory. This would likely cause a hard fault, and this is why threads should have an explicit termination handler if the if it doesn’t loop continuously.\nIn proper thread-based system, threads are not meant to loop indefinitely and should have some kind of exit strategy to deal with this, such as jumping to the thread termination handler. Without allowing a thread to return, undefined behaviour may occur, since the CPU expects a valid return context. This shows the importance of designing thread stacks and termination logic.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',2,5,'2025-06-25_23-27-27','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously, and instead printed something and returned, the CPU would try to pop a return address from the thread’s stack to continue execution. Since the thread was never meant to return and wasn’t setup for this, the return address would likely be invalid or could point to some random memory. This would likely cause a hard fault, and this is why threads should have an explicit termination handler if the if it doesn’t loop continuously.\nIn proper thread-based system, threads are not meant to loop indefinitely and should have some kind of exit strategy to deal with this, such as jumping to the thread termination handler. Without allowing a thread to return, undefined behaviour may occur, since the CPU expects a valid return context. This shows the importance of designing thread stacks and termination logic.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',3,1,'2025-06-26_19-40-53','All the c files are first individually compiled into an object file, which contains the machine code for that specific file. The linker then takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable. The linker is responsible for handling references between files, such as global variables that are defined in one file and used in another.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',3,1,'2025-06-26_21-45-13','When multiple c files are compiled into a single executable, the process involves three main stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, during preprocessing, the compiler handles directives such as #include and #define, expanding them directly into the source code. Next, during the compiling stage, each preprocessed c file is translated into an object file. These object files contain the machine code and unresolved references to functions or variables declared elsewhere. Lastly, the linker combines all object files into a single executable, resolving references between files such as global variables that are defined in one file and used in another. This modular process improves organization, helps isolate errors, and ensures the code can be compiled independently before being linked into a complete program.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',3,2,'2025-06-26_19-40-53','Using #include causes all of the contents of that file to be copied onto another file before compilation, bypassing the normal process where all c files get compiled into a single executable through linkers. This can lead to multiple definitions of the same global variables or functions, which is very inefficient and breaks modular compilation. Also, the code will be harder to debug and maintain if everything is put into one file through #include.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',3,2,'2025-06-26_21-45-13','Using #include on a c file is a bad idea because it copies all the contents of that file onto another file before compilation, bypassing the normal process of compilation and linking to create a single executable before compiling. This can lead to multiple definitions of the same global variables or functions, leading to linker errors and unpredictable behaviour. Using #include would not allow for modular compilation, making the code harder to manage, debug, and scale, especially in larger projects. The correct approach would be to compile each c file separately using header files to share declarations between them. This keeps the project modular, efficient, and easy to maintain/debug.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',3,3,'2025-06-26_19-40-53','Write some thread function in main.c\nCall osKernelInitialize() to initialize the kernel\nUse osCreateThread() and pass the thread function you wrote to create the thread\nCall osKernelStart() to begin execution of the created thread\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',3,3,'2025-06-26_21-45-13','Write some thread function in main.c\nCall osKernelInitialize() to prepare the kernel and initialize its variables\nUse osCreateThread() and pass the thread function you wrote to create the thread\nCall osKernelStart() to begin execution of the created thread\nThis process ensures the user’s created thread has the necessary stack to and context to run correctly within the kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',3,4,'2025-06-26_19-40-53','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory that the kernel uses to give each thread its own private stack. During thread creation, the kernel takes a chunk of memory from the stack pool and assigns it to that thread, ensuring that each thread has its own space for storage. In each thread’s stack pool space, things like the thread’s local variables, return addresses, and register values are stored.\nIn future labs, having a set block of memory to store each thread’s stacks will allow multiple threads to run independently without interfering with each other’s memory. By carefully tracking where the next free space in the stack pool is, the kernel can support multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',3,4,'2025-06-26_21-45-13','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory that the kernel uses to give each thread its own private stack. During thread creation, the kernel takes a chunk of memory from the stack pool and assigns it to that thread, ensuring that each thread has its own space for storage. In each thread’s stack pool space, things like the thread’s local variables, return addresses, and register values are stored.\nIn future labs, having a set block of memory to store each thread’s stacks will allow multiple threads to run independently without interfering with each other’s memory. By carefully tracking where the next free space in the stack pool is, the kernel can support multiple threads.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',3,5,'2025-06-26_19-40-53','The kernel is the safety-sensitive core of the operating system, handling low-level tasks like stack allocation, thread setup, and memory management. Kernel.c and kernel.h manage how the system works behind the scenes.\nThe user file is written by the person creating the application code, this is where thread functions and the main program goes. This code goes in main.c, where functions from the kernel are called to help with thread creation, such as osCreateThread, osKernelInitialize, and osKernelStart.\nThe distinction is importance because it keeps the system code separate from user code, which is easier and safer to maintain. It helps prevent users from accidentally changing critical OS behaviour, and it allows people to write threads without needing to deal with the complex internal portions of an OS.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',3,5,'2025-06-26_21-45-13','The kernel is the safety-sensitive core of the operating system, handling low-level tasks like stack allocation, thread setup, and memory management. Kernel.c and kernel.h manage how the system works behind the scenes.\nThe user file is written by the person creating the application code, this is where thread functions and the main program goes. This code goes in main.c, where functions from the kernel are called to help with thread creation, such as osCreateThread, osKernelInitialize, and osKernelStart.\nThe distinction is important because it keeps the system code separate from user code, which is easier and safer to maintain. It helps prevent users from accidentally changing critical OS behaviour, and it allows people to write threads without needing to deal with the complex internal portions of an OS.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',4,1,'2025-07-26_18-03-58','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a queue instead of just an array by enqueuing each thread’s control block after it finishes executing. When osYield is called, the scheduler function will dequeue the next TCB and runs that thread, then it enqueues the previously running thread to the back of the queue. This avoids having to manually track an index in an array. The main benefit of using a queue for round-robin scheduling is that queues naturally support dynamic insertion/removal of threads which simplifies the scheduling logic if threads are terminated or created dynamically. Furthermore, if threads are created or terminated at runtime, adding or removing their TCB’s from the queue is straightforward, eliminating the need to shift array element or manage unused array slots manually.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',4,1,'2025-07-26_23-31-55','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented using a queue instead of just an array by enqueuing each thread’s control block after it finishes executing. When osYield is called, the scheduler function will dequeue the next TCB and runs that thread, then it enqueues the previously running thread to the back of the queue. This avoids having to manually track an index in an array. The main benefit of using a queue for round-robin scheduling is that queues naturally support dynamic insertion/removal of threads which simplifies the scheduling logic if threads are terminated or created dynamically. Furthermore, if threads are created or terminated at runtime, adding or removing their TCB’s from the queue is straightforward, eliminating the need to shift array element or manage unused array slots manually.\nThe round-robin array-based scheduler already acts like a queue because it cycles through the thread array using an index. This gives the same first in/first out behaviour as a queue, but the array-based implementation requires specific index management. Using a queue simplifies this by handling enqueue/dequeue automatically, which is useful if threads are create or remove dynamically. However, queues can add slight overhead compared to arrays due to dynamic memory management. Arrays are faster for fixed sized threads because indexing is constant time.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',4,2,'2025-07-26_18-03-58','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the thread’s state (registers/stack pointer) and restoring the state of the next thread to run. It first stores the current thread’s stack pointer in its TCB, updates the current thread index to the next thread, and then sets the processor stack pointer (PSP) to the new thread’s stack pointer. When returning from the interrupt, the processor resumes execution from the new thread’s context it has just switched to.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',4,2,'2025-07-26_23-31-55','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the thread’s state (registers/stack pointer) and restoring the state of the next thread to run. It first stores the current thread’s stack pointer in its TCB, updates the current thread index to the next thread, and then sets the processor stack pointer (PSP) to the new thread’s stack pointer. When returning from the interrupt, the processor resumes execution from the new thread’s context it has just switched to.\nReading the process stack pointer (PSP) is critical because the PSP specially holds the current thread’s stack and pointer. It ensures the correct thread stack is saved and restored during a context switch. Registers R4-R11 are pushed to the stack in assembly before switching threads. The osSched() function is called within the PendSV_Handler to select the next thread to run by updating the currentThread index and PSP. After the PSP is updated to the new thread’s saved stack pointer, returning from the interrupt causes the processor to automatically pop the saved registers, restoring the thread’s full state and resuming execution\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',4,3,'2025-07-26_18-03-58','This is a bad idea because always selecting the highest priority task can cause lower priority tasks to never run. If a high priority task is continuously selected and scheduled again, lower priority tasks will be avoided. This lacks fairness provided by the round-robin scheduling system and can lead to unpredictable system behaviour, especially if all tasks must be periodically executed.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',4,3,'2025-07-26_23-31-55','This is a bad idea because always selecting the highest priority task can cause lower priority tasks to never run. If a high priority task is continuously selected and scheduled again, lower priority tasks will be avoided. This lacks fairness provided by the round-robin scheduling system and can lead to unpredictable system behaviour, especially if all tasks must be periodically executed.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',4,4,'2025-07-26_18-03-58','Calling the C functions osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code easier for us to read, debug, and maintain because C is higher-level and easier to understand. Writing everything in assembly could improve the performance and have finer control over the hardware; however, assembly is error prone, harder to debug, and less flexible to modify. Using C allows us to focus on the logic of our system, while leaving lower-level behaviour to be handled by the assembly code.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',4,4,'2025-07-26_23-31-55','Calling the C functions osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code easier for us to read, debug, and maintain because C is higher-level and easier to understand. Writing everything in assembly could improve the performance and have finer control over the hardware; however, assembly is error prone, harder to debug, and less flexible to modify. Using C allows us to focus on the logic of our system, while leaving lower-level behaviour to be handled by the assembly code.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',4,5,'2025-07-26_18-03-58','If only a single thread is created, the OS will still work but there wouldn’t be multithreading. The thread will yield and therefore trigger the scheduler, but there will be no other threads to be scheduled. Therefore, the scheduler will choose the same thread again. This would defeat the purpose of the OS because there would be no context switches between different tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',4,5,'2025-07-26_23-31-55','If only a single thread is created, the OS will still work but there wouldn’t be multithreading. The thread will yield and therefore trigger the scheduler, but there will be no other threads to be scheduled. Therefore, the scheduler will choose the same thread again. This would defeat the purpose of the OS because there would be no context switches between different tasks.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,1,'2025-07-26_23-45-59','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first writes their thread function and allocates the required data, such as a simple variable or a struct containing multiple variables. When creating the thread, the user also provides a point to this data as the argument. The operating system then sets up the thread’s stack so that register R0 contains the pointer. When the thread begins executing, it receives the argument through R0 and processes the user’s data. This works because using a void* pointer provides flexibility, allowing the user to pass any data type or struct into the thread.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',5,1,'2025-07-27_14-12-56','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first writes their thread function and allocates the required data, such as a simple variable or a struct containing multiple variables. When creating the thread, the user also provides a pointer to this data as the argument. The operating system then sets up the thread’s stack so that register R0 contains the pointer. When the thread begins executing, it receives the argument through R0 and processes the user’s data. This works because the ABI specifies that the first function argument is placed in R0, and using void* allows any type of data to be passed and cast inside the thread. The ABI defines how arguments are passed at runtime. By putting the pointer in R0, the OS follows the calling convention, so the thread receives it as its argument.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,1,'2025-07-27_14-28-59','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first writes their thread function and allocates the required data, such as a simple variable or a struct containing multiple variables. When creating the thread, the user also provides a pointer to this data as the argument. The operating system then sets up the thread’s stack so that register R0 contains the pointer. When the thread begins executing, it receives the argument through R0 and processes the user’s data. This works because the ABI specifies that the first function argument is placed in R0, and using void* allows any type of data to be passed and cast inside the thread. The ABI defines how arguments are passed at runtime. By putting the pointer in R0, the OS follows the calling convention, so the thread receives it as its argument.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,1,'2025-07-28_18-21-10','To pass an argument into a thread, the user first writes their thread function and allocates the required data, such as a simple variable or a struct containing multiple variables. When creating the thread, the user also provides a pointer to this data as the argument. The operating system then sets up the thread’s stack so that register R0 contains the pointer. When the thread begins executing, it receives the argument through R0 and processes the user’s data. This works because the ABI specifies that the first function argument is placed in R0, and using void* allows any type of data to be passed and cast inside the thread. The ABI defines how arguments are passed at runtime. By putting the pointer in R0, the OS follows the calling convention, so the thread receives it as its argument.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,2,'2025-07-26_23-45-59','Calling osYield inside any of the context-switching functions is not allowed because these functions already handle context switching. Using osYield during a context switch would result in nested context switching, which could corrupt the scheduler state or stack. This could cause the scheduler to re-enter while it is already switching threads, leading to unpredictable behaviour.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',5,2,'2025-07-27_14-12-56','Calling osYield inside any of the context-switching functions is not allowed because these functions already handle context switching. Using osYield during a context switch would result in nested context switching, which could corrupt the scheduler state or stack. This could cause the scheduler to re-enter while it is already switching threads, leading to unpredictable behaviour.\nosYield works by voluntarily triggering the scheduler to perform a context switch. The OS distinguishes normal context switches from those in interrupts because interrupts already run with a special exception stack frame. Calling osYield inside an interrupt will try to start another context switch before the first one finishes, leading to nested exceptions and stack corruption.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',5,2,'2025-07-27_14-28-59','Calling osYield inside any of the context-switching functions (PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, etc.) is not allowed because these already handle the context switch. osYield works by triggering the SVC exception, which requests the scheduler to perform a voluntary context switch. During normal operation, this mechanism is safe because the OS transitions into handler mode to save the current thread’s state and select the next thread.\nHowever, calling osYield while already in an interrupt or context switch will cause nested exceptions. Since interrupts already execute in a special exception stack frame, attempting another context switch at this point can corrupt the stack and scheduler state. This re-entrance problem can lead to the scheduler being used while it is already switching threads, leading to unpredictable behaviour. The corrupted stack or invalid return addressed could potentially cause a hard fault, since the processor attempts to return to a corrupted context.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,2,'2025-07-28_18-21-10','Calling osYield inside any of the context-switching functions (PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, etc.) is not allowed because these already handle the context switch. osYield works by triggering the SVC exception, which requests the scheduler to perform a voluntary context switch. During normal operation, this mechanism is safe because the OS transitions into handler mode to save the current thread’s state and select the next thread.\nHowever, calling osYield while already in an interrupt or context switch will cause nested exceptions. Since interrupts already execute in a special exception stack frame, attempting another context switch at this point can corrupt the stack and scheduler state. This re-entrance problem can lead to the scheduler being used while it is already switching threads, leading to unpredictable behaviour. The corrupted stack or invalid return addressed could potentially cause a hard fault, since the processor attempts to return to a corrupted context.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,3,'2025-07-26_23-45-59','If the fundamental OS clock were changed from 1 ms to 0.1 ms, the system would gain higher timing resolution and could respond to events more quickly. This could be beneficial for real-time applications requiring fast reaction times. However, this change would also increase CPU overhead because the SysTick_Handler interrupt would trigger ten times more frequently, leaving less time for threads to execute between ticks, working the CPU harder. As a result, the performance may decrease, and power consumption may increase due to the higher clock rate.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,3,'2025-07-27_14-12-56','If the fundamental OS clock were changed from 1 ms to 0.1 ms, the system would gain higher timing resolution and could respond to events more quickly. This could be beneficial for real-time applications requiring fast reaction times. However, this change would also increase CPU overhead because the SysTick_Handler interrupt would trigger ten times more frequently, leaving less time for threads to execute between ticks, working the CPU harder. As a result, the performance may decrease, and power consumption may increase due to the higher clock rate.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,3,'2025-07-27_14-28-59','If the fundamental OS clock were changed from 1 ms to 0.1 ms, the system would gain higher timing resolution and could respond to events more quickly. This could be beneficial for real-time applications requiring fast reaction times. However, this change would also increase CPU overhead because the SysTick_Handler interrupt would trigger ten times more frequently, leaving less time for threads to execute between ticks, working the CPU harder. As a result, the performance may decrease, and power consumption may increase due to the higher clock rate.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',5,3,'2025-07-28_18-21-10','If the fundamental OS clock were changed from 1 ms to 0.1 ms, the system would gain higher timing resolution and could respond to events more quickly. This could be beneficial for real-time applications requiring fast reaction times. However, this change would also increase CPU overhead because the SysTick_Handler interrupt would trigger ten times more frequently, leaving less time for threads to execute between ticks, working the CPU harder. As a result, the performance may decrease, and power consumption may increase due to the higher clock rate.\nA finer-grained OS clock would improve task scheduling accuracy, and it would allow for deadlines or timeouts to be enforced with higher precision. This would benefit systems that require precise synchronization or periodic tasks with short intervals, such as motor control or sensor sampling in real time systems. More frequent clock ticks could also lead to more responsive handling of interrupts and time sensitive events, reducing the latency in critical applications.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,4,'2025-07-26_23-45-59','Co-operative multitasking requires threads to explicitly call osYield to allow other threads to run. If a thread never yields, it can block all the other threads. Pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts threads after their allotted time slice using a timer interrupt such as SysTick_Handler. From the user’s perspective, pre-emptive multitasking does not require explicit yielding, while co-operative multitasking does. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking performs a context switch only when a thread yields, whereas pre-emptive multitasking forces context switches based on the timer.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,4,'2025-07-27_14-12-56','Co-operative multitasking requires threads to explicitly call osYield to allow other threads to run. If a thread never yields, it can block all the other threads. Pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts threads after their allotted time slice using a timer interrupt such as SysTick_Handler. From the user’s perspective, pre-emptive multitasking does not require explicit yielding, while co-operative multitasking does. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking performs a context switch only when a thread yields, whereas pre-emptive multitasking forces context switches based on the timer.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,4,'2025-07-27_14-28-59','Co-operative multitasking requires threads to explicitly call osYield to allow other threads to run. If a thread never yields, it can block all the other threads. Pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts threads after their allotted time slice using a timer interrupt such as SysTick_Handler. From the user’s perspective, pre-emptive multitasking does not require explicit yielding, while co-operative multitasking does. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking performs a context switch only when a thread yields, whereas pre-emptive multitasking forces context switches based on the timer.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,4,'2025-07-28_18-21-10','Co-operative multitasking requires threads to explicitly call osYield to allow other threads to run. If a thread never yields, it can block all the other threads. Pre-emptive multitasking automatically interrupts threads after their allotted time slice using a timer interrupt such as SysTick_Handler. From the user’s perspective, pre-emptive multitasking does not require explicit yielding, while co-operative multitasking does. From the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking performs a context switch only when a thread yields, whereas pre-emptive multitasking forces context switches based on the timer.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,5,'2025-07-26_23-45-59','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching can work together because both mechanisms update the thread’s state and rely on the same context-switching infrastructure. If a thread voluntarily calls osYield, the scheduler immediately switches to another thread and resets the runtime of the yielding thread. If the thread does not yield, the operating system will automatically pre-empt it once its time slice expires, ensuring fair CPU usage. This combination allows both voluntary and forced context switching to work together.\n',0,'s25'),('trutherf',5,5,'2025-07-27_14-12-56','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching can work together because both mechanisms update the thread’s state and rely on the same context-switching method handled by PendSV handler. In cooperative context switching, a thread calls osYield, which voluntarily sets the PendSV interrupt to trigger a context switch. In pre-emptive context switching, the SysTick_Handler interrupt decrements the thread’s run time, and once the time slice expires, the PendSV switches threads. Because both approaches use the same PendSV based context switch, allowing the OS to support both mechanisms with the same code.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,5,'2025-07-27_14-28-59','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching can work together because both mechanisms update the thread’s state and rely on the same context-switching method handled by PendSV handler. In cooperative context switching, a thread calls osYield, which voluntarily sets the PendSV interrupt to trigger a context switch. In pre-emptive context switching, the SysTick_Handler interrupt decrements the thread’s run time, and once the time slice expires, the PendSV switches threads. Because both approaches use the same PendSV based context switch, allowing the OS to support both mechanisms with the same code.\n',1,'s25'),('trutherf',5,5,'2025-07-28_18-21-10','Yielding and pre-emptive context switching can work together because both mechanisms update the thread’s state and rely on the same context-switching method handled by PendSV handler. In cooperative context switching, a thread calls osYield, which voluntarily sets the PendSV interrupt to trigger a context switch. In pre-emptive context switching, the SysTick_Handler interrupt decrements the thread’s run time, and once the time slice expires, the PendSV switches threads. Because both approaches use the same PendSV based context switch, allowing the OS to support both mechanisms with the same code.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',1,1,'2025-06-18_11-22-33','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is software that is prewritten for microcontrollers to make interaction with the microcontroller’s hardware simpler. It is useful for general embedded projects as you would not have to write the full, time-consuming code that is required to directly interact with the microcontroller’s hardware, instead you can use the HAL and its functions to interact with the hardware much easier. An example would\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',1,1,'2025-06-18_11-39-11','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is software that is prewritten for microcontrollers to make interaction with the microcontroller’s hardware simpler. It is useful for general embedded projects as you would not have to write the full, time-consuming code that is required to directly interact with the microcontroller’s hardware, instead you can use the HAL and its functions to interact with the hardware much easier. An example would\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',1,2,'2025-06-18_11-22-33','The __io_putchar function uses the UART transmission function to take in a char and sends it to the UART. This function is needed because the library which contains the commonly used function printf(), uses helper functions to interact with hardware. __io_putchar is one of those functions used by printf(), which is why it is needed, so printf() can send to UART.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',1,2,'2025-06-18_11-39-11','The __io_putchar function uses the UART transmission function to take in a char and sends it to the UART. This function is needed because the library which contains the commonly used function printf(), uses helper functions to interact with hardware. __io_putchar is one of those functions used by printf(), which is why it is needed, so printf() can send to UART.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',1,3,'2025-06-18_11-22-33','We used the debugger to add a breakpoint in the main function and view the value of MSP, SP, and PSP at the breakpoint we added. We used the debugger instead of printf as it allows us to pause at any line, and view the values of memory/registers as they change throughout the program. If we wanted to view the value of MSP, for example, throughout the program, we would have to use CMSIS functions throughout main, which can be tedious and difficult to get exact values. With the debugger, we can add breakpoints anywhere throughout our program and view the exact values of registers.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',1,3,'2025-06-18_11-39-11','We used the debugger to add a breakpoint in the main function and view the value of MSP, SP, and PSP at the breakpoint we added. We used the debugger instead of printf as it allows us to pause at any line, and view the values of memory/registers as they change throughout the program. If we wanted to view the value of MSP, for example, throughout the program, we would have to use CMSIS functions throughout main, which can be tedious and difficult to get exact values. With the debugger, we can add breakpoints anywhere throughout our program and view the exact values of registers.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',1,4,'2025-06-18_11-22-33','Once we have the location of MSP, we know how much memory we want to allocate for MSP (0x400), and we know that stacks grow downward (from lecture). To find the location for PSP, we just have to subtract the size we want MSP to be from its initial value, to know where PSP should be (all in hex). We needed to know the initial value of MSP for this to work because stacks grow downward, and we don’t want to overlap the PSP and MSP by not giving MSP appropriate size.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',1,4,'2025-06-18_11-39-11','Once we have the location of MSP, we know how much memory we want to allocate for MSP (0x400), and we know that stacks grow downward (from lecture). To find the location for PSP, we just have to subtract the size we want MSP to be from its initial value, to know where PSP should be (all in hex). We needed to know the initial value of MSP for this to work because stacks grow downward, and we don’t want to overlap the PSP and MSP by not giving MSP appropriate size.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',1,5,'2025-06-18_11-22-33','Depending on where we	 would want to the new stacks to go, we could subtract their sizes from known addresses of our other stack pointers such as MSP and PSP. For example, if we wanted to allocate these stacks pushed onto PSP, we would subtract the size of PSP from its initial value, and the next new stack would start there in memory. For the subsequent stacks, we would apply the same methodology but with their respective sizes instead.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',1,5,'2025-06-18_11-39-11','Depending on where we would want to the new stacks to go, we could subtract their sizes from known addresses of our other stack pointers such as MSP and PSP. For example, if we wanted to allocate these stacks pushed onto PSP, we would subtract the size of PSP from its initial value, and the next new stack would start there in memory, to ensure there isn’t any overlap. We can subtract the value of this new stack to get the new initial location for any additional stack we may add, to ensure there isn’t any overlap with the stack pushed onto PSP. We can also check the initial values and sizes of other regions in memory, to ensure the new stacks are not overlapping there, either. If the subtracted value does not align properly within the memory region, we can round down to the nearest aligned value in memory. It is also important to ensure that the stack pointers are valid memory addresses (no overflow). We can do so by checking bounds of our RAM.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',2,1,'2025-06-24_11-56-49','The PC register is the processor that determines which code is ran next. When I changed PC to point to print_continuously, PC stores the location of that function and it begins to run that code. This works because our print_continuously argument was previously in the register R0, but using the assembly code, we moved the values of R0 into PC, which included the function call of print_continuously, causing it to run by PC.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',2,1,'2025-06-24_13-17-49','The PC register is the processor that determines which code is ran next. When I changed PC to point to print_continuously, PC stores the location of that function and it begins to run that code. This works because our print_continuously argument was previously in the register R0, but using the assembly code, we moved the values of R0 into PC, which included the function call of print_continuously, causing it to run by PC.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',2,2,'2025-06-24_11-56-49','System calls are ways for programs/processes to request an action or permission from the operating system. In this lab, we used SVC (Supervisor Call), which requests actions from the OS in a privileged mode. For example, a process/thread may be running in user mode, but may need to use a System Call to request for the OS to fulfill a request that requires privilege.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',2,2,'2025-06-24_13-17-49','System calls are ways for programs/processes to request an action or permission from the operating system. System calls are a special type of function call, which is directly from user code to the OS. In this lab, we used SVC (Supervisor Call), which requests actions from the Kernel in a privileged mode, which enables user-code to access privilege in a safe manner. By forcing user-code to go through the Kernel, it protects hardware and software by validating user-code before allowing instruction. For example, a process/thread may be running in user mode, but may need to use a System Call to request for the OS to fulfill a request that requires privilege.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',2,3,'2025-06-24_11-56-49','The threads stack was set up by initializing/starting it at PSP (process stack pointer), which we established in lab 1 by finding the initial value of MSP and subtracting by MSP’s size, to find an appropriate starting value of PSP that would not overlap with any other addresses. “Setting up” the thread’s stack means tricking the chip into believing the thread was already running like it had just returned from an interrupt. We push the thread’s register values onto the stack as though the threads functions were pushed onto the stack by function call. We need these register values as they will allow the thread to run properly, and they are pushed into a very specific order, as the stack will be popped off in that order, ensuring everything works smoothly.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',2,3,'2025-06-24_13-17-49','The threads stack was set up by initializing/starting it at PSP (process stack pointer), which we established in lab 1 by finding the initial value of MSP and subtracting by MSP’s size, to find an appropriate starting value of PSP that would not overlap with any other addresses. “Setting up” the thread’s stack means tricking the chip into believing the thread was already running like it had just returned from an interrupt. We push the thread’s register values onto the stack as though the threads functions were pushed onto the stack by function call. We need these register values as they will allow the thread to run properly, and they are pushed into a very specific order, as the stack will be popped off in that order, ensuring everything works smoothly.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',2,4,'2025-06-24_11-56-49','We manually created the thread’s stack in main using stackptr. In SVC_Handler_Main, we use __set_PSP with stackptr, which tells the CPU that the pointer for stackptr is where our PSP starts. If we didn’t do this, the CPU may read registers improperly, such as PC and xPSR, so our system call would not work properly. Additionally, when come time for the stack to pop registers off, it would pop registers in a different order as our thread’s stack, which would result in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',2,4,'2025-06-24_13-17-49','We manually created the thread’s stack in main using stackptr. In SVC_Handler_Main, we use __set_PSP with stackptr, which tells the CPU that the pointer for stackptr is where our PSP starts. If we didn’t do this, the CPU may read registers improperly, such as PC and xPSR, so our system call would not work properly. Additionally, when come time for the stack to pop registers off, it would pop registers in a different order as our thread’s stack, which would result in a hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',2,5,'2025-06-24_11-56-49','If we returned, the stack would pop all of the thread’s registers off. Since we manually set up the thread’s stack and context, it will likely pop the thread’s return address off the stack. This could lead to an error which is ignored by the CPU and returns to normal operation, or this results in memory error and results in a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',2,5,'2025-06-24_13-17-49','If we returned, the stack would pop all of the thread’s registers off. Since we manually set up the thread’s stack and context, it will pop the thread’s return address off the stack. Since we manually built up the thread’s stack, we never inputted a valid return address to be popped off the stack once the thread is complete. This results in a hard fault / hard page fault, as the memory won’t be found in the RAM or the disc, which will crash the code.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',3,1,'2025-06-27_12-57-53','The process starts with the compiler preprocessing pre-compiler directives before main compilation (things such as #include, #define, etc.). At compile-time, the compiler also checks for correctness in code, such as errors and warnings. All the .c files are compiled into object files (.o), which are like incomplete executables. The unresolved links of these different object files are resolved by the linker, which makes the separate files into a single executable.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',3,1,'2025-06-27_13-59-12','The process starts with the compiler preprocessing pre-compiler directives before main compilation (things such as #include, #define, etc.). At compile-time, the compiler also checks for correctness in code, such as errors and warnings. All the .c files are compiled into object files (.o), which are like incomplete executables. The unresolved links of these different object files are resolved by the linker, which makes the separate files into a single executable.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',3,2,'2025-06-27_12-57-53','You should not include other .c files into a .c file because it will create duplicate functions that may clash with each other, which can cause errors when trying to link object files together. For example,  if you #include <kernel.c> into a file such as main.c, during compilation, main.c will compile all of its functions but also all of kernel.c’s functions, so when the compiler goes to compile the kernel.c file, it will duplicate all of kernel.c’s functions, which can lead to linking error. The correct way to include kernel.c’s functions into a file such as main.c is to use header files, such as kernel.h, which tell the main file the function names and arguments, without implementing them during compilation.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',3,2,'2025-06-27_13-59-12','You should not include other .c files into a .c file because it will create duplicate functions that may clash with each other, which can cause errors when trying to link object files together. For example,  if you #include <kernel.c> into a file such as main.c, during compilation, main.c will compile all of its functions but also all of kernel.c’s functions, so when the compiler goes to compile the kernel.c file, it will duplicate all of kernel.c’s functions, which can lead to linking error. The correct way to include kernel.c’s functions into a file such as main.c is to use header files, such as kernel.h, which tell the main file the function names and arguments, without implementing them during compilation.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',3,3,'2025-06-27_12-57-53','To set up a thread, first you need to write the thread’s function. This function will be what you want the thread to do / the code you want it to run. Next you must initialize the kernel to run this thread, by initializing any global variables such as stack information and thread structures. Next you must create the thread, which means allocates memory on the stack and sets up the stack for the thread. In our case, we are tricking the OS into believing it was already running this thread by setting up the stack. During creation, it is important to save the pointer to the thread’s stack. Finally, the last step is to run the thread, by making a system call to the kernel so the kernel can run our user functions with the necessary privileges. We use SVC to make a system call, which requires the cases to be defined within our kernel file.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',3,3,'2025-06-27_13-59-12','To set up a thread, first you need to write the thread’s function. This function will be what you want the thread to do / the code you want it to run. You must pass the address of this function to the kernel, so it knows where in memory to start execution of the address. Additionally, it allows the kernel to easily switch between user code and kernel code. Next you must initialize the kernel to run this thread, by initializing any global variables such as stack information and thread structures. Next you must create the thread, which means allocates memory on the stack and sets up the stack for the thread. In our case, we are tricking the OS into believing it was already running this thread by setting up the stack. During creation, it is important to save the pointer to the thread’s stack. Finally, the last step is to run the thread, by making a system call to the kernel so the kernel can run our user functions with the necessary privileges. The kernel must be started in order for threads to run, as the kernel only has permission to access/operate on privileged data and resources. We use SVC to make a system call, which requires the cases to be defined within our kernel file. System calls explicitly start the kernel with a function such as osKernelStart. System calls are a special type of function call, which is directly from user code to the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',3,4,'2025-06-27_12-57-53','The stack pool is a set-up / management system of memory that allows us to allocate blocks of memory to our threads, avoiding any memory overlap between thread stacks. The stack pool allocates memories to new threads, but also keeps track of memory that has been allocated / deallocated to ensure efficient memory management and avoid any overlap. It can be used in a future lab to enable multiple threads to exist in our OS as the stack pool ensures none of these simultaneous threads overlap in memory. It also ensures that we efficiently manage the thread stacks to avoid running out of memory.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',3,4,'2025-06-27_13-59-12','The stack pool is a set-up / management system of memory that allows us to allocate blocks of memory to our threads, avoiding any memory overlap between thread stacks. The stack pool is a physical block in memory reserved for stacks. The stack pool is subdivided into different sections, for separate threads in memory, to ensure each thread has its own dedicated memory that does not overlap with any other threads. The stack pool allocates memories to new threads, but also keeps track of memory that has been allocated / deallocated to ensure efficient memory management and avoid any overlap. It can be used in a future lab to enable multiple threads to exist in our OS as the stack pool ensures none of these simultaneous threads overlap in memory. It also ensures that we efficiently manage the thread stacks to avoid running out of memory. This will prevent memory corruption and safe multiprocessing of threads with dedicated memory sections.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',3,5,'2025-06-27_12-57-53','The “user” is code that is written by the user at an application level. The “kernel” is code that directly interacts with hardware and memory, which is considered privileged as direct access to hardware can cause issues if the code is unsafe. This distinction is important as separating these two separate types of code allow for better organization, security and stability. Separating the two codes allow for a clean workflow, and any updates or debugging to the code will be easier to implement when they are separated. Additionally, this separation allows for better security, as dedicated kernel code can ensure no harm is done to the hardware by mistake.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',3,5,'2025-06-27_13-59-12','The “user” is code that is written by the user at an application level. The “kernel” is code that directly interacts with hardware and memory, which is considered privileged as direct access to hardware can cause issues if the code is unsafe. The user writes the details of threads (thread functions) and makes Application Programming Interface (API) calls. The Kernel’s explicit roles include execution, controlling privileged instruction, context switching, stack setup, and enforcing security rules. This distinction is important as separating these two separate types of code allow for better organization, security and stability. Separating the two codes allow for a clean workflow, and any updates or debugging to the code will be easier to implement when they are separated. Additionally, this separation allows for better security, as dedicated kernel code can ensure no harm is done to the hardware by mistake. Finally, from the user perspective, this separation allows for users to not worry about low-level hardware code and focus on thread function. They do not require complex code to access and operate with hardware, instead using simple functions calls and code.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,1,'2025-07-08_08-09-21','Round-robin scheduling can work to store our thread control blocks (TCBs) by enqueuing each thread into a queue data structure. The round-robin works by dequeue the thread at the front of the queue, allowing it to run, and once it calls osYield, the same thread is enqueued again (now at the back), and the new thread in queue is dequeued. The benefits of using a round-robin scheduling is that it avoids any gaps in an array, such as empty indexes or null index. It also simplifies logic by removing the need to track the current thread and any arithmetic to go to the next thread. Finally, it allows for cleaner expansion, as an array must be created with a fixed size.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,1,'2025-07-08_08-23-57','Round-robin scheduling can work to store our thread control blocks (TCBs) by enqueuing each thread into a queue data structure. The round-robin works by dequeue the thread at the front of the queue, allowing it to run, and once it calls osYield, the same thread is enqueued again (now at the back), and the new thread in queue is dequeued. The benefits of using a round-robin scheduling is that it avoids any gaps in an array, such as empty indexes or null index. It also simplifies logic by removing the need to track the current thread and any arithmetic to go to the next thread. Finally, it allows for cleaner expansion, as an array must be created with a fixed size. Comparing this to an array, the round-robin scheduling approach (queue based) simplifies the logic for scheduling, as we do not need to track any indexes, increment, or apply any modulo arithmetic. The queue could add additional overhead cause it likely uses a data structure such as linked list or doubly linked list, which requires pointers. This overhead is increased if we make the queue dynamic, compared the array which is simple with fixed size. However; compared to the array, the round-robin approach has better maintainability due to its ability to be dynamic.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,1,'2025-07-08_11-16-37','Round-robin scheduling can work to store our thread control blocks (TCBs) by enqueuing each thread into a queue data structure. The round-robin works by dequeue the thread at the front of the queue, allowing it to run, and once it calls osYield, the same thread is enqueued again (now at the back), and the new thread in queue is dequeued. The benefits of using a round-robin scheduling is that it avoids any gaps in an array, such as empty indexes or null index. It also simplifies logic by removing the need to track the current thread and any arithmetic to go to the next thread. Finally, it allows for cleaner expansion, as an array must be created with a fixed size. Comparing this to an array, the round-robin scheduling approach (queue based) simplifies the logic for scheduling, as we do not need to track any indexes, increment, or apply any modulo arithmetic. The queue could add additional overhead cause it likely uses a data structure such as linked list or doubly linked list, which requires pointers. This overhead is increased if we make the queue dynamic, compared the array which is simple with fixed size. However; compared to the array, the round-robin approach has better maintainability due to its ability to be dynamic.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,1,'2025-07-08_11-30-01','Round-robin scheduling can work to store our thread control blocks (TCBs) by enqueuing each thread into a queue data structure. The round-robin works by dequeue the thread at the front of the queue, allowing it to run, and once it calls osYield, the same thread is enqueued again (now at the back), and the new thread in queue is dequeued. The benefits of using a round-robin scheduling is that it avoids any gaps in an array, such as empty indexes or null index. It also simplifies logic by removing the need to track the current thread and any arithmetic to go to the next thread. Finally, it allows for cleaner expansion, as an array must be created with a fixed size. Comparing this to an array, the round-robin scheduling approach (queue based) simplifies the logic for scheduling, as we do not need to track any indexes, increment, or apply any modulo arithmetic. The queue could add additional overhead cause it likely uses a data structure such as linked list or doubly linked list, which requires pointers. This overhead is increased if we make the queue dynamic, compared the array which is simple with fixed size. However; compared to the array, the round-robin approach has better maintainability due to its ability to be dynamic.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,1,'2025-07-08_11-42-14','Round-robin scheduling can work to store our thread control blocks (TCBs) by enqueuing each thread into a queue data structure. The round-robin works by dequeue the thread at the front of the queue, allowing it to run, and once it calls osYield, the same thread is enqueued again (now at the back), and the new thread in queue is dequeued. The benefits of using a round-robin scheduling is that it avoids any gaps in an array, such as empty indexes or null index. It also simplifies logic by removing the need to track the current thread and any arithmetic to go to the next thread. Finally, it allows for cleaner expansion, as an array must be created with a fixed size. Comparing this to an array, the round-robin scheduling approach (queue based) simplifies the logic for scheduling, as we do not need to track any indexes, increment, or apply any modulo arithmetic. The queue could add additional overhead cause it likely uses a data structure such as linked list or doubly linked list, which requires pointers. This overhead is increased if we make the queue dynamic, compared the array which is simple with fixed size. However; compared to the array, the round-robin approach has better maintainability due to its ability to be dynamic.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,1,'2025-07-22_08-27-34','Round-robin scheduling can work to store our thread control blocks (TCBs) by enqueuing each thread into a queue data structure. The round-robin works by dequeue the thread at the front of the queue, allowing it to run, and once it calls osYield, the same thread is enqueued again (now at the back), and the new thread in queue is dequeued. The benefits of using a round-robin scheduling is that it avoids any gaps in an array, such as empty indexes or null index. It also simplifies logic by removing the need to track the current thread and any arithmetic to go to the next thread. Finally, it allows for cleaner expansion, as an array must be created with a fixed size. Comparing this to an array, the round-robin scheduling approach (queue based) simplifies the logic for scheduling, as we do not need to track any indexes, increment, or apply any modulo arithmetic. The queue could add additional overhead cause it likely uses a data structure such as linked list or doubly linked list, which requires pointers. This overhead is increased if we make the queue dynamic, compared the array which is simple with fixed size. However; compared to the array, the round-robin approach has better maintainability due to its ability to be dynamic.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,2,'2025-07-08_08-09-21','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch (switching from one thread to another) 	by waiting (pending) for the SVC interrupt to complete. PendSV is a special type of interrupt (pending), which we set as a lower priority than SVC so it triggers once SVC has complete. When a thread is complete, it calls the osYield function, which triggers PendSV. PendSV then saves the current thread state, goes to the next thread in our array, and restores/starts the state of the next thread.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,2,'2025-07-08_08-23-57','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch (switching from one thread to another) 	by waiting (pending) for the SVC interrupt to complete. PendSV is a special type of interrupt (pending), which we set as a lower priority than SVC so it triggers once SVC has complete. When a thread is complete, it calls the osYield function, which triggers PendSV. PendSV then saves the current thread state, goes to the next thread in our array, and restores/starts the state of the next thread. The contents of the CPU state are pushed onto the current thread’s stack using the Program Stack Pointer (PSP). The osSched function is called to determine which thread should run next. After the next thread is selected, its saved stack pointer is loaded into PSP.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,2,'2025-07-08_11-16-37','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch (switching from one thread to another) 	by waiting (pending) for the SVC interrupt to complete. PendSV is a special type of interrupt (pending), which we set as a lower priority than SVC so it triggers once SVC has complete. When a thread is complete, it calls the osYield function, which triggers PendSV. PendSV then saves the current thread state, goes to the next thread in our array, and restores/starts the state of the next thread. To save the current thread, PendSV pushes registers R4 – R11 onto the current thread’s stack. The contents of the CPU state are pushed onto the current thread’s stack using the Program Stack Pointer (PSP). The osSched function is called to determine which thread should run next. After the next thread is selected, its saved stack pointer is loaded into PSP. The function then pops registers R4 – R11 from the stack of the new thread into the CPU. Hardware restores the other registers, such as R0-R3, R12, Xpsr).\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,2,'2025-07-08_11-30-01','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch (switching from one thread to another) 	by waiting (pending) for the SVC interrupt to complete. PendSV is a special type of interrupt (pending), which we set as a lower priority than SVC so it triggers once SVC has complete. When a thread is complete, it calls the osYield function, which triggers PendSV. PendSV then saves the current thread state, goes to the next thread in our array, and restores/starts the state of the next thread. The first step of PendSV_Handler is to read the current value of the Program Stack Pointer (PSP) into a CPU register. Next, to save the current thread, PendSV pushes CPU registers R4 – R11 onto the current thread’s stack. It is important to read the PSP before using it to save register values to ensure the thread context is saved in the current thread’s stack, to prevent any memory loss or corruption. The contents of the CPU state are pushed onto the current thread’s stack using the PSP. The osSched function is called to determine which thread should run next. After the next thread is selected, its saved stack pointer is loaded into PSP. The function then pops registers R4 – R11 from the stack of the new thread into the CPU. Hardware restores the other registers, such as R0-R3, R12, Xpsr). The correct order of operations is important so we do not lose any memory from our threads, or causing corrupted threads / undefined behaviour. We must fully save the current state before calling osSched, and loading the new thread state.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,2,'2025-07-08_11-42-14','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch (switching from one thread to another) 	by waiting (pending) for the SVC interrupt to complete. PendSV is a special type of interrupt (pending), which we set as a lower priority than SVC so it triggers once SVC has complete. When a thread is complete, it calls the osYield function, which triggers PendSV. PendSV then saves the current thread state, goes to the next thread in our array, and restores/starts the state of the next thread. The first step of PendSV_Handler is to read the current value of the Program Stack Pointer (PSP) into a CPU register. Next, to save the current thread, PendSV pushes CPU registers R4 – R11 onto the current thread’s stack. It is important to read the PSP before using it to save register values to ensure the thread context is saved in the current thread’s stack, to prevent any memory loss or corruption. The contents of the CPU state are pushed onto the current thread’s stack using the PSP. The osSched function is called to determine which thread should run next. After the next thread is selected, its saved stack pointer is loaded into PSP. The function then pops registers R4 – R11 from the stack of the new thread into the CPU. Hardware restores the other registers, such as R0-R3, R12, Xpsr). The correct order of operations is important so we do not lose any memory from our threads, or causing corrupted threads / undefined behaviour. We must fully save the current state before calling osSched, and loading the new thread state.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,2,'2025-07-22_08-27-34','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch (switching from one thread to another) 	by waiting (pending) for the SVC interrupt to complete. PendSV is a special type of interrupt (pending), which we set as a lower priority than SVC so it triggers once SVC has complete. When a thread is complete, it calls the osYield function, which triggers PendSV. PendSV then saves the current thread state, goes to the next thread in our array, and restores/starts the state of the next thread. The first step of PendSV_Handler is to read the current value of the Program Stack Pointer (PSP) into a CPU register. Next, to save the current thread, PendSV pushes CPU registers R4 – R11 onto the current thread’s stack. It is important to read the PSP before using it to save register values to ensure the thread context is saved in the current thread’s stack, to prevent any memory loss or corruption. The contents of the CPU state are pushed onto the current thread’s stack using the PSP. The osSched function is called to determine which thread should run next. After the next thread is selected, its saved stack pointer is loaded into PSP. The function then pops registers R4 – R11 from the stack of the new thread into the CPU. Hardware restores the other registers, such as R0-R3, R12, Xpsr). The correct order of operations is important so we do not lose any memory from our threads, or causing corrupted threads / undefined behaviour. We must fully save the current state before calling osSched, and loading the new thread state.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,3,'2025-07-08_08-09-21','This is a bad idea cause in some cases, it means that the lower priority threads might never get a chance to run. Although these lower functions may be “low priority,” they may still be important for function and fluidity of our OS, and should have the chance to run (every once in a while, at least). There is also the high possibility that a function or high priority thread depends on a low priority thread, meaning that if there is priority, that low priority thread will never get the chance to run, which will break the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,3,'2025-07-08_08-23-57','This is a bad idea cause in some cases, it means that the lower priority threads might never get a chance to run. Although these lower functions may be “low priority,” they may still be important for function and fluidity of our OS, and should have the chance to run (every once in a while, at least). There is also the high possibility that a function or high priority thread depends on a low priority thread, meaning that if there is priority, that low priority thread will never get the chance to run, which will break the OS.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,3,'2025-07-08_11-16-37','This is a bad idea cause in some cases, it means that the lower priority threads might never get a chance to run. Although these lower functions may be “low priority,” they may still be important for function and fluidity of our OS, and should have the chance to run (every once in a while, at least). There is also the high possibility that a function or high priority thread depends on a low priority thread, meaning that if there is priority, that low priority thread will never get the chance to run, which will break the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,3,'2025-07-08_11-30-01','This is a bad idea cause in some cases, it means that the lower priority threads might never get a chance to run. Although these lower functions may be “low priority,” they may still be important for function and fluidity of our OS, and should have the chance to run (every once in a while, at least). There is also the high possibility that a function or high priority thread depends on a low priority thread, meaning that if there is priority, that low priority thread will never get the chance to run, which will break the OS.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,3,'2025-07-08_11-42-14','This is a bad idea cause in some cases, it means that the lower priority threads might never get a chance to run. Although these lower functions may be “low priority,” they may still be important for function and fluidity of our OS, and should have the chance to run (every once in a while, at least). There is also the high possibility that a function or high priority thread depends on a low priority thread, meaning that if there is priority, that low priority thread will never get the chance to run, which will break the OS. This is very relevant in context switching, as the scheulder osSched selects the next thread to run, thus if there is a higher priority thread, the scheduler will always select this thread allowing no other threads to run. The core mechanism of the scheduler is to select the next thread to run, so implementing priority to the TCB would force the scheduler to always pick the higher priority thread. This means the schedulers output will always be that of the higher priority thread.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,3,'2025-07-22_08-27-34','This is a bad idea cause in some cases, it means that the lower priority threads might never get a chance to run. Although these lower functions may be “low priority,” they may still be important for function and fluidity of our OS, and should have the chance to run (every once in a while, at least). There is also the high possibility that a function or high priority thread depends on a low priority thread, meaning that if there is priority, that low priority thread will never get the chance to run, which will break the OS. This is very relevant in context switching, as the scheulder osSched selects the next thread to run, thus if there is a higher priority thread, the scheduler will always select this thread allowing no other threads to run. The core mechanism of the scheduler is to select the next thread to run, so implementing priority to the TCB would force the scheduler to always pick the higher priority thread. This means the schedulers output will always be that of the higher priority thread.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,4,'2025-07-08_08-09-21','The main and most obvious benefit of C functions are simplicity, readability, and updatable. Writing assembly functions are very complicated and tedious, so writing functions in C allows us to ignore that abstraction and write in something that is easier to understand. This also means that C functions are easier to read, allowing us to come back to our functions or reread them if ever need be. Also, C functions are easy to update for future use. In our labs we have already had to update many of our functions multiple times, so having them written in C makes this a lot easier and digestible. Another benefit of writing in C is the fact that writing in a simpler language is less error-prone and allows for easier debugging. The downsides of writing C functions is limited control compared to assembly. Assembly allows for full control and direct access/contact with hardware. Another downside is performance. Writing in C means the compiler must compile and convert C into assembly, which is slower performance than writing directly in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,4,'2025-07-08_08-23-57','The main and most obvious benefit of C functions are simplicity, readability, and updatable. Writing assembly functions are very complicated and tedious, so writing functions in C allows us to ignore that abstraction and write in something that is easier to understand. This also means that C functions are easier to read, allowing us to come back to our functions or reread them if ever need be. Also, C functions are easy to update for future use. In our labs we have already had to update many of our functions multiple times, so having them written in C makes this a lot easier and digestible. Another benefit of writing in C is the fact that writing in a simpler language is less error-prone and allows for easier debugging. The downsides of writing C functions is limited control compared to assembly. Assembly allows for full control and direct access/contact with hardware. Another downside is performance. Writing in C means the compiler must compile and convert C into assembly, which is slower performance than writing directly in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,4,'2025-07-08_11-16-37','The main and most obvious benefit of C functions are simplicity, readability, and updatable. Writing assembly functions are very complicated and tedious, so writing functions in C allows us to ignore that abstraction and write in something that is easier to understand. This also means that C functions are easier to read, allowing us to come back to our functions or reread them if ever need be. Also, C functions are easy to update for future use. In our labs we have already had to update many of our functions multiple times, so having them written in C makes this a lot easier and digestible. Another benefit of writing in C is the fact that writing in a simpler language is less error-prone and allows for easier debugging. The downsides of writing C functions is limited control compared to assembly. Assembly allows for full control and direct access/contact with hardware. Another downside is performance. Writing in C means the compiler must compile and convert C into assembly, which is slower performance than writing directly in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,4,'2025-07-08_11-30-01','The main and most obvious benefit of C functions are simplicity, readability, and updatable. Writing assembly functions are very complicated and tedious, so writing functions in C allows us to ignore that abstraction and write in something that is easier to understand. This also means that C functions are easier to read, allowing us to come back to our functions or reread them if ever need be. Also, C functions are easy to update for future use. In our labs we have already had to update many of our functions multiple times, so having them written in C makes this a lot easier and digestible. Another benefit of writing in C is the fact that writing in a simpler language is less error-prone and allows for easier debugging. The downsides of writing C functions is limited control compared to assembly. Assembly allows for full control and direct access/contact with hardware. Another downside is performance. Writing in C means the compiler must compile and convert C into assembly, which is slower performance than writing directly in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,4,'2025-07-08_11-42-14','The main and most obvious benefit of C functions are simplicity, readability, and updatable. Writing assembly functions are very complicated and tedious, so writing functions in C allows us to ignore that abstraction and write in something that is easier to understand. This also means that C functions are easier to read, allowing us to come back to our functions or reread them if ever need be. Also, C functions are easy to update for future use. In our labs we have already had to update many of our functions multiple times, so having them written in C makes this a lot easier and digestible. Another benefit of writing in C is the fact that writing in a simpler language is less error-prone and allows for easier debugging. The downsides of writing C functions is limited control compared to assembly. Assembly allows for full control and direct access/contact with hardware. Another downside is performance. Writing in C means the compiler must compile and convert C into assembly, which is slower performance than writing directly in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,4,'2025-07-22_08-27-34','The main and most obvious benefit of C functions are simplicity, readability, and updatable. Writing assembly functions are very complicated and tedious, so writing functions in C allows us to ignore that abstraction and write in something that is easier to understand. This also means that C functions are easier to read, allowing us to come back to our functions or reread them if ever need be. Also, C functions are easy to update for future use. In our labs we have already had to update many of our functions multiple times, so having them written in C makes this a lot easier and digestible. Another benefit of writing in C is the fact that writing in a simpler language is less error-prone and allows for easier debugging. The downsides of writing C functions is limited control compared to assembly. Assembly allows for full control and direct access/contact with hardware. Another downside is performance. Writing in C means the compiler must compile and convert C into assembly, which is slower performance than writing directly in assembly.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,5,'2025-07-08_08-09-21','If we created only a single thread, the single thread would continue to run instead of context-switching to different threads, and the OS will still work. This is because when the osYield function is called, the PendSV interrupt will attempt to do a context switch, but since there is only one thread in the array (or the structure), PendSV will just select the same thread over and over again. Although this is inefficient, the OS still works. If we wanted to run one thread function continuously, it would be better to exclude any yielding and make the function an infinite loop (like the previous two labs).\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,5,'2025-07-08_08-23-57','If we created only a single thread, the single thread would continue to run instead of context-switching to different threads, and the OS will still work. This is because when the osYield function is called, the PendSV interrupt will attempt to do a context switch, but since there is only one thread in the array (or the structure), PendSV will just select the same thread over and over again. Although this is inefficient, the OS still works. If we wanted to run one thread function continuously, it would be better to exclude any yielding and make the function an infinite loop (like the previous two labs).\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,5,'2025-07-08_11-16-37','If we created only a single thread, the single thread would continue to run instead of context-switching to different threads, and the OS will still work. This is because when the osYield function is called, the PendSV interrupt will attempt to do a context switch, but since there is only one thread in the array (or the structure), PendSV will just select the same thread over and over again. Although this is inefficient, the OS still works. If we wanted to run one thread function continuously, it would be better to exclude any yielding and make the function an infinite loop (like the previous two labs).\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,5,'2025-07-08_11-30-01','If we created only a single thread, the single thread would continue to run instead of context-switching to different threads, and the OS will still work. This is because when the osYield function is called, the PendSV interrupt will attempt to do a context switch, but since there is only one thread in the array (or the structure), PendSV will just select the same thread over and over again. Although this is inefficient, the OS still works. If we wanted to run one thread function continuously, it would be better to exclude any yielding and make the function an infinite loop (like the previous two labs).\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,5,'2025-07-08_11-42-14','If we created only a single thread, the single thread would continue to run instead of context-switching to different threads, and the OS will still work. This is because when the osYield function is called, the PendSV interrupt will attempt to do a context switch, but since there is only one thread in the array (or the structure), PendSV will just select the same thread over and over again. Although this is inefficient, the OS still works. If we wanted to run one thread function continuously, it would be better to exclude any yielding and make the function an infinite loop (like the previous two labs).\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',4,5,'2025-07-22_08-27-34','If we created only a single thread, the single thread would continue to run instead of context-switching to different threads, and the OS will still work. This is because when the osYield function is called, the PendSV interrupt will attempt to do a context switch, but since there is only one thread in the array (or the structure), PendSV will just select the same thread over and over again. Although this is inefficient, the OS still works. If we wanted to run one thread function continuously, it would be better to exclude any yielding and make the function an infinite loop (like the previous two labs).\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',5,1,'2025-07-22_07-32-54','First, the user must create a thread function that accepts an argument pointer as input. This argument must be casted to its specific type in the thread function, and then inside main the user must declare and initialize the argument, and then call the function with the address of the argument. What the OS has to do is store the argument pointer in thread’s context and set up the argument properly in the thread’s stack so it can pass through the function without any errors. This works because in the user thread function, the pointer is dereferenced because we don’t know where it is exactly stored. The function copies the data stored by the pointer, not the pointer itself. We use the register R0 in this case because a void pointer can be cast to an integer and stored entirely within R0. We do this by modifying our osCreateThread function to set up the stack frame with the pointer.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',5,1,'2025-07-22_08-16-31','First, the user must create a thread function that accepts an argument pointer as input. This argument must be casted to its specific type in the thread function, and then inside main the user must declare and initialize the argument, and then call the function with the address of the argument. What the OS has to do is store the argument pointer in thread’s context and set up the argument properly in the thread’s stack so it can pass through the function without any errors. The OS must place the argument pointer into the R0 register of the stack, as a void pointer can be cast to an integer and stored entirely within R0. This is also the register used to pass the first argument to a function in the ARM calling convention. This works because in the user thread function, the pointer is dereferenced because we don’t know where it is exactly stored. The function copies the data stored by the pointer, not the pointer itself. We use the register R0 in this case because a void pointer can be cast to an integer and stored entirely within R0. We do this by modifying our osCreateThread function to set up the stack frame with the pointer. The CPU follows ABI when restoring a thread’s context, so it pops R0 from the stack, and the function behaves as if the argument was normally there.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',5,2,'2025-07-22_07-32-54','You can’t call osYield in any of our context-switching functions because osYield already triggers PendSV when it is called, which is a software interrupt, so calling osYield again would trigger more interrupts. This means that if the OS is currently in a context-switching interrupt, and osYield is called again within the context-switching function, another context switch would be set as pending before the current one finishes. This results in unexpected priority selection and can cause a loop within the context-switching that will cause the system to crash.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',5,2,'2025-07-22_08-16-31','You can’t call osYield in any of our context-switching functions because osYield already triggers PendSV when it is called, which is a software interrupt, so calling osYield again would trigger more interrupts. This means that if the OS is currently in a context-switching interrupt, and osYield is called again within the context-switching function, another context switch would be set as pending before the current one finishes. This results in unexpected priority selection and can cause a loop within the context-switching that will cause the system to crash. Nested-context switching is problematic as PendSV interrupt is usually the lowest priority interrupt, so calling osYield in a context-switching function will result in reentrant PendSV, which results in stack corruption and register corruption. This is because the CPU may try to push the same registers onto the stack again. Eventually, when this state is restored after a context-switch, the CPU may try to access this corrupted stack which will result in a hardfault, which is a critical failure that the CPU can not recover from and will likely cause the system to reset.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',5,3,'2025-07-22_07-32-54','With a 0.1ms fundamental OS clock rate, timing and scheduling tasks becomes much more precise. This is beneficial in real-time systems where precision and timing is important. Also, deadlines and timeslices can be specified to be more specific, allowing more control of tasks, especially those that are very time sensitive. The downsides of a 0.1ms fundamental OS clock rate is that this frequency creates much more CPU overhead. Since 0.1ms is 10x more frequent than 1ms, that means the SysTick_Handler function is called 10x more often. This may waste CPU resources, since the CPU may spend too much time focusing on context switches and decrementing instead of running actual threads. The impact of the clock rate is dependent on the CPU’s capabilities and number of cores.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',5,3,'2025-07-22_08-16-31','With a 0.1ms fundamental OS clock rate, timing and scheduling tasks becomes much more precise. This is beneficial in real-time systems where precision and timing is important. Also, deadlines and timeslices can be specified to be more specific, allowing more control of tasks, especially those that are very time sensitive. The downsides of a 0.1ms fundamental OS clock rate is that this frequency creates much more CPU overhead. Since 0.1ms is 10x more frequent than 1ms, that means the SysTick_Handler function is called 10x more often. This may waste CPU resources, since the CPU may spend too much time focusing on context switches and decrementing instead of running actual threads. The impact of the clock rate is dependent on the CPU’s capabilities and number of cores.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',5,4,'2025-07-22_07-32-54','Co-operative multitasking means the user must explicitly call osYield() in their thread functions to switch threads. This means that the user must specify when in a thread function osYield() is called (usually when finished completing a certain set of code), which means it’s the user’s responsibility to fairly and responsibly write thread functions that yield appropriately, to avoid one thread starving others of their runtime. What the OS does is wait for a thread function to call osYield, and the OS then performs a context switch. Pre-emptive multitasking from the user perspective is when the context-switching interrupt is handled by the OS, by using a set timer. This means that the OS is the one determining when the context-switching interrupt is happening, by using ticks to determine a thread’s runtime. What the OS has to do is keep track of the runtime of a thread and use ticks to decrement that runtime, until the thread goes to the deadline and the OS calls for a context switch.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',5,4,'2025-07-22_08-16-31','Co-operative multitasking means the user must explicitly call osYield() in their thread functions to switch threads. This means that the user must specify when in a thread function osYield() is called (usually when finished completing a certain set of code), which means it’s the user’s responsibility to fairly and responsibly write thread functions that yield appropriately, to avoid one thread starving others of their runtime. What the OS does is wait for a thread function to call osYield, and the OS then performs a context switch. Pre-emptive multitasking from the user perspective is when the context-switching interrupt is handled by the OS, by using a set timer. This means that the OS is the one determining when the context-switching interrupt is happening, by using ticks to determine a thread’s runtime, without thread cooperation. What the OS has to do is keep track of the runtime of a thread and use ticks to decrement that runtime, until the thread goes to the deadline and the OS calls for a context switch. The OS uses the timer interrupt, SysTick_Handler, which runs at specific intervals, to decrement the time remaining in a thread’s timeslice. Once/if the timeslice reaches zero, the OS forces a context switch by calling the PendSV interrupt and resetting the thread’s runtime to it’s timeslice for future context switches.\n',1,'s25'),('ttrinh',5,5,'2025-07-22_07-32-54','It is possible for these mechanisms to work together as thread functions can have co-operative yielding written in them, but still have runtime that is interrupted for a deadline. For example, a thread may run completely and then call osYield, however; if that thread takes too long to yield, the OS can call osYield itself and perform a context switch. This is useful as it prevents any threads from hogging CPU time, and it acts as a safety net from any possible bugs that may be causing a thread to be unable to call osYield.\n',0,'s25'),('ttrinh',5,5,'2025-07-22_08-16-31','It is possible for these mechanisms to work together as thread functions can have co-operative yielding written in them, but still have runtime that is interrupted for a deadline. This works as both cooperative and preemptive multitasking use the same handler, the PendSV interrupt, to save and restore thread states. For cooperative, the user must explicitly call osYield to trigger PendSV, however; in the preemptive case, the OS uses the timer interrupt, SysTick_Handler, to decrement a thread’s runtime, and eventually call osYield to trigger PendSV when the runtime is zero. Since both mechanisms use the same handler function, osYield(), they can work together by having the osYield not only trigger the PendSV interrupt, but also reset a thread’s runtime. For example, a thread may run completely and then call osYield and reset the thread’s runtime, however; if that thread takes too long to yield, the OS can call osYield itself and perform a context switch. This is useful as it prevents any threads from hogging CPU time, and it acts as a safety net from any possible bugs that may be causing a thread to be unable to call osYield.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',1,1,'2025-06-24_23-12-14','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a programming layer in embedded systems that acts as a bridge between the hardware of a microcontroller (like registers, peripherals, or memory) and the software that runs on it (like application code or an operating system). The HAL provides standardized functions that let you control hardware (like turning on an LED or reading a sensor) without needing to write low-level register code for each specific microcontroller. It allows developers to write application code that doesn’t depend on the specific hardware. If you switch to a different microcontroller (from the same family), you may only need to update the HAL or its configuration—not the entire application. It provides pre-written functions to manage peripherals like UART, GPIO, ADC, etc., which saves time compared to writing low-level code yourself. HAL functions are also generally easier to understand and debug than raw register manipulations. Overall, HAL helps make embedded projects more modular, maintainable, and easier to scale or adapt across different hardware platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',1,1,'2025-06-24_23-22-42','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) is a programming layer in embedded systems that acts as a bridge between the hardware of a microcontroller (like registers, peripherals, or memory) and the software that runs on it (like application code or an operating system). The HAL provides standardized functions that let you control hardware (like turning on an LED or reading a sensor) without needing to write low-level register code for each specific microcontroller. It allows developers to write application code that doesn’t depend on the specific hardware. If you switch to a different microcontroller (from the same family), you may only need to update the HAL or its configuration—not the entire application. It provides pre-written functions to manage peripherals like UART, GPIO, ADC, etc., which saves time compared to writing low-level code yourself. HAL functions are also generally easier to understand and debug than raw register manipulations. Overall, HAL helps make embedded projects more modular, maintainable, and easier to scale or adapt across different hardware platforms.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',1,2,'2025-06-24_23-12-14','The __io_putchar function is used to redirect standard output (like printf) to a hardware interface, such as a UART serial port, in an embedded system. The __io_putchar function sends one character at a time through the UART (serial) port. This is useful when you want to use functions like printf() to print messages to your computer via a serial connection (e.g., for debugging or logging output). The function first takes a character ch (from printf or similar functions). It then transmits that character through UART2 using the STM32 HAL library. After, it waits until the transmission is done (HAL_MAX_DELAY makes it block until complete) and returns the character. By default, printf doesn\'t know where to send its output on a microcontroller (unlike a PC, which has a terminal or screen). The __io_putchar function tells printf() to send characters through a UART port. Without this function, printf() wouldn’t output anything. Overall, this function connects printf to your serial port, so you can see debug messages on a terminal window when the microcontroller is running.\n',0,'s25'),('u3hussai',1,2,'2025-06-24_23-22-42','On bare-metal microcontrollers, there is no default console device, so the standard printf function from the C stdio library cannot work unless low-level output functionality is explicitly provided. Since embedded systems do not include a built-in printf implementation that knows how to talk to the hardware, the __io_putchar function must be written to serve as a bridge between high-level I/O and the specific hardware peripheral—in this case, using HAL_UART_Transmit to send characters over a serial connection. This function must be customized for each microcontroller or output device to account for hardware differences. While printf and __io_putchar are both part of stdio.h, printf internally calls __io_putchar for each character in the output string. Within __io_putchar, you can configure behavior such as blocking vs. non-blocking transmission, buffering, and whether to use interrupts or polling. Without implementing this function, printf would produce no output, behave unpredictably, or default to semihosting—which may cause the program to hang if no debugger is attached.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',1,3,'2025-06-24_23-12-14','We used the debugger in this lab because it gives us deeper and more precise control over how we observe and understand the program as it runs. The debugger lets us pause execution at any line of code using breakpoints. This means we can stop the program exactly where we want and inspect things like the stack, registers, and memory values. With the debugger, we can also step through the code line by line, watching how variables change in real-time. This is essential for tasks like monitoring how the stack grows, which can only be seen while the program is actively running. While printf is useful for displaying some output, it doesn’t stop the program and it can affect timing or program behavior, especially in embedded systems with limited resources. It also can’t show low-level hardware information like register states or exact memory layouts. We needed the debugger to get an accurate and controlled view of how the stack and memory behaved, which printf simply isn’t capable of providing in this context.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',1,3,'2025-06-24_23-22-42','We used the debugger in this lab because it gives us deeper and more precise control over how we observe and understand the program as it runs. The debugger lets us pause execution at any line of code using breakpoints. This means we can stop the program exactly where we want and inspect things like the stack, registers, and memory values. With the debugger, we can also step through the code line by line, watching how variables change in real-time. This is essential for tasks like monitoring how the stack grows, which can only be seen while the program is actively running. While printf is useful for displaying some output, it doesn’t stop the program and it can affect timing or program behavior, especially in embedded systems with limited resources. It also can’t show low-level hardware information like register states or exact memory layouts. We needed the debugger to get an accurate and controlled view of how the stack and memory behaved, which printf simply isn’t capable of providing in this context.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',1,4,'2025-06-24_23-12-14','We started by getting the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) from address 0x0, which contains the reset vector table. Specifically, the first word at address 0x0 holds the initial address of the MSP. This value is critical because it tells us where the stack region in RAM begins for the main stack. To get the new value for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), we chose a fixed size for the main stack (0x400 bytes) and then subtracted that size from MSP_INIT_VAL, because stacks grow downward in memory. This new PSP_val now points to a separate region of memory below the main stack, which we’ll use for threads or user-mode code. We needed the initial MSP value because it is the starting point of all stack allocation. Without knowing where the main stack begins, we wouldn’t know where the available RAM ends, and therefore couldn’t safely allocate new stack regions (like for PSP). We treat the RAM as a contiguous block, and use MSP_INIT_VAL as the anchor to carve out fixed-size stack segments for the OS and threads.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',1,4,'2025-06-24_23-22-42','We started by getting the initial value of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) from address 0x0, which contains the reset vector table. Specifically, the first word at address 0x0 holds the initial address of the MSP. This value is critical because it tells us where the stack region in RAM begins for the main stack. To get the new value for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), we chose a fixed size for the main stack (0x400 bytes) and then subtracted that size from MSP_INIT_VAL, because stacks grow downward in memory. This new PSP_val now points to a separate region of memory below the main stack, which we’ll use for threads or user-mode code. We needed the initial MSP value because it is the starting point of all stack allocation. Without knowing where the main stack begins, we wouldn’t know where the available RAM ends, and therefore couldn’t safely allocate new stack regions (like for PSP). We treat the RAM as a contiguous block, and use MSP_INIT_VAL as the anchor to carve out fixed-size stack segments for the OS and threads.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',1,5,'2025-06-24_23-12-14','To allocate multiple thread stacks on a Cortex-M4—where stacks grow downward and must be 8-byte aligned—you begin from the known top-of-stack address, MSP_init_val, and reserve a contiguous memory region below it by subtracting N * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This gives you a base_address marking the bottom of the reserved space. For each thread index i (where 0 ≤ i < N), the stack pointer PSP_values[i] is calculated as base_address - (i + 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This ensures that each PSP points to the top of its own stack space, and since stacks grow downward, the layout prevents overlap. This method ensures all thread stacks are properly aligned, non-overlapping, and contained within the reserved region. Note that this calculation is separate from context switching, which only requires setting the current PSP using __set_PSP(PSP_values[i]) and enabling PSP mode via __set_CONTROL(2). Since each thread stack is derived from a single validated base with a fixed offset, all allocated stack positions are safe and consistent.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',1,5,'2025-06-24_23-22-42','To allocate multiple thread stacks on a Cortex-M4—where stacks grow downward and must be 8-byte aligned—you begin from the known top-of-stack address, MSP_init_val, and reserve a contiguous memory region below it by subtracting N * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This gives you a base_address marking the bottom of the reserved space. For each thread index i (where 0 ≤ i < N), the stack pointer PSP_values[i] is calculated as base_address - (i + 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. This ensures that each PSP points to the top of its own stack space, and since stacks grow downward, the layout prevents overlap. This method ensures all thread stacks are properly aligned, non-overlapping, and contained within the reserved region. Note that this calculation is separate from context switching, which only requires setting the current PSP using __set_PSP(PSP_values[i]) and enabling PSP mode via __set_CONTROL(2). Since each thread stack is derived from a single validated base with a fixed offset, all allocated stack positions are safe and consistent.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,1,'2025-06-25_16-20-38','Since the PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next, when I changed PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, I am essentially telling the processor to run the print continuously function next. When the PC register golds the function pointer to the print continuously function, and when the clock ticks, that address is decoded and the instruction cycle begins, running that function. This is like how a function call works, where the processor saves a return address and jumps to the function code. And by assigning the function directly to the PC, we skip that setup step, but it gives the same result, and the processor starts running the function. Therefore, changing PC is like calling the function at the processor level.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,1,'2025-06-26_23-31-07','Since the PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next, when I changed PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, I am essentially telling the processor to run the print continuously function next. When the PC register golds the function pointer to the print continuously function, and when the clock ticks, that address is decoded and the instruction cycle begins, running that function. This is like how a function call works, where the processor saves a return address and jumps to the function code. And by assigning the function directly to the PC, we skip that setup step, but it gives the same result, and the processor starts running the function. Therefore, changing PC is like calling the function at the processor level.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,1,'2025-06-27_01-49-56','Since the PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next, when I changed PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, I am essentially telling the processor to run the print continuously function next. When the PC register golds the function pointer to the print continuously function, and when the clock ticks, that address is decoded and the instruction cycle begins, running that function. This is like how a function call works, where the processor saves a return address and jumps to the function code. And by assigning the function directly to the PC, we skip that setup step, but it gives the same result, and the processor starts running the function. Therefore, changing PC is like calling the function at the processor level.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,1,'2025-06-27_14-35-07','Since the PC register is used by the processor to determine which instruction to run next, when I changed PC to hold the function pointer to the print continuously function, I am essentially telling the processor to run the print continuously function next. When the PC register golds the function pointer to the print continuously function, and when the clock ticks, that address is decoded and the instruction cycle begins, running that function. This is like how a function call works, where the processor saves a return address and jumps to the function code. And by assigning the function directly to the PC, we skip that setup step, but it gives the same result, and the processor starts running the function. Therefore, changing PC is like calling the function at the processor level.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,2,'2025-06-25_16-20-38','A system call is a call that goes to the operating system, which asks it to do a specific function. It is usually used to use a function that requires kernel mode access, which is something only the OS has access to. Thus, the program asks the OS to do this task using the SVC command.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,2,'2025-06-26_23-31-07','A system call is a call that goes to the operating system, which asks it to do a specific function. It is usually used to use a function that requires kernel mode access, which is something only the OS has access to. Thus, the program asks the OS to do this task using the SVC command.\n',0,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,2,'2025-06-27_01-49-56','A system call is a call that goes to the operating system, which asks it to do a specific function. It is usually used to use a function that requires kernel mode access, which is something only the OS has access to. It’s a special kind of function call that goes from user code into the kernel, which has more privileges. This is important because user programs shouldn’t be able to mess with sensitive stuff like device registers or other system resources. Instead, they use a system call to safely request that the OS do it for them. On ARM processors like the Cortex-M4, this is usually done using the SVC instruction, which switches to a more privileged mode and runs the requested service in the OS. So in short, system calls act like a controlled gateway between user programs and the hardware, keeping everything safe and organized\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,2,'2025-06-27_14-35-07','A system call is a call that goes to the operating system, which asks it to do a specific function. It is usually used to use a function that requires kernel mode access, which is something only the OS has access to. It’s a special kind of function call that goes from user code into the kernel, which has more privileges. This is important because user programs shouldn’t be able to mess with sensitive stuff like device registers or other system resources. Instead, they use a system call to safely request that the OS do it for them. On ARM processors like the Cortex-M4, this is usually done using the SVC instruction, which switches to a more privileged mode and runs the requested service in the OS. So in short, system calls act like a controlled gateway between user programs and the hardware, keeping everything safe and organized\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,3,'2025-06-25_16-20-38','To set up a thread’s stack is to prepare a section of memory so that the CPU can pretend it was already running a thread. We first reserve a valid memory area for the stack by assigning stackptr to a known memory location (a value below MSP). Then, we manually assign values into the thread’s stack in a specific order, starting with xPSR and PC, followed by LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11 to R4. This order is really important because it is the way the processor restores registers during an exception return or context switching. So it essentially tricks the processor into thinking the thread already existed and has been running, so it just uses it normally. If the order is wrong, values will be loaded into the wrong registers and the thread won’t run properly.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,3,'2025-06-26_23-31-07','To set up a thread’s stack is to prepare a section of memory so that the CPU can pretend it was already running a thread. We first reserve a valid memory area for the stack by assigning stackptr to a known memory location (a value below MSP). Then, we manually assign values into the thread’s stack in a specific order, starting with xPSR and PC, followed by LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11 to R4. This order is really important because it is the way the processor restores registers during an exception return or context switching. So it essentially tricks the processor into thinking the thread already existed and has been running, so it just uses it normally. If the order is wrong, values will be loaded into the wrong registers and the thread won’t run properly.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,3,'2025-06-27_01-49-56','To set up a thread’s stack is to prepare a section of memory so that the CPU can pretend it was already running a thread. We first reserve a valid memory area for the stack by assigning stackptr to a known memory location (a value below MSP). Then, we manually assign values into the thread’s stack in a specific order, starting with xPSR and PC, followed by LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11 to R4. This order is really important because it is the way the processor restores registers during an exception return or context switching. So it essentially tricks the processor into thinking the thread already existed and has been running, so it just uses it normally. If the order is wrong, values will be loaded into the wrong registers and the thread won’t run properly.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,3,'2025-06-27_14-35-07','To set up a thread’s stack is to prepare a section of memory so that the CPU can pretend it was already running a thread. We first reserve a valid memory area for the stack by assigning stackptr to a known memory location (a value below MSP). Then, we manually assign values into the thread’s stack in a specific order, starting with xPSR and PC, followed by LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11 to R4. This order is really important because it is the way the processor restores registers during an exception return or context switching. So it essentially tricks the processor into thinking the thread already existed and has been running, so it just uses it normally. If the order is wrong, values will be loaded into the wrong registers and the thread won’t run properly.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,4,'2025-06-25_16-20-38','The code would crash in this case because if we did not call __set_PSP(), the processor will use the MSP instead of the new stackptr. This is a problem, because the thread’s register values were placed on the thread’s new stack, and if the CPU uses MSP, it will try to pop those values from the wrong place, leading to unpredictable behaviours.\n',0,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,4,'2025-06-26_23-31-07','The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is used by the CPU to store and retrieve a thread’s register context during execution when the processor is running in thread mode. When we create a new thread, we manually place the thread’s initial register values (like PC, xPSR, R0–R12) onto a dedicated region of memory — its own stack. However, this stack is not automatically used by the CPU. By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is reserved for exceptions and system-level code. If we do not explicitly call __set_PSP() to point the PSP to the new thread’s stack, then when the thread starts running, the CPU will try to pop the register values from MSP instead of the correct thread stack. This results in corrupted register values or access to invalid memory, which causes a hard fault. Therefore, it’s not enough to just avoid using MSP — we must explicitly set the PSP to the new stack address so that the CPU knows where to find the correct context for the thread. Setting PSP ensures that the processor pops the expected values from the correct stack, allowing the thread to run safely and correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,4,'2025-06-27_01-49-56','The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is used by the CPU to store and retrieve a thread’s register context during execution when the processor is running in thread mode. When we create a new thread, we manually place the thread’s initial register values (like PC, xPSR, R0–R12) onto a dedicated region of memory — its own stack. However, this stack is not automatically used by the CPU. By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is reserved for exceptions and system-level code. If we do not explicitly call __set_PSP() to point the PSP to the new thread’s stack, then when the thread starts running, the CPU will try to pop the register values from MSP instead of the correct thread stack. This results in corrupted register values or access to invalid memory, which causes a hard fault. Therefore, it’s not enough to just avoid using MSP — we must explicitly set the PSP to the new stack address so that the CPU knows where to find the correct context for the thread. Setting PSP ensures that the processor pops the expected values from the correct stack, allowing the thread to run safely and correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,4,'2025-06-27_14-35-07','The Process Stack Pointer (PSP) is used by the CPU to store and retrieve a thread’s register context during execution when the processor is running in thread mode. When we create a new thread, we manually place the thread’s initial register values (like PC, xPSR, R0–R12) onto a dedicated region of memory — its own stack. However, this stack is not automatically used by the CPU. By default, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP), which is reserved for exceptions and system-level code. If we do not explicitly call __set_PSP() to point the PSP to the new thread’s stack, then when the thread starts running, the CPU will try to pop the register values from MSP instead of the correct thread stack. This results in corrupted register values or access to invalid memory, which causes a hard fault. Therefore, it’s not enough to just avoid using MSP — we must explicitly set the PSP to the new stack address so that the CPU knows where to find the correct context for the thread. Setting PSP ensures that the processor pops the expected values from the correct stack, allowing the thread to run safely and correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,5,'2025-06-25_16-20-38','The program would most likely crash, or go to hard fault, which is because when the thread returns, the processor tries to pop the return address from the stack, but since we pushed in values to a fake stack, like 0xA, the processor will head to that address, which has nothing valid to this, therefore it will crash.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,5,'2025-06-26_23-31-07','The program would most likely crash, or go to hard fault, which is because when the thread returns, the processor tries to pop the return address from the stack, but since we pushed in values to a fake stack, like 0xA, the processor will head to that address, which has nothing valid to this, therefore it will crash.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,5,'2025-06-27_01-49-56','The program would most likely crash, or go to hard fault, which is because when the thread returns, the processor tries to pop the return address from the stack, but since we pushed in values to a fake stack, like 0xA, the processor will head to that address, which has nothing valid to this, therefore it will crash.\n',0,'s25'),('u3hussai',2,5,'2025-06-27_14-35-07','If the thread function returned instead of looping forever, the CPU would attempt to pop the return address from the thread’s stack so it could resume execution. However, in our setup, we manually built a fake stack for the thread, and we did not place a valid return address in the expected location (i.e., the LR register value). Instead, we filled the stack with placeholder values like 0xA, which do not point to valid code. Because the return address is missing or invalid, the CPU will try to jump to an invalid memory address when the thread returns. This will trigger a hardfault, which is the Cortex-M processor’s standard response to invalid instruction fetches or execution from illegal memory regions. In summary, the thread will cause a hardfault when it returns because it has no valid return address on its stack.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',3,1,'2025-06-27_14-52-38','When building a program with multiple .c files, each file is first compiled separately by the compiler into an object file (.o or .obj), which contains machine code and symbol information (like function names and variables). During this compilation phase, the compiler only needs to see declarations (from header files) for functions or variables that are defined in other files. Once all the .c files are compiled, the linker takes over and combines all the object files into a single executable. The linker resolves symbol references between files—connecting function calls and variable accesses to their actual definitions—and produces the final executable binary that can be run on the target system\n',0,'s25'),('u3hussai',3,1,'2025-06-27_15-28-07','When building a program made up of multiple .c files, the process begins with preprocessing. In this step, the compiler handles directives such as #include and #define by copying in the contents of included header files and expanding macros. This produces a complete source file for each .c file, ready for compilation. Next, during the compilation phase, each .c file (after preprocessing) is compiled individually into an object file (.o), which contains machine code and symbolic references for functions and variables. Finally, the linker takes all the object files and combines them into a single executable file. It resolves any references between files—like calls to functions defined in other files—and ensures everything is correctly connected so the final program can run on the target system.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',3,2,'2025-06-27_14-52-38','Using #include <kernel.c> (or any #include on a .c file) is a bad idea because it defeats the purpose of separate compilation. When you include a .c file, you\'re copying and pasting its entire contents directly into the current file during preprocessing, which can lead to multiple definition errors (especially for global variables and functions). It also breaks modularity: each .c file is supposed to be compiled independently into an object file, and then linked together by the linker. Including .c files bypasses that system, makes build times longer, increases the chance of bugs, and creates tight coupling between files. Instead, you should #include the corresponding .h file, which only contains declarations and shared definitions, allowing each .c file to compile cleanly on its own.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',3,2,'2025-06-27_15-28-07','Using #include <kernel.c> (or any #include on a .c file) is a bad idea because it defeats the purpose of separate compilation. When you include a .c file, you\'re copying and pasting its entire contents directly into the current file during preprocessing, which can lead to multiple definition errors (especially for global variables and functions). It also breaks modularity: each .c file is supposed to be compiled independently into an object file, and then linked together by the linker. Including .c files bypasses that system, makes build times longer, increases the chance of bugs, and creates tight coupling between files. Instead, you should #include the corresponding .h file, which only contains declarations and shared definitions, allowing each .c file to compile cleanly on its own.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',3,3,'2025-06-27_14-52-38','To set up and start a thread, the user should first define the function that the thread will execute. This function typically takes no arguments and may include a continuous loop if it needs to stay active. Next, the user initializes the OS kernel using a dedicated function, which prepares the system by setting up internal memory structures such as the stack pool. Once the kernel is initialized, the user creates a thread by calling the thread creation function and passing it the address of the thread function. This lets the OS know which function the thread should run. Finally, the user starts the kernel by calling a function that triggers the execution of the first thread, causing the processor to switch to the thread’s stack and begin running the specified function from the start.\n',0,'s25'),('u3hussai',3,3,'2025-06-27_15-28-07','To set up and start a thread, the user should first define the function that the thread will execute. This function typically takes no arguments and may include a continuous loop if it needs to stay active. Next, the user initializes the OS kernel using a dedicated function, which prepares the system by setting up internal memory structures such as the stack pool. Once the kernel is initialized, the user creates a thread by calling the thread creation function and passing it the address of the thread function. This lets the OS know which function the thread should run. Finally, the user starts the kernel by calling a function that triggers the execution of the first thread, causing the processor to switch to the thread’s stack and begin running the specified function from the start.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',3,4,'2025-06-27_14-52-38','The stack pool is a block of memory reserved by the operating system to store the stacks used by the kernel and its threads. Each thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls and register values during execution. The kernel uses its stack—typically accessed through the Main Stack Pointer (MSP)—for system-level operations, while each thread uses a separate portion of the stack pool via the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). When a thread is created, it is assigned a section of the stack pool to use as its own stack space. This organized allocation prevents memory corruption and ensures stability. In future labs, the stack pool will allow the OS to switch between multiple threads by saving and restoring their stack contents. If the stack pool is not managed properly, the system could run out of memory or overwrite existing data, so it’s important to remember that this pool is shared between all user threads and the kernel itself.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',3,4,'2025-06-27_15-28-07','The stack pool is a block of memory reserved by the operating system to store the stacks used by the kernel and its threads. Each thread needs its own stack to keep track of function calls and register values during execution. The kernel uses its stack—typically accessed through the Main Stack Pointer (MSP)—for system-level operations, while each thread uses a separate portion of the stack pool via the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). When a thread is created, it is assigned a section of the stack pool to use as its own stack space. This organized allocation prevents memory corruption and ensures stability. In future labs, the stack pool will allow the OS to switch between multiple threads by saving and restoring their stack contents. If the stack pool is not managed properly, the system could run out of memory or overwrite existing data, so it’s important to remember that this pool is shared between all user threads and the kernel itself.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',3,5,'2025-06-27_14-52-38','The kernel handles low-level operations such as stack allocation, thread management, and hardware control, and its code is kept separate in dedicated kernel files. In contrast, the user writes the application logic (like what each thread should do) in the main file. Users interact with the kernel through API calls, which are functions provided by the kernel that allow users to create threads, initialize the system, and start execution. This separation keeps the system well-organized: users don’t need to understand or modify the kernel\'s internal workings to write their programs, and the kernel remains protected from accidental changes. The kernel also manages memory and hardware access to ensure threads operate safely and independently, allowing users to focus on their applications while the kernel takes care of critical system-level tasks in the background.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',3,5,'2025-06-27_15-28-07','The kernel handles low-level operations such as stack allocation, thread management, and hardware control, and its code is kept separate in dedicated kernel files. In contrast, the user writes the application logic (like what each thread should do) in the main file. Users interact with the kernel through API calls, which are functions provided by the kernel that allow users to create threads, initialize the system, and start execution. This separation keeps the system well-organized: users don’t need to understand or modify the kernel\'s internal workings to write their programs, and the kernel remains protected from accidental changes. The kernel also manages memory and hardware access to ensure threads operate safely and independently, allowing users to focus on their applications while the kernel takes care of critical system-level tasks in the background.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',4,1,'2025-07-28_17-24-20','Round-robin scheduling can be effectively implemented using a queue by treating each thread control block (TCB) as an item in the queue. When a thread finishes its time slice or voluntarily yields, its TCB is removed from the front of the queue and reinserted at the rear, allowing the scheduler to always run the thread at the front. This structure naturally enforces a fair, cyclical order without needing to track indices, as would be necessary in an array-based approach. The main benefit of using a queue is its flexibility. It simplifies adding and removing threads dynamically at runtime and avoids manual index management, which can reduce bugs and improve code maintainability, especially as the system scales or when implementing features like blocking and unblocking threads.\n',0,'s25'),('u3hussai',4,1,'2025-07-28_23-06-51','Round-robin scheduling can be effectively implemented using a queue by treating each thread control block (TCB) as an item in the queue. When a thread finishes its time slice or voluntarily yields, its TCB is removed from the front of the queue and reinserted at the rear, allowing the scheduler to always run the thread at the front. This structure naturally enforces a fair, cyclical order without needing to track indices, as would be necessary in an array-based approach. The current scheduler in the lab already mimics a queue-like behavior using an array and an index that cycles through available threads in round-robin fashion. Switching to a true queue structure wouldn\'t drastically change the logic, but it would explicitly manage the order using enqueue and dequeue operations rather than index arithmetic. One potential downside of using a queue is the added overhead—pushing and popping TCBs from a data structure may introduce unnecessary complexity or cost, especially in constrained embedded systems. While a queue can make thread management (like blocking or dynamic insertion/removal) more flexible and expressive, that flexibility may not be needed for a small, fixed number of threads. So, while functionally similar, switching to a queue might not offer enough benefit to justify the added complexity, unless the system requires more advanced scheduling features.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',4,2,'2025-07-28_17-24-20','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s state and loading the next thread’s state. First, it reads the current stack pointer (PSP) and pushes registers R4–R11 onto the current thread\'s stack—these are the registers that aren\'t automatically saved during an exception. Then it calls the osSched() function, which updates the global pointer to the next thread’s stack. After returning from osSched(), the handler loads the saved R4–R11 values from the new thread\'s stack, updates the PSP to point to the new stack, and finally returns using BX LR, which causes the processor to resume execution of the new thread. This entire process allows the system to \"pause\" one thread and \"resume\" another, giving the illusion of multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',4,2,'2025-07-28_23-06-51','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s state and loading the next thread’s state. First, it reads the current stack pointer (PSP) and pushes registers R4–R11 onto the current thread\'s stack—these are the registers that aren\'t automatically saved during an exception. Then it calls the osSched() function, which updates the global pointer to the next thread’s stack. After returning from osSched(), the handler loads the saved R4–R11 values from the new thread\'s stack, updates the PSP to point to the new stack, and finally returns using BX LR, which causes the processor to resume execution of the new thread. This entire process allows the system to \"pause\" one thread and \"resume\" another, giving the illusion of multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',4,3,'2025-07-28_17-24-20','Changing the scheduler to always run the task with the highest priority can lead to a problem called starvation. If there\'s always a high-priority task ready to run, then lower-priority tasks may never get a chance to execute. This is especially bad in systems where all tasks are important for correct operation—like handling input, managing sensors, or updating a display. Even if a lower-priority task is critical for long-term system stability, it could be blocked forever by a constantly busy high-priority thread. Round-robin or priority-based scheduling with time-slicing ensures fairness and avoids this issue by giving every task some CPU time.\n',0,'s25'),('u3hussai',4,3,'2025-07-28_23-06-51','In a scheduler that always picks the highest-priority ready task to run, the logic explicitly prevents lower-priority threads from being selected as long as a higher-priority task is available. This behavior can cause starvation: if the high-priority task never blocks or finishes, the scheduler keeps choosing it over and over again, so lower-priority tasks are never scheduled. Unlike round-robin or fair time-sliced scheduling, this approach lacks any mechanism to ensure that all tasks eventually get CPU time. The issue isn’t just that starvation can happen—it’s that the scheduler is hardcoded to always prefer one task, making it possible for only one thread to run indefinitely while others are completely ignored, even if they are essential to system functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',4,4,'2025-07-28_17-24-20','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_Handler_Main from the assembly handlers makes the code much easier to read, write, and maintain. C is higher-level and more familiar to most developers, so it\'s quicker to implement and debug complex logic like scheduling or service call handling in C. It also allows better use of modern development tools like debuggers and static analyzers. However, this approach can introduce slight overhead from the function call itself, and it depends on the C compiler to generate correct and efficient code. Writing everything in assembly could be faster and give finer control over the hardware, but it would make the code much harder to understand, maintain, and port to other systems. So, using C for logic and assembly only for low-level tasks (like saving and restoring registers) strikes a good balance between performance and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',4,4,'2025-07-28_23-06-51','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_Handler_Main from the assembly handlers makes the code much easier to read, write, and maintain. C is higher-level and more familiar to most developers, so it\'s quicker to implement and debug complex logic like scheduling or service call handling in C. It also allows better use of modern development tools like debuggers and static analyzers. However, this approach can introduce slight overhead from the function call itself, and it depends on the C compiler to generate correct and efficient code. Writing everything in assembly could be faster and give finer control over the hardware, but it would make the code much harder to understand, maintain, and port to other systems. So, using C for logic and assembly only for low-level tasks (like saving and restoring registers) strikes a good balance between performance and maintainability.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',4,5,'2025-07-28_17-24-20','If you create only a single thread, like the print_continuously thread, and it yields after printing, the OS will still technically work, but the behavior will be a bit pointless. When the thread yields, the OS will perform a context switch using PendSV, but since there are no other threads to switch to, the scheduler will just pick the same thread again. This results in extra overhead (saving and restoring the same context), but the system will continue running without crashing. However, the whole point of yielding is to let other threads run, so with only one thread, the yield doesn\'t serve any real purpose. It\'s a valid test case to check that the OS handles switching correctly even with a minimal setup, but it doesn\'t demonstrate the benefits of multithreading.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',4,5,'2025-07-28_23-06-51','If you create only a single thread, like the print_continuously thread, and it yields after printing, the OS will still technically work, but the behavior will be a bit pointless. When the thread yields, the OS will perform a context switch using PendSV, but since there are no other threads to switch to, the scheduler will just pick the same thread again. This results in extra overhead (saving and restoring the same context), but the system will continue running without crashing. However, the whole point of yielding is to let other threads run, so with only one thread, the yield doesn\'t serve any real purpose. It\'s a valid test case to check that the OS handles switching correctly even with a minimal setup, but it doesn\'t demonstrate the benefits of multithreading.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',5,1,'2025-07-29_17-49-05','The user defines a thread function that takes a void* argument and prepares a variable (often a struct) to pass into the thread. They then call osCreateThread() (or similar), passing both the function and a pointer to the data. Inside the OS, during thread creation, the thread’s stack is initialized so that the pointer to the user’s data is placed into register R0’s position on the stack. When the thread starts running, the CPU loads this pointer into R0, and since the ARM calling convention passes the first argument in R0, the thread function receives the correct value. This works because the stack is preloaded to mimic what the processor expects when calling a normal function.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',5,2,'2025-07-29_17-49-05','You can\'t call osYield() inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, or SysTick_Handler because osYield() triggers an SVC exception, which is itself a context switch mechanism. Calling it inside another context-switching handler would cause nested exceptions or recursive switching, which can corrupt the stack, break control flow, or crash the system. These handlers are already running in exception mode, and calling another software interrupt from within them can result in undefined or unstable behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',5,3,'2025-07-29_17-49-05','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms means threads are preempted more frequently, allowing for more responsive multitasking and finer timing precision. However, this comes with trade-offs. More frequent interrupts increase CPU overhead from context switching, which reduces the time available for actual thread work. It can also lead to faster battery drain in embedded systems and higher risk of timing bugs. In most applications, 1ms is a good balance between responsiveness and efficiency.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',5,4,'2025-07-29_17-49-05','In cooperative multitasking, the user is responsible for calling osYield() to give other threads a chance to run. This gives the user more control but risks starvation if a thread forgets to yield. In preemptive multitasking, the OS uses a timer interrupt (like SysTick) to automatically switch threads after a time slice, without user intervention. For the OS, cooperative switching is simpler because it just swaps threads when asked. Preemptive switching requires the OS to track time slices, handle timer interrupts, and force a context switch when time runs out.\n',1,'s25'),('u3hussai',5,5,'2025-07-29_17-49-05','Both mechanisms ultimately rely on triggering the same context switch routine using PendSV. osYield() does it manually by setting a bit in the interrupt control register, while the timer (SysTick) does it automatically when a thread\'s time slice expires. Because they both lead to the same scheduler, they can be mixed: yielding threads give up control willingly, and non-yielding threads are switched out automatically after their slice ends. The OS simply decides when to trigger the switch, but the actual switch logic is shared.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',1,1,'2025-05-27_15-55-48','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) consists of a set of libraries that act as an intermediary between the STM32 microcontroller hardware and the software. It acts as the interface for the operating system to interface with the STM32 without needing to know the complexities of UAR, GPIO and timers. For the case of this lab, the HAL function was used to send a byte via UART by a single function call in the function HAL_UART_Transmit().\nThe HAL is essential for portability as it hides the low-level interface that is specific to each board or microcontroller, allowing for developers to write code that is independent of the hardware being used. This allows for software to be written without needing to know the specific details of each device.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',1,1,'2025-05-29_15-26-07','The HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer) consists of a set of libraries that act as an intermediary between the STM32 microcontroller hardware and the software. It acts as the interface for the operating system to interface with the STM32 without needing to know the complexities of UAR, GPIO and timers. For the case of this lab, the HAL function was used to send a byte via UART by a single function call in the function HAL_UART_Transmit().\nThe HAL is essential for portability as it hides the low-level interface that is specific to each board or microcontroller, allowing for developers to write code that is independent of the hardware being used. This allows for software to be written without needing to know the specific details of each device.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',1,2,'2025-05-27_15-55-48','The _io_putchar function is created to allow for the printf method to work with UART on the STM32 controller. As there exists no default output screen on the microcontroller, the _io _putchar method takes a single character and sends it over UART using the HAL_UART_Trasnmit by telling the printf where and how to send its output.\nThis method is needed as it is required to bridge communication between the high-levelk print and the low-level UART hardware, ultimately making it possible to use printf for debugging and displaying messages.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',1,2,'2025-05-29_15-26-07','The __io_putchar function is created to allow for the printf method to work with UART on the STM32 controller. As there exists no default output screen on the microcontroller, the __io_putchar method takes a single character and sends it over UART using the HAL_UART_Transmit, effectively telling printf where and how to send its output. This method is needed as it is required to bridge communication between the high-level printf function and the low-level UART hardware, ultimately making it possible to use printf for debugging and displaying messages. Moreover, __io_putchar must be implemented by the programmer because the printf function does not inherently know how to interact with the specific hardware on a microcontroller. Its implementation will vary depending on the communication protocol being used. For example, if SPI were used instead of UART, the __io_putchar function would need to be rewritten to send characters through the SPI transmit buffer using the appropriate HAL or low-level SPI functions. This highlights the programmer’s role in adapting printf to match the hardware setup of the platform, ensuring compatibility and proper communication regardless of the underlying protocol.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',1,3,'2025-05-27_15-55-48','The debugger was used to observe values like the stack pointer (MSP/PSP) since they could not be determined using the printf alone. This is important so that the actual value of the stack can be monitored as the program runs by setting breakpoints at specific lines of code to trace exactly what ios happening.\nThe printf may also not be suitable in the early stages of program execution if the function is not fully initialized. This would result in the function crashing the system or giving incorrect results.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',1,3,'2025-05-29_15-26-07','The debugger was used to observe values like the stack pointer (MSP/PSP) since they could not be determined using the printf alone. This is important so that the actual value of the stack can be monitored as the program runs by setting breakpoints at specific lines of code to trace exactly what ios happening.\nThe printf may also not be suitable in the early stages of program execution if the function is not fully initialized. This would result in the function crashing the system or giving incorrect results.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',1,4,'2025-05-27_15-55-48','We first retrieve the initial valure of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) using the line in our program  ‘uint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0;’ which casts the address 0x0 into a pointer to a pointer to a 32-bit unsigned integer. Since we follow with dereferencing the doubler pointer, we get the valuer stored at address 0x0, which is the initial MSP address at the top of the stack at startup.\nThis is followed by computing a new value for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by going down in memory from the MSP using the following line: uint32_t PSP_val = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL – 0x400; which creates a new stack region of 1024 bytes below the MSP.\nWe need to know the initial MSP location as it lets users know the top of available stack memory which we can then create a new stack region below the MSP by subtracting a known amount.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',1,4,'2025-05-29_15-26-07','We first retrieve the initial valure of the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) using the line in our program  ‘uint32_t* MSP_INIT_VAL = *(uint32_t**)0x0;’ which casts the address 0x0 into a pointer to a pointer to a 32-bit unsigned integer. Since we follow with dereferencing the doubler pointer, we get the valuer stored at address 0x0, which is the initial MSP address at the top of the stack at startup.\nThis is followed by computing a new value for the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) by going down in memory from the MSP using the following line: uint32_t PSP_val = (uint32_t)MSP_INIT_VAL – 0x400; which creates a new stack region of 1024 bytes below the MSP.\nWe need to know the initial MSP location as it lets users know the top of available stack memory which we can then create a new stack region below the MSP by subtracting a known amount.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',1,5,'2025-05-27_15-55-48','To use the same stack allocation method to allocate a number of new stacks for use in multiple threads, we would need to first define the size of you main stack size, the thread stack size and the total number of threads. Since we know that the MSP will point to the top of the available stack memory and know its stack size, the thread’s memory would start immediately after the main stack ends. The offsets in memory for each thread can be computed by starting from the top of the memory MSP), skipping over the main stack, followed by skipping the space taken up by the previous thread stacks. Mathematically, the i-th thread stack memory would be determine by adding the MSP initial value and main stack size to the (i-1) * thread size.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',1,5,'2025-05-29_15-26-07','To use the same stack allocation method to allocate a number of new stacks for use in multiple threads, we would need to first define the size of the main stack, the thread stack size, and the total number of threads. Since we know that the MSP will point to the top of the available stack memory and know its stack size, the thread’s memory would start immediately after the main stack ends. The offsets in memory for each thread can be computed by starting from the top of the memory (MSP), skipping over the main stack, followed by skipping the space taken up by the previous thread stacks. Mathematically, the i-th thread stack memory would be determined by adding the MSP initial value and main stack size to the (i - 1) × thread size. To make sure this works correctly, each stack address should be aligned properly in memory—usually to an 8- or 16-byte boundary. This avoids issues with how the processor accesses memory. We also need to make sure the stacks don’t overlap with each other or with other parts of memory. Finally, we must check that there’s enough total RAM to hold the main stack and all the thread stacks—if not, it could cause serious errors or crashes.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,1,'2025-06-04_14-42-37','When changing the Program Counter to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor began executing the function normally, as if it had been called normally. This is the case as the PC register tells the processor where to get the next instruction from. Hence, by setting the program counter to the address of the function print_ continuously, the execution of the function was redirected which is why the console outputted an infinite loop inside print_ continuously which prints “Hello PC”. This demonstrates that the Program Counter directly can control which code the processor runs.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,1,'2025-06-04_14-54-27','When changing the Program Counter to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor began executing the function normally, as if it had been called normally. This is the case as the PC register tells the processor where to get the next instruction from. Hence, by setting the program counter to the address of the function print_ continuously, the execution of the function was redirected which is why the console outputted an infinite loop inside print_ continuously which prints “Hello PC”. This demonstrates that the Program Counter directly can control which code the processor runs.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,1,'2025-06-04_15-05-37','When changing the Program Counter to hold the function pointer to my print continuously function, the processor began executing the function normally, as if it had been called normally. This is the case as the PC register tells the processor where to get the next instruction from. Hence, by setting the program counter to the address of the function print_ continuously, the execution of the function was redirected which is why the console outputted an infinite loop inside print_ continuously which prints “Hello PC”. This demonstrates that the Program Counter directly can control which code the processor runs.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,2,'2025-06-04_14-42-37','A system call is a method for which the program can request a service from the operating system. As the user-level code does not have direct access to some of the hardware or operations, it calls the OS to do the specified requested task. For the case of this lab, the supervisor call (SVC) instruction was used to trigger the system call which then jumped into a specific handler function that performed the designated task based on the system call number that was passed in.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,2,'2025-06-04_14-54-27','A system call is a method for which the program can request a service from the operating system. As the user-level code does not have direct access to some of the hardware or operations, it calls the OS to do the specified requested task. For the case of this lab, the supervisor call (SVC) instruction was used to trigger the system call which then jumped into a specific handler function that performed the designated task based on the system call number that was passed in.\nMore precisely, a system call is a controlled interface that allows user-mode applications to safely access kernel-level services. It acts as a gateway between user programs and the operating system kernel, enforcing protection while enabling necessary operations like I/O, memory management, and process control. This mechanism ensures that user applications cannot directly manipulate hardware or critical resources, preserving system stability and security. In this lab, the system call mechanism demonstrated how transitions between user and privileged modes are managed, highlighting the OS\'s role in mediating access to protected functionality.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,2,'2025-06-04_15-05-37','A system call is a special type of function call that allows a program to request a service from the operating system. As the user-level code does not have direct access to some of the hardware or operations, it calls the OS to do the specified requested task. This function call serves as a controlled entry point into the kernel, allowing the user program to execute privileged operations—like I/O, memory management, or process creation—safely and securely.\nFor the case of this lab, the supervisor call (SVC) instruction was used to trigger the system call, which then jumped into a specific handler function that performed the designated task based on the system call number that was passed in. This is the mechanism used to implement the system call, but the concept itself refers to the high-level interface that enables user programs to access kernel services.\nMore precisely, a system call is a controlled interface that allows user-mode applications to safely access kernel-level services. It acts as a gateway between user programs and the operating system kernel, enforcing protection while enabling necessary operations. This mechanism ensures that user applications cannot directly manipulate hardware or critical resources, preserving system stability and security. In this lab, the system call mechanism demonstrated how transitions between user and privileged modes are managed, highlighting the OS\'s role in mediating access to protected functionality.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,3,'2025-06-04_14-42-37','Setting up a thread’s stack refers to allocating a specified segment of the memory space to the thread when it is called. In order for the thread to work as expected, the stack must be filled with the values the CPU expects to pop into the registers which include the Program Counter (PC), xPSR (status register) as well as the general-purpose registers. Storing register values on the stack is necessary because when the thread is started through the system call, the CPU expects to restore the thread’s \"context\" by popping these values off the stack. This tricks the CPU into thinking it had previously paused the thread and is now resuming it, even though we’re actually starting it for the first time.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,3,'2025-06-04_14-54-27','Setting up a thread’s stack refers to allocating a specified segment of the memory space to the thread when it is called. In order for the thread to work as expected, the stack must be filled with the values the CPU expects to pop into the registers which include the Program Counter (PC), xPSR (status register) as well as the general-purpose registers. Storing register values on the stack is necessary because when the thread is started through the system call, the CPU expects to restore the thread’s \"context\" by popping these values off the stack. This tricks the CPU into thinking it had previously paused the thread and is now resuming it, even though we’re actually starting it for the first time.\nImportantly, the values must be stored in a specific order, following the exception return frame structure defined by the ARM Cortex-M architecture. This includes placing the xPSR, PC, and LR at the correct locations, followed by R12, R3, R2, R1, and R0 in that exact sequence. Any deviation from this order could lead to incorrect behavior or faults when the CPU attempts to restore the context. The stack layout must match what the CPU expects during exception return so that the thread begins execution with the correct register values, mimicking a legitimate context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,3,'2025-06-04_15-05-37','Setting up a thread’s stack refers to allocating a specified segment of the memory space to the thread when it is called. In order for the thread to work as expected, the stack must be filled with the values the CPU expects to pop into the registers which include the Program Counter (PC), xPSR (status register) as well as the general-purpose registers. Storing register values on the stack is necessary because when the thread is started through the system call, the CPU expects to restore the thread’s \"context\" by popping these values off the stack. This tricks the CPU into thinking it had previously paused the thread and is now resuming it, even though we’re actually starting it for the first time.\nImportantly, the values must be stored in a specific order, following the exception return frame structure defined by the ARM Cortex-M architecture. This includes placing the xPSR, PC, and LR at the correct locations, followed by R12, R3, R2, R1, and R0 in that exact sequence. Any deviation from this order could lead to incorrect behavior or faults when the CPU attempts to restore the context. The stack layout must match what the CPU expects during exception return so that the thread begins execution with the correct register values, mimicking a legitimate context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,4,'2025-06-04_14-42-37','If we didn’t set the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) using __set_PSP(stackptr) before running the thread, the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread’s stack is located. When the system call returns and tries to pop values from the stack to restore the thread’s context (like PC, xPSR, and the general-purpose registers), it would use an uninitialized or incorrect stack pointer. This would cause the CPU to read garbage memory or access restricted areas, leading to invalid instructions or memory faults. As a result, the processor would trigger a hard fault. Setting the PSP ensures the CPU has the correct address to use for the thread’s stack, so when it pops the saved register values, it does so safely and correctly, allowing the thread to start without crashing.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,4,'2025-06-04_14-54-27','If we didn’t set the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) using __set_PSP(stackptr) before running the thread, the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread’s stack is located. When the system call returns and tries to pop values from the stack to restore the thread’s context (like PC, xPSR, and the general-purpose registers), it would use an uninitialized or incorrect stack pointer. This would cause the CPU to read garbage memory or access restricted areas, leading to invalid instructions or memory faults. As a result, the processor would trigger a hard fault. Setting the PSP ensures the CPU has the correct address to use for the thread’s stack, so when it pops the saved register values, it does so safely and correctly, allowing the thread to start without crashing.\nMore specifically, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) by default during exception handling, including when the SVC handler runs. However, each thread is meant to operate using its own stack, pointed to by the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is typically located in a different memory region. If we fail to explicitly switch from MSP to PSP, the thread will not use its own stack, and instead the CPU will attempt to restore context using the wrong stack location—leading to a hard fault. Explicitly setting the PSP ensures that the CPU switches to the thread’s designated stack before returning from the exception.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,4,'2025-06-04_15-05-37','If we didn’t set the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) using __set_PSP(stackptr) before running the thread, the CPU wouldn’t know where the thread’s stack is located. When the system call returns and tries to pop values from the stack to restore the thread’s context (like PC, xPSR, and the general-purpose registers), it would use an uninitialized or incorrect stack pointer. This would cause the CPU to read garbage memory or access restricted areas, leading to invalid instructions or memory faults. As a result, the processor would trigger a hard fault. Setting the PSP ensures the CPU has the correct address to use for the thread’s stack, so when it pops the saved register values, it does so safely and correctly, allowing the thread to start without crashing.\nMore specifically, the CPU uses the Main Stack Pointer (MSP) by default during exception handling, including when the SVC handler runs. However, each thread is meant to operate using its own stack, pointed to by the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is typically located in a different memory region. If we fail to explicitly switch from MSP to PSP, the thread will not use its own stack, and instead the CPU will attempt to restore context using the wrong stack location—leading to a hard fault. Explicitly setting the PSP ensures that the CPU switches to the thread’s designated stack before returning from the exception.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,5,'2025-06-04_14-42-37','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously and instead printed something once and then returned, the system would likely crash or end up in an undefined state. 3This is because when the thread returns, it tries to return to whatever address was in the Link Register (LR) when it was set up on the thread’s stack. In our lab setup, we manually filled the LR (and other registers) with arbitrary dummy values like 0xA. Since 0xA is not a valid return address (it doesn\'t point to meaningful code), the CPU would try to jump to an invalid location, causing a hard fault or other critical failure.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,5,'2025-06-04_14-54-27','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously and instead printed something once and then returned, the system would likely crash or end up in an undefined state. 3This is because when the thread returns, it tries to return to whatever address was in the Link Register (LR) when it was set up on the thread’s stack. In our lab setup, we manually filled the LR (and other registers) with arbitrary dummy values like 0xA. Since 0xA is not a valid return address (it doesn\'t point to meaningful code), the CPU would try to jump to an invalid location, causing a hard fault or other critical failure.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',2,5,'2025-06-04_15-05-37','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously and instead printed something once and then returned, the system would likely crash or end up in an undefined state. 3This is because when the thread returns, it tries to return to whatever address was in the Link Register (LR) when it was set up on the thread’s stack. In our lab setup, we manually filled the LR (and other registers) with arbitrary dummy values like 0xA. Since 0xA is not a valid return address (it doesn\'t point to meaningful code), the CPU would try to jump to an invalid location, causing a hard fault or other critical failure.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,1,'2025-06-25_15-20-26','The process begins by compiling each .c file separately into an object file (.o). This step translates human-readable C code into machine-level instructions. However, at this stage, each file is still unaware of the others because they have not yet been linked. The next step is linking, where the linker combines all the object files into a single executable. During this process, the linker resolves symbol references between files, for example, connecting function calls in one file to their definitions in another.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,1,'2025-06-26_14-51-40','The process begins by compiling each .c file separately into an object file (.o). This step translates human-readable C code into machine-level instructions. However, at this stage, each file is still unaware of the others because they have not yet been linked. Before compiling, though, each source file goes through preprocessing, where directives like #include and #define are handled. For example, header files are copied in, and macros are expanded during this stage. After preprocessing and compilation, the linker combines all the object files into a single executable. During this process, the linker resolves symbol references between files, such as connecting function calls in one file to their definitions in another. The order of these steps consisting of preprocessing, compiling, and then linking are essential for generating a correct and functional program.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,1,'2025-06-26_16-06-57','The process begins by compiling each .c file separately into an object file (.o). This step translates human-readable C code into machine-level instructions. However, at this stage, each file is still unaware of the others because they have not yet been linked. Before compiling, though, each source file goes through preprocessing, where directives like #include and #define are handled. For example, header files are copied in, and macros are expanded during this stage. After preprocessing and compilation, the linker combines all the object files into a single executable. During this process, the linker resolves symbol references between files, such as connecting function calls in one file to their definitions in another. The order of these steps consisting of preprocessing, compiling, and then linking are essential for generating a correct and functional program.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,1,'2025-06-26_18-08-28','The process begins by compiling each .c file separately into an object file (.o). This step translates human-readable C code into machine-level instructions. However, at this stage, each file is still unaware of the others because they have not yet been linked. The next step is linking, where the linker combines all the object files into a single executable. During this process, the linker resolves symbol references between files, for example, connecting function calls in one file to their definitions in another.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,1,'2025-06-26_19-16-41','To compile multiple .c files into a single executable, the process involves three key stages: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First, each .c file is sent to the preprocessor, which handles directives such as #include, #define, and #ifdef. For example, #include replaces the directive with the entire contents of the specified header file, and #define substitutes symbolic constants or macros with their values. This step generates a complete, expanded version of the source code that is ready for compilation. Preprocessing is necessary because it ensures that all external declarations, constants, and macros are correctly in place before the compiler translates the code into machine instructions. After preprocessing, the compiler translates each file into a corresponding object file (.o), which contains machine code. However, these object files are still unaware of each other. In the final linking stage, the linker combines all the object files into a single executable and resolves cross-file references—such as linking a function call in one file to its definition in another. This complete pipeline transforms separate .c files into a functional, unified program.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,2,'2025-06-25_15-20-26','Using #include <kernel.c> or including any .c file directly is a bad idea because it disrupts the proper structure of C programs. In C, each .c file is meant to be compiled separately into an object file, and then linked together by the linker. When you include a .c file, you\'re essentially copying its entire contents into another file, which can lead to duplicate definitions, symbol conflicts, and compilation errors. It also breaks the separation between interface and implementation, making your code harder to maintain. Instead, you should include header (.h) files for function declarations and compile each .c file individually before linking.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,2,'2025-06-26_14-51-40','Using #include <kernel.c> or including any .c file directly is a bad idea because it disrupts the proper structure of C programs. In C, each .c file is meant to be compiled separately into an object file, and then linked together by the linker. When you include a .c file, you\'re essentially copying its entire contents into another file, which can lead to duplicate definitions, symbol conflicts, and compilation errors. It also breaks the separation between interface and implementation, making your code harder to maintain. Instead, you should include header (.h) files for function declarations and compile each .c file individually before linking.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,2,'2025-06-26_16-06-57','Using #include <kernel.c> or including any .c file directly is a bad idea because it disrupts the proper structure of C programs. In C, each .c file is meant to be compiled separately into an object file, and then linked together by the linker. When you include a .c file, you\'re essentially copying its entire contents into another file, which can lead to duplicate definitions, symbol conflicts, and compilation errors. It also breaks the separation between interface and implementation, making your code harder to maintain. Instead, you should include header (.h) files for function declarations and compile each .c file individually before linking.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,2,'2025-06-26_18-08-28','Using #include <kernel.c> or including any .c file directly is a bad idea because it disrupts the proper structure of C programs. In C, each .c file is meant to be compiled separately into an object file, and then linked together by the linker. When you include a .c file, you\'re essentially copying its entire contents into another file, which can lead to duplicate definitions, symbol conflicts, and compilation errors. It also breaks the separation between interface and implementation, making your code harder to maintain. Instead, you should include header (.h) files for function declarations and compile each .c file individually before linking.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,2,'2025-06-26_19-16-41','Using #include <kernel.c> or including any .c file directly is a bad idea because it disrupts the proper structure of C programs. In C, each .c file is meant to be compiled separately into an object file, and then linked together by the linker. When you include a .c file, you\'re essentially copying its entire contents into another file, which can lead to duplicate definitions, symbol conflicts, and compilation errors. It also breaks the separation between interface and implementation, making your code harder to maintain. Instead, you should include header (.h) files for function declarations and compile each .c file individually before linking.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,3,'2025-06-25_15-20-26','To set up and start a thread, the user begins by initializing the kernel. This step prepares the internal data structures needed to manage threads, such as stack allocation and thread descriptors. Once the kernel is initialized, the user can create a thread by providing a pointer to the function they want the thread to execute. This function is then associated with its own stack and stored in a global thread structure. After the thread is created, the user starts the kernel, which makes a system call to switch the processor’s context to the new thread by updating the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). From that point onward, the processor begins executing the thread function, and the thread runs independently as part of the system.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,3,'2025-06-26_14-51-40','To set up and start a thread, the user begins by calling a specific initialization function, usually named osKernelInitialize. This function sets up the internal data structures needed by the kernel, such as preparing the global stack pool and resetting thread tracking variables. Once the kernel is initialized, the user proceeds to create a thread by calling another function, typically osCreateThread, and passing a pointer to the function they want the thread to execute. This step allocates a stack for the thread, sets up the initial context, and stores the function pointer in a global thread descriptor. After successfully creating the thread, the user then starts the kernel using a third function call, commonly named osKernelStart. This function triggers a system call (using the SVC instruction) which causes the processor to switch from the main context to the thread context by updating the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). These operations are not automatic as each step must be explicitly triggered by the user through these specific function calls in the correct order. The mechanism that enables the kernel to perform privileged operations like context switching is the use of software interrupts (SVC), which trap into the kernel to perform thread-related setup in a controlled and secure manner.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,3,'2025-06-26_16-06-57','To set up and start a thread, the user begins by calling a specific initialization function, usually named osKernelInitialize. This function sets up the internal data structures needed by the kernel, such as preparing the global stack pool and resetting thread tracking variables. Once the kernel is initialized, the user proceeds to create a thread by calling another function, typically osCreateThread, and passing a pointer to the function they want the thread to execute. This step allocates a stack for the thread, sets up the initial context, and stores the function pointer in a global thread descriptor. After successfully creating the thread, the user then starts the kernel using a third function call, commonly named osKernelStart. This function triggers a system call (using the SVC instruction) which causes the processor to switch from the main context to the thread context by updating the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). These operations are not automatic as each step must be explicitly triggered by the user through these specific function calls in the correct order. The mechanism that enables the kernel to perform privileged operations like context switching is the use of software interrupts (SVC), which trap into the kernel to perform thread-related setup in a controlled and secure manner.\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,3,'2025-06-26_18-08-28','To set up and start a thread, the user must follow a clear sequence of steps that are triggered through specific function calls. First and most importantly, the user must write the thread function themselves—this means authoring a C function that defines the exact behavior the thread will perform when it starts running. This is not just about referencing or passing a function; the function must be explicitly written by the user to suit the needs of their application. Without this step, there is no code for the thread to execute, so it is a critical part of the thread creation process. After writing the function, the user must call osKernelInitialize to prepare the kernel’s internal data structures, such as setting up stack pools and thread metadata. Then, the user calls osCreateThread, passing a pointer to the thread function they wrote. This function sets up a new thread by allocating a stack and associating it with the function pointer in a global thread structure. Finally, the user must call osKernelStart to begin execution. This function triggers a system call (via an SVC instruction) that causes the processor to switch context to the new thread by updating the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). From this point forward, the processor executes the user-defined function as an independent thread. Each of these steps—writing the function, creating the thread, and starting the kernel—must be performed manually by the user. These are not automatic; they are intentional and necessary actions that tell the system what work to perform and how to execute it in a threaded context.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,3,'2025-06-26_19-16-41','To set up and start a thread, the user must follow a clear sequence of steps that are triggered through specific function calls. First and most importantly, the user must write the thread function themselves—this means authoring a C function that defines the exact behavior the thread will perform when it starts running. This is not just about referencing or passing a function; the function must be explicitly written by the user to suit the needs of their application. Without this step, there is no code for the thread to execute, so it is a critical part of the thread creation process. After writing the function, the user must call osKernelInitialize to prepare the kernel’s internal data structures, such as setting up stack pools and thread metadata. Then, the user calls osCreateThread, passing a pointer to the thread function they wrote. This function sets up a new thread by allocating a stack and associating it with the function pointer in a global thread structure. Finally, the user must call osKernelStart to begin execution. This function triggers a system call (via an SVC instruction) that causes the processor to switch context to the new thread by updating the Process Stack Pointer (PSP). From this point forward, the processor executes the user-defined function as an independent thread. Each of these steps—writing the function, creating the thread, and starting the kernel—must be performed manually by the user. These are not automatic; they are intentional and necessary actions that tell the system what work to perform and how to execute it in a threaded context.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,4,'2025-06-25_15-20-26','The stack pool is a contiguous block of memory reserved specifically for allocating stacks to individual threads. Since each thread needs its own private stack to store local variables, function return addresses, and register states during context switches, the stack pool provides a centralized and organized space to manage these resources. In future labs, when we implement support for multiple threads, the stack pool will allow the kernel to assign separate stack regions to each new thread. By dividing the pool into fixed-size chunks, we can quickly allocate and deallocate stacks, enabling multiple threads to run independently without interfering with each other\'s memory. This separation is critical for maintaining thread isolation and ensuring the reliability of the operating system’s scheduling and execution model.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,4,'2025-06-26_14-51-40','The stack pool is a contiguous block of memory reserved specifically for allocating stacks to individual threads. Since each thread needs its own private stack to store local variables, function return addresses, and register states during context switches, the stack pool provides a centralized and organized space to manage these resources. In future labs, when we implement support for multiple threads, the stack pool will allow the kernel to assign separate stack regions to each new thread. By dividing the pool into fixed-size chunks, we can quickly allocate and deallocate stacks, enabling multiple threads to run independently without interfering with each other\'s memory. This separation is critical for maintaining thread isolation and ensuring the reliability of the operating system’s scheduling and execution model.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,4,'2025-06-26_16-06-57','The stack pool is a contiguous block of memory reserved specifically for allocating stacks to individual threads. Since each thread needs its own private stack to store local variables, function return addresses, and register states during context switches, the stack pool provides a centralized and organized space to manage these resources. In future labs, when we implement support for multiple threads, the stack pool will allow the kernel to assign separate stack regions to each new thread. By dividing the pool into fixed-size chunks, we can quickly allocate and deallocate stacks, enabling multiple threads to run independently without interfering with each other\'s memory. This separation is critical for maintaining thread isolation and ensuring the reliability of the operating system’s scheduling and execution model.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,4,'2025-06-26_18-08-28','The stack pool is a contiguous block of memory reserved specifically for allocating stacks to individual threads. Since each thread needs its own private stack to store local variables, function return addresses, and register states during context switches, the stack pool provides a centralized and organized space to manage these resources. In future labs, when we implement support for multiple threads, the stack pool will allow the kernel to assign separate stack regions to each new thread. By dividing the pool into fixed-size chunks, we can quickly allocate and deallocate stacks, enabling multiple threads to run independently without interfering with each other\'s memory. This separation is critical for maintaining thread isolation and ensuring the reliability of the operating system’s scheduling and execution model.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,4,'2025-06-26_19-16-41','The stack pool is a contiguous block of memory reserved specifically for allocating stacks to individual threads. Since each thread needs its own private stack to store local variables, function return addresses, and register states during context switches, the stack pool provides a centralized and organized space to manage these resources. In future labs, when we implement support for multiple threads, the stack pool will allow the kernel to assign separate stack regions to each new thread. By dividing the pool into fixed-size chunks, we can quickly allocate and deallocate stacks, enabling multiple threads to run independently without interfering with each other\'s memory. This separation is critical for maintaining thread isolation and ensuring the reliability of the operating system’s scheduling and execution model.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,5,'2025-06-25_15-20-26','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” is important for separating responsibilities in an operating system. From a file organization perspective, the “user” code goes into main.c, where the user writes application-specific logic like creating threads and calling system services. On the other hand, the “kernel” code resides in kernel.c and contains the low-level implementation of how the system manages threads, memory, and system calls. This separation is crucial because it enforces a clear boundary: the user interacts with high-level APIs without needing to understand or access the internal workings of the kernel. It also allows the kernel to maintain control over critical system behavior, ensuring stability and security while giving users the flexibility to build their applications safely on top of it.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,5,'2025-06-26_14-51-40','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” is important for separating responsibilities in an operating system. From a file organization perspective, the “user” code goes into main.c, where the user writes application-specific logic like creating threads and calling system services. On the other hand, the “kernel” code resides in kernel.c and contains the low-level implementation of how the system manages threads, memory, and system calls. This separation is crucial because it enforces a clear boundary: the user interacts with high-level APIs without needing to understand or access the internal workings of the kernel. It also allows the kernel to maintain control over critical system behavior, ensuring stability and security while giving users the flexibility to build their applications safely on top of it.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,5,'2025-06-26_16-06-57','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” is important for separating responsibilities in an operating system. From a file organization perspective, the “user” code goes into main.c, where the user writes application-specific logic like creating threads and calling system services. On the other hand, the “kernel” code resides in kernel.c and contains the low-level implementation of how the system manages threads, memory, and system calls. This separation is crucial because it enforces a clear boundary: the user interacts with high-level APIs without needing to understand or access the internal workings of the kernel. It also allows the kernel to maintain control over critical system behavior, ensuring stability and security while giving users the flexibility to build their applications safely on top of it.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,5,'2025-06-26_18-08-28','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” is important for separating responsibilities in an operating system. From a file organization perspective, the “user” code goes into main.c, where the user writes application-specific logic like creating threads and calling system services. On the other hand, the “kernel” code resides in kernel.c and contains the low-level implementation of how the system manages threads, memory, and system calls. This separation is crucial because it enforces a clear boundary: the user interacts with high-level APIs without needing to understand or access the internal workings of the kernel. It also allows the kernel to maintain control over critical system behavior, ensuring stability and security while giving users the flexibility to build their applications safely on top of it.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',3,5,'2025-06-26_19-16-41','The distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” is important for separating responsibilities in an operating system. From a file organization perspective, the “user” code goes into main.c, where the user writes application-specific logic like creating threads and calling system services. On the other hand, the “kernel” code resides in kernel.c and contains the low-level implementation of how the system manages threads, memory, and system calls. This separation is crucial because it enforces a clear boundary: the user interacts with high-level APIs without needing to understand or access the internal workings of the kernel. It also allows the kernel to maintain control over critical system behavior, ensuring stability and security while giving users the flexibility to build their applications safely on top of it.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',4,1,'2025-07-02_18-50-15','Using a queue to store Thread Control Blocks (TCBs) for round-robin scheduling offers a more structured and flexible alternative to using a simple array. In the array-based approach, each thread is stored in a fixed index, and the scheduler cycles through them using a modulo operation on an index variable. While this method is straightforward, it lacks the dynamic capabilities that a queue can offer. With a queue, the threads can be managed in a first-in, first-out (FIFO) manner. When a thread yields, it is dequeued from the front and re-enqueued at the back of the queue, making the next thread in line the one to run. This design more naturally models the round-robin concept and makes the scheduler logic clearer.\nThe main advantage of using a queue is that it allows for easier expansion to more advanced scheduling features. For instance, threads can be temporarily removed from the queue if they are blocked or waiting on a resource, and reinserted when they are ready to run again—something that\'s more cumbersome with a static array. Additionally, if you wanted to implement priority-based scheduling later, a priority queue structure could be introduced without major changes to the scheduler\'s core behavior.\n',0,'s25');
INSERT INTO `question_responses` VALUES ('v29mehta',4,1,'2025-07-02_20-41-02','Using a queue to store Thread Control Blocks (TCBs) for round-robin scheduling offers a more structured and flexible alternative to using a simple array. In the array-based approach, each thread is stored in a fixed index, and the scheduler cycles through them using a modulo operation on an index variable. While this method is straightforward, it lacks the dynamic capabilities that a queue can offer. With a queue, the threads can be managed in a first-in, first-out (FIFO) manner. When a thread yields, it is dequeued from the front and re-enqueued at the back, making the next thread in line the one to run. This behavior directly models the round-robin idea and keeps the scheduler logic cleaner and more intuitive.\nThe lab likely describes the queue-based approach as “interesting” because it opens the door to more realistic and scalable OS behaviors. For example, it allows threads to be temporarily removed from the queue when they are blocked (e.g., waiting for I/O or a resource), and reinserted when they become ready—something that is more difficult to manage with a fixed-size array. Additionally, queues abstract away the index management required in arrays, making the code easier to maintain and extend. If the OS is later upgraded to support different scheduling strategies, such as priority-based scheduling, the queue structure can be swapped with a priority queue or another data structure, without rewriting the core logic of the scheduler. Overall, using a queue not only simplifies thread management but also prepares the system for future enhancements with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',4,2,'2025-07-02_18-50-15','The PendSV_Handler function is a special interrupt service routine designed to handle context switching between threads in an ARM Cortex-M system. When a thread calls osYield(), the system triggers the PendSV interrupt, which causes PendSV_Handler to run—but only after all higher-priority interrupts (like SVC) are finished. This ensures the context switch happens safely.\nInside PendSV_Handler, the CPU first saves the current thread’s state. Specifically, it stores the values of the non-automatically saved registers (R4–R11) from the current thread onto its stack. This ensures that when the thread is resumed later, it can pick up exactly where it left off. After saving the context, the handler calls the osSched() function, which is written in C. osSched() updates the stack pointer to point to the next thread in the ready list (e.g., using a round-robin scheduler), effectively choosing the next thread to run.\nOnce the next thread is selected, PendSV_Handler finishes by restoring the new thread’s saved registers (R4–R11) from its stack and updating the CPU’s Process Stack Pointer (PSP). The function then exits the interrupt using a special return sequence that automatically restores the remaining registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR) from the new thread’s stack. As a result, the CPU resumes execution as if the new thread had been running all along. This seamless switch between thread contexts is what allows multitasking to work\n',0,'s25'),('v29mehta',4,2,'2025-07-02_20-41-02','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the state of the currently running thread and restoring the state of the next thread selected to run. This process starts by reading the Process Stack Pointer (PSP), which is essential because it tells the kernel where the current thread’s stack is located in memory. Without first reading the PSP, the system would not know where to save the current thread’s context (registers R4–R11) or how to restore it later. This initial step ensures that the thread’s state is preserved correctly.\nAfter saving the non-automatically stored registers (R4–R11) to the current thread\'s stack, the handler calls osSched(), a C function responsible for selecting the next thread to run—often using a round-robin or queue-based approach. osSched() updates the global stack pointer to point to the next thread’s stack, which was also previously set up using PSP.\nOnce the next thread is selected, PendSV_Handler loads that thread’s context by restoring R4–R11 from its stack and writing the new stack address to PSP. Finally, when the interrupt returns, the hardware automatically restores the remaining registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR), completing the switch. As a result, the processor resumes execution in the new thread as if it had never stopped. Explicitly reading and writing PSP is fundamental to correctly transitioning between threads in ARM Cortex-M4 systems.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',4,3,'2025-07-02_18-50-15','Modifying the OS to always choose the task with the highest priority sounds appealing at first—it ensures that the most \"important\" task runs as soon as possible. However, in practice, this approach leads to a major problem known as starvation. Starvation occurs when lower-priority tasks are continually ignored or never scheduled because there is always a higher-priority task ready to run.\nIn this strict priority-based system, if a high-priority thread is constantly ready (e.g., it\'s in a loop or frequently becomes ready again), lower-priority threads may never get CPU time, even if they are critical for the system\'s overall function, like handling input or producing output. This breaks fairness and can cause essential but lower-priority tasks to fail entirely or get delayed indefinitely. Additionally, it makes system behavior unpredictable and difficult to debug, especially if certain parts of the code never get executed.\nA more balanced approach is to use priority-based scheduling with aging or a real-time scheduler that ensures all threads eventually get CPU time. Round-robin or cooperative scheduling is simple but fair, ensuring all threads make progress. Always picking the highest-priority task without any fairness mechanism sacrifices this balance and makes the system unsuitable for multitasking environments.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',4,3,'2025-07-02_20-41-02','Modifying the OS to always choose the task with the highest priority sounds appealing at first—it ensures that the most \"important\" task runs as soon as possible. However, in practice, this approach leads to a major problem known as starvation. Starvation occurs when lower-priority tasks are continually ignored or never scheduled because there is always a higher-priority task ready to run.\nIn this strict priority-based system, if a high-priority thread is constantly ready (e.g., it\'s in a loop or frequently becomes ready again), lower-priority threads may never get CPU time, even if they are critical for the system\'s overall function, like handling input or producing output. This breaks fairness and can cause essential but lower-priority tasks to fail entirely or get delayed indefinitely. Additionally, it makes system behavior unpredictable and difficult to debug, especially if certain parts of the code never get executed.\nA more balanced approach is to use priority-based scheduling with aging or a real-time scheduler that ensures all threads eventually get CPU time. Round-robin or cooperative scheduling is simple but fair, ensuring all threads make progress. Always picking the highest-priority task without any fairness mechanism sacrifices this balance and makes the system unsuitable for multitasking environments.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',4,4,'2025-07-02_18-50-15','Calling C functions like osSched in PendSV_Handler and SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler offers several important benefits compared to writing everything purely in assembly. The primary advantage is readability and maintainability. C code is easier to understand, write, and modify, especially for high-level logic like selecting the next thread to run or handling different system calls. Most programmers are more familiar with C than assembly, so keeping as much logic as possible in C reduces the learning curve and the likelihood of introducing hard-to-find bugs.\nAnother benefit is portability. C code is much easier to reuse or adapt across different systems and architectures, while assembly code is specific to the CPU\'s instruction set and often even to the compiler or toolchain. This makes a C-based implementation more flexible and future-proof.\nHowever, there are downsides. Calling a C function from assembly introduces overhead—the compiler may generate extra instructions to manage the function call (such as saving registers or managing the stack), which can make the context switch slower or more complex. In timing-critical or memory-constrained systems, this overhead might be unacceptable. Additionally, the interface between assembly and C must be managed very carefully to ensure register and stack consistency, which can be tricky and error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',4,4,'2025-07-02_20-41-02','Calling C functions like osSched in PendSV_Handler and SVC_HandlerMain in SVC_Handler offers several important benefits compared to writing everything purely in assembly. The primary advantage is readability and maintainability. C code is easier to understand, write, and modify, especially for high-level logic like selecting the next thread to run or handling different system calls. Most programmers are more familiar with C than assembly, so keeping as much logic as possible in C reduces the learning curve and the likelihood of introducing hard-to-find bugs.\nAnother benefit is portability. C code is much easier to reuse or adapt across different systems and architectures, while assembly code is specific to the CPU\'s instruction set and often even to the compiler or toolchain. This makes a C-based implementation more flexible and future-proof.\nHowever, there are downsides. Calling a C function from assembly introduces overhead—the compiler may generate extra instructions to manage the function call (such as saving registers or managing the stack), which can make the context switch slower or more complex. In timing-critical or memory-constrained systems, this overhead might be unacceptable. Additionally, the interface between assembly and C must be managed very carefully to ensure register and stack consistency, which can be tricky and error-prone.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',4,5,'2025-07-02_18-50-15','If you create only a single thread—such as print_continuously, which repeatedly prints and calls osYield()—the OS will technically still run, but it won’t demonstrate true multitasking, and some behaviors will be redundant or inefficient.\nSince there is only one thread in the system, the osYield() call will trigger a context switch via PendSV_Handler, but the scheduler (osSched) will simply \"switch\" back to the same thread because it\'s the only one available. The context switching mechanism (saving and restoring the stack pointer, jumping through PendSV) still executes, but it doesn’t accomplish anything meaningful—it just wastes CPU cycles switching from the thread to itself.\nFrom the OS\'s perspective, it still works correctly: the thread runs, yields, and is scheduled again. However, this setup reveals a limitation of the round-robin scheduler without a check for the number of runnable threads. A more efficient implementation might detect that there’s only one thread and skip the unnecessary context switch altogether.\nIn summary, yes, the OS will still function with a single thread that yields, but the context switching is unnecessary and inefficient because there\'s no other thread to switch to. The system would appear to run normally (e.g., continuously printing), but it wouldn\'t be leveraging the benefits of a multitasking environment.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',4,5,'2025-07-02_20-41-02','If you create only a single thread—such as print_continuously, which repeatedly prints and calls osYield()—the OS will technically still run, but it won’t demonstrate true multitasking, and some behaviors will be redundant or inefficient.\nSince there is only one thread in the system, the osYield() call will trigger a context switch via PendSV_Handler, but the scheduler (osSched) will simply \"switch\" back to the same thread because it\'s the only one available. The context switching mechanism (saving and restoring the stack pointer, jumping through PendSV) still executes, but it doesn’t accomplish anything meaningful—it just wastes CPU cycles switching from the thread to itself.\nFrom the OS\'s perspective, it still works correctly: the thread runs, yields, and is scheduled again. However, this setup reveals a limitation of the round-robin scheduler without a check for the number of runnable threads. A more efficient implementation might detect that there’s only one thread and skip the unnecessary context switch altogether.\nIn summary, yes, the OS will still function with a single thread that yields, but the context switching is unnecessary and inefficient because there\'s no other thread to switch to. The system would appear to run normally (e.g., continuously printing), but it wouldn\'t be leveraging the benefits of a multitasking environment.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',5,1,'2025-07-28_15-52-33','To pass an argument into a thread, the process begins with the user writing their thread function to accept a single void* argument. This design allows flexibility, as the user can pass any type of data, whether a simple integer or a complex structure, by wrapping it in a pointer and casting it appropriately inside the thread. For example, if the user wants to pass an integer, they declare the integer, pass its address to the thread, and inside the thread function, they cast the void* pointer back to the correct type and dereference it to retrieve the value. On the OS side, during thread creation (within the osCreateThread function), the OS must initialize the thread’s stack such that the R0 register (the first argument register according to the ARM calling convention) contains the pointer to this user-provided data. This involves placing the pointer in the correct position on the stack during thread setup so that when the thread starts executing, R0 holds the expected value. This setup works because the ARM architecture specifies that function arguments are passed via R0 to R3, and by manually initializing the thread\'s stack with a known function signature (void thread(void* args)), the OS can ensure that the thread receives its argument exactly as it would in a standard function call. This approach provides a generic and powerful way to pass arbitrary data into threads, making the OS more flexible and user friendly.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',5,1,'2025-07-30_18-45-58','To pass an argument into a thread, the process begins with the user writing their thread function to accept a single void* argument. This design allows flexibility, as the user can pass any type of data, whether a simple integer or a complex structure, by wrapping it in a pointer and casting it appropriately inside the thread. For example, if the user wants to pass an integer, they declare the integer, pass its address to the thread, and inside the thread function, they cast the void* pointer back to the correct type and dereference it to retrieve the value. On the OS side, during thread creation (within the osCreateThread function), the OS must initialize the thread’s stack such that the R0 register (the first argument register according to the ARM calling convention) contains the pointer to this user-provided data. This involves placing the pointer in the correct position on the stack during thread setup so that when the thread starts executing, R0 holds the expected value. This setup works because the ARM architecture specifies that function arguments are passed via R0 to R3, and by manually initializing the thread\'s stack with a known function signature (void thread(void* args)), the OS can ensure that the thread receives its argument exactly as it would in a standard function call. This approach provides a generic and powerful way to pass arbitrary data into threads, making the OS more flexible and user friendly.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',5,2,'2025-07-28_15-52-33','You cannot call osYield from within any of the context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler (including SVC_Handler_Main), or SysTick_Handler because these functions are already part of the thread-switching mechanism and are themselves executed in an exception context. Calling osYield from within them would trigger another context switch request while a context switch is already in progress. This leads to nested or re-entrant context switches, which can corrupt the stack, disrupt the scheduling logic, or result in undefined behavior due to the interruption of a partially completed context switch. Since osYield typically triggers a PendSV interrupt to initiate a context switch, calling it while already handling a PendSV or SysTick interrupt would mean scheduling another switch before the current one has completed, violating the atomicity and integrity of the switch process. This recursive switching can destabilize the RTOS and make debugging extremely difficult, so it\'s critical that these core handlers remain isolated from higher-level API calls like osYield.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',5,2,'2025-07-30_18-45-58','You cannot call osYield from within any of the context-switching functions like PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler (including SVC_Handler_Main), or SysTick_Handler because these functions are already part of the thread-switching mechanism and are themselves executed in an exception context. Calling osYield from within them would trigger another context switch request while a context switch is already in progress. This leads to nested or re-entrant context switches, which can corrupt the stack, disrupt the scheduling logic, or result in undefined behavior due to the interruption of a partially completed context switch. Since osYield typically triggers a PendSV interrupt to initiate a context switch, calling it while already handling a PendSV or SysTick interrupt would mean scheduling another switch before the current one has completed, violating the atomicity and integrity of the switch process. This recursive switching can destabilize the RTOS and make debugging extremely difficult, so it\'s critical that these core handlers remain isolated from higher-level API calls like osYield.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',5,3,'2025-07-28_15-52-33','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms increases the system\'s timing resolution and responsiveness. The main benefit of this change is that the OS can respond to events and switch tasks more quickly, allowing for finer-grained timing control and better real-time performance, especially in time-sensitive applications like motor control or high-frequency sensor sampling. With a shorter tick interval, software timers can achieve more precise delays, and latency between external events and task responses can be reduced.\nHowever, there are significant downsides. First, the increased interrupt frequency (10,000 ticks per second instead of 1,000) places a much heavier load on the CPU, as it will spend more time handling SysTick interrupts and less time executing actual thread work. This can lead to reduced overall system efficiency. Additionally, more frequent context switches mean more overhead from saving and restoring thread state, which can increase power consumption and make timing jitter worse. It may also require tuning of other OS components to handle the increased tick rate correctly. Overall, while a 0.1 ms clock can improve precision, it must be balanced against system performance and resource constraints.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',5,3,'2025-07-30_18-45-58','Setting the fundamental OS clock to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms increases the system\'s timing resolution and responsiveness. The main benefit of this change is that the OS can respond to events and switch tasks more quickly, allowing for finer-grained timing control and better real-time performance, especially in time-sensitive applications like motor control or high-frequency sensor sampling. With a shorter tick interval, software timers can achieve more precise delays, and latency between external events and task responses can be reduced.\nHowever, there are significant downsides. First, the increased interrupt frequency (10,000 ticks per second instead of 1,000) places a much heavier load on the CPU, as it will spend more time handling SysTick interrupts and less time executing actual thread work. This can lead to reduced overall system efficiency. Additionally, more frequent context switches mean more overhead from saving and restoring thread state, which can increase power consumption and make timing jitter worse. It may also require tuning of other OS components to handle the increased tick rate correctly. Overall, while a 0.1 ms clock can improve precision, it must be balanced against system performance and resource constraints.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',5,4,'2025-07-28_15-52-33','From the user perspective, co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking differ in how control is transferred between threads. In co-operative multitasking, threads must explicitly yield control back to the OS by calling a function like osYield(). This means the programmer is responsible for ensuring that each thread behaves fairly and yields regularly. If a thread runs too long without yielding, it can block all other threads from running, making the system unresponsive.\nIn contrast, pre-emptive multitasking does not rely on threads to give up control voluntarily. Instead, the OS uses a timer interrupt (like SysTick) to force a context switch after a certain time slice. This ensures that all threads get a fair share of CPU time, regardless of how they are written. From the user’s point of view, this makes programming easier and more reliable, since a misbehaving thread cannot monopolize the processor.\nFrom the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler to implement. The OS only switches context when explicitly told to, so it doesn\'t need to track time slices or handle interrupts for scheduling. However, it relies on user code being well-behaved. Pre-emptive multitasking, on the other hand, requires the OS to maintain timers, track each thread\'s remaining time slice, and handle context switches from within interrupt handlers like SysTick_Handler and PendSV_Handler. This makes the OS more complex but much more robust and responsive.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',5,4,'2025-07-30_18-45-58','From the user perspective, co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking differ in how control is transferred between threads. In co-operative multitasking, threads must explicitly yield control back to the OS by calling a function like osYield(). This means the programmer is responsible for ensuring that each thread behaves fairly and yields regularly. If a thread runs too long without yielding, it can block all other threads from running, making the system unresponsive.\nIn contrast, pre-emptive multitasking does not rely on threads to give up control voluntarily. Instead, the OS uses a timer interrupt (like SysTick) to force a context switch after a certain time slice. This ensures that all threads get a fair share of CPU time, regardless of how they are written. From the user’s point of view, this makes programming easier and more reliable, since a misbehaving thread cannot monopolize the processor.\nFrom the OS perspective, co-operative multitasking is simpler to implement. The OS only switches context when explicitly told to, so it doesn\'t need to track time slices or handle interrupts for scheduling. However, it relies on user code being well-behaved. Pre-emptive multitasking, on the other hand, requires the OS to maintain timers, track each thread\'s remaining time slice, and handle context switches from within interrupt handlers like SysTick_Handler and PendSV_Handler. This makes the OS more complex but much more robust and responsive.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',5,5,'2025-07-28_15-52-33','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately rely on the same low-level mechanism to perform a context switch: the PendSV interrupt. In co-operative multitasking, a thread voluntarily calls osYield(), which sets the PendSV interrupt to trigger a context switch. In pre-emptive multitasking, the SysTick_Handler automatically sets the PendSV interrupt when a thread\'s time slice expires. In both cases, control is passed to the PendSV_Handler, which performs the actual context switch by saving the current thread’s state and restoring the next thread’s state.\nSince both mechanisms invoke the same context-switching routine, they are compatible and can coexist. A thread can run co-operatively and call osYield() to switch early, or it can run until its time slice runs out and be pre-empted by the OS. The scheduler handles both cases consistently, ensuring fair CPU sharing. This allows flexibility: time-critical tasks can rely on pre-emption, while well-behaved threads can yield voluntarily for efficiency. The key is that the OS keeps track of thread state and scheduling in a unified way, regardless of how the switch was triggered.\n',1,'s25'),('v29mehta',5,5,'2025-07-30_18-45-58','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately rely on the same low-level mechanism to perform a context switch: the PendSV interrupt. In co-operative multitasking, a thread voluntarily calls osYield(), which sets the PendSV interrupt to trigger a context switch. In pre-emptive multitasking, the SysTick_Handler automatically sets the PendSV interrupt when a thread\'s time slice expires. In both cases, control is passed to the PendSV_Handler, which performs the actual context switch by saving the current thread’s state and restoring the next thread’s state.\nSince both mechanisms invoke the same context-switching routine, they are compatible and can coexist. A thread can run co-operatively and call osYield() to switch early, or it can run until its time slice runs out and be pre-empted by the OS. The scheduler handles both cases consistently, ensuring fair CPU sharing. This allows flexibility: time-critical tasks can rely on pre-emption, while well-behaved threads can yield voluntarily for efficiency. The key is that the OS keeps track of thread state and scheduling in a unified way, regardless of how the switch was triggered.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',1,1,'2025-06-23_21-15-21','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it basically gives us a bunch of ready-made functions to talk to the microcontroller hardware. Instead of writing low-level register stuff ourselves, we just use HAL functions to do things like setting up UART or toggling pins. It saves a lot of time and helps avoid mistakes, especially for beginners or when working on bigger projects.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',1,1,'2025-06-24_11-32-04','HAL stands for Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it basically gives us a bunch of ready-made functions to talk to the microcontroller hardware. Instead of writing low-level register stuff ourselves, we just use HAL functions to do things like setting up UART or toggling pins. It saves a lot of time and helps avoid mistakes, especially for beginners or when working on bigger projects.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',1,2,'2025-06-23_21-15-21','This function is what makes printf actually work in embedded C. By default, printf doesn’t know how to send data out through UART. So we write __io_putchar to handle one character at a time and send it using HAL_UART_Transmit. It’s like a bridge between printf and the UART hardware.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',1,2,'2025-06-24_11-32-04','The __io_putchar function acts like a middleman between printf and the actual hardware we’re using to send data — in this lab, UART. Every time printf wants to print a character, it calls this function. What makes it useful is that we can change how it works depending on the hardware. For example, if we switched from UART to USB or SPI for output, we could just rewrite __io_putchar to use the new peripheral, and printf would still work the same way. This kind of setup keeps the hardware details out of printf, which is great because it means the code stays cleaner and more reusable. It also makes debugging easier since we only need to change one function when switching output devices.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',1,3,'2025-06-23_21-15-21','We needed the debugger because some things — like checking the actual stack pointer values — can’t really be done with printf alone. For example, we wanted to see if PSP and SP were being set correctly, and printf can’t show registers or stop the program mid-run. The debugger let us freeze the code and look under the hood at what the processor was doing.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',1,3,'2025-06-24_11-32-04','We needed the debugger because some things — like checking the actual stack pointer values — can’t really be done with printf alone. For example, we wanted to see if PSP and SP were being set correctly, and printf can’t show registers or stop the program mid-run. The debugger let us freeze the code and look under the hood at what the processor was doing.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',1,4,'2025-06-23_21-15-21','First, we grabbed the initial MSP value from address 0x0 since that’s where the Cortex puts it on reset. Then, to make a new stack for PSP, we just subtracted 0x400 from that value, since the stack grows downward. We had to know where MSP started so that our PSP stack wouldn’t overlap with it — basically, to avoid writing into the same memory space by accident.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',1,4,'2025-06-24_11-32-04','First, we grabbed the initial MSP value from address 0x0 since that’s where the Cortex puts it on reset. Then, to make a new stack for PSP, we just subtracted 0x400 from that value, since the stack grows downward. We had to know where MSP started so that our PSP stack wouldn’t overlap with it — basically, to avoid writing into the same memory space by accident.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',1,5,'2025-06-23_21-15-21','We’d need to define how big each thread’s stack is and how many threads we’re going to support. Then we could figure out where each stack starts using a formula. For thread j, the address would be MSP_init_val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. That way, each thread stack has its own section in memory, and we avoid them overlapping.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',1,5,'2025-06-24_11-32-04','We’d start by defining constants for the number of threads and the size of each thread stack. Using MSP_init_val as a starting point, we’d calculate each new stack’s starting address with an offset. For example, stack j would begin at: MSP_init_val + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) * THREAD_STACK_SIZE. But we also have to make sure these addresses are valid. On the Cortex M4, stack pointers need to be 8-byte aligned, so we should make sure our stack sizes are multiples of 8. We also need to be careful not to allocate beyond the available SRAM size — which means we should define a max stack area size in the linker script and stay within that limit. This kind of setup works well if we plan ahead in the memory layout, but if we don’t, stacks could end up overlapping or going into invalid memory, which would break everything. So the key is to track alignment and total memory usage carefully.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',2,1,'2025-06-25_21-10-32','When I set the PC (program counter) to the address of my print_continuously function, it basically told the processor to jump to that function and start running it immediately. Instead of calling it the normal way, I kind of \"hijacked\" the control flow and manually forced the chip to treat that function like it was next in line. Once PC held that address, the function ran as if it had been called normally, and it just kept printing like expected.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',2,1,'2025-06-25_21-26-00','When I set the PC (program counter) to the address of my print_continuously function, it basically told the processor to jump to that function and start running it immediately. Instead of calling it the normal way, I kind of \"hijacked\" the control flow and manually forced the chip to treat that function like it was next in line. Once PC held that address, the function ran as if it had been called normally, and it just kept printing like expected.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',2,2,'2025-06-25_21-10-32','A system call is a safe way for a program to ask the operating system to do something on its behalf. Instead of calling OS-level code directly, we trigger a special instruction (SVC in our case), which pauses the program and hands control over to a handler in the OS. That handler then looks at what the program is asking for (using the number we passed) and does the appropriate action, like printing a message or switching threads.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',2,2,'2025-06-25_21-26-00','A system call is a special kind of function call that lets user programs safely request services from the operating system. It’s important because it acts as a controlled gateway as it protects access to hardware or critical system resources from potentially bad or buggy user code. Instead of letting user programs interact with hardware directly, system calls ensure those requests go through the OS first, which can validate and handle them properly. In our case, we used the SVC instruction to trigger these function calls into the OS, so we could do things like print messages or launch threads in a safe, controlled way.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',2,3,'2025-06-25_21-10-32','Setting up the thread’s stack means pretending that the thread has already been running and paused, even though it hasn’t started yet. We do this by filling the stack with fake register values — like R0–R12, LR, PC, and xPSR — in the exact order the processor expects when returning from an interrupt. This way, when we switch to that stack and tell the chip to \"resume,\" it loads all those values into the actual registers and starts running the function as if it had always been there. Without setting up the stack like this, the processor wouldn\'t know where to start or what state the thread is in.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',2,3,'2025-06-25_21-26-00','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing a section of memory to make it look like the thread was already running, even though it hasn’t started yet. First, we had to choose a valid memory location for the stack (like subtracting from the main stack pointer) to make sure the thread didn’t interfere with other memory. Then, we pushed fake register values like R0-R12, LR, PC, and xPSR onto that memory in the exact order the processor expects when returning from an interrupt. We do this so that when the processor starts running the thread, it can just \"pop\" those values back into the actual hardware registers and continue as if the thread had been paused and is now resuming. If we didn’t allocate memory properly for the stack, the thread could crash or overwrite important parts of the system.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',2,4,'2025-06-25_21-10-32','If we didn’t set the PSP to our thread’s stack, the processor would keep using the MSP (main stack), which isn\'t set up with the thread’s context. When we tried to pop registers or run the thread, it would grab garbage data or go out of bounds, and that causes a hard fault. The PSP is the pointer to the stack the thread will use, so not setting it means the CPU is flying blind — it doesn\'t know where to look for return addresses or register values.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',2,4,'2025-06-25_21-26-00','If we didn’t set the PSP to our thread’s stack, the processor would keep using the MSP (main stack), which isn\'t set up with the thread’s context. When we tried to pop registers or run the thread, it would grab garbage data or go out of bounds, and that causes a hard fault. The PSP is the pointer to the stack the thread will use, so not setting it means the CPU is flying blind — it doesn\'t know where to look for return addresses or register values.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',2,5,'2025-06-25_21-10-32','If the thread function just printed once and returned, the processor would have no clue where to go next, because the stack was set up as if this function would never return. So, when it tries to return, it would pop the LR (return address) from the stack, which we just set to some placeholder value (like 0xA) and try to jump to it. That would either lead to an invalid memory address or just garbage instructions, causing the program to crash or hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',2,5,'2025-06-25_21-26-00','If the thread function just printed once and returned, the processor would have no clue where to go next, because the stack was set up as if this function would never return. So, when it tries to return, it would pop the LR (return address) from the stack, which we just set to some placeholder value (like 0xA) and try to jump to it. That would either lead to an invalid memory address or just garbage instructions, causing the program to crash or hard fault.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,1,'2025-06-26_22-30-44','When we build a project with multiple .c files, each file gets compiled separately into an object file (.o). Then, during the linking stage, all these object files are combined together into one final executable. So even though the code is split into different files, the compiler treats them like puzzle pieces and links them to create the final program.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,1,'2025-06-27_00-37-44','When we build a program with multiple .c files, it actually goes through three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking.\nPreprocessing: This is where the compiler looks at things like #include and #define statements. It basically copies in the contents of header files and replaces macros so that each .c file is ready to be compiled on its own.\nCompiling: Each .c file is then compiled separately into a .o (object) file. During this step, the compiler turns human-readable C code into machine code, but it doesn’t yet combine it with other files. If a function is used but defined in another file, it just assumes it exists and keeps a placeholder for now.\nLinking: Finally, the linker takes all those .o files and connects them together into one final executable. It matches function calls with their definitions, connects global variables, and makes sure all the pieces work together as one complete program.\nIf any of these steps fail or are skipped, the program won’t run properly, either because it’s missing pieces or because the compiler doesn’t understand something. That’s why this process is important for getting multi-file projects to work correctly.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,1,'2025-06-27_01-45-18','Including a .c file using #include is bad because it essentially copies and pastes all the code from that file directly into the one you\'re working on. This can cause problems like double definitions or broken structure in your program. Instead, .c files should be compiled on their own, and we use header files (.h) to share declarations between them. That keeps everything modular and avoids weird errors during compilation or linking.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,1,'2025-06-27_02-36-35','Including a .c file using #include is bad because it essentially copies and pastes all the code from that file directly into the one you\'re working on. This can cause problems like double definitions or broken structure in your program. Instead, .c files should be compiled on their own, and we use header files (.h) to share declarations between them. That keeps everything modular and avoids weird errors during compilation or linking.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,1,'2025-06-27_03-06-28','Including a .c file using #include is bad because it essentially copies and pastes all the code from that file directly into the one you\'re working on. This can cause problems like double definitions or broken structure in your program. Instead, .c files should be compiled on their own, and we use header files (.h) to share declarations between them. That keeps everything modular and avoids weird errors during compilation or linking.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,1,'2025-06-27_03-37-46','The process of building a program with multiple .c files happens in three main steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking. Each of these has a specific job.\nThe first step is preprocessing, this step handles all the # directives, like #include and #define. The preprocessor replaces macros and brings in code from header files. So, each .c file ends up with everything it needs before it goes to the compiler. This is important because it prepares the file to be compiled on tis own, with all the necessary definitions already in place.\nThe second step is compiling. Here, each preprocessed .c file is turned into an object file (.o). The compiler translates the human-readable C code into machine code for the CPU. If a file uses a function that’s defined in a different file, the compiler doesn’t complain, it just assumes it exist somewhere else and leaves a placeholder for the linker\nThe third step is linking. This is where the linker takes all the object files from different .c files and combines them into one final executable. It matches function call to their actual definitions, links together any global variables, and resolves all remaining references so that the program can run. Without linking, the pieces wouldn’t know how to work together.\nEach step builds on the last, and missing or messing up any of them can cause the build to fail or the program to break.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,2,'2025-06-26_22-30-44','Including a .c file using #include is bad because it essentially copies and pastes all the code from that file directly into the one you\'re working on. This can cause problems like double definitions or broken structure in your program. Instead, .c files should be compiled on their own, and we use header files (.h) to share declarations between them. That keeps everything modular and avoids weird errors during compilation or linking.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,2,'2025-06-27_00-37-44','Including a .c file using #include is bad because it essentially copies and pastes all the code from that file directly into the one you\'re working on. This can cause problems like double definitions or broken structure in your program. Instead, .c files should be compiled on their own, and we use header files (.h) to share declarations between them. That keeps everything modular and avoids weird errors during compilation or linking.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,2,'2025-06-27_01-45-18','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves a few clear steps:\nWrite the thread function, this is the actual code the thread will run. The user is responsible for creating this function.\nInitialize the kernel using the provided kernel setup function. This prepares the stack pool and other internal data.\nCreate a thread structure, which will store information about that specific thread (like its stack pointer and function).\nAssign the function to the thread, so the kernel knows which code this thread should execute.\nCreate the thread using the kernel\'s thread creation function. This sets up the stack and registers.\nStart the OS, which switches the processor into thread mode and begins running the thread.\nSo the user’s job is mainly to define the behavior of the thread and then call the right kernel functions to launch it.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,2,'2025-06-27_02-36-35','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves a few clear steps:\nWrite the thread function, this is the actual code the thread will run. The user is responsible for creating this function.\nInitialize the kernel using the provided kernel setup function. This prepares the stack pool and other internal data.\nCreate a thread structure, which will store information about that specific thread (like its stack pointer and function).\nAssign the function to the thread, so the kernel knows which code this thread should execute.\nCreate the thread using the kernel\'s thread creation function. This sets up the stack and registers.\nStart the OS, which switches the processor into thread mode and begins running the thread.\nSo the user’s job is mainly to define the behavior of the thread and then call the right kernel functions to launch it.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,2,'2025-06-27_03-06-28','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves a few clear steps:\nWrite the thread function, this is the actual code the thread will run. The user is responsible for creating this function.\nInitialize the kernel using the provided kernel setup function. This prepares the stack pool and other internal data.\nCreate a thread structure, which will store information about that specific thread (like its stack pointer and function).\nAssign the function to the thread, so the kernel knows which code this thread should execute.\nCreate the thread using the kernel\'s thread creation function. This sets up the stack and registers.\nStart the OS, which switches the processor into thread mode and begins running the thread.\nSo the user’s job is mainly to define the behavior of the thread and then call the right kernel functions to launch it.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,2,'2025-06-27_03-37-46','Including a .c file using #include is bad because it essentially copies and pastes all the code from that file directly into the one you\'re working on. This can cause problems like double definitions or broken structure in your program. Instead, .c files should be compiled on their own, and we use header files (.h) to share declarations between them. That keeps everything modular and avoids weird errors during compilation or linking.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,3,'2025-06-26_22-30-44','To set up and start a thread in our OS, we follow these steps:\nInitialize the kernel using the provided kernel function. This sets up the stack pool.\nCreate a thread structure, and assign the function you want the thread to run.\nCall the thread creation function to set up the stack and prepare the thread.\nStart the OS, which switches into thread mode and runs the thread we created.\nAfter that, the thread function takes over and keeps running until it’s done (or loops forever, like ours does right now).\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,3,'2025-06-27_00-37-44','To set up and start a thread in our OS, we follow these steps:\nInitialize the kernel using the provided kernel function. This sets up the stack pool.\nCreate a thread structure, and assign the function you want the thread to run.\nCall the thread creation function to set up the stack and prepare the thread.\nStart the OS, which switches into thread mode and runs the thread we created.\nAfter that, the thread function takes over and keeps running until it’s done (or loops forever, like ours does right now).\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,3,'2025-06-27_01-45-18','The stack pool is a chunk of memory set aside just for thread stacks. Each thread needs its own stack to store things like local variables, return addresses, and register values. In a future lab, we can use the stack pool to create multiple threads by slicing off pieces of it for each one. As long as we keep track of how much is left, we can keep allocating stacks from it until we run out of space.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,3,'2025-06-27_02-36-35','The stack pool is a chunk of memory set aside just for thread stacks. Each thread needs its own stack to store things like local variables, return addresses, and register values. In a future lab, we can use the stack pool to create multiple threads by slicing off pieces of it for each one. As long as we keep track of how much is left, we can keep allocating stacks from it until we run out of space.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,3,'2025-06-27_03-06-28','The stack pool is a block of memory that the operating system sets aside specifically for managing stacks. Its main purpose is to provide each thread with its own private stack space, which is necessary for things like storing local variables, keeping track of function calls, and saving register values during context switches. When a new thread is created, the kernel carves out a chunk of the stack pool to serve as that thread’s stack.\nBut the stack pool isn’t just for user threads, it also needs to support the operating system itself. The OS might use parts of the stack pool for managing interrupts or storing temporary data during scheduling or context switching. So, the pool must be big enough to handle not only all the user threads we might create, but also any memory the system needs to do its job.\nIn future labs, this stack pool will let us support multiple threads by allocating separate stack spaces for each one and switching between them as needed. This is how the OS keeps each thread’s state isolated while still being able to manage everything behind the scenes.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,3,'2025-06-27_03-37-46','From the user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves a few clear steps:\nWrite the thread function, this is the actual code the thread will run. The user is responsible for creating this function.\nInitialize the kernel using the provided kernel setup function. This prepares the stack pool and other internal data.\nCreate a thread structure, which will store information about that specific thread (like its stack pointer and function).\nAssign the function to the thread, so the kernel knows which code this thread should execute.\nCreate the thread using the kernel\'s thread creation function. This sets up the stack and registers.\nStart the OS, which switches the processor into thread mode and begins running the thread.\nSo the user’s job is mainly to define the behavior of the thread and then call the right kernel functions to launch it.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,4,'2025-06-26_22-30-44','The stack pool is a chunk of memory set aside just for thread stacks. Each thread needs its own stack to store things like local variables, return addresses, and register values. In a future lab, we can use the stack pool to create multiple threads by slicing off pieces of it for each one. As long as we keep track of how much is left, we can keep allocating stacks from it until we run out of space.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,4,'2025-06-27_00-37-44','The stack pool is a chunk of memory set aside just for thread stacks. Each thread needs its own stack to store things like local variables, return addresses, and register values. In a future lab, we can use the stack pool to create multiple threads by slicing off pieces of it for each one. As long as we keep track of how much is left, we can keep allocating stacks from it until we run out of space.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,4,'2025-06-27_01-45-18','The kernel contains the low-level code that controls how threads run, manages stacks, and interacts with the hardware. The user code is what we write to actually use those kernel functions — like creating threads and defining what the threads should do. Keeping them separate makes things cleaner and safer, because the user doesn’t need to know all the complicated stuff the kernel is doing in the background. From the user’s perspective, it’s like having a toolbox — I just need to know what tools are available and how to use them, not how they were built.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,4,'2025-06-27_02-36-35','The kernel contains the low-level code that controls how threads run, manages stacks, and interacts with the hardware. The user code is what we write to actually use those kernel functions — like creating threads and defining what the threads should do. Keeping them separate makes things cleaner and safer, because the user doesn’t need to know all the complicated stuff the kernel is doing in the background. From the user’s perspective, it’s like having a toolbox — I just need to know what tools are available and how to use them, not how they were built.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,4,'2025-06-27_03-06-28','The kernel contains the low-level code that controls how threads run, manages stacks, and interacts with the hardware. The user code is what we write to actually use those kernel functions — like creating threads and defining what the threads should do. Keeping them separate makes things cleaner and safer, because the user doesn’t need to know all the complicated stuff the kernel is doing in the background. From the user’s perspective, it’s like having a toolbox — I just need to know what tools are available and how to use them, not how they were built.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,4,'2025-06-27_03-37-46','The stack pool is a block of memory that the operating system sets aside specifically for managing stacks. Its main purpose is to provide each thread with its own private stack space, which is necessary for things like storing local variables, keeping track of function calls, and saving register values during context switches. When a new thread is created, the kernel carves out a chunk of the stack pool to serve as that thread’s stack.\nBut the stack pool isn’t just for user threads, it also needs to support the operating system itself. The OS might use parts of the stack pool for managing interrupts or storing temporary data during scheduling or context switching. So, the pool must be big enough to handle not only all the user threads we might create, but also any memory the system needs to do its job.\nIn future labs, this stack pool will let us support multiple threads by allocating separate stack spaces for each one and switching between them as needed. This is how the OS keeps each thread’s state isolated while still being able to manage everything behind the scenes.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,5,'2025-06-26_22-30-44','The kernel contains the low-level code that controls how threads run, manages stacks, and interacts with the hardware. The user code is what we write to actually use those kernel functions — like creating threads and defining what the threads should do. Keeping them separate makes things cleaner and safer, because the user doesn’t need to know all the complicated stuff the kernel is doing in the background. From the user’s perspective, it’s like having a toolbox — I just need to know what tools are available and how to use them, not how they were built.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,5,'2025-06-27_00-37-44','The kernel contains the low-level code that controls how threads run, manages stacks, and interacts with the hardware. The user code is what we write to actually use those kernel functions — like creating threads and defining what the threads should do. Keeping them separate makes things cleaner and safer, because the user doesn’t need to know all the complicated stuff the kernel is doing in the background. From the user’s perspective, it’s like having a toolbox — I just need to know what tools are available and how to use them, not how they were built.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',3,5,'2025-06-27_03-37-46','The kernel contains the low-level code that controls how threads run, manages stacks, and interacts with the hardware. The user code is what we write to actually use those kernel functions — like creating threads and defining what the threads should do. Keeping them separate makes things cleaner and safer, because the user doesn’t need to know all the complicated stuff the kernel is doing in the background. From the user’s perspective, it’s like having a toolbox — I just need to know what tools are available and how to use them, not how they were built.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,1,'2025-07-27_20-21-13','With a queue, round-robin scheduling becomes really natural. You just run the thread at the front of the queue, then push it to the back when it yields, and run the next one. This way, the order of threads is automatically maintained and updated. One benefit over an array is that you don’t have to keep track of an index or worry about skipping over inactive threads, just pop the next ready one and go. Also, it’s easier to support dynamic thread creation or termination since you can enqueue and dequeue without worrying about gaps in an array.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,1,'2025-07-27_20-59-46','The current scheduler already kind of works like a queue as it cycles through an array of threads using an index (current_thread = (current_thread + 1) % num_threads). A queue would do the same thing logically, but instead of managing an index, you could literally dequeue the thread that just ran and enqueue it at the back after yielding. This might make the logic a bit cleaner and more dynamic if threads can be added or removed on the fly.\nHowever, using a real queue could also add overhead as you’d need to manage memory pointers or data movement, which is a bit more complex than just incrementing an index in an array. In a small, embedded OS like this one, where memory and performance are tight, that overhead might not be worth it unless you need more flexibility. So overall, both approaches support round-robin behavior, but arrays are simpler and more lightweight, while queues offer more flexibility at the cost of some extra work.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,1,'2025-07-27_21-52-44','The current scheduler already kind of works like a queue as it cycles through an array of threads using an index (current_thread = (current_thread + 1) % num_threads). A queue would do the same thing logically, but instead of managing an index, you could literally dequeue the thread that just ran and enqueue it at the back after yielding. This might make the logic a bit cleaner and more dynamic if threads can be added or removed on the fly.\nHowever, using a real queue could also add overhead as you’d need to manage memory pointers or data movement, which is a bit more complex than just incrementing an index in an array. In a small, embedded OS like this one, where memory and performance are tight, that overhead might not be worth it unless you need more flexibility. So overall, both approaches support round-robin behavior, but arrays are simpler and more lightweight, while queues offer more flexibility at the cost of some extra work.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,2,'2025-07-27_20-21-13','The PendSV_Handler function is where the actual context switch happens. When it runs, it first saves the current thread\'s state by storing its stack pointer, and maybe some registers too. Then it calls osSched() to figure out which thread should run next. After that, it loads the new thread’s stack pointer and restores its context. It’s like freezing the current thread and unfreezing another one. The key is that PendSV_Handler runs with low priority and doesn\'t interrupt things like SysTick or SVC. Instead, it waits until nothing else is happening, which makes it perfect for switching threads.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,2,'2025-07-27_20-59-46','The PendSV_Handler is where the actual thread switching happens. When it runs, it first saves the state of the current thread — specifically the registers R4 to R11 — by pushing them onto the thread\'s stack. This is important because those registers aren\'t automatically saved during exception handling, unlike R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR, which are pushed automatically.\nIt uses the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) for this because each thread has its own stack, and the kernel runs in thread mode using PSP. After saving the current thread’s state, it calls osSched() to pick the next thread, updates the PSP to point to that thread’s saved stack, and then pops R4–R11 from that stack to restore the new thread’s context. If you skip saving or restoring those registers, the thread might come back in a weird state or crash. So, getting the PSP and register handling right is essential for reliable context switching.\n',0,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,2,'2025-07-27_21-52-44','PendSV_Handler is responsible for saving the current thread’s state and restoring the next one — it’s the core of the context switch. First, it reads the current value of the PSP (Process Stack Pointer) because that’s what the Cortex-M4 uses in thread mode to keep track of the current thread\'s stack. The handler uses this PSP value to save the thread\'s callee-saved registers (R4–R11) onto its own stack. These registers aren’t saved automatically when an exception happens, so we have to do it manually.\nOnce the current thread\'s context is saved, the handler calls osSched() (or equivalent) to update current_thread to the next thread that should run. After that, it reads the PSP from the new thread’s TCB and restores R4–R11 by popping them from that thread’s stack. Finally, it updates the PSP to point to the new stack location, and when the exception returns, the CPU automatically restores the rest of the context (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, xPSR) from that stack and resumes the new thread.\nThe order is very important, if we updated the PSP before saving the current thread\'s context, we’d overwrite the wrong stack. And if we skipped saving or restoring registers like R4–R11, the thread wouldn’t resume properly and might crash. So, every step like reading PSP, saving registers, switching threads, restoring, and writing PSP again must be done in the right order for the system to work.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,3,'2025-07-27_20-21-13','If you always pick the thread with the highest priority, then lower-priority threads might never get a chance to run. This is called starvation. For example, if one high-priority thread never finishes (like a sensor-reading loop), then anything with lower priority could be permanently ignored. That’s not good for fairness or system responsiveness. You’d have to add more complex scheduling like priority aging or time-slicing, which kind of defeats the point of keeping things simple.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,3,'2025-07-27_20-59-46','If you always pick the thread with the highest priority, then lower-priority threads might never get a chance to run. This is called starvation. For example, if one high-priority thread never finishes (like a sensor-reading loop), then anything with lower priority could be permanently ignored. That’s not good for fairness or system responsiveness. You’d have to add more complex scheduling like priority aging or time-slicing, which kind of defeats the point of keeping things simple.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,3,'2025-07-27_21-52-44','If you always pick the thread with the highest priority, then lower-priority threads might never get a chance to run. This is called starvation. For example, if one high-priority thread never finishes (like a sensor-reading loop), then anything with lower priority could be permanently ignored. That’s not good for fairness or system responsiveness. You’d have to add more complex scheduling like priority aging or time-slicing, which kind of defeats the point of keeping things simple.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,4,'2025-07-27_20-21-13','Calling C functions makes the code way easier to write and understand. It’s higher level, less error-prone, and you can use things like variables, control flow, and printf. If we did everything in assembly, we’d have more control, but it would also be more fragile and harder to debug. On the downside, calling C from inside an interrupt handler might add overhead, and the compiler might do weird optimizations if you’re not careful. But for a lab OS like this, using C inside the handlers is a good tradeoff between complexity and readability\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,4,'2025-07-27_20-59-46','Calling C functions makes the code way easier to write and understand. It’s higher level, less error-prone, and you can use things like variables, control flow, and printf. If we did everything in assembly, we’d have more control, but it would also be more fragile and harder to debug. On the downside, calling C from inside an interrupt handler might add overhead, and the compiler might do weird optimizations if you’re not careful. But for a lab OS like this, using C inside the handlers is a good tradeoff between complexity and readability\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,4,'2025-07-27_21-52-44','Calling C functions makes the code way easier to write and understand. It’s higher level, less error-prone, and you can use things like variables, control flow, and printf. If we did everything in assembly, we’d have more control, but it would also be more fragile and harder to debug. On the downside, calling C from inside an interrupt handler might add overhead, and the compiler might do weird optimizations if you’re not careful. But for a lab OS like this, using C inside the handlers is a good tradeoff between complexity and readability\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,5,'2025-07-27_20-21-13','Yes, technically it would still work, just not very usefully. The thread would run, hit the osYield(), and trigger PendSV, but the scheduler would just pick the same thread again since it’s the only one available. It would look like it’s looping normally, but the context switch is still happening in the background every time. So, the OS still runs and the kernel functions aren’t broken, but you’re wasting cycles switching back into the same thread over and over. It’s not efficient, but it does prove that your context switching setup works.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,5,'2025-07-27_20-59-46','Yes, technically it would still work, just not very usefully. The thread would run, hit the osYield(), and trigger PendSV, but the scheduler would just pick the same thread again since it’s the only one available. It would look like it’s looping normally, but the context switch is still happening in the background every time. So, the OS still runs and the kernel functions aren’t broken, but you’re wasting cycles switching back into the same thread over and over. It’s not efficient, but it does prove that your context switching setup works.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',4,5,'2025-07-27_21-52-44','Yes, technically it would still work, just not very usefully. The thread would run, hit the osYield(), and trigger PendSV, but the scheduler would just pick the same thread again since it’s the only one available. It would look like it’s looping normally, but the context switch is still happening in the background every time. So, the OS still runs and the kernel functions aren’t broken, but you’re wasting cycles switching back into the same thread over and over. It’s not efficient, but it does prove that your context switching setup works.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',5,1,'2025-07-29_23-31-08','As the user, I first define a variable (or struct) with the values I want to pass into the thread, and then I pass a pointer to that variable into osCreateThread as the second argument. Inside the thread function, I cast that void* pointer back to the correct type so I can use the values.\nOn the OS side, when setting up the thread’s stack, the OS makes sure to place that pointer into the location in the stack where register R0 would be during a context restore. Since R0 is the register used to pass the first argument to functions in ARM, when the thread starts running, it just \"receives\" the value in R0 like normal.\nIt works because we’re essentially faking the start of the thread as if it’s a regular function call, and ARM calling conventions already expect the first argument to be in R0. So, the OS sets up the stack to make it look like the CPU naturally jumped into the thread with the argument ready.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',5,2,'2025-07-29_23-31-08','You can’t call osYield in those context-switching functions because osYield itself triggers a software interrupt using SVC #10, which causes a context switch. If you call osYield while already doing a context switch (like inside PendSV_Handler or SVC_Handler), you basically interrupt the interrupt. That can lead to stack corruption, or broken thread states because you’re trying to switch threads while you\'re in the middle of switching. This is bad because these functions are supposed to be low-level and deterministic. Calling something that relies on high-level system calls from there breaks the whole control flow and can make your OS unstable.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',5,3,'2025-07-29_23-31-08','If we change the OS clock to 0.1ms, the biggest benefit is that our OS becomes more responsive. Threads with short timeslices can switch faster, and latency for real-time tasks can be improved. The downside is that we get 10 times the interrupts per second which means more CPU time is spent handling SysTick and context switching. That increases overhead and reduces how much actual work the threads can do. It might also mess with timing-sensitive functions like HAL_Delay() unless everything is updated to match the new tick rate.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',5,4,'2025-07-29_23-31-08','From the user side, cooperative multitasking means the thread has to manually give up control using something like osYield(). If the thread never calls yield, it hogs the CPU and no other thread gets to run. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically switches threads after a certain time (based on the timeslice), even if the thread doesn’t yield. From the OS side, cooperative multitasking is simpler, it just switches threads when prompted. But with pre-emptive, the OS must track how long a thread has been running and use something like SysTick_Handler to monitor and force a context switch when the time is up. That takes more setup and more careful timing management, but it’s more robust and doesn’t rely on the thread playing nice.\n',1,'s25'),('v2kumana',5,5,'2025-07-29_23-31-08','They work together because they both trigger the same thing in the end, a context switch using PendSV. If a thread calls osYield(), it voluntarily gives up the CPU and triggers a context switch. If it doesn’t, the SysTick timer will eventually hit its limit and force a switch based on the timeslice. Since the OS resets the thread\'s runtime when it yields or when it gets switched out, both mechanisms share the same logic underneath. You just end up with two ways to switch threads where one switches manually and one switches automatically, but they both use the same scheduler and context switching code. It gives more flexibility for different types of thread behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',1,1,'2025-06-27_18-18-42','The HAL is the hardware abstraction layer. The main purpose of the HAL is to standardize the interaction between the user and the hardware. It allows for faster development and prototyping since the code user code does not need to be hardware specific. The programmer no longer needs to change bits manually but can rather use standard HAL commands to simplify the process and complete low level steps automatically.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',1,2,'2025-06-27_18-18-42','The __io_putchar function is often used in embedded systems to redirect outputs from the usual standard C functions like printf() to other more useful and specific hardware interfaces like UART(Serial communication), TCPIP, CANbus, etc. This is highly useful since embedded systems often don’t have screens built into them, __io_putchar can be used as a bridge to use printf() to display on a PC terminal for debugging or logging.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',1,3,'2025-06-27_18-18-42','The debugger offers various benefits over the standard printf statements. While using the debugger we can set breakpoints to pause the execution of the program at crucial points and manually run the code line by line. The debugger also provides greater visibility into the program by indicating register states, variable values, and memory locations.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',1,4,'2025-06-27_18-18-42','Assuming we already have the initial value for MSP, we subtract the set stack size(offset) from MSP_init_val to get the value for the PSP. Using this we can essentially segment the memory into various stacks, which can be used to run various processes. We needed the initial location of the MSP and set initial value of the PSP using the offset to ensure there is no overlap between the two stacks. If there are multiple stacks setup randomly, they can lead to overlap between the stacks or leave large gaps in memory where stacks cannot be allocated. These gaps could be as big as offset-1.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',1,5,'2025-06-27_18-18-42','We can set an loop to constantly keep subtracting a preset offset from the initial MSP value. To find the initial PSP value of a stack we can subtract the multiple of the stack offset based on the stack number we want to reach. Before setting up the stacks we would need to check if the memory size is appropriate for the number of stacks required. This can be done by multiplying the offset with the number of stacks to be allocated. If this size is larger than the allocated memory size then we would need to display an error code.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',2,1,'2025-06-27_23-39-44','When I changed the PC to hold the address of my print continuously function, the processor immediately jumped to the function and started executing that function. The PC tells the CPU what instruction to execute next. By manually setting it to a function pointer, we can redirect the execution. This causes the program to enter a infinite loop of printing continuously.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',2,2,'2025-06-27_23-39-44','A system call is a way for the user level applications and codes to request a service from the operating system. It acts like a controlled gateway between privileged operations that the user level codes are not allowed to perform directly. We used the SVC calls to trigger system calls which could trigger a switch case in the SVC handler that would run the specific function passed.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',2,3,'2025-06-27_23-39-44','To setup the thread stacks, we need to prepare a memory area that the CPU will use when running the thread. We manually pushed the values onto the stack in the exact order the CPU would expect them to be during a context switch. These values is what the CPU would have pushed if the thread was running normally. This is essential because when the thread is started, the CPU will restore these registers from the stack and continue the execution as if it had been running all along. Without this CPU does not know where to start or what state the thread is in.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',2,4,'2025-06-27_23-39-44','If we didn’t set up PSP to point to the thread stack, the CPU would try to use whatever residual value was present in the load PSP register to load the values during the context switch. Since the value of the PSP could be invalid, uninitialized. The processer would attempt to access a bad memory location and this would result in a hard fault. By setting the PSP explicitly, we can prepare the thread stack to ensure the CPU pulls valid data from the correct location when the thread starts up.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',2,5,'2025-06-27_23-39-44','If the thread function just returned a value instead of looping infinitely, the processor would attempt to execute the value in the Link Register. This was manually set to 0xA in this case. Since this is not a valid memory address, it would lead to a hard fault.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',4,1,'2025-07-09_01-41-25','In round-robin scheduling, each task gets a turn in order. To use an array we track the index of the current thread and move to the next thread in the array once the current thread yields. Using a queue would naturally represent this setup. The front of the queue would be the currently executing thread. After it yields, the thread is moved to the back of the queue. The scheduler would then pick up the next queue at the front of the queue.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',4,1,'2025-07-27_19-11-12','A queue supports round-robin scheduling by using a first in, first out(FIFO) order of threads. The scheduler runs the thread at the front of the queue. When the thread yields or is pre-empted, it is put to the back of the queue. This rotation of threads in the queue, ensures that each threads gets its fair share of CPU time.\nCompared to an array with an index variable to track the current thread, a queue offers more flexibility during runtime. For example, it makes it easier to add or remove threads dynamically during runtime. This is highly beneficial in real world applications, since there are tasks that may not be static. \n\nThis comes with a performance overhead, due to enqueue and dequeue operations. Arrays have a fixed size, making them easier to manage, but queues are better suited for dynamic thread management which is often needed in complex scheduling scenarios, especially when priorities of different tasks may be different.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',4,2,'2025-07-09_01-41-25','It performs 3 main steps. It saves the context (register values) onto the current thread stack. It then calls the osSched function. This function saves the current thread’s PSP in it’s TCB and selects the next thread to run. It updates the PSP to the point in the new thread’s stack. Lastly The handler loads the registers values required from the new thread’s stack. And resume the execution of the new thread.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',4,2,'2025-07-27_19-11-12','The PendSV_Handler performs a context switch by saving the state of the currently running thread and restoring the state of the next scheduled thread. It starts by reading the PSP, which points to the current thread’s stack. It saves the registers(R4-R11) onto this stack to save the thread’s context.\nNext, it calls the osSched function, which updates the TCB of the current thread and store its new stack pointer. It then selects the next thread to run by returning its PSP. Then the PendSV_Handler, updates the PSP to the new thread’s stack and restores the saved registers R4-R11 from that stack.\nThe function concludes by exiting the interrupt and resumes the execution of the newly scheduled thread. This process ensures that each thread resumes exactly where it left off.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',4,3,'2025-07-09_01-41-25','If we always choose the task with the highest priority, the lower priority tasks might never run if there are too many higher priority tasks or if the higher priority task never yields. This is called starvation.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',4,3,'2025-07-27_19-11-12','If we modify the OS to always run the highest priority thread, it creates the risk of starvation for lower priority threads. Starvation is when the lower priority threads don’t get any CPU time as a result of the scheduler always having higher priority threads to give CPU time to. Since the high-priority threads continuously use the CPU and does not yield voluntarily, lower priority threads may never get scheduled. This leads to an unfairness in the CPU usage and can cause problems like unresponsive services or missed deadlines in real time systems.\nWithout additional functionality with some form of aging and priority management, the system becomes rigid. It lacks the fairness and adaptability needed in general purpose OS designs.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',4,4,'2025-07-09_01-41-25','Writing code in C increases the readability and maintainability of the code. Easier to debug and write logic in C rather than assembly. It is also more portable since assembly is hardware specific. \n\nFunctions in C would also be slower due to the increased overhead. Using C also complicates context switching and saving if not controlled heavily.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',4,4,'2025-07-27_19-11-12','Writing the functions like osSched in C improves the readability and maintainability of the code. It allows us to express complex logic more clearly than assembly and allows for easier and better debugging with tools like breakpoints. Code in C is also m portable and be used to run across different platforms while assembly code is hardware specific. \n\nOn the other hand, calling C functions from an interrupt handler requires some overhead. The function calls in C will follow calling conventions and this requires stack alignment which increases the complexity of the code and increases the instructions. \n\nFor real world systems, performance is crucial, and such sections are often written in assembly to reduce overhead. for example context switching. On the other hand, C is used for logic that can benefit from abstraction and clarity.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',4,5,'2025-07-09_01-41-25','Yes, even if just a single thread is running, the scheduler would keep rotating back to the same thread. It would repeatedly load and unload the thread context. It can be thought of as an array of with just one entry.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',4,5,'2025-07-27_19-11-12','Yes, the OS will still function even if only a single thread Is created. In this case the scheduler has only one threads to choose from, so the context switch will simply reload the same thread’s context. \n\nEven though this is inefficient, this setup is still valid and would still work. The thread will yield, triggering the context switch mechanism which will end up restoring the same threads. This makes it similar to having a queue or array with just one thread\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,1,'2025-07-28_17-29-57','First the user creates a thread by calling the osCreateThread() function and passes both a function pointer and the argument. The argument is often passed as a pointer to some data. The OS then prepares a stack for the new thread. During the stack setup, the OS stores the arguments to be passed in the r0 register. This will be stored when the thread first runs. When the scheduler switches to the new thread, the CPU loads the arguments from the stack into R0, so calling when the thread function starts it receives the argument as intended. The argument is particularly loaded into R0 because of the Crotex-M calling convention, which expects the first argument to be in R0.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,1,'2025-07-28_20-55-23','First the user creates a thread by calling the osCreateThread() function and passes both a function pointer and the argument. The argument is often passed as a pointer to some data. The OS then prepares a stack for the new thread. During the stack setup, the OS stores the arguments to be passed in the r0 register. This will be stored when the thread first runs. When the scheduler switches to the new thread, the CPU loads the arguments from the stack into R0, so calling when the thread function starts it receives the argument as intended. The argument is particularly loaded into R0 because of the Crotex-M calling convention, which expects the first argument to be in R0.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,1,'2025-07-28_22-06-10','First the user creates a thread by calling the osCreateThread() function and passes both a function pointer and the argument. The argument is often passed as a pointer to some data. The OS then prepares a stack for the new thread. During the stack setup, the OS stores the arguments to be passed in the r0 register. This will be stored when the thread first runs. When the scheduler switches to the new thread, the CPU loads the arguments from the stack into R0, so calling when the thread function starts it receives the argument as intended. The argument is particularly loaded into R0 because of the Crotex-M calling convention, which expects the first argument to be in R0.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,1,'2025-07-28_22-27-27','First the user calls the osCreateThread(), providing both a function pointer and the argument they want to pass as a pointer to the data. The Os’s job is to prepare the stack for this new thread. When setting up the stack, the OS place the argument at the exact location on the stack wehree the CPU will expect to find the initial value for the register R0. It is put into R0 particular because of the Cortex-M calling convention. When the scheduler switches to this new thread and the CPU performs the initial context restore, the CPU automatically loads the value from the stack into R0. This ensures that when the thread function starts running, it receives the correct argument. This approach works because the OS and CPU follows the same convention for function calls: the first argument is passed into R0, and the stack is setup so that the hardware handles it correctly when the thread begins.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,2,'2025-07-28_17-29-57','Calling the osYield() function inside a context switch would be problematic because the osYield() function triggers a supervisor call (SVC), this fires a new exception. When an exception handler like SVC or Pend_SV handler, we risk stacking exceptions which can lead to overflowing the stack or putting the CPU into an invalid state, causing the system to crash. The context switch should only be initiated from thread mode not from exception handlers.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,2,'2025-07-28_20-55-23','Calling the osYield() function inside a context switch would be problematic because the osYield() function triggers a supervisor call (SVC), this fires a new exception. When an exception handler like SVC or Pend_SV handler, we risk stacking exceptions which can lead to overflowing the stack or putting the CPU into an invalid state, causing the system to crash. The context switch should only be initiated from thread mode not from exception handlers.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,2,'2025-07-28_22-06-10','Calling the osYield() function inside a context switch would be problematic because the osYield() function triggers a supervisor call (SVC), this fires a new exception. When an exception handler like SVC or Pend_SV handler, we risk stacking exceptions which can lead to overflowing the stack or putting the CPU into an invalid state, causing the system to crash. The context switch should only be initiated from thread mode not from exception handlers.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,2,'2025-07-28_22-27-27','Calling the osYield() function inside a context switch would be problematic because the osYield() function triggers a supervisor call (SVC), this fires a new exception. When an exception handler like SVC or Pend_SV handler, we risk stacking exceptions which can lead to overflowing the stack or putting the CPU into an invalid state, causing the system to crash. The context switch should only be initiated from thread mode not from exception handlers.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,3,'2025-07-28_17-29-57','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms means the system can respond to events or preempt threads more quickly. This allows for the system to have more accurate timing and potentially lower latency for real time tasks. On the other hand, the CPU could spend a lot more time handling interrupts, leaving less time for user code to execute. This would increase the risk of missing deadlines if the system gets overloaded. A high interrupt frequency can reduce overall performance and efficiency, especially where CPU time is severely limited.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,3,'2025-07-28_20-55-23','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms means the system can respond to events or preempt threads more quickly. This allows for the system to have more accurate timing and potentially lower latency for real time tasks. On the other hand, the CPU could spend a lot more time handling interrupts, leaving less time for user code to execute. This would increase the risk of missing deadlines if the system gets overloaded. A high interrupt frequency can reduce overall performance and efficiency, especially where CPU time is severely limited.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,3,'2025-07-28_22-06-10','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms means the system can respond to events or preempt threads more quickly. This allows for the system to have more accurate timing and potentially lower latency for real time tasks. On the other hand, the CPU could spend a lot more time handling interrupts, leaving less time for user code to execute. This would increase the risk of missing deadlines if the system gets overloaded. A high interrupt frequency can reduce overall performance and efficiency, especially where CPU time is severely limited.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,3,'2025-07-28_22-27-27','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms means the system can respond to events or preempt threads more quickly. This allows for the system to have more accurate timing and potentially lower latency for real time tasks. On the other hand, the CPU could spend a lot more time handling interrupts, leaving less time for user code to execute. This would increase the risk of missing deadlines if the system gets overloaded. A high interrupt frequency can reduce overall performance and efficiency, especially where CPU time is severely limited.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,4,'2025-07-28_17-29-57','In co-operative multitasking, the threads control when they give up the CPU by calling in the osYield function. This means that threads could sabotage the system and prevent other from running by never yielding the cpu time. In preemptive multitasking, the OS uses a timer like SysTick to interrupt the threads automatically and switch between them after a certain period. From the user’s perspective, pre-emption mean they don’t have to worry about threads sharing the CPU fairly but in cooperative multitasking they must remember to yield. From the OS’s perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to execute since the context switch only takes place at well defined points, while preemptive multitasking requires handling interrupts and saving context at any moment.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,4,'2025-07-28_20-55-23','In co-operative multitasking, the threads control when they give up the CPU by calling in the osYield function. This means that threads could sabotage the system and prevent other from running by never yielding the cpu time. In preemptive multitasking, the OS uses a timer like SysTick to interrupt the threads automatically and switch between them after a certain period. From the user’s perspective, pre-emption mean they don’t have to worry about threads sharing the CPU fairly but in cooperative multitasking they must remember to yield. From the OS’s perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to execute since the context switch only takes place at well defined points, while preemptive multitasking requires handling interrupts and saving context at any moment.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,4,'2025-07-28_22-06-10','In co-operative multitasking, the threads control when they give up the CPU by calling in the osYield function. This means that threads could sabotage the system and prevent other from running by never yielding the cpu time. In preemptive multitasking, the OS uses a timer like SysTick to interrupt the threads automatically and switch between them after a certain period. From the user’s perspective, pre-emption mean they don’t have to worry about threads sharing the CPU fairly but in cooperative multitasking they must remember to yield. From the OS’s perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to execute since the context switch only takes place at well defined points, while preemptive multitasking requires handling interrupts and saving context at any moment.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,4,'2025-07-28_22-27-27','In co-operative multitasking, the threads control when they give up the CPU by calling in the osYield function. This means that threads could sabotage the system and prevent other from running by never yielding the cpu time. In preemptive multitasking, the OS uses a timer like SysTick to interrupt the threads automatically and switch between them after a certain period. From the user’s perspective, pre-emption mean they don’t have to worry about threads sharing the CPU fairly but in cooperative multitasking they must remember to yield. From the OS’s perspective, cooperative multitasking is simpler to execute since the context switch only takes place at well defined points, while preemptive multitasking requires handling interrupts and saving context at any moment.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,5,'2025-07-28_17-29-57','The two mechanisms are able to work together since they both trigger a context switch using the same OS routines. The thread can yield voluntarily, causing an immediate switch or it can be pre-empted when its time slice runs out. The scheduler doesn’t care why the switch happened, it just moves to the next thread in the array or queue. This allows both types of threads to exist, letting some threads yield when their tasks are over and other get switched out when their time slice is over.\n',0,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,5,'2025-07-28_20-55-23','Both pre-emptive(timer based) and cooperative (yield-based) context switching in our OS work together because they both use the same core mechanism to switch threads, which is the PendSV interrupt handler. When a thread yields voluntarily, it triggers an SVC or PendSV exception. This causes the CPU to enter the PendSV_Handler. Similarly, when a thread’s timeslice runs out (as tracked in the SysTick_Handler) the OS triggers the PendSV interrupt as well. In both cases, the PendSV_Handler runs the shared logic to save the current thread’s state onto its respective stack and load the next context from the next thread’s stack. The common use of the PendSV_Handler ensures that, whether a thread yields or is pre-empted by a timer, the same context switching code is used, allowing all the threads to be managed in the same way.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,5,'2025-07-28_22-06-10','Both pre-emptive(timer based) and cooperative (yield-based) context switching in our OS work together because they both use the same core mechanism to switch threads, which is the PendSV interrupt handler. When a thread yields voluntarily, it triggers an SVC or PendSV exception. This causes the CPU to enter the PendSV_Handler. Similarly, when a thread’s timeslice runs out (as tracked in the SysTick_Handler) the OS triggers the PendSV interrupt as well. In both cases, the PendSV_Handler runs the shared logic to save the current thread’s state onto its respective stack and load the next context from the next thread’s stack. The common use of the PendSV_Handler ensures that, whether a thread yields or is pre-empted by a timer, the same context switching code is used, allowing all the threads to be managed in the same way.\n',1,'s25'),('v2sharma',5,5,'2025-07-28_22-27-27','Both pre-emptive(timer based) and cooperative (yield-based) context switching in our OS work together because they both use the same core mechanism to switch threads, which is the PendSV interrupt handler. When a thread yields voluntarily, it triggers an SVC or PendSV exception. This causes the CPU to enter the PendSV_Handler. Similarly, when a thread’s timeslice runs out (as tracked in the SysTick_Handler) the OS triggers the PendSV interrupt as well. In both cases, the PendSV_Handler runs the shared logic to save the current thread’s state onto its respective stack and load the next context from the next thread’s stack. The common use of the PendSV_Handler ensures that, whether a thread yields or is pre-empted by a timer, the same context switching code is used, allowing all the threads to be managed in the same way.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',1,1,'2025-05-14_09-48-27','Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is an embedded software library for the STM32 microcontroller. HAL contains a variety of functions used to interact with the microcontroller’s hardware without having to write low-level register code. It is used in general embedded projects as it allows users to write readable higher-level code, saving on time and making microcontroller programming more accessible.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',1,2,'2025-05-14_09-48-27','In standard C, printf is used to send characters to display on the console, however in embedded systems such as the STM32 board, there is no default output for printf. __io_putchar is a custom function allowing printf to function properly on the STM32 board by telling printf how to send characters to the console through UART.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',1,3,'2025-05-14_09-48-27','The debugger was used in this lab in order to check the values of the internal CPU registers such as psp and sp are updated correctly after resuming the code at the while loop. Printf is unable to show the values of the registers directly so the debugger is needed to check these values.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',1,4,'2025-05-14_09-48-27','We need to know the initial value of msp in order to find the top of stack in memory. Knowing this value allows us to subtract 0x400 (1KB) of space from the initial value of msp in order to find a location to put psp such that it does not overlap with the space that msp takes up on the top of the stack, avoiding stack corruption.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',1,5,'2025-05-14_09-48-27','To allocate space on the stack for multiple threads (n-threads) we could subtract a fixed-size blocks of memory from the initial value of msp. In this lab we subtracted 0x400 (1KB) of memory from the initial value of msp to find where we can put psp in memory. For n-threads, each location on the stack would be MSP_init_val - 0x400 * n = location of nth thread, for example thread 1’s stack starts at MSP_init_val - 0x400, thread 2 at MSP_init_val - 0x800, and so on.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',2,1,'2025-06-26_21-10-15','The PC (Program Counter) register holds the memory address of the next instruction to be executed. By using the jumpAssembly function, the address of the print_continously() function into PC from r0. This forces the processor to jump immediately to the start of the function, bypassing the normal C function call mechanism. Thus, by changing PC to hold the function pointer of print_continously(), the processor is forced to start executing the function without the normal function call.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',2,1,'2025-06-26_22-45-44','The PC (Program Counter) register holds the memory address of the next instruction to be executed. By using the jumpAssembly function, the address of the print_continously() function into PC from r0. This forces the processor to jump immediately to the start of the function, bypassing the normal C function call mechanism. Thus, by changing PC to hold the function pointer of print_continously(), the processor is forced to start executing the function without the normal function call.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',2,2,'2025-06-26_21-10-15','A system call is a way for user-level code to request a service from the operating system kernel. The STM32 board uses the SVC (Supervisor Call) to complete the system call, in this lab __asm(“SVC #number) is used to send the system call to the system call handler where the system call tells the handler which service to perform (e.g., print a message, switch threads).\n',0,'s25'),('v7sun',2,2,'2025-06-26_22-45-44','A system call is a controlled interface that allows user-level code to request services from the operating system kernel. In programming terms, it acts like a special function call, but instead of calling a regular function, the CPU switches into privileged mode to execute code in the kernel. This transition is necessary because many hardware-related operations (like accessing peripherals, managing threads, or printing to a console) are restricted in user mode for safety and security. In this lab the STM32 uses the SVC (supervidor call) instruction to initate a system call. When a user program runs __asm(\"SVC #number\"), it triggers the SVC_Handler, which switches execution to the kernel. The number passed to SVC identifies the requested service. The kernel uses this to determine which privileged action to take such as printing a message or starting a thread.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',2,3,'2025-06-26_21-10-15','To manually allocate space for the thread’s stack, you must start with the initial MSP value, subtract memory to avoid overlap, and point stackptr to the new space. Then you must push fake register values onto the stack in the order that the CPU expects during an interrupt return (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0, R11–R4), thus simulating the state of the CPU as if the thread had already been running. Th thread stack needs to store the register states because when the CPU switches context it must restore all registers to make the thread \"resume\" correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',2,3,'2025-06-26_22-45-44','To manually allocate space for the thread’s stack, you must start with the initial MSP value, subtract memory to avoid overlap, and point stackptr to the new space. Then you must push fake register values onto the stack in the order that the CPU expects during an interrupt return (xPSR, PC, LR, R12, R3–R0, R11–R4), thus simulating the state of the CPU as if the thread had already been running. Th thread stack needs to store the register states because when the CPU switches context it must restore all registers to make the thread \"resume\" correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',2,4,'2025-06-26_21-10-15','PSP points to the stack thread, however since we are setting a custom location in memory for the thread, which will be in a different location than the default stack (MSP), we must use the  __set_PSP function in order to set our stack pointer to a specific location in memory where we want to run our thread. In this case, the location of the stack pointer will be the location we calculated from finding the initial value of MSP and offsetting it by 0x400.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',2,4,'2025-06-26_22-45-44','PSP points to the stack thread, however since we are setting a custom location in memory for the thread, which will be in a different location than the default stack (MSP), we must use the  __set_PSP function in order to set our stack pointer to a specific location in memory where we want to run our thread. In this case, the location of the stack pointer will be the location we calculated from finding the initial value of MSP and offsetting it by 0x400.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',2,5,'2025-06-26_21-10-15','When a thread returns, the CPU will attempt to pop LR (return address) off of the stack in order to return the last part of the code it was at before the thread started. But since the thread was not called normally, LR will be pointing to a garbage value which will result in a jump to an invalid memory address, causing a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',2,5,'2025-06-26_22-45-44','When a thread returns, the CPU will attempt to pop LR (return address) off of the stack in order to return the last part of the code it was at before the thread started. But since the thread was not called normally, LR will be pointing to a garbage value which will result in a jump to an invalid memory address, causing a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',3,1,'2025-06-27_01-39-21','When creating an executable from multiple .c files, the process goes through three key stages: preprocessing, compilation, and linking. In the preprocessing stage, the compiler handles directives like #include and #define by inserting the full contents of header files into the source and replacing macros with their actual values. This step produces an expanded source file that’s ready for translation. During compilation, each preprocessed .c file is converted into a machine-readable object file (.o). These object files contain low-level instructions, but may still include unresolved symbols—such as function calls or global variables that are defined in other files. Finally, during the linking stage, all the object files are brought together. The linker resolves these external references, incorporates any required libraries, and arranges the program’s memory layout to produce a single, executable binary that the system can run.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',3,1,'2025-06-27_02-31-23','When creating an executable from multiple .c files, the process goes through three key stages: preprocessing, compilation, and linking. In the preprocessing stage, the compiler handles directives like #include and #define by inserting the full contents of header files into the source and replacing macros with their actual values. This step produces an expanded source file that’s ready for translation. During compilation, each preprocessed .c file is converted into a machine-readable object file (.o). These object files contain low-level instructions, but may still include unresolved symbols—such as function calls or global variables that are defined in other files. Finally, during the linking stage, all the object files are brought together. The linker resolves these external references, incorporates any required libraries, and arranges the program’s memory layout to produce a single, executable binary that the system can run.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',3,2,'2025-06-27_01-39-21','Including a .c file is comparable to copy-pasting all of the file’s contents directly into another file before compilation. This will end up breaking the separation of compilation units and leads to multiple definitions of the same functions or variables, due to the fact that the header file will also initialize the functions listed in the .c file. The linker expects each symbol, such as a function or global variable, to have only one definition across the entire program. If duplicates are found, the linker will fail, preventing the creation of the final executable. To avoid this issue, it’s best practice to use header files (.h) for declarations and only compile each .c file separately before linking them together.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',3,2,'2025-06-27_02-31-23','Including a .c file is comparable to copy-pasting all of the file’s contents directly into another file before compilation. This will end up breaking the separation of compilation units and leads to multiple definitions of the same functions or variables, due to the fact that the header file will also initialize the functions listed in the .c file. The linker expects each symbol, such as a function or global variable, to have only one definition across the entire program. If duplicates are found, the linker will fail, preventing the creation of the final executable. To avoid this issue, it’s best practice to use header files (.h) for declarations and only compile each .c file separately before linking them together.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',3,3,'2025-06-27_01-39-21','A thread is used to run a function written by the user in order to make the computer complete a task. Before any threads can be ran, the operating system must be initialized such that it can manage memory, stacks, and other internal processes used in order to run a thread. To do this, the kernel is used to prepare memory space for stacks, and by filling up registers in order to prepare the OS to handle and track threads properly. Once the OS initialized and ready to receive a thread, a thread can be created by providing the function it should run; the OS will allocate space for it and set up its initial execution state. Finally, the OS is started, which begins executing the thread. Since only one thread exists at this point, it will immediately take control and start running its function.\n',0,'s25'),('v7sun',3,3,'2025-06-27_02-31-23','A thread allows the computer to execute a specific function written by the user to perform a task. Before any thread can run, the operating system must first be initialized so that it can manage internal processes like memory, stacks, and registers. This is done through a function call to osKernelInitialize, which prepares the OS by recording the initial location of the stack and getting the system ready to support threads. Once the kernel is initialized, a thread can be created by calling a function such as osCreateThread, which takes the user’s function as input. This function allocates a dedicated stack for the thread, sets up its register values, and stores its starting execution state. After the thread is set up, the operating system can be started using another function call, osKernelStart, which begins execution by switching to the new thread’s stack and jumping to the function the thread was created to run. Since only one thread exists at this stage, it immediately takes control and runs.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',3,4,'2025-06-27_01-39-21','The stack pool is a dedicated block of memory reserved by the operating system to store the individual stacks of threads. It’s typically a large, aligned region (such as 0x4000 bytes) that ensures proper memory boundaries. Since each thread needs its own private stack to manage function calls, local variables, and return addresses, the stack pool is divided into equal-sized segments, with each segment allocated to a single thread. In future labs, as we add support for multiple threads, the kernel will assign each one a unique portion of the stack pool. This separation prevents stacks from overlapping and allows the OS to maintain independent execution contexts, which is crucial for enabling safe and reliable multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',3,4,'2025-06-27_02-31-23','The stack pool is a dedicated block of memory reserved by the operating system to store the individual stacks of threads. It’s typically a large, aligned region (such as 0x4000 bytes) that ensures proper memory boundaries. Since each thread needs its own private stack to manage function calls, local variables, and return addresses, the stack pool is divided into equal-sized segments, with each segment allocated to a single thread. In future labs, as we add support for multiple threads, the kernel will assign each one a unique portion of the stack pool. This separation prevents stacks from overlapping and allows the OS to maintain independent execution contexts, which is crucial for enabling safe and reliable multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',3,5,'2025-06-27_01-39-21','The kernel refers to the part of the system responsible for managing hardware resources, memory, and low-level operations that are essential to running the operating system. It contains functions and data that directly control or interact with the processor, memory, and other core components. In contrast, the user side consists of code that defines application behavior—such as threads or other high-level tasks—that depend on the services provided by the kernel. Rather than accessing hardware directly, user programs must request these services through the kernel. This separation adds a critical layer of protection by preventing user programs from unintentionally or maliciously altering low-level system behavior. It also simplifies development, as users can focus on writing their application logic without needing to understand or manage hardware details.\n',0,'s25'),('v7sun',3,5,'2025-06-27_02-31-23','In the context of our operating system design, the kernel consists of the code that manages low-level hardware operations, memory allocation, and other system-level resources. This code is written in dedicated kernel files—typically kernel.c for implementation and kernel.h for declarations. These files contain functions for initializing the OS, managing stacks, creating threads, and handling system calls. The user code, on the other hand, refers to the application-level logic written by the programmer. This includes the main function, user-defined threads, and any high-level behavior of the program. These are placed in separate files, most commonly main.c, which includes the necessary kernel headers to interact with the OS through function calls. This separation of code into distinct files supports a clear boundary between what the user is allowed to control (application logic) and what is managed by the OS (system resources). It enforces modularity and prevents direct, unsafe access to critical hardware features, ensuring the system is both easier to maintain and more secure. By organizing the project in this way, the responsibilities of the kernel and the user are clearly defined and safely isolated.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,1,'2025-07-27_13-06-43','With round-robin scheduling using queues, each thread’s TCB is put into the queue when it is created. When a thread yields, its corresponding TCB is removed from the front of the queue and is put back in at the back of the queue. The scheduler will then pick the thread at the front of the queue to run next; this process will then repeat until the queue is empty from threads running to completion. A benefit of this implementation is that it ensures all threads will get an equal share of CPU time while also being able to easily manage the order of execution without relying on manual index tracking.\n',0,'s25'),('v7sun',4,1,'2025-07-27_14-06-51','With round-robin scheduling using queues, each thread’s TCB is put into the queue when it is created. When a thread yields, its corresponding TCB is removed from the front of the queue and is put back in at the back of the queue. The scheduler will then pick the thread at the front of the queue to run next; this process will then repeat until the queue is empty from threads running to completion. A benefit of this implementation is that it ensures all threads will get an equal share of CPU time while also being able to easily manage the order of execution without relying on manual index tracking. However, the array-based approach to this concept is already implemented in this lab, using an index and modulo arithmetic, as an array already behaves like a circular queue. While a queue makes insertion/removal more dynamic and may simplify the logic, it also introduces more overhead, which may be unnecessary given that the number of threads is fixed and known. In this context, the array is more efficient and provides the same behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,1,'2025-07-27_14-20-26','With round-robin scheduling using queues, each thread’s TCB is put into the queue when it is created. When a thread yields, its corresponding TCB is removed from the front of the queue and is put back in at the back of the queue. The scheduler will then pick the thread at the front of the queue to run next; this process will then repeat until the queue is empty from threads running to completion. A benefit of this implementation is that it ensures all threads will get an equal share of CPU time while also being able to easily manage the order of execution without relying on manual index tracking. However, the array-based approach to this concept is already implemented in this lab, using an index and modulo arithmetic, as an array already behaves like a circular queue. While a queue makes insertion/removal more dynamic and may simplify the logic, it also introduces more overhead, which may be unnecessary given that the number of threads is fixed and known. In this context, the array is more efficient and provides the same behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,2,'2025-07-27_13-06-43','When the PendSV_Handler runs, the processor must be in handler mode and will have the some of the threads current registers saved. Inside the handler, additional registers will be pushed to the current thread’s stack manually in assembly. Next, the handler will save the current thread’s PSP into its TCB so that later the thread can be resumed without error. The scheduler is then called by the handler to select the next thread in the queue and load it from its TCB, and then the saved registers are popped from the stack. Finally, the handler will exit and restore the new thread’s full context and returning execution to it. This switches execution cleanly from one thread to another.\n',0,'s25'),('v7sun',4,2,'2025-07-27_14-06-51','When the PendSV_Handler runs, the processor must be in handler mode and will save the software-saved registers R4–R11 by pushing them onto the current thread’s stack. Then, the current value of the PSP (now pointing to the full context) is saved into the thread’s TCB. The function osShed() is then called in order to change load the next thread’s SP from its TCB and update the PSP to point to this new thread’s stack. Finally, the handler will restore the saved registers R4-R11 from the new thread’s stack, and when the handler exits, the hardware-saved registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR) are automatically restored from the same stack, thus cleanly switching execution to the next thread.\n',0,'s25'),('v7sun',4,2,'2025-07-27_14-20-26','When PendSV_Handler runs, the processor is in handler mode initially. The first thing to do is to load the current value of Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to get the current thread\'s stack. The handler then pushes onto the current thread\'s stack the software-saved registers R4–R11 through assembly instructions. This saves the whole context of the running thread. The new PSP is saved into that thread\'s TCB (thread_array[current_thread].stackPointer) so the thread can be resumed later. Next, the scheduler function osSched() is called to update current_thread and select the next thread. It loads the new thread\'s PSP from its TCB and calls set_PSP(.) to restore the stack pointer. Upon returning from osSched(), the handler pops registers R4–R11 from the new thread\'s stack. When PendSV_Handler exits, hardware automatically recovers the remaining registers (R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR) from the new thread\'s stack. The context switch is now complete, and execution proceeds in the new thread.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,3,'2025-07-27_13-06-43','If the program ran the highest priority task and never switched task unless the current highest priority task yielded, lower priority task would never get CPU time, causing starvation to happen. In this system, if the highest-priority thread never calls osYield(), it will completely monopolize the CPU, never allowing other threads to run. This will break the fairness and predictability of system and can cause critical low-priority tasks to never run.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,3,'2025-07-27_14-06-51','If the program ran the highest priority task and never switched task unless the current highest priority task yielded, lower priority task would never get CPU time, causing starvation to happen. In this system, if the highest-priority thread never calls osYield(), it will completely monopolize the CPU, never allowing other threads to run. This will break the fairness and predictability of system and can cause critical low-priority tasks to never run.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,3,'2025-07-27_14-20-26','If the program ran the highest priority task and never switched task unless the current highest priority task yielded, lower priority task would never get CPU time, causing starvation to happen. In this system, if the highest-priority thread never calls osYield(), it will completely monopolize the CPU, never allowing other threads to run. This will break the fairness and predictability of system and can cause critical low-priority tasks to never run.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,4,'2025-07-27_13-06-43','The benefits of calling C functions as opposed to writing everything in assembly are:\nWriting logic in C is much easier to understand, modify, and debug.\nC has clearer syntax and spell-check.\nC functions allow for complex logic to be written once and reused throughout a project.\nThe downsides of calling C functions include:\nAssembly is more efficient when running code, as C introduces overhead such as pushing arguments and saving registers.\nAssembly allows the designer to have more freedom when designing logic in the software, as can control things on an extremely low level.\nC is dependent on the C compiler and ABI to handle low-level processes.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,4,'2025-07-27_14-06-51','The benefits of calling C functions as opposed to writing everything in assembly are:\nWriting logic in C is much easier to understand, modify, and debug.\nC has clearer syntax and spell-check.\nC functions allow for complex logic to be written once and reused throughout a project.\nThe downsides of calling C functions include:\nAssembly is more efficient when running code, as C introduces overhead such as pushing arguments and saving registers.\nAssembly allows the designer to have more freedom when designing logic in the software, as can control things on an extremely low level.\nC is dependent on the C compiler and ABI to handle low-level processes.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,4,'2025-07-27_14-20-26','The benefits of calling C functions as opposed to writing everything in assembly are:\nWriting logic in C is much easier to understand, modify, and debug.\nC has clearer syntax and spell-check.\nC functions allow for complex logic to be written once and reused throughout a project.\nThe downsides of calling C functions include:\nAssembly is more efficient when running code, as C introduces overhead such as pushing arguments and saving registers.\nAssembly allows the designer to have more freedom when designing logic in the software, as can control things on an extremely low level.\nC is dependent on the C compiler and ABI to handle low-level processes.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,5,'2025-07-27_13-06-43','Yes the OS will still work, however when the single thread calls osYield(), the schedule will save the context of the thread and move on to the next one in queue, but since there is only one thread in the queue, the system will just switch back to the thread that just yielded. The system will still function properly but to take advantage of multithreading, there must be multiple threads in the system.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,5,'2025-07-27_14-06-51','Yes the OS will still work, however when the single thread calls osYield(), the schedule will save the context of the thread and move on to the next one in queue, but since there is only one thread in the queue, the system will just switch back to the thread that just yielded. The system will still function properly but to take advantage of multithreading, there must be multiple threads in the system.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',4,5,'2025-07-27_14-20-26','Yes the OS will still work, however when the single thread calls osYield(), the schedule will save the context of the thread and move on to the next one in queue, but since there is only one thread in the queue, the system will just switch back to the thread that just yielded. The system will still function properly but to take advantage of multithreading, there must be multiple threads in the system.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',5,1,'2025-07-27_19-25-52','First the user has to define a thread function that accepts a void* argument and prepares the data to pass into it. When calling osCreateThread(), the user must provide both the thread function and the data pointer for the data they want to pass into the thread. In the OS, in osCreateThread, the thread stack is constructed manually so that the pointer is placed in the location that will be the R0 register when the thread starts executing. The ARM calling convention is that R0 is where the first function argument is passed. When the context switch is completed and the thread starts, it pops R0 from the stack into the actual register, and the thread receives the pointer as it expects. This works because the stm32 processor architecture guarantee that when a thread starts, R0 is set from that stack position. By aligning the stack this way, the OS seamlessly passes user arguments into threads.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',5,2,'2025-07-27_19-25-52','Calling osYield() inside a context-switching handler will cause a recursive context switch, due to the fact that osYield() triggers SVC or PendSV which will force the processor to interrupt itself while already handling a context switch. This corrupts the stack and breaks the interrupt return sequence. Since the processor is already in a privileged exception state during PendSV, SVC, or SysTick. Triggering another exception inside this state can overwrite registers, destroy the link register (LR), or confuse the return address logic, thus causing a hard fault error.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',5,3,'2025-07-27_19-25-52','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms will increase scheduling resolution which would improve the responsiveness of the system, as threads can be pre-empted faster which would improve real-time systems where tight timing is needed. However, this increases the amount of overhead in the system, as the CPU must service SysTick interrupts 10 times more often as opposed to a system with a clock time of 1ms, which reduces the time available for actual thread work. Context switches become more frequent, adding interrupt latency and wasting cycles. For simple embedded systems or those with low real-time requirements, a 0.1ms tick is unnecessarily expensive.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',5,4,'2025-07-27_19-25-52','From the user’s perspective, co-operative multitasking requires the programmer to manually yield threads using osYield(), while also ensuring that the threads they create will share the CPU correctly. Pre-emptive multitasking on the other hand does not require osYield() as the OS will automatically yield the thread once a set amount (user set or default) of time has passed. From the OS’ perspective co-operative multitasking requires simpler logic, as it will just context-switch whenever the osYield() function is called. Pre-emptive multitasking on the other hand requires a timer (SysTick), tracking timeslices, and performing context switches automatically using PendSV. This adds complexity but gives better responsiveness and fairness between threads.\n',1,'s25'),('v7sun',5,5,'2025-07-27_19-25-52','It is possible to use both yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching, as both method trigger the same switch routine PendSV. The yielding thread calls osYield() which sets the PendSV bit in the interrupt controller manually. Meanwhile, the SysTick timer counts down a thread’s timeslice and triggers PendSV automatically when it expires. Since both of these methods use PendSV to switch context, the OS does not care which method was used to call the switch, it will just save the current thread’s state and restores the next one. This allows cooperative and pre-emptive threads to run side by side.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',1,1,'2025-06-27_13-40-13','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a software layer that simplifies the communication with a microcontroller’s hardware by offering standardized, high-level function calls. It enables developers to write code without directly accessing hardware registers, making embedded projects more straightforward to develop, understand, and maintain. HAL also enhances code portability across different devices and helps shorten development time.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',1,2,'2025-06-27_13-40-13','The __io_putchar function is used to redirect character output, typically from functions like printf to a specific hardware interface, such as a UART serial port. It handles sending each character to the chosen output device so that messages can be transmitted, often for debugging. This function is essential in embedded systems because they lack a default output like a computer monitor, so we must explicitly define where and how characters are sent.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',1,3,'2025-06-27_13-40-13','We used the debugger in this lab because it provides visibility into not just variable values and program flow, but also the hardware registers values, which we care about in embedded systems. Registers like PC, SP, and LR are key to understanding context switching and stack operations. printf cannot give this informaiton, as it only accesses higher-level variables and requires additional setup like UART. The debugger offers direct, real-time insight into the hardware state, making it essential for low-level tasks such as initializing thread contexts and confirming that registers contain the expected values during execution.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',1,4,'2025-06-27_13-40-13','The stack allocation method used in this lab involves manually setting the Process Stack Pointer (PSP) based on the Main Stack Pointer (MSP). Once we obtained the initial value of MSP_init_val, we subtracted a specific amount of memory (0x400) from it to calculate a safe and separate space for the PSP. We subtract 0x400 because we want the main stack to be this size. This new address became PSP_val. We needed to know the original MSP location so that we could avoid overlapping memory regions and ensure the PSP was placed in a valid, unused section of RAM, allowing both stacks to function independently without corrupting each other\'s data.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',1,5,'2025-06-27_13-40-13','To allocate multiple stacks at compile time, we need to assign distinct, non-overlapping memory regions for each thread’s stack, ensuring each one follows the Cortex-M4’s 8-byte alignment requirement. Rather than computing stack offsets dynamically from MSP_init_val—which risks overwriting the main stack—a safer approach is to preallocate the memory. This can be done either by reserving stack regions in the linker script or by declaring a statically allocated array in C, such as uint32_t thread_stacks[NUM_THREADS][STACK_SIZE], with each sub-array aligned properly. Each thread\'s stack pointer can then be set to the top of its allocated region using something like PSP_base[i] = &thread_stacks[i][STACK_SIZE], ensuring isolated stacks and proper compliance with ARM architecture rules.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',2,1,'2025-06-27_20-51-22','When we changed the Program Counter (PC) register to hold the address of the print_continuously function using inline assembly, the processor immediately began running that function. This is because PC determines the next instruction to run. By assigning it the address of our function, we effectively forced the CPU to \"jump\" there and start running the thread. The result was a continuous print loop, showing that we successfully redirected program execution.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',2,1,'2025-06-27_23-49-09','When we changed the Program Counter (PC) register to hold the address of the print_continuously function using inline assembly, the processor immediately began running that function. This is because PC determines the next instruction to run. By assigning it the address of our function, we effectively forced the CPU to \"jump\" there and start running the thread. The result was a continuous print loop, showing that we successfully redirected program execution.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',2,2,'2025-06-27_20-51-22','A system call is a controlled way for code running in user mode to request services from the operating system or privileged-level code. In this lab, we used the SVC instruction to trigger a system call, which caused an interrupt that let us run special functions (like starting a thread or printing a message) that the main program can’t perform directly.\n',0,'s25'),('x258li',2,2,'2025-06-27_23-49-09','A system call is a software-generated interrupt to request a service from the operating system. When a system call is made, the CPU switches from user mode to kernel mode, executes the system call, and then switches back to user mode. In this lab, we used the SVC instruction to trigger a system call, which caused an interrupt that let us run special functions (like starting a thread or printing a message) that the main program can’t perform directly.\n',0,'s25'),('x258li',2,3,'2025-06-27_20-51-22','Setting up a thread’s stack means preloading its memory with fake register values, so the CPU can “restore” them as if the thread had already been running. We manually pushed values like xPSR, PC, LR, and general-purpose registers (R0–R12) onto the stack in a specific order. This is necessary because when the CPU switches context, it expects to pop these values from the stack. By preloading them, we trick the processor into starting the thread as if it were resuming from a pause.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',2,3,'2025-06-27_23-49-09','Setting up a thread’s stack means preloading its memory with fake register values, so the CPU can “restore” them as if the thread had already been running. We manually pushed values like xPSR, PC, LR, and general-purpose registers (R0–R12) onto the stack in a specific order. This is necessary because when the CPU switches context, it expects to pop these values from the stack. By preloading them, we trick the processor into starting the thread as if it were resuming from a pause.\n',0,'s25'),('x258li',2,4,'2025-06-27_20-51-22','If we don’t set the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack, the CPU will try to load registers from the wrong memory location during context switching. Since the default stack pointer (MSP) is being used by the main program, using it for a thread would cause a conflict or access invalid memory, resulting in a hard fault. By switching to the PSP, we isolate the thread’s execution and give it its own dedicated stack space.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',2,4,'2025-06-27_23-49-09','If we don’t set the PSP to point to the new thread’s stack, the CPU will try to load registers from the wrong memory location during context switching. Since the default stack pointer (MSP) is being used by the main program, using it for a thread would cause a conflict or access invalid memory, resulting in a hard fault. By switching to the PSP, we isolate the thread’s execution and give it its own dedicated stack space.\n',0,'s25'),('x258li',2,5,'2025-06-27_20-51-22','If the thread function returned, the CPU would try to execute the return address that was stored in LR (link register), which may not point to valid or meaningful code. This would likely result in a crash or undefined behavior, possibly ending in a hard fault. Since there\'s no thread scheduler or context to return to, returning from a thread like this is invalid in a bare-metal setup.\n',0,'s25'),('x258li',2,5,'2025-06-27_23-49-09','If the thread function didn’t loop and instead printed something once and returned, the system would try to continue execution from the return address stored in the LR (Link Register). However, in this lab setup, the return address is likely not valid or not meant to be returned to—so the CPU would jump to an unexpected location in memory.\nThis would likely result in a hard fault or crash, because the processor ends up trying to execute invalid instructions, or results in undefined behaviors, since there’s no scheduler or mechanism to handle a thread that exits.\n',0,'s25'),('x258li',3,1,'2025-06-27_23-15-57','Each c files get compiled separately into an object file. This translates the c code into machine code. After all .c files are compiled, the linker does a process called linking that combines all the object files into a single executable file, such as lab.elf. During linking, the linker connects function calls and variable references across files, making sure everything points to the correct memory locations. It also adds any startup code and libraries needed to run the program.\n',0,'s25'),('x258li',3,2,'2025-06-27_23-15-57','Using #include <kernel.c> (or any .c file) is a bad idea because it bypasses the proper compilation and linking process. When including a .c file, the preprocessor pastes the entire contents of the .c file to the compiling file, which can lead to duplicate definitions of functions or variables and make the code harder to manage.  .c files should be compiled separately, then linked together. You should only #include .h files to share interfaces between them. Including .c files directly is a shortcut that breaks the build process and leads to hard-to-maintain code.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',3,3,'2025-06-27_23-15-57','From a user’s perspective, setting up and starting a thread involves several key steps.\nInitialize the kernel\nStart by preparing the operating system. This includes setting up memory space for thread stacks and getting the kernel ready to manage threads.\nCreate a thread\nCall a function that registers your thread with the kernel. You provide a pointer (stackptr) to the thread function, which is the code you want the thread to run. This function also sets up a separate stack and prepares the thread’s context so it\'s ready to run.\nStart the kernel\nOnce the thread is created, you start the kernel. This tells the system to begin executing the first thread by switching to its stack and context.\n',0,'s25'),('x258li',3,4,'2025-06-27_23-15-57','The stack pool is a block of memory reserved specifically to hold the stacks for threads and some internal OS operations. Each thread needs its own independent stack to store data like local variables, return addresses, and register values during execution. The stack pool ensures that each thread gets a separate, non-overlapping section of memory for this purpose. In this lab, the stack pool is used to allocate memory for a single thread, but in future labs it will be divided into equal-sized sections so multiple threads can be supported. The operating system also uses the stack pool for its own internal operations, such as handling system calls and managing context switching. Including the operating system’s use of the stack pool gives a complete picture of how this memory region supports both the execution of threads and the core functions of the OS.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',3,5,'2025-06-27_23-15-57','The “user” refers to the part of the code that defines the behavior of the program—things like the main function and any thread functions. This code is placed in main.c. In contrast, the “kernel” is responsible for low-level system operations such as managing threads, stacks, and context switching. Kernel-related code is written in kernel.c and its functions are declared in kernel.h.\nThis distinction is important from the user’s perspective because it separates the system’s internal mechanics from the application logic. Users can focus on writing their programs without needing to deal with how threads are managed behind the scenes. It keeps the codebase cleaner, more organized, and easier to maintain or extend.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,1,'2025-07-30_19-07-28','Using a queue instead of an array for round-robin scheduling offers a more natural and extensible approach to managing thread control blocks (TCBs). In this model, the scheduler dequeues the thread at the front of the queue to run it, and when the thread yields, it saves its context and enqueues the TCB back at the end. This directly mirrors the first-in, first-out behavior of round-robin scheduling without requiring manual index management, as with arrays. With a queue, each thread\'s control block is enqueued after it yields and dequeued when it\'s time to run, automatically maintaining the correct execution order without needing to track or update an index. This eliminates potential bugs related to index wraparound or off-by-one errors common with arrays. Queues also make it easier to add or remove threads dynamically, as you don’t need to shift elements or manage fixed-size slots like you would with an array.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,1,'2025-07-30_19-38-35','Using a queue instead of an array for round-robin scheduling offers a more natural and extensible approach to managing thread control blocks (TCBs). In this model, the scheduler dequeues the thread at the front of the queue to run it, and when the thread yields, it saves its context and enqueues the TCB back at the end. This directly mirrors the first-in, first-out behavior of round-robin scheduling without requiring manual index management, as with arrays. With a queue, each thread\'s control block is enqueued after it yields and dequeued when it\'s time to run, automatically maintaining the correct execution order without needing to track or update an index. This eliminates potential bugs related to index wraparound or off-by-one errors common with arrays. Queues also make it easier to add or remove threads dynamically, as you don’t need to shift elements or manage fixed-size slots like you would with an array.\n',0,'s25'),('x258li',4,1,'2025-07-30_19-48-56','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling can offer a more intuitive and flexible way to manage thread control blocks (TCBs), since it naturally models the first-in, first-out behavior of the algorithm. When a thread runs, it is dequeued from the front, and after it yields, it is enqueued at the back—automatically maintaining execution order. This avoids the need for manual index tracking and helps prevent common errors like off-by-one bugs or incorrect wraparound logic that can occur with arrays.\nHowever, it\'s important to recognize that the array-based implementation already behaves similarly to a circular queue by cycling through thread indices using modulo arithmetic. As a result, the benefit of switching to a queue may be mostly in code clarity or extensibility, rather than in core functionality.\nThere are trade-offs as well. A queue—especially if implemented with dynamic memory or linked structures—can introduce extra memory overhead and pointer manipulation, which might not be ideal in embedded systems with limited resources. In contrast, arrays offer predictable memory layout and better cache performance. Therefore, while a queue may simplify dynamic thread management or prepare the system for more advanced scheduling strategies, it also adds complexity and potential inefficiencies that might outweigh its benefits in a simple round-robin scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,1,'2025-07-31_15-28-32','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling can offer a more intuitive and flexible way to manage thread control blocks (TCBs), since it naturally models the first-in, first-out behavior of the algorithm. When a thread runs, it is dequeued from the front, and after it yields, it is enqueued at the back—automatically maintaining execution order. This avoids the need for manual index tracking and helps prevent common errors like off-by-one bugs or incorrect wraparound logic that can occur with arrays.\nHowever, it\'s important to recognize that the array-based implementation already behaves similarly to a circular queue by cycling through thread indices using modulo arithmetic. As a result, the benefit of switching to a queue may be mostly in code clarity or extensibility, rather than in core functionality.\nThere are trade-offs as well. A queue—especially if implemented with dynamic memory or linked structures—can introduce extra memory overhead and pointer manipulation, which might not be ideal in embedded systems with limited resources. In contrast, arrays offer predictable memory layout and better cache performance. Therefore, while a queue may simplify dynamic thread management or prepare the system for more advanced scheduling strategies, it also adds complexity and potential inefficiencies that might outweigh its benefits in a simple round-robin scheduler.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,2,'2025-07-30_19-07-28','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s state and restoring the next thread’s state. It first reads the current thread’s stack pointer (PSP) into a register and stores the contents of registers R4–R11 onto that thread’s stack. Then, it calls the osSched function to update the scheduler\'s state and choose the next thread to run. After that, it loads the new thread’s stack pointer and restores its R4–R11 register values from the stack. Finally, it updates the PSP and returns to the next thread using a special return code (0xFFFFFFFD) to exit the handler and continue execution in thread mode. This allows the system to safely pause one thread and resume another, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,2,'2025-07-30_19-38-35','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s state and restoring the next thread’s state. It first reads the current thread’s stack pointer (PSP) into a register and stores the contents of registers R4–R11 onto that thread’s stack. Then, it calls the osSched function to update the scheduler\'s state and choose the next thread to run. After that, it loads the new thread’s stack pointer and restores its R4–R11 register values from the stack. Finally, it updates the PSP and returns to the next thread using a special return code (0xFFFFFFFD) to exit the handler and continue execution in thread mode. This allows the system to safely pause one thread and resume another, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,2,'2025-07-30_19-48-56','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s state and restoring the next thread’s state. It first reads the current thread’s stack pointer (PSP) into a register and stores the contents of registers R4–R11 onto that thread’s stack. Then, it calls the osSched function to update the scheduler\'s state and choose the next thread to run. After that, it loads the new thread’s stack pointer and restores its R4–R11 register values from the stack. Finally, it updates the PSP and returns to the next thread using a special return code (0xFFFFFFFD) to exit the handler and continue execution in thread mode. This allows the system to safely pause one thread and resume another, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,2,'2025-07-31_15-28-32','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s state and restoring the next thread’s state. It first reads the current thread’s stack pointer (PSP) into a register and stores the contents of registers R4–R11 onto that thread’s stack. Then, it calls the osSched function to update the scheduler\'s state and choose the next thread to run. After that, it loads the new thread’s stack pointer and restores its R4–R11 register values from the stack. Finally, it updates the PSP and returns to the next thread using a special return code (0xFFFFFFFD) to exit the handler and continue execution in thread mode. This allows the system to safely pause one thread and resume another, completing the context switch.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,3,'2025-07-30_19-07-28','Modifying the OS to always choose the task with the highest priority is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority threads. If one or more high-priority threads never yield or are frequently runnable, the scheduler will never select lower-priority threads, effectively preventing them from ever executing. This breaks fairness and defeats the purpose of multitasking, where all threads are supposed to get CPU time. Round-robin scheduling avoids this problem by giving each thread a fair chance to run. Without additional mechanisms like priority aging or time slicing, a purely priority-based scheduler can make the system unfair and unpredictable.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,3,'2025-07-30_19-38-35','Modifying the OS to always choose the task with the highest priority is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority threads. If one or more high-priority threads never yield or are frequently runnable, the scheduler will never select lower-priority threads, effectively preventing them from ever executing. This breaks fairness and defeats the purpose of multitasking, where all threads are supposed to get CPU time. Round-robin scheduling avoids this problem by giving each thread a fair chance to run. Without additional mechanisms like priority aging or time slicing, a purely priority-based scheduler can make the system unfair and unpredictable.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,3,'2025-07-30_19-48-56','Modifying the OS to always choose the task with the highest priority is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation of lower-priority threads. If one or more high-priority threads never yield or are frequently runnable, the scheduler will never select lower-priority threads, effectively preventing them from ever executing. This breaks fairness and defeats the purpose of multitasking, where all threads are supposed to get CPU time. Round-robin scheduling avoids this problem by giving each thread a fair chance to run. Without additional mechanisms like priority aging or time slicing, a purely priority-based scheduler can make the system unfair and unpredictable.\n',0,'s25'),('x258li',4,3,'2025-07-31_15-28-32','Modifying the OS to always select the task with the highest priority is a bad idea because it means that only the highest-priority task will ever run. If that task is always ready (which is common in embedded systems), the scheduler will continuously pick it, and all lower-priority tasks will be completely ignored — not just delayed but never scheduled at all. This leads to starvation for all lower-priority threads, as they are permanently blocked from execution. Without additional mechanisms like priority aging or time slicing, the system lacks fairness and predictability. In contrast, round-robin scheduling ensures that every thread, regardless of importance, gets a chance to run, preserving the core principle of multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,4,'2025-07-30_19-07-28','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code much easier to write, read, and maintain, especially for complex logic like updating thread states or scheduling decisions. C allows the use of high-level abstractions, better type safety, and easier debugging. However, calling C functions from assembly introduces overhead due to function call setup (e.g., saving/restoring registers, stack alignment) and can make timing less predictable, which is critical in low-level systems like an OS kernel. It can also lead to failures if the calling convention is not followed exactly (e.g., wrong register usage or stack misalignment). Writing everything in assembly avoids these issues and gives full control over performance, but it\'s harder to understand, more error-prone, and much less portable. Thus, using C for logic and limiting assembly to minimal context-switching code is a good balance in this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,4,'2025-07-30_19-38-35','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code much easier to write, read, and maintain, especially for complex logic like updating thread states or scheduling decisions. C allows the use of high-level abstractions, better type safety, and easier debugging. However, calling C functions from assembly introduces overhead due to function call setup (e.g., saving/restoring registers, stack alignment) and can make timing less predictable, which is critical in low-level systems like an OS kernel. It can also lead to failures if the calling convention is not followed exactly (e.g., wrong register usage or stack misalignment). Writing everything in assembly avoids these issues and gives full control over performance, but it\'s harder to understand, more error-prone, and much less portable. Thus, using C for logic and limiting assembly to minimal context-switching code is a good balance in this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,4,'2025-07-30_19-48-56','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code much easier to write, read, and maintain, especially for complex logic like updating thread states or scheduling decisions. C allows the use of high-level abstractions, better type safety, and easier debugging. However, calling C functions from assembly introduces overhead due to function call setup (e.g., saving/restoring registers, stack alignment) and can make timing less predictable, which is critical in low-level systems like an OS kernel. It can also lead to failures if the calling convention is not followed exactly (e.g., wrong register usage or stack misalignment). Writing everything in assembly avoids these issues and gives full control over performance, but it\'s harder to understand, more error-prone, and much less portable. Thus, using C for logic and limiting assembly to minimal context-switching code is a good balance in this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,4,'2025-07-31_15-28-32','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code much easier to write, read, and maintain, especially for complex logic like updating thread states or scheduling decisions. C allows the use of high-level abstractions, better type safety, and easier debugging. However, calling C functions from assembly introduces overhead due to function call setup (e.g., saving/restoring registers, stack alignment) and can make timing less predictable, which is critical in low-level systems like an OS kernel. It can also lead to failures if the calling convention is not followed exactly (e.g., wrong register usage or stack misalignment). Writing everything in assembly avoids these issues and gives full control over performance, but it\'s harder to understand, more error-prone, and much less portable. Thus, using C for logic and limiting assembly to minimal context-switching code is a good balance in this lab.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,5,'2025-07-30_19-07-28','Yes, the OS will still work if only a single thread is created. When the thread yields, the scheduler will attempt to switch to the \"next\" thread, but since there\'s only one, it will simply select the same thread again. The context switch will still occur, saving and restoring the same thread\'s state, but functionally it will behave as if no switch happened. This is inefficient but harmless. The system remains stable, though it doesn’t demonstrate true multitasking since there\'s only one runnable task.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,5,'2025-07-30_19-38-35','Yes, the OS will still work if only a single thread is created. When the thread yields, the scheduler will attempt to switch to the \"next\" thread, but since there\'s only one, it will simply select the same thread again. The context switch will still occur, saving and restoring the same thread\'s state, but functionally it will behave as if no switch happened. This is inefficient but harmless. The system remains stable, though it doesn’t demonstrate true multitasking since there\'s only one runnable task.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,5,'2025-07-30_19-48-56','Yes, the OS will still work if only a single thread is created. When the thread yields, the scheduler will attempt to switch to the \"next\" thread, but since there\'s only one, it will simply select the same thread again. The context switch will still occur, saving and restoring the same thread\'s state, but functionally it will behave as if no switch happened. This is inefficient but harmless. The system remains stable, though it doesn’t demonstrate true multitasking since there\'s only one runnable task.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',4,5,'2025-07-31_15-28-32','Yes, the OS will still work if only a single thread is created. When the thread yields, the scheduler will attempt to switch to the \"next\" thread, but since there\'s only one, it will simply select the same thread again. The context switch will still occur, saving and restoring the same thread\'s state, but functionally it will behave as if no switch happened. This is inefficient but harmless. The system remains stable, though it doesn’t demonstrate true multitasking since there\'s only one runnable task.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',5,1,'2025-07-31_15-17-53','To pass an argument into a thread, the user defines the thread function as void thread(void* args) and passes a pointer to their data (e.g., a struct) to osCreateThread. The OS sets up the thread’s stack so that register R0 holds this pointer, following the ARM ABI, which specifies that the first function argument is passed in R0. When the thread starts, a context switch loads R0 from the stack, and the thread receives the argument as intended. This causes the thread to begin execution with its data already available. This mechanism allows a uniform and flexible way to pass user-defined input to threads without modifying the OS for different types or quantities of arguments.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',5,2,'2025-07-31_15-17-53','You can’t call osYield in the context-switching function because each of PendSV, SVC_Handler/Main and SysTick_Handler already executes in exception context on the main stack pointer (MSP). osYield() itself invokes an SVC instruction to pend a context switch and assumes it’s running in thread mode on the process stack pointer (PSP). Calling it from inside an ISR would break that PSP/MSP separation and violate the CPU’s exception‐entry semantics.\nIf this happens, you’ll end up nested-pending SVC or PendSV requests while still servicing the first one. The CPU will push another exception frame onto MSP, corrupt return addresses or stack pointers, and either stall lower‐priority exceptions or overflow the MSP—typically resulting in a hard fault or silent lockup.\nIt’s bad because it corrupts your scheduler’s state and stacks, destroys real‐time determinism, and makes your system crash or behave unpredictably. Handlers become far too long and you lose the precise 1 ms timing guarantees and safe ISR exit.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',5,3,'2025-07-31_15-17-53','Setting the OS fundamental clock to 0.1 ms instead of 1 ms increases the system’s timing resolution and responsiveness but comes with notable trade-offs. On the positive side, it allows for finer granularity in scheduling and delays, enabling more accurate timeouts and faster response to real-time events. This is particularly useful in time-sensitive applications or high-frequency control systems. However, reducing the tick interval also means the SysTick interrupt triggers ten times more often, significantly increasing interrupt overhead and CPU usage. This can lead to more frequent context switches, reducing the effective runtime of threads and potentially degrading performance. Additionally, the increased activity consumes more power, which is a concern for energy-constrained systems like battery-powered devices. Code that depends on tick counts may also require scaling or modification, adding to software complexity and the chance of bugs. Therefore, while a 0.1 ms tick rate offers improved precision, it should only be used when such timing accuracy is essential, and the system can tolerate the added computational and power costs.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',5,4,'2025-07-31_15-17-53','From a user perspective, the difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking lies in who controls when a thread stops running:\nIn co-operative multitasking, the user must explicitly call a function like osYield() to give up control. The OS will not interrupt a running thread; it\'s up to the thread to behave \"politely\" and yield periodically. This gives the user full control over timing but risks system stalling if one thread never yields. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically switches between threads at regular intervals using a timer interrupt (e.g., SysTick). The user doesn’t need to call osYield()—the OS enforces fairness and ensures all threads get CPU time, even if one misbehaves.\nFrom an OS perspective, the main difference is in timing and context switching:\nFor co-operative multitasking, the OS only switches context when the running thread voluntarily yields. This makes scheduling simpler and uses fewer resources since interrupts are not required for switching. For pre-emptive multitasking, the OS must use a timer (like SysTick) and handle interrupts. It tracks thread runtime, triggers context switches via PendSV when time runs out, and resets timers. This adds complexity but ensures responsiveness and robustness against poorly behaved threads.\n',1,'s25'),('x258li',5,5,'2025-07-31_15-17-53','Yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching can work together because they both ultimately rely on the same core mechanism: the OS’s ability to perform a context switch using PendSV. In co-operative multitasking, a thread voluntarily calls osYield(), which triggers PendSV to switch to another thread. In pre-emptive multitasking, the SysTick timer interrupt (SysTick_Handler) automatically triggers PendSV when a thread’s timeslice expires. Since both approaches use the same context switching infrastructure, they can coexist without conflict. The OS simply checks whether the switch was triggered by a yield or a timeout and resets the thread’s runtime accordingly. This allows threads that yield voluntarily and threads that run until pre-empted to share the CPU fairly within the same scheduling framework.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',1,1,'2025-05-15_13-04-34','HAL is Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it is complex code that has been written by the developer, ST Microelectronics, and it acts as the set up code for the board, and it could be accessed by the user to slightly configure setup to the project requirements, and can use its available functions to build simpler ones for user access.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',1,1,'2025-05-16_09-51-00','HAL is Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it is complex code that has been written by the developer, ST Microelectronics to abstract hardware complexity, and it acts as the set up code (operating system) for the board, reducing the need for low-level programming(and removing the need to learn and use different coding structures across different hardware platforms), and it could be accessed by the user to slightly configure setup to the project requirements, and can use its available functions to build simpler ones for user access. The user then writes code, and defines some basic functions to use and communicate with the board, using basic, mostly standard coding. HAL gives the user direct functions that access different board features and interface them, such as board timers, interrupts and GPIOs.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',1,1,'2025-05-16_11-25-18','HAL is Hardware Abstraction Layer, and it is complex code that has been written by the developer, ST Microelectronics to abstract hardware complexity, and it acts as the set up code (operating system) for the board, reducing the need for low-level programming(and removing the need to learn and use different coding structures across different hardware platforms), and it could be accessed by the user to slightly configure setup to the project requirements, and can use its available functions to build simpler ones for user access. The user then writes code, and defines some basic functions to use and communicate with the board, using basic, mostly standard coding. HAL gives the user direct functions that access different board features and interface them, such as board timers, interrupts and GPIOs.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',1,2,'2025-05-15_13-04-34','The __io_putchar function takes one argument from the user, and pass it to the HAL_UART_Transmit function for serial output. Both __io_putchar and printf live in the stdio.h library, and are set to communicate together, so, internally, whenever the printf is called, it passes the string to the __io_putchar, which then does the functions programmed in it.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',1,2,'2025-05-16_09-51-00','In embedded systems’ HAL, there is no built in printf function to talk to, so the __io_putchar function provides a layer that bridges high-level I/O to the hardware unit, by using the HAL_UART_Transmit function for serial output, which would make __io_putchar implementation different between different processors with different HAL code. Both __io_putchar and printf live in the stdio.h library, and are set to communicate together, so, internally, whenever the printf is called, it ultimately calls __io_putchar for each character. You control blocking blocking vs non blocking, buffering, interrupt vs polling, all in the defined function, and without it, you would get no output, or unpredictable behaviour when printf tries to call an undefined function.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',1,2,'2025-05-16_11-25-18','Because on bare-metal MCUs there is no default console device, the stdio library’s printf needs someone to provide the low-level output code to communicate with the board, otherwise, the C function cannot communicate with the board. In embedded systems’ HAL, there is no built in printf function to talk to, so the __io_putchar function provides a layer that bridges high-level I/O to the hardware unit, by using the HAL_UART_Transmit function for serial output and must be reimplemented for each MCU or peripheral to accommodate hardware differences. Both __io_putchar and printf live in the stdio.h library, and are set to communicate together, so, internally, whenever the printf is called, it ultimately calls __io_putchar for each character. You control blocking blocking vs non blocking, buffering, interrupt vs polling, all in the defined function, and without it, you would get no output, or unpredictable behaviour, or falls back to semihosting and hangs if no debugger is attached.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',1,3,'2025-05-15_13-04-34','The debugger lets the user stop at a certain point and examine the value stored in the memory at the every step of the code, and how they change, all that without affecting the memory, whilst using printf might have an effect on the values of the memory being examined, as it uses the stack.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',1,3,'2025-05-16_09-51-00','The debugger lets the user stop and examine execution at exactly the required instruction line, and view register contents such as MSP, PSP, or SP, directly without affecting or accessing them, which cannot done with printf, as using printf uses the stack and pushes data into it, triggers the stdio library, and depends on the initialized values, s it changes the values we are trying to examine, and may not even run until after the UART is set up. Using breakpoints and stepping through the debugger shows you the live state of the register, without modifying any values, and gives you insight on how and when every task is carried out.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',1,3,'2025-05-16_11-25-18','The debugger lets the user stop and examine execution at exactly the required instruction line, and view register contents such as MSP, PSP, or SP, directly without affecting or accessing them, which cannot done with printf, as using printf uses the stack and pushes data into it, triggers the stdio library, and depends on the initialized values, s it changes the values we are trying to examine, and may not even run until after the UART is set up. Using breakpoints and stepping through the debugger shows you the live state of the register, without modifying any values, and gives you insight on how and when every task is carried out.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',1,4,'2025-05-15_13-04-34','We got the new value for PSP_val by adding the known memory size of main stack (assigned as 0x400 for this program), or in this case subtracting because the stack is going downwards.finding PSP helps manipulate registers directly, without having to iterate to from the MSP location and find them, as, if done wrong could cause problems, so we would have a PSP to the registers so they can be accessed directly.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',1,4,'2025-05-16_09-51-00','To calculate a valid PSP value, you start from MSP_init_val at the top of the main stack, then go down. By subtracting the known main stack size from MSP_init_val, you get the PSP value, at the beginning of the free stack region, underneath the main stack. If the value of PSP was randomly guessed or not properly calculated, you might have landed somewhere outside the stack, or on a memory location that is not free, causing problems, so we would calculate and have a PSP value pointing to the registers so they can be accessed directly.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',1,4,'2025-05-16_11-25-18','To calculate a valid PSP value, you start from MSP_init_val at the top of the main stack, then go down. By subtracting the known main stack size from MSP_init_val, you get the PSP value, at the beginning of the free stack region, underneath the main stack. If the value of PSP was randomly guessed or not properly calculated, you might have landed somewhere outside the stack, or on a memory location that is not free, causing problems, so we would calculate and have a PSP value pointing to the registers so they can be accessed directly.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',1,5,'2025-05-15_13-04-34','The thread stack size is the same for all threads, but not always equal to the main stack size, and is defined in the operating system (can be defined by user). To allocate multiple stacks with this same method, you will need to know the size of each thread stack, and their position (in which stack), then use the already allocated psp to iterate to those locations and create an array PSP values. Then whenever you want to use one of them, use the __set_PSP function with that one’s value, then set it to be control using __set_CONTROL(2);\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',1,5,'2025-05-16_09-51-00','To allocate a thread stack safely and properly, you start from the known position at the top at MSP_init_val, and iterate to a block below it with the number of threads required*Thread_Stack_Size underneath it, after subtracting the main stack size. When subtracting the total size of the region above the required PSP, from the MSP_init_val, you reach the required address that is guaranteed to lie in stack space. You can therefore locate the address of each PSP, at the end of its stack partition, as no two stacks overlap, and all addresses remain consistent with the specified size in the OS, then make any stack the top of the stack pointer using __set_PSP(calculated PSP value at stack i), and turn it into PSP mode, by __set_CONTROL(2). As every thread position in the stack is distinct, and a multiple of the thread size, after subtracting the main stack size.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',1,5,'2025-05-16_11-25-18','To allocate multiple thread stacks on a Cortex-M4 (stacks grow downward and must be 8-byte aligned), you start from the known top of stack address, at MSP_init_val, and reserve a region directly below it subtracting N* Thread_stack_size, which yields a base_ address guaranteed to lie in the stack. For each I 0<=i<=N, use PSP_values[i] = base address - (i+1)*Thread_stack_size, where each PSP_value points to the high end of its own partition. As the stack grows downward, this offsetting ensures no two stacks overlap and all pointers remain within the reserved region, but this address allocation is distinct from context switching, which simply does the __set_PSP(PSP_values[i]), and turn it into PSP mode, by __set_CONTROL(2). As every thread position in the stack comes from a single, validated base and a fixed-size stride, all thread stack positions would be valid and none overlapping.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',2,1,'2025-06-23_11-54-58','When changing PC to hold the function pointer to print continuously, the print continuously function ran regularly, sticking me in the while loop printing Hello, PC!. This is because, we assigned the address of the function to PC, then let the processor to start executing from there, and as PC always points to the next instruction that will be run, the CPU was forced to jump straight into the function.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',2,1,'2025-06-23_12-43-13','When changing PC to hold the function pointer to print continuously, the print continuously function ran regularly, sticking me in the while loop printing Hello, PC!. This is because, we assigned the address of the function to PC, then let the processor to start executing from there, and as PC always points to the next instruction that will be run, the CPU was forced to jump straight into the function. This is similar to how a function call works, where the processor saves a return address and jumps to the function code. And by assigning the function directly to the PC, we skip that setup step, but it gives the same result, and the processor starts running the function. Therefore, changing PC is like calling the function at the processor level.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',2,2,'2025-06-23_11-54-58','System call is a call that goes to the operating system, asking it to do a specific function, and it is usually used to use a function requiring Kernel mode access, which is something only the OS has access to, so the program asks the OS to do this task using SVC command.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',2,2,'2025-06-23_12-43-13','System call is a call that goes to the operating system, asking it to do a specific function, and it is usually used to use a function requiring Kernel mode access, which is something only the OS has access to, so the program asks the OS to do this task using SVC command.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',2,3,'2025-06-23_11-54-58','Setting up a thread’s stack is preparing a section of memory so that the CPU can pretend it was an already running thread. Here, we manually assigned values onto the thread’s stack, so that the CPU would use the stack to restore the thread’s state. This way of setup tricks the processor into thinking the thread already existed and has been running, so it just uses it normally.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',2,3,'2025-06-23_12-43-13','Setting up a thread’s stack is preparing a section of memory so that the CPU can pretend it was an already running thread. We first reserve a valid memory area for the stack by assigning stackptr to a known memory location (a value below MSP). Then, we manually assigned values onto the thread’s stack in a specific order, starting with xPSR and PC, followed by LR, R12, R3, R2, R1, R0, and finally R11 to R4. This order is important as it it is the way the processor restores registers during an exception return or context switching, so it tricks the processor into thinking the thread already existed and has been running, so it just uses it normally. If the order is wrong, values will be loaded onto the wrong registers, and the thread will not run properly. By putting the correct values in their correct order, when the CPU switches to PSP, and retrieves the registers, it resumes the thread in the correct state, and runs the correct function with known register values. Which tricks the processor to behave as if it had already been running it.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',2,4,'2025-06-23_11-54-58','Because if we did not call __set_PSP(), the processor will use MSP instead of the new stackptr, which is a problem, because the thread’s register values were placed on the thread’s new stack, and if the CPU uses MSP, it will try to pop those values from the wrong place, leading to unpredictable behaviours.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',2,4,'2025-06-23_12-43-13','Because if we did not call __set_PSP(), the processor will use MSP instead of the new stackptr, which is a problem, because the thread’s register values were placed on the thread’s new stack, and if the CPU uses MSP, it will try to pop those values from the wrong place, leading to unpredictable behaviours.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',2,5,'2025-06-23_11-54-58','The program would most likely crash, or go to hard fault, which is because when the thread returns, the processor tries to pop the return address from the stack, but since we pushed in values to a fake stack, like 0xA, the processor will head to that address, which has nothing valid to this, therefore it will crash.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',2,5,'2025-06-23_12-43-13','The program would most likely crash, or go to hard fault, which is because when the thread returns, the processor tries to pop the return address from the stack, but since we pushed in values to a fake stack, like 0xA, the processor will head to that address, which has nothing valid to this, therefore it will crash.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',3,1,'2025-06-25_21-16-05','The process would start with preprocessing, where all includes are expanded and macros are replaced, and the processor produces a pure source file without any includes or macros. It then compiles, where each .c file becomes an object file, and during this, it checks fr syntax errors, and turns code into assembly, and to machine code. Then linking starts, where the linker merges all the object files into a big final one, and it resolves function calls and variable references between different files, and brings any necessary library code. The code separation allows for better modularity, as one file could be easily modified without needing to recompile the whole project.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',3,1,'2025-06-25_21-59-30','The process would start with preprocessing, where all includes are expanded and macros are replaced, and the processor produces a pure source file without any includes or macros. It then compiles, where each .c file becomes an object file, and during this, it checks fr syntax errors, and turns code into assembly, and to machine code. Then linking starts, where the linker merges all the object files into a big final one, and it resolves function calls and variable references between different files, and brings any necessary library code. The code separation allows for better modularity, as one file could be easily modified without needing to recompile the whole project.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',3,2,'2025-06-25_21-16-05','When you include a .c file, it is as if you pasted that file’s content into this one, which causes code for this file to be compiled more than once, breaking the compilation process, leading to errors such as duplicate symbols defined, so this is avoided, and .c files are compiled separately and connected through header files, which only declares them, and does not provide actual code.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',3,2,'2025-06-25_21-59-30','When you include a .c file, it is as if you pasted that file’s content into this one, which causes code for this file to be compiled more than once, breaking the compilation process, leading to errors such as duplicate symbols defined, so this is avoided, and .c files are compiled separately and connected through header files, which only declares them, and does not provide actual code.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',3,3,'2025-06-25_21-16-05','Start by calling a function to initialize the OS kernel, which sets up the memory and prepares the stack pool. Then for each of the threads to be run, call a thread creation function and provide it with address for the function you want it t execute. Lastly, call the function to start kernel, which begins execution of the first thread.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',3,3,'2025-06-25_21-59-30','To set up and start a thread, user should start by writing the function that the thread will run in the expected format, typically with no arguments, and looping continuously if it needs to stay active. Then the OS Kernel is initialized using a dedicated function, which prepares the system by setting up internal memory structures, such as stack pool, getting the kernel ready to manage threads. After initialization, the user creates a thread using the thread creation function, and passing the address of the thread function to it. This tells the OS which function should be assigned to the new thread. Finally, the user starts the Kernel by calling a function to begin execution of the first thread, which causes the processor to switch to switch the tread’s stack and start running the function from the beginning.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',3,4,'2025-06-25_21-16-05','The stack pool is a bock of memory reserved by the OS to hold the stacks for it and its threads, where each thread required its own stack to store function call data and values of registers at execution. The OS needs the stack space to manage context switches and operations on the kernel level, usually handles by MSP. When a thread is created, it takes a portion of the stack pool, and uses PSP to access its memory, so the stack pool ensures these memory allocations are done in a proper organized wa to avoid any corruption of memory. For future labs, this stack pool allows the OS to switch between multiple threads by saving and restoring their stacks, so if the pool was not managed properly, the OS might run out of space or corrupt already existing data, so it is important notice that the pool is shared between user’s threads, and the Kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',3,4,'2025-06-25_21-59-30','The stack pool is a bock of memory reserved by the OS to hold the stacks for it and its threads, where each thread required its own stack to store function call data and values of registers at execution. The OS needs the stack space to manage context switches and operations on the kernel level, usually handles by MSP. When a thread is created, it takes a portion of the stack pool, and uses PSP to access its memory, so the stack pool ensures these memory allocations are done in a proper organized was to avoid any corruption of memory. For future labs, this stack pool allows the OS to switch between multiple threads by saving and restoring their stacks, so if the pool was not managed properly, the OS might run out of space or corrupt already existing data, so it is important notice that the pool is shared between user’s threads, and the Kernel.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',3,5,'2025-06-25_21-16-05','The Kernel deals with the low level operations, like stack allocation and thread management, and it is put in the Kernel files, however the user writes logic like what each of the threads should do, and puts it in main. Separating them keeps the system organized, as users do not need to understand the details of Kernel to write a program, and the Kernel code would not be messed with.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',3,5,'2025-06-25_21-59-30','The Kernel deals with the low level operations, like stack allocation and thread management, and it is put in the Kernel files, however the user writes logic like what each of the threads should do, and puts it in main. Users interact with kernel using API calls (function interfaces exposed by the kernel to let users create threads, initialize OS, and start execution). Separating them keeps the system organized, as users do not need to understand the details of Kernel to write a program, and the Kernel code would not be messed with. The Kernel is responsible for managing hardware and memory access as well, to ensure that threads are run correctly without interfering with each other, or with system level resources, which allows the user to focus on writing programs while Kernel deals with critical system level tasks in the background\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',4,1,'2025-07-21_22-52-20','Round-robin scheduling can be implemented in a queue-based environment by placing every thread\'s control block in a circular queue. When one thread voluntarily relinquishes the processor, its Thread Control Block (TCB) is removed from the front of the queue and inserted at the rear, and the next-in-line thread becomes the running thread. This method effectively cycles through the threads in the order they were created, so each gets an equal share of CPU time. A queue instead of an array is more abstract and modular, which means there is easier scaling or expansion to future priority-based or time-sliced scheduling. Also, queues naturally include operations like enqueue and dequeue, which fit well with the task-switch and thread management semantics.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',4,1,'2025-07-21_23-15-18','The implementation of round-robin scheduling might involve the use of a queue, wherein the TCB (Thread Control Block) of each thread is stored in a circular queue. When a thread yields, its TCB is removed from the front of the queue and later added to the back, while the thread next in position is scheduled. This is a fair and relatively predictable way to share the CPU among competing threads—every thread gets a turn. Compared to having an array and an index in place, as the lab used, this queue-based approach provides additional abstraction and modularity, which can be useful if the OS needs to scale to support features like dynamic thread creation or priority-based scheduling. Of course, with increased flexibility comes overhead: enqueueing and dequeuing may be more cumbersome or slower than direct array indexing. For a smaller embedded system like the one in the lab, the array is simpler and faster to implement and is likely the better choice. The queue is more general and future-proof but may be overkill for this specific context.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',4,1,'2025-07-21_23-33-51','The implementation of round-robin scheduling might involve the use of a queue, wherein the TCB (Thread Control Block) of each thread is stored in a circular queue. When a thread yields, its TCB is removed from the front of the queue and later added to the back, while the thread next in position is scheduled. This is a fair and relatively predictable way to share the CPU among competing threads—every thread gets a turn. Compared to having an array and an index in place, as the lab used, this queue-based approach provides additional abstraction and modularity, which can be useful if the OS needs to scale to support features like dynamic thread creation or priority-based scheduling. Of course, with increased flexibility comes overhead: enqueueing and dequeuing may be more cumbersome or slower than direct array indexing. For a smaller embedded system like the one in the lab, the array is simpler and faster to implement and is likely the better choice. The queue is more general and future-proof but may be overkill for this specific context.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',4,2,'2025-07-21_22-52-20','The PendSV_Handler function is responsible for executing a context switch between threads when multitasking is needed. After a thread calls osYield, a specific bit is set in the system\'s interrupt control register to trigger the PendSV interrupt. Once higher-priority interrupts have finished, PendSV_Handler runs and begins by saving the context of the currently running thread. The thread\'s status is maintained by relocating important CPU registers to its stack.  The osSched is the scheduler function that is used to choose the next thread to run. After updating the stack pointer, all threads are restored to their original state by popping off the saved registers from the same stack. Through this process, the processor can cleanly switch between threads, enabling multitasking within the system.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',4,2,'2025-07-21_23-15-18','The PendSV_Handler function accomplishes the switch between threads in a cooperative multitasking environment. When any thread calls osYield(), bit 28 is set in the ICSR (Interrupt Control and State Register), and thus the PendSV interrupt is set as pending. PendSV executes once all higher-level interrupts are complete. First, the currently running thread context  from registers R4 through R11  is saved in assembly onto the thread\'s stack. The stack pointer (PSP) is recorded in that thread\'s TCB, indicating where that thread was halted. Then, osSched() chooses the next thread to run by updating the current thread index and retrieving that thread\'s saved stack pointer. The PSP is updated to point to the new thread\'s stack, and the saved registers are restored to start the new thread again. Thus, these pointers to the stacks and saved contexts allow threads to be switched at any time without losing execution state.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',4,2,'2025-07-21_23-33-51','The PendSV_Handler function carries out context changes of threads in the environment of cooperative multitasking. When osYield() is called, bit 28 is set in the ICSR, which triggers the PendSV interrupt to happen. This interrupt only runs once all higher-priority interrupts have finished. The very first thing it does is read the current value of the PSP at the moment it\'s triggered. This is important because it tells the system where the current thread\'s stack is located. Then it pushes the contents of R4–R11 onto that stack, since those registers aren’t automatically saved during exception entry. The current PSP value gets stored in the thread\'s TCB so it will start execution from the same point later.\nAfter that, the scheduler function osSched() kicks in to pick the next thread to run. The scheduler keeps track of the current thread and then grabs the PSP value from the next thread\'s TCB. After that, the registers R4 to R11 are taken off the stack for that thread, and everything goes back to how it was before. When it\'s time to switch back, the processor picks up the new thread right where it stopped. This whole setup lets threads switch smoothly without messing up their current status.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',4,3,'2025-07-21_22-52-20','It may seem like a clever scheduling tactic, but adding arbitrary integers to each thread\'s control block and selecting the most important thread instead creates significant scheduling difficulties. However, it is not an effective solution. The most significant issue is that higher-priority threads could be completely depleted due to the constant readiness of lower- priority thread. Under that circumstance, the less important threads may be unable to carry out their duties, which undermined multitasking a function. It also introduces problems of fairness, and can make the system less predictable.\'\". The scheduling approach can result in priority inversion, where a low-priority thread holds resources that are necessary for arbitrary tasks, and the system is rendered impotent unless advanced solutions like priority inheritance are implemented. Although priority-based scheduling is beneficial for advanced systems, it requires careful design to avoid these issues, and a simple round-robin scheduler like the one in this lab would be unwise.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',4,3,'2025-07-21_23-15-18','Setting the scheduler to always pick the highest-priority thread would take away the multitasking nature of any operating system. If the high-priority thread is always ready to execute, it will dominate the CPU entirely, while lower-priority threads are never scheduled. This blocks all other threads and eventually leads to starvation, essentially nullifying the purpose of having multiple tasks. This gets to the heart of the problem: the scheduler becomes biased and non-preemptive. Since this kind of priority-based scheduler lacks balancing mechanisms like time-slicing or priority aging, it violates the fairness of round-robin scheduling and undermines the system’s ability to function as a cooperative multitasking OS. In the context of a straightforward scheduler like the one used in this lab, always selecting the highest-priority thread is simply unfair, as it effectively halts multitasking altogether.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',4,3,'2025-07-21_23-33-51','Setting the scheduler to always pick the highest-priority thread would take away the multitasking nature of any operating system. If the high-priority thread is always ready to execute, it will dominate the CPU entirely, while lower-priority threads are never scheduled. This blocks all other threads and eventually leads to starvation, essentially nullifying the purpose of having multiple tasks. This gets to the heart of the problem: the scheduler becomes biased and non-preemptive. Since this kind of priority-based scheduler lacks balancing mechanisms like time-slicing or priority aging, it violates the fairness of round-robin scheduling and undermines the system’s ability to function as a cooperative multitasking OS. In the context of a straightforward scheduler like the one used in this lab, always selecting the highest-priority thread is simply unfair, as it effectively halts multitasking altogether.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',4,4,'2025-07-21_22-52-20','It may seem like a clever scheduling tactic, but adding arbitrary integers to each thread\'s control block and selecting the most important thread instead creates significant scheduling difficulties. However, it is not an effective solution. The most significant issue is that higher-priority threads could be completely depleted due to the constant readiness of lower-priority threads. Under that circumstance, the less important threads may be unable to carry out their duties, which undermines multitasking\'s function. It also introduces problems of fairness and can make the system less predictable. The scheduling approach can result in priority inversion, where a low-priority thread holds resources that are necessary for arbitrary tasks, and the system is rendered impotent unless advanced solutions like priority inheritance are implemented. Although priority-based scheduling is beneficial for advanced systems, it requires careful design to avoid these issues, and a simple round-robin scheduler like the one in this lab would be unwise.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',4,4,'2025-07-21_23-15-18','Using C functions such as osSched() and SVC_Handler_Main() inside the interrupt handlers improves not only code readability and debuggability but also maintainability, compared to coding the handlers from scratch in assembly. The high-level logic of scheduling decisions and thread bookkeeping is best expressed in the C language, which is especially helpful when the system is being modified later, or for beginners. On the downside, calling C functions from assembly code requires dealing manually with stack usage, calling conventions, and related details, and such calls might incur slight performance overhead due to extra instructions introduced by the compiler. On the other hand, writing the entire interrupt handler in assembly might make it faster and give more granular control over register usage; however, the code will be harder to write and understand for complex systems. The approach examined in the lab — that is, assembly for low-level context switching and C for everything else presents a good balance of clarity and performance, perfect for teaching and small embedded OS projects.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',4,4,'2025-07-21_23-33-51','Using C functions such as osSched() and SVC_Handler_Main() inside the interrupt handlers improves not only code readability and debuggability but also maintainability, compared to coding the handlers from scratch in assembly. The high-level logic of scheduling decisions and thread bookkeeping is best expressed in the C language, which is especially helpful when the system is being modified later, or for beginners. On the downside, calling C functions from assembly code requires dealing manually with stack usage, calling conventions, and related details, and such calls might incur slight performance overhead due to extra instructions introduced by the compiler. On the other hand, writing the entire interrupt handler in assembly might make it faster and give more granular control over register usage; however, the code will be harder to write and understand for complex systems. The approach examined in the lab — that is, assembly for low-level context switching and C for everything else presents a good balance of clarity and performance, perfect for teaching and small embedded OS projects.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',4,5,'2025-07-21_22-52-20','A single thread, such as the print_continuously thread from previous labs that was modified to yield after each print, will still function properly in this scenario, but the context-switching mechanism will not have any significant impact. If the thread osYield is called, then the PendSV interrupt will be triggered automatically, and the scheduler will make an effort to move to the next thread. As a result, the scheduler will select the same thread again since there are no other options. As a result, the thread will restart its execution immediately, and the system will behave as though no context switch was necessary. While this defeats multitasking\'s purpose, it is done without any system-wide harm. The operating system is capable of handling edge scenarios, such as having only one thread. The thread will proceed as usual, with no real switching required, and the kernel\'s scheduling logic will still be employed.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',4,5,'2025-07-21_23-15-18','A single thread, such as the print_continuously thread from previous labs that was modified to yield after each print, will still function properly in this scenario, but the context-switching mechanism will not have any significant impact. If the thread osYield is called, then the PendSV interrupt will be triggered automatically, and the scheduler will make an effort to move to the next thread. As a result, the scheduler will select the same thread again since there are no other options. As a result, the thread will restart its execution immediately, and the system will behave as though no context switch was necessary. While this defeats multitasking\'s purpose, it is done without any system-wide harm. The operating system is capable of handling edge scenarios, such as having only one thread. The thread will proceed as usual, with no real switching required, and the kernel\'s scheduling logic will still be employed.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',4,5,'2025-07-21_23-33-51','A single thread, such as the print_continuously thread from previous labs that was modified to yield after each print, will still function properly in this scenario, but the context-switching mechanism will not have any significant impact. If the thread osYield is called, then the PendSV interrupt will be triggered automatically, and the scheduler will make an effort to move to the next thread. As a result, the scheduler will select the same thread again since there are no other options. As a result, the thread will restart its execution immediately, and the system will behave as though no context switch was necessary. While this defeats multitasking\'s purpose, it is done without any system-wide harm. The operating system is capable of handling edge scenarios, such as having only one thread. The thread will proceed as usual, with no real switching required, and the kernel\'s scheduling logic will still be employed.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',5,1,'2025-07-22_16-28-09','Before passing an argument to a thread, the user must first define the thread function to take one void* argument. When the user calls osCreateThread, they give it a pointer to the input they want. This could be a pointer to a more complex structure with multiple fields or just an int. By correctly casting and dereferencing the argument inside the thread function, we can get the original data. The operating system sets up the thread\'s first stack frame so that this pointer is stored in register R0. This is because the ARM Application Binary Interface (ABI) says that the first function argument must be passed in R0. Pulling this value from R0, the thread receives the argument correctly when it first starts up. This method is flexible because a void* can point to any data type, and it ensures consistency by always using the same register for input. It also keeps thread creation modular and generic, regardless of what specific data the user wants to pass in.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',5,1,'2025-07-22_16-51-53','Before passing an argument to a thread, the user must first define the thread function to take one void* argument. When the user calls osCreateThread, they give it a pointer to the input they want. This could be a pointer to a more complex structure with multiple fields or just an int. By correctly casting and dereferencing the argument inside the thread function, we can get the original data. The operating system sets up the thread\'s first stack frame so that this pointer is stored in register R0. This is because the ARM Application Binary Interface (ABI) says that the first function argument must be passed in R0. Pulling this value from R0, the thread receives the argument correctly when it first starts up. This method is flexible because a void* can point to any data type, and it ensures consistency by always using the same register for input. It also keeps thread creation modular and generic, regardless of what specific data the user wants to pass in.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',5,2,'2025-07-22_16-28-09','Since PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, and SysTick_Handler are already included in the operating system\'s context-switching system, you cannot call osYield from any function that switches contexts. Recursive context switching, which is the attempt to initiate a new context switch while one is already underway, may occur if osYield is called inside of them. This causes stack corruption and behavior that is hard to predict because the thread state being saved and restored could be interrupted or overwritten. This kind of recursive or re-entrant context switching breaks the atomicity of these important routines and can make the whole system unstable. So, to make sure that threads are handled correctly and the system is stable, osYield should only be used in normal thread code and never from inside interrupt or handler contexts.\n',0,'s25'),('y24moham',5,2,'2025-07-22_16-51-53','The osYield may never be called within PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler (or SVC_Handler_Main), or SysTick_Handler because osYield triggers a context switch by setting the PendSV bit in the ICSR register, which then causes the processor to enter the PendSV interrupt. The said handlers anyway serve the purpose of the context switch; thus, calling osYield in these becomes a case of a recursive context switch where one is interrupted by another before it completes. This violates the Cortex-M4 interrupt model, which does not allow reentrant context switching because once you start doing a context switch, it is supposed to run to completion without any interruption. With violations of this sort, the system might try to save or restore registers while the stack is already being used, causing stack corruption, invalid memory access, and therefore, a hard fault. These faults are tough to catch and will rather destabilize the system. Therefore, for the smooth management of threads and system stability, osYield must be called only from thread-level code and not from an interrupt or context-switching handler.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',5,3,'2025-07-22_16-28-09','The operating system\'s timing resolution would be improved by lowering the fundamental OS clock from 1 millisecond to 0.1 milliseconds, which would enable it to react to time-sensitive events more rapidly and accurately. In real-time applications like motor control or high-frequency signal sampling, where a finer granularity is required, this might be advantageous. But there are also serious drawbacks to this change. Most significantly, it causes a significant overhead on the system by increasing the number of SysTick interrupts per second by a factor of 10. There would be less time for the processor to run actual thread code since it would be spending more time managing interrupts and switching contexts. Power performance and overall system efficiency may suffer as a result. Furthermore, sub-millisecond resolution is not necessary for many embedded applications, so the price of higher precision may not be justifiable. For general use, a 1 ms clock offers a practical balance between responsiveness and system overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',5,3,'2025-07-22_16-51-53','The operating system\'s timing resolution would be improved by lowering the fundamental OS clock from 1 millisecond to 0.1 milliseconds, which would enable it to react to time-sensitive events more rapidly and accurately. In real-time applications like motor control or high-frequency signal sampling, where a finer granularity is required, this might be advantageous. But there are also serious drawbacks to this change. Most significantly, it causes a significant overhead on the system by increasing the number of SysTick interrupts per second by a factor of 10. There would be less time for the processor to run actual thread code since it would be spending more time managing interrupts and switching contexts. Power performance and overall system efficiency may suffer as a result. Furthermore, sub-millisecond resolution is not necessary for many embedded applications, so the price of higher precision may not be justifiable. For general use, a 1 ms clock offers a practical balance between responsiveness and system overhead.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',5,4,'2025-07-22_16-28-09','Cooperative multitasking requires threads to be fair by independently calling osYield to allow others to run. A thread that refuses to yield may wind up using up all of the CPU and preventing the system from functioning. Because of this, it is particularly crucial that the programmer write thoughtful, well-behaved code. A different strategy is used by pre-emptive multitasking, which uses a timer such as SysTick to automatically interrupt threads after a predetermined amount of time, regardless of what the thread is doing. This makes the system more reliable and responsive because it doesn’t have to depend on the user to manage sharing. Because context switches only happen at predictable points where threads yield, co-operative multitasking is easier to implement from the OS\'s point of view. Preemptive multitasking is more complicated; the operating system must keep track of timers, monitor thread runtime, and trigger interrupts to enforce switching. Despite the extra effort, pre-emptive multitasking ensures fair CPU usage and better responsiveness, especially when combining multiple independently operating threads.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',5,4,'2025-07-22_16-51-53','Cooperative multitasking requires threads to be fair by independently calling osYield to allow others to run. A thread that refuses to yield may wind up using up all of the CPU and preventing the system from functioning. Because of this, it is particularly crucial that the programmer write thoughtful, well-behaved code. A different strategy is used by pre-emptive multitasking, which uses a timer such as SysTick to automatically interrupt threads after a predetermined amount of time, regardless of what the thread is doing. This makes the system more reliable and responsive because it doesn’t have to depend on the user to manage sharing. Because context switches only happen at predictable points where threads yield, co-operative multitasking is easier to implement from the OS\'s point of view. Preemptive multitasking is more complicated; the operating system must keep track of timers, monitor thread runtime, and trigger interrupts to enforce switching. Despite the extra effort, pre-emptive multitasking ensures fair CPU usage and better responsiveness, especially when combining multiple independently operating threads.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',5,5,'2025-07-22_16-28-09','The last lab exercise combined preemptive context switching with yielding in a single system. Because both approaches ultimately rely on the same underlying mechanism using the PendSV interrupt to trigger a context switch this combination works. The OS instantly resets its runtime and starts a switch to the next thread when a thread voluntarily calls osYield. In order to reduce the amount of runtime left in the currently running thread, the system timer independently calls the SysTick_Handler every millisecond. The OS resets this runtime to zero and preempts the thread by instructing PendSV to switch the context. Both cooperative and preemptive threads can easily share the CPU because the runtime is reset after each switch, regardless of how it was triggered. This hybrid strategy allows threads to either yield manually or be interrupted by the system, enabling more flexible and fair multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('y24moham',5,5,'2025-07-22_16-51-53','The last lab exercise combined preemptive context switching with yielding in a single system. Because both approaches ultimately rely on the same underlying mechanism using the PendSV interrupt to trigger a context switch this combination works. The OS instantly resets its runtime and starts a switch to the next thread when a thread voluntarily calls osYield. In order to reduce the amount of runtime left in the currently running thread, the system timer independently calls the SysTick_Handler every millisecond. The OS resets this runtime to zero and preempts the thread by instructing PendSV to switch the context. Both cooperative and preemptive threads can easily share the CPU because the runtime is reset after each switch, regardless of how it was triggered. This hybrid strategy allows threads to either yield manually or be interrupted by the system, enabling more flexible and fair multitasking.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',1,1,'2025-06-22_08-27-30','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of prewritten low-level functions that provide a standardized interface to the microcontroller\'s hardware. It allows developers to interact with complex peripherals using simpler, higher-level code, making embedded system development more accessible, portable, and less error prone.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',1,2,'2025-06-22_08-27-30','__io_putchar is a function used to redirect low-level character output, like when you use printf() — to a specific hardware interface, such as UART. It sends one character at a time, and it\'s needed because printf doesn\'t know how to reach your terminal or serial monitor unless you define where the characters should go. By implementing __io_putchar, you\'re telling the system, \"When I print something, send it over this UART connection.\"\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',1,3,'2025-06-22_08-27-30','We had to use the debugger in this lab because printf only shows what the program decides to output. It does not let us see what is really happening inside the system. The debugger lets us pause the program and look directly at registers, memory, and the stack. This is important when working with things like context switching or setting up a new stack pointer. If something goes wrong, the program might crash before any printf message can run. Using the debugger helps us catch those problems and understand exactly what went wrong.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',1,4,'2025-06-22_08-27-30','We started with the value of MSP_init_val, which tells us where the main stack begins in memory. To get a new value for PSP_val, we subtracted a fixed number of bytes from MSP_init_val, usually 0x400, to create space for a new stack without overlapping the main one. This new lower address became the base of the process stack. We needed to know the initial location of MSP so that the PSP would point to a valid and unused memory region. If we chose a random address, we could accidentally place the PSP in a part of memory already used by the main stack, code, or peripherals, which would lead to crashes or unexpected behavior.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',1,5,'2025-06-22_08-27-30','If we wanted to allocate multiple stacks for several threads using the same method, we would need to divide the available memory below MSP_init_val into separate non-overlapping regions, one for each stack. Since the number of threads is known at compile-time, we could define a constant stack size and calculate each stack’s starting address by subtracting fixed offsets from MSP_init_val. For example, the first stack could start at MSP_init_val - 0x400, the second at MSP_init_val - 0x800, the third at MSP_init_val - 0xC00, and so on. This way, each stack would have its own space in memory, and we would avoid collisions between them. The only change needed in the code would be to repeat the calculation for each thread’s stack pointer using its index.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',2,1,'2025-06-22_08-17-25','In this lab, when I changed the PC to hold the function pointer to print_continuously, I was telling the processor to jump to that function and start executing it as if it were returning from an interrupt or exception. Since I had already set up the stack with a fake context (including xPSR, PC, and register values), the processor used that stack to load all the necessary state. As a result, it started running print_continuously like a regular thread, even though it wasn’t created by an operating system. This showed that I could manually simulate a thread switch just by setting up the stack and pointing the PC to the right function.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',2,1,'2025-06-22_13-20-08','In this lab, when I changed the PC to hold the function pointer to print_continuously, I was telling the processor to jump to that function and start executing it as if it were returning from an interrupt or exception. Since I had already set up the stack with a fake context (including xPSR, PC, and register values), the processor used that stack to load all the necessary state. As a result, it started running print_continuously like a regular thread, even though it wasn’t created by an operating system. This showed that I could manually simulate a thread switch just by setting up the stack and pointing the PC to the right function.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',2,2,'2025-06-22_08-17-25','A system call is a request from a program to the operating system to perform a task that the program is not allowed to do by itself. This includes things like accessing hardware, managing memory, or handling context switches. In embedded systems, system calls are often triggered by the SVC instruction, which causes the processor to jump into a special handler in privileged mode. This allows the operating system to safely take over and carry out the requested action before returning control to the program.\n',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',2,2,'2025-06-22_13-20-08','A system call is a special type of function call that allows user-level programs to interact with the operating system kernel. Unlike regular function calls, system calls serve as a controlled gateway for user code to request privileged services such as accessing hardware, managing memory, or creating threads that are otherwise prohibited in unprivileged mode. This interaction is typically performed using a dedicated instruction like SVC (Supervisor Call), which causes a software interrupt. The processor then switches into privileged mode and transfers control to the kernel\'s system call handler. By requiring all sensitive operations to go through these system calls, the OS ensures that hardware resources are protected from accidental or malicious misuse by untrusted user code, maintaining system stability and security.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',2,3,'2025-06-22_08-17-25','To set up a thread’s stack, we manually created the same stack frame the processor expects to see when returning from an interrupt. This includes saving values for all registers that would normally be pushed during a real context switch — like r0 to r12, LR, and especially the program counter (PC) and xPSR. By writing these values in the correct order to a spot in memory and then setting the PSP to point to that spot, we tricked the processor into thinking it\'s resuming an interrupted thread. When we called runFirstThread(), the processor popped all those values off the stack, loaded them into the CPU, and jumped to the PC we gave it — which is the print_continuously function. Storing register values on the stack is important because that\'s how the system knows what the thread was doing and where to resume it from.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',2,3,'2025-06-22_13-20-08','To set up a thread’s stack, we manually created the same stack frame the processor expects to see when returning from an interrupt. This includes saving values for all registers that would normally be pushed during a real context switch — like r0 to r12, LR, and especially the program counter (PC) and xPSR. By writing these values in the correct order to a spot in memory and then setting the PSP to point to that spot, we tricked the processor into thinking it\'s resuming an interrupted thread. When we called runFirstThread(), the processor popped all those values off the stack, loaded them into the CPU, and jumped to the PC we gave it — which is the print_continuously function. Storing register values on the stack is important because that\'s how the system knows what the thread was doing and where to resume it from.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',2,4,'2025-06-22_08-17-25','If we didn’t call __set_PSP before running the thread in the SVC_Handler_Main switch, the code would crash in a hard fault because the processor wouldn’t know where the process stack is. When we run a thread by popping values off the stack (like the program counter and registers), the CPU expects the PSP to already be pointing to a valid memory location that holds that information. If PSP is not set, it could be pointing to garbage or an invalid address, and when the processor tries to load values from that location, it causes a memory access error, which triggers a hard fault. Setting the PSP ensures the processor has a valid starting point to restore the thread’s state and continue execution safely.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',2,4,'2025-06-22_13-20-08','If we didn’t call __set_PSP before running the thread in the SVC_Handler_Main switch, the code would crash in a hard fault because the processor wouldn’t know where the process stack is. When we run a thread by popping values off the stack (like the program counter and registers), the CPU expects the PSP to already be pointing to a valid memory location that holds that information. If PSP is not set, it could be pointing to garbage or an invalid address, and when the processor tries to load values from that location, it causes a memory access error, which triggers a hard fault. Setting the PSP ensures the processor has a valid starting point to restore the thread’s state and continue execution safely.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',2,5,'2025-06-22_08-17-25','If the thread function didn’t loop and just printed something once and returned, the program would crash, likely with a hard fault. That’s because when the function returns, it tries to pop a return address from the stack using the Link Register (LR). But in our lab setup, we manually built the stack and didn’t set up a proper return path. The LR value on the stack is either missing, invalid, or arbitrary. So when the CPU tries to return to whatever is in LR, it jumps to a bad address and crashes. In a real operating system, the thread would be cleaned up or handed back to a scheduler — but we don’t have that, so it just breaks.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',2,5,'2025-06-22_13-20-08','If the thread function didn’t loop and just printed something once and returned, the program would crash, likely with a hard fault. That’s because when the function returns, it tries to pop a return address from the stack using the Link Register (LR). But in our lab setup, we manually built the stack and didn’t set up a proper return path. The LR value on the stack is either missing, invalid, or arbitrary. So when the CPU tries to return to whatever is in LR, it jumps to a bad address and crashes. In a real operating system, the thread would be cleaned up or handed back to a scheduler — but we don’t have that, so it just breaks.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,1,'2025-06-26_17-51-06','',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,1,'2025-06-27_10-14-43','Every .c file is compiled by the compiler into an object file, which contains machine code for that file. The linker then combines all these object files, along with any necessary libraries, into a single executable by resolving function calls, and variable references between each file. This process allows separate pieces of code to work together as one complete program.\n',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,1,'2025-06-27_10-49-22','The compilation process starts with preprocessing, where directives like #include and #define are handled, combining headers and expanding macros into each source file. Each preprocessed .c file is then compiled into an object file, translating the source code into machine code while performing syntax and type checks. Finally, the linker combines all the object files and any required libraries into a single executable by resolving function calls, variable references, and library dependencies across the program.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,1,'2025-06-27_11-34-07','The compilation process starts with preprocessing, where directives like #include and #define are handled, combining headers and expanding macros into each source file. Each preprocessed .c file is then compiled into an object file, translating the source code into machine code while performing syntax and type checks. Finally, the linker combines all the object files and any required libraries into a single executable by resolving function calls, variable references, and library dependencies across the program.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,1,'2025-06-27_11-48-49','The compilation process starts with preprocessing, where directives like #include and #define are handled, combining headers and expanding macros into each source file. Each preprocessed .c file is then compiled into an object file, translating the source code into machine code while performing syntax and type checks. Finally, the linker combines all the object files and any required libraries into a single executable by resolving function calls, variable references, and library dependencies across the program.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,1,'2025-06-27_12-27-20','The compilation process starts with preprocessing, where directives like #include and #define are handled, combining headers and expanding macros into each source file. Each preprocessed .c file is then compiled into an object file, translating the source code into machine code while performing syntax and type checks. Finally, the linker combines all the object files and any required libraries into a single executable by resolving function calls, variable references, and library dependencies across the program.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,2,'2025-06-26_17-51-06','',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,2,'2025-06-27_10-14-43','Using #include on a *.c file is a bad idea because it causes the entire source file to be copied into the current file during preprocessing, which can lead to multiple definitions of functions and variables if the same *.c file is included elsewhere. This breaks the purpose of separate compilation, leads to build errors, and creates messy, hard-to-maintain code.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,2,'2025-06-27_10-49-22','Using #include on a *.c file is a bad idea because it causes the entire source file to be copied into the current file during preprocessing, which can lead to multiple definitions of functions and variables if the same *.c file is included elsewhere. This breaks the purpose of separate compilation, leads to build errors, and creates messy, hard-to-maintain code.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,2,'2025-06-27_11-34-07','Using #include on a *.c file is a bad idea because it causes the entire source file to be copied into the current file during preprocessing, which can lead to multiple definitions of functions and variables if the same *.c file is included elsewhere. This breaks the purpose of separate compilation, leads to build errors, and creates messy, hard-to-maintain code.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,2,'2025-06-27_11-48-49','Using #include on a *.c file is a bad idea because it causes the entire source file to be copied into the current file during preprocessing, which can lead to multiple definitions of functions and variables if the same *.c file is included elsewhere. This breaks the purpose of separate compilation, leads to build errors, and creates messy, hard-to-maintain code.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,2,'2025-06-27_12-27-20','Using #include on a *.c file is a bad idea because it causes the entire source file to be copied into the current file during preprocessing, which can lead to multiple definitions of functions and variables if the same *.c file is included elsewhere. This breaks the purpose of separate compilation, leads to build errors, and creates messy, hard-to-maintain code.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,3,'2025-06-26_17-51-06','',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,3,'2025-06-27_10-14-43','To set up and start a thread, the user first creates the thread by providing the function it should run. Then the user initializes the kernel and starts it, allowing the system to manage and run the threads automatically.\n',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,3,'2025-06-27_10-49-22','To set up and start a thread, the user first calls a thread creation function to define the function the thread will run and register it with the kernel. The user then calls a kernel initialization function to prepare the system, and finally starts the kernel using a specific start function, allowing the system to run the threads automatically.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,3,'2025-06-27_11-34-07','To set up and start a thread, the user first calls a thread creation function to define the function the thread will run and register it with the kernel. The user then calls a kernel initialization function to prepare the system, and finally starts the kernel using a specific start function, allowing the system to run the threads automatically.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,3,'2025-06-27_11-48-49','To set up and start a thread, the user first calls a thread creation function to define the function the thread will run and register it with the kernel. The user then calls a kernel initialization function to prepare the system, and finally starts the kernel using a specific start function, allowing the system to run the threads automatically.\n',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,3,'2025-06-27_12-27-20','The user is responsible for writing the thread function that defines what the thread will do. To start a thread, the user writes this function, calls a thread creation function to pass the address of this function to the kernel for registration, initializes the kernel to prepare the system, and then starts the kernel so the system can manage and run the threads automatically.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,4,'2025-06-26_17-51-06','',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,4,'2025-06-27_10-14-43','The stack pool is a reserved memory region used to store the individual stacks for each thread. In a future lab, the stack pool will allow multiple threads to coexist by providing each thread with its own separate stack space, which is essential for saving their local variables, return addresses, and context during execution and switching.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,4,'2025-06-27_10-49-22','The stack pool is a reserved memory region used to store the individual stacks for each thread. In a future lab, the stack pool will allow multiple threads to coexist by providing each thread with its own separate stack space, which is essential for saving their local variables, return addresses, and context during execution and switching.\n',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,4,'2025-06-27_11-34-07','The stack pool is a reserved memory area used by both the operating system and user threads, providing each thread with its own stack to store local variables, return addresses, and execution context. The OS uses the stack pool to manage memory allocation for threads in a controlled way, ensuring that each stack is isolated and sized appropriately, which will enable multiple threads to run safely and switch reliably in future labs.\n',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,4,'2025-06-27_11-48-49','The stack pool is a reserved memory area used by both the operating system and user threads, and it is subdivided into smaller, fixed-size regions that are allocated to each thread to provide its own stack space. This subdivision allows the OS to manage memory efficiently and ensure that each thread has its own isolated stack for storing local variables, return addresses, and execution context, which will enable multiple threads to run safely and switch reliably in future labs.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,4,'2025-06-27_12-27-20','The stack pool is a reserved memory area used by both the operating system and user threads, and it is subdivided into smaller, fixed-size regions that are allocated to each thread to provide its own stack space. This subdivision allows the OS to manage memory efficiently and ensure that each thread has its own isolated stack for storing local variables, return addresses, and execution context, which will enable multiple threads to run safely and switch reliably in future labs.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,5,'2025-06-26_17-51-06','',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,5,'2025-06-27_10-14-43','The \"user\" code goes into files like main.c where the user defines what the program should do, while the \"kernel\" code goes into files like kernel.c where the system controls thread management and low-level operations. This distinction is important because it keeps the user’s application logic separate from the core system functions, making it easier for the user to write programs without needing to manage the internal workings of the operating system.\n',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,5,'2025-06-27_10-49-22','The \"user\" code goes into files like main.c where the user writes application logic and makes API calls to request services from the kernel, while the \"kernel\" code goes into files like kernel.c where the system manages hardware, memory, and thread execution. This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on building their program without needing to handle low-level hardware details, relying on the kernel to provide controlled access to system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,5,'2025-06-27_11-34-07','The \"user\" code goes into files like main.c where the user writes application logic and makes API calls to request services from the kernel, while the \"kernel\" code goes into files like kernel.c where the system manages hardware, memory, and thread execution. This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on building their program without needing to handle low-level hardware details, relying on the kernel to provide controlled access to system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,5,'2025-06-27_11-48-49','The \"user\" code goes into files like main.c where the user writes application logic and makes API calls to request services from the kernel, while the \"kernel\" code goes into files like kernel.c where the system manages hardware, memory, and thread execution. This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on building their program without needing to handle low-level hardware details, relying on the kernel to provide controlled access to system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',3,5,'2025-06-27_12-27-20','The \"user\" code goes into files like main.c where the user writes application logic and makes API calls to request services from the kernel, while the \"kernel\" code goes into files like kernel.c where the system manages hardware, memory, and thread execution. This separation is important because it allows the user to focus on building their program without needing to handle low-level hardware details, relying on the kernel to provide controlled access to system resources.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',4,1,'2025-07-23_16-48-46','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling allows threads to be managed in a first-in, first-out manner. When a thread finishes its timeslice or yields, it is dequeued from the front and enqueued at the back, naturally rotating execution order. This is simpler than using an array, which requires tracking indices and manually wrapping around. A queue also makes it easier to remove blocked threads and reinsert them later, supporting more dynamic scheduling. Overall, queues improve clarity, reduce manual control logic, and provide a flexible foundation for advanced schedulers.\n',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',4,1,'2025-07-23_17-09-56','Using a queue for round-robin scheduling provides a first-in, first-out structure where the currently running thread is dequeued and re-enqueued after yielding or completing its timeslice. This creates a natural rotation of threads. In contrast, the existing method uses an array and a current thread index, requiring manual index incrementing and wraparound logic. Functionally, both methods can achieve the same scheduling behavior, but the array-based approach is slightly more efficient in terms of overhead, since indexing is a constant-time operation with minimal memory use. However, a queue provides greater flexibility: it simplifies removing or reordering threads (e.g. blocked or newly created ones) without manually shifting elements or tracking multiple indices. This makes queues more scalable and extensible in larger or more dynamic systems, despite minor overhead from pointer manipulation or heap usage. Overall, while a queue may introduce slightly more complexity, it improves modularity and adaptability in systems where threads are frequently added, removed, or reprioritized.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',4,2,'2025-07-23_16-48-46','The PendSV_Handler function performs a context switch by saving the current thread’s CPU state and loading the next thread’s state. When it runs, it first stores the current thread’s registers (like R4–R11) onto its stack and updates the thread’s saved stack pointer in the thread table. Then, it selects the next ready thread (based on round-robin or priority), loads its saved stack pointer, restores its registers from that stack, and sets the processor’s PSP (Process Stack Pointer) to continue running that thread. This switches execution cleanly from one thread to another without returning to the original.\n',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',4,2,'2025-07-23_17-09-56','The PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch by first reading the current thread’s Process Stack Pointer (PSP) to locate its stack. It then saves the CPU context—specifically registers R4 through R11—onto this stack so the thread’s state can be preserved. After saving the state, the handler calls a scheduling function such as osSched to determine which thread should run next. Once the next thread is selected, the handler retrieves that thread’s saved stack pointer from the thread table, updates the PSP to this new value, and restores the previously saved registers R4 through R11 from the new thread’s stack. This process resumes the selected thread\'s execution exactly where it left off. By carefully managing PSP and register storage, PendSV_Handler switches execution cleanly and safely between threads.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',4,3,'2025-07-23_16-48-46','Always choosing the highest-priority task is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation. If one thread has the highest priority and never blocks or yields, lower-priority threads may never get a chance to run. This breaks fairness and can prevent essential background tasks from executing, especially in real-time systems. A better approach is to combine priority with aging or timeslicing to ensure that all tasks eventually make progress.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',4,3,'2025-07-23_17-09-56','Always choosing the highest-priority task is a bad idea because it can lead to starvation. If one thread has the highest priority and never blocks or yields, lower-priority threads may never get a chance to run. This breaks fairness and can prevent essential background tasks from executing, especially in real-time systems. A better approach is to combine priority with aging or timeslicing to ensure that all tasks eventually make progress.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',4,4,'2025-07-23_16-48-46','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code easier to read, write, and maintain. C offers higher-level constructs, type safety, and better debugging support, which speeds up development and reduces errors. It also allows easier integration with other kernel logic written in C. However, the downside is that calling C functions adds overhead—extra instructions and stack usage—which can impact performance and timing accuracy, especially in low-level or time-critical operations. Assembly is faster and gives full control over the CPU, but it’s harder to write, more error-prone, and much less portable.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',4,4,'2025-07-23_17-09-56','Calling C functions like osSched and SVC_HandlerMain makes the code easier to read, write, and maintain. C offers higher-level constructs, type safety, and better debugging support, which speeds up development and reduces errors. It also allows easier integration with other kernel logic written in C. However, the downside is that calling C functions adds overhead—extra instructions and stack usage—which can impact performance and timing accuracy, especially in low-level or time-critical operations. Assembly is faster and gives full control over the CPU, but it’s harder to write, more error-prone, and much less portable.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',4,5,'2025-07-23_16-48-46','Yes, the OS will still work with a single thread. Since there’s only one thread in the system, the scheduler will always select it to run. When the print_continuously thread yields, the OS will perform a context switch, but it will just switch back to the same thread, because it\'s the only runnable task. This means the thread will keep running in a loop, yielding and being reselected each time. The system won’t crash or hang, but the context switch is unnecessary overhead in this case since there\'s no other work to do.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',4,5,'2025-07-23_17-09-56','Yes, the OS will still work with a single thread. Since there’s only one thread in the system, the scheduler will always select it to run. When the print_continuously thread yields, the OS will perform a context switch, but it will just switch back to the same thread, because it\'s the only runnable task. This means the thread will keep running in a loop, yielding and being reselected each time. The system won’t crash or hang, but the context switch is unnecessary overhead in this case since there\'s no other work to do.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',5,1,'2025-07-30_10-36-27','The user writes a function that takes a void* argument. They pass a pointer to data when calling osCreateThread. The OS saves this pointer in the thread’s stack. During context switch, the OS sets register R0 to this pointer. The CPU uses R0 to pass the value into the thread function. When the thread starts, it receives the pointer as its argument. This works because the ARM calling convention uses R0 for the first argument.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',5,1,'2025-07-30_10-55-54','The user writes a function that takes a void* argument. They pass a pointer to data when calling osCreateThread. The OS saves this pointer in the thread’s stack. During context switch, the OS sets register R0 to this pointer. The CPU uses R0 to pass the value into the thread function. When the thread starts, it receives the pointer as its argument. This works because the ARM calling convention uses R0 for the first argument.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',5,2,'2025-07-30_10-36-27','You cannot call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because these are already part of the context-switching mechanism. If you call osYield inside them, it would try to trigger another context switch while one is already in progress. This causes nested context switches, which can corrupt the stack or scheduler state. It breaks the kernel’s control flow and may crash the system or cause unpredictable behavior.\n',0,'s25'),('y8zhi',5,2,'2025-07-30_10-55-54','You cannot call osYield inside PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler, SVC_Handler_Main, or SysTick_Handler because osYield is a system call that triggers an interrupt. These handlers are already running inside an interrupt context. Calling another interrupt while in an interrupt leads to nested interrupts. This causes the stack to grow deeper, which can corrupt the context or cause a hardfault. It breaks the expected control flow and may crash the system. The OS must keep interrupt levels simple and separate to avoid this kind of failure.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',5,3,'2025-07-30_10-36-27','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms increases timer resolution. This means threads can respond more quickly and delays are more precise. It improves real-time performance and scheduling accuracy. However, it also increases interrupt frequency, which adds more overhead. The CPU spends more time handling SysTick interrupts and less time running threads. This can reduce overall efficiency and increase power consumption. There is a tradeoff between precision and performance.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',5,3,'2025-07-30_10-55-54','Setting the OS clock to 0.1ms increases timer resolution. This means threads can respond more quickly and delays are more precise. It improves real-time performance and scheduling accuracy. However, it also increases interrupt frequency, which adds more overhead. The CPU spends more time handling SysTick interrupts and less time running threads. This can reduce overall efficiency and increase power consumption. There is a tradeoff between precision and performance.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',5,4,'2025-07-30_10-36-27','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking means the thread must call osYield to give up control. The user decides when switching happens. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically interrupts threads using a timer like SysTick. The user does not need to do anything for switching.\nFor co-operative multitasking, the OS saves and restores context only when osYield is called. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS must handle context switches during interrupts. This means the OS needs to manage timing and priorities more carefully in pre-emptive systems.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',5,4,'2025-07-30_10-55-54','From the user perspective, co-operative multitasking means the thread must call osYield to give up control. The user decides when switching happens. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS automatically interrupts threads using a timer like SysTick. The user does not need to do anything for switching.\nFor co-operative multitasking, the OS saves and restores context only when osYield is called. In pre-emptive multitasking, the OS must handle context switches during interrupts. This means the OS needs to manage timing and priorities more carefully in pre-emptive systems.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',5,5,'2025-07-30_10-36-27','These two mechanisms work together because they both trigger the same context switch logic. Yielding uses a software interrupt like SVC to request a switch. Pre-emptive switching uses a hardware interrupt like SysTick to force a switch. Both paths end up calling the same PendSV handler. This means the OS can handle both types of switching in a unified way. The thread either gives up control voluntarily or gets interrupted by the timer, and the OS handles saving and restoring the thread context in both cases.\n',1,'s25'),('y8zhi',5,5,'2025-07-30_10-55-54','These two mechanisms work together because they both trigger the same context switch logic. Yielding uses a software interrupt like SVC to request a switch. Pre-emptive switching uses a hardware interrupt like SysTick to force a switch. Both paths end up calling the same PendSV handler. This means the OS can handle both types of switching in a unified way. The thread either gives up control voluntarily or gets interrupted by the timer, and the OS handles saving and restoring the thread context in both cases.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,1,'2025-05-19_20-10-18','The HAL is a set of software functions provided by STMicroelectronics to simplify programming on STM32 microcontrollers. The HAL provided a standardized set of functions that hides the complexity of writing low-level code, making it faster to develop embedded applications.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',1,1,'2025-05-19_20-28-48','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of software routines that provides a standardized interface between application code and the underlying hardware. The HAL provided a standardized set of functions that hides the complexity of writing low-level code, making it faster to develop embedded applications. Instead of writing low-level, device-specific code, developers can use high-level HAL functions to perform tasks like configuring timers, handling I/O, or communicating over UART. This abstraction not only speeds up development but also improves code portability and maintainability, as the same application code can often be reused across different microcontrollers or hardware platforms with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,1,'2025-05-19_21-03-23','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of software routines that provides a standardized interface between application code and the underlying hardware. The HAL provided a standardized set of functions that hides the complexity of writing low-level code, making it faster to develop embedded applications. Instead of writing low-level, device-specific code, developers can use high-level HAL functions to perform tasks like configuring timers, handling I/O, or communicating over UART. This abstraction not only speeds up development but also improves code portability and maintainability, as the same application code can often be reused across different microcontrollers or hardware platforms with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,1,'2025-05-19_21-13-08','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of software routines that provides a standardized interface between application code and the underlying hardware. The HAL provided a standardized set of functions that hides the complexity of writing low-level code, making it faster to develop embedded applications. Instead of writing low-level, device-specific code, developers can use high-level HAL functions to perform tasks like configuring timers, handling I/O, or communicating over UART. This abstraction not only speeds up development but also improves code portability and maintainability, as the same application code can often be reused across different microcontrollers or hardware platforms with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,1,'2025-05-19_21-31-11','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of software routines that provides a standardized interface between application code and the underlying hardware. The HAL provided a standardized set of functions that hides the complexity of writing low-level code, making it faster to develop embedded applications. Instead of writing low-level, device-specific code, developers can use high-level HAL functions to perform tasks like configuring timers, handling I/O, or communicating over UART. This abstraction not only speeds up development but also improves code portability and maintainability, as the same application code can often be reused across different microcontrollers or hardware platforms with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,1,'2025-06-23_10-44-31','The Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) is a set of software routines that provides a standardized interface between application code and the underlying hardware. The HAL provided a standardized set of functions that hides the complexity of writing low-level code, making it faster to develop embedded applications. Instead of writing low-level, device-specific code, developers can use high-level HAL functions to perform tasks like configuring timers, handling I/O, or communicating over UART. This abstraction not only speeds up development but also improves code portability and maintainability, as the same application code can often be reused across different microcontrollers or hardware platforms with minimal changes.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,2,'2025-05-19_20-10-18','The __io_putchar function is used to send a single character out through the UART, and it is required in order for the printf function to work on the microcontroller. Although printf is part of the standard C library, it doesn’t know how to send characters to hardware by default. On embedded systems, we must manually connect printf to UART2.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,2,'2025-05-19_20-28-48','The __io_putchar function is used to send a single character out through the UART, and it is required in order for the printf function to work on the microcontroller. Although printf is part of the standard C library, it doesn’t know how to send characters to hardware by default. On embedded systems, we must manually connect printf to UART2.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',1,2,'2025-05-19_21-03-23','The __io_putchar function is used to send a single character from the printf function to a specific hardware output. It is necessary because while printf is part of the standard C library, it doesn\'t know how to communicate with hardware on its own. In embedded systems, we must define how printf outputs data by linking it to a hardware-specific function like __io_putchar. This function allows us to route each character from printf to the desired peripheral. The implementation of __io_putchar is hardware-dependent. For example, if we wanted to print to a USB interface or an LCD screen instead of UART, we would need to rewrite the function to match that hardware. This highlights the flexibility and customization required in embedded systems programming.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',1,2,'2025-05-19_21-13-08','The __io_putchar function is used to send a single character from the printf function to a specific hardware output. In our case, the specific hardware output is the UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver/Transmitter) interface, which allows text to be displayed in a serial console. It is necessary because while printf is part of the standard C library, it doesn\'t know how to communicate with hardware on its own. In embedded systems, we must define how printf outputs data by linking it to a hardware-specific function like __io_putchar. This function allows us to route each character from printf to the desired peripheral. The implementation of __io_putchar is hardware-dependent. For example, if we wanted to print to a USB interface or an LCD screen instead of UART UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver/Transmitter), we would need to rewrite the function to match that hardware. This highlights the flexibility and customization required in embedded systems programming.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,2,'2025-05-19_21-31-11','The __io_putchar function is used to send a single character from the printf function to a specific hardware output. In our case, the specific hardware output is the UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver/Transmitter) interface, which allows text to be displayed in a serial console. It is necessary because while printf is part of the standard C library, it doesn\'t know how to communicate with hardware on its own. In embedded systems, we must define how printf outputs data by linking it to a hardware-specific function like __io_putchar. This function allows us to route each character from printf to the desired peripheral. The implementation of __io_putchar is hardware-dependent. For example, if we wanted to print to a USB interface or an LCD screen instead of UART UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver/Transmitter), we would need to rewrite the function to match that hardware. This highlights the flexibility and customization required in embedded systems programming.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,2,'2025-06-23_10-44-31','The __io_putchar function is used to send a single character from the printf function to a specific hardware output. In our case, the specific hardware output is the UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver/Transmitter) interface, which allows text to be displayed in a serial console. It is necessary because while printf is part of the standard C library, it doesn\'t know how to communicate with hardware on its own. In embedded systems, we must define how printf outputs data by linking it to a hardware-specific function like __io_putchar. This function allows us to route each character from printf to the desired peripheral. The implementation of __io_putchar is hardware-dependent. For example, if we wanted to print to a USB interface or an LCD screen instead of UART UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver/Transmitter), we would need to rewrite the function to match that hardware. This highlights the flexibility and customization required in embedded systems programming.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,3,'2025-05-19_20-10-18','We had to use the debugger in this lab because some of the info we needed like actual values of the stack pointers cannot be fully observed or verified using just printf. Printf cannot give us live updates of what is happening inside the processor. Debugger makes accessing register values like MSP and PSP easier and more accurate.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,3,'2025-05-19_20-28-48','We had to use the debugger in this lab because some of the info we needed like actual values of the stack pointers cannot be fully observed or verified using just printf. Printf cannot give us live updates of what is happening inside the processor. Debugger makes accessing register values like MSP and PSP easier and more accurate.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,3,'2025-05-19_21-03-23','We had to use the debugger in this lab because some of the info we needed like actual values of the stack pointers cannot be fully observed or verified using just printf. Printf cannot give us live updates of what is happening inside the processor. Debugger makes accessing register values like MSP and PSP easier and more accurate.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,3,'2025-05-19_21-13-08','We had to use the debugger in this lab because some of the info we needed like actual values of the stack pointers cannot be fully observed or verified using just printf. Printf cannot give us live updates of what is happening inside the processor. Debugger makes accessing register values like MSP and PSP easier and more accurate.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,3,'2025-05-19_21-31-11','We had to use the debugger in this lab because some of the info we needed like actual values of the stack pointers cannot be fully observed or verified using just printf. Printf cannot give us live updates of what is happening inside the processor. Debugger makes accessing register values like MSP and PSP easier and more accurate.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,3,'2025-06-23_10-44-31','We had to use the debugger in this lab because some of the info we needed like actual values of the stack pointers cannot be fully observed or verified using just printf. Printf cannot give us live updates of what is happening inside the processor. Debugger makes accessing register values like MSP and PSP easier and more accurate.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,4,'2025-05-19_20-10-18','The stack allocation method we used works by treating the stack memory as a fixed-size region. We first obtain the value of MSP_INIT_VAL which tells us where the main stack starts in memory. We can then identify the value of the process stack (PSP) by subtracting a fixed amount of memory (0x400 bytes) from the main stack’s starting address. This works because the stack grows downward in memory so allocating new stack space means moving to a lower address. \n\nwe need to know the initial location of the MSP so that we can determine where the stack begins and make sure that the PSP stack doesn’t overlap with the main stack. Knowing the MSP allows us to ensure proper memory separation for multitasking and threading management.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,4,'2025-05-19_20-28-48','The stack allocation method we used works by treating the stack memory as a fixed-size region. We first obtain the value of MSP_INIT_VAL which tells us where the main stack starts in memory. We can then identify the value of the process stack (PSP) by subtracting a fixed amount of memory (0x400 bytes) from the main stack’s starting address. This works because the stack grows downward in memory so allocating new stack space means moving to a lower address. \n\nwe need to know the initial location of the MSP so that we can determine where the stack begins and make sure that the PSP stack doesn’t overlap with the main stack. Knowing the MSP allows us to ensure proper memory separation for multitasking and threading management.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,4,'2025-05-19_21-03-23','The stack allocation method we used works by treating the stack memory as a fixed-size region. We first obtain the value of MSP_INIT_VAL which tells us where the main stack starts in memory. We can then identify the value of the process stack (PSP) by subtracting a fixed amount of memory (0x400 bytes) from the main stack’s starting address. This works because the stack grows downward in memory so allocating new stack space means moving to a lower address. \n\nwe need to know the initial location of the MSP so that we can determine where the stack begins and make sure that the PSP stack doesn’t overlap with the main stack. Knowing the MSP allows us to ensure proper memory separation for multitasking and threading management.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,4,'2025-05-19_21-13-08','The stack allocation method we used works by treating the stack memory as a fixed-size region. We first obtain the value of MSP_INIT_VAL which tells us where the main stack starts in memory. We can then identify the value of the process stack (PSP) by subtracting a fixed amount of memory (0x400 bytes) from the main stack’s starting address. This works because the stack grows downward in memory so allocating new stack space means moving to a lower address. \n\nwe need to know the initial location of the MSP so that we can determine where the stack begins and make sure that the PSP stack doesn’t overlap with the main stack. Knowing the MSP allows us to ensure proper memory separation for multitasking and threading management.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',1,4,'2025-05-19_21-31-11','The stack allocation method we used works by treating the stack memory as a fixed-size region. We first obtain the value of MSP_INIT_VAL which tells us where the main stack starts in memory. It is important to know the initial value of the MSP because it ensures that the PSP points to a valid memory address allocated for stack usage. If the MSP was unknown, the PSP could fall into a reserved memory region and lead to memory corruption and access violations. We can then identify the value of the process stack (PSP) by subtracting a fixed amount of memory (0x400 bytes) from the main stack’s starting address. This works because the stack grows downward in memory so allocating new stack space means moving to a lower address. \n\nwe need to know the initial location of the MSP so that we can determine where the stack begins and make sure that the PSP stack doesn’t overlap with the main stack. Knowing the MSP allows us to ensure proper memory separation for multitasking and threading management.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,4,'2025-06-23_10-44-31','The stack allocation method we used works by treating the stack memory as a fixed-size region. We first obtain the value of MSP_INIT_VAL which tells us where the main stack starts in memory. It is important to know the initial value of the MSP because it ensures that the PSP points to a valid memory address allocated for stack usage. If the MSP was unknown, the PSP could fall into a reserved memory region and lead to memory corruption and access violations. We can then identify the value of the process stack (PSP) by subtracting a fixed amount of memory (0x400 bytes) from the main stack’s starting address. This works because the stack grows downward in memory so allocating new stack space means moving to a lower address. \n\nwe need to know the initial location of the MSP so that we can determine where the stack begins and make sure that the PSP stack doesn’t overlap with the main stack. Knowing the MSP allows us to ensure proper memory separation for multitasking and threading management.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,5,'2025-05-19_20-10-18','The starting pointer for a given stack j would be defined as follows:\nADDRESS_STACKj=MSP_INIT_VAL−MAIN_STACK_SIZE−(j−1)×THREAD_STACK_SIZE\nTHREAD_STACK_SIZE is the size of each new thread stack.\nMAIN_STACK_SIZE is the size of the main stack.\nMSP_INIT_VAL is the starting address of the main stack.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',1,5,'2025-05-19_20-28-48','To allocate multiple stacks for threads using the method from the lab, we start with the initial main stack pointer value (MSP_INIT_VAL) and divide the stack memory into fixed-size blocks. Each thread stack has a defined size (THREAD_STACK_SIZE), and the main stack also has its own size (MAIN_STACK_SIZE). The starting address for thread stack j (where j starts at 1) is calculated using the formula:\nADDRESS_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) × THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis works because we treat the stack as a large block of memory and assign each thread its own space above the main stack. The calculation ensures non-overlapping stacks, and as long as all sizes are properly aligned and within the total MAX_STACK_SIZE, this method supports safe and predictable stack allocation for multithreading.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',1,5,'2025-05-19_21-03-23','To allocate multiple stacks for threads using the method from the lab, we start with the initial main stack pointer value (MSP_INIT_VAL) and divide the stack memory into fixed-size blocks. Each thread stack has a defined size (THREAD_STACK_SIZE), and the main stack also has its own size (MAIN_STACK_SIZE). The starting address for thread stack j (where j starts at 1) is calculated using the formula:\nADDRESS_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) × THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis works because we treat the stack as a large block of memory and assign each thread its own space above the main stack. To ensure these addresses align with the Cortex-M4 architecture, we need to make sure that THREAD_STACK_SIZE and MAIN_STACK_SIZE are multiple of 8 to ensure 8-byte alignment. This will ensure that the processor will not enter a fault state. As an additional check we should also make sure that the total memory allocated for all stacks does not exceed MAX_STACK_SIZE and stays within a valid RAM region. Following these rules will allow us to compute safe, non-overlapping and properly aligned stack addresses for all threads at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,5,'2025-05-19_21-13-08','To allocate multiple stacks for threads using the method from the lab, we start with the initial main stack pointer value (MSP_INIT_VAL) and divide the stack memory into fixed-size blocks. Each thread stack has a defined size (THREAD_STACK_SIZE), and the main stack also has its own size (MAIN_STACK_SIZE). The starting address for thread stack j (where j starts at 1) is calculated using the formula:\nADDRESS_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) × THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis works because we treat the stack as a large block of memory and assign each thread its own space above the main stack. To ensure these addresses align with the Cortex-M4 architecture, we need to make sure that THREAD_STACK_SIZE and MAIN_STACK_SIZE are multiple of 8 to ensure 8-byte alignment. This will ensure that the processor will not enter a fault state. As an additional check we should also make sure that the total memory allocated for all stacks does not exceed MAX_STACK_SIZE and stays within a valid RAM region. Following these rules will allow us to compute safe, non-overlapping and properly aligned stack addresses for all threads at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,5,'2025-05-19_21-31-11','To allocate multiple stacks for threads using the method from the lab, we start with the initial main stack pointer value (MSP_INIT_VAL) and divide the stack memory into fixed-size blocks. Each thread stack has a defined size (THREAD_STACK_SIZE), and the main stack also has its own size (MAIN_STACK_SIZE). The starting address for thread stack j (where j starts at 1) is calculated using the formula:\nADDRESS_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) × THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis works because we treat the stack as a large block of memory and assign each thread its own space above the main stack. To ensure these addresses align with the Cortex-M4 architecture, we need to make sure that THREAD_STACK_SIZE and MAIN_STACK_SIZE are multiple of 8 to ensure 8-byte alignment. This will ensure that the processor will not enter a fault state. As an additional check we should also make sure that the total memory allocated for all stacks does not exceed MAX_STACK_SIZE and stays within a valid RAM region. Following these rules will allow us to compute safe, non-overlapping and properly aligned stack addresses for all threads at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',1,5,'2025-06-23_10-44-31','To allocate multiple stacks for threads using the method from the lab, we start with the initial main stack pointer value (MSP_INIT_VAL) and divide the stack memory into fixed-size blocks. Each thread stack has a defined size (THREAD_STACK_SIZE), and the main stack also has its own size (MAIN_STACK_SIZE). The starting address for thread stack j (where j starts at 1) is calculated using the formula:\nADDRESS_STACK_j = MSP_INIT_VAL + MAIN_STACK_SIZE + (j - 1) × THREAD_STACK_SIZE.\nThis works because we treat the stack as a large block of memory and assign each thread its own space above the main stack. To ensure these addresses align with the Cortex-M4 architecture, we need to make sure that THREAD_STACK_SIZE and MAIN_STACK_SIZE are multiple of 8 to ensure 8-byte alignment. This will ensure that the processor will not enter a fault state. As an additional check we should also make sure that the total memory allocated for all stacks does not exceed MAX_STACK_SIZE and stays within a valid RAM region. Following these rules will allow us to compute safe, non-overlapping and properly aligned stack addresses for all threads at compile time.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,1,'2025-06-23_11-09-45','When I changed the program counter to hold the function pointer to print_continously, I passed the address of Print_continously into R0. The jump assembly function directly moves the value in r0 into the pc register. Since pc determines what instruction to execute next, the CPU immediately started executing the instructions at the address print continuously. As a result, the program began running print continuously which is an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,1,'2025-06-23_11-43-42','When I changed the program counter to hold the function pointer to print_continously, I passed the address of Print_continously into R0. The jump assembly function directly moves the value in r0 into the pc register. Since pc determines what instruction to execute next, the CPU immediately started executing the instructions at the address print continuously. As a result, the program began running print continuously which is an infinite loop.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',2,1,'2025-06-23_12-12-38','When I changed the program counter (PC) to hold the function pointer to print_continously, I was essentially making the CPU “call” that function. The function address is first stored in R0, then the jump assembly function directly moves the value in r0 into the pc register. Since pc determines what instruction to execute next, the CPU immediately started executing the instructions at the address print continuously. This is similar to a function call in higer-level programming, except we manually redirect the execution by changing the PC. As a result, the program began running print continuously which is an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,1,'2025-06-23_18-41-41','When I changed the program counter (PC) to hold the function pointer to print_continously, I was essentially making the CPU “call” that function. The function address is first stored in R0, then the jump assembly function directly moves the value in r0 into the pc register. Since pc determines what instruction to execute next, the CPU immediately started executing the instructions at the address print continuously. This is similar to a function call in higer-level programming, except we manually redirect the execution by changing the PC. As a result, the program began running print continuously which is an infinite loop.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,2,'2025-06-23_11-09-45','A system call is an software generated interrupt, also known as a trap.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',2,2,'2025-06-23_11-43-42','A system call is a software generated interrupt, also known as a trap. A system call is a mechanism that allows a user program to request a service from the Operating system to safely execute privilege operations on behalf of the user program.\nSystem call provides controlled access to hardware and system resources, ensuring stability. For example, instead of letting a program write directly to disk, which could lead to data corruption, programs use system calls like write () to request the OS to handle that safely.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',2,2,'2025-06-23_12-12-38','A system call is a special type of function that allows a user program to request a service from the Operating system like accessing files, memory allocation, or performing I/O operations. it’s like a function but it switches the CPU from user mode to kernel mode through a software interrupt called trap to safely execute privileged operations.\nSystem calls provide controlled access to hardware and system resources, ensuring stability. For example, instead of letting a program write directly to disk, which could lead to data corruption, programs use system call like write () to request the OS to perform the operation correctly and securely\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,2,'2025-06-23_18-41-41','A system call is a special type of function that allows a user program to request a service from the Operating system like accessing files, memory allocation, or performing I/O operations. it’s like a function but it switches the CPU from user mode to kernel mode through a software interrupt called trap to safely execute privileged operations.\nSystem calls provide controlled access to hardware and system resources, ensuring stability. For example, instead of letting a program write directly to disk, which could lead to data corruption, programs use system call like write () to request the OS to perform the operation correctly and securely\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,3,'2025-06-23_11-09-45','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing the memory and register values so the CPU can begin executing the thread if it had already been running. We manually pushed values onto a new stack including program status register(xPSR), program counter (PC), dummy values for register R0-R12. This stack was then assigned to the PSP, and when the thread was started the CPU popped these values into the actual register to begin execution. Storing register values on the stack is important because it allows the system to save and restore a thread state during context switching.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,3,'2025-06-23_11-43-42','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing the memory and register values so the CPU can begin executing the thread if it had already been running. We manually pushed values onto a new stack including program status register(xPSR), program counter (PC), dummy values for register R0-R12. This stack was then assigned to the PSP, and when the thread was started the CPU popped these values into the actual register to begin execution. Storing register values on the stack is important because it allows the system to save and restore a thread state during context switching.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',2,3,'2025-06-23_12-12-38','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing the memory and register values so the CPU can begin executing the thread if it had already been running. We manually pushed values onto a new stack including program status register(xPSR), program counter (PC), dummy values for register R0-R12. This stack was then assigned to the PSP, and when the thread was started the CPU popped these values into the actual register to begin execution. Storing register values on the stack is important because it allows the system to save and restore a thread state during context switching. \n\nnote if the values were stored in the wrong order, the CPU would restore incorrect data into the wrong register, which could lead to unpredictable behaviour or a crash. The stack order is also critical because it directly affects how the processor reconstructs the thread state to resume execution correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,3,'2025-06-23_18-41-41','Setting up a thread’s stack means preparing the memory and register values so the CPU can begin executing the thread if it had already been running. We manually pushed values onto a new stack including program status register(xPSR), program counter (PC), dummy values for register R0-R12. This stack was then assigned to the PSP, and when the thread was started the CPU popped these values into the actual register to begin execution. Storing register values on the stack is important because it allows the system to save and restore a thread state during context switching. \n\nnote if the values were stored in the wrong order, the CPU would restore incorrect data into the wrong register, which could lead to unpredictable behaviour or a crash. The stack order is also critical because it directly affects how the processor reconstructs the thread state to resume execution correctly.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,4,'2025-06-23_11-09-45','If we did not set the PSP using _set_PSP, the CPU would not know where the thread’s stack is located. The thread’s execution relies on the Process Stack Pointer to find its stack-including the saved register values like PC, LR, xPSR. Without initializing PSP to point to the correct stack the CPU would attempt to pop values from an invalid and uninitialized memory location. This would lead to a hard fault which is the processor’s way of signaling a critical error like accessing invalid memory.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,4,'2025-06-23_11-43-42','If we did not set the PSP using _set_PSP, the CPU would not know where the thread’s stack is located. The thread’s execution relies on the Process Stack Pointer to find its stack-including the saved register values like PC, LR, xPSR. Without initializing PSP to point to the correct stack the CPU would attempt to pop values from an invalid and uninitialized memory location. This would lead to a hard fault which is the processor’s way of signaling a critical error like accessing invalid memory.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,4,'2025-06-23_12-12-38','If we did not set the PSP using _set_PSP, the CPU would not know where the thread’s stack is located. The thread’s execution relies on the Process Stack Pointer to find its stack-including the saved register values like PC, LR, xPSR. Without initializing PSP to point to the correct stack the CPU would attempt to pop values from an invalid and uninitialized memory location. This would lead to a hard fault which is the processor’s way of signaling a critical error like accessing invalid memory.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,4,'2025-06-23_18-41-41','If we did not set the PSP using _set_PSP, the CPU would not know where the thread’s stack is located. The thread’s execution relies on the Process Stack Pointer to find its stack-including the saved register values like PC, LR, xPSR. Without initializing PSP to point to the correct stack the CPU would attempt to pop values from an invalid and uninitialized memory location. This would lead to a hard fault which is the processor’s way of signaling a critical error like accessing invalid memory.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,5,'2025-06-23_11-09-45','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously and instead printed something once and returned, the CPU would attempt to excute whatever is stored in the Link Register (LR). Since we manually set the LR to a dummy value during setup this return address would not be valid. When the function tries to return to that address, the CPU would likely jump to an undefined or restricted memory region resulting in a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,5,'2025-06-23_11-43-42','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously and instead printed something once and returned, the CPU would attempt to excute whatever is stored in the Link Register (LR). Since we manually set the LR to a dummy value during setup this return address would not be valid. When the function tries to return to that address, the CPU would likely jump to an undefined or restricted memory region resulting in a hard fault or crash.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',2,5,'2025-06-23_12-12-38','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously and instead printed something once and returned, the CPU would attempt to excute whatever is stored in the Link Register (LR). Since we manually set the LR to a dummy value during setup this return address would not be valid. When the function tries to return to that address, the CPU would likely jump to an undefined or restricted memory region resulting in a hard fault or crash.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',2,5,'2025-06-23_18-41-41','If the thread function didn’t loop continuously and instead printed something once and returned, the CPU would attempt to execute whatever is stored in the Link Register (LR). Since we manually set the LR to a dummy value during setup, there is no valid return address stored on the thread’s stack. When the function tries to return to that address, the CPU would likely jump to an undefined or restricted memory region. This results in a hard fault because the processor can’t execute instruction from that invalid memory. This highlights how essential it is for the thread’s stack to be initialized properly with valid return path.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,1,'2025-06-23_17-26-35','',0,'s25'),('z63yang',3,1,'2025-06-23_19-54-07','The process of multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable can be broken down into compilation, assembling and linking. During compilation each .c file is converted into .o files using assembly code generated by the compiler and an assembler. The linker then combines all .0 files into one executable; it connects all function calls and variable references across files; it also links in standard libraries like printf() from the C standard library.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',3,1,'2025-06-23_20-20-55','The process of multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable can be broken down into preprocessing, compiling, assembling, and linking. First each C file is preprocessed, where directives like #include and #define are expanded and comments are removed- resulting in pure C source file with all macros and headers inlined. During compilation each .c file is converted into assembly language where syntax errors are checked and performing optimization is done. The assembly code is then passed to the assembler, which converts it into machine-level object code; .o files. Finally, the linker then combines all .o files into one executable; it connects all function calls and variable references across files; it also links in standard libraries like printf() from the C standard library. This produces a single, complete executable file.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',3,1,'2025-06-23_20-55-28','The process of multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable can be broken down into preprocessing, compiling, and linking. First each C file is preprocessed, where directives like #include and #define are expanded and comments are removed- resulting in pure C source file with all macros and headers inlined. During compilation each .c file is converted into machine-level object files where syntax errors are checked and performing optimization is done Finally, the linker then combines all .o files into one executable; it connects all function calls and variable references across files; it also links in standard libraries like printf() from the C standard library. This produces a single, complete executable file.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,2,'2025-06-23_17-26-35','',0,'s25'),('z63yang',3,2,'2025-06-23_19-54-07','The #include directive is meant for header files not source files, when you write #include<kernel.c> you are telling the compiler to copy and paste the entire content of kernel.c into another .c file before compiling. This is a bad idea because it would cause problems such as double compilation where the same code might get compiled twice leading to duplicate symbols during linking. It also breaks modularity and builds system confusion.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,2,'2025-06-23_20-20-55','The #include directive is meant for header files not source files, when you write #include<kernel.c> you are telling the compiler to copy and paste the entire content of kernel.c into another .c file before compiling. This is a bad idea because it would cause problems such as double compilation where the same code might get compiled twice leading to duplicate symbols during linking. It also breaks modularity and builds system confusion.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,2,'2025-06-23_20-55-28','The #include directive is meant for header files not source files, when you write #include<kernel.c> you are telling the compiler to copy and paste the entire content of kernel.c into another .c file before compiling. This is a bad idea because it would cause problems such as double compilation where the same code might get compiled twice leading to duplicate symbols during linking. It also breaks modularity and builds system confusion.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,3,'2025-06-23_17-26-35','',0,'s25'),('z63yang',3,3,'2025-06-23_19-54-07','Initialize the kernel - call a function to initialize the kernel which prepares internal data struct and sets up memory stack space so threads can be created\nDefine your thread function - write a function that contains the code you want the thread to run, this function should take a single void function with no return\nCreate a thread using the creation function to register the thread. This sets up it’s stack and context so it can be scheduled to run\nStart the kernel – call the function that starts the kernel. This triggers a system call that loads the thread’s stack pointer and jumps into the thread’s execution\nThread is now running independently from the rest of the program.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',3,3,'2025-06-23_20-20-55','Initialize the kernel - call a function to initialize the kernel which prepares internal data struct and sets up memory stack space so threads can be created\nDefine your thread function - write a function that contains the code you want the thread to run, this function should take a single void function with no return\nProvide the function’s address and create the thread. This calls a thread creation function, passing it the address of the thread function. This allows the kernel to know what code the new thread should execute. This step also sets up the thread’s stack and initial context so it’s ready for exacution.\nStart the kernel – call the function that starts the kernel. This triggers a system call that loads the thread’s stack pointer and jumps into the thread’s execution\nThread is now running independently from the rest of the program.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,3,'2025-06-23_20-55-28','Initialize the kernel - call a function to initialize the kernel which prepares internal data struct and sets up memory stack space so threads can be created\nDefine your thread function - write a function that contains the code you want the thread to run, this function should take a single void function with no return\nProvide the function’s address and create the thread. This calls a thread creation function, passing it the address of the thread function. This allows the kernel to know what code the new thread should execute. This step also sets up the thread’s stack and initial context so it’s ready for exacution.\nStart the kernel – call the function that starts the kernel. This triggers a system call that loads the thread’s stack pointer and jumps into the thread’s execution\nThread is now running independently from the rest of the program.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,4,'2025-06-23_17-26-35','',0,'s25'),('z63yang',3,4,'2025-06-23_19-54-07','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory in the operating system used to provide individual stacks from each thread that the OS manages the purpose of a stack pool is thread isolation where each thread in an OS needs it own stack to store local variables, function return addresses, and saved CPU registers. It is also used for memory management as the stack pool allows the OS to allocate a portion of memory to each thread’s stack in a controlled and organized manner.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,4,'2025-06-23_20-20-55','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory in the operating system used to provide individual stacks from each thread that the OS manages the purpose of a stack pool is thread isolation where each thread in an OS needs it own stack to store local variables, function return addresses, and saved CPU registers. It is also used for memory management as the stack pool allows the OS to allocate a portion of memory to each thread’s stack in a controlled and organized manner.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,4,'2025-06-23_20-55-28','The stack pool is a reserved block of memory in the operating system used to provide individual stacks from each thread that the OS manages the purpose of a stack pool is thread isolation where each thread in an OS needs it own stack to store local variables, function return addresses, and saved CPU registers. It is also used for memory management as the stack pool allows the OS to allocate a portion of memory to each thread’s stack in a controlled and organized manner.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,5,'2025-06-23_17-26-35','',0,'s25'),('z63yang',3,5,'2025-06-23_19-54-07','The distinction between user and kernel files lies in their roles and responsibilities. The kernal code typically found in kernel.c and kernel.h contains the low-level functionality that makes the operating system work. This included thread creation, stack setup, interrupt and system call handling. It provides a set of service and abstraction that the user can rely on.\nThe user code usually written in main.c contains the application logic that the develop wants to run using the operating system. This includes defining thread functions and calling kernel-provided APIs such as osCreatThread() and osKernelStart(). From the user perspective, this distinction is important because it provided a clear and simplified interface for interacting with the system. The user does not need to understand or modify the kernel internals; they can simply rely on the kernel to manage the hardware and low-level operations safely and correctly. This separation enhances modularity security and ease of development\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,5,'2025-06-23_20-20-55','The distinction between user and kernel files lies in their roles and responsibilities. The kernal code typically found in kernel.c and kernel.h contains the low-level functionality that makes the operating system work. This included thread creation, stack setup, interrupt and system call handling. It provides a set of service and abstraction that the user can rely on.\nThe user code usually written in main.c contains the application logic that the develop wants to run using the operating system. This includes defining thread functions and calling kernel-provided APIs such as osCreatThread() and osKernelStart(). From the user perspective, this distinction is important because it provided a clear and simplified interface for interacting with the system. The user does not need to understand or modify the kernel internals; they can simply rely on the kernel to manage the hardware and low-level operations safely and correctly. This separation enhances modularity security and ease of development\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',3,5,'2025-06-23_20-55-28','The distinction between user and kernel files lies in their roles and responsibilities. The kernal code typically found in kernel.c and kernel.h contains the low-level functionality that makes the operating system work. This included thread creation, stack setup, interrupt and system call handling. It provides a set of service and abstraction that the user can rely on.\nThe user code usually written in main.c contains the application logic that the develop wants to run using the operating system. This includes defining thread functions and calling kernel-provided APIs such as osCreatThread() and osKernelStart(). From the user perspective, this distinction is important because it provided a clear and simplified interface for interacting with the system. The user does not need to understand or modify the kernel internals; they can simply rely on the kernel to manage the hardware and low-level operations safely and correctly. This separation enhances modularity security and ease of development\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',4,1,'2025-07-22_14-30-29','Round-robin scheduling using a queue consists of enqueuing threads in the order in which they should be run. The scheduler will then dequeue the current thread and enqueue it again at the back. This creates a loop that allows all the threads to run in a fair rotation. The queue is conceptually cleaner then that of an array with better rotation logic. It also scales better when combined with other functions like blocking or removing threads since inserting and removing from queue is easier then array indices manipulations.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',4,1,'2025-07-22_14-53-02','Round-robin scheduling using a queue consists of enqueuing threads in the order in which they should be run. The scheduler will then dequeue the current thread and enqueue it again at the back. This creates a loop that allows all the threads to run in a fair rotation.\nWhile the current array-based scheduler already behaves in a queue like manner through index tracking and modulo operations, using queue is conceptually cleaner with better rotation logic. It also scales better when combined with other functions like blocking or removing threads since inserting and removing from queue is easier than array indices manipulations. However a queue may introduce performance overhead due to dynamic memory access or pointer manipulation, depending on the implementation. In small embedded systems the overhead may outweigh the more readable benefits.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',4,2,'2025-07-22_14-30-29','PendSV_Handler is triggered when a context switch is needed. In the handler, the current thread’s registers r4-r11 is saved to its stack. The current stack pointer (psp) is stored in the TCB, threadArray. And the osSched() function selects the next thread to run, updates the Currentthread index and sets the psp to the new thread. PendSV_Handler then restores r4-r11 of the new thread from before and resumes it’s exacution.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',4,2,'2025-07-22_14-53-02','PendSV_Handler is triggered when a context switch is needed. It begins by reading the PSP (Process stack pointer) which points to the top of the thread’s stack. The current thread’s registers r4-r11 is pushed onto the stack, saving the current cpu context. The current stack pointer (PSP) is stored in the TCB, threadArray[currentthread]. And the osSched() function is called to select the next thread to run using a round robin scheduling, updates the Currentthread index and sets the PSP to the new thread from the TCB. Finally, PendSV_Handler pops r4-r11 of the new thread (via the updated PSP) and resumes it’s execution.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',4,3,'2025-07-22_14-30-29','This will leave to starvation of lower priority tasks. If a high priority task never yields or is allows ready lower priority tasks may not see any cpu time. This violates fairness can can cause unstable and unfair system behaviours. Some critical background tasks such as I/O or maintenance may never be ran.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',4,3,'2025-07-22_14-53-02','If the scheduler always selects the highest-priority task, then only the task with the highest priority would ever run. This will leave to starvation of lower priority tasks. If a high priority task never yields or is allows ready lower priority tasks may not see any cpu time. This violates fairness can can cause unstable and unfair system behaviours. Some critical background tasks such as I/O or maintenance may never be ran.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',4,4,'2025-07-22_14-30-29','some benefits of c\nC is more readable and easier to maintain, less prone to error then assembly code\nEasier to debug and add functionality\nMost importantly, it allows high level data structures like arrays and queues without manual memory manipulation\nSome downsides of c:\nMore overhead then assembly\nNot as much precise control over low level behaviours like registers.\nRequires coordination between assembly and c calling conventions\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',4,4,'2025-07-22_14-53-02','some benefits of c\nC is more readable and easier to maintain, less prone to error then assembly code\nEasier to debug and add functionality\nMost importantly, it allows high level data structures like arrays and queues without manual memory manipulation\nSome downsides of c:\nMore overhead then assembly\nNot as much precise control over low level behaviours like registers.\nRequires coordination between assembly and c calling conventions\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',4,5,'2025-07-22_14-30-29','Yes it would still work in this case as once the single thread yields, it will rotate to  the next thread, which in this case would be the same thread again. Behaviour is similar to a cooperative single-thread, the context switch has no effect since the thread would just yield and immediately be reselected to run.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',4,5,'2025-07-22_14-53-02','Yes it would still work in this case as once the single thread yields, it will rotate to  the next thread, which in this case would be the same thread again. Behaviour is similar to a cooperative single-thread, the context switch has no effect since the thread would just yield and immediately be reselected to run.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',5,1,'2025-07-22_18-42-26','The user begins by creating a struct or variable containing the desired argument data and passes a pointer to it when calling the thread creation function. Internally the OS sets up a new stack and during the context frame setup, it places the argument pointer in register R0, which is the register used for passing the first argument in ARM calling convention. The function then receives this pointer via it’s parameter when the thread launches. This works because the thread function accept a void* and the cpu uses R0 for the first parameter automatically when starting the thread context.\n',0,'s25'),('z63yang',5,1,'2025-07-22_19-20-55','The user begins by defining an argument struct or variable and passing a pointer to a thread creation function oscreatethread() or oscreatthreadwithdeadline(). This looks like passing void* arg from the user’s perspective. \n\ninternally, the os allocates a new stack for the thread and manually constructs the initial stack frame. This includes saving what the cpu would expect to dins in registers like R0–R3, R12, LR, PC, and xPSR when the thread first runs.\nTo pass the argument, the os writes the pointer value into the memory slot that represents r0 in the simulated exception stack frame. This puts the value on the stack so that when the CPU performs exception return, the hardware automatically pops the stack and loads r0 with the correct argument value, as if the function has been called normally.\nThis works because the cortex-m4 is designed to restore processor state from the stack during context switching, and OS takes advantage of that behaviour to simulate a function call into the thread with the correct parameter preloaded.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',5,2,'2025-07-22_18-42-26','Calling osYield inside any context switching function is unsafe because it triggers a supervisor call which causes an exception while already handling another exception. This leads to nested exceptions or recursive context switching which can corrupt the stack and crash the system. These handler are meant to only perform low level context management and invoking higher level context switching within them would also break the separation between os service layers.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',5,2,'2025-07-22_19-20-55','Calling osYield inside any context switching function is unsafe because it triggers a supervisor call which causes an exception while already handling another exception. This leads to nested exceptions or recursive context switching which can corrupt the stack and crash the system. These handler are meant to only perform low level context management and invoking higher level context switching within them would also break the separation between os service layers.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',5,3,'2025-07-22_18-42-26','Setting the os clock faster to 0.1ms would increase the responsiveness and any detailed time-based features like pre-emptive context switching and delays. It can allow for finer scheduling and more precise timing. However, some downsides include significant overhead due to more frequent interrupts, which can reduce overall system performance. Each tick requires the os to run the systick_handler which would reduce the time spent in useful work. Also power consumption may increase, and debugging is harder with higher interrupt rate.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',5,3,'2025-07-22_19-20-55','Setting the os clock faster to 0.1ms would increase the responsiveness and any detailed time-based features like pre-emptive context switching and delays. It can allow for finer scheduling and more precise timing. However, some downsides include significant overhead due to more frequent interrupts, which can reduce overall system performance. Each tick requires the os to run the systick_handler which would reduce the time spent in useful work. Also power consumption may increase, and debugging is harder with higher interrupt rate.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',5,4,'2025-07-22_18-42-26','From the user perspective co-operative multitasking required each thread to explicitly call osYield() to give up the cpu, meaning the program itself is responsible for fairness and responsiveness. In pre-emptive multitasking, the os uses a timer to interrupt threads automatically and switch context, regardless of the status of the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',5,4,'2025-07-22_19-20-55','From the user perspective co-operative multitasking required each thread to explicitly call osYield() to give up the cpu, meaning the program itself is responsible for fairness and responsiveness. In pre-emptive multitasking, the os uses a timer to interrupt threads automatically and switch context, regardless of the status of the thread.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',5,5,'2025-07-22_18-42-26','Co-operative and pre-emptive context switching can coexist because they use different triggers to invoke the same context switching mechanism. OsYield (Co-operative) uses an svc call where as pre-emptive uses SysTick to invoke pendSV. Both lead to the PendSV_Handler which performs the actual context switch. They can work together because they are triggered differently even though they share the same underlying infrastructure.\n',1,'s25'),('z63yang',5,5,'2025-07-22_19-20-55','Co-operative and pre-emptive context switching can coexist because they use different triggers to invoke the same context switching mechanism. OsYield (Co-operative) uses an svc call where as pre-emptive uses SysTick to invoke pendSV. Both lead to the PendSV_Handler which performs the actual context switch. They can work together because they are triggered differently even though they share the same underlying infrastructure.\n',1,'s25');
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `question_responses` ENABLE KEYS */;
UNLOCK TABLES;

--
-- Table structure for table `rubrics`
--

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS `rubrics`;
/*!40101 SET @saved_cs_client     = @@character_set_client */;
/*!50503 SET character_set_client = utf8mb4 */;
CREATE TABLE `rubrics` (
  `lab_number` tinyint NOT NULL,
  `question_number` tinyint NOT NULL,
  `rubric_text` mediumtext COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci NOT NULL,
  `term` varchar(10) COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci NOT NULL DEFAULT 's25',
  PRIMARY KEY (`lab_number`,`question_number`)
) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4 COLLATE=utf8mb4_unicode_ci;
/*!40101 SET character_set_client = @saved_cs_client */;

--
-- Dumping data for table `rubrics`
--

LOCK TABLES `rubrics` WRITE;
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `rubrics` DISABLE KEYS */;
INSERT INTO `rubrics` VALUES (1,1,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: In your own words, explain what the HAL is and why it is used in a general embedded project.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly identify that the HAL is the Hardware Abstraction Layer. Students correctly identify that the HAL is used to simplify interaction with the hardware. Studens provide some additional, correct details regarding why it is useful to have a HAL. \n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors, omissions, or is not written with sufficient detail.','s25'),(1,2,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: In your own words, explain what the __io_putchar function does and why it is needed.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly identify that the __io_putchar function is a helper function that enables printing to the UART or console. Students correctly identify that the function is used by the printf function. Students correctly identify that the function is required because different projects may require different hardware for printing, so the programmer must write it.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions.','s25'),(1,3,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: Why did we have to use the debugger in this lab? Why didn’t we just use printf to instrument our code and make sure it worked?\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly identify that the debugger was required in order to examine the values stored in the registers. They state that printf cannot be used for this purpose, and provide a reasonable explanation as to why not.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(1,4,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students had to answer is: Explain how the stack allocation method we used works. Start by assuming that we have obtained the value for MSP_init_val. How did we get a new value for PSP_val? Why did we need to know the initial location of MSP for this to work?\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly identify that the new value for PSP is computed by offsetting the value of MSP_init_val. Students correctly identify that if we did not know the initial value for MSP, we could not be sure that our value for PSP would be a valid stack address.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(1,5,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: Say that we wanted to use the same stack allocation method to allocate a number of new stacks for use in multiple threads. Say this number is known at compile-time. What would we need to change in our code to make this work? \n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly identify that, to find multiple new stacks, we would need to use the same basic method of offsetting the address from a known, valid stack location. Students correctly identify that we would need to offset each new stack address in some way that ensures that the new address remains a valid stack address.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(2,1,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: In your own words, explain what happened in this lab when you changed PC to hold the function pointer to your print continuously function.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly explain that changing PC to hold the function pointer to a function acts like calling that function. Students explain that once PC is loaded with an instruction address, it immediately begins executing code at that address. \n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors, omissions, or is not written with sufficient detail.','s25'),(2,2,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. In your own words, explain what a system call is\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly identify that a system call is a function call. Students correctly explain that system calls are how user code (or threads or programs) interact with the kernel. Students correctly explain that system calls allow user code to request services from the kernel or that system calls allow us to ensure that hardware access is protected from bad or untrustworthy user code.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions.','s25'),(2,3,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: Explain in your own words how the thread’s stack was set up. Comment on what it means to “set up a  thread’s stack”, and why we need to store register values on the stack.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correct identify that setting up a thread\'s stack involves finding a valid location for its stack in memory then writing register values to it. Students mention that the register values must be stored in the correct order (do not expect or evaluate whether the order stated in the answer is correct. The key is that they know that the order matters, not that they know what the order is). Students mention that we need to store register values on the stack so that when we load a thread we can load the register values and the thread will have all of the data it needs to execute.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(2,4,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students had to answer is: In the switch/case in SVC_Handler_Main, we set PSP using the __set_PSP function before we could run the thread. If we didn’t do this, the code would crash in a hard fault. Explain why\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students explain that the thread\'s stack is not at the same location as the CPU\'s stack pointer. Students explain that we are using PSP for threads, so we need to switch to PSP and make sure that PSP\'s value equals the thread\'s stack pointer address, otherwise the thread would not run correctly.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(2,5,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: Our thread function looped continuously. Assume that it didn’t, and instead it simply printed something once and returned. What would happen?\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly mention that the return address that is needed to return from a thread is not stored on the thread\'s stack. Students explain that if we were to attempt to return from a thread at this point the wrong return address would be loaded and the CPU would hardfault. Students must say that the CPU would hardfault.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(3,1,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: In your own words, explain the process by which multiple *.c files get compiled into a single executable.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students must correctly explain that there are three steps: preprocessing, compiling, and linking, in that order. Students must mention all three steps and briefly explain what they each do. Students correctly explain that multiple object or .o files are put together into a single executable. \n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors, omissions, or is not written with sufficient detail.','s25'),(3,2,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. In your own words, explain why you cannot use an #include directive to on a *.c file. \n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly identify that including a .c file can cause errors with multiple definitions in the executable.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions.','s25'),(3,3,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: Write a brief explanation, from the user’s perspective, about how to set up and start a thread. Do not include code, simply explain the process in words.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students mention that the thread function or its address must be known. Students mention that the user must write the thread function. Students mention that the kernel must be initialized. Students mention that the threads must be created or registered with the kernel. Students mention that the kernel must be started. Students mention that function calls must be used for this.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(3,4,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students had to answer is: Explain what the stack pool is for, and how it can be used in a future lab to enable multiple threads to exist in our OS.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students explain that the stack pool is a region in memory where the stacks for the operating system and threads can go. Students correctly explain that the stack pool can be subdivided and pieces of it can be allocated to each thread.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(3,5,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students had to answer is: Explain the distinction between the “user” and the “kernel” in terms of what goes into which file. Why is this distinction important, from the user’s perspective?\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly mention that the user is responsible for writing the threads and interacting with the kernel via its functions or API calls. Students correctly mention that the kernel is responsible for controlling and enabling access to the hardware or other things such as memory. Students correctly mention that the separation between user and kernel is important because it simplifies the user\'s job and lets them focus on their application, rather than low-level programming.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(4,1,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: In the lab manual, it is mentioned that using a queue to store your TCBs is interesting. Explain how round-robin scheduling can work using a queue instead of just an array. Are there any benefits?\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students must correctly explain that using a queue for round-robin could work, with the top of the queue holding the task that will run next. Students must note one of two things: either that the current round-robin scheduler essentially is using this queue-type method already, or that there would be additional overhead without much functionality difference. \n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors, omissions, or is not written with sufficient detail.','s25'),(4,2,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. Explain in your own words how the PendSV_Handler function executes a context switch. \n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students must mention the following steps, in order: first, the value of PSP is read to locate the thread\'s stack. Then the registers R4-R11 are pushed onto the stack. The osSched function is called and a new thread is chosen. The new thread\'s stack is located, and the registers R4-R11 are popped from the stack.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions.','s25'),(4,3,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: Presume we modify our OS as follows: we add an integer, “priority”, to the TCB, where a higher integer means a higher priority, and we change the scheduler so that it always chooses the task with highest priority. This is a bad idea. Why?\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly mention that, with the way our scheduler works, only one task would ever run, which is the task with the highest priority. Students may also mention that this can be avoided if priority were allowed to be changed, but this is not a necessary part of the answer.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(4,4,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students had to answer is: In PendSV_Handler we called a C function, osSched. Similarly, in SVC_Handler, we called a C function, SVC_HandlerMain. Theoretically we could have done all of the required tasks in assembly. Comment on the benefits and downsides to calling the C functions like we did compared to writing everything in assembly.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Here, we are just looking for a reasonable amount of detail and correct answers. This question is mainly opinion based, so as long as it is clear what the student\'s opinion is, the answer is entirely correct, and there are at least three points made, the student meets expectation.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(4,5,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students had to answer is: What would happen if, instead of creating multiple threads as we did in the lab, you created only a single thread? For concreteness, assume that the single thread is just the print_continuously thread from the previous two labs, modified so that it yields once it has printed. Will the OS still work? Explain.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students correctly mention that the OS would still work. Students correctly mention that the thread would run, yield, and be selected by the scheduler to run again.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(5,1,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: Explain the process by which an argument gets passed into a thread. Begin with what the user has to do, then talk about what the OS has to do, and finally explain why it works.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students must correctly explain that the user creates the variable or allocates the required memory, then passes a pointer into the osCreateThread function, then the pointer gets placed onto the stack at the location corresponding to R0 by the OS. Students must also reference the ABI for why this works, or something equivalent (like saying it is the \"runtime environment\" that makes this work). Students may explain how the thread itself uses the input argument, but that is not required.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors, omissions, or is not written with sufficient detail.','s25'),(5,2,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: Why can’t you call osYield in any of our context-switching functions (PendSV_Handler, SVC_Handler and its associated SVC_Handler_Main, and SysTick_Handler)? What happens if you do, and why is it bad?\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students must explain that calling osYield triggers a system call, which is another interrupt. Since we are already in an interrupt, this can corrupt the stack, and the chip can hardfault.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions.','s25'),(5,3,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students are answering is: In this lab we set up the fundamental OS clock to be 1ms. Say we wanted it to be 0.1ms instead. Discuss the benefits and downsides of doing this.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Here, we are just looking for a reasonable amount of detail and correct answers. This question is mainly opinion based, so as long as it is clear what the student\'s opinion is, the answer is entirely correct. Students may mention many things. Examples of benefits include that the OS can respond to faster events, the OS can have finer-grained deadlines etc. Examples of downsides include the OS will become less efficient, it will be servicing more interrupts per second, and we may overload the processor. Look for two to three benefits and two to three downsides that are reasonable. Use your best jugement here.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(5,4,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students had to answer is: Explain the difference between co-operative and pre-emptive multitasking from the user perspective and in terms of what the OS has to do for each one.\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: The student must mention that the thread must yield in co-operative multitasking. The student must mention that in pre-emptive multitasking, if the thread does not yield, the os has to trigger an interrupt and force a context switch.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25'),(5,5,'You are a helpful teaching assistant giving detailed, actionable feedback to students on their lab reports so that they can improve. The lab is about systems programming on the Cortex M4 microcontroller. The question the students had to answer is: The final exercise of this lab had you mix together yielding and time-based pre-emptive context switching. How is it possible for these two mechanisms to work together?\n\nThe rubric is:\n\nMeets Expectation: Students must mention that context switching in our OS relies on the PendSV handler (or that it relies on an interrupt if they do not specify which one), and that switching between threads uses the same logic or code whether the switch happened pre-emptively or co-operatively. Therefore, how the switch was triggered doesn\'t matter.\n\nDoes not Meet Expectation: The student work either contains errors or omissions','s25');
